if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-599"]="While the present century was in its teens, and on one sunshiny morning in June, there drove up to the great iron gate of Miss Pinkerton's academy for young ladies, on Chiswick Mall, a large family coach, with two fat horses in blazing harness, driven by a fat coachman in a three-cornered hat and wig, at the rate of four miles an hour. A black servant, who reposed on the box beside the fat coachman, uncurled his bandy legs as soon as the equipage drew up opposite Miss Pinkerton's shining brass plate, and as he pulled the bell at least a score of young heads were seen peering out of the narrow windows of the stately old brick house. Nay, the acute observer might have recognized the little red nose of good-natured Miss Jemima Pinkerton herself, rising over some geranium pots in the window of that lady's own drawing-room.\n\n\"It is Mrs. Sedley's coach, sister,\" said Miss Jemima. \"Sambo, the black servant, has just rung the bell; and the coachman has a new red waistcoat.\"\n\n\"Have you completed all the necessary preparations incident to Miss Sedley's departure, Miss Jemima?\" asked Miss Pinkerton herself, that majestic lady; the Semiramis of Hammersmith, the friend of Doctor Johnson, the correspondent of Mrs. Chapone herself.\n\n\"The girls were up at four this morning, packing her trunks, sister,\" replied Miss Jemima; \"we have made her a bow-pot.\"\n\n\"Say a bouquet, sister Jemima, 'tis more genteel.\"\n\n\"Well, a booky as big almost as a haystack; I have put up two bottles of the gillyflower water for Mrs. Sedley, and the receipt for making it, in Amelia's box.\"\n\n\"And I trust, Miss Jemima, you have made a copy of Miss Sedley's account. This is it, is it? Very good--ninety-three pounds, four shillings. Be kind enough to address it to John Sedley, Esquire, and to seal this billet which I have written to his lady.\"\n\nIn Miss Jemima's eyes an autograph letter of her sister, Miss Pinkerton, was an object of as deep veneration as would have been a letter from a sovereign. Only when her pupils quitted the establishment, or when they were about to be married, and once, when poor Miss Birch died of the scarlet fever, was Miss Pinkerton known to write personally to the parents of her pupils; and it was Jemima's opinion that if anything could console Mrs. Birch for her daughter's loss, it would be that pious and eloquent composition in which Miss Pinkerton announced the event.\n\nIn the present instance Miss Pinkerton's \"billet\" was to the following effect:--\n\nThe Mall, Chiswick, June 15, 18\n\nMADAM,--After her six years' residence at the Mall, I have the honour and happiness of presenting Miss Amelia Sedley to her parents, as a young lady not unworthy to occupy a fitting position in their polished and refined circle. Those virtues which characterize the young English gentlewoman, those accomplishments which become her birth and station, will not be found wanting in the amiable Miss Sedley, whose INDUSTRY and OBEDIENCE have endeared her to her instructors, and whose delightful sweetness of temper has charmed her AGED and her YOUTHFUL companions.\n\nIn music, in dancing, in orthography, in every variety of embroidery and needlework, she will be found to have realized her friends' fondest wishes. In geography there is still much to be desired; and a careful and undeviating use of the backboard, for four hours daily during the next three years, is recommended as necessary to the acquirement of that dignified DEPORTMENT AND CARRIAGE, so requisite for every young lady of FASHION.\n\nIn the principles of religion and morality, Miss Sedley will be found worthy of an establishment which has been honoured by the presence of THE GREAT LEXICOGRAPHER, and the patronage of the admirable Mrs. Chapone. In leaving the Mall, Miss Amelia carries with her the hearts of her companions, and the affectionate regards of her mistress, who has the honour to subscribe herself,\n\nMadam, Your most obliged humble servant, BARBARA PINKERTON\n\nP.S.--Miss Sharp accompanies Miss Sedley. It is particularly requested that Miss Sharp's stay in Russell Square may not exceed ten days. The family of distinction with whom she is engaged, desire to avail themselves of her services as soon as possible.\n\nThis letter completed, Miss Pinkerton proceeded to write her own name, and Miss Sedley's, in the fly-leaf of a Johnson's Dictionary--the interesting work which she invariably presented to her scholars, on their departure from the Mall. On the cover was inserted a copy of \"Lines addressed to a young lady on quitting Miss Pinkerton's school, at the Mall; by the late revered Doctor Samuel Johnson.\" In fact, the Lexicographer's name was always on the lips of this majestic woman, and a visit he had paid to her was the cause of her reputation and her fortune.\n\nBeing commanded by her elder sister to get \"the Dictionary\" from the cupboard, Miss Jemima had extracted two copies of the book from the receptacle in question. When Miss Pinkerton had finished the inscription in the first, Jemima, with rather a dubious and timid air, handed her the second.\n\n\"For whom is this, Miss Jemima?\" said Miss Pinkerton, with awful coldness.\n\n\"For Becky Sharp,\" answered Jemima, trembling very much, and blushing over her withered face and neck, as she turned her back on her sister. \"For Becky Sharp: she's going too.\"\n\n\"MISS JEMIMA!\" exclaimed Miss Pinkerton, in the largest capitals. \"Are you in your senses? Replace the Dixonary in the closet, and never venture to take such a liberty in future.\"\n\n\"Well, sister, it's only two-and-ninepence, and poor Becky will be miserable if she don't get one.\"\n\n\"Send Miss Sedley instantly to me,\" said Miss Pinkerton. And so venturing not to say another word, poor Jemima trotted off, exceedingly flurried and nervous.\n\nMiss Sedley's papa was a merchant in London, and a man of some wealth; whereas Miss Sharp was an articled pupil, for whom Miss Pinkerton had done, as she thought, quite enough, without conferring upon her at parting the high honour of the Dixonary.\n\nAlthough schoolmistresses' letters are to be trusted no more nor less than churchyard epitaphs; yet, as it sometimes happens that a person departs this life who is really deserving of all the praises the stone cutter carves over his bones; who IS a good Christian, a good parent, child, wife, or husband; who actually DOES leave a disconsolate family to mourn his loss; so in academies of the male and female sex it occurs every now and then that the pupil is fully worthy of the praises bestowed by the disinterested instructor. Now, Miss Amelia Sedley was a young lady of this singular species; and deserved not only all that Miss Pinkerton said in her praise, but had many charming qualities which that pompous old Minerva of a woman could not see, from the differences of rank and age between her pupil and herself.\n\nFor she could not only sing like a lark, or a Mrs. Billington, and dance like Hillisberg or Parisot; and embroider beautifully; and spell as well as a Dixonary itself; but she had such a kindly, smiling, tender, gentle, generous heart of her own, as won the love of everybody who came near her, from Minerva herself down to the poor girl in the scullery, and the one-eyed tart-woman's daughter, who was permitted to vend her wares once a week to the young ladies in the Mall. She had twelve intimate and bosom friends out of the twenty-four young ladies. Even envious Miss Briggs never spoke ill of her; high and mighty Miss Saltire (Lord Dexter's granddaughter) allowed that her figure was genteel; and as for Miss Swartz, the rich woolly-haired mulatto from St. Kitt's, on the day Amelia went away, she was in such a passion of tears that they were obliged to send for Dr. Floss, and half tipsify her with salvolatile. Miss Pinkerton's attachment was, as may be supposed from the high position and eminent virtues of that lady, calm and dignified; but Miss Jemima had already whimpered several times at the idea of Amelia's departure; and, but for fear of her sister, would have gone off in downright hysterics, like the heiress (who paid double) of St. Kitt's. Such luxury of grief, however, is only allowed to parlour-boarders. Honest Jemima had all the bills, and the washing, and the mending, and the puddings, and the plate and crockery, and the servants to superintend. But why speak about her? It is probable that we shall not hear of her again from this moment to the end of time, and that when the great filigree iron gates are once closed on her, she and her awful sister will never issue therefrom into this little world of history.\n\nBut as we are to see a great deal of Amelia, there is no harm in saying, at the outset of our acquaintance, that she was a dear little creature; and a great mercy it is, both in life and in novels, which (and the latter especially) abound in villains of the most sombre sort, that we are to have for a constant companion so guileless and good-natured a person. As she is not a heroine, there is no need to describe her person; indeed I am afraid that her nose was rather short than otherwise, and her cheeks a great deal too round and red for a heroine; but her face blushed with rosy health, and her lips with the freshest of smiles, and she had a pair of eyes which sparkled with the brightest and honestest good-humour, except indeed when they filled with tears, and that was a great deal too often; for the silly thing would cry over a dead canary-bird; or over a mouse, that the cat haply had seized upon; or over the end of a novel, were it ever so stupid; and as for saying an unkind word to her, were any persons hard-hearted enough to do so--why, so much the worse for them. Even Miss Pinkerton, that austere and godlike woman, ceased scolding her after the first time, and though she no more comprehended sensibility than she did Algebra, gave all masters and teachers particular orders to treat Miss Sedley with the utmost gentleness, as harsh treatment was injurious to her.\n\nSo that when the day of departure came, between her two customs of laughing and crying, Miss Sedley was greatly puzzled how to act. She was glad to go home, and yet most woefully sad at leaving school. For three days before, little Laura Martin, the orphan, followed her about like a little dog. She had to make and receive at least fourteen presents--to make fourteen solemn promises of writing every week: \"Send my letters under cover to my grandpapa, the Earl of Dexter,\" said Miss Saltire (who, by the way, was rather shabby). \"Never mind the postage, but write every day, you dear darling,\" said the impetuous and woolly-headed, but generous and affectionate Miss Swartz; and the orphan little Laura Martin (who was just in round-hand), took her friend's hand and said, looking up in her face wistfully, \"Amelia, when I write to you I shall call you Mamma.\" All which details, I have no doubt, JONES, who reads this book at his Club, will pronounce to be excessively foolish, trivial, twaddling, and ultra-sentimental. Yes; I can see Jones at this minute (rather flushed with his joint of mutton and half pint of wine), taking out his pencil and scoring under the words \"foolish, twaddling,\" &c., and adding to them his own remark of \"QUITE TRUE.\" Well, he is a lofty man of genius, and admires the great and heroic in life and novels; and so had better take warning and go elsewhere.\n\nWell, then. The flowers, and the presents, and the trunks, and bonnet-boxes of Miss Sedley having been arranged by Mr. Sambo in the carriage, together with a very small and weather-beaten old cow's-skin trunk with Miss Sharp's card neatly nailed upon it, which was delivered by Sambo with a grin, and packed by the coachman with a corresponding sneer--the hour for parting came; and the grief of that moment was considerably lessened by the admirable discourse which Miss Pinkerton addressed to her pupil. Not that the parting speech caused Amelia to philosophise, or that it armed her in any way with a calmness, the result of argument; but it was intolerably dull, pompous, and tedious; and having the fear of her schoolmistress greatly before her eyes, Miss Sedley did not venture, in her presence, to give way to any ebullitions of private grief. A seed-cake and a bottle of wine were produced in the drawing-room, as on the solemn occasions of the visits of parents, and these refreshments being partaken of, Miss Sedley was at liberty to depart.\n\n\"You'll go in and say good-by to Miss Pinkerton, Becky!\" said Miss Jemima to a young lady of whom nobody took any notice, and who was coming downstairs with her own bandbox.\n\n\"I suppose I must,\" said Miss Sharp calmly, and much to the wonder of Miss Jemima; and the latter having knocked at the door, and receiving permission to come in, Miss Sharp advanced in a very unconcerned manner, and said in French, and with a perfect accent, \"Mademoiselle, je viens vous faire mes adieux.\"\n\nMiss Pinkerton did not understand French; she only directed those who did: but biting her lips and throwing up her venerable and Roman-nosed head (on the top of which figured a large and solemn turban), she said, \"Miss Sharp, I wish you a good morning.\" As the Hammersmith Semiramis spoke, she waved one hand, both by way of adieu, and to give Miss Sharp an opportunity of shaking one of the fingers of the hand which was left out for that purpose.\n\nMiss Sharp only folded her own hands with a very frigid smile and bow, and quite declined to accept the proffered honour; on which Semiramis tossed up her turban more indignantly than ever. In fact, it was a little battle between the young lady and the old one, and the latter was worsted. \"Heaven bless you, my child,\" said she, embracing Amelia, and scowling the while over the girl's shoulder at Miss Sharp. \"Come away, Becky,\" said Miss Jemima, pulling the young woman away in great alarm, and the drawing-room door closed upon them for ever.\n\nThen came the struggle and parting below. Words refuse to tell it. All the servants were there in the hall--all the dear friends--all the young ladies--the dancing-master who had just arrived; and there was such a scuffling, and hugging, and kissing, and crying, with the hysterical YOOPS of Miss Swartz, the parlour-boarder, from her room, as no pen can depict, and as the tender heart would fain pass over. The embracing was over; they parted--that is, Miss Sedley parted from her friends. Miss Sharp had demurely entered the carriage some minutes before. Nobody cried for leaving HER.\n\nSambo of the bandy legs slammed the carriage door on his young weeping mistress. He sprang up behind the carriage. \"Stop!\" cried Miss Jemima, rushing to the gate with a parcel.\n\n\"It's some sandwiches, my dear,\" said she to Amelia. \"You may be hungry, you know; and Becky, Becky Sharp, here's a book for you that my sister--that is, I--Johnson's Dixonary, you know; you mustn't leave us without that. Good-by. Drive on, coachman. God bless you!\"\n\nAnd the kind creature retreated into the garden, overcome with emotion.\n\nBut, lo! and just as the coach drove off, Miss Sharp put her pale face out of the window and actually flung the book back into the garden.\n\nThis almost caused Jemima to faint with terror. \"Well, I never\"--said she--\"what an audacious\"--Emotion prevented her from completing either sentence. The carriage rolled away; the great gates were closed; the bell rang for the dancing lesson. The world is before the two young ladies; and so, farewell to Chiswick Mall.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\nIn Which Miss Sharp and Miss Sedley Prepare to Open the Campaign\nWhen Miss Sharp had performed the heroical act mentioned in the last chapter, and had seen the Dixonary, flying over the pavement of the little garden, fall at length at the feet of the astonished Miss Jemima, the young lady's countenance, which had before worn an almost livid look of hatred, assumed a smile that perhaps was scarcely more agreeable, and she sank back in the carriage in an easy frame of mind, saying--\"So much for the Dixonary; and, thank God, I'm out of Chiswick.\"\n\nMiss Sedley was almost as flurried at the act of defiance as Miss Jemima had been; for, consider, it was but one minute that she had left school, and the impressions of six years are not got over in that space of time. Nay, with some persons those awes and terrors of youth last for ever and ever. I know, for instance, an old gentleman of sixty-eight, who said to me one morning at breakfast, with a very agitated countenance, \"I dreamed last night that I was flogged by Dr. Raine.\" Fancy had carried him back five-and-fifty years in the course of that evening. Dr. Raine and his rod were just as awful to him in his heart, then, at sixty-eight, as they had been at thirteen. If the Doctor, with a large birch, had appeared bodily to him, even at the age of threescore and eight, and had said in awful voice, \"Boy, take down your pant--\"? Well, well, Miss Sedley was exceedingly alarmed at this act of insubordination.\n\n\"How could you do so, Rebecca?\" at last she said, after a pause.\n\n\"Why, do you think Miss Pinkerton will come out and order me back to the black-hole?\" said Rebecca, laughing.\n\n\"No: but--\"\n\n\"I hate the whole house,\" continued Miss Sharp in a fury. \"I hope I may never set eyes on it again. I wish it were in the bottom of the Thames, I do; and if Miss Pinkerton were there, I wouldn't pick her out, that I wouldn't. O how I should like to see her floating in the water yonder, turban and all, with her train streaming after her, and her nose like the beak of a wherry.\"\n\n\"Hush!\" cried Miss Sedley.\n\n\"Why, will the black footman tell tales?\" cried Miss Rebecca, laughing. \"He may go back and tell Miss Pinkerton that I hate her with all my soul; and I wish he would; and I wish I had a means of proving it, too. For two years I have only had insults and outrage from her. I have been treated worse than any servant in the kitchen. I have never had a friend or a kind word, except from you. I have been made to tend the little girls in the lower schoolroom, and to talk French to the Misses, until I grew sick of my mother tongue. But that talking French to Miss Pinkerton was capital fun, wasn't it? She doesn't know a word of French, and was too proud to confess it. I believe it was that which made her part with me; and so thank Heaven for French. Vive la France! Vive l'Empereur! Vive Bonaparte!\"\n\n\"O Rebecca, Rebecca, for shame!\" cried Miss Sedley; for this was the greatest blasphemy Rebecca had as yet uttered; and in those days, in England, to say, \"Long live Bonaparte!\" was as much as to say, \"Long live Lucifer!\" \"How can you--how dare you have such wicked, revengeful thoughts?\"\n\n\"Revenge may be wicked, but it's natural,\" answered Miss Rebecca. \"I'm no angel.\" And, to say the truth, she certainly was not.\n\nFor it may be remarked in the course of this little conversation (which took place as the coach rolled along lazily by the river side) that though Miss Rebecca Sharp has twice had occasion to thank Heaven, it has been, in the first place, for ridding her of some person whom she hated, and secondly, for enabling her to bring her enemies to some sort of perplexity or confusion; neither of which are very amiable motives for religious gratitude, or such as would be put forward by persons of a kind and placable disposition. Miss Rebecca was not, then, in the least kind or placable. All the world used her ill, said this young misanthropist, and we may be pretty certain that persons whom all the world treats ill, deserve entirely the treatment they get. The world is a looking-glass, and gives back to every man the reflection of his own face. Frown at it, and it will in turn look sourly upon you; laugh at it and with it, and it is a jolly kind companion; and so let all young persons take their choice. This is certain, that if the world neglected Miss Sharp, she never was known to have done a good action in behalf of anybody; nor can it be expected that twenty-four young ladies should all be as amiable as the heroine of this work, Miss Sedley (whom we have selected for the very reason that she was the best-natured of all, otherwise what on earth was to have prevented us from putting up Miss Swartz, or Miss Crump, or Miss Hopkins, as heroine in her place!) it could not be expected that every one should be of the humble and gentle temper of Miss Amelia Sedley; should take every opportunity to vanquish Rebecca's hard-heartedness and ill-humour; and, by a thousand kind words and offices, overcome, for once at least, her hostility to her kind.\n\nMiss Sharp's father was an artist, and in that quality had given lessons of drawing at Miss Pinkerton's school. He was a clever man; a pleasant companion; a careless student; with a great propensity for running into debt, and a partiality for the tavern. When he was drunk, he used to beat his wife and daughter; and the next morning, with a headache, he would rail at the world for its neglect of his genius, and abuse, with a good deal of cleverness, and sometimes with perfect reason, the fools, his brother painters. As it was with the utmost difficulty that he could keep himself, and as he owed money for a mile round Soho, where he lived, he thought to better his circumstances by marrying a young woman of the French nation, who was by profession an opera-girl. The humble calling of her female parent Miss Sharp never alluded to, but used to state subsequently that the Entrechats were a noble family of Gascony, and took great pride in her descent from them. And curious it is that as she advanced in life this young lady's ancestors increased in rank and splendour.\n\nRebecca's mother had had some education somewhere, and her daughter spoke French with purity and a Parisian accent. It was in those days rather a rare accomplishment, and led to her engagement with the orthodox Miss Pinkerton. For her mother being dead, her father, finding himself not likely to recover, after his third attack of delirium tremens, wrote a manly and pathetic letter to Miss Pinkerton, recommending the orphan child to her protection, and so descended to the grave, after two bailiffs had quarrelled over his corpse. Rebecca was seventeen when she came to Chiswick, and was bound over as an articled pupil; her duties being to talk French, as we have seen; and her privileges to live cost free, and, with a few guineas a year, to gather scraps of knowledge from the professors who attended the school.\n\nShe was small and slight in person; pale, sandy-haired, and with eyes habitually cast down: when they looked up they were very large, odd, and attractive; so attractive that the Reverend Mr. Crisp, fresh from Oxford, and curate to the Vicar of Chiswick, the Reverend Mr. Flowerdew, fell in love with Miss Sharp; being shot dead by a glance of her eyes which was fired all the way across Chiswick Church from the school-pew to the reading-desk. This infatuated young man used sometimes to take tea with Miss Pinkerton, to whom he had been presented by his mamma, and actually proposed something like marriage in an intercepted note, which the one-eyed apple-woman was charged to deliver. Mrs. Crisp was summoned from Buxton, and abruptly carried off her darling boy; but the idea, even, of such an eagle in the Chiswick dovecot caused a great flutter in the breast of Miss Pinkerton, who would have sent away Miss Sharp but that she was bound to her under a forfeit, and who never could thoroughly believe the young lady's protestations that she had never exchanged a single word with Mr. Crisp, except under her own eyes on the two occasions when she had met him at tea.\n\nBy the side of many tall and bouncing young ladies in the establishment, Rebecca Sharp looked like a child. But she had the dismal precocity of poverty. Many a dun had she talked to, and turned away from her father's door; many a tradesman had she coaxed and wheedled into good-humour, and into the granting of one meal more. She sate commonly with her father, who was very proud of her wit, and heard the talk of many of his wild companions--often but ill-suited for a girl to hear. But she never had been a girl, she said; she had been a woman since she was eight years old. Oh, why did Miss Pinkerton let such a dangerous bird into her cage?\n\nThe fact is, the old lady believed Rebecca to be the meekest creature in the world, so admirably, on the occasions when her father brought her to Chiswick, used Rebecca to perform the part of the ingenue; and only a year before the arrangement by which Rebecca had been admitted into her house, and when Rebecca was sixteen years old, Miss Pinkerton majestically, and with a little speech, made her a present of a doll--which was, by the way, the confiscated property of Miss Swindle, discovered surreptitiously nursing it in school-hours. How the father and daughter laughed as they trudged home together after the evening party (it was on the occasion of the speeches, when all the professors were invited) and how Miss Pinkerton would have raged had she seen the caricature of herself which the little mimic, Rebecca, managed to make out of her doll. Becky used to go through dialogues with it; it formed the delight of Newman Street, Gerrard Street, and the Artists' quarter: and the young painters, when they came to take their gin-and-water with their lazy, dissolute, clever, jovial senior, used regularly to ask Rebecca if Miss Pinkerton was at home: she was as well known to them, poor soul! as Mr. Lawrence or President West. Once Rebecca had the honour to pass a few days at Chiswick; after which she brought back Jemima, and erected another doll as Miss Jemmy: for though that honest creature had made and given her jelly and cake enough for three children, and a seven-shilling piece at parting, the girl's sense of ridicule was far stronger than her gratitude, and she sacrificed Miss Jemmy quite as pitilessly as her sister.\n\nThe catastrophe came, and she was brought to the Mall as to her home. The rigid formality of the place suffocated her: the prayers and the meals, the lessons and the walks, which were arranged with a conventual regularity, oppressed her almost beyond endurance; and she looked back to the freedom and the beggary of the old studio in Soho with so much regret, that everybody, herself included, fancied she was consumed with grief for her father. She had a little room in the garret, where the maids heard her walking and sobbing at night; but it was with rage, and not with grief. She had not been much of a dissembler, until now her loneliness taught her to feign. She had never mingled in the society of women: her father, reprobate as he was, was a man of talent; his conversation was a thousand times more agreeable to her than the talk of such of her own sex as she now encountered. The pompous vanity of the old schoolmistress, the foolish good-humour of her sister, the silly chat and scandal of the elder girls, and the frigid correctness of the governesses equally annoyed her; and she had no soft maternal heart, this unlucky girl, otherwise the prattle and talk of the younger children, with whose care she was chiefly intrusted, might have soothed and interested her; but she lived among them two years, and not one was sorry that she went away. The gentle tender-hearted Amelia Sedley was the only person to whom she could attach herself in the least; and who could help attaching herself to Amelia?\n\nThe happiness--the superior advantages of the young women round about her, gave Rebecca inexpressible pangs of envy. \"What airs that girl gives herself, because she is an Earl's grand-daughter,\" she said of one. \"How they cringe and bow to that Creole, because of her hundred thousand pounds! I am a thousand times cleverer and more charming than that creature, for all her wealth. I am as well bred as the Earl's grand-daughter, for all her fine pedigree; and yet every one passes me by here. And yet, when I was at my father's, did not the men give up their gayest balls and parties in order to pass the evening with me?\" She determined at any rate to get free from the prison in which she found herself, and now began to act for herself, and for the first time to make connected plans for the future.\n\nShe took advantage, therefore, of the means of study the place offered her; and as she was already a musician and a good linguist, she speedily went through the little course of study which was considered necessary for ladies in those days. Her music she practised incessantly, and one day, when the girls were out, and she had remained at home, she was overheard to play a piece so well that Minerva thought, wisely, she could spare herself the expense of a master for the juniors, and intimated to Miss Sharp that she was to instruct them in music for the future.\n\nThe girl refused; and for the first time, and to the astonishment of the majestic mistress of the school. \"I am here to speak French with the children,\" Rebecca said abruptly, \"not to teach them music, and save money for you. Give me money, and I will teach them.\"\n\nMinerva was obliged to yield, and, of course, disliked her from that day. \"For five-and-thirty years,\" she said, and with great justice, \"I never have seen the individual who has dared in my own house to question my authority. I have nourished a viper in my bosom.\"\n\n\"A viper--a fiddlestick,\" said Miss Sharp to the old lady, almost fainting with astonishment. \"You took me because I was useful. There is no question of gratitude between us. I hate this place, and want to leave it. I will do nothing here but what I am obliged to do.\"\n\nIt was in vain that the old lady asked her if she was aware she was speaking to Miss Pinkerton? Rebecca laughed in her face, with a horrid sarcastic demoniacal laughter, that almost sent the schoolmistress into fits. \"Give me a sum of money,\" said the girl, \"and get rid of me--or, if you like better, get me a good place as governess in a nobleman's family--you can do so if you please.\" And in their further disputes she always returned to this point, \"Get me a situation--we hate each other, and I am ready to go.\"\n\nWorthy Miss Pinkerton, although she had a Roman nose and a turban, and was as tall as a grenadier, and had been up to this time an irresistible princess, had no will or strength like that of her little apprentice, and in vain did battle against her, and tried to overawe her. Attempting once to scold her in public, Rebecca hit upon the before-mentioned plan of answering her in French, which quite routed the old woman. In order to maintain authority in her school, it became necessary to remove this rebel, this monster, this serpent, this firebrand; and hearing about this time that Sir Pitt Crawley's family was in want of a governess, she actually recommended Miss Sharp for the situation, firebrand and serpent as she was. \"I cannot, certainly,\" she said, \"find fault with Miss Sharp's conduct, except to myself; and must allow that her talents and accomplishments are of a high order. As far as the head goes, at least, she does credit to the educational system pursued at my establishment.\"\n\nAnd so the schoolmistress reconciled the recommendation to her conscience, and the indentures were cancelled, and the apprentice was free. The battle here described in a few lines, of course, lasted for some months. And as Miss Sedley, being now in her seventeenth year, was about to leave school, and had a friendship for Miss Sharp (\"'tis the only point in Amelia's behaviour,\" said Minerva, \"which has not been satisfactory to her mistress\"), Miss Sharp was invited by her friend to pass a week with her at home, before she entered upon her duties as governess in a private family.\n\nThus the world began for these two young ladies. For Amelia it was quite a new, fresh, brilliant world, with all the bloom upon it. It was not quite a new one for Rebecca--(indeed, if the truth must be told with respect to the Crisp affair, the tart-woman hinted to somebody, who took an affidavit of the fact to somebody else, that there was a great deal more than was made public regarding Mr. Crisp and Miss Sharp, and that his letter was in answer to another letter). But who can tell you the real truth of the matter? At all events, if Rebecca was not beginning the world, she was beginning it over again.\n\nBy the time the young ladies reached Kensington turnpike, Amelia had not forgotten her companions, but had dried her tears, and had blushed very much and been delighted at a young officer of the Life Guards, who spied her as he was riding by, and said, \"A dem fine gal, egad!\" and before the carriage arrived in Russell Square, a great deal of conversation had taken place about the Drawing-room, and whether or not young ladies wore powder as well as hoops when presented, and whether she was to have that honour: to the Lord Mayor's ball she knew she was to go. And when at length home was reached, Miss Amelia Sedley skipped out on Sambo's arm, as happy and as handsome a girl as any in the whole big city of London. Both he and coachman agreed on this point, and so did her father and mother, and so did every one of the servants in the house, as they stood bobbing, and curtseying, and smiling, in the hall to welcome their young mistress.\n\nYou may be sure that she showed Rebecca over every room of the house, and everything in every one of her drawers; and her books, and her piano, and her dresses, and all her necklaces, brooches, laces, and gimcracks. She insisted upon Rebecca accepting the white cornelian and the turquoise rings, and a sweet sprigged muslin, which was too small for her now, though it would fit her friend to a nicety; and she determined in her heart to ask her mother's permission to present her white Cashmere shawl to her friend. Could she not spare it? and had not her brother Joseph just brought her two from India?\n\nWhen Rebecca saw the two magnificent Cashmere shawls which Joseph Sedley had brought home to his sister, she said, with perfect truth, \"that it must be delightful to have a brother,\" and easily got the pity of the tender-hearted Amelia for being alone in the world, an orphan without friends or kindred.\n\n\"Not alone,\" said Amelia; \"you know, Rebecca, I shall always be your friend, and love you as a sister--indeed I will.\"\n\n\"Ah, but to have parents, as you have--kind, rich, affectionate parents, who give you everything you ask for; and their love, which is more precious than all! My poor papa could give me nothing, and I had but two frocks in all the world! And then, to have a brother, a dear brother! Oh, how you must love him!\"\n\nAmelia laughed.\n\n\"What! don't you love him? you, who say you love everybody?\"\n\n\"Yes, of course, I do--only--\"\n\n\"Only what?\"\n\n\"Only Joseph doesn't seem to care much whether I love him or not. He gave me two fingers to shake when he arrived after ten years' absence! He is very kind and good, but he scarcely ever speaks to me; I think he loves his pipe a great deal better than his\"--but here Amelia checked herself, for why should she speak ill of her brother? \"He was very kind to me as a child,\" she added; \"I was but five years old when he went away.\"\n\n\"Isn't he very rich?\" said Rebecca. \"They say all Indian nabobs are enormously rich.\"\n\n\"I believe he has a very large income.\"\n\n\"And is your sister-in-law a nice pretty woman?\"\n\n\"La! Joseph is not married,\" said Amelia, laughing again.\n\nPerhaps she had mentioned the fact already to Rebecca, but that young lady did not appear to have remembered it; indeed, vowed and protested that she expected to see a number of Amelia's nephews and nieces. She was quite disappointed that Mr. Sedley was not married; she was sure Amelia had said he was, and she doted so on little children.\n\n\"I think you must have had enough of them at Chiswick,\" said Amelia, rather wondering at the sudden tenderness on her friend's part; and indeed in later days Miss Sharp would never have committed herself so far as to advance opinions, the untruth of which would have been so easily detected. But we must remember that she is but nineteen as yet, unused to the art of deceiving, poor innocent creature! and making her own experience in her own person. The meaning of the above series of queries, as translated in the heart of this ingenious young woman, was simply this: \"If Mr. Joseph Sedley is rich and unmarried, why should I not marry him? I have only a fortnight, to be sure, but there is no harm in trying.\" And she determined within herself to make this laudable attempt. She redoubled her caresses to Amelia; she kissed the white cornelian necklace as she put it on; and vowed she would never, never part with it. When the dinner-bell rang she went downstairs with her arm round her friend's waist, as is the habit of young ladies. She was so agitated at the drawing-room door, that she could hardly find courage to enter. \"Feel my heart, how it beats, dear!\" said she to her friend.\n\n\"No, it doesn't,\" said Amelia. \"Come in, don't be frightened. Papa won't do you any harm.\"\n\nRebecca Is in Presence of the Enemy\n\nA VERY stout, puffy man, in buckskins and Hessian boots, with several immense neckcloths that rose almost to his nose, with a red striped waistcoat and an apple green coat with steel buttons almost as large as crown pieces (it was the morning costume of a dandy or blood of those days) was reading the paper by the fire when the two girls entered, and bounced off his arm-chair, and blushed excessively, and hid his entire face almost in his neckcloths at this apparition.\n\n\"It's only your sister, Joseph,\" said Amelia, laughing and shaking the two fingers which he held out. \"I've come home FOR GOOD, you know; and this is my friend, Miss Sharp, whom you have heard me mention.\"\n\n\"No, never, upon my word,\" said the head under the neckcloth, shaking very much--\"that is, yes--what abominably cold weather, Miss\"--and herewith he fell to poking the fire with all his might, although it was in the middle of June.\n\n\"He's very handsome,\" whispered Rebecca to Amelia, rather loud.\n\n\"Do you think so?\" said the latter. \"I'll tell him.\"\n\n\"Darling! not for worlds,\" said Miss Sharp, starting back as timid as a fawn. She had previously made a respectful virgin-like curtsey to the gentleman, and her modest eyes gazed so perseveringly on the carpet that it was a wonder how she should have found an opportunity to see him.\n\n\"Thank you for the beautiful shawls, brother,\" said Amelia to the fire poker. \"Are they not beautiful, Rebecca?\"\n\n\"O heavenly!\" said Miss Sharp, and her eyes went from the carpet straight to the chandelier.\n\nJoseph still continued a huge clattering at the poker and tongs, puffing and blowing the while, and turning as red as his yellow face would allow him. \"I can't make you such handsome presents, Joseph,\" continued his sister, \"but while I was at school, I have embroidered for you a very beautiful pair of braces.\"\n\n\"Good Gad! Amelia,\" cried the brother, in serious alarm, \"what do you mean?\" and plunging with all his might at the bell-rope, that article of furniture came away in his hand, and increased the honest fellow's confusion. \"For heaven's sake see if my buggy's at the door. I CAN'T wait. I must go. D---- that groom of mine. I must go.\"\n\nAt this minute the father of the family walked in, rattling his seals like a true British merchant. \"What's the matter, Emmy?\" says he.\n\n\"Joseph wants me to see if his--his buggy is at the door. What is a buggy, Papa?\"\n\n\"It is a one-horse palanquin,\" said the old gentleman, who was a wag in his way.\n\nJoseph at this burst out into a wild fit of laughter; in which, encountering the eye of Miss Sharp, he stopped all of a sudden, as if he had been shot.\n\n\"This young lady is your friend? Miss Sharp, I am very happy to see you. Have you and Emmy been quarrelling already with Joseph, that he wants to be off?\"\n\n\"I promised Bonamy of our service, sir,\" said Joseph, \"to dine with him.\"\n\n\"O fie! didn't you tell your mother you would dine here?\"\n\n\"But in this dress it's impossible.\"\n\n\"Look at him, isn't he handsome enough to dine anywhere, Miss Sharp?\"\n\nOn which, of course, Miss Sharp looked at her friend, and they both set off in a fit of laughter, highly agreeable to the old gentleman.\n\n\"Did you ever see a pair of buckskins like those at Miss Pinkerton's?\" continued he, following up his advantage.\n\n\"Gracious heavens! Father,\" cried Joseph.\n\n\"There now, I have hurt his feelings. Mrs. Sedley, my dear, I have hurt your son's feelings. I have alluded to his buckskins. Ask Miss Sharp if I haven't? Come, Joseph, be friends with Miss Sharp, and let us all go to dinner.\"\n\n\"There's a pillau, Joseph, just as you like it, and Papa has brought home the best turbot in Billingsgate.\"\n\n\"Come, come, sir, walk downstairs with Miss Sharp, and I will follow with these two young women,\" said the father, and he took an arm of wife and daughter and walked merrily off.\n\nIf Miss Rebecca Sharp had determined in her heart upon making the conquest of this big beau, I don't think, ladies, we have any right to blame her; for though the task of husband-hunting is generally, and with becoming modesty, entrusted by young persons to their mammas, recollect that Miss Sharp had no kind parent to arrange these delicate matters for her, and that if she did not get a husband for herself, there was no one else in the wide world who would take the trouble off her hands. What causes young people to \"come out,\" but the noble ambition of matrimony? What sends them trooping to watering-places? What keeps them dancing till five o'clock in the morning through a whole mortal season? What causes them to labour at pianoforte sonatas, and to learn four songs from a fashionable master at a guinea a lesson, and to play the harp if they have handsome arms and neat elbows, and to wear Lincoln Green toxophilite hats and feathers, but that they may bring down some \"desirable\" young man with those killing bows and arrows of theirs? What causes respectable parents to take up their carpets, set their houses topsy-turvy, and spend a fifth of their year's income in ball suppers and iced champagne? Is it sheer love of their species, and an unadulterated wish to see young people happy and dancing? Psha! they want to marry their daughters; and, as honest Mrs. Sedley has, in the depths of her kind heart, already arranged a score of little schemes for the settlement of her Amelia, so also had our beloved but unprotected Rebecca determined to do her very best to secure the husband, who was even more necessary for her than for her friend. She had a vivid imagination; she had, besides, read the Arabian Nights and Guthrie's Geography; and it is a fact that while she was dressing for dinner, and after she had asked Amelia whether her brother was very rich, she had built for herself a most magnificent castle in the air, of which she was mistress, with a husband somewhere in the background (she had not seen him as yet, and his figure would not therefore be very distinct); she had arrayed herself in an infinity of shawls, turbans, and diamond necklaces, and had mounted upon an elephant to the sound of the march in Bluebeard, in order to pay a visit of ceremony to the Grand Mogul. Charming Alnaschar visions! it is the happy privilege of youth to construct you, and many a fanciful young creature besides Rebecca Sharp has indulged in these delightful day-dreams ere now!\n\nJoseph Sedley was twelve years older than his sister Amelia. He was in the East India Company's Civil Service, and his name appeared, at the period of which we write, in the Bengal division of the East India Register, as collector of Boggley Wollah, an honourable and lucrative post, as everybody knows: in order to know to what higher posts Joseph rose in the service, the reader is referred to the same periodical.\n\nBoggley Wollah is situated in a fine, lonely, marshy, jungly district, famous for snipe-shooting, and where not unfrequently you may flush a tiger. Ramgunge, where there is a magistrate, is only forty miles off, and there is a cavalry station about thirty miles farther; so Joseph wrote home to his parents, when he took possession of his collectorship. He had lived for about eight years of his life, quite alone, at this charming place, scarcely seeing a Christian face except twice a year, when the detachment arrived to carry off the revenues which he had collected, to Calcutta.\n\nLuckily, at this time he caught a liver complaint, for the cure of which he returned to Europe, and which was the source of great comfort and amusement to him in his native country. He did not live with his family while in London, but had lodgings of his own, like a gay young bachelor. Before he went to India he was too young to partake of the delightful pleasures of a man about town, and plunged into them on his return with considerable assiduity. He drove his horses in the Park; he dined at the fashionable taverns (for the Oriental Club was not as yet invented); he frequented the theatres, as the mode was in those days, or made his appearance at the opera, laboriously attired in tights and a cocked hat.\n\nOn returning to India, and ever after, he used to talk of the pleasure of this period of his existence with great enthusiasm, and give you to understand that he and Brummel were the leading bucks of the day. But he was as lonely here as in his jungle at Boggley Wollah. He scarcely knew a single soul in the metropolis: and were it not for his doctor, and the society of his blue-pill, and his liver complaint, he must have died of loneliness. He was lazy, peevish, and a bon-vivant; the appearance of a lady frightened him beyond measure; hence it was but seldom that he joined the paternal circle in Russell Square, where there was plenty of gaiety, and where the jokes of his good-natured old father frightened his amour-propre. His bulk caused Joseph much anxious thought and alarm; now and then he would make a desperate attempt to get rid of his superabundant fat; but his indolence and love of good living speedily got the better of these endeavours at reform, and he found himself again at his three meals a day. He never was well dressed; but he took the hugest pains to adorn his big person, and passed many hours daily in that occupation. His valet made a fortune out of his wardrobe: his toilet-table was covered with as many pomatums and essences as ever were employed by an old beauty: he had tried, in order to give himself a waist, every girth, stay, and waistband then invented. Like most fat men, he would have his clothes made too tight, and took care they should be of the most brilliant colours and youthful cut. When dressed at length, in the afternoon, he would issue forth to take a drive with nobody in the Park; and then would come back in order to dress again and go and dine with nobody at the Piazza Coffee-House. He was as vain as a girl; and perhaps his extreme shyness was one of the results of his extreme vanity. If Miss Rebecca can get the better of him, and at her first entrance into life, she is a young person of no ordinary cleverness.\n\nThe first move showed considerable skill. When she called Sedley a very handsome man, she knew that Amelia would tell her mother, who would probably tell Joseph, or who, at any rate, would be pleased by the compliment paid to her son. All mothers are. If you had told Sycorax that her son Caliban was as handsome as Apollo, she would have been pleased, witch as she was. Perhaps, too, Joseph Sedley would overhear the compliment--Rebecca spoke loud enough--and he did hear, and (thinking in his heart that he was a very fine man) the praise thrilled through every fibre of his big body, and made it tingle with pleasure. Then, however, came a recoil. \"Is the girl making fun of me?\" he thought, and straightway he bounced towards the bell, and was for retreating, as we have seen, when his father's jokes and his mother's entreaties caused him to pause and stay where he was. He conducted the young lady down to dinner in a dubious and agitated frame of mind. \"Does she really think I am handsome?\" thought he, \"or is she only making game of me?\" We have talked of Joseph Sedley being as vain as a girl. Heaven help us! the girls have only to turn the tables, and say of one of their own sex, \"She is as vain as a man,\" and they will have perfect reason. The bearded creatures are quite as eager for praise, quite as finikin over their toilettes, quite as proud of their personal advantages, quite as conscious of their powers of fascination, as any coquette in the world.\n\nDownstairs, then, they went, Joseph very red and blushing, Rebecca very modest, and holding her green eyes downwards. She was dressed in white, with bare shoulders as white as snow--the picture of youth, unprotected innocence, and humble virgin simplicity. \"I must be very quiet,\" thought Rebecca, \"and very much interested about India.\"\n\nNow we have heard how Mrs. Sedley had prepared a fine curry for her son, just as he liked it, and in the course of dinner a portion of this dish was offered to Rebecca. \"What is it?\" said she, turning an appealing look to Mr. Joseph.\n\n\"Capital,\" said he. His mouth was full of it: his face quite red with the delightful exercise of gobbling. \"Mother, it's as good as my own curries in India.\"\n\n\"Oh, I must try some, if it is an Indian dish,\" said Miss Rebecca. \"I am sure everything must be good that comes from there.\"\n\n\"Give Miss Sharp some curry, my dear,\" said Mr. Sedley, laughing.\n\nRebecca had never tasted the dish before.\n\n\"Do you find it as good as everything else from India?\" said Mr. Sedley.\n\n\"Oh, excellent!\" said Rebecca, who was suffering tortures with the cayenne pepper.\n\n\"Try a chili with it, Miss Sharp,\" said Joseph, really interested.\n\n\"A chili,\" said Rebecca, gasping. \"Oh yes!\" She thought a chili was something cool, as its name imported, and was served with some. \"How fresh and green they look,\" she said, and put one into her mouth. It was hotter than the curry; flesh and blood could bear it no longer. She laid down her fork. \"Water, for Heaven's sake, water!\" she cried. Mr. Sedley burst out laughing (he was a coarse man, from the Stock Exchange, where they love all sorts of practical jokes). \"They are real Indian, I assure you,\" said he. \"Sambo, give Miss Sharp some water.\"\n\nThe paternal laugh was echoed by Joseph, who thought the joke capital. The ladies only smiled a little. They thought poor Rebecca suffered too much. She would have liked to choke old Sedley, but she swallowed her mortification as well as she had the abominable curry before it, and as soon as she could speak, said, with a comical, good-humoured air, \"I ought to have remembered the pepper which the Princess of Persia puts in the cream-tarts in the Arabian Nights. Do you put cayenne into your cream-tarts in India, sir?\"\n\nOld Sedley began to laugh, and thought Rebecca was a good-humoured girl. Joseph simply said, \"Cream-tarts, Miss? Our cream is very bad in Bengal. We generally use goats' milk; and, 'gad, do you know, I've got to prefer it!\"\n\n\"You won't like EVERYTHING from India now, Miss Sharp,\" said the old gentleman; but when the ladies had retired after dinner, the wily old fellow said to his son, \"Have a care, Joe; that girl is setting her cap at you.\"\n\n\"Pooh! nonsense!\" said Joe, highly flattered. \"I recollect, sir, there was a girl at Dumdum, a daughter of Cutler of the Artillery, and afterwards married to Lance, the surgeon, who made a dead set at me in the year '4--at me and Mulligatawney, whom I mentioned to you before dinner--a devilish good fellow Mulligatawney--he's a magistrate at Budgebudge, and sure to be in council in five years. Well, sir, the Artillery gave a ball, and Quintin, of the King's 14th, said to me, 'Sedley,' said he, 'I bet you thirteen to ten that Sophy Cutler hooks either you or Mulligatawney before the rains.' 'Done,' says I; and egad, sir--this claret's very good. Adamson's or Carbonell's?\"\n\nA slight snore was the only reply: the honest stockbroker was asleep, and so the rest of Joseph's story was lost for that day. But he was always exceedingly communicative in a man's party, and has told this delightful tale many scores of times to his apothecary, Dr. Gollop, when he came to inquire about the liver and the blue-pill.\n\nBeing an invalid, Joseph Sedley contented himself with a bottle of claret besides his Madeira at dinner, and he managed a couple of plates full of strawberries and cream, and twenty-four little rout cakes that were lying neglected in a plate near him, and certainly (for novelists have the privilege of knowing everything) he thought a great deal about the girl upstairs. \"A nice, gay, merry young creature,\" thought he to himself. \"How she looked at me when I picked up her handkerchief at dinner! She dropped it twice. Who's that singing in the drawing-room? 'Gad! shall I go up and see?\"\n\nBut his modesty came rushing upon him with uncontrollable force. His father was asleep: his hat was in the hall: there was a hackney-coach standing hard by in Southampton Row. \"I'll go and see the Forty Thieves,\" said he, \"and Miss Decamp's dance\"; and he slipped away gently on the pointed toes of his boots, and disappeared, without waking his worthy parent.\n\n\"There goes Joseph,\" said Amelia, who was looking from the open windows of the drawing-room, while Rebecca was singing at the piano.\n\n\"Miss Sharp has frightened him away,\" said Mrs. Sedley. \"Poor Joe, why WILL he be so shy?\"\n\nThe Green Silk Purse\nPoor Joe's panic lasted for two or three days; during which he did not visit the house, nor during that period did Miss Rebecca ever mention his name. She was all respectful gratitude to Mrs. Sedley; delighted beyond measure at the Bazaars; and in a whirl of wonder at the theatre, whither the good-natured lady took her. One day, Amelia had a headache, and could not go upon some party of pleasure to which the two young people were invited: nothing could induce her friend to go without her. \"What! you who have shown the poor orphan what happiness and love are for the first time in her life--quit YOU? Never!\" and the green eyes looked up to Heaven and filled with tears; and Mrs. Sedley could not but own that her daughter's friend had a charming kind heart of her own.\n\nAs for Mr. Sedley's jokes, Rebecca laughed at them with a cordiality and perseverance which not a little pleased and softened that good-natured gentleman. Nor was it with the chiefs of the family alone that Miss Sharp found favour. She interested Mrs. Blenkinsop by evincing the deepest sympathy in the raspberry-jam preserving, which operation was then going on in the Housekeeper's room; she persisted in calling Sambo \"Sir,\" and \"Mr. Sambo,\" to the delight of that attendant; and she apologised to the lady's maid for giving her trouble in venturing to ring the bell, with such sweetness and humility, that the Servants' Hall was almost as charmed with her as the Drawing Room.\n\nOnce, in looking over some drawings which Amelia had sent from school, Rebecca suddenly came upon one which caused her to burst into tears and leave the room. It was on the day when Joe Sedley made his second appearance.\n\nAmelia hastened after her friend to know the cause of this display of feeling, and the good-natured girl came back without her companion, rather affected too. \"You know, her father was our drawing-master, Mamma, at Chiswick, and used to do all the best parts of our drawings.\"\n\n\"My love! I'm sure I always heard Miss Pinkerton say that he did not touch them--he only mounted them.\" \"It was called mounting, Mamma. Rebecca remembers the drawing, and her father working at it, and the thought of it came upon her rather suddenly--and so, you know, she--\"\n\n\"The poor child is all heart,\" said Mrs. Sedley.\n\n\"I wish she could stay with us another week,\" said Amelia.\n\n\"She's devilish like Miss Cutler that I used to meet at Dumdum, only fairer. She's married now to Lance, the Artillery Surgeon. Do you know, Ma'am, that once Quintin, of the 14th, bet me--\"\n\n\"O Joseph, we know that story,\" said Amelia, laughing. \"Never mind about telling that; but persuade Mamma to write to Sir Something Crawley for leave of absence for poor dear Rebecca: here she comes, her eyes red with weeping.\"\n\n\"I'm better, now,\" said the girl, with the sweetest smile possible, taking good-natured Mrs. Sedley's extended hand and kissing it respectfully. \"How kind you all are to me! All,\" she added, with a laugh, \"except you, Mr. Joseph.\"\n\n\"Me!\" said Joseph, meditating an instant departure. \"Gracious Heavens! Good Gad! Miss Sharp!'\n\n\"Yes; how could you be so cruel as to make me eat that horrid pepper-dish at dinner, the first day I ever saw you? You are not so good to me as dear Amelia.\"\n\n\"He doesn't know you so well,\" cried Amelia.\n\n\"I defy anybody not to be good to you, my dear,\" said her mother.\n\n\"The curry was capital; indeed it was,\" said Joe, quite gravely. \"Perhaps there was NOT enough citron juice in it--no, there was NOT.\"\n\n\"And the chilis?\"\n\n\"By Jove, how they made you cry out!\" said Joe, caught by the ridicule of the circumstance, and exploding in a fit of laughter which ended quite suddenly, as usual.\n\n\"I shall take care how I let YOU choose for me another time,\" said Rebecca, as they went down again to dinner. \"I didn't think men were fond of putting poor harmless girls to pain.\"\n\n\"By Gad, Miss Rebecca, I wouldn't hurt you for the world.\"\n\n\"No,\" said she, \"I KNOW you wouldn't\"; and then she gave him ever so gentle a pressure with her little hand, and drew it back quite frightened, and looked first for one instant in his face, and then down at the carpet-rods; and I am not prepared to say that Joe's heart did not thump at this little involuntary, timid, gentle motion of regard on the part of the simple girl.\n\nIt was an advance, and as such, perhaps, some ladies of indisputable correctness and gentility will condemn the action as immodest; but, you see, poor dear Rebecca had all this work to do for herself. If a person is too poor to keep a servant, though ever so elegant, he must sweep his own rooms: if a dear girl has no dear Mamma to settle matters with the young man, she must do it for herself. And oh, what a mercy it is that these women do not exercise their powers oftener! We can't resist them, if they do. Let them show ever so little inclination, and men go down on their knees at once: old or ugly, it is all the same. And this I set down as a positive truth. A woman with fair opportunities, and without an absolute hump, may marry WHOM SHE LIKES. Only let us be thankful that the darlings are like the beasts of the field, and don't know their own power. They would overcome us entirely if they did.\n\n\"Egad!\" thought Joseph, entering the dining-room, \"I exactly begin to feel as I did at Dumdum with Miss Cutler.\" Many sweet little appeals, half tender, half jocular, did Miss Sharp make to him about the dishes at dinner; for by this time she was on a footing of considerable familiarity with the family, and as for the girls, they loved each other like sisters. Young unmarried girls always do, if they are in a house together for ten days.\n\nAs if bent upon advancing Rebecca's plans in every way--what must Amelia do, but remind her brother of a promise made last Easter holidays--\"When I was a girl at school,\" said she, laughing--a promise that he, Joseph, would take her to Vauxhall. \"Now,\" she said, \"that Rebecca is with us, will be the very time.\"\n\n\"O, delightful!\" said Rebecca, going to clap her hands; but she recollected herself, and paused, like a modest creature, as she was.\n\n\"To-night is not the night,\" said Joe.\n\n\"Well, to-morrow.\"\n\n\"To-morrow your Papa and I dine out,\" said Mrs. Sedley.\n\n\"You don't suppose that I'm going, Mrs. Sed?\" said her husband, \"and that a woman of your years and size is to catch cold, in such an abominable damp place?\"\n\n\"The children must have someone with them,\" cried Mrs. Sedley.\n\n\"Let Joe go,\" said-his father, laughing. \"He's big enough.\" At which speech even Mr. Sambo at the sideboard burst out laughing, and poor fat Joe felt inclined to become a parricide almost.\n\n\"Undo his stays!\" continued the pitiless old gentleman. \"Fling some water in his face, Miss Sharp, or carry him upstairs: the dear creature's fainting. Poor victim! carry him up; he's as light as a feather!\"\n\n\"If I stand this, sir, I'm d------!\" roared Joseph.\n\n\"Order Mr. Jos's elephant, Sambo!\" cried the father. \"Send to Exeter 'Change, Sambo\"; but seeing Jos ready almost to cry with vexation, the old joker stopped his laughter, and said, holding out his hand to his son, \"It's all fair on the Stock Exchange, Jos--and, Sambo, never mind the elephant, but give me and Mr. Jos a glass of Champagne. Boney himself hasn't got such in his cellar, my boy!\"\n\nA goblet of Champagne restored Joseph's equanimity, and before the bottle was emptied, of which as an invalid he took two-thirds, he had agreed to take the young ladies to Vauxhall.\n\n\"The girls must have a gentleman apiece,\" said the old gentleman. \"Jos will be sure to leave Emmy in the crowd, he will be so taken up with Miss Sharp here. Send to 96, and ask George Osborne if he'll come.\"\n\nAt this, I don't know in the least for what reason, Mrs. Sedley looked at her husband and laughed. Mr. Sedley's eyes twinkled in a manner indescribably roguish, and he looked at Amelia; and Amelia, hanging down her head, blushed as only young ladies of seventeen know how to blush, and as Miss Rebecca Sharp never blushed in her life--at least not since she was eight years old, and when she was caught stealing jam out of a cupboard by her godmother. \"Amelia had better write a note,\" said her father; \"and let George Osborne see what a beautiful handwriting we have brought back from Miss Pinkerton's. Do you remember when you wrote to him to come on Twelfth-night, Emmy, and spelt twelfth without the f?\"\n\n\"That was years ago,\" said Amelia.\n\n\"It seems like yesterday, don't it, John?\" said Mrs. Sedley to her husband; and that night in a conversation which took place in a front room in the second floor, in a sort of tent, hung round with chintz of a rich and fantastic India pattern, and double with calico of a tender rose-colour; in the interior of which species of marquee was a featherbed, on which were two pillows, on which were two round red faces, one in a laced nightcap, and one in a simple cotton one, ending in a tassel--in a CURTAIN LECTURE, I say, Mrs. Sedley took her husband to task for his cruel conduct to poor Joe.\n\n\"It was quite wicked of you, Mr. Sedley,\" said she, \"to torment the poor boy so.\"\n\n\"My dear,\" said the cotton-tassel in defence of his conduct, \"Jos is a great deal vainer than you ever were in your life, and that's saying a good deal. Though, some thirty years ago, in the year seventeen hundred and eighty--what was it?--perhaps you had a right to be vain--I don't say no. But I've no patience with Jos and his dandified modesty. It is out-Josephing Joseph, my dear, and all the while the boy is only thinking of himself, and what a fine fellow he is. I doubt, Ma'am, we shall have some trouble with him yet. Here is Emmy's little friend making love to him as hard as she can; that's quite clear; and if she does not catch him some other will. That man is destined to be a prey to woman, as I am to go on 'Change every day. It's a mercy he did not bring us over a black daughter-in-law, my dear. But, mark my words, the first woman who fishes for him, hooks him.\"\n\n\"She shall go off to-morrow, the little artful creature,\" said Mrs. Sedley, with great energy.\n\n\"Why not she as well as another, Mrs. Sedley? The girl's a white face at any rate. I don't care who marries him. Let Joe please himself.\"\n\nAnd presently the voices of the two speakers were hushed, or were replaced by the gentle but unromantic music of the nose; and save when the church bells tolled the hour and the watchman called it, all was silent at the house of John Sedley, Esquire, of Russell Square, and the Stock Exchange.\n\nWhen morning came, the good-natured Mrs. Sedley no longer thought of executing her threats with regard to Miss Sharp; for though nothing is more keen, nor more common, nor more justifiable, than maternal jealousy, yet she could not bring herself to suppose that the little, humble, grateful, gentle governess would dare to look up to such a magnificent personage as the Collector of Boggley Wollah. The petition, too, for an extension of the young lady's leave of absence had already been despatched, and it would be difficult to find a pretext for abruptly dismissing her.\n\nAnd as if all things conspired in favour of the gentle Rebecca, the very elements (although she was not inclined at first to acknowledge their action in her behalf) interposed to aid her. For on the evening appointed for the Vauxhall party, George Osborne having come to dinner, and the elders of the house having departed, according to invitation, to dine with Alderman Balls at Highbury Barn, there came on such a thunder-storm as only happens on Vauxhall nights, and as obliged the young people, perforce, to remain at home. Mr. Osborne did not seem in the least disappointed at this occurrence. He and Joseph Sedley drank a fitting quantity of port-wine, tete-a-tete, in the dining-room, during the drinking of which Sedley told a number of his best Indian stories; for he was extremely talkative in man's society; and afterwards Miss Amelia Sedley did the honours of the drawing-room; and these four young persons passed such a comfortable evening together, that they declared they were rather glad of the thunder-storm than otherwise, which had caused them to put off their visit to Vauxhall.\n\nOsborne was Sedley's godson, and had been one of the family any time these three-and-twenty years. At six weeks old, he had received from John Sedley a present of a silver cup; at six months old, a coral with gold whistle and bells; from his youth upwards he was \"tipped\" regularly by the old gentleman at Christmas: and on going back to school, he remembered perfectly well being thrashed by Joseph Sedley, when the latter was a big, swaggering hobbadyhoy, and George an impudent urchin of ten years old. In a word, George was as familiar with the family as such daily acts of kindness and intercourse could make him.\n\n\"Do you remember, Sedley, what a fury you were in, when I cut off the tassels of your Hessian boots, and how Miss--hem!--how Amelia rescued me from a beating, by falling down on her knees and crying out to her brother Jos, not to beat little George?\"\n\nJos remembered this remarkable circumstance perfectly well, but vowed that he had totally forgotten it.\n\n\"Well, do you remember coming down in a gig to Dr. Swishtail's to see me, before you went to India, and giving me half a guinea and a pat on the head? I always had an idea that you were at least seven feet high, and was quite astonished at your return from India to find you no taller than myself.\"\n\n\"How good of Mr. Sedley to go to your school and give you the money!\" exclaimed Rebecca, in accents of extreme delight.\n\n\"Yes, and after I had cut the tassels of his boots too. Boys never forget those tips at school, nor the givers.\"\n\n\"I delight in Hessian boots,\" said Rebecca. Jos Sedley, who admired his own legs prodigiously, and always wore this ornamental chaussure, was extremely pleased at this remark, though he drew his legs under his chair as it was made.\n\n\"Miss Sharp!\" said George Osborne, \"you who are so clever an artist, you must make a grand historical picture of the scene of the boots. Sedley shall be represented in buckskins, and holding one of the injured boots in one hand; by the other he shall have hold of my shirt-frill. Amelia shall be kneeling near him, with her little hands up; and the picture shall have a grand allegorical title, as the frontispieces have in the Medulla and the spelling-book.\"\n\n\"I shan't have time to do it here,\" said Rebecca. \"I'll do it when--when I'm gone.\" And she dropped her voice, and looked so sad and piteous, that everybody felt how cruel her lot was, and how sorry they would be to part with her.\n\n\"O that you could stay longer, dear Rebecca,\" said Amelia.\n\n\"Why?\" answered the other, still more sadly. \"That I may be only the more unhap--unwilling to lose you?\" And she turned away her head. Amelia began to give way to that natural infirmity of tears which, we have said, was one of the defects of this silly little thing. George Osborne looked at the two young women with a touched curiosity; and Joseph Sedley heaved something very like a sigh out of his big chest, as he cast his eyes down towards his favourite Hessian boots.\n\n\"Let us have some music, Miss Sedley--Amelia,\" said George, who felt at that moment an extraordinary, almost irresistible impulse to seize the above-mentioned young woman in his arms, and to kiss her in the face of the company; and she looked at him for a moment, and if I should say that they fell in love with each other at that single instant of time, I should perhaps be telling an untruth, for the fact is that these two young people had been bred up by their parents for this very purpose, and their banns had, as it were, been read in their respective families any time these ten years. They went off to the piano, which was situated, as pianos usually are, in the back drawing-room; and as it was rather dark, Miss Amelia, in the most unaffected way in the world, put her hand into Mr. Osborne's, who, of course, could see the way among the chairs and ottomans a great deal better than she could. But this arrangement left Mr. Joseph Sedley tete-a-tete with Rebecca, at the drawing-room table, where the latter was occupied in knitting a green silk purse.\n\n\"There is no need to ask family secrets,\" said Miss Sharp. \"Those two have told theirs.\"\n\n\"As soon as he gets his company,\" said Joseph, \"I believe the affair is settled. George Osborne is a capital fellow.\"\n\n\"And your sister the dearest creature in the world,\" said Rebecca. \"Happy the man who wins her!\" With this, Miss Sharp gave a great sigh.\n\nWhen two unmarried persons get together, and talk upon such delicate subjects as the present, a great deal of confidence and intimacy is presently established between them. There is no need of giving a special report of the conversation which now took place between Mr. Sedley and the young lady; for the conversation, as may be judged from the foregoing specimen, was not especially witty or eloquent; it seldom is in private societies, or anywhere except in very high-flown and ingenious novels. As there was music in the next room, the talk was carried on, of course, in a low and becoming tone, though, for the matter of that, the couple in the next apartment would not have been disturbed had the talking been ever so loud, so occupied were they with their own pursuits.\n\nAlmost for the first time in his life, Mr. Sedley found himself talking, without the least timidity or hesitation, to a person of the other sex. Miss Rebecca asked him a great number of questions about India, which gave him an opportunity of narrating many interesting anecdotes about that country and himself. He described the balls at Government House, and the manner in which they kept themselves cool in the hot weather, with punkahs, tatties, and other contrivances; and he was very witty regarding the number of Scotchmen whom Lord Minto, the Governor-General, patronised; and then he described a tiger-hunt; and the manner in which the mahout of his elephant had been pulled off his seat by one of the infuriated animals. How delighted Miss Rebecca was at the Government balls, and how she laughed at the stories of the Scotch aides-de-camp, and called Mr. Sedley a sad wicked satirical creature; and how frightened she was at the story of the elephant! \"For your mother's sake, dear Mr. Sedley,\" she said, \"for the sake of all your friends, promise NEVER to go on one of those horrid expeditions.\"\n\n\"Pooh, pooh, Miss Sharp,\" said he, pulling up his shirt-collars; \"the danger makes the sport only the pleasanter.\" He had never been but once at a tiger-hunt, when the accident in question occurred, and when he was half killed--not by the tiger, but by the fright. And as he talked on, he grew quite bold, and actually had the audacity to ask Miss Rebecca for whom she was knitting the green silk purse? He was quite surprised and delighted at his own graceful familiar manner.\n\n\"For any one who wants a purse,\" replied Miss Rebecca, looking at him in the most gentle winning way. Sedley was going to make one of the most eloquent speeches possible, and had begun--\"O Miss Sharp, how--\" when some song which was performed in the other room came to an end, and caused him to hear his own voice so distinctly that he stopped, blushed, and blew his nose in great agitation.\n\n\"Did you ever hear anything like your brother's eloquence?\" whispered Mr. Osborne to Amelia. \"Why, your friend has worked miracles.\"\n\n\"The more the better,\" said Miss Amelia; who, like almost all women who are worth a pin, was a match-maker in her heart, and would have been delighted that Joseph should carry back a wife to India. She had, too, in the course of this few days' constant intercourse, warmed into a most tender friendship for Rebecca, and discovered a million of virtues and amiable qualities in her which she had not perceived when they were at Chiswick together. For the affection of young ladies is of as rapid growth as Jack's bean-stalk, and reaches up to the sky in a night. It is no blame to them that after marriage this Sehnsucht nach der Liebe subsides. It is what sentimentalists, who deal in very big words, call a yearning after the Ideal, and simply means that women are commonly not satisfied until they have husbands and children on whom they may centre affections, which are spent elsewhere, as it were, in small change.\n\nHaving expended her little store of songs, or having stayed long enough in the back drawing-room, it now appeared proper to Miss Amelia to ask her friend to sing. \"You would not have listened to me,\" she said to Mr. Osborne (though she knew she was telling a fib), \"had you heard Rebecca first.\"\n\n\"I give Miss Sharp warning, though,\" said Osborne, \"that, right or wrong, I consider Miss Amelia Sedley the first singer in the world.\"\n\n\"You shall hear,\" said Amelia; and Joseph Sedley was actually polite enough to carry the candles to the piano. Osborne hinted that he should like quite as well to sit in the dark; but Miss Sedley, laughing, declined to bear him company any farther, and the two accordingly followed Mr. Joseph. Rebecca sang far better than her friend (though of course Osborne was free to keep his opinion), and exerted herself to the utmost, and, indeed, to the wonder of Amelia, who had never known her perform so well. She sang a French song, which Joseph did not understand in the least, and which George confessed he did not understand, and then a number of those simple ballads which were the fashion forty years ago, and in which British tars, our King, poor Susan, blue-eyed Mary, and the like, were the principal themes. They are not, it is said, very brilliant, in a musical point of view, but contain numberless good-natured, simple appeals to the affections, which people understood better than the milk-and-water lagrime, sospiri, and felicita of the eternal Donizettian music with which we are favoured now-a-days.\n\nConversation of a sentimental sort, befitting the subject, was carried on between the songs, to which Sambo, after he had brought the tea, the delighted cook, and even Mrs. Blenkinsop, the housekeeper, condescended to listen on the landing-place.\n\nAmong these ditties was one, the last of the concert, and to the following effect:\n\nAh! bleak and barren was the moor, Ah! loud and piercing was the storm, The cottage roof was shelter'd sure, The cottage hearth was bright and warm--An orphan boy the lattice pass'd, And, as he mark'd its cheerful glow, Felt doubly keen the midnight blast, And doubly cold the fallen snow.\n\nThey mark'd him as he onward prest, With fainting heart and weary limb; Kind voices bade him turn and rest, And gentle faces welcomed him. The dawn is up--the guest is gone, The cottage hearth is blazing still; Heaven pity all poor wanderers lone! Hark to the wind upon the hill!\n\nIt was the sentiment of the before-mentioned words, \"When I'm gone,\" over again. As she came to the last words, Miss Sharp's \"deep-toned voice faltered.\" Everybody felt the allusion to her departure, and to her hapless orphan state. Joseph Sedley, who was fond of music, and soft-hearted, was in a state of ravishment during the performance of the song, and profoundly touched at its conclusion. If he had had the courage; if George and Miss Sedley had remained, according to the former's proposal, in the farther room, Joseph Sedley's bachelorhood would have been at an end, and this work would never have been written. But at the close of the ditty, Rebecca quitted the piano, and giving her hand to Amelia, walked away into the front drawing-room twilight; and, at this moment, Mr. Sambo made his appearance with a tray, containing sandwiches, jellies, and some glittering glasses and decanters, on which Joseph Sedley's attention was immediately fixed. When the parents of the house of Sedley returned from their dinner-party, they found the young people so busy in talking, that they had not heard the arrival of the carriage, and Mr. Joseph was in the act of saying, \"My dear Miss Sharp, one little teaspoonful of jelly to recruit you after your immense--your--your delightful exertions.\"\n\n\"Bravo, Jos!\" said Mr. Sedley; on hearing the bantering of which well-known voice, Jos instantly relapsed into an alarmed silence, and quickly took his departure. He did not lie awake all night thinking whether or not he was in love with Miss Sharp; the passion of love never interfered with the appetite or the slumber of Mr. Joseph Sedley; but he thought to himself how delightful it would be to hear such songs as those after Cutcherry--what a distinguee girl she was--how she could speak French better than the Governor-General's lady herself--and what a sensation she would make at the Calcutta balls. \"It's evident the poor devil's in love with me,\" thought he. \"She is just as rich as most of the girls who come out to India. I might go farther, and fare worse, egad!\" And in these meditations he fell asleep.\n\nHow Miss Sharp lay awake, thinking, will he come or not to-morrow? need not be told here. To-morrow came, and, as sure as fate, Mr. Joseph Sedley made his appearance before luncheon. He had never been known before to confer such an honour on Russell Square. George Osborne was somehow there already (sadly \"putting out\" Amelia, who was writing to her twelve dearest friends at Chiswick Mall), and Rebecca was employed upon her yesterday's work. As Joe's buggy drove up, and while, after his usual thundering knock and pompous bustle at the door, the ex-Collector of Boggley Wollah laboured up stairs to the drawing-room, knowing glances were telegraphed between Osborne and Miss Sedley, and the pair, smiling archly, looked at Rebecca, who actually blushed as she bent her fair ringlets over her knitting. How her heart beat as Joseph appeared--Joseph, puffing from the staircase in shining creaking boots--Joseph, in a new waistcoat, red with heat and nervousness, and blushing behind his wadded neckcloth. It was a nervous moment for all; and as for Amelia, I think she was more frightened than even the people most concerned.\n\nSambo, who flung open the door and announced Mr. Joseph, followed grinning, in the Collector's rear, and bearing two handsome nosegays of flowers, which the monster had actually had the gallantry to purchase in Covent Garden Market that morning--they were not as big as the haystacks which ladies carry about with them now-a-days, in cones of filigree paper; but the young women were delighted with the gift, as Joseph presented one to each, with an exceedingly solemn bow.\n\n\"Bravo, Jos!\" cried Osborne.\n\n\"Thank you, dear Joseph,\" said Amelia, quite ready to kiss her brother, if he were so minded. (And I think for a kiss from such a dear creature as Amelia, I would purchase all Mr. Lee's conservatories out of hand.)\n\n\"O heavenly, heavenly flowers!\" exclaimed Miss Sharp, and smelt them delicately, and held them to her bosom, and cast up her eyes to the ceiling, in an ecstasy of admiration. Perhaps she just looked first into the bouquet, to see whether there was a billet-doux hidden among the flowers; but there was no letter.\n\n\"Do they talk the language of flowers at Boggley Wollah, Sedley?\" asked Osborne, laughing.\n\n\"Pooh, nonsense!\" replied the sentimental youth. \"Bought 'em at Nathan's; very glad you like 'em; and eh, Amelia, my dear, I bought a pine-apple at the same time, which I gave to Sambo. Let's have it for tiffin; very cool and nice this hot weather.\" Rebecca said she had never tasted a pine, and longed beyond everything to taste one.\n\nSo the conversation went on. I don't know on what pretext Osborne left the room, or why, presently, Amelia went away, perhaps to superintend the slicing of the pine-apple; but Jos was left alone with Rebecca, who had resumed her work, and the green silk and the shining needles were quivering rapidly under her white slender fingers.\n\n\"What a beautiful, BYOO-OOTIFUL song that was you sang last night, dear Miss Sharp,\" said the Collector. \"It made me cry almost; 'pon my honour it did.\"\n\n\"Because you have a kind heart, Mr. Joseph; all the Sedleys have, I think.\"\n\n\"It kept me awake last night, and I was trying to hum it this morning, in bed; I was, upon my honour. Gollop, my doctor, came in at eleven (for I'm a sad invalid, you know, and see Gollop every day), and, 'gad! there I was, singing away like--a robin.\"\n\n\"O you droll creature! Do let me hear you sing it.\"\n\n\"Me? No, you, Miss Sharp; my dear Miss Sharp, do sing it.\" \"Not now, Mr. Sedley,\" said Rebecca, with a sigh. \"My spirits are not equal to it; besides, I must finish the purse. Will you help me, Mr. Sedley?\" And before he had time to ask how, Mr. Joseph Sedley, of the East India Company's service, was actually seated tete-a-tete with a young lady, looking at her with a most killing expression; his arms stretched out before her in an imploring attitude, and his hands bound in a web of green silk, which she was unwinding.\n\nIn this romantic position Osborne and Amelia found the interesting pair, when they entered to announce that tiffin was ready. The skein of silk was just wound round the card; but Mr. Jos had never spoken.\n\n\"I am sure he will to-night, dear,\" Amelia said, as she pressed Rebecca's hand; and Sedley, too, had communed with his soul, and said to himself, \"'Gad, I'll pop the question at Vauxhall.\"\n\nDobbin of Ours\nCuff's fight with Dobbin, and the unexpected issue of that contest, will long be remembered by every man who was educated at Dr. Swishtail's famous school. The latter Youth (who used to be called Heigh-ho Dobbin, Gee-ho Dobbin, and by many other names indicative of puerile contempt) was the quietest, the clumsiest, and, as it seemed, the dullest of all Dr. Swishtail's young gentlemen. His parent was a grocer in the city: and it was bruited abroad that he was admitted into Dr. Swishtail's academy upon what are called \"mutual principles\"--that is to say, the expenses of his board and schooling were defrayed by his father in goods, not money; and he stood there--most at the bottom of the school--in his scraggy corduroys and jacket, through the seams of which his great big bones were bursting--as the representative of so many pounds of tea, candles, sugar, mottled-soap, plums (of which a very mild proportion was supplied for the puddings of the establishment), and other commodities. A dreadful day it was for young Dobbin when one of the youngsters of the school, having run into the town upon a poaching excursion for hardbake and polonies, espied the cart of Dobbin & Rudge, Grocers and Oilmen, Thames Street, London, at the Doctor's door, discharging a cargo of the wares in which the firm dealt.\n\nYoung Dobbin had no peace after that. The jokes were frightful, and merciless against him. \"Hullo, Dobbin,\" one wag would say, \"here's good news in the paper. Sugars is ris', my boy.\" Another would set a sum--\"If a pound of mutton-candles cost sevenpence-halfpenny, how much must Dobbin cost?\" and a roar would follow from all the circle of young knaves, usher and all, who rightly considered that the selling of goods by retail is a shameful and infamous practice, meriting the contempt and scorn of all real gentlemen.\n\n\"Your father's only a merchant, Osborne,\" Dobbin said in private to the little boy who had brought down the storm upon him. At which the latter replied haughtily, \"My father's a gentleman, and keeps his carriage\"; and Mr. William Dobbin retreated to a remote outhouse in the playground, where he passed a half-holiday in the bitterest sadness and woe. Who amongst us is there that does not recollect similar hours of bitter, bitter childish grief? Who feels injustice; who shrinks before a slight; who has a sense of wrong so acute, and so glowing a gratitude for kindness, as a generous boy? and how many of those gentle souls do you degrade, estrange, torture, for the sake of a little loose arithmetic, and miserable dog-latin?\n\nNow, William Dobbin, from an incapacity to acquire the rudiments of the above language, as they are propounded in that wonderful book the Eton Latin Grammar, was compelled to remain among the very last of Doctor Swishtail's scholars, and was \"taken down\" continually by little fellows with pink faces and pinafores when he marched up with the lower form, a giant amongst them, with his downcast, stupefied look, his dog's-eared primer, and his tight corduroys. High and low, all made fun of him. They sewed up those corduroys, tight as they were. They cut his bed-strings. They upset buckets and benches, so that he might break his shins over them, which he never failed to do. They sent him parcels, which, when opened, were found to contain the paternal soap and candles. There was no little fellow but had his jeer and joke at Dobbin; and he bore everything quite patiently, and was entirely dumb and miserable.\n\nCuff, on the contrary, was the great chief and dandy of the Swishtail Seminary. He smuggled wine in. He fought the town-boys. Ponies used to come for him to ride home on Saturdays. He had his top-boots in his room, in which he used to hunt in the holidays. He had a gold repeater: and took snuff like the Doctor. He had been to the Opera, and knew the merits of the principal actors, preferring Mr. Kean to Mr. Kemble. He could knock you off forty Latin verses in an hour. He could make French poetry. What else didn't he know, or couldn't he do? They said even the Doctor himself was afraid of him.\n\nCuff, the unquestioned king of the school, ruled over his subjects, and bullied them, with splendid superiority. This one blacked his shoes: that toasted his bread, others would fag out, and give him balls at cricket during whole summer afternoons. \"Figs\" was the fellow whom he despised most, and with whom, though always abusing him, and sneering at him, he scarcely ever condescended to hold personal communication.\n\nOne day in private, the two young gentlemen had had a difference. Figs, alone in the schoolroom, was blundering over a home letter; when Cuff, entering, bade him go upon some message, of which tarts were probably the subject.\n\n\"I can't,\" says Dobbin; \"I want to finish my letter.\"\n\n\"You CAN'T?\" says Mr. Cuff, laying hold of that document (in which many words were scratched out, many were mis-spelt, on which had been spent I don't know how much thought, and labour, and tears; for the poor fellow was writing to his mother, who was fond of him, although she was a grocer's wife, and lived in a back parlour in Thames Street). \"You CAN'T?\" says Mr. Cuff: \"I should like to know why, pray? Can't you write to old Mother Figs to-morrow?\"\n\n\"Don't call names,\" Dobbin said, getting off the bench very nervous.\n\n\"Well, sir, will you go?\" crowed the cock of the school.\n\n\"Put down the letter,\" Dobbin replied; \"no gentleman readth letterth.\"\n\n\"Well, NOW will you go?\" says the other.\n\n\"No, I won't. Don't strike, or I'll THMASH you,\" roars out Dobbin, springing to a leaden inkstand, and looking so wicked, that Mr. Cuff paused, turned down his coat sleeves again, put his hands into his pockets, and walked away with a sneer. But he never meddled personally with the grocer's boy after that; though we must do him the justice to say he always spoke of Mr. Dobbin with contempt behind his back.\n\nSome time after this interview, it happened that Mr. Cuff, on a sunshiny afternoon, was in the neighbourhood of poor William Dobbin, who was lying under a tree in the playground, spelling over a favourite copy of the Arabian Nights which he had apart from the rest of the school, who were pursuing their various sports--quite lonely, and almost happy. If people would but leave children to themselves; if teachers would cease to bully them; if parents would not insist upon directing their thoughts, and dominating their feelings--those feelings and thoughts which are a mystery to all (for how much do you and I know of each other, of our children, of our fathers, of our neighbour, and how far more beautiful and sacred are the thoughts of the poor lad or girl whom you govern likely to be, than those of the dull and world-corrupted person who rules him?)--if, I say, parents and masters would leave their children alone a little more, small harm would accrue, although a less quantity of as in praesenti might be acquired.\n\nWell, William Dobbin had for once forgotten the world, and was away with Sindbad the Sailor in the Valley of Diamonds, or with Prince Ahmed and the Fairy Peribanou in that delightful cavern where the Prince found her, and whither we should all like to make a tour; when shrill cries, as of a little fellow weeping, woke up his pleasant reverie; and looking up, he saw Cuff before him, belabouring a little boy.\n\nIt was the lad who had peached upon him about the grocer's cart; but he bore little malice, not at least towards the young and small. \"How dare you, sir, break the bottle?\" says Cuff to the little urchin, swinging a yellow cricket-stump over him.\n\nThe boy had been instructed to get over the playground wall (at a selected spot where the broken glass had been removed from the top, and niches made convenient in the brick); to run a quarter of a mile; to purchase a pint of rum-shrub on credit; to brave all the Doctor's outlying spies, and to clamber back into the playground again; during the performance of which feat, his foot had slipt, and the bottle was broken, and the shrub had been spilt, and his pantaloons had been damaged, and he appeared before his employer a perfectly guilty and trembling, though harmless, wretch.\n\n\"How dare you, sir, break it?\" says Cuff; \"you blundering little thief. You drank the shrub, and now you pretend to have broken the bottle. Hold out your hand, sir.\"\n\nDown came the stump with a great heavy thump on the child's hand. A moan followed. Dobbin looked up. The Fairy Peribanou had fled into the inmost cavern with Prince Ahmed: the Roc had whisked away Sindbad the Sailor out of the Valley of Diamonds out of sight, far into the clouds: and there was everyday life before honest William; and a big boy beating a little one without cause.\n\n\"Hold out your other hand, sir,\" roars Cuff to his little schoolfellow, whose face was distorted with pain. Dobbin quivered, and gathered himself up in his narrow old clothes.\n\n\"Take that, you little devil!\" cried Mr. Cuff, and down came the wicket again on the child's hand.--Don't be horrified, ladies, every boy at a public school has done it. Your children will so do and be done by, in all probability. Down came the wicket again; and Dobbin started up.\n\nI can't tell what his motive was. Torture in a public school is as much licensed as the knout in Russia. It would be ungentlemanlike (in a manner) to resist it. Perhaps Dobbin's foolish soul revolted against that exercise of tyranny; or perhaps he had a hankering feeling of revenge in his mind, and longed to measure himself against that splendid bully and tyrant, who had all the glory, pride, pomp, circumstance, banners flying, drums beating, guards saluting, in the place. Whatever may have been his incentive, however, up he sprang, and screamed out, \"Hold off, Cuff; don't bully that child any more; or I'll--\"\n\n\"Or you'll what?\" Cuff asked in amazement at this interruption. \"Hold out your hand, you little beast.\"\n\n\"I'll give you the worst thrashing you ever had in your life,\" Dobbin said, in reply to the first part of Cuff's sentence; and little Osborne, gasping and in tears, looked up with wonder and incredulity at seeing this amazing champion put up suddenly to defend him: while Cuff's astonishment was scarcely less. Fancy our late monarch George III when he heard of the revolt of the North American colonies: fancy brazen Goliath when little David stepped forward and claimed a meeting; and you have the feelings of Mr. Reginald Cuff when this rencontre was proposed to him.\n\n\"After school,\" says he, of course; after a pause and a look, as much as to say, \"Make your will, and communicate your last wishes to your friends between this time and that.\"\n\n\"As you please,\" Dobbin said. \"You must be my bottle holder, Osborne.\"\n\n\"Well, if you like,\" little Osborne replied; for you see his papa kept a carriage, and he was rather ashamed of his champion.\n\nYes, when the hour of battle came, he was almost ashamed to say, \"Go it, Figs\"; and not a single other boy in the place uttered that cry for the first two or three rounds of this famous combat; at the commencement of which the scientific Cuff, with a contemptuous smile on his face, and as light and as gay as if he was at a ball, planted his blows upon his adversary, and floored that unlucky champion three times running. At each fall there was a cheer; and everybody was anxious to have the honour of offering the conqueror a knee.\n\n\"What a licking I shall get when it's over,\" young Osborne thought, picking up his man. \"You'd best give in,\" he said to Dobbin; \"it's only a thrashing, Figs, and you know I'm used to it.\" But Figs, all whose limbs were in a quiver, and whose nostrils were breathing rage, put his little bottle-holder aside, and went in for a fourth time.\n\nAs he did not in the least know how to parry the blows that were aimed at himself, and Cuff had begun the attack on the three preceding occasions, without ever allowing his enemy to strike, Figs now determined that he would commence the engagement by a charge on his own part; and accordingly, being a left-handed man, brought that arm into action, and hit out a couple of times with all his might--once at Mr. Cuff's left eye, and once on his beautiful Roman nose.\n\nCuff went down this time, to the astonishment of the assembly. \"Well hit, by Jove,\" says little Osborne, with the air of a connoisseur, clapping his man on the back. \"Give it him with the left, Figs my boy.\"\n\nFigs's left made terrific play during all the rest of the combat. Cuff went down every time. At the sixth round, there were almost as many fellows shouting out, \"Go it, Figs,\" as there were youths exclaiming, \"Go it, Cuff.\" At the twelfth round the latter champion was all abroad, as the saying is, and had lost all presence of mind and power of attack or defence. Figs, on the contrary, was as calm as a quaker. His face being quite pale, his eyes shining open, and a great cut on his underlip bleeding profusely, gave this young fellow a fierce and ghastly air, which perhaps struck terror into many spectators. Nevertheless, his intrepid adversary prepared to close for the thirteenth time.\n\nIf I had the pen of a Napier, or a Bell's Life, I should like to describe this combat properly. It was the last charge of the Guard--(that is, it would have been, only Waterloo had not yet taken place)--it was Ney's column breasting the hill of La Haye Sainte, bristling with ten thousand bayonets, and crowned with twenty eagles--it was the shout of the beef-eating British, as leaping down the hill they rushed to hug the enemy in the savage arms of battle--in other words, Cuff coming up full of pluck, but quite reeling and groggy, the Fig-merchant put in his left as usual on his adversary's nose, and sent him down for the last time.\n\n\"I think that will do for him,\" Figs said, as his opponent dropped as neatly on the green as I have seen Jack Spot's ball plump into the pocket at billiards; and the fact is, when time was called, Mr. Reginald Cuff was not able, or did not choose, to stand up again.\n\nAnd now all the boys set up such a shout for Figs as would have made you think he had been their darling champion through the whole battle; and as absolutely brought Dr. Swishtail out of his study, curious to know the cause of the uproar. He threatened to flog Figs violently, of course; but Cuff, who had come to himself by this time, and was washing his wounds, stood up and said, \"It's my fault, sir--not Figs'--not Dobbin's. I was bullying a little boy; and he served me right.\" By which magnanimous speech he not only saved his conqueror a whipping, but got back all his ascendancy over the boys which his defeat had nearly cost him.\n\nYoung Osborne wrote home to his parents an account of the transaction.\n\nSugarcane House, Richmond, March, 18--\n\nDEAR MAMA,--I hope you are quite well. I should be much obliged to you to send me a cake and five shillings. There has been a fight here between Cuff & Dobbin. Cuff, you know, was the Cock of the School. They fought thirteen rounds, and Dobbin Licked. So Cuff is now Only Second Cock. The fight was about me. Cuff was licking me for breaking a bottle of milk, and Figs wouldn't stand it. We call him Figs because his father is a Grocer--Figs & Rudge, Thames St., City--I think as he fought for me you ought to buy your Tea & Sugar at his father's. Cuff goes home every Saturday, but can't this, because he has 2 Black Eyes. He has a white Pony to come and fetch him, and a groom in livery on a bay mare. I wish my Papa would let me have a Pony, and I am\n\nYour dutiful Son, GEORGE SEDLEY OSBORNE\n\nP.S.--Give my love to little Emmy. I am cutting her out a Coach in cardboard. Please not a seed-cake, but a plum-cake.\n\nIn consequence of Dobbin's victory, his character rose prodigiously in the estimation of all his schoolfellows, and the name of Figs, which had been a byword of reproach, became as respectable and popular a nickname as any other in use in the school. \"After all, it's not his fault that his father's a grocer,\" George Osborne said, who, though a little chap, had a very high popularity among the Swishtail youth; and his opinion was received with great applause. It was voted low to sneer at Dobbin about this accident of birth. \"Old Figs\" grew to be a name of kindness and endearment; and the sneak of an usher jeered at him no longer.\n\nAnd Dobbin's spirit rose with his altered circumstances. He made wonderful advances in scholastic learning. The superb Cuff himself, at whose condescension Dobbin could only blush and wonder, helped him on with his Latin verses; \"coached\" him in play-hours: carried him triumphantly out of the little-boy class into the middle-sized form; and even there got a fair place for him. It was discovered, that although dull at classical learning, at mathematics he was uncommonly quick. To the contentment of all he passed third in algebra, and got a French prize-book at the public Midsummer examination. You should have seen his mother's face when Telemaque (that delicious romance) was presented to him by the Doctor in the face of the whole school and the parents and company, with an inscription to Gulielmo Dobbin. All the boys clapped hands in token of applause and sympathy. His blushes, his stumbles, his awkwardness, and the number of feet which he crushed as he went back to his place, who shall describe or calculate? Old Dobbin, his father, who now respected him for the first time, gave him two guineas publicly; most of which he spent in a general tuck-out for the school: and he came back in a tail-coat after the holidays.\n\nDobbin was much too modest a young fellow to suppose that this happy change in all his circumstances arose from his own generous and manly disposition: he chose, from some perverseness, to attribute his good fortune to the sole agency and benevolence of little George Osborne, to whom henceforth he vowed such a love and affection as is only felt by children--such an affection, as we read in the charming fairy-book, uncouth Orson had for splendid young Valentine his conqueror. He flung himself down at little Osborne's feet, and loved him. Even before they were acquainted, he had admired Osborne in secret. Now he was his valet, his dog, his man Friday. He believed Osborne to be the possessor of every perfection, to be the handsomest, the bravest, the most active, the cleverest, the most generous of created boys. He shared his money with him: bought him uncountable presents of knives, pencil-cases, gold seals, toffee, Little Warblers, and romantic books, with large coloured pictures of knights and robbers, in many of which latter you might read inscriptions to George Sedley Osborne, Esquire, from his attached friend William Dobbin--the which tokens of homage George received very graciously, as became his superior merit.\n\nSo that Lieutenant Osborne, when coming to Russell Square on the day of the Vauxhall party, said to the ladies, \"Mrs. Sedley, Ma'am, I hope you have room; I've asked Dobbin of ours to come and dine here, and go with us to Vauxhall. He's almost as modest as Jos.\"\n\n\"Modesty! pooh,\" said the stout gentleman, casting a vainqueur look at Miss Sharp.\n\n\"He is--but you are incomparably more graceful, Sedley,\" Osborne added, laughing. \"I met him at the Bedford, when I went to look for you; and I told him that Miss Amelia was come home, and that we were all bent on going out for a night's pleasuring; and that Mrs. Sedley had forgiven his breaking the punch-bowl at the child's party. Don't you remember the catastrophe, Ma'am, seven years ago?\"\n\n\"Over Mrs. Flamingo's crimson silk gown,\" said good-natured Mrs. Sedley. \"What a gawky it was! And his sisters are not much more graceful. Lady Dobbin was at Highbury last night with three of them. Such figures! my dears.\"\n\n\"The Alderman's very rich, isn't he?\" Osborne said archly. \"Don't you think one of the daughters would be a good spec for me, Ma'am?\"\n\n\"You foolish creature! Who would take you, I should like to know, with your yellow face?\"\n\n\"Mine a yellow face? Stop till you see Dobbin. Why, he had the yellow fever three times; twice at Nassau, and once at St. Kitts.\"\n\n\"Well, well; yours is quite yellow enough for us. Isn't it, Emmy?\" Mrs. Sedley said: at which speech Miss Amelia only made a smile and a blush; and looking at Mr. George Osborne's pale interesting countenance, and those beautiful black, curling, shining whiskers, which the young gentleman himself regarded with no ordinary complacency, she thought in her little heart that in His Majesty's army, or in the wide world, there never was such a face or such a hero. \"I don't care about Captain Dobbin's complexion,\" she said, \"or about his awkwardness. I shall always like him, I know,\" her little reason being, that he was the friend and champion of George.\n\n\"There's not a finer fellow in the service,\" Osborne said, \"nor a better officer, though he is not an Adonis, certainly.\" And he looked towards the glass himself with much naivete; and in so doing, caught Miss Sharp's eye fixed keenly upon him, at which he blushed a little, and Rebecca thought in her heart, \"Ah, mon beau Monsieur! I think I have YOUR gauge\"--the little artful minx!\n\nThat evening, when Amelia came tripping into the drawing-room in a white muslin frock, prepared for conquest at Vauxhall, singing like a lark, and as fresh as a rose--a very tall ungainly gentleman, with large hands and feet, and large ears, set off by a closely cropped head of black hair, and in the hideous military frogged coat and cocked hat of those times, advanced to meet her, and made her one of the clumsiest bows that was ever performed by a mortal.\n\nThis was no other than Captain William Dobbin, of His Majesty's Regiment of Foot, returned from yellow fever, in the West Indies, to which the fortune of the service had ordered his regiment, whilst so many of his gallant comrades were reaping glory in the Peninsula.\n\nHe had arrived with a knock so very timid and quiet that it was inaudible to the ladies upstairs: otherwise, you may be sure Miss Amelia would never have been so bold as to come singing into the room. As it was, the sweet fresh little voice went right into the Captain's heart, and nestled there. When she held out her hand for him to shake, before he enveloped it in his own, he paused, and thought--\"Well, is it possible--are you the little maid I remember in the pink frock, such a short time ago--the night I upset the punch-bowl, just after I was gazetted? Are you the little girl that George Osborne said should marry him? What a blooming young creature you seem, and what a prize the rogue has got!\" All this he thought, before he took Amelia's hand into his own, and as he let his cocked hat fall.\n\nHis history since he left school, until the very moment when we have the pleasure of meeting him again, although not fully narrated, has yet, I think, been indicated sufficiently for an ingenious reader by the conversation in the last page. Dobbin, the despised grocer, was Alderman Dobbin--Alderman Dobbin was Colonel of the City Light Horse, then burning with military ardour to resist the French Invasion. Colonel Dobbin's corps, in which old Mr. Osborne himself was but an indifferent corporal, had been reviewed by the Sovereign and the Duke of York; and the colonel and alderman had been knighted. His son had entered the army: and young Osborne followed presently in the same regiment. They had served in the West Indies and in Canada. Their regiment had just come home, and the attachment of Dobbin to George Osborne was as warm and generous now as it had been when the two were schoolboys.\n\nSo these worthy people sat down to dinner presently. They talked about war and glory, and Boney and Lord Wellington, and the last Gazette. In those famous days every gazette had a victory in it, and the two gallant young men longed to see their own names in the glorious list, and cursed their unlucky fate to belong to a regiment which had been away from the chances of honour. Miss Sharp kindled with this exciting talk, but Miss Sedley trembled and grew quite faint as she heard it. Mr. Jos told several of his tiger-hunting stories, finished the one about Miss Cutler and Lance the surgeon; helped Rebecca to everything on the table, and himself gobbled and drank a great deal.\n\nHe sprang to open the door for the ladies, when they retired, with the most killing grace--and coming back to the table, filled himself bumper after bumper of claret, which he swallowed with nervous rapidity.\n\n\"He's priming himself,\" Osborne whispered to Dobbin, and at length the hour and the carriage arrived for Vauxhall.\n\nVauxhall\nI know that the tune I am piping is a very mild one (although there are some terrific chapters coming presently), and must beg the good-natured reader to remember that we are only discoursing at present about a stockbroker's family in Russell Square, who are taking walks, or luncheon, or dinner, or talking and making love as people do in common life, and without a single passionate and wonderful incident to mark the progress of their loves. The argument stands thus--Osborne, in love with Amelia, has asked an old friend to dinner and to Vauxhall--Jos Sedley is in love with Rebecca. Will he marry her? That is the great subject now in hand.\n\nWe might have treated this subject in the genteel, or in the romantic, or in the facetious manner. Suppose we had laid the scene in Grosvenor Square, with the very same adventures--would not some people have listened? Suppose we had shown how Lord Joseph Sedley fell in love, and the Marquis of Osborne became attached to Lady Amelia, with the full consent of the Duke, her noble father: or instead of the supremely genteel, suppose we had resorted to the entirely low, and described what was going on in Mr. Sedley's kitchen--how black Sambo was in love with the cook (as indeed he was), and how he fought a battle with the coachman in her behalf; how the knife-boy was caught stealing a cold shoulder of mutton, and Miss Sedley's new femme de chambre refused to go to bed without a wax candle; such incidents might be made to provoke much delightful laughter, and be supposed to represent scenes of \"life.\" Or if, on the contrary, we had taken a fancy for the terrible, and made the lover of the new femme de chambre a professional burglar, who bursts into the house with his band, slaughters black Sambo at the feet of his master, and carries off Amelia in her night-dress, not to be let loose again till the third volume, we should easily have constructed a tale of thrilling interest, through the fiery chapters of which the reader should hurry, panting. But my readers must hope for no such romance, only a homely story, and must be content with a chapter about Vauxhall, which is so short that it scarce deserves to be called a chapter at all. And yet it is a chapter, and a very important one too. Are not there little chapters in everybody's life, that seem to be nothing, and yet affect all the rest of the history?\n\nLet us then step into the coach with the Russell Square party, and be off to the Gardens. There is barely room between Jos and Miss Sharp, who are on the front seat. Mr. Osborne sitting bodkin opposite, between Captain Dobbin and Amelia.\n\nEvery soul in the coach agreed that on that night Jos would propose to make Rebecca Sharp Mrs. Sedley. The parents at home had acquiesced in the arrangement, though, between ourselves, old Mr. Sedley had a feeling very much akin to contempt for his son. He said he was vain, selfish, lazy, and effeminate. He could not endure his airs as a man of fashion, and laughed heartily at his pompous braggadocio stories. \"I shall leave the fellow half my property,\" he said; \"and he will have, besides, plenty of his own; but as I am perfectly sure that if you, and I, and his sister were to die to-morrow, he would say 'Good Gad!' and eat his dinner just as well as usual, I am not going to make myself anxious about him. Let him marry whom he likes. It's no affair of mine.\"\n\nAmelia, on the other hand, as became a young woman of her prudence and temperament, was quite enthusiastic for the match. Once or twice Jos had been on the point of saying something very important to her, to which she was most willing to lend an ear, but the fat fellow could not be brought to unbosom himself of his great secret, and very much to his sister's disappointment he only rid himself of a large sigh and turned away.\n\nThis mystery served to keep Amelia's gentle bosom in a perpetual flutter of excitement. If she did not speak with Rebecca on the tender subject, she compensated herself with long and intimate conversations with Mrs. Blenkinsop, the housekeeper, who dropped some hints to the lady's-maid, who may have cursorily mentioned the matter to the cook, who carried the news, I have no doubt, to all the tradesmen, so that Mr. Jos's marriage was now talked of by a very considerable number of persons in the Russell Square world.\n\nIt was, of course, Mrs. Sedley's opinion that her son would demean himself by a marriage with an artist's daughter. \"But, lor', Ma'am,\" ejaculated Mrs. Blenkinsop, \"we was only grocers when we married Mr. S., who was a stock-broker's clerk, and we hadn't five hundred pounds among us, and we're rich enough now.\" And Amelia was entirely of this opinion, to which, gradually, the good-natured Mrs. Sedley was brought.\n\nMr. Sedley was neutral. \"Let Jos marry whom he likes,\" he said; \"it's no affair of mine. This girl has no fortune; no more had Mrs. Sedley. She seems good-humoured and clever, and will keep him in order, perhaps. Better she, my dear, than a black Mrs. Sedley, and a dozen of mahogany grandchildren.\"\n\nSo that everything seemed to smile upon Rebecca's fortunes. She took Jos's arm, as a matter of course, on going to dinner; she had sate by him on the box of his open carriage (a most tremendous \"buck\" he was, as he sat there, serene, in state, driving his greys), and though nobody said a word on the subject of the marriage, everybody seemed to understand it. All she wanted was the proposal, and ah! how Rebecca now felt the want of a mother!--a dear, tender mother, who would have managed the business in ten minutes, and, in the course of a little delicate confidential conversation, would have extracted the interesting avowal from the bashful lips of the young man!\n\nSuch was the state of affairs as the carriage crossed Westminster bridge.\n\nThe party was landed at the Royal Gardens in due time. As the majestic Jos stepped out of the creaking vehicle the crowd gave a cheer for the fat gentleman, who blushed and looked very big and mighty, as he walked away with Rebecca under his arm. George, of course, took charge of Amelia. She looked as happy as a rose-tree in sunshine.\n\n\"I say, Dobbin,\" says George, \"just look to the shawls and things, there's a good fellow.\" And so while he paired off with Miss Sedley, and Jos squeezed through the gate into the gardens with Rebecca at his side, honest Dobbin contented himself by giving an arm to the shawls, and by paying at the door for the whole party.\n\nHe walked very modestly behind them. He was not willing to spoil sport. About Rebecca and Jos he did not care a fig. But he thought Amelia worthy even of the brilliant George Osborne, and as he saw that good-looking couple threading the walks to the girl's delight and wonder, he watched her artless happiness with a sort of fatherly pleasure. Perhaps he felt that he would have liked to have something on his own arm besides a shawl (the people laughed at seeing the gawky young officer carrying this female burthen); but William Dobbin was very little addicted to selfish calculation at all; and so long as his friend was enjoying himself, how should he be discontented? And the truth is, that of all the delights of the Gardens; of the hundred thousand extra lamps, which were always lighted; the fiddlers in cocked hats, who played ravishing melodies under the gilded cockle-shell in the midst of the gardens; the singers, both of comic and sentimental ballads, who charmed the ears there; the country dances, formed by bouncing cockneys and cockneyesses, and executed amidst jumping, thumping and laughter; the signal which announced that Madame Saqui was about to mount skyward on a slack-rope ascending to the stars; the hermit that always sat in the illuminated hermitage; the dark walks, so favourable to the interviews of young lovers; the pots of stout handed about by the people in the shabby old liveries; and the twinkling boxes, in which the happy feasters made-believe to eat slices of almost invisible ham--of all these things, and of the gentle Simpson, that kind smiling idiot, who, I daresay, presided even then over the place--Captain William Dobbin did not take the slightest notice.\n\nHe carried about Amelia's white cashmere shawl, and having attended under the gilt cockle-shell, while Mrs. Salmon performed the Battle of Borodino (a savage cantata against the Corsican upstart, who had lately met with his Russian reverses)--Mr. Dobbin tried to hum it as he walked away, and found he was humming--the tune which Amelia Sedley sang on the stairs, as she came down to dinner.\n\nHe burst out laughing at himself; for the truth is, he could sing no better than an owl.\n\nIt is to be understood, as a matter of course, that our young people, being in parties of two and two, made the most solemn promises to keep together during the evening, and separated in ten minutes afterwards. Parties at Vauxhall always did separate, but 'twas only to meet again at supper-time, when they could talk of their mutual adventures in the interval.\n\nWhat were the adventures of Mr. Osborne and Miss Amelia? That is a secret. But be sure of this--they were perfectly happy, and correct in their behaviour; and as they had been in the habit of being together any time these fifteen years, their tete-a-tete offered no particular novelty.\n\nBut when Miss Rebecca Sharp and her stout companion lost themselves in a solitary walk, in which there were not above five score more of couples similarly straying, they both felt that the situation was extremely tender and critical, and now or never was the moment Miss Sharp thought, to provoke that declaration which was trembling on the timid lips of Mr. Sedley. They had previously been to the panorama of Moscow, where a rude fellow, treading on Miss Sharp's foot, caused her to fall back with a little shriek into the arms of Mr. Sedley, and this little incident increased the tenderness and confidence of that gentleman to such a degree, that he told her several of his favourite Indian stories over again for, at least, the sixth time.\n\n\"How I should like to see India!\" said Rebecca.\n\n\"SHOULD you?\" said Joseph, with a most killing tenderness; and was no doubt about to follow up this artful interrogatory by a question still more tender (for he puffed and panted a great deal, and Rebecca's hand, which was placed near his heart, could count the feverish pulsations of that organ), when, oh, provoking! the bell rang for the fireworks, and, a great scuffling and running taking place, these interesting lovers were obliged to follow in the stream of people.\n\nCaptain Dobbin had some thoughts of joining the party at supper: as, in truth, he found the Vauxhall amusements not particularly lively--but he paraded twice before the box where the now united couples were met, and nobody took any notice of him. Covers were laid for four. The mated pairs were prattling away quite happily, and Dobbin knew he was as clean forgotten as if he had never existed in this world.\n\n\"I should only be de trop,\" said the Captain, looking at them rather wistfully. \"I'd best go and talk to the hermit,\"--and so he strolled off out of the hum of men, and noise, and clatter of the banquet, into the dark walk, at the end of which lived that well-known pasteboard Solitary. It wasn't very good fun for Dobbin--and, indeed, to be alone at Vauxhall, I have found, from my own experience, to be one of the most dismal sports ever entered into by a bachelor.\n\nThe two couples were perfectly happy then in their box: where the most delightful and intimate conversation took place. Jos was in his glory, ordering about the waiters with great majesty. He made the salad; and uncorked the Champagne; and carved the chickens; and ate and drank the greater part of the refreshments on the tables. Finally, he insisted upon having a bowl of rack punch; everybody had rack punch at Vauxhall. \"Waiter, rack punch.\"\n\nThat bowl of rack punch was the cause of all this history. And why not a bowl of rack punch as well as any other cause? Was not a bowl of prussic acid the cause of Fair Rosamond's retiring from the world? Was not a bowl of wine the cause of the demise of Alexander the Great, or, at least, does not Dr. Lempriere say so?--so did this bowl of rack punch influence the fates of all the principal characters in this \"Novel without a Hero,\" which we are now relating. It influenced their life, although most of them did not taste a drop of it.\n\nThe young ladies did not drink it; Osborne did not like it; and the consequence was that Jos, that fat gourmand, drank up the whole contents of the bowl; and the consequence of his drinking up the whole contents of the bowl was a liveliness which at first was astonishing, and then became almost painful; for he talked and laughed so loud as to bring scores of listeners round the box, much to the confusion of the innocent party within it; and, volunteering to sing a song (which he did in that maudlin high key peculiar to gentlemen in an inebriated state), he almost drew away the audience who were gathered round the musicians in the gilt scollop-shell, and received from his hearers a great deal of applause.\n\n\"Brayvo, Fat un!\" said one; \"Angcore, Daniel Lambert!\" said another; \"What a figure for the tight-rope!\" exclaimed another wag, to the inexpressible alarm of the ladies, and the great anger of Mr. Osborne.\n\n\"For Heaven's sake, Jos, let us get up and go,\" cried that gentleman, and the young women rose.\n\n\"Stop, my dearest diddle-diddle-darling,\" shouted Jos, now as bold as a lion, and clasping Miss Rebecca round the waist. Rebecca started, but she could not get away her hand. The laughter outside redoubled. Jos continued to drink, to make love, and to sing; and, winking and waving his glass gracefully to his audience, challenged all or any to come in and take a share of his punch.\n\nMr. Osborne was just on the point of knocking down a gentleman in top-boots, who proposed to take advantage of this invitation, and a commotion seemed to be inevitable, when by the greatest good luck a gentleman of the name of Dobbin, who had been walking about the gardens, stepped up to the box. \"Be off, you fools!\" said this gentleman--shouldering off a great number of the crowd, who vanished presently before his cocked hat and fierce appearance--and he entered the box in a most agitated state.\n\n\"Good Heavens! Dobbin, where have you been?\" Osborne said, seizing the white cashmere shawl from his friend's arm, and huddling up Amelia in it.--\"Make yourself useful, and take charge of Jos here, whilst I take the ladies to the carriage.\"\n\nJos was for rising to interfere--but a single push from Osborne's finger sent him puffing back into his seat again, and the lieutenant was enabled to remove the ladies in safety. Jos kissed his hand to them as they retreated, and hiccupped out \"Bless you! Bless you!\" Then, seizing Captain Dobbin's hand, and weeping in the most pitiful way, he confided to that gentleman the secret of his loves. He adored that girl who had just gone out; he had broken her heart, he knew he had, by his conduct; he would marry her next morning at St. George's, Hanover Square; he'd knock up the Archbishop of Canterbury at Lambeth: he would, by Jove! and have him in readiness; and, acting on this hint, Captain Dobbin shrewdly induced him to leave the gardens and hasten to Lambeth Palace, and, when once out of the gates, easily conveyed Mr. Jos Sedley into a hackney-coach, which deposited him safely at his lodgings.\n\nGeorge Osborne conducted the girls home in safety: and when the door was closed upon them, and as he walked across Russell Square, laughed so as to astonish the watchman. Amelia looked very ruefully at her friend, as they went up stairs, and kissed her, and went to bed without any more talking.\n\n\"He must propose to-morrow,\" thought Rebecca. \"He called me his soul's darling, four times; he squeezed my hand in Amelia's presence. He must propose to-morrow.\" And so thought Amelia, too. And I dare say she thought of the dress she was to wear as bridesmaid, and of the presents which she should make to her nice little sister-in-law, and of a subsequent ceremony in which she herself might play a principal part, &c., and &c., and &c., and &c.\n\nOh, ignorant young creatures! How little do you know the effect of rack punch! What is the rack in the punch, at night, to the rack in the head of a morning? To this truth I can vouch as a man; there is no headache in the world like that caused by Vauxhall punch. Through the lapse of twenty years, I can remember the consequence of two glasses! two wine-glasses! but two, upon the honour of a gentleman; and Joseph Sedley, who had a liver complaint, had swallowed at least a quart of the abominable mixture.\n\nThat next morning, which Rebecca thought was to dawn upon her fortune, found Sedley groaning in agonies which the pen refuses to describe. Soda-water was not invented yet. Small beer--will it be believed!--was the only drink with which unhappy gentlemen soothed the fever of their previous night's potation. With this mild beverage before him, George Osborne found the ex-Collector of Boggley Wollah groaning on the sofa at his lodgings. Dobbin was already in the room, good-naturedly tending his patient of the night before. The two officers, looking at the prostrate Bacchanalian, and askance at each other, exchanged the most frightful sympathetic grins. Even Sedley's valet, the most solemn and correct of gentlemen, with the muteness and gravity of an undertaker, could hardly keep his countenance in order, as he looked at his unfortunate master.\n\n\"Mr. Sedley was uncommon wild last night, sir,\" he whispered in confidence to Osborne, as the latter mounted the stair. \"He wanted to fight the 'ackney-coachman, sir. The Capting was obliged to bring him upstairs in his harms like a babby.\" A momentary smile flickered over Mr. Brush's features as he spoke; instantly, however, they relapsed into their usual unfathomable calm, as he flung open the drawing-room door, and announced \"Mr. Hosbin.\"\n\n\"How are you, Sedley?\" that young wag began, after surveying his victim. \"No bones broke? There's a hackney-coachman downstairs with a black eye, and a tied-up head, vowing he'll have the law of you.\"\n\n\"What do you mean--law?\" Sedley faintly asked.\n\n\"For thrashing him last night--didn't he, Dobbin? You hit out, sir, like Molyneux. The watchman says he never saw a fellow go down so straight. Ask Dobbin.\"\n\n\"You DID have a round with the coachman,\" Captain Dobbin said, \"and showed plenty of fight too.\"\n\n\"And that fellow with the white coat at Vauxhall! How Jos drove at him! How the women screamed! By Jove, sir, it did my heart good to see you. I thought you civilians had no pluck; but I'll never get in your way when you are in your cups, Jos.\"\n\n\"I believe I'm very terrible, when I'm roused,\" ejaculated Jos from the sofa, and made a grimace so dreary and ludicrous, that the Captain's politeness could restrain him no longer, and he and Osborne fired off a ringing volley of laughter.\n\nOsborne pursued his advantage pitilessly. He thought Jos a milksop. He had been revolving in his mind the marriage question pending between Jos and Rebecca, and was not over well pleased that a member of a family into which he, George Osborne, of the --th, was going to marry, should make a mesalliance with a little nobody--a little upstart governess. \"You hit, you poor old fellow!\" said Osborne. \"You terrible! Why, man, you couldn't stand--you made everybody laugh in the Gardens, though you were crying yourself. You were maudlin, Jos. Don't you remember singing a song?\"\n\n\"A what?\" Jos asked.\n\n\"A sentimental song, and calling Rosa, Rebecca, what's her name, Amelia's little friend--your dearest diddle-diddle-darling?\" And this ruthless young fellow, seizing hold of Dobbin's hand, acted over the scene, to the horror of the original performer, and in spite of Dobbin's good-natured entreaties to him to have mercy.\n\n\"Why should I spare him?\" Osborne said to his friend's remonstrances, when they quitted the invalid, leaving him under the hands of Doctor Gollop. \"What the deuce right has he to give himself his patronizing airs, and make fools of us at Vauxhall? Who's this little schoolgirl that is ogling and making love to him? Hang it, the family's low enough already, without HER. A governess is all very well, but I'd rather have a lady for my sister-in-law. I'm a liberal man; but I've proper pride, and know my own station: let her know hers. And I'll take down that great hectoring Nabob, and prevent him from being made a greater fool than he is. That's why I told him to look out, lest she brought an action against him.\"\n\n\"I suppose you know best,\" Dobbin said, though rather dubiously. \"You always were a Tory, and your family's one of the oldest in England. But--\"\n\n\"Come and see the girls, and make love to Miss Sharp yourself,\" the lieutenant here interrupted his friend; but Captain Dobbin declined to join Osborne in his daily visit to the young ladies in Russell Square.\n\nAs George walked down Southampton Row, from Holborn, he laughed as he saw, at the Sedley Mansion, in two different stories two heads on the look-out.\n\nThe fact is, Miss Amelia, in the drawing-room balcony, was looking very eagerly towards the opposite side of the Square, where Mr. Osborne dwelt, on the watch for the lieutenant himself; and Miss Sharp, from her little bed-room on the second floor, was in observation until Mr. Joseph's great form should heave in sight.\n\n\"Sister Anne is on the watch-tower,\" said he to Amelia, \"but there's nobody coming\"; and laughing and enjoying the joke hugely, he described in the most ludicrous terms to Miss Sedley, the dismal condition of her brother.\n\n\"I think it's very cruel of you to laugh, George,\" she said, looking particularly unhappy; but George only laughed the more at her piteous and discomfited mien, persisted in thinking the joke a most diverting one, and when Miss Sharp came downstairs, bantered her with a great deal of liveliness upon the effect of her charms on the fat civilian.\n\n\"O Miss Sharp! if you could but see him this morning,\" he said--\"moaning in his flowered dressing-gown--writhing on his sofa; if you could but have seen him lolling out his tongue to Gollop the apothecary.\"\n\n\"See whom?\" said Miss Sharp.\n\n\"Whom? O whom? Captain Dobbin, of course, to whom we were all so attentive, by the way, last night.\"\n\n\"We were very unkind to him,\" Emmy said, blushing very much. \"I--I quite forgot him.\"\n\n\"Of course you did,\" cried Osborne, still on the laugh.\n\n\"One can't be ALWAYS thinking about Dobbin, you know, Amelia. Can one, Miss Sharp?\"\n\n\"Except when he overset the glass of wine at dinner,\" Miss Sharp said, with a haughty air and a toss of the head, \"I never gave the existence of Captain Dobbin one single moment's consideration.\"\n\n\"Very good, Miss Sharp, I'll tell him,\" Osborne said; and as he spoke Miss Sharp began to have a feeling of distrust and hatred towards this young officer, which he was quite unconscious of having inspired. \"He is to make fun of me, is he?\" thought Rebecca. \"Has he been laughing about me to Joseph? Has he frightened him? Perhaps he won't come.\"--A film passed over her eyes, and her heart beat quite quick.\n\n\"You're always joking,\" said she, smiling as innocently as she could. \"Joke away, Mr. George; there's nobody to defend ME.\" And George Osborne, as she walked away--and Amelia looked reprovingly at him--felt some little manly compunction for having inflicted any unnecessary unkindness upon this helpless creature. \"My dearest Amelia,\" said he, \"you are too good--too kind. You don't know the world. I do. And your little friend Miss Sharp must learn her station.\"\n\n\"Don't you think Jos will--\"\n\n\"Upon my word, my dear, I don't know. He may, or may not. I'm not his master. I only know he is a very foolish vain fellow, and put my dear little girl into a very painful and awkward position last night. My dearest diddle-diddle-darling!\" He was off laughing again, and he did it so drolly that Emmy laughed too.\n\nAll that day Jos never came. But Amelia had no fear about this; for the little schemer had actually sent away the page, Mr. Sambo's aide-de-camp, to Mr. Joseph's lodgings, to ask for some book he had promised, and how he was; and the reply through Jos's man, Mr. Brush, was, that his master was ill in bed, and had just had the doctor with him. He must come to-morrow, she thought, but she never had the courage to speak a word on the subject to Rebecca; nor did that young woman herself allude to it in any way during the whole evening after the night at Vauxhall.\n\nThe next day, however, as the two young ladies sate on the sofa, pretending to work, or to write letters, or to read novels, Sambo came into the room with his usual engaging grin, with a packet under his arm, and a note on a tray. \"Note from Mr. Jos, Miss,\" says Sambo.\n\nHow Amelia trembled as she opened it!\n\nDear Amelia,--I send you the \"Orphan of the Forest.\" I was too ill to come yesterday. I leave town to-day for Cheltenham. Pray excuse me, if you can, to the amiable Miss Sharp, for my conduct at Vauxhall, and entreat her to pardon and forget every word I may have uttered when excited by that fatal supper. As soon as I have recovered, for my health is very much shaken, I shall go to Scotland for some months, and am\n\nTruly yours, Jos Sedley\n\nIt was the death-warrant. All was over. Amelia did not dare to look at Rebecca's pale face and burning eyes, but she dropt the letter into her friend's lap; and got up, and went upstairs to her room, and cried her little heart out.\n\nBlenkinsop, the housekeeper, there sought her presently with consolation, on whose shoulder Amelia wept confidentially, and relieved herself a good deal. \"Don't take on, Miss. I didn't like to tell you. But none of us in the house have liked her except at fust. I sor her with my own eyes reading your Ma's letters. Pinner says she's always about your trinket-box and drawers, and everybody's drawers, and she's sure she's put your white ribbing into her box.\"\n\n\"I gave it her, I gave it her,\" Amelia said.\n\nBut this did not alter Mrs. Blenkinsop's opinion of Miss Sharp. \"I don't trust them governesses, Pinner,\" she remarked to the maid. \"They give themselves the hairs and hupstarts of ladies, and their wages is no better than you nor me.\"\n\nIt now became clear to every soul in the house, except poor Amelia, that Rebecca should take her departure, and high and low (always with the one exception) agreed that that event should take place as speedily as possible. Our good child ransacked all her drawers, cupboards, reticules, and gimcrack boxes--passed in review all her gowns, fichus, tags, bobbins, laces, silk stockings, and fallals--selecting this thing and that and the other, to make a little heap for Rebecca. And going to her Papa, that generous British merchant, who had promised to give her as many guineas as she was years old--she begged the old gentleman to give the money to dear Rebecca, who must want it, while she lacked for nothing.\n\nShe even made George Osborne contribute, and nothing loth (for he was as free-handed a young fellow as any in the army), he went to Bond Street, and bought the best hat and spenser that money could buy.\n\n\"That's George's present to you, Rebecca, dear,\" said Amelia, quite proud of the bandbox conveying these gifts. \"What a taste he has! There's nobody like him.\"\n\n\"Nobody,\" Rebecca answered. \"How thankful I am to him!\" She was thinking in her heart, \"It was George Osborne who prevented my marriage.\"--And she loved George Osborne accordingly.\n\nShe made her preparations for departure with great equanimity; and accepted all the kind little Amelia's presents, after just the proper degree of hesitation and reluctance. She vowed eternal gratitude to Mrs. Sedley, of course; but did not intrude herself upon that good lady too much, who was embarrassed, and evidently wishing to avoid her. She kissed Mr. Sedley's hand, when he presented her with the purse; and asked permission to consider him for the future as her kind, kind friend and protector. Her behaviour was so affecting that he was going to write her a cheque for twenty pounds more; but he restrained his feelings: the carriage was in waiting to take him to dinner, so he tripped away with a \"God bless you, my dear, always come here when you come to town, you know.--Drive to the Mansion House, James.\"\n\nFinally came the parting with Miss Amelia, over which picture I intend to throw a veil. But after a scene in which one person was in earnest and the other a perfect performer--after the tenderest caresses, the most pathetic tears, the smelling-bottle, and some of the very best feelings of the heart, had been called into requisition--Rebecca and Amelia parted, the former vowing to love her friend for ever and ever and ever.\n\nCrawley of Queen's Crawley\nAmong the most respected of the names beginning in C which the Court-Guide contained, in the year 18--, was that of Crawley, Sir Pitt, Baronet, Great Gaunt Street, and Queen's Crawley, Hants. This honourable name had figured constantly also in the Parliamentary list for many years, in conjunction with that of a number of other worthy gentlemen who sat in turns for the borough.\n\nIt is related, with regard to the borough of Queen's Crawley, that Queen Elizabeth in one of her progresses, stopping at Crawley to breakfast, was so delighted with some remarkably fine Hampshire beer which was then presented to her by the Crawley of the day (a handsome gentleman with a trim beard and a good leg), that she forthwith erected Crawley into a borough to send two members to Parliament; and the place, from the day of that illustrious visit, took the name of Queen's Crawley, which it holds up to the present moment. And though, by the lapse of time, and those mutations which age produces in empires, cities, and boroughs, Queen's Crawley was no longer so populous a place as it had been in Queen Bess's time--nay, was come down to that condition of borough which used to be denominated rotten--yet, as Sir Pitt Crawley would say with perfect justice in his elegant way, \"Rotten! be hanged--it produces me a good fifteen hundred a year.\"\n\nSir Pitt Crawley (named after the great Commoner) was the son of Walpole Crawley, first Baronet, of the Tape and Sealing-Wax Office in the reign of George II., when he was impeached for peculation, as were a great number of other honest gentlemen of those days; and Walpole Crawley was, as need scarcely be said, son of John Churchill Crawley, named after the celebrated military commander of the reign of Queen Anne. The family tree (which hangs up at Queen's Crawley) furthermore mentions Charles Stuart, afterwards called Barebones Crawley, son of the Crawley of James the First's time; and finally, Queen Elizabeth's Crawley, who is represented as the foreground of the picture in his forked beard and armour. Out of his waistcoat, as usual, grows a tree, on the main branches of which the above illustrious names are inscribed. Close by the name of Sir Pitt Crawley, Baronet (the subject of the present memoir), are written that of his brother, the Reverend Bute Crawley (the great Commoner was in disgrace when the reverend gentleman was born), rector of Crawley-cum-Snailby, and of various other male and female members of the Crawley family.\n\nSir Pitt was first married to Grizzel, sixth daughter of Mungo Binkie, Lord Binkie, and cousin, in consequence, of Mr. Dundas. She brought him two sons: Pitt, named not so much after his father as after the heaven-born minister; and Rawdon Crawley, from the Prince of Wales's friend, whom his Majesty George IV forgot so completely. Many years after her ladyship's demise, Sir Pitt led to the altar Rosa, daughter of Mr. G. Dawson, of Mudbury, by whom he had two daughters, for whose benefit Miss Rebecca Sharp was now engaged as governess. It will be seen that the young lady was come into a family of very genteel connexions, and was about to move in a much more distinguished circle than that humble one which she had just quitted in Russell Square.\n\nShe had received her orders to join her pupils, in a note which was written upon an old envelope, and which contained the following words:\n\nSir Pitt Crawley begs Miss Sharp and baggidge may be hear on Tuesday, as I leaf for Queen's Crawley to-morrow morning ERLY.\n\nGreat Gaunt Street.\n\nRebecca had never seen a Baronet, as far as she knew, and as soon as she had taken leave of Amelia, and counted the guineas which good-natured Mr. Sedley had put into a purse for her, and as soon as she had done wiping her eyes with her handkerchief (which operation she concluded the very moment the carriage had turned the corner of the street), she began to depict in her own mind what a Baronet must be. \"I wonder, does he wear a star?\" thought she, \"or is it only lords that wear stars? But he will be very handsomely dressed in a court suit, with ruffles, and his hair a little powdered, like Mr. Wroughton at Covent Garden. I suppose he will be awfully proud, and that I shall be treated most contemptuously. Still I must bear my hard lot as well as I can--at least, I shall be amongst GENTLEFOLKS, and not with vulgar city people\": and she fell to thinking of her Russell Square friends with that very same philosophical bitterness with which, in a certain apologue, the fox is represented as speaking of the grapes.\n\nHaving passed through Gaunt Square into Great Gaunt Street, the carriage at length stopped at a tall gloomy house between two other tall gloomy houses, each with a hatchment over the middle drawing-room window; as is the custom of houses in Great Gaunt Street, in which gloomy locality death seems to reign perpetual. The shutters of the first-floor windows of Sir Pitt's mansion were closed--those of the dining-room were partially open, and the blinds neatly covered up in old newspapers.\n\nJohn, the groom, who had driven the carriage alone, did not care to descend to ring the bell; and so prayed a passing milk-boy to perform that office for him. When the bell was rung, a head appeared between the interstices of the dining-room shutters, and the door was opened by a man in drab breeches and gaiters, with a dirty old coat, a foul old neckcloth lashed round his bristly neck, a shining bald head, a leering red face, a pair of twinkling grey eyes, and a mouth perpetually on the grin.\n\n\"This Sir Pitt Crawley's?\" says John, from the box.\n\n\"Ees,\" says the man at the door, with a nod.\n\n\"Hand down these 'ere trunks then,\" said John.\n\n\"Hand 'n down yourself,\" said the porter.\n\n\"Don't you see I can't leave my hosses? Come, bear a hand, my fine feller, and Miss will give you some beer,\" said John, with a horse-laugh, for he was no longer respectful to Miss Sharp, as her connexion with the family was broken off, and as she had given nothing to the servants on coming away.\n\nThe bald-headed man, taking his hands out of his breeches pockets, advanced on this summons, and throwing Miss Sharp's trunk over his shoulder, carried it into the house.\n\n\"Take this basket and shawl, if you please, and open the door,\" said Miss Sharp, and descended from the carriage in much indignation. \"I shall write to Mr. Sedley and inform him of your conduct,\" said she to the groom.\n\n\"Don't,\" replied that functionary. \"I hope you've forgot nothink? Miss 'Melia's gownds--have you got them--as the lady's maid was to have 'ad? I hope they'll fit you. Shut the door, Jim, you'll get no good out of 'ER,\" continued John, pointing with his thumb towards Miss Sharp: \"a bad lot, I tell you, a bad lot,\" and so saying, Mr. Sedley's groom drove away. The truth is, he was attached to the lady's maid in question, and indignant that she should have been robbed of her perquisites.\n\nOn entering the dining-room, by the orders of the individual in gaiters, Rebecca found that apartment not more cheerful than such rooms usually are, when genteel families are out of town. The faithful chambers seem, as it were, to mourn the absence of their masters. The turkey carpet has rolled itself up, and retired sulkily under the sideboard: the pictures have hidden their faces behind old sheets of brown paper: the ceiling lamp is muffled up in a dismal sack of brown holland: the window-curtains have disappeared under all sorts of shabby envelopes: the marble bust of Sir Walpole Crawley is looking from its black corner at the bare boards and the oiled fire-irons, and the empty card-racks over the mantelpiece: the cellaret has lurked away behind the carpet: the chairs are turned up heads and tails along the walls: and in the dark corner opposite the statue, is an old-fashioned crabbed knife-box, locked and sitting on a dumb waiter.\n\nTwo kitchen chairs, and a round table, and an attenuated old poker and tongs were, however, gathered round the fire-place, as was a saucepan over a feeble sputtering fire. There was a bit of cheese and bread, and a tin candlestick on the table, and a little black porter in a pint-pot.\n\n\"Had your dinner, I suppose? It is not too warm for you? Like a drop of beer?\"\n\n\"Where is Sir Pitt Crawley?\" said Miss Sharp majestically.\n\n\"He, he! I'm Sir Pitt Crawley. Reklect you owe me a pint for bringing down your luggage. He, he! Ask Tinker if I aynt. Mrs. Tinker, Miss Sharp; Miss Governess, Mrs. Charwoman. Ho, ho!\"\n\nThe lady addressed as Mrs. Tinker at this moment made her appearance with a pipe and a paper of tobacco, for which she had been despatched a minute before Miss Sharp's arrival; and she handed the articles over to Sir Pitt, who had taken his seat by the fire.\n\n\"Where's the farden?\" said he. \"I gave you three halfpence. Where's the change, old Tinker?\"\n\n\"There!\" replied Mrs. Tinker, flinging down the coin; \"it's only baronets as cares about farthings.\"\n\n\"A farthing a day is seven shillings a year,\" answered the M.P.; \"seven shillings a year is the interest of seven guineas. Take care of your farthings, old Tinker, and your guineas will come quite nat'ral.\"\n\n\"You may be sure it's Sir Pitt Crawley, young woman,\" said Mrs. Tinker, surlily; \"because he looks to his farthings. You'll know him better afore long.\"\n\n\"And like me none the worse, Miss Sharp,\" said the old gentleman, with an air almost of politeness. \"I must be just before I'm generous.\"\n\n\"He never gave away a farthing in his life,\" growled Tinker.\n\n\"Never, and never will: it's against my principle. Go and get another chair from the kitchen, Tinker, if you want to sit down; and then we'll have a bit of supper.\"\n\nPresently the baronet plunged a fork into the saucepan on the fire, and withdrew from the pot a piece of tripe and an onion, which he divided into pretty equal portions, and of which he partook with Mrs. Tinker. \"You see, Miss Sharp, when I'm not here Tinker's on board wages: when I'm in town she dines with the family. Haw! haw! I'm glad Miss Sharp's not hungry, ain't you, Tink?\" And they fell to upon their frugal supper.\n\nAfter supper Sir Pitt Crawley began to smoke his pipe; and when it became quite dark, he lighted the rushlight in the tin candlestick, and producing from an interminable pocket a huge mass of papers, began reading them, and putting them in order.\n\n\"I'm here on law business, my dear, and that's how it happens that I shall have the pleasure of such a pretty travelling companion to-morrow.\"\n\n\"He's always at law business,\" said Mrs. Tinker, taking up the pot of porter.\n\n\"Drink and drink about,\" said the Baronet. \"Yes; my dear, Tinker is quite right: I've lost and won more lawsuits than any man in England. Look here at Crawley, Bart. v. Snaffle. I'll throw him over, or my name's not Pitt Crawley. Podder and another versus Crawley, Bart. Overseers of Snaily parish against Crawley, Bart. They can't prove it's common: I'll defy 'em; the land's mine. It no more belongs to the parish than it does to you or Tinker here. I'll beat 'em, if it cost me a thousand guineas. Look over the papers; you may if you like, my dear. Do you write a good hand? I'll make you useful when we're at Queen's Crawley, depend on it, Miss Sharp. Now the dowager's dead I want some one.\"\n\n\"She was as bad as he,\" said Tinker. \"She took the law of every one of her tradesmen; and turned away forty-eight footmen in four year.\"\n\n\"She was close--very close,\" said the Baronet, simply; \"but she was a valyble woman to me, and saved me a steward.\"--And in this confidential strain, and much to the amusement of the new-comer, the conversation continued for a considerable time. Whatever Sir Pitt Crawley's qualities might be, good or bad, he did not make the least disguise of them. He talked of himself incessantly, sometimes in the coarsest and vulgarest Hampshire accent; sometimes adopting the tone of a man of the world. And so, with injunctions to Miss Sharp to be ready at five in the morning, he bade her good night. \"You'll sleep with Tinker to-night,\" he said; \"it's a big bed, and there's room for two. Lady Crawley died in it. Good night.\"\n\nSir Pitt went off after this benediction, and the solemn Tinker, rushlight in hand, led the way up the great bleak stone stairs, past the great dreary drawing-room doors, with the handles muffled up in paper, into the great front bedroom, where Lady Crawley had slept her last. The bed and chamber were so funereal and gloomy, you might have fancied, not only that Lady Crawley died in the room, but that her ghost inhabited it. Rebecca sprang about the apartment, however, with the greatest liveliness, and had peeped into the huge wardrobes, and the closets, and the cupboards, and tried the drawers which were locked, and examined the dreary pictures and toilette appointments, while the old charwoman was saying her prayers. \"I shouldn't like to sleep in this yeer bed without a good conscience, Miss,\" said the old woman. \"There's room for us and a half-dozen of ghosts in it,\" says Rebecca. \"Tell me all about Lady Crawley and Sir Pitt Crawley, and everybody, my DEAR Mrs. Tinker.\"\n\nBut old Tinker was not to be pumped by this little cross-questioner; and signifying to her that bed was a place for sleeping, not conversation, set up in her corner of the bed such a snore as only the nose of innocence can produce. Rebecca lay awake for a long, long time, thinking of the morrow, and of the new world into which she was going, and of her chances of success there. The rushlight flickered in the basin. The mantelpiece cast up a great black shadow, over half of a mouldy old sampler, which her defunct ladyship had worked, no doubt, and over two little family pictures of young lads, one in a college gown, and the other in a red jacket like a soldier. When she went to sleep, Rebecca chose that one to dream about.\n\nAt four o'clock, on such a roseate summer's morning as even made Great Gaunt Street look cheerful, the faithful Tinker, having wakened her bedfellow, and bid her prepare for departure, unbarred and unbolted the great hall door (the clanging and clapping whereof startled the sleeping echoes in the street), and taking her way into Oxford Street, summoned a coach from a stand there. It is needless to particularize the number of the vehicle, or to state that the driver was stationed thus early in the neighbourhood of Swallow Street, in hopes that some young buck, reeling homeward from the tavern, might need the aid of his vehicle, and pay him with the generosity of intoxication.\n\nIt is likewise needless to say that the driver, if he had any such hopes as those above stated, was grossly disappointed; and that the worthy Baronet whom he drove to the City did not give him one single penny more than his fare. It was in vain that Jehu appealed and stormed; that he flung down Miss Sharp's bandboxes in the gutter at the 'Necks, and swore he would take the law of his fare.\n\n\"You'd better not,\" said one of the ostlers; \"it's Sir Pitt Crawley.\"\n\n\"So it is, Joe,\" cried the Baronet, approvingly; \"and I'd like to see the man can do me.\"\n\n\"So should oi,\" said Joe, grinning sulkily, and mounting the Baronet's baggage on the roof of the coach.\n\n\"Keep the box for me, Leader,\" exclaims the Member of Parliament to the coachman; who replied, \"Yes, Sir Pitt,\" with a touch of his hat, and rage in his soul (for he had promised the box to a young gentleman from Cambridge, who would have given a crown to a certainty), and Miss Sharp was accommodated with a back seat inside the carriage, which might be said to be carrying her into the wide world.\n\nHow the young man from Cambridge sulkily put his five great-coats in front; but was reconciled when little Miss Sharp was made to quit the carriage, and mount up beside him--when he covered her up in one of his Benjamins, and became perfectly good-humoured--how the asthmatic gentleman, the prim lady, who declared upon her sacred honour she had never travelled in a public carriage before (there is always such a lady in a coach--Alas! was; for the coaches, where are they?), and the fat widow with the brandy-bottle, took their places inside--how the porter asked them all for money, and got sixpence from the gentleman and five greasy halfpence from the fat widow--and how the carriage at length drove away--now threading the dark lanes of Aldersgate, anon clattering by the Blue Cupola of St. Paul's, jingling rapidly by the strangers' entry of Fleet-Market, which, with Exeter 'Change, has now departed to the world of shadows--how they passed the White Bear in Piccadilly, and saw the dew rising up from the market-gardens of Knightsbridge--how Turnhamgreen, Brentwood, Bagshot, were passed--need not be told here. But the writer of these pages, who has pursued in former days, and in the same bright weather, the same remarkable journey, cannot but think of it with a sweet and tender regret. Where is the road now, and its merry incidents of life? Is there no Chelsea or Greenwich for the old honest pimple-nosed coachmen? I wonder where are they, those good fellows? Is old Weller alive or dead? and the waiters, yea, and the inns at which they waited, and the cold rounds of beef inside, and the stunted ostler, with his blue nose and clinking pail, where is he, and where is his generation? To those great geniuses now in petticoats, who shall write novels for the beloved reader's children, these men and things will be as much legend and history as Nineveh, or Coeur de Lion, or Jack Sheppard. For them stage-coaches will have become romances--a team of four bays as fabulous as Bucephalus or Black Bess. Ah, how their coats shone, as the stable-men pulled their clothes off, and away they went--ah, how their tails shook, as with smoking sides at the stage's end they demurely walked away into the inn-yard. Alas! we shall never hear the horn sing at midnight, or see the pike-gates fly open any more. Whither, however, is the light four-inside Trafalgar coach carrying us? Let us be set down at Queen's Crawley without further divagation, and see how Miss Rebecca Sharp speeds there.\n\nMiss Rebecca Sharp to Miss Amelia Sedley, Russell Square, London. (Free.--Pitt Crawley.)\n\n# MY DEAREST, SWEETEST AMELIA,\n\nWith what mingled joy and sorrow do I take up the pen to write to my dearest friend! Oh, what a change between to-day and yesterday! Now I am friendless and alone; yesterday I was at home, in the sweet company of a sister, whom I shall ever, ever cherish!\n\nI will not tell you in what tears and sadness I passed the fatal night in which I separated from you. YOU went on Tuesday to joy and happiness, with your mother and YOUR DEVOTED YOUNG SOLDIER by your side; and I thought of you all night, dancing at the Perkins's, the prettiest, I am sure, of all the young ladies at the Ball. I was brought by the groom in the old carriage to Sir Pitt Crawley's town house, where, after John the groom had behaved most rudely and insolently to me (alas! 'twas safe to insult poverty and misfortune!), I was given over to Sir P.'s care, and made to pass the night in an old gloomy bed, and by the side of a horrid gloomy old charwoman, who keeps the house. I did not sleep one single wink the whole night.\n\nSir Pitt is not what we silly girls, when we used to read Cecilia at Chiswick, imagined a baronet must have been. Anything, indeed, less like Lord Orville cannot be imagined. Fancy an old, stumpy, short, vulgar, and very dirty man, in old clothes and shabby old gaiters, who smokes a horrid pipe, and cooks his own horrid supper in a saucepan. He speaks with a country accent, and swore a great deal at the old charwoman, at the hackney coachman who drove us to the inn where the coach went from, and on which I made the journey OUTSIDE FOR THE GREATER PART OF THE WAY.\n\nI was awakened at daybreak by the charwoman, and having arrived at the inn, was at first placed inside the coach. But, when we got to a place called Leakington, where the rain began to fall very heavily--will you believe it?--I was forced to come outside; for Sir Pitt is a proprietor of the coach, and as a passenger came at Mudbury, who wanted an inside place, I was obliged to go outside in the rain, where, however, a young gentleman from Cambridge College sheltered me very kindly in one of his several great coats.\n\nThis gentleman and the guard seemed to know Sir Pitt very well, and laughed at him a great deal. They both agreed in calling him an old screw; which means a very stingy, avaricious person. He never gives any money to anybody, they said (and this meanness I hate); and the young gentleman made me remark that we drove very slow for the last two stages on the road, because Sir Pitt was on the box, and because he is proprietor of the horses for this part of the journey. \"But won't I flog 'em on to Squashmore, when I take the ribbons?\" said the young Cantab. \"And sarve 'em right, Master Jack,\" said the guard. When I comprehended the meaning of this phrase, and that Master Jack intended to drive the rest of the way, and revenge himself on Sir Pitt's horses, of course I laughed too.\n\nA carriage and four splendid horses, covered with armorial bearings, however, awaited us at Mudbury, four miles from Queen's Crawley, and we made our entrance to the baronet's park in state. There is a fine avenue of a mile long leading to the house, and the woman at the lodge-gate (over the pillars of which are a serpent and a dove, the supporters of the Crawley arms), made us a number of curtsies as she flung open the old iron carved doors, which are something like those at odious Chiswick.\n\n\"There's an avenue,\" said Sir Pitt, \"a mile long. There's six thousand pound of timber in them there trees. Do you call that nothing?\" He pronounced avenue--EVENUE, and nothing--NOTHINK, so droll; and he had a Mr. Hodson, his hind from Mudbury, into the carriage with him, and they talked about distraining, and selling up, and draining and subsoiling, and a great deal about tenants and farming--much more than I could understand. Sam Miles had been caught poaching, and Peter Bailey had gone to the workhouse at last. \"Serve him right,\" said Sir Pitt; \"him and his family has been cheating me on that farm these hundred and fifty years.\" Some old tenant, I suppose, who could not pay his rent. Sir Pitt might have said \"he and his family,\" to be sure; but rich baronets do not need to be careful about grammar, as poor governesses must be.\n\nAs we passed, I remarked a beautiful church-spire rising above some old elms in the park; and before them, in the midst of a lawn, and some outhouses, an old red house with tall chimneys covered with ivy, and the windows shining in the sun. \"Is that your church, sir?\" I said.\n\n\"Yes, hang it,\" (said Sir Pitt, only he used, dear, A MUCH WICKEDER WORD); \"how's Buty, Hodson? Buty's my brother Bute, my dear--my brother the parson. Buty and the Beast I call him, ha, ha!\"\n\nHodson laughed too, and then looking more grave and nodding his head, said, \"I'm afraid he's better, Sir Pitt. He was out on his pony yesterday, looking at our corn.\"\n\n\"Looking after his tithes, hang'un (only he used the same wicked word). Will brandy and water never kill him? He's as tough as old whatdyecallum--old Methusalem.\"\n\nMr. Hodson laughed again. \"The young men is home from college. They've whopped John Scroggins till he's well nigh dead.\"\n\n\"Whop my second keeper!\" roared out Sir Pitt.\n\n\"He was on the parson's ground, sir,\" replied Mr. Hodson; and Sir Pitt in a fury swore that if he ever caught 'em poaching on his ground, he'd transport 'em, by the lord he would. However, he said, \"I've sold the presentation of the living, Hodson; none of that breed shall get it, I war'nt\"; and Mr. Hodson said he was quite right: and I have no doubt from this that the two brothers are at variance--as brothers often are, and sisters too. Don't you remember the two Miss Scratchleys at Chiswick, how they used always to fight and quarrel--and Mary Box, how she was always thumping Louisa?\n\nPresently, seeing two little boys gathering sticks in the wood, Mr. Hodson jumped out of the carriage, at Sir Pitt's order, and rushed upon them with his whip. \"Pitch into 'em, Hodson,\" roared the baronet; \"flog their little souls out, and bring 'em up to the house, the vagabonds; I'll commit 'em as sure as my name's Pitt.\" And presently we heard Mr. Hodson's whip cracking on the shoulders of the poor little blubbering wretches, and Sir Pitt, seeing that the malefactors were in custody, drove on to the hall.\n\nAll the servants were ready to meet us, and . . .\n\nHere, my dear, I was interrupted last night by a dreadful thumping at my door: and who do you think it was? Sir Pitt Crawley in his night-cap and dressing-gown, such a figure! As I shrank away from such a visitor, he came forward and seized my candle. \"No candles after eleven o'clock, Miss Becky,\" said he. \"Go to bed in the dark, you pretty little hussy\" (that is what he called me), \"and unless you wish me to come for the candle every night, mind and be in bed at eleven.\" And with this, he and Mr. Horrocks the butler went off laughing. You may be sure I shall not encourage any more of their visits. They let loose two immense bloodhounds at night, which all last night were yelling and howling at the moon. \"I call the dog Gorer,\" said Sir Pitt; \"he's killed a man that dog has, and is master of a bull, and the mother I used to call Flora; but now I calls her Aroarer, for she's too old to bite. Haw, haw!\"\n\nBefore the house of Queen's Crawley, which is an odious old-fashioned red brick mansion, with tall chimneys and gables of the style of Queen Bess, there is a terrace flanked by the family dove and serpent, and on which the great hall-door opens. And oh, my dear, the great hall I am sure is as big and as glum as the great hall in the dear castle of Udolpho. It has a large fireplace, in which we might put half Miss Pinkerton's school, and the grate is big enough to roast an ox at the very least. Round the room hang I don't know how many generations of Crawleys, some with beards and ruffs, some with huge wigs and toes turned out, some dressed in long straight stays and gowns that look as stiff as towers, and some with long ringlets, and oh, my dear! scarcely any stays at all. At one end of the hall is the great staircase all in black oak, as dismal as may be, and on either side are tall doors with stags' heads over them, leading to the billiard-room and the library, and the great yellow saloon and the morning-rooms. I think there are at least twenty bedrooms on the first floor; one of them has the bed in which Queen Elizabeth slept; and I have been taken by my new pupils through all these fine apartments this morning. They are not rendered less gloomy, I promise you, by having the shutters always shut; and there is scarce one of the apartments, but when the light was let into it, I expected to see a ghost in the room. We have a schoolroom on the second floor, with my bedroom leading into it on one side, and that of the young ladies on the other. Then there are Mr. Pitt's apartments--Mr. Crawley, he is called--the eldest son, and Mr. Rawdon Crawley's rooms--he is an officer like SOMEBODY, and away with his regiment. There is no want of room I assure you. You might lodge all the people in Russell Square in the house, I think, and have space to spare.\n\nHalf an hour after our arrival, the great dinner-bell was rung, and I came down with my two pupils (they are very thin insignificant little chits of ten and eight years old). I came down in your dear muslin gown (about which that odious Mrs. Pinner was so rude, because you gave it me); for I am to be treated as one of the family, except on company days, when the young ladies and I are to dine upstairs.\n\nWell, the great dinner-bell rang, and we all assembled in the little drawing-room where my Lady Crawley sits. She is the second Lady Crawley, and mother of the young ladies. She was an ironmonger's daughter, and her marriage was thought a great match. She looks as if she had been handsome once, and her eyes are always weeping for the loss of her beauty. She is pale and meagre and high-shouldered, and has not a word to say for herself, evidently. Her stepson Mr. Crawley, was likewise in the room. He was in full dress, as pompous as an undertaker. He is pale, thin, ugly, silent; he has thin legs, no chest, hay-coloured whiskers, and straw-coloured hair. He is the very picture of his sainted mother over the mantelpiece--Griselda of the noble house of Binkie.\n\n\"This is the new governess, Mr. Crawley,\" said Lady Crawley, coming forward and taking my hand. \"Miss Sharp.\"\n\n\"O!\" said Mr. Crawley, and pushed his head once forward and began again to read a great pamphlet with which he was busy.\n\n\"I hope you will be kind to my girls,\" said Lady Crawley, with her pink eyes always full of tears.\n\n\"Law, Ma, of course she will,\" said the eldest: and I saw at a glance that I need not be afraid of THAT woman. \"My lady is served,\" says the butler in black, in an immense white shirt-frill, that looked as if it had been one of the Queen Elizabeth's ruffs depicted in the hall; and so, taking Mr. Crawley's arm, she led the way to the dining-room, whither I followed with my little pupils in each hand.\n\nSir Pitt was already in the room with a silver jug. He had just been to the cellar, and was in full dress too; that is, he had taken his gaiters off, and showed his little dumpy legs in black worsted stockings. The sideboard was covered with glistening old plate--old cups, both gold and silver; old salvers and cruet-stands, like Rundell and Bridge's shop. Everything on the table was in silver too, and two footmen, with red hair and canary-coloured liveries, stood on either side of the sideboard.\n\nMr. Crawley said a long grace, and Sir Pitt said amen, and the great silver dish-covers were removed.\n\n\"What have we for dinner, Betsy?\" said the Baronet.\n\n\"Mutton broth, I believe, Sir Pitt,\" answered Lady Crawley.\n\n\"Mouton aux navets,\" added the butler gravely (pronounce, if you please, moutongonavvy); \"and the soup is potage de mouton a l'Ecossaise. The side-dishes contain pommes de terre au naturel, and choufleur a l'eau.\"\n\n\"Mutton's mutton,\" said the Baronet, \"and a devilish good thing. What SHIP was it, Horrocks, and when did you kill?\" \"One of the black-faced Scotch, Sir Pitt: we killed on Thursday.\"\n\n\"Who took any?\"\n\n\"Steel, of Mudbury, took the saddle and two legs, Sir Pitt; but he says the last was too young and confounded woolly, Sir Pitt.\"\n\n\"Will you take some potage, Miss ah--Miss Blunt? said Mr. Crawley.\n\n\"Capital Scotch broth, my dear,\" said Sir Pitt, \"though they call it by a French name.\"\n\n\"I believe it is the custom, sir, in decent society,\" said Mr. Crawley, haughtily, \"to call the dish as I have called it\"; and it was served to us on silver soup plates by the footmen in the canary coats, with the mouton aux navets. Then \"ale and water\" were brought, and served to us young ladies in wine-glasses. I am not a judge of ale, but I can say with a clear conscience I prefer water.\n\nWhile we were enjoying our repast, Sir Pitt took occasion to ask what had become of the shoulders of the mutton.\n\n\"I believe they were eaten in the servants' hall,\" said my lady, humbly.\n\n\"They was, my lady,\" said Horrocks, \"and precious little else we get there neither.\"\n\nSir Pitt burst into a horse-laugh, and continued his conversation with Mr. Horrocks. \"That there little black pig of the Kent sow's breed must be uncommon fat now.\"\n\n\"It's not quite busting, Sir Pitt,\" said the butler with the gravest air, at which Sir Pitt, and with him the young ladies, this time, began to laugh violently.\n\n\"Miss Crawley, Miss Rose Crawley,\" said Mr. Crawley, \"your laughter strikes me as being exceedingly out of place.\"\n\n\"Never mind, my lord,\" said the Baronet, \"we'll try the porker on Saturday. Kill un on Saturday morning, John Horrocks. Miss Sharp adores pork, don't you, Miss Sharp?\"\n\nAnd I think this is all the conversation that I remember at dinner. When the repast was concluded a jug of hot water was placed before Sir Pitt, with a case-bottle containing, I believe, rum. Mr. Horrocks served myself and my pupils with three little glasses of wine, and a bumper was poured out for my lady. When we retired, she took from her work-drawer an enormous interminable piece of knitting; the young ladies began to play at cribbage with a dirty pack of cards. We had but one candle lighted, but it was in a magnificent old silver candlestick, and after a very few questions from my lady, I had my choice of amusement between a volume of sermons, and a pamphlet on the corn-laws, which Mr. Crawley had been reading before dinner.\n\nSo we sat for an hour until steps were heard.\n\n\"Put away the cards, girls,\" cried my lady, in a great tremor; \"put down Mr. Crawley's books, Miss Sharp\"; and these orders had been scarcely obeyed, when Mr. Crawley entered the room.\n\n\"We will resume yesterday's discourse, young ladies,\" said he, \"and you shall each read a page by turns; so that Miss a--Miss Short may have an opportunity of hearing you\"; and the poor girls began to spell a long dismal sermon delivered at Bethesda Chapel, Liverpool, on behalf of the mission for the Chickasaw Indians. Was it not a charming evening?\n\nAt ten the servants were told to call Sir Pitt and the household to prayers. Sir Pitt came in first, very much flushed, and rather unsteady in his gait; and after him the butler, the canaries, Mr. Crawley's man, three other men, smelling very much of the stable, and four women, one of whom, I remarked, was very much overdressed, and who flung me a look of great scorn as she plumped down on her knees.\n\nAfter Mr. Crawley had done haranguing and expounding, we received our candles, and then we went to bed; and then I was disturbed in my writing, as I have described to my dearest sweetest Amelia.\n\nGood night. A thousand, thousand, thousand kisses!\n\nSaturday.--This morning, at five, I heard the shrieking of the little black pig. Rose and Violet introduced me to it yesterday; and to the stables, and to the kennel, and to the gardener, who was picking fruit to send to market, and from whom they begged hard a bunch of hot-house grapes; but he said that Sir Pitt had numbered every \"Man Jack\" of them, and it would be as much as his place was worth to give any away. The darling girls caught a colt in a paddock, and asked me if I would ride, and began to ride themselves, when the groom, coming with horrid oaths, drove them away.\n\nLady Crawley is always knitting the worsted. Sir Pitt is always tipsy, every night; and, I believe, sits with Horrocks, the butler. Mr. Crawley always reads sermons in the evening, and in the morning is locked up in his study, or else rides to Mudbury, on county business, or to Squashmore, where he preaches, on Wednesdays and Fridays, to the tenants there.\n\nA hundred thousand grateful loves to your dear papa and mamma. Is your poor brother recovered of his rack-punch? Oh, dear! Oh, dear! How men should beware of wicked punch!\n\nEver and ever thine own REBECCA\n\nEverything considered, I think it is quite as well for our dear Amelia Sedley, in Russell Square, that Miss Sharp and she are parted. Rebecca is a droll funny creature, to be sure; and those descriptions of the poor lady weeping for the loss of her beauty, and the gentleman \"with hay-coloured whiskers and straw-coloured hair,\" are very smart, doubtless, and show a great knowledge of the world. That she might, when on her knees, have been thinking of something better than Miss Horrocks's ribbons, has possibly struck both of us. But my kind reader will please to remember that this history has \"Vanity Fair\" for a title, and that Vanity Fair is a very vain, wicked, foolish place, full of all sorts of humbugs and falsenesses and pretensions. And while the moralist, who is holding forth on the cover ( an accurate portrait of your humble servant), professes to wear neither gown nor bands, but only the very same long-eared livery in which his congregation is arrayed: yet, look you, one is bound to speak the truth as far as one knows it, whether one mounts a cap and bells or a shovel hat; and a deal of disagreeable matter must come out in the course of such an undertaking.\n\nI have heard a brother of the story-telling trade, at Naples, preaching to a pack of good-for-nothing honest lazy fellows by the sea-shore, work himself up into such a rage and passion with some of the villains whose wicked deeds he was describing and inventing, that the audience could not resist it; and they and the poet together would burst out into a roar of oaths and execrations against the fictitious monster of the tale, so that the hat went round, and the bajocchi tumbled into it, in the midst of a perfect storm of sympathy.\n\nAt the little Paris theatres, on the other hand, you will not only hear the people yelling out \"Ah gredin! Ah monstre:\" and cursing the tyrant of the play from the boxes; but the actors themselves positively refuse to play the wicked parts, such as those of infames Anglais, brutal Cossacks, and what not, and prefer to appear at a smaller salary, in their real characters as loyal Frenchmen. I set the two stories one against the other, so that you may see that it is not from mere mercenary motives that the present performer is desirous to show up and trounce his villains; but because he has a sincere hatred of them, which he cannot keep down, and which must find a vent in suitable abuse and bad language.\n\nI warn my \"kyind friends,\" then, that I am going to tell a story of harrowing villainy and complicated--but, as I trust, intensely interesting--crime. My rascals are no milk-and-water rascals, I promise you. When we come to the proper places we won't spare fine language--No, no! But when we are going over the quiet country we must perforce be calm. A tempest in a slop-basin is absurd. We will reserve that sort of thing for the mighty ocean and the lonely midnight. The present Chapter is very mild. Others--But we will not anticipate THOSE.\n\nAnd, as we bring our characters forward, I will ask leave, as a man and a brother, not only to introduce them, but occasionally to step down from the platform, and talk about them: if they are good and kindly, to love them and shake them by the hand: if they are silly, to laugh at them confidentially in the reader's sleeve: if they are wicked and heartless, to abuse them in the strongest terms which politeness admits of.\n\nOtherwise you might fancy it was I who was sneering at the practice of devotion, which Miss Sharp finds so ridiculous; that it was I who laughed good-humouredly at the reeling old Silenus of a baronet--whereas the laughter comes from one who has no reverence except for prosperity, and no eye for anything beyond success. Such people there are living and flourishing in the world--Faithless, Hopeless, Charityless: let us have at them, dear friends, with might and main. Some there are, and very successful too, mere quacks and fools: and it was to combat and expose such as those, no doubt, that Laughter was made.\n\nFamily Portraits\nSir Pitt Crawley was a philosopher with a taste for what is called low life. His first marriage with the daughter of the noble Binkie had been made under the auspices of his parents; and as he often told Lady Crawley in her lifetime she was such a confounded quarrelsome high-bred jade that when she died he was hanged if he would ever take another of her sort, at her ladyship's demise he kept his promise, and selected for a second wife Miss Rose Dawson, daughter of Mr. John Thomas Dawson, ironmonger, of Mudbury. What a happy woman was Rose to be my Lady Crawley!\n\nLet us set down the items of her happiness. In the first place, she gave up Peter Butt, a young man who kept company with her, and in consequence of his disappointment in love, took to smuggling, poaching, and a thousand other bad courses. Then she quarrelled, as in duty bound, with all the friends and intimates of her youth, who, of course, could not be received by my Lady at Queen's Crawley--nor did she find in her new rank and abode any persons who were willing to welcome her. Who ever did? Sir Huddleston Fuddleston had three daughters who all hoped to be Lady Crawley. Sir Giles Wapshot's family were insulted that one of the Wapshot girls had not the preference in the marriage, and the remaining baronets of the county were indignant at their comrade's misalliance. Never mind the commoners, whom we will leave to grumble anonymously.\n\nSir Pitt did not care, as he said, a brass farden for any one of them. He had his pretty Rose, and what more need a man require than to please himself? So he used to get drunk every night: to beat his pretty Rose sometimes: to leave her in Hampshire when he went to London for the parliamentary session, without a single friend in the wide world. Even Mrs. Bute Crawley, the Rector's wife, refused to visit her, as she said she would never give the pas to a tradesman's daughter.\n\nAs the only endowments with which Nature had gifted Lady Crawley were those of pink cheeks and a white skin, and as she had no sort of character, nor talents, nor opinions, nor occupations, nor amusements, nor that vigour of soul and ferocity of temper which often falls to the lot of entirely foolish women, her hold upon Sir Pitt's affections was not very great. Her roses faded out of her cheeks, and the pretty freshness left her figure after the birth of a couple of children, and she became a mere machine in her husband's house of no more use than the late Lady Crawley's grand piano. Being a light-complexioned woman, she wore light clothes, as most blondes will, and appeared, in preference, in draggled sea-green, or slatternly sky-blue. She worked that worsted day and night, or other pieces like it. She had counterpanes in the course of a few years to all the beds in Crawley. She had a small flower-garden, for which she had rather an affection; but beyond this no other like or disliking. When her husband was rude to her she was apathetic: whenever he struck her she cried. She had not character enough to take to drinking, and moaned about, slipshod and in curl-papers all day. O Vanity Fair--Vanity Fair! This might have been, but for you, a cheery lass--Peter Butt and Rose a happy man and wife, in a snug farm, with a hearty family; and an honest portion of pleasures, cares, hopes and struggles--but a title and a coach and four are toys more precious than happiness in Vanity Fair: and if Harry the Eighth or Bluebeard were alive now, and wanted a tenth wife, do you suppose he could not get the prettiest girl that shall be presented this season?\n\nThe languid dulness of their mamma did not, as it may be supposed, awaken much affection in her little daughters, but they were very happy in the servants' hall and in the stables; and the Scotch gardener having luckily a good wife and some good children, they got a little wholesome society and instruction in his lodge, which was the only education bestowed upon them until Miss Sharp came.\n\nHer engagement was owing to the remonstrances of Mr. Pitt Crawley, the only friend or protector Lady Crawley ever had, and the only person, besides her children, for whom she entertained a little feeble attachment. Mr. Pitt took after the noble Binkies, from whom he was descended, and was a very polite and proper gentleman. When he grew to man's estate, and came back from Christchurch, he began to reform the slackened discipline of the hall, in spite of his father, who stood in awe of him. He was a man of such rigid refinement, that he would have starved rather than have dined without a white neckcloth. Once, when just from college, and when Horrocks the butler brought him a letter without placing it previously on a tray, he gave that domestic a look, and administered to him a speech so cutting, that Horrocks ever after trembled before him; the whole household bowed to him: Lady Crawley's curl-papers came off earlier when he was at home: Sir Pitt's muddy gaiters disappeared; and if that incorrigible old man still adhered to other old habits, he never fuddled himself with rum-and-water in his son's presence, and only talked to his servants in a very reserved and polite manner; and those persons remarked that Sir Pitt never swore at Lady Crawley while his son was in the room.\n\nIt was he who taught the butler to say, \"My lady is served,\" and who insisted on handing her ladyship in to dinner. He seldom spoke to her, but when he did it was with the most powerful respect; and he never let her quit the apartment without rising in the most stately manner to open the door, and making an elegant bow at her egress.\n\nAt Eton he was called Miss Crawley; and there, I am sorry to say, his younger brother Rawdon used to lick him violently. But though his parts were not brilliant, he made up for his lack of talent by meritorious industry, and was never known, during eight years at school, to be subject to that punishment which it is generally thought none but a cherub can escape.\n\nAt college his career was of course highly creditable. And here he prepared himself for public life, into which he was to be introduced by the patronage of his grandfather, Lord Binkie, by studying the ancient and modern orators with great assiduity, and by speaking unceasingly at the debating societies. But though he had a fine flux of words, and delivered his little voice with great pomposity and pleasure to himself, and never advanced any sentiment or opinion which was not perfectly trite and stale, and supported by a Latin quotation; yet he failed somehow, in spite of a mediocrity which ought to have insured any man a success. He did not even get the prize poem, which all his friends said he was sure of.\n\nAfter leaving college he became Private Secretary to Lord Binkie, and was then appointed Attache to the Legation at Pumpernickel, which post he filled with perfect honour, and brought home despatches, consisting of Strasburg pie, to the Foreign Minister of the day. After remaining ten years Attache (several years after the lamented Lord Binkie's demise), and finding the advancement slow, he at length gave up the diplomatic service in some disgust, and began to turn country gentleman.\n\nHe wrote a pamphlet on Malt on returning to England (for he was an ambitious man, and always liked to be before the public), and took a strong part in the Negro Emancipation question. Then he became a friend of Mr. Wilberforce's, whose politics he admired, and had that famous correspondence with the Reverend Silas Hornblower, on the Ashantee Mission. He was in London, if not for the Parliament session, at least in May, for the religious meetings. In the country he was a magistrate, and an active visitor and speaker among those destitute of religious instruction. He was said to be paying his addresses to Lady Jane Sheepshanks, Lord Southdown's third daughter, and whose sister, Lady Emily, wrote those sweet tracts, \"The Sailor's True Binnacle,\" and \"The Applewoman of Finchley Common.\"\n\nMiss Sharp's accounts of his employment at Queen's Crawley were not caricatures. He subjected the servants there to the devotional exercises before mentioned, in which (and so much the better) he brought his father to join. He patronised an Independent meeting-house in Crawley parish, much to the indignation of his uncle the Rector, and to the consequent delight of Sir Pitt, who was induced to go himself once or twice, which occasioned some violent sermons at Crawley parish church, directed point-blank at the Baronet's old Gothic pew there. Honest Sir Pitt, however, did not feel the force of these discourses, as he always took his nap during sermon-time.\n\nMr. Crawley was very earnest, for the good of the nation and of the Christian world, that the old gentleman should yield him up his place in Parliament; but this the elder constantly refused to do. Both were of course too prudent to give up the fifteen hundred a year which was brought in by the second seat (at this period filled by Mr. Quadroon, with carte blanche on the Slave question); indeed the family estate was much embarrassed, and the income drawn from the borough was of great use to the house of Queen's Crawley.\n\nIt had never recovered the heavy fine imposed upon Walpole Crawley, first baronet, for peculation in the Tape and Sealing Wax Office. Sir Walpole was a jolly fellow, eager to seize and to spend money (alieni appetens, sui profusus, as Mr. Crawley would remark with a sigh), and in his day beloved by all the county for the constant drunkenness and hospitality which was maintained at Queen's Crawley. The cellars were filled with burgundy then, the kennels with hounds, and the stables with gallant hunters; now, such horses as Queen's Crawley possessed went to plough, or ran in the Trafalgar Coach; and it was with a team of these very horses, on an off-day, that Miss Sharp was brought to the Hall; for boor as he was, Sir Pitt was a stickler for his dignity while at home, and seldom drove out but with four horses, and though he dined off boiled mutton, had always three footmen to serve it.\n\nIf mere parsimony could have made a man rich, Sir Pitt Crawley might have become very wealthy--if he had been an attorney in a country town, with no capital but his brains, it is very possible that he would have turned them to good account, and might have achieved for himself a very considerable influence and competency. But he was unluckily endowed with a good name and a large though encumbered estate, both of which went rather to injure than to advance him. He had a taste for law, which cost him many thousands yearly; and being a great deal too clever to be robbed, as he said, by any single agent, allowed his affairs to be mismanaged by a dozen, whom he all equally mistrusted. He was such a sharp landlord, that he could hardly find any but bankrupt tenants; and such a close farmer, as to grudge almost the seed to the ground, whereupon revengeful Nature grudged him the crops which she granted to more liberal husbandmen. He speculated in every possible way; he worked mines; bought canal-shares; horsed coaches; took government contracts, and was the busiest man and magistrate of his county. As he would not pay honest agents at his granite quarry, he had the satisfaction of finding that four overseers ran away, and took fortunes with them to America. For want of proper precautions, his coal-mines filled with water: the government flung his contract of damaged beef upon his hands: and for his coach-horses, every mail proprietor in the kingdom knew that he lost more horses than any man in the country, from underfeeding and buying cheap. In disposition he was sociable, and far from being proud; nay, he rather preferred the society of a farmer or a horse-dealer to that of a gentleman, like my lord, his son: he was fond of drink, of swearing, of joking with the farmers' daughters: he was never known to give away a shilling or to do a good action, but was of a pleasant, sly, laughing mood, and would cut his joke and drink his glass with a tenant and sell him up the next day; or have his laugh with the poacher he was transporting with equal good humour. His politeness for the fair sex has already been hinted at by Miss Rebecca Sharp--in a word, the whole baronetage, peerage, commonage of England, did not contain a more cunning, mean, selfish, foolish, disreputable old man. That blood-red hand of Sir Pitt Crawley's would be in anybody's pocket except his own; and it is with grief and pain, that, as admirers of the British aristocracy, we find ourselves obliged to admit the existence of so many ill qualities in a person whose name is in Debrett.\n\nOne great cause why Mr. Crawley had such a hold over the affections of his father, resulted from money arrangements. The Baronet owed his son a sum of money out of the jointure of his mother, which he did not find it convenient to pay; indeed he had an almost invincible repugnance to paying anybody, and could only be brought by force to discharge his debts. Miss Sharp calculated (for she became, as we shall hear speedily, inducted into most of the secrets of the family) that the mere payment of his creditors cost the honourable Baronet several hundreds yearly; but this was a delight he could not forego; he had a savage pleasure in making the poor wretches wait, and in shifting from court to court and from term to term the period of satisfaction. What's the good of being in Parliament, he said, if you must pay your debts? Hence, indeed, his position as a senator was not a little useful to him.\n\nVanity Fair--Vanity Fair! Here was a man, who could not spell, and did not care to read--who had the habits and the cunning of a boor: whose aim in life was pettifogging: who never had a taste, or emotion, or enjoyment, but what was sordid and foul; and yet he had rank, and honours, and power, somehow: and was a dignitary of the land, and a pillar of the state. He was high sheriff, and rode in a golden coach. Great ministers and statesmen courted him; and in Vanity Fair he had a higher place than the most brilliant genius or spotless virtue.\n\nSir Pitt had an unmarried half-sister who inherited her mother's large fortune, and though the Baronet proposed to borrow this money of her on mortgage, Miss Crawley declined the offer, and preferred the security of the funds. She had signified, however, her intention of leaving her inheritance between Sir Pitt's second son and the family at the Rectory, and had once or twice paid the debts of Rawdon Crawley in his career at college and in the army. Miss Crawley was, in consequence, an object of great respect when she came to Queen's Crawley, for she had a balance at her banker's which would have made her beloved anywhere.\n\nWhat a dignity it gives an old lady, that balance at the banker's! How tenderly we look at her faults if she is a relative (and may every reader have a score of such), what a kind good-natured old creature we find her! How the junior partner of Hobbs and Dobbs leads her smiling to the carriage with the lozenge upon it, and the fat wheezy coachman! How, when she comes to pay us a visit, we generally find an opportunity to let our friends know her station in the world! We say (and with perfect truth) I wish I had Miss MacWhirter's signature to a cheque for five thousand pounds. She wouldn't miss it, says your wife. She is my aunt, say you, in an easy careless way, when your friend asks if Miss MacWhirter is any relative. Your wife is perpetually sending her little testimonies of affection, your little girls work endless worsted baskets, cushions, and footstools for her. What a good fire there is in her room when she comes to pay you a visit, although your wife laces her stays without one! The house during her stay assumes a festive, neat, warm, jovial, snug appearance not visible at other seasons. You yourself, dear sir, forget to go to sleep after dinner, and find yourself all of a sudden (though you invariably lose) very fond of a rubber. What good dinners you have--game every day, Malmsey-Madeira, and no end of fish from London. Even the servants in the kitchen share in the general prosperity; and, somehow, during the stay of Miss MacWhirter's fat coachman, the beer is grown much stronger, and the consumption of tea and sugar in the nursery (where her maid takes her meals) is not regarded in the least. Is it so, or is it not so? I appeal to the middle classes. Ah, gracious powers! I wish you would send me an old aunt--a maiden aunt--an aunt with a lozenge on her carriage, and a front of light coffee-coloured hair--how my children should work workbags for her, and my Julia and I would make her comfortable! Sweet--sweet vision! Foolish--foolish dream!\n\nMiss Sharp Begins to Make Friends\nAnd now, being received as a member of the amiable family whose portraits we have sketched in the foregoing pages, it became naturally Rebecca's duty to make herself, as she said, agreeable to her benefactors, and to gain their confidence to the utmost of her power. Who can but admire this quality of gratitude in an unprotected orphan; and, if there entered some degree of selfishness into her calculations, who can say but that her prudence was perfectly justifiable? \"I am alone in the world,\" said the friendless girl. \"I have nothing to look for but what my own labour can bring me; and while that little pink-faced chit Amelia, with not half my sense, has ten thousand pounds and an establishment secure, poor Rebecca (and my figure is far better than hers) has only herself and her own wits to trust to. Well, let us see if my wits cannot provide me with an honourable maintenance, and if some day or the other I cannot show Miss Amelia my real superiority over her. Not that I dislike poor Amelia: who can dislike such a harmless, good-natured creature?--only it will be a fine day when I can take my place above her in the world, as why, indeed, should I not?\" Thus it was that our little romantic friend formed visions of the future for herself--nor must we be scandalised that, in all her castles in the air, a husband was the principal inhabitant. Of what else have young ladies to think, but husbands? Of what else do their dear mammas think? \"I must be my own mamma,\" said Rebecca; not without a tingling consciousness of defeat, as she thought over her little misadventure with Jos Sedley.\n\nSo she wisely determined to render her position with the Queen's Crawley family comfortable and secure, and to this end resolved to make friends of every one around her who could at all interfere with her comfort.\n\nAs my Lady Crawley was not one of these personages, and a woman, moreover, so indolent and void of character as not to be of the least consequence in her own house, Rebecca soon found that it was not at all necessary to cultivate her good will--indeed, impossible to gain it. She used to talk to her pupils about their \"poor mamma\"; and, though she treated that lady with every demonstration of cool respect, it was to the rest of the family that she wisely directed the chief part of her attentions.\n\nWith the young people, whose applause she thoroughly gained, her method was pretty simple. She did not pester their young brains with too much learning, but, on the contrary, let them have their own way in regard to educating themselves; for what instruction is more effectual than self-instruction? The eldest was rather fond of books, and as there was in the old library at Queen's Crawley a considerable provision of works of light literature of the last century, both in the French and English languages (they had been purchased by the Secretary of the Tape and Sealing Wax Office at the period of his disgrace), and as nobody ever troubled the bookshelves but herself, Rebecca was enabled agreeably, and, as it were, in playing, to impart a great deal of instruction to Miss Rose Crawley.\n\nShe and Miss Rose thus read together many delightful French and English works, among which may be mentioned those of the learned Dr. Smollett, of the ingenious Mr. Henry Fielding, of the graceful and fantastic Monsieur Crebillon the younger, whom our immortal poet Gray so much admired, and of the universal Monsieur de Voltaire. Once, when Mr. Crawley asked what the young people were reading, the governess replied \"Smollett.\" \"Oh, Smollett,\" said Mr. Crawley, quite satisfied. \"His history is more dull, but by no means so dangerous as that of Mr. Hume. It is history you are reading?\" \"Yes,\" said Miss Rose; without, however, adding that it was the history of Mr. Humphrey Clinker. On another occasion he was rather scandalised at finding his sister with a book of French plays; but as the governess remarked that it was for the purpose of acquiring the French idiom in conversation, he was fain to be content. Mr. Crawley, as a diplomatist, was exceedingly proud of his own skill in speaking the French language (for he was of the world still), and not a little pleased with the compliments which the governess continually paid him upon his proficiency.\n\nMiss Violet's tastes were, on the contrary, more rude and boisterous than those of her sister. She knew the sequestered spots where the hens laid their eggs. She could climb a tree to rob the nests of the feathered songsters of their speckled spoils. And her pleasure was to ride the young colts, and to scour the plains like Camilla. She was the favourite of her father and of the stablemen. She was the darling, and withal the terror of the cook; for she discovered the haunts of the jam-pots, and would attack them when they were within her reach. She and her sister were engaged in constant battles. Any of which peccadilloes, if Miss Sharp discovered, she did not tell them to Lady Crawley; who would have told them to the father, or worse, to Mr. Crawley; but promised not to tell if Miss Violet would be a good girl and love her governess.\n\nWith Mr. Crawley Miss Sharp was respectful and obedient. She used to consult him on passages of French which she could not understand, though her mother was a Frenchwoman, and which he would construe to her satisfaction: and, besides giving her his aid in profane literature, he was kind enough to select for her books of a more serious tendency, and address to her much of his conversation. She admired, beyond measure, his speech at the Quashimaboo-Aid Society; took an interest in his pamphlet on malt: was often affected, even to tears, by his discourses of an evening, and would say--\"Oh, thank you, sir,\" with a sigh, and a look up to heaven, that made him occasionally condescend to shake hands with her. \"Blood is everything, after all,\" would that aristocratic religionist say. \"How Miss Sharp is awakened by my words, when not one of the people here is touched. I am too fine for them--too delicate. I must familiarise my style--but she understands it. Her mother was a Montmorency.\"\n\nIndeed it was from this famous family, as it appears, that Miss Sharp, by the mother's side, was descended. Of course she did not say that her mother had been on the stage; it would have shocked Mr. Crawley's religious scruples. How many noble emigres had this horrid revolution plunged in poverty! She had several stories about her ancestors ere she had been many months in the house; some of which Mr. Crawley happened to find in D'Hozier's dictionary, which was in the library, and which strengthened his belief in their truth, and in the high-breeding of Rebecca. Are we to suppose from this curiosity and prying into dictionaries, could our heroine suppose that Mr. Crawley was interested in her?--no, only in a friendly way. Have we not stated that he was attached to Lady Jane Sheepshanks?\n\nHe took Rebecca to task once or twice about the propriety of playing at backgammon with Sir Pitt, saying that it was a godless amusement, and that she would be much better engaged in reading \"Thrump's Legacy,\" or \"The Blind Washerwoman of Moorfields,\" or any work of a more serious nature; but Miss Sharp said her dear mother used often to play the same game with the old Count de Trictrac and the venerable Abbe du Cornet, and so found an excuse for this and other worldly amusements.\n\nBut it was not only by playing at backgammon with the Baronet, that the little governess rendered herself agreeable to her employer. She found many different ways of being useful to him. She read over, with indefatigable patience, all those law papers, with which, before she came to Queen's Crawley, he had promised to entertain her. She volunteered to copy many of his letters, and adroitly altered the spelling of them so as to suit the usages of the present day. She became interested in everything appertaining to the estate, to the farm, the park, the garden, and the stables; and so delightful a companion was she, that the Baronet would seldom take his after-breakfast walk without her (and the children of course), when she would give her advice as to the trees which were to be lopped in the shrubberies, the garden-beds to be dug, the crops which were to be cut, the horses which were to go to cart or plough. Before she had been a year at Queen's Crawley she had quite won the Baronet's confidence; and the conversation at the dinner-table, which before used to be held between him and Mr. Horrocks the butler, was now almost exclusively between Sir Pitt and Miss Sharp. She was almost mistress of the house when Mr. Crawley was absent, but conducted herself in her new and exalted situation with such circumspection and modesty as not to offend the authorities of the kitchen and stable, among whom her behaviour was always exceedingly modest and affable. She was quite a different person from the haughty, shy, dissatisfied little girl whom we have known previously, and this change of temper proved great prudence, a sincere desire of amendment, or at any rate great moral courage on her part. Whether it was the heart which dictated this new system of complaisance and humility adopted by our Rebecca, is to be proved by her after-history. A system of hypocrisy, which lasts through whole years, is one seldom satisfactorily practised by a person of one-and-twenty; however, our readers will recollect, that, though young in years, our heroine was old in life and experience, and we have written to no purpose if they have not discovered that she was a very clever woman.\n\nThe elder and younger son of the house of Crawley were, like the gentleman and lady in the weather-box, never at home together--they hated each other cordially: indeed, Rawdon Crawley, the dragoon, had a great contempt for the establishment altogether, and seldom came thither except when his aunt paid her annual visit.\n\nThe great good quality of this old lady has been mentioned. She possessed seventy thousand pounds, and had almost adopted Rawdon. She disliked her elder nephew exceedingly, and despised him as a milksop. In return he did not hesitate to state that her soul was irretrievably lost, and was of opinion that his brother's chance in the next world was not a whit better. \"She is a godless woman of the world,\" would Mr. Crawley say; \"she lives with atheists and Frenchmen. My mind shudders when I think of her awful, awful situation, and that, near as she is to the grave, she should be so given up to vanity, licentiousness, profaneness, and folly.\" In fact, the old lady declined altogether to hear his hour's lecture of an evening; and when she came to Queen's Crawley alone, he was obliged to pretermit his usual devotional exercises.\n\n\"Shut up your sarmons, Pitt, when Miss Crawley comes down,\" said his father; \"she has written to say that she won't stand the preachifying.\"\n\n\"O, sir! consider the servants.\"\n\n\"The servants be hanged,\" said Sir Pitt; and his son thought even worse would happen were they deprived of the benefit of his instruction.\n\n\"Why, hang it, Pitt!\" said the father to his remonstrance. \"You wouldn't be such a flat as to let three thousand a year go out of the family?\"\n\n\"What is money compared to our souls, sir?\" continued Mr. Crawley.\n\n\"You mean that the old lady won't leave the money to you?\"--and who knows but it was Mr. Crawley's meaning?\n\nOld Miss Crawley was certainly one of the reprobate. She had a snug little house in Park Lane, and, as she ate and drank a great deal too much during the season in London, she went to Harrowgate or Cheltenham for the summer. She was the most hospitable and jovial of old vestals, and had been a beauty in her day, she said. (All old women were beauties once, we very well know.) She was a bel esprit, and a dreadful Radical for those days. She had been in France (where St. Just, they say, inspired her with an unfortunate passion), and loved, ever after, French novels, French cookery, and French wines. She read Voltaire, and had Rousseau by heart; talked very lightly about divorce, and most energetically of the rights of women. She had pictures of Mr. Fox in every room in the house: when that statesman was in opposition, I am not sure that she had not flung a main with him; and when he came into office, she took great credit for bringing over to him Sir Pitt and his colleague for Queen's Crawley, although Sir Pitt would have come over himself, without any trouble on the honest lady's part. It is needless to say that Sir Pitt was brought to change his views after the death of the great Whig statesman.\n\nThis worthy old lady took a fancy to Rawdon Crawley when a boy, sent him to Cambridge (in opposition to his brother at Oxford), and, when the young man was requested by the authorities of the first-named University to quit after a residence of two years, she bought him his commission in the Life Guards Green.\n\nA perfect and celebrated \"blood,\" or dandy about town, was this young officer. Boxing, rat-hunting, the fives court, and four-in-hand driving were then the fashion of our British aristocracy; and he was an adept in all these noble sciences. And though he belonged to the household troops, who, as it was their duty to rally round the Prince Regent, had not shown their valour in foreign service yet, Rawdon Crawley had already (apropos of play, of which he was immoderately fond) fought three bloody duels, in which he gave ample proofs of his contempt for death.\n\n\"And for what follows after death,\" would Mr. Crawley observe, throwing his gooseberry-coloured eyes up to the ceiling. He was always thinking of his brother's soul, or of the souls of those who differed with him in opinion: it is a sort of comfort which many of the serious give themselves.\n\nSilly, romantic Miss Crawley, far from being horrified at the courage of her favourite, always used to pay his debts after his duels; and would not listen to a word that was whispered against his morality. \"He will sow his wild oats,\" she would say, \"and is worth far more than that puling hypocrite of a brother of his.\"\n\nArcadian Simplicity\nBesides these honest folks at the Hall (whose simplicity and sweet rural purity surely show the advantage of a country life over a town one), we must introduce the reader to their relatives and neighbours at the Rectory, Bute Crawley and his wife.\n\nThe Reverend Bute Crawley was a tall, stately, jolly, shovel-hatted man, far more popular in his county than the Baronet his brother. At college he pulled stroke-oar in the Christchurch boat, and had thrashed all the best bruisers of the \"town.\" He carried his taste for boxing and athletic exercises into private life; there was not a fight within twenty miles at which he was not present, nor a race, nor a coursing match, nor a regatta, nor a ball, nor an election, nor a visitation dinner, nor indeed a good dinner in the whole county, but he found means to attend it. You might see his bay mare and gig-lamps a score of miles away from his Rectory House, whenever there was any dinner-party at Fuddleston, or at Roxby, or at Wapshot Hall, or at the great lords of the county, with all of whom he was intimate. He had a fine voice; sang \"A southerly wind and a cloudy sky\"; and gave the \"whoop\" in chorus with general applause. He rode to hounds in a pepper-and-salt frock, and was one of the best fishermen in the county.\n\nMrs. Crawley, the rector's wife, was a smart little body, who wrote this worthy divine's sermons. Being of a domestic turn, and keeping the house a great deal with her daughters, she ruled absolutely within the Rectory, wisely giving her husband full liberty without. He was welcome to come and go, and dine abroad as many days as his fancy dictated, for Mrs. Crawley was a saving woman and knew the price of port wine. Ever since Mrs. Bute carried off the young Rector of Queen's Crawley (she was of a good family, daughter of the late Lieut.-Colonel Hector McTavish, and she and her mother played for Bute and won him at Harrowgate), she had been a prudent and thrifty wife to him. In spite of her care, however, he was always in debt. It took him at least ten years to pay off his college bills contracted during his father's lifetime. In the year 179-, when he was just clear of these incumbrances, he gave the odds of 100 to 1 (in twenties) against Kangaroo, who won the Derby. The Rector was obliged to take up the money at a ruinous interest, and had been struggling ever since. His sister helped him with a hundred now and then, but of course his great hope was in her death--when \"hang it\" (as he would say), \"Matilda must leave me half her money.\"\n\nSo that the Baronet and his brother had every reason which two brothers possibly can have for being by the ears. Sir Pitt had had the better of Bute in innumerable family transactions. Young Pitt not only did not hunt, but set up a meeting house under his uncle's very nose. Rawdon, it was known, was to come in for the bulk of Miss Crawley's property. These money transactions--these speculations in life and death--these silent battles for reversionary spoil--make brothers very loving towards each other in Vanity Fair. I, for my part, have known a five-pound note to interpose and knock up a half century's attachment between two brethren; and can't but admire, as I think what a fine and durable thing Love is among worldly people.\n\nIt cannot be supposed that the arrival of such a personage as Rebecca at Queen's Crawley, and her gradual establishment in the good graces of all people there, could be unremarked by Mrs. Bute Crawley. Mrs. Bute, who knew how many days the sirloin of beef lasted at the Hall; how much linen was got ready at the great wash; how many peaches were on the south wall; how many doses her ladyship took when she was ill--for such points are matters of intense interest to certain persons in the country--Mrs. Bute, I say, could not pass over the Hall governess without making every inquiry respecting her history and character. There was always the best understanding between the servants at the Rectory and the Hall. There was always a good glass of ale in the kitchen of the former place for the Hall people, whose ordinary drink was very small--and, indeed, the Rector's lady knew exactly how much malt went to every barrel of Hall beer--ties of relationship existed between the Hall and Rectory domestics, as between their masters; and through these channels each family was perfectly well acquainted with the doings of the other. That, by the way, may be set down as a general remark. When you and your brother are friends, his doings are indifferent to you. When you have quarrelled, all his outgoings and incomings you know, as if you were his spy.\n\nVery soon then after her arrival, Rebecca began to take a regular place in Mrs. Crawley's bulletin from the Hall. It was to this effect: \"The black porker's killed--weighed x stone--salted the sides--pig's pudding and leg of pork for dinner. Mr. Cramp from Mudbury, over with Sir Pitt about putting John Blackmore in gaol--Mr. Pitt at meeting (with all the names of the people who attended)--my lady as usual--the young ladies with the governess.\"\n\nThen the report would come--the new governess be a rare manager--Sir Pitt be very sweet on her--Mr. Crawley too--He be reading tracts to her--\"What an abandoned wretch!\" said little, eager, active, black-faced Mrs. Bute Crawley.\n\nFinally, the reports were that the governess had \"come round\" everybody, wrote Sir Pitt's letters, did his business, managed his accounts--had the upper hand of the whole house, my lady, Mr. Crawley, the girls and all--at which Mrs. Crawley declared she was an artful hussy, and had some dreadful designs in view. Thus the doings at the Hall were the great food for conversation at the Rectory, and Mrs. Bute's bright eyes spied out everything that took place in the enemy's camp--everything and a great deal besides.\n\nMrs. Bute Crawley to Miss Pinkerton, The Mall, Chiswick.\n\nRectory, Queen's Crawley, December--.\n\nMy Dear Madam,--Although it is so many years since I profited by your delightful and invaluable instructions, yet I have ever retained the FONDEST and most reverential regard for Miss Pinkerton, and DEAR Chiswick. I hope your health is GOOD. The world and the cause of education cannot afford to lose Miss Pinkerton for MANY MANY YEARS. When my friend, Lady Fuddleston, mentioned that her dear girls required an instructress (I am too poor to engage a governess for mine, but was I not educated at Chiswick?)--\"Who,\" I exclaimed, \"can we consult but the excellent, the incomparable Miss Pinkerton?\" In a word, have you, dear madam, any ladies on your list, whose services might be made available to my kind friend and neighbour? I assure you she will take no governess BUT OF YOUR CHOOSING.\n\nMy dear husband is pleased to say that he likes EVERYTHING WHICH COMES FROM MISS PINKERTON'S SCHOOL. How I wish I could present him and my beloved girls to the friend of my youth, and the ADMIRED of the great lexicographer of our country! If you ever travel into Hampshire, Mr. Crawley begs me to say, he hopes you will adorn our RURAL RECTORY with your presence. 'Tis the humble but happy home of\n\nYour affectionate Martha Crawley\n\nP.S. Mr. Crawley's brother, the baronet, with whom we are not, alas! upon those terms of UNITY in which it BECOMES BRETHREN TO DWELL, has a governess for his little girls, who, I am told, had the good fortune to be educated at Chiswick. I hear various reports of her; and as I have the tenderest interest in my dearest little nieces, whom I wish, in spite of family differences, to see among my own children--and as I long to be attentive to ANY PUPIL OF YOURS--do, my dear Miss Pinkerton, tell me the history of this young lady, whom, for YOUR SAKE, I am most anxious to befriend.--M. C.\n\nMiss Pinkerton to Mrs. Bute Crawley.\n\nJohnson House, Chiswick, Dec. 18--.\n\nDear Madam,--I have the honour to acknowledge your polite communication, to which I promptly reply. 'Tis most gratifying to one in my most arduous position to find that my maternal cares have elicited a responsive affection; and to recognize in the amiable Mrs. Bute Crawley my excellent pupil of former years, the sprightly and accomplished Miss Martha MacTavish. I am happy to have under my charge now the daughters of many of those who were your contemporaries at my establishment--what pleasure it would give me if your own beloved young ladies had need of my instructive superintendence!\n\nPresenting my respectful compliments to Lady Fuddleston, I have the honour (epistolarily) to introduce to her ladyship my two friends, Miss Tuffin and Miss Hawky.\n\nEither of these young ladies is PERFECTLY QUALIFIED to instruct in Greek, Latin, and the rudiments of Hebrew; in mathematics and history; in Spanish, French, Italian, and geography; in music, vocal and instrumental; in dancing, without the aid of a master; and in the elements of natural sciences. In the use of the globes both are proficients. In addition to these Miss Tuffin, who is daughter of the late Reverend Thomas Tuffin (Fellow of Corpus College, Cambridge), can instruct in the Syriac language, and the elements of Constitutional law. But as she is only eighteen years of age, and of exceedingly pleasing personal appearance, perhaps this young lady may be objectionable in Sir Huddleston Fuddleston's family.\n\nMiss Letitia Hawky, on the other hand, is not personally well-favoured. She is twenty-nine; her face is much pitted with the small-pox. She has a halt in her gait, red hair, and a trifling obliquity of vision. Both ladies are endowed with EVERY MORAL AND RELIGIOUS VIRTUE. Their terms, of course, are such as their accomplishments merit. With my most grateful respects to the Reverend Bute Crawley, I have the honour to be,\n\nDear Madam,\n\nYour most faithful and obedient servant, Barbara Pinkerton.\n\nP.S. The Miss Sharp, whom you mention as governess to Sir Pitt Crawley, Bart., M.P., was a pupil of mine, and I have nothing to say in her disfavour. Though her appearance is disagreeable, we cannot control the operations of nature: and though her parents were disreputable (her father being a painter, several times bankrupt, and her mother, as I have since learned, with horror, a dancer at the Opera); yet her talents are considerable, and I cannot regret that I received her OUT OF CHARITY. My dread is, lest the principles of the mother--who was represented to me as a French Countess, forced to emigrate in the late revolutionary horrors; but who, as I have since found, was a person of the very lowest order and morals--should at any time prove to be HEREDITARY in the unhappy young woman whom I took as AN OUTCAST. But her principles have hitherto been correct (I believe), and I am sure nothing will occur to injure them in the elegant and refined circle of the eminent Sir Pitt Crawley.\n\nMiss Rebecca Sharp to Miss Amelia Sedley.\n\nI have not written to my beloved Amelia for these many weeks past, for what news was there to tell of the sayings and doings at Humdrum Hall, as I have christened it; and what do you care whether the turnip crop is good or bad; whether the fat pig weighed thirteen stone or fourteen; and whether the beasts thrive well upon mangelwurzel? Every day since I last wrote has been like its neighbour. Before breakfast, a walk with Sir Pitt and his spud; after breakfast studies (such as they are) in the schoolroom; after schoolroom, reading and writing about lawyers, leases, coal-mines, canals, with Sir Pitt (whose secretary I am become); after dinner, Mr. Crawley's discourses on the baronet's backgammon; during both of which amusements my lady looks on with equal placidity. She has become rather more interesting by being ailing of late, which has brought a new visitor to the Hall, in the person of a young doctor. Well, my dear, young women need never despair. The young doctor gave a certain friend of yours to understand that, if she chose to be Mrs. Glauber, she was welcome to ornament the surgery! I told his impudence that the gilt pestle and mortar was quite ornament enough; as if I was born, indeed, to be a country surgeon's wife! Mr. Glauber went home seriously indisposed at his rebuff, took a cooling draught, and is now quite cured. Sir Pitt applauded my resolution highly; he would be sorry to lose his little secretary, I think; and I believe the old wretch likes me as much as it is in his nature to like any one. Marry, indeed! and with a country apothecary, after-- No, no, one cannot so soon forget old associations, about which I will talk no more. Let us return to Humdrum Hall.\n\nFor some time past it is Humdrum Hall no longer. My dear, Miss Crawley has arrived with her fat horses, fat servants, fat spaniel--the great rich Miss Crawley, with seventy thousand pounds in the five per cents., whom, or I had better say WHICH, her two brothers adore. She looks very apoplectic, the dear soul; no wonder her brothers are anxious about her. You should see them struggling to settle her cushions, or to hand her coffee! \"When I come into the country,\" she says (for she has a great deal of humour), \"I leave my toady, Miss Briggs, at home. My brothers are my toadies here, my dear, and a pretty pair they are!\"\n\nWhen she comes into the country our hall is thrown open, and for a month, at least, you would fancy old Sir Walpole was come to life again. We have dinner-parties, and drive out in the coach-and-four--the footmen put on their newest canary-coloured liveries; we drink claret and champagne as if we were accustomed to it every day. We have wax candles in the schoolroom, and fires to warm ourselves with. Lady Crawley is made to put on the brightest pea-green in her wardrobe, and my pupils leave off their thick shoes and tight old tartan pelisses, and wear silk stockings and muslin frocks, as fashionable baronets' daughters should. Rose came in yesterday in a sad plight--the Wiltshire sow (an enormous pet of hers) ran her down, and destroyed a most lovely flowered lilac silk dress by dancing over it--had this happened a week ago, Sir Pitt would have sworn frightfully, have boxed the poor wretch's ears, and put her upon bread and water for a month. All he said was, \"I'll serve you out, Miss, when your aunt's gone,\" and laughed off the accident as quite trivial. Let us hope his wrath will have passed away before Miss Crawley's departure. I hope so, for Miss Rose's sake, I am sure. What a charming reconciler and peacemaker money is!\n\nAnother admirable effect of Miss Crawley and her seventy thousand pounds is to be seen in the conduct of the two brothers Crawley. I mean the baronet and the rector, not OUR brothers--but the former, who hate each other all the year round, become quite loving at Christmas. I wrote to you last year how the abominable horse-racing rector was in the habit of preaching clumsy sermons at us at church, and how Sir Pitt snored in answer. When Miss Crawley arrives there is no such thing as quarrelling heard of--the Hall visits the Rectory, and vice versa--the parson and the Baronet talk about the pigs and the poachers, and the county business, in the most affable manner, and without quarrelling in their cups, I believe--indeed Miss Crawley won't hear of their quarrelling, and vows that she will leave her money to the Shropshire Crawleys if they offend her. If they were clever people, those Shropshire Crawleys, they might have it all, I think; but the Shropshire Crawley is a clergyman like his Hampshire cousin, and mortally offended Miss Crawley (who had fled thither in a fit of rage against her impracticable brethren) by some strait-laced notions of morality. He would have prayers in the house, I believe.\n\nOur sermon books are shut up when Miss Crawley arrives, and Mr. Pitt, whom she abominates, finds it convenient to go to town. On the other hand, the young dandy--\"blood,\" I believe, is the term--Captain Crawley makes his appearance, and I suppose you will like to know what sort of a person he is.\n\nWell, he is a very large young dandy. He is six feet high, and speaks with a great voice; and swears a great deal; and orders about the servants, who all adore him nevertheless; for he is very generous of his money, and the domestics will do anything for him. Last week the keepers almost killed a bailiff and his man who came down from London to arrest the Captain, and who were found lurking about the Park wall--they beat them, ducked them, and were going to shoot them for poachers, but the baronet interfered.\n\nThe Captain has a hearty contempt for his father, I can see, and calls him an old PUT, an old SNOB, an old CHAW-BACON, and numberless other pretty names. He has a DREADFUL REPUTATION among the ladies. He brings his hunters home with him, lives with the Squires of the county, asks whom he pleases to dinner, and Sir Pitt dares not say no, for fear of offending Miss Crawley, and missing his legacy when she dies of her apoplexy. Shall I tell you a compliment the Captain paid me? I must, it is so pretty. One evening we actually had a dance; there was Sir Huddleston Fuddleston and his family, Sir Giles Wapshot and his young ladies, and I don't know how many more. Well, I heard him say--\"By Jove, she's a neat little filly!\" meaning your humble servant; and he did me the honour to dance two country-dances with me. He gets on pretty gaily with the young Squires, with whom he drinks, bets, rides, and talks about hunting and shooting; but he says the country girls are BORES; indeed, I don't think he is far wrong. You should see the contempt with which they look down on poor me! When they dance I sit and play the piano very demurely; but the other night, coming in rather flushed from the dining-room, and seeing me employed in this way, he swore out loud that I was the best dancer in the room, and took a great oath that he would have the fiddlers from Mudbury.\n\n\"I'll go and play a country-dance,\" said Mrs. Bute Crawley, very readily (she is a little, black-faced old woman in a turban, rather crooked, and with very twinkling eyes); and after the Captain and your poor little Rebecca had performed a dance together, do you know she actually did me the honour to compliment me upon my steps! Such a thing was never heard of before; the proud Mrs. Bute Crawley, first cousin to the Earl of Tiptoff, who won't condescend to visit Lady Crawley, except when her sister is in the country. Poor Lady Crawley! during most part of these gaieties, she is upstairs taking pills.\n\nMrs. Bute has all of a sudden taken a great fancy to me. \"My dear Miss Sharp,\" she says, \"why not bring over your girls to the Rectory?--their cousins will be so happy to see them.\" I know what she means. Signor Clementi did not teach us the piano for nothing; at which price Mrs. Bute hopes to get a professor for her children. I can see through her schemes, as though she told them to me; but I shall go, as I am determined to make myself agreeable--is it not a poor governess's duty, who has not a friend or protector in the world? The Rector's wife paid me a score of compliments about the progress my pupils made, and thought, no doubt, to touch my heart--poor, simple, country soul!--as if I cared a fig about my pupils!\n\nYour India muslin and your pink silk, dearest Amelia, are said to become me very well. They are a good deal worn now; but, you know, we poor girls can't afford des fraiches toilettes. Happy, happy you! who have but to drive to St. James's Street, and a dear mother who will give you any thing you ask. Farewell, dearest girl,\n\nYour affectionate Rebecca.\n\nP.S.--I wish you could have seen the faces of the Miss Blackbrooks (Admiral Blackbrook's daughters, my dear), fine young ladies, with dresses from London, when Captain Rawdon selected poor me for a partner!\n\nWhen Mrs. Bute Crawley (whose artifices our ingenious Rebecca had so soon discovered) had procured from Miss Sharp the promise of a visit, she induced the all-powerful Miss Crawley to make the necessary application to Sir Pitt, and the good-natured old lady, who loved to be gay herself, and to see every one gay and happy round about her, was quite charmed, and ready to establish a reconciliation and intimacy between her two brothers. It was therefore agreed that the young people of both families should visit each other frequently for the future, and the friendship of course lasted as long as the jovial old mediatrix was there to keep the peace.\n\n\"Why did you ask that scoundrel, Rawdon Crawley, to dine?\" said the Rector to his lady, as they were walking home through the park. \"I don't want the fellow. He looks down upon us country people as so many blackamoors. He's never content unless he gets my yellow-sealed wine, which costs me ten shillings a bottle, hang him! Besides, he's such an infernal character--he's a gambler--he's a drunkard--he's a profligate in every way. He shot a man in a duel--he's over head and ears in debt, and he's robbed me and mine of the best part of Miss Crawley's fortune. Waxy says she has him\"--here the Rector shook his fist at the moon, with something very like an oath, and added, in a melancholious tone, \"--down in her will for fifty thousand; and there won't be above thirty to divide.\"\n\n\"I think she's going,\" said the Rector's wife. \"She was very red in the face when we left dinner. I was obliged to unlace her.\"\n\n\"She drank seven glasses of champagne,\" said the reverend gentleman, in a low voice; \"and filthy champagne it is, too, that my brother poisons us with--but you women never know what's what.\"\n\n\"We know nothing,\" said Mrs. Bute Crawley.\n\n\"She drank cherry-brandy after dinner,\" continued his Reverence, \"and took curacao with her coffee. I wouldn't take a glass for a five-pound note: it kills me with heartburn. She can't stand it, Mrs. Crawley--she must go--flesh and blood won't bear it! and I lay five to two, Matilda drops in a year.\"\n\nIndulging in these solemn speculations, and thinking about his debts, and his son Jim at College, and Frank at Woolwich, and the four girls, who were no beauties, poor things, and would not have a penny but what they got from the aunt's expected legacy, the Rector and his lady walked on for a while.\n\n\"Pitt can't be such an infernal villain as to sell the reversion of the living. And that Methodist milksop of an eldest son looks to Parliament,\" continued Mr. Crawley, after a pause.\n\n\"Sir Pitt Crawley will do anything,\" said the Rector's wife. \"We must get Miss Crawley to make him promise it to James.\"\n\n\"Pitt will promise anything,\" replied the brother. \"He promised he'd pay my college bills, when my father died; he promised he'd build the new wing to the Rectory; he promised he'd let me have Jibb's field and the Six-acre Meadow--and much he executed his promises! And it's to this man's son--this scoundrel, gambler, swindler, murderer of a Rawdon Crawley, that Matilda leaves the bulk of her money. I say it's un-Christian. By Jove, it is. The infamous dog has got every vice except hypocrisy, and that belongs to his brother.\"\n\n\"Hush, my dearest love! we're in Sir Pitt's grounds,\" interposed his wife.\n\n\"I say he has got every vice, Mrs. Crawley. Don't Ma'am, bully me. Didn't he shoot Captain Marker? Didn't he rob young Lord Dovedale at the Cocoa-Tree? Didn't he cross the fight between Bill Soames and the Cheshire Trump, by which I lost forty pound? You know he did; and as for the women, why, you heard that before me, in my own magistrate's room.\"\n\n\"For heaven's sake, Mr. Crawley,\" said the lady, \"spare me the details.\"\n\n\"And you ask this villain into your house!\" continued the exasperated Rector. \"You, the mother of a young family--the wife of a clergyman of the Church of England. By Jove!\"\n\n\"Bute Crawley, you are a fool,\" said the Rector's wife scornfully.\n\n\"Well, Ma'am, fool or not--and I don't say, Martha, I'm so clever as you are, I never did. But I won't meet Rawdon Crawley, that's flat. I'll go over to Huddleston, that I will, and see his black greyhound, Mrs. Crawley; and I'll run Lancelot against him for fifty. By Jove, I will; or against any dog in England. But I won't meet that beast Rawdon Crawley.\"\n\n\"Mr. Crawley, you are intoxicated, as usual,\" replied his wife. And the next morning, when the Rector woke, and called for small beer, she put him in mind of his promise to visit Sir Huddleston Fuddleston on Saturday, and as he knew he should have a wet night, it was agreed that he might gallop back again in time for church on Sunday morning. Thus it will be seen that the parishioners of Crawley were equally happy in their Squire and in their Rector.\n\nMiss Crawley had not long been established at the Hall before Rebecca's fascinations had won the heart of that good-natured London rake, as they had of the country innocents whom we have been describing. Taking her accustomed drive, one day, she thought fit to order that \"that little governess\" should accompany her to Mudbury. Before they had returned Rebecca had made a conquest of her; having made her laugh four times, and amused her during the whole of the little journey.\n\n\"Not let Miss Sharp dine at table!\" said she to Sir Pitt, who had arranged a dinner of ceremony, and asked all the neighbouring baronets. \"My dear creature, do you suppose I can talk about the nursery with Lady Fuddleston, or discuss justices' business with that goose, old Sir Giles Wapshot? I insist upon Miss Sharp appearing. Let Lady Crawley remain upstairs, if there is no room. But little Miss Sharp! Why, she's the only person fit to talk to in the county!\"\n\nOf course, after such a peremptory order as this, Miss Sharp, the governess, received commands to dine with the illustrious company below stairs. And when Sir Huddleston had, with great pomp and ceremony, handed Miss Crawley in to dinner, and was preparing to take his place by her side, the old lady cried out, in a shrill voice, \"Becky Sharp! Miss Sharp! Come you and sit by me and amuse me; and let Sir Huddleston sit by Lady Wapshot.\"\n\nWhen the parties were over, and the carriages had rolled away, the insatiable Miss Crawley would say, \"Come to my dressing room, Becky, and let us abuse the company\"--which, between them, this pair of friends did perfectly. Old Sir Huddleston wheezed a great deal at dinner; Sir Giles Wapshot had a particularly noisy manner of imbibing his soup, and her ladyship a wink of the left eye; all of which Becky caricatured to admiration; as well as the particulars of the night's conversation; the politics; the war; the quarter-sessions; the famous run with the H.H., and those heavy and dreary themes, about which country gentlemen converse. As for the Misses Wapshot's toilettes and Lady Fuddleston's famous yellow hat, Miss Sharp tore them to tatters, to the infinite amusement of her audience.\n\n\"My dear, you are a perfect trouvaille,\" Miss Crawley would say. \"I wish you could come to me in London, but I couldn't make a butt of you as I do of poor Briggs no, no, you little sly creature; you are too clever--Isn't she, Firkin?\"\n\nMrs. Firkin (who was dressing the very small remnant of hair which remained on Miss Crawley's pate), flung up her head and said, \"I think Miss is very clever,\" with the most killing sarcastic air. In fact, Mrs. Firkin had that natural jealousy which is one of the main principles of every honest woman.\n\nAfter rebuffing Sir Huddleston Fuddleston, Miss Crawley ordered that Rawdon Crawley should lead her in to dinner every day, and that Becky should follow with her cushion--or else she would have Becky's arm and Rawdon with the pillow. \"We must sit together,\" she said. \"We're the only three Christians in the county, my love\"--in which case, it must be confessed, that religion was at a very low ebb in the county of Hants.\n\nBesides being such a fine religionist, Miss Crawley was, as we have said, an Ultra-liberal in opinions, and always took occasion to express these in the most candid manner.\n\n\"What is birth, my dear!\" she would say to Rebecca--\"Look at my brother Pitt; look at the Huddlestons, who have been here since Henry II; look at poor Bute at the parsonage--is any one of them equal to you in intelligence or breeding? Equal to you--they are not even equal to poor dear Briggs, my companion, or Bowls, my butler. You, my love, are a little paragon--positively a little jewel--You have more brains than half the shire--if merit had its reward you ought to be a Duchess--no, there ought to be no duchesses at all--but you ought to have no superior, and I consider you, my love, as my equal in every respect; and--will you put some coals on the fire, my dear; and will you pick this dress of mine, and alter it, you who can do it so well?\" So this old philanthropist used to make her equal run of her errands, execute her millinery, and read her to sleep with French novels, every night.\n\nAt this time, as some old readers may recollect, the genteel world had been thrown into a considerable state of excitement by two events, which, as the papers say, might give employment to the gentlemen of the long robe. Ensign Shafton had run away with Lady Barbara Fitzurse, the Earl of Bruin's daughter and heiress; and poor Vere Vane, a gentleman who, up to forty, had maintained a most respectable character and reared a numerous family, suddenly and outrageously left his home, for the sake of Mrs. Rougemont, the actress, who was sixty-five years of age.\n\n\"That was the most beautiful part of dear Lord Nelson's character,\" Miss Crawley said. \"He went to the deuce for a woman. There must be good in a man who will do that. I adore all imprudent matches.-- What I like best, is for a nobleman to marry a miller's daughter, as Lord Flowerdale did--it makes all the women so angry--I wish some great man would run away with you, my dear; I'm sure you're pretty enough.\"\n\n\"Two post-boys!--Oh, it would be delightful!\" Rebecca owned.\n\n\"And what I like next best, is for a poor fellow to run away with a rich girl. I have set my heart on Rawdon running away with some one.\"\n\n\"A rich some one, or a poor some one?\"\n\n\"Why, you goose! Rawdon has not a shilling but what I give him. He is crible de dettes--he must repair his fortunes, and succeed in the world.\"\n\n\"Is he very clever?\" Rebecca asked.\n\n\"Clever, my love?--not an idea in the world beyond his horses, and his regiment, and his hunting, and his play; but he must succeed--he's so delightfully wicked. Don't you know he has hit a man, and shot an injured father through the hat only? He's adored in his regiment; and all the young men at Wattier's and the Cocoa-Tree swear by him.\"\n\nWhen Miss Rebecca Sharp wrote to her beloved friend the account of the little ball at Queen's Crawley, and the manner in which, for the first time, Captain Crawley had distinguished her, she did not, strange to relate, give an altogether accurate account of the transaction. The Captain had distinguished her a great number of times before. The Captain had met her in a half-score of walks. The Captain had lighted upon her in a half-hundred of corridors and passages. The Captain had hung over her piano twenty times of an evening (my Lady was now upstairs, being ill, and nobody heeded her) as Miss Sharp sang. The Captain had written her notes (the best that the great blundering dragoon could devise and spell; but dulness gets on as well as any other quality with women). But when he put the first of the notes into the leaves of the song she was singing, the little governess, rising and looking him steadily in the face, took up the triangular missive daintily, and waved it about as if it were a cocked hat, and she, advancing to the enemy, popped the note into the fire, and made him a very low curtsey, and went back to her place, and began to sing away again more merrily than ever.\n\n\"What's that?\" said Miss Crawley, interrupted in her after-dinner doze by the stoppage of the music.\n\n\"It's a false note,\" Miss Sharp said with a laugh; and Rawdon Crawley fumed with rage and mortification.\n\nSeeing the evident partiality of Miss Crawley for the new governess, how good it was of Mrs. Bute Crawley not to be jealous, and to welcome the young lady to the Rectory, and not only her, but Rawdon Crawley, her husband's rival in the Old Maid's five per cents! They became very fond of each other's society, Mrs. Crawley and her nephew. He gave up hunting; he declined entertainments at Fuddleston: he would not dine with the mess of the depot at Mudbury: his great pleasure was to stroll over to Crawley parsonage--whither Miss Crawley came too; and as their mamma was ill, why not the children with Miss Sharp? So the children (little dears!) came with Miss Sharp; and of an evening some of the party would walk back together. Not Miss Crawley--she preferred her carriage--but the walk over the Rectory fields, and in at the little park wicket, and through the dark plantation, and up the checkered avenue to Queen's Crawley, was charming in the moonlight to two such lovers of the picturesque as the Captain and Miss Rebecca.\n\n\"O those stars, those stars!\" Miss Rebecca would say, turning her twinkling green eyes up towards them. \"I feel myself almost a spirit when I gaze upon them.\"\n\n\"O--ah--Gad--yes, so do I exactly, Miss Sharp,\" the other enthusiast replied. \"You don't mind my cigar, do you, Miss Sharp?\" Miss Sharp loved the smell of a cigar out of doors beyond everything in the world--and she just tasted one too, in the prettiest way possible, and gave a little puff, and a little scream, and a little giggle, and restored the delicacy to the Captain, who twirled his moustache, and straightway puffed it into a blaze that glowed quite red in the dark plantation, and swore--\"Jove--aw--Gad--aw--it's the finest segaw I ever smoked in the world aw,\" for his intellect and conversation were alike brilliant and becoming to a heavy young dragoon.\n\nOld Sir Pitt, who was taking his pipe and beer, and talking to John Horrocks about a \"ship\" that was to be killed, espied the pair so occupied from his study-window, and with dreadful oaths swore that if it wasn't for Miss Crawley, he'd take Rawdon and bundle un out of doors, like a rogue as he was.\n\n\"He be a bad'n, sure enough,\" Mr. Horrocks remarked; \"and his man Flethers is wuss, and have made such a row in the housekeeper's room about the dinners and hale, as no lord would make--but I think Miss Sharp's a match for'n, Sir Pitt,\" he added, after a pause.\n\nAnd so, in truth, she was--for father and son too.\n\nQuite a Sentimental Chapter\nWe must now take leave of Arcadia, and those amiable people practising the rural virtues there, and travel back to London, to inquire what has become of Miss Amelia.\n\n\"We don't care a fig for her,\" writes some unknown correspondent with a pretty little handwriting and a pink seal to her note. \"She is fade and insipid,\" and adds some more kind remarks in this strain, which I should never have repeated at all, but that they are in truth prodigiously complimentary to the young lady whom they concern.\n\nHas the beloved reader, in his experience of society, never heard similar remarks by good-natured female friends; who always wonder what you CAN see in Miss Smith that is so fascinating; or what COULD induce Major Jones to propose for that silly insignificant simpering Miss Thompson, who has nothing but her wax-doll face to recommend her? What is there in a pair of pink cheeks and blue eyes forsooth? these dear Moralists ask, and hint wisely that the gifts of genius, the accomplishments of the mind, the mastery of Mangnall's Questions, and a ladylike knowledge of botany and geology, the knack of making poetry, the power of rattling sonatas in the Herz-manner, and so forth, are far more valuable endowments for a female, than those fugitive charms which a few years will inevitably tarnish. It is quite edifying to hear women speculate upon the worthlessness and the duration of beauty.\n\nBut though virtue is a much finer thing, and those hapless creatures who suffer under the misfortune of good looks ought to be continually put in mind of the fate which awaits them; and though, very likely, the heroic female character which ladies admire is a more glorious and beautiful object than the kind, fresh, smiling, artless, tender little domestic goddess, whom men are inclined to worship--yet the latter and inferior sort of women must have this consolation--that the men do admire them after all; and that, in spite of all our kind friends' warnings and protests, we go on in our desperate error and folly, and shall to the end of the chapter. Indeed, for my own part, though I have been repeatedly told by persons for whom I have the greatest respect, that Miss Brown is an insignificant chit, and Mrs. White has nothing but her petit minois chiffonne, and Mrs. Black has not a word to say for herself; yet I know that I have had the most delightful conversations with Mrs. Black (of course, my dear Madam, they are inviolable): I see all the men in a cluster round Mrs. White's chair: all the young fellows battling to dance with Miss Brown; and so I am tempted to think that to be despised by her sex is a very great compliment to a woman.\n\nThe young ladies in Amelia's society did this for her very satisfactorily. For instance, there was scarcely any point upon which the Misses Osborne, George's sisters, and the Mesdemoiselles Dobbin agreed so well as in their estimate of her very trifling merits: and their wonder that their brothers could find any charms in her. \"We are kind to her,\" the Misses Osborne said, a pair of fine black-browed young ladies who had had the best of governesses, masters, and milliners; and they treated her with such extreme kindness and condescension, and patronised her so insufferably, that the poor little thing was in fact perfectly dumb in their presence, and to all outward appearance as stupid as they thought her. She made efforts to like them, as in duty bound, and as sisters of her future husband. She passed \"long mornings\" with them--the most dreary and serious of forenoons. She drove out solemnly in their great family coach with them, and Miss Wirt their governess, that raw-boned Vestal. They took her to the ancient concerts by way of a treat, and to the oratorio, and to St. Paul's to see the charity children, where in such terror was she of her friends, she almost did not dare be affected by the hymn the children sang. Their house was comfortable; their papa's table rich and handsome; their society solemn and genteel; their self-respect prodigious; they had the best pew at the Foundling: all their habits were pompous and orderly, and all their amusements intolerably dull and decorous. After every one of her visits (and oh how glad she was when they were over!) Miss Osborne and Miss Maria Osborne, and Miss Wirt, the vestal governess, asked each other with increased wonder, \"What could George find in that creature?\"\n\nHow is this? some carping reader exclaims. How is it that Amelia, who had such a number of friends at school, and was so beloved there, comes out into the world and is spurned by her discriminating sex? My dear sir, there were no men at Miss Pinkerton's establishment except the old dancing-master; and you would not have had the girls fall out about HIM? When George, their handsome brother, ran off directly after breakfast, and dined from home half-a-dozen times a week, no wonder the neglected sisters felt a little vexation. When young Bullock (of the firm of Hulker, Bullock & Co., Bankers, Lombard Street), who had been making up to Miss Maria the last two seasons, actually asked Amelia to dance the cotillon, could you expect that the former young lady should be pleased? And yet she said she was, like an artless forgiving creature. \"I'm so delighted you like dear Amelia,\" she said quite eagerly to Mr. Bullock after the dance. \"She's engaged to my brother George; there's not much in her, but she's the best-natured and most unaffected young creature: at home we're all so fond of her.\" Dear girl! who can calculate the depth of affection expressed in that enthusiastic SO?\n\nMiss Wirt and these two affectionate young women so earnestly and frequently impressed upon George Osborne's mind the enormity of the sacrifice he was making, and his romantic generosity in throwing himself away upon Amelia, that I'm not sure but that he really thought he was one of the most deserving characters in the British army, and gave himself up to be loved with a good deal of easy resignation.\n\nSomehow, although he left home every morning, as was stated, and dined abroad six days in the week, when his sisters believed the infatuated youth to be at Miss Sedley's apron-strings: he was NOT always with Amelia, whilst the world supposed him at her feet. Certain it is that on more occasions than one, when Captain Dobbin called to look for his friend, Miss Osborne (who was very attentive to the Captain, and anxious to hear his military stories, and to know about the health of his dear Mamma), would laughingly point to the opposite side of the square, and say, \"Oh, you must go to the Sedleys' to ask for George; WE never see him from morning till night.\" At which kind of speech the Captain would laugh in rather an absurd constrained manner, and turn off the conversation, like a consummate man of the world, to some topic of general interest, such as the Opera, the Prince's last ball at Carlton House, or the weather--that blessing to society.\n\n\"What an innocent it is, that pet of yours,\" Miss Maria would then say to Miss Jane, upon the Captain's departure. \"Did you see how he blushed at the mention of poor George on duty?\"\n\n\"It's a pity Frederick Bullock hadn't some of his modesty, Maria,\" replies the elder sister, with a toss of he head.\n\n\"Modesty! Awkwardness you mean, Jane. I don't want Frederick to trample a hole in my muslin frock, as Captain Dobbin did in yours at Mrs. Perkins'.\"\n\n\"In YOUR frock, he, he! How could he? Wasn't he dancing with Amelia?\"\n\nThe fact is, when Captain Dobbin blushed so, and looked so awkward, he remembered a circumstance of which he did not think it was necessary to inform the young ladies, viz., that he had been calling at Mr. Sedley's house already, on the pretence of seeing George, of course, and George wasn't there, only poor little Amelia, with rather a sad wistful face, seated near the drawing-room window, who, after some very trifling stupid talk, ventured to ask, was there any truth in the report that the regiment was soon to be ordered abroad; and had Captain Dobbin seen Mr. Osborne that day?\n\nThe regiment was not ordered abroad as yet; and Captain Dobbin had not seen George. \"He was with his sister, most likely,\" the Captain said. \"Should he go and fetch the truant?\" So she gave him her hand kindly and gratefully: and he crossed the square; and she waited and waited, but George never came.\n\nPoor little tender heart! and so it goes on hoping and beating, and longing and trusting. You see it is not much of a life to describe. There is not much of what you call incident in it. Only one feeling all day--when will he come? only one thought to sleep and wake upon. I believe George was playing billiards with Captain Cannon in Swallow Street at the time when Amelia was asking Captain Dobbin about him; for George was a jolly sociable fellow, and excellent in all games of skill.\n\nOnce, after three days of absence, Miss Amelia put on her bonnet, and actually invaded the Osborne house. \"What! leave our brother to come to us?\" said the young ladies. \"Have you had a quarrel, Amelia? Do tell us!\" No, indeed, there had been no quarrel. \"Who could quarrel with him?\" says she, with her eyes filled with tears. She only came over to--to see her dear friends; they had not met for so long. And this day she was so perfectly stupid and awkward, that the Misses Osborne and their governess, who stared after her as she went sadly away, wondered more than ever what George could see in poor little Amelia.\n\nOf course they did. How was she to bare that timid little heart for the inspection of those young ladies with their bold black eyes? It was best that it should shrink and hide itself. I know the Misses Osborne were excellent critics of a Cashmere shawl, or a pink satin slip; and when Miss Turner had hers dyed purple, and made into a spencer; and when Miss Pickford had her ermine tippet twisted into a muff and trimmings, I warrant you the changes did not escape the two intelligent young women before mentioned. But there are things, look you, of a finer texture than fur or satin, and all Solomon's glories, and all the wardrobe of the Queen of Sheba--things whereof the beauty escapes the eyes of many connoisseurs. And there are sweet modest little souls on which you light, fragrant and blooming tenderly in quiet shady places; and there are garden-ornaments, as big as brass warming-pans, that are fit to stare the sun itself out of countenance. Miss Sedley was not of the sunflower sort; and I say it is out of the rules of all proportion to draw a violet of the size of a double dahlia.\n\nNo, indeed; the life of a good young girl who is in the paternal nest as yet, can't have many of those thrilling incidents to which the heroine of romance commonly lays claim. Snares or shot may take off the old birds foraging without--hawks may be abroad, from which they escape or by whom they suffer; but the young ones in the nest have a pretty comfortable unromantic sort of existence in the down and the straw, till it comes to their turn, too, to get on the wing. While Becky Sharp was on her own wing in the country, hopping on all sorts of twigs, and amid a multiplicity of traps, and pecking up her food quite harmless and successful, Amelia lay snug in her home of Russell Square; if she went into the world, it was under the guidance of the elders; nor did it seem that any evil could befall her or that opulent cheery comfortable home in which she was affectionately sheltered. Mamma had her morning duties, and her daily drive, and the delightful round of visits and shopping which forms the amusement, or the profession as you may call it, of the rich London lady. Papa conducted his mysterious operations in the City--a stirring place in those days, when war was raging all over Europe, and empires were being staked; when the \"Courier\" newspaper had tens of thousands of subscribers; when one day brought you a battle of Vittoria, another a burning of Moscow, or a newsman's horn blowing down Russell Square about dinner-time, announced such a fact as--\"Battle of Leipsic--six hundred thousand men engaged--total defeat of the French--two hundred thousand killed.\" Old Sedley once or twice came home with a very grave face; and no wonder, when such news as this was agitating all the hearts and all the Stocks of Europe.\n\nMeanwhile matters went on in Russell Square, Bloomsbury, just as if matters in Europe were not in the least disorganised. The retreat from Leipsic made no difference in the number of meals Mr. Sambo took in the servants' hall; the allies poured into France, and the dinner-bell rang at five o'clock just as usual. I don't think poor Amelia cared anything about Brienne and Montmirail, or was fairly interested in the war until the abdication of the Emperor; when she clapped her hands and said prayers--oh, how grateful! and flung herself into George Osborne's arms with all her soul, to the astonishment of everybody who witnessed that ebullition of sentiment. The fact is, peace was declared, Europe was going to be at rest; the Corsican was overthrown, and Lieutenant Osborne's regiment would not be ordered on service. That was the way in which Miss Amelia reasoned. The fate of Europe was Lieutenant George Osborne to her. His dangers being over, she sang Te Deum. He was her Europe: her emperor: her allied monarchs and august prince regent. He was her sun and moon; and I believe she thought the grand illumination and ball at the Mansion House, given to the sovereigns, were especially in honour of George Osborne.\n\nWe have talked of shift, self, and poverty, as those dismal instructors under whom poor Miss Becky Sharp got her education. Now, love was Miss Amelia Sedley's last tutoress, and it was amazing what progress our young lady made under that popular teacher. In the course of fifteen or eighteen months' daily and constant attention to this eminent finishing governess, what a deal of secrets Amelia learned, which Miss Wirt and the black-eyed young ladies over the way, which old Miss Pinkerton of Chiswick herself, had no cognizance of! As, indeed, how should any of those prim and reputable virgins? With Misses P. and W. the tender passion is out of the question: I would not dare to breathe such an idea regarding them. Miss Maria Osborne, it is true, was \"attached\" to Mr. Frederick Augustus Bullock, of the firm of Hulker, Bullock & Bullock; but hers was a most respectable attachment, and she would have taken Bullock Senior just the same, her mind being fixed--as that of a well-bred young woman should be--upon a house in Park Lane, a country house at Wimbledon, a handsome chariot, and two prodigious tall horses and footmen, and a fourth of the annual profits of the eminent firm of Hulker & Bullock, all of which advantages were represented in the person of Frederick Augustus. Had orange blossoms been invented then (those touching emblems of female purity imported by us from France, where people's daughters are universally sold in marriage), Miss Maria, I say, would have assumed the spotless wreath, and stepped into the travelling carriage by the side of gouty, old, bald-headed, bottle-nosed Bullock Senior; and devoted her beautiful existence to his happiness with perfect modesty--only the old gentleman was married already; so she bestowed her young affections on the junior partner. Sweet, blooming, orange flowers! The other day I saw Miss Trotter (that was), arrayed in them, trip into the travelling carriage at St. George's, Hanover Square, and Lord Methuselah hobbled in after. With what an engaging modesty she pulled down the blinds of the chariot--the dear innocent! There were half the carriages of Vanity Fair at the wedding.\n\nThis was not the sort of love that finished Amelia's education; and in the course of a year turned a good young girl into a good young woman--to be a good wife presently, when the happy time should come. This young person (perhaps it was very imprudent in her parents to encourage her, and abet her in such idolatry and silly romantic ideas) loved, with all her heart, the young officer in His Majesty's service with whom we have made a brief acquaintance. She thought about him the very first moment on waking; and his was the very last name mentioned in her prayers. She never had seen a man so beautiful or so clever: such a figure on horseback: such a dancer: such a hero in general. Talk of the Prince's bow! what was it to George's? She had seen Mr. Brummell, whom everybody praised so. Compare such a person as that to her George! Not amongst all the beaux at the Opera (and there were beaux in those days with actual opera hats) was there any one to equal him. He was only good enough to be a fairy prince; and oh, what magnanimity to stoop to such a humble Cinderella! Miss Pinkerton would have tried to check this blind devotion very likely, had she been Amelia's confidante; but not with much success, depend upon it. It is in the nature and instinct of some women. Some are made to scheme, and some to love; and I wish any respected bachelor that reads this may take the sort that best likes him.\n\nWhile under this overpowering impression, Miss Amelia neglected her twelve dear friends at Chiswick most cruelly, as such selfish people commonly will do. She had but this subject, of course, to think about; and Miss Saltire was too cold for a confidante, and she couldn't bring her mind to tell Miss Swartz, the woolly-haired young heiress from St. Kitt's. She had little Laura Martin home for the holidays; and my belief is, she made a confidante of her, and promised that Laura should come and live with her when she was married, and gave Laura a great deal of information regarding the passion of love, which must have been singularly useful and novel to that little person. Alas, alas! I fear poor Emmy had not a well-regulated mind.\n\nWhat were her parents doing, not to keep this little heart from beating so fast? Old Sedley did not seem much to notice matters. He was graver of late, and his City affairs absorbed him. Mrs. Sedley was of so easy and uninquisitive a nature that she wasn't even jealous. Mr. Jos was away, being besieged by an Irish widow at Cheltenham. Amelia had the house to herself--ah! too much to herself sometimes--not that she ever doubted; for, to be sure, George must be at the Horse Guards; and he can't always get leave from Chatham; and he must see his friends and sisters, and mingle in society when in town (he, such an ornament to every society!); and when he is with the regiment, he is too tired to write long letters. I know where she kept that packet she had--and can steal in and out of her chamber like Iachimo--like Iachimo? No--that is a bad part. I will only act Moonshine, and peep harmless into the bed where faith and beauty and innocence lie dreaming.\n\nBut if Osborne's were short and soldierlike letters, it must be confessed, that were Miss Sedley's letters to Mr. Osborne to be published, we should have to extend this novel to such a multiplicity of volumes as not the most sentimental reader could support; that she not only filled sheets of large paper, but crossed them with the most astonishing perverseness; that she wrote whole pages out of poetry-books without the least pity; that she underlined words and passages with quite a frantic emphasis; and, in fine, gave the usual tokens of her condition. She wasn't a heroine. Her letters were full of repetition. She wrote rather doubtful grammar sometimes, and in her verses took all sorts of liberties with the metre. But oh, mesdames, if you are not allowed to touch the heart sometimes in spite of syntax, and are not to be loved until you all know the difference between trimeter and tetrameter, may all Poetry go to the deuce, and every schoolmaster perish miserably!\n\nSentimental and Otherwise\nI fear the gentleman to whom Miss Amelia's letters were addressed was rather an obdurate critic. Such a number of notes followed Lieutenant Osborne about the country, that he became almost ashamed of the jokes of his mess-room companions regarding them, and ordered his servant never to deliver them except at his private apartment. He was seen lighting his cigar with one, to the horror of Captain Dobbin, who, it is my belief, would have given a bank-note for the document.\n\nFor some time George strove to keep the liaison a secret. There was a woman in the case, that he admitted. \"And not the first either,\" said Ensign Spooney to Ensign Stubble. \"That Osborne's a devil of a fellow. There was a judge's daughter at Demerara went almost mad about him; then there was that beautiful quadroon girl, Miss Pye, at St. Vincent's, you know; and since he's been home, they say he's a regular Don Giovanni, by Jove.\"\n\nStubble and Spooney thought that to be a \"regular Don Giovanni, by Jove\" was one of the finest qualities a man could possess, and Osborne's reputation was prodigious amongst the young men of the regiment. He was famous in field-sports, famous at a song, famous on parade; free with his money, which was bountifully supplied by his father. His coats were better made than any man's in the regiment, and he had more of them. He was adored by the men. He could drink more than any officer of the whole mess, including old Heavytop, the colonel. He could spar better than Knuckles, the private (who would have been a corporal but for his drunkenness, and who had been in the prize-ring); and was the best batter and bowler, out and out, of the regimental club. He rode his own horse, Greased Lightning, and won the Garrison cup at Quebec races. There were other people besides Amelia who worshipped him. Stubble and Spooney thought him a sort of Apollo; Dobbin took him to be an Admirable Crichton; and Mrs. Major O'Dowd acknowledged he was an elegant young fellow, and put her in mind of Fitzjurld Fogarty, Lord Castlefogarty's second son.\n\nWell, Stubble and Spooney and the rest indulged in most romantic conjectures regarding this female correspondent of Osborne's--opining that it was a Duchess in London who was in love with him--or that it was a General's daughter, who was engaged to somebody else, and madly attached to him--or that it was a Member of Parliament's lady, who proposed four horses and an elopement--or that it was some other victim of a passion delightfully exciting, romantic, and disgraceful to all parties, on none of which conjectures would Osborne throw the least light, leaving his young admirers and friends to invent and arrange their whole history.\n\nAnd the real state of the case would never have been known at all in the regiment but for Captain Dobbin's indiscretion. The Captain was eating his breakfast one day in the mess-room, while Cackle, the assistant-surgeon, and the two above-named worthies were speculating upon Osborne's intrigue--Stubble holding out that the lady was a Duchess about Queen Charlotte's court, and Cackle vowing she was an opera-singer of the worst reputation. At this idea Dobbin became so moved, that though his mouth was full of eggs and bread-and-butter at the time, and though he ought not to have spoken at all, yet he couldn't help blurting out, \"Cackle, you're a stupid fool. You're always talking nonsense and scandal. Osborne is not going to run off with a Duchess or ruin a milliner. Miss Sedley is one of the most charming young women that ever lived. He's been engaged to her ever so long; and the man who calls her names had better not do so in my hearing.\" With which, turning exceedingly red, Dobbin ceased speaking, and almost choked himself with a cup of tea. The story was over the regiment in half-an-hour; and that very evening Mrs. Major O'Dowd wrote off to her sister Glorvina at O'Dowdstown not to hurry from Dublin--young Osborne being prematurely engaged already.\n\nShe complimented the Lieutenant in an appropriate speech over a glass of whisky-toddy that evening, and he went home perfectly furious to quarrel with Dobbin (who had declined Mrs. Major O'Dowd's party, and sat in his own room playing the flute, and, I believe, writing poetry in a very melancholy manner)--to quarrel with Dobbin for betraying his secret.\n\n\"Who the deuce asked you to talk about my affairs?\" Osborne shouted indignantly. \"Why the devil is all the regiment to know that I am going to be married? Why is that tattling old harridan, Peggy O'Dowd, to make free with my name at her d--d supper-table, and advertise my engagement over the three kingdoms? After all, what right have you to say I am engaged, or to meddle in my business at all, Dobbin?\"\n\n\"It seems to me,\" Captain Dobbin began.\n\n\"Seems be hanged, Dobbin,\" his junior interrupted him. \"I am under obligations to you, I know it, a d--d deal too well too; but I won't be always sermonised by you because you're five years my senior. I'm hanged if I'll stand your airs of superiority and infernal pity and patronage. Pity and patronage! I should like to know in what I'm your inferior?\"\n\n\"Are you engaged?\" Captain Dobbin interposed.\n\n\"What the devil's that to you or any one here if I am?\"\n\n\"Are you ashamed of it?\" Dobbin resumed.\n\n\"What right have you to ask me that question, sir? I should like to know,\" George said.\n\n\"Good God, you don't mean to say you want to break off?\" asked Dobbin, starting up.\n\n\"In other words, you ask me if I'm a man of honour,\" said Osborne, fiercely; \"is that what you mean? You've adopted such a tone regarding me lately that I'm ------ if I'll bear it any more.\"\n\n\"What have I done? I've told you you were neglecting a sweet girl, George. I've told you that when you go to town you ought to go to her, and not to the gambling-houses about St. James's.\"\n\n\"You want your money back, I suppose,\" said George, with a sneer.\n\n\"Of course I do--I always did, didn't I?\" says Dobbin. \"You speak like a generous fellow.\"\n\n\"No, hang it, William, I beg your pardon\"--here George interposed in a fit of remorse; \"you have been my friend in a hundred ways, Heaven knows. You've got me out of a score of scrapes. When Crawley of the Guards won that sum of money of me I should have been done but for you: I know I should. But you shouldn't deal so hardly with me; you shouldn't be always catechising me. I am very fond of Amelia; I adore her, and that sort of thing. Don't look angry. She's faultless; I know she is. But you see there's no fun in winning a thing unless you play for it. Hang it: the regiment's just back from the West Indies, I must have a little fling, and then when I'm married I'll reform; I will upon my honour, now. And--I say--Dob--don't be angry with me, and I'll give you a hundred next month, when I know my father will stand something handsome; and I'll ask Heavytop for leave, and I'll go to town, and see Amelia to-morrow--there now, will that satisfy you?\"\n\n\"It is impossible to be long angry with you, George,\" said the good-natured Captain; \"and as for the money, old boy, you know if I wanted it you'd share your last shilling with me.\"\n\n\"That I would, by Jove, Dobbin,\" George said, with the greatest generosity, though by the way he never had any money to spare.\n\n\"Only I wish you had sown those wild oats of yours, George. If you could have seen poor little Miss Emmy's face when she asked me about you the other day, you would have pitched those billiard-balls to the deuce. Go and comfort her, you rascal. Go and write her a long letter. Do something to make her happy; a very little will.\"\n\n\"I believe she's d--d fond of me,\" the Lieutenant said, with a self-satisfied air; and went off to finish the evening with some jolly fellows in the mess-room.\n\nAmelia meanwhile, in Russell Square, was looking at the moon, which was shining upon that peaceful spot, as well as upon the square of the Chatham barracks, where Lieutenant Osborne was quartered, and thinking to herself how her hero was employed. Perhaps he is visiting the sentries, thought she; perhaps he is bivouacking; perhaps he is attending the couch of a wounded comrade, or studying the art of war up in his own desolate chamber. And her kind thoughts sped away as if they were angels and had wings, and flying down the river to Chatham and Rochester, strove to peep into the barracks where George was. . . . All things considered, I think it was as well the gates were shut, and the sentry allowed no one to pass; so that the poor little white-robed angel could not hear the songs those young fellows were roaring over the whisky-punch.\n\nThe day after the little conversation at Chatham barracks, young Osborne, to show that he would be as good as his word, prepared to go to town, thereby incurring Captain Dobbin's applause. \"I should have liked to make her a little present,\" Osborne said to his friend in confidence, \"only I am quite out of cash until my father tips up.\" But Dobbin would not allow this good nature and generosity to be balked, and so accommodated Mr. Osborne with a few pound notes, which the latter took after a little faint scruple.\n\nAnd I dare say he would have bought something very handsome for Amelia; only, getting off the coach in Fleet Street, he was attracted by a handsome shirt-pin in a jeweller's window, which he could not resist; and having paid for that, had very little money to spare for indulging in any further exercise of kindness. Never mind: you may be sure it was not his presents Amelia wanted. When he came to Russell Square, her face lighted up as if he had been sunshine. The little cares, fears, tears, timid misgivings, sleepless fancies of I don't know how many days and nights, were forgotten, under one moment's influence of that familiar, irresistible smile. He beamed on her from the drawing-room door--magnificent, with ambrosial whiskers, like a god. Sambo, whose face as he announced Captain Osbin (having conferred a brevet rank on that young officer) blazed with a sympathetic grin, saw the little girl start, and flush, and jump up from her watching-place in the window; and Sambo retreated: and as soon as the door was shut, she went fluttering to Lieutenant George Osborne's heart as if it was the only natural home for her to nestle in. Oh, thou poor panting little soul! The very finest tree in the whole forest, with the straightest stem, and the strongest arms, and the thickest foliage, wherein you choose to build and coo, may be marked, for what you know, and may be down with a crash ere long. What an old, old simile that is, between man and timber!\n\nIn the meanwhile, George kissed her very kindly on her forehead and glistening eyes, and was very gracious and good; and she thought his diamond shirt-pin (which she had not known him to wear before) the prettiest ornament ever seen.\n\nThe observant reader, who has marked our young Lieutenant's previous behaviour, and has preserved our report of the brief conversation which he has just had with Captain Dobbin, has possibly come to certain conclusions regarding the character of Mr. Osborne. Some cynical Frenchman has said that there are two parties to a love-transaction: the one who loves and the other who condescends to be so treated. Perhaps the love is occasionally on the man's side; perhaps on the lady's. Perhaps some infatuated swain has ere this mistaken insensibility for modesty, dulness for maiden reserve, mere vacuity for sweet bashfulness, and a goose, in a word, for a swan. Perhaps some beloved female subscriber has arrayed an ass in the splendour and glory of her imagination; admired his dulness as manly simplicity; worshipped his selfishness as manly superiority; treated his stupidity as majestic gravity, and used him as the brilliant fairy Titania did a certain weaver at Athens. I think I have seen such comedies of errors going on in the world. But this is certain, that Amelia believed her lover to be one of the most gallant and brilliant men in the empire: and it is possible Lieutenant Osborne thought so too.\n\nHe was a little wild: how many young men are; and don't girls like a rake better than a milksop? He hadn't sown his wild oats as yet, but he would soon: and quit the army now that peace was proclaimed; the Corsican monster locked up at Elba; promotion by consequence over; and no chance left for the display of his undoubted military talents and valour: and his allowance, with Amelia's settlement, would enable them to take a snug place in the country somewhere, in a good sporting neighbourhood; and he would hunt a little, and farm a little; and they would be very happy. As for remaining in the army as a married man, that was impossible. Fancy Mrs. George Osborne in lodgings in a county town; or, worse still, in the East or West Indies, with a society of officers, and patronized by Mrs. Major O'Dowd! Amelia died with laughing at Osborne's stories about Mrs. Major O'Dowd. He loved her much too fondly to subject her to that horrid woman and her vulgarities, and the rough treatment of a soldier's wife. He didn't care for himself--not he; but his dear little girl should take the place in society to which, as his wife, she was entitled: and to these proposals you may be sure she acceded, as she would to any other from the same author.\n\nHolding this kind of conversation, and building numberless castles in the air (which Amelia adorned with all sorts of flower-gardens, rustic walks, country churches, Sunday schools, and the like; while George had his mind's eye directed to the stables, the kennel, and the cellar), this young pair passed away a couple of hours very pleasantly; and as the Lieutenant had only that single day in town, and a great deal of most important business to transact, it was proposed that Miss Emmy should dine with her future sisters-in-law. This invitation was accepted joyfully. He conducted her to his sisters; where he left her talking and prattling in a way that astonished those ladies, who thought that George might make something of her; and he then went off to transact his business.\n\nIn a word, he went out and ate ices at a pastry-cook's shop in Charing Cross; tried a new coat in Pall Mall; dropped in at the Old Slaughters', and called for Captain Cannon; played eleven games at billiards with the Captain, of which he won eight, and returned to Russell Square half an hour late for dinner, but in very good humour.\n\nIt was not so with old Mr. Osborne. When that gentleman came from the City, and was welcomed in the drawing-room by his daughters and the elegant Miss Wirt, they saw at once by his face--which was puffy, solemn, and yellow at the best of times--and by the scowl and twitching of his black eyebrows, that the heart within his large white waistcoat was disturbed and uneasy. When Amelia stepped forward to salute him, which she always did with great trembling and timidity, he gave a surly grunt of recognition, and dropped the little hand out of his great hirsute paw without any attempt to hold it there. He looked round gloomily at his eldest daughter; who, comprehending the meaning of his look, which asked unmistakably, \"Why the devil is she here?\" said at once:\n\n\"George is in town, Papa; and has gone to the Horse Guards, and will be back to dinner.\"\n\n\"O he is, is he? I won't have the dinner kept waiting for him, Jane\"; with which this worthy man lapsed into his particular chair, and then the utter silence in his genteel, well-furnished drawing-room was only interrupted by the alarmed ticking of the great French clock.\n\nWhen that chronometer, which was surmounted by a cheerful brass group of the sacrifice of Iphigenia, tolled five in a heavy cathedral tone, Mr. Osborne pulled the bell at his right hand--violently, and the butler rushed up.\n\n\"Dinner!\" roared Mr. Osborne.\n\n\"Mr. George isn't come in, sir,\" interposed the man.\n\n\"Damn Mr. George, sir. Am I master of the house? DINNER!\" Mr. Osborne scowled. Amelia trembled. A telegraphic communication of eyes passed between the other three ladies. The obedient bell in the lower regions began ringing the announcement of the meal. The tolling over, the head of the family thrust his hands into the great tail-pockets of his great blue coat with brass buttons, and without waiting for a further announcement strode downstairs alone, scowling over his shoulder at the four females.\n\n\"What's the matter now, my dear?\" asked one of the other, as they rose and tripped gingerly behind the sire. \"I suppose the funds are falling,\" whispered Miss Wirt; and so, trembling and in silence, this hushed female company followed their dark leader. They took their places in silence. He growled out a blessing, which sounded as gruffly as a curse. The great silver dish-covers were removed. Amelia trembled in her place, for she was next to the awful Osborne, and alone on her side of the table--the gap being occasioned by the absence of George.\n\n\"Soup?\" says Mr. Osborne, clutching the ladle, fixing his eyes on her, in a sepulchral tone; and having helped her and the rest, did not speak for a while.\n\n\"Take Miss Sedley's plate away,\" at last he said. \"She can't eat the soup--no more can I. It's beastly. Take away the soup, Hicks, and to-morrow turn the cook out of the house, Jane.\"\n\nHaving concluded his observations upon the soup, Mr. Osborne made a few curt remarks respecting the fish, also of a savage and satirical tendency, and cursed Billingsgate with an emphasis quite worthy of the place. Then he lapsed into silence, and swallowed sundry glasses of wine, looking more and more terrible, till a brisk knock at the door told of George's arrival when everybody began to rally.\n\n\"He could not come before. General Daguilet had kept him waiting at the Horse Guards. Never mind soup or fish. Give him anything--he didn't care what. Capital mutton--capital everything.\" His good humour contrasted with his father's severity; and he rattled on unceasingly during dinner, to the delight of all--of one especially, who need not be mentioned.\n\nAs soon as the young ladies had discussed the orange and the glass of wine which formed the ordinary conclusion of the dismal banquets at Mr. Osborne's house, the signal to make sail for the drawing-room was given, and they all arose and departed. Amelia hoped George would soon join them there. She began playing some of his favourite waltzes (then newly imported) at the great carved-legged, leather-cased grand piano in the drawing-room overhead. This little artifice did not bring him. He was deaf to the waltzes; they grew fainter and fainter; the discomfited performer left the huge instrument presently; and though her three friends performed some of the loudest and most brilliant new pieces of their repertoire, she did not hear a single note, but sate thinking, and boding evil. Old Osborne's scowl, terrific always, had never before looked so deadly to her. His eyes followed her out of the room, as if she had been guilty of something. When they brought her coffee, she started as though it were a cup of poison which Mr. Hicks, the butler, wished to propose to her. What mystery was there lurking? Oh, those women! They nurse and cuddle their presentiments, and make darlings of their ugliest thoughts, as they do of their deformed children.\n\nThe gloom on the paternal countenance had also impressed George Osborne with anxiety. With such eyebrows, and a look so decidedly bilious, how was he to extract that money from the governor, of which George was consumedly in want? He began praising his father's wine. That was generally a successful means of cajoling the old gentleman.\n\n\"We never got such Madeira in the West Indies, sir, as yours. Colonel Heavytop took off three bottles of that you sent me down, under his belt the other day.\"\n\n\"Did he?\" said the old gentleman. \"It stands me in eight shillings a bottle.\"\n\n\"Will you take six guineas a dozen for it, sir?\" said George, with a laugh. \"There's one of the greatest men in the kingdom wants some.\"\n\n\"Does he?\" growled the senior. \"Wish he may get it.\"\n\n\"When General Daguilet was at Chatham, sir, Heavytop gave him a breakfast, and asked me for some of the wine. The General liked it just as well--wanted a pipe for the Commander-in-Chief. He's his Royal Highness's right-hand man.\"\n\n\"It is devilish fine wine,\" said the Eyebrows, and they looked more good-humoured; and George was going to take advantage of this complacency, and bring the supply question on the mahogany, when the father, relapsing into solemnity, though rather cordial in manner, bade him ring the bell for claret. \"And we'll see if that's as good as the Madeira, George, to which his Royal Highness is welcome, I'm sure. And as we are drinking it, I'll talk to you about a matter of importance.\"\n\nAmelia heard the claret bell ringing as she sat nervously upstairs. She thought, somehow, it was a mysterious and presentimental bell. Of the presentiments which some people are always having, some surely must come right.\n\n\"What I want to know, George,\" the old gentleman said, after slowly smacking his first bumper--\"what I want to know is, how you and--ah--that little thing upstairs, are carrying on?\"\n\n\"I think, sir, it is not hard to see,\" George said, with a self-satisfied grin. \"Pretty clear, sir.--What capital wine!\"\n\n\"What d'you mean, pretty clear, sir?\"\n\n\"Why, hang it, sir, don't push me too hard. I'm a modest man. I--ah--I don't set up to be a lady-killer; but I do own that she's as devilish fond of me as she can be. Anybody can see that with half an eye.\"\n\n\"And you yourself?\"\n\n\"Why, sir, didn't you order me to marry her, and ain't I a good boy? Haven't our Papas settled it ever so long?\"\n\n\"A pretty boy, indeed. Haven't I heard of your doings, sir, with Lord Tarquin, Captain Crawley of the Guards, the Honourable Mr. Deuceace and that set. Have a care sir, have a care.\"\n\nThe old gentleman pronounced these aristocratic names with the greatest gusto. Whenever he met a great man he grovelled before him, and my-lorded him as only a free-born Briton can do. He came home and looked out his history in the Peerage: he introduced his name into his daily conversation; he bragged about his Lordship to his daughters. He fell down prostrate and basked in him as a Neapolitan beggar does in the sun. George was alarmed when he heard the names. He feared his father might have been informed of certain transactions at play. But the old moralist eased him by saying serenely:\n\n\"Well, well, young men will be young men. And the comfort to me is, George, that living in the best society in England, as I hope you do; as I think you do; as my means will allow you to do--\"\n\n\"Thank you, sir,\" says George, making his point at once. \"One can't live with these great folks for nothing; and my purse, sir, look at it\"; and he held up a little token which had been netted by Amelia, and contained the very last of Dobbin's pound notes.\n\n\"You shan't want, sir. The British merchant's son shan't want, sir. My guineas are as good as theirs, George, my boy; and I don't grudge 'em. Call on Mr. Chopper as you go through the City to-morrow; he'll have something for you. I don't grudge money when I know you're in good society, because I know that good society can never go wrong. There's no pride in me. I was a humbly born man--but you have had advantages. Make a good use of 'em. Mix with the young nobility. There's many of 'em who can't spend a dollar to your guinea, my boy. And as for the pink bonnets (here from under the heavy eyebrows there came a knowing and not very pleasing leer)--why boys will be boys. Only there's one thing I order you to avoid, which, if you do not, I'll cut you off with a shilling, by Jove; and that's gambling.\"\n\n\"Oh, of course, sir,\" said George.\n\n\"But to return to the other business about Amelia: why shouldn't you marry higher than a stockbroker's daughter, George--that's what I want to know?\"\n\n\"It's a family business, sir,\" says George, cracking filberts. \"You and Mr. Sedley made the match a hundred years ago.\"\n\n\"I don't deny it; but people's positions alter, sir. I don't deny that Sedley made my fortune, or rather put me in the way of acquiring, by my own talents and genius, that proud position, which, I may say, I occupy in the tallow trade and the City of London. I've shown my gratitude to Sedley; and he's tried it of late, sir, as my cheque-book can show. George! I tell you in confidence I don't like the looks of Mr. Sedley's affairs. My chief clerk, Mr. Chopper, does not like the looks of 'em, and he's an old file, and knows 'Change as well as any man in London. Hulker & Bullock are looking shy at him. He's been dabbling on his own account I fear. They say the Jeune Amelie was his, which was taken by the Yankee privateer Molasses. And that's flat--unless I see Amelia's ten thousand down you don't marry her. I'll have no lame duck's daughter in my family. Pass the wine, sir--or ring for coffee.\"\n\nWith which Mr. Osborne spread out the evening paper, and George knew from this signal that the colloquy was ended, and that his papa was about to take a nap.\n\nHe hurried upstairs to Amelia in the highest spirits. What was it that made him more attentive to her on that night than he had been for a long time--more eager to amuse her, more tender, more brilliant in talk? Was it that his generous heart warmed to her at the prospect of misfortune; or that the idea of losing the dear little prize made him value it more?\n\nShe lived upon the recollections of that happy evening for many days afterwards, remembering his words; his looks; the song he sang; his attitude, as he leant over her or looked at her from a distance. As it seemed to her, no night ever passed so quickly at Mr. Osborne's house before; and for once this young person was almost provoked to be angry by the premature arrival of Mr. Sambo with her shawl.\n\nGeorge came and took a tender leave of her the next morning; and then hurried off to the City, where he visited Mr. Chopper, his father's head man, and received from that gentleman a document which he exchanged at Hulker & Bullock's for a whole pocketful of money. As George entered the house, old John Sedley was passing out of the banker's parlour, looking very dismal. But his godson was much too elated to mark the worthy stockbroker's depression, or the dreary eyes which the kind old gentleman cast upon him. Young Bullock did not come grinning out of the parlour with him as had been his wont in former years.\n\nAnd as the swinging doors of Hulker, Bullock & Co. closed upon Mr. Sedley, Mr. Quill, the cashier (whose benevolent occupation it is to hand out crisp bank-notes from a drawer and dispense sovereigns out of a copper shovel), winked at Mr. Driver, the clerk at the desk on his right. Mr. Driver winked again.\n\n\"No go,\" Mr. D. whispered.\n\n\"Not at no price,\" Mr. Q. said. \"Mr. George Osborne, sir, how will you take it?\" George crammed eagerly a quantity of notes into his pockets, and paid Dobbin fifty pounds that very evening at mess.\n\nThat very evening Amelia wrote him the tenderest of long letters. Her heart was overflowing with tenderness, but it still foreboded evil. What was the cause of Mr. Osborne's dark looks? she asked. Had any difference arisen between him and her papa? Her poor papa returned so melancholy from the City, that all were alarmed about him at home--in fine, there were four pages of loves and fears and hopes and forebodings.\n\n\"Poor little Emmy--dear little Emmy. How fond she is of me,\" George said, as he perused the missive--\"and Gad, what a headache that mixed punch has given me!\" Poor little Emmy, indeed.\n\nMiss Crawley at Home\nAbout this time there drove up to an exceedingly snug and well-appointed house in Park Lane, a travelling chariot with a lozenge on the panels, a discontented female in a green veil and crimped curls on the rumble, and a large and confidential man on the box. It was the equipage of our friend Miss Crawley, returning from Hants. The carriage windows were shut; the fat spaniel, whose head and tongue ordinarily lolled out of one of them, reposed on the lap of the discontented female. When the vehicle stopped, a large round bundle of shawls was taken out of the carriage by the aid of various domestics and a young lady who accompanied the heap of cloaks. That bundle contained Miss Crawley, who was conveyed upstairs forthwith, and put into a bed and chamber warmed properly as for the reception of an invalid. Messengers went off for her physician and medical man. They came, consulted, prescribed, vanished. The young companion of Miss Crawley, at the conclusion of their interview, came in to receive their instructions, and administered those antiphlogistic medicines which the eminent men ordered.\n\nCaptain Crawley of the Life Guards rode up from Knightsbridge Barracks the next day; his black charger pawed the straw before his invalid aunt's door. He was most affectionate in his inquiries regarding that amiable relative. There seemed to be much source of apprehension. He found Miss Crawley's maid (the discontented female) unusually sulky and despondent; he found Miss Briggs, her dame de compagnie, in tears alone in the drawing-room. She had hastened home, hearing of her beloved friend's illness. She wished to fly to her couch, that couch which she, Briggs, had so often smoothed in the hour of sickness. She was denied admission to Miss Crawley's apartment. A stranger was administering her medicines--a stranger from the country--an odious Miss ... --tears choked the utterance of the dame de compagnie, and she buried her crushed affections and her poor old red nose in her pocket handkerchief.\n\nRawdon Crawley sent up his name by the sulky femme de chambre, and Miss Crawley's new companion, coming tripping down from the sick-room, put a little hand into his as he stepped forward eagerly to meet her, gave a glance of great scorn at the bewildered Briggs, and beckoning the young Guardsman out of the back drawing-room, led him downstairs into that now desolate dining-parlour, where so many a good dinner had been celebrated.\n\nHere these two talked for ten minutes, discussing, no doubt, the symptoms of the old invalid above stairs; at the end of which period the parlour bell was rung briskly, and answered on that instant by Mr. Bowls, Miss Crawley's large confidential butler (who, indeed, happened to be at the keyhole during the most part of the interview); and the Captain coming out, curling his mustachios, mounted the black charger pawing among the straw, to the admiration of the little blackguard boys collected in the street. He looked in at the dining-room window, managing his horse, which curvetted and capered beautifully--for one instant the young person might be seen at the window, when her figure vanished, and, doubtless, she went upstairs again to resume the affecting duties of benevolence.\n\nWho could this young woman be, I wonder? That evening a little dinner for two persons was laid in the dining-room--when Mrs. Firkin, the lady's maid, pushed into her mistress's apartment, and bustled about there during the vacancy occasioned by the departure of the new nurse--and the latter and Miss Briggs sat down to the neat little meal.\n\nBriggs was so much choked by emotion that she could hardly take a morsel of meat. The young person carved a fowl with the utmost delicacy, and asked so distinctly for egg-sauce, that poor Briggs, before whom that delicious condiment was placed, started, made a great clattering with the ladle, and once more fell back in the most gushing hysterical state.\n\n\"Had you not better give Miss Briggs a glass of wine?\" said the person to Mr. Bowls, the large confidential man. He did so. Briggs seized it mechanically, gasped it down convulsively, moaned a little, and began to play with the chicken on her plate.\n\n\"I think we shall be able to help each other,\" said the person with great suavity: \"and shall have no need of Mr. Bowls's kind services. Mr. Bowls, if you please, we will ring when we want you.\" He went downstairs, where, by the way, he vented the most horrid curses upon the unoffending footman, his subordinate.\n\n\"It is a pity you take on so, Miss Briggs,\" the young lady said, with a cool, slightly sarcastic, air.\n\n\"My dearest friend is so ill, and wo-o-on't see me,\" gurgled out Briggs in an agony of renewed grief.\n\n\"She's not very ill any more. Console yourself, dear Miss Briggs. She has only overeaten herself--that is all. She is greatly better. She will soon be quite restored again. She is weak from being cupped and from medical treatment, but she will rally immediately. Pray console yourself, and take a little more wine.\"\n\n\"But why, why won't she see me again?\" Miss Briggs bleated out. \"Oh, Matilda, Matilda, after three-and-twenty years' tenderness! is this the return to your poor, poor Arabella?\"\n\n\"Don't cry too much, poor Arabella,\" the other said (with ever so little of a grin); \"she only won't see you, because she says you don't nurse her as well as I do. It's no pleasure to me to sit up all night. I wish you might do it instead.\"\n\n\"Have I not tended that dear couch for years?\" Arabella said, \"and now--\"\n\n\"Now she prefers somebody else. Well, sick people have these fancies, and must be humoured. When she's well I shall go.\"\n\n\"Never, never,\" Arabella exclaimed, madly inhaling her salts-bottle.\n\n\"Never be well or never go, Miss Briggs?\" the other said, with the same provoking good-nature. \"Pooh--she will be well in a fortnight, when I shall go back to my little pupils at Queen's Crawley, and to their mother, who is a great deal more sick than our friend. You need not be jealous about me, my dear Miss Briggs. I am a poor little girl without any friends, or any harm in me. I don't want to supplant you in Miss Crawley's good graces. She will forget me a week after I am gone: and her affection for you has been the work of years. Give me a little wine if you please, my dear Miss Briggs, and let us be friends. I'm sure I want friends.\"\n\nThe placable and soft-hearted Briggs speechlessly pushed out her hand at this appeal; but she felt the desertion most keenly for all that, and bitterly, bitterly moaned the fickleness of her Matilda. At the end of half an hour, the meal over, Miss Rebecca Sharp (for such, astonishing to state, is the name of her who has been described ingeniously as \"the person\" hitherto), went upstairs again to her patient's rooms, from which, with the most engaging politeness, she eliminated poor Firkin. \"Thank you, Mrs. Firkin, that will quite do; how nicely you make it! I will ring when anything is wanted.\" \"Thank you\"; and Firkin came downstairs in a tempest of jealousy, only the more dangerous because she was forced to confine it in her own bosom.\n\nCould it be the tempest which, as she passed the landing of the first floor, blew open the drawing-room door? No; it was stealthily opened by the hand of Briggs. Briggs had been on the watch. Briggs too well heard the creaking Firkin descend the stairs, and the clink of the spoon and gruel-basin the neglected female carried.\n\n\"Well, Firkin?\" says she, as the other entered the apartment. \"Well, Jane?\"\n\n\"Wuss and wuss, Miss B.,\" Firkin said, wagging her head.\n\n\"Is she not better then?\"\n\n\"She never spoke but once, and I asked her if she felt a little more easy, and she told me to hold my stupid tongue. Oh, Miss B., I never thought to have seen this day!\" And the water-works again began to play.\n\n\"What sort of a person is this Miss Sharp, Firkin? I little thought, while enjoying my Christmas revels in the elegant home of my firm friends, the Reverend Lionel Delamere and his amiable lady, to find a stranger had taken my place in the affections of my dearest, my still dearest Matilda!\" Miss Briggs, it will be seen by her language, was of a literary and sentimental turn, and had once published a volume of poems--\"Trills of the Nightingale\"--by subscription.\n\n\"Miss B., they are all infatyated about that young woman,\" Firkin replied. \"Sir Pitt wouldn't have let her go, but he daredn't refuse Miss Crawley anything. Mrs. Bute at the Rectory jist as bad--never happy out of her sight. The Capting quite wild about her. Mr. Crawley mortial jealous. Since Miss C. was took ill, she won't have nobody near her but Miss Sharp, I can't tell for where nor for why; and I think somethink has bewidged everybody.\"\n\nRebecca passed that night in constant watching upon Miss Crawley; the next night the old lady slept so comfortably, that Rebecca had time for several hours' comfortable repose herself on the sofa, at the foot of her patroness's bed; very soon, Miss Crawley was so well that she sat up and laughed heartily at a perfect imitation of Miss Briggs and her grief, which Rebecca described to her. Briggs' weeping snuffle, and her manner of using the handkerchief, were so completely rendered that Miss Crawley became quite cheerful, to the admiration of the doctors when they visited her, who usually found this worthy woman of the world, when the least sickness attacked her, under the most abject depression and terror of death.\n\nCaptain Crawley came every day, and received bulletins from Miss Rebecca respecting his aunt's health. This improved so rapidly, that poor Briggs was allowed to see her patroness; and persons with tender hearts may imagine the smothered emotions of that sentimental female, and the affecting nature of the interview.\n\nMiss Crawley liked to have Briggs in a good deal soon. Rebecca used to mimic her to her face with the most admirable gravity, thereby rendering the imitation doubly piquant to her worthy patroness.\n\nThe causes which had led to the deplorable illness of Miss Crawley, and her departure from her brother's house in the country, were of such an unromantic nature that they are hardly fit to be explained in this genteel and sentimental novel. For how is it possible to hint of a delicate female, living in good society, that she ate and drank too much, and that a hot supper of lobsters profusely enjoyed at the Rectory was the reason of an indisposition which Miss Crawley herself persisted was solely attributable to the dampness of the weather? The attack was so sharp that Matilda--as his Reverence expressed it--was very nearly \"off the hooks\"; all the family were in a fever of expectation regarding the will, and Rawdon Crawley was making sure of at least forty thousand pounds before the commencement of the London season. Mr. Crawley sent over a choice parcel of tracts, to prepare her for the change from Vanity Fair and Park Lane for another world; but a good doctor from Southampton being called in in time, vanquished the lobster which was so nearly fatal to her, and gave her sufficient strength to enable her to return to London. The Baronet did not disguise his exceeding mortification at the turn which affairs took.\n\nWhile everybody was attending on Miss Crawley, and messengers every hour from the Rectory were carrying news of her health to the affectionate folks there, there was a lady in another part of the house, being exceedingly ill, of whom no one took any notice at all; and this was the lady of Crawley herself. The good doctor shook his head after seeing her; to which visit Sir Pitt consented, as it could be paid without a fee; and she was left fading away in her lonely chamber, with no more heed paid to her than to a weed in the park.\n\nThe young ladies, too, lost much of the inestimable benefit of their governess's instruction, So affectionate a nurse was Miss Sharp, that Miss Crawley would take her medicines from no other hand. Firkin had been deposed long before her mistress's departure from the country. That faithful attendant found a gloomy consolation on returning to London, in seeing Miss Briggs suffer the same pangs of jealousy and undergo the same faithless treatment to which she herself had been subject.\n\nCaptain Rawdon got an extension of leave on his aunt's illness, and remained dutifully at home. He was always in her antechamber. (She lay sick in the state bedroom, into which you entered by the little blue saloon.) His father was always meeting him there; or if he came down the corridor ever so quietly, his father's door was sure to open, and the hyena face of the old gentleman to glare out. What was it set one to watch the other so? A generous rivalry, no doubt, as to which should be most attentive to the dear sufferer in the state bedroom. Rebecca used to come out and comfort both of them; or one or the other of them rather. Both of these worthy gentlemen were most anxious to have news of the invalid from her little confidential messenger.\n\nAt dinner--to which meal she descended for half an hour--she kept the peace between them: after which she disappeared for the night; when Rawdon would ride over to the depot of the 150th at Mudbury, leaving his papa to the society of Mr. Horrocks and his rum and water. She passed as weary a fortnight as ever mortal spent in Miss Crawley's sick-room; but her little nerves seemed to be of iron, as she was quite unshaken by the duty and the tedium of the sick-chamber.\n\nShe never told until long afterwards how painful that duty was; how peevish a patient was the jovial old lady; how angry; how sleepless; in what horrors of death; during what long nights she lay moaning, and in almost delirious agonies respecting that future world which she quite ignored when she was in good health.--Picture to yourself, oh fair young reader, a worldly, selfish, graceless, thankless, religionless old woman, writhing in pain and fear, and without her wig. Picture her to yourself, and ere you be old, learn to love and pray!\n\nSharp watched this graceless bedside with indomitable patience. Nothing escaped her; and, like a prudent steward, she found a use for everything. She told many a good story about Miss Crawley's illness in after days--stories which made the lady blush through her artificial carnations. During the illness she was never out of temper; always alert; she slept light, having a perfectly clear conscience; and could take that refreshment at almost any minute's warning. And so you saw very few traces of fatigue in her appearance. Her face might be a trifle paler, and the circles round her eyes a little blacker than usual; but whenever she came out from the sick-room she was always smiling, fresh, and neat, and looked as trim in her little dressing-gown and cap, as in her smartest evening suit.\n\nThe Captain thought so, and raved about her in uncouth convulsions. The barbed shaft of love had penetrated his dull hide. Six weeks--appropinquity--opportunity--had victimised him completely. He made a confidante of his aunt at the Rectory, of all persons in the world. She rallied him about it; she had perceived his folly; she warned him; she finished by owning that little Sharp was the most clever, droll, odd, good-natured, simple, kindly creature in England. Rawdon must not trifle with her affections, though--dear Miss Crawley would never pardon him for that; for she, too, was quite overcome by the little governess, and loved Sharp like a daughter. Rawdon must go away--go back to his regiment and naughty London, and not play with a poor artless girl's feelings.\n\nMany and many a time this good-natured lady, compassionating the forlorn life-guardsman's condition, gave him an opportunity of seeing Miss Sharp at the Rectory, and of walking home with her, as we have seen. When men of a certain sort, ladies, are in love, though they see the hook and the string, and the whole apparatus with which they are to be taken, they gorge the bait nevertheless--they must come to it--they must swallow it--and are presently struck and landed gasping. Rawdon saw there was a manifest intention on Mrs. Bute's part to captivate him with Rebecca. He was not very wise; but he was a man about town, and had seen several seasons. A light dawned upon his dusky soul, as he thought, through a speech of Mrs. Bute's.\n\n\"Mark my words, Rawdon,\" she said. \"You will have Miss Sharp one day for your relation.\"\n\n\"What relation--my cousin, hey, Mrs. Bute? James sweet on her, hey?\" inquired the waggish officer.\n\n\"More than that,\" Mrs. Bute said, with a flash from her black eyes.\n\n\"Not Pitt? He sha'n't have her. The sneak a'n't worthy of her. He's booked to Lady Jane Sheepshanks.\"\n\n\"You men perceive nothing. You silly, blind creature--if anything happens to Lady Crawley, Miss Sharp will be your mother-in-law; and that's what will happen.\"\n\nRawdon Crawley, Esquire, gave vent to a prodigious whistle, in token of astonishment at this announcement. He couldn't deny it. His father's evident liking for Miss Sharp had not escaped him. He knew the old gentleman's character well; and a more unscrupulous old--whyou--he did not conclude the sentence, but walked home, curling his mustachios, and convinced he had found a clue to Mrs. Bute's mystery.\n\n\"By Jove, it's too bad,\" thought Rawdon, \"too bad, by Jove! I do believe the woman wants the poor girl to be ruined, in order that she shouldn't come into the family as Lady Crawley.\"\n\nWhen he saw Rebecca alone, he rallied her about his father's attachment in his graceful way. She flung up her head scornfully, looked him full in the face, and said,\n\n\"Well, suppose he is fond of me. I know he is, and others too. You don't think I am afraid of him, Captain Crawley? You don't suppose I can't defend my own honour,\" said the little woman, looking as stately as a queen.\n\n\"Oh, ah, why--give you fair warning--look out, you know--that's all,\" said the mustachio-twiddler.\n\n\"You hint at something not honourable, then?\" said she, flashing out.\n\n\"O Gad--really--Miss Rebecca,\" the heavy dragoon interposed.\n\n\"Do you suppose I have no feeling of self-respect, because I am poor and friendless, and because rich people have none? Do you think, because I am a governess, I have not as much sense, and feeling, and good breeding as you gentlefolks in Hampshire? I'm a Montmorency. Do you suppose a Montmorency is not as good as a Crawley?\"\n\nWhen Miss Sharp was agitated, and alluded to her maternal relatives, she spoke with ever so slight a foreign accent, which gave a great charm to her clear ringing voice. \"No,\" she continued, kindling as she spoke to the Captain; \"I can endure poverty, but not shame--neglect, but not insult; and insult from--from you.\"\n\nHer feelings gave way, and she burst into tears.\n\n\"Hang it, Miss Sharp--Rebecca--by Jove--upon my soul, I wouldn't for a thousand pounds. Stop, Rebecca!\"\n\nShe was gone. She drove out with Miss Crawley that day. It was before the latter's illness. At dinner she was unusually brilliant and lively; but she would take no notice of the hints, or the nods, or the clumsy expostulations of the humiliated, infatuated guardsman. Skirmishes of this sort passed perpetually during the little campaign--tedious to relate, and similar in result. The Crawley heavy cavalry was maddened by defeat, and routed every day.\n\nIf the Baronet of Queen's Crawley had not had the fear of losing his sister's legacy before his eyes, he never would have permitted his dear girls to lose the educational blessings which their invaluable governess was conferring upon them. The old house at home seemed a desert without her, so useful and pleasant had Rebecca made herself there. Sir Pitt's letters were not copied and corrected; his books not made up; his household business and manifold schemes neglected, now that his little secretary was away. And it was easy to see how necessary such an amanuensis was to him, by the tenor and spelling of the numerous letters which he sent to her, entreating her and commanding her to return. Almost every day brought a frank from the Baronet, enclosing the most urgent prayers to Becky for her return, or conveying pathetic statements to Miss Crawley, regarding the neglected state of his daughters' education; of which documents Miss Crawley took very little heed.\n\nMiss Briggs was not formally dismissed, but her place as companion was a sinecure and a derision; and her company was the fat spaniel in the drawing-room, or occasionally the discontented Firkin in the housekeeper's closet. Nor though the old lady would by no means hear of Rebecca's departure, was the latter regularly installed in office in Park Lane. Like many wealthy people, it was Miss Crawley's habit to accept as much service as she could get from her inferiors; and good-naturedly to take leave of them when she no longer found them useful. Gratitude among certain rich folks is scarcely natural or to be thought of. They take needy people's services as their due. Nor have you, O poor parasite and humble hanger-on, much reason to complain! Your friendship for Dives is about as sincere as the return which it usually gets. It is money you love, and not the man; and were Croesus and his footman to change places you know, you poor rogue, who would have the benefit of your allegiance.\n\nAnd I am not sure that, in spite of Rebecca's simplicity and activity, and gentleness and untiring good humour, the shrewd old London lady, upon whom these treasures of friendship were lavished, had not a lurking suspicion all the while of her affectionate nurse and friend. It must have often crossed Miss Crawley's mind that nobody does anything for nothing. If she measured her own feeling towards the world, she must have been pretty well able to gauge those of the world towards herself; and perhaps she reflected that it is the ordinary lot of people to have no friends if they themselves care for nobody.\n\nWell, meanwhile Becky was the greatest comfort and convenience to her, and she gave her a couple of new gowns, and an old necklace and shawl, and showed her friendship by abusing all her intimate acquaintances to her new confidante (than which there can't be a more touching proof of regard), and meditated vaguely some great future benefit--to marry her perhaps to Clump, the apothecary, or to settle her in some advantageous way of life; or at any rate, to send her back to Queen's Crawley when she had done with her, and the full London season had begun.\n\nWhen Miss Crawley was convalescent and descended to the drawing-room, Becky sang to her, and otherwise amused her; when she was well enough to drive out, Becky accompanied her. And amongst the drives which they took, whither, of all places in the world, did Miss Crawley's admirable good-nature and friendship actually induce her to penetrate, but to Russell Square, Bloomsbury, and the house of John Sedley, Esquire.\n\nEre that event, many notes had passed, as may be imagined, between the two dear friends. During the months of Rebecca's stay in Hampshire, the eternal friendship had (must it be owned?) suffered considerable diminution, and grown so decrepit and feeble with old age as to threaten demise altogether. The fact is, both girls had their own real affairs to think of: Rebecca her advance with her employers--Amelia her own absorbing topic. When the two girls met, and flew into each other's arms with that impetuosity which distinguishes the behaviour of young ladies towards each other, Rebecca performed her part of the embrace with the most perfect briskness and energy. Poor little Amelia blushed as she kissed her friend, and thought she had been guilty of something very like coldness towards her.\n\nTheir first interview was but a very short one. Amelia was just ready to go out for a walk. Miss Crawley was waiting in her carriage below, her people wondering at the locality in which they found themselves, and gazing upon honest Sambo, the black footman of Bloomsbury, as one of the queer natives of the place. But when Amelia came down with her kind smiling looks (Rebecca must introduce her to her friend, Miss Crawley was longing to see her, and was too ill to leave her carriage)--when, I say, Amelia came down, the Park Lane shoulder-knot aristocracy wondered more and more that such a thing could come out of Bloomsbury; and Miss Crawley was fairly captivated by the sweet blushing face of the young lady who came forward so timidly and so gracefully to pay her respects to the protector of her friend.\n\n\"What a complexion, my dear! What a sweet voice!\" Miss Crawley said, as they drove away westward after the little interview. \"My dear Sharp, your young friend is charming. Send for her to Park Lane, do you hear?\" Miss Crawley had a good taste. She liked natural manners--a little timidity only set them off. She liked pretty faces near her; as she liked pretty pictures and nice china. She talked of Amelia with rapture half a dozen times that day. She mentioned her to Rawdon Crawley, who came dutifully to partake of his aunt's chicken.\n\nOf course, on this Rebecca instantly stated that Amelia was engaged to be married--to a Lieutenant Osborne--a very old flame.\n\n\"Is he a man in a line-regiment?\" Captain Crawley asked, remembering after an effort, as became a guardsman, the number of the regiment, the --th.\n\nRebecca thought that was the regiment. \"The Captain's name,\" she said, \"was Captain Dobbin.\"\n\n\"A lanky gawky fellow,\" said Crawley, \"tumbles over everybody. I know him; and Osborne's a goodish-looking fellow, with large black whiskers?\"\n\n\"Enormous,\" Miss Rebecca Sharp said, \"and enormously proud of them, I assure you.\"\n\nCaptain Rawdon Crawley burst into a horse-laugh by way of reply; and being pressed by the ladies to explain, did so when the explosion of hilarity was over. \"He fancies he can play at billiards,\" said he. \"I won two hundred of him at the Cocoa-Tree. HE play, the young flat! He'd have played for anything that day, but his friend Captain Dobbin carried him off, hang him!\"\n\n\"Rawdon, Rawdon, don't be so wicked,\" Miss Crawley remarked, highly pleased.\n\n\"Why, ma'am, of all the young fellows I've seen out of the line, I think this fellow's the greenest. Tarquin and Deuceace get what money they like out of him. He'd go to the deuce to be seen with a lord. He pays their dinners at Greenwich, and they invite the company.\"\n\n\"And very pretty company too, I dare say.\"\n\n\"Quite right, Miss Sharp. Right, as usual, Miss Sharp. Uncommon pretty company--haw, haw!\" and the Captain laughed more and more, thinking he had made a good joke.\n\n\"Rawdon, don't be naughty!\" his aunt exclaimed.\n\n\"Well, his father's a City man--immensely rich, they say. Hang those City fellows, they must bleed; and I've not done with him yet, I can tell you. Haw, haw!\"\n\n\"Fie, Captain Crawley; I shall warn Amelia. A gambling husband!\"\n\n\"Horrid, ain't he, hey?\" the Captain said with great solemnity; and then added, a sudden thought having struck him: \"Gad, I say, ma'am, we'll have him here.\"\n\n\"Is he a presentable sort of a person?\" the aunt inquired.\n\n\"Presentable?--oh, very well. You wouldn't see any difference,\" Captain Crawley answered. \"Do let's have him, when you begin to see a few people; and his whatdyecallem--his inamorato--eh, Miss Sharp; that's what you call it--comes. Gad, I'll write him a note, and have him; and I'll try if he can play piquet as well as billiards. Where does he live, Miss Sharp?\"\n\nMiss Sharp told Crawley the Lieutenant's town address; and a few days after this conversation, Lieutenant Osborne received a letter, in Captain Rawdon's schoolboy hand, and enclosing a note of invitation from Miss Crawley.\n\nRebecca despatched also an invitation to her darling Amelia, who, you may be sure, was ready enough to accept it when she heard that George was to be of the party. It was arranged that Amelia was to spend the morning with the ladies of Park Lane, where all were very kind to her. Rebecca patronised her with calm superiority: she was so much the cleverer of the two, and her friend so gentle and unassuming, that she always yielded when anybody chose to command, and so took Rebecca's orders with perfect meekness and good humour. Miss Crawley's graciousness was also remarkable. She continued her raptures about little Amelia, talked about her before her face as if she were a doll, or a servant, or a picture, and admired her with the most benevolent wonder possible. I admire that admiration which the genteel world sometimes extends to the commonalty. There is no more agreeable object in life than to see Mayfair folks condescending. Miss Crawley's prodigious benevolence rather fatigued poor little Amelia, and I am not sure that of the three ladies in Park Lane she did not find honest Miss Briggs the most agreeable. She sympathised with Briggs as with all neglected or gentle people: she wasn't what you call a woman of spirit.\n\nGeorge came to dinner--a repast en garcon with Captain Crawley.\n\nThe great family coach of the Osbornes transported him to Park Lane from Russell Square; where the young ladies, who were not themselves invited, and professed the greatest indifference at that slight, nevertheless looked at Sir Pitt Crawley's name in the baronetage; and learned everything which that work had to teach about the Crawley family and their pedigree, and the Binkies, their relatives, &c., &c. Rawdon Crawley received George Osborne with great frankness and graciousness: praised his play at billiards: asked him when he would have his revenge: was interested about Osborne's regiment: and would have proposed piquet to him that very evening, but Miss Crawley absolutely forbade any gambling in her house; so that the young Lieutenant's purse was not lightened by his gallant patron, for that day at least. However, they made an engagement for the next, somewhere: to look at a horse that Crawley had to sell, and to try him in the Park; and to dine together, and to pass the evening with some jolly fellows. \"That is, if you're not on duty to that pretty Miss Sedley,\" Crawley said, with a knowing wink. \"Monstrous nice girl, 'pon my honour, though, Osborne,\" he was good enough to add. \"Lots of tin, I suppose, eh?\"\n\nOsborne wasn't on duty; he would join Crawley with pleasure: and the latter, when they met the next day, praised his new friend's horsemanship--as he might with perfect honesty--and introduced him to three or four young men of the first fashion, whose acquaintance immensely elated the simple young officer.\n\n\"How's little Miss Sharp, by-the-bye?\" Osborne inquired of his friend over their wine, with a dandified air. \"Good-natured little girl that. Does she suit you well at Queen's Crawley? Miss Sedley liked her a good deal last year.\"\n\nCaptain Crawley looked savagely at the Lieutenant out of his little blue eyes, and watched him when he went up to resume his acquaintance with the fair governess. Her conduct must have relieved Crawley if there was any jealousy in the bosom of that life-guardsman.\n\nWhen the young men went upstairs, and after Osborne's introduction to Miss Crawley, he walked up to Rebecca with a patronising, easy swagger. He was going to be kind to her and protect her. He would even shake hands with her, as a friend of Amelia's; and saying, \"Ah, Miss Sharp! how-dy-doo?\" held out his left hand towards her, expecting that she would be quite confounded at the honour.\n\nMiss Sharp put out her right forefinger, and gave him a little nod, so cool and killing, that Rawdon Crawley, watching the operations from the other room, could hardly restrain his laughter as he saw the Lieutenant's entire discomfiture; the start he gave, the pause, and the perfect clumsiness with which he at length condescended to take the finger which was offered for his embrace.\n\n\"She'd beat the devil, by Jove!\" the Captain said, in a rapture; and the Lieutenant, by way of beginning the conversation, agreeably asked Rebecca how she liked her new place.\n\n\"My place?\" said Miss Sharp, coolly, \"how kind of you to remind me of it! It's a tolerably good place: the wages are pretty good--not so good as Miss Wirt's, I believe, with your sisters in Russell Square. How are those young ladies?--not that I ought to ask.\"\n\n\"Why not?\" Mr. Osborne said, amazed.\n\n\"Why, they never condescended to speak to me, or to ask me into their house, whilst I was staying with Amelia; but we poor governesses, you know, are used to slights of this sort.\"\n\n\"My dear Miss Sharp!\" Osborne ejaculated.\n\n\"At least in some families,\" Rebecca continued. \"You can't think what a difference there is though. We are not so wealthy in Hampshire as you lucky folks of the City. But then I am in a gentleman's family--good old English stock. I suppose you know Sir Pitt's father refused a peerage. And you see how I am treated. I am pretty comfortable. Indeed it is rather a good place. But how very good of you to inquire!\"\n\nOsborne was quite savage. The little governess patronised him and persiffled him until this young British Lion felt quite uneasy; nor could he muster sufficient presence of mind to find a pretext for backing out of this most delectable conversation.\n\n\"I thought you liked the City families pretty well,\" he said, haughtily.\n\n\"Last year you mean, when I was fresh from that horrid vulgar school? Of course I did. Doesn't every girl like to come home for the holidays? And how was I to know any better? But oh, Mr. Osborne, what a difference eighteen months' experience makes! eighteen months spent, pardon me for saying so, with gentlemen. As for dear Amelia, she, I grant you, is a pearl, and would be charming anywhere. There now, I see you are beginning to be in a good humour; but oh these queer odd City people! And Mr. Jos--how is that wonderful Mr. Joseph?\"\n\n\"It seems to me you didn't dislike that wonderful Mr. Joseph last year,\" Osborne said kindly.\n\n\"How severe of you! Well, entre nous, I didn't break my heart about him; yet if he had asked me to do what you mean by your looks (and very expressive and kind they are, too), I wouldn't have said no.\"\n\nMr. Osborne gave a look as much as to say, \"Indeed, how very obliging!\"\n\n\"What an honour to have had you for a brother-in-law, you are thinking? To be sister-in-law to George Osborne, Esquire, son of John Osborne, Esquire, son of--what was your grandpapa, Mr. Osborne? Well, don't be angry. You can't help your pedigree, and I quite agree with you that I would have married Mr. Joe Sedley; for could a poor penniless girl do better? Now you know the whole secret. I'm frank and open; considering all things, it was very kind of you to allude to the circumstance--very kind and polite. Amelia dear, Mr. Osborne and I were talking about your poor brother Joseph. How is he?\"\n\nThus was George utterly routed. Not that Rebecca was in the right; but she had managed most successfully to put him in the wrong. And he now shamefully fled, feeling, if he stayed another minute, that he would have been made to look foolish in the presence of Amelia.\n\nThough Rebecca had had the better of him, George was above the meanness of talebearing or revenge upon a lady--only he could not help cleverly confiding to Captain Crawley, next day, some notions of his regarding Miss Rebecca--that she was a sharp one, a dangerous one, a desperate flirt, &c.; in all of which opinions Crawley agreed laughingly, and with every one of which Miss Rebecca was made acquainted before twenty-four hours were over. They added to her original regard for Mr. Osborne. Her woman's instinct had told her that it was George who had interrupted the success of her first love-passage, and she esteemed him accordingly.\n\n\"I only just warn you,\" he said to Rawdon Crawley, with a knowing look--he had bought the horse, and lost some score of guineas after dinner, \"I just warn you--I know women, and counsel you to be on the look-out.\"\n\n\"Thank you, my boy,\" said Crawley, with a look of peculiar gratitude. \"You're wide awake, I see.\" And George went off, thinking Crawley was quite right.\n\nHe told Amelia of what he had done, and how he had counselled Rawdon Crawley--a devilish good, straightforward fellow--to be on his guard against that little sly, scheming Rebecca.\n\n\"Against whom?\" Amelia cried.\n\n\"Your friend the governess.--Don't look so astonished.\"\n\n\"O George, what have you done?\" Amelia said. For her woman's eyes, which Love had made sharp-sighted, had in one instant discovered a secret which was invisible to Miss Crawley, to poor virgin Briggs, and above all, to the stupid peepers of that young whiskered prig, Lieutenant Osborne.\n\nFor as Rebecca was shawling her in an upper apartment, where these two friends had an opportunity for a little of that secret talking and conspiring which form the delight of female life, Amelia, coming up to Rebecca, and taking her two little hands in hers, said, \"Rebecca, I see it all.\"\n\nRebecca kissed her.\n\nAnd regarding this delightful secret, not one syllable more was said by either of the young women. But it was destined to come out before long.\n\nSome short period after the above events, and Miss Rebecca Sharp still remaining at her patroness's house in Park Lane, one more hatchment might have been seen in Great Gaunt Street, figuring amongst the many which usually ornament that dismal quarter. It was over Sir Pitt Crawley's house; but it did not indicate the worthy baronet's demise. It was a feminine hatchment, and indeed a few years back had served as a funeral compliment to Sir Pitt's old mother, the late dowager Lady Crawley. Its period of service over, the hatchment had come down from the front of the house, and lived in retirement somewhere in the back premises of Sir Pitt's mansion. It reappeared now for poor Rose Dawson. Sir Pitt was a widower again. The arms quartered on the shield along with his own were not, to be sure, poor Rose's. She had no arms. But the cherubs painted on the scutcheon answered as well for her as for Sir Pitt's mother, and Resurgam was written under the coat, flanked by the Crawley Dove and Serpent. Arms and Hatchments, Resurgam.--Here is an opportunity for moralising!\n\nMr. Crawley had tended that otherwise friendless bedside. She went out of the world strengthened by such words and comfort as he could give her. For many years his was the only kindness she ever knew; the only friendship that solaced in any way that feeble, lonely soul. Her heart was dead long before her body. She had sold it to become Sir Pitt Crawley's wife. Mothers and daughters are making the same bargain every day in Vanity Fair.\n\nWhen the demise took place, her husband was in London attending to some of his innumerable schemes, and busy with his endless lawyers. He had found time, nevertheless, to call often in Park Lane, and to despatch many notes to Rebecca, entreating her, enjoining her, commanding her to return to her young pupils in the country, who were now utterly without companionship during their mother's illness. But Miss Crawley would not hear of her departure; for though there was no lady of fashion in London who would desert her friends more complacently as soon as she was tired of their society, and though few tired of them sooner, yet as long as her engoument lasted her attachment was prodigious, and she clung still with the greatest energy to Rebecca.\n\nThe news of Lady Crawley's death provoked no more grief or comment than might have been expected in Miss Crawley's family circle. \"I suppose I must put off my party for the 3rd,\" Miss Crawley said; and added, after a pause, \"I hope my brother will have the decency not to marry again.\" \"What a confounded rage Pitt will be in if he does,\" Rawdon remarked, with his usual regard for his elder brother. Rebecca said nothing. She seemed by far the gravest and most impressed of the family. She left the room before Rawdon went away that day; but they met by chance below, as he was going away after taking leave, and had a parley together.\n\nOn the morrow, as Rebecca was gazing from the window, she startled Miss Crawley, who was placidly occupied with a French novel, by crying out in an alarmed tone, \"Here's Sir Pitt, Ma'am!\" and the Baronet's knock followed this announcement.\n\n\"My dear, I can't see him. I won't see him. Tell Bowls not at home, or go downstairs and say I'm too ill to receive any one. My nerves really won't bear my brother at this moment,\" cried out Miss Crawley, and resumed the novel.\n\n\"She's too ill to see you, sir,\" Rebecca said, tripping down to Sir Pitt, who was preparing to ascend.\n\n\"So much the better,\" Sir Pitt answered. \"I want to see YOU, Miss Becky. Come along a me into the parlour,\" and they entered that apartment together.\n\n\"I wawnt you back at Queen's Crawley, Miss,\" the baronet said, fixing his eyes upon her, and taking off his black gloves and his hat with its great crape hat-band. His eyes had such a strange look, and fixed upon her so steadfastly, that Rebecca Sharp began almost to tremble.\n\n\"I hope to come soon,\" she said in a low voice, \"as soon as Miss Crawley is better--and return to--to the dear children.\"\n\n\"You've said so these three months, Becky,\" replied Sir Pitt, \"and still you go hanging on to my sister, who'll fling you off like an old shoe, when she's wore you out. I tell you I want you. I'm going back to the Vuneral. Will you come back? Yes or no?\"\n\n\"I daren't--I don't think--it would be right--to be alone--with you, sir,\" Becky said, seemingly in great agitation.\n\n\"I say agin, I want you,\" Sir Pitt said, thumping the table. \"I can't git on without you. I didn't see what it was till you went away. The house all goes wrong. It's not the same place. All my accounts has got muddled agin. You MUST come back. Do come back. Dear Becky, do come.\"\n\n\"Come--as what, sir?\" Rebecca gasped out.\n\n\"Come as Lady Crawley, if you like,\" the Baronet said, grasping his crape hat. \"There! will that zatusfy you? Come back and be my wife. Your vit vor't. Birth be hanged. You're as good a lady as ever I see. You've got more brains in your little vinger than any baronet's wife in the county. Will you come? Yes or no?\"\n\n\"Oh, Sir Pitt!\" Rebecca said, very much moved.\n\n\"Say yes, Becky,\" Sir Pitt continued. \"I'm an old man, but a good'n. I'm good for twenty years. I'll make you happy, zee if I don't. You shall do what you like; spend what you like; and 'ave it all your own way. I'll make you a zettlement. I'll do everything reglar. Look year!\" and the old man fell down on his knees and leered at her like a satyr.\n\nRebecca started back a picture of consternation. In the course of this history we have never seen her lose her presence of mind; but she did now, and wept some of the most genuine tears that ever fell from her eyes.\n\n\"Oh, Sir Pitt!\" she said. \"Oh, sir--I--I'm married ALREADY.\"\n\nEvery reader of a sentimental turn (and we desire no other) must have been pleased with the tableau with which the last act of our little drama concluded; for what can be prettier than an image of Love on his knees before Beauty?\n\nBut when Love heard that awful confession from Beauty that she was married already, he bounced up from his attitude of humility on the carpet, uttering exclamations which caused poor little Beauty to be more frightened than she was when she made her avowal. \"Married; you're joking,\" the Baronet cried, after the first explosion of rage and wonder. \"You're making vun of me, Becky. Who'd ever go to marry you without a shilling to your vortune?\"\n\n\"Married! married!\" Rebecca said, in an agony of tears--her voice choking with emotion, her handkerchief up to her ready eyes, fainting against the mantelpiece a figure of woe fit to melt the most obdurate heart. \"O Sir Pitt, dear Sir Pitt, do not think me ungrateful for all your goodness to me. It is only your generosity that has extorted my secret.\"\n\n\"Generosity be hanged!\" Sir Pitt roared out. \"Who is it tu, then, you're married? Where was it?\"\n\n\"Let me come back with you to the country, sir! Let me watch over you as faithfully as ever! Don't, don't separate me from dear Queen's Crawley!\"\n\n\"The feller has left you, has he?\" the Baronet said, beginning, as he fancied, to comprehend. \"Well, Becky--come back if you like. You can't eat your cake and have it. Any ways I made you a vair offer. Coom back as governess--you shall have it all your own way.\" She held out one hand. She cried fit to break her heart; her ringlets fell over her face, and over the marble mantelpiece where she laid it.\n\n\"So the rascal ran off, eh?\" Sir Pitt said, with a hideous attempt at consolation. \"Never mind, Becky, I'LL take care of 'ee.\"\n\n\"Oh, sir! it would be the pride of my life to go back to Queen's Crawley, and take care of the children, and of you as formerly, when you said you were pleased with the services of your little Rebecca. When I think of what you have just offered me, my heart fills with gratitude indeed it does. I can't be your wife, sir; let me--let me be your daughter.\" Saying which, Rebecca went down on HER knees in a most tragical way, and, taking Sir Pitt's horny black hand between her own two (which were very pretty and white, and as soft as satin), looked up in his face with an expression of exquisite pathos and confidence, when--when the door opened, and Miss Crawley sailed in.\n\nMrs. Firkin and Miss Briggs, who happened by chance to be at the parlour door soon after the Baronet and Rebecca entered the apartment, had also seen accidentally, through the keyhole, the old gentleman prostrate before the governess, and had heard the generous proposal which he made her. It was scarcely out of his mouth when Mrs. Firkin and Miss Briggs had streamed up the stairs, had rushed into the drawing-room where Miss Crawley was reading the French novel, and had given that old lady the astounding intelligence that Sir Pitt was on his knees, proposing to Miss Sharp. And if you calculate the time for the above dialogue to take place--the time for Briggs and Firkin to fly to the drawing-room--the time for Miss Crawley to be astonished, and to drop her volume of Pigault le Brun--and the time for her to come downstairs--you will see how exactly accurate this history is, and how Miss Crawley must have appeared at the very instant when Rebecca had assumed the attitude of humility.\n\n\"It is the lady on the ground, and not the gentleman,\" Miss Crawley said, with a look and voice of great scorn. \"They told me that YOU were on your knees, Sir Pitt: do kneel once more, and let me see this pretty couple!\"\n\n\"I have thanked Sir Pitt Crawley, Ma'am,\" Rebecca said, rising, \"and have told him that--that I never can become Lady Crawley.\"\n\n\"Refused him!\" Miss Crawley said, more bewildered than ever. Briggs and Firkin at the door opened the eyes of astonishment and the lips of wonder.\n\n\"Yes--refused,\" Rebecca continued, with a sad, tearful voice.\n\n\"And am I to credit my ears that you absolutely proposed to her, Sir Pitt?\" the old lady asked.\n\n\"Ees,\" said the Baronet, \"I did.\"\n\n\"And she refused you as she says?\"\n\n\"Ees,\" Sir Pitt said, his features on a broad grin.\n\n\"It does not seem to break your heart at any rate,\" Miss Crawley remarked.\n\n\"Nawt a bit,\" answered Sir Pitt, with a coolness and good-humour which set Miss Crawley almost mad with bewilderment. That an old gentleman of station should fall on his knees to a penniless governess, and burst out laughing because she refused to marry him--that a penniless governess should refuse a Baronet with four thousand a year--these were mysteries which Miss Crawley could never comprehend. It surpassed any complications of intrigue in her favourite Pigault le Brun.\n\n\"I'm glad you think it good sport, brother,\" she continued, groping wildly through this amazement.\n\n\"Vamous,\" said Sir Pitt. \"Who'd ha' thought it! what a sly little devil! what a little fox it waws!\" he muttered to himself, chuckling with pleasure.\n\n\"Who'd have thought what?\" cries Miss Crawley, stamping with her foot. \"Pray, Miss Sharp, are you waiting for the Prince Regent's divorce, that you don't think our family good enough for you?\"\n\n\"My attitude,\" Rebecca said, \"when you came in, ma'am, did not look as if I despised such an honour as this good--this noble man has deigned to offer me. Do you think I have no heart? Have you all loved me, and been so kind to the poor orphan--deserted--girl, and am I to feel nothing? O my friends! O my benefactors! may not my love, my life, my duty, try to repay the confidence you have shown me? Do you grudge me even gratitude, Miss Crawley? It is too much--my heart is too full\"; and she sank down in a chair so pathetically, that most of the audience present were perfectly melted with her sadness.\n\n\"Whether you marry me or not, you're a good little girl, Becky, and I'm your vriend, mind,\" said Sir Pitt, and putting on his crape-bound hat, he walked away--greatly to Rebecca's relief; for it was evident that her secret was unrevealed to Miss Crawley, and she had the advantage of a brief reprieve.\n\nPutting her handkerchief to her eyes, and nodding away honest Briggs, who would have followed her upstairs, she went up to her apartment; while Briggs and Miss Crawley, in a high state of excitement, remained to discuss the strange event, and Firkin, not less moved, dived down into the kitchen regions, and talked of it with all the male and female company there. And so impressed was Mrs. Firkin with the news, that she thought proper to write off by that very night's post, \"with her humble duty to Mrs. Bute Crawley and the family at the Rectory, and Sir Pitt has been and proposed for to marry Miss Sharp, wherein she has refused him, to the wonder of all.\"\n\nThe two ladies in the dining-room (where worthy Miss Briggs was delighted to be admitted once more to confidential conversation with her patroness) wondered to their hearts' content at Sir Pitt's offer, and Rebecca's refusal; Briggs very acutely suggesting that there must have been some obstacle in the shape of a previous attachment, otherwise no young woman in her senses would ever have refused so advantageous a proposal.\n\n\"You would have accepted it yourself, wouldn't you, Briggs?\" Miss Crawley said, kindly.\n\n\"Would it not be a privilege to be Miss Crawley's sister?\" Briggs replied, with meek evasion.\n\n\"Well, Becky would have made a good Lady Crawley, after all,\" Miss Crawley remarked (who was mollified by the girl's refusal, and very liberal and generous now there was no call for her sacrifices). \"She has brains in plenty (much more wit in her little finger than you have, my poor dear Briggs, in all your head). Her manners are excellent, now I have formed her. She is a Montmorency, Briggs, and blood is something, though I despise it for my part; and she would have held her own amongst those pompous stupid Hampshire people much better than that unfortunate ironmonger's daughter.\"\n\nBriggs coincided as usual, and the \"previous attachment\" was then discussed in conjectures. \"You poor friendless creatures are always having some foolish tendre,\" Miss Crawley said. \"You yourself, you know, were in love with a writing-master (don't cry, Briggs--you're always crying, and it won't bring him to life again), and I suppose this unfortunate Becky has been silly and sentimental too--some apothecary, or house-steward, or painter, or young curate, or something of that sort.\"\n\n\"Poor thing! poor thing!\" says Briggs (who was thinking of twenty-four years back, and that hectic young writing-master whose lock of yellow hair, and whose letters, beautiful in their illegibility, she cherished in her old desk upstairs). \"Poor thing, poor thing!\" says Briggs. Once more she was a fresh-cheeked lass of eighteen; she was at evening church, and the hectic writing-master and she were quavering out of the same psalm-book.\n\n\"After such conduct on Rebecca's part,\" Miss Crawley said enthusiastically, \"our family should do something. Find out who is the objet, Briggs. I'll set him up in a shop; or order my portrait of him, you know; or speak to my cousin, the Bishop and I'll doter Becky, and we'll have a wedding, Briggs, and you shall make the breakfast, and be a bridesmaid.\"\n\nBriggs declared that it would be delightful, and vowed that her dear Miss Crawley was always kind and generous, and went up to Rebecca's bedroom to console her and prattle about the offer, and the refusal, and the cause thereof; and to hint at the generous intentions of Miss Crawley, and to find out who was the gentleman that had the mastery of Miss Sharp's heart.\n\nRebecca was very kind, very affectionate and affected--responded to Briggs's offer of tenderness with grateful fervour--owned there was a secret attachment--a delicious mystery--what a pity Miss Briggs had not remained half a minute longer at the keyhole! Rebecca might, perhaps, have told more: but five minutes after Miss Briggs's arrival in Rebecca's apartment, Miss Crawley actually made her appearance there--an unheard-of honour--her impatience had overcome her; she could not wait for the tardy operations of her ambassadress: so she came in person, and ordered Briggs out of the room. And expressing her approval of Rebecca's conduct, she asked particulars of the interview, and the previous transactions which had brought about the astonishing offer of Sir Pitt.\n\nRebecca said she had long had some notion of the partiality with which Sir Pitt honoured her (for he was in the habit of making his feelings known in a very frank and unreserved manner) but, not to mention private reasons with which she would not for the present trouble Miss Crawley, Sir Pitt's age, station, and habits were such as to render a marriage quite impossible; and could a woman with any feeling of self-respect and any decency listen to proposals at such a moment, when the funeral of the lover's deceased wife had not actually taken place?\n\n\"Nonsense, my dear, you would never have refused him had there not been some one else in the case,\" Miss Crawley said, coming to her point at once. \"Tell me the private reasons; what are the private reasons? There is some one; who is it that has touched your heart?\"\n\nRebecca cast down her eyes, and owned there was. \"You have guessed right, dear lady,\" she said, with a sweet simple faltering voice. \"You wonder at one so poor and friendless having an attachment, don't you? I have never heard that poverty was any safeguard against it. I wish it were.\"\n\n\"My poor dear child,\" cried Miss Crawley, who was always quite ready to be sentimental, \"is our passion unrequited, then? Are we pining in secret? Tell me all, and let me console you.\"\n\n\"I wish you could, dear Madam,\" Rebecca said in the same tearful tone. \"Indeed, indeed, I need it.\" And she laid her head upon Miss Crawley's shoulder and wept there so naturally that the old lady, surprised into sympathy, embraced her with an almost maternal kindness, uttered many soothing protests of regard and affection for her, vowed that she loved her as a daughter, and would do everything in her power to serve her. \"And now who is it, my dear? Is it that pretty Miss Sedley's brother? You said something about an affair with him. I'll ask him here, my dear. And you shall have him: indeed you shall.\"\n\n\"Don't ask me now,\" Rebecca said. \"You shall know all soon. Indeed you shall. Dear kind Miss Crawley--dear friend, may I say so?\"\n\n\"That you may, my child,\" the old lady replied, kissing her.\n\n\"I can't tell you now,\" sobbed out Rebecca, \"I am very miserable. But O! love me always--promise you will love me always.\" And in the midst of mutual tears--for the emotions of the younger woman had awakened the sympathies of the elder--this promise was solemnly given by Miss Crawley, who left her little protege, blessing and admiring her as a dear, artless, tender-hearted, affectionate, incomprehensible creature.\n\nAnd now she was left alone to think over the sudden and wonderful events of the day, and of what had been and what might have been. What think you were the private feelings of Miss, no (begging her pardon) of Mrs. Rebecca? If, a few pages back, the present writer claimed the privilege of peeping into Miss Amelia Sedley's bedroom, and understanding with the omniscience of the novelist all the gentle pains and passions which were tossing upon that innocent pillow, why should he not declare himself to be Rebecca's confidante too, master of her secrets, and seal-keeper of that young woman's conscience?\n\nWell, then, in the first place, Rebecca gave way to some very sincere and touching regrets that a piece of marvellous good fortune should have been so near her, and she actually obliged to decline it. In this natural emotion every properly regulated mind will certainly share. What good mother is there that would not commiserate a penniless spinster, who might have been my lady, and have shared four thousand a year? What well-bred young person is there in all Vanity Fair, who will not feel for a hard-working, ingenious, meritorious girl, who gets such an honourable, advantageous, provoking offer, just at the very moment when it is out of her power to accept it? I am sure our friend Becky's disappointment deserves and will command every sympathy.\n\nI remember one night being in the Fair myself, at an evening party. I observed old Miss Toady there also present, single out for her special attentions and flattery little Mrs. Briefless, the barrister's wife, who is of a good family certainly, but, as we all know, is as poor as poor can be.\n\nWhat, I asked in my own mind, can cause this obsequiousness on the part of Miss Toady; has Briefless got a county court, or has his wife had a fortune left her? Miss Toady explained presently, with that simplicity which distinguishes all her conduct. \"You know,\" she said, \"Mrs Briefless is granddaughter of Sir John Redhand, who is so ill at Cheltenham that he can't last six months. Mrs. Briefless's papa succeeds; so you see she will be a baronet's daughter.\" And Toady asked Briefless and his wife to dinner the very next week.\n\nIf the mere chance of becoming a baronet's daughter can procure a lady such homage in the world, surely, surely we may respect the agonies of a young woman who has lost the opportunity of becoming a baronet's wife. Who would have dreamed of Lady Crawley dying so soon? She was one of those sickly women that might have lasted these ten years--Rebecca thought to herself, in all the woes of repentance--and I might have been my lady! I might have led that old man whither I would. I might have thanked Mrs. Bute for her patronage, and Mr. Pitt for his insufferable condescension. I would have had the town-house newly furnished and decorated. I would have had the handsomest carriage in London, and a box at the opera; and I would have been presented next season. All this might have been; and now--now all was doubt and mystery.\n\nBut Rebecca was a young lady of too much resolution and energy of character to permit herself much useless and unseemly sorrow for the irrevocable past; so, having devoted only the proper portion of regret to it, she wisely turned her whole attention towards the future, which was now vastly more important to her. And she surveyed her position, and its hopes, doubts, and chances.\n\nIn the first place, she was MARRIED--that was a great fact. Sir Pitt knew it. She was not so much surprised into the avowal, as induced to make it by a sudden calculation. It must have come some day: and why not now as at a later period? He who would have married her himself must at least be silent with regard to her marriage. How Miss Crawley would bear the news--was the great question. Misgivings Rebecca had; but she remembered all Miss Crawley had said; the old lady's avowed contempt for birth; her daring liberal opinions; her general romantic propensities; her almost doting attachment to her nephew, and her repeatedly expressed fondness for Rebecca herself. She is so fond of him, Rebecca thought, that she will forgive him anything: she is so used to me that I don't think she could be comfortable without me: when the eclaircissement comes there will be a scene, and hysterics, and a great quarrel, and then a great reconciliation. At all events, what use was there in delaying? the die was thrown, and now or to-morrow the issue must be the same. And so, resolved that Miss Crawley should have the news, the young person debated in her mind as to the best means of conveying it to her; and whether she should face the storm that must come, or fly and avoid it until its first fury was blown over. In this state of meditation she wrote the following letter:\n\nDearest Friend,\n\nThe great crisis which we have debated about so often is COME. Half of my secret is known, and I have thought and thought, until I am quite sure that now is the time to reveal THE WHOLE OF THE MYSTERY. Sir Pitt came to me this morning, and made--what do you think?--A DECLARATION IN FORM. Think of that! Poor little me. I might have been Lady Crawley. How pleased Mrs. Bute would have been: and ma tante if I had taken precedence of her! I might have been somebody's mamma, instead of--O, I tremble, I tremble, when I think how soon we must tell all!\n\nSir Pitt knows I am married, and not knowing to whom, is not very much displeased as yet. Ma tante is ACTUALLY ANGRY that I should have refused him. But she is all kindness and graciousness. She condescends to say I would have made him a good wife; and vows that she will be a mother to your little Rebecca. She will be shaken when she first hears the news. But need we fear anything beyond a momentary anger? I think not: I AM SURE not. She dotes upon you so (you naughty, good-for-nothing man), that she would pardon you ANYTHING: and, indeed, I believe, the next place in her heart is mine: and that she would be miserable without me. Dearest! something TELLS ME we shall conquer. You shall leave that odious regiment: quit gaming, racing, and BE A GOOD BOY; and we shall all live in Park Lane, and ma tante shall leave us all her money.\n\nI shall try and walk to-morrow at 3 in the usual place. If Miss B. accompanies me, you must come to dinner, and bring an answer, and put it in the third volume of Porteus's Sermons. But, at all events, come to your own\n\nR.\n\nTo Miss Eliza Styles, At Mr. Barnet's, Saddler, Knightsbridge.\n\nAnd I trust there is no reader of this little story who has not discernment enough to perceive that the Miss Eliza Styles (an old schoolfellow, Rebecca said, with whom she had resumed an active correspondence of late, and who used to fetch these letters from the saddler's), wore brass spurs, and large curling mustachios, and was indeed no other than Captain Rawdon Crawley.\n\nThe Letter on the Pincushion\nHow they were married is not of the slightest consequence to anybody. What is to hinder a Captain who is a major, and a young lady who is of age, from purchasing a licence, and uniting themselves at any church in this town? Who needs to be told, that if a woman has a will she will assuredly find a way?--My belief is that one day, when Miss Sharp had gone to pass the forenoon with her dear friend Miss Amelia Sedley in Russell Square, a lady very like her might have been seen entering a church in the City, in company with a gentleman with dyed mustachios, who, after a quarter of an hour's interval, escorted her back to the hackney-coach in waiting, and that this was a quiet bridal party.\n\nAnd who on earth, after the daily experience we have, can question the probability of a gentleman marrying anybody? How many of the wise and learned have married their cooks? Did not Lord Eldon himself, the most prudent of men, make a runaway match? Were not Achilles and Ajax both in love with their servant maids? And are we to expect a heavy dragoon with strong desires and small brains, who had never controlled a passion in his life, to become prudent all of a sudden, and to refuse to pay any price for an indulgence to which he had a mind? If people only made prudent marriages, what a stop to population there would be!\n\nIt seems to me, for my part, that Mr. Rawdon's marriage was one of the honestest actions which we shall have to record in any portion of that gentleman's biography which has to do with the present history. No one will say it is unmanly to be captivated by a woman, or, being captivated, to marry her; and the admiration, the delight, the passion, the wonder, the unbounded confidence, and frantic adoration with which, by degrees, this big warrior got to regard the little Rebecca, were feelings which the ladies at least will pronounce were not altogether discreditable to him. When she sang, every note thrilled in his dull soul, and tingled through his huge frame. When she spoke, he brought all the force of his brains to listen and wonder. If she was jocular, he used to revolve her jokes in his mind, and explode over them half an hour afterwards in the street, to the surprise of the groom in the tilbury by his side, or the comrade riding with him in Rotten Row. Her words were oracles to him, her smallest actions marked by an infallible grace and wisdom. \"How she sings,--how she paints,\" thought he. \"How she rode that kicking mare at Queen's Crawley!\" And he would say to her in confidential moments, \"By Jove, Beck, you're fit to be Commander-in-Chief, or Archbishop of Canterbury, by Jove.\" Is his case a rare one? and don't we see every day in the world many an honest Hercules at the apron-strings of Omphale, and great whiskered Samsons prostrate in Delilah's lap?\n\nWhen, then, Becky told him that the great crisis was near, and the time for action had arrived, Rawdon expressed himself as ready to act under her orders, as he would be to charge with his troop at the command of his colonel. There was no need for him to put his letter into the third volume of Porteus. Rebecca easily found a means to get rid of Briggs, her companion, and met her faithful friend in \"the usual place\" on the next day. She had thought over matters at night, and communicated to Rawdon the result of her determinations. He agreed, of course, to everything; was quite sure that it was all right: that what she proposed was best; that Miss Crawley would infallibly relent, or \"come round,\" as he said, after a time. Had Rebecca's resolutions been entirely different, he would have followed them as implicitly. \"You have head enough for both of us, Beck,\" said he. \"You're sure to get us out of the scrape. I never saw your equal, and I've met with some clippers in my time too.\" And with this simple confession of faith, the love-stricken dragoon left her to execute his part of the project which she had formed for the pair.\n\nIt consisted simply in the hiring of quiet lodgings at Brompton, or in the neighbourhood of the barracks, for Captain and Mrs. Crawley. For Rebecca had determined, and very prudently, we think, to fly. Rawdon was only too happy at her resolve; he had been entreating her to take this measure any time for weeks past. He pranced off to engage the lodgings with all the impetuosity of love. He agreed to pay two guineas a week so readily, that the landlady regretted she had asked him so little. He ordered in a piano, and half a nursery-house full of flowers: and a heap of good things. As for shawls, kid gloves, silk stockings, gold French watches, bracelets and perfumery, he sent them in with the profusion of blind love and unbounded credit. And having relieved his mind by this outpouring of generosity, he went and dined nervously at the club, waiting until the great moment of his life should come.\n\nThe occurrences of the previous day; the admirable conduct of Rebecca in refusing an offer so advantageous to her, the secret unhappiness preying upon her, the sweetness and silence with which she bore her affliction, made Miss Crawley much more tender than usual. An event of this nature, a marriage, or a refusal, or a proposal, thrills through a whole household of women, and sets all their hysterical sympathies at work. As an observer of human nature, I regularly frequent St. George's, Hanover Square, during the genteel marriage season; and though I have never seen the bridegroom's male friends give way to tears, or the beadles and officiating clergy any way affected, yet it is not at all uncommon to see women who are not in the least concerned in the operations going on--old ladies who are long past marrying, stout middle-aged females with plenty of sons and daughters, let alone pretty young creatures in pink bonnets, who are on their promotion, and may naturally take an interest in the ceremony--I say it is quite common to see the women present piping, sobbing, sniffling; hiding their little faces in their little useless pocket-handkerchiefs; and heaving, old and young, with emotion. When my friend, the fashionable John Pimlico, married the lovely Lady Belgravia Green Parker, the excitement was so general that even the little snuffy old pew-opener who let me into the seat was in tears. And wherefore? I inquired of my own soul: she was not going to be married.\n\nMiss Crawley and Briggs in a word, after the affair of Sir Pitt, indulged in the utmost luxury of sentiment, and Rebecca became an object of the most tender interest to them. In her absence Miss Crawley solaced herself with the most sentimental of the novels in her library. Little Sharp, with her secret griefs, was the heroine of the day.\n\nThat night Rebecca sang more sweetly and talked more pleasantly than she had ever been heard to do in Park Lane. She twined herself round the heart of Miss Crawley. She spoke lightly and laughingly of Sir Pitt's proposal, ridiculed it as the foolish fancy of an old man; and her eyes filled with tears, and Briggs's heart with unutterable pangs of defeat, as she said she desired no other lot than to remain for ever with her dear benefactress. \"My dear little creature,\" the old lady said, \"I don't intend to let you stir for years, that you may depend upon it. As for going back to that odious brother of mine after what has passed, it is out of the question. Here you stay with me and Briggs. Briggs wants to go to see her relations very often. Briggs, you may go when you like. But as for you, my dear, you must stay and take care of the old woman.\"\n\nIf Rawdon Crawley had been then and there present, instead of being at the club nervously drinking claret, the pair might have gone down on their knees before the old spinster, avowed all, and been forgiven in a twinkling. But that good chance was denied to the young couple, doubtless in order that this story might be written, in which numbers of their wonderful adventures are narrated--adventures which could never have occurred to them if they had been housed and sheltered under the comfortable uninteresting forgiveness of Miss Crawley.\n\nUnder Mrs. Firkin's orders, in the Park Lane establishment, was a young woman from Hampshire, whose business it was, among other duties, to knock at Miss Sharp's door with that jug of hot water which Firkin would rather have perished than have presented to the intruder. This girl, bred on the family estate, had a brother in Captain Crawley's troop, and if the truth were known, I daresay it would come out that she was aware of certain arrangements, which have a great deal to do with this history. At any rate she purchased a yellow shawl, a pair of green boots, and a light blue hat with a red feather with three guineas which Rebecca gave her, and as little Sharp was by no means too liberal with her money, no doubt it was for services rendered that Betty Martin was so bribed.\n\nOn the second day after Sir Pitt Crawley's offer to Miss Sharp, the sun rose as usual, and at the usual hour Betty Martin, the upstairs maid, knocked at the door of the governess's bedchamber.\n\nNo answer was returned, and she knocked again. Silence was still uninterrupted; and Betty, with the hot water, opened the door and entered the chamber.\n\nThe little white dimity bed was as smooth and trim as on the day previous, when Betty's own hands had helped to make it. Two little trunks were corded in one end of the room; and on the table before the window--on the pincushion--the great fat pincushion lined with pink inside, and twilled like a lady's nightcap--lay a letter. It had been reposing there probably all night.\n\nBetty advanced towards it on tiptoe, as if she were afraid to awake it--looked at it, and round the room, with an air of great wonder and satisfaction; took up the letter, and grinned intensely as she turned it round and over, and finally carried it into Miss Briggs's room below.\n\nHow could Betty tell that the letter was for Miss Briggs, I should like to know? All the schooling Betty had had was at Mrs. Bute Crawley's Sunday school, and she could no more read writing than Hebrew.\n\n\"La, Miss Briggs,\" the girl exclaimed, \"O, Miss, something must have happened--there's nobody in Miss Sharp's room; the bed ain't been slep in, and she've run away, and left this letter for you, Miss.\"\n\n\"WHAT!\" cries Briggs, dropping her comb, the thin wisp of faded hair falling over her shoulders; \"an elopement! Miss Sharp a fugitive! What, what is this?\" and she eagerly broke the neat seal, and, as they say, \"devoured the contents\" of the letter addressed to her.\n\nDear Miss Briggs [the refugee wrote], the kindest heart in the world, as yours is, will pity and sympathise with me and excuse me. With tears, and prayers, and blessings, I leave the home where the poor orphan has ever met with kindness and affection. Claims even superior to those of my benefactress call me hence. I go to my duty--to my HUSBAND. Yes, I am married. My husband COMMANDS me to seek the HUMBLE HOME which we call ours. Dearest Miss Briggs, break the news as your delicate sympathy will know how to do it--to my dear, my beloved friend and benefactress. Tell her, ere I went, I shed tears on her dear pillow--that pillow that I have so often soothed in sickness--that I long AGAIN to watch--Oh, with what joy shall I return to dear Park Lane! How I tremble for the answer which is to SEAL MY FATE! When Sir Pitt deigned to offer me his hand, an honour of which my beloved Miss Crawley said I was DESERVING (my blessings go with her for judging the poor orphan worthy to be HER SISTER!) I told Sir Pitt that I was already A WIFE. Even he forgave me. But my courage failed me, when I should have told him all--that I could not be his wife, for I WAS HIS DAUGHTER! I am wedded to the best and most generous of men--Miss Crawley's Rawdon is MY Rawdon. At his COMMAND I open my lips, and follow him to our humble home, as I would THROUGH THE WORLD. O, my excellent and kind friend, intercede with my Rawdon's beloved aunt for him and the poor girl to whom all HIS NOBLE RACE have shown such UNPARALLELED AFFECTION. Ask Miss Crawley to receive HER CHILDREN. I can say no more, but blessings, blessings on all in the dear house I leave, prays\n\nYour affectionate and GRATEFUL Rebecca Crawley. Midnight.\n\nJust as Briggs had finished reading this affecting and interesting document, which reinstated her in her position as first confidante of Miss Crawley, Mrs. Firkin entered the room. \"Here's Mrs. Bute Crawley just arrived by the mail from Hampshire, and wants some tea; will you come down and make breakfast, Miss?\"\n\nAnd to the surprise of Firkin, clasping her dressing-gown around her, the wisp of hair floating dishevelled behind her, the little curl-papers still sticking in bunches round her forehead, Briggs sailed down to Mrs. Bute with the letter in her hand containing the wonderful news.\n\n\"Oh, Mrs. Firkin,\" gasped Betty, \"sech a business. Miss Sharp have a gone and run away with the Capting, and they're off to Gretney Green!\" We would devote a chapter to describe the emotions of Mrs. Firkin, did not the passions of her mistresses occupy our genteeler muse.\n\nWhen Mrs. Bute Crawley, numbed with midnight travelling, and warming herself at the newly crackling parlour fire, heard from Miss Briggs the intelligence of the clandestine marriage, she declared it was quite providential that she should have arrived at such a time to assist poor dear Miss Crawley in supporting the shock--that Rebecca was an artful little hussy of whom she had always had her suspicions; and that as for Rawdon Crawley, she never could account for his aunt's infatuation regarding him, and had long considered him a profligate, lost, and abandoned being. And this awful conduct, Mrs. Bute said, will have at least this good effect, it will open poor dear Miss Crawley's eyes to the real character of this wicked man. Then Mrs. Bute had a comfortable hot toast and tea; and as there was a vacant room in the house now, there was no need for her to remain at the Gloster Coffee House where the Portsmouth mail had set her down, and whence she ordered Mr. Bowls's aide-de-camp the footman to bring away her trunks.\n\nMiss Crawley, be it known, did not leave her room until near noon--taking chocolate in bed in the morning, while Becky Sharp read the Morning Post to her, or otherwise amusing herself or dawdling. The conspirators below agreed that they would spare the dear lady's feelings until she appeared in her drawing-room: meanwhile it was announced to her that Mrs. Bute Crawley had come up from Hampshire by the mail, was staying at the Gloster, sent her love to Miss Crawley, and asked for breakfast with Miss Briggs. The arrival of Mrs. Bute, which would not have caused any extreme delight at another period, was hailed with pleasure now; Miss Crawley being pleased at the notion of a gossip with her sister-in-law regarding the late Lady Crawley, the funeral arrangements pending, and Sir Pitt's abrupt proposal to Rebecca.\n\nIt was not until the old lady was fairly ensconced in her usual arm-chair in the drawing-room, and the preliminary embraces and inquiries had taken place between the ladies, that the conspirators thought it advisable to submit her to the operation. Who has not admired the artifices and delicate approaches with which women \"prepare\" their friends for bad news? Miss Crawley's two friends made such an apparatus of mystery before they broke the intelligence to her, that they worked her up to the necessary degree of doubt and alarm.\n\n\"And she refused Sir Pitt, my dear, dear Miss Crawley, prepare yourself for it,\" Mrs. Bute said, \"because--because she couldn't help herself.\"\n\n\"Of course there was a reason,\" Miss Crawley answered. \"She liked somebody else. I told Briggs so yesterday.\"\n\n\"LIKES somebody else!\" Briggs gasped. \"O my dear friend, she is married already.\"\n\n\"Married already,\" Mrs. Bute chimed in; and both sate with clasped hands looking from each other at their victim.\n\n\"Send her to me, the instant she comes in. The little sly wretch: how dared she not tell me?\" cried out Miss Crawley.\n\n\"She won't come in soon. Prepare yourself, dear friend--she's gone out for a long time--she's--she's gone altogether.\"\n\n\"Gracious goodness, and who's to make my chocolate? Send for her and have her back; I desire that she come back,\" the old lady said.\n\n\"She decamped last night, Ma'am,\" cried Mrs. Bute.\n\n\"She left a letter for me,\" Briggs exclaimed. \"She's married to--\"\n\n\"Prepare her, for heaven's sake. Don't torture her, my dear Miss Briggs.\"\n\n\"She's married to whom?\" cries the spinster in a nervous fury.\n\n\"To--to a relation of--\"\n\n\"She refused Sir Pitt,\" cried the victim. \"Speak at once. Don't drive me mad.\"\n\n\"O Ma'am--prepare her, Miss Briggs--she's married to Rawdon Crawley.\"\n\n\"Rawdon married Rebecca--governess--nobod-- Get out of my house, you fool, you idiot--you stupid old Briggs--how dare you? You're in the plot--you made him marry, thinking that I'd leave my money from him--you did, Martha,\" the poor old lady screamed in hysteric sentences.\n\n\"I, Ma'am, ask a member of this family to marry a drawing-master's daughter?\"\n\n\"Her mother was a Montmorency,\" cried out the old lady, pulling at the bell with all her might.\n\n\"Her mother was an opera girl, and she has been on the stage or worse herself,\" said Mrs. Bute.\n\nMiss Crawley gave a final scream, and fell back in a faint. They were forced to take her back to the room which she had just quitted. One fit of hysterics succeeded another. The doctor was sent for--the apothecary arrived. Mrs. Bute took up the post of nurse by her bedside. \"Her relations ought to be round about her,\" that amiable woman said.\n\nShe had scarcely been carried up to her room, when a new person arrived to whom it was also necessary to break the news. This was Sir Pitt. \"Where's Becky?\" he said, coming in. \"Where's her traps? She's coming with me to Queen's Crawley.\"\n\n\"Have you not heard the astonishing intelligence regarding her surreptitious union?\" Briggs asked.\n\n\"What's that to me?\" Sir Pitt asked. \"I know she's married. That makes no odds. Tell her to come down at once, and not keep me.\"\n\n\"Are you not aware, sir,\" Miss Briggs asked, \"that she has left our roof, to the dismay of Miss Crawley, who is nearly killed by the intelligence of Captain Rawdon's union with her?\"\n\nWhen Sir Pitt Crawley heard that Rebecca was married to his son, he broke out into a fury of language, which it would do no good to repeat in this place, as indeed it sent poor Briggs shuddering out of the room; and with her we will shut the door upon the figure of the frenzied old man, wild with hatred and insane with baffled desire.\n\nOne day after he went to Queen's Crawley, he burst like a madman into the room she had used when there--dashed open her boxes with his foot, and flung about her papers, clothes, and other relics. Miss Horrocks, the butler's daughter, took some of them. The children dressed themselves and acted plays in the others. It was but a few days after the poor mother had gone to her lonely burying-place; and was laid, unwept and disregarded, in a vault full of strangers.\n\n\"Suppose the old lady doesn't come to,\" Rawdon said to his little wife, as they sate together in the snug little Brompton lodgings. She had been trying the new piano all the morning. The new gloves fitted her to a nicety; the new shawls became her wonderfully; the new rings glittered on her little hands, and the new watch ticked at her waist; \"suppose she don't come round, eh, Becky?\"\n\n\"I'LL make your fortune,\" she said; and Delilah patted Samson's cheek.\n\n\"You can do anything,\" he said, kissing the little hand. \"By Jove you can; and we'll drive down to the Star and Garter, and dine, by Jove.\"\n\nHow Captain Dobbin Bought a Piano\nIf there is any exhibition in all Vanity Fair which Satire and Sentiment can visit arm in arm together; where you light on the strangest contrasts laughable and tearful: where you may be gentle and pathetic, or savage and cynical with perfect propriety: it is at one of those public assemblies, a crowd of which are advertised every day in the last page of the Times newspaper, and over which the late Mr. George Robins used to preside with so much dignity. There are very few London people, as I fancy, who have not attended at these meetings, and all with a taste for moralizing must have thought, with a sensation and interest not a little startling and queer, of the day when their turn shall come too, and Mr. Hammerdown will sell by the orders of Diogenes' assignees, or will be instructed by the executors, to offer to public competition, the library, furniture, plate, wardrobe, and choice cellar of wines of Epicurus deceased.\n\nEven with the most selfish disposition, the Vanity Fairian, as he witnesses this sordid part of the obsequies of a departed friend, can't but feel some sympathies and regret. My Lord Dives's remains are in the family vault: the statuaries are cutting an inscription veraciously commemorating his virtues, and the sorrows of his heir, who is disposing of his goods. What guest at Dives's table can pass the familiar house without a sigh?--the familiar house of which the lights used to shine so cheerfully at seven o'clock, of which the hall-doors opened so readily, of which the obsequious servants, as you passed up the comfortable stair, sounded your name from landing to landing, until it reached the apartment where jolly old Dives welcomed his friends! What a number of them he had; and what a noble way of entertaining them. How witty people used to be here who were morose when they got out of the door; and how courteous and friendly men who slandered and hated each other everywhere else! He was pompous, but with such a cook what would one not swallow? he was rather dull, perhaps, but would not such wine make any conversation pleasant? We must get some of his Burgundy at any price, the mourners cry at his club. \"I got this box at old Dives's sale,\" Pincher says, handing it round, \"one of Louis XV's mistresses--pretty thing, is it not?--sweet miniature,\" and they talk of the way in which young Dives is dissipating his fortune.\n\nHow changed the house is, though! The front is patched over with bills, setting forth the particulars of the furniture in staring capitals. They have hung a shred of carpet out of an upstairs window--a half dozen of porters are lounging on the dirty steps--the hall swarms with dingy guests of oriental countenance, who thrust printed cards into your hand, and offer to bid. Old women and amateurs have invaded the upper apartments, pinching the bed-curtains, poking into the feathers, shampooing the mattresses, and clapping the wardrobe drawers to and fro. Enterprising young housekeepers are measuring the looking-glasses and hangings to see if they will suit the new menage (Snob will brag for years that he has purchased this or that at Dives's sale), and Mr. Hammerdown is sitting on the great mahogany dining-tables, in the dining-room below, waving the ivory hammer, and employing all the artifices of eloquence, enthusiasm, entreaty, reason, despair; shouting to his people; satirizing Mr. Davids for his sluggishness; inspiriting Mr. Moss into action; imploring, commanding, bellowing, until down comes the hammer like fate, and we pass to the next lot. O Dives, who would ever have thought, as we sat round the broad table sparkling with plate and spotless linen, to have seen such a dish at the head of it as that roaring auctioneer?\n\nIt was rather late in the sale. The excellent drawing-room furniture by the best makers; the rare and famous wines selected, regardless of cost, and with the well-known taste of the purchaser; the rich and complete set of family plate had been sold on the previous days. Certain of the best wines (which all had a great character among amateurs in the neighbourhood) had been purchased for his master, who knew them very well, by the butler of our friend John Osborne, Esquire, of Russell Square. A small portion of the most useful articles of the plate had been bought by some young stockbrokers from the City. And now the public being invited to the purchase of minor objects, it happened that the orator on the table was expatiating on the merits of a picture, which he sought to recommend to his audience: it was by no means so select or numerous a company as had attended the previous days of the auction.\n\n\"No. 369,\" roared Mr. Hammerdown. \"Portrait of a gentleman on an elephant. Who'll bid for the gentleman on the elephant? Lift up the picture, Blowman, and let the company examine this lot.\" A long, pale, military-looking gentleman, seated demurely at the mahogany table, could not help grinning as this valuable lot was shown by Mr. Blowman. \"Turn the elephant to the Captain, Blowman. What shall we say, sir, for the elephant?\" but the Captain, blushing in a very hurried and discomfited manner, turned away his head.\n\n\"Shall we say twenty guineas for this work of art?--fifteen, five, name your own price. The gentleman without the elephant is worth five pound.\"\n\n\"I wonder it ain't come down with him,\" said a professional wag, \"he's anyhow a precious big one\"; at which (for the elephant-rider was represented as of a very stout figure) there was a general giggle in the room.\n\n\"Don't be trying to deprecate the value of the lot, Mr. Moss,\" Mr. Hammerdown said; \"let the company examine it as a work of art--the attitude of the gallant animal quite according to natur'; the gentleman in a nankeen jacket, his gun in his hand, is going to the chase; in the distance a banyhann tree and a pagody, most likely resemblances of some interesting spot in our famous Eastern possessions. How much for this lot? Come, gentlemen, don't keep me here all day.\"\n\nSome one bid five shillings, at which the military gentleman looked towards the quarter from which this splendid offer had come, and there saw another officer with a young lady on his arm, who both appeared to be highly amused with the scene, and to whom, finally, this lot was knocked down for half a guinea. He at the table looked more surprised and discomposed than ever when he spied this pair, and his head sank into his military collar, and he turned his back upon them, so as to avoid them altogether.\n\nOf all the other articles which Mr. Hammerdown had the honour to offer for public competition that day it is not our purpose to make mention, save of one only, a little square piano, which came down from the upper regions of the house (the state grand piano having been disposed of previously); this the young lady tried with a rapid and skilful hand (making the officer blush and start again), and for it, when its turn came, her agent began to bid.\n\nBut there was an opposition here. The Hebrew aide-de-camp in the service of the officer at the table bid against the Hebrew gentleman employed by the elephant purchasers, and a brisk battle ensued over this little piano, the combatants being greatly encouraged by Mr. Hammerdown.\n\nAt last, when the competition had been prolonged for some time, the elephant captain and lady desisted from the race; and the hammer coming down, the auctioneer said:--\"Mr. Lewis, twenty-five,\" and Mr. Lewis's chief thus became the proprietor of the little square piano. Having effected the purchase, he sate up as if he was greatly relieved, and the unsuccessful competitors catching a glimpse of him at this moment, the lady said to her friend,\n\n\"Why, Rawdon, it's Captain Dobbin.\"\n\nI suppose Becky was discontented with the new piano her husband had hired for her, or perhaps the proprietors of that instrument had fetched it away, declining farther credit, or perhaps she had a particular attachment for the one which she had just tried to purchase, recollecting it in old days, when she used to play upon it, in the little sitting-room of our dear Amelia Sedley.\n\nThe sale was at the old house in Russell Square, where we passed some evenings together at the beginning of this story. Good old John Sedley was a ruined man. His name had been proclaimed as a defaulter on the Stock Exchange, and his bankruptcy and commercial extermination had followed. Mr. Osborne's butler came to buy some of the famous port wine to transfer to the cellars over the way. As for one dozen well-manufactured silver spoons and forks at per oz., and one dozen dessert ditto ditto, there were three young stockbrokers (Messrs. Dale, Spiggot, and Dale, of Threadneedle Street, indeed), who, having had dealings with the old man, and kindnesses from him in days when he was kind to everybody with whom he dealt, sent this little spar out of the wreck with their love to good Mrs. Sedley; and with respect to the piano, as it had been Amelia's, and as she might miss it and want one now, and as Captain William Dobbin could no more play upon it than he could dance on the tight rope, it is probable that he did not purchase the instrument for his own use.\n\nIn a word, it arrived that evening at a wonderful small cottage in a street leading from the Fulham Road--one of those streets which have the finest romantic names--(this was called St. Adelaide Villas, Anna-Maria Road West), where the houses look like baby-houses; where the people, looking out of the first-floor windows, must infallibly, as you think, sit with their feet in the parlours; where the shrubs in the little gardens in front bloom with a perennial display of little children's pinafores, little red socks, caps, &c. (polyandria polygynia); whence you hear the sound of jingling spinets and women singing; where little porter pots hang on the railings sunning themselves; whither of evenings you see City clerks padding wearily: here it was that Mr. Clapp, the clerk of Mr. Sedley, had his domicile, and in this asylum the good old gentleman hid his head with his wife and daughter when the crash came.\n\nJos Sedley had acted as a man of his disposition would, when the announcement of the family misfortune reached him. He did not come to London, but he wrote to his mother to draw upon his agents for whatever money was wanted, so that his kind broken-spirited old parents had no present poverty to fear. This done, Jos went on at the boarding-house at Cheltenham pretty much as before. He drove his curricle; he drank his claret; he played his rubber; he told his Indian stories, and the Irish widow consoled and flattered him as usual. His present of money, needful as it was, made little impression on his parents; and I have heard Amelia say that the first day on which she saw her father lift up his head after the failure was on the receipt of the packet of forks and spoons with the young stockbrokers' love, over which he burst out crying like a child, being greatly more affected than even his wife, to whom the present was addressed. Edward Dale, the junior of the house, who purchased the spoons for the firm, was, in fact, very sweet upon Amelia, and offered for her in spite of all. He married Miss Louisa Cutts (daughter of Higham and Cutts, the eminent cornfactors) with a handsome fortune in 1820; and is now living in splendour, and with a numerous family, at his elegant villa, Muswell Hill. But we must not let the recollections of this good fellow cause us to diverge from the principal history.\n\nI hope the reader has much too good an opinion of Captain and Mrs. Crawley to suppose that they ever would have dreamed of paying a visit to so remote a district as Bloomsbury, if they thought the family whom they proposed to honour with a visit were not merely out of fashion, but out of money, and could be serviceable to them in no possible manner. Rebecca was entirely surprised at the sight of the comfortable old house where she had met with no small kindness, ransacked by brokers and bargainers, and its quiet family treasures given up to public desecration and plunder. A month after her flight, she had bethought her of Amelia, and Rawdon, with a horse-laugh, had expressed a perfect willingness to see young George Osborne again. \"He's a very agreeable acquaintance, Beck,\" the wag added. \"I'd like to sell him another horse, Beck. I'd like to play a few more games at billiards with him. He'd be what I call useful just now, Mrs. C.--ha, ha!\" by which sort of speech it is not to be supposed that Rawdon Crawley had a deliberate desire to cheat Mr. Osborne at play, but only wished to take that fair advantage of him which almost every sporting gentleman in Vanity Fair considers to be his due from his neighbour.\n\nThe old aunt was long in \"coming-to.\" A month had elapsed. Rawdon was denied the door by Mr. Bowls; his servants could not get a lodgment in the house at Park Lane; his letters were sent back unopened. Miss Crawley never stirred out--she was unwell--and Mrs. Bute remained still and never left her. Crawley and his wife both of them augured evil from the continued presence of Mrs. Bute.\n\n\"Gad, I begin to perceive now why she was always bringing us together at Queen's Crawley,\" Rawdon said.\n\n\"What an artful little woman!\" ejaculated Rebecca.\n\n\"Well, I don't regret it, if you don't,\" the Captain cried, still in an amorous rapture with his wife, who rewarded him with a kiss by way of reply, and was indeed not a little gratified by the generous confidence of her husband.\n\n\"If he had but a little more brains,\" she thought to herself, \"I might make something of him\"; but she never let him perceive the opinion she had of him; listened with indefatigable complacency to his stories of the stable and the mess; laughed at all his jokes; felt the greatest interest in Jack Spatterdash, whose cab-horse had come down, and Bob Martingale, who had been taken up in a gambling-house, and Tom Cinqbars, who was going to ride the steeplechase. When he came home she was alert and happy: when he went out she pressed him to go: when he stayed at home, she played and sang for him, made him good drinks, superintended his dinner, warmed his slippers, and steeped his soul in comfort. The best of women (I have heard my grandmother say) are hypocrites. We don't know how much they hide from us: how watchful they are when they seem most artless and confidential: how often those frank smiles which they wear so easily, are traps to cajole or elude or disarm--I don't mean in your mere coquettes, but your domestic models, and paragons of female virtue. Who has not seen a woman hide the dulness of a stupid husband, or coax the fury of a savage one? We accept this amiable slavishness, and praise a woman for it: we call this pretty treachery truth. A good housewife is of necessity a humbug; and Cornelia's husband was hoodwinked, as Potiphar was--only in a different way.\n\nBy these attentions, that veteran rake, Rawdon Crawley, found himself converted into a very happy and submissive married man. His former haunts knew him not. They asked about him once or twice at his clubs, but did not miss him much: in those booths of Vanity Fair people seldom do miss each other. His secluded wife ever smiling and cheerful, his little comfortable lodgings, snug meals, and homely evenings, had all the charms of novelty and secrecy. The marriage was not yet declared to the world, or published in the Morning Post. All his creditors would have come rushing on him in a body, had they known that he was united to a woman without fortune. \"My relations won't cry fie upon me,\" Becky said, with rather a bitter laugh; and she was quite contented to wait until the old aunt should be reconciled, before she claimed her place in society. So she lived at Brompton, and meanwhile saw no one, or only those few of her husband's male companions who were admitted into her little dining-room. These were all charmed with her. The little dinners, the laughing and chatting, the music afterwards, delighted all who participated in these enjoyments. Major Martingale never thought about asking to see the marriage licence, Captain Cinqbars was perfectly enchanted with her skill in making punch. And young Lieutenant Spatterdash (who was fond of piquet, and whom Crawley would often invite) was evidently and quickly smitten by Mrs. Crawley; but her own circumspection and modesty never forsook her for a moment, and Crawley's reputation as a fire-eating and jealous warrior was a further and complete defence to his little wife.\n\nThere are gentlemen of very good blood and fashion in this city, who never have entered a lady's drawing-room; so that though Rawdon Crawley's marriage might be talked about in his county, where, of course, Mrs. Bute had spread the news, in London it was doubted, or not heeded, or not talked about at all. He lived comfortably on credit. He had a large capital of debts, which laid out judiciously, will carry a man along for many years, and on which certain men about town contrive to live a hundred times better than even men with ready money can do. Indeed who is there that walks London streets, but can point out a half-dozen of men riding by him splendidly, while he is on foot, courted by fashion, bowed into their carriages by tradesmen, denying themselves nothing, and living on who knows what? We see Jack Thriftless prancing in the park, or darting in his brougham down Pall Mall: we eat his dinners served on his miraculous plate. \"How did this begin,\" we say, \"or where will it end?\" \"My dear fellow,\" I heard Jack once say, \"I owe money in every capital in Europe.\" The end must come some day, but in the meantime Jack thrives as much as ever; people are glad enough to shake him by the hand, ignore the little dark stories that are whispered every now and then against him, and pronounce him a good-natured, jovial, reckless fellow.\n\nTruth obliges us to confess that Rebecca had married a gentleman of this order. Everything was plentiful in his house but ready money, of which their menage pretty early felt the want; and reading the Gazette one day, and coming upon the announcement of \"Lieutenant G. Osborne to be Captain by purchase, vice Smith, who exchanges,\" Rawdon uttered that sentiment regarding Amelia's lover, which ended in the visit to Russell Square.\n\nWhen Rawdon and his wife wished to communicate with Captain Dobbin at the sale, and to know particulars of the catastrophe which had befallen Rebecca's old acquaintances, the Captain had vanished; and such information as they got was from a stray porter or broker at the auction.\n\n\"Look at them with their hooked beaks,\" Becky said, getting into the buggy, her picture under her arm, in great glee. \"They're like vultures after a battle.\"\n\n\"Don't know. Never was in action, my dear. Ask Martingale; he was in Spain, aide-de-camp to General Blazes.\"\n\n\"He was a very kind old man, Mr. Sedley,\" Rebecca said; \"I'm really sorry he's gone wrong.\"\n\n\"O stockbrokers--bankrupts--used to it, you know,\" Rawdon replied, cutting a fly off the horse's ear.\n\n\"I wish we could have afforded some of the plate, Rawdon,\" the wife continued sentimentally. \"Five-and-twenty guineas was monstrously dear for that little piano. We chose it at Broadwood's for Amelia, when she came from school. It only cost five-and-thirty then.\"\n\n\"What-d'-ye-call'em--'Osborne,' will cry off now, I suppose, since the family is smashed. How cut up your pretty little friend will be; hey, Becky?\"\n\n\"I daresay she'll recover it,\" Becky said with a smile--and they drove on and talked about something else.\n\nWho Played on the Piano Captain Dobbin Bought\nOur surprised story now finds itself for a moment among very famous events and personages, and hanging on to the skirts of history. When the eagles of Napoleon Bonaparte, the Corsican upstart, were flying from Provence, where they had perched after a brief sojourn in Elba, and from steeple to steeple until they reached the towers of Notre Dame, I wonder whether the Imperial birds had any eye for a little corner of the parish of Bloomsbury, London, which you might have thought so quiet, that even the whirring and flapping of those mighty wings would pass unobserved there?\n\n\"Napoleon has landed at Cannes.\" Such news might create a panic at Vienna, and cause Russia to drop his cards, and take Prussia into a corner, and Talleyrand and Metternich to wag their heads together, while Prince Hardenberg, and even the present Marquis of Londonderry, were puzzled; but how was this intelligence to affect a young lady in Russell Square, before whose door the watchman sang the hours when she was asleep: who, if she strolled in the square, was guarded there by the railings and the beadle: who, if she walked ever so short a distance to buy a ribbon in Southampton Row, was followed by Black Sambo with an enormous cane: who was always cared for, dressed, put to bed, and watched over by ever so many guardian angels, with and without wages? Bon Dieu, I say, is it not hard that the fateful rush of the great Imperial struggle can't take place without affecting a poor little harmless girl of eighteen, who is occupied in billing and cooing, or working muslin collars in Russell Square? You too, kindly, homely flower!--is the great roaring war tempest coming to sweep you down, here, although cowering under the shelter of Holborn? Yes; Napoleon is flinging his last stake, and poor little Emmy Sedley's happiness forms, somehow, part of it.\n\nIn the first place, her father's fortune was swept down with that fatal news. All his speculations had of late gone wrong with the luckless old gentleman. Ventures had failed; merchants had broken; funds had risen when he calculated they would fall. What need to particularize? If success is rare and slow, everybody knows how quick and easy ruin is. Old Sedley had kept his own sad counsel. Everything seemed to go on as usual in the quiet, opulent house; the good-natured mistress pursuing, quite unsuspiciously, her bustling idleness, and daily easy avocations; the daughter absorbed still in one selfish, tender thought, and quite regardless of all the world besides, when that final crash came, under which the worthy family fell.\n\nOne night Mrs. Sedley was writing cards for a party; the Osbornes had given one, and she must not be behindhand; John Sedley, who had come home very late from the City, sate silent at the chimney side, while his wife was prattling to him; Emmy had gone up to her room ailing and low-spirited. \"She's not happy,\" the mother went on. \"George Osborne neglects her. I've no patience with the airs of those people. The girls have not been in the house these three weeks; and George has been twice in town without coming. Edward Dale saw him at the Opera. Edward would marry her I'm sure: and there's Captain Dobbin who, I think, would--only I hate all army men. Such a dandy as George has become. With his military airs, indeed! We must show some folks that we're as good as they. Only give Edward Dale any encouragement, and you'll see. We must have a party, Mr. S. Why don't you speak, John? Shall I say Tuesday fortnight? Why don't you answer? Good God, John, what has happened?\"\n\nJohn Sedley sprang up out of his chair to meet his wife, who ran to him. He seized her in his arms, and said with a hasty voice, \"We're ruined, Mary. We've got the world to begin over again, dear. It's best that you should know all, and at once.\" As he spoke, he trembled in every limb, and almost fell. He thought the news would have overpowered his wife--his wife, to whom he had never said a hard word. But it was he that was the most moved, sudden as the shock was to her. When he sank back into his seat, it was the wife that took the office of consoler. She took his trembling hand, and kissed it, and put it round her neck: she called him her John--her dear John--her old man--her kind old man; she poured out a hundred words of incoherent love and tenderness; her faithful voice and simple caresses wrought this sad heart up to an inexpressible delight and anguish, and cheered and solaced his over-burdened soul.\n\nOnly once in the course of the long night as they sate together, and poor Sedley opened his pent-up soul, and told the story of his losses and embarrassments--the treason of some of his oldest friends, the manly kindness of some, from whom he never could have expected it--in a general confession--only once did the faithful wife give way to emotion.\n\n\"My God, my God, it will break Emmy's heart,\" she said.\n\nThe father had forgotten the poor girl. She was lying, awake and unhappy, overhead. In the midst of friends, home, and kind parents, she was alone. To how many people can any one tell all? Who will be open where there is no sympathy, or has call to speak to those who never can understand? Our gentle Amelia was thus solitary. She had no confidante, so to speak, ever since she had anything to confide. She could not tell the old mother her doubts and cares; the would-be sisters seemed every day more strange to her. And she had misgivings and fears which she dared not acknowledge to herself, though she was always secretly brooding over them.\n\nHer heart tried to persist in asserting that George Osborne was worthy and faithful to her, though she knew otherwise. How many a thing had she said, and got no echo from him. How many suspicions of selfishness and indifference had she to encounter and obstinately overcome. To whom could the poor little martyr tell these daily struggles and tortures? Her hero himself only half understood her. She did not dare to own that the man she loved was her inferior; or to feel that she had given her heart away too soon. Given once, the pure bashful maiden was too modest, too tender, too trustful, too weak, too much woman to recall it. We are Turks with the affections of our women; and have made them subscribe to our doctrine too. We let their bodies go abroad liberally enough, with smiles and ringlets and pink bonnets to disguise them instead of veils and yakmaks. But their souls must be seen by only one man, and they obey not unwillingly, and consent to remain at home as our slaves--ministering to us and doing drudgery for us.\n\nSo imprisoned and tortured was this gentle little heart, when in the month of March, Anno Domini 1815, Napoleon landed at Cannes, and Louis XVIII fled, and all Europe was in alarm, and the funds fell, and old John Sedley was ruined.\n\nWe are not going to follow the worthy old stockbroker through those last pangs and agonies of ruin through which he passed before his commercial demise befell. They declared him at the Stock Exchange; he was absent from his house of business: his bills were protested: his act of bankruptcy formal. The house and furniture of Russell Square were seized and sold up, and he and his family were thrust away, as we have seen, to hide their heads where they might.\n\nJohn Sedley had not the heart to review the domestic establishment who have appeared now and anon in our pages and of whom he was now forced by poverty to take leave. The wages of those worthy people were discharged with that punctuality which men frequently show who only owe in great sums--they were sorry to leave good places--but they did not break their hearts at parting from their adored master and mistress. Amelia's maid was profuse in condolences, but went off quite resigned to better herself in a genteeler quarter of the town. Black Sambo, with the infatuation of his profession, determined on setting up a public-house. Honest old Mrs. Blenkinsop indeed, who had seen the birth of Jos and Amelia, and the wooing of John Sedley and his wife, was for staying by them without wages, having amassed a considerable sum in their service: and she accompanied the fallen people into their new and humble place of refuge, where she tended them and grumbled against them for a while.\n\nOf all Sedley's opponents in his debates with his creditors which now ensued, and harassed the feelings of the humiliated old gentleman so severely, that in six weeks he oldened more than he had done for fifteen years before--the most determined and obstinate seemed to be John Osborne, his old friend and neighbour--John Osborne, whom he had set up in life--who was under a hundred obligations to him--and whose son was to marry Sedley's daughter. Any one of these circumstances would account for the bitterness of Osborne's opposition.\n\nWhen one man has been under very remarkable obligations to another, with whom he subsequently quarrels, a common sense of decency, as it were, makes of the former a much severer enemy than a mere stranger would be. To account for your own hard-heartedness and ingratitude in such a case, you are bound to prove the other party's crime. It is not that you are selfish, brutal, and angry at the failure of a speculation--no, no--it is that your partner has led you into it by the basest treachery and with the most sinister motives. From a mere sense of consistency, a persecutor is bound to show that the fallen man is a villain--otherwise he, the persecutor, is a wretch himself.\n\nAnd as a general rule, which may make all creditors who are inclined to be severe pretty comfortable in their minds, no men embarrassed are altogether honest, very likely. They conceal something; they exaggerate chances of good luck; hide away the real state of affairs; say that things are flourishing when they are hopeless, keep a smiling face (a dreary smile it is) upon the verge of bankruptcy--are ready to lay hold of any pretext for delay or of any money, so as to stave off the inevitable ruin a few days longer. \"Down with such dishonesty,\" says the creditor in triumph, and reviles his sinking enemy. \"You fool, why do you catch at a straw?\" calm good sense says to the man that is drowning. \"You villain, why do you shrink from plunging into the irretrievable Gazette?\" says prosperity to the poor devil battling in that black gulf. Who has not remarked the readiness with which the closest of friends and honestest of men suspect and accuse each other of cheating when they fall out on money matters? Everybody does it. Everybody is right, I suppose, and the world is a rogue.\n\nThen Osborne had the intolerable sense of former benefits to goad and irritate him: these are always a cause of hostility aggravated. Finally, he had to break off the match between Sedley's daughter and his son; and as it had gone very far indeed, and as the poor girl's happiness and perhaps character were compromised, it was necessary to show the strongest reasons for the rupture, and for John Osborne to prove John Sedley to be a very bad character indeed.\n\nAt the meetings of creditors, then, he comported himself with a savageness and scorn towards Sedley, which almost succeeded in breaking the heart of that ruined bankrupt man. On George's intercourse with Amelia he put an instant veto--menacing the youth with maledictions if he broke his commands, and vilipending the poor innocent girl as the basest and most artful of vixens. One of the great conditions of anger and hatred is, that you must tell and believe lies against the hated object, in order, as we said, to be consistent.\n\nWhen the great crash came--the announcement of ruin, and the departure from Russell Square, and the declaration that all was over between her and George--all over between her and love, her and happiness, her and faith in the world--a brutal letter from John Osborne told her in a few curt lines that her father's conduct had been of such a nature that all engagements between the families were at an end--when the final award came, it did not shock her so much as her parents, as her mother rather expected (for John Sedley himself was entirely prostrate in the ruins of his own affairs and shattered honour). Amelia took the news very palely and calmly. It was only the confirmation of the dark presages which had long gone before. It was the mere reading of the sentence--of the crime she had long ago been guilty--the crime of loving wrongly, too violently, against reason. She told no more of her thoughts now than she had before. She seemed scarcely more unhappy now when convinced all hope was over, than before when she felt but dared not confess that it was gone. So she changed from the large house to the small one without any mark or difference; remained in her little room for the most part; pined silently; and died away day by day. I do not mean to say that all females are so. My dear Miss Bullock, I do not think your heart would break in this way. You are a strong-minded young woman with proper principles. I do not venture to say that mine would; it has suffered, and, it must be confessed, survived. But there are some souls thus gently constituted, thus frail, and delicate, and tender.\n\nWhenever old John Sedley thought of the affair between George and Amelia, or alluded to it, it was with bitterness almost as great as Mr. Osborne himself had shown. He cursed Osborne and his family as heartless, wicked, and ungrateful. No power on earth, he swore, would induce him to marry his daughter to the son of such a villain, and he ordered Emmy to banish George from her mind, and to return all the presents and letters which she had ever had from him.\n\nShe promised acquiescence, and tried to obey. She put up the two or three trinkets: and, as for the letters, she drew them out of the place where she kept them; and read them over--as if she did not know them by heart already: but she could not part with them. That effort was too much for her; she placed them back in her bosom again--as you have seen a woman nurse a child that is dead. Young Amelia felt that she would die or lose her senses outright, if torn away from this last consolation. How she used to blush and lighten up when those letters came! How she used to trip away with a beating heart, so that she might read unseen! If they were cold, yet how perversely this fond little soul interpreted them into warmth. If they were short or selfish, what excuses she found for the writer!\n\nIt was over these few worthless papers that she brooded and brooded. She lived in her past life--every letter seemed to recall some circumstance of it. How well she remembered them all! His looks and tones, his dress, what he said and how--these relics and remembrances of dead affection were all that were left her in the world. And the business of her life, was--to watch the corpse of Love.\n\nTo death she looked with inexpressible longing. Then, she thought, I shall always be able to follow him. I am not praising her conduct or setting her up as a model for Miss Bullock to imitate. Miss B. knows how to regulate her feelings better than this poor little creature. Miss B. would never have committed herself as that imprudent Amelia had done; pledged her love irretrievably; confessed her heart away, and got back nothing--only a brittle promise which was snapt and worthless in a moment. A long engagement is a partnership which one party is free to keep or to break, but which involves all the capital of the other.\n\nBe cautious then, young ladies; be wary how you engage. Be shy of loving frankly; never tell all you feel, or (a better way still), feel very little. See the consequences of being prematurely honest and confiding, and mistrust yourselves and everybody. Get yourselves married as they do in France, where the lawyers are the bridesmaids and confidantes. At any rate, never have any feelings which may make you uncomfortable, or make any promises which you cannot at any required moment command and withdraw. That is the way to get on, and be respected, and have a virtuous character in Vanity Fair.\n\nIf Amelia could have heard the comments regarding her which were made in the circle from which her father's ruin had just driven her, she would have seen what her own crimes were, and how entirely her character was jeopardised. Such criminal imprudence Mrs. Smith never knew of; such horrid familiarities Mrs. Brown had always condemned, and the end might be a warning to HER daughters. \"Captain Osborne, of course, could not marry a bankrupt's daughter,\" the Misses Dobbin said. \"It was quite enough to have been swindled by the father. As for that little Amelia, her folly had really passed all--\"\n\n\"All what?\" Captain Dobbin roared out. \"Haven't they been engaged ever since they were children? Wasn't it as good as a marriage? Dare any soul on earth breathe a word against the sweetest, the purest, the tenderest, the most angelical of young women?\"\n\n\"La, William, don't be so highty-tighty with US. We're not men. We can't fight you,\" Miss Jane said. \"We've said nothing against Miss Sedley: but that her conduct throughout was MOST IMPRUDENT, not to call it by any worse name; and that her parents are people who certainly merit their misfortunes.\"\n\n\"Hadn't you better, now that Miss Sedley is free, propose for her yourself, William?\" Miss Ann asked sarcastically. \"It would be a most eligible family connection. He! he!\"\n\n\"I marry her!\" Dobbin said, blushing very much, and talking quick. \"If you are so ready, young ladies, to chop and change, do you suppose that she is? Laugh and sneer at that angel. She can't hear it; and she's miserable and unfortunate, and deserves to be laughed at. Go on joking, Ann. You're the wit of the family, and the others like to hear it.\"\n\n\"I must tell you again we're not in a barrack, William,\" Miss Ann remarked.\n\n\"In a barrack, by Jove--I wish anybody in a barrack would say what you do,\" cried out this uproused British lion. \"I should like to hear a man breathe a word against her, by Jupiter. But men don't talk in this way, Ann: it's only women, who get together and hiss, and shriek, and cackle. There, get away--don't begin to cry. I only said you were a couple of geese,\" Will Dobbin said, perceiving Miss Ann's pink eyes were beginning to moisten as usual. \"Well, you're not geese, you're swans--anything you like, only do, do leave Miss Sedley alone.\"\n\nAnything like William's infatuation about that silly little flirting, ogling thing was never known, the mamma and sisters agreed together in thinking: and they trembled lest, her engagement being off with Osborne, she should take up immediately her other admirer and Captain. In which forebodings these worthy young women no doubt judged according to the best of their experience; or rather (for as yet they had had no opportunities of marrying or of jilting) according to their own notions of right and wrong.\n\n\"It is a mercy, Mamma, that the regiment is ordered abroad,\" the girls said. \"THIS danger, at any rate, is spared our brother.\"\n\nSuch, indeed, was the fact; and so it is that the French Emperor comes in to perform a part in this domestic comedy of Vanity Fair which we are now playing, and which would never have been enacted without the intervention of this august mute personage. It was he that ruined the Bourbons and Mr. John Sedley. It was he whose arrival in his capital called up all France in arms to defend him there; and all Europe to oust him. While the French nation and army were swearing fidelity round the eagles in the Champ de Mars, four mighty European hosts were getting in motion for the great chasse a l'aigle; and one of these was a British army, of which two heroes of ours, Captain Dobbin and Captain Osborne, formed a portion.\n\nThe news of Napoleon's escape and landing was received by the gallant --th with a fiery delight and enthusiasm, which everybody can understand who knows that famous corps. From the colonel to the smallest drummer in the regiment, all were filled with hope and ambition and patriotic fury; and thanked the French Emperor as for a personal kindness in coming to disturb the peace of Europe. Now was the time the --th had so long panted for, to show their comrades in arms that they could fight as well as the Peninsular veterans, and that all the pluck and valour of the --th had not been killed by the West Indies and the yellow fever. Stubble and Spooney looked to get their companies without purchase. Before the end of the campaign (which she resolved to share), Mrs. Major O'Dowd hoped to write herself Mrs. Colonel O'Dowd, C.B. Our two friends (Dobbin and Osborne) were quite as much excited as the rest: and each in his way--Mr. Dobbin very quietly, Mr. Osborne very loudly and energetically--was bent upon doing his duty, and gaining his share of honour and distinction.\n\nThe agitation thrilling through the country and army in consequence of this news was so great, that private matters were little heeded: and hence probably George Osborne, just gazetted to his company, busy with preparations for the march, which must come inevitably, and panting for further promotion--was not so much affected by other incidents which would have interested him at a more quiet period. He was not, it must be confessed, very much cast down by good old Mr. Sedley's catastrophe. He tried his new uniform, which became him very handsomely, on the day when the first meeting of the creditors of the unfortunate gentleman took place. His father told him of the wicked, rascally, shameful conduct of the bankrupt, reminded him of what he had said about Amelia, and that their connection was broken off for ever; and gave him that evening a good sum of money to pay for the new clothes and epaulets in which he looked so well. Money was always useful to this free-handed young fellow, and he took it without many words. The bills were up in the Sedley house, where he had passed so many, many happy hours. He could see them as he walked from home that night (to the Old Slaughters', where he put up when in town) shining white in the moon. That comfortable home was shut, then, upon Amelia and her parents: where had they taken refuge? The thought of their ruin affected him not a little. He was very melancholy that night in the coffee-room at the Slaughters'; and drank a good deal, as his comrades remarked there.\n\nDobbin came in presently, cautioned him about the drink, which he only took, he said, because he was deuced low; but when his friend began to put to him clumsy inquiries, and asked him for news in a significant manner, Osborne declined entering into conversation with him, avowing, however, that he was devilish disturbed and unhappy.\n\nThree days afterwards, Dobbin found Osborne in his room at the barracks--his head on the table, a number of papers about, the young Captain evidently in a state of great despondency. \"She--she's sent me back some things I gave her--some damned trinkets. Look here!\" There was a little packet directed in the well-known hand to Captain George Osborne, and some things lying about--a ring, a silver knife he had bought, as a boy, for her at a fair; a gold chain, and a locket with hair in it. \"It's all over,\" said he, with a groan of sickening remorse. \"Look, Will, you may read it if you like.\"\n\nThere was a little letter of a few lines, to which he pointed, which said:\n\nMy papa has ordered me to return to you these presents, which you made in happier days to me; and I am to write to you for the last time. I think, I know you feel as much as I do the blow which has come upon us. It is I that absolve you from an engagement which is impossible in our present misery. I am sure you had no share in it, or in the cruel suspicions of Mr. Osborne, which are the hardest of all our griefs to bear. Farewell. Farewell. I pray God to strengthen me to bear this and other calamities, and to bless you always. A.\n\nI shall often play upon the piano--your piano. It was like you to send it.\n\nDobbin was very soft-hearted. The sight of women and children in pain always used to melt him. The idea of Amelia broken-hearted and lonely tore that good-natured soul with anguish. And he broke out into an emotion, which anybody who likes may consider unmanly. He swore that Amelia was an angel, to which Osborne said aye with all his heart. He, too, had been reviewing the history of their lives--and had seen her from her childhood to her present age, so sweet, so innocent, so charmingly simple, and artlessly fond and tender.\n\nWhat a pang it was to lose all that: to have had it and not prized it! A thousand homely scenes and recollections crowded on him--in which he always saw her good and beautiful. And for himself, he blushed with remorse and shame, as the remembrance of his own selfishness and indifference contrasted with that perfect purity. For a while, glory, war, everything was forgotten, and the pair of friends talked about her only.\n\n\"Where are they?\" Osborne asked, after a long talk, and a long pause--and, in truth, with no little shame at thinking that he had taken no steps to follow her. \"Where are they? There's no address to the note.\"\n\nDobbin knew. He had not merely sent the piano; but had written a note to Mrs. Sedley, and asked permission to come and see her--and he had seen her, and Amelia too, yesterday, before he came down to Chatham; and, what is more, he had brought that farewell letter and packet which had so moved them.\n\nThe good-natured fellow had found Mrs. Sedley only too willing to receive him, and greatly agitated by the arrival of the piano, which, as she conjectured, MUST have come from George, and was a signal of amity on his part. Captain Dobbin did not correct this error of the worthy lady, but listened to all her story of complaints and misfortunes with great sympathy--condoled with her losses and privations, and agreed in reprehending the cruel conduct of Mr. Osborne towards his first benefactor. When she had eased her overflowing bosom somewhat, and poured forth many of her sorrows, he had the courage to ask actually to see Amelia, who was above in her room as usual, and whom her mother led trembling downstairs.\n\nHer appearance was so ghastly, and her look of despair so pathetic, that honest William Dobbin was frightened as he beheld it; and read the most fatal forebodings in that pale fixed face. After sitting in his company a minute or two, she put the packet into his hand, and said, \"Take this to Captain Osborne, if you please, and--and I hope he's quite well--and it was very kind of you to come and see us--and we like our new house very much. And I--I think I'll go upstairs, Mamma, for I'm not very strong.\" And with this, and a curtsey and a smile, the poor child went her way. The mother, as she led her up, cast back looks of anguish towards Dobbin. The good fellow wanted no such appeal. He loved her himself too fondly for that. Inexpressible grief, and pity, and terror pursued him, and he came away as if he was a criminal after seeing her.\n\nWhen Osborne heard that his friend had found her, he made hot and anxious inquiries regarding the poor child. How was she? How did she look? What did she say? His comrade took his hand, and looked him in the face.\n\n\"George, she's dying,\" William Dobbin said--and could speak no more.\n\nThere was a buxom Irish servant-girl, who performed all the duties of the little house where the Sedley family had found refuge: and this girl had in vain, on many previous days, striven to give Amelia aid or consolation. Emmy was much too sad to answer, or even to be aware of the attempts the other was making in her favour.\n\nFour hours after the talk between Dobbin and Osborne, this servant-maid came into Amelia's room, where she sate as usual, brooding silently over her letters--her little treasures. The girl, smiling, and looking arch and happy, made many trials to attract poor Emmy's attention, who, however, took no heed of her.\n\n\"Miss Emmy,\" said the girl.\n\n\"I'm coming,\" Emmy said, not looking round.\n\n\"There's a message,\" the maid went on. \"There's something--somebody--sure, here's a new letter for you--don't be reading them old ones any more.\" And she gave her a letter, which Emmy took, and read.\n\n\"I must see you,\" the letter said. \"Dearest Emmy--dearest love--dearest wife, come to me.\"\n\nGeorge and her mother were outside, waiting until she had read the letter.\n\nMiss Crawley at Nurse\nWe have seen how Mrs. Firkin, the lady's maid, as soon as any event of importance to the Crawley family came to her knowledge, felt bound to communicate it to Mrs. Bute Crawley, at the Rectory; and have before mentioned how particularly kind and attentive that good-natured lady was to Miss Crawley's confidential servant. She had been a gracious friend to Miss Briggs, the companion, also; and had secured the latter's good-will by a number of those attentions and promises, which cost so little in the making, and are yet so valuable and agreeable to the recipient. Indeed every good economist and manager of a household must know how cheap and yet how amiable these professions are, and what a flavour they give to the most homely dish in life. Who was the blundering idiot who said that \"fine words butter no parsnips\"? Half the parsnips of society are served and rendered palatable with no other sauce. As the immortal Alexis Soyer can make more delicious soup for a half-penny than an ignorant cook can concoct with pounds of vegetables and meat, so a skilful artist will make a few simple and pleasing phrases go farther than ever so much substantial benefit-stock in the hands of a mere bungler. Nay, we know that substantial benefits often sicken some stomachs; whereas, most will digest any amount of fine words, and be always eager for more of the same food. Mrs. Bute had told Briggs and Firkin so often of the depth of her affection for them; and what she would do, if she had Miss Crawley's fortune, for friends so excellent and attached, that the ladies in question had the deepest regard for her; and felt as much gratitude and confidence as if Mrs. Bute had loaded them with the most expensive favours.\n\nRawdon Crawley, on the other hand, like a selfish heavy dragoon as he was, never took the least trouble to conciliate his aunt's aides-de-camp, showed his contempt for the pair with entire frankness--made Firkin pull off his boots on one occasion--sent her out in the rain on ignominious messages--and if he gave her a guinea, flung it to her as if it were a box on the ear. As his aunt, too, made a butt of Briggs, the Captain followed the example, and levelled his jokes at her--jokes about as delicate as a kick from his charger. Whereas, Mrs. Bute consulted her in matters of taste or difficulty, admired her poetry, and by a thousand acts of kindness and politeness, showed her appreciation of Briggs; and if she made Firkin a twopenny-halfpenny present, accompanied it with so many compliments, that the twopence-half-penny was transmuted into gold in the heart of the grateful waiting-maid, who, besides, was looking forwards quite contentedly to some prodigious benefit which must happen to her on the day when Mrs. Bute came into her fortune.\n\nThe different conduct of these two people is pointed out respectfully to the attention of persons commencing the world. Praise everybody, I say to such: never be squeamish, but speak out your compliment both point-blank in a man's face, and behind his back, when you know there is a reasonable chance of his hearing it again. Never lose a chance of saying a kind word. As Collingwood never saw a vacant place in his estate but he took an acorn out of his pocket and popped it in; so deal with your compliments through life. An acorn costs nothing; but it may sprout into a prodigious bit of timber.\n\nIn a word, during Rawdon Crawley's prosperity, he was only obeyed with sulky acquiescence; when his disgrace came, there was nobody to help or pity him. Whereas, when Mrs. Bute took the command at Miss Crawley's house, the garrison there were charmed to act under such a leader, expecting all sorts of promotion from her promises, her generosity, and her kind words.\n\nThat he would consider himself beaten, after one defeat, and make no attempt to regain the position he had lost, Mrs. Bute Crawley never allowed herself to suppose. She knew Rebecca to be too clever and spirited and desperate a woman to submit without a struggle; and felt that she must prepare for that combat, and be incessantly watchful against assault; or mine, or surprise.\n\nIn the first place, though she held the town, was she sure of the principal inhabitant? Would Miss Crawley herself hold out; and had she not a secret longing to welcome back the ousted adversary? The old lady liked Rawdon, and Rebecca, who amused her. Mrs. Bute could not disguise from herself the fact that none of her party could so contribute to the pleasures of the town-bred lady. \"My girls' singing, after that little odious governess's, I know is unbearable,\" the candid Rector's wife owned to herself. \"She always used to go to sleep when Martha and Louisa played their duets. Jim's stiff college manners and poor dear Bute's talk about his dogs and horses always annoyed her. If I took her to the Rectory, she would grow angry with us all, and fly, I know she would; and might fall into that horrid Rawdon's clutches again, and be the victim of that little viper of a Sharp. Meanwhile, it is clear to me that she is exceedingly unwell, and cannot move for some weeks, at any rate; during which we must think of some plan to protect her from the arts of those unprincipled people.\"\n\nIn the very best of moments, if anybody told Miss Crawley that she was, or looked ill, the trembling old lady sent off for her doctor; and I daresay she was very unwell after the sudden family event, which might serve to shake stronger nerves than hers. At least, Mrs. Bute thought it was her duty to inform the physician, and the apothecary, and the dame-de-compagnie, and the domestics, that Miss Crawley was in a most critical state, and that they were to act accordingly. She had the street laid knee-deep with straw; and the knocker put by with Mr. Bowls's plate. She insisted that the Doctor should call twice a day; and deluged her patient with draughts every two hours. When anybody entered the room, she uttered a shshshsh so sibilant and ominous, that it frightened the poor old lady in her bed, from which she could not look without seeing Mrs. Bute's beady eyes eagerly fixed on her, as the latter sate steadfast in the arm-chair by the bedside. They seemed to lighten in the dark (for she kept the curtains closed) as she moved about the room on velvet paws like a cat. There Miss Crawley lay for days--ever so many days--Mr. Bute reading books of devotion to her: for nights, long nights, during which she had to hear the watchman sing, the night-light sputter; visited at midnight, the last thing, by the stealthy apothecary; and then left to look at Mrs. Bute's twinkling eyes, or the flicks of yellow that the rushlight threw on the dreary darkened ceiling. Hygeia herself would have fallen sick under such a regimen; and how much more this poor old nervous victim? It has been said that when she was in health and good spirits, this venerable inhabitant of Vanity Fair had as free notions about religion and morals as Monsieur de Voltaire himself could desire, but when illness overtook her, it was aggravated by the most dreadful terrors of death, and an utter cowardice took possession of the prostrate old sinner.\n\nSick-bed homilies and pious reflections are, to be sure, out of place in mere story-books, and we are not going (after the fashion of some novelists of the present day) to cajole the public into a sermon, when it is only a comedy that the reader pays his money to witness. But, without preaching, the truth may surely be borne in mind, that the bustle, and triumph, and laughter, and gaiety which Vanity Fair exhibits in public, do not always pursue the performer into private life, and that the most dreary depression of spirits and dismal repentances sometimes overcome him. Recollection of the best ordained banquets will scarcely cheer sick epicures. Reminiscences of the most becoming dresses and brilliant ball triumphs will go very little way to console faded beauties. Perhaps statesmen, at a particular period of existence, are not much gratified at thinking over the most triumphant divisions; and the success or the pleasure of yesterday becomes of very small account when a certain (albeit uncertain) morrow is in view, about which all of us must some day or other be speculating. O brother wearers of motley! Are there not moments when one grows sick of grinning and tumbling, and the jingling of cap and bells? This, dear friends and companions, is my amiable object--to walk with you through the Fair, to examine the shops and the shows there; and that we should all come home after the flare, and the noise, and the gaiety, and be perfectly miserable in private.\n\n\"If that poor man of mine had a head on his shoulders,\" Mrs. Bute Crawley thought to herself, \"how useful he might be, under present circumstances, to this unhappy old lady! He might make her repent of her shocking free-thinking ways; he might urge her to do her duty, and cast off that odious reprobate who has disgraced himself and his family; and he might induce her to do justice to my dear girls and the two boys, who require and deserve, I am sure, every assistance which their relatives can give them.\"\n\nAnd, as the hatred of vice is always a progress towards virtue, Mrs. Bute Crawley endeavoured to instil her sister-in-law a proper abhorrence for all Rawdon Crawley's manifold sins: of which his uncle's wife brought forward such a catalogue as indeed would have served to condemn a whole regiment of young officers. If a man has committed wrong in life, I don't know any moralist more anxious to point his errors out to the world than his own relations; so Mrs. Bute showed a perfect family interest and knowledge of Rawdon's history. She had all the particulars of that ugly quarrel with Captain Marker, in which Rawdon, wrong from the beginning, ended in shooting the Captain. She knew how the unhappy Lord Dovedale, whose mamma had taken a house at Oxford, so that he might be educated there, and who had never touched a card in his life till he came to London, was perverted by Rawdon at the Cocoa-Tree, made helplessly tipsy by this abominable seducer and perverter of youth, and fleeced of four thousand pounds. She described with the most vivid minuteness the agonies of the country families whom he had ruined--the sons whom he had plunged into dishonour and poverty--the daughters whom he had inveigled into perdition. She knew the poor tradesmen who were bankrupt by his extravagance--the mean shifts and rogueries with which he had ministered to it--the astounding falsehoods by which he had imposed upon the most generous of aunts, and the ingratitude and ridicule by which he had repaid her sacrifices. She imparted these stories gradually to Miss Crawley; gave her the whole benefit of them; felt it to be her bounden duty as a Christian woman and mother of a family to do so; had not the smallest remorse or compunction for the victim whom her tongue was immolating; nay, very likely thought her act was quite meritorious, and plumed herself upon her resolute manner of performing it. Yes, if a man's character is to be abused, say what you will, there's nobody like a relation to do the business. And one is bound to own, regarding this unfortunate wretch of a Rawdon Crawley, that the mere truth was enough to condemn him, and that all inventions of scandal were quite superfluous pains on his friends' parts.\n\nRebecca, too, being now a relative, came in for the fullest share of Mrs. Bute's kind inquiries. This indefatigable pursuer of truth (having given strict orders that the door was to be denied to all emissaries or letters from Rawdon), took Miss Crawley's carriage, and drove to her old friend Miss Pinkerton, at Minerva House, Chiswick Mall, to whom she announced the dreadful intelligence of Captain Rawdon's seduction by Miss Sharp, and from whom she got sundry strange particulars regarding the ex-governess's birth and early history. The friend of the Lexicographer had plenty of information to give. Miss Jemima was made to fetch the drawing-master's receipts and letters. This one was from a spunging-house: that entreated an advance: another was full of gratitude for Rebecca's reception by the ladies of Chiswick: and the last document from the unlucky artist's pen was that in which, from his dying bed, he recommended his orphan child to Miss Pinkerton's protection. There were juvenile letters and petitions from Rebecca, too, in the collection, imploring aid for her father or declaring her own gratitude. Perhaps in Vanity Fair there are no better satires than letters. Take a bundle of your dear friend's of ten years back--your dear friend whom you hate now. Look at a file of your sister's! how you clung to each other till you quarrelled about the twenty-pound legacy! Get down the round-hand scrawls of your son who has half broken your heart with selfish undutifulness since; or a parcel of your own, breathing endless ardour and love eternal, which were sent back by your mistress when she married the Nabob--your mistress for whom you now care no more than for Queen Elizabeth. Vows, love, promises, confidences, gratitude, how queerly they read after a while! There ought to be a law in Vanity Fair ordering the destruction of every written document (except receipted tradesmen's bills) after a certain brief and proper interval. Those quacks and misanthropes who advertise indelible Japan ink should be made to perish along with their wicked discoveries. The best ink for Vanity Fair use would be one that faded utterly in a couple of days, and left the paper clean and blank, so that you might write on it to somebody else.\n\nFrom Miss Pinkerton's the indefatigable Mrs. Bute followed the track of Sharp and his daughter back to the lodgings in Greek Street, which the defunct painter had occupied; and where portraits of the landlady in white satin, and of the husband in brass buttons, done by Sharp in lieu of a quarter's rent, still decorated the parlour walls. Mrs. Stokes was a communicative person, and quickly told all she knew about Mr. Sharp; how dissolute and poor he was; how good-natured and amusing; how he was always hunted by bailiffs and duns; how, to the landlady's horror, though she never could abide the woman, he did not marry his wife till a short time before her death; and what a queer little wild vixen his daughter was; how she kept them all laughing with her fun and mimicry; how she used to fetch the gin from the public-house, and was known in all the studios in the quarter--in brief, Mrs. Bute got such a full account of her new niece's parentage, education, and behaviour as would scarcely have pleased Rebecca, had the latter known that such inquiries were being made concerning her.\n\nOf all these industrious researches Miss Crawley had the full benefit. Mrs. Rawdon Crawley was the daughter of an opera-girl. She had danced herself. She had been a model to the painters. She was brought up as became her mother's daughter. She drank gin with her father, &c. &c. It was a lost woman who was married to a lost man; and the moral to be inferred from Mrs. Bute's tale was, that the knavery of the pair was irremediable, and that no properly conducted person should ever notice them again.\n\nThese were the materials which prudent Mrs. Bute gathered together in Park Lane, the provisions and ammunition as it were with which she fortified the house against the siege which she knew that Rawdon and his wife would lay to Miss Crawley.\n\nBut if a fault may be found with her arrangements, it is this, that she was too eager: she managed rather too well; undoubtedly she made Miss Crawley more ill than was necessary; and though the old invalid succumbed to her authority, it was so harassing and severe, that the victim would be inclined to escape at the very first chance which fell in her way. Managing women, the ornaments of their sex--women who order everything for everybody, and know so much better than any person concerned what is good for their neighbours, don't sometimes speculate upon the possibility of a domestic revolt, or upon other extreme consequences resulting from their overstrained authority.\n\nThus, for instance, Mrs. Bute, with the best intentions no doubt in the world, and wearing herself to death as she did by foregoing sleep, dinner, fresh air, for the sake of her invalid sister-in-law, carried her conviction of the old lady's illness so far that she almost managed her into her coffin. She pointed out her sacrifices and their results one day to the constant apothecary, Mr. Clump.\n\n\"I am sure, my dear Mr. Clump,\" she said, \"no efforts of mine have been wanting to restore our dear invalid, whom the ingratitude of her nephew has laid on the bed of sickness. I never shrink from personal discomfort: I never refuse to sacrifice myself.\"\n\n\"Your devotion, it must be confessed, is admirable,\" Mr. Clump says, with a low bow; \"but--\"\n\n\"I have scarcely closed my eyes since my arrival: I give up sleep, health, every comfort, to my sense of duty. When my poor James was in the smallpox, did I allow any hireling to nurse him? No.\"\n\n\"You did what became an excellent mother, my dear Madam--the best of mothers; but--\"\n\n\"As the mother of a family and the wife of an English clergyman, I humbly trust that my principles are good,\" Mrs. Bute said, with a happy solemnity of conviction; \"and, as long as Nature supports me, never, never, Mr. Clump, will I desert the post of duty. Others may bring that grey head with sorrow to the bed of sickness (here Mrs. Bute, waving her hand, pointed to one of old Miss Crawley's coffee-coloured fronts, which was perched on a stand in the dressing-room), but I will never quit it. Ah, Mr. Clump! I fear, I know, that the couch needs spiritual as well as medical consolation.\"\n\n\"What I was going to observe, my dear Madam,\"--here the resolute Clump once more interposed with a bland air--\"what I was going to observe when you gave utterance to sentiments which do you so much honour, was that I think you alarm yourself needlessly about our kind friend, and sacrifice your own health too prodigally in her favour.\"\n\n\"I would lay down my life for my duty, or for any member of my husband's family,\" Mrs. Bute interposed.\n\n\"Yes, Madam, if need were; but we don't want Mrs. Bute Crawley to be a martyr,\" Clump said gallantly. \"Dr. Squills and myself have both considered Miss Crawley's case with every anxiety and care, as you may suppose. We see her low-spirited and nervous; family events have agitated her.\"\n\n\"Her nephew will come to perdition,\" Mrs. Crawley cried.\n\n\"Have agitated her: and you arrived like a guardian angel, my dear Madam, a positive guardian angel, I assure you, to soothe her under the pressure of calamity. But Dr. Squills and I were thinking that our amiable friend is not in such a state as renders confinement to her bed necessary. She is depressed, but this confinement perhaps adds to her depression. She should have change, fresh air, gaiety; the most delightful remedies in the pharmacopoeia,\" Mr. Clump said, grinning and showing his handsome teeth. \"Persuade her to rise, dear Madam; drag her from her couch and her low spirits; insist upon her taking little drives. They will restore the roses too to your cheeks, if I may so speak to Mrs. Bute Crawley.\"\n\n\"The sight of her horrid nephew casually in the Park, where I am told the wretch drives with the brazen partner of his crimes,\" Mrs. Bute said (letting the cat of selfishness out of the bag of secrecy), \"would cause her such a shock, that we should have to bring her back to bed again. She must not go out, Mr. Clump. She shall not go out as long as I remain to watch over her; And as for my health, what matters it? I give it cheerfully, sir. I sacrifice it at the altar of my duty.\"\n\n\"Upon my word, Madam,\" Mr. Clump now said bluntly, \"I won't answer for her life if she remains locked up in that dark room. She is so nervous that we may lose her any day; and if you wish Captain Crawley to be her heir, I warn you frankly, Madam, that you are doing your very best to serve him.\"\n\n\"Gracious mercy! is her life in danger?\" Mrs. Bute cried. \"Why, why, Mr. Clump, did you not inform me sooner?\"\n\nThe night before, Mr. Clump and Dr. Squills had had a consultation (over a bottle of wine at the house of Sir Lapin Warren, whose lady was about to present him with a thirteenth blessing), regarding Miss Crawley and her case.\n\n\"What a little harpy that woman from Hampshire is, Clump,\" Squills remarked, \"that has seized upon old Tilly Crawley. Devilish good Madeira.\"\n\n\"What a fool Rawdon Crawley has been,\" Clump replied, \"to go and marry a governess! There was something about the girl, too.\"\n\n\"Green eyes, fair skin, pretty figure, famous frontal development,\" Squills remarked. \"There is something about her; and Crawley was a fool, Squills.\"\n\n\"A d---- fool--always was,\" the apothecary replied.\n\n\"Of course the old girl will fling him over,\" said the physician, and after a pause added, \"She'll cut up well, I suppose.\"\n\n\"Cut up,\" says Clump with a grin; \"I wouldn't have her cut up for two hundred a year.\"\n\n\"That Hampshire woman will kill her in two months, Clump, my boy, if she stops about her,\" Dr. Squills said. \"Old woman; full feeder; nervous subject; palpitation of the heart; pressure on the brain; apoplexy; off she goes. Get her up, Clump; get her out: or I wouldn't give many weeks' purchase for your two hundred a year.\" And it was acting upon this hint that the worthy apothecary spoke with so much candour to Mrs. Bute Crawley.\n\nHaving the old lady under her hand: in bed: with nobody near, Mrs. Bute had made more than one assault upon her, to induce her to alter her will. But Miss Crawley's usual terrors regarding death increased greatly when such dismal propositions were made to her, and Mrs. Bute saw that she must get her patient into cheerful spirits and health before she could hope to attain the pious object which she had in view. Whither to take her was the next puzzle. The only place where she is not likely to meet those odious Rawdons is at church, and that won't amuse her, Mrs. Bute justly felt. \"We must go and visit our beautiful suburbs of London,\" she then thought. \"I hear they are the most picturesque in the world\"; and so she had a sudden interest for Hampstead, and Hornsey, and found that Dulwich had great charms for her, and getting her victim into her carriage, drove her to those rustic spots, beguiling the little journeys with conversations about Rawdon and his wife, and telling every story to the old lady which could add to her indignation against this pair of reprobates.\n\nPerhaps Mrs. Bute pulled the string unnecessarily tight. For though she worked up Miss Crawley to a proper dislike of her disobedient nephew, the invalid had a great hatred and secret terror of her victimizer, and panted to escape from her. After a brief space, she rebelled against Highgate and Hornsey utterly. She would go into the Park. Mrs. Bute knew they would meet the abominable Rawdon there, and she was right. One day in the ring, Rawdon's stanhope came in sight; Rebecca was seated by him. In the enemy's equipage Miss Crawley occupied her usual place, with Mrs. Bute on her left, the poodle and Miss Briggs on the back seat. It was a nervous moment, and Rebecca's heart beat quick as she recognized the carriage; and as the two vehicles crossed each other in a line, she clasped her hands, and looked towards the spinster with a face of agonized attachment and devotion. Rawdon himself trembled, and his face grew purple behind his dyed mustachios. Only old Briggs was moved in the other carriage, and cast her great eyes nervously towards her old friends. Miss Crawley's bonnet was resolutely turned towards the Serpentine. Mrs. Bute happened to be in ecstasies with the poodle, and was calling him a little darling, and a sweet little zoggy, and a pretty pet. The carriages moved on, each in his line.\n\n\"Done, by Jove,\" Rawdon said to his wife.\n\n\"Try once more, Rawdon,\" Rebecca answered. \"Could not you lock your wheels into theirs, dearest?\"\n\nRawdon had not the heart for that manoeuvre. When the carriages met again, he stood up in his stanhope; he raised his hand ready to doff his hat; he looked with all his eyes. But this time Miss Crawley's face was not turned away; she and Mrs. Bute looked him full in the face, and cut their nephew pitilessly. He sank back in his seat with an oath, and striking out of the ring, dashed away desperately homewards.\n\nIt was a gallant and decided triumph for Mrs. Bute. But she felt the danger of many such meetings, as she saw the evident nervousness of Miss Crawley; and she determined that it was most necessary for her dear friend's health, that they should leave town for a while, and recommended Brighton very strongly.\n\nIn Which Captain Dobbin Acts as the Messenger of Hymen\nWithout knowing how, Captain William Dobbin found himself the great promoter, arranger, and manager of the match between George Osborne and Amelia. But for him it never would have taken place: he could not but confess as much to himself, and smiled rather bitterly as he thought that he of all men in the world should be the person upon whom the care of this marriage had fallen. But though indeed the conducting of this negotiation was about as painful a task as could be set to him, yet when he had a duty to perform, Captain Dobbin was accustomed to go through it without many words or much hesitation: and, having made up his mind completely, that if Miss Sedley was balked of her husband she would die of the disappointment, he was determined to use all his best endeavours to keep her alive.\n\nI forbear to enter into minute particulars of the interview between George and Amelia, when the former was brought back to the feet (or should we venture to say the arms?) of his young mistress by the intervention of his friend honest William. A much harder heart than George's would have melted at the sight of that sweet face so sadly ravaged by grief and despair, and at the simple tender accents in which she told her little broken-hearted story: but as she did not faint when her mother, trembling, brought Osborne to her; and as she only gave relief to her overcharged grief, by laying her head on her lover's shoulder and there weeping for a while the most tender, copious, and refreshing tears--old Mrs. Sedley, too greatly relieved, thought it was best to leave the young persons to themselves; and so quitted Emmy crying over George's hand, and kissing it humbly, as if he were her supreme chief and master, and as if she were quite a guilty and unworthy person needing every favour and grace from him.\n\nThis prostration and sweet unrepining obedience exquisitely touched and flattered George Osborne. He saw a slave before him in that simple yielding faithful creature, and his soul within him thrilled secretly somehow at the knowledge of his power. He would be generous-minded, Sultan as he was, and raise up this kneeling Esther and make a queen of her: besides, her sadness and beauty touched him as much as her submission, and so he cheered her, and raised her up and forgave her, so to speak. All her hopes and feelings, which were dying and withering, this her sun having been removed from her, bloomed again and at once, its light being restored. You would scarcely have recognised the beaming little face upon Amelia's pillow that night as the one that was laid there the night before, so wan, so lifeless, so careless of all round about. The honest Irish maid-servant, delighted with the change, asked leave to kiss the face that had grown all of a sudden so rosy. Amelia put her arms round the girl's neck and kissed her with all her heart, like a child. She was little more. She had that night a sweet refreshing sleep, like one--and what a spring of inexpressible happiness as she woke in the morning sunshine!\n\n\"He will be here again to-day,\" Amelia thought. \"He is the greatest and best of men.\" And the fact is, that George thought he was one of the generousest creatures alive: and that he was making a tremendous sacrifice in marrying this young creature.\n\nWhile she and Osborne were having their delightful tete-a-tete above stairs, old Mrs. Sedley and Captain Dobbin were conversing below upon the state of the affairs, and the chances and future arrangements of the young people. Mrs. Sedley having brought the two lovers together and left them embracing each other with all their might, like a true woman, was of opinion that no power on earth would induce Mr. Sedley to consent to the match between his daughter and the son of a man who had so shamefully, wickedly, and monstrously treated him. And she told a long story about happier days and their earlier splendours, when Osborne lived in a very humble way in the New Road, and his wife was too glad to receive some of Jos's little baby things, with which Mrs. Sedley accommodated her at the birth of one of Osborne's own children. The fiendish ingratitude of that man, she was sure, had broken Mr. S.'s heart: and as for a marriage, he would never, never, never, never consent.\n\n\"They must run away together, Ma'am,\" Dobbin said, laughing, \"and follow the example of Captain Rawdon Crawley, and Miss Emmy's friend the little governess.\" Was it possible? Well she never! Mrs. Sedley was all excitement about this news. She wished that Blenkinsop were here to hear it: Blenkinsop always mistrusted that Miss Sharp.-- What an escape Jos had had! and she described the already well-known love-passages between Rebecca and the Collector of Boggley Wollah.\n\nIt was not, however, Mr. Sedley's wrath which Dobbin feared, so much as that of the other parent concerned, and he owned that he had a very considerable doubt and anxiety respecting the behaviour of the black-browed old tyrant of a Russia merchant in Russell Square. He has forbidden the match peremptorily, Dobbin thought. He knew what a savage determined man Osborne was, and how he stuck by his word. \"The only chance George has of reconcilement,\" argued his friend, \"is by distinguishing himself in the coming campaign. If he dies they both go together. If he fails in distinction--what then? He has some money from his mother, I have heard enough to purchase his majority--or he must sell out and go and dig in Canada, or rough it in a cottage in the country.\" With such a partner Dobbin thought he would not mind Siberia--and, strange to say, this absurd and utterly imprudent young fellow never for a moment considered that the want of means to keep a nice carriage and horses, and of an income which should enable its possessors to entertain their friends genteelly, ought to operate as bars to the union of George and Miss Sedley.\n\nIt was these weighty considerations which made him think too that the marriage should take place as quickly as possible. Was he anxious himself, I wonder, to have it over?--as people, when death has occurred, like to press forward the funeral, or when a parting is resolved upon, hasten it. It is certain that Mr. Dobbin, having taken the matter in hand, was most extraordinarily eager in the conduct of it. He urged on George the necessity of immediate action: he showed the chances of reconciliation with his father, which a favourable mention of his name in the Gazette must bring about. If need were he would go himself and brave both the fathers in the business. At all events, he besought George to go through with it before the orders came, which everybody expected, for the departure of the regiment from England on foreign service.\n\nBent upon these hymeneal projects, and with the applause and consent of Mrs. Sedley, who did not care to break the matter personally to her husband, Mr. Dobbin went to seek John Sedley at his house of call in the City, the Tapioca Coffee-house, where, since his own offices were shut up, and fate had overtaken him, the poor broken-down old gentleman used to betake himself daily, and write letters and receive them, and tie them up into mysterious bundles, several of which he carried in the flaps of his coat. I don't know anything more dismal than that business and bustle and mystery of a ruined man: those letters from the wealthy which he shows you: those worn greasy documents promising support and offering condolence which he places wistfully before you, and on which he builds his hopes of restoration and future fortune. My beloved reader has no doubt in the course of his experience been waylaid by many such a luckless companion. He takes you into the corner; he has his bundle of papers out of his gaping coat pocket; and the tape off, and the string in his mouth, and the favourite letters selected and laid before you; and who does not know the sad eager half-crazy look which he fixes on you with his hopeless eyes?\n\nChanged into a man of this sort, Dobbin found the once florid, jovial, and prosperous John Sedley. His coat, that used to be so glossy and trim, was white at the seams, and the buttons showed the copper. His face had fallen in, and was unshorn; his frill and neckcloth hung limp under his bagging waistcoat. When he used to treat the boys in old days at a coffee-house, he would shout and laugh louder than anybody there, and have all the waiters skipping round him; it was quite painful to see how humble and civil he was to John of the Tapioca, a blear-eyed old attendant in dingy stockings and cracked pumps, whose business it was to serve glasses of wafers, and bumpers of ink in pewter, and slices of paper to the frequenters of this dreary house of entertainment, where nothing else seemed to be consumed. As for William Dobbin, whom he had tipped repeatedly in his youth, and who had been the old gentleman's butt on a thousand occasions, old Sedley gave his hand to him in a very hesitating humble manner now, and called him \"Sir.\" A feeling of shame and remorse took possession of William Dobbin as the broken old man so received and addressed him, as if he himself had been somehow guilty of the misfortunes which had brought Sedley so low.\n\n\"I am very glad to see you, Captain Dobbin, sir,\" says he, after a skulking look or two at his visitor (whose lanky figure and military appearance caused some excitement likewise to twinkle in the blear eyes of the waiter in the cracked dancing pumps, and awakened the old lady in black, who dozed among the mouldy old coffee-cups in the bar). \"How is the worthy alderman, and my lady, your excellent mother, sir?\" He looked round at the waiter as he said, \"My lady,\" as much as to say, \"Hark ye, John, I have friends still, and persons of rank and reputation, too.\" \"Are you come to do anything in my way, sir? My young friends Dale and Spiggot do all my business for me now, until my new offices are ready; for I'm only here temporarily, you know, Captain. What can we do for you, sir? Will you like to take anything?\"\n\nDobbin, with a great deal of hesitation and stuttering, protested that he was not in the least hungry or thirsty; that he had no business to transact; that he only came to ask if Mr. Sedley was well, and to shake hands with an old friend; and, he added, with a desperate perversion of truth, \"My mother is very well--that is, she's been very unwell, and is only waiting for the first fine day to go out and call upon Mrs. Sedley. How is Mrs. Sedley, sir? I hope she's quite well.\" And here he paused, reflecting on his own consummate hypocrisy; for the day was as fine, and the sunshine as bright as it ever is in Coffin Court, where the Tapioca Coffee-house is situated: and Mr. Dobbin remembered that he had seen Mrs. Sedley himself only an hour before, having driven Osborne down to Fulham in his gig, and left him there tete-a-tete with Miss Amelia.\n\n\"My wife will be very happy to see her ladyship,\" Sedley replied, pulling out his papers. \"I've a very kind letter here from your father, sir, and beg my respectful compliments to him. Lady D. will find us in rather a smaller house than we were accustomed to receive our friends in; but it's snug, and the change of air does good to my daughter, who was suffering in town rather--you remember little Emmy, sir?--yes, suffering a good deal.\" The old gentleman's eyes were wandering as he spoke, and he was thinking of something else, as he sate thrumming on his papers and fumbling at the worn red tape.\n\n\"You're a military man,\" he went on; \"I ask you, Bill Dobbin, could any man ever have speculated upon the return of that Corsican scoundrel from Elba? When the allied sovereigns were here last year, and we gave 'em that dinner in the City, sir, and we saw the Temple of Concord, and the fireworks, and the Chinese bridge in St. James's Park, could any sensible man suppose that peace wasn't really concluded, after we'd actually sung Te Deum for it, sir? I ask you, William, could I suppose that the Emperor of Austria was a damned traitor--a traitor, and nothing more? I don't mince words--a double-faced infernal traitor and schemer, who meant to have his son-in-law back all along. And I say that the escape of Boney from Elba was a damned imposition and plot, sir, in which half the powers of Europe were concerned, to bring the funds down, and to ruin this country. That's why I'm here, William. That's why my name's in the Gazette. Why, sir?--because I trusted the Emperor of Russia and the Prince Regent. Look here. Look at my papers. Look what the funds were on the 1st of March--what the French fives were when I bought for the count. And what they're at now. There was collusion, sir, or that villain never would have escaped. Where was the English Commissioner who allowed him to get away? He ought to be shot, sir--brought to a court-martial, and shot, by Jove.\"\n\n\"We're going to hunt Boney out, sir,\" Dobbin said, rather alarmed at the fury of the old man, the veins of whose forehead began to swell, and who sate drumming his papers with his clenched fist. \"We are going to hunt him out, sir--the Duke's in Belgium already, and we expect marching orders every day.\"\n\n\"Give him no quarter. Bring back the villain's head, sir. Shoot the coward down, sir,\" Sedley roared. \"I'd enlist myself, by--; but I'm a broken old man--ruined by that damned scoundrel--and by a parcel of swindling thieves in this country whom I made, sir, and who are rolling in their carriages now,\" he added, with a break in his voice.\n\nDobbin was not a little affected by the sight of this once kind old friend, crazed almost with misfortune and raving with senile anger. Pity the fallen gentleman: you to whom money and fair repute are the chiefest good; and so, surely, are they in Vanity Fair.\n\n\"Yes,\" he continued, \"there are some vipers that you warm, and they sting you afterwards. There are some beggars that you put on horseback, and they're the first to ride you down. You know whom I mean, William Dobbin, my boy. I mean a purse-proud villain in Russell Square, whom I knew without a shilling, and whom I pray and hope to see a beggar as he was when I befriended him.\"\n\n\"I have heard something of this, sir, from my friend George,\" Dobbin said, anxious to come to his point. \"The quarrel between you and his father has cut him up a great deal, sir. Indeed, I'm the bearer of a message from him.\"\n\n\"O, THAT'S your errand, is it?\" cried the old man, jumping up. \"What! perhaps he condoles with me, does he? Very kind of him, the stiff-backed prig, with his dandified airs and West End swagger. He's hankering about my house, is he still? If my son had the courage of a man, he'd shoot him. He's as big a villain as his father. I won't have his name mentioned in my house. I curse the day that ever I let him into it; and I'd rather see my daughter dead at my feet than married to him.\"\n\n\"His father's harshness is not George's fault, sir. Your daughter's love for him is as much your doing as his. Who are you, that you are to play with two young people's affections and break their hearts at your will?\"\n\n\"Recollect it's not his father that breaks the match off,\" old Sedley cried out. \"It's I that forbid it. That family and mine are separated for ever. I'm fallen low, but not so low as that: no, no. And so you may tell the whole race--son, and father and sisters, and all.\"\n\n\"It's my belief, sir, that you have not the power or the right to separate those two,\" Dobbin answered in a low voice; \"and that if you don't give your daughter your consent it will be her duty to marry without it. There's no reason she should die or live miserably because you are wrong-headed. To my thinking, she's just as much married as if the banns had been read in all the churches in London. And what better answer can there be to Osborne's charges against you, as charges there are, than that his son claims to enter your family and marry your daughter?\"\n\nA light of something like satisfaction seemed to break over old Sedley as this point was put to him: but he still persisted that with his consent the marriage between Amelia and George should never take place.\n\n\"We must do it without,\" Dobbin said, smiling, and told Mr. Sedley, as he had told Mrs. Sedley in the day, before, the story of Rebecca's elopement with Captain Crawley. It evidently amused the old gentleman. \"You're terrible fellows, you Captains,\" said he, tying up his papers; and his face wore something like a smile upon it, to the astonishment of the blear-eyed waiter who now entered, and had never seen such an expression upon Sedley's countenance since he had used the dismal coffee-house.\n\nThe idea of hitting his enemy Osborne such a blow soothed, perhaps, the old gentleman: and, their colloquy presently ending, he and Dobbin parted pretty good friends.\n\n\"My sisters say she has diamonds as big as pigeons' eggs,\" George said, laughing. \"How they must set off her complexion! A perfect illumination it must be when her jewels are on her neck. Her jet-black hair is as curly as Sambo's. I dare say she wore a nose ring when she went to court; and with a plume of feathers in her top-knot she would look a perfect Belle Sauvage.\"\n\nGeorge, in conversation with Amelia, was rallying the appearance of a young lady of whom his father and sisters had lately made the acquaintance, and who was an object of vast respect to the Russell Square family. She was reported to have I don't know how many plantations in the West Indies; a deal of money in the funds; and three stars to her name in the East India stockholders' list. She had a mansion in Surrey, and a house in Portland Place. The name of the rich West India heiress had been mentioned with applause in the Morning Post. Mrs. Haggistoun, Colonel Haggistoun's widow, her relative, \"chaperoned\" her, and kept her house. She was just from school, where she had completed her education, and George and his sisters had met her at an evening party at old Hulker's house, Devonshire Place (Hulker, Bullock, and Co. were long the correspondents of her house in the West Indies), and the girls had made the most cordial advances to her, which the heiress had received with great good humour. An orphan in her position--with her money--so interesting! the Misses Osborne said. They were full of their new friend when they returned from the Hulker ball to Miss Wirt, their companion; they had made arrangements for continually meeting, and had the carriage and drove to see her the very next day. Mrs. Haggistoun, Colonel Haggistoun's widow, a relation of Lord Binkie, and always talking of him, struck the dear unsophisticated girls as rather haughty, and too much inclined to talk about her great relations: but Rhoda was everything they could wish--the frankest, kindest, most agreeable creature--wanting a little polish, but so good-natured. The girls Christian-named each other at once.\n\n\"You should have seen her dress for court, Emmy,\" Osborne cried, laughing. \"She came to my sisters to show it off, before she was presented in state by my Lady Binkie, the Haggistoun's kinswoman. She's related to every one, that Haggistoun. Her diamonds blazed out like Vauxhall on the night we were there. (Do you remember Vauxhall, Emmy, and Jos singing to his dearest diddle diddle darling?) Diamonds and mahogany, my dear! think what an advantageous contrast--and the white feathers in her hair--I mean in her wool. She had earrings like chandeliers; you might have lighted 'em up, by Jove--and a yellow satin train that streeled after her like the tail of a comet.\"\n\n\"How old is she?\" asked Emmy, to whom George was rattling away regarding this dark paragon, on the morning of their reunion--rattling away as no other man in the world surely could.\n\n\"Why the Black Princess, though she has only just left school, must be two or three and twenty. And you should see the hand she writes! Mrs. Colonel Haggistoun usually writes her letters, but in a moment of confidence, she put pen to paper for my sisters; she spelt satin satting, and Saint James's, Saint Jams.\"\n\n\"Why, surely it must be Miss Swartz, the parlour boarder,\" Emmy said, remembering that good-natured young mulatto girl, who had been so hysterically affected when Amelia left Miss Pinkerton's academy.\n\n\"The very name,\" George said. \"Her father was a German Jew--a slave-owner they say--connected with the Cannibal Islands in some way or other. He died last year, and Miss Pinkerton has finished her education. She can play two pieces on the piano; she knows three songs; she can write when Mrs. Haggistoun is by to spell for her; and Jane and Maria already have got to love her as a sister.\"\n\n\"I wish they would have loved me,\" said Emmy, wistfully. \"They were always very cold to me.\"\n\n\"My dear child, they would have loved you if you had had two hundred thousand pounds,\" George replied. \"That is the way in which they have been brought up. Ours is a ready-money society. We live among bankers and City big-wigs, and be hanged to them, and every man, as he talks to you, is jingling his guineas in his pocket. There is that jackass Fred Bullock is going to marry Maria--there's Goldmore, the East India Director, there's Dipley, in the tallow trade--OUR trade,\" George said, with an uneasy laugh and a blush. \"Curse the whole pack of money-grubbing vulgarians! I fall asleep at their great heavy dinners. I feel ashamed in my father's great stupid parties. I've been accustomed to live with gentlemen, and men of the world and fashion, Emmy, not with a parcel of turtle-fed tradesmen. Dear little woman, you are the only person of our set who ever looked, or thought, or spoke like a lady: and you do it because you're an angel and can't help it. Don't remonstrate. You are the only lady. Didn't Miss Crawley remark it, who has lived in the best company in Europe? And as for Crawley, of the Life Guards, hang it, he's a fine fellow: and I like him for marrying the girl he had chosen.\"\n\nAmelia admired Mr. Crawley very much, too, for this; and trusted Rebecca would be happy with him, and hoped (with a laugh) Jos would be consoled. And so the pair went on prattling, as in quite early days. Amelia's confidence being perfectly restored to her, though she expressed a great deal of pretty jealousy about Miss Swartz, and professed to be dreadfully frightened--like a hypocrite as she was--lest George should forget her for the heiress and her money and her estates in Saint Kitt's. But the fact is, she was a great deal too happy to have fears or doubts or misgivings of any sort: and having George at her side again, was not afraid of any heiress or beauty, or indeed of any sort of danger.\n\nWhen Captain Dobbin came back in the afternoon to these people--which he did with a great deal of sympathy for them--it did his heart good to see how Amelia had grown young again--how she laughed, and chirped, and sang familiar old songs at the piano, which were only interrupted by the bell from without proclaiming Mr. Sedley's return from the City, before whom George received a signal to retreat.\n\nBeyond the first smile of recognition--and even that was an hypocrisy, for she thought his arrival rather provoking--Miss Sedley did not once notice Dobbin during his visit. But he was content, so that he saw her happy; and thankful to have been the means of making her so.\n\nA Quarrel About an Heiress\nLove may be felt for any young lady endowed with such qualities as Miss Swartz possessed; and a great dream of ambition entered into old Mr. Osborne's soul, which she was to realize. He encouraged, with the utmost enthusiasm and friendliness, his daughters' amiable attachment to the young heiress, and protested that it gave him the sincerest pleasure as a father to see the love of his girls so well disposed.\n\n\"You won't find,\" he would say to Miss Rhoda, \"that splendour and rank to which you are accustomed at the West End, my dear Miss, at our humble mansion in Russell Square. My daughters are plain, disinterested girls, but their hearts are in the right place, and they've conceived an attachment for you which does them honour--I say, which does them honour. I'm a plain, simple, humble British merchant--an honest one, as my respected friends Hulker and Bullock will vouch, who were the correspondents of your late lamented father. You'll find us a united, simple, happy, and I think I may say respected, family--a plain table, a plain people, but a warm welcome, my dear Miss Rhoda--Rhoda, let me say, for my heart warms to you, it does really. I'm a frank man, and I like you. A glass of Champagne! Hicks, Champagne to Miss Swartz.\"\n\nThere is little doubt that old Osborne believed all he said, and that the girls were quite earnest in their protestations of affection for Miss Swartz. People in Vanity Fair fasten on to rich folks quite naturally. If the simplest people are disposed to look not a little kindly on great Prosperity (for I defy any member of the British public to say that the notion of Wealth has not something awful and pleasing to him; and you, if you are told that the man next you at dinner has got half a million, not to look at him with a certain interest)--if the simple look benevolently on money, how much more do your old worldlings regard it! Their affections rush out to meet and welcome money. Their kind sentiments awaken spontaneously towards the interesting possessors of it. I know some respectable people who don't consider themselves at liberty to indulge in friendship for any individual who has not a certain competency, or place in society. They give a loose to their feelings on proper occasions. And the proof is, that the major part of the Osborne family, who had not, in fifteen years, been able to get up a hearty regard for Amelia Sedley, became as fond of Miss Swartz in the course of a single evening as the most romantic advocate of friendship at first sight could desire.\n\nWhat a match for George she'd be (the sisters and Miss Wirt agreed), and how much better than that insignificant little Amelia! Such a dashing young fellow as he is, with his good looks, rank, and accomplishments, would be the very husband for her. Visions of balls in Portland Place, presentations at Court, and introductions to half the peerage, filled the minds of the young ladies; who talked of nothing but George and his grand acquaintances to their beloved new friend.\n\nOld Osborne thought she would be a great match, too, for his son. He should leave the army; he should go into Parliament; he should cut a figure in the fashion and in the state. His blood boiled with honest British exultation, as he saw the name of Osborne ennobled in the person of his son, and thought that he might be the progenitor of a glorious line of baronets. He worked in the City and on 'Change, until he knew everything relating to the fortune of the heiress, how her money was placed, and where her estates lay. Young Fred Bullock, one of his chief informants, would have liked to make a bid for her himself (it was so the young banker expressed it), only he was booked to Maria Osborne. But not being able to secure her as a wife, the disinterested Fred quite approved of her as a sister-in-law. \"Let George cut in directly and win her,\" was his advice. \"Strike while the iron's hot, you know--while she's fresh to the town: in a few weeks some d---- fellow from the West End will come in with a title and a rotten rent-roll and cut all us City men out, as Lord Fitzrufus did last year with Miss Grogram, who was actually engaged to Podder, of Podder & Brown's. The sooner it is done the better, Mr. Osborne; them's my sentiments,\" the wag said; though, when Osborne had left the bank parlour, Mr. Bullock remembered Amelia, and what a pretty girl she was, and how attached to George Osborne; and he gave up at least ten seconds of his valuable time to regretting the misfortune which had befallen that unlucky young woman.\n\nWhile thus George Osborne's good feelings, and his good friend and genius, Dobbin, were carrying back the truant to Amelia's feet, George's parent and sisters were arranging this splendid match for him, which they never dreamed he would resist.\n\nWhen the elder Osborne gave what he called \"a hint,\" there was no possibility for the most obtuse to mistake his meaning. He called kicking a footman downstairs a hint to the latter to leave his service. With his usual frankness and delicacy he told Mrs. Haggistoun that he would give her a cheque for five thousand pounds on the day his son was married to her ward; and called that proposal a hint, and considered it a very dexterous piece of diplomacy. He gave George finally such another hint regarding the heiress; and ordered him to marry her out of hand, as he would have ordered his butler to draw a cork, or his clerk to write a letter.\n\nThis imperative hint disturbed George a good deal. He was in the very first enthusiasm and delight of his second courtship of Amelia, which was inexpressibly sweet to him. The contrast of her manners and appearance with those of the heiress, made the idea of a union with the latter appear doubly ludicrous and odious. Carriages and opera-boxes, thought he; fancy being seen in them by the side of such a mahogany charmer as that! Add to all that the junior Osborne was quite as obstinate as the senior: when he wanted a thing, quite as firm in his resolution to get it; and quite as violent when angered, as his father in his most stern moments.\n\nOn the first day when his father formally gave him the hint that he was to place his affections at Miss Swartz's feet, George temporised with the old gentleman. \"You should have thought of the matter sooner, sir,\" he said. \"It can't be done now, when we're expecting every day to go on foreign service. Wait till my return, if I do return\"; and then he represented, that the time when the regiment was daily expecting to quit England, was exceedingly ill-chosen: that the few days or weeks during which they were still to remain at home, must be devoted to business and not to love-making: time enough for that when he came home with his majority; \"for, I promise you,\" said he, with a satisfied air, \"that one way or other you shall read the name of George Osborne in the Gazette.\"\n\nThe father's reply to this was founded upon the information which he had got in the City: that the West End chaps would infallibly catch hold of the heiress if any delay took place: that if he didn't marry Miss S., he might at least have an engagement in writing, to come into effect when he returned to England; and that a man who could get ten thousand a year by staying at home, was a fool to risk his life abroad.\n\n\"So that you would have me shown up as a coward, sir, and our name dishonoured for the sake of Miss Swartz's money,\" George interposed.\n\nThis remark staggered the old gentleman; but as he had to reply to it, and as his mind was nevertheless made up, he said, \"You will dine here to-morrow, sir, and every day Miss Swartz comes, you will be here to pay your respects to her. If you want for money, call upon Mr. Chopper.\" Thus a new obstacle was in George's way, to interfere with his plans regarding Amelia; and about which he and Dobbin had more than one confidential consultation. His friend's opinion respecting the line of conduct which he ought to pursue, we know already. And as for Osborne, when he was once bent on a thing, a fresh obstacle or two only rendered him the more resolute.\n\nThe dark object of the conspiracy into which the chiefs of the Osborne family had entered, was quite ignorant of all their plans regarding her (which, strange to say, her friend and chaperon did not divulge), and, taking all the young ladies' flattery for genuine sentiment, and being, as we have before had occasion to show, of a very warm and impetuous nature, responded to their affection with quite a tropical ardour. And if the truth may be told, I dare say that she too had some selfish attraction in the Russell Square house; and in a word, thought George Osborne a very nice young man. His whiskers had made an impression upon her, on the very first night she beheld them at the ball at Messrs. Hulkers; and, as we know, she was not the first woman who had been charmed by them. George had an air at once swaggering and melancholy, languid and fierce. He looked like a man who had passions, secrets, and private harrowing griefs and adventures. His voice was rich and deep. He would say it was a warm evening, or ask his partner to take an ice, with a tone as sad and confidential as if he were breaking her mother's death to her, or preluding a declaration of love. He trampled over all the young bucks of his father's circle, and was the hero among those third-rate men. Some few sneered at him and hated him. Some, like Dobbin, fanatically admired him. And his whiskers had begun to do their work, and to curl themselves round the affections of Miss Swartz.\n\nWhenever there was a chance of meeting him in Russell Square, that simple and good-natured young woman was quite in a flurry to see her dear Misses Osborne. She went to great expenses in new gowns, and bracelets, and bonnets, and in prodigious feathers. She adorned her person with her utmost skill to please the Conqueror, and exhibited all her simple accomplishments to win his favour. The girls would ask her, with the greatest gravity, for a little music, and she would sing her three songs and play her two little pieces as often as ever they asked, and with an always increasing pleasure to herself. During these delectable entertainments, Miss Wirt and the chaperon sate by, and conned over the peerage, and talked about the nobility.\n\nThe day after George had his hint from his father, and a short time before the hour of dinner, he was lolling upon a sofa in the drawing-room in a very becoming and perfectly natural attitude of melancholy. He had been, at his father's request, to Mr. Chopper in the City (the old gentleman, though he gave great sums to his son, would never specify any fixed allowance for him, and rewarded him only as he was in the humour). He had then been to pass three hours with Amelia, his dear little Amelia, at Fulham; and he came home to find his sisters spread in starched muslin in the drawing-room, the dowagers cackling in the background, and honest Swartz in her favourite amber-coloured satin, with turquoise bracelets, countless rings, flowers, feathers, and all sorts of tags and gimcracks, about as elegantly decorated as a she chimney-sweep on May-day.\n\nThe girls, after vain attempts to engage him in conversation, talked about fashions and the last drawing-room until he was perfectly sick of their chatter. He contrasted their behaviour with little Emmy's--their shrill voices with her tender ringing tones; their attitudes and their elbows and their starch, with her humble soft movements and modest graces. Poor Swartz was seated in a place where Emmy had been accustomed to sit. Her bejewelled hands lay sprawling in her amber satin lap. Her tags and ear-rings twinkled, and her big eyes rolled about. She was doing nothing with perfect contentment, and thinking herself charming. Anything so becoming as the satin the sisters had never seen.\n\n\"Dammy,\" George said to a confidential friend, \"she looked like a China doll, which has nothing to do all day but to grin and wag its head. By Jove, Will, it was all I I could do to prevent myself from throwing the sofa-cushion at her.\" He restrained that exhibition of sentiment, however.\n\nThe sisters began to play the Battle of Prague. \"Stop that d---- thing,\" George howled out in a fury from the sofa. \"It makes me mad. You play us something, Miss Swartz, do. Sing something, anything but the Battle of Prague.\"\n\n\"Shall I sing 'Blue Eyed Mary' or the air from the Cabinet?\" Miss Swartz asked.\n\n\"That sweet thing from the Cabinet,\" the sisters said.\n\n\"We've had that,\" replied the misanthrope on the sofa.\n\n\"I can sing 'Fluvy du Tajy,'\" Swartz said, in a meek voice, \"if I had the words.\" It was the last of the worthy young woman's collection.\n\n\"O, 'Fleuve du Tage,'\" Miss Maria cried; \"we have the song,\" and went off to fetch the book in which it was.\n\nNow it happened that this song, then in the height of the fashion, had been given to the young ladies by a young friend of theirs, whose name was on the title, and Miss Swartz, having concluded the ditty with George's applause (for he remembered that it was a favourite of Amelia's), was hoping for an encore perhaps, and fiddling with the leaves of the music, when her eye fell upon the title, and she saw \"Amelia Sedley\" written in the comer.\n\n\"Lor!\" cried Miss Swartz, spinning swiftly round on the music-stool, \"is it my Amelia? Amelia that was at Miss P.'s at Hammersmith? I know it is. It's her, and-- Tell me about her--where is she?\"\n\n\"Don't mention her,\" Miss Maria Osborne said hastily. \"Her family has disgraced itself. Her father cheated Papa, and as for her, she is never to be mentioned HERE.\" This was Miss Maria's return for George's rudeness about the Battle of Prague.\n\n\"Are you a friend of Amelia's?\" George said, bouncing up. \"God bless you for it, Miss Swartz. Don't believe what the girls say. SHE'S not to blame at any rate. She's the best--\"\n\n\"You know you're not to speak about her, George,\" cried Jane. \"Papa forbids it.\"\n\n\"Who's to prevent me?\" George cried out. \"I will speak of her. I say she's the best, the kindest, the gentlest, the sweetest girl in England; and that, bankrupt or no, my sisters are not fit to hold candles to her. If you like her, go and see her, Miss Swartz; she wants friends now; and I say, God bless everybody who befriends her. Anybody who speaks kindly of her is my friend; anybody who speaks against her is my enemy. Thank you, Miss Swartz\"; and he went up and wrung her hand.\n\n\"George! George!\" one of the sisters cried imploringly.\n\n\"I say,\" George said fiercely, \"I thank everybody who loves Amelia Sed--\" He stopped. Old Osborne was in the room with a face livid with rage, and eyes like hot coals.\n\nThough George had stopped in his sentence, yet, his blood being up, he was not to be cowed by all the generations of Osborne; rallying instantly, he replied to the bullying look of his father, with another so indicative of resolution and defiance that the elder man quailed in his turn, and looked away. He felt that the tussle was coming. \"Mrs. Haggistoun, let me take you down to dinner,\" he said. \"Give your arm to Miss Swartz, George,\" and they marched.\n\n\"Miss Swartz, I love Amelia, and we've been engaged almost all our lives,\" Osborne said to his partner; and during all the dinner, George rattled on with a volubility which surprised himself, and made his father doubly nervous for the fight which was to take place as soon as the ladies were gone.\n\nThe difference between the pair was, that while the father was violent and a bully, the son had thrice the nerve and courage of the parent, and could not merely make an attack, but resist it; and finding that the moment was now come when the contest between him and his father was to be decided, he took his dinner with perfect coolness and appetite before the engagement began. Old Osborne, on the contrary, was nervous, and drank much. He floundered in his conversation with the ladies, his neighbours: George's coolness only rendering him more angry. It made him half mad to see the calm way in which George, flapping his napkin, and with a swaggering bow, opened the door for the ladies to leave the room; and filling himself a glass of wine, smacked it, and looked his father full in the face, as if to say, \"Gentlemen of the Guard, fire first.\" The old man also took a supply of ammunition, but his decanter clinked against the glass as he tried to fill it.\n\nAfter giving a great heave, and with a purple choking face, he then began. \"How dare you, sir, mention that person's name before Miss Swartz to-day, in my drawing-room? I ask you, sir, how dare you do it?\"\n\n\"Stop, sir,\" says George, \"don't say dare, sir. Dare isn't a word to be used to a Captain in the British Army.\"\n\n\"I shall say what I like to my son, sir. I can cut him off with a shilling if I like. I can make him a beggar if I like. I WILL say what I like,\" the elder said.\n\n\"I'm a gentleman though I AM your son, sir,\" George answered haughtily. \"Any communications which you have to make to me, or any orders which you may please to give, I beg may be couched in that kind of language which I am accustomed to hear.\"\n\nWhenever the lad assumed his haughty manner, it always created either great awe or great irritation in the parent. Old Osborne stood in secret terror of his son as a better gentleman than himself; and perhaps my readers may have remarked in their experience of this Vanity Fair of ours, that there is no character which a low-minded man so much mistrusts as that of a gentleman.\n\n\"My father didn't give me the education you have had, nor the advantages you have had, nor the money you have had. If I had kept the company SOME FOLKS have had through MY MEANS, perhaps my son wouldn't have any reason to brag, sir, of his SUPERIORITY and WEST END AIRS (these words were uttered in the elder Osborne's most sarcastic tones). But it wasn't considered the part of a gentleman, in MY time, for a man to insult his father. If I'd done any such thing, mine would have kicked me downstairs, sir.\"\n\n\"I never insulted you, sir. I said I begged you to remember your son was a gentleman as well as yourself. I know very well that you give me plenty of money,\" said George (fingering a bundle of notes which he had got in the morning from Mr. Chopper). \"You tell it me often enough, sir. There's no fear of my forgetting it.\"\n\n\"I wish you'd remember other things as well, sir,\" the sire answered. \"I wish you'd remember that in this house--so long as you choose to HONOUR it with your COMPANY, Captain--I'm the master, and that name, and that that--that you--that I say--\"\n\n\"That what, sir?\" George asked, with scarcely a sneer, filling another glass of claret.\n\n\"----!\" burst out his father with a screaming oath--\"that the name of those Sedleys never be mentioned here, sir--not one of the whole damned lot of 'em, sir.\"\n\n\"It wasn't I, sir, that introduced Miss Sedley's name. It was my sisters who spoke ill of her to Miss Swartz; and by Jove I'll defend her wherever I go. Nobody shall speak lightly of that name in my presence. Our family has done her quite enough injury already, I think, and may leave off reviling her now she's down. I'll shoot any man but you who says a word against her.\"\n\n\"Go on, sir, go on,\" the old gentleman said, his eyes starting out of his head.\n\n\"Go on about what, sir? about the way in which we've treated that angel of a girl? Who told me to love her? It was your doing. I might have chosen elsewhere, and looked higher, perhaps, than your society: but I obeyed you. And now that her heart's mine you give me orders to fling it away, and punish her, kill her perhaps--for the faults of other people. It's a shame, by Heavens,\" said George, working himself up into passion and enthusiasm as he proceeded, \"to play at fast and loose with a young girl's affections--and with such an angel as that--one so superior to the people amongst whom she lived, that she might have excited envy, only she was so good and gentle, that it's a wonder anybody dared to hate her. If I desert her, sir, do you suppose she forgets me?\"\n\n\"I ain't going to have any of this dam sentimental nonsense and humbug here, sir,\" the father cried out. \"There shall be no beggar-marriages in my family. If you choose to fling away eight thousand a year, which you may have for the asking, you may do it: but by Jove you take your pack and walk out of this house, sir. Will you do as I tell you, once for all, sir, or will you not?\"\n\n\"Marry that mulatto woman?\" George said, pulling up his shirt-collars. \"I don't like the colour, sir. Ask the black that sweeps opposite Fleet Market, sir. I'm not going to marry a Hottentot Venus.\"\n\nMr. Osborne pulled frantically at the cord by which he was accustomed to summon the butler when he wanted wine--and almost black in the face, ordered that functionary to call a coach for Captain Osborne.\n\n\"I've done it,\" said George, coming into the Slaughters' an hour afterwards, looking very pale.\n\n\"What, my boy?\" says Dobbin.\n\nGeorge told what had passed between his father and himself.\n\n\"I'll marry her to-morrow,\" he said with an oath. \"I love her more every day, Dobbin.\"\n\nA Marriage and Part of a Honeymoon\nEnemies the most obstinate and courageous can't hold out against starvation; so the elder Osborne felt himself pretty easy about his adversary in the encounter we have just described; and as soon as George's supplies fell short, confidently expected his unconditional submission. It was unlucky, to be sure, that the lad should have secured a stock of provisions on the very day when the first encounter took place; but this relief was only temporary, old Osborne thought, and would but delay George's surrender. No communication passed between father and son for some days. The former was sulky at this silence, but not disquieted; for, as he said, he knew where he could put the screw upon George, and only waited the result of that operation. He told the sisters the upshot of the dispute between them, but ordered them to take no notice of the matter, and welcome George on his return as if nothing had happened. His cover was laid as usual every day, and perhaps the old gentleman rather anxiously expected him; but he never came. Some one inquired at the Slaughters' regarding him, where it was said that he and his friend Captain Dobbin had left town.\n\nOne gusty, raw day at the end of April--the rain whipping the pavement of that ancient street where the old Slaughters' Coffee-house was once situated--George Osborne came into the coffee-room, looking very haggard and pale; although dressed rather smartly in a blue coat and brass buttons, and a neat buff waistcoat of the fashion of those days. Here was his friend Captain Dobbin, in blue and brass too, having abandoned the military frock and French-grey trousers, which were the usual coverings of his lanky person.\n\nDobbin had been in the coffee-room for an hour or more. He had tried all the papers, but could not read them. He had looked at the clock many scores of times; and at the street, where the rain was pattering down, and the people as they clinked by in pattens, left long reflections on the shining stone: he tattooed at the table: he bit his nails most completely, and nearly to the quick (he was accustomed to ornament his great big hands in this way): he balanced the tea-spoon dexterously on the milk jug: upset it, &c., &c.; and in fact showed those signs of disquietude, and practised those desperate attempts at amusement, which men are accustomed to employ when very anxious, and expectant, and perturbed in mind.\n\nSome of his comrades, gentlemen who used the room, joked him about the splendour of his costume and his agitation of manner. One asked him if he was going to be married? Dobbin laughed, and said he would send his acquaintance (Major Wagstaff of the Engineers) a piece of cake when that event took place. At length Captain Osborne made his appearance, very smartly dressed, but very pale and agitated as we have said. He wiped his pale face with a large yellow bandanna pocket-handkerchief that was prodigiously scented. He shook hands with Dobbin, looked at the clock, and told John, the waiter, to bring him some curacao. Of this cordial he swallowed off a couple of glasses with nervous eagerness. His friend asked with some interest about his health.\n\n\"Couldn't get a wink of sleep till daylight, Dob,\" said he. \"Infernal headache and fever. Got up at nine, and went down to the Hummums for a bath. I say, Dob, I feel just as I did on the morning I went out with Rocket at Quebec.\"\n\n\"So do I,\" William responded. \"I was a deuced deal more nervous than you were that morning. You made a famous breakfast, I remember. Eat something now.\"\n\n\"You're a good old fellow, Will. I'll drink your health, old boy, and farewell to--\"\n\n\"No, no; two glasses are enough,\" Dobbin interrupted him. \"Here, take away the liqueurs, John. Have some cayenne-pepper with your fowl. Make haste though, for it is time we were there.\"\n\nIt was about half an hour from twelve when this brief meeting and colloquy took place between the two captains. A coach, into which Captain Osborne's servant put his master's desk and dressing-case, had been in waiting for some time; and into this the two gentlemen hurried under an umbrella, and the valet mounted on the box, cursing the rain and the dampness of the coachman who was steaming beside him. \"We shall find a better trap than this at the church-door,\" says he; \"that's a comfort.\" And the carriage drove on, taking the road down Piccadilly, where Apsley House and St. George's Hospital wore red jackets still; where there were oil-lamps; where Achilles was not yet born; nor the Pimlico arch raised; nor the hideous equestrian monster which pervades it and the neighbourhood; and so they drove down by Brompton to a certain chapel near the Fulham Road there.\n\nA chariot was in waiting with four horses; likewise a coach of the kind called glass coaches. Only a very few idlers were collected on account of the dismal rain.\n\n\"Hang it!\" said George, \"I said only a pair.\"\n\n\"My master would have four,\" said Mr. Joseph Sedley's servant, who was in waiting; and he and Mr. Osborne's man agreed as they followed George and William into the church, that it was a \"reg'lar shabby turn hout; and with scarce so much as a breakfast or a wedding faviour.\"\n\n\"Here you are,\" said our old friend, Jos Sedley, coming forward. \"You're five minutes late, George, my boy. What a day, eh? Demmy, it's like the commencement of the rainy season in Bengal. But you'll find my carriage is watertight. Come along, my mother and Emmy are in the vestry.\"\n\nJos Sedley was splendid. He was fatter than ever. His shirt collars were higher; his face was redder; his shirt-frill flaunted gorgeously out of his variegated waistcoat. Varnished boots were not invented as yet; but the Hessians on his beautiful legs shone so, that they must have been the identical pair in which the gentleman in the old picture used to shave himself; and on his light green coat there bloomed a fine wedding favour, like a great white spreading magnolia.\n\nIn a word, George had thrown the great cast. He was going to be married. Hence his pallor and nervousness--his sleepless night and agitation in the morning. I have heard people who have gone through the same thing own to the same emotion. After three or four ceremonies, you get accustomed to it, no doubt; but the first dip, everybody allows, is awful.\n\nThe bride was dressed in a brown silk pelisse (as Captain Dobbin has since informed me), and wore a straw bonnet with a pink ribbon; over the bonnet she had a veil of white Chantilly lace, a gift from Mr. Joseph Sedley, her brother. Captain Dobbin himself had asked leave to present her with a gold chain and watch, which she sported on this occasion; and her mother gave her her diamond brooch--almost the only trinket which was left to the old lady. As the service went on, Mrs. Sedley sat and whimpered a great deal in a pew, consoled by the Irish maid-servant and Mrs. Clapp from the lodgings. Old Sedley would not be present. Jos acted for his father, giving away the bride, whilst Captain Dobbin stepped up as groomsman to his friend George.\n\nThere was nobody in the church besides the officiating persons and the small marriage party and their attendants. The two valets sat aloof superciliously. The rain came rattling down on the windows. In the intervals of the service you heard it, and the sobbing of old Mrs. Sedley in the pew. The parson's tones echoed sadly through the empty walls. Osborne's \"I will\" was sounded in very deep bass. Emmy's response came fluttering up to her lips from her heart, but was scarcely heard by anybody except Captain Dobbin.\n\nWhen the service was completed, Jos Sedley came forward and kissed his sister, the bride, for the first time for many months--George's look of gloom had gone, and he seemed quite proud and radiant. \"It's your turn, William,\" says he, putting his hand fondly upon Dobbin's shoulder; and Dobbin went up and touched Amelia on the cheek.\n\nThen they went into the vestry and signed the register. \"God bless you, Old Dobbin,\" George said, grasping him by the hand, with something very like moisture glistening in his eyes. William replied only by nodding his head. His heart was too full to say much.\n\n\"Write directly, and come down as soon as you can, you know,\" Osborne said. After Mrs. Sedley had taken an hysterical adieu of her daughter, the pair went off to the carriage. \"Get out of the way, you little devils,\" George cried to a small crowd of damp urchins, that were hanging about the chapel-door. The rain drove into the bride and bridegroom's faces as they passed to the chariot. The postilions' favours draggled on their dripping jackets. The few children made a dismal cheer, as the carriage, splashing mud, drove away.\n\nWilliam Dobbin stood in the church-porch, looking at it, a queer figure. The small crew of spectators jeered him. He was not thinking about them or their laughter.\n\n\"Come home and have some tiffin, Dobbin,\" a voice cried behind him; as a pudgy hand was laid on his shoulder, and the honest fellow's reverie was interrupted. But the Captain had no heart to go a-feasting with Jos Sedley. He put the weeping old lady and her attendants into the carriage along with Jos, and left them without any farther words passing. This carriage, too, drove away, and the urchins gave another sarcastical cheer.\n\n\"Here, you little beggars,\" Dobbin said, giving some sixpences amongst them, and then went off by himself through the rain. It was all over. They were married, and happy, he prayed God. Never since he was a boy had he felt so miserable and so lonely. He longed with a heart-sick yearning for the first few days to be over, that he might see her again.\n\nSome ten days after the above ceremony, three young men of our acquaintance were enjoying that beautiful prospect of bow windows on the one side and blue sea on the other, which Brighton affords to the traveller. Sometimes it is towards the ocean--smiling with countless dimples, speckled with white sails, with a hundred bathing-machines kissing the skirt of his blue garment--that the Londoner looks enraptured: sometimes, on the contrary, a lover of human nature rather than of prospects of any kind, it is towards the bow windows that he turns, and that swarm of human life which they exhibit. From one issue the notes of a piano, which a young lady in ringlets practises six hours daily, to the delight of the fellow-lodgers: at another, lovely Polly, the nurse-maid, may be seen dandling Master Omnium in her arms: whilst Jacob, his papa, is beheld eating prawns, and devouring the Times for breakfast, at the window below. Yonder are the Misses Leery, who are looking out for the young officers of the Heavies, who are pretty sure to be pacing the cliff; or again it is a City man, with a nautical turn, and a telescope, the size of a six-pounder, who has his instrument pointed seawards, so as to command every pleasure-boat, herring-boat, or bathing-machine that comes to, or quits, the shore, &c., &c. But have we any leisure for a description of Brighton?--for Brighton, a clean Naples with genteel lazzaroni--for Brighton, that always looks brisk, gay, and gaudy, like a harlequin's jacket--for Brighton, which used to be seven hours distant from London at the time of our story; which is now only a hundred minutes off; and which may approach who knows how much nearer, unless Joinville comes and untimely bombards it?\n\n\"What a monstrous fine girl that is in the lodgings over the milliner's,\" one of these three promenaders remarked to the other; \"Gad, Crawley, did you see what a wink she gave me as I passed?\"\n\n\"Don't break her heart, Jos, you rascal,\" said another. \"Don't trifle with her affections, you Don Juan!\"\n\n\"Get away,\" said Jos Sedley, quite pleased, and leering up at the maid-servant in question with a most killing ogle. Jos was even more splendid at Brighton than he had been at his sister's marriage. He had brilliant under-waistcoats, any one of which would have set up a moderate buck. He sported a military frock-coat, ornamented with frogs, knobs, black buttons, and meandering embroidery. He had affected a military appearance and habits of late; and he walked with his two friends, who were of that profession, clinking his boot-spurs, swaggering prodigiously, and shooting death-glances at all the servant girls who were worthy to be slain.\n\n\"What shall we do, boys, till the ladies return?\" the buck asked. The ladies were out to Rottingdean in his carriage on a drive.\n\n\"Let's have a game at billiards,\" one of his friends said--the tall one, with lacquered mustachios.\n\n\"No, dammy; no, Captain,\" Jos replied, rather alarmed. \"No billiards to-day, Crawley, my boy; yesterday was enough.\"\n\n\"You play very well,\" said Crawley, laughing. \"Don't he, Osborne? How well he made that five stroke, eh?\"\n\n\"Famous,\" Osborne said. \"Jos is a devil of a fellow at billiards, and at everything else, too. I wish there were any tiger-hunting about here! we might go and kill a few before dinner. (There goes a fine girl! what an ankle, eh, Jos?) Tell us that story about the tiger-hunt, and the way you did for him in the jungle--it's a wonderful story that, Crawley.\" Here George Osborne gave a yawn. \"It's rather slow work,\" said he, \"down here; what shall we do?\"\n\n\"Shall we go and look at some horses that Snaffler's just brought from Lewes fair?\" Crawley said.\n\n\"Suppose we go and have some jellies at Dutton's,\" and the rogue Jos, willing to kill two birds with one stone. \"Devilish fine gal at Dutton's.\"\n\n\"Suppose we go and see the Lightning come in, it's just about time?\" George said. This advice prevailing over the stables and the jelly, they turned towards the coach-office to witness the Lightning's arrival.\n\nAs they passed, they met the carriage--Jos Sedley's open carriage, with its magnificent armorial bearings--that splendid conveyance in which he used to drive, about at Cheltenham, majestic and solitary, with his arms folded, and his hat cocked; or, more happy, with ladies by his side.\n\nTwo were in the carriage now: one a little person, with light hair, and dressed in the height of the fashion; the other in a brown silk pelisse, and a straw bonnet with pink ribbons, with a rosy, round, happy face, that did you good to behold. She checked the carriage as it neared the three gentlemen, after which exercise of authority she looked rather nervous, and then began to blush most absurdly. \"We have had a delightful drive, George,\" she said, \"and--and we're so glad to come back; and, Joseph, don't let him be late.\"\n\n\"Don't be leading our husbands into mischief, Mr. Sedley, you wicked, wicked man you,\" Rebecca said, shaking at Jos a pretty little finger covered with the neatest French kid glove. \"No billiards, no smoking, no naughtiness!\"\n\n\"My dear Mrs. Crawley--Ah now! upon my honour!\" was all Jos could ejaculate by way of reply; but he managed to fall into a tolerable attitude, with his head lying on his shoulder, grinning upwards at his victim, with one hand at his back, which he supported on his cane, and the other hand (the one with the diamond ring) fumbling in his shirt-frill and among his under-waistcoats. As the carriage drove off he kissed the diamond hand to the fair ladies within. He wished all Cheltenham, all Chowringhee, all Calcutta, could see him in that position, waving his hand to such a beauty, and in company with such a famous buck as Rawdon Crawley of the Guards.\n\nOur young bride and bridegroom had chosen Brighton as the place where they would pass the first few days after their marriage; and having engaged apartments at the Ship Inn, enjoyed themselves there in great comfort and quietude, until Jos presently joined them. Nor was he the only companion they found there. As they were coming into the hotel from a sea-side walk one afternoon, on whom should they light but Rebecca and her husband. The recognition was immediate. Rebecca flew into the arms of her dearest friend. Crawley and Osborne shook hands together cordially enough: and Becky, in the course of a very few hours, found means to make the latter forget that little unpleasant passage of words which had happened between them. \"Do you remember the last time we met at Miss Crawley's, when I was so rude to you, dear Captain Osborne? I thought you seemed careless about dear Amelia. It was that made me angry: and so pert: and so unkind: and so ungrateful. Do forgive me!\" Rebecca said, and she held out her hand with so frank and winning a grace, that Osborne could not but take it. By humbly and frankly acknowledging yourself to be in the wrong, there is no knowing, my son, what good you may do. I knew once a gentleman and very worthy practitioner in Vanity Fair, who used to do little wrongs to his neighbours on purpose, and in order to apologise for them in an open and manly way afterwards--and what ensued? My friend Crocky Doyle was liked everywhere, and deemed to be rather impetuous--but the honestest fellow. Becky's humility passed for sincerity with George Osborne.\n\nThese two young couples had plenty of tales to relate to each other. The marriages of either were discussed; and their prospects in life canvassed with the greatest frankness and interest on both sides. George's marriage was to be made known to his father by his friend Captain Dobbin; and young Osborne trembled rather for the result of that communication. Miss Crawley, on whom all Rawdon's hopes depended, still held out. Unable to make an entry into her house in Park Lane, her affectionate nephew and niece had followed her to Brighton, where they had emissaries continually planted at her door.\n\n\"I wish you could see some of Rawdon's friends who are always about our door,\" Rebecca said, laughing. \"Did you ever see a dun, my dear; or a bailiff and his man? Two of the abominable wretches watched all last week at the greengrocer's opposite, and we could not get away until Sunday. If Aunty does not relent, what shall we do?\"\n\nRawdon, with roars of laughter, related a dozen amusing anecdotes of his duns, and Rebecca's adroit treatment of them. He vowed with a great oath that there was no woman in Europe who could talk a creditor over as she could. Almost immediately after their marriage, her practice had begun, and her husband found the immense value of such a wife. They had credit in plenty, but they had bills also in abundance, and laboured under a scarcity of ready money. Did these debt-difficulties affect Rawdon's good spirits? No. Everybody in Vanity Fair must have remarked how well those live who are comfortably and thoroughly in debt: how they deny themselves nothing; how jolly and easy they are in their minds. Rawdon and his wife had the very best apartments at the inn at Brighton; the landlord, as he brought in the first dish, bowed before them as to his greatest customers: and Rawdon abused the dinners and wine with an audacity which no grandee in the land could surpass. Long custom, a manly appearance, faultless boots and clothes, and a happy fierceness of manner, will often help a man as much as a great balance at the banker's.\n\nThe two wedding parties met constantly in each other's apartments. After two or three nights the gentlemen of an evening had a little piquet, as their wives sate and chatted apart. This pastime, and the arrival of Jos Sedley, who made his appearance in his grand open carriage, and who played a few games at billiards with Captain Crawley, replenished Rawdon's purse somewhat, and gave him the benefit of that ready money for which the greatest spirits are sometimes at a stand-still.\n\nSo the three gentlemen walked down to see the Lightning coach come in. Punctual to the minute, the coach crowded inside and out, the guard blowing his accustomed tune on the horn--the Lightning came tearing down the street, and pulled up at the coach-office.\n\n\"Hullo! there's old Dobbin,\" George cried, quite delighted to see his old friend perched on the roof; and whose promised visit to Brighton had been delayed until now. \"How are you, old fellow? Glad you're come down. Emmy'll be delighted to see you,\" Osborne said, shaking his comrade warmly by the hand as soon as his descent from the vehicle was effected--and then he added, in a lower and agitated voice, \"What's the news? Have you been in Russell Square? What does the governor say? Tell me everything.\"\n\nDobbin looked very pale and grave. \"I've seen your father,\" said he. \"How's Amelia--Mrs. George? I'll tell you all the news presently: but I've brought the great news of all: and that is--\"\n\n\"Out with it, old fellow,\" George said.\n\n\"We're ordered to Belgium. All the army goes--guards and all. Heavytop's got the gout, and is mad at not being able to move. O'Dowd goes in command, and we embark from Chatham next week.\" This news of war could not but come with a shock upon our lovers, and caused all these gentlemen to look very serious.\n\nCaptain Dobbin Proceeds on His Canvass\nWhat is the secret mesmerism which friendship possesses, and under the operation of which a person ordinarily sluggish, or cold, or timid, becomes wise, active, and resolute, in another's behalf? As Alexis, after a few passes from Dr. Elliotson, despises pain, reads with the back of his head, sees miles off, looks into next week, and performs other wonders, of which, in his own private normal condition, he is quite incapable; so you see, in the affairs of the world and under the magnetism of friendships, the modest man becomes bold, the shy confident, the lazy active, or the impetuous prudent and peaceful. What is it, on the other hand, that makes the lawyer eschew his own cause, and call in his learned brother as an adviser? And what causes the doctor, when ailing, to send for his rival, and not sit down and examine his own tongue in the chimney glass, or write his own prescription at his study-table? I throw out these queries for intelligent readers to answer, who know, at once, how credulous we are, and how sceptical, how soft and how obstinate, how firm for others and how diffident about ourselves: meanwhile, it is certain that our friend William Dobbin, who was personally of so complying a disposition that if his parents had pressed him much, it is probable he would have stepped down into the kitchen and married the cook, and who, to further his own interests, would have found the most insuperable difficulty in walking across the street, found himself as busy and eager in the conduct of George Osborne's affairs, as the most selfish tactician could be in the pursuit of his own.\n\nWhilst our friend George and his young wife were enjoying the first blushing days of the honeymoon at Brighton, honest William was left as George's plenipotentiary in London, to transact all the business part of the marriage. His duty it was to call upon old Sedley and his wife, and to keep the former in good humour: to draw Jos and his brother-in-law nearer together, so that Jos's position and dignity, as collector of Boggley Wollah, might compensate for his father's loss of station, and tend to reconcile old Osborne to the alliance: and finally, to communicate it to the latter in such a way as should least irritate the old gentleman.\n\nNow, before he faced the head of the Osborne house with the news which it was his duty to tell, Dobbin bethought him that it would be politic to make friends of the rest of the family, and, if possible, have the ladies on his side. They can't be angry in their hearts, thought he. No woman ever was really angry at a romantic marriage. A little crying out, and they must come round to their brother; when the three of us will lay siege to old Mr. Osborne. So this Machiavellian captain of infantry cast about him for some happy means or stratagem by which he could gently and gradually bring the Misses Osborne to a knowledge of their brother's secret.\n\nBy a little inquiry regarding his mother's engagements, he was pretty soon able to find out by whom of her ladyship's friends parties were given at that season; where he would be likely to meet Osborne's sisters; and, though he had that abhorrence of routs and evening parties which many sensible men, alas! entertain, he soon found one where the Misses Osborne were to be present. Making his appearance at the ball, where he danced a couple of sets with both of them, and was prodigiously polite, he actually had the courage to ask Miss Osborne for a few minutes' conversation at an early hour the next day, when he had, he said, to communicate to her news of the very greatest interest.\n\nWhat was it that made her start back, and gaze upon him for a moment, and then on the ground at her feet, and make as if she would faint on his arm, had he not by opportunely treading on her toes, brought the young lady back to self-control? Why was she so violently agitated at Dobbin's request? This can never be known. But when he came the next day, Maria was not in the drawing-room with her sister, and Miss Wirt went off for the purpose of fetching the latter, and the Captain and Miss Osborne were left together. They were both so silent that the ticktock of the Sacrifice of Iphigenia clock on the mantelpiece became quite rudely audible.\n\n\"What a nice party it was last night,\" Miss Osborne at length began, encouragingly; \"and--and how you're improved in your dancing, Captain Dobbin. Surely somebody has taught you,\" she added, with amiable archness.\n\n\"You should see me dance a reel with Mrs. Major O'Dowd of ours; and a jig--did you ever see a jig? But I think anybody could dance with you, Miss Osborne, who dance so well.\"\n\n\"Is the Major's lady young and beautiful, Captain?\" the fair questioner continued. \"Ah, what a terrible thing it must be to be a soldier's wife! I wonder they have any spirits to dance, and in these dreadful times of war, too! O Captain Dobbin, I tremble sometimes when I think of our dearest George, and the dangers of the poor soldier. Are there many married officers of the --th, Captain Dobbin?\"\n\n\"Upon my word, she's playing her hand rather too openly,\" Miss Wirt thought; but this observation is merely parenthetic, and was not heard through the crevice of the door at which the governess uttered it.\n\n\"One of our young men is just married,\" Dobbin said, now coming to the point. \"It was a very old attachment, and the young couple are as poor as church mice.\" \"O, how delightful! O, how romantic!\" Miss Osborne cried, as the Captain said \"old attachment\" and \"poor.\" Her sympathy encouraged him.\n\n\"The finest young fellow in the regiment,\" he continued. \"Not a braver or handsomer officer in the army; and such a charming wife! How you would like her! how you will like her when you know her, Miss Osborne.\" The young lady thought the actual moment had arrived, and that Dobbin's nervousness which now came on and was visible in many twitchings of his face, in his manner of beating the ground with his great feet, in the rapid buttoning and unbuttoning of his frock-coat, &c.--Miss Osborne, I say, thought that when he had given himself a little air, he would unbosom himself entirely, and prepared eagerly to listen. And the clock, in the altar on which Iphigenia was situated, beginning, after a preparatory convulsion, to toll twelve, the mere tolling seemed as if it would last until one--so prolonged was the knell to the anxious spinster.\n\n\"But it's not about marriage that I came to speak--that is that marriage--that is--no, I mean--my dear Miss Osborne, it's about our dear friend George,\" Dobbin said.\n\n\"About George?\" she said in a tone so discomfited that Maria and Miss Wirt laughed at the other side of the door, and even that abandoned wretch of a Dobbin felt inclined to smile himself; for he was not altogether unconscious of the state of affairs: George having often bantered him gracefully and said, \"Hang it, Will, why don't you take old Jane? She'll have you if you ask her. I'll bet you five to two she will.\"\n\n\"Yes, about George, then,\" he continued. \"There has been a difference between him and Mr. Osborne. And I regard him so much--for you know we have been like brothers--that I hope and pray the quarrel may be settled. We must go abroad, Miss Osborne. We may be ordered off at a day's warning. Who knows what may happen in the campaign? Don't be agitated, dear Miss Osborne; and those two at least should part friends.\"\n\n\"There has been no quarrel, Captain Dobbin, except a little usual scene with Papa,\" the lady said. \"We are expecting George back daily. What Papa wanted was only for his good. He has but to come back, and I'm sure all will be well; and dear Rhoda, who went away from here in sad sad anger, I know will forgive him. Woman forgives but too readily, Captain.\"\n\n\"Such an angel as YOU I am sure would,\" Mr. Dobbin said, with atrocious astuteness. \"And no man can pardon himself for giving a woman pain. What would you feel, if a man were faithless to you?\"\n\n\"I should perish--I should throw myself out of window--I should take poison--I should pine and die. I know I should,\" Miss cried, who had nevertheless gone through one or two affairs of the heart without any idea of suicide.\n\n\"And there are others,\" Dobbin continued, \"as true and as kind-hearted as yourself. I'm not speaking about the West Indian heiress, Miss Osborne, but about a poor girl whom George once loved, and who was bred from her childhood to think of nobody but him. I've seen her in her poverty uncomplaining, broken-hearted, without a fault. It is of Miss Sedley I speak. Dear Miss Osborne, can your generous heart quarrel with your brother for being faithful to her? Could his own conscience ever forgive him if he deserted her? Be her friend--she always loved you--and--and I am come here charged by George to tell you that he holds his engagement to her as the most sacred duty he has; and to entreat you, at least, to be on his side.\"\n\nWhen any strong emotion took possession of Mr. Dobbin, and after the first word or two of hesitation, he could speak with perfect fluency, and it was evident that his eloquence on this occasion made some impression upon the lady whom he addressed.\n\n\"Well,\" said she, \"this is--most surprising--most painful--most extraordinary--what will Papa say?--that George should fling away such a superb establishment as was offered to him but at any rate he has found a very brave champion in you, Captain Dobbin. It is of no use, however,\" she continued, after a pause; \"I feel for poor Miss Sedley, most certainly--most sincerely, you know. We never thought the match a good one, though we were always very kind to her here--very. But Papa will never consent, I am sure. And a well brought up young woman, you know--with a well-regulated mind, must--George must give her up, dear Captain Dobbin, indeed he must.\"\n\n\"Ought a man to give up the woman he loved, just when misfortune befell her?\" Dobbin said, holding out his hand. \"Dear Miss Osborne, is this the counsel I hear from you? My dear young lady! you must befriend her. He can't give her up. He must not give her up. Would a man, think you, give YOU up if you were poor?\"\n\nThis adroit question touched the heart of Miss Jane Osborne not a little. \"I don't know whether we poor girls ought to believe what you men say, Captain,\" she said. \"There is that in woman's tenderness which induces her to believe too easily. I'm afraid you are cruel, cruel deceivers,\"--and Dobbin certainly thought he felt a pressure of the hand which Miss Osborne had extended to him.\n\nHe dropped it in some alarm. \"Deceivers!\" said he. \"No, dear Miss Osborne, all men are not; your brother is not; George has loved Amelia Sedley ever since they were children; no wealth would make him marry any but her. Ought he to forsake her? Would you counsel him to do so?\"\n\nWhat could Miss Jane say to such a question, and with her own peculiar views? She could not answer it, so she parried it by saying, \"Well, if you are not a deceiver, at least you are very romantic\"; and Captain William let this observation pass without challenge.\n\nAt length when, by the help of farther polite speeches, he deemed that Miss Osborne was sufficiently prepared to receive the whole news, he poured it into her ear. \"George could not give up Amelia--George was married to her\"--and then he related the circumstances of the marriage as we know them already: how the poor girl would have died had not her lover kept his faith: how Old Sedley had refused all consent to the match, and a licence had been got: and Jos Sedley had come from Cheltenham to give away the bride: how they had gone to Brighton in Jos's chariot-and-four to pass the honeymoon: and how George counted on his dear kind sisters to befriend him with their father, as women--so true and tender as they were--assuredly would do. And so, asking permission (readily granted) to see her again, and rightly conjecturing that the news he had brought would be told in the next five minutes to the other ladies, Captain Dobbin made his bow and took his leave.\n\nHe was scarcely out of the house, when Miss Maria and Miss Wirt rushed in to Miss Osborne, and the whole wonderful secret was imparted to them by that lady. To do them justice, neither of the sisters was very much displeased. There is something about a runaway match with which few ladies can be seriously angry, and Amelia rather rose in their estimation, from the spirit which she had displayed in consenting to the union. As they debated the story, and prattled about it, and wondered what Papa would do and say, came a loud knock, as of an avenging thunder-clap, at the door, which made these conspirators start. It must be Papa, they thought. But it was not he. It was only Mr. Frederick Bullock, who had come from the City according to appointment, to conduct the ladies to a flower-show.\n\nThis gentleman, as may be imagined, was not kept long in ignorance of the secret. But his face, when he heard it, showed an amazement which was very different to that look of sentimental wonder which the countenances of the sisters wore. Mr. Bullock was a man of the world, and a junior partner of a wealthy firm. He knew what money was, and the value of it: and a delightful throb of expectation lighted up his little eyes, and caused him to smile on his Maria, as he thought that by this piece of folly of Mr. George's she might be worth thirty thousand pounds more than he had ever hoped to get with her.\n\n\"Gad! Jane,\" said he, surveying even the elder sister with some interest, \"Eels will be sorry he cried off. You may be a fifty thousand pounder yet.\"\n\nThe sisters had never thought of the money question up to that moment, but Fred Bullock bantered them with graceful gaiety about it during their forenoon's excursion; and they had risen not a little in their own esteem by the time when, the morning amusement over, they drove back to dinner. And do not let my respected reader exclaim against this selfishness as unnatural. It was but this present morning, as he rode on the omnibus from Richmond; while it changed horses, this present chronicler, being on the roof, marked three little children playing in a puddle below, very dirty, and friendly, and happy. To these three presently came another little one. \"POLLY,\" says she, \"YOUR SISTER'S GOT A PENNY.\" At which the children got up from the puddle instantly, and ran off to pay their court to Peggy. And as the omnibus drove off I saw Peggy with the infantine procession at her tail, marching with great dignity towards the stall of a neighbouring lollipop-woman.\n\nIn Which Mr. Osborne Takes Down the Family Bible\nSo having prepared the sisters, Dobbin hastened away to the City to perform the rest and more difficult part of the task which he had undertaken. The idea of facing old Osborne rendered him not a little nervous, and more than once he thought of leaving the young ladies to communicate the secret, which, as he was aware, they could not long retain. But he had promised to report to George upon the manner in which the elder Osborne bore the intelligence; so going into the City to the paternal counting-house in Thames Street, he despatched thence a note to Mr. Osborne begging for a half-hour's conversation relative to the affairs of his son George. Dobbin's messenger returned from Mr. Osborne's house of business, with the compliments of the latter, who would be very happy to see the Captain immediately, and away accordingly Dobbin went to confront him.\n\nThe Captain, with a half-guilty secret to confess, and with the prospect of a painful and stormy interview before him, entered Mr. Osborne's offices with a most dismal countenance and abashed gait, and, passing through the outer room where Mr. Chopper presided, was greeted by that functionary from his desk with a waggish air which farther discomfited him. Mr. Chopper winked and nodded and pointed his pen towards his patron's door, and said, \"You'll find the governor all right,\" with the most provoking good humour.\n\nOsborne rose too, and shook him heartily by the hand, and said, \"How do, my dear boy?\" with a cordiality that made poor George's ambassador feel doubly guilty. His hand lay as if dead in the old gentleman's grasp. He felt that he, Dobbin, was more or less the cause of all that had happened. It was he had brought back George to Amelia: it was he had applauded, encouraged, transacted almost the marriage which he was come to reveal to George's father: and the latter was receiving him with smiles of welcome; patting him on the shoulder, and calling him \"Dobbin, my dear boy.\" The envoy had indeed good reason to hang his head.\n\nOsborne fully believed that Dobbin had come to announce his son's surrender. Mr. Chopper and his principal were talking over the matter between George and his father, at the very moment when Dobbin's messenger arrived. Both agreed that George was sending in his submission. Both had been expecting it for some days--and \"Lord! Chopper, what a marriage we'll have!\" Mr. Osborne said to his clerk, snapping his big fingers, and jingling all the guineas and shillings in his great pockets as he eyed his subordinate with a look of triumph.\n\nWith similar operations conducted in both pockets, and a knowing jolly air, Osborne from his chair regarded Dobbin seated blank and silent opposite to him. \"What a bumpkin he is for a Captain in the army,\" old Osborne thought. \"I wonder George hasn't taught him better manners.\"\n\nAt last Dobbin summoned courage to begin. \"Sir,\" said he, \"I've brought you some very grave news. I have been at the Horse Guards this morning, and there's no doubt that our regiment will be ordered abroad, and on its way to Belgium before the week is over. And you know, sir, that we shan't be home again before a tussle which may be fatal to many of us.\" Osborne looked grave. \"My s--, the regiment will do its duty, sir, I daresay,\" he said.\n\n\"The French are very strong, sir,\" Dobbin went on. \"The Russians and Austrians will be a long time before they can bring their troops down. We shall have the first of the fight, sir; and depend on it Boney will take care that it shall be a hard one.\"\n\n\"What are you driving at, Dobbin?\" his interlocutor said, uneasy and with a scowl. \"I suppose no Briton's afraid of any d---- Frenchman, hey?\"\n\n\"I only mean, that before we go, and considering the great and certain risk that hangs over every one of us--if there are any differences between you and George--it would be as well, sir, that--that you should shake hands: wouldn't it? Should anything happen to him, I think you would never forgive yourself if you hadn't parted in charity.\"\n\nAs he said this, poor William Dobbin blushed crimson, and felt and owned that he himself was a traitor. But for him, perhaps, this severance need never have taken place. Why had not George's marriage been delayed? What call was there to press it on so eagerly? He felt that George would have parted from Amelia at any rate without a mortal pang. Amelia, too, MIGHT have recovered the shock of losing him. It was his counsel had brought about this marriage, and all that was to ensue from it. And why was it? Because he loved her so much that he could not bear to see her unhappy: or because his own sufferings of suspense were so unendurable that he was glad to crush them at once--as we hasten a funeral after a death, or, when a separation from those we love is imminent, cannot rest until the parting be over.\n\n\"You are a good fellow, William,\" said Mr. Osborne in a softened voice; \"and me and George shouldn't part in anger, that is true. Look here. I've done for him as much as any father ever did. He's had three times as much money from me, as I warrant your father ever gave you. But I don't brag about that. How I've toiled for him, and worked and employed my talents and energy, I won't say. Ask Chopper. Ask himself. Ask the City of London. Well, I propose to him such a marriage as any nobleman in the land might be proud of--the only thing in life I ever asked him--and he refuses me. Am I wrong? Is the quarrel of MY making? What do I seek but his good, for which I've been toiling like a convict ever since he was born? Nobody can say there's anything selfish in me. Let him come back. I say, here's my hand. I say, forget and forgive. As for marrying now, it's out of the question. Let him and Miss S. make it up, and make out the marriage afterwards, when he comes back a Colonel; for he shall be a Colonel, by G-- he shall, if money can do it. I'm glad you've brought him round. I know it's you, Dobbin. You've took him out of many a scrape before. Let him come. I shan't be hard. Come along, and dine in Russell Square to-day: both of you. The old shop, the old hour. You'll find a neck of venison, and no questions asked.\"\n\nThis praise and confidence smote Dobbin's heart very keenly. Every moment the colloquy continued in this tone, he felt more and more guilty. \"Sir,\" said he, \"I fear you deceive yourself. I am sure you do. George is much too high-minded a man ever to marry for money. A threat on your part that you would disinherit him in case of disobedience would only be followed by resistance on his.\"\n\n\"Why, hang it, man, you don't call offering him eight or ten thousand a year threatening him?\" Mr. Osborne said, with still provoking good humour. \"'Gad, if Miss S. will have me, I'm her man. I ain't particular about a shade or so of tawny.\" And the old gentleman gave his knowing grin and coarse laugh.\n\n\"You forget, sir, previous engagements into which Captain Osborne had entered,\" the ambassador said, gravely.\n\n\"What engagements? What the devil do you mean? You don't mean,\" Mr. Osborne continued, gathering wrath and astonishment as the thought now first came upon him; \"you don't mean that he's such a d---- fool as to be still hankering after that swindling old bankrupt's daughter? You've not come here for to make me suppose that he wants to marry HER? Marry HER, that IS a good one. My son and heir marry a beggar's girl out of a gutter. D---- him, if he does, let him buy a broom and sweep a crossing. She was always dangling and ogling after him, I recollect now; and I've no doubt she was put on by her old sharper of a father.\"\n\n\"Mr. Sedley was your very good friend, sir,\" Dobbin interposed, almost pleased at finding himself growing angry. \"Time was you called him better names than rogue and swindler. The match was of your making. George had no right to play fast and loose--\"\n\n\"Fast and loose!\" howled out old Osborne. \"Fast and loose! Why, hang me, those are the very words my gentleman used himself when he gave himself airs, last Thursday was a fortnight, and talked about the British army to his father who made him. What, it's you who have been a setting of him up--is it? and my service to you, CAPTAIN. It's you who want to introduce beggars into my family. Thank you for nothing, Captain. Marry HER indeed--he, he! why should he? I warrant you she'd go to him fast enough without.\"\n\n\"Sir,\" said Dobbin, starting up in undisguised anger; \"no man shall abuse that lady in my hearing, and you least of all.\"\n\n\"O, you're a-going to call me out, are you? Stop, let me ring the bell for pistols for two. Mr. George sent you here to insult his father, did he?\" Osborne said, pulling at the bell-cord.\n\n\"Mr. Osborne,\" said Dobbin, with a faltering voice, \"it's you who are insulting the best creature in the world. You had best spare her, sir, for she's your son's wife.\"\n\nAnd with this, feeling that he could say no more, Dobbin went away, Osborne sinking back in his chair, and looking wildly after him. A clerk came in, obedient to the bell; and the Captain was scarcely out of the court where Mr. Osborne's offices were, when Mr. Chopper the chief clerk came rushing hatless after him.\n\n\"For God's sake, what is it?\" Mr. Chopper said, catching the Captain by the skirt. \"The governor's in a fit. What has Mr. George been doing?\"\n\n\"He married Miss Sedley five days ago,\" Dobbin replied. \"I was his groomsman, Mr. Chopper, and you must stand his friend.\"\n\nThe old clerk shook his head. \"If that's your news, Captain, it's bad. The governor will never forgive him.\"\n\nDobbin begged Chopper to report progress to him at the hotel where he was stopping, and walked off moodily westwards, greatly perturbed as to the past and the future.\n\nWhen the Russell Square family came to dinner that evening, they found the father of the house seated in his usual place, but with that air of gloom on his face, which, whenever it appeared there, kept the whole circle silent. The ladies, and Mr. Bullock who dined with them, felt that the news had been communicated to Mr. Osborne. His dark looks affected Mr. Bullock so far as to render him still and quiet: but he was unusually bland and attentive to Miss Maria, by whom he sat, and to her sister presiding at the head of the table.\n\nMiss Wirt, by consequence, was alone on her side of the board, a gap being left between her and Miss Jane Osborne. Now this was George's place when he dined at home; and his cover, as we said, was laid for him in expectation of that truant's return. Nothing occurred during dinner-time except smiling Mr. Frederick's flagging confidential whispers, and the clinking of plate and china, to interrupt the silence of the repast. The servants went about stealthily doing their duty. Mutes at funerals could not look more glum than the domestics of Mr. Osborne The neck of venison of which he had invited Dobbin to partake, was carved by him in perfect silence; but his own share went away almost untasted, though he drank much, and the butler assiduously filled his glass.\n\nAt last, just at the end of the dinner, his eyes, which had been staring at everybody in turn, fixed themselves for a while upon the plate laid for George. He pointed to it presently with his left hand. His daughters looked at him and did not comprehend, or choose to comprehend, the signal; nor did the servants at first understand it.\n\n\"Take that plate away,\" at last he said, getting up with an oath--and with this pushing his chair back, he walked into his own room.\n\nBehind Mr. Osborne's dining-room was the usual apartment which went in his house by the name of the study; and was sacred to the master of the house. Hither Mr. Osborne would retire of a Sunday forenoon when not minded to go to church; and here pass the morning in his crimson leather chair, reading the paper. A couple of glazed book-cases were here, containing standard works in stout gilt bindings. The \"Annual Register,\" the \"Gentleman's Magazine,\" \"Blair's Sermons,\" and \"Hume and Smollett.\" From year's end to year's end he never took one of these volumes from the shelf; but there was no member of the family that would dare for his life to touch one of the books, except upon those rare Sunday evenings when there was no dinner-party, and when the great scarlet Bible and Prayer-book were taken out from the corner where they stood beside his copy of the Peerage, and the servants being rung up to the dining parlour, Osborne read the evening service to his family in a loud grating pompous voice. No member of the household, child, or domestic, ever entered that room without a certain terror. Here he checked the housekeeper's accounts, and overhauled the butler's cellar-book. Hence he could command, across the clean gravel court-yard, the back entrance of the stables with which one of his bells communicated, and into this yard the coachman issued from his premises as into a dock, and Osborne swore at him from the study window. Four times a year Miss Wirt entered this apartment to get her salary; and his daughters to receive their quarterly allowance. George as a boy had been horsewhipped in this room many times; his mother sitting sick on the stair listening to the cuts of the whip. The boy was scarcely ever known to cry under the punishment; the poor woman used to fondle and kiss him secretly, and give him money to soothe him when he came out.\n\nThere was a picture of the family over the mantelpiece, removed thither from the front room after Mrs. Osborne's death--George was on a pony, the elder sister holding him up a bunch of flowers; the younger led by her mother's hand; all with red cheeks and large red mouths, simpering on each other in the approved family-portrait manner. The mother lay underground now, long since forgotten--the sisters and brother had a hundred different interests of their own, and, familiar still, were utterly estranged from each other. Some few score of years afterwards, when all the parties represented are grown old, what bitter satire there is in those flaunting childish family-portraits, with their farce of sentiment and smiling lies, and innocence so self-conscious and self-satisfied. Osborne's own state portrait, with that of his great silver inkstand and arm-chair, had taken the place of honour in the dining-room, vacated by the family-piece.\n\nTo this study old Osborne retired then, greatly to the relief of the small party whom he left. When the servants had withdrawn, they began to talk for a while volubly but very low; then they went upstairs quietly, Mr. Bullock accompanying them stealthily on his creaking shoes. He had no heart to sit alone drinking wine, and so close to the terrible old gentleman in the study hard at hand.\n\nAn hour at least after dark, the butler, not having received any summons, ventured to tap at his door and take him in wax candles and tea. The master of the house sate in his chair, pretending to read the paper, and when the servant, placing the lights and refreshment on the table by him, retired, Mr. Osborne got up and locked the door after him. This time there was no mistaking the matter; all the household knew that some great catastrophe was going to happen which was likely direly to affect Master George.\n\nIn the large shining mahogany escritoire Mr. Osborne had a drawer especially devoted to his son's affairs and papers. Here he kept all the documents relating to him ever since he had been a boy: here were his prize copy-books and drawing-books, all bearing George's hand, and that of the master: here were his first letters in large round-hand sending his love to papa and mamma, and conveying his petitions for a cake. His dear godpapa Sedley was more than once mentioned in them. Curses quivered on old Osborne's livid lips, and horrid hatred and disappointment writhed in his heart, as looking through some of these papers he came on that name. They were all marked and docketed, and tied with red tape. It was--\"From Georgy, requesting 5s., April 23, 18--; answered, April 25\"--or \"Georgy about a pony, October 13\"--and so forth. In another packet were \"Dr. S.'s accounts\"--\"G.'s tailor's bills and outfits, drafts on me by G. Osborne, jun.,\" &c.--his letters from the West Indies--his agent's letters, and the newspapers containing his commissions: here was a whip he had when a boy, and in a paper a locket containing his hair, which his mother used to wear.\n\nTurning one over after another, and musing over these memorials, the unhappy man passed many hours. His dearest vanities, ambitious hopes, had all been here. What pride he had in his boy! He was the handsomest child ever seen. Everybody said he was like a nobleman's son. A royal princess had remarked him, and kissed him, and asked his name in Kew Gardens. What City man could show such another? Could a prince have been better cared for? Anything that money could buy had been his son's. He used to go down on speech-days with four horses and new liveries, and scatter new shillings among the boys at the school where George was: when he went with George to the depot of his regiment, before the boy embarked for Canada, he gave the officers such a dinner as the Duke of York might have sat down to. Had he ever refused a bill when George drew one? There they were--paid without a word. Many a general in the army couldn't ride the horses he had! He had the child before his eyes, on a hundred different days when he remembered George after dinner, when he used to come in as bold as a lord and drink off his glass by his father's side, at the head of the table--on the pony at Brighton, when he cleared the hedge and kept up with the huntsman--on the day when he was presented to the Prince Regent at the levee, when all Saint James's couldn't produce a finer young fellow. And this, this was the end of all!--to marry a bankrupt and fly in the face of duty and fortune! What humiliation and fury: what pangs of sickening rage, balked ambition and love; what wounds of outraged vanity, tenderness even, had this old worldling now to suffer under!\n\nHaving examined these papers, and pondered over this one and the other, in that bitterest of all helpless woe, with which miserable men think of happy past times--George's father took the whole of the documents out of the drawer in which he had kept them so long, and locked them into a writing-box, which he tied, and sealed with his seal. Then he opened the book-case, and took down the great red Bible we have spoken of a pompous book, seldom looked at, and shining all over with gold. There was a frontispiece to the volume, representing Abraham sacrificing Isaac. Here, according to custom, Osborne had recorded on the fly-leaf, and in his large clerk-like hand, the dates of his marriage and his wife's death, and the births and Christian names of his children. Jane came first, then George Sedley Osborne, then Maria Frances, and the days of the christening of each. Taking a pen, he carefully obliterated George's names from the page; and when the leaf was quite dry, restored the volume to the place from which he had moved it. Then he took a document out of another drawer, where his own private papers were kept; and having read it, crumpled it up and lighted it at one of the candles, and saw it burn entirely away in the grate. It was his will; which being burned, he sate down and wrote off a letter, and rang for his servant, whom he charged to deliver it in the morning. It was morning already: as he went up to bed, the whole house was alight with the sunshine; and the birds were singing among the fresh green leaves in Russell Square.\n\nAnxious to keep all Mr. Osborne's family and dependants in good humour, and to make as many friends as possible for George in his hour of adversity, William Dobbin, who knew the effect which good dinners and good wines have upon the soul of man, wrote off immediately on his return to his inn the most hospitable of invitations to Thomas Chopper, Esquire, begging that gentleman to dine with him at the Slaughters' next day. The note reached Mr. Chopper before he left the City, and the instant reply was, that \"Mr. Chopper presents his respectful compliments, and will have the honour and pleasure of waiting on Captain D.\" The invitation and the rough draft of the answer were shown to Mrs. Chopper and her daughters on his return to Somers' Town that evening, and they talked about military gents and West End men with great exultation as the family sate and partook of tea. When the girls had gone to rest, Mr. and Mrs. C. discoursed upon the strange events which were occurring in the governor's family. Never had the clerk seen his principal so moved. When he went in to Mr. Osborne, after Captain Dobbin's departure, Mr. Chopper found his chief black in the face, and all but in a fit: some dreadful quarrel, he was certain, had occurred between Mr. O. and the young Captain. Chopper had been instructed to make out an account of all sums paid to Captain Osborne within the last three years. \"And a precious lot of money he has had too,\" the chief clerk said, and respected his old and young master the more, for the liberal way in which the guineas had been flung about. The dispute was something about Miss Sedley. Mrs. Chopper vowed and declared she pitied that poor young lady to lose such a handsome young fellow as the Capting. As the daughter of an unlucky speculator, who had paid a very shabby dividend, Mr. Chopper had no great regard for Miss Sedley. He respected the house of Osborne before all others in the City of London: and his hope and wish was that Captain George should marry a nobleman's daughter. The clerk slept a great deal sounder than his principal that night; and, cuddling his children after breakfast (of which he partook with a very hearty appetite, though his modest cup of life was only sweetened with brown sugar), he set off in his best Sunday suit and frilled shirt for business, promising his admiring wife not to punish Captain D.'s port too severely that evening.\n\nMr. Osborne's countenance, when he arrived in the City at his usual time, struck those dependants who were accustomed, for good reasons, to watch its expression, as peculiarly ghastly and worn. At twelve o'clock Mr. Higgs (of the firm of Higgs & Blatherwick, solicitors, Bedford Row) called by appointment, and was ushered into the governor's private room, and closeted there for more than an hour. At about one Mr. Chopper received a note brought by Captain Dobbin's man, and containing an inclosure for Mr. Osborne, which the clerk went in and delivered. A short time afterwards Mr. Chopper and Mr. Birch, the next clerk, were summoned, and requested to witness a paper. \"I've been making a new will,\" Mr. Osborne said, to which these gentlemen appended their names accordingly. No conversation passed. Mr. Higgs looked exceedingly grave as he came into the outer rooms, and very hard in Mr. Chopper's face; but there were not any explanations. It was remarked that Mr. Osborne was particularly quiet and gentle all day, to the surprise of those who had augured ill from his darkling demeanour. He called no man names that day, and was not heard to swear once. He left business early; and before going away, summoned his chief clerk once more, and having given him general instructions, asked him, after some seeming hesitation and reluctance to speak, if he knew whether Captain Dobbin was in town?\n\nChopper said he believed he was. Indeed both of them knew the fact perfectly.\n\nOsborne took a letter directed to that officer, and giving it to the clerk, requested the latter to deliver it into Dobbin's own hands immediately.\n\n\"And now, Chopper,\" says he, taking his hat, and with a strange look, \"my mind will be easy.\" Exactly as the clock struck two (there was no doubt an appointment between the pair) Mr. Frederick Bullock called, and he and Mr. Osborne walked away together.\n\nThe Colonel of the --th regiment, in which Messieurs Dobbin and Osborne had companies, was an old General who had made his first campaign under Wolfe at Quebec, and was long since quite too old and feeble for command; but he took some interest in the regiment of which he was the nominal head, and made certain of his young officers welcome at his table, a kind of hospitality which I believe is not now common amongst his brethren. Captain Dobbin was an especial favourite of this old General. Dobbin was versed in the literature of his profession, and could talk about the great Frederick, and the Empress Queen, and their wars, almost as well as the General himself, who was indifferent to the triumphs of the present day, and whose heart was with the tacticians of fifty years back. This officer sent a summons to Dobbin to come and breakfast with him, on the morning when Mr. Osborne altered his will and Mr. Chopper put on his best shirt frill, and then informed his young favourite, a couple of days in advance, of that which they were all expecting--a marching order to go to Belgium. The order for the regiment to hold itself in readiness would leave the Horse Guards in a day or two; and as transports were in plenty, they would get their route before the week was over. Recruits had come in during the stay of the regiment at Chatham; and the old General hoped that the regiment which had helped to beat Montcalm in Canada, and to rout Mr. Washington on Long Island, would prove itself worthy of its historical reputation on the oft-trodden battle-grounds of the Low Countries. \"And so, my good friend, if you have any affaire la,\" said the old General, taking a pinch of snuff with his trembling white old hand, and then pointing to the spot of his robe de chambre under which his heart was still feebly beating, \"if you have any Phillis to console, or to bid farewell to papa and mamma, or any will to make, I recommend you to set about your business without delay.\" With which the General gave his young friend a finger to shake, and a good-natured nod of his powdered and pigtailed head; and the door being closed upon Dobbin, sate down to pen a poulet (he was exceedingly vain of his French) to Mademoiselle Amenaide of His Majesty's Theatre.\n\nThis news made Dobbin grave, and he thought of our friends at Brighton, and then he was ashamed of himself that Amelia was always the first thing in his thoughts (always before anybody--before father and mother, sisters and duty--always at waking and sleeping indeed, and all day long); and returning to his hotel, he sent off a brief note to Mr. Osborne acquainting him with the information which he had received, and which might tend farther, he hoped, to bring about a reconciliation with George.\n\nThis note, despatched by the same messenger who had carried the invitation to Chopper on the previous day, alarmed the worthy clerk not a little. It was inclosed to him, and as he opened the letter he trembled lest the dinner should be put off on which he was calculating. His mind was inexpressibly relieved when he found that the envelope was only a reminder for himself. (\"I shall expect you at half-past five,\" Captain Dobbin wrote.) He was very much interested about his employer's family; but, que voulez-vous? a grand dinner was of more concern to him than the affairs of any other mortal.\n\nDobbin was quite justified in repeating the General's information to any officers of the regiment whom he should see in the course of his peregrinations; accordingly he imparted it to Ensign Stubble, whom he met at the agent's, and who--such was his military ardour--went off instantly to purchase a new sword at the accoutrement-maker's. Here this young fellow, who, though only seventeen years of age, and about sixty-five inches high, with a constitution naturally rickety and much impaired by premature brandy and water, had an undoubted courage and a lion's heart, poised, tried, bent, and balanced a weapon such as he thought would do execution amongst Frenchmen. Shouting \"Ha, ha!\" and stamping his little feet with tremendous energy, he delivered the point twice or thrice at Captain Dobbin, who parried the thrust laughingly with his bamboo walking-stick.\n\nMr. Stubble, as may be supposed from his size and slenderness, was of the Light Bobs. Ensign Spooney, on the contrary, was a tall youth, and belonged to (Captain Dobbin's) the Grenadier Company, and he tried on a new bearskin cap, under which he looked savage beyond his years. Then these two lads went off to the Slaughters', and having ordered a famous dinner, sate down and wrote off letters to the kind anxious parents at home--letters full of love and heartiness, and pluck and bad spelling. Ah! there were many anxious hearts beating through England at that time; and mothers' prayers and tears flowing in many homesteads.\n\nSeeing young Stubble engaged in composition at one of the coffee-room tables at the Slaughters', and the tears trickling down his nose on to the paper (for the youngster was thinking of his mamma, and that he might never see her again), Dobbin, who was going to write off a letter to George Osborne, relented, and locked up his desk. \"Why should I?\" said he. \"Let her have this night happy. I'll go and see my parents early in the morning, and go down to Brighton myself to-morrow.\"\n\nSo he went up and laid his big hand on young Stubble's shoulder, and backed up that young champion, and told him if he would leave off brandy and water he would be a good soldier, as he always was a gentlemanly good-hearted fellow. Young Stubble's eyes brightened up at this, for Dobbin was greatly respected in the regiment, as the best officer and the cleverest man in it.\n\n\"Thank you, Dobbin,\" he said, rubbing his eyes with his knuckles, \"I was just--just telling her I would. And, O Sir, she's so dam kind to me.\" The water pumps were at work again, and I am not sure that the soft-hearted Captain's eyes did not also twinkle.\n\nThe two ensigns, the Captain, and Mr. Chopper, dined together in the same box. Chopper brought the letter from Mr. Osborne, in which the latter briefly presented his compliments to Captain Dobbin, and requested him to forward the inclosed to Captain George Osborne. Chopper knew nothing further; he described Mr. Osborne's appearance, it is true, and his interview with his lawyer, wondered how the governor had sworn at nobody, and--especially as the wine circled round--abounded in speculations and conjectures. But these grew more vague with every glass, and at length became perfectly unintelligible. At a late hour Captain Dobbin put his guest into a hackney coach, in a hiccupping state, and swearing that he would be the kick--the kick--Captain's friend for ever and ever.\n\nWhen Captain Dobbin took leave of Miss Osborne we have said that he asked leave to come and pay her another visit, and the spinster expected him for some hours the next day, when, perhaps, had he come, and had he asked her that question which she was prepared to answer, she would have declared herself as her brother's friend, and a reconciliation might have been effected between George and his angry father. But though she waited at home the Captain never came. He had his own affairs to pursue; his own parents to visit and console; and at an early hour of the day to take his place on the Lightning coach, and go down to his friends at Brighton. In the course of the day Miss Osborne heard her father give orders that that meddling scoundrel, Captain Dobbin, should never be admitted within his doors again, and any hopes in which she may have indulged privately were thus abruptly brought to an end. Mr. Frederick Bullock came, and was particularly affectionate to Maria, and attentive to the broken-spirited old gentleman. For though he said his mind would be easy, the means which he had taken to secure quiet did not seem to have succeeded as yet, and the events of the past two days had visibly shattered him.\n\n# CHAPTER XXV\n\nIn Which All the Principal Personages Think Fit to Leave Brighton\nConducted to the ladies, at the Ship Inn, Dobbin assumed a jovial and rattling manner, which proved that this young officer was becoming a more consummate hypocrite every day of his life. He was trying to hide his own private feelings, first upon seeing Mrs. George Osborne in her new condition, and secondly to mask the apprehensions he entertained as to the effect which the dismal news brought down by him would certainly have upon her.\n\n\"It is my opinion, George,\" he said, \"that the French Emperor will be upon us, horse and foot, before three weeks are over, and will give the Duke such a dance as shall make the Peninsula appear mere child's play. But you need not say that to Mrs. Osborne, you know. There mayn't be any fighting on our side after all, and our business in Belgium may turn out to be a mere military occupation. Many persons think so; and Brussels is full of fine people and ladies of fashion.\" So it was agreed to represent the duty of the British army in Belgium in this harmless light to Amelia.\n\nThis plot being arranged, the hypocritical Dobbin saluted Mrs. George Osborne quite gaily, tried to pay her one or two compliments relative to her new position as a bride (which compliments, it must be confessed, were exceedingly clumsy and hung fire woefully), and then fell to talking about Brighton, and the sea-air, and the gaieties of the place, and the beauties of the road and the merits of the Lightning coach and horses--all in a manner quite incomprehensible to Amelia, and very amusing to Rebecca, who was watching the Captain, as indeed she watched every one near whom she came.\n\nLittle Amelia, it must be owned, had rather a mean opinion of her husband's friend, Captain Dobbin. He lisped--he was very plain and homely-looking: and exceedingly awkward and ungainly. She liked him for his attachment to her husband (to be sure there was very little merit in that), and she thought George was most generous and kind in extending his friendship to his brother officer. George had mimicked Dobbin's lisp and queer manners many times to her, though to do him justice, he always spoke most highly of his friend's good qualities. In her little day of triumph, and not knowing him intimately as yet, she made light of honest William--and he knew her opinions of him quite well, and acquiesced in them very humbly. A time came when she knew him better, and changed her notions regarding him; but that was distant as yet.\n\nAs for Rebecca, Captain Dobbin had not been two hours in the ladies' company before she understood his secret perfectly. She did not like him, and feared him privately; nor was he very much prepossessed in her favour. He was so honest, that her arts and cajoleries did not affect him, and he shrank from her with instinctive repulsion. And, as she was by no means so far superior to her sex as to be above jealousy, she disliked him the more for his adoration of Amelia. Nevertheless, she was very respectful and cordial in her manner towards him. A friend to the Osbornes! a friend to her dearest benefactors! She vowed she should always love him sincerely: she remembered him quite well on the Vauxhall night, as she told Amelia archly, and she made a little fun of him when the two ladies went to dress for dinner. Rawdon Crawley paid scarcely any attention to Dobbin, looking upon him as a good-natured nincompoop and under-bred City man. Jos patronised him with much dignity.\n\nWhen George and Dobbin were alone in the latter's room, to which George had followed him, Dobbin took from his desk the letter which he had been charged by Mr. Osborne to deliver to his son. \"It's not in my father's handwriting,\" said George, looking rather alarmed; nor was it: the letter was from Mr. Osborne's lawyer, and to the following effect:\n\n\"Bedford Row, May 7, 1815.\n\n\"I am commissioned by Mr. Osborne to inform you, that he abides by the determination which he before expressed to you, and that in consequence of the marriage which you have been pleased to contract, he ceases to consider you henceforth as a member of his family. This determination is final and irrevocable.\n\n\"Although the monies expended upon you in your minority, and the bills which you have drawn upon him so unsparingly of late years, far exceed in amount the sum to which you are entitled in your own right (being the third part of the fortune of your mother, the late Mrs. Osborne and which reverted to you at her decease, and to Miss Jane Osborne and Miss Maria Frances Osborne); yet I am instructed by Mr. Osborne to say, that he waives all claim upon your estate, and that the sum of 2,000 pounds, 4 per cent. annuities, at the value of the day (being your one-third share of the sum of 6,000 pounds), shall be paid over to yourself or your agents upon your receipt for the same, by\n\n\"Your obedient Servt., \"S. HIGGS.\n\n\"P.S.--Mr. Osborne desires me to say, once for all, that he declines to receive any messages, letters, or communications from you on this or any other subject.\n\n\"A pretty way you have managed the affair,\" said George, looking savagely at William Dobbin. \"Look there, Dobbin,\" and he flung over to the latter his parent's letter. \"A beggar, by Jove, and all in consequence of my d--d sentimentality. Why couldn't we have waited? A ball might have done for me in the course of the war, and may still, and how will Emmy be bettered by being left a beggar's widow? It was all your doing. You were never easy until you had got me married and ruined. What the deuce am I to do with two thousand pounds? Such a sum won't last two years. I've lost a hundred and forty to Crawley at cards and billiards since I've been down here. A pretty manager of a man's matters YOU are, forsooth.\"\n\n\"There's no denying that the position is a hard one,\" Dobbin replied, after reading over the letter with a blank countenance; \"and as you say, it is partly of my making. There are some men who wouldn't mind changing with you,\" he added, with a bitter smile. \"How many captains in the regiment have two thousand pounds to the fore, think you? You must live on your pay till your father relents, and if you die, you leave your wife a hundred a year.\"\n\n\"Do you suppose a man of my habits can live on his pay and a hundred a year?\" George cried out in great anger. \"You must be a fool to talk so, Dobbin. How the deuce am I to keep up my position in the world upon such a pitiful pittance? I can't change my habits. I must have my comforts. I wasn't brought up on porridge, like MacWhirter, or on potatoes, like old O'Dowd. Do you expect my wife to take in soldiers' washing, or ride after the regiment in a baggage waggon?\"\n\n\"Well, well,\" said Dobbin, still good-naturedly, \"we'll get her a better conveyance. But try and remember that you are only a dethroned prince now, George, my boy; and be quiet whilst the tempest lasts. It won't be for long. Let your name be mentioned in the Gazette, and I'll engage the old father relents towards you:\"\n\n\"Mentioned in the Gazette!\" George answered. \"And in what part of it? Among the killed and wounded returns, and at the top of the list, very likely.\"\n\n\"Psha! It will be time enough to cry out when we are hurt,\" Dobbin said. \"And if anything happens, you know, George, I have got a little, and I am not a marrying man, and I shall not forget my godson in my will,\" he added, with a smile. Whereupon the dispute ended--as many scores of such conversations between Osborne and his friend had concluded previously--by the former declaring there was no possibility of being angry with Dobbin long, and forgiving him very generously after abusing him without cause.\n\n\"I say, Becky,\" cried Rawdon Crawley out of his dressing-room, to his lady, who was attiring herself for dinner in her own chamber.\n\n\"What?\" said Becky's shrill voice. She was looking over her shoulder in the glass. She had put on the neatest and freshest white frock imaginable, and with bare shoulders and a little necklace, and a light blue sash, she looked the image of youthful innocence and girlish happiness.\n\n\"I say, what'll Mrs. O. do, when O. goes out with the regiment?\" Crawley said coming into the room, performing a duet on his head with two huge hair-brushes, and looking out from under his hair with admiration on his pretty little wife.\n\n\"I suppose she'll cry her eyes out,\" Becky answered. \"She has been whimpering half a dozen times, at the very notion of it, already to me.\"\n\n\"YOU don't care, I suppose?\" Rawdon said, half angry at his wife's want of feeling.\n\n\"You wretch! don't you know that I intend to go with you,\" Becky replied. \"Besides, you're different. You go as General Tufto's aide-de-camp. We don't belong to the line,\" Mrs. Crawley said, throwing up her head with an air that so enchanted her husband that he stooped down and kissed it.\n\n\"Rawdon dear--don't you think--you'd better get that--money from Cupid, before he goes?\" Becky continued, fixing on a killing bow. She called George Osborne, Cupid. She had flattered him about his good looks a score of times already. She watched over him kindly at ecarte of a night when he would drop in to Rawdon's quarters for a half-hour before bed-time.\n\nShe had often called him a horrid dissipated wretch, and threatened to tell Emmy of his wicked ways and naughty extravagant habits. She brought his cigar and lighted it for him; she knew the effect of that manoeuvre, having practised it in former days upon Rawdon Crawley. He thought her gay, brisk, arch, distinguee, delightful. In their little drives and dinners, Becky, of course, quite outshone poor Emmy, who remained very mute and timid while Mrs. Crawley and her husband rattled away together, and Captain Crawley (and Jos after he joined the young married people) gobbled in silence.\n\nEmmy's mind somehow misgave her about her friend. Rebecca's wit, spirits, and accomplishments troubled her with a rueful disquiet. They were only a week married, and here was George already suffering ennui, and eager for others' society! She trembled for the future. How shall I be a companion for him, she thought--so clever and so brilliant, and I such a humble foolish creature? How noble it was of him to marry me--to give up everything and stoop down to me! I ought to have refused him, only I had not the heart. I ought to have stopped at home and taken care of poor Papa. And her neglect of her parents (and indeed there was some foundation for this charge which the poor child's uneasy conscience brought against her) was now remembered for the first time, and caused her to blush with humiliation. Oh! thought she, I have been very wicked and selfish--selfish in forgetting them in their sorrows--selfish in forcing George to marry me. I know I'm not worthy of him--I know he would have been happy without me--and yet--I tried, I tried to give him up.\n\nIt is hard when, before seven days of marriage are over, such thoughts and confessions as these force themselves on a little bride's mind. But so it was, and the night before Dobbin came to join these young people--on a fine brilliant moonlight night of May--so warm and balmy that the windows were flung open to the balcony, from which George and Mrs. Crawley were gazing upon the calm ocean spread shining before them, while Rawdon and Jos were engaged at backgammon within--Amelia couched in a great chair quite neglected, and watching both these parties, felt a despair and remorse such as were bitter companions for that tender lonely soul. Scarce a week was past, and it was come to this! The future, had she regarded it, offered a dismal prospect; but Emmy was too shy, so to speak, to look to that, and embark alone on that wide sea, and unfit to navigate it without a guide and protector. I know Miss Smith has a mean opinion of her. But how many, my dear Madam, are endowed with your prodigious strength of mind?\n\n\"Gad, what a fine night, and how bright the moon is!\" George said, with a puff of his cigar, which went soaring up skywards.\n\n\"How delicious they smell in the open air! I adore them. Who'd think the moon was two hundred and thirty-six thousand eight hundred and forty-seven miles off?\" Becky added, gazing at that orb with a smile. \"Isn't it clever of me to remember that? Pooh! we learned it all at Miss Pinkerton's! How calm the sea is, and how clear everything. I declare I can almost see the coast of France!\" and her bright green eyes streamed out, and shot into the night as if they could see through it.\n\n\"Do you know what I intend to do one morning?\" she said; \"I find I can swim beautifully, and some day, when my Aunt Crawley's companion--old Briggs, you know--you remember her--that hook-nosed woman, with the long wisps of hair--when Briggs goes out to bathe, I intend to dive under her awning, and insist on a reconciliation in the water. Isn't that a stratagem?\"\n\nGeorge burst out laughing at the idea of this aquatic meeting. \"What's the row there, you two?\" Rawdon shouted out, rattling the box. Amelia was making a fool of herself in an absurd hysterical manner, and retired to her own room to whimper in private.\n\nOur history is destined in this chapter to go backwards and forwards in a very irresolute manner seemingly, and having conducted our story to to-morrow presently, we shall immediately again have occasion to step back to yesterday, so that the whole of the tale may get a hearing. As you behold at her Majesty's drawing-room, the ambassadors' and high dignitaries' carriages whisk off from a private door, while Captain Jones's ladies are waiting for their fly: as you see in the Secretary of the Treasury's antechamber, a half-dozen of petitioners waiting patiently for their audience, and called out one by one, when suddenly an Irish member or some eminent personage enters the apartment, and instantly walks into Mr. Under-Secretary over the heads of all the people present: so in the conduct of a tale, the romancer is obliged to exercise this most partial sort of justice. Although all the little incidents must be heard, yet they must be put off when the great events make their appearance; and surely such a circumstance as that which brought Dobbin to Brighton, viz., the ordering out of the Guards and the line to Belgium, and the mustering of the allied armies in that country under the command of his Grace the Duke of Wellington--such a dignified circumstance as that, I say, was entitled to the pas over all minor occurrences whereof this history is composed mainly, and hence a little trifling disarrangement and disorder was excusable and becoming. We have only now advanced in time so far beyond Chapter XXII as to have got our various characters up into their dressing-rooms before the dinner, which took place as usual on the day of Dobbin's arrival.\n\nGeorge was too humane or too much occupied with the tie of his neckcloth to convey at once all the news to Amelia which his comrade had brought with him from London. He came into her room, however, holding the attorney's letter in his hand, and with so solemn and important an air that his wife, always ingeniously on the watch for calamity, thought the worst was about to befall, and running up to her husband, besought her dearest George to tell her everything--he was ordered abroad; there would be a battle next week--she knew there would.\n\nDearest George parried the question about foreign service, and with a melancholy shake of the head said, \"No, Emmy; it isn't that: it's not myself I care about: it's you. I have had bad news from my father. He refuses any communication with me; he has flung us off; and leaves us to poverty. I can rough it well enough; but you, my dear, how will you bear it? read here.\" And he handed her over the letter.\n\nAmelia, with a look of tender alarm in her eyes, listened to her noble hero as he uttered the above generous sentiments, and sitting down on the bed, read the letter which George gave her with such a pompous martyr-like air. Her face cleared up as she read the document, however. The idea of sharing poverty and privation in company with the beloved object is, as we have before said, far from being disagreeable to a warm-hearted woman. The notion was actually pleasant to little Amelia. Then, as usual, she was ashamed of herself for feeling happy at such an indecorous moment, and checked her pleasure, saying demurely, \"O, George, how your poor heart must bleed at the idea of being separated from your papa!\"\n\n\"It does,\" said George, with an agonised countenance.\n\n\"But he can't be angry with you long,\" she continued. \"Nobody could, I'm sure. He must forgive you, my dearest, kindest husband. O, I shall never forgive myself if he does not.\"\n\n\"What vexes me, my poor Emmy, is not my misfortune, but yours,\" George said. \"I don't care for a little poverty; and I think, without vanity, I've talents enough to make my own way.\"\n\n\"That you have,\" interposed his wife, who thought that war should cease, and her husband should be made a general instantly.\n\n\"Yes, I shall make my way as well as another,\" Osborne went on; \"but you, my dear girl, how can I bear your being deprived of the comforts and station in society which my wife had a right to expect? My dearest girl in barracks; the wife of a soldier in a marching regiment; subject to all sorts of annoyance and privation! It makes me miserable.\"\n\nEmmy, quite at ease, as this was her husband's only cause of disquiet, took his hand, and with a radiant face and smile began to warble that stanza from the favourite song of \"Wapping Old Stairs,\" in which the heroine, after rebuking her Tom for inattention, promises \"his trousers to mend, and his grog too to make,\" if he will be constant and kind, and not forsake her. \"Besides,\" she said, after a pause, during which she looked as pretty and happy as any young woman need, \"isn't two thousand pounds an immense deal of money, George?\"\n\nGeorge laughed at her naivete; and finally they went down to dinner, Amelia clinging to George's arm, still warbling the tune of \"Wapping Old Stairs,\" and more pleased and light of mind than she had been for some days past.\n\nThus the repast, which at length came off, instead of being dismal, was an exceedingly brisk and merry one. The excitement of the campaign counteracted in George's mind the depression occasioned by the disinheriting letter. Dobbin still kept up his character of rattle. He amused the company with accounts of the army in Belgium; where nothing but fetes and gaiety and fashion were going on. Then, having a particular end in view, this dexterous captain proceeded to describe Mrs. Major O'Dowd packing her own and her Major's wardrobe, and how his best epaulets had been stowed into a tea canister, whilst her own famous yellow turban, with the bird of paradise wrapped in brown paper, was locked up in the Major's tin cocked-hat case, and wondered what effect it would have at the French king's court at Ghent, or the great military balls at Brussels.\n\n\"Ghent! Brussels!\" cried out Amelia with a sudden shock and start. \"Is the regiment ordered away, George--is it ordered away?\" A look of terror came over the sweet smiling face, and she clung to George as by an instinct.\n\n\"Don't be afraid, dear,\" he said good-naturedly; \"it is but a twelve hours' passage. It won't hurt you. You shall go, too, Emmy.\"\n\n\"I intend to go,\" said Becky. \"I'm on the staff. General Tufto is a great flirt of mine. Isn't he, Rawdon?\" Rawdon laughed out with his usual roar. William Dobbin flushed up quite red. \"She can't go,\" he said; \"think of the--of the danger,\" he was going to add; but had not all his conversation during dinner-time tended to prove there was none? He became very confused and silent.\n\n\"I must and will go,\" Amelia cried with the greatest spirit; and George, applauding her resolution, patted her under the chin, and asked all the persons present if they ever saw such a termagant of a wife, and agreed that the lady should bear him company. \"We'll have Mrs. O'Dowd to chaperon you,\" he said. What cared she so long as her husband was near her? Thus somehow the bitterness of a parting was juggled away. Though war and danger were in store, war and danger might not befall for months to come. There was a respite at any rate, which made the timid little Amelia almost as happy as a full reprieve would have done, and which even Dobbin owned in his heart was very welcome. For, to be permitted to see her was now the greatest privilege and hope of his life, and he thought with himself secretly how he would watch and protect her. I wouldn't have let her go if I had been married to her, he thought. But George was the master, and his friend did not think fit to remonstrate.\n\nPutting her arm round her friend's waist, Rebecca at length carried Amelia off from the dinner-table where so much business of importance had been discussed, and left the gentlemen in a highly exhilarated state, drinking and talking very gaily.\n\nIn the course of the evening Rawdon got a little family-note from his wife, which, although he crumpled it up and burnt it instantly in the candle, we had the good luck to read over Rebecca's shoulder. \"Great news,\" she wrote. \"Mrs. Bute is gone. Get the money from Cupid tonight, as he'll be off to-morrow most likely. Mind this.--R.\" So when the little company was about adjourning to coffee in the women's apartment, Rawdon touched Osborne on the elbow, and said gracefully, \"I say, Osborne, my boy, if quite convenient, I'll trouble you for that 'ere small trifle.\" It was not quite convenient, but nevertheless George gave him a considerable present instalment in bank-notes from his pocket-book, and a bill on his agents at a week's date, for the remaining sum.\n\nThis matter arranged, George, and Jos, and Dobbin, held a council of war over their cigars, and agreed that a general move should be made for London in Jos's open carriage the next day. Jos, I think, would have preferred staying until Rawdon Crawley quitted Brighton, but Dobbin and George overruled him, and he agreed to carry the party to town, and ordered four horses, as became his dignity. With these they set off in state, after breakfast, the next day. Amelia had risen very early in the morning, and packed her little trunks with the greatest alacrity, while Osborne lay in bed deploring that she had not a maid to help her. She was only too glad, however, to perform this office for herself. A dim uneasy sentiment about Rebecca filled her mind already; and although they kissed each other most tenderly at parting, yet we know what jealousy is; and Mrs. Amelia possessed that among other virtues of her sex.\n\nBesides these characters who are coming and going away, we must remember that there were some other old friends of ours at Brighton; Miss Crawley, namely, and the suite in attendance upon her. Now, although Rebecca and her husband were but at a few stones' throw of the lodgings which the invalid Miss Crawley occupied, the old lady's door remained as pitilessly closed to them as it had been heretofore in London. As long as she remained by the side of her sister-in-law, Mrs. Bute Crawley took care that her beloved Matilda should not be agitated by a meeting with her nephew. When the spinster took her drive, the faithful Mrs. Bute sate beside her in the carriage. When Miss Crawley took the air in a chair, Mrs. Bute marched on one side of the vehicle, whilst honest Briggs occupied the other wing. And if they met Rawdon and his wife by chance--although the former constantly and obsequiously took off his hat, the Miss-Crawley party passed him by with such a frigid and killing indifference, that Rawdon began to despair.\n\n\"We might as well be in London as here,\" Captain Rawdon often said, with a downcast air.\n\n\"A comfortable inn in Brighton is better than a spunging-house in Chancery Lane,\" his wife answered, who was of a more cheerful temperament. \"Think of those two aides-de-camp of Mr. Moses, the sheriff's-officer, who watched our lodging for a week. Our friends here are very stupid, but Mr. Jos and Captain Cupid are better companions than Mr. Moses's men, Rawdon, my love.\"\n\n\"I wonder the writs haven't followed me down here,\" Rawdon continued, still desponding.\n\n\"When they do, we'll find means to give them the slip,\" said dauntless little Becky, and further pointed out to her husband the great comfort and advantage of meeting Jos and Osborne, whose acquaintance had brought to Rawdon Crawley a most timely little supply of ready money.\n\n\"It will hardly be enough to pay the inn bill,\" grumbled the Guardsman.\n\n\"Why need we pay it?\" said the lady, who had an answer for everything.\n\nThrough Rawdon's valet, who still kept up a trifling acquaintance with the male inhabitants of Miss Crawley's servants' hall, and was instructed to treat the coachman to drink whenever they met, old Miss Crawley's movements were pretty well known by our young couple; and Rebecca luckily bethought herself of being unwell, and of calling in the same apothecary who was in attendance upon the spinster, so that their information was on the whole tolerably complete. Nor was Miss Briggs, although forced to adopt a hostile attitude, secretly inimical to Rawdon and his wife. She was naturally of a kindly and forgiving disposition. Now that the cause of jealousy was removed, her dislike for Rebecca disappeared also, and she remembered the latter's invariable good words and good humour. And, indeed, she and Mrs. Firkin, the lady's-maid, and the whole of Miss Crawley's household, groaned under the tyranny of the triumphant Mrs. Bute.\n\nAs often will be the case, that good but imperious woman pushed her advantages too far, and her successes quite unmercifully. She had in the course of a few weeks brought the invalid to such a state of helpless docility, that the poor soul yielded herself entirely to her sister's orders, and did not even dare to complain of her slavery to Briggs or Firkin. Mrs. Bute measured out the glasses of wine which Miss Crawley was daily allowed to take, with irresistible accuracy, greatly to the annoyance of Firkin and the butler, who found themselves deprived of control over even the sherry-bottle. She apportioned the sweetbreads, jellies, chickens; their quantity and order. Night and noon and morning she brought the abominable drinks ordained by the Doctor, and made her patient swallow them with so affecting an obedience that Firkin said \"my poor Missus du take her physic like a lamb.\" She prescribed the drive in the carriage or the ride in the chair, and, in a word, ground down the old lady in her convalescence in such a way as only belongs to your proper-managing, motherly moral woman. If ever the patient faintly resisted, and pleaded for a little bit more dinner or a little drop less medicine, the nurse threatened her with instantaneous death, when Miss Crawley instantly gave in. \"She's no spirit left in her,\" Firkin remarked to Briggs; \"she ain't ave called me a fool these three weeks.\" Finally, Mrs. Bute had made up her mind to dismiss the aforesaid honest lady's-maid, Mr. Bowls the large confidential man, and Briggs herself, and to send for her daughters from the Rectory, previous to removing the dear invalid bodily to Queen's Crawley, when an odious accident happened which called her away from duties so pleasing. The Reverend Bute Crawley, her husband, riding home one night, fell with his horse and broke his collar-bone. Fever and inflammatory symptoms set in, and Mrs. Bute was forced to leave Sussex for Hampshire. As soon as ever Bute was restored, she promised to return to her dearest friend, and departed, leaving the strongest injunctions with the household regarding their behaviour to their mistress; and as soon as she got into the Southampton coach, there was such a jubilee and sense of relief in all Miss Crawley's house, as the company of persons assembled there had not experienced for many a week before. That very day Miss Crawley left off her afternoon dose of medicine: that afternoon Bowls opened an independent bottle of sherry for himself and Mrs. Firkin: that night Miss Crawley and Miss Briggs indulged in a game of piquet instead of one of Porteus's sermons. It was as in the old nursery-story, when the stick forgot to beat the dog, and the whole course of events underwent a peaceful and happy revolution.\n\nAt a very early hour in the morning, twice or thrice a week, Miss Briggs used to betake herself to a bathing-machine, and disport in the water in a flannel gown and an oilskin cap. Rebecca, as we have seen, was aware of this circumstance, and though she did not attempt to storm Briggs as she had threatened, and actually dive into that lady's presence and surprise her under the sacredness of the awning, Mrs. Rawdon determined to attack Briggs as she came away from her bath, refreshed and invigorated by her dip, and likely to be in good humour.\n\nSo getting up very early the next morning, Becky brought the telescope in their sitting-room, which faced the sea, to bear upon the bathing-machines on the beach; saw Briggs arrive, enter her box; and put out to sea; and was on the shore just as the nymph of whom she came in quest stepped out of the little caravan on to the shingles. It was a pretty picture: the beach; the bathing-women's faces; the long line of rocks and building were blushing and bright in the sunshine. Rebecca wore a kind, tender smile on her face, and was holding out her pretty white hand as Briggs emerged from the box. What could Briggs do but accept the salutation?\n\n\"Miss Sh--Mrs. Crawley,\" she said.\n\nMrs. Crawley seized her hand, pressed it to her heart, and with a sudden impulse, flinging her arms round Briggs, kissed her affectionately. \"Dear, dear friend!\" she said, with a touch of such natural feeling, that Miss Briggs of course at once began to melt, and even the bathing-woman was mollified.\n\nRebecca found no difficulty in engaging Briggs in a long, intimate, and delightful conversation. Everything that had passed since the morning of Becky's sudden departure from Miss Crawley's house in Park Lane up to the present day, and Mrs. Bute's happy retreat, was discussed and described by Briggs. All Miss Crawley's symptoms, and the particulars of her illness and medical treatment, were narrated by the confidante with that fulness and accuracy which women delight in. About their complaints and their doctors do ladies ever tire of talking to each other? Briggs did not on this occasion; nor did Rebecca weary of listening. She was thankful, truly thankful, that the dear kind Briggs, that the faithful, the invaluable Firkin, had been permitted to remain with their benefactress through her illness. Heaven bless her! though she, Rebecca, had seemed to act undutifully towards Miss Crawley; yet was not her fault a natural and excusable one? Could she help giving her hand to the man who had won her heart? Briggs, the sentimental, could only turn up her eyes to heaven at this appeal, and heave a sympathetic sigh, and think that she, too, had given away her affections long years ago, and own that Rebecca was no very great criminal.\n\n\"Can I ever forget her who so befriended the friendless orphan? No, though she has cast me off,\" the latter said, \"I shall never cease to love her, and I would devote my life to her service. As my own benefactress, as my beloved Rawdon's adored relative, I love and admire Miss Crawley, dear Miss Briggs, beyond any woman in the world, and next to her I love all those who are faithful to her. I would never have treated Miss Crawley's faithful friends as that odious designing Mrs. Bute has done. Rawdon, who was all heart,\" Rebecca continued, \"although his outward manners might seem rough and careless, had said a hundred times, with tears in his eyes, that he blessed Heaven for sending his dearest Aunty two such admirable nurses as her attached Firkin and her admirable Miss Briggs. Should the machinations of the horrible Mrs. Bute end, as she too much feared they would, in banishing everybody that Miss Crawley loved from her side, and leaving that poor lady a victim to those harpies at the Rectory, Rebecca besought her (Miss Briggs) to remember that her own home, humble as it was, was always open to receive Briggs. Dear friend,\" she exclaimed, in a transport of enthusiasm, \"some hearts can never forget benefits; all women are not Bute Crawleys! Though why should I complain of her,\" Rebecca added; \"though I have been her tool and the victim to her arts, do I not owe my dearest Rawdon to her?\" And Rebecca unfolded to Briggs all Mrs. Bute's conduct at Queen's Crawley, which, though unintelligible to her then, was clearly enough explained by the events now--now that the attachment had sprung up which Mrs. Bute had encouraged by a thousand artifices--now that two innocent people had fallen into the snares which she had laid for them, and loved and married and been ruined through her schemes.\n\nIt was all very true. Briggs saw the stratagems as clearly as possible. Mrs. Bute had made the match between Rawdon and Rebecca. Yet, though the latter was a perfectly innocent victim, Miss Briggs could not disguise from her friend her fear that Miss Crawley's affections were hopelessly estranged from Rebecca, and that the old lady would never forgive her nephew for making so imprudent a marriage.\n\nOn this point Rebecca had her own opinion, and still kept up a good heart. If Miss Crawley did not forgive them at present, she might at least relent on a future day. Even now, there was only that puling, sickly Pitt Crawley between Rawdon and a baronetcy; and should anything happen to the former, all would be well. At all events, to have Mrs. Bute's designs exposed, and herself well abused, was a satisfaction, and might be advantageous to Rawdon's interest; and Rebecca, after an hour's chat with her recovered friend, left her with the most tender demonstrations of regard, and quite assured that the conversation they had had together would be reported to Miss Crawley before many hours were over.\n\nThis interview ended, it became full time for Rebecca to return to her inn, where all the party of the previous day were assembled at a farewell breakfast. Rebecca took such a tender leave of Amelia as became two women who loved each other as sisters; and having used her handkerchief plentifully, and hung on her friend's neck as if they were parting for ever, and waved the handkerchief (which was quite dry, by the way) out of window, as the carriage drove off, she came back to the breakfast table, and ate some prawns with a good deal of appetite, considering her emotion; and while she was munching these delicacies, explained to Rawdon what had occurred in her morning walk between herself and Briggs. Her hopes were very high: she made her husband share them. She generally succeeded in making her husband share all her opinions, whether melancholy or cheerful.\n\n\"You will now, if you please, my dear, sit down at the writing-table and pen me a pretty little letter to Miss Crawley, in which you'll say that you are a good boy, and that sort of thing.\" So Rawdon sate down, and wrote off, \"Brighton, Thursday,\" and \"My dear Aunt,\" with great rapidity: but there the gallant officer's imagination failed him. He mumbled the end of his pen, and looked up in his wife's face. She could not help laughing at his rueful countenance, and marching up and down the room with her hands behind her, the little woman began to dictate a letter, which he took down.\n\n\"Before quitting the country and commencing a campaign, which very possibly may be fatal.\"\n\n\"What?\" said Rawdon, rather surprised, but took the humour of the phrase, and presently wrote it down with a grin.\n\n\"Which very possibly may be fatal, I have come hither--\"\n\n\"Why not say come here, Becky? Come here's grammar,\" the dragoon interposed.\n\n\"I have come hither,\" Rebecca insisted, with a stamp of her foot, \"to say farewell to my dearest and earliest friend. I beseech you before I go, not perhaps to return, once more to let me press the hand from which I have received nothing but kindnesses all my life.\"\n\n\"Kindnesses all my life,\" echoed Rawdon, scratching down the words, and quite amazed at his own facility of composition.\n\n\"I ask nothing from you but that we should part not in anger. I have the pride of my family on some points, though not on all. I married a painter's daughter, and am not ashamed of the union.\"\n\n\"No, run me through the body if I am!\" Rawdon ejaculated.\n\n\"You old booby,\" Rebecca said, pinching his ear and looking over to see that he made no mistakes in spelling--\"beseech is not spelt with an a, and earliest is.\" So he altered these words, bowing to the superior knowledge of his little Missis.\n\n\"I thought that you were aware of the progress of my attachment,\" Rebecca continued: \"I knew that Mrs. Bute Crawley confirmed and encouraged it. But I make no reproaches. I married a poor woman, and am content to abide by what I have done. Leave your property, dear Aunt, as you will. I shall never complain of the way in which you dispose of it. I would have you believe that I love you for yourself, and not for money's sake. I want to be reconciled to you ere I leave England. Let me, let me see you before I go. A few weeks or months hence it may be too late, and I cannot bear the notion of quitting the country without a kind word of farewell from you.\"\n\n\"She won't recognise my style in that,\" said Becky. \"I made the sentences short and brisk on purpose.\" And this authentic missive was despatched under cover to Miss Briggs.\n\nOld Miss Crawley laughed when Briggs, with great mystery, handed her over this candid and simple statement. \"We may read it now Mrs. Bute is away,\" she said. \"Read it to me, Briggs.\"\n\nWhen Briggs had read the epistle out, her patroness laughed more. \"Don't you see, you goose,\" she said to Briggs, who professed to be much touched by the honest affection which pervaded the composition, \"don't you see that Rawdon never wrote a word of it. He never wrote to me without asking for money in his life, and all his letters are full of bad spelling, and dashes, and bad grammar. It is that little serpent of a governess who rules him.\" They are all alike, Miss Crawley thought in her heart. They all want me dead, and are hankering for my money.\n\n\"I don't mind seeing Rawdon,\" she added, after a pause, and in a tone of perfect indifference. \"I had just as soon shake hands with him as not. Provided there is no scene, why shouldn't we meet? I don't mind. But human patience has its limits; and mind, my dear, I respectfully decline to receive Mrs. Rawdon--I can't support that quite\"--and Miss Briggs was fain to be content with this half-message of conciliation; and thought that the best method of bringing the old lady and her nephew together, was to warn Rawdon to be in waiting on the Cliff, when Miss Crawley went out for her air in her chair. There they met. I don't know whether Miss Crawley had any private feeling of regard or emotion upon seeing her old favourite; but she held out a couple of fingers to him with as smiling and good-humoured an air, as if they had met only the day before. And as for Rawdon, he turned as red as scarlet, and wrung off Briggs's hand, so great was his rapture and his confusion at the meeting. Perhaps it was interest that moved him: or perhaps affection: perhaps he was touched by the change which the illness of the last weeks had wrought in his aunt.\n\n\"The old girl has always acted like a trump to me,\" he said to his wife, as he narrated the interview, \"and I felt, you know, rather queer, and that sort of thing. I walked by the side of the what-dy'e-call-'em, you know, and to her own door, where Bowls came to help her in. And I wanted to go in very much, only--\"\n\n\"YOU DIDN'T GO IN, Rawdon!\" screamed his wife.\n\n\"No, my dear; I'm hanged if I wasn't afraid when it came to the point.\"\n\n\"You fool! you ought to have gone in, and never come out again,\" Rebecca said.\n\n\"Don't call me names,\" said the big Guardsman, sulkily. \"Perhaps I WAS a fool, Becky, but you shouldn't say so\"; and he gave his wife a look, such as his countenance could wear when angered, and such as was not pleasant to face.\n\n\"Well, dearest, to-morrow you must be on the look-out, and go and see her, mind, whether she asks you or no,\" Rebecca said, trying to soothe her angry yoke-mate. On which he replied, that he would do exactly as he liked, and would just thank her to keep a civil tongue in her head--and the wounded husband went away, and passed the forenoon at the billiard-room, sulky, silent, and suspicious.\n\nBut before the night was over he was compelled to give in, and own, as usual, to his wife's superior prudence and foresight, by the most melancholy confirmation of the presentiments which she had regarding the consequences of the mistake which he had made. Miss Crawley must have had some emotion upon seeing him and shaking hands with him after so long a rupture. She mused upon the meeting a considerable time. \"Rawdon is getting very fat and old, Briggs,\" she said to her companion. \"His nose has become red, and he is exceedingly coarse in appearance. His marriage to that woman has hopelessly vulgarised him. Mrs. Bute always said they drank together; and I have no doubt they do. Yes: he smelt of gin abominably. I remarked it. Didn't you?\"\n\nIn vain Briggs interposed that Mrs. Bute spoke ill of everybody: and, as far as a person in her humble position could judge, was an--\n\n\"An artful designing woman? Yes, so she is, and she does speak ill of every one--but I am certain that woman has made Rawdon drink. All those low people do--\"\n\n\"He was very much affected at seeing you, ma'am,\" the companion said; \"and I am sure, when you remember that he is going to the field of danger--\"\n\n\"How much money has he promised you, Briggs?\" the old spinster cried out, working herself into a nervous rage--\"there now, of course you begin to cry. I hate scenes. Why am I always to be worried? Go and cry up in your own room, and send Firkin to me--no, stop, sit down and blow your nose, and leave off crying, and write a letter to Captain Crawley.\" Poor Briggs went and placed herself obediently at the writing-book. Its leaves were blotted all over with relics of the firm, strong, rapid handwriting of the spinster's late amanuensis, Mrs. Bute Crawley.\n\n\"Begin 'My dear sir,' or 'Dear sir,' that will be better, and say you are desired by Miss Crawley--no, by Miss Crawley's medical man, by Mr. Creamer, to state that my health is such that all strong emotions would be dangerous in my present delicate condition--and that I must decline any family discussions or interviews whatever. And thank him for coming to Brighton, and so forth, and beg him not to stay any longer on my account. And, Miss Briggs, you may add that I wish him a bon voyage, and that if he will take the trouble to call upon my lawyer's in Gray's Inn Square, he will find there a communication for him. Yes, that will do; and that will make him leave Brighton.\" The benevolent Briggs penned this sentence with the utmost satisfaction.\n\n\"To seize upon me the very day after Mrs. Bute was gone,\" the old lady prattled on; \"it was too indecent. Briggs, my dear, write to Mrs. Crawley, and say SHE needn't come back. No--she needn't--and she shan't--and I won't be a slave in my own house--and I won't be starved and choked with poison. They all want to kill me--all--all\"--and with this the lonely old woman burst into a scream of hysterical tears.\n\nThe last scene of her dismal Vanity Fair comedy was fast approaching; the tawdry lamps were going out one by one; and the dark curtain was almost ready to descend.\n\nThat final paragraph, which referred Rawdon to Miss Crawley's solicitor in London, and which Briggs had written so good-naturedly, consoled the dragoon and his wife somewhat, after their first blank disappointment, on reading the spinster's refusal of a reconciliation. And it effected the purpose for which the old lady had caused it to be written, by making Rawdon very eager to get to London.\n\nOut of Jos's losings and George Osborne's bank-notes, he paid his bill at the inn, the landlord whereof does not probably know to this day how doubtfully his account once stood. For, as a general sends his baggage to the rear before an action, Rebecca had wisely packed up all their chief valuables and sent them off under care of George's servant, who went in charge of the trunks on the coach back to London. Rawdon and his wife returned by the same conveyance next day.\n\n\"I should have liked to see the old girl before we went,\" Rawdon said. \"She looks so cut up and altered that I'm sure she can't last long. I wonder what sort of a cheque I shall have at Waxy's. Two hundred--it can't be less than two hundred--hey, Becky?\"\n\nIn consequence of the repeated visits of the aides-de-camp of the Sheriff of Middlesex, Rawdon and his wife did not go back to their lodgings at Brompton, but put up at an inn. Early the next morning, Rebecca had an opportunity of seeing them as she skirted that suburb on her road to old Mrs. Sedley's house at Fulham, whither she went to look for her dear Amelia and her Brighton friends. They were all off to Chatham, thence to Harwich, to take shipping for Belgium with the regiment--kind old Mrs. Sedley very much depressed and tearful, solitary. Returning from this visit, Rebecca found her husband, who had been off to Gray's Inn, and learnt his fate. He came back furious.\n\n\"By Jove, Becky,\" says he, \"she's only given me twenty pound!\"\n\nThough it told against themselves, the joke was too good, and Becky burst out laughing at Rawdon's discomfiture.\n\nBetween London and Chatham\nOn quitting Brighton, our friend George, as became a person of rank and fashion travelling in a barouche with four horses, drove in state to a fine hotel in Cavendish Square, where a suite of splendid rooms, and a table magnificently furnished with plate and surrounded by a half-dozen of black and silent waiters, was ready to receive the young gentleman and his bride. George did the honours of the place with a princely air to Jos and Dobbin; and Amelia, for the first time, and with exceeding shyness and timidity, presided at what George called her own table.\n\nGeorge pooh-poohed the wine and bullied the waiters royally, and Jos gobbled the turtle with immense satisfaction. Dobbin helped him to it; for the lady of the house, before whom the tureen was placed, was so ignorant of the contents, that she was going to help Mr. Sedley without bestowing upon him either calipash or calipee.\n\nThe splendour of the entertainment, and the apartments in which it was given, alarmed Mr. Dobbin, who remonstrated after dinner, when Jos was asleep in the great chair. But in vain he cried out against the enormity of turtle and champagne that was fit for an archbishop. \"I've always been accustomed to travel like a gentleman,\" George said, \"and, damme, my wife shall travel like a lady. As long as there's a shot in the locker, she shall want for nothing,\" said the generous fellow, quite pleased with himself for his magnificence of spirit. Nor did Dobbin try and convince him that Amelia's happiness was not centred in turtle-soup.\n\nA while after dinner, Amelia timidly expressed a wish to go and see her mamma, at Fulham: which permission George granted her with some grumbling. And she tripped away to her enormous bedroom, in the centre of which stood the enormous funereal bed, \"that the Emperor Halixander's sister slep in when the allied sufferings was here,\" and put on her little bonnet and shawl with the utmost eagerness and pleasure. George was still drinking claret when she returned to the dining-room, and made no signs of moving. \"Ar'n't you coming with me, dearest?\" she asked him. No; the \"dearest\" had \"business\" that night. His man should get her a coach and go with her. And the coach being at the door of the hotel, Amelia made George a little disappointed curtsey after looking vainly into his face once or twice, and went sadly down the great staircase, Captain Dobbin after, who handed her into the vehicle, and saw it drive away to its destination. The very valet was ashamed of mentioning the address to the hackney-coachman before the hotel waiters, and promised to instruct him when they got further on.\n\nDobbin walked home to his old quarters and the Slaughters', thinking very likely that it would be delightful to be in that hackney-coach, along with Mrs. Osborne. George was evidently of quite a different taste; for when he had taken wine enough, he went off to half-price at the play, to see Mr. Kean perform in Shylock. Captain Osborne was a great lover of the drama, and had himself performed high-comedy characters with great distinction in several garrison theatrical entertainments. Jos slept on until long after dark, when he woke up with a start at the motions of his servant, who was removing and emptying the decanters on the table; and the hackney-coach stand was again put into requisition for a carriage to convey this stout hero to his lodgings and bed.\n\nMrs. Sedley, you may be sure, clasped her daughter to her heart with all maternal eagerness and affection, running out of the door as the carriage drew up before the little garden-gate, to welcome the weeping, trembling, young bride. Old Mr. Clapp, who was in his shirt-sleeves, trimming the garden-plot, shrank back alarmed. The Irish servant-lass rushed up from the kitchen and smiled a \"God bless you.\" Amelia could hardly walk along the flags and up the steps into the parlour.\n\nHow the floodgates were opened, and mother and daughter wept, when they were together embracing each other in this sanctuary, may readily be imagined by every reader who possesses the least sentimental turn. When don't ladies weep? At what occasion of joy, sorrow, or other business of life, and, after such an event as a marriage, mother and daughter were surely at liberty to give way to a sensibility which is as tender as it is refreshing. About a question of marriage I have seen women who hate each other kiss and cry together quite fondly. How much more do they feel when they love! Good mothers are married over again at their daughters' weddings: and as for subsequent events, who does not know how ultra-maternal grandmothers are?--in fact a woman, until she is a grandmother, does not often really know what to be a mother is. Let us respect Amelia and her mamma whispering and whimpering and laughing and crying in the parlour and the twilight. Old Mr. Sedley did. HE had not divined who was in the carriage when it drove up. He had not flown out to meet his daughter, though he kissed her very warmly when she entered the room (where he was occupied, as usual, with his papers and tapes and statements of accounts), and after sitting with the mother and daughter for a short time, he very wisely left the little apartment in their possession.\n\nGeorge's valet was looking on in a very supercilious manner at Mr. Clapp in his shirt-sleeves, watering his rose-bushes. He took off his hat, however, with much condescension to Mr. Sedley, who asked news about his son-in-law, and about Jos's carriage, and whether his horses had been down to Brighton, and about that infernal traitor Bonaparty, and the war; until the Irish maid-servant came with a plate and a bottle of wine, from which the old gentleman insisted upon helping the valet. He gave him a half-guinea too, which the servant pocketed with a mixture of wonder and contempt. \"To the health of your master and mistress, Trotter,\" Mr. Sedley said, \"and here's something to drink your health when you get home, Trotter.\"\n\nThere were but nine days past since Amelia had left that little cottage and home--and yet how far off the time seemed since she had bidden it farewell. What a gulf lay between her and that past life. She could look back to it from her present standing-place, and contemplate, almost as another being, the young unmarried girl absorbed in her love, having no eyes but for one special object, receiving parental affection if not ungratefully, at least indifferently, and as if it were her due--her whole heart and thoughts bent on the accomplishment of one desire. The review of those days, so lately gone yet so far away, touched her with shame; and the aspect of the kind parents filled her with tender remorse. Was the prize gained--the heaven of life--and the winner still doubtful and unsatisfied? As his hero and heroine pass the matrimonial barrier, the novelist generally drops the curtain, as if the drama were over then: the doubts and struggles of life ended: as if, once landed in the marriage country, all were green and pleasant there: and wife and husband had nothing to do but to link each other's arms together, and wander gently downwards towards old age in happy and perfect fruition. But our little Amelia was just on the bank of her new country, and was already looking anxiously back towards the sad friendly figures waving farewell to her across the stream, from the other distant shore.\n\nIn honour of the young bride's arrival, her mother thought it necessary to prepare I don't know what festive entertainment, and after the first ebullition of talk, took leave of Mrs. George Osborne for a while, and dived down to the lower regions of the house to a sort of kitchen-parlour (occupied by Mr. and Mrs. Clapp, and in the evening, when her dishes were washed and her curl-papers removed, by Miss Flannigan, the Irish servant), there to take measures for the preparing of a magnificent ornamented tea. All people have their ways of expressing kindness, and it seemed to Mrs. Sedley that a muffin and a quantity of orange marmalade spread out in a little cut-glass saucer would be peculiarly agreeable refreshments to Amelia in her most interesting situation.\n\nWhile these delicacies were being transacted below, Amelia, leaving the drawing-room, walked upstairs and found herself, she scarce knew how, in the little room which she had occupied before her marriage, and in that very chair in which she had passed so many bitter hours. She sank back in its arms as if it were an old friend; and fell to thinking over the past week, and the life beyond it. Already to be looking sadly and vaguely back: always to be pining for something which, when obtained, brought doubt and sadness rather than pleasure; here was the lot of our poor little creature and harmless lost wanderer in the great struggling crowds of Vanity Fair.\n\nHere she sate, and recalled to herself fondly that image of George to which she had knelt before marriage. Did she own to herself how different the real man was from that superb young hero whom she had worshipped? It requires many, many years--and a man must be very bad indeed--before a woman's pride and vanity will let her own to such a confession. Then Rebecca's twinkling green eyes and baleful smile lighted upon her, and filled her with dismay. And so she sate for awhile indulging in her usual mood of selfish brooding, in that very listless melancholy attitude in which the honest maid-servant had found her, on the day when she brought up the letter in which George renewed his offer of marriage.\n\nShe looked at the little white bed, which had been hers a few days before, and thought she would like to sleep in it that night, and wake, as formerly, with her mother smiling over her in the morning: Then she thought with terror of the great funereal damask pavilion in the vast and dingy state bedroom, which was awaiting her at the grand hotel in Cavendish Square. Dear little white bed! how many a long night had she wept on its pillow! How she had despaired and hoped to die there; and now were not all her wishes accomplished, and the lover of whom she had despaired her own for ever? Kind mother! how patiently and tenderly she had watched round that bed! She went and knelt down by the bedside; and there this wounded and timorous, but gentle and loving soul, sought for consolation, where as yet, it must be owned, our little girl had but seldom looked for it. Love had been her faith hitherto; and the sad, bleeding disappointed heart began to feel the want of another consoler.\n\nHave we a right to repeat or to overhear her prayers? These, brother, are secrets, and out of the domain of Vanity Fair, in which our story lies.\n\nBut this may be said, that when the tea was finally announced, our young lady came downstairs a great deal more cheerful; that she did not despond, or deplore her fate, or think about George's coldness, or Rebecca's eyes, as she had been wont to do of late. She went downstairs, and kissed her father and mother, and talked to the old gentleman, and made him more merry than he had been for many a day. She sate down at the piano which Dobbin had bought for her, and sang over all her father's favourite old songs. She pronounced the tea to be excellent, and praised the exquisite taste in which the marmalade was arranged in the saucers. And in determining to make everybody else happy, she found herself so; and was sound asleep in the great funereal pavilion, and only woke up with a smile when George arrived from the theatre.\n\nFor the next day, George had more important \"business\" to transact than that which took him to see Mr. Kean in Shylock. Immediately on his arrival in London he had written off to his father's solicitors, signifying his royal pleasure that an interview should take place between them on the morrow. His hotel bill, losses at billiards and cards to Captain Crawley had almost drained the young man's purse, which wanted replenishing before he set out on his travels, and he had no resource but to infringe upon the two thousand pounds which the attorneys were commissioned to pay over to him. He had a perfect belief in his own mind that his father would relent before very long. How could any parent be obdurate for a length of time against such a paragon as he was? If his mere past and personal merits did not succeed in mollifying his father, George determined that he would distinguish himself so prodigiously in the ensuing campaign that the old gentleman must give in to him. And if not? Bah! the world was before him. His luck might change at cards, and there was a deal of spending in two thousand pounds.\n\nSo he sent off Amelia once more in a carriage to her mamma, with strict orders and carte blanche to the two ladies to purchase everything requisite for a lady of Mrs. George Osborne's fashion, who was going on a foreign tour. They had but one day to complete the outfit, and it may be imagined that their business therefore occupied them pretty fully. In a carriage once more, bustling about from milliner to linen-draper, escorted back to the carriage by obsequious shopmen or polite owners, Mrs. Sedley was herself again almost, and sincerely happy for the first time since their misfortunes. Nor was Mrs. Amelia at all above the pleasure of shopping, and bargaining, and seeing and buying pretty things. (Would any man, the most philosophic, give twopence for a woman who was?) She gave herself a little treat, obedient to her husband's orders, and purchased a quantity of lady's gear, showing a great deal of taste and elegant discernment, as all the shopfolks said.\n\nAnd about the war that was ensuing, Mrs. Osborne was not much alarmed; Bonaparty was to be crushed almost without a struggle. Margate packets were sailing every day, filled with men of fashion and ladies of note, on their way to Brussels and Ghent. People were going not so much to a war as to a fashionable tour. The newspapers laughed the wretched upstart and swindler to scorn. Such a Corsican wretch as that withstand the armies of Europe and the genius of the immortal Wellington! Amelia held him in utter contempt; for it needs not to be said that this soft and gentle creature took her opinions from those people who surrounded her, such fidelity being much too humble-minded to think for itself. Well, in a word, she and her mother performed a great day's shopping, and she acquitted herself with considerable liveliness and credit on this her first appearance in the genteel world of London.\n\nGeorge meanwhile, with his hat on one side, his elbows squared, and his swaggering martial air, made for Bedford Row, and stalked into the attorney's offices as if he was lord of every pale-faced clerk who was scribbling there. He ordered somebody to inform Mr. Higgs that Captain Osborne was waiting, in a fierce and patronizing way, as if the pekin of an attorney, who had thrice his brains, fifty times his money, and a thousand times his experience, was a wretched underling who should instantly leave all his business in life to attend on the Captain's pleasure. He did not see the sneer of contempt which passed all round the room, from the first clerk to the articled gents, from the articled gents to the ragged writers and white-faced runners, in clothes too tight for them, as he sate there tapping his boot with his cane, and thinking what a parcel of miserable poor devils these were. The miserable poor devils knew all about his affairs. They talked about them over their pints of beer at their public-house clubs to other clerks of a night. Ye gods, what do not attorneys and attorneys' clerks know in London! Nothing is hidden from their inquisition, and their families mutely rule our city.\n\nPerhaps George expected, when he entered Mr. Higgs's apartment, to find that gentleman commissioned to give him some message of compromise or conciliation from his father; perhaps his haughty and cold demeanour was adopted as a sign of his spirit and resolution: but if so, his fierceness was met by a chilling coolness and indifference on the attorney's part, that rendered swaggering absurd. He pretended to be writing at a paper, when the Captain entered. \"Pray, sit down, sir,\" said he, \"and I will attend to your little affair in a moment. Mr. Poe, get the release papers, if you please\"; and then he fell to writing again.\n\nPoe having produced those papers, his chief calculated the amount of two thousand pounds stock at the rate of the day; and asked Captain Osborne whether he would take the sum in a cheque upon the bankers, or whether he should direct the latter to purchase stock to that amount. \"One of the late Mrs. Osborne's trustees is out of town,\" he said indifferently, \"but my client wishes to meet your wishes, and have done with the business as quick as possible.\"\n\n\"Give me a cheque, sir,\" said the Captain very surlily. \"Damn the shillings and halfpence, sir,\" he added, as the lawyer was making out the amount of the draft; and, flattering himself that by this stroke of magnanimity he had put the old quiz to the blush, he stalked out of the office with the paper in his pocket.\n\n\"That chap will be in gaol in two years,\" Mr. Higgs said to Mr. Poe.\n\n\"Won't O. come round, sir, don't you think?\"\n\n\"Won't the monument come round,\" Mr. Higgs replied.\n\n\"He's going it pretty fast,\" said the clerk. \"He's only married a week, and I saw him and some other military chaps handing Mrs. Highflyer to her carriage after the play.\" And then another case was called, and Mr. George Osborne thenceforth dismissed from these worthy gentlemen's memory.\n\nThe draft was upon our friends Hulker and Bullock of Lombard Street, to whose house, still thinking he was doing business, George bent his way, and from whom he received his money. Frederick Bullock, Esq., whose yellow face was over a ledger, at which sate a demure clerk, happened to be in the banking-room when George entered. His yellow face turned to a more deadly colour when he saw the Captain, and he slunk back guiltily into the inmost parlour. George was too busy gloating over the money (for he had never had such a sum before), to mark the countenance or flight of the cadaverous suitor of his sister.\n\nFred Bullock told old Osborne of his son's appearance and conduct. \"He came in as bold as brass,\" said Frederick. \"He has drawn out every shilling. How long will a few hundred pounds last such a chap as that?\" Osborne swore with a great oath that he little cared when or how soon he spent it. Fred dined every day in Russell Square now. But altogether, George was highly pleased with his day's business. All his own baggage and outfit was put into a state of speedy preparation, and he paid Amelia's purchases with cheques on his agents, and with the splendour of a lord.\n\nIn Which Amelia Joins Her Regiment\nWhen Jos's fine carriage drove up to the inn door at Chatham, the first face which Amelia recognized was the friendly countenance of Captain Dobbin, who had been pacing the street for an hour past in expectation of his friends' arrival. The Captain, with shells on his frockcoat, and a crimson sash and sabre, presented a military appearance, which made Jos quite proud to be able to claim such an acquaintance, and the stout civilian hailed him with a cordiality very different from the reception which Jos vouchsafed to his friend in Brighton and Bond Street.\n\nAlong with the Captain was Ensign Stubble; who, as the barouche neared the inn, burst out with an exclamation of \"By Jove! what a pretty girl\"; highly applauding Osborne's choice. Indeed, Amelia dressed in her wedding-pelisse and pink ribbons, with a flush in her face, occasioned by rapid travel through the open air, looked so fresh and pretty, as fully to justify the Ensign's compliment. Dobbin liked him for making it. As he stepped forward to help the lady out of the carriage, Stubble saw what a pretty little hand she gave him, and what a sweet pretty little foot came tripping down the step. He blushed profusely, and made the very best bow of which he was capable; to which Amelia, seeing the number of the the regiment embroidered on the Ensign's cap, replied with a blushing smile, and a curtsey on her part; which finished the young Ensign on the spot. Dobbin took most kindly to Mr. Stubble from that day, and encouraged him to talk about Amelia in their private walks, and at each other's quarters. It became the fashion, indeed, among all the honest young fellows of the --th to adore and admire Mrs. Osborne. Her simple artless behaviour, and modest kindness of demeanour, won all their unsophisticated hearts; all which simplicity and sweetness are quite impossible to describe in print. But who has not beheld these among women, and recognised the presence of all sorts of qualities in them, even though they say no more to you than that they are engaged to dance the next quadrille, or that it is very hot weather? George, always the champion of his regiment, rose immensely in the opinion of the youth of the corps, by his gallantry in marrying this portionless young creature, and by his choice of such a pretty kind partner.\n\nIn the sitting-room which was awaiting the travellers, Amelia, to her surprise, found a letter addressed to Mrs. Captain Osborne. It was a triangular billet, on pink paper, and sealed with a dove and an olive branch, and a profusion of light blue sealing wax, and it was written in a very large, though undecided female hand.\n\n\"It's Peggy O'Dowd's fist,\" said George, laughing. \"I know it by the kisses on the seal.\" And in fact, it was a note from Mrs. Major O'Dowd, requesting the pleasure of Mrs. Osborne's company that very evening to a small friendly party. \"You must go,\" George said. \"You will make acquaintance with the regiment there. O'Dowd goes in command of the regiment, and Peggy goes in command.\"\n\nBut they had not been for many minutes in the enjoyment of Mrs. O'Dowd's letter, when the door was flung open, and a stout jolly lady, in a riding-habit, followed by a couple of officers of Ours, entered the room.\n\n\"Sure, I couldn't stop till tay-time. Present me, Garge, my dear fellow, to your lady. Madam, I'm deloighted to see ye; and to present to you me husband, Meejor O'Dowd\"; and with this, the jolly lady in the riding-habit grasped Amelia's hand very warmly, and the latter knew at once that the lady was before her whom her husband had so often laughed at. \"You've often heard of me from that husband of yours,\" said the lady, with great vivacity.\n\n\"You've often heard of her,\" echoed her husband, the Major.\n\nAmelia answered, smiling, \"that she had.\"\n\n\"And small good he's told you of me,\" Mrs. O'Dowd replied; adding that \"George was a wicked divvle.\"\n\n\"That I'll go bail for,\" said the Major, trying to look knowing, at which George laughed; and Mrs. O'Dowd, with a tap of her whip, told the Major to be quiet; and then requested to be presented in form to Mrs. Captain Osborne.\n\n\"This, my dear,\" said George with great gravity, \"is my very good, kind, and excellent friend, Auralia Margaretta, otherwise called Peggy.\"\n\n\"Faith, you're right,\" interposed the Major.\n\n\"Otherwise called Peggy, lady of Major Michael O'Dowd, of our regiment, and daughter of Fitzjurld Ber'sford de Burgo Malony of Glenmalony, County Kildare.\"\n\n\"And Muryan Squeer, Doblin,\" said the lady with calm superiority.\n\n\"And Muryan Square, sure enough,\" the Major whispered.\n\n\"'Twas there ye coorted me, Meejor dear,\" the lady said; and the Major assented to this as to every other proposition which was made generally in company.\n\nMajor O'Dowd, who had served his sovereign in every quarter of the world, and had paid for every step in his profession by some more than equivalent act of daring and gallantry, was the most modest, silent, sheep-faced and meek of little men, and as obedient to his wife as if he had been her tay-boy. At the mess-table he sat silently, and drank a great deal. When full of liquor, he reeled silently home. When he spoke, it was to agree with everybody on every conceivable point; and he passed through life in perfect ease and good-humour. The hottest suns of India never heated his temper; and the Walcheren ague never shook it. He walked up to a battery with just as much indifference as to a dinner-table; had dined on horse-flesh and turtle with equal relish and appetite; and had an old mother, Mrs. O'Dowd of O'Dowdstown indeed, whom he had never disobeyed but when he ran away and enlisted, and when he persisted in marrying that odious Peggy Malony.\n\nPeggy was one of five sisters, and eleven children of the noble house of Glenmalony; but her husband, though her own cousin, was of the mother's side, and so had not the inestimable advantage of being allied to the Malonys, whom she believed to be the most famous family in the world. Having tried nine seasons at Dublin and two at Bath and Cheltenham, and not finding a partner for life, Miss Malony ordered her cousin Mick to marry her when she was about thirty-three years of age; and the honest fellow obeying, carried her off to the West Indies, to preside over the ladies of the --th regiment, into which he had just exchanged.\n\nBefore Mrs. O'Dowd was half an hour in Amelia's (or indeed in anybody else's) company, this amiable lady told all her birth and pedigree to her new friend. \"My dear,\" said she, good-naturedly, \"it was my intention that Garge should be a brother of my own, and my sister Glorvina would have suited him entirely. But as bygones are bygones, and he was engaged to yourself, why, I'm determined to take you as a sister instead, and to look upon you as such, and to love you as one of the family. Faith, you've got such a nice good-natured face and way widg you, that I'm sure we'll agree; and that you'll be an addition to our family anyway.\"\n\n\"'Deed and she will,\" said O'Dowd, with an approving air, and Amelia felt herself not a little amused and grateful to be thus suddenly introduced to so large a party of relations.\n\n\"We're all good fellows here,\" the Major's lady continued. \"There's not a regiment in the service where you'll find a more united society nor a more agreeable mess-room. There's no quarrelling, bickering, slandthering, nor small talk amongst us. We all love each other.\"\n\n\"Especially Mrs. Magenis,\" said George, laughing.\n\n\"Mrs. Captain Magenis and me has made up, though her treatment of me would bring me gray hairs with sorrow to the grave.\"\n\n\"And you with such a beautiful front of black, Peggy, my dear,\" the Major cried.\n\n\"Hould your tongue, Mick, you booby. Them husbands are always in the way, Mrs. Osborne, my dear; and as for my Mick, I often tell him he should never open his mouth but to give the word of command, or to put meat and drink into it. I'll tell you about the regiment, and warn you when we're alone. Introduce me to your brother now; sure he's a mighty fine man, and reminds me of me cousin, Dan Malony (Malony of Ballymalony, my dear, you know who mar'ied Ophalia Scully, of Oystherstown, own cousin to Lord Poldoody). Mr. Sedley, sir, I'm deloighted to be made known te ye. I suppose you'll dine at the mess to-day. (Mind that divvle of a docther, Mick, and whatever ye du, keep yourself sober for me party this evening.)\"\n\n\"It's the 150th gives us a farewell dinner, my love,\" interposed the Major, \"but we'll easy get a card for Mr. Sedley.\"\n\n\"Run Simple (Ensign Simple, of Ours, my dear Amelia. I forgot to introjuice him to ye). Run in a hurry, with Mrs. Major O'Dowd's compliments to Colonel Tavish, and Captain Osborne has brought his brothernlaw down, and will bring him to the 150th mess at five o'clock sharp--when you and I, my dear, will take a snack here, if you like.\" Before Mrs. O'Dowd's speech was concluded, the young Ensign was trotting downstairs on his commission.\n\n\"Obedience is the soul of the army. We will go to our duty while Mrs. O'Dowd will stay and enlighten you, Emmy,\" Captain Osborne said; and the two gentlemen, taking each a wing of the Major, walked out with that officer, grinning at each other over his head.\n\nAnd, now having her new friend to herself, the impetuous Mrs. O'Dowd proceeded to pour out such a quantity of information as no poor little woman's memory could ever tax itself to bear. She told Amelia a thousand particulars relative to the very numerous family of which the amazed young lady found herself a member. \"Mrs. Heavytop, the Colonel's wife, died in Jamaica of the yellow faver and a broken heart comboined, for the horrud old Colonel, with a head as bald as a cannon-ball, was making sheep's eyes at a half-caste girl there. Mrs. Magenis, though without education, was a good woman, but she had the divvle's tongue, and would cheat her own mother at whist. Mrs. Captain Kirk must turn up her lobster eyes forsooth at the idea of an honest round game (wherein me fawther, as pious a man as ever went to church, me uncle Dane Malony, and our cousin the Bishop, took a hand at loo, or whist, every night of their lives). Nayther of 'em's goin' with the regiment this time,\" Mrs. O'Dowd added. \"Fanny Magenis stops with her mother, who sells small coal and potatoes, most likely, in Islington-town, hard by London, though she's always bragging of her father's ships, and pointing them out to us as they go up the river: and Mrs. Kirk and her children will stop here in Bethesda Place, to be nigh to her favourite preacher, Dr. Ramshorn. Mrs. Bunny's in an interesting situation--faith, and she always is, then--and has given the Lieutenant seven already. And Ensign Posky's wife, who joined two months before you, my dear, has quarl'd with Tom Posky a score of times, till you can hear'm all over the bar'ck (they say they're come to broken pleets, and Tom never accounted for his black oi), and she'll go back to her mother, who keeps a ladies' siminary at Richmond--bad luck to her for running away from it! Where did ye get your finishing, my dear? I had moin, and no expince spared, at Madame Flanahan's, at Ilyssus Grove, Booterstown, near Dublin, wid a Marchioness to teach us the true Parisian pronunciation, and a retired Mejor-General of the French service to put us through the exercise.\"\n\nOf this incongruous family our astonished Amelia found herself all of a sudden a member: with Mrs. O'Dowd as an elder sister. She was presented to her other female relations at tea-time, on whom, as she was quiet, good-natured, and not too handsome, she made rather an agreeable impression until the arrival of the gentlemen from the mess of the 150th, who all admired her so, that her sisters began, of course, to find fault with her.\n\n\"I hope Osborne has sown his wild oats,\" said Mrs. Magenis to Mrs. Bunny. \"If a reformed rake makes a good husband, sure it's she will have the fine chance with Garge,\" Mrs. O'Dowd remarked to Posky, who had lost her position as bride in the regiment, and was quite angry with the usurper. And as for Mrs. Kirk: that disciple of Dr. Ramshorn put one or two leading professional questions to Amelia, to see whether she was awakened, whether she was a professing Christian and so forth, and finding from the simplicity of Mrs. Osborne's replies that she was yet in utter darkness, put into her hands three little penny books with pictures, viz., the \"Howling Wilderness,\" the \"Washerwoman of Wandsworth Common,\" and the \"British Soldier's best Bayonet,\" which, bent upon awakening her before she slept, Mrs. Kirk begged Amelia to read that night ere she went to bed.\n\nBut all the men, like good fellows as they were, rallied round their comrade's pretty wife, and paid her their court with soldierly gallantry. She had a little triumph, which flushed her spirits and made her eyes sparkle. George was proud of her popularity, and pleased with the manner (which was very gay and graceful, though naive and a little timid) with which she received the gentlemen's attentions, and answered their compliments. And he in his uniform--how much handsomer he was than any man in the room! She felt that he was affectionately watching her, and glowed with pleasure at his kindness. \"I will make all his friends welcome,\" she resolved in her heart. \"I will love all as I love him. I will always try and be gay and good-humoured and make his home happy.\"\n\nThe regiment indeed adopted her with acclamation. The Captains approved, the Lieutenants applauded, the Ensigns admired. Old Cutler, the Doctor, made one or two jokes, which, being professional, need not be repeated; and Cackle, the Assistant M.D. of Edinburgh, condescended to examine her upon leeterature, and tried her with his three best French quotations. Young Stubble went about from man to man whispering, \"Jove, isn't she a pretty gal?\" and never took his eyes off her except when the negus came in.\n\nAs for Captain Dobbin, he never so much as spoke to her during the whole evening. But he and Captain Porter of the 150th took home Jos to the hotel, who was in a very maudlin state, and had told his tiger-hunt story with great effect, both at the mess-table and at the soiree, to Mrs. O'Dowd in her turban and bird of paradise. Having put the Collector into the hands of his servant, Dobbin loitered about, smoking his cigar before the inn door. George had meanwhile very carefully shawled his wife, and brought her away from Mrs. O'Dowd's after a general handshaking from the young officers, who accompanied her to the fly, and cheered that vehicle as it drove off. So Amelia gave Dobbin her little hand as she got out of the carriage, and rebuked him smilingly for not having taken any notice of her all night.\n\nThe Captain continued that deleterious amusement of smoking, long after the inn and the street were gone to bed. He watched the lights vanish from George's sitting-room windows, and shine out in the bedroom close at hand. It was almost morning when he returned to his own quarters. He could hear the cheering from the ships in the river, where the transports were already taking in their cargoes preparatory to dropping down the Thames.\n\nIn Which Amelia Invades the Low Countries\nThe regiment with its officers was to be transported in ships provided by His Majesty's government for the occasion: and in two days after the festive assembly at Mrs. O'Dowd's apartments, in the midst of cheering from all the East India ships in the river, and the military on shore, the band playing \"God Save the King,\" the officers waving their hats, and the crews hurrahing gallantly, the transports went down the river and proceeded under convoy to Ostend. Meanwhile the gallant Jos had agreed to escort his sister and the Major's wife, the bulk of whose goods and chattels, including the famous bird of paradise and turban, were with the regimental baggage: so that our two heroines drove pretty much unencumbered to Ramsgate, where there were plenty of packets plying, in one of which they had a speedy passage to Ostend.\n\nThat period of Jos's life which now ensued was so full of incident, that it served him for conversation for many years after, and even the tiger-hunt story was put aside for more stirring narratives which he had to tell about the great campaign of Waterloo. As soon as he had agreed to escort his sister abroad, it was remarked that he ceased shaving his upper lip. At Chatham he followed the parades and drills with great assiduity. He listened with the utmost attention to the conversation of his brother officers (as he called them in after days sometimes), and learned as many military names as he could. In these studies the excellent Mrs. O'Dowd was of great assistance to him; and on the day finally when they embarked on board the Lovely Rose, which was to carry them to their destination, he made his appearance in a braided frock-coat and duck trousers, with a foraging cap ornamented with a smart gold band. Having his carriage with him, and informing everybody on board confidentially that he was going to join the Duke of Wellington's army, folks mistook him for a great personage, a commissary-general, or a government courier at the very least.\n\nHe suffered hugely on the voyage, during which the ladies were likewise prostrate; but Amelia was brought to life again as the packet made Ostend, by the sight of the transports conveying her regiment, which entered the harbour almost at the same time with the Lovely Rose. Jos went in a collapsed state to an inn, while Captain Dobbin escorted the ladies, and then busied himself in freeing Jos's carriage and luggage from the ship and the custom-house, for Mr. Jos was at present without a servant, Osborne's man and his own pampered menial having conspired together at Chatham, and refused point-blank to cross the water. This revolt, which came very suddenly, and on the last day, so alarmed Mr. Sedley, junior, that he was on the point of giving up the expedition, but Captain Dobbin (who made himself immensely officious in the business, Jos said), rated him and laughed at him soundly: the mustachios were grown in advance, and Jos finally was persuaded to embark. In place of the well-bred and well-fed London domestics, who could only speak English, Dobbin procured for Jos's party a swarthy little Belgian servant who could speak no language at all; but who, by his bustling behaviour, and by invariably addressing Mr. Sedley as \"My lord,\" speedily acquired that gentleman's favour. Times are altered at Ostend now; of the Britons who go thither, very few look like lords, or act like those members of our hereditary aristocracy. They seem for the most part shabby in attire, dingy of linen, lovers of billiards and brandy, and cigars and greasy ordinaries.\n\nBut it may be said as a rule, that every Englishman in the Duke of Wellington's army paid his way. The remembrance of such a fact surely becomes a nation of shopkeepers. It was a blessing for a commerce-loving country to be overrun by such an army of customers: and to have such creditable warriors to feed. And the country which they came to protect is not military. For a long period of history they have let other people fight there. When the present writer went to survey with eagle glance the field of Waterloo, we asked the conductor of the diligence, a portly warlike-looking veteran, whether he had been at the battle. \"Pas si bete\"--such an answer and sentiment as no Frenchman would own to--was his reply. But, on the other hand, the postilion who drove us was a Viscount, a son of some bankrupt Imperial General, who accepted a pennyworth of beer on the road. The moral is surely a good one.\n\nThis flat, flourishing, easy country never could have looked more rich and prosperous than in that opening summer of 1815, when its green fields and quiet cities were enlivened by multiplied red-coats: when its wide chaussees swarmed with brilliant English equipages: when its great canal-boats, gliding by rich pastures and pleasant quaint old villages, by old chateaux lying amongst old trees, were all crowded with well-to-do English travellers: when the soldier who drank at the village inn, not only drank, but paid his score; and Donald, the Highlander, billeted in the Flemish farm-house, rocked the baby's cradle, while Jean and Jeannette were out getting in the hay. As our painters are bent on military subjects just now, I throw out this as a good subject for the pencil, to illustrate the principle of an honest English war. All looked as brilliant and harmless as a Hyde Park review. Meanwhile, Napoleon screened behind his curtain of frontier-fortresses, was preparing for the outbreak which was to drive all these orderly people into fury and blood; and lay so many of them low.\n\nEverybody had such a perfect feeling of confidence in the leader (for the resolute faith which the Duke of Wellington had inspired in the whole English nation was as intense as that more frantic enthusiasm with which at one time the French regarded Napoleon), the country seemed in so perfect a state of orderly defence, and the help at hand in case of need so near and overwhelming, that alarm was unknown, and our travellers, among whom two were naturally of a very timid sort, were, like all the other multiplied English tourists, entirely at ease. The famous regiment, with so many of whose officers we have made acquaintance, was drafted in canal boats to Bruges and Ghent, thence to march to Brussels. Jos accompanied the ladies in the public boats; the which all old travellers in Flanders must remember for the luxury and accommodation they afforded. So prodigiously good was the eating and drinking on board these sluggish but most comfortable vessels, that there are legends extant of an English traveller, who, coming to Belgium for a week, and travelling in one of these boats, was so delighted with the fare there that he went backwards and forwards from Ghent to Bruges perpetually until the railroads were invented, when he drowned himself on the last trip of the passage-boat. Jos's death was not to be of this sort, but his comfort was exceeding, and Mrs. O'Dowd insisted that he only wanted her sister Glorvina to make his happiness complete. He sate on the roof of the cabin all day drinking Flemish beer, shouting for Isidor, his servant, and talking gallantly to the ladies.\n\nHis courage was prodigious. \"Boney attack us!\" he cried. \"My dear creature, my poor Emmy, don't be frightened. There's no danger. The allies will be in Paris in two months, I tell you; when I'll take you to dine in the Palais Royal, by Jove! There are three hundred thousand Rooshians, I tell you, now entering France by Mayence and the Rhine--three hundred thousand under Wittgenstein and Barclay de Tolly, my poor love. You don't know military affairs, my dear. I do, and I tell you there's no infantry in France can stand against Rooshian infantry, and no general of Boney's that's fit to hold a candle to Wittgenstein. Then there are the Austrians, they are five hundred thousand if a man, and they are within ten marches of the frontier by this time, under Schwartzenberg and Prince Charles. Then there are the Prooshians under the gallant Prince Marshal. Show me a cavalry chief like him now that Murat is gone. Hey, Mrs. O'Dowd? Do you think our little girl here need be afraid? Is there any cause for fear, Isidor? Hey, sir? Get some more beer.\"\n\nMrs. O'Dowd said that her \"Glorvina was not afraid of any man alive, let alone a Frenchman,\" and tossed off a glass of beer with a wink which expressed her liking for the beverage.\n\nHaving frequently been in presence of the enemy, or, in other words, faced the ladies at Cheltenham and Bath, our friend, the Collector, had lost a great deal of his pristine timidity, and was now, especially when fortified with liquor, as talkative as might be. He was rather a favourite with the regiment, treating the young officers with sumptuosity, and amusing them by his military airs. And as there is one well-known regiment of the army which travels with a goat heading the column, whilst another is led by a deer, George said with respect to his brother-in-law, that his regiment marched with an elephant.\n\nSince Amelia's introduction to the regiment, George began to be rather ashamed of some of the company to which he had been forced to present her; and determined, as he told Dobbin (with what satisfaction to the latter it need not be said), to exchange into some better regiment soon, and to get his wife away from those damned vulgar women. But this vulgarity of being ashamed of one's society is much more common among men than women (except very great ladies of fashion, who, to be sure, indulge in it); and Mrs. Amelia, a natural and unaffected person, had none of that artificial shamefacedness which her husband mistook for delicacy on his own part. Thus Mrs. O'Dowd had a cock's plume in her hat, and a very large \"repayther\" on her stomach, which she used to ring on all occasions, narrating how it had been presented to her by her fawther, as she stipt into the car'ge after her mar'ge; and these ornaments, with other outward peculiarities of the Major's wife, gave excruciating agonies to Captain Osborne, when his wife and the Major's came in contact; whereas Amelia was only amused by the honest lady's eccentricities, and not in the least ashamed of her company.\n\nAs they made that well-known journey, which almost every Englishman of middle rank has travelled since, there might have been more instructive, but few more entertaining, companions than Mrs. Major O'Dowd. \"Talk about kenal boats; my dear! Ye should see the kenal boats between Dublin and Ballinasloe. It's there the rapid travelling is; and the beautiful cattle. Sure me fawther got a goold medal (and his Excellency himself eat a slice of it, and said never was finer mate in his loif) for a four-year-old heifer, the like of which ye never saw in this country any day.\" And Jos owned with a sigh, \"that for good streaky beef, really mingled with fat and lean, there was no country like England.\"\n\n\"Except Ireland, where all your best mate comes from,\" said the Major's lady; proceeding, as is not unusual with patriots of her nation, to make comparisons greatly in favour of her own country. The idea of comparing the market at Bruges with those of Dublin, although she had suggested it herself, caused immense scorn and derision on her part. \"I'll thank ye tell me what they mean by that old gazabo on the top of the market-place,\" said she, in a burst of ridicule fit to have brought the old tower down. The place was full of English soldiery as they passed. English bugles woke them in the morning; at nightfall they went to bed to the note of the British fife and drum: all the country and Europe was in arms, and the greatest event of history pending: and honest Peggy O'Dowd, whom it concerned as well as another, went on prattling about Ballinafad, and the horses in the stables at Glenmalony, and the clar't drunk there; and Jos Sedley interposed about curry and rice at Dumdum; and Amelia thought about her husband, and how best she should show her love for him; as if these were the great topics of the world.\n\nThose who like to lay down the History-book, and to speculate upon what MIGHT have happened in the world, but for the fatal occurrence of what actually did take place (a most puzzling, amusing, ingenious, and profitable kind of meditation), have no doubt often thought to themselves what a specially bad time Napoleon took to come back from Elba, and to let loose his eagle from Gulf San Juan to Notre Dame. The historians on our side tell us that the armies of the allied powers were all providentially on a war-footing, and ready to bear down at a moment's notice upon the Elban Emperor. The august jobbers assembled at Vienna, and carving out the kingdoms of Europe according to their wisdom, had such causes of quarrel among themselves as might have set the armies which had overcome Napoleon to fight against each other, but for the return of the object of unanimous hatred and fear. This monarch had an army in full force because he had jobbed to himself Poland, and was determined to keep it: another had robbed half Saxony, and was bent upon maintaining his acquisition: Italy was the object of a third's solicitude. Each was protesting against the rapacity of the other; and could the Corsican but have waited in prison until all these parties were by the ears, he might have returned and reigned unmolested. But what would have become of our story and all our friends, then? If all the drops in it were dried up, what would become of the sea?\n\nIn the meanwhile the business of life and living, and the pursuits of pleasure, especially, went on as if no end were to be expected to them, and no enemy in front. When our travellers arrived at Brussels, in which their regiment was quartered, a great piece of good fortune, as all said, they found themselves in one of the gayest and most brilliant little capitals in Europe, and where all the Vanity Fair booths were laid out with the most tempting liveliness and splendour. Gambling was here in profusion, and dancing in plenty: feasting was there to fill with delight that great gourmand of a Jos: there was a theatre where a miraculous Catalani was delighting all hearers: beautiful rides, all enlivened with martial splendour; a rare old city, with strange costumes and wonderful architecture, to delight the eyes of little Amelia, who had never before seen a foreign country, and fill her with charming surprises: so that now and for a few weeks' space in a fine handsome lodging, whereof the expenses were borne by Jos and Osborne, who was flush of money and full of kind attentions to his wife--for about a fortnight, I say, during which her honeymoon ended, Mrs. Amelia was as pleased and happy as any little bride out of England.\n\nEvery day during this happy time there was novelty and amusement for all parties. There was a church to see, or a picture-gallery--there was a ride, or an opera. The bands of the regiments were making music at all hours. The greatest folks of England walked in the Park--there was a perpetual military festival. George, taking out his wife to a new jaunt or junket every night, was quite pleased with himself as usual, and swore he was becoming quite a domestic character. And a jaunt or a junket with HIM! Was it not enough to set this little heart beating with joy? Her letters home to her mother were filled with delight and gratitude at this season. Her husband bade her buy laces, millinery, jewels, and gimcracks of all sorts. Oh, he was the kindest, best, and most generous of men!\n\nThe sight of the very great company of lords and ladies and fashionable persons who thronged the town, and appeared in every public place, filled George's truly British soul with intense delight. They flung off that happy frigidity and insolence of demeanour which occasionally characterises the great at home, and appearing in numberless public places, condescended to mingle with the rest of the company whom they met there. One night at a party given by the general of the division to which George's regiment belonged, he had the honour of dancing with Lady Blanche Thistlewood, Lord Bareacres' daughter; he bustled for ices and refreshments for the two noble ladies; he pushed and squeezed for Lady Bareacres' carriage; he bragged about the Countess when he got home, in a way which his own father could not have surpassed. He called upon the ladies the next day; he rode by their side in the Park; he asked their party to a great dinner at a restaurateur's, and was quite wild with exultation when they agreed to come. Old Bareacres, who had not much pride and a large appetite, would go for a dinner anywhere.\n\n\"I hope there will be no women besides our own party,\" Lady Bareacres said, after reflecting upon the invitation which had been made, and accepted with too much precipitancy.\n\n\"Gracious Heaven, Mamma--you don't suppose the man would bring his wife,\" shrieked Lady Blanche, who had been languishing in George's arms in the newly imported waltz for hours the night before. \"The men are bearable, but their women--\"\n\n\"Wife, just married, dev'lish pretty woman, I hear,\" the old Earl said.\n\n\"Well, my dear Blanche,\" said the mother, \"I suppose, as Papa wants to go, we must go; but we needn't know them in England, you know.\" And so, determined to cut their new acquaintance in Bond Street, these great folks went to eat his dinner at Brussels, and condescending to make him pay for their pleasure, showed their dignity by making his wife uncomfortable, and carefully excluding her from the conversation. This is a species of dignity in which the high-bred British female reigns supreme. To watch the behaviour of a fine lady to other and humbler women, is a very good sport for a philosophical frequenter of Vanity Fair.\n\nThis festival, on which honest George spent a great deal of money, was the very dismallest of all the entertainments which Amelia had in her honeymoon. She wrote the most piteous accounts of the feast home to her mamma: how the Countess of Bareacres would not answer when spoken to; how Lady Blanche stared at her with her eye-glass; and what a rage Captain Dobbin was in at their behaviour; and how my lord, as they came away from the feast, asked to see the bill, and pronounced it a d---- bad dinner, and d---- dear. But though Amelia told all these stories, and wrote home regarding her guests' rudeness, and her own discomfiture, old Mrs. Sedley was mightily pleased nevertheless, and talked about Emmy's friend, the Countess of Bareacres, with such assiduity that the news how his son was entertaining peers and peeresses actually came to Osborne's ears in the City.\n\nThose who know the present Lieutenant-General Sir George Tufto, K.C.B., and have seen him, as they may on most days in the season, padded and in stays, strutting down Pall Mall with a rickety swagger on his high-heeled lacquered boots, leering under the bonnets of passers-by, or riding a showy chestnut, and ogling broughams in the Parks--those who know the present Sir George Tufto would hardly recognise the daring Peninsular and Waterloo officer. He has thick curling brown hair and black eyebrows now, and his whiskers are of the deepest purple. He was light-haired and bald in 1815, and stouter in the person and in the limbs, which especially have shrunk very much of late. When he was about seventy years of age (he is now nearly eighty), his hair, which was very scarce and quite white, suddenly grew thick, and brown, and curly, and his whiskers and eyebrows took their present colour. Ill-natured people say that his chest is all wool, and that his hair, because it never grows, is a wig. Tom Tufto, with whose father he quarrelled ever so many years ago, declares that Mademoiselle de Jaisey, of the French theatre, pulled his grandpapa's hair off in the green-room; but Tom is notoriously spiteful and jealous; and the General's wig has nothing to do with our story.\n\nOne day, as some of our friends of the --th were sauntering in the flower-market of Brussels, having been to see the Hotel de Ville, which Mrs. Major O'Dowd declared was not near so large or handsome as her fawther's mansion of Glenmalony, an officer of rank, with an orderly behind him, rode up to the market, and descending from his horse, came amongst the flowers, and selected the very finest bouquet which money could buy. The beautiful bundle being tied up in a paper, the officer remounted, giving the nosegay into the charge of his military groom, who carried it with a grin, following his chief, who rode away in great state and self-satisfaction.\n\n\"You should see the flowers at Glenmalony,\" Mrs. O'Dowd was remarking. \"Me fawther has three Scotch garners with nine helpers. We have an acre of hot-houses, and pines as common as pays in the sayson. Our greeps weighs six pounds every bunch of 'em, and upon me honour and conscience I think our magnolias is as big as taykettles.\"\n\nDobbin, who never used to \"draw out\" Mrs. O'Dowd as that wicked Osborne delighted in doing (much to Amelia's terror, who implored him to spare her), fell back in the crowd, crowing and sputtering until he reached a safe distance, when he exploded amongst the astonished market-people with shrieks of yelling laughter.\n\n\"Hwhat's that gawky guggling about?\" said Mrs. O'Dowd. \"Is it his nose bleedn? He always used to say 'twas his nose bleedn, till he must have pomped all the blood out of 'um. An't the magnolias at Glenmalony as big as taykettles, O'Dowd?\"\n\n\"'Deed then they are, and bigger, Peggy,\" the Major said. When the conversation was interrupted in the manner stated by the arrival of the officer who purchased the bouquet.\n\n\"Devlish fine horse--who is it?\" George asked.\n\n\"You should see me brother Molloy Malony's horse, Molasses, that won the cop at the Curragh,\" the Major's wife was exclaiming, and was continuing the family history, when her husband interrupted her by saying--\n\n\"It's General Tufto, who commands the ---- cavalry division\"; adding quietly, \"he and I were both shot in the same leg at Talavera.\"\n\n\"Where you got your step,\" said George with a laugh. \"General Tufto! Then, my dear, the Crawleys are come.\"\n\nAmelia's heart fell--she knew not why. The sun did not seem to shine so bright. The tall old roofs and gables looked less picturesque all of a sudden, though it was a brilliant sunset, and one of the brightest and most beautiful days at the end of May.\n\nBrussels\nMr. Jos had hired a pair of horses for his open carriage, with which cattle, and the smart London vehicle, he made a very tolerable figure in the drives about Brussels. George purchased a horse for his private riding, and he and Captain Dobbin would often accompany the carriage in which Jos and his sister took daily excursions of pleasure. They went out that day in the park for their accustomed diversion, and there, sure enough, George's remark with regard to the arrival of Rawdon Crawley and his wife proved to be correct. In the midst of a little troop of horsemen, consisting of some of the very greatest persons in Brussels, Rebecca was seen in the prettiest and tightest of riding-habits, mounted on a beautiful little Arab, which she rode to perfection (having acquired the art at Queen's Crawley, where the Baronet, Mr. Pitt, and Rawdon himself had given her many lessons), and by the side of the gallant General Tufto.\n\n\"Sure it's the Juke himself,\" cried Mrs. Major O'Dowd to Jos, who began to blush violently; \"and that's Lord Uxbridge on the bay. How elegant he looks! Me brother, Molloy Malony, is as like him as two pays.\"\n\nRebecca did not make for the carriage; but as soon as she perceived her old acquaintance Amelia seated in it, acknowledged her presence by a gracious nod and smile, and by kissing and shaking her fingers playfully in the direction of the vehicle. Then she resumed her conversation with General Tufto, who asked \"who the fat officer was in the gold-laced cap?\" on which Becky replied, \"that he was an officer in the East Indian service.\" But Rawdon Crawley rode out of the ranks of his company, and came up and shook hands heartily with Amelia, and said to Jos, \"Well, old boy, how are you?\" and stared in Mrs. O'Dowd's face and at the black cock's feathers until she began to think she had made a conquest of him.\n\nGeorge, who had been delayed behind, rode up almost immediately with Dobbin, and they touched their caps to the august personages, among whom Osborne at once perceived Mrs. Crawley. He was delighted to see Rawdon leaning over his carriage familiarly and talking to Amelia, and met the aide-de-camp's cordial greeting with more than corresponding warmth. The nods between Rawdon and Dobbin were of the very faintest specimens of politeness.\n\nCrawley told George where they were stopping with General Tufto at the Hotel du Parc, and George made his friend promise to come speedily to Osborne's own residence. \"Sorry I hadn't seen you three days ago,\" George said. \"Had a dinner at the Restaurateur's--rather a nice thing. Lord Bareacres, and the Countess, and Lady Blanche, were good enough to dine with us--wish we'd had you.\" Having thus let his friend know his claims to be a man of fashion, Osborne parted from Rawdon, who followed the august squadron down an alley into which they cantered, while George and Dobbin resumed their places, one on each side of Amelia's carriage.\n\n\"How well the Juke looked,\" Mrs. O'Dowd remarked. \"The Wellesleys and Malonys are related; but, of course, poor I would never dream of introjuicing myself unless his Grace thought proper to remember our family-tie.\"\n\n\"He's a great soldier,\" Jos said, much more at ease now the great man was gone. \"Was there ever a battle won like Salamanca? Hey, Dobbin? But where was it he learnt his art? In India, my boy! The jungle's the school for a general, mark me that. I knew him myself, too, Mrs. O'Dowd: we both of us danced the same evening with Miss Cutler, daughter of Cutler of the Artillery, and a devilish fine girl, at Dumdum.\"\n\nThe apparition of the great personages held them all in talk during the drive; and at dinner; and until the hour came when they were all to go to the Opera.\n\nIt was almost like Old England. The house was filled with familiar British faces, and those toilettes for which the British female has long been celebrated. Mrs. O'Dowd's was not the least splendid amongst these, and she had a curl on her forehead, and a set of Irish diamonds and Cairngorms, which outshone all the decorations in the house, in her notion. Her presence used to excruciate Osborne; but go she would upon all parties of pleasure on which she heard her young friends were bent. It never entered into her thought but that they must be charmed with her company.\n\n\"She's been useful to you, my dear,\" George said to his wife, whom he could leave alone with less scruple when she had this society. \"But what a comfort it is that Rebecca's come: you will have her for a friend, and we may get rid now of this damn'd Irishwoman.\" To this Amelia did not answer, yes or no: and how do we know what her thoughts were?\n\nThe coup d'oeil of the Brussels opera-house did not strike Mrs. O'Dowd as being so fine as the theatre in Fishamble Street, Dublin, nor was French music at all equal, in her opinion, to the melodies of her native country. She favoured her friends with these and other opinions in a very loud tone of voice, and tossed about a great clattering fan she sported, with the most splendid complacency.\n\n\"Who is that wonderful woman with Amelia, Rawdon, love?\" said a lady in an opposite box (who, almost always civil to her husband in private, was more fond than ever of him in company).\n\n\"Don't you see that creature with a yellow thing in her turban, and a red satin gown, and a great watch?\"\n\n\"Near the pretty little woman in white?\" asked a middle-aged gentleman seated by the querist's side, with orders in his button, and several under-waistcoats, and a great, choky, white stock.\n\n\"That pretty woman in white is Amelia, General: you are remarking all the pretty women, you naughty man.\"\n\n\"Only one, begad, in the world!\" said the General, delighted, and the lady gave him a tap with a large bouquet which she had.\n\n\"Bedad it's him,\" said Mrs. O'Dowd; \"and that's the very bokay he bought in the Marshy aux Flures!\" and when Rebecca, having caught her friend's eye, performed the little hand-kissing operation once more, Mrs. Major O'D., taking the compliment to herself, returned the salute with a gracious smile, which sent that unfortunate Dobbin shrieking out of the box again.\n\nAt the end of the act, George was out of the box in a moment, and he was even going to pay his respects to Rebecca in her loge. He met Crawley in the lobby, however, where they exchanged a few sentences upon the occurrences of the last fortnight.\n\n\"You found my cheque all right at the agent's? George said, with a knowing air.\n\n\"All right, my boy,\" Rawdon answered. \"Happy to give you your revenge. Governor come round?\"\n\n\"Not yet,\" said George, \"but he will; and you know I've some private fortune through my mother. Has Aunty relented?\"\n\n\"Sent me twenty pound, damned old screw. When shall we have a meet? The General dines out on Tuesday. Can't you come Tuesday? I say, make Sedley cut off his moustache. What the devil does a civilian mean with a moustache and those infernal frogs to his coat! By-bye. Try and come on Tuesday\"; and Rawdon was going-off with two brilliant young gentlemen of fashion, who were, like himself, on the staff of a general officer.\n\nGeorge was only half pleased to be asked to dinner on that particular day when the General was not to dine. \"I will go in and pay my respects to your wife,\" said he; at which Rawdon said, \"Hm, as you please,\" looking very glum, and at which the two young officers exchanged knowing glances. George parted from them and strutted down the lobby to the General's box, the number of which he had carefully counted.\n\n\"Entrez,\" said a clear little voice, and our friend found himself in Rebecca's presence; who jumped up, clapped her hands together, and held out both of them to George, so charmed was she to see him. The General, with the orders in his button, stared at the newcomer with a sulky scowl, as much as to say, who the devil are you?\n\n\"My dear Captain George!\" cried little Rebecca in an ecstasy. \"How good of you to come. The General and I were moping together tete-a-tete. General, this is my Captain George of whom you heard me talk.\"\n\n\"Indeed,\" said the General, with a very small bow; \"of what regiment is Captain George?\"\n\nGeorge mentioned the --th: how he wished he could have said it was a crack cavalry corps.\n\n\"Come home lately from the West Indies, I believe. Not seen much service in the late war. Quartered here, Captain George?\"--the General went on with killing haughtiness.\n\n\"Not Captain George, you stupid man; Captain Osborne,\" Rebecca said. The General all the while was looking savagely from one to the other.\n\n\"Captain Osborne, indeed! Any relation to the L------ Osbornes?\"\n\n\"We bear the same arms,\" George said, as indeed was the fact; Mr. Osborne having consulted with a herald in Long Acre, and picked the L------ arms out of the peerage, when he set up his carriage fifteen years before. The General made no reply to this announcement; but took up his opera-glass--the double-barrelled lorgnon was not invented in those days--and pretended to examine the house; but Rebecca saw that his disengaged eye was working round in her direction, and shooting out bloodshot glances at her and George.\n\nShe redoubled in cordiality. \"How is dearest Amelia? But I needn't ask: how pretty she looks! And who is that nice good-natured looking creature with her--a flame of yours? O, you wicked men! And there is Mr. Sedley eating ice, I declare: how he seems to enjoy it! General, why have we not had any ices?\"\n\n\"Shall I go and fetch you some?\" said the General, bursting with wrath.\n\n\"Let ME go, I entreat you,\" George said.\n\n\"No, I will go to Amelia's box. Dear, sweet girl! Give me your arm, Captain George\"; and so saying, and with a nod to the General, she tripped into the lobby. She gave George the queerest, knowingest look, when they were together, a look which might have been interpreted, \"Don't you see the state of affairs, and what a fool I'm making of him?\" But he did not perceive it. He was thinking of his own plans, and lost in pompous admiration of his own irresistible powers of pleasing.\n\nThe curses to which the General gave a low utterance, as soon as Rebecca and her conqueror had quitted him, were so deep, that I am sure no compositor would venture to print them were they written down. They came from the General's heart; and a wonderful thing it is to think that the human heart is capable of generating such produce, and can throw out, as occasion demands, such a supply of lust and fury, rage and hatred.\n\nAmelia's gentle eyes, too, had been fixed anxiously on the pair, whose conduct had so chafed the jealous General; but when Rebecca entered her box, she flew to her friend with an affectionate rapture which showed itself, in spite of the publicity of the place; for she embraced her dearest friend in the presence of the whole house, at least in full view of the General's glass, now brought to bear upon the Osborne party. Mrs. Rawdon saluted Jos, too, with the kindliest greeting: she admired Mrs. O'Dowd's large Cairngorm brooch and superb Irish diamonds, and wouldn't believe that they were not from Golconda direct. She bustled, she chattered, she turned and twisted, and smiled upon one, and smirked on another, all in full view of the jealous opera-glass opposite. And when the time for the ballet came (in which there was no dancer that went through her grimaces or performed her comedy of action better), she skipped back to her own box, leaning on Captain Dobbin's arm this time. No, she would not have George's: he must stay and talk to his dearest, best, little Amelia.\n\n\"What a humbug that woman is!\" honest old Dobbin mumbled to George, when he came back from Rebecca's box, whither he had conducted her in perfect silence, and with a countenance as glum as an undertaker's. \"She writhes and twists about like a snake. All the time she was here, didn't you see, George, how she was acting at the General over the way?\"\n\n\"Humbug--acting! Hang it, she's the nicest little woman in England,\" George replied, showing his white teeth, and giving his ambrosial whiskers a twirl. \"You ain't a man of the world, Dobbin. Dammy, look at her now, she's talked over Tufto in no time. Look how he's laughing! Gad, what a shoulder she has! Emmy, why didn't you have a bouquet? Everybody has a bouquet.\"\n\n\"Faith, then, why didn't you BOY one?\" Mrs. O'Dowd said; and both Amelia and William Dobbin thanked her for this timely observation. But beyond this neither of the ladies rallied. Amelia was overpowered by the flash and the dazzle and the fashionable talk of her worldly rival. Even the O'Dowd was silent and subdued after Becky's brilliant apparition, and scarcely said a word more about Glenmalony all the evening.\n\n\"When do you intend to give up play, George, as you have promised me, any time these hundred years?\" Dobbin said to his friend a few days after the night at the Opera. \"When do you intend to give up sermonising?\" was the other's reply. \"What the deuce, man, are you alarmed about? We play low; I won last night. You don't suppose Crawley cheats? With fair play it comes to pretty much the same thing at the year's end.\"\n\n\"But I don't think he could pay if he lost,\" Dobbin said; and his advice met with the success which advice usually commands. Osborne and Crawley were repeatedly together now. General Tufto dined abroad almost constantly. George was always welcome in the apartments (very close indeed to those of the General) which the aide-de-camp and his wife occupied in the hotel.\n\nAmelia's manners were such when she and George visited Crawley and his wife at these quarters, that they had very nearly come to their first quarrel; that is, George scolded his wife violently for her evident unwillingness to go, and the high and mighty manner in which she comported herself towards Mrs. Crawley, her old friend; and Amelia did not say one single word in reply; but with her husband's eye upon her, and Rebecca scanning her as she felt, was, if possible, more bashful and awkward on the second visit which she paid to Mrs. Rawdon, than on her first call.\n\nRebecca was doubly affectionate, of course, and would not take notice, in the least, of her friend's coolness. \"I think Emmy has become prouder since her father's name was in the--since Mr. Sedley's MISFORTUNES,\" Rebecca said, softening the phrase charitably for George's ear.\n\n\"Upon my word, I thought when we were at Brighton she was doing me the honour to be jealous of me; and now I suppose she is scandalised because Rawdon, and I, and the General live together. Why, my dear creature, how could we, with our means, live at all, but for a friend to share expenses? And do you suppose that Rawdon is not big enough to take care of my honour? But I'm very much obliged to Emmy, very,\" Mrs. Rawdon said.\n\n\"Pooh, jealousy!\" answered George, \"all women are jealous.\"\n\n\"And all men too. Weren't you jealous of General Tufto, and the General of you, on the night of the Opera? Why, he was ready to eat me for going with you to visit that foolish little wife of yours; as if I care a pin for either of you,\" Crawley's wife said, with a pert toss of her head. \"Will you dine here? The dragon dines with the Commander-in-Chief. Great news is stirring. They say the French have crossed the frontier. We shall have a quiet dinner.\"\n\nGeorge accepted the invitation, although his wife was a little ailing. They were now not quite six weeks married. Another woman was laughing or sneering at her expense, and he not angry. He was not even angry with himself, this good-natured fellow. It is a shame, he owned to himself; but hang it, if a pretty woman WILL throw herself in your way, why, what can a fellow do, you know? I AM rather free about women, he had often said, smiling and nodding knowingly to Stubble and Spooney, and other comrades of the mess-table; and they rather respected him than otherwise for this prowess. Next to conquering in war, conquering in love has been a source of pride, time out of mind, amongst men in Vanity Fair, or how should schoolboys brag of their amours, or Don Juan be popular?\n\nSo Mr. Osborne, having a firm conviction in his own mind that he was a woman-killer and destined to conquer, did not run counter to his fate, but yielded himself up to it quite complacently. And as Emmy did not say much or plague him with her jealousy, but merely became unhappy and pined over it miserably in secret, he chose to fancy that she was not suspicious of what all his acquaintance were perfectly aware--namely, that he was carrying on a desperate flirtation with Mrs. Crawley. He rode with her whenever she was free. He pretended regimental business to Amelia (by which falsehood she was not in the least deceived), and consigning his wife to solitude or her brother's society, passed his evenings in the Crawleys' company; losing money to the husband and flattering himself that the wife was dying of love for him. It is very likely that this worthy couple never absolutely conspired and agreed together in so many words: the one to cajole the young gentleman, whilst the other won his money at cards: but they understood each other perfectly well, and Rawdon let Osborne come and go with entire good humour.\n\nGeorge was so occupied with his new acquaintances that he and William Dobbin were by no means so much together as formerly. George avoided him in public and in the regiment, and, as we see, did not like those sermons which his senior was disposed to inflict upon him. If some parts of his conduct made Captain Dobbin exceedingly grave and cool; of what use was it to tell George that, though his whiskers were large, and his own opinion of his knowingness great, he was as green as a schoolboy? that Rawdon was making a victim of him as he had done of many before, and as soon as he had used him would fling him off with scorn? He would not listen: and so, as Dobbin, upon those days when he visited the Osborne house, seldom had the advantage of meeting his old friend, much painful and unavailing talk between them was spared. Our friend George was in the full career of the pleasures of Vanity Fair.\n\nThere never was, since the days of Darius, such a brilliant train of camp-followers as hung round the Duke of Wellington's army in the Low Countries, in 1815; and led it dancing and feasting, as it were, up to the very brink of battle. A certain ball which a noble Duchess gave at Brussels on the 15th of June in the above-named year is historical. All Brussels had been in a state of excitement about it, and I have heard from ladies who were in that town at the period, that the talk and interest of persons of their own sex regarding the ball was much greater even than in respect of the enemy in their front. The struggles, intrigues, and prayers to get tickets were such as only English ladies will employ, in order to gain admission to the society of the great of their own nation.\n\nJos and Mrs. O'Dowd, who were panting to be asked, strove in vain to procure tickets; but others of our friends were more lucky. For instance, through the interest of my Lord Bareacres, and as a set-off for the dinner at the restaurateur's, George got a card for Captain and Mrs. Osborne; which circumstance greatly elated him. Dobbin, who was a friend of the General commanding the division in which their regiment was, came laughing one day to Mrs. Osborne, and displayed a similar invitation, which made Jos envious, and George wonder how the deuce he should be getting into society. Mr. and Mrs. Rawdon, finally, were of course invited; as became the friends of a General commanding a cavalry brigade.\n\nOn the appointed night, George, having commanded new dresses and ornaments of all sorts for Amelia, drove to the famous ball, where his wife did not know a single soul. After looking about for Lady Bareacres, who cut him, thinking the card was quite enough--and after placing Amelia on a bench, he left her to her own cogitations there, thinking, on his own part, that he had behaved very handsomely in getting her new clothes, and bringing her to the ball, where she was free to amuse herself as she liked. Her thoughts were not of the pleasantest, and nobody except honest Dobbin came to disturb them.\n\nWhilst her appearance was an utter failure (as her husband felt with a sort of rage), Mrs. Rawdon Crawley's debut was, on the contrary, very brilliant. She arrived very late. Her face was radiant; her dress perfection. In the midst of the great persons assembled, and the eye-glasses directed to her, Rebecca seemed to be as cool and collected as when she used to marshal Miss Pinkerton's little girls to church. Numbers of the men she knew already, and the dandies thronged round her. As for the ladies, it was whispered among them that Rawdon had run away with her from out of a convent, and that she was a relation of the Montmorency family. She spoke French so perfectly that there might be some truth in this report, and it was agreed that her manners were fine, and her air distingue. Fifty would-be partners thronged round her at once, and pressed to have the honour to dance with her. But she said she was engaged, and only going to dance very little; and made her way at once to the place where Emmy sate quite unnoticed, and dismally unhappy. And so, to finish the poor child at once, Mrs. Rawdon ran and greeted affectionately her dearest Amelia, and began forthwith to patronise her. She found fault with her friend's dress, and her hairdresser, and wondered how she could be so chaussee, and vowed that she must send her corsetiere the next morning. She vowed that it was a delightful ball; that there was everybody that every one knew, and only a VERY few nobodies in the whole room. It is a fact, that in a fortnight, and after three dinners in general society, this young woman had got up the genteel jargon so well, that a native could not speak it better; and it was only from her French being so good, that you could know she was not a born woman of fashion.\n\nGeorge, who had left Emmy on her bench on entering the ball-room, very soon found his way back when Rebecca was by her dear friend's side. Becky was just lecturing Mrs. Osborne upon the follies which her husband was committing. \"For God's sake, stop him from gambling, my dear,\" she said, \"or he will ruin himself. He and Rawdon are playing at cards every night, and you know he is very poor, and Rawdon will win every shilling from him if he does not take care. Why don't you prevent him, you little careless creature? Why don't you come to us of an evening, instead of moping at home with that Captain Dobbin? I dare say he is tres aimable; but how could one love a man with feet of such size? Your husband's feet are darlings--Here he comes. Where have you been, wretch? Here is Emmy crying her eyes out for you. Are you coming to fetch me for the quadrille?\" And she left her bouquet and shawl by Amelia's side, and tripped off with George to dance. Women only know how to wound so. There is a poison on the tips of their little shafts, which stings a thousand times more than a man's blunter weapon. Our poor Emmy, who had never hated, never sneered all her life, was powerless in the hands of her remorseless little enemy.\n\nGeorge danced with Rebecca twice or thrice--how many times Amelia scarcely knew. She sat quite unnoticed in her corner, except when Rawdon came up with some words of clumsy conversation: and later in the evening, when Captain Dobbin made so bold as to bring her refreshments and sit beside her. He did not like to ask her why she was so sad; but as a pretext for the tears which were filling in her eyes, she told him that Mrs. Crawley had alarmed her by telling her that George would go on playing.\n\n\"It is curious, when a man is bent upon play, by what clumsy rogues he will allow himself to be cheated,\" Dobbin said; and Emmy said, \"Indeed.\" She was thinking of something else. It was not the loss of the money that grieved her.\n\nAt last George came back for Rebecca's shawl and flowers. She was going away. She did not even condescend to come back and say good-bye to Amelia. The poor girl let her husband come and go without saying a word, and her head fell on her breast. Dobbin had been called away, and was whispering deep in conversation with the General of the division, his friend, and had not seen this last parting. George went away then with the bouquet; but when he gave it to the owner, there lay a note, coiled like a snake among the flowers. Rebecca's eye caught it at once. She had been used to deal with notes in early life. She put out her hand and took the nosegay. He saw by her eyes as they met, that she was aware what she should find there. Her husband hurried her away, still too intent upon his own thoughts, seemingly, to take note of any marks of recognition which might pass between his friend and his wife. These were, however, but trifling. Rebecca gave George her hand with one of her usual quick knowing glances, and made a curtsey and walked away. George bowed over the hand, said nothing in reply to a remark of Crawley's, did not hear it even, his brain was so throbbing with triumph and excitement, and allowed them to go away without a word.\n\nHis wife saw the one part at least of the bouquet-scene. It was quite natural that George should come at Rebecca's request to get her her scarf and flowers: it was no more than he had done twenty times before in the course of the last few days; but now it was too much for her. \"William,\" she said, suddenly clinging to Dobbin, who was near her, \"you've always been very kind to me--I'm--I'm not well. Take me home.\" She did not know she called him by his Christian name, as George was accustomed to do. He went away with her quickly. Her lodgings were hard by; and they threaded through the crowd without, where everything seemed to be more astir than even in the ball-room within.\n\nGeorge had been angry twice or thrice at finding his wife up on his return from the parties which he frequented: so she went straight to bed now; but although she did not sleep, and although the din and clatter, and the galloping of horsemen were incessant, she never heard any of these noises, having quite other disturbances to keep her awake.\n\nOsborne meanwhile, wild with elation, went off to a play-table, and began to bet frantically. He won repeatedly. \"Everything succeeds with me to-night,\" he said. But his luck at play even did not cure him of his restlessness, and he started up after awhile, pocketing his winnings, and went to a buffet, where he drank off many bumpers of wine.\n\nHere, as he was rattling away to the people around, laughing loudly and wild with spirits, Dobbin found him. He had been to the card-tables to look there for his friend. Dobbin looked as pale and grave as his comrade was flushed and jovial.\n\n\"Hullo, Dob! Come and drink, old Dob! The Duke's wine is famous. Give me some more, you sir\"; and he held out a trembling glass for the liquor.\n\n\"Come out, George,\" said Dobbin, still gravely; \"don't drink.\"\n\n\"Drink! there's nothing like it. Drink yourself, and light up your lantern jaws, old boy. Here's to you.\"\n\nDobbin went up and whispered something to him, at which George, giving a start and a wild hurray, tossed off his glass, clapped it on the table, and walked away speedily on his friend's arm. \"The enemy has passed the Sambre,\" William said, \"and our left is already engaged. Come away. We are to march in three hours.\"\n\nAway went George, his nerves quivering with excitement at the news so long looked for, so sudden when it came. What were love and intrigue now? He thought about a thousand things but these in his rapid walk to his quarters--his past life and future chances--the fate which might be before him--the wife, the child perhaps, from whom unseen he might be about to part. Oh, how he wished that night's work undone! and that with a clear conscience at least he might say farewell to the tender and guileless being by whose love he had set such little store!\n\nHe thought over his brief married life. In those few weeks he had frightfully dissipated his little capital. How wild and reckless he had been! Should any mischance befall him: what was then left for her? How unworthy he was of her. Why had he married her? He was not fit for marriage. Why had he disobeyed his father, who had been always so generous to him? Hope, remorse, ambition, tenderness, and selfish regret filled his heart. He sate down and wrote to his father, remembering what he had said once before, when he was engaged to fight a duel. Dawn faintly streaked the sky as he closed this farewell letter. He sealed it, and kissed the superscription. He thought how he had deserted that generous father, and of the thousand kindnesses which the stern old man had done him.\n\nHe had looked into Amelia's bedroom when he entered; she lay quiet, and her eyes seemed closed, and he was glad that she was asleep. On arriving at his quarters from the ball, he had found his regimental servant already making preparations for his departure: the man had understood his signal to be still, and these arrangements were very quickly and silently made. Should he go in and wake Amelia, he thought, or leave a note for her brother to break the news of departure to her? He went in to look at her once again.\n\nShe had been awake when he first entered her room, but had kept her eyes closed, so that even her wakefulness should not seem to reproach him. But when he had returned, so soon after herself, too, this timid little heart had felt more at ease, and turning towards him as he stept softly out of the room, she had fallen into a light sleep. George came in and looked at her again, entering still more softly. By the pale night-lamp he could see her sweet, pale face--the purple eyelids were fringed and closed, and one round arm, smooth and white, lay outside of the coverlet. Good God! how pure she was; how gentle, how tender, and how friendless! and he, how selfish, brutal, and black with crime! Heart-stained, and shame-stricken, he stood at the bed's foot, and looked at the sleeping girl. How dared he--who was he, to pray for one so spotless! God bless her! God bless her! He came to the bedside, and looked at the hand, the little soft hand, lying asleep; and he bent over the pillow noiselessly towards the gentle pale face.\n\nTwo fair arms closed tenderly round his neck as he stooped down. \"I am awake, George,\" the poor child said, with a sob fit to break the little heart that nestled so closely by his own. She was awake, poor soul, and to what? At that moment a bugle from the Place of Arms began sounding clearly, and was taken up through the town; and amidst the drums of the infantry, and the shrill pipes of the Scotch, the whole city awoke.\n\n\"The Girl I Left Behind Me\"\nWe do not claim to rank among the military novelists. Our place is with the non-combatants. When the decks are cleared for action we go below and wait meekly. We should only be in the way of the manoeuvres that the gallant fellows are performing overhead. We shall go no farther with the --th than to the city gate: and leaving Major O'Dowd to his duty, come back to the Major's wife, and the ladies and the baggage.\n\nNow the Major and his lady, who had not been invited to the ball at which in our last chapter other of our friends figured, had much more time to take their wholesome natural rest in bed, than was accorded to people who wished to enjoy pleasure as well as to do duty. \"It's my belief, Peggy, my dear,\" said he, as he placidly pulled his nightcap over his ears, \"that there will be such a ball danced in a day or two as some of 'em has never heard the chune of\"; and he was much more happy to retire to rest after partaking of a quiet tumbler, than to figure at any other sort of amusement. Peggy, for her part, would have liked to have shown her turban and bird of paradise at the ball, but for the information which her husband had given her, and which made her very grave.\n\n\"I'd like ye wake me about half an hour before the assembly beats,\" the Major said to his lady. \"Call me at half-past one, Peggy dear, and see me things is ready. May be I'll not come back to breakfast, Mrs. O'D.\" With which words, which signified his opinion that the regiment would march the next morning, the Major ceased talking, and fell asleep.\n\nMrs. O'Dowd, the good housewife, arrayed in curl papers and a camisole, felt that her duty was to act, and not to sleep, at this juncture. \"Time enough for that,\" she said, \"when Mick's gone\"; and so she packed his travelling valise ready for the march, brushed his cloak, his cap, and other warlike habiliments, set them out in order for him; and stowed away in the cloak pockets a light package of portable refreshments, and a wicker-covered flask or pocket-pistol, containing near a pint of a remarkably sound Cognac brandy, of which she and the Major approved very much; and as soon as the hands of the \"repayther\" pointed to half-past one, and its interior arrangements (it had a tone quite equal to a cathaydral, its fair owner considered) knelled forth that fatal hour, Mrs. O'Dowd woke up her Major, and had as comfortable a cup of coffee prepared for him as any made that morning in Brussels. And who is there will deny that this worthy lady's preparations betokened affection as much as the fits of tears and hysterics by which more sensitive females exhibited their love, and that their partaking of this coffee, which they drank together while the bugles were sounding the turn-out and the drums beating in the various quarters of the town, was not more useful and to the purpose than the outpouring of any mere sentiment could be? The consequence was, that the Major appeared on parade quite trim, fresh, and alert, his well-shaved rosy countenance, as he sate on horseback, giving cheerfulness and confidence to the whole corps. All the officers saluted her when the regiment marched by the balcony on which this brave woman stood, and waved them a cheer as they passed; and I daresay it was not from want of courage, but from a sense of female delicacy and propriety, that she refrained from leading the gallant--th personally into action.\n\nOn Sundays, and at periods of a solemn nature, Mrs. O'Dowd used to read with great gravity out of a large volume of her uncle the Dean's sermons. It had been of great comfort to her on board the transport as they were coming home, and were very nearly wrecked, on their return from the West Indies. After the regiment's departure she betook herself to this volume for meditation; perhaps she did not understand much of what she was reading, and her thoughts were elsewhere: but the sleep project, with poor Mick's nightcap there on the pillow, was quite a vain one. So it is in the world. Jack or Donald marches away to glory with his knapsack on his shoulder, stepping out briskly to the tune of \"The Girl I Left Behind Me.\" It is she who remains and suffers--and has the leisure to think, and brood, and remember.\n\nKnowing how useless regrets are, and how the indulgence of sentiment only serves to make people more miserable, Mrs. Rebecca wisely determined to give way to no vain feelings of sorrow, and bore the parting from her husband with quite a Spartan equanimity. Indeed Captain Rawdon himself was much more affected at the leave-taking than the resolute little woman to whom he bade farewell. She had mastered this rude coarse nature; and he loved and worshipped her with all his faculties of regard and admiration. In all his life he had never been so happy, as, during the past few months, his wife had made him. All former delights of turf, mess, hunting-field, and gambling-table; all previous loves and courtships of milliners, opera-dancers, and the like easy triumphs of the clumsy military Adonis, were quite insipid when compared to the lawful matrimonial pleasures which of late he had enjoyed. She had known perpetually how to divert him; and he had found his house and her society a thousand times more pleasant than any place or company which he had ever frequented from his childhood until now. And he cursed his past follies and extravagances, and bemoaned his vast outlying debts above all, which must remain for ever as obstacles to prevent his wife's advancement in the world. He had often groaned over these in midnight conversations with Rebecca, although as a bachelor they had never given him any disquiet. He himself was struck with this phenomenon. \"Hang it,\" he would say (or perhaps use a still stronger expression out of his simple vocabulary), \"before I was married I didn't care what bills I put my name to, and so long as Moses would wait or Levy would renew for three months, I kept on never minding. But since I'm married, except renewing, of course, I give you my honour I've not touched a bit of stamped paper.\"\n\nRebecca always knew how to conjure away these moods of melancholy. \"Why, my stupid love,\" she would say, \"we have not done with your aunt yet. If she fails us, isn't there what you call the Gazette? or, stop, when your uncle Bute's life drops, I have another scheme. The living has always belonged to the younger brother, and why shouldn't you sell out and go into the Church?\" The idea of this conversion set Rawdon into roars of laughter: you might have heard the explosion through the hotel at midnight, and the haw-haws of the great dragoon's voice. General Tufto heard him from his quarters on the first floor above them; and Rebecca acted the scene with great spirit, and preached Rawdon's first sermon, to the immense delight of the General at breakfast.\n\nBut these were mere by-gone days and talk. When the final news arrived that the campaign was opened, and the troops were to march, Rawdon's gravity became such that Becky rallied him about it in a manner which rather hurt the feelings of the Guardsman. \"You don't suppose I'm afraid, Becky, I should think,\" he said, with a tremor in his voice. \"But I'm a pretty good mark for a shot, and you see if it brings me down, why I leave one and perhaps two behind me whom I should wish to provide for, as I brought 'em into the scrape. It is no laughing matter that, Mrs. C., anyways.\"\n\nRebecca by a hundred caresses and kind words tried to soothe the feelings of the wounded lover. It was only when her vivacity and sense of humour got the better of this sprightly creature (as they would do under most circumstances of life indeed) that she would break out with her satire, but she could soon put on a demure face. \"Dearest love,\" she said, \"do you suppose I feel nothing?\" and hastily dashing something from her eyes, she looked up in her husband's face with a smile.\n\n\"Look here,\" said he. \"If I drop, let us see what there is for you. I have had a pretty good run of luck here, and here's two hundred and thirty pounds. I have got ten Napoleons in my pocket. That is as much as I shall want; for the General pays everything like a prince; and if I'm hit, why you know I cost nothing. Don't cry, little woman; I may live to vex you yet. Well, I shan't take either of my horses, but shall ride the General's grey charger: it's cheaper, and I told him mine was lame. If I'm done, those two ought to fetch you something. Grigg offered ninety for the mare yesterday, before this confounded news came, and like a fool I wouldn't let her go under the two o's. Bullfinch will fetch his price any day, only you'd better sell him in this country, because the dealers have so many bills of mine, and so I'd rather he shouldn't go back to England. Your little mare the General gave you will fetch something, and there's no d--d livery stable bills here as there are in London,\" Rawdon added, with a laugh. \"There's that dressing-case cost me two hundred--that is, I owe two for it; and the gold tops and bottles must be worth thirty or forty. Please to put THAT up the spout, ma'am, with my pins, and rings, and watch and chain, and things. They cost a precious lot of money. Miss Crawley, I know, paid a hundred down for the chain and ticker. Gold tops and bottles, indeed! dammy, I'm sorry I didn't take more now. Edwards pressed on me a silver-gilt boot-jack, and I might have had a dressing-case fitted up with a silver warming-pan, and a service of plate. But we must make the best of what we've got, Becky, you know.\"\n\nAnd so, making his last dispositions, Captain Crawley, who had seldom thought about anything but himself, until the last few months of his life, when Love had obtained the mastery over the dragoon, went through the various items of his little catalogue of effects, striving to see how they might be turned into money for his wife's benefit, in case any accident should befall him. He pleased himself by noting down with a pencil, in his big schoolboy handwriting, the various items of his portable property which might be sold for his widow's advantage as, for example, \"My double-barril by Manton, say 40 guineas; my driving cloak, lined with sable fur, 50 pounds; my duelling pistols in rosewood case (same which I shot Captain Marker), 20 pounds; my regulation saddle-holsters and housings; my Laurie ditto,\" and so forth, over all of which articles he made Rebecca the mistress.\n\nFaithful to his plan of economy, the Captain dressed himself in his oldest and shabbiest uniform and epaulets, leaving the newest behind, under his wife's (or it might be his widow's) guardianship. And this famous dandy of Windsor and Hyde Park went off on his campaign with a kit as modest as that of a sergeant, and with something like a prayer on his lips for the woman he was leaving. He took her up from the ground, and held her in his arms for a minute, tight pressed against his strong-beating heart. His face was purple and his eyes dim, as he put her down and left her. He rode by his General's side, and smoked his cigar in silence as they hastened after the troops of the General's brigade, which preceded them; and it was not until they were some miles on their way that he left off twirling his moustache and broke silence.\n\nAnd Rebecca, as we have said, wisely determined not to give way to unavailing sentimentality on her husband's departure. She waved him an adieu from the window, and stood there for a moment looking out after he was gone. The cathedral towers and the full gables of the quaint old houses were just beginning to blush in the sunrise. There had been no rest for her that night. She was still in her pretty ball-dress, her fair hair hanging somewhat out of curl on her neck, and the circles round her eyes dark with watching. \"What a fright I seem,\" she said, examining herself in the glass, \"and how pale this pink makes one look!\" So she divested herself of this pink raiment; in doing which a note fell out from her corsage, which she picked up with a smile, and locked into her dressing-box. And then she put her bouquet of the ball into a glass of water, and went to bed, and slept very comfortably.\n\nThe town was quite quiet when she woke up at ten o'clock, and partook of coffee, very requisite and comforting after the exhaustion and grief of the morning's occurrences.\n\nThis meal over, she resumed honest Rawdon's calculations of the night previous, and surveyed her position. Should the worst befall, all things considered, she was pretty well to do. There were her own trinkets and trousseau, in addition to those which her husband had left behind. Rawdon's generosity, when they were first married, has already been described and lauded. Besides these, and the little mare, the General, her slave and worshipper, had made her many very handsome presents, in the shape of cashmere shawls bought at the auction of a bankrupt French general's lady, and numerous tributes from the jewellers' shops, all of which betokened her admirer's taste and wealth. As for \"tickers,\" as poor Rawdon called watches, her apartments were alive with their clicking. For, happening to mention one night that hers, which Rawdon had given to her, was of English workmanship, and went ill, on the very next morning there came to her a little bijou marked Leroy, with a chain and cover charmingly set with turquoises, and another signed Brequet, which was covered with pearls, and yet scarcely bigger than a half-crown. General Tufto had bought one, and Captain Osborne had gallantly presented the other. Mrs. Osborne had no watch, though, to do George justice, she might have had one for the asking, and the Honourable Mrs. Tufto in England had an old instrument of her mother's that might have served for the plate-warming pan which Rawdon talked about. If Messrs. Howell and James were to publish a list of the purchasers of all the trinkets which they sell, how surprised would some families be: and if all these ornaments went to gentlemen's lawful wives and daughters, what a profusion of jewellery there would be exhibited in the genteelest homes of Vanity Fair!\n\nEvery calculation made of these valuables Mrs. Rebecca found, not without a pungent feeling of triumph and self-satisfaction, that should circumstances occur, she might reckon on six or seven hundred pounds at the very least, to begin the world with; and she passed the morning disposing, ordering, looking out, and locking up her properties in the most agreeable manner. Among the notes in Rawdon's pocket-book was a draft for twenty pounds on Osborne's banker. This made her think about Mrs. Osborne. \"I will go and get the draft cashed,\" she said, \"and pay a visit afterwards to poor little Emmy.\" If this is a novel without a hero, at least let us lay claim to a heroine. No man in the British army which has marched away, not the great Duke himself, could be more cool or collected in the presence of doubts and difficulties, than the indomitable little aide-de-camp's wife.\n\nAnd there was another of our acquaintances who was also to be left behind, a non-combatant, and whose emotions and behaviour we have therefore a right to know. This was our friend the ex-collector of Boggley Wollah, whose rest was broken, like other people's, by the sounding of the bugles in the early morning. Being a great sleeper, and fond of his bed, it is possible he would have snoozed on until his usual hour of rising in the forenoon, in spite of all the drums, bugles, and bagpipes in the British army, but for an interruption, which did not come from George Osborne, who shared Jos's quarters with him, and was as usual occupied too much with his own affairs or with grief at parting with his wife, to think of taking leave of his slumbering brother-in-law--it was not George, we say, who interposed between Jos Sedley and sleep, but Captain Dobbin, who came and roused him up, insisting on shaking hands with him before his departure.\n\n\"Very kind of you,\" said Jos, yawning, and wishing the Captain at the deuce.\n\n\"I--I didn't like to go off without saying good-bye, you know,\" Dobbin said in a very incoherent manner; \"because you know some of us mayn't come back again, and I like to see you all well, and--and that sort of thing, you know.\"\n\n\"What do you mean?\" Jos asked, rubbing his eyes. The Captain did not in the least hear him or look at the stout gentleman in the nightcap, about whom he professed to have such a tender interest. The hypocrite was looking and listening with all his might in the direction of George's apartments, striding about the room, upsetting the chairs, beating the tattoo, biting his nails, and showing other signs of great inward emotion.\n\nJos had always had rather a mean opinion of the Captain, and now began to think his courage was somewhat equivocal. \"What is it I can do for you, Dobbin?\" he said, in a sarcastic tone.\n\n\"I tell you what you can do,\" the Captain replied, coming up to the bed; \"we march in a quarter of an hour, Sedley, and neither George nor I may ever come back. Mind you, you are not to stir from this town until you ascertain how things go. You are to stay here and watch over your sister, and comfort her, and see that no harm comes to her. If anything happens to George, remember she has no one but you in the world to look to. If it goes wrong with the army, you'll see her safe back to England; and you will promise me on your word that you will never desert her. I know you won't: as far as money goes, you were always free enough with that. Do you want any? I mean, have you enough gold to take you back to England in case of a misfortune?\"\n\n\"Sir,\" said Jos, majestically, \"when I want money, I know where to ask for it. And as for my sister, you needn't tell me how I ought to behave to her.\"\n\n\"You speak like a man of spirit, Jos,\" the other answered good-naturedly, \"and I am glad that George can leave her in such good hands. So I may give him your word of honour, may I, that in case of extremity you will stand by her?\"\n\n\"Of course, of course,\" answered Mr. Jos, whose generosity in money matters Dobbin estimated quite correctly.\n\n\"And you'll see her safe out of Brussels in the event of a defeat?\"\n\n\"A defeat! D---- it, sir, it's impossible. Don't try and frighten ME,\" the hero cried from his bed; and Dobbin's mind was thus perfectly set at ease now that Jos had spoken out so resolutely respecting his conduct to his sister. \"At least,\" thought the Captain, \"there will be a retreat secured for her in case the worst should ensue.\"\n\nIf Captain Dobbin expected to get any personal comfort and satisfaction from having one more view of Amelia before the regiment marched away, his selfishness was punished just as such odious egotism deserved to be. The door of Jos's bedroom opened into the sitting-room which was common to the family party, and opposite this door was that of Amelia's chamber. The bugles had wakened everybody: there was no use in concealment now. George's servant was packing in this room: Osborne coming in and out of the contiguous bedroom, flinging to the man such articles as he thought fit to carry on the campaign. And presently Dobbin had the opportunity which his heart coveted, and he got sight of Amelia's face once more. But what a face it was! So white, so wild and despair-stricken, that the remembrance of it haunted him afterwards like a crime, and the sight smote him with inexpressible pangs of longing and pity.\n\nShe was wrapped in a white morning dress, her hair falling on her shoulders, and her large eyes fixed and without light. By way of helping on the preparations for the departure, and showing that she too could be useful at a moment so critical, this poor soul had taken up a sash of George's from the drawers whereon it lay, and followed him to and fro with the sash in her hand, looking on mutely as his packing proceeded. She came out and stood, leaning at the wall, holding this sash against her bosom, from which the heavy net of crimson dropped like a large stain of blood. Our gentle-hearted Captain felt a guilty shock as he looked at her. \"Good God,\" thought he, \"and is it grief like this I dared to pry into?\" And there was no help: no means to soothe and comfort this helpless, speechless misery. He stood for a moment and looked at her, powerless and torn with pity, as a parent regards an infant in pain.\n\nAt last, George took Emmy's hand, and led her back into the bedroom, from whence he came out alone. The parting had taken place in that moment, and he was gone.\n\n\"Thank Heaven that is over,\" George thought, bounding down the stair, his sword under his arm, as he ran swiftly to the alarm ground, where the regiment was mustered, and whither trooped men and officers hurrying from their billets; his pulse was throbbing and his cheeks flushed: the great game of war was going to be played, and he one of the players. What a fierce excitement of doubt, hope, and pleasure! What tremendous hazards of loss or gain! What were all the games of chance he had ever played compared to this one? Into all contests requiring athletic skill and courage, the young man, from his boyhood upwards, had flung himself with all his might. The champion of his school and his regiment, the bravos of his companions had followed him everywhere; from the boys' cricket-match to the garrison-races, he had won a hundred of triumphs; and wherever he went women and men had admired and envied him. What qualities are there for which a man gets so speedy a return of applause, as those of bodily superiority, activity, and valour? Time out of mind strength and courage have been the theme of bards and romances; and from the story of Troy down to to-day, poetry has always chosen a soldier for a hero. I wonder is it because men are cowards in heart that they admire bravery so much, and place military valour so far beyond every other quality for reward and worship?\n\nSo, at the sound of that stirring call to battle, George jumped away from the gentle arms in which he had been dallying; not without a feeling of shame (although his wife's hold on him had been but feeble), that he should have been detained there so long. The same feeling of eagerness and excitement was amongst all those friends of his of whom we have had occasional glimpses, from the stout senior Major, who led the regiment into action, to little Stubble, the Ensign, who was to bear its colours on that day.\n\nThe sun was just rising as the march began--it was a gallant sight--the band led the column, playing the regimental march--then came the Major in command, riding upon Pyramus, his stout charger--then marched the grenadiers, their Captain at their head; in the centre were the colours, borne by the senior and junior Ensigns--then George came marching at the head of his company. He looked up, and smiled at Amelia, and passed on; and even the sound of the music died away.\n\nIn Which Jos Sedley Takes Care of His Sister\nThus all the superior officers being summoned on duty elsewhere, Jos Sedley was left in command of the little colony at Brussels, with Amelia invalided, Isidor, his Belgian servant, and the bonne, who was maid-of-all-work for the establishment, as a garrison under him. Though he was disturbed in spirit, and his rest destroyed by Dobbin's interruption and the occurrences of the morning, Jos nevertheless remained for many hours in bed, wakeful and rolling about there until his usual hour of rising had arrived. The sun was high in the heavens, and our gallant friends of the --th miles on their march, before the civilian appeared in his flowered dressing-gown at breakfast.\n\nAbout George's absence, his brother-in-law was very easy in mind. Perhaps Jos was rather pleased in his heart that Osborne was gone, for during George's presence, the other had played but a very secondary part in the household, and Osborne did not scruple to show his contempt for the stout civilian. But Emmy had always been good and attentive to him. It was she who ministered to his comforts, who superintended the dishes that he liked, who walked or rode with him (as she had many, too many, opportunities of doing, for where was George?) and who interposed her sweet face between his anger and her husband's scorn. Many timid remonstrances had she uttered to George in behalf of her brother, but the former in his trenchant way cut these entreaties short. \"I'm an honest man,\" he said, \"and if I have a feeling I show it, as an honest man will. How the deuce, my dear, would you have me behave respectfully to such a fool as your brother?\" So Jos was pleased with George's absence. His plain hat, and gloves on a sideboard, and the idea that the owner was away, caused Jos I don't know what secret thrill of pleasure. \"HE won't be troubling me this morning,\" Jos thought, \"with his dandified airs and his impudence.\"\n\n\"Put the Captain's hat into the ante-room,\" he said to Isidor, the servant.\n\n\"Perhaps he won't want it again,\" replied the lackey, looking knowingly at his master. He hated George too, whose insolence towards him was quite of the English sort.\n\n\"And ask if Madame is coming to breakfast,\" Mr. Sedley said with great majesty, ashamed to enter with a servant upon the subject of his dislike for George. The truth is, he had abused his brother to the valet a score of times before.\n\nAlas! Madame could not come to breakfast, and cut the tartines that Mr. Jos liked. Madame was a great deal too ill, and had been in a frightful state ever since her husband's departure, so her bonne said. Jos showed his sympathy by pouring her out a large cup of tea It was his way of exhibiting kindness: and he improved on this; he not only sent her breakfast, but he bethought him what delicacies she would most like for dinner.\n\nIsidor, the valet, had looked on very sulkily, while Osborne's servant was disposing of his master's baggage previous to the Captain's departure: for in the first place he hated Mr. Osborne, whose conduct to him, and to all inferiors, was generally overbearing (nor does the continental domestic like to be treated with insolence as our own better-tempered servants do), and secondly, he was angry that so many valuables should be removed from under his hands, to fall into other people's possession when the English discomfiture should arrive. Of this defeat he and a vast number of other persons in Brussels and Belgium did not make the slightest doubt. The almost universal belief was, that the Emperor would divide the Prussian and English armies, annihilate one after the other, and march into Brussels before three days were over: when all the movables of his present masters, who would be killed, or fugitives, or prisoners, would lawfully become the property of Monsieur Isidor.\n\nAs he helped Jos through his toilsome and complicated daily toilette, this faithful servant would calculate what he should do with the very articles with which he was decorating his master's person. He would make a present of the silver essence-bottles and toilet knicknacks to a young lady of whom he was fond; and keep the English cutlery and the large ruby pin for himself. It would look very smart upon one of the fine frilled shirts, which, with the gold-laced cap and the frogged frock coat, that might easily be cut down to suit his shape, and the Captain's gold-headed cane, and the great double ring with the rubies, which he would have made into a pair of beautiful earrings, he calculated would make a perfect Adonis of himself, and render Mademoiselle Reine an easy prey. \"How those sleeve-buttons will suit me!\" thought he, as he fixed a pair on the fat pudgy wrists of Mr. Sedley. \"I long for sleeve-buttons; and the Captain's boots with brass spurs, in the next room, corbleu! what an effect they will make in the Allee Verte!\" So while Monsieur Isidor with bodily fingers was holding on to his master's nose, and shaving the lower part of Jos's face, his imagination was rambling along the Green Avenue, dressed out in a frogged coat and lace, and in company with Mademoiselle Reine; he was loitering in spirit on the banks, and examining the barges sailing slowly under the cool shadows of the trees by the canal, or refreshing himself with a mug of Faro at the bench of a beer-house on the road to Laeken.\n\nBut Mr. Joseph Sedley, luckily for his own peace, no more knew what was passing in his domestic's mind than the respected reader, and I suspect what John or Mary, whose wages we pay, think of ourselves. What our servants think of us!--Did we know what our intimates and dear relations thought of us, we should live in a world that we should be glad to quit, and in a frame of mind and a constant terror, that would be perfectly unbearable. So Jos's man was marking his victim down, as you see one of Mr. Paynter's assistants in Leadenhall Street ornament an unconscious turtle with a placard on which is written, \"Soup to-morrow.\"\n\nAmelia's attendant was much less selfishly disposed. Few dependents could come near that kind and gentle creature without paying their usual tribute of loyalty and affection to her sweet and affectionate nature. And it is a fact that Pauline, the cook, consoled her mistress more than anybody whom she saw on this wretched morning; for when she found how Amelia remained for hours, silent, motionless, and haggard, by the windows in which she had placed herself to watch the last bayonets of the column as it marched away, the honest girl took the lady's hand, and said, Tenez, Madame, est-ce qu'il n'est pas aussi a l'armee, mon homme a moi? with which she burst into tears, and Amelia falling into her arms, did likewise, and so each pitied and soothed the other.\n\nSeveral times during the forenoon Mr. Jos's Isidor went from his lodgings into the town, and to the gates of the hotels and lodging-houses round about the Parc, where the English were congregated, and there mingled with other valets, couriers, and lackeys, gathered such news as was abroad, and brought back bulletins for his master's information. Almost all these gentlemen were in heart partisans of the Emperor, and had their opinions about the speedy end of the campaign. The Emperor's proclamation from Avesnes had been distributed everywhere plentifully in Brussels. \"Soldiers!\" it said, \"this is the anniversary of Marengo and Friedland, by which the destinies of Europe were twice decided. Then, as after Austerlitz, as after Wagram, we were too generous. We believed in the oaths and promises of princes whom we suffered to remain upon their thrones. Let us march once more to meet them. We and they, are we not still the same men? Soldiers! these same Prussians who are so arrogant to-day, were three to one against you at Jena, and six to one at Montmirail. Those among you who were prisoners in England can tell their comrades what frightful torments they suffered on board the English hulks. Madmen! a moment of prosperity has blinded them, and if they enter into France it will be to find a grave there!\" But the partisans of the French prophesied a more speedy extermination of the Emperor's enemies than this; and it was agreed on all hands that Prussians and British would never return except as prisoners in the rear of the conquering army.\n\nThese opinions in the course of the day were brought to operate upon Mr. Sedley. He was told that the Duke of Wellington had gone to try and rally his army, the advance of which had been utterly crushed the night before.\n\n\"Crushed, psha!\" said Jos, whose heart was pretty stout at breakfast-time. \"The Duke has gone to beat the Emperor as he has beaten all his generals before.\"\n\n\"His papers are burned, his effects are removed, and his quarters are being got ready for the Duke of Dalmatia,\" Jos's informant replied. \"I had it from his own maitre d'hotel. Milor Duc de Richemont's people are packing up everything. His Grace has fled already, and the Duchess is only waiting to see the plate packed to join the King of France at Ostend.\"\n\n\"The King of France is at Ghent, fellow,\" replied Jos, affecting incredulity.\n\n\"He fled last night to Bruges, and embarks today from Ostend. The Duc de Berri is taken prisoner. Those who wish to be safe had better go soon, for the dykes will be opened to-morrow, and who can fly when the whole country is under water?\"\n\n\"Nonsense, sir, we are three to one, sir, against any force Boney can bring into the field,\" Mr. Sedley objected; \"the Austrians and the Russians are on their march. He must, he shall be crushed,\" Jos said, slapping his hand on the table.\n\n\"The Prussians were three to one at Jena, and he took their army and kingdom in a week. They were six to one at Montmirail, and he scattered them like sheep. The Austrian army is coming, but with the Empress and the King of Rome at its head; and the Russians, bah! the Russians will withdraw. No quarter is to be given to the English, on account of their cruelty to our braves on board the infamous pontoons. Look here, here it is in black and white. Here's the proclamation of his Majesty the Emperor and King,\" said the now declared partisan of Napoleon, and taking the document from his pocket, Isidor sternly thrust it into his master's face, and already looked upon the frogged coat and valuables as his own spoil.\n\nJos was, if not seriously alarmed as yet, at least considerably disturbed in mind. \"Give me my coat and cap, sir,\" said he, \"and follow me. I will go myself and learn the truth of these reports.\" Isidor was furious as Jos put on the braided frock. \"Milor had better not wear that military coat,\" said he; \"the Frenchmen have sworn not to give quarter to a single British soldier.\"\n\n\"Silence, sirrah!\" said Jos, with a resolute countenance still, and thrust his arm into the sleeve with indomitable resolution, in the performance of which heroic act he was found by Mrs. Rawdon Crawley, who at this juncture came up to visit Amelia, and entered without ringing at the antechamber door.\n\nRebecca was dressed very neatly and smartly, as usual: her quiet sleep after Rawdon's departure had refreshed her, and her pink smiling cheeks were quite pleasant to look at, in a town and on a day when everybody else's countenance wore the appearance of the deepest anxiety and gloom. She laughed at the attitude in which Jos was discovered, and the struggles and convulsions with which the stout gentleman thrust himself into the braided coat.\n\n\"Are you preparing to join the army, Mr. Joseph?\" she said. \"Is there to be nobody left in Brussels to protect us poor women?\" Jos succeeded in plunging into the coat, and came forward blushing and stuttering out excuses to his fair visitor. \"How was she after the events of the morning--after the fatigues of the ball the night before?\" Monsieur Isidor disappeared into his master's adjacent bedroom, bearing off the flowered dressing-gown.\n\n\"How good of you to ask,\" said she, pressing one of his hands in both her own. \"How cool and collected you look when everybody else is frightened! How is our dear little Emmy? It must have been an awful, awful parting.\"\n\n\"Tremendous,\" Jos said.\n\n\"You men can bear anything,\" replied the lady. \"Parting or danger are nothing to you. Own now that you were going to join the army and leave us to our fate. I know you were--something tells me you were. I was so frightened, when the thought came into my head (for I do sometimes think of you when I am alone, Mr. Joseph), that I ran off immediately to beg and entreat you not to fly from us.\"\n\nThis speech might be interpreted, \"My dear sir, should an accident befall the army, and a retreat be necessary, you have a very comfortable carriage, in which I propose to take a seat.\" I don't know whether Jos understood the words in this sense. But he was profoundly mortified by the lady's inattention to him during their stay at Brussels. He had never been presented to any of Rawdon Crawley's great acquaintances: he had scarcely been invited to Rebecca's parties; for he was too timid to play much, and his presence bored George and Rawdon equally, who neither of them, perhaps, liked to have a witness of the amusements in which the pair chose to indulge. \"Ah!\" thought Jos, \"now she wants me she comes to me. When there is nobody else in the way she can think about old Joseph Sedley!\" But besides these doubts he felt flattered at the idea Rebecca expressed of his courage.\n\nHe blushed a good deal, and put on an air of importance. \"I should like to see the action,\" he said. \"Every man of any spirit would, you know. I've seen a little service in India, but nothing on this grand scale.\"\n\n\"You men would sacrifice anything for a pleasure,\" Rebecca answered. \"Captain Crawley left me this morning as gay as if he were going to a hunting party. What does he care? What do any of you care for the agonies and tortures of a poor forsaken woman? (I wonder whether he could really have been going to the troops, this great lazy gourmand?) Oh! dear Mr. Sedley, I have come to you for comfort--for consolation. I have been on my knees all the morning. I tremble at the frightful danger into which our husbands, our friends, our brave troops and allies, are rushing. And I come here for shelter, and find another of my friends--the last remaining to me--bent upon plunging into the dreadful scene!\"\n\n\"My dear madam,\" Jos replied, now beginning to be quite soothed, \"don't be alarmed. I only said I should like to go--what Briton would not? But my duty keeps me here: I can't leave that poor creature in the next room.\" And he pointed with his finger to the door of the chamber in which Amelia was.\n\n\"Good noble brother!\" Rebecca said, putting her handkerchief to her eyes, and smelling the eau-de-cologne with which it was scented. \"I have done you injustice: you have got a heart. I thought you had not.\"\n\n\"O, upon my honour!\" Jos said, making a motion as if he would lay his hand upon the spot in question. \"You do me injustice, indeed you do--my dear Mrs. Crawley.\"\n\n\"I do, now your heart is true to your sister. But I remember two years ago--when it was false to me!\" Rebecca said, fixing her eyes upon him for an instant, and then turning away into the window.\n\nJos blushed violently. That organ which he was accused by Rebecca of not possessing began to thump tumultuously. He recalled the days when he had fled from her, and the passion which had once inflamed him--the days when he had driven her in his curricle: when she had knit the green purse for him: when he had sate enraptured gazing at her white arms and bright eyes.\n\n\"I know you think me ungrateful,\" Rebecca continued, coming out of the window, and once more looking at him and addressing him in a low tremulous voice. \"Your coldness, your averted looks, your manner when we have met of late--when I came in just now, all proved it to me. But were there no reasons why I should avoid you? Let your own heart answer that question. Do you think my husband was too much inclined to welcome you? The only unkind words I have ever had from him (I will do Captain Crawley that justice) have been about you--and most cruel, cruel words they were.\"\n\n\"Good gracious! what have I done?\" asked Jos in a flurry of pleasure and perplexity; \"what have I done--to--to--?\"\n\n\"Is jealousy nothing?\" said Rebecca. \"He makes me miserable about you. And whatever it might have been once--my heart is all his. I am innocent now. Am I not, Mr. Sedley?\"\n\nAll Jos's blood tingled with delight, as he surveyed this victim to his attractions. A few adroit words, one or two knowing tender glances of the eyes, and his heart was inflamed again and his doubts and suspicions forgotten. From Solomon downwards, have not wiser men than he been cajoled and befooled by women? \"If the worst comes to the worst,\" Becky thought, \"my retreat is secure; and I have a right-hand seat in the barouche.\"\n\nThere is no knowing into what declarations of love and ardour the tumultuous passions of Mr. Joseph might have led him, if Isidor the valet had not made his reappearance at this minute, and begun to busy himself about the domestic affairs. Jos, who was just going to gasp out an avowal, choked almost with the emotion that he was obliged to restrain. Rebecca too bethought her that it was time she should go in and comfort her dearest Amelia. \"Au revoir,\" she said, kissing her hand to Mr. Joseph, and tapped gently at the door of his sister's apartment. As she entered and closed the door on herself, he sank down in a chair, and gazed and sighed and puffed portentously. \"That coat is very tight for Milor,\" Isidor said, still having his eye on the frogs; but his master heard him not: his thoughts were elsewhere: now glowing, maddening, upon the contemplation of the enchanting Rebecca: anon shrinking guiltily before the vision of the jealous Rawdon Crawley, with his curling, fierce mustachios, and his terrible duelling pistols loaded and cocked.\n\nRebecca's appearance struck Amelia with terror, and made her shrink back. It recalled her to the world and the remembrance of yesterday. In the overpowering fears about to-morrow she had forgotten Rebecca--jealousy--everything except that her husband was gone and was in danger. Until this dauntless worldling came in and broke the spell, and lifted the latch, we too have forborne to enter into that sad chamber. How long had that poor girl been on her knees! what hours of speechless prayer and bitter prostration had she passed there! The war-chroniclers who write brilliant stories of fight and triumph scarcely tell us of these. These are too mean parts of the pageant: and you don't hear widows' cries or mothers' sobs in the midst of the shouts and jubilation in the great Chorus of Victory. And yet when was the time that such have not cried out: heart-broken, humble protestants, unheard in the uproar of the triumph!\n\nAfter the first movement of terror in Amelia's mind--when Rebecca's green eyes lighted upon her, and rustling in her fresh silks and brilliant ornaments, the latter tripped up with extended arms to embrace her--a feeling of anger succeeded, and from being deadly pale before, her face flushed up red, and she returned Rebecca's look after a moment with a steadiness which surprised and somewhat abashed her rival.\n\n\"Dearest Amelia, you are very unwell,\" the visitor said, putting forth her hand to take Amelia's. \"What is it? I could not rest until I knew how you were.\"\n\nAmelia drew back her hand--never since her life began had that gentle soul refused to believe or to answer any demonstration of good-will or affection. But she drew back her hand, and trembled all over. \"Why are you here, Rebecca?\" she said, still looking at her solemnly with her large eyes. These glances troubled her visitor.\n\n\"She must have seen him give me the letter at the ball,\" Rebecca thought. \"Don't be agitated, dear Amelia,\" she said, looking down. \"I came but to see if I could--if you were well.\"\n\n\"Are you well?\" said Amelia. \"I dare say you are. You don't love your husband. You would not be here if you did. Tell me, Rebecca, did I ever do you anything but kindness?\"\n\n\"Indeed, Amelia, no,\" the other said, still hanging down her head.\n\n\"When you were quite poor, who was it that befriended you? Was I not a sister to you? You saw us all in happier days before he married me. I was all in all then to him; or would he have given up his fortune, his family, as he nobly did to make me happy? Why did you come between my love and me? Who sent you to separate those whom God joined, and take my darling's heart from me--my own husband? Do you think you could love him as I did? His love was everything to me. You knew it, and wanted to rob me of it. For shame, Rebecca; bad and wicked woman--false friend and false wife.\"\n\n\"Amelia, I protest before God, I have done my husband no wrong,\" Rebecca said, turning from her.\n\n\"Have you done me no wrong, Rebecca? You did not succeed, but you tried. Ask your heart if you did not.\"\n\nShe knows nothing, Rebecca thought.\n\n\"He came back to me. I knew he would. I knew that no falsehood, no flattery, could keep him from me long. I knew he would come. I prayed so that he should.\"\n\nThe poor girl spoke these words with a spirit and volubility which Rebecca had never before seen in her, and before which the latter was quite dumb. \"But what have I done to you,\" she continued in a more pitiful tone, \"that you should try and take him from me? I had him but for six weeks. You might have spared me those, Rebecca. And yet, from the very first day of our wedding, you came and blighted it. Now he is gone, are you come to see how unhappy I am?\" she continued. \"You made me wretched enough for the past fortnight: you might have spared me to-day.\"\n\n\"I--I never came here,\" interposed Rebecca, with unlucky truth.\n\n\"No. You didn't come. You took him away. Are you come to fetch him from me?\" she continued in a wilder tone. \"He was here, but he is gone now. There on that very sofa he sate. Don't touch it. We sate and talked there. I was on his knee, and my arms were round his neck, and we said 'Our Father.' Yes, he was here: and they came and took him away, but he promised me to come back.\"\n\n\"He will come back, my dear,\" said Rebecca, touched in spite of herself.\n\n\"Look,\" said Amelia, \"this is his sash--isn't it a pretty colour?\" and she took up the fringe and kissed it. She had tied it round her waist at some part of the day. She had forgotten her anger, her jealousy, the very presence of her rival seemingly. For she walked silently and almost with a smile on her face, towards the bed, and began to smooth down George's pillow.\n\nRebecca walked, too, silently away. \"How is Amelia?\" asked Jos, who still held his position in the chair.\n\n\"There should be somebody with her,\" said Rebecca. \"I think she is very unwell\": and she went away with a very grave face, refusing Mr. Sedley's entreaties that she would stay and partake of the early dinner which he had ordered.\n\nRebecca was of a good-natured and obliging disposition; and she liked Amelia rather than otherwise. Even her hard words, reproachful as they were, were complimentary--the groans of a person stinging under defeat. Meeting Mrs. O'Dowd, whom the Dean's sermons had by no means comforted, and who was walking very disconsolately in the Parc, Rebecca accosted the latter, rather to the surprise of the Major's wife, who was not accustomed to such marks of politeness from Mrs. Rawdon Crawley, and informing her that poor little Mrs. Osborne was in a desperate condition, and almost mad with grief, sent off the good-natured Irishwoman straight to see if she could console her young favourite.\n\n\"I've cares of my own enough,\" Mrs. O'Dowd said, gravely, \"and I thought poor Amelia would be little wanting for company this day. But if she's so bad as you say, and you can't attend to her, who used to be so fond of her, faith I'll see if I can be of service. And so good marning to ye, Madam\"; with which speech and a toss of her head, the lady of the repayther took a farewell of Mrs. Crawley, whose company she by no means courted.\n\nBecky watched her marching off, with a smile on her lip. She had the keenest sense of humour, and the Parthian look which the retreating Mrs. O'Dowd flung over her shoulder almost upset Mrs. Crawley's gravity. \"My service to ye, me fine Madam, and I'm glad to see ye so cheerful,\" thought Peggy. \"It's not YOU that will cry your eyes out with grief, anyway.\" And with this she passed on, and speedily found her way to Mrs. Osborne's lodgings.\n\nThe poor soul was still at the bedside, where Rebecca had left her, and stood almost crazy with grief. The Major's wife, a stronger-minded woman, endeavoured her best to comfort her young friend. \"You must bear up, Amelia, dear,\" she said kindly, \"for he mustn't find you ill when he sends for you after the victory. It's not you are the only woman that are in the hands of God this day.\"\n\n\"I know that. I am very wicked, very weak,\" Amelia said. She knew her own weakness well enough. The presence of the more resolute friend checked it, however; and she was the better of this control and company. They went on till two o'clock; their hearts were with the column as it marched farther and farther away. Dreadful doubt and anguish--prayers and fears and griefs unspeakable--followed the regiment. It was the women's tribute to the war. It taxes both alike, and takes the blood of the men, and the tears of the women.\n\nAt half-past two, an event occurred of daily importance to Mr. Joseph: the dinner-hour arrived. Warriors may fight and perish, but he must dine. He came into Amelia's room to see if he could coax her to share that meal. \"Try,\" said he; \"the soup is very good. Do try, Emmy,\" and he kissed her hand. Except when she was married, he had not done so much for years before. \"You are very good and kind, Joseph,\" she said. \"Everybody is, but, if you please, I will stay in my room to-day.\"\n\nThe savour of the soup, however, was agreeable to Mrs. O'Dowd's nostrils: and she thought she would bear Mr. Jos company. So the two sate down to their meal. \"God bless the meat,\" said the Major's wife, solemnly: she was thinking of her honest Mick, riding at the head of his regiment: \"'Tis but a bad dinner those poor boys will get to-day,\" she said, with a sigh, and then, like a philosopher, fell to.\n\nJos's spirits rose with his meal. He would drink the regiment's health; or, indeed, take any other excuse to indulge in a glass of champagne. \"We'll drink to O'Dowd and the brave --th,\" said he, bowing gallantly to his guest. \"Hey, Mrs. O'Dowd? Fill Mrs. O'Dowd's glass, Isidor.\"\n\nBut all of a sudden, Isidor started, and the Major's wife laid down her knife and fork. The windows of the room were open, and looked southward, and a dull distant sound came over the sun-lighted roofs from that direction. \"What is it?\" said Jos. \"Why don't you pour, you rascal?\"\n\n\"Cest le feu!\" said Isidor, running to the balcony.\n\n\"God defend us; it's cannon!\" Mrs. O'Dowd cried, starting up, and followed too to the window. A thousand pale and anxious faces might have been seen looking from other casements. And presently it seemed as if the whole population of the city rushed into the streets.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXII\n\nIn Which Jos Takes Flight, and the War Is Brought to a Close\nWe of peaceful London City have never beheld--and please God never shall witness--such a scene of hurry and alarm, as that which Brussels presented. Crowds rushed to the Namur gate, from which direction the noise proceeded, and many rode along the level chaussee, to be in advance of any intelligence from the army. Each man asked his neighbour for news; and even great English lords and ladies condescended to speak to persons whom they did not know. The friends of the French went abroad, wild with excitement, and prophesying the triumph of their Emperor. The merchants closed their shops, and came out to swell the general chorus of alarm and clamour. Women rushed to the churches, and crowded the chapels, and knelt and prayed on the flags and steps. The dull sound of the cannon went on rolling, rolling. Presently carriages with travellers began to leave the town, galloping away by the Ghent barrier. The prophecies of the French partisans began to pass for facts. \"He has cut the armies in two,\" it was said. \"He is marching straight on Brussels. He will overpower the English, and be here to-night.\" \"He will overpower the English,\" shrieked Isidor to his master, \"and will be here to-night.\" The man bounded in and out from the lodgings to the street, always returning with some fresh particulars of disaster. Jos's face grew paler and paler. Alarm began to take entire possession of the stout civilian. All the champagne he drank brought no courage to him. Before sunset he was worked up to such a pitch of nervousness as gratified his friend Isidor to behold, who now counted surely upon the spoils of the owner of the laced coat.\n\nThe women were away all this time. After hearing the firing for a moment, the stout Major's wife bethought her of her friend in the next chamber, and ran in to watch, and if possible to console, Amelia. The idea that she had that helpless and gentle creature to protect, gave additional strength to the natural courage of the honest Irishwoman. She passed five hours by her friend's side, sometimes in remonstrance, sometimes talking cheerfully, oftener in silence and terrified mental supplication. \"I never let go her hand once,\" said the stout lady afterwards, \"until after sunset, when the firing was over.\" Pauline, the bonne, was on her knees at church hard by, praying for son homme a elle.\n\nWhen the noise of the cannonading was over, Mrs. O'Dowd issued out of Amelia's room into the parlour adjoining, where Jos sate with two emptied flasks, and courage entirely gone. Once or twice he had ventured into his sister's bedroom, looking very much alarmed, and as if he would say something. But the Major's wife kept her place, and he went away without disburthening himself of his speech. He was ashamed to tell her that he wanted to fly.\n\nBut when she made her appearance in the dining-room, where he sate in the twilight in the cheerless company of his empty champagne bottles, he began to open his mind to her.\n\n\"Mrs. O'Dowd,\" he said, \"hadn't you better get Amelia ready?\"\n\n\"Are you going to take her out for a walk?\" said the Major's lady; \"sure she's too weak to stir.\"\n\n\"I--I've ordered the carriage,\" he said, \"and--and post-horses; Isidor is gone for them,\" Jos continued.\n\n\"What do you want with driving to-night?\" answered the lady. \"Isn't she better on her bed? I've just got her to lie down.\"\n\n\"Get her up,\" said Jos; \"she must get up, I say\": and he stamped his foot energetically. \"I say the horses are ordered--yes, the horses are ordered. It's all over, and--\"\n\n\"And what?\" asked Mrs. O'Dowd.\n\n\"I'm off for Ghent,\" Jos answered. \"Everybody is going; there's a place for you! We shall start in half-an-hour.\"\n\nThe Major's wife looked at him with infinite scorn. \"I don't move till O'Dowd gives me the route,\" said she. \"You may go if you like, Mr. Sedley; but, faith, Amelia and I stop here.\"\n\n\"She SHALL go,\" said Jos, with another stamp of his foot. Mrs. O'Dowd put herself with arms akimbo before the bedroom door.\n\n\"Is it her mother you're going to take her to?\" she said; \"or do you want to go to Mamma yourself, Mr. Sedley? Good marning--a pleasant journey to ye, sir. Bon voyage, as they say, and take my counsel, and shave off them mustachios, or they'll bring you into mischief.\"\n\n\"D--n!\" yelled out Jos, wild with fear, rage, and mortification; and Isidor came in at this juncture, swearing in his turn. \"Pas de chevaux, sacre bleu!\" hissed out the furious domestic. All the horses were gone. Jos was not the only man in Brussels seized with panic that day.\n\nBut Jos's fears, great and cruel as they were already, were destined to increase to an almost frantic pitch before the night was over. It has been mentioned how Pauline, the bonne, had son homme a elle also in the ranks of the army that had gone out to meet the Emperor Napoleon. This lover was a native of Brussels, and a Belgian hussar. The troops of his nation signalised themselves in this war for anything but courage, and young Van Cutsum, Pauline's admirer, was too good a soldier to disobey his Colonel's orders to run away. Whilst in garrison at Brussels young Regulus (he had been born in the revolutionary times) found his great comfort, and passed almost all his leisure moments, in Pauline's kitchen; and it was with pockets and holsters crammed full of good things from her larder, that he had take leave of his weeping sweetheart, to proceed upon the campaign a few days before.\n\nAs far as his regiment was concerned, this campaign was over now. They had formed a part of the division under the command of his Sovereign apparent, the Prince of Orange, and as respected length of swords and mustachios, and the richness of uniform and equipments, Regulus and his comrades looked to be as gallant a body of men as ever trumpet sounded for.\n\nWhen Ney dashed upon the advance of the allied troops, carrying one position after the other, until the arrival of the great body of the British army from Brussels changed the aspect of the combat of Quatre Bras, the squadrons among which Regulus rode showed the greatest activity in retreating before the French, and were dislodged from one post and another which they occupied with perfect alacrity on their part. Their movements were only checked by the advance of the British in their rear. Thus forced to halt, the enemy's cavalry (whose bloodthirsty obstinacy cannot be too severely reprehended) had at length an opportunity of coming to close quarters with the brave Belgians before them; who preferred to encounter the British rather than the French, and at once turning tail rode through the English regiments that were behind them, and scattered in all directions. The regiment in fact did not exist any more. It was nowhere. It had no head-quarters. Regulus found himself galloping many miles from the field of action, entirely alone; and whither should he fly for refuge so naturally as to that kitchen and those faithful arms in which Pauline had so often welcomed him?\n\nAt some ten o'clock the clinking of a sabre might have been heard up the stair of the house where the Osbornes occupied a story in the continental fashion. A knock might have been heard at the kitchen door; and poor Pauline, come back from church, fainted almost with terror as she opened it and saw before her her haggard hussar. He looked as pale as the midnight dragoon who came to disturb Leonora. Pauline would have screamed, but that her cry would have called her masters, and discovered her friend. She stifled her scream, then, and leading her hero into the kitchen, gave him beer, and the choice bits from the dinner, which Jos had not had the heart to taste. The hussar showed he was no ghost by the prodigious quantity of flesh and beer which he devoured--and during the mouthfuls he told his tale of disaster.\n\nHis regiment had performed prodigies of courage, and had withstood for a while the onset of the whole French army. But they were overwhelmed at last, as was the whole British army by this time. Ney destroyed each regiment as it came up. The Belgians in vain interposed to prevent the butchery of the English. The Brunswickers were routed and had fled--their Duke was killed. It was a general debacle. He sought to drown his sorrow for the defeat in floods of beer.\n\nIsidor, who had come into the kitchen, heard the conversation and rushed out to inform his master. \"It is all over,\" he shrieked to Jos. \"Milor Duke is a prisoner; the Duke of Brunswick is killed; the British army is in full flight; there is only one man escaped, and he is in the kitchen now--come and hear him.\" So Jos tottered into that apartment where Regulus still sate on the kitchen table, and clung fast to his flagon of beer. In the best French which he could muster, and which was in sooth of a very ungrammatical sort, Jos besought the hussar to tell his tale. The disasters deepened as Regulus spoke. He was the only man of his regiment not slain on the field. He had seen the Duke of Brunswick fall, the black hussars fly, the Ecossais pounded down by the cannon. \"And the --th?\" gasped Jos.\n\n\"Cut in pieces,\" said the hussar--upon which Pauline cried out, \"O my mistress, ma bonne petite dame,\" went off fairly into hysterics, and filled the house with her screams.\n\nWild with terror, Mr. Sedley knew not how or where to seek for safety. He rushed from the kitchen back to the sitting-room, and cast an appealing look at Amelia's door, which Mrs. O'Dowd had closed and locked in his face; but he remembered how scornfully the latter had received him, and after pausing and listening for a brief space at the door, he left it, and resolved to go into the street, for the first time that day. So, seizing a candle, he looked about for his gold-laced cap, and found it lying in its usual place, on a console-table, in the anteroom, placed before a mirror at which Jos used to coquet, always giving his side-locks a twirl, and his cap the proper cock over his eye, before he went forth to make appearance in public. Such is the force of habit, that even in the midst of his terror he began mechanically to twiddle with his hair, and arrange the cock of his hat. Then he looked amazed at the pale face in the glass before him, and especially at his mustachios, which had attained a rich growth in the course of near seven weeks, since they had come into the world. They WILL mistake me for a military man, thought he, remembering Isidor's warning as to the massacre with which all the defeated British army was threatened; and staggering back to his bedchamber, he began wildly pulling the bell which summoned his valet.\n\nIsidor answered that summons. Jos had sunk in a chair--he had torn off his neckcloths, and turned down his collars, and was sitting with both his hands lifted to his throat.\n\n\"Coupez-moi, Isidor,\" shouted he; \"vite! Coupez-moi!\"\n\nIsidor thought for a moment he had gone mad, and that he wished his valet to cut his throat.\n\n\"Les moustaches,\" gasped Joe; \"les moustaches--coupy, rasy, vite!\"--his French was of this sort--voluble, as we have said, but not remarkable for grammar.\n\nIsidor swept off the mustachios in no time with the razor, and heard with inexpressible delight his master's orders that he should fetch a hat and a plain coat. \"Ne porty ploo--habit militair--bonn--bonny a voo, prenny dehors\"--were Jos's words--the coat and cap were at last his property.\n\nThis gift being made, Jos selected a plain black coat and waistcoat from his stock, and put on a large white neckcloth, and a plain beaver. If he could have got a shovel hat he would have worn it. As it was, you would have fancied he was a flourishing, large parson of the Church of England.\n\n\"Venny maintenong,\" he continued, \"sweevy--ally--party--dong la roo.\" And so having said, he plunged swiftly down the stairs of the house, and passed into the street.\n\nAlthough Regulus had vowed that he was the only man of his regiment or of the allied army, almost, who had escaped being cut to pieces by Ney, it appeared that his statement was incorrect, and that a good number more of the supposed victims had survived the massacre. Many scores of Regulus's comrades had found their way back to Brussels, and all agreeing that they had run away--filled the whole town with an idea of the defeat of the allies. The arrival of the French was expected hourly; the panic continued, and preparations for flight went on everywhere. No horses! thought Jos, in terror. He made Isidor inquire of scores of persons, whether they had any to lend or sell, and his heart sank within him, at the negative answers returned everywhere. Should he take the journey on foot? Even fear could not render that ponderous body so active.\n\nAlmost all the hotels occupied by the English in Brussels face the Parc, and Jos wandered irresolutely about in this quarter, with crowds of other people, oppressed as he was by fear and curiosity. Some families he saw more happy than himself, having discovered a team of horses, and rattling through the streets in retreat; others again there were whose case was like his own, and who could not for any bribes or entreaties procure the necessary means of flight. Amongst these would-be fugitives, Jos remarked the Lady Bareacres and her daughter, who sate in their carriage in the porte-cochere of their hotel, all their imperials packed, and the only drawback to whose flight was the same want of motive power which kept Jos stationary.\n\nRebecca Crawley occupied apartments in this hotel; and had before this period had sundry hostile meetings with the ladies of the Bareacres family. My Lady Bareacres cut Mrs. Crawley on the stairs when they met by chance; and in all places where the latter's name was mentioned, spoke perseveringly ill of her neighbour. The Countess was shocked at the familiarity of General Tufto with the aide-de-camp's wife. The Lady Blanche avoided her as if she had been an infectious disease. Only the Earl himself kept up a sly occasional acquaintance with her, when out of the jurisdiction of his ladies.\n\nRebecca had her revenge now upon these insolent enemies. It became known in the hotel that Captain Crawley's horses had been left behind, and when the panic began, Lady Bareacres condescended to send her maid to the Captain's wife with her Ladyship's compliments, and a desire to know the price of Mrs. Crawley's horses. Mrs. Crawley returned a note with her compliments, and an intimation that it was not her custom to transact bargains with ladies' maids.\n\nThis curt reply brought the Earl in person to Becky's apartment; but he could get no more success than the first ambassador. \"Send a lady's maid to ME!\" Mrs. Crawley cried in great anger; \"why didn't my Lady Bareacres tell me to go and saddle the horses! Is it her Ladyship that wants to escape, or her Ladyship's femme de chambre?\" And this was all the answer that the Earl bore back to his Countess.\n\nWhat will not necessity do? The Countess herself actually came to wait upon Mrs. Crawley on the failure of her second envoy. She entreated her to name her own price; she even offered to invite Becky to Bareacres House, if the latter would but give her the means of returning to that residence. Mrs. Crawley sneered at her.\n\n\"I don't want to be waited on by bailiffs in livery,\" she said; \"you will never get back though most probably--at least not you and your diamonds together. The French will have those. They will be here in two hours, and I shall be half way to Ghent by that time. I would not sell you my horses, no, not for the two largest diamonds that your Ladyship wore at the ball.\" Lady Bareacres trembled with rage and terror. The diamonds were sewed into her habit, and secreted in my Lord's padding and boots. \"Woman, the diamonds are at the banker's, and I WILL have the horses,\" she said. Rebecca laughed in her face. The infuriate Countess went below, and sate in her carriage; her maid, her courier, and her husband were sent once more through the town, each to look for cattle; and woe betide those who came last! Her Ladyship was resolved on departing the very instant the horses arrived from any quarter--with her husband or without him.\n\nRebecca had the pleasure of seeing her Ladyship in the horseless carriage, and keeping her eyes fixed upon her, and bewailing, in the loudest tone of voice, the Countess's perplexities. \"Not to be able to get horses!\" she said, \"and to have all those diamonds sewed into the carriage cushions! What a prize it will be for the French when they come!--the carriage and the diamonds, I mean; not the lady!\" She gave this information to the landlord, to the servants, to the guests, and the innumerable stragglers about the courtyard. Lady Bareacres could have shot her from the carriage window.\n\nIt was while enjoying the humiliation of her enemy that Rebecca caught sight of Jos, who made towards her directly he perceived her.\n\nThat altered, frightened, fat face, told his secret well enough. He too wanted to fly, and was on the look-out for the means of escape. \"HE shall buy my horses,\" thought Rebecca, \"and I'll ride the mare.\"\n\nJos walked up to his friend, and put the question for the hundredth time during the past hour, \"Did she know where horses were to be had?\"\n\n\"What, YOU fly?\" said Rebecca, with a laugh. \"I thought you were the champion of all the ladies, Mr. Sedley.\"\n\n\"I--I'm not a military man,\" gasped he.\n\n\"And Amelia?--Who is to protect that poor little sister of yours?\" asked Rebecca. \"You surely would not desert her?\"\n\n\"What good can I do her, suppose--suppose the enemy arrive?\" Jos answered. \"They'll spare the women; but my man tells me that they have taken an oath to give no quarter to the men--the dastardly cowards.\"\n\n\"Horrid!\" cried Rebecca, enjoying his perplexity.\n\n\"Besides, I don't want to desert her,\" cried the brother. \"She SHAN'T be deserted. There is a seat for her in my carriage, and one for you, dear Mrs. Crawley, if you will come; and if we can get horses--\" sighed he--\n\n\"I have two to sell,\" the lady said. Jos could have flung himself into her arms at the news. \"Get the carriage, Isidor,\" he cried; \"we've found them--we have found them.\"\n\n\"My horses never were in harness,\" added the lady. \"Bullfinch would kick the carriage to pieces, if you put him in the traces.\"\n\n\"But he is quiet to ride?\" asked the civilian.\n\n\"As quiet as a lamb, and as fast as a hare,\" answered Rebecca.\n\n\"Do you think he is up to my weight?\" Jos said. He was already on his back, in imagination, without ever so much as a thought for poor Amelia. What person who loved a horse-speculation could resist such a temptation?\n\nIn reply, Rebecca asked him to come into her room, whither he followed her quite breathless to conclude the bargain. Jos seldom spent a half-hour in his life which cost him so much money. Rebecca, measuring the value of the goods which she had for sale by Jos's eagerness to purchase, as well as by the scarcity of the article, put upon her horses a price so prodigious as to make even the civilian draw back. \"She would sell both or neither,\" she said, resolutely. Rawdon had ordered her not to part with them for a price less than that which she specified. Lord Bareacres below would give her the same money--and with all her love and regard for the Sedley family, her dear Mr. Joseph must conceive that poor people must live--nobody, in a word, could be more affectionate, but more firm about the matter of business.\n\nJos ended by agreeing, as might be supposed of him. The sum he had to give her was so large that he was obliged to ask for time; so large as to be a little fortune to Rebecca, who rapidly calculated that with this sum, and the sale of the residue of Rawdon's effects, and her pension as a widow should he fall, she would now be absolutely independent of the world, and might look her weeds steadily in the face.\n\nOnce or twice in the day she certainly had herself thought about flying. But her reason gave her better counsel. \"Suppose the French do come,\" thought Becky, \"what can they do to a poor officer's widow? Bah! the times of sacks and sieges are over. We shall be let to go home quietly, or I may live pleasantly abroad with a snug little income.\"\n\nMeanwhile Jos and Isidor went off to the stables to inspect the newly purchased cattle. Jos bade his man saddle the horses at once. He would ride away that very night, that very hour. And he left the valet busy in getting the horses ready, and went homewards himself to prepare for his departure. It must be secret. He would go to his chamber by the back entrance. He did not care to face Mrs. O'Dowd and Amelia, and own to them that he was about to run.\n\nBy the time Jos's bargain with Rebecca was completed, and his horses had been visited and examined, it was almost morning once more. But though midnight was long passed, there was no rest for the city; the people were up, the lights in the houses flamed, crowds were still about the doors, and the streets were busy. Rumours of various natures went still from mouth to mouth: one report averred that the Prussians had been utterly defeated; another that it was the English who had been attacked and conquered: a third that the latter had held their ground. This last rumour gradually got strength. No Frenchmen had made their appearance. Stragglers had come in from the army bringing reports more and more favourable: at last an aide-de-camp actually reached Brussels with despatches for the Commandant of the place, who placarded presently through the town an official announcement of the success of the allies at Quatre Bras, and the entire repulse of the French under Ney after a six hours' battle. The aide-de-camp must have arrived sometime while Jos and Rebecca were making their bargain together, or the latter was inspecting his purchase. When he reached his own hotel, he found a score of its numerous inhabitants on the threshold discoursing of the news; there was no doubt as to its truth. And he went up to communicate it to the ladies under his charge. He did not think it was necessary to tell them how he had intended to take leave of them, how he had bought horses, and what a price he had paid for them.\n\nBut success or defeat was a minor matter to them, who had only thought for the safety of those they loved. Amelia, at the news of the victory, became still more agitated even than before. She was for going that moment to the army. She besought her brother with tears to conduct her thither. Her doubts and terrors reached their paroxysm; and the poor girl, who for many hours had been plunged into stupor, raved and ran hither and thither in hysteric insanity--a piteous sight. No man writhing in pain on the hard-fought field fifteen miles off, where lay, after their struggles, so many of the brave--no man suffered more keenly than this poor harmless victim of the war. Jos could not bear the sight of her pain. He left his sister in the charge of her stouter female companion, and descended once more to the threshold of the hotel, where everybody still lingered, and talked, and waited for more news.\n\nIt grew to be broad daylight as they stood here, and fresh news began to arrive from the war, brought by men who had been actors in the scene. Wagons and long country carts laden with wounded came rolling into the town; ghastly groans came from within them, and haggard faces looked up sadly from out of the straw. Jos Sedley was looking at one of these carriages with a painful curiosity--the moans of the people within were frightful--the wearied horses could hardly pull the cart. \"Stop! stop!\" a feeble voice cried from the straw, and the carriage stopped opposite Mr. Sedley's hotel.\n\n\"It is George, I know it is!\" cried Amelia, rushing in a moment to the balcony, with a pallid face and loose flowing hair. It was not George, however, but it was the next best thing: it was news of him.\n\nIt was poor Tom Stubble, who had marched out of Brussels so gallantly twenty-four hours before, bearing the colours of the regiment, which he had defended very gallantly upon the field. A French lancer had speared the young ensign in the leg, who fell, still bravely holding to his flag. At the conclusion of the engagement, a place had been found for the poor boy in a cart, and he had been brought back to Brussels.\n\n\"Mr. Sedley, Mr. Sedley!\" cried the boy, faintly, and Jos came up almost frightened at the appeal. He had not at first distinguished who it was that called him.\n\nLittle Tom Stubble held out his hot and feeble hand. \"I'm to be taken in here,\" he said. \"Osborne--and--and Dobbin said I was; and you are to give the man two napoleons: my mother will pay you.\" This young fellow's thoughts, during the long feverish hours passed in the cart, had been wandering to his father's parsonage which he had quitted only a few months before, and he had sometimes forgotten his pain in that delirium.\n\nThe hotel was large, and the people kind, and all the inmates of the cart were taken in and placed on various couches. The young ensign was conveyed upstairs to Osborne's quarters. Amelia and the Major's wife had rushed down to him, when the latter had recognised him from the balcony. You may fancy the feelings of these women when they were told that the day was over, and both their husbands were safe; in what mute rapture Amelia fell on her good friend's neck, and embraced her; in what a grateful passion of prayer she fell on her knees, and thanked the Power which had saved her husband.\n\nOur young lady, in her fevered and nervous condition, could have had no more salutary medicine prescribed for her by any physician than that which chance put in her way. She and Mrs. O'Dowd watched incessantly by the wounded lad, whose pains were very severe, and in the duty thus forced upon her, Amelia had not time to brood over her personal anxieties, or to give herself up to her own fears and forebodings after her wont. The young patient told in his simple fashion the events of the day, and the actions of our friends of the gallant --th. They had suffered severely. They had lost very many officers and men. The Major's horse had been shot under him as the regiment charged, and they all thought that O'Dowd was gone, and that Dobbin had got his majority, until on their return from the charge to their old ground, the Major was discovered seated on Pyramus's carcase, refreshing him-self from a case-bottle. It was Captain Osborne that cut down the French lancer who had speared the ensign. Amelia turned so pale at the notion, that Mrs. O'Dowd stopped the young ensign in this story. And it was Captain Dobbin who at the end of the day, though wounded himself, took up the lad in his arms and carried him to the surgeon, and thence to the cart which was to bring him back to Brussels. And it was he who promised the driver two louis if he would make his way to Mr. Sedley's hotel in the city; and tell Mrs. Captain Osborne that the action was over, and that her husband was unhurt and well.\n\n\"Indeed, but he has a good heart that William Dobbin,\" Mrs. O'Dowd said, \"though he is always laughing at me.\"\n\nYoung Stubble vowed there was not such another officer in the army, and never ceased his praises of the senior captain, his modesty, his kindness, and his admirable coolness in the field. To these parts of the conversation, Amelia lent a very distracted attention: it was only when George was spoken of that she listened, and when he was not mentioned, she thought about him.\n\nIn tending her patient, and in thinking of the wonderful escapes of the day before, her second day passed away not too slowly with Amelia. There was only one man in the army for her: and as long as he was well, it must be owned that its movements interested her little. All the reports which Jos brought from the streets fell very vaguely on her ears; though they were sufficient to give that timorous gentleman, and many other people then in Brussels, every disquiet. The French had been repulsed certainly, but it was after a severe and doubtful struggle, and with only a division of the French army. The Emperor, with the main body, was away at Ligny, where he had utterly annihilated the Prussians, and was now free to bring his whole force to bear upon the allies. The Duke of Wellington was retreating upon the capital, and a great battle must be fought under its walls probably, of which the chances were more than doubtful. The Duke of Wellington had but twenty thousand British troops on whom he could rely, for the Germans were raw militia, the Belgians disaffected, and with this handful his Grace had to resist a hundred and fifty thousand men that had broken into Belgium under Napoleon. Under Napoleon! What warrior was there, however famous and skilful, that could fight at odds with him?\n\nJos thought of all these things, and trembled. So did all the rest of Brussels--where people felt that the fight of the day before was but the prelude to the greater combat which was imminent. One of the armies opposed to the Emperor was scattered to the winds already. The few English that could be brought to resist him would perish at their posts, and the conqueror would pass over their bodies into the city. Woe be to those whom he found there! Addresses were prepared, public functionaries assembled and debated secretly, apartments were got ready, and tricoloured banners and triumphal emblems manufactured, to welcome the arrival of His Majesty the Emperor and King.\n\nThe emigration still continued, and wherever families could find means of departure, they fled. When Jos, on the afternoon of the 17th of June, went to Rebecca's hotel, he found that the great Bareacres' carriage had at length rolled away from the porte-cochere. The Earl had procured a pair of horses somehow, in spite of Mrs. Crawley, and was rolling on the road to Ghent. Louis the Desired was getting ready his portmanteau in that city, too. It seemed as if Misfortune was never tired of worrying into motion that unwieldy exile.\n\nJos felt that the delay of yesterday had been only a respite, and that his dearly bought horses must of a surety be put into requisition. His agonies were very severe all this day. As long as there was an English army between Brussels and Napoleon, there was no need of immediate flight; but he had his horses brought from their distant stables, to the stables in the court-yard of the hotel where he lived; so that they might be under his own eyes, and beyond the risk of violent abduction. Isidor watched the stable-door constantly, and had the horses saddled, to be ready for the start. He longed intensely for that event.\n\nAfter the reception of the previous day, Rebecca did not care to come near her dear Amelia. She clipped the bouquet which George had brought her, and gave fresh water to the flowers, and read over the letter which he had sent her. \"Poor wretch,\" she said, twirling round the little bit of paper in her fingers, \"how I could crush her with this!--and it is for a thing like this that she must break her heart, forsooth--for a man who is stupid--a coxcomb--and who does not care for her. My poor good Rawdon is worth ten of this creature.\" And then she fell to thinking what she should do if--if anything happened to poor good Rawdon, and what a great piece of luck it was that he had left his horses behind.\n\nIn the course of this day too, Mrs. Crawley, who saw not without anger the Bareacres party drive off, bethought her of the precaution which the Countess had taken, and did a little needlework for her own advantage; she stitched away the major part of her trinkets, bills, and bank-notes about her person, and so prepared, was ready for any event--to fly if she thought fit, or to stay and welcome the conqueror, were he Englishman or Frenchman. And I am not sure that she did not dream that night of becoming a duchess and Madame la Marechale, while Rawdon wrapped in his cloak, and making his bivouac under the rain at Mount Saint John, was thinking, with all the force of his heart, about the little wife whom he had left behind him.\n\nThe next day was a Sunday. And Mrs. Major O'Dowd had the satisfaction of seeing both her patients refreshed in health and spirits by some rest which they had taken during the night. She herself had slept on a great chair in Amelia's room, ready to wait upon her poor friend or the ensign, should either need her nursing. When morning came, this robust woman went back to the house where she and her Major had their billet; and here performed an elaborate and splendid toilette, befitting the day. And it is very possible that whilst alone in that chamber, which her husband had inhabited, and where his cap still lay on the pillow, and his cane stood in the corner, one prayer at least was sent up to Heaven for the welfare of the brave soldier, Michael O'Dowd.\n\nWhen she returned she brought her prayer-book with her, and her uncle the Dean's famous book of sermons, out of which she never failed to read every Sabbath; not understanding all, haply, not pronouncing many of the words aright, which were long and abstruse--for the Dean was a learned man, and loved long Latin words--but with great gravity, vast emphasis, and with tolerable correctness in the main. How often has my Mick listened to these sermons, she thought, and me reading in the cabin of a calm! She proposed to resume this exercise on the present day, with Amelia and the wounded ensign for a congregation. The same service was read on that day in twenty thousand churches at the same hour; and millions of British men and women, on their knees, implored protection of the Father of all.\n\nThey did not hear the noise which disturbed our little congregation at Brussels. Much louder than that which had interrupted them two days previously, as Mrs. O'Dowd was reading the service in her best voice, the cannon of Waterloo began to roar.\n\nWhen Jos heard that dreadful sound, he made up his mind that he would bear this perpetual recurrence of terrors no longer, and would fly at once. He rushed into the sick man's room, where our three friends had paused in their prayers, and further interrupted them by a passionate appeal to Amelia.\n\n\"I can't stand it any more, Emmy,\" he said; \"I won't stand it; and you must come with me. I have bought a horse for you--never mind at what price--and you must dress and come with me, and ride behind Isidor.\"\n\n\"God forgive me, Mr. Sedley, but you are no better than a coward,\" Mrs. O'Dowd said, laying down the book.\n\n\"I say come, Amelia,\" the civilian went on; \"never mind what she says; why are we to stop here and be butchered by the Frenchmen?\"\n\n\"You forget the --th, my boy,\" said the little Stubble, the wounded hero, from his bed--\"and and you won't leave me, will you, Mrs. O'Dowd?\"\n\n\"No, my dear fellow,\" said she, going up and kissing the boy. \"No harm shall come to you while I stand by. I don't budge till I get the word from Mick. A pretty figure I'd be, wouldn't I, stuck behind that chap on a pillion?\"\n\nThis image caused the young patient to burst out laughing in his bed, and even made Amelia smile. \"I don't ask her,\" Jos shouted out--\"I don't ask that--that Irishwoman, but you Amelia; once for all, will you come?\"\n\n\"Without my husband, Joseph?\" Amelia said, with a look of wonder, and gave her hand to the Major's wife. Jos's patience was exhausted.\n\n\"Good-bye, then,\" he said, shaking his fist in a rage, and slamming the door by which he retreated. And this time he really gave his order for march: and mounted in the court-yard. Mrs. O'Dowd heard the clattering hoofs of the horses as they issued from the gate; and looking on, made many scornful remarks on poor Joseph as he rode down the street with Isidor after him in the laced cap. The horses, which had not been exercised for some days, were lively, and sprang about the street. Jos, a clumsy and timid horseman, did not look to advantage in the saddle. \"Look at him, Amelia dear, driving into the parlour window. Such a bull in a china-shop I never saw.\" And presently the pair of riders disappeared at a canter down the street leading in the direction of the Ghent road, Mrs. O'Dowd pursuing them with a fire of sarcasm so long as they were in sight.\n\nAll that day from morning until past sunset, the cannon never ceased to roar. It was dark when the cannonading stopped all of a sudden.\n\nAll of us have read of what occurred during that interval. The tale is in every Englishman's mouth; and you and I, who were children when the great battle was won and lost, are never tired of hearing and recounting the history of that famous action. Its remembrance rankles still in the bosoms of millions of the countrymen of those brave men who lost the day. They pant for an opportunity of revenging that humiliation; and if a contest, ending in a victory on their part, should ensue, elating them in their turn, and leaving its cursed legacy of hatred and rage behind to us, there is no end to the so-called glory and shame, and to the alternations of successful and unsuccessful murder, in which two high-spirited nations might engage. Centuries hence, we Frenchmen and Englishmen might be boasting and killing each other still, carrying out bravely the Devil's code of honour.\n\nAll our friends took their share and fought like men in the great field. All day long, whilst the women were praying ten miles away, the lines of the dauntless English infantry were receiving and repelling the furious charges of the French horsemen. Guns which were heard at Brussels were ploughing up their ranks, and comrades falling, and the resolute survivors closing in. Towards evening, the attack of the French, repeated and resisted so bravely, slackened in its fury. They had other foes besides the British to engage, or were preparing for a final onset. It came at last: the columns of the Imperial Guard marched up the hill of Saint Jean, at length and at once to sweep the English from the height which they had maintained all day, and spite of all: unscared by the thunder of the artillery, which hurled death from the English line--the dark rolling column pressed on and up the hill. It seemed almost to crest the eminence, when it began to wave and falter. Then it stopped, still facing the shot. Then at last the English troops rushed from the post from which no enemy had been able to dislodge them, and the Guard turned and fled.\n\nNo more firing was heard at Brussels--the pursuit rolled miles away. Darkness came down on the field and city: and Amelia was praying for George, who was lying on his face, dead, with a bullet through his heart.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIII\n\nIn Which Miss Crawley's Relations Are Very Anxious About Her\nThe kind reader must please to remember--while the army is marching from Flanders, and, after its heroic actions there, is advancing to take the fortifications on the frontiers of France, previous to an occupation of that country--that there are a number of persons living peaceably in England who have to do with the history at present in hand, and must come in for their share of the chronicle. During the time of these battles and dangers, old Miss Crawley was living at Brighton, very moderately moved by the great events that were going on. The great events rendered the newspapers rather interesting, to be sure, and Briggs read out the Gazette, in which Rawdon Crawley's gallantry was mentioned with honour, and his promotion was presently recorded.\n\n\"What a pity that young man has taken such an irretrievable step in the world!\" his aunt said; \"with his rank and distinction he might have married a brewer's daughter with a quarter of a million--like Miss Grains; or have looked to ally himself with the best families in England. He would have had my money some day or other; or his children would--for I'm not in a hurry to go, Miss Briggs, although you may be in a hurry to be rid of me; and instead of that, he is a doomed pauper, with a dancing-girl for a wife.\"\n\n\"Will my dear Miss Crawley not cast an eye of compassion upon the heroic soldier, whose name is inscribed in the annals of his country's glory?\" said Miss Briggs, who was greatly excited by the Waterloo proceedings, and loved speaking romantically when there was an occasion. \"Has not the Captain--or the Colonel as I may now style him--done deeds which make the name of Crawley illustrious?\"\n\n\"Briggs, you are a fool,\" said Miss Crawley: \"Colonel Crawley has dragged the name of Crawley through the mud, Miss Briggs. Marry a drawing-master's daughter, indeed!--marry a dame de compagnie--for she was no better, Briggs; no, she was just what you are--only younger, and a great deal prettier and cleverer. Were you an accomplice of that abandoned wretch, I wonder, of whose vile arts he became a victim, and of whom you used to be such an admirer? Yes, I daresay you were an accomplice. But you will find yourself disappointed in my will, I can tell you: and you will have the goodness to write to Mr. Waxy, and say that I desire to see him immediately.\" Miss Crawley was now in the habit of writing to Mr. Waxy her solicitor almost every day in the week, for her arrangements respecting her property were all revoked, and her perplexity was great as to the future disposition of her money.\n\nThe spinster had, however, rallied considerably; as was proved by the increased vigour and frequency of her sarcasms upon Miss Briggs, all which attacks the poor companion bore with meekness, with cowardice, with a resignation that was half generous and half hypocritical--with the slavish submission, in a word, that women of her disposition and station are compelled to show. Who has not seen how women bully women? What tortures have men to endure, comparable to those daily repeated shafts of scorn and cruelty with which poor women are riddled by the tyrants of their sex? Poor victims! But we are starting from our proposition, which is, that Miss Crawley was always particularly annoying and savage when she was rallying from illness--as they say wounds tingle most when they are about to heal.\n\nWhile thus approaching, as all hoped, to convalescence, Miss Briggs was the only victim admitted into the presence of the invalid; yet Miss Crawley's relatives afar off did not forget their beloved kinswoman, and by a number of tokens, presents, and kind affectionate messages, strove to keep themselves alive in her recollection.\n\nIn the first place, let us mention her nephew, Rawdon Crawley. A few weeks after the famous fight of Waterloo, and after the Gazette had made known to her the promotion and gallantry of that distinguished officer, the Dieppe packet brought over to Miss Crawley at Brighton, a box containing presents, and a dutiful letter, from the Colonel her nephew. In the box were a pair of French epaulets, a Cross of the Legion of Honour, and the hilt of a sword--relics from the field of battle: and the letter described with a good deal of humour how the latter belonged to a commanding officer of the Guard, who having sworn that \"the Guard died, but never surrendered,\" was taken prisoner the next minute by a private soldier, who broke the Frenchman's sword with the butt of his musket, when Rawdon made himself master of the shattered weapon. As for the cross and epaulets, they came from a Colonel of French cavalry, who had fallen under the aide-de-camp's arm in the battle: and Rawdon Crawley did not know what better to do with the spoils than to send them to his kindest and most affectionate old friend. Should he continue to write to her from Paris, whither the army was marching? He might be able to give her interesting news from that capital, and of some of Miss Crawley's old friends of the emigration, to whom she had shown so much kindness during their distress.\n\nThe spinster caused Briggs to write back to the Colonel a gracious and complimentary letter, encouraging him to continue his correspondence. His first letter was so excessively lively and amusing that she should look with pleasure for its successors.--\"Of course, I know,\" she explained to Miss Briggs, \"that Rawdon could not write such a good letter any more than you could, my poor Briggs, and that it is that clever little wretch of a Rebecca, who dictates every word to him; but that is no reason why my nephew should not amuse me; and so I wish to let him understand that I am in high good humour.\"\n\nI wonder whether she knew that it was not only Becky who wrote the letters, but that Mrs. Rawdon actually took and sent home the trophies which she bought for a few francs, from one of the innumerable pedlars who immediately began to deal in relics of the war. The novelist, who knows everything, knows this also. Be this, however, as it may, Miss Crawley's gracious reply greatly encouraged our young friends, Rawdon and his lady, who hoped for the best from their aunt's evidently pacified humour: and they took care to entertain her with many delightful letters from Paris, whither, as Rawdon said, they had the good luck to go in the track of the conquering army.\n\nTo the rector's lady, who went off to tend her husband's broken collar-bone at the Rectory at Queen's Crawley, the spinster's communications were by no means so gracious. Mrs. Bute, that brisk, managing, lively, imperious woman, had committed the most fatal of all errors with regard to her sister-in-law. She had not merely oppressed her and her household--she had bored Miss Crawley; and if poor Miss Briggs had been a woman of any spirit, she might have been made happy by the commission which her principal gave her to write a letter to Mrs. Bute Crawley, saying that Miss Crawley's health was greatly improved since Mrs. Bute had left her, and begging the latter on no account to put herself to trouble, or quit her family for Miss Crawley's sake. This triumph over a lady who had been very haughty and cruel in her behaviour to Miss Briggs, would have rejoiced most women; but the truth is, Briggs was a woman of no spirit at all, and the moment her enemy was discomfited, she began to feel compassion in her favour.\n\n\"How silly I was,\" Mrs. Bute thought, and with reason, \"ever to hint that I was coming, as I did, in that foolish letter when we sent Miss Crawley the guinea-fowls. I ought to have gone without a word to the poor dear doting old creature, and taken her out of the hands of that ninny Briggs, and that harpy of a femme de chambre. Oh! Bute, Bute, why did you break your collar-bone?\"\n\nWhy, indeed? We have seen how Mrs. Bute, having the game in her hands, had really played her cards too well. She had ruled over Miss Crawley's household utterly and completely, to be utterly and completely routed when a favourable opportunity for rebellion came. She and her household, however, considered that she had been the victim of horrible selfishness and treason, and that her sacrifices in Miss Crawley's behalf had met with the most savage ingratitude. Rawdon's promotion, and the honourable mention made of his name in the Gazette, filled this good Christian lady also with alarm. Would his aunt relent towards him now that he was a Lieutenant-Colonel and a C.B.? and would that odious Rebecca once more get into favour? The Rector's wife wrote a sermon for her husband about the vanity of military glory and the prosperity of the wicked, which the worthy parson read in his best voice and without understanding one syllable of it. He had Pitt Crawley for one of his auditors--Pitt, who had come with his two half-sisters to church, which the old Baronet could now by no means be brought to frequent.\n\nSince the departure of Becky Sharp, that old wretch had given himself up entirely to his bad courses, to the great scandal of the county and the mute horror of his son. The ribbons in Miss Horrocks's cap became more splendid than ever. The polite families fled the hall and its owner in terror. Sir Pitt went about tippling at his tenants' houses; and drank rum-and-water with the farmers at Mudbury and the neighbouring places on market-days. He drove the family coach-and-four to Southampton with Miss Horrocks inside: and the county people expected, every week, as his son did in speechless agony, that his marriage with her would be announced in the provincial paper. It was indeed a rude burthen for Mr. Crawley to bear. His eloquence was palsied at the missionary meetings, and other religious assemblies in the neighbourhood, where he had been in the habit of presiding, and of speaking for hours; for he felt, when he rose, that the audience said, \"That is the son of the old reprobate Sir Pitt, who is very likely drinking at the public house at this very moment.\" And once when he was speaking of the benighted condition of the king of Timbuctoo, and the number of his wives who were likewise in darkness, some gipsy miscreant from the crowd asked, \"How many is there at Queen's Crawley, Young Squaretoes?\" to the surprise of the platform, and the ruin of Mr. Pitt's speech. And the two daughters of the house of Queen's Crawley would have been allowed to run utterly wild (for Sir Pitt swore that no governess should ever enter into his doors again), had not Mr. Crawley, by threatening the old gentleman, forced the latter to send them to school.\n\nMeanwhile, as we have said, whatever individual differences there might be between them all, Miss Crawley's dear nephews and nieces were unanimous in loving her and sending her tokens of affection. Thus Mrs. Bute sent guinea-fowls, and some remarkably fine cauliflowers, and a pretty purse or pincushion worked by her darling girls, who begged to keep a LITTLE place in the recollection of their dear aunt, while Mr. Pitt sent peaches and grapes and venison from the Hall. The Southampton coach used to carry these tokens of affection to Miss Crawley at Brighton: it used sometimes to convey Mr. Pitt thither too: for his differences with Sir Pitt caused Mr. Crawley to absent himself a good deal from home now: and besides, he had an attraction at Brighton in the person of the Lady Jane Sheepshanks, whose engagement to Mr. Crawley has been formerly mentioned in this history. Her Ladyship and her sisters lived at Brighton with their mamma, the Countess Southdown, that strong-minded woman so favourably known in the serious world.\n\nA few words ought to be said regarding her Ladyship and her noble family, who are bound by ties of present and future relationship to the house of Crawley. Respecting the chief of the Southdown family, Clement William, fourth Earl of Southdown, little need be told, except that his Lordship came into Parliament (as Lord Wolsey) under the auspices of Mr. Wilberforce, and for a time was a credit to his political sponsor, and decidedly a serious young man. But words cannot describe the feelings of his admirable mother, when she learned, very shortly after her noble husband's demise, that her son was a member of several worldly clubs, had lost largely at play at Wattier's and the Cocoa Tree; that he had raised money on post-obits, and encumbered the family estate; that he drove four-in-hand, and patronised the ring; and that he actually had an opera-box, where he entertained the most dangerous bachelor company. His name was only mentioned with groans in the dowager's circle.\n\nThe Lady Emily was her brother's senior by many years; and took considerable rank in the serious world as author of some of the delightful tracts before mentioned, and of many hymns and spiritual pieces. A mature spinster, and having but faint ideas of marriage, her love for the blacks occupied almost all her feelings. It is to her, I believe, we owe that beautiful poem.\n\nLead us to some sunny isle, Yonder in the western deep; Where the skies for ever smile, And the blacks for ever weep, &c.\n\nShe had correspondences with clerical gentlemen in most of our East and West India possessions; and was secretly attached to the Reverend Silas Hornblower, who was tattooed in the South Sea Islands.\n\nAs for the Lady Jane, on whom, as it has been said, Mr. Pitt Crawley's affection had been placed, she was gentle, blushing, silent, and timid. In spite of his falling away, she wept for her brother, and was quite ashamed of loving him still. Even yet she used to send him little hurried smuggled notes, and pop them into the post in private. The one dreadful secret which weighed upon her life was, that she and the old housekeeper had been to pay Southdown a furtive visit at his chambers in the Albany; and found him--O the naughty dear abandoned wretch!--smoking a cigar with a bottle of Curacao before him. She admired her sister, she adored her mother, she thought Mr. Crawley the most delightful and accomplished of men, after Southdown, that fallen angel: and her mamma and sister, who were ladies of the most superior sort, managed everything for her, and regarded her with that amiable pity, of which your really superior woman always has such a share to give away. Her mamma ordered her dresses, her books, her bonnets, and her ideas for her. She was made to take pony-riding, or piano-exercise, or any other sort of bodily medicament, according as my Lady Southdown saw meet; and her ladyship would have kept her daughter in pinafores up to her present age of six-and-twenty, but that they were thrown off when Lady Jane was presented to Queen Charlotte.\n\nWhen these ladies first came to their house at Brighton, it was to them alone that Mr. Crawley paid his personal visits, contenting himself by leaving a card at his aunt's house, and making a modest inquiry of Mr. Bowls or his assistant footman, with respect to the health of the invalid. When he met Miss Briggs coming home from the library with a cargo of novels under her arm, Mr. Crawley blushed in a manner quite unusual to him, as he stepped forward and shook Miss Crawley's companion by the hand. He introduced Miss Briggs to the lady with whom he happened to be walking, the Lady Jane Sheepshanks, saying, \"Lady Jane, permit me to introduce to you my aunt's kindest friend and most affectionate companion, Miss Briggs, whom you know under another title, as authoress of the delightful 'Lyrics of the Heart,' of which you are so fond.\" Lady Jane blushed too as she held out a kind little hand to Miss Briggs, and said something very civil and incoherent about mamma, and proposing to call on Miss Crawley, and being glad to be made known to the friends and relatives of Mr. Crawley; and with soft dove-like eyes saluted Miss Briggs as they separated, while Pitt Crawley treated her to a profound courtly bow, such as he had used to H.H. the Duchess of Pumpernickel, when he was attache at that court.\n\nThe artful diplomatist and disciple of the Machiavellian Binkie! It was he who had given Lady Jane that copy of poor Briggs's early poems, which he remembered to have seen at Queen's Crawley, with a dedication from the poetess to his father's late wife; and he brought the volume with him to Brighton, reading it in the Southampton coach and marking it with his own pencil, before he presented it to the gentle Lady Jane.\n\nIt was he, too, who laid before Lady Southdown the great advantages which might occur from an intimacy between her family and Miss Crawley--advantages both worldly and spiritual, he said: for Miss Crawley was now quite alone; the monstrous dissipation and alliance of his brother Rawdon had estranged her affections from that reprobate young man; the greedy tyranny and avarice of Mrs. Bute Crawley had caused the old lady to revolt against the exorbitant pretensions of that part of the family; and though he himself had held off all his life from cultivating Miss Crawley's friendship, with perhaps an improper pride, he thought now that every becoming means should be taken, both to save her soul from perdition, and to secure her fortune to himself as the head of the house of Crawley.\n\nThe strong-minded Lady Southdown quite agreed in both proposals of her son-in-law, and was for converting Miss Crawley off-hand. At her own home, both at Southdown and at Trottermore Castle, this tall and awful missionary of the truth rode about the country in her barouche with outriders, launched packets of tracts among the cottagers and tenants, and would order Gaffer Jones to be converted, as she would order Goody Hicks to take a James's powder, without appeal, resistance, or benefit of clergy. My Lord Southdown, her late husband, an epileptic and simple-minded nobleman, was in the habit of approving of everything which his Matilda did and thought. So that whatever changes her own belief might undergo (and it accommodated itself to a prodigious variety of opinion, taken from all sorts of doctors among the Dissenters) she had not the least scruple in ordering all her tenants and inferiors to follow and believe after her. Thus whether she received the Reverend Saunders McNitre, the Scotch divine; or the Reverend Luke Waters, the mild Wesleyan; or the Reverend Giles Jowls, the illuminated Cobbler, who dubbed himself Reverend as Napoleon crowned himself Emperor--the household, children, tenantry of my Lady Southdown were expected to go down on their knees with her Ladyship, and say Amen to the prayers of either Doctor. During these exercises old Southdown, on account of his invalid condition, was allowed to sit in his own room, and have negus and the paper read to him. Lady Jane was the old Earl's favourite daughter, and tended him and loved him sincerely: as for Lady Emily, the authoress of the \"Washerwoman of Finchley Common,\" her denunciations of future punishment (at this period, for her opinions modified afterwards) were so awful that they used to frighten the timid old gentleman her father, and the physicians declared his fits always occurred after one of her Ladyship's sermons.\n\n\"I will certainly call,\" said Lady Southdown then, in reply to the exhortation of her daughter's pretendu, Mr. Pitt Crawley--\"Who is Miss Crawley's medical man?\"\n\nMr. Crawley mentioned the name of Mr. Creamer.\n\n\"A most dangerous and ignorant practitioner, my dear Pitt. I have providentially been the means of removing him from several houses: though in one or two instances I did not arrive in time. I could not save poor dear General Glanders, who was dying under the hands of that ignorant man--dying. He rallied a little under the Podgers' pills which I administered to him; but alas! it was too late. His death was delightful, however; and his change was only for the better; Creamer, my dear Pitt, must leave your aunt.\"\n\nPitt expressed his perfect acquiescence. He, too, had been carried along by the energy of his noble kinswoman, and future mother-in-law. He had been made to accept Saunders McNitre, Luke Waters, Giles Jowls, Podgers' Pills, Rodgers' Pills, Pokey's Elixir, every one of her Ladyship's remedies spiritual or temporal. He never left her house without carrying respectfully away with him piles of her quack theology and medicine. O, my dear brethren and fellow-sojourners in Vanity Fair, which among you does not know and suffer under such benevolent despots? It is in vain you say to them, \"Dear Madam, I took Podgers' specific at your orders last year, and believe in it. Why, why am I to recant and accept the Rodgers' articles now?\" There is no help for it; the faithful proselytizer, if she cannot convince by argument, bursts into tears, and the refusant finds himself, at the end of the contest, taking down the bolus, and saying, \"Well, well, Rodgers' be it.\"\n\n\"And as for her spiritual state,\" continued the Lady, \"that of course must be looked to immediately: with Creamer about her, she may go off any day: and in what a condition, my dear Pitt, in what a dreadful condition! I will send the Reverend Mr. Irons to her instantly. Jane, write a line to the Reverend Bartholomew Irons, in the third person, and say that I desire the pleasure of his company this evening at tea at half-past six. He is an awakening man; he ought to see Miss Crawley before she rests this night. And Emily, my love, get ready a packet of books for Miss Crawley. Put up 'A Voice from the Flames,' 'A Trumpet-warning to Jericho,' and the 'Fleshpots Broken; or, the Converted Cannibal.'\"\n\n\"And the 'Washerwoman of Finchley Common,' Mamma,\" said Lady Emily. \"It is as well to begin soothingly at first.\"\n\n\"Stop, my dear ladies,\" said Pitt, the diplomatist. \"With every deference to the opinion of my beloved and respected Lady Southdown, I think it would be quite unadvisable to commence so early upon serious topics with Miss Crawley. Remember her delicate condition, and how little, how very little accustomed she has hitherto been to considerations connected with her immortal welfare.\"\n\n\"Can we then begin too early, Pitt?\" said Lady Emily, rising with six little books already in her hand.\n\n\"If you begin abruptly, you will frighten her altogether. I know my aunt's worldly nature so well as to be sure that any abrupt attempt at conversion will be the very worst means that can be employed for the welfare of that unfortunate lady. You will only frighten and annoy her. She will very likely fling the books away, and refuse all acquaintance with the givers.\"\n\n\"You are as worldly as Miss Crawley, Pitt,\" said Lady Emily, tossing out of the room, her books in her hand.\n\n\"And I need not tell you, my dear Lady Southdown,\" Pitt continued, in a low voice, and without heeding the interruption, \"how fatal a little want of gentleness and caution may be to any hopes which we may entertain with regard to the worldly possessions of my aunt. Remember she has seventy thousand pounds; think of her age, and her highly nervous and delicate condition; I know that she has destroyed the will which was made in my brother's (Colonel Crawley's) favour: it is by soothing that wounded spirit that we must lead it into the right path, and not by frightening it; and so I think you will agree with me that--that--'\n\n\"Of course, of course,\" Lady Southdown remarked. \"Jane, my love, you need not send that note to Mr. Irons. If her health is such that discussions fatigue her, we will wait her amendment. I will call upon Miss Crawley tomorrow.\"\n\n\"And if I might suggest, my sweet lady,\" Pitt said in a bland tone, \"it would be as well not to take our precious Emily, who is too enthusiastic; but rather that you should be accompanied by our sweet and dear Lady Jane.\"\n\n\"Most certainly, Emily would ruin everything,\" Lady Southdown said; and this time agreed to forego her usual practice, which was, as we have said, before she bore down personally upon any individual whom she proposed to subjugate, to fire in a quantity of tracts upon the menaced party (as a charge of the French was always preceded by a furious cannonade). Lady Southdown, we say, for the sake of the invalid's health, or for the sake of her soul's ultimate welfare, or for the sake of her money, agreed to temporise.\n\nThe next day, the great Southdown female family carriage, with the Earl's coronet and the lozenge (upon which the three lambs trottant argent upon the field vert of the Southdowns, were quartered with sable on a bend or, three snuff-mulls gules, the cognizance of the house of Binkie), drove up in state to Miss Crawley's door, and the tall serious footman handed in to Mr. Bowls her Ladyship's cards for Miss Crawley, and one likewise for Miss Briggs. By way of compromise, Lady Emily sent in a packet in the evening for the latter lady, containing copies of the \"Washerwoman,\" and other mild and favourite tracts for Miss B.'s own perusal; and a few for the servants' hall, viz.: \"Crumbs from the Pantry,\" \"The Frying Pan and the Fire,\" and \"The Livery of Sin,\" of a much stronger kind.\n\nJames Crawley's Pipe Is Put Out\nThe amiable behaviour of Mr. Crawley, and Lady Jane's kind reception of her, highly flattered Miss Briggs, who was enabled to speak a good word for the latter, after the cards of the Southdown family had been presented to Miss Crawley. A Countess's card left personally too for her, Briggs, was not a little pleasing to the poor friendless companion. \"What could Lady Southdown mean by leaving a card upon you, I wonder, Miss Briggs?\" said the republican Miss Crawley; upon which the companion meekly said \"that she hoped there could be no harm in a lady of rank taking notice of a poor gentlewoman,\" and she put away this card in her work-box amongst her most cherished personal treasures. Furthermore, Miss Briggs explained how she had met Mr. Crawley walking with his cousin and long affianced bride the day before: and she told how kind and gentle-looking the lady was, and what a plain, not to say common, dress she had, all the articles of which, from the bonnet down to the boots, she described and estimated with female accuracy.\n\nMiss Crawley allowed Briggs to prattle on without interrupting her too much. As she got well, she was pining for society. Mr. Creamer, her medical man, would not hear of her returning to her old haunts and dissipation in London. The old spinster was too glad to find any companionship at Brighton, and not only were the cards acknowledged the very next day, but Pitt Crawley was graciously invited to come and see his aunt. He came, bringing with him Lady Southdown and her daughter. The dowager did not say a word about the state of Miss Crawley's soul; but talked with much discretion about the weather: about the war and the downfall of the monster Bonaparte: and above all, about doctors, quacks, and the particular merits of Dr. Podgers, whom she then patronised.\n\nDuring their interview Pitt Crawley made a great stroke, and one which showed that, had his diplomatic career not been blighted by early neglect, he might have risen to a high rank in his profession. When the Countess Dowager of Southdown fell foul of the Corsican upstart, as the fashion was in those days, and showed that he was a monster stained with every conceivable crime, a coward and a tyrant not fit to live, one whose fall was predicted, &c., Pitt Crawley suddenly took up the cudgels in favour of the man of Destiny. He described the First Consul as he saw him at Paris at the peace of Amiens; when he, Pitt Crawley, had the gratification of making the acquaintance of the great and good Mr. Fox, a statesman whom, however much he might differ with him, it was impossible not to admire fervently--a statesman who had always had the highest opinion of the Emperor Napoleon. And he spoke in terms of the strongest indignation of the faithless conduct of the allies towards this dethroned monarch, who, after giving himself generously up to their mercy, was consigned to an ignoble and cruel banishment, while a bigoted Popish rabble was tyrannising over France in his stead.\n\nThis orthodox horror of Romish superstition saved Pitt Crawley in Lady Southdown's opinion, whilst his admiration for Fox and Napoleon raised him immeasurably in Miss Crawley's eyes. Her friendship with that defunct British statesman was mentioned when we first introduced her in this history. A true Whig, Miss Crawley had been in opposition all through the war, and though, to be sure, the downfall of the Emperor did not very much agitate the old lady, or his ill-treatment tend to shorten her life or natural rest, yet Pitt spoke to her heart when he lauded both her idols; and by that single speech made immense progress in her favour.\n\n\"And what do you think, my dear?\" Miss Crawley said to the young lady, for whom she had taken a liking at first sight, as she always did for pretty and modest young people; though it must be owned her affections cooled as rapidly as they rose.\n\nLady Jane blushed very much, and said \"that she did not understand politics, which she left to wiser heads than hers; but though Mamma was, no doubt, correct, Mr. Crawley had spoken beautifully.\" And when the ladies were retiring at the conclusion of their visit, Miss Crawley hoped \"Lady Southdown would be so kind as to send her Lady Jane sometimes, if she could be spared to come down and console a poor sick lonely old woman.\" This promise was graciously accorded, and they separated upon great terms of amity.\n\n\"Don't let Lady Southdown come again, Pitt,\" said the old lady. \"She is stupid and pompous, like all your mother's family, whom I never could endure. But bring that nice good-natured little Jane as often as ever you please.\" Pitt promised that he would do so. He did not tell the Countess of Southdown what opinion his aunt had formed of her Ladyship, who, on the contrary, thought that she had made a most delightful and majestic impression on Miss Crawley.\n\nAnd so, nothing loth to comfort a sick lady, and perhaps not sorry in her heart to be freed now and again from the dreary spouting of the Reverend Bartholomew Irons, and the serious toadies who gathered round the footstool of the pompous Countess, her mamma, Lady Jane became a pretty constant visitor to Miss Crawley, accompanied her in her drives, and solaced many of her evenings. She was so naturally good and soft, that even Firkin was not jealous of her; and the gentle Briggs thought her friend was less cruel to her when kind Lady Jane was by. Towards her Ladyship Miss Crawley's manners were charming. The old spinster told her a thousand anecdotes about her youth, talking to her in a very different strain from that in which she had been accustomed to converse with the godless little Rebecca; for there was that in Lady Jane's innocence which rendered light talking impertinence before her, and Miss Crawley was too much of a gentlewoman to offend such purity. The young lady herself had never received kindness except from this old spinster, and her brother and father: and she repaid Miss Crawley's engoument by artless sweetness and friendship.\n\nIn the autumn evenings (when Rebecca was flaunting at Paris, the gayest among the gay conquerors there, and our Amelia, our dear wounded Amelia, ah! where was she?) Lady Jane would be sitting in Miss Crawley's drawing-room singing sweetly to her, in the twilight, her little simple songs and hymns, while the sun was setting and the sea was roaring on the beach. The old spinster used to wake up when these ditties ceased, and ask for more. As for Briggs, and the quantity of tears of happiness which she now shed as she pretended to knit, and looked out at the splendid ocean darkling before the windows, and the lamps of heaven beginning more brightly to shine--who, I say can measure the happiness and sensibility of Briggs?\n\nPitt meanwhile in the dining-room, with a pamphlet on the Corn Laws or a Missionary Register by his side, took that kind of recreation which suits romantic and unromantic men after dinner. He sipped Madeira: built castles in the air: thought himself a fine fellow: felt himself much more in love with Jane than he had been any time these seven years, during which their liaison had lasted without the slightest impatience on Pitt's part--and slept a good deal. When the time for coffee came, Mr. Bowls used to enter in a noisy manner, and summon Squire Pitt, who would be found in the dark very busy with his pamphlet.\n\n\"I wish, my love, I could get somebody to play piquet with me,\" Miss Crawley said one night when this functionary made his appearance with the candles and the coffee. \"Poor Briggs can no more play than an owl, she is so stupid\" (the spinster always took an opportunity of abusing Briggs before the servants); \"and I think I should sleep better if I had my game.\"\n\nAt this Lady Jane blushed to the tips of her little ears, and down to the ends of her pretty fingers; and when Mr. Bowls had quitted the room, and the door was quite shut, she said:\n\n\"Miss Crawley, I can play a little. I used to--to play a little with poor dear papa.\"\n\n\"Come and kiss me. Come and kiss me this instant, you dear good little soul,\" cried Miss Crawley in an ecstasy: and in this picturesque and friendly occupation Mr. Pitt found the old lady and the young one, when he came upstairs with him pamphlet in his hand. How she did blush all the evening, that poor Lady Jane!\n\nIt must not be imagined that Mr. Pitt Crawley's artifices escaped the attention of his dear relations at the Rectory at Queen's Crawley. Hampshire and Sussex lie very close together, and Mrs. Bute had friends in the latter county who took care to inform her of all, and a great deal more than all, that passed at Miss Crawley's house at Brighton. Pitt was there more and more. He did not come for months together to the Hall, where his abominable old father abandoned himself completely to rum-and-water, and the odious society of the Horrocks family. Pitt's success rendered the Rector's family furious, and Mrs. Bute regretted more (though she confessed less) than ever her monstrous fault in so insulting Miss Briggs, and in being so haughty and parsimonious to Bowls and Firkin, that she had not a single person left in Miss Crawley's household to give her information of what took place there. \"It was all Bute's collar-bone,\" she persisted in saying; \"if that had not broke, I never would have left her. I am a martyr to duty and to your odious unclerical habit of hunting, Bute.\"\n\n\"Hunting; nonsense! It was you that frightened her, Barbara,\" the divine interposed. \"You're a clever woman, but you've got a devil of a temper; and you're a screw with your money, Barbara.\"\n\n\"You'd have been screwed in gaol, Bute, if I had not kept your money.\"\n\n\"I know I would, my dear,\" said the Rector, good-naturedly. \"You ARE a clever woman, but you manage too well, you know\": and the pious man consoled himself with a big glass of port.\n\n\"What the deuce can she find in that spooney of a Pitt Crawley?\" he continued. \"The fellow has not pluck enough to say Bo to a goose. I remember when Rawdon, who is a man, and be hanged to him, used to flog him round the stables as if he was a whipping-top: and Pitt would go howling home to his ma--ha, ha! Why, either of my boys would whop him with one hand. Jim says he's remembered at Oxford as Miss Crawley still--the spooney.\n\n\"I say, Barbara,\" his reverence continued, after a pause.\n\n\"What?\" said Barbara, who was biting her nails, and drumming the table.\n\n\"I say, why not send Jim over to Brighton to see if he can do anything with the old lady. He's very near getting his degree, you know. He's only been plucked twice--so was I--but he's had the advantages of Oxford and a university education. He knows some of the best chaps there. He pulls stroke in the Boniface boat. He's a handsome feller. D---- it, ma'am, let's put him on the old woman, hey, and tell him to thrash Pitt if he says anything. Ha, ha, ha!\n\n\"Jim might go down and see her, certainly,\" the housewife said; adding with a sigh, \"If we could but get one of the girls into the house; but she could never endure them, because they are not pretty!\" Those unfortunate and well-educated women made themselves heard from the neighbouring drawing-room, where they were thrumming away, with hard fingers, an elaborate music-piece on the piano-forte, as their mother spoke; and indeed, they were at music, or at backboard, or at geography, or at history, the whole day long. But what avail all these accomplishments, in Vanity Fair, to girls who are short, poor, plain, and have a bad complexion? Mrs. Bute could think of nobody but the Curate to take one of them off her hands; and Jim coming in from the stable at this minute, through the parlour window, with a short pipe stuck in his oilskin cap, he and his father fell to talking about odds on the St. Leger, and the colloquy between the Rector and his wife ended.\n\nMrs. Bute did not augur much good to the cause from the sending of her son James as an ambassador, and saw him depart in rather a despairing mood. Nor did the young fellow himself, when told what his mission was to be, expect much pleasure or benefit from it; but he was consoled by the thought that possibly the old lady would give him some handsome remembrance of her, which would pay a few of his most pressing bills at the commencement of the ensuing Oxford term, and so took his place by the coach from Southampton, and was safely landed at Brighton on the same evening with his portmanteau, his favourite bull-dog Towzer, and an immense basket of farm and garden produce, from the dear Rectory folks to the dear Miss Crawley. Considering it was too late to disturb the invalid lady on the first night of his arrival, he put up at an inn, and did not wait upon Miss Crawley until a late hour in the noon of next day.\n\nJames Crawley, when his aunt had last beheld him, was a gawky lad, at that uncomfortable age when the voice varies between an unearthly treble and a preternatural bass; when the face not uncommonly blooms out with appearances for which Rowland's Kalydor is said to act as a cure; when boys are seen to shave furtively with their sister's scissors, and the sight of other young women produces intolerable sensations of terror in them; when the great hands and ankles protrude a long way from garments which have grown too tight for them; when their presence after dinner is at once frightful to the ladies, who are whispering in the twilight in the drawing-room, and inexpressibly odious to the gentlemen over the mahogany, who are restrained from freedom of intercourse and delightful interchange of wit by the presence of that gawky innocence; when, at the conclusion of the second glass, papa says, \"Jack, my boy, go out and see if the evening holds up,\" and the youth, willing to be free, yet hurt at not being yet a man, quits the incomplete banquet. James, then a hobbadehoy, was now become a young man, having had the benefits of a university education, and acquired the inestimable polish which is gained by living in a fast set at a small college, and contracting debts, and being rusticated, and being plucked.\n\nHe was a handsome lad, however, when he came to present himself to his aunt at Brighton, and good looks were always a title to the fickle old lady's favour. Nor did his blushes and awkwardness take away from it: she was pleased with these healthy tokens of the young gentleman's ingenuousness.\n\nHe said \"he had come down for a couple of days to see a man of his college, and--and to pay my respects to you, Ma'am, and my father's and mother's, who hope you are well.\"\n\nPitt was in the room with Miss Crawley when the lad was announced, and looked very blank when his name was mentioned. The old lady had plenty of humour, and enjoyed her correct nephew's perplexity. She asked after all the people at the Rectory with great interest; and said she was thinking of paying them a visit. She praised the lad to his face, and said he was well-grown and very much improved, and that it was a pity his sisters had not some of his good looks; and finding, on inquiry, that he had taken up his quarters at an hotel, would not hear of his stopping there, but bade Mr. Bowls send for Mr. James Crawley's things instantly; \"and hark ye, Bowls,\" she added, with great graciousness, \"you will have the goodness to pay Mr. James's bill.\"\n\nShe flung Pitt a look of arch triumph, which caused that diplomatist almost to choke with envy. Much as he had ingratiated himself with his aunt, she had never yet invited him to stay under her roof, and here was a young whipper-snapper, who at first sight was made welcome there.\n\n\"I beg your pardon, sir,\" says Bowls, advancing with a profound bow; \"what 'otel, sir, shall Thomas fetch the luggage from?\"\n\n\"O, dam,\" said young James, starting up, as if in some alarm, \"I'll go.\"\n\n\"What!\" said Miss Crawley.\n\n\"The Tom Cribb's Arms,\" said James, blushing deeply.\n\nMiss Crawley burst out laughing at this title. Mr. Bowls gave one abrupt guffaw, as a confidential servant of the family, but choked the rest of the volley; the diplomatist only smiled.\n\n\"I--I didn't know any better,\" said James, looking down. \"I've never been here before; it was the coachman told me.\" The young story-teller! The fact is, that on the Southampton coach, the day previous, James Crawley had met the Tutbury Pet, who was coming to Brighton to make a match with the Rottingdean Fibber; and enchanted by the Pet's conversation, had passed the evening in company with that scientific man and his friends, at the inn in question.\n\n\"I--I'd best go and settle the score,\" James continued. \"Couldn't think of asking you, Ma'am,\" he added, generously.\n\nThis delicacy made his aunt laugh the more.\n\n\"Go and settle the bill, Bowls,\" she said, with a wave of her hand, \"and bring it to me.\"\n\nPoor lady, she did not know what she had done! \"There--there's a little dawg,\" said James, looking frightfully guilty. \"I'd best go for him. He bites footmen's calves.\"\n\nAll the party cried out with laughing at this description; even Briggs and Lady Jane, who was sitting mute during the interview between Miss Crawley and her nephew: and Bowls, without a word, quitted the room.\n\nStill, by way of punishing her elder nephew, Miss Crawley persisted in being gracious to the young Oxonian. There were no limits to her kindness or her compliments when they once began. She told Pitt he might come to dinner, and insisted that James should accompany her in her drive, and paraded him solemnly up and down the cliff, on the back seat of the barouche. During all this excursion, she condescended to say civil things to him: she quoted Italian and French poetry to the poor bewildered lad, and persisted that he was a fine scholar, and was perfectly sure he would gain a gold medal, and be a Senior Wrangler.\n\n\"Haw, haw,\" laughed James, encouraged by these compliments; \"Senior Wrangler, indeed; that's at the other shop.\"\n\n\"What is the other shop, my dear child?\" said the lady.\n\n\"Senior Wranglers at Cambridge, not Oxford,\" said the scholar, with a knowing air; and would probably have been more confidential, but that suddenly there appeared on the cliff in a tax-cart, drawn by a bang-up pony, dressed in white flannel coats, with mother-of-pearl buttons, his friends the Tutbury Pet and the Rottingdean Fibber, with three other gentlemen of their acquaintance, who all saluted poor James there in the carriage as he sate. This incident damped the ingenuous youth's spirits, and no word of yea or nay could he be induced to utter during the rest of the drive.\n\nOn his return he found his room prepared, and his portmanteau ready, and might have remarked that Mr. Bowls's countenance, when the latter conducted him to his apartments, wore a look of gravity, wonder, and compassion. But the thought of Mr. Bowls did not enter his head. He was deploring the dreadful predicament in which he found himself, in a house full of old women, jabbering French and Italian, and talking poetry to him. \"Reglarly up a tree, by jingo!\" exclaimed the modest boy, who could not face the gentlest of her sex--not even Briggs--when she began to talk to him; whereas, put him at Iffley Lock, and he could out-slang the boldest bargeman.\n\nAt dinner, James appeared choking in a white neckcloth, and had the honour of handing my Lady Jane downstairs, while Briggs and Mr. Crawley followed afterwards, conducting the old lady, with her apparatus of bundles, and shawls, and cushions. Half of Briggs's time at dinner was spent in superintending the invalid's comfort, and in cutting up chicken for her fat spaniel. James did not talk much, but he made a point of asking all the ladies to drink wine, and accepted Mr. Crawley's challenge, and consumed the greater part of a bottle of champagne which Mr. Bowls was ordered to produce in his honour. The ladies having withdrawn, and the two cousins being left together, Pitt, the ex-diplomatist, he came very communicative and friendly. He asked after James's career at college--what his prospects in life were--hoped heartily he would get on; and, in a word, was frank and amiable. James's tongue unloosed with the port, and he told his cousin his life, his prospects, his debts, his troubles at the little-go, and his rows with the proctors, filling rapidly from the bottles before him, and flying from Port to Madeira with joyous activity.\n\n\"The chief pleasure which my aunt has,\" said Mr. Crawley, filling his glass, \"is that people should do as they like in her house. This is Liberty Hall, James, and you can't do Miss Crawley a greater kindness than to do as you please, and ask for what you will. I know you have all sneered at me in the country for being a Tory. Miss Crawley is liberal enough to suit any fancy. She is a Republican in principle, and despises everything like rank or title.\"\n\n\"Why are you going to marry an Earl's daughter?\" said James.\n\n\"My dear friend, remember it is not poor Lady Jane's fault that she is well born,\" Pitt replied, with a courtly air. \"She cannot help being a lady. Besides, I am a Tory, you know.\"\n\n\"Oh, as for that,\" said Jim, \"there's nothing like old blood; no, dammy, nothing like it. I'm none of your radicals. I know what it is to be a gentleman, dammy. See the chaps in a boat-race; look at the fellers in a fight; aye, look at a dawg killing rats--which is it wins? the good-blooded ones. Get some more port, Bowls, old boy, whilst I buzz this bottle here. What was I asaying?\"\n\n\"I think you were speaking of dogs killing rats,\" Pitt remarked mildly, handing his cousin the decanter to \"buzz.\"\n\n\"Killing rats was I? Well, Pitt, are you a sporting man? Do you want to see a dawg as CAN kill a rat? If you do, come down with me to Tom Corduroy's, in Castle Street Mews, and I'll show you such a bull-terrier as--Pooh! gammon,\" cried James, bursting out laughing at his own absurdity--\"YOU don't care about a dawg or rat; it's all nonsense. I'm blest if I think you know the difference between a dog and a duck.\"\n\n\"No; by the way,\" Pitt continued with increased blandness, \"it was about blood you were talking, and the personal advantages which people derive from patrician birth. Here's the fresh bottle.\"\n\n\"Blood's the word,\" said James, gulping the ruby fluid down. \"Nothing like blood, sir, in hosses, dawgs, AND men. Why, only last term, just before I was rusticated, that is, I mean just before I had the measles, ha, ha--there was me and Ringwood of Christchurch, Bob Ringwood, Lord Cinqbars' son, having our beer at the Bell at Blenheim, when the Banbury bargeman offered to fight either of us for a bowl of punch. I couldn't. My arm was in a sling; couldn't even take the drag down--a brute of a mare of mine had fell with me only two days before, out with the Abingdon, and I thought my arm was broke. Well, sir, I couldn't finish him, but Bob had his coat off at once--he stood up to the Banbury man for three minutes, and polished him off in four rounds easy. Gad, how he did drop, sir, and what was it? Blood, sir, all blood.\"\n\n\"You don't drink, James,\" the ex-attache continued. \"In my time at Oxford, the men passed round the bottle a little quicker than you young fellows seem to do.\"\n\n\"Come, come,\" said James, putting his hand to his nose and winking at his cousin with a pair of vinous eyes, \"no jokes, old boy; no trying it on on me. You want to trot me out, but it's no go. In vino veritas, old boy. Mars, Bacchus, Apollo virorum, hey? I wish my aunt would send down some of this to the governor; it's a precious good tap.\"\n\n\"You had better ask her,\" Machiavel continued, \"or make the best of your time now. What says the bard? 'Nunc vino pellite curas, Cras ingens iterabimus aequor,'\" and the Bacchanalian, quoting the above with a House of Commons air, tossed off nearly a thimbleful of wine with an immense flourish of his glass.\n\nAt the Rectory, when the bottle of port wine was opened after dinner, the young ladies had each a glass from a bottle of currant wine. Mrs. Bute took one glass of port, honest James had a couple commonly, but as his father grew very sulky if he made further inroads on the bottle, the good lad generally refrained from trying for more, and subsided either into the currant wine, or to some private gin-and-water in the stables, which he enjoyed in the company of the coachman and his pipe. At Oxford, the quantity of wine was unlimited, but the quality was inferior: but when quantity and quality united as at his aunt's house, James showed that he could appreciate them indeed; and hardly needed any of his cousin's encouragement in draining off the second bottle supplied by Mr. Bowls.\n\nWhen the time for coffee came, however, and for a return to the ladies, of whom he stood in awe, the young gentleman's agreeable frankness left him, and he relapsed into his usual surly timidity; contenting himself by saying yes and no, by scowling at Lady Jane, and by upsetting one cup of coffee during the evening.\n\nIf he did not speak he yawned in a pitiable manner, and his presence threw a damp upon the modest proceedings of the evening, for Miss Crawley and Lady Jane at their piquet, and Miss Briggs at her work, felt that his eyes were wildly fixed on them, and were uneasy under that maudlin look.\n\n\"He seems a very silent, awkward, bashful lad,\" said Miss Crawley to Mr. Pitt.\n\n\"He is more communicative in men's society than with ladies,\" Machiavel dryly replied: perhaps rather disappointed that the port wine had not made Jim speak more.\n\nHe had spent the early part of the next morning in writing home to his mother a most flourishing account of his reception by Miss Crawley. But ah! he little knew what evils the day was bringing for him, and how short his reign of favour was destined to be. A circumstance which Jim had forgotten--a trivial but fatal circumstance--had taken place at the Cribb's Arms on the night before he had come to his aunt's house. It was no other than this--Jim, who was always of a generous disposition, and when in his cups especially hospitable, had in the course of the night treated the Tutbury champion and the Rottingdean man, and their friends, twice or thrice to the refreshment of gin-and-water--so that no less than eighteen glasses of that fluid at eightpence per glass were charged in Mr. James Crawley's bill. It was not the amount of eightpences, but the quantity of gin which told fatally against poor James's character, when his aunt's butler, Mr. Bowls, went down at his mistress's request to pay the young gentleman's bill. The landlord, fearing lest the account should be refused altogether, swore solemnly that the young gent had consumed personally every farthing's worth of the liquor: and Bowls paid the bill finally, and showed it on his return home to Mrs. Firkin, who was shocked at the frightful prodigality of gin; and took the bill to Miss Briggs as accountant-general; who thought it her duty to mention the circumstance to her principal, Miss Crawley.\n\nHad he drunk a dozen bottles of claret, the old spinster could have pardoned him. Mr. Fox and Mr. Sheridan drank claret. Gentlemen drank claret. But eighteen glasses of gin consumed among boxers in an ignoble pot-house--it was an odious crime and not to be pardoned readily. Everything went against the lad: he came home perfumed from the stables, whither he had been to pay his dog Towzer a visit--and whence he was going to take his friend out for an airing, when he met Miss Crawley and her wheezy Blenheim spaniel, which Towzer would have eaten up had not the Blenheim fled squealing to the protection of Miss Briggs, while the atrocious master of the bull-dog stood laughing at the horrible persecution.\n\nThis day too the unlucky boy's modesty had likewise forsaken him. He was lively and facetious at dinner. During the repast he levelled one or two jokes against Pitt Crawley: he drank as much wine as upon the previous day; and going quite unsuspiciously to the drawing-room, began to entertain the ladies there with some choice Oxford stories. He described the different pugilistic qualities of Molyneux and Dutch Sam, offered playfully to give Lady Jane the odds upon the Tutbury Pet against the Rottingdean man, or take them, as her Ladyship chose: and crowned the pleasantry by proposing to back himself against his cousin Pitt Crawley, either with or without the gloves. \"And that's a fair offer, my buck,\" he said, with a loud laugh, slapping Pitt on the shoulder, \"and my father told me to make it too, and he'll go halves in the bet, ha, ha!\" So saying, the engaging youth nodded knowingly at poor Miss Briggs, and pointed his thumb over his shoulder at Pitt Crawley in a jocular and exulting manner.\n\nPitt was not pleased altogether perhaps, but still not unhappy in the main. Poor Jim had his laugh out: and staggered across the room with his aunt's candle, when the old lady moved to retire, and offered to salute her with the blandest tipsy smile: and he took his own leave and went upstairs to his bedroom perfectly satisfied with himself, and with a pleased notion that his aunt's money would be left to him in preference to his father and all the rest of the family.\n\nOnce up in the bedroom, one would have thought he could not make matters worse; and yet this unlucky boy did. The moon was shining very pleasantly out on the sea, and Jim, attracted to the window by the romantic appearance of the ocean and the heavens, thought he would further enjoy them while smoking. Nobody would smell the tobacco, he thought, if he cunningly opened the window and kept his head and pipe in the fresh air. This he did: but being in an excited state, poor Jim had forgotten that his door was open all this time, so that the breeze blowing inwards and a fine thorough draught being established, the clouds of tobacco were carried downstairs, and arrived with quite undiminished fragrance to Miss Crawley and Miss Briggs.\n\nThe pipe of tobacco finished the business: and the Bute-Crawleys never knew how many thousand pounds it cost them. Firkin rushed downstairs to Bowls who was reading out the \"Fire and the Frying Pan\" to his aide-de-camp in a loud and ghostly voice. The dreadful secret was told to him by Firkin with so frightened a look, that for the first moment Mr. Bowls and his young man thought that robbers were in the house, the legs of whom had probably been discovered by the woman under Miss Crawley's bed. When made aware of the fact, however--to rush upstairs at three steps at a time to enter the unconscious James's apartment, calling out, \"Mr. James,\" in a voice stifled with alarm, and to cry, \"For Gawd's sake, sir, stop that 'ere pipe,\" was the work of a minute with Mr. Bowls. \"O, Mr. James, what 'AVE you done!\" he said in a voice of the deepest pathos, as he threw the implement out of the window. \"What 'ave you done, sir! Missis can't abide 'em.\"\n\n\"Missis needn't smoke,\" said James with a frantic misplaced laugh, and thought the whole matter an excellent joke. But his feelings were very different in the morning, when Mr. Bowls's young man, who operated upon Mr. James's boots, and brought him his hot water to shave that beard which he was so anxiously expecting, handed a note in to Mr. James in bed, in the handwriting of Miss Briggs.\n\n\"Dear sir,\" it said, \"Miss Crawley has passed an exceedingly disturbed night, owing to the shocking manner in which the house has been polluted by tobacco; Miss Crawley bids me say she regrets that she is too unwell to see you before you go--and above all that she ever induced you to remove from the ale-house, where she is sure you will be much more comfortable during the rest of your stay at Brighton.\"\n\nAnd herewith honest James's career as a candidate for his aunt's favour ended. He had in fact, and without knowing it, done what he menaced to do. He had fought his cousin Pitt with the gloves.\n\nWhere meanwhile was he who had been once first favourite for this race for money? Becky and Rawdon, as we have seen, were come together after Waterloo, and were passing the winter of 1815 at Paris in great splendour and gaiety. Rebecca was a good economist, and the price poor Jos Sedley had paid for her two horses was in itself sufficient to keep their little establishment afloat for a year, at the least; there was no occasion to turn into money \"my pistols, the same which I shot Captain Marker,\" or the gold dressing-case, or the cloak lined with sable. Becky had it made into a pelisse for herself, in which she rode in the Bois de Boulogne to the admiration of all: and you should have seen the scene between her and her delighted husband, whom she rejoined after the army had entered Cambray, and when she unsewed herself, and let out of her dress all those watches, knick-knacks, bank-notes, cheques, and valuables, which she had secreted in the wadding, previous to her meditated flight from Brussels! Tufto was charmed, and Rawdon roared with delighted laughter, and swore that she was better than any play he ever saw, by Jove. And the way in which she jockeyed Jos, and which she described with infinite fun, carried up his delight to a pitch of quite insane enthusiasm. He believed in his wife as much as the French soldiers in Napoleon.\n\nHer success in Paris was remarkable. All the French ladies voted her charming. She spoke their language admirably. She adopted at once their grace, their liveliness, their manner. Her husband was stupid certainly--all English are stupid--and, besides, a dull husband at Paris is always a point in a lady's favour. He was the heir of the rich and spirituelle Miss Crawley, whose house had been open to so many of the French noblesse during the emigration. They received the colonel's wife in their own hotels--\"Why,\" wrote a great lady to Miss Crawley, who had bought her lace and trinkets at the Duchess's own price, and given her many a dinner during the pinching times after the Revolution--\"Why does not our dear Miss come to her nephew and niece, and her attached friends in Paris? All the world raffoles of the charming Mistress and her espiegle beauty. Yes, we see in her the grace, the charm, the wit of our dear friend Miss Crawley! The King took notice of her yesterday at the Tuileries, and we are all jealous of the attention which Monsieur pays her. If you could have seen the spite of a certain stupid Miladi Bareacres (whose eagle-beak and toque and feathers may be seen peering over the heads of all assemblies) when Madame, the Duchess of Angouleme, the august daughter and companion of kings, desired especially to be presented to Mrs. Crawley, as your dear daughter and protegee, and thanked her in the name of France, for all your benevolence towards our unfortunates during their exile! She is of all the societies, of all the balls--of the balls--yes--of the dances, no; and yet how interesting and pretty this fair creature looks surrounded by the homage of the men, and so soon to be a mother! To hear her speak of you, her protectress, her mother, would bring tears to the eyes of ogres. How she loves you! how we all love our admirable, our respectable Miss Crawley!\"\n\nIt is to be feared that this letter of the Parisian great lady did not by any means advance Mrs. Becky's interest with her admirable, her respectable, relative. On the contrary, the fury of the old spinster was beyond bounds, when she found what was Rebecca's situation, and how audaciously she had made use of Miss Crawley's name, to get an entree into Parisian society. Too much shaken in mind and body to compose a letter in the French language in reply to that of her correspondent, she dictated to Briggs a furious answer in her own native tongue, repudiating Mrs. Rawdon Crawley altogether, and warning the public to beware of her as a most artful and dangerous person. But as Madame the Duchess of X--had only been twenty years in England, she did not understand a single word of the language, and contented herself by informing Mrs. Rawdon Crawley at their next meeting, that she had received a charming letter from that chere Mees, and that it was full of benevolent things for Mrs. Crawley, who began seriously to have hopes that the spinster would relent.\n\nMeanwhile, she was the gayest and most admired of Englishwomen: and had a little European congress on her reception-night. Prussians and Cossacks, Spanish and English--all the world was at Paris during this famous winter: to have seen the stars and cordons in Rebecca's humble saloon would have made all Baker Street pale with envy. Famous warriors rode by her carriage in the Bois, or crowded her modest little box at the Opera. Rawdon was in the highest spirits. There were no duns in Paris as yet: there were parties every day at Very's or Beauvilliers'; play was plentiful and his luck good. Tufto perhaps was sulky. Mrs. Tufto had come over to Paris at her own invitation, and besides this contretemps, there were a score of generals now round Becky's chair, and she might take her choice of a dozen bouquets when she went to the play. Lady Bareacres and the chiefs of the English society, stupid and irreproachable females, writhed with anguish at the success of the little upstart Becky, whose poisoned jokes quivered and rankled in their chaste breasts. But she had all the men on her side. She fought the women with indomitable courage, and they could not talk scandal in any tongue but their own.\n\nSo in fetes, pleasures, and prosperity, the winter of 1815-16 passed away with Mrs. Rawdon Crawley, who accommodated herself to polite life as if her ancestors had been people of fashion for centuries past--and who from her wit, talent, and energy, indeed merited a place of honour in Vanity Fair. In the early spring of 1816, Galignani's Journal contained the following announcement in an interesting corner of the paper: \"On the 26th of March--the Lady of Lieutenant-Colonel Crawley, of the Life Guards Green--of a son and heir.\"\n\nThis event was copied into the London papers, out of which Miss Briggs read the statement to Miss Crawley, at breakfast, at Brighton. The intelligence, expected as it might have been, caused a crisis in the affairs of the Crawley family. The spinster's rage rose to its height, and sending instantly for Pitt, her nephew, and for the Lady Southdown, from Brunswick Square, she requested an immediate celebration of the marriage which had been so long pending between the two families. And she announced that it was her intention to allow the young couple a thousand a year during her lifetime, at the expiration of which the bulk of her property would be settled upon her nephew and her dear niece, Lady Jane Crawley. Waxy came down to ratify the deeds--Lord Southdown gave away his sister--she was married by a Bishop, and not by the Rev. Bartholomew Irons--to the disappointment of the irregular prelate.\n\nWhen they were married, Pitt would have liked to take a hymeneal tour with his bride, as became people of their condition. But the affection of the old lady towards Lady Jane had grown so strong, that she fairly owned she could not part with her favourite. Pitt and his wife came therefore and lived with Miss Crawley: and (greatly to the annoyance of poor Pitt, who conceived himself a most injured character--being subject to the humours of his aunt on one side, and of his mother-in-law on the other). Lady Southdown, from her neighbouring house, reigned over the whole family--Pitt, Lady Jane, Miss Crawley, Briggs, Bowls, Firkin, and all. She pitilessly dosed them with her tracts and her medicine, she dismissed Creamer, she installed Rodgers, and soon stripped Miss Crawley of even the semblance of authority. The poor soul grew so timid that she actually left off bullying Briggs any more, and clung to her niece, more fond and terrified every day. Peace to thee, kind and selfish, vain and generous old heathen!--We shall see thee no more. Let us hope that Lady Jane supported her kindly, and led her with gentle hand out of the busy struggle of Vanity Fair.\n\nWidow and Mother\nThe news of the great fights of Quatre Bras and Waterloo reached England at the same time. The Gazette first published the result of the two battles; at which glorious intelligence all England thrilled with triumph and fear. Particulars then followed; and after the announcement of the victories came the list of the wounded and the slain. Who can tell the dread with which that catalogue was opened and read! Fancy, at every village and homestead almost through the three kingdoms, the great news coming of the battles in Flanders, and the feelings of exultation and gratitude, bereavement and sickening dismay, when the lists of the regimental losses were gone through, and it became known whether the dear friend and relative had escaped or fallen. Anybody who will take the trouble of looking back to a file of the newspapers of the time, must, even now, feel at second-hand this breathless pause of expectation. The lists of casualties are carried on from day to day: you stop in the midst as in a story which is to be continued in our next. Think what the feelings must have been as those papers followed each other fresh from the press; and if such an interest could be felt in our country, and about a battle where but twenty thousand of our people were engaged, think of the condition of Europe for twenty years before, where people were fighting, not by thousands, but by millions; each one of whom as he struck his enemy wounded horribly some other innocent heart far away.\n\nThe news which that famous Gazette brought to the Osbornes gave a dreadful shock to the family and its chief. The girls indulged unrestrained in their grief. The gloom-stricken old father was still more borne down by his fate and sorrow. He strove to think that a judgment was on the boy for his disobedience. He dared not own that the severity of the sentence frightened him, and that its fulfilment had come too soon upon his curses. Sometimes a shuddering terror struck him, as if he had been the author of the doom which he had called down on his son. There was a chance before of reconciliation. The boy's wife might have died; or he might have come back and said, Father I have sinned. But there was no hope now. He stood on the other side of the gulf impassable, haunting his parent with sad eyes. He remembered them once before so in a fever, when every one thought the lad was dying, and he lay on his bed speechless, and gazing with a dreadful gloom. Good God! how the father clung to the doctor then, and with what a sickening anxiety he followed him: what a weight of grief was off his mind when, after the crisis of the fever, the lad recovered, and looked at his father once more with eyes that recognised him. But now there was no help or cure, or chance of reconcilement: above all, there were no humble words to soothe vanity outraged and furious, or bring to its natural flow the poisoned, angry blood. And it is hard to say which pang it was that tore the proud father's heart most keenly--that his son should have gone out of the reach of his forgiveness, or that the apology which his own pride expected should have escaped him.\n\nWhatever his sensations might have been, however, the stern old man would have no confidant. He never mentioned his son's name to his daughters; but ordered the elder to place all the females of the establishment in mourning; and desired that the male servants should be similarly attired in deep black. All parties and entertainments, of course, were to be put off. No communications were made to his future son-in-law, whose marriage-day had been fixed: but there was enough in Mr. Osborne's appearance to prevent Mr. Bullock from making any inquiries, or in any way pressing forward that ceremony. He and the ladies whispered about it under their voices in the drawing-room sometimes, whither the father never came. He remained constantly in his own study; the whole front part of the house being closed until some time after the completion of the general mourning.\n\nAbout three weeks after the 18th of June, Mr. Osborne's acquaintance, Sir William Dobbin, called at Mr. Osborne's house in Russell Square, with a very pale and agitated face, and insisted upon seeing that gentleman. Ushered into his room, and after a few words, which neither the speaker nor the host understood, the former produced from an inclosure a letter sealed with a large red seal. \"My son, Major Dobbin,\" the Alderman said, with some hesitation, \"despatched me a letter by an officer of the --th, who arrived in town to-day. My son's letter contains one for you, Osborne.\" The Alderman placed the letter on the table, and Osborne stared at him for a moment or two in silence. His looks frightened the ambassador, who after looking guiltily for a little time at the grief-stricken man, hurried away without another word.\n\nThe letter was in George's well-known bold handwriting. It was that one which he had written before daybreak on the 16th of June, and just before he took leave of Amelia. The great red seal was emblazoned with the sham coat of arms which Osborne had assumed from the Peerage, with \"Pax in bello\" for a motto; that of the ducal house with which the vain old man tried to fancy himself connected. The hand that signed it would never hold pen or sword more. The very seal that sealed it had been robbed from George's dead body as it lay on the field of battle. The father knew nothing of this, but sat and looked at the letter in terrified vacancy. He almost fell when he went to open it.\n\nHave you ever had a difference with a dear friend? How his letters, written in the period of love and confidence, sicken and rebuke you! What a dreary mourning it is to dwell upon those vehement protests of dead affection! What lying epitaphs they make over the corpse of love! What dark, cruel comments upon Life and Vanities! Most of us have got or written drawers full of them. They are closet-skeletons which we keep and shun. Osborne trembled long before the letter from his dead son.\n\nThe poor boy's letter did not say much. He had been too proud to acknowledge the tenderness which his heart felt. He only said, that on the eve of a great battle, he wished to bid his father farewell, and solemnly to implore his good offices for the wife--it might be for the child--whom he left behind him. He owned with contrition that his irregularities and his extravagance had already wasted a large part of his mother's little fortune. He thanked his father for his former generous conduct; and he promised him that if he fell on the field or survived it, he would act in a manner worthy of the name of George Osborne.\n\nHis English habit, pride, awkwardness perhaps, had prevented him from saying more. His father could not see the kiss George had placed on the superscription of his letter. Mr. Osborne dropped it with the bitterest, deadliest pang of balked affection and revenge. His son was still beloved and unforgiven.\n\nAbout two months afterwards, however, as the young ladies of the family went to church with their father, they remarked how he took a different seat from that which he usually occupied when he chose to attend divine worship; and that from his cushion opposite, he looked up at the wall over their heads. This caused the young women likewise to gaze in the direction towards which their father's gloomy eyes pointed: and they saw an elaborate monument upon the wall, where Britannia was represented weeping over an urn, and a broken sword and a couchant lion indicated that the piece of sculpture had been erected in honour of a deceased warrior. The sculptors of those days had stocks of such funereal emblems in hand; as you may see still on the walls of St. Paul's, which are covered with hundreds of these braggart heathen allegories. There was a constant demand for them during the first fifteen years of the present century.\n\nUnder the memorial in question were emblazoned the well-known and pompous Osborne arms; and the inscription said, that the monument was \"Sacred to the memory of George Osborne, Junior, Esq., late a Captain in his Majesty's --th regiment of foot, who fell on the 18th of June, 1815, aged 28 years, while fighting for his king and country in the glorious victory of Waterloo. Dulce et decorum est pro patria mori.\"\n\nThe sight of that stone agitated the nerves of the sisters so much, that Miss Maria was compelled to leave the church. The congregation made way respectfully for those sobbing girls clothed in deep black, and pitied the stern old father seated opposite the memorial of the dead soldier. \"Will he forgive Mrs. George?\" the girls said to themselves as soon as their ebullition of grief was over. Much conversation passed too among the acquaintances of the Osborne family, who knew of the rupture between the son and father caused by the former's marriage, as to the chance of a reconciliation with the young widow. There were bets among the gentlemen both about Russell Square and in the City.\n\nIf the sisters had any anxiety regarding the possible recognition of Amelia as a daughter of the family, it was increased presently, and towards the end of the autumn, by their father's announcement that he was going abroad. He did not say whither, but they knew at once that his steps would be turned towards Belgium, and were aware that George's widow was still in Brussels. They had pretty accurate news indeed of poor Amelia from Lady Dobbin and her daughters. Our honest Captain had been promoted in consequence of the death of the second Major of the regiment on the field; and the brave O'Dowd, who had distinguished himself greatly here as upon all occasions where he had a chance to show his coolness and valour, was a Colonel and Companion of the Bath.\n\nVery many of the brave --th, who had suffered severely upon both days of action, were still at Brussels in the autumn, recovering of their wounds. The city was a vast military hospital for months after the great battles; and as men and officers began to rally from their hurts, the gardens and places of public resort swarmed with maimed warriors, old and young, who, just rescued out of death, fell to gambling, and gaiety, and love-making, as people of Vanity Fair will do. Mr. Osborne found out some of the --th easily. He knew their uniform quite well, and had been used to follow all the promotions and exchanges in the regiment, and loved to talk about it and its officers as if he had been one of the number. On the day after his arrival at Brussels, and as he issued from his hotel, which faced the park, he saw a soldier in the well-known facings, reposing on a stone bench in the garden, and went and sate down trembling by the wounded convalescent man.\n\n\"Were you in Captain Osborne's company?\" he said, and added, after a pause, \"he was my son, sir.\"\n\nThe man was not of the Captain's company, but he lifted up his unwounded arm and touched his cap sadly and respectfully to the haggard broken-spirited gentleman who questioned him. \"The whole army didn't contain a finer or a better officer,\" the soldier said. \"The Sergeant of the Captain's company (Captain Raymond had it now), was in town, though, and was just well of a shot in the shoulder. His honour might see him if he liked, who could tell him anything he wanted to know about--about the --th's actions. But his honour had seen Major Dobbin, no doubt, the brave Captain's great friend; and Mrs. Osborne, who was here too, and had been very bad, he heard everybody say. They say she was out of her mind like for six weeks or more. But your honour knows all about that--and asking your pardon\"--the man added.\n\nOsborne put a guinea into the soldier's hand, and told him he should have another if he would bring the Sergeant to the Hotel du Parc; a promise which very soon brought the desired officer to Mr. Osborne's presence. And the first soldier went away; and after telling a comrade or two how Captain Osborne's father was arrived, and what a free-handed generous gentleman he was, they went and made good cheer with drink and feasting, as long as the guineas lasted which had come from the proud purse of the mourning old father.\n\nIn the Sergeant's company, who was also just convalescent, Osborne made the journey of Waterloo and Quatre Bras, a journey which thousands of his countrymen were then taking. He took the Sergeant with him in his carriage, and went through both fields under his guidance. He saw the point of the road where the regiment marched into action on the 16th, and the slope down which they drove the French cavalry who were pressing on the retreating Belgians. There was the spot where the noble Captain cut down the French officer who was grappling with the young Ensign for the colours, the Colour-Sergeants having been shot down. Along this road they retreated on the next day, and here was the bank at which the regiment bivouacked under the rain of the night of the seventeenth. Further on was the position which they took and held during the day, forming time after time to receive the charge of the enemy's horsemen and lying down under the shelter of the bank from the furious French cannonade. And it was at this declivity when at evening the whole English line received the order to advance, as the enemy fell back after his last charge, that the Captain, hurraying and rushing down the hill waving his sword, received a shot and fell dead. \"It was Major Dobbin who took back the Captain's body to Brussels,\" the Sergeant said, in a low voice, \"and had him buried, as your honour knows.\" The peasants and relic-hunters about the place were screaming round the pair, as the soldier told his story, offering for sale all sorts of mementoes of the fight, crosses, and epaulets, and shattered cuirasses, and eagles.\n\nOsborne gave a sumptuous reward to the Sergeant when he parted with him, after having visited the scenes of his son's last exploits. His burial-place he had already seen. Indeed, he had driven thither immediately after his arrival at Brussels. George's body lay in the pretty burial-ground of Laeken, near the city; in which place, having once visited it on a party of pleasure, he had lightly expressed a wish to have his grave made. And there the young officer was laid by his friend, in the unconsecrated corner of the garden, separated by a little hedge from the temples and towers and plantations of flowers and shrubs, under which the Roman Catholic dead repose. It seemed a humiliation to old Osborne to think that his son, an English gentleman, a captain in the famous British army, should not be found worthy to lie in ground where mere foreigners were buried. Which of us is there can tell how much vanity lurks in our warmest regard for others, and how selfish our love is? Old Osborne did not speculate much upon the mingled nature of his feelings, and how his instinct and selfishness were combating together. He firmly believed that everything he did was right, that he ought on all occasions to have his own way--and like the sting of a wasp or serpent his hatred rushed out armed and poisonous against anything like opposition. He was proud of his hatred as of everything else. Always to be right, always to trample forward, and never to doubt, are not these the great qualities with which dullness takes the lead in the world?\n\nAs after the drive to Waterloo, Mr. Osborne's carriage was nearing the gates of the city at sunset, they met another open barouche, in which were a couple of ladies and a gentleman, and by the side of which an officer was riding. Osborne gave a start back, and the Sergeant, seated with him, cast a look of surprise at his neighbour, as he touched his cap to the officer, who mechanically returned his salute. It was Amelia, with the lame young Ensign by her side, and opposite to her her faithful friend Mrs. O'Dowd. It was Amelia, but how changed from the fresh and comely girl Osborne knew. Her face was white and thin. Her pretty brown hair was parted under a widow's cap--the poor child. Her eyes were fixed, and looking nowhere. They stared blank in the face of Osborne, as the carriages crossed each other, but she did not know him; nor did he recognise her, until looking up, he saw Dobbin riding by her: and then he knew who it was. He hated her. He did not know how much until he saw her there. When her carriage had passed on, he turned and stared at the Sergeant, with a curse and defiance in his eye cast at his companion, who could not help looking at him--as much as to say \"How dare you look at me? Damn you! I do hate her. It is she who has tumbled my hopes and all my pride down.\" \"Tell the scoundrel to drive on quick,\" he shouted with an oath, to the lackey on the box. A minute afterwards, a horse came clattering over the pavement behind Osborne's carriage, and Dobbin rode up. His thoughts had been elsewhere as the carriages passed each other, and it was not until he had ridden some paces forward, that he remembered it was Osborne who had just passed him. Then he turned to examine if the sight of her father-in-law had made any impression on Amelia, but the poor girl did not know who had passed. Then William, who daily used to accompany her in his drives, taking out his watch, made some excuse about an engagement which he suddenly recollected, and so rode off. She did not remark that either: but sate looking before her, over the homely landscape towards the woods in the distance, by which George marched away.\n\n\"Mr. Osborne, Mr. Osborne!\" cried Dobbin, as he rode up and held out his hand. Osborne made no motion to take it, but shouted out once more and with another curse to his servant to drive on.\n\nDobbin laid his hand on the carriage side. \"I will see you, sir,\" he said. \"I have a message for you.\"\n\n\"From that woman?\" said Osborne, fiercely.\n\n\"No,\" replied the other, \"from your son\"; at which Osborne fell back into the corner of his carriage, and Dobbin allowing it to pass on, rode close behind it, and so through the town until they reached Mr. Osborne's hotel, and without a word. There he followed Osborne up to his apartments. George had often been in the rooms; they were the lodgings which the Crawleys had occupied during their stay in Brussels.\n\n\"Pray, have you any commands for me, Captain Dobbin, or, I beg your pardon, I should say MAJOR Dobbin, since better men than you are dead, and you step into their SHOES?\" said Mr. Osborne, in that sarcastic tone which he sometimes was pleased to assume.\n\n\"Better men ARE dead,\" Dobbin replied. \"I want to speak to you about one.\"\n\n\"Make it short, sir,\" said the other with an oath, scowling at his visitor.\n\n\"I am here as his closest friend,\" the Major resumed, \"and the executor of his will. He made it before he went into action. Are you aware how small his means are, and of the straitened circumstances of his widow?\"\n\n\"I don't know his widow, sir,\" Osborne said. \"Let her go back to her father.\" But the gentleman whom he addressed was determined to remain in good temper, and went on without heeding the interruption.\n\n\"Do you know, sir, Mrs. Osborne's condition? Her life and her reason almost have been shaken by the blow which has fallen on her. It is very doubtful whether she will rally. There is a chance left for her, however, and it is about this I came to speak to you. She will be a mother soon. Will you visit the parent's offence upon the child's head? or will you forgive the child for poor George's sake?\"\n\nOsborne broke out into a rhapsody of self-praise and imprecations;--by the first, excusing himself to his own conscience for his conduct; by the second, exaggerating the undutifulness of George. No father in all England could have behaved more generously to a son, who had rebelled against him wickedly. He had died without even so much as confessing he was wrong. Let him take the consequences of his undutifulness and folly. As for himself, Mr. Osborne, he was a man of his word. He had sworn never to speak to that woman, or to recognize her as his son's wife. \"And that's what you may tell her,\" he concluded with an oath; \"and that's what I will stick to to the last day of my life.\"\n\nThere was no hope from that quarter then. The widow must live on her slender pittance, or on such aid as Jos could give her. \"I might tell her, and she would not heed it,\" thought Dobbin, sadly: for the poor girl's thoughts were not here at all since her catastrophe, and, stupefied under the pressure of her sorrow, good and evil were alike indifferent to her.\n\nSo, indeed, were even friendship and kindness. She received them both uncomplainingly, and having accepted them, relapsed into her grief.\n\nSuppose some twelve months after the above conversation took place to have passed in the life of our poor Amelia. She has spent the first portion of that time in a sorrow so profound and pitiable, that we who have been watching and describing some of the emotions of that weak and tender heart, must draw back in the presence of the cruel grief under which it is bleeding. Tread silently round the hapless couch of the poor prostrate soul. Shut gently the door of the dark chamber wherein she suffers, as those kind people did who nursed her through the first months of her pain, and never left her until heaven had sent her consolation. A day came--of almost terrified delight and wonder--when the poor widowed girl pressed a child upon her breast--a child, with the eyes of George who was gone--a little boy, as beautiful as a cherub. What a miracle it was to hear its first cry! How she laughed and wept over it--how love, and hope, and prayer woke again in her bosom as the baby nestled there. She was safe. The doctors who attended her, and had feared for her life or for her brain, had waited anxiously for this crisis before they could pronounce that either was secure. It was worth the long months of doubt and dread which the persons who had constantly been with her had passed, to see her eyes once more beaming tenderly upon them.\n\nOur friend Dobbin was one of them. It was he who brought her back to England and to her mother's house; when Mrs. O'Dowd, receiving a peremptory summons from her Colonel, had been forced to quit her patient. To see Dobbin holding the infant, and to hear Amelia's laugh of triumph as she watched him, would have done any man good who had a sense of humour. William was the godfather of the child, and exerted his ingenuity in the purchase of cups, spoons, pap-boats, and corals for this little Christian.\n\nHow his mother nursed him, and dressed him, and lived upon him; how she drove away all nurses, and would scarce allow any hand but her own to touch him; how she considered that the greatest favour she could confer upon his godfather, Major Dobbin, was to allow the Major occasionally to dandle him, need not be told here. This child was her being. Her existence was a maternal caress. She enveloped the feeble and unconscious creature with love and worship. It was her life which the baby drank in from her bosom. Of nights, and when alone, she had stealthy and intense raptures of motherly love, such as God's marvellous care has awarded to the female instinct--joys how far higher and lower than reason--blind beautiful devotions which only women's hearts know. It was William Dobbin's task to muse upon these movements of Amelia's, and to watch her heart; and if his love made him divine almost all the feelings which agitated it, alas! he could see with a fatal perspicuity that there was no place there for him. And so, gently, he bore his fate, knowing it, and content to bear it.\n\nI suppose Amelia's father and mother saw through the intentions of the Major, and were not ill-disposed to encourage him; for Dobbin visited their house daily, and stayed for hours with them, or with Amelia, or with the honest landlord, Mr. Clapp, and his family. He brought, on one pretext or another, presents to everybody, and almost every day; and went, with the landlord's little girl, who was rather a favourite with Amelia, by the name of Major Sugarplums. It was this little child who commonly acted as mistress of the ceremonies to introduce him to Mrs. Osborne. She laughed one day when Major Sugarplums' cab drove up to Fulham, and he descended from it, bringing out a wooden horse, a drum, a trumpet, and other warlike toys, for little Georgy, who was scarcely six months old, and for whom the articles in question were entirely premature.\n\nThe child was asleep. \"Hush,\" said Amelia, annoyed, perhaps, at the creaking of the Major's boots; and she held out her hand; smiling because William could not take it until he had rid himself of his cargo of toys. \"Go downstairs, little Mary,\" said he presently to the child, \"I want to speak to Mrs. Osborne.\" She looked up rather astonished, and laid down the infant on its bed.\n\n\"I am come to say good-bye, Amelia,\" said he, taking her slender little white hand gently.\n\n\"Good-bye? and where are you going?\" she said, with a smile.\n\n\"Send the letters to the agents,\" he said; \"they will forward them; for you will write to me, won't you? I shall be away a long time.\"\n\n\"I'll write to you about Georgy,\" she said. \"Dear William, how good you have been to him and to me. Look at him. Isn't he like an angel?\"\n\nThe little pink hands of the child closed mechanically round the honest soldier's finger, and Amelia looked up in his face with bright maternal pleasure. The cruellest looks could not have wounded him more than that glance of hopeless kindness. He bent over the child and mother. He could not speak for a moment. And it was only with all his strength that he could force himself to say a God bless you. \"God bless you,\" said Amelia, and held up her face and kissed him.\n\n\"Hush! Don't wake Georgy!\" she added, as William Dobbin went to the door with heavy steps. She did not hear the noise of his cab-wheels as he drove away: she was looking at the child, who was laughing in his sleep.\n\nHow to Live Well on Nothing a Year\nI suppose there is no man in this Vanity Fair of ours so little observant as not to think sometimes about the worldly affairs of his acquaintances, or so extremely charitable as not to wonder how his neighbour Jones, or his neighbour Smith, can make both ends meet at the end of the year. With the utmost regard for the family, for instance (for I dine with them twice or thrice in the season), I cannot but own that the appearance of the Jenkinses in the park, in the large barouche with the grenadier-footmen, will surprise and mystify me to my dying day: for though I know the equipage is only jobbed, and all the Jenkins people are on board wages, yet those three men and the carriage must represent an expense of six hundred a year at the very least--and then there are the splendid dinners, the two boys at Eton, the prize governess and masters for the girls, the trip abroad, or to Eastbourne or Worthing, in the autumn, the annual ball with a supper from Gunter's (who, by the way, supplies most of the first-rate dinners which J. gives, as I know very well, having been invited to one of them to fill a vacant place, when I saw at once that these repasts are very superior to the common run of entertainments for which the humbler sort of J.'s acquaintances get cards)--who, I say, with the most good-natured feelings in the world, can help wondering how the Jenkinses make out matters? What is Jenkins? We all know--Commissioner of the Tape and Sealing Wax Office, with 1200 pounds a year for a salary. Had his wife a private fortune? Pooh!--Miss Flint--one of eleven children of a small squire in Buckinghamshire. All she ever gets from her family is a turkey at Christmas, in exchange for which she has to board two or three of her sisters in the off season, and lodge and feed her brothers when they come to town. How does Jenkins balance his income? I say, as every friend of his must say, How is it that he has not been outlawed long since, and that he ever came back (as he did to the surprise of everybody) last year from Boulogne?\n\n\"I\" is here introduced to personify the world in general--the Mrs. Grundy of each respected reader's private circle--every one of whom can point to some families of his acquaintance who live nobody knows how. Many a glass of wine have we all of us drunk, I have very little doubt, hob-and-nobbing with the hospitable giver and wondering how the deuce he paid for it.\n\nSome three or four years after his stay in Paris, when Rawdon Crawley and his wife were established in a very small comfortable house in Curzon Street, May Fair, there was scarcely one of the numerous friends whom they entertained at dinner that did not ask the above question regarding them. The novelist, it has been said before, knows everything, and as I am in a situation to be able to tell the public how Crawley and his wife lived without any income, may I entreat the public newspapers which are in the habit of extracting portions of the various periodical works now published not to reprint the following exact narrative and calculations--of which I ought, as the discoverer (and at some expense, too), to have the benefit? My son, I would say, were I blessed with a child--you may by deep inquiry and constant intercourse with him learn how a man lives comfortably on nothing a year. But it is best not to be intimate with gentlemen of this profession and to take the calculations at second hand, as you do logarithms, for to work them yourself, depend upon it, will cost you something considerable.\n\nOn nothing per annum then, and during a course of some two or three years, of which we can afford to give but a very brief history, Crawley and his wife lived very happily and comfortably at Paris. It was in this period that he quitted the Guards and sold out of the army. When we find him again, his mustachios and the title of Colonel on his card are the only relics of his military profession.\n\nIt has been mentioned that Rebecca, soon after her arrival in Paris, took a very smart and leading position in the society of that capital, and was welcomed at some of the most distinguished houses of the restored French nobility. The English men of fashion in Paris courted her, too, to the disgust of the ladies their wives, who could not bear the parvenue. For some months the salons of the Faubourg St. Germain, in which her place was secured, and the splendours of the new Court, where she was received with much distinction, delighted and perhaps a little intoxicated Mrs. Crawley, who may have been disposed during this period of elation to slight the people--honest young military men mostly--who formed her husband's chief society.\n\nBut the Colonel yawned sadly among the Duchesses and great ladies of the Court. The old women who played ecarte made such a noise about a five-franc piece that it was not worth Colonel Crawley's while to sit down at a card-table. The wit of their conversation he could not appreciate, being ignorant of their language. And what good could his wife get, he urged, by making curtsies every night to a whole circle of Princesses? He left Rebecca presently to frequent these parties alone, resuming his own simple pursuits and amusements amongst the amiable friends of his own choice.\n\nThe truth is, when we say of a gentleman that he lives elegantly on nothing a year, we use the word \"nothing\" to signify something unknown; meaning, simply, that we don't know how the gentleman in question defrays the expenses of his establishment. Now, our friend the Colonel had a great aptitude for all games of chance: and exercising himself, as he continually did, with the cards, the dice-box, or the cue, it is natural to suppose that he attained a much greater skill in the use of these articles than men can possess who only occasionally handle them. To use a cue at billiards well is like using a pencil, or a German flute, or a small-sword--you cannot master any one of these implements at first, and it is only by repeated study and perseverance, joined to a natural taste, that a man can excel in the handling of either. Now Crawley, from being only a brilliant amateur, had grown to be a consummate master of billiards. Like a great General, his genius used to rise with the danger, and when the luck had been unfavourable to him for a whole game, and the bets were consequently against him, he would, with consummate skill and boldness, make some prodigious hits which would restore the battle, and come in a victor at the end, to the astonishment of everybody--of everybody, that is, who was a stranger to his play. Those who were accustomed to see it were cautious how they staked their money against a man of such sudden resources and brilliant and overpowering skill.\n\nAt games of cards he was equally skilful; for though he would constantly lose money at the commencement of an evening, playing so carelessly and making such blunders, that newcomers were often inclined to think meanly of his talent; yet when roused to action and awakened to caution by repeated small losses, it was remarked that Crawley's play became quite different, and that he was pretty sure of beating his enemy thoroughly before the night was over. Indeed, very few men could say that they ever had the better of him. His successes were so repeated that no wonder the envious and the vanquished spoke sometimes with bitterness regarding them. And as the French say of the Duke of Wellington, who never suffered a defeat, that only an astonishing series of lucky accidents enabled him to be an invariable winner; yet even they allow that he cheated at Waterloo, and was enabled to win the last great trick: so it was hinted at headquarters in England that some foul play must have taken place in order to account for the continuous successes of Colonel Crawley.\n\nThough Frascati's and the Salon were open at that time in Paris, the mania for play was so widely spread that the public gambling-rooms did not suffice for the general ardour, and gambling went on in private houses as much as if there had been no public means for gratifying the passion. At Crawley's charming little reunions of an evening this fatal amusement commonly was practised--much to good-natured little Mrs. Crawley's annoyance. She spoke about her husband's passion for dice with the deepest grief; she bewailed it to everybody who came to her house. She besought the young fellows never, never to touch a box; and when young Green, of the Rifles, lost a very considerable sum of money, Rebecca passed a whole night in tears, as the servant told the unfortunate young gentleman, and actually went on her knees to her husband to beseech him to remit the debt, and burn the acknowledgement. How could he? He had lost just as much himself to Blackstone of the Hussars, and Count Punter of the Hanoverian Cavalry. Green might have any decent time; but pay?--of course he must pay; to talk of burning IOU's was child's play.\n\nOther officers, chiefly young--for the young fellows gathered round Mrs. Crawley--came from her parties with long faces, having dropped more or less money at her fatal card-tables. Her house began to have an unfortunate reputation. The old hands warned the less experienced of their danger. Colonel O'Dowd, of the --th regiment, one of those occupying in Paris, warned Lieutenant Spooney of that corps. A loud and violent fracas took place between the infantry Colonel and his lady, who were dining at the Cafe de Paris, and Colonel and Mrs. Crawley; who were also taking their meal there. The ladies engaged on both sides. Mrs. O'Dowd snapped her fingers in Mrs. Crawley's face and called her husband \"no betther than a black-leg.\" Colonel Crawley challenged Colonel O'Dowd, C.B. The Commander-in-Chief hearing of the dispute sent for Colonel Crawley, who was getting ready the same pistols \"which he shot Captain Marker,\" and had such a conversation with him that no duel took place. If Rebecca had not gone on her knees to General Tufto, Crawley would have been sent back to England; and he did not play, except with civilians, for some weeks after.\n\nBut, in spite of Rawdon's undoubted skill and constant successes, it became evident to Rebecca, considering these things, that their position was but a precarious one, and that, even although they paid scarcely anybody, their little capital would end one day by dwindling into zero. \"Gambling,\" she would say, \"dear, is good to help your income, but not as an income itself. Some day people may be tired of play, and then where are we?\" Rawdon acquiesced in the justice of her opinion; and in truth he had remarked that after a few nights of his little suppers, &c., gentlemen were tired of play with him, and, in spite of Rebecca's charms, did not present themselves very eagerly.\n\nEasy and pleasant as their life at Paris was, it was after all only an idle dalliance and amiable trifling; and Rebecca saw that she must push Rawdon's fortune in their own country. She must get him a place or appointment at home or in the colonies, and she determined to make a move upon England as soon as the way could be cleared for her. As a first step she had made Crawley sell out of the Guards and go on half-pay. His function as aide-de-camp to General Tufto had ceased previously. Rebecca laughed in all companies at that officer, at his toupee (which he mounted on coming to Paris), at his waistband, at his false teeth, at his pretensions to be a lady-killer above all, and his absurd vanity in fancying every woman whom he came near was in love with him. It was to Mrs. Brent, the beetle-browed wife of Mr. Commissary Brent, to whom the general transferred his attentions now--his bouquets, his dinners at the restaurateurs', his opera-boxes, and his knick-knacks. Poor Mrs. Tufto was no more happy than before, and had still to pass long evenings alone with her daughters, knowing that her General was gone off scented and curled to stand behind Mrs. Brent's chair at the play. Becky had a dozen admirers in his place, to be sure, and could cut her rival to pieces with her wit. But, as we have said, she was growing tired of this idle social life: opera-boxes and restaurateur dinners palled upon her: nosegays could not be laid by as a provision for future years: and she could not live upon knick-knacks, laced handkerchiefs, and kid gloves. She felt the frivolity of pleasure and longed for more substantial benefits.\n\nAt this juncture news arrived which was spread among the many creditors of the Colonel at Paris, and which caused them great satisfaction. Miss Crawley, the rich aunt from whom he expected his immense inheritance, was dying; the Colonel must haste to her bedside. Mrs. Crawley and her child would remain behind until he came to reclaim them. He departed for Calais, and having reached that place in safety, it might have been supposed that he went to Dover; but instead he took the diligence to Dunkirk, and thence travelled to Brussels, for which place he had a former predilection. The fact is, he owed more money at London than at Paris; and he preferred the quiet little Belgian city to either of the more noisy capitals.\n\nHer aunt was dead. Mrs. Crawley ordered the most intense mourning for herself and little Rawdon. The Colonel was busy arranging the affairs of the inheritance. They could take the premier now, instead of the little entresol of the hotel which they occupied. Mrs. Crawley and the landlord had a consultation about the new hangings, an amicable wrangle about the carpets, and a final adjustment of everything except the bill. She went off in one of his carriages; her French bonne with her; the child by her side; the admirable landlord and landlady smiling farewell to her from the gate. General Tufto was furious when he heard she was gone, and Mrs. Brent furious with him for being furious; Lieutenant Spooney was cut to the heart; and the landlord got ready his best apartments previous to the return of the fascinating little woman and her husband. He serréd the trunks which she left in his charge with the greatest care. They had been especially recommended to him by Madame Crawley. They were not, however, found to be particularly valuable when opened some time after.\n\nBut before she went to join her husband in the Belgic capital, Mrs. Crawley made an expedition into England, leaving behind her her little son upon the continent, under the care of her French maid.\n\nThe parting between Rebecca and the little Rawdon did not cause either party much pain. She had not, to say truth, seen much of the young gentleman since his birth. After the amiable fashion of French mothers, she had placed him out at nurse in a village in the neighbourhood of Paris, where little Rawdon passed the first months of his life, not unhappily, with a numerous family of foster-brothers in wooden shoes. His father would ride over many a time to see him here, and the elder Rawdon's paternal heart glowed to see him rosy and dirty, shouting lustily, and happy in the making of mud-pies under the superintendence of the gardener's wife, his nurse.\n\nRebecca did not care much to go and see the son and heir. Once he spoiled a new dove-coloured pelisse of hers. He preferred his nurse's caresses to his mamma's, and when finally he quitted that jolly nurse and almost parent, he cried loudly for hours. He was only consoled by his mother's promise that he should return to his nurse the next day; indeed the nurse herself, who probably would have been pained at the parting too, was told that the child would immediately be restored to her, and for some time awaited quite anxiously his return.\n\nIn fact, our friends may be said to have been among the first of that brood of hardy English adventurers who have subsequently invaded the Continent and swindled in all the capitals of Europe. The respect in those happy days of 1817-18 was very great for the wealth and honour of Britons. They had not then learned, as I am told, to haggle for bargains with the pertinacity which now distinguishes them. The great cities of Europe had not been as yet open to the enterprise of our rascals. And whereas there is now hardly a town of France or Italy in which you shall not see some noble countryman of our own, with that happy swagger and insolence of demeanour which we carry everywhere, swindling inn-landlords, passing fictitious cheques upon credulous bankers, robbing coach-makers of their carriages, goldsmiths of their trinkets, easy travellers of their money at cards, even public libraries of their books--thirty years ago you needed but to be a Milor Anglais, travelling in a private carriage, and credit was at your hand wherever you chose to seek it, and gentlemen, instead of cheating, were cheated. It was not for some weeks after the Crawleys' departure that the landlord of the hotel which they occupied during their residence at Paris found out the losses which he had sustained: not until Madame Marabou, the milliner, made repeated visits with her little bill for articles supplied to Madame Crawley; not until Monsieur Didelot from Boule d'Or in the Palais Royal had asked half a dozen times whether cette charmante Miladi who had bought watches and bracelets of him was de retour. It is a fact that even the poor gardener's wife, who had nursed madame's child, was never paid after the first six months for that supply of the milk of human kindness with which she had furnished the lusty and healthy little Rawdon. No, not even the nurse was paid--the Crawleys were in too great a hurry to remember their trifling debt to her. As for the landlord of the hotel, his curses against the English nation were violent for the rest of his natural life. He asked all travellers whether they knew a certain Colonel Lor Crawley--avec sa femme une petite dame, tres spirituelle. \"Ah, Monsieur!\" he would add--\"ils m'ont affreusement vole.\" It was melancholy to hear his accents as he spoke of that catastrophe.\n\nRebecca's object in her journey to London was to effect a kind of compromise with her husband's numerous creditors, and by offering them a dividend of ninepence or a shilling in the pound, to secure a return for him into his own country. It does not become us to trace the steps which she took in the conduct of this most difficult negotiation; but, having shown them to their satisfaction that the sum which she was empowered to offer was all her husband's available capital, and having convinced them that Colonel Crawley would prefer a perpetual retirement on the Continent to a residence in this country with his debts unsettled; having proved to them that there was no possibility of money accruing to him from other quarters, and no earthly chance of their getting a larger dividend than that which she was empowered to offer, she brought the Colonel's creditors unanimously to accept her proposals, and purchased with fifteen hundred pounds of ready money more than ten times that amount of debts.\n\nMrs. Crawley employed no lawyer in the transaction. The matter was so simple, to have or to leave, as she justly observed, that she made the lawyers of the creditors themselves do the business. And Mr. Lewis representing Mr. Davids, of Red Lion Square, and Mr. Moss acting for Mr. Manasseh of Cursitor Street (chief creditors of the Colonel's), complimented his lady upon the brilliant way in which she did business, and declared that there was no professional man who could beat her.\n\nRebecca received their congratulations with perfect modesty; ordered a bottle of sherry and a bread cake to the little dingy lodgings where she dwelt, while conducting the business, to treat the enemy's lawyers: shook hands with them at parting, in excellent good humour, and returned straightway to the Continent, to rejoin her husband and son and acquaint the former with the glad news of his entire liberation. As for the latter, he had been considerably neglected during his mother's absence by Mademoiselle Genevieve, her French maid; for that young woman, contracting an attachment for a soldier in the garrison of Calais, forgot her charge in the society of this militaire, and little Rawdon very narrowly escaped drowning on Calais sands at this period, where the absent Genevieve had left and lost him.\n\nAnd so, Colonel and Mrs. Crawley came to London: and it is at their house in Curzon Street, May Fair, that they really showed the skill which must be possessed by those who would live on the resources above named.\n\nThe Subject Continued\nIn the first place, and as a matter of the greatest necessity, we are bound to describe how a house may be got for nothing a year. These mansions are to be had either unfurnished, where, if you have credit with Messrs. Gillows or Bantings, you can get them splendidly montees and decorated entirely according to your own fancy; or they are to be let furnished, a less troublesome and complicated arrangement to most parties. It was so that Crawley and his wife preferred to hire their house.\n\nBefore Mr. Bowls came to preside over Miss Crawley's house and cellar in Park Lane, that lady had had for a butler a Mr. Raggles, who was born on the family estate of Queen's Crawley, and indeed was a younger son of a gardener there. By good conduct, a handsome person and calves, and a grave demeanour, Raggles rose from the knife-board to the footboard of the carriage; from the footboard to the butler's pantry. When he had been a certain number of years at the head of Miss Crawley's establishment, where he had had good wages, fat perquisites, and plenty of opportunities of saving, he announced that he was about to contract a matrimonial alliance with a late cook of Miss Crawley's, who had subsisted in an honourable manner by the exercise of a mangle, and the keeping of a small greengrocer's shop in the neighbourhood. The truth is, that the ceremony had been clandestinely performed some years back; although the news of Mr. Raggles' marriage was first brought to Miss Crawley by a little boy and girl of seven and eight years of age, whose continual presence in the kitchen had attracted the attention of Miss Briggs.\n\nMr. Raggles then retired and personally undertook the superintendence of the small shop and the greens. He added milk and cream, eggs and country-fed pork to his stores, contenting himself whilst other retired butlers were vending spirits in public houses, by dealing in the simplest country produce. And having a good connection amongst the butlers in the neighbourhood, and a snug back parlour where he and Mrs. Raggles received them, his milk, cream, and eggs got to be adopted by many of the fraternity, and his profits increased every year. Year after year he quietly and modestly amassed money, and when at length that snug and complete bachelor's residence at No. 201, Curzon Street, May Fair, lately the residence of the Honourable Frederick Deuceace, gone abroad, with its rich and appropriate furniture by the first makers, was brought to the hammer, who should go in and purchase the lease and furniture of the house but Charles Raggles? A part of the money he borrowed, it is true, and at rather a high interest, from a brother butler, but the chief part he paid down, and it was with no small pride that Mrs. Raggles found herself sleeping in a bed of carved mahogany, with silk curtains, with a prodigious cheval glass opposite to her, and a wardrobe which would contain her, and Raggles, and all the family.\n\nOf course, they did not intend to occupy permanently an apartment so splendid. It was in order to let the house again that Raggles purchased it. As soon as a tenant was found, he subsided into the greengrocer's shop once more; but a happy thing it was for him to walk out of that tenement and into Curzon Street, and there survey his house--his own house--with geraniums in the window and a carved bronze knocker. The footman occasionally lounging at the area railing, treated him with respect; the cook took her green stuff at his house and called him Mr. Landlord, and there was not one thing the tenants did, or one dish which they had for dinner, that Raggles might not know of, if he liked.\n\nHe was a good man; good and happy. The house brought him in so handsome a yearly income that he was determined to send his children to good schools, and accordingly, regardless of expense, Charles was sent to boarding at Dr. Swishtail's, Sugar-cane Lodge, and little Matilda to Miss Peckover's, Laurentinum House, Clapham.\n\nRaggles loved and adored the Crawley family as the author of all his prosperity in life. He had a silhouette of his mistress in his back shop, and a drawing of the Porter's Lodge at Queen's Crawley, done by that spinster herself in India ink--and the only addition he made to the decorations of the Curzon Street House was a print of Queen's Crawley in Hampshire, the seat of Sir Walpole Crawley, Baronet, who was represented in a gilded car drawn by six white horses, and passing by a lake covered with swans, and barges containing ladies in hoops, and musicians with flags and periwigs. Indeed Raggles thought there was no such palace in all the world, and no such august family.\n\nAs luck would have it, Raggles' house in Curzon Street was to let when Rawdon and his wife returned to London. The Colonel knew it and its owner quite well; the latter's connection with the Crawley family had been kept up constantly, for Raggles helped Mr. Bowls whenever Miss Crawley received friends. And the old man not only let his house to the Colonel but officiated as his butler whenever he had company; Mrs. Raggles operating in the kitchen below and sending up dinners of which old Miss Crawley herself might have approved. This was the way, then, Crawley got his house for nothing; for though Raggles had to pay taxes and rates, and the interest of the mortgage to the brother butler; and the insurance of his life; and the charges for his children at school; and the value of the meat and drink which his own family--and for a time that of Colonel Crawley too--consumed; and though the poor wretch was utterly ruined by the transaction, his children being flung on the streets, and himself driven into the Fleet Prison: yet somebody must pay even for gentlemen who live for nothing a year--and so it was this unlucky Raggles was made the representative of Colonel Crawley's defective capital.\n\nI wonder how many families are driven to roguery and to ruin by great practitioners in Crawley's way?--how many great noblemen rob their petty tradesmen, condescend to swindle their poor retainers out of wretched little sums and cheat for a few shillings? When we read that a noble nobleman has left for the Continent, or that another noble nobleman has an execution in his house--and that one or other owes six or seven millions, the defeat seems glorious even, and we respect the victim in the vastness of his ruin. But who pities a poor barber who can't get his money for powdering the footmen's heads; or a poor carpenter who has ruined himself by fixing up ornaments and pavilions for my lady's dejeuner; or the poor devil of a tailor whom the steward patronizes, and who has pledged all he is worth, and more, to get the liveries ready, which my lord has done him the honour to bespeak? When the great house tumbles down, these miserable wretches fall under it unnoticed: as they say in the old legends, before a man goes to the devil himself, he sends plenty of other souls thither.\n\nRawdon and his wife generously gave their patronage to all such of Miss Crawley's tradesmen and purveyors as chose to serve them. Some were willing enough, especially the poor ones. It was wonderful to see the pertinacity with which the washerwoman from Tooting brought the cart every Saturday, and her bills week after week. Mr. Raggles himself had to supply the greengroceries. The bill for servants' porter at the Fortune of War public house is a curiosity in the chronicles of beer. Every servant also was owed the greater part of his wages, and thus kept up perforce an interest in the house. Nobody in fact was paid. Not the blacksmith who opened the lock; nor the glazier who mended the pane; nor the jobber who let the carriage; nor the groom who drove it; nor the butcher who provided the leg of mutton; nor the coals which roasted it; nor the cook who basted it; nor the servants who ate it: and this I am given to understand is not unfrequently the way in which people live elegantly on nothing a year.\n\nIn a little town such things cannot be done without remark. We know there the quantity of milk our neighbour takes and espy the joint or the fowls which are going in for his dinner. So, probably, 200 and 202 in Curzon Street might know what was going on in the house between them, the servants communicating through the area-railings; but Crawley and his wife and his friends did not know 200 and 202. When you came to 201 there was a hearty welcome, a kind smile, a good dinner, and a jolly shake of the hand from the host and hostess there, just for all the world as if they had been undisputed masters of three or four thousand a year--and so they were, not in money, but in produce and labour--if they did not pay for the mutton, they had it: if they did not give bullion in exchange for their wine, how should we know? Never was better claret at any man's table than at honest Rawdon's; dinners more gay and neatly served. His drawing-rooms were the prettiest, little, modest salons conceivable: they were decorated with the greatest taste, and a thousand knick-knacks from Paris, by Rebecca: and when she sat at her piano trilling songs with a lightsome heart, the stranger voted himself in a little paradise of domestic comfort and agreed that, if the husband was rather stupid, the wife was charming, and the dinners the pleasantest in the world.\n\nRebecca's wit, cleverness, and flippancy made her speedily the vogue in London among a certain class. You saw demure chariots at her door, out of which stepped very great people. You beheld her carriage in the park, surrounded by dandies of note. The little box in the third tier of the opera was crowded with heads constantly changing; but it must be confessed that the ladies held aloof from her, and that their doors were shut to our little adventurer.\n\nWith regard to the world of female fashion and its customs, the present writer of course can only speak at second hand. A man can no more penetrate or understand those mysteries than he can know what the ladies talk about when they go upstairs after dinner. It is only by inquiry and perseverance that one sometimes gets hints of those secrets; and by a similar diligence every person who treads the Pall Mall pavement and frequents the clubs of this metropolis knows, either through his own experience or through some acquaintance with whom he plays at billiards or shares the joint, something about the genteel world of London, and how, as there are men (such as Rawdon Crawley, whose position we mentioned before) who cut a good figure to the eyes of the ignorant world and to the apprentices in the park, who behold them consorting with the most notorious dandies there, so there are ladies, who may be called men's women, being welcomed entirely by all the gentlemen and cut or slighted by all their wives. Mrs. Firebrace is of this sort; the lady with the beautiful fair ringlets whom you see every day in Hyde Park, surrounded by the greatest and most famous dandies of this empire. Mrs. Rockwood is another, whose parties are announced laboriously in the fashionable newspapers and with whom you see that all sorts of ambassadors and great noblemen dine; and many more might be mentioned had they to do with the history at present in hand. But while simple folks who are out of the world, or country people with a taste for the genteel, behold these ladies in their seeming glory in public places, or envy them from afar off, persons who are better instructed could inform them that these envied ladies have no more chance of establishing themselves in \"society,\" than the benighted squire's wife in Somersetshire who reads of their doings in the Morning Post. Men living about London are aware of these awful truths. You hear how pitilessly many ladies of seeming rank and wealth are excluded from this \"society.\" The frantic efforts which they make to enter this circle, the meannesses to which they submit, the insults which they undergo, are matters of wonder to those who take human or womankind for a study; and the pursuit of fashion under difficulties would be a fine theme for any very great person who had the wit, the leisure, and the knowledge of the English language necessary for the compiling of such a history.\n\nNow the few female acquaintances whom Mrs. Crawley had known abroad not only declined to visit her when she came to this side of the Channel, but cut her severely when they met in public places. It was curious to see how the great ladies forgot her, and no doubt not altogether a pleasant study to Rebecca. When Lady Bareacres met her in the waiting-room at the opera, she gathered her daughters about her as if they would be contaminated by a touch of Becky, and retreating a step or two, placed herself in front of them, and stared at her little enemy. To stare Becky out of countenance required a severer glance than even the frigid old Bareacres could shoot out of her dismal eyes. When Lady de la Mole, who had ridden a score of times by Becky's side at Brussels, met Mrs. Crawley's open carriage in Hyde Park, her Ladyship was quite blind, and could not in the least recognize her former friend. Even Mrs. Blenkinsop, the banker's wife, cut her at church. Becky went regularly to church now; it was edifying to see her enter there with Rawdon by her side, carrying a couple of large gilt prayer-books, and afterwards going through the ceremony with the gravest resignation.\n\nRawdon at first felt very acutely the slights which were passed upon his wife, and was inclined to be gloomy and savage. He talked of calling out the husbands or brothers of every one of the insolent women who did not pay a proper respect to his wife; and it was only by the strongest commands and entreaties on her part that he was brought into keeping a decent behaviour. \"You can't shoot me into society,\" she said good-naturedly. \"Remember, my dear, that I was but a governess, and you, you poor silly old man, have the worst reputation for debt, and dice, and all sorts of wickedness. We shall get quite as many friends as we want by and by, and in the meanwhile you must be a good boy and obey your schoolmistress in everything she tells you to do. When we heard that your aunt had left almost everything to Pitt and his wife, do you remember what a rage you were in? You would have told all Paris, if I had not made you keep your temper, and where would you have been now?--in prison at Ste. Pelagie for debt, and not established in London in a handsome house, with every comfort about you--you were in such a fury you were ready to murder your brother, you wicked Cain you, and what good would have come of remaining angry? All the rage in the world won't get us your aunt's money; and it is much better that we should be friends with your brother's family than enemies, as those foolish Butes are. When your father dies, Queen's Crawley will be a pleasant house for you and me to pass the winter in. If we are ruined, you can carve and take charge of the stable, and I can be a governess to Lady Jane's children. Ruined! fiddlede-dee! I will get you a good place before that; or Pitt and his little boy will die, and we will be Sir Rawdon and my lady. While there is life, there is hope, my dear, and I intend to make a man of you yet. Who sold your horses for you? Who paid your debts for you?\" Rawdon was obliged to confess that he owed all these benefits to his wife, and to trust himself to her guidance for the future.\n\nIndeed, when Miss Crawley quitted the world, and that money for which all her relatives had been fighting so eagerly was finally left to Pitt, Bute Crawley, who found that only five thousand pounds had been left to him instead of the twenty upon which he calculated, was in such a fury at his disappointment that he vented it in savage abuse upon his nephew; and the quarrel always rankling between them ended in an utter breach of intercourse. Rawdon Crawley's conduct, on the other hand, who got but a hundred pounds, was such as to astonish his brother and delight his sister-in-law, who was disposed to look kindly upon all the members of her husband's family. He wrote to his brother a very frank, manly, good-humoured letter from Paris. He was aware, he said, that by his own marriage he had forfeited his aunt's favour; and though he did not disguise his disappointment that she should have been so entirely relentless towards him, he was glad that the money was still kept in their branch of the family, and heartily congratulated his brother on his good fortune. He sent his affectionate remembrances to his sister, and hoped to have her good-will for Mrs. Rawdon; and the letter concluded with a postscript to Pitt in the latter lady's own handwriting. She, too, begged to join in her husband's congratulations. She should ever remember Mr. Crawley's kindness to her in early days when she was a friendless orphan, the instructress of his little sisters, in whose welfare she still took the tenderest interest. She wished him every happiness in his married life, and, asking his permission to offer her remembrances to Lady Jane (of whose goodness all the world informed her), she hoped that one day she might be allowed to present her little boy to his uncle and aunt, and begged to bespeak for him their good-will and protection.\n\nPitt Crawley received this communication very graciously--more graciously than Miss Crawley had received some of Rebecca's previous compositions in Rawdon's handwriting; and as for Lady Jane, she was so charmed with the letter that she expected her husband would instantly divide his aunt's legacy into two equal portions and send off one-half to his brother at Paris.\n\nTo her Ladyship's surprise, however, Pitt declined to accommodate his brother with a cheque for thirty thousand pounds. But he made Rawdon a handsome offer of his hand whenever the latter should come to England and choose to take it; and, thanking Mrs. Crawley for her good opinion of himself and Lady Jane, he graciously pronounced his willingness to take any opportunity to serve her little boy.\n\nThus an almost reconciliation was brought about between the brothers. When Rebecca came to town Pitt and his wife were not in London. Many a time she drove by the old door in Park Lane to see whether they had taken possession of Miss Crawley's house there. But the new family did not make its appearance; it was only through Raggles that she heard of their movements--how Miss Crawley's domestics had been dismissed with decent gratuities, and how Mr. Pitt had only once made his appearance in London, when he stopped for a few days at the house, did business with his lawyers there, and sold off all Miss Crawley's French novels to a bookseller out of Bond Street. Becky had reasons of her own which caused her to long for the arrival of her new relation. \"When Lady Jane comes,\" thought she, \"she shall be my sponsor in London society; and as for the women! bah! the women will ask me when they find the men want to see me.\"\n\nAn article as necessary to a lady in this position as her brougham or her bouquet is her companion. I have always admired the way in which the tender creatures, who cannot exist without sympathy, hire an exceedingly plain friend of their own sex from whom they are almost inseparable. The sight of that inevitable woman in her faded gown seated behind her dear friend in the opera-box, or occupying the back seat of the barouche, is always a wholesome and moral one to me, as jolly a reminder as that of the Death's-head which figured in the repasts of Egyptian bon-vivants, a strange sardonic memorial of Vanity Fair. What? even battered, brazen, beautiful, conscienceless, heartless, Mrs. Firebrace, whose father died of her shame: even lovely, daring Mrs. Mantrap, who will ride at any fence which any man in England will take, and who drives her greys in the park, while her mother keeps a huckster's stall in Bath still--even those who are so bold, one might fancy they could face anything, dare not face the world without a female friend. They must have somebody to cling to, the affectionate creatures! And you will hardly see them in any public place without a shabby companion in a dyed silk, sitting somewhere in the shade close behind them.\n\n\"Rawdon,\" said Becky, very late one night, as a party of gentlemen were seated round her crackling drawing-room fire (for the men came to her house to finish the night; and she had ice and coffee for them, the best in London): \"I must have a sheep-dog.\"\n\n\"A what?\" said Rawdon, looking up from an ecarte table.\n\n\"A sheep-dog!\" said young Lord Southdown. \"My dear Mrs. Crawley, what a fancy! Why not have a Danish dog? I know of one as big as a camel-leopard, by Jove. It would almost pull your brougham. Or a Persian greyhound, eh? (I propose, if you please); or a little pug that would go into one of Lord Steyne's snuff-boxes? There's a man at Bayswater got one with such a nose that you might--I mark the king and play--that you might hang your hat on it.\"\n\n\"I mark the trick,\" Rawdon gravely said. He attended to his game commonly and didn't much meddle with the conversation, except when it was about horses and betting.\n\n\"What CAN you want with a shepherd's dog?\" the lively little Southdown continued.\n\n\"I mean a MORAL shepherd's dog,\" said Becky, laughing and looking up at Lord Steyne.\n\n\"What the devil's that?\" said his Lordship.\n\n\"A dog to keep the wolves off me,\" Rebecca continued. \"A companion.\"\n\n\"Dear little innocent lamb, you want one,\" said the marquis; and his jaw thrust out, and he began to grin hideously, his little eyes leering towards Rebecca.\n\nThe great Lord of Steyne was standing by the fire sipping coffee. The fire crackled and blazed pleasantly. There was a score of candles sparkling round the mantel piece, in all sorts of quaint sconces, of gilt and bronze and porcelain. They lighted up Rebecca's figure to admiration, as she sat on a sofa covered with a pattern of gaudy flowers. She was in a pink dress that looked as fresh as a rose; her dazzling white arms and shoulders were half-covered with a thin hazy scarf through which they sparkled; her hair hung in curls round her neck; one of her little feet peeped out from the fresh crisp folds of the silk: the prettiest little foot in the prettiest little sandal in the finest silk stocking in the world.\n\nThe candles lighted up Lord Steyne's shining bald head, which was fringed with red hair. He had thick bushy eyebrows, with little twinkling bloodshot eyes, surrounded by a thousand wrinkles. His jaw was underhung, and when he laughed, two white buck-teeth protruded themselves and glistened savagely in the midst of the grin. He had been dining with royal personages, and wore his garter and ribbon. A short man was his Lordship, broad-chested and bow-legged, but proud of the fineness of his foot and ankle, and always caressing his garter-knee.\n\n\"And so the shepherd is not enough,\" said he, \"to defend his lambkin?\"\n\n\"The shepherd is too fond of playing at cards and going to his clubs,\" answered Becky, laughing.\n\n\"'Gad, what a debauched Corydon!\" said my lord--\"what a mouth for a pipe!\"\n\n\"I take your three to two,\" here said Rawdon, at the card-table.\n\n\"Hark at Meliboeus,\" snarled the noble marquis; \"he's pastorally occupied too: he's shearing a Southdown. What an innocent mutton, hey? Damme, what a snowy fleece!\"\n\nRebecca's eyes shot out gleams of scornful humour. \"My lord,\" she said, \"you are a knight of the Order.\" He had the collar round his neck, indeed--a gift of the restored princes of Spain.\n\nLord Steyne in early life had been notorious for his daring and his success at play. He had sat up two days and two nights with Mr. Fox at hazard. He had won money of the most august personages of the realm: he had won his marquisate, it was said, at the gaming-table; but he did not like an allusion to those bygone fredaines. Rebecca saw the scowl gathering over his heavy brow.\n\nShe rose up from her sofa and went and took his coffee cup out of his hand with a little curtsey. \"Yes,\" she said, \"I must get a watchdog. But he won't bark at YOU.\" And, going into the other drawing-room, she sat down to the piano and began to sing little French songs in such a charming, thrilling voice that the mollified nobleman speedily followed her into that chamber, and might be seen nodding his head and bowing time over her.\n\nRawdon and his friend meanwhile played ecarte until they had enough. The Colonel won; but, say that he won ever so much and often, nights like these, which occurred many times in the week--his wife having all the talk and all the admiration, and he sitting silent without the circle, not comprehending a word of the jokes, the allusions, the mystical language within--must have been rather wearisome to the ex-dragoon.\n\n\"How is Mrs. Crawley's husband?\" Lord Steyne used to say to him by way of a good day when they met; and indeed that was now his avocation in life. He was Colonel Crawley no more. He was Mrs. Crawley's husband.\n\nAbout the little Rawdon, if nothing has been said all this while, it is because he is hidden upstairs in a garret somewhere, or has crawled below into the kitchen for companionship. His mother scarcely ever took notice of him. He passed the days with his French bonne as long as that domestic remained in Mr. Crawley's family, and when the Frenchwoman went away, the little fellow, howling in the loneliness of the night, had compassion taken on him by a housemaid, who took him out of his solitary nursery into her bed in the garret hard by and comforted him.\n\nRebecca, my Lord Steyne, and one or two more were in the drawing-room taking tea after the opera, when this shouting was heard overhead. \"It's my cherub crying for his nurse,\" she said. She did not offer to move to go and see the child. \"Don't agitate your feelings by going to look for him,\" said Lord Steyne sardonically. \"Bah!\" replied the other, with a sort of blush, \"he'll cry himself to sleep\"; and they fell to talking about the opera.\n\nRawdon had stolen off though, to look after his son and heir; and came back to the company when he found that honest Dolly was consoling the child. The Colonel's dressing-room was in those upper regions. He used to see the boy there in private. They had interviews together every morning when he shaved; Rawdon minor sitting on a box by his father's side and watching the operation with never-ceasing pleasure. He and the sire were great friends. The father would bring him sweetmeats from the dessert and hide them in a certain old epaulet box, where the child went to seek them, and laughed with joy on discovering the treasure; laughed, but not too loud: for mamma was below asleep and must not be disturbed. She did not go to rest till very late and seldom rose till after noon.\n\nRawdon bought the boy plenty of picture-books and crammed his nursery with toys. Its walls were covered with pictures pasted up by the father's own hand and purchased by him for ready money. When he was off duty with Mrs. Rawdon in the park, he would sit up here, passing hours with the boy; who rode on his chest, who pulled his great mustachios as if they were driving-reins, and spent days with him in indefatigable gambols. The room was a low room, and once, when the child was not five years old, his father, who was tossing him wildly up in his arms, hit the poor little chap's skull so violently against the ceiling that he almost dropped the child, so terrified was he at the disaster.\n\nRawdon minor had made up his face for a tremendous howl--the severity of the blow indeed authorized that indulgence; but just as he was going to begin, the father interposed.\n\n\"For God's sake, Rawdy, don't wake Mamma,\" he cried. And the child, looking in a very hard and piteous way at his father, bit his lips, clenched his hands, and didn't cry a bit. Rawdon told that story at the clubs, at the mess, to everybody in town. \"By Gad, sir,\" he explained to the public in general, \"what a good plucked one that boy of mine is--what a trump he is! I half-sent his head through the ceiling, by Gad, and he wouldn't cry for fear of disturbing his mother.\"\n\nSometimes--once or twice in a week--that lady visited the upper regions in which the child lived. She came like a vivified figure out of the Magasin des Modes--blandly smiling in the most beautiful new clothes and little gloves and boots. Wonderful scarfs, laces, and jewels glittered about her. She had always a new bonnet on, and flowers bloomed perpetually in it, or else magnificent curling ostrich feathers, soft and snowy as camellias. She nodded twice or thrice patronizingly to the little boy, who looked up from his dinner or from the pictures of soldiers he was painting. When she left the room, an odour of rose, or some other magical fragrance, lingered about the nursery. She was an unearthly being in his eyes, superior to his father--to all the world: to be worshipped and admired at a distance. To drive with that lady in the carriage was an awful rite: he sat up in the back seat and did not dare to speak: he gazed with all his eyes at the beautifully dressed Princess opposite to him. Gentlemen on splendid prancing horses came up and smiled and talked with her. How her eyes beamed upon all of them! Her hand used to quiver and wave gracefully as they passed. When he went out with her he had his new red dress on. His old brown holland was good enough when he stayed at home. Sometimes, when she was away, and Dolly his maid was making his bed, he came into his mother's room. It was as the abode of a fairy to him--a mystic chamber of splendour and delights. There in the wardrobe hung those wonderful robes--pink and blue and many-tinted. There was the jewel-case, silver-clasped, and the wondrous bronze hand on the dressing-table, glistening all over with a hundred rings. There was the cheval-glass, that miracle of art, in which he could just see his own wondering head and the reflection of Dolly (queerly distorted, and as if up in the ceiling), plumping and patting the pillows of the bed. Oh, thou poor lonely little benighted boy! Mother is the name for God in the lips and hearts of little children; and here was one who was worshipping a stone!\n\nNow Rawdon Crawley, rascal as the Colonel was, had certain manly tendencies of affection in his heart and could love a child and a woman still. For Rawdon minor he had a great secret tenderness then, which did not escape Rebecca, though she did not talk about it to her husband. It did not annoy her: she was too good-natured. It only increased her scorn for him. He felt somehow ashamed of this paternal softness and hid it from his wife--only indulging in it when alone with the boy.\n\nHe used to take him out of mornings when they would go to the stables together and to the park. Little Lord Southdown, the best-natured of men, who would make you a present of the hat from his head, and whose main occupation in life was to buy knick-knacks that he might give them away afterwards, bought the little chap a pony not much bigger than a large rat, the donor said, and on this little black Shetland pygmy young Rawdon's great father was pleased to mount the boy, and to walk by his side in the park. It pleased him to see his old quarters, and his old fellow-guardsmen at Knightsbridge: he had begun to think of his bachelorhood with something like regret. The old troopers were glad to recognize their ancient officer and dandle the little colonel. Colonel Crawley found dining at mess and with his brother-officers very pleasant. \"Hang it, I ain't clever enough for her--I know it. She won't miss me,\" he used to say: and he was right, his wife did not miss him.\n\nRebecca was fond of her husband. She was always perfectly good-humoured and kind to him. She did not even show her scorn much for him; perhaps she liked him the better for being a fool. He was her upper servant and maitre d'hotel. He went on her errands; obeyed her orders without question; drove in the carriage in the ring with her without repining; took her to the opera-box, solaced himself at his club during the performance, and came punctually back to fetch her when due. He would have liked her to be a little fonder of the boy, but even to that he reconciled himself. \"Hang it, you know she's so clever,\" he said, \"and I'm not literary and that, you know.\" For, as we have said before, it requires no great wisdom to be able to win at cards and billiards, and Rawdon made no pretensions to any other sort of skill.\n\nWhen the companion came, his domestic duties became very light. His wife encouraged him to dine abroad: she would let him off duty at the opera. \"Don't stay and stupefy yourself at home to-night, my dear,\" she would say. \"Some men are coming who will only bore you. I would not ask them, but you know it's for your good, and now I have a sheep-dog, I need not be afraid to be alone.\"\n\n\"A sheep-dog--a companion! Becky Sharp with a companion! Isn't it good fun?\" thought Mrs. Crawley to herself. The notion tickled hugely her sense of humour.\n\nOne Sunday morning, as Rawdon Crawley, his little son, and the pony were taking their accustomed walk in the park, they passed by an old acquaintance of the Colonel's, Corporal Clink, of the regiment, who was in conversation with a friend, an old gentleman, who held a boy in his arms about the age of little Rawdon. This other youngster had seized hold of the Waterloo medal which the Corporal wore, and was examining it with delight.\n\n\"Good morning, your Honour,\" said Clink, in reply to the \"How do, Clink?\" of the Colonel. \"This ere young gentleman is about the little Colonel's age, sir,\" continued the corporal.\n\n\"His father was a Waterloo man, too,\" said the old gentleman, who carried the boy. \"Wasn't he, Georgy?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Georgy. He and the little chap on the pony were looking at each other with all their might--solemnly scanning each other as children do.\n\n\"In a line regiment,\" Clink said with a patronizing air.\n\n\"He was a Captain in the --th regiment,\" said the old gentleman rather pompously. \"Captain George Osborne, sir--perhaps you knew him. He died the death of a hero, sir, fighting against the Corsican tyrant.\" Colonel Crawley blushed quite red. \"I knew him very well, sir,\" he said, \"and his wife, his dear little wife, sir--how is she?\"\n\n\"She is my daughter, sir,\" said the old gentleman, putting down the boy and taking out a card with great solemnity, which he handed to the Colonel. On it written--\n\n\"Mr. Sedley, Sole Agent for the Black Diamond and Anti-Cinder Coal Association, Bunker's Wharf, Thames Street, and Anna-Maria Cottages, Fulham Road West.\"\n\nLittle Georgy went up and looked at the Shetland pony.\n\n\"Should you like to have a ride?\" said Rawdon minor from the saddle.\n\n\"Yes,\" said Georgy. The Colonel, who had been looking at him with some interest, took up the child and put him on the pony behind Rawdon minor.\n\n\"Take hold of him, Georgy,\" he said--\"take my little boy round the waist--his name is Rawdon.\" And both the children began to laugh.\n\n\"You won't see a prettier pair I think, THIS summer's day, sir,\" said the good-natured Corporal; and the Colonel, the Corporal, and old Mr. Sedley with his umbrella, walked by the side of the children.\n\nA Family in a Very Small Way\nWe must suppose little George Osborne has ridden from Knightsbridge towards Fulham, and will stop and make inquiries at that village regarding some friends whom we have left there. How is Mrs. Amelia after the storm of Waterloo? Is she living and thriving? What has come of Major Dobbin, whose cab was always hankering about her premises? And is there any news of the Collector of Boggley Wollah? The facts concerning the latter are briefly these:\n\nOur worthy fat friend Joseph Sedley returned to India not long after his escape from Brussels. Either his furlough was up, or he dreaded to meet any witnesses of his Waterloo flight. However it might be, he went back to his duties in Bengal very soon after Napoleon had taken up his residence at St. Helena, where Jos saw the ex-Emperor. To hear Mr. Sedley talk on board ship you would have supposed that it was not the first time he and the Corsican had met, and that the civilian had bearded the French General at Mount St. John. He had a thousand anecdotes about the famous battles; he knew the position of every regiment and the loss which each had incurred. He did not deny that he had been concerned in those victories--that he had been with the army and carried despatches for the Duke of Wellington. And he described what the Duke did and said on every conceivable moment of the day of Waterloo, with such an accurate knowledge of his Grace's sentiments and proceedings that it was clear he must have been by the conqueror's side throughout the day; though, as a non-combatant, his name was not mentioned in the public documents relative to the battle. Perhaps he actually worked himself up to believe that he had been engaged with the army; certain it is that he made a prodigious sensation for some time at Calcutta, and was called Waterloo Sedley during the whole of his subsequent stay in Bengal.\n\nThe bills which Jos had given for the purchase of those unlucky horses were paid without question by him and his agents. He never was heard to allude to the bargain, and nobody knows for a certainty what became of the horses, or how he got rid of them, or of Isidor, his Belgian servant, who sold a grey horse, very like the one which Jos rode, at Valenciennes sometime during the autumn of 1815.\n\nJos's London agents had orders to pay one hundred and twenty pounds yearly to his parents at Fulham. It was the chief support of the old couple; for Mr. Sedley's speculations in life subsequent to his bankruptcy did not by any means retrieve the broken old gentleman's fortune. He tried to be a wine-merchant, a coal-merchant, a commission lottery agent, &c., &c. He sent round prospectuses to his friends whenever he took a new trade, and ordered a new brass plate for the door, and talked pompously about making his fortune still. But Fortune never came back to the feeble and stricken old man. One by one his friends dropped off, and were weary of buying dear coals and bad wine from him; and there was only his wife in all the world who fancied, when he tottered off to the City of a morning, that he was still doing any business there. At evening he crawled slowly back; and he used to go of nights to a little club at a tavern, where he disposed of the finances of the nation. It was wonderful to hear him talk about millions, and agios, and discounts, and what Rothschild was doing, and Baring Brothers. He talked of such vast sums that the gentlemen of the club (the apothecary, the undertaker, the great carpenter and builder, the parish clerk, who was allowed to come stealthily, and Mr. Clapp, our old acquaintance,) respected the old gentleman. \"I was better off once, sir,\" he did not fail to tell everybody who \"used the room.\" \"My son, sir, is at this minute chief magistrate of Ramgunge in the Presidency of Bengal, and touching his four thousand rupees per mensem. My daughter might be a Colonel's lady if she liked. I might draw upon my son, the first magistrate, sir, for two thousand pounds to-morrow, and Alexander would cash my bill, down sir, down on the counter, sir. But the Sedleys were always a proud family.\" You and I, my dear reader, may drop into this condition one day: for have not many of our friends attained it? Our luck may fail: our powers forsake us: our place on the boards be taken by better and younger mimes--the chance of life roll away and leave us shattered and stranded. Then men will walk across the road when they meet you--or, worse still, hold you out a couple of fingers and patronize you in a pitying way--then you will know, as soon as your back is turned, that your friend begins with a \"Poor devil, what imprudences he has committed, what chances that chap has thrown away!\" Well, well--a carriage and three thousand a year is not the summit of the reward nor the end of God's judgment of men. If quacks prosper as often as they go to the wall--if zanies succeed and knaves arrive at fortune, and, vice versa, sharing ill luck and prosperity for all the world like the ablest and most honest amongst us--I say, brother, the gifts and pleasures of Vanity Fair cannot be held of any great account, and that it is probable . . . but we are wandering out of the domain of the story.\n\nHad Mrs. Sedley been a woman of energy, she would have exerted it after her husband's ruin and, occupying a large house, would have taken in boarders. The broken Sedley would have acted well as the boarding-house landlady's husband; the Munoz of private life; the titular lord and master: the carver, house-steward, and humble husband of the occupier of the dingy throne. I have seen men of good brains and breeding, and of good hopes and vigour once, who feasted squires and kept hunters in their youth, meekly cutting up legs of mutton for rancorous old harridans and pretending to preside over their dreary tables--but Mrs. Sedley, we say, had not spirit enough to bustle about for \"a few select inmates to join a cheerful musical family,\" such as one reads of in the Times. She was content to lie on the shore where fortune had stranded her--and you could see that the career of this old couple was over.\n\nI don't think they were unhappy. Perhaps they were a little prouder in their downfall than in their prosperity. Mrs. Sedley was always a great person for her landlady, Mrs. Clapp, when she descended and passed many hours with her in the basement or ornamented kitchen. The Irish maid Betty Flanagan's bonnets and ribbons, her sauciness, her idleness, her reckless prodigality of kitchen candles, her consumption of tea and sugar, and so forth occupied and amused the old lady almost as much as the doings of her former household, when she had Sambo and the coachman, and a groom, and a footboy, and a housekeeper with a regiment of female domestics--her former household, about which the good lady talked a hundred times a day. And besides Betty Flanagan, Mrs. Sedley had all the maids-of-all-work in the street to superintend. She knew how each tenant of the cottages paid or owed his little rent. She stepped aside when Mrs. Rougemont the actress passed with her dubious family. She flung up her head when Mrs. Pestler, the apothecary's lady, drove by in her husband's professional one-horse chaise. She had colloquies with the greengrocer about the pennorth of turnips which Mr. Sedley loved; she kept an eye upon the milkman and the baker's boy; and made visitations to the butcher, who sold hundreds of oxen very likely with less ado than was made about Mrs. Sedley's loin of mutton: and she counted the potatoes under the joint on Sundays, on which days, dressed in her best, she went to church twice and read Blair's Sermons in the evening.\n\nOn that day, for \"business\" prevented him on weekdays from taking such a pleasure, it was old Sedley's delight to take out his little grandson Georgy to the neighbouring parks or Kensington Gardens, to see the soldiers or to feed the ducks. Georgy loved the redcoats, and his grandpapa told him how his father had been a famous soldier, and introduced him to many sergeants and others with Waterloo medals on their breasts, to whom the old grandfather pompously presented the child as the son of Captain Osborne of the --th, who died gloriously on the glorious eighteenth. He has been known to treat some of these non-commissioned gentlemen to a glass of porter, and, indeed, in their first Sunday walks was disposed to spoil little Georgy, sadly gorging the boy with apples and parliament, to the detriment of his health--until Amelia declared that George should never go out with his grandpapa unless the latter promised solemnly, and on his honour, not to give the child any cakes, lollipops, or stall produce whatever.\n\nBetween Mrs. Sedley and her daughter there was a sort of coolness about this boy, and a secret jealousy--for one evening in George's very early days, Amelia, who had been seated at work in their little parlour scarcely remarking that the old lady had quitted the room, ran upstairs instinctively to the nursery at the cries of the child, who had been asleep until that moment--and there found Mrs. Sedley in the act of surreptitiously administering Daffy's Elixir to the infant. Amelia, the gentlest and sweetest of everyday mortals, when she found this meddling with her maternal authority, thrilled and trembled all over with anger. Her cheeks, ordinarily pale, now flushed up, until they were as red as they used to be when she was a child of twelve years old. She seized the baby out of her mother's arms and then grasped at the bottle, leaving the old lady gaping at her, furious, and holding the guilty tea-spoon.\n\nAmelia flung the bottle crashing into the fire-place. \"I will NOT have baby poisoned, Mamma,\" cried Emmy, rocking the infant about violently with both her arms round him and turning with flashing eyes at her mother.\n\n\"Poisoned, Amelia!\" said the old lady; \"this language to me?\"\n\n\"He shall not have any medicine but that which Mr. Pestler sends for him. He told me that Daffy's Elixir was poison.\"\n\n\"Very good: you think I'm a murderess then,\" replied Mrs. Sedley. \"This is the language you use to your mother. I have met with misfortunes: I have sunk low in life: I have kept my carriage, and now walk on foot: but I did not know I was a murderess before, and thank you for the NEWS.\"\n\n\"Mamma,\" said the poor girl, who was always ready for tears--\"you shouldn't be hard upon me. I--I didn't mean--I mean, I did not wish to say you would do any wrong to this dear child, only--\"\n\n\"Oh, no, my love,--only that I was a murderess; in which case I had better go to the Old Bailey. Though I didn't poison YOU, when you were a child, but gave you the best of education and the most expensive masters money could procure. Yes; I've nursed five children and buried three; and the one I loved the best of all, and tended through croup, and teething, and measles, and hooping-cough, and brought up with foreign masters, regardless of expense, and with accomplishments at Minerva House--which I never had when I was a girl--when I was too glad to honour my father and mother, that I might live long in the land, and to be useful, and not to mope all day in my room and act the fine lady--says I'm a murderess. Ah, Mrs. Osborne! may YOU never nourish a viper in your bosom, that's MY prayer.\"\n\n\"Mamma, Mamma!\" cried the bewildered girl; and the child in her arms set up a frantic chorus of shouts. \"A murderess, indeed! Go down on your knees and pray to God to cleanse your wicked ungrateful heart, Amelia, and may He forgive you as I do.\" And Mrs. Sedley tossed out of the room, hissing out the word poison once more, and so ending her charitable benediction.\n\nTill the termination of her natural life, this breach between Mrs. Sedley and her daughter was never thoroughly mended. The quarrel gave the elder lady numberless advantages which she did not fail to turn to account with female ingenuity and perseverance. For instance, she scarcely spoke to Amelia for many weeks afterwards. She warned the domestics not to touch the child, as Mrs. Osborne might be offended. She asked her daughter to see and satisfy herself that there was no poison prepared in the little daily messes that were concocted for Georgy. When neighbours asked after the boy's health, she referred them pointedly to Mrs. Osborne. SHE never ventured to ask whether the baby was well or not. SHE would not touch the child although he was her grandson, and own precious darling, for she was not USED to children, and might kill it. And whenever Mr. Pestler came upon his healing inquisition, she received the doctor with such a sarcastic and scornful demeanour, as made the surgeon declare that not Lady Thistlewood herself, whom he had the honour of attending professionally, could give herself greater airs than old Mrs. Sedley, from whom he never took a fee. And very likely Emmy was jealous too, upon her own part, as what mother is not, of those who would manage her children for her, or become candidates for the first place in their affections. It is certain that when anybody nursed the child, she was uneasy, and that she would no more allow Mrs. Clapp or the domestic to dress or tend him than she would have let them wash her husband's miniature which hung up over her little bed--the same little bed from which the poor girl had gone to his; and to which she retired now for many long, silent, tearful, but happy years.\n\nIn this room was all Amelia's heart and treasure. Here it was that she tended her boy and watched him through the many ills of childhood, with a constant passion of love. The elder George returned in him somehow, only improved, and as if come back from heaven. In a hundred little tones, looks, and movements, the child was so like his father that the widow's heart thrilled as she held him to it; and he would often ask the cause of her tears. It was because of his likeness to his father, she did not scruple to tell him. She talked constantly to him about this dead father, and spoke of her love for George to the innocent and wondering child; much more than she ever had done to George himself, or to any confidante of her youth. To her parents she never talked about this matter, shrinking from baring her heart to them. Little George very likely could understand no better than they, but into his ears she poured her sentimental secrets unreservedly, and into his only. The very joy of this woman was a sort of grief, or so tender, at least, that its expression was tears. Her sensibilities were so weak and tremulous that perhaps they ought not to be talked about in a book. I was told by Dr. Pestler (now a most flourishing lady's physician, with a sumptuous dark green carriage, a prospect of speedy knighthood, and a house in Manchester Square) that her grief at weaning the child was a sight that would have unmanned a Herod. He was very soft-hearted many years ago, and his wife was mortally jealous of Mrs. Amelia, then and long afterwards.\n\nPerhaps the doctor's lady had good reason for her jealousy: most women shared it, of those who formed the small circle of Amelia's acquaintance, and were quite angry at the enthusiasm with which the other sex regarded her. For almost all men who came near her loved her; though no doubt they would be at a loss to tell you why. She was not brilliant, nor witty, nor wise over much, nor extraordinarily handsome. But wherever she went she touched and charmed every one of the male sex, as invariably as she awakened the scorn and incredulity of her own sisterhood. I think it was her weakness which was her principal charm--a kind of sweet submission and softness, which seemed to appeal to each man she met for his sympathy and protection. We have seen how in the regiment, though she spoke but to few of George's comrades there, all the swords of the young fellows at the mess-table would have leapt from their scabbards to fight round her; and so it was in the little narrow lodging-house and circle at Fulham, she interested and pleased everybody. If she had been Mrs. Mango herself, of the great house of Mango, Plantain, and Co., Crutched Friars, and the magnificent proprietress of the Pineries, Fulham, who gave summer dejeuners frequented by Dukes and Earls, and drove about the parish with magnificent yellow liveries and bay horses, such as the royal stables at Kensington themselves could not turn out--I say had she been Mrs. Mango herself, or her son's wife, Lady Mary Mango (daughter of the Earl of Castlemouldy, who condescended to marry the head of the firm), the tradesmen of the neighbourhood could not pay her more honour than they invariably showed to the gentle young widow, when she passed by their doors, or made her humble purchases at their shops.\n\nThus it was not only Mr. Pestler, the medical man, but Mr. Linton the young assistant, who doctored the servant maids and small tradesmen, and might be seen any day reading the Times in the surgery, who openly declared himself the slave of Mrs. Osborne. He was a personable young gentleman, more welcome at Mrs. Sedley's lodgings than his principal; and if anything went wrong with Georgy, he would drop in twice or thrice in the day to see the little chap, and without so much as the thought of a fee. He would abstract lozenges, tamarinds, and other produce from the surgery-drawers for little Georgy's benefit, and compounded draughts and mixtures for him of miraculous sweetness, so that it was quite a pleasure to the child to be ailing. He and Pestler, his chief, sat up two whole nights by the boy in that momentous and awful week when Georgy had the measles; and when you would have thought, from the mother's terror, that there had never been measles in the world before. Would they have done as much for other people? Did they sit up for the folks at the Pineries, when Ralph Plantagenet, and Gwendoline, and Guinever Mango had the same juvenile complaint? Did they sit up for little Mary Clapp, the landlord's daughter, who actually caught the disease of little Georgy? Truth compels one to say, no. They slept quite undisturbed, at least as far as she was concerned--pronounced hers to be a slight case, which would almost cure itself, sent her in a draught or two, and threw in bark when the child rallied, with perfect indifference, and just for form's sake.\n\nAgain, there was the little French chevalier opposite, who gave lessons in his native tongue at various schools in the neighbourhood, and who might be heard in his apartment of nights playing tremulous old gavottes and minuets on a wheezy old fiddle. Whenever this powdered and courteous old man, who never missed a Sunday at the convent chapel at Hammersmith, and who was in all respects, thoughts, conduct, and bearing utterly unlike the bearded savages of his nation, who curse perfidious Albion, and scowl at you from over their cigars, in the Quadrant arcades at the present day--whenever the old Chevalier de Talonrouge spoke of Mistress Osborne, he would first finish his pinch of snuff, flick away the remaining particles of dust with a graceful wave of his hand, gather up his fingers again into a bunch, and, bringing them up to his mouth, blow them open with a kiss, exclaiming, Ah! la divine creature! He vowed and protested that when Amelia walked in the Brompton Lanes flowers grew in profusion under her feet. He called little Georgy Cupid, and asked him news of Venus, his mamma; and told the astonished Betty Flanagan that she was one of the Graces, and the favourite attendant of the Reine des Amours.\n\nInstances might be multiplied of this easily gained and unconscious popularity. Did not Mr. Binny, the mild and genteel curate of the district chapel, which the family attended, call assiduously upon the widow, dandle the little boy on his knee, and offer to teach him Latin, to the anger of the elderly virgin, his sister, who kept house for him? \"There is nothing in her, Beilby,\" the latter lady would say. \"When she comes to tea here she does not speak a word during the whole evening. She is but a poor lackadaisical creature, and it is my belief has no heart at all. It is only her pretty face which all you gentlemen admire so. Miss Grits, who has five thousand pounds, and expectations besides, has twice as much character, and is a thousand times more agreeable to my taste; and if she were good-looking I know that you would think her perfection.\"\n\nVery likely Miss Binny was right to a great extent. It IS the pretty face which creates sympathy in the hearts of men, those wicked rogues. A woman may possess the wisdom and chastity of Minerva, and we give no heed to her, if she has a plain face. What folly will not a pair of bright eyes make pardonable? What dulness may not red lips and sweet accents render pleasant? And so, with their usual sense of justice, ladies argue that because a woman is handsome, therefore she is a fool. O ladies, ladies! there are some of you who are neither handsome nor wise.\n\nThese are but trivial incidents to recount in the life of our heroine. Her tale does not deal in wonders, as the gentle reader has already no doubt perceived; and if a journal had been kept of her proceedings during the seven years after the birth of her son, there would be found few incidents more remarkable in it than that of the measles, recorded in the foregoing page. Yes, one day, and greatly to her wonder, the Reverend Mr. Binny, just mentioned, asked her to change her name of Osborne for his own; when, with deep blushes and tears in her eyes and voice, she thanked him for his regard for her, expressed gratitude for his attentions to her and to her poor little boy, but said that she never, never could think of any but--but the husband whom she had lost.\n\nOn the twenty-fifth of April, and the eighteenth of June, the days of marriage and widowhood, she kept her room entirely, consecrating them (and we do not know how many hours of solitary night-thought, her little boy sleeping in his crib by her bedside) to the memory of that departed friend. During the day she was more active. She had to teach George to read and to write and a little to draw. She read books, in order that she might tell him stories from them. As his eyes opened and his mind expanded under the influence of the outward nature round about him, she taught the child, to the best of her humble power, to acknowledge the Maker of all, and every night and every morning he and she--(in that awful and touching communion which I think must bring a thrill to the heart of every man who witnesses or who remembers it)--the mother and the little boy--prayed to Our Father together, the mother pleading with all her gentle heart, the child lisping after her as she spoke. And each time they prayed to God to bless dear Papa, as if he were alive and in the room with them. To wash and dress this young gentleman--to take him for a run of the mornings, before breakfast, and the retreat of grandpapa for \"business\"--to make for him the most wonderful and ingenious dresses, for which end the thrifty widow cut up and altered every available little bit of finery which she possessed out of her wardrobe during her marriage--for Mrs. Osborne herself (greatly to her mother's vexation, who preferred fine clothes, especially since her misfortunes) always wore a black gown and a straw bonnet with a black ribbon--occupied her many hours of the day. Others she had to spare, at the service of her mother and her old father. She had taken the pains to learn, and used to play cribbage with this gentleman on the nights when he did not go to his club. She sang for him when he was so minded, and it was a good sign, for he invariably fell into a comfortable sleep during the music. She wrote out his numerous memorials, letters, prospectuses, and projects. It was in her handwriting that most of the old gentleman's former acquaintances were informed that he had become an agent for the Black Diamond and Anti-Cinder Coal Company and could supply his friends and the public with the best coals at --s. per chaldron. All he did was to sign the circulars with his flourish and signature, and direct them in a shaky, clerklike hand. One of these papers was sent to Major Dobbin,--Regt., care of Messrs. Cox and Greenwood; but the Major being in Madras at the time, had no particular call for coals. He knew, though, the hand which had written the prospectus. Good God! what would he not have given to hold it in his own! A second prospectus came out, informing the Major that J. Sedley and Company, having established agencies at Oporto, Bordeaux, and St. Mary's, were enabled to offer to their friends and the public generally the finest and most celebrated growths of ports, sherries, and claret wines at reasonable prices and under extraordinary advantages. Acting upon this hint, Dobbin furiously canvassed the governor, the commander-in-chief, the judges, the regiments, and everybody whom he knew in the Presidency, and sent home to Sedley and Co. orders for wine which perfectly astonished Mr. Sedley and Mr. Clapp, who was the Co. in the business. But no more orders came after that first burst of good fortune, on which poor old Sedley was about to build a house in the City, a regiment of clerks, a dock to himself, and correspondents all over the world. The old gentleman's former taste in wine had gone: the curses of the mess-room assailed Major Dobbin for the vile drinks he had been the means of introducing there; and he bought back a great quantity of the wine and sold it at public outcry, at an enormous loss to himself. As for Jos, who was by this time promoted to a seat at the Revenue Board at Calcutta, he was wild with rage when the post brought him out a bundle of these Bacchanalian prospectuses, with a private note from his father, telling Jos that his senior counted upon him in this enterprise, and had consigned a quantity of select wines to him, as per invoice, drawing bills upon him for the amount of the same. Jos, who would no more have it supposed that his father, Jos Sedley's father, of the Board of Revenue, was a wine merchant asking for orders, than that he was Jack Ketch, refused the bills with scorn, wrote back contumeliously to the old gentleman, bidding him to mind his own affairs; and the protested paper coming back, Sedley and Co. had to take it up, with the profits which they had made out of the Madras venture, and with a little portion of Emmy's savings.\n\nBesides her pension of fifty pounds a year, there had been five hundred pounds, as her husband's executor stated, left in the agent's hands at the time of Osborne's demise, which sum, as George's guardian, Dobbin proposed to put out at 8 per cent in an Indian house of agency. Mr. Sedley, who thought the Major had some roguish intentions of his own about the money, was strongly against this plan; and he went to the agents to protest personally against the employment of the money in question, when he learned, to his surprise, that there had been no such sum in their hands, that all the late Captain's assets did not amount to a hundred pounds, and that the five hundred pounds in question must be a separate sum, of which Major Dobbin knew the particulars. More than ever convinced that there was some roguery, old Sedley pursued the Major. As his daughter's nearest friend, he demanded with a high hand a statement of the late Captain's accounts. Dobbin's stammering, blushing, and awkwardness added to the other's convictions that he had a rogue to deal with, and in a majestic tone he told that officer a piece of his mind, as he called it, simply stating his belief that the Major was unlawfully detaining his late son-in-law's money.\n\nDobbin at this lost all patience, and if his accuser had not been so old and so broken, a quarrel might have ensued between them at the Slaughters' Coffee-house, in a box of which place of entertainment the gentlemen had their colloquy. \"Come upstairs, sir,\" lisped out the Major. \"I insist on your coming up the stairs, and I will show which is the injured party, poor George or I\"; and, dragging the old gentleman up to his bedroom, he produced from his desk Osborne's accounts, and a bundle of IOU's which the latter had given, who, to do him justice, was always ready to give an IOU. \"He paid his bills in England,\" Dobbin added, \"but he had not a hundred pounds in the world when he fell. I and one or two of his brother officers made up the little sum, which was all that we could spare, and you dare tell us that we are trying to cheat the widow and the orphan.\" Sedley was very contrite and humbled, though the fact is that William Dobbin had told a great falsehood to the old gentleman; having himself given every shilling of the money, having buried his friend, and paid all the fees and charges incident upon the calamity and removal of poor Amelia.\n\nAbout these expenses old Osborne had never given himself any trouble to think, nor any other relative of Amelia, nor Amelia herself, indeed. She trusted to Major Dobbin as an accountant, took his somewhat confused calculations for granted, and never once suspected how much she was in his debt.\n\nTwice or thrice in the year, according to her promise, she wrote him letters to Madras, letters all about little Georgy. How he treasured these papers! Whenever Amelia wrote he answered, and not until then. But he sent over endless remembrances of himself to his godson and to her. He ordered and sent a box of scarfs and a grand ivory set of chess-men from China. The pawns were little green and white men, with real swords and shields; the knights were on horseback, the castles were on the backs of elephants. \"Mrs. Mango's own set at the Pineries was not so fine,\" Mr. Pestler remarked. These chess-men were the delight of Georgy's life, who printed his first letter in acknowledgement of this gift of his godpapa. He sent over preserves and pickles, which latter the young gentleman tried surreptitiously in the sideboard and half-killed himself with eating. He thought it was a judgement upon him for stealing, they were so hot. Emmy wrote a comical little account of this mishap to the Major: it pleased him to think that her spirits were rallying and that she could be merry sometimes now. He sent over a pair of shawls, a white one for her and a black one with palm-leaves for her mother, and a pair of red scarfs, as winter wrappers, for old Mr. Sedley and George. The shawls were worth fifty guineas apiece at the very least, as Mrs. Sedley knew. She wore hers in state at church at Brompton, and was congratulated by her female friends upon the splendid acquisition. Emmy's, too, became prettily her modest black gown. \"What a pity it is she won't think of him!\" Mrs. Sedley remarked to Mrs. Clapp and to all her friends of Brompton. \"Jos never sent us such presents, I am sure, and grudges us everything. It is evident that the Major is over head and ears in love with her; and yet, whenever I so much as hint it, she turns red and begins to cry and goes and sits upstairs with her miniature. I'm sick of that miniature. I wish we had never seen those odious purse-proud Osbornes.\"\n\nAmidst such humble scenes and associates George's early youth was passed, and the boy grew up delicate, sensitive, imperious, woman-bred--domineering the gentle mother whom he loved with passionate affection. He ruled all the rest of the little world round about him. As he grew, the elders were amazed at his haughty manner and his constant likeness to his father. He asked questions about everything, as inquiring youth will do. The profundity of his remarks and interrogatories astonished his old grandfather, who perfectly bored the club at the tavern with stories about the little lad's learning and genius. He suffered his grandmother with a good-humoured indifference. The small circle round about him believed that the equal of the boy did not exist upon the earth. Georgy inherited his father's pride, and perhaps thought they were not wrong.\n\nWhen he grew to be about six years old, Dobbin began to write to him very much. The Major wanted to hear that Georgy was going to a school and hoped he would acquit himself with credit there: or would he have a good tutor at home? It was time that he should begin to learn; and his godfather and guardian hinted that he hoped to be allowed to defray the charges of the boy's education, which would fall heavily upon his mother's straitened income. The Major, in a word, was always thinking about Amelia and her little boy, and by orders to his agents kept the latter provided with picture-books, paint-boxes, desks, and all conceivable implements of amusement and instruction. Three days before George's sixth birthday a gentleman in a gig, accompanied by a servant, drove up to Mr. Sedley's house and asked to see Master George Osborne: it was Mr. Woolsey, military tailor, of Conduit Street, who came at the Major's order to measure the young gentleman for a suit of clothes. He had had the honour of making for the Captain, the young gentleman's father. Sometimes, too, and by the Major's desire no doubt, his sisters, the Misses Dobbin, would call in the family carriage to take Amelia and the little boy to drive if they were so inclined. The patronage and kindness of these ladies was very uncomfortable to Amelia, but she bore it meekly enough, for her nature was to yield; and, besides, the carriage and its splendours gave little Georgy immense pleasure. The ladies begged occasionally that the child might pass a day with them, and he was always glad to go to that fine garden-house at Denmark Hill, where they lived, and where there were such fine grapes in the hot-houses and peaches on the walls.\n\nOne day they kindly came over to Amelia with news which they were SURE would delight her--something VERY interesting about their dear William.\n\n\"What was it: was he coming home?\" she asked with pleasure beaming in her eyes.\n\n\"Oh, no--not the least--but they had very good reason to believe that dear William was about to be married--and to a relation of a very dear friend of Amelia's--to Miss Glorvina O'Dowd, Sir Michael O'Dowd's sister, who had gone out to join Lady O'Dowd at Madras--a very beautiful and accomplished girl, everybody said.\"\n\nAmelia said \"Oh!\" Amelia was very VERY happy indeed. But she supposed Glorvina could not be like her old acquaintance, who was most kind--but--but she was very happy indeed. And by some impulse of which I cannot explain the meaning, she took George in her arms and kissed him with an extraordinary tenderness. Her eyes were quite moist when she put the child down; and she scarcely spoke a word during the whole of the drive--though she was so very happy indeed.\n\nA Cynical Chapter\nOur duty now takes us back for a brief space to some old Hampshire acquaintances of ours, whose hopes respecting the disposal of their rich kinswoman's property were so woefully disappointed. After counting upon thirty thousand pounds from his sister, it was a heavy blow to Bute Crawley to receive but five; out of which sum, when he had paid his own debts and those of Jim, his son at college, a very small fragment remained to portion off his four plain daughters. Mrs. Bute never knew, or at least never acknowledged, how far her own tyrannous behaviour had tended to ruin her husband. All that woman could do, she vowed and protested she had done. Was it her fault if she did not possess those sycophantic arts which her hypocritical nephew, Pitt Crawley, practised? She wished him all the happiness which he merited out of his ill-gotten gains. \"At least the money will remain in the family,\" she said charitably. \"Pitt will never spend it, my dear, that is quite certain; for a greater miser does not exist in England, and he is as odious, though in a different way, as his spendthrift brother, the abandoned Rawdon.\"\n\nSo Mrs. Bute, after the first shock of rage and disappointment, began to accommodate herself as best she could to her altered fortunes and to save and retrench with all her might. She instructed her daughters how to bear poverty cheerfully, and invented a thousand notable methods to conceal or evade it. She took them about to balls and public places in the neighbourhood, with praiseworthy energy; nay, she entertained her friends in a hospitable comfortable manner at the Rectory, and much more frequently than before dear Miss Crawley's legacy had fallen in. From her outward bearing nobody would have supposed that the family had been disappointed in their expectations, or have guessed from her frequent appearance in public how she pinched and starved at home. Her girls had more milliners' furniture than they had ever enjoyed before. They appeared perseveringly at the Winchester and Southampton assemblies; they penetrated to Cowes for the race-balls and regatta-gaieties there; and their carriage, with the horses taken from the plough, was at work perpetually, until it began almost to be believed that the four sisters had had fortunes left them by their aunt, whose name the family never mentioned in public but with the most tender gratitude and regard. I know no sort of lying which is more frequent in Vanity Fair than this, and it may be remarked how people who practise it take credit to themselves for their hypocrisy, and fancy that they are exceedingly virtuous and praiseworthy, because they are able to deceive the world with regard to the extent of their means.\n\nMrs. Bute certainly thought herself one of the most virtuous women in England, and the sight of her happy family was an edifying one to strangers. They were so cheerful, so loving, so well-educated, so simple! Martha painted flowers exquisitely and furnished half the charity bazaars in the county. Emma was a regular County Bulbul, and her verses in the Hampshire Telegraph were the glory of its Poet's Corner. Fanny and Matilda sang duets together, Mamma playing the piano, and the other two sisters sitting with their arms round each other's waists and listening affectionately. Nobody saw the poor girls drumming at the duets in private. No one saw Mamma drilling them rigidly hour after hour. In a word, Mrs. Bute put a good face against fortune and kept up appearances in the most virtuous manner.\n\nEverything that a good and respectable mother could do Mrs. Bute did. She got over yachting men from Southampton, parsons from the Cathedral Close at Winchester, and officers from the barracks there. She tried to inveigle the young barristers at assizes and encouraged Jim to bring home friends with whom he went out hunting with the H. H. What will not a mother do for the benefit of her beloved ones?\n\nBetween such a woman and her brother-in-law, the odious Baronet at the Hall, it is manifest that there could be very little in common. The rupture between Bute and his brother Sir Pitt was complete; indeed, between Sir Pitt and the whole county, to which the old man was a scandal. His dislike for respectable society increased with age, and the lodge-gates had not opened to a gentleman's carriage-wheels since Pitt and Lady Jane came to pay their visit of duty after their marriage.\n\nThat was an awful and unfortunate visit, never to be thought of by the family without horror. Pitt begged his wife, with a ghastly countenance, never to speak of it, and it was only through Mrs. Bute herself, who still knew everything which took place at the Hall, that the circumstances of Sir Pitt's reception of his son and daughter-in-law were ever known at all.\n\nAs they drove up the avenue of the park in their neat and well-appointed carriage, Pitt remarked with dismay and wrath great gaps among the trees--his trees--which the old Baronet was felling entirely without license. The park wore an aspect of utter dreariness and ruin. The drives were ill kept, and the neat carriage splashed and floundered in muddy pools along the road. The great sweep in front of the terrace and entrance stair was black and covered with mosses; the once trim flower-beds rank and weedy. Shutters were up along almost the whole line of the house; the great hall-door was unbarred after much ringing of the bell; an individual in ribbons was seen flitting up the black oak stair, as Horrocks at length admitted the heir of Queen's Crawley and his bride into the halls of their fathers. He led the way into Sir Pitt's \"Library,\" as it was called, the fumes of tobacco growing stronger as Pitt and Lady Jane approached that apartment, \"Sir Pitt ain't very well,\" Horrocks remarked apologetically and hinted that his master was afflicted with lumbago.\n\nThe library looked out on the front walk and park. Sir Pitt had opened one of the windows, and was bawling out thence to the postilion and Pitt's servant, who seemed to be about to take the baggage down.\n\n\"Don't move none of them trunks,\" he cried, pointing with a pipe which he held in his hand. \"It's only a morning visit, Tucker, you fool. Lor, what cracks that off hoss has in his heels! Ain't there no one at the King's Head to rub 'em a little? How do, Pitt? How do, my dear? Come to see the old man, hay? 'Gad--you've a pretty face, too. You ain't like that old horse-godmother, your mother. Come and give old Pitt a kiss, like a good little gal.\"\n\nThe embrace disconcerted the daughter-in-law somewhat, as the caresses of the old gentleman, unshorn and perfumed with tobacco, might well do. But she remembered that her brother Southdown had mustachios, and smoked cigars, and submitted to the Baronet with a tolerable grace.\n\n\"Pitt has got vat,\" said the Baronet, after this mark of affection. \"Does he read ee very long zermons, my dear? Hundredth Psalm, Evening Hymn, hay Pitt? Go and get a glass of Malmsey and a cake for my Lady Jane, Horrocks, you great big booby, and don't stand stearing there like a fat pig. I won't ask you to stop, my dear; you'll find it too stoopid, and so should I too along a Pitt. I'm an old man now, and like my own ways, and my pipe and backgammon of a night.\"\n\n\"I can play at backgammon, sir,\" said Lady Jane, laughing. \"I used to play with Papa and Miss Crawley, didn't I, Mr. Crawley?\"\n\n\"Lady Jane can play, sir, at the game to which you state that you are so partial,\" Pitt said haughtily.\n\n\"But she wawn't stop for all that. Naw, naw, goo back to Mudbury and give Mrs. Rincer a benefit; or drive down to the Rectory and ask Buty for a dinner. He'll be charmed to see you, you know; he's so much obliged to you for gettin' the old woman's money. Ha, ha! Some of it will do to patch up the Hall when I'm gone.\"\n\n\"I perceive, sir,\" said Pitt with a heightened voice, \"that your people will cut down the timber.\"\n\n\"Yees, yees, very fine weather, and seasonable for the time of year,\" Sir Pitt answered, who had suddenly grown deaf. \"But I'm gittin' old, Pitt, now. Law bless you, you ain't far from fifty yourself. But he wears well, my pretty Lady Jane, don't he? It's all godliness, sobriety, and a moral life. Look at me, I'm not very fur from fowr-score--he, he\"; and he laughed, and took snuff, and leered at her and pinched her hand.\n\nPitt once more brought the conversation back to the timber, but the Baronet was deaf again in an instant.\n\n\"I'm gittin' very old, and have been cruel bad this year with the lumbago. I shan't be here now for long; but I'm glad ee've come, daughter-in-law. I like your face, Lady Jane: it's got none of the damned high-boned Binkie look in it; and I'll give ee something pretty, my dear, to go to Court in.\" And he shuffled across the room to a cupboard, from which he took a little old case containing jewels of some value. \"Take that,\" said he, \"my dear; it belonged to my mother, and afterwards to the first Lady Binkie. Pretty pearls--never gave 'em the ironmonger's daughter. No, no. Take 'em and put 'em up quick,\" said he, thrusting the case into his daughter's hand, and clapping the door of the cabinet to, as Horrocks entered with a salver and refreshments.\n\n\"What have you a been and given Pitt's wife?\" said the individual in ribbons, when Pitt and Lady Jane had taken leave of the old gentleman. It was Miss Horrocks, the butler's daughter--the cause of the scandal throughout the county--the lady who reigned now almost supreme at Queen's Crawley.\n\nThe rise and progress of those Ribbons had been marked with dismay by the county and family. The Ribbons opened an account at the Mudbury Branch Savings Bank; the Ribbons drove to church, monopolising the pony-chaise, which was for the use of the servants at the Hall. The domestics were dismissed at her pleasure. The Scotch gardener, who still lingered on the premises, taking a pride in his walls and hot-houses, and indeed making a pretty good livelihood by the garden, which he farmed, and of which he sold the produce at Southampton, found the Ribbons eating peaches on a sunshiny morning at the south-wall, and had his ears boxed when he remonstrated about this attack on his property. He and his Scotch wife and his Scotch children, the only respectable inhabitants of Queen's Crawley, were forced to migrate, with their goods and their chattels, and left the stately comfortable gardens to go to waste, and the flower-beds to run to seed. Poor Lady Crawley's rose-garden became the dreariest wilderness. Only two or three domestics shuddered in the bleak old servants' hall. The stables and offices were vacant, and shut up, and half ruined. Sir Pitt lived in private, and boozed nightly with Horrocks, his butler or house-steward (as he now began to be called), and the abandoned Ribbons. The times were very much changed since the period when she drove to Mudbury in the spring-cart and called the small tradesmen \"Sir.\" It may have been shame, or it may have been dislike of his neighbours, but the old Cynic of Queen's Crawley hardly issued from his park-gates at all now. He quarrelled with his agents and screwed his tenants by letter. His days were passed in conducting his own correspondence; the lawyers and farm-bailiffs who had to do business with him could not reach him but through the Ribbons, who received them at the door of the housekeeper's room, which commanded the back entrance by which they were admitted; and so the Baronet's daily perplexities increased, and his embarrassments multiplied round him.\n\nThe horror of Pitt Crawley may be imagined, as these reports of his father's dotage reached the most exemplary and correct of gentlemen. He trembled daily lest he should hear that the Ribbons was proclaimed his second legal mother-in-law. After that first and last visit, his father's name was never mentioned in Pitt's polite and genteel establishment. It was the skeleton in his house, and all the family walked by it in terror and silence. The Countess Southdown kept on dropping per coach at the lodge-gate the most exciting tracts, tracts which ought to frighten the hair off your head. Mrs. Bute at the parsonage nightly looked out to see if the sky was red over the elms behind which the Hall stood, and the mansion was on fire. Sir G. Wapshot and Sir H. Fuddlestone, old friends of the house, wouldn't sit on the bench with Sir Pitt at Quarter Sessions, and cut him dead in the High Street of Southampton, where the reprobate stood offering his dirty old hands to them. Nothing had any effect upon him; he put his hands into his pockets, and burst out laughing, as he scrambled into his carriage and four; he used to burst out laughing at Lady Southdown's tracts; and he laughed at his sons, and at the world, and at the Ribbons when she was angry, which was not seldom.\n\nMiss Horrocks was installed as housekeeper at Queen's Crawley, and ruled all the domestics there with great majesty and rigour. All the servants were instructed to address her as \"Mum,\" or \"Madam\"--and there was one little maid, on her promotion, who persisted in calling her \"My Lady,\" without any rebuke on the part of the housekeeper. \"There has been better ladies, and there has been worser, Hester,\" was Miss Horrocks' reply to this compliment of her inferior; so she ruled, having supreme power over all except her father, whom, however, she treated with considerable haughtiness, warning him not to be too familiar in his behaviour to one \"as was to be a Baronet's lady.\" Indeed, she rehearsed that exalted part in life with great satisfaction to herself, and to the amusement of old Sir Pitt, who chuckled at her airs and graces, and would laugh by the hour together at her assumptions of dignity and imitations of genteel life. He swore it was as good as a play to see her in the character of a fine dame, and he made her put on one of the first Lady Crawley's court-dresses, swearing (entirely to Miss Horrocks' own concurrence) that the dress became her prodigiously, and threatening to drive her off that very instant to Court in a coach-and-four. She had the ransacking of the wardrobes of the two defunct ladies, and cut and hacked their posthumous finery so as to suit her own tastes and figure. And she would have liked to take possession of their jewels and trinkets too; but the old Baronet had locked them away in his private cabinet; nor could she coax or wheedle him out of the keys. And it is a fact, that some time after she left Queen's Crawley a copy-book belonging to this lady was discovered, which showed that she had taken great pains in private to learn the art of writing in general, and especially of writing her own name as Lady Crawley, Lady Betsy Horrocks, Lady Elizabeth Crawley, &c.\n\nThough the good people of the Parsonage never went to the Hall and shunned the horrid old dotard its owner, yet they kept a strict knowledge of all that happened there, and were looking out every day for the catastrophe for which Miss Horrocks was also eager. But Fate intervened enviously and prevented her from receiving the reward due to such immaculate love and virtue.\n\nOne day the Baronet surprised \"her ladyship,\" as he jocularly called her, seated at that old and tuneless piano in the drawing-room, which had scarcely been touched since Becky Sharp played quadrilles upon it--seated at the piano with the utmost gravity and squalling to the best of her power in imitation of the music which she had sometimes heard. The little kitchen-maid on her promotion was standing at her mistress's side, quite delighted during the operation, and wagging her head up and down and crying, \"Lor, Mum, 'tis bittiful\"--just like a genteel sycophant in a real drawing-room.\n\nThis incident made the old Baronet roar with laughter, as usual. He narrated the circumstance a dozen times to Horrocks in the course of the evening, and greatly to the discomfiture of Miss Horrocks. He thrummed on the table as if it had been a musical instrument, and squalled in imitation of her manner of singing. He vowed that such a beautiful voice ought to be cultivated and declared she ought to have singing-masters, in which proposals she saw nothing ridiculous. He was in great spirits that night, and drank with his friend and butler an extraordinary quantity of rum-and-water--at a very late hour the faithful friend and domestic conducted his master to his bedroom.\n\nHalf an hour afterwards there was a great hurry and bustle in the house. Lights went about from window to window in the lonely desolate old Hall, whereof but two or three rooms were ordinarily occupied by its owner. Presently, a boy on a pony went galloping off to Mudbury, to the Doctor's house there. And in another hour (by which fact we ascertain how carefully the excellent Mrs. Bute Crawley had always kept up an understanding with the great house), that lady in her clogs and calash, the Reverend Bute Crawley, and James Crawley, her son, had walked over from the Rectory through the park, and had entered the mansion by the open hall-door.\n\nThey passed through the hall and the small oak parlour, on the table of which stood the three tumblers and the empty rum-bottle which had served for Sir Pitt's carouse, and through that apartment into Sir Pitt's study, where they found Miss Horrocks, of the guilty ribbons, with a wild air, trying at the presses and escritoires with a bunch of keys. She dropped them with a scream of terror, as little Mrs. Bute's eyes flashed out at her from under her black calash.\n\n\"Look at that, James and Mr. Crawley,\" cried Mrs. Bute, pointing at the scared figure of the black-eyed, guilty wench.\n\n\"He gave 'em me; he gave 'em me!\" she cried.\n\n\"Gave them you, you abandoned creature!\" screamed Mrs. Bute. \"Bear witness, Mr. Crawley, we found this good-for-nothing woman in the act of stealing your brother's property; and she will be hanged, as I always said she would.\"\n\nBetsy Horrocks, quite daunted, flung herself down on her knees, bursting into tears. But those who know a really good woman are aware that she is not in a hurry to forgive, and that the humiliation of an enemy is a triumph to her soul.\n\n\"Ring the bell, James,\" Mrs. Bute said. \"Go on ringing it till the people come.\" The three or four domestics resident in the deserted old house came presently at that jangling and continued summons.\n\n\"Put that woman in the strong-room,\" she said. \"We caught her in the act of robbing Sir Pitt. Mr. Crawley, you'll make out her committal--and, Beddoes, you'll drive her over in the spring cart, in the morning, to Southampton Gaol.\"\n\n\"My dear,\" interposed the Magistrate and Rector--\"she's only--\"\n\n\"Are there no handcuffs?\" Mrs. Bute continued, stamping in her clogs. \"There used to be handcuffs. Where's the creature's abominable father?\"\n\n\"He DID give 'em me,\" still cried poor Betsy; \"didn't he, Hester? You saw Sir Pitt--you know you did--give 'em me, ever so long ago--the day after Mudbury fair: not that I want 'em. Take 'em if you think they ain't mine.\" And here the unhappy wretch pulled out from her pocket a large pair of paste shoe-buckles which had excited her admiration, and which she had just appropriated out of one of the bookcases in the study, where they had lain.\n\n\"Law, Betsy, how could you go for to tell such a wicked story!\" said Hester, the little kitchen-maid late on her promotion--\"and to Madame Crawley, so good and kind, and his Rev'rince (with a curtsey), and you may search all MY boxes, Mum, I'm sure, and here's my keys as I'm an honest girl, though of pore parents and workhouse bred--and if you find so much as a beggarly bit of lace or a silk stocking out of all the gownds as YOU'VE had the picking of, may I never go to church agin.\"\n\n\"Give up your keys, you hardened hussy,\" hissed out the virtuous little lady in the calash.\n\n\"And here's a candle, Mum, and if you please, Mum, I can show you her room, Mum, and the press in the housekeeper's room, Mum, where she keeps heaps and heaps of things, Mum,\" cried out the eager little Hester with a profusion of curtseys.\n\n\"Hold your tongue, if you please. I know the room which the creature occupies perfectly well. Mrs. Brown, have the goodness to come with me, and Beddoes don't you lose sight of that woman,\" said Mrs. Bute, seizing the candle. \"Mr. Crawley, you had better go upstairs and see that they are not murdering your unfortunate brother\"--and the calash, escorted by Mrs. Brown, walked away to the apartment which, as she said truly, she knew perfectly well.\n\nBute went upstairs and found the Doctor from Mudbury, with the frightened Horrocks over his master in a chair. They were trying to bleed Sir Pitt Crawley.\n\nWith the early morning an express was sent off to Mr. Pitt Crawley by the Rector's lady, who assumed the command of everything, and had watched the old Baronet through the night. He had been brought back to a sort of life; he could not speak, but seemed to recognize people. Mrs. Bute kept resolutely by his bedside. She never seemed to want to sleep, that little woman, and did not close her fiery black eyes once, though the Doctor snored in the arm-chair. Horrocks made some wild efforts to assert his authority and assist his master; but Mrs. Bute called him a tipsy old wretch and bade him never show his face again in that house, or he should be transported like his abominable daughter.\n\nTerrified by her manner, he slunk down to the oak parlour where Mr. James was, who, having tried the bottle standing there and found no liquor in it, ordered Mr. Horrocks to get another bottle of rum, which he fetched, with clean glasses, and to which the Rector and his son sat down, ordering Horrocks to put down the keys at that instant and never to show his face again.\n\nCowed by this behaviour, Horrocks gave up the keys, and he and his daughter slunk off silently through the night and gave up possession of the house of Queen's Crawley.\n\nIn Which Becky Is Recognized by the Family\nThe heir of Crawley arrived at home, in due time, after this catastrophe, and henceforth may be said to have reigned in Queen's Crawley. For though the old Baronet survived many months, he never recovered the use of his intellect or his speech completely, and the government of the estate devolved upon his elder son. In a strange condition Pitt found it. Sir Pitt was always buying and mortgaging; he had twenty men of business, and quarrels with each; quarrels with all his tenants, and lawsuits with them; lawsuits with the lawyers; lawsuits with the Mining and Dock Companies in which he was proprietor; and with every person with whom he had business. To unravel these difficulties and to set the estate clear was a task worthy of the orderly and persevering diplomatist of Pumpernickel, and he set himself to work with prodigious assiduity. His whole family, of course, was transported to Queen's Crawley, whither Lady Southdown, of course, came too; and she set about converting the parish under the Rector's nose, and brought down her irregular clergy to the dismay of the angry Mrs Bute. Sir Pitt had concluded no bargain for the sale of the living of Queen's Crawley; when it should drop, her Ladyship proposed to take the patronage into her own hands and present a young protege to the Rectory, on which subject the diplomatic Pitt said nothing.\n\nMrs. Bute's intentions with regard to Miss Betsy Horrocks were not carried into effect, and she paid no visit to Southampton Gaol. She and her father left the Hall when the latter took possession of the Crawley Arms in the village, of which he had got a lease from Sir Pitt. The ex-butler had obtained a small freehold there likewise, which gave him a vote for the borough. The Rector had another of these votes, and these and four others formed the representative body which returned the two members for Queen's Crawley.\n\nThere was a show of courtesy kept up between the Rectory and the Hall ladies, between the younger ones at least, for Mrs. Bute and Lady Southdown never could meet without battles, and gradually ceased seeing each other. Her Ladyship kept her room when the ladies from the Rectory visited their cousins at the Hall. Perhaps Mr. Pitt was not very much displeased at these occasional absences of his mamma-in-law. He believed the Binkie family to be the greatest and wisest and most interesting in the world, and her Ladyship and his aunt had long held ascendency over him; but sometimes he felt that she commanded him too much. To be considered young was complimentary, doubtless, but at six-and-forty to be treated as a boy was sometimes mortifying. Lady Jane yielded up everything, however, to her mother. She was only fond of her children in private, and it was lucky for her that Lady Southdown's multifarious business, her conferences with ministers, and her correspondence with all the missionaries of Africa, Asia, and Australasia, &c., occupied the venerable Countess a great deal, so that she had but little time to devote to her granddaughter, the little Matilda, and her grandson, Master Pitt Crawley. The latter was a feeble child, and it was only by prodigious quantities of calomel that Lady Southdown was able to keep him in life at all.\n\nAs for Sir Pitt he retired into those very apartments where Lady Crawley had been previously extinguished, and here was tended by Miss Hester, the girl upon her promotion, with constant care and assiduity. What love, what fidelity, what constancy is there equal to that of a nurse with good wages? They smooth pillows; and make arrowroot; they get up at nights; they bear complaints and querulousness; they see the sun shining out of doors and don't want to go abroad; they sleep on arm-chairs and eat their meals in solitude; they pass long long evenings doing nothing, watching the embers, and the patient's drink simmering in the jug; they read the weekly paper the whole week through; and Law's Serious Call or the Whole Duty of Man suffices them for literature for the year--and we quarrel with them because, when their relations come to see them once a week, a little gin is smuggled in in their linen basket. Ladies, what man's love is there that would stand a year's nursing of the object of his affection? Whereas a nurse will stand by you for ten pounds a quarter, and we think her too highly paid. At least Mr. Crawley grumbled a good deal about paying half as much to Miss Hester for her constant attendance upon the Baronet his father.\n\nOf sunshiny days this old gentleman was taken out in a chair on the terrace--the very chair which Miss Crawley had had at Brighton, and which had been transported thence with a number of Lady Southdown's effects to Queen's Crawley. Lady Jane always walked by the old man, and was an evident favourite with him. He used to nod many times to her and smile when she came in, and utter inarticulate deprecatory moans when she was going away. When the door shut upon her he would cry and sob--whereupon Hester's face and manner, which was always exceedingly bland and gentle while her lady was present, would change at once, and she would make faces at him and clench her fist and scream out \"Hold your tongue, you stoopid old fool,\" and twirl away his chair from the fire which he loved to look at--at which he would cry more. For this was all that was left after more than seventy years of cunning, and struggling, and drinking, and scheming, and sin and selfishness--a whimpering old idiot put in and out of bed and cleaned and fed like a baby.\n\nAt last a day came when the nurse's occupation was over. Early one morning, as Pitt Crawley was at his steward's and bailiff's books in the study, a knock came to the door, and Hester presented herself, dropping a curtsey, and said,\n\n\"If you please, Sir Pitt, Sir Pitt died this morning, Sir Pitt. I was a-making of his toast, Sir Pitt, for his gruel, Sir Pitt, which he took every morning regular at six, Sir Pitt, and--I thought I heard a moan-like, Sir Pitt--and--and--and--\" She dropped another curtsey.\n\nWhat was it that made Pitt's pale face flush quite red? Was it because he was Sir Pitt at last, with a seat in Parliament, and perhaps future honours in prospect? \"I'll clear the estate now with the ready money,\" he thought and rapidly calculated its incumbrances and the improvements which he would make. He would not use his aunt's money previously lest Sir Pitt should recover and his outlay be in vain.\n\nAll the blinds were pulled down at the Hall and Rectory: the church bell was tolled, and the chancel hung in black; and Bute Crawley didn't go to a coursing meeting, but went and dined quietly at Fuddleston, where they talked about his deceased brother and young Sir Pitt over their port. Miss Betsy, who was by this time married to a saddler at Mudbury, cried a good deal. The family surgeon rode over and paid his respectful compliments, and inquiries for the health of their ladyships. The death was talked about at Mudbury and at the Crawley Arms, the landlord whereof had become reconciled with the Rector of late, who was occasionally known to step into the parlour and taste Mr. Horrocks' mild beer.\n\n\"Shall I write to your brother--or will you?\" asked Lady Jane of her husband, Sir Pitt.\n\n\"I will write, of course,\" Sir Pitt said, \"and invite him to the funeral: it will be but becoming.\"\n\n\"And--and--Mrs. Rawdon,\" said Lady Jane timidly.\n\n\"Jane!\" said Lady Southdown, \"how can you think of such a thing?\"\n\n\"Mrs. Rawdon must of course be asked,\" said Sir Pitt, resolutely.\n\n\"Not whilst I am in the house!\" said Lady Southdown.\n\n\"Your Ladyship will be pleased to recollect that I am the head of this family,\" Sir Pitt replied. \"If you please, Lady Jane, you will write a letter to Mrs. Rawdon Crawley, requesting her presence upon this melancholy occasion.\"\n\n\"Jane, I forbid you to put pen to paper!\" cried the Countess.\n\n\"I believe I am the head of this family,\" Sir Pitt repeated; \"and however much I may regret any circumstance which may lead to your Ladyship quitting this house, must, if you please, continue to govern it as I see fit.\"\n\nLady Southdown rose up as magnificent as Mrs. Siddons in Lady Macbeth and ordered that horses might be put to her carriage. If her son and daughter turned her out of their house, she would hide her sorrows somewhere in loneliness and pray for their conversion to better thoughts.\n\n\"We don't turn you out of our house, Mamma,\" said the timid Lady Jane imploringly.\n\n\"You invite such company to it as no Christian lady should meet, and I will have my horses to-morrow morning.\"\n\n\"Have the goodness to write, Jane, under my dictation,\" said Sir Pitt, rising and throwing himself into an attitude of command, like the portrait of a Gentleman in the Exhibition, \"and begin. 'Queen's Crawley, September 14, 1822.--My dear brother--'\"\n\nHearing these decisive and terrible words, Lady Macbeth, who had been waiting for a sign of weakness or vacillation on the part of her son-in-law, rose and, with a scared look, left the library. Lady Jane looked up to her husband as if she would fain follow and soothe her mamma, but Pitt forbade his wife to move.\n\n\"She won't go away,\" he said. \"She has let her house at Brighton and has spent her last half-year's dividends. A Countess living at an inn is a ruined woman. I have been waiting long for an opportunity--to take this--this decisive step, my love; for, as you must perceive, it is impossible that there should be two chiefs in a family: and now, if you please, we will resume the dictation. 'My dear brother, the melancholy intelligence which it is my duty to convey to my family must have been long anticipated by,'\" &c.\n\nIn a word, Pitt having come to his kingdom, and having by good luck, or desert rather, as he considered, assumed almost all the fortune which his other relatives had expected, was determined to treat his family kindly and respectably and make a house of Queen's Crawley once more. It pleased him to think that he should be its chief. He proposed to use the vast influence that his commanding talents and position must speedily acquire for him in the county to get his brother placed and his cousins decently provided for, and perhaps had a little sting of repentance as he thought that he was the proprietor of all that they had hoped for. In the course of three or four days' reign his bearing was changed and his plans quite fixed: he determined to rule justly and honestly, to depose Lady Southdown, and to be on the friendliest possible terms with all the relations of his blood.\n\nSo he dictated a letter to his brother Rawdon--a solemn and elaborate letter, containing the profoundest observations, couched in the longest words, and filling with wonder the simple little secretary, who wrote under her husband's order. \"What an orator this will be,\" thought she, \"when he enters the House of Commons\" (on which point, and on the tyranny of Lady Southdown, Pitt had sometimes dropped hints to his wife in bed); \"how wise and good, and what a genius my husband is! I fancied him a little cold; but how good, and what a genius!\"\n\nThe fact is, Pitt Crawley had got every word of the letter by heart and had studied it, with diplomatic secrecy, deeply and perfectly, long before he thought fit to communicate it to his astonished wife.\n\nThis letter, with a huge black border and seal, was accordingly despatched by Sir Pitt Crawley to his brother the Colonel, in London. Rawdon Crawley was but half-pleased at the receipt of it. \"What's the use of going down to that stupid place?\" thought he. \"I can't stand being alone with Pitt after dinner, and horses there and back will cost us twenty pound.\"\n\nHe carried the letter, as he did all difficulties, to Becky, upstairs in her bedroom--with her chocolate, which he always made and took to her of a morning.\n\nHe put the tray with the breakfast and the letter on the dressing-table, before which Becky sat combing her yellow hair. She took up the black-edged missive, and having read it, she jumped up from the chair, crying \"Hurray!\" and waving the note round her head.\n\n\"Hurray?\" said Rawdon, wondering at the little figure capering about in a streaming flannel dressing-gown, with tawny locks dishevelled. \"He's not left us anything, Becky. I had my share when I came of age.\"\n\n\"You'll never be of age, you silly old man,\" Becky replied. \"Run out now to Madam Brunoy's, for I must have some mourning: and get a crape on your hat, and a black waistcoat--I don't think you've got one; order it to be brought home to-morrow, so that we may be able to start on Thursday.\"\n\n\"You don't mean to go?\" Rawdon interposed.\n\n\"Of course I mean to go. I mean that Lady Jane shall present me at Court next year. I mean that your brother shall give you a seat in Parliament, you stupid old creature. I mean that Lord Steyne shall have your vote and his, my dear, old silly man; and that you shall be an Irish Secretary, or a West Indian Governor: or a Treasurer, or a Consul, or some such thing.\"\n\n\"Posting will cost a dooce of a lot of money,\" grumbled Rawdon.\n\n\"We might take Southdown's carriage, which ought to be present at the funeral, as he is a relation of the family: but, no--I intend that we shall go by the coach. They'll like it better. It seems more humble--\"\n\n\"Rawdy goes, of course?\" the Colonel asked.\n\n\"No such thing; why pay an extra place? He's too big to travel bodkin between you and me. Let him stay here in the nursery, and Briggs can make him a black frock. Go you, and do as I bid you. And you had best tell Sparks, your man, that old Sir Pitt is dead and that you will come in for something considerable when the affairs are arranged. He'll tell this to Raggles, who has been pressing for money, and it will console poor Raggles.\" And so Becky began sipping her chocolate.\n\nWhen the faithful Lord Steyne arrived in the evening, he found Becky and her companion, who was no other than our friend Briggs, busy cutting, ripping, snipping, and tearing all sorts of black stuffs available for the melancholy occasion.\n\n\"Miss Briggs and I are plunged in grief and despondency for the death of our Papa,\" Rebecca said. \"Sir Pitt Crawley is dead, my lord. We have been tearing our hair all the morning, and now we are tearing up our old clothes.\"\n\n\"Oh, Rebecca, how can you--\" was all that Briggs could say as she turned up her eyes.\n\n\"Oh, Rebecca, how can you--\" echoed my Lord. \"So that old scoundrel's dead, is he? He might have been a Peer if he had played his cards better. Mr. Pitt had very nearly made him; but he ratted always at the wrong time. What an old Silenus it was!\"\n\n\"I might have been Silenus's widow,\" said Rebecca. \"Don't you remember, Miss Briggs, how you peeped in at the door and saw old Sir Pitt on his knees to me?\" Miss Briggs, our old friend, blushed very much at this reminiscence, and was glad when Lord Steyne ordered her to go downstairs and make him a cup of tea.\n\nBriggs was the house-dog whom Rebecca had provided as guardian of her innocence and reputation. Miss Crawley had left her a little annuity. She would have been content to remain in the Crawley family with Lady Jane, who was good to her and to everybody; but Lady Southdown dismissed poor Briggs as quickly as decency permitted; and Mr. Pitt (who thought himself much injured by the uncalled-for generosity of his deceased relative towards a lady who had only been Miss Crawley's faithful retainer a score of years) made no objection to that exercise of the dowager's authority. Bowls and Firkin likewise received their legacies and their dismissals, and married and set up a lodging-house, according to the custom of their kind.\n\nBriggs tried to live with her relations in the country, but found that attempt was vain after the better society to which she had been accustomed. Briggs's friends, small tradesmen, in a country town, quarrelled over Miss Briggs's forty pounds a year as eagerly and more openly than Miss Crawley's kinsfolk had for that lady's inheritance. Briggs's brother, a radical hatter and grocer, called his sister a purse-proud aristocrat, because she would not advance a part of her capital to stock his shop; and she would have done so most likely, but that their sister, a dissenting shoemaker's lady, at variance with the hatter and grocer, who went to another chapel, showed how their brother was on the verge of bankruptcy, and took possession of Briggs for a while. The dissenting shoemaker wanted Miss Briggs to send his son to college and make a gentleman of him. Between them the two families got a great portion of her private savings out of her, and finally she fled to London followed by the anathemas of both, and determined to seek for servitude again as infinitely less onerous than liberty. And advertising in the papers that a \"Gentlewoman of agreeable manners, and accustomed to the best society, was anxious to,\" &c., she took up her residence with Mr. Bowls in Half Moon Street, and waited the result of the advertisement.\n\nSo it was that she fell in with Rebecca. Mrs. Rawdon's dashing little carriage and ponies was whirling down the street one day, just as Miss Briggs, fatigued, had reached Mr. Bowls's door, after a weary walk to the Times Office in the City to insert her advertisement for the sixth time. Rebecca was driving, and at once recognized the gentlewoman with agreeable manners, and being a perfectly good-humoured woman, as we have seen, and having a regard for Briggs, she pulled up the ponies at the doorsteps, gave the reins to the groom, and jumping out, had hold of both Briggs's hands, before she of the agreeable manners had recovered from the shock of seeing an old friend.\n\nBriggs cried, and Becky laughed a great deal and kissed the gentlewoman as soon as they got into the passage; and thence into Mrs. Bowls's front parlour, with the red moreen curtains, and the round looking-glass, with the chained eagle above, gazing upon the back of the ticket in the window which announced \"Apartments to Let.\"\n\nBriggs told all her history amidst those perfectly uncalled-for sobs and ejaculations of wonder with which women of her soft nature salute an old acquaintance, or regard a rencontre in the street; for though people meet other people every day, yet some there are who insist upon discovering miracles; and women, even though they have disliked each other, begin to cry when they meet, deploring and remembering the time when they last quarrelled. So, in a word, Briggs told all her history, and Becky gave a narrative of her own life, with her usual artlessness and candour.\n\nMrs. Bowls, late Firkin, came and listened grimly in the passage to the hysterical sniffling and giggling which went on in the front parlour. Becky had never been a favourite of hers. Since the establishment of the married couple in London they had frequented their former friends of the house of Raggles, and did not like the latter's account of the Colonel's menage. \"I wouldn't trust him, Ragg, my boy,\" Bowls remarked; and his wife, when Mrs. Rawdon issued from the parlour, only saluted the lady with a very sour curtsey; and her fingers were like so many sausages, cold and lifeless, when she held them out in deference to Mrs. Rawdon, who persisted in shaking hands with the retired lady's maid. She whirled away into Piccadilly, nodding with the sweetest of smiles towards Miss Briggs, who hung nodding at the window close under the advertisement-card, and at the next moment was in the park with a half-dozen of dandies cantering after her carriage.\n\nWhen she found how her friend was situated, and how having a snug legacy from Miss Crawley, salary was no object to our gentlewoman, Becky instantly formed some benevolent little domestic plans concerning her. This was just such a companion as would suit her establishment, and she invited Briggs to come to dinner with her that very evening, when she should see Becky's dear little darling Rawdon.\n\nMrs. Bowls cautioned her lodger against venturing into the lion's den, \"wherein you will rue it, Miss B., mark my words, and as sure as my name is Bowls.\" And Briggs promised to be very cautious. The upshot of which caution was that she went to live with Mrs. Rawdon the next week, and had lent Rawdon Crawley six hundred pounds upon annuity before six months were over.\n\nIn Which Becky Revisits the Halls of Her Ancestors\nSo the mourning being ready, and Sir Pitt Crawley warned of their arrival, Colonel Crawley and his wife took a couple of places in the same old High-flyer coach by which Rebecca had travelled in the defunct Baronet's company, on her first journey into the world some nine years before. How well she remembered the Inn Yard, and the ostler to whom she refused money, and the insinuating Cambridge lad who wrapped her in his coat on the journey! Rawdon took his place outside, and would have liked to drive, but his grief forbade him. He sat by the coachman and talked about horses and the road the whole way; and who kept the inns, and who horsed the coach by which he had travelled so many a time, when he and Pitt were boys going to Eton. At Mudbury a carriage and a pair of horses received them, with a coachman in black. \"It's the old drag, Rawdon,\" Rebecca said as they got in. \"The worms have eaten the cloth a good deal--there's the stain which Sir Pitt--ha! I see Dawson the Ironmonger has his shutters up--which Sir Pitt made such a noise about. It was a bottle of cherry brandy he broke which we went to fetch for your aunt from Southampton. How time flies, to be sure! That can't be Polly Talboys, that bouncing girl standing by her mother at the cottage there. I remember her a mangy little urchin picking weeds in the garden.\"\n\n\"Fine gal,\" said Rawdon, returning the salute which the cottage gave him, by two fingers applied to his crape hatband. Becky bowed and saluted, and recognized people here and there graciously. These recognitions were inexpressibly pleasant to her. It seemed as if she was not an imposter any more, and was coming to the home of her ancestors. Rawdon was rather abashed and cast down, on the other hand. What recollections of boyhood and innocence might have been flitting across his brain? What pangs of dim remorse and doubt and shame?\n\n\"Your sisters must be young women now,\" Rebecca said, thinking of those girls for the first time perhaps since she had left them.\n\n\"Don't know, I'm shaw,\" replied the Colonel. \"Hullo! here's old Mother Lock. How-dy-do, Mrs. Lock? Remember me, don't you? Master Rawdon, hey? Dammy how those old women last; she was a hundred when I was a boy.\"\n\nThey were going through the lodge-gates kept by old Mrs. Lock, whose hand Rebecca insisted upon shaking, as she flung open the creaking old iron gate, and the carriage passed between the two moss-grown pillars surmounted by the dove and serpent.\n\n\"The governor has cut into the timber,\" Rawdon said, looking about, and then was silent--so was Becky. Both of them were rather agitated, and thinking of old times. He about Eton, and his mother, whom he remembered, a frigid demure woman, and a sister who died, of whom he had been passionately fond; and how he used to thrash Pitt; and about little Rawdy at home. And Rebecca thought about her own youth and the dark secrets of those early tainted days; and of her entrance into life by yonder gates; and of Miss Pinkerton, and Joe, and Amelia.\n\nThe gravel walk and terrace had been scraped quite clean. A grand painted hatchment was already over the great entrance, and two very solemn and tall personages in black flung open each a leaf of the door as the carriage pulled up at the familiar steps. Rawdon turned red, and Becky somewhat pale, as they passed through the old hall, arm in arm. She pinched her husband's arm as they entered the oak parlour, where Sir Pitt and his wife were ready to receive them. Sir Pitt in black, Lady Jane in black, and my Lady Southdown with a large black head-piece of bugles and feathers, which waved on her Ladyship's head like an undertaker's tray.\n\nSir Pitt had judged correctly, that she would not quit the premises. She contented herself by preserving a solemn and stony silence, when in company of Pitt and his rebellious wife, and by frightening the children in the nursery by the ghastly gloom of her demeanour. Only a very faint bending of the head-dress and plumes welcomed Rawdon and his wife, as those prodigals returned to their family.\n\nTo say the truth, they were not affected very much one way or other by this coolness. Her Ladyship was a person only of secondary consideration in their minds just then--they were intent upon the reception which the reigning brother and sister would afford them.\n\nPitt, with rather a heightened colour, went up and shook his brother by the hand, and saluted Rebecca with a hand-shake and a very low bow. But Lady Jane took both the hands of her sister-in-law and kissed her affectionately. The embrace somehow brought tears into the eyes of the little adventuress--which ornaments, as we know, she wore very seldom. The artless mark of kindness and confidence touched and pleased her; and Rawdon, encouraged by this demonstration on his sister's part, twirled up his mustachios and took leave to salute Lady Jane with a kiss, which caused her Ladyship to blush exceedingly.\n\n\"Dev'lish nice little woman, Lady Jane,\" was his verdict, when he and his wife were together again. \"Pitt's got fat, too, and is doing the thing handsomely.\" \"He can afford it,\" said Rebecca and agreed in her husband's farther opinion \"that the mother-in-law was a tremendous old Guy--and that the sisters were rather well-looking young women.\"\n\nThey, too, had been summoned from school to attend the funeral ceremonies. It seemed Sir Pitt Crawley, for the dignity of the house and family, had thought right to have about the place as many persons in black as could possibly be assembled. All the men and maids of the house, the old women of the Alms House, whom the elder Sir Pitt had cheated out of a great portion of their due, the parish clerk's family, and the special retainers of both Hall and Rectory were habited in sable; added to these, the undertaker's men, at least a score, with crapes and hatbands, and who made goodly show when the great burying show took place--but these are mute personages in our drama; and having nothing to do or say, need occupy a very little space here.\n\nWith regard to her sisters-in-law Rebecca did not attempt to forget her former position of Governess towards them, but recalled it frankly and kindly, and asked them about their studies with great gravity, and told them that she had thought of them many and many a day, and longed to know of their welfare. In fact you would have supposed that ever since she had left them she had not ceased to keep them uppermost in her thoughts and to take the tenderest interest in their welfare. So supposed Lady Crawley herself and her young sisters.\n\n\"She's hardly changed since eight years,\" said Miss Rosalind to Miss Violet, as they were preparing for dinner.\n\n\"Those red-haired women look wonderfully well,\" replied the other.\n\n\"Hers is much darker than it was; I think she must dye it,\" Miss Rosalind added. \"She is stouter, too, and altogether improved,\" continued Miss Rosalind, who was disposed to be very fat.\n\n\"At least she gives herself no airs and remembers that she was our Governess once,\" Miss Violet said, intimating that it befitted all governesses to keep their proper place, and forgetting altogether that she was granddaughter not only of Sir Walpole Crawley, but of Mr. Dawson of Mudbury, and so had a coal-scuttle in her scutcheon. There are other very well-meaning people whom one meets every day in Vanity Fair who are surely equally oblivious.\n\n\"It can't be true what the girls at the Rectory said, that her mother was an opera-dancer--\"\n\n\"A person can't help their birth,\" Rosalind replied with great liberality. \"And I agree with our brother, that as she is in the family, of course we are bound to notice her. I am sure Aunt Bute need not talk; she wants to marry Kate to young Hooper, the wine-merchant, and absolutely asked him to come to the Rectory for orders.\"\n\n\"I wonder whether Lady Southdown will go away, she looked very glum upon Mrs. Rawdon,\" the other said.\n\n\"I wish she would. I won't read the Washerwoman of Finchley Common,\" vowed Violet; and so saying, and avoiding a passage at the end of which a certain coffin was placed with a couple of watchers, and lights perpetually burning in the closed room, these young women came down to the family dinner, for which the bell rang as usual.\n\nBut before this, Lady Jane conducted Rebecca to the apartments prepared for her, which, with the rest of the house, had assumed a very much improved appearance of order and comfort during Pitt's regency, and here beholding that Mrs. Rawdon's modest little trunks had arrived, and were placed in the bedroom and dressing-room adjoining, helped her to take off her neat black bonnet and cloak, and asked her sister-in-law in what more she could be useful.\n\n\"What I should like best,\" said Rebecca, \"would be to go to the nursery and see your dear little children.\" On which the two ladies looked very kindly at each other and went to that apartment hand in hand.\n\nBecky admired little Matilda, who was not quite four years old, as the most charming little love in the world; and the boy, a little fellow of two years--pale, heavy-eyed, and large-headed--she pronounced to be a perfect prodigy in point of size, intelligence, and beauty.\n\n\"I wish Mamma would not insist on giving him so much medicine,\" Lady Jane said with a sigh. \"I often think we should all be better without it.\" And then Lady Jane and her new-found friend had one of those confidential medical conversations about the children, which all mothers, and most women, as I am given to understand, delight in. Fifty years ago, and when the present writer, being an interesting little boy, was ordered out of the room with the ladies after dinner, I remember quite well that their talk was chiefly about their ailments; and putting this question directly to two or three since, I have always got from them the acknowledgement that times are not changed. Let my fair readers remark for themselves this very evening when they quit the dessert-table and assemble to celebrate the drawing-room mysteries. Well--in half an hour Becky and Lady Jane were close and intimate friends--and in the course of the evening her Ladyship informed Sir Pitt that she thought her new sister-in-law was a kind, frank, unaffected, and affectionate young woman.\n\nAnd so having easily won the daughter's good-will, the indefatigable little woman bent herself to conciliate the august Lady Southdown. As soon as she found her Ladyship alone, Rebecca attacked her on the nursery question at once and said that her own little boy was saved, actually saved, by calomel, freely administered, when all the physicians in Paris had given the dear child up. And then she mentioned how often she had heard of Lady Southdown from that excellent man the Reverend Lawrence Grills, Minister of the chapel in May Fair, which she frequented; and how her views were very much changed by circumstances and misfortunes; and how she hoped that a past life spent in worldliness and error might not incapacitate her from more serious thought for the future. She described how in former days she had been indebted to Mr. Crawley for religious instruction, touched upon the Washerwoman of Finchley Common, which she had read with the greatest profit, and asked about Lady Emily, its gifted author, now Lady Emily Hornblower, at Cape Town, where her husband had strong hopes of becoming Bishop of Caffraria.\n\nBut she crowned all, and confirmed herself in Lady Southdown's favour, by feeling very much agitated and unwell after the funeral and requesting her Ladyship's medical advice, which the Dowager not only gave, but, wrapped up in a bed-gown and looking more like Lady Macbeth than ever, came privately in the night to Becky's room with a parcel of favourite tracts, and a medicine of her own composition, which she insisted that Mrs. Rawdon should take.\n\nBecky first accepted the tracts and began to examine them with great interest, engaging the Dowager in a conversation concerning them and the welfare of her soul, by which means she hoped that her body might escape medication. But after the religious topics were exhausted, Lady Macbeth would not quit Becky's chamber until her cup of night-drink was emptied too; and poor Mrs. Rawdon was compelled actually to assume a look of gratitude, and to swallow the medicine under the unyielding old Dowager's nose, who left her victim finally with a benediction.\n\nIt did not much comfort Mrs. Rawdon; her countenance was very queer when Rawdon came in and heard what had happened; and his explosions of laughter were as loud as usual, when Becky, with a fun which she could not disguise, even though it was at her own expense, described the occurrence and how she had been victimized by Lady Southdown. Lord Steyne, and her son in London, had many a laugh over the story when Rawdon and his wife returned to their quarters in May Fair. Becky acted the whole scene for them. She put on a night-cap and gown. She preached a great sermon in the true serious manner; she lectured on the virtue of the medicine which she pretended to administer, with a gravity of imitation so perfect that you would have thought it was the Countess's own Roman nose through which she snuffled. \"Give us Lady Southdown and the black dose,\" was a constant cry amongst the folks in Becky's little drawing-room in May Fair. And for the first time in her life the Dowager Countess of Southdown was made amusing.\n\nSir Pitt remembered the testimonies of respect and veneration which Rebecca had paid personally to himself in early days, and was tolerably well disposed towards her. The marriage, ill-advised as it was, had improved Rawdon very much--that was clear from the Colonel's altered habits and demeanour--and had it not been a lucky union as regarded Pitt himself? The cunning diplomatist smiled inwardly as he owned that he owed his fortune to it, and acknowledged that he at least ought not to cry out against it. His satisfaction was not removed by Rebecca's own statements, behaviour, and conversation.\n\nShe doubled the deference which before had charmed him, calling out his conversational powers in such a manner as quite to surprise Pitt himself, who, always inclined to respect his own talents, admired them the more when Rebecca pointed them out to him. With her sister-in-law, Rebecca was satisfactorily able to prove that it was Mrs. Bute Crawley who brought about the marriage which she afterwards so calumniated; that it was Mrs. Bute's avarice--who hoped to gain all Miss Crawley's fortune and deprive Rawdon of his aunt's favour--which caused and invented all the wicked reports against Rebecca. \"She succeeded in making us poor,\" Rebecca said with an air of angelical patience; \"but how can I be angry with a woman who has given me one of the best husbands in the world? And has not her own avarice been sufficiently punished by the ruin of her own hopes and the loss of the property by which she set so much store? Poor!\" she cried. \"Dear Lady Jane, what care we for poverty? I am used to it from childhood, and I am often thankful that Miss Crawley's money has gone to restore the splendour of the noble old family of which I am so proud to be a member. I am sure Sir Pitt will make a much better use of it than Rawdon would.\"\n\nAll these speeches were reported to Sir Pitt by the most faithful of wives, and increased the favourable impression which Rebecca made; so much so that when, on the third day after the funeral, the family party were at dinner, Sir Pitt Crawley, carving fowls at the head of the table, actually said to Mrs. Rawdon, \"Ahem! Rebecca, may I give you a wing?\"--a speech which made the little woman's eyes sparkle with pleasure.\n\nWhile Rebecca was prosecuting the above schemes and hopes, and Pitt Crawley arranging the funeral ceremonial and other matters connected with his future progress and dignity, and Lady Jane busy with her nursery, as far as her mother would let her, and the sun rising and setting, and the clock-tower bell of the Hall ringing to dinner and to prayers as usual, the body of the late owner of Queen's Crawley lay in the apartment which he had occupied, watched unceasingly by the professional attendants who were engaged for that rite. A woman or two, and three or four undertaker's men, the best whom Southampton could furnish, dressed in black, and of a proper stealthy and tragical demeanour, had charge of the remains which they watched turn about, having the housekeeper's room for their place of rendezvous when off duty, where they played at cards in privacy and drank their beer.\n\nThe members of the family and servants of the house kept away from the gloomy spot, where the bones of the descendant of an ancient line of knights and gentlemen lay, awaiting their final consignment to the family crypt. No regrets attended them, save those of the poor woman who had hoped to be Sir Pitt's wife and widow and who had fled in disgrace from the Hall over which she had so nearly been a ruler. Beyond her and a favourite old pointer he had, and between whom and himself an attachment subsisted during the period of his imbecility, the old man had not a single friend to mourn him, having indeed, during the whole course of his life, never taken the least pains to secure one. Could the best and kindest of us who depart from the earth have an opportunity of revisiting it, I suppose he or she (assuming that any Vanity Fair feelings subsist in the sphere whither we are bound) would have a pang of mortification at finding how soon our survivors were consoled. And so Sir Pitt was forgotten--like the kindest and best of us--only a few weeks sooner.\n\nThose who will may follow his remains to the grave, whither they were borne on the appointed day, in the most becoming manner, the family in black coaches, with their handkerchiefs up to their noses, ready for the tears which did not come; the undertaker and his gentlemen in deep tribulation; the select tenantry mourning out of compliment to the new landlord; the neighbouring gentry's carriages at three miles an hour, empty, and in profound affliction; the parson speaking out the formula about \"our dear brother departed.\" As long as we have a man's body, we play our Vanities upon it, surrounding it with humbug and ceremonies, laying it in state, and packing it up in gilt nails and velvet; and we finish our duty by placing over it a stone, written all over with lies. Bute's curate, a smart young fellow from Oxford, and Sir Pitt Crawley composed between them an appropriate Latin epitaph for the late lamented Baronet, and the former preached a classical sermon, exhorting the survivors not to give way to grief and informing them in the most respectful terms that they also would be one day called upon to pass that gloomy and mysterious portal which had just closed upon the remains of their lamented brother. Then the tenantry mounted on horseback again, or stayed and refreshed themselves at the Crawley Arms. Then, after a lunch in the servants' hall at Queen's Crawley, the gentry's carriages wheeled off to their different destinations: then the undertaker's men, taking the ropes, palls, velvets, ostrich feathers, and other mortuary properties, clambered up on the roof of the hearse and rode off to Southampton. Their faces relapsed into a natural expression as the horses, clearing the lodge-gates, got into a brisker trot on the open road; and squads of them might have been seen, speckling with black the public-house entrances, with pewter-pots flashing in the sunshine. Sir Pitt's invalid chair was wheeled away into a tool-house in the garden; the old pointer used to howl sometimes at first, but these were the only accents of grief which were heard in the Hall of which Sir Pitt Crawley, Baronet, had been master for some threescore years.\n\nAs the birds were pretty plentiful, and partridge shooting is as it were the duty of an English gentleman of statesmanlike propensities, Sir Pitt Crawley, the first shock of grief over, went out a little and partook of that diversion in a white hat with crape round it. The sight of those fields of stubble and turnips, now his own, gave him many secret joys. Sometimes, and with an exquisite humility, he took no gun, but went out with a peaceful bamboo cane; Rawdon, his big brother, and the keepers blazing away at his side. Pitt's money and acres had a great effect upon his brother. The penniless Colonel became quite obsequious and respectful to the head of his house, and despised the milksop Pitt no longer. Rawdon listened with sympathy to his senior's prospects of planting and draining, gave his advice about the stables and cattle, rode over to Mudbury to look at a mare, which he thought would carry Lady Jane, and offered to break her, &c.: the rebellious dragoon was quite humbled and subdued, and became a most creditable younger brother. He had constant bulletins from Miss Briggs in London respecting little Rawdon, who was left behind there, who sent messages of his own. \"I am very well,\" he wrote. \"I hope you are very well. I hope Mamma is very well. The pony is very well. Grey takes me to ride in the park. I can canter. I met the little boy who rode before. He cried when he cantered. I do not cry.\" Rawdon read these letters to his brother and Lady Jane, who was delighted with them. The Baronet promised to take charge of the lad at school, and his kind-hearted wife gave Rebecca a bank-note, begging her to buy a present with it for her little nephew.\n\nOne day followed another, and the ladies of the house passed their life in those calm pursuits and amusements which satisfy country ladies. Bells rang to meals and to prayers. The young ladies took exercise on the pianoforte every morning after breakfast, Rebecca giving them the benefit of her instruction. Then they put on thick shoes and walked in the park or shrubberies, or beyond the palings into the village, descending upon the cottages, with Lady Southdown's medicine and tracts for the sick people there. Lady Southdown drove out in a pony-chaise, when Rebecca would take her place by the Dowager's side and listen to her solemn talk with the utmost interest. She sang Handel and Haydn to the family of evenings, and engaged in a large piece of worsted work, as if she had been born to the business and as if this kind of life was to continue with her until she should sink to the grave in a polite old age, leaving regrets and a great quantity of consols behind her--as if there were not cares and duns, schemes, shifts, and poverty waiting outside the park gates, to pounce upon her when she issued into the world again.\n\n\"It isn't difficult to be a country gentleman's wife,\" Rebecca thought. \"I think I could be a good woman if I had five thousand a year. I could dawdle about in the nursery and count the apricots on the wall. I could water plants in a green-house and pick off dead leaves from the geraniums. I could ask old women about their rheumatisms and order half-a-crown's worth of soup for the poor. I shouldn't miss it much, out of five thousand a year. I could even drive out ten miles to dine at a neighbour's, and dress in the fashions of the year before last. I could go to church and keep awake in the great family pew, or go to sleep behind the curtains, with my veil down, if I only had practice. I could pay everybody, if I had but the money. This is what the conjurors here pride themselves upon doing. They look down with pity upon us miserable sinners who have none. They think themselves generous if they give our children a five-pound note, and us contemptible if we are without one.\" And who knows but Rebecca was right in her speculations--and that it was only a question of money and fortune which made the difference between her and an honest woman? If you take temptations into account, who is to say that he is better than his neighbour? A comfortable career of prosperity, if it does not make people honest, at least keeps them so. An alderman coming from a turtle feast will not step out of his carriage to steal a leg of mutton; but put him to starve, and see if he will not purloin a loaf. Becky consoled herself by so balancing the chances and equalizing the distribution of good and evil in the world.\n\nThe old haunts, the old fields and woods, the copses, ponds, and gardens, the rooms of the old house where she had spent a couple of years seven years ago, were all carefully revisited by her. She had been young there, or comparatively so, for she forgot the time when she ever WAS young--but she remembered her thoughts and feelings seven years back and contrasted them with those which she had at present, now that she had seen the world, and lived with great people, and raised herself far beyond her original humble station.\n\n\"I have passed beyond it, because I have brains,\" Becky thought, \"and almost all the rest of the world are fools. I could not go back and consort with those people now, whom I used to meet in my father's studio. Lords come up to my door with stars and garters, instead of poor artists with screws of tobacco in their pockets. I have a gentleman for my husband, and an Earl's daughter for my sister, in the very house where I was little better than a servant a few years ago. But am I much better to do now in the world than I was when I was the poor painter's daughter and wheedled the grocer round the corner for sugar and tea? Suppose I had married Francis who was so fond of me--I couldn't have been much poorer than I am now. Heigho! I wish I could exchange my position in society, and all my relations for a snug sum in the Three Per Cent. Consols\"; for so it was that Becky felt the Vanity of human affairs, and it was in those securities that she would have liked to cast anchor.\n\nIt may, perhaps, have struck her that to have been honest and humble, to have done her duty, and to have marched straightforward on her way, would have brought her as near happiness as that path by which she was striving to attain it. But--just as the children at Queen's Crawley went round the room where the body of their father lay--if ever Becky had these thoughts, she was accustomed to walk round them and not look in. She eluded them and despised them--or at least she was committed to the other path from which retreat was now impossible. And for my part I believe that remorse is the least active of all a man's moral senses--the very easiest to be deadened when wakened, and in some never wakened at all. We grieve at being found out and at the idea of shame or punishment, but the mere sense of wrong makes very few people unhappy in Vanity Fair.\n\nSo Rebecca, during her stay at Queen's Crawley, made as many friends of the Mammon of Unrighteousness as she could possibly bring under control. Lady Jane and her husband bade her farewell with the warmest demonstrations of good-will. They looked forward with pleasure to the time when, the family house in Gaunt Street being repaired and beautified, they were to meet again in London. Lady Southdown made her up a packet of medicine and sent a letter by her to the Rev. Lawrence Grills, exhorting that gentleman to save the brand who \"honoured\" the letter from the burning. Pitt accompanied them with four horses in the carriage to Mudbury, having sent on their baggage in a cart previously, accompanied with loads of game.\n\n\"How happy you will be to see your darling little boy again!\" Lady Crawley said, taking leave of her kinswoman.\n\n\"Oh so happy!\" said Rebecca, throwing up the green eyes. She was immensely happy to be free of the place, and yet loath to go. Queen's Crawley was abominably stupid, and yet the air there was somehow purer than that which she had been accustomed to breathe. Everybody had been dull, but had been kind in their way. \"It is all the influence of a long course of Three Per Cents,\" Becky said to herself, and was right very likely.\n\nHowever, the London lamps flashed joyfully as the stage rolled into Piccadilly, and Briggs had made a beautiful fire in Curzon Street, and little Rawdon was up to welcome back his papa and mamma.\n\nWhich Treats of the Osborne Family\nConsiderable time has elapsed since we have seen our respectable friend, old Mr. Osborne of Russell Square. He has not been the happiest of mortals since last we met him. Events have occurred which have not improved his temper, and in more instances than one he has not been allowed to have his own way. To be thwarted in this reasonable desire was always very injurious to the old gentleman; and resistance became doubly exasperating when gout, age, loneliness, and the force of many disappointments combined to weigh him down. His stiff black hair began to grow quite white soon after his son's death; his face grew redder; his hands trembled more and more as he poured out his glass of port wine. He led his clerks a dire life in the City: his family at home were not much happier. I doubt if Rebecca, whom we have seen piously praying for Consols, would have exchanged her poverty and the dare-devil excitement and chances of her life for Osborne's money and the humdrum gloom which enveloped him. He had proposed for Miss Swartz, but had been rejected scornfully by the partisans of that lady, who married her to a young sprig of Scotch nobility. He was a man to have married a woman out of low life and bullied her dreadfully afterwards; but no person presented herself suitable to his taste, and, instead, he tyrannized over his unmarried daughter, at home. She had a fine carriage and fine horses and sat at the head of a table loaded with the grandest plate. She had a cheque-book, a prize footman to follow her when she walked, unlimited credit, and bows and compliments from all the tradesmen, and all the appurtenances of an heiress; but she spent a woeful time. The little charity-girls at the Foundling, the sweeperess at the crossing, the poorest under-kitchen-maid in the servants' hall, was happy compared to that unfortunate and now middle-aged young lady.\n\nFrederick Bullock, Esq., of the house of Bullock, Hulker, and Bullock, had married Maria Osborne, not without a great deal of difficulty and grumbling on Mr. Bullock's part. George being dead and cut out of his father's will, Frederick insisted that the half of the old gentleman's property should be settled upon his Maria, and indeed, for a long time, refused, \"to come to the scratch\" (it was Mr. Frederick's own expression) on any other terms. Osborne said Fred had agreed to take his daughter with twenty thousand, and he should bind himself to no more. \"Fred might take it, and welcome, or leave it, and go and be hanged.\" Fred, whose hopes had been raised when George had been disinherited, thought himself infamously swindled by the old merchant, and for some time made as if he would break off the match altogether. Osborne withdrew his account from Bullock and Hulker's, went on 'Change with a horsewhip which he swore he would lay across the back of a certain scoundrel that should be nameless, and demeaned himself in his usual violent manner. Jane Osborne condoled with her sister Maria during this family feud. \"I always told you, Maria, that it was your money he loved and not you,\" she said, soothingly.\n\n\"He selected me and my money at any rate; he didn't choose you and yours,\" replied Maria, tossing up her head.\n\nThe rapture was, however, only temporary. Fred's father and senior partners counselled him to take Maria, even with the twenty thousand settled, half down, and half at the death of Mr. Osborne, with the chances of the further division of the property. So he \"knuckled down,\" again to use his own phrase, and sent old Hulker with peaceable overtures to Osborne. It was his father, he said, who would not hear of the match, and had made the difficulties; he was most anxious to keep the engagement. The excuse was sulkily accepted by Mr. Osborne. Hulker and Bullock were a high family of the City aristocracy, and connected with the \"nobs\" at the West End. It was something for the old man to be able to say, \"My son, sir, of the house of Hulker, Bullock, and Co., sir; my daughter's cousin, Lady Mary Mango, sir, daughter of the Right Hon. The Earl of Castlemouldy.\" In his imagination he saw his house peopled by the \"nobs.\" So he forgave young Bullock and consented that the marriage should take place.\n\nIt was a grand affair--the bridegroom's relatives giving the breakfast, their habitations being near St. George's, Hanover Square, where the business took place. The \"nobs of the West End\" were invited, and many of them signed the book. Mr. Mango and Lady Mary Mango were there, with the dear young Gwendoline and Guinever Mango as bridesmaids; Colonel Bludyer of the Dragoon Guards (eldest son of the house of Bludyer Brothers, Mincing Lane), another cousin of the bridegroom, and the Honourable Mrs. Bludyer; the Honourable George Boulter, Lord Levant's son, and his lady, Miss Mango that was; Lord Viscount Castletoddy; Honourable James McMull and Mrs. McMull (formerly Miss Swartz); and a host of fashionables, who have all married into Lombard Street and done a great deal to ennoble Cornhill.\n\nThe young couple had a house near Berkeley Square and a small villa at Roehampton, among the banking colony there. Fred was considered to have made rather a mesalliance by the ladies of his family, whose grandfather had been in a Charity School, and who were allied through the husbands with some of the best blood in England. And Maria was bound, by superior pride and great care in the composition of her visiting-book, to make up for the defects of birth, and felt it her duty to see her father and sister as little as possible.\n\nThat she should utterly break with the old man, who had still so many scores of thousand pounds to give away, is absurd to suppose. Fred Bullock would never allow her to do that. But she was still young and incapable of hiding her feelings; and by inviting her papa and sister to her third-rate parties, and behaving very coldly to them when they came, and by avoiding Russell Square, and indiscreetly begging her father to quit that odious vulgar place, she did more harm than all Frederick's diplomacy could repair, and perilled her chance of her inheritance like a giddy heedless creature as she was.\n\n\"So Russell Square is not good enough for Mrs. Maria, hay?\" said the old gentleman, rattling up the carriage windows as he and his daughter drove away one night from Mrs. Frederick Bullock's, after dinner. \"So she invites her father and sister to a second day's dinner (if those sides, or ontrys, as she calls 'em, weren't served yesterday, I'm d--d), and to meet City folks and littery men, and keeps the Earls and the Ladies, and the Honourables to herself. Honourables? Damn Honourables. I am a plain British merchant I am, and could buy the beggarly hounds over and over. Lords, indeed!--why, at one of her swarreys I saw one of 'em speak to a dam fiddler--a fellar I despise. And they won't come to Russell Square, won't they? Why, I'll lay my life I've got a better glass of wine, and pay a better figure for it, and can show a handsomer service of silver, and can lay a better dinner on my mahogany, than ever they see on theirs--the cringing, sneaking, stuck-up fools. Drive on quick, James: I want to get back to Russell Square--ha, ha!\" and he sank back into the corner with a furious laugh. With such reflections on his own superior merit, it was the custom of the old gentleman not unfrequently to console himself.\n\nJane Osborne could not but concur in these opinions respecting her sister's conduct; and when Mrs. Frederick's first-born, Frederick Augustus Howard Stanley Devereux Bullock, was born, old Osborne, who was invited to the christening and to be godfather, contented himself with sending the child a gold cup, with twenty guineas inside it for the nurse. \"That's more than any of your Lords will give, I'LL warrant,\" he said and refused to attend at the ceremony.\n\nThe splendour of the gift, however, caused great satisfaction to the house of Bullock. Maria thought that her father was very much pleased with her, and Frederick augured the best for his little son and heir.\n\nOne can fancy the pangs with which Miss Osborne in her solitude in Russell Square read the Morning Post, where her sister's name occurred every now and then, in the articles headed \"Fashionable Reunions,\" and where she had an opportunity of reading a description of Mrs. F. Bullock's costume, when presented at the drawing room by Lady Frederica Bullock. Jane's own life, as we have said, admitted of no such grandeur. It was an awful existence. She had to get up of black winter's mornings to make breakfast for her scowling old father, who would have turned the whole house out of doors if his tea had not been ready at half-past eight. She remained silent opposite to him, listening to the urn hissing, and sitting in tremor while the parent read his paper and consumed his accustomed portion of muffins and tea. At half-past nine he rose and went to the City, and she was almost free till dinner-time, to make visitations in the kitchen and to scold the servants; to drive abroad and descend upon the tradesmen, who were prodigiously respectful; to leave her cards and her papa's at the great glum respectable houses of their City friends; or to sit alone in the large drawing-room, expecting visitors; and working at a huge piece of worsted by the fire, on the sofa, hard by the great Iphigenia clock, which ticked and tolled with mournful loudness in the dreary room. The great glass over the mantelpiece, faced by the other great console glass at the opposite end of the room, increased and multiplied between them the brown Holland bag in which the chandelier hung, until you saw these brown Holland bags fading away in endless perspectives, and this apartment of Miss Osborne's seemed the centre of a system of drawing-rooms. When she removed the cordovan leather from the grand piano and ventured to play a few notes on it, it sounded with a mournful sadness, startling the dismal echoes of the house. George's picture was gone, and laid upstairs in a lumber-room in the garret; and though there was a consciousness of him, and father and daughter often instinctively knew that they were thinking of him, no mention was ever made of the brave and once darling son.\n\nAt five o'clock Mr. Osborne came back to his dinner, which he and his daughter took in silence (seldom broken, except when he swore and was savage, if the cooking was not to his liking), or which they shared twice in a month with a party of dismal friends of Osborne's rank and age. Old Dr. Gulp and his lady from Bloomsbury Square; old Mr. Frowser, the attorney, from Bedford Row, a very great man, and from his business, hand-in-glove with the \"nobs at the West End\"; old Colonel Livermore, of the Bombay Army, and Mrs. Livermore, from Upper Bedford Place; old Sergeant Toffy and Mrs. Toffy; and sometimes old Sir Thomas Coffin and Lady Coffin, from Bedford Square. Sir Thomas was celebrated as a hanging judge, and the particular tawny port was produced when he dined with Mr. Osborne.\n\nThese people and their like gave the pompous Russell Square merchant pompous dinners back again. They had solemn rubbers of whist, when they went upstairs after drinking, and their carriages were called at half past ten. Many rich people, whom we poor devils are in the habit of envying, lead contentedly an existence like that above described. Jane Osborne scarcely ever met a man under sixty, and almost the only bachelor who appeared in their society was Mr. Smirk, the celebrated ladies' doctor.\n\nI can't say that nothing had occurred to disturb the monotony of this awful existence: the fact is, there had been a secret in poor Jane's life which had made her father more savage and morose than even nature, pride, and over-feeding had made him. This secret was connected with Miss Wirt, who had a cousin an artist, Mr. Smee, very celebrated since as a portrait-painter and R.A., but who once was glad enough to give drawing lessons to ladies of fashion. Mr. Smee has forgotten where Russell Square is now, but he was glad enough to visit it in the year 1818, when Miss Osborne had instruction from him.\n\nSmee (formerly a pupil of Sharpe of Frith Street, a dissolute, irregular, and unsuccessful man, but a man with great knowledge of his art) being the cousin of Miss Wirt, we say, and introduced by her to Miss Osborne, whose hand and heart were still free after various incomplete love affairs, felt a great attachment for this lady, and it is believed inspired one in her bosom. Miss Wirt was the confidante of this intrigue. I know not whether she used to leave the room where the master and his pupil were painting, in order to give them an opportunity for exchanging those vows and sentiments which cannot be uttered advantageously in the presence of a third party; I know not whether she hoped that should her cousin succeed in carrying off the rich merchant's daughter, he would give Miss Wirt a portion of the wealth which she had enabled him to win--all that is certain is that Mr. Osborne got some hint of the transaction, came back from the City abruptly, and entered the drawing-room with his bamboo cane; found the painter, the pupil, and the companion all looking exceedingly pale there; turned the former out of doors with menaces that he would break every bone in his skin, and half an hour afterwards dismissed Miss Wirt likewise, kicking her trunks down the stairs, trampling on her bandboxes, and shaking his fist at her hackney coach as it bore her away.\n\nJane Osborne kept her bedroom for many days. She was not allowed to have a companion afterwards. Her father swore to her that she should not have a shilling of his money if she made any match without his concurrence; and as he wanted a woman to keep his house, he did not choose that she should marry, so that she was obliged to give up all projects with which Cupid had any share. During her papa's life, then, she resigned herself to the manner of existence here described, and was content to be an old maid. Her sister, meanwhile, was having children with finer names every year and the intercourse between the two grew fainter continually. \"Jane and I do not move in the same sphere of life,\" Mrs. Bullock said. \"I regard her as a sister, of course\"--which means--what does it mean when a lady says that she regards Jane as a sister?\n\nIt has been described how the Misses Dobbin lived with their father at a fine villa at Denmark Hill, where there were beautiful graperies and peach-trees which delighted little Georgy Osborne. The Misses Dobbin, who drove often to Brompton to see our dear Amelia, came sometimes to Russell Square too, to pay a visit to their old acquaintance Miss Osborne. I believe it was in consequence of the commands of their brother the Major in India (for whom their papa had a prodigious respect), that they paid attention to Mrs. George; for the Major, the godfather and guardian of Amelia's little boy, still hoped that the child's grandfather might be induced to relent towards him and acknowledge him for the sake of his son. The Misses Dobbin kept Miss Osborne acquainted with the state of Amelia's affairs; how she was living with her father and mother; how poor they were; how they wondered what men, and such men as their brother and dear Captain Osborne, could find in such an insignificant little chit; how she was still, as heretofore, a namby-pamby milk-and-water affected creature--but how the boy was really the noblest little boy ever seen--for the hearts of all women warm towards young children, and the sourest spinster is kind to them.\n\nOne day, after great entreaties on the part of the Misses Dobbin, Amelia allowed little George to go and pass a day with them at Denmark Hill--a part of which day she spent herself in writing to the Major in India. She congratulated him on the happy news which his sisters had just conveyed to her. She prayed for his prosperity and that of the bride he had chosen. She thanked him for a thousand thousand kind offices and proofs of steadfast friendship to her in her affliction. She told him the last news about little Georgy, and how he was gone to spend that very day with his sisters in the country. She underlined the letter a great deal, and she signed herself affectionately his friend, Amelia Osborne. She forgot to send any message of kindness to Lady O'Dowd, as her wont was--and did not mention Glorvina by name, and only in italics, as the Major's BRIDE, for whom she begged blessings. But the news of the marriage removed the reserve which she had kept up towards him. She was glad to be able to own and feel how warmly and gratefully she regarded him--and as for the idea of being jealous of Glorvina (Glorvina, indeed!), Amelia would have scouted it, if an angel from heaven had hinted it to her. That night, when Georgy came back in the pony-carriage in which he rejoiced, and in which he was driven by Sir Wm. Dobbin's old coachman, he had round his neck a fine gold chain and watch. He said an old lady, not pretty, had given it him, who cried and kissed him a great deal. But he didn't like her. He liked grapes very much. And he only liked his mamma. Amelia shrank and started; the timid soul felt a presentiment of terror when she heard that the relations of the child's father had seen him.\n\nMiss Osborne came back to give her father his dinner. He had made a good speculation in the City, and was rather in a good humour that day, and chanced to remark the agitation under which she laboured. \"What's the matter, Miss Osborne?\" he deigned to say.\n\nThe woman burst into tears. \"Oh, sir,\" she said, \"I've seen little George. He is as beautiful as an angel--and so like him!\" The old man opposite to her did not say a word, but flushed up and began to tremble in every limb.\n\nIn Which the Reader Has to Double the Cape\nThe astonished reader must be called upon to transport himself ten thousand miles to the military station of Bundlegunge, in the Madras division of our Indian empire, where our gallant old friends of the --th regiment are quartered under the command of the brave Colonel, Sir Michael O'Dowd. Time has dealt kindly with that stout officer, as it does ordinarily with men who have good stomachs and good tempers and are not perplexed over much by fatigue of the brain. The Colonel plays a good knife and fork at tiffin and resumes those weapons with great success at dinner. He smokes his hookah after both meals and puffs as quietly while his wife scolds him as he did under the fire of the French at Waterloo. Age and heat have not diminished the activity or the eloquence of the descendant of the Malonys and the Molloys. Her Ladyship, our old acquaintance, is as much at home at Madras as at Brussels in the cantonment as under the tents. On the march you saw her at the head of the regiment seated on a royal elephant, a noble sight. Mounted on that beast, she has been into action with tigers in the jungle, she has been received by native princes, who have welcomed her and Glorvina into the recesses of their zenanas and offered her shawls and jewels which it went to her heart to refuse. The sentries of all arms salute her wherever she makes her appearance, and she touches her hat gravely to their salutation. Lady O'Dowd is one of the greatest ladies in the Presidency of Madras--her quarrel with Lady Smith, wife of Sir Minos Smith the puisne judge, is still remembered by some at Madras, when the Colonel's lady snapped her fingers in the Judge's lady's face and said SHE'D never walk behind ever a beggarly civilian. Even now, though it is five-and-twenty years ago, people remember Lady O'Dowd performing a jig at Government House, where she danced down two Aides-de-Camp, a Major of Madras cavalry, and two gentlemen of the Civil Service; and, persuaded by Major Dobbin, C.B., second in command of the --th, to retire to the supper-room, lassata nondum satiata recessit.\n\nPeggy O'Dowd is indeed the same as ever, kind in act and thought; impetuous in temper; eager to command; a tyrant over her Michael; a dragon amongst all the ladies of the regiment; a mother to all the young men, whom she tends in their sickness, defends in all their scrapes, and with whom Lady Peggy is immensely popular. But the Subalterns' and Captains' ladies (the Major is unmarried) cabal against her a good deal. They say that Glorvina gives herself airs and that Peggy herself is intolerably domineering. She interfered with a little congregation which Mrs. Kirk had got up and laughed the young men away from her sermons, stating that a soldier's wife had no business to be a parson--that Mrs. Kirk would be much better mending her husband's clothes; and, if the regiment wanted sermons, that she had the finest in the world, those of her uncle, the Dean. She abruptly put a termination to a flirtation which Lieutenant Stubble of the regiment had commenced with the Surgeon's wife, threatening to come down upon Stubble for the money which he had borrowed from her (for the young fellow was still of an extravagant turn) unless he broke off at once and went to the Cape on sick leave. On the other hand, she housed and sheltered Mrs. Posky, who fled from her bungalow one night, pursued by her infuriate husband, wielding his second brandy bottle, and actually carried Posky through the delirium tremens and broke him of the habit of drinking, which had grown upon that officer, as all evil habits will grow upon men. In a word, in adversity she was the best of comforters, in good fortune the most troublesome of friends, having a perfectly good opinion of herself always and an indomitable resolution to have her own way.\n\nAmong other points, she had made up her mind that Glorvina should marry our old friend Dobbin. Mrs. O'Dowd knew the Major's expectations and appreciated his good qualities and the high character which he enjoyed in his profession. Glorvina, a very handsome, fresh-coloured, black-haired, blue-eyed young lady, who could ride a horse, or play a sonata with any girl out of the County Cork, seemed to be the very person destined to insure Dobbin's happiness--much more than that poor good little weak-spur'ted Amelia, about whom he used to take on so.--\"Look at Glorvina enter a room,\" Mrs. O'Dowd would say, \"and compare her with that poor Mrs. Osborne, who couldn't say boo to a goose. She'd be worthy of you, Major--you're a quiet man yourself, and want some one to talk for ye. And though she does not come of such good blood as the Malonys or Molloys, let me tell ye, she's of an ancient family that any nobleman might be proud to marry into.\"\n\nBut before she had come to such a resolution and determined to subjugate Major Dobbin by her endearments, it must be owned that Glorvina had practised them a good deal elsewhere. She had had a season in Dublin, and who knows how many in Cork, Killarney, and Mallow? She had flirted with all the marriageable officers whom the depots of her country afforded, and all the bachelor squires who seemed eligible. She had been engaged to be married a half-score times in Ireland, besides the clergyman at Bath who used her so ill. She had flirted all the way to Madras with the Captain and chief mate of the Ramchunder East Indiaman, and had a season at the Presidency with her brother and Mrs. O'Dowd, who was staying there, while the Major of the regiment was in command at the station. Everybody admired her there; everybody danced with her; but no one proposed who was worth the marrying--one or two exceedingly young subalterns sighed after her, and a beardless civilian or two, but she rejected these as beneath her pretensions--and other and younger virgins than Glorvina were married before her. There are women, and handsome women too, who have this fortune in life. They fall in love with the utmost generosity; they ride and walk with half the Army-list, though they draw near to forty, and yet the Misses O'Grady are the Misses O'Grady still: Glorvina persisted that but for Lady O'Dowd's unlucky quarrel with the Judge's lady, she would have made a good match at Madras, where old Mr. Chutney, who was at the head of the civil service (and who afterwards married Miss Dolby, a young lady only thirteen years of age who had just arrived from school in Europe), was just at the point of proposing to her.\n\nWell, although Lady O'Dowd and Glorvina quarrelled a great number of times every day, and upon almost every conceivable subject--indeed, if Mick O'Dowd had not possessed the temper of an angel two such women constantly about his ears would have driven him out of his senses--yet they agreed between themselves on this point, that Glorvina should marry Major Dobbin, and were determined that the Major should have no rest until the arrangement was brought about. Undismayed by forty or fifty previous defeats, Glorvina laid siege to him. She sang Irish melodies at him unceasingly. She asked him so frequently and pathetically, Will ye come to the bower? that it is a wonder how any man of feeling could have resisted the invitation. She was never tired of inquiring, if Sorrow had his young days faded, and was ready to listen and weep like Desdemona at the stories of his dangers and his campaigns. It has been said that our honest and dear old friend used to perform on the flute in private; Glorvina insisted upon having duets with him, and Lady O'Dowd would rise and artlessly quit the room when the young couple were so engaged. Glorvina forced the Major to ride with her of mornings. The whole cantonment saw them set out and return. She was constantly writing notes over to him at his house, borrowing his books, and scoring with her great pencil-marks such passages of sentiment or humour as awakened her sympathy. She borrowed his horses, his servants, his spoons, and palanquin--no wonder that public rumour assigned her to him, and that the Major's sisters in England should fancy they were about to have a sister-in-law.\n\nDobbin, who was thus vigorously besieged, was in the meanwhile in a state of the most odious tranquillity. He used to laugh when the young fellows of the regiment joked him about Glorvina's manifest attentions to him. \"Bah!\" said he, \"she is only keeping her hand in--she practises upon me as she does upon Mrs. Tozer's piano, because it's the most handy instrument in the station. I am much too battered and old for such a fine young lady as Glorvina.\" And so he went on riding with her, and copying music and verses into her albums, and playing at chess with her very submissively; for it is with these simple amusements that some officers in India are accustomed to while away their leisure moments, while others of a less domestic turn hunt hogs, and shoot snipes, or gamble and smoke cheroots, and betake themselves to brandy-and-water. As for Sir Michael O'Dowd, though his lady and her sister both urged him to call upon the Major to explain himself and not keep on torturing a poor innocent girl in that shameful way, the old soldier refused point-blank to have anything to do with the conspiracy. \"Faith, the Major's big enough to choose for himself,\" Sir Michael said; \"he'll ask ye when he wants ye\"; or else he would turn the matter off jocularly, declaring that \"Dobbin was too young to keep house, and had written home to ask lave of his mamma.\" Nay, he went farther, and in private communications with his Major would caution and rally him, crying, \"Mind your oi, Dob, my boy, them girls is bent on mischief--me Lady has just got a box of gowns from Europe, and there's a pink satin for Glorvina, which will finish ye, Dob, if it's in the power of woman or satin to move ye.\"\n\nBut the truth is, neither beauty nor fashion could conquer him. Our honest friend had but one idea of a woman in his head, and that one did not in the least resemble Miss Glorvina O'Dowd in pink satin. A gentle little woman in black, with large eyes and brown hair, seldom speaking, save when spoken to, and then in a voice not the least resembling Miss Glorvina's--a soft young mother tending an infant and beckoning the Major up with a smile to look at him--a rosy-cheeked lass coming singing into the room in Russell Square or hanging on George Osborne's arm, happy and loving--there was but this image that filled our honest Major's mind, by day and by night, and reigned over it always. Very likely Amelia was not like the portrait the Major had formed of her: there was a figure in a book of fashions which his sisters had in England, and with which William had made away privately, pasting it into the lid of his desk, and fancying he saw some resemblance to Mrs. Osborne in the print, whereas I have seen it, and can vouch that it is but the picture of a high-waisted gown with an impossible doll's face simpering over it--and, perhaps, Mr. Dobbin's sentimental Amelia was no more like the real one than this absurd little print which he cherished. But what man in love, of us, is better informed?--or is he much happier when he sees and owns his delusion? Dobbin was under this spell. He did not bother his friends and the public much about his feelings, or indeed lose his natural rest or appetite on account of them. His head has grizzled since we saw him last, and a line or two of silver may be seen in the soft brown hair likewise. But his feelings are not in the least changed or oldened, and his love remains as fresh as a man's recollections of boyhood are.\n\nWe have said how the two Misses Dobbin and Amelia, the Major's correspondents in Europe, wrote him letters from England, Mrs. Osborne congratulating him with great candour and cordiality upon his approaching nuptials with Miss O'Dowd. \"Your sister has just kindly visited me,\" Amelia wrote in her letter, \"and informed me of an INTERESTING EVENT, upon which I beg to offer my MOST SINCERE CONGRATULATIONS. I hope the young lady to whom I hear you are to be UNITED will in every respect prove worthy of one who is himself all kindness and goodness. The poor widow has only her prayers to offer and her cordial cordial wishes for YOUR PROSPERITY! Georgy sends his love to HIS DEAR GODPAPA and hopes that you will not forget him. I tell him that you are about to form OTHER TIES, with one who I am sure merits ALL YOUR AFFECTION, but that, although such ties must of course be the strongest and most sacred, and supersede ALL OTHERS, yet that I am sure the widow and the child whom you have ever protected and loved will always HAVE A CORNER IN YOUR HEART.\" The letter, which has been before alluded to, went on in this strain, protesting throughout as to the extreme satisfaction of the writer.\n\nThis letter, which arrived by the very same ship which brought out Lady O'Dowd's box of millinery from London (and which you may be sure Dobbin opened before any one of the other packets which the mail brought him), put the receiver into such a state of mind that Glorvina, and her pink satin, and everything belonging to her became perfectly odious to him. The Major cursed the talk of women, and the sex in general. Everything annoyed him that day--the parade was insufferably hot and wearisome. Good heavens! was a man of intellect to waste his life, day after day, inspecting cross-belts and putting fools through their manoeuvres? The senseless chatter of the young men at mess was more than ever jarring. What cared he, a man on the high road to forty, to know how many snipes Lieutenant Smith had shot, or what were the performances of Ensign Brown's mare? The jokes about the table filled him with shame. He was too old to listen to the banter of the assistant surgeon and the slang of the youngsters, at which old O'Dowd, with his bald head and red face, laughed quite easily. The old man had listened to those jokes any time these thirty years--Dobbin himself had been fifteen years hearing them. And after the boisterous dulness of the mess-table, the quarrels and scandal of the ladies of the regiment! It was unbearable, shameful. \"O Amelia, Amelia,\" he thought, \"you to whom I have been so faithful--you reproach me! It is because you cannot feel for me that I drag on this wearisome life. And you reward me after years of devotion by giving me your blessing upon my marriage, forsooth, with this flaunting Irish girl!\" Sick and sorry felt poor William; more than ever wretched and lonely. He would like to have done with life and its vanity altogether--so bootless and unsatisfactory the struggle, so cheerless and dreary the prospect seemed to him. He lay all that night sleepless, and yearning to go home. Amelia's letter had fallen as a blank upon him. No fidelity, no constant truth and passion, could move her into warmth. She would not see that he loved her. Tossing in his bed, he spoke out to her. \"Good God, Amelia!\" he said, \"don't you know that I only love you in the world--you, who are a stone to me--you, whom I tended through months and months of illness and grief, and who bade me farewell with a smile on your face, and forgot me before the door shut between us!\" The native servants lying outside his verandas beheld with wonder the Major, so cold and quiet ordinarily, at present so passionately moved and cast down. Would she have pitied him had she seen him? He read over and over all the letters which he ever had from her--letters of business relative to the little property which he had made her believe her husband had left to her--brief notes of invitation--every scrap of writing that she had ever sent to him--how cold, how kind, how hopeless, how selfish they were!\n\nHad there been some kind gentle soul near at hand who could read and appreciate this silent generous heart, who knows but that the reign of Amelia might have been over, and that friend William's love might have flowed into a kinder channel? But there was only Glorvina of the jetty ringlets with whom his intercourse was familiar, and this dashing young woman was not bent upon loving the Major, but rather on making the Major admire HER--a most vain and hopeless task, too, at least considering the means that the poor girl possessed to carry it out. She curled her hair and showed her shoulders at him, as much as to say, did ye ever see such jet ringlets and such a complexion? She grinned at him so that he might see that every tooth in her head was sound--and he never heeded all these charms. Very soon after the arrival of the box of millinery, and perhaps indeed in honour of it, Lady O'Dowd and the ladies of the King's Regiment gave a ball to the Company's Regiments and the civilians at the station. Glorvina sported the killing pink frock, and the Major, who attended the party and walked very ruefully up and down the rooms, never so much as perceived the pink garment. Glorvina danced past him in a fury with all the young subalterns of the station, and the Major was not in the least jealous of her performance, or angry because Captain Bangles of the Cavalry handed her to supper. It was not jealousy, or frocks, or shoulders that could move him, and Glorvina had nothing more.\n\nSo these two were each exemplifying the Vanity of this life, and each longing for what he or she could not get. Glorvina cried with rage at the failure. She had set her mind on the Major \"more than on any of the others,\" she owned, sobbing. \"He'll break my heart, he will, Peggy,\" she would whimper to her sister-in-law when they were good friends; \"sure every one of me frocks must be taken in--it's such a skeleton I'm growing.\" Fat or thin, laughing or melancholy, on horseback or the music-stool, it was all the same to the Major. And the Colonel, puffing his pipe and listening to these complaints, would suggest that Glory should have some black frocks out in the next box from London, and told a mysterious story of a lady in Ireland who died of grief for the loss of her husband before she got ere a one.\n\nWhile the Major was going on in this tantalizing way, not proposing, and declining to fall in love, there came another ship from Europe bringing letters on board, and amongst them some more for the heartless man. These were home letters bearing an earlier postmark than that of the former packets, and as Major Dobbin recognized among his the handwriting of his sister, who always crossed and recrossed her letters to her brother--gathered together all the possible bad news which she could collect, abused him and read him lectures with sisterly frankness, and always left him miserable for the day after \"dearest William\" had achieved the perusal of one of her epistles--the truth must be told that dearest William did not hurry himself to break the seal of Miss Dobbin's letter, but waited for a particularly favourable day and mood for doing so. A fortnight before, moreover, he had written to scold her for telling those absurd stories to Mrs. Osborne, and had despatched a letter in reply to that lady, undeceiving her with respect to the reports concerning him and assuring her that \"he had no sort of present intention of altering his condition.\"\n\nTwo or three nights after the arrival of the second package of letters, the Major had passed the evening pretty cheerfully at Lady O'Dowd's house, where Glorvina thought that he listened with rather more attention than usual to the Meeting of the Wathers, the Minsthrel Boy, and one or two other specimens of song with which she favoured him (the truth is, he was no more listening to Glorvina than to the howling of the jackals in the moonlight outside, and the delusion was hers as usual), and having played his game at chess with her (cribbage with the surgeon was Lady O'Dowd's favourite evening pastime), Major Dobbin took leave of the Colonel's family at his usual hour and retired to his own house.\n\nThere on his table, his sister's letter lay reproaching him. He took it up, ashamed rather of his negligence regarding it, and prepared himself for a disagreeable hour's communing with that crabbed-handed absent relative. . . . It may have been an hour after the Major's departure from the Colonel's house--Sir Michael was sleeping the sleep of the just; Glorvina had arranged her black ringlets in the innumerable little bits of paper, in which it was her habit to confine them; Lady O'Dowd, too, had gone to her bed in the nuptial chamber, on the ground-floor, and had tucked her musquito curtains round her fair form, when the guard at the gates of the Commanding-Officer's compound beheld Major Dobbin, in the moonlight, rushing towards the house with a swift step and a very agitated countenance, and he passed the sentinel and went up to the windows of the Colonel's bedchamber.\n\n\"O'Dowd--Colonel!\" said Dobbin and kept up a great shouting.\n\n\"Heavens, Meejor!\" said Glorvina of the curl-papers, putting out her head too, from her window.\n\n\"What is it, Dob, me boy?\" said the Colonel, expecting there was a fire in the station, or that the route had come from headquarters.\n\n\"I--I must have leave of absence. I must go to England--on the most urgent private affairs,\" Dobbin said.\n\n\"Good heavens, what has happened!\" thought Glorvina, trembling with all the papillotes.\n\n\"I want to be off--now--to-night,\" Dobbin continued; and the Colonel getting up, came out to parley with him.\n\nIn the postscript of Miss Dobbin's cross-letter, the Major had just come upon a paragraph, to the following effect:--\"I drove yesterday to see your old ACQUAINTANCE, Mrs. Osborne. The wretched place they live at, since they were bankrupts, you know--Mr. S., to judge from a BRASS PLATE on the door of his hut (it is little better) is a coal-merchant. The little boy, your godson, is certainly a fine child, though forward, and inclined to be saucy and self-willed. But we have taken notice of him as you wish it, and have introduced him to his aunt, Miss O., who was rather pleased with him. Perhaps his grandpapa, not the bankrupt one, who is almost doting, but Mr. Osborne, of Russell Square, may be induced to relent towards the child of your friend, HIS ERRING AND SELF-WILLED SON. And Amelia will not be ill-disposed to give him up. The widow is CONSOLED, and is about to marry a reverend gentleman, the Rev. Mr. Binny, one of the curates of Brompton. A poor match. But Mrs. O. is getting old, and I saw a great deal of grey in her hair--she was in very good spirits: and your little godson overate himself at our house. Mamma sends her love with that of your affectionate, Ann Dobbin.\"\n\nA Round-about Chapter between London and Hampshire\nOur old friends the Crawleys' family house, in Great Gaunt Street, still bore over its front the hatchment which had been placed there as a token of mourning for Sir Pitt Crawley's demise, yet this heraldic emblem was in itself a very splendid and gaudy piece of furniture, and all the rest of the mansion became more brilliant than it had ever been during the late baronet's reign. The black outer-coating of the bricks was removed, and they appeared with a cheerful, blushing face streaked with white: the old bronze lions of the knocker were gilt handsomely, the railings painted, and the dismallest house in Great Gaunt Street became the smartest in the whole quarter, before the green leaves in Hampshire had replaced those yellowing ones which were on the trees in Queen's Crawley Avenue when old Sir Pitt Crawley passed under them for the last time.\n\nA little woman, with a carriage to correspond, was perpetually seen about this mansion; an elderly spinster, accompanied by a little boy, also might be remarked coming thither daily. It was Miss Briggs and little Rawdon, whose business it was to see to the inward renovation of Sir Pitt's house, to superintend the female band engaged in stitching the blinds and hangings, to poke and rummage in the drawers and cupboards crammed with the dirty relics and congregated trumperies of a couple of generations of Lady Crawleys, and to take inventories of the china, the glass, and other properties in the closets and store-rooms.\n\nMrs. Rawdon Crawley was general-in-chief over these arrangements, with full orders from Sir Pitt to sell, barter, confiscate, or purchase furniture, and she enjoyed herself not a little in an occupation which gave full scope to her taste and ingenuity. The renovation of the house was determined upon when Sir Pitt came to town in November to see his lawyers, and when he passed nearly a week in Curzon Street, under the roof of his affectionate brother and sister.\n\nHe had put up at an hotel at first, but, Becky, as soon as she heard of the Baronet's arrival, went off alone to greet him, and returned in an hour to Curzon Street with Sir Pitt in the carriage by her side. It was impossible sometimes to resist this artless little creature's hospitalities, so kindly were they pressed, so frankly and amiably offered. Becky seized Pitt's hand in a transport of gratitude when he agreed to come. \"Thank you,\" she said, squeezing it and looking into the Baronet's eyes, who blushed a good deal; \"how happy this will make Rawdon!\" She bustled up to Pitt's bedroom, leading on the servants, who were carrying his trunks thither. She came in herself laughing, with a coal-scuttle out of her own room.\n\nA fire was blazing already in Sir Pitt's apartment (it was Miss Briggs's room, by the way, who was sent upstairs to sleep with the maid). \"I knew I should bring you,\" she said with pleasure beaming in her glance. Indeed, she was really sincerely happy at having him for a guest.\n\nBecky made Rawdon dine out once or twice on business, while Pitt stayed with them, and the Baronet passed the happy evening alone with her and Briggs. She went downstairs to the kitchen and actually cooked little dishes for him. \"Isn't it a good salmi?\" she said; \"I made it for you. I can make you better dishes than that, and will when you come to see me.\"\n\n\"Everything you do, you do well,\" said the Baronet gallantly. \"The salmi is excellent indeed.\"\n\n\"A poor man's wife,\" Rebecca replied gaily, \"must make herself useful, you know\"; on which her brother-in-law vowed that \"she was fit to be the wife of an Emperor, and that to be skilful in domestic duties was surely one of the most charming of woman's qualities.\" And Sir Pitt thought, with something like mortification, of Lady Jane at home, and of a certain pie which she had insisted on making, and serving to him at dinner--a most abominable pie.\n\nBesides the salmi, which was made of Lord Steyne's pheasants from his lordship's cottage of Stillbrook, Becky gave her brother-in-law a bottle of white wine, some that Rawdon had brought with him from France, and had picked up for nothing, the little story-teller said; whereas the liquor was, in truth, some White Hermitage from the Marquis of Steyne's famous cellars, which brought fire into the Baronet's pallid cheeks and a glow into his feeble frame.\n\nThen when he had drunk up the bottle of petit vin blanc, she gave him her hand, and took him up to the drawing-room, and made him snug on the sofa by the fire, and let him talk as she listened with the tenderest kindly interest, sitting by him, and hemming a shirt for her dear little boy. Whenever Mrs. Rawdon wished to be particularly humble and virtuous, this little shirt used to come out of her work-box. It had got to be too small for Rawdon long before it was finished.\n\nWell, Rebecca listened to Pitt, she talked to him, she sang to him, she coaxed him, and cuddled him, so that he found himself more and more glad every day to get back from the lawyer's at Gray's Inn, to the blazing fire in Curzon Street--a gladness in which the men of law likewise participated, for Pitt's harangues were of the longest--and so that when he went away he felt quite a pang at departing. How pretty she looked kissing her hand to him from the carriage and waving her handkerchief when he had taken his place in the mail! She put the handkerchief to her eyes once. He pulled his sealskin cap over his, as the coach drove away, and, sinking back, he thought to himself how she respected him and how he deserved it, and how Rawdon was a foolish dull fellow who didn't half appreciate his wife; and how mum and stupid his own wife was compared to that brilliant little Becky. Becky had hinted every one of these things herself, perhaps, but so delicately and gently that you hardly knew when or where. And, before they parted, it was agreed that the house in London should be redecorated for the next season, and that the brothers' families should meet again in the country at Christmas.\n\n\"I wish you could have got a little money out of him,\" Rawdon said to his wife moodily when the Baronet was gone. \"I should like to give something to old Raggles, hanged if I shouldn't. It ain't right, you know, that the old fellow should be kept out of all his money. It may be inconvenient, and he might let to somebody else besides us, you know.\"\n\n\"Tell him,\" said Becky, \"that as soon as Sir Pitt's affairs are settled, everybody will be paid, and give him a little something on account. Here's a cheque that Pitt left for the boy,\" and she took from her bag and gave her husband a paper which his brother had handed over to her, on behalf of the little son and heir of the younger branch of the Crawleys.\n\nThe truth is, she had tried personally the ground on which her husband expressed a wish that she should venture--tried it ever so delicately, and found it unsafe. Even at a hint about embarrassments, Sir Pitt Crawley was off and alarmed. And he began a long speech, explaining how straitened he himself was in money matters; how the tenants would not pay; how his father's affairs, and the expenses attendant upon the demise of the old gentleman, had involved him; how he wanted to pay off incumbrances; and how the bankers and agents were overdrawn; and Pitt Crawley ended by making a compromise with his sister-in-law and giving her a very small sum for the benefit of her little boy.\n\nPitt knew how poor his brother and his brother's family must be. It could not have escaped the notice of such a cool and experienced old diplomatist that Rawdon's family had nothing to live upon, and that houses and carriages are not to be kept for nothing. He knew very well that he was the proprietor or appropriator of the money, which, according to all proper calculation, ought to have fallen to his younger brother, and he had, we may be sure, some secret pangs of remorse within him, which warned him that he ought to perform some act of justice, or, let us say, compensation, towards these disappointed relations. A just, decent man, not without brains, who said his prayers, and knew his catechism, and did his duty outwardly through life, he could not be otherwise than aware that something was due to his brother at his hands, and that morally he was Rawdon's debtor.\n\nBut, as one reads in the columns of the Times newspaper every now and then, queer announcements from the Chancellor of the Exchequer, acknowledging the receipt of 50 pounds from A. B., or 10 pounds from W. T., as conscience-money, on account of taxes due by the said A. B. or W. T., which payments the penitents beg the Right Honourable gentleman to acknowledge through the medium of the public press--so is the Chancellor no doubt, and the reader likewise, always perfectly sure that the above-named A. B. and W. T. are only paying a very small instalment of what they really owe, and that the man who sends up a twenty-pound note has very likely hundreds or thousands more for which he ought to account. Such, at least, are my feelings, when I see A. B. or W. T.'s insufficient acts of repentance. And I have no doubt that Pitt Crawley's contrition, or kindness if you will, towards his younger brother, by whom he had so much profited, was only a very small dividend upon the capital sum in which he was indebted to Rawdon. Not everybody is willing to pay even so much. To part with money is a sacrifice beyond almost all men endowed with a sense of order. There is scarcely any man alive who does not think himself meritorious for giving his neighbour five pounds. Thriftless gives, not from a beneficent pleasure in giving, but from a lazy delight in spending. He would not deny himself one enjoyment; not his opera-stall, not his horse, not his dinner, not even the pleasure of giving Lazarus the five pounds. Thrifty, who is good, wise, just, and owes no man a penny, turns from a beggar, haggles with a hackney-coachman, or denies a poor relation, and I doubt which is the most selfish of the two. Money has only a different value in the eyes of each.\n\nSo, in a word, Pitt Crawley thought he would do something for his brother, and then thought that he would think about it some other time.\n\nAnd with regard to Becky, she was not a woman who expected too much from the generosity of her neighbours, and so was quite content with all that Pitt Crawley had done for her. She was acknowledged by the head of the family. If Pitt would not give her anything, he would get something for her some day. If she got no money from her brother-in-law, she got what was as good as money--credit. Raggles was made rather easy in his mind by the spectacle of the union between the brothers, by a small payment on the spot, and by the promise of a much larger sum speedily to be assigned to him. And Rebecca told Miss Briggs, whose Christmas dividend upon the little sum lent by her Becky paid with an air of candid joy, and as if her exchequer was brimming over with gold--Rebecca, we say, told Miss Briggs, in strict confidence that she had conferred with Sir Pitt, who was famous as a financier, on Briggs's special behalf, as to the most profitable investment of Miss B.'s remaining capital; that Sir Pitt, after much consideration, had thought of a most safe and advantageous way in which Briggs could lay out her money; that, being especially interested in her as an attached friend of the late Miss Crawley, and of the whole family, and that long before he left town, he had recommended that she should be ready with the money at a moment's notice, so as to purchase at the most favourable opportunity the shares which Sir Pitt had in his eye. Poor Miss Briggs was very grateful for this mark of Sir Pitt's attention--it came so unsolicited, she said, for she never should have thought of removing the money from the funds--and the delicacy enhanced the kindness of the office; and she promised to see her man of business immediately and be ready with her little cash at the proper hour.\n\nAnd this worthy woman was so grateful for the kindness of Rebecca in the matter, and for that of her generous benefactor, the Colonel, that she went out and spent a great part of her half-year's dividend in the purchase of a black velvet coat for little Rawdon, who, by the way, was grown almost too big for black velvet now, and was of a size and age befitting him for the assumption of the virile jacket and pantaloons.\n\nHe was a fine open-faced boy, with blue eyes and waving flaxen hair, sturdy in limb, but generous and soft in heart, fondly attaching himself to all who were good to him--to the pony--to Lord Southdown, who gave him the horse (he used to blush and glow all over when he saw that kind young nobleman)--to the groom who had charge of the pony--to Molly, the cook, who crammed him with ghost stories at night, and with good things from the dinner--to Briggs, whom he plagued and laughed at--and to his father especially, whose attachment towards the lad was curious too to witness. Here, as he grew to be about eight years old, his attachments may be said to have ended. The beautiful mother-vision had faded away after a while. During near two years she had scarcely spoken to the child. She disliked him. He had the measles and the hooping-cough. He bored her. One day when he was standing at the landing-place, having crept down from the upper regions, attracted by the sound of his mother's voice, who was singing to Lord Steyne, the drawing room door opening suddenly, discovered the little spy, who but a moment before had been rapt in delight, and listening to the music.\n\nHis mother came out and struck him violently a couple of boxes on the ear. He heard a laugh from the Marquis in the inner room (who was amused by this free and artless exhibition of Becky's temper) and fled down below to his friends of the kitchen, bursting in an agony of grief.\n\n\"It is not because it hurts me,\" little Rawdon gasped out--\"only--only\"--sobs and tears wound up the sentence in a storm. It was the little boy's heart that was bleeding. \"Why mayn't I hear her singing? Why don't she ever sing to me--as she does to that baldheaded man with the large teeth?\" He gasped out at various intervals these exclamations of rage and grief. The cook looked at the housemaid, the housemaid looked knowingly at the footman--the awful kitchen inquisition which sits in judgement in every house and knows everything--sat on Rebecca at that moment.\n\nAfter this incident, the mother's dislike increased to hatred; the consciousness that the child was in the house was a reproach and a pain to her. His very sight annoyed her. Fear, doubt, and resistance sprang up, too, in the boy's own bosom. They were separated from that day of the boxes on the ear.\n\nLord Steyne also heartily disliked the boy. When they met by mischance, he made sarcastic bows or remarks to the child, or glared at him with savage-looking eyes. Rawdon used to stare him in the face and double his little fists in return. He knew his enemy, and this gentleman, of all who came to the house, was the one who angered him most. One day the footman found him squaring his fists at Lord Steyne's hat in the hall. The footman told the circumstance as a good joke to Lord Steyne's coachman; that officer imparted it to Lord Steyne's gentleman, and to the servants' hall in general. And very soon afterwards, when Mrs. Rawdon Crawley made her appearance at Gaunt House, the porter who unbarred the gates, the servants of all uniforms in the hall, the functionaries in white waistcoats, who bawled out from landing to landing the names of Colonel and Mrs. Rawdon Crawley, knew about her, or fancied they did. The man who brought her refreshment and stood behind her chair, had talked her character over with the large gentleman in motley-coloured clothes at his side. Bon Dieu! it is awful, that servants' inquisition! You see a woman in a great party in a splendid saloon, surrounded by faithful admirers, distributing sparkling glances, dressed to perfection, curled, rouged, smiling and happy--Discovery walks respectfully up to her, in the shape of a huge powdered man with large calves and a tray of ices--with Calumny (which is as fatal as truth) behind him, in the shape of the hulking fellow carrying the wafer-biscuits. Madam, your secret will be talked over by those men at their club at the public-house to-night. Jeames will tell Chawles his notions about you over their pipes and pewter beer-pots. Some people ought to have mutes for servants in Vanity Fair--mutes who could not write. If you are guilty, tremble. That fellow behind your chair may be a Janissary with a bow-string in his plush breeches pocket. If you are not guilty, have a care of appearances, which are as ruinous as guilt.\n\n\"Was Rebecca guilty or not?\" the Vehmgericht of the servants' hall had pronounced against her.\n\nAnd, I shame to say, she would not have got credit had they not believed her to be guilty. It was the sight of the Marquis of Steyne's carriage-lamps at her door, contemplated by Raggles, burning in the blackness of midnight, \"that kep him up,\" as he afterwards said, that even more than Rebecca's arts and coaxings.\n\nAnd so--guiltless very likely--she was writhing and pushing onward towards what they call \"a position in society,\" and the servants were pointing at her as lost and ruined. So you see Molly, the housemaid, of a morning, watching a spider in the doorpost lay his thread and laboriously crawl up it, until, tired of the sport, she raises her broom and sweeps away the thread and the artificer.\n\nA day or two before Christmas, Becky, her husband and her son made ready and went to pass the holidays at the seat of their ancestors at Queen's Crawley. Becky would have liked to leave the little brat behind, and would have done so but for Lady Jane's urgent invitations to the youngster, and the symptoms of revolt and discontent which Rawdon manifested at her neglect of her son. \"He's the finest boy in England,\" the father said in a tone of reproach to her, \"and you don't seem to care for him, Becky, as much as you do for your spaniel. He shan't bother you much; at home he will be away from you in the nursery, and he shall go outside on the coach with me.\"\n\n\"Where you go yourself because you want to smoke those filthy cigars,\" replied Mrs. Rawdon.\n\n\"I remember when you liked 'em though,\" answered the husband.\n\nBecky laughed; she was almost always good-humoured. \"That was when I was on my promotion, Goosey,\" she said. \"Take Rawdon outside with you and give him a cigar too if you like.\"\n\nRawdon did not warm his little son for the winter's journey in this way, but he and Briggs wrapped up the child in shawls and comforters, and he was hoisted respectfully onto the roof of the coach in the dark morning, under the lamps of the White Horse Cellar; and with no small delight he watched the dawn rise and made his first journey to the place which his father still called home. It was a journey of infinite pleasure to the boy, to whom the incidents of the road afforded endless interest, his father answering to him all questions connected with it and telling him who lived in the great white house to the right, and whom the park belonged to. His mother, inside the vehicle, with her maid and her furs, her wrappers, and her scent bottles, made such a to-do that you would have thought she never had been in a stage-coach before--much less, that she had been turned out of this very one to make room for a paying passenger on a certain journey performed some half-score years ago.\n\nIt was dark again when little Rawdon was wakened up to enter his uncle's carriage at Mudbury, and he sat and looked out of it wondering as the great iron gates flew open, and at the white trunks of the limes as they swept by, until they stopped, at length, before the light windows of the Hall, which were blazing and comfortable with Christmas welcome. The hall-door was flung open--a big fire was burning in the great old fire-place--a carpet was down over the chequered black flags--\"It's the old Turkey one that used to be in the Ladies' Gallery,\" thought Rebecca, and the next instant was kissing Lady Jane.\n\nShe and Sir Pitt performed the same salute with great gravity; but Rawdon, having been smoking, hung back rather from his sister-in-law, whose two children came up to their cousin; and, while Matilda held out her hand and kissed him, Pitt Binkie Southdown, the son and heir, stood aloof rather and examined him as a little dog does a big dog.\n\nThen the kind hostess conducted her guests to the snug apartments blazing with cheerful fires. Then the young ladies came and knocked at Mrs. Rawdon's door, under the pretence that they were desirous to be useful, but in reality to have the pleasure of inspecting the contents of her band and bonnet-boxes, and her dresses which, though black, were of the newest London fashion. And they told her how much the Hall was changed for the better, and how old Lady Southdown was gone, and how Pitt was taking his station in the county, as became a Crawley in fact. Then the great dinner-bell having rung, the family assembled at dinner, at which meal Rawdon Junior was placed by his aunt, the good-natured lady of the house, Sir Pitt being uncommonly attentive to his sister-in-law at his own right hand.\n\nLittle Rawdon exhibited a fine appetite and showed a gentlemanlike behaviour.\n\n\"I like to dine here,\" he said to his aunt when he had completed his meal, at the conclusion of which, and after a decent grace by Sir Pitt, the younger son and heir was introduced, and was perched on a high chair by the Baronet's side, while the daughter took possession of the place and the little wine-glass prepared for her near her mother. \"I like to dine here,\" said Rawdon Minor, looking up at his relation's kind face.\n\n\"Why?\" said the good Lady Jane.\n\n\"I dine in the kitchen when I am at home,\" replied Rawdon Minor, \"or else with Briggs.\" But Becky was so engaged with the Baronet, her host, pouring out a flood of compliments and delights and raptures, and admiring young Pitt Binkie, whom she declared to be the most beautiful, intelligent, noble-looking little creature, and so like his father, that she did not hear the remarks of her own flesh and blood at the other end of the broad shining table.\n\nAs a guest, and it being the first night of his arrival, Rawdon the Second was allowed to sit up until the hour when tea being over, and a great gilt book being laid on the table before Sir Pitt, all the domestics of the family streamed in, and Sir Pitt read prayers. It was the first time the poor little boy had ever witnessed or heard of such a ceremonial.\n\nThe house had been much improved even since the Baronet's brief reign, and was pronounced by Becky to be perfect, charming, delightful, when she surveyed it in his company. As for little Rawdon, who examined it with the children for his guides, it seemed to him a perfect palace of enchantment and wonder. There were long galleries, and ancient state bedrooms, there were pictures and old China, and armour. There were the rooms in which Grandpapa died, and by which the children walked with terrified looks. \"Who was Grandpapa?\" he asked; and they told him how he used to be very old, and used to be wheeled about in a garden-chair, and they showed him the garden-chair one day rotting in the out-house in which it had lain since the old gentleman had been wheeled away yonder to the church, of which the spire was glittering over the park elms.\n\nThe brothers had good occupation for several mornings in examining the improvements which had been effected by Sir Pitt's genius and economy. And as they walked or rode, and looked at them, they could talk without too much boring each other. And Pitt took care to tell Rawdon what a heavy outlay of money these improvements had occasioned, and that a man of landed and funded property was often very hard pressed for twenty pounds. \"There is that new lodge-gate,\" said Pitt, pointing to it humbly with the bamboo cane, \"I can no more pay for it before the dividends in January than I can fly.\"\n\n\"I can lend you, Pitt, till then,\" Rawdon answered rather ruefully; and they went in and looked at the restored lodge, where the family arms were just new scraped in stone, and where old Mrs. Lock, for the first time these many long years, had tight doors, sound roofs, and whole windows.\n\nBetween Hampshire and London\nSir Pitt Crawley had done more than repair fences and restore dilapidated lodges on the Queen's Crawley estate. Like a wise man he had set to work to rebuild the injured popularity of his house and stop up the gaps and ruins in which his name had been left by his disreputable and thriftless old predecessor. He was elected for the borough speedily after his father's demise; a magistrate, a member of parliament, a county magnate and representative of an ancient family, he made it his duty to show himself before the Hampshire public, subscribed handsomely to the county charities, called assiduously upon all the county folk, and laid himself out in a word to take that position in Hampshire, and in the Empire afterwards, to which he thought his prodigious talents justly entitled him. Lady Jane was instructed to be friendly with the Fuddlestones, and the Wapshots, and the other famous baronets, their neighbours. Their carriages might frequently be seen in the Queen's Crawley avenue now; they dined pretty frequently at the Hall (where the cookery was so good that it was clear Lady Jane very seldom had a hand in it), and in return Pitt and his wife most energetically dined out in all sorts of weather and at all sorts of distances. For though Pitt did not care for joviality, being a frigid man of poor hearth and appetite, yet he considered that to be hospitable and condescending was quite incumbent on his station, and every time that he got a headache from too long an after-dinner sitting, he felt that he was a martyr to duty. He talked about crops, corn-laws, politics, with the best country gentlemen. He (who had been formerly inclined to be a sad free-thinker on these points) entered into poaching and game preserving with ardour. He didn't hunt; he wasn't a hunting man; he was a man of books and peaceful habits; but he thought that the breed of horses must be kept up in the country, and that the breed of foxes must therefore be looked to, and for his part, if his friend, Sir Huddlestone Fuddlestone, liked to draw his country and meet as of old the F. hounds used to do at Queen's Crawley, he should be happy to see him there, and the gentlemen of the Fuddlestone hunt. And to Lady Southdown's dismay too he became more orthodox in his tendencies every day; gave up preaching in public and attending meeting-houses; went stoutly to church; called on the Bishop and all the Clergy at Winchester; and made no objection when the Venerable Archdeacon Trumper asked for a game of whist. What pangs must have been those of Lady Southdown, and what an utter castaway she must have thought her son-in-law for permitting such a godless diversion! And when, on the return of the family from an oratorio at Winchester, the Baronet announced to the young ladies that he should next year very probably take them to the \"county balls,\" they worshipped him for his kindness. Lady Jane was only too obedient, and perhaps glad herself to go. The Dowager wrote off the direst descriptions of her daughter's worldly behaviour to the authoress of the Washerwoman of Finchley Common at the Cape; and her house in Brighton being about this time unoccupied, returned to that watering-place, her absence being not very much deplored by her children. We may suppose, too, that Rebecca, on paying a second visit to Queen's Crawley, did not feel particularly grieved at the absence of the lady of the medicine chest; though she wrote a Christmas letter to her Ladyship, in which she respectfully recalled herself to Lady Southdown's recollection, spoke with gratitude of the delight which her Ladyship's conversation had given her on the former visit, dilated on the kindness with which her Ladyship had treated her in sickness, and declared that everything at Queen's Crawley reminded her of her absent friend.\n\nA great part of the altered demeanour and popularity of Sir Pitt Crawley might have been traced to the counsels of that astute little lady of Curzon Street. \"You remain a Baronet--you consent to be a mere country gentleman,\" she said to him, while he had been her guest in London. \"No, Sir Pitt Crawley, I know you better. I know your talents and your ambition. You fancy you hide them both, but you can conceal neither from me. I showed Lord Steyne your pamphlet on malt. He was familiar with it, and said it was in the opinion of the whole Cabinet the most masterly thing that had appeared on the subject. The Ministry has its eye upon you, and I know what you want. You want to distinguish yourself in Parliament; every one says you are the finest speaker in England (for your speeches at Oxford are still remembered). You want to be Member for the County, where, with your own vote and your borough at your back, you can command anything. And you want to be Baron Crawley of Queen's Crawley, and will be before you die. I saw it all. I could read your heart, Sir Pitt. If I had a husband who possessed your intellect as he does your name, I sometimes think I should not be unworthy of him--but--but I am your kinswoman now,\" she added with a laugh. \"Poor little penniless, I have got a little interest--and who knows, perhaps the mouse may be able to aid the lion.\" Pitt Crawley was amazed and enraptured with her speech. \"How that woman comprehends me!\" he said. \"I never could get Jane to read three pages of the malt pamphlet. She has no idea that I have commanding talents or secret ambition. So they remember my speaking at Oxford, do they? The rascals! Now that I represent my borough and may sit for the county, they begin to recollect me! Why, Lord Steyne cut me at the levee last year; they are beginning to find out that Pitt Crawley is some one at last. Yes, the man was always the same whom these people neglected: it was only the opportunity that was wanting, and I will show them now that I can speak and act as well as write. Achilles did not declare himself until they gave him the sword. I hold it now, and the world shall yet hear of Pitt Crawley.\"\n\nTherefore it was that this roguish diplomatist has grown so hospitable; that he was so civil to oratorios and hospitals; so kind to Deans and Chapters; so generous in giving and accepting dinners; so uncommonly gracious to farmers on market-days; and so much interested about county business; and that the Christmas at the Hall was the gayest which had been known there for many a long day.\n\nOn Christmas Day a great family gathering took place. All the Crawleys from the Rectory came to dine. Rebecca was as frank and fond of Mrs. Bute as if the other had never been her enemy; she was affectionately interested in the dear girls, and surprised at the progress which they had made in music since her time, and insisted upon encoring one of the duets out of the great song-books which Jim, grumbling, had been forced to bring under his arm from the Rectory. Mrs. Bute, perforce, was obliged to adopt a decent demeanour towards the little adventuress--of course being free to discourse with her daughters afterwards about the absurd respect with which Sir Pitt treated his sister-in-law. But Jim, who had sat next to her at dinner, declared she was a trump, and one and all of the Rector's family agreed that the little Rawdon was a fine boy. They respected a possible baronet in the boy, between whom and the title there was only the little sickly pale Pitt Binkie.\n\nThe children were very good friends. Pitt Binkie was too little a dog for such a big dog as Rawdon to play with; and Matilda being only a girl, of course not fit companion for a young gentleman who was near eight years old, and going into jackets very soon. He took the command of this small party at once--the little girl and the little boy following him about with great reverence at such times as he condescended to sport with them. His happiness and pleasure in the country were extreme. The kitchen garden pleased him hugely, the flowers moderately, but the pigeons and the poultry, and the stables when he was allowed to visit them, were delightful objects to him. He resisted being kissed by the Misses Crawley, but he allowed Lady Jane sometimes to embrace him, and it was by her side that he liked to sit when, the signal to retire to the drawing-room being given, the ladies left the gentlemen to their claret--by her side rather than by his mother. For Rebecca, seeing that tenderness was the fashion, called Rawdon to her one evening and stooped down and kissed him in the presence of all the ladies.\n\nHe looked her full in the face after the operation, trembling and turning very red, as his wont was when moved. \"You never kiss me at home, Mamma,\" he said, at which there was a general silence and consternation and a by no means pleasant look in Becky's eyes.\n\nRawdon was fond of his sister-in-law, for her regard for his son. Lady Jane and Becky did not get on quite so well at this visit as on occasion of the former one, when the Colonel's wife was bent upon pleasing. Those two speeches of the child struck rather a chill. Perhaps Sir Pitt was rather too attentive to her.\n\nBut Rawdon, as became his age and size, was fonder of the society of the men than of the women, and never wearied of accompanying his sire to the stables, whither the Colonel retired to smoke his cigar--Jim, the Rector's son, sometimes joining his cousin in that and other amusements. He and the Baronet's keeper were very close friends, their mutual taste for \"dawgs\" bringing them much together. On one day, Mr. James, the Colonel, and Horn, the keeper, went and shot pheasants, taking little Rawdon with them. On another most blissful morning, these four gentlemen partook of the amusement of rat-hunting in a barn, than which sport Rawdon as yet had never seen anything more noble. They stopped up the ends of certain drains in the barn, into the other openings of which ferrets were inserted, and then stood silently aloof, with uplifted stakes in their hands, and an anxious little terrier (Mr. James's celebrated \"dawg\" Forceps, indeed) scarcely breathing from excitement, listening motionless on three legs, to the faint squeaking of the rats below. Desperately bold at last, the persecuted animals bolted above-ground--the terrier accounted for one, the keeper for another; Rawdon, from flurry and excitement, missed his rat, but on the other hand he half-murdered a ferret.\n\nBut the greatest day of all was that on which Sir Huddlestone Fuddlestone's hounds met upon the lawn at Queen's Crawley.\n\nThat was a famous sight for little Rawdon. At half-past ten, Tom Moody, Sir Huddlestone Fuddlestone's huntsman, was seen trotting up the avenue, followed by the noble pack of hounds in a compact body--the rear being brought up by the two whips clad in stained scarlet frocks--light hard-featured lads on well-bred lean horses, possessing marvellous dexterity in casting the points of their long heavy whips at the thinnest part of any dog's skin who dares to straggle from the main body, or to take the slightest notice, or even so much as wink, at the hares and rabbits starting under their noses.\n\nNext comes boy Jack, Tom Moody's son, who weighs five stone, measures eight-and-forty inches, and will never be any bigger. He is perched on a large raw-boned hunter, half-covered by a capacious saddle. This animal is Sir Huddlestone Fuddlestone's favourite horse the Nob. Other horses, ridden by other small boys, arrive from time to time, awaiting their masters, who will come cantering on anon.\n\nTom Moody rides up to the door of the Hall, where he is welcomed by the butler, who offers him drink, which he declines. He and his pack then draw off into a sheltered corner of the lawn, where the dogs roll on the grass, and play or growl angrily at one another, ever and anon breaking out into furious fight speedily to be quelled by Tom's voice, unmatched at rating, or the snaky thongs of the whips.\n\nMany young gentlemen canter up on thoroughbred hacks, spatter-dashed to the knee, and enter the house to drink cherry-brandy and pay their respects to the ladies, or, more modest and sportsmanlike, divest themselves of their mud-boots, exchange their hacks for their hunters, and warm their blood by a preliminary gallop round the lawn. Then they collect round the pack in the corner and talk with Tom Moody of past sport, and the merits of Sniveller and Diamond, and of the state of the country and of the wretched breed of foxes.\n\nSir Huddlestone presently appears mounted on a clever cob and rides up to the Hall, where he enters and does the civil thing by the ladies, after which, being a man of few words, he proceeds to business. The hounds are drawn up to the hall-door, and little Rawdon descends amongst them, excited yet half-alarmed by the caresses which they bestow upon him, at the thumps he receives from their waving tails, and at their canine bickerings, scarcely restrained by Tom Moody's tongue and lash.\n\nMeanwhile, Sir Huddlestone has hoisted himself unwieldily on the Nob: \"Let's try Sowster's Spinney, Tom,\" says the Baronet, \"Farmer Mangle tells me there are two foxes in it.\" Tom blows his horn and trots off, followed by the pack, by the whips, by the young gents from Winchester, by the farmers of the neighbourhood, by the labourers of the parish on foot, with whom the day is a great holiday, Sir Huddlestone bringing up the rear with Colonel Crawley, and the whole cortege disappears down the avenue.\n\nThe Reverend Bute Crawley (who has been too modest to appear at the public meet before his nephew's windows), whom Tom Moody remembers forty years back a slender divine riding the wildest horses, jumping the widest brooks, and larking over the newest gates in the country--his Reverence, we say, happens to trot out from the Rectory Lane on his powerful black horse just as Sir Huddlestone passes; he joins the worthy Baronet. Hounds and horsemen disappear, and little Rawdon remains on the doorsteps, wondering and happy.\n\nDuring the progress of this memorable holiday, little Rawdon, if he had got no special liking for his uncle, always awful and cold and locked up in his study, plunged in justice-business and surrounded by bailiffs and farmers--has gained the good graces of his married and maiden aunts, of the two little folks of the Hall, and of Jim of the Rectory, whom Sir Pitt is encouraging to pay his addresses to one of the young ladies, with an understanding doubtless that he shall be presented to the living when it shall be vacated by his fox-hunting old sire. Jim has given up that sport himself and confines himself to a little harmless duck- or snipe-shooting, or a little quiet trifling with the rats during the Christmas holidays, after which he will return to the University and try and not be plucked, once more. He has already eschewed green coats, red neckcloths, and other worldly ornaments, and is preparing himself for a change in his condition. In this cheap and thrifty way Sir Pitt tries to pay off his debt to his family.\n\nAlso before this merry Christmas was over, the Baronet had screwed up courage enough to give his brother another draft on his bankers, and for no less a sum than a hundred pounds, an act which caused Sir Pitt cruel pangs at first, but which made him glow afterwards to think himself one of the most generous of men. Rawdon and his son went away with the utmost heaviness of heart. Becky and the ladies parted with some alacrity, however, and our friend returned to London to commence those avocations with which we find her occupied when this chapter begins. Under her care the Crawley House in Great Gaunt Street was quite rejuvenescent and ready for the reception of Sir Pitt and his family, when the Baronet came to London to attend his duties in Parliament and to assume that position in the country for which his vast genius fitted him.\n\nFor the first session, this profound dissembler hid his projects and never opened his lips but to present a petition from Mudbury. But he attended assiduously in his place and learned thoroughly the routine and business of the House. At home he gave himself up to the perusal of Blue Books, to the alarm and wonder of Lady Jane, who thought he was killing himself by late hours and intense application. And he made acquaintance with the ministers, and the chiefs of his party, determining to rank as one of them before many years were over.\n\nLady Jane's sweetness and kindness had inspired Rebecca with such a contempt for her ladyship as the little woman found no small difficulty in concealing. That sort of goodness and simplicity which Lady Jane possessed annoyed our friend Becky, and it was impossible for her at times not to show, or to let the other divine, her scorn. Her presence, too, rendered Lady Jane uneasy. Her husband talked constantly with Becky. Signs of intelligence seemed to pass between them, and Pitt spoke with her on subjects on which he never thought of discoursing with Lady Jane. The latter did not understand them, to be sure, but it was mortifying to remain silent; still more mortifying to know that you had nothing to say, and hear that little audacious Mrs. Rawdon dashing on from subject to subject, with a word for every man, and a joke always pat; and to sit in one's own house alone, by the fireside, and watching all the men round your rival.\n\nIn the country, when Lady Jane was telling stories to the children, who clustered about her knees (little Rawdon into the bargain, who was very fond of her), and Becky came into the room, sneering with green scornful eyes, poor Lady Jane grew silent under those baleful glances. Her simple little fancies shrank away tremulously, as fairies in the story-books, before a superior bad angel. She could not go on, although Rebecca, with the smallest inflection of sarcasm in her voice, besought her to continue that charming story. And on her side gentle thoughts and simple pleasures were odious to Mrs. Becky; they discorded with her; she hated people for liking them; she spurned children and children-lovers. \"I have no taste for bread and butter,\" she would say, when caricaturing Lady Jane and her ways to my Lord Steyne.\n\n\"No more has a certain person for holy water,\" his lordship replied with a bow and a grin and a great jarring laugh afterwards.\n\nSo these two ladies did not see much of each other except upon those occasions when the younger brother's wife, having an object to gain from the other, frequented her. They my-loved and my-deared each other assiduously, but kept apart generally, whereas Sir Pitt, in the midst of his multiplied avocations, found daily time to see his sister-in-law.\n\nOn the occasion of his first Speaker's dinner, Sir Pitt took the opportunity of appearing before his sister-in-law in his uniform--that old diplomatic suit which he had worn when attache to the Pumpernickel legation.\n\nBecky complimented him upon that dress and admired him almost as much as his own wife and children, to whom he displayed himself before he set out. She said that it was only the thoroughbred gentleman who could wear the Court suit with advantage: it was only your men of ancient race whom the culotte courte became. Pitt looked down with complacency at his legs, which had not, in truth, much more symmetry or swell than the lean Court sword which dangled by his side--looked down at his legs, and thought in his heart that he was killing.\n\nWhen he was gone, Mrs. Becky made a caricature of his figure, which she showed to Lord Steyne when he arrived. His lordship carried off the sketch, delighted with the accuracy of the resemblance. He had done Sir Pitt Crawley the honour to meet him at Mrs. Becky's house and had been most gracious to the new Baronet and member. Pitt was struck too by the deference with which the great Peer treated his sister-in-law, by her ease and sprightliness in the conversation, and by the delight with which the other men of the party listened to her talk. Lord Steyne made no doubt but that the Baronet had only commenced his career in public life, and expected rather anxiously to hear him as an orator; as they were neighbours (for Great Gaunt Street leads into Gaunt Square, whereof Gaunt House, as everybody knows, forms one side) my lord hoped that as soon as Lady Steyne arrived in London she would have the honour of making the acquaintance of Lady Crawley. He left a card upon his neighbour in the course of a day or two, having never thought fit to notice his predecessor, though they had lived near each other for near a century past.\n\nIn the midst of these intrigues and fine parties and wise and brilliant personages Rawdon felt himself more and more isolated every day. He was allowed to go to the club more; to dine abroad with bachelor friends; to come and go when he liked, without any questions being asked. And he and Rawdon the younger many a time would walk to Gaunt Street and sit with the lady and the children there while Sir Pitt was closeted with Rebecca, on his way to the House, or on his return from it.\n\nThe ex-Colonel would sit for hours in his brother's house very silent, and thinking and doing as little as possible. He was glad to be employed of an errand; to go and make inquiries about a horse or a servant, or to carve the roast mutton for the dinner of the children. He was beat and cowed into laziness and submission. Delilah had imprisoned him and cut his hair off, too. The bold and reckless young blood of ten-years back was subjugated and was turned into a torpid, submissive, middle-aged, stout gentleman.\n\nAnd poor Lady Jane was aware that Rebecca had captivated her husband, although she and Mrs. Rawdon my-deared and my-loved each other every day they met.\n\nStruggles and Trials\nOur friends at Brompton were meanwhile passing their Christmas after their fashion and in a manner by no means too cheerful.\n\nOut of the hundred pounds a year, which was about the amount of her income, the Widow Osborne had been in the habit of giving up nearly three-fourths to her father and mother, for the expenses of herself and her little boy. With 120l. more, supplied by Jos, this family of four people, attended by a single Irish servant who also did for Clapp and his wife, might manage to live in decent comfort through the year, and hold up their heads yet, and be able to give a friend a dish of tea still, after the storms and disappointments of their early life. Sedley still maintained his ascendency over the family of Mr. Clapp, his ex-clerk. Clapp remembered the time when, sitting on the edge of the chair, he tossed off a bumper to the health of \"Mrs. S--, Miss Emmy, and Mr. Joseph in India,\" at the merchant's rich table in Russell Square. Time magnified the splendour of those recollections in the honest clerk's bosom. Whenever he came up from the kitchen-parlour to the drawing-room and partook of tea or gin-and-water with Mr. Sedley, he would say, \"This was not what you was accustomed to once, sir,\" and as gravely and reverentially drink the health of the ladies as he had done in the days of their utmost prosperity. He thought Miss 'Melia's playing the divinest music ever performed, and her the finest lady. He never would sit down before Sedley at the club even, nor would he have that gentleman's character abused by any member of the society. He had seen the first men in London shaking hands with Mr. S--; he said, \"He'd known him in times when Rothschild might be seen on 'Change with him any day, and he owed him personally everythink.\"\n\nClapp, with the best of characters and handwritings, had been able very soon after his master's disaster to find other employment for himself. \"Such a little fish as me can swim in any bucket,\" he used to remark, and a member of the house from which old Sedley had seceded was very glad to make use of Mr. Clapp's services and to reward them with a comfortable salary. In fine, all Sedley's wealthy friends had dropped off one by one, and this poor ex-dependent still remained faithfully attached to him.\n\nOut of the small residue of her income which Amelia kept back for herself, the widow had need of all the thrift and care possible in order to enable her to keep her darling boy dressed in such a manner as became George Osborne's son, and to defray the expenses of the little school to which, after much misgiving and reluctance and many secret pangs and fears on her own part, she had been induced to send the lad. She had sat up of nights conning lessons and spelling over crabbed grammars and geography books in order to teach them to Georgy. She had worked even at the Latin accidence, fondly hoping that she might be capable of instructing him in that language. To part with him all day, to send him out to the mercy of a schoolmaster's cane and his schoolfellows' roughness, was almost like weaning him over again to that weak mother, so tremulous and full of sensibility. He, for his part, rushed off to the school with the utmost happiness. He was longing for the change. That childish gladness wounded his mother, who was herself so grieved to part with him. She would rather have had him more sorry, she thought, and then was deeply repentant within herself for daring to be so selfish as to wish her own son to be unhappy.\n\nGeorgy made great progress in the school, which was kept by a friend of his mother's constant admirer, the Rev. Mr. Binny. He brought home numberless prizes and testimonials of ability. He told his mother countless stories every night about his school-companions: and what a fine fellow Lyons was, and what a sneak Sniffin was, and how Steel's father actually supplied the meat for the establishment, whereas Golding's mother came in a carriage to fetch him every Saturday, and how Neat had straps to his trowsers--might he have straps?--and how Bull Major was so strong (though only in Eutropius) that it was believed he could lick the Usher, Mr. Ward, himself. So Amelia learned to know every one of the boys in that school as well as Georgy himself, and of nights she used to help him in his exercises and puzzle her little head over his lessons as eagerly as if she was herself going in the morning into the presence of the master. Once, after a certain combat with Master Smith, George came home to his mother with a black eye, and bragged prodigiously to his parent and his delighted old grandfather about his valour in the fight, in which, if the truth was known he did not behave with particular heroism, and in which he decidedly had the worst. But Amelia has never forgiven that Smith to this day, though he is now a peaceful apothecary near Leicester Square.\n\nIn these quiet labours and harmless cares the gentle widow's life was passing away, a silver hair or two marking the progress of time on her head and a line deepening ever so little on her fair forehead. She used to smile at these marks of time. \"What matters it,\" she asked, \"For an old woman like me?\" All she hoped for was to live to see her son great, famous, and glorious, as he deserved to be. She kept his copy-books, his drawings, and compositions, and showed them about in her little circle as if they were miracles of genius. She confided some of these specimens to Miss Dobbin, to show them to Miss Osborne, George's aunt, to show them to Mr. Osborne himself--to make that old man repent of his cruelty and ill feeling towards him who was gone. All her husband's faults and foibles she had buried in the grave with him: she only remembered the lover, who had married her at all sacrifices, the noble husband, so brave and beautiful, in whose arms she had hung on the morning when he had gone away to fight, and die gloriously for his king. From heaven the hero must be smiling down upon that paragon of a boy whom he had left to comfort and console her. We have seen how one of George's grandfathers (Mr. Osborne), in his easy chair in Russell Square, daily grew more violent and moody, and how his daughter, with her fine carriage, and her fine horses, and her name on half the public charity-lists of the town, was a lonely, miserable, persecuted old maid. She thought again and again of the beautiful little boy, her brother's son, whom she had seen. She longed to be allowed to drive in the fine carriage to the house in which he lived, and she used to look out day after day as she took her solitary drive in the park, in hopes that she might see him. Her sister, the banker's lady, occasionally condescended to pay her old home and companion a visit in Russell Square. She brought a couple of sickly children attended by a prim nurse, and in a faint genteel giggling tone cackled to her sister about her fine acquaintance, and how her little Frederick was the image of Lord Claud Lollypop and her sweet Maria had been noticed by the Baroness as they were driving in their donkey-chaise at Roehampton. She urged her to make her papa do something for the darlings. Frederick she had determined should go into the Guards; and if they made an elder son of him (and Mr. Bullock was positively ruining and pinching himself to death to buy land), how was the darling girl to be provided for? \"I expect YOU, dear,\" Mrs. Bullock would say, \"for of course my share of our Papa's property must go to the head of the house, you know. Dear Rhoda McMull will disengage the whole of the Castletoddy property as soon as poor dear Lord Castletoddy dies, who is quite epileptic; and little Macduff McMull will be Viscount Castletoddy. Both the Mr. Bludyers of Mincing Lane have settled their fortunes on Fanny Bludyer's little boy. My darling Frederick must positively be an eldest son; and--and do ask Papa to bring us back his account in Lombard Street, will you, dear? It doesn't look well, his going to Stumpy and Rowdy's.\" After which kind of speeches, in which fashion and the main chance were blended together, and after a kiss, which was like the contact of an oyster--Mrs. Frederick Bullock would gather her starched nurslings and simper back into her carriage.\n\nEvery visit which this leader of ton paid to her family was more unlucky for her. Her father paid more money into Stumpy and Rowdy's. Her patronage became more and more insufferable. The poor widow in the little cottage at Brompton, guarding her treasure there, little knew how eagerly some people coveted it.\n\nOn that night when Jane Osborne had told her father that she had seen his grandson, the old man had made her no reply, but he had shown no anger--and had bade her good-night on going himself to his room in rather a kindly voice. And he must have meditated on what she said and have made some inquiries of the Dobbin family regarding her visit, for a fortnight after it took place, he asked her where was her little French watch and chain she used to wear?\n\n\"I bought it with my money, sir,\" she said in a great fright.\n\n\"Go and order another like it, or a better if you can get it,\" said the old gentleman and lapsed again into silence.\n\nOf late the Misses Dobbin more than once repeated their entreaties to Amelia, to allow George to visit them. His aunt had shown her inclination; perhaps his grandfather himself, they hinted, might be disposed to be reconciled to him. Surely, Amelia could not refuse such advantageous chances for the boy. Nor could she, but she acceded to their overtures with a very heavy and suspicious heart, was always uneasy during the child's absence from her, and welcomed him back as if he was rescued out of some danger. He brought back money and toys, at which the widow looked with alarm and jealousy; she asked him always if he had seen any gentleman--\"Only old Sir William, who drove him about in the four-wheeled chaise, and Mr. Dobbin, who arrived on the beautiful bay horse in the afternoon--in the green coat and pink neck-cloth, with the gold-headed whip, who promised to show him the Tower of London and take him out with the Surrey hounds.\" At last, he said, \"There was an old gentleman, with thick eyebrows, and a broad hat, and large chain and seals.\" He came one day as the coachman was lunging Georgy round the lawn on the gray pony. \"He looked at me very much. He shook very much. I said 'My name is Norval' after dinner. My aunt began to cry. She is always crying.\" Such was George's report on that night.\n\nThen Amelia knew that the boy had seen his grandfather; and looked out feverishly for a proposal which she was sure would follow, and which came, in fact, in a few days afterwards. Mr. Osborne formally offered to take the boy and make him heir to the fortune which he had intended that his father should inherit. He would make Mrs. George Osborne an allowance, such as to assure her a decent competency. If Mrs. George Osborne proposed to marry again, as Mr. O. heard was her intention, he would not withdraw that allowance. But it must be understood that the child would live entirely with his grandfather in Russell Square, or at whatever other place Mr. O. should select, and that he would be occasionally permitted to see Mrs. George Osborne at her own residence. This message was brought or read to her in a letter one day, when her mother was from home and her father absent as usual in the City.\n\nShe was never seen angry but twice or thrice in her life, and it was in one of these moods that Mr. Osborne's attorney had the fortune to behold her. She rose up trembling and flushing very much as soon as, after reading the letter, Mr. Poe handed it to her, and she tore the paper into a hundred fragments, which she trod on. \"I marry again! I take money to part from my child! Who dares insult me by proposing such a thing? Tell Mr. Osborne it is a cowardly letter, sir--a cowardly letter--I will not answer it. I wish you good morning, sir--and she bowed me out of the room like a tragedy Queen,\" said the lawyer who told the story.\n\nHer parents never remarked her agitation on that day, and she never told them of the interview. They had their own affairs to interest them, affairs which deeply interested this innocent and unconscious lady. The old gentleman, her father, was always dabbling in speculation. We have seen how the wine company and the coal company had failed him. But, prowling about the City always eagerly and restlessly still, he lighted upon some other scheme, of which he thought so well that he embarked in it in spite of the remonstrances of Mr. Clapp, to whom indeed he never dared to tell how far he had engaged himself in it. And as it was always Mr. Sedley's maxim not to talk about money matters before women, they had no inkling of the misfortunes that were in store for them until the unhappy old gentleman was forced to make gradual confessions.\n\nThe bills of the little household, which had been settled weekly, first fell into arrear. The remittances had not arrived from India, Mr. Sedley told his wife with a disturbed face. As she had paid her bills very regularly hitherto, one or two of the tradesmen to whom the poor lady was obliged to go round asking for time were very angry at a delay to which they were perfectly used from more irregular customers. Emmy's contribution, paid over cheerfully without any questions, kept the little company in half-rations however. And the first six months passed away pretty easily, old Sedley still keeping up with the notion that his shares must rise and that all would be well.\n\nNo sixty pounds, however, came to help the household at the end of the half year, and it fell deeper and deeper into trouble--Mrs. Sedley, who was growing infirm and was much shaken, remained silent or wept a great deal with Mrs. Clapp in the kitchen. The butcher was particularly surly, the grocer insolent: once or twice little Georgy had grumbled about the dinners, and Amelia, who still would have been satisfied with a slice of bread for her own dinner, could not but perceive that her son was neglected and purchased little things out of her private purse to keep the boy in health.\n\nAt last they told her, or told her such a garbled story as people in difficulties tell. One day, her own money having been received, and Amelia about to pay it over, she, who had kept an account of the moneys expended by her, proposed to keep a certain portion back out of her dividend, having contracted engagements for a new suit for Georgy.\n\nThen it came out that Jos's remittances were not paid, that the house was in difficulties, which Amelia ought to have seen before, her mother said, but she cared for nothing or nobody except Georgy. At this she passed all her money across the table, without a word, to her mother, and returned to her room to cry her eyes out. She had a great access of sensibility too that day, when obliged to go and countermand the clothes, the darling clothes on which she had set her heart for Christmas Day, and the cut and fashion of which she had arranged in many conversations with a small milliner, her friend.\n\nHardest of all, she had to break the matter to Georgy, who made a loud outcry. Everybody had new clothes at Christmas. The others would laugh at him. He would have new clothes. She had promised them to him. The poor widow had only kisses to give him. She darned the old suit in tears. She cast about among her little ornaments to see if she could sell anything to procure the desired novelties. There was her India shawl that Dobbin had sent her. She remembered in former days going with her mother to a fine India shop on Ludgate Hill, where the ladies had all sorts of dealings and bargains in these articles. Her cheeks flushed and her eyes shone with pleasure as she thought of this resource, and she kissed away George to school in the morning, smiling brightly after him. The boy felt that there was good news in her look.\n\nPacking up her shawl in a handkerchief (another of the gifts of the good Major), she hid them under her cloak and walked flushed and eager all the way to Ludgate Hill, tripping along by the park wall and running over the crossings, so that many a man turned as she hurried by him and looked after her rosy pretty face. She calculated how she should spend the proceeds of her shawl--how, besides the clothes, she would buy the books that he longed for, and pay his half-year's schooling; and how she would buy a cloak for her father instead of that old great-coat which he wore. She was not mistaken as to the value of the Major's gift. It was a very fine and beautiful web, and the merchant made a very good bargain when he gave her twenty guineas for her shawl.\n\nShe ran on amazed and flurried with her riches to Darton's shop, in St. Paul's Churchyard, and there purchased the Parents' Assistant and the Sandford and Merton Georgy longed for, and got into the coach there with her parcel, and went home exulting. And she pleased herself by writing in the fly-leaf in her neatest little hand, \"George Osborne, A Christmas gift from his affectionate mother.\" The books are extant to this day, with the fair delicate superscription.\n\nShe was going from her own room with the books in her hand to place them on George's table, where he might find them on his return from school, when in the passage, she and her mother met. The gilt bindings of the seven handsome little volumes caught the old lady's eye.\n\n\"What are those?\" she said.\n\n\"Some books for Georgy,\" Amelia replied--\"I--I promised them to him at Christmas.\"\n\n\"Books!\" cried the elder lady indignantly, \"Books, when the whole house wants bread! Books, when to keep you and your son in luxury, and your dear father out of gaol, I've sold every trinket I had, the India shawl from my back even down to the very spoons, that our tradesmen mightn't insult us, and that Mr. Clapp, which indeed he is justly entitled, being not a hard landlord, and a civil man, and a father, might have his rent. Oh, Amelia! you break my heart with your books and that boy of yours, whom you are ruining, though part with him you will not. Oh, Amelia, may God send you a more dutiful child than I have had! There's Jos, deserts his father in his old age; and there's George, who might be provided for, and who might be rich, going to school like a lord, with a gold watch and chain round his neck--while my dear, dear old man is without a sh--shilling.\" Hysteric sobs and cries ended Mrs. Sedley's speech--it echoed through every room in the small house, whereof the other female inmates heard every word of the colloquy.\n\n\"Oh, Mother, Mother!\" cried poor Amelia in reply. \"You told me nothing--I--I promised him the books. I--I only sold my shawl this morning. Take the money--take everything\"--and with quivering hands she took out her silver, and her sovereigns--her precious golden sovereigns, which she thrust into the hands of her mother, whence they overflowed and tumbled, rolling down the stairs.\n\nAnd then she went into her room, and sank down in despair and utter misery. She saw it all now. Her selfishness was sacrificing the boy. But for her he might have wealth, station, education, and his father's place, which the elder George had forfeited for her sake. She had but to speak the words, and her father was restored to competency and the boy raised to fortune. Oh, what a conviction it was to that tender and stricken heart!\n\nGaunt House\nAll the world knows that Lord Steyne's town palace stands in Gaunt Square, out of which Great Gaunt Street leads, whither we first conducted Rebecca, in the time of the departed Sir Pitt Crawley. Peering over the railings and through the black trees into the garden of the Square, you see a few miserable governesses with wan-faced pupils wandering round and round it, and round the dreary grass-plot in the centre of which rises the statue of Lord Gaunt, who fought at Minden, in a three-tailed wig, and otherwise habited like a Roman Emperor. Gaunt House occupies nearly a side of the Square. The remaining three sides are composed of mansions that have passed away into dowagerism--tall, dark houses, with window-frames of stone, or picked out of a lighter red. Little light seems to be behind those lean, comfortless casements now, and hospitality to have passed away from those doors as much as the laced lacqueys and link-boys of old times, who used to put out their torches in the blank iron extinguishers that still flank the lamps over the steps. Brass plates have penetrated into the square--Doctors, the Diddlesex Bank Western Branch--the English and European Reunion, &c.--it has a dreary look--nor is my Lord Steyne's palace less dreary. All I have ever seen of it is the vast wall in front, with the rustic columns at the great gate, through which an old porter peers sometimes with a fat and gloomy red face--and over the wall the garret and bedroom windows, and the chimneys, out of which there seldom comes any smoke now. For the present Lord Steyne lives at Naples, preferring the view of the Bay and Capri and Vesuvius to the dreary aspect of the wall in Gaunt Square.\n\nA few score yards down New Gaunt Street, and leading into Gaunt Mews indeed, is a little modest back door, which you would not remark from that of any of the other stables. But many a little close carriage has stopped at that door, as my informant (little Tom Eaves, who knows everything, and who showed me the place) told me. \"The Prince and Perdita have been in and out of that door, sir,\" he had often told me; \"Marianne Clarke has entered it with the Duke of ------. It conducts to the famous petits appartements of Lord Steyne--one, sir, fitted up all in ivory and white satin, another in ebony and black velvet; there is a little banqueting-room taken from Sallust's house at Pompeii, and painted by Cosway--a little private kitchen, in which every saucepan was silver and all the spits were gold. It was there that Egalite Orleans roasted partridges on the night when he and the Marquis of Steyne won a hundred thousand from a great personage at ombre. Half of the money went to the French Revolution, half to purchase Lord Gaunt's Marquisate and Garter--and the remainder--\" but it forms no part of our scheme to tell what became of the remainder, for every shilling of which, and a great deal more, little Tom Eaves, who knows everybody's affairs, is ready to account.\n\nBesides his town palace, the Marquis had castles and palaces in various quarters of the three kingdoms, whereof the descriptions may be found in the road-books--Castle Strongbow, with its woods, on the Shannon shore; Gaunt Castle, in Carmarthenshire, where Richard II was taken prisoner--Gauntly Hall in Yorkshire, where I have been informed there were two hundred silver teapots for the breakfasts of the guests of the house, with everything to correspond in splendour; and Stillbrook in Hampshire, which was my lord's farm, an humble place of residence, of which we all remember the wonderful furniture which was sold at my lord's demise by a late celebrated auctioneer.\n\nThe Marchioness of Steyne was of the renowned and ancient family of the Caerlyons, Marquises of Camelot, who have preserved the old faith ever since the conversion of the venerable Druid, their first ancestor, and whose pedigree goes far beyond the date of the arrival of King Brute in these islands. Pendragon is the title of the eldest son of the house. The sons have been called Arthurs, Uthers, and Caradocs, from immemorial time. Their heads have fallen in many a loyal conspiracy. Elizabeth chopped off the head of the Arthur of her day, who had been Chamberlain to Philip and Mary, and carried letters between the Queen of Scots and her uncles the Guises. A cadet of the house was an officer of the great Duke and distinguished in the famous Saint Bartholomew conspiracy. During the whole of Mary's confinement, the house of Camelot conspired in her behalf. It was as much injured by its charges in fitting out an armament against the Spaniards, during the time of the Armada, as by the fines and confiscations levied on it by Elizabeth for harbouring of priests, obstinate recusancy, and popish misdoings. A recreant of James's time was momentarily perverted from his religion by the arguments of that great theologian, and the fortunes of the family somewhat restored by his timely weakness. But the Earl of Camelot, of the reign of Charles, returned to the old creed of his family, and they continued to fight for it, and ruin themselves for it, as long as there was a Stuart left to head or to instigate a rebellion.\n\nLady Mary Caerlyon was brought up at a Parisian convent; the Dauphiness Marie Antoinette was her godmother. In the pride of her beauty she had been married--sold, it was said--to Lord Gaunt, then at Paris, who won vast sums from the lady's brother at some of Philip of Orleans's banquets. The Earl of Gaunt's famous duel with the Count de la Marche, of the Grey Musqueteers, was attributed by common report to the pretensions of that officer (who had been a page, and remained a favourite of the Queen) to the hand of the beautiful Lady Mary Caerlyon. She was married to Lord Gaunt while the Count lay ill of his wound, and came to dwell at Gaunt House, and to figure for a short time in the splendid Court of the Prince of Wales. Fox had toasted her. Morris and Sheridan had written songs about her. Malmesbury had made her his best bow; Walpole had pronounced her charming; Devonshire had been almost jealous of her; but she was scared by the wild pleasures and gaieties of the society into which she was flung, and after she had borne a couple of sons, shrank away into a life of devout seclusion. No wonder that my Lord Steyne, who liked pleasure and cheerfulness, was not often seen after their marriage by the side of this trembling, silent, superstitious, unhappy lady.\n\nThe before-mentioned Tom Eaves (who has no part in this history, except that he knew all the great folks in London, and the stories and mysteries of each family) had further information regarding my Lady Steyne, which may or may not be true. \"The humiliations,\" Tom used to say, \"which that woman has been made to undergo, in her own house, have been frightful; Lord Steyne has made her sit down to table with women with whom I would rather die than allow Mrs. Eaves to associate--with Lady Crackenbury, with Mrs. Chippenham, with Madame de la Cruchecassee, the French secretary's wife (from every one of which ladies Tom Eaves--who would have sacrificed his wife for knowing them--was too glad to get a bow or a dinner) with the REIGNING FAVOURITE in a word. And do you suppose that that woman, of that family, who are as proud as the Bourbons, and to whom the Steynes are but lackeys, mushrooms of yesterday (for after all, they are not of the Old Gaunts, but of a minor and doubtful branch of the house); do you suppose, I say (the reader must bear in mind that it is always Tom Eaves who speaks) that the Marchioness of Steyne, the haughtiest woman in England, would bend down to her husband so submissively if there were not some cause? Pooh! I tell you there are secret reasons. I tell you that, in the emigration, the Abbe de la Marche who was here and was employed in the Quiberoon business with Puisaye and Tinteniac, was the same Colonel of Mousquetaires Gris with whom Steyne fought in the year '86--that he and the Marchioness met again--that it was after the Reverend Colonel was shot in Brittany that Lady Steyne took to those extreme practices of devotion which she carries on now; for she is closeted with her director every day--she is at service at Spanish Place, every morning, I've watched her there--that is, I've happened to be passing there--and depend on it, there's a mystery in her case. People are not so unhappy unless they have something to repent of,\" added Tom Eaves with a knowing wag of his head; \"and depend on it, that woman would not be so submissive as she is if the Marquis had not some sword to hold over her.\"\n\nSo, if Mr. Eaves's information be correct, it is very likely that this lady, in her high station, had to submit to many a private indignity and to hide many secret griefs under a calm face. And let us, my brethren who have not our names in the Red Book, console ourselves by thinking comfortably how miserable our betters may be, and that Damocles, who sits on satin cushions and is served on gold plate, has an awful sword hanging over his head in the shape of a bailiff, or an hereditary disease, or a family secret, which peeps out every now and then from the embroidered arras in a ghastly manner, and will be sure to drop one day or the other in the right place.\n\nIn comparing, too, the poor man's situation with that of the great, there is (always according to Mr. Eaves) another source of comfort for the former. You who have little or no patrimony to bequeath or to inherit, may be on good terms with your father or your son, whereas the heir of a great prince, such as my Lord Steyne, must naturally be angry at being kept out of his kingdom, and eye the occupant of it with no very agreeable glances. \"Take it as a rule,\" this sardonic old Eaves would say, \"the fathers and elder sons of all great families hate each other. The Crown Prince is always in opposition to the crown or hankering after it. Shakespeare knew the world, my good sir, and when he describes Prince Hal (from whose family the Gaunts pretend to be descended, though they are no more related to John of Gaunt than you are) trying on his father's coronet, he gives you a natural description of all heirs apparent. If you were heir to a dukedom and a thousand pounds a day, do you mean to say you would not wish for possession? Pooh! And it stands to reason that every great man, having experienced this feeling towards his father, must be aware that his son entertains it towards himself; and so they can't but be suspicious and hostile.\n\n\"Then again, as to the feeling of elder towards younger sons. My dear sir, you ought to know that every elder brother looks upon the cadets of the house as his natural enemies, who deprive him of so much ready money which ought to be his by right. I have often heard George Mac Turk, Lord Bajazet's eldest son, say that if he had his will when he came to the title, he would do what the sultans do, and clear the estate by chopping off all his younger brothers' heads at once; and so the case is, more or less, with them all. I tell you they are all Turks in their hearts. Pooh! sir, they know the world.\" And here, haply, a great man coming up, Tom Eaves's hat would drop off his head, and he would rush forward with a bow and a grin, which showed that he knew the world too--in the Tomeavesian way, that is. And having laid out every shilling of his fortune on an annuity, Tom could afford to bear no malice to his nephews and nieces, and to have no other feeling with regard to his betters but a constant and generous desire to dine with them.\n\nBetween the Marchioness and the natural and tender regard of mother for children, there was that cruel barrier placed of difference of faith. The very love which she might feel for her sons only served to render the timid and pious lady more fearful and unhappy. The gulf which separated them was fatal and impassable. She could not stretch her weak arms across it, or draw her children over to that side away from which her belief told her there was no safety. During the youth of his sons, Lord Steyne, who was a good scholar and amateur casuist, had no better sport in the evening after dinner in the country than in setting the boys' tutor, the Reverend Mr. Trail (now my Lord Bishop of Ealing) on her ladyship's director, Father Mole, over their wine, and in pitting Oxford against St. Acheul. He cried \"Bravo, Latimer! Well said, Loyola!\" alternately; he promised Mole a bishopric if he would come over, and vowed he would use all his influence to get Trail a cardinal's hat if he would secede. Neither divine allowed himself to be conquered, and though the fond mother hoped that her youngest and favourite son would be reconciled to her church--his mother church--a sad and awful disappointment awaited the devout lady--a disappointment which seemed to be a judgement upon her for the sin of her marriage.\n\nMy Lord Gaunt married, as every person who frequents the Peerage knows, the Lady Blanche Thistlewood, a daughter of the noble house of Bareacres, before mentioned in this veracious history. A wing of Gaunt House was assigned to this couple; for the head of the family chose to govern it, and while he reigned to reign supreme; his son and heir, however, living little at home, disagreeing with his wife, and borrowing upon post-obits such moneys as he required beyond the very moderate sums which his father was disposed to allow him. The Marquis knew every shilling of his son's debts. At his lamented demise, he was found himself to be possessor of many of his heir's bonds, purchased for their benefit, and devised by his Lordship to the children of his younger son.\n\nAs, to my Lord Gaunt's dismay, and the chuckling delight of his natural enemy and father, the Lady Gaunt had no children--the Lord George Gaunt was desired to return from Vienna, where he was engaged in waltzing and diplomacy, and to contract a matrimonial alliance with the Honourable Joan, only daughter of John Johnes, First Baron Helvellyn, and head of the firm of Jones, Brown, and Robinson, of Threadneedle Street, Bankers; from which union sprang several sons and daughters, whose doings do not appertain to this story.\n\nThe marriage at first was a happy and prosperous one. My Lord George Gaunt could not only read, but write pretty correctly. He spoke French with considerable fluency; and was one of the finest waltzers in Europe. With these talents, and his interest at home, there was little doubt that his lordship would rise to the highest dignities in his profession. The lady, his wife, felt that courts were her sphere, and her wealth enabled her to receive splendidly in those continental towns whither her husband's diplomatic duties led him. There was talk of appointing him minister, and bets were laid at the Travellers' that he would be ambassador ere long, when of a sudden, rumours arrived of the secretary's extraordinary behaviour. At a grand diplomatic dinner given by his chief, he had started up and declared that a pate de foie gras was poisoned. He went to a ball at the hotel of the Bavarian envoy, the Count de Springbock-Hohenlaufen, with his head shaved and dressed as a Capuchin friar. It was not a masked ball, as some folks wanted to persuade you. It was something queer, people whispered. His grandfather was so. It was in the family.\n\nHis wife and family returned to this country and took up their abode at Gaunt House. Lord George gave up his post on the European continent, and was gazetted to Brazil. But people knew better; he never returned from that Brazil expedition--never died there--never lived there--never was there at all. He was nowhere; he was gone out altogether. \"Brazil,\" said one gossip to another, with a grin--\"Brazil is St. John's Wood. Rio de Janeiro is a cottage surrounded by four walls, and George Gaunt is accredited to a keeper, who has invested him with the order of the Strait-Waistcoat.\" These are the kinds of epitaphs which men pass over one another in Vanity Fair.\n\nTwice or thrice in a week, in the earliest morning, the poor mother went for her sins and saw the poor invalid. Sometimes he laughed at her (and his laughter was more pitiful than to hear him cry); sometimes she found the brilliant dandy diplomatist of the Congress of Vienna dragging about a child's toy, or nursing the keeper's baby's doll. Sometimes he knew her and Father Mole, her director and companion; oftener he forgot her, as he had done wife, children, love, ambition, vanity. But he remembered his dinner-hour, and used to cry if his wine-and-water was not strong enough.\n\nIt was the mysterious taint of the blood; the poor mother had brought it from her own ancient race. The evil had broken out once or twice in the father's family, long before Lady Steyne's sins had begun, or her fasts and tears and penances had been offered in their expiation. The pride of the race was struck down as the first-born of Pharaoh. The dark mark of fate and doom was on the threshold--the tall old threshold surmounted by coronets and caned heraldry.\n\nThe absent lord's children meanwhile prattled and grew on quite unconscious that the doom was over them too. First they talked of their father and devised plans against his return. Then the name of the living dead man was less frequently in their mouth--then not mentioned at all. But the stricken old grandmother trembled to think that these too were the inheritors of their father's shame as well as of his honours, and watched sickening for the day when the awful ancestral curse should come down on them.\n\nThis dark presentiment also haunted Lord Steyne. He tried to lay the horrid bedside ghost in Red Seas of wine and jollity, and lost sight of it sometimes in the crowd and rout of his pleasures. But it always came back to him when alone, and seemed to grow more threatening with years. \"I have taken your son,\" it said, \"why not you? I may shut you up in a prison some day like your son George. I may tap you on the head to-morrow, and away go pleasure and honours, feasts and beauty, friends, flatterers, French cooks, fine horses and houses--in exchange for a prison, a keeper, and a straw mattress like George Gaunt's.\" And then my lord would defy the ghost which threatened him, for he knew of a remedy by which he could baulk his enemy.\n\nSo there was splendour and wealth, but no great happiness perchance, behind the tall caned portals of Gaunt House with its smoky coronets and ciphers. The feasts there were of the grandest in London, but there was not overmuch content therewith, except among the guests who sat at my lord's table. Had he not been so great a Prince very few possibly would have visited him; but in Vanity Fair the sins of very great personages are looked at indulgently. \"Nous regardons a deux fois\" (as the French lady said) before we condemn a person of my lord's undoubted quality. Some notorious carpers and squeamish moralists might be sulky with Lord Steyne, but they were glad enough to come when he asked them.\n\n\"Lord Steyne is really too bad,\" Lady Slingstone said, \"but everybody goes, and of course I shall see that my girls come to no harm.\" \"His lordship is a man to whom I owe much, everything in life,\" said the Right Reverend Doctor Trail, thinking that the Archbishop was rather shaky, and Mrs. Trail and the young ladies would as soon have missed going to church as to one of his lordship's parties. \"His morals are bad,\" said little Lord Southdown to his sister, who meekly expostulated, having heard terrific legends from her mamma with respect to the doings at Gaunt House; \"but hang it, he's got the best dry Sillery in Europe!\" And as for Sir Pitt Crawley, Bart.--Sir Pitt that pattern of decorum, Sir Pitt who had led off at missionary meetings--he never for one moment thought of not going too. \"Where you see such persons as the Bishop of Ealing and the Countess of Slingstone, you may be pretty sure, Jane,\" the Baronet would say, \"that we cannot be wrong. The great rank and station of Lord Steyne put him in a position to command people in our station in life. The Lord Lieutenant of a County, my dear, is a respectable man. Besides, George Gaunt and I were intimate in early life; he was my junior when we were attaches at Pumpernickel together.\"\n\nIn a word everybody went to wait upon this great man--everybody who was asked, as you the reader (do not say nay) or I the writer hereof would go if we had an invitation.\n\n# CHAPTER XLVIII\n\nIn Which the Reader Is Introduced to the Very Best of Company\nAt last Becky's kindness and attention to the chief of her husband's family were destined to meet with an exceeding great reward, a reward which, though certainly somewhat unsubstantial, the little woman coveted with greater eagerness than more positive benefits. If she did not wish to lead a virtuous life, at least she desired to enjoy a character for virtue, and we know that no lady in the genteel world can possess this desideratum, until she has put on a train and feathers and has been presented to her Sovereign at Court. From that august interview they come out stamped as honest women. The Lord Chamberlain gives them a certificate of virtue. And as dubious goods or letters are passed through an oven at quarantine, sprinkled with aromatic vinegar, and then pronounced clean, many a lady, whose reputation would be doubtful otherwise and liable to give infection, passes through the wholesome ordeal of the Royal presence and issues from it free from all taint.\n\nIt might be very well for my Lady Bareacres, my Lady Tufto, Mrs. Bute Crawley in the country, and other ladies who had come into contact with Mrs. Rawdon Crawley to cry fie at the idea of the odious little adventuress making her curtsey before the Sovereign, and to declare that, if dear good Queen Charlotte had been alive, she never would have admitted such an extremely ill-regulated personage into her chaste drawing-room. But when we consider that it was the First Gentleman in Europe in whose high presence Mrs. Rawdon passed her examination, and as it were, took her degree in reputation, it surely must be flat disloyalty to doubt any more about her virtue. I, for my part, look back with love and awe to that Great Character in history. Ah, what a high and noble appreciation of Gentlewomanhood there must have been in Vanity Fair, when that revered and august being was invested, by the universal acclaim of the refined and educated portion of this empire, with the title of Premier Gentilhomme of his Kingdom. Do you remember, dear M--, oh friend of my youth, how one blissful night five-and-twenty years since, the \"Hypocrite\" being acted, Elliston being manager, Dowton and Liston performers, two boys had leave from their loyal masters to go out from Slaughter-House School where they were educated and to appear on Drury Lane stage, amongst a crowd which assembled there to greet the king. THE KING? There he was. Beefeaters were before the august box; the Marquis of Steyne (Lord of the Powder Closet) and other great officers of state were behind the chair on which he sat, HE sat--florid of face, portly of person, covered with orders, and in a rich curling head of hair--how we sang God save him! How the house rocked and shouted with that magnificent music. How they cheered, and cried, and waved handkerchiefs. Ladies wept; mothers clasped their children; some fainted with emotion. People were suffocated in the pit, shrieks and groans rising up amidst the writhing and shouting mass there of his people who were, and indeed showed themselves almost to be, ready to die for him. Yes, we saw him. Fate cannot deprive us of THAT. Others have seen Napoleon. Some few still exist who have beheld Frederick the Great, Doctor Johnson, Marie Antoinette, &c.--be it our reasonable boast to our children, that we saw George the Good, the Magnificent, the Great.\n\nWell, there came a happy day in Mrs. Rawdon Crawley's existence when this angel was admitted into the paradise of a Court which she coveted, her sister-in-law acting as her godmother. On the appointed day, Sir Pitt and his lady, in their great family carriage (just newly built, and ready for the Baronet's assumption of the office of High Sheriff of his county), drove up to the little house in Curzon Street, to the edification of Raggles, who was watching from his greengrocer's shop, and saw fine plumes within, and enormous bunches of flowers in the breasts of the new livery-coats of the footmen.\n\nSir Pitt, in a glittering uniform, descended and went into Curzon Street, his sword between his legs. Little Rawdon stood with his face against the parlour window-panes, smiling and nodding with all his might to his aunt in the carriage within; and presently Sir Pitt issued forth from the house again, leading forth a lady with grand feathers, covered in a white shawl, and holding up daintily a train of magnificent brocade. She stepped into the vehicle as if she were a princess and accustomed all her life to go to Court, smiling graciously on the footman at the door and on Sir Pitt, who followed her into the carriage.\n\nThen Rawdon followed in his old Guards' uniform, which had grown woefully shabby, and was much too tight. He was to have followed the procession and waited upon his sovereign in a cab, but that his good-natured sister-in-law insisted that they should be a family party. The coach was large, the ladies not very big, they would hold their trains in their laps--finally, the four went fraternally together, and their carriage presently joined the line of royal equipages which was making its way down Piccadilly and St. James's Street, towards the old brick palace where the Star of Brunswick was in waiting to receive his nobles and gentlefolks.\n\nBecky felt as if she could bless the people out of the carriage windows, so elated was she in spirit, and so strong a sense had she of the dignified position which she had at last attained in life. Even our Becky had her weaknesses, and as one often sees how men pride themselves upon excellences which others are slow to perceive: how, for instance, Comus firmly believes that he is the greatest tragic actor in England; how Brown, the famous novelist, longs to be considered, not a man of genius, but a man of fashion; while Robinson, the great lawyer, does not in the least care about his reputation in Westminster Hall, but believes himself incomparable across country and at a five-barred gate--so to be, and to be thought, a respectable woman was Becky's aim in life, and she got up the genteel with amazing assiduity, readiness, and success. We have said, there were times when she believed herself to be a fine lady and forgot that there was no money in the chest at home--duns round the gate, tradesmen to coax and wheedle--no ground to walk upon, in a word. And as she went to Court in the carriage, the family carriage, she adopted a demeanour so grand, self-satisfied, deliberate, and imposing that it made even Lady Jane laugh. She walked into the royal apartments with a toss of the head which would have befitted an empress, and I have no doubt had she been one, she would have become the character perfectly.\n\nWe are authorized to state that Mrs. Rawdon Crawley's costume de cour on the occasion of her presentation to the Sovereign was of the most elegant and brilliant description. Some ladies we may have seen--we who wear stars and cordons and attend the St. James's assemblies, or we, who, in muddy boots, dawdle up and down Pall Mall and peep into the coaches as they drive up with the great folks in their feathers--some ladies of fashion, I say, we may have seen, about two o'clock of the forenoon of a levee day, as the laced-jacketed band of the Life Guards are blowing triumphal marches seated on those prancing music-stools, their cream-coloured chargers--who are by no means lovely and enticing objects at that early period of noon. A stout countess of sixty, decolletee, painted, wrinkled with rouge up to her drooping eyelids, and diamonds twinkling in her wig, is a wholesome and edifying, but not a pleasant sight. She has the faded look of a St. James's Street illumination, as it may be seen of an early morning, when half the lamps are out, and the others are blinking wanly, as if they were about to vanish like ghosts before the dawn. Such charms as those of which we catch glimpses while her ladyship's carriage passes should appear abroad at night alone. If even Cynthia looks haggard of an afternoon, as we may see her sometimes in the present winter season, with Phoebus staring her out of countenance from the opposite side of the heavens, how much more can old Lady Castlemouldy keep her head up when the sun is shining full upon it through the chariot windows, and showing all the chinks and crannies with which time has marked her face! No. Drawing-rooms should be announced for November, or the first foggy day, or the elderly sultanas of our Vanity Fair should drive up in closed litters, descend in a covered way, and make their curtsey to the Sovereign under the protection of lamplight.\n\nOur beloved Rebecca had no need, however, of any such a friendly halo to set off her beauty. Her complexion could bear any sunshine as yet, and her dress, though if you were to see it now, any present lady of Vanity Fair would pronounce it to be the most foolish and preposterous attire ever worn, was as handsome in her eyes and those of the public, some five-and-twenty years since, as the most brilliant costume of the most famous beauty of the present season. A score of years hence that too, that milliner's wonder, will have passed into the domain of the absurd, along with all previous vanities. But we are wandering too much. Mrs. Rawdon's dress was pronounced to be charmante on the eventful day of her presentation. Even good little Lady Jane was forced to acknowledge this effect, as she looked at her kinswoman, and owned sorrowfully to herself that she was quite inferior in taste to Mrs. Becky.\n\nShe did not know how much care, thought, and genius Mrs. Rawdon had bestowed upon that garment. Rebecca had as good taste as any milliner in Europe, and such a clever way of doing things as Lady Jane little understood. The latter quickly spied out the magnificence of the brocade of Becky's train, and the splendour of the lace on her dress.\n\nThe brocade was an old remnant, Becky said; and as for the lace, it was a great bargain. She had had it these hundred years.\n\n\"My dear Mrs. Crawley, it must have cost a little fortune,\" Lady Jane said, looking down at her own lace, which was not nearly so good; and then examining the quality of the ancient brocade which formed the material of Mrs. Rawdon's Court dress, she felt inclined to say that she could not afford such fine clothing, but checked that speech, with an effort, as one uncharitable to her kinswoman.\n\nAnd yet, if Lady Jane had known all, I think even her kindly temper would have failed her. The fact is, when she was putting Sir Pitt's house in order, Mrs. Rawdon had found the lace and the brocade in old wardrobes, the property of the former ladies of the house, and had quietly carried the goods home, and had suited them to her own little person. Briggs saw her take them, asked no questions, told no stories; but I believe quite sympathised with her on this matter, and so would many another honest woman.\n\nAnd the diamonds--\"Where the doose did you get the diamonds, Becky?\" said her husband, admiring some jewels which he had never seen before and which sparkled in her ears and on her neck with brilliance and profusion.\n\nBecky blushed a little and looked at him hard for a moment. Pitt Crawley blushed a little too, and looked out of window. The fact is, he had given her a very small portion of the brilliants; a pretty diamond clasp, which confined a pearl necklace which she wore--and the Baronet had omitted to mention the circumstance to his lady.\n\nBecky looked at her husband, and then at Sir Pitt, with an air of saucy triumph--as much as to say, \"Shall I betray you?\"\n\n\"Guess!\" she said to her husband. \"Why, you silly man,\" she continued, \"where do you suppose I got them?--all except the little clasp, which a dear friend of mine gave me long ago. I hired them, to be sure. I hired them at Mr. Polonius's, in Coventry Street. You don't suppose that all the diamonds which go to Court belong to the wearers; like those beautiful stones which Lady Jane has, and which are much handsomer than any which I have, I am certain.\"\n\n\"They are family jewels,\" said Sir Pitt, again looking uneasy. And in this family conversation the carriage rolled down the street, until its cargo was finally discharged at the gates of the palace where the Sovereign was sitting in state.\n\nThe diamonds, which had created Rawdon's admiration, never went back to Mr. Polonius, of Coventry Street, and that gentleman never applied for their restoration, but they retired into a little private repository, in an old desk, which Amelia Sedley had given her years and years ago, and in which Becky kept a number of useful and, perhaps, valuable things, about which her husband knew nothing. To know nothing, or little, is in the nature of some husbands. To hide, in the nature of how many women? Oh, ladies! how many of you have surreptitious milliners' bills? How many of you have gowns and bracelets which you daren't show, or which you wear trembling?--trembling, and coaxing with smiles the husband by your side, who does not know the new velvet gown from the old one, or the new bracelet from last year's, or has any notion that the ragged-looking yellow lace scarf cost forty guineas and that Madame Bobinot is writing dunning letters every week for the money!\n\nThus Rawdon knew nothing about the brilliant diamond ear-rings, or the superb brilliant ornament which decorated the fair bosom of his lady; but Lord Steyne, who was in his place at Court, as Lord of the Powder Closet, and one of the great dignitaries and illustrious defences of the throne of England, and came up with all his stars, garters, collars, and cordons, and paid particular attention to the little woman, knew whence the jewels came and who paid for them.\n\nAs he bowed over her he smiled, and quoted the hackneyed and beautiful lines from The Rape of the Lock about Belinda's diamonds, \"which Jews might kiss and infidels adore.\"\n\n\"But I hope your lordship is orthodox,\" said the little lady with a toss of her head. And many ladies round about whispered and talked, and many gentlemen nodded and whispered, as they saw what marked attention the great nobleman was paying to the little adventuress.\n\nWhat were the circumstances of the interview between Rebecca Crawley, nee Sharp, and her Imperial Master, it does not become such a feeble and inexperienced pen as mine to attempt to relate. The dazzled eyes close before that Magnificent Idea. Loyal respect and decency tell even the imagination not to look too keenly and audaciously about the sacred audience-chamber, but to back away rapidly, silently, and respectfully, making profound bows out of the August Presence.\n\nThis may be said, that in all London there was no more loyal heart than Becky's after this interview. The name of her king was always on her lips, and he was proclaimed by her to be the most charming of men. She went to Colnaghi's and ordered the finest portrait of him that art had produced, and credit could supply. She chose that famous one in which the best of monarchs is represented in a frock-coat with a fur collar, and breeches and silk stockings, simpering on a sofa from under his curly brown wig. She had him painted in a brooch and wore it--indeed she amused and somewhat pestered her acquaintance with her perpetual talk about his urbanity and beauty. Who knows! Perhaps the little woman thought she might play the part of a Maintenon or a Pompadour.\n\nBut the finest sport of all after her presentation was to hear her talk virtuously. She had a few female acquaintances, not, it must be owned, of the very highest reputation in Vanity Fair. But being made an honest woman of, so to speak, Becky would not consort any longer with these dubious ones, and cut Lady Crackenbury when the latter nodded to her from her opera-box, and gave Mrs. Washington White the go-by in the Ring. \"One must, my dear, show one is somebody,\" she said. \"One mustn't be seen with doubtful people. I pity Lady Crackenbury from my heart, and Mrs. Washington White may be a very good-natured person. YOU may go and dine with them, as you like your rubber. But I mustn't, and won't; and you will have the goodness to tell Smith to say I am not at home when either of them calls.\"\n\nThe particulars of Becky's costume were in the newspapers--feathers, lappets, superb diamonds, and all the rest. Lady Crackenbury read the paragraph in bitterness of spirit and discoursed to her followers about the airs which that woman was giving herself. Mrs. Bute Crawley and her young ladies in the country had a copy of the Morning Post from town, and gave a vent to their honest indignation. \"If you had been sandy-haired, green-eyed, and a French rope-dancer's daughter,\" Mrs. Bute said to her eldest girl (who, on the contrary, was a very swarthy, short, and snub-nosed young lady), \"You might have had superb diamonds forsooth, and have been presented at Court by your cousin, the Lady Jane. But you're only a gentlewoman, my poor dear child. You have only some of the best blood in England in your veins, and good principles and piety for your portion. I, myself, the wife of a Baronet's younger brother, too, never thought of such a thing as going to Court--nor would other people, if good Queen Charlotte had been alive.\" In this way the worthy Rectoress consoled herself, and her daughters sighed and sat over the Peerage all night.\n\nA few days after the famous presentation, another great and exceeding honour was vouchsafed to the virtuous Becky. Lady Steyne's carriage drove up to Mr. Rawdon Crawley's door, and the footman, instead of driving down the front of the house, as by his tremendous knocking he appeared to be inclined to do, relented and only delivered in a couple of cards, on which were engraven the names of the Marchioness of Steyne and the Countess of Gaunt. If these bits of pasteboard had been beautiful pictures, or had had a hundred yards of Malines lace rolled round them, worth twice the number of guineas, Becky could not have regarded them with more pleasure. You may be sure they occupied a conspicuous place in the china bowl on the drawing-room table, where Becky kept the cards of her visitors. Lord! lord! how poor Mrs. Washington White's card and Lady Crackenbury's card--which our little friend had been glad enough to get a few months back, and of which the silly little creature was rather proud once--Lord! lord! I say, how soon at the appearance of these grand court cards, did those poor little neglected deuces sink down to the bottom of the pack. Steyne! Bareacres, Johnes of Helvellyn! and Caerylon of Camelot! we may be sure that Becky and Briggs looked out those august names in the Peerage, and followed the noble races up through all the ramifications of the family tree.\n\nMy Lord Steyne coming to call a couple of hours afterwards, and looking about him, and observing everything as was his wont, found his ladies' cards already ranged as the trumps of Becky's hand, and grinned, as this old cynic always did at any naive display of human weakness. Becky came down to him presently; whenever the dear girl expected his lordship, her toilette was prepared, her hair in perfect order, her mouchoirs, aprons, scarfs, little morocco slippers, and other female gimcracks arranged, and she seated in some artless and agreeable posture ready to receive him--whenever she was surprised, of course, she had to fly to her apartment to take a rapid survey of matters in the glass, and to trip down again to wait upon the great peer.\n\nShe found him grinning over the bowl. She was discovered, and she blushed a little. \"Thank you, Monseigneur,\" she said. \"You see your ladies have been here. How good of you! I couldn't come before--I was in the kitchen making a pudding.\"\n\n\"I know you were, I saw you through the area-railings as I drove up,\" replied the old gentleman.\n\n\"You see everything,\" she replied.\n\n\"A few things, but not that, my pretty lady,\" he said good-naturedly. \"You silly little fibster! I heard you in the room overhead, where I have no doubt you were putting a little rouge on--you must give some of yours to my Lady Gaunt, whose complexion is quite preposterous--and I heard the bedroom door open, and then you came downstairs.\"\n\n\"Is it a crime to try and look my best when YOU come here?\" answered Mrs. Rawdon plaintively, and she rubbed her cheek with her handkerchief as if to show there was no rouge at all, only genuine blushes and modesty in her case. About this who can tell? I know there is some rouge that won't come off on a pocket-handkerchief, and some so good that even tears will not disturb it.\n\n\"Well,\" said the old gentleman, twiddling round his wife's card, \"you are bent on becoming a fine lady. You pester my poor old life out to get you into the world. You won't be able to hold your own there, you silly little fool. You've got no money.\"\n\n\"You will get us a place,\" interposed Becky, \"as quick as possible.\"\n\n\"You've got no money, and you want to compete with those who have. You poor little earthenware pipkin, you want to swim down the stream along with the great copper kettles. All women are alike. Everybody is striving for what is not worth the having! Gad! I dined with the King yesterday, and we had neck of mutton and turnips. A dinner of herbs is better than a stalled ox very often. You will go to Gaunt House. You give an old fellow no rest until you get there. It's not half so nice as here. You'll be bored there. I am. My wife is as gay as Lady Macbeth, and my daughters as cheerful as Regan and Goneril. I daren't sleep in what they call my bedroom. The bed is like the baldaquin of St. Peter's, and the pictures frighten me. I have a little brass bed in a dressing-room, and a little hair mattress like an anchorite. I am an anchorite. Ho! ho! You'll be asked to dinner next week. And gare aux femmes, look out and hold your own! How the women will bully you!\" This was a very long speech for a man of few words like my Lord Steyne; nor was it the first which he uttered for Becky's benefit on that day.\n\nBriggs looked up from the work-table at which she was seated in the farther room and gave a deep sigh as she heard the great Marquis speak so lightly of her sex.\n\n\"If you don't turn off that abominable sheep-dog,\" said Lord Steyne, with a savage look over his shoulder at her, \"I will have her poisoned.\"\n\n\"I always give my dog dinner from my own plate,\" said Rebecca, laughing mischievously; and having enjoyed for some time the discomfiture of my lord, who hated poor Briggs for interrupting his tete-a-tete with the fair Colonel's wife, Mrs. Rawdon at length had pity upon her admirer, and calling to Briggs, praised the fineness of the weather to her and bade her to take out the child for a walk.\n\n\"I can't send her away,\" Becky said presently, after a pause, and in a very sad voice. Her eyes filled with tears as she spoke, and she turned away her head.\n\n\"You owe her her wages, I suppose?\" said the Peer.\n\n\"Worse than that,\" said Becky, still casting down her eyes; \"I have ruined her.\"\n\n\"Ruined her? Then why don't you turn her out?\" the gentleman asked.\n\n\"Men do that,\" Becky answered bitterly. \"Women are not so bad as you. Last year, when we were reduced to our last guinea, she gave us everything. She shall never leave me, until we are ruined utterly ourselves, which does not seem far off, or until I can pay her the utmost farthing.\"\n\n\"------ it, how much is it?\" said the Peer with an oath. And Becky, reflecting on the largeness of his means, mentioned not only the sum which she had borrowed from Miss Briggs, but one of nearly double the amount.\n\nThis caused the Lord Steyne to break out in another brief and energetic expression of anger, at which Rebecca held down her head the more and cried bitterly. \"I could not help it. It was my only chance. I dare not tell my husband. He would kill me if I told him what I have done. I have kept it a secret from everybody but you--and you forced it from me. Ah, what shall I do, Lord Steyne? for I am very, very unhappy!\"\n\nLord Steyne made no reply except by beating the devil's tattoo and biting his nails. At last he clapped his hat on his head and flung out of the room. Rebecca did not rise from her attitude of misery until the door slammed upon him and his carriage whirled away. Then she rose up with the queerest expression of victorious mischief glittering in her green eyes. She burst out laughing once or twice to herself, as she sat at work, and sitting down to the piano, she rattled away a triumphant voluntary on the keys, which made the people pause under her window to listen to her brilliant music.\n\nThat night, there came two notes from Gaunt House for the little woman, the one containing a card of invitation from Lord and Lady Steyne to a dinner at Gaunt House next Friday, while the other enclosed a slip of gray paper bearing Lord Steyne's signature and the address of Messrs. Jones, Brown, and Robinson, Lombard Street.\n\nRawdon heard Becky laughing in the night once or twice. It was only her delight at going to Gaunt House and facing the ladies there, she said, which amused her so. But the truth was that she was occupied with a great number of other thoughts. Should she pay off old Briggs and give her her conge? Should she astonish Raggles by settling his account? She turned over all these thoughts on her pillow, and on the next day, when Rawdon went out to pay his morning visit to the Club, Mrs. Crawley (in a modest dress with a veil on) whipped off in a hackney-coach to the City: and being landed at Messrs. Jones and Robinson's bank, presented a document there to the authority at the desk, who, in reply, asked her \"How she would take it?\"\n\nShe gently said \"she would take a hundred and fifty pounds in small notes and the remainder in one note\": and passing through St. Paul's Churchyard stopped there and bought the handsomest black silk gown for Briggs which money could buy; and which, with a kiss and the kindest speeches, she presented to the simple old spinster.\n\nThen she walked to Mr. Raggles, inquired about his children affectionately, and gave him fifty pounds on account. Then she went to the livery-man from whom she jobbed her carriages and gratified him with a similar sum. \"And I hope this will be a lesson to you, Spavin,\" she said, \"and that on the next drawing-room day my brother, Sir Pitt, will not be inconvenienced by being obliged to take four of us in his carriage to wait upon His Majesty, because my own carriage is not forthcoming.\" It appears there had been a difference on the last drawing-room day. Hence the degradation which the Colonel had almost suffered, of being obliged to enter the presence of his Sovereign in a hack cab.\n\nThese arrangements concluded, Becky paid a visit upstairs to the before-mentioned desk, which Amelia Sedley had given her years and years ago, and which contained a number of useful and valuable little things--in which private museum she placed the one note which Messrs. Jones and Robinson's cashier had given her.\n\nIn Which We Enjoy Three Courses and a Dessert\nWhen the ladies of Gaunt House were at breakfast that morning, Lord Steyne (who took his chocolate in private and seldom disturbed the females of his household, or saw them except upon public days, or when they crossed each other in the hall, or when from his pit-box at the opera he surveyed them in their box on the grand tier) his lordship, we say, appeared among the ladies and the children who were assembled over the tea and toast, and a battle royal ensued apropos of Rebecca.\n\n\"My Lady Steyne,\" he said, \"I want to see the list for your dinner on Friday; and I want you, if you please, to write a card for Colonel and Mrs. Crawley.\"\n\n\"Blanche writes them,\" Lady Steyne said in a flutter. \"Lady Gaunt writes them.\"\n\n\"I will not write to that person,\" Lady Gaunt said, a tall and stately lady, who looked up for an instant and then down again after she had spoken. It was not good to meet Lord Steyne's eyes for those who had offended him.\n\n\"Send the children out of the room. Go!\" said he pulling at the bell-rope. The urchins, always frightened before him, retired: their mother would have followed too. \"Not you,\" he said. \"You stop.\"\n\n\"My Lady Steyne,\" he said, \"once more will you have the goodness to go to the desk and write that card for your dinner on Friday?\"\n\n\"My Lord, I will not be present at it,\" Lady Gaunt said; \"I will go home.\"\n\n\"I wish you would, and stay there. You will find the bailiffs at Bareacres very pleasant company, and I shall be freed from lending money to your relations and from your own damned tragedy airs. Who are you to give orders here? You have no money. You've got no brains. You were here to have children, and you have not had any. Gaunt's tired of you, and George's wife is the only person in the family who doesn't wish you were dead. Gaunt would marry again if you were.\"\n\n\"I wish I were,\" her Ladyship answered with tears and rage in her eyes.\n\n\"You, forsooth, must give yourself airs of virtue, while my wife, who is an immaculate saint, as everybody knows, and never did wrong in her life, has no objection to meet my young friend Mrs. Crawley. My Lady Steyne knows that appearances are sometimes against the best of women; that lies are often told about the most innocent of them. Pray, madam, shall I tell you some little anecdotes about my Lady Bareacres, your mamma?\"\n\n\"You may strike me if you like, sir, or hit any cruel blow,\" Lady Gaunt said. To see his wife and daughter suffering always put his Lordship into a good humour.\n\n\"My sweet Blanche,\" he said, \"I am a gentleman, and never lay my hand upon a woman, save in the way of kindness. I only wish to correct little faults in your character. You women are too proud, and sadly lack humility, as Father Mole, I'm sure, would tell my Lady Steyne if he were here. You mustn't give yourselves airs; you must be meek and humble, my blessings. For all Lady Steyne knows, this calumniated, simple, good-humoured Mrs. Crawley is quite innocent--even more innocent than herself. Her husband's character is not good, but it is as good as Bareacres', who has played a little and not paid a great deal, who cheated you out of the only legacy you ever had and left you a pauper on my hands. And Mrs. Crawley is not very well-born, but she is not worse than Fanny's illustrious ancestor, the first de la Jones.\"\n\n\"The money which I brought into the family, sir,\" Lady George cried out--\n\n\"You purchased a contingent reversion with it,\" the Marquis said darkly. \"If Gaunt dies, your husband may come to his honours; your little boys may inherit them, and who knows what besides? In the meanwhile, ladies, be as proud and virtuous as you like abroad, but don't give ME any airs. As for Mrs. Crawley's character, I shan't demean myself or that most spotless and perfectly irreproachable lady by even hinting that it requires a defence. You will be pleased to receive her with the utmost cordiality, as you will receive all persons whom I present in this house. This house?\" He broke out with a laugh. \"Who is the master of it? and what is it? This Temple of Virtue belongs to me. And if I invite all Newgate or all Bedlam here, by ------ they shall be welcome.\"\n\nAfter this vigorous allocution, to one of which sort Lord Steyne treated his \"Hareem\" whenever symptoms of insubordination appeared in his household, the crestfallen women had nothing for it but to obey. Lady Gaunt wrote the invitation which his Lordship required, and she and her mother-in-law drove in person, and with bitter and humiliated hearts, to leave the cards on Mrs. Rawdon, the reception of which caused that innocent woman so much pleasure.\n\nThere were families in London who would have sacrificed a year's income to receive such an honour at the hands of those great ladies. Mrs. Frederick Bullock, for instance, would have gone on her knees from May Fair to Lombard Street, if Lady Steyne and Lady Gaunt had been waiting in the City to raise her up and say, \"Come to us next Friday\"--not to one of the great crushes and grand balls of Gaunt House, whither everybody went, but to the sacred, unapproachable, mysterious, delicious entertainments, to be admitted to one of which was a privilege, and an honour, and a blessing indeed.\n\nSevere, spotless, and beautiful, Lady Gaunt held the very highest rank in Vanity Fair. The distinguished courtesy with which Lord Steyne treated her charmed everybody who witnessed his behaviour, caused the severest critics to admit how perfect a gentleman he was, and to own that his Lordship's heart at least was in the right place.\n\nThe ladies of Gaunt House called Lady Bareacres in to their aid, in order to repulse the common enemy. One of Lady Gaunt's carriages went to Hill Street for her Ladyship's mother, all whose equipages were in the hands of the bailiffs, whose very jewels and wardrobe, it was said, had been seized by those inexorable Israelites. Bareacres Castle was theirs, too, with all its costly pictures, furniture, and articles of vertu--the magnificent Vandykes; the noble Reynolds pictures; the Lawrence portraits, tawdry and beautiful, and, thirty years ago, deemed as precious as works of real genius; the matchless Dancing Nymph of Canova, for which Lady Bareacres had sat in her youth--Lady Bareacres splendid then, and radiant in wealth, rank, and beauty--a toothless, bald, old woman now--a mere rag of a former robe of state. Her lord, painted at the same time by Lawrence, as waving his sabre in front of Bareacres Castle, and clothed in his uniform as Colonel of the Thistlewood Yeomanry, was a withered, old, lean man in a greatcoat and a Brutus wig, slinking about Gray's Inn of mornings chiefly and dining alone at clubs. He did not like to dine with Steyne now. They had run races of pleasure together in youth when Bareacres was the winner. But Steyne had more bottom than he and had lasted him out. The Marquis was ten times a greater man now than the young Lord Gaunt of '85, and Bareacres nowhere in the race--old, beaten, bankrupt, and broken down. He had borrowed too much money of Steyne to find it pleasant to meet his old comrade often. The latter, whenever he wished to be merry, used jeeringly to ask Lady Gaunt why her father had not come to see her. \"He has not been here for four months,\" Lord Steyne would say. \"I can always tell by my cheque-book afterwards, when I get a visit from Bareacres. What a comfort it is, my ladies, I bank with one of my sons' fathers-in-law, and the other banks with me!\"\n\nOf the other illustrious persons whom Becky had the honour to encounter on this her first presentation to the grand world, it does not become the present historian to say much. There was his Excellency the Prince of Peterwaradin, with his Princess--a nobleman tightly girthed, with a large military chest, on which the plaque of his order shone magnificently, and wearing the red collar of the Golden Fleece round his neck. He was the owner of countless flocks. \"Look at his face. I think he must be descended from a sheep,\" Becky whispered to Lord Steyne. Indeed, his Excellency's countenance, long, solemn, and white, with the ornament round his neck, bore some resemblance to that of a venerable bell-wether.\n\nThere was Mr. John Paul Jefferson Jones, titularly attached to the American Embassy and correspondent of the New York Demagogue, who, by way of making himself agreeable to the company, asked Lady Steyne, during a pause in the conversation at dinner, how his dear friend, George Gaunt, liked the Brazils? He and George had been most intimate at Naples and had gone up Vesuvius together. Mr. Jones wrote a full and particular account of the dinner, which appeared duly in the Demagogue. He mentioned the names and titles of all the guests, giving biographical sketches of the principal people. He described the persons of the ladies with great eloquence; the service of the table; the size and costume of the servants; enumerated the dishes and wines served; the ornaments of the sideboard; and the probable value of the plate. Such a dinner he calculated could not be dished up under fifteen or eighteen dollars per head. And he was in the habit, until very lately, of sending over proteges, with letters of recommendation to the present Marquis of Steyne, encouraged to do so by the intimate terms on which he had lived with his dear friend, the late lord. He was most indignant that a young and insignificant aristocrat, the Earl of Southdown, should have taken the pas of him in their procession to the dining-room. \"Just as I was stepping up to offer my hand to a very pleasing and witty fashionable, the brilliant and exclusive Mrs. Rawdon Crawley,\"--he wrote--\"the young patrician interposed between me and the lady and whisked my Helen off without a word of apology. I was fain to bring up the rear with the Colonel, the lady's husband, a stout red-faced warrior who distinguished himself at Waterloo, where he had better luck than befell some of his brother redcoats at New Orleans.\"\n\nThe Colonel's countenance on coming into this polite society wore as many blushes as the face of a boy of sixteen assumes when he is confronted with his sister's schoolfellows. It has been told before that honest Rawdon had not been much used at any period of his life to ladies' company. With the men at the Club or the mess room, he was well enough; and could ride, bet, smoke, or play at billiards with the boldest of them. He had had his time for female friendships too, but that was twenty years ago, and the ladies were of the rank of those with whom Young Marlow in the comedy is represented as having been familiar before he became abashed in the presence of Miss Hardcastle. The times are such that one scarcely dares to allude to that kind of company which thousands of our young men in Vanity Fair are frequenting every day, which nightly fills casinos and dancing-rooms, which is known to exist as well as the Ring in Hyde Park or the Congregation at St. James's--but which the most squeamish if not the most moral of societies is determined to ignore. In a word, although Colonel Crawley was now five-and-forty years of age, it had not been his lot in life to meet with a half dozen good women, besides his paragon of a wife. All except her and his kind sister Lady Jane, whose gentle nature had tamed and won him, scared the worthy Colonel, and on occasion of his first dinner at Gaunt House he was not heard to make a single remark except to state that the weather was very hot. Indeed Becky would have left him at home, but that virtue ordained that her husband should be by her side to protect the timid and fluttering little creature on her first appearance in polite society.\n\nOn her first appearance Lord Steyne stepped forward, taking her hand, and greeting her with great courtesy, and presenting her to Lady Steyne, and their ladyships, her daughters. Their ladyships made three stately curtsies, and the elder lady to be sure gave her hand to the newcomer, but it was as cold and lifeless as marble.\n\nBecky took it, however, with grateful humility, and performing a reverence which would have done credit to the best dancer-master, put herself at Lady Steyne's feet, as it were, by saying that his Lordship had been her father's earliest friend and patron, and that she, Becky, had learned to honour and respect the Steyne family from the days of her childhood. The fact is that Lord Steyne had once purchased a couple of pictures of the late Sharp, and the affectionate orphan could never forget her gratitude for that favour.\n\nThe Lady Bareacres then came under Becky's cognizance--to whom the Colonel's lady made also a most respectful obeisance: it was returned with severe dignity by the exalted person in question.\n\n\"I had the pleasure of making your Ladyship's acquaintance at Brussels, ten years ago,\" Becky said in the most winning manner. \"I had the good fortune to meet Lady Bareacres at the Duchess of Richmond's ball, the night before the Battle of Waterloo. And I recollect your Ladyship, and my Lady Blanche, your daughter, sitting in the carriage in the porte-cochere at the Inn, waiting for horses. I hope your Ladyship's diamonds are safe.\"\n\nEverybody's eyes looked into their neighbour's. The famous diamonds had undergone a famous seizure, it appears, about which Becky, of course, knew nothing. Rawdon Crawley retreated with Lord Southdown into a window, where the latter was heard to laugh immoderately, as Rawdon told him the story of Lady Bareacres wanting horses and \"knuckling down by Jove,\" to Mrs. Crawley. \"I think I needn't be afraid of THAT woman,\" Becky thought. Indeed, Lady Bareacres exchanged terrified and angry looks with her daughter and retreated to a table, where she began to look at pictures with great energy.\n\nWhen the Potentate from the Danube made his appearance, the conversation was carried on in the French language, and the Lady Bareacres and the younger ladies found, to their farther mortification, that Mrs. Crawley was much better acquainted with that tongue, and spoke it with a much better accent than they. Becky had met other Hungarian magnates with the army in France in 1816-17. She asked after her friends with great interest. The foreign personages thought that she was a lady of great distinction, and the Prince and the Princess asked severally of Lord Steyne and the Marchioness, whom they conducted to dinner, who was that petite dame who spoke so well?\n\nFinally, the procession being formed in the order described by the American diplomatist, they marched into the apartment where the banquet was served, and which, as I have promised the reader he shall enjoy it, he shall have the liberty of ordering himself so as to suit his fancy.\n\nBut it was when the ladies were alone that Becky knew the tug of war would come. And then indeed the little woman found herself in such a situation as made her acknowledge the correctness of Lord Steyne's caution to her to beware of the society of ladies above her own sphere. As they say, the persons who hate Irishmen most are Irishmen; so, assuredly, the greatest tyrants over women are women. When poor little Becky, alone with the ladies, went up to the fire-place whither the great ladies had repaired, the great ladies marched away and took possession of a table of drawings. When Becky followed them to the table of drawings, they dropped off one by one to the fire again. She tried to speak to one of the children (of whom she was commonly fond in public places), but Master George Gaunt was called away by his mamma; and the stranger was treated with such cruelty finally, that even Lady Steyne herself pitied her and went up to speak to the friendless little woman.\n\n\"Lord Steyne,\" said her Ladyship, as her wan cheeks glowed with a blush, \"says you sing and play very beautifully, Mrs. Crawley--I wish you would do me the kindness to sing to me.\"\n\n\"I will do anything that may give pleasure to my Lord Steyne or to you,\" said Rebecca, sincerely grateful, and seating herself at the piano, began to sing.\n\nShe sang religious songs of Mozart, which had been early favourites of Lady Steyne, and with such sweetness and tenderness that the lady, lingering round the piano, sat down by its side and listened until the tears rolled down her eyes. It is true that the opposition ladies at the other end of the room kept up a loud and ceaseless buzzing and talking, but the Lady Steyne did not hear those rumours. She was a child again--and had wandered back through a forty years' wilderness to her convent garden. The chapel organ had pealed the same tones, the organist, the sister whom she loved best of the community, had taught them to her in those early happy days. She was a girl once more, and the brief period of her happiness bloomed out again for an hour--she started when the jarring doors were flung open, and with a loud laugh from Lord Steyne, the men of the party entered full of gaiety.\n\nHe saw at a glance what had happened in his absence, and was grateful to his wife for once. He went and spoke to her, and called her by her Christian name, so as again to bring blushes to her pale face--\"My wife says you have been singing like an angel,\" he said to Becky. Now there are angels of two kinds, and both sorts, it is said, are charming in their way.\n\nWhatever the previous portion of the evening had been, the rest of that night was a great triumph for Becky. She sang her very best, and it was so good that every one of the men came and crowded round the piano. The women, her enemies, were left quite alone. And Mr. Paul Jefferson Jones thought he had made a conquest of Lady Gaunt by going up to her Ladyship and praising her delightful friend's first-rate singing.\n\nContains a Vulgar Incident\nThe Muse, whoever she be, who presides over this Comic History must now descend from the genteel heights in which she has been soaring and have the goodness to drop down upon the lowly roof of John Sedley at Brompton, and describe what events are taking place there. Here, too, in this humble tenement, live care, and distrust, and dismay. Mrs. Clapp in the kitchen is grumbling in secret to her husband about the rent, and urging the good fellow to rebel against his old friend and patron and his present lodger. Mrs. Sedley has ceased to visit her landlady in the lower regions now, and indeed is in a position to patronize Mrs. Clapp no longer. How can one be condescending to a lady to whom one owes a matter of forty pounds, and who is perpetually throwing out hints for the money? The Irish maidservant has not altered in the least in her kind and respectful behaviour; but Mrs. Sedley fancies that she is growing insolent and ungrateful, and, as the guilty thief who fears each bush an officer, sees threatening innuendoes and hints of capture in all the girl's speeches and answers. Miss Clapp, grown quite a young woman now, is declared by the soured old lady to be an unbearable and impudent little minx. Why Amelia can be so fond of her, or have her in her room so much, or walk out with her so constantly, Mrs. Sedley cannot conceive. The bitterness of poverty has poisoned the life of the once cheerful and kindly woman. She is thankless for Amelia's constant and gentle bearing towards her; carps at her for her efforts at kindness or service; rails at her for her silly pride in her child and her neglect of her parents. Georgy's house is not a very lively one since Uncle Jos's annuity has been withdrawn and the little family are almost upon famine diet.\n\nAmelia thinks, and thinks, and racks her brain, to find some means of increasing the small pittance upon which the household is starving. Can she give lessons in anything? paint card-racks? do fine work? She finds that women are working hard, and better than she can, for twopence a day. She buys a couple of begilt Bristol boards at the Fancy Stationer's and paints her very best upon them--a shepherd with a red waistcoat on one, and a pink face smiling in the midst of a pencil landscape--a shepherdess on the other, crossing a little bridge, with a little dog, nicely shaded. The man of the Fancy Repository and Brompton Emporium of Fine Arts (of whom she bought the screens, vainly hoping that he would repurchase them when ornamented by her hand) can hardly hide the sneer with which he examines these feeble works of art. He looks askance at the lady who waits in the shop, and ties up the cards again in their envelope of whitey-brown paper, and hands them to the poor widow and Miss Clapp, who had never seen such beautiful things in her life, and had been quite confident that the man must give at least two guineas for the screens. They try at other shops in the interior of London, with faint sickening hopes. \"Don't want 'em,\" says one. \"Be off,\" says another fiercely. Three-and-sixpence has been spent in vain--the screens retire to Miss Clapp's bedroom, who persists in thinking them lovely.\n\nShe writes out a little card in her neatest hand, and after long thought and labour of composition, in which the public is informed that \"A Lady who has some time at her disposal, wishes to undertake the education of some little girls, whom she would instruct in English, in French, in Geography, in History, and in Music--address A. O., at Mr. Brown's\"; and she confides the card to the gentleman of the Fine Art Repository, who consents to allow it to lie upon the counter, where it grows dingy and fly-blown. Amelia passes the door wistfully many a time, in hopes that Mr. Brown will have some news to give her, but he never beckons her in. When she goes to make little purchases, there is no news for her. Poor simple lady, tender and weak--how are you to battle with the struggling violent world?\n\nShe grows daily more care-worn and sad, fixing upon her child alarmed eyes, whereof the little boy cannot interpret the expression. She starts up of a night and peeps into his room stealthily, to see that he is sleeping and not stolen away. She sleeps but little now. A constant thought and terror is haunting her. How she weeps and prays in the long silent nights--how she tries to hide from herself the thought which will return to her, that she ought to part with the boy, that she is the only barrier between him and prosperity. She can't, she can't. Not now, at least. Some other day. Oh! it is too hard to think of and to bear.\n\nA thought comes over her which makes her blush and turn from herself--her parents might keep the annuity--the curate would marry her and give a home to her and the boy. But George's picture and dearest memory are there to rebuke her. Shame and love say no to the sacrifice. She shrinks from it as from something unholy, and such thoughts never found a resting-place in that pure and gentle bosom.\n\nThe combat, which we describe in a sentence or two, lasted for many weeks in poor Amelia's heart, during which she had no confidante; indeed, she could never have one, as she would not allow to herself the possibility of yielding, though she was giving way daily before the enemy with whom she had to battle. One truth after another was marshalling itself silently against her and keeping its ground. Poverty and misery for all, want and degradation for her parents, injustice to the boy--one by one the outworks of the little citadel were taken, in which the poor soul passionately guarded her only love and treasure.\n\nAt the beginning of the struggle, she had written off a letter of tender supplication to her brother at Calcutta, imploring him not to withdraw the support which he had granted to their parents and painting in terms of artless pathos their lonely and hapless condition. She did not know the truth of the matter. The payment of Jos's annuity was still regular, but it was a money-lender in the City who was receiving it: old Sedley had sold it for a sum of money wherewith to prosecute his bootless schemes. Emmy was calculating eagerly the time that would elapse before the letter would arrive and be answered. She had written down the date in her pocket-book of the day when she dispatched it. To her son's guardian, the good Major at Madras, she had not communicated any of her griefs and perplexities. She had not written to him since she wrote to congratulate him on his approaching marriage. She thought with sickening despondency, that that friend--the only one, the one who had felt such a regard for her--was fallen away.\n\nOne day, when things had come to a very bad pass--when the creditors were pressing, the mother in hysteric grief, the father in more than usual gloom, the inmates of the family avoiding each other, each secretly oppressed with his private unhappiness and notion of wrong--the father and daughter happened to be left alone together, and Amelia thought to comfort her father by telling him what she had done. She had written to Joseph--an answer must come in three or four months. He was always generous, though careless. He could not refuse, when he knew how straitened were the circumstances of his parents.\n\nThen the poor old gentleman revealed the whole truth to her--that his son was still paying the annuity, which his own imprudence had flung away. He had not dared to tell it sooner. He thought Amelia's ghastly and terrified look, when, with a trembling, miserable voice he made the confession, conveyed reproaches to him for his concealment. \"Ah!\" said he with quivering lips and turning away, \"you despise your old father now!\"\n\n\"Oh, papa! it is not that,\" Amelia cried out, falling on his neck and kissing him many times. \"You are always good and kind. You did it for the best. It is not for the money--it is--my God! my God! have mercy upon me, and give me strength to bear this trial\"; and she kissed him again wildly and went away.\n\nStill the father did not know what that explanation meant, and the burst of anguish with which the poor girl left him. It was that she was conquered. The sentence was passed. The child must go from her--to others--to forget her. Her heart and her treasure--her joy, hope, love, worship--her God, almost! She must give him up, and then--and then she would go to George, and they would watch over the child and wait for him until he came to them in Heaven.\n\nShe put on her bonnet, scarcely knowing what she did, and went out to walk in the lanes by which George used to come back from school, and where she was in the habit of going on his return to meet the boy. It was May, a half-holiday. The leaves were all coming out, the weather was brilliant; the boy came running to her flushed with health, singing, his bundle of school-books hanging by a thong. There he was. Both her arms were round him. No, it was impossible. They could not be going to part. \"What is the matter, Mother?\" said he; \"you look very pale.\"\n\n\"Nothing, my child,\" she said and stooped down and kissed him.\n\nThat night Amelia made the boy read the story of Samuel to her, and how Hannah, his mother, having weaned him, brought him to Eli the High Priest to minister before the Lord. And he read the song of gratitude which Hannah sang, and which says, who it is who maketh poor and maketh rich, and bringeth low and exalteth--how the poor shall be raised up out of the dust, and how, in his own might, no man shall be strong. Then he read how Samuel's mother made him a little coat and brought it to him from year to year when she came up to offer the yearly sacrifice. And then, in her sweet simple way, George's mother made commentaries to the boy upon this affecting story. How Hannah, though she loved her son so much, yet gave him up because of her vow. And how she must always have thought of him as she sat at home, far away, making the little coat; and Samuel, she was sure, never forgot his mother; and how happy she must have been as the time came (and the years pass away very quick) when she should see her boy and how good and wise he had grown. This little sermon she spoke with a gentle solemn voice, and dry eyes, until she came to the account of their meeting--then the discourse broke off suddenly, the tender heart overflowed, and taking the boy to her breast, she rocked him in her arms and wept silently over him in a sainted agony of tears.\n\nHer mind being made up, the widow began to take such measures as seemed right to her for advancing the end which she proposed. One day, Miss Osborne, in Russell Square (Amelia had not written the name or number of the house for ten years--her youth, her early story came back to her as she wrote the superscription) one day Miss Osborne got a letter from Amelia which made her blush very much and look towards her father, sitting glooming in his place at the other end of the table.\n\nIn simple terms, Amelia told her the reasons which had induced her to change her mind respecting her boy. Her father had met with fresh misfortunes which had entirely ruined him. Her own pittance was so small that it would barely enable her to support her parents and would not suffice to give George the advantages which were his due. Great as her sufferings would be at parting with him she would, by God's help, endure them for the boy's sake. She knew that those to whom he was going would do all in their power to make him happy. She described his disposition, such as she fancied it--quick and impatient of control or harshness, easily to be moved by love and kindness. In a postscript, she stipulated that she should have a written agreement, that she should see the child as often as she wished--she could not part with him under any other terms.\n\n\"What? Mrs. Pride has come down, has she?\" old Osborne said, when with a tremulous eager voice Miss Osborne read him the letter. \"Reg'lar starved out, hey? Ha, ha! I knew she would.\" He tried to keep his dignity and to read his paper as usual--but he could not follow it. He chuckled and swore to himself behind the sheet.\n\nAt last he flung it down and, scowling at his daughter, as his wont was, went out of the room into his study adjoining, from whence he presently returned with a key. He flung it to Miss Osborne.\n\n\"Get the room over mine--his room that was--ready,\" he said. \"Yes, sir,\" his daughter replied in a tremble. It was George's room. It had not been opened for more than ten years. Some of his clothes, papers, handkerchiefs, whips and caps, fishing-rods and sporting gear, were still there. An Army list of 1814, with his name written on the cover; a little dictionary he was wont to use in writing; and the Bible his mother had given him, were on the mantelpiece, with a pair of spurs and a dried inkstand covered with the dust of ten years. Ah! since that ink was wet, what days and people had passed away! The writing-book, still on the table, was blotted with his hand.\n\nMiss Osborne was much affected when she first entered this room with the servants under her. She sank quite pale on the little bed. \"This is blessed news, m'am--indeed, m'am,\" the housekeeper said; \"and the good old times is returning, m'am. The dear little feller, to be sure, m'am; how happy he will be! But some folks in May Fair, m'am, will owe him a grudge, m'am\"; and she clicked back the bolt which held the window-sash and let the air into the chamber.\n\n\"You had better send that woman some money,\" Mr. Osborne said, before he went out. \"She shan't want for nothing. Send her a hundred pound.\"\n\n\"And I'll go and see her to-morrow?\" Miss Osborne asked.\n\n\"That's your look out. She don't come in here, mind. No, by ------, not for all the money in London. But she mustn't want now. So look out, and get things right.\" With which brief speeches Mr. Osborne took leave of his daughter and went on his accustomed way into the City.\n\n\"Here, Papa, is some money,\" Amelia said that night, kissing the old man, her father, and putting a bill for a hundred pounds into his hands. \"And--and, Mamma, don't be harsh with Georgy. He--he is not going to stop with us long.\" She could say nothing more, and walked away silently to her room. Let us close it upon her prayers and her sorrow. I think we had best speak little about so much love and grief.\n\nMiss Osborne came the next day, according to the promise contained in her note, and saw Amelia. The meeting between them was friendly. A look and a few words from Miss Osborne showed the poor widow that, with regard to this woman at least, there need be no fear lest she should take the first place in her son's affection. She was cold, sensible, not unkind. The mother had not been so well pleased, perhaps, had the rival been better looking, younger, more affectionate, warmer-hearted. Miss Osborne, on the other hand, thought of old times and memories and could not but be touched with the poor mother's pitiful situation. She was conquered, and laying down her arms, as it were, she humbly submitted. That day they arranged together the preliminaries of the treaty of capitulation.\n\nGeorge was kept from school the next day, and saw his aunt. Amelia left them alone together and went to her room. She was trying the separation--as that poor gentle Lady Jane Grey felt the edge of the axe that was to come down and sever her slender life. Days were passed in parleys, visits, preparations. The widow broke the matter to Georgy with great caution; she looked to see him very much affected by the intelligence. He was rather elated than otherwise, and the poor woman turned sadly away. He bragged about the news that day to the boys at school; told them how he was going to live with his grandpapa, his father's father, not the one who comes here sometimes; and that he would be very rich, and have a carriage, and a pony, and go to a much finer school, and when he was rich he would buy Leader's pencil-case and pay the tart-woman. The boy was the image of his father, as his fond mother thought.\n\nIndeed I have no heart, on account of our dear Amelia's sake, to go through the story of George's last days at home.\n\nAt last the day came, the carriage drove up, the little humble packets containing tokens of love and remembrance were ready and disposed in the hall long since--George was in his new suit, for which the tailor had come previously to measure him. He had sprung up with the sun and put on the new clothes, his mother hearing him from the room close by, in which she had been lying, in speechless grief and watching. Days before she had been making preparations for the end, purchasing little stores for the boy's use, marking his books and linen, talking with him and preparing him for the change--fondly fancying that he needed preparation.\n\nSo that he had change, what cared he? He was longing for it. By a thousand eager declarations as to what he would do, when he went to live with his grandfather, he had shown the poor widow how little the idea of parting had cast him down. \"He would come and see his mamma often on the pony,\" he said. \"He would come and fetch her in the carriage; they would drive in the park, and she should have everything she wanted.\" The poor mother was fain to content herself with these selfish demonstrations of attachment, and tried to convince herself how sincerely her son loved her. He must love her. All children were so: a little anxious for novelty, and--no, not selfish, but self-willed. Her child must have his enjoyments and ambition in the world. She herself, by her own selfishness and imprudent love for him had denied him his just rights and pleasures hitherto.\n\nI know few things more affecting than that timorous debasement and self-humiliation of a woman. How she owns that it is she and not the man who is guilty; how she takes all the faults on her side; how she courts in a manner punishment for the wrongs which she has not committed and persists in shielding the real culprit! It is those who injure women who get the most kindness from them--they are born timid and tyrants and maltreat those who are humblest before them.\n\nSo poor Amelia had been getting ready in silent misery for her son's departure, and had passed many and many a long solitary hour in making preparations for the end. George stood by his mother, watching her arrangements without the least concern. Tears had fallen into his boxes; passages had been scored in his favourite books; old toys, relics, treasures had been hoarded away for him, and packed with strange neatness and care--and of all these things the boy took no note. The child goes away smiling as the mother breaks her heart. By heavens it is pitiful, the bootless love of women for children in Vanity Fair.\n\nA few days are past, and the great event of Amelia's life is consummated. No angel has intervened. The child is sacrificed and offered up to fate, and the widow is quite alone.\n\nThe boy comes to see her often, to be sure. He rides on a pony with a coachman behind him, to the delight of his old grandfather, Sedley, who walks proudly down the lane by his side. She sees him, but he is not her boy any more. Why, he rides to see the boys at the little school, too, and to show off before them his new wealth and splendour. In two days he has adopted a slightly imperious air and patronizing manner. He was born to command, his mother thinks, as his father was before him.\n\nIt is fine weather now. Of evenings on the days when he does not come, she takes a long walk into London--yes, as far as Russell Square, and rests on the stone by the railing of the garden opposite Mr. Osborne's house. It is so pleasant and cool. She can look up and see the drawing-room windows illuminated, and, at about nine o'clock, the chamber in the upper story where Georgy sleeps. She knows--he has told her. She prays there as the light goes out, prays with an humble heart, and walks home shrinking and silent. She is very tired when she comes home. Perhaps she will sleep the better for that long weary walk, and she may dream about Georgy.\n\nOne Sunday she happened to be walking in Russell Square, at some distance from Mr. Osborne's house (she could see it from a distance though) when all the bells of Sabbath were ringing, and George and his aunt came out to go to church; a little sweep asked for charity, and the footman, who carried the books, tried to drive him away; but Georgy stopped and gave him money. May God's blessing be on the boy! Emmy ran round the square and, coming up to the sweep, gave him her mite too. All the bells of Sabbath were ringing, and she followed them until she came to the Foundling Church, into which she went. There she sat in a place whence she could see the head of the boy under his father's tombstone. Many hundred fresh children's voices rose up there and sang hymns to the Father Beneficent, and little George's soul thrilled with delight at the burst of glorious psalmody. His mother could not see him for awhile, through the mist that dimmed her eyes.\n\n# CHAPTER LI\n\nIn Which a Charade Is Acted Which May or May Not Puzzle the Reader\n\nAfter Becky's appearance at my Lord Steyne's private and select parties, the claims of that estimable woman as regards fashion were settled, and some of the very greatest and tallest doors in the metropolis were speedily opened to her--doors so great and tall that the beloved reader and writer hereof may hope in vain to enter at them. Dear brethren, let us tremble before those august portals. I fancy them guarded by grooms of the chamber with flaming silver forks with which they prong all those who have not the right of the entree. They say the honest newspaper-fellow who sits in the hall and takes down the names of the great ones who are admitted to the feasts dies after a little time. He can't survive the glare of fashion long. It scorches him up, as the presence of Jupiter in full dress wasted that poor imprudent Semele--a giddy moth of a creature who ruined herself by venturing out of her natural atmosphere. Her myth ought to be taken to heart amongst the Tyburnians, the Belgravians--her story, and perhaps Becky's too. Ah, ladies!--ask the Reverend Mr. Thurifer if Belgravia is not a sounding brass and Tyburnia a tinkling cymbal. These are vanities. Even these will pass away. And some day or other (but it will be after our time, thank goodness) Hyde Park Gardens will be no better known than the celebrated horticultural outskirts of Babylon, and Belgrave Square will be as desolate as Baker Street, or Tadmor in the wilderness.\n\nLadies, are you aware that the great Pitt lived in Baker Street? What would not your grandmothers have given to be asked to Lady Hester's parties in that now decayed mansion? I have dined in it--moi qui vous parle, I peopled the chamber with ghosts of the mighty dead. As we sat soberly drinking claret there with men of to-day, the spirits of the departed came in and took their places round the darksome board. The pilot who weathered the storm tossed off great bumpers of spiritual port; the shade of Dundas did not leave the ghost of a heeltap. Addington sat bowing and smirking in a ghastly manner, and would not be behindhand when the noiseless bottle went round; Scott, from under bushy eyebrows, winked at the apparition of a beeswing; Wilberforce's eyes went up to the ceiling, so that he did not seem to know how his glass went up full to his mouth and came down empty; up to the ceiling which was above us only yesterday, and which the great of the past days have all looked at. They let the house as a furnished lodging now. Yes, Lady Hester once lived in Baker Street, and lies asleep in the wilderness. Eothen saw her there--not in Baker Street, but in the other solitude.\n\nIt is all vanity to be sure, but who will not own to liking a little of it? I should like to know what well-constituted mind, merely because it is transitory, dislikes roast beef? That is a vanity, but may every man who reads this have a wholesome portion of it through life, I beg: aye, though my readers were five hundred thousand. Sit down, gentlemen, and fall to, with a good hearty appetite; the fat, the lean, the gravy, the horse-radish as you like it--don't spare it. Another glass of wine, Jones, my boy--a little bit of the Sunday side. Yes, let us eat our fill of the vain thing and be thankful therefor. And let us make the best of Becky's aristocratic pleasures likewise--for these too, like all other mortal delights, were but transitory.\n\nThe upshot of her visit to Lord Steyne was that His Highness the Prince of Peterwaradin took occasion to renew his acquaintance with Colonel Crawley, when they met on the next day at the Club, and to compliment Mrs. Crawley in the Ring of Hyde Park with a profound salute of the hat. She and her husband were invited immediately to one of the Prince's small parties at Levant House, then occupied by His Highness during the temporary absence from England of its noble proprietor. She sang after dinner to a very little comite. The Marquis of Steyne was present, paternally superintending the progress of his pupil.\n\nAt Levant House Becky met one of the finest gentlemen and greatest ministers that Europe has produced--the Duc de la Jabotiere, then Ambassador from the Most Christian King, and subsequently Minister to that monarch. I declare I swell with pride as these august names are\n\nBecky is moving. She became a constant guest at the French Embassy, where no party was considered to be complete without the presence of the charming Madame Ravdonn Cravley. Messieurs de Truffigny (of the Perigord family) and Champignac, both attaches of the Embassy, were straightway smitten by the charms of the fair Colonel's wife, and both declared, according to the wont of their nation (for who ever yet met a Frenchman, come out of England, that has not left half a dozen families miserable, and brought away as many hearts in his pocket-book?), both, I say, declared that they were au mieux with the charming Madame Ravdonn.\n\nBut I doubt the correctness of the assertion. Champignac was very fond of ecarte, and made many parties with the Colonel of evenings, while Becky was singing to Lord Steyne in the other room; and as for Truffigny, it is a well-known fact that he dared not go to the Travellers', where he owed money to the waiters, and if he had not had the Embassy as a dining-place, the worthy young gentleman must have starved. I doubt, I say, that Becky would have selected either of these young men as a person on whom she would bestow her special regard. They ran of her messages, purchased her gloves and flowers, went in debt for opera-boxes for her, and made themselves amiable in a thousand ways. And they talked English with adorable simplicity, and to the constant amusement of Becky and my Lord Steyne, she would mimic one or other to his face, and compliment him on his advance in the English language with a gravity which never failed to tickle the Marquis, her sardonic old patron. Truffigny gave Briggs a shawl by way of winning over Becky's confidante, and asked her to take charge of a letter which the simple spinster handed over in public to the person to whom it was addressed, and the composition of which amused everybody who read it greatly. Lord Steyne read it, everybody but honest Rawdon, to whom it was not necessary to tell everything that passed in the little house in May Fair.\n\nHere, before long, Becky received not only \"the best\" foreigners (as the phrase is in our noble and admirable society slang), but some of the best English people too. I don't mean the most virtuous, or indeed the least virtuous, or the cleverest, or the stupidest, or the richest, or the best born, but \"the best,\"--in a word, people about whom there is no question--such as the great Lady Fitz-Willis, that Patron Saint of Almack's, the great Lady Slowbore, the great Lady Grizzel Macbeth (she was Lady G. Glowry, daughter of Lord Grey of Glowry), and the like. When the Countess of Fitz-Willis (her Ladyship is of the Kingstreet family, see Debrett and Burke) takes up a person, he or she is safe. There is no question about them any more. Not that my Lady Fitz-Willis is any better than anybody else, being, on the contrary, a faded person, fifty-seven years of age, and neither handsome, nor wealthy, nor entertaining; but it is agreed on all sides that she is of the \"best people.\" Those who go to her are of the best: and from an old grudge probably to Lady Steyne (for whose coronet her ladyship, then the youthful Georgina Frederica, daughter of the Prince of Wales's favourite, the Earl of Portansherry, had once tried), this great and famous leader of the fashion chose to acknowledge Mrs. Rawdon Crawley; made her a most marked curtsey at the assembly over which she presided; and not only encouraged her son, St. Kitts (his lordship got his place through Lord Steyne's interest), to frequent Mrs. Crawley's house, but asked her to her own mansion and spoke to her twice in the most public and condescending manner during dinner. The important fact was known all over London that night. People who had been crying fie about Mrs. Crawley were silent. Wenham, the wit and lawyer, Lord Steyne's right-hand man, went about everywhere praising her: some who had hesitated, came forward at once and welcomed her; little Tom Toady, who had warned Southdown about visiting such an abandoned woman, now besought to be introduced to her. In a word, she was admitted to be among the \"best\" people. Ah, my beloved readers and brethren, do not envy poor Becky prematurely--glory like this is said to be fugitive. It is currently reported that even in the very inmost circles, they are no happier than the poor wanderers outside the zone; and Becky, who penetrated into the very centre of fashion and saw the great George IV face to face, has owned since that there too was Vanity.\n\nWe must be brief in descanting upon this part of her career. As I cannot describe the mysteries of freemasonry, although I have a shrewd idea that it is a humbug, so an uninitiated man cannot take upon himself to portray the great world accurately, and had best keep his opinions to himself, whatever they are.\n\nBecky has often spoken in subsequent years of this season of her life, when she moved among the very greatest circles of the London fashion. Her success excited, elated, and then bored her. At first no occupation was more pleasant than to invent and procure (the latter a work of no small trouble and ingenuity, by the way, in a person of Mrs. Rawdon Crawley's very narrow means)--to procure, we say, the prettiest new dresses and ornaments; to drive to fine dinner parties, where she was welcomed by great people; and from the fine dinner parties to fine assemblies, whither the same people came with whom she had been dining, whom she had met the night before, and would see on the morrow--the young men faultlessly appointed, handsomely cravatted, with the neatest glossy boots and white gloves--the elders portly, brass-buttoned, noble-looking, polite, and prosy--the young ladies blonde, timid, and in pink--the mothers grand, beautiful, sumptuous, solemn, and in diamonds. They talked in English, not in bad French, as they do in the novels. They talked about each others' houses, and characters, and families--just as the Joneses do about the Smiths. Becky's former acquaintances hated and envied her; the poor woman herself was yawning in spirit. \"I wish I were out of it,\" she said to herself. \"I would rather be a parson's wife and teach a Sunday school than this; or a sergeant's lady and ride in the regimental waggon; or, oh, how much gayer it would be to wear spangles and trousers and dance before a booth at a fair.\"\n\n\"You would do it very well,\" said Lord Steyne, laughing. She used to tell the great man her ennuis and perplexities in her artless way--they amused him.\n\n\"Rawdon would make a very good Ecuyer--Master of the Ceremonies--what do you call him--the man in the large boots and the uniform, who goes round the ring cracking the whip? He is large, heavy, and of a military figure. I recollect,\" Becky continued pensively, \"my father took me to see a show at Brookgreen Fair when I was a child, and when we came home, I made myself a pair of stilts and danced in the studio to the wonder of all the pupils.\"\n\n\"I should have liked to see it,\" said Lord Steyne.\n\n\"I should like to do it now,\" Becky continued. \"How Lady Blinkey would open her eyes, and Lady Grizzel Macbeth would stare! Hush! silence! there is Pasta beginning to sing.\" Becky always made a point of being conspicuously polite to the professional ladies and gentlemen who attended at these aristocratic parties--of following them into the corners where they sat in silence, and shaking hands with them, and smiling in the view of all persons. She was an artist herself, as she said very truly; there was a frankness and humility in the manner in which she acknowledged her origin, which provoked, or disarmed, or amused lookers-on, as the case might be. \"How cool that woman is,\" said one; \"what airs of independence she assumes, where she ought to sit still and be thankful if anybody speaks to her!\" \"What an honest and good-natured soul she is!\" said another. \"What an artful little minx\" said a third. They were all right very likely, but Becky went her own way, and so fascinated the professional personages that they would leave off their sore throats in order to sing at her parties and give her lessons for nothing.\n\nYes, she gave parties in the little house in Curzon Street. Many scores of carriages, with blazing lamps, blocked up the street, to the disgust of No. 100, who could not rest for the thunder of the knocking, and of 102, who could not sleep for envy. The gigantic footmen who accompanied the vehicles were too big to be contained in Becky's little hall, and were billeted off in the neighbouring public-houses, whence, when they were wanted, call-boys summoned them from their beer. Scores of the great dandies of London squeezed and trod on each other on the little stairs, laughing to find themselves there; and many spotless and severe ladies of ton were seated in the little drawing-room, listening to the professional singers, who were singing according to their wont, and as if they wished to blow the windows down. And the day after, there appeared among the fashionable reunions in the Morning Post a paragraph to the following effect:\n\n\"Yesterday, Colonel and Mrs. Crawley entertained a select party at dinner at their house in May Fair. Their Excellencies the Prince and Princess of Peterwaradin, H. E. Papoosh Pasha, the Turkish Ambassador (attended by Kibob Bey, dragoman of the mission), the Marquess of Steyne, Earl of Southdown, Sir Pitt and Lady Jane Crawley, Mr. Wagg, &c. After dinner Mrs. Crawley had an assembly which was attended by the Duchess (Dowager) of Stilton, Duc de la Gruyere, Marchioness of Cheshire, Marchese Alessandro Strachino, Comte de Brie, Baron Schapzuger, Chevalier Tosti, Countess of Slingstone, and Lady F. Macadam, Major-General and Lady G. Macbeth, and (2) Miss Macbeths; Viscount Paddington, Sir Horace Fogey, Hon. Sands Bedwin, Bobachy Bahawder,\" and an &c., which the reader may fill at his pleasure through a dozen close lines of small type.\n\nAnd in her commerce with the great our dear friend showed the same frankness which distinguished her transactions with the lowly in station. On one occasion, when out at a very fine house, Rebecca was (perhaps rather ostentatiously) holding a conversation in the French language with a celebrated tenor singer of that nation, while the Lady Grizzel Macbeth looked over her shoulder scowling at the pair.\n\n\"How very well you speak French,\" Lady Grizzel said, who herself spoke the tongue in an Edinburgh accent most remarkable to hear.\n\n\"I ought to know it,\" Becky modestly said, casting down her eyes. \"I taught it in a school, and my mother was a Frenchwoman.\"\n\nLady Grizzel was won by her humility and was mollified towards the little woman. She deplored the fatal levelling tendencies of the age, which admitted persons of all classes into the society of their superiors, but her ladyship owned that this one at least was well behaved and never forgot her place in life. She was a very good woman: good to the poor; stupid, blameless, unsuspicious. It is not her ladyship's fault that she fancies herself better than you and me. The skirts of her ancestors' garments have been kissed for centuries; it is a thousand years, they say, since the tartans of the head of the family were embraced by the defunct Duncan's lords and councillors, when the great ancestor of the House became King of Scotland.\n\nLady Steyne, after the music scene, succumbed before Becky, and perhaps was not disinclined to her. The younger ladies of the house of Gaunt were also compelled into submission. Once or twice they set people at her, but they failed. The brilliant Lady Stunnington tried a passage of arms with her, but was routed with great slaughter by the intrepid little Becky. When attacked sometimes, Becky had a knack of adopting a demure ingenue air, under which she was most dangerous. She said the wickedest things with the most simple unaffected air when in this mood, and would take care artlessly to apologize for her blunders, so that all the world should know that she had made them.\n\nMr. Wagg, the celebrated wit, and a led captain and trencher-man of my Lord Steyne, was caused by the ladies to charge her; and the worthy fellow, leering at his patronesses and giving them a wink, as much as to say, \"Now look out for sport,\" one evening began an assault upon Becky, who was unsuspiciously eating her dinner. The little woman, attacked on a sudden, but never without arms, lighted up in an instant, parried and riposted with a home-thrust, which made Wagg's face tingle with shame; then she returned to her soup with the most perfect calm and a quiet smile on her face. Wagg's great patron, who gave him dinners and lent him a little money sometimes, and whose election, newspaper, and other jobs Wagg did, gave the luckless fellow such a savage glance with the eyes as almost made him sink under the table and burst into tears. He looked piteously at my lord, who never spoke to him during dinner, and at the ladies, who disowned him. At last Becky herself took compassion upon him and tried to engage him in talk. He was not asked to dinner again for six weeks; and Fiche, my lord's confidential man, to whom Wagg naturally paid a good deal of court, was instructed to tell him that if he ever dared to say a rude thing to Mrs. Crawley again, or make her the butt of his stupid jokes, Milor would put every one of his notes of hand into his lawyer's hands and sell him up without mercy. Wagg wept before Fiche and implored his dear friend to intercede for him. He wrote a poem in favour of Mrs. R. C., which appeared in the very next number of the Harum-scarum Magazine, which he conducted. He implored her good-will at parties where he met her. He cringed and coaxed Rawdon at the club. He was allowed to come back to Gaunt House after a while. Becky was always good to him, always amused, never angry.\n\nHis lordship's vizier and chief confidential servant (with a seat in parliament and at the dinner table), Mr. Wenham, was much more prudent in his behaviour and opinions than Mr. Wagg. However much he might be disposed to hate all parvenus (Mr. Wenham himself was a staunch old True Blue Tory, and his father a small coal-merchant in the north of England), this aide-de-camp of the Marquis never showed any sort of hostility to the new favourite, but pursued her with stealthy kindnesses and a sly and deferential politeness which somehow made Becky more uneasy than other people's overt hostilities.\n\nHow the Crawleys got the money which was spent upon the entertainments with which they treated the polite world was a mystery which gave rise to some conversation at the time, and probably added zest to these little festivities. Some persons averred that Sir Pitt Crawley gave his brother a handsome allowance; if he did, Becky's power over the Baronet must have been extraordinary indeed, and his character greatly changed in his advanced age. Other parties hinted that it was Becky's habit to levy contributions on all her husband's friends: going to this one in tears with an account that there was an execution in the house; falling on her knees to that one and declaring that the whole family must go to gaol or commit suicide unless such and such a bill could be paid. Lord Southdown, it was said, had been induced to give many hundreds through these pathetic representations. Young Feltham, of the --th Dragoons (and son of the firm of Tiler and Feltham, hatters and army accoutrement makers), and whom the Crawleys introduced into fashionable life, was also cited as one of Becky's victims in the pecuniary way. People declared that she got money from various simply disposed persons, under pretence of getting them confidential appointments under Government. Who knows what stories were or were not told of our dear and innocent friend? Certain it is that if she had had all the money which she was said to have begged or borrowed or stolen, she might have capitalized and been honest for life, whereas,--but this is advancing matters.\n\nThe truth is, that by economy and good management--by a sparing use of ready money and by paying scarcely anybody--people can manage, for a time at least, to make a great show with very little means: and it is our belief that Becky's much-talked-of parties, which were not, after all was said, very numerous, cost this lady very little more than the wax candles which lighted the walls. Stillbrook and Queen's Crawley supplied her with game and fruit in abundance. Lord Steyne's cellars were at her disposal, and that excellent nobleman's famous cooks presided over her little kitchen, or sent by my lord's order the rarest delicacies from their own. I protest it is quite shameful in the world to abuse a simple creature, as people of her time abuse Becky, and I warn the public against believing one-tenth of the stories against her. If every person is to be banished from society who runs into debt and cannot pay--if we are to be peering into everybody's private life, speculating upon their income, and cutting them if we don't approve of their expenditure--why, what a howling wilderness and intolerable dwelling Vanity Fair would be! Every man's hand would be against his neighbour in this case, my dear sir, and the benefits of civilization would be done away with. We should be quarrelling, abusing, avoiding one another. Our houses would become caverns, and we should go in rags because we cared for nobody. Rents would go down. Parties wouldn't be given any more. All the tradesmen of the town would be bankrupt. Wine, wax-lights, comestibles, rouge, crinoline-petticoats, diamonds, wigs, Louis-Quatorze gimcracks, and old china, park hacks, and splendid high-stepping carriage horses--all the delights of life, I say,--would go to the deuce, if people did but act upon their silly principles and avoid those whom they dislike and abuse. Whereas, by a little charity and mutual forbearance, things are made to go on pleasantly enough: we may abuse a man as much as we like, and call him the greatest rascal unhanged--but do we wish to hang him therefore? No. We shake hands when we meet. If his cook is good we forgive him and go and dine with him, and we expect he will do the same by us. Thus trade flourishes--civilization advances; peace is kept; new dresses are wanted for new assemblies every week; and the last year's vintage of Lafitte will remunerate the honest proprietor who reared it.\n\nAt the time whereof we are writing, though the Great George was on the throne and ladies wore gigots and large combs like tortoise-shell shovels in their hair, instead of the simple sleeves and lovely wreaths which are actually in fashion, the manners of the very polite world were not, I take it, essentially different from those of the present day: and their amusements pretty similar. To us, from the outside, gazing over the policeman's shoulders at the bewildering beauties as they pass into Court or ball, they may seem beings of unearthly splendour and in the enjoyment of an exquisite happiness by us unattainable. It is to console some of these dissatisfied beings that we are narrating our dear Becky's struggles, and triumphs, and disappointments, of all of which, indeed, as is the case with all persons of merit, she had her share.\n\nAt this time the amiable amusement of acting charades had come among us from France, and was considerably in vogue in this country, enabling the many ladies amongst us who had beauty to display their charms, and the fewer number who had cleverness to exhibit their wit. My Lord Steyne was incited by Becky, who perhaps believed herself endowed with both the above qualifications, to give an entertainment at Gaunt House, which should include some of these little dramas--and we must take leave to introduce the reader to this brilliant reunion, and, with a melancholy welcome too, for it will be among the very last of the fashionable entertainments to which it will be our fortune to conduct him.\n\nA portion of that splendid room, the picture gallery of Gaunt House, was arranged as the charade theatre. It had been so used when George III was king; and a picture of the Marquis of Gaunt is still extant, with his hair in powder and a pink ribbon, in a Roman shape, as it was called, enacting the part of Cato in Mr. Addison's tragedy of that name, performed before their Royal Highnesses the Prince of Wales, the Bishop of Osnaburgh, and Prince William Henry, then children like the actor. One or two of the old properties were drawn out of the garrets, where they had lain ever since, and furbished up anew for the present festivities.\n\nYoung Bedwin Sands, then an elegant dandy and Eastern traveller, was manager of the revels. An Eastern traveller was somebody in those days, and the adventurous Bedwin, who had published his quarto and passed some months under the tents in the desert, was a personage of no small importance. In his volume there were several pictures of Sands in various oriental costumes; and he travelled about with a black attendant of most unprepossessing appearance, just like another Brian de Bois Guilbert. Bedwin, his costumes, and black man, were hailed at Gaunt House as very valuable acquisitions.\n\nHe led off the first charade. A Turkish officer with an immense plume of feathers (the Janizaries were supposed to be still in existence, and the tarboosh had not as yet displaced the ancient and majestic head-dress of the true believers) was seen couched on a divan, and making believe to puff at a narghile, in which, however, for the sake of the ladies, only a fragrant pastille was allowed to smoke. The Turkish dignitary yawns and expresses signs of weariness and idleness. He claps his hands and Mesrour the Nubian appears, with bare arms, bangles, yataghans, and every Eastern ornament--gaunt, tall, and hideous. He makes a salaam before my lord the Aga.\n\nA thrill of terror and delight runs through the assembly. The ladies whisper to one another. The black slave was given to Bedwin Sands by an Egyptian pasha in exchange for three dozen of Maraschino. He has sewn up ever so many odalisques in sacks and tilted them into the Nile.\n\n\"Bid the slave-merchant enter,\" says the Turkish voluptuary with a wave of his hand. Mesrour conducts the slave-merchant into my lord's presence; he brings a veiled female with him. He removes the veil. A thrill of applause bursts through the house. It is Mrs. Winkworth (she was a Miss Absolom) with the beautiful eyes and hair. She is in a gorgeous oriental costume; the black braided locks are twined with innumerable jewels; her dress is covered over with gold piastres. The odious Mahometan expresses himself charmed by her beauty. She falls down on her knees and entreats him to restore her to the mountains where she was born, and where her Circassian lover is still deploring the absence of his Zuleikah. No entreaties will move the obdurate Hassan. He laughs at the notion of the Circassian bridegroom. Zuleikah covers her face with her hands and drops down in an attitude of the most beautiful despair. There seems to be no hope for her, when--when the Kislar Aga appears.\n\nThe Kislar Aga brings a letter from the Sultan. Hassan receives and places on his head the dread firman. A ghastly terror seizes him, while on the Negro's face (it is Mesrour again in another costume) appears a ghastly joy. \"Mercy! mercy!\" cries the Pasha: while the Kislar Aga, grinning horribly, pulls out--a bow-string.\n\nThe curtain draws just as he is going to use that awful weapon. Hassan from within bawls out, \"First two syllables\"--and Mrs. Rawdon Crawley, who is going to act in the charade, comes forward and compliments Mrs. Winkworth on the admirable taste and beauty of her costume.\n\nThe second part of the charade takes place. It is still an Eastern scene. Hassan, in another dress, is in an attitude by Zuleikah, who is perfectly reconciled to him. The Kislar Aga has become a peaceful black slave. It is sunrise on the desert, and the Turks turn their heads eastwards and bow to the sand. As there are no dromedaries at hand, the band facetiously plays \"The Camels are coming.\" An enormous Egyptian head figures in the scene. It is a musical one--and, to the surprise of the oriental travellers, sings a comic song, composed by Mr. Wagg. The Eastern voyagers go off dancing, like Papageno and the Moorish King in The Magic Flute. \"Last two syllables,\" roars the head.\n\nThe last act opens. It is a Grecian tent this time. A tall and stalwart man reposes on a couch there. Above him hang his helmet and shield. There is no need for them now. Ilium is down. Iphigenia is slain. Cassandra is a prisoner in his outer halls. The king of men (it is Colonel Crawley, who, indeed, has no notion about the sack of Ilium or the conquest of Cassandra), the anax andron is asleep in his chamber at Argos. A lamp casts the broad shadow of the sleeping warrior flickering on the wall--the sword and shield of Troy glitter in its light. The band plays the awful music of Don Juan, before the statue enters.\n\nAegisthus steals in pale and on tiptoe. What is that ghastly face looking out balefully after him from behind the arras? He raises his dagger to strike the sleeper, who turns in his bed, and opens his broad chest as if for the blow. He cannot strike the noble slumbering chieftain. Clytemnestra glides swiftly into the room like an apparition--her arms are bare and white--her tawny hair floats down her shoulders--her face is deadly pale--and her eyes are lighted up with a smile so ghastly that people quake as they look at her.\n\nA tremor ran through the room. \"Good God!\" somebody said, \"it's Mrs. Rawdon Crawley.\"\n\nScornfully she snatches the dagger out of Aegisthus's hand and advances to the bed. You see it shining over her head in the glimmer of the lamp, and--and the lamp goes out, with a groan, and all is dark.\n\nThe darkness and the scene frightened people. Rebecca performed her part so well, and with such ghastly truth, that the spectators were all dumb, until, with a burst, all the lamps of the hall blazed out again, when everybody began to shout applause. \"Brava! brava!\" old Steyne's strident voice was heard roaring over all the rest. \"By--, she'd do it too,\" he said between his teeth. The performers were called by the whole house, which sounded with cries of \"Manager! Clytemnestra!\" Agamemnon could not be got to show in his classical tunic, but stood in the background with Aegisthus and others of the performers of the little play. Mr. Bedwin Sands led on Zuleikah and Clytemnestra. A great personage insisted on being presented to the charming Clytemnestra. \"Heigh ha? Run him through the body. Marry somebody else, hay?\" was the apposite remark made by His Royal Highness.\n\n\"Mrs. Rawdon Crawley was quite killing in the part,\" said Lord Steyne. Becky laughed, gay and saucy looking, and swept the prettiest little curtsey ever seen.\n\nServants brought in salvers covered with numerous cool dainties, and the performers disappeared to get ready for the second charade-tableau.\n\nThe three syllables of this charade were to be depicted in pantomime, and the performance took place in the following wise:\n\nFirst syllable. Colonel Rawdon Crawley, C.B., with a slouched hat and a staff, a great-coat, and a lantern borrowed from the stables, passed across the stage bawling out, as if warning the inhabitants of the hour. In the lower window are seen two bagmen playing apparently at the game of cribbage, over which they yawn much. To them enters one looking like Boots (the Honourable G. Ringwood), which character the young gentleman performed to perfection, and divests them of their lower coverings; and presently Chambermaid (the Right Honourable Lord Southdown) with two candlesticks, and a warming-pan. She ascends to the upper apartment and warms the bed. She uses the warming-pan as a weapon wherewith she wards off the attention of the bagmen. She exits. They put on their night-caps and pull down the blinds. Boots comes out and closes the shutters of the ground-floor chamber. You hear him bolting and chaining the door within. All the lights go out. The music plays Dormez, dormez, chers Amours. A voice from behind the curtain says, \"First syllable.\"\n\nSecond syllable. The lamps are lighted up all of a sudden. The music plays the old air from John of Paris, Ah quel plaisir d'etre en voyage. It is the same scene. Between the first and second floors of the house represented, you behold a sign on which the Steyne arms are painted. All the bells are ringing all over the house. In the lower apartment you see a man with a long slip of paper presenting it to another, who shakes his fists, threatens and vows that it is monstrous. \"Ostler, bring round my gig,\" cries another at the door. He chucks Chambermaid (the Right Honourable Lord Southdown) under the chin; she seems to deplore his absence, as Calypso did that of that other eminent traveller Ulysses. Boots (the Honourable G. Ringwood) passes with a wooden box, containing silver flagons, and cries \"Pots\" with such exquisite humour and naturalness that the whole house rings with applause, and a bouquet is thrown to him. Crack, crack, crack, go the whips. Landlord, chambermaid, waiter rush to the door, but just as some distinguished guest is arriving, the curtains close, and the invisible theatrical manager cries out \"Second syllable.\"\n\n\"I think it must be 'Hotel,'\" says Captain Grigg of the Life Guards; there is a general laugh at the Captain's cleverness. He is not very far from the mark.\n\nWhile the third syllable is in preparation, the band begins a nautical medley--\"All in the Downs,\" \"Cease Rude Boreas,\" \"Rule Britannia,\" \"In the Bay of Biscay O!\"--some maritime event is about to take place. A bell is heard ringing as the curtain draws aside. \"Now, gents, for the shore!\" a voice exclaims. People take leave of each other. They point anxiously as if towards the clouds, which are represented by a dark curtain, and they nod their heads in fear. Lady Squeams (the Right Honourable Lord Southdown), her lap-dog, her bags, reticules, and husband sit down, and cling hold of some ropes. It is evidently a ship.\n\nThe Captain (Colonel Crawley, C.B.), with a cocked hat and a telescope, comes in, holding his hat on his head, and looks out; his coat tails fly about as if in the wind. When he leaves go of his hat to use his telescope, his hat flies off, with immense applause. It is blowing fresh. The music rises and whistles louder and louder; the mariners go across the stage staggering, as if the ship was in severe motion. The Steward (the Honourable G. Ringwood) passes reeling by, holding six basins. He puts one rapidly by Lord Squeams--Lady Squeams, giving a pinch to her dog, which begins to howl piteously, puts her pocket-handkerchief to her face, and rushes away as for the cabin. The music rises up to the wildest pitch of stormy excitement, and the third syllable is concluded.\n\nThere was a little ballet, \"Le Rossignol,\" in which Montessu and Noblet used to be famous in those days, and which Mr. Wagg transferred to the English stage as an opera, putting his verse, of which he was a skilful writer, to the pretty airs of the ballet. It was dressed in old French costume, and little Lord Southdown now appeared admirably attired in the disguise of an old woman hobbling about the stage with a faultless crooked stick.\n\nTrills of melody were heard behind the scenes, and gurgling from a sweet pasteboard cottage covered with roses and trellis work. \"Philomele, Philomele,\" cries the old woman, and Philomele comes out.\n\nMore applause--it is Mrs. Rawdon Crawley in powder and patches, the most ravissante little Marquise in the world.\n\nShe comes in laughing, humming, and frisks about the stage with all the innocence of theatrical youth--she makes a curtsey. Mamma says \"Why, child, you are always laughing and singing,\" and away she goes, with--\n\n# THE ROSE UPON MY BALCONY\n\nThe rose upon my balcony the morning air perfuming Was leafless all the winter time and pining for the spring; You ask me why her breath is sweet and why her cheek is blooming, It is because the sun is out and birds begin to sing.\n\nThe nightingale, whose melody is through the greenwood ringing, Was silent when the boughs were bare and winds were blowing keen: And if, Mamma, you ask of me the reason of his singing, It is because the sun is out and all the leaves are green.\n\nThus each performs his part, Mamma, the birds have found their voices, The blowing rose a flush, Mamma, her bonny cheek to dye; And there's sunshine in my heart, Mamma, which wakens and rejoices, And so I sing and blush, Mamma, and that's the reason why.\n\nDuring the intervals of the stanzas of this ditty, the good-natured personage addressed as Mamma by the singer, and whose large whiskers appeared under her cap, seemed very anxious to exhibit her maternal affection by embracing the innocent creature who performed the daughter's part. Every caress was received with loud acclamations of laughter by the sympathizing audience. At its conclusion (while the music was performing a symphony as if ever so many birds were warbling) the whole house was unanimous for an encore: and applause and bouquets without end were showered upon the Nightingale of the evening. Lord Steyne's voice of applause was loudest of all. Becky, the nightingale, took the flowers which he threw to her and pressed them to her heart with the air of a consummate comedian. Lord Steyne was frantic with delight. His guests' enthusiasm harmonized with his own. Where was the beautiful black-eyed Houri whose appearance in the first charade had caused such delight? She was twice as handsome as Becky, but the brilliancy of the latter had quite eclipsed her. All voices were for her. Stephens, Caradori, Ronzi de Begnis, people compared her to one or the other, and agreed with good reason, very likely, that had she been an actress none on the stage could have surpassed her. She had reached her culmination: her voice rose trilling and bright over the storm of applause, and soared as high and joyful as her triumph. There was a ball after the dramatic entertainments, and everybody pressed round Becky as the great point of attraction of the evening. The Royal Personage declared with an oath that she was perfection, and engaged her again and again in conversation. Little Becky's soul swelled with pride and delight at these honours; she saw fortune, fame, fashion before her. Lord Steyne was her slave, followed her everywhere, and scarcely spoke to any one in the room beside, and paid her the most marked compliments and attention. She still appeared in her Marquise costume and danced a minuet with Monsieur de Truffigny, Monsieur Le Duc de la Jabotiere's attache; and the Duke, who had all the traditions of the ancient court, pronounced that Madame Crawley was worthy to have been a pupil of Vestris, or to have figured at Versailles. Only a feeling of dignity, the gout, and the strongest sense of duty and personal sacrifice prevented his Excellency from dancing with her himself, and he declared in public that a lady who could talk and dance like Mrs. Rawdon was fit to be ambassadress at any court in Europe. He was only consoled when he heard that she was half a Frenchwoman by birth. \"None but a compatriot,\" his Excellency declared, \"could have performed that majestic dance in such a way.\"\n\nThen she figured in a waltz with Monsieur de Klingenspohr, the Prince of Peterwaradin's cousin and attache. The delighted Prince, having less retenue than his French diplomatic colleague, insisted upon taking a turn with the charming creature, and twirled round the ball-room with her, scattering the diamonds out of his boot-tassels and hussar jacket until his Highness was fairly out of breath. Papoosh Pasha himself would have liked to dance with her if that amusement had been the custom of his country. The company made a circle round her and applauded as wildly as if she had been a Noblet or a Taglioni. Everybody was in ecstacy; and Becky too, you may be sure. She passed by Lady Stunnington with a look of scorn. She patronized Lady Gaunt and her astonished and mortified sister-in-law--she ecrased all rival charmers. As for poor Mrs. Winkworth, and her long hair and great eyes, which had made such an effect at the commencement of the evening--where was she now? Nowhere in the race. She might tear her long hair and cry her great eyes out, but there was not a person to heed or to deplore the discomfiture.\n\nThe greatest triumph of all was at supper time. She was placed at the grand exclusive table with his Royal Highness the exalted personage before mentioned, and the rest of the great guests. She was served on gold plate. She might have had pearls melted into her champagne if she liked--another Cleopatra--and the potentate of Peterwaradin would have given half the brilliants off his jacket for a kind glance from those dazzling eyes. Jabotiere wrote home about her to his government. The ladies at the other tables, who supped off mere silver and marked Lord Steyne's constant attention to her, vowed it was a monstrous infatuation, a gross insult to ladies of rank. If sarcasm could have killed, Lady Stunnington would have slain her on the spot.\n\nRawdon Crawley was scared at these triumphs. They seemed to separate his wife farther than ever from him somehow. He thought with a feeling very like pain how immeasurably she was his superior.\n\nWhen the hour of departure came, a crowd of young men followed her to her carriage, for which the people without bawled, the cry being caught up by the link-men who were stationed outside the tall gates of Gaunt House, congratulating each person who issued from the gate and hoping his Lordship had enjoyed this noble party.\n\nMrs. Rawdon Crawley's carriage, coming up to the gate after due shouting, rattled into the illuminated court-yard and drove up to the covered way. Rawdon put his wife into the carriage, which drove off. Mr. Wenham had proposed to him to walk home, and offered the Colonel the refreshment of a cigar.\n\nThey lighted their cigars by the lamp of one of the many link-boys outside, and Rawdon walked on with his friend Wenham. Two persons separated from the crowd and followed the two gentlemen; and when they had walked down Gaunt Square a few score of paces, one of the men came up and, touching Rawdon on the shoulder, said, \"Beg your pardon, Colonel, I vish to speak to you most particular.\" This gentleman's acquaintance gave a loud whistle as the latter spoke, at which signal a cab came clattering up from those stationed at the gate of Gaunt House--and the aide-de-camp ran round and placed himself in front of Colonel Crawley.\n\nThat gallant officer at once knew what had befallen him. He was in the hands of the bailiffs. He started back, falling against the man who had first touched him.\n\n\"We're three on us--it's no use bolting,\" the man behind said.\n\n\"It's you, Moss, is it?\" said the Colonel, who appeared to know his interlocutor. \"How much is it?\"\n\n\"Only a small thing,\" whispered Mr. Moss, of Cursitor Street, Chancery Lane, and assistant officer to the Sheriff of Middlesex--\"One hundred and sixty-six, six and eight-pence, at the suit of Mr. Nathan.\"\n\n\"Lend me a hundred, Wenham, for God's sake,\" poor Rawdon said--\"I've got seventy at home.\"\n\n\"I've not got ten pounds in the world,\" said poor Mr. Wenham--\"Good night, my dear fellow.\"\n\n\"Good night,\" said Rawdon ruefully. And Wenham walked away--and Rawdon Crawley finished his cigar as the cab drove under Temple Bar.\n\nIn Which Lord Steyne Shows Himself in a Most Amiable Light\nWhen Lord Steyne was benevolently disposed, he did nothing by halves, and his kindness towards the Crawley family did the greatest honour to his benevolent discrimination. His lordship extended his good-will to little Rawdon: he pointed out to the boy's parents the necessity of sending him to a public school, that he was of an age now when emulation, the first principles of the Latin language, pugilistic exercises, and the society of his fellow-boys would be of the greatest benefit to the boy. His father objected that he was not rich enough to send the child to a good public school; his mother that Briggs was a capital mistress for him, and had brought him on (as indeed was the fact) famously in English, the Latin rudiments, and in general learning: but all these objections disappeared before the generous perseverance of the Marquis of Steyne. His lordship was one of the governors of that famous old collegiate institution called the Whitefriars. It had been a Cistercian Convent in old days, when the Smithfield, which is contiguous to it, was a tournament ground. Obstinate heretics used to be brought thither convenient for burning hard by. Henry VIII, the Defender of the Faith, seized upon the monastery and its possessions and hanged and tortured some of the monks who could not accommodate themselves to the pace of his reform. Finally, a great merchant bought the house and land adjoining, in which, and with the help of other wealthy endowments of land and money, he established a famous foundation hospital for old men and children. An extern school grew round the old almost monastic foundation, which subsists still with its middle-age costume and usages--and all Cistercians pray that it may long flourish.\n\nOf this famous house, some of the greatest noblemen, prelates, and dignitaries in England are governors: and as the boys are very comfortably lodged, fed, and educated, and subsequently inducted to good scholarships at the University and livings in the Church, many little gentlemen are devoted to the ecclesiastical profession from their tenderest years, and there is considerable emulation to procure nominations for the foundation. It was originally intended for the sons of poor and deserving clerics and laics, but many of the noble governors of the Institution, with an enlarged and rather capricious benevolence, selected all sorts of objects for their bounty. To get an education for nothing, and a future livelihood and profession assured, was so excellent a scheme that some of the richest people did not disdain it; and not only great men's relations, but great men themselves, sent their sons to profit by the chance--Right Rev. prelates sent their own kinsmen or the sons of their clergy, while, on the other hand, some great noblemen did not disdain to patronize the children of their confidential servants--so that a lad entering this establishment had every variety of youthful society wherewith to mingle.\n\nRawdon Crawley, though the only book which he studied was the Racing Calendar, and though his chief recollections of polite learning were connected with the floggings which he received at Eton in his early youth, had that decent and honest reverence for classical learning which all English gentlemen feel, and was glad to think that his son was to have a provision for life, perhaps, and a certain opportunity of becoming a scholar. And although his boy was his chief solace and companion, and endeared to him by a thousand small ties, about which he did not care to speak to his wife, who had all along shown the utmost indifference to their son, yet Rawdon agreed at once to part with him and to give up his own greatest comfort and benefit for the sake of the welfare of the little lad. He did not know how fond he was of the child until it became necessary to let him go away. When he was gone, he felt more sad and downcast than he cared to own--far sadder than the boy himself, who was happy enough to enter a new career and find companions of his own age. Becky burst out laughing once or twice when the Colonel, in his clumsy, incoherent way, tried to express his sentimental sorrows at the boy's departure. The poor fellow felt that his dearest pleasure and closest friend was taken from him. He looked often and wistfully at the little vacant bed in his dressing-room, where the child used to sleep. He missed him sadly of mornings and tried in vain to walk in the park without him. He did not know how solitary he was until little Rawdon was gone. He liked the people who were fond of him, and would go and sit for long hours with his good-natured sister Lady Jane, and talk to her about the virtues, and good looks, and hundred good qualities of the child.\n\nYoung Rawdon's aunt, we have said, was very fond of him, as was her little girl, who wept copiously when the time for her cousin's departure came. The elder Rawdon was thankful for the fondness of mother and daughter. The very best and honestest feelings of the man came out in these artless outpourings of paternal feeling in which he indulged in their presence, and encouraged by their sympathy. He secured not only Lady Jane's kindness, but her sincere regard, by the feelings which he manifested, and which he could not show to his own wife. The two kinswomen met as seldom as possible. Becky laughed bitterly at Jane's feelings and softness; the other's kindly and gentle nature could not but revolt at her sister's callous behaviour.\n\nIt estranged Rawdon from his wife more than he knew or acknowledged to himself. She did not care for the estrangement. Indeed, she did not miss him or anybody. She looked upon him as her errand-man and humble slave. He might be ever so depressed or sulky, and she did not mark his demeanour, or only treated it with a sneer. She was busy thinking about her position, or her pleasures, or her advancement in society; she ought to have held a great place in it, that is certain.\n\nIt was honest Briggs who made up the little kit for the boy which he was to take to school. Molly, the housemaid, blubbered in the passage when he went away--Molly kind and faithful in spite of a long arrear of unpaid wages. Mrs. Becky could not let her husband have the carriage to take the boy to school. Take the horses into the City!--such a thing was never heard of. Let a cab be brought. She did not offer to kiss him when he went, nor did the child propose to embrace her; but gave a kiss to old Briggs (whom, in general, he was very shy of caressing), and consoled her by pointing out that he was to come home on Saturdays, when she would have the benefit of seeing him. As the cab rolled towards the City, Becky's carriage rattled off to the park. She was chattering and laughing with a score of young dandies by the Serpentine as the father and son entered at the old gates of the school--where Rawdon left the child and came away with a sadder purer feeling in his heart than perhaps that poor battered fellow had ever known since he himself came out of the nursery.\n\nHe walked all the way home very dismally, and dined alone with Briggs. He was very kind to her and grateful for her love and watchfulness over the boy. His conscience smote him that he had borrowed Briggs's money and aided in deceiving her. They talked about little Rawdon a long time, for Becky only came home to dress and go out to dinner--and then he went off uneasily to drink tea with Lady Jane, and tell her of what had happened, and how little Rawdon went off like a trump, and how he was to wear a gown and little knee-breeches, and how young Blackball, Jack Blackball's son, of the old regiment, had taken him in charge and promised to be kind to him.\n\nIn the course of a week, young Blackball had constituted little Rawdon his fag, shoe-black, and breakfast toaster; initiated him into the mysteries of the Latin Grammar; and thrashed him three or four times, but not severely. The little chap's good-natured honest face won his way for him. He only got that degree of beating which was, no doubt, good for him; and as for blacking shoes, toasting bread, and fagging in general, were these offices not deemed to be necessary parts of every young English gentleman's education?\n\nOur business does not lie with the second generation and Master Rawdon's life at school, otherwise the present tale might be carried to any indefinite length. The Colonel went to see his son a short time afterwards and found the lad sufficiently well and happy, grinning and laughing in his little black gown and little breeches.\n\nHis father sagaciously tipped Blackball, his master, a sovereign, and secured that young gentleman's good-will towards his fag. As a protege of the great Lord Steyne, the nephew of a County member, and son of a Colonel and C.B., whose name appeared in some of the most fashionable parties in the Morning Post, perhaps the school authorities were disposed not to look unkindly on the child. He had plenty of pocket-money, which he spent in treating his comrades royally to raspberry tarts, and he was often allowed to come home on Saturdays to his father, who always made a jubilee of that day. When free, Rawdon would take him to the play, or send him thither with the footman; and on Sundays he went to church with Briggs and Lady Jane and his cousins. Rawdon marvelled over his stories about school, and fights, and fagging. Before long, he knew the names of all the masters and the principal boys as well as little Rawdon himself. He invited little Rawdon's crony from school, and made both the children sick with pastry, and oysters, and porter after the play. He tried to look knowing over the Latin grammar when little Rawdon showed him what part of that work he was \"in.\" \"Stick to it, my boy,\" he said to him with much gravity, \"there's nothing like a good classical education! Nothing!\"\n\nBecky's contempt for her husband grew greater every day. \"Do what you like--dine where you please--go and have ginger-beer and sawdust at Astley's, or psalm-singing with Lady Jane--only don't expect me to busy myself with the boy. I have your interests to attend to, as you can't attend to them yourself. I should like to know where you would have been now, and in what sort of a position in society, if I had not looked after you.\" Indeed, nobody wanted poor old Rawdon at the parties whither Becky used to go. She was often asked without him now. She talked about great people as if she had the fee-simple of May Fair, and when the Court went into mourning, she always wore black.\n\nLittle Rawdon being disposed of, Lord Steyne, who took such a parental interest in the affairs of this amiable poor family, thought that their expenses might be very advantageously curtailed by the departure of Miss Briggs, and that Becky was quite clever enough to take the management of her own house. It has been narrated in a former chapter how the benevolent nobleman had given his protegee money to pay off her little debt to Miss Briggs, who however still remained behind with her friends; whence my lord came to the painful conclusion that Mrs. Crawley had made some other use of the money confided to her than that for which her generous patron had given the loan. However, Lord Steyne was not so rude as to impart his suspicions upon this head to Mrs. Becky, whose feelings might be hurt by any controversy on the money-question, and who might have a thousand painful reasons for disposing otherwise of his lordship's generous loan. But he determined to satisfy himself of the real state of the case, and instituted the necessary inquiries in a most cautious and delicate manner.\n\nIn the first place he took an early opportunity of pumping Miss Briggs. That was not a difficult operation. A very little encouragement would set that worthy woman to talk volubly and pour out all within her. And one day when Mrs. Rawdon had gone out to drive (as Mr. Fiche, his lordship's confidential servant, easily learned at the livery stables where the Crawleys kept their carriage and horses, or rather, where the livery-man kept a carriage and horses for Mr. and Mrs. Crawley)--my lord dropped in upon the Curzon Street house--asked Briggs for a cup of coffee--told her that he had good accounts of the little boy at school--and in five minutes found out from her that Mrs. Rawdon had given her nothing except a black silk gown, for which Miss Briggs was immensely grateful.\n\nHe laughed within himself at this artless story. For the truth is, our dear friend Rebecca had given him a most circumstantial narration of Briggs's delight at receiving her money--eleven hundred and twenty-five pounds--and in what securities she had invested it; and what a pang Becky herself felt in being obliged to pay away such a delightful sum of money. \"Who knows,\" the dear woman may have thought within herself, \"perhaps he may give me a little more?\" My lord, however, made no such proposal to the little schemer--very likely thinking that he had been sufficiently generous already.\n\nHe had the curiosity, then, to ask Miss Briggs about the state of her private affairs--and she told his lordship candidly what her position was--how Miss Crawley had left her a legacy--how her relatives had had part of it--how Colonel Crawley had put out another portion, for which she had the best security and interest--and how Mr. and Mrs. Rawdon had kindly busied themselves with Sir Pitt, who was to dispose of the remainder most advantageously for her, when he had time. My lord asked how much the Colonel had already invested for her, and Miss Briggs at once and truly told him that the sum was six hundred and odd pounds.\n\nBut as soon as she had told her story, the voluble Briggs repented of her frankness and besought my lord not to tell Mr. Crawley of the confessions which she had made. \"The Colonel was so kind--Mr. Crawley might be offended and pay back the money, for which she could get no such good interest anywhere else.\" Lord Steyne, laughing, promised he never would divulge their conversation, and when he and Miss Briggs parted he laughed still more.\n\n\"What an accomplished little devil it is!\" thought he. \"What a splendid actress and manager! She had almost got a second supply out of me the other day; with her coaxing ways. She beats all the women I have ever seen in the course of all my well-spent life. They are babies compared to her. I am a greenhorn myself, and a fool in her hands--an old fool. She is unsurpassable in lies.\" His lordship's admiration for Becky rose immeasurably at this proof of her cleverness. Getting the money was nothing--but getting double the sum she wanted, and paying nobody--it was a magnificent stroke. And Crawley, my lord thought--Crawley is not such a fool as he looks and seems. He has managed the matter cleverly enough on his side. Nobody would ever have supposed from his face and demeanour that he knew anything about this money business; and yet he put her up to it, and has spent the money, no doubt. In this opinion my lord, we know, was mistaken, but it influenced a good deal his behaviour towards Colonel Crawley, whom he began to treat with even less than that semblance of respect which he had formerly shown towards that gentleman. It never entered into the head of Mrs. Crawley's patron that the little lady might be making a purse for herself; and, perhaps, if the truth must be told, he judged of Colonel Crawley by his experience of other husbands, whom he had known in the course of the long and well-spent life which had made him acquainted with a great deal of the weakness of mankind. My lord had bought so many men during his life that he was surely to be pardoned for supposing that he had found the price of this one.\n\nHe taxed Becky upon the point on the very first occasion when he met her alone, and he complimented her, good-humouredly, on her cleverness in getting more than the money which she required. Becky was only a little taken aback. It was not the habit of this dear creature to tell falsehoods, except when necessity compelled, but in these great emergencies it was her practice to lie very freely; and in an instant she was ready with another neat plausible circumstantial story which she administered to her patron. The previous statement which she had made to him was a falsehood--a wicked falsehood--she owned it. But who had made her tell it? \"Ah, my Lord,\" she said, \"you don't know all I have to suffer and bear in silence; you see me gay and happy before you--you little know what I have to endure when there is no protector near me. It was my husband, by threats and the most savage treatment, forced me to ask for that sum about which I deceived you. It was he who, foreseeing that questions might be asked regarding the disposal of the money, forced me to account for it as I did. He took the money. He told me he had paid Miss Briggs; I did not want, I did not dare to doubt him. Pardon the wrong which a desperate man is forced to commit, and pity a miserable, miserable woman.\" She burst into tears as she spoke. Persecuted virtue never looked more bewitchingly wretched.\n\nThey had a long conversation, driving round and round the Regent's Park in Mrs. Crawley's carriage together, a conversation of which it is not necessary to repeat the details, but the upshot of it was that, when Becky came home, she flew to her dear Briggs with a smiling face and announced that she had some very good news for her. Lord Steyne had acted in the noblest and most generous manner. He was always thinking how and when he could do good. Now that little Rawdon was gone to school, a dear companion and friend was no longer necessary to her. She was grieved beyond measure to part with Briggs, but her means required that she should practise every retrenchment, and her sorrow was mitigated by the idea that her dear Briggs would be far better provided for by her generous patron than in her humble home. Mrs. Pilkington, the housekeeper at Gauntly Hall, was growing exceedingly old, feeble, and rheumatic: she was not equal to the work of superintending that vast mansion, and must be on the look out for a successor. It was a splendid position. The family did not go to Gauntly once in two years. At other times the housekeeper was the mistress of the magnificent mansion--had four covers daily for her table; was visited by the clergy and the most respectable people of the county--was the lady of Gauntly, in fact; and the two last housekeepers before Mrs. Pilkington had married rectors of Gauntly--but Mrs. P. could not, being the aunt of the present Rector. The place was not to be hers yet, but she might go down on a visit to Mrs. Pilkington and see whether she would like to succeed her.\n\nWhat words can paint the ecstatic gratitude of Briggs! All she stipulated for was that little Rawdon should be allowed to come down and see her at the Hall. Becky promised this--anything. She ran up to her husband when he came home and told him the joyful news. Rawdon was glad, deuced glad; the weight was off his conscience about poor Briggs's money. She was provided for, at any rate, but--but his mind was disquiet. He did not seem to be all right, somehow. He told little Southdown what Lord Steyne had done, and the young man eyed Crawley with an air which surprised the latter.\n\nHe told Lady Jane of this second proof of Steyne's bounty, and she, too, looked odd and alarmed; so did Sir Pitt. \"She is too clever and--and gay to be allowed to go from party to party without a companion,\" both said. \"You must go with her, Rawdon, wherever she goes, and you must have somebody with her--one of the girls from Queen's Crawley, perhaps, though they were rather giddy guardians for her.\"\n\nSomebody Becky should have. But in the meantime it was clear that honest Briggs must not lose her chance of settlement for life, and so she and her bags were packed, and she set off on her journey. And so two of Rawdon's out-sentinels were in the hands of the enemy.\n\nSir Pitt went and expostulated with his sister-in-law upon the subject of the dismissal of Briggs and other matters of delicate family interest. In vain she pointed out to him how necessary was the protection of Lord Steyne for her poor husband; how cruel it would be on their part to deprive Briggs of the position offered to her. Cajolements, coaxings, smiles, tears could not satisfy Sir Pitt, and he had something very like a quarrel with his once admired Becky. He spoke of the honour of the family, the unsullied reputation of the Crawleys; expressed himself in indignant tones about her receiving those young Frenchmen--those wild young men of fashion, my Lord Steyne himself, whose carriage was always at her door, who passed hours daily in her company, and whose constant presence made the world talk about her. As the head of the house he implored her to be more prudent. Society was already speaking lightly of her. Lord Steyne, though a nobleman of the greatest station and talents, was a man whose attentions would compromise any woman; he besought, he implored, he commanded his sister-in-law to be watchful in her intercourse with that nobleman.\n\nBecky promised anything and everything Pitt wanted; but Lord Steyne came to her house as often as ever, and Sir Pitt's anger increased. I wonder was Lady Jane angry or pleased that her husband at last found fault with his favourite Rebecca? Lord Steyne's visits continuing, his own ceased, and his wife was for refusing all further intercourse with that nobleman and declining the invitation to the charade-night which the marchioness sent to her; but Sir Pitt thought it was necessary to accept it, as his Royal Highness would be there.\n\nAlthough he went to the party in question, Sir Pitt quitted it very early, and his wife, too, was very glad to come away. Becky hardly so much as spoke to him or noticed her sister-in-law. Pitt Crawley declared her behaviour was monstrously indecorous, reprobated in strong terms the habit of play-acting and fancy dressing as highly unbecoming a British female, and after the charades were over, took his brother Rawdon severely to task for appearing himself and allowing his wife to join in such improper exhibitions.\n\nRawdon said she should not join in any more such amusements--but indeed, and perhaps from hints from his elder brother and sister, he had already become a very watchful and exemplary domestic character. He left off his clubs and billiards. He never left home. He took Becky out to drive; he went laboriously with her to all her parties. Whenever my Lord Steyne called, he was sure to find the Colonel. And when Becky proposed to go out without her husband, or received invitations for herself, he peremptorily ordered her to refuse them: and there was that in the gentleman's manner which enforced obedience. Little Becky, to do her justice, was charmed with Rawdon's gallantry. If he was surly, she never was. Whether friends were present or absent, she had always a kind smile for him and was attentive to his pleasure and comfort. It was the early days of their marriage over again: the same good humour, prevenances, merriment, and artless confidence and regard. \"How much pleasanter it is,\" she would say, \"to have you by my side in the carriage than that foolish old Briggs! Let us always go on so, dear Rawdon. How nice it would be, and how happy we should always be, if we had but the money!\" He fell asleep after dinner in his chair; he did not see the face opposite to him, haggard, weary, and terrible; it lighted up with fresh candid smiles when he woke. It kissed him gaily. He wondered that he had ever had suspicions. No, he never had suspicions; all those dumb doubts and surly misgivings which had been gathering on his mind were mere idle jealousies. She was fond of him; she always had been. As for her shining in society, it was no fault of hers; she was formed to shine there. Was there any woman who could talk, or sing, or do anything like her? If she would but like the boy! Rawdon thought. But the mother and son never could be brought together.\n\nAnd it was while Rawdon's mind was agitated with these doubts and perplexities that the incident occurred which was mentioned in the last chapter, and the unfortunate Colonel found himself a prisoner away from home.\n\nA Rescue and a Catastrophe\nFriend Rawdon drove on then to Mr. Moss's mansion in Cursitor Street, and was duly inducted into that dismal place of hospitality. Morning was breaking over the cheerful house-tops of Chancery Lane as the rattling cab woke up the echoes there. A little pink-eyed Jew-boy, with a head as ruddy as the rising morn, let the party into the house, and Rawdon was welcomed to the ground-floor apartments by Mr. Moss, his travelling companion and host, who cheerfully asked him if he would like a glass of something warm after his drive.\n\nThe Colonel was not so depressed as some mortals would be, who, quitting a palace and a placens uxor, find themselves barred into a spunging-house; for, if the truth must be told, he had been a lodger at Mr. Moss's establishment once or twice before. We have not thought it necessary in the previous course of this narrative to mention these trivial little domestic incidents: but the reader may be assured that they can't unfrequently occur in the life of a man who lives on nothing a year.\n\nUpon his first visit to Mr. Moss, the Colonel, then a bachelor, had been liberated by the generosity of his aunt; on the second mishap, little Becky, with the greatest spirit and kindness, had borrowed a sum of money from Lord Southdown and had coaxed her husband's creditor (who was her shawl, velvet-gown, lace pocket-handkerchief, trinket, and gim-crack purveyor, indeed) to take a portion of the sum claimed and Rawdon's promissory note for the remainder: so on both these occasions the capture and release had been conducted with the utmost gallantry on all sides, and Moss and the Colonel were therefore on the very best of terms.\n\n\"You'll find your old bed, Colonel, and everything comfortable,\" that gentleman said, \"as I may honestly say. You may be pretty sure its kep aired, and by the best of company, too. It was slep in the night afore last by the Honorable Capting Famish, of the Fiftieth Dragoons, whose Mar took him out, after a fortnight, jest to punish him, she said. But, Law bless you, I promise you, he punished my champagne, and had a party ere every night--reglar tip-top swells, down from the clubs and the West End--Capting Ragg, the Honorable Deuceace, who lives in the Temple, and some fellers as knows a good glass of wine, I warrant you. I've got a Doctor of Diwinity upstairs, five gents in the coffee-room, and Mrs. Moss has a tably-dy-hoty at half-past five, and a little cards or music afterwards, when we shall be most happy to see you.\"\n\n\"I'll ring when I want anything,\" said Rawdon and went quietly to his bedroom. He was an old soldier, we have said, and not to be disturbed by any little shocks of fate. A weaker man would have sent off a letter to his wife on the instant of his capture. \"But what is the use of disturbing her night's rest?\" thought Rawdon. \"She won't know whether I am in my room or not. It will be time enough to write to her when she has had her sleep out, and I have had mine. It's only a hundred-and-seventy, and the deuce is in it if we can't raise that.\" And so, thinking about little Rawdon (whom he would not have know that he was in such a queer place), the Colonel turned into the bed lately occupied by Captain Famish and fell asleep. It was ten o'clock when he woke up, and the ruddy-headed youth brought him, with conscious pride, a fine silver dressing-case, wherewith he might perform the operation of shaving. Indeed Mr. Moss's house, though somewhat dirty, was splendid throughout. There were dirty trays, and wine-coolers en permanence on the sideboard, huge dirty gilt cornices, with dingy yellow satin hangings to the barred windows which looked into Cursitor Street--vast and dirty gilt picture frames surrounding pieces sporting and sacred, all of which works were by the greatest masters--and fetched the greatest prices, too, in the bill transactions, in the course of which they were sold and bought over and over again. The Colonel's breakfast was served to him in the same dingy and gorgeous plated ware. Miss Moss, a dark-eyed maid in curl-papers, appeared with the teapot, and, smiling, asked the Colonel how he had slep? And she brought him in the Morning Post, with the names of all the great people who had figured at Lord Steyne's entertainment the night before. It contained a brilliant account of the festivities and of the beautiful and accomplished Mrs. Rawdon Crawley's admirable personifications.\n\nAfter a lively chat with this lady (who sat on the edge of the breakfast table in an easy attitude displaying the drapery of her stocking and an ex-white satin shoe, which was down at heel), Colonel Crawley called for pens and ink, and paper, and being asked how many sheets, chose one which was brought to him between Miss Moss's own finger and thumb. Many a sheet had that dark-eyed damsel brought in; many a poor fellow had scrawled and blotted hurried lines of entreaty and paced up and down that awful room until his messenger brought back the reply. Poor men always use messengers instead of the post. Who has not had their letters, with the wafers wet, and the announcement that a person is waiting in the hall?\n\nNow on the score of his application, Rawdon had not many misgivings.\n\nDEAR BECKY, (Rawdon wrote)\n\nI HOPE YOU SLEPT WELL. Don't be FRIGHTENED if I don't bring you in your COFFY. Last night as I was coming home smoaking, I met with an ACCADENT. I was NABBED by Moss of Cursitor Street--from whose GILT AND SPLENDID PARLER I write this--the same that had me this time two years. Miss Moss brought in my tea--she is grown very FAT, and, as usual, had her STOCKENS DOWN AT HEAL.\n\nIt's Nathan's business--a hundred-and-fifty--with costs, hundred-and-seventy. Please send me my desk and some CLOTHS--I'm in pumps and a white tye (something like Miss M's stockings)--I've seventy in it. And as soon as you get this, Drive to Nathan's--offer him seventy-five down, and ASK HIM TO RENEW--say I'll take wine--we may as well have some dinner sherry; but not PICTURS, they're too dear.\n\nIf he won't stand it. Take my ticker and such of your things as you can SPARE, and send them to Balls--we must, of coarse, have the sum to-night. It won't do to let it stand over, as to-morrow's Sunday; the beds here are not very CLEAN, and there may be other things out against me--I'm glad it an't Rawdon's Saturday for coming home. God bless you.\n\nYours in haste, R. C. P.S. Make haste and come.\n\nThis letter, sealed with a wafer, was dispatched by one of the messengers who are always hanging about Mr. Moss's establishment, and Rawdon, having seen him depart, went out in the court-yard and smoked his cigar with a tolerably easy mind--in spite of the bars overhead--for Mr. Moss's court-yard is railed in like a cage, lest the gentlemen who are boarding with him should take a fancy to escape from his hospitality.\n\nThree hours, he calculated, would be the utmost time required, before Becky should arrive and open his prison doors, and he passed these pretty cheerfully in smoking, in reading the paper, and in the coffee-room with an acquaintance, Captain Walker, who happened to be there, and with whom he cut for sixpences for some hours, with pretty equal luck on either side.\n\nBut the day passed away and no messenger returned--no Becky. Mr. Moss's tably-dy-hoty was served at the appointed hour of half-past five, when such of the gentlemen lodging in the house as could afford to pay for the banquet came and partook of it in the splendid front parlour before described, and with which Mr. Crawley's temporary lodging communicated, when Miss M. (Miss Hem, as her papa called her) appeared without the curl-papers of the morning, and Mrs. Hem did the honours of a prime boiled leg of mutton and turnips, of which the Colonel ate with a very faint appetite. Asked whether he would \"stand\" a bottle of champagne for the company, he consented, and the ladies drank to his 'ealth, and Mr. Moss, in the most polite manner, \"looked towards him.\"\n\nIn the midst of this repast, however, the doorbell was heard--young Moss of the ruddy hair rose up with the keys and answered the summons, and coming back, told the Colonel that the messenger had returned with a bag, a desk and a letter, which he gave him. \"No ceramony, Colonel, I beg,\" said Mrs. Moss with a wave of her hand, and he opened the letter rather tremulously. It was a beautiful letter, highly scented, on a pink paper, and with a light green seal.\n\nMON PAUVRE CHER PETIT, (Mrs. Crawley wrote)\n\nI could not sleep ONE WINK for thinking of what had become of my odious old monstre, and only got to rest in the morning after sending for Mr. Blench (for I was in a fever), who gave me a composing draught and left orders with Finette that I should be disturbed ON NO ACCOUNT. So that my poor old man's messenger, who had bien mauvaise mine Finette says, and sentoit le Genievre, remained in the hall for some hours waiting my bell. You may fancy my state when I read your poor dear old ill-spelt letter.\n\nIll as I was, I instantly called for the carriage, and as soon as I was dressed (though I couldn't drink a drop of chocolate--I assure you I couldn't without my monstre to bring it to me), I drove ventre a terre to Nathan's. I saw him--I wept--I cried--I fell at his odious knees. Nothing would mollify the horrid man. He would have all the money, he said, or keep my poor monstre in prison. I drove home with the intention of paying that triste visite chez mon oncle (when every trinket I have should be at your disposal though they would not fetch a hundred pounds, for some, you know, are with ce cher oncle already), and found Milor there with the Bulgarian old sheep-faced monster, who had come to compliment me upon last night's performances. Paddington came in, too, drawling and lisping and twiddling his hair; so did Champignac, and his chef--everybody with foison of compliments and pretty speeches--plaguing poor me, who longed to be rid of them, and was thinking every moment of the time of mon pauvre prisonnier.\n\nWhen they were gone, I went down on my knees to Milor; told him we were going to pawn everything, and begged and prayed him to give me two hundred pounds. He pish'd and psha'd in a fury--told me not to be such a fool as to pawn--and said he would see whether he could lend me the money. At last he went away, promising that he would send it me in the morning: when I will bring it to my poor old monster with a kiss from his affectionate\n\nI am writing in bed. Oh I have such a headache and such a heartache!\n\nWhen Rawdon read over this letter, he turned so red and looked so savage that the company at the table d'hote easily perceived that bad news had reached him. All his suspicions, which he had been trying to banish, returned upon him. She could not even go out and sell her trinkets to free him. She could laugh and talk about compliments paid to her, whilst he was in prison. Who had put him there? Wenham had walked with him. Was there.... He could hardly bear to think of what he suspected. Leaving the room hurriedly, he ran into his own--opened his desk, wrote two hurried lines, which he directed to Sir Pitt or Lady Crawley, and bade the messenger carry them at once to Gaunt Street, bidding him to take a cab, and promising him a guinea if he was back in an hour.\n\nIn the note he besought his dear brother and sister, for the sake of God, for the sake of his dear child and his honour, to come to him and relieve him from his difficulty. He was in prison, he wanted a hundred pounds to set him free--he entreated them to come to him.\n\nHe went back to the dining-room after dispatching his messenger and called for more wine. He laughed and talked with a strange boisterousness, as the people thought. Sometimes he laughed madly at his own fears and went on drinking for an hour, listening all the while for the carriage which was to bring his fate back.\n\nAt the expiration of that time, wheels were heard whirling up to the gate--the young janitor went out with his gate-keys. It was a lady whom he let in at the bailiff's door.\n\n\"Colonel Crawley,\" she said, trembling very much. He, with a knowing look, locked the outer door upon her--then unlocked and opened the inner one, and calling out, \"Colonel, you're wanted,\" led her into the back parlour, which he occupied.\n\nRawdon came in from the dining-parlour where all those people were carousing, into his back room; a flare of coarse light following him into the apartment where the lady stood, still very nervous.\n\n\"It is I, Rawdon,\" she said in a timid voice, which she strove to render cheerful. \"It is Jane.\" Rawdon was quite overcome by that kind voice and presence. He ran up to her--caught her in his arms--gasped out some inarticulate words of thanks and fairly sobbed on her shoulder. She did not know the cause of his emotion.\n\nThe bills of Mr. Moss were quickly settled, perhaps to the disappointment of that gentleman, who had counted on having the Colonel as his guest over Sunday at least; and Jane, with beaming smiles and happiness in her eyes, carried away Rawdon from the bailiff's house, and they went homewards in the cab in which she had hastened to his release. \"Pitt was gone to a parliamentary dinner,\" she said, \"when Rawdon's note came, and so, dear Rawdon, I--I came myself\"; and she put her kind hand in his. Perhaps it was well for Rawdon Crawley that Pitt was away at that dinner. Rawdon thanked his sister a hundred times, and with an ardour of gratitude which touched and almost alarmed that soft-hearted woman. \"Oh,\" said he, in his rude, artless way, \"you--you don't know how I'm changed since I've known you, and--and little Rawdy. I--I'd like to change somehow. You see I want--I want--to be--\" He did not finish the sentence, but she could interpret it. And that night after he left her, and as she sat by her own little boy's bed, she prayed humbly for that poor way-worn sinner.\n\nRawdon left her and walked home rapidly. It was nine o'clock at night. He ran across the streets and the great squares of Vanity Fair, and at length came up breathless opposite his own house. He started back and fell against the railings, trembling as he looked up. The drawing-room windows were blazing with light. She had said that she was in bed and ill. He stood there for some time, the light from the rooms on his pale face.\n\nHe took out his door-key and let himself into the house. He could hear laughter in the upper rooms. He was in the ball-dress in which he had been captured the night before. He went silently up the stairs, leaning against the banisters at the stair-head. Nobody was stirring in the house besides--all the servants had been sent away. Rawdon heard laughter within--laughter and singing. Becky was singing a snatch of the song of the night before; a hoarse voice shouted \"Brava! Brava!\"--it was Lord Steyne's.\n\nRawdon opened the door and went in. A little table with a dinner was laid out--and wine and plate. Steyne was hanging over the sofa on which Becky sat. The wretched woman was in a brilliant full toilette, her arms and all her fingers sparkling with bracelets and rings, and the brilliants on her breast which Steyne had given her. He had her hand in his, and was bowing over it to kiss it, when Becky started up with a faint scream as she caught sight of Rawdon's white face. At the next instant she tried a smile, a horrid smile, as if to welcome her husband; and Steyne rose up, grinding his teeth, pale, and with fury in his looks.\n\nHe, too, attempted a laugh--and came forward holding out his hand. \"What, come back! How d'ye do, Crawley?\" he said, the nerves of his mouth twitching as he tried to grin at the intruder.\n\nThere was that in Rawdon's face which caused Becky to fling herself before him. \"I am innocent, Rawdon,\" she said; \"before God, I am innocent.\" She clung hold of his coat, of his hands; her own were all covered with serpents, and rings, and baubles. \"I am innocent. Say I am innocent,\" she said to Lord Steyne.\n\nHe thought a trap had been laid for him, and was as furious with the wife as with the husband. \"You innocent! Damn you,\" he screamed out. \"You innocent! Why every trinket you have on your body is paid for by me. I have given you thousands of pounds, which this fellow has spent and for which he has sold you. Innocent, by ----! You're as innocent as your mother, the ballet-girl, and your husband the bully. Don't think to frighten me as you have done others. Make way, sir, and let me pass\"; and Lord Steyne seized up his hat, and, with flame in his eyes, and looking his enemy fiercely in the face, marched upon him, never for a moment doubting that the other would give way.\n\nBut Rawdon Crawley springing out, seized him by the neckcloth, until Steyne, almost strangled, writhed and bent under his arm. \"You lie, you dog!\" said Rawdon. \"You lie, you coward and villain!\" And he struck the Peer twice over the face with his open hand and flung him bleeding to the ground. It was all done before Rebecca could interpose. She stood there trembling before him. She admired her husband, strong, brave, and victorious.\n\n\"Come here,\" he said. She came up at once.\n\n\"Take off those things.\" She began, trembling, pulling the jewels from her arms, and the rings from her shaking fingers, and held them all in a heap, quivering and looking up at him. \"Throw them down,\" he said, and she dropped them. He tore the diamond ornament out of her breast and flung it at Lord Steyne. It cut him on his bald forehead. Steyne wore the scar to his dying day.\n\n\"Come upstairs,\" Rawdon said to his wife. \"Don't kill me, Rawdon,\" she said. He laughed savagely. \"I want to see if that man lies about the money as he has about me. Has he given you any?\"\n\n\"No,\" said Rebecca, \"that is--\"\n\n\"Give me your keys,\" Rawdon answered, and they went out together.\n\nRebecca gave him all the keys but one, and she was in hopes that he would not have remarked the absence of that. It belonged to the little desk which Amelia had given her in early days, and which she kept in a secret place. But Rawdon flung open boxes and wardrobes, throwing the multifarious trumpery of their contents here and there, and at last he found the desk. The woman was forced to open it. It contained papers, love-letters many years old--all sorts of small trinkets and woman's memoranda. And it contained a pocket-book with bank-notes. Some of these were dated ten years back, too, and one was quite a fresh one--a note for a thousand pounds which Lord Steyne had given her.\n\n\"Did he give you this?\" Rawdon said.\n\n\"Yes,\" Rebecca answered.\n\n\"I'll send it to him to-day,\" Rawdon said (for day had dawned again, and many hours had passed in this search), \"and I will pay Briggs, who was kind to the boy, and some of the debts. You will let me know where I shall send the rest to you. You might have spared me a hundred pounds, Becky, out of all this--I have always shared with you.\"\n\n\"I am innocent,\" said Becky. And he left her without another word.\n\nWhat were her thoughts when he left her? She remained for hours after he was gone, the sunshine pouring into the room, and Rebecca sitting alone on the bed's edge. The drawers were all opened and their contents scattered about--dresses and feathers, scarfs and trinkets, a heap of tumbled vanities lying in a wreck. Her hair was falling over her shoulders; her gown was torn where Rawdon had wrenched the brilliants out of it. She heard him go downstairs a few minutes after he left her, and the door slamming and closing on him. She knew he would never come back. He was gone forever. Would he kill himself?--she thought--not until after he had met Lord Steyne. She thought of her long past life, and all the dismal incidents of it. Ah, how dreary it seemed, how miserable, lonely and profitless! Should she take laudanum, and end it, to have done with all hopes, schemes, debts, and triumphs? The French maid found her in this position--sitting in the midst of her miserable ruins with clasped hands and dry eyes. The woman was her accomplice and in Steyne's pay. \"Mon Dieu, madame, what has happened?\" she asked.\n\nWhat had happened? Was she guilty or not? She said not, but who could tell what was truth which came from those lips, or if that corrupt heart was in this case pure?\n\nAll her lies and her schemes, and her selfishness and her wiles, all her wit and genius had come to this bankruptcy. The woman closed the curtains and, with some entreaty and show of kindness, persuaded her mistress to lie down on the bed. Then she went below and gathered up the trinkets which had been lying on the floor since Rebecca dropped them there at her husband's orders, and Lord Steyne went away.\n\nSunday After the Battle\nThe mansion of Sir Pitt Crawley, in Great Gaunt Street, was just beginning to dress itself for the day, as Rawdon, in his evening costume, which he had now worn two days, passed by the scared female who was scouring the steps and entered into his brother's study. Lady Jane, in her morning-gown, was up and above stairs in the nursery superintending the toilettes of her children and listening to the morning prayers which the little creatures performed at her knee. Every morning she and they performed this duty privately, and before the public ceremonial at which Sir Pitt presided and at which all the people of the household were expected to assemble. Rawdon sat down in the study before the Baronet's table, set out with the orderly blue books and the letters, the neatly docketed bills and symmetrical pamphlets, the locked account-books, desks, and dispatch boxes, the Bible, the Quarterly Review, and the Court Guide, which all stood as if on parade awaiting the inspection of their chief.\n\nA book of family sermons, one of which Sir Pitt was in the habit of administering to his family on Sunday mornings, lay ready on the study table, and awaiting his judicious selection. And by the sermon-book was the Observer newspaper, damp and neatly folded, and for Sir Pitt's own private use. His gentleman alone took the opportunity of perusing the newspaper before he laid it by his master's desk. Before he had brought it into the study that morning, he had read in the journal a flaming account of \"Festivities at Gaunt House,\" with the names of all the distinguished personages invited by the Marquis of Steyne to meet his Royal Highness. Having made comments upon this entertainment to the housekeeper and her niece as they were taking early tea and hot buttered toast in the former lady's apartment, and wondered how the Rawding Crawleys could git on, the valet had damped and folded the paper once more, so that it looked quite fresh and innocent against the arrival of the master of the house.\n\nPoor Rawdon took up the paper and began to try and read it until his brother should arrive. But the print fell blank upon his eyes, and he did not know in the least what he was reading. The Government news and appointments (which Sir Pitt as a public man was bound to peruse, otherwise he would by no means permit the introduction of Sunday papers into his household), the theatrical criticisms, the fight for a hundred pounds a side between the Barking Butcher and the Tutbury Pet, the Gaunt House chronicle itself, which contained a most complimentary though guarded account of the famous charades of which Mrs. Becky had been the heroine--all these passed as in a haze before Rawdon, as he sat waiting the arrival of the chief of the family.\n\nPunctually, as the shrill-toned bell of the black marble study clock began to chime nine, Sir Pitt made his appearance, fresh, neat, smugly shaved, with a waxy clean face, and stiff shirt collar, his scanty hair combed and oiled, trimming his nails as he descended the stairs majestically, in a starched cravat and a grey flannel dressing-gown--a real old English gentleman, in a word--a model of neatness and every propriety. He started when he saw poor Rawdon in his study in tumbled clothes, with blood-shot eyes, and his hair over his face. He thought his brother was not sober, and had been out all night on some orgy. \"Good gracious, Rawdon,\" he said, with a blank face, \"what brings you here at this time of the morning? Why ain't you at home?\"\n\n\"Home,\" said Rawdon with a wild laugh. \"Don't be frightened, Pitt. I'm not drunk. Shut the door; I want to speak to you.\"\n\nPitt closed the door and came up to the table, where he sat down in the other arm-chair--that one placed for the reception of the steward, agent, or confidential visitor who came to transact business with the Baronet--and trimmed his nails more vehemently than ever.\n\n\"Pitt, it's all over with me,\" the Colonel said after a pause. \"I'm done.\"\n\n\"I always said it would come to this,\" the Baronet cried peevishly, and beating a tune with his clean-trimmed nails. \"I warned you a thousand times. I can't help you any more. Every shilling of my money is tied up. Even the hundred pounds that Jane took you last night were promised to my lawyer to-morrow morning, and the want of it will put me to great inconvenience. I don't mean to say that I won't assist you ultimately. But as for paying your creditors in full, I might as well hope to pay the National Debt. It is madness, sheer madness, to think of such a thing. You must come to a compromise. It's a painful thing for the family, but everybody does it. There was George Kitely, Lord Ragland's son, went through the Court last week, and was what they call whitewashed, I believe. Lord Ragland would not pay a shilling for him, and--\"\n\n\"It's not money I want,\" Rawdon broke in. \"I'm not come to you about myself. Never mind what happens to me.\"\n\n\"What is the matter, then?\" said Pitt, somewhat relieved.\n\n\"It's the boy,\" said Rawdon in a husky voice. \"I want you to promise me that you will take charge of him when I'm gone. That dear good wife of yours has always been good to him; and he's fonder of her than he is of his . . .--Damn it. Look here, Pitt--you know that I was to have had Miss Crawley's money. I wasn't brought up like a younger brother, but was always encouraged to be extravagant and kep idle. But for this I might have been quite a different man. I didn't do my duty with the regiment so bad. You know how I was thrown over about the money, and who got it.\"\n\n\"After the sacrifices I have made, and the manner in which I have stood by you, I think this sort of reproach is useless,\" Sir Pitt said. \"Your marriage was your own doing, not mine.\"\n\n\"That's over now,\" said Rawdon. \"That's over now.\" And the words were wrenched from him with a groan, which made his brother start.\n\n\"Good God! is she dead?\" Sir Pitt said with a voice of genuine alarm and commiseration.\n\n\"I wish I was,\" Rawdon replied. \"If it wasn't for little Rawdon I'd have cut my throat this morning--and that damned villain's too.\"\n\nSir Pitt instantly guessed the truth and surmised that Lord Steyne was the person whose life Rawdon wished to take. The Colonel told his senior briefly, and in broken accents, the circumstances of the case. \"It was a regular plan between that scoundrel and her,\" he said. \"The bailiffs were put upon me; I was taken as I was going out of his house; when I wrote to her for money, she said she was ill in bed and put me off to another day. And when I got home I found her in diamonds and sitting with that villain alone.\" He then went on to describe hurriedly the personal conflict with Lord Steyne. To an affair of that nature, of course, he said, there was but one issue, and after his conference with his brother, he was going away to make the necessary arrangements for the meeting which must ensue. \"And as it may end fatally with me,\" Rawdon said with a broken voice, \"and as the boy has no mother, I must leave him to you and Jane, Pitt--only it will be a comfort to me if you will promise me to be his friend.\"\n\nThe elder brother was much affected, and shook Rawdon's hand with a cordiality seldom exhibited by him. Rawdon passed his hand over his shaggy eyebrows. \"Thank you, brother,\" said he. \"I know I can trust your word.\"\n\n\"I will, upon my honour,\" the Baronet said. And thus, and almost mutely, this bargain was struck between them.\n\nThen Rawdon took out of his pocket the little pocket-book which he had discovered in Becky's desk, and from which he drew a bundle of the notes which it contained. \"Here's six hundred,\" he said--\"you didn't know I was so rich. I want you to give the money to Briggs, who lent it to us--and who was kind to the boy--and I've always felt ashamed of having taken the poor old woman's money. And here's some more--I've only kept back a few pounds--which Becky may as well have, to get on with.\" As he spoke he took hold of the other notes to give to his brother, but his hands shook, and he was so agitated that the pocket-book fell from him, and out of it the thousand-pound note which had been the last of the unlucky Becky's winnings.\n\nPitt stooped and picked them up, amazed at so much wealth. \"Not that,\" Rawdon said. \"I hope to put a bullet into the man whom that belongs to.\" He had thought to himself, it would be a fine revenge to wrap a ball in the note and kill Steyne with it.\n\nAfter this colloquy the brothers once more shook hands and parted. Lady Jane had heard of the Colonel's arrival, and was waiting for her husband in the adjoining dining-room, with female instinct, auguring evil. The door of the dining-room happened to be left open, and the lady of course was issuing from it as the two brothers passed out of the study. She held out her hand to Rawdon and said she was glad he was come to breakfast, though she could perceive, by his haggard unshorn face and the dark looks of her husband, that there was very little question of breakfast between them. Rawdon muttered some excuses about an engagement, squeezing hard the timid little hand which his sister-in-law reached out to him. Her imploring eyes could read nothing but calamity in his face, but he went away without another word. Nor did Sir Pitt vouchsafe her any explanation. The children came up to salute him, and he kissed them in his usual frigid manner. The mother took both of them close to herself, and held a hand of each of them as they knelt down to prayers, which Sir Pitt read to them, and to the servants in their Sunday suits or liveries, ranged upon chairs on the other side of the hissing tea-urn. Breakfast was so late that day, in consequence of the delays which had occurred, that the church-bells began to ring whilst they were sitting over their meal; and Lady Jane was too ill, she said, to go to church, though her thoughts had been entirely astray during the period of family devotion.\n\nRawdon Crawley meanwhile hurried on from Great Gaunt Street, and knocking at the great bronze Medusa's head which stands on the portal of Gaunt House, brought out the purple Silenus in a red and silver waistcoat who acts as porter of that palace. The man was scared also by the Colonel's dishevelled appearance, and barred the way as if afraid that the other was going to force it. But Colonel Crawley only took out a card and enjoined him particularly to send it in to Lord Steyne, and to mark the address written on it, and say that Colonel Crawley would be all day after one o'clock at the Regent Club in St. James's Street--not at home. The fat red-faced man looked after him with astonishment as he strode away; so did the people in their Sunday clothes who were out so early; the charity-boys with shining faces, the greengrocer lolling at his door, and the publican shutting his shutters in the sunshine, against service commenced. The people joked at the cab-stand about his appearance, as he took a carriage there, and told the driver to drive him to Knightsbridge Barracks.\n\nAll the bells were jangling and tolling as he reached that place. He might have seen his old acquaintance Amelia on her way from Brompton to Russell Square, had he been looking out. Troops of schools were on their march to church, the shiny pavement and outsides of coaches in the suburbs were thronged with people out upon their Sunday pleasure; but the Colonel was much too busy to take any heed of these phenomena, and, arriving at Knightsbridge, speedily made his way up to the room of his old friend and comrade Captain Macmurdo, who Crawley found, to his satisfaction, was in barracks.\n\nCaptain Macmurdo, a veteran officer and Waterloo man, greatly liked by his regiment, in which want of money alone prevented him from attaining the highest ranks, was enjoying the forenoon calmly in bed. He had been at a fast supper-party, given the night before by Captain the Honourable George Cinqbars, at his house in Brompton Square, to several young men of the regiment, and a number of ladies of the corps de ballet, and old Mac, who was at home with people of all ages and ranks, and consorted with generals, dog-fanciers, opera-dancers, bruisers, and every kind of person, in a word, was resting himself after the night's labours, and, not being on duty, was in bed.\n\nHis room was hung round with boxing, sporting, and dancing pictures, presented to him by comrades as they retired from the regiment, and married and settled into quiet life. And as he was now nearly fifty years of age, twenty-four of which he had passed in the corps, he had a singular museum. He was one of the best shots in England, and, for a heavy man, one of the best riders; indeed, he and Crawley had been rivals when the latter was in the Army. To be brief, Mr. Macmurdo was lying in bed, reading in Bell's Life an account of that very fight between the Tutbury Pet and the Barking Butcher, which has been before mentioned--a venerable bristly warrior, with a little close-shaved grey head, with a silk nightcap, a red face and nose, and a great dyed moustache.\n\nWhen Rawdon told the Captain he wanted a friend, the latter knew perfectly well on what duty of friendship he was called to act, and indeed had conducted scores of affairs for his acquaintances with the greatest prudence and skill. His Royal Highness the late lamented Commander-in-Chief had had the greatest regard for Macmurdo on this account, and he was the common refuge of gentlemen in trouble.\n\n\"What's the row about, Crawley, my boy?\" said the old warrior. \"No more gambling business, hay, like that when we shot Captain Marker?\"\n\n\"It's about--about my wife,\" Crawley answered, casting down his eyes and turning very red.\n\nThe other gave a whistle. \"I always said she'd throw you over,\" he began--indeed there were bets in the regiment and at the clubs regarding the probable fate of Colonel Crawley, so lightly was his wife's character esteemed by his comrades and the world; but seeing the savage look with which Rawdon answered the expression of this opinion, Macmurdo did not think fit to enlarge upon it further.\n\n\"Is there no way out of it, old boy?\" the Captain continued in a grave tone. \"Is it only suspicion, you know, or--or what is it? Any letters? Can't you keep it quiet? Best not make any noise about a thing of that sort if you can help it.\" \"Think of his only finding her out now,\" the Captain thought to himself, and remembered a hundred particular conversations at the mess-table, in which Mrs. Crawley's reputation had been torn to shreds.\n\n\"There's no way but one out of it,\" Rawdon replied--\"and there's only a way out of it for one of us, Mac--do you understand? I was put out of the way--arrested--I found 'em alone together. I told him he was a liar and a coward, and knocked him down and thrashed him.\"\n\n\"Serve him right,\" Macmurdo said. \"Who is it?\"\n\nRawdon answered it was Lord Steyne.\n\n\"The deuce! a Marquis! they said he--that is, they said you--\"\n\n\"What the devil do you mean?\" roared out Rawdon; \"do you mean that you ever heard a fellow doubt about my wife and didn't tell me, Mac?\"\n\n\"The world's very censorious, old boy,\" the other replied. \"What the deuce was the good of my telling you what any tom-fools talked about?\"\n\n\"It was damned unfriendly, Mac,\" said Rawdon, quite overcome; and, covering his face with his hands, he gave way to an emotion, the sight of which caused the tough old campaigner opposite him to wince with sympathy. \"Hold up, old boy,\" he said; \"great man or not, we'll put a bullet in him, damn him. As for women, they're all so.\"\n\n\"You don't know how fond I was of that one,\" Rawdon said, half-inarticulately. \"Damme, I followed her like a footman. I gave up everything I had to her. I'm a beggar because I would marry her. By Jove, sir, I've pawned my own watch in order to get her anything she fancied; and she--she's been making a purse for herself all the time, and grudged me a hundred pound to get me out of quod.\" He then fiercely and incoherently, and with an agitation under which his counsellor had never before seen him labour, told Macmurdo the circumstances of the story. His adviser caught at some stray hints in it. \"She may be innocent, after all,\" he said. \"She says so. Steyne has been a hundred times alone with her in the house before.\"\n\n\"It may be so,\" Rawdon answered sadly, \"but this don't look very innocent\": and he showed the Captain the thousand-pound note which he had found in Becky's pocket-book. \"This is what he gave her, Mac, and she kep it unknown to me; and with this money in the house, she refused to stand by me when I was locked up.\" The Captain could not but own that the secreting of the money had a very ugly look.\n\nWhilst they were engaged in their conference, Rawdon dispatched Captain Macmurdo's servant to Curzon Street, with an order to the domestic there to give up a bag of clothes of which the Colonel had great need. And during the man's absence, and with great labour and a Johnson's Dictionary, which stood them in much stead, Rawdon and his second composed a letter, which the latter was to send to Lord Steyne. Captain Macmurdo had the honour of waiting upon the Marquis of Steyne, on the part of Colonel Rawdon Crawley, and begged to intimate that he was empowered by the Colonel to make any arrangements for the meeting which, he had no doubt, it was his Lordship's intention to demand, and which the circumstances of the morning had rendered inevitable. Captain Macmurdo begged Lord Steyne, in the most polite manner, to appoint a friend, with whom he (Captain M.M.) might communicate, and desired that the meeting might take place with as little delay as possible.\n\nIn a postscript the Captain stated that he had in his possession a bank-note for a large amount, which Colonel Crawley had reason to suppose was the property of the Marquis of Steyne. And he was anxious, on the Colonel's behalf, to give up the note to its owner.\n\nBy the time this note was composed, the Captain's servant returned from his mission to Colonel Crawley's house in Curzon Street, but without the carpet-bag and portmanteau, for which he had been sent, and with a very puzzled and odd face.\n\n\"They won't give 'em up,\" said the man; \"there's a regular shinty in the house, and everything at sixes and sevens. The landlord's come in and took possession. The servants was a drinkin' up in the drawingroom. They said--they said you had gone off with the plate, Colonel\"--the man added after a pause--\"One of the servants is off already. And Simpson, the man as was very noisy and drunk indeed, says nothing shall go out of the house until his wages is paid up.\"\n\nThe account of this little revolution in May Fair astonished and gave a little gaiety to an otherwise very triste conversation. The two officers laughed at Rawdon's discomfiture.\n\n\"I'm glad the little 'un isn't at home,\" Rawdon said, biting his nails. \"You remember him, Mac, don't you, in the Riding School? How he sat the kicker to be sure! didn't he?\"\n\n\"That he did, old boy,\" said the good-natured Captain.\n\nLittle Rawdon was then sitting, one of fifty gown boys, in the Chapel of Whitefriars School, thinking, not about the sermon, but about going home next Saturday, when his father would certainly tip him and perhaps would take him to the play.\n\n\"He's a regular trump, that boy,\" the father went on, still musing about his son. \"I say, Mac, if anything goes wrong--if I drop--I should like you to--to go and see him, you know, and say that I was very fond of him, and that. And--dash it--old chap, give him these gold sleeve-buttons: it's all I've got.\" He covered his face with his black hands, over which the tears rolled and made furrows of white. Mr. Macmurdo had also occasion to take off his silk night-cap and rub it across his eyes.\n\n\"Go down and order some breakfast,\" he said to his man in a loud cheerful voice. \"What'll you have, Crawley? Some devilled kidneys and a herring--let's say. And, Clay, lay out some dressing things for the Colonel: we were always pretty much of a size, Rawdon, my boy, and neither of us ride so light as we did when we first entered the corps.\" With which, and leaving the Colonel to dress himself, Macmurdo turned round towards the wall, and resumed the perusal of Bell's Life, until such time as his friend's toilette was complete and he was at liberty to commence his own.\n\nThis, as he was about to meet a lord, Captain Macmurdo performed with particular care. He waxed his mustachios into a state of brilliant polish and put on a tight cravat and a trim buff waistcoat, so that all the young officers in the mess-room, whither Crawley had preceded his friend, complimented Mac on his appearance at breakfast and asked if he was going to be married that Sunday.\n\nIn Which the Same Subject is Pursued\nBecky did not rally from the state of stupor and confusion in which the events of the previous night had plunged her intrepid spirit until the bells of the Curzon Street Chapels were ringing for afternoon service, and rising from her bed she began to ply her own bell, in order to summon the French maid who had left her some hours before.\n\nMrs. Rawdon Crawley rang many times in vain; and though, on the last occasion, she rang with such vehemence as to pull down the bell-rope, Mademoiselle Fifine did not make her appearance--no, not though her mistress, in a great pet, and with the bell-rope in her hand, came out to the landing-place with her hair over her shoulders and screamed out repeatedly for her attendant.\n\nThe truth is, she had quitted the premises for many hours, and upon that permission which is called French leave among us. After picking up the trinkets in the drawing-room, Mademoiselle had ascended to her own apartments, packed and corded her own boxes there, tripped out and called a cab for herself, brought down her trunks with her own hand, and without ever so much as asking the aid of any of the other servants, who would probably have refused it, as they hated her cordially, and without wishing any one of them good-bye, had made her exit from Curzon Street.\n\nThe game, in her opinion, was over in that little domestic establishment. Fifine went off in a cab, as we have known more exalted persons of her nation to do under similar circumstances: but, more provident or lucky than these, she secured not only her own property, but some of her mistress's (if indeed that lady could be said to have any property at all)--and not only carried off the trinkets before alluded to, and some favourite dresses on which she had long kept her eye, but four richly gilt Louis Quatorze candlesticks, six gilt albums, keepsakes, and Books of Beauty, a gold enamelled snuff-box which had once belonged to Madame du Barri, and the sweetest little inkstand and mother-of-pearl blotting book, which Becky used when she composed her charming little pink notes, had vanished from the premises in Curzon Street together with Mademoiselle Fifine, and all the silver laid on the table for the little festin which Rawdon interrupted. The plated ware Mademoiselle left behind her was too cumbrous, probably for which reason, no doubt, she also left the fire irons, the chimney-glasses, and the rosewood cottage piano.\n\nA lady very like her subsequently kept a milliner's shop in the Rue du Helder at Paris, where she lived with great credit and enjoyed the patronage of my Lord Steyne. This person always spoke of England as of the most treacherous country in the world, and stated to her young pupils that she had been affreusement vole by natives of that island. It was no doubt compassion for her misfortunes which induced the Marquis of Steyne to be so very kind to Madame de Saint-Amaranthe. May she flourish as she deserves--she appears no more in our quarter of Vanity Fair.\n\nHearing a buzz and a stir below, and indignant at the impudence of those servants who would not answer her summons, Mrs. Crawley flung her morning robe round her and descended majestically to the drawing-room, whence the noise proceeded.\n\nThe cook was there with blackened face, seated on the beautiful chintz sofa by the side of Mrs. Raggles, to whom she was administering Maraschino. The page with the sugar-loaf buttons, who carried about Becky's pink notes, and jumped about her little carriage with such alacrity, was now engaged putting his fingers into a cream dish; the footman was talking to Raggles, who had a face full of perplexity and woe--and yet, though the door was open, and Becky had been screaming a half-dozen of times a few feet off, not one of her attendants had obeyed her call. \"Have a little drop, do'ee now, Mrs. Raggles,\" the cook was saying as Becky entered, the white cashmere dressing-gown flouncing around her.\n\n\"Simpson! Trotter!\" the mistress of the house cried in great wrath. \"How dare you stay here when you heard me call? How dare you sit down in my presence? Where's my maid?\" The page withdrew his fingers from his mouth with a momentary terror, but the cook took off a glass of Maraschino, of which Mrs. Raggles had had enough, staring at Becky over the little gilt glass as she drained its contents. The liquor appeared to give the odious rebel courage.\n\n\"YOUR sofy, indeed!\" Mrs. Cook said. \"I'm a settin' on Mrs. Raggles's sofy. Don't you stir, Mrs. Raggles, Mum. I'm a settin' on Mr. and Mrs. Raggles's sofy, which they bought with honest money, and very dear it cost 'em, too. And I'm thinkin' if I set here until I'm paid my wages, I shall set a precious long time, Mrs. Raggles; and set I will, too--ha! ha!\" and with this she filled herself another glass of the liquor and drank it with a more hideously satirical air.\n\n\"Trotter! Simpson! turn that drunken wretch out,\" screamed Mrs. Crawley.\n\n\"I shawn't,\" said Trotter the footman; \"turn out yourself. Pay our selleries, and turn me out too. WE'LL go fast enough.\"\n\n\"Are you all here to insult me?\" cried Becky in a fury; \"when Colonel Crawley comes home I'll--\"\n\nAt this the servants burst into a horse haw-haw, in which, however, Raggles, who still kept a most melancholy countenance, did not join. \"He ain't a coming back,\" Mr. Trotter resumed. \"He sent for his things, and I wouldn't let 'em go, although Mr. Raggles would; and I don't b'lieve he's no more a Colonel than I am. He's hoff, and I suppose you're a goin' after him. You're no better than swindlers, both on you. Don't be a bullyin' ME. I won't stand it. Pay us our selleries, I say. Pay us our selleries.\" It was evident, from Mr. Trotter's flushed countenance and defective intonation, that he, too, had had recourse to vinous stimulus.\n\n\"Mr. Raggles,\" said Becky in a passion of vexation, \"you will not surely let me be insulted by that drunken man?\" \"Hold your noise, Trotter; do now,\" said Simpson the page. He was affected by his mistress's deplorable situation, and succeeded in preventing an outrageous denial of the epithet \"drunken\" on the footman's part.\n\n\"Oh, M'am,\" said Raggles, \"I never thought to live to see this year day: I've known the Crawley family ever since I was born. I lived butler with Miss Crawley for thirty years; and I little thought one of that family was a goin' to ruing me--yes, ruing me\"--said the poor fellow with tears in his eyes. \"Har you a goin' to pay me? You've lived in this 'ouse four year. You've 'ad my substance: my plate and linning. You ho me a milk and butter bill of two 'undred pound, you must 'ave noo laid heggs for your homlets, and cream for your spanil dog.\"\n\n\"She didn't care what her own flesh and blood had,\" interposed the cook. \"Many's the time, he'd have starved but for me.\"\n\n\"He's a charaty-boy now, Cooky,\" said Mr. Trotter, with a drunken \"ha! ha!\"--and honest Raggles continued, in a lamentable tone, an enumeration of his griefs. All he said was true. Becky and her husband had ruined him. He had bills coming due next week and no means to meet them. He would be sold up and turned out of his shop and his house, because he had trusted to the Crawley family. His tears and lamentations made Becky more peevish than ever.\n\n\"You all seem to be against me,\" she said bitterly. \"What do you want? I can't pay you on Sunday. Come back to-morrow and I'll pay you everything. I thought Colonel Crawley had settled with you. He will to-morrow. I declare to you upon my honour that he left home this morning with fifteen hundred pounds in his pocket-book. He has left me nothing. Apply to him. Give me a bonnet and shawl and let me go out and find him. There was a difference between us this morning. You all seem to know it. I promise you upon my word that you shall all be paid. He has got a good appointment. Let me go out and find him.\"\n\nThis audacious statement caused Raggles and the other personages present to look at one another with a wild surprise, and with it Rebecca left them. She went upstairs and dressed herself this time without the aid of her French maid. She went into Rawdon's room, and there saw that a trunk and bag were packed ready for removal, with a pencil direction that they should be given when called for; then she went into the Frenchwoman's garret; everything was clean, and all the drawers emptied there. She bethought herself of the trinkets which had been left on the ground and felt certain that the woman had fled. \"Good Heavens! was ever such ill luck as mine?\" she said; \"to be so near, and to lose all. Is it all too late?\" No; there was one chance more.\n\nShe dressed herself and went away unmolested this time, but alone. It was four o'clock. She went swiftly down the streets (she had no money to pay for a carriage), and never stopped until she came to Sir Pitt Crawley's door, in Great Gaunt Street. Where was Lady Jane Crawley? She was at church. Becky was not sorry. Sir Pitt was in his study, and had given orders not to be disturbed--she must see him--she slipped by the sentinel in livery at once, and was in Sir Pitt's room before the astonished Baronet had even laid down the paper.\n\nHe turned red and started back from her with a look of great alarm and horror.\n\n\"Do not look so,\" she said. \"I am not guilty, Pitt, dear Pitt; you were my friend once. Before God, I am not guilty. I seem so. Everything is against me. And oh! at such a moment! just when all my hopes were about to be realized: just when happiness was in store for us.\"\n\n\"Is this true, what I see in the paper then?\" Sir Pitt said--a paragraph in which had greatly surprised him.\n\n\"It is true. Lord Steyne told me on Friday night, the night of that fatal ball. He has been promised an appointment any time these six months. Mr. Martyr, the Colonial Secretary, told him yesterday that it was made out. That unlucky arrest ensued; that horrible meeting. I was only guilty of too much devotedness to Rawdon's service. I have received Lord Steyne alone a hundred times before. I confess I had money of which Rawdon knew nothing. Don't you know how careless he is of it, and could I dare to confide it to him?\" And so she went on with a perfectly connected story, which she poured into the ears of her perplexed kinsman.\n\nIt was to the following effect. Becky owned, and with perfect frankness, but deep contrition, that having remarked Lord Steyne's partiality for her (at the mention of which Pitt blushed), and being secure of her own virtue, she had determined to turn the great peer's attachment to the advantage of herself and her family. \"I looked for a peerage for you, Pitt,\" she said (the brother-in-law again turned red). \"We have talked about it. Your genius and Lord Steyne's interest made it more than probable, had not this dreadful calamity come to put an end to all our hopes. But, first, I own that it was my object to rescue my dear husband--him whom I love in spite of all his ill usage and suspicions of me--to remove him from the poverty and ruin which was impending over us. I saw Lord Steyne's partiality for me,\" she said, casting down her eyes. \"I own that I did everything in my power to make myself pleasing to him, and as far as an honest woman may, to secure his--his esteem. It was only on Friday morning that the news arrived of the death of the Governor of Coventry Island, and my Lord instantly secured the appointment for my dear husband. It was intended as a surprise for him--he was to see it in the papers to-day. Even after that horrid arrest took place (the expenses of which Lord Steyne generously said he would settle, so that I was in a manner prevented from coming to my husband's assistance), my Lord was laughing with me, and saying that my dearest Rawdon would be consoled when he read of his appointment in the paper, in that shocking spun--bailiff's house. And then--then he came home. His suspicions were excited,--the dreadful scene took place between my Lord and my cruel, cruel Rawdon--and, O my God, what will happen next? Pitt, dear Pitt! pity me, and reconcile us!\" And as she spoke she flung herself down on her knees, and bursting into tears, seized hold of Pitt's hand, which she kissed passionately.\n\nIt was in this very attitude that Lady Jane, who, returning from church, ran to her husband's room directly she heard Mrs. Rawdon Crawley was closeted there, found the Baronet and his sister-in-law.\n\n\"I am surprised that woman has the audacity to enter this house,\" Lady Jane said, trembling in every limb and turning quite pale. (Her Ladyship had sent out her maid directly after breakfast, who had communicated with Raggles and Rawdon Crawley's household, who had told her all, and a great deal more than they knew, of that story, and many others besides). \"How dare Mrs. Crawley to enter the house of--of an honest family?\"\n\nSir Pitt started back, amazed at his wife's display of vigour. Becky still kept her kneeling posture and clung to Sir Pitt's hand.\n\n\"Tell her that she does not know all: Tell her that I am innocent, dear Pitt,\" she whimpered out.\n\n\"Upon my word, my love, I think you do Mrs. Crawley injustice,\" Sir Pitt said; at which speech Rebecca was vastly relieved. \"Indeed I believe her to be--\"\n\n\"To be what?\" cried out Lady Jane, her clear voice thrilling and, her heart beating violently as she spoke. \"To be a wicked woman--a heartless mother, a false wife? She never loved her dear little boy, who used to fly here and tell me of her cruelty to him. She never came into a family but she strove to bring misery with her and to weaken the most sacred affections with her wicked flattery and falsehoods. She has deceived her husband, as she has deceived everybody; her soul is black with vanity, worldliness, and all sorts of crime. I tremble when I touch her. I keep my children out of her sight.\"\n\n\"Lady Jane!\" cried Sir Pitt, starting up, \"this is really language--\" \"I have been a true and faithful wife to you, Sir Pitt,\" Lady Jane continued, intrepidly; \"I have kept my marriage vow as I made it to God and have been obedient and gentle as a wife should. But righteous obedience has its limits, and I declare that I will not bear that--that woman again under my roof; if she enters it, I and my children will leave it. She is not worthy to sit down with Christian people. You--you must choose, sir, between her and me\"; and with this my Lady swept out of the room, fluttering with her own audacity, and leaving Rebecca and Sir Pitt not a little astonished at it.\n\nAs for Becky, she was not hurt; nay, she was pleased. \"It was the diamond-clasp you gave me,\" she said to Sir Pitt, reaching him out her hand; and before she left him (for which event you may be sure my Lady Jane was looking out from her dressing-room window in the upper story) the Baronet had promised to go and seek out his brother, and endeavour to bring about a reconciliation.\n\nRawdon found some of the young fellows of the regiment seated in the mess-room at breakfast, and was induced without much difficulty to partake of that meal, and of the devilled legs of fowls and soda-water with which these young gentlemen fortified themselves. Then they had a conversation befitting the day and their time of life: about the next pigeon-match at Battersea, with relative bets upon Ross and Osbaldiston; about Mademoiselle Ariane of the French Opera, and who had left her, and how she was consoled by Panther Carr; and about the fight between the Butcher and the Pet, and the probabilities that it was a cross. Young Tandyman, a hero of seventeen, laboriously endeavouring to get up a pair of mustachios, had seen the fight, and spoke in the most scientific manner about the battle and the condition of the men. It was he who had driven the Butcher on to the ground in his drag and passed the whole of the previous night with him. Had there not been foul play he must have won it. All the old files of the Ring were in it; and Tandyman wouldn't pay; no, dammy, he wouldn't pay. It was but a year since the young Cornet, now so knowing a hand in Cribb's parlour, had a still lingering liking for toffy, and used to be birched at Eton.\n\nSo they went on talking about dancers, fights, drinking, demireps, until Macmurdo came down and joined the boys and the conversation. He did not appear to think that any especial reverence was due to their boyhood; the old fellow cut in with stories, to the full as choice as any the youngest rake present had to tell--nor did his own grey hairs nor their smooth faces detain him. Old Mac was famous for his good stories. He was not exactly a lady's man; that is, men asked him to dine rather at the houses of their mistresses than of their mothers. There can scarcely be a life lower, perhaps, than his, but he was quite contented with it, such as it was, and led it in perfect good nature, simplicity, and modesty of demeanour.\n\nBy the time Mac had finished a copious breakfast, most of the others had concluded their meal. Young Lord Varinas was smoking an immense Meerschaum pipe, while Captain Hugues was employed with a cigar: that violent little devil Tandyman, with his little bull-terrier between his legs, was tossing for shillings with all his might (that fellow was always at some game or other) against Captain Deuceace; and Mac and Rawdon walked off to the Club, neither, of course, having given any hint of the business which was occupying their minds. Both, on the other hand, had joined pretty gaily in the conversation, for why should they interrupt it? Feasting, drinking, ribaldry, laughter, go on alongside of all sorts of other occupations in Vanity Fair--the crowds were pouring out of church as Rawdon and his friend passed down St. James's Street and entered into their Club.\n\nThe old bucks and habitues, who ordinarily stand gaping and grinning out of the great front window of the Club, had not arrived at their posts as yet--the newspaper-room was almost empty. One man was present whom Rawdon did not know; another to whom he owed a little score for whist, and whom, in consequence, he did not care to meet; a third was reading the Royalist (a periodical famous for its scandal and its attachment to Church and King) Sunday paper at the table, and looking up at Crawley with some interest, said, \"Crawley, I congratulate you.\"\n\n\"What do you mean?\" said the Colonel.\n\n\"It's in the Observer and the Royalist too,\" said Mr. Smith.\n\n\"What?\" Rawdon cried, turning very red. He thought that the affair with Lord Steyne was already in the public prints. Smith looked up wondering and smiling at the agitation which the Colonel exhibited as he took up the paper and, trembling, began to read.\n\nMr. Smith and Mr. Brown (the gentleman with whom Rawdon had the outstanding whist account) had been talking about the Colonel just before he came in.\n\n\"It is come just in the nick of time,\" said Smith. \"I suppose Crawley had not a shilling in the world.\"\n\n\"It's a wind that blows everybody good,\" Mr. Brown said. \"He can't go away without paying me a pony he owes me.\"\n\n\"What's the salary?\" asked Smith.\n\n\"Two or three thousand,\" answered the other. \"But the climate's so infernal, they don't enjoy it long. Liverseege died after eighteen months of it, and the man before went off in six weeks, I hear.\"\n\n\"Some people say his brother is a very clever man. I always found him a d------ bore,\" Smith ejaculated. \"He must have good interest, though. He must have got the Colonel the place.\"\n\n\"He!\" said Brown, with a sneer. \"Pooh. It was Lord Steyne got it.\"\n\n\"How do you mean?\"\n\n\"A virtuous woman is a crown to her husband,\" answered the other enigmatically, and went to read his papers.\n\nRawdon, for his part, read in the Royalist the following astonishing paragraph:\n\nGOVERNORSHIP OF COVENTRY ISLAND.--H.M.S. Yellowjack, Commander Jaunders, has brought letters and papers from Coventry Island. H. E. Sir Thomas Liverseege had fallen a victim to the prevailing fever at Swampton. His loss is deeply felt in the flourishing colony. We hear that the Governorship has been offered to Colonel Rawdon Crawley, C.B., a distinguished Waterloo officer. We need not only men of acknowledged bravery, but men of administrative talents to superintend the affairs of our colonies, and we have no doubt that the gentleman selected by the Colonial Office to fill the lamented vacancy which has occurred at Coventry Island is admirably calculated for the post which he is about to occupy.\n\n\"Coventry Island! Where was it? Who had appointed him to the government? You must take me out as your secretary, old boy,\" Captain Macmurdo said laughing; and as Crawley and his friend sat wondering and perplexed over the announcement, the Club waiter brought in to the Colonel a card on which the name of Mr. Wenham was engraved, who begged to see Colonel Crawley.\n\nThe Colonel and his aide-de-camp went out to meet the gentleman, rightly conjecturing that he was an emissary of Lord Steyne. \"How d'ye do, Crawley? I am glad to see you,\" said Mr. Wenham with a bland smile, and grasping Crawley's hand with great cordiality.\n\n\"You come, I suppose, from--\"\n\n\"Exactly,\" said Mr. Wenham.\n\n\"Then this is my friend Captain Macmurdo, of the Life Guards Green.\"\n\n\"Delighted to know Captain Macmurdo, I'm sure,\" Mr. Wenham said and tendered another smile and shake of the hand to the second, as he had done to the principal. Mac put out one finger, armed with a buckskin glove, and made a very frigid bow to Mr. Wenham over his tight cravat. He was, perhaps, discontented at being put in communication with a pekin, and thought that Lord Steyne should have sent him a Colonel at the very least.\n\n\"As Macmurdo acts for me, and knows what I mean,\" Crawley said, \"I had better retire and leave you together.\"\n\n\"Of course,\" said Macmurdo.\n\n\"By no means, my dear Colonel,\" Mr. Wenham said; \"the interview which I had the honour of requesting was with you personally, though the company of Captain Macmurdo cannot fail to be also most pleasing. In fact, Captain, I hope that our conversation will lead to none but the most agreeable results, very different from those which my friend Colonel Crawley appears to anticipate.\"\n\n\"Humph!\" said Captain Macmurdo. Be hanged to these civilians, he thought to himself, they are always for arranging and speechifying. Mr. Wenham took a chair which was not offered to him--took a paper from his pocket, and resumed--\n\n\"You have seen this gratifying announcement in the papers this morning, Colonel? Government has secured a most valuable servant, and you, if you accept office, as I presume you will, an excellent appointment. Three thousand a year, delightful climate, excellent government-house, all your own way in the Colony, and a certain promotion. I congratulate you with all my heart. I presume you know, gentlemen, to whom my friend is indebted for this piece of patronage?\"\n\n\"Hanged if I know,\" the Captain said; his principal turned very red.\n\n\"To one of the most generous and kindest men in the world, as he is one of the greatest--to my excellent friend, the Marquis of Steyne.\"\n\n\"I'll see him d---- before I take his place,\" growled out Rawdon.\n\n\"You are irritated against my noble friend,\" Mr. Wenham calmly resumed; \"and now, in the name of common sense and justice, tell me why?\"\n\n\"WHY?\" cried Rawdon in surprise.\n\n\"Why? Dammy!\" said the Captain, ringing his stick on the ground.\n\n\"Dammy, indeed,\" said Mr. Wenham with the most agreeable smile; \"still, look at the matter as a man of the world--as an honest man--and see if you have not been in the wrong. You come home from a journey, and find--what?--my Lord Steyne supping at your house in Curzon Street with Mrs. Crawley. Is the circumstance strange or novel? Has he not been a hundred times before in the same position? Upon my honour and word as a gentleman\"--Mr. Wenham here put his hand on his waistcoat with a parliamentary air--\"I declare I think that your suspicions are monstrous and utterly unfounded, and that they injure an honourable gentleman who has proved his good-will towards you by a thousand benefactions--and a most spotless and innocent lady.\"\n\n\"You don't mean to say that--that Crawley's mistaken?\" said Mr. Macmurdo.\n\n\"I believe that Mrs. Crawley is as innocent as my wife, Mrs. Wenham,\" Mr. Wenham said with great energy. \"I believe that, misled by an infernal jealousy, my friend here strikes a blow against not only an infirm and old man of high station, his constant friend and benefactor, but against his wife, his own dearest honour, his son's future reputation, and his own prospects in life.\"\n\n\"I will tell you what happened,\" Mr. Wenham continued with great solemnity; \"I was sent for this morning by my Lord Steyne, and found him in a pitiable state, as, I need hardly inform Colonel Crawley, any man of age and infirmity would be after a personal conflict with a man of your strength. I say to your face; it was a cruel advantage you took of that strength, Colonel Crawley. It was not only the body of my noble and excellent friend which was wounded--his heart, sir, was bleeding. A man whom he had loaded with benefits and regarded with affection had subjected him to the foulest indignity. What was this very appointment, which appears in the journals of to-day, but a proof of his kindness to you? When I saw his Lordship this morning I found him in a state pitiable indeed to see, and as anxious as you are to revenge the outrage committed upon him, by blood. You know he has given his proofs, I presume, Colonel Crawley?\"\n\n\"He has plenty of pluck,\" said the Colonel. \"Nobody ever said he hadn't.\"\n\n\"His first order to me was to write a letter of challenge, and to carry it to Colonel Crawley. One or other of us,\" he said, \"must not survive the outrage of last night.\"\n\nCrawley nodded. \"You're coming to the point, Wenham,\" he said.\n\n\"I tried my utmost to calm Lord Steyne. 'Good God! sir,' I said, 'how I regret that Mrs. Wenham and myself had not accepted Mrs. Crawley's invitation to sup with her!'\"\n\n\"She asked you to sup with her?\" Captain Macmurdo said.\n\n\"After the opera. Here's the note of invitation--stop--no, this is another paper--I thought I had it, but it's of no consequence, and I pledge you my word to the fact. If we had come--and it was only one of Mrs. Wenham's headaches which prevented us--she suffers under them a good deal, especially in the spring--if we had come, and you had returned home, there would have been no quarrel, no insult, no suspicion--and so it is positively because my poor wife has a headache that you are to bring death down upon two men of honour and plunge two of the most excellent and ancient families in the kingdom into disgrace and sorrow.\"\n\nMr. Macmurdo looked at his principal with the air of a man profoundly puzzled, and Rawdon felt with a kind of rage that his prey was escaping him. He did not believe a word of the story, and yet, how discredit or disprove it?\n\nMr. Wenham continued with the same fluent oratory, which in his place in Parliament he had so often practised--\"I sat for an hour or more by Lord Steyne's bedside, beseeching, imploring Lord Steyne to forego his intention of demanding a meeting. I pointed out to him that the circumstances were after all suspicious--they were suspicious. I acknowledge it--any man in your position might have been taken in--I said that a man furious with jealousy is to all intents and purposes a madman, and should be as such regarded--that a duel between you must lead to the disgrace of all parties concerned--that a man of his Lordship's exalted station had no right in these days, when the most atrocious revolutionary principles, and the most dangerous levelling doctrines are preached among the vulgar, to create a public scandal; and that, however innocent, the common people would insist that he was guilty. In fine, I implored him not to send the challenge.\"\n\n\"I don't believe one word of the whole story,\" said Rawdon, grinding his teeth. \"I believe it a d------ lie, and that you're in it, Mr. Wenham. If the challenge don't come from him, by Jove it shall come from me.\"\n\nMr. Wenham turned deadly pale at this savage interruption of the Colonel and looked towards the door.\n\nBut he found a champion in Captain Macmurdo. That gentleman rose up with an oath and rebuked Rawdon for his language. \"You put the affair into my hands, and you shall act as I think fit, by Jove, and not as you do. You have no right to insult Mr. Wenham with this sort of language; and dammy, Mr. Wenham, you deserve an apology. And as for a challenge to Lord Steyne, you may get somebody else to carry it, I won't. If my lord, after being thrashed, chooses to sit still, dammy let him. And as for the affair with--with Mrs. Crawley, my belief is, there's nothing proved at all: that your wife's innocent, as innocent as Mr. Wenham says she is; and at any rate that you would be a d--fool not to take the place and hold your tongue.\"\n\n\"Captain Macmurdo, you speak like a man of sense,\" Mr. Wenham cried out, immensely relieved--\"I forget any words that Colonel Crawley has used in the irritation of the moment.\"\n\n\"I thought you would,\" Rawdon said with a sneer.\n\n\"Shut your mouth, you old stoopid,\" the Captain said good-naturedly. \"Mr. Wenham ain't a fighting man; and quite right, too.\"\n\n\"This matter, in my belief,\" the Steyne emissary cried, \"ought to be buried in the most profound oblivion. A word concerning it should never pass these doors. I speak in the interest of my friend, as well as of Colonel Crawley, who persists in considering me his enemy.\"\n\n\"I suppose Lord Steyne won't talk about it very much,\" said Captain Macmurdo; \"and I don't see why our side should. The affair ain't a very pretty one, any way you take it, and the less said about it the better. It's you are thrashed, and not us; and if you are satisfied, why, I think, we should be.\"\n\nMr. Wenham took his hat, upon this, and Captain Macmurdo following him to the door, shut it upon himself and Lord Steyne's agent, leaving Rawdon chafing within. When the two were on the other side, Macmurdo looked hard at the other ambassador and with an expression of anything but respect on his round jolly face.\n\n\"You don't stick at a trifle, Mr. Wenham,\" he said.\n\n\"You flatter me, Captain Macmurdo,\" answered the other with a smile. \"Upon my honour and conscience now, Mrs. Crawley did ask us to sup after the opera.\"\n\n\"Of course; and Mrs. Wenham had one of her head-aches. I say, I've got a thousand-pound note here, which I will give you if you will give me a receipt, please; and I will put the note up in an envelope for Lord Steyne. My man shan't fight him. But we had rather not take his money.\"\n\n\"It was all a mistake--all a mistake, my dear sir,\" the other said with the utmost innocence of manner; and was bowed down the Club steps by Captain Macmurdo, just as Sir Pitt Crawley ascended them. There was a slight acquaintance between these two gentlemen, and the Captain, going back with the Baronet to the room where the latter's brother was, told Sir Pitt, in confidence, that he had made the affair all right between Lord Steyne and the Colonel.\n\nSir Pitt was well pleased, of course, at this intelligence, and congratulated his brother warmly upon the peaceful issue of the affair, making appropriate moral remarks upon the evils of duelling and the unsatisfactory nature of that sort of settlement of disputes.\n\nAnd after this preface, he tried with all his eloquence to effect a reconciliation between Rawdon and his wife. He recapitulated the statements which Becky had made, pointed out the probabilities of their truth, and asserted his own firm belief in her innocence.\n\nBut Rawdon would not hear of it. \"She has kep money concealed from me these ten years,\" he said \"She swore, last night only, she had none from Steyne. She knew it was all up, directly I found it. If she's not guilty, Pitt, she's as bad as guilty, and I'll never see her again--never.\" His head sank down on his chest as he spoke the words, and he looked quite broken and sad.\n\n\"Poor old boy,\" Macmurdo said, shaking his head.\n\nRawdon Crawley resisted for some time the idea of taking the place which had been procured for him by so odious a patron, and was also for removing the boy from the school where Lord Steyne's interest had placed him. He was induced, however, to acquiesce in these benefits by the entreaties of his brother and Macmurdo, but mainly by the latter, pointing out to him what a fury Steyne would be in to think that his enemy's fortune was made through his means.\n\nWhen the Marquis of Steyne came abroad after his accident, the Colonial Secretary bowed up to him and congratulated himself and the Service upon having made so excellent an appointment. These congratulations were received with a degree of gratitude which may be imagined on the part of Lord Steyne.\n\nThe secret of the rencontre between him and Colonel Crawley was buried in the profoundest oblivion, as Wenham said; that is, by the seconds and the principals. But before that evening was over it was talked of at fifty dinner-tables in Vanity Fair. Little Cackleby himself went to seven evening parties and told the story with comments and emendations at each place. How Mrs. Washington White revelled in it! The Bishopess of Ealing was shocked beyond expression; the Bishop went and wrote his name down in the visiting-book at Gaunt House that very day. Little Southdown was sorry; so you may be sure was his sister Lady Jane, very sorry. Lady Southdown wrote it off to her other daughter at the Cape of Good Hope. It was town-talk for at least three days, and was only kept out of the newspapers by the exertions of Mr. Wagg, acting upon a hint from Mr. Wenham.\n\nThe bailiffs and brokers seized upon poor Raggles in Curzon Street, and the late fair tenant of that poor little mansion was in the meanwhile--where? Who cared! Who asked after a day or two? Was she guilty or not? We all know how charitable the world is, and how the verdict of Vanity Fair goes when there is a doubt. Some people said she had gone to Naples in pursuit of Lord Steyne, whilst others averred that his Lordship quitted that city and fled to Palermo on hearing of Becky's arrival; some said she was living in Bierstadt, and had become a dame d'honneur to the Queen of Bulgaria; some that she was at Boulogne; and others, at a boarding-house at Cheltenham.\n\nRawdon made her a tolerable annuity, and we may be sure that she was a woman who could make a little money go a great way, as the saying is. He would have paid his debts on leaving England, could he have got any Insurance Office to take his life, but the climate of Coventry Island was so bad that he could borrow no money on the strength of his salary. He remitted, however, to his brother punctually, and wrote to his little boy regularly every mail. He kept Macmurdo in cigars and sent over quantities of shells, cayenne pepper, hot pickles, guava jelly, and colonial produce to Lady Jane. He sent his brother home the Swamp Town Gazette, in which the new Governor was praised with immense enthusiasm; whereas the Swamp Town Sentinel, whose wife was not asked to Government House, declared that his Excellency was a tyrant, compared to whom Nero was an enlightened philanthropist. Little Rawdon used to like to get the papers and read about his Excellency.\n\nHis mother never made any movement to see the child. He went home to his aunt for Sundays and holidays; he soon knew every bird's nest about Queen's Crawley, and rode out with Sir Huddlestone's hounds, which he admired so on his first well-remembered visit to Hampshire.\n\nGeorgy is Made a Gentleman\nGeorgy Osborne was now fairly established in his grandfather's mansion in Russell Square, occupant of his father's room in the house and heir apparent of all the splendours there. The good looks, gallant bearing, and gentlemanlike appearance of the boy won the grandsire's heart for him. Mr. Osborne was as proud of him as ever he had been of the elder George.\n\nThe child had many more luxuries and indulgences than had been awarded his father. Osborne's commerce had prospered greatly of late years. His wealth and importance in the City had very much increased. He had been glad enough in former days to put the elder George to a good private school; and a commission in the army for his son had been a source of no small pride to him; for little George and his future prospects the old man looked much higher. He would make a gentleman of the little chap, was Mr. Osborne's constant saying regarding little Georgy. He saw him in his mind's eye, a collegian, a Parliament man, a Baronet, perhaps. The old man thought he would die contented if he could see his grandson in a fair way to such honours. He would have none but a tip-top college man to educate him--none of your quacks and pretenders--no, no. A few years before, he used to be savage, and inveigh against all parsons, scholars, and the like declaring that they were a pack of humbugs, and quacks that weren't fit to get their living but by grinding Latin and Greek, and a set of supercilious dogs that pretended to look down upon British merchants and gentlemen, who could buy up half a hundred of 'em. He would mourn now, in a very solemn manner, that his own education had been neglected, and repeatedly point out, in pompous orations to Georgy, the necessity and excellence of classical acquirements.\n\nWhen they met at dinner the grandsire used to ask the lad what he had been reading during the day, and was greatly interested at the report the boy gave of his own studies, pretending to understand little George when he spoke regarding them. He made a hundred blunders and showed his ignorance many a time. It did not increase the respect which the child had for his senior. A quick brain and a better education elsewhere showed the boy very soon that his grandsire was a dullard, and he began accordingly to command him and to look down upon him; for his previous education, humble and contracted as it had been, had made a much better gentleman of Georgy than any plans of his grandfather could make him. He had been brought up by a kind, weak, and tender woman, who had no pride about anything but about him, and whose heart was so pure and whose bearing was so meek and humble that she could not but needs be a true lady. She busied herself in gentle offices and quiet duties; if she never said brilliant things, she never spoke or thought unkind ones; guileless and artless, loving and pure, indeed how could our poor little Amelia be other than a real gentlewoman!\n\nYoung Georgy lorded over this soft and yielding nature; and the contrast of its simplicity and delicacy with the coarse pomposity of the dull old man with whom he next came in contact made him lord over the latter too. If he had been a Prince Royal he could not have been better brought up to think well of himself.\n\nWhilst his mother was yearning after him at home, and I do believe every hour of the day, and during most hours of the sad lonely nights, thinking of him, this young gentleman had a number of pleasures and consolations administered to him, which made him for his part bear the separation from Amelia very easily. Little boys who cry when they are going to school cry because they are going to a very uncomfortable place. It is only a few who weep from sheer affection. When you think that the eyes of your childhood dried at the sight of a piece of gingerbread, and that a plum cake was a compensation for the agony of parting with your mamma and sisters, oh my friend and brother, you need not be too confident of your own fine feelings.\n\nWell, then, Master George Osborne had every comfort and luxury that a wealthy and lavish old grandfather thought fit to provide. The coachman was instructed to purchase for him the handsomest pony which could be bought for money, and on this George was taught to ride, first at a riding-school, whence, after having performed satisfactorily without stirrups, and over the leaping-bar, he was conducted through the New Road to Regent's Park, and then to Hyde Park, where he rode in state with Martin the coachman behind him. Old Osborne, who took matters more easily in the City now, where he left his affairs to his junior partners, would often ride out with Miss O. in the same fashionable direction. As little Georgy came cantering up with his dandified air and his heels down, his grandfather would nudge the lad's aunt and say, \"Look, Miss O.\" And he would laugh, and his face would grow red with pleasure, as he nodded out of the window to the boy, as the groom saluted the carriage, and the footman saluted Master George. Here too his aunt, Mrs. Frederick Bullock (whose chariot might daily be seen in the Ring, with bullocks or emblazoned on the panels and harness, and three pasty-faced little Bullocks, covered with cockades and feathers, staring from the windows) Mrs. Frederick Bullock, I say, flung glances of the bitterest hatred at the little upstart as he rode by with his hand on his side and his hat on one ear, as proud as a lord.\n\nThough he was scarcely eleven years of age, Master George wore straps and the most beautiful little boots like a man. He had gilt spurs, and a gold-headed whip, and a fine pin in his handkerchief, and the neatest little kid gloves which Lamb's Conduit Street could furnish. His mother had given him a couple of neckcloths, and carefully hemmed and made some little shirts for him; but when her Eli came to see the widow, they were replaced by much finer linen. He had little jewelled buttons in the lawn shirt fronts. Her humble presents had been put aside--I believe Miss Osborne had given them to the coachman's boy. Amelia tried to think she was pleased at the change. Indeed, she was happy and charmed to see the boy looking so beautiful.\n\nShe had had a little black profile of him done for a shilling, and this was hung up by the side of another portrait over her bed. One day the boy came on his accustomed visit, galloping down the little street at Brompton, and bringing, as usual, all the inhabitants to the windows to admire his splendour, and with great eagerness and a look of triumph in his face, he pulled a case out of his great-coat--it was a natty white great-coat, with a cape and a velvet collar--pulled out a red morocco case, which he gave her.\n\n\"I bought it with my own money, Mamma,\" he said. \"I thought you'd like it.\"\n\nAmelia opened the case, and giving a little cry of delighted affection, seized the boy and embraced him a hundred times. It was a miniature of himself, very prettily done (though not half handsome enough, we may be sure, the widow thought). His grandfather had wished to have a picture of him by an artist whose works, exhibited in a shop-window, in Southampton Row, had caught the old gentleman's eye; and George, who had plenty of money, bethought him of asking the painter how much a copy of the little portrait would cost, saying that he would pay for it out of his own money and that he wanted to give it to his mother. The pleased painter executed it for a small price, and old Osborne himself, when he heard of the incident, growled out his satisfaction and gave the boy twice as many sovereigns as he paid for the miniature.\n\nBut what was the grandfather's pleasure compared to Amelia's ecstacy? That proof of the boy's affection charmed her so that she thought no child in the world was like hers for goodness. For long weeks after, the thought of his love made her happy. She slept better with the picture under her pillow, and how many many times did she kiss it and weep and pray over it! A small kindness from those she loved made that timid heart grateful. Since her parting with George she had had no such joy and consolation.\n\nAt his new home Master George ruled like a lord; at dinner he invited the ladies to drink wine with the utmost coolness, and took off his champagne in a way which charmed his old grandfather. \"Look at him,\" the old man would say, nudging his neighbour with a delighted purple face, \"did you ever see such a chap? Lord, Lord! he'll be ordering a dressing-case next, and razors to shave with; I'm blessed if he won't.\"\n\nThe antics of the lad did not, however, delight Mr. Osborne's friends so much as they pleased the old gentleman. It gave Mr. Justice Coffin no pleasure to hear Georgy cut into the conversation and spoil his stories. Colonel Fogey was not interested in seeing the little boy half tipsy. Mr. Sergeant Toffy's lady felt no particular gratitude, when, with a twist of his elbow, he tilted a glass of port-wine over her yellow satin and laughed at the disaster; nor was she better pleased, although old Osborne was highly delighted, when Georgy \"whopped\" her third boy (a young gentleman a year older than Georgy, and by chance home for the holidays from Dr. Tickleus's at Ealing School) in Russell Square. George's grandfather gave the boy a couple of sovereigns for that feat and promised to reward him further for every boy above his own size and age whom he whopped in a similar manner. It is difficult to say what good the old man saw in these combats; he had a vague notion that quarrelling made boys hardy, and that tyranny was a useful accomplishment for them to learn. English youth have been so educated time out of mind, and we have hundreds of thousands of apologists and admirers of injustice, misery, and brutality, as perpetrated among children. Flushed with praise and victory over Master Toffy, George wished naturally to pursue his conquests further, and one day as he was strutting about in prodigiously dandified new clothes, near St. Pancras, and a young baker's boy made sarcastic comments upon his appearance, the youthful patrician pulled off his dandy jacket with great spirit, and giving it in charge to the friend who accompanied him (Master Todd, of Great Coram Street, Russell Square, son of the junior partner of the house of Osborne and Co.), George tried to whop the little baker. But the chances of war were unfavourable this time, and the little baker whopped Georgy, who came home with a rueful black eye and all his fine shirt frill dabbled with the claret drawn from his own little nose. He told his grandfather that he had been in combat with a giant, and frightened his poor mother at Brompton with long, and by no means authentic, accounts of the battle.\n\nThis young Todd, of Coram Street, Russell Square, was Master George's great friend and admirer. They both had a taste for painting theatrical characters; for hardbake and raspberry tarts; for sliding and skating in the Regent's Park and the Serpentine, when the weather permitted; for going to the play, whither they were often conducted, by Mr. Osborne's orders, by Rowson, Master George's appointed body-servant, with whom they sat in great comfort in the pit.\n\nIn the company of this gentleman they visited all the principal theatres of the metropolis; knew the names of all the actors from Drury Lane to Sadler's Wells; and performed, indeed, many of the plays to the Todd family and their youthful friends, with West's famous characters, on their pasteboard theatre. Rowson, the footman, who was of a generous disposition, would not unfrequently, when in cash, treat his young master to oysters after the play, and to a glass of rum-shrub for a night-cap. We may be pretty certain that Mr. Rowson profited in his turn by his young master's liberality and gratitude for the pleasures to which the footman inducted him.\n\nA famous tailor from the West End of the town--Mr. Osborne would have none of your City or Holborn bunglers, he said, for the boy (though a City tailor was good enough for HIM)--was summoned to ornament little George's person, and was told to spare no expense in so doing. So, Mr. Woolsey, of Conduit Street, gave a loose to his imagination and sent the child home fancy trousers, fancy waistcoats, and fancy jackets enough to furnish a school of little dandies. Georgy had little white waistcoats for evening parties, and little cut velvet waistcoats for dinners, and a dear little darling shawl dressing-gown, for all the world like a little man. He dressed for dinner every day, \"like a regular West End swell,\" as his grandfather remarked; one of the domestics was affected to his special service, attended him at his toilette, answered his bell, and brought him his letters always on a silver tray.\n\nGeorgy, after breakfast, would sit in the arm-chair in the dining-room and read the Morning Post, just like a grown-up man. \"How he DU dam and swear,\" the servants would cry, delighted at his precocity. Those who remembered the Captain his father, declared Master George was his Pa, every inch of him. He made the house lively by his activity, his imperiousness, his scolding, and his good-nature.\n\nGeorge's education was confided to a neighbouring scholar and private pedagogue who \"prepared young noblemen and gentlemen for the Universities, the senate, and the learned professions: whose system did not embrace the degrading corporal severities still practised at the ancient places of education, and in whose family the pupils would find the elegances of refined society and the confidence and affection of a home.\" It was in this way that the Reverend Lawrence Veal of Hart Street, Bloomsbury, and domestic Chaplain to the Earl of Bareacres, strove with Mrs. Veal his wife to entice pupils.\n\nBy thus advertising and pushing sedulously, the domestic Chaplain and his Lady generally succeeded in having one or two scholars by them--who paid a high figure and were thought to be in uncommonly comfortable quarters. There was a large West Indian, whom nobody came to see, with a mahogany complexion, a woolly head, and an exceedingly dandyfied appearance; there was another hulking boy of three-and-twenty whose education had been neglected and whom Mr. and Mrs. Veal were to introduce into the polite world; there were two sons of Colonel Bangles of the East India Company's Service: these four sat down to dinner at Mrs. Veal's genteel board, when Georgy was introduced to her establishment.\n\nGeorgy was, like some dozen other pupils, only a day boy; he arrived in the morning under the guardianship of his friend Mr. Rowson, and if it was fine, would ride away in the afternoon on his pony, followed by the groom. The wealth of his grandfather was reported in the school to be prodigious. The Rev. Mr. Veal used to compliment Georgy upon it personally, warning him that he was destined for a high station; that it became him to prepare, by sedulity and docility in youth, for the lofty duties to which he would be called in mature age; that obedience in the child was the best preparation for command in the man; and that he therefore begged George would not bring toffee into the school and ruin the health of the Masters Bangles, who had everything they wanted at the elegant and abundant table of Mrs. Veal.\n\nWith respect to learning, \"the Curriculum,\" as Mr. Veal loved to call it, was of prodigious extent, and the young gentlemen in Hart Street might learn a something of every known science. The Rev. Mr. Veal had an orrery, an electrifying machine, a turning lathe, a theatre (in the wash-house), a chemical apparatus, and what he called a select library of all the works of the best authors of ancient and modern times and languages. He took the boys to the British Museum and descanted upon the antiquities and the specimens of natural history there, so that audiences would gather round him as he spoke, and all Bloomsbury highly admired him as a prodigiously well-informed man. And whenever he spoke (which he did almost always), he took care to produce the very finest and longest words of which the vocabulary gave him the use, rightly judging that it was as cheap to employ a handsome, large, and sonorous epithet, as to use a little stingy one.\n\nThus he would say to George in school, \"I observed on my return home from taking the indulgence of an evening's scientific conversation with my excellent friend Doctor Bulders--a true archaeologian, gentlemen, a true archaeologian--that the windows of your venerated grandfather's almost princely mansion in Russell Square were illuminated as if for the purposes of festivity. Am I right in my conjecture that Mr. Osborne entertained a society of chosen spirits round his sumptuous board last night?\"\n\nLittle Georgy, who had considerable humour, and used to mimic Mr. Veal to his face with great spirit and dexterity, would reply that Mr. V. was quite correct in his surmise.\n\n\"Then those friends who had the honour of partaking of Mr. Osborne's hospitality, gentlemen, had no reason, I will lay any wager, to complain of their repast. I myself have been more than once so favoured. (By the way, Master Osborne, you came a little late this morning, and have been a defaulter in this respect more than once.) I myself, I say, gentlemen, humble as I am, have been found not unworthy to share Mr. Osborne's elegant hospitality. And though I have feasted with the great and noble of the world--for I presume that I may call my excellent friend and patron, the Right Honourable George Earl of Bareacres, one of the number--yet I assure you that the board of the British merchant was to the full as richly served, and his reception as gratifying and noble. Mr. Bluck, sir, we will resume, if you please, that passage of Eutropis, which was interrupted by the late arrival of Master Osborne.\"\n\nTo this great man George's education was for some time entrusted. Amelia was bewildered by his phrases, but thought him a prodigy of learning. That poor widow made friends of Mrs. Veal, for reasons of her own. She liked to be in the house and see Georgy coming to school there. She liked to be asked to Mrs. Veal's conversazioni, which took place once a month (as you were informed on pink cards, with AOHNH [Transcriber's Note: The name of the Greek goddess Athene; the \"O\" represents a capital theta.] engraved on them), and where the professor welcomed his pupils and their friends to weak tea and scientific conversation. Poor little Amelia never missed one of these entertainments and thought them delicious so long as she might have Georgy sitting by her. And she would walk from Brompton in any weather, and embrace Mrs. Veal with tearful gratitude for the delightful evening she had passed, when, the company having retired and Georgy gone off with Mr. Rowson, his attendant, poor Mrs. Osborne put on her cloaks and her shawls preparatory to walking home.\n\nAs for the learning which Georgy imbibed under this valuable master of a hundred sciences, to judge from the weekly reports which the lad took home to his grandfather, his progress was remarkable. The names of a score or more of desirable branches of knowledge were printed in a table, and the pupil's progress in each was marked by the professor. In Greek Georgy was pronounced aristos, in Latin optimus, in French tres bien, and so forth; and everybody had prizes for everything at the end of the year. Even Mr. Swartz, the wooly-headed young gentleman, and half-brother to the Honourable Mrs. Mac Mull, and Mr. Bluck, the neglected young pupil of three-and-twenty from the agricultural district, and that idle young scapegrace of a Master Todd before mentioned, received little eighteen-penny books, with \"Athene\" engraved on them, and a pompous Latin inscription from the professor to his young friends.\n\nThe family of this Master Todd were hangers-on of the house of Osborne. The old gentleman had advanced Todd from being a clerk to be a junior partner in his establishment.\n\nMr. Osborne was the godfather of young Master Todd (who in subsequent life wrote Mr. Osborne Todd on his cards and became a man of decided fashion), while Miss Osborne had accompanied Miss Maria Todd to the font, and gave her protegee a prayer-book, a collection of tracts, a volume of very low church poetry, or some such memento of her goodness every year. Miss O. drove the Todds out in her carriage now and then; when they were ill, her footman, in large plush smalls and waistcoat, brought jellies and delicacies from Russell Square to Coram Street. Coram Street trembled and looked up to Russell Square indeed, and Mrs. Todd, who had a pretty hand at cutting out paper trimmings for haunches of mutton, and could make flowers, ducks, &c., out of turnips and carrots in a very creditable manner, would go to \"the Square,\" as it was called, and assist in the preparations incident to a great dinner, without even so much as thinking of sitting down to the banquet. If any guest failed at the eleventh hour, Todd was asked to dine. Mrs. Todd and Maria came across in the evening, slipped in with a muffled knock, and were in the drawing-room by the time Miss Osborne and the ladies under her convoy reached that apartment--and ready to fire off duets and sing until the gentlemen came up. Poor Maria Todd; poor young lady! How she had to work and thrum at these duets and sonatas in the Street, before they appeared in public in the Square!\n\nThus it seemed to be decreed by fate that Georgy was to domineer over everybody with whom he came in contact, and that friends, relatives, and domestics were all to bow the knee before the little fellow. It must be owned that he accommodated himself very willingly to this arrangement. Most people do so. And Georgy liked to play the part of master and perhaps had a natural aptitude for it.\n\nIn Russell Square everybody was afraid of Mr. Osborne, and Mr. Osborne was afraid of Georgy. The boy's dashing manners, and offhand rattle about books and learning, his likeness to his father (dead unreconciled in Brussels yonder) awed the old gentleman and gave the young boy the mastery. The old man would start at some hereditary feature or tone unconsciously used by the little lad, and fancy that George's father was again before him. He tried by indulgence to the grandson to make up for harshness to the elder George. People were surprised at his gentleness to the boy. He growled and swore at Miss Osborne as usual, and would smile when George came down late for breakfast.\n\nMiss Osborne, George's aunt, was a faded old spinster, broken down by more than forty years of dulness and coarse usage. It was easy for a lad of spirit to master her. And whenever George wanted anything from her, from the jam-pots in her cupboards to the cracked and dry old colours in her paint-box (the old paint-box which she had had when she was a pupil of Mr. Smee and was still almost young and blooming), Georgy took possession of the object of his desire, which obtained, he took no further notice of his aunt.\n\nFor his friends and cronies, he had a pompous old schoolmaster, who flattered him, and a toady, his senior, whom he could thrash. It was dear Mrs. Todd's delight to leave him with her youngest daughter, Rosa Jemima, a darling child of eight years old. The little pair looked so well together, she would say (but not to the folks in \"the Square,\" we may be sure) \"who knows what might happen? Don't they make a pretty little couple?\" the fond mother thought.\n\nThe broken-spirited, old, maternal grandfather was likewise subject to the little tyrant. He could not help respecting a lad who had such fine clothes and rode with a groom behind him. Georgy, on his side, was in the constant habit of hearing coarse abuse and vulgar satire levelled at John Sedley by his pitiless old enemy, Mr. Osborne. Osborne used to call the other the old pauper, the old coal-man, the old bankrupt, and by many other such names of brutal contumely. How was little George to respect a man so prostrate? A few months after he was with his paternal grandfather, Mrs. Sedley died. There had been little love between her and the child. He did not care to show much grief. He came down to visit his mother in a fine new suit of mourning, and was very angry that he could not go to a play upon which he had set his heart.\n\nThe illness of that old lady had been the occupation and perhaps the safeguard of Amelia. What do men know about women's martyrdoms? We should go mad had we to endure the hundredth part of those daily pains which are meekly borne by many women. Ceaseless slavery meeting with no reward; constant gentleness and kindness met by cruelty as constant; love, labour, patience, watchfulness, without even so much as the acknowledgement of a good word; all this, how many of them have to bear in quiet, and appear abroad with cheerful faces as if they felt nothing. Tender slaves that they are, they must needs be hypocrites and weak.\n\nFrom her chair Amelia's mother had taken to her bed, which she had never left, and from which Mrs. Osborne herself was never absent except when she ran to see George. The old lady grudged her even those rare visits; she, who had been a kind, smiling, good-natured mother once, in the days of her prosperity, but whom poverty and infirmities had broken down. Her illness or estrangement did not affect Amelia. They rather enabled her to support the other calamity under which she was suffering, and from the thoughts of which she was kept by the ceaseless calls of the invalid. Amelia bore her harshness quite gently; smoothed the uneasy pillow; was always ready with a soft answer to the watchful, querulous voice; soothed the sufferer with words of hope, such as her pious simple heart could best feel and utter, and closed the eyes that had once looked so tenderly upon her.\n\nThen all her time and tenderness were devoted to the consolation and comfort of the bereaved old father, who was stunned by the blow which had befallen him, and stood utterly alone in the world. His wife, his honour, his fortune, everything he loved best had fallen away from him. There was only Amelia to stand by and support with her gentle arms the tottering, heart-broken old man. We are not going to write the history: it would be too dreary and stupid. I can see Vanity Fair yawning over it d'avance.\n\nOne day as the young gentlemen were assembled in the study at the Rev. Mr. Veal's, and the domestic chaplain to the Right Honourable the Earl of Bareacres was spouting away as usual, a smart carriage drove up to the door decorated with the statue of Athene, and two gentlemen stepped out. The young Masters Bangles rushed to the window with a vague notion that their father might have arrived from Bombay. The great hulking scholar of three-and-twenty, who was crying secretly over a passage of Eutropius, flattened his neglected nose against the panes and looked at the drag, as the laquais de place sprang from the box and let out the persons in the carriage.\n\n\"It's a fat one and a thin one,\" Mr. Bluck said as a thundering knock came to the door.\n\nEverybody was interested, from the domestic chaplain himself, who hoped he saw the fathers of some future pupils, down to Master Georgy, glad of any pretext for laying his book down.\n\nThe boy in the shabby livery with the faded copper buttons, who always thrust himself into the tight coat to open the door, came into the study and said, \"Two gentlemen want to see Master Osborne.\" The professor had had a trifling altercation in the morning with that young gentleman, owing to a difference about the introduction of crackers in school-time; but his face resumed its habitual expression of bland courtesy as he said, \"Master Osborne, I give you full permission to go and see your carriage friends--to whom I beg you to convey the respectful compliments of myself and Mrs. Veal.\"\n\nGeorgy went into the reception-room and saw two strangers, whom he looked at with his head up, in his usual haughty manner. One was fat, with mustachios, and the other was lean and long, in a blue frock-coat, with a brown face and a grizzled head.\n\n\"My God, how like he is!\" said the long gentleman with a start. \"Can you guess who we are, George?\"\n\nThe boy's face flushed up, as it did usually when he was moved, and his eyes brightened. \"I don't know the other,\" he said, \"but I should think you must be Major Dobbin.\"\n\nIndeed it was our old friend. His voice trembled with pleasure as he greeted the boy, and taking both the other's hands in his own, drew the lad to him.\n\n\"Your mother has talked to you about me--has she?\" he said.\n\n\"That she has,\" Georgy answered, \"hundreds and hundreds of times.\"\n\nEothen\nIt was one of the many causes for personal pride with which old Osborne chose to recreate himself that Sedley, his ancient rival, enemy, and benefactor, was in his last days so utterly defeated and humiliated as to be forced to accept pecuniary obligations at the hands of the man who had most injured and insulted him. The successful man of the world cursed the old pauper and relieved him from time to time. As he furnished George with money for his mother, he gave the boy to understand by hints, delivered in his brutal, coarse way, that George's maternal grandfather was but a wretched old bankrupt and dependant, and that John Sedley might thank the man to whom he already owed ever so much money for the aid which his generosity now chose to administer. George carried the pompous supplies to his mother and the shattered old widower whom it was now the main business of her life to tend and comfort. The little fellow patronized the feeble and disappointed old man.\n\nIt may have shown a want of \"proper pride\" in Amelia that she chose to accept these money benefits at the hands of her father's enemy. But proper pride and this poor lady had never had much acquaintance together. A disposition naturally simple and demanding protection; a long course of poverty and humility, of daily privations, and hard words, of kind offices and no returns, had been her lot ever since womanhood almost, or since her luckless marriage with George Osborne. You who see your betters bearing up under this shame every day, meekly suffering under the slights of fortune, gentle and unpitied, poor, and rather despised for their poverty, do you ever step down from your prosperity and wash the feet of these poor wearied beggars? The very thought of them is odious and low. \"There must be classes--there must be rich and poor,\" Dives says, smacking his claret (it is well if he even sends the broken meat out to Lazarus sitting under the window). Very true; but think how mysterious and often unaccountable it is--that lottery of life which gives to this man the purple and fine linen and sends to the other rags for garments and dogs for comforters.\n\nSo I must own that, without much repining, on the contrary with something akin to gratitude, Amelia took the crumbs that her father-in-law let drop now and then, and with them fed her own parent. Directly she understood it to be her duty, it was this young woman's nature (ladies, she is but thirty still, and we choose to call her a young woman even at that age) it was, I say, her nature to sacrifice herself and to fling all that she had at the feet of the beloved object. During what long thankless nights had she worked out her fingers for little Georgy whilst at home with her; what buffets, scorns, privations, poverties had she endured for father and mother! And in the midst of all these solitary resignations and unseen sacrifices, she did not respect herself any more than the world respected her, but I believe thought in her heart that she was a poor-spirited, despicable little creature, whose luck in life was only too good for her merits. O you poor women! O you poor secret martyrs and victims, whose life is a torture, who are stretched on racks in your bedrooms, and who lay your heads down on the block daily at the drawing-room table; every man who watches your pains, or peers into those dark places where the torture is administered to you, must pity you--and--and thank God that he has a beard. I recollect seeing, years ago, at the prisons for idiots and madmen at Bicetre, near Paris, a poor wretch bent down under the bondage of his imprisonment and his personal infirmity, to whom one of our party gave a halfpenny worth of snuff in a cornet or \"screw\" of paper. The kindness was too much for the poor epileptic creature. He cried in an anguish of delight and gratitude: if anybody gave you and me a thousand a year, or saved our lives, we could not be so affected. And so, if you properly tyrannize over a woman, you will find a ha'p'orth of kindness act upon her and bring tears into her eyes, as though you were an angel benefiting her.\n\nSome such boons as these were the best which Fortune allotted to poor little Amelia. Her life, begun not unprosperously, had come down to this--to a mean prison and a long, ignoble bondage. Little George visited her captivity sometimes and consoled it with feeble gleams of encouragement. Russell Square was the boundary of her prison: she might walk thither occasionally, but was always back to sleep in her cell at night; to perform cheerless duties; to watch by thankless sick-beds; to suffer the harassment and tyranny of querulous disappointed old age. How many thousands of people are there, women for the most part, who are doomed to endure this long slavery?--who are hospital nurses without wages--sisters of Charity, if you like, without the romance and the sentiment of sacrifice--who strive, fast, watch, and suffer, unpitied, and fade away ignobly and unknown.\n\nThe hidden and awful Wisdom which apportions the destinies of mankind is pleased so to humiliate and cast down the tender, good, and wise, and to set up the selfish, the foolish, or the wicked. Oh, be humble, my brother, in your prosperity! Be gentle with those who are less lucky, if not more deserving. Think, what right have you to be scornful, whose virtue is a deficiency of temptation, whose success may be a chance, whose rank may be an ancestor's accident, whose prosperity is very likely a satire.\n\nThey buried Amelia's mother in the churchyard at Brompton, upon just such a rainy, dark day as Amelia recollected when first she had been there to marry George. Her little boy sat by her side in pompous new sables. She remembered the old pew-woman and clerk. Her thoughts were away in other times as the parson read. But that she held George's hand in her own, perhaps she would have liked to change places with.... Then, as usual, she felt ashamed of her selfish thoughts and prayed inwardly to be strengthened to do her duty.\n\nSo she determined with all her might and strength to try and make her old father happy. She slaved, toiled, patched, and mended, sang and played backgammon, read out the newspaper, cooked dishes for old Sedley, walked him out sedulously into Kensington Gardens or the Brompton Lanes, listened to his stories with untiring smiles and affectionate hypocrisy, or sat musing by his side and communing with her own thoughts and reminiscences, as the old man, feeble and querulous, sunned himself on the garden benches and prattled about his wrongs or his sorrows. What sad, unsatisfactory thoughts those of the widow were! The children running up and down the slopes and broad paths in the gardens reminded her of George, who was taken from her; the first George was taken from her; her selfish, guilty love, in both instances, had been rebuked and bitterly chastised. She strove to think it was right that she should be so punished. She was such a miserable wicked sinner. She was quite alone in the world.\n\nI know that the account of this kind of solitary imprisonment is insufferably tedious, unless there is some cheerful or humorous incident to enliven it--a tender gaoler, for instance, or a waggish commandant of the fortress, or a mouse to come out and play about Latude's beard and whiskers, or a subterranean passage under the castle, dug by Trenck with his nails and a toothpick: the historian has no such enlivening incident to relate in the narrative of Amelia's captivity. Fancy her, if you please, during this period, very sad, but always ready to smile when spoken to; in a very mean, poor, not to say vulgar position of life; singing songs, making puddings, playing cards, mending stockings, for her old father's benefit. So, never mind, whether she be a heroine or no; or you and I, however old, scolding, and bankrupt--may we have in our last days a kind soft shoulder on which to lean and a gentle hand to soothe our gouty old pillows.\n\nOld Sedley grew very fond of his daughter after his wife's death, and Amelia had her consolation in doing her duty by the old man.\n\nBut we are not going to leave these two people long in such a low and ungenteel station of life. Better days, as far as worldly prosperity went, were in store for both. Perhaps the ingenious reader has guessed who was the stout gentleman who called upon Georgy at his school in company with our old friend Major Dobbin. It was another old acquaintance returned to England, and at a time when his presence was likely to be of great comfort to his relatives there.\n\nMajor Dobbin having easily succeeded in getting leave from his good-natured commandant to proceed to Madras, and thence probably to Europe, on urgent private affairs, never ceased travelling night and day until he reached his journey's end, and had directed his march with such celerity that he arrived at Madras in a high fever. His servants who accompanied him brought him to the house of the friend with whom he had resolved to stay until his departure for Europe in a state of delirium; and it was thought for many, many days that he would never travel farther than the burying-ground of the church of St. George's, where the troops should fire a salvo over his grave, and where many a gallant officer lies far away from his home.\n\nHere, as the poor fellow lay tossing in his fever, the people who watched him might have heard him raving about Amelia. The idea that he should never see her again depressed him in his lucid hours. He thought his last day was come, and he made his solemn preparations for departure, setting his affairs in this world in order and leaving the little property of which he was possessed to those whom he most desired to benefit. The friend in whose house he was located witnessed his testament. He desired to be buried with a little brown hair-chain which he wore round his neck and which, if the truth must be known, he had got from Amelia's maid at Brussels, when the young widow's hair was cut off, during the fever which prostrated her after the death of George Osborne on the plateau at Mount St. John.\n\nHe recovered, rallied, relapsed again, having undergone such a process of blood-letting and calomel as showed the strength of his original constitution. He was almost a skeleton when they put him on board the Ramchunder East Indiaman, Captain Bragg, from Calcutta, touching at Madras, and so weak and prostrate that his friend who had tended him through his illness prophesied that the honest Major would never survive the voyage, and that he would pass some morning, shrouded in flag and hammock, over the ship's side, and carrying down to the sea with him the relic that he wore at his heart. But whether it was the sea air, or the hope which sprung up in him afresh, from the day that the ship spread her canvas and stood out of the roads towards home, our friend began to amend, and he was quite well (though as gaunt as a greyhound) before they reached the Cape. \"Kirk will be disappointed of his majority this time,\" he said with a smile; \"he will expect to find himself gazetted by the time the regiment reaches home.\" For it must be premised that while the Major was lying ill at Madras, having made such prodigious haste to go thither, the gallant --th, which had passed many years abroad, which after its return from the West Indies had been baulked of its stay at home by the Waterloo campaign, and had been ordered from Flanders to India, had received orders home; and the Major might have accompanied his comrades, had he chosen to wait for their arrival at Madras.\n\nPerhaps he was not inclined to put himself in his exhausted state again under the guardianship of Glorvina. \"I think Miss O'Dowd would have done for me,\" he said laughingly to a fellow-passenger, \"if we had had her on board, and when she had sunk me, she would have fallen upon you, depend upon it, and carried you in as a prize to Southampton, Jos, my boy.\"\n\nFor indeed it was no other than our stout friend who was also a passenger on board the Ramchunder. He had passed ten years in Bengal. Constant dinners, tiffins, pale ale and claret, the prodigious labour of cutcherry, and the refreshment of brandy-pawnee which he was forced to take there, had their effect upon Waterloo Sedley. A voyage to Europe was pronounced necessary for him--and having served his full time in India and had fine appointments which had enabled him to lay by a considerable sum of money, he was free to come home and stay with a good pension, or to return and resume that rank in the service to which his seniority and his vast talents entitled him.\n\nHe was rather thinner than when we last saw him, but had gained in majesty and solemnity of demeanour. He had resumed the mustachios to which his services at Waterloo entitled him, and swaggered about on deck in a magnificent velvet cap with a gold band and a profuse ornamentation of pins and jewellery about his person. He took breakfast in his cabin and dressed as solemnly to appear on the quarter-deck as if he were going to turn out for Bond Street, or the Course at Calcutta. He brought a native servant with him, who was his valet and pipe-bearer and who wore the Sedley crest in silver on his turban. That oriental menial had a wretched life under the tyranny of Jos Sedley. Jos was as vain of his person as a woman, and took as long a time at his toilette as any fading beauty. The youngsters among the passengers, Young Chaffers of the 150th, and poor little Ricketts, coming home after his third fever, used to draw out Sedley at the cuddy-table and make him tell prodigious stories about himself and his exploits against tigers and Napoleon. He was great when he visited the Emperor's tomb at Longwood, when to these gentlemen and the young officers of the ship, Major Dobbin not being by, he described the whole battle of Waterloo and all but announced that Napoleon never would have gone to Saint Helena at all but for him, Jos Sedley.\n\nAfter leaving St. Helena he became very generous, disposing of a great quantity of ship stores, claret, preserved meats, and great casks packed with soda-water, brought out for his private delectation. There were no ladies on board; the Major gave the pas of precedency to the civilian, so that he was the first dignitary at table, and treated by Captain Bragg and the officers of the Ramchunder with the respect which his rank warranted. He disappeared rather in a panic during a two-days' gale, in which he had the portholes of his cabin battened down, and remained in his cot reading the Washerwoman of Finchley Common, left on board the Ramchunder by the Right Honourable the Lady Emily Hornblower, wife of the Rev. Silas Hornblower, when on their passage out to the Cape, where the Reverend gentleman was a missionary; but, for common reading, he had brought a stock of novels and plays which he lent to the rest of the ship, and rendered himself agreeable to all by his kindness and condescension.\n\nMany and many a night as the ship was cutting through the roaring dark sea, the moon and stars shining overhead and the bell singing out the watch, Mr. Sedley and the Major would sit on the quarter-deck of the vessel talking about home, as the Major smoked his cheroot and the civilian puffed at the hookah which his servant prepared for him.\n\nIn these conversations it was wonderful with what perseverance and ingenuity Major Dobbin would manage to bring the talk round to the subject of Amelia and her little boy. Jos, a little testy about his father's misfortunes and unceremonious applications to him, was soothed down by the Major, who pointed out the elder's ill fortunes and old age. He would not perhaps like to live with the old couple, whose ways and hours might not agree with those of a younger man, accustomed to different society (Jos bowed at this compliment); but, the Major pointed out, how advantageous it would be for Jos Sedley to have a house of his own in London, and not a mere bachelor's establishment as before; how his sister Amelia would be the very person to preside over it; how elegant, how gentle she was, and of what refined good manners. He recounted stories of the success which Mrs. George Osborne had had in former days at Brussels, and in London, where she was much admired by people of very great fashion; and he then hinted how becoming it would be for Jos to send Georgy to a good school and make a man of him, for his mother and her parents would be sure to spoil him. In a word, this artful Major made the civilian promise to take charge of Amelia and her unprotected child. He did not know as yet what events had happened in the little Sedley family, and how death had removed the mother, and riches had carried off George from Amelia. But the fact is that every day and always, this love-smitten and middle-aged gentleman was thinking about Mrs. Osborne, and his whole heart was bent upon doing her good. He coaxed, wheedled, cajoled, and complimented Jos Sedley with a perseverance and cordiality of which he was not aware himself, very likely; but some men who have unmarried sisters or daughters even, may remember how uncommonly agreeable gentlemen are to the male relations when they are courting the females; and perhaps this rogue of a Dobbin was urged by a similar hypocrisy.\n\nThe truth is, when Major Dobbin came on board the Ramchumder, very sick, and for the three days she lay in the Madras Roads, he did not begin to rally, nor did even the appearance and recognition of his old acquaintance, Mr. Sedley, on board much cheer him, until after a conversation which they had one day, as the Major was laid languidly on the deck. He said then he thought he was doomed; he had left a little something to his godson in his will, and he trusted Mrs. Osborne would remember him kindly and be happy in the marriage she was about to make. \"Married? not the least,\" Jos answered; \"he had heard from her: she made no mention of the marriage, and by the way, it was curious, she wrote to say that Major Dobbin was going to be married, and hoped that HE would be happy.\" What were the dates of Sedley's letters from Europe? The civilian fetched them. They were two months later than the Major's; and the ship's surgeon congratulated himself upon the treatment adopted by him towards his new patient, who had been consigned to shipboard by the Madras practitioner with very small hopes indeed; for, from that day, the very day that he changed the draught, Major Dobbin began to mend. And thus it was that deserving officer, Captain Kirk, was disappointed of his majority.\n\nAfter they passed St. Helena, Major Dobbin's gaiety and strength was such as to astonish all his fellow passengers. He larked with the midshipmen, played single-stick with the mates, ran up the shrouds like a boy, sang a comic song one night to the amusement of the whole party assembled over their grog after supper, and rendered himself so gay, lively, and amiable that even Captain Bragg, who thought there was nothing in his passenger, and considered he was a poor-spirited feller at first, was constrained to own that the Major was a reserved but well-informed and meritorious officer. \"He ain't got distangy manners, dammy,\" Bragg observed to his first mate; \"he wouldn't do at Government House, Roper, where his Lordship and Lady William was as kind to me, and shook hands with me before the whole company, and asking me at dinner to take beer with him, before the Commander-in-Chief himself; he ain't got manners, but there's something about him--\" And thus Captain Bragg showed that he possessed discrimination as a man, as well as ability as a commander.\n\nBut a calm taking place when the Ramchunder was within ten days' sail of England, Dobbin became so impatient and ill-humoured as to surprise those comrades who had before admired his vivacity and good temper. He did not recover until the breeze sprang up again, and was in a highly excited state when the pilot came on board. Good God, how his heart beat as the two friendly spires of Southampton came in sight.\n\nOur Friend the Major\nOur Major had rendered himself so popular on board the Ramchunder that when he and Mr. Sedley descended into the welcome shore-boat which was to take them from the ship, the whole crew, men and officers, the great Captain Bragg himself leading off, gave three cheers for Major Dobbin, who blushed very much and ducked his head in token of thanks. Jos, who very likely thought the cheers were for himself, took off his gold-laced cap and waved it majestically to his friends, and they were pulled to shore and landed with great dignity at the pier, whence they proceeded to the Royal George Hotel.\n\nAlthough the sight of that magnificent round of beef, and the silver tankard suggestive of real British home-brewed ale and porter, which perennially greet the eyes of the traveller returning from foreign parts who enters the coffee-room of the George, are so invigorating and delightful that a man entering such a comfortable snug homely English inn might well like to stop some days there, yet Dobbin began to talk about a post-chaise instantly, and was no sooner at Southampton than he wished to be on the road to London. Jos, however, would not hear of moving that evening. Why was he to pass a night in a post-chaise instead of a great large undulating downy feather-bed which was there ready to replace the horrid little narrow crib in which the portly Bengal gentleman had been confined during the voyage? He could not think of moving till his baggage was cleared, or of travelling until he could do so with his chillum. So the Major was forced to wait over that night, and dispatched a letter to his family announcing his arrival, entreating from Jos a promise to write to his own friends. Jos promised, but didn't keep his promise. The Captain, the surgeon, and one or two passengers came and dined with our two gentlemen at the inn, Jos exerting himself in a sumptuous way in ordering the dinner and promising to go to town the next day with the Major. The landlord said it did his eyes good to see Mr. Sedley take off his first pint of porter. If I had time and dared to enter into digressions, I would write a chapter about that first pint of porter drunk upon English ground. Ah, how good it is! It is worth-while to leave home for a year, just to enjoy that one draught.\n\nMajor Dobbin made his appearance the next morning very neatly shaved and dressed, according to his wont. Indeed, it was so early in the morning that nobody was up in the house except that wonderful Boots of an inn who never seems to want sleep; and the Major could hear the snores of the various inmates of the house roaring through the corridors as he creaked about in those dim passages. Then the sleepless Boots went shirking round from door to door, gathering up at each the Bluchers, Wellingtons, Oxonians, which stood outside. Then Jos's native servant arose and began to get ready his master's ponderous dressing apparatus and prepare his hookah; then the maidservants got up, and meeting the dark man in the passages, shrieked, and mistook him for the devil. He and Dobbin stumbled over their pails in the passages as they were scouring the decks of the Royal George. When the first unshorn waiter appeared and unbarred the door of the inn, the Major thought that the time for departure was arrived, and ordered a post-chaise to be fetched instantly, that they might set off.\n\nHe then directed his steps to Mr. Sedley's room and opened the curtains of the great large family bed wherein Mr. Jos was snoring. \"Come, up! Sedley,\" the Major said, \"it's time to be off; the chaise will be at the door in half an hour.\"\n\nJos growled from under the counterpane to know what the time was; but when he at last extorted from the blushing Major (who never told fibs, however they might be to his advantage) what was the real hour of the morning, he broke out into a volley of bad language, which we will not repeat here, but by which he gave Dobbin to understand that he would jeopardy his soul if he got up at that moment, that the Major might go and be hanged, that he would not travel with Dobbin, and that it was most unkind and ungentlemanlike to disturb a man out of his sleep in that way; on which the discomfited Major was obliged to retreat, leaving Jos to resume his interrupted slumbers.\n\nThe chaise came up presently, and the Major would wait no longer.\n\nIf he had been an English nobleman travelling on a pleasure tour, or a newspaper courier bearing dispatches (government messages are generally carried much more quietly), he could not have travelled more quickly. The post-boys wondered at the fees he flung amongst them. How happy and green the country looked as the chaise whirled rapidly from mile-stone to mile-stone, through neat country towns where landlords came out to welcome him with smiles and bows; by pretty roadside inns, where the signs hung on the elms, and horses and waggoners were drinking under the chequered shadow of the trees; by old halls and parks; rustic hamlets clustered round ancient grey churches--and through the charming friendly English landscape. Is there any in the world like it? To a traveller returning home it looks so kind--it seems to shake hands with you as you pass through it. Well, Major Dobbin passed through all this from Southampton to London, and without noting much beyond the milestones along the road. You see he was so eager to see his parents at Camberwell.\n\nHe grudged the time lost between Piccadilly and his old haunt at the Slaughters', whither he drove faithfully. Long years had passed since he saw it last, since he and George, as young men, had enjoyed many a feast, and held many a revel there. He had now passed into the stage of old-fellow-hood. His hair was grizzled, and many a passion and feeling of his youth had grown grey in that interval. There, however, stood the old waiter at the door, in the same greasy black suit, with the same double chin and flaccid face, with the same huge bunch of seals at his fob, rattling his money in his pockets as before, and receiving the Major as if he had gone away only a week ago. \"Put the Major's things in twenty-three, that's his room,\" John said, exhibiting not the least surprise. \"Roast fowl for your dinner, I suppose. You ain't got married? They said you was married--the Scotch surgeon of yours was here. No, it was Captain Humby of the thirty-third, as was quartered with the --th in Injee. Like any warm water? What do you come in a chay for--ain't the coach good enough?\" And with this, the faithful waiter, who knew and remembered every officer who used the house, and with whom ten years were but as yesterday, led the way up to Dobbin's old room, where stood the great moreen bed, and the shabby carpet, a thought more dingy, and all the old black furniture covered with faded chintz, just as the Major recollected them in his youth.\n\nHe remembered George pacing up and down the room, and biting his nails, and swearing that the Governor must come round, and that if he didn't, he didn't care a straw, on the day before he was married. He could fancy him walking in, banging the door of Dobbin's room, and his own hard by--\n\n\"You ain't got young,\" John said, calmly surveying his friend of former days.\n\nDobbin laughed. \"Ten years and a fever don't make a man young, John,\" he said. \"It is you that are always young--no, you are always old.\"\n\n\"What became of Captain Osborne's widow?\" John said. \"Fine young fellow that. Lord, how he used to spend his money. He never came back after that day he was marched from here. He owes me three pound at this minute. Look here, I have it in my book. 'April 10, 1815, Captain Osborne: 3 pounds.' I wonder whether his father would pay me,\" and so saying, John of the Slaughters' pulled out the very morocco pocket-book in which he had noted his loan to the Captain, upon a greasy faded page still extant, with many other scrawled memoranda regarding the bygone frequenters of the house.\n\nHaving inducted his customer into the room, John retired with perfect calmness; and Major Dobbin, not without a blush and a grin at his own absurdity, chose out of his kit the very smartest and most becoming civil costume he possessed, and laughed at his own tanned face and grey hair, as he surveyed them in the dreary little toilet-glass on the dressing-table.\n\n\"I'm glad old John didn't forget me,\" he thought. \"She'll know me, too, I hope.\" And he sallied out of the inn, bending his steps once more in the direction of Brompton.\n\nEvery minute incident of his last meeting with Amelia was present to the constant man's mind as he walked towards her house. The arch and the Achilles statue were up since he had last been in Piccadilly; a hundred changes had occurred which his eye and mind vaguely noted. He began to tremble as he walked up the lane from Brompton, that well-remembered lane leading to the street where she lived. Was she going to be married or not? If he were to meet her with the little boy--Good God, what should he do? He saw a woman coming to him with a child of five years old--was that she? He began to shake at the mere possibility. When he came up to the row of houses, at last, where she lived, and to the gate, he caught hold of it and paused. He might have heard the thumping of his own heart. \"May God Almighty bless her, whatever has happened,\" he thought to himself. \"Psha! she may be gone from here,\" he said and went in through the gate.\n\nThe window of the parlour which she used to occupy was open, and there were no inmates in the room. The Major thought he recognized the piano, though, with the picture over it, as it used to be in former days, and his perturbations were renewed. Mr. Clapp's brass plate was still on the door, at the knocker of which Dobbin performed a summons.\n\nA buxom-looking lass of sixteen, with bright eyes and purple cheeks, came to answer the knock and looked hard at the Major as he leant back against the little porch.\n\nHe was as pale as a ghost and could hardly falter out the words--\"Does Mrs. Osborne live here?\"\n\nShe looked him hard in the face for a moment--and then turning white too--said, \"Lord bless me--it's Major Dobbin.\" She held out both her hands shaking--\"Don't you remember me?\" she said. \"I used to call you Major Sugarplums.\" On which, and I believe it was for the first time that he ever so conducted himself in his life, the Major took the girl in his arms and kissed her. She began to laugh and cry hysterically, and calling out \"Ma, Pa!\" with all her voice, brought up those worthy people, who had already been surveying the Major from the casement of the ornamental kitchen, and were astonished to find their daughter in the little passage in the embrace of a great tall man in a blue frock-coat and white duck trousers.\n\n\"I'm an old friend,\" he said--not without blushing though. \"Don't you remember me, Mrs. Clapp, and those good cakes you used to make for tea? Don't you recollect me, Clapp? I'm George's godfather, and just come back from India.\" A great shaking of hands ensued--Mrs. Clapp was greatly affected and delighted; she called upon heaven to interpose a vast many times in that passage.\n\nThe landlord and landlady of the house led the worthy Major into the Sedleys' room (whereof he remembered every single article of furniture, from the old brass ornamented piano, once a natty little instrument, Stothard maker, to the screens and the alabaster miniature tombstone, in the midst of which ticked Mr. Sedley's gold watch), and there, as he sat down in the lodger's vacant arm-chair, the father, the mother, and the daughter, with a thousand ejaculatory breaks in the narrative, informed Major Dobbin of what we know already, but of particulars in Amelia's history of which he was not aware--namely of Mrs. Sedley's death, of George's reconcilement with his grandfather Osborne, of the way in which the widow took on at leaving him, and of other particulars of her life. Twice or thrice he was going to ask about the marriage question, but his heart failed him. He did not care to lay it bare to these people. Finally, he was informed that Mrs. O. was gone to walk with her pa in Kensington Gardens, whither she always went with the old gentleman (who was very weak and peevish now, and led her a sad life, though she behaved to him like an angel, to be sure), of a fine afternoon, after dinner.\n\n\"I'm very much pressed for time,\" the Major said, \"and have business to-night of importance. I should like to see Mrs. Osborne tho'. Suppose Miss Polly would come with me and show me the way?\"\n\nMiss Polly was charmed and astonished at this proposal. She knew the way. She would show Major Dobbin. She had often been with Mr. Sedley when Mrs. O. was gone--was gone Russell Square way--and knew the bench where he liked to sit. She bounced away to her apartment and appeared presently in her best bonnet and her mamma's yellow shawl and large pebble brooch, of which she assumed the loan in order to make herself a worthy companion for the Major.\n\nThat officer, then, in his blue frock-coat and buckskin gloves, gave the young lady his arm, and they walked away very gaily. He was glad to have a friend at hand for the scene which he dreaded somehow. He asked a thousand more questions from his companion about Amelia: his kind heart grieved to think that she should have had to part with her son. How did she bear it? Did she see him often? Was Mr. Sedley pretty comfortable now in a worldly point of view? Polly answered all these questions of Major Sugarplums to the very best of her power.\n\nAnd in the midst of their walk an incident occurred which, though very simple in its nature, was productive of the greatest delight to Major Dobbin. A pale young man with feeble whiskers and a stiff white neckcloth came walking down the lane, en sandwich--having a lady, that is, on each arm. One was a tall and commanding middle-aged female, with features and a complexion similar to those of the clergyman of the Church of England by whose side she marched, and the other a stunted little woman with a dark face, ornamented by a fine new bonnet and white ribbons, and in a smart pelisse, with a rich gold watch in the midst of her person. The gentleman, pinioned as he was by these two ladies, carried further a parasol, shawl, and basket, so that his arms were entirely engaged, and of course he was unable to touch his hat in acknowledgement of the curtsey with which Miss Mary Clapp greeted him.\n\nHe merely bowed his head in reply to her salutation, which the two ladies returned with a patronizing air, and at the same time looking severely at the individual in the blue coat and bamboo cane who accompanied Miss Polly.\n\n\"Who's that?\" asked the Major, amused by the group, and after he had made way for the three to pass up the lane. Mary looked at him rather roguishly.\n\n\"That is our curate, the Reverend Mr. Binny (a twitch from Major Dobbin), and his sister Miss B. Lord bless us, how she did use to worret us at Sunday-school; and the other lady, the little one with a cast in her eye and the handsome watch, is Mrs. Binny--Miss Grits that was; her pa was a grocer, and kept the Little Original Gold Tea Pot in Kensington Gravel Pits. They were married last month, and are just come back from Margate. She's five thousand pound to her fortune; but her and Miss B., who made the match, have quarrelled already.\"\n\nIf the Major had twitched before, he started now, and slapped the bamboo on the ground with an emphasis which made Miss Clapp cry, \"Law,\" and laugh too. He stood for a moment, silent, with open mouth, looking after the retreating young couple, while Miss Mary told their history; but he did not hear beyond the announcement of the reverend gentleman's marriage; his head was swimming with felicity. After this rencontre he began to walk double quick towards the place of his destination--and yet they were too soon (for he was in a great tremor at the idea of a meeting for which he had been longing any time these ten years)--through the Brompton lanes, and entering at the little old portal in Kensington Garden wall.\n\n\"There they are,\" said Miss Polly, and she felt him again start back on her arm. She was a confidante at once of the whole business. She knew the story as well as if she had read it in one of her favourite novel-books--Fatherless Fanny, or the Scottish Chiefs.\n\n\"Suppose you were to run on and tell her,\" the Major said. Polly ran forward, her yellow shawl streaming in the breeze.\n\nOld Sedley was seated on a bench, his handkerchief placed over his knees, prattling away, according to his wont, with some old story about old times to which Amelia had listened and awarded a patient smile many a time before. She could of late think of her own affairs, and smile or make other marks of recognition of her father's stories, scarcely hearing a word of the old man's tales. As Mary came bouncing along, and Amelia caught sight of her, she started up from her bench. Her first thought was that something had happened to Georgy, but the sight of the messenger's eager and happy face dissipated that fear in the timorous mother's bosom.\n\n\"News! News!\" cried the emissary of Major Dobbin. \"He's come! He's come!\"\n\n\"Who is come?\" said Emmy, still thinking of her son.\n\n\"Look there,\" answered Miss Clapp, turning round and pointing; in which direction Amelia looking, saw Dobbin's lean figure and long shadow stalking across the grass. Amelia started in her turn, blushed up, and, of course, began to cry. At all this simple little creature's fetes, the grandes eaux were accustomed to play. He looked at her--oh, how fondly--as she came running towards him, her hands before her, ready to give them to him. She wasn't changed. She was a little pale, a little stouter in figure. Her eyes were the same, the kind trustful eyes. There were scarce three lines of silver in her soft brown hair. She gave him both her hands as she looked up flushing and smiling through her tears into his honest homely face. He took the two little hands between his two and held them there. He was speechless for a moment. Why did he not take her in his arms and swear that he would never leave her? She must have yielded: she could not but have obeyed him.\n\n\"I--I've another arrival to announce,\" he said after a pause.\n\n\"Mrs. Dobbin?\" Amelia said, making a movement back--why didn't he speak?\n\n\"No,\" he said, letting her hands go: \"Who has told you those lies? I mean, your brother Jos came in the same ship with me, and is come home to make you all happy.\"\n\n\"Papa, Papa!\" Emmy cried out, \"here are news! My brother is in England. He is come to take care of you. Here is Major Dobbin.\"\n\nMr. Sedley started up, shaking a great deal and gathering up his thoughts. Then he stepped forward and made an old-fashioned bow to the Major, whom he called Mr. Dobbin, and hoped his worthy father, Sir William, was quite well. He proposed to call upon Sir William, who had done him the honour of a visit a short time ago. Sir William had not called upon the old gentleman for eight years--it was that visit he was thinking of returning.\n\n\"He is very much shaken,\" Emmy whispered as Dobbin went up and cordially shook hands with the old man.\n\nAlthough he had such particular business in London that evening, the Major consented to forego it upon Mr. Sedley's invitation to him to come home and partake of tea. Amelia put her arm under that of her young friend with the yellow shawl and headed the party on their return homewards, so that Mr. Sedley fell to Dobbin's share. The old man walked very slowly and told a number of ancient histories about himself and his poor Bessy, his former prosperity, and his bankruptcy. His thoughts, as is usual with failing old men, were quite in former times. The present, with the exception of the one catastrophe which he felt, he knew little about. The Major was glad to let him talk on. His eyes were fixed upon the figure in front of him--the dear little figure always present to his imagination and in his prayers, and visiting his dreams wakeful or slumbering.\n\nAmelia was very happy, smiling, and active all that evening, performing her duties as hostess of the little entertainment with the utmost grace and propriety, as Dobbin thought. His eyes followed her about as they sat in the twilight. How many a time had he longed for that moment and thought of her far away under hot winds and in weary marches, gentle and happy, kindly ministering to the wants of old age, and decorating poverty with sweet submission--as he saw her now. I do not say that his taste was the highest, or that it is the duty of great intellects to be content with a bread-and-butter paradise, such as sufficed our simple old friend; but his desires were of this sort, whether for good or bad, and, with Amelia to help him, he was as ready to drink as many cups of tea as Doctor Johnson.\n\nAmelia seeing this propensity, laughingly encouraged it and looked exceedingly roguish as she administered to him cup after cup. It is true she did not know that the Major had had no dinner and that the cloth was laid for him at the Slaughters', and a plate laid thereon to mark that the table was retained, in that very box in which the Major and George had sat many a time carousing, when she was a child just come home from Miss Pinkerton's school.\n\nThe first thing Mrs. Osborne showed the Major was Georgy's miniature, for which she ran upstairs on her arrival at home. It was not half handsome enough of course for the boy, but wasn't it noble of him to think of bringing it to his mother? Whilst her papa was awake she did not talk much about Georgy. To hear about Mr. Osborne and Russell Square was not agreeable to the old man, who very likely was unconscious that he had been living for some months past mainly on the bounty of his richer rival, and lost his temper if allusion was made to the other.\n\nDobbin told him all, and a little more perhaps than all, that had happened on board the Ramchunder, and exaggerated Jos's benevolent dispositions towards his father and resolution to make him comfortable in his old days. The truth is that during the voyage the Major had impressed this duty most strongly upon his fellow-passenger and extorted promises from him that he would take charge of his sister and her child. He soothed Jos's irritation with regard to the bills which the old gentleman had drawn upon him, gave a laughing account of his own sufferings on the same score and of the famous consignment of wine with which the old man had favoured him, and brought Mr. Jos, who was by no means an ill-natured person when well-pleased and moderately flattered, to a very good state of feeling regarding his relatives in Europe.\n\nAnd in fine I am ashamed to say that the Major stretched the truth so far as to tell old Mr. Sedley that it was mainly a desire to see his parent which brought Jos once more to Europe.\n\nAt his accustomed hour Mr. Sedley began to doze in his chair, and then it was Amelia's opportunity to commence her conversation, which she did with great eagerness--it related exclusively to Georgy. She did not talk at all about her own sufferings at breaking from him, for indeed, this worthy woman, though she was half-killed by the separation from the child, yet thought it was very wicked in her to repine at losing him; but everything concerning him, his virtues, talents, and prospects, she poured out. She described his angelic beauty; narrated a hundred instances of his generosity and greatness of mind whilst living with her; how a Royal Duchess had stopped and admired him in Kensington Gardens; how splendidly he was cared for now, and how he had a groom and a pony; what quickness and cleverness he had, and what a prodigiously well-read and delightful person the Reverend Lawrence Veal was, George's master. \"He knows EVERYTHING,\" Amelia said. \"He has the most delightful parties. You who are so learned yourself, and have read so much, and are so clever and accomplished--don't shake your head and say no--HE always used to say you were--you will be charmed with Mr. Veal's parties. The last Tuesday in every month. He says there is no place in the bar or the senate that Georgy may not aspire to. Look here,\" and she went to the piano-drawer and drew out a theme of Georgy's composition. This great effort of genius, which is still in the possession of George's mother, is as follows:\n\nOn Selfishness--Of all the vices which degrade the human character, Selfishness is the most odious and contemptible. An undue love of Self leads to the most monstrous crimes and occasions the greatest misfortunes both in States and Families. As a selfish man will impoverish his family and often bring them to ruin, so a selfish king brings ruin on his people and often plunges them into war.\n\nExample: The selfishness of Achilles, as remarked by the poet Homer, occasioned a thousand woes to the Greeks--muri Achaiois alge etheke--(Hom. Il. A. 2). The selfishness of the late Napoleon Bonaparte occasioned innumerable wars in Europe and caused him to perish, himself, in a miserable island--that of Saint Helena in the Atlantic Ocean.\n\nWe see by these examples that we are not to consult our own interest and ambition, but that we are to consider the interests of others as well as our own.\n\nGeorge S. Osborne Athene House, 24 April, 1827\n\n\"Think of him writing such a hand, and quoting Greek too, at his age,\" the delighted mother said. \"Oh, William,\" she added, holding out her hand to the Major, \"what a treasure Heaven has given me in that boy! He is the comfort of my life--and he is the image of--of him that's gone!\"\n\n\"Ought I to be angry with her for being faithful to him?\" William thought. \"Ought I to be jealous of my friend in the grave, or hurt that such a heart as Amelia's can love only once and for ever? Oh, George, George, how little you knew the prize you had, though.\" This sentiment passed rapidly through William's mind as he was holding Amelia's hand, whilst the handkerchief was veiling her eyes.\n\n\"Dear friend,\" she said, pressing the hand which held hers, \"how good, how kind you always have been to me! See! Papa is stirring. You will go and see Georgy tomorrow, won't you?\"\n\n\"Not to-morrow,\" said poor old Dobbin. \"I have business.\" He did not like to own that he had not as yet been to his parents' and his dear sister Anne--a remissness for which I am sure every well-regulated person will blame the Major. And presently he took his leave, leaving his address behind him for Jos, against the latter's arrival. And so the first day was over, and he had seen her.\n\nWhen he got back to the Slaughters', the roast fowl was of course cold, in which condition he ate it for supper. And knowing what early hours his family kept, and that it would be needless to disturb their slumbers at so late an hour, it is on record, that Major Dobbin treated himself to half-price at the Haymarket Theatre that evening, where let us hope he enjoyed himself.\n\nThe Old Piano\nThe Major's visit left old John Sedley in a great state of agitation and excitement. His daughter could not induce him to settle down to his customary occupations or amusements that night. He passed the evening fumbling amongst his boxes and desks, untying his papers with trembling hands, and sorting and arranging them against Jos's arrival. He had them in the greatest order--his tapes and his files, his receipts, and his letters with lawyers and correspondents; the documents relative to the wine project (which failed from a most unaccountable accident, after commencing with the most splendid prospects), the coal project (which only a want of capital prevented from becoming the most successful scheme ever put before the public), the patent saw-mills and sawdust consolidation project, &c., &c. All night, until a very late hour, he passed in the preparation of these documents, trembling about from one room to another, with a quivering candle and shaky hands. Here's the wine papers, here's the sawdust, here's the coals; here's my letters to Calcutta and Madras, and replies from Major Dobbin, C.B., and Mr. Joseph Sedley to the same. \"He shall find no irregularity about ME, Emmy,\" the old gentleman said.\n\nEmmy smiled. \"I don't think Jos will care about seeing those papers, Papa,\" she said.\n\n\"You don't know anything about business, my dear,\" answered the sire, shaking his head with an important air. And it must be confessed that on this point Emmy was very ignorant, and that is a pity some people are so knowing. All these twopenny documents arranged on a side table, old Sedley covered them carefully over with a clean bandanna handkerchief (one out of Major Dobbin's lot) and enjoined the maid and landlady of the house, in the most solemn way, not to disturb those papers, which were arranged for the arrival of Mr. Joseph Sedley the next morning, \"Mr. Joseph Sedley of the Honourable East India Company's Bengal Civil Service.\"\n\nAmelia found him up very early the next morning, more eager, more hectic, and more shaky than ever. \"I didn't sleep much, Emmy, my dear,\" he said. \"I was thinking of my poor Bessy. I wish she was alive, to ride in Jos's carriage once again. She kept her own and became it very well.\" And his eyes filled with tears, which trickled down his furrowed old face. Amelia wiped them away, and smilingly kissed him, and tied the old man's neckcloth in a smart bow, and put his brooch into his best shirt frill, in which, in his Sunday suit of mourning, he sat from six o'clock in the morning awaiting the arrival of his son.\n\nHowever, when the postman made his appearance, the little party were put out of suspense by the receipt of a letter from Jos to his sister, who announced that he felt a little fatigued after his voyage, and should not be able to move on that day, but that he would leave Southampton early the next morning and be with his father and mother at evening. Amelia, as she read out the letter to her father, paused over the latter word; her brother, it was clear, did not know what had happened in the family. Nor could he, for the fact is that, though the Major rightly suspected that his travelling companion never would be got into motion in so short a space as twenty-four hours, and would find some excuse for delaying, yet Dobbin had not written to Jos to inform him of the calamity which had befallen the Sedley family, being occupied in talking with Amelia until long after post-hour.\n\nThere are some splendid tailors' shops in the High Street of Southampton, in the fine plate-glass windows of which hang gorgeous waistcoats of all sorts, of silk and velvet, and gold and crimson, and pictures of the last new fashions, in which those wonderful gentlemen with quizzing glasses, and holding on to little boys with the exceeding large eyes and curly hair, ogle ladies in riding habits prancing by the Statue of Achilles at Apsley House. Jos, although provided with some of the most splendid vests that Calcutta could furnish, thought he could not go to town until he was supplied with one or two of these garments, and selected a crimson satin, embroidered with gold butterflies, and a black and red velvet tartan with white stripes and a rolling collar, with which, and a rich blue satin stock and a gold pin, consisting of a five-barred gate with a horseman in pink enamel jumping over it, he thought he might make his entry into London with some dignity. For Jos's former shyness and blundering blushing timidity had given way to a more candid and courageous self-assertion of his worth. \"I don't care about owning it,\" Waterloo Sedley would say to his friends, \"I am a dressy man\"; and though rather uneasy if the ladies looked at him at the Government House balls, and though he blushed and turned away alarmed under their glances, it was chiefly from a dread lest they should make love to him that he avoided them, being averse to marriage altogether. But there was no such swell in Calcutta as Waterloo Sedley, I have heard say, and he had the handsomest turn-out, gave the best bachelor dinners, and had the finest plate in the whole place.\n\nTo make these waistcoats for a man of his size and dignity took at least a day, part of which he employed in hiring a servant to wait upon him and his native and in instructing the agent who cleared his baggage, his boxes, his books, which he never read, his chests of mangoes, chutney, and curry-powders, his shawls for presents to people whom he didn't know as yet, and the rest of his Persicos apparatus.\n\nAt length, he drove leisurely to London on the third day and in the new waistcoat, the native, with chattering teeth, shuddering in a shawl on the box by the side of the new European servant; Jos puffing his pipe at intervals within and looking so majestic that the little boys cried Hooray, and many people thought he must be a Governor-General. HE, I promise, did not decline the obsequious invitation of the landlords to alight and refresh himself in the neat country towns. Having partaken of a copious breakfast, with fish, and rice, and hard eggs, at Southampton, he had so far rallied at Winchester as to think a glass of sherry necessary. At Alton he stepped out of the carriage at his servant's request and imbibed some of the ale for which the place is famous. At Farnham he stopped to view the Bishop's Castle and to partake of a light dinner of stewed eels, veal cutlets, and French beans, with a bottle of claret. He was cold over Bagshot Heath, where the native chattered more and more, and Jos Sahib took some brandy-and-water; in fact, when he drove into town he was as full of wine, beer, meat, pickles, cherry-brandy, and tobacco as the steward's cabin of a steam-packet. It was evening when his carriage thundered up to the little door in Brompton, whither the affectionate fellow drove first, and before hieing to the apartments secured for him by Mr. Dobbin at the Slaughters'.\n\nAll the faces in the street were in the windows; the little maidservant flew to the wicket-gate; the Mesdames Clapp looked out from the casement of the ornamented kitchen; Emmy, in a great flutter, was in the passage among the hats and coats; and old Sedley in the parlour inside, shaking all over. Jos descended from the post-chaise and down the creaking swaying steps in awful state, supported by the new valet from Southampton and the shuddering native, whose brown face was now livid with cold and of the colour of a turkey's gizzard. He created an immense sensation in the passage presently, where Mrs. and Miss Clapp, coming perhaps to listen at the parlour door, found Loll Jewab shaking upon the hall-bench under the coats, moaning in a strange piteous way, and showing his yellow eyeballs and white teeth.\n\nFor, you see, we have adroitly shut the door upon the meeting between Jos and the old father and the poor little gentle sister inside. The old man was very much affected; so, of course, was his daughter; nor was Jos without feeling. In that long absence of ten years, the most selfish will think about home and early ties. Distance sanctifies both. Long brooding over those lost pleasures exaggerates their charm and sweetness. Jos was unaffectedly glad to see and shake the hand of his father, between whom and himself there had been a coolness--glad to see his little sister, whom he remembered so pretty and smiling, and pained at the alteration which time, grief, and misfortune had made in the shattered old man. Emmy had come out to the door in her black clothes and whispered to him of her mother's death, and not to speak of it to their father. There was no need of this caution, for the elder Sedley himself began immediately to speak of the event, and prattled about it, and wept over it plenteously. It shocked the Indian not a little and made him think of himself less than the poor fellow was accustomed to do.\n\nThe result of the interview must have been very satisfactory, for when Jos had reascended his post-chaise and had driven away to his hotel, Emmy embraced her father tenderly, appealing to him with an air of triumph, and asking the old man whether she did not always say that her brother had a good heart?\n\nIndeed, Joseph Sedley, affected by the humble position in which he found his relations, and in the expansiveness and overflowing of heart occasioned by the first meeting, declared that they should never suffer want or discomfort any more, that he was at home for some time at any rate, during which his house and everything he had should be theirs: and that Amelia would look very pretty at the head of his table--until she would accept one of her own.\n\nShe shook her head sadly and had, as usual, recourse to the waterworks. She knew what he meant. She and her young confidante, Miss Mary, had talked over the matter most fully, the very night of the Major's visit, beyond which time the impetuous Polly could not refrain from talking of the discovery which she had made, and describing the start and tremor of joy by which Major Dobbin betrayed himself when Mr. Binny passed with his bride and the Major learned that he had no longer a rival to fear. \"Didn't you see how he shook all over when you asked if he was married and he said, 'Who told you those lies?' Oh, M'am,\" Polly said, \"he never kept his eyes off you, and I'm sure he's grown grey athinking of you.\"\n\nBut Amelia, looking up at her bed, over which hung the portraits of her husband and son, told her young protegee never, never, to speak on that subject again; that Major Dobbin had been her husband's dearest friend and her own and George's most kind and affectionate guardian; that she loved him as a brother--but that a woman who had been married to such an angel as that, and she pointed to the wall, could never think of any other union. Poor Polly sighed: she thought what she should do if young Mr. Tomkins, at the surgery, who always looked at her so at church, and who, by those mere aggressive glances had put her timorous little heart into such a flutter that she was ready to surrender at once,--what she should do if he were to die? She knew he was consumptive, his cheeks were so red and he was so uncommon thin in the waist.\n\nNot that Emmy, being made aware of the honest Major's passion, rebuffed him in any way, or felt displeased with him. Such an attachment from so true and loyal a gentleman could make no woman angry. Desdemona was not angry with Cassio, though there is very little doubt she saw the Lieutenant's partiality for her (and I for my part believe that many more things took place in that sad affair than the worthy Moorish officer ever knew of); why, Miranda was even very kind to Caliban, and we may be pretty sure for the same reason. Not that she would encourage him in the least--the poor uncouth monster--of course not. No more would Emmy by any means encourage her admirer, the Major. She would give him that friendly regard, which so much excellence and fidelity merited; she would treat him with perfect cordiality and frankness until he made his proposals, and THEN it would be time enough for her to speak and to put an end to hopes which never could be realized.\n\nShe slept, therefore, very soundly that evening, after the conversation with Miss Polly, and was more than ordinarily happy, in spite of Jos's delaying. \"I am glad he is not going to marry that Miss O'Dowd,\" she thought. \"Colonel O'Dowd never could have a sister fit for such an accomplished man as Major William.\" Who was there amongst her little circle who would make him a good wife? Not Miss Binny, she was too old and ill-tempered; Miss Osborne? too old too. Little Polly was too young. Mrs. Osborne could not find anybody to suit the Major before she went to sleep.\n\nThe same morning brought Major Dobbin a letter to the Slaughters' Coffee-house from his friend at Southampton, begging dear Dob to excuse Jos for being in a rage when awakened the day before (he had a confounded headache, and was just in his first sleep), and entreating Dob to engage comfortable rooms at the Slaughters' for Mr. Sedley and his servants. The Major had become necessary to Jos during the voyage. He was attached to him, and hung upon him. The other passengers were away to London. Young Ricketts and little Chaffers went away on the coach that day--Ricketts on the box, and taking the reins from Botley; the Doctor was off to his family at Portsea; Bragg gone to town to his co-partners; and the first mate busy in the unloading of the Ramchunder. Mr. Joe was very lonely at Southampton, and got the landlord of the George to take a glass of wine with him that day, at the very hour at which Major Dobbin was seated at the table of his father, Sir William, where his sister found out (for it was impossible for the Major to tell fibs) that he had been to see Mrs. George Osborne.\n\nJos was so comfortably situated in St. Martin's Lane, he could enjoy his hookah there with such perfect ease, and could swagger down to the theatres, when minded, so agreeably, that, perhaps, he would have remained altogether at the Slaughters' had not his friend, the Major, been at his elbow. That gentleman would not let the Bengalee rest until he had executed his promise of having a home for Amelia and his father. Jos was a soft fellow in anybody's hands, Dobbin most active in anybody's concerns but his own; the civilian was, therefore, an easy victim to the guileless arts of this good-natured diplomatist and was ready to do, to purchase, hire, or relinquish whatever his friend thought fit. Loll Jewab, of whom the boys about St. Martin's Lane used to make cruel fun whenever he showed his dusky countenance in the street, was sent back to Calcutta in the Lady Kicklebury East Indiaman, in which Sir William Dobbin had a share, having previously taught Jos's European the art of preparing curries, pilaus, and pipes. It was a matter of great delight and occupation to Jos to superintend the building of a smart chariot which he and the Major ordered in the neighbouring Long Acre: and a pair of handsome horses were jobbed, with which Jos drove about in state in the park, or to call upon his Indian friends. Amelia was not seldom by his side on these excursions, when also Major Dobbin would be seen in the back seat of the carriage. At other times old Sedley and his daughter took advantage of it, and Miss Clapp, who frequently accompanied her friend, had great pleasure in being recognized as she sat in the carriage, dressed in the famous yellow shawl, by the young gentleman at the surgery, whose face might commonly be seen over the window-blinds as she passed.\n\nShortly after Jos's first appearance at Brompton, a dismal scene, indeed, took place at that humble cottage at which the Sedleys had passed the last ten years of their life. Jos's carriage (the temporary one, not the chariot under construction) arrived one day and carried off old Sedley and his daughter--to return no more. The tears that were shed by the landlady and the landlady's daughter at that event were as genuine tears of sorrow as any that have been outpoured in the course of this history. In their long acquaintanceship and intimacy they could not recall a harsh word that had been uttered by Amelia. She had been all sweetness and kindness, always thankful, always gentle, even when Mrs. Clapp lost her own temper and pressed for the rent. When the kind creature was going away for good and all, the landlady reproached herself bitterly for ever having used a rough expression to her--how she wept, as they stuck up with wafers on the window, a paper notifying that the little rooms so long occupied were to let! They never would have such lodgers again, that was quite clear. After-life proved the truth of this melancholy prophecy, and Mrs. Clapp revenged herself for the deterioration of mankind by levying the most savage contributions upon the tea-caddies and legs of mutton of her locataires. Most of them scolded and grumbled; some of them did not pay; none of them stayed. The landlady might well regret those old, old friends, who had left her.\n\nAs for Miss Mary, her sorrow at Amelia's departure was such as I shall not attempt to depict. From childhood upwards she had been with her daily and had attached herself so passionately to that dear good lady that when the grand barouche came to carry her off into splendour, she fainted in the arms of her friend, who was indeed scarcely less affected than the good-natured girl. Amelia loved her like a daughter. During eleven years the girl had been her constant friend and associate. The separation was a very painful one indeed to her. But it was of course arranged that Mary was to come and stay often at the grand new house whither Mrs. Osborne was going, and where Mary was sure she would never be so happy as she had been in their humble cot, as Miss Clapp called it, in the language of the novels which she loved.\n\nLet us hope she was wrong in her judgement. Poor Emmy's days of happiness had been very few in that humble cot. A gloomy Fate had oppressed her there. She never liked to come back to the house after she had left it, or to face the landlady who had tyrannized over her when ill-humoured and unpaid, or when pleased had treated her with a coarse familiarity scarcely less odious. Her servility and fulsome compliments when Emmy was in prosperity were not more to that lady's liking. She cast about notes of admiration all over the new house, extolling every article of furniture or ornament; she fingered Mrs. Osborne's dresses and calculated their price. Nothing could be too good for that sweet lady, she vowed and protested. But in the vulgar sycophant who now paid court to her, Emmy always remembered the coarse tyrant who had made her miserable many a time, to whom she had been forced to put up petitions for time, when the rent was overdue; who cried out at her extravagance if she bought delicacies for her ailing mother or father; who had seen her humble and trampled upon her.\n\nNobody ever heard of these griefs, which had been part of our poor little woman's lot in life. She kept them secret from her father, whose improvidence was the cause of much of her misery. She had to bear all the blame of his misdoings, and indeed was so utterly gentle and humble as to be made by nature for a victim.\n\nI hope she is not to suffer much more of that hard usage. And, as in all griefs there is said to be some consolation, I may mention that poor Mary, when left at her friend's departure in a hysterical condition, was placed under the medical treatment of the young fellow from the surgery, under whose care she rallied after a short period. Emmy, when she went away from Brompton, endowed Mary with every article of furniture that the house contained, only taking away her pictures (the two pictures over the bed) and her piano--that little old piano which had now passed into a plaintive jingling old age, but which she loved for reasons of her own. She was a child when first she played on it, and her parents gave it her. It had been given to her again since, as the reader may remember, when her father's house was gone to ruin and the instrument was recovered out of the wreck.\n\nMajor Dobbin was exceedingly pleased when, as he was superintending the arrangements of Jos's new house--which the Major insisted should be very handsome and comfortable--the cart arrived from Brompton, bringing the trunks and bandboxes of the emigrants from that village, and with them the old piano. Amelia would have it up in her sitting-room, a neat little apartment on the second floor, adjoining her father's chamber, and where the old gentleman sat commonly of evenings.\n\nWhen the men appeared then bearing this old music-box, and Amelia gave orders that it should be placed in the chamber aforesaid, Dobbin was quite elated. \"I'm glad you've kept it,\" he said in a very sentimental manner. \"I was afraid you didn't care about it.\"\n\n\"I value it more than anything I have in the world,\" said Amelia.\n\n\"Do you, Amelia?\" cried the Major. The fact was, as he had bought it himself, though he never said anything about it, it never entered into his head to suppose that Emmy should think anybody else was the purchaser, and as a matter of course he fancied that she knew the gift came from him. \"Do you, Amelia?\" he said; and the question, the great question of all, was trembling on his lips, when Emmy replied--\n\n\"Can I do otherwise?--did not he give it me?\"\n\n\"I did not know,\" said poor old Dob, and his countenance fell.\n\nEmmy did not note the circumstance at the time, nor take immediate heed of the very dismal expression which honest Dobbin's countenance assumed, but she thought of it afterwards. And then it struck her, with inexpressible pain and mortification too, that it was William who was the giver of the piano, and not George, as she had fancied. It was not George's gift; the only one which she had received from her lover, as she thought--the thing she had cherished beyond all others--her dearest relic and prize. She had spoken to it about George; played his favourite airs upon it; sat for long evening hours, touching, to the best of her simple art, melancholy harmonies on the keys, and weeping over them in silence. It was not George's relic. It was valueless now. The next time that old Sedley asked her to play, she said it was shockingly out of tune, that she had a headache, that she couldn't play.\n\nThen, according to her custom, she rebuked herself for her pettishness and ingratitude and determined to make a reparation to honest William for the slight she had not expressed to him, but had felt for his piano. A few days afterwards, as they were seated in the drawing-room, where Jos had fallen asleep with great comfort after dinner, Amelia said with rather a faltering voice to Major Dobbin--\n\n\"I have to beg your pardon for something.\"\n\n\"About what?\" said he.\n\n\"About--about that little square piano. I never thanked you for it when you gave it me, many, many years ago, before I was married. I thought somebody else had given it. Thank you, William.\" She held out her hand, but the poor little woman's heart was bleeding; and as for her eyes, of course they were at their work.\n\nBut William could hold no more. \"Amelia, Amelia,\" he said, \"I did buy it for you. I loved you then as I do now. I must tell you. I think I loved you from the first minute that I saw you, when George brought me to your house, to show me the Amelia whom he was engaged to. You were but a girl, in white, with large ringlets; you came down singing--do you remember?--and we went to Vauxhall. Since then I have thought of but one woman in the world, and that was you. I think there is no hour in the day has passed for twelve years that I haven't thought of you. I came to tell you this before I went to India, but you did not care, and I hadn't the heart to speak. You did not care whether I stayed or went.\"\n\n\"I was very ungrateful,\" Amelia said.\n\n\"No, only indifferent,\" Dobbin continued desperately. \"I have nothing to make a woman to be otherwise. I know what you are feeling now. You are hurt in your heart at the discovery about the piano, and that it came from me and not from George. I forgot, or I should never have spoken of it so. It is for me to ask your pardon for being a fool for a moment, and thinking that years of constancy and devotion might have pleaded with you.\"\n\n\"It is you who are cruel now,\" Amelia said with some spirit. \"George is my husband, here and in heaven. How could I love any other but him? I am his now as when you first saw me, dear William. It was he who told me how good and generous you were, and who taught me to love you as a brother. Have you not been everything to me and my boy? Our dearest, truest, kindest friend and protector? Had you come a few months sooner perhaps you might have spared me that--that dreadful parting. Oh, it nearly killed me, William--but you didn't come, though I wished and prayed for you to come, and they took him too away from me. Isn't he a noble boy, William? Be his friend still and mine\"--and here her voice broke, and she hid her face on his shoulder.\n\nThe Major folded his arms round her, holding her to him as if she was a child, and kissed her head. \"I will not change, dear Amelia,\" he said. \"I ask for no more than your love. I think I would not have it otherwise. Only let me stay near you and see you often.\"\n\n\"Yes, often,\" Amelia said. And so William was at liberty to look and long--as the poor boy at school who has no money may sigh after the contents of the tart-woman's tray.\n\nReturns to the Genteel World\nGood fortune now begins to smile upon Amelia. We are glad to get her out of that low sphere in which she has been creeping hitherto and introduce her into a polite circle--not so grand and refined as that in which our other female friend, Mrs. Becky, has appeared, but still having no small pretensions to gentility and fashion. Jos's friends were all from the three presidencies, and his new house was in the comfortable Anglo-Indian district of which Moira Place is the centre. Minto Square, Great Clive Street, Warren Street, Hastings Street, Ochterlony Place, Plassy Square, Assaye Terrace (\"gardens\" was a felicitous word not applied to stucco houses with asphalt terraces in front, so early as 1827)--who does not know these respectable abodes of the retired Indian aristocracy, and the quarter which Mr. Wenham calls the Black Hole, in a word? Jos's position in life was not grand enough to entitle him to a house in Moira Place, where none can live but retired Members of Council, and partners of Indian firms (who break, after having settled a hundred thousand pounds on their wives, and retire into comparative penury to a country place and four thousand a year); he engaged a comfortable house of a second- or third-rate order in Gillespie Street, purchasing the carpets, costly mirrors, and handsome and appropriate planned furniture by Seddons from the assignees of Mr. Scape, lately admitted partner into the great Calcutta House of Fogle, Fake, and Cracksman, in which poor Scape had embarked seventy thousand pounds, the earnings of a long and honourable life, taking Fake's place, who retired to a princely park in Sussex (the Fogles have been long out of the firm, and Sir Horace Fogle is about to be raised to the peerage as Baron Bandanna)--admitted, I say, partner into the great agency house of Fogle and Fake two years before it failed for a million and plunged half the Indian public into misery and ruin.\n\nScape, ruined, honest, and broken-hearted at sixty-five years of age, went out to Calcutta to wind up the affairs of the house. Walter Scape was withdrawn from Eton and put into a merchant's house. Florence Scape, Fanny Scape, and their mother faded away to Boulogne, and will be heard of no more. To be brief, Jos stepped in and bought their carpets and sideboards and admired himself in the mirrors which had reflected their kind handsome faces. The Scape tradesmen, all honourably paid, left their cards, and were eager to supply the new household. The large men in white waistcoats who waited at Scape's dinners, greengrocers, bank-porters, and milkmen in their private capacity, left their addresses and ingratiated themselves with the butler. Mr. Chummy, the chimney-purifier, who had swept the last three families, tried to coax the butler and the boy under him, whose duty it was to go out covered with buttons and with stripes down his trousers, for the protection of Mrs. Amelia whenever she chose to walk abroad.\n\nIt was a modest establishment. The butler was Jos's valet also, and never was more drunk than a butler in a small family should be who has a proper regard for his master's wine. Emmy was supplied with a maid, grown on Sir William Dobbin's suburban estate; a good girl, whose kindness and humility disarmed Mrs. Osborne, who was at first terrified at the idea of having a servant to wait upon herself, who did not in the least know how to use one, and who always spoke to domestics with the most reverential politeness. But this maid was very useful in the family, in dexterously tending old Mr. Sedley, who kept almost entirely to his own quarter of the house and never mixed in any of the gay doings which took place there.\n\nNumbers of people came to see Mrs. Osborne. Lady Dobbin and daughters were delighted at her change of fortune, and waited upon her. Miss Osborne from Russell Square came in her grand chariot with the flaming hammer-cloth emblazoned with the Leeds arms. Jos was reported to be immensely rich. Old Osborne had no objection that Georgy should inherit his uncle's property as well as his own. \"Damn it, we will make a man of the feller,\" he said; \"and I'll see him in Parliament before I die. You may go and see his mother, Miss O., though I'll never set eyes on her\": and Miss Osborne came. Emmy, you may be sure, was very glad to see her, and so be brought nearer to George. That young fellow was allowed to come much more frequently than before to visit his mother. He dined once or twice a week in Gillespie Street and bullied the servants and his relations there, just as he did in Russell Square.\n\nHe was always respectful to Major Dobbin, however, and more modest in his demeanour when that gentleman was present. He was a clever lad and afraid of the Major. George could not help admiring his friend's simplicity, his good humour, his various learning quietly imparted, his general love of truth and justice. He had met no such man as yet in the course of his experience, and he had an instinctive liking for a gentleman. He hung fondly by his godfather's side, and it was his delight to walk in the parks and hear Dobbin talk. William told George about his father, about India and Waterloo, about everything but himself. When George was more than usually pert and conceited, the Major made jokes at him, which Mrs. Osborne thought very cruel. One day, taking him to the play, and the boy declining to go into the pit because it was vulgar, the Major took him to the boxes, left him there, and went down himself to the pit. He had not been seated there very long before he felt an arm thrust under his and a dandy little hand in a kid glove squeezing his arm. George had seen the absurdity of his ways and come down from the upper region. A tender laugh of benevolence lighted up old Dobbin's face and eyes as he looked at the repentant little prodigal. He loved the boy, as he did everything that belonged to Amelia. How charmed she was when she heard of this instance of George's goodness! Her eyes looked more kindly on Dobbin than they ever had done. She blushed, he thought, after looking at him so.\n\nGeorgy never tired of his praises of the Major to his mother. \"I like him, Mamma, because he knows such lots of things; and he ain't like old Veal, who is always bragging and using such long words, don't you know? The chaps call him 'Longtail' at school. I gave him the name; ain't it capital? But Dob reads Latin like English, and French and that; and when we go out together he tells me stories about my Papa, and never about himself; though I heard Colonel Buckler, at Grandpapa's, say that he was one of the bravest officers in the army, and had distinguished himself ever so much. Grandpapa was quite surprised, and said, 'THAT feller! Why, I didn't think he could say Bo to a goose'--but I know he could, couldn't he, Mamma?\"\n\nEmmy laughed: she thought it was very likely the Major could do thus much.\n\nIf there was a sincere liking between George and the Major, it must be confessed that between the boy and his uncle no great love existed. George had got a way of blowing out his cheeks, and putting his hands in his waistcoat pockets, and saying, \"God bless my soul, you don't say so,\" so exactly after the fashion of old Jos that it was impossible to refrain from laughter. The servants would explode at dinner if the lad, asking for something which wasn't at table, put on that countenance and used that favourite phrase. Even Dobbin would shoot out a sudden peal at the boy's mimicry. If George did not mimic his uncle to his face, it was only by Dobbin's rebukes and Amelia's terrified entreaties that the little scapegrace was induced to desist. And the worthy civilian being haunted by a dim consciousness that the lad thought him an ass, and was inclined to turn him into ridicule, used to be extremely timorous and, of course, doubly pompous and dignified in the presence of Master Georgy. When it was announced that the young gentleman was expected in Gillespie Street to dine with his mother, Mr. Jos commonly found that he had an engagement at the Club. Perhaps nobody was much grieved at his absence. On those days Mr. Sedley would commonly be induced to come out from his place of refuge in the upper stories, and there would be a small family party, whereof Major Dobbin pretty generally formed one. He was the ami de la maison--old Sedley's friend, Emmy's friend, Georgy's friend, Jos's counsel and adviser. \"He might almost as well be at Madras for anything WE see of him,\" Miss Ann Dobbin remarked at Camberwell. Ah! Miss Ann, did it not strike you that it was not YOU whom the Major wanted to marry?\n\nJoseph Sedley then led a life of dignified otiosity such as became a person of his eminence. His very first point, of course, was to become a member of the Oriental Club, where he spent his mornings in the company of his brother Indians, where he dined, or whence he brought home men to dine.\n\nAmelia had to receive and entertain these gentlemen and their ladies. From these she heard how soon Smith would be in Council; how many lacs Jones had brought home with him, how Thomson's House in London had refused the bills drawn by Thomson, Kibobjee, and Co., the Bombay House, and how it was thought the Calcutta House must go too; how very imprudent, to say the least of it, Mrs. Brown's conduct (wife of Brown of the Ahmednuggur Irregulars) had been with young Swankey of the Body Guard, sitting up with him on deck until all hours, and losing themselves as they were riding out at the Cape; how Mrs. Hardyman had had out her thirteen sisters, daughters of a country curate, the Rev: Felix Rabbits, and married eleven of them, seven high up in the service; how Hornby was wild because his wife would stay in Europe, and Trotter was appointed Collector at Ummerapoora. This and similar talk took place at the grand dinners all round. They had the same conversation; the same silver dishes; the same saddles of mutton, boiled turkeys, and entrees. Politics set in a short time after dessert, when the ladies retired upstairs and talked about their complaints and their children.\n\nMutato nomine, it is all the same. Don't the barristers' wives talk about Circuit? Don't the soldiers' ladies gossip about the Regiment? Don't the clergymen's ladies discourse about Sunday-schools and who takes whose duty? Don't the very greatest ladies of all talk about that small clique of persons to whom they belong? And why should our Indian friends not have their own conversation?--only I admit it is slow for the laymen whose fate it sometimes is to sit by and listen.\n\nBefore long Emmy had a visiting-book, and was driving about regularly in a carriage, calling upon Lady Bludyer (wife of Major-General Sir Roger Bludyer, K.C.B., Bengal Army); Lady Huff, wife of Sir G. Huff, Bombay ditto; Mrs. Pice, the Lady of Pice the Director, &c. We are not long in using ourselves to changes in life. That carriage came round to Gillespie Street every day; that buttony boy sprang up and down from the box with Emmy's and Jos's visiting-cards; at stated hours Emmy and the carriage went for Jos to the Club and took him an airing; or, putting old Sedley into the vehicle, she drove the old man round the Regent's Park. The lady's maid and the chariot, the visiting-book and the buttony page, became soon as familiar to Amelia as the humble routine of Brompton. She accommodated herself to one as to the other. If Fate had ordained that she should be a Duchess, she would even have done that duty too. She was voted, in Jos's female society, rather a pleasing young person--not much in her, but pleasing, and that sort of thing.\n\nThe men, as usual, liked her artless kindness and simple refined demeanour. The gallant young Indian dandies at home on furlough--immense dandies these--chained and moustached--driving in tearing cabs, the pillars of the theatres, living at West End hotels--nevertheless admired Mrs. Osborne, liked to bow to her carriage in the park, and to be admitted to have the honour of paying her a morning visit. Swankey of the Body Guard himself, that dangerous youth, and the greatest buck of all the Indian army now on leave, was one day discovered by Major Dobbin tete-a-tete with Amelia, and describing the sport of pig-sticking to her with great humour and eloquence; and he spoke afterwards of a d--d king's officer that's always hanging about the house--a long, thin, queer-looking, oldish fellow--a dry fellow though, that took the shine out of a man in the talking line.\n\nHad the Major possessed a little more personal vanity he would have been jealous of so dangerous a young buck as that fascinating Bengal Captain. But Dobbin was of too simple and generous a nature to have any doubts about Amelia. He was glad that the young men should pay her respect, and that others should admire her. Ever since her womanhood almost, had she not been persecuted and undervalued? It pleased him to see how kindness bought out her good qualities and how her spirits gently rose with her prosperity. Any person who appreciated her paid a compliment to the Major's good judgement--that is, if a man may be said to have good judgement who is under the influence of Love's delusion.\n\nAfter Jos went to Court, which we may be sure he did as a loyal subject of his Sovereign (showing himself in his full court suit at the Club, whither Dobbin came to fetch him in a very shabby old uniform) he who had always been a staunch Loyalist and admirer of George IV, became such a tremendous Tory and pillar of the State that he was for having Amelia to go to a Drawing-room, too. He somehow had worked himself up to believe that he was implicated in the maintenance of the public welfare and that the Sovereign would not be happy unless Jos Sedley and his family appeared to rally round him at St. James's.\n\nEmmy laughed. \"Shall I wear the family diamonds, Jos?\" she said.\n\n\"I wish you would let me buy you some,\" thought the Major. \"I should like to see any that were too good for you.\"\n\nIn Which Two Lights are Put Out\n\nThere came a day when the round of decorous pleasures and solemn gaieties in which Mr. Jos Sedley's family indulged was interrupted by an event which happens in most houses. As you ascend the staircase of your house from the drawing towards the bedroom floors, you may have remarked a little arch in the wall right before you, which at once gives light to the stair which leads from the second story to the third (where the nursery and servants' chambers commonly are) and serves for another purpose of utility, of which the undertaker's men can give you a notion. They rest the coffins upon that arch, or pass them through it so as not to disturb in any unseemly manner the cold tenant slumbering within the black ark.\n\nThat second-floor arch in a London house, looking up and down the well of the staircase and commanding the main thoroughfare by which the inhabitants are passing; by which cook lurks down before daylight to scour her pots and pans in the kitchen; by which young master stealthily ascends, having left his boots in the hall, and let himself in after dawn from a jolly night at the Club; down which miss comes rustling in fresh ribbons and spreading muslins, brilliant and beautiful, and prepared for conquest and the ball; or Master Tommy slides, preferring the banisters for a mode of conveyance, and disdaining danger and the stair; down which the mother is fondly carried smiling in her strong husband's arms, as he steps steadily step by step, and followed by the monthly nurse, on the day when the medical man has pronounced that the charming patient may go downstairs; up which John lurks to bed, yawning, with a sputtering tallow candle, and to gather up before sunrise the boots which are awaiting him in the passages--that stair, up or down which babies are carried, old people are helped, guests are marshalled to the ball, the parson walks to the christening, the doctor to the sick-room, and the undertaker's men to the upper floor--what a memento of Life, Death, and Vanity it is--that arch and stair--if you choose to consider it, and sit on the landing, looking up and down the well! The doctor will come up to us too for the last time there, my friend in motley. The nurse will look in at the curtains, and you take no notice--and then she will fling open the windows for a little and let in the air. Then they will pull down all the front blinds of the house and live in the back rooms--then they will send for the lawyer and other men in black, &c. Your comedy and mine will have been played then, and we shall be removed, oh, how far, from the trumpets, and the shouting, and the posture-making. If we are gentlefolks they will put hatchments over our late domicile, with gilt cherubim, and mottoes stating that there is \"Quiet in Heaven.\" Your son will new furnish the house, or perhaps let it, and go into a more modern quarter; your name will be among the \"Members Deceased\" in the lists of your clubs next year. However much you may be mourned, your widow will like to have her weeds neatly made--the cook will send or come up to ask about dinner--the survivor will soon bear to look at your picture over the mantelpiece, which will presently be deposed from the place of honour, to make way for the portrait of the son who reigns.\n\nWhich of the dead are most tenderly and passionately deplored? Those who love the survivors the least, I believe. The death of a child occasions a passion of grief and frantic tears, such as your end, brother reader, will never inspire. The death of an infant which scarce knew you, which a week's absence from you would have caused to forget you, will strike you down more than the loss of your closest friend, or your first-born son--a man grown like yourself, with children of his own. We may be harsh and stern with Judah and Simeon--our love and pity gush out for Benjamin, the little one. And if you are old, as some reader of this may be or shall be old and rich, or old and poor--you may one day be thinking for yourself--\"These people are very good round about me, but they won't grieve too much when I am gone. I am very rich, and they want my inheritance--or very poor, and they are tired of supporting me.\"\n\nThe period of mourning for Mrs. Sedley's death was only just concluded, and Jos scarcely had had time to cast off his black and appear in the splendid waistcoats which he loved, when it became evident to those about Mr. Sedley that another event was at hand, and that the old man was about to go seek for his wife in the dark land whither she had preceded him. \"The state of my father's health,\" Jos Sedley solemnly remarked at the Club, \"prevents me from giving any LARGE parties this season: but if you will come in quietly at half-past six, Chutney, my boy, and fake a homely dinner with one or two of the old set--I shall be always glad to see you.\" So Jos and his acquaintances dined and drank their claret among themselves in silence, whilst the sands of life were running out in the old man's glass upstairs. The velvet-footed butler brought them their wine, and they composed themselves to a rubber after dinner, at which Major Dobbin would sometimes come and take a hand; and Mrs. Osborne would occasionally descend, when her patient above was settled for the night, and had commenced one of those lightly troubled slumbers which visit the pillow of old age.\n\nThe old man clung to his daughter during this sickness. He would take his broths and medicines from scarcely any other hand. To tend him became almost the sole business of her life. Her bed was placed close by the door which opened into his chamber, and she was alive at the slightest noise or disturbance from the couch of the querulous invalid. Though, to do him justice, he lay awake many an hour, silent and without stirring, unwilling to awaken his kind and vigilant nurse.\n\nHe loved his daughter with more fondness now, perhaps, than ever he had done since the days of her childhood. In the discharge of gentle offices and kind filial duties, this simple creature shone most especially. \"She walks into the room as silently as a sunbeam,\" Mr. Dobbin thought as he saw her passing in and out from her father's room, a cheerful sweetness lighting up her face as she moved to and fro, graceful and noiseless. When women are brooding over their children, or busied in a sick-room, who has not seen in their faces those sweet angelic beams of love and pity?\n\nA secret feud of some years' standing was thus healed, and with a tacit reconciliation. In these last hours, and touched by her love and goodness, the old man forgot all his grief against her, and wrongs which he and his wife had many a long night debated: how she had given up everything for her boy; how she was careless of her parents in their old age and misfortune, and only thought of the child; how absurdly and foolishly, impiously indeed, she took on when George was removed from her. Old Sedley forgot these charges as he was making up his last account, and did justice to the gentle and uncomplaining little martyr. One night when she stole into his room, she found him awake, when the broken old man made his confession. \"Oh, Emmy, I've been thinking we were very unkind and unjust to you,\" he said and put out his cold and feeble hand to her. She knelt down and prayed by his bedside, as he did too, having still hold of her hand. When our turn comes, friend, may we have such company in our prayers!\n\nPerhaps as he was lying awake then, his life may have passed before him--his early hopeful struggles, his manly successes and prosperity, his downfall in his declining years, and his present helpless condition--no chance of revenge against Fortune, which had had the better of him--neither name nor money to bequeath--a spent-out, bootless life of defeat and disappointment, and the end here! Which, I wonder, brother reader, is the better lot, to die prosperous and famous, or poor and disappointed? To have, and to be forced to yield; or to sink out of life, having played and lost the game? That must be a strange feeling, when a day of our life comes and we say, \"To-morrow, success or failure won't matter much, and the sun will rise, and all the myriads of mankind go to their work or their pleasure as usual, but I shall be out of the turmoil.\"\n\nSo there came one morning and sunrise when all the world got up and set about its various works and pleasures, with the exception of old John Sedley, who was not to fight with fortune, or to hope or scheme any more, but to go and take up a quiet and utterly unknown residence in a churchyard at Brompton by the side of his old wife.\n\nMajor Dobbin, Jos, and Georgy followed his remains to the grave, in a black cloth coach. Jos came on purpose from the Star and Garter at Richmond, whither he retreated after the deplorable event. He did not care to remain in the house, with the--under the circumstances, you understand. But Emmy stayed and did her duty as usual. She was bowed down by no especial grief, and rather solemn than sorrowful. She prayed that her own end might be as calm and painless, and thought with trust and reverence of the words which she had heard from her father during his illness, indicative of his faith, his resignation, and his future hope.\n\nYes, I think that will be the better ending of the two, after all. Suppose you are particularly rich and well-to-do and say on that last day, \"I am very rich; I am tolerably well known; I have lived all my life in the best society, and thank Heaven, come of a most respectable family. I have served my King and country with honour. I was in Parliament for several years, where, I may say, my speeches were listened to and pretty well received. I don't owe any man a shilling: on the contrary, I lent my old college friend, Jack Lazarus, fifty pounds, for which my executors will not press him. I leave my daughters with ten thousand pounds apiece--very good portions for girls; I bequeath my plate and furniture, my house in Baker Street, with a handsome jointure, to my widow for her life; and my landed property, besides money in the funds, and my cellar of well-selected wine in Baker Street, to my son. I leave twenty pound a year to my valet; and I defy any man after I have gone to find anything against my character.\" Or suppose, on the other hand, your swan sings quite a different sort of dirge and you say, \"I am a poor blighted, disappointed old fellow, and have made an utter failure through life. I was not endowed either with brains or with good fortune, and confess that I have committed a hundred mistakes and blunders. I own to having forgotten my duty many a time. I can't pay what I owe. On my last bed I lie utterly helpless and humble, and I pray forgiveness for my weakness and throw myself, with a contrite heart, at the feet of the Divine Mercy.\" Which of these two speeches, think you, would be the best oration for your own funeral? Old Sedley made the last; and in that humble frame of mind, and holding by the hand of his daughter, life and disappointment and vanity sank away from under him.\n\n\"You see,\" said old Osborne to George, \"what comes of merit, and industry, and judicious speculations, and that. Look at me and my banker's account. Look at your poor Grandfather Sedley and his failure. And yet he was a better man than I was, this day twenty years--a better man, I should say, by ten thousand pound.\"\n\nBeyond these people and Mr. Clapp's family, who came over from Brompton to pay a visit of condolence, not a single soul alive ever cared a penny piece about old John Sedley, or remembered the existence of such a person.\n\nWhen old Osborne first heard from his friend Colonel Buckler (as little Georgy had already informed us) how distinguished an officer Major Dobbin was, he exhibited a great deal of scornful incredulity and expressed his surprise how ever such a feller as that should possess either brains or reputation. But he heard of the Major's fame from various members of his society. Sir William Dobbin had a great opinion of his son and narrated many stories illustrative of the Major's learning, valour, and estimation in the world's opinion. Finally, his name appeared in the lists of one or two great parties of the nobility, and this circumstance had a prodigious effect upon the old aristocrat of Russell Square.\n\nThe Major's position, as guardian to Georgy, whose possession had been ceded to his grandfather, rendered some meetings between the two gentlemen inevitable; and it was in one of these that old Osborne, a keen man of business, looking into the Major's accounts with his ward and the boy's mother, got a hint, which staggered him very much, and at once pained and pleased him, that it was out of William Dobbin's own pocket that a part of the fund had been supplied upon which the poor widow and the child had subsisted.\n\nWhen pressed upon the point, Dobbin, who could not tell lies, blushed and stammered a good deal and finally confessed. \"The marriage,\" he said (at which his interlocutor's face grew dark) \"was very much my doing. I thought my poor friend had gone so far that retreat from his engagement would have been dishonour to him and death to Mrs. Osborne, and I could do no less, when she was left without resources, than give what money I could spare to maintain her.\"\n\n\"Major D.,\" Mr. Osborne said, looking hard at him and turning very red too--\"you did me a great injury; but give me leave to tell you, sir, you are an honest feller. There's my hand, sir, though I little thought that my flesh and blood was living on you--\" and the pair shook hands, with great confusion on Major Dobbin's part, thus found out in his act of charitable hypocrisy.\n\nHe strove to soften the old man and reconcile him towards his son's memory. \"He was such a noble fellow,\" he said, \"that all of us loved him, and would have done anything for him. I, as a young man in those days, was flattered beyond measure by his preference for me, and was more pleased to be seen in his company than in that of the Commander-in-Chief. I never saw his equal for pluck and daring and all the qualities of a soldier\"; and Dobbin told the old father as many stories as he could remember regarding the gallantry and achievements of his son. \"And Georgy is so like him,\" the Major added.\n\n\"He's so like him that he makes me tremble sometimes,\" the grandfather said.\n\nOn one or two evenings the Major came to dine with Mr. Osborne (it was during the time of the sickness of Mr. Sedley), and as the two sat together in the evening after dinner, all their talk was about the departed hero. The father boasted about him according to his wont, glorifying himself in recounting his son's feats and gallantry, but his mood was at any rate better and more charitable than that in which he had been disposed until now to regard the poor fellow; and the Christian heart of the kind Major was pleased at these symptoms of returning peace and good-will. On the second evening old Osborne called Dobbin William, just as he used to do at the time when Dobbin and George were boys together, and the honest gentleman was pleased by that mark of reconciliation.\n\nOn the next day at breakfast, when Miss Osborne, with the asperity of her age and character, ventured to make some remark reflecting slightingly upon the Major's appearance or behaviour--the master of the house interrupted her. \"You'd have been glad enough to git him for yourself, Miss O. But them grapes are sour. Ha! ha! Major William is a fine feller.\"\n\n\"That he is, Grandpapa,\" said Georgy approvingly; and going up close to the old gentleman, he took a hold of his large grey whiskers, and laughed in his face good-humouredly, and kissed him. And he told the story at night to his mother, who fully agreed with the boy. \"Indeed he is,\" she said. \"Your dear father always said so. He is one of the best and most upright of men.\" Dobbin happened to drop in very soon after this conversation, which made Amelia blush perhaps, and the young scapegrace increased the confusion by telling Dobbin the other part of the story. \"I say, Dob,\" he said, \"there's such an uncommon nice girl wants to marry you. She's plenty of tin; she wears a front; and she scolds the servants from morning till night.\" \"Who is it?\" asked Dobbin. \"It's Aunt O.,\" the boy answered. \"Grandpapa said so. And I say, Dob, how prime it would be to have you for my uncle.\" Old Sedley's quavering voice from the next room at this moment weakly called for Amelia, and the laughing ended.\n\nThat old Osborne's mind was changing was pretty clear. He asked George about his uncle sometimes, and laughed at the boy's imitation of the way in which Jos said \"God-bless-my-soul\" and gobbled his soup. Then he said, \"It's not respectful, sir, of you younkers to be imitating of your relations. Miss O., when you go out adriving to-day, leave my card upon Mr. Sedley, do you hear? There's no quarrel betwigst me and him anyhow.\"\n\nThe card was returned, and Jos and the Major were asked to dinner--to a dinner the most splendid and stupid that perhaps ever Mr. Osborne gave; every inch of the family plate was exhibited, and the best company was asked. Mr. Sedley took down Miss O. to dinner, and she was very gracious to him; whereas she hardly spoke to the Major, who sat apart from her, and by the side of Mr. Osborne, very timid. Jos said, with great solemnity, it was the best turtle soup he had ever tasted in his life, and asked Mr. Osborne where he got his Madeira.\n\n\"It is some of Sedley's wine,\" whispered the butler to his master. \"I've had it a long time, and paid a good figure for it, too,\" Mr. Osborne said aloud to his guest, and then whispered to his right-hand neighbour how he had got it \"at the old chap's sale.\"\n\nMore than once he asked the Major about--about Mrs. George Osborne--a theme on which the Major could be very eloquent when he chose. He told Mr. Osborne of her sufferings--of her passionate attachment to her husband, whose memory she worshipped still--of the tender and dutiful manner in which she had supported her parents, and given up her boy, when it seemed to her her duty to do so. \"You don't know what she endured, sir,\" said honest Dobbin with a tremor in his voice, \"and I hope and trust you will be reconciled to her. If she took your son away from you, she gave hers to you; and however much you loved your George, depend on it, she loved hers ten times more.\"\n\n\"By God, you are a good feller, sir,\" was all Mr. Osborne said. It had never struck him that the widow would feel any pain at parting from the boy, or that his having a fine fortune could grieve her. A reconciliation was announced as speedy and inevitable, and Amelia's heart already began to beat at the notion of the awful meeting with George's father.\n\nIt was never, however, destined to take place. Old Sedley's lingering illness and death supervened, after which a meeting was for some time impossible. That catastrophe and other events may have worked upon Mr. Osborne. He was much shaken of late, and aged, and his mind was working inwardly. He had sent for his lawyers, and probably changed something in his will. The medical man who looked in pronounced him shaky, agitated, and talked of a little blood and the seaside; but he took neither of these remedies.\n\nOne day when he should have come down to breakfast, his servant missing him, went into his dressing-room and found him lying at the foot of the dressing-table in a fit. Miss Osborne was apprised; the doctors were sent for; Georgy stopped away from school; the bleeders and cuppers came. Osborne partially regained cognizance, but never could speak again, though he tried dreadfully once or twice, and in four days he died. The doctors went down, and the undertaker's men went up the stairs, and all the shutters were shut towards the garden in Russell Square. Bullock rushed from the City in a hurry. \"How much money had he left to that boy? Not half, surely? Surely share and share alike between the three?\" It was an agitating moment.\n\nWhat was it that poor old man tried once or twice in vain to say? I hope it was that he wanted to see Amelia and be reconciled before he left the world to one dear and faithful wife of his son: it was most likely that, for his will showed that the hatred which he had so long cherished had gone out of his heart.\n\nThey found in the pocket of his dressing-gown the letter with the great red seal which George had written him from Waterloo. He had looked at the other papers too, relative to his son, for the key of the box in which he kept them was also in his pocket, and it was found the seals and envelopes had been broken--very likely on the night before the seizure--when the butler had taken him tea into his study, and found him reading in the great red family Bible.\n\nWhen the will was opened, it was found that half the property was left to George, and the remainder between the two sisters. Mr. Bullock to continue, for their joint benefit, the affairs of the commercial house, or to go out, as he thought fit. An annuity of five hundred pounds, chargeable on George's property, was left to his mother, \"the widow of my beloved son, George Osborne,\" who was to resume the guardianship of the boy.\n\n\"Major William Dobbin, my beloved son's friend,\" was appointed executor; \"and as out of his kindness and bounty, and with his own private funds, he maintained my grandson and my son's widow, when they were otherwise without means of support\" (the testator went on to say) \"I hereby thank him heartily for his love and regard for them, and beseech him to accept such a sum as may be sufficient to purchase his commission as a Lieutenant-Colonel, or to be disposed of in any way he may think fit.\"\n\nWhen Amelia heard that her father-in-law was reconciled to her, her heart melted, and she was grateful for the fortune left to her. But when she heard how Georgy was restored to her, and knew how and by whom, and how it was William's bounty that supported her in poverty, how it was William who gave her her husband and her son--oh, then she sank on her knees, and prayed for blessings on that constant and kind heart; she bowed down and humbled herself, and kissed the feet, as it were, of that beautiful and generous affection.\n\nAnd gratitude was all that she had to pay back for such admirable devotion and benefits--only gratitude! If she thought of any other return, the image of George stood up out of the grave and said, \"You are mine, and mine only, now and forever.\"\n\nWilliam knew her feelings: had he not passed his whole life in divining them?\n\nWhen the nature of Mr. Osborne's will became known to the world, it was edifying to remark how Mrs. George Osborne rose in the estimation of the people forming her circle of acquaintance. The servants of Jos's establishment, who used to question her humble orders and say they would \"ask Master\" whether or not they could obey, never thought now of that sort of appeal. The cook forgot to sneer at her shabby old gowns (which, indeed, were quite eclipsed by that lady's finery when she was dressed to go to church of a Sunday evening), the others no longer grumbled at the sound of her bell, or delayed to answer that summons. The coachman, who grumbled that his 'osses should be brought out and his carriage made into an hospital for that old feller and Mrs. O., drove her with the utmost alacrity now, and trembling lest he should be superseded by Mr. Osborne's coachman, asked \"what them there Russell Square coachmen knew about town, and whether they was fit to sit on a box before a lady?\" Jos's friends, male and female, suddenly became interested about Emmy, and cards of condolence multiplied on her hall table. Jos himself, who had looked on her as a good-natured harmless pauper, to whom it was his duty to give victuals and shelter, paid her and the rich little boy, his nephew, the greatest respect--was anxious that she should have change and amusement after her troubles and trials, \"poor dear girl\"--and began to appear at the breakfast-table, and most particularly to ask how she would like to dispose of the day.\n\nIn her capacity of guardian to Georgy, she, with the consent of the Major, her fellow-trustee, begged Miss Osborne to live in the Russell Square house as long as ever she chose to dwell there; but that lady, with thanks, declared that she never could think of remaining alone in that melancholy mansion, and departed in deep mourning to Cheltenham, with a couple of her old domestics. The rest were liberally paid and dismissed, the faithful old butler, whom Mrs. Osborne proposed to retain, resigning and preferring to invest his savings in a public-house, where, let us hope, he was not unprosperous. Miss Osborne not choosing to live in Russell Square, Mrs. Osborne also, after consultation, declined to occupy the gloomy old mansion there. The house was dismantled; the rich furniture and effects, the awful chandeliers and dreary blank mirrors packed away and hidden, the rich rosewood drawing-room suite was muffled in straw, the carpets were rolled up and corded, the small select library of well-bound books was stowed into two wine-chests, and the whole paraphernalia rolled away in several enormous vans to the Pantechnicon, where they were to lie until Georgy's majority. And the great heavy dark plate-chests went off to Messrs. Stumpy and Rowdy, to lie in the cellars of those eminent bankers until the same period should arrive.\n\nOne day Emmy, with George in her hand and clad in deep sables, went to visit the deserted mansion which she had not entered since she was a girl. The place in front was littered with straw where the vans had been laden and rolled off. They went into the great blank rooms, the walls of which bore the marks where the pictures and mirrors had hung. Then they went up the great blank stone staircases into the upper rooms, into that where grandpapa died, as George said in a whisper, and then higher still into George's own room. The boy was still clinging by her side, but she thought of another besides him. She knew that it had been his father's room as well as his own.\n\nShe went up to one of the open windows (one of those at which she used to gaze with a sick heart when the child was first taken from her), and thence as she looked out she could see, over the trees of Russell Square, the old house in which she herself was born, and where she had passed so many happy days of sacred youth. They all came back to her, the pleasant holidays, the kind faces, the careless, joyful past times, and the long pains and trials that had since cast her down. She thought of these and of the man who had been her constant protector, her good genius, her sole benefactor, her tender and generous friend.\n\n\"Look here, Mother,\" said Georgy, \"here's a G.O. scratched on the glass with a diamond, I never saw it before, I never did it.\"\n\n\"It was your father's room long before you were born, George,\" she said, and she blushed as she kissed the boy.\n\nShe was very silent as they drove back to Richmond, where they had taken a temporary house: where the smiling lawyers used to come bustling over to see her (and we may be sure noted the visit in the bill): and where of course there was a room for Major Dobbin too, who rode over frequently, having much business to transact on behalf of his little ward.\n\nGeorgy at this time was removed from Mr. Veal's on an unlimited holiday, and that gentleman was engaged to prepare an inscription for a fine marble slab, to be placed up in the Foundling under the monument of Captain George Osborne.\n\nThe female Bullock, aunt of Georgy, although despoiled by that little monster of one-half of the sum which she expected from her father, nevertheless showed her charitableness of spirit by being reconciled to the mother and the boy. Roehampton is not far from Richmond, and one day the chariot, with the golden bullocks emblazoned on the panels, and the flaccid children within, drove to Amelia's house at Richmond; and the Bullock family made an irruption into the garden, where Amelia was reading a book, Jos was in an arbour placidly dipping strawberries into wine, and the Major in one of his Indian jackets was giving a back to Georgy, who chose to jump over him. He went over his head and bounded into the little advance of Bullocks, with immense black bows in their hats, and huge black sashes, accompanying their mourning mamma.\n\n\"He is just of the age for Rosa,\" the fond parent thought, and glanced towards that dear child, an unwholesome little miss of seven years of age.\n\n\"Rosa, go and kiss your dear cousin,\" Mrs. Frederick said. \"Don't you know me, George? I am your aunt.\"\n\n\"I know you well enough,\" George said; \"but I don't like kissing, please\"; and he retreated from the obedient caresses of his cousin.\n\n\"Take me to your dear mamma, you droll child,\" Mrs. Frederick said, and those ladies accordingly met, after an absence of more than fifteen years. During Emmy's cares and poverty the other had never once thought about coming to see her, but now that she was decently prosperous in the world, her sister-in-law came to her as a matter of course.\n\nSo did numbers more. Our old friend, Miss Swartz, and her husband came thundering over from Hampton Court, with flaming yellow liveries, and was as impetuously fond of Amelia as ever. Miss Swartz would have liked her always if she could have seen her. One must do her that justice. But, que voulez vous?--in this vast town one has not the time to go and seek one's friends; if they drop out of the rank they disappear, and we march on without them. Who is ever missed in Vanity Fair?\n\nBut so, in a word, and before the period of grief for Mr. Osborne's death had subsided, Emmy found herself in the centre of a very genteel circle indeed, the members of which could not conceive that anybody belonging to it was not very lucky. There was scarce one of the ladies that hadn't a relation a Peer, though the husband might be a drysalter in the City. Some of the ladies were very blue and well informed, reading Mrs. Somerville and frequenting the Royal Institution; others were severe and Evangelical, and held by Exeter Hall. Emmy, it must be owned, found herself entirely at a loss in the midst of their clavers, and suffered woefully on the one or two occasions on which she was compelled to accept Mrs. Frederick Bullock's hospitalities. That lady persisted in patronizing her and determined most graciously to form her. She found Amelia's milliners for her and regulated her household and her manners. She drove over constantly from Roehampton and entertained her friend with faint fashionable fiddle-faddle and feeble Court slip-slop. Jos liked to hear it, but the Major used to go off growling at the appearance of this woman, with her twopenny gentility. He went to sleep under Frederick Bullock's bald head, after dinner, at one of the banker's best parties (Fred was still anxious that the balance of the Osborne property should be transferred from Stumpy and Rowdy's to them), and whilst Amelia, who did not know Latin, or who wrote the last crack article in the Edinburgh, and did not in the least deplore, or otherwise, Mr. Peel's late extraordinary tergiversation on the fatal Catholic Relief Bill, sat dumb amongst the ladies in the grand drawing-room, looking out upon velvet lawns, trim gravel walks, and glistening hot-houses.\n\n\"She seems good-natured but insipid,\" said Mrs. Rowdy; \"that Major seems to be particularly epris.\"\n\n\"She wants ton sadly,\" said Mrs. Hollyock. \"My dear creature, you never will be able to form her.\"\n\n\"She is dreadfully ignorant or indifferent,\" said Mrs. Glowry with a voice as if from the grave, and a sad shake of the head and turban. \"I asked her if she thought that it was in 1836, according to Mr. Jowls, or in 1839, according to Mr. Wapshot, that the Pope was to fall: and she said--'Poor Pope! I hope not--What has he done?'\"\n\n\"She is my brother's widow, my dear friends,\" Mrs. Frederick replied, \"and as such I think we're all bound to give her every attention and instruction on entering into the world. You may fancy there can be no MERCENARY motives in those whose DISAPPOINTMENTS are well known.\"\n\n\"That poor dear Mrs. Bullock,\" said Rowdy to Hollyock, as they drove away together--\"she is always scheming and managing. She wants Mrs. Osborne's account to be taken from our house to hers--and the way in which she coaxes that boy and makes him sit by that blear-eyed little Rosa is perfectly ridiculous.\"\n\n\"I wish Glowry was choked with her Man of Sin and her Battle of Armageddon,\" cried the other, and the carriage rolled away over Putney Bridge.\n\nBut this sort of society was too cruelly genteel for Emmy, and all jumped for joy when a foreign tour was proposed.\n\nAm Rhein\nThe above everyday events had occurred, and a few weeks had passed, when on one fine morning, Parliament being over, the summer advanced, and all the good company in London about to quit that city for their annual tour in search of pleasure or health, the Batavier steamboat left the Tower-stairs laden with a goodly company of English fugitives. The quarter-deck awnings were up, and the benches and gangways crowded with scores of rosy children, bustling nursemaids; ladies in the prettiest pink bonnets and summer dresses; gentlemen in travelling caps and linen-jackets, whose mustachios had just begun to sprout for the ensuing tour; and stout trim old veterans with starched neckcloths and neat-brushed hats, such as have invaded Europe any time since the conclusion of the war, and carry the national Goddem into every city of the Continent. The congregation of hat-boxes, and Bramah desks, and dressing-cases was prodigious. There were jaunty young Cambridge-men travelling with their tutor, and going for a reading excursion to Nonnenwerth or Konigswinter; there were Irish gentlemen, with the most dashing whiskers and jewellery, talking about horses incessantly, and prodigiously polite to the young ladies on board, whom, on the contrary, the Cambridge lads and their pale-faced tutor avoided with maiden coyness; there were old Pall Mall loungers bound for Ems and Wiesbaden and a course of waters to clear off the dinners of the season, and a little roulette and trente-et-quarante to keep the excitement going; there was old Methuselah, who had married his young wife, with Captain Papillon of the Guards holding her parasol and guide-books; there was young May who was carrying off his bride on a pleasure tour (Mrs. Winter that was, and who had been at school with May's grandmother); there was Sir John and my Lady with a dozen children, and corresponding nursemaids; and the great grandee Bareacres family that sat by themselves near the wheel, stared at everybody, and spoke to no one. Their carriages, emblazoned with coronets and heaped with shining imperials, were on the foredeck, locked in with a dozen more such vehicles: it was difficult to pass in and out amongst them; and the poor inmates of the fore-cabin had scarcely any space for locomotion. These consisted of a few magnificently attired gentlemen from Houndsditch, who brought their own provisions, and could have bought half the gay people in the grand saloon; a few honest fellows with mustachios and portfolios, who set to sketching before they had been half an hour on board; one or two French femmes de chambre who began to be dreadfully ill by the time the boat had passed Greenwich; a groom or two who lounged in the neighbourhood of the horse-boxes under their charge, or leaned over the side by the paddle-wheels, and talked about who was good for the Leger, and what they stood to win or lose for the Goodwood cup.\n\nAll the couriers, when they had done plunging about the ship and had settled their various masters in the cabins or on the deck, congregated together and began to chatter and smoke; the Hebrew gentlemen joining them and looking at the carriages. There was Sir John's great carriage that would hold thirteen people; my Lord Methuselah's carriage, my Lord Bareacres' chariot, britzska, and fourgon, that anybody might pay for who liked. It was a wonder how my Lord got the ready money to pay for the expenses of the journey. The Hebrew gentlemen knew how he got it. They knew what money his Lordship had in his pocket at that instant, and what interest he paid for it, and who gave it him. Finally there was a very neat, handsome travelling carriage, about which the gentlemen speculated.\n\n\"A qui cette voiture la?\" said one gentleman-courier with a large morocco money-bag and ear-rings to another with ear-rings and a large morocco money-bag.\n\n\"C'est a Kirsch je bense--je l'ai vu toute a l'heure--qui brenoit des sangviches dans la voiture,\" said the courier in a fine German French.\n\nKirsch emerging presently from the neighbourhood of the hold, where he had been bellowing instructions intermingled with polyglot oaths to the ship's men engaged in secreting the passengers' luggage, came to give an account of himself to his brother interpreters. He informed them that the carriage belonged to a Nabob from Calcutta and Jamaica enormously rich, and with whom he was engaged to travel; and at this moment a young gentleman who had been warned off the bridge between the paddle-boxes, and who had dropped thence on to the roof of Lord Methuselah's carriage, from which he made his way over other carriages and imperials until he had clambered on to his own, descended thence and through the window into the body of the carriage, to the applause of the couriers looking on.\n\n\"Nous allons avoir une belle traversee, Monsieur George,\" said the courier with a grin, as he lifted his gold-laced cap.\n\n\"D---- your French,\" said the young gentleman, \"where's the biscuits, ay?\" Whereupon Kirsch answered him in the English language or in such an imitation of it as he could command--for though he was familiar with all languages, Mr. Kirsch was not acquainted with a single one, and spoke all with indifferent volubility and incorrectness.\n\nThe imperious young gentleman who gobbled the biscuits (and indeed it was time to refresh himself, for he had breakfasted at Richmond full three hours before) was our young friend George Osborne. Uncle Jos and his mamma were on the quarter-deck with a gentleman of whom they used to see a good deal, and the four were about to make a summer tour.\n\nJos was seated at that moment on deck under the awning, and pretty nearly opposite to the Earl of Bareacres and his family, whose proceedings absorbed the Bengalee almost entirely. Both the noble couple looked rather younger than in the eventful year '15, when Jos remembered to have seen them at Brussels (indeed, he always gave out in India that he was intimately acquainted with them). Lady Bareacres' hair, which was then dark, was now a beautiful golden auburn, whereas Lord Bareacres' whiskers, formerly red, were at present of a rich black with purple and green reflections in the light. But changed as they were, the movements of the noble pair occupied Jos's mind entirely. The presence of a Lord fascinated him, and he could look at nothing else.\n\n\"Those people seem to interest you a good deal,\" said Dobbin, laughing and watching him. Amelia too laughed. She was in a straw bonnet with black ribbons, and otherwise dressed in mourning, but the little bustle and holiday of the journey pleased and excited her, and she looked particularly happy.\n\n\"What a heavenly day!\" Emmy said and added, with great originality, \"I hope we shall have a calm passage.\"\n\nJos waved his hand, scornfully glancing at the same time under his eyelids at the great folks opposite. \"If you had made the voyages we have,\" he said, \"you wouldn't much care about the weather.\" But nevertheless, traveller as he was, he passed the night direfully sick in his carriage, where his courier tended him with brandy-and-water and every luxury.\n\nIn due time this happy party landed at the quays of Rotterdam, whence they were transported by another steamer to the city of Cologne. Here the carriage and the family took to the shore, and Jos was not a little gratified to see his arrival announced in the Cologne newspapers as \"Herr Graf Lord von Sedley nebst Begleitung aus London.\" He had his court dress with him; he had insisted that Dobbin should bring his regimental paraphernalia; he announced that it was his intention to be presented at some foreign courts, and pay his respects to the Sovereigns of the countries which he honoured with a visit.\n\nWherever the party stopped, and an opportunity was offered, Mr. Jos left his own card and the Major's upon \"Our Minister.\" It was with great difficulty that he could be restrained from putting on his cocked hat and tights to wait upon the English consul at the Free City of Judenstadt, when that hospitable functionary asked our travellers to dinner. He kept a journal of his voyage and noted elaborately the defects or excellences of the various inns at which he put up, and of the wines and dishes of which he partook.\n\nAs for Emmy, she was very happy and pleased. Dobbin used to carry about for her her stool and sketch-book, and admired the drawings of the good-natured little artist as they never had been admired before. She sat upon steamers' decks and drew crags and castles, or she mounted upon donkeys and ascended to ancient robber-towers, attended by her two aides-de-camp, Georgy and Dobbin. She laughed, and the Major did too, at his droll figure on donkey-back, with his long legs touching the ground. He was the interpreter for the party; having a good military knowledge of the German language, and he and the delighted George fought the campaigns of the Rhine and the Palatinate. In the course of a few weeks, and by assiduously conversing with Herr Kirsch on the box of the carriage, Georgy made prodigious advance in the knowledge of High Dutch, and could talk to hotel waiters and postilions in a way that charmed his mother and amused his guardian.\n\nMr. Jos did not much engage in the afternoon excursions of his fellow-travellers. He slept a good deal after dinner, or basked in the arbours of the pleasant inn-gardens. Pleasant Rhine gardens! Fair scenes of peace and sunshine--noble purple mountains, whose crests are reflected in the magnificent stream--who has ever seen you that has not a grateful memory of those scenes of friendly repose and beauty? To lay down the pen and even to think of that beautiful Rhineland makes one happy. At this time of summer evening, the cows are trooping down from the hills, lowing and with their bells tinkling, to the old town, with its old moats, and gates, and spires, and chestnut-trees, with long blue shadows stretching over the grass; the sky and the river below flame in crimson and gold; and the moon is already out, looking pale towards the sunset. The sun sinks behind the great castle-crested mountains, the night falls suddenly, the river grows darker and darker, lights quiver in it from the windows in the old ramparts, and twinkle peacefully in the villages under the hills on the opposite shore.\n\nSo Jos used to go to sleep a good deal with his bandanna over his face and be very comfortable, and read all the English news, and every word of Galignani's admirable newspaper (may the blessings of all Englishmen who have ever been abroad rest on the founders and proprietors of that piratical print! ) and whether he woke or slept, his friends did not very much miss him. Yes, they were very happy. They went to the opera often of evenings--to those snug, unassuming, dear old operas in the German towns, where the noblesse sits and cries, and knits stockings on the one side, over against the bourgeoisie on the other; and His Transparency the Duke and his Transparent family, all very fat and good-natured, come and occupy the great box in the middle; and the pit is full of the most elegant slim-waisted officers with straw-coloured mustachios, and twopence a day on full pay. Here it was that Emmy found her delight, and was introduced for the first time to the wonders of Mozart and Cimarosa. The Major's musical taste has been before alluded to, and his performances on the flute commended. But perhaps the chief pleasure he had in these operas was in watching Emmy's rapture while listening to them. A new world of love and beauty broke upon her when she was introduced to those divine compositions; this lady had the keenest and finest sensibility, and how could she be indifferent when she heard Mozart? The tender parts of \"Don Juan\" awakened in her raptures so exquisite that she would ask herself when she went to say her prayers of a night whether it was not wicked to feel so much delight as that with which \"Vedrai Carino\" and \"Batti Batti\" filled her gentle little bosom? But the Major, whom she consulted upon this head, as her theological adviser (and who himself had a pious and reverent soul), said that for his part, every beauty of art or nature made him thankful as well as happy, and that the pleasure to be had in listening to fine music, as in looking at the stars in the sky, or at a beautiful landscape or picture, was a benefit for which we might thank Heaven as sincerely as for any other worldly blessing. And in reply to some faint objections of Mrs. Amelia's (taken from certain theological works like the Washerwoman of Finchley Common and others of that school, with which Mrs. Osborne had been furnished during her life at Brompton) he told her an Eastern fable of the Owl who thought that the sunshine was unbearable for the eyes and that the Nightingale was a most overrated bird. \"It is one's nature to sing and the other's to hoot,\" he said, laughing, \"and with such a sweet voice as you have yourself, you must belong to the Bulbul faction.\"\n\nI like to dwell upon this period of her life and to think that she was cheerful and happy. You see, she has not had too much of that sort of existence as yet, and has not fallen in the way of means to educate her tastes or her intelligence. She has been domineered over hitherto by vulgar intellects. It is the lot of many a woman. And as every one of the dear sex is the rival of the rest of her kind, timidity passes for folly in their charitable judgments; and gentleness for dulness; and silence--which is but timid denial of the unwelcome assertion of ruling folks, and tacit protestantism--above all, finds no mercy at the hands of the female Inquisition. Thus, my dear and civilized reader, if you and I were to find ourselves this evening in a society of greengrocers, let us say, it is probable that our conversation would not be brilliant; if, on the other hand, a greengrocer should find himself at your refined and polite tea-table, where everybody was saying witty things, and everybody of fashion and repute tearing her friends to pieces in the most delightful manner, it is possible that the stranger would not be very talkative and by no means interesting or interested.\n\nAnd it must be remembered that this poor lady had never met a gentleman in her life until this present moment. Perhaps these are rarer personages than some of us think for. Which of us can point out many such in his circle--men whose aims are generous, whose truth is constant, and not only constant in its kind but elevated in its degree; whose want of meanness makes them simple; who can look the world honestly in the face with an equal manly sympathy for the great and the small? We all know a hundred whose coats are very well made, and a score who have excellent manners, and one or two happy beings who are what they call in the inner circles, and have shot into the very centre and bull's-eye of the fashion; but of gentlemen how many? Let us take a little scrap of paper and each make out his list.\n\nMy friend the Major I write, without any doubt, in mine. He had very long legs, a yellow face, and a slight lisp, which at first was rather ridiculous. But his thoughts were just, his brains were fairly good, his life was honest and pure, and his heart warm and humble. He certainly had very large hands and feet, which the two George Osbornes used to caricature and laugh at; and their jeers and laughter perhaps led poor little Emmy astray as to his worth. But have we not all been misled about our heroes and changed our opinions a hundred times? Emmy, in this happy time, found that hers underwent a very great change in respect of the merits of the Major.\n\nPerhaps it was the happiest time of both their lives, indeed, if they did but know it--and who does? Which of us can point out and say that was the culmination--that was the summit of human joy? But at all events, this couple were very decently contented, and enjoyed as pleasant a summer tour as any pair that left England that year. Georgy was always present at the play, but it was the Major who put Emmy's shawl on after the entertainment; and in the walks and excursions the young lad would be on ahead, and up a tower-stair or a tree, whilst the soberer couple were below, the Major smoking his cigar with great placidity and constancy, whilst Emmy sketched the site or the ruin. It was on this very tour that I, the present writer of a history of which every word is true, had the pleasure to see them first and to make their acquaintance.\n\nIt was at the little comfortable Ducal town of Pumpernickel (that very place where Sir Pitt Crawley had been so distinguished as an attache; but that was in early early days, and before the news of the Battle of Austerlitz sent all the English diplomatists in Germany to the right about) that I first saw Colonel Dobbin and his party. They had arrived with the carriage and courier at the Erbprinz Hotel, the best of the town, and the whole party dined at the table d'hote. Everybody remarked the majesty of Jos and the knowing way in which he sipped, or rather sucked, the Johannisberger, which he ordered for dinner. The little boy, too, we observed, had a famous appetite, and consumed schinken, and braten, and kartoffeln, and cranberry jam, and salad, and pudding, and roast fowls, and sweetmeats, with a gallantry that did honour to his nation. After about fifteen dishes, he concluded the repast with dessert, some of which he even carried out of doors, for some young gentlemen at table, amused with his coolness and gallant free-and-easy manner, induced him to pocket a handful of macaroons, which he discussed on his way to the theatre, whither everybody went in the cheery social little German place. The lady in black, the boy's mamma, laughed and blushed, and looked exceedingly pleased and shy as the dinner went on, and at the various feats and instances of espieglerie on the part of her son. The Colonel--for so he became very soon afterwards--I remember joked the boy with a great deal of grave fun, pointing out dishes which he hadn't tried, and entreating him not to baulk his appetite, but to have a second supply of this or that.\n\nIt was what they call a gast-rolle night at the Royal Grand Ducal Pumpernickelisch Hof--or Court theatre--and Madame Schroeder Devrient, then in the bloom of her beauty and genius, performed the part of the heroine in the wonderful opera of Fidelio. From our places in the stalls we could see our four friends of the table d'hote in the loge which Schwendler of the Erbprinz kept for his best guests, and I could not help remarking the effect which the magnificent actress and music produced upon Mrs. Osborne, for so we heard the stout gentleman in the mustachios call her. During the astonishing Chorus of the Prisoners, over which the delightful voice of the actress rose and soared in the most ravishing harmony, the English lady's face wore such an expression of wonder and delight that it struck even little Fipps, the blase attache, who drawled out, as he fixed his glass upon her, \"Gayd, it really does one good to see a woman caypable of that stayt of excaytement.\" And in the Prison Scene, where Fidelio, rushing to her husband, cries, \"Nichts, nichts, mein Florestan,\" she fairly lost herself and covered her face with her handkerchief. Every woman in the house was snivelling at the time, but I suppose it was because it was predestined that I was to write this particular lady's memoirs that I remarked her.\n\nThe next day they gave another piece of Beethoven, Die Schlacht bei Vittoria. Malbrook is introduced at the beginning of the performance, as indicative of the brisk advance of the French army. Then come drums, trumpets, thunders of artillery, and groans of the dying, and at last, in a grand triumphal swell, \"God Save the King\" is performed.\n\nThere may have been a score of Englishmen in the house, but at the burst of that beloved and well-known music, every one of them, we young fellows in the stalls, Sir John and Lady Bullminster (who had taken a house at Pumpernickel for the education of their nine children), the fat gentleman with the mustachios, the long Major in white duck trousers, and the lady with the little boy upon whom he was so sweet, even Kirsch, the courier in the gallery, stood bolt upright in their places and proclaimed themselves to be members of the dear old British nation. As for Tapeworm, the Charge d'Affaires, he rose up in his box and bowed and simpered, as if he would represent the whole empire. Tapeworm was nephew and heir of old Marshal Tiptoff, who has been introduced in this story as General Tiptoff, just before Waterloo, who was Colonel of the --th regiment in which Major Dobbin served, and who died in this year full of honours, and of an aspic of plovers' eggs; when the regiment was graciously given by his Majesty to Colonel Sir Michael O'Dowd, K.C.B. who had commanded it in many glorious fields.\n\nTapeworm must have met with Colonel Dobbin at the house of the Colonel's Colonel, the Marshal, for he recognized him on this night at the theatre, and with the utmost condescension, his Majesty's minister came over from his own box and publicly shook hands with his new-found friend.\n\n\"Look at that infernal sly-boots of a Tapeworm,\" Fipps whispered, examining his chief from the stalls. \"Wherever there's a pretty woman he always twists himself in.\" And I wonder what were diplomatists made for but for that?\n\n\"Have I the honour of addressing myself to Mrs. Dobbin?\" asked the Secretary with a most insinuating grin.\n\nGeorgy burst out laughing and said, \"By Jove, that was a good 'un.\" Emmy and the Major blushed: we saw them from the stalls.\n\n\"This lady is Mrs. George Osborne,\" said the Major, \"and this is her brother, Mr. Sedley, a distinguished officer of the Bengal Civil Service: permit me to introduce him to your lordship.\"\n\nMy lord nearly sent Jos off his legs with the most fascinating smile. \"Are you going to stop in Pumpernickel?\" he said. \"It is a dull place, but we want some nice people, and we would try and make it SO agreeable to you. Mr.--Ahum--Mrs.--Oho. I shall do myself the honour of calling upon you to-morrow at your inn.\" And he went away with a Parthian grin and glance which he thought must finish Mrs. Osborne completely.\n\nThe performance over, the young fellows lounged about the lobbies, and we saw the society take its departure. The Duchess Dowager went off in her jingling old coach, attended by two faithful and withered old maids of honour, and a little snuffy spindle-shanked gentleman in waiting, in a brown jasey and a green coat covered with orders--of which the star and the grand yellow cordon of the order of St. Michael of Pumpernickel were most conspicuous. The drums rolled, the guards saluted, and the old carriage drove away.\n\nThen came his Transparency the Duke and Transparent family, with his great officers of state and household. He bowed serenely to everybody. And amid the saluting of the guards and the flaring of the torches of the running footmen, clad in scarlet, the Transparent carriages drove away to the old Ducal schloss, with its towers and pinacles standing on the schlossberg. Everybody in Pumpernickel knew everybody. No sooner was a foreigner seen there than the Minister of Foreign Affairs, or some other great or small officer of state, went round to the Erbprinz and found out the name of the new arrival.\n\nWe watched them, too, out of the theatre. Tapeworm had just walked off, enveloped in his cloak, with which his gigantic chasseur was always in attendance, and looking as much as possible like Don Juan. The Prime Minister's lady had just squeezed herself into her sedan, and her daughter, the charming Ida, had put on her calash and clogs; when the English party came out, the boy yawning drearily, the Major taking great pains in keeping the shawl over Mrs. Osborne's head, and Mr. Sedley looking grand, with a crush opera-hat on one side of his head and his hand in the stomach of a voluminous white waistcoat. We took off our hats to our acquaintances of the table d'hote, and the lady, in return, presented us with a little smile and a curtsey, for which everybody might be thankful.\n\nThe carriage from the inn, under the superintendence of the bustling Mr. Kirsch, was in waiting to convey the party; but the fat man said he would walk and smoke his cigar on his way homewards, so the other three, with nods and smiles to us, went without Mr. Sedley, Kirsch, with the cigar case, following in his master's wake.\n\nWe all walked together and talked to the stout gentleman about the agremens of the place. It was very agreeable for the English. There were shooting-parties and battues; there was a plenty of balls and entertainments at the hospitable Court; the society was generally good; the theatre excellent; and the living cheap.\n\n\"And our Minister seems a most delightful and affable person,\" our new friend said. \"With such a representative, and--and a good medical man, I can fancy the place to be most eligible. Good-night, gentlemen.\" And Jos creaked up the stairs to bedward, followed by Kirsch with a flambeau. We rather hoped that nice-looking woman would be induced to stay some time in the town.\n\nIn Which We Meet an Old Acquaintance\n\nSuch polite behaviour as that of Lord Tapeworm did not fail to have the most favourable effect upon Mr. Sedley's mind, and the very next morning, at breakfast, he pronounced his opinion that Pumpernickel was the pleasantest little place of any which he had visited on their tour. Jos's motives and artifices were not very difficult of comprehension, and Dobbin laughed in his sleeve, like a hypocrite as he was, when he found, by the knowing air of the civilian and the offhand manner in which the latter talked about Tapeworm Castle and the other members of the family, that Jos had been up already in the morning, consulting his travelling Peerage. Yes, he had seen the Right Honourable the Earl of Bagwig, his lordship's father; he was sure he had, he had met him at--at the Levee--didn't Dob remember? and when the Diplomatist called on the party, faithful to his promise, Jos received him with such a salute and honours as were seldom accorded to the little Envoy. He winked at Kirsch on his Excellency's arrival, and that emissary, instructed before-hand, went out and superintended an entertainment of cold meats, jellies, and other delicacies, brought in upon trays, and of which Mr. Jos absolutely insisted that his noble guest should partake.\n\nTapeworm, so long as he could have an opportunity of admiring the bright eyes of Mrs. Osborne (whose freshness of complexion bore daylight remarkably well) was not ill pleased to accept any invitation to stay in Mr. Sedley's lodgings; he put one or two dexterous questions to him about India and the dancing-girls there; asked Amelia about that beautiful boy who had been with her; and complimented the astonished little woman upon the prodigious sensation which she had made in the house; and tried to fascinate Dobbin by talking of the late war and the exploits of the Pumpernickel contingent under the command of the Hereditary Prince, now Duke of Pumpernickel.\n\nLord Tapeworm inherited no little portion of the family gallantry, and it was his happy belief that almost every woman upon whom he himself cast friendly eyes was in love with him. He left Emmy under the persuasion that she was slain by his wit and attractions and went home to his lodgings to write a pretty little note to her. She was not fascinated, only puzzled, by his grinning, his simpering, his scented cambric handkerchief, and his high-heeled lacquered boots. She did not understand one-half the compliments which he paid; she had never, in her small experience of mankind, met a professional ladies' man as yet, and looked upon my lord as something curious rather than pleasant; and if she did not admire, certainly wondered at him. Jos, on the contrary, was delighted. \"How very affable his Lordship is,\" he said; \"How very kind of his Lordship to say he would send his medical man! Kirsch, you will carry our cards to the Count de Schlusselback directly; the Major and I will have the greatest pleasure in paying our respects at Court as soon as possible. Put out my uniform, Kirsch--both our uniforms. It is a mark of politeness which every English gentleman ought to show to the countries which he visits to pay his respects to the sovereigns of those countries as to the representatives of his own.\"\n\nWhen Tapeworm's doctor came, Doctor von Glauber, Body Physician to H.S.H. the Duke, he speedily convinced Jos that the Pumpernickel mineral springs and the Doctor's particular treatment would infallibly restore the Bengalee to youth and slimness. \"Dere came here last year,\" he said, \"Sheneral Bulkeley, an English Sheneral, tvice so pic as you, sir. I sent him back qvite tin after tree months, and he danced vid Baroness Glauber at the end of two.\"\n\nJos's mind was made up; the springs, the Doctor, the Court, and the Charge d'Affaires convinced him, and he proposed to spend the autumn in these delightful quarters. And punctual to his word, on the next day the Charge d'Affaires presented Jos and the Major to Victor Aurelius XVII, being conducted to their audience with that sovereign by the Count de Schlusselback, Marshal of the Court.\n\nThey were straightway invited to dinner at Court, and their intention of staying in the town being announced, the politest ladies of the whole town instantly called upon Mrs. Osborne; and as not one of these, however poor they might be, was under the rank of a Baroness, Jos's delight was beyond expression. He wrote off to Chutney at the Club to say that the Service was highly appreciated in Germany, that he was going to show his friend, the Count de Schlusselback, how to stick a pig in the Indian fashion, and that his august friends, the Duke and Duchess, were everything that was kind and civil.\n\nEmmy, too, was presented to the august family, and as mourning is not admitted in Court on certain days, she appeared in a pink crape dress with a diamond ornament in the corsage, presented to her by her brother, and she looked so pretty in this costume that the Duke and Court (putting out of the question the Major, who had scarcely ever seen her before in an evening dress, and vowed that she did not look five-and-twenty) all admired her excessively.\n\nIn this dress she walked a Polonaise with Major Dobbin at a Court ball, in which easy dance Mr. Jos had the honour of leading out the Countess of Schlusselback, an old lady with a hump back, but with sixteen good quarters of nobility and related to half the royal houses of Germany.\n\nPumpernickel stands in the midst of a happy valley through which sparkles--to mingle with the Rhine somewhere, but I have not the map at hand to say exactly at what point--the fertilizing stream of the Pump. In some places the river is big enough to support a ferry-boat, in others to turn a mill; in Pumpernickel itself, the last Transparency but three, the great and renowned Victor Aurelius XIV built a magnificent bridge, on which his own statue rises, surrounded by water-nymphs and emblems of victory, peace, and plenty; he has his foot on the neck of a prostrate Turk--history says he engaged and ran a Janissary through the body at the relief of Vienna by Sobieski--but, quite undisturbed by the agonies of that prostrate Mahometan, who writhes at his feet in the most ghastly manner, the Prince smiles blandly and points with his truncheon in the direction of the Aurelius Platz, where he began to erect a new palace that would have been the wonder of his age had the great-souled Prince but had funds to complete it. But the completion of Monplaisir (Monblaisir the honest German folks call it) was stopped for lack of ready money, and it and its park and garden are now in rather a faded condition, and not more than ten times big enough to accommodate the Court of the reigning Sovereign.\n\nThe gardens were arranged to emulate those of Versailles, and amidst the terraces and groves there are some huge allegorical waterworks still, which spout and froth stupendously upon fete-days, and frighten one with their enormous aquatic insurrections. There is the Trophonius' cave in which, by some artifice, the leaden Tritons are made not only to spout water, but to play the most dreadful groans out of their lead conchs--there is the nymphbath and the Niagara cataract, which the people of the neighbourhood admire beyond expression, when they come to the yearly fair at the opening of the Chamber, or to the fetes with which the happy little nation still celebrates the birthdays and marriage-days of its princely governors.\n\nThen from all the towns of the Duchy, which stretches for nearly ten mile--from Bolkum, which lies on its western frontier bidding defiance to Prussia, from Grogwitz, where the Prince has a hunting-lodge, and where his dominions are separated by the Pump River from those of the neighbouring Prince of Potzenthal; from all the little villages, which besides these three great cities, dot over the happy principality--from the farms and the mills along the Pump come troops of people in red petticoats and velvet head-dresses, or with three-cornered hats and pipes in their mouths, who flock to the Residenz and share in the pleasures of the fair and the festivities there. Then the theatre is open for nothing, then the waters of Monblaisir begin to play (it is lucky that there is company to behold them, for one would be afraid to see them alone)--then there come mountebanks and riding troops (the way in which his Transparency was fascinated by one of the horse-riders is well known, and it is believed that La Petite Vivandiere, as she was called, was a spy in the French interest), and the delighted people are permitted to march through room after room of the Grand Ducal palace and admire the slippery floor, the rich hangings, and the spittoons at the doors of all the innumerable chambers. There is one Pavilion at Monblaisir which Aurelius Victor XV had arranged--a great Prince but too fond of pleasure--and which I am told is a perfect wonder of licentious elegance. It is painted with the story of Bacchus and Ariadne, and the table works in and out of the room by means of a windlass, so that the company was served without any intervention of domestics. But the place was shut up by Barbara, Aurelius XV's widow, a severe and devout Princess of the House of Bolkum and Regent of the Duchy during her son's glorious minority, and after the death of her husband, cut off in the pride of his pleasures.\n\nThe theatre of Pumpernickel is known and famous in that quarter of Germany. It languished a little when the present Duke in his youth insisted upon having his own operas played there, and it is said one day, in a fury, from his place in the orchestra, when he attended a rehearsal, broke a bassoon on the head of the Chapel Master, who was conducting, and led too slow; and during which time the Duchess Sophia wrote domestic comedies, which must have been very dreary to witness. But the Prince executes his music in private now, and the Duchess only gives away her plays to the foreigners of distinction who visit her kind little Court.\n\nIt is conducted with no small comfort and splendour. When there are balls, though there may be four hundred people at supper, there is a servant in scarlet and lace to attend upon every four, and every one is served on silver. There are festivals and entertainments going continually on, and the Duke has his chamberlains and equerries, and the Duchess her mistress of the wardrobe and ladies of honour, just like any other and more potent potentates.\n\nThe Constitution is or was a moderate despotism, tempered by a Chamber that might or might not be elected. I never certainly could hear of its sitting in my time at Pumpernickel. The Prime Minister had lodgings in a second floor, and the Foreign Secretary occupied the comfortable lodgings over Zwieback's Conditorey. The army consisted of a magnificent band that also did duty on the stage, where it was quite pleasant to see the worthy fellows marching in Turkish dresses with rouge on and wooden scimitars, or as Roman warriors with ophicleides and trombones--to see them again, I say, at night, after one had listened to them all the morning in the Aurelius Platz, where they performed opposite the cafe where we breakfasted. Besides the band, there was a rich and numerous staff of officers, and, I believe, a few men. Besides the regular sentries, three or four men, habited as hussars, used to do duty at the Palace, but I never saw them on horseback, and au fait, what was the use of cavalry in a time of profound peace?--and whither the deuce should the hussars ride?\n\nEverybody--everybody that was noble of course, for as for the bourgeois we could not quite be expected to take notice of THEM--visited his neighbour. H. E. Madame de Burst received once a week, H. E. Madame de Schnurrbart had her night--the theatre was open twice a week, the Court graciously received once, so that a man's life might in fact be a perfect round of pleasure in the unpretending Pumpernickel way.\n\nThat there were feuds in the place, no one can deny. Politics ran very high at Pumpernickel, and parties were very bitter. There was the Strumpff faction and the Lederlung party, the one supported by our envoy and the other by the French Charge d'Affaires, M. de Macabau. Indeed it sufficed for our Minister to stand up for Madame Strumpff, who was clearly the greater singer of the two, and had three more notes in her voice than Madame Lederlung her rival--it sufficed, I say, for our Minister to advance any opinion to have it instantly contradicted by the French diplomatist.\n\nEverybody in the town was ranged in one or other of these factions. The Lederlung was a prettyish little creature certainly, and her voice (what there was of it) was very sweet, and there is no doubt that the Strumpff was not in her first youth and beauty, and certainly too stout; when she came on in the last scene of the Sonnambula, for instance, in her night-chemise with a lamp in her hand, and had to go out of the window, and pass over the plank of the mill, it was all she could do to squeeze out of the window, and the plank used to bend and creak again under her weight--but how she poured out the finale of the opera! and with what a burst of feeling she rushed into Elvino's arms--almost fit to smother him! Whereas the little Lederlung--but a truce to this gossip--the fact is that these two women were the two flags of the French and the English party at Pumpernickel, and the society was divided in its allegiance to those two great nations.\n\nWe had on our side the Home Minister, the Master of the Horse, the Duke's Private Secretary, and the Prince's Tutor; whereas of the French party were the Foreign Minister, the Commander-in-Chief's Lady, who had served under Napoleon, and the Hof-Marschall and his wife, who was glad enough to get the fashions from Paris, and always had them and her caps by M. de Macabau's courier. The Secretary of his Chancery was little Grignac, a young fellow, as malicious as Satan, and who made caricatures of Tapeworm in all the albums of the place.\n\nTheir headquarters and table d'hote were established at the Pariser Hof, the other inn of the town; and though, of course, these gentlemen were obliged to be civil in public, yet they cut at each other with epigrams that were as sharp as razors, as I have seen a couple of wrestlers in Devonshire, lashing at each other's shins and never showing their agony upon a muscle of their faces. Neither Tapeworm nor Macabau ever sent home a dispatch to his government without a most savage series of attacks upon his rival. For instance, on our side we would write, \"The interests of Great Britain in this place, and throughout the whole of Germany, are perilled by the continuance in office of the present French envoy; this man is of a character so infamous that he will stick at no falsehood, or hesitate at no crime, to attain his ends. He poisons the mind of the Court against the English minister, represents the conduct of Great Britain in the most odious and atrocious light, and is unhappily backed by a minister whose ignorance and necessities are as notorious as his influence is fatal.\" On their side they would say, \"M. de Tapeworm continues his system of stupid insular arrogance and vulgar falsehood against the greatest nation in the world. Yesterday he was heard to speak lightly of Her Royal Highness Madame the Duchess of Berri; on a former occasion he insulted the heroic Duke of Angouleme and dared to insinuate that H.R.H. the Duke of Orleans was conspiring against the august throne of the lilies. His gold is prodigated in every direction which his stupid menaces fail to frighten. By one and the other, he has won over creatures of the Court here--and, in fine, Pumpernickel will not be quiet, Germany tranquil, France respected, or Europe content until this poisonous viper be crushed under heel\": and so on. When one side or the other had written any particularly spicy dispatch, news of it was sure to slip out.\n\nBefore the winter was far advanced, it is actually on record that Emmy took a night and received company with great propriety and modesty. She had a French master, who complimented her upon the purity of her accent and her facility of learning; the fact is she had learned long ago and grounded herself subsequently in the grammar so as to be able to teach it to George; and Madam Strumpff came to give her lessons in singing, which she performed so well and with such a true voice that the Major's windows, who had lodgings opposite under the Prime Minister, were always open to hear the lesson. Some of the German ladies, who are very sentimental and simple in their tastes, fell in love with her and began to call her du at once. These are trivial details, but they relate to happy times. The Major made himself George's tutor and read Caesar and mathematics with him, and they had a German master and rode out of evenings by the side of Emmy's carriage--she was always too timid, and made a dreadful outcry at the slightest disturbance on horse-back. So she drove about with one of her dear German friends, and Jos asleep on the back-seat of the barouche.\n\nHe was becoming very sweet upon the Grafinn Fanny de Butterbrod, a very gentle tender-hearted and unassuming young creature, a Canoness and Countess in her own right, but with scarcely ten pounds per year to her fortune, and Fanny for her part declared that to be Amelia's sister was the greatest delight that Heaven could bestow on her, and Jos might have put a Countess's shield and coronet by the side of his own arms on his carriage and forks; when--when events occurred, and those grand fetes given upon the marriage of the Hereditary Prince of Pumpernickel with the lovely Princess Amelia of Humbourg-Schlippenschloppen took place.\n\nAt this festival the magnificence displayed was such as had not been known in the little German place since the days of the prodigal Victor XIV. All the neighbouring Princes, Princesses, and Grandees were invited to the feast. Beds rose to half a crown per night in Pumpernickel, and the Army was exhausted in providing guards of honour for the Highnesses, Serenities, and Excellencies who arrived from all quarters. The Princess was married by proxy, at her father's residence, by the Count de Schlusselback. Snuff-boxes were given away in profusion (as we learned from the Court jeweller, who sold and afterwards bought them again), and bushels of the Order of Saint Michael of Pumpernickel were sent to the nobles of the Court, while hampers of the cordons and decorations of the Wheel of St. Catherine of Schlippenschloppen were brought to ours. The French envoy got both. \"He is covered with ribbons like a prize cart-horse,\" Tapeworm said, who was not allowed by the rules of his service to take any decorations: \"Let him have the cordons; but with whom is the victory?\" The fact is, it was a triumph of British diplomacy, the French party having proposed and tried their utmost to carry a marriage with a Princess of the House of Potztausend-Donnerwetter, whom, as a matter of course, we opposed.\n\nEverybody was asked to the fetes of the marriage. Garlands and triumphal arches were hung across the road to welcome the young bride. The great Saint Michael's Fountain ran with uncommonly sour wine, while that in the Artillery Place frothed with beer. The great waters played; and poles were put up in the park and gardens for the happy peasantry, which they might climb at their leisure, carrying off watches, silver forks, prize sausages hung with pink ribbon, &c., at the top. Georgy got one, wrenching it off, having swarmed up the pole to the delight of the spectators, and sliding down with the rapidity of a fall of water. But it was for the glory's sake merely. The boy gave the sausage to a peasant, who had very nearly seized it, and stood at the foot of the mast, blubbering, because he was unsuccessful.\n\nAt the French Chancellerie they had six more lampions in their illumination than ours had; but our transparency, which represented the young Couple advancing and Discord flying away, with the most ludicrous likeness to the French Ambassador, beat the French picture hollow; and I have no doubt got Tapeworm the advancement and the Cross of the Bath which he subsequently attained.\n\nCrowds of foreigners arrived for the fetes, and of English, of course. Besides the Court balls, public balls were given at the Town Hall and the Redoute, and in the former place there was a room for trente-et-quarante and roulette established, for the week of the festivities only, and by one of the great German companies from Ems or Aix-la-Chapelle. The officers or inhabitants of the town were not allowed to play at these games, but strangers, peasants, ladies were admitted, and any one who chose to lose or win money.\n\nThat little scapegrace Georgy Osborne amongst others, whose pockets were always full of dollars and whose relations were away at the grand festival of the Court, came to the Stadthaus Ball in company of his uncle's courier, Mr. Kirsch, and having only peeped into a play-room at Baden-Baden when he hung on Dobbin's arm, and where, of course, he was not permitted to gamble, came eagerly to this part of the entertainment and hankered round the tables where the croupiers and the punters were at work. Women were playing; they were masked, some of them; this license was allowed in these wild times of carnival.\n\nA woman with light hair, in a low dress by no means so fresh as it had been, and with a black mask on, through the eyelets of which her eyes twinkled strangely, was seated at one of the roulette-tables with a card and a pin and a couple of florins before her. As the croupier called out the colour and number, she pricked on the card with great care and regularity, and only ventured her money on the colours after the red or black had come up a certain number of times. It was strange to look at her.\n\nBut in spite of her care and assiduity she guessed wrong and the last two florins followed each other under the croupier's rake, as he cried out with his inexorable voice the winning colour and number. She gave a sigh, a shrug with her shoulders, which were already too much out of her gown, and dashing the pin through the card on to the table, sat thrumming it for a while. Then she looked round her and saw Georgy's honest face staring at the scene. The little scamp! What business had he to be there?\n\nWhen she saw the boy, at whose face she looked hard through her shining eyes and mask, she said, \"Monsieur n'est pas joueur?\"\n\n\"Non, Madame,\" said the boy; but she must have known, from his accent, of what country he was, for she answered him with a slight foreign tone. \"You have nevare played--will you do me a littl' favor?\"\n\n\"What is it?\" said Georgy, blushing again. Mr. Kirsch was at work for his part at the rouge et noir and did not see his young master.\n\n\"Play this for me, if you please; put it on any number, any number.\" And she took from her bosom a purse, and out of it a gold piece, the only coin there, and she put it into George's hand. The boy laughed and did as he was bid.\n\nThe number came up sure enough. There is a power that arranges that, they say, for beginners.\n\n\"Thank you,\" said she, pulling the money towards her, \"thank you. What is your name?\"\n\n\"My name's Osborne,\" said Georgy, and was fingering in his own pockets for dollars, and just about to make a trial, when the Major, in his uniform, and Jos, en Marquis, from the Court ball, made their appearance. Other people, finding the entertainment stupid and preferring the fun at the Stadthaus, had quitted the Palace ball earlier; but it is probable the Major and Jos had gone home and found the boy's absence, for the former instantly went up to him and, taking him by the shoulder, pulled him briskly back from the place of temptation. Then, looking round the room, he saw Kirsch employed as we have said, and going up to him, asked how he dared to bring Mr. George to such a place.\n\n\"Laissez-moi tranquille,\" said Mr. Kirsch, very much excited by play and wine. \"Il faut s'amuser, parbleu. Je ne suis pas au service de Monsieur.\"\n\nSeeing his condition the Major did not choose to argue with the man, but contented himself with drawing away George and asking Jos if he would come away. He was standing close by the lady in the mask, who was playing with pretty good luck now, and looking on much interested at the game.\n\n\"Hadn't you better come, Jos,\" the Major said, \"with George and me?\"\n\n\"I'll stop and go home with that rascal, Kirsch,\" Jos said; and for the same reason of modesty, which he thought ought to be preserved before the boy, Dobbin did not care to remonstrate with Jos, but left him and walked home with Georgy.\n\n\"Did you play?\" asked the Major when they were out and on their way home.\n\nThe boy said \"No.\"\n\n\"Give me your word of honour as a gentleman that you never will.\"\n\n\"Why?\" said the boy; \"it seems very good fun.\" And, in a very eloquent and impressive manner, the Major showed him why he shouldn't, and would have enforced his precepts by the example of Georgy's own father, had he liked to say anything that should reflect on the other's memory. When he had housed him, he went to bed and saw his light, in the little room outside of Amelia's, presently disappear. Amelia's followed half an hour afterwards. I don't know what made the Major note it so accurately.\n\nJos, however, remained behind over the play-table; he was no gambler, but not averse to the little excitement of the sport now and then, and he had some Napoleons chinking in the embroidered pockets of his court waistcoat. He put down one over the fair shoulder of the little gambler before him, and they won. She made a little movement to make room for him by her side, and just took the skirt of her gown from a vacant chair there.\n\n\"Come and give me good luck,\" she said, still in a foreign accent, quite different from that frank and perfectly English \"Thank you,\" with which she had saluted Georgy's coup in her favour. The portly gentleman, looking round to see that nobody of rank observed him, sat down; he muttered--\"Ah, really, well now, God bless my soul. I'm very fortunate; I'm sure to give you good fortune,\" and other words of compliment and confusion. \"Do you play much?\" the foreign mask said.\n\n\"I put a Nap or two down,\" said Jos with a superb air, flinging down a gold piece.\n\n\"Yes; ay nap after dinner,\" said the mask archly. But Jos looking frightened, she continued, in her pretty French accent, \"You do not play to win. No more do I. I play to forget, but I cannot. I cannot forget old times, monsieur. Your little nephew is the image of his father; and you--you are not changed--but yes, you are. Everybody changes, everybody forgets; nobody has any heart.\"\n\n\"Good God, who is it?\" asked Jos in a flutter.\n\n\"Can't you guess, Joseph Sedley?\" said the little woman in a sad voice, and undoing her mask, she looked at him. \"You have forgotten me.\"\n\n\"Good heavens! Mrs. Crawley!\" gasped out Jos.\n\n\"Rebecca,\" said the other, putting her hand on his; but she followed the game still, all the time she was looking at him.\n\n\"I am stopping at the Elephant,\" she continued. \"Ask for Madame de Raudon. I saw my dear Amelia to-day; how pretty she looked, and how happy! So do you! Everybody but me, who am wretched, Joseph Sedley.\" And she put her money over from the red to the black, as if by a chance movement of her hand, and while she was wiping her eyes with a pocket-handkerchief fringed with torn lace.\n\nThe red came up again, and she lost the whole of that stake. \"Come away,\" she said. \"Come with me a little--we are old friends, are we not, dear Mr. Sedley?\"\n\nAnd Mr. Kirsch having lost all his money by this time, followed his master out into the moonlight, where the illuminations were winking out and the transparency over our mission was scarcely visible.\n\nA Vagabond Chapter\nWe must pass over a part of Mrs. Rebecca Crawley's biography with that lightness and delicacy which the world demands--the moral world, that has, perhaps, no particular objection to vice, but an insuperable repugnance to hearing vice called by its proper name. There are things we do and know perfectly well in Vanity Fair, though we never speak of them: as the Ahrimanians worship the devil, but don't mention him: and a polite public will no more bear to read an authentic description of vice than a truly refined English or American female will permit the word breeches to be pronounced in her chaste hearing. And yet, madam, both are walking the world before our faces every day, without much shocking us. If you were to blush every time they went by, what complexions you would have! It is only when their naughty names are called out that your modesty has any occasion to show alarm or sense of outrage, and it has been the wish of the present writer, all through this story, deferentially to submit to the fashion at present prevailing, and only to hint at the existence of wickedness in a light, easy, and agreeable manner, so that nobody's fine feelings may be offended. I defy any one to say that our Becky, who has certainly some vices, has not been presented to the public in a perfectly genteel and inoffensive manner. In describing this Siren, singing and smiling, coaxing and cajoling, the author, with modest pride, asks his readers all round, has he once forgotten the laws of politeness, and showed the monster's hideous tail above water? No! Those who like may peep down under waves that are pretty transparent and see it writhing and twirling, diabolically hideous and slimy, flapping amongst bones, or curling round corpses; but above the waterline, I ask, has not everything been proper, agreeable, and decorous, and has any the most squeamish immoralist in Vanity Fair a right to cry fie? When, however, the Siren disappears and dives below, down among the dead men, the water of course grows turbid over her, and it is labour lost to look into it ever so curiously. They look pretty enough when they sit upon a rock, twanging their harps and combing their hair, and sing, and beckon to you to come and hold the looking-glass; but when they sink into their native element, depend on it, those mermaids are about no good, and we had best not examine the fiendish marine cannibals, revelling and feasting on their wretched pickled victims. And so, when Becky is out of the way, be sure that she is not particularly well employed, and that the less that is said about her doings is in fact the better.\n\nIf we were to give a full account of her proceedings during a couple of years that followed after the Curzon Street catastrophe, there might be some reason for people to say this book was improper. The actions of very vain, heartless, pleasure-seeking people are very often improper (as are many of yours, my friend with the grave face and spotless reputation--but that is merely by the way); and what are those of a woman without faith--or love--or character? And I am inclined to think that there was a period in Mrs. Becky's life when she was seized, not by remorse, but by a kind of despair, and absolutely neglected her person and did not even care for her reputation.\n\nThis abattement and degradation did not take place all at once; it was brought about by degrees, after her calamity, and after many struggles to keep up--as a man who goes overboard hangs on to a spar whilst any hope is left, and then flings it away and goes down, when he finds that struggling is in vain.\n\nShe lingered about London whilst her husband was making preparations for his departure to his seat of government, and it is believed made more than one attempt to see her brother-in-law, Sir Pitt Crawley, and to work upon his feelings, which she had almost enlisted in her favour. As Sir Pitt and Mr. Wenham were walking down to the House of Commons, the latter spied Mrs. Rawdon in a black veil, and lurking near the palace of the legislature. She sneaked away when her eyes met those of Wenham, and indeed never succeeded in her designs upon the Baronet.\n\nProbably Lady Jane interposed. I have heard that she quite astonished her husband by the spirit which she exhibited in this quarrel, and her determination to disown Mrs. Becky. Of her own movement, she invited Rawdon to come and stop in Gaunt Street until his departure for Coventry Island, knowing that with him for a guard Mrs. Becky would not try to force her door; and she looked curiously at the superscriptions of all the letters which arrived for Sir Pitt, lest he and his sister-in-law should be corresponding. Not but that Rebecca could have written had she a mind, but she did not try to see or to write to Pitt at his own house, and after one or two attempts consented to his demand that the correspondence regarding her conjugal differences should be carried on by lawyers only.\n\nThe fact was that Pitt's mind had been poisoned against her. A short time after Lord Steyne's accident Wenham had been with the Baronet and given him such a biography of Mrs. Becky as had astonished the member for Queen's Crawley. He knew everything regarding her: who her father was; in what year her mother danced at the opera; what had been her previous history; and what her conduct during her married life--as I have no doubt that the greater part of the story was false and dictated by interested malevolence, it shall not be repeated here. But Becky was left with a sad sad reputation in the esteem of a country gentleman and relative who had been once rather partial to her.\n\nThe revenues of the Governor of Coventry Island are not large. A part of them were set aside by his Excellency for the payment of certain outstanding debts and liabilities, the charges incident on his high situation required considerable expense; finally, it was found that he could not spare to his wife more than three hundred pounds a year, which he proposed to pay to her on an undertaking that she would never trouble him. Otherwise, scandal, separation, Doctors' Commons would ensue. But it was Mr. Wenham's business, Lord Steyne's business, Rawdon's, everybody's--to get her out of the country, and hush up a most disagreeable affair.\n\nShe was probably so much occupied in arranging these affairs of business with her husband's lawyers that she forgot to take any step whatever about her son, the little Rawdon, and did not even once propose to go and see him. That young gentleman was consigned to the entire guardianship of his aunt and uncle, the former of whom had always possessed a great share of the child's affection. His mamma wrote him a neat letter from Boulogne, when she quitted England, in which she requested him to mind his book, and said she was going to take a Continental tour, during which she would have the pleasure of writing to him again. But she never did for a year afterwards, and not, indeed, until Sir Pitt's only boy, always sickly, died of hooping-cough and measles--then Rawdon's mamma wrote the most affectionate composition to her darling son, who was made heir of Queen's Crawley by this accident, and drawn more closely than ever to the kind lady, whose tender heart had already adopted him. Rawdon Crawley, then grown a tall, fine lad, blushed when he got the letter. \"Oh, Aunt Jane, you are my mother!\" he said; \"and not--and not that one.\" But he wrote back a kind and respectful letter to Mrs. Rebecca, then living at a boarding-house at Florence. But we are advancing matters.\n\nOur darling Becky's first flight was not very far. She perched upon the French coast at Boulogne, that refuge of so much exiled English innocence, and there lived in rather a genteel, widowed manner, with a femme de chambre and a couple of rooms, at an hotel. She dined at the table d'hote, where people thought her very pleasant, and where she entertained her neighbours by stories of her brother, Sir Pitt, and her great London acquaintance, talking that easy, fashionable slip-slop which has so much effect upon certain folks of small breeding. She passed with many of them for a person of importance; she gave little tea-parties in her private room and shared in the innocent amusements of the place in sea-bathing, and in jaunts in open carriages, in strolls on the sands, and in visits to the play. Mrs. Burjoice, the printer's lady, who was boarding with her family at the hotel for the summer, and to whom her Burjoice came of a Saturday and Sunday, voted her charming, until that little rogue of a Burjoice began to pay her too much attention. But there was nothing in the story, only that Becky was always affable, easy, and good-natured--and with men especially.\n\nNumbers of people were going abroad as usual at the end of the season, and Becky had plenty of opportunities of finding out by the behaviour of her acquaintances of the great London world the opinion of \"society\" as regarded her conduct. One day it was Lady Partlet and her daughters whom Becky confronted as she was walking modestly on Boulogne pier, the cliffs of Albion shining in the distance across the deep blue sea. Lady Partlet marshalled all her daughters round her with a sweep of her parasol and retreated from the pier, darting savage glances at poor little Becky who stood alone there.\n\nOn another day the packet came in. It had been blowing fresh, and it always suited Becky's humour to see the droll woe-begone faces of the people as they emerged from the boat. Lady Slingstone happened to be on board this day. Her ladyship had been exceedingly ill in her carriage, and was greatly exhausted and scarcely fit to walk up the plank from the ship to the pier. But all her energies rallied the instant she saw Becky smiling roguishly under a pink bonnet, and giving her a glance of scorn such as would have shrivelled up most women, she walked into the Custom House quite unsupported. Becky only laughed: but I don't think she liked it. She felt she was alone, quite alone, and the far-off shining cliffs of England were impassable to her.\n\nThe behaviour of the men had undergone too I don't know what change. Grinstone showed his teeth and laughed in her face with a familiarity that was not pleasant. Little Bob Suckling, who was cap in hand to her three months before, and would walk a mile in the rain to see for her carriage in the line at Gaunt House, was talking to Fitzoof of the Guards (Lord Heehaw's son) one day upon the jetty, as Becky took her walk there. Little Bobby nodded to her over his shoulder, without moving his hat, and continued his conversation with the heir of Heehaw. Tom Raikes tried to walk into her sitting-room at the inn with a cigar in his mouth, but she closed the door upon him, and would have locked it, only that his fingers were inside. She began to feel that she was very lonely indeed. \"If HE'D been here,\" she said, \"those cowards would never have dared to insult me.\" She thought about \"him\" with great sadness and perhaps longing--about his honest, stupid, constant kindness and fidelity; his never-ceasing obedience; his good humour; his bravery and courage. Very likely she cried, for she was particularly lively, and had put on a little extra rouge, when she came down to dinner.\n\nShe rouged regularly now; and--and her maid got Cognac for her besides that which was charged in the hotel bill.\n\nPerhaps the insults of the men were not, however, so intolerable to her as the sympathy of certain women. Mrs. Crackenbury and Mrs. Washington White passed through Boulogne on their way to Switzerland. The party were protected by Colonel Horner, young Beaumoris, and of course old Crackenbury, and Mrs. White's little girl. THEY did not avoid her. They giggled, cackled, tattled, condoled, consoled, and patronized her until they drove her almost wild with rage. To be patronized by THEM! she thought, as they went away simpering after kissing her. And she heard Beaumoris's laugh ringing on the stair and knew quite well how to interpret his hilarity.\n\nIt was after this visit that Becky, who had paid her weekly bills, Becky who had made herself agreeable to everybody in the house, who smiled at the landlady, called the waiters \"monsieur,\" and paid the chambermaids in politeness and apologies, what far more than compensated for a little niggardliness in point of money (of which Becky never was free), that Becky, we say, received a notice to quit from the landlord, who had been told by some one that she was quite an unfit person to have at his hotel, where English ladies would not sit down with her. And she was forced to fly into lodgings of which the dulness and solitude were most wearisome to her.\n\nStill she held up, in spite of these rebuffs, and tried to make a character for herself and conquer scandal. She went to church very regularly and sang louder than anybody there. She took up the cause of the widows of the shipwrecked fishermen, and gave work and drawings for the Quashyboo Mission; she subscribed to the Assembly and WOULDN'T waltz. In a word, she did everything that was respectable, and that is why we dwell upon this part of her career with more fondness than upon subsequent parts of her history, which are not so pleasant. She saw people avoiding her, and still laboriously smiled upon them; you never could suppose from her countenance what pangs of humiliation she might be enduring inwardly.\n\nHer history was after all a mystery. Parties were divided about her. Some people who took the trouble to busy themselves in the matter said that she was the criminal, whilst others vowed that she was as innocent as a lamb and that her odious husband was in fault. She won over a good many by bursting into tears about her boy and exhibiting the most frantic grief when his name was mentioned, or she saw anybody like him. She gained good Mrs. Alderney's heart in that way, who was rather the Queen of British Boulogne and gave the most dinners and balls of all the residents there, by weeping when Master Alderney came from Dr. Swishtail's academy to pass his holidays with his mother. \"He and her Rawdon were of the same age, and so like,\" Becky said in a voice choking with agony; whereas there was five years' difference between the boys' ages, and no more likeness between them than between my respected reader and his humble servant. Wenham, when he was going abroad, on his way to Kissingen to join Lord Steyne, enlightened Mrs. Alderney on this point and told her how he was much more able to describe little Rawdon than his mamma, who notoriously hated him and never saw him; how he was thirteen years old, while little Alderney was but nine, fair, while the other darling was dark--in a word, caused the lady in question to repent of her good humour.\n\nWhenever Becky made a little circle for herself with incredible toils and labour, somebody came and swept it down rudely, and she had all her work to begin over again. It was very hard; very hard; lonely and disheartening.\n\nThere was Mrs. Newbright, who took her up for some time, attracted by the sweetness of her singing at church and by her proper views upon serious subjects, concerning which in former days, at Queen's Crawley, Mrs. Becky had had a good deal of instruction. Well, she not only took tracts, but she read them. She worked flannel petticoats for the Quashyboos--cotton night-caps for the Cocoanut Indians--painted handscreens for the conversion of the Pope and the Jews--sat under Mr. Rowls on Wednesdays, Mr. Huggleton on Thursdays, attended two Sunday services at church, besides Mr. Bawler, the Darbyite, in the evening, and all in vain. Mrs. Newbright had occasion to correspond with the Countess of Southdown about the Warmingpan Fund for the Fiji Islanders (for the management of which admirable charity both these ladies formed part of a female committee), and having mentioned her \"sweet friend,\" Mrs. Rawdon Crawley, the Dowager Countess wrote back such a letter regarding Becky, with such particulars, hints, facts, falsehoods, and general comminations, that intimacy between Mrs. Newbright and Mrs. Crawley ceased forthwith, and all the serious world of Tours, where this misfortune took place, immediately parted company with the reprobate. Those who know the English Colonies abroad know that we carry with us us our pride, pills, prejudices, Harvey-sauces, cayenne-peppers, and other Lares, making a little Britain wherever we settle down.\n\nFrom one colony to another Becky fled uneasily. From Boulogne to Dieppe, from Dieppe to Caen, from Caen to Tours--trying with all her might to be respectable, and alas! always found out some day or other and pecked out of the cage by the real daws.\n\nMrs. Hook Eagles took her up at one of these places--a woman without a blemish in her character and a house in Portman Square. She was staying at the hotel at Dieppe, whither Becky fled, and they made each other's acquaintance first at sea, where they were swimming together, and subsequently at the table d'hote of the hotel. Mrs. Eagles had heard--who indeed had not?--some of the scandal of the Steyne affair; but after a conversation with Becky, she pronounced that Mrs. Crawley was an angel, her husband a ruffian, Lord Steyne an unprincipled wretch, as everybody knew, and the whole case against Mrs. Crawley an infamous and wicked conspiracy of that rascal Wenham. \"If you were a man of any spirit, Mr. Eagles, you would box the wretch's ears the next time you see him at the Club,\" she said to her husband. But Eagles was only a quiet old gentleman, husband to Mrs. Eagles, with a taste for geology, and not tall enough to reach anybody's ears.\n\nThe Eagles then patronized Mrs. Rawdon, took her to live with her at her own house at Paris, quarrelled with the ambassador's wife because she would not receive her protegee, and did all that lay in woman's power to keep Becky straight in the paths of virtue and good repute.\n\nBecky was very respectable and orderly at first, but the life of humdrum virtue grew utterly tedious to her before long. It was the same routine every day, the same dulness and comfort, the same drive over the same stupid Bois de Boulogne, the same company of an evening, the same Blair's Sermon of a Sunday night--the same opera always being acted over and over again; Becky was dying of weariness, when, luckily for her, young Mr. Eagles came from Cambridge, and his mother, seeing the impression which her little friend made upon him, straightway gave Becky warning.\n\nThen she tried keeping house with a female friend; then the double menage began to quarrel and get into debt. Then she determined upon a boarding-house existence and lived for some time at that famous mansion kept by Madame de Saint Amour, in the Rue Royale, at Paris, where she began exercising her graces and fascinations upon the shabby dandies and fly-blown beauties who frequented her landlady's salons. Becky loved society and, indeed, could no more exist without it than an opium-eater without his dram, and she was happy enough at the period of her boarding-house life. \"The women here are as amusing as those in May Fair,\" she told an old London friend who met her, \"only, their dresses are not quite so fresh. The men wear cleaned gloves, and are sad rogues, certainly, but they are not worse than Jack This and Tom That. The mistress of the house is a little vulgar, but I don't think she is so vulgar as Lady ------\" and here she named the name of a great leader of fashion that I would die rather than reveal. In fact, when you saw Madame de Saint Amour's rooms lighted up of a night, men with plaques and cordons at the ecarte tables, and the women at a little distance, you might fancy yourself for a while in good society, and that Madame was a real Countess. Many people did so fancy, and Becky was for a while one of the most dashing ladies of the Countess's salons.\n\nBut it is probable that her old creditors of 1815 found her out and caused her to leave Paris, for the poor little woman was forced to fly from the city rather suddenly, and went thence to Brussels.\n\nHow well she remembered the place! She grinned as she looked up at the little entresol which she had occupied, and thought of the Bareacres family, bawling for horses and flight, as their carriage stood in the porte-cochere of the hotel. She went to Waterloo and to Laeken, where George Osborne's monument much struck her. She made a little sketch of it. \"That poor Cupid!\" she said; \"how dreadfully he was in love with me, and what a fool he was! I wonder whether little Emmy is alive. It was a good little creature; and that fat brother of hers. I have his funny fat picture still among my papers. They were kind simple people.\"\n\nAt Brussels Becky arrived, recommended by Madame de Saint Amour to her friend, Madame la Comtesse de Borodino, widow of Napoleon's General, the famous Count de Borodino, who was left with no resource by the deceased hero but that of a table d'hote and an ecarte table. Second-rate dandies and roues, widow-ladies who always have a lawsuit, and very simple English folks, who fancy they see \"Continental society\" at these houses, put down their money, or ate their meals, at Madame de Borodino's tables. The gallant young fellows treated the company round to champagne at the table d'hote, rode out with the women, or hired horses on country excursions, clubbed money to take boxes at the play or the opera, betted over the fair shoulders of the ladies at the ecarte tables, and wrote home to their parents in Devonshire about their felicitous introduction to foreign society.\n\nHere, as at Paris, Becky was a boarding-house queen, and ruled in select pensions. She never refused the champagne, or the bouquets, or the drives into the country, or the private boxes; but what she preferred was the ecarte at night,--and she played audaciously. First she played only for a little, then for five-franc pieces, then for Napoleons, then for notes: then she would not be able to pay her month's pension: then she borrowed from the young gentlemen: then she got into cash again and bullied Madame de Borodino, whom she had coaxed and wheedled before: then she was playing for ten sous at a time, and in a dire state of poverty: then her quarter's allowance would come in, and she would pay off Madame de Borodino's score and would once more take the cards against Monsieur de Rossignol, or the Chevalier de Raff.\n\nWhen Becky left Brussels, the sad truth is that she owed three months' pension to Madame de Borodino, of which fact, and of the gambling, and of the drinking, and of the going down on her knees to the Reverend Mr. Muff, Ministre Anglican, and borrowing money of him, and of her coaxing and flirting with Milor Noodle, son of Sir Noodle, pupil of the Rev. Mr. Muff, whom she used to take into her private room, and of whom she won large sums at ecarte--of which fact, I say, and of a hundred of her other knaveries, the Countess de Borodino informs every English person who stops at her establishment, and announces that Madame Rawdon was no better than a vipere.\n\nSo our little wanderer went about setting up her tent in various cities of Europe, as restless as Ulysses or Bampfylde Moore Carew. Her taste for disrespectability grew more and more remarkable. She became a perfect Bohemian ere long, herding with people whom it would make your hair stand on end to meet.\n\nThere is no town of any mark in Europe but it has its little colony of English raffs--men whose names Mr. Hemp the officer reads out periodically at the Sheriffs' Court--young gentlemen of very good family often, only that the latter disowns them; frequenters of billiard-rooms and estaminets, patrons of foreign races and gaming-tables. They people the debtors' prisons--they drink and swagger--they fight and brawl--they run away without paying--they have duels with French and German officers--they cheat Mr. Spooney at ecarte--they get the money and drive off to Baden in magnificent britzkas--they try their infallible martingale and lurk about the tables with empty pockets, shabby bullies, penniless bucks, until they can swindle a Jew banker with a sham bill of exchange, or find another Mr. Spooney to rob. The alternations of splendour and misery which these people undergo are very queer to view. Their life must be one of great excitement. Becky--must it be owned?--took to this life, and took to it not unkindly. She went about from town to town among these Bohemians. The lucky Mrs. Rawdon was known at every play-table in Germany. She and Madame de Cruchecassee kept house at Florence together. It is said she was ordered out of Munich, and my friend Mr. Frederick Pigeon avers that it was at her house at Lausanne that he was hocussed at supper and lost eight hundred pounds to Major Loder and the Honourable Mr. Deuceace. We are bound, you see, to give some account of Becky's biography, but of this part, the less, perhaps, that is said the better.\n\nThey say that, when Mrs. Crawley was particularly down on her luck, she gave concerts and lessons in music here and there. There was a Madame de Raudon, who certainly had a matinee musicale at Wildbad, accompanied by Herr Spoff, premier pianist to the Hospodar of Wallachia, and my little friend Mr. Eaves, who knew everybody and had travelled everywhere, always used to declare that he was at Strasburg in the year 1830, when a certain Madame Rebecque made her appearance in the opera of the Dame Blanche, giving occasion to a furious row in the theatre there. She was hissed off the stage by the audience, partly from her own incompetency, but chiefly from the ill-advised sympathy of some persons in the parquet, (where the officers of the garrison had their admissions); and Eaves was certain that the unfortunate debutante in question was no other than Mrs. Rawdon Crawley.\n\nShe was, in fact, no better than a vagabond upon this earth. When she got her money she gambled; when she had gambled it she was put to shifts to live; who knows how or by what means she succeeded? It is said that she was once seen at St. Petersburg, but was summarily dismissed from that capital by the police, so that there cannot be any possibility of truth in the report that she was a Russian spy at Toplitz and Vienna afterwards. I have even been informed that at Paris she discovered a relation of her own, no less a person than her maternal grandmother, who was not by any means a Montmorenci, but a hideous old box-opener at a theatre on the Boulevards. The meeting between them, of which other persons, as it is hinted elsewhere, seem to have been acquainted, must have been a very affecting interview. The present historian can give no certain details regarding the event.\n\nIt happened at Rome once that Mrs. de Rawdon's half-year's salary had just been paid into the principal banker's there, and, as everybody who had a balance of above five hundred scudi was invited to the balls which this prince of merchants gave during the winter, Becky had the honour of a card, and appeared at one of the Prince and Princess Polonia's splendid evening entertainments. The Princess was of the family of Pompili, lineally descended from the second king of Rome, and Egeria of the house of Olympus, while the Prince's grandfather, Alessandro Polonia, sold wash-balls, essences, tobacco, and pocket-handkerchiefs, ran errands for gentlemen, and lent money in a small way. All the great company in Rome thronged to his saloons--Princes, Dukes, Ambassadors, artists, fiddlers, monsignori, young bears with their leaders--every rank and condition of man. His halls blazed with light and magnificence; were resplendent with gilt frames (containing pictures), and dubious antiques; and the enormous gilt crown and arms of the princely owner, a gold mushroom on a crimson field (the colour of the pocket-handkerchiefs which he sold), and the silver fountain of the Pompili family shone all over the roof, doors, and panels of the house, and over the grand velvet baldaquins prepared to receive Popes and Emperors.\n\nSo Becky, who had arrived in the diligence from Florence, and was lodged at an inn in a very modest way, got a card for Prince Polonia's entertainment, and her maid dressed her with unusual care, and she went to this fine ball leaning on the arm of Major Loder, with whom she happened to be travelling at the time--(the same man who shot Prince Ravoli at Naples the next year, and was caned by Sir John Buckskin for carrying four kings in his hat besides those which he used in playing at ecarte )--and this pair went into the rooms together, and Becky saw a number of old faces which she remembered in happier days, when she was not innocent, but not found out. Major Loder knew a great number of foreigners, keen-looking whiskered men with dirty striped ribbons in their buttonholes, and a very small display of linen; but his own countrymen, it might be remarked, eschewed the Major. Becky, too, knew some ladies here and there--French widows, dubious Italian countesses, whose husbands had treated them ill--faugh--what shall we say, we who have moved among some of the finest company of Vanity Fair, of this refuse and sediment of rascals? If we play, let it be with clean cards, and not with this dirty pack. But every man who has formed one of the innumerable army of travellers has seen these marauding irregulars hanging on, like Nym and Pistol, to the main force, wearing the king's colours and boasting of his commission, but pillaging for themselves, and occasionally gibbeted by the roadside.\n\nWell, she was hanging on the arm of Major Loder, and they went through the rooms together, and drank a great quantity of champagne at the buffet, where the people, and especially the Major's irregular corps, struggled furiously for refreshments, of which when the pair had had enough, they pushed on until they reached the Duchess's own pink velvet saloon, at the end of the suite of apartments (where the statue of the Venus is, and the great Venice looking-glasses, framed in silver), and where the princely family were entertaining their most distinguished guests at a round table at supper. It was just such a little select banquet as that of which Becky recollected that she had partaken at Lord Steyne's--and there he sat at Polonia's table, and she saw him. The scar cut by the diamond on his white, bald, shining forehead made a burning red mark; his red whiskers were dyed of a purple hue, which made his pale face look still paler. He wore his collar and orders, his blue ribbon and garter. He was a greater Prince than any there, though there was a reigning Duke and a Royal Highness, with their princesses, and near his Lordship was seated the beautiful Countess of Belladonna, nee de Glandier, whose husband (the Count Paolo della Belladonna), so well known for his brilliant entomological collections, had been long absent on a mission to the Emperor of Morocco.\n\nWhen Becky beheld that familiar and illustrious face, how vulgar all of a sudden did Major Loder appear to her, and how that odious Captain Rook did smell of tobacco! In one instant she reassumed her fine-ladyship and tried to look and feel as if she were in May Fair once more. \"That woman looks stupid and ill-humoured,\" she thought; \"I am sure she can't amuse him. No, he must be bored by her--he never was by me.\" A hundred such touching hopes, fears, and memories palpitated in her little heart, as she looked with her brightest eyes (the rouge which she wore up to her eyelids made them twinkle) towards the great nobleman. Of a Star and Garter night Lord Steyne used also to put on his grandest manner and to look and speak like a great prince, as he was. Becky admired him smiling sumptuously, easy, lofty, and stately. Ah, bon Dieu, what a pleasant companion he was, what a brilliant wit, what a rich fund of talk, what a grand manner!--and she had exchanged this for Major Loder, reeking of cigars and brandy-and-water, and Captain Rook with his horsejockey jokes and prize-ring slang, and their like. \"I wonder whether he will know me,\" she thought. Lord Steyne was talking and laughing with a great and illustrious lady at his side, when he looked up and saw Becky.\n\nShe was all over in a flutter as their eyes met, and she put on the very best smile she could muster, and dropped him a little, timid, imploring curtsey. He stared aghast at her for a minute, as Macbeth might on beholding Banquo's sudden appearance at his ball-supper, and remained looking at her with open mouth, when that horrid Major Loder pulled her away.\n\n\"Come away into the supper-room, Mrs. R.,\" was that gentleman's remark: \"seeing these nobs grubbing away has made me peckish too. Let's go and try the old governor's champagne.\" Becky thought the Major had had a great deal too much already.\n\nThe day after she went to walk on the Pincian Hill--the Hyde Park of the Roman idlers--possibly in hopes to have another sight of Lord Steyne. But she met another acquaintance there: it was Mr. Fiche, his lordship's confidential man, who came up nodding to her rather familiarly and putting a finger to his hat. \"I knew that Madame was here,\" he said; \"I followed her from her hotel. I have some advice to give Madame.\"\n\n\"From the Marquis of Steyne?\" Becky asked, resuming as much of her dignity as she could muster, and not a little agitated by hope and expectation.\n\n\"No,\" said the valet; \"it is from me. Rome is very unwholesome.\"\n\n\"Not at this season, Monsieur Fiche--not till after Easter.\"\n\n\"I tell Madame it is unwholesome now. There is always malaria for some people. That cursed marsh wind kills many at all seasons. Look, Madame Crawley, you were always bon enfant, and I have an interest in you, parole d'honneur. Be warned. Go away from Rome, I tell you--or you will be ill and die.\"\n\nBecky laughed, though in rage and fury. \"What! assassinate poor little me?\" she said. \"How romantic! Does my lord carry bravos for couriers, and stilettos in the fourgons? Bah! I will stay, if but to plague him. I have those who will defend me whilst I am here.\"\n\nIt was Monsieur Fiche's turn to laugh now. \"Defend you,\" he said, \"and who? The Major, the Captain, any one of those gambling men whom Madame sees would take her life for a hundred louis. We know things about Major Loder (he is no more a Major than I am my Lord the Marquis) which would send him to the galleys or worse. We know everything and have friends everywhere. We know whom you saw at Paris, and what relations you found there. Yes, Madame may stare, but we do. How was it that no minister on the Continent would receive Madame? She has offended somebody: who never forgives--whose rage redoubled when he saw you. He was like a madman last night when he came home. Madame de Belladonna made him a scene about you and fired off in one of her furies.\"\n\n\"Oh, it was Madame de Belladonna, was it?\" Becky said, relieved a little, for the information she had just got had scared her.\n\n\"No--she does not matter--she is always jealous. I tell you it was Monseigneur. You did wrong to show yourself to him. And if you stay here you will repent it. Mark my words. Go. Here is my lord's carriage\"--and seizing Becky's arm, he rushed down an alley of the garden as Lord Steyne's barouche, blazing with heraldic devices, came whirling along the avenue, borne by the almost priceless horses, and bearing Madame de Belladonna lolling on the cushions, dark, sulky, and blooming, a King Charles in her lap, a white parasol swaying over her head, and old Steyne stretched at her side with a livid face and ghastly eyes. Hate, or anger, or desire caused them to brighten now and then still, but ordinarily, they gave no light, and seemed tired of looking out on a world of which almost all the pleasure and all the best beauty had palled upon the worn-out wicked old man.\n\n\"Monseigneur has never recovered the shock of that night, never,\" Monsieur Fiche whispered to Mrs. Crawley as the carriage flashed by, and she peeped out at it from behind the shrubs that hid her. \"That was a consolation at any rate,\" Becky thought.\n\nWhether my lord really had murderous intentions towards Mrs. Becky as Monsieur Fiche said (since Monseigneur's death he has returned to his native country, where he lives much respected, and has purchased from his Prince the title of Baron Ficci), and the factotum objected to have to do with assassination; or whether he simply had a commission to frighten Mrs. Crawley out of a city where his Lordship proposed to pass the winter, and the sight of her would be eminently disagreeable to the great nobleman, is a point which has never been ascertained: but the threat had its effect upon the little woman, and she sought no more to intrude herself upon the presence of her old patron.\n\nEverybody knows the melancholy end of that nobleman, which befell at Naples two months after the French Revolution of 1830; when the Most Honourable George Gustavus, Marquis of Steyne, Earl of Gaunt and of Gaunt Castle, in the Peerage of Ireland, Viscount Hellborough, Baron Pitchley and Grillsby, a Knight of the Most Noble Order of the Garter, of the Golden Fleece of Spain, of the Russian Order of Saint Nicholas of the First Class, of the Turkish Order of the Crescent, First Lord of the Powder Closet and Groom of the Back Stairs, Colonel of the Gaunt or Regent's Own Regiment of Militia, a Trustee of the British Museum, an Elder Brother of the Trinity House, a Governor of the White Friars, and D.C.L.--died after a series of fits brought on, as the papers said, by the shock occasioned to his lordship's sensibilities by the downfall of the ancient French monarchy.\n\nAn eloquent catalogue appeared in a weekly print, describing his virtues, his magnificence, his talents, and his good actions. His sensibility, his attachment to the illustrious House of Bourbon, with which he claimed an alliance, were such that he could not survive the misfortunes of his august kinsmen. His body was buried at Naples, and his heart--that heart which always beat with every generous and noble emotion was brought back to Castle Gaunt in a silver urn. \"In him,\" Mr. Wagg said, \"the poor and the Fine Arts have lost a beneficent patron, society one of its most brilliant ornaments, and England one of her loftiest patriots and statesmen,\" &c., &c.\n\nHis will was a good deal disputed, and an attempt was made to force from Madame de Belladonna the celebrated jewel called the \"Jew's-eye\" diamond, which his lordship always wore on his forefinger, and which it was said that she removed from it after his lamented demise. But his confidential friend and attendant, Monsieur Fiche proved that the ring had been presented to the said Madame de Belladonna two days before the Marquis's death, as were the bank-notes, jewels, Neapolitan and French bonds, &c., found in his lordship's secretaire and claimed by his heirs from that injured woman.\n\nFull of Business and Pleasure\nThe day after the meeting at the play-table, Jos had himself arrayed with unusual care and splendour, and without thinking it necessary to say a word to any member of his family regarding the occurrences of the previous night, or asking for their company in his walk, he sallied forth at an early hour, and was presently seen making inquiries at the door of the Elephant Hotel. In consequence of the fetes the house was full of company, the tables in the street were already surrounded by persons smoking and drinking the national small-beer, the public rooms were in a cloud of smoke, and Mr. Jos having, in his pompous way, and with his clumsy German, made inquiries for the person of whom he was in search, was directed to the very top of the house, above the first-floor rooms where some travelling pedlars had lived, and were exhibiting their jewellery and brocades; above the second-floor apartments occupied by the etat major of the gambling firm; above the third-floor rooms, tenanted by the band of renowned Bohemian vaulters and tumblers; and so on to the little cabins of the roof, where, among students, bagmen, small tradesmen, and country-folks come in for the festival, Becky had found a little nest--as dirty a little refuge as ever beauty lay hid in.\n\nBecky liked the life. She was at home with everybody in the place, pedlars, punters, tumblers, students and all. She was of a wild, roving nature, inherited from father and mother, who were both Bohemians, by taste and circumstance; if a lord was not by, she would talk to his courier with the greatest pleasure; the din, the stir, the drink, the smoke, the tattle of the Hebrew pedlars, the solemn, braggart ways of the poor tumblers, the sournois talk of the gambling-table officials, the songs and swagger of the students, and the general buzz and hum of the place had pleased and tickled the little woman, even when her luck was down and she had not wherewithal to pay her bill. How pleasant was all the bustle to her now that her purse was full of the money which little Georgy had won for her the night before!\n\nAs Jos came creaking and puffing up the final stairs, and was speechless when he got to the landing, and began to wipe his face and then to look for No. 92, the room where he was directed to seek for the person he wanted, the door of the opposite chamber, No. 90, was open, and a student, in jack-boots and a dirty schlafrock, was lying on the bed smoking a long pipe; whilst another student in long yellow hair and a braided coat, exceeding smart and dirty too, was actually on his knees at No. 92, bawling through the keyhole supplications to the person within.\n\n\"Go away,\" said a well-known voice, which made Jos thrill, \"I expect somebody; I expect my grandpapa. He mustn't see you there.\"\n\n\"Angel Englanderinn!\" bellowed the kneeling student with the whity-brown ringlets and the large finger-ring, \"do take compassion upon us. Make an appointment. Dine with me and Fritz at the inn in the park. We will have roast pheasants and porter, plum-pudding and French wine. We shall die if you don't.\"\n\n\"That we will,\" said the young nobleman on the bed; and this colloquy Jos overheard, though he did not comprehend it, for the reason that he had never studied the language in which it was carried on.\n\n\"Newmero kattervang dooze, si vous plait,\" Jos said in his grandest manner, when he was able to speak.\n\n\"Quater fang tooce!\" said the student, starting up, and he bounced into his own room, where he locked the door, and where Jos heard him laughing with his comrade on the bed.\n\nThe gentleman from Bengal was standing, disconcerted by this incident, when the door of the 92 opened of itself and Becky's little head peeped out full of archness and mischief. She lighted on Jos. \"It's you,\" she said, coming out. \"How I have been waiting for you! Stop! not yet--in one minute you shall come in.\" In that instant she put a rouge-pot, a brandy bottle, and a plate of broken meat into the bed, gave one smooth to her hair, and finally let in her visitor.\n\nShe had, by way of morning robe, a pink domino, a trifle faded and soiled, and marked here and there with pomaturn; but her arms shone out from the loose sleeves of the dress very white and fair, and it was tied round her little waist so as not ill to set off the trim little figure of the wearer. She led Jos by the hand into her garret. \"Come in,\" she said. \"Come and talk to me. Sit yonder on the chair\"; and she gave the civilian's hand a little squeeze and laughingly placed him upon it. As for herself, she placed herself on the bed--not on the bottle and plate, you may be sure--on which Jos might have reposed, had he chosen that seat; and so there she sat and talked with her old admirer. \"How little years have changed you,\" she said with a look of tender interest. \"I should have known you anywhere. What a comfort it is amongst strangers to see once more the frank honest face of an old friend!\"\n\nThe frank honest face, to tell the truth, at this moment bore any expression but one of openness and honesty: it was, on the contrary, much perturbed and puzzled in look. Jos was surveying the queer little apartment in which he found his old flame. One of her gowns hung over the bed, another depending from a hook of the door; her bonnet obscured half the looking-glass, on which, too, lay the prettiest little pair of bronze boots; a French novel was on the table by the bedside, with a candle, not of wax. Becky thought of popping that into the bed too, but she only put in the little paper night-cap with which she had put the candle out on going to sleep.\n\n\"I should have known you anywhere,\" she continued; \"a woman never forgets some things. And you were the first man I ever--I ever saw.\"\n\n\"Was I really?\" said Jos. \"God bless my soul, you--you don't say so.\"\n\n\"When I came with your sister from Chiswick, I was scarcely more than a child,\" Becky said. \"How is that, dear love? Oh, her husband was a sad wicked man, and of course it was of me that the poor dear was jealous. As if I cared about him, heigho! when there was somebody--but no--don't let us talk of old times\"; and she passed her handkerchief with the tattered lace across her eyelids.\n\n\"Is not this a strange place,\" she continued, \"for a woman, who has lived in a very different world too, to be found in? I have had so many griefs and wrongs, Joseph Sedley; I have been made to suffer so cruelly that I am almost made mad sometimes. I can't stay still in any place, but wander about always restless and unhappy. All my friends have been false to me--all. There is no such thing as an honest man in the world. I was the truest wife that ever lived, though I married my husband out of pique, because somebody else--but never mind that. I was true, and he trampled upon me and deserted me. I was the fondest mother. I had but one child, one darling, one hope, one joy, which I held to my heart with a mother's affection, which was my life, my prayer, my--my blessing; and they--they tore it from me--tore it from me\"; and she put her hand to her heart with a passionate gesture of despair, burying her face for a moment on the bed.\n\nThe brandy-bottle inside clinked up against the plate which held the cold sausage. Both were moved, no doubt, by the exhibition of so much grief. Max and Fritz were at the door, listening with wonder to Mrs. Becky's sobs and cries. Jos, too, was a good deal frightened and affected at seeing his old flame in this condition. And she began, forthwith, to tell her story--a tale so neat, simple, and artless that it was quite evident from hearing her that if ever there was a white-robed angel escaped from heaven to be subject to the infernal machinations and villainy of fiends here below, that spotless being--that miserable unsullied martyr, was present on the bed before Jos--on the bed, sitting on the brandy-bottle.\n\nThey had a very long, amicable, and confidential talk there, in the course of which Jos Sedley was somehow made aware (but in a manner that did not in the least scare or offend him) that Becky's heart had first learned to beat at his enchanting presence; that George Osborne had certainly paid an unjustifiable court to HER, which might account for Amelia's jealousy and their little rupture; but that Becky never gave the least encouragement to the unfortunate officer, and that she had never ceased to think about Jos from the very first day she had seen him, though, of course, her duties as a married woman were paramount--duties which she had always preserved, and would, to her dying day, or until the proverbially bad climate in which Colonel Crawley was living should release her from a yoke which his cruelty had rendered odious to her.\n\nJos went away, convinced that she was the most virtuous, as she was one of the most fascinating of women, and revolving in his mind all sorts of benevolent schemes for her welfare. Her persecutions ought to be ended: she ought to return to the society of which she was an ornament. He would see what ought to be done. She must quit that place and take a quiet lodging. Amelia must come and see her and befriend her. He would go and settle about it, and consult with the Major. She wept tears of heart-felt gratitude as she parted from him, and pressed his hand as the gallant stout gentleman stooped down to kiss hers.\n\nSo Becky bowed Jos out of her little garret with as much grace as if it was a palace of which she did the honours; and that heavy gentleman having disappeared down the stairs, Max and Fritz came out of their hole, pipe in mouth, and she amused herself by mimicking Jos to them as she munched her cold bread and sausage and took draughts of her favourite brandy-and-water.\n\nJos walked over to Dobbin's lodgings with great solemnity and there imparted to him the affecting history with which he had just been made acquainted, without, however, mentioning the play business of the night before. And the two gentlemen were laying their heads together and consulting as to the best means of being useful to Mrs. Becky, while she was finishing her interrupted dejeuner a la fourchette.\n\nHow was it that she had come to that little town? How was it that she had no friends and was wandering about alone? Little boys at school are taught in their earliest Latin book that the path of Avernus is very easy of descent. Let us skip over the interval in the history of her downward progress. She was not worse now than she had been in the days of her prosperity--only a little down on her luck.\n\nAs for Mrs. Amelia, she was a woman of such a soft and foolish disposition that when she heard of anybody unhappy, her heart straightway melted towards the sufferer; and as she had never thought or done anything mortally guilty herself, she had not that abhorrence for wickedness which distinguishes moralists much more knowing. If she spoiled everybody who came near her with kindness and compliments--if she begged pardon of all her servants for troubling them to answer the bell--if she apologized to a shopboy who showed her a piece of silk, or made a curtsey to a street-sweeper with a complimentary remark upon the elegant state of his crossing--and she was almost capable of every one of these follies--the notion that an old acquaintance was miserable was sure to soften her heart; nor would she hear of anybody's being deservedly unhappy. A world under such legislation as hers would not be a very orderly place of abode; but there are not many women, at least not of the rulers, who are of her sort. This lady, I believe, would have abolished all gaols, punishments, handcuffs, whippings, poverty, sickness, hunger, in the world, and was such a mean-spirited creature that--we are obliged to confess it--she could even forget a mortal injury.\n\nWhen the Major heard from Jos of the sentimental adventure which had just befallen the latter, he was not, it must be owned, nearly as much interested as the gentleman from Bengal. On the contrary, his excitement was quite the reverse from a pleasurable one; he made use of a brief but improper expression regarding a poor woman in distress, saying, in fact, \"The little minx, has she come to light again?\" He never had had the slightest liking for her, but had heartily mistrusted her from the very first moment when her green eyes had looked at, and turned away from, his own.\n\n\"That little devil brings mischief wherever she goes,\" the Major said disrespectfully. \"Who knows what sort of life she has been leading? And what business has she here abroad and alone? Don't tell me about persecutors and enemies; an honest woman always has friends and never is separated from her family. Why has she left her husband? He may have been disreputable and wicked, as you say. He always was. I remember the confounded blackleg and the way in which he used to cheat and hoodwink poor George. Wasn't there a scandal about their separation? I think I heard something,\" cried out Major Dobbin, who did not care much about gossip, and whom Jos tried in vain to convince that Mrs. Becky was in all respects a most injured and virtuous female.\n\n\"Well, well; let's ask Mrs. George,\" said that arch-diplomatist of a Major. \"Only let us go and consult her. I suppose you will allow that she is a good judge at any rate, and knows what is right in such matters.\"\n\n\"Hm! Emmy is very well,\" said Jos, who did not happen to be in love with his sister.\n\n\"Very well? By Gad, sir, she's the finest lady I ever met in my life,\" bounced out the Major. \"I say at once, let us go and ask her if this woman ought to be visited or not--I will be content with her verdict.\" Now this odious, artful rogue of a Major was thinking in his own mind that he was sure of his case. Emmy, he remembered, was at one time cruelly and deservedly jealous of Rebecca, never mentioned her name but with a shrinking and terror--a jealous woman never forgives, thought Dobbin: and so the pair went across the street to Mrs. George's house, where she was contentedly warbling at a music lesson with Madame Strumpff.\n\nWhen that lady took her leave, Jos opened the business with his usual pomp of words. \"Amelia, my dear,\" said he, \"I have just had the most extraordinary--yes--God bless my soul! the most extraordinary adventure--an old friend--yes, a most interesting old friend of yours, and I may say in old times, has just arrived here, and I should like you to see her.\"\n\n\"Her!\" said Amelia, \"who is it? Major Dobbin, if you please not to break my scissors.\" The Major was twirling them round by the little chain from which they sometimes hung to their lady's waist, and was thereby endangering his own eye.\n\n\"It is a woman whom I dislike very much,\" said the Major, doggedly, \"and whom you have no cause to love.\"\n\n\"It is Rebecca, I'm sure it is Rebecca,\" Amelia said, blushing and being very much agitated.\n\n\"You are right; you always are,\" Dobbin answered. Brussels, Waterloo, old, old times, griefs, pangs, remembrances, rushed back into Amelia's gentle heart and caused a cruel agitation there.\n\n\"Don't let me see her,\" Emmy continued. \"I couldn't see her.\"\n\n\"I told you so,\" Dobbin said to Jos.\n\n\"She is very unhappy, and--and that sort of thing,\" Jos urged. \"She is very poor and unprotected, and has been ill--exceedingly ill--and that scoundrel of a husband has deserted her.\"\n\n\"Ah!\" said Amelia.\n\n\"She hasn't a friend in the world,\" Jos went on, not undexterously, \"and she said she thought she might trust in you. She's so miserable, Emmy. She has been almost mad with grief. Her story quite affected me--'pon my word and honour, it did--never was such a cruel persecution borne so angelically, I may say. Her family has been most cruel to her.\"\n\n\"Poor creature!\" Amelia said.\n\n\"And if she can get no friend, she says she thinks she'll die,\" Jos proceeded in a low tremulous voice. \"God bless my soul! do you know that she tried to kill herself? She carries laudanum with her--I saw the bottle in her room--such a miserable little room--at a third-rate house, the Elephant, up in the roof at the top of all. I went there.\"\n\nThis did not seem to affect Emmy. She even smiled a little. Perhaps she figured Jos to herself panting up the stair.\n\n\"She's beside herself with grief,\" he resumed. \"The agonies that woman has endured are quite frightful to hear of. She had a little boy, of the same age as Georgy.\"\n\n\"Yes, yes, I think I remember,\" Emmy remarked. \"Well?\"\n\n\"The most beautiful child ever seen,\" Jos said, who was very fat, and easily moved, and had been touched by the story Becky told; \"a perfect angel, who adored his mother. The ruffians tore him shrieking out of her arms, and have never allowed him to see her.\"\n\n\"Dear Joseph,\" Emmy cried out, starting up at once, \"let us go and see her this minute.\" And she ran into her adjoining bedchamber, tied on her bonnet in a flutter, came out with her shawl on her arm, and ordered Dobbin to follow.\n\nHe went and put her shawl--it was a white cashmere, consigned to her by the Major himself from India--over her shoulders. He saw there was nothing for it but to obey, and she put her hand into his arm, and they went away.\n\n\"It is number 92, up four pair of stairs,\" Jos said, perhaps not very willing to ascend the steps again; but he placed himself in the window of his drawing-room, which commands the place on which the Elephant stands, and saw the pair marching through the market.\n\nIt was as well that Becky saw them too from her garret, for she and the two students were chattering and laughing there; they had been joking about the appearance of Becky's grandpapa--whose arrival and departure they had witnessed--but she had time to dismiss them, and have her little room clear before the landlord of the Elephant, who knew that Mrs. Osborne was a great favourite at the Serene Court, and respected her accordingly, led the way up the stairs to the roof story, encouraging Miladi and the Herr Major as they achieved the ascent.\n\n\"Gracious lady, gracious lady!\" said the landlord, knocking at Becky's door; he had called her Madame the day before, and was by no means courteous to her.\n\n\"Who is it?\" Becky said, putting out her head, and she gave a little scream. There stood Emmy in a tremble, and Dobbin, the tall Major, with his cane.\n\nHe stood still watching, and very much interested at the scene; but Emmy sprang forward with open arms towards Rebecca, and forgave her at that moment, and embraced her and kissed her with all her heart. Ah, poor wretch, when was your lip pressed before by such pure kisses?\n\nAmantium Irae\n\nFrankness and kindness like Amelia's were likely to touch even such a hardened little reprobate as Becky. She returned Emmy's caresses and kind speeches with something very like gratitude, and an emotion which, if it was not lasting, for a moment was almost genuine. That was a lucky stroke of hers about the child \"torn from her arms shrieking.\" It was by that harrowing misfortune that Becky had won her friend back, and it was one of the very first points, we may be certain, upon which our poor simple little Emmy began to talk to her new-found acquaintance.\n\n\"And so they took your darling child from you?\" our simpleton cried out. \"Oh, Rebecca, my poor dear suffering friend, I know what it is to lose a boy, and to feel for those who have lost one. But please Heaven yours will be restored to you, as a merciful merciful Providence has brought me back mine.\"\n\n\"The child, my child? Oh, yes, my agonies were frightful,\" Becky owned, not perhaps without a twinge of conscience. It jarred upon her to be obliged to commence instantly to tell lies in reply to so much confidence and simplicity. But that is the misfortune of beginning with this kind of forgery. When one fib becomes due as it were, you must forge another to take up the old acceptance; and so the stock of your lies in circulation inevitably multiplies, and the danger of detection increases every day.\n\n\"My agonies,\" Becky continued, \"were terrible (I hope she won't sit down on the bottle) when they took him away from me; I thought I should die; but I fortunately had a brain fever, during which my doctor gave me up, and--and I recovered, and--and here I am, poor and friendless.\"\n\n\"How old is he?\" Emmy asked.\n\n\"Eleven,\" said Becky.\n\n\"Eleven!\" cried the other. \"Why, he was born the same year with Georgy, who is--\"\n\n\"I know, I know,\" Becky cried out, who had in fact quite forgotten all about little Rawdon's age. \"Grief has made me forget so many things, dearest Amelia. I am very much changed: half-wild sometimes. He was eleven when they took him away from me. Bless his sweet face; I have never seen it again.\"\n\n\"Was he fair or dark?\" went on that absurd little Emmy. \"Show me his hair.\"\n\nBecky almost laughed at her simplicity. \"Not to-day, love--some other time, when my trunks arrive from Leipzig, whence I came to this place--and a little drawing of him, which I made in happy days.\"\n\n\"Poor Becky, poor Becky!\" said Emmy. \"How thankful, how thankful I ought to be\"; (though I doubt whether that practice of piety inculcated upon us by our womankind in early youth, namely, to be thankful because we are better off than somebody else, be a very rational religious exercise) and then she began to think, as usual, how her son was the handsomest, the best, and the cleverest boy in the whole world.\n\n\"You will see my Georgy,\" was the best thing Emmy could think of to console Becky. If anything could make her comfortable that would.\n\nAnd so the two women continued talking for an hour or more, during which Becky had the opportunity of giving her new friend a full and complete version of her private history. She showed how her marriage with Rawdon Crawley had always been viewed by the family with feelings of the utmost hostility; how her sister-in-law (an artful woman) had poisoned her husband's mind against her; how he had formed odious connections, which had estranged his affections from her: how she had borne everything--poverty, neglect, coldness from the being whom she most loved--and all for the sake of her child; how, finally, and by the most flagrant outrage, she had been driven into demanding a separation from her husband, when the wretch did not scruple to ask that she should sacrifice her own fair fame so that he might procure advancement through the means of a very great and powerful but unprincipled man--the Marquis of Steyne, indeed. The atrocious monster!\n\nThis part of her eventful history Becky gave with the utmost feminine delicacy and the most indignant virtue. Forced to fly her husband's roof by this insult, the coward had pursued his revenge by taking her child from her. And thus Becky said she was a wanderer, poor, unprotected, friendless, and wretched.\n\nEmmy received this story, which was told at some length, as those persons who are acquainted with her character may imagine that she would. She quivered with indignation at the account of the conduct of the miserable Rawdon and the unprincipled Steyne. Her eyes made notes of admiration for every one of the sentences in which Becky described the persecutions of her aristocratic relatives and the falling away of her husband. (Becky did not abuse him. She spoke rather in sorrow than in anger. She had loved him only too fondly: and was he not the father of her boy?) And as for the separation scene from the child, while Becky was reciting it, Emmy retired altogether behind her pocket-handkerchief, so that the consummate little tragedian must have been charmed to see the effect which her performance produced on her audience.\n\nWhilst the ladies were carrying on their conversation, Amelia's constant escort, the Major (who, of course, did not wish to interrupt their conference, and found himself rather tired of creaking about the narrow stair passage of which the roof brushed the nap from his hat) descended to the ground-floor of the house and into the great room common to all the frequenters of the Elephant, out of which the stair led. This apartment is always in a fume of smoke and liberally sprinkled with beer. On a dirty table stand scores of corresponding brass candlesticks with tallow candles for the lodgers, whose keys hang up in rows over the candles. Emmy had passed blushing through the room anon, where all sorts of people were collected; Tyrolese glove-sellers and Danubian linen-merchants, with their packs; students recruiting themselves with butterbrods and meat; idlers, playing cards or dominoes on the sloppy, beery tables; tumblers refreshing during the cessation of their performances--in a word, all the fumum and strepitus of a German inn in fair time. The waiter brought the Major a mug of beer, as a matter of course, and he took out a cigar and amused himself with that pernicious vegetable and a newspaper until his charge should come down to claim him.\n\nMax and Fritz came presently downstairs, their caps on one side, their spurs jingling, their pipes splendid with coats of arms and full-blown tassels, and they hung up the key of No. 90 on the board and called for the ration of butterbrod and beer. The pair sat down by the Major and fell into a conversation of which he could not help hearing somewhat. It was mainly about \"Fuchs\" and \"Philister,\" and duels and drinking-bouts at the neighbouring University of Schoppenhausen, from which renowned seat of learning they had just come in the Eilwagen, with Becky, as it appeared, by their side, and in order to be present at the bridal fetes at Pumpernickel.\n\n\"The title Englanderinn seems to be en bays de gonnoisance,\" said Max, who knew the French language, to Fritz, his comrade. \"After the fat grandfather went away, there came a pretty little compatriot. I heard them chattering and whimpering together in the little woman's chamber.\"\n\n\"We must take the tickets for her concert,\" Fritz said. \"Hast thou any money, Max?\"\n\n\"Bah,\" said the other, \"the concert is a concert in nubibus. Hans said that she advertised one at Leipzig, and the Burschen took many tickets. But she went off without singing. She said in the coach yesterday that her pianist had fallen ill at Dresden. She cannot sing, it is my belief: her voice is as cracked as thine, O thou beer-soaking Renowner!\"\n\n\"It is cracked; I hear her trying out of her window a schrecklich English ballad, called 'De Rose upon de Balgony.'\"\n\n\"Saufen and singen go not together,\" observed Fritz with the red nose, who evidently preferred the former amusement. \"No, thou shalt take none of her tickets. She won money at the trente and quarante last night. I saw her: she made a little English boy play for her. We will spend thy money there or at the theatre, or we will treat her to French wine or Cognac in the Aurelius Garden, but the tickets we will not buy. What sayest thou? Yet, another mug of beer?\" and one and another successively having buried their blond whiskers in the mawkish draught, curled them and swaggered off into the fair.\n\nThe Major, who had seen the key of No. 90 put up on its hook and had heard the conversation of the two young University bloods, was not at a loss to understand that their talk related to Becky. \"The little devil is at her old tricks,\" he thought, and he smiled as he recalled old days, when he had witnessed the desperate flirtation with Jos and the ludicrous end of that adventure. He and George had often laughed over it subsequently, and until a few weeks after George's marriage, when he also was caught in the little Circe's toils, and had an understanding with her which his comrade certainly suspected, but preferred to ignore. William was too much hurt or ashamed to ask to fathom that disgraceful mystery, although once, and evidently with remorse on his mind, George had alluded to it. It was on the morning of Waterloo, as the young men stood together in front of their line, surveying the black masses of Frenchmen who crowned the opposite heights, and as the rain was coming down, \"I have been mixing in a foolish intrigue with a woman,\" George said. \"I am glad we were marched away. If I drop, I hope Emmy will never know of that business. I wish to God it had never been begun!\" And William was pleased to think, and had more than once soothed poor George's widow with the narrative, that Osborne, after quitting his wife, and after the action of Quatre Bras, on the first day, spoke gravely and affectionately to his comrade of his father and his wife. On these facts, too, William had insisted very strongly in his conversations with the elder Osborne, and had thus been the means of reconciling the old gentleman to his son's memory, just at the close of the elder man's life.\n\n\"And so this devil is still going on with her intrigues,\" thought William. \"I wish she were a hundred miles from here. She brings mischief wherever she goes.\" And he was pursuing these forebodings and this uncomfortable train of thought, with his head between his hands, and the Pumpernickel Gazette of last week unread under his nose, when somebody tapped his shoulder with a parasol, and he looked up and saw Mrs. Amelia.\n\nThis woman had a way of tyrannizing over Major Dobbin (for the weakest of all people will domineer over somebody), and she ordered him about, and patted him, and made him fetch and carry just as if he was a great Newfoundland dog. He liked, so to speak, to jump into the water if she said \"High, Dobbin!\" and to trot behind her with her reticule in his mouth. This history has been written to very little purpose if the reader has not perceived that the Major was a spooney.\n\n\"Why did you not wait for me, sir, to escort me downstairs?\" she said, giving a little toss of her head and a most sarcastic curtsey.\n\n\"I couldn't stand up in the passage,\" he answered with a comical deprecatory look; and, delighted to give her his arm and to take her out of the horrid smoky place, he would have walked off without even so much as remembering the waiter, had not the young fellow run after him and stopped him on the threshold of the Elephant to make him pay for the beer which he had not consumed. Emmy laughed: she called him a naughty man, who wanted to run away in debt, and, in fact, made some jokes suitable to the occasion and the small-beer. She was in high spirits and good humour, and tripped across the market-place very briskly. She wanted to see Jos that instant. The Major laughed at the impetuous affection Mrs. Amelia exhibited; for, in truth, it was not very often that she wanted her brother \"that instant.\" They found the civilian in his saloon on the first-floor; he had been pacing the room, and biting his nails, and looking over the market-place towards the Elephant a hundred times at least during the past hour whilst Emmy was closeted with her friend in the garret and the Major was beating the tattoo on the sloppy tables of the public room below, and he was, on his side too, very anxious to see Mrs. Osborne.\n\n\"Well?\" said he.\n\n\"The poor dear creature, how she has suffered!\" Emmy said.\n\n\"God bless my soul, yes,\" Jos said, wagging his head, so that his cheeks quivered like jellies.\n\n\"She may have Payne's room, who can go upstairs,\" Emmy continued. Payne was a staid English maid and personal attendant upon Mrs. Osborne, to whom the courier, as in duty bound, paid court, and whom Georgy used to \"lark\" dreadfully with accounts of German robbers and ghosts. She passed her time chiefly in grumbling, in ordering about her mistress, and in stating her intention to return the next morning to her native village of Clapham. \"She may have Payne's room,\" Emmy said.\n\n\"Why, you don't mean to say you are going to have that woman into the house?\" bounced out the Major, jumping up.\n\n\"Of course we are,\" said Amelia in the most innocent way in the world. \"Don't be angry and break the furniture, Major Dobbin. Of course we are going to have her here.\"\n\n\"Of course, my dear,\" Jos said.\n\n\"The poor creature, after all her sufferings,\" Emmy continued; \"her horrid banker broken and run away; her husband--wicked wretch--having deserted her and taken her child away from her\" (here she doubled her two little fists and held them in a most menacing attitude before her, so that the Major was charmed to see such a dauntless virago) \"the poor dear thing! quite alone and absolutely forced to give lessons in singing to get her bread--and not have her here!\"\n\n\"Take lessons, my dear Mrs. George,\" cried the Major, \"but don't have her in the house. I implore you don't.\"\n\n\"Pooh,\" said Jos.\n\n\"You who are always good and kind--always used to be at any rate--I'm astonished at you, Major William,\" Amelia cried. \"Why, what is the moment to help her but when she is so miserable? Now is the time to be of service to her. The oldest friend I ever had, and not--\"\n\n\"She was not always your friend, Amelia,\" the Major said, for he was quite angry. This allusion was too much for Emmy, who, looking the Major almost fiercely in the face, said, \"For shame, Major Dobbin!\" and after having fired this shot, she walked out of the room with a most majestic air and shut her own door briskly on herself and her outraged dignity.\n\n\"To allude to THAT!\" she said, when the door was closed. \"Oh, it was cruel of him to remind me of it,\" and she looked up at George's picture, which hung there as usual, with the portrait of the boy underneath. \"It was cruel of him. If I had forgiven it, ought he to have spoken? No. And it is from his own lips that I know how wicked and groundless my jealousy was; and that you were pure--oh, yes, you were pure, my saint in heaven!\"\n\nShe paced the room, trembling and indignant. She went and leaned on the chest of drawers over which the picture hung, and gazed and gazed at it. Its eyes seemed to look down on her with a reproach that deepened as she looked. The early dear, dear memories of that brief prime of love rushed back upon her. The wound which years had scarcely cicatrized bled afresh, and oh, how bitterly! She could not bear the reproaches of the husband there before her. It couldn't be. Never, never.\n\nPoor Dobbin; poor old William! That unlucky word had undone the work of many a year--the long laborious edifice of a life of love and constancy--raised too upon what secret and hidden foundations, wherein lay buried passions, uncounted struggles, unknown sacrifices--a little word was spoken, and down fell the fair palace of hope--one word, and away flew the bird which he had been trying all his life to lure!\n\nWilliam, though he saw by Amelia's looks that a great crisis had come, nevertheless continued to implore Sedley, in the most energetic terms, to beware of Rebecca; and he eagerly, almost frantically, adjured Jos not to receive her. He besought Mr. Sedley to inquire at least regarding her; told him how he had heard that she was in the company of gamblers and people of ill repute; pointed out what evil she had done in former days, how she and Crawley had misled poor George into ruin, how she was now parted from her husband, by her own confession, and, perhaps, for good reason. What a dangerous companion she would be for his sister, who knew nothing of the affairs of the world! William implored Jos, with all the eloquence which he could bring to bear, and a great deal more energy than this quiet gentleman was ordinarily in the habit of showing, to keep Rebecca out of his household.\n\nHad he been less violent, or more dexterous, he might have succeeded in his supplications to Jos; but the civilian was not a little jealous of the airs of superiority which the Major constantly exhibited towards him, as he fancied (indeed, he had imparted his opinions to Mr. Kirsch, the courier, whose bills Major Dobbin checked on this journey, and who sided with his master), and he began a blustering speech about his competency to defend his own honour, his desire not to have his affairs meddled with, his intention, in fine, to rebel against the Major, when the colloquy--rather a long and stormy one--was put an end to in the simplest way possible, namely, by the arrival of Mrs. Becky, with a porter from the Elephant Hotel in charge of her very meagre baggage.\n\nShe greeted her host with affectionate respect and made a shrinking, but amicable salutation to Major Dobbin, who, as her instinct assured her at once, was her enemy, and had been speaking against her; and the bustle and clatter consequent upon her arrival brought Amelia out of her room. Emmy went up and embraced her guest with the greatest warmth, and took no notice of the Major, except to fling him an angry look--the most unjust and scornful glance that had perhaps ever appeared in that poor little woman's face since she was born. But she had private reasons of her own, and was bent upon being angry with him. And Dobbin, indignant at the injustice, not at the defeat, went off, making her a bow quite as haughty as the killing curtsey with which the little woman chose to bid him farewell.\n\nHe being gone, Emmy was particularly lively and affectionate to Rebecca, and bustled about the apartments and installed her guest in her room with an eagerness and activity seldom exhibited by our placid little friend. But when an act of injustice is to be done, especially by weak people, it is best that it should be done quickly, and Emmy thought she was displaying a great deal of firmness and proper feeling and veneration for the late Captain Osborne in her present behaviour.\n\nGeorgy came in from the fetes for dinner-time and found four covers laid as usual; but one of the places was occupied by a lady, instead of by Major Dobbin. \"Hullo! where's Dob?\" the young gentleman asked with his usual simplicity of language. \"Major Dobbin is dining out, I suppose,\" his mother said, and, drawing the boy to her, kissed him a great deal, and put his hair off his forehead, and introduced him to Mrs. Crawley. \"This is my boy, Rebecca,\" Mrs. Osborne said--as much as to say--can the world produce anything like that? Becky looked at him with rapture and pressed his hand fondly. \"Dear boy!\" she said--\"he is just like my--\" Emotion choked her further utterance, but Amelia understood, as well as if she had spoken, that Becky was thinking of her own blessed child. However, the company of her friend consoled Mrs. Crawley, and she ate a very good dinner.\n\nDuring the repast, she had occasion to speak several times, when Georgy eyed her and listened to her. At the desert Emmy was gone out to superintend further domestic arrangements; Jos was in his great chair dozing over Galignani; Georgy and the new arrival sat close to each other--he had continued to look at her knowingly more than once, and at last he laid down the nutcrackers.\n\n\"I say,\" said Georgy.\n\n\"What do you say?\" Becky said, laughing.\n\n\"You're the lady I saw in the mask at the Rouge et Noir.\"\n\n\"Hush! you little sly creature,\" Becky said, taking up his hand and kissing it. \"Your uncle was there too, and Mamma mustn't know.\"\n\n\"Oh, no--not by no means,\" answered the little fellow.\n\n\"You see we are quite good friends already,\" Becky said to Emmy, who now re-entered; and it must be owned that Mrs. Osborne had introduced a most judicious and amiable companion into her house.\n\nWilliam, in a state of great indignation, though still unaware of all the treason that was in store for him, walked about the town wildly until he fell upon the Secretary of Legation, Tapeworm, who invited him to dinner. As they were discussing that meal, he took occasion to ask the Secretary whether he knew anything about a certain Mrs. Rawdon Crawley, who had, he believed, made some noise in London; and then Tapeworm, who of course knew all the London gossip, and was besides a relative of Lady Gaunt, poured out into the astonished Major's ears such a history about Becky and her husband as astonished the querist, and supplied all the points of this narrative, for it was at that very table years ago that the present writer had the pleasure of hearing the tale. Tufto, Steyne, the Crawleys, and their history--everything connected with Becky and her previous life passed under the record of the bitter diplomatist. He knew everything and a great deal besides, about all the world--in a word, he made the most astounding revelations to the simple-hearted Major. When Dobbin said that Mrs. Osborne and Mr. Sedley had taken her into their house, Tapeworm burst into a peal of laughter which shocked the Major, and asked if they had not better send into the prison and take in one or two of the gentlemen in shaved heads and yellow jackets who swept the streets of Pumpernickel, chained in pairs, to board and lodge, and act as tutor to that little scapegrace Georgy.\n\nThis information astonished and horrified the Major not a little. It had been agreed in the morning (before meeting with Rebecca) that Amelia should go to the Court ball that night. There would be the place where he should tell her. The Major went home, and dressed himself in his uniform, and repaired to Court, in hopes to see Mrs. Osborne. She never came. When he returned to his lodgings all the lights in the Sedley tenement were put out. He could not see her till the morning. I don't know what sort of a night's rest he had with this frightful secret in bed with him.\n\nAt the earliest convenient hour in the morning he sent his servant across the way with a note, saying that he wished very particularly to speak with her. A message came back to say that Mrs. Osborne was exceedingly unwell and was keeping her room.\n\nShe, too, had been awake all that night. She had been thinking of a thing which had agitated her mind a hundred times before. A hundred times on the point of yielding, she had shrunk back from a sacrifice which she felt was too much for her. She couldn't, in spite of his love and constancy and her own acknowledged regard, respect, and gratitude. What are benefits, what is constancy, or merit? One curl of a girl's ringlet, one hair of a whisker, will turn the scale against them all in a minute. They did not weigh with Emmy more than with other women. She had tried them; wanted to make them pass; could not; and the pitiless little woman had found a pretext, and determined to be free.\n\nWhen at length, in the afternoon, the Major gained admission to Amelia, instead of the cordial and affectionate greeting, to which he had been accustomed now for many a long day, he received the salutation of a curtsey, and of a little gloved hand, retracted the moment after it was accorded to him.\n\nRebecca, too, was in the room, and advanced to meet him with a smile and an extended hand. Dobbin drew back rather confusedly, \"I--I beg your pardon, m'am,\" he said; \"but I am bound to tell you that it is not as your friend that I am come here now.\"\n\n\"Pooh! damn; don't let us have this sort of thing!\" Jos cried out, alarmed, and anxious to get rid of a scene.\n\n\"I wonder what Major Dobbin has to say against Rebecca?\" Amelia said in a low, clear voice with a slight quiver in it, and a very determined look about the eyes.\n\n\"I will not have this sort of thing in my house,\" Jos again interposed. \"I say I will not have it; and Dobbin, I beg, sir, you'll stop it.\" And he looked round, trembling and turning very red, and gave a great puff, and made for his door.\n\n\"Dear friend!\" Rebecca said with angelic sweetness, \"do hear what Major Dobbin has to say against me.\"\n\n\"I will not hear it, I say,\" squeaked out Jos at the top of his voice, and, gathering up his dressing-gown, he was gone.\n\n\"We are only two women,\" Amelia said. \"You can speak now, sir.\"\n\n\"This manner towards me is one which scarcely becomes you, Amelia,\" the Major answered haughtily; \"nor I believe am I guilty of habitual harshness to women. It is not a pleasure to me to do the duty which I am come to do.\"\n\n\"Pray proceed with it quickly, if you please, Major Dobbin,\" said Amelia, who was more and more in a pet. The expression of Dobbin's face, as she spoke in this imperious manner, was not pleasant.\n\n\"I came to say--and as you stay, Mrs. Crawley, I must say it in your presence--that I think you--you ought not to form a member of the family of my friends. A lady who is separated from her husband, who travels not under her own name, who frequents public gaming-tables--\"\n\n\"It was to the ball I went,\" cried out Becky.\n\n\"--is not a fit companion for Mrs. Osborne and her son,\" Dobbin went on: \"and I may add that there are people here who know you, and who profess to know that regarding your conduct about which I don't even wish to speak before--before Mrs. Osborne.\"\n\n\"Yours is a very modest and convenient sort of calumny, Major Dobbin,\" Rebecca said. \"You leave me under the weight of an accusation which, after all, is unsaid. What is it? Is it unfaithfulness to my husband? I scorn it and defy anybody to prove it--I defy you, I say. My honour is as untouched as that of the bitterest enemy who ever maligned me. Is it of being poor, forsaken, wretched, that you accuse me? Yes, I am guilty of those faults, and punished for them every day. Let me go, Emmy. It is only to suppose that I have not met you, and I am no worse to-day than I was yesterday. It is only to suppose that the night is over and the poor wanderer is on her way. Don't you remember the song we used to sing in old, dear old days? I have been wandering ever since then--a poor castaway, scorned for being miserable, and insulted because I am alone. Let me go: my stay here interferes with the plans of this gentleman.\"\n\n\"Indeed it does, madam,\" said the Major. \"If I have any authority in this house--\"\n\n\"Authority, none!\" broke out Amelia. \"Rebecca, you stay with me. I won't desert you because you have been persecuted, or insult you because--because Major Dobbin chooses to do so. Come away, dear.\" And the two women made towards the door.\n\nWilliam opened it. As they were going out, however, he took Amelia's hand and said--\"Will you stay a moment and speak to me?\"\n\n\"He wishes to speak to you away from me,\" said Becky, looking like a martyr. Amelia gripped her hand in reply.\n\n\"Upon my honour it is not about you that I am going to speak,\" Dobbin said. \"Come back, Amelia,\" and she came. Dobbin bowed to Mrs. Crawley, as he shut the door upon her. Amelia looked at him, leaning against the glass: her face and her lips were quite white.\n\n\"I was confused when I spoke just now,\" the Major said after a pause, \"and I misused the word authority.\"\n\n\"You did,\" said Amelia with her teeth chattering.\n\n\"At least I have claims to be heard,\" Dobbin continued.\n\n\"It is generous to remind me of our obligations to you,\" the woman answered.\n\n\"The claims I mean are those left me by George's father,\" William said.\n\n\"Yes, and you insulted his memory. You did yesterday. You know you did. And I will never forgive you. Never!\" said Amelia. She shot out each little sentence in a tremor of anger and emotion.\n\n\"You don't mean that, Amelia?\" William said sadly. \"You don't mean that these words, uttered in a hurried moment, are to weigh against a whole life's devotion? I think that George's memory has not been injured by the way in which I have dealt with it, and if we are come to bandying reproaches, I at least merit none from his widow and the mother of his son. Reflect, afterwards when--when you are at leisure, and your conscience will withdraw this accusation. It does even now.\" Amelia held down her head.\n\n\"It is not that speech of yesterday,\" he continued, \"which moves you. That is but the pretext, Amelia, or I have loved you and watched you for fifteen years in vain. Have I not learned in that time to read all your feelings and look into your thoughts? I know what your heart is capable of: it can cling faithfully to a recollection and cherish a fancy, but it can't feel such an attachment as mine deserves to mate with, and such as I would have won from a woman more generous than you. No, you are not worthy of the love which I have devoted to you. I knew all along that the prize I had set my life on was not worth the winning; that I was a fool, with fond fancies, too, bartering away my all of truth and ardour against your little feeble remnant of love. I will bargain no more: I withdraw. I find no fault with you. You are very good-natured, and have done your best, but you couldn't--you couldn't reach up to the height of the attachment which I bore you, and which a loftier soul than yours might have been proud to share. Good-bye, Amelia! I have watched your struggle. Let it end. We are both weary of it.\"\n\nAmelia stood scared and silent as William thus suddenly broke the chain by which she held him and declared his independence and superiority. He had placed himself at her feet so long that the poor little woman had been accustomed to trample upon him. She didn't wish to marry him, but she wished to keep him. She wished to give him nothing, but that he should give her all. It is a bargain not unfrequently levied in love.\n\nWilliam's sally had quite broken and cast her down. HER assault was long since over and beaten back.\n\n\"Am I to understand then, that you are going--away, William?\" she said.\n\nHe gave a sad laugh. \"I went once before,\" he said, \"and came back after twelve years. We were young then, Amelia. Good-bye. I have spent enough of my life at this play.\"\n\nWhilst they had been talking, the door into Mrs. Osborne's room had opened ever so little; indeed, Becky had kept a hold of the handle and had turned it on the instant when Dobbin quitted it, and she heard every word of the conversation that had passed between these two. \"What a noble heart that man has,\" she thought, \"and how shamefully that woman plays with it!\" She admired Dobbin; she bore him no rancour for the part he had taken against her. It was an open move in the game, and played fairly. \"Ah!\" she thought, \"if I could have had such a husband as that--a man with a heart and brains too! I would not have minded his large feet\"; and running into her room, she absolutely bethought herself of something, and wrote him a note, beseeching him to stop for a few days--not to think of going--and that she could serve him with A.\n\nThe parting was over. Once more poor William walked to the door and was gone; and the little widow, the author of all this work, had her will, and had won her victory, and was left to enjoy it as she best might. Let the ladies envy her triumph.\n\nAt the romantic hour of dinner, Mr. Georgy made his appearance and again remarked the absence of \"Old Dob.\" The meal was eaten in silence by the party. Jos's appetite not being diminished, but Emmy taking nothing at all.\n\nAfter the meal, Georgy was lolling in the cushions of the old window, a large window, with three sides of glass abutting from the gable, and commanding on one side the market-place, where the Elephant is, his mother being busy hard by, when he remarked symptoms of movement at the Major's house on the other side of the street.\n\n\"Hullo!\" said he, \"there's Dob's trap--they are bringing it out of the court-yard.\" The \"trap\" in question was a carriage which the Major had bought for six pounds sterling, and about which they used to rally him a good deal.\n\nEmmy gave a little start, but said nothing.\n\n\"Hullo!\" Georgy continued, \"there's Francis coming out with the portmanteaus, and Kunz, the one-eyed postilion, coming down the market with three schimmels. Look at his boots and yellow jacket--ain't he a rum one? Why--they're putting the horses to Dob's carriage. Is he going anywhere?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Emmy, \"he is going on a journey.\"\n\n\"Going on a journey; and when is he coming back?\"\n\n\"He is--not coming back,\" answered Emmy.\n\n\"Not coming back!\" cried out Georgy, jumping up. \"Stay here, sir,\" roared out Jos. \"Stay, Georgy,\" said his mother with a very sad face. The boy stopped, kicked about the room, jumped up and down from the window-seat with his knees, and showed every symptom of uneasiness and curiosity.\n\nThe horses were put to. The baggage was strapped on. Francis came out with his master's sword, cane, and umbrella tied up together, and laid them in the well, and his desk and old tin cocked-hat case, which he placed under the seat. Francis brought out the stained old blue cloak lined with red camlet, which had wrapped the owner up any time these fifteen years, and had manchen Sturm erlebt, as a favourite song of those days said. It had been new for the campaign of Waterloo and had covered George and William after the night of Quatre Bras.\n\nOld Burcke, the landlord of the lodgings, came out, then Francis, with more packages--final packages--then Major William--Burcke wanted to kiss him. The Major was adored by all people with whom he had to do. It was with difficulty he could escape from this demonstration of attachment.\n\n\"By Jove, I will go!\" screamed out George. \"Give him this,\" said Becky, quite interested, and put a paper into the boy's hand. He had rushed down the stairs and flung across the street in a minute--the yellow postilion was cracking his whip gently.\n\nWilliam had got into the carriage, released from the embraces of his landlord. George bounded in afterwards, and flung his arms round the Major's neck (as they saw from the window), and began asking him multiplied questions. Then he felt in his waistcoat pocket and gave him a note. William seized at it rather eagerly, he opened it trembling, but instantly his countenance changed, and he tore the paper in two and dropped it out of the carriage. He kissed Georgy on the head, and the boy got out, doubling his fists into his eyes, and with the aid of Francis. He lingered with his hand on the panel. Fort, Schwager! The yellow postilion cracked his whip prodigiously, up sprang Francis to the box, away went the schimmels, and Dobbin with his head on his breast. He never looked up as they passed under Amelia's window, and Georgy, left alone in the street, burst out crying in the face of all the crowd.\n\nEmmy's maid heard him howling again during the night and brought him some preserved apricots to console him. She mingled her lamentations with his. All the poor, all the humble, all honest folks, all good men who knew him, loved that kind-hearted and simple gentleman.\n\nAs for Emmy, had she not done her duty? She had her picture of George for a consolation.\n\nWhich Contains Births, Marriages, and Deaths\nWhatever Becky's private plan might be by which Dobbin's true love was to be crowned with success, the little woman thought that the secret might keep, and indeed, being by no means so much interested about anybody's welfare as about her own, she had a great number of things pertaining to herself to consider, and which concerned her a great deal more than Major Dobbin's happiness in this life.\n\nShe found herself suddenly and unexpectedly in snug comfortable quarters, surrounded by friends, kindness, and good-natured simple people such as she had not met with for many a long day; and, wanderer as she was by force and inclination, there were moments when rest was pleasant to her. As the most hardened Arab that ever careered across the desert over the hump of a dromedary likes to repose sometimes under the date-trees by the water, or to come into the cities, walk into the bazaars, refresh himself in the baths, and say his prayers in the mosques, before he goes out again marauding, so Jos's tents and pilau were pleasant to this little Ishmaelite. She picketed her steed, hung up her weapons, and warmed herself comfortably by his fire. The halt in that roving, restless life was inexpressibly soothing and pleasant to her.\n\nSo, pleased herself, she tried with all her might to please everybody; and we know that she was eminent and successful as a practitioner in the art of giving pleasure. As for Jos, even in that little interview in the garret at the Elephant Inn, she had found means to win back a great deal of his good-will. In the course of a week, the civilian was her sworn slave and frantic admirer. He didn't go to sleep after dinner, as his custom was in the much less lively society of Amelia. He drove out with Becky in his open carriage. He asked little parties and invented festivities to do her honour.\n\nTapeworm, the Charge d'Affaires, who had abused her so cruelly, came to dine with Jos, and then came every day to pay his respects to Becky. Poor Emmy, who was never very talkative, and more glum and silent than ever after Dobbin's departure, was quite forgotten when this superior genius made her appearance. The French Minister was as much charmed with her as his English rival. The German ladies, never particularly squeamish as regards morals, especially in English people, were delighted with the cleverness and wit of Mrs. Osborne's charming friend, and though she did not ask to go to Court, yet the most august and Transparent Personages there heard of her fascinations and were quite curious to know her. When it became known that she was noble, of an ancient English family, that her husband was a Colonel of the Guard, Excellenz and Governor of an island, only separated from his lady by one of those trifling differences which are of little account in a country where Werther is still read and the Wahlverwandtschaften of Goethe is considered an edifying moral book, nobody thought of refusing to receive her in the very highest society of the little Duchy; and the ladies were even more ready to call her du and to swear eternal friendship for her than they had been to bestow the same inestimable benefits upon Amelia. Love and Liberty are interpreted by those simple Germans in a way which honest folks in Yorkshire and Somersetshire little understand, and a lady might, in some philosophic and civilized towns, be divorced ever so many times from her respective husbands and keep her character in society. Jos's house never was so pleasant since he had a house of his own as Rebecca caused it to be. She sang, she played, she laughed, she talked in two or three languages, she brought everybody to the house, and she made Jos believe that it was his own great social talents and wit which gathered the society of the place round about him.\n\nAs for Emmy, who found herself not in the least mistress of her own house, except when the bills were to be paid, Becky soon discovered the way to soothe and please her. She talked to her perpetually about Major Dobbin sent about his business, and made no scruple of declaring her admiration for that excellent, high-minded gentleman, and of telling Emmy that she had behaved most cruelly regarding him. Emmy defended her conduct and showed that it was dictated only by the purest religious principles; that a woman once, &c., and to such an angel as him whom she had had the good fortune to marry, was married forever; but she had no objection to hear the Major praised as much as ever Becky chose to praise him, and indeed, brought the conversation round to the Dobbin subject a score of times every day.\n\nMeans were easily found to win the favour of Georgy and the servants. Amelia's maid, it has been said, was heart and soul in favour of the generous Major. Having at first disliked Becky for being the means of dismissing him from the presence of her mistress, she was reconciled to Mrs. Crawley subsequently, because the latter became William's most ardent admirer and champion. And in those nightly conclaves in which the two ladies indulged after their parties, and while Miss Payne was \"brushing their 'airs,\" as she called the yellow locks of the one and the soft brown tresses of the other, this girl always put in her word for that dear good gentleman Major Dobbin. Her advocacy did not make Amelia angry any more than Rebecca's admiration of him. She made George write to him constantly and persisted in sending Mamma's kind love in a postscript. And as she looked at her husband's portrait of nights, it no longer reproached her--perhaps she reproached it, now William was gone.\n\nEmmy was not very happy after her heroic sacrifice. She was very distraite, nervous, silent, and ill to please. The family had never known her so peevish. She grew pale and ill. She used to try to sing certain songs (\"Einsam bin ich nicht alleine,\" was one of them, that tender love-song of Weber's which in old-fashioned days, young ladies, and when you were scarcely born, showed that those who lived before you knew too how to love and to sing) certain songs, I say, to which the Major was partial; and as she warbled them in the twilight in the drawing-room, she would break off in the midst of the song, and walk into her neighbouring apartment, and there, no doubt, take refuge in the miniature of her husband.\n\nSome books still subsisted, after Dobbin's departure, with his name written in them; a German dictionary, for instance, with \"William Dobbin, --th Reg.,\" in the fly-leaf; a guide-book with his initials; and one or two other volumes which belonged to the Major. Emmy cleared these away and put them on the drawers, where she placed her work-box, her desk, her Bible, and prayer-book, under the pictures of the two Georges. And the Major, on going away, having left his gloves behind him, it is a fact that Georgy, rummaging his mother's desk some time afterwards, found the gloves neatly folded up and put away in what they call the secret-drawers of the desk.\n\nNot caring for society, and moping there a great deal, Emmy's chief pleasure in the summer evenings was to take long walks with Georgy (during which Rebecca was left to the society of Mr. Joseph), and then the mother and son used to talk about the Major in a way which even made the boy smile. She told him that she thought Major William was the best man in all the world--the gentlest and the kindest, the bravest and the humblest. Over and over again she told him how they owed everything which they possessed in the world to that kind friend's benevolent care of them; how he had befriended them all through their poverty and misfortunes; watched over them when nobody cared for them; how all his comrades admired him though he never spoke of his own gallant actions; how Georgy's father trusted him beyond all other men, and had been constantly befriended by the good William. \"Why, when your papa was a little boy,\" she said, \"he often told me that it was William who defended him against a tyrant at the school where they were; and their friendship never ceased from that day until the last, when your dear father fell.\"\n\n\"Did Dobbin kill the man who killed Papa?\" Georgy said. \"I'm sure he did, or he would if he could have caught him, wouldn't he, Mother? When I'm in the Army, won't I hate the French?--that's all.\"\n\nIn such colloquies the mother and the child passed a great deal of their time together. The artless woman had made a confidant of the boy. He was as much William's friend as everybody else who knew him well.\n\nBy the way, Mrs. Becky, not to be behind hand in sentiment, had got a miniature too hanging up in her room, to the surprise and amusement of most people, and the delight of the original, who was no other than our friend Jos. On her first coming to favour the Sedleys with a visit, the little woman, who had arrived with a remarkably small shabby kit, was perhaps ashamed of the meanness of her trunks and bandboxes, and often spoke with great respect about her baggage left behind at Leipzig, which she must have from that city. When a traveller talks to you perpetually about the splendour of his luggage, which he does not happen to have with him, my son, beware of that traveller! He is, ten to one, an impostor.\n\nNeither Jos nor Emmy knew this important maxim. It seemed to them of no consequence whether Becky had a quantity of very fine clothes in invisible trunks; but as her present supply was exceedingly shabby, Emmy supplied her out of her own stores, or took her to the best milliner in the town and there fitted her out. It was no more torn collars now, I promise you, and faded silks trailing off at the shoulder. Becky changed her habits with her situation in life--the rouge-pot was suspended--another excitement to which she had accustomed herself was also put aside, or at least only indulged in in privacy, as when she was prevailed on by Jos of a summer evening, Emmy and the boy being absent on their walks, to take a little spirit-and-water. But if she did not indulge--the courier did: that rascal Kirsch could not be kept from the bottle, nor could he tell how much he took when he applied to it. He was sometimes surprised himself at the way in which Mr. Sedley's Cognac diminished. Well, well, this is a painful subject. Becky did not very likely indulge so much as she used before she entered a decorous family.\n\nAt last the much-bragged-about boxes arrived from Leipzig; three of them not by any means large or splendid; nor did Becky appear to take out any sort of dresses or ornaments from the boxes when they did arrive. But out of one, which contained a mass of her papers (it was that very box which Rawdon Crawley had ransacked in his furious hunt for Becky's concealed money), she took a picture with great glee, which she pinned up in her room, and to which she introduced Jos. It was the portrait of a gentleman in pencil, his face having the advantage of being painted up in pink. He was riding on an elephant away from some cocoa-nut trees and a pagoda: it was an Eastern scene.\n\n\"God bless my soul, it is my portrait,\" Jos cried out. It was he indeed, blooming in youth and beauty, in a nankeen jacket of the cut of 1804. It was the old picture that used to hang up in Russell Square.\n\n\"I bought it,\" said Becky in a voice trembling with emotion; \"I went to see if I could be of any use to my kind friends. I have never parted with that picture--I never will.\"\n\n\"Won't you?\" Jos cried with a look of unutterable rapture and satisfaction. \"Did you really now value it for my sake?\"\n\n\"You know I did, well enough,\" said Becky; \"but why speak--why think--why look back! It is too late now!\"\n\nThat evening's conversation was delicious for Jos. Emmy only came in to go to bed very tired and unwell. Jos and his fair guest had a charming tete-a-tete, and his sister could hear, as she lay awake in her adjoining chamber, Rebecca singing over to Jos the old songs of 1815. He did not sleep, for a wonder, that night, any more than Amelia.\n\nIt was June, and, by consequence, high season in London; Jos, who read the incomparable Galignani (the exile's best friend) through every day, used to favour the ladies with extracts from his paper during their breakfast. Every week in this paper there is a full account of military movements, in which Jos, as a man who had seen service, was especially interested. On one occasion he read out--\"Arrival of the --th regiment. Gravesend, June 20.--The Ramchunder, East Indiaman, came into the river this morning, having on board 14 officers, and 132 rank and file of this gallant corps. They have been absent from England fourteen years, having been embarked the year after Waterloo, in which glorious conflict they took an active part, and having subsequently distinguished themselves in the Burmese war. The veteran colonel, Sir Michael O'Dowd, K.C.B., with his lady and sister, landed here yesterday, with Captains Posky, Stubble, Macraw, Malony; Lieutenants Smith, Jones, Thompson, F. Thomson; Ensigns Hicks and Grady; the band on the pier playing the national anthem, and the crowd loudly cheering the gallant veterans as they went into Wayte's hotel, where a sumptuous banquet was provided for the defenders of Old England. During the repast, which we need not say was served up in Wayte's best style, the cheering continued so enthusiastically that Lady O'Dowd and the Colonel came forward to the balcony and drank the healths of their fellow-countrymen in a bumper of Wayte's best claret.\"\n\nOn a second occasion Jos read a brief announcement--Major Dobbin had joined the --th regiment at Chatham; and subsequently he promulgated accounts of the presentations at the Drawing-room of Colonel Sir Michael O'Dowd, K.C.B., Lady O'Dowd (by Mrs. Malloy Malony of Ballymalony), and Miss Glorvina O'Dowd (by Lady O'Dowd). Almost directly after this, Dobbin's name appeared among the Lieutenant-Colonels: for old Marshal Tiptoff had died during the passage of the --th from Madras, and the Sovereign was pleased to advance Colonel Sir Michael O'Dowd to the rank of Major-General on his return to England, with an intimation that he should be Colonel of the distinguished regiment which he had so long commanded.\n\nAmelia had been made aware of some of these movements. The correspondence between George and his guardian had not ceased by any means: William had even written once or twice to her since his departure, but in a manner so unconstrainedly cold that the poor woman felt now in her turn that she had lost her power over him and that, as he had said, he was free. He had left her, and she was wretched. The memory of his almost countless services, and lofty and affectionate regard, now presented itself to her and rebuked her day and night. She brooded over those recollections according to her wont, saw the purity and beauty of the affection with which she had trifled, and reproached herself for having flung away such a treasure.\n\nIt was gone indeed. William had spent it all out. He loved her no more, he thought, as he had loved her. He never could again. That sort of regard, which he had proffered to her for so many faithful years, can't be flung down and shattered and mended so as to show no scars. The little heedless tyrant had so destroyed it. No, William thought again and again, \"It was myself I deluded and persisted in cajoling; had she been worthy of the love I gave her, she would have returned it long ago. It was a fond mistake. Isn't the whole course of life made up of such? And suppose I had won her, should I not have been disenchanted the day after my victory? Why pine, or be ashamed of my defeat?\" The more he thought of this long passage of his life, the more clearly he saw his deception. \"I'll go into harness again,\" he said, \"and do my duty in that state of life in which it has pleased Heaven to place me. I will see that the buttons of the recruits are properly bright and that the sergeants make no mistakes in their accounts. I will dine at mess and listen to the Scotch surgeon telling his stories. When I am old and broken, I will go on half-pay, and my old sisters shall scold me. I have geliebt und gelebet, as the girl in 'Wallenstein' says. I am done. Pay the bills and get me a cigar: find out what there is at the play to-night, Francis; to-morrow we cross by the Batavier.\" He made the above speech, whereof Francis only heard the last two lines, pacing up and down the Boompjes at Rotterdam. The Batavier was lying in the basin. He could see the place on the quarter-deck where he and Emmy had sat on the happy voyage out. What had that little Mrs. Crawley to say to him? Psha; to-morrow we will put to sea, and return to England, home, and duty!\n\nAfter June all the little Court Society of Pumpernickel used to separate, according to the German plan, and make for a hundred watering-places, where they drank at the wells, rode upon donkeys, gambled at the redoutes if they had money and a mind, rushed with hundreds of their kind to gourmandise at the tables d'hote, and idled away the summer. The English diplomatists went off to Teoplitz and Kissingen, their French rivals shut up their chancellerie and whisked away to their darling Boulevard de Gand. The Transparent reigning family took too to the waters, or retired to their hunting lodges. Everybody went away having any pretensions to politeness, and of course, with them, Doctor von Glauber, the Court Doctor, and his Baroness. The seasons for the baths were the most productive periods of the Doctor's practice--he united business with pleasure, and his chief place of resort was Ostend, which is much frequented by Germans, and where the Doctor treated himself and his spouse to what he called a \"dib\" in the sea.\n\nHis interesting patient, Jos, was a regular milch-cow to the Doctor, and he easily persuaded the civilian, both for his own health's sake and that of his charming sister, which was really very much shattered, to pass the summer at that hideous seaport town. Emmy did not care where she went much. Georgy jumped at the idea of a move. As for Becky, she came as a matter of course in the fourth place inside of the fine barouche Mr. Jos had bought, the two domestics being on the box in front. She might have some misgivings about the friends whom she should meet at Ostend, and who might be likely to tell ugly stories--but bah! she was strong enough to hold her own. She had cast such an anchor in Jos now as would require a strong storm to shake. That incident of the picture had finished him. Becky took down her elephant and put it into the little box which she had had from Amelia ever so many years ago. Emmy also came off with her Lares--her two pictures--and the party, finally, were, lodged in an exceedingly dear and uncomfortable house at Ostend.\n\nThere Amelia began to take baths and get what good she could from them, and though scores of people of Becky's acquaintance passed her and cut her, yet Mrs. Osborne, who walked about with her, and who knew nobody, was not aware of the treatment experienced by the friend whom she had chosen so judiciously as a companion; indeed, Becky never thought fit to tell her what was passing under her innocent eyes.\n\nSome of Mrs. Rawdon Crawley's acquaintances, however, acknowledged her readily enough,--perhaps more readily than she would have desired. Among those were Major Loder (unattached), and Captain Rook (late of the Rifles), who might be seen any day on the Dike, smoking and staring at the women, and who speedily got an introduction to the hospitable board and select circle of Mr. Joseph Sedley. In fact they would take no denial; they burst into the house whether Becky was at home or not, walked into Mrs. Osborne's drawing-room, which they perfumed with their coats and mustachios, called Jos \"Old buck,\" and invaded his dinner-table, and laughed and drank for long hours there.\n\n\"What can they mean?\" asked Georgy, who did not like these gentlemen. \"I heard the Major say to Mrs. Crawley yesterday, 'No, no, Becky, you shan't keep the old buck to yourself. We must have the bones in, or, dammy, I'll split.' What could the Major mean, Mamma?\"\n\n\"Major! don't call him Major!\" Emmy said. \"I'm sure I can't tell what he meant.\" His presence and that of his friend inspired the little lady with intolerable terror and aversion. They paid her tipsy compliments; they leered at her over the dinner-table. And the Captain made her advances that filled her with sickening dismay, nor would she ever see him unless she had George by her side.\n\nRebecca, to do her justice, never would let either of these men remain alone with Amelia; the Major was disengaged too, and swore he would be the winner of her. A couple of ruffians were fighting for this innocent creature, gambling for her at her own table, and though she was not aware of the rascals' designs upon her, yet she felt a horror and uneasiness in their presence and longed to fly.\n\nShe besought, she entreated Jos to go. Not he. He was slow of movement, tied to his Doctor, and perhaps to some other leading-strings. At least Becky was not anxious to go to England.\n\nAt last she took a great resolution--made the great plunge. She wrote off a letter to a friend whom she had on the other side of the water, a letter about which she did not speak a word to anybody, which she carried herself to the post under her shawl; nor was any remark made about it, only that she looked very much flushed and agitated when Georgy met her, and she kissed him, and hung over him a great deal that night. She did not come out of her room after her return from her walk. Becky thought it was Major Loder and the Captain who frightened her.\n\n\"She mustn't stop here,\" Becky reasoned with herself. \"She must go away, the silly little fool. She is still whimpering after that gaby of a husband--dead (and served right!) these fifteen years. She shan't marry either of these men. It's too bad of Loder. No; she shall marry the bamboo cane, I'll settle it this very night.\"\n\nSo Becky took a cup of tea to Amelia in her private apartment and found that lady in the company of her miniatures, and in a most melancholy and nervous condition. She laid down the cup of tea.\n\n\"Thank you,\" said Amelia.\n\n\"Listen to me, Amelia,\" said Becky, marching up and down the room before the other and surveying her with a sort of contemptuous kindness. \"I want to talk to you. You must go away from here and from the impertinences of these men. I won't have you harassed by them: and they will insult you if you stay. I tell you they are rascals: men fit to send to the hulks. Never mind how I know them. I know everybody. Jos can't protect you; he is too weak and wants a protector himself. You are no more fit to live in the world than a baby in arms. You must marry, or you and your precious boy will go to ruin. You must have a husband, you fool; and one of the best gentlemen I ever saw has offered you a hundred times, and you have rejected him, you silly, heartless, ungrateful little creature!\"\n\n\"I tried--I tried my best, indeed I did, Rebecca,\" said Amelia deprecatingly, \"but I couldn't forget--\"; and she finished the sentence by looking up at the portrait.\n\n\"Couldn't forget HIM!\" cried out Becky, \"that selfish humbug, that low-bred cockney dandy, that padded booby, who had neither wit, nor manners, nor heart, and was no more to be compared to your friend with the bamboo cane than you are to Queen Elizabeth. Why, the man was weary of you, and would have jilted you, but that Dobbin forced him to keep his word. He owned it to me. He never cared for you. He used to sneer about you to me, time after time, and made love to me the week after he married you.\"\n\n\"It's false! It's false! Rebecca,\" cried out Amelia, starting up.\n\n\"Look there, you fool,\" Becky said, still with provoking good humour, and taking a little paper out of her belt, she opened it and flung it into Emmy's lap. \"You know his handwriting. He wrote that to me--wanted me to run away with him--gave it me under your nose, the day before he was shot--and served him right!\" Becky repeated.\n\nEmmy did not hear her; she was looking at the letter. It was that which George had put into the bouquet and given to Becky on the night of the Duchess of Richmond's ball. It was as she said: the foolish young man had asked her to fly.\n\nEmmy's head sank down, and for almost the last time in which she shall be called upon to weep in this history, she commenced that work. Her head fell to her bosom, and her hands went up to her eyes; and there for a while, she gave way to her emotions, as Becky stood on and regarded her. Who shall analyse those tears and say whether they were sweet or bitter? Was she most grieved because the idol of her life was tumbled down and shivered at her feet, or indignant that her love had been so despised, or glad because the barrier was removed which modesty had placed between her and a new, a real affection? \"There is nothing to forbid me now,\" she thought. \"I may love him with all my heart now. Oh, I will, I will, if he will but let me and forgive me.\" I believe it was this feeling rushed over all the others which agitated that gentle little bosom.\n\nIndeed, she did not cry so much as Becky expected--the other soothed and kissed her--a rare mark of sympathy with Mrs. Becky. She treated Emmy like a child and patted her head. \"And now let us get pen and ink and write to him to come this minute,\" she said.\n\n\"I--I wrote to him this morning,\" Emmy said, blushing exceedingly. Becky screamed with laughter--\"Un biglietto,\" she sang out with Rosina, \"eccolo qua!\"--the whole house echoed with her shrill singing.\n\nTwo mornings after this little scene, although the day was rainy and gusty, and Amelia had had an exceedingly wakeful night, listening to the wind roaring, and pitying all travellers by land and by water, yet she got up early and insisted upon taking a walk on the Dike with Georgy; and there she paced as the rain beat into her face, and she looked out westward across the dark sea line and over the swollen billows which came tumbling and frothing to the shore. Neither spoke much, except now and then, when the boy said a few words to his timid companion, indicative of sympathy and protection.\n\n\"I hope he won't cross in such weather,\" Emmy said.\n\n\"I bet ten to one he does,\" the boy answered. \"Look, Mother, there's the smoke of the steamer.\" It was that signal, sure enough.\n\nBut though the steamer was under way, he might not be on board; he might not have got the letter; he might not choose to come. A hundred fears poured one over the other into the little heart, as fast as the waves on to the Dike.\n\nThe boat followed the smoke into sight. Georgy had a dandy telescope and got the vessel under view in the most skilful manner. And he made appropriate nautical comments upon the manner of the approach of the steamer as she came nearer and nearer, dipping and rising in the water. The signal of an English steamer in sight went fluttering up to the mast on the pier. I daresay Mrs. Amelia's heart was in a similar flutter.\n\nEmmy tried to look through the telescope over George's shoulder, but she could make nothing of it. She only saw a black eclipse bobbing up and down before her eyes.\n\nGeorge took the glass again and raked the vessel. \"How she does pitch!\" he said. \"There goes a wave slap over her bows. There's only two people on deck besides the steersman. There's a man lying down, and a--chap in a--cloak with a--Hooray!--it's Dob, by Jingo!\" He clapped to the telescope and flung his arms round his mother. As for that lady, let us say what she did in the words of a favourite poet--\"Dakruoen gelasasa.\" She was sure it was William. It could be no other. What she had said about hoping that he would not come was all hypocrisy. Of course he would come; what could he do else but come? She knew he would come.\n\nThe ship came swiftly nearer and nearer. As they went in to meet her at the landing-place at the quay, Emmy's knees trembled so that she scarcely could run. She would have liked to kneel down and say her prayers of thanks there. Oh, she thought, she would be all her life saying them!\n\nIt was such a bad day that as the vessel came alongside of the quay there were no idlers abroad, scarcely even a commissioner on the look out for the few passengers in the steamer. That young scapegrace George had fled too, and as the gentleman in the old cloak lined with red stuff stepped on to the shore, there was scarcely any one present to see what took place, which was briefly this:\n\nA lady in a dripping white bonnet and shawl, with her two little hands out before her, went up to him, and in the next minute she had altogether disappeared under the folds of the old cloak, and was kissing one of his hands with all her might; whilst the other, I suppose, was engaged in holding her to his heart (which her head just about reached) and in preventing her from tumbling down. She was murmuring something about--forgive--dear William--dear, dear, dearest friend--kiss, kiss, kiss, and so forth--and in fact went on under the cloak in an absurd manner.\n\nWhen Emmy emerged from it, she still kept tight hold of one of William's hands, and looked up in his face. It was full of sadness and tender love and pity. She understood its reproach and hung down her head.\n\n\"It was time you sent for me, dear Amelia,\" he said.\n\n\"You will never go again, William?\"\n\n\"No, never,\" he answered, and pressed the dear little soul once more to his heart.\n\nAs they issued out of the custom-house precincts, Georgy broke out on them, with his telescope up to his eye, and a loud laugh of welcome; he danced round the couple and performed many facetious antics as he led them up to the house. Jos wasn't up yet; Becky not visible (though she looked at them through the blinds). Georgy ran off to see about breakfast. Emmy, whose shawl and bonnet were off in the passage in the hands of Mrs. Payne, now went to undo the clasp of William's cloak, and--we will, if you please, go with George, and look after breakfast for the Colonel. The vessel is in port. He has got the prize he has been trying for all his life. The bird has come in at last. There it is with its head on his shoulder, billing and cooing close up to his heart, with soft outstretched fluttering wings. This is what he has asked for every day and hour for eighteen years. This is what he pined after. Here it is--the summit, the end--the last page of the third volume. Good-bye, Colonel--God bless you, honest William!--Farewell, dear Amelia--Grow green again, tender little parasite, round the rugged old oak to which you cling!\n\nPerhaps it was compunction towards the kind and simple creature, who had been the first in life to defend her, perhaps it was a dislike to all such sentimental scenes--but Rebecca, satisfied with her part in the transaction, never presented herself before Colonel Dobbin and the lady whom he married. \"Particular business,\" she said, took her to Bruges, whither she went, and only Georgy and his uncle were present at the marriage ceremony. When it was over, and Georgy had rejoined his parents, Mrs. Becky returned (just for a few days) to comfort the solitary bachelor, Joseph Sedley. He preferred a continental life, he said, and declined to join in housekeeping with his sister and her husband.\n\nEmmy was very glad in her heart to think that she had written to her husband before she read or knew of that letter of George's. \"I knew it all along,\" William said; \"but could I use that weapon against the poor fellow's memory? It was that which made me suffer so when you--\"\n\n\"Never speak of that day again,\" Emmy cried out, so contrite and humble that William turned off the conversation by his account of Glorvina and dear old Peggy O'Dowd, with whom he was sitting when the letter of recall reached him. \"If you hadn't sent for me,\" he added with a laugh, \"who knows what Glorvina's name might be now?\"\n\nAt present it is Glorvina Posky (now Mrs. Major Posky); she took him on the death of his first wife, having resolved never to marry out of the regiment. Lady O'Dowd is also so attached to it that, she says, if anything were to happen to Mick, bedad she'd come back and marry some of 'em. But the Major-General is quite well and lives in great splendour at O'Dowdstown, with a pack of beagles, and (with the exception of perhaps their neighbour, Hoggarty of Castle Hoggarty) he is the first man of his county. Her Ladyship still dances jigs, and insisted on standing up with the Master of the Horse at the Lord Lieutenant's last ball. Both she and Glorvina declared that Dobbin had used the latter SHEAMFULLY, but Posky falling in, Glorvina was consoled, and a beautiful turban from Paris appeased the wrath of Lady O'Dowd.\n\nWhen Colonel Dobbin quitted the service, which he did immediately after his marriage, he rented a pretty little country place in Hampshire, not far from Queen's Crawley, where, after the passing of the Reform Bill, Sir Pitt and his family constantly resided now. All idea of a Peerage was out of the question, the Baronet's two seats in Parliament being lost. He was both out of pocket and out of spirits by that catastrophe, failed in his health, and prophesied the speedy ruin of the Empire.\n\nLady Jane and Mrs. Dobbin became great friends--there was a perpetual crossing of pony-chaises between the Hall and the Evergreens, the Colonel's place (rented of his friend Major Ponto, who was abroad with his family). Her Ladyship was godmother to Mrs. Dobbin's child, which bore her name, and was christened by the Rev. James Crawley, who succeeded his father in the living: and a pretty close friendship subsisted between the two lads, George and Rawdon, who hunted and shot together in the vacations, were both entered of the same college at Cambridge, and quarrelled with each other about Lady Jane's daughter, with whom they were both, of course, in love. A match between George and that young lady was long a favourite scheme of both the matrons, though I have heard that Miss Crawley herself inclined towards her cousin.\n\nMrs. Rawdon Crawley's name was never mentioned by either family. There were reasons why all should be silent regarding her. For wherever Mr. Joseph Sedley went, she travelled likewise, and that infatuated man seemed to be entirely her slave. The Colonel's lawyers informed him that his brother-in-law had effected a heavy insurance upon his life, whence it was probable that he had been raising money to discharge debts. He procured prolonged leave of absence from the East India House, and indeed, his infirmities were daily increasing.\n\nOn hearing the news about the insurance, Amelia, in a good deal of alarm, entreated her husband to go to Brussels, where Jos then was, and inquire into the state of his affairs. The Colonel quitted home with reluctance (for he was deeply immersed in his History of the Punjaub which still occupies him, and much alarmed about his little daughter, whom he idolizes, and who was just recovering from the chicken-pox) and went to Brussels and found Jos living at one of the enormous hotels in that city. Mrs. Crawley, who had her carriage, gave entertainments, and lived in a very genteel manner, occupied another suite of apartments in the same hotel.\n\nThe Colonel, of course, did not desire to see that lady, or even think proper to notify his arrival at Brussels, except privately to Jos by a message through his valet. Jos begged the Colonel to come and see him that night, when Mrs. Crawley would be at a soiree, and when they could meet alone. He found his brother-in-law in a condition of pitiable infirmity--and dreadfully afraid of Rebecca, though eager in his praises of her. She tended him through a series of unheard-of illnesses with a fidelity most admirable. She had been a daughter to him. \"But--but--oh, for God's sake, do come and live near me, and--and--see me sometimes,\" whimpered out the unfortunate man.\n\nThe Colonel's brow darkened at this. \"We can't, Jos,\" he said. \"Considering the circumstances, Amelia can't visit you.\"\n\n\"I swear to you--I swear to you on the Bible,\" gasped out Joseph, wanting to kiss the book, \"that she is as innocent as a child, as spotless as your own wife.\"\n\n\"It may be so,\" said the Colonel gloomily, \"but Emmy can't come to you. Be a man, Jos: break off this disreputable connection. Come home to your family. We hear your affairs are involved.\"\n\n\"Involved!\" cried Jos. \"Who has told such calumnies? All my money is placed out most advantageously. Mrs. Crawley--that is--I mean--it is laid out to the best interest.\"\n\n\"You are not in debt, then? Why did you insure your life?\"\n\n\"I thought--a little present to her--in case anything happened; and you know my health is so delicate--common gratitude you know--and I intend to leave all my money to you--and I can spare it out of my income, indeed I can,\" cried out William's weak brother-in-law.\n\nThe Colonel besought Jos to fly at once--to go back to India, whither Mrs. Crawley could not follow him; to do anything to break off a connection which might have the most fatal consequences to him.\n\nJos clasped his hands and cried, \"He would go back to India. He would do anything, only he must have time: they mustn't say anything to Mrs. Crawley--she'd--she'd kill me if she knew it. You don't know what a terrible woman she is,\" the poor wretch said.\n\n\"Then, why not come away with me?\" said Dobbin in reply; but Jos had not the courage. \"He would see Dobbin again in the morning; he must on no account say that he had been there. He must go now. Becky might come in.\" And Dobbin quitted him, full of forebodings.\n\nHe never saw Jos more. Three months afterwards Joseph Sedley died at Aix-la-Chapelle. It was found that all his property had been muddled away in speculations, and was represented by valueless shares in different bubble companies. All his available assets were the two thousand pounds for which his life was insured, and which were left equally between his beloved \"sister Amelia, wife of, &c., and his friend and invaluable attendant during sickness, Rebecca, wife of Lieutenant-Colonel Rawdon Crawley, C.B.,\" who was appointed administratrix.\n\nThe solicitor of the insurance company swore it was the blackest case that ever had come before him, talked of sending a commission to Aix to examine into the death, and the Company refused payment of the policy. But Mrs., or Lady Crawley, as she styled herself, came to town at once (attended with her solicitors, Messrs. Burke, Thurtell, and Hayes, of Thavies Inn) and dared the Company to refuse the payment. They invited examination, they declared that she was the object of an infamous conspiracy, which had been pursuing her all through life, and triumphed finally. The money was paid, and her character established, but Colonel Dobbin sent back his share of the legacy to the insurance office and rigidly declined to hold any communication with Rebecca.\n\nShe never was Lady Crawley, though she continued so to call herself. His Excellency Colonel Rawdon Crawley died of yellow fever at Coventry Island, most deeply beloved and deplored, and six weeks before the demise of his brother, Sir Pitt. The estate consequently devolved upon the present Sir Rawdon Crawley, Bart.\n\nHe, too, has declined to see his mother, to whom he makes a liberal allowance, and who, besides, appears to be very wealthy. The Baronet lives entirely at Queen's Crawley, with Lady Jane and her daughter, whilst Rebecca, Lady Crawley, chiefly hangs about Bath and Cheltenham, where a very strong party of excellent people consider her to be a most injured woman. She has her enemies. Who has not? Her life is her answer to them. She busies herself in works of piety. She goes to church, and never without a footman. Her name is in all the Charity Lists. The destitute orange-girl, the neglected washerwoman, the distressed muffin-man find in her a fast and generous friend. She is always having stalls at Fancy Fairs for the benefit of these hapless beings. Emmy, her children, and the Colonel, coming to London some time back, found themselves suddenly before her at one of these fairs. She cast down her eyes demurely and smiled as they started away from her; Emmy scurrying off on the arm of George (now grown a dashing young gentleman) and the Colonel seizing up his little Janey, of whom he is fonder than of anything in the world--fonder even than of his History of the Punjaub.\n\n\"Fonder than he is of me,\" Emmy thinks with a sigh. But he never said a word to Amelia that was not kind and gentle, or thought of a want of hers that he did not try to gratify.\n\nAh! Vanitas Vanitatum! which of us is happy in this world? Which of us has his desire? or, having it, is satisfied?--come, children, let us shut up the box and the puppets, for our play is played out.";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-599"]="금세기가 아직 십 대였던 어느 시절, 유월의 어느 햇살 따스한 아침이었다. 치즈윅 거리에 자리한 핑커튼 여사의 숙녀 교육원, 그 커다란 철문 앞으로 큼직한 가족용 마차 한 대가 다가와 멈춰 섰다. 번쩍이는 마구를 두른 살진 말 두 마리가 끄는 마차였고, 삼각모를 쓰고 가발을 얹은 살진 마부가 시속 사 마일의 속도로 몰고 온 것이었다. 살진 마부 옆 마부석에 늘어져 있던 흑인 하인은, 마차가 핑커튼 여사의 반짝이는 놋쇠 명패 맞은편에 멈추자마자 휘어진 다리를 펴고 일어나 종을 당겼다. 그러자 위풍당당한 낡은 벽돌 저택의 좁은 창문마다 적어도 스무 개는 됨직한 어린 머리들이 빼꼼히 내다보았다. 아니, 눈썰미 있는 관찰자라면 인자한 제미마 핑커튼 양 본인의 자그마한 빨간 코가, 응접실 창가의 제라늄 화분들 너머로 솟아오르는 것까지 알아보았을지도 모른다.\n\n\"세들리 부인의 마차예요, 언니.\" 제미마 양이 말했다. \"흑인 하인 샘보가 방금 종을 울렸어요. 마부는 빨간 조끼를 새로 입었네요.\"\n\n\"세들리 양의 출발에 따르는 필요한 준비는 모두 마쳤느냐, 제미마?\" 핑커튼 여사 본인이 물었다. 저 위엄 넘치는 부인, 해머스미스의 세미라미스, 존슨 박사의 벗이자 채폰 부인 본인과 서신을 주고받는 사이인 그분이었다.\n\n\"여자아이들이 오늘 아침 네 시에 일어나서 트렁크를 쌌어요, 언니.\" 제미마 양이 대답했다. \"꽃다발도 만들어 주었고요.\"\n\n\"부케라고 하렴, 제미마. 그게 더 품위 있는 표현이지.\"\n\n\"네, 거의 건초더미만 한 부케예요. 세들리 부인을 위해 향꽃무 화장수 두 병이랑, 그 만드는 법까지 애밀리아의 상자에 넣어 두었어요.\"\n\n\"그리고 제미마, 세들리 양의 계산서 사본을 만들어 두었으리라 믿는다. 이게 그것이냐? 그렇구나, 아주 좋아. 구십삼 파운드 사 실링이라. 수고스럽겠지만 존 세들리 귀하 앞으로 주소를 적어 두고, 내가 그 부인께 쓴 이 편지에 봉인을 해 다오.\"\n\n제미마 양의 눈에는 언니인 핑커튼 여사가 친필로 쓴 편지란, 군주에게서 온 편지나 다름없이 깊은 경외의 대상이었다. 핑커튼 여사가 제자들의 부모에게 손수 편지를 쓰는 일이라고는, 제자가 학원을 떠날 때나 결혼을 앞두었을 때, 그리고 딱 한 번, 가엾은 버치 양이 성홍열로 세상을 떠났을 때뿐이었다. 그러니 만약 버치 부인이 딸을 잃은 슬픔을 무엇으로든 달랠 수 있다면, 그것은 핑커튼 여사가 그 비보를 알리며 써 보낸 경건하고 유려한 문장일 것이라는 게 제미마의 생각이었다.\n\n이번 경우, 핑커튼 여사의 \"편지\"는 다음과 같은 내용이었다.\n\n치즈윅 거리에서, 18××년 6월 15일\n\n부인께. 따님께서 본원에 육 년간 머무신 끝에, 저는 애밀리아 세들리 양을 그 부모님께 돌려보내는 영광과 기쁨을 누리게 되었습니다. 세들리 양은 부모님의 세련되고 고상한 교제권에서 어울리는 자리를 차지하기에 부족함이 없는 숙녀입니다. 영국 양가의 젊은 숙녀를 특징짓는 미덕들, 그 출생과 신분에 걸맞은 교양들을 이 사랑스러운 세들리 양에게서 찾아보지 못하는 일은 없을 것입니다. 그 근면함과 순종이 교사들의 사랑을 한 몸에 받았고, 그 더없이 사랑스러운 온화한 성정이 나이 든 동무들과 어린 동무들 모두를 매료시켰습니다.\n\n음악, 무용, 철자법, 그리고 온갖 종류의 자수와 바느질에서 세들리 양은 벗들의 가장 흐뭇한 기대를 모두 실현했음이 드러날 것입니다. 다만 지리 과목에서는 아직 바라는 바가 많이 남아 있습니다. 또한 모든 유행을 좇는 숙녀에게 그토록 긴요한, 저 기품 있는 몸가짐과 자태를 익히기 위해서는, 앞으로 삼 년간 매일 네 시간씩 자세 교정판을 한결같이 어김없이 사용하기를 권하는 바입니다.\n\n종교와 도덕의 원리에서도 세들리 양은, 저 위대한 사전 편찬자가 친히 왕림하시는 영광을 누렸고 또한 흠모할 만한 채폰 부인의 후원을 받아 온 본원에 부끄럽지 않은 인물임이 드러날 것입니다. 이 거리를 떠나면서 애밀리아 양은 동무들의 마음과, 이 글을 적어 올리는 영광을 누리는 그 스승의 애정 어린 마음을 함께 가져갈 것입니다.\n\n부인께, 부인의 더없이 황송한 종복 바버라 핑커튼 올림.\n\n추신. 샤프 양이 세들리 양과 동행합니다. 샤프 양이 러셀 광장에 머무는 기간이 열흘을 넘지 않도록 각별히 부탁드립니다. 샤프 양을 고용한 명문가에서 가능한 한 빨리 그녀의 봉사를 받고자 하기 때문입니다.\n\n이 편지를 다 쓰고 나서, 핑커튼 여사는 존슨 사전의 속표지에 자신의 이름과 세들리 양의 이름을 적었다. 이 흥미로운 저작은 제자들이 이 거리를 떠날 때마다 그녀가 반드시 선사하는 것이었다. 그 표지에는 \"치즈윅 거리, 핑커튼 여사의 학원을 떠나는 어느 젊은 숙녀에게 바치는 시. 고인이 되신 존경하는 새뮤얼 존슨 박사 작\"이라는 시구의 사본이 끼워져 있었다. 사실 이 위엄 넘치는 부인의 입에는 늘 그 사전 편찬자의 이름이 오르내렸고, 그분이 한 번 찾아왔던 일이 그녀의 명성과 재산의 밑천이 된 것이었다.\n\n언니에게서 찬장에서 \"사전\"을 꺼내 오라는 명을 받자, 제미마 양은 문제의 그 보관함에서 책 두 권을 꺼냈다. 핑커튼 여사가 첫 권에 적어 넣기를 마치자, 제미마는 다소 미심쩍고 머뭇거리는 기색으로 두 번째 권을 건넸다.\n\n\"이건 누구 것이냐, 제미마?\" 핑커튼 여사가 소름 끼치도록 싸늘하게 말했다.\n\n\"베키 샤프 거예요.\" 제미마가 몹시 떨면서, 시든 얼굴과 목까지 붉히며, 언니에게 등을 돌린 채 대답했다. \"베키 샤프요. 그 애도 떠나잖아요.\"\n\n\"제미마!\" 핑커튼 여사가 가장 큰 대문자로 외쳤다. \"제정신이냐? 그 사전을 도로 찬장에 넣어라. 앞으로 다시는 그런 방자한 짓을 저지를 엄두도 내지 말아라.\"\n\n\"하지만 언니, 겨우 이 실링 구 펜스인걸요. 가엾은 베키가 못 받으면 비참해할 거예요.\"\n\n\"세들리 양을 당장 내게 보내라.\" 핑커튼 여사가 말했다. 그래서 가엾은 제미마는 감히 한마디도 더 못 하고, 몹시 허둥대며 안절부절못한 채 종종걸음으로 물러갔다.\n\n세들리 양의 아버지는 런던의 상인으로, 제법 재산이 있는 사람이었다. 반면 샤프 양은 계약을 맺고 들어온 장학생이었다. 핑커튼 여사는 작별의 자리에서 사전이라는 높은 영예까지 베풀지 않더라도, 자기 딴에는 그녀에게 충분히 해 줄 만큼 해 주었다고 여기는 터였다.\n\n여교장의 편지란 묘지의 비문 이상으로도 이하로도 믿을 게 못 되는 법이다. 그러나 때로는 정말로 비석에 새겨진 온갖 찬사를 받아 마땅한 사람이 세상을 떠나는 일도 있는 법이다. 참으로 훌륭한 그리스도교도였고, 훌륭한 부모요 자식이요 아내요 남편이었으며, 실제로 슬픔에 잠긴 유족을 남기고 떠난 사람 말이다. 그와 마찬가지로, 남녀를 막론하고 교육원에서도 이따금, 사심 없는 교사가 베푼 찬사를 받기에 온전히 합당한 제자가 나오는 법이다. 그런데 애밀리아 세들리 양이야말로 이 보기 드문 부류의 젊은 숙녀였다. 핑커튼 여사가 칭찬한 모든 것을 받아 마땅했을 뿐 아니라, 저 거드름 피우는 늙은 미네르바 같은 부인이 제자와 자신 사이의 신분과 나이 차이 때문에 미처 보지 못한 매력적인 자질들도 숱하게 지니고 있었다.\n\n그녀는 종달새처럼, 혹은 빌링턴 부인처럼 노래할 줄 알았고, 힐리스버그나 파리소처럼 춤출 줄 알았으며, 자수도 아름답게 놓고, 철자법은 사전 그 자체만큼이나 정확했다. 그뿐 아니라 그녀에게는 다정하고, 잘 웃고, 부드럽고, 온화하고, 너그러운 마음씨가 있어서, 미네르바 본인부터 부엌데기 가엾은 하녀에 이르기까지, 또 일주일에 한 번 이 거리의 숙녀들에게 물건을 팔도록 허락받은 외눈박이 파이 장수의 딸에 이르기까지, 곁에 다가오는 모든 이의 사랑을 얻었다. 스물네 명의 젊은 숙녀 가운데 열두 명이 그녀의 마음을 터놓는 절친한 벗이었다. 시샘 많은 브리그스 양조차 그녀를 험담하는 법이 없었고, 도도하고 콧대 높은 솔타이어 양(덱스터 경의 손녀)도 그녀의 자태만큼은 기품 있다고 인정했다. 그리고 세인트키츠 출신의 부유한 곱슬머리 혼혈인 스워츠 양으로 말하자면, 애밀리아가 떠나던 날 어찌나 격렬하게 눈물을 쏟던지 플로스 박사를 부르고 각성제로 반쯤 취하게 만들어야 할 지경이었다. 핑커튼 여사의 애정으로 말하면, 그분의 높은 지위와 빼어난 덕망으로 미루어 짐작할 수 있듯, 차분하고 위엄 있는 것이었다. 그러나 제미마 양은 애밀리아가 떠난다는 생각에 벌써 몇 번이나 훌쩍거렸고, 언니가 무섭지만 않았더라면 세인트키츠 출신의 그 (수업료를 곱절로 내는) 상속녀처럼 그야말로 발작이라도 일으켰을 것이다. 하지만 그런 사치스러운 슬픔이란 응접실에 묵는 특별 기숙생에게나 허락되는 법이었다. 정직한 제미마는 온갖 계산서며 빨래며 수선이며 푸딩이며 식기며 그릇이며 하인들까지 죄다 도맡아 관리해야 했다. 그런데 그녀 이야기를 해서 무엇하겠는가? 아마도 우리는 이 순간부터 시간의 끝에 이르도록 다시는 그녀의 소식을 듣지 못할 것이고, 저 커다란 세공 철문이 그녀를 향해 한 번 닫히고 나면, 그녀와 그 무시무시한 언니는 이 자그마한 이야기의 세계로 다시는 나오지 못할 것이다.\n\n그러나 우리가 앞으로 애밀리아를 자주 보게 될 터이니, 알게 된 첫머리에서 그녀가 사랑스러운 작은 사람이었다고 말해 두어도 해될 것은 없겠다. 인생에서나 소설에서나 가장 음울한 부류의 악당들이 넘쳐나는데(특히 소설이 그렇다), 이토록 천진하고 선량한 사람을 변함없는 길동무로 삼게 된 것은 참으로 큰 자비이다. 그녀는 여주인공이 아니니 그 용모를 묘사할 필요는 없겠다. 사실 그녀의 코는 오히려 짧은 편이었고, 양 볼은 여주인공치고는 너무 둥글고 발그레했던 것 같아 걱정이긴 하다. 그러나 그 얼굴은 장밋빛 건강으로 발그레했고, 입술에는 더없이 싱그러운 미소가 어렸으며, 더없이 밝고 정직한 명랑함으로 반짝이는 두 눈을 지니고 있었다. 그 눈이 눈물로 가득 차는 때만 빼면 말이다. 그런데 그런 때가 너무도 잦았다. 이 어리석은 아이는 죽은 카나리아를 두고도 울었고, 어쩌다 고양이에게 잡힌 생쥐를 두고도 울었으며, 소설의 결말을 두고도, 그게 아무리 시시한 것이라도 울었기 때문이다. 그리고 그녀에게 모진 말을 한다는 것으로 말할 것 같으면, 어느 누가 그토록 모진 마음을 먹고 그런 짓을 한다면 그건 그 사람만 더 손해 볼 일이었다. 저 준엄하고 신과도 같은 부인 핑커튼 여사조차 처음 한 번 꾸짖은 뒤로는 다시 야단치지 않았고, 비록 감수성이라는 것을 대수롭게 여기는 정도가 대수학을 여기는 정도와 다를 바 없었지만, 모진 대우는 그녀에게 해롭다 하여 모든 교사와 선생에게 세들리 양을 더없이 부드럽게 대하라는 특별한 지시를 내렸던 것이다.\n\n그리하여 떠나는 날이 오자, 세들리 양은 웃는 버릇과 우는 버릇 사이에서 어떻게 처신해야 할지 몹시 갈피를 잡지 못했다. 집에 가게 되어 기쁘면서도, 학교를 떠나는 일이 더없이 서글펐던 것이다. 사흘 전부터 고아 소녀인 어린 로라 마틴은 강아지처럼 그녀를 졸졸 따라다녔다. 그녀는 적어도 열네 개의 선물을 만들어 주고 또 받아야 했으며, 매주 편지하겠다는 엄숙한 약속을 열네 번이나 해야 했다. \"내 편지는 우리 할아버지 덱스터 백작 앞으로 봉투에 싸서 보내.\" 솔타이어 양이 말했다(덧붙이자면, 이 아이는 행색이 다소 초라했다). \"우편 요금일랑 신경 쓰지 말고 매일 써 보내, 우리 귀여운 애야.\" 충동적이고 곱슬머리지만 너그럽고 정 많은 스워츠 양이 말했다. 그리고 고아인 어린 로라 마틴은(이제 막 정자체를 배우는 참이었다) 친구의 손을 잡고 애틋하게 그 얼굴을 올려다보며 말했다. \"애밀리아, 내가 너한테 편지 쓸 땐 너를 엄마라고 부를래.\" 이 모든 자질구레한 이야기를, 자기 클럽에서 이 책을 읽고 있는 존스라면 분명 지나치게 어리석고, 하찮고, 시답잖고, 극도로 감상적이라고 단언하리라는 것을 나는 의심치 않는다. 그렇다, 나는 지금 이 순간 존스가(양고기 한 점과 와인 반 파인트로 얼굴이 다소 불콰해진 채) 연필을 꺼내 들고 \"어리석은, 시답잖은\" 따위의 낱말에 밑줄을 그으며 거기에 자기 나름의 평인 \"정말 옳은 말이로다\"를 적어 넣는 모습이 눈에 선하다. 그래, 그는 고상한 천재라서 인생에서나 소설에서나 위대하고 영웅적인 것을 흠모하는 사람이니, 부디 경고를 새겨듣고 딴 데로 가는 편이 나을 것이다.\n\n자, 그럼 이야기를 이어 가자. 세들리 양의 꽃이며 선물이며 트렁크며 모자 상자가 샘보의 손에 마차에 실리고 나서, 거기에 더해 샤프 양의 명함이 단정하게 못으로 박힌, 비바람에 닳은 자그마한 쇠가죽 트렁크 하나가 실렸다. 샘보는 그것을 히죽거리며 건넸고 마부도 거기에 걸맞은 비웃음을 지으며 실었다. 이윽고 작별의 시간이 왔다. 그러나 그 순간의 슬픔은 핑커튼 여사가 제자에게 들려준 훌륭한 일장 연설 덕분에 상당히 누그러졌다. 그 작별 연설이 애밀리아를 사색에 잠기게 했다거나, 논리에서 우러난 평정심으로 어떤 식으로든 그녀를 무장시켰다는 말은 아니다. 다만 그 연설이 견딜 수 없이 따분하고, 거드름 가득하고, 지루했던 데다, 여교장에 대한 두려움이 눈앞에 크게 어른거린 탓에, 세들리 양은 그분 면전에서 감히 사사로운 슬픔을 터뜨릴 엄두를 내지 못했던 것이다. 응접실에는 부모님이 방문하는 엄숙한 행사 때처럼 씨앗 케이크 한 조각과 와인 한 병이 나왔고, 이 다과를 나눈 뒤에야 세들리 양은 비로소 떠나도 좋다는 허락을 받았다.\n\n\"들어가서 핑커튼 여사님께 작별 인사 드려야지, 베키!\" 제미마 양이, 아무도 눈여겨보지 않는 한 젊은 숙녀에게 말했다. 그녀는 자기 모자 상자를 직접 들고 계단을 내려오던 참이었다.\n\n\"그래야겠지요.\" 샤프 양이 차분하게 대답하니, 제미마 양은 적잖이 놀랐다. 제미마가 문을 두드리고 들어와도 좋다는 허락을 받자, 샤프 양은 더없이 무심한 태도로 나아가 완벽한 억양의 프랑스어로 말했다. \"마드무아젤, 작별 인사를 드리러 왔습니다.\"\n\n핑커튼 여사는 프랑스어를 알아듣지 못했다. 그저 그것을 아는 이들을 부릴 따름이었다. 그러나 그녀는 입술을 깨물고, 위풍당당한 로마식 매부리코가 달린 머리를(그 위에는 크고 근엄한 터번이 얹혀 있었다) 한껏 치켜들며 말했다. \"샤프 양, 좋은 아침 보내기를 바라네.\" 해머스미스의 세미라미스는 그렇게 말하면서, 작별의 뜻을 표하는 동시에 샤프 양이 그 목적으로 내민 손가락 하나를 잡고 흔들 기회를 주려고 한 손을 흔들어 보였다.\n\n샤프 양은 그저 더없이 싸늘한 미소와 인사로 자기 두 손을 마주 모을 뿐, 베풀어진 그 영예를 받아들이기를 한사코 거절했다. 그러자 세미라미스는 그 어느 때보다 분개하여 터번을 치켜 흔들었다. 사실 이것은 젊은 숙녀와 늙은 숙녀 사이의 자그마한 한판 싸움이었고, 패한 쪽은 늙은 숙녀였다. \"하느님의 가호가 있기를, 얘야.\" 그녀가 애밀리아를 끌어안으며, 그러는 내내 소녀의 어깨너머로 샤프 양을 노려보면서 말했다. \"이리 와, 베키.\" 제미마 양이 잔뜩 겁에 질려 그 젊은 여자를 끌어당기며 말했고, 응접실 문은 그들 뒤로 영영 닫혔다.\n\n이윽고 아래층에서는 부둥켜안고 헤어지는 한바탕 소동이 벌어졌다. 말로는 다 전할 수가 없다. 하인들이 모두 현관에 나와 있었고, 정다운 벗들도, 젊은 숙녀들도 다 모여 있었으며, 막 도착한 무용 선생도 있었다. 그리하여 서로 밀치고, 부둥켜안고, 입 맞추고, 우는 통에, 거기다 자기 방에서 들려오는 응접실 기숙생 스워츠 양의 발작적인 흐느낌까지 더해져, 그 어떤 붓으로도 그릴 수 없고 다정한 마음이라면 차마 그냥 지나치고 싶을 광경이 펼쳐졌다. 포옹이 끝나자 그들은 헤어졌다. 그러니까, 세들리 양이 벗들과 헤어졌다는 말이다. 샤프 양은 몇 분 전에 이미 새침하게 마차에 올라타 있었다. 그녀가 떠난다고 우는 이는 아무도 없었다.\n\n휘어진 다리의 샘보가 눈물짓는 어린 여주인을 향해 마차 문을 쾅 닫았다. 그러고는 마차 뒤로 훌쩍 올라탔다. \"잠깐만요!\" 제미마 양이 꾸러미 하나를 들고 철문으로 달려 나오며 외쳤다.\n\n\"샌드위치란다, 얘야.\" 그녀가 애밀리아에게 말했다. \"가다 보면 배가 고플지도 모르니까. 그리고 베키, 베키 샤프, 여기 네게 줄 책이야. 우리 언니가, 아니 내가, 그러니까 존슨 사전 말이다. 이건 꼭 챙겨 가야지. 잘 가렴. 어서 모세요, 마부 양반. 하느님의 가호가 있기를!\"\n\n그러고는 마음씨 고운 그녀는 북받치는 감정에 못 이겨 정원 안으로 물러갔다.\n\n그런데, 이게 웬일인가! 마차가 막 출발하려는 찰나, 샤프 양이 창밖으로 핼쑥한 얼굴을 내밀더니 정말로 그 책을 정원 안으로 도로 내던지는 것이 아닌가.\n\n이 일로 제미마는 하마터면 공포에 질려 기절할 뻔했다. \"어머나, 세상에.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"어쩜 저렇게 뻔뻔한——\" 북받치는 감정이 두 문장 다 끝맺지 못하게 가로막았다. 마차는 굴러갔고, 커다란 철문은 닫혔으며, 무용 수업을 알리는 종이 울렸다. 이제 두 젊은 숙녀 앞에는 온 세상이 펼쳐져 있었다. 그러니, 치즈윅 거리여, 안녕히.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\n샤프 양이 앞 장에서 언급한 그 영웅적인 행위를 해치우고, 도서 사전이 작은 정원의 포장길 위를 날아가 마침내 어안이 벙벙해진 제미마 양의 발치에 떨어지는 것을 지켜보았을 때, 조금 전까지 거의 시퍼렇게 질린 증오의 빛을 띠고 있던 그 젊은 숙녀의 얼굴은 어쩌면 그보다 별로 더 보기 좋다고 할 것도 없는 미소로 바뀌었고, 그녀는 홀가분한 심정으로 마차 좌석에 등을 기대며 말했다. \"사전은 이걸로 끝, 그리고 다행스럽게도, 나는 치즈윅을 벗어났어.\"\n\n세들리 양은 이 반항적인 행동에 제미마 양 못지않게 당황했다. 생각해 보라, 그녀가 학교를 떠난 지는 겨우 일 분밖에 되지 않았고, 육 년간 쌓인 인상이 그 짧은 시간 안에 사라질 리는 없는 것이다. 아니, 어떤 사람들에게는 젊은 시절의 그 두려움과 공포가 영영 사라지지 않고 남기도 한다. 이를테면 나는 예순여덟 살 된 어느 노신사를 아는데, 그는 어느 날 아침 식사 자리에서 몹시 동요된 얼굴로 내게 이렇게 말했다. \"어젯밤 꿈에 레인 박사한테 매를 맞았지 뭔가.\" 그날 저녁 동안 환상이 그를 오십오 년 전으로 되돌려 놓았던 것이다. 레인 박사와 그의 회초리는 예순여덟이 된 지금도 그의 마음속에서, 열세 살 때와 똑같이 무시무시했던 것이다. 만일 그 박사가 큼직한 자작나무 회초리를 들고, 예순여덟의 나이에 이른 그의 앞에 몸소 나타나, 무시무시한 목소리로 \"이놈, 바지를 내려라—\" 하고 말하기라도 한다면? 그래, 그래, 어쨌든 세들리 양은 이 항명 행위에 몹시 놀란 것이었다.\n\n\"어쩌면 그럴 수가 있어, 레베카?\" 한참 뜸을 들인 끝에 마침내 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"왜, 핑커튼 여사가 쫓아 나와서 나더러 어둠의 골방으로 돌아가라고 명령이라도 할 것 같아?\" 레베카가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 하지만—\"\n\n\"난 그 집 전체가 다 싫어.\" 샤프 양이 격분하여 말을 이었다. \"다시는 그 꼴을 안 봤으면 좋겠어. 그게 템스강 바닥에 가라앉아 버렸으면 좋겠다, 정말로. 그리고 핑커튼 여사가 거기 빠져 있다면, 난 그 여자를 건져 내지 않을 거야, 정말로 안 건져. 아, 그 여자가 저 물 위에 둥둥 떠 있는 꼴을 보면 얼마나 좋을까, 터번이고 뭐고 다 쓴 채로, 옷자락은 뒤로 길게 끌리고, 코는 거룻배 뱃머리처럼 삐죽 솟아서 말이야.\"\n\n\"쉿!\" 세들리 양이 외쳤다.\n\n\"왜, 저 흑인 하인이 일러바치기라도 할까 봐?\" 레베카 양이 웃으며 외쳤다. \"돌아가서 핑커튼 여사한테 내가 온 영혼을 다해 그 여자를 미워한다고 일러도 돼. 차라리 그래 줬으면 좋겠어. 그리고 그걸 증명할 방법이 있었으면 좋겠다고. 이태 동안 난 그 여자한테서 모욕과 학대만 받았어. 부엌의 어떤 하인보다도 못한 대접을 받았다고. 너 말고는 친구 한 명, 다정한 말 한마디 들어 본 적이 없어. 아래층 교실에서 어린애들 시중을 들어야 했고, 그 아가씨들이랑 프랑스어로 떠들어야 해서 결국 내 모국어가 지긋지긋해질 지경이었어. 그래도 핑커튼 여사한테 프랑스어로 말한 건 정말 신나는 일이었지, 안 그래? 그 여자는 프랑스어라곤 한마디도 모르는데, 그걸 자존심 때문에 인정하지 못했거든. 그것 때문에 그 여자가 날 떠나보낸 거라고 생각해. 그러니 프랑스어에 감사할 따름이지. 프랑스 만세! 황제 만세! 보나파르트 만세!\"\n\n\"아 레베카, 레베카, 부끄러운 줄 알아!\" 세들리 양이 외쳤다. 이것은 레베카가 그때껏 입에 담은 가장 불경한 말이었기 때문이다. 그 시절 영국에서 \"보나파르트 만세!\"라고 말하는 것은 \"루시퍼 만세!\"라고 말하는 것이나 다름없었던 것이다. \"어떻게 너는—어떻게 감히 그런 사악하고 앙심에 찬 생각을 할 수가 있어?\"\n\n\"복수가 사악할지는 몰라도, 그건 자연스러운 거야.\" 레베카 양이 대답했다. \"난 천사가 아니거든.\" 그리고, 사실대로 말하자면, 그녀는 분명 천사가 아니었다.\n\n이 짧은 대화가 오가는 동안(마차가 강가를 따라 느릿느릿 굴러가는 사이에 벌어진 일이었다) 한 가지 짚어 둘 만한 점이 있으니, 레베카 샤프 양이 두 번이나 하늘에 감사할 일이 있었는데, 그 첫째는 자신이 미워하던 어떤 사람에게서 벗어나게 해 준 것에 대해서였고, 둘째는 자신의 적들을 어떤 식으로든 곤혹스럽고 낭패한 처지에 몰아넣을 수 있게 해 준 것에 대해서였다는 사실이다. 그 어느 쪽도 종교적 감사를 바칠 만한 그리 갸륵한 동기는 아니며, 다정하고 너그러운 성품을 지닌 사람이라면 내세울 법한 것도 아니었다. 그러니 레베카 양은 조금도 다정하거나 너그러운 사람이 아니었던 것이다. 온 세상이 자기를 모질게 대한다고 이 젊은 염세가는 말했지만, 온 세상이 모질게 대하는 사람이라면 그가 받는 대접을 받아 마땅하다고 우리는 제법 확신해도 좋을 것이다. 세상은 거울이어서, 누구에게나 제 얼굴의 반영을 되비쳐 주는 법이다. 거울을 향해 찡그리면 거울도 시큰둥한 얼굴로 너를 마주 볼 것이요, 거울을 보고 웃으면 또 거울과 함께 웃으면, 거울은 유쾌하고 다정한 길동무가 되어 주는 것이다. 그러니 모든 젊은이들이여, 알아서들 고를 일이다. 한 가지는 분명하니, 세상이 샤프 양을 푸대접했다 하더라도, 그녀가 그 누구를 위해 착한 일 한 번 했다는 소문은 들린 적이 없다는 사실이다. 또한 스물네 명의 젊은 숙녀가 모조리 이 작품의 여주인공인 세들리 양처럼 사랑스러우리라 기대할 수도 없는 노릇이며(우리가 그녀를 고른 것은 다름 아니라 그녀가 누구보다 천성이 착했기 때문이지, 그렇지 않았다면 도대체 무엇이 우리를 가로막아 스워츠 양이나 크럼프 양이나 홉킨스 양을 그 자리에 여주인공으로 내세우지 못하게 했겠는가!), 모든 사람이 애밀리아 세들리 양처럼 겸손하고 온화한 성정을 지니리라 기대할 수도, 기회가 닿을 때마다 레베카의 모진 마음과 심술을 누그러뜨리려 들고, 천 마디의 다정한 말과 보살핌으로 적어도 한 번만이라도 동족을 향한 그녀의 적의를 이겨 내리라 기대할 수도 없는 것이다.\n\n샤프 양의 아버지는 화가였고, 그 자격으로 핑커튼 여사의 학원에서 그림 수업을 맡았던 사람이다. 그는 영리한 사람이었고, 유쾌한 길동무였으며, 게으른 학도였고, 빚을 지는 데 큰 소질이 있었으며, 술집을 각별히 좋아했다. 술에 취하면 아내와 딸을 때리곤 했고, 이튿날 아침이면 두통에 시달리며 자기 천재성을 알아주지 않는 세상을 욕하고, 어리석은 자들이라며 동료 화가들을 상당히 영리하게, 때로는 더없이 지당한 논리로 비방하곤 했다. 그는 제 한 몸 건사하기조차 무척 어려웠던 데다, 자기가 살던 소호 일대 사방 일 마일 안에 빚을 지고 있던 터라, 직업이 오페라 무용수인 프랑스 출신의 젊은 여자와 결혼해서 형편을 좀 펴 볼 궁리를 했다. 그 어머니의 보잘것없는 직업에 대해서는 샤프 양은 한 번도 언급한 적이 없었고, 다만 그 후로는 앙트르샤 가문이 가스코뉴의 명문가였다고 말하면서 그 가문의 후손이라는 것을 무척 자랑스러워하곤 했다. 그리고 신기한 일은, 이 젊은 숙녀가 인생을 살아갈수록 그녀의 조상들의 지위와 광휘가 점점 높아졌다는 사실이다.\n\n레베카의 어머니는 어딘가에서 약간의 교육을 받은 적이 있어서, 그 딸은 순수한 파리식 억양의 프랑스어를 구사했다. 그 시절에는 이것이 제법 드문 재주였고, 덕분에 그녀는 격식을 따지는 핑커튼 여사에게 채용될 수 있었던 것이다. 어머니가 세상을 떠난 데다, 아버지가 세 번째 진전섬망 발작 끝에 자신이 회복할 가망이 없음을 깨닫고, 핑커튼 여사에게 그 고아가 된 자식을 보살펴 달라 부탁하는 사내답고도 애절한 편지를 써 보낸 뒤, 집행관 두 명이 그 시신을 두고 다툰 끝에 무덤으로 내려갔던 것이다. 레베카는 치즈윅에 왔을 때 열일곱 살이었고, 계약 장학생 신분으로 묶여 있었다. 앞서 보았다시피 그녀의 임무는 프랑스어로 말하는 것이었고, 그 특전이란 비용 없이 지내면서 일 년에 몇 기니씩 받으며 학원에 출강하는 교수들에게서 토막 지식을 주워 모으는 것이었다.\n\n그녀는 몸집이 작고 가냘팠으며, 안색은 창백하고 머리카락은 모래빛이었고, 두 눈은 늘 내리깔고 있었다. 그러나 그 눈을 들어 올리면 무척 크고 묘하고 매혹적이었다. 어찌나 매혹적이었던지 옥스퍼드를 갓 나와 치즈윅 교구목사 플라워듀 목사 밑에서 부목사로 있던 크리스프 목사가 샤프 양에게 반해 버렸을 정도다. 학교 전용석에서 성서대까지 치즈윅 교회를 가로질러 쏘아 보낸 그녀의 눈길 한 방에 그는 그 자리에서 거꾸러진 것이었다. 이 넋이 나간 청년은 이따금 핑커튼 여사와 차를 마시곤 했는데(그의 어머니가 그를 핑커튼 여사에게 소개해 준 사이였다), 급기야 외눈박이 사과 장수에게 전해 달라고 맡긴 편지에서 결혼 비슷한 것을 제안하기에 이르렀는데, 그 편지가 도중에 가로채진 것이다. 크리스프 부인이 벅스턴에서 급히 불려 와 사랑하는 아들을 황급히 데려가 버렸다. 그러나 치즈윅이라는 비둘기장에 그런 독수리 한 마리가 들어왔다는 생각만으로도 핑커튼 여사의 가슴은 크게 술렁였고, 그녀는 위약금 때문에 샤프 양을 묶어 두고 있지만 않았더라면 당장 내쫓았을 것이다. 그리고 차를 마시며 두 차례 그를 만났을 때 자기가 보는 앞에서가 아니고는 크리스프 씨와 단 한마디도 주고받은 적이 없다는 그 젊은 숙녀의 항변을, 핑커튼 여사는 끝내 온전히 믿지 못했던 것이다.\n\n그 학원의 키 크고 발랄한 젊은 숙녀들 곁에 서면 레베카 샤프는 어린아이처럼 보였다. 그러나 그녀에게는 가난이 길러 낸 음울한 조숙함이 있었다. 그녀는 빚쟁이를 숱하게 상대하여 아버지의 집 문전에서 돌려보냈고, 장사치들을 어르고 구슬려 기분 좋게 만들어 한 끼 식사를 더 외상으로 얻어 내기도 한두 번이 아니었다. 그녀는 흔히 아버지 곁에 앉아 있었는데, 아버지는 딸의 재치를 무척 자랑스러워했고, 그녀는 그 막돼먹은 동료들이 나누는 이야기를—소녀가 듣기에는 어울리지 않을 때가 많은 이야기를—들었다. 그러나 자기는 한 번도 소녀였던 적이 없었다고, 여덟 살 때부터 이미 여자였다고 그녀는 말했다. 아, 어찌하여 핑커튼 여사는 그토록 위험한 새를 자기 새장 안에 들였단 말인가?\n\n사실인즉, 이 노부인은 레베카가 세상에서 가장 온순한 사람이라고 믿었던 것이다. 아버지가 그녀를 치즈윅에 데려왔을 때 레베카가 어찌나 감쪽같이 순진한 처녀 역할을 해냈던지 말이다. 레베카가 그 집에 들어오게 한 약조가 맺어지기 겨우 일 년 전, 레베카가 열여섯 살이었을 때, 핑커튼 여사는 위풍당당하게 짤막한 연설까지 곁들이며 그녀에게 인형 하나를 선물한 적이 있었다. 그런데 그 인형은 사실 스윈들 양이 수업 시간에 몰래 어르고 있는 것이 발각되어 압수당한 물건이었다. 그날 저녁 모임이 끝난 뒤(교수들이 죄다 초대받았던 연설 행사 때의 일이었다) 그 부녀가 함께 터덜터덜 집으로 걸어가며 얼마나 웃어 댔던가. 그리고 그 작은 흉내쟁이 레베카가 그 인형으로 만들어 낸 자신의 풍자 인형을 핑커튼 여사가 보았더라면 얼마나 펄펄 뛰었을 것인가. 베키는 그 인형을 데리고 대화 장면을 연기하곤 했다. 그것은 뉴먼 거리, 제라드 거리, 그리고 화가촌의 즐길 거리였다. 젊은 화가들이 그 게으르고 방탕하고 영리하고 유쾌한 선배 화가와 진토닉을 마시러 올 때면 으레 레베카에게 핑커튼 여사가 댁에 계시냐고 물어보곤 했다. 가엾게도, 그 여사는 화가들에게 로런스 씨나 웨스트 원장만큼이나 잘 알려진 사람이 되어 있었던 것이다! 한번은 레베카가 치즈윅에서 며칠을 보내는 영광을 누렸는데, 그 후 그녀는 제미마를 데려와 또 다른 인형을 제미 양이라며 세워 놓았다. 그 정직한 여인이 그녀에게 아이 셋이 먹어도 남을 만큼 젤리와 케이크를 만들어 주었고 헤어질 때 칠 실링짜리 동전까지 쥐여 주었건만, 이 소녀의 비웃는 감각은 고마워하는 마음보다 훨씬 더 강했던지라, 그녀는 그 언니 못지않게 가차 없이 제미 양을 제물로 삼았던 것이다.\n\n급기야 파국이 닥쳤고, 그녀는 자기 집으로 가듯이 그 거리로 보내졌다. 그곳의 엄격한 격식은 그녀를 숨 막히게 했다. 수도원 같은 규칙성으로 짜인 기도며 식사며 수업이며 산책이 그녀를 거의 견딜 수 없을 만큼 짓눌렀다. 그래서 그녀는 소호의 그 낡은 작업실에서 누리던 자유와 가난을 어찌나 애틋하게 돌아보았던지, 그녀 자신을 포함해 모두가 그녀가 아버지를 잃은 슬픔에 사무쳐 있다고 여길 정도였다. 그녀는 다락방의 작은 방을 썼는데, 하녀들은 밤이면 그녀가 서성이며 흐느끼는 소리를 들었다. 그러나 그것은 슬픔이 아니라 분노에서 나온 것이었다. 그녀는 그때까지는 그리 능숙한 위선자가 아니었으나, 이제는 외로움이 그녀에게 가식을 가르쳤던 것이다. 그녀는 여자들의 사교에 끼어 본 적이 없었다. 그녀의 아버지는 비록 막돼먹은 인간이었으나 재능 있는 사람이었고, 그의 대화는 이제 그녀가 마주하게 된 같은 성의 여자들이 나누는 이야기보다 천 배는 더 흥미로웠다. 거드름 피우는 늙은 여교장의 허영, 그 동생의 어리석은 사람 좋음, 나이 많은 여학생들의 시시한 잡담과 험담, 가정교사들의 싸늘한 모범 의식이 한결같이 그녀의 신경을 거슬렀다. 그리고 이 불운한 소녀에게는 부드러운 모성애라곤 없었으니, 그렇지만 않았더라면 주로 그녀가 돌보도록 맡겨진 어린아이들의 재잘거림과 수다가 그녀를 달래 주고 즐겁게 해 주었을지도 모른다. 그러나 그녀는 이 년이나 그들 틈에서 살았건만, 그녀가 떠난다고 아쉬워하는 아이는 하나도 없었다. 온화하고 마음씨 다정한 애밀리아 세들리만이 그녀가 조금이나마 정을 붙일 수 있는 유일한 사람이었다. 하기야 애밀리아에게 정을 붙이지 않을 사람이 어디 있겠는가?\n\n주변 젊은 여자들의 행복이—그 우월한 처지가—레베카에게 이루 말할 수 없는 질투의 고통을 안겼다. \"저 계집애가 백작의 손녀라고 어찌나 거드름을 피우는지.\" 그녀는 한 아이를 두고 그렇게 말했다. \"저 크리올 계집애 앞에서 십만 파운드 때문에 다들 어찌나 굽실거리며 알랑대는지! 난 저따위 것보다 천 배는 더 영리하고 매력적이야, 그 재산이 다 무슨 소용이람. 난 저 백작의 손녀 못지않게 잘 자랐어, 그 잘난 혈통이 다 무슨 소용이람. 그런데도 여기서는 다들 날 거들떠보지도 않아. 그렇지만 내가 아버지 집에 있을 때는, 남자들이 제일 화려한 무도회며 파티를 마다하고 나하고 저녁을 보내려 하지 않았던가?\" 그녀는 어찌 됐든 자기가 갇혀 있는 이 감옥에서 벗어나기로 작정하고, 이제 스스로를 위해 행동하기 시작했으며, 난생처음으로 앞날을 위한 짜임새 있는 계획을 세우기 시작했다.\n\n그리하여 그녀는 그곳이 제공하는 학습의 기회를 이용했다. 그녀는 이미 음악가이자 뛰어난 어학자였던지라, 그 시절 숙녀들에게 필요하다고 여겨지던 그 자그마한 학습 과정을 금세 마쳐 버렸다. 음악은 쉴 새 없이 연습했는데, 어느 날 여자아이들이 외출하고 그녀 혼자 집에 남아 있던 차에 한 곡을 어찌나 잘 연주하던지 그 소리를 엿들은 미네르바가 현명하게도 어린 학생들을 위한 교사 비용을 아낄 수 있겠다 싶어, 앞으로 그 아이들에게 음악을 가르치라고 샤프 양에게 일렀다.\n\n소녀는 거절했다. 난생처음으로, 그것도 그 위풍당당한 여교장을 깜짝 놀라게 하면서 말이다. \"저는 여기 아이들과 프랑스어로 말하러 온 거지,\" 레베카가 퉁명스레 말했다. \"음악을 가르쳐서 당신 돈을 아껴 주러 온 게 아니에요. 돈을 주시면 가르치죠.\"\n\n미네르바는 양보할 수밖에 없었고, 물론 그날부터 그녀를 미워했다. \"삼십오 년 동안,\" 그녀는 말했는데, 참으로 지당한 말이었다. \"내 집에서 감히 내 권위에 의문을 제기한 인간은 본 적이 없어. 내가 품 안에 독사를 길렀구나.\"\n\n\"독사라니—말도 안 되는 소리.\" 샤프 양이 놀라 거의 까무러칠 지경인 노부인에게 말했다. \"당신은 내가 쓸모 있어서 받아들인 거예요. 우리 사이에 감사 따위는 없어요. 난 이곳이 싫고, 떠나고 싶어요. 여기서는 꼭 해야 하는 일 말고는 아무것도 안 할 거예요.\"\n\n지금 핑커튼 여사에게 말하고 있다는 걸 아느냐고 노부인이 물어봐도 소용없었다. 레베카는 그 얼굴에 대고, 여교장이 거의 발작을 일으킬 만큼 끔찍하고 빈정대는 악마 같은 웃음을 터뜨렸다. \"목돈을 주고,\" 소녀가 말했다. \"저를 떼어 내세요. 아니면, 그게 더 좋으시다면, 귀족 가문에 가정교사 좋은 자리를 구해 주시든가요. 마음만 먹으면 하실 수 있잖아요.\" 그리고 그 후의 입씨름에서도 그녀는 늘 이 점으로 돌아왔다. \"자리를 구해 줘요. 우린 서로 미워하고, 난 떠날 준비가 됐어요.\"\n\n훌륭한 핑커튼 여사는 비록 로마식 코에 터번을 둘렀고 척탄병만큼 키가 컸으며 그때까지는 거역할 수 없는 공주님이었으나, 그 자그마한 견습생만 한 의지나 기력이 없었으니, 맞서 싸워도, 위세로 윽박지르려 해도 헛수고였다. 한번은 사람들 앞에서 그녀를 꾸짖으려 했다가, 레베카가 앞서 말한 그 수법대로 프랑스어로 대꾸하는 바람에 노부인은 완전히 무너지고 말았다. 자기 학원의 권위를 지키려면 이 반역자, 이 괴물, 이 뱀, 이 화근을 치워 버리는 수밖에 없었다. 그러던 차에 그 무렵 핏 크롤리 경의 집안에서 가정교사를 구한다는 소식을 듣자, 그녀는 화근이자 뱀인 샤프 양을 그 자리에 정말로 추천했던 것이다. \"분명히,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"나에게 한 짓 말고는 샤프 양의 처신에서 흠잡을 데를 찾을 수가 없고, 그 재능과 교양이 높은 수준임은 인정해야겠지. 적어도 머리로 따지자면, 그 애는 우리 학원에서 추구하는 교육 방식에 영예를 안겨 주는 인물이야.\"\n\n그리하여 여교장은 그 추천을 자기 양심과 화해시켰고, 계약서는 파기되었으며, 견습생은 자유로워졌다. 여기 몇 줄로 적어 놓은 이 싸움은 물론 몇 달에 걸쳐 이어진 것이었다. 그리고 세들리 양이 이제 열일곱 살이 되어 학교를 떠나려던 참이었고, 또 샤프 양과 우정을 나누고 있던 터라(\"그 점이,\" 미네르바는 말했다. \"애밀리아의 처신 가운데 그 스승의 마음에 차지 않는 유일한 대목이야\"), 샤프 양은 어느 개인 가정에 가정교사로 들어가는 임무를 시작하기 전에 친구의 초대를 받아 그 집에서 일주일을 보내게 되었다.\n\n이렇게 하여 이 두 젊은 숙녀에게 세상이 열리기 시작했다. 애밀리아에게 그것은 온통 꽃이 만발한, 더없이 새롭고 싱그럽고 찬란한 세상이었다. 레베카에게는 그다지 새로운 세상이 아니었으나—(사실 크리스프 사건에 관해 굳이 진실을 밝히자면, 그 파이 장수 여인이 누군가에게 귀띔하기를, 또 그 누군가가 그 사실을 다른 누군가에게 진술서까지 만들어 전하기를, 크리스프 씨와 샤프 양에 관해 세상에 알려진 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 일이 있었으며, 그의 편지는 또 다른 편지에 대한 답장이었다고 했다). 하지만 이 일의 진짜 진실을 누가 말해 줄 수 있겠는가? 어쨌든 레베카가 세상을 처음 시작하는 것은 아니었다 해도, 다시 시작하고 있는 것만은 분명했다.\n\n두 젊은 숙녀가 켄싱턴 통행료 징수소에 이르렀을 무렵, 애밀리아는 동무들을 잊지는 않았으나 눈물은 이미 거두었고, 마차를 타고 지나가다 그녀를 흘끗 본 어느 젊은 근위 기병 장교가 \"이런, 정말 멋진 아가씨로군!\" 하고 말하는 통에 얼굴을 잔뜩 붉히면서도 기뻐하고 있었다. 그리고 마차가 러셀 광장에 닿기도 전에, 알현실에 관해, 또 알현할 때 젊은 숙녀들이 후프 치마와 함께 머리에 분도 뿌리는지, 자기에게도 그런 영광이 주어질지에 관해 한참 이야기가 오갔다. 시장 주최 무도회에 가게 되리라는 것만은 그녀도 알고 있었다. 그리고 마침내 집에 다다르자, 애밀리아 세들리 양은 샘보의 팔에 의지해 폴짝 뛰어내렸으니, 런던이라는 큰 도시 안의 그 어느 아가씨 못지않게 행복하고 어여쁜 아가씨였다. 샘보도 마부도 이 점에는 의견이 일치했고, 그녀의 부모도, 또 어린 여주인을 맞으러 현관에 늘어서서 고개를 까딱이고 무릎을 굽혀 인사하며 웃음 짓던 집안 하인들 한 명 한 명도 다 그렇게 여겼다.\n\n그녀가 레베카에게 집안의 방이란 방을, 또 자기 서랍이란 서랍 속에 든 것이란 것은 죄다 구경시켜 주었으리라는 것은 짐작하고도 남으리라. 책이며 피아노며 드레스며, 그 온갖 목걸이며 브로치며 레이스며 자질구레한 장신구까지 말이다. 그녀는 레베카에게 흰 홍옥수 반지와 터키석 반지를, 또 이제는 자기에게는 작지만 친구에게는 꼭 맞을 어여쁜 잔무늬 모슬린 드레스를 한사코 받게 했다. 그리고 마음속으로는 어머니의 허락을 받아 자기 흰 캐시미어 숄을 친구에게 선물하기로 작정했다. 그것 하나쯤 못 내줄 것도 없지 않은가? 게다가 오라버니 조지프가 인도에서 그녀에게 막 두 장이나 가져다주지 않았던가?\n\n조지프 세들리가 누이에게 가져다준 그 두 장의 으리으리한 캐시미어 숄을 보았을 때, 레베카는 더없이 진심으로 \"오라버니가 있다는 건 정말 좋은 일이겠다\"고 말하고는, 친구도 친지도 없이 세상에 홀로 남은 고아라는 점을 내세워 마음씨 다정한 애밀리아의 동정을 쉽사리 얻어 냈다.\n\n\"혼자가 아니야.\" 애밀리아가 말했다. \"있잖아, 레베카, 나는 언제까지나 네 친구가 되어 줄 거고, 자매처럼 너를 사랑할 거야. 정말 그럴 거야.\"\n\n\"아, 하지만 너처럼 부모님이 계시다면—다정하고, 부유하고, 자애로운 부모님이, 네가 달라는 건 뭐든 다 주시고, 게다가 그 무엇보다도 소중한 사랑까지 주시잖아! 가엾은 우리 아빠는 내게 아무것도 못 주셨고, 난 세상에 옷이라곤 두 벌뿐이었어! 게다가 오라버니가, 사랑하는 오라버니가 있다니! 아, 넌 그분을 얼마나 사랑하겠니!\"\n\n애밀리아가 웃었다.\n\n\"아니! 넌 오라버니를 사랑하지 않아? 누구든 다 사랑한다는 네가?\"\n\n\"응, 당연히 사랑하지—다만—\"\n\n\"다만 뭐?\"\n\n\"다만 조지프 오라버니는 내가 자기를 사랑하든 말든 그다지 신경 쓰지 않는 것 같아. 십 년이나 떨어져 있다가 돌아와서는 악수하라고 손가락 두 개만 내미는걸! 무척 친절하고 좋은 분이지만, 나한테는 거의 말을 안 걸어. 내 생각엔 자기—보다 담뱃대를 훨씬 더 사랑하는 것 같아.\" 그러나 여기서 애밀리아는 입을 다물었다. 무엇 하러 오라버니를 험담하겠는가? \"어렸을 때는 나한테 무척 잘해 주셨어.\" 그녀가 덧붙였다. \"그분이 떠나실 때 나는 겨우 다섯 살이었거든.\"\n\n\"오라버니가 무척 부자 아니야?\" 레베카가 말했다. \"인도의 나리들은 다들 엄청난 부자라던데.\"\n\n\"수입이 아주 많은 것 같긴 해.\"\n\n\"그럼 올케언니는 착하고 예쁜 분이야?\"\n\n\"어머! 조지프 오라버니는 결혼 안 했어.\" 애밀리아가 다시 웃으며 말했다.\n\n어쩌면 그녀는 이미 그 사실을 레베카에게 말했는지도 모르나, 그 젊은 숙녀는 그걸 기억하는 기색이 전혀 없었다. 아니, 애밀리아의 조카들을 잔뜩 보게 되리라 기대하고 있었노라고 맹세하고 우겨 댔다. 세들리 씨가 결혼하지 않았다니 무척 실망스럽다는 것이었다. 분명 애밀리아가 그가 결혼했다고 말한 줄 알았다면서, 자기는 어린아이들을 그렇게도 좋아한다는 것이었다.\n\n\"치즈윅에서 애들이라면 실컷 겪지 않았어?\" 애밀리아가 친구의 그 느닷없는 다정함에 다소 의아해하며 말했다. 사실 훗날의 샤프 양이었다면 그 거짓이 그토록 쉽게 들통날 의견을 함부로 내세우는 따위의 실수는 결코 저지르지 않았을 것이다. 하지만 그녀가 아직 열아홉 살에 지나지 않으며, 속이는 기술에 서투른, 가엾고 순진한 사람이며, 제 몸으로 직접 경험을 쌓아 가는 중임을 우리는 기억해야 한다! 앞서 늘어놓은 일련의 물음이 이 영리한 젊은 여인의 마음속에서 번역된 뜻은 그저 이런 것이었다. \"조지프 세들리 씨가 부자에 미혼이라면, 내가 그와 결혼하지 못할 이유가 어디 있담? 시간이 고작 이 주뿐이긴 하지만, 한번 시도해 본다고 해될 것도 없지.\" 그리고 그녀는 마음속으로 이 갸륵한 시도를 해 보기로 작정했다. 그녀는 애밀리아를 더욱 살갑게 어루만지고, 흰 홍옥수 목걸이를 목에 걸면서 거기에 입을 맞추었으며, 결코, 결코 이것을 손에서 놓지 않겠노라고 맹세했다. 저녁 식사를 알리는 종이 울리자 그녀는 젊은 숙녀들의 버릇대로 친구의 허리에 팔을 두르고 아래층으로 내려갔다. 응접실 문 앞에서 어찌나 떨리던지 들어갈 용기를 좀처럼 낼 수 없었다. \"얘, 내 심장 좀 만져 봐, 어떻게 뛰는지!\" 그녀가 친구에게 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 안 뛰는데.\" 애밀리아가 말했다. \"들어가, 무서워하지 말고. 우리 아빠는 너를 어떻게 하지 않으셔.\"\n\n리베카, 적과 마주하다\n\n두 소녀가 들어섰을 때, 몹시 살집 좋고 부석부석한 한 남자가 난롯가에서 신문을 읽고 있었다. 사슴 가죽 바지에 헤시안 부츠를 신고, 거의 코까지 치솟은 여러 겹의 큼직한 목도리를 두르고, 붉은 줄무늬 조끼에 왕관 동전만큼이나 큼지막한 강철 단추가 달린 사과 빛 초록색 외투를 걸치고 있었으니(이는 그 시절 멋쟁이나 한량의 아침 차림새였다), 그는 이 출현에 화들짝 안락의자에서 튀어 일어나 얼굴이 새빨개지더니 거의 온 얼굴을 목도리 속에 파묻어 버렸다.\n\n\"오라버니 동생이에요, 조지프.\" 애밀리아가 웃으며, 그가 내민 두 손가락을 흔들며 말했다. \"이제 아주 돌아왔답니다. 그리고 이쪽은 제 친구 샤프 양인데, 제가 말씀드린 적 있죠.\"\n\n\"아니, 들은 적 없는데, 정말이야.\" 목도리 속의 머리통이 몹시 떨면서 말했다. \"그러니까, 그래, 날씨가 어찌나 지긋지긋하게 추운지요, 그—\" 그러더니 그는 유월 한복판인데도 온 힘을 다해 부지깽이로 불을 쑤셔 대기 시작했다.\n\n\"정말 미남이시구나.\" 리베카가 애밀리아에게 제법 큰 소리로 속삭였다.\n\n\"그렇게 생각해?\" 애밀리아가 말했다. \"그럼 오라버니한테 일러 줄게.\"\n\n\"얘! 무슨 일이 있어도 안 돼.\" 샤프 양이 새끼 사슴처럼 수줍게 흠칫 물러서며 말했다. 그녀는 앞서 그 신사에게 처녀답고 정중한 인사를 올렸고, 정숙한 두 눈이 어찌나 한결같이 양탄자만 응시하던지, 대체 어느 틈에 그를 볼 기회가 있었는지 신기할 따름이었다.\n\n\"아름다운 숄, 고마워요, 오라버니.\" 애밀리아가 부지깽이를 향해 말했다. \"정말 아름답지 않니, 리베카?\"\n\n\"오, 천상의 것이네!\" 샤프 양이 말하니, 그 두 눈이 양탄자에서 곧장 샹들리에로 올라갔다.\n\n조지프는 여전히 부지깽이와 부집게를 요란하게 달그락거리며 숨을 헐떡이고 씩씩거렸고, 누런 얼굴이 허락하는 한껏 새빨개졌다. \"난 너한테 이런 멋진 선물은 못 해 준단다, 조지프.\" 동생이 말을 이었다. \"하지만 학교에 있는 동안 오라버니 드리려고 아주 아름다운 멜빵 한 쌍을 수놓았어요.\"\n\n\"맙소사! 애밀리아.\" 오라버니가 정말로 기겁하며 외쳤다. \"그게 무슨 소리냐?\" 그러고는 온 힘을 다해 종 끈을 잡아당기는 바람에 그 가구 부속이 손에 쑥 빠져나와, 이 정직한 친구의 당혹스러움만 더하고 말았다. \"제발 내 마차가 문 앞에 와 있는지 좀 봐 다오. 못 기다린다. 가 봐야 해. 빌어먹을 마부 녀석 같으니. 가 봐야 해.\"\n\n바로 이 순간 이 집안의 가장이, 진정한 영국 상인답게 회중시계 줄 장식을 짤랑거리며 들어왔다. \"무슨 일이냐, 에미?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"조지프 오라버니가 자기—자기 버기가 문 앞에 와 있는지 봐 달래요. 버기가 뭐예요, 아버지?\"\n\n\"말 한 필이 끄는 가마 같은 거란다.\" 노신사가 말했는데, 그는 그 나름대로 익살꾼이었다.\n\n이에 조지프가 와락 미친 듯한 웃음을 터뜨렸으나, 샤프 양의 시선과 마주치자 마치 총에라도 맞은 듯 뚝 멈췄다.\n\n\"이 젊은 숙녀가 네 친구냐? 샤프 양, 만나서 정말 반갑소. 그래, 너랑 에미가 벌써 조지프와 다투기라도 했길래 이 녀석이 나가려는 거냐?\"\n\n\"제가 우리 부서의 보너미와 함께 식사하기로 약속했거든요, 아버지.\" 조지프가 말했다.\n\n\"이런! 어머니께 여기서 저녁을 들겠다고 하지 않았느냐?\"\n\n\"하지만 이런 차림으로는 어림없어요.\"\n\n\"이 녀석 좀 보시오, 어디 가서 식사한들 손색없을 만큼 미남이지 않소, 샤프 양?\"\n\n이에 샤프 양은 당연히 친구를 쳐다보았고, 두 사람은 와락 웃음을 터뜨렸으니, 노신사에게는 더할 나위 없이 흐뭇한 일이었다.\n\n\"핑커튼 여사 댁에서 저런 사슴 가죽 바지를 본 적이 있소?\" 그가 유리한 고지를 놓치지 않고 밀어붙였다.\n\n\"하늘에 맙소사! 아버지.\" 조지프가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그래, 이 녀석 기분을 상하게 했군. 여보, 부인, 내가 당신 아들 기분을 상하게 했소. 사슴 가죽 바지 얘기를 꺼냈으니 말이오. 안 그런지 샤프 양한테 물어보구려. 자, 조지프, 샤프 양과 화해하고 다 같이 저녁이나 들러 가자.\"\n\n\"필라우가 있단다, 조지프, 네가 좋아하는 그대로 말이다. 게다가 아버지가 빌링즈게이트에서 제일 좋은 가자미를 사 오셨어.\"\n\n\"자, 자, 어서, 샤프 양과 함께 아래층으로 내려가게나. 나는 이 두 부인을 데리고 뒤따라가겠네.\" 가장이 이렇게 말하고는 아내와 딸의 팔을 한쪽씩 끼고 흥겹게 걸어갔다.\n\n만약 리베카 샤프 양이 이 덩치 큰 멋쟁이를 정복하기로 마음먹었다 한들, 숙녀 여러분, 우리에게 그녀를 탓할 권리가 있다고는 생각지 않는다. 남편 사냥이라는 일은 대개 적당한 겸양과 함께 젊은 사람들이 자기 어머니에게 맡기는 법이지만, 샤프 양에게는 이 미묘한 일을 주선해 줄 다정한 부모가 없었음을, 그리고 그녀가 스스로 남편을 구하지 못한다면 이 넓은 세상천지에 그 수고를 대신 떠맡아 줄 사람이 아무도 없었음을 기억하라. 젊은 사람들이 사교계에 \"데뷔\"하게 만드는 것이 결혼이라는 고귀한 야망 말고 무엇이겠는가? 그들을 떼 지어 온천지로 몰려가게 하는 것은 무엇인가? 한 시즌 내내 죽을 둥 살 둥 새벽 다섯 시까지 춤추게 하는 것은 무엇인가? 그들로 하여금 피아노포르테 소나타를 익히느라 애쓰게 하고, 한 번 수업에 기니씩이나 받는 유행하는 선생에게서 노래 네 곡을 배우게 하고, 팔이 곱고 팔꿈치가 매끈하면 하프를 켜게 하고, 링컨 그린의 궁사 모자에 깃털을 꽂게 하는 것이 무엇이겠는가? 그 죽여주는 활과 화살로 어떤 \"탐나는\" 젊은 남자를 쏘아 떨어뜨리려는 속셈 말고 무엇이겠는가? 점잖은 부모들로 하여금 양탄자를 걷어 내고 집안을 발칵 뒤집으며 한 해 수입의 오분의 일을 무도회 만찬과 얼음 채운 샴페인에 쏟아붓게 하는 것은 무엇인가? 그게 순전히 같은 인류에 대한 사랑이며, 젊은이들이 행복하게 춤추는 모습을 보고 싶은 순수한 마음에서일까? 흥! 그들은 딸을 시집보내고 싶은 것이다. 정직한 세들리 부인이 그 다정한 마음 깊은 곳에서 이미 자기 애밀리아의 안착을 위해 자잘한 계략을 스무 가지나 짜 두었듯이, 우리의 사랑스럽지만 보호자 없는 리베카 역시 친구에게보다도 자신에게 더 절실히 필요한 그 남편을 손에 넣으려고 최선을 다하기로 작정한 것이었다. 그녀에게는 생생한 상상력이 있었다. 게다가 그녀는 「아라비안나이트」와 거스리의 「지리서」를 읽은 터였다. 그리하여 그녀가 저녁 식사를 위해 옷을 차려입는 동안, 그리고 애밀리아에게 오라버니가 아주 부자냐고 물어본 뒤에, 그녀는 스스로가 안주인이 되는 더없이 으리으리한 공중누각을 지어 올렸으니, 어딘가 배경에는 남편이 하나 있고(아직 그를 본 적이 없으니 그 모습이 그리 또렷하지는 않았다), 그녀는 숄과 터번과 다이아몬드 목걸이를 무수히 두르고 「푸른 수염」의 행진곡 가락에 맞춰 코끼리에 올라타고 대(大)무굴 황제에게 의례적 방문을 다니는 것이었다. 매혹적인 알나샤르의 환상이여! 그대를 지어 올리는 것은 젊음의 행복한 특권이니, 리베카 샤프 말고도 공상에 잠긴 숱한 젊은이들이 지금껏 이 즐거운 백일몽에 빠져들어 왔으리라!\n\n조지프 세들리는 누이동생 애밀리아보다 열두 살 위였다. 그는 동인도회사의 문관(文官)이었고, 우리가 글을 쓰는 이 시기에 그의 이름은 동인도 명부의 벵골 지부에 보글리 월라의 징세관으로 올라 있었으니, 누구나 알다시피 명예롭고 잇속 좋은 자리였다. 조지프가 그 후 이 회사에서 어떤 더 높은 자리에 올랐는지 알고 싶은 독자는 같은 간행물을 참고하시라.\n\n보글리 월라는 도요새 사냥으로 유명하고 심심찮게 호랑이를 몰아낼 수 있는, 멋지고 외지고 늪지투성이에 정글이 우거진 지역에 자리하고 있었다. 치안판사가 있는 람겅게가 겨우 사십 마일 떨어져 있고, 거기서 한 삼십 마일을 더 가면 기병대 주둔지가 있었다. 그가 징세관 자리를 떠맡았을 때 조지프는 부모에게 보낸 편지에 그렇게 적었다. 그는 일 년에 두 번, 그가 거둬들인 세금을 캘커타로 실어 가려고 파견대가 올 때를 빼고는 기독교도의 얼굴이라곤 거의 구경도 못 한 채, 이 매력적인 곳에서 팔 년 가까운 세월을 그야말로 홀로 살았다.\n\n다행히 이 무렵 그는 간장병에 걸렸고, 그것을 고치려고 유럽으로 돌아왔으니, 이 병은 고국에서 그에게 더없는 위안과 즐거움의 원천이 되었다. 그는 런던에 있는 동안 가족과 함께 살지 않고, 명랑한 젊은 독신 남자답게 자기만의 숙소를 따로 두었다. 인도로 가기 전에는 도회지 한량의 즐거운 쾌락을 누리기에 너무 어렸으나, 돌아온 뒤로는 상당한 열성으로 거기에 푹 빠져들었다. 그는 공원에서 말을 몰았고, 유행하는 선술집에서 식사했으며(오리엔탈 클럽이 아직 생기기 전이었다), 그 시절 유행대로 극장을 들락거리거나, 몸에 딱 붙는 타이츠에 삼각모를 갖춰 입고 애써 오페라에 모습을 드러내곤 했다.\n\n인도로 돌아간 뒤로도, 그리고 그 후로도 줄곧 그는 인생의 이 시기에 누린 쾌락을 무척 열을 올려 떠벌렸고, 자기와 브러멜이 당대의 으뜸가는 멋쟁이였다는 식으로 떠들어 댔다. 그러나 그는 여기 런던에서도 보글리 월라의 정글에서만큼이나 외로웠다. 이 대도시에서 그가 아는 사람이라곤 거의 한 명도 없었으니, 의사와 청환약 약병의 벗과 간장병이 없었더라면 그는 외로움에 죽고 말았을 것이다. 그는 게으르고, 까탈스럽고, 미식가였다. 숙녀의 출현은 그를 한없이 겁먹게 했으니, 그래서 그는 흥겨운 일이 가득하고 마음씨 좋은 늙은 아버지의 농담이 그의 자존심을 겁박하는 러셀 광장의 가족 모임에 좀처럼 끼지 않았던 것이다. 그의 비대한 몸집은 조지프에게 적잖은 근심과 불안을 안겨 주었다. 이따금 그는 넘쳐 나는 군살을 빼 보려고 필사적인 시도를 하곤 했으나, 그의 게으름과 잘 먹는 데 대한 사랑이 이내 그 개혁의 노력을 이기고 말아, 그는 어느새 다시 하루 세 끼를 챙겨 먹고 있는 자신을 발견했다. 그는 결코 옷을 잘 차려입은 적이 없었으나, 그 거대한 몸뚱이를 꾸미느라 엄청난 공을 들였고, 매일 그 일에 여러 시간을 쏟았다. 그의 시종은 그 의상실 덕에 한밑천 잡았고, 그의 화장대는 늙은 미인이 쓰던 만큼이나 많은 머릿기름과 향수로 뒤덮여 있었다. 그는 허리를 만들어 보려고 그 시절 발명된 온갖 복대며 코르셋이며 허리띠를 죄다 시험해 보았다. 대부분의 뚱뚱한 남자가 그러하듯, 그는 옷을 너무 꽉 끼게 지어 입었고, 옷이 더없이 화사한 빛깔에 젊은이다운 재단이 되도록 신경을 썼다. 마침내 차려입고 나면, 오후에 그는 공원으로 아무도 없이 홀로 마차 산책을 나섰고, 그러고는 다시 차려입으러 돌아왔다가 피아자 커피하우스로 가서 아무하고도 없이 홀로 저녁을 들었다. 그는 처녀처럼 허영이 심했다. 어쩌면 그의 극심한 수줍음도 극심한 허영의 한 결과였는지 모른다. 만약 리베카 양이 사교계에 첫발을 들이자마자 이 남자를 손에 넣을 수 있다면, 그녀는 예사롭지 않은 영리함을 지닌 젊은이일 것이다.\n\n첫수부터 그녀는 상당한 솜씨를 보였다. 세들리를 아주 잘생긴 남자라고 말했을 때, 그녀는 애밀리아가 그 말을 어머니에게 전하고, 어머니는 아마 조지프에게 전하리라는 것을, 아니 적어도 아들에게 바쳐진 그 찬사에 어머니가 기뻐하리라는 것을 알았던 것이다. 어머니란 다 그런 법이다. 시코락스에게 그녀의 아들 캘리밴이 아폴로만큼 잘생겼다고 말했다면, 마녀인 그녀조차 기뻐했을 것이다. 게다가 어쩌면 조지프 세들리가 그 칭찬을 엿들었을지도 모르고—리베카는 들릴 만큼 충분히 큰 소리로 말했다—실제로 그는 들었다. 그리하여 (속으로 자기가 아주 근사한 남자라고 여기던) 그의 거대한 몸뚱이는 그 칭찬에 온 신경이 짜릿하게 떨려 와 쾌감에 저렸다. 그러나 곧이어 반동이 왔다. \"이 처녀가 날 놀리는 건가?\" 그는 생각했고, 곧장 종 쪽으로 튀어 가서, 앞서 본 대로 물러나려 했으나, 아버지의 농담과 어머니의 간청에 멈칫하여 그 자리에 머물게 되었던 것이다. 그는 미심쩍고 동요하는 마음으로 그 젊은 숙녀를 데리고 아래층 식사 자리로 내려갔다. \"이 처녀가 정말 날 잘생겼다고 여기는 걸까,\" 그는 생각했다. \"아니면 그저 날 놀리는 건가?\" 우리는 조지프 세들리가 처녀처럼 허영이 심하다고 말한 바 있다. 맙소사! 처녀들이 입장을 뒤집어 자기네 동성 가운데 누군가를 두고 \"쟤는 남자처럼 허영이 심해\"라고 말하기만 한다면, 그 말은 더없이 지당할 것이다. 턱수염 난 족속들도 세상 어느 요염한 여자 못지않게 칭찬에 안달이고, 못지않게 몸치장에 까탈스럽고, 못지않게 제 외모를 뽐내며, 못지않게 제 매혹의 힘을 의식하는 것이다.\n\n그리하여 두 사람은 아래층으로 내려갔다. 조지프는 얼굴이 새빨개져 홍조를 띠었고, 리베카는 더없이 정숙하게 그 초록빛 두 눈을 내리뜨고 있었다. 그녀는 흰옷을 입었고, 드러난 두 어깨는 눈처럼 희었으니—젊음과, 보호받지 못하는 순결과, 겸손한 처녀의 순박함 그 자체였다. \"아주 얌전하게 굴어야지,\" 리베카는 생각했다. \"그리고 인도 얘기에 무척 관심 있는 척해야지.\"\n\n자, 우리는 세들리 부인이 아들이 좋아하는 그대로 멋진 카레를 그를 위해 마련해 두었다는 이야기를 들은 바 있는데, 저녁 식사 도중에 이 요리 한 접시가 리베카에게도 권해졌다. \"이게 뭔가요?\" 그녀가 애원하는 눈빛을 조지프 쪽으로 돌리며 말했다.\n\n\"기막히지요.\" 그가 말했다. 입에는 카레가 가득했고, 얼굴은 게걸스레 먹어 대는 그 즐거운 운동으로 한껏 붉어져 있었다. \"어머니, 인도에서 제가 직접 만들던 카레만큼이나 맛있는데요.\"\n\n\"오, 인도 요리라면 저도 좀 먹어 봐야겠어요.\" 리베카 양이 말했다. \"거기서 온 거라면 무엇이든 분명 좋을 거예요.\"\n\n\"샤프 양한테 카레 좀 드리렴, 얘야.\" 세들리 씨가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n리베카는 그 음식을 한 번도 맛본 적이 없었다.\n\n\"인도에서 온 다른 모든 것만큼이나 좋소?\" 세들리 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 일품이에요!\" 카옌 고추 때문에 고문 같은 고통을 겪으며 리베카가 말했다.\n\n\"고추를 곁들여 드셔 보세요, 샤프 양.\" 정말로 관심을 보이며 조지프가 말했다.\n\n\"고추요.\" 리베카가 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다. \"오, 좋아요!\" 그녀는 그 이름이 시사하듯 고추가 뭔가 시원한 것이리라 여겼고, 그래서 고추 몇 개를 받았다.* \"어쩜 이렇게 싱싱하고 푸르네요.\" 그녀가 그렇게 말하며 하나를 입에 넣었다. 그것은 카레보다 더 매웠다. 살과 피로 된 인간으로서는 더 이상 견딜 수가 없었다. 그녀는 포크를 내려놓았다. \"물 좀, 제발, 물 좀!\" 그녀가 외쳤다. 세들리 씨가 와락 웃음을 터뜨렸다(그는 증권거래소 출신의 거친 남자였는데, 그곳 사람들은 온갖 종류의 짓궂은 장난을 좋아한다). \"정말 인도 본토 것이오, 장담하리다.\" 그가 말했다. \"샘보, 샤프 양께 물 좀 갖다 드려라.\"\n\n아버지의 웃음에 조지프도 메아리쳤으니, 그는 그 장난이 기막히다고 여겼다. 부인들은 그저 살짝 미소 지을 뿐이었다. 그들은 가엾은 리베카가 너무 심한 고생을 한다고 여겼다. 그녀는 늙은 세들리의 목이라도 조르고 싶은 심정이었으나, 앞서 그 끔찍한 카레를 삼켰던 만큼이나 잘도 그 굴욕을 삼켰고, 입을 뗄 수 있게 되자마자 익살스럽고 너그러운 태도로 말했다. \"「아라비안나이트」에서 페르시아 공주가 크림 타르트에 넣는 후추를 기억했어야 했는데. 어르신, 인도에서는 크림 타르트에 카옌을 넣나요?\"\n\n늙은 세들리는 웃기 시작했고, 리베카가 너그러운 처녀라고 여겼다. 조지프는 그저 이렇게 말했다. \"크림 타르트요, 양? 벵골에서는 크림이 아주 형편없습니다. 우리는 대개 염소젖을 쓰는데, 이거 참, 그거 아세요, 제가 그쪽을 더 좋아하게 됐지 뭡니까!\"\n\n\"이제는 인도에서 온 거라고 죄다 좋아할 수는 없겠지요, 샤프 양.\" 노신사가 말했다. 그러나 저녁 식사 후 부인들이 물러가자, 이 능청맞은 노인은 아들에게 말했다. \"조심해라, 조. 저 처녀가 너한테 눈독을 들이고 있어.\"\n\n\"흥! 말도 안 돼요!\" 조가 한껏 우쭐해져서 말했다. \"기억하시는지요, 아버지, 덤덤에 한 처녀가 있었지요. 포병대 커틀러의 딸인데, 나중에 외과의 랜스와 결혼했지요. 그 처녀가 4년에 저한테 작정하고 덤벼들었지 뭡니까—저랑 멀리거토니한테요, 저녁 식사 전에 말씀드린 그 친구 말이에요—멀리거토니는 정말 끝내주게 좋은 친구지요—버지버지의 치안판사인데, 오 년 안에 틀림없이 참사회에 들어갈 겁니다. 그래, 아버지, 포병대가 무도회를 열었는데, 국왕 직속 14연대의 퀸틴이 저한테 그러더군요. '세들리,' 그러는 거예요, '우기 오기 전에 소피 커틀러가 자네나 멀리거토니 둘 중 하나를 낚는다는 데 십 대 십삼으로 걸지.' '좋아,' 제가 그랬지요. 그래서, 이런 참—이 클라레, 아주 좋은데요. 애덤슨 거예요, 아니면 카보넬 거예요?\"\n\n가벼운 코 고는 소리만이 유일한 대답이었다. 정직한 주식 중개인은 잠들어 있었으니, 그리하여 조지프의 나머지 이야기는 그날은 묻혀 버리고 말았다. 그러나 그는 남자들끼리의 술자리에서는 늘 입이 무척 헤펐으니, 약제사 골롭 박사가 간장과 청환약에 관해 물으러 올 때마다 이 즐거운 이야기를 수십 번이고 들려주곤 했다.\n\n병자인지라 조지프 세들리는 저녁 식사 때 마데이라주(酒) 말고는 클라레 한 병으로 만족했고, 거기에 딸기와 크림을 두 접시나 가득 비우고, 곁의 접시에 손도 안 댄 채 놓여 있던 자그마한 라우트 케이크 스물네 개를 해치웠다. 그리고 (소설가에게는 모든 것을 알 특권이 있으니) 그가 위층의 그 처녀를 두고 무던히도 생각에 잠겼던 것은 분명하다. \"참하고, 발랄하고, 명랑한 처녀로군,\" 그는 속으로 생각했다. \"식사 때 내가 손수건을 주워 줄 때 날 어찌나 쳐다보던지! 두 번이나 떨어뜨렸지. 응접실에서 노래하는 게 누구지? 이런! 올라가서 봐 볼까?\"\n\n그러나 그의 수줍음이 걷잡을 수 없는 기세로 그에게 밀려들었다. 아버지는 잠들어 있고, 모자는 현관에 있고, 사우샘프턴 거리 바로 옆에는 삯마차 한 대가 서 있었다. \"「사십인의 도둑」이나 보러 가야지,\" 그가 말했다. \"그리고 디캠프 양의 춤도.\" 그러고는 그는 부츠의 뾰족한 발끝으로 살그머니 빠져나가, 훌륭한 부모님을 깨우지 않고 사라져 버렸다.\n\n\"저기 조지프가 가네.\" 애밀리아가 응접실의 열린 창밖을 내다보며 말했다. 그동안 리베카는 피아노 앞에서 노래를 부르고 있었다.\n\n\"샤프 양이 그 애를 겁줘서 쫓아버렸군요.\" 세들리 부인이 말했다. \"가엾은 조, 어쩜 저렇게 수줍음이 많을까요?\"\n\n가엾은 조의 공황 상태는 이삼 일 동안 이어졌다. 그동안 그는 그 집에 발걸음을 하지 않았고, 그 기간 내내 레베카 양도 그의 이름을 입에 올리지 않았다. 그녀는 세들리 부인에게 더없이 깍듯한 감사를 표했고, 바자회에서는 이루 말할 수 없이 즐거워했으며, 마음씨 고운 부인이 데려가 준 극장에서는 경이로움의 소용돌이에 휩싸였다. 어느 날 애밀리아가 두통이 나서, 두 젊은이가 초대받은 어느 유흥 모임에 갈 수 없게 되었다. 그러자 무슨 수를 써도 그녀의 친구는 애밀리아 없이 가려 하지 않았다. \"뭐라고요! 이 가엾은 고아에게 생애 처음으로 행복과 사랑이 무엇인지 알려 준 당신을 두고 가라고요? 절대로요!\" 그러면서 초록빛 눈동자가 하늘을 우러러보며 눈물로 가득 찼다. 세들리 부인은 딸의 친구가 정말이지 사랑스럽고 다정한 마음씨를 지녔음을 인정하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n세들리 씨의 농담으로 말하자면, 레베카는 거기에 어찌나 진심 어린 웃음을 거듭 터뜨리던지, 그 마음씨 좋은 신사를 적잖이 기쁘게 하고 마음을 누그러뜨렸다. 샤프 양이 호감을 얻은 것은 비단 그 집안의 가장들에게만이 아니었다. 그녀는 가정부의 방에서 한창 진행 중이던 산딸기 잼 졸이는 일에 더없이 깊은 관심을 보여 블렌킨솝 부인의 환심을 샀고, 흑인 하인 샘보를 한사코 \"나리\", \"샘보 나리\"라고 불러 그 하인을 흐뭇하게 했으며, 감히 종을 울려 수고를 끼쳐 미안하다고 시녀에게 어찌나 상냥하고 겸손하게 사과를 하던지, 하인들 방까지도 응접실 못지않게 그녀에게 매료되었다.\n\n한번은 애밀리아가 학교에서 보내온 그림 몇 점을 들춰 보다가, 레베카가 느닷없이 어떤 그림 한 점을 발견하고는 눈물을 왈칵 쏟으며 방을 뛰쳐나간 적이 있었다. 그날은 조 세들리가 두 번째로 모습을 드러낸 날이었다.\n\n애밀리아는 이 감정의 표출이 무슨 까닭인지 알아보려고 친구를 뒤쫓아 갔고, 그 마음씨 고운 소녀는 친구를 데려오지 못한 채 자기도 다소 감정이 북받친 채로 돌아왔다. \"엄마, 있잖아요, 그 애 아버지가 치즈윅에서 우리 그림 선생님이셨잖아요. 늘 우리 그림에서 제일 잘된 부분들을 손봐 주셨고요.\"\n\n\"얘야! 핑커튼 여사님 말씀으로는 분명 그분이 손을 댄 게 아니라 그저 표구만 해 주셨다고 들었던 것 같은데.\" \"표구라고 했어요, 엄마. 레베카는 그 그림이며 자기 아버지가 거기 매달려 작업하시던 게 기억나는데, 그 생각이 그만 불쑥 떠오르는 바람에, 그러니까, 그 애가……\"\n\n\"가엾은 것, 정이 많은 아이로구나.\" 세들리 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그 애가 우리랑 한 주만 더 있을 수 있으면 좋겠어요.\" 애밀리아가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 덤덤에서 만나곤 했던 커틀러 양이랑 어쩌면 그렇게 똑 닮았는지, 다만 그 애가 더 살결이 흴 뿐이야. 그 양반은 지금 포병대 군의관 랜스랑 결혼했지. 그런데 어머니, 그거 아세요? 한번은 14연대의 퀸틴이 나한테 내기를 걸었는데……\"\n\n\"아유, 조지프, 그 이야기는 우리도 알아요.\" 애밀리아가 웃으며 말했다. \"그 얘기는 그만하시고, 그것보다 엄마를 설득해서 가엾은 레베카가 좀 더 머물 수 있게 그 무슨 크롤리 경한테 휴가 연장을 부탁하는 편지를 써 주세요. 저기 오네요, 눈이 울어서 빨개진 채로요.\"\n\n\"이제 좀 괜찮아졌어요.\" 그 소녀가 더없이 사랑스러운 미소를 지으며, 마음씨 고운 세들리 부인이 내민 손을 잡고 공손히 입을 맞추며 말했다. \"다들 어쩜 이렇게 저한테 잘해 주시는지! 다들요.\" 그녀가 웃으며 덧붙였다. \"조지프 씨, 당신만 빼고요.\"\n\n\"나라고요!\" 조지프가 당장이라도 자리를 뜰 궁리를 하며 말했다. \"맙소사! 이런! 샤프 양!\"\n\n\"네. 어쩌면 그렇게 모질게도 우리가 처음 만난 그날 저녁 식사에서 저더러 그 끔찍한 후추 요리를 먹게 만드실 수가 있었어요? 당신은 사랑스러운 애밀리아만큼 저한테 잘해 주지 않는걸요.\"\n\n\"오빠는 너를 그만큼 잘 몰라서 그래.\" 애밀리아가 외쳤다.\n\n\"누구든 너한테 잘해 주지 않을 사람은 없을 거다, 얘야.\" 그녀의 어머니가 말했다.\n\n\"그 카레는 일품이었어요. 정말이지 그랬다고요.\" 조가 자못 진지하게 말했다. \"어쩌면 레몬즙이 좀 모자랐는지도—아니, 모자라지 않았어요.\"\n\n\"그럼 고추는요?\"\n\n\"이런, 그것 때문에 당신이 어찌나 비명을 질러 댔는지!\" 그 상황의 우스꽝스러움에 사로잡힌 조가 웃음을 터뜨리며 말하더니, 늘 그렇듯 이내 뚝 그쳐 버렸다.\n\n\"다음번엔 절대로 당신더러 제 음식을 골라 달라고 하지 않게 조심해야겠어요.\" 그들이 다시 저녁을 먹으러 내려가며 레베카가 말했다. \"남자들이 가엾고 죄 없는 여자애를 괴롭히는 걸 좋아하는 줄은 미처 몰랐네요.\"\n\n\"맹세코, 레베카 양, 난 무슨 일이 있어도 당신을 다치게 하진 않을 거요.\"\n\n\"네.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그러지 않으실 줄 알아요.\" 그러고는 그 작은 손으로 그에게 더없이 부드럽게 힘을 주어 누르더니, 화들짝 놀란 듯 손을 거두고는, 먼저 한순간 그의 얼굴을 올려다보았다가 이내 카펫 고정봉으로 눈길을 떨어뜨렸다. 그러니 이 순박한 소녀가 보인 이 작고 무심결의, 수줍고도 다정한 호의의 몸짓에 조의 심장이 쿵쾅거리지 않았다고는 나로서는 단언할 수 없는 노릇이다.\n\n그것은 한 걸음 다가선 것이었고, 그런 까닭에 더없이 올바르고 품위 있다고 자부하는 어떤 숙녀들은 이 행동을 정숙하지 못하다고 비난할지도 모른다. 그러나 보다시피 가엾은 레베카는 이 모든 일을 스스로 해내야 하는 처지였다. 아무리 우아한들 너무 가난해서 하인을 둘 수 없는 사람은 제 방을 손수 쓸어야 하는 법이다. 사랑스러운 아가씨에게 젊은 남자와 일을 매듭지어 줄 사랑스러운 엄마가 없다면, 그 아가씨는 스스로 그 일을 해야 하는 법이다. 그러니 아, 이 여인들이 그 힘을 더 자주 휘두르지 않는다는 것은 얼마나 다행한 일인가! 만일 그들이 그 힘을 쓴다면 우리는 도무지 당해 낼 재간이 없으니 말이다. 그들이 아주 조금이라도 끌리는 기색을 내비치기만 하면 남자들은 당장 무릎을 꿇는다. 늙었든 못생겼든 매한가지다. 그리고 나는 이것을 명백한 진리로 적어 둔다. 웬만한 기회를 갖춘 여자라면, 그것도 등에 두드러진 혹만 없다면, 자기가 원하는 남자라면 누구든 결혼할 수 있는 것이다. 다만 이 사랑스러운 존재들이 들판의 짐승들처럼 제 힘을 알지 못한다는 것에 감사할 따름이다. 만일 그들이 그것을 안다면 우리를 완전히 정복하고 말 것이다.\n\n\"세상에!\" 조지프가 식당으로 들어서며 생각했다. \"딱 덤덤에서 커틀러 양과 있을 때 같은 기분이 들기 시작하는걸.\" 샤프 양은 저녁 식탁에서 음식을 두고 절반은 다정하고 절반은 익살스러운 사랑스러운 응석을 그에게 숱하게 부렸다. 이즈음 그녀는 이미 그 집안과 상당히 허물없는 사이가 되어 있었고, 두 아가씨로 말하자면 자매처럼 서로를 아꼈다. 결혼 안 한 젊은 처녀들이란 열흘만 한집에 함께 지내면 으레 그렇게 되는 법이다.\n\n마치 무슨 수를 써서라도 레베카의 계획을 진척시키려고 작정이라도 한 듯이—애밀리아가 한 일이라곤 다름 아니라 지난 부활절 휴가 때 오빠가 한 약속을 일깨워 준 것이었으니, \"내가 학교 다니는 계집애였을 때 말이에요.\" 그녀가 웃으며 말했다. 다름 아니라 그, 조지프가 자기를 보크홀에 데려가 주겠다고 한 약속이었다. \"이제 레베카가 우리와 함께 있으니, 지금이 바로 딱 좋은 때예요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"어머, 신난다!\" 레베카가 손뼉을 치려다가, 정숙한 여인답게 제풀에 정신을 차리고 멈칫했다.\n\n\"오늘 밤은 갈 때가 아니지.\" 조가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 내일이요.\"\n\n\"내일은 네 아버지하고 내가 밖에서 저녁을 먹는단다.\" 세들리 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"설마 내가 간다고 생각하는 건 아니겠지, 세들리 부인?\" 그녀의 남편이 말했다. \"당신 나이에 그 덩치로, 그렇게 끔찍하게 눅눅한 곳에서 감기에 걸리란 말이오?\"\n\n\"애들한테는 누가 같이 있어 줘야지요.\" 세들리 부인이 외쳤다.\n\n\"조더러 가라지.\" 그녀의 남편이 웃으며 말했다. \"충분히 컸으니까.\" 이 말에 식기대 앞에 서 있던 샘보까지 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 가엾은 뚱뚱보 조는 그야말로 아비를 죽이고 싶은 심정이 되었다.\n\n\"코르셋을 풀어 줘라!\" 인정사정없는 노신사가 계속 말했다. \"샤프 양, 얼굴에 물이나 좀 끼얹어 주든지, 아니면 위층으로 데리고 올라가구려. 이 사랑스러운 양반이 기절하시는구먼. 가엾은 희생양 같으니! 위층으로 모셔 가요. 깃털처럼 가벼우니까!\"\n\n\"이걸 참으면, 제가, 제가 사람이 아닙니다!\" 조지프가 고함을 질렀다.\n\n\"샘보, 조스 도련님 코끼리를 대령하렷다!\" 아버지가 외쳤다. \"엑서터 체인지로 사람을 보내라, 샘보.\" 그러나 화가 치밀어 거의 울 지경이 된 조스를 보더니, 이 늙은 익살꾼은 웃음을 멈추고 아들에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"증권 거래소에서야 다 정당한 거래지, 조스. 그리고 샘보, 코끼리는 됐고, 나하고 조스 도련님한테 샴페인이나 한 잔씩 다오. 보니 그 작자라도 제 술 창고에 이런 건 없을 게다, 얘야!\"\n\n샴페인 한 잔이 조지프의 평정을 되찾아 주었고, 환자랍시고 그가 삼분의 이를 마셔 버린 그 병이 비기도 전에, 그는 두 아가씨를 보크홀에 데려가기로 합의했다.\n\n\"여자애들한테는 각각 신사가 하나씩 붙어야지.\" 노신사가 말했다. \"조스는 여기 샤프 양한테 정신이 팔려서 분명 사람들 틈에 에미를 내버려 둘 게 뻔하니까. 96번지로 사람을 보내서 조지 오스본더러 와 줄 수 있는지 물어보거라.\"\n\n이 말에, 무슨 영문인지 나로서는 도무지 알 수 없지만, 세들리 부인이 남편을 바라보며 웃었다. 세들리 씨의 두 눈은 형언할 수 없으리만치 짓궂게 반짝였고, 그는 애밀리아를 바라보았다. 그러자 애밀리아는 고개를 떨군 채, 열일곱 살 아가씨만이 짓는 법을 아는 그런 식으로 얼굴을 붉혔으니, 레베카 샤프 양은 평생 그렇게 얼굴을 붉혀 본 적이 없었다—적어도 여덟 살 적, 대모에게 찬장에서 잼을 훔쳐 먹다 들켰던 그 이후로는 말이다. \"애밀리아가 쪽지를 쓰는 게 좋겠구나.\" 그녀의 아버지가 말했다. \"우리가 핑커튼 여사님 학원에서 얼마나 아름다운 필체를 배워 왔는지 조지 오스본한테 보여 주려무나. 에미, 그 애한테 십이야에 오라고 편지 쓰면서 'twelfth'를 f 없이 적었던 거 기억하니?\"\n\n\"그건 한참 전 일이잖아요.\" 애밀리아가 말했다.\n\n\"꼭 어제 일 같지 않소, 존?\" 세들리 부인이 남편에게 말했다. 그리고 그날 밤, 이층 앞방에서 나눈 한 대화 속에서—그 방은 화려하고 기묘한 인도풍 무늬의 사라사 천을 두르고 그 안쪽으로 연한 장밋빛 옥양목을 덧대어 일종의 천막처럼 꾸며져 있었고, 그 천막 같은 것 안에는 깃털 침대가 있었으며, 그 위에는 베개 두 개가, 그 위에는 둥글고 불그레한 두 얼굴이 놓여 있었으니, 하나는 레이스 달린 나이트캡을, 다른 하나는 끝에 술이 달린 수수한 무명 모자를 쓰고 있었다—그러니까 이 '잠자리 잔소리' 속에서, 세들리 부인은 가엾은 조에게 모질게 군 일을 두고 남편을 나무랐다.\n\n\"정말이지 당신도 너무하셨어요, 세들리 씨.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그 가엾은 애를 그렇게나 들들 볶다니요.\"\n\n\"여보.\" 무명 술 모자가 자기 행동을 변호하며 말했다. \"조스는 당신이 평생 그랬던 것보다 훨씬 더 허영이 심한 녀석이오. 이건 보통 말이 아니지. 하긴 한 삼십 년 전, 천칠백팔십—그게 몇 년이었더라?—그 무렵에야 당신도 우쭐할 만했을지 모르겠소. 아니라고는 안 하겠소. 하지만 조스와 그 멋쟁이 티 내는 수줍음에는 도무지 참을 수가 없단 말이오. 그건 요셉보다 더 요셉다운 척하는 짓이오, 여보. 그러면서 내내 그 녀석은 제 생각만 하면서, 자기가 얼마나 잘난 사내인지에만 빠져 있다오. 여보, 우리가 그 애 때문에 골치깨나 썩게 될 것 같소. 보시오, 에미의 그 어린 친구가 죽기 살기로 그 녀석한테 추파를 던지고 있잖소. 그건 너무도 빤한 일이지. 저 애가 그 녀석을 낚지 못하면 다른 누군가가 낚을 거요. 내가 날마다 증권 거래소에 나가게 되어 있듯이, 그 사내는 여자의 먹잇감이 될 운명이라오. 그 녀석이 우리한테 흑인 며느리를 데려오지 않은 것만도 다행이오, 여보. 하지만 내 말 새겨들으시오. 그 녀석한테 처음 낚싯대를 던지는 여자가 그 녀석을 낚고 말 거요.\"\n\n\"내일로 당장 내보내야겠어요, 그 음흉한 계집애를.\" 세들리 부인이 잔뜩 기를 쓰며 말했다.\n\n\"다른 누구라고 안 될 거 뭐 있소, 세들리 부인? 그 애가 그래도 살결은 흰 얼굴이잖소. 누가 그 녀석과 결혼하든 난 상관없소. 조더러 제 좋을 대로 하라지.\"\n\n이윽고 두 사람의 목소리가 잦아들거나, 부드럽지만 결코 낭만적이지 않은 코의 음악으로 바뀌었다. 그리하여 교회 종이 시간을 알리고 야경꾼이 그것을 외칠 때를 빼면, 러셀 광장과 증권 거래소의 존 세들리 귀하 댁은 온통 고요했다.\n\n아침이 오자, 마음씨 고운 세들리 부인은 더 이상 샤프 양에 관한 자신의 으름장을 실행에 옮길 생각을 하지 않았다. 어머니의 질투란 더없이 날카롭고, 더없이 흔하며, 더없이 정당한 것이긴 하나, 그토록 작고 비천하고 고분고분하고 온순한 가정교사가 보글리 월라의 징세관이라는 그토록 으리으리한 인물을 감히 우러러볼 엄두를 내리라고는 도무지 상상할 수가 없었던 것이다. 게다가 그 아가씨의 휴가를 연장해 달라는 청원서도 이미 발송된 터라, 그녀를 느닷없이 내쫓을 구실을 찾기란 어려운 노릇이었다.\n\n그리고 마치 만물이 온순한 레베카의 편을 들기로 모의라도 한 듯이, 자연의 요소들마저(처음에 그녀는 그것들이 자기를 위해 나섰음을 인정하려 들지 않았지만) 끼어들어 그녀를 거들었다. 보크홀 모임으로 정해진 날 저녁, 조지 오스본이 저녁을 먹으러 왔고, 그 집의 어른들은 초대받은 대로 하이버리 반에서 볼스 시의원과 저녁을 먹으러 떠난 뒤였는데, 오로지 보크홀에 가는 날 밤에만 일어날 법한 그런 천둥 번개를 동반한 폭풍우가 닥쳐와 젊은이들이 어쩔 수 없이 집에 머물러야 했던 것이다. 오스본 씨는 이 일에 조금도 실망한 기색이 아니었다. 그와 조지프 세들리는 식당에서 단둘이 적당한 양의 포도주를 마셨고, 그 술을 마시는 동안 세들리는 자기의 가장 멋진 인도 이야기들을 한 보따리 풀어놓았다. 그는 남자들끼리 있을 때면 더없이 말이 많았기 때문이다. 그 뒤에는 애밀리아 세들리 양이 응접실에서 손님을 접대했고, 이 네 젊은이는 함께 어찌나 아늑한 저녁을 보냈던지, 보크홀 나들이를 미루게 만든 그 천둥 폭풍우가 차라리 다행스럽다고들 입을 모았다.\n\n오스본은 세들리의 대자였고, 지난 스물세 해 동안 줄곧 이 집안의 일원이나 다름없었다. 생후 육 주에 그는 존 세들리에게서 은잔을 선물로 받았고, 생후 육 개월에는 금 호루라기와 방울이 달린 산호를 받았다. 어린 시절 내내 크리스마스 때면 노신사에게서 어김없이 용돈을 \"받았다.\" 그리고 학교로 돌아갈 때면, 조지프 세들리가 덩치 크고 으스대는 멋대가리 없는 풋내기였고 조지가 열 살배기 건방진 개구쟁이였던 시절, 그에게 두들겨 맞았던 일을 그는 똑똑히 기억했다. 한마디로, 조지는 그런 날마다의 친절과 왕래로 빚어질 수 있는 만큼 그 집안과 허물없는 사이였다.\n\n\"세들리, 기억나요? 내가 당신 헤시안 부츠의 술을 잘라 버렸을 때 당신이 얼마나 펄펄 뛰었는지, 그리고 그—흠!—애밀리아가 무릎을 꿇고 오빠 조스한테 어린 조지를 때리지 말라고 울며 빌어서 날 매질에서 구해 줬던 거?\"\n\n조스는 이 잊지 못할 사건을 똑똑히 기억했지만, 까맣게 잊어버렸노라고 우겨 댔다.\n\n\"그럼, 당신이 인도로 떠나기 전에 이륜마차를 타고 스위시테일 박사님 학교로 날 보러 와서, 반 기니랑 머리 한 번 쓰다듬어 주고 갔던 건 기억나요? 난 늘 당신이 적어도 칠 피트는 될 거라고 생각했는데, 인도에서 돌아온 당신이 나보다 키가 크지도 않은 걸 보고 어찌나 놀랐는지.\"\n\n\"세들리 씨가 당신 학교에까지 찾아가서 용돈을 주시다니 어쩜 그렇게 자상하실까!\" 레베카가 더없이 기쁜 어조로 외쳤다.\n\n\"그래요, 그것도 내가 그 양반 부츠 술을 잘라 버린 뒤에 말이오. 사내애들은 학교에서 받은 그런 용돈도, 준 사람도 결코 잊지 않는 법이지.\"\n\n\"전 헤시안 부츠가 참 좋더라고요.\" 레베카가 말했다. 제 다리를 엄청나게 자랑스러워해서 늘 이 멋들어진 신발을 신고 다니던 조스 세들리는, 비록 그 신발을 신은 채 다리를 의자 밑으로 끌어들이긴 했어도, 이 말에 더없이 흐뭇해했다.\n\n\"샤프 양!\" 조지 오스본이 말했다. \"당신처럼 그토록 솜씨 좋은 화가시라면, 그 부츠 장면을 멋진 역사화로 그려야 해요. 세들리는 사슴 가죽 바지를 입고 한 손에는 그 수난당한 부츠 한 짝을 들고 있는 모습으로 그리고, 다른 한 손으로는 내 셔츠 주름 장식을 움켜쥐고 있게 하는 거죠. 애밀리아는 그 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 작은 두 손을 치켜든 채로 그리고요. 그리고 『메둘라』나 철자 교본의 권두 삽화처럼, 그 그림엔 거창한 우의적 제목을 붙이는 거예요.\"\n\n\"여기서는 그릴 시간이 없을 거예요.\" 레베카가 말했다. \"제가, 제가 떠나고 나면 그리지요.\" 그러고는 목소리를 낮추며 어찌나 슬프고 애처로운 표정을 짓던지, 모두가 그녀의 처지가 얼마나 모진지, 또 그녀와 헤어지는 일이 얼마나 서글플지를 절감했다.\n\n\"네가 좀 더 머물 수 있으면 좋겠다, 레베카.\" 애밀리아가 말했다.\n\n\"왜?\" 다른 쪽이 한층 더 서글프게 대답했다. \"그러면 그저 더 불행—너와 헤어지기가 더 싫어지기밖에 더 하겠어?\" 그러고는 고개를 돌렸다. 애밀리아는, 우리가 말했다시피 이 어리석은 어린 것의 결함 중 하나인 그 타고난 눈물병에 굴복하기 시작했다. 조지 오스본은 마음이 움직인 듯한 호기심으로 두 젊은 여인을 바라보았고, 조지프 세들리는 자기가 가장 아끼는 헤시안 부츠로 눈길을 떨구며 그 널따란 가슴에서 한숨과도 같은 무언가를 토해 냈다.\n\n\"음악이나 좀 들읍시다, 세들리 양—애밀리아.\" 조지가 말했다. 그는 그 순간 앞서 말한 그 젊은 여인을 두 팔로 끌어안고 좌중이 보는 앞에서 그 얼굴에 입을 맞추고 싶은, 거의 거부할 수 없는 유별난 충동을 느꼈던 것이다. 그녀가 한순간 그를 바라보았는데, 만일 내가 두 사람이 바로 그 찰나에 서로 사랑에 빠졌다고 말한다면 아마도 거짓을 고하는 셈이 될 것이다. 사실인즉 이 두 젊은이는 다름 아닌 바로 이 목적을 위해 부모 손에 길러졌고, 말하자면 그들의 결혼 예고는 지난 십 년간 줄곧 양가에서 낭독되어 온 것이나 다름없었으니 말이다. 그들은 피아노로 갔는데, 그것은 피아노가 으레 그렇듯 안쪽 응접실에 놓여 있었다. 그리고 안이 다소 어두웠던 터라, 애밀리아 양은 세상에 둘도 없이 천연덕스러운 태도로 오스본 씨의 손에 제 손을 맡겼는데, 그야 물론 그가 그녀보다 의자며 긴 의자 사이의 길을 훨씬 더 잘 분간했기 때문이다. 그러나 이 배치 덕분에 조지프 세들리 씨는 응접실 탁자 곁에서 레베카와 단둘이 마주 앉게 되었고, 그녀는 거기서 초록빛 비단 지갑을 뜨고 있었다.\n\n\"집안 비밀이야 물어볼 것도 없네요.\" 샤프 양이 말했다. \"저 두 분은 자기들 비밀을 다 털어놓았으니까요.\"\n\n\"저 친구가 중대장만 되면,\" 조지프가 말했다. \"그 일은 정해진 거나 다름없지요. 조지 오스본은 더할 나위 없이 멋진 사내요.\"\n\n\"그리고 당신 누이동생은 세상에 둘도 없이 사랑스러운 사람이지요.\" 레베카가 말했다. \"그녀를 얻는 사람은 행복할 거예요!\" 이 말과 함께 샤프 양은 깊은 한숨을 내쉬었다.\n\n미혼의 두 사람이 한자리에 모여 지금처럼 그런 미묘한 화제를 두고 이야기를 나누다 보면, 이내 둘 사이에는 상당한 신뢰와 친밀감이 자리 잡기 마련이다. 이제 세들리 씨와 그 젊은 아가씨 사이에 오간 대화를 굳이 특별히 보고할 필요는 없겠다. 앞의 본보기에서 짐작할 수 있듯, 그 대화는 딱히 재치 있다거나 능변이라 할 만한 것이 아니었으니 말이다. 사사로운 자리에서나, 아니 매우 과장되고 기발한 소설을 제외한 그 어디에서도, 대화란 좀처럼 그렇지 못한 법이다. 옆방에서 음악이 흐르고 있던 터라, 두 사람의 이야기는 물론 낮고 알맞은 어조로 이어졌다. 하지만 그건 그렇다 치고, 옆방의 한 쌍은 아무리 큰 소리로 떠들었어도 방해받지 않았을 것이다. 그만큼 그들은 제 일에 빠져 있었으니 말이다.\n\n세들리 씨는 평생 거의 처음으로, 조금의 수줍음이나 머뭇거림도 없이 이성과 이야기를 나누는 자신을 발견했다. 레베카 양은 인도에 관해 그에게 숱하게 질문을 던졌고, 덕분에 그는 그 나라와 자신에 관한 흥미로운 일화들을 여럿 늘어놓을 기회를 얻었다. 그는 총독 관저의 무도회며, 부채와 갈대 발과 그 밖의 갖가지 장치로 더운 날씨에 몸을 시원하게 유지하던 방식을 묘사했다. 또한 총독인 민토 경이 비호한 스코틀랜드인의 수에 관해 자못 재치 있게 떠들어 댔고, 그러고는 호랑이 사냥과, 자기가 탄 코끼리의 몰이꾼이 성난 짐승 한 마리에게 자리에서 끌려 떨어진 광경을 묘사했다. 레베카 양은 그 총독 관저 무도회 이야기에 어찌나 흐뭇해하고, 스코틀랜드인 부관들 이야기에 어찌나 깔깔거리며 세들리 씨를 짓궂고 못된 풍자꾼이라고 불러 댔던지, 또 코끼리 이야기에는 어찌나 겁을 집어먹었던지! \"어머님을 봐서라도, 세들리 씨,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신의 모든 벗들을 봐서라도, 그런 끔찍한 원정에는 다시는 절대 나서지 않겠다고 약속해 주세요.\"\n\n\"쳇, 쳇, 샤프 양.\" 그가 셔츠 깃을 추켜올리며 말했다. \"위험이 있어야 사냥이 그만큼 더 재미있는 법이오.\" 그는 평생 호랑이 사냥에 딱 한 번 나가 보았을 뿐인데, 바로 그때 문제의 그 사고가 일어났고, 그는 거의 죽을 뻔했다—호랑이 때문이 아니라 겁에 질려서 말이다. 그리고 이야기를 이어 가는 동안 그는 자못 대담해져서, 급기야 레베카 양에게 그 초록빛 비단 지갑은 누구를 위해 뜨고 있느냐고 물어볼 만큼 뻔뻔해졌다. 그는 자신의 그 우아하고 허물없는 태도에 스스로도 적잖이 놀라고 흐뭇해했다.\n\n\"지갑이 필요한 누군가를 위해서요.\" 레베카 양이 더없이 부드럽고 마음을 끄는 태도로 그를 바라보며 대답했다. 세들리는 더없이 능란한 연설을 한바탕 늘어놓으려고 \"아, 샤프 양, 어쩌면……\" 하고 막 입을 떼었는데, 그때 옆방에서 부르던 노래가 끝나는 바람에 제 목소리가 너무도 또렷이 들려, 그는 그만 말을 멈추고 얼굴을 붉히더니 잔뜩 당황한 채 코를 풀었다.\n\n\"당신 오빠의 능변 같은 거 들어 본 적 있어요?\" 오스본 씨가 애밀리아에게 속삭였다. \"당신 친구가 기적을 일으켰군요.\"\n\n\"기적은 많을수록 좋지요.\" 애밀리아 양이 말했다. 그녀는 핀만 한 값어치라도 있는 거의 모든 여자가 그렇듯 마음속으로는 중매쟁이였고, 조지프가 인도로 아내를 데려간다면 더없이 기뻤을 것이다. 게다가 그녀는 이 며칠간의 끊임없는 왕래 속에서 레베카를 향한 더없이 다정한 우정에 흠뻑 젖어들었고, 함께 치즈윅에 있을 때는 미처 알아보지 못했던 백만 가지 미덕과 사랑스러운 자질들을 그녀에게서 발견했다. 젊은 아가씨들의 애정이란 잭의 콩나무처럼 빠르게 자라나 하룻밤 새 하늘까지 닿는 법이니까. 결혼 후에 이 '사랑을 향한 그리움'이 가라앉는다 해도 그들을 탓할 일은 아니다. 그것은 대단히 거창한 낱말을 즐겨 쓰는 감상주의자들이 이상을 향한 갈망이라 일컫는 것이며, 쉽게 말하자면 여자들이란 대개 자신의 애정을—말하자면 다른 데서 잔돈처럼 쓰던 그 애정을—쏟아부을 남편과 아이가 생기기 전에는 좀처럼 만족하지 못한다는 뜻일 따름이다.\n\n자기가 가진 얼마 안 되는 노래 밑천을 다 써 버렸든, 아니면 안쪽 응접실에 충분히 오래 머물렀든, 이제 애밀리아 양에게는 친구에게 노래를 청하는 것이 마땅해 보였다. \"당신은 제 노래엔 귀 기울이지 않으셨을 거예요.\" 그녀가 오스본 씨에게 말했다(그게 거짓말인 줄 그녀도 알면서 말이다). \"레베카 노래를 먼저 들으셨더라면요.\"\n\n\"하지만 샤프 양께 미리 일러두겠는데,\" 오스본이 말했다. \"옳든 그르든, 나는 애밀리아 세들리 양이 세상에서 으뜸가는 가수라고 여깁니다.\"\n\n\"들어 보세요.\" 애밀리아가 말했고, 조지프 세들리는 실제로 촛대를 피아노까지 날라다 줄 만큼 정중하게 굴었다. 오스본은 어둠 속에 앉아 있어도 그에 못지않게 좋겠다고 넌지시 비쳤지만, 세들리 양은 웃으며 더 이상 그와 함께 있어 주기를 마다했고, 그리하여 두 사람은 조지프 씨를 따라갔다. 레베카는 친구보다 훨씬 더 노래를 잘했고(물론 오스본은 제 견해를 고수할 자유가 있었지만), 있는 힘을 다해 부른 데다, 정말이지 그녀가 그토록 잘 부르는 걸 한 번도 본 적 없던 애밀리아를 놀라게 했다. 그녀는 조지프가 한마디도 알아듣지 못하고 조지마저 못 알아듣겠다고 실토한 프랑스 노래를 한 곡 부른 다음, 사십 년 전 유행하던 그 소박한 발라드 몇 곡을 불렀다. 그 노래들에서는 영국 수병이며, 우리 임금이며, 가엾은 수전이며, 푸른 눈의 메리며 하는 것들이 주된 주제였다. 그것들은 음악적인 관점에서야 그다지 빼어나지 않다고들 하지만, 마음을 향한 한없이 따뜻하고 소박한 호소를 담고 있어, 요즘 우리에게 베풀어지는 저 영원한 도니체티풍 음악의 밍밍한 '눈물'이며 '한숨'이며 '행복' 따위보다 사람들이 한결 잘 이해했던 것이다.\n\n노래와 노래 사이에는 그 주제에 걸맞은 감상적인 대화가 오갔는데, 샘보는 차를 내온 뒤에, 그리고 그 흐뭇해하는 요리사며, 심지어 가정부 블렌킨솝 부인까지도 황송하게 층계참에서 그것에 귀를 기울였다.\n\n이 노래들 가운데, 그 음악회의 마지막을 장식한 한 곡은 다음과 같은 내용이었다.\n\n아! 황량하고 메마른 황야여, 아! 거세고 살을 에는 폭풍이여, 오두막 지붕은 든든히 막아 주고, 오두막 화롯불은 밝고도 따스했네—한 고아 소년이 창살 곁을 지나며, 그 정겨운 불빛을 바라보자니, 한밤의 칼바람은 곱절로 매섭고, 내려앉은 눈은 곱절로 시렸어라.\n\n그들은 가냘픈 마음과 지친 다리로 앞으로 나아가는 그를 알아보았네. 다정한 목소리들이 돌아와 쉬어 가라 하고, 부드러운 얼굴들이 그를 맞아 주었네. 동이 트고—손님은 떠났건만, 오두막 화롯불은 여전히 타오르네. 하늘이여 외로이 떠도는 가엾은 이들을 모두 굽어살피소서! 언덕 위로 부는 저 바람 소리를 들으라!\n\n그것은 앞서 말한 그 \"제가 떠나고 나면\"이라는 말의 정조를 거듭하는 것이었다. 그녀가 마지막 구절에 이르자, 샤프 양의 \"낮게 울리는 목소리가 떨렸다.\" 모두가 그녀의 떠남과, 의지할 데 없는 고아의 처지를 빗댄 것임을 알아챘다. 음악을 좋아하고 마음이 여린 조지프 세들리는 그 노래가 흐르는 동안 황홀경에 빠져 있었고, 끝날 무렵에는 깊이 마음이 움직였다. 만일 그에게 용기가 있었더라면, 만일 조지와 세들리 양이 앞서 조지가 제안한 대로 안쪽 방에 그대로 머물러 있었더라면, 조지프 세들리의 독신 생활은 그것으로 끝장났을 것이고, 이 작품은 영영 쓰이지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 그 노래가 끝나자 레베카는 피아노 곁을 떠나 애밀리아에게 손을 내밀고는 어슴푸레한 앞쪽 응접실로 걸어 들어갔다. 그리고 바로 그 순간, 샘보 씨가 샌드위치며 젤리며, 번쩍이는 잔 몇 개와 술병을 담은 쟁반을 들고 모습을 드러냈으니, 조지프 세들리의 시선은 즉각 거기에 붙박이고 말았다. 세들리 집안의 부모가 만찬 모임에서 돌아왔을 때, 젊은이들은 마차가 도착한 소리도 듣지 못할 만큼 이야기에 푹 빠져 있었고, 조지프 씨는 마침 이렇게 말하던 참이었다. \"친애하는 샤프 양, 그 엄청난—당신의—당신의 즐거운 수고 끝에 기운을 차리시라고, 젤리 한 작은술만 들어 보시지요.\"\n\n\"잘한다, 조스!\" 세들리 씨가 말했다. 그 익숙한 목소리의 놀림조를 듣자마자, 조스는 즉각 화들짝 놀라 입을 다물고는 황급히 자리를 떴다. 그는 밤새 자기가 샤프 양을 사랑하는지 아닌지를 곱씹으며 잠 못 이루지는 않았다. 사랑이라는 열정이 조지프 세들리 씨의 식욕이나 잠을 방해하는 법은 결코 없었으니까. 다만 그는 재판소 일을 마친 뒤에 그런 노래를 듣는다면 얼마나 즐거울까—그 아가씨는 얼마나 기품 있는 여자인가—총독 부인보다도 프랑스어를 더 잘하지 않던가—그리고 캘커타 무도회에서 얼마나 사람들의 이목을 끌까 하는 생각에 잠겼다. \"저 가엾은 것이 나한테 반한 게 분명해.\" 그가 생각했다. \"인도로 건너오는 여자애들 대부분만큼이나 재산도 넉넉하고. 더 멀리 가 봤자 더 못한 짝이나 만날지도 모르지, 암!\" 그리고 이런 상념에 잠긴 채 그는 잠이 들었다.\n\n샤프 양이 내일 그가 올까 안 올까 생각하며 어떻게 잠 못 이루고 누워 있었는지는, 여기서 구태여 이야기할 필요가 없겠다. 내일이 왔고, 운명이라도 정한 듯 어김없이, 조지프 세들리 씨는 점심 식사 전에 모습을 드러냈다. 그가 러셀 광장에 그런 영예를 베푼 적은 전에 한 번도 없었다. 어찌 된 영문인지 조지 오스본은 이미 와 있었고(치즈윅 거리의 열두 절친한 벗들에게 편지를 쓰던 애밀리아를 딱하게도 \"방해하면서\" 말이다), 레베카는 어제 하던 일에 매달려 있었다. 조의 이륜마차가 다가왔을 때, 그리고 보글리 월라의 전직 징세관이 여느 때처럼 천둥 같은 노크 소리와 거드름 가득한 부산을 떨며 응접실로 끙끙대고 올라오는 동안, 오스본과 세들리 양 사이에는 뜻이 통하는 눈짓이 오갔고, 그 한 쌍은 짓궂게 미소 지으며 레베카를 바라보았으니, 그녀는 정말로 얼굴을 붉히며 뜨개질 위로 고운 곱슬머리를 숙였다. 조지프가 나타나자 그녀의 가슴은 얼마나 두근거렸던지—번쩍이며 삐걱거리는 부츠를 신고 계단에서 헐떡이며 올라오는 조지프—새 조끼를 걸치고, 더위와 긴장으로 얼굴이 벌게진 채, 속을 댄 목도리 뒤에서 얼굴을 붉히는 조지프. 모두에게 조마조마한 순간이었다. 그리고 애밀리아로 말하자면, 정작 가장 관계된 당사자들보다도 그녀가 더 겁을 집어먹었던 것 같다.\n\n문을 활짝 열어젖히고 조지프 씨를 알린 샘보는, 히죽거리며 징세관의 뒤를 따라 들어왔는데, 손에는 멋들어진 꽃다발 두 개를 받쳐 들고 있었다. 그 괴물 같은 양반이 정말로 그날 아침 코번트 가든 시장에서 그것을 사 오는 호기를 부렸던 것이다—요즘 숙녀들이 가는 종이를 원뿔 모양으로 말아 들고 다니는 그 건초더미만 한 것들만큼 크지는 않았지만, 조지프가 더없이 엄숙하게 절을 하며 두 아가씨에게 하나씩 건네자, 그들은 그 선물에 더없이 기뻐했다.\n\n\"잘한다, 조스!\" 오스본이 외쳤다.\n\n\"고마워요, 조지프 오빠.\" 애밀리아가, 오빠가 원하기만 한다면 입맞춤이라도 해 줄 양으로 말했다.(그리고 내 생각에, 애밀리아처럼 사랑스러운 존재의 입맞춤 한 번을 위해서라면, 나는 리 씨의 온실을 전부 당장 사들이고도 남을 것이다.)\n\n\"어머나, 천상의, 천상의 꽃이네요!\" 샤프 양이 탄성을 지르며, 더없이 우아하게 꽃향기를 맡고는, 그것을 가슴에 끌어안고 두 눈을 천장으로 치켜뜨며 황홀한 감탄에 젖었다. 어쩌면 그녀는 꽃 사이에 연애편지가 숨겨져 있지는 않은지 보려고 먼저 그 부케 안부터 들여다보았는지도 모른다. 그러나 편지는 없었다.\n\n\"보글리 월라에서는 꽃말을 쓰나요, 세들리?\" 오스본이 웃으며 물었다.\n\n\"쳇, 무슨 말도 안 되는 소리.\" 그 감상적인 청년이 대꾸했다. \"네이선 가게에서 산 거요. 마음에 든다니 기쁘구려. 그리고 어, 애밀리아, 얘야, 그때 파인애플도 하나 샀는데, 그건 샘보한테 줬다. 점심에 그걸 먹자꾸나. 이 더운 날씨에 아주 시원하고 좋을 거야.\" 레베카는 파인애플은 한 번도 맛본 적이 없다며, 무엇보다도 그것을 맛보고 싶어 못 견디겠다고 말했다.\n\n그렇게 대화가 이어졌다. 무슨 핑계로 오스본이 방을 나갔는지, 또 어째서 이내 애밀리아까지 자리를 떴는지—아마 파인애플 써는 일을 챙기러 갔으리라—나로서는 알 길이 없다. 어쨌든 조스는 레베카와 단둘이 남게 되었고, 그녀는 다시 일감을 손에 잡았으니, 초록빛 비단실과 번쩍이는 바늘이 그녀의 희고 가느다란 손가락 아래에서 빠르게 떨리고 있었다.\n\n\"어젯밤에 당신이 부른 그 노래, 정말이지 아름답고, 아—름다운 노래였소, 친애하는 샤프 양.\" 징세관이 말했다. \"거의 울 뻔했다오. 정말이지 그랬소, 명예를 걸고.\"\n\n\"당신이 다정한 마음씨를 지녔으니 그렇지요, 조지프 씨. 세들리 집안 분들은 다들 그런 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"그 노래 때문에 어젯밤 한숨도 못 잤다오. 오늘 아침엔 침대에서 그걸 흥얼거려 보기까지 했지. 정말이라오, 명예를 걸고. 우리 의사 골롭이 열한 시에 왔는데(난 가엾게도 환자라서, 알다시피 날마다 골롭을 본다오), 아 글쎄, 그때 내가 거기서 노래를 불러 대고 있더란 말이오—꼭 울새처럼.\"\n\n\"어머, 익살꾼 같으니라고! 어디 한번 불러 보세요.\"\n\n\"내가? 안 되오, 당신이, 샤프 양. 친애하는 샤프 양, 어서 불러 봐요.\" \"지금은 안 돼요, 세들리 씨.\" 레베카가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"그럴 만한 기분이 못 되네요. 게다가 이 지갑도 마저 떠야 하고요. 좀 도와주시겠어요, 세들리 씨?\" 그리고 그가 어떻게 도와야 하는지 물어볼 새도 없이, 동인도 회사에 봉직하는 조지프 세들리 씨는 실제로 한 젊은 아가씨와 단둘이 마주 앉아, 사람 잡을 듯한 표정으로 그녀를 바라보고 있었다. 두 팔은 애원하는 자세로 그녀 앞에 쭉 뻗은 채, 두 손은 그녀가 풀어내고 있는 초록빛 비단실 그물에 묶인 채로 말이다.\n\n이 낭만적인 자세를 취한 흥미로운 한 쌍을, 오스본과 애밀리아가 점심이 준비되었음을 알리러 들어왔을 때 발견했다. 비단실 타래는 마침 카드에 다 감겼지만, 조스 씨는 끝내 입을 떼지 못한 터였다.\n\n\"오늘 밤엔 분명 그럴 거야, 얘.\" 애밀리아가 레베카의 손을 꼭 쥐며 말했다. 그리고 세들리 역시 제 영혼과 이야기를 나누고는, 스스로에게 이렇게 다짐했다. \"암, 보크홀에서 청혼을 해야겠어.\"\n\n스위시테일 박사의 그 유명한 학교에서 교육받은 사람이라면 누구나, 커프와 도빈의 그 싸움과 그 시합의 뜻밖의 결말을 두고두고 기억할 것이다. 이 후자의 소년(흔히들 워워 도빈이니, 이랴이랴 도빈이니, 그 밖에도 유치한 경멸이 담긴 온갖 별명으로 불리던 아이)은 스위시테일 박사의 그 모든 도련님들 가운데 가장 말수가 적고, 가장 어설프며, 또 보기에 가장 우둔한 아이였다. 그의 아버지는 도심의 식료품상이었다. 그리고 그가 이른바 \"상호주의 원칙\"에 따라 스위시테일 박사의 학원에 입학을 허락받았다는 소문이 자자했다. 다시 말해 그의 기숙비와 수업료가 돈이 아니라 물품으로 그 아버지에 의해 치러졌다는 것이다. 그래서 그는 거기에, 학교에서도 가장 밑바닥에, 깡마른 코듀로이 바지와 재킷을 걸친 채 서 있었다. 그 솔기 사이로 큼직한 뼈마디가 비어져 나올 지경이었으니, 그는 차며 양초며 설탕이며 얼룩 비누며 (그 학원의 푸딩용으로는 지극히 인색한 분량만 공급되던) 자두며, 그 밖의 온갖 상품을 몇 파운드어치씩 대표하는 존재로 거기 서 있는 셈이었다. 어린 도빈에게는 끔찍한 날이었다. 학교의 한 아이가 단단한 캐러멜과 폴로니 소시지를 노리고 마을로 몰래 빠져나갔다가, 박사의 문 앞에서 \"런던 템스 거리, 식료품 및 기름 상회, 도빈 앤드 러지\"라고 적힌 짐수레가 그 상회에서 취급하는 물품을 부려 놓는 광경을 목격한 그날 말이다.\n\n그 후로 어린 도빈에게는 평온이라곤 없었다. 그를 향한 농담들은 무시무시했고 또 무자비했다. \"이봐, 도빈.\" 한 익살꾼이 말하곤 했다. \"신문에 좋은 소식이 났네. 설탕값이 올랐대, 얘.\" 또 다른 아이는 산수 문제를 내곤 했다. \"양 기름 양초 한 파운드가 칠 펜스 반이라면, 도빈은 얼마쯤 나가야 할까?\" 그러면 그 어린 악동들의 무리에서, 사감까지 포함해서, 한바탕 폭소가 터져 나오는 것이었다. 그들은 물건을 소매로 파는 것이야말로 부끄럽고 수치스러운 짓이며, 진짜 신사라면 누구나 경멸하고 비웃어 마땅한 행위라고 옳게 여겼던 것이다.\n\n\"네 아버진 그저 장사꾼일 뿐이잖아, 오스본.\" 도빈은 자기 머리 위로 그 폭풍을 불러온 그 꼬마에게 단둘이 있을 때 말했다. 그러자 그 아이는 거만하게 대꾸했다. \"우리 아버진 신사고, 마차도 두신다고.\" 그러자 윌리엄 도빈은 운동장 한구석의 외딴 헛간으로 물러나, 더없이 쓰라린 슬픔과 비탄 속에서 반공일을 보냈다. 우리 가운데 누가 이와 비슷한, 쓰라리고 또 쓰라린 어린 시절의 슬픔의 시간을 기억하지 못하겠는가? 너그러운 소년만큼 부당함을 절감하고, 모욕 앞에 움츠러들며, 자신이 받은 부당한 처사를 그토록 예민하게 느끼고, 또 친절에는 그토록 뜨거운 감사를 품는 이가 어디 있겠는가? 그런데도 너희는 그 가냘픈 영혼들 가운데 얼마나 많은 수를, 한 줌의 엉성한 산수와 비참한 엉터리 라틴어 따위를 위해 깎아내리고, 멀리하고, 괴롭히는 것이냐?\n\n자, 윌리엄 도빈은 저 놀라운 책, 이튼 라틴어 문법서에 제시된 그 언어의 기초조차 익히지 못하는 탓에, 스위시테일 박사의 학생들 가운데 맨 꼴찌 축에 머물 수밖에 없었다. 그가 거인처럼 우뚝하니, 풀 죽고 멍한 표정에, 귀퉁이가 접힌 입문서를 들고 꼭 끼는 코듀로이 바지를 입은 채 하급반과 함께 진급해 올라가면, 발그레한 얼굴에 앞치마를 두른 꼬마들에게 끊임없이 \"강등당하기\" 일쑤였다. 위아래 할 것 없이 모두가 그를 놀려 댔다. 그들은 그 꼭 끼는 코듀로이 바지를, 그렇게 끼는데도 더 꿰매 붙여 놓았다. 그의 침대 끈을 잘라 놓았다. 양동이며 벤치를 엎어 놓아 그가 거기에 정강이를 부딪쳐 까지게 만들었는데, 그는 어김없이 매번 그렇게 까이고 말았다. 그들은 그에게 소포를 보냈는데, 뜯어 보면 그 안에는 아버지가 만든 비누와 양초가 들어 있었다. 도빈을 향해 야유와 농을 던지지 않는 꼬마가 하나도 없었다. 그리고 그는 모든 것을 잠자코 견디며, 그저 입을 다문 채 비참하게 지냈다.\n\n반면 커프는 스위시테일 학원의 위대한 우두머리이자 멋쟁이였다. 그는 술을 몰래 들여왔다. 마을 아이들과 싸웠다. 토요일이면 그를 집으로 태우고 갈 조랑말이 오곤 했다. 그는 자기 방에 사냥용 장화를 두고, 방학이면 그것을 신고 사냥을 다녔다. 금딱지 회중시계도 차고 있었고, 박사처럼 코담배도 피웠다. 오페라에도 가 본 적이 있어 주연 배우들의 우열을 가릴 줄 알았는데, 켐블 씨보다 킨 씨를 더 높이 쳤다. 한 시간에 라틴어 시 마흔 줄을 척척 지어낼 수 있었다. 프랑스어 시도 지을 줄 알았다. 그가 모르는 것, 그가 못 하는 것이 그 밖에 또 무엇이 있었겠는가? 심지어 박사 본인조차 그를 두려워한다는 말까지 나돌았다.\n\n학교의 명실상부한 왕인 커프는 신하들 위에 군림하며, 빼어난 우월감으로 그들을 부려 먹었다. 이 아이는 그의 구두를 닦았다. 저 아이는 그의 빵을 구웠고, 또 다른 아이들은 잔심부름을 하거나 여름 오후 내내 그에게 크리켓 공을 던져 주곤 했다. \"무화과\"는 그가 가장 멸시하는 녀석이었고, 늘 욕하고 비웃으면서도 도무지 몸소 말을 섞으려 들지 않는 상대였다.\n\n어느 날, 단둘이 있을 때 두 도련님 사이에 다툼이 벌어졌다. 무화과가 교실에 혼자 남아 집으로 보낼 편지를 끙끙대며 쓰고 있는데, 커프가 들어오더니 무슨 심부름을 가라고 시켰다. 아마도 타르트가 그 주제였을 것이다.\n\n\"못 가.\" 도빈이 말했다. \"편지를 마저 써야 해.\"\n\n\"못 간다고?\" 커프 씨가 말하며 그 문서를 움켜쥐었다(거기에는 지운 단어도 많고 틀리게 쓴 단어도 많았으며, 그 위에 얼마나 많은 고민과 수고와 눈물이 쏟아부어졌는지 알 수 없었다. 그 가엾은 녀석은 어머니에게 편지를 쓰고 있었던 것이다. 식료품상의 아내로 템스 거리 뒷방에 사는 처지였지만, 그를 끔찍이 아끼는 어머니에게 말이다). \"못 간다고?\" 커프 씨가 말했다. \"그 까닭이 뭔지 좀 알았으면 싶은데? 무화과 노친네한테는 내일 편지하면 될 것 아니냐?\"\n\n\"이름 부르지 마.\" 도빈이 잔뜩 긴장한 채 벤치에서 일어나며 말했다.\n\n\"그래서, 갈 거야 말 거야?\" 학교의 수탉이 의기양양하게 외쳤다.\n\n\"편지 내려놔.\" 도빈이 대꾸했다. \"신사라면 남의 편지를 읽지 않는 법이야.\"\n\n\"그래, 이제 갈 거냐고?\" 상대가 말했다.\n\n\"안 가. 때리지 마, 안 그럼 박살을 내 버릴 테니까.\" 도빈이 납으로 된 잉크병으로 달려들며, 어찌나 험악한 표정을 짓던지, 커프 씨는 멈칫하더니 다시 코트 소매를 내려 접고 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고는 비웃음을 지으며 가 버렸다. 그러나 그 후로 그는 그 식료품상 아들에게 몸소 시비를 걸지는 않았다. 다만 그의 명예를 위해 한마디 보태자면, 그는 등 뒤에서는 늘 도빈 씨를 경멸하며 입에 올리곤 했다.\n\n이 면담이 있고 얼마 뒤, 어느 화창한 오후에 커프 씨가 가엾은 윌리엄 도빈의 근처에 있게 된 일이 있었다. 도빈은 운동장 나무 아래 누워, 다른 아이들과 떨어진 곳에서 자기가 아끼는 『아라비안나이트』 한 권을 한 자 한 자 더듬어 읽고 있었다. 다른 아이들이 저마다 온갖 놀이에 빠져 있는 동안, 그는 외톨이로, 그러나 거의 행복하게 있었던 것이다. 사람들이 아이들을 그저 제멋대로 내버려 두기만 한다면, 교사들이 아이들을 들볶기를 그만두기만 한다면, 부모들이 아이들의 생각을 끝끝내 지도하려 들고 그 감정을 지배하려 들지만 않는다면—누구에게나 신비로 남아 있는 그 감정과 생각을(우리가 서로를, 우리 아이들을, 우리 아버지들을, 우리 이웃을 대체 얼마나 알겠는가, 또 너희가 다스리는 그 가엾은 소년이나 소녀의 생각이, 그를 지배하는 저 우둔하고 세속에 찌든 인간의 생각보다 얼마나 더 아름답고 거룩할 법한가?)—내 말은, 부모와 스승이 아이들을 조금만 더 내버려 둔다면, 비록 현재형 라틴어 어미를 익히는 양은 조금 줄어들지언정, 해될 일은 거의 없으리라는 것이다.\n\n자, 윌리엄 도빈은 모처럼 세상을 잊고서, 다이아몬드 골짜기의 뱃사람 신드바드와 함께, 혹은 아메드 왕자가 페리바누 요정을 만난 그 황홀한 동굴—우리 모두 한 번쯤 가 보고 싶은 그곳—에서 왕자와 요정과 함께 멀리 떠나 있었다. 그런데 어린아이가 우는 듯한 날카로운 울음소리가 그 즐거운 공상을 깨뜨렸다. 고개를 들어 보니, 커프가 그의 앞에서 한 꼬마를 두들겨 패고 있었다.\n\n식료품상 짐수레 일을 일러바친 바로 그 아이였다. 그러나 그는 별로 앙심을 품지 않았다. 적어도 어리고 작은 아이에게는 그랬다. \"이놈, 어디 감히 병을 깨뜨려?\" 커프가 노란 크리켓 막대기를 그 꼬마 위로 휘두르며 말했다.\n\n그 아이는 운동장 담을 넘어가라는 지시를 받았었다(깨진 유리가 위쪽에서 치워지고 벽돌에 발 디딜 홈이 편리하게 파인, 미리 골라 둔 지점에서 말이다). 사 분의 일 마일을 달려가, 외상으로 럼주 시럽 한 파인트를 사 오고, 학교 밖에 깔린 박사의 첩자들을 모두 무릅쓰고, 다시 운동장 안으로 기어 올라오라는 것이었다. 그런데 이 묘기를 부리는 동안 발이 미끄러져 병이 깨졌고, 시럽이 쏟아졌으며, 바지까지 못 쓰게 되어, 그는 비록 해를 끼친 것은 없으나 완전히 죄지은 듯 벌벌 떨며 자기 고용주 앞에 나타나게 된 것이었다.\n\n\"이놈, 어디 감히 그걸 깨뜨려?\" 커프가 말했다. \"이 얼간이 좀도둑 같으니. 시럽을 네가 마셔 놓고 이제 와서 병을 깨뜨린 척하는 게로구나. 손 내밀어, 이놈.\"\n\n막대기가 그 아이의 손바닥 위로 묵직하게 쾅 내리쳐졌다. 신음 소리가 뒤따랐다. 도빈이 고개를 들었다. 페리바누 요정은 아메드 왕자와 함께 동굴 가장 깊은 곳으로 달아나 버렸다. 로크 새는 뱃사람 신드바드를 다이아몬드 골짜기에서 휙 낚아채 시야 밖, 저 멀리 구름 속으로 데려가 버렸다. 그리고 정직한 윌리엄의 눈앞에는 일상의 현실이 펼쳐져 있었다. 큰 아이 하나가 까닭 없이 작은 아이를 때리고 있는 광경 말이다.\n\n\"다른 손 내밀어, 이놈.\" 커프가 그 어린 학우에게 호통쳤고, 그 아이의 얼굴은 고통으로 일그러져 있었다. 도빈은 부르르 떨더니, 작고 낡은 옷을 추슬러 몸을 일으켰다.\n\n\"이거나 받아라, 이 꼬마 악마야!\" 커프 씨가 외쳤고, 막대기가 다시금 그 아이의 손 위로 내리쳐졌다. 부디 질겁하지 마시라, 숙녀분들이여. 공립학교에 다니는 사내아이라면 누구나 한 번쯤 해 본 짓이니까. 당신네 자녀들도 십중팔구 그런 짓을 하고 또 당할 것이다. 막대기가 다시 내리쳐졌다. 그러자 도빈이 벌떡 일어섰다.\n\n그의 동기가 무엇이었는지 나로서는 알 수 없다. 공립학교에서의 가혹 행위란 러시아의 채찍형만큼이나 공인된 것이다. 그것에 저항한다는 것은 (어떤 의미로는) 신사답지 못한 일이었다. 어쩌면 도빈의 어리석은 영혼이 그 폭정의 행사에 반기를 들었는지도 모른다. 아니면 마음속에 복수심이 꿈틀거려, 영광과 자긍심과 위세와 위엄을 한 몸에 두르고, 깃발이 나부끼고 북이 울리고 호위병이 경례하는 가운데 그 자리에 군림하던 저 눈부신 폭군이자 골목대장과 한번 겨뤄 보고 싶었는지도 모른다. 그러나 그 동기가 무엇이었든 간에, 그는 벌떡 일어서더니 외쳤다. \"그만둬, 커프. 그 애 더는 괴롭히지 마. 안 그러면 내가—\"\n\n\"안 그러면 네가 뭘 어쩐다고?\" 이 훼방에 어안이 벙벙해진 커프가 물었다. \"손 내밀어, 이 짐승만도 못한 놈아.\"\n\n\"네가 평생 당해 본 적 없는 가장 호된 매질을 안겨 주겠어.\" 도빈이 커프의 말 앞부분에 대꾸하며 말했다. 그러자 헐떡이며 눈물 흘리던 어린 오스본은, 이 놀라운 투사가 느닷없이 자기를 지키러 나선 것을 보고는 경이와 의심이 뒤섞인 눈으로 올려다보았다. 한편 커프의 경악도 그에 못지않았다. 우리의 작고하신 군주 조지 3세께서 북아메리카 식민지의 반란 소식을 들었을 때를 떠올려 보시라. 작은 다윗이 앞으로 나서서 결투를 청했을 때 그 뻔뻔한 골리앗을 떠올려 보시라. 그러면 이 한판 시합을 제안받은 레지널드 커프 씨의 심정을 짐작할 수 있으리라.\n\n\"수업 끝나고.\" 그가 말했다. 물론, 잠시 뜸을 들이고 한 번 흘겨본 뒤였는데, 그 표정인즉슨 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"지금부터 그때까지 유언장이나 작성하고, 마지막 소원이나 친구들에게 전해 두시지.\"\n\n\"좋을 대로.\" 도빈이 말했다. \"네가 내 세컨드를 봐 줘야겠어, 오스본.\"\n\n\"뭐, 정 그렇다면.\" 어린 오스본이 대꾸했다. 그도 그럴 것이, 아시다시피 그의 아버지는 마차를 두는 사람이었고, 그는 자기 편을 들어 줄 이 투사가 어쩐지 좀 부끄러웠던 것이다.\n\n그렇다, 결투의 시간이 닥쳐오자, 그는 \"잘해라, 무화과\"라고 말하기조차 거의 부끄러울 지경이었다. 그리고 이 유명한 시합의 처음 두세 회 동안에는 그 자리의 그 어느 아이도 그런 응원의 함성을 내지르지 않았다. 시합 초반, 기술이 능숙한 커프는 얼굴에 경멸의 미소를 띤 채, 마치 무도회에라도 온 듯 가뿐하고 명랑하게 상대에게 주먹을 꽂아 넣어, 그 운 없는 투사를 내리 세 번이나 바닥에 쓰러뜨렸다. 쓰러질 때마다 환호성이 터졌고, 다들 그 승자에게 무릎을 받쳐 주는 영예를 누리고 싶어 안달이었다.\n\n\"이게 끝나면 난 또 얼마나 두들겨 맞을까.\" 어린 오스본은 자기 편을 일으켜 세우며 생각했다. \"그냥 항복하는 게 낫겠어.\" 그가 도빈에게 말했다. \"그래 봤자 매질 한 번이잖아, 무화과. 너도 알다시피 난 익숙하니까.\" 그러나 온 사지를 부들부들 떨고 콧구멍으로 분노를 뿜어내던 무화과는, 그 작은 세컨드를 옆으로 밀쳐 두고 네 번째로 맞붙으러 들어갔다.\n\n자기를 향해 날아드는 주먹을 막아 낼 줄은 전혀 몰랐던 데다, 앞선 세 차례 모두 커프가 적에게 손쓸 틈도 주지 않고 먼저 공격을 시작했던 터라, 무화과는 이번엔 자기 쪽에서 먼저 돌격해 시합을 개시하기로 마음먹었다. 그리하여 왼손잡이였던 그는 그 팔을 휘둘러, 있는 힘을 다해 두 번을 내질렀다. 한 번은 커프 씨의 왼쪽 눈에, 또 한 번은 그 멋진 로마식 코에 말이다.\n\n이번에는 커프가 쓰러졌고, 모여 있던 아이들은 깜짝 놀랐다. \"야, 멋지게 한 방 먹였다, 세상에.\" 어린 오스본이 감식가다운 태도로 자기 편의 등을 두드리며 말했다. \"왼손으로 먹여, 무화과, 이 친구야.\"\n\n남은 시합 내내 무화과의 왼손은 무시무시한 활약을 펼쳤다. 커프는 번번이 나가떨어졌다. 여섯 번째 회에 이르자 \"잘한다, 커프\"를 외치는 아이들만큼이나 \"잘한다, 무화과\"를 외치는 아이들이 거의 같은 수로 늘어났다. 열두 번째 회에 이르자 후자의 투사는, 흔히들 하는 말로 정신을 놓아 버려서, 침착함도 공격력도 방어력도 모조리 잃은 상태였다. 반면 무화과는 퀘이커 교도처럼 차분했다. 얼굴은 새파랗게 질리고, 두 눈은 부릅뜬 채 번득이며, 아랫입술에 난 큼직한 상처에서 피가 줄줄 흐르는 그 모습은 이 젊은 친구에게 사납고 섬뜩한 기운을 더해 주어, 아마도 적잖은 구경꾼들의 가슴에 공포를 새겨 넣었을 것이다. 그럼에도 그 겁 없는 적수는 열세 번째로 맞붙을 채비를 했다.\n\n내게 네이피어나 『벨스 라이프』 같은 필력이 있다면, 이 시합을 제대로 묘사해 보고 싶다. 그것은 근위대의 마지막 돌격이었다(라고 해야겠지만, 다만 워털루 전투는 아직 벌어지지 않았으니). 그것은 일만 자루의 총검으로 곤두서고 스무 개의 독수리 군기로 빛나며 라에생트 언덕을 밀고 올라가는 네 장군의 종대였다. 그것은 언덕을 뛰어 내려가 야만적인 전투의 품으로 적을 끌어안으러 돌진하는, 쇠고기 먹는 영국 병사들의 함성이었다. 다시 말해, 커프가 기개를 잔뜩 끌어모아 다가왔으나 완전히 비틀비틀 휘청거리는 가운데, 그 무화과 상인은 여느 때처럼 적의 코에 왼손을 꽂아 넣어 마지막으로 그를 쓰러뜨린 것이다.\n\n\"이걸로 끝장이 났을걸.\" 무화과가 말했다. 그의 상대가 풀밭 위로 어찌나 깔끔하게 곤두박질치던지, 마치 잭 스폿의 공이 당구대 포켓으로 쏙 떨어지는 것을 내가 본 그 모양 그대로였다. 그리고 사실인즉, 시작 신호가 떨어졌을 때 레지널드 커프 씨는 다시 일어설 수 없었거나, 일어서지 않기로 한 것이었다.\n\n그러자 이제 아이들이 모두 무화과를 향해 어찌나 큰 함성을 질러 대던지, 마치 그가 시합 내내 자기네의 사랑받는 투사였던 것처럼 여겨질 지경이었다. 그리고 그 함성이 어찌나 컸던지, 그 소동의 까닭이 궁금해진 스위시테일 박사가 서재에서 영락없이 뛰쳐나오고 말았다. 물론 그는 무화과를 호되게 매질하겠다고 으름장을 놓았다. 그러나 그새 정신을 차리고 상처를 씻고 있던 커프가 일어서서 말했다. \"제 잘못입니다, 선생님. 무화과 잘못도, 도빈 잘못도 아닙니다. 제가 어린아이를 괴롭히고 있었고, 그래서 당해도 싼 거였습니다.\" 이 도량 넓은 한마디로 그는 자기를 이긴 자가 매질당하는 것을 막아 주었을 뿐 아니라, 패배로 하마터면 잃을 뻔했던 아이들에 대한 자신의 위세를 고스란히 되찾았다.\n\n어린 오스본은 부모에게 이 사건의 전말을 적어 집으로 부쳤다.\n\n리치먼드, 슈거케인 하우스, 18××년 3월\n\n엄마께,—건강히 잘 지내시기를 바라요. 케이크 하나랑 오 실링을 보내 주시면 정말 고맙겠어요. 여기서 커프랑 도빈이 싸웠어요. 아시다시피 커프는 학교의 수탉이었거든요. 두 사람이 열세 번을 맞붙었는데, 도빈이 이겼어요. 그래서 커프는 이제 둘째 수탉밖에 안 돼요. 이 싸움은 저 때문에 난 거예요. 제가 우유병을 깨뜨려서 커프가 저를 두들겨 패고 있었는데, 무화과가 그걸 못 참았거든요. 우리는 그 애를 무화과라고 불러요, 아버지가 식료품상이라서요—무화과 앤드 러지, 도심 템스 거리. 그 애가 저를 위해 싸워 줬으니, 엄마도 차랑 설탕은 그 애 아버지네서 사 주시면 좋겠어요. 커프는 토요일마다 집에 가는데, 이번엔 눈이 두 개나 멍들어서 못 가요. 그 애는 데리러 오는 흰 조랑말이 있고, 제복 입은 마부가 적갈색 암말을 타고 와요. 우리 아빠도 조랑말을 갖게 해 주시면 좋겠어요. 저는\n\n엄마의 효성스러운 아들, 조지 세들리 오스본 올림\n\n추신.—꼬마 에미한테 사랑한다고 전해 주세요. 그 애한테 줄 마차를 마분지로 오려서 만들고 있어요. 씨앗 케이크 말고 자두 케이크로 보내 주세요.\n\n도빈의 승리 덕분에 모든 학우들의 평가 속에서 그의 평판은 엄청나게 치솟았고, 비난의 대명사였던 무화과라는 이름은 학교에서 통용되는 그 어떤 별명 못지않게 점잖고 인기 있는 별명이 되었다. \"결국, 아버지가 식료품상인 게 그 애 잘못은 아니잖아.\" 조지 오스본이 말했다. 그는 비록 꼬맹이였지만 스위시테일의 아이들 사이에서 대단한 인기를 누리고 있었던지라, 그의 의견은 우레와 같은 박수로 받아들여졌다. 도빈을 두고 그 출생의 우연을 비웃는 것은 천박한 짓이라고 결의되었다. \"늙은 무화과\"는 다정하고 정겨운 이름이 되었고, 그 비열한 사감조차 더는 그를 야유하지 않았다.\n\n그리고 도빈의 기개도 달라진 처지와 함께 솟아올랐다. 그는 학업에서도 놀라운 진전을 보였다. 도빈으로서는 그 황송함에 그저 얼굴을 붉히며 놀랄 따름이던 저 빼어난 커프가, 몸소 그의 라틴어 시 작문을 도와주었다. 놀이 시간에 그를 \"지도해\" 주었고, 그를 의기양양하게 꼬맹이 반에서 중간 반으로 끌어올려 주었으며, 거기서도 그에게 그럴듯한 자리를 마련해 주었다. 그가 비록 고전 학문에는 둔하지만 수학에는 유달리 빠르다는 사실이 밝혀졌다. 모두가 흐뭇해하는 가운데 그는 대수학에서 삼등을 했고, 한여름 공개 시험에서는 프랑스어 상으로 책을 받았다. 박사가 온 학교 학생들과 부모들, 그리고 손님들이 보는 앞에서 \"굴리엘무스 도빈에게\"라는 헌사를 적은 『텔레마크』(그 감미로운 로맨스)를 그에게 수여하던 그때 그 어머니의 표정을 보셨어야 하는 건데 말이다. 아이들이 모두 박수를 쳐 갈채와 공감을 표했다. 얼굴을 붉히고, 발을 헛디디고, 어색해하고, 또 자기 자리로 돌아가는 길에 짓밟은 발이 도대체 몇 개였는지, 그 누가 묘사하거나 헤아릴 수 있겠는가? 이때 처음으로 그를 자랑스러워하게 된 그의 아버지 늙은 도빈은 여러 사람 앞에서 그에게 이 기니를 주었는데, 그는 그 대부분을 학교 아이들 모두에게 잔치를 베푸는 데 써 버렸다. 그리고 그는 방학이 끝난 뒤 연미복을 입고 돌아왔다.\n\n도빈은 너무도 겸손한 젊은이여서, 자기 처지의 이 행복한 변화가 자신의 너그럽고 사내다운 기질에서 비롯되었다고는 생각조차 하지 못했다. 그는 어떤 삐딱한 심사에서, 자신의 행운을 오로지 어린 조지 오스본의 주선과 호의 덕으로 돌리기를 택했고, 그 후로 그에게 오직 아이들만이 느낄 수 있는 그런 사랑과 애정을 바치기로 마음먹었다. 저 매혹적인 동화책에서 읽듯, 거친 오르송이 자기를 이긴 눈부신 젊은이 발렌타인에게 품었던 바로 그런 애정 말이다. 그는 어린 오스본의 발치에 몸을 던지며 그를 사랑했다. 서로 알기도 전부터 그는 남몰래 오스본을 흠모했었다. 이제 그는 그의 시종이요, 그의 개요, 그의 충복 프라이데이였다. 그는 오스본이야말로 온갖 완벽함을 다 갖춘 존재이며, 세상에 태어난 소년들 가운데 가장 잘생기고, 가장 용감하고, 가장 활동적이고, 가장 영리하고, 가장 너그러운 아이라고 믿었다. 그는 자기 돈을 그와 나누었고, 칼이며 필통이며 금딱지 도장이며 토피며 『리틀 워블러』며, 기사와 도적들의 큼직한 채색 삽화가 든 모험 소설들이며, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 선물을 그에게 사 주었다. 그 후자의 책들 가운데 여러 권에서는 \"조지 세들리 오스본 귀하께, 다정한 벗 윌리엄 도빈으로부터\"라는 헌사를 읽을 수 있었는데, 이 충정의 표시를 조지는 자기의 빼어난 자질에 걸맞게 더없이 너그럽게 받아들였다.\n\n그리하여 오스본 중위는 복스홀 나들이가 있던 날 러셀 광장으로 와서 숙녀들에게 이렇게 말했던 것이다. \"세들리 부인, 자리가 있었으면 합니다만. 저희 연대의 도빈한테 여기 와서 같이 저녁을 먹고 복스홀에 함께 가자고 청해 두었거든요. 그 친구는 조스만큼이나 숫기가 없는 친구입니다.\"\n\n\"숫기가 없다니! 흥.\" 그 뚱뚱한 신사가 샤프 양에게 정복자 같은 눈길을 던지며 말했다.\n\n\"정말 그렇다니까요—하지만 자네야 비할 데 없이 더 우아하지, 세들리.\" 오스본이 웃으며 덧붙였다. \"자네를 찾으러 갔다가 베드퍼드에서 그 친구를 만났어요. 그래서 그 친구한테 애밀리아 양이 집에 돌아왔고, 저희가 다들 하룻밤 즐기러 나가기로 했다고, 그리고 세들리 부인께서 그 친구가 아이들 잔치 때 펀치 사발을 깨뜨린 일을 용서해 주셨다고 말해 줬지요. 칠 년 전의 그 참사 기억 안 나세요, 부인?\"\n\n\"플라밍고 부인의 진홍색 비단 가운 위에다 엎질렀잖아요.\" 마음씨 좋은 세들리 부인이 말했다. \"어찌나 어설픈 녀석이던지! 게다가 그 누이들도 별로 더 우아하지가 않아요. 어젯밤 도빈 부인이 그중 셋을 데리고 하이베리에 왔던걸요. 어찌나 볼품없던지, 얘들아.\"\n\n\"그 시의원 양반이 아주 부자라면서요, 안 그래요?\" 오스본이 짓궂게 말했다. \"그 딸들 중 하나가 저한테 괜찮은 투자가 되지 않을까요, 부인?\"\n\n\"이 어리석은 녀석 같으니! 그 누런 얼굴을 하고서 도대체 누가 너를 데려가겠니, 내가 좀 알았으면 좋겠구나?\"\n\n\"제 얼굴이 누렇다고요? 도빈을 보실 때까지 기다려 보세요. 아니, 그 친구는 황열병을 세 번이나 앓았다고요. 나소에서 두 번, 세인트키츠에서 한 번.\"\n\n\"그래그래, 우리가 보기엔 네 얼굴도 누렇기로는 충분하단다. 안 그러니, 에미?\" 세들리 부인이 말했다. 이 말에 애밀리아 양은 그저 미소를 짓고 얼굴을 붉힐 뿐이었다. 조지 오스본 씨의 창백하고도 흥미로운 얼굴과, 그 젊은이 자신이 적잖은 만족감으로 바라보던 저 아름답고 검은, 곱슬곱슬 윤기 나는 구레나룻을 바라보면서, 그녀는 작은 가슴속으로 폐하의 군대에도, 아니 이 넓은 세상천지에도 저런 얼굴이나 저런 영웅은 다시없으리라고 생각했다. \"도빈 대위의 얼굴빛이야 난 상관없어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그 어색한 거동도요. 난 그분을 늘 좋아할 거예요, 그럼요.\" 그 작은 이유인즉, 그가 조지의 벗이자 그를 지켜 준 투사였기 때문이다.\n\n\"군에 그 친구보다 더 훌륭한 사내는 없습니다.\" 오스본이 말했다. \"또 그보다 더 나은 장교도 없고요, 물론 미남은 아니지만 말입니다.\" 그러면서 그는 사뭇 천연덕스럽게 거울 쪽으로 눈길을 던졌는데, 그러다가 자기에게 날카롭게 고정된 샤프 양의 시선과 마주치고는 살짝 얼굴을 붉혔다. 그리고 레베카는 속으로 생각했다. \"아, 나의 잘생긴 신사 양반! 당신 속내쯤은 내가 다 꿰뚫어 본 것 같은데\"—이 약삭빠른 작은 여우 같으니!\n\n그날 저녁, 복스홀에서 한껏 사람들을 사로잡을 채비를 하고 흰 모슬린 드레스를 차려입은 애밀리아가, 종달새처럼 노래하며 장미처럼 싱그러운 모습으로 사뿐사뿐 응접실로 들어서는데—손과 발이 큼직하고 귀도 큼직한, 거기에 검은 머리를 바짝 깎아 그것이 더욱 도드라지는, 게다가 그 시절의 흉측한 장식 끈 달린 군복과 삼각모를 걸친 대단히 키 크고 볼품없는 신사 하나가 그녀를 맞으러 나아오더니, 인간이 일찍이 해 본 적 없을 만큼 어설픈 인사를 한 번 꾸벅했다.\n\n이는 다름 아닌 폐하의 보병 연대 소속 윌리엄 도빈 대위였다. 그 많은 용감한 전우들이 이베리아반도에서 영광을 거두고 있는 동안, 그의 연대는 군 복무의 운명에 따라 서인도제도로 보내졌고, 그는 거기서 황열병을 앓고 막 돌아온 참이었다.\n\n그가 도착해 문을 두드린 소리가 어찌나 소심하고 조용하던지 위층의 숙녀들에게는 들리지 않을 정도였다. 그렇지 않았더라면, 장담컨대 애밀리아 양이 그토록 대담하게 노래를 부르며 방으로 들어섰을 리 없다. 어쨌거나 그 달콤하고 싱그러운 작은 목소리는 곧장 대위의 가슴속으로 파고들어 거기 둥지를 틀었다. 그녀가 악수를 청하며 손을 내밀었을 때, 그는 그 손을 자기 손으로 감싸 쥐기에 앞서 잠시 멈추고 생각했다—\"아니, 이게 정말 가능한 일인가—그대가 정말, 그토록 얼마 전에 분홍 드레스를 입고 있던, 내가 기억하는 그 어린 아가씨란 말인가—내가 임관 발령을 받고 나서 막 그 펀치 사발을 엎질렀던 그 밤의? 그대가 조지 오스본이 자기랑 결혼할 거라고 말하던 그 어린 소녀란 말인가? 그대는 어쩜 이리도 활짝 피어난 젊은이로 보이는지, 또 그 악동 녀석은 어쩜 이런 보물을 거머쥐었는지!\" 애밀리아의 손을 자기 손에 쥐기 전에, 삼각모를 떨어뜨리면서, 그는 이 모든 것을 생각했던 것이다.\n\n학교를 떠난 뒤로 우리가 그를 다시 만나는 기쁨을 누리는 바로 이 순간까지의 그의 내력은, 비록 온전히 다 이야기되지는 않았으나, 앞 페이지의 대화를 통해 영리한 독자에게는 충분히 암시되었으리라고 나는 생각한다. 멸시받던 식료품상 도빈은 도빈 시의원이 되었고—도빈 시의원은 도심 경기병대의 대령이 되었으니, 당시 그 부대는 프랑스의 침공에 맞서겠다는 군사적 열정으로 불타고 있었다. 늙은 오스본 씨 본인은 보잘것없는 상병에 지나지 않았던 그 도빈 대령의 부대는 군주와 요크 공의 사열을 받았고, 그리하여 그 대령이자 시의원은 기사 작위를 받았다. 그의 아들은 군에 입대했다. 그리고 젊은 오스본도 이내 같은 연대를 따라 들어갔다. 그들은 서인도제도와 캐나다에서 복무했다. 그들의 연대는 이제 막 본국으로 돌아온 참이었고, 도빈이 조지 오스본에게 품은 애정은 두 사람이 학생이던 시절 그대로 지금도 따뜻하고 너그러웠다.\n\n그리하여 이 훌륭한 사람들은 이내 저녁 식탁에 둘러앉았다. 그들은 전쟁과 영광에 대해, 그리고 보니와 웰링턴 경에 대해, 또 최근의 관보에 대해 이야기했다. 그 유명한 시절에는 관보마다 승전보가 실려 있었고, 이 두 늠름한 젊은이는 그 영광스러운 명단에서 제 이름을 보고 싶어 애가 탔으며, 명예의 기회에서 멀리 떨어져 있던 연대에 속한 자신들의 불운한 팔자를 저주했다. 샤프 양은 이 흥미진진한 이야기에 신이 났으나, 세들리 양은 그것을 들으며 부르르 떨더니 아주 기진맥진해졌다. 조스 씨는 자기의 호랑이 사냥 이야기를 여러 편 늘어놓았고, 커틀러 양과 군의관 랜스에 관한 그 이야기를 마저 끝냈으며, 식탁 위의 온갖 음식을 레베카에게 권하면서, 자기 자신도 잔뜩 먹어 치우고 마구 들이켰다.\n\n숙녀들이 자리를 뜰 때, 그는 더없이 매혹적인 우아함으로 벌떡 일어나 문을 열어 주었고—식탁으로 돌아와서는 클라레를 잔 가득 따라 연거푸 들이켰는데, 신경질적으로 다급하게 꿀꺽꿀꺽 삼키는 것이었다.\n\n\"저 친구가 미리 기운을 돋우고 있는 거예요.\" 오스본이 도빈에게 속삭였고, 이윽고 복스홀로 떠날 시간과 함께 마차가 당도했다.\n\n내가 지금 연주하는 곡조가 매우 잔잔한 것임을 나도 안다(물론 머지않아 가공할 만한 장들이 다가오고 있긴 하지만). 그러니 마음씨 너그러운 독자께서는, 지금 우리가 다루고 있는 것이 그저 러셀 광장에 사는 어느 주식 중개인 집안의 이야기일 뿐이라는 점을 기억해 주시기를 청한다. 그들은 평범한 사람들이 흔히 그러하듯 산책을 하거나, 점심을 들거나, 저녁을 먹거나, 담소를 나누고 사랑을 속삭일 따름이며, 그들의 연애 과정을 수놓을 만한 격정적이고 놀라운 사건이라곤 단 하나도 없는 것이다. 사정은 이러하다. 애밀리아에게 반한 오스본이 옛 친구를 저녁 식사와 보크스홀에 초대했고, 조스 세들리는 레베카에게 반해 있다. 과연 그가 그녀와 결혼할 것인가? 이것이 지금 우리 손에 놓인 중대한 주제인 것이다.\n\n우리는 이 주제를 점잖은 방식으로, 또는 낭만적인 방식으로, 또는 익살스러운 방식으로 다룰 수도 있었을 것이다. 만약 우리가 똑같은 사건들을 그로스브너 광장을 무대로 펼쳐 놓았다고 가정해 보자. 그랬다면 귀 기울여 들을 사람도 더러 있지 않았겠는가? 조지프 세들리 경이 어떻게 사랑에 빠졌는지, 그리고 오스본 후작이 그 고귀한 아버지 공작의 흔쾌한 승낙을 받아 애밀리아 영애에게 어떻게 마음을 주게 되었는지를 보여 주었다고 가정해 보자. 혹은 그토록 더없이 점잖은 방식 대신, 정반대로 완전히 비천한 쪽으로 눈을 돌려, 세들리 씨 댁 부엌에서 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있었는지를 묘사했다고 가정해 보자. 흑인 샘보가 요리사에게 어떻게 반했는지(실제로 그랬다), 그리고 그녀를 두고 마부와 어떻게 한바탕 싸움을 벌였는지, 칼 닦는 소년이 식어 버린 양 어깨살을 훔치다 어떻게 들켰는지, 그리고 세들리 양의 새 몸종이 밀랍 양초가 없으면 잠자리에 들기를 거부했다는 이야기 말이다. 그런 사건들이라면 흥겨운 웃음을 자아내게 꾸밀 수도 있을 것이고, \"인생\"의 한 장면을 보여 주는 것으로 여겨질 수도 있을 것이다. 혹은 이와 반대로, 우리가 무시무시한 것을 좋아하는 취향이 동하여, 새 몸종의 연인을 직업적인 강도로 만들고, 그가 패거리를 이끌고 집에 들이닥쳐 주인의 발치에서 흑인 샘보를 살육하고 애밀리아를 잠옷 바람으로 납치해 가서는 제3권에 이르도록 풀어 주지 않는 식으로 그렸다고 가정해 보자. 그랬다면 우리는 손쉽게 손에 땀을 쥐게 하는 흥미진진한 이야기를 지어냈을 것이고, 그 불타는 장들을 독자는 숨을 헐떡이며 허겁지겁 읽어 나갔을 것이다. 그러나 내 독자께서는 그런 로맨스를 바라서는 안 된다. 그저 소박한 이야기뿐이니, 보크스홀에 관한 한 장으로 만족하셔야 한다. 그 장이 너무나 짧아서 장이라고 부르기조차 민망할 지경이긴 하다. 그럼에도 그것은 분명 하나의 장이며, 그것도 매우 중요한 장인 것이다. 누구의 인생에나, 아무것도 아닌 듯 보이지만 그 나머지 이야기 전부에 영향을 미치는 자그마한 장들이 있지 않은가?\n\n그러면 우리도 러셀 광장 일행과 함께 마차에 올라타 정원으로 떠나 보자. 앞자리에 앉은 조스와 샤프 양 사이에는 비집고 앉을 자리가 겨우 있을 뿐이다. 오스본 씨는 그 맞은편에서 도빈 대위와 애밀리아 사이에 바늘처럼 끼어 앉아 있다.\n\n마차에 탄 사람이라면 한 사람도 빠짐없이, 그날 밤 조스가 레베카 샤프를 세들리 부인으로 만들겠노라 청혼하리라는 데 의견이 일치했다. 집에 있는 부모도 이 일을 묵인했다. 다만 우리끼리 이야기지만, 늙은 세들리 씨는 자기 아들에게 경멸에 가까운 감정을 품고 있었다. 그는 아들이 허영심 많고, 이기적이고, 게으르고, 사내답지 못하다고 했다. 그는 멋쟁이 행세를 하는 아들의 거드름을 견디지 못했고, 거들먹거리며 늘어놓는 허풍 섞인 이야기들을 듣고는 실컷 비웃었다. \"내 재산의 절반은 저 녀석한테 물려줄 작정이야.\" 그가 말했다. \"게다가 제 몫의 재산도 넉넉히 가지게 될 테고. 하지만 만약 너랑 나랑 제 누이까지 내일 당장 죽는다 해도, 저 녀석은 '저런!' 한마디 내뱉고는 평소와 다름없이 저녁밥을 잘만 먹을 게 분명해. 그러니 나는 저 녀석 일로 마음 졸일 생각이 없네. 마음에 드는 여자랑 결혼하라지. 나하고는 상관없는 일이야.\"\n\n반면에 애밀리아는, 그 신중함과 성품을 지닌 젊은 여자답게, 이 혼사에 자못 열의를 보였다. 한두 번쯤 조스가 그녀에게 무언가 매우 중요한 말을 꺼내려 한 적이 있었고, 그녀도 기꺼이 귀 기울일 참이었다. 그러나 그 뚱뚱한 사내는 끝내 그 엄청난 비밀을 가슴에서 털어놓지 못했고, 누이를 몹시 실망시키며 그저 큰 한숨 한 번을 토해 내고는 고개를 돌려 버릴 뿐이었다.\n\n이 수수께끼는 애밀리아의 여린 가슴을 끊임없는 흥분으로 두근거리게 만들었다. 그녀는 레베카와 그 애틋한 주제를 두고 이야기를 나누지는 못한 대신, 가정부 블렌킨솝 부인과 길고 은밀한 대화를 나누며 마음을 달랬다. 블렌킨솝 부인은 그 이야기를 몸종에게 슬쩍 흘렸고, 몸종은 그것을 지나가는 말처럼 요리사에게 언급했을 것이며, 요리사는 그 소식을 모든 상인에게 전했을 것이 분명하다. 그리하여 조스 씨의 결혼은 이제 러셀 광장 세계의 상당히 많은 사람들 입에 오르내리게 된 것이었다.\n\n물론 세들리 부인의 생각으로는, 아들이 화가의 딸과 결혼하는 것은 스스로 격을 떨어뜨리는 일이었다. \"하지만 어머나, 마님.\" 블렌킨솝 부인이 외쳤다. \"저희가 세들리 나리와 혼인했을 적엔 한낱 식료품 장수였습죠. 나리도 그때는 주식 중개인 밑에서 일하는 서기에 불과하셨고, 둘이 합쳐 오백 파운드도 없었습니다요. 그래도 지금은 이렇게 부유하지 않습니까.\" 애밀리아도 전적으로 같은 생각이었고, 마음씨 고운 세들리 부인도 차츰 그 의견 쪽으로 기울게 되었다.\n\n세들리 씨는 중립이었다. \"조스가 좋아하는 여자랑 결혼하라지.\" 그가 말했다. \"나하고는 상관없는 일이야. 이 처녀는 재산이 없지. 하지만 세들리 부인도 마찬가지였어. 성격도 쾌활하고 영리해 보이니, 어쩌면 저 녀석을 잘 다스릴지도 모르지. 흑인 세들리 부인에다 마호가니 빛깔 손주 열두 명을 보느니, 여보, 차라리 저 처녀가 낫지.\"\n\n그리하여 모든 일이 레베카의 운수를 향해 미소 짓는 듯 보였다. 그녀는 저녁 식사 자리에 갈 때면 당연한 듯 조스의 팔짱을 꼈고, 그의 무개마차 마부석에 그와 나란히 앉기도 했다(그는 회색 말들을 몰며 그 자리에 의젓하고 위풍당당하게 앉아 있을 때면 그야말로 굉장한 \"멋쟁이\"였다). 그리고 비록 누구 하나 결혼 이야기를 입 밖에 내지는 않았지만, 모두가 그 일을 이해하고 있는 듯했다. 그녀에게 필요한 것은 청혼뿐이었다. 아, 이때 레베카는 어머니가 없다는 사실을 얼마나 절감했던가! 다정하고 자상한 어머니라면 단 십 분 만에 그 일을 처리하고, 은밀하고 섬세한 대화를 조금 나누는 사이에, 그 수줍은 청년의 입술에서 흥미로운 고백을 끄집어냈을 텐데 말이다!\n\n마차가 웨스트민스터 다리를 건널 무렵의 사정이 이러했다.\n\n일행은 때맞춰 왕립 정원에 도착했다. 위풍당당한 조스가 삐걱대는 마차에서 내리자 군중이 그 뚱뚱한 신사를 향해 환호를 보냈고, 그는 레베카를 팔에 낀 채 걸어가면서 얼굴을 붉히면서도 자못 거창하고 위풍당당해 보였다. 조지는 물론 애밀리아를 맡았다. 그녀는 햇살을 받은 장미나무처럼 행복해 보였다.\n\n\"이봐, 도빈.\" 조지가 말했다. \"숄이랑 물건들 좀 맡아 주게나. 자네는 참 좋은 친구야.\" 그리하여 그가 세들리 양과 짝을 이루고, 조스가 레베카를 곁에 낀 채 문을 비집고 정원 안으로 들어가는 동안, 정직한 도빈은 숄에 팔을 내어 주고 일행 전체의 입장료를 문간에서 치르는 것으로 만족했다.\n\n그는 무척이나 겸손하게 그들 뒤를 따라 걸었다. 흥을 깨고 싶지 않았던 것이다. 레베카와 조스에 대해서는 손톱만큼도 관심이 없었다. 다만 그는 애밀리아라면 저 빛나는 조지 오스본에게도 부족함이 없는 여자라고 여겼고, 그 잘 어울리는 한 쌍이 산책로를 누비며 소녀를 기쁘고 황홀하게 만드는 모습을 지켜보면서, 그녀의 천진한 행복을 일종의 아버지 같은 흐뭇함으로 바라보았다. 어쩌면 그는 자기 팔에도 숄 말고 다른 무언가를 걸쳐 봤으면 좋겠다고 느꼈는지도 모른다(사람들은 그 꺽다리 젊은 장교가 여자의 짐을 들고 다니는 꼴을 보며 웃었다). 그러나 윌리엄 도빈은 이기적인 계산이라는 것에는 도무지 빠지는 일이 거의 없는 사람이었다. 그러니 친구가 즐거운 시간을 보내고 있는 한, 그가 어찌 불만스러울 수 있었겠는가? 그리고 실은, 그 정원의 온갖 즐길 거리들, 그러니까 늘 환히 밝혀 두는 십만 개의 여분 등불이며, 정원 한복판 금빛 조가비 차양 아래서 황홀한 가락을 연주하던 삼각모 쓴 바이올린 연주자들이며, 희극적인 노래든 감상적인 노래든 그곳에서 사람들의 귀를 사로잡던 가수들이며, 펄쩍거리는 런던내기 남녀들이 짝을 지어 펄쩍거리고 쿵쾅대고 웃어 가며 추던 시골 춤이며, 사키 부인이 별까지 닿는 느슨한 밧줄을 타고 하늘로 솟구쳐 오를 참이라고 알리던 신호며, 늘 등불 밝힌 은둔자 오두막에 앉아 있던 은둔자며, 젊은 연인들의 밀회에 그토록 안성맞춤이던 어두운 산책로며, 허름하고 낡은 제복 차림의 사람들이 돌리던 흑맥주 잔이며, 행복한 만찬객들이 거의 보이지도 않는 햄 조각을 먹는 시늉을 하던 반짝이는 칸막이 좌석이며, 그리고 그 마음씨 좋고 미소 짓는 얼간이 같은, 짐작건대 그 시절에도 그곳을 관장하고 있었을 점잖은 심프슨이며, 이 모든 것에 대해 윌리엄 도빈 대위는 눈곱만치의 관심도 두지 않았다.\n\n그는 애밀리아의 흰 캐시미어 숄을 들고 다녔고, 금빛 조가비 차양 아래서 새먼 부인이 \"보로디노 전투\"(최근 러시아에서 참패를 맛본 그 코르시카 출신 벼락출세자를 겨냥한 사나운 합창곡)를 노래하는 동안 그 옆을 지키고 서 있었다. 그러다 도빈 씨는 자리를 뜨면서 그 곡을 흥얼거려 보려 했는데, 정작 자기가 흥얼거리고 있는 것이 애밀리아 세들리가 저녁 식사를 하러 계단을 내려오며 부르던 그 곡조임을 깨달았다.\n\n그는 자기 자신에게 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 실은 그가 올빼미보다 나을 것 없는 노래 솜씨를 가졌기 때문이다.\n\n물론 두 명씩 짝을 이룬 우리의 젊은이들이, 저녁 내내 함께 붙어 다니자고 더없이 엄숙한 약속을 했다가 그러고는 십 분 만에 뿔뿔이 흩어졌다는 것은 두말할 나위 없는 일이다. 보크스홀에서의 일행이란 늘 흩어지기 마련이었으나, 그것은 그저 저녁 식사 때 다시 모여 그동안 각자 겪은 모험을 이야기하기 위함일 뿐이었다.\n\n오스본 씨와 애밀리아 양의 모험은 어떠했던가? 그것은 비밀이다. 그러나 이것만은 분명히 해 두자. 그들은 더없이 행복했고, 행실은 나무랄 데 없었다. 그리고 그들은 지난 십오 년 동안 줄곧 함께 지내 온 사이였으므로, 그 단둘만의 시간이라고 해서 딱히 새로울 것은 없었다.\n\n그러나 레베카 샤프 양과 그 뚱뚱한 동행이 호젓한 산책로로 접어들어 둘만의 길을 잃었을 때(물론 거기에도 비슷하게 거니는 한 백 쌍쯤은 더 있었지만), 두 사람은 그 상황이 더없이 애틋하고 결정적이라는 것을 느꼈다. 그리고 샤프 양은 지금이 아니면 영영 안 될 순간이라고, 세들리 씨의 그 소심한 입술에서 떨고 있는 고백을 끌어낼 순간이라고 생각했다. 두 사람은 앞서 모스크바 파노라마를 구경했는데, 거기서 어떤 무례한 사내가 샤프 양의 발을 밟는 바람에 그녀가 작은 비명을 지르며 세들리 씨의 품으로 넘어진 일이 있었다. 그리고 이 자그마한 사건은 그 신사의 애틋함과 자신감을 어찌나 북돋아 놓았던지, 그는 자기가 좋아하는 인도 이야기 몇 토막을 적어도 여섯 번째로 그녀에게 또다시 늘어놓았던 것이다.\n\n\"저도 인도를 한번 보고 싶어요!\" 레베카가 말했다.\n\n\"그래요?\" 조지프가 더없이 애간장을 녹이는 다정함으로 말했다. 그러고는 이 교묘한 질문에 뒤이어 한층 더 다정한 물음을 던지려던 참이 분명했다(그는 연신 헐떡이며 숨을 몰아쉬고 있었고, 그의 심장께에 놓인 레베카의 손은 그 기관이 열에 들떠 두근거리는 박동을 헤아릴 수 있을 정도였으니까). 그런데, 아, 얄궂게도! 불꽃놀이를 알리는 종이 울렸고, 한바탕 부산하게 떠밀고 내달리는 소동이 벌어지는 통에, 이 흥미로운 연인들도 사람들의 물결을 따라가지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n도빈 대위는 저녁 식사 자리에 합석할까 하는 생각을 잠시 품었다. 사실 그는 보크스홀의 오락거리가 딱히 흥겹지 않다고 느꼈던 것이다. 그러나 그는 이제 다시 모인 두 쌍이 마주 앉은 칸막이 좌석 앞을 두 번이나 어슬렁거렸건만, 아무도 그에게 눈길 한번 주지 않았다. 식기는 네 사람 분이 차려져 있었다. 짝지은 두 쌍은 더없이 행복하게 재잘거리고 있었고, 도빈은 마치 자기가 이 세상에 존재한 적조차 없는 양 깨끗이 잊혔다는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"내가 끼면 거추장스럽기만 하겠군.\" 대위가 다소 쓸쓸하게 그들을 바라보며 말했다. \"차라리 은둔자한테 가서 이야기나 나누는 게 낫겠어.\" 그러고는 그는 사람들의 웅성거림과 떠들썩한 소리, 잔치의 달그락거리는 소음에서 벗어나, 그 끝에 저 유명한 판지로 만든 은둔자가 살고 있는 어두운 산책로로 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 들어갔다. 도빈에게는 그다지 즐거운 일이 아니었다. 그리고 사실 보크스홀에서 혼자가 된다는 것은, 내 경험에 비추어 보건대, 독신 남자가 빠져들 수 있는 가장 처량한 소일거리 중 하나인 것이다.\n\n그러는 동안 두 쌍은 칸막이 좌석에서 더없이 행복했고, 그곳에서는 가장 유쾌하고 다정한 대화가 오갔다. 조스는 한껏 신이 나서 위풍당당하게 종업원들을 이리저리 부려 먹었다. 그는 샐러드를 만들고, 샴페인 코르크를 뽑고, 닭고기를 썰고, 식탁에 차려진 음식 대부분을 먹고 마셨다. 마침내 그는 럭 펀치를 한 사발 가져오라고 우겼다. 보크스홀에서는 다들 럭 펀치를 마셨던 것이다. \"여보게, 럭 펀치.\"\n\n바로 그 럭 펀치 한 사발이 이 모든 이야기의 원인이 되었다. 그리고 다른 어떤 것 못지않게, 럭 펀치 한 사발이라고 해서 원인이 되지 못할 까닭이 무엇이란 말인가? 청산가리 한 사발이 아름다운 로저먼드가 세상을 등진 원인이 아니었던가? 포도주 한 사발이 알렉산드로스 대왕이 세상을 떠난 원인이 아니었던가? 적어도 렘프리에르 박사는 그렇게 말하지 않던가? 그와 마찬가지로, 이 럭 펀치 한 사발이 우리가 지금 들려주고 있는 이 \"영웅 없는 소설\"의 주요 등장인물 모두의 운명에 영향을 미쳤던 것이다. 비록 그들 대부분은 그 펀치를 한 방울도 입에 대지 않았건만, 그것은 그들의 삶에 영향을 미쳤다.\n\n젊은 숙녀들은 그것을 마시지 않았다. 오스본도 그것을 좋아하지 않았다. 그 결과 그 뚱뚱한 식도락가 조스가 사발에 담긴 것을 죄다 마셔 버렸고, 그가 사발을 통째로 비운 결과는, 처음에는 놀랍기 그지없다가 이내 거의 보기 딱할 지경에 이른 흥겨움이었다. 그는 어찌나 큰 소리로 떠들고 웃어 댔던지 수십 명의 구경꾼이 좌석 주위로 몰려들었고, 그 안에 있던 죄 없는 일행은 몹시 난처해졌다. 게다가 그는 자청해서 노래까지 불렀는데(술 취한 신사들 특유의 청승맞게 높은 가락으로 불러 댔다), 금빛 조가비 차양 아래 악사들 주위에 모여 있던 청중까지 거의 빼앗다시피 끌어모았고, 듣는 이들로부터 대단한 갈채를 받았다.\n\n\"잘한다, 뚱보!\" 누군가 외쳤다. \"한 번 더, 대니얼 램버트!\" 또 다른 사람이 외쳤다. \"줄타기에 딱 어울리는 몸매로구먼!\" 또 다른 익살꾼이 소리치자, 숙녀들은 이루 말할 수 없이 놀랐고 오스본 씨는 크게 노했다.\n\n\"제발, 조스, 우리 일어나서 갑시다.\" 그 신사가 외쳤고, 젊은 여자들도 자리에서 일어섰다.\n\n\"잠깐, 내 사랑스러운 둥기둥기 아가씨.\" 이제 사자처럼 대담해진 조스가 외치며 레베카 양의 허리를 끌어안았다. 레베카는 흠칫했지만, 손을 빼낼 수가 없었다. 바깥의 웃음소리가 곱절로 커졌다. 조스는 계속 마시고, 사랑을 속삭이고, 노래를 불렀다. 그러고는 눈을 찡긋거리며 청중을 향해 잔을 우아하게 흔들어 보이고는, 너 나 할 것 없이 누구든 들어와서 자기 펀치를 함께 들자고 청했다.\n\n오스본 씨가 이 초대를 틈타 한몫 끼려는 장화 신은 한 신사를 막 때려눕히려던 참이었고, 한바탕 소동이 피할 수 없어 보였다. 그런데 천만다행으로, 정원을 거닐고 있던 도빈이라는 이름의 한 신사가 그 좌석으로 다가왔다. \"꺼져, 이 멍청이들아!\" 이 신사는 그렇게 말하며 군중을 어깨로 우르르 밀쳐 냈고, 군중은 이내 그의 삼각모와 험상궂은 기색 앞에서 사라져 버렸다. 그러고는 그가 몹시 격앙된 상태로 좌석 안으로 들어섰다.\n\n\"맙소사! 도빈, 자네 어디 있었던 거야?\" 오스본이 친구의 팔에서 흰 캐시미어 숄을 낚아채 애밀리아를 둘둘 감싸며 말했다. \"쓸모 있게 좀 굴어서 여기 조스 좀 맡아 주게. 그동안 내가 숙녀들을 마차로 모실 테니.\"\n\n조스가 끼어들겠다고 일어서려 했으나, 오스본의 손가락 한 번 미는 힘에 그는 헐떡이며 도로 자리에 주저앉았고, 덕분에 중위는 무사히 숙녀들을 데리고 나갈 수 있었다. 조스는 그들이 물러가는 동안 손에 입을 맞춰 작별 인사를 보내며 \"잘 가요! 잘 가요!\" 하고 딸꾹질 섞인 소리를 내뱉었다. 그러더니 도빈 대위의 손을 붙들고 더없이 가련하게 흐느끼며, 그 신사에게 자기 사랑의 비밀을 털어놓았다. 그는 방금 나간 그 처녀를 흠모한다고, 자기 행실 때문에 그녀의 마음을 아프게 했음을 안다고, 분명히 그랬다고 했다. 그는 내일 아침 하노버 광장의 세인트 조지 교회에서 그녀와 결혼하겠노라고, 램버스에 있는 캔터베리 대주교를 깨워서라도 그렇게 하겠노라고, 정말이지 맹세코 그러겠노라고, 대주교를 대기시켜 두겠노라고 했다. 그러자 도빈 대위는 이 말을 빌미 삼아, 정원을 나가 어서 램버스 궁으로 가자고 그를 교묘하게 구슬렸고, 일단 문밖으로 나오자 손쉽게 조스 세들리 씨를 삯마차에 태워 무사히 그의 숙소까지 데려다 놓았다.\n\n조지 오스본은 처녀들을 무사히 집까지 바래다주었다. 그러고는 그들 뒤로 문이 닫히자, 러셀 광장을 가로질러 걸어가면서 야경꾼이 깜짝 놀랄 만큼 큰 소리로 웃어 댔다. 애밀리아는 두 사람이 위층으로 올라가는 동안 친구를 몹시 안쓰러운 눈빛으로 바라보더니, 그녀에게 입을 맞추고는 더 이상 아무 말도 없이 잠자리에 들었다.\n\n\"그이는 내일 청혼할 거야.\" 레베카는 생각했다. \"그이는 나를 네 번이나 마음속 연인이라고 불렀어. 애밀리아가 보는 앞에서 내 손을 꼭 쥐었지. 그이는 분명 내일 청혼할 거야.\" 애밀리아도 똑같이 생각했다. 그리고 짐작건대 그녀는 자기가 신부 들러리로 입을 옷이며, 그 사랑스러운 새 올케에게 해 줄 선물이며, 머지않아 자기 자신이 주인공 노릇을 하게 될지도 모를 또 다른 예식 따위를 생각했을 것이다. 등등, 등등, 등등.\n\n오, 철없는 젊은이들이여! 그대들은 럭 펀치의 위력을 어찌 그리도 모르는가! 밤에 펀치 속에 든 럭이, 아침에 머릿속에 든 고통(rack)에 견주면 무엇이란 말인가? 이 진실에 대해서라면 나는 한 사람의 남자로서 보증할 수 있다. 세상에 보크스홀 펀치가 일으키는 두통만 한 두통은 없다. 이십 년의 세월이 흘렀건만, 나는 단 두 잔이 가져온 결과를 아직도 기억한다. 두 잔! 그것도 와인 잔으로 겨우 두 잔이었으니, 신사의 명예를 걸고 하는 말이다. 그런데 간이 좋지 않던 조지프 세들리는 그 끔찍한 혼합물을 적어도 한 쿼트는 들이켰던 것이다.\n\n레베카가 자기 운수가 환히 밝아 오리라 여겼던 그 이튿날 아침, 세들리는 붓으로는 차마 묘사할 수 없는 고통 속에서 신음하고 있었다. 소다수는 아직 발명되기 전이었다. 믿어지겠는가만, 묽은 맥주가 전날 밤의 폭음으로 생긴 열기를 불행한 신사들이 달랠 수 있는 유일한 음료였다. 그 순한 음료를 앞에 놓고 신음하고 있는 보글리 월라의 전직 징세관을, 조지 오스본은 그의 숙소 소파에서 발견했다. 도빈은 이미 그 방에 와서, 마음씨 좋게 전날 밤의 환자를 돌보고 있었다. 두 장교는 뻗어 있는 그 주신(酒神)의 신도를 바라보다가 서로를 곁눈질하며, 더없이 끔찍한 동정의 웃음을 주고받았다. 신사 중에서도 가장 근엄하고 빈틈없는 사람인 세들리의 시종조차, 장의사 같은 침묵과 엄숙함을 지녔건만, 그 가엾은 주인을 바라보며 표정을 가다듬기가 여간 힘들지 않았다.\n\n\"세들리 나리께서 어젯밤에 어찌나 사납게 구셨는지 모릅니다요, 나리.\" 오스본이 계단을 올라올 때 그가 은밀히 귓속말로 속삭였다. \"삯마차 마부와 한판 붙으려 하셨답니다요, 나리. 대위 나리께서 어쩔 수 없이 어린애처럼 두 팔로 안아서 위층까지 모셔 왔습죠.\" 브러시 씨가 말하는 동안 그 얼굴에 순간적으로 미소가 어른거렸다. 그러나 그것도 잠시, 그는 응접실 문을 활짝 열어젖히고 \"호스빈 나리\"라고 알리면서, 이내 평소의 헤아릴 길 없는 평정으로 돌아갔다.\n\n\"좀 어떤가, 세들리?\" 그 젊은 익살꾼은 자기 희생양을 한번 훑어보고는 말문을 열었다. \"부러진 데는 없고? 아래층에 눈이 시퍼렇게 멍들고 머리에 붕대 감은 삯마차 마부가 와서는, 자네를 법대로 처리하겠다고 별러 대던데.\"\n\n\"무슨 소린가, 법이라니?\" 세들리가 힘없이 물었다.\n\n\"어젯밤에 그자를 흠씬 두들겨 팼잖나. 안 그런가, 도빈? 자네 그 몰리뉴처럼 주먹을 휘둘러 댔다고. 야경꾼 말로는 사내가 그렇게 곧장 나가떨어지는 건 처음 봤다더군. 도빈한테 물어보게.\"\n\n\"자네가 마부랑 한판 붙은 건 맞아.\" 도빈 대위가 말했다. \"게다가 제법 사납게 싸우기도 했고.\"\n\n\"그리고 보크스홀에서 그 흰 코트 입은 친구는 또 어땠고! 조스가 어찌나 달려들던지! 여자들은 또 어찌나 비명을 질러 댔는지! 정말이지, 자네 그 모습을 보니 내 속이 다 시원하더군. 난 자네 같은 민간인들은 배짱이 없는 줄 알았는데. 하지만 조스, 자네가 술이 거나하게 올랐을 땐 절대로 자네 앞길을 막지 않겠네.\"\n\n\"내가 화가 나면 꽤나 무서운 모양이지.\" 조스가 소파에서 내뱉으며, 어찌나 처량하고도 우스꽝스러운 표정을 지었던지, 대위의 예의범절도 더는 그를 억누르지 못했고, 그와 오스본은 한바탕 쩌렁쩌렁한 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n오스본은 인정사정없이 우세를 몰아붙였다. 그는 조스를 겁쟁이로 여겼다. 그는 조스와 레베카 사이에 걸려 있는 결혼 문제를 마음속으로 곱씹어 보고 있던 터였고, 자기, 그러니까 ××연대의 조지 오스본이 장차 장가들 집안의 일원이 한낱 보잘것없는 계집, 한낱 벼락 출세한 가정교사 따위와 격에 안 맞는 혼인을 한다는 것이 영 마뜩잖았던 것이다. \"자네가 주먹질을 했다고, 이 가엾은 친구야!\" 오스본이 말했다. \"자네가 무섭다고? 이런, 이 사람아, 자네는 제대로 서 있지도 못했어. 자네 자신은 울고 있으면서도 정원에 있는 사람들을 죄다 웃겼다고. 자네 그렇게 청승맞게 굴었어, 조스. 자네가 노래 부른 거 기억 안 나나?\"\n\n\"뭘 불렀다고?\" 조스가 물었다.\n\n\"감상적인 노래 말이야. 그리고 로자인지, 레베카인지, 그 이름이 뭐더라, 애밀리아의 그 자그마한 친구를 '내 사랑스러운 둥기둥기 아가씨'라고 부른 거 말이야.\" 그러더니 이 인정머리 없는 젊은이는 도빈의 손을 덥석 붙들고 그 장면을 그대로 재연해 보였고, 그 원조 연기자는 기겁을 했으며, 도빈이 마음씨 좋게 제발 좀 봐주라고 사정했건만 막무가내였다.\n\n\"내가 저 친구를 봐줘야 할 까닭이 뭔가?\" 친구가 만류하자 오스본이 말했다. 그들이 골롭 박사에게 환자를 맡겨 두고 자리를 뜬 뒤였다. \"도대체 무슨 권리로 저 친구가 잘난 척 거들먹거리며 우리를 보크스홀에서 바보로 만든단 말인가? 저 친구한테 추파를 던지며 사랑을 속삭이는 그 자그마한 여학생은 또 뭐고? 젠장, 그 여자가 끼어들지 않아도 저 집안은 이미 충분히 격이 떨어진다고. 가정교사야 뭐 그럭저럭 괜찮지만, 난 내 처남댁만큼은 숙녀이길 바라네. 난 마음이 너그러운 사람이야. 하지만 나름의 자존심이 있고 내 분수를 알지. 그 여자도 제 분수를 알게 해야 해. 그리고 난 저 거들먹대는 인도 부자 나리를 한 수 꺾어서, 지금보다 더한 바보가 되는 걸 막을 작정이야. 그래서 그 여자가 저 친구를 상대로 소송이라도 걸까 조심하라고 일러둔 거라네.\"\n\n\"자네가 잘 알아서 하겠지.\" 도빈이 자못 미심쩍은 투로 말했다. \"자네야 늘 토리당원이었고, 자네 집안은 영국에서 가장 오래된 가문 중 하나니까. 하지만……\"\n\n\"가서 처녀들이나 보세. 그리고 자네는 자네대로 샤프 양한테 사랑을 속삭여 보든가.\" 여기서 중위가 친구의 말을 가로막았다. 그러나 도빈 대위는 러셀 광장의 젊은 숙녀들을 날마다 찾아가는 오스본의 방문길에 동참하기를 사양했다.\n\n조지가 홀번에서 사우샘프턴 거리로 내려가다가, 세들리 저택의 서로 다른 두 층에서 망을 보고 있는 두 개의 머리를 보고는 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n사실인즉, 응접실 발코니에 나와 있던 애밀리아 양은 오스본 씨가 사는 광장 건너편을 향해 무척이나 간절하게 시선을 던지며 바로 그 중위를 기다리고 있었고, 삼 층 자기 자그마한 침실에 있던 샤프 양은 조지프 씨의 그 거대한 형체가 시야에 나타날 때까지 망을 보고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"앤 언니가 망루에서 망을 보고 있군그래.\" 그가 애밀리아에게 말했다. \"하지만 오는 사람은 아무도 없어.\" 그러고는 웃음을 터뜨리며 이 농담을 한껏 즐기면서, 세들리 양에게 그 오라비의 비참한 처지를 더없이 우스꽝스러운 말로 묘사해 보였다.\n\n\"그렇게 웃다니 정말 너무 매정해요, 조지.\" 그녀가 유난히 시무룩한 표정으로 말했다. 그러나 조지는 그 가련하고 풀 죽은 기색에 한층 더 웃어 댈 뿐, 이 농담이 더없이 재미나다고 고집하면서, 샤프 양이 아래층으로 내려오자 그 매력이 그 뚱뚱한 민간인에게 미친 영향을 두고 자못 흥겹게 그녀를 놀려 댔다.\n\n\"오, 샤프 양! 당신이 오늘 아침 그 사람 꼴을 봤어야 하는 건데.\" 그가 말했다. \"꽃무늬 실내복을 입고 끙끙 앓는 꼴이며, 소파에서 몸부림치는 꼴이라니. 약제사 골롭한테 혀를 쑥 빼물어 보이는 꼴을 봤어야 하는 건데 말이오.\"\n\n\"누구를요?\" 샤프 양이 말했다.\n\n\"누구라니? 오, 누구긴요? 물론 도빈 대위지요. 그러고 보니 어젯밤에 우리 모두가 그렇게나 살뜰히 챙겨 드린 그 양반 말입니다.\"\n\n\"우리가 그분께 너무 박정했어요.\" 에미가 얼굴을 몹시 붉히며 말했다. \"전, 전 그분을 까맣게 잊고 있었어요.\"\n\n\"그야 물론 그랬겠지요.\" 오스본이 여전히 웃음을 머금은 채 외쳤다.\n\n\"늘 도빈만 생각하고 있을 수야 없지 않소, 애밀리아. 안 그렇소, 샤프 양?\"\n\n\"저녁 식사 때 그분이 와인 잔을 엎질렀던 일 말고는,\" 샤프 양이 도도한 기색으로 고개를 홱 젖히며 말했다. \"전 도빈 대위라는 사람이 이 세상에 있다는 사실에 단 한순간도 마음을 쓴 적이 없답니다.\"\n\n\"좋아요, 샤프 양, 그 사람한테 그대로 전하지요.\" 오스본이 말했다. 그가 이렇게 말하는 사이, 샤프 양은 이 젊은 장교에게 불신과 증오의 감정을 품기 시작했는데, 정작 그는 자기가 그런 감정을 불러일으켰다는 것을 전혀 알아채지 못했다. \"이 사람이 나를 놀림감으로 삼겠다 이거지?\" 레베카는 생각했다. \"이 사람이 조지프한테 내 험담을 늘어놓은 걸까? 그래서 그이한테 겁을 준 걸까? 어쩌면 그이는 오지 않을지도 몰라.\" 그녀의 눈앞에 막이 한 겹 드리워졌고, 가슴이 몹시 빠르게 뛰었다.\n\n\"당신은 늘 농담만 하시는군요.\" 그녀가 최대한 천진하게 미소 지으며 말했다. \"마음껏 농담하세요, 조지 씨. 저를 두둔해 줄 사람은 아무도 없으니까요.\" 그리고 그녀가 걸어 나가는 동안 애밀리아가 자기를 나무라는 눈빛으로 바라보자, 조지 오스본은 이 가련하고 가냘픈 사람에게 공연한 박정함을 베푼 데 대해 사내로서 약간의 양심의 가책을 느꼈다. \"사랑하는 애밀리아.\" 그가 말했다. \"당신은 너무 착하고, 너무 마음씨가 고와요. 당신은 세상을 모르오. 나는 알지. 그리고 당신의 그 자그마한 친구 샤프 양도 제 분수를 깨우쳐야 해요.\"\n\n\"당신은 조스가 정말……\"\n\n\"솔직히 말해서, 여보, 나도 모르겠소. 그럴 수도 있고, 안 그럴 수도 있고. 내가 그 사람 주인은 아니니까. 다만 내가 아는 건, 그 사람이 아주 어리석고 허영심 많은 친구라는 것, 그리고 어젯밤에 내 사랑스러운 아가씨를 아주 곤혹스럽고 난처한 처지에 몰아넣었다는 것뿐이오. 내 사랑스러운 둥기둥기 아가씨!\" 그는 또다시 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 어찌나 익살스럽게 굴던지 에미도 따라 웃고 말았다.\n\n그날 하루 종일 조스는 끝내 오지 않았다. 그러나 애밀리아는 이 일로 걱정하지 않았다. 그 자그마한 책략가가 실은 샘보 씨의 부관 격인 사환을 조지프 씨의 숙소로 보내, 그가 약속했던 책을 가져오게 하고 또 그가 어떤지 안부를 물어 오게 했던 것이다. 조스의 하인 브러시 씨를 통해 돌아온 대답은, 주인이 앓아누워 있으며 방금 의사가 다녀갔다는 것이었다. 그이는 내일은 올 거야, 그녀는 생각했지만, 이 일에 대해서는 레베카에게 한마디도 꺼낼 용기가 차마 나지 않았다. 그리고 그 젊은 여자 자신도 보크스홀에서의 그날 밤 이후 저녁 내내 어떤 식으로도 그 일을 입에 올리지 않았다.\n\n그러나 그 이튿날, 두 젊은 숙녀가 소파에 앉아 바느질을 하는 척, 편지를 쓰는 척, 혹은 소설을 읽는 척하고 있을 때, 샘보가 여느 때처럼 사람 좋은 웃음을 띠고 겨드랑이에 꾸러미 하나를 끼고 쟁반에 쪽지 하나를 받쳐 든 채 방으로 들어왔다. \"조스 나리께서 보내신 쪽지입니다요, 아가씨.\" 샘보가 말했다.\n\n애밀리아는 그것을 펼치며 얼마나 떨었던가!\n\n쪽지의 내용은 이러했다.\n사랑하는 애밀리아. 「숲속의 고아」를 보낸다. 어제는 너무 아파서 못 갔구나. 나는 오늘 첼트넘으로 떠난다. 보크스홀에서 한 내 행실에 대해, 가능하다면 그 상냥한 샤프 양에게 부디 사과를 전해 다오. 그리고 그 끔찍한 저녁 식사에 들떠서 내가 내뱉었을지 모를 말들을 모두 용서하고 잊어 달라고 그분께 간청해 다오. 건강이 몹시 상한 터라, 회복되는 대로 나는 몇 달간 스코틀랜드로 떠날 생각이다. 그럼 이만.\n\n너의 진실한 조스 세들리로부터.\n\n그것은 사형 선고였다. 모든 것이 끝나 버렸다. 애밀리아는 차마 레베카의 창백한 얼굴과 불타는 눈을 바라볼 엄두가 나지 않아, 친구의 무릎 위에 그 편지를 떨어뜨리고는 자리에서 일어나 위층 자기 방으로 올라가, 그 가냘픈 가슴이 미어지도록 울었다.\n\n이윽고 가정부 블렌킨솝이 위로의 말을 들고 그녀를 찾아왔고, 애밀리아는 그 어깨에 기대어 마음을 터놓고 울며 한결 시름을 덜었다. \"너무 상심 마세요, 아가씨. 말씀드리기가 뭣했습니다만, 이 집 식구들 가운데 처음 말고는 그 아가씨를 좋아한 사람이 아무도 없답니다. 제 두 눈으로 그 아가씨가 마님 편지를 읽는 걸 똑똑히 봤어요. 피너 말로는, 그 아가씨가 늘 아가씨 패물 상자며 서랍이며, 또 누구 할 것 없이 다들 서랍을 뒤지고 다닌다는데요, 아가씨 그 흰 리본도 자기 상자에 넣었을 게 분명하다지 뭡니까.\"\n\n\"그건 내가 준 거야, 내가 줬다고.\" 애밀리아가 말했다.\n\n그러나 이 말도 블렌킨솝 부인이 샤프 양에 대해 품은 생각을 바꾸지는 못했다. \"난 그런 가정교사들은 못 믿어, 피너.\" 그녀가 하녀에게 말했다. \"숙녀랍시고 거드름을 피우고 벼락출세한 티를 내지만, 받는 삯이라곤 너나 나보다 나을 게 없는 것들이야.\"\n\n이제 가엾은 애밀리아만 빼고 이 집안의 모든 사람에게, 레베카가 떠나야 한다는 것이 분명해졌고, 위아래 할 것 없이(언제나 그 한 사람만은 예외였다) 그 일이 되도록 빨리 이루어져야 한다는 데 의견을 같이했다. 우리의 착한 아이는 자기 서랍이며 찬장이며 손가방이며 자질구레한 상자들을 죄다 뒤져, 자기 가운이며 목도리며 장식끈이며 실패며 레이스며 비단 양말이며 온갖 자잘한 장신구를 하나하나 살펴보고는, 이것저것을 골라내어 레베카에게 줄 자그마한 꾸러미를 만들었다. 그리고 자기 나이만큼의 기니를 주겠노라 약속했던 그 너그러운 영국 상인 아버지를 찾아가서는, 자기는 아쉬울 게 하나도 없지만 분명 그 돈이 필요할 사랑하는 레베카에게 그 돈을 주시라고 노신사에게 졸랐다.\n\n그녀는 심지어 조지 오스본에게도 한몫 보태게 했고, 조지도 기꺼이(그는 군대를 통틀어 둘째가라면 서러울 만큼 손이 큰 젊은이였으니까) 본드 거리로 가서 돈으로 살 수 있는 가장 좋은 모자와 짧은 외투를 사 왔다.\n\n\"이건 조지가 너한테 주는 선물이야, 사랑하는 레베카.\" 애밀리아가 이 선물들이 담긴 상자를 자못 자랑스러워하며 말했다. \"그이는 정말 안목이 뛰어나지! 그이 같은 사람은 또 없을 거야.\"\n\n\"그래, 또 없겠지.\" 레베카가 대답했다. \"그분께 얼마나 감사한지 몰라!\" 그러나 그녀는 속으로 이렇게 생각하고 있었다. \"내 결혼을 막은 건 바로 그 조지 오스본이야.\" 그리고 그녀는 그만큼 조지 오스본을 사랑했다.\n\n그녀는 더없이 평온한 마음으로 떠날 채비를 했고, 착한 애밀리아가 주는 온갖 선물을 꼭 알맞을 만큼만 망설이고 사양하는 시늉을 한 끝에 받아들였다. 그녀는 물론 세들리 부인에게 영원한 감사를 맹세했다. 그러나 그 마음씨 좋은 부인에게 지나치게 들러붙지는 않았는데, 부인 쪽에서 거북해하며 자기를 피하고 싶어 하는 기색이 역력했기 때문이다. 세들리 씨가 돈주머니를 건네자 그녀는 그의 손에 입을 맞추었고, 앞으로 그를 자기의 더없이 자상한 벗이자 보호자로 여기게 해 달라고 청했다. 그녀의 태도가 어찌나 감동적이었던지 그는 이십 파운드짜리 수표를 한 장 더 써 줄 뻔했다. 그러나 그는 감정을 억눌렀다. 그를 저녁 식사 자리로 데려갈 마차가 기다리고 있었으므로, 그는 이렇게 말하며 총총히 자리를 떴다. \"잘 가게, 얘야. 런던에 올 때면 언제든 이리로 오너라, 알겠지? 제임스, 맨션 하우스로 가세.\"\n\n마침내 애밀리아 양과의 작별이 다가왔는데, 나는 이 장면 위로는 장막을 드리울 작정이다. 그러나 한 사람은 진심이고 다른 한 사람은 완벽한 연기자였던 한바탕 장면이 지나가고, 더없이 다정한 포옹과 더없이 애절한 눈물과 각성제 병과 마음속 가장 진실한 감정의 일부까지 죄다 동원된 끝에, 레베카와 애밀리아는 헤어졌다. 전자는 영원토록, 영원토록, 또 영원토록 친구를 사랑하겠노라 맹세하면서.\n\n18××년, 궁정 인명록에 실린 C로 시작하는 이름들 가운데 가장 존경받는 축에 드는 것이 바로 크롤리라는 이름이었다. 햄프셔주 그레이트곤트가와 퀸스크롤리에 적을 둔 준남작 핏 크롤리 경의 이름 말이다. 이 명예로운 이름은 또한 여러 해에 걸쳐 의회 명부에도 변함없이 등장했는데, 그 선거구를 번갈아 대표해 온 여러 다른 훌륭한 신사들의 이름과 나란히 올라 있었다.\n\n퀸스크롤리 선거구와 관련해서는 이런 이야기가 전한다. 엘리자베스 여왕이 순행 중에 크롤리에 들러 아침 식사를 하게 되었는데, 그때 그 시절의 크롤리(말끔하게 다듬은 수염에 잘빠진 다리를 지닌 잘생긴 신사였다)가 진상한 유난히 빼어난 햄프셔 맥주에 어찌나 흡족했던지, 즉석에서 크롤리를 의원 두 사람을 의회에 보낼 수 있는 선거구로 승격시켰다는 것이다. 그리고 이 고장은 그 영광스러운 행차의 날 이래로 퀸스크롤리라는 이름을 얻게 되었고, 그 이름을 지금 이 순간까지 지니고 있는 것이다. 그러나 세월이 흐르고, 제국이며 도시며 선거구에 나이가 가져오는 그 온갖 변천을 겪으면서, 퀸스크롤리는 엘리자베스 여왕 시절만큼 인구가 많은 고장이 더는 아니게 되었다. 아니, 이른바 부패 선거구라 일컬어지던 그런 처지로까지 영락하고 말았다. 그래도 핏 크롤리 경이 특유의 우아한 말투로 지극히 정당하게 내뱉곤 했듯이, \"부패라니, 그까짓 거 집어치우게나. 그 덕분에 내가 일 년에 천오백 파운드는 너끈히 벌어들이고 있잖나.\"\n\n핏 크롤리 경(저 위대한 평민 정치가의 이름을 딴 것이었다)은 초대 준남작 월폴 크롤리의 아들이었는데, 월폴 크롤리는 조지 2세 치세에 봉인과 봉랍 관리국에 몸담았다가, 그 시절의 다른 정직한 신사들 상당수가 그러했듯, 공금 횡령 혐의로 탄핵당한 인물이었다. 그리고 월폴 크롤리는 굳이 말할 것도 없이 존 처칠 크롤리의 아들이었으니, 이 이름은 앤 여왕 치세의 그 저명한 군사령관의 이름을 딴 것이었다. 그 가계도(퀸스크롤리에 걸려 있다)에는 그 밖에도 찰스 스튜어트 크롤리, 훗날 베어본즈 크롤리라 불린 인물이 언급되어 있는데, 그는 제임스 1세 시절 크롤리의 아들이었다. 그리고 마지막으로 엘리자베스 여왕 시절의 크롤리가 있었으니, 그는 갈라진 수염에 갑옷을 두른 모습으로 그림의 맨 앞에 그려져 있었다. 그의 조끼에서는 으레 그러하듯 나무 한 그루가 자라나고, 그 큰 가지마다 위에서 말한 그 영예로운 이름들이 새겨져 있었다. 준남작 핏 크롤리 경(이 전기의 주인공이다)의 이름 바로 곁에는 그의 동생인 목사 뷰트 크롤리(이 목사 양반이 태어났을 때 그 위대한 평민 정치가는 실각해 있었다)의 이름이 적혀 있었는데, 그는 크롤리컴스네일비의 교구 목사였다. 그 밖에도 크롤리 가문의 여러 남녀 일족의 이름이 적혀 있었다.\n\n핏 경은 처음에 그리젤과 결혼했는데, 그녀는 빙키 경 멍고 빙키의 여섯째 딸이었고, 따라서 던다스 씨의 사촌이 되는 셈이었다. 그녀는 그에게 두 아들을 낳아 주었다. 핏은 아버지를 따랐다기보다는 저 하늘이 점지한 재상의 이름을 딴 것이었고, 로던 크롤리는 황태자의 벗이었던 인물의 이름을 딴 것이었으니, 그 인물을 국왕 조지 4세는 어찌나 깡그리 잊어버렸던지. 부인이 세상을 떠난 지 여러 해 뒤에 핏 경은 로자를 제단으로 이끌었으니, 그녀는 머드베리의 G. 도슨 씨의 딸이었다. 그는 그녀에게서 두 딸을 얻었는데, 이제 그 딸들을 위하여 레베카 샤프 양이 가정교사로 고용된 것이었다. 보다시피 이 젊은 숙녀는 매우 품위 있는 연줄을 지닌 집안으로 들어가게 되었고, 방금 러셀 광장에서 떠나온 그 보잘것없는 무리보다 훨씬 더 격조 높은 사교계에서 활동하게 될 참이었다.\n\n그녀가 제자들에게 합류하라는 명을 받은 것은 헌 봉투에 적힌 쪽지를 통해서였는데, 그 내용은 다음과 같았다.\n\n핏 크롤리 경은 샤프 양과 짐이 화요일까지 이리로 오기를 바라오. 나는 내일 아침 일찍 퀸스크롤리로 떠나기 때문이오.\n\n그레이트곤트가에서.\n\n레베카는 자기가 아는 한 준남작이라는 사람을 본 적이 한 번도 없었다. 그래서 애밀리아와 작별을 고하고, 마음씨 고운 세들리 씨가 지갑에 넣어 준 기니를 헤아려 본 다음, 손수건으로 눈물을 닦는 일을 마치고 나자(이 일은 마차가 거리 모퉁이를 돌자마자 곧바로 끝내 버렸다), 그녀는 준남작이라는 사람이 대체 어떤 인물일지 마음속으로 그려 보기 시작했다. \"별 모양 훈장을 달고 다닐까?\" 그녀는 생각했다. \"아니면 별 훈장은 귀족들만 다는 건가? 어쨌든 그는 주름 장식이 달린 궁정 예복을 더없이 멋들어지게 차려입고, 코번트가든의 로턴 씨처럼 머리에는 분을 살짝 뿌렸겠지. 분명 무섭도록 거만할 테고, 나는 더없이 업신여기는 대접을 받겠지. 그래도 내 고된 운명은 되도록 잘 견뎌 내야 해. 적어도 나는 점잖은 양반들 틈에 있게 될 테고, 천박한 도회지 사람들과 함께 있지는 않을 테니까.\" 그러고는 어느 우화에서 여우가 포도를 두고 말하는 그 모습 그대로의, 바로 그 철학적인 쓰라림을 품고서, 러셀 광장의 벗들을 떠올리기 시작했다.\n\n곤트 광장을 지나 그레이트곤트가로 접어든 마차는 마침내 키 큰 음침한 두 집 사이에 끼인 키 큰 음침한 집 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 그 집들은 저마다 이층 응접실 한가운데 창문 위에 상문장이 걸려 있었는데, 그레이트곤트가의 집들 풍습이 다 그러했고, 이 음울한 동네에는 죽음이 영영 군림하는 듯했다. 핏 경 저택의 이층 창문 덧문은 닫혀 있었고, 식당 덧문은 반쯤 열려 있었으며, 차양은 헌 신문지로 말끔하게 덮여 있었다.\n\n마차를 혼자 몰고 온 마부 존은 굳이 내려서 종을 울리고 싶지 않았다. 그래서 지나가던 우유 배달 소년에게 그 일을 좀 해 달라고 부탁했다. 종이 울리자 식당 덧문 틈새로 머리 하나가 나타났고, 이윽고 문이 열리더니 한 남자가 모습을 드러냈다. 칙칙한 황갈색 반바지에 각반을 차고, 더러운 헌 외투를 걸쳤으며, 뻣뻣한 수염이 돋은 목에는 추레한 헌 목도리를 칭칭 동여매고 있었다. 번들거리는 대머리에, 곁눈질하는 벌건 얼굴, 반짝거리는 잿빛 눈 한 쌍, 그리고 줄곧 히죽거리는 입을 한 남자였다.\n\n\"여기가 핏 크롤리 경 댁이오?\" 마부석에서 존이 물었다.\n\n\"그렇소.\" 문가의 남자가 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 여기 이 트렁크들 좀 내려 받으쇼.\" 존이 말했다.\n\n\"댁이 직접 내리시구려.\" 문지기가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 말들을 떠날 수 없는 거 안 보이오? 자, 손 좀 거들어 주쇼, 양반. 그럼 아가씨가 맥주값이라도 줄 거요.\" 존이 너털웃음을 터뜨리며 말했으니, 그는 이제 더 이상 샤프 양에게 공손하게 굴지 않았던 것이다. 그녀와 그 집안의 연이 끊긴 데다, 떠나오면서 하인들에게 아무것도 쥐여 주지 않았기 때문이었다.\n\n대머리 남자는 반바지 주머니에서 두 손을 빼내더니 이 부름에 응해 다가왔고, 샤프 양의 트렁크를 어깨에 둘러메고 집 안으로 날랐다.\n\n\"미안하지만 이 바구니랑 숄도 좀 받아 주고, 문도 열어 주세요.\" 샤프 양이 말하고는, 잔뜩 분개한 채 마차에서 내렸다. \"세들리 씨께 편지를 써서 당신이 어떻게 처신했는지 알려 드리겠어요.\" 그녀가 마부에게 말했다.\n\n\"그러지 마쇼.\" 그 직책의 사내가 대꾸했다. \"아무것도 안 빠뜨렸길 바라오. 애밀리아 아가씨 드레스들 말이오, 챙겼소? 본래 몸종이 받기로 돼 있던 거 말이오. 댁한테 잘 맞기를 바라오. 문 닫게, 짐. 저 여자한테선 콩고물 하나 안 떨어질 테니까.\" 존은 엄지손가락으로 샤프 양을 가리키며 계속 지껄였다. \"못된 인간이야, 내 말 믿게, 못된 인간이라고.\" 그러고는 세들리 씨의 마부는 마차를 몰고 떠나가 버렸다. 사실인즉 그는 문제의 그 몸종에게 마음을 두고 있었고, 그녀가 응당 받을 부수입을 빼앗겼다는 데에 분개했던 것이다.\n\n각반을 찬 그 사내의 안내에 따라 식당에 들어선 레베카는, 점잖은 집안 식구들이 시골에 가 있을 때면 으레 그러하듯, 그 방이 그런 방들이 흔히 그렇듯 조금도 화사하지 않다는 것을 알아차렸다. 충직한 방들은 마치 주인들의 부재를 애도하는 듯했다. 터키산 양탄자는 둘둘 말려 시무룩하게 찬장 밑으로 물러나 있었다. 그림들은 헌 갈색 종이 뒤에 얼굴을 감추고 있었다. 천장의 등은 음울한 갈색 삼베 자루에 싸여 있었다. 창문 커튼은 온갖 추레한 덮개 밑으로 자취를 감추었다. 월폴 크롤리 경의 대리석 흉상은 그 시커먼 구석에서 맨바닥과 기름칠한 부지깽이 한 벌, 그리고 벽난로 선반 위 텅 빈 명함꽂이를 바라보고 있었다. 술 보관함은 양탄자 뒤로 숨어들어 있었다. 의자들은 벽을 따라 위아래로 뒤집힌 채 포개져 있었다. 그리고 그 조각상 맞은편 어두운 구석에는 자물쇠가 채워진 구식 칼 보관함 하나가 까다로운 표정으로 식기 받침대 위에 놓여 있었다.\n\n그래도 부엌 의자 두 개와 둥근 탁자 하나, 그리고 여위어 빠진 헌 부지깽이와 부젓가락은 벽난로 둘레에 모여 있었고, 약하게 지글거리는 불 위에는 냄비 하나가 걸려 있었다. 탁자 위에는 치즈 한 조각과 빵, 양철 촛대 하나가 놓여 있었고, 한 파인트들이 단지에는 검은 흑맥주가 조금 담겨 있었다.\n\n\"저녁은 드셨겠지? 너무 덥진 않소? 맥주 한 잔 어떻소?\"\n\n\"핏 크롤리 경은 어디 계신가요?\" 샤프 양이 위엄 있게 말했다.\n\n\"히, 히! 내가 핏 크롤리 경이오. 댁 짐을 내려 줬으니 맥주 한 파인트 빚진 거 잊지 마쇼. 히, 히! 정 못 믿겠거든 팅커한테 물어보쇼. 팅커 부인, 이쪽은 샤프 양이오. 가정교사 아가씨, 이쪽은 잡일 하는 아주머니요. 호, 호!\"\n\n이때 팅커 부인이라 불린 그 여인이 파이프 하나와 담배 한 봉지를 들고 나타났는데, 그것은 샤프 양이 도착하기 일 분 전에 그녀를 시켜 사 오게 한 것이었다. 그녀는 그 물건들을 벽난로 옆에 자리를 잡고 앉은 핏 경에게 건넸다.\n\n\"잔돈은 어디 있나?\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 일 페니 반을 줬는데. 거스름돈은 어디 갔어, 늙은 팅커?\"\n\n\"여기요!\" 팅커 부인이 동전을 탁 내던지며 대꾸했다. \"파딩에 신경 쓰는 건 준남작이나 하는 짓이지.\"\n\n\"하루에 일 파딩이면 일 년이면 칠 실링이야.\" 그 하원의원이 대꾸했다. \"칠 실링이면 칠 기니에 붙는 이자란 말이지. 파딩을 잘 간수해, 늙은 팅커, 그럼 기니는 절로 굴러들어 올 테니까.\"\n\n\"이분이 핏 크롤리 경이 틀림없다는 건, 아가씨, 안심하셔도 돼요.\" 팅커 부인이 퉁명스럽게 말했다. \"파딩까지 챙기시는 분이니 말이에요. 머지않아 이분을 더 잘 알게 되실 거예요.\"\n\n\"그래도 나를 조금도 덜 좋아하진 않을 거요, 샤프 양.\" 그 노신사가 거의 정중하다 할 만한 태도로 말했다. \"후하게 베풀기 전에 우선 공정해야 하는 법이니까.\"\n\n\"이분은 평생 단 한 푼도 거저 내준 적이 없으세요.\" 팅커가 으르렁대듯 말했다.\n\n\"그렇지, 앞으로도 그럴 거고. 그건 내 신조에 어긋나거든. 앉고 싶거든 부엌에 가서 의자 하나 더 가져와, 팅커. 그럼 우리도 저녁 좀 먹어 보세.\"\n\n이윽고 준남작은 불 위의 냄비에 포크를 푹 찔러 넣더니, 단지에서 양 곱창 한 점과 양파 한 개를 건져 냈다. 그는 그것을 거의 똑같은 몫으로 나누어, 팅커 부인과 함께 나눠 먹었다. \"보다시피 샤프 양, 내가 여기 없을 때면 팅커는 식대만 받고 알아서 끼니를 해결하지. 내가 시내에 와 있을 땐 식구들과 함께 먹고. 하! 하! 샤프 양이 시장하지 않다니 다행이군, 안 그래, 팅크?\" 그러고는 그들은 그 소박한 저녁상에 달려들었다.\n\n저녁을 먹고 나서 핏 크롤리 경은 파이프를 피우기 시작했다. 그리고 완전히 어두워지자, 그는 양철 촛대에 꽂힌 골풀 양초에 불을 붙이고는, 끝도 없이 깊은 주머니에서 어마어마한 서류 뭉치를 꺼내 그것을 읽으며 정리하기 시작했다.\n\n\"내가 여기 온 건 소송 일 때문이라오, 아가씨. 그러니 내일 이렇게 예쁜 길동무와 함께 가는 즐거움을 누리게 된 거지.\"\n\n\"이분은 늘 소송 일에 매달려 계세요.\" 팅커 부인이 흑맥주 단지를 집어 들며 말했다.\n\n\"한 모금씩 돌려 마시지.\" 준남작이 말했다. \"그래, 아가씨, 팅커 말이 아주 옳아. 나는 영국의 그 누구보다도 많은 소송에서 지기도 하고 이기기도 했지. 여기 이걸 보라고, 준남작 크롤리 대 스내플. 이 자를 보기 좋게 뒤엎어 줄 테야, 안 그러면 내 이름이 핏 크롤리가 아니지. 포더 외 일인 대 준남작 크롤리. 스네일리 교구 감독관들 대 준남작 크롤리. 그자들은 그게 공유지라는 걸 증명할 수가 없어. 어디 한번 해 보라지. 그 땅은 내 거야. 그게 교구 것이 아닌 건 댁이나 여기 팅커 것이 아닌 거나 마찬가지지. 천 기니가 들더라도 그자들을 박살 내 줄 거요. 이 서류들 한번 훑어보구려. 보고 싶으면 봐도 좋소, 아가씨. 글씨는 좀 잘 쓰오? 퀸스크롤리에 가면 댁을 요긴하게 부려 먹어야겠어, 두고 보쇼, 샤프 양. 이제 노부인이 죽고 없으니 누가 좀 있어야겠단 말이지.\"\n\n\"그 부인도 이분 못지않게 지독했어요.\" 팅커가 말했다. \"거래하는 상인이란 상인은 죄다 상대로 소송을 걸어 댔고, 사 년 동안 하인을 마흔여덟 명이나 내쫓았으니까요.\"\n\n\"그 사람 인색했지, 아주 인색했어.\" 준남작이 담담하게 말했다. \"하지만 내겐 값진 여자였지. 집사 하나 둘 돈을 아껴 줬으니까.\" 그리고 이런 흉허물 없는 어조로, 새로 온 손님을 무던히도 재미나게 해 주며 대화는 꽤 오랫동안 이어졌다. 핏 크롤리 경의 자질이 좋든 나쁘든, 그게 무엇이건 간에, 그는 그것을 조금도 감추려 들지 않았다. 그는 쉴 새 없이 자기 이야기를 늘어놓았는데, 때로는 더없이 거칠고 천박한 햄프셔 사투리로, 때로는 세상 물정에 밝은 사람의 말투를 빌려서였다. 그렇게, 그는 샤프 양에게 아침 다섯 시까지 채비를 마치라고 분부한 뒤 작별 인사를 건넸다. \"오늘 밤은 팅커와 함께 자도록 하시오.\" 그가 말했다. \"큰 침대니까 둘이 자도 자리가 넉넉하지. 크롤리 부인이 그 침대에서 죽었다오. 잘 자시구려.\"\n\n이 축복의 인사를 건넨 뒤 핏 경은 가 버렸고, 근엄한 팅커는 골풀 양초를 손에 들고 앞장서서 그 크고 황량한 돌계단을 올라, 손잡이가 종이로 둘둘 싸인 그 크고 음산한 응접실 문들을 지나, 크롤리 부인이 마지막 잠을 잤던 그 커다란 앞쪽 침실로 들어갔다. 그 침대와 방은 어찌나 장례식장 같고 음울하던지, 크롤리 부인이 그 방에서 죽었을 뿐 아니라 그 유령이 거기 깃들어 있다고 여겨도 무방할 정도였다. 그러나 레베카는 더없이 발랄하게 방 안을 이리저리 돌아다니며, 늙은 잡역부가 기도를 올리는 동안, 그 거대한 옷장들과 벽장들과 찬장들을 들여다보고, 잠긴 서랍들을 당겨 보고, 그 음산한 그림들과 화장대 비품들을 살펴보았다. \"저 같으면 떳떳한 양심 없이는 이 침대에서 자고 싶지 않을 거예요, 아가씨.\" 늙은 여인이 말했다. \"우리 둘에다 유령 여섯쯤 더 들어와도 자리가 넉넉하겠는걸요.\" 레베카가 말했다. \"크롤리 부인이랑 핏 크롤리 경이랑, 다들 어떤 사람들인지 죄다 들려주세요, 친애하는 팅커 부인.\"\n\n그러나 늙은 팅커는 이 어린 캐묻기 선수에게 호락호락 속을 내줄 사람이 아니었다. 그녀는 침대란 잠을 자는 곳이지 이야기를 나누는 곳이 아니라는 뜻을 내비치고는, 침대 자기 쪽 구석에서, 오직 순결한 자의 코만이 낼 수 있는 그런 코골이를 시작했다. 레베카는 오래오래 잠 못 이룬 채 누워, 내일 일을, 자기가 들어가게 될 그 새로운 세계를, 그리고 거기서 성공할 가능성을 곰곰이 헤아렸다. 골풀 양초는 받침 접시 안에서 가물거렸다. 벽난로 선반은 커다란 검은 그림자를 드리워, 곰팡이 핀 헌 자수본 절반을 뒤덮었는데, 그것은 분명 고인이 된 그 부인이 손수 놓은 것이리라. 그리고 그 그림자는 어린 두 소년을 그린 자그마한 가족 초상화 두 점도 덮었으니, 하나는 대학 가운을 입은 모습이었고, 다른 하나는 군인처럼 붉은 상의를 걸친 모습이었다. 잠이 들 때, 레베카는 꿈에 그릴 대상으로 그 후자를 골랐다.\n\n그레이트곤트가마저 화사해 보이게 만들 만큼 그토록 장밋빛으로 물든 어느 여름날 아침 네 시, 충직한 팅커는 잠동무를 깨워 떠날 채비를 하라 이른 다음, 큰 현관문의 빗장과 자물쇠를 끌렀고(그 철컹거리고 쾅 닫히는 소리가 거리에서 잠들어 있던 메아리들을 놀래켰다), 옥스퍼드가로 접어들어 거기 마차 대기소에서 삯마차 하나를 불러 세웠다. 그 마차의 번호가 무엇이었는지 시시콜콜 밝힐 것도 없고, 그 마부가 이렇게 이른 시각에 스왈로가 근처에 진을 치고 있었던 것은 어느 술집에서 비틀비틀 집으로 돌아가던 젊은 멋쟁이가 제 마차의 신세를 필요로 하여, 취기의 호기로 후한 삯을 쳐주지 않을까 하는 바람에서였다고 굳이 일러둘 것도 없다.\n\n마찬가지로 굳이 말할 것도 없이, 그 마부가 위에서 말한 그런 바람을 품었더라면 그는 톡톡히 실망했을 터였다. 그가 시내까지 태워다 준 그 훌륭한 준남작은 그에게 정해진 삯에서 단 한 푼도 더 얹어 주지 않았으니 말이다. 그 마부가 사정하고 으름장을 놓아 봐야 다 헛수고였다. 그는 '넥스' 앞에서 샤프 양의 모자 상자들을 도랑에 내동댕이치며, 손님을 상대로 소송을 걸겠다고 욕설을 퍼부었다.\n\n\"그러지 않는 게 좋을 거요.\" 마구간 일꾼 하나가 말했다. \"저분이 핏 크롤리 경이라고.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고, 조.\" 준남작이 흡족한 듯 외쳤다. \"나를 어떻게 해 볼 수 있는 자가 있거든 어디 한번 봤으면 좋겠군.\"\n\n\"저도 봤으면 좋겠네요.\" 조가 시무룩하게 히죽거리며 말하고는, 준남작의 짐을 마차 지붕 위에 올려 실었다.\n\n\"그 마부석은 내 자리로 비워 두게, 리더.\" 그 하원의원이 마부에게 외치자, 마부는 모자에 손을 살짝 갖다 대며 \"예, 핏 경\" 하고 대꾸했지만, 속으로는 분이 끓어올랐다(그는 그 자리를 케임브리지에서 온 어느 젊은 신사에게 약속해 둔 터였는데, 그 신사라면 영락없이 일 크라운은 쥐여 주었을 것이었다). 그리고 샤프 양은 마차 안쪽 뒷좌석에 자리를 잡게 되었으니, 그 마차가 그녀를 드넓은 세상 속으로 실어 나르는 셈이라 해도 좋을 것이었다.\n\n케임브리지에서 온 그 젊은이가 시무룩하게 제 외투 다섯 벌을 앞쪽에 쌓아 두었다가, 어린 샤프 양이 마차에서 내려 자기 곁에 올라앉게 되자 마음이 풀렸다는 것, 그리고 자기 두툼한 외투 한 벌로 그녀를 폭 덮어 주고는 더없이 기분이 좋아졌다는 것, 천식을 앓는 신사 하나와, 명예를 걸고 맹세컨대 대중 마차란 걸 평생 한 번도 타 본 적이 없다고 우기는 새침한 부인 하나(마차에는 늘 그런 부인이 한 명씩 있는 법이다. 아, 있었던 법이다. 그 마차들은, 지금 다 어디로 갔단 말인가?), 그리고 브랜디 병을 든 뚱뚱한 과부 하나가 안쪽에 자리를 잡았다는 것, 짐꾼이 그들 모두에게 돈을 청해 그 신사에게서는 육 펜스를, 뚱뚱한 과부에게서는 기름때 묻은 반 페니짜리 다섯 닢을 받아 냈다는 것, 그리고 마차가 마침내 출발하여, 어느새 올더스게이트의 어두운 골목들을 누비는가 하면, 이내 세인트폴 대성당의 푸른 둥근 지붕 곁을 덜컹거리며 지나고, 플리트 시장의 객 출입구를 딸랑딸랑 내달려 지났다는 것 그 출입구는 이제 엑서터 체인지와 함께 그림자의 세계로 떠나가 버렸다 그리고 그들이 피커딜리의 화이트베어 앞을 지나, 나이츠브리지의 채소밭에서 이슬이 피어오르는 광경을 보았다는 것, 그리고 턴햄그린과 브렌트우드와 백쇼트를 지났다는 것은 여기서 굳이 이야기할 필요가 없다. 그러나 이 글을 쓰는 사람은, 지난날, 똑같이 화창한 날씨에, 바로 그 잊지 못할 여정을 따라가 본 적이 있는지라, 달콤하고도 애틋한 회한 없이는 그 길을 떠올릴 수가 없다. 지금 그 길은 어디에 있으며, 그 길에 깃들었던 인생의 흥겨운 일화들은 다 어디로 갔는가? 정직하고 코에 뾰루지가 돋은 그 늙은 마부들에게는 첼시도 그리니치도 없단 말인가? 그 좋은 친구들, 다들 어디로 갔는지 궁금하구나. 늙은 웰러는 살았는가 죽었는가? 그리고 그 종업원들, 그래, 그들이 시중들던 그 여관들, 그 안에 차려져 있던 차가운 쇠고기 덩어리, 그리고 파란 코에 양동이를 절그럭거리던 그 땅딸막한 마구간 일꾼은, 그는 어디에 있으며 그의 후예들은 또 어디에 있는가? 지금은 치마를 입은 어린 나이인 그 위대한 천재들, 장차 사랑하는 독자의 자녀들을 위해 소설을 쓰게 될 그들에게, 이 사람들과 사물들은 니네베나 사자심왕이나 잭 셰퍼드 못지않은 전설이요 역사가 될 것이다. 그들에게 역마차란 낭만의 이야기가 되어 있을 것이요, 네 마리 밤색 말 한 조는 부케팔로스나 블랙 베스만큼이나 신화 속 존재가 되어 있을 것이다. 아, 마구간지기들이 덮개 옷을 벗겨 내면 그 말들의 털가죽은 어찌나 윤이 났던지, 그러고는 쏜살같이 내달려 가던 모습이라니. 아, 여정 끝에 옆구리에서 김을 내뿜으며 얌전히 여관 마당으로 걸어 들어갈 때, 그 꼬리들은 또 어찌나 흔들거렸던지. 아, 슬프도다! 우리는 이제 다시는 한밤중에 울리는 그 나팔 소리를 듣지 못할 것이요, 통행세 관문이 활짝 열리는 광경도 두 번 다시 보지 못할 것이다. 그건 그렇고, 안에 네 사람을 태운 그 가벼운 트라팔가르 마차는 우리를 대체 어디로 실어 가고 있는가? 더는 옆길로 새지 말고 퀸스크롤리에 내려서, 레베카 샤프 양이 거기서 어떻게 일이 풀려 나가는지 지켜보기로 하자.\n\n런던 러셀 광장의 애밀리아 세들리 양에게, 리베카 샤프 양으로부터.\n(무료 배송. — 핏 크롤리.)\n\n# MY DEAREST, SWEETEST AMELIA,\n\n더없이 사랑하고 더없이 다정한 애밀리아에게,\n이토록 기쁨과 슬픔이 뒤섞인 마음으로 더없이 소중한 벗에게 편지를 쓰려 펜을 드는구나! 아, 어제와 오늘이 이렇게나 다를 수가! 지금 나는 벗도 없이 외톨이가 되었는데, 어제까지만 해도 나는 집에서, 언제까지나 영원히 아끼고 또 아낄 한 자매의 다정한 벗 노릇을 하고 있었지!\n\n너와 헤어진 그 운명의 밤을 내가 얼마나 눈물과 슬픔 속에 보냈는지는 말하지 않으련다. 너는 화요일에 어머니와, 또 네 곁에 있는 너의 헌신적인 젊은 군인과 함께 즐거움과 행복을 향해 떠났지. 나는 밤새도록 퍼킨스 댁에서 춤추는 네 모습을, 분명 무도회의 그 어느 젊은 숙녀보다도 아름다웠을 네 모습을 떠올렸단다. 나는 마부의 손에 이끌려 낡은 마차를 타고 핏 크롤리 경의 시내 저택으로 보내졌어. 그곳에서 마부 존이 나를 더없이 무례하고 거만하게 대한 뒤에(아아! 가난과 불운을 모욕하는 일이란 참으로 안전한 일이지!), 나는 핏 경의 보살핌에 맡겨졌고, 음침한 낡은 침대에서, 이 집을 지키는 끔찍하고 음침한 늙은 잡일꾼 노파 곁에서 밤을 지새워야 했단다. 나는 밤새 단 한순간도 눈을 붙이지 못했어.\n\n핏 경은 우리 어리석은 계집애들이 치즈윅에서 「세실리아」를 읽으며 준남작이라면 응당 이러이러하리라 상상하던 그런 사람이 아니야. 정말이지, 오빌 경과 이보다 더 닮지 않은 사람은 상상조차 할 수 없을 거야. 늙고, 땅딸막하고, 키 작고, 천박하고, 몹시 지저분한 사내가 낡은 옷과 해진 낡은 각반 차림으로 끔찍한 파이프 담배를 피우며, 소스 냄비에 자기가 먹을 끔찍한 저녁밥을 손수 끓여 먹는 모습을 한번 떠올려 봐. 그는 촌스러운 사투리로 말하고, 늙은 노파에게며, 우리를 역마차가 출발하는 여관까지 데려다준 삯마차 마부에게며 욕설을 끝없이 퍼부어 댔어. 게다가 그 역마차 여정의 대부분을 나는 바깥자리에서 가야 했단다.\n\n나는 동틀 녘 그 노파에게 잠을 깼고, 여관에 도착해서는 처음엔 마차 안자리에 앉혀졌어. 그런데 리킹턴이라는 곳에 이르러 비가 몹시 쏟아지기 시작하자 — 너 이걸 믿을 수 있겠니? — 나는 어쩔 수 없이 바깥자리로 나가야 했단다. 핏 경이 그 마차의 소유주인 데다, 머드베리에서 안자리를 원하는 승객이 탔기 때문에, 나는 비를 맞으며 바깥으로 나가야만 했어. 그래도 케임브리지 칼리지에서 온 한 젊은 신사가 자기가 가진 여러 외투 가운데 하나로 나를 친절히 감싸 주었단다.\n\n이 신사와 마차 차장은 핏 경을 아주 잘 아는 듯, 그를 두고 한참을 비웃더구나. 둘 다 입을 모아 그를 늙은 구두쇠라고 불렀어. 그건 몹시 인색하고 탐욕스러운 사람이란 뜻이야. 그는 누구에게도 한 푼 주는 법이 없다더구나(이런 비열함이 나는 정말 싫어). 그리고 그 젊은 신사는 마지막 두 구간을 우리가 아주 느릿느릿 달린 까닭을 내게 일깨워 주었는데, 그건 핏 경이 마부석에 앉아 있어서이고, 또 그 구간의 말들이 그의 소유였기 때문이래. \"내가 고삐를 잡으면 스쿼시모어까지 저놈들을 채찍질로 몰아붙이지 않겠어?\" 그 젊은 케임브리지 학생이 말했어. \"그래야 싸지요, 잭 도련님.\" 차장이 말했지. 이 말의 뜻을, 그러니까 잭 도련님이 남은 길을 직접 몰아 핏 경의 말들에게 분풀이할 작정이라는 걸 알아듣고는, 나도 물론 웃고 말았단다.\n\n하지만 머드베리에서, 퀸스 크롤리에서 사 마일 떨어진 그곳에서, 문장을 두른 훌륭한 말 네 필이 끄는 마차가 우리를 기다리고 있었고, 우리는 위풍당당하게 준남작의 영지로 들어섰어. 저택으로 이어지는 일 마일 길이의 멋진 가로수길이 있었는데, 문지기 집 문(그 기둥들 위에는 크롤리 가문 문장을 받치는 뱀과 비둘기가 얹혀 있었어) 앞의 여인이 그 낡은 무쇠 세공 문을 활짝 열어젖히며 우리에게 연거푸 절을 했단다. 그 문은 그 지긋지긋한 치즈윅의 문과 어딘가 비슷하더구나.\n\n\"여기 가로수길은,\" 핏 경이 말했어. \"일 마일이나 되지. 저 나무들에 육천 파운드어치 목재가 들어 있어. 그래도 그게 아무것도 아니라고 하겠나?\" 그는 가로수길을 '가루수길'이라고, 아무것도를 '아무거또'라고 발음했는데, 어찌나 우습던지. 그리고 그는 머드베리에서 온 농장 일꾼 호드슨 씨를 마차에 함께 태웠는데, 둘은 압류며, 재산 처분이며, 배수며, 심토 갈이며, 소작인이며 농사 이야기를 끝없이 나누었어 — 내가 알아듣기엔 너무 많은 이야기였지. 샘 마일스가 밀렵을 하다 잡혔다고 하고, 피터 베일리는 결국 구빈원에 들어갔다더라. \"그래 싸지.\" 핏 경이 말했어. \"그자와 그자 집안이 백오십 년 동안 그 농장에서 날 속여 먹었으니까.\" 짐작건대 소작료를 못 내는 어느 늙은 소작인인 듯해. 핏 경은 물론 문법에 맞게 말할 수도 있었겠지만, 가난한 가정교사와 달리 부유한 준남작은 문법 따위를 신경 쓸 필요가 없는 법이지.\n\n지나가는 길에 나는 영지 안의 오래된 느릅나무들 위로 솟아오른 아름다운 교회 첨탑을 보았어. 그리고 그 앞쪽, 잔디밭 한가운데, 몇몇 별채들 곁에는, 담쟁이덩굴로 뒤덮인 높은 굴뚝이 달린 낡은 붉은 벽돌집이 있고, 창문들이 햇빛에 반짝이고 있더구나. \"저게 어르신 교회인가요?\" 내가 물었어.\n\n\"그래, 빌어먹을.\" (핏 경이 말했지. 다만, 얘야, 훨씬 더 고약한 말을 썼단다.) \"버티는 어떤가, 호드슨? 버티는 내 동생 뷰트야, 얘야 — 목사인 내 동생 말이지. 나는 그놈을 버티와 야수라고 부른단다, 하하!\"\n\n호드슨도 따라 웃더니, 이내 좀 더 진지한 표정으로 고개를 끄덕이며 말했어. \"유감스럽지만 좀 나아지신 모양입니다, 핏 경. 어제 망아지를 타고 나오셔서 저희 밀밭을 둘러보셨어요.\"\n\n\"십일조를 챙기러 나왔겠지, 빌어먹을 놈.\" (다만 그 똑같은 고약한 말을 썼어.) \"브랜디에 물 탄 것도 그놈을 못 죽이나? 그 늙은 뭐냐그거 — 늙은 므두셀라처럼 질긴 놈이라니까.\"\n\n호드슨 씨는 또 웃었어. \"젊은 도련님들이 대학에서 돌아오셨습니다. 존 스크로긴스를 거의 죽도록 두들겨 팼지 뭡니까.\"\n\n\"내 둘째 사냥터지기를 두들겨 패!\" 핏 경이 고함을 질렀어.\n\n\"목사님 땅에 들어가 있었답니다, 어르신.\" 호드슨 씨가 대답했지. 그러자 핏 경은 노발대발하며, 만약 그놈들이 자기 땅에서 밀렵하는 걸 한 번이라도 잡으면 유배 보내 버리겠다고, 맹세코 그러겠다고 욕설을 퍼부었어. 그러면서도 그는 이렇게 말했어. \"성직록 추천권은 내가 팔아 버렸네, 호드슨. 그 핏줄에겐 절대 안 넘어가게 해 놨지, 장담하네.\" 그러자 호드슨 씨는 지당하신 말씀이라고 했어. 이것만 봐도 나는 그 두 형제가 사이가 틀어졌다는 걸 의심치 않아 — 형제끼리, 또 자매끼리 흔히 그렇듯이 말이야. 치즈윅의 스크래츨리 자매 둘 기억나니? 어떻게 늘 다투고 으르렁대던지 — 그리고 메리 박스가 어떻게 늘 루이자를 두들겨 패던지 말이야.\n\n이윽고 숲에서 막대기를 줍고 있는 두 어린 사내아이를 보더니, 호드슨 씨가 핏 경의 명령에 따라 마차에서 뛰어내려 채찍을 들고 그 애들에게 달려들었어. \"혼쭐을 내, 호드슨.\" 준남작이 고함쳤어. \"고 조그만 것들 영혼이 빠지도록 두들겨 패서 집으로 끌고 와, 저 부랑자 놈들을. 내 이름이 핏인 게 분명하듯이 저놈들을 영락없이 잡아넣고 말 테니.\" 그러더니 이내 호드슨 씨의 채찍이 가엾게 훌쩍이는 그 조그만 것들의 어깨를 후려치는 소리가 들렸고, 핏 경은 그 범죄자들이 붙잡힌 걸 확인하고는 저택을 향해 마차를 몰았단다.\n\n하인들이 모두 우리를 맞으러 나와 있었고, 그리고…\n\n얘야, 여기서 어젯밤 나는 문을 무섭게 두드리는 소리에 글쓰기를 멈췄단다. 그게 누구였을 것 같니? 잠자리 모자를 쓰고 실내복을 걸친 핏 크롤리 경이었어, 그 꼴이라니! 내가 그런 방문객을 보고 움찔 물러서자, 그는 앞으로 다가와 내 촛불을 낚아챘어. \"열한 시 넘어서는 촛불 안 돼, 베키 양.\" 그가 말했어. \"어둠 속에서 자라고, 요 예쁘장한 말괄량이야\"(그가 나를 그렇게 불렀어). \"그리고 내가 매일 밤 촛불 가지러 오게 만들고 싶지 않거든, 명심하고 열한 시엔 잠자리에 들어.\" 그러고는 집사 호록스 씨와 함께 웃으며 가 버렸어. 내가 다시는 그들의 방문을 부추기지 않으리란 건 너도 분명히 알 거야. 그들은 밤이면 거대한 사냥개 두 마리를 풀어놓는데, 어젯밤 내내 그놈들이 달을 향해 짖고 울부짖었단다. \"이 개는 고러라고 부르지.\" 핏 경이 말했어. \"사람을 한 명 죽인 놈이야, 이 개 말이야. 황소도 거뜬히 잡는 놈이지. 어미는 플로라라고 부르곤 했는데, 지금은 어로어러라고 부른단다. 이젠 너무 늙어서 물지도 못하거든. 하, 하!\"\n\n퀸스 크롤리 저택은 엘리자베스 여왕 시대 양식의 높은 굴뚝과 박공이 달린, 지긋지긋한 구식 붉은 벽돌 저택인데, 그 앞에는 가문의 비둘기와 뱀이 양옆에 늘어선 테라스가 있고, 거기로 커다란 현관 대문이 열린단다. 그리고 아, 얘야, 그 커다란 홀은 분명 그 우돌포 성의 커다란 홀만큼이나 크고 음침할 거야. 거기엔 핑커튼 여사 학원 절반은 들어갈 만한 커다란 벽난로가 있고, 쇠살대는 적어도 황소 한 마리는 구울 만큼 크단다. 방 둘레에는 몇 대인지 모를 크롤리 가문 사람들의 초상화가 걸려 있는데, 더러는 턱수염에 주름 깃을 두른 모습이고, 더러는 거대한 가발을 쓰고 발끝을 바깥으로 벌린 모습이고, 더러는 탑처럼 빳빳해 보이는 길고 곧은 코르셋과 가운 차림이고, 더러는 긴 곱슬머리를 늘어뜨린 채, 아, 얘야! 코르셋이라곤 거의 걸치지 않은 모습이란다. 홀 한쪽 끝에는 더할 나위 없이 음울한 까만 떡갈나무 대계단이 있고, 양옆에는 위에 사슴 머리가 걸린 높다란 문들이 있어서 당구실과 서재로, 또 커다란 노란색 응접실과 모닝룸으로 이어진단다. 이 층에는 침실이 적어도 스무 개는 있는 것 같아. 그 가운데 하나에는 엘리자베스 여왕이 주무셨다는 침대가 있어. 오늘 아침 나는 새 제자들에게 이끌려 이 멋진 방들을 죄다 구경했단다. 정말이지, 덧문을 늘 닫아 두는 통에 그 방들이 조금이라도 덜 음침해 보이는 건 아니야. 어느 방이든 빛이 들어올 때면 나는 그 안에서 유령이 보이지나 않을까 했을 정도란다. 우리 공부방은 삼 층에 있는데, 한쪽으로는 내 침실이, 다른 쪽으로는 아가씨들의 침실이 이어져 있어. 그다음엔 핏 씨의 방들이 있어 — 그는 크롤리 씨라고 불리는데 — 맏아들이지. 그리고 로던 크롤리 씨의 방들도 있는데 — 그는 누구처럼 장교라서 연대를 따라 멀리 떠나 있단다. 정말이지 방이 부족할 일은 없어. 내 생각엔 러셀 광장 사람들을 죄다 이 집에 들여도 자리가 남을 거야.\n\n우리가 도착한 지 삼십 분 만에 커다란 만찬 종이 울렸고, 나는 두 제자(열 살, 여덟 살짜리, 비쩍 마르고 보잘것없는 꼬마들이야)를 데리고 내려갔어. 나는 네가 준 그 소중한 모슬린 가운을 입고 내려갔단다(네가 그걸 내게 주었다고 그 지긋지긋한 피너 부인이 어찌나 무례하게 굴었던지). 손님 오는 날만 빼면 나는 식구 가운데 한 사람으로 대접받기로 되어 있거든. 손님 오는 날엔 아가씨들과 나는 위층에서 식사를 해야 한단다.\n\n자, 그 커다란 만찬 종이 울리자, 우리는 모두 크롤리 마님이 계시는 작은 응접실에 모였어. 그분은 두 번째 크롤리 마님이고, 아가씨들의 어머니야. 철물상의 딸이었는데, 그 결혼은 굉장한 출세로 여겨졌다더라. 한때는 미인이었던 듯한 모습인데, 그 눈은 잃어버린 미모를 슬퍼하며 늘 눈물에 젖어 있어. 창백하고, 여위고, 어깨가 솟았고, 자기 의견이라곤 한마디도 못 하는 게 분명해. 의붓아들인 크롤리 씨도 방에 함께 있었어. 정장 차림인데, 장의사처럼 거드름을 피우더구나. 창백하고, 마르고, 못생기고, 말이 없어. 다리는 가늘고, 가슴팍은 빈약하고, 건초빛 구레나룻에 지푸라기빛 머리칼을 하고 있어. 벽난로 위에 걸린 성녀 같은 자기 어머니의 모습 그대로야 — 빙키 명문가의 그리젤다 말이야.\n\n\"이 사람이 새 가정교사예요, 크롤리 씨.\" 크롤리 마님이 앞으로 나와 내 손을 잡으며 말했어. \"샤프 양이에요.\"\n\n\"오!\" 크롤리 씨는 그렇게 말하고는 고개를 한 번 앞으로 까딱하더니, 다시 자기가 열중하던 두꺼운 소책자를 읽기 시작했어.\n\n\"제 딸들에게 친절히 대해 주시기를 바라요.\" 크롤리 마님이, 늘 눈물에 그렁그렁한 발그레한 눈으로 말했어.\n\n\"어머, 엄마, 당연히 그러시겠죠.\" 맏이가 말했어. 나는 한눈에 저 여자는 두려워할 필요가 없다는 걸 알아챘단다. \"마님, 식사 준비되었습니다.\" 검은 옷을 입고, 마치 홀에 그려진 엘리자베스 여왕의 주름 깃 가운데 하나 같은 거대한 흰 셔츠 깃을 두른 집사가 말했어. 그러자 마님은 크롤리 씨의 팔을 잡고 식당으로 앞장섰고, 나는 양손에 두 어린 제자를 하나씩 잡고 그 뒤를 따랐단다.\n\n핏 경은 은 주전자를 들고 이미 방에 와 있었어. 막 지하 저장고에 다녀온 참이었고, 그도 정장 차림이었어. 그러니까, 각반을 벗고서 까만 양모 양말 신은 짤막하고 땅딸한 다리를 드러내고 있었단다. 식기대에는 번쩍이는 낡은 은식기가 가득했어 — 금이며 은이며 낡은 잔들, 낡은 쟁반과 양념병 받침대까지, 마치 런들 앤드 브리지 가게 같았어. 식탁 위 모든 것도 은제였고, 빨간 머리에 카나리아빛 제복을 입은 하인 둘이 식기대 양옆에 서 있었어.\n\n크롤리 씨가 긴 식전 기도를 올렸고, 핏 경이 아멘 하자, 커다란 은제 접시 덮개들이 치워졌어.\n\n\"오늘 저녁은 뭔가, 베치?\" 준남작이 말했어.\n\n\"양고기 수프인 것 같아요, 핏 경.\" 크롤리 마님이 대답했어.\n\n\"무통 오 나베입니다.\" 집사가 근엄하게 덧붙였어(부디 '무통고나비'라고 발음해 주렴). \"그리고 수프는 포타주 드 무통 아 레코세즈입니다. 곁들이 요리로는 폼 드 테르 오 나튀렐과 슈플뢰르 아 로가 있습니다.\"\n\n\"양고기는 양고기지.\" 준남작이 말했어. \"지독하게 맛 좋은 것이고. 무슨 양이었나, 호록스, 언제 잡았지?\" \"검은 얼굴 스코틀랜드 양 가운데 하나입니다, 핏 경. 목요일에 잡았습니다.\"\n\n\"누가 좀 가져갔나?\"\n\n\"머드베리의 스틸이 등심과 다리 두 짝을 가져갔습니다, 핏 경. 다만 마지막 것은 너무 어리고 지독하게 양털이 많았다고 합니다, 핏 경.\"\n\n\"수프 좀 드시겠습니까, 어 — 블런트 양?\" 크롤리 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"훌륭한 스코틀랜드 수프야, 아가씨.\" 핏 경이 말했어. \"저들이 프랑스 이름으로 부르긴 하지만 말이야.\"\n\n\"제대로 된 사교계에서는,\" 크롤리 씨가 도도하게 말했어. \"이 요리를 제가 부른 대로 부르는 게 관례인 줄로 압니다.\" 그러고 나서 그것은 카나리아빛 옷을 입은 하인들이 은제 수프 접시에 담아 무통 오 나베와 함께 우리에게 내왔어. 그다음엔 '에일에 물 탄 것'이 나와서, 우리 어린 아가씨들에겐 포도주 잔에 따라 주었어. 나는 에일을 가릴 줄 모르지만, 양심에 거리낌 없이 말하건대 나는 물이 더 좋아.\n\n우리가 식사를 즐기는 동안, 핏 경이 양고기 어깨살은 어떻게 되었느냐고 물을 기회를 잡았어.\n\n\"하인들 방에서 먹은 것 같아요.\" 마님이 겸손하게 말했어.\n\n\"먹었지요, 마님.\" 호록스가 말했어. \"거기서야 그것 말고는 변변히 얻어먹는 게 거의 없으니까요.\"\n\n핏 경은 말처럼 껄껄대며 웃음을 터뜨리더니, 호록스 씨와 다시 이야기를 이어 갔어. \"그 켄트 암퇘지 새끼인 까만 새끼돼지가 이제 어지간히 살이 올랐겠는데.\"\n\n\"아직 터질 정도는 아닙니다, 핏 경.\" 집사가 더없이 근엄한 표정으로 말했어. 그러자 핏 경이, 이번엔 아가씨들까지 함께, 격렬하게 웃어 대기 시작했어.\n\n\"크롤리 양, 로즈 크롤리 양.\" 크롤리 씨가 말했어. \"너희 웃음소리가 내겐 몹시 부적절하게 들리는구나.\"\n\n\"신경 쓰지 마라, 도련님.\" 준남작이 말했어. \"토요일에 그 돼지를 한번 잡아 보자고. 토요일 아침에 잡아라, 존 호록스. 샤프 양은 돼지고기를 무척 좋아하지요, 안 그렇소, 샤프 양?\"\n\n만찬에서 내가 기억하는 대화는 이게 다인 것 같아. 식사가 끝나자 핏 경 앞에 뜨거운 물 주전자가 놓였고, 럼주가 든 듯한 각진 병이 함께 놓였어. 호록스 씨가 나와 제자들에게 포도주 작은 잔 세 개를 따라 주었고, 마님에게는 가득 찬 잔이 따라졌어. 우리가 물러 나오자, 마님은 일감 서랍에서 어마어마하게 끝도 없는 뜨개질감을 꺼냈고, 아가씨들은 꾀죄죄한 카드 한 벌로 크리비지 놀이를 시작했어. 촛불이라곤 하나만 켜져 있었는데, 그래도 그건 으리으리한 낡은 은촛대에 꽂혀 있었단다. 마님이 몇 마디 물어본 뒤로, 나는 설교집 한 권과, 크롤리 씨가 저녁 식사 전에 읽던 곡물법 소책자 가운데에서 마음껏 골라 소일거리로 삼을 수 있었어.\n\n그렇게 우리는 발소리가 들릴 때까지 한 시간을 앉아 있었어.\n\n\"카드 치워라, 얘들아.\" 마님이 몹시 떨면서 외쳤어. \"크롤리 씨 책 내려놓으세요, 샤프 양.\" 그리고 이 명령을 미처 다 따르기도 전에 크롤리 씨가 방으로 들어왔어.\n\n\"어제 하던 설교를 마저 보자, 아가씨들.\" 그가 말했어. \"한 사람씩 번갈아 한 쪽씩 읽거라. 그래야 어 — 쇼트 양도 들을 기회가 있을 테니.\" 그러자 가엾은 아가씨들은 리버풀 베데스다 예배당에서 치카소 인디언 선교를 위해 행해진, 길고 음울한 설교문을 더듬더듬 읽기 시작했어. 정말 멋진 저녁이지 않니?\n\n열 시가 되자 하인들에게 핏 경과 온 식구를 기도에 불러 모으라는 명이 내려졌어. 핏 경이 맨 먼저 들어왔는데, 얼굴이 잔뜩 붉어진 채 걸음걸이가 다소 비틀거리더구나. 그 뒤로 집사와, 카나리아빛 옷을 입은 하인들과, 크롤리 씨의 시종과, 마구간 냄새가 물씬 풍기는 다른 사내 셋과, 여자 넷이 들어왔는데, 그 가운데 한 여자는 보아하니 몹시 지나치게 차려입었고, 무릎을 털썩 꿇으면서 내게 몹시 경멸 어린 눈길을 던지더라.\n\n크롤리 씨가 일장 연설과 설교를 마치고 나자, 우리는 촛불을 받아 들고 잠자리에 들었어. 그러고 나서 내가 더없이 사랑하고 다정한 애밀리아 너에게 이야기한 대로 글쓰기를 방해받았던 거란다.\n\n잘 자렴. 천 번, 천 번, 천 번의 입맞춤을 보낸다!\n\n토요일. — 오늘 아침 다섯 시에 나는 그 까만 새끼돼지의 비명을 들었어. 로즈와 바이올렛이 어제 내게 그 돼지를 소개해 주었거든. 그리고 마구간이며, 개집이며, 시장에 보낼 과일을 따고 있던 정원사도 소개해 주었어. 아가씨들이 온실 포도 한 송이를 달라고 그에게 한참 졸라 댔지만, 그는 핏 경이 그 포도알 하나하나까지 다 세어 두었기 때문에, 하나라도 내주었다간 자기 일자리가 날아갈 거라고 했어. 사랑스러운 아가씨들은 울안에서 망아지 한 마리를 붙잡고는 내게 말을 타겠느냐고 물었고, 자기들이 타기 시작했는데, 마부가 끔찍한 욕설을 퍼부으며 다가와 그 애들을 쫓아 버렸어.\n\n크롤리 마님은 늘 양모 뜨개질을 하고 있어. 핏 경은 늘 밤마다 거나하게 취해서, 내 생각엔 집사 호록스와 함께 앉아 있는 것 같아. 크롤리 씨는 저녁이면 늘 설교집을 읽고, 아침이면 서재에 틀어박혀 있거나, 아니면 군의 공무로 머드베리로 말을 달리거나, 수요일과 금요일마다 스쿼시모어로 가서 그곳 소작인들에게 설교를 한단다.\n\n네 사랑하는 아버님과 어머님께 십만 번의 다정한 안부를 전해 줘. 네 가엾은 오빠는 그 럼펀치 후유증에서 회복되었니? 아아, 저런! 아아, 저런! 사내들이란 그 고약한 펀치를 어찌나 조심해야 하는지!\n\n언제까지나 영원히 너의 리베카로부터.\n\n모든 걸 고려해 볼 때, 러셀 광장의 우리 사랑스러운 애밀리아 세들리에게는 샤프 양과 헤어진 것이 차라리 잘된 일이라고 나는 생각한다. 리베카가 익살맞고 우스운 인물인 것은 분명한 사실이다. 또한 잃어버린 미모를 슬퍼하며 우는 가엾은 마님이라든가, '건초빛 구레나룻에 지푸라기빛 머리칼'을 한 신사를 묘사한 대목은 의심할 바 없이 무척 재기가 넘치며, 세상물정에 밝다는 것을 보여 준다. 다만 그녀가 무릎을 꿇고 있을 때 호록스 양의 리본보다 더 나은 무언가를 생각하고 있었을 법도 하다는 점은, 아마 우리 둘 다 떠올렸을 것이다. 그러나 나의 다정한 독자께서는 이 이야기의 제목이 '허영의 시장'이라는 것을, 그리고 허영의 시장이란 온갖 허풍과 거짓과 허세로 가득한, 몹시 허황되고 사악하고 어리석은 곳이라는 것을 부디 기억해 주시기 바란다. 그리고 표지에서 일장 설교를 늘어놓는 그 도덕가는(이 보잘것없는 종복의 정확한 초상이다) 목사 가운도 띠도 두르지 않고, 다만 자기 신도들이 걸치고 있는 그 똑같은 당나귀 귀 달린 어릿광대 옷을 입었노라 공언하는 바이지만, 자, 보시라, 사람이란 어릿광대 모자를 쓰든 삽 모양 모자를 쓰든, 자기가 아는 한 진실을 말해야 하는 법이며, 이런 일을 해 나가는 과정에서는 적잖이 불쾌한 사실들이 드러날 수밖에 없는 것이다.\n\n나는 나폴리에서, 이야기꾼 동업자 한 사람이 바닷가에서 쓸모없이 게으르기만 한 정직한 무리에게 설교하는 것을 들은 적이 있다. 그는 자기가 묘사하고 지어내는 악당들의 못된 행실에 어찌나 격분하고 열을 올리던지, 청중들이 도저히 견디지 못할 지경이었다. 그리하여 그들과 그 시인이 한데 어우러져, 이야기 속의 가공의 괴물을 향해 욕설과 저주를 한바탕 퍼부어 댔고, 그 통에 모자가 한 바퀴 돌면서, 완벽한 공감의 폭풍 한가운데서 동전들이 그 안으로 쏟아져 들어갔다.\n\n한편 파리의 작은 극장들에서는, 관객들이 박스석에서 \"아, 악당! 아, 괴물 같으니!\" 하고 고래고래 소리치며 극 중 폭군을 저주할 뿐 아니라, 배우들 자신이 그 악역, 이를테면 비열한 영국인이라든가 야만적인 코사크 따위의 배역을 맡기를 한사코 거부하고, 더 적은 봉급을 받더라도 충직한 프랑스인이라는 자기 본래의 모습으로 무대에 서기를 더 좋아하는 것을 보게 될 것이다. 내가 이 두 이야기를 나란히 견주어 놓는 것은, 지금 이 공연자가 자기 악당들을 까발리고 혼내 주고자 하는 것이 단지 돈 때문이 아님을, 다만 그가 그들을 진심으로 미워하여 도저히 억누를 수 없는 마음이 마땅한 욕설과 험한 말로 분출구를 찾을 수밖에 없기 때문임을 그대들이 알아주기를 바라서다.\n\n그러므로 나의 '다정한 벗들'에게 미리 일러두건대, 나는 앞으로 가슴을 도려내는 악행과, 복잡하게 얽힌 — 그러나 내가 믿건대 더없이 흥미진진할 — 범죄의 이야기를 들려줄 작정이다. 장담컨대 내 악당들은 맹물에 물 탄 듯 밍밍한 악당들이 아니다. 알맞은 대목에 이르면 우리는 험한 말을 아끼지 않을 것이다 — 결코, 결코! 그러나 조용한 시골을 지나는 동안에는 어쩔 수 없이 차분해야만 한다. 죽사발 속에서 일으키는 폭풍이란 우스꽝스러운 법이다. 그런 일은 거대한 대양과 외로운 한밤을 위해 아껴 두기로 하자. 지금 이 장은 무척 점잖다. 다른 장들은 — 하지만 그것은 미리 내다보지 말기로 하자.\n\n그리고 우리의 등장인물들을 차례로 내세우는 동안, 나는 한 사람으로서, 또 한 형제로서, 그들을 소개할 뿐 아니라 이따금 단상에서 내려와 그들에 관해 이야기할 수 있도록 양해를 구하고자 한다. 그들이 선량하고 다정하면 그들을 사랑하고 손을 맞잡을 것이며, 그들이 어리석으면 독자의 소매 뒤에서 은근히 그들을 비웃을 것이고, 그들이 사악하고 무정하면 예의가 허락하는 가장 강한 말로 그들을 매도할 것이다.\n\n그러지 않으면, 샤프 양이 그토록 우스꽝스럽게 여기는 신앙의 관행을 비웃는 것이 바로 나인 줄로, 또 비틀거리는 늙은 실레노스 같은 준남작을 너그러이 비웃은 것이 바로 나인 줄로 그대들은 여길지도 모른다. 하지만 그 웃음은, 번영 말고는 아무것도 떠받들 줄 모르고 성공 너머의 것은 아무것도 볼 줄 모르는 자에게서 나온 것이다. 그런 자들이 세상에 살아 번성하고 있다 — 믿음도 없고, 희망도 없고, 사랑도 없는 자들이. 자, 다정한 벗들이여, 온 힘을 다해 그들에게 맞서자. 또 어떤 자들은, 그것도 아주 성공한 자들은, 한낱 돌팔이요 바보일 뿐이다. 의심할 바 없이, 바로 그런 자들과 맞서 싸우고 그들을 까발리라고 웃음이라는 것이 생겨난 것이다.\n\n피트 크롤리 경은 흔히 천박한 삶이라 일컫는 것에 취미를 둔 철학자였다. 명문 빙키 가문의 딸과 한 그의 첫 결혼은 양친의 주선으로 이루어진 것이었다. 그래서 그는 크롤리 부인이 살아 있는 동안 곧잘 그녀에게 말하기를, 당신이란 사람은 빌어먹게 싸움질이나 좋아하는 도도한 잡것이라, 만일 당신이 죽거든 두 번 다시 그런 부류를 아내로 들이는 일이 있다면 내 목을 매달아도 좋다고 했다. 그리고 그 부인이 세상을 떠나자 그는 약속을 지켜, 두 번째 아내로 머드베리의 철물상 존 토머스 도슨 씨의 딸 로즈 도슨 양을 택했다. 크롤리 부인이 된 로즈는 얼마나 행복한 여인이었던가!\n\n자, 그녀가 누린 행복의 항목들을 하나하나 적어 보기로 하자. 우선 그녀는 자기와 사귀던 청년 피터 버트를 버렸고, 그는 실연의 상심 끝에 밀수와 밀렵을 비롯한 천 가지 못된 길로 빠져들었다. 다음으로 그녀는 마땅한 도리로서 젊은 날의 벗과 가까운 이들 모두와 절교했는데, 물론 그들은 퀸스크롤리에서 크롤리 부인의 접대를 받을 만한 처지가 못 되었다. 그렇다고 그녀가 새로 얻은 신분과 거처에서 자기를 기꺼이 맞아 줄 사람들을 찾은 것도 아니었다. 대체 누가 그러겠는가? 허들스턴 퍼들스턴 경에게는 딸이 셋 있었는데 하나같이 크롤리 부인이 되기를 바라던 터였다. 자일스 왑샷 경의 가문은 왑샷 가의 딸들 가운데 하나가 그 혼인에서 우선권을 얻지 못했다 하여 모욕으로 받아들였고, 그 고장에 남은 준남작들도 동료의 격에 맞지 않는 혼인에 분개했다. 평민들이야 굳이 신경 쓸 것 없으니, 익명으로 투덜대도록 내버려 두기로 하자.\n\n피트 경은 자기 말마따나 그들 가운데 누구에게도 놋쇠 동전 한 닢만큼의 관심도 없었다. 그에게는 어여쁜 로즈가 있었으니, 사내가 제 마음에 흡족한 것 말고 더 무엇을 바라겠는가? 그리하여 그는 밤마다 곤드레만드레 취하곤 했고, 이따금 그 어여쁜 로즈를 두들겨 패기도 했으며, 의회 회기로 런던에 갈 때면 이 너른 세상에 친구라곤 단 한 사람도 없는 그녀를 햄프셔에 홀로 남겨 두곤 했다. 목사의 아내인 뷰트 크롤리 부인조차 그녀를 방문하기를 거절했으니, 자기는 장사치의 딸에게 결코 윗자리를 내주지 않겠노라는 것이었다.\n\n* 윗자리: 원문 'le pas'로, 사교 자리에서 신분에 따라 앞서 나가는 우선권을 뜻한다.\n자연이 크롤리 부인에게 베푼 천부의 자질이라곤 발그레한 두 볼과 흰 살결뿐이었고, 이렇다 할 성격도, 재능도, 견해도, 일거리도, 소일거리도, 또 어리석기 짝이 없는 여인들에게 흔히 주어지는 저 영혼의 기개나 사나운 기질조차 없었던 까닭에, 피트 경의 애정을 붙들어 두는 그녀의 힘은 그리 대단하지 못했다. 두 볼에서 장미빛이 시들었고, 아이를 둘 낳은 뒤로는 자태에서 그 어여쁜 싱그러움도 사라져, 그녀는 남편의 집에서 한낱 기계로 전락하고 말았으니, 작고한 전 크롤리 부인의 그랜드 피아노보다 나을 게 없는 존재였다. 살빛이 옅은 여자였던 그녀는 대개의 금발 여인이 그러하듯 옅은 빛깔의 옷을 입었고, 무엇보다 칙칙하게 끌리는 바다빛 초록이나 단정치 못한 하늘빛 옷차림으로 나타나곤 했다. 그녀는 그 털실 자수를 밤낮없이 떴고, 그와 비슷한 다른 것들도 떴다. 몇 해가 지나는 동안 그녀는 크롤리의 모든 침대에 깔 침대보를 마련했다. 그녀에게는 자그마한 꽃밭이 하나 있었는데 그것만은 제법 애착을 두었으나, 그 밖에 좋아하거나 싫어하는 것이라곤 없었다. 남편이 그녀에게 무례하게 굴면 그녀는 무덤덤했고, 그가 손찌검을 할 때면 울었다. 술에 빠질 만한 기개조차 없었던 그녀는 종일 신을 질질 끌고 머리에 종이를 만 채로 신음하듯 돌아다녔다. 오, 허영의 시장이여, 허영의 시장이여! 그대만 아니었던들 이 여인은 명랑한 처녀였으리라. 피터 버트와 로즈는 단란한 농가에서 화목한 식구들을 거느린 행복한 부부가 되어, 기쁨과 근심과 희망과 분투의 정직한 몫을 누렸으리라. 그러나 작위와 사두마차란 허영의 시장에서는 행복보다 더 값진 장난감인 것이다. 그리고 만일 헨리 8세나 푸른 수염이 지금 살아 있어 열 번째 아내를 구한다면, 이번 시즌에 사교계에 나올 가장 어여쁜 처녀를 손에 넣지 못하리라고 그대는 생각하는가?\n\n으레 짐작할 수 있듯이, 어머니의 맥없는 둔함은 두 어린 딸의 마음에 그다지 애정을 불러일으키지 못했다. 그러나 아이들은 하인 방과 마구간에서 매우 즐겁게 지냈다. 그리고 스코틀랜드인 정원사가 다행히 착한 아내와 착한 아이들을 두고 있었던 덕분에, 아이들은 그의 오두막에서 조금이나마 유익한 교제와 가르침을 얻었으니, 이것이 샤프 양이 오기 전까지 아이들에게 베풀어진 유일한 교육이었다.\n\n그녀를 고용하게 된 것은 피트 크롤리 씨의 간청 덕분이었다. 그는 크롤리 부인이 평생 가졌던 유일한 벗이자 보호자였고, 자식들 말고 그녀가 가냘프게나마 애정을 품은 유일한 사람이었다. 피트 씨는 자기가 그 핏줄을 이어받은 명문 빙키 가문을 닮아, 매우 예의 바르고 반듯한 신사였다. 어른이 되어 크라이스트처치에서 돌아온 그는, 자기를 두려워하던 아버지의 반대에도 아랑곳없이 느슨해진 저택의 기강을 바로잡기 시작했다. 그는 어찌나 엄격하게 세련을 따지는 사람이었던지, 흰 넥타이 없이 식사하느니 차라리 굶기를 택할 위인이었다. 한번은 그가 막 대학에서 돌아왔을 무렵, 집사 호록스가 편지를 쟁반에 받쳐 들지 않고 그냥 가져온 일이 있었는데, 그는 그 하인에게 한 번 눈길을 던지고는 어찌나 신랄한 말을 퍼부었던지, 그 뒤로 호록스는 그의 앞에서 늘 벌벌 떨었다. 온 집안이 그에게 머리를 조아렸다. 그가 집에 와 있을 때면 크롤리 부인은 일찌감치 머리의 종이를 풀었고, 피트 경의 진흙투성이 각반도 자취를 감췄다. 그리고 그 구제 불능의 늙은이가 여전히 다른 옛 버릇은 못 버렸을지언정, 아들이 보는 앞에서는 럼과 물을 들이켜 곤드레가 되는 법이 없었고, 하인들에게도 그저 매우 점잖고 예의 바르게 말할 따름이었다. 또 사람들의 말로는, 아들이 방에 있는 동안에는 피트 경이 크롤리 부인에게 결코 욕설을 퍼붓지 않았다고 한다.\n\n집사에게 \"마님의 식사가 준비되었습니다\"라고 아뢰도록 가르친 것도 그였고, 부인을 식탁으로 모시고 들어가기를 한사코 고집한 것도 그였다. 그는 부인에게 좀처럼 말을 걸지 않았으나, 말을 할 때면 더없이 깍듯한 존경을 담았다. 그리고 부인이 방을 나설 때면 언제나 더없이 위엄 있는 자세로 일어나 문을 열어 주고, 부인이 나가는 길에 우아하게 절을 하지 않는 법이 없었다.\n\n이튼에서 그는 크롤리 양이라 불렸다. 그리고 거기서, 말하기 안타깝지만, 그의 동생 로던이 곧잘 그를 사납게 두들겨 패곤 했다. 그러나 비록 그의 재주는 빼어나지 못했어도, 그는 재능의 부족을 가상한 근면으로 메웠고, 학교에서 보낸 팔 년 동안 천사 말고는 그 누구도 면치 못한다고 흔히들 여기는 그 체벌을 한 번도 받은 적이 없었다.\n\n대학에서의 그의 이력은 물론 더없이 훌륭한 것이었다. 그리고 그는 이곳에서 공적인 삶을 준비했는데, 그 길로 들어서는 데는 외조부인 빈키 경의 후원이 있을 터였다. 그는 고금의 웅변가들을 더없이 부지런히 연구하고, 토론회에서 쉴 새 없이 발언함으로써 그 준비에 임했다. 그러나 비록 그가 막힘없이 술술 말을 쏟아 냈고, 그 가느다란 목소리를 한껏 거드름을 피우며 스스로 흡족해 가며 내질렀으며, 라틴어 인용으로 뒷받침되지 않은, 그리고 완벽하게 진부하고 케케묵지 않은 감상이나 의견이란 단 한 번도 내놓은 적이 없었음에도, 그는 어찌 된 일인지 실패하고 말았다. 누구에게나 성공을 보장해 주었어야 마땅할 그 범용함에도 불구하고 말이다. 그는 친구들이 하나같이 따 놓은 당상이라 장담하던 시 부문 상조차 받지 못했다.\n\n대학을 떠난 뒤 그는 빈키 경의 개인 비서가 되었고, 이어 품퍼니켈 공사관의 수습 외교관으로 임명되었다. 그는 더없이 명예롭게 그 직책을 수행했으며, 스트라스부르 파이로 이루어진 외교 문서를 당대 외무 장관에게 가지고 돌아왔다. 십 년간 수습 외교관으로 머문(애도받아 마땅한 빈키 경이 서거한 뒤로도 몇 해가 더 지난) 끝에, 승진이 더디다는 것을 깨닫고는 마침내 다소 환멸을 느끼며 외교 업무를 그만두고 시골 신사로 처신하기 시작했다.\n\n그는 영국으로 돌아오면서 맥아에 관한 소책자를 한 편 썼고(그는 야심 찬 사람이라 늘 대중 앞에 나서기를 좋아했다), 흑인 노예 해방 문제에 강력히 가담했다. 그러고는 윌버포스 씨의 벗이 되어 그의 정치를 흠모했으며, 아샨티 선교 문제를 두고 사일러스 혼블로어 목사와 그 유명한 서신 왕래를 나누었다. 그는 의회 회기 때는 아닐지언정, 적어도 종교 모임이 열리는 오월이면 런던에 있었다. 시골에서는 치안 판사였고, 종교적 가르침을 받지 못한 이들 사이를 부지런히 방문하고 연설하는 사람이었다. 그는 사우스다운 경의 셋째 딸인 제인 시프섕크스 부인에게 구애 중이라는 말이 돌았는데, 그 부인의 언니 에밀리 부인은 \"선원의 진정한 나침반함\"과 \"핀칠리 공유지의 사과 파는 여인\"이라는 그 감미로운 소책자들을 쓴 이였다.\n\n샤프 양이 전한 그가 퀸스크롤리에서 하는 일들에 대한 묘사는 결코 과장이 아니었다. 그는 그곳의 하인들에게 앞서 언급한 신앙 예배를 강요했고, 거기에(그러는 편이 차라리 나았다) 자기 아버지까지 끌어들여 동참하게 만들었다. 그는 크롤리 교구의 한 독립 교회를 후원했는데, 이는 목사인 숙부의 분노를 크게 샀고, 그 결과 피트 경을 기쁘게 했다. 피트 경은 한두 번 손수 그곳에 나가도록 부추겨졌고, 이 일은 크롤리 교구 교회에서 격렬한 설교 몇 차례를 불러일으켰으니, 그 설교들은 거기 자리한 준남작의 낡은 고딕식 좌석을 정면으로 겨냥한 것이었다. 그러나 정직한 피트 경은 설교 시간 내내 늘 낮잠을 잤던 탓에, 그 일장 설교의 위력을 조금도 느끼지 못했다.\n\n크롤리 씨는 나라와 그리스도교 세계의 이익을 위해 그 노신사가 자신에게 의회 의석을 양보해 주기를 더없이 간절히 바랐다. 그러나 아버지는 한결같이 이를 거절했다. 둘 다 물론 두 번째 의석에서 나오는 연 천오백 파운드를 포기하기에는 너무 신중했다(당시 이 의석은 콰드룬 씨가 차지하고 있었는데, 그는 노예 문제에 관해 백지위임을 받은 처지였다). 사실 가문의 영지는 빚에 크게 옭매여 있었고, 그 자치구에서 끌어오는 수입은 퀸스크롤리 가문에 큰 보탬이 되었다.\n\n그 가문은 초대 준남작 월폴 크롤리가 노끈 봉랍 관리국에서 공금을 횡령한 죄로 부과된 막대한 벌금에서 끝내 회복하지 못했다. 월폴 경은 유쾌한 사람으로, 돈을 거머쥐고 또 쓰는 데 열을 올렸으며(남의 것은 탐하고 제 것은 흥청망청 써 댔다고, 크롤리 씨라면 한숨지으며 말했을 것이다), 그 시절 퀸스크롤리에서 끊임없이 이어진 폭음과 환대 덕분에 온 고을의 사랑을 받았다. 그때는 술 창고에 부르고뉴 포도주가 가득했고, 개집에는 사냥개가, 마구간에는 늠름한 사냥말이 가득했다. 그런데 지금은, 퀸스크롤리가 가진 말이라고 해 봐야 밭을 갈거나 트라팔가르 역마차를 끄는 처지였다. 샤프 양이 바로 이런 말들로 이루어진 한 무리에 실려, 휴마일에 저택으로 끌려온 것이었다. 비록 촌뜨기였으나, 피트 경은 집에 있을 때만큼은 자기 체면에 깐깐한 사람이라 좀처럼 네 마리 말이 아니고서는 외출하는 법이 없었고, 비록 삶은 양고기로 끼니를 때울지언정 그것을 내오는 데는 늘 하인 셋을 두었다.\n\n단순한 인색함만으로 사람이 부자가 될 수 있었다면, 피트 크롤리 경은 어마어마한 부자가 되었을지도 모른다. 만일 그가 밑천이라고는 제 머리밖에 없는 시골 마을의 변호사였다면, 그 머리를 잘 굴려서 자기 앞으로 상당한 영향력과 재산을 거머쥐었을 공산이 매우 크다. 그러나 그는 불운하게도 훌륭한 이름과, 빚에 옭매였을지언정 큰 영지를 타고났는데, 이 둘은 모두 그를 키우기보다는 해치는 쪽으로 작용했다. 그는 소송을 즐기는 취향이 있었고, 이는 해마다 그에게 수천 파운드를 치르게 했다. 그리고 그가 말하듯 어느 한 대리인 손에 등쳐먹히기에는 자기가 너무 영리한 탓에, 그는 자기 일을 열두 명에게 맡겨 엉망으로 처리되게 내버려 두었으니, 그 열두 명을 하나같이 똑같이 불신했다. 그는 어찌나 야박한 지주였던지 파산한 소작인밖에는 도무지 구할 수가 없었고, 어찌나 인색한 농사꾼이었던지 땅에 뿌릴 씨앗마저 아까워했다. 그러자 앙심을 품은 자연이, 더 너그러운 농부에게는 베푸는 수확을 그에게는 인색하게 굴었다. 그는 가능한 온갖 방식으로 투기에 손을 댔다. 광산을 파고, 운하 주식을 사고, 역마차에 말을 대고, 정부와 계약을 맺었으며, 그 고을에서 가장 분주한 사람이자 치안 판사였다. 그가 화강암 채석장의 정직한 일꾼들에게 품삯을 치르지 않으려 한 탓에, 그는 감독관 넷이 달아나면서 재산을 챙겨 미국으로 가 버리는 흡족한 꼴을 맛보았다. 제대로 된 대비책을 마련하지 않은 탓에 그의 탄광은 물로 가득 찼고, 정부는 상한 쇠고기 납품 계약을 그에게 도로 떠안겼다. 또 그의 역마차용 말로 말하자면, 왕국의 우편마차 주인이라면 누구나, 사료를 박하게 먹이고 싸구려로 사들이는 탓에 그가 이 고을의 그 누구보다 많은 말을 잃는다는 것을 알았다. 기질로 말하면 그는 사교적이었고 거만함과는 거리가 멀었다. 아니, 그는 자기 아들인 나리 같은 신사보다 차라리 농부나 말 거간꾼과 어울리는 편을 더 좋아했다. 그는 술을 좋아하고, 욕설을 좋아하고, 농부의 딸들과 시시덕거리기를 좋아했다. 그가 일 실링이라도 베풀거나 선행을 했다는 말은 한 번도 들리지 않았으나, 그는 유쾌하고, 능청스럽고, 잘 웃는 기질이라, 소작인과 농담을 주고받고 술잔을 기울이고는 이튿날 그를 알거지로 만들어 쫓아내는가 하면, 똑같이 흐뭇한 기분으로 자기가 유배 보내는 밀렵꾼과 한바탕 웃음을 나누곤 했다. 여성에 대한 그의 정중함은 이미 리베카 샤프 양이 넌지시 비친 바 있거니와, 한마디로 영국의 준남작이며 귀족이며 평민을 통틀어, 이보다 더 교활하고, 비열하고, 이기적이고, 어리석고, 평판 나쁜 늙은이는 없었다. 피트 크롤리 경의 그 핏빛 손은 제 주머니만 빼고 누구의 주머니든 넘나들었다. 영국 귀족을 흠모하는 사람으로서, 데브렛 명감에 이름이 오른 인물에게 그토록 많은 못된 자질이 깃들어 있음을 인정하지 않을 수 없으니, 그것이 슬프고도 가슴 아픈 일이다.\n\n크롤리 씨가 아버지의 애정을 그토록 단단히 거머쥔 큰 까닭 하나는 금전 관계에서 비롯된 것이었다. 준남작은 아들의 모친이 남긴 과부 재산 가운데 일부를 아들에게 빚지고 있었으나, 그것을 갚는 것이 편치 않았다. 사실 그는 누구에게든 돈을 치른다는 데 거의 이겨 낼 수 없는 거부감을 품고 있어서, 빚을 청산하게 만들려면 억지로 강제하는 수밖에 없었다. 샤프 양은(우리가 곧 듣게 되겠지만, 그녀는 이 집안의 거의 모든 비밀을 꿰뚫게 되었다) 그 명예로운 준남작이 단지 채권자들에게 돈을 치르는 데만도 해마다 수백 파운드를 쓴다고 계산해 냈다. 그러나 이것이야말로 그가 결코 포기할 수 없는 낙이었다. 그는 그 가엾은 작자들을 기다리게 하고, 변제 시기를 이 법정에서 저 법정으로, 이 개정기에서 저 개정기로 미루는 데서 야만적인 즐거움을 느꼈다. 빚을 갚아야 한다면 의회에 들어가는 게 무슨 소용이냐고, 그는 말했다. 그러니 사실 상원 의원으로서의 그의 지위는 그에게 적잖이 쓸모가 있었던 것이다.\n\n허영의 시장이여, 허영의 시장이여! 여기, 철자도 못 쓰고 읽을 마음도 없는 한 사내가 있었다. 촌뜨기의 습성과 교활함을 지녔고, 인생의 목표라고는 좀스러운 송사뿐이며, 추잡하고 더러운 것이 아니고서는 취향도 감흥도 즐거움도 가져 본 적 없는 자였다. 그런데도 그는 어찌 된 셈인지 지위와 명예와 권력을 지녔고, 이 나라의 고관이자 국가의 기둥이었다. 그는 주의 장관이었고 황금 마차를 타고 다녔다. 위대한 장관과 정치가 들이 그에게 아첨했으며, 허영의 시장에서 그는 가장 눈부신 천재나 한 점 티 없는 미덕보다 더 높은 자리를 차지했다.\n\n피트 경에게는 미혼인 이복 누이가 하나 있었는데, 그녀는 자기 어머니의 막대한 재산을 물려받았다. 준남작은 이 돈을 담보 잡고 그녀에게서 빌리려고 제안했으나, 크롤리 양은 그 제안을 거절하고 공채의 안전함을 선호했다. 그러나 그녀는 자기 유산을 피트 경의 둘째 아들과 목사관 식구들에게 나누어 주겠다는 뜻을 내비친 바 있었고, 로던 크롤리가 대학과 군대에서 지내는 동안 한두 번 그의 빚을 갚아 준 적도 있었다. 그 결과 크롤리 양은 퀸스크롤리에 올 때마다 깊은 존경의 대상이 되었으니, 어디에서든 사랑받게 해 줄 만한 잔액이 그녀의 은행에 있었기 때문이다.\n\n은행 잔액이 늙은 부인에게 부여하는 위엄이라니! 그녀가 친척이라면 우리는 그 결점들을 어찌나 너그럽게 봐주는지(부디 모든 독자께 그런 친척이 한 다스씩 있기를), 어찌나 친절하고 마음씨 고운 늙은이로 여기는지! 홉스 앤드 돕스 상회의 후배 동업자는 그녀를, 마름모꼴 가문 문장이 박히고 살지고 씩씩대는 마부가 모는 마차로 어찌나 웃는 낯으로 모셔다 드리는지! 그녀가 우리를 방문하러 오면, 우리는 대개 어떻게든 기회를 잡아 그녀가 세상에서 차지하는 지위를 친구들에게 알리는지! 우리는(그리고 더없이 진실되게) 맥휘터 양의 서명이 든 오천 파운드짜리 수표가 있었으면 좋겠다고 말한다. 그 정도야 그분께 아쉬울 것도 없죠, 하고 아내가 말한다. 친구가 맥휘터 양이 친척이냐고 물으면 자네는 짐짓 태평하고 무심한 투로 우리 고모님이시라네, 하고 말한다. 자네 아내는 끊임없이 그녀에게 자그마한 애정의 표시를 보내고, 자네의 어린 딸들은 끝도 없이 털실로 바구니와 방석과 발받침을 짠다. 그녀가 자네를 방문하러 오면 그녀의 방에는 어찌나 따뜻한 불이 활활 타는지, 정작 자네 아내는 불도 없이 코르셋 끈을 조이는데 말이다! 그녀가 머무는 동안 집은 다른 철에는 볼 수 없는 흥겹고, 말끔하고, 따스하고, 유쾌하고, 아늑한 모습을 띤다. 친애하는 선생, 자네 자신부터 저녁 식사 뒤에 깜빡 잠드는 것을 잊고, 어느새 카드 한판을(자네는 늘 지면서도) 무척 좋아하는 자신을 발견하게 된다. 자네는 또 어찌나 잘 먹는지, 날마다 사냥한 고기에, 맘지 마데이라 포도주에, 런던에서 끝도 없이 올라오는 생선이라니. 부엌의 하인들마저 이 전반적인 번영을 함께 누린다. 그리고 어찌 된 셈인지, 맥휘터 양의 살진 마부가 머무는 동안에는 맥주가 한결 독해지고, 아이 방에서(그녀의 하녀가 거기서 끼니를 든다) 차와 설탕을 소비하는 것쯤은 조금도 따지지 않게 된다. 그러한가, 그렇지 아니한가? 나는 중산층에게 묻겠다. 아, 자비로운 신이여! 부디 제게 늙은 고모 한 분을 보내 주소서. 미혼의 고모, 마차에 가문 문장을 박고, 연한 커피색 앞머리를 얹은 고모를 보내 주소서. 그러면 우리 아이들이 그분을 위해 어찌나 손가방을 짜 댈 것이며, 우리 줄리아와 나는 그분을 어찌나 편안히 모실 것인가! 감미로운, 감미로운 환상이여! 어리석은, 어리석은 꿈이여!\n\n이제 앞선 페이지들에서 그 초상을 그려 보인 그 사랑스러운 가족의 일원으로 받아들여졌으니, 레베카로서는 본인의 표현대로 은인들의 마음에 들도록 처신하고, 제 능력이 닿는 데까지 그들의 신임을 얻는 것이 자연스러운 의무가 되었다. 보호자 없는 고아가 지닌 이 감사라는 미덕을 어찌 감탄하지 않을 수 있겠는가. 그리고 만약 그녀의 계산에 얼마간의 이기심이 섞여 들었다 한들, 그 신중함이 더없이 정당한 것이 아니라고 누가 단언할 수 있겠는가? \"나는 이 세상에 혼자야.\" 의지가지없는 그 소녀는 말했다. \"내 손으로 벌어들이는 것 말고는 기댈 데가 없어. 게다가 나보다 분별력이 절반도 안 되는 저 발그레한 얼굴의 꼬맹이 애밀리아는 만 파운드에다 탄탄한 살림까지 가졌는데, 가엾은 레베카는(게다가 내 몸매가 그 애보다 훨씬 낫지) 오직 제 자신과 제 재치만 믿을 수밖에 없는 신세지. 자, 내 재치가 떳떳한 생계를 마련해 줄 수 있을지, 그리고 언젠가는 내가 애밀리아 양에게 그 애보다 내가 진정 우월하다는 걸 보여 줄 수 있을지 어디 두고 보자고. 가엾은 애밀리아가 싫다는 건 아니야. 그토록 악의 없고 선량한 사람을 누가 싫어할 수 있겠어? 다만 내가 세상에서 그 애 위에 자리 잡는 날이 온다면 그건 참 멋진 날일 거야. 그러지 못할 까닭이 대체 어디 있겠어?\" 우리의 작고 낭만적인 벗은 그렇게 제 앞날에 대한 환상을 빚어 갔다. 그녀가 쌓아 올린 모든 공중누각에 남편이 주요 거주자로 자리 잡고 있다 하여 우리가 충격받을 필요는 없다. 젊은 숙녀들이 남편 말고 무슨 생각을 하겠는가? 그들의 사랑스러운 어머니들 또한 그것 말고 무슨 생각을 하겠는가? \"내 어머니 노릇은 내가 해야지.\" 레베카는 그렇게 말했다. 조스 세들리와의 그 자그마한 낭패를 떠올리며, 패배감이 짜릿하게 스쳐 가는 것을 느끼면서.\n\n그리하여 그녀는 퀸스크롤리 가에서 자신의 처지를 편안하고 안전하게 만들겠노라 현명하게 마음먹었고, 그 목적을 위해 조금이라도 자기 안락에 영향을 끼칠 만한 주위 모든 이들을 벗으로 삼기로 결심했다.\n\n크롤리 부인은 그런 인물 축에 들지 못했을 뿐 아니라, 워낙 게으르고 줏대가 없어 제 집안에서조차 털끝만큼의 비중도 없는 여자였으므로, 레베카는 곧 그 호의를 가꿀 필요가 전혀 없다는 것을, 아니 얻는 것 자체가 불가능하다는 것을 깨달았다. 그녀는 제자들에게 그들의 \"가엾은 어머니\" 이야기를 하곤 했다. 그리고 그 부인을 향해서는 온갖 냉랭한 존경의 표시로 대하긴 했으나, 정작 정성의 큰 몫은 현명하게도 가족의 나머지 사람들에게 쏟았다.\n\n마음껏 갈채를 얻어 낸 아이들을 다루는 그녀의 방법은 꽤나 단순했다. 그녀는 아이들의 어린 머릿속을 지나친 학식으로 들볶지 않았고, 오히려 스스로 깨치도록 제멋대로 내버려 두었다. 자습보다 더 효과적인 가르침이 어디 있겠는가? 큰딸은 책을 제법 좋아했는데, 퀸스크롤리의 낡은 서재에는 지난 세기의 가벼운 문학 작품들이 프랑스어와 영어로 상당량 갖춰져 있었고(끈과 봉랍 관리국 장관이 실각하던 무렵에 사들인 것들이었다), 그 책꽂이를 건드리는 이라곤 그녀밖에 없었으므로, 레베카는 즐겁게, 말하자면 놀이를 하듯이, 로즈 크롤리 양에게 많은 것을 가르칠 수 있었다.\n\n그리하여 그녀와 로즈 양은 함께 수많은 유쾌한 프랑스어와 영어 작품을 읽었는데, 그중에는 박식한 스몰릿 박사, 재기 넘치는 헨리 필딩 씨, 우리의 불멸의 시인 그레이가 그토록 흠모했던 우아하고 환상적인 크레비용 2세, 그리고 만물에 통달한 볼테르 씨의 작품도 끼어 있었다. 한번은 크롤리 씨가 아이들이 무엇을 읽고 있느냐고 묻자 가정교사는 \"스몰릿입니다\"라고 대답했다. \"오, 스몰릿이라.\" 크롤리 씨는 자못 흡족해하며 말했다. \"그의 역사서는 흄 씨의 것보다 더 따분하긴 해도, 결코 그만큼 위험하지는 않지. 자네가 읽는 게 역사서인가?\" \"네.\" 로즈 양이 대답했다. 다만 그것이 험프리 클링커 씨의 사연이라는 말은 덧붙이지 않았다. 또 한번은 누이가 프랑스 희곡집을 들고 있는 것을 보고 크롤리 씨가 다소 못마땅해했으나, 가정교사가 회화에서 프랑스어 관용 표현을 익히기 위함이라고 일러 주자 그도 마지못해 흡족해하지 않을 수 없었다. 크롤리 씨는 외교관답게 자신의 프랑스어 구사 솜씨를 무척 자랑스러워했고(아직은 세속에 발을 담그고 있는 사람이었으니까), 그 능숙함을 두고 가정교사가 끊임없이 바치는 찬사에 적잖이 우쭐해했다.\n\n이와는 반대로 바이올렛 양의 취향은 언니보다 거칠고 왁자했다. 그녀는 암탉들이 알을 낳는 외진 자리들을 죄다 알고 있었다. 나무에 기어올라 깃털 가진 가수들의 둥지를 털어 알록달록한 전리품을 빼앗을 줄도 알았다. 또 어린 망아지를 타고서 카밀라처럼 들판을 내달리는 것을 낙으로 삼았다. 그녀는 아버지와 마구간 일꾼들의 귀염둥이였다. 요리사에게는 사랑스러운 존재인 동시에 골칫거리였는데, 잼 단지들이 숨겨진 곳을 기어이 찾아내어 손에 닿기만 하면 들이쳤기 때문이다. 그녀와 언니는 끊임없이 싸움을 벌였다. 이런 자잘한 비행들을 샤프 양은 설령 알아챈다 해도 크롤리 부인에게는 일러바치지 않았다. 그랬다가는 부인이 아버지에게, 더 나쁘게는 크롤리 씨에게 일러바칠 터였으니까. 대신 그녀는 바이올렛 양이 착하게 굴고 가정교사를 사랑하기만 한다면 말하지 않겠노라 약속했다.\n\n크롤리 씨에게 샤프 양은 공손하고 순종적이었다. 어머니가 프랑스 여인이었음에도 도무지 이해할 수 없는 프랑스어 구절이 나오면 그에게 여쭙곤 했고, 그러면 그는 그녀가 흡족하도록 풀이해 주었다. 게다가 그는 속된 문학에서 그녀를 도와줄 뿐 아니라, 친절하게도 좀 더 진중한 경향의 책들을 골라 주고, 자기 이야기의 많은 부분을 그녀에게 들려주었다. 그녀는 그가 콰시마부 구호 협회에서 한 연설을 한없이 우러러보았고, 맥아에 관한 그의 소책자에 관심을 보였으며, 저녁이면 그가 늘어놓는 설교에 눈물까지 흘릴 만큼 자주 감동하여 한숨과 함께 하늘을 올려다보며 \"오, 감사합니다, 선생님\" 하고 말하곤 했는데, 그러면 그는 이따금 황송하게도 그녀와 악수까지 해 주었다. \"역시 핏줄이 모든 걸 결정하는 법이지.\" 이 귀족적인 신앙가는 그렇게 말하곤 했다. \"이곳 사람들은 하나도 감동받지 못하는데, 샤프 양은 내 말에 어쩌면 저토록 일깨워지는지. 내가 저들에게는 너무 고상한 거야. 너무 섬세한 거지. 내 화법을 좀 더 평이하게 길들여야겠어. 하지만 저 사람은 알아들어. 그 어머니가 몽모랑시 가문 출신이거든.\"\n\n알려진 바로는, 샤프 양은 모친 쪽으로 바로 이 유서 깊은 가문의 후손이었다. 물론 그녀는 어머니가 무대에 섰었다는 말은 하지 않았다. 그랬다가는 크롤리 씨의 신앙적 양심에 충격을 주었을 테니까. 저 끔찍한 혁명이 얼마나 많은 고귀한 망명객을 가난 속에 빠뜨렸던가! 그 집에 들어온 지 몇 달 되지도 않아 그녀는 자기 조상들에 관한 이야기를 여럿 풀어놓았고, 그중 몇몇을 크롤리 씨는 마침 서재에 있던 도지에의 인명사전에서 찾아냈는데, 이것이 그 이야기들의 진실성과 레베카의 고귀한 혈통에 대한 그의 믿음을 한층 굳건히 해 주었다. 사전을 뒤지며 캐고 다닌 이 호기심에서 우리는 무엇을 짐작해야 할까. 우리의 여주인공은 크롤리 씨가 자기에게 마음이 있다고 짐작할 수 있었을까? 아니, 그저 우정 어린 관심일 뿐이었다. 그가 제인 시프섕크스 영애에게 마음을 두고 있다고 이미 말하지 않았던가?\n\n그는 한두 번 레베카에게 피트 경과 주사위놀이를 하는 것이 온당한지를 두고 따져 물으며, 그것이 신을 모르는 오락이니 차라리 「스럼프의 유산」이나 「무어필즈의 눈먼 빨래꾼」, 아니면 좀 더 진중한 성격의 책을 읽는 편이 훨씬 나을 거라고 했다. 그러나 샤프 양은 사랑하는 어머니가 늙은 트릭트랙 백작이며 점잖은 뒤코르네 신부와 곧잘 똑같은 놀이를 하시곤 했다고 말하며, 이 오락을 비롯한 여러 세속적 즐길거리에 대한 핑계를 마련했다.\n\n그런데 이 작은 가정교사가 고용주의 마음에 든 것은 비단 준남작과 주사위놀이를 해 준 덕분만은 아니었다. 그녀는 그에게 쓸모 있는 사람이 될 갖가지 방법을 찾아냈다. 그녀가 퀸스크롤리에 오기 전에 그가 즐겁게 해 주겠노라 호언했던 그 모든 법률 서류를, 그녀는 지칠 줄 모르는 인내심으로 끝까지 읽어 냈다. 그의 편지를 여러 통 베껴 쓰는 일도 자청했고, 그 철자법을 솜씨 좋게 고쳐 오늘날의 관용에 맞도록 손보았다. 그녀는 영지며 농장이며 정원이며 화원이며 마구간에 이르기까지 관련된 모든 일에 관심을 기울이게 되었다. 그리하여 어찌나 유쾌한 동반자였던지, 준남작은 아침 식사 후 산책을 그녀(물론 아이들도 함께였다) 없이는 좀처럼 나서지 않게 되었으니, 그럴 때면 그녀는 관목 숲에서 쳐 내야 할 나무며, 갈아엎어야 할 화단이며, 베어야 할 작물이며, 수레나 쟁기에 매야 할 말들에 관해 조언을 건넸다. 퀸스크롤리에 온 지 일 년도 채 되기 전에 그녀는 준남작의 신임을 완전히 얻어 냈다. 식탁에서의 대화도 예전에는 그와 집사 호록스 씨 사이에서 오가던 것이, 이제는 거의 전적으로 피트 경과 샤프 양 사이에서만 오갔다. 크롤리 씨가 자리를 비울 때면 그녀는 사실상 이 집의 안주인이나 다름없었으나, 새로이 격상된 자기 처지에서도 어찌나 신중하고 겸손하게 처신했던지 부엌과 마구간의 실권자들의 비위를 거스르는 법이 없었으니, 그들 사이에서 그녀의 태도는 언제나 더없이 겸손하고 상냥했다. 그녀는 우리가 앞서 알던, 오만하고 수줍고 불평 많던 그 작은 소녀와는 사뭇 딴사람이었고, 이런 성정의 변화는 그녀 편에서 대단한 신중함이나, 고치려는 진실한 열망, 적어도 대단한 도덕적 용기를 입증하는 것이었다. 우리의 레베카가 새로이 채택한 이 친절과 겸손의 방침을 명령한 것이 과연 그 마음이었는지는, 앞으로 펼쳐질 그녀의 행적이 증명해 줄 것이다. 몇 해를 통째로 이어 가는 위선의 방침이란 스물한 살 먹은 사람이 흡족하게 실행하기에는 좀처럼 어려운 노릇이다. 그러나 독자들은 우리의 여주인공이 나이는 어려도 인생과 경험에서는 노숙했음을 기억할 것이며, 만약 그녀가 무척 영리한 여자임을 독자들이 진작 알아채지 못했다면 우리가 헛글을 써 온 셈이리라.\n\n크롤리 가의 장남과 차남은 날씨 상자 속의 신사와 숙녀처럼 결코 한집에 함께 머무는 법이 없었다. 둘은 진심으로 서로를 미워했다. 실제로 용기병인 로던 크롤리는 이 집안 전체를 몹시 경멸하여, 고모가 해마다 방문할 때를 빼고는 좀처럼 이곳에 발걸음하지 않았다.\n\n이 노부인의 빼어난 장점은 앞서 언급한 바 있다. 그녀는 칠만 파운드를 지녔고, 로던을 거의 양자나 다름없이 거두었다. 그녀는 큰조카를 몹시 싫어하여 물러 터진 겁쟁이라고 깔보았다. 그 답례로 그는 서슴없이 고모의 영혼이 돌이킬 수 없이 타락했다고 단언했고, 동생이 내세에서 누릴 가망 또한 털끝만큼도 나을 게 없다는 견해를 품었다. \"그 양반은 신을 모르는 세속의 여인이야.\" 크롤리 씨는 그렇게 말하곤 했다. \"무신론자들이며 프랑스인들과 어울려 사니까. 그 끔찍하고도 끔찍한 처지를, 무덤이 코앞에 닥쳤는데도 허영과 방종과 불경과 어리석음에 그토록 빠져 있는 꼴을 생각하면 내 마음이 다 떨려.\" 사실 노부인은 저녁마다 그가 한 시간씩 늘어놓는 설교를 듣기를 아예 거부했다. 그래서 그녀가 혼자 퀸스크롤리에 올 때면 그는 평소의 신앙 의식을 부득이 중단해야 했다.\n\n\"크롤리 양이 내려오거든 설교일랑 집어치워라, 피트.\" 아버지가 말했다. \"설교는 못 참겠다고 편지에 써 보냈으니 말이다.\"\n\n\"하지만 아버지, 하인들을 생각하셔야지요.\"\n\n\"하인들이야 어찌 되든 알 게 뭐냐.\" 피트 경이 말했다. 아들은 만약 하인들이 자신의 가르침이라는 은혜를 박탈당한다면 그보다 더 고약한 일이 벌어지리라 생각했다.\n\n\"이런 제기랄, 피트!\" 아들의 항변에 아버지가 말했다. \"설마 연 삼천 파운드를 집안 밖으로 흘려보낼 만큼 멍청이는 아니겠지?\"\n\n\"우리 영혼에 비하면 돈이 다 무엇입니까, 아버지?\" 크롤리 씨가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그러니까 노부인이 너한테 그 돈을 안 남길까 봐 그러는 게냐?\" 그런데 그것이 크롤리 씨의 본심이었는지 누가 알겠는가?\n\n크롤리 노양은 분명 타락한 무리의 하나였다. 그녀는 파크 레인에 아담하고 안락한 집을 한 채 가지고 있었는데, 런던 사교철 동안 너무 과하게 먹고 마신 탓에 여름이면 해러게이트나 첼트넘으로 떠났다. 그녀는 늙은 베스타 처녀들 가운데 가장 손님 대접을 잘하고 가장 쾌활한 축이었으며, 자기 말로는 한창때 미인이었다고 했다.(늙은 여인은 누구나 한때 미인이었다는 것을 우리는 익히 알고 있다.) 그녀는 재사였고, 그 시절치고는 무시무시한 급진파였다. 프랑스에 다녀온 적이 있었고(그곳에서 생쥐스트가 그녀에게 불운한 연정을 불러일으켰다고들 한다), 그 후로 줄곧 프랑스 소설과 프랑스 요리와 프랑스 포도주를 사랑했다. 그녀는 볼테르를 읽었고 루소를 줄줄 외웠으며, 이혼에 관해서는 무척 가볍게, 여성의 권리에 관해서는 무척 열성적으로 떠들어 댔다. 집안 방마다 폭스 씨의 초상이 걸려 있었다. 그 정치가가 야당에 있던 시절에 그녀가 그와 한판 도박을 벌이지 않았다고는 장담 못 하겠다. 그리고 그가 입각하자, 그녀는 피트 경과 그의 퀸스크롤리 동료를 그쪽으로 끌어왔노라며 큰 공을 내세웠다. 사실 이 정직한 부인이 수고를 들이지 않았어도 피트 경은 제 발로 넘어갔을 테지만 말이다. 그 위대한 휘그당 정치가가 죽은 뒤 피트 경이 견해를 바꾸었다는 것은 굳이 말할 필요도 없겠다.\n\n이 갸륵한 노부인은 로던 크롤리가 소년이던 시절부터 그를 어여삐 여겨 케임브리지로 보냈고(옥스퍼드에 다니던 형에 맞서서였다), 그 대학 당국이 이 년간 머문 그를 떠나 달라고 요청하자, 녹색 근위 기병대의 장교직을 사 주었다.\n\n이 젊은 장교는 완벽하고도 이름난 \"혈기 넘치는 멋쟁이\", 곧 도회의 한량이었다. 권투며 쥐 사냥이며 핸드볼 경기며 사두마차 몰이가 그 무렵 우리 영국 귀족 사회의 유행이었는데, 그는 이 모든 고귀한 기예에 통달한 명수였다. 그리고 비록 그가 섭정 왕세자 주위에 결집하는 것이 본분이라 아직 외국에서의 복무로 무용을 떨치지는 못한 근위 부대 소속이었으나, 로던 크롤리는 이미(그가 분에 넘치게 좋아하던 도박과 관련하여) 피비린내 나는 결투를 세 차례나 벌여, 죽음을 두려워하지 않는다는 충분한 증거를 보여 준 터였다.\n\n\"그리고 죽음 다음에 오는 것도 두려워하지 않지.\" 크롤리 씨는 그렇게 말하며 구스베리빛 두 눈을 천장으로 치떴다. 그는 늘 동생의 영혼을, 아니면 자기와 견해가 다른 이들의 영혼을 염려했다. 이것은 신앙심 깊은 많은 이들이 스스로에게 베푸는 일종의 위안이다.\n\n어리석고 낭만적인 크롤리 양은 자기가 아끼는 조카의 무용에 질겁하기는커녕, 결투를 치를 때마다 늘 그의 빚을 갚아 주었고, 그의 품행에 대해 수군대는 말이라곤 한마디도 들으려 하지 않았다. \"젊은 혈기야 풀고 다니는 거지.\" 그녀는 그렇게 말하곤 했다. \"그래도 저 칭얼대는 위선자 같은 제 형보다는 훨씬 값진 아이야.\"\n\n홀에 사는 이 정직한 사람들 외에도(그들의 소박함과 감미로운 시골다운 순수함이야말로 도회 생활에 비한 전원 생활의 이점을 분명히 보여 주는 것이렷다), 우리는 독자에게 목사관에 사는 그들의 친척이자 이웃인 뷰트 크롤리와 그 아내를 소개해야겠다.\n\n뷰트 크롤리 목사는 키가 크고 위풍당당하며 쾌활한, 사제용 챙 넓은 모자를 쓴 사람으로, 준남작인 형보다 이 고장에서 훨씬 더 인기가 있었다. 대학 시절 그는 크라이스트처치 보트의 선두 노잡이를 맡았고, \"읍내\"의 내로라하는 권투꾼들을 죄다 두들겨 패 본 사람이었다. 그는 권투며 운동에 대한 취미를 사생활에까지 끌고 들어왔다. 이십 마일 안에서 벌어지는 싸움이라면 그가 빠지는 법이 없었고, 경마든 사냥개 시합이든 보트 경주든 무도회든 선거든 시찰 만찬이든, 아니 사실 온 고장의 어떤 훌륭한 만찬이든 그가 참석할 방도를 찾아내지 못하는 자리란 없었다. 퍼들스턴이나 록스비, 와프숏 홀, 혹은 그가 두루 친분을 맺고 있던 고장의 대귀족들 댁에서 만찬회라도 열리는 날이면, 목사관에서 이십 마일이나 떨어진 곳에서도 그의 밤색 암말과 마차 등불을 볼 수 있었다. 그는 좋은 목소리를 지녀서 \"남풍에 흐린 하늘\"을 불렀고, 합창에서 \"와!\" 하는 추임새를 넣어 만장의 갈채를 받았다. 그는 후추와 소금을 섞은 듯한 잿빛 프록코트 차림으로 사냥개를 따라 말을 달렸고, 고장에서 손꼽히는 낚시꾼이기도 했다.\n\n목사의 아내 크롤리 부인은 영리한 자그마한 여자로, 이 훌륭한 성직자의 설교문을 대신 써 주는 사람이었다. 살림에 능하고 딸들과 함께 집에서 지내는 일이 많았던 터라, 그녀는 목사관 안을 절대적으로 다스리는 한편 남편에게는 바깥에서의 완전한 자유를 현명하게 허락했다. 남편은 마음 내키는 대로 오가도, 또 변덕이 시키는 만큼 며칠이고 바깥에서 식사를 하고 와도 좋았다. 크롤리 부인은 알뜰한 여자였고 포트와인 값이 얼마인지 잘 알았기 때문이다. 뷰트 부인이 퀸스 크롤리의 젊은 목사를 낚아챈 이래로(그녀는 좋은 집안 출신으로, 고인이 된 헥터 맥태비시 중령의 딸이었으며, 그녀와 그 어머니가 해러게이트에서 뷰트를 걸고 한판 벌여 그를 따낸 것이었다) 그녀는 그에게 신중하고 검소한 아내가 되어 주었다. 그러나 그녀의 알뜰함에도 불구하고 그는 늘 빚에 쪼들렸다. 아버지가 살아 있을 적에 진 대학 빚을 갚는 데만도 적어도 십 년이 걸렸다. 179×년, 이 빚더미에서 막 벗어났을 때 그는 더비 경마에서 우승한 캥거루를 상대로 일대 백(이십 파운드씩)의 내기를 걸었다. 목사는 파멸적인 이자로 돈을 끌어다 써야 했고, 그 후로 줄곧 허덕여 왔다. 누이가 이따금 백 파운드씩 도와주긴 했지만, 물론 그의 가장 큰 희망은 누이가 죽는 데 있었다. 그때가 되면 \"젠장\"(그의 말버릇대로) \"마틸다도 내게 재산의 절반은 남겨 줄 수밖에 없을 테니\" 말이다.\n\n그리하여 준남작과 그 아우는 두 형제가 도무지 사이가 틀어질 만한 온갖 이유를 다 갖추고 있었던 셈이다. 핏 경은 무수한 집안 거래에서 뷰트보다 유리한 고지를 차지해 왔다. 젊은 핏은 사냥을 안 할 뿐 아니라 숙부의 코앞에 예배소를 세워 놓기까지 했다. 로던은, 알려진 바로는, 크롤리 양 재산의 대부분을 물려받게 되어 있었다. 이런 돈 거래, 이런 생사를 건 투기, 상속될 전리품을 둘러싼 이런 소리 없는 다툼이야말로 허영의 시장에서 형제들을 서로 더없이 살갑게 만드는 법이다. 나로 말하자면, 오 파운드짜리 지폐 한 장이 끼어들어 형제간의 반세기에 걸친 우애를 단번에 깨뜨려 버린 경우를 알고 있다. 그러니 세속의 사람들 사이에서 사랑이란 얼마나 곱고도 질긴 것인가를 떠올리면 그저 감탄하지 않을 수 없는 것이다.\n\n레베카 같은 인물이 퀸스 크롤리에 도착하고, 그곳 모든 사람의 환심을 차차 사들였던 일이 뷰트 크롤리 부인의 눈에 띄지 않았으리라고는 생각할 수 없다. 홀에서 소 등심 한 덩이가 며칠을 가는지, 큰 빨래 때 아마포가 얼마나 마련되는지, 남쪽 담장에 복숭아가 몇 개나 열렸는지, 마님이 앓을 때 약을 몇 첩이나 드시는지까지 훤히 꿰고 있던 뷰트 부인이었으니(시골의 어떤 사람들에게는 이런 사소한 일들이 더없이 큰 관심사인 법이다), 그런 뷰트 부인이, 다시 말해, 홀에 새로 온 가정교사의 내력과 됨됨이에 관해 온갖 것을 캐묻지 않고 그냥 넘어갈 리 없었던 것이다. 목사관과 홀의 하인들 사이에는 늘 더없이 좋은 양해가 오갔다. 목사관 부엌에는 늘 홀 사람들을 위한 좋은 맥주 한 잔이 마련되어 있었으니, 그도 그럴 것이 홀에서 평소 마시는 술은 워낙 묽었기 때문이다. 사실 목사 부인은 홀의 맥주 한 통에 맥아가 얼마나 들어가는지까지 정확히 알고 있었다. 주인들 사이에 친척의 연이 닿아 있듯, 홀과 목사관의 하인들 사이에도 그러한 연이 얽혀 있었다. 그리고 이런 통로를 거쳐 양가는 서로의 동정을 완벽하게 알고 지냈다. 덧붙여 말하건대, 이것은 일반적인 진리로 적어 둘 만하다. 당신과 당신 형제가 사이가 좋을 때는 그가 무엇을 하든 당신은 무심하다. 그러나 다투고 나면 그가 드나드는 일거수일투족을, 마치 당신이 그의 첩자라도 된 양 죄다 알게 되는 것이다.\n\n그리하여 레베카가 도착하고 얼마 지나지 않아, 홀에서 크롤리 부인에게 올라오는 소식 보고에는 그녀가 으레 한자리를 차지하게 되었다. 그 보고란 이런 식이었다. \"검은 돼지를 잡았는데 무게가 몇 스톤이고, 옆구리 살은 소금에 절였으며, 저녁상에는 돼지 순대와 다리 고기가 올랐다. 머드베리의 크램프 씨가 존 블랙모어를 감옥에 처넣는 문제로 핏 경을 찾아왔다. 핏 씨는 예배소에 갔다(참석한 사람들의 이름을 죄다 곁들여서). 마님은 여느 때와 다름없으시다. 아가씨들은 가정교사와 함께 있다.\"\n\n이윽고 이런 보고가 들어왔다. 새 가정교사는 보기 드문 살림꾼이라더라, 핏 경이 그녀에게 무척 살갑게 굴더라, 크롤리 씨도 그렇더라, 그녀에게 종교 소책자를 읽어 주더라. \"저런 막돼먹은 여편네 같으니라고!\" 자그맣고 성마르며 부산스러운, 거무튀튀한 얼굴의 뷰트 크롤리 부인이 말했다.\n\n마침내 들어온 보고에 따르면, 그 가정교사가 모든 이를 \"구워삶아\" 핏 경의 편지를 대신 쓰고, 그의 사무를 처리하고, 그의 회계를 관리하더라는 것이었다. 마님이며 크롤리 씨며 딸들이며 할 것 없이 온 집안을 손아귀에 쥐고 있다는 것이었다. 이에 크롤리 부인은 그녀가 교활한 계집이며 뭔가 무시무시한 속셈을 품고 있는 게 분명하다고 단언했다. 이렇듯 홀에서 벌어지는 일들은 목사관에서 더없이 좋은 이야깃거리가 되었고, 뷰트 부인의 반짝이는 두 눈은 적진에서 일어나는 모든 일을 염탐해 냈다. 모든 일을, 그리고 그 밖의 숱한 일까지 말이다.\n\n뷰트 크롤리 부인이 치즈윅 거리의 핑커튼 여사에게.\n\n퀸스 크롤리 목사관에서, 십이월—.\n\n친애하는 부인께. 부인의 그 즐겁고도 더없이 값진 가르침을 받은 지 어느덧 여러 해가 흘렀지만, 저는 핑커튼 여사님과 그리운 치즈윅을 향해 늘 더없이 깊고 더없이 경건한 애정을 간직해 왔습니다. 부디 건강하시기를 바랍니다. 세상도, 교육이라는 대의도 핑커튼 여사님을 오래오래 잃어서는 안 될 것입니다. 제 벗인 퍼들스턴 부인이 그 사랑스러운 딸들에게 여교사가 필요하다고 말했을 때(저는 너무 가난해서 제 딸들을 위해서는 가정교사를 둘 수 없으나, 저 자신이 치즈윅에서 교육받지 않았던가요?), 저는 외쳤습니다. \"그 탁월하시고 비길 데 없는 핑커튼 여사님 말고 누구와 의논하겠어요?\" 한마디로, 친애하는 부인께서 명단에 올려 두신 숙녀들 가운데, 저의 친절한 벗이자 이웃에게 그 봉사를 보내 줄 만한 분이 혹 있으신지요? 분명히 말씀드리지만, 그 부인은 오직 부인께서 골라 주시는 가정교사만을 받아들일 것입니다.\n\n제 사랑하는 남편은 핑커튼 여사님의 학원에서 나오는 것이라면 무엇이든 마음에 든다고 흔쾌히 말합니다. 그이와 제 사랑하는 딸들을, 제 젊은 날의 벗이자 우리나라 저 위대한 사전 편찬자께서 흠모하시는 그분께 소개할 수 있다면 얼마나 좋을까요! 행여 햄프셔로 여행하시는 일이 있거든, 부디 부인의 왕림으로 저희의 시골 목사관을 빛내 주시기를 바란다고, 크롤리 씨가 제게 꼭 전해 달라 합니다. 비록 누추하나 행복한 보금자리인 이곳을 말입니다.\n\n부인을 사랑하는 마사 크롤리 올림.\n\n추신. 크롤리 씨의 형님이자 준남작 되시는 분과 저희는, 아아, 형제가 마땅히 누려야 할 그런 화목한 사이가 못 됩니다만, 그분이 어린 딸들을 위해 가정교사를 두고 있는데, 듣자 하니 그 여자가 다행히도 치즈윅에서 교육받은 행운을 누렸다 합니다. 그녀에 관해 이런저런 소문이 들려옵니다. 그런데 저는 제 더없이 사랑스러운 어린 조카딸들에게 더없이 애틋한 관심을 품고 있어, 집안 간의 불화에도 불구하고 그 아이들을 제 자식들 틈에 보고 싶을 뿐 아니라, 부인의 제자라면 누구에게든 마음을 쓰고 싶은 터이니, 친애하는 핑커튼 여사님, 부디 이 젊은 숙녀의 내력을 좀 알려 주십시오. 부인을 봐서라도 저는 그녀와 벗이 되어 주고 싶은 마음이 간절합니다. ㅡM. C.\n\n핑커튼 여사가 뷰트 크롤리 부인에게.\n\n치즈윅, 존슨관에서, 십이월 18××년.\n\n친애하는 부인께. 부인의 정중한 서신을 받게 되어 영광이며, 이에 곧바로 답신을 올립니다. 저처럼 더없이 고된 자리에 있는 사람으로서, 저의 어머니 같은 보살핌이 화답하는 애정을 이끌어 냈음을 알게 되고, 또 저 상냥한 뷰트 크롤리 부인에게서 지난날 저의 빼어난 제자, 명랑하고 재주 많던 마사 맥태비시 양을 알아보게 되니 더없이 흐뭇합니다. 지금 제 보살핌 아래에는 본원에서 부인과 동기였던 많은 분들의 따님들이 있으니, 만약 부인의 사랑스러운 따님들이 저의 가르치는 보살핌을 필요로 한다면 제게 얼마나 큰 기쁨이 될까요!\n\n퍼들스턴 부인께 정중한 인사를 전하며, 저는 (서신으로나마) 부인께 저의 두 벗, 터핀 양과 호키 양을 소개하는 영광을 누립니다.\n\n이 두 젊은 숙녀는 그리스어, 라틴어, 히브리어 기초를, 수학과 역사를, 스페인어, 프랑스어, 이탈리아어와 지리를, 성악과 기악을, 선생의 도움 없이 무용을, 그리고 자연과학의 기초를 가르치기에 완벽하게 자격을 갖추고 있습니다. 지구의 사용에서도 둘 다 능숙합니다. 이에 더하여 터핀 양은, 고인이 된 토머스 터핀 목사(케임브리지 코퍼스 칼리지의 특별연구원)의 따님으로서, 시리아어와 헌법학 기초까지 가르칠 수 있습니다. 다만 이 젊은 숙녀는 이제 겨우 열여덟 살이고 용모가 대단히 빼어난지라, 어쩌면 허들스턴 퍼들스턴 경의 가정에는 다소 거슬릴는지도 모르겠습니다.\n\n반면에 러티시아 호키 양은 용모가 빼어나지 못합니다. 나이는 스물아홉이며, 얼굴은 천연두 자국으로 몹시 얽었습니다. 걸음걸이에 절뚝거림이 있고, 머리는 붉으며, 사시기가 약간 있습니다. 두 숙녀 모두 온갖 도덕적, 종교적 미덕을 갖추고 있습니다. 그들의 보수는 물론 그 재능에 걸맞은 수준입니다. 뷰트 크롤리 목사님께 더없이 감사한 경의를 전하며, 영광스럽게 인사 올립니다.\n\n친애하는 부인께,\n\n부인의 더없이 충실하고 순종하는 종복 바버라 핑커튼 올림.\n\n추신. 부인께서 준남작이자 하원 의원이신 핏 크롤리 경의 가정교사로 언급하신 샤프 양은 저의 제자였으며, 그녀에게 불리한 말은 저로서는 한마디도 할 게 없습니다. 비록 그 외양이 호감을 주지는 못하나, 우리가 자연의 조화를 어찌할 수는 없는 노릇이지요. 또한 그녀의 부모가 변변치 못했으나(그 아버지는 화가로 여러 차례 파산했고, 그 어머니는 제가 나중에 소름 끼치는 심정으로 알게 된 바로는 오페라극장의 무희였습니다), 그래도 그녀의 재능만은 상당하니, 제가 자선을 베풀어 그녀를 거두어들인 일을 후회할 수는 없습니다. 다만 제가 두려워하는 것은, 그 어미의 성향이 ㅡ제게는 그녀가 지난날 혁명의 참화 속에 어쩔 수 없이 망명한 프랑스 백작 부인이라 소개되었으나, 나중에 알고 보니 그녀는 더없이 천한 신분과 품행의 인물이었습니다ㅡ 제가 버려진 아이로 거두었던 그 가엾은 젊은 여자에게서 언젠가 유전으로 드러나지나 않을까 하는 것입니다. 그러나 지금까지 그녀의 성향은 (제가 믿기로) 올발랐으며, 저 저명하신 핏 크롤리 경의 우아하고 세련된 교제권 안에서 그것이 해를 입을 일은 결코 없으리라 확신합니다.\n\n레베카 샤프 양이 애밀리아 세들리 양에게.\n\n지난 여러 주 동안 나의 사랑하는 애밀리아에게 편지를 쓰지 못했어. 내가 따분한 홀이라 이름 붙인 이곳에서 벌어지는 일이며 오가는 말 가운데 전할 만한 소식이 뭐가 있었겠니. 또 순무 농사가 잘됐는지 못됐는지, 살진 돼지가 열세 스톤이나 나갔는지 열네 스톤이나 나갔는지, 짐승들이 사료용 무를 잘 먹고 토실토실해지는지 따위가 네게 무슨 상관이 있겠어? 지난번 편지를 쓴 이래로 하루하루가 그저 그 옆날과 다를 바 없었단다. 아침을 먹기 전에는 핏 경과 그의 잡초 뽑는 괭이를 따라 산책을 하고, 아침을 먹은 뒤에는 교실에서 공부랍시고 (그게 공부라면 말이지만) 하고, 교실 일이 끝나면 핏 경과 함께 변호사, 임대차, 탄광, 운하에 관한 글을 읽고 쓰는 거야(나는 그분의 비서가 되어 버렸거든). 저녁을 먹은 뒤에는 준남작의 주사위 놀이를 두고 크롤리 씨가 일장 연설을 늘어놓고. 그 두 가지 소일거리 내내 마님은 한결같이 평온한 얼굴로 지켜보고 계시지. 마님은 근래 들어 몸이 편찮으셔서 다소 흥미로운 분이 되셨는데, 그 덕에 젊은 의사라는 새 손님이 홀을 드나들게 되었단다. 자, 얘야, 젊은 여자란 결코 절망할 필요가 없는 법이야. 그 젊은 의사가 너의 어떤 벗에게, 만약 그녀가 글라우버 부인이 되기로 마음먹기만 한다면 기꺼이 그 진료소를 빛내 주어도 좋다는 뜻을 넌지시 비치지 뭐니! 나는 그 뻔뻔한 양반에게, 금박을 입힌 절구공이와 절구만으로도 장식이라면 차고 넘친다고 대꾸해 주었지. 내가 무슨 시골 의사의 아내가 되려고 태어나기라도 한 듯이 말이야! 글라우버 씨는 그 퇴짜에 단단히 풀이 죽어 집으로 돌아갔고, 열을 내리는 약을 들이켰으며, 이제는 말끔히 나았단다. 핏 경은 내 결단을 크게 칭찬하셨어. 그분은 당신의 작은 비서를 잃기가 아쉬우신 거겠지. 그리고 내 생각엔 그 늙은 양반이, 그의 천성으로 누군가를 좋아할 수 있는 만큼은 나를 좋아하시는 것 같아. 결혼이라니, 흥! 게다가 시골 약제사하고, 그것도 ㅡ 아니, 아니, 사람이란 옛정을 그리 쉽게 잊을 수 있는 게 아니지. 그 이야기는 더 하지 않으련다. 따분한 홀로 되돌아가자꾸나.\n\n얼마 전부터 이곳은 더 이상 따분한 홀이 아니란다. 얘야, 크롤리 양이 살진 말이며 살진 하인들이며 살진 스패니얼을 거느리고 도착하셨거든. 오 퍼센트 공채로 칠만 파운드를 가진 그 어마어마한 부자 크롤리 양 말이야. 두 형제가 그분을, 아니 그 재산을 떠받든다고 하는 편이 낫겠구나. 그 어른은 무척 졸도하실 것처럼 보이는데, 가엾기도 하시지. 두 형제가 그분 걱정에 안달하는 것도 무리는 아니야. 두 분이 그분의 방석을 매만지고 커피를 받쳐 올리려 다투는 꼴을 너도 봤어야 하는 건데! \"내가 시골에 올 때면\" 그분이 말씀하시지(워낙 익살이 넘치는 분이거든). \"내 아첨꾼 브리그스 양은 집에 두고 온단다. 여기서는 내 두 형제가 내 아첨꾼이지, 얘야. 어찌나 잘 어울리는 한 쌍인지!\"\n\n그분이 시골에 오시면 우리 홀은 활짝 문을 열고, 적어도 한 달 동안은 늙은 월폴 경이 되살아나기라도 한 게 아닌가 싶을 정도란다. 만찬회를 열고, 사두마차를 타고 나들이를 하지. 하인들은 가장 새것인 카나리아색 제복을 차려입고, 우리는 마치 날마다 그래 온 사람들처럼 클라레와 샴페인을 마신단다. 교실에는 밀랍 양초가 켜지고, 몸을 녹일 난롯불도 지펴지지. 크롤리 부인은 옷장에서 가장 화사한 완두콩빛 초록 드레스를 꺼내 입게 되고, 내 학생들은 두꺼운 신발과 꽉 끼는 낡은 격자무늬 외투를 벗어 던지고, 유행하는 준남작의 딸들답게 비단 양말과 모슬린 드레스를 입는단다. 어제 로즈가 가엾은 꼴로 들어왔어. 윌트셔 암퇘지가(그 애가 끔찍이도 아끼는 놈이지) 그 애를 들이받고는 더없이 사랑스러운 라일락빛 꽃무늬 비단 드레스 위를 마구 짓밟아 망쳐 놓았거든. 이런 일이 일주일 전에 일어났더라면 핏 경은 무시무시하게 욕을 퍼붓고 그 가엾은 것의 따귀를 올려붙인 다음 한 달 동안 빵과 물만 먹게 했을 거야. 그런데 그분은 고작 이렇게 말씀하셨을 뿐이야. \"이놈아, 네 고모 떠나고 나면 두고 보자.\" 그러고는 그 사고를 아주 사소한 일인 양 웃어넘기셨지. 크롤리 양이 떠나기 전에 그분의 노여움이 가라앉기를 바라야겠어. 정말이지 로즈 양을 위해서 그러기를 바란다. 돈이란 얼마나 멋진 화해자요 중재자인지!\n\n크롤리 양과 그 칠만 파운드가 빚어내는 또 하나의 놀라운 효과는 두 크롤리 형제의 처신에서 볼 수 있단다. 준남작과 목사 말이야. 우리 형제가 아니라ㅡ아무튼 일 년 내내 서로 미워하던 그 두 사람이 크리스마스만 되면 아주 살가워지지 뭐니. 그 가증스러운 경마꾼 목사가 교회에서 우리에게 어설픈 설교를 늘어놓곤 하고, 그러면 핏 경이 화답으로 코를 골곤 하던 일을 작년에 네게 써 보냈었지. 그런데 크롤리 양이 오시면 다투는 소리란 도무지 들리지 않는단다. 홀이 목사관을 찾고 그 반대도 마찬가지고, 목사와 준남작은 돼지며 밀렵꾼이며 고장 일에 관해 더없이 정답게 이야기를 나누고, 술기운에 다투는 법도 없는 듯해. 사실 크롤리 양은 두 분이 다투는 꼴을 못 견뎌 하시고, 자기 비위를 거스르면 재산을 슈롭셔 크롤리 가에 남기겠다고 으름장을 놓으시거든. 만약 그 슈롭셔 크롤리 가가 영리한 사람들이라면 그 재산을 죄다 차지할 수도 있을 텐데. 하지만 슈롭셔 크롤리도 그 햄프셔 사촌과 마찬가지로 성직자여서, 도덕에 관한 어떤 고지식한 생각들로 크롤리 양의 비위를 단단히 거슬러 놓았다는구나(그분은 다루기 힘든 형제들에게 화가 치밀어 그리로 도망쳐 갔던 거였지). 내가 듣기로 그 사람은 집 안에서 기도를 올리려 했다나 봐.\n\n크롤리 양이 오시면 우리 설교집은 덮여 버리고, 그분이 질색하는 핏 씨는 슬그머니 런던으로 가 버리는 게 편리하다고 여기지. 그 대신에 젊은 멋쟁이가, 아마 \"혈기 왕성한 한량\"이라고들 하는 것 같은데, 그 크롤리 대위가 나타난단다. 그가 어떤 사람인지 너도 궁금할 테지.\n\n자, 그는 무척 덩치 큰 젊은 멋쟁이야. 키는 육 피트에 목소리가 우렁차고, 욕을 입에 달고 살며, 하인들을 마구 부려 먹는단다. 그런데도 하인들은 다들 그를 떠받들지. 돈을 후하게 쓰는 사람이라, 하인들은 그를 위해서라면 무엇이든 하거든. 지난주에는 그 대위를 체포하러 런던에서 내려온 집행관과 그 수하가 공원 담장 근처를 어슬렁거리다 발각됐는데, 사냥터지기들이 그들을 거의 죽일 뻔했단다. 그들을 두들겨 패고 물에 처넣고는 밀렵꾼이라며 총으로 쏘아 버리려 했는데, 준남작이 끼어들어 말렸지.\n\n그 대위는 자기 아버지를 진심으로 경멸하는 게 내 눈에도 보여. 그를 늙다리, 늙은 속물, 늙은 촌뜨기, 그 밖에 무수히 듣기 좋은 이름으로 부르거든. 그는 숙녀들 사이에서 무시무시한 평판을 얻고 있어. 자기 사냥말들을 끌고 와서는 고장의 향사들과 어울려 지내고, 제멋대로 아무나 저녁 식사에 부르는데, 핏 경은 감히 안 된다는 말을 못 해. 크롤리 양의 비위를 거슬렀다가 그분이 졸도로 돌아가실 때 유산을 놓치게 될까 봐 두려운 거지. 그 대위가 내게 건넨 찬사 하나를 너에게 들려줄까? 들려줘야겠어. 어찌나 듣기 좋던지. 어느 저녁 우리가 정말로 춤판을 벌였는데, 허들스턴 퍼들스턴 경과 그 식구들, 자일스 와프숏 경과 그 딸들, 그리고 몇 사람이나 더 왔는지 모르겠어. 그런데 그가 이렇게 말하는 걸 들었지 뭐니. \"이런, 참 맵시 있는 망아지로구먼!\" 다름 아닌 너의 변변찮은 벗을 두고 한 말이었어. 그러고는 영광스럽게도 나와 컨트리댄스를 두 곡이나 추어 주었단다. 그는 함께 술 마시고 내기하고 말을 달리고 사냥이며 사격 이야기를 나누는 젊은 향사들과는 꽤 즐겁게 어울리지만, 시골 처녀들은 따분하다고 한단다. 사실 나도 그 말이 그리 틀렸다고는 보지 않아. 그 처녀들이 가엾은 나를 얼마나 깔보는 눈으로 내려다보는지 너도 봤어야 하는 건데! 그들이 춤출 때 나는 한쪽에 앉아 얌전히 피아노를 친단다. 그런데 며칠 전 밤에 그가 식당에서 다소 불콰해진 얼굴로 들어오다가 내가 그렇게 피아노 치는 모습을 보고는, 내가 그 방에서 가장 춤을 잘 추는 사람이라고 큰 소리로 떠들어 댔고, 머드베리에서 바이올린 악사들을 불러오겠다고 큰소리쳤단다.\n\n\"내가 가서 컨트리댄스 곡을 쳐 주마.\" 뷰트 크롤리 부인이 선뜻 나서며 말했어(그 부인은 거무튀튀한 얼굴에 터번을 쓴 자그마한 노부인인데, 허리가 좀 굽었고 두 눈은 무척 반짝거리지). 그러고는 그 대위와 너의 가엾은 작은 레베카가 함께 춤을 추고 나자, 글쎄, 그분이 정말로 영광스럽게도 내 스텝을 칭찬해 주지 뭐니! 이런 일은 일찍이 들어 본 적이 없어. 티프토프 백작의 사촌 누이이면서, 자기 언니가 시골에 와 있을 때가 아니면 크롤리 부인을 찾아보는 일조차 황송해 마지않는 그 콧대 높은 뷰트 크롤리 부인이 말이야. 가엾은 크롤리 부인! 이 흥겨운 자리 내내 그분은 거의 위층에서 알약이나 삼키고 계신단다.\n\n뷰트 부인은 별안간 내게 푹 빠지셨어. \"친애하는 샤프 양.\" 그분이 말씀하시지. \"학생들을 목사관으로 데려오는 게 어때요? 사촌들이 그 애들을 보면 무척 기뻐할 텐데.\" 나는 그분의 속셈을 안단다. 클레멘티 선생이 공짜로 우리에게 피아노를 가르쳐 준 게 아니었으니, 그분은 그 비용으로 제 자식들을 위한 교사를 얻으려는 거지. 그 속셈을, 마치 그분이 내게 직접 털어놓기라도 한 듯 훤히 꿰뚫어 보고 있어. 하지만 나는 가야겠어. 상냥하게 처신하기로 마음먹었거든. 세상에 벗 하나, 보호자 하나 없는 가엾은 가정교사의 의무가 아니겠니? 목사 부인은 내 학생들이 거둔 진보에 관해 수십 번이나 칭찬을 늘어놓으며, 분명 내 마음을 사로잡을 수 있으리라 여겼겠지ㅡ가엾고 순진한 시골 사람 같으니!ㅡ내가 마치 내 학생들을 손톱만큼이라도 마음에 두고 있기라도 한 듯이 말이야!\n\n가장 사랑하는 애밀리아, 너의 인도산 모슬린과 분홍빛 비단이 내게 무척 잘 어울린다더라. 이젠 꽤 낡았지만, 너도 알다시피 우리 같은 가난한 처녀들은 새 옷을 마련할 형편이 못 되잖니. 행복한, 행복한 너! 너는 그저 세인트제임스 거리로 마차를 몰고 가기만 하면 되고, 무엇이든 달라는 대로 주시는 다정한 어머니가 계시니 말이야. 잘 있어, 가장 사랑하는 친구야.\n\n너를 사랑하는 레베카가.\n\n추신. 로던 대위가 가엾은 나를 짝으로 골랐을 때, 블랙브룩 양들의(블랙브룩 제독의 딸들 말이야, 얘야) 얼굴을 네가 봤어야 하는 건데. 런던에서 마련한 드레스를 차려입은 그 잘난 젊은 숙녀들 말이야!\n\n뷰트 크롤리 부인이(영리한 우리 레베카가 일찌감치 그 속셈을 간파한 그 부인 말이다) 샤프 양에게서 방문하겠다는 약속을 받아 내자, 그녀는 막강한 크롤리 양을 부추겨 핏 경에게 필요한 청을 넣게 했다. 그러자 본인부터 흥겨운 것을 좋아하고 주위 사람 모두가 흥겹고 행복한 꼴을 보기 좋아하던 그 마음씨 좋은 노부인은 무척 흡족해하며, 기꺼이 두 형제 사이의 화해와 친교를 주선하려 들었다. 그리하여 앞으로 양가의 젊은이들이 서로 자주 왕래하기로 합의가 이루어졌고, 물론 그 우정은 그 흥겨운 늙은 중재자가 평화를 지키며 그곳에 머무는 동안만 이어졌다.\n\n\"왜 그 망나니 로던 크롤리를 저녁 식사에 불렀소?\" 공원을 가로질러 집으로 걸어가며 목사가 아내에게 말했다. \"난 그자가 싫소. 그자는 우리 시골 사람들을 새까만 깜둥이쯤으로 깔보는 작자란 말이오. 한 병에 십 실링이나 하는 내 노란 봉인 와인을 얻어 마시지 않고는 결코 만족하는 법이 없으니, 빌어먹을! 게다가 그자는 그야말로 고약한 인간이오ㅡ노름꾼에다 술꾼에다 모든 면에서 방탕한 작자라고. 결투에서 사람을 쏘아 죽였고, 빚이 목까지 차 있는 데다, 나와 내 식구들에게 돌아왔어야 할 크롤리 양 재산의 알짜를 가로채 갔단 말이오. 왁시 말로는 누님이 그자에게ㅡ\" 여기서 목사는 욕설에 가까운 무언가를 내뱉으며 달을 향해 주먹을 흔들고는, 침울한 어조로 덧붙였다. \"ㅡ유언장에 오만 파운드를 정해 놓았다더군. 그러면 나눠 가질 게 삼만 파운드밖에 안 남는단 말이오.\"\n\n\"누님은 가실 때가 가까운 것 같아요.\" 목사 부인이 말했다. \"저녁상에서 일어날 때 얼굴이 몹시 붉으시던걸요. 코르셋 끈을 풀어 드려야 했다니까요.\"\n\n\"누님은 샴페인을 일곱 잔이나 드셨소.\" 목사가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"게다가 그것도 우리 형이 우리한테 먹이는 그 형편없는 샴페인이지ㅡ허나 당신네 여자들은 도무지 뭐가 뭔지를 모른다니까.\"\n\n\"우린 아무것도 모르지요.\" 뷰트 크롤리 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"누님은 저녁 식사 뒤에 체리브랜디를 드셨고,\" 목사가 말을 이었다. \"커피에는 큐라소까지 타서 드셨소. 나라면 오 파운드를 준다 해도 그건 한 잔도 입에 못 댈 거요. 속이 쓰려서 죽을 지경이거든. 누님 몸이 그걸 견뎌 낼 리가 없소, 크롤리 부인ㅡ가실 수밖에 없다고ㅡ피와 살로 된 몸이 그걸 어찌 버티겠소! 내 오 대 이로 걸겠는데, 마틸다는 일 년 안에 쓰러질 거요.\"\n\n이런 엄숙한 추측에 빠져 자신의 빚이며, 대학에 다니는 아들 짐이며, 울위치에 있는 프랭크며, 가엾게도 미색이라곤 없어 고모가 남길 유산이 아니고서는 한 푼도 가질 게 없는 네 딸 생각에 잠긴 채, 목사와 그 아내는 한동안 말없이 걸었다.\n\n\"핏이 아무리 그래도 성직록의 상속권을 팔아 치울 만큼 악독한 놈은 아닐 테지. 게다가 그 감리교쟁이 겁쟁이 맏아들 놈은 의회만 바라보고 있으니.\" 잠시 뒤 크롤리 씨가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"핏 크롤리 경은 무슨 짓이든 할 사람이에요.\" 목사 부인이 말했다. \"크롤리 양을 시켜서 그 자리를 제임스에게 약속하게 만들어야 해요.\"\n\n\"핏은 무슨 약속이든 다 할 거요.\" 아우가 대꾸했다. \"아버지가 돌아가셨을 때 내 대학 빚을 갚아 주겠다고 약속했지. 목사관에 새 별채를 지어 주겠다고 약속했고, 지브의 밭과 육 에이커 목초지를 내게 주겠다고 약속했어ㅡ그런데 그 약속을 얼마나 잘도 지켰는지! 그런데 바로 그 작자의 아들, 그 망나니에 노름꾼에 사기꾼에 살인자인 로던 크롤리에게 마틸다가 재산의 알짜를 남긴다 이거지. 이건 그리스도교인답지 못한 짓이오. 정말이지 그렇소. 그 파렴치한 개자식은 위선만 빼고 온갖 악덕을 다 갖췄어. 그 위선이라는 건 제 형 몫이고.\"\n\n\"쉿, 여보! 우린 지금 핏 경 영지 안에 있어요.\" 아내가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"내 말은 그자가 온갖 악덕을 다 갖췄다는 거요, 크롤리 부인. 부인, 날 윽박지르지 마시오. 그자가 마커 대위를 쏘지 않았소? 코코아트리에서 젊은 도브데일 경의 돈을 털지 않았소? 빌 솜스와 체셔 트럼프의 시합을 농간 부려서 내가 사십 파운드를 날리게 하지 않았느냐 말이오? 당신도 그자가 그랬다는 걸 알잖소. 그리고 여자들로 말할 것 같으면, 아니, 당신은 내 치안판사실에서 바로 내 앞에서 그 얘길 듣지 않았소.\"\n\n\"제발 부탁이에요, 크롤리 씨.\" 부인이 말했다. \"그 자질구레한 이야기는 그만하세요.\"\n\n\"그런 악당을 당신 집에 부르다니!\" 격분한 목사가 말을 이었다. \"당신, 어린 자식들을 둔 어미요ㅡ잉글랜드 국교회 성직자의 아내가 말이오. 맙소사!\"\n\n\"뷰트 크롤리, 당신은 바보예요.\" 목사 부인이 경멸하듯 말했다.\n\n\"흥, 부인, 바보든 아니든ㅡ게다가 마사, 나는 내가 당신만큼 영리하다고 한 적도 없고, 단 한 번도 그런 적 없소. 하지만 난 로던 크롤리하고는 절대 안 만날 거요, 그건 분명하오. 허들스턴 댁에나 다녀와야겠소, 정말 그래야겠어. 그 댁 검은 그레이하운드를 보러 가서 말이오, 크롤리 부인. 그리고 오십 파운드를 걸고 우리 랜슬롯을 그놈하고 붙여 봐야겠소. 맙소사, 정말 그러겠소. 잉글랜드의 어떤 개를 상대로든 말이오. 하지만 그 짐승 같은 로던 크롤리하고는 절대 안 만날 거요.\"\n\n\"크롤리 씨, 당신은 늘 그렇듯 또 취하셨군요.\" 아내가 대꾸했다. 그리고 이튿날 아침 목사가 잠에서 깨어 묽은 맥주를 찾자, 부인은 토요일에 허들스턴 퍼들스턴 경을 찾아가기로 한 그의 약속을 일깨워 주었다. 그날 밤 진탕 마시게 되리라는 것을 그도 알았으므로, 일요일 아침 예배 시간에 맞춰 다시 말을 달려 돌아오기로 합의가 이루어졌다. 이로써 알 수 있듯, 크롤리의 교구민들은 그들의 향사로 보나 그들의 목사로 보나 그저 행복하기 그지없었던 셈이다.\n\n크롤리 양이 홀에 자리 잡은 지 얼마 되지 않아, 레베카의 매력은 우리가 묘사해 온 시골의 순박한 이들의 마음을 사로잡았듯, 그 마음씨 좋은 런던 난봉꾼의 마음마저 사로잡아 버렸다. 어느 날 평소처럼 마차를 타고 나서면서, 크롤리 양은 \"그 작은 가정교사\"가 머드베리까지 자기와 동행하도록 분부를 내리는 게 좋겠다고 여겼다. 그들이 돌아오기도 전에 레베카는 이미 그분을 완전히 정복해 버렸다. 그분을 네 번이나 웃게 만들었고, 그 짧은 나들이 내내 즐겁게 해 드렸던 것이다.\n\n\"샤프 양을 식탁에서 식사 못 하게 한다고요!\" 그분은, 격식 차린 만찬을 마련하고 인근의 준남작들을 죄다 불러 놓은 핏 경에게 말했다. \"이런 딱한 양반 같으니, 내가 퍼들스턴 부인하고 육아 이야기나 하고, 저 멍청한 늙은 자일스 와프숏 경하고 치안판사 일이나 논하고 있어야 한다고 생각하는 거예요? 나는 샤프 양이 꼭 나와야겠어요. 자리가 없으면 크롤리 부인더러 위층에 그냥 있으라고 해요. 하지만 우리 작은 샤프 양은! 아니, 이 고장에서 말 상대가 될 만한 사람은 그 애뿐이라고요!\"\n\n물론 이렇게 단호한 분부가 떨어진 뒤로, 가정교사 샤프 양은 아래층에서 그 쟁쟁한 손님들과 함께 식사하라는 명을 받게 되었다. 그리고 허들스턴 경이 한껏 위세를 부리며 크롤리 양을 식탁으로 모시고 들어와 그 곁자리에 막 앉으려 하자, 노부인이 날카로운 목소리로 외쳤다. \"베키 샤프! 샤프 양! 이리 와서 내 옆에 앉아 날 즐겁게 해 주렴. 허들스턴 경은 와프숏 부인 옆에 앉으시고.\"\n\n연회가 끝나고 마차들이 줄지어 떠나가고 나면, 도무지 만족을 모르는 크롤리 양은 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"내 화장실로 오렴, 베키. 우리 손님들 흉이나 보자꾸나.\" 그리하여 이 한 쌍의 벗은 둘이서 그 일을 더없이 완벽하게 해냈다. 늙은 허들스턴 경은 저녁 식사 내내 쌕쌕거리며 숨을 몰아쉬었고, 자일스 와프숏 경은 유난히 소리를 내며 수프를 들이켰으며, 그 부인은 왼쪽 눈을 찡긋거리는 버릇이 있었다. 베키는 이 모든 것을 기막히게 흉내 냈다. 그뿐 아니라 그날 밤 오간 대화의 시시콜콜한 내용, 정치, 전쟁, 분기 순회재판, 그 유명한 H.H. 사냥 이야기며, 시골 신사들이 입에 올리는 그 묵직하고 따분한 화제들까지 죄다 흉내 냈다. 와프숏 양들의 차림새와 퍼들스턴 부인의 그 유명한 노란 모자로 말하자면, 샤프 양은 그것들을 갈가리 찢어발겨 청중에게 무한한 즐거움을 안겨 주었다.\n\n\"얘야, 너는 그야말로 보물이로구나.\" 크롤리 양은 말하곤 했다. \"네가 런던에 있는 내게 와 줄 수 있다면 좋으련만. 하지만 나는 가엾은 브리그스를 놀려 먹듯 너를 놀림감으로 삼을 수는 없겠지ㅡ아니, 아니, 너 같은 작고 능청스러운 것을. 너는 너무 영리하거든ㅡ안 그래, 퍼킨?\"\n\n(크롤리 양의 정수리에 얼마 남지 않은 머리카락을 매만지고 있던) 퍼킨 부인은 고개를 홱 쳐들며, 더없이 사람 잡을 듯한 비꼬는 투로 말했다. \"제 생각에도 아가씨는 무척 영리하지요.\" 사실 퍼킨 부인에게는, 정직한 여자라면 누구나 지니는 주요 원리 가운데 하나인 그 타고난 질투심이 있었던 것이다.\n\n허들스턴 퍼들스턴 경을 퇴짜 놓은 뒤로, 크롤리 양은 날마다 로던 크롤리가 자기를 식탁으로 모시고 들어가고, 베키가 방석을 들고 그 뒤를 따르도록 분부했다. 아니면 베키의 팔에 의지하고 로던에게 방석을 들게 했다. \"우린 함께 앉아야 해.\" 그분은 말했다. \"이 고장에서 우리 셋만이 유일한 그리스도교인이거든, 얘야.\" 이 말대로라면 햄프셔 지방의 신앙심이란 그야말로 바닥까지 떨어져 있었다고 인정할 수밖에 없겠다.\n\n이렇듯 신앙심이 깊었을 뿐 아니라, 앞서 말했듯 크롤리 양은 사상에서 극단적 자유주의자였으며, 늘 기회만 닿으면 그 사상을 더없이 솔직하게 표명하곤 했다.\n\n\"가문이 다 무어냐, 얘야!\" 그분은 레베카에게 말하곤 했다. \"우리 오라버니 핏을 보렴. 헨리 2세 이래로 이 고장에 살아온 허들스턴 가를 보렴. 목사관에 있는 가엾은 뷰트를 보렴ㅡ그중 누가 지성에서나 교양에서 너와 견줄 만하더냐? 너와 견주기는커녕, 내 말동무 가엾은 브리그스나 내 집사 보울스에도 못 미친단다. 얘야, 너는 작은 본보기 같은 아이야ㅡ정말이지 작은 보석이지ㅡ너는 이 고장 사람 절반보다 더 영리한 머리를 가졌어ㅡ공덕에 마땅한 보답이 따른다면 너는 공작부인이 되어야 마땅하지ㅡ아니, 공작부인이라는 것 자체가 아예 없어야 옳아ㅡ하지만 너는 누구의 아래에도 있어선 안 돼. 그리고 얘야, 나는 너를 모든 면에서 나와 동등한 사람으로 여긴단다. 그러니ㅡ얘야, 난로에 석탄 좀 더 넣어 주겠니? 그리고 이 내 드레스 좀 뜯어서 고쳐 주련? 너는 그 일을 어찌나 잘하는지.\" 이렇듯 이 늙은 박애주의자는 자기가 동등하다고 한 그 아이에게 심부름을 시키고, 바느질을 맡기며, 밤마다 프랑스 소설을 읽어 주어 자기를 잠들게 했던 것이다.\n\n이 무렵, 어떤 나이 든 독자들은 기억할지도 모르겠지만, 상류 사회는 두 사건으로 적잖이 들썩이고 있었다. 신문들이 흔히 말하듯, 법조계 양반들에게 일거리를 안겨 줄 만한 사건이었다. 샤프턴 소위가 브루인 백작의 딸이자 상속녀인 바버라 피츠어스 영애를 데리고 달아났던 것이다. 그리고 가엾은 비어 베인은, 마흔이 되도록 더없이 존경할 만한 인품을 유지하고 자식도 여럿 키운 신사였건만, 별안간 터무니없이도 예순다섯 살 먹은 여배우 루즈몽 부인을 위해 제 집을 버렸던 것이다.\n\n\"그것이야말로 그리운 넬슨 경의 인품에서 가장 아름다운 대목이지.\" 크롤리 양이 말했다. \"그분은 한 여자를 위해 파멸의 길로 가셨거든. 그렇게 할 수 있는 남자에게는 분명 무언가 좋은 점이 있는 거야. 나는 무모한 결혼이라면 다 좋아한단다.ㅡ내가 가장 좋아하는 건, 플라워데일 경이 그랬듯 귀족이 방앗간 집 딸과 결혼하는 거야ㅡ그러면 온 여자들이 어찌나 분개하는지ㅡ어떤 대단한 양반이 너를 데리고 달아나 주면 좋으련만, 얘야. 너는 분명 그러기에 충분히 예쁘니까.\"\n\n\"우편마차 마부가 둘이나!ㅡ아, 정말 멋질 거예요!\" 레베카는 그렇다고 맞장구쳤다.\n\n\"그다음으로 내가 좋아하는 건, 가난한 사내가 부잣집 처녀를 데리고 달아나는 거야. 나는 로던이 누군가를 데리고 달아나면 좋겠다고 마음먹고 있단다.\"\n\n\"부잣집 처녀요, 아니면 가난한 집 처녀요?\"\n\n\"아니, 이런 멍청한 것! 로던은 내가 주는 것 말고는 단돈 한 푼도 없는 처지야. 그 애는 빚에 짓눌려 있단다ㅡ재산을 다시 일으켜 세상에서 성공해야 한다고.\"\n\n\"그분은 무척 영리한가요?\" 레베카가 물었다.\n\n\"영리하냐고, 얘야?ㅡ자기 말이며 연대며 사냥이며 노름 말고는 머릿속에 든 생각이 하나도 없는 애야. 하지만 그 앤 성공해야 해ㅡ어찌나 사랑스럽도록 못된 녀석인지. 그 애가 사람을 한 명 때렸고, 분기탱천한 어떤 아버지를 쏘아 모자만 꿰뚫어 놓은 거 모르니? 그 앤 자기 연대에서 떠받들어지고 있단다. 워티어스며 코코아트리에 드나드는 젊은이들도 다 그 애라면 사족을 못 쓰지.\"\n\n레베카 샤프 양이 사랑하는 벗에게 퀸스 크롤리에서 열린 그 작은 무도회와, 크롤리 대위가 처음으로 자기를 각별히 대해 준 정황을 적어 보냈을 때, 이상하게도 그녀는 그 일을 온전히 정확하게 적지는 않았다. 그 대위가 그녀를 각별히 대한 건 그전에도 여러 번이었다. 그 대위는 산책길에서 십여 차례 그녀와 마주쳤다. 그 대위는 복도며 통로에서 쉰 번도 넘게 그녀와 맞닥뜨렸다. 그 대위는 샤프 양이 노래할 때면(마님은 이제 위층에서 앓고 있었고 아무도 그녀를 거들떠보지 않았다) 어느 저녁이고 스무 번씩 그녀의 피아노에 몸을 기울였다. 그 대위는 그녀에게 쪽지도 써 보냈다(덩치만 크고 어수룩한 기병이 궁리해 짜내고 철자를 더듬어 쓴, 그나마 최선의 쪽지였다. 하지만 여자에게는 우둔함도 다른 어떤 자질 못지않게 잘 통하는 법이다). 그러나 그가 첫 번째 쪽지를 그녀가 부르던 노래의 악보 갈피에 끼워 넣자, 그 작은 가정교사는 일어서서 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 쳐다보며 그 세모진 편지를 곱게 집어 들더니, 그것을 마치 삼각모라도 되는 양 흔들어 보였다. 그러고는 적을 향해 나아가듯 그것을 난롯불 속에 톡 던져 넣고, 그에게 더없이 깊숙이 무릎을 굽혀 인사한 뒤, 제자리로 돌아가 그 어느 때보다 흥겹게 다시 노래하기 시작했다.\n\n\"무슨 일이냐?\" 음악이 멎는 바람에 저녁 식사 뒤의 선잠에서 깨어난 크롤리 양이 물었다.\n\n\"틀린 음이 나서요.\" 샤프 양이 웃으며 말했다. 그러는 동안 로던 크롤리는 분과 굴욕으로 부글부글 끓고 있었다.\n\n크롤리 양이 새 가정교사를 그토록 노골적으로 편애하는 것을 보면서도, 뷰트 크롤리 부인은 질투하지 않고 그 젊은 숙녀를 목사관에 맞아들였으니 어찌나 마음씨가 고운지! 그녀뿐 아니라, 그 노처녀의 오 퍼센트 공채를 두고 자기 남편의 경쟁자인 로던 크롤리까지 맞아들였으니 말이다! 크롤리 부인과 그 조카는 서로의 벗을 무척 좋아하게 되었다. 그는 사냥을 그만두었고, 퍼들스턴 댁의 연회도 마다했으며, 머드베리 주둔지의 장교 식당에서 식사하는 것도 싫다 했다. 그의 가장 큰 즐거움은 크롤리 목사관까지 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어가는 것이었다. 그곳에는 크롤리 양도 왔으니, 그 애들 어머니가 앓고 있으니 아이들이 샤프 양과 함께 못 갈 까닭이 있겠는가? 그리하여 아이들은(귀여운 것들!) 샤프 양과 함께 왔고, 저녁이면 일행 중 몇몇이 함께 걸어 돌아오곤 했다. 크롤리 양은 아니었다. 그분은 마차를 더 좋아했으니까. 그러나 목사관 들판을 가로질러, 자그마한 공원 쪽문을 지나, 어두운 농원을 지나, 얼룩진 가로수 길을 올라 퀸스 크롤리로 가는 그 길은, 달빛 아래에서 그 대위와 레베카 양처럼 풍경을 사랑하는 두 사람에게는 더없이 매혹적이었다.\n\n\"아, 저 별들, 저 별들!\" 레베카 양은 그 반짝이는 초록 눈을 별들을 향해 치켜올리며 말하곤 했다. \"저것들을 바라보고 있노라면 나 자신이 거의 영혼이 된 것만 같아요.\"\n\n\"오ㅡ아ㅡ저런ㅡ그렇소, 나도 꼭 그렇소, 샤프 양.\" 또 한 사람의 열광자가 대꾸했다. \"내 시가 연기가 거슬리지는 않겠지요, 샤프 양?\" 샤프 양은 세상 그 무엇보다도 야외에서 맡는 시가 냄새를 좋아했다. 그뿐 아니라 그녀는 더없이 어여쁜 자태로 시가를 한 모금 맛보기까지 하더니, 살짝 연기를 내뿜고는 작게 비명을 지르고 작게 키득거리고 나서, 그 진귀한 물건을 대위에게 돌려주었다. 대위는 콧수염을 배배 꼬며 그것을 단번에 피워 올려 어두운 농원에서 시뻘겋게 타오르게 하고는 이렇게 내뱉었다. \"저런ㅡ어ㅡ맙소사ㅡ어ㅡ이건 내 평생 피워 본 중에 가장 좋은 시가요, 어.\" 그의 지성과 화술은 둘 다, 둔중한 젊은 기병에게 어울릴 만큼 똑같이 빛나는 것이었던 셈이다.\n\n파이프 담배를 피우며 맥주를 들이켜고는 잡을 \"양\" 한 마리에 관해 존 호럭스와 이야기를 나누던 늙은 핏 경이, 서재 창문으로 그렇게 시시덕거리는 두 사람을 발견하고는, 만약 크롤리 양만 아니라면 로던을 잡아다 망나니 같은 놈을 내쫓듯 문밖으로 내던져 버리겠다며 무시무시하게 욕을 퍼부었다.\n\n\"그자가 못된 놈인 건 분명합지요.\" 호럭스 씨가 말했다. \"그 하인 플레더스란 작자는 한술 더 떠서, 저녁이며 맥주를 두고 가정부 방에서 어찌나 한바탕 소란을 피우는지, 어느 귀족 나리도 그렇게는 안 하실 겁니다요ㅡ허나 제 생각엔 샤프 양이 그자한테는 만만찮은 상대일 듯합니다요, 핏 경.\" 그는 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 덧붙였다.\n\n그리고 사실 그녀는 그러했다ㅡ아버지에게도, 아들에게도 만만찮은 상대였던 것이다.\n\n이제 우리는 아르카디아와 그곳에서 시골의 미덕을 실천하는 그 사랑스러운 사람들에게 작별을 고하고, 런던으로 되돌아가 애밀리아 양이 어찌 되었는지를 살펴보아야겠다.\n\n\"우리는 그 애 따위 조금도 신경 쓰지 않아요.\" 어느 이름 모를 여인이 예쁘장한 필체로, 분홍빛 봉인을 찍은 편지에 이렇게 적어 보낸다. \"그 애는 김빠지고 밍밍해요.\" 그러고는 이런 투의 친절한 평을 몇 마디 더 덧붙이는데, 그 말들이 사실은 거기 거론된 그 젊은 숙녀에게 엄청난 찬사가 된다는 점이 아니었더라면 나는 그것들을 도무지 옮겨 적지 않았을 것이다.\n\n사랑하는 독자께서는, 사교계 경험 가운데 마음씨 고운 여자 친구들에게서 이런 비슷한 평을 들어 보신 적이 한 번도 없으신가? 그들은 늘 의아해한다. 도대체 스미스 양의 무엇이 그토록 매혹적이라는 건지, 혹은 도대체 무엇이 존스 소령으로 하여금 저 어리석고 하찮고 실실 웃기만 하는 톰슨 양에게 청혼하게 만들었다는 건지 말이다. 그 여자라곤 밀랍 인형 같은 얼굴 말고는 내세울 게 하나도 없는데! 분홍빛 두 볼과 파란 두 눈에 대체 뭐가 있다는 거예요? 이 다정한 도덕가들은 이렇게 묻고는, 천재적 재능이라든가, 정신의 교양이라든가, 망널 문답서의 통달이라든가, 식물학과 지질학에 관한 숙녀다운 지식이라든가, 시를 짓는 솜씨라든가, 헤르츠 풍으로 소나타를 좔좔 쳐 내는 능력 따위가, 몇 해만 지나면 어쩔 수 없이 빛바래고 마는 저 덧없는 매력보다 여자에게는 훨씬 더 값진 자질이라고 슬며시 지혜로운 척 일러 준다. 여자들이 아름다움의 무가치함과 그 수명에 대해 이러쿵저러쿵 따지는 것을 듣고 있노라면 참으로 교화가 되는 일이다.\n* 망널 문답서: 19세기 영국에서 널리 쓰인 여학생용 일반상식 학습서.\n\n그러나 미덕이 훨씬 더 훌륭한 것이고, 미모라는 불운에 시달리는 저 가엾은 피조물들에게는 그들을 기다리고 있는 운명을 끊임없이 일깨워 주어야 마땅하며, 또한 십중팔구 숙녀들이 흠모하는 저 영웅적 여성상이, 남자들이 떠받들고 싶어 하는 친절하고 싱그럽고 미소 짓고 꾸밈없고 다정한 자그마한 가정의 여신보다 더 영광스럽고 아름다운 대상이라 하더라도, 후자에 속하는 그 열등한 부류의 여자들에게는 이런 위안이 남아 있는 법이다. 즉, 남자들은 결국 그런 여자들을 흠모한다는 것, 그리고 우리의 그 다정한 친구들이 아무리 경고하고 항변해도 우리는 한사코 그 절망적인 오류와 어리석음을 고집할 것이며, 이야기가 끝나는 그날까지 그러리라는 것이다. 사실 나로 말하자면, 내가 더없이 존경하는 사람들에게서 브라운 양은 하찮은 계집애에 불과하고, 화이트 부인은 자그마한 흐트러진 얼굴 말고는 아무것도 없으며, 블랙 부인은 자기 입으로 할 말 한마디 없는 사람이라는 말을 거듭 들었음에도, 나는 블랙 부인과 더없이 즐거운 대화를 나누어 왔음을 안다(물론 친애하는 부인, 그 대화들은 결코 발설치 않을 것입니다). 또한 모든 남자가 화이트 부인의 의자 둘레에 떼 지어 모여 있는 것을, 모든 젊은이가 브라운 양과 춤추려고 다투는 것을 나는 본다. 그래서 나는 같은 여자들에게 멸시받는 것이야말로 한 여자에게는 대단한 찬사라고 여기고 싶어지는 것이다.\n\n애밀리아의 교제권에 있는 젊은 숙녀들이야말로 그녀를 위해 이 일을 더없이 흡족하게 해 주었다. 이를테면 조지의 누이들인 오스본 자매와 도빈 자매가 그토록 의견 일치를 본 점이라곤, 그녀의 보잘것없는 장점에 대한 평가와, 자기네 오라비들이 도대체 그녀에게서 무슨 매력을 찾아낼 수 있단 말인가 하는 의아함 말고는 거의 없을 정도였다. \"우리는 그 애한테 친절하게 대해 줘요.\" 오스본 자매는 이렇게 말했는데, 검은 눈썹의 잘난 두 젊은 숙녀로 최고의 가정교사와 선생들과 모자 장수의 보살핌을 받아 온 처지였다. 그리고 그들은 그녀를 어찌나 극진히 친절하고 생색내며 대해 주는지, 또 어찌나 못 견디게 후원해 주는 척하는지, 그 가엾은 작은 사람은 사실 그들 앞에만 서면 완전히 입이 굳어 버려서, 겉으로 보기에는 영락없이 그들이 여기는 만큼 멍청해 보였다. 그녀는 도리상 마땅한 일로, 또 장차 남편이 될 사람의 누이들이라는 이유로 그들을 좋아하려고 애를 썼다. 그녀는 그들과 함께 \"긴 오전\"을 보냈는데, 그것은 더없이 음울하고 엄숙한 오전 시간이었다. 그녀는 그들과, 그리고 그들의 가정교사인 저 깡마른 노처녀 위트 양과 함께 그 커다란 가족용 마차를 타고 점잖게 드라이브를 나갔다. 그들은 호의랍시고 그녀를 고전 음악회며 오라토리오며, 또 자선 학교 아이들을 구경하는 세인트폴 대성당까지 데려갔는데, 그곳에서 그녀는 친구들이 어찌나 무서웠던지 아이들이 부르는 찬송가에 감동하는 것조차 거의 감히 하지 못할 지경이었다. 그들의 집은 안락했고, 아버지의 식탁은 풍성하고 훌륭했으며, 그들의 교제권은 엄숙하고 점잖았고, 그들의 자존심은 엄청났으며, 고아원 예배당에서도 가장 좋은 자리를 차지하고 있었다. 그들의 온갖 습관은 거드름 가득하고 질서 정연했으며, 그들의 온갖 오락은 견딜 수 없이 따분하고 점잔을 뺐다. 그녀가 방문할 때마다(아, 그것이 끝났을 때 그녀가 얼마나 기뻐했던지!) 오스본 양과 마리아 오스본 양과 노처녀 가정교사 위트 양은 갈수록 더 의아해하며 서로에게 물었다. \"조지는 도대체 저 애한테서 무얼 보는 걸까?\"\n\n어째서 이렇단 말인가? 트집 잡기 좋아하는 어느 독자가 이렇게 외친다. 학교에서는 그토록 많은 친구를 거느렸고 그토록 사랑받던 애밀리아가, 어째서 세상에 나오자마자 안목 높은 같은 여자들에게 퇴짜를 맞는단 말인가? 친애하는 선생, 핑커튼 여사의 학원에는 늙은 무용 선생 말고는 남자라곤 없었다오. 설마 여자아이들이 그 사람을 두고 다투기를 바라셨던 건 아니겠지요? 잘생긴 오라비 조지가 아침 식사가 끝나기 무섭게 곧장 빠져나가 일주일에 대여섯 번씩 밖에서 저녁을 먹는데, 버림받은 누이들이 다소 분이 났던 것도 무리는 아니다. 지난 두 사교철 동안 마리아 양에게 구애해 오던 젊은 불록(롬바드 거리의 헐커 불록 상회 은행가)이 정작 애밀리아에게 코티용을 추자고 청했을 때, 앞서의 그 젊은 숙녀가 기뻐하리라 기대할 수 있었겠는가? 그런데도 그녀는, 꾸밈없고 너그러운 사람인 양 기쁘다고 말했다. \"오빠가 우리 애밀리아를 좋아해 주시니 정말 기뻐요.\" 그녀는 춤이 끝난 뒤 불록 씨에게 자못 열을 올리며 말했다. \"그 애는 우리 오빠 조지와 약혼한 사이예요. 별것 없는 애지만 성품 하나는 더없이 착하고 꾸밈이 없답니다. 우리 집에서는 다들 그 애를 무척 아껴요.\" 사랑스러운 아가씨! 그 열렬한 \"정말\" 속에 담긴 애정의 깊이를 누가 가늠할 수 있으랴?\n\n위트 양과 이 다정한 두 젊은 여자는 조지 오스본의 마음에, 그가 치르고 있는 희생이 얼마나 엄청난 것인지, 또 애밀리아 같은 여자에게 자신을 내던지는 그의 낭만적인 도량이 얼마나 대단한 것인지를 어찌나 열심히 또 빈번히 새겨 넣었던지, 나는 그가 정말로 자신을 영국군 안에서 가장 자격 있는 인물 가운데 하나로 여겼고, 또 사랑받는 일에 상당히 느긋한 체념의 자세로 자신을 내맡겼던 것이 아닐까 싶다.\n\n어찌 된 일인지, 앞서 말한 대로 그는 아침마다 집을 나섰고 일주일에 엿새를 밖에서 저녁을 먹었지만, 그 정신 나간 청년이 세들리 양의 치맛자락에 매달려 있으리라 누이들이 믿고 있을 때에도, 또 세상이 그가 그녀의 발치에 엎드려 있으리라 여기고 있을 때에도, 그는 늘 애밀리아와 함께 있는 것은 아니었다. 한 번이 아니라 여러 차례, 도빈 대위가 친구를 찾아왔을 때, 오스본 양은(대위에게 무척 살갑게 굴며 그의 군대 이야기를 듣고 싶어 했고 그의 사랑하는 어머니의 안부를 알고 싶어 했다) 웃으면서 광장 맞은편을 가리키며 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"어머, 조지를 찾으시려면 세들리 댁으로 가 보셔야 해요. 우리는 아침부터 밤까지 그 애 얼굴 한 번 못 본답니다.\" 이런 말을 들으면 대위는 다소 어색하고 억지스러운 태도로 웃어넘기고는, 노련한 세상 물정 밝은 사내답게 오페라라든가, 카를턴 하우스에서 열린 황태자의 지난번 무도회라든가, 날씨라든가 하는, 사교계의 축복인 그런 일반적인 화제로 이야기를 돌리는 것이었다.\n\n\"댁의 그 귀여운 분은 어쩌면 저렇게 순진할까.\" 그러면 마리아 양은 대위가 떠난 뒤 제인 양에게 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"근무 중인 가엾은 조지 이야기가 나오니까 얼굴이 발그레해지는 거 봤니?\"\n\n\"프레더릭 불록한테 저 사람 겸손함이 조금이라도 있었으면 좋으련만, 마리아.\" 언니가 머리를 한 번 까딱하며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"겸손이라니! 어색한 거겠지, 제인 언니. 난 프레더릭이 퍼킨스 부인 댁에서 도빈 대위가 언니 모슬린 드레스에 그랬듯이 내 드레스를 밟아 구멍 내는 건 싫어.\"\n\n\"내 드레스에, 호호! 그 사람이 어떻게 그래? 그 사람 애밀리아랑 춤추고 있지 않았어?\"\n\n사실인즉, 도빈 대위가 그토록 얼굴을 붉히고 그토록 어색해 보였던 것은, 굳이 젊은 숙녀들에게 알릴 필요는 없다고 여긴 어떤 사정을 떠올렸기 때문이었다. 즉, 그는 물론 조지를 본다는 핑계로 이미 세들리 씨 댁에 들렀었는데, 조지는 거기 없었고, 다만 응접실 창가에 다소 슬프고 애틋한 얼굴로 앉아 있는 가엾은 작은 애밀리아만 있었다는 것이다. 그녀는 몇 마디 시답잖고 어색한 이야기를 나눈 끝에, 연대가 곧 해외로 파병될 것이라는 소문이 사실이냐고, 또 그날 오스본 씨를 보셨느냐고 용기를 내어 물었던 것이다.\n\n연대는 아직 해외로 파병 명령을 받지 않았고, 도빈 대위는 조지를 보지 못했다. \"아마 누이들과 함께 있을 겁니다.\" 대위가 말했다. \"그 도망자를 데리러 가 볼까요?\" 그러자 그녀는 친절하고 고마운 마음으로 그에게 손을 내밀었다. 그리고 그는 광장을 가로질러 갔다. 그녀는 기다리고 또 기다렸으나, 조지는 끝내 오지 않았다.\n\n가엾은 작고 여린 마음이여! 그렇게 그것은 끊임없이 바라고 두근거리고 갈망하고 믿는다. 보다시피 묘사할 만한 인생이랄 것도 못 된다. 거기에는 사람들이 사건이라 부를 만한 것도 별로 없다. 온종일 단 하나의 감정뿐이다. 언제쯤 그가 올까? 잠들 때나 깨어날 때나 단 하나의 생각뿐이다. 짐작건대 애밀리아가 도빈 대위에게 그에 대해 묻고 있던 바로 그 시각, 조지는 스왈로 거리에서 캐넌 대위와 당구를 치고 있었을 것이다. 조지는 쾌활하고 붙임성 좋은 사내였고, 온갖 기량을 겨루는 놀이마다 뛰어났기 때문이다.\n\n한번은 사흘이나 그의 발길이 끊긴 끝에, 애밀리아 양이 모자를 쓰고 정말로 오스본 댁에 쳐들어갔다. \"어머! 오빠를 내버려 두고 우리한테 오다니?\" 젊은 숙녀들이 말했다. \"둘이 다퉜니, 애밀리아? 어서 말해 봐!\" 아니, 정말이지 다툰 적 없었다. \"그이와 어떻게 다툴 수가 있겠어요?\" 그녀가 두 눈에 눈물을 가득 머금고 말했다. 그저 사랑하는 친구들을 보러 왔을 뿐이었다. 너무 오래 못 만났으니까. 그런데 이날 그녀가 어찌나 완전히 멍청하고 어색했던지, 그녀가 슬프게 떠나는 뒷모습을 빤히 바라보던 오스본 자매와 그 가정교사는 그 어느 때보다 더 의아해하며, 조지가 도대체 가엾은 작은 애밀리아에게서 무얼 본다는 걸까 궁금해했다.\n\n물론 그들은 의아해할 만했다. 그녀가 어찌 그 소심하고 작은 마음을, 대담한 검은 눈을 가진 그 젊은 숙녀들의 검열 앞에 발가벗겨 보일 수 있었겠는가? 그것은 움츠러들어 숨어 버리는 편이 나았다. 나는 오스본 자매가 캐시미어 숄이나 분홍빛 새틴 속치마에 대해서는 빼어난 감식가였음을 안다. 터너 양이 자기 것을 자줏빛으로 물들여 짧은 재킷으로 고쳤을 때, 또 픽퍼드 양이 어민 목도리를 비틀어 토시와 장식으로 만들었을 때, 장담컨대 그 변화는 앞서 말한 두 영리한 젊은 여자의 눈을 결코 피해 가지 못했다. 그러나, 보시라, 모피나 새틴보다, 또 솔로몬의 온갖 영화와 시바 여왕의 온갖 옷장보다 더 고운 결을 지닌 것들이 있는 법이다. 그 아름다움이 숱한 감식가의 눈을 비껴가는 것들 말이다. 그리고 그대가 우연히 마주치는, 그윽하고 그늘진 곳에서 향기를 풍기며 다정스레 피어나는 사랑스럽고 수수한 작은 영혼들도 있는가 하면, 놋쇠 보온기만큼이나 커다래서 태양마저 무색하게 만들기에 알맞은 정원 장식물 같은 것들도 있다. 세들리 양은 해바라기 부류가 아니었다. 그리고 나는 겹달리아만 한 크기의 제비꽃을 그린다는 것은 온갖 균형의 법칙에 어긋나는 일이라고 말하겠다.\n\n그렇다, 정말이지, 아직 부모의 둥지 안에 있는 착한 처녀의 삶에는, 소설 속 여주인공이 으레 주장하는 그런 짜릿한 사건들이 많을 수가 없다. 밖에서 먹이를 구하는 어미 새들은 덫이나 총탄에 목숨을 잃을 수도 있고, 그들이 피하거나 혹은 그들에게 화를 입히는 매가 하늘을 떠돌 수도 있겠지만, 둥지 안의 어린 새들은 솜털과 지푸라기 속에서 제법 안락하고 낭만이라곤 없는 삶을 누리다가, 마침내 자기네도 날개를 펼 차례가 오는 법이다. 베키 샤프가 시골에서 제 날개로 날아다니며 온갖 잔가지 위를 폴짝거리고, 수없이 많은 덫 사이를 오가며, 멀쩡하고 솜씨 좋게 제 먹이를 쪼아 먹고 있는 동안, 애밀리아는 러셀 광장의 제 집에 아늑하게 들어앉아 있었다. 그녀가 세상에 나갈 때면 어른들의 인도를 받았고, 그녀에게도, 또 그녀가 다정스레 보호받고 있는 그 풍족하고 명랑하고 안락한 집에도 어떤 화가 닥칠 것 같지는 않았다. 어머니에게는 아침나절의 일과가 있었고, 매일의 드라이브가 있었으며, 부유한 런던 부인의 오락 — 혹은 직업이라 불러도 좋을 — 을 이루는 저 즐거운 방문과 쇼핑의 순행이 있었다. 아버지는 시티에서 자신의 알 수 없는 사업을 꾸려 나갔다. 그곳은 그 시절 들끓던 곳이었으니, 전쟁이 유럽 전역에 휘몰아치고 제국들의 운명이 걸려 있던 때였다. 그때 \"쿠리에\" 신문은 수만 명의 구독자를 거느렸고, 어느 날은 비토리아 전투 소식을, 또 어느 날은 모스크바 화재 소식을 전해 주었으며, 저녁 무렵이면 신문팔이의 나팔이 러셀 광장을 따라 울려 퍼지며 이런 사실을 알리는 것이었다. \"라이프치히 전투 — 육십만 병력 교전 — 프랑스군 완패 — 이십만 전사.\" 노 세들리는 한두 번 매우 심각한 얼굴로 귀가했는데, 이런 소식이 유럽의 온 인심과 온 주식 시장을 뒤흔들고 있었으니 그도 무리는 아니었다.\n\n그러는 동안 블룸즈버리의 러셀 광장에서는, 마치 유럽의 사정이 조금도 어지럽지 않은 것처럼 일상이 흘러갔다. 라이프치히에서의 퇴각도 하인 방에서 샘보 씨가 먹는 끼니 수에는 아무런 차이를 내지 않았다. 동맹군이 프랑스로 쏟아져 들어가도, 저녁 식사를 알리는 종은 여느 때와 다름없이 다섯 시에 울렸다. 짐작건대 가엾은 애밀리아는 브리엔이나 몽미라유 따위에는 아무 관심도 없었고, 황제의 퇴위가 있기 전까지는 그 전쟁에 도무지 흥미를 느끼지 못했던 것 같다. 황제가 퇴위하자 그녀는 두 손을 마주치며 기도를 올렸다. 아, 어찌나 감격에 겨워했던지! 그러고는 그 감정의 격발을 목격한 모든 이가 깜짝 놀라도록, 온 영혼을 다해 조지 오스본의 품에 몸을 던졌다. 사실인즉, 평화가 선포되었고, 유럽은 안정을 찾으려는 참이었다. 저 코르시카 사내는 타도되었고, 오스본 중위의 연대는 출정 명령을 받지 않게 될 터였다. 애밀리아 양이 따져 본 방식은 바로 이러했다. 그녀에게 유럽의 운명이란 곧 조지 오스본 중위였던 것이다. 그의 위험이 사라졌으니 그녀는 감사의 찬가를 불렀다. 그가 곧 그녀의 유럽이요, 그녀의 황제요, 그녀의 동맹 군주들이요, 위엄 넘치는 황태자였다. 그가 그녀의 해요 달이었다. 그리고 짐작건대 그녀는, 여러 군주를 위해 맨션 하우스에서 베풀어진 그 성대한 불꽃놀이와 무도회가 특별히 조지 오스본을 기리는 것이라고 여겼던 것 같다.\n\n우리는 가엾은 베키 샤프 양이 그 아래에서 교육을 받은 저 음울한 스승들로 임기응변과 자아와 가난을 이야기한 바 있다. 그런데 애밀리아 세들리 양의 마지막 가정교사는 사랑이었고, 우리의 젊은 숙녀가 그 인기 높은 선생 아래에서 이룬 진보는 참으로 놀라운 것이었다. 이 빼어난 교양 가정교사에게 열다섯 달이며 열여덟 달이 넘도록 날마다 한결같이 마음을 기울인 끝에, 애밀리아는 위트 양도, 또 길 건너편의 그 검은 눈을 가진 젊은 숙녀들도, 또 치즈윅의 늙은 핑커튼 여사 본인도 도무지 알지 못하는 그 얼마나 많은 비밀을 깨우쳤던가! 하기야 그 새침하고 평판 좋은 처녀들 가운데 누가 그런 것을 알 수 있었겠는가? 핑커튼 양과 위트 양으로 말하자면, 그 다정한 정념이란 애초에 논외였다. 나는 감히 그들에 대해 그런 생각을 입에 담을 엄두조차 내지 못하겠다. 마리아 오스본 양이 헐커 불록 앤드 불록 상회의 프레더릭 오거스터스 불록 씨에게 \"마음을 두고\" 있었던 것은 사실이지만, 그녀의 것은 더없이 존경할 만한 종류의 애정이어서, 만약 그녀의 마음이 — 교양 있는 젊은 여자의 마음이 으레 그래야 하듯 — 파크 레인의 저택, 윔블던의 시골 별장, 멋진 사륜마차, 엄청나게 키 큰 말 두 필과 하인들, 그리고 명망 높은 헐커 앤드 불록 상회의 연간 수익 사분의 일에 단단히 고정되어 있는 한, 그녀는 늙은 불록을 데려가도 똑같이 좋아했을 것이다. 그 온갖 이점들이 프레더릭 오거스터스라는 사람으로 대표되었던 것이다. 만약 그 시절에 오렌지 꽃이 발명되어 있었더라면(처녀의 순결을 나타내는 저 가슴 뭉클한 상징물로, 딸들이 너 나 할 것 없이 혼인으로 팔려 가는 프랑스에서 우리가 수입해 온 것이다), 단언컨대 마리아 양은 그 티 없는 화관을 쓰고, 통풍에 걸린 늙고 대머리에 코끝이 붉은 늙은 불록 곁에 나란히 여행용 마차에 올랐을 것이며, 더없이 정숙한 자태로 그 아름다운 평생을 그의 행복에 바쳤을 것이다. 다만 그 노신사는 이미 기혼이었던 까닭에, 그녀는 자기 젊은 애정을 젊은 동업자에게 베풀었던 것이다. 사랑스럽고 활짝 핀 오렌지 꽃이여! 며칠 전에 나는 (한때) 트로터 양이었던 분이 그 꽃을 두르고 하노버 광장의 세인트조지 교회에서 여행용 마차로 사뿐히 올라타는 것을 보았는데, 그 뒤로 므두셀라 경이 절뚝거리며 따라 들어갔다. 그녀가 어찌나 사람 마음을 끄는 정숙한 태도로 마차의 차양을 끌어 내리던지 — 그 사랑스러운 순진한 분! 그 결혼식에는 허영의 시장 마차의 절반은 모여 있었다.\n\n이런 종류의 사랑이 애밀리아의 교육을 완성하고, 일 년 사이에 착한 처녀를 착한 젊은 여인으로 — 머지않아 행복한 시절이 오면 착한 아내가 될 사람으로 — 탈바꿈시킨 것은 아니었다. 이 젊은 사람은(어쩌면 그녀의 부모가 그런 우상 숭배와 어리석고 낭만적인 생각을 부추기고 거든 것은 매우 경솔한 일이었는지도 모른다) 우리가 잠시 알게 된 그 폐하의 군대에 복무하는 젊은 장교를 온 마음을 다해 사랑했다. 그녀는 잠에서 깨는 바로 그 첫 순간 그를 생각했고, 기도 가운데 가장 마지막으로 부르는 이름도 그의 것이었다. 그녀는 그토록 잘생기고 그토록 영리한 남자를 본 적이 없었다. 말 위에서의 그토록 멋진 자태, 그토록 빼어난 춤꾼, 한마디로 그토록 대단한 영웅을 말이다. 황태자의 인사 솜씨를 말한들 무엇하랴! 그것이 조지의 것에 비하면 무엇이란 말인가? 그녀는 다들 그토록 칭송하는 브러멜 씨도 본 적이 있었다. 그런 사람을 그녀의 조지에 견주다니! 오페라의 모든 멋쟁이들 가운데에서도(그 시절에는 진짜 오페라 모자를 쓴 멋쟁이들이 있었다) 그에 견줄 만한 이는 하나도 없었다. 그는 동화 속 왕자가 되기에 딱 알맞을 만큼만 훌륭했다. 그러니 오, 그토록 미천한 신데렐라에게 몸을 굽히다니, 그 얼마나 큰 도량인가! 만약 핑커튼 여사가 애밀리아의 속내를 털어놓는 상대였더라면, 십중팔구 이 맹목적인 헌신을 막아 보려 했을 것이다. 그러나, 장담컨대, 별 성과는 없었을 것이다. 그것은 어떤 여자들의 천성이요 본능이기 때문이다. 어떤 여자들은 책략을 꾸미도록 만들어졌고, 어떤 여자들은 사랑하도록 만들어졌다. 그러니 이 글을 읽는 존경하는 독신 신사가 있다면, 부디 자기에게 가장 잘 맞는 쪽을 고르시기를 바란다.\n\n이 압도적인 감흥에 사로잡혀 있는 동안, 애밀리아 양은 그런 이기적인 사람들이 으레 그러하듯, 치즈윅의 그 사랑하는 열두 친구를 더없이 매정하게 소홀히 했다. 물론 그녀에게는 생각할 거리라곤 이 한 가지 주제뿐이었다. 그런데 솔타이어 양은 속내를 털어놓기에는 너무 차가웠고, 세인트키츠 출신의 곱슬머리 젊은 상속녀 스워츠 양에게는 도무지 마음이 내키지 않았다. 그녀는 방학 동안 어린 로라 마틴을 집에 데리고 있었다. 그래서 내 생각에 그녀는 로라를 속내 털어놓는 상대로 삼아, 자기가 결혼하면 로라가 와서 함께 살게 해 주겠다고 약속하고, 사랑이라는 정념에 관해 로라에게 숱한 정보를 일러 주었던 것 같다. 그것은 그 어린 사람에게 유달리 유익하고 신기한 일이었음에 틀림없다. 아아, 슬프도다! 가엾은 에미는 정돈된 마음을 지니지 못했던 것 같아 걱정스럽다.\n\n이 작은 가슴이 그토록 빠르게 두근거리지 않도록 단속하기는커녕, 그녀의 부모는 대체 무얼 하고 있었단 말인가? 노 세들리는 사정을 그다지 눈여겨보지 않는 듯했다. 그는 근래 들어 더 심각해졌고, 시티의 사업에 정신이 팔려 있었다. 세들리 부인은 워낙 태평하고 캐묻기 싫어하는 성미여서 질투조차 하지 않았다. 조스 씨는 첼튼엄에서 어느 아일랜드 과부에게 포위 공격을 당하며 집을 비우고 있었다. 애밀리아는 집을 독차지하다시피 했다. 아, 때로는 너무 독차지했다! 그렇다고 그녀가 의심을 품었다는 말은 아니다. 왜냐하면, 그야 물론, 조지는 근위 사령부에 가 있을 게 분명하고, 채텀에서 늘 휴가를 얻을 수 있는 것도 아니며, 시내에 있을 때면 친구들과 누이들을 만나고 사교계에 어울려야 마땅하니까(그토록 어느 모임에서나 빛나는 사람이!). 또 연대에 있을 때면 너무 지쳐서 긴 편지를 쓸 수 없는 것이니까. 나는 그녀가 가지고 있던 그 편지 꾸러미를 어디에 간직했는지 안다. 그리고 야키모처럼 그녀의 침실을 몰래 드나들 수도 있다. 야키모처럼? 아니, 그건 못된 역할이다. 나는 그저 달빛 역할만 맡아, 신의와 아름다움과 순결이 누워 꿈꾸고 있는 그 침상을 해롭잖게 들여다보기만 하겠다.\n\n그러나 오스본의 편지가 짧고 군인다운 것이었다 해도, 이것만은 인정해야겠다. 만약 세들리 양이 오스본 씨에게 보낸 편지들을 출판한다면, 우리는 이 소설을 어찌나 많은 권수로 늘려야 할지, 아무리 감상적인 독자라도 견디지 못할 지경이 될 것이다. 그녀는 큼직한 종이를 빼곡히 채웠을 뿐 아니라, 더없이 놀라운 고집으로 그 위에 가로로 겹쳐 썼고, 시집에서 통째로 몇 쪽씩이나 조금도 가차 없이 베껴 적었으며, 거의 광적인 강조로 낱말과 구절에 밑줄을 그었고, 한마디로 자기 처지를 드러내는 온갖 징표를 다 보여 주었다. 그녀는 여주인공이 아니었다. 그녀의 편지는 같은 말의 반복으로 가득했다. 때로는 다소 미심쩍은 문법으로 글을 썼고, 시구에서는 운율을 두고 온갖 자유를 누렸다. 그러나 오, 부인들이여, 만약 그대들이 통사론에도 불구하고 이따금 사람의 마음을 울리는 것이 허락되지 않는다면, 또 그대들 모두가 삼음보와 사음보의 차이를 알기 전에는 사랑받지 못한다면, 모든 시 따위는 악마에게나 가 버리고, 모든 학교 선생은 비참하게 스러져 버려라!\n\n애밀리아 양의 편지를 받는 그 신사는, 안타깝게도 다소 무정한 비평가였던 모양이다. 어찌나 많은 편지가 시골 곳곳으로 오스본 중위를 따라다녔던지, 그는 그 편지들을 두고 떠들어대는 식당 동료들의 농담이 거의 부끄러울 지경이 되어, 하인에게 절대로 자기 사실(私室) 말고는 편지를 전하지 말라고 일렀다. 그가 그중 한 통으로 시가에 불을 붙이는 것이 목격되어 도빈 대위를 경악하게 했는데, 내 생각에 도빈은 그 편지 한 장을 손에 넣을 수만 있다면 지폐 한 장이라도 내놓았을 것이다.\n\n한동안 조지는 그 관계를 비밀로 하려 애썼다. 한 여자가 얽혀 있다는 것만은 그도 인정했다. \"그것도 처음이 아니라니까.\" 소위 스푸니가 소위 스터블에게 말했다. \"그 오스본이란 작자, 보통내기가 아니야. 데메라라에서는 어떤 판사 딸이 그놈한테 빠져서 거의 미칠 지경이 됐다더군. 그리고 너도 알다시피 세인트빈센트에는 그 아름다운 혼혈 아가씨 파이 양이 있었고. 게다가 본국에 돌아온 뒤로는, 글쎄 영락없는 돈 조반니라잖아, 정말이지.\"\n\n스터블과 스푸니는 \"정말이지 영락없는 돈 조반니\"라는 것이야말로 사내가 가질 수 있는 가장 멋진 자질 가운데 하나라고 여겼고, 그래서 연대의 젊은 친구들 사이에서 오스본의 명성은 대단한 것이었다. 그는 야외 경기로 이름났고, 노래로 이름났으며, 사열장에서도 이름났다. 게다가 아버지가 넉넉하게 대주는 돈을 아낌없이 썼다. 그의 코트는 연대의 누구 것보다도 잘 지어졌고, 또 누구보다도 많았다. 그는 부하들에게 흠모의 대상이었다. 연대 식당의 어떤 장교보다도, 늙은 대령 헤비탑까지 포함해서도, 그가 술을 더 많이 마실 수 있었다. 권투 시합 무대에 섰던 적도 있고 술버릇만 아니었으면 하사가 됐을 사병 너클스보다도 권투를 더 잘했다. 연대 클럽에서는 단연 최고의 타자요 투수였다. 그리고 자기 말 '기름칠한 번개'를 타고 퀘벡 경마에서 수비대 컵을 따냈다. 애밀리아 말고도 그를 숭배하는 사람은 또 있었다. 스터블과 스푸니는 그를 일종의 아폴론으로 여겼고, 도빈은 그를 흠잡을 데 없는 만능인 크라이턴으로 생각했으며, 오다우드 소령 부인은 그가 우아한 청년이라고 인정하며 캐슬포가티 경의 차남 피츠제럴드 포가티를 떠올리게 한다고 말했다.\n\n자, 스터블과 스푸니와 그 나머지 패거리는 오스본의 그 여자 편지 상대를 두고 더없이 낭만적인 억측을 즐겼다. 그를 사랑하는 런던의 어느 공작부인이라거나, 다른 누군가와 약혼한 처지이면서 그에게 미친 듯이 빠진 어느 장군의 딸이라거나, 말 네 필과 야반도주를 제안한 어느 의원의 부인이라거나, 아니면 더없이 짜릿하고 낭만적이며 모든 당사자에게 수치스러운 그 어떤 격정의 또 다른 희생자라거나 하는 것이었다. 그러나 오스본은 그 어느 억측에도 일말의 단서조차 흘리지 않았고, 그리하여 그의 젊은 숭배자들과 친구들은 그 사연 전부를 제멋대로 지어내고 꾸며 맞춰야 했다.\n\n그리고 도빈 대위의 경솔한 입놀림만 아니었다면, 일의 실상은 연대 안에서 끝내 알려지지 않았을 것이다. 어느 날 대위가 식당에서 아침을 먹고 있는데, 군의관 보조 캐클과 앞서 말한 두 호걸이 오스본의 밀애를 두고 추측을 늘어놓고 있었다. 스터블은 그 부인이 샬럿 왕비 궁정의 어느 공작부인이라고 우겼고, 캐클은 그녀가 최악의 평판을 지닌 오페라 가수라고 장담했다. 이 말에 도빈은 어찌나 동요했던지, 마침 입안에 달걀과 버터 바른 빵이 가득해 도무지 입을 열 처지가 아니었는데도, 그만 불쑥 내뱉지 않을 수 없었다. \"캐클, 자네 멍청한 바보로군. 늘 헛소리에 험담만 늘어놓는다니까. 오스본은 공작부인과 달아날 작자도, 모자 가게 아가씨를 망칠 작자도 아니야. 세들리 양은 이제껏 살았던 가장 매력적인 젊은 여성 가운데 한 분일세. 오래전부터 그분과 약혼한 사이라고. 그러니 그분을 헐뜯는 자는 내 귀에 들리는 데서는 그러지 않는 게 좋을 거야.\" 그 말과 함께 도빈은 얼굴이 새빨개져서 입을 다물었고, 차 한 잔에 거의 목이 멜 뻔했다. 그 이야기는 삼십 분 만에 온 연대에 쫙 퍼졌다. 그리고 바로 그날 저녁, 오다우드 소령 부인은 오다우즈타운에 있는 동생 글로비나에게 더블린에서 서둘러 올 것 없다고 편지를 써 보냈다. 젊은 오스본이 이미 진작에 약혼한 몸이라는 것이었다.\n\n그날 저녁 부인은 위스키 토디 한 잔을 들며 적절한 인사말로 중위를 추켜세웠고, 그는 자기 비밀을 누설한 죄로 도빈과 한판 다투려고 잔뜩 화가 나서 집으로 돌아갔다(도빈은 오다우드 소령 부인의 파티를 사양하고 제 방에 앉아 플루트를 불고 있었는데, 내가 알기로는 자못 우울한 투로 시까지 짓고 있었다). 도빈이 자기 비밀을 발설한 데 따지러 간 것이었다.\n\n\"대체 누가 자네더러 내 일을 떠들어대라고 했나?\" 오스본이 분개하여 소리쳤다. \"내가 결혼할 거라는 걸 온 연대가 다 알아야 할 까닭이 뭐냐고? 그 수다쟁이 늙은 마귀할멈 페기 오다우드가 그 빌어먹을 만찬 식탁에서 내 이름을 함부로 입에 올리고, 세 왕국 천지에 내 약혼을 광고하고 다니는 게 말이 되나? 따지고 보면, 자네가 무슨 권리로 내가 약혼했다고 떠들고, 도무지 무슨 권리로 내 일에 참견하나, 도빈?\"\n\n\"내가 보기엔 말일세.\" 도빈 대위가 입을 떼었다.\n\n\"보기엔이고 뭐고, 집어치우게, 도빈.\" 후배가 그의 말을 가로막았다. \"내가 자네한테 신세진 거 알아. 그것도 빌어먹을 정도로 잘 알지. 하지만 자네가 나보다 다섯 살 위라고 해서 노상 설교나 듣고 있지는 않겠네. 자네의 그 잘난 척이며 빌어먹을 동정이며 생색이며, 더는 못 참아. 동정이라니, 생색이라니! 내가 자네보다 못한 게 대체 뭔지 알고 싶군.\"\n\n\"자네 약혼했나?\" 도빈 대위가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"내가 약혼했든 말든 그게 자네나 여기 누구하고 무슨 상관인가?\"\n\n\"부끄러운가?\" 도빈이 다시 물었다.\n\n\"무슨 권리로 그런 걸 묻나, 응? 정말이지 알고 싶군.\" 조지가 말했다.\n\n\"맙소사, 설마 파혼하고 싶다는 말은 아니겠지?\" 도빈이 벌떡 일어서며 물었다.\n\n\"바꿔 말하면, 내가 명예를 아는 사내냐고 묻는 거로군.\" 오스본이 사납게 말했다. \"그게 자네 말뜻인가? 요즘 자네는 나를 대하는 투가 영 그래서, 더는 못 참겠네.\"\n\n\"내가 뭘 어쨌다고? 자네가 어여쁜 아가씨를 소홀히 하고 있다고 했을 뿐이야, 조지. 런던에 가거든 세인트제임스 근처 도박장이 아니라 그 아가씨한테 가야 한다고 했을 뿐이라고.\"\n\n\"돈을 돌려받고 싶은 모양이지.\" 조지가 비웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"물론 돌려받고 싶지. 늘 그랬지, 안 그런가?\" 도빈이 말했다. \"참 너그러운 친구로구먼.\"\n\n\"아니, 젠장, 윌리엄, 내가 잘못했네.\" 여기서 조지가 후회에 사로잡혀 끼어들었다. \"자넨 백 가지로 내 친구였어, 하늘이 알지. 숱한 곤경에서 날 건져줬고. 근위대 크롤리가 나한테서 그 돈을 따갔을 때, 자네 아니었으면 난 끝장났을 거야. 그건 나도 알아. 정말이라고. 하지만 자네도 날 그렇게 모질게 다루면 안 돼. 노상 나를 캐묻고 따지면 안 된다고. 난 애밀리아가 무척 좋아. 그녀를 흠모해, 그런 거란 말이야. 그렇게 화난 얼굴 하지 말게. 그녀는 흠잡을 데 없어. 나도 알아. 하지만 알다시피, 따려고 겨뤄보지 않으면 뭔가를 손에 넣어도 재미가 없는 법이거든. 젠장, 연대가 이제 막 서인도제도에서 돌아왔으니, 나도 좀 놀아야겠네. 그러고 나서 결혼하면 마음을 고쳐먹지. 명예를 걸고 그러겠네, 정말로. 그리고 말이야, 도브, 나한테 화내지 말게. 다음 달에 백 파운드 줄게. 아버지가 후하게 한몫 떼주실 게 분명하니까. 헤비탑한테 휴가를 청해서 런던에 가, 내일 애밀리아를 보러 가겠네. 자, 그러면 됐나?\"\n\n\"자네한테는 오래 화내고 있을 수가 없다니까, 조지.\" 사람 좋은 대위가 말했다. \"그리고 돈으로 말하면, 이 친구야, 내가 만약 돈이 궁하면 자네가 마지막 한 푼까지 나눠주리란 걸 알지 않나.\"\n\n\"암, 그렇고말고, 도빈.\" 조지가 더없이 너그러운 태도로 말했다. 하긴 그에게는 남에게 떼줄 만한 돈이 있어본 적이 없었지만 말이다.\n\n\"다만 자네가 그 방종한 짓들을 어서 끝냈으면 하는 마음뿐일세, 조지. 며칠 전 가엾은 어린 에미 양이 자네 안부를 물을 때 그 얼굴을 자네가 봤더라면, 그 당구공들일랑 진작에 내던졌을 걸세. 어서 가서 그녀를 위로해주게, 이 못된 친구야. 가서 긴 편지를 써. 그녀를 행복하게 해줄 무언가를 하라고. 아주 사소한 것으로도 충분할 테니.\"\n\n\"내 보기에 그녀는 날 끔찍이도 좋아하지.\" 중위가 흡족한 표정으로 말했다. 그러고는 식당의 흥겨운 친구들과 어울려 저녁을 마저 보내려고 자리를 떴다.\n\n한편 그 무렵 애밀리아는 러셀 광장에서 달을 바라보고 있었다. 그 평화로운 곳을 비추는 달은, 오스본 중위가 주둔한 채텀 병영의 연병장에도 똑같이 비치고 있었다. 그녀는 자기 영웅이 지금 무엇을 하고 있을까 홀로 생각에 잠겼다. 보초들을 둘러보고 있을지도 몰라, 그녀는 생각했다. 야영하고 있을지도, 다친 전우의 자리를 지켜주고 있을지도, 아니면 자기 쓸쓸한 방에서 병법을 연구하고 있을지도 몰라. 그러자 그녀의 다정한 상념은 천사라도 되어 날개를 단 듯 멀리 날아가, 강을 따라 채텀과 로체스터로 내려가 조지가 있는 병영을 들여다보려 안간힘을 썼다…… 하지만 이런저런 사정을 헤아려보건대, 문이 닫혀 있고 보초가 아무도 들이지 않은 것이 오히려 다행이었지 싶다. 덕분에 그 가엾고 자그마한 흰옷 입은 천사는, 그 젊은 친구들이 위스키 펀치를 들이켜며 고래고래 부르던 노래를 듣지 않을 수 있었으니 말이다.\n\n채텀 병영에서 그 짤막한 대화가 오간 다음 날, 젊은 오스본은 자기가 한 말을 지키겠다는 것을 보여주려고 런던에 갈 채비를 했고, 그리하여 도빈 대위의 갈채를 받았다. \"그녀한테 자그마한 선물이라도 해주고 싶은데 말이야.\" 오스본이 친구에게 은근히 털어놓았다. \"아버지가 한몫 챙겨주실 때까지는 완전히 빈털터리라서.\" 그러나 도빈은 이 선량함과 너그러움이 좌절되도록 내버려두지 않고, 오스본에게 지폐 몇 장을 마련해주었다. 오스본은 짐짓 한번 망설이는 시늉을 한 뒤 그것을 받았다.\n\n내 짐작에 그는 애밀리아에게 무척 근사한 것을 사주었을 법도 하다. 다만 플리트 거리에서 마차를 내리다가 어느 보석상 진열창의 멋진 셔츠핀에 마음을 빼앗겨 도무지 뿌리칠 수가 없었고, 그 값을 치르고 나니 더 이상의 친절을 베풀 만한 돈이 거의 남지 않았던 것이다. 하지만 상관없다. 애밀리아가 원한 것이 그의 선물이 아니었음은 틀림없으니 말이다. 그가 러셀 광장에 이르자, 그녀의 얼굴은 그가 햇살이라도 되는 양 환히 밝아졌다. 며칠 밤낮인지 모를 자그마한 근심들, 두려움, 눈물, 소심한 의구심, 잠 못 이루던 망상들이, 그 낯익고 거역할 수 없는 미소가 한순간 미친 영향 아래 모조리 잊혔다. 그는 응접실 문간에서 그녀를 향해 환히 빛났다. 신들의 양식 같은 구레나룻을 두른, 한 분의 신처럼 늠름한 모습이었다. 삼보는 그 젊은 장교에게 멋대로 임시 계급을 한 단계 올려 \"오스본 대위\"라고 알리면서, 동조하는 듯한 함박웃음으로 얼굴이 환했는데, 그 어린 아가씨가 창가 망보던 자리에서 화들짝 놀라 얼굴을 붉히며 벌떡 일어서는 것을 보았다. 그리고 삼보는 물러갔다. 문이 닫히기가 무섭게 그녀는, 마치 깃들기에 그곳만이 유일하게 자연스러운 보금자리라는 듯, 파닥파닥 조지 오스본 중위의 가슴으로 날아들었다. 오, 가엾고 가쁜 숨 몰아쉬는 작은 영혼이여! 숲에서 가장 좋은 나무, 줄기가 가장 곧고 가지가 가장 튼튼하며 잎이 가장 무성하여 그대가 그 위에 둥지를 틀고 구구거리려는 그 나무가, 그대도 알지 못하는 새 표시가 찍혀 머지않아 우지끈 쓰러질지도 모르는 일이다. 사람과 나무를 견주는 이 비유라니, 어찌나 오래고도 오랜 것인지!\n\n그러는 사이 조지는 그녀의 이마와 반짝이는 두 눈에 무척 다정하게 입을 맞추었고, 더없이 자상하고 살가웠다. 그녀는 그의 다이아몬드 셔츠핀이(전에는 그가 그것을 단 것을 본 적이 없었다) 여태껏 본 가장 예쁜 장신구라고 생각했다.\n\n우리의 젊은 중위가 앞서 보인 행실을 눈여겨보고, 또 그가 방금 도빈 대위와 나눈 그 짤막한 대화의 보고를 기억해둔 세심한 독자라면, 어쩌면 오스본의 인품에 대해 어떤 결론에 다다랐을지도 모른다. 어느 냉소적인 프랑스인이 말하기를, 연애라는 거래에는 두 당사자가 있는데, 하나는 사랑하는 쪽이요 다른 하나는 그렇게 대접받기를 황송히 허락해주는 쪽이라고 했다. 어쩌면 사랑은 때로 남자 쪽에 있고, 어쩌면 여자 쪽에 있다. 어쩌면 어떤 홀딱 반한 시골 총각은 진작에 무신경을 정숙함으로, 둔함을 처녀다운 수줍음으로, 그저 텅 빈 머릿속을 사랑스러운 부끄럼으로, 한마디로 거위를 백조로 오해했을지도 모른다. 어쩌면 사랑받는 어느 여성 독자께서는 한 마리 당나귀를 자기 상상의 광채와 영광으로 치장해, 그 둔함을 사내다운 소박함으로 흠모하고, 그 이기심을 사내다운 우월함으로 숭배하며, 그 어리석음을 위엄 있는 진중함으로 떠받들고, 저 눈부신 요정 티타니아가 아테네의 어느 직조공을 부린 것처럼 그를 부렸을지도 모른다. 내 생각에 나는 세상에서 이런 착각의 희극들이 벌어지는 것을 본 적이 있다. 그러나 한 가지만은 분명하다. 애밀리아는 자기 연인을 이 제국에서 가장 늠름하고 빛나는 사내 가운데 하나라고 믿었다는 것이다. 그리고 어쩌면 오스본 중위도 스스로 그렇게 생각했을 것이다.\n\n그는 약간 방종했다. 하지만 젊은 사내들 가운데 그렇지 않은 이가 얼마나 되겠는가. 그리고 아가씨들이란 겁쟁이보다 난봉꾼을 더 좋아하지 않던가? 그는 아직 그 방종한 짓을 다 끝내지 못했지만, 머지않아 끝낼 참이었다. 그리고 이제 평화가 선포되었으니 군대를 떠날 작정이었다. 코르시카의 괴물은 엘바에 갇혔고, 따라서 진급도 끝났으며, 그의 의심할 바 없는 군사적 재능과 용맹을 펼칠 기회도 남지 않았다. 게다가 그의 봉급에 애밀리아의 지참금까지 보태면, 어딘가 사냥하기 좋은 시골에 아늑한 거처를 마련할 수 있을 터였다. 거기서 사냥도 좀 하고 농사도 좀 지으며, 둘이서 무척 행복하게 살 것이었다. 결혼한 몸으로 군대에 남는다는 것은 어림없는 일이었다. 조지 오스본 부인이 어느 지방 소도시의 셋방에 사는 꼴이라니, 아니 더 끔찍하게는 장교 패거리 틈에 끼어 오다우드 소령 부인의 비호를 받으며 동인도나 서인도제도에 사는 꼴이라니, 어디 상상이나 되는가! 애밀리아는 오다우드 소령 부인을 두고 오스본이 들려주는 이야기에 자지러지게 웃어댔다. 그는 그녀를 너무도 애틋이 사랑한 나머지, 그 끔찍한 여자와 그 천박함에, 군인 아내의 거친 처지에 그녀를 내맡길 수가 없었다. 그는 자기 자신은 아무래도 좋았다. 정말로 그랬다. 다만 그의 사랑스러운 어린 아가씨만큼은, 자기 아내로서 마땅히 누려야 할 사회적 자리를 차지하게 해주고 싶었다. 그리고 이런 제안들에 그녀가 동의했으리란 것은 여러분도 틀림없이 짐작할 것이다. 같은 사람에게서 나온 그 어떤 제안에도 그녀는 그렇게 동의했을 테니 말이다.\n\n이런 식의 이야기를 나누며 수없이 많은 공중누각을 쌓아 올리면서(애밀리아는 거기에 온갖 꽃밭이며 시골 오솔길이며 시골 교회며 주일학교 따위로 단장을 했고, 그동안 조지의 마음의 눈은 마구간과 개집과 술 저장고를 향해 있었다), 이 젊은 한 쌍은 두어 시간을 무척 즐겁게 흘려보냈다. 그런데 중위는 런던에 머물 날이 그 하루뿐인 데다 처리해야 할 무척 중대한 일이 산더미였으므로, 에미 양이 장차 시누이가 될 사람들과 함께 저녁을 들면 어떻겠느냐는 제안이 나왔다. 이 초대는 기쁘게 받아들여졌다. 그는 그녀를 누이들에게 데려갔고, 그녀가 떠들고 재잘대는 모양이 그 숙녀들을 어찌나 놀라게 했던지, 그들은 조지가 그녀를 제법 그럴듯하게 키워낼 수 있겠다고 생각했다. 그런 뒤 그는 자기 볼일을 보러 떠났다.\n\n한마디로, 그는 밖으로 나가 채링크로스의 어느 제과점에서 아이스크림을 먹고, 폴몰에서 새 코트를 입어보고, 올드슬로터스에 들러 캐넌 대위를 찾았으며, 대위와 당구를 열한 판 쳐서 그중 여덟 판을 이기고는, 저녁 식사 시간에 삼십 분 늦게, 그러나 무척 기분 좋게 러셀 광장으로 돌아왔다.\n\n늙은 오스본 씨는 사정이 그렇지 못했다. 그 신사가 시티에서 돌아와 응접실에서 딸들과 우아한 워트 양의 영접을 받았을 때, 그들은 그의 얼굴만 보고도 — 평소 좋을 때조차 부석부석하고 근엄하며 누렇던 얼굴이었다 — 또 그 검은 눈썹의 찌푸림과 꿈틀거림만 보고도, 그 커다란 흰 조끼 속의 심장이 어지럽고 불편하다는 것을 단박에 알아차렸다. 애밀리아가 늘 그러듯 잔뜩 떨고 주눅이 들어 그에게 인사하러 앞으로 나서자, 그는 알은체로 무뚝뚝하게 한 번 끙 소리를 내고는, 그 크고 텁수룩한 손아귀에 잡은 작은 손을, 거기 붙잡아두려는 기색 하나 없이 그냥 떨어뜨려버렸다. 그는 음울하게 맏딸을 둘러보았다. 그 눈빛이 분명 \"대체 저 애가 왜 여기 있는 거냐?\" 하고 묻는 뜻임을 알아챈 맏딸이 곧바로 말했다.\n\n\"조지가 런던에 왔어요, 아버지. 호스가즈에 갔다가 저녁 시간에 맞춰 돌아올 거예요.\"\n\n\"오 그래? 그 녀석 때문에 저녁을 미뤄둘 생각은 없다, 제인.\" 그 점잖은 사내는 이 말과 함께 자기 전용 의자에 털썩 주저앉았다. 그러자 품위 있고 가구가 잘 갖춰진 응접실에는 적막만이 감돌았고, 그 적막을 깨는 것이라곤 커다란 프랑스제 시계의 겁먹은 듯한 똑딱거림뿐이었다.\n\n이피게네이아의 희생을 묘사한 명랑한 황동 군상이 얹힌 그 시계가 무거운 대성당 종소리 같은 가락으로 다섯 시를 알리자, 오스본 씨는 오른편의 종 줄을 사납게 잡아당겼고, 집사가 부랴부랴 올라왔다.\n\n\"저녁!\" 오스본 씨가 으르렁거렸다.\n\n\"조지 도련님이 아직 안 들어오셨습니다, 나리.\" 집사가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"조지 따위 알 게 뭐냐. 이 집 주인이 나냐 아니냐? 저녁 내와!\" 오스본 씨가 얼굴을 찌푸렸다. 애밀리아는 떨었다. 나머지 세 숙녀 사이에 눈짓으로 전보 같은 신호가 오갔다. 아래층의 고분고분한 종이 식사를 알리는 소리를 울리기 시작했다. 종소리가 그치자, 이 집안의 가장은 황동 단추가 달린 커다란 파란 코트의 그 큼직한 뒷주머니에 두 손을 찔러 넣고는, 더 이상의 통보를 기다리지도 않고 어깨너머로 네 여인을 노려보며 혼자 성큼성큼 아래층으로 내려갔다.\n\n\"이번엔 또 무슨 일이야?\" 그들이 자리에서 일어나 아버지 뒤를 조심조심 종종걸음으로 따르며 서로에게 물었다. \"공채 시세가 떨어지는 모양이지.\" 워트 양이 속삭였다. 그렇게, 떨면서 말없이, 이 숨죽인 여인들은 자신들의 어두운 인도자를 뒤따랐다. 그들은 말없이 제자리에 앉았다. 그가 으르렁거리듯 식전 기도를 읊었는데, 그 소리가 저주만큼이나 무뚝뚝하게 들렸다. 커다란 은제 음식 덮개들이 벗겨졌다. 애밀리아는 자기 자리에서 떨었다. 그도 그럴 것이, 그녀는 그 무시무시한 오스본 씨 곁에, 게다가 식탁의 자기 쪽에 혼자 앉아 있었는데, 그 빈자리는 조지가 없는 탓에 생긴 것이었다.\n\n\"수프?\" 오스본 씨가 국자를 움켜쥐고 그녀에게 눈을 고정한 채 무덤 속에서 울리는 듯한 목소리로 말했다. 그러고는 그녀와 나머지에게 떠주고 나서, 한동안 입을 열지 않았다.\n\n\"세들리 양 접시 치워라.\" 마침내 그가 말했다. \"이 애는 수프를 못 먹어. 나도 못 먹겠다. 형편없으니까. 수프 치워, 힉스. 그리고 제인, 내일 요리사를 이 집에서 내쫓아라.\"\n\n수프에 관한 소견을 마친 오스본 씨는 생선에 대해서도 몇 마디 짤막하게 평했는데, 이 또한 사납고 빈정대는 투였으며, 그 어시장 이름값을 톡톡히 하리만치 힘주어 빌링스게이트를 저주했다. 그러고는 다시 침묵에 빠져 와인을 몇 잔이나 들이켰고, 점점 더 무시무시한 표정이 되어갔다. 그러다 문에서 경쾌한 노크 소리가 나며 조지가 도착했음을 알리자, 그제야 모두가 기운을 차리기 시작했다.\n\n더 일찍은 올 수가 없었다는 것이었다. 다길레 장군이 호스가즈에서 그를 기다리게 했다나. 수프든 생선이든 상관없다. 아무거나 달라, 뭐든 괜찮다. 양고기 훌륭하군, 죄다 훌륭해. 그의 좋은 기분은 아버지의 험상궂음과 대조를 이루었고, 그는 저녁 내내 쉴 새 없이 재잘거려 모두를, 특히 굳이 이름을 댈 것도 없는 한 사람을 기쁘게 했다.\n\n오스본 씨 댁의 그 음울한 만찬을 으레 마무리 짓는 오렌지 한 알과 와인 한 잔을 젊은 숙녀들이 비우기가 무섭게, 응접실로 출항하라는 신호가 떨어졌고, 그들은 모두 일어나 자리를 떴다. 애밀리아는 조지가 곧 그리로 합류하기를 바랐다. 그녀는 위층 응접실의, 다리에 큼직한 조각을 새기고 가죽 덮개를 씌운 그랜드피아노 앞에서 그가 좋아하는 왈츠 몇 곡을(당시 막 들여온 곡들이었다) 치기 시작했다. 그러나 이 자그마한 술책도 그를 불러오지 못했다. 그는 왈츠 소리에 귀를 막은 듯했다. 곡조는 점점 희미해졌고, 의기소침해진 연주자는 이윽고 그 커다란 악기를 떠났다. 그리고 세 친구가 자신들의 연주 목록 가운데 가장 우렁차고 가장 화려한 신곡 몇 곡을 연주했지만, 그녀는 단 한 음도 듣지 못한 채, 그저 앉아서 생각에 잠겨 불길한 예감에 사로잡혀 있었다. 늙은 오스본의 찌푸린 얼굴은 언제나 무시무시했지만, 그날만큼 죽일 듯이 보인 적은 없었다. 그가 방을 나설 때 그의 눈은, 마치 그녀가 무슨 죄라도 지은 양 그녀를 좇았다. 그들이 커피를 내오자, 그녀는 마치 집사 힉스 씨가 권하려는 독배라도 되는 양 화들짝 놀랐다. 어떤 비밀이 도사리고 있는 것일까? 오, 저 여인들이란! 그들은 자신의 예감을 보듬고 어르며, 마치 기형의 자식을 그러듯 자신의 가장 추한 생각을 애지중지하는 것이다.\n\n아버지의 침울한 안색은 조지 오스본에게도 불안감을 심어주었다. 저런 눈썹에, 저토록 결정적으로 심사 뒤틀린 표정을 하고 있으니, 조지가 그토록 절실히 원하는 그 돈을 어떻게 아버지한테서 우려낸단 말인가? 그는 아버지의 와인을 칭찬하기 시작했다. 그것은 대개 그 노신사를 구슬리는 데 성공적인 수단이었다.\n\n\"서인도제도에서는 아버지 것 같은 마데이라는 도무지 구경도 못 했답니다, 아버지. 며칠 전에 아버지가 보내주신 그 와인을 헤비탑 대령이 세 병이나 단숨에 비워버렸지 뭡니까.\"\n\n\"그랬어?\" 노신사가 말했다. \"그게 한 병에 팔 실링이나 든단다.\"\n\n\"한 다스에 육 기니에 파시지 않겠습니까, 아버지?\" 조지가 웃으며 말했다. \"이 왕국에서 가장 높은 분 중 한 분이 좀 구하고 싶어 하시거든요.\"\n\n\"그래?\" 노인이 으르렁거렸다. \"구할 수 있으면 좋겠구먼.\"\n\n\"다길레 장군이 채텀에 오셨을 때요, 아버지, 헤비탑이 장군께 아침을 대접하면서 저더러 그 와인을 좀 달라고 했지요. 장군도 어찌나 맘에 들어 하시던지, 총사령관께 드릴 큰 통으로 하나 원하셨답니다. 그분이 전하의 오른팔이시거든요.\"\n\n\"기막히게 좋은 와인이지.\" 그 눈썹이 말했고, 표정이 한결 누그러진 듯했다. 조지는 이 흡족함을 틈타 이 마호가니 식탁 위로 자금 문제를 꺼내려던 참이었는데, 아버지가 다시 근엄함으로 돌아가, 그러나 태도는 제법 다정하게, 그에게 클라레를 가져오게 종을 울리라고 일렀다. \"그 클라레가 마데이라만큼 좋은지 어디 보자꾸나, 조지. 그 마데이라야 물론 전하께서 기꺼이 가져가셔도 좋고. 그리고 그걸 마시면서, 네게 중요한 일을 한 가지 이야기하마.\"\n\n애밀리아는 위층에 불안하게 앉아 있다가 클라레 종이 울리는 소리를 들었다. 그녀는 어쩐지 그것이 알 수 없는, 예감을 품은 종소리라고 생각했다. 어떤 이들이 노상 품는 그 예감들 가운데, 분명 더러는 들어맞기 마련인 법이다.\n\n\"내가 알고 싶은 건 말이다, 조지.\" 노신사가 첫 잔을 천천히 쩝쩝 비우고 나서 말했다. \"내가 알고 싶은 건, 너와 — 어 — 저 위층의 그 자그마한 것이, 어떻게 지내고 있느냐 하는 거다.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔 말입니다, 아버지, 보기 어려운 일은 아니지요.\" 조지가 흡족한 미소를 띠고 말했다. \"꽤 분명하지 않습니까, 아버지. 이 와인 정말 기막히군요!\"\n\n\"꽤 분명하다니, 그게 무슨 소리냐?\"\n\n\"아니, 젠장, 아버지, 저를 너무 몰아붙이진 마세요. 저는 겸손한 사내랍니다. 저는 — 어 — 여자깨나 후린다고 으스댈 생각은 없어요. 하지만 그녀가 저를 더할 나위 없이 끔찍이 좋아한다는 것만은 인정합니다. 누가 봐도 한눈에 알 수 있는 일이지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 너 자신은?\"\n\n\"아니, 아버지, 그녀와 결혼하라고 명하신 게 아버지 아니십니까. 그래서 제가 착한 아들 노릇 하고 있는 거고요. 우리 아버님들이 진작에 정해두신 일 아닙니까?\"\n\n\"참 착한 아들이로구나, 그래. 근위대 크롤리 대위며 명예직의 듀세이스 씨며 타퀸 경이며 그 패거리와 어울려 네가 벌인 짓들을 내가 못 들은 줄 아느냐. 조심해라, 조심해.\"\n\n노신사는 이 귀족들의 이름을 더없이 흐뭇한 투로 발음했다. 그는 지체 높은 인사를 만나면 그 앞에서 굽실거리며, 자유민으로 태어난 영국인만이 할 수 있는 식으로 \"각하, 각하\" 하고 떠받들었다. 그는 집에 돌아와 귀족 명감에서 그 가문 내력을 찾아보았고, 일상 대화에 그 이름을 슬쩍 끼워 넣었으며, 딸들에게 그 각하 자랑을 늘어놓았다. 그는 나폴리의 거지가 햇볕을 쬐듯, 그 앞에 넙죽 엎드려 그 빛을 쬐었다. 조지는 그 이름들을 듣고 흠칫했다. 아버지가 도박판에서 벌어진 어떤 거래들에 대해 전해 들었을까 봐 겁이 났던 것이다. 그러나 그 늙은 도덕가는 태연히 말하며 그를 안심시켰다.\n\n\"뭐, 뭐, 젊은 사내들이야 젊은 사내들답기 마련이지. 그리고 내게 위안이 되는 건 말이다, 조지, 네가 영국 최상류 사회에서 어울려 지내고 있다는 거다. 그러길 바라고, 또 그러고 있다고 믿는다. 내 재력이 너에게 허락하는 만큼은 말이야.\"\n\n\"감사합니다, 아버지.\" 조지가 곧바로 자기 요점을 짚으며 말했다. \"그런 지체 높은 분들과 어울리려면 공짜로는 안 되지요. 그리고 제 지갑이라고는, 아버지, 이걸 좀 보세요.\" 그러면서 그는 애밀리아가 그물뜨기로 짠 자그마한 패물 같은 지갑을 들어 보였는데, 그 안에는 도빈의 지폐 가운데 정말 마지막 한 장이 들어 있었다.\n\n\"부족함이 없게 해주마. 영국 상인의 아들이 궁색해서야 쓰겠느냐. 내 기니도 그자들 것 못지않게 좋은 거다, 조지, 이 녀석아. 나는 그걸 아끼지 않아. 내일 시티를 지나거든 초퍼 씨한테 들러라. 네게 줄 게 있을 테니. 네가 좋은 사회에 있다는 걸 아는 한, 나는 돈을 아끼지 않는다. 좋은 사회는 결코 그릇될 리 없다는 걸 아니까 말이야. 내게는 오만이라곤 없다. 나야 비천하게 태어난 몸이지만, 너는 좋은 여건을 누려왔어. 그걸 잘 활용해라. 젊은 귀족들과 어울리거라. 그자들 중에는 네 기니에 맞먹는 일 달러도 못 쓰는 자들이 수두룩하다, 이 녀석아. 그리고 분홍 보닛으로 말하면(여기서 그 짙은 눈썹 밑으로 무언가 알겠다는 듯한, 그다지 유쾌하지 못한 곁눈질이 나왔다) — 뭐, 사내아이들은 사내아이들답기 마련이니까. 다만 한 가지, 내가 네게 피하라고 명하는 게 있다. 그걸 어기면 정말이지 한 푼도 안 남기고 너를 의절해버릴 테다. 그건 바로 도박이야.\"\n\n\"아, 물론이지요, 아버지.\" 조지가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 애밀리아에 관한 그 다른 일로 돌아가서 말이다. 어째서 너는 주식 중개인의 딸보다 더 높은 데로 장가들면 안 되는 거냐, 조지? 그게 내가 알고 싶은 거다.\"\n\n\"그건 집안끼리의 일이지 않습니까, 아버지.\" 조지가 개암을 까며 말했다. \"아버지와 세들리 씨가 백 년 전에 맺어주신 혼사인데요.\"\n\n\"그걸 부정하지는 않는다. 하지만 사람의 처지란 변하는 법이다, 얘야. 세들리가 내 재산의 밑천이 됐다는 것도, 아니 정확히 말하면 내 재능과 천재로 그 떳떳한 자리, 그러니까 수지 장사와 런던 시티에서 내가 차지하고 있다 할 만한 그 자리를 일굴 길을 열어줬다는 것도 부정하지 않아. 나는 세들리에게 그 은혜를 갚아왔다. 그런데 그 사람이 요즘 들어 그 은혜를 시험하고 있어, 얘야. 내 수표책이 그 증거다. 조지! 너한테만 은밀히 하는 말이다만, 나는 세들리 씨의 사업 형편이 어째 미덥지가 않아. 내 수석 서기 초퍼 씨도 그 형편을 영 못 미더워하는데, 그 사람은 닳고 닳은 인물이라 런던의 누구 못지않게 거래소 사정을 훤히 안다. 헐커 앤드 불록도 그 사람을 미심쩍게 보고 있어. 내 짐작에 그 사람이 제 잇속으로 투기에 손을 댄 모양이다. 양키 사략선 몰래시스호에 나포된 죈아멜리호가 그 사람 것이었다는 말도 있어. 그러니 분명히 말해두마. 애밀리아의 만 파운드를 내 눈앞에 현금으로 내놓지 않는 한 너는 그 애와 결혼 못 한다. 절름발이 오리 같은 자의 딸을 내 집안에 들이진 않을 테니까. 와인 이리 주거라, 얘야. 아니면 종을 울려 커피를 가져오게 하든지.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 오스본 씨는 석간신문을 펼쳐 들었고, 조지는 이 신호에서 대화가 끝났으며 아버지가 이제 한숨 졸려 한다는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n그는 더없이 들뜬 기분으로 애밀리아에게로 서둘러 올라갔다. 그날 밤 그가 오랜만에 그녀에게 그토록 다정해진 것은, 그녀를 즐겁게 해주려 그토록 애쓰고, 그토록 부드러워지고, 그토록 말솜씨가 빛난 것은 대체 무엇 때문이었을까? 불행이 닥쳐올 전망 앞에서 그의 너그러운 가슴이 그녀에게 따뜻해진 것이었을까, 아니면 그 사랑스러운 어린 상품을 잃을지도 모른다는 생각이 그것을 더 귀하게 여기게 만든 것이었을까?\n\n그녀는 그 후로 여러 날 동안 그 행복한 저녁의 추억에 기대어 살았다. 그의 말, 그의 눈빛, 그가 부른 노래, 그가 자기 위로 몸을 기울이거나 멀리서 자기를 바라보던 그 자태를 떠올리며 말이다. 그녀가 느끼기에, 오스본 씨 댁에서 그토록 빨리 지나간 밤은 일찍이 없었다. 그리고 이 젊은 사람은 단 한 번, 삼보 씨가 숄을 들고 너무 일찍 나타난 통에 하마터면 화가 날 뻔했다.\n\n조지는 다음 날 아침 와서 그녀에게 다정하게 작별 인사를 한 뒤, 시티로 서둘러 갔다. 거기서 그는 아버지의 수석 서기 초퍼 씨를 찾아가 그에게서 서류 한 장을 받았고, 그것을 헐커 앤드 불록에서 주머니 가득한 돈으로 바꾸었다. 조지가 그 건물에 들어설 때, 늙은 존 세들리가 은행가의 응접실에서 무척 침울한 얼굴로 나오던 참이었다. 그러나 그의 대자는 너무도 의기양양한 나머지, 그 점잖은 주식 중개인의 풀 죽은 모습도, 그 인자한 노신사가 자기에게 던진 쓸쓸한 눈길도 알아채지 못했다. 젊은 불록도 예년처럼 그와 함께 히죽거리며 응접실에서 나오지 않았다.\n\n그리고 헐커 앤드 불록 상회의 여닫이문이 세들리 씨의 등 뒤로 닫히자, 출납계 퀼 씨가(서랍에서 빳빳한 지폐를 꺼내주고 구리 삽으로 금화를 떠 나눠주는 것이 그의 자비로운 소임이었다) 오른편 책상에 앉은 서기 드라이버 씨에게 윙크를 보냈다. 드라이버 씨도 마주 윙크했다.\n\n\"가망 없어.\" 드라이버 씨가 속삭였다.\n\n\"어떤 값에도 안 돼.\" 퀼 씨가 말했다. \"조지 오스본 씨, 어떻게 받으시겠습니까?\" 조지는 지폐 한 뭉치를 게걸스레 주머니에 쑤셔 넣고는, 바로 그날 저녁 식당에서 도빈에게 오십 파운드를 갚았다.\n\n바로 그날 저녁 애밀리아는 그에게 더없이 다정한 긴 편지를 썼다. 그녀의 마음은 애정으로 넘쳐흘렀으나, 여전히 불행을 예감하고 있었다. 오스본 씨의 어두운 표정은 무엇 때문이었을까? 그녀가 물었다. 그분과 자기 아버지 사이에 무슨 갈등이라도 생긴 것일까? 가엾은 아버지는 시티에서 어찌나 침울하게 돌아오셨던지 집안의 모두가 그분을 걱정한다는 것이었다. 요컨대, 사랑과 두려움과 희망과 불길한 예감으로 채워진 넉 장이었다.\n\n\"가엾은 어린 에미, 사랑스러운 어린 에미. 어쩜 이리도 날 좋아할까.\" 조지가 그 편지를 읽으며 말했다. \"그나저나 젠장, 그 섞어 만든 펀치 때문에 머리가 어찌나 지끈거리는지!\" 가엾은 어린 에미, 정말이지.\n\n그 무렵, 파크 레인의 더없이 아늑하고 잘 꾸며진 저택 앞으로 여행용 마차 한 대가 다가와 멈춰 섰다. 마차 옆면에는 마름모꼴 문장이 그려져 있었고, 뒷자리에는 초록색 베일을 쓰고 곱슬머리를 지진 시무룩한 여자가, 마부석에는 덩치 크고 믿음직한 사내가 앉아 있었다. 그것은 햄프셔에서 돌아오는 우리의 벗 크롤리 양의 마차였다. 마차의 창문들은 모두 닫혀 있었고, 평소라면 그중 한 창으로 머리와 혀를 늘어뜨리고 있을 살진 스패니얼은 시무룩한 여자의 무릎 위에 얌전히 엎드려 있었다. 마차가 멈추자 여러 하인들과, 그 외투 더미를 따라온 한 젊은 숙녀의 도움으로 숄로 둘둘 감싼 커다랗고 둥근 꾸러미가 마차에서 내려졌다. 그 꾸러미 안에 들어 있던 이가 바로 크롤리 양이었으니, 그녀는 즉시 위층으로 옮겨져, 환자를 맞이하기에 알맞게 데워진 방의 침대에 뉘어졌다. 그녀의 주치의와 의사를 부르러 심부름꾼들이 떠났다. 그들은 와서, 의논하고, 처방을 내리고, 사라졌다. 크롤리 양의 젊은 말동무는 그 진찰이 끝나자 들어와 지시를 받았고, 그 명망 높은 분들이 처방한 소염제를 직접 복용시켰다.\n\n이튿날 근위 기병대 소속의 크롤리 대위가 나이츠브리지 병영에서 말을 달려왔다. 그의 검은 군마는 병석에 누운 고모의 문 앞에서 깔린 지푸라기를 앞발로 긁었다. 그는 그 인자한 친척의 안부를 더없이 다정하게 물었다. 걱정거리가 한둘이 아닌 듯했다. 그는 크롤리 양의 하녀(그 시무룩한 여자)가 유난히 뚱하고 풀이 죽어 있는 것을 보았다. 또한 그녀의 말동무인 브리그스 양이 응접실에 홀로 앉아 눈물짓는 것도 보았다. 그녀는 사랑하는 벗이 병들었다는 소식을 듣고 부랴부랴 집으로 달려온 것이었다. 그녀는 자신이, 즉 브리그스가 병상의 시간마다 그토록 자주 매만져 주던 그 침상으로 날아가고 싶었다. 그러나 그녀는 크롤리 양의 방에 들어가는 것을 거절당했다. 어떤 낯선 여자가, 시골에서 온 낯선 여자가, 그 가증스러운 샤프라는 양이 그녀의 약을 챙기고 있었던 것이다. 그 말동무는 눈물에 목이 메어 말을 잇지 못했고, 짓밟힌 애정과 가엾고 늙어 빨개진 코를 손수건에 파묻었다.\n\n로던 크롤리는 그 뚱한 하녀를 시켜 자기 이름을 위층에 전했고, 크롤리 양의 새 말동무가 병실에서 사뿐사뿐 내려와, 그가 반갑게 맞으러 나서자 작은 손을 그의 손에 얹고는, 어리둥절해하는 브리그스를 향해 더없이 경멸 어린 눈길을 던졌다. 그러고는 그 젊은 근위병에게 안쪽 응접실 밖으로 나오라고 손짓하며, 그를 아래층의 이제는 적막해진 식당으로 이끌었다. 한때 그토록 숱한 훌륭한 만찬이 베풀어졌던 그 방으로 말이다.\n\n여기서 이 두 사람은 십 분간 이야기를 나누었는데, 의심할 바 없이 위층에 누운 늙은 환자의 병세를 논하던 것이었다. 그 시간이 끝날 무렵 식당의 종이 힘차게 울렸고, 즉시 크롤리 양의 덩치 큰 믿음직한 집사 보울스 씨가 그 부름에 응했다(사실 그는 면담 내내 거의 줄곧 열쇠 구멍에 붙어 있었던 것이다). 이윽고 대위는 콧수염을 비비 꼬며 나와, 지푸라기 사이에서 발을 구르던 검은 군마에 올라탔는데, 거리에 모여든 자그마한 부랑아 녀석들의 감탄을 자아냈다. 그는 말을 다루며 식당 창문 안을 들여다보았고, 말은 아름답게 껑충거리며 뛰놀았다. 한순간 그 젊은 여자가 창가에 보이는가 싶더니, 이내 그 모습이 사라졌다. 필시 그녀는 다시 위층으로 올라가 그 가슴 뭉클한 자비의 의무를 계속하러 간 것이리라.\n\n대체 이 젊은 여자가 누구인지, 나는 궁금하다. 그날 저녁 식당에는 두 사람을 위한 자그마한 만찬이 차려졌다. 마침 하녀 퍼킨 부인이 새 간병인이 자리를 비운 틈을 타 안주인의 방으로 비집고 들어가 그 안을 부산스레 오가던 차였고, 그 새 간병인과 브리그스 양은 그 단출한 식탁에 마주 앉았다.\n\n브리그스는 북받치는 감정에 목이 너무 메어 고기 한 점도 제대로 넘기지 못했다. 젊은 여자는 더없이 우아하게 닭고기를 발라 냈고, 어찌나 또렷한 목소리로 달걀 소스를 청하던지, 그 맛깔스러운 양념이 앞에 놓인 가엾은 브리그스는 흠칫 놀라 국자를 요란하게 달그락거리더니, 다시 한번 걷잡을 수 없이 흐느끼는 발작 상태에 빠지고 말았다.\n\n\"브리그스 양에게 포도주 한 잔 드리는 게 낫지 않겠어요?\" 그 여자가 덩치 큰 믿음직한 사내 보울스 씨에게 말했다. 그는 그대로 했다. 브리그스는 기계적으로 잔을 움켜쥐고 경련하듯 단숨에 들이켜더니, 잠시 신음하다가 접시 위의 닭고기를 깨작거리기 시작했다.\n\n\"우리 둘이서 서로 도울 수 있을 것 같네요.\" 여자가 더없이 상냥하게 말했다. \"보울스 씨의 친절한 수고는 필요 없을 거예요. 보울스 씨, 괜찮으시다면 필요할 때 종을 울리겠어요.\" 그는 아래층으로 내려갔는데, 가는 길에 아무 잘못도 없는 자기 부하 하인에게 차마 입에 담지 못할 욕설을 퍼부었다.\n\n\"그렇게 마음 졸이시다니 딱하네요, 브리그스 양.\" 젊은 숙녀가 차갑고 살짝 빈정대는 투로 말했다.\n\n\"내 더없이 소중한 벗이 저토록 아픈데, 나를 보려고도, 안 하-하-하잖아요.\" 브리그스가 새삼 북받친 슬픔에 겨워 꺽꺽거리며 내뱉었다.\n\n\"이제 그리 많이 편찮으신 것도 아니에요. 마음 푸세요, 브리그스 양. 그저 과식하셨을 뿐이에요. 그게 다예요. 많이 나아지셨어요. 곧 완전히 회복되실 거예요. 부항을 뜨고 치료를 받느라 기운이 빠지셨을 뿐, 금세 기력을 되찾으실 거예요. 부디 마음 푸시고, 포도주 좀 더 드세요.\"\n\n\"하지만 왜, 왜 나를 다시 안 보려 하실까요?\" 브리그스가 가냘프게 울먹였다. \"오, 머틸다, 머틸다, 스물세 해나 정을 나누었는데! 이게 그대의 가엾고 가엾은 아라벨라에게 돌아오는 보답인가요?\"\n\n\"너무 울지 마세요, 가엾은 아라벨라.\" 상대가 (아주 살짝 비웃음을 띠며) 말했다. \"그분이 당신을 안 보려 하는 건 그저, 당신이 나만큼 간병을 잘 못한다고 하시기 때문이에요. 밤새 곁을 지키는 게 나라고 즐거울 리 있겠어요. 차라리 당신이 대신해 주면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"내가 그 소중한 침상을 여러 해 동안 돌보지 않았던가요?\" 아라벨라가 말했다. \"그런데 이제는…\"\n\n\"이제는 다른 사람을 더 좋아하시는 거죠. 뭐, 병든 사람들은 이런 변덕을 부리기 마련이고, 비위를 맞춰 드려야 하잖아요. 그분이 나으시면 나는 떠날 거예요.\"\n\n\"안 돼요, 절대 안 돼요.\" 아라벨라가 미친 듯이 각성제 병을 들이마시며 외쳤다.\n\n\"절대로 안 나으신다는 거예요, 아니면 내가 절대 안 떠난다는 거예요, 브리그스 양?\" 상대가 똑같이 약 올리는 듯한 너그러운 투로 말했다. \"쳇, 그분은 보름이면 나으실 테고, 그러면 나는 퀸스크롤리의 어린 제자들에게로, 그리고 우리 벗보다 훨씬 더 편찮으신 그 애들 어머니에게로 돌아갈 거예요. 나 때문에 질투하실 필요 없어요, 브리그스 양. 나는 벗도 없고 악의도 없는 가엾은 계집애일 뿐이에요. 크롤리 양의 총애를 두고 당신 자리를 빼앗을 생각은 없어요. 내가 떠나면 그분은 일주일 만에 나를 잊으실 거예요. 그런데 당신을 향한 애정은 여러 해에 걸쳐 쌓인 것이잖아요. 괜찮으시면 포도주 좀 따라 주세요, 브리그스 양, 그리고 우리 벗이 되어요. 나야말로 정말 벗이 필요하거든요.\"\n\n마음 약하고 무던한 브리그스는 이 호소에 말없이 손을 내밀었다. 그러나 그러면서도 자신이 버림받았다는 사실을 뼈저리게 느꼈고, 머틸다의 변덕을 두고 쓰디쓰게, 쓰디쓰게 한탄했다. 반 시간이 지나 식사가 끝나자, 리베카 샤프 양은(놀랍게도 이것이 바로 지금껏 교묘하게 \"그 여자\"라고 묘사되어 온 이의 이름이다) 다시 환자의 방으로 올라갔고, 더없이 상냥한 예의를 갖추어 가엾은 퍼킨을 그 방에서 몰아냈다. \"고마워요, 퍼킨 부인, 그만하면 됐어요. 어쩜 이리 잘 만드세요! 필요한 게 있으면 종을 울릴게요. 고마워요.\" 그러자 퍼킨은 질투의 폭풍에 휩싸여 아래층으로 내려갔는데, 그 폭풍을 제 가슴속에 가둬 둘 수밖에 없는 처지인 만큼 더더욱 위험한 것이었다.\n\n이층 층계참을 지날 때 응접실 문이 활짝 열린 것은 그 폭풍 때문이었을까? 아니었다. 그것은 브리그스의 손이 살그머니 연 것이었다. 브리그스는 줄곧 망을 보고 있었던 것이다. 브리그스는 삐걱대는 발소리와 함께 퍼킨이 계단을 내려가는 소리며, 그 푸대접받은 여자가 들고 가는 숟가락과 죽 그릇이 달그락대는 소리를 너무도 똑똑히 들었던 것이다.\n\n\"어때요, 퍼킨?\" 그녀가 상대가 방에 들어서자 말했다. \"어때요, 제인?\"\n\n\"점점 더 안 좋아져요, 브리그스 양.\" 퍼킨이 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 차도가 없으신 거예요?\"\n\n\"딱 한 번 말씀하셨는데, 제가 좀 편안해지셨냐고 여쭈니까 그 멍청한 혀 좀 닥치라고 하시잖아요. 오, 브리그스 양, 이런 날을 보게 될 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐어요!\" 그러더니 그 분수 같은 눈물이 다시 솟구치기 시작했다.\n\n\"이 샤프 양이라는 사람은 대체 어떤 사람이에요, 퍼킨? 나의 굳건한 벗인 라이오넬 들라미어 목사님과 그 인자한 사모님의 우아한 댁에서 성탄절 흥겨운 잔치를 즐기는 동안, 낯선 여자가 나의 더없이 소중한, 지금도 더없이 소중한 머틸다의 애정 속에서 내 자리를 차지해 버릴 줄은 정말 꿈에도 몰랐어요!\" 그 말투에서 알 수 있듯, 브리그스 양은 문학적이고 감상적인 기질의 소유자로, 예전에 시집 한 권을 「나이팅게일의 떨림」이라는 제목으로 예약 출판한 적이 있었다.\n\n\"브리그스 양, 다들 그 젊은 여자한테 홀딱 빠져 버렸어요.\" 퍼킨이 대답했다. \"피트 경께서도 그 여자를 보내려 하지 않으셨지만, 크롤리 양 말씀이라면 감히 거절을 못 하시잖아요. 목사관의 뷰트 부인도 어찌나 똑같은지, 그 여자가 눈앞에 없으면 한시도 마음을 못 놓으세요. 대위님은 아주 그 여자한테 미쳐 계시고요. 크롤리 씨는 죽도록 질투하시고요. 크롤리 양께서 병이 나신 뒤로는 샤프 양 말고는 아무도 곁에 못 오게 하시는데, 어디서 왜 그러시는지 도무지 알 수가 없어요. 제 생각엔 뭔가가 모두를 홀려 버린 것 같아요.\"\n\n리베카는 그날 밤을 꼬박 크롤리 양 곁에서 지새우며 간호했다. 이튿날 밤에는 노부인이 어찌나 편안히 잠을 자던지, 리베카도 환자의 침대 발치 소파에서 몇 시간 단잠을 잘 수 있었다. 머지않아 크롤리 양은 상태가 좋아져 자리에 일어나 앉았고, 리베카가 들려준 브리그스 양과 그 슬픔에 대한 완벽한 흉내에 깔깔거리며 웃었다. 브리그스의 훌쩍이는 콧소리며 손수건 쓰는 모양새까지 어찌나 똑같이 재현하던지, 크롤리 양은 자못 유쾌해졌고, 이는 진찰하러 온 의사들의 감탄을 자아냈다. 평소에 이 세상 물정에 밝은 부인은 아주 사소한 병에만 걸려도 더없이 비참하게 풀이 죽어 죽음을 두려워하기 일쑤였기 때문이다.\n\n크롤리 대위는 날마다 찾아와 리베카 양에게서 고모의 건강에 관한 소식을 들었다. 병세가 어찌나 빨리 호전되던지, 가엾은 브리그스도 마침내 자신이 모시는 부인을 만나도록 허락되었다. 다정한 마음을 지닌 이들이라면 그 감상적인 여자가 짓눌렀던 감정과, 그 만남의 가슴 뭉클한 광경을 능히 상상할 수 있으리라.\n\n이내 크롤리 양은 브리그스를 자주 곁에 두고 싶어 했다. 리베카는 더없이 진지한 표정으로 그녀의 면전에서 그녀를 흉내 내곤 했는데, 덕분에 그 흉내는 점잖은 부인에게 갑절로 짜릿한 재미를 안겨 주는 것이었다.\n\n크롤리 양을 그토록 한심한 병으로 몰아넣고, 시골에 있는 동생의 집을 떠나게 만든 원인이라는 것은 어찌나 낭만과는 거리가 먼 것이던지, 이 점잖고 감상적인 소설에서 차마 설명하기에 적당치가 않다. 좋은 사회에 몸담은 어느 우아한 부인이 너무 먹고 너무 마셨다느니, 목사관에서 거하게 즐긴 따끈한 바닷가재 야식이 병의 원인이었다느니 하는 것을 어찌 입에 담을 수 있겠는가. 정작 크롤리 양 본인은 그 병이 오로지 궂은 날씨 탓이라고 우겨 댔는데 말이다. 발작이 어찌나 심했던지, 목사님의 표현을 빌리자면 머틸다는 하마터면 \"황천길에 오를\" 뻔했다. 온 집안이 유언장을 두고 들뜬 기대에 들떠 있었고, 로던 크롤리는 런던 사교철이 시작되기 전에 적어도 사만 파운드는 확실히 손에 넣게 되었다고 믿어 의심치 않았다. 크롤리 씨는 그녀가 허영의 시장과 파크 레인을 떠나 저세상으로 옮겨 갈 채비를 하도록 엄선한 전도서 한 꾸러미를 보내왔다. 그러나 사우샘프턴에서 온 솜씨 좋은 의사가 때맞춰 불려 와, 그녀를 거의 죽음으로 몰아넣을 뻔한 바닷가재를 물리치고, 그녀가 런던으로 돌아갈 수 있을 만큼 기력을 회복시켜 주었다. 준남작은 일이 이렇게 돌아간 데 대한 극심한 분함을 굳이 감추려 하지 않았다.\n\n모두가 크롤리 양을 시중들고, 목사관의 다정한 식구들에게 그녀의 건강 소식을 전하느라 한 시간마다 심부름꾼이 오가는 동안, 집의 다른 한편에는 몹시 앓고 있으면서도 아무도 거들떠보지 않는 부인이 한 사람 있었으니, 바로 크롤리 부인 본인이었다. 그 솜씨 좋은 의사는 그녀를 보고 나서 고개를 가로저었다. 진찰료가 들지 않는다는 이유로 피트 경이 그 왕진을 허락했던 것이다. 그리하여 그녀는 외딴 방에서, 정원의 잡초만큼도 관심을 받지 못한 채 시들어 가도록 방치되었다.\n\n젊은 숙녀들 또한 그 가정교사가 베푸는 더없이 값진 가르침의 혜택을 크게 잃고 말았다. 샤프 양은 어찌나 정성스러운 간병인이던지, 크롤리 양은 다른 사람의 손으로는 약을 받으려 하지 않았다. 퍼킨은 안주인이 시골을 떠나기 한참 전에 이미 그 자리에서 밀려난 처지였다. 그 충직한 시중꾼은 런던으로 돌아와, 브리그스 양이 자신이 겪었던 것과 똑같은 질투의 고통을 겪고 똑같은 배신을 당하는 꼴을 보며 울적한 위안을 얻었다.\n\n로던 대위는 고모가 병든 것을 핑계로 휴가를 연장하고는 효성스럽게 집에 머물렀다. 그는 늘 고모의 대기실에 붙어 있었다(고모는 작은 푸른색 응접실을 지나야 들어갈 수 있는 안방에 누워 앓고 있었다). 그의 아버지는 늘 거기서 그와 마주쳤다. 혹은 그가 아무리 조용히 복도를 내려와도 어김없이 아버지의 방문이 열리며 그 노신사의 하이에나 같은 얼굴이 빠끔 내다보는 것이었다. 무엇이 한 사람으로 하여금 다른 사람을 그토록 감시하게 만들었을까? 안방에 누운 그 소중한 환자에게 누가 더 정성을 쏟느냐를 두고 벌어진, 의심할 바 없이 너그러운 경쟁이었다. 리베카는 나와서 두 사람을 다 위로하곤 했다. 아니, 정확히는 둘 중 어느 한쪽을 말이다. 이 점잖은 두 신사는 그 작은 심복 전령으로부터 환자의 소식을 듣고 싶어 안달이 나 있었던 것이다.\n\n만찬 자리에서, 그녀는 반 시간 정도 아래층에 내려와 두 사람 사이의 평화를 지켰다. 그 후 그녀는 밤을 보내러 사라졌고, 그러면 로던은 머드베리에 있는 백오십 연대의 주둔지로 말을 달려갔으며, 그의 아버지는 호록스 씨와 럼주를 탄 물을 벗 삼아 남았다. 그녀는 누구든 크롤리 양의 병실에서 보낸 적이 없을 만큼 진을 빼는 보름을 보냈다. 그러나 그녀의 가냘픈 신경은 쇠로 되어 있는 듯, 병실의 의무와 따분함에도 조금도 흔들리지 않았다.\n\n그 의무가 얼마나 고통스러웠는지, 그 유쾌하던 노부인이 환자로서 얼마나 짜증스러웠는지, 얼마나 화를 잘 냈는지, 얼마나 잠을 못 이루었는지, 얼마나 죽음의 공포에 시달렸는지, 또 얼마나 긴 밤들을 신음하며, 건강할 때는 까맣게 무시하던 저세상을 두고 거의 헛소리에 가까운 고뇌에 빠져 누워 있었는지를, 그녀는 한참 뒤에야 비로소 털어놓았다. 오, 어여쁜 젊은 독자여, 속물적이고 이기적이고 천박하고 은혜를 모르고 신앙심도 없는 노파가 고통과 두려움에 몸부림치며, 그것도 가발마저 벗겨진 채 누운 모습을 한번 상상해 보라. 그 모습을 그려 보고, 그대 늙기 전에 사랑하고 기도하는 법을 배우라!\n\n샤프는 그 천박한 병상을 불굴의 인내로 지켜보았다. 그 무엇도 그녀의 눈을 벗어나지 못했고, 그녀는 분별 있는 집사처럼 무엇이든 쓸모를 찾아냈다. 훗날 그녀는 크롤리 양의 병에 관한 그럴듯한 이야기를 숱하게 들려주었는데, 그것은 그 부인이 인공적인 분홍 화장 너머로 얼굴을 붉히게 만드는 이야기들이었다. 병간호를 하는 동안 그녀는 결코 신경질을 부리지 않았고, 늘 기민했다. 양심이 더없이 깨끗한 덕에 잠도 얕게 들었고, 거의 어느 순간에든 한마디만 일러 주면 그 휴식을 취할 수 있었다. 그래서 그녀의 모습에서는 피로의 흔적을 좀처럼 찾아볼 수 없었다. 얼굴빛이 살짝 더 창백하고 눈가의 그늘이 평소보다 조금 더 거뭇할 뿐, 병실에서 나올 때면 그녀는 언제나 미소를 띠고, 산뜻하고, 단정했으며, 자그마한 실내복과 모자 차림이 가장 멋들어진 야회복 차림 못지않게 맵시 있었다.\n\n대위도 그렇게 여겼고, 어쭙잖은 발작을 일으키며 그녀에게 열을 올렸다. 사랑의 미늘 박힌 화살이 그의 둔한 가죽을 꿰뚫었던 것이다. 여섯 주, 가까이 지냄, 기회, 이것들이 그를 완전히 사로잡고 말았다. 그는 하필이면 이 세상 모든 사람 중에서 목사관에 있는 고모를 속내를 털어놓는 상대로 삼았다. 그녀는 그 일로 그를 놀려 댔다. 그녀는 그의 어리석음을 진작 알아챘던 것이다. 그녀는 그에게 경고했다. 그러고는 마침내, 샤프야말로 영국에서 가장 영리하고 익살맞고 별나고 마음씨 곱고 꾸밈없고 다정한 사람이라고 인정했다. 하지만 로던은 그녀의 마음을 가지고 놀아서는 안 된다, 소중한 크롤리 양께서 그것만큼은 결코 용서하지 않으실 테니까. 크롤리 양 역시 그 자그마한 가정교사에게 홀딱 반해 샤프를 친딸처럼 사랑하셨기 때문이다. 로던은 떠나야 한다, 자기 연대로, 또 그 못된 런던으로 돌아가야지, 가엾고 순진한 처녀의 감정을 가지고 놀아서는 안 된다는 것이었다.\n\n이 마음씨 좋은 부인은 그 가련한 근위병의 처지를 가엾이 여겨, 우리가 앞서 본 대로 그가 목사관에서 샤프 양을 만나고 또 함께 집까지 걸어올 기회를 몇 번이고, 몇 번이고 만들어 주었다. 숙녀 여러분, 어떤 부류의 남자들은 사랑에 빠지면, 자기를 낚으려는 그 낚싯바늘과 낚싯줄과 온갖 장치를 빤히 보면서도 기어이 미끼를 덥석 무는 법이다. 그들은 다가올 수밖에 없고, 삼킬 수밖에 없으며, 이내 낚여 올라와 가쁜 숨을 헐떡이게 마련이다. 로던은 뷰트 부인이 리베카로 자신을 사로잡으려는 속셈이 빤히 드러나는 것을 보았다. 그는 그리 영리하지는 않았으나, 도시 물을 먹은 사내였고 사교철도 여러 번 겪어 본 터였다. 뷰트 부인의 어떤 말 한마디를 통해, 그의 둔한 영혼에 한 줄기 빛이 비쳤다고 그는 생각했다.\n\n\"내 말 명심해, 로던.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"자네는 언젠가 샤프 양을 친척으로 두게 될 거야.\"\n\n\"무슨 친척요? 제 사촌형수요, 뷰트 부인? 제임스가 그 여자한테 반했나요?\" 익살맞은 장교가 물었다.\n\n\"그 이상이지.\" 뷰트 부인이 검은 눈을 번뜩이며 말했다.\n\n\"피트 형은 아니죠? 형은 그 여자를 가질 수 없어요. 그 비열한 작자한테는 과분한 여자라고요. 형은 제인 십섕크스 아가씨와 약혼이 정해져 있잖아요.\"\n\n\"자네들 남자들은 아무것도 못 알아챈다니까. 이 어리석고 눈먼 양반아. 크롤리 부인에게 무슨 일이라도 생기면, 샤프 양은 자네 계모가 될 거야. 바로 그렇게 될 거라고.\"\n\n로던 크롤리 귀하는 이 소식에 놀랐다는 표시로 어마어마한 휘파람을 길게 내뿜었다. 그는 그것을 부인할 수 없었다. 아버지가 샤프 양을 노골적으로 마음에 들어 하는 것을 그도 진작 눈치챘던 것이다. 그는 그 노신사의 성품을 잘 알았다. 그토록 파렴치한 늙은… 빌어먹을… 그는 말을 끝맺지 않고, 콧수염을 꼬며 집으로 걸어갔다. 뷰트 부인의 수수께끼를 풀 실마리를 찾았다고 확신하면서 말이다.\n\n\"이런, 너무하잖아.\" 로던은 생각했다. \"정말 너무해! 저 여자는 그 가엾은 처녀가 크롤리 부인으로 집안에 들어오지 못하게 하려고, 차라리 그 애를 망쳐 버리고 싶은 거야.\"\n\n그는 리베카와 단둘이 있을 때, 특유의 멋들어진 태도로 아버지가 그녀에게 빠진 일을 두고 놀려 댔다. 그녀는 도도하게 고개를 쳐들고 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 쳐다보며 말했다.\n\n\"그래요, 그분이 나를 좋아한다고 칩시다. 그러시는 걸 나도 알아요, 다른 분들도요. 내가 그분을 무서워하는 줄 아세요, 크롤리 대위님? 내가 내 명예 하나 못 지킬 줄 아세요?\" 그 작은 여자가 여왕처럼 위엄 있는 모습으로 말했다.\n\n\"오, 아, 글쎄, 그저 미리 일러두는 거예요. 조심하라고요, 그뿐이에요.\" 콧수염을 만지작거리는 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 떳떳하지 못한 무언가를 넌지시 비추시는 거군요?\" 그녀가 발끈하며 말했다.\n\n\"오, 이런, 정말이지, 리베카 양.\" 그 둔한 용기병이 끼어들었다.\n\n\"내가 가난하고 의지할 데 없다고 해서, 또 부자들에게 그런 게 없다고 해서, 나에게도 자존심이라는 게 없는 줄 아세요? 내가 가정교사라고 해서, 햄프셔의 점잖으신 양반들만큼 분별도, 감정도, 교양도 없는 줄 아세요? 나는 몽모랑시 가문 사람이에요. 몽모랑시 가문이 크롤리 가문만 못한 줄 아세요?\"\n\n샤프 양은 흥분해서 외가 친척들을 들먹일 때면, 아주 살짝 이국적인 억양으로 말했는데, 그것이 그녀의 또랑또랑하고 낭랑한 목소리에 큰 매력을 더해 주었다. \"아니요.\" 그녀가 대위를 향해 점점 더 격해지며 말을 이었다. \"나는 가난은 견딜 수 있어도 치욕은 못 견뎌요. 무시는 견뎌도 모욕은 못 견뎌요. 게다가 다른 누구도 아닌… 당신에게서 받는 모욕은요.\"\n\n복받친 감정에 그녀는 무너져 내려 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"제기랄, 샤프 양, 리베카, 맹세코, 정말이지, 천 파운드를 준대도 그런 뜻은 아니었어요. 그만, 리베카!\"\n\n그녀는 가 버렸다. 그날 그녀는 크롤리 양과 함께 마차를 타고 외출했다. 크롤리 양이 병에 걸리기 전의 일이었다. 만찬 자리에서 그녀는 유난히 눈부시고 활기찼으나, 그 굴욕당한, 정신 못 차리는 근위병이 보내는 암시며, 끄덕임이며, 어설픈 항변에는 전혀 아랑곳하지 않았다. 이런 식의 자그마한 접전이 그 짤막한 전투 내내 끊임없이 이어졌는데, 일일이 늘어놓기에는 따분하고 결과도 비슷했다. 크롤리 가의 중기병은 패배에 미쳐 날뛰었고, 날마다 패주했다.\n\n퀸스크롤리의 준남작이 누이의 유산을 잃을지 모른다는 두려움을 눈앞에 두지 않았더라면, 그는 결코 사랑하는 딸들이 그 더없이 값진 가정교사가 베푸는 교육의 축복을 잃도록 내버려 두지 않았으리라. 리베카가 그곳에서 그토록 쓸모 있고 즐거운 존재가 되어 준 덕에, 그녀가 없는 시골의 낡은 저택은 황량한 사막 같았다. 그 작은 비서가 떠나고 나니 피트 경의 편지는 정서되지도 교정되지도 않았고, 장부는 정리되지 않았으며, 집안일과 갖가지 계획도 등한시되었다. 그녀에게 보낸 수많은 편지의 내용과 철자만 보아도, 그런 필경사가 그에게 얼마나 긴요한 존재였는지 쉽게 알 수 있었다. 그 편지들은 그녀에게 돌아와 달라고 애원하고 또 명령하는 것이었다. 거의 날마다 준남작의 무료 우편이 도착했는데, 베키에게 돌아와 달라는 더없이 절박한 간청을 담고 있거나, 방치된 딸들의 교육 상태를 두고 크롤리 양에게 호소하는 애절한 사연을 담고 있었다. 그런 문서에 크롤리 양은 거의 신경을 쓰지 않았다.\n\n브리그스 양이 정식으로 해고된 것은 아니었으나, 말동무로서의 그녀의 자리는 한직이자 웃음거리로 전락했다. 그녀의 벗이라고는 응접실의 살진 스패니얼이거나, 이따금 가정부 방에서 만나는 시무룩한 퍼킨뿐이었다. 또한 노부인이 리베카가 떠난다는 말은 결코 들으려 하지 않았으면서도, 리베카가 파크 레인에 정식으로 자리를 잡은 것도 아니었다. 부유한 사람들이 흔히 그렇듯, 크롤리 양은 아랫사람들에게서 받아 낼 수 있는 만큼 시중을 받아 내다가, 더 이상 쓸모가 없어지면 너그러운 척 작별을 고하는 것이 버릇이었다. 어떤 부류의 부자들에게 감사라는 것은 좀처럼 자연스럽지도, 기대할 만하지도 않은 것이다. 그들은 가난한 이들의 시중을 당연한 자기 몫으로 받아들인다. 그렇다고 오, 가엾은 식객이요 비천한 빌붙이여, 그대 또한 그리 불평할 처지는 못 된다! 부자에 대한 그대의 우정이란 으레 그것이 받는 보답만큼이나 진실한 것이니 말이다. 그대가 사랑하는 것은 돈이지 사람이 아니다. 그러니 크로이소스와 그의 하인이 자리를 바꾼다면, 이 가엾은 악당아, 그대도 알다시피 누가 그대의 충성에서 덕을 볼지는 뻔한 노릇이다.\n\n그리고 나는, 리베카의 꾸밈없음과 부지런함과 상냥함과 지칠 줄 모르는 명랑함에도 불구하고, 그 우정의 보물을 듬뿍 받은 그 약빠른 런던 노부인이 줄곧 자신의 다정한 간병인이자 벗을 어렴풋이 의심하고 있지 않았다고는 장담하지 못하겠다. 세상에 거저 무언가를 해 주는 사람은 없다는 생각이 크롤리 양의 머릿속을 자주 스쳤음이 분명하다. 그녀가 세상을 향한 자신의 감정을 가늠해 본다면, 세상이 자신을 향해 품은 감정도 제법 잘 헤아릴 수 있었을 터이다. 또한 어쩌면 그녀는, 스스로 아무도 아끼지 않는 사람에게는 벗이 없는 것이 인지상정임을 떠올렸을지도 모른다.\n\n어쨌든 그러는 동안 베키는 그녀에게 더없는 위안이자 편의가 되어 주었고, 그래서 그녀는 베키에게 새 드레스 두 벌과 낡은 목걸이와 숄을 주었으며, 새로 마음을 터놓는 그 상대에게 자신의 모든 절친한 지인들을 헐뜯음으로써 우정을 표시했고(이보다 더 가슴 뭉클한 호의의 증거는 없는 법이다), 막연하나마 장차 베키에게 큰 은혜를 베풀 궁리를 했다. 이를테면 약제사 클럼프와 짝지어 주거나, 무언가 유리한 살길에 정착시켜 주거나, 아니면 적어도 그녀가 더 이상 필요 없어지고 런던 사교철이 본격적으로 시작되면 퀸스크롤리로 돌려보내는 것이었다.\n\n크롤리 양이 회복기에 접어들어 응접실로 내려오게 되자, 베키는 그녀에게 노래를 불러 주고 이런저런 방법으로 그녀를 즐겁게 해 주었다. 그녀가 마차로 외출할 만큼 건강해지자 베키도 동행했다. 그런데 그 숱한 외출 가운데, 크롤리 양의 그 갸륵한 너그러움과 우정이 그녀를 이끌어 기어이 발을 들여놓게 한 곳이, 이 세상 그 많은 곳 중에 하필 어디였겠는가. 바로 블룸즈버리의 러셀 광장, 존 세들리 귀하의 집이었다.\n\n그 일이 있기에 앞서, 능히 짐작할 수 있듯, 두 절친한 벗 사이에 숱한 편지가 오갔다. 리베카가 햄프셔에 머무는 몇 달 동안, 그 영원한 우정은(인정해야 할까?) 상당히 시들어, 노쇠한 나머지 늙고 쇠약해져 아예 숨을 거둘 지경에 이르렀던 것이다. 사실인즉 두 처녀 모두 골몰할 자기만의 진짜 관심사가 있었다. 리베카에게는 고용주들 사이에서 자기 입지를 다지는 일이, 애밀리아에게는 그녀 자신을 온통 사로잡은 화제가 있었다. 두 처녀가 만나 젊은 숙녀들이 서로를 대할 때 특유의 그 격정으로 와락 부둥켜안았을 때, 리베카는 더없이 활기차고 힘차게 자기 몫의 포옹을 해냈다. 가엾은 어린 애밀리아는 벗에게 입맞추며 얼굴을 붉혔는데, 자신이 그녀에게 어딘가 냉담함에 가까운 잘못을 저질러 온 것은 아닐까 싶어서였다.\n\n두 사람의 첫 만남은 그저 아주 짧은 것이었다. 애밀리아는 막 산책을 나서려던 참이었다. 크롤리 양은 아래층에서 마차에 앉아 기다리고 있었는데, 그녀의 하인들은 자기들이 와 있는 동네에 어리둥절해하며, 블룸즈버리의 흑인 하인인 정직한 샘보를 마치 이 지방의 진기한 토박이라도 되는 양 빤히 쳐다보았다. 그러나 애밀리아가 그 다정한 미소 띤 얼굴로 내려왔을 때(리베카가 그녀를 자기 벗에게 소개해야 했다. 크롤리 양은 그녀를 몹시 보고 싶어 했으나, 너무 편찮아 마차에서 내릴 수가 없었다), 그러니까 내 말은, 애밀리아가 내려왔을 때, 파크 레인의 견장 단 귀족 하인 무리는 이런 사람이 블룸즈버리에서 나올 수 있다는 데에 점점 더 놀라워했다. 그리고 크롤리 양은 자기 벗의 보호자에게 인사를 드리러 그토록 수줍고도 우아하게 다가오는 그 젊은 숙녀의, 발그레 물든 사랑스러운 얼굴에 그야말로 마음을 빼앗기고 말았다.\n\n\"어쩜 저런 살결이람, 얘야! 어쩜 저리 고운 목소리람!\" 그 짤막한 만남이 끝난 뒤 일행이 서쪽으로 마차를 달릴 때 크롤리 양이 말했다. \"샤프 양, 네 어린 벗은 정말 매력적이구나. 그 애를 파크 레인으로 부르렴, 알겠지?\" 크롤리 양은 안목이 있었다. 그녀는 자연스러운 몸가짐을 좋아했고, 약간의 수줍음은 그 몸가짐을 한층 돋보이게 할 뿐이었다. 그녀는 예쁜 그림과 고운 도자기를 좋아하듯, 자기 곁에 예쁜 얼굴을 두기를 좋아했다. 그날 그녀는 애밀리아 이야기를 예닐곱 번이나 황홀하게 늘어놓았다. 고모의 닭고기를 함께 들겠다고 효성스레 찾아온 로던 크롤리에게도 그녀 이야기를 꺼냈다.\n\n물론 이 말에 리베카는 즉시, 애밀리아가 어느 중위와, 오스본 중위라는 아주 오래된 옛 연인과 약혼한 사이라고 일러 주었다.\n\n\"전열 보병 연대 소속의 사내인가?\" 크롤리 대위가, 근위병답게 한참 애를 쓴 끝에 그 연대 번호 제몇 연대를 떠올리며 물었다.\n\n리베카는 그 연대가 맞는 것 같다고 했다. \"그 대위 이름은,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"도빈 대위였어요.\"\n\n\"껑충하고 어설픈 친구지.\" 크롤리가 말했다. \"아무한테나 부딪치고 다니는. 내가 그 친구를 알아. 그리고 오스본은 검은 구레나룻이 무성한, 제법 잘생긴 친구지?\"\n\n\"엄청나죠.\" 리베카 샤프 양이 말했다. \"그리고 장담컨대, 그 구레나룻을 엄청나게 자랑스러워하는걸요.\"\n\n크롤리 대위는 대답 삼아 말처럼 껄껄 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 숙녀들이 설명해 보라고 다그치자, 그 웃음의 폭발이 가라앉은 뒤에야 입을 열었다. \"그 친구는 자기가 당구를 칠 줄 안다고 여기더라고요.\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 코코아트리에서 그 친구한테 이백 파운드를 땄어요. 그 친구가 당구를 친다니, 그 풋내기가 말이죠! 그날 무엇을 걸고든 칠 기세였는데, 그 친구 도빈 대위가 데려가 버렸지 뭐예요, 빌어먹을 작자 같으니!\"\n\n\"로던, 로던, 그렇게 못되게 굴지 마라.\" 크롤리 양이 무척 흐뭇해하며 한마디 했다.\n\n\"아니, 고모, 근위대 밖에서 본 젊은 친구들 중에서 이 친구가 제일 풋내기인 것 같아요. 타퀸이랑 듀세이스가 마음껏 돈을 우려내고 있죠. 그 친구는 귀족과 함께 있는 모습을 보이려고 지옥에라도 갈 위인이에요. 그리니치에서 그자들 만찬값을 다 치르는데, 정작 손님은 그자들이 부른다니까요.\"\n\n\"손님들도 아주 훌륭하겠구나, 짐작건대.\"\n\n\"바로 맞혔어요, 샤프 양. 늘 그렇듯 정확해요, 샤프 양. 더없이 훌륭한 손님들이죠, 하, 하!\" 대위는 자기가 그럴듯한 농담을 했다고 여기며 점점 더 크게 웃어 댔다.\n\n\"로던, 짓궂게 굴지 말렴!\" 고모가 외쳤다.\n\n\"뭐, 그 친구 아버지가 시내 상인인데, 어마어마한 부자라더군요. 그 시내 작자들이란, 빌어먹을, 단단히 피를 봐야 마땅하죠. 그리고 그 친구는 아직 끝난 게 아니에요, 장담하죠. 하, 하!\"\n\n\"저런, 크롤리 대위. 내가 애밀리아에게 일러 줘야겠구나. 노름꾼 남편이라니!\"\n\n\"끔찍하죠, 안 그래요?\" 대위가 자못 엄숙하게 말하더니, 문득 어떤 생각이 떠올라 덧붙였다. \"이런, 그렇지, 고모, 그 친구를 여기로 부릅시다.\"\n\n\"점잖은 자리에 내놓을 만한 사람이냐?\" 고모가 물었다.\n\n\"내놓을 만하냐고요? 오, 더없이요. 차이를 못 느끼실걸요.\" 크롤리 대위가 대답했다. \"고모가 손님을 몇 분 맞기 시작하면 꼭 부릅시다. 그리고 그 친구 뭐라더라, 그 친구의 정인 말이죠. 안 그래요, 샤프 양? 그렇게 부른다죠? 그 아가씨도 오라고 하고요. 이런, 내가 그 친구한테 편지를 써서 부를게요. 그리고 그 친구가 당구만큼 피켓도 잘 치는지 한번 시험해 봐야지. 그 친구가 어디 산다고 했죠, 샤프 양?\"\n\n샤프 양은 크롤리에게 그 중위의 도시 주소를 일러 주었다. 이 대화가 있은 지 며칠 뒤, 오스본 중위는 로던 대위의 학생 같은 필체로 쓰인, 크롤리 양의 초대장을 동봉한 편지를 받았다.\n\n리베카는 또한 자신의 사랑하는 애밀리아에게도 초대장을 띄웠는데, 조지가 그 모임에 끼게 된다는 말을 들은 그녀가 흔쾌히 그것을 받아들였으리라는 것은 능히 짐작할 수 있다. 애밀리아는 아침나절을 파크 레인의 숙녀들과 보내기로 정해졌고, 그곳에서 다들 그녀에게 무척 친절했다. 리베카는 침착한 우월감으로 그녀를 보살피듯 대했다. 둘 중에 그녀가 훨씬 영리했고, 그녀의 벗은 어찌나 온순하고 겸손한지 누구든 명령하려 들면 늘 따랐던 터라, 리베카의 분부도 더없이 순순하고 기분 좋게 받아들였다. 크롤리 양의 자상함 또한 눈여겨볼 만했다. 그녀는 어린 애밀리아를 두고 황홀해하기를 그치지 않았고, 그녀 면전에서 마치 인형이나 하인이나 그림 이야기라도 하듯 그녀에 대해 떠들어 대며, 더없이 자애로운 경탄으로 그녀를 감탄해 마지않았다. 나는 점잖은 세상이 이따금 평민에게 베푸는 그런 감탄을 흠모해 마지않는다. 인생에서 메이페어 사람들이 황송하게 몸을 낮추는 광경만큼 흐뭇한 구경거리도 없는 법이다. 크롤리 양의 어마어마한 자애로움은 가엾은 어린 애밀리아를 차라리 지치게 만들었으니, 나는 파크 레인의 세 숙녀 중에서 그녀가 정직한 브리그스 양을 가장 마음에 들어 한 것은 아닐까 싶다. 그녀는 푸대접받거나 온순한 모든 이들에게 그러하듯 브리그스에게도 공감했던 것이다. 그녀는 이른바 기개 있는 여자가 못 되었다.\n\n조지가 만찬에 왔는데, 크롤리 대위와 함께하는 남자들만의 식사였다.\n\n오스본가의 큼직한 가족용 마차가 그를 러셀 광장에서 파크 레인까지 실어 날랐다. 정작 자신들은 초대받지 못해 그 푸대접에 짐짓 더없이 무관심한 척하던 그 집 젊은 숙녀들도, 준남작 명감에서 피트 크롤리 경의 이름을 들여다보고는, 그 책이 가르쳐 줄 수 있는 크롤리 가문과 그 혈통, 그들의 친척인 빙키 가문 따위에 관한 모든 것을 죄다 익혔다. 로던 크롤리는 조지 오스본을 더없이 솔직하고 자상하게 맞이했다. 그의 당구 솜씨를 칭찬하고, 언제 설욕전을 치를 거냐고 물었으며, 오스본의 연대에 관심을 보였고, 바로 그날 저녁에라도 피켓을 한판 청할 참이었다. 그러나 크롤리 양이 자기 집에서의 도박은 일절 금했던 터라, 적어도 그날만큼은 젊은 중위의 지갑이 그 호기로운 후원자의 손에 가벼워지지는 않았다. 그래도 두 사람은 이튿날 어디선가 만나기로 약속을 잡았다. 크롤리가 팔려고 내놓은 말을 보고, 그것을 공원에서 한번 몰아 보고, 함께 만찬을 들고, 유쾌한 친구들과 저녁을 보내자는 것이었다. \"그러니까, 자네가 그 예쁜 세들리 양에게 매여 있지만 않다면 말이지.\" 크롤리가 의미심장하게 윙크하며 말했다. \"그래도 정말 굉장히 예쁜 아가씨야, 명예를 걸고, 오스본.\" 그는 친절하게도 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"돈도 두둑하겠지, 어때?\"\n\n오스본은 매여 있지 않았다. 그는 기꺼이 크롤리와 어울리겠다고 했다. 이튿날 두 사람이 만나자 크롤리는 새 벗의 승마 솜씨를 칭찬했는데, 이는 완벽히 정직하게 할 수 있는 칭찬이었다. 그러고는 그를 최신 유행의 멋쟁이 젊은이 서너 명에게 소개했는데, 그들과 알게 된 일에 그 순박한 젊은 장교는 한껏 우쭐해졌다.\n\n\"그나저나 그 어린 샤프 양은 어떻게 지내나?\" 오스본이 포도주를 들며 멋쟁이인 척하는 태도로 벗에게 물었다. \"마음씨 고운 아가씨지. 퀸스크롤리에서 자네한테 잘 맞나? 세들리 양이 작년에 그 아가씨를 무척 좋아했지.\"\n\n크롤리 대위는 그 작은 푸른 눈으로 중위를 사납게 쏘아보더니, 그가 일어나 그 어여쁜 가정교사와 다시 인사를 나누러 다가가는 동안 그를 지켜보았다. 만일 그 근위병의 가슴속에 조금이라도 질투심이 있었다면, 그녀의 처신은 분명 크롤리를 안심시켰을 것이다.\n\n두 젊은이가 위층으로 올라가, 오스본이 크롤리 양에게 인사를 드린 뒤, 그는 보살피는 듯한 느긋한 거드름을 부리며 리베카에게 다가갔다. 그는 그녀에게 친절을 베풀고 그녀를 감싸 줄 작정이었다. 심지어 애밀리아의 벗이라는 명목으로 그녀와 악수까지 할 생각이었다. 그래서 \"아, 샤프 양! 어떻게 지내요?\" 하고 말하며, 그녀가 그 영광에 어쩔 줄 몰라 하리라 기대하면서 왼손을 그녀에게 내밀었다.\n\n샤프 양은 오른손 집게손가락을 내밀더니, 어찌나 싸늘하고 사람 잡는 듯한 가벼운 목례를 보내던지, 옆방에서 이 광경을 지켜보던 로던 크롤리는 중위가 완전히 당황하는 꼴을 보며 터져 나오는 웃음을 참기 어려웠다. 그가 움찔하는 모양새며, 멈칫하는 모양새며, 마침내 황송하게도 그 포옹을 위해 내밀어진 손가락 하나를 잡을 때의 그 완벽한 어색함이라니.\n\n\"맹세코, 저 여자라면 악마도 이기겠어!\" 대위가 황홀해하며 말했다. 한편 중위는 대화의 물꼬를 트느라, 리베카에게 새 일자리가 마음에 드느냐고 상냥하게 물었다.\n\n\"제 일자리요?\" 샤프 양이 싸늘하게 말했다. \"그걸 일깨워 주시다니 참 친절도 하셔라! 그런대로 괜찮은 자리예요. 급료도 제법 좋고요. 러셀 광장 댁 누이분들을 가르치는 워트 양만큼은 못 되는 것 같지만요. 그 젊은 숙녀분들은 어떻게 지내세요? 제가 여쭐 처지는 아니지만요.\"\n\n\"왜 그런 말씀을?\" 오스본 씨가 어리둥절해하며 말했다.\n\n\"아, 그분들은 제가 애밀리아네 묵는 동안 제게 말을 거시거나 댁으로 청하시는 일이 한 번도 없으셨거든요. 하지만 우리 같은 가난한 가정교사들은, 아시다시피, 이런 푸대접에 익숙하답니다.\"\n\n\"이런, 샤프 양!\" 오스본이 탄식하듯 내뱉었다.\n\n\"적어도 어떤 집안에서는 그렇다는 거예요.\" 리베카가 말을 이었다. \"그래도 그 차이가 얼마나 큰지 모르실 거예요. 우리 햄프셔 사람들은 당신 같은 운 좋은 시내 분들만큼 부유하진 않아요. 하지만 그래도 저는 신사의 집안에, 유서 깊은 옛 영국 가문에 있는걸요. 피트 경의 부친께서 작위를 사양하셨다는 건 아실 테죠. 그러니 제가 어떻게 대접받는지 보세요. 저는 꽤 편안하답니다. 정말이지 제법 좋은 자리예요. 그나저나 이렇게 물어봐 주시다니 어쩜 그리 친절하셔라!\"\n\n오스본은 그야말로 약이 올랐다. 그 자그마한 가정교사가 그를 깔보듯 대하고 비아냥거리는 통에, 이 젊은 영국 사자는 자못 거북해졌다. 게다가 그는 이 더없이 유쾌한 대화에서 빠져나올 핑계를 찾을 만큼의 침착함조차 끌어모으지 못했다.\n\n\"나는 당신이 시내 집안들을 제법 좋아하는 줄 알았는데요.\" 그가 거만하게 말했다.\n\n\"작년 말씀이시군요, 제가 그 끔찍하고 천박한 학교에서 갓 나왔을 때요? 물론 좋아했죠. 어느 처녀가 방학을 맞아 집에 돌아가는 걸 안 좋아하겠어요? 그리고 제가 어떻게 그보다 나은 걸 알았겠어요? 하지만 오, 오스본 씨, 일 년 반의 경험이 얼마나 큰 차이를 만드는지 몰라요! 그 일 년 반을, 이런 말씀 드려 죄송하지만, 신사분들과 함께 보냈으니까요. 사랑하는 애밀리아로 말하면, 그 애야말로 진주 같은 사람이고 어디에 있든 매력적일 거라는 점은 인정해요. 자, 이제 보니 기분이 좀 풀리기 시작하시네요. 하지만 오, 그 별나고 괴상한 시내 사람들이라니! 그리고 조스 씨는요, 그 굉장하신 조지프 씨는 어떻게 지내세요?\"\n\n\"제가 보기엔 당신도 작년에는 그 굉장하신 조지프 씨를 싫어하지 않으셨던 것 같은데요.\" 오스본이 상냥하게 말했다.\n\n\"어쩜 그리 짓궂으세요! 뭐, 우리끼리 이야기지만, 그분 때문에 제 가슴이 찢어지진 않았어요. 하지만 만약 그분이 당신 그 눈빛이 뜻하는 바를(아주 의미심장하고 친절한 눈빛이기도 하죠) 제게 청했더라면, 저는 싫다고 하진 않았을 거예요.\"\n\n오스본 씨는 \"거참, 어찌나 고마우신지!\" 하고 말하는 듯한 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"저를 처제로 두었더라면 얼마나 영광이었을까, 그렇게 생각하시는 거죠? 조지 오스본 귀하의 처제가 된다니, 존 오스본 귀하의 아드님이자, 또 누구의 아드님이시더라, 오스본 씨 할아버님은 무엇 하시던 분이셨죠? 자, 화내지 마세요. 자기 혈통이야 어쩔 수 없는 노릇이고, 저도 제가 조 세들리 씨와 결혼했더라면 좋았으리라는 데에 전적으로 동감이에요. 가난하고 한 푼 없는 처녀가 그보다 더 잘할 수 있었겠어요? 자, 이제 비밀을 죄다 아셨네요. 저는 솔직하고 거리낌이 없는 사람이에요. 모든 걸 따져 볼 때, 그 일을 들먹여 주시다니 참 친절하셨어요. 참 친절하고 정중하셨죠. 애밀리아, 얘, 오스본 씨하고 내가 마침 네 가엾은 오라버니 조지프 이야기를 하던 참이야. 그분은 어떻게 지내시니?\"\n\n이리하여 조지는 완전히 패퇴하고 말았다. 리베카가 옳았다는 게 아니라, 그녀가 그를 그른 처지로 몰아넣는 데 더없이 성공적으로 수완을 발휘했다는 말이다. 이제 그는 부끄럽게도 달아나고 말았으니, 일 분만 더 머물렀다가는 애밀리아 앞에서 바보 꼴이 되고 말리라는 느낌이 들었기 때문이다.\n\n리베카가 자기보다 한 수 위였음에도, 조지는 숙녀를 두고 고자질을 하거나 보복을 할 만큼 비열한 위인은 아니었다. 다만 그는 이튿날 크롤리 대위에게 리베카 양에 관한 자기 나름의 견해를 슬쩍 털어놓지 않을 수 없었을 뿐이다. 그녀가 만만치 않은 여자요, 위험한 여자요, 지독한 바람둥이라는 둥 말이다. 크롤리는 그 모든 견해에 웃으며 동의했고, 그 견해 하나하나는 스물네 시간이 채 지나기도 전에 리베카 양의 귀에 죄다 들어갔다. 그 견해들은 오스본 씨에 대한 그녀의 본래 감정에 한층 무게를 더했다. 자신의 첫 연애 행각의 성공을 가로막은 이가 바로 조지였다고 그녀의 여자다운 직감이 일러 주었던 터라, 그녀는 그에 걸맞게 그를 높이 평가했던 것이다.\n\n\"내 그저 미리 일러두는 건데,\" 그가 의미심장한 눈빛으로 로던 크롤리에게 말했다. 그는 말을 사들였고 만찬 후에 몇십 기니를 잃은 참이었다. \"내 그저 일러두지. 난 여자들을 잘 알거든. 그러니 자네는 조심하는 게 좋을 거야.\"\n\n\"고맙네, 이 친구야.\" 크롤리가 유난히 고마워하는 표정으로 말했다. \"보아하니 자네는 빈틈이 없구먼.\" 그러자 조지는 크롤리가 정말 옳다고 여기며 자리를 떴다.\n\n그는 애밀리아에게 자기가 한 일을 들려주었다. 자기가 그 더없이 솔직하고 시원시원한 친구 로던 크롤리에게, 그 음흉하고 잔꾀 부리는 어린 리베카를 경계하라고 일러 주었노라고 말이다.\n\n\"누구를요?\" 애밀리아가 외쳤다.\n\n\"네 친구 그 가정교사 말이야. 그렇게 놀란 표정 짓지 마.\"\n\n\"오, 조지, 무슨 짓을 하신 거예요?\" 애밀리아가 말했다. 사랑이 예리하게 만들어 준 그녀의 여자다운 눈은, 크롤리 양에게도, 가엾은 노처녀 브리그스에게도, 무엇보다 저 구레나룻 기른 젊은 잘난 척쟁이 오스본 중위의 멍청한 눈깔에게도 보이지 않던 비밀을 한순간에 알아채고 만 것이다.\n\n리베카가 위층 어느 방에서 그녀에게 숄을 둘러 주던 차에, 그 두 벗은 여자들의 삶에 더없는 즐거움이 되어 주는 그 은밀한 속닥임과 모의를 나눌 기회를 얻었는데, 그때 애밀리아가 리베카에게 다가가 그녀의 작은 두 손을 자기 손에 감싸 쥐고 말했다. \"리베카, 나 다 알아.\"\n\n리베카는 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다.\n\n그리고 이 즐거운 비밀에 대해서는, 두 젊은 여자 중 누구도 더 이상 단 한마디도 입에 올리지 않았다. 그러나 그것은 머지않아 드러날 운명이었다.\n\n위의 사건들이 있은 지 얼마 지나지 않아, 리베카 샤프 양이 여전히 자신이 모시는 부인의 파크 레인 저택에 머물고 있을 무렵, 그레이트곤트 거리에서는 그 음울한 동네를 으레 장식하곤 하는 숱한 상문 가운데 또 하나의 상문이 내걸린 것을 볼 수 있었다. 그것은 피트 크롤리 경의 저택 위에 걸려 있었으나, 그 갸륵한 준남작의 별세를 알리는 것은 아니었다. 그것은 여인의 상문이었고, 실은 몇 해 전에 피트 경의 노모인 고 크롤리 노부인을 위한 장례의 예로 쓰였던 것이었다. 그 쓰임의 기간이 끝나자 상문은 저택 정면에서 내려져, 피트 경 저택의 어딘가 뒤편 살림 공간에 들어앉아 은퇴 생활을 하고 있었다. 그것이 이제 가엾은 로즈 도슨을 위해 다시 모습을 드러낸 것이다. 피트 경은 다시 홀아비가 되었다. 방패 위에 그의 문장과 나란히 사등분되어 새겨진 문장은, 물론 가엾은 로즈의 것이 아니었다. 그녀에게는 문장이 없었다. 그러나 그 문장 방패에 그려진 천사들은 피트 경의 모친에게나 그녀에게나 무난히 들어맞았고, 문장 아래에는 크롤리 가의 비둘기와 뱀을 양옆에 거느린 채 \"레수르감\"이 적혀 있었다. 문장과 상문, 그리고 레수르감이라니. 여기 교훈을 새길 만한 기회가 있도다!\n\n크롤리 씨는 그 외로운 병상을 지켜 주었다. 그녀는 그가 베풀 수 있는 그런 말과 위로에 힘을 얻어 세상을 떠났다. 여러 해 동안 그가 베푼 친절만이 그녀가 아는 유일한 친절이었고, 그 가냘프고 외로운 영혼을 그나마 달래 준 유일한 우정이었다. 그녀의 마음은 육신이 죽기 한참 전에 이미 죽어 있었다. 그녀는 피트 크롤리 경의 아내가 되려고 그것을 팔아 버렸던 것이다. 허영의 시장에서는 날마다 어머니들과 딸들이 똑같은 거래를 하고 있다.\n\n별세가 일어났을 때, 그녀의 남편은 런던에서 그 수없이 많은 계획 가운데 몇 가지를 챙기느라, 끝도 없는 변호사들과 씨름하느라 바빴다. 그래도 그는 파크 레인에 자주 들르고, 리베카에게 숱한 편지를 띄울 짬은 냈으니, 어머니가 병든 동안 이제 동무 하나 없이 지내게 된 시골의 어린 제자들에게 돌아와 달라고 그녀에게 애원하고, 당부하고, 명령하는 내용이었다. 그러나 크롤리 양은 그녀가 떠난다는 말은 들으려고도 하지 않았다. 런던의 어느 사교계 부인도, 일단 누군가와 어울리는 데 싫증이 나면 크롤리 양만큼 태연히 벗을 저버리는 이가 없었고, 또 그토록 빨리 싫증을 내는 이도 드물었으나, 그 열중이 지속되는 동안만큼은 그녀의 애착이 어마어마했던 터라, 그녀는 여전히 더없는 열의로 리베카에게 매달려 있었던 것이다.\n\n크롤리 부인의 별세 소식은 크롤리 양의 집안에서 예상할 수 있는 정도 이상의 슬픔이나 논평을 불러일으키지 않았다. \"삼일에 열기로 한 모임을 미뤄야겠구나.\" 크롤리 양이 말하더니, 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 덧붙였다. \"내 동생이 염치가 있다면 다시 결혼하진 않겠지.\" \"형이 만약 그러면 무지하게 길길이 날뛸 거예요.\" 로던이 형에 대한 평소의 애정을 담아 한마디 했다. 리베카는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그녀는 집안에서 단연 가장 침통하고 가장 깊이 마음을 쓰는 듯이 보였다. 그날 로던이 떠나기 전에 그녀는 방을 나섰으나, 작별 인사를 마친 그가 막 떠나려던 차에 두 사람은 아래층에서 우연히 마주쳤고, 함께 잠시 이야기를 나누었다.\n\n이튿날, 리베카가 창밖을 바라보다가, 프랑스 소설을 차분히 읽고 있던 크롤리 양을 깜짝 놀라게 했다. 그녀가 다급한 목소리로 외친 것이다. \"피트 경께서 오셨어요, 마님!\" 그리고 이 알림에 뒤이어 준남작의 문 두드리는 소리가 들렸다.\n\n\"얘야, 난 그를 못 만나겠다. 안 만날 거야. 보울스에게 출타 중이라고 하렴. 아니면 아래층으로 내려가서 내가 너무 아파서 아무도 못 맞겠다고 하든지. 지금 이 순간엔 내 신경이 도저히 내 동생을 견뎌 내질 못하겠구나.\" 크롤리 양이 이렇게 외치고는 다시 소설책을 집어 들었다.\n\n\"부인께서 너무 편찮으셔서 어른을 못 뵙겠답니다.\" 리베카가 막 계단을 오르려던 피트 경에게 사뿐사뿐 내려가며 말했다.\n\n\"그게 더 잘됐군.\" 피트 경이 대답했다. \"내가 보고 싶은 건 바로 자네야, 베키 양. 나하고 응접실로 들어가지.\" 그리하여 두 사람은 함께 그 방으로 들어갔다.\n\n\"자네가 퀸스크롤리로 돌아와 줬으면 하네, 양.\" 준남작이 그녀에게 시선을 고정한 채, 검은 장갑과 커다란 검은 상장(喪章)을 두른 모자를 벗으며 말했다. 그의 눈에는 어찌나 묘한 빛이 어려 있던지, 그녀를 어찌나 한결같이 빤히 응시하던지, 리베카 샤프는 그만 떨릴 지경이었다.\n\n\"곧 돌아가고 싶어요.\" 그녀가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"크롤리 양께서 좀 나아지시는 대로요. 그리고 그 사랑스러운 아이들에게로 돌아갈게요.\"\n\n\"자네는 이 석 달 내내 그렇게 말해 왔지, 베키.\" 피트 경이 대답했다. \"그런데도 여태 내 누이한테 매달려 있잖나. 누이는 자네를 다 부려먹고 나면 헌 신짝처럼 내팽개칠 텐데. 내 말하지만 난 자네가 필요해. 난 장례식 치르러 돌아가는 길이야. 돌아오겠나? 예야, 아니야?\"\n\n\"감히… 그럴 수는… 옳지 않을 것… 같아요… 어른과 단둘이 있는 건요.\" 베키가 자못 몹시 동요한 듯이 말했다.\n\n\"다시 말하지만, 난 자네가 필요해.\" 피트 경이 탁자를 쾅 내리치며 말했다. \"자네 없이는 일이 안 돌아가. 자네가 떠나기 전까진 그게 뭔지 몰랐어. 집안이 죄다 엉망이야. 예전 같지가 않아. 내 장부도 죄다 또 뒤죽박죽이 됐어. 자넨 꼭 돌아와야 해. 제발 돌아와. 베키, 제발 돌아와 줘.\"\n\n\"돌아가서… 무엇이 되는 건가요, 어른?\" 리베카가 숨을 몰아쉬며 내뱉었다.\n\n\"원한다면 크롤리 부인이 되어 돌아오게.\" 준남작이 상장 두른 모자를 움켜쥐며 말했다. \"자! 그럼 만족하겠나? 돌아와서 내 아내가 되어 줘. 자넨 그럴 자격이 있어. 가문 따위 집어치워. 자넨 내가 본 그 어떤 숙녀 못지않은 숙녀야. 자네 그 작은 손가락 하나에 든 머리가 이 고을 어느 준남작 부인의 머리보다 낫다고. 돌아오겠나? 예야, 아니야?\"\n\n\"오, 피트 경!\" 리베카가 몹시 마음이 흔들려 말했다.\n\n\"예라고 해, 베키.\" 피트 경이 말을 이었다. \"난 늙은이지만 좋은 사람이야. 앞으로 이십 년은 끄떡없어. 자네를 행복하게 해 주겠네, 두고 보라고. 자넨 하고 싶은 대로 하면 돼. 쓰고 싶은 대로 쓰고. 죄다 자네 마음대로 해. 재산도 떼어 주겠어. 모든 걸 격식대로 해 주겠네. 자, 보라고!\" 그러더니 노인은 무릎을 꿇고 엎드려, 사티로스처럼 음흉하게 그녀를 곁눈질했다.\n\n리베카는 기겁한 모습으로 뒷걸음질 쳤다. 이 이야기를 거쳐 오는 동안 우리는 그녀가 침착성을 잃는 모습을 한 번도 본 적이 없다. 그러나 이번에는 그러고 말았으니, 그녀의 두 눈에서 일찍이 흘러내린 적 없는 더없이 진솔한 눈물을 몇 방울 떨구었던 것이다.\n\n\"오, 피트 경!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"오, 어른… 저는… 저는 이미 결혼한 몸이에요.\"\n\n어떤 감상적 기질을 지닌 독자라면(우리는 다른 부류의 독자는 바라지도 않거니와), 우리의 자그마한 연극의 마지막 막을 매듭짓던 그 활인화 같은 장면에 분명 흐뭇해했을 것이다. 미인 앞에 무릎 꿇은 사랑의 모습보다 더 어여쁜 그림이 어디 있겠는가?\n\n그러나 사랑은 미인의 입에서 자기가 이미 결혼한 몸이라는 그 끔찍한 고백을 듣자, 융단 위에 무릎 꿇었던 겸손한 자세를 박차고 벌떡 일어나며 탄성을 내질렀는데, 그 바람에 가엾은 작은 미인은 고백을 했을 때보다 한층 더 겁에 질리고 말았다. \"결혼이라니, 농담이겠지.\" 분노와 경악이 한바탕 터져 나온 끝에 준남작이 외쳤다. \"나를 놀리는 거지, 베키. 땡전 한 푼 없는 너 같은 애를 누가 결혼 상대로 거두겠어?\"\n\n\"결혼했어요! 결혼했다고요!\" 리베카가 고통스러운 눈물을 흘리며 말했다. 감정에 북받쳐 목이 메고, 어느새 그렁그렁한 두 눈에 손수건을 갖다 대며, 벽난로 선반에 기대 정신을 잃을 듯한 그 비탄의 자태는 더없이 모진 마음마저 녹여 버릴 만한 것이었다. \"오, 피트 경, 피트 경, 저에게 베풀어 주신 그 모든 호의를 제가 고마워하지 않는다고 여기지 말아 주세요. 오로지 경의 너그러우심이 제 비밀을 끌어낸 것이랍니다.\"\n\n\"너그러움은 무슨 빌어먹을!\" 피트 경이 버럭 소리쳤다. \"그럼 도대체 누구하고 결혼한 거야? 어디서 했어?\"\n\n\"저를 시골로 다시 데려가 주세요, 경! 예전처럼 한결같이 충실하게 경을 보살피게 해 주세요! 제발, 제발 저를 그리운 퀸스크롤리에서 떼어 놓지 말아 주세요!\"\n\n\"그놈이 너를 버린 거로구나, 그렇지?\" 준남작이 자기 딴에는 이제 사정을 알아챘다는 듯 말했다. \"그럼 됐다, 베키, 원한다면 돌아오너라. 한 입으로 두말은 못 하는 법이지. 어쨌거나 나는 너한테 정당한 제안을 했어. 가정교사로 돌아오면 돼, 다 네 마음대로 하게 해 주마.\" 그녀는 한 손을 내밀었다. 가슴이 찢어지도록 울었다. 곱슬머리가 얼굴 위로, 또 그녀가 얼굴을 묻은 대리석 벽난로 선반 위로 흘러내렸다.\n\n\"그래서 그 못된 놈이 달아났다는 거지, 응?\" 피트 경이 끔찍할 만큼 어설프게 위로하려 들며 말했다. \"괜찮다, 베키, 내가 너를 돌봐 주마.\"\n\n\"오, 경! 퀸스크롤리로 돌아가, 예전처럼 아이들과 경을 보살피는 것이야말로 제 평생의 자랑이 될 거예요. 경께서 이 작은 리베카의 봉사가 마음에 든다고 말씀하시던 그때처럼요. 방금 저에게 베풀어 주신 그 제안을 생각하면, 정말이지 제 가슴은 감사로 가득 찬답니다. 저는 경의 아내가 될 수는 없어요. 그저, 그저 경의 딸이 되게 해 주세요.\" 이렇게 말하며 리베카는 더없이 비극적인 몸짓으로 무릎을 꿇고는, 피트 경의 거칠고 검은 손을 자신의 두 손(아주 곱고 희며 공단처럼 부드러운 손이었다) 사이에 감싸 쥐고, 더없이 절절한 애원과 신뢰의 표정으로 그 얼굴을 올려다보았다. 그런데 바로 그때, 문이 열리고 크롤리 양이 미끄러지듯 들어왔다.\n\n준남작과 리베카가 방에 들어선 직후 우연히 응접실 문가에 있던 퍼킨 부인과 브리그스 양은, 열쇠 구멍을 통해 노신사가 가정교사 앞에 엎드린 광경을 우연히 보았고, 그가 그녀에게 한 그 너그러운 제안도 들었던 것이다. 그 말이 채 그의 입에서 떨어지기도 전에 퍼킨 부인과 브리그스 양은 계단을 줄달음쳐 올라가, 크롤리 양이 프랑스 소설을 읽고 있던 응접실로 뛰어들어, 피트 경이 무릎을 꿇고 샤프 양에게 청혼하고 있다는 그 기절초풍할 소식을 노부인에게 전했다. 그리고 위의 대화가 오가는 데 걸린 시간이며, 브리그스와 퍼킨이 응접실로 날듯이 달려간 시간이며, 크롤리 양이 깜짝 놀라 피고 르브룅의 책을 떨어뜨린 시간이며, 그녀가 계단을 내려온 시간까지 헤아려 본다면, 이 이야기가 얼마나 정확한지, 또 어찌하여 크롤리 양이 리베카가 막 겸손한 자세를 취한 바로 그 순간에 나타날 수밖에 없었는지 알 수 있을 것이다.\n\n\"무릎을 꿇은 건 신사가 아니라 바닥에 엎드린 숙녀로군요.\" 크롤리 양이 잔뜩 경멸하는 표정과 목소리로 말했다. \"사람들이 그러길 당신이 무릎을 꿇고 있다더군요, 피트 경. 어디 한 번 더 꿇어 보시지요. 이 어여쁜 한 쌍을 좀 구경하게요!\"\n\n\"피트 크롤리 경께 감사를 드렸어요, 부인.\" 리베카가 일어서며 말했다. \"그리고 말씀드렸지요, 저는, 저는 결코 크롤리 부인이 될 수 없다고요.\"\n\n\"거절했다고!\" 크롤리 양이 그 어느 때보다 어리둥절해하며 말했다. 문가의 브리그스와 퍼킨은 경악으로 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨고 입을 떡 벌렸다.\n\n\"네, 거절했어요.\" 리베카가 슬프고 눈물 어린 목소리로 이었다.\n\n\"정말로 자네가 그녀에게 청혼을 했단 말인가, 피트 경? 내 귀를 믿어도 되는 건가요?\" 노부인이 물었다.\n\n\"그려.\" 준남작이 말했다. \"내가 했지.\"\n\n\"그런데 이 애 말대로 거절을 했고요?\"\n\n\"그려.\" 피트 경이 입이 함박만 하게 벌어지도록 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"어쨌거나 가슴이 미어지는 표정은 아니로군요.\" 크롤리 양이 말했다.\n\n\"눈곱만큼도 아니지.\" 피트 경이 어찌나 태연하고 유쾌하게 대답하던지, 크롤리 양은 어리둥절한 나머지 거의 미칠 지경이 되었다. 지체 높은 노신사가 한 푼 없는 가정교사 앞에 무릎을 꿇고, 게다가 그녀가 결혼을 거절했다고 너털웃음을 터뜨리다니, 또 한 푼 없는 가정교사가 연 사천 파운드를 가진 준남작을 거절하다니, 이는 크롤리 양으로서는 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 수수께끼였다. 그녀가 애독하는 피고 르브룅의 그 어떤 복잡한 음모 줄거리보다도 더한 일이었다.\n\n\"오라버니가 이걸 재미난 놀음으로 여기니 다행이군요.\" 크롤리 양이 이 경악 속을 마구잡이로 더듬거리며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"굉장하지.\" 피트 경이 말했다. \"누가 짐작이나 했겠어! 어찌나 앙큼한 계집인지! 어찌나 여우 같은 것인지!\" 그는 흐뭇한 듯 낄낄대며 혼자 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"무엇을 누가 짐작했겠다는 거예요?\" 크롤리 양이 발을 구르며 외쳤다. \"여보세요, 샤프 양, 혹시 섭정 왕세자의 이혼이라도 기다리느라 우리 집안이 자네한테는 성에 차지 않는다는 건가?\"\n\n\"부인께서 들어오셨을 때 제 자세는,\" 리베카가 말했다, \"이 선량한, 이 고결한 분께서 황송하게도 베풀어 주신 그런 영예를 제가 업신여기는 것처럼 보이지는 않았을 거예요. 제게 마음이 없다고 생각하세요? 여러분 모두가 저를 사랑해 주시고, 의지할 데 없는 가엾은 고아 소녀에게 그토록 친절히 대해 주셨는데, 제가 아무것도 느끼지 말아야 하나요? 오, 나의 벗들이여! 오, 나의 은인들이여! 제 사랑과 제 삶과 제 본분으로, 여러분이 베풀어 주신 그 신뢰에 보답해 보려 하면 안 되는 건가요? 크롤리 양, 저에게 감사하는 마음마저도 아까워하시는 건가요? 너무하세요, 제 가슴이 벅차서 견딜 수가 없어요.\" 그러고는 어찌나 애처롭게 의자에 풀썩 주저앉던지, 그 자리에 있던 청중 대부분이 그녀의 슬픔에 완전히 녹아 버렸다.\n\n\"나하고 결혼을 하든 안 하든, 너는 착한 아이야, 베키. 그리고 나는 네 편이라는 걸 잊지 마라.\" 피트 경은 이렇게 말하고는 검은 띠를 두른 모자를 쓰고 걸어 나갔다. 리베카로서는 무척이나 마음이 놓이는 일이었으니, 그녀의 비밀이 크롤리 양에게 아직 드러나지 않았음이 분명했고, 그리하여 잠시나마 유예를 얻은 셈이었기 때문이다.\n\n손수건을 두 눈에 갖다 대고, 따라 올라오려는 착한 브리그스를 고갯짓으로 물리치며 그녀는 자기 방으로 올라갔다. 그동안 브리그스와 크롤리 양은 잔뜩 흥분한 상태로 남아 이 기묘한 사건을 논했고, 못지않게 들뜬 퍼킨은 부엌 영역으로 내려가 거기 있는 남녀 하인들과 그 이야기를 나누었다. 그리고 퍼킨 부인은 이 소식에 어찌나 감동을 받았던지, 바로 그날 밤 우편으로 편지를 부치는 것이 마땅하다 여겨, \"목사관의 뷰트 크롤리 부인과 가족 여러분께 삼가 문안 올리오며, 피트 경께서 샤프 양에게 청혼을 하셨는데, 모두가 놀랍게도 샤프 양이 거절하였다\"고 적어 보냈다.\n\n식당에 있던 두 숙녀(여기서 착한 브리그스 양은 다시금 후원자와 은밀한 대화를 나눌 자격을 얻은 것이 기뻐 어쩔 줄 몰랐다)는 피트 경의 청혼과 리베카의 거절을 두고 마음껏 의아해했다. 브리그스는 무척 예리하게도, 무언가 먼저 정을 둔 상대라는 형태의 장애물이 있는 게 틀림없다고, 그렇지 않고서야 제정신인 젊은 여자가 그토록 유리한 청혼을 거절했을 리 없다고 넌지시 짚었다.\n\n\"브리그스, 당신 같으면 받아들였겠지요, 안 그래요?\" 크롤리 양이 다정하게 말했다.\n\n\"크롤리 양의 올케가 된다면 그야말로 영광이 아니겠어요?\" 브리그스가 얌전하게 둘러대며 대답했다.\n\n\"하긴, 베키라면 그래도 훌륭한 크롤리 부인이 되었을 거예요.\" 크롤리 양이 말했다(그 아이가 거절한 덕분에 마음이 누그러졌고, 이제 자기가 희생을 치를 일이 없어지자 한결 너그럽고 후해진 것이다). \"머리가 비상한 아이지요(가엾은 브리그스, 당신 머릿속 전부보다 그 애 새끼손가락 하나에 든 재치가 훨씬 많다오). 게다가 내가 가르쳐 놓았으니 몸가짐도 더없이 훌륭하고요. 그 애는 몽모랑시 가문 사람이에요, 브리그스. 핏줄이란 게 무언가 있긴 한 법이지요, 나야 그런 걸 경멸하지만 말이에요. 게다가 그 애라면 그 거드름이나 피우는 멍청한 햄프셔 사람들 틈에서, 그 불운한 철물상 딸보다 훨씬 잘 제 몫을 해냈을 거예요.\"\n\n브리그스는 늘 그렇듯 맞장구를 쳤고, 이어 \"먼저 정을 둔 상대\"가 누구일지를 두고 갖가지 억측이 오갔다. \"당신처럼 의지할 데 없는 가엾은 사람들은 늘 어리석은 연정 같은 걸 품기 마련이지요.\" 크롤리 양이 말했다. \"당신만 해도 그렇잖아요, 글씨 선생을 사모했었잖아요(울지 말아요, 브리그스, 당신은 늘 운다니까, 운다고 그 사람이 다시 살아나는 것도 아닌데). 이 가엾은 베키도 짐작건대 어리석고 감상적이었던 게지요. 약제사라든가, 집사라든가, 화가라든가, 젊은 부목사라든가, 뭐 그런 부류겠지요.\"\n\n\"가엾어라! 가엾어라!\" 브리그스가 말했다(그녀는 이십사 년 전을, 그리고 그 병약했던 젊은 글씨 선생을 떠올리고 있었다. 그의 노란 머리카락 한 타래와, 알아볼 수 없을 만큼 휘갈겨 쓴 것이 도리어 아름답던 그의 편지들을, 그녀는 위층 낡은 책상 속에 고이 간직하고 있었다). \"가엾어라, 가엾어라!\" 브리그스가 말했다. 그 순간 그녀는 다시 한 번 발그레한 볼을 가진 열여덟 살 처녀가 되었다. 저녁 예배에 나가, 병약한 글씨 선생과 한 권의 찬송가책을 함께 들여다보며 떨리는 목소리로 노래하고 있었다.\n\n\"리베카가 그렇게 처신했으니,\" 크롤리 양이 열의를 담아 말했다, \"우리 집안이 무언가 해 주어야지요. 그 정인이 누구인지 알아봐 줘요, 브리그스. 내가 그 사람한테 가게를 차려 주든가, 알다시피 그 사람한테 내 초상화를 주문하든가, 아니면 내 사촌인 주교한테 말을 넣든가 하겠어요. 그리고 베키한테 지참금을 마련해 주고, 결혼식을 올립시다, 브리그스. 당신이 피로연 음식을 차리고 신부 들러리도 서요.\"\n\n브리그스는 그거 참 즐거운 일이겠다고 단언하고는, 친애하는 크롤리 양은 언제나 다정하고 너그럽다고 거듭 칭송하며, 리베카의 침실로 올라갔다. 그녀를 위로하고, 청혼이며 거절이며 그 까닭에 대해 재잘대고, 크롤리 양의 너그러운 의향을 넌지시 비치고, 또 샤프 양의 마음을 사로잡은 신사가 누구인지 알아내기 위해서였다.\n\n리베카는 무척 다정하고, 무척 살갑고, 또 무척이나 짐짓 꾸민 태도였다. 브리그스가 베푸는 다정함에 감사 어린 열의로 화답했고, 은밀히 정을 둔 상대가 있음을, 달콤한 비밀이 있음을 인정했다. 브리그스 양이 열쇠 구멍에 반 분만 더 붙어 있었더라면 얼마나 좋았으랴! 리베카는 어쩌면 더 많은 이야기를 들려주었을지도 모른다. 그러나 브리그스 양이 리베카의 방에 들어온 지 오 분 만에, 크롤리 양이 친히 그곳에 모습을 드러냈다. 전례 없는 영광이었다. 조바심을 이기지 못한 것이다. 사절로 보낸 이가 더디게 일을 처리하는 꼴을 차마 기다릴 수 없어, 몸소 찾아와 브리그스에게 방에서 나가라 명한 것이었다. 그리고 리베카의 처신을 칭찬하면서, 면담의 자초지종과 피트 경의 그 놀라운 청혼에 이르게 된 그간의 일들을 자세히 캐물었다.\n\n리베카가 말하기를, 피트 경이 자기를 각별히 어여삐 여긴다는 것은 진작부터 어렴풋이 알고 있었다고 했다(그가 워낙 자기 감정을 거리낌 없이 솔직하게 드러내는 버릇이 있었기 때문이다). 그러나 지금으로서는 크롤리 양을 번거롭게 하고 싶지 않은 사적인 이유들은 차치하더라도, 피트 경의 나이와 신분과 생활 습관으로 미루어 결혼은 도저히 불가능한 일이며, 더구나 정인의 죽은 아내 장례조차 아직 치르지 않은 그런 마당에, 조금이라도 자존심과 염치가 있는 여자라면 어찌 청혼 따위에 귀를 기울일 수 있겠느냐고 했다.\n\n\"말도 안 돼, 얘야. 다른 누군가가 끼어 있지 않고서야 네가 그를 거절했을 리 없지.\" 크롤리 양이 단번에 본론으로 들어가며 말했다. \"사적인 이유를 말해 봐. 그 사적인 이유가 뭐냐? 누군가 있구나. 네 마음을 사로잡은 게 누구냐?\"\n\n리베카는 눈을 내리깔고, 그렇다고 인정했다. \"맞히셨어요, 다정한 부인.\" 그녀가 달콤하고 순박하게 더듬거리는 목소리로 말했다. \"이렇게 가난하고 의지할 데 없는 애가 사랑에 빠진 게 의아하시겠지요? 하지만 가난이 사랑을 막아 준다는 말은 들어 본 적이 없는걸요. 그랬으면 좋으련만요.\"\n\n\"가엾은 내 아가야,\" 언제든 감상에 젖을 채비가 되어 있던 크롤리 양이 외쳤다, \"그럼 우리의 연정이 보답을 못 받고 있는 거냐? 우리가 남몰래 애를 태우고 있는 거야? 다 털어놓으렴, 내가 위로해 줄 테니.\"\n\n\"부인께서 그래 주실 수 있다면 좋겠어요.\" 리베카가 똑같이 눈물 어린 어조로 말했다. \"정말이지, 정말이지 위로가 필요하답니다.\" 그러고는 크롤리 양의 어깨에 머리를 기대고 어찌나 자연스럽게 울던지, 얼결에 연민에 사로잡힌 노부인은 거의 어머니 같은 다정함으로 그녀를 끌어안고, 애정과 보살핌을 약속하는 위로의 말을 숱하게 늘어놓으며, 그녀를 친딸처럼 사랑하노라고, 그녀를 위해서라면 할 수 있는 일은 무엇이든 다 하겠노라고 다짐했다. \"자, 그래 그게 누구냐, 얘야? 그 어여쁜 세들리 양의 오라버니냐? 그 사람과 무슨 일이 있다는 듯이 말했었지. 그 사람을 이리로 부르마, 얘야. 그래서 네가 그 사람을 갖게 해 주마. 정말이지 그래 주마.\"\n\n\"지금은 묻지 말아 주세요.\" 리베카가 말했다. \"곧 다 알게 되실 거예요. 정말 그러실 거예요. 다정하고 친절한 크롤리 양, 그리운 벗이라고 불러도 될까요?\"\n\n\"그럼, 되고말고, 얘야.\" 노부인이 그녀에게 입을 맞추며 대답했다.\n\n\"지금은 말씀드릴 수가 없어요.\" 리베카가 흐느끼며 토해 냈다. \"저는 너무나 비참해요. 하지만 오! 언제까지나 저를 사랑해 주세요, 언제까지나 사랑하시겠다고 약속해 주세요.\" 그리고 서로 눈물을 흘리는 가운데(젊은 여자의 격정이 나이 든 여자의 연민을 불러일으켰던 것이다) 크롤리 양은 이 약속을 엄숙히 해 주었고, 자기의 작은 피후견인을 그리운, 천진한, 마음 여린, 정 많은, 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 아이라며 축복하고 감탄하면서 그 곁을 떠났다.\n\n이제 그녀는 홀로 남아, 그날의 갑작스럽고 놀라운 사건들을, 또 있었던 일과 있을 수도 있었던 일들을 곰곰이 되새기게 되었다. 샤프 양, 아니(실례를 무릅쓰자면) 리베카 부인의 속마음이 어떠했으리라고 생각하는가? 몇 쪽 앞에서, 이 글을 쓰는 사람이 애밀리아 세들리 양의 침실을 엿보고, 그 순결한 베개 위에서 일렁이던 온갖 부드러운 아픔과 격정을 소설가의 전지함으로 헤아릴 특권을 주장했다면, 어찌하여 리베카의 속내를 털어놓는 벗이요, 그 비밀의 주인이요, 이 젊은 여자의 양심을 봉인해 두는 자라고 자처하지 못하겠는가?\n\n자, 그렇다면 우선 리베카는, 그토록 굉장한 행운이 코앞까지 와 있었건만 그것을 사양할 수밖에 없었다는 데 대해 자못 진실하고 가슴 저린 회한에 빠져들었다. 올바르게 다스려진 마음을 지닌 사람이라면 누구든 분명 이 자연스러운 심정에 공감할 것이다. 귀부인이 되어 연 사천 파운드를 함께 누릴 수도 있었던, 한 푼 없는 노처녀를 가엾이 여기지 않을 어진 어머니가 어디 있겠는가? 그토록 명예롭고 유리하고 애간장 타는 청혼을, 하필이면 그것을 받아들일 도리가 없는 바로 그 순간에 받게 된, 부지런하고 영리하고 갸륵한 아가씨를 두고 동정심을 느끼지 않을 교양 있는 젊은이가 이 허영의 시장 어디에 있겠는가? 우리의 벗 베키의 낙담은 마땅히 온갖 동정을 받을 만하며, 또 반드시 받게 될 것이다.\n\n언젠가 밤에 나 자신이 이 시장의 어느 야회에 가 있었던 일이 떠오른다. 그 자리에 늙은 토디 양도 와 있는 것을 보았는데, 그녀가 특별한 관심과 아첨의 대상으로 콕 집어낸 이는 변호사의 아내인 자그마한 브리플리스 부인이었다. 분명 그 부인은 양가 출신이긴 하나, 우리 모두가 알다시피 더없이 가난한 처지였다.\n\n토디 양 쪽에서 이런 굽실거림을 보이는 까닭이 무엇일까, 나는 속으로 자문했다. 브리플리스가 무슨 주 법원 자리라도 얻었나, 아니면 그 아내한테 무슨 유산이라도 굴러들어 왔나? 토디 양은 곧, 그녀의 온갖 처신에서 한결같이 드러나는 그 천연덕스러움으로 까닭을 설명해 주었다. \"있잖아요,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"브리플리스 부인이 존 레드핸드 경의 손녀딸이에요. 그분이 첼튼햄에서 어찌나 위중한지 반년도 못 버틸 거래요. 그러면 브리플리스 부인의 아버지가 작위를 잇게 되니, 보다시피 그 부인은 준남작의 딸이 되는 거지요.\" 그러고서 토디는 바로 다음 주에 브리플리스 부부를 만찬에 초대했다.\n\n준남작의 딸이 될 가망만으로도 어떤 부인이 세상에서 그런 떠받듦을 받을 수 있다면, 준남작의 아내가 될 기회를 놓친 젊은 여인의 고뇌야말로 분명히, 분명히 우리가 존중해 마땅하지 않겠는가? 크롤리 부인이 그토록 빨리 세상을 뜰 줄 누가 꿈엔들 알았겠는가? 그 사람은 앞으로 십 년은 더 버틸 법한 그런 병약한 여자였는데, 하고 리베카는 후회의 온갖 비통함 속에서 속으로 생각했다. 그랬다면 나는 귀부인이 되었을 텐데! 그 늙은 양반을 내 마음대로 어디로든 끌고 다닐 수 있었을 텐데. 뷰트 부인의 후원에도 감사 인사를 해 주고, 피트 씨의 그 견딜 수 없는 거드름에도 고개를 숙여 줄 수 있었을 텐데. 런던 저택은 가구며 장식을 새로 들였을 테고. 런던에서 제일 멋진 마차에, 오페라 극장에 전용석도 가졌을 테고, 다음 사교철에는 궁정에 알현도 했을 텐데. 이 모든 것이 가능했으련만. 그런데 이제, 이제는 모든 것이 불안과 수수께끼뿐이었다.\n\n그러나 리베카는, 돌이킬 수 없는 과거를 두고 부질없고 꼴사납게 오래 슬퍼하기에는 너무나 결단력 있고 기개 넘치는 성격의 젊은 숙녀였다. 그리하여 거기에 딱 알맞은 몫의 회한만을 바친 다음, 그녀는 현명하게도 온 정신을 미래로 돌렸으니, 이제 그것이야말로 그녀에게 비할 데 없이 더 중요한 일이었다. 그리고 자신의 처지와, 그 처지에 깃든 희망과 불안과 가능성을 두루 살폈다.\n\n우선 첫째로, 그녀는 결혼한 몸이었다. 그것은 엄연한 사실이었다. 피트 경이 그 사실을 알았다. 그녀가 그 고백을 한 것은 얼결에 몰려서라기보다, 순간적인 계산에 이끌린 것이었다. 언젠가는 밝혀질 수밖에 없는 일이었다. 그러니 나중이 아니라 지금인들 어떠랴? 자기가 직접 그녀와 결혼하려 했던 사람이니, 적어도 그녀의 결혼에 대해서는 입을 다물 수밖에 없을 터였다. 크롤리 양이 그 소식을 어떻게 받아들일까, 그것이 가장 큰 문제였다. 리베카에게 불안이 없지는 않았다. 그러나 그녀는 크롤리 양이 한 말을 죄다 떠올렸다. 노부인이 공공연히 드러내는 가문에 대한 경멸, 그 대담하고 진보적인 견해, 전반적으로 낭만을 좇는 기질, 조카를 향한 거의 맹목적인 애착, 그리고 리베카 자신에게 거듭 표한 다정함을. 이모님은 그 사람을 어찌나 끔찍이 위하는지 무슨 짓이든 용서하고 말 거야, 하고 리베카는 생각했다. 또 나한테도 어찌나 익숙해졌는지, 내가 없으면 영 마음이 편치 않으실걸. 모든 것이 밝혀지고 나면 한바탕 소동이 일고, 히스테리가 터지고, 큰 다툼이 벌어졌다가, 이윽고 큰 화해가 찾아오겠지. 어차피 미룬다고 무슨 소용이 있겠는가? 주사위는 던져졌고, 결과란 지금이든 내일이든 매한가지일 터였다. 그리하여 크롤리 양에게 이 소식을 알리기로 마음을 굳힌 이 젊은 여인은, 그것을 그녀에게 전할 가장 좋은 방도가 무엇일지를 마음속으로 따져 보았다. 닥쳐올 게 뻔한 그 폭풍을 정면으로 맞을 것인가, 아니면 첫 분노가 가라앉을 때까지 달아나 피해 있을 것인가. 이런 궁리에 잠긴 채 그녀는 다음과 같은 편지를 썼다.\n\n더없이 그리운 벗에게.\n\n우리가 그토록 자주 의논했던 그 큰 위기가 닥쳤어요. 제 비밀의 절반이 알려지고 말았어요. 그래서 생각하고 또 생각한 끝에, 이제야말로 그 수수께끼 전부를 밝힐 때라고 확신하게 되었답니다. 오늘 아침 피트 경이 저를 찾아와서, 무엇을 했는지 아세요? 정식으로 청혼을 했답니다. 생각 좀 해 보세요! 가엾은 이 몸이 크롤리 부인이 될 수도 있었던 거예요. 뷰트 부인이 얼마나 흐뭇해했을까요. 그리고 제가 이모님(ma tante)을 제치고 윗자리를 차지했다면 이모님은 또 어땠을까요! 저는 누군가의 어머니가 될 수도 있었던 거예요. 그런데 그 대신에, 오, 우리가 머잖아 모든 걸 털어놓아야 한다는 걸 생각하면, 저는 떨려요, 정말 떨려요!\n\n피트 경은 제가 결혼한 몸이라는 걸 알아요. 다만 상대가 누구인지는 모르니, 아직은 그리 언짢아하지 않아요. 이모님은 제가 그를 거절했다고 정말로 화를 내신답니다. 그래도 더없이 다정하고 너그러우세요. 황송하게도 제가 그분의 좋은 아내가 되었을 거라고 말씀하시고, 당신의 이 작은 리베카에게 어머니가 되어 주겠다고 다짐하세요. 처음 그 소식을 들으면 이모님도 충격을 받으실 테지요. 하지만 한순간의 노여움 너머의 무언가를 우리가 두려워할 필요가 있을까요? 저는 그렇지 않다고 생각해요. 그렇지 않다고 확신해요. 이모님은 당신을 어찌나 끔찍이 위하시는지(이 짓궂고 쓸모없는 양반아), 당신이라면 무슨 짓이든 용서하실 거예요. 그리고 정말이지, 그분 마음속에서 그다음 자리는 제 차지라고 믿어요. 제가 없으면 비참해하실 거예요. 더없이 그리운 이여! 무언가가 제게 일러 주네요, 우리가 이겨 내리라고. 당신은 그 지긋지긋한 연대를 떠나, 노름이며 경마를 끊고 착한 사람이 되세요. 그러면 우리 모두 파크 레인에 살게 될 테고, 이모님은 우리에게 그 재산을 전부 물려주실 거예요.\n\n내일 세 시에 여느 때 그 자리로 산책 나가 보려고 해요. 만약 B 양이 저와 동행하거든, 당신은 만찬에 와서 답장을 가져와 포티어스 설교집 셋째 권에 끼워 두세요. 하지만 어떻든, 당신의 사람인 이 몸에게로 와 주세요.\n\nR.\n\n엘리자 스타일스 양 앞, 나이츠브리지 마구상 바넷 씨 댁 전교.\n\n그리고 나는 이 자그마한 이야기의 독자들 가운데, 그 엘리자 스타일스 양이란(리베카의 말로는 옛 학교 동무로, 요즘 들어 다시 부지런히 편지를 주고받게 되었으며, 마구상에게서 이 편지들을 받아 오곤 했다는 그 사람이) 놋쇠 박차를 차고 크게 말아 올린 콧수염을 기른, 실은 다름 아닌 로던 크롤리 대위였음을 알아챌 만한 분별력조차 없는 이는 한 사람도 없으리라 믿는다.\n\n그들이 어떻게 결혼했는가 하는 문제는 누구에게도 털끝만큼도 중요하지 않다. 소령이기도 한 대위와 성년에 이른 젊은 숙녀가 혼인 허가서를 사들여 이 도시의 어느 교회에서든 백년가약을 맺는 것을 무엇이 막을 수 있단 말인가? 여자에게 의지가 있다면 반드시 방도를 찾아내고야 만다는 사실을 굳이 누구에게 일러 줄 필요가 있겠는가? 내 짐작으로는, 어느 날 샤프 양이 러셀 광장에 사는 그 다정한 벗 애밀리아 세들리 양과 오전을 보내러 갔다던 그날, 그녀와 무척 닮은 한 숙녀가 도심의 어느 교회로 들어서는 모습을 본 사람이 있을 법하다. 그녀는 콧수염을 물들인 한 신사와 동행이었으며, 그 신사는 십오 분쯤 지난 뒤 그녀를 대기하고 있던 삯마차까지 바래다주었으니, 이것이 바로 어느 조촐한 혼례의 일행이었던 것이다.\n\n게다가, 우리가 날마다 겪는 경험에 비추어 볼 때, 신사가 누군가와 결혼할 개연성을 도대체 누가 의심할 수 있단 말인가? 현명하고 학식 있는 이들 가운데 자기 집 요리사와 결혼한 자가 또 얼마나 많은가? 더없이 신중한 인물인 엘던 경 본인조차 야반도주식 혼인을 하지 않았던가? 아킬레우스와 아이아스 또한 모두 자기네 하녀를 사랑하지 않았던가? 그런데 욕망은 거센데 두뇌는 빈약하고, 평생 단 한 번도 격정을 다스려 본 적이 없는 우람한 용기병이 어느 날 갑자기 신중해져서, 마음에 둔 향락에 대가 치르기를 거부하리라고 기대해야 한단 말인가? 사람들이 신중한 결혼만 한다면, 인구 증가에 그 얼마나 큰 제동이 걸리겠는가!\n\n내 생각에는, 로던 씨의 결혼이야말로 이번 이야기와 관련된 그 신사의 전기 어느 대목에서든 우리가 기록하게 될 가장 정직한 행동의 하나였던 셈이다. 한 여인에게 사로잡히는 것이, 혹은 사로잡힌 끝에 그 여인과 결혼하는 것이 사내답지 못한 일이라고 말할 사람은 아무도 없으리라. 그리고 이 거구의 전사가 그 자그마한 레베카를 차츰 바라보게 된 그 흠모며 환희며 격정이며 경탄이며 그 무한한 신뢰와 미칠 듯한 숭배는, 적어도 숙녀분들이라면 그에게 그다지 불명예스러운 감정만은 아니라고 단언할 그런 감정들이었다. 그녀가 노래하면 음표 하나하나가 그의 둔한 영혼을 전율케 했고 그 거대한 몸뚱이를 짜릿하게 관통했다. 그녀가 말하면 그는 온 두뇌의 힘을 그러모아 귀 기울이며 경탄했다. 그녀가 농을 던지면 그는 그 농담을 머릿속에서 곱씹다가 반 시간 뒤 거리에서 별안간 웃음을 터뜨려, 곁에 탄 이륜마차의 마부나 로튼 거리를 함께 말 타고 가던 동료를 놀라게 하곤 했다. 그녀의 말은 그에게 신탁이었고, 그녀의 사소한 행동 하나하나도 빈틈없는 우아함과 지혜의 표시였다. \"저렇게 노래를 잘하다니, 그림도 어찌나 잘 그리는지.\" 그는 생각했다. \"퀸스크롤리에서 그 발길질해 대는 암말을 타던 모습이라니!\" 그러고는 은밀한 순간이면 그녀에게 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"이런, 베키, 당신은 총사령관이라도, 아니 캔터베리 대주교라도 될 만한 사람이야, 정말로.\" 그의 경우가 과연 드문 일이던가? 우리는 옴팔레의 앞치마 끈에 매여 사는 정직한 헤라클레스며, 데릴라의 무릎에 엎드린 구레나룻 무성한 삼손을 세상에서 날마다 보고 있지 않은가?\n\n그리하여 베키가 그에게 중대한 고비가 가까웠고 행동에 나설 때가 왔다고 일러 주자, 로던은 마치 대령의 명령에 따라 부대를 이끌고 돌격할 때처럼 그녀의 명령에 따라 행동할 준비가 되어 있노라 밝혔다. 그가 자기 편지를 포티어스 설교집 제3권 사이에 끼워 둘 필요도 없었다. 레베카는 손쉽게 방도를 찾아 자기 말동무인 브리그스를 떼어 내고는, 이튿날 \"늘 만나던 곳\"에서 그 충직한 벗과 만났다. 그녀는 밤새 일을 곰곰이 따져 보았고, 그 결심의 결과를 로던에게 알렸다. 그는 물론 모든 일에 동의했다. 다 잘될 것이 틀림없으며, 그녀가 제안한 것이 최선이며, 크롤리 양도 시간이 좀 지나면 어김없이 마음을 누그러뜨릴 것이라고, 그의 표현대로라면 \"돌아설\" 것이라고 굳게 믿었다. 설령 레베카의 결심이 전혀 딴판이었다 한들, 그는 똑같이 고분고분 그것을 따랐을 것이다. \"당신 머리 하나면 우리 둘 몫은 충분해, 베키.\" 그가 말했다. \"당신이라면 이 곤경에서 우리를 틀림없이 빼내 줄 거야. 나는 당신 같은 사람을 본 적이 없어. 그래도 내 평생 제법 똑 부러진 사람들을 만나 봤는데도 말이야.\" 그러고는 이 소박한 신앙 고백과 함께, 사랑에 빠진 그 용기병은 그녀가 두 사람을 위해 짜 놓은 계획에서 자기 몫을 실행하러 그녀 곁을 떠났다.\n\n그 몫이란 그저 브롬프턴, 혹은 병영 근처에 크롤리 대위 부부가 묵을 조용한 셋방을 구하는 일이었다. 레베카가, 우리 보기에 매우 신중하게도, 달아나기로 결심했기 때문이다. 로던은 그녀의 결심에 더없이 기뻐했다. 그는 지난 몇 주 내내 그녀에게 이렇게 하자고 졸라 오던 터였다. 그는 사랑의 온갖 조급함을 안고 의기양양하게 셋방을 구하러 나섰다. 그가 일주일에 이 기니를 내겠노라 어찌나 선뜻 응하던지, 집주인 여자는 자기가 너무 적게 불렀다고 후회할 지경이었다. 그는 피아노 한 대를 주문해 들이고, 묘목원 절반을 비울 만큼 꽃을 들였으며, 온갖 좋은 물건을 산더미처럼 사들였다. 숄이며 새끼 염소 가죽 장갑이며 비단 양말이며 프랑스산 금시계며 팔찌며 향수로 말하자면, 그는 눈먼 사랑과 무한한 외상의 아낌없는 씀씀이로 그것들을 보내 댔다. 그렇게 이 너그러움을 쏟아 내어 마음의 짐을 던 그는, 자기 인생의 위대한 순간이 오기를 기다리며 클럽에 가서 안절부절못하며 저녁을 들었다.\n\n전날의 사건들, 그러니까 자신에게 그토록 유리한 청혼을 거절한 레베카의 갸륵한 처신과, 그녀를 좀먹는 남모를 불행과, 그 고통을 견디는 다소곳하고 그윽한 침묵은, 크롤리 양을 여느 때보다 한결 다정하게 만들었다. 이런 종류의 사건, 즉 결혼이며 거절이며 청혼이라는 것은 한집안 여자들 전체를 전율케 하고 그들의 발작적인 동정심을 죄다 발동시키는 법이다. 인간 본성의 관찰자로서, 나는 점잖은 결혼철이면 어김없이 하노버 광장의 세인트조지 교회를 드나든다. 비록 신랑의 남자 친구들이 눈물에 무너지는 모습이나, 교회 안내인이며 식을 집전하는 성직자가 조금이라도 마음 흔들리는 모습은 한 번도 본 적이 없지만, 그 진행되는 일과 손톱만큼도 관계없는 여자들이, 그러니까 혼인할 나이가 한참 지난 노부인들이며, 아들딸을 잔뜩 둔 살집 좋은 중년 여인들이, 게다가 이제 한창 시집갈 나이라 자연히 그 예식에 관심을 가질 법한 분홍 모자 쓴 어여쁜 젊은 처자들은 말할 것도 없고, 하여튼 그런 여자들이 훌쩍이고, 흐느끼고, 코를 훌쩍이며, 그 자그마한 얼굴을 쓸모도 없는 자그마한 손수건에 파묻은 채, 늙은이고 젊은이고 할 것 없이 감정에 들썩이는 모습을 보는 일은 결코 드물지 않다. 내 친구인 멋쟁이 존 핌리코가 어여쁜 벨그레이비어 그린파커 영애와 결혼하던 날에는, 그 흥분이 어찌나 두루 번졌던지 나를 자리로 안내한 코담배 냄새 풍기는 자그마한 늙은 좌석 안내원 노파마저 눈물을 글썽였다. 한데 그건 어째서인가? 나는 내 영혼에게 물었다. 그 노파가 결혼하는 것도 아니었거늘.\n\n요컨대 크롤리 양과 브리그스는 피트 경의 사건이 있은 뒤로 더없이 호사스러운 감상에 흠뻑 빠져들었고, 레베카는 그들에게 더없이 애틋한 관심의 대상이 되었다. 그녀가 없는 동안 크롤리 양은 서재에 있는 가장 감상적인 소설들로 마음을 달랬다. 남모를 비탄을 품은 자그마한 샤프야말로 그 시절의 여주인공이었던 것이다.\n\n그날 밤 레베카는 파크 레인에서 일찍이 들려준 적 없을 만큼 감미롭게 노래하고 즐겁게 이야기했다. 그녀는 크롤리 양의 마음을 칭칭 휘감았다. 그녀는 피트 경의 청혼을 가볍고 우스개 삼아 이야기하며, 늙은이의 어리석은 망상이라고 비웃었다. 그러고는 사랑하는 은인 곁에 영영 머무는 것 말고는 다른 운명을 바라지 않는다고 말할 때, 그녀의 두 눈은 눈물로 가득 찼고 브리그스의 가슴은 말로 다 할 수 없는 패배의 고통으로 가득 찼다. \"내 귀여운 것아.\" 노부인이 말했다. \"앞으로 몇 해든 너를 곁에서 한 발짝도 못 떠나게 할 작정이다, 그건 믿어도 좋아. 이런 일이 있고 난 마당에 그 밉살스러운 내 동생한테 돌아간다는 건 어림도 없는 소리지. 너는 여기서 나와 브리그스랑 함께 지내는 거야. 브리그스는 친척들 보러 가고 싶어 자주 그러니까, 브리그스, 너야 가고 싶을 때 가도 좋다. 하지만 너만은, 얘야, 여기 남아서 이 늙은이를 돌봐 줘야 한다.\"\n\n만약 로던 크롤리가 클럽에서 안절부절못하며 적포도주나 들이켜는 대신 그때 그 자리에 있었더라면, 두 사람은 그 늙은 노처녀 앞에 무릎 꿇고 죄다 털어놓아 눈 깜짝할 새에 용서받았을지도 모른다. 그러나 그 좋은 기회는 이 젊은 부부에게 허락되지 않았으니, 이는 분명 이 이야기가 쓰이게 하기 위함이었던 것이다. 이 이야기에는 그들의 놀라운 모험이 숱하게 펼쳐지거니와, 만약 그들이 크롤리 양의 안락하고 따분한 용서 아래 깃들어 보살핌을 받았더라면 결코 그들에게 일어날 수 없었을 모험들인 것이다.\n\n파크 레인 저택에서 퍼킨 부인의 지휘를 받는 사람들 가운데, 햄프셔 출신의 젊은 여자가 하나 있었다. 그녀가 맡은 여러 일 가운데 하나는, 퍼킨이라면 차라리 죽어 버릴지언정 그 굴러든 식객에게는 결코 들여놓지 않았을 그 뜨거운 물 주전자를 들고 샤프 양의 방문을 두드리는 것이었다. 가문의 영지에서 자란 이 여자에게는 크롤리 대위의 부대에 든 오라비가 있었으니, 사실인즉 이 이야기와 깊이 관련된 어떤 모의를 그녀가 알고 있었다는 것이 드러나리라 나는 감히 짐작한다. 어쨌거나 그녀는 레베카가 준 삼 기니로 노란 숄 하나와 초록색 부츠 한 켤레와 빨간 깃털 꽂힌 연한 파란 모자 하나를 사들였다. 자그마한 샤프가 결코 제 돈을 헤프게 쓰는 사람이 아니었던 만큼, 베티 마틴이 그렇게 매수당한 것은 의심할 바 없이 그녀가 제공한 어떤 봉사에 대한 대가였던 것이다.\n\n피트 크롤리 경이 샤프 양에게 청혼한 지 이틀째 되던 날, 해는 여느 때처럼 떠올랐고, 여느 때와 같은 시각에 이층 담당 하녀 베티 마틴이 가정교사 침실의 방문을 두드렸다.\n\n아무런 응답이 없자 그녀는 다시 두드렸다. 침묵은 여전히 깨지지 않았다. 베티는 뜨거운 물을 든 채 문을 열고 방 안으로 들어섰다.\n\n자그마한 흰 무명 침대는 어제, 베티 자신의 손으로 정돈을 거들었던 그날과 다름없이 매끈하고 가지런했다. 자그마한 트렁크 두 개가 방 한쪽 끝에 끈으로 묶여 있었고, 창가의 탁자 위에는, 그러니까 바늘꽂이 위에는 — 안에 분홍색 천을 댄, 숙녀의 나이트캡처럼 골이 진 그 크고 통통한 바늘꽂이 위에는 — 편지 한 통이 놓여 있었다. 아마 밤새 그 자리에 놓여 있었던 모양이었다.\n\n베티는 그것을 깨우기라도 할까 두려운 듯 발끝으로 살금살금 다가가서는, 자못 놀랍고도 흡족한 기색으로 편지를 보고 또 방 안을 둘러보았다. 그러고는 편지를 집어 들어 이리저리 뒤집어 보며 함박 히죽거리더니, 마침내 그것을 아래층 브리그스 양의 방으로 가지고 갔다.\n\n대체 베티가 그 편지가 브리그스 양에게 온 것인 줄 어떻게 알았는지, 나는 정말 알고 싶다. 베티가 받은 교육이라고는 죄다 뷰트 크롤리 부인의 주일학교에서 배운 것이 전부였고, 그녀로서는 글씨를 읽는 것이 히브리어를 읽는 것이나 진배없었던 것이다.\n\n\"어머나, 브리그스 아씨.\" 처녀가 외쳤다. \"아씨, 무슨 일이 났나 봐요. 샤프 아씨 방에 아무도 없어요. 침대에선 잔 흔적도 없고요, 도망쳐 버린 거예요, 아씨한테 이 편지를 남겨 놓고요.\"\n\n\"뭐라고!\" 브리그스가 빗을 떨어뜨리며 외치니, 빛바랜 머리카락 한 가닥이 어깨 위로 흘러내렸다. \"야반도주라니! 샤프 양이 달아나다니! 이게, 이게 무슨 일이람?\" 그러고는 그녀는 다급하게 그 단정한 봉인을 뜯고는, 말마따나 자신 앞으로 온 그 편지의 \"내용을 게걸스레 삼켰다.\"\n\n친애하는 브리그스 양 [도망친 여인은 이렇게 적었다], 당신처럼 세상에서 가장 어진 마음을 지닌 분이라면 저를 가엾이 여겨 동정하고 또 용서해 주시겠지요. 눈물과 기도와 축복으로, 이 가엾은 고아가 한결같이 친절과 애정을 받아 온 그 보금자리를 저는 떠납니다. 저의 은인보다도 더 우선하는 도리가 저를 이곳에서 불러내는 것입니다. 저는 제 도리를 다하러 — 제 남편에게로 갑니다. 그렇습니다, 저는 결혼했습니다. 제 남편이 우리의 것이라 부르는 그 초라한 보금자리를 찾아오라 명하는 것입니다. 더없이 친애하는 브리그스 양, 부디 그 섬세한 동정심으로 잘 아실 그 방법대로, 저의 사랑하고 흠모하는 벗이자 은인께 이 소식을 전해 주세요. 제가 떠나기 전, 그분의 다정한 베개에 — 병석에 누우셨을 때 제가 그토록 자주 매만져 드리던 그 베개에, 다시금 곁에서 지켜보기를 간절히 바라는 그 베개에 눈물을 떨구었노라고 전해 주세요. 아, 그리운 파크 레인으로 돌아갈 때 저는 얼마나 큰 기쁨에 젖을는지요! 제 운명을 결정지을 그 답을 생각하면 어찌나 떨리는지요! 피트 경께서 황송하게도 제게 손을 내미셨을 때, 사랑하는 크롤리 양께서 제가 그럴 자격이 있다고 말씀하셨던 그 영광을 (이 가엾은 고아를 당신의 동생감으로 합당하게 보아 주신 그분께 축복이 함께하기를!) 받았을 때, 저는 피트 경께 이미 한 사람의 아내라고 말씀드렸습니다. 그분조차 저를 용서하셨지요. 그러나 모든 것을 말씀드려야 했을 때 — 그분의 아내가 될 수 없는 까닭이 제가 바로 그분의 딸이기 때문이라는 것을 말씀드려야 했을 때, 제겐 용기가 나지 않았습니다! 저는 가장 선량하고 가장 너그러운 사내와 부부의 연을 맺었습니다 — 크롤리 양의 로던이 바로 저의 로던입니다. 그이의 명에 따라 저는 입을 열어, 온 세상을 헤쳐서라도 따르듯 그 초라한 보금자리로 그이를 따라갑니다. 아, 더없이 갸륵하고 어진 벗이여, 그이와, 그이의 그 고귀한 가문 모두가 이토록 견줄 데 없는 애정을 베풀어 주신 이 가엾은 처자를 위하여, 부디 제 로던의 사랑하는 고모님께 잘 말씀드려 주세요. 부디 크롤리 양께서 자기 자식들을 거두어 주십사 청해 주세요. 더는 드릴 말씀이 없습니다. 다만 제가 떠나는 그 정든 집의 모든 분께 축복이, 축복이 함께하기를 비는 사람,\n\n당신을 아끼고 또 고마워하는 레베카 크롤리 올림. 한밤중에.\n\n브리그스가 이 가슴 저미고 흥미로운 문서를 — 자신을 크롤리 양의 으뜸가는 심복 자리로 되돌려 놓은 그 문서를 — 막 다 읽고 났을 때, 퍼킨 부인이 방으로 들어왔다. \"방금 뷰트 크롤리 부인께서 햄프셔에서 우편마차로 도착하셨는데, 차를 좀 드시겠대요. 내려오셔서 아침상 좀 봐 주시겠어요, 아씨?\"\n\n그러자 퍼킨이 놀랍게도, 브리그스는 실내복을 몸에 단단히 여민 채, 흐트러진 머리카락 한 가닥을 뒤로 풀어 헤치고, 컬을 만 종이 뭉치들을 아직 이마 둘레에 다발다발 매단 그대로, 그 놀라운 소식이 담긴 편지를 손에 쥐고 미끄러지듯 뷰트 부인에게 내려갔다.\n\n\"아유, 퍼킨 부인.\" 베티가 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다. \"이런 난리가 다 있어요. 샤프 아씨가 대위님이랑 달아나 버렸지 뭐예요, 둘이서 그레트나그린으로 줄행랑을 쳤대요!\" 우리는 퍼킨 부인의 격정을 묘사하는 데 한 장을 통째로 바쳤을 것이다. 다만 우리의 한층 점잖은 시신(詩神)이 그 여주인들의 격정에 매여 있지 않았더라면 말이다.\n\n뷰트 크롤리 부인은 한밤중의 여행으로 몸이 곱은 채 새로 타닥타닥 타오르는 응접실 난롯불에 몸을 녹이다가, 브리그스 양에게서 그 은밀한 결혼 소식을 전해 듣자, 가엾고 사랑하는 크롤리 양이 그 충격을 견디도록 도우러 마침 이런 때에 자신이 당도한 것은 참으로 하늘의 뜻이라고 단언했다. 그러면서 레베카란 진작부터 자신이 의심을 품어 온 교활한 계집이며, 로던 크롤리로 말하자면 그 고모가 그자한테 그토록 홀딱 빠진 까닭을 도무지 헤아릴 수 없었거니와 오래전부터 그자를 방탕하고 타락한 폐인으로 여겨 왔노라 했다. 그러고는 이 끔찍한 행실에도 적어도 한 가지 좋은 점은 있을 것이라고, 즉 가엾고 사랑하는 크롤리 양이 이 사악한 사내의 본색에 눈을 뜨게 될 것이라고 뷰트 부인은 말했다. 그러고 나서 뷰트 부인은 흐뭇하게 따끈한 토스트와 차를 들었다. 그리고 이제 집 안에 빈방이 하나 생겼으니, 포츠머스 우편마차가 자신을 내려놓은 글로스터 커피하우스에 더 머물 필요가 없었다. 그래서 그녀는 보울스 씨의 부관 격인 하인을 시켜 그곳에서 자기 트렁크들을 가져오게 했다.\n\n밝혀 두자면, 크롤리 양은 정오 무렵까지는 방에서 나오지 않았다. 아침이면 침대에서 초콜릿을 들었고, 그동안 베키 샤프가 그녀에게 모닝 포스트를 읽어 주었으며, 그러지 않을 때는 무언가로 소일하거나 빈둥거리며 시간을 보냈다. 아래층의 모의자들은 그 다정한 부인이 응접실에 나타날 때까지는 그 마음을 다치게 하지 않기로 합의를 보았다. 그동안 뷰트 크롤리 부인이 햄프셔에서 우편마차로 올라와 글로스터에 묵고 있으며, 크롤리 양에게 안부를 전하고 브리그스 양과 아침을 들겠다 청한다는 전갈이 그녀에게 전해졌다. 다른 때 같으면 그다지 큰 반가움을 자아내지 못했을 뷰트 부인의 도착이 이번에는 기쁘게 환영받았다. 크롤리 양으로서는 고인이 된 크롤리 부인이며, 미루어진 장례 준비며, 피트 경이 레베카에게 별안간 청혼한 일을 두고 그 올케와 한바탕 수다를 떨 생각에 흐뭇했던 것이다.\n\n노부인이 응접실의 늘 앉는 안락의자에 제대로 자리 잡고, 두 부인 사이에 첫 인사와 안부 묻기가 오간 뒤에야, 모의자들은 그녀에게 그 시술을 가하는 것이 좋겠다고 판단했다. 여자들이 벗들에게 나쁜 소식을 받아들일 \"채비를 시키는\" 그 술책과 섬세한 접근을 누군들 감탄하지 않으랴? 크롤리 양의 두 벗은 그 소식을 꺼내기에 앞서 어찌나 신비스러운 분위기를 잔뜩 차려 놓던지, 그녀를 필요한 만큼의 의심과 경악으로 몰아넣고야 말았다.\n\n\"그래서 그 애가 피트 경을 거절했지 뭐예요, 친애하는, 친애하는 크롤리 양, 마음 단단히 잡수세요.\" 뷰트 부인이 말했다. \"왜냐하면 — 왜냐하면 그 애로서는 어쩔 도리가 없었던 거예요.\"\n\n\"물론 무슨 까닭이 있었겠지.\" 크롤리 양이 대답했다. \"다른 누군가를 좋아한 게야. 내가 어제 브리그스한테도 그렇게 말했잖아.\"\n\n\"다른 누군가를 좋아하다뇨!\" 브리그스가 숨을 헐떡였다. \"아, 친애하는 마님, 그 애는 벌써 결혼했답니다.\"\n\n\"벌써 결혼했어요.\" 뷰트 부인이 맞장구를 쳤다. 그러고는 두 사람 다 두 손을 맞잡은 채 서로를 번갈아 보고 또 그 희생자를 바라보며 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"그 애가 들어오는 즉시 내게 보내라. 그 교활한 계집 같으니, 어떻게 감히 내게 말도 안 했단 말이냐?\" 크롤리 양이 소리쳤다.\n\n\"그 애는 곧 들어오지 않을 거예요. 마음 단단히 잡수세요, 친애하는 마님 — 그 애는 한동안 나가 버렸어요 — 그 애는, 그 애는 아예 떠나 버렸어요.\"\n\n\"아이고 맙소사, 그럼 누가 내 초콜릿을 만든단 말이냐? 사람을 보내서 그 애를 도로 데려와라. 그 애가 돌아오기를 명한다.\" 노부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그 애는 간밤에 야반도주를 했답니다, 마님.\" 뷰트 부인이 외쳤다.\n\n\"그 애가 제게 편지를 남겼어요.\" 브리그스가 외쳤다. \"그 애는 결혼했어요, 상대는—\"\n\n\"제발 마님 마음 다잡게 해 드려요. 그렇게 마님을 괴롭히지 말아요, 친애하는 브리그스 양.\"\n\n\"그 애가 누구랑 결혼했단 말이냐?\" 노처녀가 안절부절못하며 격노하여 외쳤다.\n\n\"그게 — 그게 어느 친척과—\"\n\n\"그 애가 피트 경을 거절했다고.\" 희생자가 외쳤다. \"당장 말해라. 날 미치게 만들지 말고.\"\n\n\"아, 마님 — 마님 마음 단단히 잡수시게 해 드려요, 브리그스 양 — 그 애는 로던 크롤리와 결혼했어요.\"\n\n\"로던이 레베카랑 결혼을 해 — 가정교사 따위 — 근본도 없는 — 내 집에서 썩 나가라, 이 멍청아, 이 얼간아 — 이 어리석은 늙은 브리그스 같으니 — 어떻게 감히? 너도 한패로구나 — 내가 그 애한테서 재산을 떼어 놓을 줄 알고 네가 그 애를 부추겨 결혼시킨 게야 — 네가 그랬지, 마사.\" 가엾은 노부인이 발작하듯 토막 난 말로 비명을 질러 댔다.\n\n\"제가요, 마님, 이 집안 사람더러 그림 선생 딸과 결혼하라고 부추겼다고요?\"\n\n\"그 애 어미는 몽모랑시 가문 사람이었어.\" 노부인이 온 힘을 다해 종을 잡아당기며 외쳤다.\n\n\"그 애 어미는 오페라 무대의 계집이었고, 그 애 자신도 무대에 섰거나 아니면 그보다 더한 짓을 했답니다.\" 뷰트 부인이 말했다.\n\n크롤리 양은 마지막으로 한 번 비명을 지르더니 까무러쳐 뒤로 넘어갔다. 사람들은 어쩔 수 없이 그녀를 방금 나온 그 방으로 도로 데려가야 했다. 발작이 한 차례 또 한 차례 잇따랐다. 사람을 보내 의사를 불렀고, 약제사가 당도했다. 뷰트 부인은 그 머리맡에서 간병인 자리를 차지했다. \"친척들이 곁에서 둘러앉아 있어야지.\" 그 어진 여인은 말했다.\n\n그녀가 위층 방으로 옮겨지자마자, 또 한 사람이 당도했으니 그에게도 이 소식을 전해야 했다. 바로 피트 경이었다. \"베키는 어디 있느냐?\" 그가 들어서며 말했다. \"그 애 짐은 어디 있고? 그 애는 나랑 퀸스크롤리로 가기로 했단 말이다.\"\n\n\"그 애가 몰래 혼인한 그 놀라운 소식을 못 들으셨나요?\" 브리그스가 물었다.\n\n\"그게 나랑 무슨 상관이냐?\" 피트 경이 물었다. \"그 애가 결혼했다는 건 나도 안다. 그건 아무 차이도 없어. 당장 내려오라고 일러라, 날 기다리게 하지 말고.\"\n\n\"나리, 모르셨나요.\" 브리그스 양이 물었다. \"그 애가 이 집을 떠나 버려서 크롤리 양께서 망연자실하셨답니다, 로던 대위가 그 애와 혼인했다는 소식에 거의 돌아가실 지경이 되셨다고요?\"\n\n피트 크롤리 경은 레베카가 자기 아들과 결혼했다는 말을 듣자, 차마 이 자리에서 옮겨 봐야 아무 득 될 것 없는 험한 말을 한바탕 욕설로 쏟아 냈다. 실제로 그 욕설에 가엾은 브리그스는 부들부들 떨며 방에서 빠져나갔다. 그리하여 우리는 그녀와 함께, 증오로 광란하고 좌절된 욕망으로 실성한 그 미친 늙은이의 모습 위로 문을 닫아 두기로 하자.\n\n그가 퀸스크롤리로 내려간 다음 날, 그는 미치광이처럼 그녀가 거기 있을 적에 쓰던 방으로 뛰어 들어가서는 — 발길질로 그녀의 상자들을 박살 내고 — 그녀의 서류며 옷가지며 그 밖의 유품들을 마구 내던졌다. 집사의 딸 호럭스 양이 그 가운데 몇 가지를 챙겼다. 아이들은 나머지를 걸치고 연극 놀이를 했다. 그것은 가엾은 어머니가 외로운 무덤으로 떠난 지 겨우 며칠 뒤의 일이었다. 그녀는 아무도 울어 주지 않고 거들떠보지도 않는 가운데, 낯선 이들로 가득한 납골당에 안치되었던 것이다.\n\n\"그 늙은 부인이 마음을 안 돌리면 어쩐다.\" 로던이 아늑하고 자그마한 브롬프턴 셋방에 자기 어린 아내와 함께 앉아서 말했다. 그녀는 아침 내내 새 피아노를 만지작거려 보던 참이었다. 새 장갑은 그녀에게 딱 맞아떨어졌고, 새 숄은 놀랍도록 잘 어울렸으며, 새 반지들은 그 자그마한 손에서 반짝였고, 새 시계는 허리춤에서 똑딱거렸다. \"마음을 안 돌리면, 응, 베키?\"\n\n\"내가 당신 한밑천 잡게 해 줄게요.\" 그녀가 말했다. 그러고는 데릴라가 삼손의 뺨을 토닥였다.\n\n\"당신이라면 무엇이든 할 수 있어.\" 그가 그 자그마한 손에 입 맞추며 말했다. \"정말이지 당신은 할 수 있어. 그러니 우리 스타 앤드 가터로 마차나 몰고 가서, 한 끼 거하게 먹자고, 정말로.\"\n\n이 허영의 시장 전체에서, 풍자와 감상이 서로 팔짱을 끼고 함께 거닐 수 있는 구경거리가 있다면, 우습기도 하고 눈물겹기도 한 더없이 기묘한 대조와 마주치는 곳, 점잖고 애처롭게 굴어도 좋고 사납고 냉소적으로 굴어도 더없이 어울리는 곳이 있다면, 그것은 바로 저 공개 경매장들 가운데 하나일 것이다. 그런 경매는 날마다 무더기로 「타임스」 신문 마지막 면에 광고가 실리며, 고인이 된 조지 로빈스 씨가 그토록 위엄 있게 진행을 맡곤 하던 바로 그것이다. 내 짐작으로는, 이런 모임에 한 번도 참석해 보지 않은 런던 사람은 극히 드물 것이며, 무릇 도덕적 사색을 즐기는 이라면 누구나 적잖이 놀랍고도 야릇한 어떤 느낌과 흥미를 품은 채, 언젠가 자기 차례도 돌아오리라는 것을 떠올려 보았음이 틀림없다. 그날이 오면 해머다운 씨가 디오게네스의 파산 관재인의 명을 받들어, 혹은 유언 집행인의 위촉을 받아, 고인이 된 에피쿠로스의 장서며 가구며 식기며 의복이며, 그리고 엄선된 포도주 저장고를 공개 경쟁에 부쳐 처분할 것이다.\n\n아무리 이기적인 천성을 지닌 허영의 시장 사람이라 해도, 떠나간 벗의 장례식 가운데 이토록 추레한 대목을 목도하노라면 얼마간의 연민과 회한을 느끼지 않을 수 없는 법이다. 다이브스 경의 유해는 가족 납골당에 안치되어 있다. 조각가들은 그의 미덕과, 그의 유산을 처분하고 있는 상속인의 슬픔을 진실되게 기리는 비문을 새기고 있는 중이다. 다이브스의 식탁에 둘러앉았던 손님이라면 그 누구인들 이 낯익은 집을 한숨 한 번 없이 지나칠 수 있겠는가. 일곱 시면 불빛이 그토록 흥겹게 빛나던 낯익은 집, 현관문이 그토록 기꺼이 열리던 집, 그 안락한 계단을 올라가노라면 굽실거리는 하인들이 층계참마다 당신의 이름을 외쳐 전하여 마침내 명랑한 노신사 다이브스가 벗들을 맞이하던 그 방에 이르게 하던 집을 말이다! 그에게는 또 얼마나 많은 벗이 있었으며, 그들을 대접하는 솜씨는 또 얼마나 고상했던가. 문밖에 나서면 시무룩하던 사람들이 이곳에서는 또 얼마나 재치가 넘쳤으며, 다른 데서는 서로 헐뜯고 미워하던 사내들이 여기서는 또 얼마나 정중하고 다정했던가! 그는 거드름을 피우는 사람이었으나, 그런 요리사를 둔 마당에 무엇인들 못 삼키겠는가. 어쩌면 좀 따분한 사람이었는지도 모르나, 그런 포도주라면 무슨 대화인들 즐겁게 만들지 않겠는가. \"값이 얼마가 들든 그의 부르고뉴 포도주는 꼭 좀 손에 넣어야겠어.\" 조문객들이 클럽에서 부르짖는다. \"이 담뱃갑은 다이브스 영감 경매에서 산 거라네.\" 핀처가 그것을 돌려 보이며 말한다. \"루이 십오세의 정부 가운데 하나라지. 예쁘지 않은가? 아주 고운 세밀화일세.\" 그러고는 젊은 다이브스가 어떤 식으로 재산을 탕진하고 있는지 이야기꽃을 피운다.\n\n그런데 그 집은 또 얼마나 변해 버렸는가! 정면은 가구의 명세를 큼직한 대문자로 떡하니 박아 넣은 광고지들로 온통 도배되어 있다. 위층 창밖으로는 양탄자 한 조각이 늘어져 나와 있고, 더러운 계단에는 짐꾼 대여섯이 빈둥거리고 있으며, 현관에는 동방인 같은 얼굴빛을 한 꾀죄죄한 손님들이 우글거리며 당신 손에 인쇄된 명함을 떠안기고는 응찰을 청한다. 위층 방들은 노파들과 호사가들이 점령하여, 침대 휘장을 만지작거리고, 깃털 이불을 쿡쿡 찔러 보고, 매트리스를 주물러 보고, 옷장 서랍을 이리저리 여닫는다. 진취적인 젊은 살림꾼들은 새 살림에 맞을지 알아보려고 거울이며 휘장이며 치수를 재고 있다(속물들은 이것저것을 다이브스 경 경매에서 샀노라고 몇 해를 두고 자랑할 것이다). 그리고 아래층 식당에서는 해머다운 씨가 커다란 마호가니 식탁 위에 걸터앉아 상아 망치를 흔들어 대며, 웅변과 열정과 간청과 논리와 절망의 온갖 술수를 부린다. 부하들에게 소리치고, 데이비즈 씨의 굼뜬 동작을 비꼬고, 모스 씨를 부추겨 움직이게 하고, 애원하고 명령하고 고함치다가, 마침내 운명처럼 망치가 떨어지면 우리는 다음 물건으로 넘어가는 것이다. 오 다이브스여, 식기와 티 한 점 없는 식탁보로 반짝이던 그 너른 식탁에 둘러앉았을 적에, 그 상석에 저 고래고래 소리치는 경매인 같은 요리가 놓이게 되리라고 그 누가 생각이나 했겠는가?\n\n경매도 제법 막바지에 이른 무렵이었다. 일류 장인들이 만든 빼어난 응접실 가구며, 비용을 따지지 않고 구매자의 정평 있는 안목으로 엄선해 둔 진귀하고 이름난 포도주며, 호화롭고 빠짐없이 갖춰진 가족용 은식기 한 벌은 지난 며칠 동안 이미 다 팔린 뒤였다. 그 가운데 으뜸가는 포도주 몇 가지(이웃의 호사가들 사이에서 두루 명성이 자자한 것들이었다)는 러셀 광장에 사는 우리의 벗 존 오스본 귀하의 집사가, 그것들을 훤히 꿰고 있는 자기 주인을 위해 사들였다. 가장 요긴한 은식기들 가운데 일부는 시티에서 온 어느 젊은 주식 중개인들이 사 갔다. 그리고 이제 자질구레한 물건들을 사라고 대중에게 권하는 차례가 되어, 마침 식탁 위의 그 웅변가는 청중에게 추천하려는 어느 그림 한 점의 장점을 늘어놓고 있었다. 경매가 진행되던 지난 며칠에 비하면, 오늘 모인 무리는 그리 고상하지도 많지도 않았다.\n\n\"삼백육십구 번이오.\" 해머다운 씨가 고함쳤다. \"코끼리에 탄 신사의 초상화올시다. 코끼리에 탄 신사에 응찰하실 분 안 계시오? 블로먼, 그림을 들어 올려서 여러분이 이 물건을 살펴보시게 하게.\" 마호가니 식탁에 점잖게 앉아 있던, 키가 크고 핼쑥하며 군인 같은 풍채의 한 신사는, 블로먼 씨가 이 진귀한 물건을 들어 보이자 씩 웃지 않을 수 없었다. \"블로먼, 그 코끼리를 대위 양반 쪽으로 돌려 보이게. 자, 신사 양반, 이 코끼리에 얼마를 부르시겠소?\" 그러나 대위는 몹시 황급하고 당황한 기색으로 얼굴을 붉히며 고개를 돌려 버렸다.\n\n\"이 예술 작품에 이십 기니를 부를까요? 십오 기니, 오 기니, 부르고 싶은 값을 말씀하시오. 코끼리를 빼고 신사만 쳐도 오 파운드는 나갈 물건이오.\"\n\n\"신사가 그 위에서 안 굴러떨어진 게 신기할 노릇이오.\" 직업적인 익살꾼 하나가 말했다. \"어쨌든 무지하게 큰 분이시니 말이지.\" 이 말에(코끼리에 탄 사람이 자못 뚱뚱한 풍채로 그려져 있던 터라) 장내에 한바탕 키득거림이 일었다.\n\n\"이 물건의 값어치를 깎아내리려 들지 마시오, 모스 씨.\" 해머다운 씨가 말했다. \"여러분, 예술 작품으로서 이것을 살펴보시오. 이 늠름한 짐승의 자세는 그야말로 자연 그대로올시다. 면직 재킷을 입고 총을 손에 쥔 신사는 사냥을 나가는 길이지요. 멀리 보이는 저 반얀나무며 탑은 십중팔구 우리의 저 이름난 동방 영토 어느 흥미로운 명소를 본뜬 것이올시다. 이 물건에 얼마를 부르시겠소? 자, 신사 여러분, 나를 여기 온종일 붙들어 두지 마시고.\"\n\n누군가 오 실링을 불렀다. 그러자 군인 신사는 이 훌륭한 제안이 날아온 쪽으로 시선을 돌렸고, 거기서 젊은 숙녀 한 사람을 팔에 낀 또 다른 장교를 보았다. 두 사람 다 이 광경을 자못 재미있어하는 듯했고, 결국 이 물건은 반 기니에 그들에게 낙찰되었다. 식탁에 앉아 있던 그 사내는 이 한 쌍을 알아보고 어느 때보다 놀라고 당황한 표정이 되더니, 고개를 군복 옷깃 속으로 푹 파묻고는 그들을 아예 외면하려는 듯 등을 돌렸다.\n\n그날 해머다운 씨가 공개 경쟁에 부치는 영광을 누린 다른 온갖 물건들에 대해서는 일일이 언급할 생각이 없으나, 딱 하나만은 예외로 하자. 그것은 작은 사각형 피아노로, 집 위층에서 내려온 것이었다(예식용 그랜드피아노는 진작에 처분된 뒤였다). 젊은 숙녀는 빠르고 능숙한 손놀림으로 그것을 한번 쳐 보았고(그 바람에 장교는 다시 얼굴을 붉히고 흠칫했다), 그 차례가 돌아오자 그녀의 대리인이 응찰을 시작했다.\n\n그런데 여기서 경쟁이 붙었다. 식탁에 앉은 장교를 모시는 히브리인 부관이, 코끼리 그림을 산 이들이 고용한 히브리인 신사를 상대로 응찰에 나섰고, 이 작은 피아노를 두고 한바탕 치열한 싸움이 벌어졌다. 경쟁자들은 해머다운 씨의 부추김에 한껏 기세가 올랐다.\n\n마침내 경쟁이 한동안 길어진 끝에, 코끼리 대위와 숙녀는 다툼에서 물러났다. 그리고 망치가 떨어지자 경매인이 말했다. \"루이스 씨, 이십오 기니.\" 이리하여 루이스 씨의 주인이 이 작은 사각형 피아노의 임자가 되었다. 그 사내는 구매를 마치자 크게 안도한 듯 몸을 곧추세웠고, 입찰에 실패한 경쟁자들이 마침 그 순간 그를 얼핏 알아보았다. 숙녀가 자기 벗에게 말했다.\n\n\"어머나, 로던, 저분 도빈 대위잖아요.\"\n\n내 짐작으로는, 베키가 남편이 빌려다 준 새 피아노에 만족하지 못했거나, 아니면 그 악기 주인들이 더는 외상을 주기 싫다며 그것을 도로 가져가 버렸거나, 그도 아니면 그녀가 방금 사려고 했던 그 피아노에 각별한 애착을 품고 있었던 것이리라. 옛날에 우리의 사랑스러운 애밀리아 세들리의 그 자그마한 거실에서 그것을 치곤 하던 시절을 떠올리면서 말이다.\n\n경매는 러셀 광장의 그 오래된 집에서 열리고 있었는데, 이 이야기의 첫머리에서 우리가 함께 몇 차례 저녁나절을 보냈던 바로 그 집이다. 선량한 노인 존 세들리는 영락한 사람이 되어 있었다. 그의 이름이 증권 거래소에서 채무 불이행자로 공표되었고, 곧이어 파산과 상업적 파멸이 뒤따랐다. 오스본 씨의 집사는 그 이름난 포트와인을 사다가 길 건너 저장고로 옮기려고 찾아왔다. 온스 단위로 정교하게 만들어진 은수저와 은포크 열두 벌, 그리고 후식용 작은 것들 또한 열두 벌로 말하면, 세 사람의 젊은 주식 중개인이 있었는데(실은 스레드니들 거리의 데일·스피곳·데일 상회였다), 그들은 이 노인과 거래해 온 사이로, 그가 거래하는 모든 이에게 친절을 베풀던 시절에 그의 호의를 입은 적이 있었다. 그래서 이들은 난파선에서 건져 낸 이 자그마한 널빤지 한 조각을, 선량한 세들리 부인에게 자기들의 애정을 담아 보냈다. 그리고 피아노로 말하자면, 그것이 애밀리아의 것이었던 데다, 그녀가 그것을 그리워하며 이제 한 대 필요로 할지도 모르는 데다, 윌리엄 도빈 대위로 말하면 줄타기를 못하는 것만큼이나 피아노를 칠 줄 모르는 사람이었으니, 그가 자기 쓰려고 그 악기를 산 것은 아닐 공산이 크다.\n\n한마디로, 그것은 그날 저녁 풀럼 거리에서 갈라져 나온 어느 길에 자리한, 놀랄 만큼 자그마한 집에 도착했다. 그 길은 더없이 낭만적인 이름을 단 거리들 가운데 하나였는데(이름이 성 아델라이드 빌라, 애나마리아 로드 웨스트였다), 집들이 죄다 인형의 집처럼 생긴 곳이요, 이층 창밖을 내다보는 사람들은 당신이 보기에 영락없이 두 발을 응접실에 둔 채 앉아 있을 수밖에 없겠다 싶은 곳이며, 앞쪽 작은 정원의 관목들에는 어린아이들의 턱받이며, 작은 빨간 양말이며, 모자 따위(다우男 다우女 한해살이)가 사철 내내 피어나는 곳이요, 어디선가 딸랑거리는 스피넷 소리와 여자들의 노랫소리가 들려오는 곳이며, 작은 흑맥주 단지들이 난간에 걸려 볕을 쬐는 곳이요, 저녁이면 시티의 사무원들이 지친 걸음으로 터덜터덜 돌아오는 모습이 보이는 곳이었다. 바로 이곳에 세들리 씨의 사무원 클랩 씨가 거처를 두고 있었고, 파국이 닥치자 선량한 노신사는 이 피난처에 아내와 딸과 함께 몸을 숨겼던 것이다.\n\n조스 세들리는 집안의 불행을 알리는 소식이 당도하자, 자기 같은 천성을 지닌 사람이 으레 할 법한 대로 처신했다. 그는 런던에 오지는 않았으나, 어머니에게 편지를 써서 필요한 돈은 얼마든 자기 대리인에게서 받아 쓰라고 했고, 그리하여 마음씨 곱고 풀 죽은 노부모는 당장의 가난을 두려워할 일은 없게 되었다. 이렇게 해 놓고 조스는 첼트넘의 하숙집에서 거의 예전과 다름없이 지냈다. 이륜마차를 몰고, 클라레를 마시고, 카드놀이를 하고, 인도 시절 이야기를 늘어놓았으며, 아일랜드 출신 과부는 여느 때처럼 그를 위로하고 추어올렸다. 그가 보낸 돈은 절실하기는 했으나 부모에게 별다른 감흥을 주지 못했다. 애밀리아의 말로는, 파산 이후 아버지가 처음으로 고개를 든 날은 바로 그 젊은 주식 중개인들의 애정이 담긴 포크와 수저 꾸러미를 받은 날이었다고 한다. 아버지는 그것을 받고 어린아이처럼 울음을 터뜨렸으니, 정작 그 선물을 받은 당사자인 아내보다도 훨씬 더 깊이 감동했던 것이다. 상회를 대신해 그 수저를 사들인, 상회의 막내 에드워드 데일은 사실 애밀리아에게 자못 마음을 두고 있어서, 그 모든 사정에도 불구하고 그녀에게 청혼했다. 그는 1820년에 상당한 재산을 지닌 루이자 커츠 양(이름난 곡물상 하이엄·커츠 상회의 딸)과 결혼했고, 지금은 머스웰 힐의 우아한 별장에서 식구를 줄줄이 거느린 채 호화롭게 살고 있다. 그러나 이 선량한 친구에 대한 추억 때문에 본 줄거리에서 벗어나서는 안 되겠다.\n\n독자가 크롤리 대위 부부를 너무도 좋게 보아 준 나머지, 그들이 블룸즈베리처럼 외진 동네에까지 방문할 꿈을 꾸었으리라 여기지는 않으리라 믿는다. 방문하는 영예를 베풀려는 그 집안이 단지 유행에 뒤떨어졌을 뿐 아니라 돈마저 없어서 그들에게 어떤 식으로도 쓸모가 없으리라고 생각했다면 말이다. 레베카는 적잖은 친절을 입었던 그 안락한 옛집이 중개인들과 흥정꾼들에게 샅샅이 뒤집어지고, 그 조용한 집안의 보물들이 공공연한 모독과 약탈에 내맡겨진 광경을 보고 그야말로 깜짝 놀랐다. 도망친 지 한 달이 지나서야 그녀는 애밀리아를 떠올렸고, 로던은 껄껄 웃으며 다시 젊은 조지 오스본을 만나고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같다고 했다. \"그 친구 정말 마음에 드는 지인이지, 벡.\" 익살꾼이 덧붙였다. \"그 친구한테 말을 한 마리 더 팔고 싶단 말이야, 벡. 그 친구랑 당구나 몇 판 더 치고 싶고. 지금 같은 때 그 친구야말로 이를테면 요긴한 사람이지, 부인. 하하!\" 그러나 이런 식의 말이라 해서 로던 크롤리가 노름판에서 오스본 씨를 작정하고 속여먹을 마음을 품었다고 여겨서는 안 된다. 다만 허영의 시장의 거의 모든 노름 좋아하는 신사가 자기 이웃에게서 마땅히 누려야 할 몫이라고 여기는, 그 정도의 유리한 이득을 챙기고 싶었을 따름이다.\n\n노숙모는 좀처럼 \"마음을 풀 줄\" 몰랐다. 한 달이 흘렀다. 로던은 보울스 씨에게 문전박대당했고, 그의 하인들은 파크 레인의 집에 발붙일 데가 없었으며, 그의 편지는 뜯기지도 않은 채 되돌아왔다. 크롤리 양은 한 번도 바깥출입을 하지 않았으니, 몸이 편치 않았던 것이다. 그리고 뷰트 부인은 줄곧 자리를 지키며 곁을 떠나지 않았다. 크롤리와 그의 아내는 둘 다 뷰트 부인의 이 끈질긴 체류에서 불길한 조짐을 점쳤다.\n\n\"이런, 이제야 그 여자가 왜 줄곧 퀸스 크롤리에서 우리 둘을 붙여 놓으려 했는지 알 것 같군.\" 로던이 말했다.\n\n\"어쩌면 그렇게 교활한 여자가 다 있을까!\" 레베카가 내뱉었다.\n\n\"흠, 당신이 후회 안 한다면 나도 후회 안 해.\" 대위가 외쳤다. 그는 여전히 아내에게 푹 빠진 사랑의 황홀경에 젖어 있었고, 아내는 답례로 입맞춤을 선사했다. 그녀는 사실 남편의 그 너그러운 신뢰에 적잖이 흐뭇해하고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"이이가 머리만 조금 더 좋았더라면,\" 그녀는 속으로 생각했다. \"뭔가 큰일을 시켜 볼 수도 있을 텐데.\" 그러나 그녀는 자기가 남편을 어떻게 여기는지 결코 내색하지 않았다. 마구간과 장교 식당 이야기를 지칠 줄 모르는 흐뭇한 표정으로 들어 주었고, 그의 농담에는 죄다 웃어 주었으며, 마차용 말이 쓰러진 잭 스패터대시며, 도박장에서 붙들려 간 밥 마틴게일이며, 장애물 경마에 나갈 톰 생크바스에게 더없이 큰 관심을 보였다. 그가 집에 돌아오면 그녀는 생기 있고 행복해했으며, 그가 나갈 때면 어서 다녀오라고 떠밀었고, 그가 집에 머물면 그를 위해 피아노를 치고 노래를 불렀으며, 맛있는 음료를 만들어 주고, 저녁 식사를 챙기고, 슬리퍼를 데워 주고, 그의 영혼을 안락 속에 푹 잠기게 했다. 여자 중에서도 으뜸가는 여자가(우리 할머니가 하시던 말씀이다) 위선자라는 것이다. 우리는 그네들이 얼마나 많은 것을 우리에게 숨기는지, 더없이 천진하고 흉허물 없어 보일 때 얼마나 빈틈없이 경계하는지, 그토록 수월하게 짓는 그 솔직한 미소가 어르고 구슬리거나 따돌리거나 무장해제시키려는 덫인 경우가 얼마나 잦은지 알지 못한다. 단순한 바람둥이 여자들 이야기가 아니라, 가정의 귀감이요 여성 미덕의 본보기 같은 여자들을 두고 하는 말이다. 어리석은 남편의 둔함을 가려 주거나, 사나운 남편의 노여움을 어르고 달래는 여자를 보지 못한 이가 어디 있겠는가? 우리는 이 사랑스러운 종복 같은 태도를 받아들이고, 그래서 여자를 칭송한다. 우리는 이 어여쁜 배신을 진실이라 부른다. 훌륭한 살림꾼이란 어쩔 수 없이 사기꾼인 법이요, 코르넬리아의 남편 또한 보디발이 그랬듯 속아 넘어갔던 것이다. 다만 그 방식이 달랐을 뿐.\n\n이런 정성스러운 보살핌 덕분에, 저 산전수전 다 겪은 난봉꾼 로던 크롤리는 어느덧 더없이 행복하고 고분고분한 유부남으로 탈바꿈해 있었다. 그가 옛날에 들락거리던 곳들은 이제 그를 알아보지 못했다. 클럽에서 한두 번 그의 안부를 묻기는 했으나, 딱히 그를 그리워하지는 않았다. 허영의 시장의 그 노점들에서는 사람들이 좀처럼 서로를 그리워하는 법이 없다. 늘 미소 짓고 명랑한, 세상과 떨어져 사는 아내, 자그마하고 안락한 거처, 아늑한 식사, 정겨운 저녁나절은 죄다 새로움과 은밀함의 매력을 지니고 있었다. 결혼은 아직 세상에 공표되지도, 「모닝 포스트」에 발표되지도 않은 상태였다. 그가 재산 없는 여자와 맺어졌다는 것을 알았더라면, 그의 채권자들이 죄다 떼를 지어 그에게 몰려들었을 것이다. \"우리 친척들이야 나한테 손가락질이야 안 하겠지.\" 베키가 다소 씁쓸한 웃음을 지으며 말했다. 그래서 그녀는 사교계에서 자기 자리를 주장하기에 앞서, 노숙모가 화해할 마음이 들 때까지 기꺼이 기다리기로 했다. 그리하여 그녀는 브롬프턴에서 살았고, 그동안 아무도 만나지 않거나, 만나더라도 그녀의 자그마한 식당에 출입이 허락된 남편의 남자 친구 몇몇만을 만났다. 이들은 죄다 그녀에게 매료되었다. 그 자그마한 만찬이며, 웃고 떠드는 일이며, 식사 뒤의 음악은 이 즐거움에 끼어든 모든 이를 기쁘게 했다. 마틴게일 소령은 결혼 증명서를 보여 달라고 청할 생각조차 하지 않았고, 생크바스 대위는 그녀의 펀치 만드는 솜씨에 그야말로 홀딱 반했다. 그리고 젊은 스패터대시 중위로 말하면(피케 놀이를 좋아해서 크롤리가 곧잘 초대하던 사람이었는데) 크롤리 부인에게 보란 듯이 또 순식간에 마음을 빼앗겼다. 그러나 그녀 자신의 신중함과 정숙함은 한순간도 그녀를 저버리지 않았고, 불을 뿜듯 사납고 질투심 많은 무사라는 크롤리의 평판은 그 자그마한 아내에게 한층 더 완벽한 방패가 되어 주었다.\n\n이 도시에는 혈통도 좋고 행세깨나 하면서도 숙녀의 응접실에 단 한 번도 발을 들여 본 적 없는 신사들이 있다. 그러니 로던 크롤리의 결혼이 그의 고향 지방에서는, 물론 뷰트 부인이 소식을 퍼뜨려 놓은 터라, 입에 오르내렸을지언정, 런던에서는 의심을 받거나, 아무도 거들떠보지 않거나, 아예 입에 오르지조차 않았다. 그는 외상으로 안락하게 살았다. 그에게는 거액의 빚이라는 밑천이 있었는데, 빚이란 영리하게 부려 쓰면 사람을 여러 해 동안 너끈히 지탱해 주는 법이요, 도시의 어떤 사내들은 그 빚으로 현금을 가진 자들보다도 백 배는 더 떵떵거리며 사는 재간을 부린다. 실로, 런던 거리를 걷는 이라면 누구나 자기는 걸어가는데 자기 옆을 으리으리하게 말을 타고 지나가는 사내 대여섯쯤은 짚어 낼 수 있지 않겠는가. 유행의 총아로 떠받들리고, 상인들이 굽실거리며 마차에 태워 올리고, 무엇 하나 마다하는 법 없이, 대체 무슨 돈으로 사는지 알 수 없는 그런 자들 말이다. 우리는 잭 스리프트리스가 공원에서 뽐내며 말을 달리거나, 사륜마차를 타고 펠 멜 거리를 쏜살같이 내려가는 모습을 본다. 그의 그 기적 같은 식기에 차려진 만찬을 먹는다. \"이게 대체 어떻게 시작된 거지,\" 우리는 말한다. \"또 어떻게 끝나려나?\" \"이보게,\" 언젠가 잭이 하는 말을 들었다. \"난 유럽의 수도란 수도에는 죄다 빚을 지고 있다네.\" 끝장은 언젠가 반드시 오고야 말 테지만, 그러는 동안에도 잭은 여전히 잘만 살아간다. 사람들은 그와 기꺼이 악수를 나누고, 이따금 그를 두고 수군거리는 자잘하고 음침한 소문들은 못 들은 척하며, 그를 두고 마음씨 좋고 유쾌하고 거침없는 친구라고 일컫는다.\n\n레베카가 바로 이런 부류의 신사와 결혼했음을 우리는 진실의 명령에 따라 고백하지 않을 수 없다. 그의 집에는 무엇이든 풍족했으나 오직 현금만은 없었고, 그들의 살림은 일찌감치 그 부족함을 느꼈다. 그러던 어느 날 「관보」를 읽다가 \"G. 오스본 중위, 전출하는 스미스의 후임으로 대위직을 매수함\"이라는 발표와 마주치자, 로던은 애밀리아의 연인에 관한 그 소회를 입에 올렸고, 그것이 결국 러셀 광장 방문으로 이어졌던 것이다.\n\n로던과 그의 아내가 경매장에서 도빈 대위에게 말을 붙여 레베카의 옛 지인들에게 닥친 그 변고의 내막을 알아보려 했을 때, 대위는 이미 자취를 감춘 뒤였다. 그래서 그들이 알아낸 정보라고는 경매장의 떠돌이 짐꾼이나 중개인에게서 주워들은 것이 전부였다.\n\n\"저 갈고리 같은 부리들 좀 봐요.\" 베키가 그림을 옆구리에 낀 채 신이 나서 이륜마차에 오르며 말했다. \"꼭 전투 끝난 뒤에 몰려드는 독수리 떼 같다니까요.\"\n\n\"글쎄. 난 전투에 나가 본 적이 없어서 말이야, 여보. 마틴게일한테 물어봐. 그 친구는 스페인에서 블레이즈 장군의 부관으로 있었으니까.\"\n\n\"세들리 씨는 참 친절한 노인이셨는데.\" 레베카가 말했다. \"그분이 영락하셨다니 정말 안됐어요.\"\n\n\"아, 주식 중개인들이야 파산이 다반사지. 흔한 일이라고, 알잖아.\" 로던이 말의 귀에 앉은 파리를 쳐 내며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"우리가 그 은식기를 좀 살 형편이 됐더라면 좋았을 텐데요, 로던.\" 아내가 감상에 젖어 말을 이었다. \"스물다섯 기니는 그 작은 피아노 값치고는 어처구니없이 비쌌어요. 애밀리아가 학교를 마치고 나왔을 때, 우리가 브로드우드 가게에서 그 애 주려고 골라 준 거였거든요. 그때는 겨우 서른다섯 기니밖에 안 들었는데.\"\n\n\"그 뭐냐, 그 '오스본'인가 하는 친구는 이제 발을 빼겠지, 아마. 집안이 거덜났으니 말이야. 당신의 그 예쁘장한 친구가 얼마나 풀이 죽겠어. 안 그래, 베키?\"\n\n\"그 앤 잘 추스를 거예요.\" 베키가 미소 지으며 말했다. 그러고는 두 사람은 마차를 몰아 가며 다른 이야기로 화제를 돌렸다.\n\n이 어리둥절한 이야기는 이제 잠시 더없이 유명한 사건들과 인물들 틈에 끼어들어, 역사의 옷자락에 매달리게 되었다. 코르시카 출신의 벼락출세자 나폴레옹 보나파르트의 독수리들이, 엘바섬에 잠시 머문 뒤 둥지를 틀었던 프로방스에서 날아올라, 첨탑에서 첨탑으로 옮겨 가며 마침내 노트르담의 탑들에 이르렀을 때, 저 황제의 새들이 과연 런던 블룸즈버리 교구의 어느 자그마한 한구석에까지 눈길을 주었을지 나는 자못 궁금하다. 그토록 고요하여, 저 거대한 날개가 윙윙거리며 퍼덕이는 소리마저 거기서는 누구의 주목도 받지 못하고 지나갔으리라 여길 법한 그곳 말이다.\n\n\"나폴레옹이 칸에 상륙했다.\" 이런 소식은 빈에서는 공황을 불러일으키고, 러시아로 하여금 패를 내려놓게 하고, 프로이센을 한구석으로 끌고 가게 하고, 탈레랑과 메테르니히로 하여금 머리를 맞대고 끄덕이게 하며, 하르덴베르크 공작은 물론 지금의 런던데리 후작까지도 당혹게 했을지 모른다. 하지만 이 소식이 러셀 광장의 한 젊은 숙녀에게 무슨 영향을 끼친단 말인가. 그녀가 잠들어 있는 동안 그 집 문 앞에서는 야경꾼이 시각을 알리며 노래를 불렀고, 그녀가 광장을 거닐기라도 하면 그곳의 철책과 교구 관리가 그녀를 지켜 주었으며, 사우샘프턴 가에 리본을 사러 그 짧디짧은 거리를 걸어갈 때조차 흑인 하인 샘보가 커다란 지팡이를 들고 뒤를 따랐고, 봉급을 받는 자든 받지 않는 자든 수많은 수호천사가 늘 그녀를 보살피고, 입혀 주고, 잠자리에 들여 주고, 지켜 주지 않았던가. 봉 디외, 내 말하건대, 저 위대한 황제의 투쟁이 운명적으로 휘몰아치는 일이, 러셀 광장에서 사랑을 속삭이거나 모슬린 옷깃에 수를 놓는 일에 몰두해 있는 가엾고 작고 무해한 열여덟 소녀에게 영향을 끼치지 않고서는 일어날 수 없다니, 이 어찌 가혹한 일이 아니겠는가? 너 또한, 다정하고 소박한 꽃이여! 비록 홀번의 그늘 아래 웅크리고 있다 한들, 저 거대하게 포효하는 전쟁의 폭풍이 여기까지 와서 너를 쓸어 가려는 것이냐? 그렇다. 나폴레옹은 마지막 판돈을 던지고 있고, 가엾고 작은 에미 세들리의 행복도 어찌어찌 그 일부를 이루고 있는 것이다.\n\n* 봉 디외: 프랑스어로 '맙소사' 정도의 감탄사.\n우선, 그 운명적인 소식과 함께 그녀 아버지의 재산이 휩쓸려 내려갔다. 운 없는 이 노신사의 투기는 근래 들어 죄다 어긋나기만 했다. 투자가 실패하고, 거래처가 파산하고, 그가 떨어지리라 계산한 채권은 도리어 올랐다. 굳이 일일이 따져 무엇하겠는가? 성공이란 드물고 더디지만, 파멸이 얼마나 빠르고 손쉬운지는 누구나 아는 법이다. 세들리 노인은 그 서글픈 속사정을 혼자만 끌어안고 있었다. 그 조용하고 풍족한 집 안에서는 만사가 여느 때와 다름없이 굴러가는 듯했다. 사람 좋은 안주인은 추호의 의심도 없이 분주한 듯한 게으름과 나날의 한가로운 소일거리를 좇았고, 딸은 여전히 이기적이고도 애틋한 한 가지 생각에 빠져 그 밖의 온 세상에는 도무지 무심했으니, 그러던 차에 마침내 그 마지막 파국이 들이닥쳐, 이 훌륭한 가족이 그 아래 쓰러지고 만 것이다.\n\n어느 날 밤 세들리 부인은 파티 초대장을 쓰고 있었다. 오스본 집안에서 파티를 열었으니 그녀도 뒤질 수는 없는 노릇이었다. 시내에서 몹시 늦게 돌아온 존 세들리는 벽난로 옆에 말없이 앉아 있었고, 그 사이 아내는 그에게 재잘거리고 있었다. 에미는 몸이 좋지 않고 풀이 죽어 제 방으로 올라간 뒤였다. \"그 애가 행복하질 않아요.\" 어머니가 말을 이었다. \"조지 오스본이 그 애를 소홀히 한다고요. 난 그 사람들 거드름에 진절머리가 나요. 그 집 딸들은 이 삼 주째 우리 집엔 코빼기도 안 비치고, 조지는 시내에 두 번이나 왔다 가면서 들르지도 않았어요. 에드워드 데일이 오페라에서 그 애를 봤대요. 에드워드라면 분명 그 애와 결혼할 거예요. 게다가 도빈 대위라는 사람도, 내 보기엔 그럴 것 같고요. 다만 난 군인이라면 죄다 싫지만요. 조지가 어쩜 그리 멋쟁이가 됐는지. 그 군인 티 내는 꼴이라니, 정말! 우리도 그 사람들 못지않다는 걸 어떤 사람들한텐 보여 줘야 해요. 에드워드 데일한테 조금만 부추겨 주면, 두고 보세요. 우리도 파티를 열어야겠어요, 여보. 왜 말이 없어요, 존? 다음다음 주 화요일이라고 할까요? 왜 대답이 없어요? 맙소사, 존, 무슨 일이 있었어요?\"\n\n존 세들리는 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 자기에게 달려오는 아내를 맞았다. 그는 아내를 두 팔로 끌어안고 다급한 목소리로 말했다. \"우린 망했소, 메리. 처음부터 다시 시작해야 하오, 여보. 당신이 다 알아 두는 게, 그것도 당장 아는 게 좋겠소.\" 이렇게 말하면서 그는 온몸을 부들부들 떨었고, 거의 쓰러질 지경이었다. 그는 이 소식이 아내를 짓눌러 버리리라 생각했다. 단 한 번도 모진 말을 건넨 적 없는 그 아내를 말이다. 그러나 그녀에게 그 충격이 아무리 갑작스러웠다 해도, 정작 가장 크게 흔들린 사람은 그 자신이었다. 그가 다시 의자에 주저앉자, 위로하는 역할을 맡은 것은 아내였다. 그녀는 떨리는 그의 손을 잡아 입을 맞추고는 제 목에 둘렀다. 그녀는 그를 자기 존이라고, 사랑하는 존이라고, 자기 영감이라고, 자상한 영감이라고 불렀다. 그녀는 두서없는 사랑과 다정함의 말을 수없이 쏟아 냈다. 그 한결같은 목소리와 꾸밈없는 손길은 이 서글픈 마음을 형언할 수 없는 기쁨과 고뇌로 끌어올렸고, 무겁게 짓눌린 그의 영혼을 북돋우고 어루만져 주었다.\n\n긴 밤이 흐르는 동안 둘이 함께 앉아 있었고, 가엾은 세들리가 꽉 막혔던 속을 털어놓으며 자신의 손실과 곤경의 사연을 — 가장 오랜 벗 몇몇의 배신과, 도무지 기대조차 하지 못했던 이들의 사내다운 친절을 — 온전히 고백하던 그 긴 밤에, 단 한 번, 오직 단 한 번만 그 한결같은 아내가 감정에 무너졌다.\n\n\"맙소사, 맙소사, 이건 에미의 가슴을 찢어 놓을 거예요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n아버지는 그 가엾은 딸을 잊고 있었다. 그 애는 머리 위층에서 잠들지 못한 채 불행하게 누워 있었다. 벗들과 집과 자상한 부모에 둘러싸여 있으면서도, 그녀는 혼자였다. 사람이 누군가에게 모든 것을 털어놓을 수 있는 상대가 과연 몇이나 되겠는가? 공감해 주는 이가 없는데 누가 마음을 열겠으며, 도무지 이해하지 못할 이들에게 말할 까닭이 어디 있겠는가? 우리의 온순한 애밀리아가 바로 그렇게 외로웠다. 털어놓을 무언가가 생긴 그 순간부터, 말하자면 그녀에게는 속을 터놓을 상대가 없었던 것이다. 그녀는 늙은 어머니에게 자신의 의혹과 근심을 말할 수 없었고, 언니가 되어 줄 뻔했던 그 여자들은 날이 갈수록 더 서먹하게만 느껴졌다. 그리고 그녀에게는 차마 스스로에게조차 인정할 수 없는 불안과 두려움이 있었지만, 늘 남몰래 그것을 곱씹고 있었다.\n\n그녀의 마음은 조지 오스본이 자기에게 합당하고 충실한 사람이라고 우겨 보려 안간힘을 썼지만, 실은 그렇지 않다는 걸 그녀도 알고 있었다. 그녀가 얼마나 많은 말을 건넸으되, 그에게서 아무런 메아리도 얻지 못했던가. 이기심과 무관심을 얼마나 많이 짐작하고 마주하면서, 또 얼마나 끈질기게 떨쳐 내야 했던가. 이 가엾고 작은 순교자가 나날의 이 싸움과 고통을 대체 누구에게 털어놓을 수 있었겠는가? 그녀의 영웅조차 그녀를 절반밖에 이해하지 못했다. 그녀는 자기가 사랑하는 그 남자가 자기보다 못한 사람임을 차마 인정할 수 없었고, 너무 일찍 마음을 줘 버렸다고 느낄 수도 없었다. 한번 내준 이상, 그 순결하고 수줍은 처녀는 너무도 정숙하고, 너무도 다정하고, 너무도 잘 믿고, 너무도 여리고, 너무나 여자였기에 그것을 도로 거두어들이지 못했다. 우리는 여자들의 애정 앞에서 터키 사람들이나 다름없고, 게다가 그녀들로 하여금 우리의 교리에 따르도록 만들어 놓았다. 우리는 그녀들의 몸뚱이가 바깥나들이하는 것은 제법 너그럽게 허락하여, 베일과 야크마크 대신 미소와 곱슬머리와 분홍 보닛으로 가리게 한다. 그러나 그녀들의 영혼만큼은 오직 한 남자에게만 보여야 하는 법이고, 그녀들은 마지못한 기색도 없이 순순히 따르며, 우리의 노예로서 집 안에 머무는 데 — 우리를 섬기고 우리를 위해 궂은일을 하는 데 동의하는 것이다.\n\n* 야크마크: 이슬람 여성이 얼굴을 가리는 데 쓰던 천.\n이 온순하고 작은 마음이 그토록 갇히고 시달리고 있던 차에, 서기 1815년 3월, 나폴레옹이 칸에 상륙하고, 루이 18세가 달아나고, 온 유럽이 경악에 빠지고, 채권이 폭락하고, 늙은 존 세들리가 파산했던 것이다.\n\n우리는 이 훌륭한 노 주식 중개인이 상업적 죽음을 맞기에 앞서 겪어야 했던 그 파멸의 마지막 고통과 단말마를 일일이 뒤쫓을 생각은 없다. 사람들은 그를 증권 거래소에서 파산자로 선언했고, 그는 자신의 사업장에 나타나지 않았으며, 그의 어음은 부도 처리되었고, 그의 파산 행위는 공식화되었다. 러셀 광장의 집과 가구는 압류되어 경매에 부쳐졌고, 우리가 본 대로 그와 그 가족은 어디든 머리를 숨길 만한 곳으로 내쫓겼다.\n\n존 세들리는, 그동안 우리 지면에 이따금 등장했던, 그리고 이제 가난 탓에 어쩔 수 없이 작별해야 하는 그 집안의 하인들을 차마 돌아볼 마음이 나지 않았다. 그 훌륭한 사람들의 봉급은, 큰 액수의 빚만 지는 이들이 흔히 보이는 그 정확함으로 깔끔하게 청산되었다. 그들은 좋은 일자리를 떠나는 것을 아쉬워했으나, 흠모하던 주인 내외와 헤어진다고 가슴이 미어지지는 않았다. 애밀리아의 하녀는 위로의 말을 아낌없이 늘어놓았지만, 시내의 좀 더 점잖은 동네에서 제 신세를 고쳐 보겠다며 아주 체념한 듯 떠나갔다. 흑인 하인 샘보는 제 직종에 대한 묘한 집착으로 술집을 차리기로 마음먹었다. 정직한 노파 블렌킨솝만은 정말로, 조스와 애밀리아가 태어나는 것을 보았고 존 세들리와 그 아내가 연애하던 시절까지 지켜본 터라, 봉급도 없이 그들 곁에 남으려 했다. 그동안 그 집에서 일하며 제법 큰돈을 모아 두었던 것이다. 그녀는 영락한 사람들을 따라 그들의 새롭고 초라한 피난처까지 동행하여, 한동안 그들을 보살피기도 하고 그들에게 투덜대기도 했다.\n\n뒤이어 벌어진 채권자들과의 분쟁에서, 모욕당한 이 노신사의 심사를 어찌나 모질게 들쑤셔 놓던지 그가 여섯 주 만에 지난 십오 년보다 더 늙어 버릴 지경이었는데, 세들리의 그 모든 적수 가운데 가장 단호하고 완강했던 이는 다름 아닌 그의 오랜 벗이자 이웃인 존 오스본이었던 듯하다. 세들리가 인생을 일으켜 세워 준 사람, 세들리에게 백 가지 은혜를 입은 사람, 그 아들이 세들리의 딸과 결혼하기로 되어 있던 바로 그 사람 말이다. 이 사정 가운데 어느 하나만으로도 오스본의 반목이 그토록 쓰라렸던 까닭을 설명하기에는 충분할 것이다.\n\n어떤 사람이 다른 이에게 매우 두드러진 은혜를 입고 나서 훗날 그와 다투게 되면, 이른바 일반적인 염치의 감각이 그 전자를, 한낱 낯선 사람보다 훨씬 더 가혹한 원수로 만들어 버린다. 그런 경우 자기 자신의 모진 심보와 배은망덕을 변명하자면, 상대편의 죄를 입증하지 않을 수 없는 법이다. 당신이 이기적이고 잔인하며 투기 실패에 화가 난 것이 아니라 — 아니, 아니다 — 당신의 동업자가 더없이 비열한 배신과 더없이 음흉한 속셈으로 당신을 그 일에 끌어들였다는 것이다. 단지 앞뒤를 맞추려는 일념에서, 박해자는 영락한 자가 악당임을 보여야만 하는 법이다. 그러지 않으면 박해자인 자기 자신이 비열한 인간이 되어 버리니까.\n\n그리고 일반적인 규칙으로 말하건대 — 이는 모질게 굴려는 마음을 먹은 모든 채권자의 마음을 제법 편안하게 해 줄 만한데 — 곤경에 처한 사람치고 완전히 정직한 자란 아마 없을 것이다. 그들은 무언가를 숨긴다. 행운의 가능성을 부풀린다. 일의 실상을 감춘다. 가망이 없는데도 잘되어 간다고 말하고, 파산의 벼랑 끝에서도 미소 띤 얼굴을(그것은 음울한 미소다) 유지하며, 피할 수 없는 파멸을 며칠이라도 더 미루려고 무슨 핑계든 무슨 돈이든 붙잡으려 든다. \"그런 부정직함은 끌어내려야지.\" 채권자가 의기양양하게 말하며 가라앉아 가는 적을 욕한다. \"이 바보야, 왜 지푸라기를 붙잡느냐?\" 차분한 양식이 물에 빠진 사람에게 이렇게 말한다. \"이 악당아, 돌이킬 수 없는 파산 공고에 뛰어들기를 왜 꺼리느냐?\" 번영이, 저 검은 심연에서 허우적대는 가엾은 자에게 이렇게 말한다. 더없이 가까운 벗들, 더없이 정직한 사람들이 돈 문제로 틀어지면 서로를 사기꾼이라 의심하고 비난하기를 얼마나 선뜻 해치우는지, 이를 눈여겨보지 않은 이가 어디 있겠는가? 누구나 그렇게 한다. 누구나 다 옳은 것이리라. 그리고 세상은 악당인 것이다.\n\n게다가 오스본에게는 지난날 베푼 은혜에 대한 견딜 수 없는 의식이 있어 그를 부추기고 들쑤셨으니, 이런 것은 늘 적의를 더 키우는 원인이 되는 법이다. 끝으로, 그는 세들리의 딸과 자기 아들 사이의 혼약을 깨뜨려야 했다. 그런데 그 일이 이미 상당히 깊이 진척된 데다, 그 가엾은 처녀의 행복과 어쩌면 평판까지도 위태로워진 터라, 파혼의 더없이 강력한 사유를 내세울 필요가 있었고, 그러자면 존 오스본으로서는 존 세들리가 정말이지 아주 못된 인간임을 입증해야만 했다.\n\n그리하여 채권자 모임에서 그는 세들리를 향해 어찌나 사나운 멸시를 드러냈던지, 그 영락한 파산자의 가슴을 거의 찢어 놓을 뻔했다. 그는 조지가 애밀리아와 교제하는 것을 즉각 금지하면서, 만약 그 명령을 어긴다면 저주를 퍼붓겠다고 젊은 아들을 위협했고, 그 가엾고 죄 없는 처녀를 더없이 비열하고 더없이 교활한 여우라고 헐뜯었다. 분노와 증오의 큰 조건 가운데 하나는, 앞서 말했듯 앞뒤를 맞추기 위해, 미워하는 대상을 두고 거짓말을 지어내고 또 그것을 믿어야 한다는 점이다.\n\n저 큰 파국이 닥쳤을 때 — 파멸의 통보와, 러셀 광장에서의 퇴거와, 그녀와 조지 사이의 모든 것이 끝났다는 선언이, 다시 말해 그녀와 사랑 사이가, 그녀와 행복 사이가, 그녀와 세상에 대한 믿음 사이가 다 끝났다는 선언이 닥쳤을 때 — 존 오스본의 모진 편지가 몇 줄 무뚝뚝한 문장으로 그녀에게 알리기를, 그녀 아버지의 행실이 그러한 것이었으니 두 집안 사이의 모든 약속은 끝났다고 했을 때 — 그 마지막 판결이 내려졌을 때, 그것은 그녀의 부모만큼, 또 그녀의 어머니가 짐작했던 만큼 그녀에게 큰 충격을 주지는 않았다(존 세들리 본인은 자기 사업과 산산조각 난 명예의 폐허 속에서 완전히 무너져 있었으니까). 애밀리아는 그 소식을 몹시 창백하고 차분하게 받아들였다. 그것은 오래전부터 앞서갔던 어두운 예감을 그저 확인해 주는 것일 뿐이었다. 그것은 한낱 판결문의 낭독, 그러니까 그녀가 오래전에 저지른 죄 — 잘못 사랑한 죄, 이성을 거슬러 너무도 격렬하게 사랑한 죄에 대한 판결문의 낭독일 뿐이었다. 그녀는 이제 자기 생각을 전보다 더 털어놓지도 않았다. 모든 희망이 끝났음을 확신하게 된 지금이, 희망이 사라졌음을 느끼면서도 차마 고백하지 못하던 이전보다 그다지 더 불행해 보이지도 않았다. 그렇게 그녀는 아무런 표시도 변화도 없이 큰 집에서 작은 집으로 옮겨 갔다. 거의 내내 자기 작은 방에 틀어박혀, 말없이 야위어 가며, 하루하루 시들어 갔다. 모든 여자가 다 그렇다는 말은 아니다. 사랑하는 불록 양, 나는 당신의 가슴은 이런 식으로 무너지지 않으리라 생각한다. 당신은 올바른 신념을 지닌, 의지가 굳센 젊은 여인이니까. 감히 내 가슴이 그러리라고도 말하지 않겠다. 그것도 괴로움을 겪긴 했으나, 고백건대 살아남았으니 말이다. 하지만 그렇게도 곱게 빚어진, 그렇게도 여리고, 섬세하고, 다정한 영혼들이 더러 있는 법이다.\n\n늙은 존 세들리는 조지와 애밀리아 사이의 일을 떠올리거나 입에 올릴 때마다, 오스본 씨가 보였던 것에 거의 맞먹는 쓰라림을 드러냈다. 그는 오스본과 그 가족을 매정하고, 사악하고, 배은망덕한 자들이라며 저주했다. 이 땅의 어떤 힘으로도 자기 딸을 그런 악당의 아들에게 시집보내도록 자기를 부추길 수는 없으리라고 그는 맹세했고, 에미에게 조지를 마음에서 몰아내고 그에게서 받은 선물과 편지를 죄다 돌려보내라고 명령했다.\n\n그녀는 따르겠노라 약속하고 순종하려 애썼다. 그녀는 두세 가지 패물을 챙겨 두었다. 그리고 편지들은, 보관해 두었던 곳에서 꺼내 — 이미 줄줄 외우고 있지 않기라도 한 듯 — 다시 읽고 또 읽었지만, 차마 그것들과는 헤어질 수 없었다. 그 일만큼은 그녀에게 너무 벅찼다. 그녀는 그것들을 다시 가슴에 품었다 — 죽은 아이를 품에 안고 어르는 여인을 당신도 본 적이 있으리라, 꼭 그처럼. 어린 애밀리아는 이 마지막 위안마저 빼앗긴다면 자기가 죽거나 아예 정신을 놓아 버리리라 느꼈다. 그 편지들이 오면 그녀는 얼마나 얼굴을 붉히며 환해지곤 했던가! 남몰래 읽으려고 가슴을 두근거리며 얼마나 종종걸음으로 빠져나가곤 했던가! 편지가 냉랭할 때조차, 이 애틋하고 작은 영혼은 그것을 얼마나 비뚤어지게 따스함으로 해석해 냈던가. 편지가 짧거나 이기적일 때면, 쓴 사람을 위해 또 얼마나 많은 변명을 찾아냈던가!\n\n그녀가 곱씹고 또 곱씹은 것은 바로 이 몇 장 안 되는 하찮은 종이들이었다. 그녀는 지난 삶 속에서 살았다. 편지 한 장 한 장이 그 시절의 어떤 정황을 되살려 내는 듯했다. 그녀는 그 모든 것을 얼마나 또렷이 기억하고 있었던가! 그의 표정과 말투, 그의 옷차림, 그가 한 말과 그 말투 — 죽어 버린 애정의 이 유물과 추억들이 이 세상에서 그녀에게 남은 전부였다. 그리고 그녀 삶의 일과란, 바로 사랑의 시신을 지키는 일이었다.\n\n그녀는 형언할 수 없는 그리움으로 죽음을 바라보았다. 그러면, 하고 그녀는 생각했다, 나는 언제까지나 그분을 따라갈 수 있을 거야. 나는 그녀의 행실을 칭찬하거나, 불록 양이 본받을 본보기로 떠받드는 것이 아니다. 불록 양은 이 가엾고 작은 사람보다 자기 감정을 다스리는 법을 더 잘 안다. 불록 양이라면 저 경솔한 애밀리아처럼 자신을 내맡기는 짓은 결코 하지 않았으리라. 돌이킬 수 없이 사랑을 맹세하고, 마음을 죄다 털어 고백해 버리고도, 얻은 것이라곤 아무것도 없으니 — 그저 한순간에 뚝 부러져 버리는 하찮은 약속 하나뿐이었다. 긴 약혼이란, 한쪽은 마음대로 지키거나 깨뜨릴 수 있으면서 다른 쪽의 모든 밑천은 죄다 걸리게 만드는 동업이다.\n\n그러니 조심하라, 젊은 숙녀들이여. 약혼에는 신중하라. 솔직하게 사랑하기를 꺼려라. 느끼는 바를 결코 다 말하지 말라. 아니, (더 나은 방법은) 아주 조금만 느껴라. 너무 일찍 정직하고 마음을 다 내준 결과가 어떠한지를 보라. 그리고 너 자신도 모든 이도 다 믿지 말라. 변호사가 들러리와 속내를 터놓는 친구 노릇을 하는 프랑스에서 하듯, 그렇게 결혼을 성사시켜라. 어쨌든, 너를 불편하게 만들 만한 감정일랑 결코 품지 말고, 필요한 순간에 언제든 거두어들일 수 없는 약속일랑 결코 하지 말라. 그것이 허영의 시장에서 출세하고, 존경받고, 정숙한 평판을 얻는 길이다.\n\n만약 애밀리아가, 아버지의 파멸로 자신이 막 쫓겨난 그 사교계에서 자기를 두고 오가는 평을 들을 수 있었다면, 자신의 죄가 무엇인지, 자신의 평판이 얼마나 완전히 위태로워졌는지 알았을 것이다. 그토록 죄스러운 경솔함이라니, 스미스 부인은 들어 본 적도 없었다. 그토록 흉측한 허물없음이라니, 브라운 부인은 늘 비난해 오던 바였고, 그 결말은 자기 딸들에게 좋은 경고가 될 만했다. \"오스본 대위가 파산자의 딸과 결혼할 수야 없는 노릇이지요.\" 도빈 댁 따님들이 말했다. \"그 아비한테 사기당한 것만 해도 진절머리가 나는데. 그리고 그 애밀리아라는 애로 말하면, 그 어리석음이란 정말이지 도를 넘어서—\"\n\n\"도를 넘다니 뭐가 도를 넘었다는 거요?\" 도빈 대위가 버럭 소리쳤다. \"그 둘이 어릴 적부터 약혼한 사이가 아니었소? 그게 결혼이나 다름없지 않았소? 이 땅의 어느 누가, 저토록 더없이 사랑스럽고, 더없이 순결하고, 더없이 다정하고, 더없이 천사 같은 처녀를 두고 감히 한마디라도 입에 올린단 말이오?\"\n\n\"어머, 윌리엄, 우리한테까지 그렇게 거만하게 굴진 마. 우린 남자가 아니잖아. 너랑 싸울 수도 없는걸.\" 제인 양이 말했다. \"우린 세들리 양을 두고 아무 험담도 안 했어. 다만 그 애 행실이 처음부터 끝까지 더없이 경솔했다는 거지, 그보다 더한 말은 안 하더라도. 그리고 그 부모는 분명 그런 불운을 당해도 싼 사람들이라는 거고.\"\n\n\"이제 세들리 양도 자유로운 몸이 됐으니, 네가 직접 청혼하는 게 어때, 윌리엄?\" 앤 양이 비꼬듯 물었다. \"더없이 어울리는 집안끼리의 인연이 될 텐데. 호! 호!\"\n\n\"내가 그 여자와 결혼한다고!\" 도빈이 얼굴을 잔뜩 붉히며 빠르게 말했다. \"당신들이야 그렇게 손바닥 뒤집듯 마음을 바꿔 대니, 그 여자도 그러리라 여기는 거요? 그 천사를 비웃고 빈정대 보시오. 그녀는 듣지도 못하니까. 게다가 그녀는 비참하고 불운하니, 비웃음을 받아 마땅하겠지. 계속 농담이나 하시오, 앤. 당신은 이 집안의 익살꾼이고, 다른 사람들도 그걸 듣길 좋아하니까.\"\n\n\"다시 말하지만 우린 병영에 있는 게 아니야, 윌리엄.\" 앤 양이 한마디 했다.\n\n\"병영이라, 맙소사 — 병영에서 누가 당신들처럼 지껄여 보라지.\" 발끈한 이 영국 사자가 소리쳤다. \"주피터께 맹세코, 어떤 사내가 그녀를 두고 한마디라도 입에 올리는 걸 한번 들어 봤으면 좋겠소. 하지만 사내들은 이런 식으로 떠들지 않아, 앤. 떼 지어 모여서 쉭쉭대고, 빽빽거리고, 꽥꽥대는 건 여자들뿐이지. 자, 저리 가시오 — 울기는 또 왜 우는 거요. 난 그저 당신들이 거위 한 쌍이라고 했을 뿐이오.\" 앤 양의 발그레한 눈이 여느 때처럼 촉촉해지기 시작하는 것을 알아채고 윌 도빈이 말했다. \"그래, 거위가 아니라 백조요 — 뭐든 좋을 대로 하시오, 다만 제발, 제발 세들리 양만은 좀 내버려 두시오.\"\n\n저 어리석고, 사내들에게 추파나 던지고 곁눈질이나 해 대는 것에게 윌리엄이 보이는 그런 맹목적인 빠짐이란 일찍이 들어 본 적이 없다고, 어머니와 누이들은 한데 입을 모았다. 그리고 그 여자가 오스본과의 약혼이 깨졌으니 곧장 또 다른 숭배자이자 대위인 그 사람을 잡아채지나 않을까 전전긍긍했다. 이런 우려 속에서, 이 훌륭한 젊은 여인들은 분명 자기네 경험에 비추어, 아니 (아직은 결혼하거나 약혼자를 차 버릴 기회조차 없었으니) 차라리 옳고 그름에 대한 자기네 나름의 관념에 비추어 판단했던 것이다.\n\n\"연대가 해외로 파견 명령을 받았으니 천만다행이에요, 어머니.\" 딸들이 말했다. \"이 위험만큼은 적어도 우리 오빠가 면하게 됐으니까요.\"\n\n과연 사실이 그러했다. 그리하여, 우리가 지금 펼치고 있는 이 허영의 시장의 가정 희극에 한 배역을 맡아, 프랑스 황제가 등장하게 되는 것이다. 이 위풍당당한 무언의 인물이 끼어들지 않았던들 이 희극은 결코 무대에 오르지 못했으리라. 부르봉 왕가와 존 세들리 씨를 망가뜨린 것이 바로 그였다. 그가 수도에 당도하자 그를 거기서 지켜 내려고 온 프랑스가 무장하고 일어섰으며, 그를 몰아내려고 온 유럽이 일어선 것이다. 프랑스 국민과 군대가 샹드마르스에서 독수리 깃발을 둘러싸고 충성을 맹세하는 동안, 네 갈래의 막강한 유럽 대군이 저 거대한 '독수리 사냥'을 위해 움직이기 시작했다. 그 가운데 하나가 영국군이었으니, 우리의 두 영웅 도빈 대위와 오스본 대위도 그 일부를 이루고 있었다.\n\n나폴레옹의 탈출과 상륙 소식은 용맹한 ○○연대에 불같은 환희와 열광으로 받아들여졌는데, 그 유명한 부대를 아는 이라면 누구든 이해할 만한 일이다. 연대장에서부터 부대 안의 가장 어린 고수에 이르기까지, 모두가 희망과 야망과 애국적 열기로 가득 차서, 유럽의 평화를 흩뜨려 놓으러 와 준 것을 마치 개인적 호의라도 되는 양 프랑스 황제에게 감사했다. 이제야말로 ○○연대가 그토록 오래 갈망해 온 때, 곧 자기네도 이베리아 반도의 노병들 못지않게 싸울 수 있음을, 또 ○○연대의 그 모든 기개와 용맹이 서인도 제도와 황열병에 죽어 없어지지 않았음을 전우들에게 보여 줄 때가 온 것이었다. 스터블과 스푸니는 돈을 들이지 않고도 자기 중대를 얻기를 기대했다. 이번 출정이 끝나기 전에(그녀는 그 출정에 함께하기로 작정했다), 소령 부인 오다우드는 자신을 '오다우드 대령 부인, 바스 훈장 수훈자'라고 서명하게 되기를 바랐다. 우리의 두 친구(도빈과 오스본)도 다른 이들 못지않게 들떠 있었으니, 저마다 제 방식대로 — 도빈 씨는 아주 조용하게, 오스본 씨는 아주 떠들썩하고 활기차게 — 제 임무를 다하고 제 몫의 명예와 영광을 거머쥐겠다고 단단히 벼르고 있었다.\n\n이 소식의 여파로 나라와 군대를 휩쓴 동요가 어찌나 컸던지 사사로운 일은 거의 안중에도 없었다. 그러니 아마도 조지 오스본은, 막 자기 중대 지휘관으로 임명되어 머지않아 반드시 닥칠 행군 채비에 분주하고 더 높은 진급을 갈망하던 터라, 좀 더 평온한 시절이었다면 관심을 가졌을 다른 일들에는 그다지 마음이 흔들리지 않았던 것이다. 고백건대, 그는 사람 좋은 늙은 세들리 씨의 파국에도 그리 크게 풀이 죽지는 않았다. 그 불운한 신사의 채권자들이 처음 모임을 갖던 날, 그는 자기에게 아주 잘 어울리는 새 군복을 입어 보고 있었다. 아버지는 그에게 그 파산자의 사악하고, 비열하고, 수치스러운 행실을 일러 주었고, 애밀리아에 관해 자기가 했던 말을 상기시키며 두 집안의 인연이 영영 끊겼음을 알렸다. 그리고 그날 저녁 그에게 두둑한 돈을 주어, 그가 그토록 멋지게 차려입은 새 옷과 견장 값을 치르게 했다. 돈이란 이 씀씀이 헤픈 젊은이에게 늘 요긴한 것이었으니, 그는 별말 없이 그것을 받아 챙겼다. 그가 그토록 많고도 많은 행복한 시간을 보냈던 세들리 댁에는 압류 딱지가 붙어 있었다. 그날 밤 집을 나서며(그는 시내에 있을 때면 묵던 올드슬로터스로 향하던 참이었다) 그는 달빛 아래 하얗게 빛나는 그 딱지들을 볼 수 있었다. 그렇다면 그 아늑한 집은 애밀리아와 그 부모를 안에 가둔 채 닫혀 버린 것이었다. 그들은 어디로 피신한 것일까? 그들의 파멸을 생각하니 그도 적잖이 마음이 아렸다. 그날 밤 그는 슬로터스의 커피룸에서 몹시 우울해했고, 거기 있던 전우들이 한마디 했을 만큼 술을 꽤나 마셨다.\n\n이윽고 도빈이 들어와 술을 삼가라고 일렀으나, 오스본은 그저 기분이 지독히 가라앉아서 마셨을 뿐이라고 했다. 하지만 친구가 어설프게 이것저것 캐묻기 시작하며 의미심장한 투로 무슨 소식이 없느냐고 묻자, 오스본은 그와 대화하기를 거절하면서도 자기가 지독히 심란하고 불행하다는 것만은 털어놓았다.\n\n사흘 뒤, 도빈은 병영의 제 방에 있는 오스본을 발견했다. 그는 탁자에 머리를 박은 채였고, 주위에는 종이 몇 장이 흩어져 있었으며, 이 젊은 대위는 누가 봐도 깊은 실의에 빠진 모습이었다. \"그녀가 — 그녀가 내가 줬던 물건들을 돌려보냈어 — 그 빌어먹을 패물들 말이야. 여기 좀 봐!\" 낯익은 필체로 '조지 오스본 대위께'라고 적힌 자그마한 꾸러미가 있었고, 그 주위에 물건들이 흩어져 있었다. 반지 하나, 그가 어릴 적 장에서 그녀에게 사 주었던 은제 칼 한 자루, 금줄 하나, 그리고 머리카락이 들어 있는 로켓 하나였다. \"다 끝났어.\" 그가 메스꺼운 회한의 신음을 토하며 말했다. \"봐, 윌, 원하면 읽어 봐도 돼.\"\n\n그가 가리키는 곳에 몇 줄짜리 짤막한 편지가 있었고, 거기엔 이렇게 적혀 있었다.\n\n저의 아빠가 제게 명하시기를, 더 행복했던 시절에 당신이 제게 주신 이 선물들을 당신께 돌려보내라 하셨습니다. 그래서 마지막으로 당신께 편지를 씁니다. 우리에게 닥친 이 타격을 당신도 저만큼 느끼시리라 저는 생각합니다, 아니 확신합니다. 지금 우리의 이 비참한 처지에서는 도저히 지킬 수 없는 그 약혼에서, 제가 당신을 풀어 드립니다. 당신은 그 일에도, 또 우리의 온갖 슬픔 가운데 가장 견디기 힘든 저 오스본 씨의 매정한 의심에도 아무런 몫이 없었음을 저는 확신합니다. 안녕히 계세요. 안녕히. 이 일과 또 다른 재난들을 견딜 수 있도록 하느님께서 저에게 힘을 주시기를, 그리고 언제까지나 당신을 축복해 주시기를 기도합니다. A.\n\n저는 자주 피아노를 칠 거예요 — 당신의 피아노를요. 그걸 보내 주시다니 정말 당신다웠어요.\n\n도빈은 마음이 몹시 여린 사람이었다. 고통받는 여인들과 아이들의 모습은 늘 그의 마음을 녹이곤 했다. 애밀리아가 가슴이 찢긴 채 외로이 있다는 생각은 그 마음씨 고운 영혼을 고뇌로 후벼 팠다. 그리하여 그는 한바탕 감정을 터뜨렸는데, 누구든 좋을 대로 사내답지 못한 짓이라 여겨도 좋다. 그는 애밀리아가 천사라고 맹세했고, 오스본도 진심으로 거기에 그렇다고 맞장구쳤다. 그 역시 두 사람의 삶의 역사를 되짚어 보던 참이었다. 그래서 어린 시절부터 지금의 나이에 이르기까지, 그토록 사랑스럽고, 그토록 순진하고, 그토록 매혹적으로 꾸밈없으며, 천진하게 다정하고 살가운 그녀를 떠올려 보고 있었던 것이다.\n\n그 모든 것을 잃다니, 또 그것을 가졌으면서도 소중히 여기지 않았다니, 이 얼마나 가슴 아픈 일인가! 천 가지의 소박한 정경과 추억이 그에게 밀려들었고, 그 속에서 그는 언제나 선량하고 아름다운 그녀를 보았다. 그리고 자기 자신으로 말하면, 자신의 이기심과 무관심에 대한 기억이 그 완벽한 순결함과 대비되니 그는 회한과 수치로 얼굴을 붉혔다. 한동안 영광이며 전쟁이며 모든 것이 잊혔고, 두 친구는 오직 그녀에 대해서만 이야기했다.\n\n\"그들은 어디 있지?\" 오랜 이야기 끝에 한참을 침묵하다가 오스본이 물었다. 사실 그녀를 뒤쫓을 아무런 조치도 취하지 않았다는 생각에 적잖이 부끄러워하면서. \"그들이 어디 있느냐고? 이 편지엔 주소가 없잖아.\"\n\n도빈은 알고 있었다. 그는 그저 피아노만 보낸 것이 아니라, 세들리 부인에게 편지를 써서 찾아뵙고 싶다고 청을 넣었던 것이다. 그리고 그는 채텀으로 내려오기 전인 어제 그녀를, 또한 애밀리아까지 만났다. 더욱이 그토록 그들의 마음을 흔들어 놓은 그 작별 편지와 꾸러미를 가져온 것도 그였다.\n\n이 마음씨 고운 친구는 세들리 부인이 자기를 너무도 기꺼이 맞아 줄 뿐 아니라, 피아노가 도착한 일로 무척 들떠 있는 것을 보았다. 그녀의 짐작으로는 그것이 분명 조지에게서 온 것이며, 그의 화해 신호임에 틀림없다는 것이었다. 도빈 대위는 이 선량한 부인의 오해를 바로잡지 않고, 그녀가 늘어놓는 온갖 하소연과 불행의 사연을 깊은 공감으로 다 들어 주었다. 그녀의 손실과 궁핍을 위로하고, 자신의 첫 은인을 향한 오스본 씨의 매정한 처사를 함께 나무랐다. 그녀가 벅차오르던 가슴을 어느 정도 가라앉히고 슬픔을 한껏 쏟아 낸 뒤, 그는 용기를 내어 정말로 애밀리아를 만나게 해 달라고 청했다. 그녀는 여느 때처럼 위층 제 방에 있었고, 어머니가 떨고 있는 그녀를 데리고 아래층으로 내려왔다.\n\n그녀의 행색이 어찌나 처참하고, 그 절망의 빛이 어찌나 애처롭던지, 그것을 본 순간 정직한 윌리엄 도빈은 겁이 났다. 그 창백하게 굳은 얼굴에서 그는 더없이 불길한 예감을 읽었다. 그녀는 그와 한두 마디 함께 앉아 있다가, 꾸러미를 그의 손에 쥐여 주며 말했다. \"이걸 오스본 대위께 전해 주세요, 부디. 그리고 — 그리고 그분이 안녕하시길 바라요 — 그리고 이렇게 찾아와 주시다니 정말 친절하셔요 — 우린 새집이 무척 마음에 들어요. 그리고 저는 — 저는 위층으로 올라가는 게 좋겠어요, 어머니. 제가 그리 튼튼하질 못해서요.\" 이 말과 함께, 무릎을 살짝 굽히는 인사와 미소를 지으며 그 가엾은 아이는 제 갈 길로 갔다. 어머니는 그녀를 데리고 올라가면서 고뇌에 찬 눈길을 도빈 쪽으로 거듭 던졌다. 마음씨 고운 그 친구에게는 그런 호소가 필요치도 않았다. 그는 그러기에는 그녀를 너무도 애틋하게 사랑하고 있었으니까. 형언할 수 없는 슬픔과 연민과 두려움이 그를 따라붙었고, 그는 마치 죄인이라도 된 양 그녀를 보고 나서 그곳을 떠나왔다.\n\n친구가 그녀를 만났다는 말을 듣자, 오스본은 그 가엾은 아이에 관해 다급하고 안타까운 물음을 쏟아 냈다. 그녀는 어떻던가? 그녀의 안색은 어땠나? 그녀가 뭐라고 했나? 그의 전우는 그의 손을 잡고 그의 얼굴을 가만히 들여다보았다.\n\n\"조지, 그녀는 죽어 가고 있어.\" 윌리엄 도빈이 말했다. 그리고 그는 더 말을 잇지 못했다.\n\n세들리 가족이 피신한 그 작은 집의 온갖 살림을 도맡아 하는 통통한 아일랜드 출신 하녀가 하나 있었다. 이 처녀는 앞서 여러 날 동안 애밀리아에게 도움이나 위안을 주어 보려 했으나 헛수고였다. 에미는 너무도 슬픔에 잠긴 나머지 대답조차 하지 못했고, 심지어 그녀가 자기를 위해 애쓰고 있다는 것조차 알아채지 못했다.\n\n도빈과 오스본이 이야기를 나눈 지 네 시간쯤 뒤, 이 하녀가 애밀리아의 방으로 들어왔다. 애밀리아는 여느 때처럼 거기 앉아 자기 편지들을, 그 작은 보물들을 말없이 곱씹고 있었다. 처녀는 미소를 지으며, 짐짓 장난스럽고 행복한 표정으로 가엾은 에미의 주의를 끌어 보려 갖은 애를 썼지만, 에미는 그녀에게 도통 눈길도 주지 않았다.\n\n\"에미 아가씨.\" 처녀가 말했다.\n\n\"가요.\" 에미가 돌아보지도 않고 말했다.\n\n\"전갈이 왔어요.\" 하녀가 말을 이었다. \"뭔가가 — 누군가가 — 이거 봐요, 아가씨한테 새 편지가 왔다니까요 — 그 헌 편지들은 이제 그만 좀 읽으시고요.\" 그러고는 그녀에게 편지 한 통을 건넸고, 에미는 그것을 받아 읽었다.\n\n\"당신을 봐야겠소.\" 편지에는 그렇게 적혀 있었다. \"더없이 사랑하는 에미 — 더없이 사랑하는 이여 — 더없이 사랑하는 나의 아내여, 내게로 와요.\"\n\n조지와 그녀의 어머니가 그녀가 편지를 다 읽기를 기다리며 밖에 서 있었다.\n\n우리는 앞서 보았듯, 시녀인 퍼킨 부인은 크롤리 가문에 중대한 일이 생겼다는 것을 알게 되면 으레 목사관에 있는 뷰트 크롤리 부인에게 그것을 전해야 한다는 의무감을 느꼈다. 그리고 그 마음씨 좋은 부인이 크롤리 양의 심복 하녀에게 얼마나 각별히 친절하고 살뜰했는지도 앞서 언급한 바 있다. 그녀는 말동무인 브리그스 양에게도 자상한 벗이었으며, 베푸는 데는 거의 비용이 들지 않으면서도 받는 이에게는 그토록 값지고 흐뭇한 그 숱한 호의와 약속으로 그녀의 호감을 단단히 사 두었다. 사실 알뜰한 살림꾼이자 능숙한 가정 관리자라면 누구나 알 것이다. 이런 식의 입에 발린 말들이 얼마나 값싸면서도 또 얼마나 사랑스럽게 들리는지를, 그리고 그것이 세상에서 가장 변변찮은 음식에마저 얼마나 맛깔스러운 풍미를 더해 주는지를 말이다. \"고운 말로는 무 한 쪽도 못 버무린다\"고 지껄인 멍청이는 대체 누구였던가? 이 세상에 차려지는 무 요리의 절반은 다름 아닌 그 양념으로만 그럴듯하게 입맛을 돋우는 것이다. 불멸의 알렉시스 소이어가 반 페니어치로 무지한 요리사가 몇 파운드어치의 채소와 고기로 끓여 내는 것보다 더 맛난 수프를 만들어 내듯, 솜씨 좋은 명인은 어설픈 풋내기가 산더미 같은 실질적 은혜의 밑천을 쥐고도 못 해내는 일을, 간소하고 듣기 좋은 몇 마디 말로 훨씬 멀리까지 해낸다. 아니, 우리는 안다. 실질적 은혜란 더러 어떤 위장에는 거북함을 안기지만, 입에 발린 고운 말이라면 대개 얼마든지 소화해 내고, 늘 그 같은 음식을 더 달라며 안달한다는 것을. 뷰트 부인은 브리그스와 퍼킨에게 자신이 그들을 얼마나 깊이 아끼는지, 또 만약 크롤리 양의 재산이 자기 것이 된다면 이토록 훌륭하고 충직한 벗들을 위해 무엇을 해 줄 것인지 어찌나 자주 일러 두었던지, 그 두 숙녀는 그녀에게 더없이 깊은 경의를 품게 되었고, 마치 뷰트 부인이 더없이 값진 은혜를 그득히 베풀기라도 한 양 고마움과 신뢰를 느꼈던 것이다.\n\n반면 로던 크롤리는 이기적이고 둔중한 용기병답게, 고모의 부관들의 환심을 사려는 노력 따위는 손톱만큼도 기울이지 않았다. 그는 그 두 사람에 대한 경멸을 거리낌 없이 고스란히 드러냈다. 한번은 퍼킨에게 자기 장화를 벗기게 했고, 빗속에 치욕스러운 심부름을 보냈으며, 행여 일 기니를 줄 때에도 마치 귀싸대기라도 갈기듯 그녀에게 휙 던져 주었던 것이다. 게다가 고모가 브리그스를 놀림감으로 삼았으니, 대위 역시 그 본을 따라 그녀에게 농지거리를 퍼부었다. 그 농담이란 그의 군마에게 한 방 걷어차이는 것만큼이나 섬세한 것이었다. 그러나 뷰트 부인은 취향이 걸리거나 까다로운 일이 있으면 그녀에게 의견을 구했고, 그녀의 시를 칭송했으며, 수천 가지 친절과 정중한 행동으로 브리그스를 높이 사고 있음을 보여 주었다. 그리고 행여 퍼킨에게 이 펜스 반페니짜리 선물을 줄 때에도 어찌나 많은 찬사를 곁들였던지, 그 이 펜스 반페니는 고마워하는 하녀의 가슴속에서 금덩이로 변모하는 것이었다. 게다가 그 하녀는 뷰트 부인이 재산을 물려받는 그날 자기에게 떨어질 어마어마한 은혜를 자못 흐뭇하게 고대하고 있었던 것이다.\n\n이 두 사람의 상반된 처신은, 막 세상에 발을 들이는 이들의 주의를 끌도록 삼가 권해 두는 바이다. 누구든 칭찬하라, 나는 그런 이들에게 이렇게 말한다. 결코 까다롭게 굴지 말고, 상대의 면전에 대고 곧장 찬사를 늘어놓되, 그가 다시 전해 들을 만한 합당한 기회가 있다 싶으면 등 뒤에서도 그리하라. 다정한 말 한마디 건넬 기회를 결코 놓치지 말라. 콜링우드는 자기 영지에 빈자리가 보이면 으레 주머니에서 도토리 한 알을 꺼내 슬쩍 심었으니, 평생 그대의 찬사도 그렇게 다루라. 도토리 한 알은 거저나 다름없지만, 자라나면 엄청난 재목이 될지도 모르는 것이다.\n\n요컨대 로던 크롤리가 잘나가던 시절에도 사람들은 그저 시무룩하게 마지못해 그를 따랐을 뿐이고, 그가 낯을 못 들게 되자 그를 도와주거나 가엾이 여기는 이는 아무도 없었다. 그에 반해 뷰트 부인이 크롤리 양의 집에서 지휘권을 쥐자, 그곳 수비대는 그런 지휘관 아래에서 일하게 된 것을 더없이 기뻐하며, 그녀의 약속과 너그러움과 다정한 말에서 온갖 영전을 기대했던 것이다.\n\n뷰트 크롤리 부인은 로던이 한 번 패했다고 스스로 졌다 여기며, 잃어버린 자리를 되찾으려는 시도조차 하지 않으리라고는 결코 생각하지 않았다. 그녀는 레베카가 너무도 영리하고 기개 있으며 또 절박한 여자라서, 한바탕 싸워 보지도 않고 순순히 굴복할 리 없다는 것을 알았다. 그리하여 그 싸움에 대비해야 하고, 기습이든 매복이든 불시의 공격이든 끊임없이 경계해야 한다고 느꼈다.\n\n우선, 비록 그녀가 이 도성을 장악했다 한들 그곳의 주된 주민까지 확실히 제 편으로 두었다고 할 수 있겠는가? 크롤리 양 본인이 과연 버텨 줄 것인가? 그리고 쫓겨난 적을 다시 맞아들이고 싶은 은밀한 바람을 품고 있지는 않을까? 그 노부인은 자신을 즐겁게 해 주던 로던과 레베카를 마음에 들어 했다. 뷰트 부인은 자기 편의 그 누구도 이 도회풍 숙녀를 그만큼 즐겁게 해 줄 수 없다는 사실을 스스로 감출 수 없었다. \"우리 애들 노래는, 그 가증스러운 가정교사 계집애 노래를 들은 다음이니, 영 못 들어 줄 지경이라는 걸 나도 안다니까.\" 솔직한 목사 부인은 속으로 인정했다. \"마사랑 루이자가 이중주를 켤 때면 형님은 늘 잠이 들곤 했지. 짐의 뻣뻣한 대학물 든 태도며, 가엾은 뷰트가 개니 말이니 늘어놓는 이야기에 형님은 늘 진저리를 냈고. 내가 형님을 목사관으로 모셔 간다면 우리 모두에게 화를 내고 달아나 버릴 테지, 나는 알아. 그러고는 다시 그 끔찍한 로던의 손아귀에 떨어져 그 살무사 같은 샤프 계집애의 먹잇감이 되고 말 거야. 어쨌든 형님이 몹시 편찮으시고, 적어도 몇 주는 꼼짝 못 하시리라는 건 분명해. 그동안 우리는 그 파렴치한 자들의 농간에서 형님을 지킬 무슨 방도를 궁리해 두어야 해.\"\n\n크롤리 양은 더없이 기분 좋은 순간에도, 누군가 그녀더러 편찮으시다거나 안색이 안 좋아 보인다고 말하기만 하면, 벌벌 떨며 의사를 부르러 사람을 보내는 노부인이었다. 그러니 단언컨대 그 같은 갑작스러운 집안 사건 뒤에 그녀는 몹시 편찮았을 것이다. 하물며 그녀보다 더 단단한 신경의 소유자라도 흔들어 놓았을 만한 일이었으니 말이다. 적어도 뷰트 부인은 크롤리 양이 더없이 위중한 상태이며, 그러니 그에 맞추어 처신하라고 의사며 약제사며 말동무며 하인들에게 알리는 것이 자기 의무라 여겼다. 그녀는 거리에 짚을 무릎 높이까지 깔게 했고, 문에 달린 노커는 보울스 씨의 식기와 함께 치워 두게 했다. 의사가 하루 두 번씩 들르도록 못을 박았고, 두 시간마다 환자에게 물약을 들이부었다. 누군가 방에 들어오기라도 하면 그녀는 \"쉬이이\" 하고 어찌나 새된 소리로, 또 어찌나 불길하게 입을 막던지, 침대에 누운 가엾은 노부인을 겁먹게 했다. 노부인은 누운 채로 눈만 뜨면, 침대 곁 안락의자에 꼼짝 않고 앉은 뷰트 부인의 구슬 같은 눈이 자신에게 간절히 박혀 있는 것을 보지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀가 고양이처럼 벨벳 같은 발로 방 안을 돌아다닐 때면, 그 눈은 어둠 속에서 번득이는 듯했다(그녀가 커튼을 닫아 두었기 때문이다). 크롤리 양은 그렇게 며칠을, 그것도 한없이 여러 날을, 뷰트 씨가 신앙 서적을 읽어 주는 가운데 누워 있었다. 밤이면, 길고 긴 밤이면, 그녀는 야경꾼이 외치는 소리와 등잔 심지가 타닥거리는 소리를 들어야 했고, 한밤중 마지막 순서로 약제사가 발소리를 죽이고 다녀가면, 그저 뷰트 부인의 반짝이는 눈이나, 등심초 촛불이 음산하게 어두워진 천장에 던지는 노란 불빛의 가물거림을 바라보도록 남겨졌다. 건강의 여신 히게이아 본인이라도 이런 양생법 아래에서라면 병이 났을 터인데, 하물며 이 가엾고 늙고 신경질적인 희생자야 오죽했겠는가? 일찍이 이런 말이 있었다. 건강하고 기분이 좋을 때면 이 유서 깊은 허영의 시장 주민은 볼테르 씨조차 흡족해할 만큼 종교와 도덕에 대해 자유분방한 생각을 품었으나, 일단 병이 덮치면 죽음에 대한 더없이 끔찍한 공포가 그것을 한층 부채질했고, 드러누운 늙은 죄인은 완전히 겁쟁이가 되어 버렸다는 것이다.\n\n병상에서의 설교와 경건한 성찰이란 한낱 이야기책에는 어울리지 않는 법이고, 우리는(요즘 어떤 소설가들이 하는 식으로) 독자가 그저 희극 한 편을 구경하려고 돈을 낸 마당에 설교를 들이밀어 대중을 어르려는 것이 아니다. 그러나 설교를 늘어놓지 않더라도, 이 진실만큼은 분명 마음에 새겨 둘 만하다. 허영의 시장이 사람들 앞에 펼쳐 보이는 그 부산함과 승리와 웃음과 흥겨움이, 언제나 그 연기자를 사사로운 삶에까지 따라가 주는 것은 아니며, 때로는 더없이 음울한 의기소침과 참담한 회한이 그를 덮치기도 한다는 것이다. 더없이 잘 차려졌던 연회를 회상한들 병든 미식가의 기운을 북돋우기는 어려울 것이다. 더없이 잘 어울리던 드레스며 무도회에서 거둔 눈부신 승리를 떠올린들 시든 미인을 위로하는 데는 거의 아무 소용이 없을 것이다. 어쩌면 정치가들도 인생의 어느 시기에 이르면, 가장 통쾌했던 표결을 곱씹어 본들 그다지 흐뭇하지 않을 것이다. 그리고 어떤 (비록 불확실하긴 해도) 확실한 내일이 눈앞에 보일 때면 어제의 성공이나 쾌락은 지극히 하찮은 것이 되고 마는 법이니, 그 내일에 대해서는 우리 모두가 언젠가는 헤아려 보아야 하는 것이다. 오, 어릿광대 옷을 함께 걸친 형제들이여! 히죽거리고 재주넘는 일에, 또 광대 모자에 달린 방울 소리에 진력이 나는 순간이 어찌 없겠는가? 사랑하는 벗이자 길동무들이여, 이것이 바로 나의 정겨운 목적이라네. 그대들과 함께 이 시장을 거닐며 그곳의 가게와 구경거리를 들여다보고, 그러다 그 번쩍임과 소란과 흥겨움이 다 지나고 나면 다 함께 집으로 돌아와, 저마다 홀로 더없이 비참해지자는 것 말일세.\n\n\"내 남편이라는 그 가엾은 사람이 어깨 위에 머리라는 걸 제대로 얹고만 있었어도,\" 뷰트 크롤리 부인은 혼자 생각했다. \"지금 같은 상황에서 이 가엾은 노부인께 얼마나 쓸모가 있었을까! 그이라면 형님이 그 충격적인 자유사상의 버릇을 뉘우치게 만들 수도 있었을 테고, 의무를 다하시라고, 그래서 제 몸과 제 집안을 망신시킨 그 가증스러운 망나니를 내치시라고 다그칠 수도 있었을 거야. 그리고 우리 사랑스러운 딸들과 두 아들에게 마땅히 베푸셔야 할 정당한 몫을 베풀게 할 수도 있었을 테지. 그 애들이야말로 친척이 베풀 수 있는 온갖 도움을 필요로 하고, 또 분명 받을 자격이 있는 애들이니까.\"\n\n그리고 악덕을 미워하는 것은 언제나 미덕으로 나아가는 한 걸음이므로, 뷰트 크롤리 부인은 시누이에게 로던 크롤리의 그 갖가지 죄악에 대한 마땅한 혐오를 불어넣으려 애썼다. 그 죄목으로 말하면 작은어머니 되는 이가 어찌나 줄줄이 늘어놓던지, 실로 젊은 장교 한 연대를 통째로 단죄하고도 남을 정도였다. 사람이 살면서 잘못을 저질렀을 때, 그 잘못을 세상에 일러바치는 데 자기 친척만큼 안달하는 도덕가를 나는 달리 알지 못한다. 그리하여 뷰트 부인은 로던의 내력에 대해 완벽한 집안 식구다운 관심과 해박함을 드러내 보였다. 그녀는 마커 대위와 벌인 그 추악한 다툼의 전말을 죄다 꿰고 있었다. 처음부터 잘못은 로던에게 있었으나 끝내 그가 대위를 쏘아 버리고 만 사건 말이다. 또한 그녀는 가엾은 도브데일 경이 어떻게 망가졌는지도 알고 있었다. 그의 어머니가 아들을 거기서 교육시키려고 옥스퍼드에 집을 한 채 얻었고, 그래서 런던에 오기 전까지는 평생 카드 한 장 만져 본 적 없던 그가, 코코아트리 클럽에서 로던에게 타락하여, 이 가증스러운 유혹자이자 청년을 망치는 자의 손에 어쩔 도리 없이 곤드레만드레 취해서는 사천 파운드를 뜯기고 만 것이다. 그녀는 그가 파멸시킨 시골 양가들의 고통을 더없이 생생하고 세세하게 그려 보였다. 그가 불명예와 가난 속으로 처박은 아들들, 그가 꾀어 파멸로 몰아넣은 딸들을. 그녀는 그의 낭비로 파산한 가엾은 상인들도, 그가 그 낭비를 메우려 부린 치사한 술수와 사기 행각도, 더없이 너그러운 고모를 속여 넘긴 어처구니없는 거짓말도, 또 고모의 희생을 배은망덕과 조롱으로 갚은 일도 죄다 알고 있었다. 그녀는 이런 이야기들을 조금씩 조금씩 크롤리 양에게 풀어놓았다. 그것을 빠짐없이 들려주었고, 그리스도교도이자 한 집안의 어머니로서 그리하는 것이 자신의 마땅한 의무라 여겼다. 자기 혀가 제물로 바치고 있는 그 희생자에 대해서는 손톱만큼의 가책이나 거리낌도 없었다. 아니, 십중팔구는 자기 행위가 자못 가상한 일이라 여기며, 그것을 단호하게 수행하는 자신의 처신에 우쭐했던 것이다. 그렇다, 누군가의 인격을 헐뜯으려 들 때면, 뭐라 해도 그 일에는 친척만 한 적임자가 없다. 그리고 이 가엾은 한심한 자 로던 크롤리에 관해서는 한 가지 인정하지 않을 수 없다. 곧 한낱 진실만으로도 그를 단죄하기에 충분했고, 온갖 추문을 꾸며내는 것은 그 친지들로서는 영 부질없는 수고였다는 점이다.\n\n이제 친척이 된 레베카 역시 뷰트 부인의 자상한 탐문의 몫을 가장 톡톡히 차지했다. 이 지칠 줄 모르는 진실 추적자는(로던이 보내는 모든 밀사나 편지에 대해 문을 걸어 잠그라고 엄명을 내려 둔 뒤) 크롤리 양의 마차를 타고, 치즈윅 거리 미네르바 하우스에 있는 오랜 벗 핑커튼 양에게로 달려갔다. 그녀에게 로던 대위가 샤프 양에게 꾀임을 당했다는 끔찍한 소식을 알리는 동시에, 그 전직 가정교사의 출생과 어린 시절 내력에 관한 갖가지 기이한 사연을 캐냈다. 그 사전 편찬자의 벗에게는 들려줄 정보가 차고 넘쳤다. 제미마 양은 그림 선생의 영수증과 편지를 가져오라는 분부를 받았다. 그중 하나는 채무자 임시 구류소에서 온 것이었고, 다른 하나는 돈을 좀 미리 달라고 사정하는 것이었으며, 또 하나는 레베카가 치즈윅 숙녀들에게 받아들여진 데 대한 감사로 가득했다. 그리고 그 불운한 화가의 펜에서 나온 마지막 서한은, 임종의 자리에서 자기 고아 딸을 핑커튼 양의 보호에 부탁한다는 것이었다. 그 묶음 속에는 레베카가 어린 시절에 쓴 편지와 청원서도 있었는데, 아버지를 도와 달라 애원하거나 제 고마움을 토로하는 것들이었다. 어쩌면 허영의 시장에서 편지만큼 훌륭한 풍자도 없을 것이다. 십 년 전 그대의 소중한 벗이 보낸 편지 한 묶음을 꺼내 보라. 지금은 그대가 미워하는 그 소중한 벗 말이다. 그대 누이의 편지철을 들여다보라. 그대들은 이십 파운드짜리 유산을 두고 다투기 전까지 어찌나 서로 매달려 살았던가! 그 뒤로 이기적인 불효로 그대 가슴을 반쯤 찢어 놓은 아들이 또박또박 갈겨쓴 글씨를 꺼내 보라. 아니면 끝없는 열정과 영원한 사랑을 토로하는, 그대 자신이 보냈던 편지 꾸러미를 보라. 그것은 그대의 연인이 그 인도 갑부와 결혼하면서 그대에게 되돌려 보낸 것이다. 이제 와서는 엘리자베스 여왕만큼이나 아무런 마음도 가지 않는 그 연인 말이다. 맹세며 사랑이며 약속이며 속내며 감사며, 한참 지나고 나서 다시 읽으면 그 모든 것이 어찌나 기묘하게 읽히는가! 허영의 시장에는, 적당히 짧고 합당한 기간이 지나면 (영수 처리된 상인의 청구서를 빼고) 모든 문서를 파기하라고 명하는 법이 하나쯤 있어야 마땅하다. 지워지지 않는 일본제 먹물을 광고하는 그 돌팔이며 인간 혐오자들은 그 사악한 발명품과 함께 진작에 사라져야 할 것이다. 허영의 시장에서 쓰기에 가장 좋은 먹물은, 이틀쯤 지나면 흔적도 없이 바래어 종이를 깨끗하고 텅 빈 백지로 되돌려 줌으로써, 그 위에 다른 누군가에게 또 글을 쓸 수 있게 해 주는 그런 먹물일 것이다.\n\n핑커튼 양에게서 나온 지칠 줄 모르는 뷰트 부인은 샤프와 그 딸의 자취를 좇아, 그 작고한 화가가 살던 그리스 거리의 셋집까지 거슬러 올라갔다. 그곳 응접실 벽에는 샤프가 한 분기치 집세 대신 그려 준, 흰 새틴 차림의 안주인과 놋쇠 단추를 단 그 남편의 초상화가 여태 걸려 있었다. 스토크스 부인은 말이 많은 사람이라, 샤프 씨에 관해 자기가 아는 것을 죄다 술술 털어놓았다. 그가 얼마나 방탕하고 가난했는지, 또 얼마나 사람 좋고 익살스러웠는지, 늘 집달리며 빚쟁이들에게 쫓겨 다녔는지, 또 안주인의 경악스럽게도 — 비록 그 여자라면 도무지 좋게 볼 수가 없었지만 — 죽기 얼마 전에야 비로소 제 아내와 정식으로 혼인을 했는지, 그리고 그 딸이 또 얼마나 기묘하고 자그마한 들고양이 같은 말괄량이였는지를 말이다. 그 애가 어찌나 재담과 흉내로 다들 웃겨 댔는지, 어떻게 술집에서 진을 받아 오곤 했으며, 동네 화실마다 모르는 이가 없었는지 — 요컨대 뷰트 부인은 새로 생긴 조카며느리의 가문과 교육과 행실에 관해 더없이 소상한 이야기를 얻어 냈으니, 만약 레베카가 자기에 관해 이런 탐문이 벌어지고 있음을 알았더라면 결코 달가워하지 않았을 그런 이야기였다.\n\n이 모든 부지런한 조사의 혜택은 고스란히 크롤리 양에게 돌아갔다. 로던 크롤리 부인은 오페라 무용수의 딸이었다. 그녀 자신도 춤을 추었다. 화가들의 모델 노릇도 했다. 그녀는 그 어미의 딸답게 자라났다. 아비와 함께 진을 마셨다, 운운. 그것은 타락한 남자와 결혼한 타락한 여자였다. 그리고 뷰트 부인의 이야기에서 끌어낼 교훈인즉, 이 한 쌍의 악행은 도저히 구제할 길이 없으며, 점잖게 처신하는 사람이라면 결코 다시는 그들을 아는 체해서는 안 된다는 것이었다.\n\n이것들이 바로 신중한 뷰트 부인이 파크 레인에서 그러모은 재료, 말하자면 로던 부부가 크롤리 양에게 가하리라 짐작되는 공성전에 대비해 집을 요새화하는 데 쓴 군량이자 탄약이었다.\n\n그러나 그녀의 작전에 흠을 하나 잡을 수 있다면 바로 이것이다. 곧 그녀가 지나치게 서둘렀다는 점, 일을 좀 지나치게 잘 꾸려 나갔다는 점이다. 그녀는 의심할 여지 없이 크롤리 양을 필요 이상으로 더 앓게 만들었다. 그리고 그 늙은 환자가 그녀의 권위에 굴복하긴 했으나, 그 권위가 어찌나 괴롭히고 가혹했던지, 희생자는 처음 걸려드는 기회만 생기면 당장 달아나고 싶어졌던 것이다. 살림을 휘어잡는 여인들, 곧 제 성(性)의 자랑거리들 — 만사를 만인을 위해 척척 지시하고, 이웃에게 무엇이 좋은지를 정작 당사자보다도 훨씬 잘 안다는 그 여인들은, 더러 집안의 반란이 일어날 가능성이나, 도를 넘은 권위에서 비롯되는 그 밖의 극단적 결과에 대해서는 미처 헤아리지 못하는 법이다.\n\n가령 뷰트 부인은, 두말할 것 없이 세상에서 가장 갸륵한 뜻을 품고서, 또 병든 시누이를 위해 잠도 끼니도 바깥바람도 마다하며 제 몸이 망가지도록 애쓰면서도, 노부인이 병중이라는 자신의 확신을 어찌나 멀리까지 밀어붙였던지, 하마터면 그녀를 관 속으로 떠밀어 넣을 뻔했다. 어느 날 그녀는 한결같이 드나드는 약제사 클럼프 씨에게 자신의 희생과 그 성과를 일러 보였다.\n\n\"분명 말씀드리는데요, 클럼프 선생,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"조카의 배은망덕 탓에 병석에 누우신 우리 가엾은 환자분을 일으켜 세우려고, 저는 한순간도 정성을 게을리하지 않았답니다. 저는 제 몸 불편한 것 따위 결코 마다하지 않아요. 결코 제 한 몸 바치기를 거절하지 않지요.\"\n\n\"부인의 헌신은, 두말할 것 없이 감탄할 만합니다.\" 클럼프 씨가 깊이 허리를 숙이며 말했다. \"그러나—\"\n\n\"이곳에 온 뒤로 저는 거의 눈도 붙이지 못했어요. 의무감을 위해 잠도, 건강도, 온갖 안락도 다 내려놓았답니다. 우리 가엾은 제임스가 천연두를 앓을 때, 제가 삯꾼 따위에게 그 애 간병을 맡기던가요? 천만에요.\"\n\n\"훌륭한 어머니라면, 그것도 더없이 훌륭한 어머니라면 마땅히 하실 일을 하신 거지요, 부인. 그러나—\"\n\n\"한 집안의 어미이자 영국 성직자의 아내로서, 제 신조만큼은 옳다고 감히 믿습니다.\" 뷰트 부인이 확신에 찬 흐뭇하고 엄숙한 어조로 말했다. \"그러니 클럼프 선생, 하늘이 저를 지탱해 주는 한, 저는 결코, 결코 이 의무의 자리를 저버리지 않을 거예요. 다른 이들이야 저 잿빛 머리를 슬픔과 함께 병상으로 내몰지도 모르지만(여기서 뷰트 부인은 손을 내저으며, 화장실 받침대 위에 얹혀 있던 늙은 크롤리 양의 커피색 가발 한 점을 가리켰다), 저는 결코 이 자리를 뜨지 않겠어요. 아아, 클럼프 선생! 저 병상에는 의술의 위로뿐 아니라 영혼의 위로도 필요하다는 것을, 저는 두렵지만 잘 알고 있답니다.\"\n\n\"제가 말씀드리려던 것은요, 부인,\" 단호한 클럼프가 다시금 부드러운 태도로 끼어들었다. \"그토록 부인의 명예를 드높이는 심정을 토로하셨을 때 제가 말씀드리려던 것은, 부인께서 우리 자상한 벗에 대해 공연히 스스로를 놀라게 하시고, 또 그분을 위해 부인 자신의 건강을 너무 아낌없이 희생하고 계신다는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"저는 제 의무를 위해서라면, 또 제 남편 집안의 어느 분을 위해서라면 목숨이라도 내놓겠어요.\" 뷰트 부인이 가로막으며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇지요, 부인, 정 그래야 한다면요. 하지만 우리는 뷰트 크롤리 부인께서 순교자가 되시기를 바라지 않습니다.\" 클럼프가 정중하게 말했다. \"스퀼스 박사와 저는, 짐작하시다시피 온갖 걱정과 정성을 기울여 크롤리 양의 병세를 살펴보았습니다. 보아하니 그분은 의기소침하고 신경이 예민해져 있어요. 집안일로 마음이 흔들리신 거지요.\"\n\n\"그 조카 녀석은 끝내 파멸하고 말 거예요.\" 크롤리 부인이 외쳤다.\n\n\"마음이 흔들리신 겁니다. 그리고 부인께서는 마치 수호천사처럼, 정말이지 더없는 수호천사처럼 오셔서, 재난의 압박 속에 계신 그분을 다독여 드린 거예요. 그러나 스퀼스 박사와 저는, 우리 사랑스러운 벗께서 침상에만 누워 계셔야 할 만한 상태는 아니라고 생각하고 있습니다. 그분은 가라앉아 계시지만, 어쩌면 이렇게 갇혀 지내시는 것이 그 침울함을 더하는지도 모릅니다. 변화가, 바깥바람이, 즐거움이 필요해요. 약전(藥典)을 통틀어 가장 흐뭇한 처방이지요.\" 클럼프 씨가 히죽 웃으며 그 잘생긴 이를 드러냈다. \"일어나시라고 권해 보세요, 부인. 침상과 그 침울함에서 그분을 끌어내세요. 잠깐씩이라도 마차를 타고 나가시라고 단단히 일러 두세요. 그러면 부인의 두 뺨에도 다시 장밋빛이 돌아올 겁니다. 뷰트 크롤리 부인께 이런 말씀을 드려도 된다면 말이지요.\"\n\n\"공원에서 우연히 그 끔찍한 조카 녀석과 마주치기라도 하면요. 듣자 하니 그 망나니가 제 죄악의 뻔뻔스러운 동반자를 태우고 거길 다닌다던데.\" 뷰트 부인이 말했다(비밀의 자루 속에서 이기심이라는 고양이를 슬쩍 내보이며). \"그 충격에 그분은 다시 자리보전을 하셔야 할 거예요. 그분은 바깥출입을 하시면 안 돼요, 클럼프 선생. 제가 곁에서 지켜드리는 한, 절대 못 나가십니다. 그리고 제 건강이야 무슨 대수겠어요? 저는 기꺼이 그것을 바치겠어요, 선생. 의무의 제단에 그것을 제물로 올리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"솔직히 말씀드리면, 부인,\" 이제 클럼프 씨가 단도직입으로 말했다. \"그분이 저 어두운 방 안에 갇혀 계신다면, 저는 그분의 목숨을 장담하지 못합니다. 그분은 신경이 어찌나 예민한지, 언제 어느 날 우리가 그분을 잃을지 모릅니다. 그리고 만약 부인께서 크롤리 대위가 상속자가 되기를 바라신다면, 솔직히 경고드리는데, 부인께서는 그 대위를 위해 지금 최선을 다하고 계신 셈입니다.\"\n\n\"맙소사! 그분 목숨이 위태롭다는 건가요?\" 뷰트 부인이 외쳤다. \"어쩌자고, 어쩌자고 클럼프 선생, 더 일찍 제게 알려 주지 않으셨어요?\"\n\n그 전날 밤, 클럼프 씨와 스퀼스 박사는 (래핀 워런 경의 집에서 와인 한 병을 기울이며 — 그 댁 부인이 마침 그에게 열세 번째 경사를 안겨 드리려던 참이었다) 크롤리 양과 그 병세에 관해 의논을 나눈 터였다.\n\n\"늙은 틸리 크롤리를 단단히 거머쥔 그 햄프셔 여자 말이야, 클럼프, 어찌나 자그마한 독수리 같은지.\" 스퀼스가 한마디 했다. \"이 마데이라 와인 한번 끝내주는군.\"\n\n\"로던 크롤리도 참 멍청하지.\" 클럼프가 받았다. \"가정교사 따위와 결혼을 하다니! 하기야 그 계집애한테도 뭔가가 있긴 했지.\"\n\n\"초록빛 눈에, 흰 살결에, 어여쁜 몸매에, 게다가 그 유명한 앞가슴의 발육이라니.\" 스퀼스가 한마디 했다. \"확실히 그 계집애한텐 뭔가가 있어. 그리고 크롤리는 멍청이였지, 스퀼스.\"\n\n\"빌어먹을 멍청이지, 늘 그랬고말고.\" 약제사가 받았다.\n\n\"당연히 그 노친네는 그자를 내칠 거야.\" 의사가 말했고, 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 덧붙였다. \"그 양반, 죽고 나면 제법 알차게 나눠질 텐데.\"\n\n\"나눠지긴.\" 클럼프가 히죽 웃으며 말했다. \"나라면 연 이백 파운드를 준대도 그 양반이 나눠지는 꼴은 보고 싶지 않은데.\"\n\n\"그 햄프셔 여자가 곁에 붙어 있다간 두 달 안에 그 양반을 죽이고 말 걸세, 클럼프 군.\" 스퀼스 박사가 말했다. \"늙은 데다, 잘 먹고, 신경은 예민하고, 심장은 두근거리고, 뇌에는 압박이 가고, 그러다 졸중이 오면, 그걸로 끝장이지. 어서 일으켜 세우게, 클럼프. 바깥으로 내보내게. 안 그러면 자네 그 연 이백 파운드, 앞으로 몇 주치 값어치도 못 쳐줄 테니.\" 그리고 바로 이 귀띔에 따라, 이 갸륵한 약제사는 뷰트 크롤리 부인에게 그토록 솔직하게 말을 건넸던 것이다.\n\n침대에 누워 곁에 아무도 없는 노부인을 제 손아귀에 쥔 채로, 뷰트 부인은 그녀에게 유언장을 고치도록 부추기려고 한두 차례가 아니게 공세를 펼쳤다. 그러나 그 음울한 제안이 나올 때마다 죽음에 대한 크롤리 양의 평소 공포는 한층 더 커지기만 했다. 그래서 뷰트 부인은 자신이 마음에 품은 그 경건한 목적을 이루려면, 먼저 환자를 명랑한 기분과 건강한 상태로 만들어 놓아야겠다는 것을 깨달았다. 다음 골칫거리는 그녀를 어디로 데려가느냐 하는 것이었다. 그 가증스러운 로던 부부와 마주칠 염려가 없는 곳이라고는 교회뿐인데, 거기서는 흥이 날 리 없다고 뷰트 부인은 옳게 판단했다. \"런던의 그 아름다운 교외로 나가 봐야겠어.\" 그녀는 그렇게 생각했다. \"거기가 세상에서 가장 그림 같은 곳이라던데.\" 그리하여 그녀는 별안간 햄스테드와 혼지에 흥미를 갖게 되었고, 덜위치가 자기에게 큰 매력으로 다가오는 것을 발견했다. 그러고는 희생자를 마차에 태워 그 한적한 곳들로 몰고 다녔는데, 그 짧은 여정마다 로던과 그 아내에 관한 이야기로 시간을 때웠고, 이 한 쌍의 망나니에 대한 노부인의 분노를 한층 돋울 만한 이야기란 이야기는 죄다 들려주었다.\n\n어쩌면 뷰트 부인은 고삐를 공연히 너무 바짝 당겼는지도 모른다. 그녀가 크롤리 양을 부추겨 그 말 안 듣는 조카를 마땅히 싫어하게 만들긴 했으나, 정작 그 환자는 자신을 괴롭히는 자에게 깊은 증오와 은밀한 공포를 품은 채 그녀에게서 벗어나기를 갈망하고 있었던 것이다. 얼마 지나지 않아 노부인은 하이게이트며 혼지에 완전히 반기를 들었다. 그녀는 공원으로 가겠다고 했다. 뷰트 부인은 거기서 그 가증스러운 로던과 마주치게 되리라는 것을 알았는데, 과연 그녀의 짐작이 맞았다. 어느 날 마차길에서 로던의 이륜마차가 시야에 들어왔다. 레베카가 그 곁에 앉아 있었다. 적의 마차에는 크롤리 양이 여느 때의 자리를 차지하고 있었고, 왼편에는 뷰트 부인이, 뒷좌석에는 푸들과 브리그스 양이 있었다. 마음 졸이는 순간이었다. 레베카는 그 마차를 알아보자 가슴이 빠르게 뛰었다. 그리고 두 마차가 한 줄로 엇갈리는 순간, 그녀는 두 손을 마주 잡고서, 애절한 애정과 헌신이 어린 얼굴로 그 노처녀 쪽을 바라보았다. 로던 본인도 부르르 떨었고, 물들인 콧수염 위로 얼굴이 검붉게 달아올랐다. 저쪽 마차에서 마음이 움직인 사람이라고는 늙은 브리그스뿐이어서, 그녀는 그 큰 눈을 불안하게 옛 벗들 쪽으로 던졌다. 크롤리 양의 보닛은 단호하게 서펜타인 호수 쪽으로 돌려져 있었다. 뷰트 부인은 마침 푸들에게 푹 빠져, 그놈을 작은 귀염둥이라느니, 어여쁜 멍멍이라느니, 예쁜 강아지라느니 불러 대고 있었다. 마차는 저마다 제 줄을 따라 계속 나아갔다.\n\n\"틀렸어, 젠장.\" 로던이 아내에게 말했다.\n\n\"한 번만 더 해 봐요, 로던.\" 레베카가 대답했다. \"여보, 우리 마차 바퀴를 저쪽 바퀴에 걸어 버릴 순 없어요?\"\n\n로던은 그런 술책을 부릴 만한 배짱이 없었다. 두 마차가 다시 마주쳤을 때, 그는 이륜마차 안에서 벌떡 일어섰다. 모자를 벗어 들 채비로 손을 들어 올렸다. 두 눈을 부릅뜨고 바라보았다. 그러나 이번에는 크롤리 양의 얼굴이 돌려져 있지 않았다. 그녀와 뷰트 부인은 그의 얼굴을 정면으로 빤히 쳐다보면서, 인정사정없이 조카를 모른 체했다. 그는 욕설을 내뱉으며 자리에 털썩 주저앉았고, 마차길에서 벗어나더니 절망에 차 집을 향해 내달렸다.\n\n그것은 뷰트 부인으로서는 늠름하고도 결정적인 승리였다. 그러나 그녀는 크롤리 양의 역력한 불안을 보며 그런 마주침이 거듭되는 데 따르는 위험을 느꼈다. 그리하여 사랑하는 벗의 건강을 위해서는 한동안 도성을 떠나는 것이 무엇보다 긴요하다고 마음먹고는, 브라이턴을 아주 강력히 권했다.\n\n윌리엄 도빈 대위는 자기도 어찌 된 영문인지 모르는 사이에, 조지 오스본과 애밀리아의 혼인을 추진하고 주선하고 관리하는 막중한 역할을 떠맡고 있는 자신을 발견했다. 그가 아니었다면 이 일은 결코 성사되지 않았을 것이다. 그 점만큼은 스스로도 인정하지 않을 수 없었고, 세상 모든 남자들 가운데 하필 자기에게 이 혼사의 책임이 떨어졌다는 생각에 그는 다소 씁쓸하게 웃었다. 그러나 이 협상을 끌고 나가는 일이 그에게 주어질 수 있는 임무 중 가장 고통스러운 것이긴 했어도, 도빈 대위는 일단 수행해야 할 의무가 있으면 말을 많이 하거나 망설이는 일 없이 끝까지 밀고 나가는 사람이었다. 그리고 만약 세들리 양이 남편을 빼앗긴다면 그 실망감에 죽고 말리라고 완전히 마음을 굳힌 그는, 그녀를 살려 두기 위해 자신이 가진 온 힘을 다하기로 작정했던 것이다.\n\n정직한 윌리엄, 그 벗의 중재로 조지가 다시 어린 연인의 발치(아니, 차라리 품이라 해야 할까?)로 돌아왔을 때, 조지와 애밀리아 사이에 오간 면담의 시시콜콜한 내막까지는 적지 않으련다. 조지보다 훨씬 모진 마음이라도, 슬픔과 절망으로 처연하게 망가진 그 고운 얼굴을 보고, 또 가슴이 미어지는 자신의 작은 이야기를 들려주는 그 소박하고 애틋한 음성을 들었다면 녹아내리고 말았을 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 어머니가 떨면서 오스본을 데려왔을 때 기절하지 않았고, 그저 연인의 어깨에 머리를 기댄 채 한동안 더없이 애틋하고, 흠뻑 쏟아지고, 마음을 씻어 주는 눈물을 흘리며 북받친 슬픔을 달랬을 뿐이었다. 그래서 늙은 세들리 부인은 너무도 안도한 나머지 젊은 두 사람만 남겨 두는 것이 상책이라 여겼다. 에미가 조지의 손에 엎드려 울며, 마치 그가 자기 지존의 주인이기라도 한 듯, 또 자기는 그에게서 온갖 은혜와 자비를 구걸해야 할 죄 많고 보잘것없는 사람이기라도 한 듯 겸손하게 그 손에 입 맞추는 모습을 뒤로하고 부인은 자리를 떴다.\n\n이 엎드린 자세, 그리고 불평 한마디 없는 이 달콤한 순종은 조지 오스본의 마음을 더없이 절묘하게 건드리고 흐뭇하게 했다. 그는 저 소박하고 고분고분하고 충직한 존재 앞에서 한 노예를 보았고, 자신의 권능을 깨닫는 순간 그의 영혼은 왠지 은밀하게 짜릿한 전율에 휩싸였다. 그는 비록 군주이긴 하나 너그러운 마음을 베풀어, 무릎 꿇은 이 에스더를 일으켜 세워 그녀를 왕비로 만들어 주리라. 게다가 그녀의 슬픔과 아름다움도 그 순종 못지않게 그의 마음을 움직였으니, 그리하여 그는 그녀를 다독이고, 일으켜 세우고, 말하자면 용서해 주었던 것이다. 자신의 태양이 사라진 탓에 시들어 죽어 가던 그녀의 온갖 희망과 감정은, 그 빛이 되돌아오자 일시에 다시 활짝 피어났다. 그날 밤 애밀리아의 베개에 누인, 환하게 빛나는 그 작은 얼굴을, 그토록 핼쑥하고, 그토록 생기 없고, 그토록 만사에 무심한 채 전날 밤 그 자리에 뉘었던 얼굴과 같은 것이라고는 차마 알아보기 어려웠을 것이다. 그 변화를 보고 기뻐한 정직한 아일랜드인 하녀는 갑자기 그토록 발그레해진 그 얼굴에 입 맞추게 해 달라고 청했다. 애밀리아는 두 팔로 그 처녀의 목을 감싸 안고 마치 어린아이처럼 온 마음을 다해 입 맞추었다. 사실 그녀는 어린아이나 다름없었다. 그날 밤 그녀는 달게 잠들어 한껏 원기를 되찾았고, 아침 햇살 속에서 깨어났을 때 그 가슴에 차오른 형언할 수 없는 행복의 샘물이란!\n\n\"오늘 또 오실 거야.\" 애밀리아는 생각했다. \"그분은 세상에서 가장 위대하고 가장 훌륭한 분이야.\" 그리고 사실인즉, 조지는 자기야말로 살아 있는 가장 너그러운 사람 가운데 하나이며, 이 어린 처녀와 결혼함으로써 어마어마한 희생을 치르고 있는 셈이라고 생각하는 것이었다.\n\n그녀와 오스본이 위층에서 즐거운 단둘만의 시간을 보내는 동안, 늙은 세들리 부인과 도빈 대위는 아래층에서 일의 형편이며, 젊은 두 사람의 앞날의 가능성이며 향후 준비에 관해 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 두 연인을 한자리에 데려다 놓고는 힘껏 서로 부둥켜안도록 내버려 두고 나온 세들리 부인은, 영락없는 여인답게, 세상 그 어떤 힘으로도 세들리 씨를 설득해 딸과, 자신을 그토록 수치스럽고, 사악하고, 흉악하게 대한 사내의 아들 사이의 혼인에 동의하게 만들 수는 없으리라는 의견이었다. 그러면서 부인은 더 행복했던 시절과 지난날의 영화에 대해 길게 이야기를 늘어놓았는데, 그 시절 오스본은 뉴로드에서 아주 초라하게 살았고, 그의 아내는 세들리 부인이 오스본네 아이 하나가 태어났을 때 마련해 준 조스의 자그마한 배냇저고리 따위를 기꺼이 받아 들고 좋아했다는 것이었다. 그 사내의 악마 같은 배은망덕이 세들리 씨의 가슴을 찢어 놓은 게 분명하다고 부인은 확신했다. 그리고 혼인으로 말할 것 같으면, 남편은 결단코, 결단코, 결단코, 결단코 동의하지 않으리라는 것이었다.\n\n\"두 사람이 같이 도망쳐야겠군요, 부인.\" 도빈이 웃으며 말했다. \"로던 크롤리 대위하고, 에미 양의 벗인 그 자그마한 가정교사 본을 따라서 말이지요.\" 그게 정말일까? 세상에, 어쩜! 세들리 부인은 이 소식에 잔뜩 흥분했다. 부인은 블렌킨숍이 여기 있어 이 이야기를 들었으면 했다. 블렌킨숍은 늘 그 샤프 양을 미심쩍게 여겼던 것이다. 조스가 얼마나 다행스럽게 위기를 모면했는지! 그러면서 부인은 이미 다 알려진 레베카와 보글리 월라 징세관 사이의 연애담을 늘어놓았다.\n\n그러나 도빈이 두려워한 것은 세들리 씨의 노여움이라기보다, 이 일에 얽힌 다른 한쪽 아버지의 노여움이었으니, 그는 러셀 광장에 사는, 눈썹 검은 늙은 폭군 같은 러시아 무역상이 어떻게 나올지에 대해 적잖은 의심과 불안을 품고 있음을 스스로 인정했다. 그는 이 혼사를 단호히 금지했어, 도빈은 생각했다. 그는 오스본이 얼마나 야만스럽고 고집스러운 사람인지, 또 한번 내뱉은 말을 얼마나 끝까지 고수하는지 잘 알고 있었다. \"조지가 화해할 수 있는 유일한 길은,\" 그의 벗은 따져 보았다, \"이번 출정에서 두각을 나타내는 거야. 만일 그가 죽으면 둘은 함께 가는 셈이고. 만일 두각을 나타내는 데 실패하면, 그땐 어찌 될까? 그 어머니에게서 받은 돈이 좀 있으니, 듣자 하니 소령직을 사기엔 충분하겠지. 아니면 군적을 팔고 캐나다로 가서 땅을 일구거나, 시골 오두막에서 고생스럽게 살아야겠지.\" 그런 반려와 함께라면 시베리아라도 마다하지 않으리라고 도빈은 생각했다. 그리고 이상한 말이지만, 이 터무니없고 전혀 분별없는 젊은 친구는, 번듯한 마차와 말을 건사할 재력이 없다는 것, 또 그 소유주가 벗들을 품위 있게 대접할 만한 수입이 없다는 것이 조지와 세들리 양의 결합을 가로막는 장애가 되어야 마땅하다는 생각은 단 한순간도 하지 않았던 것이다.\n\n혼인이 되도록 빨리 이루어져야 한다고 그가 생각하게 된 것도 바로 이런 무게 있는 고려들 때문이었다. 그 자신도 이 일을 어서 끝내 버리고 싶어 안달했던 것일까, 나는 궁금하다. 사람들이 누가 죽으면 장례를 어서 치르려 하고, 이별이 정해지면 그것을 서두르듯이 말이다. 어쨌든 도빈 씨가 일단 이 일을 손에 쥐고 나서는 그 처리에 유난히도 열을 올렸음은 분명하다. 그는 조지에게 즉각 행동에 나설 필요가 있음을 역설했다. 관보에 그의 이름이 좋게 거론되기만 하면 아버지와 화해할 가능성이 생긴다는 점을 일깨워 주었다. 필요하다면 자기가 직접 가서 이 일로 양쪽 아버지 모두에게 맞서겠노라 했다. 어쨌든 그는, 누구나 예상하고 있던, 연대가 영국을 떠나 해외 근무에 나서라는 명령이 떨어지기 전에 이 일을 매듭짓기를 조지에게 간곡히 청했다.\n\n이 혼인 계획에 골몰한 채, 그리고 남편에게 직접 이 일을 꺼낼 엄두가 나지 않았던 세들리 부인의 찬동과 동의를 얻은 도빈 씨는, 시내에 있는 존 세들리의 단골 출입처인 타피오카 커피하우스로 그를 찾아 나섰다. 자기 사무소가 문을 닫고 운명의 손길이 그를 덮친 이래로, 그 가엾고 영락한 노신사는 매일 그곳으로 향하곤 했으니, 편지를 쓰고 또 받아서는 그것을 수상쩍은 꾸러미로 묶어 두었고, 그 가운데 여러 묶음을 외투 자락 안에 넣고 다녔다. 나는 파산한 사람의 그 분주함이며 부산함이며 비밀스러움보다 더 음울한 것을 알지 못한다. 그가 보여 주는 부유한 이들의 편지들, 후원을 약속하고 위로를 건네는 그 닳고 기름때 묻은 서류들을, 그는 애처롭게 당신 앞에 펼쳐 놓고는 그 위에 재기와 미래의 부에 대한 희망을 쌓아 올리는 것이다. 나의 사랑하는 독자께서도 살아오면서 분명 이런 운수 사나운 동무에게 숱하게 붙들려 보았을 것이다. 그는 당신을 구석으로 끌고 가서는, 헤벌어진 외투 주머니에서 서류 뭉치를 꺼내고, 묶은 끈을 풀어 입에 문 채, 아끼는 편지들을 골라 당신 앞에 펼쳐 놓는다. 그 절망에 찬 눈으로 당신을 응시하는 그 서글프고 간절하고 반쯤 미친 듯한 표정을, 모르는 이가 어디 있겠는가?\n\n도빈이 마주한 이는, 한때 혈색 좋고, 쾌활하고, 번창했던 존 세들리가 바로 이런 부류의 사내로 변해 버린 모습이었다. 그토록 윤기 흐르고 단정하던 외투는 솔기마다 허옇게 닳았고, 단추는 구릿빛 바탕이 드러나 있었다. 얼굴은 홀쭉하게 꺼졌고 수염도 깎지 않은 채였으며, 가슴 주름 장식과 목도리는 헐렁해진 조끼 아래로 축 늘어져 있었다. 지난날 커피하우스에서 청년들에게 한턱낼 때면 그는 거기 있는 누구보다 크게 외치고 크게 웃었으며, 종업원들이 그의 주위를 종종걸음으로 분주히 돌게 만들었다. 그런 그가 타피오카의 존에게, 침침한 눈에 칙칙한 양말과 갈라진 단화를 신은 그 늙은 종업원에게 그토록 비굴하고 굽실거리는 모습을 보는 것은 참으로 가슴 아픈 일이었다. 그 종업원의 일이라곤 이 음산한 유흥소를 드나드는 손님들에게 웨이퍼 과자가 담긴 잔이며, 백랍 잔에 가득 담긴 잉크며, 종이 몇 장을 내다 주는 것뿐이었는데, 이곳에서는 그 밖에 달리 소비되는 것이라곤 없어 보였다. 윌리엄 도빈으로 말하자면, 노신사가 그의 젊은 시절에 거듭 용돈을 쥐여 주었고 또 천 번도 넘게 놀림감으로 삼았던 사람이건만, 이제 늙은 세들리는 몹시 머뭇거리며 비굴한 태도로 그에게 손을 내밀고는 그를 \"선생\"이라 불렀다. 그 영락한 노인이 자기를 그렇게 맞이하고 그렇게 부르는 것을 보자, 윌리엄 도빈은 마치 세들리를 이토록 낮은 처지로 떨어뜨린 그 불운에 자신이 어떤 식으로든 책임이라도 있는 것처럼, 수치심과 회한에 사로잡혔다.\n\n\"이렇게 만나니 정말 반갑소이다, 도빈 대위 선생.\" 그는 방문객을 한두 번 슬그머니 곁눈질하고 나서 말했다(도빈의 호리호리한 체구와 군인다운 풍모는, 갈라진 무도화를 신은 그 종업원의 침침한 눈에도 얼마간 흥미의 빛을 반짝이게 했고, 곰팡내 나는 낡은 커피 잔들 틈에서 졸고 있던 검은 옷의 노부인까지 깨워 놓았다). \"그 훌륭하신 시참사회 의원께서는 안녕하시고, 마님이신 자네의 그 인자하신 모친께서도 평안하신가, 선생?\" 그는 \"마님\"이라고 말하면서 종업원을 둘러보았는데, 그 모양새가 마치 \"이보게, 존, 내게도 아직 벗들이 있다네, 그것도 지체 높고 명망 있는 분들이 말이야\" 하고 말하는 듯했다. \"내 쪽 일로 무슨 볼일이라도 보러 온 겐가, 선생? 새 사무소가 준비될 때까지는 내 젊은 친구들인 데일과 스피곳이 내 모든 업무를 대신 봐주고 있다네. 알다시피 나는 여기 잠시 머물 뿐이라서 말일세, 대위. 무엇을 도와드리면 되겠나, 선생? 뭐라도 좀 드시겠는가?\"\n\n도빈은 잔뜩 머뭇거리며 더듬더듬, 자기는 조금도 시장하거나 목마르지 않으며, 처리할 볼일이 있는 것도 아니고, 그저 세들리 씨가 평안하신지 여쭙고 옛 친구와 악수나 나누려고 들렀을 뿐이라고 둘러댔다. 그러고는 진실을 절박하게 비틀어 가며 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"제 어머니께서는 아주 평안하십니다. 그러니까, 한동안 몹시 편찮으셨는데, 이제 날이 처음 좋아지는 대로 나가셔서 세들리 부인을 방문하시려고 기다리고 계십니다. 부인께서는 어떠십니까, 선생? 부디 아주 평안하시기를 바랍니다.\" 그러고는 그는 말을 멈추고 자신의 완벽한 위선을 곱씹었다. 그날은 타피오카 커피하우스가 자리한 코핀 코트에서도 더없이 화창했고 햇살도 더없이 밝게 비치고 있었던 것이다. 게다가 도빈 씨는 바로 한 시간 전에 자기 이륜마차로 오스본을 풀럼까지 데려다주고 그를 애밀리아 양과 단둘이 남겨 두고 오면서 세들리 부인을 직접 보았던 터였다.\n\n\"제 아내가 마님을 뵈면 무척 기뻐할 겁니다.\" 세들리는 서류를 꺼내며 대답했다. \"여기 자네 부친께서 보내신 아주 친절한 편지가 있는데, 부디 그분께 내 정중한 인사를 전해 주게. 디 마님께서는 우리가 옛날 벗들을 맞이하던 데보다 다소 작은 집에 사는 우리를 보시게 될 걸세. 하지만 아늑하다네. 게다가 공기가 바뀌니 내 딸한테도 좋아. 그 애가 시내에서는 좀 시달렸거든. 어린 에미를 기억하지, 선생? 그래, 꽤나 많이 시달렸다네.\" 노신사는 이렇게 말하는 동안 눈길이 이리저리 방황했고, 서류를 손가락으로 두드리며 닳은 붉은 끈을 만지작거리는 그의 머릿속은 딴생각에 빠져 있었다.\n\n\"자네는 군인이지.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"내 묻겠네, 빌 도빈, 그 코르시카 출신 악당이 엘바에서 돌아오리라고 누가 짐작이나 할 수 있었겠나? 작년에 연합국 군주들이 여기 왔을 때, 우리가 시내에서 그분들께 그 만찬을 베풀고, 화합의 신전이며, 불꽃놀이며, 세인트제임스 공원의 중국식 다리를 구경했을 때, 우리가 실제로 그 평화를 위해 테 데움까지 불렀는데, 분별 있는 사람이라면 그 누가 평화가 정말로 체결된 게 아니라고 생각이나 할 수 있었겠나, 선생? 내 묻겠네, 윌리엄, 내가 어찌 오스트리아 황제가 빌어먹을 배신자라고, 배신자에 지나지 않는다고 짐작이나 할 수 있었겠나? 나는 말을 에두르지 않네. 처음부터 제 사위를 되돌려 놓으려 작정한, 두 얼굴의 흉악한 배신자요 음모꾼이었던 거야. 그리고 단언하건대, 보니가 엘바에서 탈출한 건 빌어먹을 사기요 음모였네, 선생. 공채 시세를 떨어뜨려 이 나라를 결딴내려고 유럽 열강의 절반이 가담한 음모였단 말이야. 그래서 내가 이 꼴이 된 걸세, 윌리엄. 그래서 내 이름이 관보에 오른 거야. 어째서겠나, 선생? 내가 러시아 황제와 섭정공을 믿었기 때문이지. 자, 보게. 내 서류를 보란 말이야. 3월 1일에 공채가 어땠는지 보게. 내가 거래처를 위해 사들였을 때 프랑스 오 푼리 공채가 얼마였는지. 그리고 지금은 얼마인지. 결탁이 있었던 거야, 선생. 그렇지 않고서야 그 악당이 결코 탈출할 수 없었지. 그를 놓아준 영국 측 위원은 대체 어디 있었나? 그자는 총살당해 마땅하네, 선생. 군법회의에 회부해 총살시켜야 마땅하다고, 맹세코.\"\n\n\"저희가 보니를 몰아내러 갈 겁니다, 선생.\" 도빈은 노인의 분노에 다소 놀라 말했다. 노인의 이마에 핏줄이 부풀어 오르기 시작했고, 그는 꽉 쥔 주먹으로 서류를 두드리며 앉아 있었다. \"저희가 그를 몰아내러 갈 겁니다, 선생. 공작께서는 이미 벨기에에 가 계시고, 저희도 진군 명령이 날마다 떨어지기를 기다리고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그놈에게 자비를 베풀지 말게. 그 악당의 목을 베어 오게, 선생. 그 비겁자를 쏘아 죽이게, 선생.\" 세들리가 고함쳤다. \"나도 입대했을 걸세, 정말이지. 하지만 나는 영락한 늙은이라네. 그 빌어먹을 악당에게 결딴난 신세야. 그리고 이 나라의 사기꾼 도둑놈 한 무리에게 결딴이 났지. 내가 키워 준 놈들인데, 선생, 이제 와선 제 마차를 굴리며 떵떵거리고들 있다네.\" 그가 목멘 소리로 덧붙였다.\n\n도빈은 한때 인자했던 이 옛 친구가, 불운으로 거의 실성하다시피 하여 노망 든 노여움에 길길이 날뛰는 모습을 보고 적잖이 마음이 흔들렸다. 돈과 좋은 평판을 으뜸가는 선으로 여기는 그대여, 이 영락한 신사를 가엾이 여기라. 그리고 허영의 시장에서야 분명 그것들이 으뜸가는 선이렷다.\n\n\"그래.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"품어 따뜻이 데워 주면 나중에 도리어 무는 독사들이 있지. 말 등에 태워 주면 제일 먼저 그 위로 자네를 짓밟고 지나가는 거지들이 있단 말이야. 내가 누구 얘기를 하는지 알 걸세, 윌리엄 도빈, 이 사람아. 한 푼 없을 적부터 내가 알고 지낸, 러셀 광장의 그 돈 자랑이나 일삼는 악당 얘기야. 내가 도와주기 전 그 거지 신세로 다시 돌아가는 꼴을 보게 해 달라고 기도하고 또 바라는 그 작자 말일세.\"\n\n\"제 친구 조지에게서 그 일에 관해 좀 들었습니다, 선생.\" 도빈은 본론으로 들어가고 싶어 안달하며 말했다. \"선생과 그의 부친 사이의 다툼이 그를 무척 괴롭게 하고 있습니다, 선생. 실은, 제가 그에게서 전할 말을 가지고 왔습니다.\"\n\n\"오, 그게 자네 심부름이로군, 그렇지?\" 노인이 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다. \"뭐라고! 어쩌면 그가 내게 위로의 말이라도 전하던가, 응? 참으로 친절하기도 하지, 그 뻣뻣하고 잘난 척하는 멋쟁이 녀석이, 그 맵시 부리는 거드름과 웨스트엔드식 허세를 부려 가면서 말이야. 그래, 아직도 내 집 주위를 기웃거리고 있나? 내 아들놈이 사내다운 용기가 있었다면 그를 쏘아 버렸을 텐데. 그놈은 제 애비 못지않은 악당이야. 내 집에서 그놈 이름이 입에 오르내리는 건 용납 못 하네. 그놈을 내 집에 들인 그날을 저주하네. 내 딸이 그놈과 결혼하는 꼴을 보느니 차라리 그 애가 내 발치에 죽어 있는 꼴을 보겠어.\"\n\n\"그 부친의 모진 처사가 조지의 잘못은 아닙니다, 선생. 따님이 그를 사랑하게 된 것도 그 못지않게 선생께서 하신 일입니다. 선생이 대체 누구시기에, 젊은 두 사람의 애정을 가지고 놀며 멋대로 그들의 가슴을 찢어 놓으시는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"이 혼사를 깨는 게 그놈 애비가 아니라는 걸 명심하게.\" 늙은 세들리가 소리쳤다. \"그걸 금하는 건 바로 날세. 그 집안과 우리 집안은 영영 갈라섰어. 내가 영락하긴 했네만, 그 정도까지 떨어지진 않았네. 아니고말고. 그러니 자네는 그 집안 전부에게 그렇게 전하게. 아들이고, 애비고, 누이들이고, 죄다 말일세.\"\n\n\"제 생각에는, 선생, 선생께 그 두 사람을 갈라놓을 권한도 권리도 없습니다.\" 도빈이 낮은 목소리로 대답했다. \"그리고 선생께서 따님께 동의해 주지 않으신다면, 그 동의 없이라도 결혼하는 것이 따님의 도리가 될 겁니다. 선생이 고집을 부리신다고 해서 따님이 죽거나 비참하게 살아야 할 이유는 없습니다. 제가 보기에 따님은 런던의 모든 교회에서 결혼 예고가 낭독된 것이나 다름없을 만큼 이미 결혼한 셈입니다. 게다가 오스본이 선생께 퍼붓는 비난에 대해, 비난이 있다면 말이지요, 그 아들이 선생의 집안에 들어와 따님과 혼인하기를 청한다는 것보다 더 나은 대답이 또 어디 있겠습니까?\"\n\n이 점을 들이대자 늙은 세들리에게 만족 비슷한 기색이 언뜻 떠오르는 듯했다. 그러나 그는 자기 동의를 얻어서는 애밀리아와 조지의 혼인이 결코 이루어질 수 없다고 여전히 고집했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 동의 없이 해치우는 수밖에요.\" 도빈이 빙긋 웃으며 말하고는, 전날 세들리 부인에게 들려주었던 대로 레베카가 크롤리 대위와 사랑의 도피를 한 이야기를 세들리 씨에게 들려주었다. 그것은 분명 노신사를 즐겁게 했다. \"자네들은 정말 무서운 작자들이야, 자네들 대위라는 족속들은.\" 그가 서류를 묶으며 말했다. 그 얼굴에는 미소 비슷한 것이 어렸는데, 마침 들어서던 침침한 눈의 종업원은 세들리가 이 음울한 커피하우스를 드나든 이래 그 얼굴에서 그런 표정을 한 번도 본 적이 없었던지라 깜짝 놀랐다.\n\n자기 원수 오스본에게 그런 한 방을 먹인다는 생각이 어쩌면 노신사의 마음을 달래 주었는지도 모른다. 그리고 이내 그들의 담화가 끝나자, 그와 도빈은 꽤 좋은 친구로 헤어졌다.\n\n\"우리 누이들 말로는 그 여자가 비둘기 알만 한 다이아몬드를 가지고 있다더군.\" 조지가 웃으며 말했다. \"그게 그 살빛을 얼마나 돋보이게 하겠어! 그 보석들을 목에 걸면 그야말로 휘황찬란하겠지. 그 새까만 머리는 샘보 머리만큼이나 곱슬곱슬하고. 장담컨대 궁정에 들 땐 코걸이를 했을 거야. 머리 꼭대기에 깃털 장식까지 꽂으면 영락없는 미개의 미녀로 보이겠지.\"\n\n조지는 애밀리아와 이야기를 나누며, 얼마 전 그의 부친과 누이들이 새로 알게 된, 러셀 광장 집안의 지극한 존경을 한 몸에 받는 한 젊은 숙녀의 외모를 놀려 대고 있었다. 소문에 따르면 그녀는 서인도제도에 농장을 몇 개나 가지고 있는지 모르고, 공채에도 막대한 돈을 넣어 두었으며, 동인도회사 주주 명부에 그 이름 옆에 별이 세 개나 붙어 있다고 했다. 그녀는 서리에 대저택이 있고, 포틀랜드 플레이스에도 집이 한 채 있었다. 이 부유한 서인도제도 상속녀의 이름은 모닝 포스트지에 찬사와 함께 거론된 바 있었다. 해기스턴 대령의 미망인이자 그녀의 친척인 해기스턴 부인이 그녀의 보호자 노릇을 하며 살림을 맡고 있었다. 그녀는 학업을 마친 학교에서 갓 나온 참이었는데, 조지와 그의 누이들은 데번셔 플레이스의 늙은 헐커네 집 야회에서 그녀와 마주쳤고(헐커, 불록 상회는 오랫동안 그녀 집안의 서인도제도 거래처였다), 자매는 그녀에게 더없이 다정하게 접근했으며 그 상속녀도 이를 무척 흔쾌히 받아들였다. 저런 처지의 고아가, 저만한 돈을 가지고, 어쩜 저리도 흥미로울까! 오스본 자매는 그렇게들 말했다. 그들은 헐커네 무도회에서 돌아와 말동무인 워트 양에게 자기들의 새 친구 이야기로 입에 침이 마를 줄 몰랐다. 계속 만나기로 약속을 잡았고, 바로 그 이튿날 마차를 몰아 그녀를 만나러 갔다. 빙키 경의 친척으로 늘 그분 이야기를 입에 달고 다니는 해기스턴 대령의 미망인 해기스턴 부인은, 이 순진하고 세상 물정 모르는 아가씨들에게는 다소 거만하고 자기 지체 높은 친척들 이야기를 너무 좋아하는 사람으로 비쳤다. 그러나 로다는 그들이 바랄 수 있는 모든 것을 갖추고 있었으니, 더없이 솔직하고, 더없이 다정하고, 더없이 상냥한 사람이었다. 세련미가 조금 부족하긴 해도 그토록 마음씨가 곱지 않은가. 아가씨들은 당장에 서로 이름을 부르는 사이가 되었다.\n\n\"그 여자가 궁정에 들려고 차려입은 모습을 봤어야 하는 건데, 에미.\" 오스본이 웃으며 외쳤다. \"해기스턴의 친척인 빙키 마님의 안내로 정식으로 입궐하기 전에, 우리 누이들한테 그 옷을 자랑하러 왔더라고. 그 해기스턴이라는 여자는 안 닿는 데가 없는 친척이야. 그 여자 다이아몬드는 우리가 갔던 날 밤 복스홀처럼 번쩍번쩍하더군. (복스홀 기억해, 에미? 그리고 조스가 자기 더없이 사랑스러운 딩들딩들 자기야 어쩌고 노래 부르던 것도?) 다이아몬드에 마호가니 빛깔이라니, 이봐! 얼마나 유리한 대조인지 생각해 봐. 게다가 머리에 꽂은 하얀 깃털이라니, 아니 양털 같은 머리에 말이야. 귀걸이는 샹들리에만 했어. 거기다 불을 켜도 됐을 정도지, 정말이지. 그리고 노란 새틴 자락을 혜성 꼬리처럼 질질 끌고 다니더라고.\"\n\n\"그 여자 몇 살인데?\" 에미가 물었다. 조지는 재회한 그날 아침 이 검은 피부의 모범적 인물에 관해 그녀에게 쉴 새 없이 떠들어 대고 있었으니, 세상 그 누구도 흉내 낼 수 없을 만큼 쉴 새 없이 떠들어 댔다.\n\n\"글쎄, 그 흑인 공주님은 이제 막 학교를 나왔다곤 해도 스물둘이나 셋은 됐을 거야. 그리고 그 여자 글씨를 봐야 하는 건데! 보통은 해기스턴 대령 부인이 편지를 대신 써 주는데, 한번은 친밀감이 솟구쳤는지 우리 누이들한테 직접 펜을 들었더라고. 새틴(satin)을 새팅(satting)이라고 쓰고, 세인트제임스(Saint James's)를 세인트잼스(Saint Jams)라고 썼지 뭐야.\"\n\n\"어머, 그럼 분명 응접실 기숙생인 스워츠 양이겠네.\" 에미가 말했다. 애밀리아가 핑커튼 양의 학원을 떠날 때 발작이라도 일으킬 듯 격하게 슬퍼하던, 그 마음씨 고운 젊은 혼혈 아가씨가 떠올랐던 것이다.\n\n\"바로 그 이름이야.\" 조지가 말했다. \"그 여자 아버지는 독일계 유대인이었어. 노예주였다더군. 어떤 식으로든 식인종 섬과 연이 닿아 있었나 봐. 작년에 죽었고, 핑커튼 양이 그 여자 교육을 마무리해 줬지. 피아노로 두 곡 칠 줄 알고, 노래 세 곡 알고, 해기스턴 부인이 곁에서 철자를 일러 주면 글도 쓸 줄 알아. 그리고 우리 제인이랑 마리아는 벌써 그 여자를 친자매처럼 사랑하게 됐고.\"\n\n\"누이들이 나도 사랑해 줬으면 좋았을 텐데.\" 에미가 애틋하게 말했다. \"나한텐 늘 무척 쌀쌀맞았잖아.\"\n\n\"이봐 자기, 자기가 이십만 파운드를 가지고 있었으면 누이들도 자길 사랑했을 거야.\" 조지가 대답했다. \"그렇게들 자라 왔으니까. 우리 사회는 현찰이 곧 전부인 사회야. 은행가들이며 시내 거물들 틈에서 사는데, 그까짓 것들, 누구든 자네한테 말을 걸 때면 주머니 속 금화를 짤랑거리고 있다고. 저 멍청이 프레드 불록은 마리아랑 결혼할 거고, 동인도회사 중역인 골드모어가 있고, 수지 장사를 하는 디플리도 있지. 우리 장사 말이야.\" 조지가 불편한 웃음을 지으며 얼굴을 붉히고 말했다. \"돈만 긁어모으는 그 천박한 족속들을 전부 저주하라지! 난 그자들의 거하고 묵직한 만찬 자리에선 졸음이 쏟아진다고. 우리 아버지의 그 거창하고 멍청한 파티 자리에선 부끄럽기만 하고. 난 신사들하고, 세상 물정에 밝고 멋을 아는 사람들하고 어울려 사는 데 익숙해, 에미. 거북 요리나 처먹는 장사꾼 무리하고가 아니라. 사랑스러운 자기, 우리 패거리 중에 숙녀처럼 보이고, 숙녀처럼 생각하고, 숙녀처럼 말하는 사람은 자기뿐이야. 그것도 자기가 천사라서, 어쩔 수 없이 그러는 거지. 따지지 마. 자기야말로 유일한 숙녀라니까. 유럽 최고의 사교계에서 살아온 크롤리 양이 그걸 알아보지 않았겠어? 그리고 근위 기병대의 크롤리로 말하면, 이런, 멋진 친구야. 자기가 고른 여자랑 결혼했다는 점에서 난 그 친구가 마음에 들어.\"\n\n애밀리아도 그 점에서 크롤리 씨에게 무척 감탄했다. 레베카가 그와 함께 행복하기를 빌었고, (웃으며) 조스도 위로를 받기를 바랐다. 그렇게 두 사람은 아주 옛날처럼 도란도란 수다를 떨었다. 애밀리아의 자신감이 완벽하게 되돌아온 것이다. 비록 그녀는 스워츠 양을 두고 어여쁜 질투를 잔뜩 부리며, 위선자답게도, 조지가 그 상속녀와 그 돈과 세인트키츠의 영지에 혹해 자기를 잊어버릴까 봐 끔찍이도 무섭다고 엄살을 떨긴 했지만 말이다. 그러나 사실인즉 그녀는 너무도 행복해서 그 어떤 두려움이나 의심이나 불안도 품을 수가 없었다. 다시 조지가 곁에 있으니, 그 어떤 상속녀도 미녀도, 아니 그 어떤 종류의 위험도 두렵지 않았던 것이다.\n\n오후에 도빈 대위가 이 사람들에게로 돌아왔을 때, 그는 그들을 향한 깊은 연민을 품고 돌아온 것이었는데, 애밀리아가 어찌나 다시 젊어졌는지를 보니 절로 마음이 흐뭇해졌다. 그녀가 웃고, 재잘대고, 피아노 앞에서 정겨운 옛 노래들을 부르는 모습이라니. 그 노래는 바깥에서 세들리 씨가 시내에서 돌아왔음을 알리는 종소리가 울려서야 비로소 중단되었고, 그 소리에 조지는 물러가라는 신호를 받았다.\n\n세들리 양은 처음에 알아보는 미소를 한 번 지어 보인 것을 빼면, 그 미소조차 위선이었으니 그가 온 것을 그녀는 도리어 성가시게 여겼던 것이다, 그가 머무는 내내 도빈에게 단 한 번도 눈길을 주지 않았다. 그러나 그는 만족했다. 그녀가 행복한 모습을 보았으니 그것으로 족했고, 그녀를 그렇게 만든 수단이 되었다는 데 감사할 따름이었다.\n\n미스 스워츠가 지닌 것과 같은 자질을 갖춘 젊은 숙녀라면 그 누구라도 사랑받을 수 있는 법이다. 그리하여 늙은 오스본 씨의 영혼에는 야심의 원대한 꿈이 자리 잡았으니, 그 꿈을 실현해 줄 사람이 바로 그녀였던 것이다. 그는 더없는 열정과 우애로 두 딸이 이 젊은 상속녀에게 품은 살가운 애정을 부추겼고, 자기 딸들의 사랑이 이토록 합당한 곳에 쏠리는 것을 보는 일이 아버지로서 더없이 진실한 기쁨이라고 떠벌렸다.\n\n\"우리 누추한 러셀 광장 저택에서는 말입니다, 아가씨.\" 그는 로다 양에게 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"웨스트엔드에서 익숙하시던 그런 화려함이나 신분은 찾아보실 수 없을 겁니다. 제 딸들은 수수하고 사심 없는 아이들이지요. 하지만 그 마음만은 제자리에 똑바로 박혀 있어서, 아가씨께 애정을 품게 되었으니, 그건 그 애들에게 명예로운 일입니다. 명예로운 일이고말고요. 저는 수수하고 단순하고 보잘것없는 영국 상인입니다. 정직한 상인이지요. 작고하신 아가씨의 선친과 거래하던 제 존경하는 벗 헐커와 불록이 이를 보증해 줄 겁니다. 우리는 화목하고, 단순하고, 행복하고, 또 감히 말씀드리건대 존경받는 가족임을 알게 되실 겁니다. 소박한 밥상에, 소박한 사람들이지만, 따뜻한 환대만큼은 보장합니다, 사랑하는 로다 양. 로다라고 부르게 해 주십시오. 제 마음이 아가씨께 따뜻하게 끌리는 것이, 정말 그렇답니다. 저는 솔직한 사람이고, 아가씨가 마음에 듭니다. 샴페인 한 잔! 힉스, 미스 스워츠께 샴페인을 올려라.\"\n\n늙은 오스본이 자기가 한 말을 모두 진심으로 믿었으리라는 것, 그리고 딸들이 미스 스워츠에 대한 애정의 맹세를 자못 진지하게 했으리라는 것은 의심할 여지가 거의 없다. 허영의 시장 사람들은 부자에게 들러붙는 일을 지극히 자연스러운 것으로 여긴다. 가장 순박한 사람들조차 위대한 번영을 적잖이 흐뭇한 눈길로 바라보기 마련이거늘(부의 관념에 무언가 경외롭고도 흐뭇한 구석이 없다고 단언할 수 있는 영국 국민이 한 사람이라도 있다면 어디 나와 보라. 또한 만찬 자리에서 옆자리 사람이 오십만 파운드를 가졌다는 말을 듣고서도 어떤 흥미도 없이 그를 바라볼 수 있다면 어디 나와 보라), 순박한 이들조차 돈을 그토록 자애로운 눈길로 바라본다면, 하물며 산전수전 다 겪은 늙은 속물들이야 돈을 어찌 여기겠는가! 그들의 애정은 앞다투어 달려 나가 돈을 맞이하고 환영한다. 그 흥미로운 돈의 임자를 향해 그들의 따뜻한 정감이 절로 깨어나는 것이다. 나는 일정한 재산이나 사회적 지위가 없는 사람과는 친분을 나눌 자유가 자기에게 없다고 여기는 점잖은 사람들 몇몇을 알고 있다. 그들은 적절한 경우가 오면 그 감정에 고삐를 풀어 준다. 그 증거로, 십오 년 동안 애밀리아 세들리에게 진심 어린 호의 한 자락 품지 못했던 오스본 가문 사람들 대다수가, 단 하룻저녁 만에 미스 스워츠에게 흠뻑 빠져들어, 첫눈에 반하는 우정을 가장 낭만적으로 옹호하는 자라도 흡족해할 정도가 된 것이다.\n\n그녀야말로 조지에게 얼마나 어울리는 짝인가(자매와 워트 양은 의견을 같이했다). 게다가 저 보잘것없고 하찮은 애밀리아보다 얼마나 더 나은가! 저토록 늠름한 청년이, 그 잘난 용모와 신분과 교양을 두루 갖춘 그가 그녀에게 더없이 알맞은 남편이 될 게 아닌가. 포틀랜드 광장에서 열릴 무도회며, 궁정 알현이며, 귀족 절반과의 소개 자리가 어른어른 떠올라 젊은 숙녀들의 마음을 채웠고, 그들은 사랑하는 새 친구에게 조지와 그의 으리으리한 인맥 이야기밖에는 다른 말을 하지 않았다.\n\n늙은 오스본 역시 그녀가 아들에게 더없이 좋은 짝이 되리라고 생각했다. 아들은 군대를 떠나 의회에 들어가고, 사교계와 정계에서 두각을 나타내야 마땅했다. 오스본이라는 이름이 아들을 통해 작위를 얻는 광경을 떠올리며, 자신이 영광스러운 준남작 가문의 시조가 될지도 모른다고 생각하니, 그의 피는 정직한 영국식 환희로 끓어올랐다. 그는 상속녀의 재산에 관한 모든 것, 즉 그 돈이 어디에 투자되어 있고 영지가 어디에 자리 잡고 있는지까지 죄다 알아낼 때까지 시내며 증권거래소에서 부지런히 발품을 팔았다. 그의 주요 정보통 가운데 하나인 젊은 프레드 불록은 자기가 직접 그녀에게 값을 매겨 보고 싶기도 했지만(젊은 은행가는 그렇게 표현했다), 이미 마리아 오스본에게 예약된 몸이었다. 그러나 그녀를 아내로 차지할 수 없게 된 사심 없는 프레드는, 그녀를 동서로 맞이하는 데는 전적으로 찬성이었다. \"조지더러 당장 끼어들어서 차지하라고 하세요.\" 이것이 그의 충고였다. \"쇠는 달궈졌을 때 두드려야지요. 그녀가 아직 사교계에 갓 나온 풋풋한 동안에 말입니다. 몇 주만 지나면 웨스트엔드에서 작위에 거덜 난 영지 따위나 가진 어떤 빌어먹을 작자가 끼어들어서 우리 시내 사람들을 죄다 밀어낼 겁니다. 작년에 피츠루퍼스 경이 그로그램 양을 가로챈 것처럼 말이지요. 그 아가씨는 사실 포더 앤드 브라운 상회의 포더와 약혼한 사이였는데도요. 빠르면 빠를수록 좋습니다, 오스본 씨. 그게 제 생각입니다.\" 이 익살꾼은 그렇게 말했다. 다만 오스본이 은행 응접실을 떠나고 나자, 불록 씨는 애밀리아를 떠올리고는, 그 아이가 얼마나 예쁜 처녀였으며 조지 오스본에게 얼마나 깊이 정을 주었던가를 생각했다. 그리하여 그는 자기 귀한 시간 가운데 적어도 십 초가량을, 그 불운한 젊은 여인에게 닥친 불행을 안타까워하는 데 바쳤던 것이다.\n\n이렇듯 조지 오스본의 선량한 감정과, 그 선량한 벗이자 수호신인 도빈이 길 잃은 그를 다시 애밀리아의 발치로 데려가고 있는 동안, 조지의 아버지와 누이들은 그가 거부하리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못한 채 그를 위해 이 으리으리한 혼사를 꾸미고 있었다.\n\n연장자 오스본이 이른바 \"암시\"라는 것을 줄 때면, 아무리 둔한 자라도 그 뜻을 오해할 가능성은 없었다. 그는 하인을 계단 아래로 걷어차는 것을 두고 그 하인더러 일을 그만두라는 암시라고 불렀다. 그는 여느 때의 그 솔직함과 섬세함으로 해기스턴 부인에게, 아들이 그녀가 후견하는 처녀와 혼인하는 날 오천 파운드짜리 수표를 끊어 주겠다고 말했다. 그러고는 그 제안을 암시라 일컬으며, 자못 능란한 외교적 수완이라 여겼다. 마침내 그는 상속녀에 관해서도 조지에게 그런 식의 암시를 하나 던졌으니, 집사에게 와인 병마개를 뽑으라 명하거나 서기에게 편지를 쓰라 명하듯, 두말 말고 그녀와 혼인하라고 명령했던 것이다.\n\n이 단호한 암시는 조지를 적잖이 동요케 했다. 그는 애밀리아를 향한 두 번째 구애의 더없이 싱그러운 열정과 기쁨에 막 젖어들던 참이었고, 그 기쁨은 그에게 말할 수 없이 달콤했다. 그녀의 몸가짐과 용모를 상속녀의 그것과 견주어 보면, 상속녀와 맺어진다는 생각이 갑절로 우스꽝스럽고 역겹게 느껴지는 것이었다. 마차며 오페라 특별석이라니, 하고 그는 생각했다. 저런 마호가니빛 미녀를 곁에 두고 그런 데서 사람들 눈에 띄는 꼴이라니! 게다가 젊은 오스본은 늙은 오스본 못지않게 고집이 셌다. 무언가를 원할 때면 그것을 손에 넣으려는 결심이 아비만큼이나 굳었고, 화가 났을 때면 아비가 가장 준엄하던 순간 못지않게 사나웠다.\n\n아버지가 미스 스워츠의 발치에 애정을 바치라고 정식으로 암시를 던진 첫날, 조지는 그 노신사에게 시간을 끌었다. \"더 일찍 생각하셨어야지요, 아버지.\" 그가 말했다. \"지금은 안 됩니다. 날마다 외국 파병을 기다리는 처지인걸요. 제가 돌아올 때까지 기다리세요. 돌아온다면 말이지만요.\" 그러고는 연대가 영국을 곧 떠나리라고 날마다 대기하는 이런 때야말로 더없이 부적절한 시기라고 둘러댔다. 아직 본국에 머무를 며칠 혹은 몇 주는 연애가 아니라 일에 바쳐야 마땅하며, 그런 일이야 자기가 소령으로 진급해 돌아온 뒤에 해도 늦지 않다는 것이었다. \"약속드리지요.\" 그는 흡족한 기색으로 말했다. \"어떻게든 조지 오스본이라는 이름을 관보에서 읽으시게 해 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n이에 대한 아버지의 대답은 그가 시내에서 입수한 정보에 근거한 것이었다. 즉 조금이라도 지체했다가는 웨스트엔드의 작자들이 영락없이 상속녀를 낚아챌 것이며, 미스 스워츠와 혼인하지 못한다 해도 적어도 영국에 돌아오면 효력이 발생하는 서면 약혼이라도 맺어 둬야 하고, 집에 가만히 앉아 연 만 파운드를 거머쥘 수 있는 사내가 외국에서 목숨을 거는 짓은 바보나 할 짓이라는 것이었다.\n\n\"그러니까 저더러 겁쟁이로 손가락질받게 하고, 미스 스워츠의 돈을 위해 우리 가문의 이름에 먹칠을 하라는 말씀이로군요.\" 조지가 끼어들었다.\n\n이 말에 노신사는 휘청했지만, 그래도 대꾸는 해야 했고 또 마음만은 이미 굳혀 둔 터라 이렇게 말했다. \"내일 여기서 저녁을 먹어라. 그리고 미스 스워츠가 오는 날이면 날마다 여기 와서 그녀에게 인사를 올려라. 돈이 궁하거든 초퍼 씨를 찾아가고.\" 이렇게 하여 조지가 애밀리아를 두고 세운 계획을 방해하는 새로운 장애물이 그의 앞길에 놓이게 되었으니, 그와 도빈은 이 일을 두고 한두 번이 아니게 은밀한 의논을 나누었다. 그가 취해야 할 처신에 관한 친구의 의견이야 우리는 이미 알고 있다. 그리고 오스본으로 말하자면, 일단 무언가에 마음을 굳히고 나면 한두 개쯤 새로운 장애물이 생긴들 그를 한층 더 결연하게 만들 뿐이었다.\n\n오스본 가문의 수뇌들이 꾸민 이 음모의 가무잡잡한 표적은, 자기에 관한 그들의 온갖 계획을 까맣게 모르고 있었다(이상한 일이지만, 그녀의 친구이자 보호자도 이를 발설하지 않았다). 그리하여 젊은 숙녀들의 온갖 아첨을 진심에서 우러난 감정으로 받아들였고, 앞서 보일 기회가 있었듯이 본디 자못 다정하고 격정적인 천성인지라, 그 애정에 자못 열대 지방다운 열정으로 화답했다. 그리고 사실대로 말하자면, 그녀 역시 러셀 광장 저택에 무언가 이기적인 끌림이 있었으리라고 나는 감히 짐작한다. 한마디로, 그녀는 조지 오스본을 자못 멋진 청년이라고 여겼던 것이다. 헐커 상회의 무도회에서 그를 처음 본 바로 그날 밤부터, 그의 구레나룻은 그녀에게 깊은 인상을 남겼다. 그리고 우리도 알다시피, 그 구레나룻에 매혹된 여인이 그녀가 처음은 아니었다. 조지에게는 거들먹거리면서도 우수에 잠긴 듯한, 나른하면서도 사나운 분위기가 한꺼번에 어려 있었다. 그는 격정과 비밀과, 남에게 말 못 할 가슴 저미는 슬픔과 모험을 간직한 사내처럼 보였다. 그의 목소리는 풍부하고 깊었다. 그가 날이 따뜻하다고 말하거나 상대에게 아이스크림을 권할 때면, 마치 어머니의 부음을 전하거나 사랑 고백의 서두를 떼는 사람처럼 슬프고도 은밀한 어조였다. 그는 아버지 사교계의 젊은 멋쟁이들을 죄다 짓밟았고, 그 삼류들 사이에서 영웅이었다. 더러는 그를 비웃고 미워했다. 더러는 도빈처럼 그를 광적으로 흠모했다. 그리고 그의 구레나룻은 이미 제 몫을 하기 시작하여, 미스 스워츠의 애정을 휘감아 들어가고 있었다.\n\n러셀 광장에서 그를 만날 가망이 보일 때면, 그 순박하고 마음씨 고운 젊은 여인은 사랑하는 오스본 자매를 만나러 갈 생각에 안절부절못했다. 그녀는 새 가운이며 팔찌며 보닛이며 엄청난 깃털 장식에 막대한 돈을 쏟아부었다. 정복자의 마음에 들고자 온갖 솜씨를 다해 몸치장을 했고, 그의 환심을 사고자 자기의 소박한 재주를 죄다 펼쳐 보였다. 자매가 더없이 진지한 얼굴로 음악을 좀 들려 달라 청하면, 그녀는 청할 때마다 노래 세 곡을 부르고 짧은 곡 두 가지를 연주했으니, 그럴수록 스스로 느끼는 기쁨도 점점 커져 갔다. 이 즐거운 여흥이 펼쳐지는 동안, 워트 양과 보호자는 곁에 앉아 귀족 명감을 들춰 보며 귀족 이야기를 나누었다.\n\n조지가 아버지에게서 암시를 받은 이튿날, 저녁 식사 시간을 조금 앞두고, 그는 응접실 소파에 늘어진 채로 더없이 어울리면서도 지극히 자연스러운 우수의 자세를 취하고 있었다. 그는 아버지의 분부대로 시내의 초퍼 씨에게 다녀온 참이었다(노신사는 아들에게 큰돈을 주면서도 일정한 용돈을 정해 주는 법이 결코 없었고, 그저 기분 내키는 대로 상을 내릴 따름이었다). 그러고 나서 풀럼에 가 사랑하는 자기 어린 애밀리아와 세 시간을 보냈고, 집에 돌아와 보니 누이들은 풀 먹인 모슬린을 펼쳐 입고 응접실에 앉아 있었으며, 노부인들은 뒤편에서 재잘대고 있었고, 정직한 스워츠는 그 좋아하는 호박색 새틴 드레스에 터키석 팔찌, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 반지며 꽃이며 깃털이며 온갖 끈과 싸구려 장신구를 주렁주렁 매단 채, 오월제의 여자 굴뚝 청소부만큼이나 우아하게 차려입고 있었다.\n\n자매는 그를 대화에 끌어들이려다 번번이 실패하자, 그가 그 수다에 진력이 날 때까지 유행이며 지난번 궁정 알현 이야기를 늘어놓았다. 그는 그들의 행동거지를 어린 에미의 그것과 견주었다. 그들의 새된 목소리를 그녀의 부드럽고 낭랑한 음성과, 그들의 자세며 팔꿈치며 풀 먹인 옷차림을 그녀의 다소곳하고 부드러운 몸짓이며 단아한 기품과 견주어 본 것이다. 가엾은 스워츠는 에미가 늘 앉던 자리에 앉아 있었다. 보석을 주렁주렁 단 손은 호박색 새틴 무릎 위에 아무렇게나 늘어져 있었다. 그녀의 장식 끈과 귀고리는 반짝였고, 큰 눈은 두리번두리번 굴러다녔다. 그녀는 더없이 만족스러운 얼굴로 아무것도 하지 않고 있었고, 자기가 매력적이라고 여기고 있었다. 누이들은 그 새틴만큼 어울리는 옷을 본 적이 없었다.\n\n\"제기랄.\" 조지가 절친한 벗에게 말했다. \"하루 종일 히죽거리며 고개나 까딱대는 것밖에는 할 일이 없는 도자기 인형 같더라니까. 맙소사, 윌, 소파 쿠션을 그 여자한테 집어 던지지 않으려고 안간힘을 다 썼다네.\" 그렇긴 해도 그는 그런 감정 표출만큼은 억눌렀다.\n\n자매는 「프라하 전투」를 연주하기 시작했다. \"그 빌어먹을 거 좀 그만둬.\" 조지가 소파에서 노발대발 고함쳤다. \"미쳐 버리겠으니. 미스 스워츠, 당신이 뭐든 좀 연주해 주시오. 뭐든 좋으니 노래를 불러요. 「프라하 전투」만 빼고요.\"\n\n\"「푸른 눈의 메리」를 부를까요, 아니면 「캐비닛」에 나오는 노래를 부를까요?\" 미스 스워츠가 물었다.\n\n\"「캐비닛」에 나오는 그 감미로운 곡이요.\" 자매가 말했다.\n\n\"그건 이미 들었잖소.\" 소파에 앉은 인간 혐오자가 대꾸했다.\n\n\"「플루비 뒤 타지」를 부를 수 있어요.\" 스워츠가 다소곳한 목소리로 말했다. \"가사만 있다면요.\" 이것이 이 갸륵한 젊은 여인의 보유 목록 가운데 마지막 곡이었다.\n\n\"아, 「플뢰브 뒤 타주」 말이군요.\" 마리아 양이 외쳤다. \"그 노래라면 우리한테 있어요.\" 그러고는 그 곡이 실린 악보집을 가지러 갔다.\n\n그런데 마침 그 무렵 한창 유행하던 이 노래는, 표지에 이름이 적힌 그들의 어느 젊은 친구가 선물한 것이었다. 미스 스워츠가 조지의 박수를 받으며 그 노래를 끝마치고는(그가 그 곡을 애밀리아가 좋아하던 것으로 기억해 두었기 때문이다), 어쩌면 앙코르를 바라며 악보 책장을 만지작거리고 있을 때, 그녀의 눈이 표지에 가닿았고, 구석에 \"애밀리아 세들리\"라고 적힌 것을 발견했다.\n\n\"어머나!\" 미스 스워츠가 피아노 의자 위에서 휙 몸을 돌리며 외쳤다. \"이거 우리 애밀리아 아니에요? 해머스미스의 핑커튼 여사님 학원에 있던 그 애밀리아 말이에요? 그 애가 틀림없어요. 그 애예요. 그리고…… 그 애 얘기 좀 해 주세요. 지금 어디 있어요?\"\n\n\"그 애 얘긴 꺼내지 마세요.\" 마리아 오스본 양이 황급히 말했다. \"그 집안은 망신을 자초했어요. 그 애 아버지가 우리 아빠를 속였고, 그 애로 말하자면 이 집에서는 절대 입에 올려서는 안 되는 사람이에요.\" 이것은 「프라하 전투」를 두고 조지가 무례하게 군 데 대한 마리아 양의 앙갚음이었다.\n\n\"당신이 애밀리아의 친구요?\" 조지가 벌떡 일어서며 말했다. \"그것만으로도 하느님의 축복이 있기를 빌겠소, 미스 스워츠. 누이들 말은 믿지 마시오. 어쨌든 그녀에게는 아무 잘못도 없으니까. 그녀는 세상에서 가장……\"\n\n\"오라버니, 그 애 얘긴 하면 안 되는 거 알잖아요.\" 제인이 외쳤다. \"아빠가 금하셨다고요.\"\n\n\"누가 날 막는단 말이냐?\" 조지가 소리쳤다. \"난 그녀 얘기를 하겠다. 그녀는 영국에서 가장 착하고, 가장 다정하고, 가장 온화하고, 가장 사랑스러운 처녀라고 말하겠다. 그리고 파산을 했건 안 했건, 내 누이들은 그녀 앞에서 촛불을 들 자격조차 없다. 당신이 그녀를 좋아한다면, 가서 만나 보시오, 미스 스워츠. 지금 그녀에겐 친구가 필요하오. 그리고 나는 말하겠소, 그녀를 도와주는 모든 이에게 하느님의 축복이 있기를. 그녀를 다정하게 말하는 사람은 누구든 내 친구요, 그녀를 헐뜯는 사람은 누구든 내 적이오. 고맙소, 미스 스워츠.\" 그러고는 그녀에게 다가가 그 손을 힘껏 쥐었다.\n\n\"오라버니! 오라버니!\" 자매 가운데 하나가 애원하듯 외쳤다.\n\n\"내 말하겠다.\" 조지가 사납게 말했다. \"애밀리아 세들리를 사랑하는 사람이라면 누구에게나 감사를……\" 그는 말을 멈췄다. 늙은 오스본이 분노로 새파랗게 질린 얼굴에 시뻘건 숯덩이 같은 눈을 하고 방 안에 들어와 있었다.\n\n조지는 말을 멈추긴 했어도, 일단 피가 끓어오른 터라 오스본 가문 전 세대가 다 덤벼든들 주눅 들 사람이 아니었다. 그는 즉시 기운을 추슬러, 으름장 놓는 아버지의 눈길에 또 다른 눈길로 맞섰으니, 그 눈빛에 어린 결연함과 반항의 기색이 어찌나 또렷했던지 이번엔 연장자 쪽이 움찔하며 시선을 돌려 버렸다. 그는 한판 싸움이 다가오고 있음을 느꼈다. \"해기스턴 부인, 제가 식당까지 모시겠습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"조지, 미스 스워츠께 팔을 내드려라.\" 그리하여 그들은 줄지어 내려갔다.\n\n\"미스 스워츠, 나는 애밀리아를 사랑하오. 우리는 거의 평생 동안 약혼한 사이였소.\" 오스본이 옆자리 짝에게 말했다. 그리고 만찬 내내 조지는 자기 스스로도 놀랄 만큼 청산유수로 떠들어 댔으니, 그 통에 아버지는 숙녀들이 자리를 뜨는 즉시 벌어질 싸움을 두고 갑절로 안절부절못했다.\n\n이 부자 사이의 차이라면, 아버지는 난폭하고 으름장을 잘 놓는 자였던 반면, 아들은 부모보다 세 곱절은 더 담대하고 용기가 있어서, 단지 공격을 가할 뿐 아니라 그것을 막아 낼 줄도 안다는 점이었다. 그리하여 아버지와 자기 사이의 결판이 이제 곧 날 순간이 왔음을 알아채고도, 그는 전투가 시작되기 전까지 더없이 침착하게, 또 입맛 좋게 저녁을 먹었다. 반면 늙은 오스본은 안절부절못하며 술을 많이 마셨다. 그는 옆자리 숙녀들과의 대화에서 갈팡질팡했고, 조지의 침착함은 그를 한층 더 화나게 할 뿐이었다. 조지가 냅킨을 탁탁 털며 거들먹거리는 자세로 허리를 굽혀 숙녀들이 방을 나가도록 문을 열어 주고는, 자기 잔에 술을 가득 따라 입맛을 다시며, 마치 \"근위대 신사 여러분, 먼저 쏘시지요\" 하고 말하기라도 하듯 아버지의 얼굴을 똑바로 쳐다보는 그 태연한 꼴을 보고 있자니, 늙은이는 거의 미칠 지경이었다. 노인도 탄약을 채워 넣었지만, 술을 따르려는 그의 손에 술병이 잔에 부딪쳐 쨍그랑 소리를 냈다.\n\n이윽고 한 번 크게 숨을 몰아쉬더니, 자줏빛으로 숨이 막힐 듯한 얼굴로 그는 입을 열었다. \"이놈, 네가 어찌 감히 오늘 내 응접실에서, 미스 스워츠 앞에서 그 계집의 이름을 입에 올렸느냐? 묻겠다, 이놈아, 네가 어찌 감히 그런 짓을 했느냔 말이다.\"\n\n\"그만하세요, 아버지.\" 조지가 말했다. \"감히라는 말은 쓰지 마십시오. 감히라는 말은 영국 육군 대위에게 쓸 말이 못 됩니다.\"\n\n\"난 내 아들한테 하고 싶은 말은 뭐든 할 테다, 이놈아. 내키면 한 푼만 쥐여 주고 의절할 수도 있어. 내키면 거지로 만들어 버릴 수도 있고. 난 하고 싶은 말은 하고야 말 테다.\" 연장자가 말했다.\n\n\"제가 아버지의 아들이긴 해도 신사입니다, 아버지.\" 조지가 거만하게 대꾸했다. \"저에게 하실 말씀이나 내리실 분부가 있다면, 제가 익히 들어 온 그런 종류의 언사로 표현해 주시기를 청합니다.\"\n\n이 젊은이가 거만한 태도를 취할 때면, 그것은 어김없이 아비에게 커다란 경외감이나 커다란 분노를 불러일으켰다. 늙은 오스본은 아들이 자기보다 더 나은 신사라는 점에서 남몰래 그를 두려워했다. 그리고 우리 독자 여러분께서도 우리네 이 허영의 시장에서 겪어 본 바, 비루한 인간이 가장 미더워하지 못하는 인물이 바로 신사라는 사실을 더러 눈여겨보았을 것이다.\n\n\"우리 아버진 너처럼 교육을 시켜 주지도 않았고, 너처럼 혜택을 베풀어 주지도 않았고, 너처럼 돈을 주지도 않았다. 만일 내가 어떤 사람들이 내 덕분에 어울리고 다닌 그런 패거리들과 어울렸더라면, 아마 내 자식이 제 잘난 우월함이니 웨스트엔드의 거드름이니 하는 걸 떠벌릴 거리도 없었을 게다(이 말은 연장자 오스본 특유의 더없이 빈정대는 어조로 흘러나왔다). 하지만 내가 젊었던 시절엔, 사내가 제 아비를 모욕하는 게 신사가 할 짓으로 여겨지지 않았어. 내가 그런 짓을 했더라면 우리 아버지가 날 계단 아래로 걷어차 버렸을 게다, 이놈아.\"\n\n\"전 아버지를 모욕한 적이 없습니다. 다만 아버지의 아들도 아버지 못지않은 신사라는 점을 기억해 주십사 청했을 따름입니다. 아버지가 제게 돈을 넉넉히 주신다는 건 저도 잘 압니다.\" 조지가 (그날 아침 초퍼 씨에게서 받은 지폐 뭉치를 만지작거리며) 말했다. \"그 말씀은 충분히 자주 하시니까요, 아버지. 잊어버릴 염려는 없습니다.\"\n\n\"다른 일들도 그만큼 잘 기억해 주면 좋겠구나, 이놈아.\" 아비가 대꾸했다. \"이 집에서는 말이다, 네놈이 그 잘난 행차로 이 집을 빛내 주시는 동안에는 말이지, 대위 나리, 주인은 나란 걸 기억해 주면 좋겠다. 그리고 그 이름은, 그 이름은…… 네놈이…… 내 말은……\"\n\n\"그 이름이 뭐 어떻다는 겁니까, 아버지?\" 조지가 거의 비웃음에 가까운 기색으로 클라레를 한 잔 더 따르며 물었다.\n\n\"……!\" 아버지가 째지는 소리로 욕설을 터뜨리며 폭발했다. \"그 세들리네 이름은 이 집에서 두 번 다시 입에 올리지 말란 말이다, 이놈아. 그 빌어먹을 족속 중에 단 한 놈도, 이놈아.\"\n\n\"세들리 양의 이름을 꺼낸 건 제가 아닙니다, 아버지. 미스 스워츠에게 그녀를 험담한 건 제 누이들이었어요. 그리고 맹세코 저는 어딜 가든 그녀를 옹호하겠습니다. 제 앞에서 그 이름을 함부로 말하게 두지 않겠어요. 우리 집안은 그녀에게 이미 충분히 못할 짓을 했다고 봅니다. 이제 그녀가 영락했으니 헐뜯는 짓은 그만둬도 좋을 텐데요. 그녀를 두고 한마디라도 험담하는 자가 있다면, 아버지만 빼고 누구든 총으로 쏘아 버리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"계속해 봐, 이놈, 계속해 봐.\" 노신사가 눈알이 튀어나올 듯한 얼굴로 말했다.\n\n\"뭘 계속하라는 겁니까, 아버지? 우리가 그 천사 같은 처녀를 대접한 꼴 말입니까? 누가 저더러 그녀를 사랑하라 했습니까? 아버지가 그러셨잖아요. 다른 데서 짝을 고를 수도 있었고, 어쩌면 아버지 사교계보다 더 높은 데를 넘볼 수도 있었지만, 저는 아버지께 순종했습니다. 그런데 이제 와 그녀의 마음이 제 것이 되자, 그걸 내던지고 그녀를 벌하라고, 어쩌면 죽이라고 명령하시는군요. 그것도 남들의 잘못 때문에 말입니다. 맹세코 이건 부끄러운 짓입니다.\" 조지는 말을 이어 갈수록 격정과 흥분에 점점 더 사로잡혀 갔다. \"어린 처녀의 애정을 가지고 이랬다저랬다 농락하다니요. 그것도 그런 천사를요. 함께 살던 사람들보다 어찌나 빼어났던지 시샘을 살 만도 했건만, 워낙 착하고 온화한 사람이라 감히 그녀를 미워할 엄두를 낸 자가 있었다는 게 신기할 지경이었지요. 제가 그녀를 버린다면, 아버지, 그녀가 절 잊을 것 같습니까?\"\n\n\"이 집에서 그따위 빌어먹을 감상적인 헛소리며 허튼수작은 더는 못 듣겠다, 이놈아.\" 아비가 고래고래 소리쳤다. \"내 집안엔 거지와의 혼인 따윈 없을 게다. 청하기만 하면 굴러들어 올 연 팔천 파운드를 내던지고 싶거든, 그래 던져 버려라. 하지만 맹세코 네 보따리나 챙겨서 이 집을 나가거라, 이놈아. 내 말대로 하겠느냐, 이번 한 번으로 끝장을 보자, 하겠느냐 말겠느냐?\"\n\n\"그 물라토 여자랑 혼인하라고요?\" 조지가 셔츠 깃을 추켜올리며 말했다. \"전 그 색깔이 마음에 안 듭니다, 아버지. 플리트 시장 맞은편을 쓰는 흑인한테나 청해 보시지요. 전 호텐토트의 비너스랑은 혼인하지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n오스본 씨는 와인이 필요할 때 집사를 부르려고 늘 잡아당기던 그 줄을 미친 듯이 잡아챘고, 얼굴이 거의 시커멓게 변한 채로 그 하인에게 오스본 대위를 위해 마차를 불러오라 명했다.\n\n\"끝장을 봤다네.\" 한 시간 뒤 슬로터스 식당으로 들어서며 조지가 새파랗게 질린 얼굴로 말했다.\n\n\"뭘 말인가, 이 친구야?\" 도빈이 물었다.\n\n조지는 아버지와 자기 사이에 오간 이야기를 들려주었다.\n\n\"난 내일 그녀와 혼인하겠네.\" 그가 맹세코 다짐하듯 말했다. \"날이 갈수록 그녀가 더 사랑스러워, 도빈.\"\n\n아무리 완강하고 용맹한 적이라 해도 굶주림 앞에서는 버틸 재간이 없는 법이다. 그러니 앞서 묘사한 그 대결에서 늙은 오즈본은 자기 적수에 대해 꽤나 마음을 놓고 있었다. 조지의 보급이 바닥나기만 하면 무조건 항복해 올 것이라고 자신만만하게 기대했던 것이다. 물론 첫 대결이 벌어진 바로 그날 그 녀석이 식량을 한 무더기 확보해 두었다는 것은 유감스러운 일이었다. 하지만 늙은 오즈본의 생각으로는 그런 구원이란 일시적인 것에 지나지 않으며, 조지의 항복을 잠시 늦출 뿐이었다. 며칠 동안 부자 사이에는 아무런 연락도 오가지 않았다. 아버지는 이 침묵에 부루퉁했지만 불안해하지는 않았다. 자기 말마따나, 조지에게 어디다 죔쇠를 들이댈 수 있는지 잘 알고 있었고, 그저 그 작전의 결과를 기다릴 뿐이었기 때문이다. 그는 딸들에게 둘 사이에 벌어진 다툼의 자초지종을 들려주면서도, 그 일에 대해서는 일절 내색하지 말고 조지가 돌아오면 아무 일도 없었다는 듯이 맞아 주라고 일러두었다. 그의 식사 자리는 날마다 여느 때처럼 차려졌고, 어쩌면 이 노신사는 다소 애타게 아들을 기다렸는지도 모른다. 그러나 조지는 끝내 오지 않았다. 누군가가 슬로터스에 들러 그의 소식을 묻자, 그가 친구인 도빈 대위와 함께 런던을 떠났다는 대답이 돌아왔다.\n\n사월 말의 어느 거센 바람이 부는 을씨년스러운 날이었다. 옛 슬로터스 커피하우스가 자리했던 그 오래된 거리의 포도를 빗줄기가 후려치고 있었다. 조지 오즈본은 커피하우스 안으로 들어섰는데, 몹시 초췌하고 핼쑥해 보였다. 그래도 차림새는 제법 말쑥해서, 놋쇠 단추가 달린 파란 외투에 그 시절 유행하던 단정한 담황색 조끼를 입고 있었다. 그곳에는 그의 친구 도빈 대위도 역시 파란 옷에 놋쇠 단추 차림으로 와 있었는데, 그의 호리호리한 몸을 늘 감싸던 군용 프록코트와 회청색 바지는 벗어 둔 모습이었다.\n\n도빈은 한 시간 남짓 커피하우스에 있었다. 신문이란 신문은 죄다 들춰 보았으나 도무지 읽히지가 않았다. 시계를 수십 번도 더 쳐다보았고, 빗방울이 후드득 떨어지는 거리도 내다보았다. 사람들이 나막신을 신고 또각또각 지나가며 번들거리는 돌바닥 위에 긴 그림자를 남기고 있었다. 그는 탁자를 손가락으로 두드렸고, 손톱을 거의 속살이 드러나도록 깡그리 물어뜯었으며(원래 자기 그 커다란 두 손을 이런 식으로 꾸미는 버릇이 있었다), 찻숟가락을 우유 단지 위에 능숙하게 올려 균형을 잡다가 엎질러 버리는 등등의 짓을 했다. 요컨대 그는 마음이 몹시 초조하고 무언가를 기다리며 안절부절못할 때 사람들이 으레 드러내는 그런 불안의 징후를 내보였고, 또 그런 때 으레 시도하는 그 필사적인 소일거리들을 부려 보고 있었던 것이다.\n\n이 방을 드나드는 신사인 그의 동료들 가운데 몇몇이, 그 화려한 차림새와 들떠 있는 거동을 두고 그를 놀려 댔다. 한 사람은 그에게 결혼이라도 하느냐고 물었다. 도빈은 웃으며, 그런 일이 벌어지면 그 지인(공병대의 왜그스태프 소령)에게 케이크 한 조각을 보내 주마고 대답했다. 마침내 오즈본 대위가 모습을 드러냈는데, 차림은 무척 말쑥했으나 앞서 말했듯이 몹시 핼쑥하고 동요한 기색이었다. 그는 향수를 엄청나게 뿌린 커다란 노란색 반다나 손수건으로 핼쑥한 얼굴을 닦았다. 그는 도빈과 악수를 나누고, 시계를 본 뒤, 웨이터 존에게 퀴라소를 가져오라고 일렀다. 그러고는 이 술을 두어 잔, 신경질적인 조급함으로 단숨에 들이켰다. 그의 친구는 다소 걱정스레 그의 건강을 물었다.\n\n\"날이 샐 때까지 한숨도 못 잤어, 도브.\" 그가 말했다. \"지독한 두통에 열까지 나서 말이야. 아홉 시에 일어나서 허멈스로 목욕하러 갔지. 이봐, 도브, 꼭 퀘벡에서 로켓을 타고 출진하던 그날 아침 같은 기분이야.\"\n\n\"나도 그래.\" 윌리엄이 대답했다. \"그날 아침엔 너보다 내가 훨씬 더 떨었지. 너는 아침을 푸짐하게 먹었던 게 기억나는걸. 자, 지금도 뭐 좀 들어.\"\n\n\"넌 참 좋은 친구야, 윌. 네 건강을 위해 한잔 들겠어, 친구. 그리고 작별을 고하지, 저—\"\n\n\"안 돼, 안 돼. 두 잔이면 충분해.\" 도빈이 그의 말을 가로막았다. \"이봐, 존, 술은 치워 주게. 닭 요리에는 카옌 후추를 좀 쳐서 먹어. 그래도 서둘러야 해. 이제 그곳에 가 있어야 할 시간이니까.\"\n\n이 두 대위 사이에 이 짧은 만남과 대화가 오간 것은 열두 시가 되기 삼십 분쯤 전이었다. 오즈본 대위의 하인이 주인의 책상과 화장 도구함을 실어 둔 마차가 한동안 대기하고 있었다. 두 신사는 우산 하나를 받쳐 들고 그 마차 안으로 황급히 올라탔고, 시종은 빗줄기와 옆자리에서 김을 피워 올리고 있는 마부의 축축함을 욕하며 마부석에 올라앉았다. \"교회 앞에 가면 이것보다 나은 마차가 있을 거야.\" 그가 말했다. \"그게 그나마 위안이지.\" 마차는 피커딜리 길을 따라 달려 내려갔다. 그곳은 앱슬리 하우스와 세인트조지 병원이 아직 붉은 외투를 걸치고 있던 시절이요, 기름등이 켜져 있고, 아킬레우스 상은 아직 태어나지도 않았으며, 핌리코 아치도 세워지지 않았고, 그 일대를 온통 뒤덮은 흉측한 기마상 괴물도 없던 시절이었다. 그리하여 그들은 브롬프턴을 지나 풀럼 로드 근처의 어느 예배당으로 마차를 몰아갔다.\n\n말 네 마리가 끄는 사륜마차 한 대가 대기하고 있었고, 유리 마차라 불리는 종류의 마차도 한 대 있었다. 음울한 비 탓에 한가한 구경꾼은 아주 적게 모여 있었다.\n\n\"제기랄!\" 조지가 말했다. \"난 두 마리면 된다고 했는데.\"\n\n\"저희 주인 나리께서 네 마리를 원하셨습죠.\" 대기하고 있던 조지프 세들리 씨의 하인이 말했다. 그와 오즈본 씨의 하인은 조지와 윌리엄을 따라 교회로 들어가면서, 이건 \"영 초라한 행차다, 아침 식사 한 끼는커녕 결혼 장식 리본 하나 변변히 없으니\" 하고 의견을 모았다.\n\n\"여기 왔네그려.\" 우리의 옛 친구 조스 세들리가 앞으로 나서며 말했다. \"오 분 늦었어, 조지, 이 친구야. 날씨 한번 고약하군, 응? 제기랄, 벵골의 우기가 시작될 때랑 똑같구먼. 그래도 내 마차는 물 한 방울 안 새니까 걱정 말게. 자, 어서 오게. 어머니와 에미는 제의실에 계시네.\"\n\n조스 세들리는 휘황찬란했다. 어느 때보다도 더 살이 쪄 있었다. 셔츠 칼라는 더 높아졌고, 얼굴은 더 붉어졌으며, 셔츠 주름 장식이 알록달록한 조끼 사이로 화려하게 펄럭였다. 에나멜 부츠란 아직 발명되기 전이었으나, 그의 멋진 다리를 감싼 헤시안 부츠가 어찌나 반들거리던지, 저 옛 그림 속 신사가 면도할 때 거울 삼았다는 바로 그 부츠임에 틀림없어 보일 지경이었다. 그리고 그의 연녹색 외투에는, 활짝 핀 커다란 흰 목련처럼 멋들어진 결혼 장식 리본이 피어 있었다.\n\n한마디로, 조지는 운명의 큰 주사위를 던진 것이었다. 그는 결혼하려던 참이었다. 그의 창백함과 초조함, 잠 못 이룬 밤과 아침의 동요는 다 거기서 비롯된 것이었다. 나는 같은 일을 겪어 본 사람들이 똑같은 심경을 토로하는 것을 들은 적이 있다. 서너 번쯤 그 의식을 치르고 나면 분명 익숙해지겠지만, 첫 번째 입수만큼은 누구나 끔찍하다고 인정하는 것이다.\n\n신부는 갈색 비단 펠리스를 입고(이 점은 그 뒤 도빈 대위가 내게 일러 준 바이다), 분홍 리본을 단 밀짚 보닛을 쓰고 있었다. 보닛 위로는 흰 샹티이 레이스 베일을 드리웠는데, 이는 그녀의 오라비 조지프 세들리 씨가 준 선물이었다. 도빈 대위 본인은 그녀에게 금줄 시계를 선물하게 해 달라고 청한 터였고, 그녀는 이날 그것을 차고 있었다. 그녀의 어머니는 다이아몬드 브로치를 주었는데, 이 노부인에게 남은 거의 유일한 패물이었다. 예식이 진행되는 동안 세들리 부인은 신도석에 앉아 줄곧 훌쩍였고, 아일랜드인 하녀와 하숙집의 클랩 부인이 그녀를 달랬다. 늙은 세들리는 참석하려 들지 않았다. 조스가 아버지를 대신해 신부를 인도했고, 도빈 대위는 친구 조지의 들러리로 나섰다.\n\n교회 안에는 예식을 집전하는 이들과 조촐한 결혼식 일행, 그리고 그들의 시중꾼 말고는 아무도 없었다. 두 하인은 거만하게 떨어져 앉아 있었다. 빗줄기가 창문을 후드득 때렸다. 예식이 잠시 끊길 때면 그 빗소리와, 신도석에서 흐느끼는 늙은 세들리 부인의 울음소리가 들렸다. 목사의 음성이 텅 빈 벽을 따라 구슬프게 울렸다. 오즈본의 \"맹세합니다\"는 아주 깊은 저음으로 울려 나왔다. 에미의 응답은 마음속에서 떨리며 입술로 솟아올랐으나, 도빈 대위 말고는 누구의 귀에도 거의 닿지 않았다.\n\n예식이 끝나자 조스 세들리가 앞으로 나서서, 여러 달 만에 처음으로 신부가 된 누이에게 입을 맞추었다. 조지의 침울한 기색은 사라지고, 그는 자못 자랑스럽고 환한 표정이었다. \"이제 네 차례야, 윌리엄.\" 그가 도빈의 어깨에 다정하게 손을 얹으며 말했다. 도빈은 다가가 애밀리아의 뺨에 가볍게 입을 맞추었다.\n\n그러고 나서 그들은 제의실로 들어가 혼인부에 서명했다. \"하느님의 가호가 있기를, 도빈 친구.\" 조지가 그의 손을 꽉 움켜쥐며 말했는데, 그 두 눈에는 물기 같은 것이 어른거렸다. 윌리엄은 그저 고개만 끄덕여 답했다. 가슴이 너무 벅차서 많은 말을 할 수 없었던 것이다.\n\n\"바로 편지해, 그리고 되도록 빨리 내려오라고, 알지?\" 오즈본이 말했다. 세들리 부인이 발작하듯 딸과 작별 인사를 나눈 뒤, 신랑 신부는 마차로 향했다. \"비켜라, 이 작은 악동들아.\" 조지가 예배당 문간에 몰려 있는 비에 젖은 부랑아 떼를 향해 소리쳤다. 신랑 신부가 사륜마차로 걸어가는 동안 빗줄기가 그들의 얼굴을 후려쳤다. 마부들의 장식 리본이 흠뻑 젖은 외투 위로 축 늘어졌다. 마차가 진흙을 튀기며 떠나가자, 몇 안 되는 아이들이 음울한 환호를 보냈다.\n\n윌리엄 도빈은 교회 현관에 서서 그 광경을 바라보았는데, 묘한 행색이었다. 조무래기 구경꾼 무리가 그를 비웃었다. 그는 그들도, 그들의 비웃음도 안중에 없었다.\n\n\"집에 가서 점심이나 좀 들세, 도빈.\" 그의 등 뒤에서 한 목소리가 외쳤고, 통통한 손 하나가 그의 어깨에 얹히면서 이 정직한 사내의 상념이 깨졌다. 그러나 대위는 조스 세들리와 어울려 잔치를 즐길 마음이 없었다. 그는 우는 노부인과 그 시중꾼들을 조스와 함께 마차에 태우고는, 더 이상 아무 말도 나누지 않고 그들과 헤어졌다. 이 마차도 떠나갔고, 부랑아들이 또 한 번 빈정대는 환호를 보냈다.\n\n\"자, 받아라, 이 거지 녀석들아.\" 도빈이 그들에게 육 펜스짜리 동전을 몇 닢 나눠 주며 말한 뒤, 빗속을 홀로 걸어갔다. 모든 게 끝났다. 그들은 결혼했고, 부디 행복하기를 그는 하느님께 빌었다. 소년 시절 이후로 그가 이토록 비참하고 외롭게 느낀 적은 없었다. 그는 가슴앓이하는 듯한 그리움으로, 그녀를 다시 볼 수 있도록 이 처음 며칠이 어서 지나가기를 간절히 바랐다.\n\n위의 예식이 있은 지 열흘쯤 지나서, 우리가 아는 세 젊은이가 브라이턴이 나그네에게 선사하는 그 아름다운 풍경, 즉 한쪽으로는 활처럼 휜 출창들, 다른 쪽으로는 푸른 바다가 펼쳐지는 그 경치를 즐기고 있었다. 런던에서 온 사람은 때로는 바다 쪽을 황홀하게 바라본다. 수없는 보조개로 미소 짓고, 흰 돛들로 점점이 수놓이고, 수백 대의 해수욕 탈의 마차가 그 푸른 옷자락에 입 맞추는 바다 쪽을. 그런가 하면 때로는, 어떤 종류의 경치보다도 인간 본성을 더 사랑하는 이라면 도리어 그 출창들 쪽으로, 그리고 그 안에 들끓는 인간 군상 쪽으로 눈길을 돌린다. 어느 창에서는 피아노 소리가 흘러나오는데, 곱슬머리 아가씨가 같은 하숙인들을 기쁘게 하느라 날마다 여섯 시간씩 연습하는 것이다. 또 다른 창에서는 사랑스러운 보모 폴리가 옴니엄 도련님을 품에 안고 어르는 모습이 보이고, 아래쪽 창에서는 그 도련님의 아빠 제이컵이 새우를 까먹으며 아침 식사 삼아 타임스지를 게걸스레 읽고 있는 모습이 보인다. 저쪽엔 리어리 자매가 있어, 분명 절벽 위를 거닐고 있을 중기병대 젊은 장교들을 내다보고 있다. 또는 항해에 취미가 있는 어느 시티 사람이 육 파운드 포만 한 망원경을 들고서, 해안에 닿거나 떠나는 유람선이며 청어잡이 배며 해수욕 탈의 마차를 죄다 한눈에 살피려고 그 기구를 바다 쪽으로 겨누고 있다, 운운. 그런데 우리에게 브라이턴을 묘사할 겨를이 있던가? 점잖은 부랑자들이 사는 깨끗한 나폴리 같은 브라이턴, 어릿광대의 저고리처럼 늘 활기차고 흥겹고 야단스러워 보이는 브라이턴, 우리 이야기 무렵에는 런던에서 일곱 시간 거리였으나 지금은 백 분밖에 안 걸리는 브라이턴, 그리고 주앵빌이 와서 때아니게 포격이라도 하지 않는 한 앞으로 또 얼마나 더 가까워질지 모를 브라이턴을 말이다.\n\n\"모자 가게 위층 셋방에 사는 저 아가씨는 어찌나 끝내주게 예쁜지.\" 이 세 산책자 가운데 하나가 다른 이에게 한마디 했다. \"이런, 크롤리, 내가 지나갈 때 저 여자가 나한테 윙크하는 거 봤나?\"\n\n\"그 여자 마음을 아프게 하지 말게, 조스, 이 악당아.\" 또 한 사람이 말했다. \"그 여자의 애정을 가지고 장난치지 말라고, 이 돈 후안 같으니!\"\n\n\"이 사람 참.\" 조스 세들리가 자못 흐뭇해하며, 문제의 그 하녀를 향해 더없이 살인적인 추파를 흘끔 던지며 말했다. 조스는 누이의 결혼식 때보다도 브라이턴에서 한층 더 휘황찬란했다. 그가 입은 화려한 속조끼는 어느 것 하나만으로도 어지간한 멋쟁이를 차려 줄 만했다. 그는 장식 끈과 단추, 검은 단추, 구불구불한 자수로 꾸민 군용 프록코트를 떨쳐입고 있었다. 그는 근래 군인 같은 풍채와 습성을 흉내 내고 있었다. 그래서 그 직업에 종사하는 두 친구와 함께 부츠 박차를 절그럭거리고 어마어마하게 으스대며 걸으면서, 죽임을 당해 마땅한 모든 하녀들에게 치명적인 추파를 쏘아 댔다.\n\n\"이보게들, 숙녀들이 돌아올 때까지 뭘 하지?\" 멋쟁이가 물었다. 숙녀들은 그의 마차를 타고 로팅딘으로 드라이브를 나간 참이었다.\n\n\"당구나 한판 칠까.\" 그의 친구 중 하나가 말했다. 옻칠한 듯한 콧수염을 기른 키 큰 사내였다.\n\n\"안 돼, 제기랄. 안 되네, 대위.\" 조스가 다소 질겁하며 대답했다. \"오늘은 당구 안 되네, 크롤리, 이 친구야. 어제로 충분해.\"\n\n\"자네 당구 솜씨가 아주 좋잖나.\" 크롤리가 웃으며 말했다. \"안 그런가, 오즈본? 어제 그 다섯 점짜리 한 방, 어찌나 멋지게 치던지, 응?\"\n\n\"기막혔지.\" 오즈본이 말했다. \"조스는 당구에 귀신이야, 다른 것도 다 마찬가지고. 이 근처에 호랑이 사냥이라도 있으면 좋으련만! 저녁 먹기 전에 몇 마리 잡으러 갈 수 있을 텐데. (저기 예쁜 아가씨가 지나가네! 발목 좀 보게, 응, 조스?) 그 호랑이 사냥 이야기 좀 들려주게, 정글에서 그놈을 어떻게 해치웠는지 말이야. 그거 참 굉장한 이야기지, 크롤리.\" 여기서 조지 오즈본은 하품을 했다. \"여긴 영 따분하단 말이지.\" 그가 말했다. \"뭘 하면 좋을까?\"\n\n\"스내플러가 루이스 장에서 막 끌고 온 말들이나 구경하러 갈까?\" 크롤리가 말했다.\n\n\"더턴 가게에 가서 젤리나 좀 먹는 게 어때.\" 능청맞은 조스가, 일석이조를 노리고 말했다. \"더턴 가게에 끝내주는 아가씨가 있거든.\"\n\n\"라이트닝호가 들어오는 거나 보러 가는 게 어떤가, 마침 그럴 때가 됐는데?\" 조지가 말했다. 이 제안이 마구간과 젤리를 누르고 채택되어, 그들은 라이트닝호가 도착하는 것을 구경하려고 역마차 사무소 쪽으로 향했다.\n\n그렇게 지나가던 길에 그들은 마차 한 대와 마주쳤다. 조스 세들리의 무개 마차로, 그 위에 화려한 문장이 새겨진 그 으리으리한 탈것이었다. 그가 첼튼엄에서 팔짱을 끼고 모자를 비스듬히 쓴 채 위풍당당하게 홀로, 혹은 좀 더 흐뭇하게는 숙녀들을 곁에 태우고 몰고 다니던 그 마차였다.\n\n지금 마차 안에는 둘이 타고 있었다. 하나는 자그마한 사람으로, 머리칼이 옅은 빛깔이고 최신 유행에 따라 차려입고 있었다. 다른 하나는 갈색 비단 펠리스에 분홍 리본을 단 밀짚 보닛 차림이었으며, 보고만 있어도 마음이 흐뭇해지는 발그레하고 둥글고 행복한 얼굴이었다. 그녀는 마차가 세 신사에게 다가가자 마차를 멈춰 세웠는데, 그렇게 권위를 한 번 행사하고 나더니 자못 긴장한 기색으로 어처구니없을 만큼 얼굴을 붉히기 시작했다. \"정말 즐거운 드라이브였어요, 조지.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그리고—그리고 돌아오니 너무 기뻐요. 그리고 조지프, 이이가 늦지 않게 해 주세요.\"\n\n\"우리 남편들을 못된 길로 끌어들이지 마세요, 세들리 씨, 이 못되고 못된 양반 같으니.\" 레베카가 말하며, 더없이 단정한 프랑스 새끼염소 가죽 장갑을 낀 예쁜 새끼손가락을 조스를 향해 까딱거렸다. \"당구도 안 돼요, 담배도 안 되고, 어떤 못된 짓도 안 돼요!\"\n\n\"친애하는 크롤리 부인—아, 이거 참! 명예를 걸고 맹세컨대!\" 조스가 답이랍시고 더듬거린 말은 그것이 전부였다. 그러나 그는 그럭저럭 봐줄 만한 자세를 취하는 데에는 성공했으니, 머리를 한쪽 어깨에 기댄 채 자기 희생자를 향해 위로 히죽거리고, 한 손은 등 뒤로 돌려 지팡이에 기대고, 다른 한 손(다이아몬드 반지를 낀 손)은 셔츠 주름 장식과 속조끼들 사이를 만지작거렸다. 마차가 떠나가자 그는 그 다이아몬드 손에 입 맞춰 안에 있는 어여쁜 숙녀들에게 키스를 날렸다. 그는 첼튼엄 사람들 모두가, 차우링기 사람들 모두가, 캘커타 사람들 모두가, 그런 미인에게 손을 흔들며, 게다가 근위대의 로던 크롤리 같은 이름난 멋쟁이와 어울려 있는 그 모습을 봐 주었으면 싶었다.\n\n우리의 젊은 신랑 신부는 결혼 후 첫 며칠을 보낼 곳으로 브라이턴을 골랐다. 그들은 십 인 여관에 방을 잡고, 조스가 곧 합류하기 전까지 그곳에서 더없이 편안하고 조용하게 지냈다. 게다가 거기서 만난 길동무가 조스 하나만은 아니었다. 어느 날 오후 그들이 바닷가 산책을 마치고 호텔로 들어오는데, 마주친 이가 다름 아닌 레베카와 그녀의 남편이었던 것이다. 둘은 곧바로 서로를 알아보았다. 레베카는 그토록 사랑하는 벗의 품으로 날아들었다. 크롤리와 오즈본도 제법 다정하게 악수를 나누었다. 그리고 베키는 불과 몇 시간 만에, 전에 둘 사이에 오갔던 그 자그마한 불쾌한 말다툼을 오즈본이 잊게 만들 방도를 찾아냈다. \"지난번 크롤리 양 댁에서 우리가 만났을 때, 제가 그토록 무례하게 굴었던 거 기억하세요, 친애하는 오즈본 대위님? 그때 전 대위님이 사랑스러운 애밀리아에게 무심한 것 같다고 생각했어요. 그래서 화가 났고, 그래서 그렇게 건방지게, 그렇게 매정하게, 그렇게 배은망덕하게 굴었던 거예요. 부디 용서해 주세요!\" 레베카가 이렇게 말하며, 어찌나 솔직하고 마음을 끄는 우아함으로 손을 내밀던지, 오즈본은 그 손을 잡지 않을 수 없었다. 겸손하고 솔직하게 자기 잘못을 인정하면, 그것이 어떤 좋은 결과를 가져올지 모르는 법이다, 내 아들아. 나는 일찍이 허영의 시장에서 아주 능란한 술수가요 신사인 어떤 이를 알았는데, 그는 일부러 이웃들에게 자잘한 잘못을 저질러 놓고, 나중에 사내답게 떳떳이 사과하곤 했다. 그래서 결과가 어떻게 되었느냐? 내 벗 크로키 도일은 어딜 가나 사랑을 받았고, 다소 다혈질이긴 해도 더없이 정직한 사내라는 평을 들었던 것이다. 베키의 겸손은 조지 오즈본에게는 진심으로 통했다.\n\n이 두 젊은 부부에게는 서로에게 들려줄 이야기가 넘쳐 났다. 양쪽의 결혼이 화제에 올랐고, 인생에서의 전망도 양편 다 더없는 솔직함과 흥미로 헤아려졌다. 조지의 결혼은 그의 친구 도빈 대위가 그 아버지에게 알리기로 되어 있었는데, 젊은 오즈본은 그 통보의 결과를 두고 다소 떨고 있었다. 로던의 모든 희망이 걸려 있는 크롤리 양은 여전히 마음을 풀지 않고 있었다. 파크 레인의 그 집에 발도 들이지 못하게 된 다정한 조카 부부는 그녀를 따라 브라이턴까지 왔고, 그곳에서 끊임없이 그녀의 집 문 앞에 밀사를 심어 두고 있었다.\n\n\"우리 집 문간을 늘 어슬렁거리는 로던의 친구들을 좀 보여 드리고 싶네요.\" 레베카가 웃으며 말했다. \"빚 독촉꾼을 본 적 있으세요, 자기? 아니면 집행관이랑 그 수하 말이에요. 지난주 내내 그 흉측한 작자들 둘이 길 건너 채소 가게에서 망을 보는 통에, 우린 일요일이 되어서야 겨우 나올 수 있었다니까요. 고모님이 마음을 풀지 않으시면, 우린 어쩌면 좋죠?\"\n\n로던은 껄껄 웃음을 터뜨리며, 자기 빚쟁이들과 그들을 능숙하게 다루는 레베카의 수완에 관한 재미난 일화를 열두 가지나 늘어놓았다. 그는 거창한 맹세까지 곁들이며, 그녀처럼 채권자를 구워삶을 줄 아는 여자는 유럽 천지에 없다고 단언했다. 결혼하고 거의 곧바로 그녀의 솜씨가 발휘되기 시작했고, 남편은 그런 아내의 어마어마한 가치를 알게 되었다. 그들은 외상은 넉넉했으나 청구서 또한 차고 넘쳤고, 현금이 모자라 쪼들렸다. 그런데 이런 빚 문제가 로던의 좋은 기분을 망쳤을까? 천만에. 허영의 시장에 사는 누구나 보았을 테지만, 편안하고 철저하게 빚에 잠긴 이들이 얼마나 잘 사는지, 얼마나 무엇 하나 마다하지 않는지, 마음이 얼마나 유쾌하고 태평한지 모를 일이다. 로던과 그의 아내는 브라이턴 여관에서 가장 좋은 방을 차지하고 있었다. 여관 주인은 첫 요리를 내올 때 마치 자기 최고의 단골에게 하듯 그들 앞에서 머리를 조아렸고, 로던은 이 나라 어떤 귀족도 능가하지 못할 대담함으로 음식과 와인에 트집을 잡았다. 오랜 관록과 사내다운 풍채, 흠잡을 데 없는 부츠와 옷차림, 그리고 행복에 겨운 그 거만한 태도란 종종 은행에 거액의 잔고가 있는 것 못지않게 사람을 도와주는 법이다.\n\n두 결혼식 패거리는 끊임없이 서로의 방을 드나들며 만났다. 두세 밤이 지나자 신사들은 저녁이면 피케 놀이를 조금씩 하게 되었고, 그동안 그들의 아내들은 따로 앉아 수다를 떨었다. 이 소일거리에, 그리고 으리으리한 무개 마차를 타고 나타나 크롤리 대위와 당구를 몇 판 친 조스 세들리의 등장까지 더해져, 로던의 지갑은 다소 두둑해졌고, 아무리 호기로운 이라 해도 때로는 그것이 없어 옴짝달싹 못 하는 그 현금의 덕을 그도 누리게 되었다.\n\n그렇게 세 신사는 라이트닝호 마차가 들어오는 것을 보러 걸어 내려갔다. 일분도 어김없이, 안팎으로 손님을 빼곡히 태우고, 차장이 늘 부는 곡조를 나팔로 불어 대며, 라이트닝호가 거리를 질주해 내려와 역마차 사무소 앞에 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"이런! 저기 도빈 친구가 있네.\" 조지가 옛 친구가 지붕 위에 올라앉아 있는 것을 보고 무척 반가워하며 외쳤다. 도빈이 브라이턴에 오마던 약속은 이제껏 미뤄져 오던 터였다. \"잘 지냈나, 이 친구야? 와 줘서 고맙네. 에미가 자네를 보면 무척 기뻐할 거야.\" 오즈본이 친구가 마차에서 내려서자마자 그 손을 따뜻하게 움켜잡으며 말했다. 그러고는 한층 낮고 동요한 목소리로 덧붙였다. \"무슨 소식이라도 있나? 러셀 광장에 다녀왔어? 아버지가 뭐라셔? 죄다 얘기해 봐.\"\n\n도빈은 몹시 핼쑥하고 심각한 얼굴이었다. \"자네 아버지를 뵙고 왔네.\" 그가 말했다. \"애밀리아는—조지 부인은 어떤가? 소식은 이따 다 얘기하지. 하지만 무엇보다 큰 소식을 가져왔어. 그건 바로—\"\n\n\"어서 말해 보게, 이 친구야.\" 조지가 말했다.\n\n\"우리 벨기에로 출동 명령이 떨어졌어. 군 전체가 다 가네. 근위대까지 몽땅. 헤비톱은 통풍에 걸려서 움직이지도 못하게 됐다고 펄펄 뛰고 있어. 오다우드가 지휘를 맡고, 우린 다음 주에 채텀에서 승선한다네.\" 이 전쟁 소식은 우리 연인들에게 충격을 안기지 않을 수 없었고, 이 신사들 모두를 자못 심각한 표정으로 만들었다.\n\n우정이 지닌 그 은밀한 최면술이란 대체 무엇인가? 평소 굼뜨거나 차갑거나 소심한 사람이, 그 작용을 받으면 남을 위해 슬기롭고 민첩하고 단호해지니 말이다. 알렉시스가 엘리엇슨 박사의 손짓 몇 번에 통증을 멸시하고 뒤통수로 글을 읽으며 몇 마일 밖을 내다보고 다음 주를 꿰뚫어 보며, 평소 멀쩡한 제 상태에서는 도저히 해낼 수 없는 온갖 신기를 부리는 것처럼, 세상사에 있어서도 우정이라는 자력에 끌리면 겸손한 사람이 대담해지고, 수줍은 사람이 자신만만해지고, 게으른 사람이 부지런해지고, 성급한 사람이 신중하고 평온해지는 것을 우리는 볼 수 있다. 반면에 변호사로 하여금 자기 사건을 회피하고 학식 깊은 동료를 자문으로 불러들이게 만드는 것은 무엇인가? 또 의사가 병이 나면 굳이 경쟁자를 부르고, 정작 벽난로 거울 앞에 앉아 제 혀를 들여다보거나 서재 책상에서 제 처방전을 쓰지 않게 만드는 것은 무엇인가? 나는 이런 의문들을 던져 영민한 독자들이 답하도록 맡기련다. 우리가 얼마나 잘 속으면서도 얼마나 회의적이며, 얼마나 무르면서도 얼마나 고집스럽고, 남을 위해서는 얼마나 굳건하면서도 자신에 대해서는 얼마나 자신 없는지를 단번에 아는 그분들에게 말이다. 어쨌든 분명한 것은, 우리의 벗 윌리엄 도빈으로 말하자면 본디 그 성정이 어찌나 순응적이었던지, 만약 부모가 다그쳤더라면 필시 부엌으로 내려가 요리사와 결혼했을 법한 위인이요, 제 잇속을 챙기는 일이라면 길 하나 건너는 것조차 도저히 넘기 어려운 난관으로 여겼을 사람이건만, 막상 조지 오스본의 일을 처리하는 데에는 가장 이기적인 책략가가 제 잇속을 좇을 때만큼이나 분주하고 열성적이었다는 사실이다.\n\n우리의 벗 조지와 그의 젊은 아내가 브라이튼에서 신혼의 발그레한 첫 나날을 즐기는 동안, 정직한 윌리엄은 런던에 남아 조지의 전권 대사로서 결혼에 따르는 온갖 사무를 도맡아 처리했다. 그가 맡은 임무는 늙은 세들리 부부를 찾아뵙고 노인의 기분을 풀어 드리는 일, 조스와 그 매부를 더욱 가까이 묶어 놓아, 보글리 월라의 징세관이라는 조스의 지위와 위신이 그 아버지의 신분 추락을 메우고 늙은 오스본이 이 혼사를 받아들이도록 이끄는 일, 그리고 마지막으로 늙은 신사의 노여움을 가장 덜 사는 방식으로 그에게 소식을 전하는 일이었다.\n\n이제 마땅히 알려야 할 소식을 들고 오스본 가의 가장을 마주하기에 앞서, 도빈은 나머지 가족과 친분을 맺어 두는 것이 지혜로우며, 가능하다면 그 집 숙녀들을 제 편으로 끌어들이는 것이 상책이라는 생각이 들었다. 마음속으로야 화를 낼 리 없지, 하고 그는 생각했다. 낭만적인 결혼에 진심으로 화를 내는 여자란 일찍이 없었다. 조금 소리 높여 떠들고 나면 결국 오라비 편을 들게 마련이고, 그러면 우리 셋이서 늙은 오스본 씨를 포위 공격할 수 있겠지. 그리하여 이 마키아벨리 같은 보병 대위는 오스본 아가씨들에게 오라비의 비밀을 부드럽고도 차근차근 알려 줄 어떤 묘책이나 책략은 없을까 하고 궁리에 궁리를 거듭했다.\n\n어머니인 부인의 약속들을 조금 캐물어 본 끝에, 그는 그 계절에 부인의 친구들 가운데 누가 모임을 여는지, 어디 가면 오스본의 누이들을 만날 수 있을지를 머잖아 알아낼 수 있었다. 그리고 분별 있는 사내들이 흔히—아아!—품기 마련인 그 떠들썩한 사교 모임과 야회에 대한 혐오를 그도 지니고 있었으나, 이내 오스본 아가씨들이 참석할 모임 하나를 찾아냈다. 무도회에 모습을 드러낸 그는 두 아가씨와 두어 차례 짝을 지어 춤을 추었고 더없이 정중하게 처신했다. 그러더니 정말로 용기를 내어, 오스본 양에게 다음 날 이른 시각에 몇 분만 이야기를 나눌 수 있겠느냐고, 더없이 중대한 소식을 전할 것이 있노라고 청하기까지 한 것이다.\n\n그녀가 흠칫 뒤로 물러나 잠시 그를 응시하다가 이내 제 발치 바닥을 내려다보고, 마치 그의 팔에 쓰러져 기절할 것처럼 굴게 만든 것은 대체 무엇이었을까? 만약 그가 때맞춰 그녀의 발가락을 밟아 이 젊은 숙녀를 정신 차리게 해 주지 않았더라면 말이다. 어째서 그녀는 도빈의 청에 그토록 격렬하게 동요했을까? 이는 결코 알 수 없는 노릇이다. 어쨌든 그가 이튿날 찾아왔을 때, 마리아는 언니와 함께 응접실에 있지 않았고, 워트 양이 그녀를 데리러 자리를 떴기에 대위와 오스본 양만 남게 되었다. 둘 다 어찌나 말이 없던지, 벽난로 위에 놓인 '이피게네이아의 희생' 시계의 째깍거리는 소리가 무례할 정도로 또렷하게 들려왔다.\n\n\"어젯밤 모임은 참 즐거웠어요.\" 마침내 오스본 양이 격려하듯 말문을 열었다. \"그리고… 그리고 도빈 대위님은 춤 솜씨가 정말 느셨더군요. 분명 누군가가 가르쳐 드린 거예요.\" 그녀가 상냥한 짓궂음을 담아 덧붙였다.\n\n\"우리 부대 오다우드 소령 부인하고 릴을 추는 걸 한번 보셔야 하는 건데요. 그리고 지그도요. 지그 춤 보신 적 있으세요? 하지만 이렇게 춤을 잘 추시는 오스본 양하고라면 누구든 잘 출 수 있을 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"그 소령 부인은 젊고 아름다운가요, 대위님?\" 어여쁜 질문자가 말을 이었다. \"아, 군인의 아내가 된다는 건 얼마나 끔찍한 일일까요! 어떻게 춤출 기운이 나는지 모르겠어요, 게다가 이런 무시무시한 전쟁의 시국에 말이에요! 오, 도빈 대위님, 저는 우리 가장 소중한 조지와 가엾은 군인들이 처한 위험을 떠올리면 이따금 몸이 떨려요. ××연대에는 결혼한 장교가 많은가요, 도빈 대위님?\"\n\n\"이런, 패를 너무 대놓고 까는구나.\" 워트 양은 생각했다. 그러나 이 논평은 그저 곁다리로 흘린 말이었을 뿐, 가정교사가 문틈에 대고 중얼거린 것이라 그 사이로 들리지는 않았다.\n\n\"우리 부대 젊은이 하나가 막 결혼했답니다.\" 이제 본론으로 들어서며 도빈이 말했다. \"아주 오래된 인연이었지요. 그런데 그 젊은 부부는 교회 생쥐처럼 가난하답니다.\" \"오, 정말 멋져요! 오, 정말 낭만적이에요!\" 대위가 \"오래된 인연\"이며 \"가난한\"이라는 말을 하자 오스본 양이 외쳤다. 그녀의 공감에 그는 용기를 얻었다.\n\n\"연대에서 가장 훌륭한 젊은이지요.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"군대를 통틀어도 그보다 용감하거나 잘생긴 장교는 없을 겁니다. 게다가 그 아내가 어찌나 사랑스러운지요! 얼마나 그 사람을 좋아하시게 될지! 알게 되시면 분명 좋아하실 거예요, 오스본 양.\" 젊은 숙녀는 드디어 결정적인 순간이 왔다고 여겼다. 그리고 이제 막 도빈에게 밀려든 그 긴장, 그의 얼굴이 여러 번 씰룩거리는 데서, 그 커다란 발로 바닥을 구르는 모양새에서, 프록코트 단추를 잽싸게 채웠다 풀었다 하는 데서 역력히 드러나던 그 긴장—요컨대 오스본 양은, 그가 한숨 돌리고 나면 마음속을 죄다 털어놓으리라 여기고 열심히 귀 기울일 채비를 했다. 그리고 이피게네이아가 놓인 그 제단 안의 시계가, 한 차례 예비 경련을 거친 뒤 열두 시를 알리기 시작했는데, 그 종소리가 어찌나 길게 늘어지던지 이 애타는 노처녀에게는 마치 한 시까지 이어질 것만 같았다.\n\n\"하지만 제가 말씀드리려고 온 건 그 결혼에 관한 게 아니라—그러니까 그 결혼이—그게—아니, 제 말은—친애하는 오스본 양, 우리의 소중한 벗 조지에 관한 거예요.\" 도빈이 말했다.\n\n\"조지에 관해서요?\" 그녀가 어찌나 낙담한 어조로 말하던지, 문 저편에서 마리아와 워트 양이 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 저 파렴치한 도빈마저 절로 미소가 나오려 했다. 사실 그도 사태의 정황을 아주 모르는 바는 아니었던 것이다. 조지가 종종 그를 점잖게 놀리며 이렇게 말하곤 했으니 말이다. \"에이, 윌, 자네 늙은 제인이나 데려가지 그래? 청하면 분명 받아 줄 텐데. 받아 준다는 데 오 대 이로 걸지.\"\n\n\"네, 그러니까 조지에 관한 거예요.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"그 친구와 오스본 씨 사이에 불화가 생겼답니다. 그런데 저는 그 친구를 무척이나 아끼는 터라—아시다시피 우리는 형제나 다름없었으니까요—이 다툼이 부디 가라앉기를 바라고 또 기도하는 마음입니다. 우리는 외국으로 떠나야 합니다, 오스본 양. 하루 전 통보만으로 출정 명령이 떨어질지도 몰라요. 전투에서 무슨 일이 일어날지 누가 알겠습니까? 동요하지 마세요, 친애하는 오스본 양. 그래도 적어도 그 두 분만은 벗으로서 헤어져야 하지 않겠습니까.\"\n\n\"다툼은 없었어요, 도빈 대위님. 아버지하고 늘 있던 그저 사소한 한바탕 소동이 있었을 뿐이에요.\" 숙녀가 말했다. \"우리는 날마다 조지가 돌아오기를 기다리고 있어요. 아버지가 바라셨던 건 오직 그 애를 위한 것이었을 뿐이에요. 그저 돌아오기만 하면, 다 잘될 거라고 확신해요. 그리고 여기서 몹시도, 몹시도 화가 나서 떠나간 그 다정한 로다도, 분명 그 애를 용서할 거예요. 여자는 너무도 쉽게 용서하는 법이니까요, 대위님.\"\n\n\"오스본 양 같은 천사라면 분명 그러시겠지요.\" 도빈 씨가 무시무시한 능청을 떨며 말했다. \"여자에게 아픔을 준 사내라면 결코 스스로를 용서할 수 없는 법이고요. 만약 어떤 사내가 오스본 양께 신의를 저버린다면 기분이 어떠시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"저는 죽어 버릴 거예요—창밖으로 몸을 던질 거예요—독약을 마실 거예요—애태우다 시들어 죽을 거예요. 분명 그럴 거예요.\" 아가씨가 외쳤다. 그렇지만 그녀는 그동안 한두 차례 연애를 겪으면서도 자살이라고는 추호도 떠올린 적이 없는 터였다.\n\n\"그런데 다른 여인들 중에도 있답니다.\" 도빈이 말을 이었다. \"오스본 양만큼이나 진실하고 마음 고운 여인이 말이에요. 저는 그 서인도 제도의 상속녀를 두고 하는 말이 아니에요, 오스본 양. 조지가 한때 사랑했던, 그리고 어릴 적부터 오직 그 사람만을 생각하도록 자라난 가엾은 처녀를 두고 하는 말입니다. 저는 그 처녀가 가난 속에서도 불평 없이, 가슴이 미어지면서도 아무런 흠 없이 지내는 것을 보았습니다. 제가 말하는 건 세들리 양입니다. 친애하는 오스본 양, 오라버니가 그녀에게 신의를 지킨다고 해서 그 너그러운 마음으로 오라버니를 탓하실 수 있겠습니까? 만약 그녀를 버린다면 오라버니가 제 양심에 비추어 스스로를 용서할 수 있겠습니까? 부디 그녀의 벗이 되어 주세요—그녀는 늘 오스본 양을 사랑했으니까요—그리고—그리고 저는 조지의 부탁을 받고 이 자리에 왔습니다. 그가 그녀와의 약혼을 자신이 짊어진 가장 신성한 의무로 여기고 있음을 전하고, 적어도 오스본 양만은 그의 편이 되어 달라고 간청하기 위해서요.\"\n\n도빈 씨는 어떤 강렬한 감정에 사로잡히면, 처음 한두 마디를 머뭇거린 뒤에는 완벽하게 막힘없이 말할 수 있었다. 그리고 이번 경우 그의 웅변이 그가 말을 건넨 숙녀에게 어느 정도 감명을 주었음이 역력했다.\n\n\"글쎄요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이건—정말 놀라운 일이군요—정말 가슴 아프고—정말 기이한 일이에요—아버지가 뭐라고 하실까요?—조지가 그 애한테 주어졌던 그토록 훌륭한 혼처를 내던지다니, 하지만 어쨌든 그 애는 대위님이라는 아주 용감한 옹호자를 얻은 셈이군요, 도빈 대위님. 하지만 다 소용없는 일이에요.\" 그녀가 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 말을 이었다. \"저는 가엾은 세들리 양이 정말 안쓰러워요—정말로 진심으로요, 아시다시피. 우리는 그 혼사를 한 번도 좋게 여긴 적이 없었어요. 여기서는 늘 그녀에게 무척 친절하게 대했지만요—정말 무척요. 하지만 아버지는 절대 허락하지 않으실 거예요, 분명히요. 그리고 잘 자란 처녀라면, 아시다시피—절제된 마음을 지닌 처녀라면—조지는 그녀를 단념해야 해요, 친애하는 도빈 대위님, 정말로 그래야만 해요.\"\n\n\"한 사내가 사랑하는 여인을, 하필 그 여인에게 불운이 닥친 바로 그 순간에 단념해야 마땅합니까?\" 도빈이 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"친애하는 오스본 양, 제가 지금 오스본 양께 듣고 있는 충고가 이런 것입니까? 친애하는 젊은 숙녀시여! 오스본 양은 반드시 그녀의 벗이 되어 주셔야 합니다. 그는 그녀를 단념할 수 없어요. 단념해서도 안 됩니다. 한번 생각해 보세요, 만약 오스본 양이 가난해진다면 어떤 사내가 오스본 양을 단념하겠습니까?\"\n\n이 능란한 질문은 제인 오스본 양의 마음을 적잖이 건드렸다. \"우리 같은 가난한 처녀들이 대위님 같은 사내들의 말을 믿어야 하는 건지 모르겠어요, 대위님.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"여자의 다정함에는 너무도 쉽게 믿어 버리게 만드는 무언가가 있지요. 아무래도 대위님들은 잔인하고도 잔인한 사기꾼들 같아요.\" 그러는데 도빈은 분명, 오스본 양이 자신에게 내밀었던 그 손이 슬며시 죄어 오는 것을 느꼈다고 여겼다.\n\n그는 다소 놀라 그 손을 놓아 버렸다. \"사기꾼이라니요!\" 그가 말했다. \"아니에요, 친애하는 오스본 양, 모든 사내가 그렇진 않습니다. 오스본 양의 오라버니는 그렇지 않아요. 조지는 어린 시절부터 줄곧 애밀리아 세들리를 사랑해 왔어요. 그 어떤 재산으로도 그녀가 아닌 다른 여자와 결혼하게 만들 수는 없을 겁니다. 그가 그녀를 저버려야 마땅합니까? 오스본 양은 그에게 그러라고 권하시겠습니까?\"\n\n이런 질문에, 그것도 자기만의 독특한 견해를 지닌 제인 오스본 양이 무어라 답할 수 있었겠는가? 그녀는 그에 답할 수 없었기에 이렇게 받아넘겼다. \"글쎄요, 대위님이 사기꾼이 아니라면, 적어도 무척 낭만적인 분이긴 하시군요.\" 그리고 윌리엄 대위는 이 논평을 굳이 따지지 않고 그냥 넘겼다.\n\n마침내 그가 한층 더 정중한 말들을 거든 끝에, 오스본 양이 이제 모든 소식을 받아들일 만큼 충분히 채비가 되었다고 여겨졌을 때, 그는 그것을 그녀의 귀에 쏟아부었다. \"조지는 애밀리아를 단념할 수 없었습니다—조지는 이미 그녀와 결혼했답니다\"—그러고는 우리가 이미 아는 그 결혼의 정황을 들려주었다. 그 가엾은 처녀가 연인이 신의를 지키지 않았더라면 죽고 말았으리라는 것, 늙은 세들리가 이 혼사에 일절 동의를 거부했으나 결혼 허가증을 얻었다는 것, 조스 세들리가 신부를 인도하러 첼튼엄에서 올라왔다는 것, 그들이 조스의 사두마차를 타고 신혼을 보내러 브라이튼으로 떠났다는 것, 그리고 조지가, 그토록 진실하고 다정한 여인들이라면 분명 그래 주리라 믿으며, 사랑하는 다정한 누이들이 아버지 앞에서 자기 편이 되어 주기를 기대하고 있다는 것까지 죄다 이야기했다. 그러고는 다시 한번 만나 뵙기를 청하여(흔쾌히 허락받았다), 또한 자신이 가져온 소식이 앞으로 오 분 안에 다른 숙녀들에게도 전해지리라 정확히 짐작하면서, 도빈 대위는 고개를 숙여 인사하고 작별을 고했다.\n\n그가 채 집을 벗어나기도 전에, 마리아 양과 워트 양이 오스본 양에게 달려들었고, 그 경이로운 비밀의 전모가 그녀의 입을 통해 두 사람에게 전해졌다. 공평하게 말하자면, 두 자매 중 누구도 그리 언짢아하지는 않았다. 달아나서 한 결혼이란 어지간한 숙녀라면 진심으로 화내기 어려운 무언가가 있는 법이고, 애밀리아는 그 결혼에 동의하면서 보여 준 기개 덕분에 오히려 그들의 평가에서 한 단계 올라간 셈이었다. 그들이 그 이야기를 두고 갑론을박하고, 재잘거리고, 아버지가 어떻게 하고 무어라 하실지 궁금해하고 있는데, 마치 복수의 천둥소리 같은 요란한 노크 소리가 문에서 울려 이 음모자들을 화들짝 놀라게 했다. 아버지가 분명해, 하고 그들은 생각했다. 그러나 그는 아니었다. 그저 약속대로 숙녀들을 화훼 전시회에 데려가려고 시티에서 온 프레더릭 불록 씨일 뿐이었다.\n\n짐작하다시피, 이 신사 역시 그 비밀을 오래 모르고 있지는 않았다. 그러나 그것을 들었을 때 그의 얼굴에 떠오른 것은, 자매들의 얼굴에 어렸던 그 감상적인 경이의 빛과는 사뭇 다른 경악이었다. 불록 씨는 세상 물정에 밝은 사내요, 어느 부유한 상회의 젊은 동업자였다. 그는 돈이 무엇인지, 그 값어치가 얼마인지 알았다. 그래서 흐뭇한 기대감의 전율이 그 작은 두 눈에 불을 밝혔고, 조지 씨의 이 어리석은 짓 덕분에 마리아가 자신이 일찍이 그녀와 함께 얻기를 바랐던 것보다 삼만 파운드는 더 값나가는 신붓감이 될지도 모른다는 생각에, 그는 자기 마리아를 향해 미소 짓게 되었다.\n\n\"이런! 제인.\" 그가 손위 누이까지도 제법 흥미롭게 뜯어보며 말했다. \"일스는 발을 뺀 걸 후회하겠는걸요. 당신은 어쩌면 오만 파운드짜리가 될지도 모르겠어요.\"\n\n자매는 그 순간까지 돈 문제는 생각조차 해 본 적이 없었으나, 프레드 불록은 오전 나들이 내내 우아하고 명랑하게 그것을 두고 둘을 놀려 댔다. 그리하여 오전의 여흥이 끝나고 만찬을 들러 마차를 몰아 돌아올 즈음에는, 두 사람의 자존감이 적잖이 높아져 있었다. 그러니 나의 존경하는 독자께서는 이 이기심을 부자연스럽다 하여 부디 나무라지 마시기를. 바로 오늘 아침, 이 글을 적는 자가 리치먼드에서 합승마차를 타고 오는 길에, 말을 바꾸어 매는 동안 마차 지붕 위에 앉아 있던 나는, 아래쪽 웅덩이에서 노는 어린아이 셋을 눈여겨보았다. 잔뜩 더럽고, 사이좋고, 행복한 아이들이었다. 그 셋에게 이내 또 다른 꼬마 하나가 다가왔다. \"폴리.\" 그 아이가 말했다. \"너네 언니가 일 페니 생겼대.\" 그러자 아이들은 즉각 웅덩이에서 일어나, 페기에게 알랑거리러 달려가는 것이었다. 그리고 합승마차가 떠날 무렵, 나는 페기가 그 어린 행렬을 꽁무니에 거느린 채, 더없이 위엄 있게 이웃 사탕 장수 아낙의 좌판을 향해 행진해 가는 모습을 보았다.\n\n그리하여 자매들을 미리 대비시켜 둔 다음, 도빈은 자신이 떠맡은 임무 가운데 나머지이자 더 까다로운 부분을 처리하기 위해 서둘러 시내로 향했다. 늙은 오스본을 마주할 생각을 하니 적잖이 마음이 졸아들어서, 그는 한 번 이상이나 젊은 숙녀들에게 비밀을 전하는 일을 맡겨 버릴까 하고 생각했다. 그가 알기로 그들은 그 비밀을 오래 간직하지 못할 것이었기 때문이다. 그러나 그는 큰오스본이 그 소식을 어떻게 받아들이는지 조지에게 보고하기로 약속한 터였다. 그래서 시내에 있는 템스 거리의 부친 회계 사무소로 들어가서, 그곳에서 오스본 씨에게 아들 조지의 일과 관련하여 삼십 분만 이야기를 나누었으면 한다는 쪽지를 띄웠다. 도빈의 심부름꾼은 오스본 씨의 사업소에서 돌아왔는데, 그 신사가 대위를 즉시 만나면 더없이 기쁘겠다는 인사를 전해 왔다. 그리하여 도빈은 그를 마주하러 길을 나섰다.\n\n대위는 절반쯤 죄책감이 서린 비밀을 털어놓아야 하는 데다, 앞으로 고통스럽고 험악한 면담이 펼쳐질 것을 내다보면서, 더없이 침울한 낯빛과 주눅 든 걸음걸이로 오스본 씨의 사무실에 들어섰다. 그러고는 초퍼 씨가 자리를 지키는 바깥방을 지나는데, 그 사무원이 책상에서 익살스러운 기색으로 그를 맞이하니 그는 한층 더 면구스러워졌다. 초퍼 씨는 눈을 찡긋하고 고개를 끄덕이더니, 펜으로 자기 주인의 방문을 가리키며 더없이 약 올리는 듯한 쾌활한 어투로 말했다. \"주인 나리는 아주 흡족해하고 계실 겁니다요.\"\n\n오스본 역시 일어나 진심으로 그의 손을 잡고 흔들며 말했다. \"잘 지냈는가, 이 사람아?\" 그 살가운 인사에 가엾은 조지의 사절은 곱절로 죄스러운 기분이 들었다. 도빈의 손은 그 노신사의 손아귀 안에서 마치 죽은 듯이 늘어져 있었다. 그는 자신이, 다시 말해 도빈 자신이 어느 정도는 벌어진 모든 일의 원인이라고 느꼈다. 조지를 애밀리아에게 다시 데려간 것도 그였고, 이제 조지의 아버지에게 밝히러 온 그 결혼을 갈채하고 부추기고 거의 직접 성사시키다시피 한 것도 그였다. 그런데 그 아버지는 환영의 미소로 그를 맞이하며, 어깨를 토닥이고 \"도빈, 이 사람아\"라고 부르고 있었다. 그 사절이 고개를 떨굴 만한 충분한 이유가 있었던 것이다.\n\n오스본은 도빈이 아들의 항복을 알리러 온 것이라고 철석같이 믿고 있었다. 도빈의 심부름꾼이 막 도착한 바로 그 순간에, 초퍼 씨와 그 주인은 조지와 그 아버지 사이의 문제를 두고 이야기를 나누던 참이었다. 두 사람은 조지가 굴복을 청해 왔다는 데 의견을 같이했다. 둘 다 며칠 전부터 그것을 기다리고 있었던 것이다. \"이런, 초퍼, 우리가 얼마나 굉장한 혼사를 치르게 될지!\" 오스본 씨는 사무원에게 그렇게 말하며, 굵직한 손가락을 딱 소리 나게 튕기고는, 큼직한 주머니 속의 기니와 실링을 짤랑짤랑 흔들며 의기양양한 눈길로 부하를 바라보았다.\n\n양쪽 주머니에서 똑같은 동작을 해 가며, 다 안다는 듯 유쾌한 기색으로, 오스본은 의자에 앉은 채 맞은편에 멍하니 입을 다물고 앉아 있는 도빈을 바라보았다. '군대 대위라는 자가 어찌 저리 촌뜨기 같담.' 늙은 오스본은 생각했다. '조지가 어째서 저자한테 좀 더 나은 예법을 가르치지 않았을꼬.'\n\n마침내 도빈이 용기를 그러모아 입을 열었다. \"어르신,\" 그가 말했다. \"아주 엄중한 소식을 가져왔습니다. 오늘 아침 호스 가즈에 다녀왔는데, 우리 연대가 해외로 파병 명령을 받게 될 것이 분명합니다. 이번 주가 가기 전에 벨기에로 향할 겁니다. 그리고 어르신께서도 아시다시피, 우리 가운데 많은 이에게 치명적일 수 있는 한바탕 격전을 치르기 전에는 다시 고국으로 돌아오지 못할 것입니다.\" 오스본의 낯빛이 굳어졌다. \"내 아들은, 아니, 연대는 제 본분을 다할 걸세, 그건 틀림없겠지.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"프랑스군은 아주 강력합니다, 어르신.\" 도빈이 말을 이었다. \"러시아군과 오스트리아군이 병력을 끌어내리기까지는 오랜 시간이 걸릴 겁니다. 첫 전투는 우리 몫이 될 겁니다, 어르신. 그리고 보니가 그 싸움을 호되게 만들 것은 분명합니다.\"\n\n\"대체 무슨 말을 하려는 건가, 도빈?\" 그의 상대가 불안한 기색으로 인상을 찌푸리며 말했다. \"설마 영국 사람 가운데 빌어먹을 프랑스 놈을 두려워할 자가 있다는 건 아니겠지, 응?\"\n\n\"제 말씀은 그저, 우리가 떠나기 전에, 그리고 우리 한 사람 한 사람에게 닥쳐 있는 그 크고 분명한 위험을 생각할 때, 만일 어르신과 조지 사이에 어떤 불화가 있다면, 두 분이 화해의 악수를 나누시는 편이 좋지 않겠습니까? 만에 하나 그에게 무슨 일이라도 생긴다면, 너그러이 작별하지 못한 것을 어르신께서 평생 자책하시게 될 것 같습니다.\"\n\n그렇게 말하는 동안, 가엾은 윌리엄 도빈은 시뻘겋게 얼굴을 붉히며, 자기 자신이야말로 배신자라고 느끼고 또 그것을 인정했다. 그가 아니었더라면 어쩌면 이런 단절은 일어날 필요조차 없었을지 모른다. 어째서 조지의 결혼을 미루지 않았던가? 무엇 때문에 그토록 열렬하게 그것을 밀어붙였단 말인가? 조지였더라면 어떻게든 죽을 만큼 괴로워하는 일 없이 애밀리아와 헤어졌으리라는 것을 그는 느꼈다. 애밀리아 역시 그를 잃은 충격에서 회복했을지도 모른다. 이 결혼과 거기서 비롯될 모든 일을 불러온 것은 그의 조언이었다. 그런데 어째서였던가? 그가 그녀를 너무도 사랑한 나머지 그녀가 불행해하는 꼴을 차마 볼 수 없었기 때문이거나, 아니면 마음 졸이며 견디는 자신의 고통이 너무도 참기 힘들어서 단번에 그것을 짓눌러 버리는 편이 차라리 반가웠기 때문일 것이다. 마치 우리가 죽음 뒤에 장례를 서둘러 치르듯, 혹은 사랑하는 이들과의 이별이 임박했을 때 그 작별이 끝나기 전에는 마음을 놓지 못하듯이 말이다.\n\n\"자네는 좋은 사람일세, 윌리엄.\" 오스본 씨가 누그러진 목소리로 말했다. \"나와 조지가 노여움을 품고 헤어져서야 안 될 일이지, 그건 옳은 말이야. 이보게. 나는 그 아이를 위해 어느 아비 못지않게 해 주었네. 그 아이는 내게서, 분명 자네 아버지가 자네한테 준 것의 세 곱절은 되는 돈을 받았어. 하지만 그걸 자랑하려는 건 아니야. 내가 그 아이를 위해 얼마나 애쓰고, 일하고, 재능과 기운을 쏟아부었는지는 말하지 않겠네. 초퍼한테 물어보게. 그 아이한테 물어봐. 런던 시내 사람들한테 물어보라고. 자, 나는 그 아이한테 이 나라 어느 귀족이라도 자랑스러워할 만한 혼처를 마련해 주었네. 내가 평생 그 아이한테 청한 단 한 가지가 그것이었는데, 그 아이가 나를 거절한 거야. 내가 잘못인가? 이 다툼이 내가 만든 것인가? 그 아이가 태어난 이래로 죄수처럼 고되게 일해 온 게 다 그 아이를 잘되게 하려는 것 말고 무엇이었나? 나한테 사사로운 욕심이 있다고는 아무도 말 못 해. 그 아이더러 돌아오라고 하게. 자, 여기 내 손이 있네. 잊고 용서하겠다 이 말이야. 지금 당장 결혼하는 건 어림없는 일이지만 말일세. 그 아이하고 S 양이 화해하고, 나중에 그 아이가 대령이 되어 돌아오면 그때 혼사를 치르게 하면 돼. 맹세코 그 아이는 대령이 될 거야, 돈으로 될 일이라면 반드시 그렇게 만들지. 자네가 그 아이를 돌려놓아 주어서 기쁘네. 자네 솜씨라는 걸 내가 알아, 도빈. 전에도 자네가 그 아이를 여러 곤경에서 빼내 주었지. 그 아이더러 오라고 하게. 나도 모질게 굴지 않겠네. 자, 오늘 러셀 광장에 와서 함께 저녁이나 들지, 자네들 둘 다 말이야. 늘 가던 그 집에서, 늘 그 시각에. 사슴 목살 고기가 있을 테니, 아무것도 캐묻지 않고 말일세.\"\n\n이 칭찬과 신뢰가 도빈의 마음을 몹시도 매섭게 후벼 팠다. 대화가 이런 어조로 이어지는 매 순간, 그는 점점 더 죄스러워졌다. \"어르신,\" 그가 말했다. \"어르신께서 스스로를 속이고 계신 게 아닌가 합니다. 분명 그러십니다. 조지는 돈을 보고 결혼할 만큼 그릇이 작은 사람이 결코 아닙니다. 어르신께서 말을 듣지 않으면 상속에서 제하겠다고 으름장을 놓으셔 봐야, 그 아이로서는 저항으로 맞설 뿐일 겁니다.\"\n\n\"이런, 빌어먹을, 이 사람아, 그 아이한테 연간 팔구천 파운드를 주겠다는 게 으름장이란 말인가?\" 오스본 씨가 여전히 약 올리는 듯한 쾌활한 어투로 말했다. \"원, S 양이 나를 받아 준다면야 나라도 그 사람이 되겠네. 살결이 좀 거무튀튀하다 한들 나는 까다롭게 굴지 않아.\" 그러고는 노신사는 다 안다는 듯한 능글맞은 웃음과 상스러운 너털웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"어르신께서는 조지 대위가 이미 맺어 놓은 약속을 잊고 계십니다.\" 사절이 엄숙하게 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 약속? 대체 무슨 소리를 하는 거야? 설마,\" 오스본 씨가 이제야 그 생각이 처음 떠오르는 듯 노여움과 경악을 한껏 끌어올리며 말을 이었다. \"설마 그 아이가 그 사기꾼 같은 늙은 파산자 딸한테 아직도 미련을 두는, 그런 망할 바보 천치란 말은 아니겠지? 자네 설마 그 아이가 그 계집애와 결혼하고 싶어 한다고 내가 믿게 하려고 여기 온 건 아니겠지? 그 계집애와 결혼이라니, 그것참 가관이로군. 내 아들이자 상속자가 시궁창 출신 거지 계집애와 결혼을 해? 빌어먹을, 정 그렇게 한다면 빗자루나 사서 길거리나 쓸라고 하게. 이제 와 생각해 보니 그 계집애가 늘 그 아이한테 들러붙어 추파를 던지더군. 그 늙은 사기꾼 아비가 시킨 게 분명해.\"\n\n\"세들리 씨는 어르신의 더없이 절친한 벗이었습니다, 어르신.\" 도빈이 끼어들었는데, 자기 안에서 노여움이 솟구치는 것을 깨닫자 거의 흐뭇할 지경이었다. \"한때 어르신께서는 그분을 악당이니 사기꾼이니 하는 것보다 더 좋은 이름으로 부르셨지요. 그 혼사는 어르신께서 만드신 것입니다. 조지는 함부로 변덕을 부릴 권리가 없었—\"\n\n\"변덕이라니!\" 늙은 오스본이 고래고래 소리쳤다. \"함부로 변덕이라니! 이런, 빌어먹을, 그건 바로 우리 도련님께서 두 주 전 목요일에 거드름을 피우면서 직접 입에 담은 말이 아닌가. 자기를 만들어 준 아비한테 영국 군대 운운하면서 말이야. 뭐, 그 아이를 그렇게 부추긴 게 바로 자네란 말이지? 정말 고맙구먼, 대위 나리. 우리 집안에 거지들을 들이려는 게 바로 자네였어. 천만에, 사양하네, 대위. 그 계집애와 결혼이라니, 원, 히히! 어째서 그래야 하지? 장담컨대 그 계집애는 결혼하지 않고도 그 아이한테 냉큼 달려갈 텐데.\"\n\n\"어르신,\" 도빈이 노여움을 감추지 않고 벌떡 일어서며 말했다. \"그 누구도 제가 듣는 데서 그 숙녀를 욕보일 수는 없습니다. 더구나 어르신은 더더욱 안 됩니다.\"\n\n\"오, 그래서 나한테 결투라도 신청하겠다 이건가? 잠깐, 두 사람 몫의 권총을 가져오라고 종을 울려야겠군. 조지 도련님이 제 아비를 모욕하라고 자네를 여기 보낸 모양이지?\" 오스본이 종 줄을 잡아당기며 말했다.\n\n\"오스본 어르신,\" 도빈이 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"이 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 사람을 모욕하고 계신 건 바로 어르신이십니다. 그 사람을 너그러이 봐주시는 게 좋을 겁니다, 어르신. 그 사람은 어르신 아드님의 아내니까요.\"\n\n그러고는 더는 할 말이 없다고 느낀 도빈은 자리를 떠났고, 오스본은 의자에 풀썩 주저앉아 그의 뒷모습을 망연히 바라보았다. 종소리에 따라 사무원이 들어왔다. 그런데 대위가 오스본 씨의 사무실이 있는 안마당을 미처 빠져나오기도 전에, 수석 사무원 초퍼 씨가 모자도 쓰지 않은 채 그를 뒤쫓아 부랴부랴 달려 나왔다.\n\n\"제발, 대체 무슨 일입니까?\" 초퍼 씨가 대위의 외투 자락을 붙잡으며 말했다. \"주인 나리가 발작을 일으키셨습니다. 조지 도련님이 무슨 일을 저지른 겁니까?\"\n\n\"닷새 전에 세들리 양과 결혼했소.\" 도빈이 대답했다. \"내가 그 신랑 들러리였소, 초퍼 씨. 그러니 당신이 그의 편이 되어 주어야 하오.\"\n\n늙은 사무원은 고개를 가로저었다. \"그게 전하시려는 소식이라면, 대위님, 그건 나쁜 소식입니다. 주인 나리는 결코 도련님을 용서하지 않으실 겁니다.\"\n\n도빈은 초퍼에게 자신이 묵고 있는 호텔로 진행 상황을 알려 달라고 청한 뒤, 과거와 미래에 대한 생각으로 몹시 심란한 채 침울하게 서쪽으로 발걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n그날 저녁 러셀 광장의 가족이 저녁 식사 자리에 모였을 때, 그들은 가장이 늘 앉던 자리에 앉아 있는 것을 보았다. 다만 그 얼굴에는 침울한 기색이 서려 있었는데, 그것이 떠오를 때면 언제나 온 식구가 입을 다물게 마련이었다. 숙녀들과 함께 식사하던 불럭 씨는, 그 소식이 오스본 씨에게 전해졌음을 직감했다. 그의 어두운 낯빛은 불럭 씨까지 잠잠하고 조용하게 만들 만큼 영향을 미쳤다. 그러나 그는 옆에 앉은 마리아 양과, 식탁 상석을 맡은 그 언니에게는 평소답지 않게 상냥하고 살뜰하게 굴었다.\n\n그러다 보니 워트 양은 자기 쪽 식탁에 혼자 덩그러니 앉게 되었고, 그녀와 제인 오스본 양 사이에는 빈자리가 하나 남았다. 그런데 이 자리는 조지가 집에서 식사할 때 앉던 자리였다. 그리고 앞서 말했듯, 그 가출한 아들이 돌아오리라는 기대 속에 그를 위한 식기까지 차려져 있었다. 식사 시간 내내, 미소 짓는 프레더릭 씨의 점점 힘 빠지는 은밀한 속삭임과 접시며 도자기가 부딪치는 달그락 소리 말고는, 그 식사의 침묵을 깨뜨리는 것이라곤 아무것도 없었다. 하인들은 살금살금 돌아다니며 제 일을 했다. 장례식의 곡꾼이라도 오스본 씨네 하인들보다 더 침울해 보이지는 못할 것이었다. 그가 도빈에게 함께 들자고 청했던 그 사슴 목살 고기를 그는 더없이 침묵한 채로 손수 썰어 나누었다. 그러나 그의 몫은 거의 입에도 대지 않은 채 물려졌다. 술은 많이 마셨고, 집사는 부지런히 그의 잔을 채워 댔지만 말이다.\n\n마침내 식사가 막 끝나갈 무렵, 차례로 모든 이를 노려보던 그의 두 눈이 한동안 조지를 위해 차려진 식기 위에 멎었다. 이윽고 그는 왼손으로 그것을 가리켰다. 딸들은 그를 바라보았으나 그 신호를 알아차리지 못했다. 아니면 알아차리고 싶지 않았던 것이리라. 하인들도 처음에는 그 뜻을 이해하지 못했다.\n\n\"저 식기를 치워라.\" 마침내 그가 욕설을 내뱉으며 일어나 말했다. 그러면서 의자를 뒤로 밀치고는 자기 방으로 걸어 들어갔다.\n\n오스본 씨의 식당 뒤편에는, 그 집에서 서재라는 이름으로 통하는 으레 있는 방이 하나 있었는데, 그 집 주인만의 신성한 공간이었다. 오스본 씨는 일요일 오전에 교회에 갈 마음이 내키지 않을 때면 이곳으로 물러나, 진홍빛 가죽 의자에 앉아 신문을 읽으며 오전을 보내곤 했다. 이곳에는 유리문이 달린 책장 두 개가 있었는데, 묵직한 금박 장정의 표준 저작들이 담겨 있었다. 「연감」, 「젠틀맨스 매거진」, 「블레어의 설교집」, 그리고 「흄과 스몰릿의 영국사」 같은 것들이었다. 그는 한 해가 다 가도록 이 책들 가운데 단 한 권도 선반에서 꺼내는 법이 없었다. 그러나 식구들 가운데 누구도 목숨을 걸고 그 책 한 권에 손댈 엄두를 내지 못했다. 단, 만찬 손님이 없는 그 드문 일요일 저녁만은 예외였다. 그런 날이면, 「귀족 명감」 옆 구석에 꽂혀 있던 그 큼직한 진홍빛 성서와 기도서를 꺼내고, 하인들을 식당으로 불러 모은 뒤, 오스본이 크고 거슬리는 거드름 가득한 목소리로 식구들에게 저녁 기도를 읽어 주는 것이었다. 식구든, 아이든, 하인이든 그 누구도 일말의 두려움 없이는 그 방에 들어서지 못했다. 그는 이곳에서 가정부의 장부를 점검했고, 집사의 술 창고 장부를 샅샅이 살폈다. 또한 이곳에서는 말끔하게 자갈을 깐 안마당 너머로, 그의 종 하나와 연결되어 있는 마구간 뒷문을 한눈에 굽어볼 수 있었다. 마부는 그 안마당으로, 마치 피고석에 들어서듯 자기 처소에서 나왔고, 오스본은 서재 창문에서 그에게 욕설을 퍼붓곤 했다. 일 년에 네 번, 워트 양은 봉급을 받으러 이 방에 들어왔고, 딸들은 분기별 용돈을 받으러 들어왔다. 조지는 어릴 적 이 방에서 여러 번 채찍질을 당했고, 그 어머니는 계단에 앓는 듯 앉아 채찍이 가르는 소리에 귀를 기울이곤 했다. 그 아이가 그 벌을 받으면서 우는 일은 좀처럼 없었다. 가엾은 그 여인은 아이가 나오면 몰래 어루만지고 입 맞추며, 달래느라 돈을 쥐여 주곤 했다.\n\n벽난로 선반 위에는 가족 초상화가 한 점 걸려 있었는데, 오스본 부인이 죽은 뒤 앞방에서 이리로 옮겨 온 것이었다. 조지는 조랑말을 타고 있고, 큰언니가 그에게 꽃다발을 들어 올려 주고 있으며, 작은딸은 어머니의 손에 이끌리고 있었다. 모두 발그레한 볼과 큼직하고 붉은 입을 하고서, 으레 가족 초상화에서 그러듯 서로를 향해 야릇하게 미소 짓고 있었다. 이제 어머니는 땅속에 묻혀 오래전에 잊혔고, 자매와 형제는 저마다 백 가지 다른 관심사를 좇으며, 여전히 가까운 사이이면서도 서로에게서 완전히 멀어져 있었다. 그로부터 몇십 년이 지나, 거기 그려진 모든 이가 늙어 버렸을 때, 저 으스대는 어린 시절 가족 초상화들이란 얼마나 쓰디쓴 풍자란 말인가. 그 감상의 익살과 미소 짓는 거짓, 그리고 그토록 자의식 강하고 자족적인 천진함이라니. 오스본 자신의 위풍당당한 초상화는, 그 큼직한 은 잉크병과 안락의자가 함께 그려진 채로, 가족 초상화가 비운 자리, 곧 식당의 영예로운 자리를 차지하게 되었다.\n\n그리하여 늙은 오스본은 이 서재로 물러갔고, 뒤에 남겨진 그 자그마한 식구들은 크게 안도했다. 하인들이 물러가자, 그들은 잠시 동안 말은 많되 아주 나직하게 이야기를 나누었다. 그러고는 조용히 위층으로 올라갔는데, 불럭 씨도 삐걱대는 구두를 신은 채 살금살금 그들을 뒤따랐다. 그는 가까이에 있는 서재 안의 그 무서운 노신사 곁에서, 혼자 앉아 술을 마실 엄두가 나지 않았던 것이다.\n\n날이 어두워지고 적어도 한 시간이 지나도록 아무런 부름이 없자, 집사는 큰맘 먹고 그 방문을 두드려 밀랍 양초와 차를 들여놓았다. 집주인은 의자에 앉아 신문을 읽는 척하고 있었는데, 하인이 등불과 다과를 그 옆 탁자에 놓고 물러가자, 오스본 씨는 일어나 그 뒤로 문을 잠갔다. 이번에는 사정을 잘못 짚을 여지가 없었다. 온 식구가, 조지 도련님에게 끔찍한 영향을 미칠 법한 어떤 큰 재앙이 닥쳐오고 있음을 알아차렸다.\n\n번쩍이는 큼직한 마호가니 사무용 책상 안에는, 오스본 씨가 특별히 아들의 일과 서류만을 위해 마련해 둔 서랍이 하나 있었다. 그는 아들이 어린아이였을 적부터 그와 관련된 모든 서류를 이곳에 보관해 왔다. 이곳에는 조지의 필체와 선생의 필체가 함께 담긴 상장이며 습자 공책이며 그림 공책이 있었고, 또박또박한 큰 글씨로 아빠와 엄마에게 사랑을 전하고 케이크를 사 달라고 조르던 그의 첫 편지들도 있었다. 그 편지들에는 그의 사랑하는 대부 세들리가 한 번 이상 언급되어 있었다. 그 서류들을 들춰 보다가 그 이름을 마주치자, 늙은 오스본의 핏기 가신 입술에 저주가 떨리며 새어 나왔고, 끔찍한 증오와 실망이 그 가슴속에서 꿈틀거렸다. 그것들은 하나하나 표시되고 정리되어 붉은 끈으로 묶여 있었다. \"조지가 5실링을 청함, 18××년 4월 23일, 4월 25일에 답함\"이라거나 \"조지가 조랑말에 관하여, 10월 13일\" 따위로 적혀 있었다. 또 다른 묶음에는 \"S 박사의 청구서\", \"조지의 양복점 청구서 및 의복비, 주니어 조지 오스본이 본인에게 발행한 어음\" 등이 들어 있었다. 서인도제도에서 온 그의 편지들, 그의 대리인이 보낸 편지들, 그리고 그의 임관 소식이 실린 신문들도 있었다. 이곳에는 그가 어릴 적 쓰던 채찍 한 자루도 있었고, 종이 속에는 그의 어머니가 늘 지니고 다니던, 그의 머리카락이 담긴 작은 로켓도 있었다.\n\n하나씩 차례로 뒤집어 보며 이 추억의 물건들을 곱씹는 사이, 이 불행한 사내는 여러 시간을 흘려보냈다. 그의 가장 소중한 허영과 야심 찬 희망이 모두 이곳에 있었던 것이다. 그는 자기 아들을 얼마나 자랑스러워했던가! 그 아이는 일찍이 본 적 없을 만큼 잘생긴 아이였다. 다들 그 아이가 귀족의 아들 같다고 했다. 왕가의 어느 공주가 큐 정원에서 그 아이를 눈여겨보고는 입을 맞추며 이름을 물어보기까지 했다. 시내의 어느 사업가가 이만한 아들을 또 내보일 수 있겠는가? 왕자라 한들 이보다 더 잘 보살핌을 받을 수 있었겠는가? 돈으로 살 수 있는 것이라면 무엇이든 그의 아들 차지였다. 그는 학예 발표회 날이면 말 네 필을 거느리고 새 제복을 입혀 내려가, 조지가 다니던 학교의 소년들에게 새 실링 동전을 뿌리곤 했다. 아이가 캐나다로 떠나기 전, 그가 조지를 데리고 연대 주둔지로 갔을 때는, 요크 공작이라도 자리에 앉을 만한 만찬을 장교들에게 대접했다. 조지가 어음을 발행했을 때 그가 한 번이라도 그것을 거절한 적이 있었던가? 그 어음들이 여기 있었다. 군말 한마디 없이 지불된 채로. 군대의 숱한 장군들도 그가 가진 말들을 타지 못했을 것이다! 그는 백 가지 다른 날들의 모습으로 그 아이를 눈앞에 떠올렸다. 저녁 식사 뒤 조지를 떠올릴 때면, 식탁 상석 아버지 곁에서 귀족처럼 당당하게 들어와 잔을 단숨에 비우던 모습이며, 브라이턴에서 조랑말을 타고 산울타리를 훌쩍 뛰어넘어 사냥꾼들과 나란히 내달리던 모습이며, 궁중 알현식에서 섭정 왕세자를 알현하던 그날, 온 세인트제임스를 통틀어도 그보다 더 멋진 젊은이를 내놓을 수 없었던 그 모습이 떠올랐다. 그런데 이것이, 이것이 그 모든 것의 끝이라니! 파산자의 딸과 결혼하여 본분과 행운을 정면으로 거스르다니! 이 늙은 속물이 이제 견뎌야 하는 것은 그 얼마나 큰 굴욕과 격분이었던가. 속이 메스꺼울 만큼 들끓는 분노와, 좌절된 야심과 사랑의 고통과, 짓밟힌 허영과 심지어 부정(父情)에 입은 상처는 또 어떠했던가!\n\n이 서류들을 살펴보고, 비참한 사내가 행복했던 지난날을 떠올릴 때 으레 겪는 그 어떤 무력한 비애보다도 쓰라린 심정으로 이것저것을 곱씹은 뒤, 조지의 아버지는 그토록 오래 간직해 온 그 서랍에서 서류 일체를 꺼내, 필기함 안에 넣고 끈으로 묶은 다음 자신의 인장으로 봉인했다. 그러고는 책장을 열어, 앞서 말한 그 큼직한 붉은 성서를 꺼냈다. 좀처럼 펼쳐 보는 일 없는, 겉면이 온통 금빛으로 번쩍이는 거창한 책이었다. 그 책의 권두 삽화에는 아브라함이 이삭을 제물로 바치는 장면이 그려져 있었다. 이곳, 관례에 따라 오스본은 그 면지에 자기 특유의 사무원 같은 큰 글씨로 자신의 결혼 날짜와 아내의 사망 날짜, 그리고 자녀들의 출생일과 세례명을 적어 두었다. 제인이 맨 처음이고, 그다음이 조지 세들리 오스본, 그다음이 마리아 프랜시스였으며, 저마다의 세례일도 함께 적혀 있었다. 그는 펜을 들어, 조지의 이름을 그 면지에서 정성스레 지워 버렸다. 그리고 그 면지가 완전히 마르자, 책을 옮겨 두었던 자리에 도로 꽂아 두었다. 그러고는 자신의 사사로운 서류들을 보관하는 또 다른 서랍에서 문서 한 장을 꺼내, 그것을 읽은 뒤 구겨서 촛불 하나에 불을 붙여, 벽난로 안에서 그것이 완전히 타 없어지는 것을 지켜보았다. 그것은 그의 유언장이었다. 그것을 태워 버린 뒤, 그는 자리에 앉아 편지 한 통을 써 내려갔고, 하인을 불러 아침에 그것을 전하라고 일렀다. 이미 아침이 밝아 있었다. 그가 잠자리에 들러 올라갈 무렵, 온 집안은 햇살로 환했고, 새들은 러셀 광장의 싱그러운 푸른 잎새 사이에서 지저귀고 있었다.\n\n오스본 씨의 온 식구와 부하들의 비위를 맞추고, 역경에 처한 조지를 위해 되도록 많은 벗을 만들어 두고 싶었던 윌리엄 도빈은, 좋은 만찬과 좋은 술이 사람의 영혼에 미치는 효과를 잘 알고 있었던 터라, 자기 숙소로 돌아오자마자 즉시 토머스 초퍼 귀하에게 더없이 후한 초대장을 써 보냈다. 이튿날 슬로터스 식당에서 함께 저녁을 들자고 그 신사에게 청하는 내용이었다. 그 쪽지는 초퍼 씨가 시내를 떠나기 전에 그에게 닿았고, 즉각 돌아온 답은 \"초퍼 씨가 정중히 인사를 올리며, D 대위님을 모시는 영광과 기쁨을 누리겠습니다\"라는 것이었다. 그날 저녁 서머스 타운으로 돌아온 그는 초퍼 부인과 딸들에게 그 초대장과 답장 초안을 보여 주었고, 식구들이 둘러앉아 차를 마시는 동안 그들은 군인 양반들이며 웨스트엔드 신사들에 관한 이야기를 한껏 들떠 나누었다. 딸들이 잠자리에 들고 나자, 초퍼 부부는 주인 나리네 집안에서 벌어지고 있는 기이한 사건들에 관해 이야기를 주고받았다. 사무원은 자기 주인이 그토록 격앙된 모습을 일찍이 본 적이 없었다. 도빈 대위가 떠난 뒤 오스본 씨에게 들어갔을 때, 초퍼 씨는 자기 상관의 얼굴이 시퍼렇게 질려 거의 발작을 일으킬 지경인 것을 보았다. 오스본 씨와 젊은 대위 사이에 어떤 끔찍한 다툼이 벌어졌음이 분명하다고 그는 확신했다. 초퍼는 지난 삼 년 동안 오스본 대위에게 지급된 금액 일체의 명세서를 작성하라는 지시를 받았다. \"게다가 어지간히도 많은 돈을 받았더란 말입니다.\" 수석 사무원은 그렇게 말하면서, 그토록 후하게 기니를 뿌려 댄 것을 두고 그 늙은 주인과 젊은 주인을 한층 더 존경했다. 그 다툼은 세들리 양에 관한 무언가였다. 초퍼 부인은 그 대위처럼 잘생긴 젊은이를 놓치게 된 그 가엾은 아가씨가 딱하다며 입에 침이 마르도록 말했다. 변변찮은 배당금을 지불한 불운한 투기꾼의 딸이라는 점에서, 초퍼 씨는 세들리 양에게 그다지 큰 호감이 없었다. 그는 런던 시내의 그 무엇보다도 오스본 집안을 존경했다. 그래서 그의 바람이자 소망은 조지 대위가 귀족의 딸과 결혼하는 것이었다. 그날 밤 사무원은 그 주인보다 훨씬 더 깊이 잠들었다. 그리고 아침 식사를 마친 뒤(비록 그의 검소한 인생의 잔은 흑설탕으로만 달큼해질 뿐이었지만, 그는 아주 왕성한 식욕으로 그것을 들었다) 아이들을 끌어안고는, 그날 저녁 D 대위의 포도주를 너무 가혹하게 축내지는 않겠다고 흠모하는 아내에게 약속하며, 가장 좋은 나들이옷에 주름 잡힌 셔츠 차림으로 일하러 길을 나섰다.\n\n오스본 씨가 평소 시각에 시내에 도착했을 때, 그 얼굴은—나름의 이유로 그 표정을 살피는 데 익숙한 부하들의 눈에—유난히 송장 같고 초췌해 보였다. 열두 시에 히그스 씨(베드퍼드 거리의 변호사 사무소 히그스 앤드 블래더윅의 일원이었다)가 약속대로 찾아와, 주인의 개인 방으로 안내되어 한 시간 넘게 그 안에 틀어박혀 있었다. 한 시쯤 초퍼 씨는 도빈 대위의 하인이 가져온 쪽지를 받았는데, 그 안에는 오스본 씨에게 전할 봉투가 동봉되어 있어 사무원이 들어가 그것을 전달했다. 얼마 지나지 않아 초퍼 씨와 그다음 사무원인 버치 씨가 불려 들어가, 문서 한 장에 증인으로 서명해 달라는 요청을 받았다. \"새 유언장을 작성했네.\" 오스본 씨가 말했고, 이에 두 사람은 각자 거기에 이름을 적어 넣었다. 아무런 대화도 오가지 않았다. 히그스 씨는 바깥방으로 나오면서 몹시 엄숙한 표정을 지었고, 초퍼 씨의 얼굴을 아주 뚫어지게 바라보았다. 그러나 아무런 설명도 없었다. 사람들은 오스본 씨가 그날 하루 종일 유난히 조용하고 온화하다는 것을 알아챘는데, 그 어두운 거동을 보고 불길한 일을 점쳤던 이들에게는 놀라운 노릇이었다. 그는 그날 누구에게도 험한 말을 하지 않았고, 욕설을 내뱉는 것도 단 한 번 들리지 않았다. 그는 일찍 일을 파했다. 그리고 자리를 뜨기 전에 수석 사무원을 다시 한번 불러, 전반적인 지시를 내린 뒤, 말을 꺼내기를 망설이고 꺼리는 듯하다가, 도빈 대위가 시내에 있는지 아느냐고 물었다.\n\n초퍼는 그런 것 같다고 대답했다. 사실 두 사람 다 그 사실을 빤히 알고 있었다.\n\n오스본은 그 장교 앞으로 된 편지 한 통을 집어 사무원에게 건네며, 그것을 즉시 도빈 본인의 손에 직접 전하라고 청했다.\n\n\"자, 이제 됐네, 초퍼.\" 그가 모자를 집어 들며, 묘한 표정으로 말했다. \"내 마음이 편안해지겠군.\" 시계가 두 시를 치는 바로 그 순간(둘 사이에 약속이 있었음이 분명했다) 프레더릭 불럭 씨가 찾아왔고, 그와 오스본 씨는 함께 걸어 나갔다.\n\n도빈과 오스본 두 사람이 각각 중대를 맡고 있는 ××연대의 대령은, 퀘벡에서 울프 장군 휘하로 첫 출정을 치른 늙은 장군이었다. 지휘를 맡기에는 이미 오래전에 너무 늙고 노쇠해진 인물이었으나, 그는 자신이 명목상의 수장으로 있는 그 연대에 어느 정도 관심을 두고 있어, 젊은 장교 몇몇을 자기 식탁에 기꺼이 맞아들이곤 했다. 그런 환대란 요즘 그의 동료들 사이에서는 흔치 않은 일이지 싶다. 도빈 대위는 이 늙은 장군이 특히 아끼는 사람이었다. 도빈은 자기 직업의 문헌에 정통해서, 위대한 프리드리히와 여제이자 왕비였던 분, 그리고 그들의 전쟁에 관하여 장군 본인 못지않게 능숙하게 이야기를 나눌 수 있었다. 장군은 오늘날의 승리 따위에는 무심했고, 그 마음은 오십 년 전의 전술가들에게 가 있었다. 이 장교는 오스본 씨가 유언장을 고치고 초퍼 씨가 가장 좋은 셔츠 주름을 차려입던 그날 아침, 도빈에게 함께 아침 식사를 들자고 청했고, 그러고는 그 젊은 총신에게 모두가 예상하고 있던 일을 이틀 앞서 알려 주었다. 바로 벨기에로 진군하라는 명령이었다. 연대더러 대기 태세를 갖추라는 명령이 하루 이틀 안에 호스 가즈에서 떨어질 것이고, 수송선이 넉넉하니 이번 주가 가기 전에 진군로를 받게 되리라는 것이었다. 채텀에 연대가 머무는 동안 신병들이 들어와 있었다. 늙은 장군은, 캐나다에서 몽칼름을 무찌르고 롱아일랜드에서 워싱턴 씨를 패주시키는 데 한몫했던 그 연대가, 숱하게 짓밟힌 저지대의 전장에서 그 역사적 명성에 부끄럽지 않은 모습을 보여 주기를 바랐다. \"그러니, 이보게, 만약 자네한테 무슨 볼일이라도 있다면,\" 늙은 장군은 떨리는 희고 늙은 손으로 코담배를 한 줌 집으며, 이어서 아직 가냘프게 뛰고 있는 심장이 자리한 실내복 자락을 가리키며 말했다. \"달래 줄 처녀라도 있다거나, 아버지 어머니께 작별 인사를 드린다거나, 작성해 둘 유언이라도 있다면, 지체 없이 그 일에 착수하기를 권하네.\" 그러면서 장군은 젊은 벗에게 손가락 하나를 내밀어 흔들게 하고는, 분칠하고 변발한 머리를 사람 좋게 끄덕여 주었다. 그리고 도빈을 향해 문이 닫히자, 자리에 앉아 국립 극장의 마드무아젤 아메나이드에게 연애편지(그는 자신의 프랑스어 솜씨를 몹시도 우쭐해했다)를 끄적이기 시작했다.\n\n이 소식은 도빈을 침울하게 만들었고, 그는 브라이턴에 있는 우리 벗들을 떠올렸다. 그러다가 그는 자신의 생각 속에서 늘 애밀리아가 가장 먼저라는 것이(늘 누구보다도 먼저였다. 아버지와 어머니, 누이들과 본분보다도 먼저였고, 실은 잠에서 깰 때나 잠들 때나, 또 온종일 그러했다) 부끄러워졌다. 그래서 호텔로 돌아온 그는 오스본 씨에게 짤막한 쪽지를 띄워, 자신이 들은 소식을 알렸다. 그것이 조지와의 화해를 한층 더 이끌어 내는 데 보탬이 되기를 바라면서 말이다.\n\n전날 초퍼에게 초대장을 전했던 바로 그 심부름꾼 편에 보내진 이 쪽지는, 그 어진 사무원을 적잖이 놀라게 했다. 그 쪽지는 그 앞으로 봉해져 있었는데, 그는 편지를 뜯으면서 자기가 잔뜩 기대하고 있던 그 만찬이 미뤄지는 것은 아닐까 하여 벌벌 떨었다. 봉투 안에 든 것이 그저 그 자신을 위한 알림에 지나지 않음을 알았을 때, 그의 마음은 이루 말할 수 없이 놓였다. (\"다섯 시 반에 기다리겠소\"라고 도빈 대위는 적어 두었다.) 그는 자기 고용주의 집안일에 무척 관심이 많았으나, 어쩌겠는가? 거창한 만찬이 그에게는 다른 어떤 사람의 일보다도 더 큰 관심사였던 것이다.\n\n도빈은 자신이 여기저기 돌아다니는 길에 만나는 연대 장교라면 누구에게든 장군의 소식을 전해도 무방한 처지였다. 그리하여 그는 대리인의 사무소에서 마주친 스터블 소위에게 그것을 알렸는데, 군인다운 열정이 어찌나 대단하던지 그 소위는 곧장 군장 제작자에게 새 검을 사러 달려갔다. 이 젊은이는, 비록 겨우 열일곱 살에 키도 예순다섯 인치 남짓이고, 타고나기를 곱사등이 기질에 때 이른 브랜디와 물 탓에 몸이 많이 상해 있었지만, 의심할 바 없는 용기와 사자의 심장을 지니고 있었다. 이곳에서 그는 프랑스 놈들 사이에서 한몫 단단히 해낼 만하다 싶은 무기 하나를, 손에 쥐고 가늠하고 휘둘러 보고 굽혀 보고 무게를 재 보았다. \"하, 하!\" 하고 외치며 작은 발을 어마어마한 기세로 굴러 대고는, 도빈 대위를 향해 두세 번 찌르기 동작을 날렸는데, 대위는 웃으며 자신의 대나무 지팡이로 그 찌르기를 막아 냈다.\n\n스터블 씨는, 그 체구와 호리호리함으로 짐작할 수 있듯 경보병 소속이었다. 반면 스푸니 소위는 키가 큰 젊은이로 (도빈 대위가 속한) 척탄병 중대 소속이었는데, 새로 받은 곰가죽 모자를 써 보니 나이답지 않게 사납게 보였다. 그러고는 이 두 젊은이는 슬로터스 식당으로 가서 근사한 만찬을 주문해 놓고 자리에 앉아, 고국의 다정하고 마음 졸이는 부모에게 편지를 써 내려갔다. 사랑과 진정과 패기, 그리고 엉터리 철자가 가득한 편지였다. 아! 그 무렵 영국 곳곳에서는 마음 졸이는 숱한 가슴이 두근거리고 있었다. 그리고 어머니들의 기도와 눈물이 숱한 가정에서 흘러내리고 있었다.\n\n슬로터스 식당의 다방 탁자 하나에서 어린 스터블이 글짓기에 골몰해 있고, 그 콧등을 타고 눈물이 종이 위로 뚝뚝 떨어지는 것을 보자(그 젊은이는 자기 엄마를, 그리고 어쩌면 다시는 엄마를 보지 못할지도 모른다는 것을 생각하고 있었다), 막 조지 오스본에게 편지를 써 보내려던 도빈은 마음이 누그러져 자기 책상을 도로 닫았다. \"내가 왜 굳이?\" 그는 말했다. \"그녀에게 오늘 밤만은 행복하게 보내게 하자. 내일 아침 일찍 부모님을 찾아뵙고, 직접 브라이턴으로 내려가야겠다.\"\n\n그래서 그는 다가가 어린 스터블의 어깨에 자기 큼직한 손을 얹고는, 그 젊은 용사를 격려해 주었다. 그리고 브랜디와 물을 끊기만 하면, 늘 그래 왔듯 신사답고 마음씨 고운 친구이니 훌륭한 군인이 될 거라고 일러 주었다. 이 말에 어린 스터블의 두 눈이 환해졌다. 도빈은 연대 안에서 가장 훌륭한 장교이자 가장 영리한 사람으로 크게 존경받고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"고맙습니다, 도빈.\" 그가 주먹으로 눈을 비비며 말했다. \"마침 그냥, 그냥 엄마한테 그러겠다고 쓰고 있던 참이에요. 그리고, 아, 대위님, 엄마가 저한테 어찌나 끔찍이도 잘해 주시는지요.\" 물 펌프가 다시 작동하기 시작했고, 마음 여린 대위의 두 눈에도 물기가 어렸던 게 아닐까 싶다.\n\n두 소위와 대위, 그리고 초퍼 씨는 같은 칸막이 자리에서 함께 저녁을 들었다. 초퍼는 오스본 씨가 보낸 편지를 가져왔는데, 거기서 오스본 씨는 도빈 대위에게 짤막하게 인사를 전하며 동봉한 것을 조지 오스본 대위에게 전해 달라고 청하고 있었다. 초퍼는 그 이상은 아무것도 몰랐다. 사실 그는 오스본 씨의 모습이며 변호사와의 면담을 묘사하고, 주인 나리가 어째서 아무한테도 욕을 하지 않았는지 의아해하며, 특히 술이 돌아갈수록 온갖 추측과 억측을 늘어놓았다. 그러나 그 추측은 잔을 거듭할 때마다 점점 더 막연해지더니, 마침내는 도무지 알아들을 수 없는 지경이 되었다. 늦은 시각에 도빈 대위는 자기 손님을, 딸꾹질을 해 대는 상태로 삯마차에 태워 보냈는데, 그는 영원무궁토록 그 끝내주는, 끝내주는 대위님의 벗이 되겠노라고 맹세하고 있었다.\n\n앞서 말했듯, 도빈 대위는 오스본 양에게 작별을 고할 때 다시 한번 찾아뵙겠노라고 청을 넣었고, 그 노처녀는 이튿날 몇 시간이나 그를 기다렸다. 어쩌면 그가 찾아와, 그녀가 답할 준비를 해 두었던 그 질문을 던졌더라면, 그녀는 자신이 오빠의 편이라고 밝혔을 것이고, 그리하여 조지와 노여워하는 그 아버지 사이에 화해가 이루어졌을지도 모른다. 그러나 그녀가 집에서 기다렸음에도 대위는 끝내 오지 않았다. 그에게는 좇아야 할 자기 일이 있었고, 찾아뵙고 위로해 드려야 할 부모가 있었으며, 그날 이른 시각에 라이트닝 마차에 자리를 잡고 브라이턴의 벗들에게로 내려가야 했다. 그날 하루가 지나는 동안 오스본 양은 아버지가, 그 참견 잘하는 악당 도빈 대위를 두 번 다시 자기 집 문 안에 들이지 말라고 명을 내리는 것을 들었고, 그리하여 그녀가 남몰래 품었을지도 모를 어떤 희망은 이렇게 돌연 끝장이 나고 말았다. 프레더릭 불럭 씨가 찾아와, 마리아에게 유난히 다정하게 굴고, 기가 꺾인 노신사에게 살뜰히 마음을 썼다. 비록 마음이 편안해지겠다고 말은 했으나, 그가 평온을 얻고자 취한 방편들은 아직 성공한 것 같지 않았고, 지난 이틀간의 일들은 눈에 띄게 그를 무너뜨려 놓았던 것이다.\n\n# 제3장\n\n쉽 여관에 있는 숙녀들에게로 안내되어 간 도빈은 쾌활하고 수다스러운 태도를 꾸며 보였는데, 이는 이 젊은 장교가 날이 갈수록 더할 나위 없는 위선의 명수가 되어 가고 있음을 입증하는 것이었다. 그는 자신의 사사로운 감정을 감추려 애쓰고 있었으니, 첫째는 새로운 처지에 놓인 조지 오스본 부인을 보았을 때의 감정이었고, 둘째는 자신이 가지고 내려온 음울한 소식이 그녀에게 틀림없이 미칠 영향에 대한 우려를 감추려는 것이었다.\n\n\"내 생각엔 말이야, 조지.\" 그가 말했다. \"프랑스 황제가 삼 주 안에 기병이고 보병이고 죄다 끌고 우리한테 밀고 들어와서, 공작 각하한테 한바탕 호된 춤을 추게 해서 반도 전쟁쯤은 애들 장난처럼 보이게 만들 거라고. 하지만 오스본 부인한테는 그런 얘기 할 필요 없어, 알지. 따지고 보면 우리 쪽에선 전투가 전혀 없을 수도 있고, 벨기에에서 우리가 할 일이 그저 군사 점령에 그칠지도 모르니까. 많은 사람들이 그렇게 생각해. 게다가 브뤼셀에는 멋진 사람들이며 유행을 좇는 숙녀들이 넘쳐 난다고.\" 그리하여 벨기에에서 영국군이 맡은 임무를 애밀리아에게는 이처럼 무해한 빛깔로 그려 보이기로 합의가 이루어졌다.\n\n이렇게 모의가 짜여진 뒤, 위선자 도빈은 조지 오스본 부인에게 사뭇 명랑하게 인사를 건네고, 신부라는 그녀의 새 처지에 관해 한두 마디 찬사를 건네려 애썼는데(고백하건대 그 찬사라는 것이 지독히 어설펐고 비참하리만치 어색하게 머뭇거렸다), 그러고는 브라이턴이며, 바닷바람이며, 그곳의 흥겨운 풍경이며, 길의 아름다움이며, 라이트닝 역마차와 말들의 장점에 대해 떠들기 시작했다. 그 모두가 애밀리아로서는 도무지 알아들을 수 없는 식이었으나, 도빈 대위를 지켜보고 있던 레베카에게는 무척이나 흥미로웠다. 사실 그녀는 자기 곁에 다가오는 사람이라면 누구든 지켜보았던 것이다.\n\n고백하건대 어린 애밀리아는 남편의 친구인 도빈 대위를 다소 낮잡아 보고 있었다. 그는 혀짤배기소리를 냈고, 생김새가 매우 평범하고 볼품없었으며, 몹시 어색하고 굼떴다. 그녀가 그를 좋아한 것은 남편을 향한 그의 충심 때문이었으나(물론 그 점에는 칭찬할 만한 구석이 거의 없었다), 동료 장교에게까지 우정을 베푸는 조지야말로 더없이 너그럽고 친절하다고 그녀는 생각했다. 조지는 도빈의 혀짤배기소리와 괴상한 거동을 그녀 앞에서 여러 번 흉내 내곤 했는데, 그를 위해 한마디 변호하자면, 친구의 훌륭한 자질에 대해서는 늘 더없이 높이 칭송하기는 했다. 자신의 짧은 승리의 나날 속에서, 그리고 아직 그를 친밀하게 알지 못한 까닭에, 그녀는 정직한 윌리엄을 가볍게 여겼다. 그리고 그는 자신에 대한 그녀의 생각을 아주 잘 알면서도 더없이 겸허하게 거기에 따랐다. 훗날 그녀가 그를 더 잘 알게 되어 그에 대한 생각을 바꾸는 때가 오기는 했으나, 그것은 아직 먼 훗날의 일이었다.\n\n레베카로 말할 것 같으면, 도빈 대위가 숙녀들과 함께한 지 두 시간도 채 되기 전에 그녀는 그의 비밀을 완벽하게 꿰뚫어 보았다. 그녀는 그를 좋아하지 않았고 속으로 두려워했다. 그 또한 그녀에게 그다지 호감을 품지 않았다. 그가 어찌나 정직한지 그녀의 술수와 아양이 그에게는 통하지 않았고, 그는 본능적인 거부감으로 그녀에게서 움츠러들었다. 게다가 그녀 역시 시샘을 초월할 만큼 자기 성별을 한참 뛰어넘은 사람은 결코 아니었던지라, 그가 애밀리아를 흠모하는 것을 보고 더욱 그를 싫어했다. 그럼에도 그녀는 그를 대하는 태도에서 매우 깍듯하고 다정했다. 오스본 부부의 친구이지 않은가! 자기의 더없이 소중한 은인들의 친구이지 않은가! 그녀는 언제까지나 그를 진심으로 사랑하겠노라 다짐했다. 그녀는 그를 복스홀에서 보냈던 그날 밤 또렷이 기억한다고 애밀리아에게 짓궂게 말했고, 두 숙녀가 저녁 식사 채비를 하러 들어갔을 때는 그를 두고 조금 우스갯소리를 했다. 로던 크롤리는 도빈에게 거의 눈길도 주지 않았으니, 그를 마음씨 좋은 멍청이요 교양 없는 런던 장사치쯤으로 여겼던 것이다. 조스는 자못 위엄을 부리며 그를 너그러이 대했다.\n\n조지와 도빈이, 조지가 따라 들어간 도빈의 방에 단둘이 있게 되자, 도빈은 오스본 씨에게서 그 아들에게 전하라는 부탁을 받은 편지를 책상에서 꺼냈다. \"아버지 필체가 아닌데.\" 조지가 다소 놀란 기색으로 말했다. 과연 그러했다. 그 편지는 오스본 씨의 변호사가 보낸 것으로, 내용은 다음과 같았다.\n\n\"베드퍼드 로에서, 1815년 5월 7일.\n\n귀하.\n본인은 오스본 씨로부터 위임을 받아 다음을 알려 드립니다. 그분께서는 이전에 귀하에게 표명하신 결심을 그대로 고수하시며, 귀하가 기어이 맺으신 혼인의 결과로 이후로는 귀하를 가족의 일원으로 여기지 않으십니다. 이 결심은 최종적이며 번복할 수 없습니다.\n\n귀하의 미성년기에 귀하에게 지출된 금액과, 근년에 귀하가 그분 앞으로 그토록 아낌없이 발행한 어음들은, 귀하가 응당 자기 몫으로 받을 권리가 있는 금액(작고하신 모친 오스본 부인의 재산 가운데 삼분의 일로, 부인이 별세하시면서 귀하와 제인 오스본 양, 마리아 프랜시스 오스본 양에게 돌아간 것입니다)을 그 액수에서 훨씬 웃돕니다. 그럼에도 본인은 오스본 씨의 지시에 따라, 그분께서 귀하의 상속분에 대한 모든 청구권을 포기하시며, 당일 시세로 환산한 사 푼 이자의 연금 이천 파운드(육천 파운드 가운데 귀하 몫인 삼분의 일에 해당합니다)를, 귀하가 수령증을 제출하시는 대로 귀하 본인 또는 귀하의 대리인에게 지불하리라는 것을 전합니다.\n\n귀하의 충실한 종복, S. 힉스 올림.\n\n추신. 오스본 씨께서는 이번 일이든 다른 어떤 일이든, 귀하로부터 어떠한 전갈이나 편지나 연락도 받기를 거부하신다는 것을 단번에 분명히 밝히라 하셨습니다.\"\n\n\"네가 일을 참 멋들어지게도 처리해 놨구나.\" 조지가 윌리엄 도빈을 사납게 노려보며 말했다. \"이것 봐, 도빈.\" 그러고는 부친의 편지를 그에게 휙 던졌다. \"거지가 됐어, 맙소사, 그게 다 빌어먹을 내 감상벽 때문이라고. 왜 우리가 기다릴 수 없었던 거지? 전쟁 중에 총알 한 방이면 내 목숨이 끝장날 수도 있고, 지금도 여전히 그럴 수 있는데, 에미가 거지의 미망인으로 남는다고 무슨 형편이 나아진단 말이야? 다 네가 한 짓이야. 너는 나를 결혼시키고 파멸시켜 놓기 전엔 도무지 마음을 못 놓았지. 이천 파운드 가지고 대체 나더러 어쩌라는 거냐? 그만한 돈은 이 년도 못 버텨. 여기 내려와서 카드며 당구로 크롤리한테 백사십 파운드나 잃었다고. 정말이지 너야말로 남의 살림을 잘도 꾸려 주는 사람이로구나.\"\n\n\"이 처지가 고된 것이야 부인할 수 없지.\" 멍한 표정으로 편지를 다 읽고 나서 도빈이 대답했다. \"그리고 네 말대로, 절반은 내 탓이고. 하지만 너랑 처지를 바꾸고 싶어 할 사람도 더러 있을걸.\" 그가 씁쓸한 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다. \"연대에서 이천 파운드를 쥐고 있는 대위가 몇이나 될 것 같아? 아버님 노여움이 풀리실 때까진 봉급으로 살아야지. 그리고 만약 네가 죽으면, 아내한테 연 백 파운드는 남겨 줄 수 있고.\"\n\n\"나 같은 생활 습관을 가진 사람이 봉급에 연 백 파운드로 살 수 있을 거라고 생각하는 거냐?\" 조지가 잔뜩 성을 내며 소리쳤다. \"그런 소릴 하다니 넌 바보가 틀림없어, 도빈. 그 쥐꼬리만 한 푼돈으로 대체 내가 세상에서 내 지위를 어떻게 지킨단 말이야? 난 습관을 바꿀 수 없어. 누릴 건 누려야 한다고. 난 맥워터처럼 죽으로 자란 것도 아니고, 늙은 오다우드처럼 감자로 자란 것도 아니야. 내 아내더러 병사들 빨래를 받아서 하거나, 짐마차에 실려 연대를 따라다니라는 거냐?\"\n\n\"자, 자.\" 도빈이 여전히 너그럽게 말했다. \"부인한테는 더 나은 탈것을 마련해 드리자. 다만 너는 이제 폐위된 왕자에 불과하다는 걸 잊지 말려무나, 친구야. 그리고 폭풍이 몰아치는 동안에는 가만히 있어. 오래가진 않을 테니. 네 이름이 관보에 한번 오르기만 하면, 노아버님이 너한테 마음을 푸시리라고 내가 장담하지.\"\n\n\"관보에 오른다고!\" 조지가 대꾸했다. \"그것도 어느 난에 말이야? 전사자며 부상자 명단에, 그것도 십중팔구 맨 윗줄에 오르겠지.\"\n\n\"체! 다치고 나서 비명을 질러도 늦지 않아.\" 도빈이 말했다. \"그리고 무슨 일이 생기면 말이지, 조지, 내가 가진 게 좀 있고, 난 결혼할 사람도 아니니, 유언장에서 내 대자를 잊지 않으마.\" 그가 미소 지으며 덧붙였다. 이로써 다툼은 끝이 났으니, 오스본과 그 친구 사이에서 이런 대화가 수십 번이나 이전에도 그러했듯, 전자가 도빈에게 오래 화를 낼 도리가 없다고 선언하며, 까닭 없이 욕을 퍼붓고는 더없이 너그럽게 그를 용서하는 것으로 매듭지어진 것이다.\n\n\"이봐, 베키.\" 로던 크롤리가 자기 화장방에서 아내를 향해 소리쳤다. 아내는 제 방에서 저녁 식사를 위해 단장하는 중이었다.\n\n\"왜요?\" 베키의 날카로운 목소리가 들렸다. 그녀는 거울 속으로 어깨너머를 살펴보고 있었다. 상상할 수 있는 가장 정갈하고 산뜻한 흰 드레스를 차려입고, 드러난 어깨에 자그마한 목걸이를 두르고, 연푸른 띠를 맨 그녀는 그야말로 젊디젊은 순진무구함과 소녀다운 행복의 화신처럼 보였다.\n\n\"이봐, 오스본 부인은 어쩐대, 오스본이 연대 따라 출정하고 나면?\" 크롤리가 커다란 머리빗 두 개로 자기 머리에 이중주를 연주하면서, 머리카락 사이로 어여쁜 어린 아내를 흠모하듯 내다보며 방으로 들어와 말했다.\n\n\"눈이 빠지도록 울겠죠 뭐.\" 베키가 대답했다. \"벌써 그 생각만으로도 나한테 대여섯 번은 훌쩍거렸는걸요.\"\n\n\"당신은 신경도 안 쓰나 보군, 아마.\" 로던이 아내의 무정함에 반쯤 성이 나서 말했다.\n\n\"이 못된 양반! 내가 당신을 따라갈 작정인 걸 모르세요?\" 베키가 대꾸했다. \"게다가 당신은 경우가 다르잖아요. 당신은 터프토 장군의 부관으로 가는 거고. 우린 전열 부대 소속이 아니잖아요.\" 크롤리 부인이 그렇게 말하며 고개를 한껏 치켜드니, 그 모습에 남편이 어찌나 황홀해졌던지 몸을 숙여 그 머리에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"로던, 여보, 그 돈 말이에요, 큐피드한테서, 그가 떠나기 전에, 받아 두는 게 낫지 않겠어요?\" 베키가 사람 잡는 매듭리본을 매만지며 말을 이었다. 그녀는 조지 오스본을 큐피드라고 불렀다. 그녀는 벌써 스무 번도 넘게 그의 잘생긴 외모를 추켜세웠던 것이다. 그가 밤마다 잠자리에 들기 전 삼십 분쯤 로던의 처소에 들르면, 그녀는 에카르테 놀이를 하는 그를 다정하게 살펴 주곤 했다.\n\n그녀는 그를 끔찍하게 방탕한 못된 사람이라고 곧잘 불렀고, 그의 못된 행실과 짓궂은 낭비벽을 에미에게 일러바치겠다고 으름장을 놓곤 했다. 그녀는 그의 시가를 가져다 불을 붙여 주었다. 그녀는 그 수법의 효력을 알고 있었으니, 지난날 로던 크롤리에게 써 본 적이 있었기 때문이다. 그는 그녀가 명랑하고, 발랄하고, 짓궂고, 기품 있고, 즐거운 사람이라고 생각했다. 함께한 자그마한 드라이브며 만찬에서 베키는 물론 가엾은 에미를 한참 무색하게 만들었다. 크롤리 부인과 그 남편이 함께 떠들썩하게 수다를 떠는 동안 에미는 매우 말이 없고 주눅 든 채로 있었고, 크롤리 대위는(그리고 이 신혼부부와 합류한 뒤로는 조스도) 말없이 게걸스레 먹어 댔다.\n\n에미는 어쩐 일인지 친구에 대해 마음이 자꾸 불안했다. 레베카의 재치며 활기며 재주가 그녀를 서글픈 불안으로 어지럽혔다. 결혼한 지 겨우 일주일인데, 벌써 조지는 권태로워하며 다른 이들과 어울리기를 갈망하고 있지 않은가! 그녀는 앞날이 두려워 떨었다. 어떻게 하면 내가 저이의 길동무가 될 수 있을까, 그녀는 생각했다. 저토록 영리하고 빛나는 사람인데, 나는 이토록 보잘것없고 어리석은 존재이니. 나와 결혼해 주다니, 모든 걸 버리고 내게로 몸을 굽혀 주다니, 저이는 얼마나 고귀한가! 나는 저이를 거절했어야 했어, 다만 그럴 용기가 없었을 뿐. 집에 남아 가엾은 아버지를 돌봤어야 했어. 그리고 부모를 소홀히 한 일이(사실 이 가엾은 아이의 불안한 양심이 스스로에게 들이댄 그 자책에는 어느 정도 근거가 있었다) 이제야 처음으로 떠올라, 그녀는 부끄러움에 얼굴을 붉혔다. 아아! 그녀는 생각했다. 나는 참으로 못되고 이기적이었어. 부모님이 슬픔에 잠겨 계실 때 잊고 있었으니 이기적이었고, 조지더러 억지로 나와 결혼하게 했으니 이기적이었어. 나는 저이에게 걸맞은 사람이 아니야. 나 없이도 저이는 행복했으리란 걸 알아. 그런데도, 그래도, 나는 애썼어, 저이를 단념하려고 애썼어.\n\n결혼한 지 이레도 채 지나기 전에 이런 생각과 고백이 어린 신부의 마음속으로 밀려든다는 것은 가혹한 일이다. 그러나 사정은 그러했다. 그리고 도빈이 이 젊은이들과 합류하러 오기 전날 밤, 오월의 어느 환하게 빛나는 달밤이었다. 어찌나 따스하고 부드러운 밤이었던지 발코니로 난 창문을 활짝 열어 두었고, 거기서 조지와 크롤리 부인이 눈앞에 반짝이며 펼쳐진 고요한 대양을 바라보고 있었으며, 그러는 동안 로던과 조스는 안에서 주사위놀이에 빠져 있었다. 애밀리아는 큼직한 안락의자에 완전히 외면당한 채 파묻혀, 이 두 무리를 모두 지켜보며, 그 여리고 외로운 영혼에게는 쓰디쓴 길동무인 절망과 회한을 느꼈다. 채 일주일도 지나지 않았는데, 일이 이 지경에 이른 것이다! 앞날은, 만약 그녀가 그것을 들여다보았다면, 음울한 전망을 펼쳐 보였을 것이다. 그러나 에미는, 말하자면 너무도 수줍은 나머지 그 앞날을 바라보고 홀로 그 망망대해로 배를 띄울 엄두를 내지 못했고, 길잡이와 보호자 없이 그 바다를 항해할 만한 사람도 못 되었다. 스미스 양이 그녀를 낮잡아 본다는 것을 나는 안다. 그러나 친애하는 부인, 그대처럼 어마어마한 정신력을 타고난 이가 과연 몇이나 되겠는가?\n\n\"이런, 정말 멋진 밤이군, 달도 참 밝고!\" 조지가 시가를 한 모금 빨며 말하니, 연기가 하늘을 향해 솟구쳐 올랐다.\n\n\"탁 트인 데서 피우니 냄새가 어찌나 향긋한지! 난 시가가 너무 좋아요. 달이 이십삼만 육천팔백사십칠 마일이나 떨어져 있다고 누가 생각이나 하겠어요?\" 베키가 그 천체를 미소 띤 얼굴로 올려다보며 덧붙였다. \"내가 그걸 다 기억하다니 영리하지 않아요? 흥! 그것도 다 핑커튼 여사님 학원에서 배운 거예요! 바다가 어쩌면 이렇게 잔잔하고, 모든 게 이리도 또렷할까. 정말이지 프랑스 해안이 거의 다 보일 것 같아!\" 그러면서 그녀의 밝은 초록빛 눈이 흘러넘치듯 어둠 속으로 쏘아져, 마치 어둠을 꿰뚫어 보기라도 할 듯했다.\n\n\"내가 어느 날 아침에 무슨 일을 할 작정인지 아세요?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"내가 수영을 기막히게 잘한다는 걸 알았거든요. 그래서 언젠가, 크롤리 고모님의 말동무가, 그러니까 늙은 브리그스 말이에요, 당신도 기억하죠, 그 매부리코에 머리카락을 길게 늘어뜨린 여자요, 그 브리그스가 멱을 감으러 나오면, 내가 그 차일 밑으로 잠수해 들어가서 물속에서 기어이 화해를 하자고 우길 작정이에요. 멋진 계략 아니에요?\"\n\n조지는 이 물속 만남을 상상하며 폭소를 터뜨렸다. \"거기 둘이서 무슨 소동이야?\" 로던이 주사위통을 달그락거리며 외쳤다. 애밀리아는 어처구니없게도 발작적으로 흐느끼며 바보 같은 꼴을 하고는, 남몰래 훌쩍이려고 제 방으로 물러갔다.\n\n이 장에서 우리의 이야기는 겉보기에 매우 우유부단한 방식으로 앞으로 갔다 뒤로 갔다 할 운명이니, 이제 곧 이야기를 내일로 끌고 갔다가도 곧장 다시 어제로 되돌아가야 할 일이 생길 것이고, 그리하여 이야기 전체가 빠짐없이 귀에 들어오게 될 것이다. 여왕 폐하의 알현실에서 보다시피, 대사들과 고관대작들의 마차는 전용 문으로 휙휙 빠져나가는데, 그동안 존스 대위의 부인들은 자기네 삯마차를 기다리고 있다. 또 재무성 장관의 대기실에서 보다시피, 대여섯 명의 청원자들이 알현을 기다리며 끈기 있게 앉아 한 명씩 호명되는데, 그때 느닷없이 어느 아일랜드 의원이나 어느 저명한 인사가 방에 들어와 곧장 그 자리에 있는 모든 이들의 머리 위로 차관 나리에게로 곧장 걸어 들어가 버린다. 이야기를 이끌어 나가는 데서도 이와 마찬가지로, 소설가는 이런 더없이 편파적인 종류의 정의를 행사하지 않을 수 없다. 온갖 사소한 사건들도 모두 들려주어야 마땅하지만, 그래도 위대한 사건들이 등장할 때는 뒤로 미루어 두어야 한다. 그리고 도빈을 브라이턴으로 데려온 정황, 즉 근위대와 전열 부대의 벨기에 출동 명령, 그리고 그 나라에서 웰링턴 공작 각하의 지휘 아래 연합군이 집결한 일, 분명히 말하건대 그처럼 위엄 있는 정황이야말로, 이 이야기를 주로 이루는 온갖 사소한 사건들보다 앞자리를 차지할 자격이 있는 것이며, 따라서 약간의 사소한 뒤죽박죽과 무질서쯤은 너그러이 보아 넘길 만하고 또 마땅한 일이었다. 우리는 지금 겨우 제22장보다 시간을 조금 앞질러 왔을 뿐이니, 도빈이 도착한 그날 여느 때처럼 차려진 저녁 식사를 앞두고 여러 등장인물들을 저마다의 화장방으로 들여보낸 참이다.\n\n조지는 너무 인정이 많았던지, 아니면 넥타이 매듭에 너무 정신이 팔려 있었던지, 동료가 런던에서 가지고 온 소식을 한꺼번에 애밀리아에게 전하지 못했다. 그러나 그는 변호사의 편지를 손에 들고, 어찌나 엄숙하고 비장한 기색이었던지, 늘 재앙을 기민하게 살피고 있던 그의 아내는 최악의 사태가 닥치려는 줄로 여기고, 남편에게 달려가 더없이 소중한 조지에게 모든 걸 말해 달라고 애원했다. 저이가 해외로 출정 명령을 받았구나, 다음 주에 전투가 있겠구나, 그녀는 분명 그러리라 여겼다.\n\n더없이 소중한 조지는 해외 출정에 관한 물음을 슬쩍 받아넘기고는, 우울하게 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"아니, 에미, 그게 아니야. 나 자신이 걱정되는 게 아니라 당신이 걱정되는 거야. 아버지한테서 나쁜 소식이 왔어. 아버지가 나와 어떤 연락도 거부하셔. 우리를 내치고, 가난 속에 내버려 두셨어. 나야 그럭저럭 견딜 수 있지만, 당신은, 여보, 그걸 어떻게 견디겠어? 여기 읽어 봐.\" 그러고는 그녀에게 편지를 건넸다.\n\n애밀리아는 다정한 놀라움이 어린 눈빛으로, 위와 같은 너그러운 심정을 토로하는 자기의 고귀한 영웅의 말에 귀를 기울이고는, 침대에 걸터앉아 조지가 그토록 거창하고 순교자 같은 기색으로 건넨 편지를 읽었다. 그러나 그녀의 얼굴은 그 문서를 읽어 내려가는 동안 도리어 환해졌다. 사랑하는 사람과 함께 가난과 궁핍을 나눈다는 생각은, 앞서도 말했듯이, 따뜻한 마음을 지닌 여인에게는 결코 싫은 일이 아닌 것이다. 그 생각은 어린 애밀리아에게 실로 즐거운 것이었다. 그러다가 그녀는 여느 때처럼, 이런 부적절한 순간에 행복을 느낀 자신이 부끄러워져, 기쁨을 억누르며 새침하게 말했다. \"오, 조지, 아버님과 헤어진다는 생각에 당신의 가엾은 가슴이 얼마나 미어지겠어요!\"\n\n\"그래, 미어진다오.\" 조지가 고뇌에 찬 표정으로 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 아버님도 오래 화내실 순 없을 거예요.\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"누구라도 그럴 순 없을 거예요, 분명히. 아버님은 당신을 용서하실 거예요, 더없이 소중하고 더없이 다정한 내 남편이여. 오, 만약 그러지 않으신다면 난 결코 나 자신을 용서할 수 없을 거예요.\"\n\n\"내 가엾은 에미, 나를 괴롭히는 건 내 불행이 아니라 당신의 불행이야.\" 조지가 말했다. \"나야 약간의 가난쯤은 개의치 않아. 그리고 자만이 아니라, 내겐 스스로 길을 헤쳐 나갈 만한 재능이 충분하다고 생각해.\"\n\n\"맞아요, 당신은 그래요.\" 아내가 끼어들었다. 그녀는 전쟁이 당장 멈추고 남편이 즉시 장군이 되어야 마땅하다고 생각했다.\n\n\"그래, 나도 남들 못지않게 잘 헤쳐 나갈 거야.\" 오스본이 말을 이었다. \"하지만 당신은, 사랑스러운 그대여, 내 아내라면 당연히 누려야 할 안락함과 사회적 지위를 빼앗긴다니, 내가 그걸 어떻게 견딘단 말이야? 더없이 소중한 내 사람이 병영에 살고, 행군하는 연대 군인의 아내가 되어, 온갖 성가심과 궁핍에 시달리다니! 그 생각만 하면 비참해져.\"\n\n에미는, 이것이 남편의 유일한 근심거리였던지라 아주 마음이 놓여, 그의 손을 잡고 환한 얼굴로 미소 지으며 즐겨 부르던 노래 「와핑 올드 스테어스」의 한 소절을 흥얼거리기 시작했다. 그 노래에서 여주인공은 무심하다고 톰을 나무란 끝에, 그가 변함없이 다정하게 자기를 저버리지만 않는다면 \"그의 바지를 기워 주고, 그의 그로그주도 타 주겠노라\"고 약속한다. \"게다가,\" 그녀가 잠시 말을 멈춘 사이, 어느 젊은 여인 못지않게 어여쁘고 행복해 보이는 얼굴로 말했다. \"이천 파운드면 어마어마하게 큰돈 아니에요, 조지?\"\n\n조지는 그녀의 순진함에 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그리하여 마침내 두 사람은 저녁을 먹으러 아래층으로 내려갔는데, 애밀리아는 여전히 「와핑 올드 스테어스」의 가락을 흥얼거리며 조지의 팔에 매달려, 지난 며칠 동안보다 한결 기쁘고 홀가분한 마음이었다.\n\n그리하여 마침내 차려진 식사는, 음울하기는커녕 더없이 활기차고 흥겨운 자리가 되었다. 출정의 흥분이 조지의 마음속에서 상속권 박탈 편지가 불러온 침울함을 상쇄했던 것이다. 도빈은 여전히 수다스러운 역할을 이어 갔다. 그는 벨기에 주둔군에 관한 이야기로 좌중을 즐겁게 했는데, 그곳에서는 잔치며 흥겨움이며 유행이 끊일 새 없이 펼쳐지고 있다고 했다. 그러고는 한 가지 특정한 목적을 염두에 두고, 이 능란한 대위는 오다우드 소령 부인이 자기 옷가지와 소령의 옷가지를 꾸리던 모습을 묘사해 보였다. 소령의 가장 좋은 견장이 차통에 처박힌 사연이며, 갈색 종이에 싼 극락조 깃털을 단 부인의 그 유명한 노란 터번이 소령의 양철 삼각모 상자 속에 들어가 잠겨 있던 일을 이야기하면서, 그것이 헨트에 있는 프랑스 왕의 궁정이나 브뤼셀의 성대한 군대 무도회에서 과연 어떤 효과를 낼지 궁금하다고 했다.\n\n\"헨트라니! 브뤼셀이라니!\" 애밀리아가 별안간 충격을 받고 흠칫 놀라며 소리쳤다. \"연대가 출동 명령을 받았어요, 조지, 출동 명령을 받은 거예요?\" 그 다정하고 미소 짓던 얼굴에 공포의 빛이 어렸고, 그녀는 본능에 이끌리듯 조지에게 매달렸다.\n\n\"두려워 말아요, 여보.\" 그가 너그럽게 말했다. \"고작 열두 시간 항해일 뿐이야. 당신을 해치지 않아. 당신도 가게 될 거야, 에미.\"\n\n\"저는 갈 작정이에요.\" 베키가 말했다. \"전 참모부 소속이거든요. 터프토 장군이 나한테 홀딱 빠져 있어요. 안 그래요, 로던?\" 로던이 여느 때처럼 우렁차게 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 윌리엄 도빈은 새빨갛게 얼굴을 붉혔다. \"부인은 갈 수 없습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"위험을, 그 위험을 생각해 보십시오.\" 그는 그렇게 덧붙이려다가, 식사 내내 그가 한 이야기가 죄다 위험이란 없다는 것을 입증하려는 게 아니었던가? 그는 몹시 당황하여 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"저는 가야 하고, 또 갈 거예요.\" 애밀리아가 더없이 기운차게 외쳤다. 그러자 조지가 그녀의 결심을 칭찬하며 턱을 다독여 주고, 그 자리에 있는 모든 이들에게 이런 표독스러운 아내를 본 적이 있느냐고 물었고, 다들 부인이 그와 동행해야 마땅하다는 데 동의했다. \"오다우드 부인을 당신 보호자로 모셔야겠군.\" 그가 말했다. 남편이 곁에 있는 한 그녀가 무엇을 개의하겠는가? 그리하여 어쩐 일인지 이별의 쓰라림은 슬쩍 따돌려졌다. 비록 전쟁과 위험이 앞에 도사리고 있었지만, 전쟁과 위험은 앞으로 몇 달간은 닥치지 않을지도 몰랐다. 어쨌거나 한숨 돌릴 유예가 생긴 것이고, 그것이 겁 많은 어린 애밀리아를 완전한 사면을 받았을 때나 다름없을 만큼 행복하게 만들었으며, 도빈조차 속으로 그것이 무척 반갑다고 인정하는 바였다. 이제 그녀를 볼 수 있다는 것이 그의 인생에서 가장 큰 특권이요 희망이었기에, 그는 남몰래 어떻게 그녀를 지켜보고 보호할지 마음속으로 헤아렸다. 내가 만약 그녀와 결혼한 사이라면 그녀를 보내지 않았을 텐데, 그는 생각했다. 그러나 조지가 주인이었고, 그 친구는 굳이 간언할 일이 못 된다고 여겼다.\n\n레베카는 마침내 친구의 허리에 팔을 두르고 애밀리아를 그토록 중대한 일들이 논의된 식탁에서 데리고 나갔고, 잔뜩 들뜬 채로 매우 흥겹게 술을 마시며 떠드는 신사들을 남겨 두었다.\n\n저녁이 무르익는 동안 로던은 아내에게서 자그마한 집안 쪽지 하나를 받았는데, 비록 그가 그것을 구겨서 촛불에 곧장 태워 버렸지만, 우리는 운 좋게도 레베카의 어깨너머로 그것을 읽을 수 있었다. \"굉장한 소식.\" 그녀는 그렇게 적었다. \"뷰트 부인이 떠났어요. 큐피드한테서 오늘 밤 돈을 받아 둬요. 그가 십중팔구 내일 떠날 테니까. 명심해요.—R.\" 그리하여 조촐한 일행이 여인들의 거처로 자리를 옮겨 커피를 마시려 할 즈음, 로던이 오스본의 팔꿈치를 툭 건드리며 우아하게 말했다. \"이봐, 오스본, 친구, 만약 별 어려움이 없거든, 그 자그마한 돈 좀 신세 좀 지세나.\" 별 어려움이 없는 것은 아니었으나, 그럼에도 조지는 지갑에서 상당한 액수의 일부를 은행권으로 그에게 건네주고, 나머지 금액에 대해서는 일주일 후 만기로 자기 대리인 앞으로 어음을 끊어 주었다.\n\n이 일이 처리되자, 조지와 조스와 도빈은 시가를 피우며 작전 회의를 열고, 이튿날 조스의 무개마차를 타고 다 함께 런던으로 떠나기로 합의했다. 조스는, 내 생각에, 로던 크롤리가 브라이턴을 떠날 때까지 머물기를 더 바랐을 테지만, 도빈과 조지가 그의 뜻을 눌렀고, 그리하여 그는 일행을 도시로 실어 나르기로 동의하고는, 자기 체면에 걸맞게 말 네 필을 주문했다. 이튿날 아침 식사를 마친 뒤, 그들은 그 말들과 함께 위풍당당하게 출발했다. 애밀리아는 아침 일찍 일어나 더없이 부지런하게 자기 자그마한 트렁크들을 꾸렸고, 그동안 오스본은 침대에 누운 채 도와줄 하녀가 그녀에게 없음을 한탄했다. 그러나 그녀는 이 일을 손수 하게 되어 그저 기쁘기만 했다. 레베카에 대한 어렴풋하고 불안한 감정이 벌써 그녀의 마음을 채우고 있었다. 그리고 비록 두 사람이 헤어지면서 더없이 다정하게 입을 맞추기는 했으나, 우리는 질투라는 것이 어떤 것인지 알지 않는가. 그리고 애밀리아 부인 역시 자기 성별의 다른 미덕들과 더불어 그 질투라는 것을 지니고 있었다.\n\n이렇게 오고 가는 인물들 외에도, 우리는 브라이턴에 우리의 오랜 벗 몇몇이 더 있었음을 잊어서는 안 된다. 다름 아니라 크롤리 양과, 그녀를 시중드는 수행원들 말이다. 그런데 레베카와 그녀의 남편이 병약한 크롤리 양이 묵고 있는 숙소에서 돌멩이 몇 개 던질 거리밖에 떨어져 있지 않았건만, 그 노부인의 문은 일찍이 런던에서 그러했듯 그들에게 가차 없이 닫혀 있었다. 올케 곁에 머무는 동안, 뷰트 크롤리 부인은 사랑하는 마틸다가 조카와 마주쳐 동요하는 일이 없도록 단단히 단속했다. 노처녀가 드라이브를 나갈 때면 충직한 뷰트 부인이 그녀 곁에 앉아 마차에 동승했다. 크롤리 양이 가마를 타고 바람을 쐬러 나갈 때면, 뷰트 부인이 가마 한쪽에서 행진하듯 걸었고, 정직한 브리그스가 다른 쪽 날개를 맡았다. 그리고 어쩌다 로던과 그 아내를 마주치기라도 하면, 전자가 노상 굽실거리며 모자를 벗었음에도, 크롤리 양 일행은 어찌나 싸늘하고 사람 잡는 무관심으로 그를 지나쳐 버리던지, 로던은 절망하기 시작했다.\n\n\"여기 있으나 런던에 있으나 매한가지로군.\" 로던 대위는 풀 죽은 기색으로 곧잘 말했다.\n\n\"브라이턴의 안락한 여관이 챈서리 레인의 빚쟁이 유치소보다는 낫잖아요.\" 한결 명랑한 기질을 지닌 그의 아내가 대꾸했다. \"그 집행관 모지스 씨의 부관 둘이 일주일 내내 우리 숙소를 지키고 있던 걸 생각해 봐요. 여기 우리 친구들이 아주 어리석긴 해도, 조스 씨와 큐피드 대위가 모지스 씨의 부하들보다는 나은 길동무잖아요, 로던, 내 사랑.\"\n\n\"여기까지 영장이 따라 내려오지 않은 게 이상하단 말이야.\" 로던이 여전히 풀 죽어 말을 이었다.\n\n\"따라오면, 그때 가서 따돌릴 방도를 찾으면 돼요.\" 겁 없는 어린 베키가 말하고는, 조스와 오스본을 만난 일이 얼마나 큰 위안이자 이득인지를 남편에게 거듭 일깨워 주었다. 그들과의 친분이 로던 크롤리에게 더없이 때맞춰 자그마한 현금을 마련해 주었던 것이다.\n\n\"여관 계산서 치를 만큼도 안 될 텐데.\" 근위병이 투덜거렸다.\n\n\"우리가 그걸 왜 치러요?\" 무엇에든 답을 가진 부인이 말했다.\n\n크롤리 양의 하인들과 여전히 사소한 친분을 유지하며, 마주칠 때마다 마부에게 술을 사라는 지시를 받은 로던의 시종을 통해, 늙은 크롤리 양의 동정은 우리 젊은 부부에게 제법 소상히 알려졌다. 그리고 레베카는 다행스럽게도 자기가 몸이 안 좋은 척하면서, 노처녀를 진료하는 바로 그 약제사를 불러들일 생각을 해냈고, 그리하여 그들의 정보는 대체로 그런대로 빈틈없었다. 브리그스 양 역시 비록 적대적인 태도를 취하지 않을 수 없었으나, 속으로는 로던과 그 아내에게 적의를 품지 않았다. 그녀는 천성이 다정하고 너그러운 기질이었다. 이제 질투의 빌미가 사라지자 레베카에 대한 반감도 함께 사라졌고, 그녀는 레베카가 한결같이 건네던 다정한 말과 명랑한 기분을 떠올렸다. 그리고 실로 그녀와, 시녀인 퍼킨 부인, 그리고 크롤리 양 집안 전체가 의기양양한 뷰트 부인의 폭정 아래 신음하고 있었다.\n\n흔히 그러하듯, 그 선량하나 위압적인 여인은 자기 잇속을 너무 멀리까지 밀어붙였고, 그 승리를 가차 없이 누렸다. 그녀는 몇 주 만에 그 병약한 부인을 어찌나 속수무책의 고분고분한 상태로 만들어 놓았던지, 가엾은 이는 올케의 명령에 전적으로 자신을 내맡기고, 브리그스나 퍼킨에게 자기의 노예 신세를 하소연할 엄두조차 내지 못했다. 뷰트 부인은 크롤리 양이 날마다 마실 수 있도록 허락된 포도주 잔을 거역할 수 없는 정확함으로 일일이 헤아려 따랐는데, 이는 셰리주 병에 대한 통제권마저 빼앗긴 퍼킨과 집사에게 큰 분통의 씨앗이었다. 그녀는 흉선 요리며 젤리며 닭고기의 양과 순서를 배정했다. 밤이고 낮이고 아침이고, 그녀는 의사가 정해 준 그 끔찍한 약을 가져와 환자에게 어찌나 애처로운 순종으로 삼키게 했던지, 퍼킨은 \"우리 가엾은 마님이 양처럼 약을 잘도 잡수신다니까\"라고 말했다. 그녀는 마차로 드라이브를 하라느니 가마로 산책을 나가라느니 지시했고, 한마디로 그 노부인의 회복기를, 오로지 살림 잘하고 모성 넘치는 도덕적인 여인만이 할 법한 방식으로 짓눌렀다. 환자가 어쩌다 가냘프게 저항하며 식사를 조금만 더 하게 해 달라거나 약을 조금만 덜 먹게 해 달라고 사정하기라도 하면, 간병인은 당장 죽을 수도 있다고 으름장을 놓았고, 그러면 크롤리 양은 즉시 굴복했다. \"마님한테 기운이라곤 안 남았다니까.\" 퍼킨이 브리그스에게 말했다. \"삼 주째 나더러 바보라는 소릴 안 하셨거든.\" 마침내 뷰트 부인은, 사랑하는 병약한 부인을 통째로 퀸스 크롤리로 옮기기에 앞서, 앞서 말한 정직한 시녀와 덩치 크고 믿음직한 사내 볼스 씨, 그리고 브리그스 본인까지 내보내고, 목사관에서 자기 딸들을 불러올 작정이었다. 그런데 그 흐뭇한 임무로부터 그녀를 불러내는 고약한 사고가 일어났다. 그녀의 남편인 뷰트 크롤리 목사가 어느 날 밤 말을 타고 집으로 돌아오다가 말과 함께 넘어져 쇄골이 부러진 것이다. 열이 오르고 염증 증세가 나타나자, 뷰트 부인은 부득이 서식스를 떠나 햄프셔로 가야 했다. 그녀는 뷰트가 회복하는 대로 더없이 소중한 벗에게 돌아오겠노라 약속하고, 안주인을 대하는 처신에 관해 집안사람들에게 더없이 단단히 분부를 내린 뒤 떠났다. 그리고 그녀가 사우샘프턴행 마차에 오르자마자, 크롤리 양의 집 안 전체에는 거기 모인 사람들이 지난 몇 주 동안 맛보지 못한 환희와 안도감이 감돌았다. 바로 그날 크롤리 양은 오후의 약 복용을 그만두었고, 그날 오후 볼스는 자신과 퍼킨 부인을 위해 셰리주 한 병을 따로 땄으며, 그날 밤 크롤리 양과 브리그스 양은 포티어스의 설교집 대신 피케 한 판을 즐겼다. 그것은 마치 옛 동화에서, 막대기가 개를 때리기를 잊자 사건의 온 흐름이 평화롭고 행복한 혁명을 겪던 것과 같았다.\n\n일주일에 두세 번, 아침 아주 이른 시각에 브리그스 양은 해수욕용 마차로 가서, 플란넬 가운에 기름 먹인 모자를 쓰고 물놀이를 하곤 했다. 우리가 보았듯이 레베카는 이 사정을 알고 있었고, 비록 으름장 놓았던 대로 브리그스에게 들이닥치거나 실제로 그 부인의 면전으로 잠수해 들어가 차일의 신성함 속에서 그녀를 놀라게 하려 들지는 않았으나, 로던 부인은 브리그스가 물놀이로 상쾌하고 기운이 나서 기분이 좋을 법한, 목욕을 마치고 나오는 길에 그녀를 공략하기로 마음먹었다.\n\n그리하여 이튿날 아침 아주 일찍 일어난 베키는, 바다를 마주한 거실로 망원경을 가져와 해변의 해수욕용 마차들에 초점을 맞추었다. 그녀는 브리그스가 도착해 자기 칸으로 들어가 바다로 나아가는 것을 보았고, 자기가 찾아온 그 물의 요정이 자그마한 이동 칸에서 자갈밭으로 막 내려설 무렵 해변에 가 있었다. 그것은 아름다운 그림이었다. 해변이며, 해수욕하는 여인들의 얼굴이며, 길게 늘어선 바위와 건물들이 햇살 속에서 발갛게 물들어 반짝였다. 레베카는 얼굴에 다정하고 부드러운 미소를 띠고, 브리그스가 칸에서 나오는 순간 어여쁜 흰 손을 내밀고 있었다. 브리그스가 그 인사를 받는 것 말고 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가?\n\n\"샤 양, 아니 크롤리 부인.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n크롤리 부인은 그녀의 손을 붙잡아 가슴에 꼭 갖다 대더니, 별안간 충동에 휩싸여 브리그스에게 두 팔을 두르고 다정하게 입을 맞추었다. \"사랑하는, 사랑하는 벗이여!\" 그녀가 어찌나 자연스러운 감정의 빛을 띠며 말했던지, 브리그스 양은 물론 즉시 마음이 녹기 시작했고, 해수욕하던 여인마저 누그러졌다.\n\n레베카는 브리그스를 길고도 친밀하며 유쾌한 대화로 끌어들이는 데 아무 어려움이 없었다. 베키가 파크 레인의 크롤리 양 집을 별안간 떠나던 그 아침부터 오늘에 이르기까지 일어난 모든 일과, 뷰트 부인의 다행스러운 퇴장에 이르기까지, 브리그스가 논하고 묘사했다. 크롤리 양의 온갖 증세며, 병세와 의료 처치의 시시콜콜한 내막을, 그 심복은 여인들이 즐기는 그 충만함과 정확함으로 풀어놓았다. 자기네 병이며 의사 이야기를 두고 숙녀들이 서로 떠드는 데 지치는 법이 있던가? 브리그스는 이번에도 지치지 않았고, 레베카 또한 듣는 데 싫증 내지 않았다. 그녀는 정말로, 진심으로 고마워했다. 그 다정한 브리그스가, 그 충직하고 더없이 소중한 퍼킨이, 그 은인이 앓는 동안 곁에 머물도록 허락받았다는 것이. 부디 그분께 하늘의 축복이 있기를! 비록 그녀, 레베카가 크롤리 양에게 도리에 어긋나게 행동한 듯 보였을지언정, 그 잘못이란 자연스럽고 너그러이 보아 넘길 만한 것이 아니었던가? 자기 마음을 사로잡은 남자에게 손을 내미는 것을 어찌 마다할 수 있었겠는가? 감상적인 브리그스는 이 호소에 그저 눈을 들어 하늘을 우러르고 공감의 한숨을 내쉬며, 자기 또한 오래전에 마음을 내준 적이 있음을 떠올리고는, 레베카가 그리 대단한 죄인은 아니라고 인정할 수밖에 없었다.\n\n\"의지가지없는 고아에게 그토록 벗이 되어 주신 분을 제가 어찌 잊을 수 있겠어요? 아니에요, 비록 그분이 저를 내치셨다 해도,\" 레베카가 말했다. \"저는 결코 그분을 사랑하기를 멈추지 않을 것이고, 제 목숨을 바쳐 그분을 섬길 거예요. 저의 은인이시고, 제 사랑하는 로던이 흠모하는 친척이시니, 사랑하는 브리그스 양, 저는 이 세상 어느 여인보다도 크롤리 양을 사랑하고 흠모하며, 그분 다음으로는 그분께 충실한 모든 분들을 사랑해요. 저라면 결코 저 고약하고 음흉한 뷰트 부인처럼 크롤리 양의 충직한 벗들을 대하지 않았을 거예요. 온통 마음뿐인 로던은,\" 레베카가 말을 이었다. \"비록 겉으로는 거칠고 무심해 보일지 몰라도, 눈물을 글썽이며 백 번도 더 말했답니다. 더없이 소중한 고모님께 충성스러운 퍼킨과 훌륭한 브리그스 양 같은 그토록 훌륭한 간병인 두 사람을 보내 주신 하늘에 감사한다고요.\" 만약 그 끔찍한 뷰트 부인의 음모가, 그녀가 너무도 두려워하던 대로, 크롤리 양이 사랑하는 모든 이를 그 곁에서 내쫓고 그 가엾은 부인을 목사관의 그 독수리들에게 제물로 내버려 두는 것으로 끝난다면, 레베카는 그녀(브리그스 양)에게 부디 기억해 달라고 간청했다. 비록 누추할지언정 자기 집은 언제나 브리그스를 맞이할 수 있도록 열려 있다는 것을. \"사랑하는 벗이여,\" 그녀가 열정에 북받쳐 외쳤다. \"어떤 마음은 결코 은혜를 잊지 못하는 법이에요. 모든 여자가 다 뷰트 크롤리 같지는 않으니까요! 하기야 내가 왜 그 여자를 탓하겠어요,\" 레베카가 덧붙였다. \"비록 내가 그 여자의 도구요 술책의 희생물이 되었다 해도, 더없이 소중한 내 로던을 그 여자 덕에 얻은 게 아니던가요?\" 그러고는 레베카가 퀸스 크롤리에서 뷰트 부인이 한 모든 행동을 브리그스에게 낱낱이 펼쳐 보였는데, 그 행동이란 당시에는 그녀로서도 알 수 없는 것이었으나, 이제 와서는 그 사건들로 충분히 명백하게 설명되었다. 이제는, 뷰트 부인이 온갖 술수로 부추긴 그 애정이 싹튼 지금에 와서는, 두 순진한 사람이 그 여자가 놓은 덫에 빠져 사랑하고 결혼하고 그 여자의 책략 탓에 파멸하고 만 지금에 와서는 말이다.\n\n그것은 모두 사실이었다. 브리그스는 그 계략들을 더없이 또렷이 알아보았다. 뷰트 부인이 로던과 레베카의 혼사를 꾸민 것이었다. 그러나 후자가 더없이 죄 없는 희생물임에도, 브리그스 양은 친구에게 한 가지 두려움을 감추지 못했다. 크롤리 양의 애정이 레베카에게서 가망 없이 멀어져 버렸고, 그 노부인이 그토록 무분별한 혼인을 한 조카를 결코 용서하지 않으리라는 두려움이었다.\n\n이 점에 관해서 레베카는 나름의 견해가 있었고, 여전히 마음을 굳게 다잡았다. 크롤리 양이 지금은 그들을 용서하지 않는다 해도, 적어도 훗날 어느 날엔가 마음을 누그러뜨릴지도 몰랐다. 지금조차도 로던과 준남작 작위 사이를 가로막는 것이라곤 그 칭얼대는 병약한 핏 크롤리뿐이었으니, 그 전자에게 무슨 일이 생긴다면 만사가 잘 풀릴 터였다. 어쨌거나 뷰트 부인의 계략이 폭로되고 그 여자가 톡톡히 욕을 먹게 된 것은 흐뭇한 일이었고, 로던의 잇속에 이로울 수도 있었다. 그리하여 레베카는 되찾은 친구와 한 시간 동안 담소를 나눈 뒤, 더없이 다정한 존경의 표시를 남기고 그녀와 헤어졌으니, 두 사람이 함께 나눈 대화가 몇 시간도 지나기 전에 크롤리 양에게 고스란히 전해지리라 확신하면서였다.\n\n이 만남이 끝나자, 레베카로서는 자기 여관으로 돌아가야 할 때가 무르익었다. 그곳에서는 전날의 일행이 모두 작별의 아침 식사 자리에 모여 있었다. 레베카는 자매처럼 서로 사랑하는 두 여인에게 어울리는 더없이 다정한 작별을 애밀리아에게 고했다. 손수건을 아낌없이 적시고, 마치 영영 헤어지기라도 하듯 친구의 목에 매달리고, 마차가 떠날 때 (그나저나 바싹 마른) 손수건을 창밖으로 흔들고 나서, 그녀는 아침 식탁으로 돌아와, 그 감정의 격앙에 비추어 보자면 자못 왕성한 식욕으로 새우를 좀 먹었다. 그리고 이 별미들을 우물거리는 동안, 아침 산책길에 자신과 브리그스 사이에 있었던 일을 로던에게 설명해 주었다. 그녀의 기대는 매우 컸고, 그녀는 남편도 그 기대를 나누게 했다. 그녀는 대체로 침울한 것이든 명랑한 것이든 자기 의견을 남편이 함께 나누게 하는 데 성공하곤 했다.\n\n\"여보, 이제 괜찮다면, 책상 앞에 앉아 크롤리 양에게 보낼 어여쁜 편지를 한 통 써 줘요. 당신이 착한 아이라느니 뭐니 하는 그런 식으로 말이에요.\" 그리하여 로던은 자리에 앉아 \"브라이턴에서, 목요일\"과 \"사랑하는 고모님께\"라고 매우 빠르게 써 내려갔으나, 거기서 그 용맹한 장교의 상상력이 바닥나고 말았다. 그는 펜 끝을 우물거리며 아내의 얼굴을 올려다보았다. 아내는 그 처량한 표정에 웃음을 참지 못하고, 두 손을 등 뒤로 하고 방 안을 오락가락하며 편지를 받아쓰게 하기 시작했고, 그는 그것을 받아 적었다.\n\n\"이 나라를 떠나, 십중팔구 목숨을 앗아 갈지도 모르는 출정을 앞두고.\"\n\n\"뭐라고?\" 로던이 다소 놀라 말했으나, 그 표현의 우스움을 알아채고는 이내 씩 웃으며 받아 적었다.\n\n\"십중팔구 목숨을 앗아 갈지도 모르는 출정을 앞두고, 저는 이리로 왔습니다.\"\n\n\"그냥 여기로 왔다고 하면 안 돼, 베키? 여기로 왔다가 문법에 맞잖아.\" 용기병이 끼어들었다.\n\n\"저는 이리로 왔습니다.\" 레베카가 발을 한 번 구르며 고집했다. \"더없이 소중하고 더없이 오래된 벗에게 작별을 고하고자. 떠나기 전에, 어쩌면 다시 돌아오지 못할 길을 떠나기 전에, 평생토록 친절만을 베풀어 주신 그 손을 한 번만 더 잡게 해 주시기를 간청드립니다.\"\n\n\"평생토록 친절만을.\" 로던이 그 말을 휘갈겨 적으며 따라 읊고는, 자기 자신의 문장 짓는 재주에 적잖이 놀랐다.\n\n\"제가 고모님께 바라는 것은 다른 것이 아니라, 우리가 노여움 속에서 헤어지지 않는 것뿐입니다. 저는 어떤 점에서는 가문의 자존심을 지니고 있으나, 모든 점에서 그런 것은 아닙니다. 저는 화가의 딸과 결혼했으며, 그 결합을 부끄러워하지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 부끄러워하면 내 몸을 칼로 꿰뚫어도 좋아!\" 로던이 외쳤다.\n\n\"이런 멍청이 같으니.\" 레베카가 그의 귀를 꼬집으며, 철자에 틀린 데가 없는지 넘겨다보며 말했다. \"간청드립니다(beseech)에는 a가 안 들어가고, 오래된(earliest)에는 들어가요.\" 그리하여 그는 이 낱말들을 고쳤으니, 자기의 어린 마님이 지닌 우월한 지식에 머리를 조아린 셈이었다.\n\n\"저는 고모님께서 제 애정의 진척을 알고 계신 줄로 여겼습니다.\" 레베카가 말을 이었다. \"저는 뷰트 크롤리 부인이 그것을 다지고 부추겼다는 것을 알고 있었습니다. 그러나 저는 누구도 책망하지 않습니다. 저는 가난한 여인과 결혼했으며, 제가 한 일을 기꺼이 감내하고자 합니다. 사랑하는 고모님, 고모님의 재산은 뜻대로 처분하십시오. 저는 고모님께서 그것을 어떻게 처분하시든 결코 불평하지 않겠습니다. 부디 제가 돈 때문이 아니라 고모님 그 자체를 사랑한다는 것을 믿어 주시기를 바랍니다. 저는 영국을 떠나기 전에 고모님과 화해하고 싶습니다. 떠나기 전에, 부디, 부디 고모님을 뵙게 해 주십시오. 몇 주, 혹은 몇 달 뒤면 너무 늦을지도 모르며, 저는 고모님의 다정한 작별 인사 한마디 없이 이 나라를 떠난다는 생각을 견딜 수가 없습니다.\"\n\n\"고모님은 이걸 내 문체로 알아보지 못할 거예요.\" 베키가 말했다. \"일부러 문장을 짧고 시원시원하게 만들었거든요.\" 그리하여 이 진짜 같은 서신은 브리그스 양 앞으로 봉해져 발송되었다.\n\n브리그스가 잔뜩 비밀스러운 기색으로 이 솔직하고 단순한 진술을 건네자, 늙은 크롤리 양은 웃음을 터뜨렸다. \"뷰트 부인이 떠났으니 이제 읽어 봐도 되겠지.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"읽어 봐라, 브리그스.\"\n\n브리그스가 그 편지를 소리 내어 다 읽고 나자, 그 후원자는 한층 더 웃었다. \"모르겠느냐, 이 멍청아.\" 그녀가 그 글에 깃든 정직한 애정에 무척 감동했다고 공언하는 브리그스에게 말했다. \"이걸 로던이 단 한 자도 쓰지 않았다는 게 안 보이느냐? 그 애는 평생 돈을 달라는 말 없이 나한테 편지한 적이 없고, 그 애 편지란 죄다 틀린 철자며 줄표며 엉터리 문법투성이야. 그 애를 쥐고 흔드는 건 그 가정교사라는 자그마한 독사라고.\" 다들 한통속이지, 크롤리 양은 속으로 생각했다. 죄다 내가 죽기만을 바라며 내 돈을 노리고 안달이라니까.\n\n\"로던을 만나는 거야 상관없다.\" 그녀가 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤, 더없이 무심한 어조로 덧붙였다. \"그 애랑 악수하는 거야 안 할 것도 없고. 한바탕 소동만 없다면, 만나지 못할 게 뭐 있겠느냐? 난 상관없다. 하지만 사람의 인내심에도 한계가 있는 법이야. 그러니 명심해라, 얘야, 나는 정중히 로던 부인 맞이하기를 사절하겠다. 그것까지는 도저히 못 견디겠어.\" 그리하여 브리그스 양은 이 절반의 화해 전갈에 만족하지 않을 수 없었다. 그러고는 그 노부인과 그 조카를 만나게 할 최선의 방법이란, 크롤리 양이 가마를 타고 바람을 쐬러 나갈 때 로던더러 절벽 위에서 대기하고 있으라 일러 두는 것이라고 생각했다. 거기서 그들은 만났다. 크롤리 양이 옛 총아를 보고 어떤 사사로운 정이나 감정을 느꼈는지는 나도 모르겠으나, 그녀는 마치 바로 어제 만나기라도 한 듯 더없이 미소 짓고 더없이 기분 좋은 기색으로 그에게 손가락 두 개를 내밀었다. 그리고 로던으로 말하면, 그는 새빨갛게 얼굴을 붉히고는 브리그스의 손을 으스러져라 잡아 흔들었으니, 이 만남에 대한 그의 환희와 당혹감이 그토록 컸던 것이다. 어쩌면 그를 움직인 것은 잇속이었는지도 모르고, 어쩌면 애정이었는지도 모르며, 어쩌면 그는 지난 몇 주의 병환이 고모에게 일으킨 변화에 마음이 동했는지도 몰랐다.\n\n\"노부인이 늘 나한테 더없이 잘해 주셨거든.\" 그가 그 만남을 들려주며 아내에게 말했다. \"그래서, 그게 말이지, 어쩐지 좀 묘한 기분이 들고, 뭐 그런 거였어. 나는 그 뭐냐 하는 거 옆에서 같이 걸었지, 알지, 그 집 문 앞까지 말이야. 거기서 볼스가 나와서 고모님을 부축해 들였고. 나도 정말 들어가고 싶었는데, 다만—\"\n\n\"안 들어갔단 말이에요, 로던!\" 그의 아내가 비명을 질렀다.\n\n\"그래, 여보. 막상 그 순간이 되니까 겁이 나더라고, 빌어먹을.\"\n\n\"이 바보! 들어가서 다시는 안 나왔어야죠.\" 레베카가 말했다.\n\n\"나한테 욕하지 마.\" 덩치 큰 근위병이 시무룩하게 말했다. \"어쩌면 내가 바보였는지도 모르지, 베키, 하지만 당신이 그렇게 말하면 안 되지.\" 그러고는 그가 화났을 때 지을 법한, 마주하기에 그리 유쾌하지 않은 그런 눈초리로 아내를 쏘아보았다.\n\n\"자, 여보, 내일은 잘 살피고 있다가 가서 고모님을 뵈요. 그분이 부르든 안 부르든, 명심해요.\" 레베카가 성난 멍에짝을 달래려 애쓰며 말했다. 그러자 그는 자기 좋을 대로 하겠노라고, 그리고 입조심이나 좀 해 주면 고맙겠다고 대꾸했다. 상처 입은 남편은 자리를 떠나, 시무룩하고 말없이 의심에 잠긴 채 당구장에서 오전을 보냈다.\n\n그러나 밤이 다 가기도 전에 그는 굴복하지 않을 수 없었고, 여느 때처럼 아내의 우월한 분별과 선견지명을 인정하지 않을 수 없었으니, 자기가 저지른 실수의 결과에 관해 그녀가 품었던 예감이 더없이 음울하게 확인되었기 때문이다. 크롤리 양은 그토록 오랜 절교 끝에 그를 보고 악수를 하면서 어떤 감정을 느꼈음에 틀림없었다. 그녀는 그 만남을 한참 곱씹었다. \"로던이 아주 살이 찌고 늙었더구나, 브리그스.\" 그녀가 말동무에게 말했다. \"코는 빨개졌고, 외모가 지독히 천박해졌어. 그 여자와 결혼하더니 가망 없이 상스러워졌어. 뷰트 부인이 늘 그 둘이 함께 술을 마신다고 했는데, 정말 그러는 게 틀림없어. 그래, 그 애한테서 진 냄새가 지독하게 났어. 내가 알아챘다. 너도 못 맡았느냐?\"\n\n뷰트 부인은 누구에 대해서나 나쁘게 말한다고, 그리고 자기처럼 미천한 처지의 사람이 판단할 수 있는 한 그 부인이야말로—이라고 브리그스가 끼어들었으나 헛수고였다.\n\n\"음흉하고 책략을 꾸미는 여자라고? 그래, 그 여자가 그렇지, 누구에 대해서나 나쁘게 말하고. 하지만 그 여자가 로던을 술꾼으로 만들었다는 건 내가 확신해. 그런 천한 사람들은 죄다—\"\n\n\"그분은 마님을 뵙고 무척 감동하셨어요, 마님.\" 말동무가 말했다. \"그리고 그분이 위험천만한 전쟁터로 떠나신다는 걸 떠올리시면, 정말이지—\"\n\n\"그 애가 너한테 돈을 얼마나 약속하더냐, 브리그스?\" 늙은 노처녀가 신경질적인 노여움에 휩싸여 외쳤다. \"거봐라, 또 울기 시작하는구나. 난 소동이 질색이야. 어째서 난 늘 이렇게 시달려야 하는 거지? 네 방에 올라가서 울고, 퍼킨을 보내라. 아니, 잠깐, 앉아서 코를 풀고, 그만 울고, 크롤리 대위한테 편지나 써라.\" 가엾은 브리그스는 가서 고분고분 필기책 앞에 자리를 잡았다. 그 낱장들은 온통, 노처녀의 작고한 필경사였던 뷰트 크롤리 부인의 굳세고 힘차고 날쌘 필체의 흔적으로 얼룩져 있었다.\n\n\"'친애하는 귀하'라고 시작하거나, 아니 '귀하'가 낫겠다, 그렇게 시작해서, 크롤리 양께서, 아니, 크롤리 양의 주치의이신 크리머 씨께서 분부하시기를, 내 건강이 이러이러하여 지금의 이 미묘한 상태에서는 어떤 격한 감정도 위험할 수 있으니, 어떠한 집안 논의나 면담도 사절할 수밖에 없다고 적어라. 그리고 브라이턴까지 와 준 데 대해 감사하고, 어쩌고저쩌고, 나 때문에 더 이상 머물지 말아 달라고 청해라. 그리고 브리그스, 그가 부디 좋은 여행 되기를 바라며, 만약 수고스럽게 그레이즈 인 광장에 있는 내 변호사를 찾아간다면 거기서 그에게 전할 연락이 있을 것이라고 덧붙여도 좋다. 그래, 그러면 되겠다. 그러면 그 애가 브라이턴을 떠날 게야.\" 인정 많은 브리그스는 이 문장을 더없이 흐뭇한 마음으로 적어 넣었다.\n\n\"뷰트 부인이 떠난 바로 그 이튿날 나를 덮치다니,\" 노부인이 계속 재잘거렸다. \"너무 염치없는 짓이었어. 브리그스, 얘야, 크롤리 부인한테 편지를 써서, 그 여자는 돌아올 필요 없다고 일러라. 아니, 돌아올 필요 없어, 돌아와서도 안 되고, 나는 내 집에서 노예 노릇은 하지 않을 거야, 굶주리고 독약에 목이 메는 일도 없을 거야. 다들 나를 죽이고 싶어 해, 다들, 다들.\" 그러더니 이 외로운 노파는 발작적인 눈물의 비명을 터뜨렸다.\n\n그녀의 음울한 허영의 시장 희극의 마지막 장면이 빠르게 다가오고 있었다. 번지르르한 등불이 하나둘 꺼져 가고 있었고, 어두운 막이 이제 막 내려올 채비를 하고 있었다.\n\n로던을 런던의 크롤리 양 변호사에게 보내라고 일러 둔, 브리그스가 그토록 인정스럽게 적어 넣은 그 마지막 단락은, 노처녀가 화해를 거절했다는 것을 읽고 처음의 막막한 실망에 빠진 용기병과 그 아내를 다소나마 위로해 주었다. 그리고 그것은 노부인이 그것을 쓰게 한 본디 목적을 달성했으니, 로던으로 하여금 런던에 가고 싶어 안달하게 만들었던 것이다.\n\n조스가 잃은 돈과 조지 오스본의 은행권으로, 그는 여관 계산서를 치렀다. 그 여관 주인은 십중팔구 자기 외상값이 한때 얼마나 위태로웠는지를 오늘날까지도 모를 것이다. 장군이 전투에 앞서 짐을 후방으로 보내듯, 레베카는 현명하게도 그들의 주요 귀중품을 죄다 꾸려 조지의 하인에게 맡겨 보냈고, 그 하인은 트렁크들을 책임지고 마차에 실어 런던으로 돌아갔다. 로던과 그 아내는 이튿날 같은 탈것을 타고 돌아왔다.\n\n\"떠나기 전에 그 노부인을 한번 봤으면 좋았을 텐데.\" 로던이 말했다. \"어찌나 수척하고 변하셨는지, 분명 오래 못 사실 거야. 왁시한테서 어떤 수표를 받게 될지 궁금하군. 이백 파운드, 이백보다 적지는 않겠지, 응, 베키?\"\n\n미들섹스 집행관의 부관들이 거듭 찾아온 탓에, 로던과 그 아내는 브롬프턴의 숙소로 돌아가지 않고 여관에 묵었다. 이튿날 아침 일찍, 레베카는 풀럼에 있는 늙은 세들리 부인의 집으로 가는 길에 그 변두리를 지나치다가 그들을 볼 기회가 있었는데, 그녀는 더없이 소중한 애밀리아와 브라이턴 친구들을 찾아가던 참이었다. 그들은 모두 채텀으로, 거기서 다시 하리치로 떠나, 연대와 함께 벨기에로 가는 배를 타려는 참이었다. 마음씨 고운 늙은 세들리 부인은 무척 풀이 죽고 눈물에 젖은 채 외로이 남아 있었다. 이 방문에서 돌아온 레베카는, 그레이즈 인에 다녀와 자기 운명을 알게 된 남편을 발견했다. 그는 잔뜩 성이 나서 돌아와 있었다.\n\n\"맙소사, 베키.\" 그가 말했다. \"고모님이 나한테 겨우 이십 파운드를 주셨어!\"\n\n그것이 자기네에게 불리한 일이긴 했으나, 그 농담이 너무도 절묘했던지라, 베키는 로던의 낭패에 그만 폭소를 터뜨리고 말았다.\n\n브라이턴을 떠나면서, 우리의 친구 조지는 네 마리 말이 끄는 사륜마차로 여행하는 지위와 풍채를 갖춘 인물답게 위풍당당하게 캐번디시 광장의 어느 멋진 호텔로 향했다. 그곳에는 화려한 객실 한 채와, 은식기가 그득하고 말없이 시중드는 흑인 급사 대여섯이 둘러선 호화로운 식탁이, 그 젊은 신사와 신부를 맞이할 채비를 갖추고 있었다. 조지는 조스와 도빈에게 마치 군주와도 같은 태도로 이 자리의 주인 노릇을 했고, 애밀리아는 처음으로, 그것도 더없는 수줍음과 소심함을 내비치며 조지가 \"그녀 자신의 식탁\"이라 부른 자리의 안주인 노릇을 했다.\n\n조지는 와인을 두고 흠을 잡으며 급사들을 임금님처럼 들들 볶았고, 조스는 거북 요리를 한껏 흡족하게 게걸스레 먹어 치웠다. 도빈이 그에게 음식을 덜어 주었다. 수프 그릇이 놓인 앞자리의 안주인이 그 안에 무엇이 들었는지 도통 몰라서, 세들리 씨에게 등딱지 살도 배딱지 살도 떠 주지 않은 채 그냥 덜어 주려 했기 때문이다.\n\n* 등딱지 살(calipash)과 배딱지 살(calipee): 거북 요리에서 별미로 치는 부위.\n이 향응의 호사스러움과 그것이 베풀어진 객실의 화려함에 도빈 씨는 마음이 불안해져서, 식사 후 조스가 큼직한 안락의자에서 잠든 사이에 한마디 충고를 했다. 그러나 대주교에게나 어울릴 법한 거북 요리와 샴페인의 그 엄청남에 대고 항의해 본들 소용이 없었다. \"난 늘 신사답게 여행하는 데 익숙했고,\" 조지가 말했다. \"제기랄, 내 아내는 숙녀답게 여행할 거란 말이야. 주머니에 한 푼이라도 남아 있는 한, 내 아내한테는 아쉬운 게 하나도 없게 할 거라고.\" 그 너그러운 친구는 자신의 통 큰 기개에 스스로 무척 흐뭇해하며 그렇게 말했다. 도빈도 애밀리아의 행복이 거북 수프에 달려 있는 것은 아니라고 그를 설득하려 들지는 않았다.\n\n식사가 끝나고 얼마쯤 지나, 애밀리아는 풀럼에 계신 어머니를 뵈러 가고 싶다고 수줍게 청을 했다. 조지는 다소 투덜대면서도 그 허락을 내어 주었다. 그러자 그녀는 자신의 그 어마어마한 침실로 사뿐사뿐 달려갔다. 방 한가운데에는 어마어마한 영구대 같은 침대가 놓여 있었는데, \"황제 핼릭샌더 폐하의 누이께서 연합한 고난자들께서 여기 머무실 적에 주무신\" 침대라는 것이었다. 그녀는 더없는 열의와 기쁨으로 자그마한 보닛과 숄을 둘렀다. 그녀가 식당으로 돌아왔을 때 조지는 여전히 클라레를 마시며 일어설 기색도 보이지 않았다. \"같이 안 가시는 거예요, 여보?\" 그녀가 그에게 물었다. 안 갈 거였다. \"여보\"께서는 그날 밤 \"볼일\"이 있으셨다. 그의 하인이 마차를 잡아 그녀와 동행할 것이었다. 마차가 호텔 문 앞에 대령하자, 애밀리아는 그의 얼굴을 한두 번 부질없이 들여다본 뒤 다소 실망한 기색으로 조지에게 살짝 무릎을 굽혀 인사하고는 서글피 그 커다란 계단을 내려갔다. 도빈 대위가 그 뒤를 따라 그녀를 마차에 태워 주고, 마차가 목적지를 향해 떠나가는 것을 지켜보았다. 그 하인조차 호텔 급사들 앞에서 삯마차 마부에게 주소를 말하기가 부끄러워, 좀 더 가서 일러 주겠노라고 했다.\n\n* 핼릭샌더 폐하, 연합한 고난자들: 알렉산드르 황제(러시아)와 동맹국(연합군)을 무식한 하녀가 잘못 발음한 것.\n도빈은 옛 숙소인 슬로터스로 걸어서 돌아가면서, 오스본 부인과 함께 저 삯마차에 타고 있다면 얼마나 즐거울까, 하는 생각을 했을 법하다. 조지는 분명 취향이 사뭇 달랐다. 와인을 충분히 마시고 나자, 그는 케인 씨가 샤일록을 연기하는 것을 보러 반값 입장 시간에 맞춰 극장으로 떠나 버렸으니 말이다. 오스본 대위는 연극을 무척 좋아하는 사람이었고, 본인도 수비대의 여러 연극 공연에서 고급 희극의 배역을 빼어나게 연기한 적이 있었다. 조스는 날이 한참 어두워진 뒤까지 계속 잠을 잤고, 식탁 위 유리병들을 치우고 비우던 하인의 손놀림에 화들짝 놀라 깨어났다. 그리하여 이 건장한 영웅을 숙소와 잠자리로 모셔 갈 마차를 부르느라 삯마차 정거장이 또 한 번 동원되었다.\n\n세들리 부인이 온갖 모성의 열렬함과 애정으로 딸을 가슴에 끌어안았으리라는 것은 능히 짐작할 만하다. 마차가 자그마한 정원 문 앞에 멈춰 서자 그녀는 문밖으로 달려 나와, 울먹이며 떨고 있는 젊은 신부를 맞이했다. 셔츠 바람으로 정원 화단을 다듬고 있던 늙은 클랩 씨는 깜짝 놀라 뒤로 물러섰다. 아일랜드인 하녀는 부엌에서 뛰쳐나와 \"하느님의 가호가 있기를\" 하고 미소 지었다. 애밀리아는 포석을 따라 걸어 계단을 올라 거실로 들어서기조차 힘에 부쳤다.\n\n이 성소 같은 곳에서 함께 부둥켜안았을 때, 어머니와 딸의 눈물의 봇물이 어떻게 터졌는지는, 조금이라도 감상적인 기질을 지닌 독자라면 누구나 쉽게 그려 볼 수 있을 것이다. 숙녀들이 울지 않는 때가 언제 있던가? 기쁨이든, 슬픔이든, 그 밖의 인생사 어느 대목에서든 말이다. 더구나 결혼 같은 사건을 치른 뒤라면, 어머니와 딸이 그 부드럽고도 후련한 감성에 마음껏 자신을 내맡길 자유가 분명 있는 법이다. 나는 서로를 미워하는 여자들이 결혼 문제를 두고는 무척이나 다정하게 입 맞추고 함께 우는 것을 본 적이 있다. 하물며 서로 사랑하는 사이라면 얼마나 더 깊이 느끼겠는가! 착한 어머니들은 딸의 결혼식에서 자신이 다시 결혼하는 셈이다. 그리고 그 뒤에 일어나는 일들로 말하자면, 할머니들이 얼마나 극성스러운 모성을 발휘하는지 모르는 이가 어디 있겠는가? 사실 여자란 할머니가 되기 전까지는 어머니가 된다는 것이 무엇인지를 정말로는 잘 모르는 법이다. 거실에서, 황혼녘에 소곤거리고 훌쩍이고 웃고 우는 애밀리아와 그 어머니를 우리도 존중해 주기로 하자. 늙은 세들리 씨가 그러했던 것처럼. 마차가 멈춰 섰을 때 그는 그 안에 누가 타고 있는지 미처 알아채지 못했다. 그는 딸을 맞으러 뛰쳐나가지는 않았으나, 딸이 방에 들어서자(그는 여느 때처럼 서류며 끈이며 계산서를 만지작거리고 있었다) 더없이 따뜻하게 입을 맞추었고, 잠시 모녀와 함께 앉아 있다가 매우 현명하게도 그 자그마한 방을 두 사람에게 내어 주고 자리를 떴다.\n\n조지의 하인은 셔츠 바람으로 장미 덤불에 물을 주는 클랩 씨를 자못 거만한 태도로 지켜보고 있었다. 그래도 그는 세들리 씨에게는 무척 황송해하며 모자를 벗어 인사했다. 세들리 씨는 사위에 대해, 또 조스의 마차에 대해, 그의 말들이 브라이턴까지 내려갔는지, 또 저 빌어먹을 반역자 보나파르티와 전쟁에 대해 이런저런 소식을 물었다. 그러다 아일랜드인 하녀가 접시 하나와 와인 한 병을 들고 나오자, 노신사는 굳이 그 하인에게 술을 따라 주겠다고 고집을 부렸다. 그는 하인에게 반 기니까지 쥐여 주었고, 하인은 그것을 놀라움 반 경멸 반으로 주머니에 챙겨 넣었다. \"자네 주인 내외의 건강을 위하여, 트로터.\" 세들리 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 이건 집에 돌아가거든 자네 건강을 위해 한잔하라고 주는 것일세, 트로터.\"\n\n애밀리아가 그 자그마한 오두막집을 떠난 지 겨우 아흐레가 지났을 뿐이건만, 그곳에 작별을 고하던 때가 어쩌면 이리도 까마득하게 느껴지던지. 그녀와 그 지난날 사이에 얼마나 깊은 심연이 가로놓여 있던가. 그녀는 지금 서 있는 자리에서 그 시절을 돌아보며, 사랑에 푹 빠진 채 단 하나의 특별한 대상밖에 보이지 않던, 부모의 애정을 배은망덕하게는 아닐지언정 적어도 무심하게, 마치 당연한 제 몫인 양 받아들이던, 온 마음과 생각이 오로지 하나의 소망을 이루는 데 쏠려 있던 그 결혼 전의 처녀를 거의 딴사람을 보듯 바라볼 수 있었다. 그토록 가까운 과거인데도 그토록 멀어진 그 시절을 돌이켜보니 부끄러움이 밀려왔고, 다정한 부모님의 모습에 애틋한 회한이 가득 차올랐다. 상은 손에 넣었건만, 그 인생의 천국을 거머쥐었건만, 그것을 차지한 이는 여전히 미심쩍어하고 만족하지 못하는 것이던가? 주인공 남녀가 결혼이라는 관문을 통과하면, 소설가는 으레 막을 내려 버리고 만다. 마치 그것으로 극이 끝나 버린 양, 인생의 의심과 투쟁이 막을 내린 양, 일단 결혼이라는 나라에 발을 들이고 나면 그곳은 온통 푸르고 즐겁기만 한 양, 아내와 남편은 그저 서로 팔짱을 끼고 행복하고 완벽한 결실 속에서 노년을 향해 다정히 내리막길을 거닐기만 하면 되는 양 말이다. 그러나 우리의 가엾은 애밀리아는 이제 막 그 새 나라의 강기슭에 발을 디뎠을 뿐이건만, 벌써부터 강 건너 저 멀리 다른 기슭에서 자신을 향해 작별의 손을 흔드는 슬프고 다정한 형상들을 불안하게 돌아보고 있었다.\n\n젊은 신부의 도착을 축하하는 뜻에서, 그녀의 어머니는 무슨 잔치 같은 향응을 차려야겠다고 마음먹고는, 한바탕 이야기보따리를 풀어 놓은 뒤 조지 오스본 부인에게 잠시 양해를 구하고 집의 아래층 구역으로, 일종의 부엌 겸 거실로 내려갔다(그곳은 클랩 부부가 쓰는 방이었고, 저녁이면 설거지를 마치고 머리 마는 종이를 떼어 낸 아일랜드인 하녀 플래니건 양도 함께 썼다). 거기서 그녀는 멋들어지게 차린 차 자리를 준비할 방도를 강구하려는 것이었다. 사람마다 다정함을 표현하는 저마다의 방식이 있는 법인데, 세들리 부인이 보기에는 머핀과, 자그마한 컷글라스 받침 접시에 듬뿍 펴 바른 오렌지 마멀레이드야말로, 더없이 흥미로운 처지에 놓인 애밀리아에게 각별히 흐뭇한 다과가 되리라 여겨졌던 것이다.\n\n이런 진미들이 아래층에서 한창 마련되는 동안, 애밀리아는 거실을 빠져나와 위층으로 올라갔고, 어찌 된 영문인지도 모르게 결혼 전에 쓰던 그 자그마한 방, 그토록 숱한 쓰라린 시간을 보냈던 바로 그 의자에 와 있는 자신을 발견했다. 그녀는 오랜 벗이라도 되는 양 그 의자의 팔걸이에 몸을 묻고, 지난 한 주와 그 너머의 인생을 곰곰이 되짚어 보기 시작했다. 벌써부터 슬프고도 막연하게 과거를 돌아보고, 늘 무언가를 애타게 그리워하되, 막상 손에 넣고 보면 기쁨보다는 의심과 슬픔만 안겨 주는 것을 그리워하는 신세. 이것이 바로 허영의 시장의 그 거대하고 아등바등하는 군중 속에서 길 잃은, 죄 없는 방랑자, 우리의 가엾은 작은 존재의 운명이었던 것이다.\n\n그녀는 거기 앉아, 결혼 전에 무릎 꿇고 우러르던 조지의 그 모습을 애틋하게 떠올렸다. 실제의 그 남자가, 자신이 숭배하던 그 늠름한 젊은 영웅과 얼마나 다른지를 그녀는 스스로 인정했을까? 여자의 자존심과 허영심이 그런 고백을 스스로에게 허락하기까지는 아주, 아주 오랜 세월이 걸리는 법이고, 또 남자가 정말이지 형편없는 작자여야만 하는 법이다. 이윽고 리베카의 반짝이는 초록빛 눈동자와 표독스러운 미소가 그녀의 머릿속에 떠올라 그녀를 낙담으로 가득 채웠다. 그리하여 그녀는 한동안 평소의 이기적인 골똘함에 잠겨 거기 앉아 있었다. 조지가 청혼을 새로이 해 온 편지를 정직한 하녀가 들고 올라왔던 그날, 그 하녀가 그녀를 발견했을 때와 똑같은, 무기력하고 우울하기 짝이 없는 그 자세 그대로.\n\n그녀는 며칠 전까지 자기 것이었던 그 자그마한 흰 침대를 바라보며, 오늘 밤은 저기서 자고, 예전처럼 아침에 어머니가 자기를 굽어보며 미소 짓는 가운데 깨어나면 좋겠다고 생각했다. 그러다 캐번디시 광장의 그 으리으리한 호텔에서 자기를 기다리고 있는, 그 광막하고 칙칙한 특실 침실의, 영구대 같은 커다란 다마스크 천막 침대를 떠올리고는 몸서리를 쳤다. 정다운 작은 흰 침대! 저 베개에 엎드려 얼마나 숱한 긴 밤을 울었던가! 저기서 얼마나 절망하고, 또 차라리 죽어 버리기를 바랐던가. 그런데 이제 자신의 소망은 죄다 이루어지지 않았는가, 절망하며 단념했던 그 연인이 영영 자기 것이 되지 않았는가? 자상한 어머니! 그 침대맡을 얼마나 참을성 있게, 또 다정하게 지켜 주셨던가! 그녀는 침대 곁으로 가 무릎을 꿇었다. 그리고 거기서, 상처 입고 겁에 질렸으나 온순하고 사랑스러운 이 영혼은, 솔직히 말해 우리의 어린 소녀가 여태껏 좀처럼 찾지 않았던 그곳에서 위안을 구했다. 사랑이 지금껏 그녀의 신앙이었으나, 슬프고, 피 흘리고, 환멸에 빠진 마음은 비로소 또 다른 위로자가 절실함을 느끼기 시작했던 것이다.\n\n우리에게 그녀의 기도를 옮겨 적거나 엿들을 권리가 있을까? 형제여, 이것들은 비밀이며, 우리 이야기가 놓여 있는 허영의 시장의 영역 밖에 있는 것이다.\n\n그러나 이 말만은 해 둘 수 있겠다. 마침내 차가 준비되었다는 전갈이 오자, 우리의 젊은 숙녀는 한결 명랑해진 모습으로 아래층에 내려왔다는 것을. 요즈음 곧잘 그러던 것처럼 풀이 죽거나, 자신의 신세를 한탄하거나, 조지의 냉담함이나 리베카의 눈동자를 떠올리지 않았다는 것을. 그녀는 아래층으로 내려가 아버지와 어머니에게 입을 맞추고, 노신사와 이야기를 나누며 그를 여러 날 만에 처음으로 흥겹게 해 드렸다. 그녀는 도빈이 사 준 피아노 앞에 앉아 아버지가 좋아하는 옛 노래들을 죄다 불러 드렸다. 그녀는 차가 일품이라고 단언하고, 마멀레이드를 받침 접시에 담아낸 그 빼어난 솜씨를 칭찬했다. 그리하여 다른 사람들을 모두 행복하게 해 주리라 마음먹는 가운데, 그녀 자신이 그렇게 행복해진 것을 깨달았다. 그러고는 그 영구대 같은 커다란 천막 침대에서 곤히 잠들었다가, 조지가 극장에서 돌아왔을 때에야 비로소 미소를 지으며 잠에서 깨어났다.\n\n이튿날, 조지에게는 케인 씨가 샤일록을 연기하는 것을 보러 가는 것보다 더 중대한 \"볼일\"이 있었다. 런던에 도착하자마자 그는 부친의 변호사들에게 편지를 띄워, 이튿날 그들 사이에 면담이 이루어지기를 바란다는 자신의 지엄하신 뜻을 전했던 것이다. 호텔 숙박비에다, 당구와 카드로 크롤리 대위에게 잃은 돈으로 그 젊은이의 지갑은 거의 바닥이 난 터라, 여행길에 오르기 전에 채워 넣어야 했고, 변호사들이 그에게 지급하도록 위임받은 이천 파운드에 손을 대는 것 말고는 달리 방도가 없었다. 그는 마음속으로 머지않아 아버지가 누그러지리라고 철석같이 믿고 있었다. 자기처럼 더없이 훌륭한 본보기 같은 자식에게 어느 부모가 오래도록 모질게 굴 수 있단 말인가? 만약 그의 과거 행적과 개인적 미덕만으로 아버지의 마음을 누그러뜨리지 못한다면, 다가올 전쟁에서 어찌나 굉장하게 공을 세울 작정인지, 그러면 노신사도 그에게 굴복하고 말 것이었다. 그래도 안 되면? 흥! 세상은 그의 앞에 활짝 펼쳐져 있었다. 카드 운이 트일 수도 있었고, 이천 파운드면 쓸 만큼 쓸 수 있는 큰돈이었다.\n\n그리하여 그는 애밀리아를 다시 한번 마차에 태워 어머니에게로 보내면서, 두 부인에게 외국 여행을 떠나는 조지 오스본 부인 정도 되는 숙녀에게 필요한 것은 무엇이든 다 사 오라고 엄히 일러 백지 위임장을 내주었다. 채비를 갖출 시간이라고는 단 하루뿐이었으니, 따라서 두 사람이 그 일에 꽤나 바쁘게 매달렸으리라는 것은 능히 짐작이 갈 것이다. 다시 한번 마차에 올라, 모자 가게에서 포목점으로 분주히 돌아다니며, 굽실거리는 점원이나 정중한 주인들의 배웅을 받으며 마차로 돌아오니, 세들리 부인은 거의 예전의 자기 모습으로 돌아와, 가세가 기운 이래 처음으로 진심으로 행복해했다. 그렇다고 애밀리아 부인이 물건을 사고, 값을 흥정하고, 예쁜 것들을 구경하고 사들이는 즐거움을 마다할 만큼 도도한 것도 아니었다(아무리 철학자연하는 사내라도, 그런 것을 마다하는 여자에게 단돈 이 펜스라도 내려 하겠는가?). 그녀는 남편의 분부에 따라 자신에게 자그마한 호사를 베풀어, 숱한 숙녀용 물품을 사들였는데, 모든 점원의 말마따나 빼어난 취향과 우아한 안목을 한껏 드러냈다.\n\n그리고 막 닥쳐올 전쟁에 대해서는, 오스본 부인은 그다지 걱정하지 않았다. 보나파르티는 거의 싸움다운 싸움도 없이 분쇄되고 말 것이었다. 마게이트행 정기선들은 날마다 출항했고, 브뤼셀과 헨트로 향하는 멋쟁이 신사들과 명망 있는 숙녀들로 가득했다. 사람들이 가는 것은 전쟁터라기보다 유행을 좇는 관광 여행이라 해야 옳았다. 신문들은 그 가련한 벼락출세자 사기꾼을 비웃으며 조롱했다. 저따위 코르시카 출신 비렁뱅이가 유럽의 군대들과 불멸의 웰링턴의 천재성에 맞선다니! 애밀리아는 그를 철저히 경멸했다. 굳이 말할 것도 없이, 이 여리고 온순한 존재는 자기를 둘러싼 사람들에게서 의견을 그대로 받아들였으니, 그 충실함이란 스스로 사고하기에는 너무나 겸손한 것이었다. 어쨌든, 한마디로 그녀는 어머니와 함께 하루 종일 굉장한 쇼핑을 해냈고, 런던의 고상한 사교계에 처음 모습을 드러내는 이 자리를 상당한 활기와 평판 속에 무사히 치러 냈다.\n\n그러는 동안 조지는 모자를 비스듬히 눌러쓰고, 두 팔꿈치를 네모지게 벌린 채, 으스대는 군인다운 풍채로 베드퍼드 거리를 향해, 거기서 끼적거리고 있는 핏기 없는 서기들 하나하나가 모두 제 종이라도 되는 양 변호사 사무실로 거들먹거리며 들어섰다. 그는 누군가에게 오스본 대위가 기다린다고 히그스 씨에게 전하라고, 사납고도 거들먹거리는 태도로 명령했다. 마치 그 자기보다 머리가 세 배는 좋고, 돈은 쉰 배는 많고, 경험은 천 배는 많은 한낱 변호사 따위가, 당장 제 모든 일을 팽개치고 대위의 비위나 맞춰야 할 가련한 아랫것이라도 되는 양 말이다. 그가 거기 앉아 지팡이로 장화를 톡톡 두드리며 이자들은 참으로 비참하고 가련한 위인들이로구나, 하고 생각하는 사이, 수석 서기에서 견습 사무원에게로, 견습 사무원에서 몸에 너무 끼는 옷을 걸친 누더기 차림의 필경사들과 핏기 없는 잔심부름꾼들에게로, 방 안을 두루 돌며 번지던 그 경멸의 비웃음을 그는 알아차리지 못했다. 그 비참하고 가련한 위인들은 그의 사정을 죄다 알고 있었다. 그들은 밤이면 단골 술집 모임에서 맥주잔을 기울이며 다른 사무실 서기들에게 그 이야기를 떠벌렸다. 오, 신들이시여, 런던의 변호사들과 변호사 사무실 서기들이 모르는 것이 무엇이 있단 말인가! 그들의 캐묻는 눈을 피할 수 있는 것은 아무것도 없으며, 그들의 식솔들이 말없이 이 도시를 지배하고 있는 것이다.\n\n어쩌면 조지는 히그스 씨의 방에 들어서면서, 그 신사가 아버지로부터 무슨 타협이나 화해의 전갈을 전하라는 부탁이라도 받았으리라 기대했는지도 모른다. 어쩌면 그의 그 오만하고 차가운 태도는 자신의 기개와 결의를 내보이려는 표시였는지도 모른다. 그러나 설사 그렇다 한들, 그의 그 사나운 기세는 변호사 쪽의 싸늘한 냉정함과 무관심에 부딪혀 으스대는 짓이 무색해지고 말았다. 대위가 들어서자 그는 무슨 서류에 글을 쓰는 척했다. \"앉으시지요, 선생.\" 그가 말했다. \"선생의 그 자그마한 용건은 곧 처리해 드리겠습니다. 포 군, 미안하지만 해제 서류를 가져오게.\" 그러고는 다시 글쓰기에 몰두했다.\n\n포가 그 서류들을 가져오자, 그 우두머리는 이천 파운드어치 증권을 그날 시세로 계산해 보고는, 오스본 대위에게 그 금액을 은행 앞으로 발행한 수표로 받겠느냐, 아니면 은행에 그 액수만큼 증권을 매입하라고 지시할까 하고 물었다. \"고인이 되신 오스본 부인의 신탁 관리인 한 분이 지금 출타 중입니다만,\" 그는 무심하게 말했다. \"제 의뢰인께서는 선생의 뜻에 따라 이 일을 되도록 빨리 마무리 짓기를 바라십니다.\"\n\n\"수표로 주시오, 선생.\" 대위가 무척 퉁명스럽게 말했다. \"실링이고 반 페니고 다 집어치우시오, 선생.\" 변호사가 어음 액수를 적어 내려가던 참에 그가 덧붙였다. 그리고 이 한 번의 통 큰 처사로 저 늙은 익살꾼을 무안하게 만들었다고 혼자 우쭐해하며, 그는 수표를 주머니에 챙겨 넣고 사무실을 거들먹거리며 나섰다.\n\n\"저 친구는 이 년 안에 감옥에 가 있을 걸세.\" 히그스 씨가 포 씨에게 말했다.\n\n\"오스본 씨가 마음을 돌리지 않을까요, 선생, 그렇게 생각하지 않으세요?\"\n\n\"기념비가 걸어 돌아오겠는가.\" 히그스 씨가 대꾸했다.\n\n\"꽤나 빨리 거덜 내고 있네요.\" 서기가 말했다. \"결혼한 지 겨우 일주일인데, 며칠 전엔 그자가 다른 군인 몇이랑 같이 연극이 끝난 뒤 하이플라이어 부인을 마차로 모시는 걸 봤다니까요.\" 그러고 나서 또 다른 사건이 호명되었고, 조지 오스본 씨는 그 길로 이 훌륭한 신사분들의 기억에서 깨끗이 잊혔다.\n\n그 수표는 우리의 친구 롬바드 거리의 헐커 앤드 불록 은행 앞으로 발행된 것이었으니, 조지는 여전히 사업이랍시고 그 집으로 발길을 옮겼고, 거기서 돈을 받았다. 누런 얼굴을 장부 위에 박고 있던 프레더릭 불록 씨는—그 곁에는 얌전한 서기 하나가 앉아 있었다—마침 조지가 들어섰을 때 영업실에 있었다. 대위를 보자 그의 누런 얼굴은 더욱 송장 같은 빛으로 변했고, 그는 죄지은 사람처럼 안쪽 응접실로 슬그머니 몸을 숨겼다. 조지는 그 돈을 흐뭇하게 들여다보느라(그토록 큰돈은 여태 가져 본 적이 없었으므로) 너무 바빠서, 제 누이를 노리던 그 송장 같은 구혼자의 표정도, 줄행랑도 미처 알아채지 못했다.\n\n프레드 불록은 늙은 오스본에게 그 아들의 행색과 행실을 일러바쳤다. \"어찌나 뻔뻔하게 들어오던지요.\" 프레더릭이 말했다. \"한 푼도 안 남기고 죄다 인출해 갔습니다. 저런 작자한테 몇백 파운드가 얼마나 가겠습니까?\" 오스본은 그자가 그 돈을 언제 어떻게 빨리 써 버리든 알 바 아니라고 험한 욕을 섞어 가며 맹세하듯 내뱉었다. 프레드는 이제 날마다 러셀 광장에서 식사를 했다. 어쨌든 통틀어 조지는 그날의 볼일에 무척 만족했다. 그는 자기 짐과 여행 채비를 죄다 부랴부랴 갖추도록 했고, 애밀리아가 사들인 물건들의 값은 자기 대리인 앞으로 발행한 수표로, 그것도 귀족 같은 호기를 부리며 치러 주었다.\n\n조스의 멋들어진 마차가 채텀의 여관 문 앞으로 들어섰을 때, 애밀리아가 가장 먼저 알아본 얼굴은 다정한 도빈 대위의 모습이었다. 그는 벗들이 도착하기를 기다리며 지난 한 시간 동안 거리를 서성이고 있던 참이었다. 프록코트에 견장을 달고 진홍색 장식띠를 두르고 군도를 찬 대위는 자못 군인다운 풍채를 풍겼고, 그 덕분에 조스는 이런 사람을 아는 사이라고 내세울 수 있다는 사실에 적잖이 우쭐해졌다. 그리하여 이 살집 좋은 민간인은, 브라이튼과 본드 거리에서 자기 친구를 맞이하던 때와는 사뭇 다른 정겨움으로 그를 반겼다.\n\n대위와 함께 있던 사람은 소위 스터블이었다. 사륜마차가 여관에 가까워지자 그는 \"이런 세상에! 어쩌면 저렇게 예쁜 아가씨가!\" 하고 탄성을 터뜨리며 오스본의 안목을 한껏 칭송했다. 사실 애밀리아는 혼인 예복용 외투에 분홍 리본을 두르고, 탁 트인 공기를 가르며 빠르게 달려온 탓에 두 볼이 발그레 상기되어, 그토록 싱그럽고 어여뻐 보였으니 소위의 찬사도 충분히 받아 마땅했다. 도빈은 그가 그런 말을 해 준 것이 기뻤다. 그가 숙녀를 마차에서 내리도록 돕고자 앞으로 나서던 차에, 스터블은 그녀가 자기에게 내미는 작고 예쁜 손이며, 발판을 사뿐히 디디고 내려오는 더없이 곱고 작은 발을 보았다. 그는 얼굴이 온통 새빨개져서, 할 수 있는 한 최고의 인사를 올렸다. 그러자 애밀리아는 소위의 모자에 수놓인 연대 번호를 보고는, 발그레한 미소와 함께 답례로 무릎을 살짝 굽혀 인사를 했고, 그 한 방에 이 젊은 소위는 그 자리에서 완전히 넘어가 버렸다. 그날 이후로 도빈은 스터블 군을 무척이나 아끼게 되어, 단둘이 산책할 때나 서로의 숙소에서 애밀리아 이야기를 마음껏 하도록 부추겼다. 사실 ××연대의 정직한 젊은이들 사이에서는 오스본 부인을 흠모하고 떠받드는 것이 하나의 유행처럼 되었다. 그녀의 꾸밈없고 천진한 행동거지와 겸손하고 다정한 태도가 그들의 순박한 마음을 모조리 사로잡았던 것이다. 그 모든 소박함과 사랑스러움이란 글로는 도무지 그려 낼 수 없는 것이다. 그러나 여인들에게서 이런 모습을 보지 못한 이가 어디 있으며, 그 안에 온갖 미덕이 깃들어 있음을 알아보지 못한 이가 어디 있겠는가. 설령 그녀들이 당신에게 건넨 말이라곤 다음 카드리유 춤은 약속이 있다거나, 날씨가 무척 덥다는 것뿐이라 해도 말이다. 늘 자기 연대의 자랑거리였던 조지는, 이 한 푼 없는 어린 처녀와 혼인하는 의협심을 발휘하고 또 이토록 어여쁘고 다정한 짝을 고른 덕분에, 부대 젊은이들 사이에서 평판이 한껏 치솟았다.\n\n길손들을 기다리고 있던 응접실에서, 애밀리아는 뜻밖에도 오스본 대위 부인 앞으로 온 편지 한 통을 발견했다. 분홍색 종이에 쓴 삼각형 모양의 짧은 편지로, 비둘기와 올리브 가지가 새겨진 봉인에 옅은 파란 봉랍을 잔뜩 발랐고, 매우 큼직하면서도 어딘가 미덥지 못한 여자 필체로 적혀 있었다.\n\n\"페기 오다우드의 글씨로군.\" 조지가 웃으며 말했다. \"봉인에 찍힌 입맞춤 자국을 보면 알지.\" 과연 그것은 오다우드 소령 부인이 보낸 짧은 편지로, 바로 그날 저녁 자그마한 친목 모임에 오스본 부인이 함께해 주는 기쁨을 누리게 해 달라는 청이었다. \"당신은 꼭 가야 해.\" 조지가 말했다. \"거기서 연대 사람들과 인사를 트게 될 테니까. 오다우드가 연대를 지휘하고, 페기가 그 오다우드를 지휘하지.\"\n\n그러나 그들이 오다우드 부인의 편지를 즐긴 지 몇 분도 채 되지 않아, 문이 벌컥 열리더니 승마복 차림의 통통하고 쾌활한 부인이 우리 연대 장교 두 사람을 거느리고 방 안으로 들어왔다.\n\n\"글쎄, 차 마실 시간까지 도저히 못 기다리겠지 뭐예요. 가지, 이봐요, 날 부인께 소개해 줘요. 부인, 만나 뵙게 되어 정말 기뻐요. 그리고 제 남편 오다우드 소령을 소개할게요.\" 이렇게 말하며 승마복 차림의 쾌활한 부인은 애밀리아의 손을 더없이 따뜻하게 부여잡았고, 애밀리아는 이 부인이 바로 자기 남편이 그토록 자주 웃음거리로 삼던 그 사람임을 단박에 알아차렸다. \"댁의 그 남편한테서 제 이야기를 자주 들으셨을 거예요.\" 부인이 무척 활기차게 말했다.\n\n\"이 사람 이야기를 자주 들으셨을 거요.\" 그녀의 남편인 소령이 따라서 말했다.\n\n애밀리아는 미소 지으며 \"그렇다\"고 대답했다.\n\n\"그런데 그이가 저에 대해 좋은 말이라곤 거의 안 했겠지요.\" 오다우드 부인이 대꾸하더니, \"가지가 못된 악당\"이라고 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그건 내가 보증하지요.\" 소령이 뭔가 아는 체하는 표정을 지으려 애쓰며 말하자 조지가 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 오다우드 부인은 채찍으로 톡 치며 소령에게 조용히 하라고 이른 뒤, 오스본 대위 부인께 격식을 갖추어 자신을 소개해 달라고 청했다.\n\n\"여보, 이분으로 말할 것 같으면,\" 조지가 자못 엄숙하게 말했다. \"나의 더없이 훌륭하고, 다정하고, 빼어난 벗, 오릴리아 마거리타, 다른 이름으로는 페기라 불리는 분이오.\"\n\n\"맞아, 옳은 말이오.\" 소령이 끼어들었다.\n\n\"다른 이름으로는 페기, 우리 연대 마이클 오다우드 소령의 부인이자, 킬데어 주 글렌맬로니의 피츠제럴드 베리스퍼드 디 버고 맬로니의 따님이시지.\"\n\n\"그리고 더블린 머라이언 광장 출신이고요.\" 부인이 차분하게 우쭐대며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇지, 머라이언 광장이고말고.\" 소령이 속삭이듯 말했다.\n\n\"바로 거기서 당신이 날 쫓아다니며 구애했잖아요, 여보.\" 부인이 말했고, 소령은 사람들 앞에서 으레 나오는 다른 모든 말과 마찬가지로 이 말에도 고개를 끄덕여 동의했다.\n\n세계 방방곡곡에서 군주를 섬겨 왔고, 그 직업의 한 계단 한 계단을 그에 넘치고도 남을 만한 담대함과 무용으로 사들여 온 오다우드 소령이었지만, 정작 그는 세상에서 가장 겸손하고, 말수 적고, 양처럼 순하고, 온순한 자그마한 사내였으며, 마치 아내의 차 시중꾼이라도 되는 양 그녀에게 고분고분했다. 회식 자리에서 그는 말없이 앉아 술만 잔뜩 들이켰다. 술이 잔뜩 오르면 말없이 비틀거리며 집으로 돌아갔다. 입을 열 때라곤 무슨 일에서든 누구에게나 맞장구를 칠 때뿐이었고, 그렇게 더없는 평온과 호인다운 기질로 삶을 헤쳐 나갔다. 인도의 그 뜨거운 태양도 그의 성미를 달군 적이 없고, 발헤런의 학질도 그것을 떨게 한 적이 없었다. 그는 만찬 식탁으로 가듯 태연히 포대를 향해 걸어 올라갔고, 말고기든 거북이 요리든 똑같이 흡족하게 식욕을 돋우며 먹어 치웠다. 그에게는 오다우드스타운의 오다우드 노부인이라는 노모가 있었는데, 그가 노모의 뜻을 거스른 적이라곤 집을 뛰쳐나가 입대했을 때와, 저 가증스러운 페기 맬로니와 기어이 혼인하겠다고 고집을 부렸을 때뿐이었다.\n\n페기는 명문가 글렌맬로니의 다섯 자매 중 하나이자 열한 남매 중 하나였다. 그러나 그녀의 남편은 사촌지간이긴 했어도 어머니 쪽 사촌이라, 그녀가 세상에서 가장 명망 높은 가문이라 믿어 의심치 않는 맬로니가와 혈연으로 맺어지는 그 더없이 값진 이점은 누리지 못했다. 더블린에서 아홉 시즌, 배스와 첼트넘에서 두 시즌을 보내고도 평생의 짝을 찾지 못하자, 맬로니 양은 서른세 살쯤 되었을 때 사촌 미크에게 자기와 혼인하라 명했다. 정직한 사내는 그 말에 따라 그녀를 서인도제도로 데려갔고, 그가 막 전입해 온 ××연대 부인네들을 다스리는 안주인 자리에 앉혔다.\n\n오다우드 부인은 애밀리아와(아니, 실은 그 누구와든) 함께 있은 지 반 시간도 채 되기 전에, 이 상냥한 부인은 자기 출생과 가문 내력을 새 친구에게 죄다 늘어놓았다. \"이봐요,\" 그녀가 사람 좋게 말했다. \"나는 본래 가지를 내 친동생으로 삼을 작정이었어요. 내 여동생 글로비나가 그이한테 더없이 잘 어울렸을 텐데 말이지요. 하지만 지난 일은 지난 일이고, 그이가 댁과 약혼을 했으니, 그렇담 나는 그 대신 댁을 동생으로 삼기로 단단히 마음먹었어요. 댁을 동생으로 여기고, 한 식구처럼 사랑할 거예요. 정말이지 댁은 얼굴도 곱고 마음씨도 좋아 보이고 몸가짐도 어찌나 사랑스러운지, 우리는 분명 잘 지낼 거고, 어쨌거나 댁은 우리 집안에 보탬이 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"암, 그렇고말고.\" 오다우드가 흡족한 기색으로 말했고, 애밀리아는 이렇게 느닷없이 이토록 많은 친척 무리에 소개받게 된 것이 적잖이 재미있으면서도 고맙게 느껴졌다.\n\n\"여기 사람들은 다 좋은 이들이에요.\" 소령 부인이 말을 이었다. \"군대를 통틀어 우리만큼 화목한 사회도, 우리 회식실만큼 유쾌한 곳도 없을걸요. 우리 사이엔 다툼도, 입씨름도, 험담도, 쓸데없는 수다도 없어요. 우리는 다 서로 사랑한답니다.\"\n\n\"특히 머제니스 부인을 말이지요.\" 조지가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"머제니스 대위 부인하고는 화해했어요. 하긴 그 여자가 나한테 한 짓을 생각하면 슬픔에 백발이 되어 무덤까지 갈 지경이지만요.\"\n\n\"당신은 그렇게 새카만 머리가 멋들어진데 말이오, 페기, 여보.\" 소령이 외쳤다.\n\n\"입 좀 다물어요, 미크, 이 멍청한 양반아. 남편이란 작자들은 늘 거치적거린다니까요, 오스본 부인. 우리 미크로 말할 것 같으면, 나는 늘 이이한테 명령을 내릴 때나 입에 음식이며 술을 넣을 때 말고는 절대 입을 벌리지 말라고 한답니다. 연대 이야기는 우리 둘만 있을 때 내가 다 들려주고 일러 줄게요. 자, 이제 댁의 오라버니를 소개해 줘요. 정말 훤칠한 분이시네, 내 사촌 댄 맬로니가 떠오르는걸요(밸리맬로니의 맬로니 말이에요, 알잖아요, 오이스터스타운의 오필리아 스컬리와 혼인한 사람, 폴두디 경의 친사촌인 그 여자 말이에요). 세들리 씨, 만나 뵙게 되어 정말 기뻐요. 오늘 회식 자리에 함께하시겠지요. (저 망할 군의관 좀 잘 살펴요, 미크, 그리고 무슨 일이 있어도 오늘 저녁 내 모임을 위해 정신만은 멀쩡하게 차리고 있어요.)\"\n\n\"여보, 오늘은 150연대가 우리한테 송별 만찬을 베푸는 날이오.\" 소령이 끼어들었다. \"하지만 세들리 씨 자리야 손쉽게 마련할 수 있을 거요.\"\n\n\"심플더러 뛰어가라고 해요(우리 연대 심플 소위 말이에요, 애밀리아. 깜빡하고 소개를 못 했네요). 어서 뛰어가서, 오다우드 소령 부인의 인사와 함께 테이비시 대령께 전하라고 해요. 오스본 대위가 처남을 데려왔으니 다섯 시 정각에 150연대 회식 자리로 모셔 가겠다고요. 그동안 우리 둘은, 댁만 좋다면, 여기서 간단히 요기나 하자고요.\" 오다우드 부인의 말이 채 끝나기도 전에, 젊은 소위는 그 임무를 받들어 계단을 종종걸음으로 내려가고 있었다.\n\n\"복종이야말로 군대의 혼이지. 오다우드 부인이 남아서 당신을 가르쳐 주는 동안 우리는 우리 임무를 보러 가야겠소, 에미.\" 오스본 대위가 말했다. 그러고는 두 신사가 소령의 양옆을 한 사람씩 붙잡고, 그의 머리 너머로 서로 히죽거리며 그 장교와 함께 걸어 나갔다.\n\n이제 새 친구를 독차지하게 된 성마른 오다우드 부인은, 그 가엾고 자그마한 여인의 기억력으로는 도무지 감당할 수 없을 만큼 어마어마한 정보를 쏟아 내기 시작했다. 그녀는 어리둥절해진 이 젊은 숙녀가 일원이 되었음을 깨닫게 된 그 어마어마하게 대식구인 집안에 관해, 애밀리아에게 천 가지나 되는 시시콜콜한 사정을 들려주었다. \"대령 부인인 헤비탑 부인은 자메이카에서 황열병에다 상심까지 겹쳐 세상을 떴어요. 그 끔찍한 늙은 대령이, 머리통이 대포알처럼 반들반들 벗어진 양반이, 거기서 어떤 혼혈 처녀한테 추파를 던지고 있었거든요. 머제니스 부인은 배운 건 없어도 좋은 여자이긴 한데, 혀가 어찌나 독한지 휘스트 칠 땐 제 친어미라도 속여 먹을 사람이에요. 커크 대위 부인으로 말하자면, 글쎄, 점잖은 카드놀이 이야기만 나와도 그 새우 눈을 치뜨고 본단 말이에요(우리 아버지로 말하면 교회 다니는 그 누구보다 경건한 분이었고, 우리 삼촌 댄 맬로니랑 우리 사촌 주교님까지도 평생 매일 밤 루나 휘스트를 한 판씩 즐기셨는데 말이지요). 이번엔 두 사람 다 연대를 따라가지 않는답니다.\" 오다우드 부인이 덧붙였다. \"패니 머제니스는 제 어미한테 가 있어요, 런던 바로 옆 이즐링턴 마을에서 십중팔구 석탄 부스러기랑 감자 따위를 파는 여자한테요. 그러면서도 늘 제 아비 배 자랑을 늘어놓고, 강을 거슬러 올라가는 배들을 우리한테 가리켜 보이곤 하지요. 그리고 커크 부인이랑 그 애들은 여기 베세다 플레이스에 머무를 거예요, 제가 떠받드는 설교자 램스혼 박사 가까이 있으려고요. 버니 부인은 흥미로운 처지에 놓여 있는데, 원, 그 여자야 늘 그 모양이지만요, 벌써 중위한테 일곱을 안겨 주었답니다. 그리고 포스키 소위 부인은, 댁보다 두 달 먼저 들어온 사람인데, 톰 포스키하고 스무 번도 넘게 싸워 댔어요. 어찌나 시끄러운지 막사 전체에 다 들릴 정도였지요(접시까지 깨부수는 지경에 이르렀다나요, 그리고 톰은 그 시퍼렇게 멍든 눈을 끝내 해명하지 못했고요). 그러다 결국 제 어미한테 돌아갈 거예요. 리치먼드에서 숙녀 교육원을 하는 여자한테요. 거기서 도망쳐 나온 게 그 여자한텐 불운이었지요! 댁은 어디서 마무리 교육을 받았나요? 나는 더블린 근처 부터스타운, 일리수스 그로브에 있는 플래너핸 부인 학원에서 받았는데, 돈 한 푼 아끼지 않았답니다. 후작 부인이 진짜 파리식 발음을 가르쳐 주었고, 프랑스 군대에서 퇴역한 소장 한 분이 우리한테 훈련을 시켜 주었지요.\"\n\n이 뒤죽박죽인 집안에, 우리의 어리둥절한 애밀리아는 느닷없이 일원이 되어 버렸다. 오다우드 부인을 손위 언니로 둔 채로 말이다. 그녀는 차 마실 시간에 다른 여자 친척들에게도 소개되었는데, 조용하고, 마음씨 곱고, 그리 빼어난 미인도 아니었던 까닭에, 150연대 회식 자리에서 신사들이 도착하기 전까지는 제법 호감을 사고 있었다. 그러나 그 신사들이 하나같이 그녀를 어찌나 흠모하던지, 자매들은 당연하다는 듯 그녀의 흠을 잡기 시작했다.\n\n\"오스본이 이제 바람기는 다 잡았으면 좋겠네요.\" 머제니스 부인이 버니 부인에게 말했다. \"개과천선한 난봉꾼이 좋은 남편이 된다는 게 사실이라면야, 가지를 만난 저 여자는 정말 운수 대통한 셈이지요.\" 오다우드 부인이 포스키 부인에게 한마디 했다. 연대의 새색시 자리를 빼앗긴 포스키 부인은 그 찬탈자에게 잔뜩 화가 나 있던 터였다. 커크 부인으로 말하자면, 램스혼 박사의 그 제자는 애밀리아에게 신앙에 관한 핵심적인 물음을 한두 가지 던져 보았다. 그녀가 영적으로 깨어 있는지, 신앙을 고백하는 그리스도교도인지 따위를 알아보려는 것이었다. 그러다 오스본 부인의 대답이 너무도 순진한 것을 보고 그녀가 아직 칠흑 같은 어둠 속에 있음을 알아채자, 그림이 든 한 푼짜리 작은 책자 세 권을 그녀의 손에 쥐여 주었다. 곧 「울부짖는 광야」, 「완즈워스 공유지의 빨래하는 여인」, 「영국 병사의 가장 훌륭한 총검」이라는 것이었다. 그녀가 잠들기 전에 그녀를 깨우치겠다는 일념으로, 커크 부인은 애밀리아에게 그날 밤 잠자리에 들기 전에 이것을 읽어 보라고 신신당부했다.\n\n그러나 남자들은 하나같이 호인답게 동료의 어여쁜 아내 곁으로 모여들어, 군인다운 의협심으로 그녀에게 정중히 구애를 펼쳤다. 그녀는 자그마한 승리를 거두었고, 그 덕분에 기분이 한껏 들떠 두 눈이 반짝거렸다. 조지는 아내의 인기가 자랑스러웠고, 그녀가 신사들의 관심을 받아들이고 그 찬사에 답하는 태도(무척 명랑하고 우아하면서도 천진하고 조금은 수줍은 태도)가 흐뭇했다. 그리고 군복을 입은 그는, 그 방 안 어느 누구보다 훨씬 더 늠름했다! 그녀는 그가 다정하게 자신을 지켜보고 있음을 느끼며, 그 따스함에 기쁨으로 발그레 달아올랐다. \"이이의 벗들을 모두 반갑게 맞이하리라.\" 그녀는 마음속으로 다짐했다. \"이이를 사랑하듯 모두를 사랑하리라. 언제나 명랑하고 쾌활하게 지내며 이이의 가정을 행복하게 만들리라.\"\n\n과연 연대는 환호로 그녀를 받아들였다. 대위들은 흡족해했고, 중위들은 갈채를 보냈으며, 소위들은 흠모했다. 늙은 군의관 커틀러는 농담을 한두 마디 했는데, 직업과 관련된 것이라 여기 옮길 필요는 없겠다. 그리고 에든버러 출신의 군의관 보조 캐클은 황송하게도 그녀에게 문학에 관한 질문을 던지고, 자기가 아는 가장 그럴듯한 프랑스어 인용구 세 가지로 그녀를 시험해 보았다. 젊은 스터블은 이 사람 저 사람에게 돌아다니며 \"이런, 정말 예쁜 아가씨 아닌가?\" 하고 속삭였고, 니거스 술이 들어올 때 말고는 그녀에게서 한시도 눈을 떼지 않았다.\n\n도빈 대위로 말하자면, 그는 저녁 내내 그녀에게 말 한마디 건네지 않았다. 다만 그와 150연대의 포터 대위가 조스를 호텔까지 데려다주었다. 조스는 잔뜩 취해 청승맞은 꼴이 되어, 회식 식탁에서나 야회에서나 자기 호랑이 사냥 이야기를 자못 그럴듯하게 늘어놓았던 터였다. 터번에 극락조 깃털을 꽂은 오다우드 부인을 상대로 말이다. 도빈은 이 징세관을 그의 하인에게 맡긴 뒤, 여관 문 앞에서 시가를 피우며 어슬렁거렸다. 그러는 동안 조지는 무척 정성스레 아내에게 숄을 둘러 주고, 젊은 장교들이 다 함께 악수를 청한 뒤 오다우드 부인의 집에서 그녀를 데리고 나왔다. 장교들은 그녀를 마차까지 배웅하고는, 마차가 떠나갈 때 그것을 향해 환호를 보냈다. 그리하여 애밀리아는 마차에서 내리며 도빈에게 자그마한 손을 내밀었고, 저녁 내내 자기한테 도통 눈길 한 번 주지 않았다며 미소 띤 얼굴로 그를 나무랐다.\n\n대위는 여관도 거리도 모두 잠든 뒤로도 한참 동안 그 해로운 흡연의 즐거움을 이어 갔다. 그는 조지의 응접실 창에서 불빛이 사라지고, 바로 곁의 침실에서 불빛이 새어 나오는 것을 지켜보았다. 그가 자기 숙소로 돌아온 것은 거의 동틀 무렵이었다. 강에 떠 있는 배들에서 환호 소리가 들려왔다. 수송선들이 벌써 화물을 싣고 템스강을 따라 내려갈 채비를 하고 있던 것이다.\n\n연대는 장교들과 함께, 이번 일을 위해 폐하의 정부가 마련한 배편으로 수송될 예정이었다. 오다우드 부인의 거처에서 그 흥겨운 연회가 열린 지 이틀 후, 강에 떠 있는 모든 동인도 선박과 뭍에 늘어선 군대의 환호 속에서, 군악대가 \"신이여 국왕을 지키소서\"를 연주하고 장교들이 모자를 흔들며 선원들이 호기롭게 만세를 외치는 가운데, 수송선들은 강을 따라 내려가 호위를 받으며 오스텐드로 향했다. 한편 용맹한 조스는 누이동생과 소령 부인을 호위하기로 약속한 터였고, 그 유명한 극락조 깃털과 터번을 비롯한 부인의 살림살이 대부분은 연대 화물과 함께 부쳐졌다. 그리하여 우리의 두 여주인공은 별다른 짐 없이 홀가분하게 마차를 타고 램즈게이트까지 갔는데, 그곳에는 정기선이 숱하게 오가고 있어서 그중 한 척에 올라 오스텐드까지 빠르게 건너갔다.\n\n이제부터 펼쳐질 조스의 인생 한 토막은 사건으로 가득 차서, 이후로 여러 해 동안 그의 입담거리가 되어 주었으니, 워털루 대전에 관해 들려줄 더 손에 땀을 쥐게 하는 이야기들 앞에서는 호랑이 사냥 이야기조차 뒷전으로 밀려나는 형편이었다. 누이동생을 외국까지 호위하기로 약속하자마자, 그는 윗입술 면도를 그만두었다는 소문이 돌았다. 채텀에서 그는 더없이 부지런히 사열과 훈련을 따라다녔다. 그는 (훗날 그가 이따금 일컫던 대로) 자기 \"동료 장교들\"의 대화에 지극한 주의를 기울여 귀담아들었고, 군대 용어를 닥치는 대로 익혔다. 이런 학습에서 훌륭한 오다우드 부인이 그에게 큰 도움이 되어 주었다. 그리고 마침내 그들을 목적지로 실어다 줄 러블리 로즈 호에 오르던 날, 그는 가두리 장식이 달린 프록코트에 흰 무명 바지를 입고, 멋들어진 금색 띠로 장식한 군용 약모를 쓰고 나타났다. 자기 마차를 가지고 온 데다 배에 탄 모든 이에게 자기가 웰링턴 공작의 군대에 합류하러 가는 길이라고 은밀히 일러두니, 사람들은 그를 대단한 인물, 적어도 병참감이나 정부 특사쯤으로 오해했다.\n\n항해 동안 그는 끔찍이 고생했고, 그동안 두 숙녀 역시 뱃멀미로 늘어져 있었다. 그러나 애밀리아는 정기선이 오스텐드에 다다르자, 러블리 로즈 호와 거의 동시에 항구로 들어서는 자기 연대의 수송선들을 보고는 다시 생기를 되찾았다. 조스는 기진맥진한 채로 여관으로 갔고, 그동안 도빈 대위는 두 숙녀를 호위한 뒤, 조스의 마차와 짐을 배와 세관에서 빼내는 일에 분주히 매달렸다. 지금 조스에게는 하인이 없었기 때문이다. 오즈번의 하인과 그 자신의 어리광받이 하인이 채텀에서 작당하여, 바다를 건너기를 딱 잘라 거부한 것이다. 마지막 날에 느닷없이 터진 이 반란에 어찌나 놀랐던지, 세들리 가의 아들은 이 원정을 단념할 뻔했다. 그러나 도빈 대위가 (조스의 말에 따르면 이 일에 엄청나게 주제넘게 나섰다) 그를 호되게 나무라고 실컷 비웃었다. 콧수염은 이미 미리 길러 놓은 터라, 조스도 결국 배에 오르도록 설득당했다. 영어밖에 할 줄 모르는, 교양 있고 잘 먹은 런던 하인들 대신, 도빈은 조스 일행을 위해 어느 나라 말도 할 줄 모르는 거무튀튀한 자그마한 벨기에 하인을 구해 주었다. 그러나 이자는 부산스러운 처신과, 세들리 씨를 한결같이 \"나리\"라고 부르는 덕분에 금세 그분의 환심을 샀다. 오스텐드는 이제 시절이 달라졌다. 그곳을 찾는 영국인 가운데 나리처럼 보이거나, 우리네 세습 귀족답게 처신하는 이는 거의 없다. 그들 대부분은 행색이 초라하고, 셔츠가 꾀죄죄하며, 당구와 브랜디와 엽궐련과 기름진 정식을 좋아하는 부류로 보인다.\n\n그러나 한 가지 일반론으로 말하건대, 웰링턴 공작의 군대에 속한 영국인이라면 누구나 제 셈은 제가 치렀다. 이런 사실을 떠올리면 분명 가게 주인들의 나라다워진다. 상업을 사랑하는 나라로서는, 이만큼 손님 떼 같은 군대가 몰려오고, 이토록 신용 있는 전사들을 먹이게 된 것은 큰 복이었던 것이다. 그리고 그들이 지키러 온 나라는 군사적인 나라가 아니다. 역사상 오랜 기간 그들은 다른 사람들이 그곳에서 싸우도록 내버려 두었다. 지금 이 글을 쓰는 이가 매서운 눈초리로 워털루 전장을 둘러보러 갔을 때, 우리는 역마차의 마부에게 — 풍채 좋고 전사처럼 생긴 노병이었다 — 그 전투에 참가한 적이 있느냐고 물었다. \"그렇게 멍청하진 않았지요.\" 그 어떤 프랑스인도 인정하려 들지 않을 그런 대답과 그런 정서가 그의 답이었다. 그러나 다른 한편, 우리를 태운 역마차 마부는 자작이었으니, 파산한 어느 제정 시대 장군의 아들로서 길에서 일 페니어치 맥주를 받아 마시는 자였다. 그 교훈인즉 분명 좋은 것이렷다.\n\n이 평평하고 풍요롭고 살기 편한 나라가 1815년 그해 초여름만큼 부유하고 번창해 보인 적은 없었으리라. 그때 그곳의 푸른 들판과 고요한 도시들은 곳곳에 늘어난 붉은 군복으로 활기를 띠었고, 너른 큰길은 화려한 영국 마차들로 북적였으며, 기름진 목초지와 정겹고 예스러운 옛 마을들을 지나, 고목 사이에 자리한 오래된 성관들 곁을 미끄러져 가는 커다란 운하 배들은 죄다 부유한 영국 여행객들로 가득했다. 그때 시골 여관에서 술을 마시는 병사는 마시기만 하는 게 아니라 셈도 치렀으며, 플랑드르 농가에 숙영하게 된 고지인 도널드는 장과 자네트가 건초를 거두러 나간 동안 아기 요람을 흔들어 주었다. 요즘 우리네 화가들이 군사적 소재에 열을 올리고 있으니, 나는 이것을 정직한 영국식 전쟁의 원리를 그려 낼 좋은 화제로 던져 두는 바이다. 모든 것이 하이드 파크의 사열식만큼이나 눈부시고 해롭지 않아 보였다. 그동안 국경 요새의 장막 뒤에 몸을 숨긴 나폴레옹은, 이 모든 질서 정연한 사람들을 광기와 피바다로 몰아넣고 그토록 많은 이들을 쓰러뜨릴 그 발발을 준비하고 있었던 것이다.\n\n모두가 그 지휘관에게 더없는 신뢰를 품고 있었고(웰링턴 공작이 영국 국민 전체에게 불어넣은 그 굳건한 믿음은, 한때 프랑스인들이 나폴레옹을 우러러보던 그 광적인 열광 못지않게 강렬한 것이었다), 나라는 더없이 완벽한 방어 태세를 갖춘 듯 보였으며, 필요할 때 닥쳐올 원군은 그토록 가깝고 압도적이어서, 불안이라는 것은 알려진 바가 없었다. 그리하여 우리 여행객들은 — 그중 둘은 천성이 무척 겁이 많은 부류였지만 — 그 밖의 숱하게 늘어난 영국인 관광객들과 마찬가지로 더할 나위 없이 마음이 편했다. 우리가 그 장교들 가운데 여럿과 안면을 튼 그 유명한 연대는 운하 배편으로 브뤼주와 헨트로 보내졌고, 거기서 브뤼셀까지 행군할 예정이었다. 조스는 공용 배편으로 두 숙녀와 동행했는데, 플랑드르를 여행해 본 옛 여행객이라면 누구나 그 배들이 베푼 호사와 안락함을 기억할 것이 분명하다. 이 굼뜨지만 더없이 안락한 배에서의 먹을거리와 마실 거리가 어찌나 엄청나게 좋았던지, 일주일 일정으로 벨기에에 왔다가 이런 배 한 척에 올라 그곳 음식에 어찌나 흡족했던지 철도가 발명될 때까지 헨트와 브뤼주 사이를 끝없이 오갔고, 그 정기선의 마지막 운항 길에서 스스로 몸을 던져 익사했다는 어느 영국인 여행객의 전설이 전해 내려온다. 조스의 죽음은 이런 종류는 아닐 터였으나, 그의 안락함은 그야말로 지극했고, 오다우드 부인은 그의 행복을 온전케 하는 데 모자란 것이라곤 제 누이동생 글로비나뿐이라고 우겨 댔다. 그는 온종일 선실 지붕 위에 앉아 플랑드르 맥주를 마시고, 제 하인 이지도르를 큰 소리로 불러 대며, 두 숙녀에게 호기롭게 말을 건넸다.\n\n그의 용기는 가히 굉장했다. \"보니가 우리를 공격한다고!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"내 사랑하는 누이야, 가엾은 우리 에미, 겁먹을 것 없다. 위험 따윈 없어. 내 장담하건대 두 달 안에 동맹군이 파리에 입성할 거다. 그러면 내가 너를 팔레 루아얄로 데려가 식사를 시켜 주마, 맹세코! 내 너한테 말하지만, 지금 삼십만 명의 러시아군이 마인츠와 라인강을 거쳐 프랑스로 들어서고 있어. 비트겐슈타인과 바클라이 드 톨리 휘하의 삼십만이란 말이다, 가엾은 내 누이야. 너는 군사 일을 모르지, 얘야. 나는 알아. 내 단언컨대 프랑스에는 러시아 보병에 맞설 보병이 없고, 보니의 장군들 가운데 비트겐슈타인의 발끝에라도 미칠 자가 없다. 그다음엔 오스트리아군이 있지, 어림잡아도 오십만은 되는데, 지금쯤이면 슈바르첸베르크와 카를 대공 휘하로 국경에서 열흘 행군 거리 안에 들어와 있을 거다. 또 용맹한 원수 공작 휘하의 프로이센군도 있지. 뮈라가 사라진 지금, 그만한 기병 지휘관을 어디 한번 대 보라고. 어때요, 오다우드 부인? 여기 우리 작은 아가씨가 겁낼 필요가 있다고 생각하시오? 무슨 두려워할 까닭이라도 있느냐, 이지도르? 어떠냐, 이놈아? 맥주 좀 더 가져와라.\"\n\n오다우드 부인은 자기 \"글로비나는 프랑스인은 말할 것도 없고 살아 있는 그 어떤 사내도 두려워하지 않는다\"고 말하더니, 그 음료에 대한 애정을 드러내는 눈짓과 함께 맥주 한 잔을 쭉 들이켰다.\n\n여러 차례 적과 마주한 적이 있는, 다시 말해 첼트넘과 바스에서 숙녀들과 맞선 적이 있는 우리의 벗 징세관은, 본래의 소심함을 상당 부분 잃어버린 터라, 이제는 특히 술기운으로 무장했을 때면 더없이 입담 좋게 떠들어 댔다. 그는 연대 안에서 제법 인기를 누렸으니, 젊은 장교들을 푸짐하게 대접하고 군인 흉내로 그들을 즐겁게 해 준 덕이었다. 그리고 군대 안에 잘 알려진 한 연대가 염소를 행렬 선두에 세우고 행군하고, 또 다른 연대는 사슴을 앞세워 행군하듯이, 조지는 제 처남에 관해 그의 연대는 코끼리를 앞세우고 행군한다고 말했다.\n\n애밀리아가 연대에 소개된 이래로, 조지는 어쩔 수 없이 그녀를 인사시켜야 했던 몇몇 사람들이 다소 부끄러워지기 시작했다. 그래서 그는 도빈에게 말한 대로(상대방이 이를 얼마나 흡족하게 여겼는지는 말할 것도 없다), 머지않아 좀 더 나은 연대로 전속하여, 그 빌어먹을 천박한 여자들에게서 제 아내를 떼어 내기로 마음먹었다. 그러나 제가 어울리는 무리를 부끄러워하는 이 천박함은 여자들보다 남자들에게 훨씬 흔한 것이며(물론 더없이 대단한 유행계의 귀부인들은 예외로 이런 짓을 즐기지만), 천성이 꾸밈없고 가식 없는 애밀리아 부인에게는 남편이 제 나름의 섬세함으로 착각한 그런 인위적인 수치심이라곤 없었다. 그리하여 오다우드 부인은 모자에 수탉 깃털을 꽂고, 배 위에는 아주 커다란 \"회중시게\"를 늘어뜨려 두고는 무슨 일이 있을 때마다 그것을 울려 대며, 그것이 \"혼인\" 후 \"마차\"에 오를 적에 자기 \"아부지\"가 선물해 준 것이라고 이야기하곤 했다. 이런 장신구들이며 소령 부인의 다른 외양상의 별난 점들은, 제 아내와 소령 부인이 한자리에 마주칠 때면 오즈번 대위에게 견딜 수 없는 고통을 안겨 주었다. 반면 애밀리아는 그 정직한 부인의 별난 점들에 그저 즐거워할 뿐, 그녀와 함께 있는 것을 조금도 부끄러워하지 않았다.\n\n그 후로 거의 모든 중간 계층 영국인이 다녀온 그 유명한 여정을 그들이 밟아 가는 동안, 더 유익한 길동무는 더러 있었을지 몰라도 소령 부인 오다우드보다 더 즐거운 길동무는 좀처럼 없었으리라. \"운하 배 얘기를 하시는데, 글쎄요! 더블린과 밸리나슬로 사이를 오가는 운하 배를 보셔야 하는데 말이에요. 거기야말로 쾌속 여행이라는 게 있고, 또 그 멋진 가축들이라니. 우리 아부지가 글쎄 금메달을 타셨다니까요(각하께서 친히 그 고기 한 점을 잡수시고는 평생 그보다 좋은 고기는 없었다고 하셨대요), 네 살배기 암송아지로 말이에요. 이 나라에선 죽었다 깨어나도 못 볼 그런 놈이지요.\" 그러자 조스가 한숨을 쉬며 인정하기를, \"기름기와 살코기가 정말로 알맞게 섞인 좋은 줄무늬 쇠고기로 말할 것 같으면, 영국만 한 나라가 없지요.\"\n\n\"당신네 최상품 고기가 죄다 나오는 아일랜드만 빼면 그렇지요.\" 소령 부인은 그렇게 말하고는, 자기 나라의 애국자들에게 드물지 않은 일이듯, 제 나라에 크게 유리하도록 비교를 늘어놓기 시작했다. 브뤼주의 시장을 더블린의 시장과 비교한다는 발상은, 비록 본인이 먼저 꺼낸 것이긴 했으나, 그녀로서는 엄청난 경멸과 조롱을 자아내는 것이었다. \"저 시장 꼭대기에 있는 저 낡아 빠진 물건은 대체 무슨 뜻인지 좀 일러 주시면 고맙겠수다.\" 그녀가 그 낡은 탑이라도 무너뜨릴 만한 조롱을 터뜨리며 말했다. 그들이 지나가는 동안 그 도시는 영국 군인들로 가득했다. 아침이면 영국 나팔 소리가 그들을 깨웠고, 해 질 무렵이면 영국 횡적과 북소리에 맞춰 잠자리에 들었다. 온 나라와 유럽이 무장을 갖추고 있었고, 역사상 가장 위대한 사건이 임박해 있었다. 그런데도 그 못지않게 이 일과 관계가 있는 정직한 페기 오다우드는, 밸리나패드며, 글렌멀로니의 마구간에 있는 말들이며, 거기서 마시던 \"포두주\" 이야기를 줄줄이 늘어놓았고, 조스 세들리는 덤덤에서 먹던 카레와 쌀밥 이야기로 끼어들었으며, 애밀리아는 제 남편 생각과, 그를 향한 사랑을 어떻게 가장 잘 보여 줄까 하는 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 마치 이런 것들이야말로 세상의 중대한 화제인 양 말이다.\n\n역사책을 내려놓고서, 실제로 일어난 일이라는 운명적인 사태만 없었더라면 세상에 무슨 일이 일어날 수 있었을까를 추측해 보기를 좋아하는 이들이라면(더없이 골치 아프면서도 재미있고, 기발하고, 유익한 종류의 사색이다), 분명 나폴레옹이 엘바섬에서 돌아와 그 독수리를 산후안만에서 노트르담까지 풀어놓기에 유독 고약한 시기를 골랐구나 하고 속으로 생각해 본 적이 있을 것이다. 우리 편 사가들은 동맹 열강의 군대가 하나같이 천우신조로 전투태세를 갖추고서, 한순간의 통보만으로도 엘바섬의 황제에게 들이닥칠 채비가 되어 있었다고 일러 준다. 빈에 모여서 제 나름의 지혜에 따라 유럽의 왕국들을 나누어 먹던 그 존엄한 협잡꾼들은, 만장일치로 미움받고 두려움받던 그 대상이 돌아오지만 않았더라면, 나폴레옹을 무찔렀던 그 군대들끼리 서로 싸움 붙일 만한 다툼거리를 저희들 사이에 안고 있었던 것이다. 어느 군주는 폴란드를 제 몫으로 가로채고 그것을 지키기로 작정한 터라 전력을 갖춘 군대를 거느리고 있었고, 또 다른 군주는 작센의 절반을 약탈해 놓고는 그 획득물을 지키려 안달이었으며, 이탈리아는 세 번째 군주의 관심사였다. 저마다 상대방의 탐욕에 항의를 늘어놓고 있었으니, 만일 그 코르시카 사람이 이 모든 무리가 서로 멱살을 잡을 때까지 감옥에서 기다리기만 했더라면, 돌아와 아무 방해도 받지 않고 군림할 수 있었으리라. 그러나 그랬다면 우리 이야기와 우리의 모든 벗들은 어찌 되었겠는가? 그 안의 물방울이 죄다 말라 버린다면, 바다는 어찌 되겠는가?\n\n그동안에도 삶을 꾸리고 살아가는 일이며, 특히 즐거움을 좇는 일은, 마치 거기에 끝이라곤 기대할 수 없고 눈앞에 적이라곤 없다는 듯이 이어졌다. 우리 여행객들이 자기네 연대가 주둔하고 있는 브뤼셀에 도착했을 때, 모두가 입을 모아 큰 행운이라 했듯이, 그들은 유럽에서 가장 흥겹고 가장 눈부신 작은 수도 가운데 하나에 발을 들였음을 알게 되었으니, 그곳에는 허영의 시장의 온갖 노점이 더없이 유혹적인 활기와 화려함으로 펼쳐져 있었다. 이곳에는 도박이 흘러넘쳤고 춤판도 무성했으며, 저 대식가 조스를 환희로 가득 채워 줄 잔치가 있었고, 신기에 가까운 카탈라니가 모든 청중을 매혹시키는 극장이 있었으며, 군사적 화려함으로 활기를 띤 멋진 승마길이 있었다. 외국이라곤 한 번도 본 적이 없는 어린 애밀리아의 눈을 즐겁게 하고 매혹적인 놀라움으로 채워 줄, 진기한 옷차림과 경이로운 건축을 자랑하는 보기 드문 옛 도시가 있었다. 그리하여 지금부터 몇 주 동안, 조스와 오즈번이 비용을 댄 멋지고 훌륭한 숙소에서 — 오즈번은 돈주머니가 두둑했고 제 아내에게 다정한 배려를 아끼지 않았다 — 그러니까 그녀의 신혼 기간이 끝나기까지 약 보름 동안, 애밀리아 부인은 영국에서 나온 그 어떤 어린 신부 못지않게 기뻐하고 행복해했다.\n\n이 행복한 시절 내내 날마다 모두에게 새로움과 즐길 거리가 있었다. 구경할 교회가 있는가 하면 화랑이 있었고, 승마가 있는가 하면 오페라가 있었다. 연대의 군악대는 시도 때도 없이 음악을 연주했다. 영국에서 가장 대단한 사람들이 공원을 거닐었으니, 끝없는 군사 축제였던 것이다. 조지는 밤마다 아내를 데리고 새로운 나들이며 잔치에 나서면서, 여느 때처럼 제 자신에게 무척 흡족해하며, 자기가 아주 가정적인 사람이 되어 가고 있노라 큰소리쳤다. 게다가 그와 함께하는 나들이나 잔치라니! 이 작은 가슴을 기쁨으로 뛰게 하기에 그것으로 충분하지 않았겠는가? 이 무렵 그녀가 친정어머니에게 보낸 편지들은 환희와 감사로 가득했다. 남편은 그녀에게 레이스며, 모자며, 보석이며, 온갖 자질구레한 장신구를 사라고 일렀다. 오, 그는 세상에서 가장 다정하고, 가장 훌륭하고, 가장 너그러운 사내였던 것이다!\n\n이 도시로 몰려들어 모든 공공장소에 나타나는, 그 숱한 귀족 나리들과 귀부인들과 유행계 인사들을 보는 것은 조지의 더없이 영국인다운 영혼을 강렬한 환희로 채워 주었다. 그들은 본국에서는 이따금 대단한 이들의 특징이 되는 그 잘난 척하는 냉담함과 거만한 태도를 벗어던지고, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 공공장소에 모습을 드러내며, 거기서 마주치는 나머지 사람들과 황송하게도 섞여 어울렸다. 어느 밤, 조지의 연대가 속한 사단의 장군이 베푼 연회에서, 그는 베리에이커스 경의 딸인 블랜치 시슬우드 영애와 춤추는 영광을 누렸다. 그는 그 두 고귀한 숙녀를 위해 아이스크림과 다과를 가져오려 부산을 떨었고, 베리에이커스 부인의 마차를 잡으려 밀치고 비집고 다녔으며, 집에 돌아와서는 제 아버지조차 능가하지 못할 만큼 그 백작 부인 자랑을 늘어놓았다. 이튿날 그는 그 숙녀들을 방문했고, 공원에서 그들 곁에서 말을 달렸으며, 어느 요릿집의 성대한 만찬에 그 일행을 초대하고는, 그들이 오겠노라 응하자 기뻐 어쩔 줄을 몰라 했다. 자존심이라곤 별로 없고 식욕만 왕성한 늙은 베리에이커스는 만찬이라면 어디든 갈 위인이었다.\n\n\"우리 일행 말고 다른 여자들은 없으면 좋겠구나.\" 베리에이커스 부인은 너무 성급하게 응낙해 버린 그 초대를 곱씹어 본 뒤 말했다.\n\n\"어머나 맙소사, 어머니, 그 사람이 설마 제 아내를 데려오기야 하겠어요.\" 블랜치 영애가 새된 소리로 말했다. 그녀는 바로 전날 밤, 갓 들여온 왈츠를 추며 몇 시간이나 조지의 품에서 나른하게 몸을 맡겼던 터였다. \"남자들은 봐줄 만하지만, 그 여자들이란—\"\n\n\"아내가, 갓 결혼했다는데, 듣자 하니 지독하게 예쁜 여자라더라.\" 늙은 백작이 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 사랑하는 블랜치야,\" 어머니가 말했다. \"아무래도 네 아버지가 가고 싶어 하시니 우리도 가야겠지. 하지만 영국에선 그 사람들과 모르는 척하면 그만 아니겠니.\" 그리하여 본드 거리에서는 그 새 친지들을 모른 척하기로 작정한 채, 이 대단한 사람들은 브뤼셀에서 그의 만찬을 먹으러 갔고, 황송하게도 제 즐거움의 값을 그가 치르게 하면서, 그의 아내를 거북하게 만들고 대화에서 그녀를 용의주도하게 따돌리는 것으로 제 위엄을 과시했다. 이것은 가문 좋은 영국 여성이 단연 으뜸으로 군림하는 종류의 위엄이다. 우아한 귀부인이 자기보다 못하고 미천한 여자들을 대하는 태도를 지켜보는 것은, 허영의 시장을 철학적으로 드나드는 이에게는 더없이 좋은 구경거리다.\n\n정직한 조지가 큰돈을 쓴 이 잔치는, 애밀리아가 신혼 동안 누린 모든 여흥 가운데 가장 음울하기 짝이 없는 것이었다. 그녀는 그 잔치에 관해 더없이 가련한 내용을 친정어머니에게 적어 보냈다. 베리에이커스 백작 부인이 말을 걸어도 대꾸하지 않더라는 것, 블랜치 영애가 외알 안경으로 자기를 빤히 쳐다보더라는 것, 그들의 행태에 도빈 대위가 어찌나 화를 냈는지, 또 백작 나리는 잔치가 파해 자리를 뜨면서 계산서를 보자고 하더니 빌어먹게 형편없는 만찬에 빌어먹게 비싸다고 내뱉었다는 것 따위를 말이다. 그러나 애밀리아가 이 모든 이야기를 전하며 손님들의 무례함과 자신의 당혹스러움에 관해 적어 보냈는데도, 늙은 세들리 부인은 그럼에도 한껏 기뻐하며, 에미의 친구인 베리에이커스 백작 부인 이야기를 어찌나 부지런히 떠벌렸던지, 제 아들이 귀족 나리들과 귀부인들을 대접하고 있다는 소식이 시내에 있는 오즈번의 귀에까지 들어가고 말았다.\n\n현재의 중장 조지 터프토 경, 바스 훈장 이등 기사를 아는 이들, 그리고 사교철이면 거의 매일 볼 수 있듯이, 속을 채워 넣고 코르셋으로 조인 채, 굽 높은 칠피 장화를 신고 비틀거리는 거드름을 떨며 폴 몰 거리를 활보하면서, 지나가는 여인들의 보닛 아래를 곁눈질하거나, 화려한 밤색 말을 타고 공원의 사륜마차들에 추파를 던지는 그를 본 적이 있는 이들이라면 — 현재의 터프토 경을 아는 이들이라면, 저 대담했던 이베리아 반도와 워털루의 장교를 좀처럼 알아보지 못할 것이다. 그는 지금은 곱슬곱슬한 짙은 갈색 머리에 검은 눈썹을 하고 있고, 구레나룻은 더없이 짙은 자줏빛이다. 1815년에 그는 머리숱이 옅고 대머리였으며, 몸피와 팔다리도 더 굵었는데, 특히 팔다리는 근래에 들어 무척 쪼그라들었다. 그가 일흔 살쯤 되었을 때(지금은 여든에 가깝다), 무척 성기고 새하얗던 그의 머리카락이 별안간 숱 많고 갈색이며 곱슬곱슬하게 자라났고, 구레나룻과 눈썹도 지금의 색을 띠게 되었다. 심술궂은 사람들은 그의 가슴팍이 죄다 양털이고, 그의 머리카락은 도무지 자라는 법이 없으니 가발이라고들 한다. 여러 해 전에 그 아비와 다툰 적이 있는 톰 터프토는, 프랑스 극장의 마드무아젤 드 제지가 분장실에서 자기 할아버지의 머리카락을 잡아 뽑았다고 단언한다. 그러나 톰은 악명 높게 심술궂고 시샘이 많은 자이니, 그 장군의 가발은 우리 이야기와는 아무 상관이 없다.\n\n어느 날, 우리 ××연대의 벗들 몇몇이 브뤼셀의 꽃시장을 거닐고 있었다. 시청을 구경하고 난 참이었는데, 소령 부인 오다우드는 그것이 자기 \"아부지\"의 글렌멀로니 저택만큼 크지도 멋지지도 않다고 단언했다. 그때 뒤에 전령을 거느린 고위 장교 한 사람이 시장으로 말을 몰고 와 말에서 내리더니, 꽃들 사이로 들어와 돈으로 살 수 있는 가장 멋진 꽃다발을 골랐다. 그 아름다운 꽃다발을 종이에 싸자, 장교는 다시 말에 올라 그 꽃다발을 자기 군속 마부에게 맡겼고, 마부는 그것을 히죽거리며 받아 들고, 한껏 위풍당당하게 자기만족에 차서 말을 몰아 떠나는 상관을 뒤따랐다.\n\n\"글렌멀로니의 꽃들을 보셔야 하는데 말이에요.\" 오다우드 부인이 말하고 있었다. \"우리 아부지는 스코틀랜드 정원사를 셋이나 두셨는데, 보조 일꾼이 아홉이라니까요. 우리 집엔 온실이 일 에이커나 되고, 파인애플이 제철엔 완두콩만큼이나 흔하답니다. 우리 집 포도는 한 송이가 죄다 육 파운드씩 나가고, 제 명예와 양심을 걸고 말씀드리는데 우리 집 목련은 주전자만 한 것 같아요.\"\n\n저 못된 오즈번이 즐겨 하듯 오다우드 부인을 \"부추겨\" 떠벌리게 만드는 짓이라곤 결코 하지 않던(애밀리아는 그럴 때마다 질겁하며 제발 그러지 말라고 오즈번에게 애원했다) 도빈은, 안전한 거리에 이를 때까지 컥컥거리고 푸푸대며 사람들 틈으로 물러나더니, 마침내 그 거리에 다다르자 놀란 시장 사람들 한복판에서 째지는 듯한 웃음을 폭발시켰다.\n\n\"저 멀대 같은 사람은 무얼 저리 컬컬대는 거예요?\" 오다우드 부인이 말했다. \"코피라도 터졌나? 늘 코피가 났다고 우기더니, 이젠 몸속 피를 죄다 펌프질해 뺐을 텐데 말이지. 글렌멀로니의 목련이 주전자만큼 크지 않수, 오다우드?\"\n\n\"암, 그렇고말고, 그보다 더 크지, 페기.\" 소령이 말했다. 그때 대화는 앞서 말한 대로 그 꽃다발을 산 장교의 등장으로 끊겼다.\n\n\"기막히게 멋진 말인데, 누구지요?\" 조지가 물었다.\n\n\"우리 오빠 멀로이 멀로니의 말 몰래시스를 보셔야 하는데, 커러에서 우승컵을 탔다니까요.\" 소령 부인이 외치면서 집안 내력을 계속 늘어놓으려 하는데, 남편이 이렇게 말하며 그녀를 가로막았다.\n\n\"저분은 ××기병 사단을 지휘하시는 터프토 장군이오.\" 그러더니 조용히 덧붙였다. \"그분과 나는 탈라베라에서 같은 다리에 총을 맞았지.\"\n\n\"그 덕에 당신이 진급했지요.\" 조지가 웃으며 말했다. \"터프토 장군이라! 그렇다면, 여보, 크롤리 부부가 온 거로군.\"\n\n애밀리아는 가슴이 내려앉았다 — 왜 그런지는 알 수 없었다. 햇살이 그리 밝게 빛나는 것 같지 않았다. 비록 눈부신 일몰이요, 오월 끝자락의 가장 밝고 아름다운 날들 가운데 하루였는데도, 높다란 옛 지붕들과 박공들이 별안간 한결 운치 없어 보였다.\n\n조스는 자신의 무개마차를 끌 말 두 마리를 빌렸는데, 그 말과 런던에서 가져온 맵시 좋은 마차 덕에 브뤼셀 인근을 드라이브할 때면 제법 그럴듯한 행색이 되었다. 조지는 자신이 직접 탈 말 한 필을 샀고, 그와 도빈 대위는 조스와 그 누이가 날마다 즐기는 유람에 나설 때 그 마차를 따라나서곤 했다. 그날 그들은 늘 하던 대로 공원으로 기분 전환을 나갔는데, 거기서 로던 크롤리 부부의 도착에 관한 조지의 말이 어김없이 사실로 드러났다. 브뤼셀에서도 가장 지체 높은 인사들로 이루어진 한 무리의 기마행렬 한가운데, 더없이 어여쁘고 몸에 꼭 맞는 승마복 차림의 레베카가 보였다. 그녀는 아름다운 작은 아라비아 말에 올라 더할 나위 없이 능숙하게 말을 몰았는데(이 재주는 퀸스크롤리에서 익힌 것으로, 그곳에서 준남작과 피트 씨, 그리고 로던 본인까지 그녀에게 숱한 가르침을 베풀었던 것이다), 그 옆에는 늠름한 터프토 장군이 있었다.\n\n\"세상에, 저분이 바로 공작님이세요.\" 오다우드 소령 부인이 조스에게 외치자, 조스는 격하게 얼굴을 붉히기 시작했다. \"저기 밤색 말 위에 계신 분이 억스브리지 경이고요. 어쩜 저리 우아하실까! 우리 오라버니 몰로이 말로니가 저분을 두 콩쪽처럼 빼닮았답니다.\"\n\n레베카는 마차 쪽으로 다가오지는 않았다. 그러나 거기에 옛 지인인 애밀리아가 앉아 있는 것을 알아보자마자, 우아하게 고개를 끄덕이고 미소 지으며, 마차 쪽으로 손끝에 입을 맞춰 장난스럽게 흔들어 보이는 것으로 그 존재를 알은체했다. 그러고는 다시 터프토 장군과 이야기를 이어갔는데, 장군이 \"저 금줄 두른 모자를 쓴 뚱뚱한 장교는 누구냐?\"고 묻자, 베키는 \"동인도 회사에 복무하는 장교\"라고 대답했다. 그런데 로던 크롤리가 자기 일행의 대열에서 빠져나와 다가오더니 애밀리아와 다정하게 악수를 나누고는, 조스에게 \"어이, 친구, 잘 지냈나?\" 하고 말했다. 그러고는 오다우드 부인의 얼굴과 검은 수탉 깃털을 어찌나 빤히 쳐다보던지, 부인은 자기가 그를 사로잡았다고 생각하기 시작했다.\n\n뒤처져 있던 조지는 도빈과 함께 거의 곧바로 말을 몰고 다가와, 그 지체 높은 인사들에게 모자를 들어 인사했는데, 오즈번은 그 무리 가운데서 단번에 크롤리 부인을 알아보았다. 그는 로던이 자기 마차에 친근하게 기대어 애밀리아와 이야기하는 모습을 보고 기뻐했고, 부관의 정겨운 인사에 그에 못지않은, 아니 그 이상의 온정으로 응했다. 로던과 도빈이 주고받은 목례는 예의의 표본 가운데서도 더없이 희미한 축에 드는 것이었다.\n\n크롤리는 자기들이 터프토 장군과 함께 파르크 호텔에 묵고 있다고 조지에게 일러주었고, 조지는 친구더러 어서 오즈번 본인의 거처로 와달라고 약조를 받아냈다. \"사흘 전에 자넬 못 만난 게 아쉽군.\" 조지가 말했다. \"식당에서 만찬을 했거든. 제법 괜찮은 자리였어. 베어에이커스 경과 백작부인, 블랜치 영애까지 황송하게도 우리와 함께 식사해주셨지. 자네도 있었더라면 좋았을 텐데.\" 이렇게 자기가 사교계 인사임을 친구에게 알린 다음, 오즈번은 로던과 헤어졌다. 로던은 그 지체 높은 기마대를 따라 어느 샛길로 빠르게 말을 몰아 사라졌고, 한편 조지와 도빈은 다시 애밀리아의 마차 양옆에 한 사람씩 자리를 잡았다.\n\n\"공작님이 어쩜 그리 멋져 보이시던지.\" 오다우드 부인이 한마디 했다. \"웰즐리 가문과 말로니 가문은 친척간이랍니다. 하지만 물론 보잘것없는 저야, 공작 각하께서 우리 집안의 인연을 굳이 기억해주시는 게 마땅하다 여기지 않으시는 한, 감히 제 소개를 들이밀 꿈도 못 꾸지요.\"\n\n\"대단한 군인이지요.\" 조스가 말했다. 그 위대한 인물이 떠나고 나니 한결 마음이 편해진 것이었다. \"살라망카 같은 승전이 또 어디 있었겠소? 어떻소, 도빈? 그런데 그분이 그 병법을 어디서 익혔겠소? 인도라네, 이 사람아! 정글이야말로 장군을 길러내는 학교지, 내 말 명심하시오. 나도 그분을 직접 알았다오, 오다우드 부인. 우리 둘이 같은 날 저녁에 컷틀러 양과 춤을 췄지요. 포병대 컷틀러의 딸인데, 덤덤에서였소, 기막히게 예쁜 아가씨였지요.\"\n\n그 지체 높은 인사들이 나타난 일은 드라이브 내내, 또 만찬 자리에서, 그리고 다들 오페라 극장으로 떠날 시간이 될 때까지 그들의 이야깃거리가 되었다.\n\n거의 옛 잉글랜드에 온 것이나 다름없었다. 극장은 낯익은 영국인 얼굴들로 가득했고, 영국 여인들이 오래도록 명성을 떨쳐온 그 차림새들로 그득했다. 오다우드 부인의 차림 또한 그 가운데서 결코 덜 화려한 축이 아니었으니, 이마에는 곱슬머리 한 가닥을 늘어뜨리고, 아일랜드 다이아몬드며 케언곰 보석을 한 벌 갖춰 둘렀는데, 그녀의 생각으로는 극장 안 그 어떤 장신구도 빛이 바랠 지경이었다. 그녀가 동석하면 오즈번은 늘 진땀을 흘렸지만, 젊은 벗들이 마음먹은 유흥거리라는 소문만 들으면 부인은 어디든 따라나서고야 말았다. 그들이 자신과 동행하면서 황홀해하지 않을 수도 있다는 생각은 그녀의 머릿속에 떠오른 적조차 없었던 것이다.\n\n\"저 여자가 자네한테 쓸모는 있었지.\" 조지가 아내에게 말했다. 그는 아내에게 이런 동무가 있으면 한결 거리낌 없이 혼자 둘 수 있었던 것이다. \"하지만 레베카가 와줘서 얼마나 다행인지 몰라. 자네한테 벗이 생긴 거니, 이젠 저 빌어먹을 아일랜드 여편네를 떼어버릴 수 있겠어.\" 이 말에 애밀리아는 그렇다고도 아니라고도 대답하지 않았다. 그녀가 무슨 생각을 하고 있었는지를 우리가 어찌 알겠는가?\n\n브뤼셀 오페라 극장의 전경은 오다우드 부인이 보기에 더블린 피섐블 거리의 극장만큼 훌륭하지는 않았고, 프랑스 음악 역시 그녀의 생각으로는 제 고향의 가락에 결코 견줄 바가 못 되었다. 그녀는 이런저런 견해를 더없이 큰 목소리로 벗들에게 들려주었고, 자기가 자랑삼아 든 요란하게 달그락거리는 부채를 더없이 만족스러운 기색으로 휘둘러댔다.\n\n\"애밀리아 곁에 있는 저 굉장한 여자는 누구예요, 로던, 여보?\" 맞은편 칸막이석에 앉은 한 부인이 말했다(이 부인은 단둘이 있을 때는 거의 늘 남편에게 깍듯하더니, 사람들 앞에서는 어느 때보다도 그에게 살가웠다).\n\n\"터번에 노란 것을 달고, 빨간 새틴 가운을 입고, 커다란 회중시계를 찬 저 여자 안 보이세요?\"\n\n\"흰옷 입은 어여쁜 자그마한 여인 옆에 있는 사람 말이오?\" 묻는 부인 곁에 앉은 중년의 신사가 물었다. 단춧구멍에는 훈장을 달고, 조끼를 여러 겹 껴입고, 숨이 막힐 듯 큼직한 흰 스톡을 두른 사람이었다.\n\n\"흰옷 입은 저 어여쁜 여인이 애밀리아예요, 장군님. 장군님은 어여쁜 여자들을 죄다 눈여겨보시는군요, 이 짓궂은 양반 같으니.\"\n\n\"맹세컨대, 세상에 오직 한 사람뿐이라오!\" 장군이 흐뭇해하며 말하자, 부인은 손에 든 커다란 꽃다발로 그를 톡 쳤다.\n\n\"세상에, 저 사람이 맞네요.\" 오다우드 부인이 말했다. \"그리고 저게 바로 그 사람이 마르셰 오 플뢰르에서 산 그 꽃다발이고요!\" 그러고는 레베카가 벗의 눈길을 알아채고 다시 한 번 손에 입을 맞추는 그 자그마한 동작을 해 보이자, 오다우드 소령 부인은 그 인사가 자기에게 보낸 것이라 여기고 우아한 미소로 답례를 했는데, 그 바람에 가엾은 도빈은 또 한 번 비명을 지르며 칸막이석에서 달아나버렸다.\n\n막이 끝나자 조지는 순식간에 칸막이석을 빠져나가, 레베카의 좌석으로 찾아가 인사를 올릴 참이었다. 그러나 로비에서 크롤리와 마주쳤고, 거기서 두 사람은 지난 보름간의 일들에 관해 몇 마디를 주고받았다.\n\n\"대리인한테서 내 수표 제대로 받았겠지?\" 조지가 짐짓 아는 체하는 투로 말했다.\n\n\"제대로 받았네, 이 사람아.\" 로던이 대답했다. \"기꺼이 자네한테 설욕할 기회를 주지. 영감은 마음 좀 돌렸나?\"\n\n\"아직은.\" 조지가 말했다. \"하지만 곧 그러실 거야. 그리고 알다시피 어머니 쪽으로 물려받은 내 개인 재산이 좀 있으니까. 고모님은 누그러지셨나?\"\n\n\"이십 파운드 부쳐주셨지, 빌어먹을 노랭이 같으니. 언제 한판 붙을까? 장군이 화요일에 밖에서 식사하거든. 화요일에 못 오나? 참, 세들리한테 콧수염 좀 깎으라고 하게. 도대체 민간인이 콧수염에다 그 빌어먹을 장식 단추까지 달고 무슨 짓이람! 잘 가게. 화요일에 꼭 와보게.\" 그러고는 로던은 자기처럼 어느 장군의 참모로 있는 두 명의 화려한 젊은 멋쟁이 신사들과 함께 자리를 떴다.\n\n조지는 하필 장군이 식사 자리에 없는 그날 만찬에 초대받은 것이 절반밖에 달갑지 않았다. \"들어가서 자네 부인께 인사 좀 드려야겠네.\" 그가 말하자, 로던은 \"흠, 좋을 대로 하게\" 하고 무척 시무룩한 표정으로 대답했고, 그 말에 두 젊은 장교는 의미심장한 눈길을 주고받았다. 조지는 그들과 헤어져 로비를 따라 으스대며 걸어가 장군의 칸막이석으로 향했는데, 그 번호는 미리 꼼꼼히 세어둔 터였다.\n\n\"들어오세요.\" 맑고 자그마한 목소리가 들렸고, 우리의 친구는 어느새 레베카 앞에 서 있었다. 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 두 손을 마주치고는, 조지를 보고 어찌나 반가워하던지 두 손을 다 내밀었다. 단춧구멍에 훈장을 단 장군은 새로 온 사람을 시무룩하게 노려보았는데, 그 표정이 마치 \"넌 도대체 누구냐?\" 하고 묻는 듯했다.\n\n\"나의 조지 대위님!\" 작은 레베카가 황홀해하며 외쳤다. \"와주시다니 얼마나 친절하신지. 장군님하고 저는 단둘이 마주 앉아 따분해하던 참이었어요. 장군님, 이분이 제가 말씀드렸던 그 조지 대위님이세요.\"\n\n\"그렇소이까.\" 장군이 아주 짧게 고개를 까딱하며 말했다. \"조지 대위는 어느 연대 소속이오?\"\n\n조지는 ××연대라고 말했다. 정예 기병대라고 말할 수 있었더라면 얼마나 좋았을까 싶었다.\n\n\"근래에 서인도제도에서 돌아왔다지요, 아마. 지난 전쟁에선 실전 경험을 별로 못 했고. 여기 주둔 중이오, 조지 대위?\" 장군은 사람 죽일 듯한 거만함으로 말을 이어갔다.\n\n\"조지 대위가 아니에요, 이 멍청한 양반아. 오즈번 대위지요.\" 레베카가 말했다. 그러는 내내 장군은 두 사람을 험상궂게 번갈아 노려보고 있었다.\n\n\"오즈번 대위라, 그렇군! 혹시 L가의 오즈번 집안과 친척이오?\"\n\n\"같은 문장(紋章)을 쓰지요.\" 조지가 말했는데, 실제로도 그러했다. 오즈번 씨가 십오 년 전 자기 마차를 처음 갖출 때 롱에이커의 문장관과 상의하여 귀족 명감에서 L가의 문장을 골라냈던 것이다. 장군은 이 발언에 아무 대꾸도 하지 않고, 오페라글라스를 집어들더니(겹렌즈 오페라 안경은 그 시절엔 아직 발명되지 않았다) 극장 안을 살피는 척했다. 그러나 레베카는 그의 한쪽 자유로운 눈이 자기 쪽을 향해 돌아오며, 자기와 조지를 향해 핏발 선 눈길을 쏘아대는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n그녀는 갑절로 살갑게 굴었다. \"사랑스러운 애밀리아는 어떻게 지내나요? 굳이 물을 필요도 없겠네요, 어쩜 저리 어여쁠까! 그리고 애밀리아 옆에 있는 저 마음씨 좋아 보이는 멋진 분은 누구예요? 대위님의 연인인가요? 아유, 이 못된 양반들 같으니! 어머, 정말로 저기 세들리 씨가 아이스크림을 드시고 있네요. 어찌나 맛있게 드시는지! 장군님, 우리는 어째서 아이스크림이 없는 거죠?\"\n\n\"내가 가서 좀 가져다드릴까?\" 장군이 화를 못 이겨 터질 듯한 기색으로 말했다.\n\n\"제가 가겠습니다, 부디.\" 조지가 말했다.\n\n\"아니요, 제가 애밀리아의 칸막이석으로 갈래요. 사랑스럽고 어여쁜 애밀리아! 팔을 좀 빌려주세요, 조지 대위님.\" 이렇게 말하고 장군에게 고개를 까딱한 다음, 그녀는 사뿐사뿐 로비로 나섰다. 둘만 남게 되자 그녀는 조지에게 더없이 묘하고 의미심장한 눈길을 던졌는데, 그 눈길은 \"이 판국이 어떻게 돌아가는지, 내가 저 사람을 어떻게 바보로 만들고 있는지 안 보이세요?\" 하는 뜻으로 풀이될 만한 것이었다. 그러나 그는 그것을 알아채지 못했다. 그는 자기 자신의 속셈에 골몰해 있었고, 거역할 수 없는 자신의 매력에 도취되어 우쭐한 자아도취에 빠져 있었다.\n\n레베카와 그녀의 정복자가 자리를 뜨자마자 장군이 낮은 소리로 내뱉은 저주들은 어찌나 지독했던지, 그것을 글로 옮겨놓는다 한들 어떤 식자공도 감히 활자로 박으려 들지 않을 것이라 나는 확신한다. 그것은 장군의 가슴 밑바닥에서 우러나온 것이었으니, 인간의 마음이 그런 산물을 빚어낼 수 있다는 것, 또 필요에 따라 그토록 많은 욕정과 분노, 격노와 증오를 쏟아낼 수 있다는 것은 참으로 놀라운 일이다.\n\n애밀리아의 온순한 두 눈 또한 그 한 쌍을 근심스레 좇고 있었으니, 그들의 행동거지가 그 질투 어린 장군의 속을 그토록 뒤집어놓았던 것이다. 그러나 레베카가 칸막이석으로 들어서자, 애밀리아는 벗에게 달려가 더없이 다정한 환희를 터뜨렸는데, 그 환희는 장소가 공공연한 곳임에도 불구하고 고스란히 드러나고 말았다. 극장 전체가 보는 앞에서, 적어도 이제 오즈번 일행 쪽을 겨누고 있던 장군의 안경에 환히 보이는 데서, 그녀가 더없이 사랑하는 벗을 끌어안았던 것이다. 로던 부인 또한 더없이 다정한 인사로 조스에게도 알은체를 했다. 그녀는 오다우드 부인의 커다란 케언곰 브로치와 빼어난 아일랜드 다이아몬드를 찬탄하며, 그것들이 골콘다에서 곧장 온 것이 아니라고는 도무지 믿지 못하겠다고 했다. 그녀는 부산스레 움직이고, 재잘대고, 몸을 돌렸다 비틀었다 하며, 이 사람에게 미소 짓고 저 사람에게 생긋거렸는데, 이 모든 것이 맞은편의 질투 어린 오페라글라스가 환히 보는 앞에서 벌어진 일이었다. 그리고 발레가 시작될 시간이 되자(그 발레에 등장하는 어떤 무용수도 그녀만큼 능숙하게 표정 연기를 해내거나 몸짓의 희극을 펼쳐 보이지는 못했다), 그녀는 자기 칸막이석으로 깡충깡충 돌아갔는데, 이번에는 도빈 대위의 팔에 기댄 채였다. 아니, 그녀는 조지의 팔은 마다했다. 그는 남아서 더없이 사랑스럽고 가장 좋은 작은 애밀리아와 이야기를 나누어야 한다는 것이었다.\n\n\"저 여자는 어찌 저리 사기꾼 같은지!\" 정직한 늙은 도빈이 레베카의 칸막이석에서 돌아와 조지에게 중얼거렸다. 그는 더없이 침묵한 채, 장의사처럼 음울한 표정으로 그녀를 그곳까지 데려다준 참이었다. \"꼭 뱀처럼 몸을 비틀고 뒤트는 꼴이라니. 여기 있는 내내, 못 봤나, 조지, 저 여자가 어떻게 저 건너편 장군을 상대로 연기를 하고 있었는지?\"\n\n\"사기꾼이라니, 연기라니! 제기랄, 저 여자는 잉글랜드에서 제일 사랑스러운 작은 여인이야.\" 조지가 하얀 이를 드러내며, 향기로운 구레나룻을 한 번 꼬며 대꾸했다. \"자넨 세상 물정을 모르는 사람이야, 도빈. 빌어먹을, 지금 저 여자를 좀 보게, 순식간에 터프토를 구워삶았잖아. 저 사람 웃는 꼴 좀 보라고! 세상에, 저 어깨선 좀 봐! 에미, 자넨 왜 꽃다발이 없나? 다들 꽃다발을 들었잖아.\"\n\n\"어머나, 그럼 왜 직접 하나 사주지 않으셨나요?\" 오다우드 부인이 말했고, 애밀리아와 윌리엄 도빈은 둘 다 그 시의적절한 한마디에 그녀에게 고마워했다. 그러나 그 이상으로는 두 숙녀 가운데 누구도 기운을 차리지 못했다. 애밀리아는 세속적인 연적의 화려함과 눈부심, 그리고 사교계의 언변에 짓눌리고 말았다. 오다우드 부인조차 베키의 눈부신 출현 이후로는 입을 다물고 풀이 죽어, 저녁 내내 글렌말로니에 관해서는 더 이상 한마디도 거의 하지 않았다.\n\n\"백 년 동안이나 입버릇처럼 약속했으면서, 자넨 대체 언제 노름을 그만둘 셈인가, 조지?\" 오페라를 보던 그날 밤이 지나고 며칠 뒤, 도빈이 친구에게 말했다. \"자넨 대체 언제 설교질을 그만둘 셈인가?\" 상대방의 대답은 그러했다. \"도대체 이 사람아, 뭐가 그리 걱정인가? 우린 판돈 적게 친다고. 어젯밤엔 내가 땄어. 설마 크롤리가 속임수를 쓴다고 생각하는 건 아니겠지? 정정당당히 치면 한 해 끝에 가선 거기서 거기인 거야.\"\n\n\"하지만 그자가 잃으면 갚을 수나 있을지 모르겠군.\" 도빈이 말했다. 그리고 그의 충고는 충고라는 것이 으레 거두는 만큼의 성공을 거두었다. 이제 오즈번과 크롤리는 거듭 어울려 다녔다. 터프토 장군은 거의 늘 밖에서 식사를 했다. 부관 부부가 호텔에서 쓰는 거처(실은 장군의 거처와 아주 가까웠다)에서 조지는 언제나 환영받았다.\n\n조지와 애밀리아가 그 거처로 크롤리 부부를 찾아갔을 때 애밀리아의 태도가 어찌나 거북했던지, 두 사람은 하마터면 첫 부부싸움을 벌일 뻔했다. 그러니까 조지가, 가기 싫어하는 기색이 역력한 데다 옛 벗인 크롤리 부인을 대하는 도도하고 거만한 태도를 두고 아내를 격하게 나무란 것이었다. 한데 애밀리아는 단 한마디도 대꾸하지 않았다. 그러나 남편의 시선이 자기에게 꽂혀 있고, 레베카가 자기를 빤히 살피고 있다고 느껴지는 가운데, 애밀리아는 로던 부인을 두 번째로 방문했을 때 처음 찾아갔을 때보다도, 가능하다면, 한층 더 수줍어하고 어색해했다.\n\n물론 레베카는 갑절로 다정했고, 벗의 냉랭함을 조금도 알은체하지 않았다. \"에미는 아버지 이름이 그… 세들리 씨의 불행 이후로 더 도도해진 것 같아요.\" 레베카가 조지의 귀를 의식해 그 표현을 자비롭게 누그러뜨리며 말했다.\n\n\"맹세컨대, 우리가 브라이턴에 있을 땐 저 사람이 황송하게도 나를 질투하는구나 싶었어요. 그런데 지금은 로던과 나, 그리고 장군이 함께 지낸다고 못마땅해하는 모양이지요. 아니, 이 사람아, 우리 형편에 비용을 나눠 낼 벗이 없으면 도대체 무슨 수로 살아가겠어요? 게다가 로던이 내 명예 하나 지킬 만큼의 위인이 못 된다고 생각하는 거예요? 하지만 에미한테 정말 고맙군요, 정말로.\" 로던 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"흥, 질투라니!\" 조지가 대꾸했다. \"여자들은 죄다 질투쟁이라니까.\"\n\n\"남자들도 다 그렇죠. 오페라를 보던 그날 밤, 대위님은 터프토 장군을 질투하고, 장군은 대위님을 질투하지 않으셨던가요? 아니, 그 양반은 내가 대위님을 따라 그 바보 같은 작은 부인을 찾아갔다고 나를 잡아먹을 기세였다고요. 마치 내가 당신네 둘 중 누구라도 손톱만큼 신경 쓴다는 듯이 말이에요.\" 크롤리의 아내가 톡 쏘듯 고개를 까딱하며 말했다. \"여기서 식사하실래요? 그 용 같은 양반은 총사령관님과 식사한답니다. 큰 소식이 돌고 있어요. 프랑스군이 국경을 넘었다더군요. 우린 조용히 식사할 수 있을 거예요.\"\n\n조지는 아내가 몸이 좀 좋지 않은데도 그 초대를 받아들였다. 이제 두 사람은 결혼한 지 채 여섯 주도 되지 않은 터였다. 다른 여자가 아내를 두고 비웃거나 조롱하고 있는데도, 그는 화를 내지 않았다. 이 마음씨 좋은 사내는 자기 자신에게조차 화를 내지 않았다. 부끄러운 일이지, 하고 그는 속으로 인정했다. 하지만 빌어먹을, 어여쁜 여자가 제 발로 다가오는데, 사내가 어쩌란 말인가? 나는 여자 문제에선 좀 자유로운 편이지, 하고 그는 종종 스터블이나 스푸니를 비롯한 식탁의 다른 전우들에게 미소 지으며 의미심장하게 고개를 끄덕이곤 했다. 그러면 그들은 이 무용담 때문에 그를 깔보기는커녕 오히려 그를 우러러보았다. 전쟁에서 승리하는 것 다음으로, 사랑에서 정복하는 것은 아득한 옛날부터 허영의 시장에 사는 사내들 사이에서 자랑거리의 원천이 되어왔다. 그렇지 않고서야 학교 다니는 사내아이들이 어찌 제 연애담을 떠벌리며, 돈 후안이 어찌 인기를 끌겠는가?\n\n그리하여 오즈번 씨는 스스로가 여자 사냥꾼이며 정복할 운명을 타고났다는 굳은 신념을 품은 채, 제 운명을 거스르기는커녕 더없이 흐뭇한 마음으로 거기에 몸을 내맡겼다. 그리고 에미가 말수가 적은 데다 질투로 그를 들볶지도 않고, 그저 남몰래 불행해하며 비참하게 속을 끓일 뿐이었으므로, 그는 모든 지인이 빤히 알고 있는 사실을, 즉 자기가 크롤리 부인과 필사적으로 시시덕거리고 있다는 사실을 아내가 눈치채지 못한다고 멋대로 믿어버렸다. 그는 그녀가 시간이 날 때면 언제든 함께 말을 탔다. 애밀리아에게는 연대 업무라는 핑계를 댔고(이 거짓말에 그녀가 조금이라도 속아 넘어간 것은 아니었다), 아내를 고독에, 혹은 오라비의 동무에게 맡겨둔 채 저녁이면 크롤리 부부와 어울리며 시간을 보냈다. 남편에게 돈을 잃으면서도, 그 아내가 자기에게 반해 애가 타고 있다고 우쭐대면서 말이다. 이 어엿한 부부가 한쪽은 젊은 신사를 구슬리고 한쪽은 카드로 그의 돈을 따먹자고 말 한마디 한마디로 작당하여 합의했을 리는 십중팔구 없을 것이다. 그러나 그들은 서로를 완벽하게 이해하고 있었으니, 로던은 오즈번이 드나드는 것을 더없이 너그러운 기분으로 내버려두었다.\n\n조지는 새로 사귄 지인들에게 어찌나 빠져 있던지, 그와 윌리엄 도빈은 예전만큼 함께 어울리는 일이 결코 없었다. 조지는 사람들 앞에서나 연대에서나 그를 피했고, 우리가 보다시피, 윗사람이 자기에게 기어이 늘어놓으려 드는 그 설교를 좋아하지 않았다. 조지의 행실 가운데 어떤 부분이 도빈 대위를 몹시 진지하고 냉랭하게 만들었다 한들, 구레나룻은 큼직하고 자기 영민함에 대한 자부심도 대단하지만 실은 학생만큼이나 풋내기라고, 로던이 전에 숱한 이들을 그랬듯 그를 제물 삼고 있으며 일단 부려먹고 나면 경멸하며 내팽개칠 것이라고 조지에게 말해준들 무슨 소용이 있었겠는가? 그는 들으려 하지 않았다. 그래서 도빈은 오즈번의 집을 찾는 날에도 옛 벗을 만날 기회가 좀처럼 없었으니, 두 사람 사이의 고통스럽고도 부질없는 대화가 그만큼 덜어진 셈이었다. 우리의 친구 조지는 허영의 시장의 쾌락을 한껏 누리는 중이었다.\n\n다리우스 시절 이래로, 1815년 저지대 나라들에서 웰링턴 공작의 군대를 에워싸고, 그것을 춤추고 잔치를 벌이게 하며, 이를테면 전투의 벼랑 끝까지 이끌고 간 그토록 화려한 종군자 행렬은 일찍이 없었다. 위에서 말한 그해 6월 15일에 어느 고귀한 공작부인이 브뤼셀에서 연 어떤 무도회는 역사에 남은 일이다. 브뤼셀 전체가 그 일로 들떠 있었으니, 그 무렵 그 도시에 있었던 부인들에게서 내가 들은 바로는, 무도회를 둘러싼 자기네 여성들의 이야기와 관심은 코앞에 닥친 적군에 대한 것보다도 한층 더 컸다고 한다. 입장권을 얻으려는 그 분투며 책략이며 애원은, 자기 나라 지체 높은 인사들의 사교계에 끼어들고자 할 때 오직 영국 부인들만이 부릴 법한 것이었다.\n\n초대받기를 애타게 바라던 조스와 오다우드 부인은 입장권을 구하려 애썼지만 허사였다. 그러나 우리의 다른 벗들은 한결 운이 좋았다. 이를테면 베어에이커스 경의 호의를 통해, 또 식당에서의 그 만찬에 대한 보답으로, 조지는 오즈번 대위 부부 앞으로 된 초대장을 받았고, 그 일로 그는 크게 우쭐해졌다. 도빈은 그들의 연대가 속한 사단을 지휘하는 장군의 벗이었던지라, 어느 날 웃으며 오즈번 부인을 찾아와 비슷한 초대장을 내보였는데, 그 일로 조스는 부러워했고 조지는 도대체 저 친구가 어떻게 사교계에 끼어드는 건지 의아해했다. 마지막으로 로던 부부도 물론 초대받았으니, 어느 기병 여단을 지휘하는 장군의 벗다운 처사였다.\n\n약속된 그날 밤, 조지는 애밀리아를 위해 새 드레스며 온갖 장신구를 마련해주고는, 그 유명한 무도회로 마차를 몰았는데, 정작 거기서 그의 아내는 아는 사람이 단 한 명도 없었다. 베어에이커스 부인을 두리번거리며 찾았으나, 부인은 초대장 하나면 충분하다 여기고 그를 본 척도 하지 않았다. 조지는 애밀리아를 긴 의자에 앉혀두고는, 자기 딴에는 새 옷을 마련해주고 무도회에 데려와 마음껏 즐기도록 해주었으니 참으로 훌륭하게 처신했다고 여기며, 그녀를 거기 혼자만의 상념에 내버려두었다. 그녀의 생각은 그리 유쾌한 것이 못 되었고, 정직한 도빈 말고는 아무도 그 상념을 방해하러 오지 않았다.\n\n그녀의 출현이 완전한 실패였던(이는 그녀의 남편이 일종의 분노 어린 심정으로 느낀 바였다) 반면, 로던 크롤리 부인의 첫 등장은 그와 정반대로 무척이나 눈부셨다. 그녀는 아주 늦게 도착했다. 얼굴은 환히 빛났고, 옷차림은 완벽했다. 모여든 지체 높은 인사들 한가운데서, 오페라글라스들이 일제히 자기에게 향하는 가운데, 레베카는 핑커튼 여사의 어린 소녀들을 줄 세워 교회로 데려가던 시절만큼이나 침착하고 차분해 보였다. 사내들 가운데 다수가 이미 그녀와 안면이 있었고, 멋쟁이들이 그녀 주위로 몰려들었다. 부인들로 말할 것 같으면, 로던이 수녀원에서 그녀를 데리고 달아났다느니, 그녀가 몽모랑시 가문의 친척이라느니 하는 말이 그들 사이에서 수군수군 오갔다. 그녀가 프랑스어를 어찌나 완벽하게 구사하던지 이 소문에는 얼마간 진실이 담겼을지도 모를 일이었고, 그녀의 몸가짐이 우아하고 그 분위기가 기품 있다는 데에는 다들 의견이 일치했다. 한꺼번에 쉰 명은 됨직한 춤 상대 지망자들이 그녀 주위로 몰려들어 그녀와 춤추는 영광을 누리게 해달라고 졸랐다. 그러나 그녀는 이미 약속이 있는 데다 춤은 아주 조금만 출 작정이라고 말하고는, 곧장 에미가 아무도 거들떠보지 않는 채 처량하게 불행해하며 앉아 있는 곳으로 향했다. 그리하여 그 가엾은 아이의 숨통을 단번에 끊어놓으려는 듯, 로던 부인은 달려가 더없이 사랑하는 애밀리아에게 다정하게 인사하고는 곧장 그녀를 깔보듯 챙기기 시작했다. 그녀는 벗의 드레스며 미용사를 흠잡고, 어쩌면 이런 신발을 신었느냐며 의아해하더니, 다음 날 아침 자기 코르셋 재봉사를 꼭 보내주겠노라 다짐했다. 그녀는 정말 즐거운 무도회라고, 누구나 아는 사람은 다 와 있고 방 안 전체에 이름 없는 사람이라곤 아주 극소수뿐이라고 호언했다. 사실 이 젊은 여자는 단 보름 만에, 그것도 사교계에서 만찬을 세 번 한 끝에 그 우아한 은어를 어찌나 잘 익혔던지, 그곳 토박이라도 그보다 더 잘 구사하지는 못할 정도였다. 다만 그녀의 프랑스어가 너무도 훌륭한 탓에, 그녀가 사교계에서 나고 자란 여인이 아니라는 것을 겨우 알아챌 수 있을 뿐이었다.\n\n무도회장에 들어서며 에미를 긴 의자에 두고 갔던 조지는, 레베카가 사랑하는 벗 곁에 다가서자 이내 그쪽으로 돌아오는 길을 찾아냈다. 베키는 마침 오즈번 부인에게 그 남편이 저지르고 있는 어리석은 짓들에 관해 한바탕 일장 훈계를 늘어놓던 참이었다. \"제발 부탁이니 저 양반 노름 좀 말려요, 이 사람아.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"안 그러면 신세 망친다고요. 저이하고 로던이 밤마다 카드를 친단 말이에요. 게다가 알다시피 저이는 형편이 무척 빈한하니, 정신 안 차리면 로던이 저이한테서 한 푼도 남김없이 따먹을 거예요. 어째서 못 말리는 거예요, 이 무심한 사람아? 어째서 저녁이면 그 도빈 대위와 집에 처박혀 시무룩하게 있지 말고 우리한테 안 오는 거죠? 그 양반이 무척 다정한 분이라는 건 인정해요. 하지만 발이 그렇게 큰 남자를 어떻게 사랑할 수 있겠어요? 당신 남편 발은 어찌나 사랑스러운지. 마침 저기 오네요. 어디 있었어요, 못된 양반? 여기 에미가 당신 보고 싶어서 눈이 빠지게 울고 있었어요. 카드리유 추러 나 데리러 오는 거예요?\" 그러고는 꽃다발과 숄을 애밀리아 곁에 내려놓고, 조지와 함께 사뿐사뿐 춤추러 가버렸다. 여자만이 이토록 사람의 가슴을 후벼낼 줄 안다. 그들의 자그마한 화살촉 끝에는 독이 묻어 있어, 사내의 무딘 무기보다 천 배는 더 따끔하게 쏘는 법이다. 평생 누구를 미워해본 적도, 비웃어본 적도 없는 우리의 가엾은 에미는 무자비한 작은 적의 손아귀에서 속수무책이었다.\n\n조지는 레베카와 두세 번 춤을 추었다. 몇 번이나 추었는지 애밀리아는 거의 헤아리지도 못했다. 그녀는 자기 구석 자리에 아무도 거들떠보지 않는 채 앉아 있었는데, 다만 로던이 다가와 어설픈 말 몇 마디를 건넬 때나, 저녁이 깊어 도빈 대위가 용기를 내어 다과를 가져다주고 곁에 앉아줄 때만은 예외였다. 그는 그녀에게 어째서 그리 서글퍼하느냐고 차마 묻지 못했다. 그러나 그녀는 눈에 차오르는 눈물의 핑계로, 조지가 노름을 계속할 거라는 크롤리 부인의 말에 겁이 났다고 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"한 사내가 노름에 빠지면, 어찌나 어설픈 악당들한테까지 제 발로 속아 넘어가주는지 참 묘한 일이지요.\" 도빈이 말했고, 에미는 \"정말 그러네요\" 하고 대답했다. 그녀는 다른 무언가를 생각하고 있었다. 그녀를 슬프게 한 것은 돈을 잃는 일이 아니었다.\n\n마침내 조지가 레베카의 숄과 꽃을 가지러 돌아왔다. 그녀는 떠나려던 참이었다. 그녀는 다시 와서 애밀리아에게 작별 인사를 건네는 수고조차 베풀지 않았다. 그 가엾은 아이는 남편이 오가는 것을 한마디 말도 없이 내버려둔 채, 고개를 가슴께로 떨구었다. 도빈은 어디론가 불려가, 벗인 사단장 장군과 깊숙이 머리를 맞대고 속삭이듯 이야기를 나누느라 이 마지막 작별을 보지 못했다. 그리하여 조지는 꽃다발을 들고 떠났다. 그러나 그 주인에게 그것을 건넬 때, 꽃 사이에 뱀처럼 똬리를 튼 쪽지 한 장이 놓여 있었다. 레베카의 눈은 단번에 그것을 알아챘다. 그녀는 어린 시절부터 쪽지를 다루는 데 이골이 난 사람이었다. 그녀는 손을 내밀어 꽃다발을 받아 들었다. 두 사람의 눈길이 마주치는 순간, 그는 그녀가 거기서 무엇을 찾게 될지 알고 있음을 그 눈빛으로 알아챘다. 그녀의 남편은 그녀를 서둘러 데리고 갔는데, 여전히 제 생각에 너무 골몰한 나머지 자기 벗과 자기 아내 사이에 오갔을 법한 어떤 알은체의 기색도 알아차리지 못하는 듯했다. 하지만 그것은 그저 사소한 것들뿐이었다. 레베카는 늘 짓던 그 빠르고 의미심장한 눈길 가운데 하나를 던지며 조지에게 손을 내밀었고, 무릎을 굽혀 인사한 다음 걸어가버렸다. 조지는 그 손에 머리를 숙였고, 크롤리의 말에 아무 대답도 하지 않았으며, 실은 듣지조차 못했으니, 그의 머릿속은 승리감과 흥분으로 어찌나 욱신거리던지, 그들이 한마디 말도 없이 떠나가도록 내버려두고 말았다.\n\n그의 아내는 적어도 그 꽃다발 장면의 한 대목만큼은 보았다. 레베카의 청을 받고 조지가 그녀의 스카프와 꽃을 가지러 가는 것은 지극히 자연스러운 일이었으니, 지난 며칠 사이에 그가 스무 번은 했던 일에 지나지 않았다. 그러나 이제 그것은 그녀에게 너무도 벅찬 일이었다. \"윌리엄.\" 그녀가 가까이 있던 도빈에게 불쑥 매달리며 말했다. \"당신은 늘 제게 무척 친절하셨어요. 저… 저는 몸이 좋지 않아요. 집에 데려다주세요.\" 그녀는 자기가 그를 세례명으로 부른 줄도 몰랐으니, 조지가 늘 그렇게 부르던 터였다. 그는 그녀와 함께 서둘러 자리를 떴다. 그녀의 숙소는 바로 근처였다. 두 사람은 바깥 인파를 헤치고 나아갔는데, 그곳은 무도회장 안보다도 모든 것이 한층 더 술렁이는 듯했다.\n\n조지는 자기가 자주 드나드는 모임에서 돌아왔을 때 아내가 아직 깨어 있는 것을 보고 두세 번 화를 낸 적이 있었다. 그래서 그녀는 이제 곧장 잠자리에 들었다. 그러나 잠은 오지 않았고, 소란과 덜컹거림과 기마병들이 질주하는 소리가 끊이지 않았는데도, 그녀는 그 어떤 소음도 듣지 못했으니, 잠을 앗아갈 전혀 다른 동요가 그녀에게 있었기 때문이다.\n\n한편 오즈번은 의기양양함에 들떠 노름판으로 가서 미친 듯이 판돈을 걸기 시작했다. 그는 거듭 돈을 땄다. \"오늘 밤엔 뭐든 다 잘 풀리는군.\" 그가 말했다. 그러나 노름판에서의 그 행운조차 그의 들뜬 마음을 가라앉혀주지는 못해, 얼마 뒤 그는 딴 돈을 주머니에 챙겨 넣고 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 간이주점으로 가서 포도주를 몇 잔이나 들이켰다.\n\n거기서 그가 주위 사람들에게 떠벌리며 큰 소리로 웃고 잔뜩 들떠 있을 때, 도빈이 그를 찾아냈다. 그는 친구를 찾으러 카드 탁자들을 둘러보고 온 참이었다. 그의 동무가 얼굴이 불콰하고 흥겨운 만큼이나, 도빈은 창백하고 침통해 보였다.\n\n\"어이, 도브! 와서 한잔하게, 우리 도브! 공작의 포도주는 일품이라네. 더 따라주게, 자네.\" 그러고는 떨리는 잔을 내밀어 술을 청했다.\n\n\"나오게, 조지.\" 도빈이 여전히 침통하게 말했다. \"마시지 말게.\"\n\n\"마시라니! 이만한 게 없는데. 자네도 한잔하고 그 홀쭉한 턱에 불 좀 밝히게, 이 사람아. 자네를 위하여.\"\n\n도빈이 다가가 그에게 무어라 속삭이자, 조지는 흠칫 놀라더니 거칠게 만세를 외치며 잔을 단숨에 비워 탁자에 탁 내려놓고는 친구의 팔에 의지해 황급히 걸어 나갔다. \"적군이 상브르강을 건넜네.\" 윌리엄이 말했다. \"그리고 우리 좌익은 이미 교전 중이야. 어서 나오게. 우린 세 시간 안에 진군해야 해.\"\n\n조지는 자리를 떴다. 그토록 오래 기다려왔으나 막상 닥치니 그토록 갑작스러운 그 소식에, 그의 신경은 흥분으로 떨렸다. 이제 사랑이며 밀회 따위가 다 무엇이란 말인가? 그는 거처로 빠르게 걸어가는 동안 그것 말고 천 가지 일들을 떠올렸다. 지나온 삶과 앞날의 가망, 자기 앞에 놓였을지 모를 운명, 그리고 그가 보지도 못한 채 영영 헤어지게 될지 모를 아내와, 어쩌면 아이까지. 아, 그는 그날 밤에 저지른 일을 되돌릴 수만 있다면 얼마나 좋을까 싶었다! 그래서 적어도 떳떳한 양심으로, 그토록 하찮게 여겼던 그 사랑을 품은 다정하고 순진한 사람에게 작별을 고할 수만 있다면 얼마나 좋을까!\n\n그는 짧았던 결혼 생활을 되짚어보았다. 그 몇 주 사이에 그는 자신의 보잘것없는 밑천을 끔찍하게 탕진해버렸다. 얼마나 무모하고 분별없이 굴었던가! 자기에게 무슨 변고라도 닥친다면, 그땐 그녀에게 무엇이 남을 것인가? 자기는 얼마나 그녀에게 어울리지 않는 사내인가. 어째서 그녀와 결혼했단 말인가? 그는 결혼할 깜냥이 못 되는 사람이었다. 어째서 그는, 늘 그토록 자기에게 너그러웠던 아버지의 뜻을 거역했단 말인가? 희망과 회한, 야망과 다정함, 그리고 이기적인 후회가 그의 가슴을 가득 채웠다. 그는 자리에 앉아 아버지에게 편지를 썼는데, 언젠가 결투를 앞두었던 시절 자기가 했던 말을 떠올리면서였다. 이 작별의 편지를 다 썼을 무렵, 동이 희미하게 하늘에 줄을 긋고 있었다. 그는 봉인을 하고 겉봉에 입을 맞추었다. 그는 자기가 그 너그러운 아버지를 저버렸던 일을, 또 그 준엄한 노인이 자기에게 베풀었던 수천 가지 친절을 떠올렸다.\n\n그는 들어올 때 애밀리아의 침실을 들여다보았다. 그녀는 고요히 누워 있었고 눈은 감긴 듯했으니, 그는 그녀가 잠들어 있어 다행이라 여겼다. 무도회에서 거처로 돌아왔을 때, 그는 연대의 종졸이 벌써 자기 출발 채비를 하고 있는 것을 발견했다. 그 사내는 조용히 하라는 그의 신호를 알아챘고, 그리하여 이 준비는 무척 빠르고 소리 없이 이루어졌다. 들어가서 애밀리아를 깨워야 할까, 아니면 그녀의 오라비에게 쪽지를 남겨 출발 소식을 그녀에게 전하게 할까, 하고 그는 생각했다. 그는 그녀를 다시 한 번 보려고 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n그가 처음 방에 들어왔을 때 그녀는 깨어 있었으나 눈을 감은 채로 있었으니, 깨어 있다는 사실조차 그를 책망하는 것처럼 보이지 않게 하려는 것이었다. 그러나 그가, 그것도 자기 뒤를 이어 그토록 빨리 돌아오자, 이 겁 많은 작은 가슴은 한결 마음이 놓였고, 그가 발소리를 죽여 방을 나설 때 그를 향해 돌아누우며 그녀는 얕은 잠에 빠졌던 것이다. 조지는 한층 더 발소리를 죽이며 들어와 그녀를 다시 한 번 바라보았다. 창백한 야등 불빛 아래 그는 그녀의 사랑스럽고 창백한 얼굴을 볼 수 있었다. 보랏빛 도는 눈꺼풀은 속눈썹을 드리운 채 감겨 있었고, 매끄럽고 흰 둥근 팔 하나가 이불 밖으로 나와 있었다. 맙소사! 그녀는 얼마나 순결한가. 얼마나 온순하고, 얼마나 다정하며, 또 얼마나 의지가지없는가! 그런데 자기는 얼마나 이기적이고, 잔인하며, 죄로 새카만 사내인가! 마음이 더럽혀지고 수치심에 사로잡힌 채, 그는 침대 발치에 서서 잠든 소녀를 바라보았다. 그가 감히 어찌, 자기 같은 위인이 무엇이라고, 이토록 티 없는 사람을 위해 기도한단 말인가! 그녀에게 신의 가호가 있기를! 신의 가호가 있기를! 그는 침대맡으로 다가가, 잠든 채 놓인 그 손을, 그 자그럽고 보드라운 손을 바라보았다. 그러고는 소리 없이 베개 위로 몸을 숙여 그 온순하고 창백한 얼굴 쪽으로 다가갔다.\n\n그가 몸을 숙이자 어여쁜 두 팔이 다정하게 그의 목을 감아 안았다. \"저 깨어 있어요, 조지.\" 그 가엾은 아이가 말했다. 그토록 가까이 그의 가슴에 안긴 그 작은 가슴이 찢어질 듯한 흐느낌과 함께였다. 그녀는 깨어 있었다, 가엾은 영혼, 그런데 무엇 때문에 깨어 있었던가? 바로 그 순간, 광장 쪽에서 나팔 소리가 맑게 울리기 시작해 온 도시로 퍼져나갔다. 그리고 보병대의 북소리와 스코틀랜드 부대의 째지는 피리 소리 가운데, 도시 전체가 잠에서 깨어났다.\n\n우리는 군대 소설가의 반열에 끼기를 자처하지 않는다. 우리의 자리는 비전투원들 틈이다. 갑판이 전투 채비로 비워지면 우리는 아래로 내려가 얌전히 기다린다. 머리 위에서 용감한 사내들이 펼치는 작전에 방해만 될 뿐이니까. 우리는 ××연대를 따라 시 성문까지만 갈 것이다. 그러고는 오다우드 소령은 그 임무에 맡겨 둔 채, 소령의 부인과 숙녀들과 짐꾸러미에게로 되돌아오기로 한다.\n\n자, 지난 장에서 다른 벗들이 등장했던 그 무도회에 초대받지 못했던 소령과 그 부인은, 의무를 다하면서 동시에 즐거움도 누리고자 했던 이들보다 침대에서 건강하고 자연스러운 휴식을 취할 시간이 훨씬 넉넉했다. \"내 생각엔 말이오, 페기, 여보.\" 그가 잠자리 모자를 두 귀까지 차분히 끌어내리며 말했다. \"하루 이틀 안에 어떤 작자들은 그 가락도 들어 본 적 없는 그런 무도회가 한바탕 벌어질 것 같소.\" 그러고는 그는 다른 어떤 종류의 여흥에 모습을 드러내느니, 조용히 한잔 걸친 뒤 잠자리에 드는 편이 훨씬 행복했다. 페기로 말하자면, 그 무도회에서 터번과 극락조 깃털을 뽐내고 싶었겠지만, 남편이 들려준 소식 때문에 마음이 몹시 무거워졌다.\n\n\"집합 나팔이 울리기 한 삼십 분 전쯤에 날 깨워 주면 좋겠소.\" 소령이 부인에게 말했다. \"한 시 반에 깨워 주오, 페기, 그리고 내 물건들이 준비됐는지 살펴봐 주고. 어쩌면 아침은 먹으러 못 돌아올지도 모르겠소, 오다우드 부인.\" 이 말은 곧 연대가 이튿날 아침 행군하리라는 그의 견해를 뜻하는 것이었고, 그 말과 함께 소령은 말을 그치고 잠이 들었다.\n\n알뜰한 살림꾼 오다우드 부인은, 종이로 만 컬에 속옷 차림으로, 이 중대한 고비에 자신의 의무란 잠자는 것이 아니라 움직이는 것임을 절감했다. \"그건 믹이 떠나고 나서 해도 늦지 않아.\" 그녀는 그렇게 말하고는, 행군에 대비해 그의 여행 가방을 꾸리고, 그의 외투와 군모와 그 밖의 군장을 솔질하여 차곡차곡 챙겨 두었으며, 외투 주머니에는 가볍게 들고 다닐 만한 간식 꾸러미와, 등나무로 감싼 휴대용 술병 하나를 넣어 두었다. 그 병에는 그녀와 소령이 무척 흡족해하는, 한 파인트 가까이 되는 빼어나게 좋은 코냑 브랜디가 담겨 있었다. 그리고 \"기보종\"의 바늘이 한 시 반을 가리키고, 그 내부 장치가(그 가락이 대성당 종소리에 견줄 만하다고 어여쁜 주인은 여겼다) 그 운명의 시각을 울려 알리자마자, 오다우드 부인은 소령을 깨우고, 그날 아침 브뤼셀에서 끓인 그 어떤 것 못지않게 안락한 커피 한 잔을 그를 위해 마련해 두었다. 그러니 이 갸륵한 부인의 채비가, 더 예민한 여인네들이 눈물과 발작으로 제 사랑을 드러내는 것 못지않게 애정을 담은 것이었음을, 또한 도시 곳곳에서 출동 나팔이 울리고 북이 울리는 동안 둘이 함께 마신 그 커피가, 한낱 감상의 토로 따위보다 훨씬 쓸모 있고 요긴한 것이었음을, 누가 부인하겠는가? 그 결과 소령은 사열장에 더없이 단정하고, 생기 넘치고, 기민한 모습으로 나타났으니, 말 위에 앉은 그의 깔끔히 면도한 발그레한 얼굴은 부대 전체에 쾌활함과 자신감을 안겨 주었다. 이 용감한 여인이 서 있던 발코니 앞으로 연대가 행군해 지날 때 모든 장교가 그녀에게 경례했고, 지나가면서 그녀에게 환호를 보냈다. 그리고 그녀가 그 용맹한 ××연대를 몸소 전투로 이끌지 않은 것은, 단언컨대 용기가 부족해서가 아니라 여성으로서의 섬세함과 분별 때문이었으리라.\n\n일요일이나 엄숙한 성격의 시기에는, 오다우드 부인은 숙부인 사제께서 쓰신 설교집 큰 책을 무척 근엄하게 읽곤 했다. 그 책은 서인도제도에서 돌아오던 길에 수송선이 거의 난파될 뻔했을 때 그녀에게 큰 위안이 되어 주었다. 연대가 떠난 뒤 그녀는 묵상을 위해 이 책을 펼쳐 들었다. 어쩌면 읽고 있는 내용을 그리 많이 이해하지 못했고, 마음은 딴 데 가 있었을지도 모른다. 그러나 가엾은 믹의 잠자리 모자가 저기 베개 위에 놓여 있는 마당에, 잠을 자겠다는 계획은 부질없는 것이었다. 세상사가 다 그런 법이다. 잭이든 도널드든 배낭을 어깨에 메고 \"내가 두고 온 여인\"의 가락에 맞추어 경쾌하게 발을 내디디며 영광을 향해 행군해 간다. 남아서 고통받는 이는 그녀다. 그리고 생각하고, 곱씹고, 추억할 여유를 가진 이도 그녀다.\n\n후회란 얼마나 부질없는 것이며, 감상에 빠지는 일이 사람을 더 비참하게 만들 뿐임을 잘 아는 레베카 부인은, 슬픔이라는 헛된 감정에 결코 굴하지 않기로 현명하게 결심하고, 남편과의 작별을 그야말로 스파르타식 평정으로 견뎌 냈다. 사실 로던 대위 본인이 작별의 순간에, 그가 작별을 고하는 그 단호한 작은 여인보다 훨씬 더 감정에 북받쳐 있었다. 그녀는 이 거칠고 투박한 사내를 완전히 휘어잡았던 것이다. 그리고 그는 자신이 지닌 온갖 애모와 흠모의 능력을 다해 그녀를 사랑하고 떠받들었다. 평생토록 그는, 지난 몇 달 동안 아내가 그를 행복하게 해 준 만큼 행복했던 적이 없었다. 경마장이며, 장교 식당이며, 사냥터며, 도박판에서 누렸던 지난날의 온갖 즐거움, 모자 가게 처녀들이며 오페라 무희들이며 하는, 저 어설픈 군인 아도니스가 거두던 손쉬운 승리 따위의 지난 연애와 구애가, 근래 그가 누린 합법적인 부부의 기쁨에 견주면 죄다 시들하기 짝이 없었다. 그녀는 끊임없이 그를 즐겁게 해 줄 줄 알았다. 그래서 그는 어릴 적부터 지금까지 드나든 그 어떤 장소나 모임보다도 제 집과 그녀와 함께하는 시간이 천배는 더 즐겁다는 것을 깨달았다. 그리고 그는 지난날의 어리석음과 방탕을 저주했고, 무엇보다 산더미 같은 미결제 빚을 한탄했으니, 그것이 아내가 세상에서 출세하는 것을 가로막는 영원한 걸림돌로 남으리라는 것이었다. 그는 레베카와 한밤중에 이야기를 나누다가 이 빚들을 두고 자주 신음했다. 총각 시절에는 그것이 한 번도 그를 불안하게 만든 적이 없었는데도 말이다. 그 자신도 이 현상에 적잖이 놀랐다. \"제기랄,\" 그는 말하곤 했다(어쩌면 그 소박한 어휘에서 더 험한 표현을 골라 썼을지도 모른다). \"결혼하기 전엔 무슨 어음에 내 이름을 적든 신경도 안 썼지. 모지스가 좀 기다려 주거나 레비가 석 달 연장만 해 주면, 난 아무 걱정 없이 계속 그러고 살았소. 그런데 결혼하고 나선, 물론 연장하는 거야 빼고, 맹세코 인지 붙은 종이엔 손도 안 댔다오.\"\n\n레베카는 이런 우울한 기분을 마법처럼 떨쳐 버리는 법을 늘 알고 있었다. \"어머, 우리 멍청한 양반.\" 그녀는 말하곤 했다. \"아직 당신 고모님 일이 끝난 게 아니잖아요. 그분이 우릴 저버린다 해도, 당신이 말하던 그 관보라는 게 있지 않아요? 아니, 가만, 당신 숙부 뷰트 씨가 돌아가시면 내게 또 다른 묘안이 있어요. 그 목사 자리는 늘 동생 쪽에 돌아갔으니, 당신이 군적을 팔고 성직에 들면 안 될 게 뭐예요?\" 이렇게 개종한다는 발상에 로던은 폭소를 터뜨렸다. 한밤중에 호텔 전체에 그 폭소가 울려 퍼지고, 이 덩치 큰 용기병의 우렁찬 너털웃음 소리가 들렸을 정도였다. 터프토 장군은 그들 위층인 이 층 자기 숙소에서 그 소리를 들었다. 그리고 레베카는 그 장면을 아주 신명 나게 연기하며, 로던의 첫 설교를 늘어놓아, 아침 식사 자리에서 장군을 더할 나위 없이 즐겁게 했다.\n\n그러나 이것은 한낱 지나간 날들이며 지나간 이야기였다. 전역이 개시되어 부대가 행군하게 되었다는 최종 소식이 전해지자, 로던의 표정이 어찌나 심각해졌던지, 베키가 그 일로 그를 놀려 댔는데, 그것이 이 근위병의 마음을 다소 상하게 했다. \"내가 겁먹었다고 생각하는 건 아니겠지, 베키, 그건 아닐 테고.\" 그가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"하지만 난 총알 맞기 딱 좋은 표적이거든. 만약 그게 날 쓰러뜨리면, 봐, 난 내가 끌어들여 이 곤경에 빠뜨린, 내가 보살펴 주고 싶은 사람을 하나, 어쩌면 둘이나 남기고 가는 거잖아. 그건 어찌 됐든 웃을 일이 아니야, 크롤리 부인.\"\n\n레베카는 갖은 애무와 다정한 말로 상처받은 연인의 마음을 달래려 했다. 이 발랄한 사람은 발랄함과 익살이 도를 넘칠 때라야(사실 인생의 거의 모든 상황에서 그렇기 마련이지만) 풍자를 터뜨렸으나, 이내 다소곳한 표정을 지을 줄도 알았다. \"더없이 사랑하는 양반.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"내가 아무것도 못 느낄 것 같아요?\" 그러고는 황급히 눈가에서 무언가를 훔쳐 내며, 미소 띤 얼굴로 남편의 얼굴을 올려다보았다.\n\n\"이거 봐.\" 그가 말했다. \"만약 내가 쓰러지면, 당신한테 뭐가 남는지 한번 따져 보자고. 여기서 운이 제법 좋았어. 여기 이백삼십 파운드가 있고. 주머니엔 나폴레옹 금화 열 닢이 있어. 그 정도면 내겐 충분하지. 장군이 뭐든 왕자처럼 다 내주니까. 그리고 내가 총에 맞으면, 알다시피 난 돈 한 푼 안 드는 셈이고. 울지 마, 작은 여인, 살아 돌아와서 또 당신 속을 썩일지도 모르잖아. 그래, 내 말 두 마리는 둘 다 안 데려가고 장군의 회색 군마를 탈 거야. 그게 더 싸게 먹히고, 게다가 장군한테는 내 말이 절름발이라고 해 뒀거든. 내가 잘못되면, 그 두 마리가 당신한테 돈푼깨나 만들어 줄 거야. 어제 이 빌어먹을 소식이 오기 전에 그리그가 암말한테 구십을 부르던데, 멍청하게도 난 이백 아래로는 못 넘긴다고 했지. 불핀치는 언제 팔아도 제값을 받을 거야. 다만 이 나라에서 파는 게 나아, 거래상들이 내 어음을 잔뜩 쥐고 있으니까 영국으로 도로 가지 않는 편이 낫거든. 장군이 당신한테 준 작은 암말도 얼마간 값이 나갈 거고, 여긴 런던처럼 빌어먹을 마구간 외상값 같은 것도 없으니까.\" 로던이 웃으며 덧붙였다. \"저 화장 도구 상자는 이백을 줬어. 아니, 그거 값으로 이백 빚을 졌단 말이지. 금 뚜껑이랑 병들은 삼사십은 나갈 거야. 그건 전당포에 맡겨요, 부인, 내 핀이랑 반지랑 시계랑 시곗줄이랑 다 같이. 그것들 값이 어마어마하게 나갔거든. 크롤리 고모가 그 줄이랑 회중시계에 현금으로 백을 냈다는 거 내가 알지. 금 뚜껑에 병이라니, 참! 빌어먹을, 이젠 그때 더 안 챙긴 게 후회되네. 에드워즈가 은도금 장화 벗개를 억지로 떠안겼는데, 은 탕파에다 식기 한 벌까지 갖춘 화장 도구 상자도 받아 둘 수 있었을 텐데. 하지만 가진 걸로 최선을 다해야지, 베키, 알잖아.\"\n\n그리하여 인생의 마지막 몇 달, 사랑이 이 용기병을 완전히 사로잡기 전까지는 자기 자신 말고는 좀처럼 무엇도 생각해 본 적 없던 크롤리 대위는, 마지막 처분을 매기며, 혹여 자신에게 무슨 변고가 닥칠 경우 그것들을 어떻게 아내에게 득이 되도록 돈으로 바꿀 수 있을지 궁리하면서, 자신의 자그마한 재산 목록을 항목별로 하나하나 짚어 나갔다. 그는 그 큼직한 학동 같은 필체로, 미망인이 된 아내에게 득이 되도록 팔 수 있을 만한 휴대 재산의 갖가지 항목을 연필로 적어 내려가며 스스로 흐뭇해했다. 가령 \"맨턴제 쌍열총, 사십 기니쯤; 검은담비 모피 안감을 댄 내 마차용 외투, 오십 파운드; 자단 케이스에 든 내 결투용 권총(마커 대위를 쏜 바로 그것), 이십 파운드; 내 규격 안장 권총집과 마구 덮개; 내 로리제 동품\" 따위로, 이 모든 물품의 주인을 레베카로 정했다.\n\n절약 계획에 충실하게도, 대위는 가장 낡고 허름한 군복과 견장을 걸치고, 가장 새것은 아내(혹은 미망인이 될지도 모를 사람)의 보관에 맡겨 두고 떠났다. 그리하여 윈저와 하이드파크에서 이름난 이 멋쟁이는, 하사관 못지않게 수수한 군장을 갖추고, 두고 떠나는 여인을 위한 기도 비슷한 것을 입가에 머금은 채 전역에 나섰다. 그는 그녀를 바닥에서 들어 올려, 세차게 뛰는 자신의 강인한 가슴에 꼭 끌어안은 채 잠시 품에 안고 있었다. 그녀를 내려놓고 떠날 때 그의 얼굴은 검붉었고 두 눈은 흐릿했다. 그는 장군의 곁에서 말을 몰며, 그들에 앞서 가는 장군 여단의 부대를 서둘러 뒤쫓는 동안 말없이 시가를 피웠다. 그리고 길을 따라 몇 마일을 간 뒤에야 콧수염을 비비 꼬던 손을 멈추고 침묵을 깼다.\n\n그리고 레베카는, 앞서 말했듯이, 남편의 출발에 부질없는 감상에 빠지지 않기로 현명하게 결심했다. 그녀는 창가에서 그에게 작별의 손짓을 보내고, 그가 떠난 뒤에도 잠시 그 자리에 서서 그가 간 쪽을 바라보았다. 대성당의 탑들과 고풍스러운 옛집들의 둥근 박공이 막 해돋이에 발그레 물들기 시작하고 있었다. 그날 밤 그녀에게는 휴식이라곤 없었다. 그녀는 아직도 그 어여쁜 무도회복 차림이었고, 금빛 머리칼은 컬이 다소 풀린 채 목덜미로 흘러내렸으며, 밤을 새운 탓에 두 눈가에는 거뭇한 그늘이 드리워 있었다. \"내 꼴이 영 흉측하잖아.\" 그녀가 거울에 자신을 비추어 보며 말했다. \"그리고 이 분홍색 옷은 사람을 어찌나 창백해 보이게 하는지!\" 그래서 그녀는 이 분홍빛 의상을 벗었는데, 그러는 사이 보디스에서 쪽지 하나가 떨어졌다. 그녀는 미소 지으며 그것을 주워 화장 상자에 넣고 잠가 두었다. 그러고는 무도회에서 받은 꽃다발을 물 잔에 꽂아 두고, 잠자리에 들어 아주 편안하게 잤다.\n\n그녀가 열 시에 깨어나, 그날 아침 사건의 피로와 슬픔 뒤에 더없이 요긴하고 위안이 되는 커피를 마실 때, 도시는 완전히 잠잠했다.\n\n이 식사를 마치고 나서, 그녀는 정직한 로던이 전날 밤에 한 계산을 다시 이어, 자신의 처지를 점검했다. 최악의 사태가 닥친다 해도, 이모저모 따져 보니 그녀의 형편은 꽤 괜찮았다. 남편이 남기고 간 것들에 더해, 그녀 자신의 패물과 혼수도 있었다. 처음 결혼했을 때 로던이 보인 너그러움은 이미 묘사하고 칭송한 바 있다. 이것들 말고도, 또 작은 암말 말고도, 그녀의 노예이자 숭배자인 장군이 그녀에게 아주 값진 선물을 숱하게 안겨 주었으니, 파산한 어느 프랑스 장군 부인의 경매에서 사들인 캐시미어 숄이며, 보석상에서 바쳐 온 무수한 공물 따위로, 이 모두가 이 흠모자의 안목과 재력을 말해 주는 것이었다. 가엾은 로던이 회중시계를 부르던 말마따나 \"딸깍이\"로 말하자면, 그녀의 거처는 그것들의 딸깍거리는 소리로 생기가 넘쳤다. 어느 날 밤 그녀가 무심코, 로던이 준 자기 시계가 영국제라 잘 안 맞는다고 말했더니, 바로 이튿날 아침 르루아라는 상표가 박힌 자그마한 보석 하나가, 터키석으로 매혹적으로 장식한 줄과 뚜껑이 달린 채로 그녀에게 왔고, 또 브레게라는 서명이 든 다른 하나는, 진주로 뒤덮였으면서도 반 크라운 동전보다 겨우 큰 정도였다. 터프토 장군이 하나를 샀고, 오즈번 대위가 다른 하나를 늠름하게 선사한 것이었다. 그런데 오즈번 부인에게는 시계가 없었다. 조지의 명예를 위해 덧붙이자면, 그녀가 달라고만 했으면 하나쯤 가질 수도 있었을 텐데 말이다. 그리고 영국에 있는 명예로운 터프토 부인에게는 어머니에게서 물려받은 낡은 물건이 하나 있었는데, 로던이 말하던 그 탕파 노릇이나 할 만한 것이었다. 만약 하월 앤드 제임스 상회가 자기네가 파는 온갖 패물의 구매자 명단을 공표한다면, 몇몇 가문이 얼마나 놀랄 것인가. 그리고 이 모든 장신구가 신사들의 합법적인 아내와 딸에게로 갔다면, 허영의 시장의 가장 점잖은 가정마다 얼마나 휘황한 보석이 진열되어 있을 것인가!\n\n이 귀중품들을 일일이 계산하고 나서, 레베카 부인은, 짜릿한 승리감과 자족감이 없지 않은 채로, 만약 그런 사태가 닥친다면 새 인생을 시작하는 데 적어도 육칠백 파운드는 셈할 수 있겠다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그리고 그녀는 더없이 흐뭇하게 자기 재산을 처분하고, 정리하고, 골라내고, 챙겨 넣으며 오전을 보냈다. 로던의 수첩 속 쪽지들 가운데에는 오즈번의 거래 은행 앞으로 발행된 이십 파운드짜리 어음이 한 장 있었다. 이 때문에 그녀는 오즈번 부인을 떠올렸다. \"가서 이 어음을 현금으로 바꿔야지.\" 그녀는 말했다. \"그러고 나서 가엾은 어린 에미를 한번 찾아가 봐야겠어.\" 이것이 영웅 없는 소설이라면, 적어도 여주인공만큼은 있다고 주장하자. 행군해 떠난 영국군 가운데 그 누구도, 그 위대한 공작 본인조차도, 의심과 곤경 앞에서 이 불굴의 작은 부관 아내보다 더 냉정하고 침착할 수는 없었을 것이다.\n\n그리고 뒤에 남겨지게 된, 비전투원인 우리 지인이 또 한 사람 있었으니, 그러므로 우리는 그의 감정과 행동을 알 권리가 있다. 바로 보글리 월라의 전직 징세관인 우리 벗이었으니, 그 역시 다른 이들과 마찬가지로 이른 아침 나팔 소리에 휴식이 깨졌다. 잠이 많고 침대를 좋아하는지라, 영국군의 그 모든 북과 나팔과 백파이프에도 아랑곳없이, 평소 늦은 오전에 일어나는 시각까지 계속 졸고 있었을지도 모른다. 한 가지 방해만 없었더라면 말이다. 그런데 그 방해는 조스와 숙소를 함께 쓰던 조지 오즈번에게서 온 것이 아니었으니, 그는 늘 그렇듯 제 일에 너무 골몰해 있거나 아내와 헤어지는 슬픔에 잠겨, 잠든 처남에게 작별을 고할 생각 따위는 하지 못했다. 조스 세들리와 잠 사이에 끼어든 이는, 그러니까 조지가 아니라 도빈 대위였다. 그가 와서 그를 깨우고는, 떠나기 전에 기어이 악수를 하겠다고 고집을 부렸던 것이다.\n\n\"참 친절하시구려.\" 조스가 하품을 하며 말했는데, 속으로는 대위가 지옥에나 가 버렸으면 싶었다.\n\n\"나, 나는 작별 인사도 없이 떠나기가 좀 그래서요, 아시잖소.\" 도빈이 몹시 두서없는 투로 말했다. \"왜냐하면 아시다시피 우리 중 누구는 다시 못 돌아올 수도 있고, 난 여러분 다 잘 지내는 모습을 보고 싶고, 그, 그런 거 말이오, 아시잖소.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이신지?\" 조스가 눈을 비비며 물었다. 대위는 그의 말을 조금도 듣지 못했고, 그토록 애틋한 관심을 보인다던, 잠자리 모자를 쓴 그 뚱뚱한 신사를 쳐다보지도 않았다. 이 위선자는 조지의 방 쪽을 향해 온 힘을 다해 보고 듣느라, 방 안을 성큼성큼 거닐고, 의자들을 넘어뜨리고, 손가락으로 북 치듯 두드리고, 손톱을 물어뜯으며, 속으로 큰 동요를 일으키고 있다는 온갖 기색을 내비치고 있었다.\n\n조스는 늘 대위를 다소 하찮게 여겨 왔는데, 이제 그의 용기마저 어딘가 미심쩍다고 생각하기 시작했다. \"내가 당신을 위해 뭘 해 드리면 되겠소, 도빈?\" 그가 빈정대는 투로 말했다.\n\n\"내 말해 드리리다, 무얼 해 주면 되는지.\" 대위가 침대로 다가오며 대답했다. \"우린 십오 분 후에 행군하오, 세들리, 그리고 조지든 나든 영영 못 돌아올지도 모르오. 명심하시오, 당신은 일이 어찌 돌아가는지 확실히 알기 전까지는 이 도시를 떠나면 안 되오. 여기 남아 누이를 지키고, 위로하고, 누이에게 아무 해도 미치지 않도록 살펴야 하오. 조지에게 무슨 일이 생기면, 그녀가 이 세상에서 기댈 사람은 당신밖에 없다는 걸 기억하시오. 군에 일이 잘못되면, 당신이 누이를 무사히 영국으로 데려가야 하오. 그리고 결코 누이를 저버리지 않겠다고 명예를 걸고 내게 약속해 주시오. 당신이 그러지 않으리란 걸 나도 아오. 돈에 관해서라면, 당신은 늘 후한 편이었으니까. 돈이 좀 필요하오? 그러니까, 혹여 변고가 생겼을 때 영국으로 돌아갈 만한 금화는 충분히 가지고 있소?\"\n\n\"이보시오.\" 조스가 위엄 있게 말했다. \"돈이 필요하면, 어디 가서 청해야 할지는 내가 알고 있소. 그리고 내 누이로 말할 것 같으면, 내가 그 애한테 어떻게 처신해야 하는지는 당신이 일러 줄 필요 없소.\"\n\n\"기개 있는 사내답게 말하는구려, 조스.\" 상대가 너그럽게 대꾸했다. \"조지가 누이를 이렇게 든든한 손에 맡길 수 있게 되어 나도 기쁘오. 그럼 그에게 당신의 명예를 건 약속을 전해도 되겠소, 그러니까 위급한 경우엔 당신이 누이 곁을 지키리란 걸 말이오?\"\n\n\"물론이오, 물론이지요.\" 조스가 대답했는데, 돈 문제에서 그가 보이는 너그러움을 도빈은 아주 정확히 가늠하고 있었다.\n\n\"그리고 패전할 경우엔 누이를 무사히 브뤼셀에서 빼내 주겠지요?\"\n\n\"패전이라니! 빌어먹을, 이보시오, 그건 있을 수 없는 일이오. 날 겁주려 들지 마시오.\" 영웅이 침대에서 외쳤다. 이렇게 조스가 누이에 대한 자신의 처신을 그토록 단호히 밝혀 주자, 도빈의 마음은 그제야 완전히 놓였다. \"적어도,\" 대위는 생각했다. \"최악의 사태가 벌어진다 해도 그녀에게는 피신처 하나는 확보된 셈이군.\"\n\n도빈 대위가 연대가 행군해 떠나기 전에 애밀리아를 한 번 더 보아 무슨 개인적인 위안과 만족을 얻으리라 기대했다면, 그의 이기심은 그런 가증스러운 자기중심성이 응당 받아야 할 만큼 그대로 벌을 받았다. 조스의 침실 문은 가족 일행이 함께 쓰는 거실로 통했고, 이 문 맞은편에 애밀리아의 방문이 있었다. 나팔 소리가 모두를 깨워 놓았으니, 이제 숨길 것도 없었다. 조지의 하인은 이 방에서 짐을 꾸리고 있었고, 오즈번은 옆방 침실을 들락거리며 전역에 가져가기에 적당하다 싶은 물건들을 하인에게 던져 주고 있었다. 이윽고 도빈은 제 마음이 그토록 갈망하던 기회를 얻어, 애밀리아의 얼굴을 다시 한번 보게 되었다. 그러나 그 얼굴이라니! 어찌나 창백하고, 어찌나 넋이 나가고 절망에 짓눌렸던지, 그 모습의 기억이 훗날 죄악처럼 그를 따라다녔고, 그 광경은 이루 말할 수 없는 갈망과 연민의 고통으로 그를 후려쳤다.\n\n그녀는 흰 실내복으로 몸을 감싼 채, 머리칼은 어깨 위로 흘러내렸고, 커다란 두 눈은 빛을 잃고 한곳에 박혀 있었다. 출발 준비를 거들겠다고, 또 이토록 위급한 순간에 자신도 쓸모가 있음을 보이려고, 이 가엾은 사람은 서랍장 위에 놓인 조지의 장식띠를 집어 들고, 그 띠를 손에 쥔 채 그를 이리저리 따라다니며, 그의 짐 꾸리기가 진행되는 것을 말없이 바라보고 있었다. 그녀는 방에서 나와 벽에 기대어 서서, 그 띠를 가슴에 꼭 댄 채 있었는데, 그 가슴에서 진홍빛 그물 장식이 마치 큼직한 핏자국처럼 늘어져 내렸다. 마음씨 고운 우리 대위는 그녀를 바라보며 죄지은 듯한 충격을 느꼈다. \"맙소사,\" 그는 생각했다. \"내가 감히 엿보려 한 것이 바로 이런 슬픔이었단 말인가?\" 그리고 어찌해 볼 도리가 없었다. 이 무력하고 말 없는 비탄을 달래고 위로할 길이 없었다. 그는 잠시 서서 그녀를 바라보았으니, 아파하는 어린아이를 바라보는 어버이처럼, 아무것도 할 수 없는 채 연민에 가슴이 찢어졌다.\n\n마침내 조지가 에미의 손을 잡고 그녀를 침실로 도로 데려갔다가, 혼자서 나왔다. 작별은 그 순간에 이루어졌고, 그는 떠났다.\n\n\"이제 끝났으니 천만다행이군.\" 조지는 칼을 옆구리에 낀 채 계단을 뛰어 내려가며 생각했다. 그는 연대가 집결한 비상소집장으로 재빨리 달려갔는데, 그리로 병사와 장교들이 제 숙영지에서 서둘러 떼 지어 몰려들고 있었다. 그의 맥박은 고동치고 두 뺨은 상기되었다. 전쟁이라는 거대한 승부가 막 펼쳐지려는 참이었고, 그는 그 노름꾼 가운데 하나였다. 의심과 희망과 쾌감의 그 얼마나 격렬한 흥분인가! 잃거나 따는 그 얼마나 어마어마한 도박인가! 그가 여태껏 해 본 그 어떤 도박이 이 한판에 견줄 수 있겠는가? 운동 솜씨와 용기를 요하는 온갖 겨룸에, 이 젊은이는 소년 시절부터 줄곧 온 힘을 다해 뛰어들곤 했다. 학교에서도 연대에서도 으뜸가는 선수였고, 동료들의 갈채가 어디서나 그를 따라다녔다. 소년들의 크리켓 시합에서 수비대 경마에 이르기까지 그는 백 번의 승리를 거두었고, 그가 가는 곳마다 여자들도 남자들도 그를 흠모하고 부러워했다. 사내가 그토록 신속하게 갈채로 보답받는 자질로, 육체의 우월함과 민첩함과 용맹만 한 것이 또 어디 있겠는가? 아득한 옛날부터 힘과 용기는 음유시인과 로맨스의 주제였다. 그리고 트로이 이야기에서 오늘날에 이르기까지, 시는 언제나 군인을 영웅으로 골라 왔다. 사내들이 용맹을 그토록 흠모하고, 군사적 용맹을 다른 모든 자질보다 그토록 높이 보상하고 떠받드는 것은, 혹시 그들이 마음속으로는 겁쟁이이기 때문은 아닐까 하는 생각이 든다.\n\n그리하여 전투를 알리는 그 가슴 뛰는 나팔 소리에, 조지는 자신이 어르며 안겨 있던 그 부드러운 두 팔에서 훌쩍 떨쳐 일어났다. 그토록 오래 그곳에 붙들려 있었다는 데에 일말의 수치심이 없지 않은 채로 말이다(아내가 그를 붙든 힘이라곤 미약하기 그지없었는데도). 우리가 이따금 언뜻언뜻 보아 온 그의 벗들 모두에게도 똑같은 열의와 흥분의 감정이 감돌았으니, 연대를 이끌고 전투에 나서는 노련한 선임 소령부터, 그날 군기를 받들게 된 어린 소위 스터블에 이르기까지 그러했다.\n\n행군이 시작될 무렵 해가 막 떠오르고 있었다. 늠름한 광경이었다. 군악대가 종대의 선두에 서서 연대 행진곡을 연주했다. 이어 지휘를 맡은 소령이 자신의 듬직한 군마 피라무스에 올라타 나아갔다. 그다음 척탄병들이 그 대위를 앞세우고 행군했고, 중앙에는 선임 소위와 후임 소위가 받든 군기가 있었다. 그러고는 조지가 자기 중대의 선두에 서서 행군해 왔다. 그는 위를 올려다보며 애밀리아에게 미소 지어 보이고는 지나갔다. 그리고 음악 소리마저 이윽고 잦아들었다.\n\n그리하여 상급 장교들이 모두 다른 곳으로 차출되어 떠나고 나니, 조스 세들리가 브뤼셀에 남은 이 작은 식민지의 지휘를 맡게 된 셈이었다. 병자가 된 애밀리아, 그의 벨기에인 하인 이지도르, 그리고 이 집안의 온갖 잡일을 도맡은 하녀 본느가 그의 휘하 수비대였다. 도빈의 방해와 그날 아침의 소동으로 마음이 어수선해지고 단잠을 망쳐 버렸음에도, 조스는 그럼에도 평소 일어나는 시각이 될 때까지 여러 시간을 잠 못 이룬 채 자리에 누워 이리저리 뒤척이며 침대에 머물러 있었다. 해가 하늘 높이 솟아오르고, 우리의 용맹한 ××연대 친구들이 행군길을 몇 마일이나 나아간 뒤에야, 이 민간인 신사는 꽃무늬 실내복 차림으로 아침 식사 자리에 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n조지가 자리에 없는 것에 대해서는 그 처남도 마음이 아주 편했다. 어쩌면 조스는 속으로 오스본이 떠난 것이 오히려 흐뭇했을지도 모른다. 조지가 있는 동안 그는 집안에서 한낱 들러리 역할밖에 못 했고, 오스본은 이 뚱뚱한 민간인에 대한 경멸을 거리낌 없이 드러내곤 했기 때문이다. 그러나 에미만은 늘 그에게 다정하고 살뜰했다. 그의 편의를 보살피고, 그가 좋아하는 음식을 챙기고, 그와 함께 산책하거나 마차를 타 주고(조지는 대체 어디 있었던지, 그녀에게는 그럴 기회가 많고도 너무 많았다), 그의 노여움과 남편의 비웃음 사이에 자신의 고운 얼굴을 들이밀어 막아 준 것도 그녀였다. 오빠를 위해 그녀는 몇 번이나 조심스레 조지에게 항변해 보았으나, 조지는 그 직설적인 투로 그 애원을 짧게 잘라 버리곤 했다. \"난 정직한 사람이야.\" 그가 말했다. \"감정이 있으면, 정직한 사람답게 그걸 드러내는 거지. 여보, 대체 자네 오빠 같은 바보한테 나더러 어떻게 깍듯이 대하란 말이오?\" 그러니 조스는 조지가 없는 게 흐뭇했던 것이다. 찬장 위에 놓인 그 수수한 모자와 장갑, 그리고 그 주인이 떠나고 없다는 생각이 조스에게 어떤 은밀한 기쁨의 전율 같은 것을 일으켰다. \"오늘 아침엔 저 친구가 그 멋쟁이 티와 무례함으로 날 성가시게 굴진 않겠군.\" 조스는 그렇게 생각했다.\n\n\"대위의 모자를 대기실로 갖다 놓아라.\" 그가 하인 이지도르에게 말했다.\n\n\"어쩌면 다시는 필요 없으실지도 모르지요.\" 하인이 안다는 듯한 눈빛으로 주인을 바라보며 대답했다. 그 역시 조지를 미워했으니, 자기에게 대하는 조지의 거만함이 영락없이 영국식이었던 것이다.\n\n\"그리고 마님께서 아침 식사 하러 나오실지 여쭤봐라.\" 세들리 씨가 자못 위엄을 부리며 말했다. 하인을 상대로 조지에 대한 자신의 반감을 입에 올리는 게 부끄러웠던 것이다. 사실인즉 그는 이 하인에게 처남 욕을 이미 스무 번도 더 했던 터였다.\n\n아아! 마님은 아침 식사 자리에 나와 조스 씨가 좋아하는 타르틴을 썰어 줄 수가 없었다. 마님은 너무도 심하게 앓고 계셨고, 남편이 떠난 뒤로 줄곧 끔찍한 지경에 빠져 있다고 그녀의 본느가 전했다. 조스는 그녀에게 차를 큰 잔으로 가득 따라 보내는 것으로 동정을 표했다. 그것이 그가 다정함을 드러내는 방식이었다. 게다가 그는 거기서 한술 더 떴다. 아침 식사를 보냈을 뿐 아니라, 저녁상에는 그녀가 무엇을 가장 좋아할지까지 헤아렸던 것이다.\n\n하인 이지도르는, 대위가 떠나기에 앞서 오스본의 하인이 주인의 짐을 챙기는 동안 몹시 시무룩한 얼굴로 지켜보았다. 첫째로 그는 오스본 씨를 미워했는데, 자기를 비롯한 모든 아랫사람을 대하는 오스본의 태도가 대체로 거만했기 때문이다(게다가 대륙의 하인이란, 성정이 더 너그러운 우리네 하인들처럼 무례한 대접을 순순히 견디지 못하는 법이다). 둘째로, 그렇게나 많은 값진 물건들이 자기 손아귀에서 빠져나가, 영국군이 패주하는 날이 오면 마땅히 자기 차지가 되었어야 할 것들이 남의 손에 들어가 버리는 것이 분했다. 이 패배에 관해서라면 그도, 또 브뤼셀과 벨기에의 수많은 사람들도 추호의 의심조차 품지 않았다. 거의 모두가 믿는 바로는, 황제가 프로이센군과 영국군을 갈라놓고 차례로 하나씩 섬멸한 뒤 사흘이 채 지나기 전에 브뤼셀로 진군해 들어오리라는 것이었다. 그때가 되면 죽거나 도망치거나 포로가 될 지금 주인들의 동산은 죄다 적법하게 이지도르 씨의 소유가 될 터였다.\n\n조스가 고되고 복잡한 매일의 몸치장을 마치도록 거드는 동안, 이 충직한 하인은 자기가 지금 주인의 몸을 꾸미는 데 쓰고 있는 바로 그 물건들을 가지고 무엇을 할지 궁리하곤 했다. 은제 향수병과 화장 도구들은 자기가 마음에 둔 어느 아가씨에게 선물할 것이요, 영국제 면도칼과 큼직한 루비 핀은 자기가 챙겨 둘 작정이었다. 멋들어진 주름 셔츠 가운데 하나에 그 핀을 꽂으면 무척이나 근사해 보일 테고, 거기에 금몰을 두른 모자와 자기 몸에 맞게 줄여 입기 쉬운 장식 단추 달린 프록코트, 대위의 금장식 지팡이, 그리고 한 쌍의 아름다운 귀고리로 만들어 둘 작정인 루비 박힌 커다란 겹반지까지 갖추면, 그야말로 자기 자신이 완벽한 아도니스가 되어 렌 양쯤은 손쉬운 먹잇감이 되리라 그는 계산했다. \"저 소매 단추가 내게 얼마나 잘 어울릴까!\" 그는 세들리 씨의 살지고 통통한 손목에 단추 한 쌍을 채우면서 생각했다. \"소매 단추가 정말 갖고 싶어. 그리고 옆방에 있는 놋쇠 박차 달린 대위의 부츠, 코르블뢰! 알레 베르트 거리에서 얼마나 멋들어진 효과를 낼까!\" 그리하여 이지도르 씨가 육신의 손가락으로는 주인의 코를 붙잡고 조스의 얼굴 아랫부분을 면도하는 동안, 그의 상상은 장식 단추 달린 코트에 레이스까지 차려입고 렌 양과 짝을 지어 그린 애비뉴를 거닐고 있었다. 그는 마음속으로 운하 둑을 어슬렁거리며, 시원한 나무 그늘 아래 천천히 미끄러져 가는 거룻배들을 살펴보거나, 라켄으로 가는 길가 맥줏집 벤치에 앉아 파로 한 잔으로 목을 축이고 있었던 것이다.\n* 코르블뢰: \"맙소사\"에 해당하는 프랑스어 감탄사. 파로: 벨기에산 맥주의 일종.\n\n그러나 조지프 세들리 씨는, 다행스럽게도 자신의 마음의 평화를 위해서는, 자기 하인의 마음속에 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 전혀 알지 못했다. 마치 존경하는 독자 여러분도 모르고, 또 우리가 품삯을 주는 존이나 메리가 우리를 어떻게 여기는지 우리도 알지 못하는 것이나 매한가지였다. 우리 하인들이 우리를 어떻게 생각하는지! 만약 우리가 가까운 벗과 정다운 친척들이 우리를 어떻게 여기는지 알게 된다면, 우리는 차라리 떠나고 싶은 세상에서, 도저히 견딜 수 없는 마음가짐과 끊임없는 공포 속에서 살아가게 될 것이다. 그리하여 조스의 하인은 자기 희생양을 점찍어 두고 있었던 것이다. 마치 리든홀 거리에서 페인터 씨의 점원이 영문도 모르는 거북에게 \"내일 수프\"라고 적힌 푯말을 걸어 장식해 두는 것을 보듯이 말이다.\n\n애밀리아의 시중을 드는 이는 훨씬 덜 이기적인 성품이었다. 그 다정하고 온화한 사람 곁에 다가오는 아랫사람이라면, 그 사랑스럽고 정 많은 천성에 으레 바치게 되는 충성과 애정을 바치지 않고는 못 배기는 법이었다. 그리고 사실인즉, 요리사 폴린은 이 비참한 아침에 애밀리아가 만난 그 누구보다도 안주인을 더 많이 위로해 주었다. 행군해 나가는 종대의 마지막 총검이라도 지켜보려고 창가에 자리 잡고 앉은 애밀리아가 몇 시간이고 말없이, 미동도 없이, 초췌한 얼굴로 머물러 있는 것을 보고, 이 정직한 처녀는 마님의 손을 잡고 말했다. \"있잖아요, 마님, 제 남자도 군대에 가 있는 것 아닌가요?\" 그러고는 울음을 터뜨렸고, 애밀리아도 그녀의 품에 안겨 똑같이 울음을 쏟으니, 둘은 서로를 가엾이 여기며 위로했던 것이다.\n\n오전 동안 여러 차례, 조스 씨의 하인 이지도르는 거처에서 시내로 나갔다. 영국 사람들이 모여 있는 파르크 주변의 호텔과 하숙집 문간으로 가서, 다른 하인들과 사자(使者)들과 종복들 틈에 끼어 떠도는 소식을 그러모아, 주인에게 알려 줄 소식 보따리를 들고 돌아왔다. 이들 신사 양반들은 거의 모두가 속으로는 황제의 지지자였고, 이 전역(戰役)이 곧 끝나리라는 것에 저마다 의견이 있었다. 황제가 아베슨에서 내린 포고문은 브뤼셀 곳곳에 넘쳐나게 뿌려져 있었다. \"병사들이여!\" 포고문은 이렇게 말했다. \"오늘은 유럽의 운명을 두 번이나 결정지은 마렝고와 프리틀란트의 기념일이다. 그때, 아우스터리츠 이후나 바그람 이후처럼, 우리는 너무 관대했다. 우리는 그 왕좌에 그대로 머물도록 허락한 군주들의 맹세와 약속을 믿었다. 다시 한번 진군하여 그들을 맞이하자. 우리와 그들이, 우리는 여전히 같은 사람들이 아니던가? 병사들이여! 오늘날 저토록 오만한 바로 그 프로이센군은 예나에서 너희에게 삼 대 일로 맞섰고, 몽미라유에서는 육 대 일이었다. 너희 가운데 영국에 포로로 잡혔던 자들은 영국 감옥선 위에서 얼마나 끔찍한 고초를 겪었는지 동료들에게 말해 줄 수 있으리라. 미친 자들이여! 한순간의 번영이 그들의 눈을 멀게 했으니, 그들이 프랑스로 쳐들어온다면 그것은 거기서 무덤을 찾기 위함이리라!\" 그러나 프랑스 지지자들은 이보다도 더 빠른 황제의 적들의 섬멸을 예언했고, 프로이센군과 영국군은 승리한 군대의 후미에 포로로 끌려오지 않는 한 결코 돌아오지 못하리라는 데에 모두 입을 모았다.\n\n이 의견들이 그날이 가는 동안 세들리 씨에게도 작용을 미치게 되었다. 그는 웰링턴 공작이 전날 밤 완전히 박살 난 자기 군대의 선봉을 다시 추스르려고 떠났다는 말을 들었다.\n\n\"박살이라니, 흥!\" 조스가 말했다. 아침 식사 때라 그의 배짱은 제법 두둑했다. \"공작은 황제를 무찌르러 가신 거야. 예전에 황제의 장군들을 죄다 무찌른 것처럼 말이지.\"\n\n\"황제의 서류는 불태워졌고, 소지품은 옮겨졌으며, 그 거처는 달마티아 공작을 맞을 채비를 하고 있답니다.\" 조스에게 소식을 전하는 자가 대답했다. \"그분의 집사한테서 직접 들은 얘기입니다. 리슈몽 공작 댁 사람들은 죄다 짐을 싸고 있어요. 공작 각하는 이미 달아나셨고, 공작부인은 식기 챙기는 것만 보고 오스텐더에서 프랑스 왕과 합류하려고 기다리는 중이고요.\"\n\n\"프랑스 왕은 헨트에 계신다, 이 사람아.\" 조스가 못 믿겠다는 시늉을 하며 대답했다.\n\n\"어젯밤 브뤼주로 달아나셔서, 오늘 오스텐더에서 배를 타십니다. 베리 공작은 포로로 잡혔고요. 무사하고 싶은 자들은 어서 떠나는 게 좋을 겁니다. 내일이면 제방을 터뜨릴 테니, 온 나라가 물에 잠기면 누가 달아날 수 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"허튼소리, 이 사람아. 우리는 보니가 전장에 끌어낼 수 있는 어떤 병력에도 삼 대 일로 우세하단 말이다.\" 세들리 씨가 반박했다. \"오스트리아군과 러시아군이 진군해 오고 있어. 황제는 박살 날 수밖에 없고, 박살 나고 말 거야.\" 조스가 손바닥으로 탁자를 내리치며 말했다.\n\n\"프로이센군은 예나에서 삼 대 일이었지만, 황제는 일주일 만에 그들의 군대와 왕국을 차지했습니다. 몽미라유에서는 육 대 일이었지만, 황제는 그들을 양 떼처럼 흩어 버렸지요. 오스트리아 군대가 오고는 있지만, 황후와 로마 왕을 선두에 앞세우고 옵니다. 러시아군은, 흥! 러시아군은 물러갈 겁니다. 영국군에게는 자비를 베풀지 않을 거예요. 그 악명 높은 부교(浮橋) 위에서 우리 용사들에게 저지른 잔학 행위 때문이지요. 여기 보십시오, 흑백으로 똑똑히 적혀 있잖습니까. 여기 황제 폐하이자 왕이신 분의 포고문이 있어요.\" 이제 노골적으로 나폴레옹의 지지자가 된 그가 말했다. 그러고는 주머니에서 그 문서를 꺼내 이지도르는 그것을 주인의 얼굴 앞에 매섭게 들이밀었고, 이미 그 장식 단추 코트며 값진 물건들을 제 전리품으로 여기는 눈치였다.\n\n조스는, 아직 심각하게 겁먹은 것은 아닐지언정, 적어도 마음이 상당히 어지러워졌다. \"내 코트와 모자를 다오.\" 그가 말했다. \"그리고 나를 따라오너라. 내가 직접 가서 이 소문들의 진위를 알아보겠다.\" 조스가 장식 단추 달린 프록코트를 걸치자 이지도르는 노발대발했다. \"나리, 그 군복 같은 코트는 입지 않으시는 게 좋겠습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"프랑스 놈들은 영국 군인이라면 단 한 명도 살려 두지 않겠다고 맹세했으니까요.\"\n\n\"닥쳐라, 이놈!\" 조스가 여전히 단호한 표정으로 말하고는, 굽힐 줄 모르는 결의로 소매에 팔을 쑤셔 넣었다. 바로 이 영웅적인 동작을 행하는 중에 그는 로던 크롤리 부인에게 발각되고 말았으니, 그녀는 마침 이때 애밀리아를 찾아왔다가 대기실 문에서 종도 울리지 않고 들어선 참이었다.\n\n레베카는 여느 때처럼 아주 단정하고 맵시 있게 차려입은 모습이었다. 로던이 떠난 뒤의 차분한 잠이 그녀를 산뜻하게 회복시켜 주었고, 분홍빛으로 미소 짓는 그녀의 두 볼은, 다른 사람들은 죄다 더없이 깊은 근심과 우수가 어린 얼굴을 하고 있는 이 도시, 이런 날에 보기에 무척 흐뭇한 것이었다. 그녀는 조스가 발각된 그 자세하며, 이 뚱뚱한 신사가 장식 단추 코트에 몸을 쑤셔 넣느라 벌이는 안간힘과 발버둥을 보고 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"군대에 합류하실 준비를 하시는 건가요, 조지프 씨?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"브뤼셀에는 우리 가엾은 여자들을 지켜 줄 사람 하나 남지 않는단 말인가요?\" 조스는 가까스로 코트 속으로 파고드는 데 성공하고는, 얼굴을 붉히며 더듬더듬 변명을 늘어놓으면서 어여쁜 방문객 앞으로 나아갔다. \"오늘 아침의 일들을 겪고 나서, 또 어젯밤 무도회의 피로 끝에 그녀는 좀 어떠한지?\" 이지도르 씨는 꽃무늬 실내복을 들고 주인의 옆방 침실로 사라졌다.\n\n\"이렇게 물어봐 주시다니 어쩜 이리도 친절하실까.\" 그녀가 두 손으로 그의 한 손을 꼭 잡으며 말했다. \"다들 겁에 질려 있는데 이토록 침착하고 의젓해 보이시다니! 우리 사랑스러운 어린 에미는 좀 어때요? 정말 끔찍하고도 끔찍한 이별이었겠지요.\"\n\n\"끔찍했지요.\" 조스가 말했다.\n\n\"남자분들은 무엇이든 견뎌 내실 수 있지요.\" 부인이 대답했다. \"이별이든 위험이든 당신들께는 아무것도 아니니까요. 자, 솔직히 인정하세요. 당신도 군대에 합류해서 우리를 운명에 내맡긴 채 떠나려 하셨던 거잖아요. 다 알아요. 무언가가 제게 그렇게 일러 준다고요. 그 생각이 머릿속에 떠오르자(혼자 있을 때면 저도 가끔은 당신을 생각한답니다, 조지프 씨) 어찌나 겁이 나던지, 우리를 버리고 달아나지 말아 달라고 빌고 또 애원하려고 곧장 달려온 거예요.\"\n\n이 말은 이렇게 풀이할 수도 있었다. \"친애하는 선생님, 만에 하나 군대에 변고가 생겨 후퇴가 불가피해진다면, 선생님께는 아주 안락한 마차가 있으니 저는 거기 한자리 차지하고 싶답니다.\" 조스가 이 말을 그런 뜻으로 알아들었는지는 모르겠다. 그러나 그는 브뤼셀에 머무는 동안 부인이 자기에게 무심했던 일로 마음 깊이 상해 있던 터였다. 그는 로던 크롤리의 대단한 지인 중 누구에게도 소개받은 적이 없었고, 레베카의 모임에 초대받은 적도 거의 없었다. 카드놀이를 크게 할 만큼 배짱이 없는 데다, 그가 자리에 있으면 조지와 로던 둘 다 똑같이 지루해했기 때문이다. 어쩌면 둘 중 누구도 그 두 사람이 즐기기로 작정한 유흥을 지켜보는 목격자를 곁에 두고 싶지 않았던 것이리라. \"흥!\" 조스는 생각했다. \"이제 내가 필요하니까 내게 오는군. 곁에 아무도 없을 때라야 늙은 조지프 세들리를 생각해 준단 말이지!\" 그러나 이런 의구심에도 불구하고, 그는 레베카가 자기 용기를 두고 한 말에 우쭐한 기분이 들었다.\n\n그는 얼굴을 한껏 붉히고는 거드름 피우는 태도를 지었다. \"나도 그 전투를 보고는 싶소.\" 그가 말했다. \"기개 있는 사내라면 누구나 그렇겠지, 알다시피. 인도에서 자그마한 군 경험을 좀 쌓아 보긴 했지만, 이만한 규모는 처음이오.\"\n\n\"당신들 남자분들은 즐거움을 위해서라면 무엇이든 희생하시지요.\" 레베카가 대답했다. \"크롤리 대위는 오늘 아침 마치 사냥 모임에라도 가는 사람처럼 신이 나서 절 떠났답니다. 그이가 무얼 신경 쓰겠어요? 당신들 가운데 누가 버림받은 가엾은 여자의 고통과 괴로움을 신경이나 쓰겠어요?(그이가 정말로 부대로 가긴 갔으려나, 이 게으르고 먹성 좋은 양반이?) 오! 친애하는 세들리 씨, 저는 당신께 위안을 구하러, 위로를 구하러 온 거예요. 아침 내내 무릎 꿇고 기도했답니다. 우리 남편들이, 우리 벗들이, 우리 용감한 군대와 동맹군이 뛰어들고 있는 그 끔찍한 위험을 생각하면 몸이 떨려요. 그래서 피신처를 찾아 여기 왔더니, 또 다른 벗이, 제게 마지막 남은 벗이 그 무시무시한 전장에 뛰어들기로 작정하고 있지 뭐예요!\"\n\n\"친애하는 부인.\" 이제 마음이 자못 풀어지기 시작한 조스가 대답했다. \"겁내지 마시오. 나는 그저 가 보고 싶다고 말했을 뿐이오. 어느 영국인인들 그러지 않겠소? 하지만 내 의무가 나를 여기 붙들어 두고 있소. 옆방의 저 가엾은 사람을 두고 떠날 수가 없으니까.\" 그러면서 그는 애밀리아가 있는 방문 쪽을 손가락으로 가리켰다.\n\n\"착하고 고결하신 오라버니!\" 레베카가 손수건을 눈에 갖다 대며, 거기 배인 오드콜로뉴 향을 맡으면서 말했다. \"제가 당신을 잘못 보았네요. 당신께는 따뜻한 마음이 있는걸요. 없는 줄로만 알았는데.\"\n\n\"오, 내 명예를 걸고!\" 조스가 마치 그 문제의 부위에 손을 얹기라도 하려는 듯한 시늉을 하며 말했다. \"당신은 정말 나를 잘못 보고 있소, 정말이오. 친애하는 크롤리 부인.\"\n\n\"네, 이제 당신의 마음이 누이동생에게 진실하니 그렇네요. 하지만 이 년 전 일은 기억하고 있어요. 그 마음이 제게는 거짓이었던 때 말이에요!\" 레베카가 한순간 그에게 시선을 박았다가, 이내 돌아서서 창가로 향하며 말했다.\n\n조스는 격렬하게 얼굴을 붉혔다. 레베카에게 없다고 책망당한 바로 그 기관이 요란하게 쿵쿵 뛰기 시작했다. 그는 그녀에게서 달아났던 날들과, 한때 자기를 불타게 했던 그 열정을 떠올렸다. 이륜마차에 그녀를 태우고 몰던 날들, 그녀가 그에게 초록색 돈지갑을 떠 주던 날들, 그녀의 흰 팔과 빛나는 두 눈을 황홀히 바라보며 넋을 잃고 앉아 있던 날들을.\n\n\"저를 배은망덕하다고 생각하시겠지요.\" 레베카가 창가에서 나오며, 다시 한번 그를 바라보고는 나직하게 떨리는 목소리로 그에게 말을 이었다. \"당신의 그 냉랭함, 외면하는 눈길, 요즘 우리가 마주칠 때마다의 그 태도, 방금 제가 들어왔을 때 그 모습, 모두 그걸 제게 증명해 주었어요. 하지만 제가 당신을 피할 만한 이유가 없었을까요? 그 물음에는 당신의 마음이 답하게 하세요. 제 남편이 당신을 그리도 반겨 맞이하려 했다고 보세요? 제가 그이에게서 들은 모진 말이라고는(크롤리 대위에게 이만한 공정함은 베풀어야겠지요) 죄다 당신에 관한 것이었어요. 그리고 그건 더없이 모질고도 모진 말들이었지요.\"\n\n\"이런 맙소사! 내가 대체 무엇을 했단 말이오?\" 조스가 기쁨과 당혹이 뒤섞인 허둥거림 속에서 물었다. \"내가 대체 무엇을 했길래, 그, 그, 그래서?\"\n\n\"질투가 아무것도 아니란 말인가요?\" 레베카가 말했다. \"그이는 당신 때문에 저를 비참하게 만들어요. 그리고 한때야 어떠했든, 제 마음은 온전히 그이의 것이랍니다. 저는 이제 결백해요. 그렇지 않나요, 세들리 씨?\"\n\n자기 매력에 희생된 이 여인을 찬찬히 바라보는 사이, 조스의 온몸의 피가 환희로 짜릿짜릿해졌다. 능란한 몇 마디 말, 다 안다는 듯한 다정한 눈길 한두 번에, 그의 마음은 다시 불붙고 의혹과 의심은 까맣게 잊혔다. 솔로몬 이래로, 그보다 더 지혜로운 사내들도 여자에게 농락당하고 우롱당하지 않았던가? \"최악의 경우가 닥치더라도,\" 베키는 생각했다. \"내 퇴로는 확보된 셈이지. 사륜마차의 오른쪽 좋은 자리도 차지할 권리가 생겼고.\"\n\n만약 바로 이 순간 하인 이지도르가 다시 나타나 집안일로 부산을 떨기 시작하지 않았더라면, 조지프 씨의 들끓는 열정이 그를 어떤 사랑과 정열의 고백으로 몰고 갔을지는 알 수 없는 일이다. 막 한마디 고백을 토해 내려던 조스는 억눌러야만 했던 그 격정에 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이었다. 레베카 역시 이제는 안으로 들어가 사랑하는 애밀리아를 위로해 줄 때라는 데에 생각이 미쳤다. \"오 르부아르.\" 그녀는 조지프 씨에게 손에 입맞춤을 던지며 그렇게 말하고는, 그의 누이가 있는 방문을 가볍게 두드렸다. 그녀가 안으로 들어가 등 뒤로 문을 닫자, 그는 의자에 털썩 주저앉아 멍하니 바라보며 한숨을 쉬고 의미심장하게 씩씩거렸다. \"그 코트는 나리께 너무 꽉 낍니다.\" 여전히 장식 단추에 눈독을 들이며 이지도르가 말했지만, 주인은 그 말을 듣지 못했다. 그의 생각은 다른 데 가 있었던 것이다. 어떤 때는 매혹적인 레베카를 그리며 달아오르고 미칠 듯하다가, 또 어떤 때는 곱슬곱슬 사나운 콧수염을 기르고 무시무시한 결투용 권총에 탄을 재어 공이를 당겨 든 질투심에 찬 로던 크롤리의 환영 앞에서 죄지은 듯 움츠러들었다.\n* 오 르부아르: \"또 봬요\"에 해당하는 프랑스어 작별 인사.\n\n레베카가 나타나자 애밀리아는 공포에 사로잡혀 뒷걸음질 쳤다. 그것은 그녀를 다시 현실 세계로, 어제의 기억으로 불러들였던 것이다. 내일에 대한 압도적인 두려움 속에서 그녀는 레베카도, 질투도, 남편이 떠나 위험에 처해 있다는 사실 외에는 모든 것을 잊고 있었던 것이다. 이 겁 없는 속물이 들어와 그 주술을 깨뜨리고 빗장을 들어 올리기까지는, 우리 또한 그 슬픈 방 안으로 차마 들어서지 못했던 것이다. 그 가엾은 처녀가 무릎 꿇고 있던 시간이 얼마였던가! 그녀가 거기서 보낸 말없는 기도와 쓰라린 부복(俯伏)의 시간들이 얼마였던가! 싸움과 승리의 찬란한 이야기를 써 내려가는 전쟁 연대기 작가들은 이런 것들에 대해서는 좀처럼 들려주지 않는다. 이런 것들은 그 화려한 행렬에서 너무도 하찮은 부분인 것이다. 그리고 여러분은 승리의 위대한 합창 한가운데서 터지는 함성과 환호 속에서는 과부의 울부짖음이나 어머니의 흐느낌을 듣지 못한다. 그러나 그런 이들이 울부짖지 않았던 때가 언제 한 번이라도 있었던가. 가슴 찢어진, 비천한 항변자들이여, 승리의 소란 속에 묻혀 끝내 들리지 못하는구나!\n\n애밀리아의 마음에 첫 공포의 동요가 일고 나서, 레베카의 초록빛 두 눈이 그녀에게 와닿고, 후자가 산뜻한 비단옷과 번쩍이는 장신구를 바스락거리며 두 팔을 벌리고 안으려 종종걸음으로 다가오자, 뒤이어 노여움의 감정이 솟구쳤다. 그리하여 좀 전까지 죽은 듯 창백하던 그녀의 얼굴이 붉게 달아올랐고, 한순간 뒤 그녀는 그 큰 눈으로 레베카의 시선을 흔들림 없이 되받았으니, 그 단호함에 연적은 놀라고 다소 무안해질 지경이었다.\n\n\"사랑하는 애밀리아, 안색이 무척 안 좋구나.\" 방문객이 애밀리아의 손을 잡으려 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"무슨 일이니? 네가 어떤지 알기 전까지는 도무지 마음을 놓을 수가 없었어.\"\n\n애밀리아는 손을 도로 거두어들였다. 그 온화한 영혼은 평생 단 한 번도 호의나 애정의 표시를 믿지 않거나 거기에 응하기를 거부한 적이 없었다. 그러나 그녀는 손을 도로 거두고 온몸을 부들부들 떨었다. \"여긴 왜 왔니, 레베카?\" 그녀가 여전히 큰 눈으로 엄숙히 그녀를 바라보며 말했다. 이 눈빛이 방문객을 불안하게 했다.\n\n'무도회에서 그이가 내게 편지를 건네는 걸 본 게 틀림없어.' 레베카는 생각했다. \"흥분하지 마, 사랑하는 애밀리아.\" 그녀가 눈을 내리깔며 말했다. \"난 그저 네가 좀, 네가 괜찮은지 보려고 왔을 뿐이야.\"\n\n\"넌 괜찮니?\" 애밀리아가 말했다. \"그렇겠지. 넌 네 남편을 사랑하지 않으니까. 사랑한다면 여기 와 있지도 않았을 테지. 말해 봐, 레베카, 내가 너한테 친절 말고 다른 짓을 한 적이 있었니?\"\n\n\"정말이지, 애밀리아, 없었어.\" 상대가 여전히 고개를 떨군 채 말했다.\n\n\"네가 무일푼으로 가난했을 때, 너를 거두어 준 게 누구였니? 내가 네게 자매 같은 사람이 아니었니? 그이가 나랑 결혼하기 전, 더 행복했던 시절에 넌 우리 모두를 보았잖아. 그때 난 그이에게 전부였어. 아니면 그이가 나를 행복하게 해 주려고 그토록 고결하게 자기 재산과 가문을 포기했겠니? 그런데 넌 왜 내 사랑과 나 사이에 끼어들었니? 하느님이 맺어 주신 사람들을 갈라놓고, 내 사랑하는 이의 마음을, 내 남편의 마음을 내게서 빼앗아 가라고 누가 널 보냈니? 네가 나만큼 그이를 사랑할 수 있을 것 같니? 그이의 사랑은 내게 전부였어. 넌 그걸 알면서도 내게서 그걸 훔쳐 가려 했지. 부끄러운 줄 알아, 레베카. 나쁘고 사악한 여자. 거짓된 벗에 거짓된 아내.\"\n\n\"애밀리아, 하느님 앞에 맹세코 난 내 남편에게 아무 잘못도 하지 않았어.\" 레베카가 그녀에게서 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n\"내겐 아무 잘못도 안 했고, 레베카? 넌 성공하진 못했지만, 시도는 했잖아. 그러지 않았는지 네 마음에 물어봐.\"\n\n'아무것도 모르는군.' 레베카는 생각했다.\n\n\"그이는 내게 돌아왔어. 그럴 줄 알았어. 그 어떤 거짓도, 그 어떤 아첨도 그이를 내게서 오래 떼어 놓을 수 없다는 걸 난 알고 있었어. 그이가 돌아오리란 걸 알았어. 그렇게 되게 해 달라고 그토록 기도했으니까.\"\n\n가엾은 처녀는 레베카가 전에 한 번도 본 적 없는 기개와 거침없는 말솜씨로 이 말들을 쏟아 냈고, 그 앞에서 레베카는 그만 말문이 막혀 버렸다. \"하지만 내가 너한테 무슨 잘못을 했길래,\" 그녀가 더 가련한 어조로 말을 이었다. \"넌 그이를 내게서 빼앗으려 한 거니? 난 겨우 육 주 동안 그이를 가졌을 뿐인데. 그것만은 내게 남겨 둘 수 있었잖아, 레베카. 그런데도 넌 우리 결혼 첫날부터 찾아와서 그걸 망쳐 놓았어. 이제 그이가 떠나고 없으니, 내가 얼마나 불행한지 구경하러 온 거니?\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"지난 보름 동안 넌 나를 충분히 비참하게 만들었어. 오늘만은 나를 놔둘 수도 있었잖아.\"\n\n\"난, 난 여기 온 적 없어.\" 레베카가 운 나쁘게도 사실대로 끼어들었다.\n\n\"그래. 넌 온 적 없지. 넌 그이를 데려갔으니까. 이제 그이를 내게서 데려가려고 온 거니?\" 그녀가 더 격앙된 어조로 말을 이었다. \"그이가 여기 있었는데, 이젠 가 버렸어. 저기 바로 저 소파에 그이가 앉았었어. 손대지 마. 우린 거기 앉아서 이야기를 나눴어. 난 그이 무릎에 앉았고, 두 팔로 그이 목을 감았고, 우린 '주기도문'을 외웠어. 그래, 그이는 여기 있었어. 그런데 사람들이 와서 그이를 데려갔지만, 그이는 내게 돌아오겠다고 약속했어.\"\n\n\"그이는 돌아올 거야, 얘야.\" 레베카가 자기도 모르게 마음이 움직여 말했다.\n\n\"봐.\" 애밀리아가 말했다. \"이게 그이의 장식 띠야. 빛깔이 예쁘지 않니?\" 그러면서 그녀는 그 술 장식을 집어 들어 입을 맞추었다. 그녀는 그날 어느 무렵엔가 그것을 자기 허리에 둘러 매어 두었던 것이다. 그녀는 노여움도, 질투도, 심지어 연적이 곁에 있다는 것조차 잊은 듯했다. 말없이, 거의 얼굴에 미소를 띤 채 침대 쪽으로 걸어가 조지의 베개를 매만지기 시작했으니 말이다.\n\n레베카도 말없이 자리를 떴다. \"애밀리아는 좀 어떻소?\" 여전히 의자에 자리 잡고 앉아 있던 조스가 물었다.\n\n\"누군가 곁에 있어 주어야겠어요.\" 레베카가 말했다. \"제 생각엔 무척 안 좋은 것 같아요.\" 그러고는 무척 침통한 얼굴로 자리를 떠났다. 자기가 시켜 둔 이른 저녁을 함께 들고 가라는 세들리 씨의 간청도 마다하고서였다.\n\n레베카는 성품이 선량하고 남을 잘 위하는 사람이었다. 그리고 애밀리아를 싫어한다기보다는 오히려 좋아하는 편이었다. 책망하는 말이긴 했어도, 그 모진 말들조차 그녀에게는 일종의 찬사였다. 패배의 아픔에 따끔거리는 사람이 내지르는 신음이었던 것이다. 학장의 설교에도 도무지 위안을 얻지 못한 채 파르크를 더없이 쓸쓸하게 거닐던 오다우드 부인과 마주치자, 레베카가 먼저 그녀에게 말을 건넸다. 로던 크롤리 부인에게서 이런 정중한 표시를 받는 데 익숙지 않았던 소령 부인은 다소 놀랐다. 레베카는 가엾은 어린 오스본 부인이 절망적인 지경에 빠져 슬픔으로 거의 실성할 지경이라고 알리고는, 그 마음씨 고운 아일랜드 여인을 곧장 보내어 자기가 아끼는 어린 벗을 위로할 수 있을지 가 보게 했다.\n\n\"제 코가 석 자랍니다.\" 오다우드 부인이 침통하게 말했다. \"그리고 가엾은 애밀리아는 오늘 같은 날 곁에 있어 줄 사람이 거의 없겠거니 했지요. 하지만 당신 말처럼 그 애가 그렇게나 안 좋고, 그토록 그 애를 아끼던 당신마저 돌볼 수 없다면, 정말이지 내가 도움이 될 수 있을지 가 보지요. 그럼 안녕히 계세요, 부인.\" 이 말과 함께 고개를 한 번 까딱하고는, 그 회중시계의 임자이신 부인은 크롤리 부인에게 작별을 고했다. 그녀와 어울리는 것 따위는 조금도 바라지 않았던 것이다.\n\n베키는 그녀가 성큼성큼 떠나가는 모습을 입가에 미소를 머금고 지켜보았다. 그녀에게는 더없이 예리한 유머 감각이 있었던지라, 물러가는 오다우드 부인이 어깨너머로 던진 파르티아식 눈초리에 크롤리 부인은 하마터면 그 침착함을 잃을 뻔했다. '잘 가시구려, 곱디고운 부인, 그리도 명랑하신 걸 보니 반갑소.' 페기는 생각했다. '어쨌든 당신은 슬픔에 겨워 눈이 짓무르도록 울 사람은 아니지.' 그러고는 그녀는 계속 나아가, 이내 오스본 부인의 거처로 향하는 길을 찾아냈다.\n\n* 파르티아식 눈초리: 고대 파르티아 기병이 후퇴하는 척하다 돌아서서 활을 쏘던 전술에서 유래한 표현으로, 떠나면서 던지는 마지막 일격을 뜻한다.\n가엾은 그 영혼은 레베카가 두고 떠난 그 침대 곁에 여전히 있었고, 슬픔으로 거의 실성한 채 서 있었다. 마음이 더 굳센 여인인 소령 부인은 어린 벗을 위로하려 최선을 다했다. \"정신을 차려야 한단다, 사랑하는 애밀리아.\" 그녀가 다정하게 말했다. \"승리한 뒤에 그이가 너를 부를 때, 네가 앓고 있는 걸 보여선 안 되잖니. 오늘 하느님의 손에 맡겨진 여자가 너 혼자만은 아니란다.\"\n\n\"알아요. 전 너무 못됐고, 너무 약해요.\" 애밀리아가 말했다. 그녀는 자기 자신의 약함을 잘 알고 있었다. 그러나 더 결연한 벗이 곁에 있으니 그 약함이 다잡아졌고, 그녀는 이 다독임과 동무 덕에 한결 나아졌다. 두 사람은 두 시까지 그렇게 함께 있었다. 그들의 마음은 점점 더 멀리 행군해 가는 그 종대와 함께 있었던 것이다. 끔찍한 의혹과 고뇌, 말로 다 못 할 기도와 두려움과 비탄이 연대를 뒤따랐다. 그것은 여인들이 전쟁에 바치는 공물이었다. 전쟁은 남녀 양쪽에 똑같이 세금을 매겨, 남자에게서는 피를, 여자에게서는 눈물을 거두어 가는 것이다.\n\n두 시 반, 조지프 씨에게는 날마다 중요한 사건이 일어났다. 저녁 시간이 닥친 것이다. 전사들이야 싸우다 스러질지언정, 그는 식사를 해야만 했다. 그는 애밀리아의 방으로 들어가 그녀를 구슬려 그 식사를 함께하게 할 수 있을지 보았다. \"좀 들어 봐.\" 그가 말했다. \"수프가 아주 맛있단다. 한번 들어 보렴, 에미.\" 그러고는 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추었다. 그녀가 결혼하던 때를 빼면, 그가 몇 해 동안 그토록 다정하게 군 적이 없었다. \"오라버니는 참 착하고 친절하세요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"다들 그래요. 하지만 괜찮으시면, 전 오늘 제 방에 있을래요.\"\n\n그러나 수프의 향내는 오다우드 부인의 콧구멍에는 흡족한 것이었다. 그래서 그녀는 조스 씨와 동무해 주어야겠다고 생각했다. 그리하여 두 사람은 식사 앞에 마주 앉았다. \"이 음식에 하느님의 축복이 있기를.\" 소령 부인이 엄숙히 말했다. 그녀는 자기 연대의 선두에서 말을 몰고 있을 정직한 믹을 생각하고 있었던 것이다. \"오늘 그 가엾은 아이들이 먹게 될 저녁이라곤 보잘것없겠지요.\" 그녀가 한숨지으며 말하고는, 이내 철학자처럼 식사에 달려들었다.\n\n조스는 식사와 함께 기분이 좋아졌다. 그는 연대의 건강을 위해 건배라도 하고 싶었다. 아니, 사실 샴페인 한 잔을 들이켜기 위해서라면 무슨 핑계라도 댈 참이었다. \"오다우드와 용감한 ××연대를 위하여 마십시다.\" 그가 손님에게 늠름하게 고개 숙여 인사하며 말했다. \"어떻소, 오다우드 부인? 이지도르, 오다우드 부인 잔을 채워라.\"\n\n그런데 별안간 이지도르가 흠칫 놀랐고, 소령 부인은 나이프와 포크를 내려놓았다. 남쪽으로 난 그 방의 창문들이 열려 있었는데, 햇살 비치는 지붕들 너머 그쪽 방향에서 둔탁하고 아득한 소리가 밀려왔던 것이다. \"저게 뭐지?\" 조스가 말했다. \"이놈아, 왜 안 따르고 있는 게냐?\"\n\n\"세 르 푀!\" 이지도르가 발코니로 달려가며 말했다.\n\n\"하느님, 우리를 지켜 주소서. 대포예요!\" 오다우드 부인이 벌떡 일어서며 외치고는, 그녀 역시 창가로 따라갔다. 다른 창문들에서도 새파랗게 질려 근심에 찬 수천의 얼굴이 내다보는 것이 보였으리라. 이윽고 도시의 온 주민이 거리로 쏟아져 나오는 것만 같았다.\n* 세 르 푀: \"포격이에요!\"에 해당하는 프랑스어.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXII\n\n평화로운 런던 시에 사는 우리는 브뤼셀이 보여 준 그런 황급함과 경악의 광경을 일찍이 목도한 적이 없으며, 부디 신의 가호로 앞으로도 보지 않기를 바란다. 군중은 소음이 들려오는 방향, 나뮈르 성문 쪽으로 몰려갔고, 많은 이들이 군대로부터 들려올 어떤 소식이라도 먼저 알아내려고 평탄한 큰길을 따라 말을 달렸다. 사람마다 옆 사람에게 소식을 물었고, 신분 높은 영국 귀족 신사 숙녀들조차 알지도 못하는 사람들에게 말을 거는 일까지 마다하지 않았다. 프랑스 편을 드는 이들은 흥분에 들떠 밖으로 나돌며 자기네 황제의 승리를 예언했다. 상인들은 가게를 닫고 거리로 나와 경악과 아우성의 전체 합창에 가세했다. 여인들은 교회로 몰려가 예배당을 가득 메우고는 돌바닥과 계단에 무릎 꿇고 기도했다. 대포의 둔중한 소리는 끊임없이 우르릉, 우르릉 굴러갔다. 이윽고 여행객을 태운 마차들이 헨트 관문 쪽으로 질주하며 도시를 떠나기 시작했다. 프랑스 편을 드는 자들의 예언이 사실로 통하기 시작했다. \"그가 양쪽 군대를 둘로 갈라놓았다네.\" 사람들이 말했다. \"곧장 브뤼셀로 진군하고 있다는군. 영국군을 짓밟고 오늘 밤이면 여기 들이닥칠 거야.\" \"영국군을 짓밟고,\" 이지도르가 주인에게 비명을 지르듯 말했다. \"오늘 밤이면 여기 들이닥칠 겁니다.\" 이 하인은 숙소와 거리 사이를 들락날락 뛰어다니며 그때마다 새로운 참사의 세부 소식을 물어 왔다. 조스의 얼굴은 점점 더 창백해졌다. 이 살진 민간인은 차츰 공포에 완전히 사로잡히기 시작했다. 그가 마신 샴페인은 그 어느 것도 그에게 용기를 가져다주지 못했다. 해가 지기 전에 그는 신경이 곤두설 대로 곤두선 지경에 이르렀고, 그 친구 이지도르는 그 꼴을 보며 흡족해했다. 이지도르는 이제 그 금몰을 두른 외투 주인의 노획물을 틀림없는 제 것으로 셈하고 있었던 것이다.\n\n여자들은 이 내내 자리에 없었다. 한순간 포성을 듣고 나자, 그 건장한 소령의 부인은 옆방의 벗이 떠올라 애밀리아를 살피고 가능하면 위로하려고 안으로 달려 들어갔다. 저 가련하고 가냘픈 사람을 자기가 지켜 주어야 한다는 생각이, 정직한 아일랜드 여인이 본래 지닌 용기에 한층 힘을 보태 주었다. 그녀는 다섯 시간을 벗의 곁에서 보냈다. 때로는 타이르고, 때로는 명랑하게 이야기를 건네고, 더 자주는 말없이 겁에 질린 마음속 기도를 올리면서였다. \"단 한 번도 그 애 손을 놓지 않았답니다.\" 그 건장한 부인은 나중에 이렇게 말했다. \"해가 지고 포성이 멎을 때까지는요.\" 하녀 폴린은 가까운 교회에서 무릎을 꿇고 자기 사내를 위해 기도하고 있었다.\n\n포성이 멎자 오다우드 부인은 애밀리아의 방에서 나와 붙어 있는 거실로 들어갔다. 거기에는 조스가 빈 술병 두 개를 앞에 놓고, 용기란 용기는 죄다 사라진 채 앉아 있었다. 그는 한두 번 누이의 침실로 들어가 보려고 했는데, 몹시 겁에 질린 얼굴로 마치 무슨 말이라도 하려는 듯했다. 그러나 소령의 부인이 자리를 지키고 있었으므로, 그는 가슴에 담은 말을 털어놓지 못한 채 물러났다. 그는 자기가 달아나고 싶다는 말을 그녀에게 차마 하지 못했던 것이다.\n\n그러나 그녀가 식당에 모습을 드러냈을 때―그는 황혼 속에 빈 샴페인 병들의 적막한 동무를 곁에 두고 앉아 있었다―그는 비로소 그녀에게 속내를 열기 시작했다.\n\n\"오다우드 부인,\" 그가 말했다. \"애밀리아를 채비시키는 게 좋지 않겠습니까?\"\n\n\"산책이라도 데리고 나가시려고요?\" 소령의 부인이 말했다. \"그 애는 너무 약해져서 꼼짝도 못 할 텐데요.\"\n\n\"내가―내가 마차를 불러 두었습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"그리고―그리고 역마도요. 이지도르가 구하러 갔지요.\" 조스가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"이 밤중에 마차를 몰아서 뭘 하시려고요?\" 부인이 대답했다. \"그 애는 침대에 누워 있는 게 낫지 않겠어요? 이제 막 눕혀 놓았는데요.\"\n\n\"일으키세요.\" 조스가 말했다. \"그 애는 일어나야 합니다, 내 말은. 말을 불러 두었다니까요―그래요, 말을 불러 두었어요. 이제 다 끝났고, 그리고―\"\n\n\"그리고 뭐요?\" 오다우드 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"나는 헨트로 떠납니다.\" 조스가 대답했다. \"다들 떠나고 있어요. 부인 자리도 하나 있고요! 삼십 분 안에 출발할 겁니다.\"\n\n소령의 부인은 한없는 경멸을 담아 그를 바라보았다. \"오다우드가 진로를 알려 줄 때까지는 한 발짝도 움직이지 않겠어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"원하신다면 떠나세요, 세들리 씨. 하지만 정말이지, 애밀리아와 나는 여기 남겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그 애는 가야 합니다.\" 조스가 또 한 번 발을 구르며 말했다. 오다우드 부인은 두 팔을 허리에 떡 붙이고 침실 문 앞에 버티고 섰다.\n\n\"그 애를 어미한테 데려다주시려는 거예요?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"아니면 본인이 어머니한테 가고 싶으신 건가요, 세들리 씨? 그럼 안녕히, 즐거운 여행 되시길요, 나리. 봉 부아야주, 그쪽 말로 하자면 말이지요. 그리고 내 충고를 하나 새겨들으세요, 그 콧수염일랑 밀어 버리는 게 좋을 거예요. 안 그러면 그게 당신을 화근으로 끌어들일 테니까요.\"\n\n\"빌어먹을!\" 조스가 공포와 분노와 굴욕에 미쳐 날뛰며 고함을 질렀다. 마침 이 순간 이지도르가 들어왔는데, 그도 그 나름대로 욕설을 퍼붓고 있었다. \"빌어먹을, 말이 없어요!\" 분노에 찬 하인이 씩씩거리며 내뱉었다. 말이란 말은 죄다 사라지고 없었다. 그날 브뤼셀에서 공황에 사로잡힌 사람은 조스만이 아니었던 것이다.\n\n그러나 조스의 공포는, 이미 크고 가혹한 것이긴 했지만, 그 밤이 다 가기 전에 거의 광란의 지경에까지 커지도록 운명 지어져 있었다. 앞서 말했듯이, 하녀 폴린에게도 나폴레옹 황제를 맞아 싸우러 나간 군대의 대열 속에 자기 사내가 있었다. 이 연인은 브뤼셀 토박이로, 벨기에 경기병이었다. 그 나라 군대는 이 전쟁에서 용기 말고는 무엇으로든 명성을 떨쳤고, 폴린의 흠모하는 이 젊은 판 카쇰은 줄행랑을 치라는 대령의 명령을 어기기에는 지나치게 훌륭한 군인이었다. 브뤼셀에 주둔하는 동안 이 젊은 레굴루스는(그는 혁명기에 태어났다) 폴린의 부엌에서 더없는 위안을 찾았고 거의 모든 한가한 시간을 거기서 보냈다. 그리고 그가 며칠 전 출정에 나서며 울먹이는 연인과 작별했을 때, 그 주머니며 권총집은 그녀의 식료품 저장실에서 나온 좋은 것들로 가득 채워져 있었다.\n\n그의 연대로 말하자면, 이 출정은 이제 끝난 것이나 다름없었다. 그들은 그의 명목상 군주인 오라녜 공의 지휘 아래 있는 사단의 일부를 이루고 있었는데, 칼의 길이와 콧수염, 군복과 장비의 화려함으로 따지자면 레굴루스와 그 전우들은 나팔 소리에 맞춰 진군한 그 어떤 부대 못지않게 용맹스러워 보이는 무리였다.\n\n네가 연합군의 선봉을 향해 돌격하여 진지를 하나씩 차례로 빼앗아 나가다가, 브뤼셀에서 영국군 본대가 도착하면서 카트르브라 전투의 양상이 뒤바뀌었을 때, 레굴루스가 속한 기병대는 프랑스군 앞에서 후퇴하는 데 가장 활발한 동작을 보였고, 자기네가 차지하고 있던 진지를 하나씩 더없이 민첩하게 내주며 물러났다. 그들의 움직임을 멈춰 세운 것은 오로지 후방에서 진격해 오는 영국군뿐이었다. 그렇게 멈춰 설 수밖에 없게 되자, 적의 기병대는(그 피에 굶주린 집요함은 아무리 준엄하게 나무라도 지나치지 않다) 마침내 앞에 선 용감한 벨기에군과 백병전을 벌일 기회를 잡았다. 그러나 벨기에군은 프랑스군보다는 영국군과 부딪치는 편이 낫겠다 여겨, 곧장 꽁무니를 빼고는 자기네 뒤에 있던 영국 연대들 사이를 뚫고 달아나 사방으로 흩어졌다. 사실 그 연대는 이제 더는 존재하지 않았다. 어디에도 없었다. 본부도 없었다. 레굴루스는 전장에서 수 마일이나 떨어진 곳을 홀로 내달리고 있는 자신을 발견했다. 그러니 피신처를 찾아 달려갈 곳이라면, 폴린이 그토록 자주 그를 반겨 주었던 그 부엌과 그 충실한 두 팔 말고 어디가 더 자연스러웠겠는가?\n\n밤 열 시쯤, 오스본 일가가 대륙식으로 한 층을 차지하고 있던 집의 계단을 올라오는 군도가 짤그랑거리는 소리가 들렸을지도 모른다. 부엌문을 두드리는 소리도 들렸을 것이다. 교회에서 돌아온 가엾은 폴린은 문을 열고 자기 앞에 선 초췌한 경기병을 보고는 공포로 거의 까무러칠 뻔했다. 그는 레오노라를 깨우러 온 한밤중의 기병처럼 창백한 얼굴이었다. 폴린은 비명을 지를 뻔했지만, 그 소리가 주인들을 부르고 제 연인을 들통나게 할까 봐 비명을 삼켰다. 그러고는 자기 영웅을 부엌으로 이끌어 들여 맥주와, 조스가 차마 입에 댈 엄두를 내지 못한 만찬에서 가장 좋은 부위들을 내주었다. 경기병은 자기가 유령이 아니라는 것을, 게걸스레 먹어 치운 어마어마한 양의 고기와 맥주로 증명해 보였다. 그리고 우물거리는 사이사이 자기 참사의 이야기를 들려주었다.\n\n그의 연대는 엄청난 용기를 발휘하여 한동안 프랑스 전군의 공세를 버텨 냈다. 그러나 결국 압도당하고 말았으니, 그때쯤이면 영국군 전체가 그러했듯이 말이다. 네는 밀려드는 연대를 하나씩 박살 냈다. 벨기에군이 영국군의 학살을 막아 보려 끼어들었으나 헛일이었다. 브룬스비크군은 패주하여 달아났고, 그 공작은 전사했다. 그것은 전반적인 궤멸이었다. 그는 패배의 슬픔을 맥주의 홍수에 빠뜨려 잊어 보려 했다.\n\n부엌에 들어왔다가 이 대화를 들은 이지도르는 주인에게 알리려고 뛰쳐나갔다. \"다 끝났습니다.\" 그가 조스에게 비명을 지르듯 외쳤다. \"공작 나리는 포로가 되었고, 브룬스비크 공작은 전사했고, 영국군은 완전히 패주 중이랍니다. 살아남은 사람은 단 하나뿐인데, 지금 부엌에 있어요. 와서 직접 들어 보세요.\" 그리하여 조스는 휘청거리며 그 방으로 들어갔다. 레굴루스는 여전히 부엌 식탁에 앉아 맥주 단지를 꼭 붙들고 있었다. 조스는 자기가 짜낼 수 있는 가장 그럴듯한 프랑스어로―실상 문법이라고는 형편없는 종류의 것이었지만―경기병에게 그 이야기를 들려 달라고 간청했다. 레굴루스가 말을 이어 갈수록 참사는 깊어졌다. 그는 자기 연대에서 전장에서 죽지 않은 유일한 사람이었다. 그는 브룬스비크 공작이 쓰러지고, 검은 경기병들이 달아나고, 스코틀랜드 부대가 포화에 박살 나는 것을 보았다. \"그럼 ××연대는?\" 조스가 헐떡이며 물었다.\n\n\"산산조각 났지요.\" 경기병이 말했다. 그 말에 폴린이 \"오 우리 아씨, 우리 가엾은 아씨\" 하고 울부짖더니 그대로 발작을 일으켜 온 집 안을 비명으로 가득 채웠다.\n\n공포에 미쳐 날뛰던 세들리 씨는 어떻게 어디서 안전을 구해야 할지 알 수가 없었다. 그는 부엌에서 거실로 다시 뛰쳐나가, 오다우드 부인이 면전에서 닫고 잠가 버린 애밀리아의 방문을 향해 애원하는 눈길을 던졌다. 그러나 그 부인이 자기를 얼마나 경멸하며 맞이했는지 떠올리고는, 문 앞에서 잠시 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울이다가 문을 떠나 그날 처음으로 거리로 나가기로 마음먹었다. 그리하여 그는 촛불을 집어 들고 금몰을 두른 모자를 두리번거리며 찾았는데, 그것은 평소 놓이던 자리, 곧 대기실의 콘솔 탁자 위, 조스가 모양을 내곤 하던 거울 앞에 놓여 있었다. 그는 공식 석상에 모습을 드러내러 나가기 전이면 늘 그 거울 앞에서 옆머리를 비비 꼬고 모자를 눈 위로 적당히 비스듬히 얹어 보곤 했던 것이다. 습관의 힘이란 이러해서, 그는 공포의 한가운데서도 기계적으로 머리카락을 만지작거리고 모자의 기울기를 매만지기 시작했다. 그러다 그는 앞에 놓인 거울 속의 창백한 얼굴을, 특히 세상에 나온 지 일곱 주가량 되는 동안 무성하게 자라난 콧수염을 보고 화들짝 놀랐다. 패배한 영국군 전체가 위협받고 있다는 학살에 대한 이지도르의 경고를 떠올리며, 그는 생각했다. 저자들이 나를 군인으로 오해하겠구나. 그러고는 비틀거리며 침실로 돌아가, 하인을 부르는 종을 미친 듯이 당기기 시작했다.\n\n이지도르가 그 부름에 응답해 왔다. 조스는 의자에 푹 주저앉아 있었다. 그는 목도리를 잡아 뜯고 옷깃을 접어 내린 채, 두 손을 다 목께로 들어 올리고 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"잘라 줘, 이지도르.\" 그가 소리쳤다. \"빨리! 날 잘라 줘!\"\n\n이지도르는 한순간 주인이 미쳐서 제 하인더러 목을 따 달라는 줄로 생각했다.\n\n\"콧수염 말이야.\" 조스가 헐떡이며 말했다. \"콧수염―잘라, 밀어, 빨리!\" 그의 프랑스어란 이런 식이었다―앞서 말했듯이 거침은 없으나 문법으로 칭찬받을 만한 것은 못 되었다.\n\n이지도르는 면도칼로 순식간에 콧수염을 밀어 버렸고, 모자와 평범한 외투를 가져오라는 주인의 명령을 형언할 수 없는 기쁨으로 들었다. \"이제 안 입어―군복―좋아―네 거야, 가져가\"가 조스의 말이었다. 그 외투와 모자는 마침내 이지도르의 것이 되었다.\n\n이 선물을 베푼 뒤, 조스는 가진 옷가지 중에서 수수한 검정 외투와 조끼를 골라 입고, 커다란 흰 목도리를 두르고 평범한 비버 모자를 썼다. 챙 넓은 성직자 모자를 구할 수 있었다면 그것을 썼을 것이다. 그 차림으로 보자면, 누구라도 그를 잘나가는 풍채 좋은 영국 국교회 목사쯤으로 여겼을 것이다.\n\n\"이제 와.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"따라와―가자―출발―거리로.\" 이렇게 말하고 나서 그는 잽싸게 계단을 곤두박질치듯 내려가 거리로 나섰다.\n\n레굴루스는 자기가 그 연대에서, 아니 연합군 전체에서 거의 유일하게 네의 칼날에 산산조각 나는 것을 면한 사람이라고 단언했지만, 그 진술은 부정확했던 모양으로, 희생되었으리라 여겨진 자들 가운데 상당수가 그 학살에서 살아남은 듯했다. 레굴루스의 전우 수십 명이 브뤼셀로 돌아오는 길을 찾아냈고, 하나같이 자기가 달아났다고 입을 모았으니, 온 도시가 연합군이 패했다는 생각으로 가득 찼다. 시시각각 프랑스군의 도래가 예상되었고, 공황은 계속되었으며, 도주를 위한 채비가 곳곳에서 진행되었다. 말이 없다니! 조스는 공포에 사로잡혀 생각했다. 그는 이지도르를 시켜 수십 명에게 빌려주거나 팔 말이 있는지 물어보게 했는데, 어디서나 돌아오는 부정의 대답에 가슴이 철렁 내려앉았다. 걸어서 가야 하나? 그러나 공포조차도 그 육중한 몸뚱이를 그토록 날래게 만들어 주지는 못했다.\n\n브뤼셀에서 영국인들이 묵는 호텔은 거의 다 공원을 마주 보고 있었고, 조스는 공포와 호기심에 짓눌린 채 다른 무수한 사람들과 함께 이 일대를 우유부단하게 어슬렁거렸다. 자기보다 운 좋은 가족들도 더러 보였으니, 그들은 말 한 무리를 구해 거리를 덜컹거리며 빠져나가는 중이었다. 또 더러는 그와 처지가 같아, 아무리 뇌물을 쓰고 사정을 해도 도주에 필요한 수단을 구하지 못하는 이들도 있었다. 이 달아나려는 무리 가운데 조스는 베리에이커스 부인과 그 딸을 알아보았는데, 그들은 호텔의 마차 출입구에 세워 둔 마차에 앉아 있었고, 큰 짐가방은 죄다 꾸려져 있었으며, 그들의 도주를 가로막는 단 한 가지 걸림돌이란 조스를 발 묶어 놓은 것과 똑같은 동력의 부재였다.\n\n리베카 크롤리는 이 호텔에 거처를 두고 있었는데, 그전에 이미 베리에이커스 일가의 부인들과 여러 차례 적대적인 마주침을 겪은 터였다. 베리에이커스 부인은 우연히 계단에서 마주칠 때면 크롤리 부인을 본체만체했고, 후자의 이름이 거론되는 자리마다 그 이웃을 끈질기게 험담했다. 백작 부인은 터프토 장군이 부관의 아내와 허물없이 구는 것에 충격을 받았다. 블랜치 양은 그녀를 전염병이라도 되는 양 피했다. 오직 백작 본인만이 자기 집안 여자들의 관할에서 벗어나 있을 때면 이따금 그녀와 은근한 친분을 이어 갔다.\n\n리베카는 이제 이 오만한 적들에게 복수할 기회를 잡았다. 크롤리 대위의 말들이 남겨져 있다는 사실이 호텔에 알려졌고, 공황이 시작되자 베리에이커스 부인은 황송하게도 제 하녀를 대위 부인에게 보내 부인의 안부 인사와 함께 크롤리 부인의 말값을 알고 싶다는 뜻을 전했다. 크롤리 부인은 안부 인사와 함께, 자기는 하녀와 흥정하는 법이 없다는 뜻을 적은 쪽지로 답했다.\n\n이 무뚝뚝한 답장에 백작이 몸소 베키의 거처로 찾아왔지만, 첫 번째 사절보다 나을 것이 없었다. \"나한테 하녀를 보내다니!\" 크롤리 부인이 노발대발하며 외쳤다. \"차라리 베리에이커스 부인이 나더러 가서 말에 안장을 얹으라고 하시지 그래요! 도망치고 싶은 게 부인이에요, 아니면 부인의 하녀예요?\" 그리고 이것이 백작이 자기 부인에게 가지고 돌아간 대답의 전부였다.\n\n궁하면 못 할 일이 무엇이랴? 두 번째 사절마저 실패하자 백작 부인이 친히 크롤리 부인을 찾아왔다. 그녀는 부르는 값을 직접 매겨 보라고 간청했다. 심지어 자기 저택으로 돌아갈 수단만 마련해 준다면 베키를 베리에이커스 저택으로 초대하겠다고까지 했다. 크롤리 부인은 그녀를 비웃었다.\n\n\"제복 입은 빚쟁이의 시중을 받고 싶지는 않네요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"하긴 부인은 십중팔구 돌아가지도 못하실 텐데요. 적어도 부인과 다이아몬드가 함께는 말이죠. 그건 프랑스군 차지가 될 테니까요. 두 시간 안에 여기 들이닥칠 테고, 그때쯤이면 나는 헨트로 가는 길 절반쯤에 가 있을 거예요. 부인께 내 말을 팔지는 않겠어요. 안 팔아요, 무도회에서 부인이 걸쳤던 가장 큰 다이아몬드 두 개를 준다 해도 말이죠.\" 베리에이커스 부인은 분노와 공포로 부들부들 떨었다. 그 다이아몬드들은 그녀의 옷에 꿰매어져 있었고, 백작의 패딩과 장화 속에 숨겨져 있었다. \"이 계집아, 다이아몬드는 은행에 있고, 나는 기어이 그 말을 가져가야겠다.\" 그녀가 말했다. 리베카는 그 얼굴에 대고 웃었다. 격분한 백작 부인은 아래층으로 내려가 마차에 앉았다. 그녀의 하녀와 심부름꾼과 남편이 다시 한번 도시를 누비러 보내졌으니, 저마다 말을 찾으러였다. 그리고 가장 늦게 돌아오는 자에게 화 있을진저! 부인은 어느 쪽에서든 말이 도착하는 바로 그 순간 떠나기로―남편이 있든 없든―단단히 마음먹고 있었다.\n\n리베카는 말 없는 마차에 앉은 부인을 바라보며, 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 않고 더없이 큰 목소리로 백작 부인의 곤경을 애통해하는 즐거움을 누렸다. \"말을 못 구하시다니!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"게다가 그 다이아몬드를 죄다 마차 방석에 꿰매어 두셨다니! 프랑스군이 오면 얼마나 횡재가 되겠어요! 마차와 다이아몬드 말이에요. 부인 말고요!\" 그녀는 이 소식을 주인장에게, 하인들에게, 손님들에게, 그리고 안뜰을 어슬렁대는 무수한 부랑자들에게 떠벌렸다. 베리에이커스 부인은 마차 창문에서 그녀를 쏘아 죽이고 싶을 지경이었다.\n\n리베카가 이렇게 적의 굴욕을 즐기고 있을 때, 그녀의 눈에 조스가 들어왔다. 그는 그녀를 알아보자마자 곧장 그녀에게로 다가왔다.\n\n저 달라지고 겁에 질린 살진 얼굴이 그의 속내를 충분히 일러 주었다. 그 역시 달아나고 싶어 했고, 도주의 수단을 두리번거리고 있었던 것이다. \"저자가 내 말들을 사게 만들어야지.\" 리베카는 생각했다. \"그리고 나는 암말을 타면 되겠어.\"\n\n조스는 자기 벗에게로 걸어가, 지난 한 시간 동안 백 번째로 그 질문을 던졌다. 어디서 말을 구할 수 있는지 아십니까?\n\n\"아니, 당신이 달아난다고요?\" 리베카가 웃으며 말했다. \"나는 당신이 모든 숙녀의 수호자인 줄 알았는데요, 세들리 씨.\"\n\n\"나는―나는 군인이 아니라서요.\" 그가 헐떡이며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 애밀리아는요? 그 가엾은 여동생은 누가 지켜 주죠?\" 리베카가 물었다. \"설마 그 애를 버리지는 않으시겠죠?\"\n\n\"내가 그 애한테 무슨 도움이 되겠어요, 만약―만약 적이 들이닥친다면요?\" 조스가 대답했다. \"그자들은 여자들은 살려 줄 겁니다. 하지만 내 하인 말로는, 남자들에게는 절대 자비를 베풀지 않겠다고 맹세했다는군요. 그 비겁한 겁쟁이들이 말이에요.\"\n\n\"끔찍하기도 해라!\" 리베카가 그의 곤혹을 즐기며 외쳤다.\n\n\"게다가 나는 그 애를 버리고 싶지 않아요.\" 오라비가 외쳤다. \"그 애를 버려서는 안 되죠. 내 마차에 그 애 자리가 하나 있고, 당신 자리도 하나 있어요, 친애하는 크롤리 부인, 만약 함께 가 주신다면요. 그리고 말만 구할 수 있다면―\" 그가 한숨지었다.\n\n\"내가 팔 말이 두 마리 있어요.\" 부인이 말했다. 조스는 그 소식에 그녀의 품에라도 뛰어들 뻔했다. \"마차를 가져와, 이지도르.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"찾았다―말을 찾았어.\"\n\n\"내 말들은 마구를 채워 본 적이 없어요.\" 부인이 덧붙였다. \"불핀치는 멍에를 씌우면 마차를 발길질로 박살 내 버릴 거예요.\"\n\n\"하지만 타기에는 얌전한가요?\" 민간인이 물었다.\n\n\"양처럼 얌전하고 토끼처럼 빠르죠.\" 리베카가 대답했다.\n\n\"내 몸무게를 감당할 수 있을까요?\" 조스가 말했다. 그는 가엾은 애밀리아는 까맣게 잊은 채 벌써 상상 속에서 그 말 등에 올라타 있었다. 말 흥정을 좋아하는 사람이라면 누군들 이런 유혹을 떨칠 수 있겠는가?\n\n대답 대신 리베카는 그에게 자기 방으로 들어오라고 청했고, 그는 흥정을 매듭짓고자 숨을 헐떡이며 그녀를 따라 들어갔다. 조스가 평생 보낸 시간 중에 그토록 큰돈이 든 삼십 분은 좀처럼 없었다. 리베카는 팔 물건의 값을 조스가 사고 싶어 안달하는 정도와 그 물건의 희소함으로 가늠하여, 자기 말들에 어마어마한 값을 매겨 그 민간인조차 움찔 물러서게 만들었다. \"두 마리 다 사든지 아니면 한 마리도 안 팔든지 하겠어요.\" 그녀가 단호하게 말했다. 로던이 자기가 정한 값보다 싸게는 절대 내주지 말라고 명했다는 것이었다. 아래층의 베리에이커스 경도 같은 값을 쳐줄 거라고, 그리고 세들리 일가를 향한 온갖 애정과 존경에도 불구하고, 친애하는 조지프 씨도 가난한 사람은 먹고살아야 한다는 것쯤은 이해해 주셔야 한다고, 한마디로 그녀보다 더 다정한 사람은 없을 테지만 사업 문제에서만큼은 그녀보다 더 단호한 사람도 없을 거라는 것이었다.\n\n조스는 결국, 그다운 일이지만, 응낙하고 말았다. 그가 그녀에게 치러야 할 액수가 너무도 커서 그는 기한을 늦춰 달라고 청할 수밖에 없었다. 그것은 리베카에게는 작은 재산이라 할 만큼 큰 액수였으니, 그녀는 재빨리 계산해 보았다. 이 돈에다가 로던의 남은 물건들을 판 돈, 그리고 그가 쓰러질 경우 받을 미망인 연금까지 더하면, 이제 자기는 세상으로부터 완전히 독립하여 상복을 입은 자기 모습도 흔들림 없이 마주할 수 있겠다고 말이다.\n\n그날 하루에도 한두 번쯤은 그녀 자신도 분명 도주를 생각해 보았다. 그러나 이성이 그녀에게 더 나은 조언을 들려주었다. \"정말로 프랑스군이 온다 한들,\" 베키는 생각했다. \"가난한 장교의 미망인에게 무슨 짓을 할 수 있겠어? 흥! 약탈하고 포위하던 시대는 이제 지났어. 우리는 조용히 집으로 돌려보내질 테고, 아니면 나는 아담한 수입으로 외국에서 즐겁게 살 수도 있겠지.\"\n\n그러는 사이 조스와 이지도르는 새로 산 말을 살펴보러 마구간으로 갔다. 조스는 하인에게 당장 말에 안장을 얹으라 일렀다. 그는 바로 그날 밤, 바로 그 시각에 말을 타고 떠날 작정이었다. 그러고는 하인에게 말 채비를 시켜 놓고 자기는 떠날 준비를 하러 집으로 향했다. 이는 비밀이어야 했다. 그는 뒷문으로 해서 자기 방으로 갈 참이었다. 그는 오다우드 부인과 애밀리아를 마주하고서 자기가 달아나려 한다고 실토할 마음이 내키지 않았던 것이다.\n\n조스가 리베카와의 흥정을 매듭짓고 자기 말들을 둘러보고 살펴보았을 무렵에는 다시 거의 아침이 되어 있었다. 그러나 자정이 한참 지났는데도 도시에는 쉴 틈이 없었다. 사람들은 깨어 있었고, 집집마다 불빛이 타올랐으며, 군중은 여전히 문간을 에워싸고 거리는 분주했다. 갖가지 소문이 여전히 입에서 입으로 옮겨 다녔다. 한 소문은 프로이센군이 완전히 패했다 주장했고, 또 하나는 공격당해 정복된 것이 영국군이라 했으며, 세 번째 것은 영국군이 진지를 지켜 냈다고 했다. 이 마지막 소문이 차츰 힘을 얻어 갔다. 프랑스군은 모습을 드러내지 않았다. 낙오병들이 점점 더 유리한 소식을 가지고 군대에서 들어왔다. 마침내 한 부관이 실제로 브뤼셀에 도착하여 이 도시의 사령관에게 전갈을 전했고, 사령관은 이내 도시 곳곳에 카트르브라에서 연합군이 거둔 승리와 여섯 시간의 전투 끝에 네 휘하의 프랑스군을 완전히 격퇴했다는 공식 발표를 내붙였다. 그 부관은 조스와 리베카가 흥정을 벌이거나 그가 사들인 말을 둘러보던 어느 사이에 도착했음이 틀림없었다. 그가 자기 호텔에 이르렀을 때, 그 안에 묵는 수많은 거주민 중 스무 명가량이 문턱에 모여 그 소식을 논하고 있었다. 그것이 사실임에는 의심의 여지가 없었다. 그래서 그는 자기가 책임진 부인들에게 그것을 알리러 올라갔다. 다만 자기가 어떻게 그들과 작별하려 했는지, 어떻게 말을 샀는지, 그 값으로 얼마를 치렀는지는 그들에게 말할 필요가 없다고 여겼다.\n\n그러나 사랑하는 이들의 안위만을 생각하는 그들에게 승리냐 패배냐는 사소한 문제였다. 애밀리아는 승전 소식에 전보다도 한층 더 동요했다. 그녀는 당장이라도 군대로 가겠다고 했다. 눈물을 흘리며 오라비에게 자기를 그리로 데려다 달라고 간청했다. 그녀의 의심과 공포는 극에 달했고, 여러 시간 혼수에 가까운 상태에 빠져 있던 가엾은 처녀는 발작적인 광란에 사로잡혀 이리저리 헛소리를 하며 뛰어다녔으니, 보기에도 가련한 광경이었다. 십오 마일 떨어진 그 격전지에서―숱한 용사가 분투 끝에 누워 있는 그곳에서―고통에 몸부림치는 그 어떤 사내도, 전쟁의 이 가엾고 죄 없는 희생자보다 더 모질게 괴로워하지는 않았다. 조스는 그녀의 고통을 차마 볼 수가 없었다. 그는 누이를 더 건장한 여자 동무에게 맡겨 두고, 다시 한번 호텔 문턱으로 내려갔다. 거기에는 여전히 모두가 서성이며 이야기를 나누고 더 많은 소식을 기다리고 있었다.\n\n그들이 그곳에 서 있는 사이 날이 훤히 밝았고, 전장의 실제 당사자였던 이들이 가져온 새로운 소식이 도착하기 시작했다. 부상병을 가득 실은 짐마차와 기다란 시골 수레들이 덜컹거리며 도시로 굴러 들어왔다. 그 안에서는 끔찍한 신음 소리가 새어 나왔고, 초췌한 얼굴들이 짚더미 사이로 슬프게 올려다보았다. 조스 세들리는 고통스러운 호기심으로 그런 수레 하나를 바라보고 있었다. 안에 탄 사람들의 신음은 무시무시했고, 지친 말들은 수레를 좀처럼 끌지 못했다. \"멈춰요! 멈춰요!\" 짚더미 속에서 가냘픈 목소리가 외쳤고, 수레는 세들리 씨의 호텔 맞은편에 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"조지예요, 분명 조지라고요!\" 애밀리아가 외치며, 창백한 얼굴에 머리를 풀어 헤친 채 순식간에 발코니로 달려 나왔다. 그러나 조지는 아니었다. 다만 그다음으로 좋은 것이었으니, 그에 관한 소식이었다.\n\n그것은 가엾은 톰 스터블이었다. 그는 스물네 시간 전 연대기를 들고 그토록 늠름하게 브뤼셀을 행군해 나갔고, 전장에서 그 깃발을 더없이 용감하게 지켜 냈다. 프랑스 창기병 하나가 이 젊은 기수의 다리를 창으로 찔렀고, 그는 쓰러지면서도 여전히 용감하게 깃발을 부여잡고 있었다. 교전이 끝나자 사람들이 이 가엾은 소년에게 수레의 자리 하나를 마련해 주었고, 그는 브뤼셀로 실려 돌아온 것이었다.\n\n\"세들리 씨, 세들리 씨!\" 소년이 가냘프게 외쳤고, 조스는 그 부름에 거의 겁먹은 채로 다가갔다. 그는 처음에는 자기를 부른 사람이 누구인지 알아보지 못했다.\n\n어린 톰 스터블은 뜨겁고 힘없는 손을 내밀었다. \"저는 여기로 들어가게 될 거예요.\" 그가 말했다. \"오스본이―그리고―그리고 도빈이 그랬어요. 그리고 마부에게 나폴레옹 금화 두 닢을 주셔야 해요. 우리 어머니가 갚으실 거예요.\" 수레에서 열에 들떠 보낸 그 긴 시간 동안, 이 젊은 친구의 생각은 불과 몇 달 전에 떠나온 아버지의 목사관을 헤매고 있었고, 그 헛소리 속에서 그는 이따금 고통조차 잊었던 것이다.\n\n호텔은 넓었고 사람들은 친절했으므로, 수레에 탄 이들은 모두 안으로 옮겨져 갖가지 침상에 뉘었다. 젊은 기수는 위층 오스본의 거처로 옮겨졌다. 발코니에서 그를 알아본 소령의 부인과 애밀리아가 그에게로 달려 내려와 있었다. 그날의 전투가 끝났고 두 사람의 남편이 모두 무사하다는 말을 들었을 때 이 여인들의 심정이 어떠했을지 짐작이 갈 것이다. 애밀리아가 어떤 말 없는 환희에 젖어 그 좋은 벗의 목에 매달려 끌어안았는지, 어떤 감사에 사무친 기도의 격정으로 무릎을 꿇고 자기 남편을 구해 주신 그분께 감사드렸는지를 말이다.\n\n열에 들뜨고 신경이 곤두선 우리 아가씨에게는, 그 어떤 의사가 처방해 준다 한들 우연이 그녀 앞에 놓아 준 것보다 더 약이 되는 것은 없었을 것이다. 그녀와 오다우드 부인은 고통이 몹시 심한 부상당한 소년의 곁을 잠시도 떠나지 않고 지켰고, 그리하여 떠맡게 된 이 임무 덕분에 애밀리아는 제 개인적인 근심을 곱씹거나 평소 버릇대로 제 공포와 불길한 예감에 자신을 내맡길 겨를이 없었다. 젊은 환자는 그 소박한 방식으로 그날의 사건들과, 그 용맹한 ××연대에 속한 우리 벗들의 활약을 들려주었다. 그들은 큰 피해를 입었다. 숱한 장교와 병사를 잃었다. 연대가 돌격할 때 소령은 타고 있던 말이 그 밑에서 총에 맞아 쓰러졌고, 다들 오다우드가 죽었으며 도빈이 소령 자리를 물려받았으리라 여겼다. 그러나 돌격을 마치고 원래 자리로 돌아와 보니, 소령은 죽은 피라무스의 시체 위에 걸터앉아 휴대용 술병으로 기운을 차리고 있었다. 그 기수를 창으로 찌른 프랑스 창기병을 베어 쓰러뜨린 것은 오스본 대위였다. 이 대목에서 애밀리아가 어찌나 새파랗게 질렸던지, 오다우드 부인은 젊은 기수의 이야기를 멈춰 세웠다. 그리고 그날이 저물 무렵, 자기도 부상을 입은 몸으로 소년을 두 팔에 안아 군의관에게로, 다시 그를 브뤼셀로 실어다 줄 수레로 데려간 것은 도빈 대위였다. 그리고 마부에게 도시에 있는 세들리 씨의 호텔까지 길을 찾아가 주면 루이 금화 두 닢을 주겠노라 약속하고, 오스본 대위 부인에게 전투가 끝났으며 남편은 다치지 않고 무사하다고 전해 달라 한 것도 바로 그였다.\n\n\"정말이지, 그 윌리엄 도빈은 마음씨가 참 곱답니다.\" 오다우드 부인이 말했다. \"늘 나를 보고 웃어 대긴 하지만요.\"\n\n어린 스터블은 군대에 그런 장교는 또 없다고 단언하며, 그 선임 대위의 겸손함과 친절함과 전장에서의 감탄스러운 침착함에 대한 칭찬을 그칠 줄 몰랐다. 이런 대목의 이야기에 애밀리아는 몹시 산만한 주의밖에 기울이지 못했다. 그녀가 귀를 기울이는 것은 오로지 조지의 이야기가 나올 때뿐이었고, 그가 거론되지 않을 때면 그녀는 그를 생각하고 있었다.\n\n환자를 돌보는 일과, 전날의 그 놀라운 모면들을 생각하는 일로, 애밀리아의 둘째 날은 너무 더디지 않게 지나갔다. 그녀에게 군대 안에 사내라고는 오직 하나뿐이었다. 그리고 그가 무사한 한, 군대의 움직임이 그녀의 관심을 거의 끌지 못했다는 것은 인정해야 하리라. 조스가 거리에서 가져온 온갖 소식은 그녀의 귀에 매우 어렴풋하게만 들렸다. 비록 그 소식이 저 겁 많은 신사와 그때 브뤼셀에 있던 숱한 사람들에게는 온갖 불안을 안겨 주기에 충분했지만 말이다. 프랑스군이 격퇴된 것은 분명했으나, 그것은 혹독하고 승패를 가늠하기 어려운 격전 끝에, 그것도 프랑스군의 한 개 사단만을 상대로 한 일이었다. 본대를 거느린 황제는 리니에 가 있었는데, 거기서 프로이센군을 완전히 섬멸하고, 이제 전군을 연합군에 쏟아부을 수 있게 자유로운 처지였다. 웰링턴 공작은 수도를 향해 후퇴하고 있었고, 십중팔구 그 성벽 아래서 대전투를 치러야 할 터인데 그 승산이란 매우 미덥지 못했다. 웰링턴 공작에게는 믿을 만한 영국군이 이만 명뿐이었으니, 독일군은 풋내기 민병대요 벨기에군은 불만에 차 있는 터라, 이 한 줌의 병력으로 공작 각하는 나폴레옹의 지휘 아래 벨기에로 쳐들어온 십오만 대군에 맞서야 했다. 나폴레옹의 지휘라니! 아무리 이름났고 솜씨 좋은 무장이라 한들, 그를 상대로 불리한 형세에서 싸울 수 있는 자가 어디 있단 말인가?\n\n조스는 이 모든 것을 생각하며 떨었다. 브뤼셀의 나머지 사람들도 다 마찬가지였으니, 사람들은 전날의 싸움이 임박한 더 큰 전투의 서막에 불과하다고 느끼고 있었다. 황제에 맞선 두 군대 중 하나는 이미 바람결에 흩어져 버렸다. 그에게 맞서 싸우러 나설 수 있는 얼마 안 되는 영국군은 제 자리에서 스러질 것이고, 정복자는 그들의 시체를 밟고 도시로 들어설 것이었다. 그가 거기서 발견하는 자들에게 화 있을진저! 환영의 인사말이 준비되었고, 공직자들이 모여 은밀히 의논했으며, 거처들이 갖춰지고, 황제 폐하이자 국왕이신 분의 도래를 맞이하기 위해 삼색기와 개선의 상징물들이 만들어졌다.\n\n피난은 여전히 계속되었고, 가족들은 떠날 방도를 찾을 수 있는 곳이면 어디서든 달아났다. 조스가 6월 17일 오후에 리베카의 호텔로 가 보니, 그 으리으리한 베리에이커스의 마차가 마침내 마차 출입구를 빠져나가 굴러간 뒤였다. 백작은 크롤리 부인에도 아랑곳없이 어떻게든 말 한 쌍을 손에 넣어, 헨트로 가는 길을 굴러가고 있었다. 백성들이 그토록 고대하던 루이 왕도 그 도시에서 자기 여행 가방을 꾸리고 있는 참이었다. 불운은 저 굼뜬 망명객을 들쑤셔 몰아대는 일에 도무지 지칠 줄을 모르는 듯했다.\n\n조스는 어제의 지체가 한낱 잠깐의 유예에 지나지 않았으며, 비싼 값을 치르고 산 자기 말들을 분명 곧 동원해야 하리라는 것을 느꼈다. 그날 내내 그의 고뇌는 몹시 혹독했다. 브뤼셀과 나폴레옹 사이에 영국군이 버티고 있는 한 당장 달아날 필요는 없었다. 그러나 그는 멀리 떨어진 마구간에 있던 자기 말들을 자기가 사는 호텔 안뜰의 마구간으로 옮겨 오게 했으니, 그래야 제 눈으로 지켜볼 수 있고 난폭하게 빼앗길 위험에서도 벗어날 수 있었기 때문이다. 이지도르는 마구간 문을 끊임없이 지키며 말에 안장을 얹어, 언제든 출발할 수 있게 준비해 두었다. 그는 그 순간이 오기를 간절히 갈망했다.\n\n전날의 푸대접을 받은 뒤로, 리베카는 사랑하는 애밀리아 곁에 갈 마음이 내키지 않았다. 그녀는 조지가 갖다준 꽃다발을 다듬고, 꽃에 새 물을 주고, 그가 보낸 편지를 거듭 읽었다. \"가엾은 것.\" 그녀가 그 작은 종잇조각을 손가락으로 빙글빙글 돌리며 말했다. \"이걸로 내가 저 애를 짓뭉갤 수도 있는데! 게다가 이런 것 때문에 저 애가 가슴이 찢어진다는 거잖아. 멍청하고, 멋이나 부리고, 저 애한테는 관심도 없는 사내 때문에 말이지. 우리 가엾고 착한 로던이 이런 작자 열 명만큼은 값어치가 있는데.\" 그러고는 만약 가엾고 착한 로던에게 무슨 일이 생긴다면 자기는 어찌해야 할지를 생각하기 시작했고, 그가 말들을 남기고 떠난 것이 얼마나 큰 행운인지를 곱씹었다.\n\n또한 이날 하루 동안, 베리에이커스 일행이 떠나는 것을 노여움 없이 보지는 못했던 크롤리 부인은, 백작 부인이 취했던 예방책을 떠올리고는 제 잇속을 위해 약간의 바느질을 했다. 그녀는 제 패물과 어음과 지폐의 태반을 몸에 꿰매어 두었고, 그렇게 채비를 갖추고는 어떤 일이 닥치든 대비할 수 있었다. 적당하다 싶으면 달아날 수도, 아니면 남아서 정복자를 맞이할 수도 있었으니, 그가 영국인이든 프랑스인이든 상관없었다. 그리고 나는 그녀가 그날 밤 공작 부인이나 원수 부인이 되는 꿈을 꾸지 않았으리라고는 장담하지 못하겠다. 그러는 사이 로던은 외투를 두르고 몽생장의 빗속에서 노숙하며, 자기가 뒤에 두고 온 그 작은 아내를 가슴이 사무치도록 온 힘을 다해 생각하고 있었다.\n\n이튿날은 일요일이었다. 그리고 오다우드 소령 부인은 자기가 돌보는 두 환자가 밤사이 취한 약간의 휴식으로 건강과 기운을 되찾은 것을 보고 흐뭇해했다. 그녀 자신은 애밀리아의 방 큰 의자에서 잠을 잤는데, 가엾은 벗이나 기수가 간호를 필요로 하면 언제든 시중들 채비를 한 채였다. 아침이 오자 이 건장한 여인은 자기와 소령이 묵는 숙소로 돌아가, 그날에 걸맞은 공들이고 화려한 몸단장을 했다. 그리고 남편이 거처했던 그 방, 그의 모자가 여전히 베개 위에 놓이고 지팡이가 구석에 세워져 있는 그 방에 홀로 있는 동안, 적어도 한 가지 기도만큼은 그 용감한 군인 마이클 오다우드의 안녕을 위해 하늘로 올려보냈을 가능성이 매우 높다.\n\n돌아올 때 그녀는 기도서와, 학장인 숙부의 그 유명한 설교집을 가지고 왔다. 그녀는 안식일이면 어김없이 그 책에서 한 대목을 읽곤 했는데, 다 이해하지는 못했을 테고, 길고 난해한 낱말들을 더러 제대로 발음하지도 못했을 것이다. 학장이 학식 있는 인물이라 긴 라틴어 낱말을 좋아한 까닭이었다. 그러나 그녀는 자못 엄숙하게, 한껏 힘주어, 대체로는 그런대로 정확하게 읽어 나갔다. 우리 미크가 이 설교를 얼마나 자주 들었던가, 그녀는 생각했다. 잔잔한 바다 위 선실에서 내가 읽어 주면 말이지! 그녀는 오늘은 애밀리아와 부상당한 기수를 회중 삼아 이 일과를 다시 해 볼 작정이었다. 같은 예배가 그날 그 시각에 이만 곳의 교회에서 거행되고 있었고, 수백만의 영국 남녀가 무릎을 꿇고 만유의 아버지께 가호를 빌고 있었다.\n\n그들에게는 브뤼셀의 우리 작은 회중을 어지럽힌 그 소음이 들리지 않았다. 이틀 전 그들의 예배를 가로막았던 것보다 훨씬 더 우렁차게, 오다우드 부인이 가장 고운 목소리로 예배문을 읽고 있던 그때, 워털루의 대포가 포효하기 시작했다.\n\n조스는 그 무시무시한 소리를 듣자, 이 끝없이 되풀이되는 공포를 더는 견디지 못하겠으며 당장 달아나겠노라 마음을 굳혔다. 그는 우리 세 벗이 기도를 멈춘 그 병자의 방으로 뛰어들어, 애밀리아에게 격정에 찬 호소를 퍼부으며 그들을 한 번 더 가로막았다.\n\n\"더는 못 참겠다, 에미.\" 그가 말했다. \"안 참을 거야. 그러니 너는 나랑 같이 가야 해. 너를 위해 말 한 마리를 사 두었어―값이 얼마든 상관없으니까―어서 채비하고 나랑 같이 가서 이지도르 뒤에 타거라.\"\n\n\"하느님 용서하소서, 세들리 씨, 하지만 당신은 겁쟁이나 진배없군요.\" 오다우드 부인이 책을 내려놓으며 말했다.\n\n\"가자니까, 애밀리아.\" 민간인이 말을 이었다. \"저 여자가 뭐라든 신경 쓰지 마. 우리가 왜 여기 남아서 프랑스 놈들한테 도륙당해야 하느냐고?\"\n\n\"형은 ××연대를 잊으셨어요.\" 부상당한 영웅 어린 스터블이 침대에서 말했다. \"그리고―그리고 부인은 저를 두고 가지 않으실 거죠, 오다우드 부인?\"\n\n\"그럼, 안 두고말고, 얘야.\" 그녀가 다가가 소년에게 입을 맞추며 말했다. \"내가 곁에 있는 한 너한테는 아무 일도 없을 거다. 나는 미크한테서 기별이 올 때까지는 꿈쩍도 안 할 거야. 안 그래도 저 사내 뒤에 안장에 끼어 앉으면 내 꼴이 가관이겠다, 안 그러냐?\"\n\n이 광경이 떠올라 젊은 환자는 침대에서 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 애밀리아마저 미소 짓게 만들었다. \"저 여자한테 부탁하는 게 아니야.\" 조스가 소리쳤다. \"저―저 아일랜드 여자한테 부탁하는 게 아니라 너한테 하는 거다, 애밀리아. 마지막으로 묻는다, 가겠느냐?\"\n\n\"제 남편도 없이요, 조지프 오라버니?\" 애밀리아가 의아한 눈빛으로 말하며 소령의 부인에게 손을 내밀었다. 조스의 인내심은 바닥나고 말았다.\n\n\"그럼 잘 있어라.\" 그가 화가 나서 주먹을 흔들며 말하고는, 물러나면서 문을 쾅 닫았다. 그리고 이번에는 정말로 진군 명령을 내렸으니, 안뜰에서 말에 올라탔다. 오다우드 부인은 말들이 문을 빠져나가며 또각거리는 발굽 소리를 들었다. 그리고 내다보며, 가엾은 조지프가 금몰 모자를 쓴 이지도르를 뒤에 거느리고 거리를 내려가는 모습을 두고 갖은 비웃음 섞인 말을 늘어놓았다. 며칠 동안 부려지지 않은 말들은 기운이 펄펄해서 거리를 이리저리 날뛰었다. 둔하고 겁 많은 기수인 조스는 안장 위에서 볼품없는 꼴이었다. \"저것 좀 봐라, 애밀리아야, 응접실 창문으로 처박을 기세구나. 도자기 가게에 든 황소가 따로 없네.\" 그러더니 이내 두 기수는 헨트 가도로 이어지는 거리를 따라 느린 구보로 사라져 갔고, 오다우드 부인은 그들이 시야에 보이는 동안 내내 빈정거림의 포화를 퍼부어 댔다.\n\n그날 아침부터 해 질 녘이 지나도록 대포는 포효를 멈추지 않았다. 포성이 별안간 그쳤을 때는 이미 어둑했다.\n\n우리 모두 그 사이에 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 읽어서 안다. 그 이야기는 영국인이라면 누구나 입에 올리는 것이며, 그 대전투가 이기고 진 무렵에 어린아이였던 당신과 나는 그 유명한 전투의 역사를 듣고 또 들려주기를 결코 지겨워하지 않는다. 그날의 패배를 맛본 저 용감한 자들의 동포 수백만의 가슴속에는 그 기억이 여전히 사무쳐 있다. 그들은 그 굴욕을 갚을 기회를 갈망한다. 그리고 만약 그들 편의 승리로 끝나는 또 한 번의 다툼이 벌어져, 이번에는 그들을 우쭐하게 만들고 그 저주받은 증오와 분노의 유산을 우리에게 남긴다면, 두 혈기 왕성한 민족이 벌일 그 이른바 영광과 치욕, 성공한 살육과 실패한 살육의 번갈음에는 끝이 없을 것이다. 수백 년이 흐른 뒤에도 우리 프랑스인과 영국인은 여전히 서로 으스대며 죽이고 있을지도 모른다. 악마의 명예 규범을 용감하게 받들면서 말이다.\n\n우리 벗들은 모두 그 대전장에서 제 몫을 다하며 사내답게 싸웠다. 온종일, 여인들이 십 마일 떨어진 곳에서 기도하는 동안, 겁 없는 영국 보병의 대열은 프랑스 기병의 맹렬한 돌격을 받아 내고 또 물리쳤다. 브뤼셀에서까지 들리던 대포가 그들의 대열을 갈아엎었고, 전우들이 쓰러졌으며, 굳센 생존자들은 빈자리를 메우며 다가섰다. 저녁 무렵이 되자, 그토록 거듭되고 그토록 용감하게 저지당한 프랑스군의 공격은 그 맹위가 누그러졌다. 그들에게는 영국군 말고도 상대해야 할 다른 적들이 있었거나, 아니면 마지막 일격을 준비하고 있었던 것이다. 마침내 그것이 왔다. 황제 근위대의 종대가 생장 언덕을 올라왔다. 마침내, 그리고 단번에, 영국군을 그들이 온종일 무슨 일이 있어도 지켜 낸 그 고지에서 쓸어버리기 위해서였다. 영국군 대열에서 죽음을 쏟아붓는 포의 천둥소리에도 겁먹지 않고, 그 검은 종대는 언덕을 따라 위로 밀고 올라왔다. 그것이 거의 그 정상을 차지할 듯하더니, 흔들리며 휘청거리기 시작했다. 그러고는 멈춰 섰으니, 여전히 포화를 마주한 채였다. 그러다 마침내 영국군이, 그 어떤 적도 몰아내지 못했던 그 진지에서 쏟아져 나왔고, 근위대는 등을 돌려 달아났다.\n\n브뤼셀에서는 더 이상 포성이 들리지 않았다. 추격은 수 마일 멀리로 굴러갔다. 어둠이 전장과 도시 위로 내려앉았다. 그리고 애밀리아는 조지를 위해 기도하고 있었다. 그 조지는 얼굴을 땅에 묻은 채, 심장을 꿰뚫은 총탄을 맞고 죽어 누워 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIII\n\n친애하는 독자께서는, 군대가 플랑드르에서 진군해 나와 그곳에서 영웅적인 전투를 치른 뒤 프랑스 국경의 요새를 점령하고자, 그 나라를 점령하기에 앞서 나아가고 있는 동안에도, 지금 다루고 있는 이야기와 관련된 사람들이 영국에서 평온하게 살아가고 있으며, 그들 또한 이 연대기에서 제 몫을 차지해야 마땅하다는 점을 부디 기억해 주시기 바란다. 이 전투와 위난의 시절 내내, 늙은 크롤리 양은 브라이턴에서 지내며, 한창 벌어지고 있는 그 위대한 사건들에 그다지 마음을 동요하지 않았다. 물론 그 위대한 사건들 덕분에 신문이 제법 흥미로워지기는 했고, 브리그스가 관보를 소리 내어 읽어 주었는데, 거기에는 로던 크롤리의 무용이 명예롭게 언급되어 있었으며, 그의 진급도 곧 기록되었다.\n\n\"그 젊은이가 세상에서 그토록 돌이킬 수 없는 걸음을 내디뎠다니 얼마나 딱한 일인가!\" 그의 고모가 말했다. \"그만한 지위와 명망이라면 그레인스 양처럼 이십오만 파운드를 지닌 양조업자의 딸과 결혼할 수도 있었을 테고, 아니면 영국에서 가장 좋은 가문들과 연을 맺어 볼 수도 있었을 게다. 언젠가는 내 재산을 물려받았을 테고, 아니면 그 자식들이라도 물려받았을 테지. 나는 그리 서둘러 갈 생각이 없으니까 말이다, 브리그스 양, 비록 자네는 어서 나를 떨쳐 버리고 싶어 안달이 났을지 몰라도. 그런데 그러기는커녕, 그 애는 춤추는 계집을 아내로 둔, 파멸한 거지 신세로구나.\"\n\n\"그렇지만 친애하는 크롤리 양께서는, 조국 영광의 연대기에 그 이름이 새겨진 그 영웅적인 군인에게 연민의 눈길 한 번 주시지 않으렵니까?\" 브리그스 양이 말했다. 그녀는 워털루 사건에 몹시 흥분해 있었고, 기회만 있으면 낭만적으로 말하기를 좋아하는 사람이었다. \"그 대위께서—아니 이제는 대령이라 불러도 될 분이지요—크롤리라는 이름을 빛나게 할 위업을 이루지 않으셨습니까?\"\n\n\"브리그스, 자네는 바보로구나.\" 크롤리 양이 말했다. \"크롤리 대령은 크롤리라는 이름을 진흙탕에 끌고 들어갔어, 브리그스 양. 미술 선생의 딸과 결혼을 하다니, 원!—말동무로 데리고 있던 계집과 결혼을 하다니. 그 애도 그 이상은 못 되는 것이었어, 브리그스. 아니, 그 애는 딱 자네 같은 처지였지—다만 더 젊고, 훨씬 더 예쁘고 영악했을 뿐. 자네는 혹시 그 못된 계집의 공범이었던 게 아닌가 싶구나. 그 애의 더러운 술수에 걸려든 게 그 애였고, 자네는 그 계집을 그토록 떠받들었으니 말이야. 그래, 분명 자네도 공범이었을 게다. 하지만 내 유언장에서는 실망하게 될 게야, 그건 내가 장담하지. 그러니 수고스럽겠지만 왁시 씨에게 편지를 써서, 내가 그 사람을 당장 보고 싶어 한다고 전해 주게.\" 크롤리 양은 이제 거의 일주일에 하루도 빠짐없이 변호사 왁시 씨에게 편지를 쓰는 버릇이 들어 있었다. 자기 재산에 관한 처분을 모조리 무효로 돌려놓은 터라, 앞으로 그 돈을 어떻게 처리할지를 두고 그녀의 고민이 이만저만이 아니었기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 이 노처녀는 상당히 기력을 되찾은 상태였다. 브리그스 양을 향한 그녀의 빈정거림이 한층 기세등등해지고 잦아진 것이 그 증거였다. 가엾은 말동무는 그 모든 공격을 온순하게, 겁먹은 채로, 반쯤은 너그럽고 반쯤은 위선적인 체념으로—한마디로, 그녀와 같은 기질과 처지의 여자들이 어쩔 수 없이 보여야 하는 노예 같은 굴종으로 견뎌 냈다. 여자가 여자를 어떻게 들볶는지 보지 못한 사람이 어디 있겠는가? 가엾은 여자들이 같은 성(性)의 폭군들에게서 날마다 거듭 쏘아 맞는 그 멸시와 잔혹의 화살에 견줄 만한 고문을, 남자들이 과연 견뎌 본 적이 있던가? 가엾은 희생자들이여! 그런데 우리는 우리의 명제에서 벗어나고 있다. 그 명제란, 크롤리 양은 병에서 회복될 때면 언제나 유난히 사람을 성가시게 하고 사나워졌다는 것이다—상처가 아물려고 할 때 가장 욱신거린다고들 하듯이 말이다.\n\n이렇듯 모두가 바라던 대로 회복기에 접어드는 동안, 브리그스 양만이 그 환자 앞에 들 수 있는 유일한 희생양이었다. 그렇지만 멀리 떨어진 크롤리 양의 친척들은 그 사랑하는 친족을 잊지 않았으니, 갖가지 정표와 선물, 다정하고 애정 어린 안부를 끊임없이 보내며 그녀의 기억 속에서 자기들을 잊히지 않게 하려고 애를 썼다.\n\n먼저, 그녀의 조카 로던 크롤리부터 이야기하자. 저 유명한 워털루 전투가 끝나고 몇 주 뒤, 관보가 그 빼어난 장교의 진급과 무용을 그녀에게 알린 다음, 디에프발(發) 우편선이 그 조카 대령이 보낸 선물 상자 하나와 정중한 편지 한 통을 브라이턴의 크롤리 양에게 실어 왔다. 상자 안에는 프랑스군 견장 한 쌍과 레지옹 도뇌르 십자 훈장 하나, 그리고 칼자루 하나가 들어 있었다—모두 전쟁터에서 거둔 유물이었다. 그리고 편지에는 그 칼자루에 얽힌 사연이 제법 익살스럽게 적혀 있었다. 그것은 어느 근위대 지휘관의 것이었는데, 그자가 \"근위대는 죽을지언정 결코 항복하지 않는다\"고 호언장담해 놓고는 바로 다음 순간 일개 사병에게 포로로 잡혔고, 그 사병이 머스킷 개머리판으로 그 프랑스인의 칼을 부러뜨리자 로던이 그 부서진 무기를 차지하게 되었다는 것이었다. 십자 훈장과 견장으로 말하자면, 그것은 전투 중 부관 로던의 손에 쓰러진 어느 프랑스 기병대 대령에게서 나온 것이었다. 그리고 로던 크롤리는 그 전리품을 자신의 더없이 다정하고 더없이 애정 깊은 늙은 벗에게 보내는 것보다 더 좋은 처리법을 알지 못했다. 군대가 진군해 가는 파리에서도 계속 그녀에게 편지를 써도 되겠습니까? 그 수도에서 벌어지는 흥미로운 소식과, 어려운 시절 크롤리 양이 그토록 친절을 베풀어 주었던 망명객 시절의 옛 벗들 몇몇에 관한 소식도 전해 드릴 수 있을 것입니다.\n\n이 노처녀는 브리그스를 시켜 대령에게 우아하고 칭찬 가득한 답장을 써 보내, 계속 편지를 보내라고 격려하게 했다. 그의 첫 편지가 어찌나 활기차고 재미있었던지, 그녀는 다음 편지들을 즐거운 마음으로 기다리겠노라고 했다. \"물론 나도 알지.\" 그녀가 브리그스 양에게 설명했다. \"로던이 그렇게 잘 쓴 편지를 쓸 리가 없다는 건, 가엾은 우리 브리그스 자네가 못 쓰는 것이나 마찬가지야. 그건 그 영악하고 못된 작은 계집 리베카가 한 마디 한 마디 받아쓰게 한 게 분명해. 하지만 그렇다고 내 조카가 나를 즐겁게 해 주면 안 될 까닭은 없지. 그러니 내가 아주 기분이 좋다는 걸 그 애가 알게 해 주고 싶구나.\"\n\n편지를 쓴 것이 베키만이 아니라, 실은 로던 부인이 그 전리품들을, 전쟁이 끝나기 무섭게 그 유물들을 팔기 시작한 수많은 행상 가운데 하나에게서 몇 프랑에 사들여 직접 집으로 부쳤다는 사실을, 과연 크롤리 양이 알고 있었을까. 모든 것을 아는 소설가는 이것 또한 알고 있다. 그러나 어찌 되었든, 크롤리 양의 우아한 답장은 우리의 젊은 벗들, 즉 로던과 그의 아내에게 큰 용기를 주었다. 분명 누그러진 고모의 기분으로 미루어 그들은 잘되리라 기대했고, 그래서 파리에서 보내는 즐거운 편지를 잔뜩 띄워 그녀를 즐겁게 해 주는 데 신경을 썼다. 로던의 말마따나, 그들은 운 좋게도 승리한 군대의 뒤를 따라 파리로 가게 된 것이었다.\n\n그러나 남편의 부러진 쇄골을 돌보러 퀸스크롤리의 목사관으로 떠나 버린 목사 부인에게 보낸 이 노처녀의 소식은 결코 그토록 우아하지가 않았다. 저 활기차고 일 잘하고 발랄하면서도 위압적인 여인 뷰트 부인은, 시누이를 대하는 일에서 무엇보다 치명적인 실수를 저지르고 만 것이었다. 그녀는 크롤리 양과 그 살림을 억압했을 뿐만 아니라—크롤리 양을 따분하게 했던 것이다. 그러니 가엾은 브리그스 양이 조금이라도 기개가 있는 여자였더라면, 자기 주인이 그녀에게 맡긴 임무에 흐뭇해했을 만도 했다. 그 임무란, 뷰트 크롤리 부인에게 편지를 써서 크롤리 양의 건강이 뷰트 부인이 떠난 뒤로 크게 좋아졌으니, 부디 크롤리 양을 위해 수고를 하거나 가족을 버려두고 오는 일은 결코 하지 말라고 전하라는 것이었다. 브리그스 양에게 그토록 거만하고 모질게 굴었던 부인을 상대로 거둔 이 승리를 대부분의 여자라면 기뻐했을 테지만, 사실 브리그스는 기개라고는 도무지 없는 여자여서, 자기 적이 낭패를 보는 순간 도리어 그 편을 동정하기 시작했다.\n\n\"내가 어쩌면 그리도 어리석었던가.\" 뷰트 부인은 그런 생각이 들었고, 그럴 만도 했다. \"크롤리 양에게 뿔닭을 보내면서 그 어리석은 편지에다 내가 가겠노라고 넌지시 비춘 게 잘못이었어. 그 가엾고 망령 난 늙은이를 두고 한마디 말도 없이 그냥 가서, 저 멍청한 브리그스와 하피 같은 그 시녀 손아귀에서 빼내 왔어야 했는데. 오! 뷰트, 뷰트, 당신은 어쩌자고 쇄골을 부러뜨린 거예요?\"\n\n정말이지, 어쩌자고? 뷰트 부인이 패를 손에 쥐고도 정작 카드를 너무 잘 쳐 버린 셈이었음을 우리는 이미 보았다. 그녀는 크롤리 양의 살림을 완전무결하게 틀어쥐고 다스렸으나, 반란의 절호의 기회가 찾아오자 완전무결하게 패주하고 만 것이었다. 그러나 그녀와 그 식구들은 자기들이 끔찍한 이기심과 배신의 희생양이 되었으며, 크롤리 양을 위해 바친 그 모든 희생이 더없이 야만적인 배은망덕으로 되돌아왔다고 여겼다. 로던의 진급과, 관보에 그의 이름이 명예롭게 언급된 일은 이 선량한 그리스도교도 부인 또한 불안에 빠뜨렸다. 그가 이제 중령에다 바스 훈장 수훈자가 되었으니 고모가 그에게 마음을 누그러뜨리지나 않을까? 그리고 저 가증스러운 리베카가 다시 한번 총애를 얻게 되지나 않을까? 목사의 아내는 군사적 영광의 헛됨과 악인의 번영에 관한 설교문을 한 편 써서 남편에게 주었고, 이 훌륭한 목사는 그것을 한 음절도 이해하지 못한 채 가장 멋들어진 목소리로 낭독했다. 그 청중 가운데에는 피트 크롤리도 있었다—두 이복 누이를 데리고 교회에 온 피트였다. 늙은 준남작은 이제 도무지 교회에 발걸음하게 만들 수가 없는 형편이었기 때문이다.\n\n베키 샤프가 떠난 뒤로, 그 늙은 망나니는 온통 못된 짓에 빠져 지냈으니, 그 일대의 큰 추문거리가 되었고 아들에게는 말 못 할 공포의 근원이 되었다. 호럭스 양의 모자에 달린 리본은 그 어느 때보다 화려해졌다. 점잖은 가문들은 겁에 질려 그 저택과 주인을 피해 달아났다. 피트 경은 소작인들의 집을 돌아다니며 술을 퍼마셨고, 장날이면 머드베리와 인근 마을에서 농부들과 럼주에 물을 타 마셨다. 그는 호럭스 양을 안에 태우고 사두마차를 몰아 사우샘프턴까지 갔다. 그러니 그 지방 사람들은 매주, 그 아들이 말문이 막힌 채 괴로워하며 예상하듯, 신문에 그와 그녀의 결혼이 공표되지나 않을까 기다리는 것이었다. 그것은 참으로 크롤리 씨에게 견디기 힘든 무거운 짐이었다. 그가 으레 좌장을 맡아 몇 시간씩 연설을 늘어놓곤 하던 인근의 선교 집회며 다른 종교 모임에서, 그의 웅변은 마비되어 버렸다. 그가 일어설 때면 청중이 \"저자가 바로 그 늙은 난봉꾼 피트 경의 아들이렷다. 그 아비는 바로 이 순간에도 술집에서 술을 퍼마시고 있기 십상이지\" 하고 수군거리는 것만 같았기 때문이다. 한번은 그가 팀북투 왕의 어두운 처지와, 마찬가지로 어둠 속에 있는 그 수많은 아내들 이야기를 하고 있는데, 군중 속의 어떤 집시 불량배가 \"퀸스크롤리에는 몇이나 있소, 점잔 빼는 젊은 양반?\" 하고 물어, 연단 사람들을 놀라게 하고 피트 씨의 연설을 망쳐 버렸다. 또한 퀸스크롤리 가문의 두 딸은 하마터면 아주 제멋대로 자라도록 방치될 뻔했다(피트 경이 다시는 가정교사 따위가 자기 집 문턱을 넘게 두지 않겠다고 맹세했기 때문이다). 크롤리 씨가 그 늙은 양반을 으름장으로 윽박질러, 기어이 두 딸을 학교에 보내게 만들지 않았더라면 말이다.\n\n그러는 동안, 앞서 말했듯, 그들 사이에 어떤 개인적인 알력이 있건 간에, 크롤리 양의 사랑하는 조카와 조카딸들은 한결같이 그녀를 사랑했고 애정의 정표를 보내는 데 의견이 일치했다. 그리하여 뷰트 부인은 뿔닭과 유난히 실한 콜리플라워 몇 통, 그리고 자기 귀여운 딸들이 손수 수놓은 예쁜 지갑인지 바늘꽂이인지를 보냈다. 딸들은 사랑하는 고모할머니의 기억 속에 조그만 자리나마 남게 해 달라고 청했다. 한편 피트 씨는 저택에서 복숭아와 포도, 사슴 고기를 보냈다. 사우샘프턴발 마차가 이 애정의 정표들을 브라이턴의 크롤리 양에게 실어 나르곤 했고, 때로는 피트 씨 본인을 그곳으로 실어 가기도 했다. 피트 경과의 불화 때문에 크롤리 씨가 요즘 집을 자주 비우게 된 데다, 게다가 브라이턴에는 그를 끌어당기는 사람이 하나 있었기 때문이다. 바로 레이디 제인 십섕크스로, 그녀와 크롤리 씨의 약혼은 이 이야기에서 앞서 언급한 바 있다. 그녀와 자매들은 어머니인 사우스다운 백작 부인과 함께 브라이턴에서 지냈는데, 이 백작 부인은 신앙심 깊은 세계에서 좋은 평판을 누리는 저 의지 굳센 여인이었다.\n\n현재와 장래의 인척 관계로 크롤리 가문과 얽혀 있는 이 부인과 그 고귀한 가문에 관해서는 몇 마디 적어 두어야겠다. 사우스다운 가문의 수장인 클레먼트 윌리엄, 즉 제4대 사우스다운 백작으로 말하자면, 윌버포스 씨의 후원 아래 (울지 경이라는 이름으로) 의회에 입성했고 한동안은 그 정치적 후견인에게 면목이 서는 인물이자 분명 진중한 청년이었다는 것 말고는 별로 할 이야기가 없다. 그러나 그 훌륭한 어머니가, 고귀한 남편이 세상을 떠난 지 얼마 지나지 않아, 자기 아들이 여러 속된 클럽의 회원이고 워티어와 코코아 트리에서 노름으로 크게 잃었으며, 사망 후 상환 조건으로 돈을 빌려 가문의 영지를 저당 잡혔고, 사두마차를 몰며 권투계를 후원하고, 실제로 오페라 박스 좌석까지 두어 거기서 가장 위험한 독신 패거리를 접대한다는 사실을 알게 되었을 때 그 심정은 이루 말로 다할 수가 없었다. 백작 미망인의 모임에서 그의 이름은 오로지 한탄과 함께 입에 오르내릴 뿐이었다.\n\n레이디 에밀리는 오빠보다 여러 살 위였고, 앞서 언급한 그 즐거운 소책자 몇 권과 수많은 찬송가며 신앙 시편의 저자로서 신앙심 깊은 세계에서 상당한 지위를 누렸다. 혼기가 무르익은 노처녀로 결혼에는 그저 희미한 생각밖에 없었던 그녀는, 흑인들에 대한 사랑이 그 감정의 거의 전부를 차지했다. 저 아름다운 시도 바로 그녀가 지은 것이라고 나는 믿는다.\n\n우리를 어느 양지바른 섬으로 이끄소서, 저 서쪽 바다 깊은 곳에 있는 그 섬으로. 하늘은 영원히 미소 짓고, 흑인들은 영원히 눈물 흘리는 그곳으로, 운운.\n\n그녀는 동인도와 서인도의 우리 영지 대부분에 있는 성직자 신사들과 서신을 주고받았고, 남태평양 제도에서 문신을 한 사일러스 혼블로어 목사를 남몰래 사모하고 있었다.\n\n앞서 말했듯 피트 크롤리 씨가 애정을 쏟은 레이디 제인으로 말하자면, 그녀는 온화하고, 잘 얼굴을 붉히고, 말이 없고, 수줍은 사람이었다. 오빠가 타락했음에도 그녀는 오빠를 위해 눈물 흘렸고, 그러면서도 여전히 그를 사랑하는 자신이 무척 부끄러웠다. 그녀는 지금도 짧고 황급히 쓴 쪽지를 몰래 그에게 보내며, 남몰래 우체통에 넣곤 했다. 그녀의 삶을 짓누르는 한 가지 무서운 비밀은, 그녀와 늙은 가정부가 앨버니에 있는 사우스다운의 거처로 몰래 그를 찾아간 일이었다. 그리고 그가—오, 그 못된, 사랑스럽고도 타락한 망나니!—퀴라소 한 병을 앞에 두고 시가를 피우고 있는 것을 본 것이었다. 그녀는 언니를 흠모했고, 어머니를 떠받들었으며, 크롤리 씨야말로 저 타락한 천사 사우스다운 다음으로 가장 즐겁고 교양 있는 남자라고 여겼다. 그녀의 어머니와 언니는 더없이 우월한 부류의 숙녀들이어서 그녀를 대신해 모든 일을 처리해 주었고, 정말로 우월한 여자라면 늘 그토록 넉넉히 베풀게 마련인 그 상냥한 연민의 눈길로 그녀를 바라보았다. 어머니는 그녀의 옷이며 책이며 보닛이며 생각까지 대신 정해 주었다. 그녀는 레이디 사우스다운이 적당하다고 보는 대로 조랑말 타기나 피아노 연습, 혹은 그 밖의 갖가지 신체 처방을 받게 되었다. 그리고 백작 부인은 딸을 지금의 나이인 스물여섯이 되도록 앞치마 차림으로 묶어 두었을 테지만, 레이디 제인이 샬럿 왕비를 알현하면서 그 앞치마를 벗어 던지게 된 것이었다.\n\n이 숙녀들이 처음 브라이턴 저택에 왔을 때, 크롤리 씨가 손수 방문을 한 것은 오직 그들에게뿐이었고, 고모의 집에는 명함 한 장을 두고 보울스 씨나 그 하급 하인에게 환자의 건강에 관해 조심스레 묻는 것으로 만족했다. 도서관에서 소설을 한 아름 옆구리에 끼고 집으로 돌아오던 브리그스 양과 마주쳤을 때, 크롤리 씨는 그로서는 무척이나 드물게 얼굴을 붉히며 앞으로 나서서 고모의 말동무와 악수를 나누었다. 그는 마침 함께 걷고 있던 부인, 즉 레이디 제인 십섕크스에게 브리그스 양을 소개하며 말했다. \"레이디 제인, 제 고모의 더없이 다정한 벗이자 더없이 애정 깊은 말동무인 브리그스 양을 소개해 드리겠습니다. 당신이 그토록 좋아하시는 저 즐거운 시집 『마음의 노래』의 저자로, 다른 이름으로 알고 계신 바로 그분이지요.\" 레이디 제인도 얼굴을 붉히며 브리그스 양에게 다정한 작은 손을 내밀고는, 어머니에 관해 무어라 매우 공손하지만 두서없는 말을 하며 크롤리 양을 방문하겠다는 뜻과, 크롤리 씨의 벗과 친척들을 알게 되어 기쁘다는 말을 했다. 그러고는 헤어지면서 비둘기처럼 부드러운 눈길로 브리그스 양에게 인사를 건넸고, 피트 크롤리는 그가 풍퍼니켈 공작 부인 전하의 궁정에서 수행원으로 있던 시절 그분께 올리던 것 같은, 더없이 정중하고 격식 차린 인사를 그녀에게 보냈다.\n\n저 교활한 외교가, 마키아벨리식 빈키의 제자여! 가엾은 브리그스의 초기 시집을 레이디 제인에게 선물한 것도 바로 그였다. 그는 그 시집을 퀸스크롤리에서 본 기억이 있었는데, 거기에는 그 시인이 자기 아버지의 작고한 전처에게 바친 헌사가 적혀 있었다. 그는 그 책을 브라이턴까지 가져와, 사우샘프턴발 마차 안에서 읽으며 손수 연필로 표시를 해 둔 다음, 그 온화한 레이디 제인에게 건넨 것이었다.\n\n레이디 사우스다운 앞에, 자기 가문과 크롤리 양 사이의 친교에서 생겨날 수 있는 큰 이점을—세속적인 이점과 영적인 이점 양쪽 모두를, 그라고 했다—펼쳐 보인 것도 바로 그였다. 크롤리 양은 이제 완전히 혼자였다. 동생 로던의 그 끔찍한 방탕과 혼인이 그 망나니 같은 젊은이로부터 그녀의 애정을 멀어지게 했고, 뷰트 크롤리 부인의 탐욕스러운 횡포와 인색함이 그 노부인으로 하여금 그 식구들의 지나친 요구에 반기를 들게 만들었다. 그리고 비록 그 자신은 평생토록, 어쩌면 부적절한 자존심으로 크롤리 양과의 친교를 가꾸기를 멀리해 왔으나, 이제는 그녀의 영혼을 파멸에서 구하기 위해서도, 또 크롤리 가문의 수장인 자신에게 그 재산을 확보하기 위해서도 온갖 합당한 수단을 강구해야 마땅하다고 그는 생각했다.\n\n저 의지 굳센 레이디 사우스다운은 사위 될 사람의 두 가지 제안에 전적으로 동의했고, 당장에 크롤리 양을 개종시키자는 쪽이었다. 자기 영지인 사우스다운과 트로터모어 성에서 진리의 이 키 크고 위풍당당한 선교사는 마부를 거느린 사륜마차를 타고 시골을 누비며, 오두막 사람들과 소작인들에게 소책자 꾸러미를 뿌려 댔고, 가퍼 존스를 개종시키라고 명하기를, 마치 구디 힉스에게 제임스 가루약을 먹으라고 명하듯 했으니, 항변이고 저항이고 성직자의 면죄도 허락되지 않았다. 그녀의 작고한 남편 사우스다운 경은 간질을 앓는 단순한 성품의 귀족으로, 자기 마틸다가 하고 생각하는 모든 일을 으레 옳다 하는 사람이었다. 그러니 그녀 자신의 믿음이 어떤 변화를 겪든(그것은 비국교파의 온갖 종류의 박사들에게서 따온 실로 어마어마하게 다양한 견해에 맞춰 변신했다), 그녀는 자기 소작인과 아랫사람들에게 자기를 따라 자기처럼 믿으라고 명하는 데 조금의 거리낌도 없었다. 그리하여 그녀가 스코틀랜드 신학자 손더스 맥니터 목사를 맞이하든, 온건한 웨슬리파 루크 워터스 목사를 맞이하든, 나폴레옹이 스스로 황제 관을 썼듯 스스로를 목사라 칭한 저 깨우친 구두장이 자일스 자울스 목사를 맞이하든, 레이디 사우스다운의 식솔과 자식과 소작인들은 부인과 함께 무릎을 꿇고 그 어느 박사의 기도에든 아멘을 외쳐야 했다. 이런 예배가 진행되는 동안, 늙은 사우스다운은 병약한 처지인 까닭에 자기 방에 앉아 니거스를 마시며 신문을 읽어 주는 것을 들어도 좋다는 허락을 받았다. 레이디 제인은 늙은 백작이 가장 아끼는 딸로, 그를 돌보며 진심으로 사랑했다. 한편 『핀칠리 공유지의 빨래하는 여인』의 저자 레이디 에밀리로 말하자면, 그녀가 (이 무렵에 그러했다는 것이고, 그녀의 견해는 나중에 누그러졌다) 내세의 형벌을 규탄하는 것이 어찌나 무시무시했던지, 아버지인 그 소심한 노인을 늘 겁에 질리게 했고, 의사들은 그의 발작이 언제나 부인의 설교 뒤에 일어난다고 단언할 정도였다.\n\n\"꼭 방문하지요.\" 그러자 레이디 사우스다운이 딸의 약혼자 피트 크롤리 씨의 간곡한 권유에 답하여 말했다. \"크롤리 양의 주치의는 누구인가요?\"\n\n크롤리 씨는 크리머 씨의 이름을 댔다.\n\n\"더없이 위험하고 무지한 돌팔이로구려, 친애하는 피트. 나는 섭리의 인도로 여러 집에서 그자를 쫓아내는 수단이 되어 주었지요. 한두 경우에는 제때 당도하지 못하기도 했지만 말이오. 그 가엾은 글랜더스 장군은 끝내 구하지 못했어요. 그 무지한 자의 손에서 죽어 가고 있었지—죽어 가고 있었어요. 내가 먹인 포저스 환약 덕분에 잠시 기운을 차리긴 했지만, 아! 너무 늦었던 거지요. 그렇지만 그의 임종은 더없이 평안했고, 그의 변화는 오직 더 나은 쪽으로의 것이었어요. 크리머는, 친애하는 피트, 당신 고모 곁에서 반드시 떠나야 합니다.\"\n\n피트는 전적으로 찬동하는 뜻을 표했다. 그 역시 이 고귀한 친족이자 장래의 장모 될 사람의 기세에 휩쓸려 온 터였다. 그는 손더스 맥니터, 루크 워터스, 자일스 자울스, 포저스 환약, 로저스 환약, 포키 영약(靈藥) 등, 영적이든 세속적이든 부인의 온갖 처방을 받아들이게 되었다. 그는 그 집을 나설 때마다 부인의 엉터리 신학서와 약을 한 무더기씩 공손히 가지고 나오지 않은 적이 없었다. 오, 허영의 시장에서 함께 떠도는 나의 형제 동무들이여, 그대들 가운데 이런 인자한 폭군을 알지도, 그 밑에서 고통받지도 않는 사람이 어디 있겠는가? 그들에게 \"친애하는 부인, 작년에 부인의 명대로 포저스 특효약을 먹었고 그것을 믿고 있습니다. 한데 왜, 어째서 이제 와서 그것을 부정하고 로저스의 신조를 받아들여야 한단 말입니까?\" 하고 말해 봐야 소용없는 일이다. 도리가 없다. 그 독실한 전도사는 논리로 설득하지 못하면 눈물을 터뜨리고, 그러면 거부하던 사람은 다툼의 끝에 가서 결국 그 알약을 받아 삼키며 \"알겠습니다, 알겠어요, 로저스 약으로 하지요\" 하고 마는 것이다.\n\n\"그리고 그분의 영적인 상태로 말할 것 같으면,\" 부인이 말을 이었다. \"그건 물론 당장 살펴봐야 할 일이지요. 크리머가 곁에 붙어 있으니 언제 떠나실지 모르는 노릇이고, 그것도 어떤 처지로 떠나신단 말입니까, 친애하는 피트, 얼마나 끔찍한 처지로! 당장 아이언스 목사를 그분께 보내겠어요. 제인, 바살러뮤 아이언스 목사께 삼인칭으로 짧은 편지를 써서, 오늘 저녁 여섯 시 반에 차를 함께 들 영광을 베풀어 주십사 청한다고 전하렴. 그분은 영혼을 일깨우는 분이야. 크롤리 양이 오늘 밤 잠들기 전에 꼭 그분을 뵈어야 한다. 그리고 에밀리, 얘야, 크롤리 양에게 보낼 책 꾸러미를 준비하렴. 『불길 속에서 들려오는 목소리』, 『예리코를 향한 나팔 경고』, 그리고 『깨진 고기 가마솥, 혹은 개종한 식인종』을 챙겨 넣어라.\"\n\n\"그리고 『핀칠리 공유지의 빨래하는 여인』도요, 어머니.\" 레이디 에밀리가 말했다. \"처음에는 부드럽게 시작하는 편이 나으니까요.\"\n\n\"잠깐만요, 친애하는 숙녀분들.\" 외교가 피트가 말했다. \"제가 사랑하고 존경하는 레이디 사우스다운의 의견을 전적으로 존중하옵니다만, 크롤리 양에게 그토록 일찍부터 진중한 주제로 시작하는 것은 결코 권할 만한 일이 못 된다고 생각합니다. 그분의 섬약한 상태와, 또 그분이 지금까지 자신의 불멸의 안녕에 관한 일을 얼마나 적게, 정말이지 얼마나 적게 생각하며 지내 오셨는지를 헤아려 주십시오.\"\n\n\"그러면 우리가 너무 일찍 시작할 수도 있다는 건가요, 피트?\" 레이디 에밀리가 벌써 작은 책 여섯 권을 손에 든 채 일어서며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게 다짜고짜 시작하면 그분을 통째로 겁먹게 할 뿐입니다. 저는 제 고모의 세속적인 천성을 너무도 잘 알기에, 어떤 식으로든 다짜고짜 개종을 시도하는 것이야말로 그 불운한 부인의 안녕을 위해 쓸 수 있는 가장 나쁜 수단이 되리라고 확신합니다. 그분을 겁먹고 성가시게 할 뿐이지요. 십중팔구 책을 내던지고는 그것을 준 사람들과는 아예 상종도 하지 않으려 할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"당신도 크롤리 양만큼이나 세속적이군요, 피트.\" 레이디 에밀리가 책을 손에 든 채 방 밖으로 휙 나가며 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 굳이 말씀드릴 것까지도 없겠습니다만, 친애하는 레이디 사우스다운,\" 피트가 그 방해에 아랑곳하지 않고 낮은 목소리로 말을 이었다. \"온화함과 신중함이 조금만 모자라도, 제 고모의 세속적 재산에 관해 우리가 품을 만한 어떤 기대에든 얼마나 치명적일 수 있는지 말입니다. 그분에게 칠만 파운드가 있다는 것을 기억해 주십시오. 그분의 연세와, 더없이 신경이 예민하고 섬약한 상태를 헤아려 주십시오. 저는 그분이 제 동생(크롤리 대령)에게 유리하게 작성했던 유언장을 이미 없애 버렸다는 것을 알고 있습니다. 그 상처받은 마음을 어루만져 줌으로써 올바른 길로 이끌어야지, 겁을 주어서는 안 됩니다. 그러니 부인께서도 제 말에 동의하시리라 생각합니다, 그러니까—그러니까—\"\n\n\"물론이지요, 물론이고말고요.\" 레이디 사우스다운이 말했다. \"제인, 얘야, 그 편지는 아이언스 씨께 보내지 않아도 되겠다. 그분 건강이 토론으로 피로해질 정도라면, 차도를 기다리도록 하자. 내일 크롤리 양을 방문하마.\"\n\n\"그리고 한 가지 여쭙자면, 친애하는 부인,\" 피트가 부드러운 어조로 말했다. \"너무 열성적인 우리 소중한 에밀리는 데려가지 않는 편이 좋겠습니다. 오히려 우리의 상냥하고 다정한 레이디 제인을 동행하게 하시는 것이 어떨까 합니다.\"\n\n\"틀림없이 에밀리는 모든 걸 망쳐 놓을 거예요.\" 레이디 사우스다운이 말했다. 그리하여 그녀는 이번만큼은 평소의 관행을 포기하기로 했으니, 그 관행이란, 앞서 말했듯 굴복시키려고 점찍은 사람에게 몸소 들이닥치기에 앞서 그 위협받는 대상에게 소책자를 한 무더기 퍼붓는 것이었다(마치 프랑스군의 돌격에 앞서 늘 맹렬한 포격이 선행되었듯이 말이다). 다시 말하건대, 레이디 사우스다운은 환자의 건강을 위해서든, 그 영혼의 궁극적 안녕을 위해서든, 아니면 그 돈을 위해서든, 잠시 형편을 보아 가며 미루기로 한 것이었다.\n\n이튿날, 백작의 관(冠)과 마름모꼴 문장(거기에는 사우스다운가의 초록 바탕에 은빛으로 종종걸음 치는 양 세 마리가, 빈키 가문의 표지인 금빛 띠 위 붉은 코담뱃갑 세 개와 검은빛으로 사등분되어 있었다)을 새긴 사우스다운가의 거대한 여성용 가족 마차가 위풍당당하게 크롤리 양의 집 문 앞에 다다랐고, 키 크고 진중한 하인이 보울스 씨에게 크롤리 양 앞으로 된 부인의 명함과 브리그스 양 앞으로 된 명함을 한 장씩 건넸다. 타협의 표시로 레이디 에밀리는 그날 저녁 브리그스 양에게 꾸러미를 하나 보냈는데, 거기에는 브리그스 양 본인이 읽을 『빨래하는 여인』 몇 부와 그 밖의 온건하고 애독되는 소책자들이 들어 있었고, 하인들 방에 둘 것으로 훨씬 강도 높은 책 몇 권, 즉 『식료품 저장실에서 나온 부스러기』, 『프라이팬과 불』, 『죄의 제복』 따위도 들어 있었다.\n\n크롤리 씨의 상냥한 처신과 제인 부인의 다정한 환대는 브리그스 양을 무척이나 우쭐하게 만들었으니, 사우스다운 가문의 명함이 크롤리 양에게 전해진 뒤로 그녀는 제인 부인을 위해 좋은 말 한마디쯤 거들 수 있게 된 것이었다. 게다가 백작 부인의 명함이 가엾고 의지가지없는 말동무인 브리그스 자신 앞으로도 직접 남겨졌다는 사실은 적잖이 기쁜 일이었다. \"사우스다운 부인이 대체 무슨 생각으로 자네에게까지 명함을 남기고 갔는지 모르겠군, 브리그스 양?\" 공화주의자인 크롤리 양이 말했다. 그러자 말동무는 다소곳이 대꾸하기를 \"지체 높은 부인께서 가난한 양가 출신 여인을 눈여겨봐 주신다고 해서 나쁠 것은 없지 않겠어요\"라고 했고, 그 명함을 자신의 반짇고리 안, 가장 소중히 간직하는 개인 보물들 사이에 고이 넣어 두었다. 더 나아가 브리그스 양은 전날 크롤리 씨가 사촌이자 오랜 약혼녀와 함께 산책하는 모습을 어떻게 마주쳤는지 설명했다. 그러면서 그 아가씨가 얼마나 상냥하고 온화해 보이던지, 또 차림새가 얼마나 수수하다 못해 평범하던지 이야기했고, 보닛에서 부츠에 이르기까지 그 모든 물건을 여성 특유의 정확함으로 묘사하고 값을 매겨 보였다.\n\n크롤리 양은 브리그스가 너무 많이 끊기지 않게 두면서 마음껏 재잘대게 내버려 두었다. 몸이 차차 나아지자 그녀는 사람들과 어울리고 싶어 안달이 났다. 주치의인 크리머 씨는 그녀가 런던의 옛 소굴과 방탕한 생활로 돌아가는 것을 들으려 하지 않았다. 늙은 노처녀는 브라이턴에서나마 말벗을 찾을 수 있어 더없이 기뻤고, 그리하여 바로 다음 날 명함에 답례를 했을 뿐 아니라, 피트 크롤리는 고모를 뵈러 오라는 황송한 초대까지 받았다. 그는 사우스다운 부인과 그 딸을 데리고 찾아왔다. 노부인은 크롤리 양의 영혼의 상태에 대해서는 한마디도 꺼내지 않았다. 다만 날씨에 대해, 전쟁과 저 괴물 보나파르트의 몰락에 대해, 그리고 무엇보다 의사들이며 돌팔이들이며 당시 자신이 후원하던 포저스 박사의 각별한 미덕에 대해 무척 신중하게 이야기를 늘어놓았다.\n\n이 회견 도중 피트 크롤리는 큰 한 수를 두었으니, 만약 그의 외교관 경력이 일찌감치 도외시되어 망가지지만 않았던들 그가 그 분야에서 높은 지위까지 올랐으리라는 것을 보여 주는 한 수였다. 당시 유행에 따라 사우스다운 백작 미망인이 저 코르시카 출신의 벼락출세한 자를 맹렬히 비난하며, 그가 상상할 수 있는 온갖 죄로 더럽혀진 괴물이요, 살아 있을 자격도 없는 겁쟁이이자 폭군이요, 그 몰락이 이미 예언된 자라는 둥 떠들어 대자, 피트 크롤리는 별안간 저 운명의 사나이를 옹호하며 무기를 들고 나선 것이었다. 그는 아미앵 평화조약 때 파리에서 본 제1통령의 모습을 묘사했다. 바로 그때 그 자신, 피트 크롤리는 위대하고 선량한 폭스 씨와 친분을 맺는 만족을 누렸던 것이다. 폭스 씨로 말하면, 그와 견해가 아무리 다르다 한들 뜨겁게 흠모하지 않을 수 없는 정치가요, 나폴레옹 황제를 늘 더없이 높이 평가해 온 정치가였다. 그리고 그는 폐위된 그 군주를 향한 연합국들의 신의 없는 처사에 대해 가장 강한 분노의 말을 쏟아 냈으니, 너그럽게 제 몸을 그들의 자비에 맡긴 그를 비천하고 잔혹한 유배지로 보내 버린 한편, 그를 대신해 편협한 가톨릭 무리가 프랑스를 폭정으로 다스리고 있다는 것이었다.\n\n가톨릭 미신에 대한 이 정통 신앙적 혐오 덕분에 피트 크롤리는 사우스다운 부인의 눈에 들었고, 폭스와 나폴레옹에 대한 그의 흠모는 크롤리 양의 눈에 그를 한량없이 높여 놓았다. 이 이야기 첫머리에 그녀를 처음 소개할 때 언급했듯, 크롤리 양은 이미 고인이 된 그 영국 정치가와 친분이 있었다. 진정한 휘그당원인 크롤리 양은 전쟁 내내 야당 편이었고, 물론 황제의 몰락이 노부인을 그리 동요시키지 않았으며 그가 받은 학대가 그녀의 수명이나 타고난 안식을 줄일 만한 것도 아니었지만, 피트가 그녀의 두 우상을 모두 칭송하자 그것은 그녀의 마음을 정통으로 울렸다. 그리고 그 한 차례 연설만으로 그는 그녀의 호감을 사는 데 엄청난 진척을 이루었다.\n\n\"넌 어떻게 생각하니, 얘야?\" 크롤리 양이 그 젊은 아가씨에게 물었다. 그녀는 늘 그러하듯 예쁘고 얌전한 젊은 사람에게는 첫눈에 호감을 품었기에 이 아가씨도 마음에 들어 했던 것이다. 다만 그 애정이 솟구치는 속도만큼이나 빨리 식어 버린다는 점은 인정하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n제인 부인은 얼굴이 새빨개져서는 이렇게 말했다. \"저는 정치는 잘 몰라서, 그건 저보다 현명한 분들께 맡겨 두는 편이에요. 하지만 어머니 말씀이 옳으신 것은 분명하지만, 크롤리 씨가 참 아름답게 말씀하셨어요.\" 그리고 방문을 마치고 숙녀들이 물러가려 할 때, 크롤리 양은 \"사우스다운 부인께서 부디 친절을 베푸셔서, 가엾고 병들고 외로운 늙은이를 위로해 줄 수 있도록 이따금 제인 부인을 보내 주셨으면\" 한다고 청했다. 이 약속은 흔쾌히 받아들여졌고, 그들은 더없이 정다운 사이가 되어 헤어졌다.\n\n\"사우스다운 부인은 다시 오지 못하게 하렴, 피트.\" 노부인이 말했다. \"그 사람은 네 어머니 집안 사람들이 다 그렇듯 멍청하고 거드름만 피우는데, 난 그 집안 사람들을 도무지 견딜 수가 없었지. 하지만 그 착하고 마음씨 고운 어린 제인은 네가 원하는 만큼 얼마든지 자주 데려오너라.\" 피트는 그렇게 하겠노라 약속했다. 그는 고모가 사우스다운 백작 부인에 대해 어떤 평가를 내렸는지 그 부인에게 일러바치지 않았으니, 그 부인은 도리어 자신이 크롤리 양에게 더없이 유쾌하고 위엄 있는 인상을 남겼다고 여기고 있었다.\n\n그리하여 병든 부인을 위로하는 일이 싫지 않았던 데다, 바살러뮤 아이언스 목사의 따분한 설교와, 거드름 피우는 백작 부인인 제 어머니의 발치에 모여드는 진지한 아첨꾼들에게서 이따금 풀려나는 것이 마음속으로 그리 서운하지도 않았던 제인 부인은 크롤리 양의 꽤 단골 방문객이 되어, 마차 산책을 함께하고 적적한 저녁나절을 여러 차례 달래 주었다. 그녀는 천성이 어찌나 선하고 부드러운지 퍼킨조차 그녀를 질투하지 않았고, 온순한 브리그스는 다정한 제인 부인이 곁에 있을 때면 친구가 자신을 덜 모질게 대한다고 생각했다. 제인 부인을 향한 크롤리 양의 태도는 더없이 매력적이었다. 늙은 노처녀는 자신의 젊은 시절에 관한 일화를 수천 가지나 들려주었는데, 그 어조는 신을 모르는 어린 레베카와 이야기를 나눌 때와는 사뭇 달랐다. 제인 부인의 순진무구함에는 그녀 앞에서 가벼운 말을 무례하게 만드는 무언가가 있었던 데다, 크롤리 양 또한 그런 순결함을 더럽히기에는 너무나 양가의 여인이었던 까닭이다. 이 젊은 아가씨는 이 늙은 노처녀와 자신의 오라비와 아버지를 제외하고는 그 누구에게서도 다정한 대접을 받아 본 적이 없었기에, 크롤리 양의 열성적인 호의를 꾸밈없는 다정함과 우정으로 갚았다.\n\n가을 저녁이면(레베카가 파리에서 가장 들떠 있는 정복자들 가운데서도 가장 화려하게 으스대고 있을 때, 또 우리의 애밀리아, 우리의 가엾고 상처 입은 애밀리아, 아아! 그녀는 어디 있었던가?) 제인 부인은 크롤리 양의 응접실에 앉아 황혼 녘에 자신의 소박한 노래와 찬송가를 그녀에게 다정스레 불러 주곤 했으니, 그동안 해는 저물어 가고 바다는 모래밭에 부딪쳐 울부짖었다. 늙은 노처녀는 이 가락들이 멎으면 잠에서 깨어나 더 불러 달라고 청하는 것이었다. 브리그스로 말할 것 같으면, 뜨개질을 하는 척하면서 창밖으로 어둑해지는 찬란한 바다와 더 환히 빛나기 시작하는 하늘의 등불들을 내다보며 그녀가 흘리던 그 숱한 행복의 눈물 — 대체 누가, 내 말하건대, 브리그스의 행복과 감수성을 가늠할 수 있단 말인가?\n\n그러는 동안 피트는 식당에서 옥수수법에 관한 소책자나 선교 잡지를 곁에 두고, 저녁 식사 후 낭만적인 남자에게도 그렇지 못한 남자에게도 어울리는 그런 종류의 휴식을 취했다. 그는 마데이라 포도주를 홀짝이고, 공중누각을 짓고, 자신을 멋진 사내라 여기고, 지난 칠 년 동안 — 그동안 두 사람의 연애는 피트 쪽에서 추호의 조바심도 없이 이어져 왔는데 — 어느 때보다도 제인을 사랑한다고 느끼고, 그러고는 한참을 잤다. 커피 시간이 되면 보울스 씨가 요란하게 들어와 피트 도련님을 불렀고, 그러면 도련님은 어둠 속에서 소책자에 골몰해 있는 모습으로 발견되곤 했다.\n\n\"얘야, 누가 나하고 피케 한판 쳐 줄 사람이 있었으면 좋겠구나.\" 어느 날 밤, 이 일꾼이 촛불과 커피를 들고 나타났을 때 크롤리 양이 말했다. \"가엾은 브리그스는 어찌나 멍청한지 올빼미만큼도 카드를 못 친단다\"(노처녀는 늘 하인들 앞에서 브리그스를 욕할 기회를 놓치지 않았다). \"한판 치고 나면 잠이 더 잘 올 것 같은데 말이야.\"\n\n이 말에 제인 부인은 그 작은 귓불 끝까지, 또 그 예쁜 손가락 끝까지 새빨개졌다. 그리고 보울스 씨가 방을 나가고 문이 완전히 닫히자, 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"크롤리 양, 저 조금 칠 줄 알아요. 예전에 — 가엾고 그리운 아버지하고 조금 치곤 했어요.\"\n\n\"이리 와서 입 맞춰 다오. 당장 이리 와서 입 맞춰 다오, 이 사랑스럽고 착한 것아.\" 크롤리 양이 황홀해하며 외쳤다. 그리고 피트가 소책자를 손에 든 채 위층으로 올라왔을 때, 그는 늙은 부인과 젊은 부인이 이 그림 같고 다정한 일에 빠져 있는 것을 발견했다. 그 가엾은 제인 부인이 저녁 내내 얼마나 얼굴을 붉히던지!\n\n피트 크롤리 씨의 술책이 퀸스크롤리의 목사관에 사는 그의 정다운 친척들의 주의를 비켜 갔으리라 여겨서는 안 된다. 햄프셔와 서식스는 아주 가까이 붙어 있는 데다, 뷰트 부인은 서식스 주에 친구들이 있어서, 브라이턴의 크롤리 양 집에서 벌어지는 일이라면 무엇이든, 아니 그 전부에다 훨씬 더 많은 것까지 그녀에게 빠짐없이 알려 주었던 것이다. 피트는 점점 더 그 집에 드나들었다. 그가 몇 달이고 본가에는 한 번도 들르지 않았으니, 그곳에서는 그의 가증스러운 늙은 아버지가 럼주에 물 탄 술과 호록스 가족의 역겨운 무리에 완전히 빠져 있었다. 피트의 성공은 목사 가족을 격분케 했고, 뷰트 부인은 브리그스 양을 그토록 모욕하고 보울스와 퍼킨에게 그토록 거만하고 인색하게 굴어, 크롤리 양의 집안에서 그곳 일을 알려 줄 사람이 단 한 명도 남지 않게 만든 자신의 끔찍한 실수를 그 어느 때보다도 더 후회했다(물론 입 밖으로 내는 것은 덜했지만). \"다 뷰트의 쇄골 탓이었어요.\" 그녀는 끈질기게 주장했다. \"그게 부러지지만 않았어도 절대 그분 곁을 떠나지 않았을 거예요. 나는 의무와, 당신의 그 역겹고 목사답지 못한 사냥 버릇 때문에 순교한 거라고요, 뷰트.\"\n\n\"사냥이라니, 말도 안 되는 소리! 그 사람을 겁먹게 한 건 당신이었어, 바버라.\" 목사가 끼어들었다. \"당신은 똑똑한 여자지, 그렇지만 성질머리가 지독해. 게다가 돈에는 어찌나 인색한지, 바버라.\"\n\n\"내가 당신 돈을 챙겨 두지 않았으면 당신은 빚더미에 눌려 감옥에 갇혔을걸요, 뷰트.\"\n\n\"그랬을 테지, 나도 알아, 여보.\" 목사가 사람 좋게 말했다. \"당신은 똑똑한 여자야, 그렇지만 일을 너무 야무지게 처리한단 말이야, 알지?\" 그러고는 이 경건한 사내는 큼직한 포도주 한 잔으로 스스로를 위로했다.\n\n\"대체 그 사람이 그 얼간이 피트 크롤리한테서 뭘 본단 말이야?\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"그 녀석은 거위한테 야단칠 배짱도 없는 위인이라고. 사내대장부인 로던이, 빌어먹을 녀석이지만, 그놈을 팽이 돌리듯 마구간 둘레로 채찍질하며 쫓아다니던 게 기억나는데. 그러면 피트는 엄마한테로 엉엉 울며 달려가곤 했지 — 하, 하! 아니, 우리 집 애들 중 누구라도 한 손으로 그놈을 두들겨 팰 수 있을걸. 짐 말로는 옥스퍼드에서 아직도 그놈을 '크롤리 양'이라고 부른다더군 — 그 얼간이를.\"\n\n\"여보, 바버라.\" 잠시 후 목사가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"뭐예요?\" 손톱을 물어뜯으며 식탁을 톡톡 두드리던 바버라가 말했다.\n\n\"내 말은, 짐을 브라이턴에 보내서 그 노부인한테 뭐라도 해 볼 수 있는지 알아보면 어떻겠냐는 거야. 알다시피 그 녀석 학위도 거의 다 따 가잖아. 두 번밖에 낙제 안 했는데 — 나도 그랬지 — 어쨌든 그 녀석은 옥스퍼드와 대학 교육의 혜택을 받았어. 거기 제일 잘나가는 친구들도 몇 알고 지내고. 보니페이스 보트팀에서 스트로크도 맡고 있어. 잘생긴 녀석이지. 제기랄, 부인, 그 녀석을 그 노파한테 들이밀어 봅시다, 응? 그리고 피트가 뭐라도 지껄이거든 흠씬 패 주라고 일러 두는 거지. 하, 하, 하!\"\n\n\"짐이 내려가서 그분을 뵐 수야 있겠지요.\" 안주인이 말하고는, 한숨을 쉬며 덧붙였다. \"딸애들 중 하나라도 그 집에 들여놓을 수 있다면 좋으련만, 그분은 예쁘지 않다고 도무지 우리 애들을 못 견뎌 하니.\" 그 불운하고 잘 교육받은 처녀들은 어머니가 말하는 동안 옆 응접실에서 자기들 존재를 알리고 있었으니, 거기서 그들은 굳은 손가락으로 피아노포르테로 복잡한 곡 하나를 둥당거리고 있던 참이었다. 실은 그 처녀들은 하루 종일 음악이 아니면 자세 교정판이나 지리나 역사에 매달려 살았다. 그러나 허영의 시장에서 키 작고 가난하고 못생긴 데다 안색마저 나쁜 처녀들에게, 이 모든 교양이 다 무슨 소용이란 말인가? 뷰트 부인은 그 딸들 중 하나를 떠맡아 줄 사람으로 보좌 목사 말고는 아무도 떠올릴 수 없었다. 바로 이때 짐이 기름 먹인 모자에 짧은 파이프를 꽂은 채 응접실 창문을 통해 마구간에서 들어왔고, 그와 그의 아버지는 세인트레저 경마의 배당률 이야기로 빠져들었으니, 그리하여 목사와 그 아내 사이의 대화는 끝이 났다.\n\n뷰트 부인은 아들 제임스를 사절로 보낸다고 해서 이 일에 큰 성과가 있으리라고는 기대하지 않았기에, 자못 절망적인 심정으로 그가 떠나는 것을 지켜보았다. 또한 자신의 임무가 무엇인지 들었을 때, 젊은이 자신도 거기서 큰 즐거움이나 이득을 바라지 않았다. 다만 그는 어쩌면 그 노부인이 자신을 기념할 만한 멋진 물건을 줄지도 모르고, 그러면 다가오는 옥스퍼드 학기 초에 가장 급한 청구서 몇 장은 갚을 수 있으리라는 생각으로 위안을 삼고는, 사우샘프턴발 마차에 자리를 잡고 같은 날 저녁 브라이턴에 무사히 내렸다. 그와 함께 여행 가방과, 그가 아끼는 불도그 타우저와, 정다운 목사관 식구들이 정다운 크롤리 양에게 보내는 농장과 정원의 산물을 가득 담은 어마어마한 바구니도 도착했다. 도착한 첫날 밤에 병든 부인을 깨우기에는 너무 늦었다고 여겨, 그는 여관에 묵었고, 이튿날 한낮이 다 되어서야 크롤리 양을 찾았다.\n\n제임스 크롤리는 고모가 마지막으로 보았을 때만 해도 어색한 풋내기였으니, 목소리가 기괴한 고음과 비정상적인 저음 사이를 오가는 그 거북한 나이였고, 흔히 얼굴에는 롤런드의 칼리도르가 치료제 노릇을 한다는 그런 것들이 만발하는 나이였고, 사내아이들이 누이의 가위로 몰래 면도하는 모습이 눈에 띄고, 다른 젊은 여자들을 보기만 해도 견딜 수 없는 공포의 감정에 휩싸이는 나이였고, 큼직한 손과 발목이 너무 작아진 옷자락에서 한참이나 비어져 나오는 나이였으며, 저녁 식사 후의 그 존재가 황혼 녘 응접실에서 소곤대는 숙녀들에게는 무섭게 느껴지는 동시에, 마호가니 식탁에 둘러앉은 신사들에게는 형언할 수 없이 거슬리는 — 그 어색한 순진함의 존재 탓에 자유로운 교류와 유쾌한 재담의 주고받음이 가로막히는 — 그런 나이였고, 두 번째 잔을 비울 무렵이면 아버지가 \"잭, 얘야, 나가서 저녁 날씨가 갤 것 같은지 좀 보고 오너라\" 하고, 그러면 자유로워지고 싶으면서도 아직 어른 대접을 못 받는 것이 서운한 그 청년이 미완성의 연회 자리를 떠나는, 그런 나이였다. 그때만 해도 풋내기였던 제임스가 이제는 어엿한 청년이 되어 있었으니, 대학 교육의 혜택을 누렸고, 작은 칼리지에서 방탕한 무리와 어울리며 빚을 지고, 정학을 당하고, 낙제하는 과정에서 얻는 그 값을 매길 수 없는 세련됨까지 익힌 터였다.\n\n그래도 브라이턴에서 고모 앞에 인사를 드리러 나섰을 때 그는 잘생긴 청년이었고, 잘생긴 외모란 늘 저 변덕스러운 노부인의 호감을 사는 자격이 되어 주었다. 그의 얼굴 붉힘과 어색함도 그 호감을 깎아내리지 않았으니, 그녀는 이 젊은 신사의 천진함을 드러내는 그 건강한 표시들이 마음에 들었던 것이다.\n\n그는 이렇게 말했다. \"제 칼리지 친구 하나를 만나러 이틀쯤 내려왔는데요, 그리고 — 그리고 고모님께 문안 인사도 드리려고요. 아버지하고 어머니가 고모님 안녕하시길 바라신다고 하셨어요.\"\n\n청년의 도착이 알려졌을 때 피트는 크롤리 양과 함께 방에 있었고, 그 이름이 거론되자 매우 떨떠름한 표정을 지었다. 노부인은 유머가 넘치는 사람이라 그 반듯한 조카의 당혹스러움을 즐겼다. 그녀는 목사관 식구들 모두의 안부를 큰 관심을 보이며 물었고, 그들을 한번 찾아갈까 생각 중이라고 했다. 그녀는 청년을 면전에서 칭찬하며, 잘 자랐고 아주 많이 나아졌다고, 또 그의 누이들이 그 잘생긴 외모를 조금이라도 닮지 못한 것이 안타깝다고 했다. 그리고 물어보아 그가 호텔에 묵고 있다는 것을 알고는, 그곳에 머무는 것을 들으려고도 하지 않고, 보울스 씨에게 당장 제임스 크롤리 씨의 짐을 가져오라고 명했다. \"그리고 잘 듣게, 보울스.\" 그녀가 자못 너그럽게 덧붙였다. \"수고스럽겠지만 제임스 씨의 숙박비도 좀 치러 주게나.\"\n\n그녀는 피트에게 짓궂은 승리의 눈길을 던졌고, 그 바람에 이 외교가는 시샘으로 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이었다. 그토록 고모의 환심을 사 왔건만 그녀는 그를 단 한 번도 자기 집에 머물도록 청한 적이 없었는데, 여기 한 애송이가 첫눈에 그곳에서 환대를 받고 있는 것이었다.\n\n\"송구합니다만, 나리.\" 보울스가 정중히 절하며 다가와 말했다. \"토머스를 시켜 어느 호텔에서 짐을 가져오게 할까요, 나리?\"\n\n\"아, 이런.\" 젊은 제임스가 무언가 놀란 듯 벌떡 일어서며 말했다. \"제가 가겠습니다.\"\n\n\"무어라고?\" 크롤리 양이 말했다.\n\n\"톰 크립스 암스요.\" 제임스가 얼굴이 시뻘게져서 말했다.\n\n크롤리 양은 그 이름에 폭소를 터뜨렸다. 보울스 씨도 이 집안의 믿음직한 하인으로서 갑작스레 껄껄 한바탕 웃음을 터뜨렸으나, 나머지 한바탕은 꿀꺽 삼켰다. 외교가는 그저 빙긋 웃을 뿐이었다.\n\n\"저 — 저는 더 좋은 데를 몰라서요.\" 제임스가 고개를 숙이며 말했다. \"여기 와 본 적이 없어서, 마부가 일러 준 데로 갔어요.\" 이 어린 거짓말쟁이 같으니! 사실인즉, 전날 사우샘프턴 마차에서 제임스 크롤리는 로팅딘 피버와 시합을 하러 브라이턴으로 오던 터트베리 펫을 만났고, 그 펫의 입담에 홀딱 빠진 나머지 바로 그 여관에서 그 권투 전문가와 그 친구들과 어울려 저녁나절을 보냈던 것이다.\n\n\"제 — 제가 가서 셈을 치르는 게 제일 좋겠어요.\" 제임스가 말을 이었다. \"고모님께 부탁드릴 생각은 없었어요.\" 그가 후하게 덧붙였다.\n\n이 갸륵함에 고모는 더욱 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"가서 셈을 치르게, 보울스.\" 그녀가 한 손을 휘저으며 말했다. \"그리고 청구서는 내게 가져오고.\"\n\n가엾은 부인, 그녀는 자신이 무슨 일을 저질렀는지 알지 못했다! \"저 — 저기 작은 개도 한 마리 있는데요.\" 제임스가 끔찍하게 켕기는 표정으로 말했다. \"제가 가서 데려오는 게 좋겠어요. 그놈이 하인들 장딴지를 물거든요.\"\n\n이 묘사에 자리에 있던 사람들이 모두 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 크롤리 양과 조카가 회견하는 동안 말없이 앉아 있던 브리그스와 제인 부인까지도 그랬다. 그리고 보울스는 한마디 없이 방을 나갔다.\n\n그래도 손위 조카를 벌주려는 셈으로, 크롤리 양은 그 젊은 옥스퍼드 학생에게 한사코 자상하게 굴었다. 그녀의 친절과 칭찬은 일단 시작되면 한도 끝도 없었다. 그녀는 피트에게 저녁 식사에 와도 좋다고 했고, 제임스가 자기 마차 산책에 동행해야 한다고 우기며, 사륜마차 뒷좌석에 그를 태우고 절벽 위를 점잖게 오르내리며 데리고 다녔다. 이 외출 내내 그녀는 황송하게도 그에게 정겨운 말을 건넸다. 그녀는 이탈리아어와 프랑스어 시를 인용해 이 가엾고 어리둥절한 청년에게 들려주었고, 그가 훌륭한 학자라고 우기며, 틀림없이 금메달을 따고 수석 우등생이 될 것이라고 단언했다.\n\n\"하, 하.\" 제임스가 이 칭찬에 우쭐해져 웃었다. \"수석 우등생이라니, 원, 그건 다른 가게 이야기예요.\"\n\n\"다른 가게라니 그게 무슨 소리니, 얘야?\" 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"수석 우등생은 옥스퍼드가 아니라 케임브리지에 있는 거예요.\" 학자가 아는 체하는 태도로 말했다. 그러고는 아마 더 허물없이 이야기를 늘어놓았을 테지만, 별안간 절벽 위에 멋들어진 조랑말이 끄는 짐마차 한 대가 나타났으니, 자개 단추가 달린 흰 플란넬 외투를 입은 그의 친구들, 터트베리 펫과 로팅딘 피버가, 안면 있는 다른 신사 세 명과 함께 타고 있었고, 그들 모두가 마차에 앉아 있는 가엾은 제임스에게 인사를 건넸다. 이 일로 천진한 청년은 기가 꺾여, 남은 산책 내내 예니 아니오니 한마디도 입에 올리게 만들 수가 없었다.\n\n돌아와 보니 그의 방은 준비되어 있었고 여행 가방도 풀려 있었다. 보울스 씨가 그를 방으로 안내할 때 그 얼굴에 자못 엄숙하고 의아하고 측은해하는 빛이 어려 있는 것을 알아챘을 법도 하건만, 보울스 씨에 대한 생각 따위는 그의 머리에 들어오지 않았다. 그는 자신이 처한 끔찍한 처지를 한탄하느라 정신이 없었으니, 프랑스어와 이탈리아어를 지껄여 대고 자신에게 시를 읊어 대는 늙은 여자들로 가득한 집에 갇혀 버린 것이었다. \"이거 완전히 나무 위에 갇힌 신세로군, 제기랄!\" 이 수줍은 청년이 외쳤다. 그는 여자라면 누구든, 가장 온순한 브리그스조차도, 자신에게 말을 걸기 시작하면 마주 볼 수가 없었다. 그러면서도 이프리 갑문에 그를 갖다 놓으면 가장 입 거친 뱃사공도 욕설로 눌러 버릴 수 있는 위인이었다.\n\n저녁 식사 자리에 제임스는 흰 넥타이에 목이 졸리는 듯한 모습으로 나타났고, 제인 부인을 아래층으로 모시고 내려가는 영광을 누렸으며, 그동안 브리그스와 크롤리 씨는 보따리며 숄이며 방석이며 온갖 장비를 갖춘 노부인을 부축하며 뒤따라 내려왔다. 식사 동안 브리그스는 절반의 시간을 병든 부인의 편의를 살피고 그녀의 살진 스패니얼에게 닭고기를 잘라 주는 데 썼다. 제임스는 말은 별로 하지 않았으나, 모든 숙녀에게 술을 권하기를 잊지 않았고, 크롤리 씨의 잔 권유에 응했으며, 보울스 씨가 그를 위해 내오라는 명을 받은 샴페인 한 병의 대부분을 비웠다. 숙녀들이 물러가고 두 사촌만 남게 되자, 전직 외교관 피트는 매우 살갑고 다정해졌다. 그는 제임스의 대학 생활은 어떤지, 앞날의 전망은 어떤지 물었고, 잘되기를 진심으로 바란다고 했으니, 한마디로 솔직하고 상냥했다. 포도주가 들어가자 제임스의 혀가 풀려, 그는 사촌에게 자신의 삶이며 전망이며 빚이며 예비시험에서 겪은 고초며 학감들과의 다툼까지 털어놓았으니, 앞에 놓인 병들에서 부지런히 잔을 채우며 즐거이 포트와인에서 마데이라로 옮겨 다녔다.\n\n\"우리 고모님이 가장 즐기시는 일은 말이야,\" 크롤리 씨가 자기 잔을 채우며 말했다. \"사람들이 당신 집에서 제 좋을 대로 하는 거란다. 여긴 자유의 전당이야, 제임스, 그러니 자네가 하고 싶은 대로 하고 원하는 걸 청하는 것보다 크롤리 양을 더 기쁘게 해 드릴 길은 없어. 자네들이 시골에서 내가 토리당이라고 다들 비웃었던 걸 안다. 크롤리 양은 어떤 취향에도 다 맞출 만큼 진보적인 분이지. 원칙으로는 공화주의자라서, 신분이니 작위니 하는 것은 죄다 경멸하신다고.\"\n\n\"그럼 형님은 왜 백작 따님하고 결혼하려는 거예요?\" 제임스가 말했다.\n\n\"이보게, 가엾은 제인 부인이 좋은 가문에서 태어난 게 그 사람 잘못이 아니라는 걸 잊지 말게.\" 피트가 점잖은 태도로 대꾸했다. \"그 사람이 숙녀로 태어난 걸 어쩌겠나. 게다가 나는 토리당이잖나, 알다시피.\"\n\n\"오, 그거라면,\" 짐이 말했다. \"오래된 혈통만 한 게 없죠. 암요, 빌어먹을, 그만한 게 없고말고요. 저는 형님 같은 급진주의자가 아니에요. 신사가 뭔지 저도 안다고요, 빌어먹을. 보트 경주에서 사내들을 보세요. 격투하는 녀석들을 보시고요. 그래요, 쥐 잡는 개를 보시라고요 — 어느 놈이 이기나요? 혈통 좋은 놈들이라고요. 보울스, 영감, 포트 좀 더 가져와요, 내가 여기 이 병을 비울 동안에. 내가 무슨 말 하고 있었지?\"\n\n\"개가 쥐 잡는 이야기를 하던 것 같은데.\" 피트가 점잖게 말하며, 사촌에게 \"비우라고\" 술병을 건넸다.\n\n\"쥐 잡는 거요? 그래, 피트 형님, 형님은 운동 좋아해요? 쥐를 정말 잘 잡는 개를 보고 싶어요? 그렇다면 저랑 캐슬 가 마구간 골목에 있는 톰 코듀로이네로 같이 가요, 제가 그런 불테리어를 보여 드리죠 — 풉! 시시한 소리.\" 제임스가 자기 헛소리에 웃음을 터뜨리며 외쳤다. \"형님은 개니 쥐니 관심도 없잖아요. 다 헛소리지. 장담컨대 형님은 개하고 오리도 구별 못 할걸요.\"\n\n\"아니지, 그건 그렇고,\" 피트가 한층 더 부드러워진 태도로 말을 이었다. \"자네가 이야기하던 건 혈통이었지, 그리고 귀족으로 태어나서 사람들이 누리는 개인적 이점 말이야. 자, 새 병 여기 있네.\"\n\n\"혈통이 핵심이죠.\" 제임스가 그 루비빛 액체를 꿀꺽 들이켜며 말했다. \"혈통만 한 게 없어요, 형님, 말이든 개든, 사람이든요. 아니, 바로 지난 학기에, 제가 정학당하기 직전에, 그러니까 제 말은, 홍역에 걸리기 직전에 말예요, 하, 하 — 저랑 크라이스트처치 칼리지의 링우드, 그러니까 시크바스 경 아들 밥 링우드가 블레넘의 벨에서 맥주를 마시고 있는데, 밴버리 뱃사공 녀석이 펀치 한 사발 내기로 우리 둘 중 누구든 한판 붙자고 하지 뭐예요. 저는 못 했어요. 팔을 삼각건에 매고 있었거든요. 마차 브레이크조차 내릴 수가 없었어요 — 그 이틀 전에 애빙던 사냥에 나갔다가 제 암말, 그 망할 놈의 짐승이 저를 넘어뜨려서, 팔이 부러진 줄 알았다니까요. 그래서, 제가 그놈을 못 끝장냈는데, 밥은 단번에 웃옷을 벗어 던지고는 — 그 밴버리 녀석을 상대로 삼 분을 버티다가 네 라운드 만에 가뿐히 해치워 버렸어요. 세상에, 그놈이 어떻게 나가떨어지던지, 형님, 근데 그게 뭐 때문이었겠어요? 혈통이에요, 형님, 다 혈통 덕이라고요.\"\n\n\"자네 안 마시는군, 제임스.\" 전직 대사관원이 말을 이었다. \"내가 옥스퍼드 다닐 때는 다들 자네 젊은 친구들보다 술병을 좀 더 빨리 돌렸는데 말이야.\"\n\n\"이러지, 이러지 마세요.\" 제임스가 코에 손을 갖다 대고 술기운 어린 두 눈으로 사촌에게 윙크하며 말했다. \"농담 마세요, 형님, 저한테 수작 부리지 마시라고요. 절 떠보려는 거지만, 안 통해요. 인 비노 베리타스, 형님. 마르스, 바쿠스, 아폴로 비로룸, 안 그래요? 우리 고모님이 이걸 좀 아버지한테 보내 주셨으면 좋겠네, 끝내주는 술인데 말이야.\"\n\n\"그럼 고모님께 청해 보지 그러나.\" 마키아벨리 같은 피트가 말을 이었다. \"아니면 지금 이 시간을 최대한 즐기든가. 시인이 뭐라 했더라? '눈크 비노 펠리테 쿠라스, 크라스 인겐스 이테라비무스 아이쿠오르.'\" 그러자 이 술꾼은 하원 의원 같은 태도로 위 구절을 읊으며, 잔을 어마어마하게 휘저어 골무만큼의 포도주를 털어 넣었다.\n\n목사관에서는 저녁 식사 후 포트와인 한 병을 따면, 처녀들은 각자 까치밥나무 열매주 한 병에서 한 잔씩 받았다. 뷰트 부인은 포트와인 한 잔을 들었고, 정직한 제임스는 보통 두어 잔을 마셨지만, 그가 그 병에 더 손을 대면 아버지가 몹시 심통을 부리는 통에, 이 착한 청년은 대개 더 마시려는 시도를 삼가고 까치밥나무 열매주로 옮겨 가거나, 아니면 마구간에서 마부와 자기 파이프를 벗 삼아 몰래 마시는 진토닉으로 만족하곤 했다. 옥스퍼드에서는 포도주의 양이 무제한이었으나 질은 떨어졌다. 그런데 고모 집에서처럼 양과 질이 하나로 합쳐지자, 제임스는 자신이 그것을 정말로 음미할 줄 안다는 것을 보여 주었으니, 보울스 씨가 내온 두 번째 병을 비우는 데 사촌의 부추김 따위는 거의 필요치 않았다.\n\n그러나 커피 시간이 되고, 그가 어려워하는 숙녀들에게로 돌아갈 때가 되자, 이 젊은 신사의 그 유쾌한 솔직함은 사라져 버리고, 그는 평소의 무뚝뚝한 소심함으로 되돌아가 그저 예니 아니오니 하는 것으로, 제인 부인을 노려보는 것으로, 그리고 저녁나절 커피 한 잔을 엎지르는 것으로 만족했다.\n\n말을 하지 않을 때면 그는 가엾을 만큼 하품을 해 댔고, 그의 존재는 저녁의 조촐한 일과에 찬물을 끼얹었다. 피케를 치는 크롤리 양과 제인 부인도, 뜨개질을 하는 브리그스 양도, 그의 두 눈이 자기들에게 게슴츠레 고정되어 있는 것을 느끼고는 그 게슴츠레한 시선 아래 불편해했던 것이다.\n\n\"저 애는 무척 과묵하고 어색하고 수줍음 많은 청년인 것 같구나.\" 크롤리 양이 피트 씨에게 말했다.\n\n\"숙녀들과 있을 때보다 남자들 사이에서 더 말이 많은 편이지요.\" 마키아벨리 같은 피트가 무뚝뚝하게 대꾸했다. 어쩌면 포트와인이 짐의 입을 더 열어 주지 못한 것에 다소 실망한 것이었으리라.\n\n그는 이튿날 아침 일찍 어머니에게 크롤리 양의 환대를 더없이 화려하게 부풀린 편지를 쓰며 보냈다. 그러나 아아! 그는 그날이 자신에게 어떤 재앙을 가져올지, 또 그의 총애받는 치세가 얼마나 짧게 끝나도록 정해져 있는지 까맣게 몰랐다. 짐이 까맣게 잊고 있던 한 가지 사정 — 사소하지만 치명적인 한 가지 사정이 — 그가 고모 집에 오기 전날 밤 크립스 암스에서 벌어졌던 것이다. 다름이 아니라 — 늘 후한 성품인 데다 특히 술에 취하면 더욱 손님 대접이 융숭한 짐이, 그날 밤 내내 터트베리 챔피언과 로팅딘 사내와 그들의 친구들에게 진토닉을 두세 차례나 대접한 것이었다 — 그리하여 한 잔에 팔 펜스 하는 그 술이 무려 열여덟 잔이나 제임스 크롤리 씨의 계산서에 올랐다. 고모의 집사 보울스 씨가 안주인의 부탁으로 이 젊은 신사의 청구서를 치르러 내려갔을 때, 가엾은 제임스의 평판에 치명타를 가한 것은 그 팔 펜스의 합계가 아니라 진의 양이었다. 여관 주인은 행여 그 계산이 통째로 거부될까 두려워, 그 젊은 신사가 그 술을 한 푼어치도 빠짐없이 혼자 다 마셨노라고 엄숙히 맹세했다. 결국 보울스는 청구서를 치렀고, 집에 돌아와 그것을 퍼킨 부인에게 보여 주었으니, 그녀는 그 무시무시한 진 낭비에 경악했다. 그리고 그 청구서는 회계 총책임자 격인 브리그스 양에게 전해졌고, 그녀는 그 사정을 자신의 주인인 크롤리 양에게 고하는 것이 자신의 의무라고 여겼다.\n\n만약 그가 클라레 열두 병을 마셨더라면 늙은 노처녀는 그를 용서할 수 있었으리라. 폭스 씨도 셰리든 씨도 클라레를 마셨다. 신사들은 클라레를 마셨다. 그러나 비천한 술집에서 권투 선수들 틈에 끼어 진 열여덟 잔을 비웠다니 — 그것은 가증스러운 죄였고 선뜻 용서할 수 있는 일이 아니었다. 모든 것이 그 청년에게 불리하게 돌아갔다. 그는 마구간에서 향수 냄새를 풍기며 돌아왔으니, 거기서 자기 개 타우저를 보러 갔다가, 그 친구를 데리고 바람을 쐬러 나오려던 참에 크롤리 양과 그녀의 쌕쌕거리는 블레넘 스패니얼과 마주쳤던 것이다. 그 블레넘이 깩깩거리며 브리그스 양의 보호 아래로 달아나지 않았더라면 타우저가 그놈을 잡아먹었을 터인데, 그 흉포한 불도그의 주인은 그 끔찍한 박해를 보며 웃고 서 있었다.\n\n이날따라 그 불운한 청년에게서는 수줍음마저 떠나 버렸다. 그는 저녁 식사 자리에서 활기차고 익살을 부렸다. 식사 중에 그는 피트 크롤리를 겨냥해 한두 마디 농을 던졌고, 전날만큼이나 포도주를 들이켰으며, 아무런 의심 없이 응접실로 가서는 옥스퍼드의 한가락 하는 이야기들로 숙녀들을 즐겁게 해 주기 시작했다. 그는 몰리뉴와 더치 샘의 권투 기량의 차이를 묘사했고, 제인 부인에게 로팅딘 사내를 상대로 터트베리 펫에게 배당을 걸어 주겠다고, 혹은 부인이 원한다면 그 반대로 걸겠다고 장난스레 제안했다. 그러고는 사촌 피트 크롤리를 상대로, 글러브를 끼든 안 끼든 자기 자신에게 돈을 걸겠노라 제안하는 것으로 그 익살의 절정을 장식했다. \"그건 공정한 제안이라고요, 형님.\" 그가 큰 소리로 웃으며 피트의 어깨를 탁 치며 말했다. \"그리고 우리 아버지도 이렇게 제안하라고 하셨다고요, 아버지도 그 내기에 절반 거실 거예요, 하, 하!\" 그렇게 말하면서, 이 사랑스러운 청년은 가엾은 브리그스 양에게 아는 체하며 고갯짓을 하고는, 어깨너머로 엄지손가락을 들어 피트 크롤리를 장난스럽고도 의기양양하게 가리켰다.\n\n피트는 어쩌면 그리 유쾌하지는 않았겠으나, 그래도 대체로 기분이 나쁘지는 않았다. 가엾은 짐은 실컷 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그리고 노부인이 자리를 뜨려 하자 그녀의 촛불을 들고 비틀비틀 방을 가로질렀고, 더없이 부드러운 취중 미소로 그녀에게 입을 맞추려 했다. 그러고는 스스로 작별을 고하고 위층 자기 침실로 올라갔으니, 자기 자신에게 더없이 만족했고, 고모의 재산이 아버지와 나머지 온 가족을 제치고 자신에게 남겨지리라는 흐뭇한 생각에 젖어 있었다.\n\n일단 침실에 올라가고 나면 더는 일을 그르칠 수 없으리라 여길 법도 하건만, 이 불운한 청년은 기어이 그르치고 말았다. 달이 바다 위로 무척 기분 좋게 빛나고 있었고, 짐은 바다와 하늘의 낭만적인 정경에 이끌려 창가로 가서는, 담배를 피우며 그것을 더 만끽하리라 생각했다. 창문을 슬쩍 열고 머리와 파이프를 바깥 신선한 공기 속에 내밀고 있으면 아무도 담배 냄새를 맡지 못하리라고 그는 생각했다. 그는 그렇게 했다. 그러나 흥분한 상태였던 가엾은 짐은 그동안 내내 방문이 열려 있다는 것을 깜박 잊고 있었으니, 그리하여 바람이 안으로 불어 들어 멋들어진 통풍이 이루어지면서, 담배 연기 구름이 아래층으로 실려 내려가 그 향기를 조금도 잃지 않은 채 크롤리 양과 브리그스 양에게 당도하고 만 것이다.\n\n담배 한 파이프가 모든 일을 결딴내고 말았으니, 뷰트 크롤리 일가는 그것이 자기들에게 수천 파운드의 대가를 치르게 했다는 것을 끝내 알지 못했다. 퍼킨이 아래층 보울스에게 달려 내려갔는데, 그는 부관 격인 젊은이에게 크고 으스스한 목소리로 「불과 프라이팬」을 낭독해 주고 있던 참이었다. 퍼킨이 어찌나 겁에 질린 얼굴로 그 끔찍한 비밀을 그에게 일러 주던지, 처음 한순간 보울스 씨와 그 젊은이는 집 안에 강도가 들었다고, 아마 크롤리 양의 침대 밑에서 그 여자가 강도의 다리를 발견한 것이라고 여겼다. 그러나 사실을 알게 되자 — 한 번에 세 계단씩 위층으로 달려 올라가, 아무것도 모르는 제임스의 방으로 들어가 놀라움에 숨이 막힌 목소리로 \"제임스 씨\"를 부르고는 \"제발이지, 나리, 그 파이프 좀 그만 피우십시오\" 하고 외치는 것이, 보울스 씨에게는 일 분 만에 해치운 일이었다. \"오, 제임스 씨, 대체 무슨 짓을 하신 겁니까!\" 그가 그 물건을 창밖으로 내던지며 더없이 비통한 목소리로 말했다. \"무슨 짓을 하신 거예요, 나리! 마님은 그걸 도무지 못 견디신다고요.\"\n\n\"마님은 안 피우면 되잖아요.\" 제임스가 미친 듯한, 번지수 틀린 웃음을 터뜨리며 말했으니, 그는 이 모든 일을 그저 멋들어진 농담쯤으로 여겼다. 그러나 이튿날 아침 그의 심정은 사뭇 달라졌다. 보울스 씨의 젊은이가 제임스 씨의 부츠를 손질하고, 그가 그토록 애타게 기다리던 그 수염을 깎으라고 더운물을 가져다주면서, 침대에 누운 제임스 씨에게 브리그스 양의 필체로 쓰인 쪽지 한 장을 건넸던 것이다.\n\n\"친애하는 선생께.\" 쪽지에는 이렇게 적혀 있었다. \"크롤리 양께서는 집 안이 담배로 더럽혀진 그 충격적인 처사로 인해 지극히 어지러운 밤을 보내셨습니다. 크롤리 양께서는 떠나시기 전에 뵙기에는 몸이 너무 편찮으신 것이 유감이라고, 또 무엇보다 선생을 그 술집에서 옮겨 오시게 한 것 자체를 유감으로 여기신다고 전하라 하셨습니다. 브라이턴에 머무시는 남은 기간 동안 그곳이 분명 훨씬 더 편안하시리라는 것이지요.\"\n\n이로써 고모의 총애를 얻고자 한 정직한 제임스의 입후보 경력은 막을 내렸다. 그는 사실, 자기도 모르게, 자신이 으름장 놓았던 바로 그 일을 해낸 셈이었다. 그는 사촌 피트와 글러브를 끼고 한판 붙은 것이었다.\n\n그렇다면 그동안, 한때 이 돈을 노린 경주에서 가장 유력한 후보였던 그 사내는 어디에 있었던가? 베키와 로던은, 우리가 이미 보았듯, 워털루 이후 다시 합쳐져 1815년 겨울을 파리에서 대단한 호화로움과 흥겨움 속에 보내고 있었다. 레베카는 살림을 잘 꾸리는 솜씨가 있었으니, 가엾은 조스 세들리가 그녀에게서 말 두 마리를 사느라 치른 값만으로도 그들의 자그마한 살림을 적어도 일 년은 너끈히 꾸려 갈 만했다. \"마커 대위를 쏘았던 바로 그 권총\"이라든가, 금 화장도구함이라든가, 검은담비 모피를 댄 망토 따위를 돈으로 바꿀 필요는 없었다. 베키는 그 망토를 자기 외투로 고쳐 만들어, 그것을 입고 불로뉴 숲을 거닐며 모든 이의 찬탄을 받았다. 또 군대가 캉브레에 입성한 뒤 그녀가 남편과 다시 만났을 때, 그녀가 옷의 솔기를 뜯어 브뤼셀에서 도주를 궁리하기에 앞서 속을 채운 솜 속에 감춰 두었던 그 온갖 시계며 자질구레한 장신구며 지폐며 수표며 귀중품을 죄다 꺼내 보였을 때, 그녀와 기뻐 어쩔 줄 모르는 남편 사이에 벌어진 그 광경을 여러분이 보았어야 하는데! 터프토는 매료되었고, 로던은 기뻐서 너털웃음을 터뜨리며, 맹세코 그녀가 자기가 본 어떤 연극보다도 낫다고 단언했다. 그리고 그녀가 조스를 어떻게 속여 넘겼는지, 그것을 한없이 재미나게 묘사하자 그의 기쁨은 그야말로 미친 듯한 열광의 경지에까지 치솟았다. 그는 프랑스 병사들이 나폴레옹을 믿었던 만큼이나 자기 아내를 믿었다.\n\n파리에서 그녀의 성공은 눈부셨다. 프랑스 부인들은 모두 그녀를 매력적이라고 평했다. 그녀는 그들의 언어를 훌륭하게 구사했다. 그녀는 그들의 우아함이며 활기며 몸가짐을 단번에 제 것으로 만들었다. 그녀의 남편은 분명 멍청했고 — 영국인은 다 멍청하니까 — 게다가 파리에서 따분한 남편이란 늘 부인에게 유리한 점이 되어 주었다. 그는 부유하고 재기 넘치는 크롤리 양의 상속자였는데, 그 부인의 집은 망명 시절 프랑스 귀족들에게 두루 열려 있었던 터였다. 그들은 대령의 아내를 자기들 저택으로 맞아들였다. 어느 지체 높은 부인이 크롤리 양에게 편지를 썼는데 — 그녀는 그 공작부인이 부르는 값으로 레이스와 장신구를 사 주고, 혁명 이후의 궁핍한 시절에 여러 차례 만찬에 초대해 주었던 사람이었다 — \"우리의 다정한 미스께서는 어찌하여 파리에 있는 조카 내외분과, 또 그토록 정다운 벗들에게 와 주시지 않는 건가요? 온 세상이 그 매력적인 부인과 그 장난스러운 미모에 홀딱 빠져 있답니다. 그래요, 우리는 그분에게서 우리의 다정한 벗 크롤리 양의 우아함과 매력과 재치를 본답니다! 어제 튀일리궁에서 국왕께서 그분을 눈여겨보셨고, 무슈께서 그분에게 베푸시는 관심에 우리 모두 질투가 날 지경이에요. 어떤 멍청한 베어에이커스 부인이 — 그 매부리코며 토크 모자며 깃털 장식이 어느 모임에서나 사람들 머리 위로 삐죽 솟아오르는 그 부인이 — 얼마나 심술이 났던지 여러분이 보셨어야 하는데, 왕가의 고귀한 따님이자 동반자이신 앙굴렘 공작부인께서 특별히 크롤리 부인을, 당신의 그 사랑하는 딸이자 피후견인을 소개받고 싶다 청하셔서, 망명 시절 우리 불우한 이들에게 베풀어 주신 당신의 온갖 자비에 대해 프랑스의 이름으로 그분께 감사를 표하셨을 때 말이에요! 그분은 모든 모임에, 모든 무도회에 — 무도회에는요 — 그래요 — 춤을 추느냐 하면, 그건 아니에요. 그런데도 사내들의 흠모에 둘러싸여, 또 머지않아 어머니가 될 이 어여쁜 여인이 어찌나 흥미롭고 예뻐 보이는지요! 그분이 당신을, 자신의 보호자요 어머니인 당신을 이야기하는 것을 들으면 식인귀의 눈에도 눈물이 맺힐 거예요. 그분이 당신을 얼마나 사랑하는지요! 우리 모두 우리의 훌륭하고 존경스러운 크롤리 양을 얼마나 사랑하는지요!\"\n\n이 파리 귀부인의 편지가 베키 부인의 이익을 그 훌륭하고 존경스러운 친척에게 조금도 보탬이 되게 하지 못했으리라는 것은 짐작하고도 남는다. 도리어 늙은 노처녀의 분노는, 레베카의 처지가 어떠한지, 또 파리 사교계에 발을 들이려고 그녀가 얼마나 뻔뻔스럽게 크롤리 양의 이름을 이용했는지 알게 되자 그야말로 끝 간 데를 몰랐다. 너무도 마음이 흔들리고 몸이 쇠약해진 탓에 그 서신의 답장을 프랑스어로 지을 수가 없어, 그녀는 브리그스에게 자기 모국어로 격노에 찬 답장을 받아쓰게 했으니, 로던 크롤리 부인을 통째로 부인하고, 세상 사람들에게 그녀가 더없이 교활하고 위험한 인물이니 조심하라고 경고하는 내용이었다. 그러나 X 공작부인은 영국에 와서 산 지 겨우 이십 년밖에 되지 않은 터라 그 언어를 한마디도 알아듣지 못했고, 다음번에 만났을 때 로던 크롤리 부인에게, 그 사랑하는 미스에게서 매력적인 편지를 받았는데 크롤리 부인을 위한 자애로운 말들로 가득하더라고 일러 주는 것으로 만족했으니, 그리하여 크롤리 부인은 그 노처녀가 마음을 누그러뜨릴지도 모른다고 진지하게 기대하기 시작했다.\n\n그러는 동안 그녀는 영국 여인들 가운데 가장 흥겹고 가장 흠모받는 여인이었고, 손님 맞는 날 밤이면 자신의 집에서 자그마한 유럽 회의를 열었다. 프로이센인이며 코사크인이며 스페인인이며 영국인이며 — 이 유명한 겨울 동안 온 세상이 파리에 와 있었던 것이다. 레베카의 소박한 응접실에 모인 그 훈장들과 약장들을 보았더라면 베이커 거리 전체가 시샘으로 새파래졌으리라. 이름난 무사들이 불로뉴 숲에서 그녀의 마차 곁을 말 달렸고, 오페라극장의 그녀의 자그마한 박스석에 빼곡히 모여들었다. 로던은 더없이 기분이 좋았다. 파리에는 아직 빚쟁이가 없었고, 베리네나 보빌리에네에서 날마다 파티가 열렸으며, 도박은 흔했고 그의 운도 좋았다. 터프토는 어쩌면 심통이 났을 것이다. 터프토 부인이 자청해서 파리로 건너온 데다, 이 낭패에 더하여 이제는 수십 명의 장군들이 베키의 의자를 에워쌌고, 그녀가 연극을 보러 갈 때면 열 몇 개의 꽃다발 가운데서 고를 수 있게 되었던 것이다. 베어에이커스 부인과 영국 사교계의 우두머리들, 그 멍청하고 나무랄 데 없는 여인들은 저 자그마한 벼락출세자 베키의 성공에 고통으로 몸부림쳤으니, 베키의 독 묻은 농담이 그들의 정숙한 가슴속에 박혀 부르르 떨며 욱신거렸다. 그러나 사내들은 죄다 그녀 편이었다. 그녀는 불굴의 용기로 여인들과 맞서 싸웠고, 여인들은 자기네 언어가 아니고서는 험담조차 할 수가 없었다.\n\n그리하여 갖가지 축제와 향락과 번영 속에서 1815년에서 16년에 걸친 겨울이 로던 크롤리 부인과 함께 지나갔으니, 그녀는 마치 자기 조상들이 수백 년 전부터 멋쟁이였기라도 한 듯 상류 생활에 척척 적응했고, 그 재치와 재능과 활력으로 보아 참으로 허영의 시장에서 명예로운 자리를 차지할 만했다. 1816년 이른 봄, 갈리냐니 신문의 한 흥미로운 구석에 다음과 같은 소식이 실렸다. \"3월 26일 — 근위 기병대 그린 소속 크롤리 중령의 부인, 대를 이을 아들을 출산.\"\n\n이 소식은 런던 신문들에도 옮겨 실렸고, 브라이턴에서 아침 식사 자리에 브리그스 양이 그 기사를 크롤리 양에게 읽어 주었다. 어느 정도 예상되었던 일이긴 했으나, 이 소식은 크롤리 가문의 일에 한바탕 위기를 불러왔다. 노처녀의 분노는 절정에 달했으니, 그녀는 당장 조카 피트와, 브런즈윅 광장에 있는 사우스다운 부인을 불러, 두 집안 사이에 그토록 오래 미루어져 온 혼인을 즉각 거행해 달라고 청했다. 그러고는 자신이 살아 있는 동안 그 젊은 부부에게 해마다 천 파운드를 주고, 자신이 세상을 떠나면 재산의 대부분을 조카와, 사랑하는 조카며느리 제인 크롤리 부인에게 물려줄 작정이라고 선언했다. 왁시가 그 증서들을 확정하러 내려왔고 — 사우스다운 경이 누이를 신랑에게 넘겨주었으며 — 그녀는 바살러뮤 아이언스 목사가 아니라 주교의 주례로 결혼했으니 — 그 변칙적인 고위 성직자에게는 실망스러운 일이었다.\n\n결혼하고 나자 피트는 자기들 처지에 걸맞게 신부와 함께 신혼여행을 떠나고 싶었다. 그러나 제인 부인을 향한 노부인의 애정이 어찌나 깊어졌던지, 그녀는 자기가 그 귀염둥이와 떨어질 수 없노라고 솔직히 털어놓았다. 그리하여 피트와 그 아내는 들어와 크롤리 양과 함께 살게 되었다. 그리고 (가엾은 피트로서는 큰 골칫거리였으니, 그는 한쪽에서는 고모의 변덕에, 다른 쪽에서는 장모의 변덕에 시달리며 자신이 더없이 부당한 처지에 놓였다고 여겼다) 사우스다운 부인은 이웃한 자기 집에서 온 식구를 — 피트며, 제인 부인이며, 크롤리 양이며, 브리그스며, 보울스며, 퍼킨이며 죄다 — 군림하며 다스렸다. 그녀는 가차 없이 그들에게 자기 소책자와 약을 들이부었고, 크리머를 내쫓고 로저스를 앉혔으며, 머지않아 크롤리 양에게서 권위라고는 그 시늉마저 죄다 벗겨 버렸다. 가엾은 그녀는 어찌나 소심해졌던지 실제로 더는 브리그스를 들볶지 않게 되었고, 날마다 더욱 다정하면서도 겁에 질려 조카며느리에게 매달렸다. 다정하면서도 이기적이고, 허영에 차 있으면서도 너그러웠던, 늙은 이교도여, 그대에게 평안이 깃들기를! 우리는 이제 다시 그대를 보지 못하리라. 부디 제인 부인이 그녀를 다정히 부축하여, 허영의 시장의 분주한 아귀다툼에서 부드러운 손으로 이끌어 내 주었기를 바라자.\n\n카트르브라와 워털루의 대전투 소식은 같은 시각에 영국에 당도했다. 관보가 가장 먼저 그 두 전투의 결과를 발표했고, 그 영광스러운 소식에 온 영국이 승리감과 두려움으로 전율했다. 이어 자세한 내용이 뒤따랐다. 그리고 승전 소식 다음에는 부상자와 전사자 명단이 실렸다. 그 명단을 펼쳐 읽어 내려갈 때의 그 공포를 누가 능히 말할 수 있겠는가! 세 왕국에 걸친 거의 모든 마을과 농가에 플랑드르 전투의 대뉴스가 당도하는 광경을, 그리고 연대별 손실 명단을 훑어 내려가며 사랑하는 벗이며 친지가 무사한지 쓰러졌는지를 알게 될 때의 그 환희와 감사, 사별과 속이 메스꺼워지는 절망의 심정을 떠올려 보라. 수고를 마다하지 않고 그 시절의 신문철을 들춰 보는 사람이라면, 누구든 지금이라도 그 숨 막히는 기대의 정적을 간접적으로나마 느낄 수 있을 것이다. 사상자 명단은 날마다 계속 이어진다. 다음 호에 계속되는 이야기를 읽다가 한복판에서 멈추는 것처럼 말이다. 그 신문들이 인쇄기에서 갓 나온 채 연달아 당도할 때의 심정이 어떠했을지 생각해 보라. 그리고 우리 국민 가운데 겨우 이만 명이 참전한 전투 하나를 두고 우리나라에서 그토록 큰 관심이 일었다면, 그 이십 년 전, 사람들이 수천 명이 아니라 수백만 명 단위로 싸웠던 유럽의 형편을 생각해 보라. 그 한 사람 한 사람이 적을 칠 때마다 저 멀리 어딘가에 있는 또 다른 죄 없는 가슴 하나를 끔찍하게 찢어 놓았던 것이다.\n\n그 유명한 관보가 오스본 일가에 전한 소식은 가족과 그 가장에게 무서운 충격을 안겼다. 딸들은 아무런 거리낌 없이 슬픔에 몸을 내맡겼다. 침통에 짓눌린 늙은 아버지는 그 운명과 비탄에 더더욱 짓눌렸다. 그는 아들의 불순종에 내려진 심판이라고 생각하려 애썼다. 그 선고의 가혹함이 자신을 두렵게 한다는 것을, 그리고 그 저주에 너무도 빨리 응보가 떨어졌다는 것을 차마 인정할 수 없었다. 때로는 마치 자신이 아들에게 내려 달라고 빌었던 그 파멸의 장본인이라도 되는 양, 부르르 떨리는 공포가 그를 엄습했다. 전에는 화해의 기회가 있었다. 아들의 아내가 죽을 수도 있었고, 아니면 아들이 돌아와 \"아버지, 제가 죄를 지었습니다\"라고 말할 수도 있었다. 그러나 이제는 아무 희망도 없었다. 아들은 건널 수 없는 심연의 저편에 서서, 슬픈 눈으로 아비를 따라다니며 괴롭혔다. 그는 전에 한 번 그 눈을 본 일을 떠올렸다. 열병에 걸려 모두가 그 아이가 죽어 간다고 여겼을 때, 그 아이는 침대에 누워 말도 못 한 채 무서운 침울함을 띠고 멍하니 바라보고 있었다. 오, 하느님! 그때 아비가 의사에게 얼마나 매달렸던지, 또 얼마나 속이 타들어 가는 불안으로 그의 뒤를 좇았던지. 열병의 고비가 지나고 그 아이가 회복되어 다시금 아비를 알아보는 눈으로 그를 바라보았을 때, 그의 마음에서 떨어져 나간 비탄의 무게는 또 얼마나 컸던지. 그러나 지금은 도움도, 치유도, 화해의 기회도 없었다. 무엇보다도, 모욕당해 격노한 허영을 달래거나 독과 분노로 끓어오른 피를 본래의 흐름으로 되돌릴, 그 어떤 겸손한 말 한마디도 없었다. 그리고 이 거만한 아비의 가슴을 가장 날카롭게 찢어 놓은 고통이 어느 것이었는지는 말하기 어렵다. 아들이 그의 용서가 미칠 수 없는 곳으로 가 버렸다는 것인지, 아니면 그 자신의 자존심이 기대해 마지않던 그 사과의 말을 영영 듣지 못하게 되었다는 것인지.\n\n그러나 그의 심정이 어떠했든, 이 준엄한 늙은이는 누구에게도 속을 털어놓으려 하지 않았다. 그는 딸들에게 아들의 이름을 결코 입에 올리지 않았다. 다만 큰딸에게 명하여 집안의 모든 여자에게 상복을 입히게 했고, 남자 하인들도 그와 마찬가지로 짙은 검정 옷을 입히도록 분부했다. 물론 모든 파티와 연회는 미루기로 되었다. 혼삿날까지 정해져 있던 장차의 사위에게는 아무런 통보도 가지 않았다. 그러나 오스본 씨의 안색만으로도 불록 씨가 어떤 문의를 하거나 어떤 식으로든 그 예식을 서두르지 못하기에 충분했다. 그와 숙녀들은 이따금 응접실에서 그 일을 두고 목소리를 낮춰 소곤거렸는데, 그곳에 아버지는 결코 발을 들이지 않았다. 그는 줄곧 자기 서재에만 틀어박혀 있었고, 전체 상기(喪期)가 끝나고도 한참 뒤까지 집의 앞쪽 부분은 모두 닫혀 있었다.\n\n6월 18일로부터 삼 주쯤 지난 어느 날, 오스본 씨의 지인인 윌리엄 도빈 경이 몹시 창백하고 동요한 얼굴로 러셀 광장의 오스본 씨 댁을 찾아와 한사코 그를 만나야겠다고 청했다. 방으로 안내되어 들어가서, 말하는 쪽도 듣는 쪽도 알아듣지 못한 몇 마디를 나눈 뒤, 손님은 봉투에서 커다란 빨간 봉인이 찍힌 편지 한 통을 꺼냈다. \"내 아들 도빈 소령이,\" 시 참사회원이 다소 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"오늘 시내에 도착한 ××연대의 한 장교 편에 내게 편지를 보냈소. 그 아이 편지 안에 당신 앞으로 온 편지가 한 통 들어 있더이다, 오스본.\" 참사회원은 그 편지를 탁자 위에 올려놓았고, 오스본은 잠시 말없이 그를 빤히 바라보았다. 그의 눈빛에 그 사자(使者)는 겁을 먹었고, 슬픔에 짓눌린 사내를 잠깐 죄지은 듯한 표정으로 바라보다가 더는 한마디도 없이 황급히 자리를 떴다.\n\n그 편지는 조지의 익히 알려진, 굵고 대담한 필체로 쓰여 있었다. 그것은 그가 6월 16일 동트기 전, 애밀리아와 작별하기 직전에 쓴 바로 그 편지였다. 그 큰 빨간 봉인에는 오스본이 귀족 명감에서 빌려와 제멋대로 쓰던 가짜 문장(紋章)이 새겨져 있었고, \"전쟁 중에도 평화\"라는 명구가 적혀 있었다. 이 허영심 많은 늙은이가 자신과 인연이 닿는다고 멋대로 상상하던 어느 공작 가문의 문장이었다. 그 편지에 서명한 손은 이제 다시는 펜도 칼도 쥐지 못할 터였다. 그것을 봉한 바로 그 봉인은 전장에 쓰러져 있던 조지의 시신에서 약탈당한 것이었다. 아비는 그런 사실을 전혀 알지 못한 채, 앉아서 겁에 질린 공허한 눈으로 편지를 바라보았다. 그것을 열려고 했을 때 그는 거의 쓰러질 뻔했다.\n\n당신은 사랑하는 벗과 사이가 틀어져 본 적이 있는가? 사랑과 신뢰의 시절에 쓰인 그 사람의 편지들이 얼마나 사람의 속을 메스껍게 하고 또 책망하는지! 죽어 버린 애정의 그 격렬한 맹세들을 곱씹는 일이 얼마나 음울한 애도인지! 그것들은 사랑의 시신 위에 새겨진 얼마나 거짓된 비문(碑文)인가! 인생과 허영에 대한 얼마나 어둡고 잔인한 주석인가! 우리 대부분은 그런 편지가 서랍 가득 들어 있거나 그런 편지를 써 본 적이 있다. 그것들은 우리가 간직하면서도 외면하는, 벽장 속 해골이다. 오스본은 죽은 아들의 편지 앞에서 오랫동안 떨었다.\n\n그 가엾은 아이의 편지에는 별다른 말이 없었다. 그는 너무도 거만해서 자기 가슴에 어린 애정을 인정하지 못했던 것이다. 그는 다만 대전투를 앞둔 전날 밤, 아버지께 작별을 고하고 싶다는 것과, 자기가 뒤에 남기고 가는 아내를—어쩌면 그 아이까지도—위해 부디 보살펴 주시기를 엄숙히 간청한다고만 적었다. 그는 자신의 방탕과 사치로 이미 어머니가 남긴 얼마 안 되는 재산의 큰 부분을 탕진했음을 뉘우치며 고백했다. 또한 지난날 아버지가 베풀어 준 너그러운 처사에 감사하며, 전장에서 쓰러지든 살아남든 조지 오스본이라는 이름에 부끄럽지 않게 처신하겠노라고 약속했다.\n\n그의 영국식 습성과 자존심, 어쩌면 서투름이 더는 말을 하지 못하게 막았던 것이다. 아비는 조지가 편지 겉봉에 입맞춤을 남긴 것을 알아보지 못했다. 오스본 씨는 애정과 복수가 함께 좌절된 가장 쓰라리고 치명적인 고통과 더불어 그 편지를 떨어뜨렸다. 그의 아들은 여전히 사랑받았으나 용서받지는 못했다.\n\n그러나 그로부터 두 달쯤 지났을 무렵, 이 집안의 아가씨들이 아버지와 함께 교회에 갔다가, 아버지가 예배에 참석하기로 마음먹었을 때 늘 앉던 자리와 다른 자리를 잡는 것을, 그리고 맞은편 방석에서 자기들 머리 너머의 벽을 올려다보는 것을 알아챘다. 이에 아가씨들도 아버지의 침울한 눈길이 향하는 쪽을 바라보게 되었고, 벽에 정교한 기념비 하나가 걸려 있는 것을 보았다. 거기에는 브리타니아 여신이 유골 단지 위에서 눈물 흘리는 모습이 새겨져 있었고, 부러진 검과 엎드린 사자가 그 조각이 어느 전사한 무인을 기리어 세워진 것임을 일러 주고 있었다. 그 시절의 조각가들은 그런 장례용 표상들을 잔뜩 갖춰 두고 있었으니, 지금도 세인트폴 대성당의 벽에서 볼 수 있듯, 그 벽은 이런 허세 가득한 이교적 우의(寓意) 수백 개로 뒤덮여 있다. 금세기의 첫 십오 년 동안 그런 것들에 대한 수요가 끊이지 않았던 것이다.\n\n문제의 그 기념비 아래에는 그 유명하고 거창한 오스본 가문의 문장이 새겨져 있었다. 그리고 비문에는 이 기념비가 \"국왕 폐하의 ××보병연대 대위로 복무하다가 1815년 6월 18일, 워털루의 영광스러운 승전에서 국왕과 조국을 위해 싸우던 중 향년 스물여덟에 쓰러진 고(故) 조지 오스본 2세 님을 추모하여 바친다. 조국을 위해 죽는 것은 감미롭고 명예로운 일이로다\"라고 적혀 있었다.\n\n그 비석을 보고 자매들은 어찌나 신경이 동요했던지, 마리아 양은 그만 교회를 나가지 않을 수 없었다. 신자들은 짙은 상복 차림으로 흐느끼는 그 아가씨들에게 정중하게 길을 비켜 주었고, 전사한 군인의 기념비 맞은편에 앉은 준엄한 늙은 아버지를 가엾이 여겼다. \"아버지가 조지의 부인을 용서하실까?\" 슬픔의 격발이 가라앉자마자 아가씨들은 속으로 그렇게 생각했다. 아들의 결혼이 빚어낸 부자간의 절연을 알고 있던 오스본 가문의 지인들 사이에서도, 그 젊은 미망인과 화해할 가능성을 두고 많은 이야기가 오갔다. 러셀 광장 일대에서도 시티에서도 신사들 사이에 내기가 벌어졌다.\n\n만약 자매들이 애밀리아가 이 집안의 딸로 인정받을지도 모른다는 데 대해 조금이라도 불안을 품고 있었다면, 그 불안은 이내, 그러니까 가을이 저물어 갈 무렵, 아버지가 외국에 나가겠다고 알리면서 한층 커졌다. 그는 어디로 가는지는 말하지 않았으나, 자매들은 그의 발길이 벨기에로 향하리라는 것을 단번에 알아챘고, 조지의 미망인이 아직 브뤼셀에 머물고 있음을 알고 있었다. 사실 그들은 도빈 부인과 그 딸들을 통해 가엾은 애밀리아의 소식을 제법 정확히 듣고 있었다. 우리의 정직한 대위는 연대의 둘째 소령이 전장에서 전사한 덕분에 진급했고, 용맹한 오다우드는 침착함과 용기를 보일 기회가 있을 때마다 늘 그러했듯 이번에도 크게 무공을 떨쳐 대령에 올랐고 바스 훈장 동반 작위를 받았다.\n\n양일간의 전투에서 큰 손실을 입은 용맹한 ××연대의 수많은 병사들이 가을에도 여전히 브뤼셀에 남아 부상을 회복하고 있었다. 그 도시는 대전투가 끝난 뒤 여러 달 동안 거대한 군 병원이나 다름없었다. 그리고 병사며 장교들이 부상에서 기운을 차리기 시작하자, 정원이며 공공장소마다 사지가 성치 않은 노소(老少)의 무인들이 우글거렸으니, 죽음에서 막 건져 올려진 이들이 허영의 시장 사람들이 으레 그러하듯 도박과 흥청거림과 사랑놀음에 빠져들었던 것이다. 오스본 씨는 ××연대 사람들 몇몇을 손쉽게 찾아냈다. 그는 그 군복을 아주 잘 알고 있었고, 연대의 모든 진급과 전출을 빠짐없이 챙겨 보는 데 익숙했으며, 마치 자신이 그 일원이라도 되는 양 그 연대와 장교들에 대해 이야기하기를 좋아했다. 브뤼셀에 도착한 다음 날, 공원을 마주한 호텔에서 나오다가 그는 익히 아는 그 군복 깃을 한 병사 하나가 정원의 돌 벤치에서 쉬고 있는 것을 보았고, 다가가 그 부상에서 회복 중인 사내 곁에 떨면서 앉았다.\n\n\"오스본 대위 중대에 있었나?\" 그가 물었고, 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 덧붙였다. \"그 사람이 내 아들이라네.\"\n\n그 사내는 대위의 중대 소속이 아니었으나, 성한 팔을 들어 올려 자기에게 묻는 그 초췌하고 기운 꺾인 신사에게 슬프고도 정중하게 군모에 손을 가져다 댔다. \"전군을 통틀어도 그분보다 더 훌륭하고 더 나은 장교는 없었습니다.\" 병사가 말했다. \"다만 대위 중대(이제는 레이먼드 대위가 맡고 있습니다)의 부사관 하나가 시내에 있는데, 어깨에 총상을 입었다가 막 회복한 참입니다. 나리께서 원하시면 그 사람을 만나 보실 수 있을 겁니다. ××연대의 전투에 관해 알고 싶으신 건 무엇이든 그가 말씀드릴 수 있을 테니까요. 하지만 나리께서는 분명 도빈 소령님을 뵈셨겠지요, 그 용맹한 대위의 둘도 없는 벗 말입니다. 그리고 여기 계셨던 오스본 부인도요. 다들 그분이 몹시 안 좋으셨다고들 하더군요. 한 육 주 넘게 거의 정신이 나가 계셨다고들 합니다. 하지만 나리께서야 그런 일은 다 알고 계실 테고요—외람된 말씀입니다만,\" 사내가 덧붙였다.\n\n오스본은 병사의 손에 1기니를 쥐여 주며, 그 부사관을 파르크 호텔로 데려와 준다면 한 닢을 더 주겠다고 일렀다. 그 약속에 곧바로 원하던 장교가 오스본 씨 앞에 나타났다. 그리고 처음의 그 병사는 자리를 떴고, 동료 한둘에게 오스본 대위의 아버지가 와 있다는 것과 그가 얼마나 손이 큰 너그러운 신사인지를 떠벌린 뒤, 그들은 상중인 늙은 아버지의 거만한 지갑에서 나온 그 기니가 다 떨어질 때까지 술과 잔치로 한껏 흥청거렸다.\n\n오스본은 그 부사관과 동행하여—이 부사관 역시 막 회복한 참이었다—워털루와 카트르브라로 여정을 떠났으니, 그 무렵 그의 동포 수천 명이 똑같이 밟고 있던 길이었다. 그는 부사관을 자기 마차에 태우고 그 안내를 받으며 두 전장을 두루 돌아보았다. 그는 16일에 연대가 전투에 투입되며 행군해 들어간 길목과, 퇴각하는 벨기에군을 압박해 오던 프랑스 기병대를 몰아붙여 내려간 비탈을 보았다. 군기 부사관들이 모두 총에 맞아 쓰러지자, 군기를 두고 젊은 기수와 맞붙어 싸우던 프랑스 장교를 그 고결한 대위가 베어 넘긴 바로 그 자리도 있었다. 그들이 이튿날 후퇴해 간 길도 이 길을 따라 나 있었고, 17일 밤 빗속에서 연대가 노숙했던 둑도 여기 있었다. 더 멀리에는 그들이 그날 하루 동안 점령하고 지켜 낸 진지가 있었으니, 거기서 그들은 적 기병대의 돌격을 받기 위해 몇 번이고 대형을 짜며, 프랑스군의 맹렬한 포격을 피해 둑 그늘에 엎드려 있었다. 그리고 적이 마지막 돌격 끝에 물러서고, 저녁이 되어 영국 전선 전체가 진격하라는 명령을 받았을 때, 바로 이 비탈에서 대위가 환호하며 칼을 휘두르며 언덕 아래로 내달리다가 총에 맞아 쓰러져 숨졌던 것이다. \"대위의 시신을 브뤼셀로 모셔 온 것은 도빈 소령님이었습니다.\" 부사관이 나직이 말했다. \"그리고 나리께서도 아시다시피, 매장을 치러 주셨지요.\" 병사가 그 이야기를 풀어놓는 동안, 그 일대의 농부들과 유물 사냥꾼들이 두 사람 주위에 몰려들어 빽빽 소리치며, 십자 훈장이며 견장이며 박살 난 흉갑이며 독수리 군기 장식이며 온갖 전투 기념품을 사라고 내밀었다.\n\n오스본은 아들의 마지막 무용담의 현장들을 둘러본 뒤, 부사관과 헤어지면서 그에게 후한 보수를 베풀었다. 아들의 매장지는 이미 본 터였다. 사실 그는 브뤼셀에 도착하자마자 곧장 그리로 마차를 몰았던 것이다. 조지의 시신은 도시 인근 라켄의 아름다운 묘지에 안치되어 있었다. 일찍이 유람 삼아 한 번 그곳을 찾았을 때, 그는 가벼운 마음으로 자기 무덤을 거기에 두고 싶다는 뜻을 비친 적이 있었다. 그리하여 이 젊은 장교는 벗의 손에 의해, 정원의 축성받지 못한 한구석에 묻혔으니, 로마 가톨릭교도 망자들이 잠든 사원이며 탑이며 꽃과 관목을 심은 화단으로부터 자그마한 산울타리 하나로 갈라진 자리였다. 영국 신사이자 명예로운 영국군의 대위였던 자기 아들이, 한낱 외국인들이 묻힌 땅에 누울 자격조차 갖지 못한 것으로 여겨졌다는 사실이, 늙은 오스본에게는 모욕처럼 느껴졌다. 우리 가운데 누가 능히 말할 수 있겠는가, 남을 향한 우리의 가장 따뜻한 정 속에 얼마나 많은 허영이 도사리고 있는지, 또 우리의 사랑이 얼마나 이기적인지를? 늙은 오스본은 자기 감정의 그 뒤섞인 본질에 대해, 또 자신의 본능과 이기심이 어떻게 서로 다투고 있는지에 대해 깊이 헤아려 보지 않았다. 그는 자신이 하는 모든 일이 옳으며, 어떤 경우에도 자기 뜻대로 해야 마땅하다고 굳게 믿었다. 그리고 말벌이나 뱀의 독침처럼, 그의 증오는 무엇이든 반대 비슷한 것을 향해 독을 품고 무장한 채 튀어나왔다. 그는 다른 모든 것에 대해서와 마찬가지로 자신의 증오를 자랑스러워했다. 늘 옳은 것, 늘 짓밟으며 나아가는 것, 결코 의심하지 않는 것—이것들이야말로 우둔함이 세상에서 앞장서 나아가게 하는 그 위대한 자질들이 아니겠는가?\n\n워털루로 다녀온 길에, 해 질 녘 오스본 씨의 마차가 도시의 성문에 가까워졌을 때, 그들은 또 다른 무개 사륜마차 한 대와 마주쳤다. 그 안에는 숙녀 두 사람과 신사 한 사람이 타고 있었고, 그 옆으로는 장교 하나가 말을 타고 따르고 있었다. 오스본은 흠칫 몸을 뒤로 뺐고, 그와 함께 앉아 있던 부사관은 옆 사람을 놀란 눈으로 흘끗 보면서 그 장교에게 군모에 손을 가져다 대 인사했고, 장교는 기계적으로 그 경례에 답했다. 다리를 저는 젊은 기수를 곁에 두고, 맞은편에는 충직한 벗 오다우드 부인을 앉힌 애밀리아였다. 애밀리아였다, 그러나 오스본이 알던 그 싱그럽고 어여쁜 아가씨에서 얼마나 변해 버렸는지. 그녀의 얼굴은 핏기 없이 야위어 있었다. 그 고운 갈색 머리는 미망인의 모자 아래로 가르마가 타져 있었으니—가엾은 아이였다. 그녀의 두 눈은 한곳에 고정된 채 아무 데도 보고 있지 않았다. 마차가 서로 엇갈릴 때 그 눈은 오스본의 얼굴을 멍하니 응시했으나, 그녀는 그를 알아보지 못했다. 그도 고개를 들어 그녀 곁에서 말을 타고 가는 도빈을 보기 전까지는 그녀를 알아보지 못했다. 그러고 나서야 그는 그게 누구인지 알았다. 그는 그녀를 증오했다. 그녀가 거기 있는 것을 보기 전까지는 자신이 얼마나 그녀를 증오하는지 미처 알지 못했다. 그녀의 마차가 지나가 버리자, 그는 돌아앉아 부사관을 노려보았으니, 그 눈에는 저주와 적의가 어려 있어 자기 옆 사람을 향해 던져졌고, 부사관은 그를 쳐다보지 않을 수 없었다. 그 눈빛은 마치 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"어디 감히 나를 쳐다보는가? 빌어먹을! 그래, 나는 저 여자가 증오스럽다. 내 모든 희망과 내 모든 자존심을 무너뜨린 게 바로 저 여자란 말이다.\" \"저 망할 놈더러 어서 빨리 몰라고 해라.\" 그가 욕설을 내뱉으며 마부석의 하인에게 소리쳤다. 잠시 후, 오스본의 마차 뒤로 말 한 마리가 포석 위를 또각또각 달려왔고, 도빈이 말을 몰아 다가왔다. 마차들이 서로 엇갈릴 때 그의 생각은 다른 데 가 있었던 터라, 몇 걸음 앞으로 나아가고 나서야 비로소 방금 자기를 지나친 사람이 오스본이었음을 기억해 냈다. 그리고 그는 장인을 본 것이 애밀리아에게 어떤 동요라도 일으켰는지 살피려고 돌아보았으나, 가엾은 아가씨는 누가 지나갔는지조차 알지 못했다. 그러자 날마다 그녀의 마차 산책에 동행하곤 하던 윌리엄은 시계를 꺼내 보더니, 문득 떠오른 약속이 있다는 둥 둘러대고는 말을 몰아 가 버렸다. 그녀는 그것 또한 알아채지 못했다. 다만 앞만 바라보며 앉아, 조지가 행군해 떠나간 저 멀리 숲 쪽으로 펼쳐진 수수한 풍경을 응시할 따름이었다.\n\n\"오스본 씨, 오스본 씨!\" 도빈이 말을 몰아 다가가 손을 내밀며 외쳤다. 오스본은 그 손을 잡을 기색도 없이, 또 한 번 욕설과 함께 하인에게 어서 몰라고 소리쳤다.\n\n도빈이 마차 옆면에 손을 얹었다. \"당신을 꼭 만나야겠습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"전해 드릴 말씀이 있어서요.\"\n\n\"그 여자한테서 온 말이오?\" 오스본이 사납게 말했다.\n\n\"아닙니다.\" 상대가 대답했다. \"당신 아드님에게서 온 것입니다.\" 그 말에 오스본은 마차 구석으로 털썩 물러앉았고, 도빈은 마차가 지나가게 둔 채 그 바로 뒤를 바싹 따라 말을 몰아, 한마디도 없이 그렇게 시내를 지나 마침내 오스본 씨의 호텔에 이르렀다. 거기서 그는 오스본을 따라 그의 방까지 올라갔다. 조지도 그 방에 자주 와 있던 적이 있었으니, 크롤리 부부가 브뤼셀에 머무는 동안 묵었던 바로 그 셋방이었다.\n\n\"그래, 내게 무슨 분부가 있으신가, 도빈 대위? 아, 이거 실례했군, 도빈 소령이라고 해야겠지. 당신보다 나은 사람들이 죽었으니 당신이 그 자리를 꿰찼을 테니 말이오.\" 오스본 씨가 이따금 기꺼이 취하곤 하던 그 빈정대는 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"더 나은 사람들이 죽은 게 맞습니다.\" 도빈이 대답했다. \"바로 그 한 사람에 대해 당신과 이야기를 나누고 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"짧게 하시오.\" 상대가 욕설을 내뱉으며, 손님에게 험한 눈초리를 보내며 말했다.\n\n\"저는 그의 가장 가까운 벗으로서,\" 소령이 말을 이었다. \"또 그의 유언 집행인으로서 여기 왔습니다. 그는 전투에 나가기 전에 유언장을 작성했습니다. 그의 재산이 얼마나 보잘것없는지, 또 그 미망인의 형편이 얼마나 옹색한지 알고 계십니까?\"\n\n\"나는 그 사람 미망인을 모르오.\" 오스본이 말했다. \"그 여자는 제 아비한테로 돌아가라고 하시오.\" 그러나 그가 말을 건넨 신사는 평정을 잃지 않기로 작정한 터라, 그 방해에도 아랑곳없이 말을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"오스본 부인의 처지를 아십니까? 그녀에게 떨어진 그 일격으로 그녀의 목숨과 정신이 거의 다 흔들리고 말았습니다. 그녀가 다시 기력을 차릴지조차 매우 불확실합니다. 그러나 그녀에게 한 가지 희망이 남아 있고, 바로 그 일로 당신께 말씀드리러 온 것입니다. 그녀는 머지않아 어머니가 됩니다. 당신은 아비의 죄를 그 아이의 머리에 물으시렵니까? 아니면 가엾은 조지를 봐서라도 그 아이를 용서하시렵니까?\"\n\n오스본은 자화자찬과 저주를 한바탕 늘어놓기 시작했다. 자화자찬으로는 자기 양심에 대고 자신의 처사를 변명했고, 저주로는 조지의 불효를 부풀렸다. 영국 천지 어느 아비도, 그토록 사악하게 자신에게 반역한 아들에게 그보다 더 너그럽게 행동할 수는 없었으리라. 그 아이는 자기가 잘못했다는 말 한마디조차 없이 죽었다. 제 불효와 어리석음의 대가를 치르게 하라. 자신으로 말하자면, 오스본 씨는 한번 한 말은 지키는 사람이었다. 그는 그 여자에게는 결코 말을 걸지도, 그 여자를 자기 아들의 아내로 인정하지도 않겠다고 맹세했다. \"그게 당신이 그 여자에게 전할 말이오.\" 그가 욕설로 매듭지었다. \"그리고 그게 내가 죽는 날까지 지킬 바요.\"\n\n그렇다면 그쪽에서는 아무런 희망도 없었다. 미망인은 그 쥐꼬리만 한 푼돈으로 살아가거나, 조스가 보태 줄 수 있는 만큼의 도움에 기대어 살아가는 수밖에 없었다. '그녀에게 말해 본들, 그녀는 마음에 두지도 않겠지.' 도빈은 서글피 생각했다. 그 가엾은 아가씨의 마음은 그 참변 이후로 여기에 조금도 가 있지 않았고, 슬픔의 압박에 멍하니 마비된 채 선이든 악이든 그녀에게는 매한가지로 무심한 것이 되어 버렸기 때문이다.\n\n그렇다, 우정이나 친절조차도 정말이지 그러했다. 그녀는 그 두 가지를 모두 군말 없이 받아들였고, 받아들이고 나서는 다시 슬픔 속으로 가라앉았다.\n\n위의 대화가 오간 뒤로 우리의 가엾은 애밀리아의 삶에 열두 달쯤이 흘렀다고 치자. 그녀는 그 첫머리를 너무도 깊고 가련한 슬픔 속에서 보냈으니, 그 연약하고 다정한 가슴의 몇몇 감정을 지켜보고 또 그려 온 우리로서도, 그 가슴이 피 흘리고 있는 그 잔혹한 비탄 앞에서는 물러서지 않을 수 없다. 가엾게 쓰러진 영혼의 그 불행한 침상 둘레를 소리 없이 걸으라. 그 첫 몇 달의 고통을 견디도록 그녀를 간호하고, 하늘이 그녀에게 위안을 보내 줄 때까지 곁을 결코 떠나지 않았던 그 친절한 이들이 그러했듯, 그녀가 괴로워하는 어두운 방의 문을 가만히 닫으라. 어느 날—거의 두려움마저 깃든 기쁨과 경이의 날—마침내 그 가엾은 미망인 아가씨가 한 아기를 가슴에 끌어안았다. 떠나 버린 조지의 그 눈을 닮은 아기를, 천사처럼 어여쁜 사내아이를. 그 첫 울음소리를 듣는 것이 얼마나 큰 기적이었던지! 그녀가 그 아이를 두고 얼마나 웃고 또 울었던지—그 아기가 가슴에 파고들 때, 사랑과 희망과 기도가 그녀의 가슴속에서 얼마나 다시 깨어났던지. 그녀는 무사했다. 그녀를 돌보며 그 목숨과 정신을 염려하던 의사들은, 둘 중 어느 쪽도 안전하다고 단언하기에 앞서 바로 이 고비를 애타게 기다려 온 터였다. 그녀의 눈이 다시금 그들을 다정하게 바라보며 빛나는 것을 보는 일은, 줄곧 그녀 곁을 지켜 온 이들이 견뎌 낸 그 길고 긴 의혹과 공포의 여러 달과 맞바꿀 만한 가치가 있었다.\n\n우리의 벗 도빈도 그들 가운데 하나였다. 오다우드 부인이 자기 대령에게서 단호한 호출을 받고 환자 곁을 부득이 떠나야 했을 때, 그녀를 영국으로, 그리고 그 어머니의 집으로 데려온 사람도 바로 그였다. 도빈이 아기를 안고 있는 모습과, 그것을 지켜보며 의기양양하게 웃는 애밀리아의 웃음소리는, 익살을 아는 사람이라면 누구에게나 흐뭇한 광경이었을 것이다. 윌리엄은 그 아이의 대부가 되었고, 이 어린 그리스도교도를 위해 잔이며 숟가락이며 미음 그릇이며 산호 노리개를 사들이는 데 온 재간을 다 짜냈다.\n\n그 어머니가 어떻게 아이를 보살피고, 옷을 입히고, 그 아이만 바라보며 살았는지, 어떻게 모든 유모를 물리치고 자기 손 말고는 어떤 손도 아이에게 닿는 것을 좀처럼 허락하지 않았는지, 또 아이의 대부인 도빈 소령에게 베풀 수 있는 가장 큰 호의란 이따금 소령이 아이를 어르도록 허락해 주는 것이라고 여겼는지는, 여기서 굳이 말할 필요가 없다. 이 아이가 그녀의 존재 그 자체였다. 그녀의 삶은 어머니의 애무 한 가지였다. 그녀는 그 가냘프고 아무것도 모르는 생명을 사랑과 경배로 감쌌다. 아기가 그녀의 가슴에서 빨아들이는 것은 바로 그녀의 생명이었다. 밤이면, 그리고 홀로 있을 때면, 그녀는 은밀하고도 격렬한 모성의 황홀경에 빠졌으니, 하느님의 그 놀라운 보살핌이 여성의 본능에 내려 준 것—이성보다 한없이 더 높고도 한없이 더 낮은 기쁨—오직 여인의 가슴만이 아는 그 눈멀고도 아름다운 헌신이었다. 애밀리아의 이런 마음의 움직임을 곰곰이 헤아리고 그 가슴을 지켜보는 것이 윌리엄 도빈의 몫이었다. 그리고 그의 사랑이 그로 하여금 그 가슴을 뒤흔드는 거의 모든 감정을 꿰뚫어 보게 했을지언정, 아아! 그는 거기에 자기가 들어설 자리가 없다는 것을 치명적인 명민함으로 똑똑히 볼 수 있었다. 그리하여 그는 자신의 운명을 알면서도 잠자코 견뎌 냈고, 그것을 견디는 데 만족했다.\n\n애밀리아의 아버지와 어머니는 소령의 속내를 꿰뚫어 보고도 그를 부추기는 데 그리 박하지 않았던 듯하다. 도빈이 날마다 그들의 집을 찾아와 그들과, 혹은 애밀리아와, 혹은 정직한 집주인 클랩 씨와 그 가족과 몇 시간씩 머물렀기 때문이다. 그는 이런저런 핑계로 거의 날마다 모두에게 선물을 가져왔고, 애밀리아가 제법 귀여워하던 집주인의 어린 딸아이에게서 '사탕과자 소령님'이라는 별명을 얻었다. 그를 오스본 부인에게 안내하는 의전관 노릇을 으레 도맡은 것이 바로 이 어린 아이였다. 어느 날 '사탕과자 소령님'의 마차가 풀럼으로 들어와 그가 거기서 내리며, 태어난 지 겨우 여섯 달 남짓밖에 안 된 어린 조지를 위해 나무 말이며 북이며 나팔이며 그 밖의 전쟁놀이 장난감들을 꺼내 오는 것을 보고 그녀는 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 문제의 그 물건들은 아이에게는 한참 이른 것들이었던 것이다.\n\n아이는 잠들어 있었다. \"쉿.\" 애밀리아가, 어쩌면 소령의 장화가 삐걱대는 소리에 짜증이 났는지 그렇게 말하며 손을 내밀었다. 윌리엄이 한 짐 가득한 장난감을 내려놓기 전에는 그 손을 잡을 수 없었으므로 그녀는 미소 지었다. \"아래층으로 내려가렴, 메리야.\" 그가 이내 아이에게 말했다. \"오스본 부인과 할 이야기가 있단다.\" 아이는 다소 놀란 듯 그를 올려다보고는 아기를 침대에 내려놓았다.\n\n\"작별 인사를 하러 왔습니다, 애밀리아.\" 그가 그녀의 가냘프고 자그마한 하얀 손을 가만히 잡으며 말했다.\n\n\"작별이라고요? 어디로 가시는데요?\" 그녀가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"편지는 대리인들에게 보내세요.\" 그가 말했다. \"그들이 전달해 줄 겁니다. 편지해 주실 거지요? 한참 동안 떠나 있을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"조지에 관한 소식을 편지로 전할게요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"고마운 윌리엄, 이 아이한테도 저한테도 어쩌면 그렇게 잘해 주셨는지요. 이 아이 좀 보세요. 꼭 천사 같지 않아요?\"\n\n아이의 작은 분홍빛 손이 기계적으로 그 정직한 군인의 손가락을 감싸 쥐었고, 애밀리아는 환한 모성의 기쁨에 차 그의 얼굴을 올려다보았다. 그 어떤 모진 눈빛도, 희망 없는 친절이 담긴 그 눈길보다 더 그를 아프게 할 수는 없었으리라. 그는 아이와 어머니 위로 몸을 숙였다. 한순간 그는 말을 할 수가 없었다. 그리고 온 힘을 다 짜내고서야 겨우 \"신의 가호가 있기를\"이라는 말을 억지로 내뱉을 수 있었다. \"신의 가호가 있기를.\" 애밀리아가 그렇게 말하며 얼굴을 들어 그에게 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"쉿! 조지를 깨우지 마세요!\" 윌리엄 도빈이 무거운 걸음으로 문을 향해 갈 때, 그녀가 덧붙였다. 그녀는 그가 마차를 몰고 떠나며 내는 바퀴 소리를 듣지 못했다. 그녀는 잠결에 웃고 있는 아이를 바라보고 있었던 것이다.\n\n무릇 우리네 이 허영의 시장에 사는 사람치고, 이따금 자기 지인들의 살림 형편을 헤아려 보지 않을 만큼 무심한 이도 없을 것이고, 옆집 사는 존스나 또 옆집 사는 스미스가 어떻게 연말에 수지를 맞추는지 궁금해하지 않을 만큼 한없이 너그러운 이도 없을 것이라 짐작한다. 일례로 나는 젱킨스 일가를 더없이 존중하면서도(한 철에 두세 번은 그 집에서 만찬을 드는 처지이니), 죽는 날까지 한 가지만은 솔직히 인정하지 않을 수 없다. 척탄병처럼 늠름한 하인들이 딸린 커다란 사륜마차를 타고 공원에 나타나는 젱킨스 일가의 모습은 두고두고 나를 놀라게 하고 어리둥절하게 만들 것이라는 사실 말이다. 그 마차가 그저 세를 낸 것이고 젱킨스네 사람들이 죄다 식사를 곁들인 급료로 부려지는 처지임을 안다 해도, 그 세 사내와 마차를 유지하자면 적어도 일 년에 육백 파운드는 들 것임에 틀림없다. 게다가 그 호화로운 만찬이며, 이튼에 다니는 두 아들이며, 딸들에게 붙인 일류 가정교사와 선생들이며, 가을이면 떠나는 외국 여행이나 이스트본 또는 워딩으로의 나들이며, 건터 가게에서 야식까지 대 가며 해마다 여는 무도회까지 있는 것이다(여담이지만 J.가 베푸는 일류 만찬은 대개 이 건터 가게에서 대는 것임을 나는 익히 알고 있다. 한번은 빈자리를 채우라고 그런 만찬에 초대받은 적이 있었는데, 그 자리에서 단번에 알아챘던 것이다. 이런 진수성찬은, J.의 지인들 가운데 좀 더 변변찮은 축들이 초대장을 받는 흔해 빠진 연회와는 격이 사뭇 다르다는 것을). 그러니 세상에 둘도 없이 선량한 마음을 지닌 사람이라 한들, 젱킨스 일가가 대체 어떻게 살림을 꾸려 가는지 궁금해하지 않을 도리가 어디 있겠는가? 젱킨스가 무엇 하는 사람인가? 우리 모두가 알다시피, 봉인용 끈과 봉랍을 관장하는 관청의 위원으로, 연봉이 천이백 파운드인 사람이다. 그 아내에게 따로 재산이라도 있었던가? 천만에! 그녀는 플린트 양이었으니, 버킹엄셔의 자그마한 시골 지주의 열한 자녀 가운데 하나였다. 친정에서 받는 것이라고는 크리스마스 칠면조 한 마리가 고작이건만, 그 대가로 그녀는 한가한 철이면 제 자매 두셋을 거두어 먹여야 하고, 형제들이 상경할 때면 재워 주고 먹여 주어야 하는 것이다. 젱킨스가 어떻게 수입의 균형을 맞춘단 말인가? 그의 벗이라면 누구나 그러하듯, 나도 묻지 않을 수 없다. 어찌하여 그는 진작 파산자로 추방당하지 않았으며, 어떻게 작년에(모두를 놀라게 하며 그랬듯이) 불로뉴에서 돌아올 수 있었단 말인가?\n\n여기서 \"나\"라는 것은 세상 일반을, 곧 존경하는 독자 여러분 각자가 속한 사교계의 그 그런디 부인을 의인화한 것이니, 독자 모두가 자기 지인들 가운데 무슨 수로 사는지 아무도 모르는 집안 몇몇을 손꼽을 수 있을 것이다. 우리 모두가 후하게 술을 권하는 주인과 잔을 부딪치며 술을 들이켜면서도, 대체 무슨 돈으로 이걸 다 댔을까 의아해한 적이 한두 번이 아니었음을 나는 거의 의심치 않는다.\n\n파리에 머물던 때로부터 서너 해가 지나, 로던 크롤리와 그의 아내가 메이페어의 커즌 거리에 자리한 아주 자그마하면서도 안락한 집에 자리 잡았을 무렵, 그들이 만찬에 청한 그 숱한 벗들 가운데 그들을 두고 앞서와 같은 의문을 품지 않은 이는 거의 없었다. 앞서도 말했듯이 소설가는 모든 것을 알고 있는 법이거니와, 나는 크롤리 부부가 한 푼의 수입도 없이 어떻게 살았는지를 세상에 알려 줄 수 있는 처지에 있다. 그러니 요즘 발간되는 갖가지 정기 간행물에서 글의 일부를 발췌해 싣곤 하는 공공 신문들에 한 가지 간청을 해도 될는지. 부디 다음의 정확한 서술과 계산을 베껴 싣지 말아 달라는 것이다. 발견자인 나로서는(그것도 적잖은 비용을 들여 알아낸 것이니) 마땅히 그 덕을 보아야 할 것 아닌가? 나에게 자식 복이 있어 아이가 하나 있다면, 나는 이렇게 말하리라. 아들아, 이런 부류의 사람과 깊이 캐묻고 끊임없이 어울려 지내다 보면, 한 사람이 한 푼의 수입도 없이 어떻게 안락하게 살아가는지를 배울 수 있을 게다. 허나 이런 직업을 가진 신사들과는 가까이 지내지 않는 편이 상책이요, 그 계산법은 로그표를 쓰듯 남이 해 놓은 것을 받아 쓰는 편이 낫다. 직접 풀어 보려 들다가는 분명 적잖은 대가를 치르게 될 테니 말이다.\n\n그리하여 한 푼의 연 수입도 없이, 두세 해에 걸쳐 크롤리 부부는 파리에서 더없이 행복하고 안락하게 살았다. 그 사연은 아주 간략하게밖에 전할 수 없지만 말이다. 그가 근위대를 그만두고 군적을 판 것도 이 무렵이었다. 다시 그를 만나게 될 즈음에는, 콧수염과 명함에 박힌 대령이라는 직함만이 그의 군인 시절을 말해 주는 유일한 흔적으로 남게 된다.\n\n레베카는 파리에 도착하고 얼마 안 되어 그 수도의 사교계에서 아주 멋스럽고 앞서가는 자리를 차지했으며, 왕정복고로 되돌아온 프랑스 귀족들의 가장 이름난 저택 몇몇에서 환대받았다는 것은 앞서 언급한 바 있다. 파리에 있던 영국의 멋쟁이 신사들 또한 그녀에게 추파를 보냈으니, 이는 그 아내인 숙녀들의 비위를 거스르는 일이었다. 그들은 이 벼락출세한 여자를 견디지 못했던 것이다. 몇 달 동안 포부르 생제르맹의 살롱들에서 그녀의 자리는 굳건했고, 그녀가 융숭한 대접을 받았던 새 궁정의 화려함은 크롤리 부인을 기쁘게 했으며, 어쩌면 다소 도취하게 만들었는지도 모른다. 이 의기양양한 시절 동안 그녀는, 남편의 주된 교제 상대인 사람들, 대개는 정직한 젊은 군인들을 업신여기는 마음을 품었을 법도 했다.\n\n그러나 대령은 궁정의 공작부인들과 귀부인들 틈에서 서글프게 하품만 해 댔다. 에카르테 노름을 하는 노부인들은 오 프랑짜리 동전 한 닢을 두고도 어찌나 시끄럽게 떠들어 대는지, 크롤리 대령으로서는 카드 탁자에 앉을 만한 가치도 없었다. 그들 대화의 재치는 그 언어를 모르는 그로서는 음미할 도리가 없었다. 게다가 아내가 밤마다 공주님들이 빙 둘러앉은 자리에서 무릎을 굽혀 절을 해 댄들 무슨 좋은 일이 생기겠느냐고 그는 따져 물었다. 그는 이내 레베카가 이런 모임에 혼자 드나들도록 내버려 두고는, 제 손으로 고른 마음 맞는 벗들 틈에서 자기만의 소박한 소일거리와 오락을 다시 즐기기 시작했다.\n\n사실인즉, 한 신사가 한 푼의 연 수입도 없이 우아하게 산다고 말할 때, 우리는 그 \"한 푼도 없다\"는 말을 무언가 알 수 없는 것을 가리키는 뜻으로 쓰는 것이다. 다시 말해, 문제의 그 신사가 어떻게 살림 비용을 대는지 우리가 모른다는 뜻일 따름이다. 그런데 우리의 벗인 대령은 온갖 도박에 대단한 소질이 있었다. 늘 그러했듯 카드며 주사위 통이며 당구 큐를 끊임없이 놀려 단련한 터이니, 어쩌다 한 번씩만 이런 물건들을 다루는 사람들보다 훨씬 뛰어난 솜씨를 익혔으리라 짐작하는 것은 당연한 일이다. 당구에서 큐를 능란하게 다루는 것은 연필이나 독일제 플루트나 세검을 다루는 것과 같다. 이런 도구는 무엇 하나 처음부터 통달할 수 있는 것이 아니어서, 타고난 소질에다 거듭된 연마와 끈기가 보태져야만 비로소 어느 것이든 뛰어나게 다룰 수 있는 것이다. 그런데 크롤리는 한낱 빼어난 아마추어에서 출발하여 마침내 당구의 완벽한 명수가 되었다. 위대한 장군이 그러하듯 그의 천재성은 위기가 닥칠수록 솟구쳤으니, 한 판 내내 운이 따라 주지 않아 판돈이 불리하게 걸린 상황에서도 더없이 완숙한 솜씨와 대담함으로 놀라운 한 방을 날려 전세를 뒤집고는, 끝내 모두를 경악케 하며 승자로 마무리하곤 했다. 그의 노름 솜씨를 모르는 사람이라면 누구나 경악했다는 말이다. 그것을 익히 보아 온 이들은 그처럼 느닷없는 임기응변과 눈부시고 압도적인 솜씨를 지닌 사내를 상대로 돈을 거는 데 신중을 기했다.\n\n카드 노름에서도 그는 그에 못지않은 솜씨를 발휘했다. 저녁나절 처음에는 어찌나 무심하게 노름을 하고 또 어찌나 실수를 연발하는지 줄곧 돈을 잃기 일쑤여서, 처음 보는 이들은 흔히 그의 재주를 얕잡아 보기 십상이었다. 그러나 자잘한 손실을 거듭 보다 못해 정신을 차리고 분발하게 되면, 크롤리의 노름 솜씨는 사뭇 달라져서 밤이 다 가기 전에 상대를 보기 좋게 이겨 먹는 것이 거의 틀림없는 일이라고들 했다. 정말이지 그를 이겨 본 적이 있다고 말할 수 있는 사람은 극히 드물었다. 그의 승전이 하도 거듭되니, 시샘하는 자들과 패한 자들이 이따금 그 일을 두고 신랄하게 입을 놀린 것도 무리는 아니었다. 프랑스인들이 단 한 번도 패배한 적 없는 웰링턴 공작을 두고, 그저 놀라우리만치 잇따른 행운의 우연 덕분에 한결같이 승자가 될 수 있었을 뿐이라 말하면서도 워털루에서는 속임수를 써서 마지막 큰 판을 거머쥘 수 있었던 것이라고 인정하듯이, 영국의 군 수뇌부에서도 크롤리 대령이 줄곧 이긴 까닭을 설명하려면 필시 어떤 부정한 짓이 있었을 거라는 수군거림이 일었다.\n\n당시 파리에는 프라스카티며 살롱 같은 도박장이 열려 있었지만, 노름에 대한 광기가 어찌나 널리 퍼졌는지 공공 도박장만으로는 그 보편적인 열기를 다 감당할 수가 없어, 마치 그 열정을 채울 공공의 수단이 아예 없기라도 한 양 개인 저택에서도 노름판이 벌어졌다. 크롤리네 저녁의 그 멋들어진 모임에서도 이 치명적인 오락이 으레 행해졌으니, 이는 마음씨 고운 가냘픈 크롤리 부인을 적잖이 속상하게 하는 일이었다. 그녀는 더없이 깊은 슬픔에 잠겨 남편의 노름벽을 입에 올렸으며, 자기 집을 찾는 이마다 붙잡고 그 일을 한탄했다. 그녀는 젊은 친구들에게 절대로, 절대로 주사위 통에 손대지 말라고 간청했다. 소총대의 젊은 그린이 상당한 액수의 돈을 잃었을 때는, 하인이 그 가엾은 젊은 신사에게 전한 바에 따르면, 레베카가 밤새도록 눈물로 지새우고는 남편 앞에 무릎까지 꿇고서 그 빚을 탕감해 주고 차용증을 태워 버리라고 애원했다는 것이다. 어찌 그럴 수 있었겠는가? 그 자신도 경기병대의 블랙스톤과 하노버 기병대의 푼터 백작에게 그만한 돈을 잃은 터였다. 그린에게 갚을 시간이야 얼마든지 줄 수 있지만, 갚는 것 자체야 물론 갚아야지. 차용증을 태운다는 둥 하는 소리는 애들 장난 같은 소리였다.\n\n다른 장교들, 주로 젊은 축들도(젊은 친구들이 크롤리 부인 둘레로 몰려들었으니) 그녀의 그 치명적인 카드 탁자에서 적든 많든 돈을 떨군 채 울상이 되어 그 집 모임을 나서곤 했다. 그녀의 집은 좋지 못한 평판을 얻기 시작했다. 노련한 자들은 풋내기들에게 그 위험을 일러 주었다. 파리에 주둔한 부대 가운데 하나인 제 모 연대의 오다우드 대령은 그 부대의 스푸니 중위에게 경고를 했다. 파리 카페에서 식사를 하던 그 보병 대령 부부와, 역시 그곳에서 끼니를 들고 있던 크롤리 대령 부부 사이에 요란하고 격렬한 한판 소동이 벌어졌다. 양편의 부인들이 가세했다. 오다우드 부인은 크롤리 부인의 얼굴에 대고 손가락을 튕기며 그 남편을 \"사기 도박꾼이나 다름없는 자\"라고 불렀다. 크롤리 대령은 무공훈장 수훈자인 오다우드 대령에게 결투를 신청했다. 그 분쟁을 전해 들은 총사령관은 크롤리 대령을 불러들였는데, 마침 대령은 \"마커 대위를 쏘았던\" 바로 그 권총을 챙기던 참이었다. 사령관이 그와 어떤 이야기를 나눈 끝에 결투는 끝내 벌어지지 않았다. 레베카가 터프토 장군 앞에 무릎 꿇고 매달리지 않았던들 크롤리는 영국으로 송환되었을 것이며, 그 뒤로 몇 주 동안 그는 민간인을 상대로 하는 노름밖에는 하지 않았다.\n\n그러나 로던의 의심할 바 없는 솜씨와 끊임없는 승전에도 불구하고, 이런저런 사정을 헤아려 본 레베카에게는 그들의 처지가 위태롭기 짝이 없으며, 갚을 사람에게 거의 한 푼도 갚지 않는다 해도 그들의 보잘것없는 밑천이 언젠가는 결국 영으로 쪼그라들고 말 것임이 분명해졌다. 그녀는 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"여보, 노름은 수입을 거드는 데는 좋지만, 그 자체를 수입으로 삼을 수는 없는 거예요. 언젠가 사람들이 노름에 싫증을 내면, 그땐 우리 신세가 어찌 되겠어요?\" 로던도 그 말이 옳다고 수긍했다. 사실 그도, 자기가 베푸는 그 자그마한 야식 모임 따위가 며칠 밤 거듭되고 나면 신사들이 자기와 노름하는 데 싫증을 내고, 레베카의 매력에도 불구하고 그리 신통하게 모여들지 않는다는 것을 눈치챈 터였다.\n\n파리에서의 그들의 삶이 아무리 수월하고 즐거웠다 한들, 그것은 결국 한가로운 희롱이요 사근사근한 시간 죽이기에 지나지 않았다. 레베카는 자기네 나라에서 로던의 출셋길을 밀어주어야겠다고 깨달았다. 본국에서든 식민지에서든 그에게 자리나 직책 하나를 얻어 주어야 했기에, 길이 트이는 대로 곧장 영국으로 나아가기로 마음먹었다. 그 첫걸음으로 그녀는 크롤리에게 근위대 군적을 팔고 반액 봉급 신세가 되게 했다. 터프토 장군의 부관 직무는 그보다 앞서 끝난 터였다. 레베카는 어느 자리에서나 그 장교를 두고 웃음거리로 삼았으니, 그가 파리에 오면서 얹은 가발이며, 허리띠며, 틀니며, 무엇보다 여자 깨나 후린다는 그 허세며, 가까이 가는 여자마다 죄다 자기에게 반했다고 믿는 그 우스꽝스러운 허영심을 비웃었다. 이제 장군이 관심을 옮긴 상대는 군수품 담당관 브렌트 씨의, 눈썹이 짙은 아내 브렌트 부인이었다. 꽃다발이며 음식점 만찬이며 오페라 관람석이며 자질구레한 장신구가 죄다 그리로 갔다. 가엾은 터프토 부인은 예전만큼도 행복하지 못해서, 여전히 긴긴 저녁을 딸들과 단둘이 보내야 했다. 제 장군이 향수 뿌리고 머리 지지고는 극장에서 브렌트 부인의 의자 뒤에 서 있으려 나갔다는 것을 빤히 알면서 말이다. 물론 베키에게는 그를 대신할 흠모자가 여남은 명이나 있었고, 재치로 연적을 갈가리 난도질할 수도 있었다. 그러나 앞서 말했듯이 그녀는 이 한가로운 사교 생활에 점점 싫증이 나고 있었다. 오페라 관람석이며 음식점 만찬에 물려 버린 것이다. 꽃다발은 훗날을 위한 밑천으로 갈무리해 둘 수 있는 것이 아니었고, 자질구레한 장신구며 레이스 손수건이며 새끼 염소 가죽 장갑만으로 먹고살 수도 없는 노릇이었다. 그녀는 쾌락의 덧없음을 절감하며 좀 더 알찬 실속을 갈망했다.\n\n이런 고비에 한 가지 소식이 날아들어 파리에 있는 대령의 숱한 채권자들 사이에 퍼졌으니, 그들에게는 더없이 흡족한 소식이었다. 그가 그 막대한 유산을 물려받으리라 기대하던 부유한 고모 크롤리 양이 죽어 가고 있었던 것이다. 대령은 서둘러 그 머리맡으로 달려가야 했다. 크롤리 부인과 아이는 그가 와서 다시 데려갈 때까지 뒤에 남기로 했다. 그는 칼레로 떠났고, 무사히 그곳에 닿았으니 응당 도버로 건너갔으리라 짐작할 만했다. 그러나 그는 도버 대신 됭케르크행 역마차를 잡아탔고, 거기서 브뤼셀로 갔다. 전부터 마음이 끌리던 곳이었다. 실은 그는 파리보다 런던에 더 많은 빚을 지고 있었던 데다, 그 두 시끄러운 수도보다 조용하고 자그마한 그 벨기에 도시를 더 좋아했던 것이다.\n\n고모는 세상을 떠났다. 크롤리 부인은 자기와 어린 로던을 위해 더없이 짙은 상복을 맞추었다. 대령은 유산을 정리하는 일로 분주했다. 이제 그들은 묵고 있던 호텔의 자그마한 중이층 대신 가장 좋은 층을 쓸 수 있게 되었다. 크롤리 부인은 새 커튼을 두고 주인과 상의를 했고, 양탄자를 두고 다정하게 옥신각신했으며, 청구서만 빼고 모든 것을 마침내 매듭지었다. 그녀는 주인의 마차 한 대를 얻어 타고 떠났다. 프랑스인 하녀를 데리고, 아이는 곁에 앉히고서. 더없이 훌륭한 주인 내외는 대문에서 그녀에게 미소 지으며 작별을 고했다. 그녀가 떠났다는 소식을 듣고 터프토 장군은 노발대발했으며, 브렌트 부인은 그가 노발대발한다 하여 그에게 노발대발했다. 스푸니 중위는 가슴이 찢어지는 듯했고, 호텔 주인은 그 매혹적인 가냘픈 여인과 그 남편이 돌아올 것에 대비해 가장 좋은 방을 준비해 두었다. 그는 그녀가 맡기고 간 트렁크들을 더없이 정성스레 간수했다. 크롤리 부인이 그에게 각별히 잘 보아 달라 당부한 것들이었다. 그러나 얼마 뒤 열어 보니, 그것들은 딱히 값나가는 물건이 아닌 것으로 드러났다.\n\n그러나 벨기에 수도로 남편을 만나러 가기에 앞서, 크롤리 부인은 어린 아들을 프랑스인 하녀에게 맡겨 대륙에 남겨 둔 채 영국으로 한 차례 길을 떠났다.\n\n레베카와 어린 로던의 이별은 어느 쪽에도 그리 큰 아픔을 안기지 않았다. 사실대로 말하자면 그녀는 이 어린 신사가 태어난 뒤로 그를 자주 본 적이 없었다. 프랑스 어머니들의 그 사근사근한 방식을 좇아, 그녀는 아이를 파리 인근 마을의 유모에게 맡겨 길렀던 것이다. 어린 로던은 거기서 나막신을 신은 숱한 젖형제들 틈에 끼여 생애 첫 몇 달을 그런대로 불행하지 않게 보냈다. 아버지는 여러 번 말을 달려 아이를 보러 왔는데, 아이가 발그레하고 꾀죄죄한 얼굴로 우렁차게 소리를 질러 대며 유모인 정원사 아내의 보살핌 아래 진흙 떡 빚기에 신이 난 모습을 보면, 아버지 로던의 부정 어린 가슴은 따뜻하게 달아올랐다.\n\n레베카는 그 아들이자 후계자를 보러 가는 일에 그다지 마음을 쓰지 않았다. 한번은 아이가 그녀의 새 비둘기색 외투를 망가뜨린 적이 있었다. 아이는 엄마의 손길보다 유모의 손길을 더 좋아했으니, 마침내 그 정 많은 유모이자 거의 부모나 다름없던 이의 곁을 떠나게 되었을 때는 몇 시간이고 목 놓아 울었다. 그 애는 내일이면 유모에게 돌아가게 해 주겠다는 어머니의 약속에야 비로소 달래어졌다. 정작 그 유모 역시 이별을 슬퍼했을 법한데도, 아이를 곧 도로 데려다주겠노라는 말을 들은 터라 한동안은 자못 애타게 아이가 돌아오기를 기다렸다.\n\n사실 우리의 벗들은, 훗날 대륙으로 쳐들어가 유럽의 온갖 수도에서 사기 행각을 벌인 그 강인한 영국 모험가 무리의 첫 축에 든다고 해도 좋을 것이다. 1817년에서 18년에 이르는 그 행복한 시절에는 영국인의 부와 명예에 대한 존경심이 대단했다. 내가 듣기로 그때는 영국인들이 아직, 오늘날 그들을 특징짓는 그 끈질긴 흥정 솜씨를 익히지 못한 때였다. 유럽의 큰 도시들은 아직 우리네 악당들의 모험에 문을 열지 않은 터였다. 오늘날에는 프랑스든 이탈리아든 어디를 가든, 우리가 가는 곳마다 떨치는 그 거들먹거리는 행색과 오만한 거동으로 여관 주인을 등쳐 먹고, 곧이듣는 은행가에게 가짜 수표를 떠넘기고, 마차 제조공에게서 마차를, 금세공인에게서 장신구를, 만만한 여행객에게서 카드로 돈을, 심지어 공공 도서관에서 책까지 털어 가는 우리 동포 귀족을 보지 않을 도시가 거의 없게 되었지만, 삼십 년 전만 해도 그저 자가용 마차를 타고 여행하는 영국 귀족 나리이기만 하면, 어디서든 청하는 곳마다 외상이 척척 통했고, 신사들은 속이기는커녕 도리어 속아 넘어가는 쪽이었던 것이다. 크롤리 부부가 파리에 머무는 동안 묵었던 그 호텔 주인이 자기가 입은 손실을 알아챈 것은 그들이 떠나고도 몇 주가 지나서였다. 모자 장수 마라부 부인이 크롤리 부인에게 대 준 물건값 청구서를 들고 거듭 찾아오고 나서야, 또 팔레 루아얄의 불 도르 상점에서 온 디들로 씨가 자기에게서 시계며 팔찌를 사 간 그 매력 넘치는 마님이 돌아왔느냐고 예닐곱 번이나 묻고 나서야 알아챈 것이다. 심지어 마님의 아이에게 젖을 먹여 키운 그 가엾은 정원사 아내조차, 그 토실토실하고 튼실한 어린 로던에게 베푼 인정의 젖값을 처음 여섯 달치 말고는 끝내 한 푼도 받지 못했다는 것이 엄연한 사실이다. 그렇다, 유모조차 돈을 받지 못한 것이다. 크롤리 부부는 그녀에게 진 그 보잘것없는 빚을 떠올리기에는 너무도 황망히 서두르는 처지였던 것이다. 호텔 주인으로 말하면, 그는 타고난 수명이 다하는 날까지 영국이라는 나라에 대고 격렬한 저주를 퍼부었다. 그는 여행객을 만날 때마다 크롤리 로 대령이라는 자를 아느냐고, 아주 재치 있는 자그마한 부인을 데리고 다니던 그 사람을 아느냐고 물었다. \"아, 손님!\" 그는 이렇게 덧붙이곤 했다. \"그자들이 나를 끔찍하게 등쳐 먹었지 뭡니까.\" 그 참변을 이야기할 때 그의 어조는 듣기에 처량했다.\n\n레베카가 런던으로 길을 떠난 목적은, 남편의 숱한 채권자들과 일종의 화해를 매듭짓고, 그들에게 일 파운드당 구 펜스나 일 실링의 배당을 제안함으로써 남편이 제 나라로 돌아올 길을 마련하려는 것이었다. 이 더없이 까다로운 협상을 이끌어 가며 그녀가 밟은 단계를 일일이 좇는 것은 우리가 할 일이 아니다. 그러나 그녀는 자기가 내놓을 수 있는 금액이 남편이 동원할 수 있는 자본의 전부임을 그들이 흡족하도록 보여 주었고, 크롤리 대령이 빚을 청산하지 않은 채 본국에 머무느니 차라리 대륙에서 영영 은둔하는 편을 택하리라는 것을 납득시켰으며, 다른 데서 그에게 돈이 들어올 가능성이라고는 없고 자기가 내놓는 것보다 더 큰 배당을 받을 가망 또한 이 세상에 없음을 입증해 보였다. 그리하여 그녀는 대령의 채권자들이 만장일치로 자기 제안을 받아들이게 만들었고, 현금 천오백 파운드로 그 열 배가 넘는 빚을 사들였다.\n\n크롤리 부인은 이 거래에 변호사를 한 사람도 쓰지 않았다. 그녀가 정확히 짚었듯이 받든지 말든지 하라는 식의 더없이 단순한 사안이었으니, 그녀는 채권자들 자신의 변호사들에게 그 일을 떠맡겼다. 그래서 레드 라이언 광장의 데이비즈 씨를 대리하는 루이스 씨와, 커시터 거리의 마나세 씨를(이 둘이 대령의 주된 채권자였다) 대리하는 모스 씨는 그 일을 처리하는 그녀의 눈부신 솜씨에 찬사를 보내며, 그 어떤 전문가도 그녀를 당해 낼 수 없을 것이라고 단언했다.\n\n레베카는 완벽한 겸양으로 그들의 찬사를 받았다. 그녀는 그 일을 처리하는 동안 묵었던 자그맣고 누추한 셋방으로 셰리주 한 병과 빵 과자를 시켜다가 적군의 변호사들을 대접했으며, 헤어질 때는 더없이 기분 좋게 그들과 악수를 나누고는 곧장 대륙으로 돌아가, 남편과 아들을 다시 만나고 남편에게 그가 빚에서 완전히 풀려났다는 기쁜 소식을 전했다. 아들로 말하자면, 어머니가 없는 동안 그녀의 프랑스인 하녀 주느비에브 양에게 적잖이 방치되어 있었다. 그 젊은 여자가 칼레 수비대의 한 병사에게 정을 붙이는 바람에, 이 군인과 어울리느라 제가 맡은 아이를 까맣게 잊었던 것이다. 어린 로던은 이 무렵 칼레 모래밭에서 하마터면 물에 빠져 죽을 뻔했다. 자리를 비운 주느비에브가 거기에 아이를 놓아둔 채 잃어버렸던 것이다.\n\n그리하여 크롤리 대령 부부는 런던으로 왔다. 그들이 앞서 말한 그런 밑천으로 살아가려는 자들이라면 반드시 지녀야 할 그 솜씨를 진정으로 보여 준 곳이, 바로 메이페어의 커즌 거리에 자리한 그들의 집이었다.\n\n우선, 무엇보다 절실한 문제부터 짚어 두자면, 어떻게 하면 집을 일 년에 한 푼도 안 들이고 얻을 수 있는지를 설명할 의무가 우리에게 있다. 이런 저택들은 가구 없이 빌릴 수도 있는데, 길로 상회나 밴팅 상회와 거래할 신용이 있다면 전적으로 자기 취향에 따라 훌륭하게 꾸미고 장식할 수 있다. 아니면 가구가 딸린 채로 세를 줄 수도 있는데, 대부분의 사람에게는 이쪽이 덜 번거롭고 덜 복잡한 방식이다. 크롤리와 그 아내가 집을 빌리며 택한 쪽이 바로 이 방식이었다.\n\n* 몽테: 가구를 갖추어 꾸민, 정돈된 상태를 뜻하는 프랑스어.\n볼스 씨가 파크 레인에 있는 크롤리 양의 저택과 술 창고를 관리하게 되기 전에, 그 부인에게는 래글스 씨라는 집사가 있었다. 그는 퀸스크롤리의 가문 영지에서 태어났는데, 사실 그곳 정원사의 막내아들이었다. 품행이 단정하고, 풍채와 종아리가 훤칠하며, 점잖은 몸가짐을 갖춘 덕분에 래글스는 칼 닦는 자리에서 마차의 발판으로, 다시 발판에서 집사의 식료품 창고로 출세를 거듭했다. 크롤리 양의 살림을 도맡은 지 몇 해가 지나 후한 급료와 두둑한 부수입, 그리고 저축할 기회를 잔뜩 누린 끝에, 그는 크롤리 양 댁의 전직 요리사와 혼인 동맹을 맺을 참이라고 밝혔다. 그 여자는 다림질 기계를 부리고 인근에서 자그마한 채소 가게를 꾸리며 떳떳하게 생계를 이어 온 사람이었다. 사실인즉, 그 혼례는 몇 년 전에 이미 은밀히 치러진 터였다. 다만 래글스 씨가 혼인했다는 소식이 크롤리 양에게 처음 전해진 것은, 일곱 살과 여덟 살쯤 된 사내아이와 계집아이를 통해서였다. 그 아이들이 줄곧 부엌에 들락거리는 바람에 브리그스 양의 눈에 띄었던 것이다.\n\n이윽고 래글스 씨는 은퇴하여 자그마한 가게와 채소 장사를 손수 도맡아 꾸려 나갔다. 다른 은퇴한 집사들이 술집에서 독한 술을 팔고 다니는 동안, 그는 가게에 우유와 크림, 달걀과 시골에서 기른 돼지고기까지 들여놓고 가장 소박한 시골 산물을 다루는 것으로 만족했다. 그리고 인근 집사들과 두루 좋은 친분을 쌓아 두었고, 그와 래글스 부인이 그들을 맞이하는 아늑한 안쪽 응접실까지 갖추고 있던 터라, 그의 우유와 크림과 달걀은 그 동업자들 다수에게 채택되었고 이익은 해마다 늘어났다. 해가 가고 또 가는 동안 그는 조용히, 그리고 겸손하게 돈을 모았다. 마침내 메이페어 커즌 가 201번지에 있던 그 아늑하고 완벽한 독신자 거처가, 곧 외국으로 떠난 프레더릭 듀스에이스 귀하의 거처였던 그곳이, 일류 제작자들이 만든 호화롭고 격에 맞는 가구와 더불어 경매에 부쳐지자, 들어가서 그 집의 임차권과 가구를 사들인 이가 다름 아닌 찰스 래글스였으니 말이다. 물론 그 돈의 일부는 어느 동업 집사에게서, 그것도 제법 높은 이자를 물고 빌린 것이 사실이었다. 그러나 대부분은 현금으로 치렀고, 래글스 부인이 비단 휘장이 드리워진 조각 마호가니 침대에 누워, 맞은편의 어마어마한 체경과, 자신과 래글스와 온 가족이 다 들어가고도 남을 옷장을 바라보게 되었을 때 느낀 자부심은 결코 작지 않았다.\n\n물론 그들은 그토록 호화로운 거처에 영영 머무를 작정은 아니었다. 래글스가 그 집을 산 것은 다시 세를 놓기 위함이었다. 세입자가 나타나기가 무섭게 그는 또다시 채소 가게로 가라앉았다. 그러나 그 셋집에서 나와 커즌 가로 걸어 들어가, 거기 서서 자기 집을—자기 소유의 집을—창가에 제라늄이 놓이고 청동 조각 문고리가 달린 그 집을 바라보는 것은 그에게 더없이 흐뭇한 일이었다. 이따금 지하 출입구 난간에 기대어 빈둥거리던 하인은 그를 정중히 대했고, 요리사는 그의 가게에서 채소를 사며 그를 \"집주인 어른\"이라 불렀다. 그리고 세입자들이 하는 일이나 저녁 식탁에 올리는 요리 가운데, 마음만 먹으면 래글스가 모를 일이라곤 하나도 없었다.\n\n그는 선량한 사람이었다. 선량하고 행복했다. 그 집이 어찌나 두둑한 연 수입을 안겨 주던지, 그는 아이들을 좋은 학교에 보내기로 마음먹었다. 그리하여 비용은 아랑곳하지 않고 찰스는 슈거케인 로지에 있는 스위시테일 박사의 기숙학교로 보냈고, 어린 머틸다는 클래펌의 로렌티넘 하우스에 있는 페코버 양의 학교로 보냈다.\n\n래글스는 크롤리 가문을 평생 누린 모든 번영의 원천으로 여겨 사랑하고 떠받들었다. 그는 안쪽 가게에 여주인의 실루엣 초상을 걸어 두었고, 그 노처녀가 손수 먹물로 그린 퀸스크롤리 수위실 그림도 간직하고 있었다. 그리고 커즌 가 집의 장식에 그가 보탠 단 하나의 물건은 햄프셔에 있는 퀸스크롤리, 곧 준남작 월폴 크롤리 경의 저택을 그린 판화였다. 그 그림 속에서 경은 백마 여섯 필이 끄는 금박 마차에 탄 모습으로, 백조들과, 후프 스커트를 입은 숙녀들이며 깃발과 가발을 갖춘 악사들을 태운 거룻배들로 뒤덮인 호수 곁을 지나가고 있었다. 정말이지 래글스는 온 세상에 그만한 궁전도, 그만큼 위풍당당한 가문도 없다고 여겼다.\n\n운 좋게도, 로던과 그 아내가 런던으로 돌아왔을 때 커즌 가의 래글스 집은 세를 놓을 참이었다. 대령은 그 집과 그 주인을 아주 잘 알았다. 래글스가 크롤리 가문과 맺은 인연은 한결같이 이어져 왔으니, 크롤리 양이 손님을 맞을 때마다 그가 볼스 씨를 거들었기 때문이다. 그리고 그 노인은 대령에게 집을 세놓았을 뿐 아니라, 대령이 손님을 칠 때마다 집사 노릇까지 해 주었다. 래글스 부인은 아래층 부엌에서 솜씨를 부려, 늙은 크롤리 양조차 흡족해할 만한 만찬을 차려 올렸다. 자, 이것이 바로 크롤리가 집을 한 푼도 안 들이고 얻은 방식이었다. 비록 래글스가 세금과 부과금을 물어야 했고, 동업 집사에게 저당 이자를 치러야 했으며, 자기 생명 보험료도, 아이들 학비도, 자기 가족이—그리고 한동안은 크롤리 대령의 가족까지—먹고 마신 음식 값도 다 부담해야 했지만, 그리고 비록 이 가엾은 사내가 이 거래로 완전히 거덜이 나 아이들은 거리로 내쫓기고 자신은 플리트 감옥으로 끌려가게 되었지만, 일 년에 한 푼도 안 들이고 사는 신사들을 위해서도 누군가는 값을 치러야 하는 법이다. 그렇게 이 불운한 래글스가 크롤리 대령의 모자란 자본을 대신하는 인물로 세워진 것이다.\n\n* 플리트 감옥: 빚을 갚지 못한 채무자를 가두던 런던의 감옥.\n대체 얼마나 많은 가족이 크롤리 같은 식의 대가들 손에 부정과 파멸로 내몰리고 있는 것일까? 얼마나 많은 위대한 귀족이 자기 거래하는 영세 상인의 것을 빼앗고, 가엾은 하인들에게서 비참할 만큼 푼돈을 갈취하며, 고작 몇 실링을 두고 사기를 치는 것일까? 어느 고귀하신 귀족이 대륙으로 떠났다거나, 또 다른 고귀하신 귀족의 집에 차압이 들어갔다거나, 둘 중 하나가 육칠백만 파운드를 빚졌다는 글을 읽으면, 그 몰락마저 영예롭게 보이고, 우리는 그 거대한 파멸 속의 희생자를 존경하기까지 한다. 그러나 하인들 머리에 분 발라 준 값을 받지 못하는 가엾은 이발사를, 마님의 오찬을 위해 장식과 정자를 꾸미느라 거덜 난 가엾은 목수를, 집사의 비호를 받으며 영주께서 황송하게도 주문해 주신 제복을 마련하느라 가진 것을 죄다, 아니 그 이상을 저당 잡힌 그 가엾은 재단사 녀석을 가엾이 여기는 이가 어디 있단 말인가? 그 거대한 저택이 무너져 내릴 때, 이 비참한 녀석들은 그 아래 깔려 아무도 모르게 사라지고 만다. 옛 전설에 이르듯, 사람이 제 발로 악마에게 가기에 앞서, 다른 영혼들을 잔뜩 먼저 그리로 보내는 법이다.\n\n로던과 그 아내는 크롤리 양의 거래 상인이며 납품업자 가운데 자기들을 모시겠다고 나서는 이들에게는 누구에게나 후하게 단골 노릇을 베풀었다. 더러는 기꺼이 응했는데, 특히 가난한 이들이 그랬다. 투팅에서 온 세탁부 여인이 토요일마다 수레를 끌고 오고 주마다 계산서를 들이미는 그 끈질김이란 참으로 볼만한 것이었다. 래글스 씨 본인은 채소를 대야 했다. '전쟁의 운수'라는 술집에서 하인들에게 외상으로 댄 흑맥주 값의 청구서는 맥주의 연대기에서 진귀한 물건감이었다. 하인들도 저마다 급료의 태반을 못 받은 상태였으니, 그렇게 어쩔 수 없이 그 집에 매여 있는 셈이었다. 사실 누구 하나 돈을 받지 못했다. 자물쇠를 따 준 대장장이도, 깨진 유리창을 갈아 끼운 유리장이도, 마차를 빌려준 임대업자도, 그것을 몬 마부도, 양 다릿살을 댄 푸주한도, 그것을 구운 석탄 값도, 그것에 기름을 발라 구운 요리사도, 그것을 먹은 하인들도 마찬가지였다. 그리고 듣자 하니, 일 년에 한 푼도 안 들이고 우아하게 사는 이들의 방식이 이러한 경우가 적지 않다고 한다.\n\n자그마한 동네에서라면 이런 일들이 입길에 오르지 않고 넘어갈 리 없다. 그런 데서는 이웃이 우유를 얼마나 들이는지 우리가 알고, 그 집 저녁상에 올라가는 고깃점이나 닭을 곁눈질로 살핀다. 그러니 아마 커즌 가의 200번지와 202번지는 그 사이에 낀 집에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 알고 있었을 것이다. 하인들이 지하 출입구 난간 너머로 소식을 주고받았을 테니 말이다. 그러나 크롤리와 그 아내, 그 친구들은 200번지도 202번지도 몰랐다. 201번지에 들어서면 진심 어린 환대와 다정한 미소, 훌륭한 만찬, 그리고 주인 내외의 정겨운 악수가 있었으니, 마치 그들이 일 년에 삼사천 파운드를 거느린 명실상부한 주인이기라도 한 듯했다. 사실 그들은 그런 주인이었다—돈으로가 아니라 산물과 노동으로 말이다. 양고기 값을 치르지는 않았어도 양고기는 손에 넣었고, 포도주 값으로 금붙이를 내놓지 않았다 한들 그걸 우리가 어찌 알겠는가? 정직한 로던의 식탁만큼 좋은 클라레가 오르는 식탁도 없었고, 그만큼 흥겹고 말끔하게 차려진 만찬도 없었다. 그의 응접실은 상상할 수 있는 가장 어여쁘고 자그마하며 정갈한 살롱이었다. 더없이 뛰어난 안목으로, 파리에서 가져온 천 가지 자질구레한 장식품으로, 레베카가 손수 꾸민 방이었다. 그리고 그녀가 피아노 앞에 앉아 경쾌한 마음으로 노래를 가락 굴려 부르노라면, 손님은 자신이 가정의 안락이 깃든 자그마한 낙원에 든 듯 여기며, 비록 남편은 다소 우둔할지언정 아내는 매혹적이고 만찬은 세상에서 가장 즐겁다는 데 동의하는 것이었다.\n\n레베카의 재치와 영민함, 그리고 가벼운 입담은 곧 런던의 어떤 부류 사이에서 그녀를 인기인으로 만들었다. 그녀의 문 앞에는 점잖은 사두마차들이 늘어섰고, 거기서 대단히 지체 높은 이들이 내렸다. 그녀의 마차가 공원에서 이름난 멋쟁이들에게 둘러싸인 모습도 볼 수 있었다. 오페라극장 삼층의 자그마한 특별석은 끊임없이 바뀌는 머리들로 붐볐다. 그러나 인정해야 할 것은, 숙녀들만은 그녀를 멀리했고, 우리의 자그마한 모험가에게 그들의 문은 닫혀 있었다는 점이다.\n\n여성 사교계와 그 관습에 관해서라면, 물론 이 글을 쓰는 사람으로서는 전해 들은 것만 이야기할 수 있을 따름이다. 남자란, 숙녀들이 만찬 뒤 위층으로 올라가 무슨 이야기를 나누는지 알 수 없는 것이나 마찬가지로, 그 신비를 꿰뚫어 보거나 이해할 수 없는 법이다. 다만 캐묻고 끈기를 부린 끝에야 이따금 그런 비밀의 단서를 얻게 될 뿐이다. 그와 같은 부지런함으로, 펠멜의 보도를 밟고 이 대도시의 클럽들을 드나드는 이라면 누구나, 제 경험을 통해서든, 함께 당구를 치거나 고기를 나눠 먹는 어떤 지인을 통해서든, 런던의 점잖은 세계에 대해 무언가를 알게 된다. 그리고 어떻게 해서, 무지한 세상의 눈이나 공원의 견습공들 눈에는 그럴듯해 보이는 남자들이—이를테면 앞서 그 처지를 언급한 로던 크롤리 같은 이들, 거기서 가장 악명 높은 멋쟁이들과 어울리는 모습을 견습공들이 목도하는 그런 남자들이—있는 것처럼, 이른바 남자들의 여자라 불릴 만한 숙녀들도 있어, 신사들에게는 죄다 환대받으면서도 그들의 아내들에게는 죄다 외면당하거나 푸대접받는다는 것을 알게 되는 것이다. 파이어브레이스 부인이 그런 부류다. 날마다 하이드 파크에서 이 제국의 가장 위대하고 이름난 멋쟁이들에게 둘러싸인, 아름다운 금빛 곱슬머리를 늘어뜨린 그 부인 말이다. 록우드 부인도 또 한 사람인데, 그녀가 여는 연회는 유행지 신문마다 애써 알려지고, 온갖 대사며 위대한 귀족들이 그녀와 함께 식사하는 모습을 볼 수 있다. 지금 다루는 이야기와 관련만 있다면 더 많은 이를 들 수도 있으리라. 그러나 세상 물정에 어두운 순박한 이들이나, 점잖은 것에 취미를 둔 시골 사람들이 이 숙녀들을 공공장소에서 짐짓 영광스러운 모습으로 바라보거나 멀찍이서 부러워하는 동안, 더 잘 아는 이들은 그들에게 일러 줄 수 있으리라—그 부러움을 사는 숙녀들이 \"사교계\"에 자리 잡을 가망이란, 「모닝 포스트」에서 그들의 행적을 읽는 서머싯셔의 무지한 시골 지주 아내만큼이나 없다는 것을 말이다. 런던 일대에 사는 남자들은 이 무서운 진실들을 알고 있다. 지체와 부를 갖춘 듯 보이는 숱한 숙녀들이 이 \"사교계\"에서 얼마나 무자비하게 배제되는지 듣게 되는 것이다. 이 무리에 끼어들려고 그들이 벌이는 광적인 노력, 굴종하는 비굴함, 견뎌 내는 모욕은, 인간이나 여성을 연구 대상으로 삼는 이들에게 경탄거리이다. 그리고 온갖 난관 속에서 유행을 좇는 일이란, 그런 역사를 엮어 내는 데 필요한 재치와 여가와 영어 지식을 갖춘 어느 대단한 인물에게는 더없이 훌륭한 주제가 될 것이다.\n\n자, 크롤리 부인이 외국에서 알고 지내던 몇 안 되는 여성 지인들은 그녀가 해협 이편으로 건너오자 방문을 마다했을 뿐 아니라, 공공장소에서 마주치면 매몰차게 외면했다. 그 지체 높은 숙녀들이 어찌나 그녀를 깡그리 잊어버리던지 보기에 흥미로울 지경이었고, 레베카에게는 분명 그리 유쾌한 연구거리는 아니었으리라. 베어에이커스 부인은 오페라극장 대기실에서 그녀와 마주치자, 베키의 손길 한 번에 더럽혀지기라도 할 듯 딸들을 제 곁으로 그러모으고는, 한두 걸음 물러서서 그들 앞을 막아서며 자그마한 적수를 쏘아보았다. 베키를 노려보아 무안하게 만들려면, 그 싸늘한 노부인 베어에이커스가 음울한 눈에서 쏘아 보낼 수 있는 것보다 한층 매서운 눈빛이 필요했다. 브뤼셀에서 베키 곁을 스무 번도 넘게 말 달리던 드 라 몰 부인은, 하이드 파크에서 크롤리 부인의 무개 마차와 마주치자 영락없이 눈먼 사람이 되어, 옛 친구를 조금도 알아보지 못했다. 은행가의 아내인 블렌킨솝 부인조차 교회에서 그녀를 외면했다. 베키는 이제 꼬박꼬박 교회에 다녔는데, 그녀가 로던을 곁에 거느린 채, 큼직한 금박 기도서 두 권을 들고 들어와, 그 뒤로 더없이 경건한 체념의 자세로 예배를 치르는 모습은 가히 교화적이었다.\n\n로던은 처음에는 아내에게 가해지는 푸대접을 몹시 예민하게 느껴, 침울하고 사나워지기 일쑤였다. 그는 자기 아내에게 마땅한 경의를 표하지 않는 그 무례한 여자들의 남편이나 형제를 하나하나 결투에 불러내겠다고 떠들어 댔다. 그가 점잖은 처신을 지키게 된 것은 오로지 아내가 가장 단호히 명하고 간청한 덕분이었다. \"당신이 나를 사교계로 쏘아 넣어 줄 수는 없잖아요.\" 그녀가 너그러이 말했다. \"잊지 말아요, 여보, 나는 한낱 가정교사였고, 당신은, 이 가엾고 어리석은 양반은, 빚이며 노름이며 온갖 못된 짓으로 더없이 나쁜 평판을 달고 있다는 걸요. 머지않아 우리도 필요한 만큼 친구를 두루 얻게 될 거예요. 그러니 그동안 당신은 착한 아이처럼 굴면서, 당신 여선생이 시키는 건 뭐든지 고분고분 따라야 해요. 당신 고모님이 거의 전부를 피트와 그 아내에게 남겼다는 소식을 들었을 때, 당신이 얼마나 길길이 날뛰었는지 기억나요? 내가 화를 누르게 하지 않았더라면 당신은 온 파리에 떠들고 다녔을 테고, 그랬으면 지금 어디 있겠어요? 빚 때문에 생트펠라지 감옥에 갇혀 있을 거예요. 런던의 멋진 집에서 온갖 안락을 누리며 자리 잡는 대신에 말이에요. 당신은 어찌나 펄펄 뛰던지 동생을 죽일 기세였지요, 이 못된 카인 같은 양반아. 한데 화를 품고 있어서 좋을 게 뭐가 있었겠어요? 세상의 온갖 분노를 다 끌어모은들 고모님 돈을 우리한테 가져다주지는 못해요. 그러니 저 어리석은 뷰트 식구들처럼 당신 동생네와 원수가 되느니, 친구로 지내는 편이 훨씬 나아요. 당신 아버님이 돌아가시면, 퀸스크롤리는 당신과 내가 겨울을 나기에 아주 쾌적한 집이 될 거예요. 우리가 망한다 해도, 당신은 고기를 썰고 마구간을 맡으면 되고, 나는 제인 부인의 아이들 가정교사가 되면 그만이지요. 망한다고요! 어림없는 소리! 그 전에 내가 당신한테 좋은 자리를 마련해 줄 테니까요. 아니면 피트와 그 어린 아들이 죽어서, 우리가 로던 경과 마님이 될 수도 있고요. 목숨이 붙어 있는 한 희망은 있는 거예요, 여보. 나는 기어이 당신을 어엿한 인물로 만들 작정이라고요. 누가 당신 말들을 팔아 줬어요? 누가 당신 빚을 갚아 줬고요?\" 로던은 이 모든 은혜를 아내에게 입었음을, 그리고 앞날을 그녀의 인도에 맡기겠음을 인정하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n실로, 크롤리 양이 세상을 뜨고, 모든 친척이 그토록 악착같이 다투던 그 돈이 마침내 피트에게 돌아가자, 자기가 헤아리던 이만 파운드 대신 고작 오천 파운드만 받게 된 것을 안 뷰트 크롤리는, 그 낭패에 어찌나 격분했던지 조카에게 사나운 욕설을 퍼붓는 것으로 화풀이를 했다. 그리고 둘 사이에 늘 응어리진 그 다툼은 끝내 왕래의 완전한 단절로 이어졌다. 한편 고작 백 파운드만 받은 로던 크롤리의 처신은 형을 놀라게 하고 형수를 기쁘게 할 만한 것이었으니, 형수는 남편 집안 사람이라면 누구에게나 너그러운 마음을 품으려는 사람이었다. 그는 파리에서 형에게 더없이 솔직하고 사내답고 호의 어린 편지를 써 보냈다. 자신이 멋대로 혼인함으로써 고모의 총애를 잃었음을 잘 알고 있노라고 그는 적었다. 그리고 고모가 자기에게 그토록 끝끝내 매정했다는 데 대한 실망을 감추지는 않으면서도, 그 돈이 그래도 자기네 집안 줄기 안에 남게 되어 기쁘며, 형의 행운을 진심으로 축하한다고 했다. 그는 형수에게 다정한 안부를 전하며, 자기 아내 로던 부인에게도 호의를 베풀어 주기를 바란다고 했다. 그리고 편지는 그 아내가 손수 쓴, 피트에게 보내는 추신으로 끝맺어졌다. 그녀 역시 남편의 축하에 함께하기를 청한다고 했다. 자신이 의지할 곳 없는 고아로서, 그분의 어린 누이들을 가르치던 시절에 크롤리 씨가 베풀어 준 친절을 영원히 기억하겠으며, 그 누이들의 안녕에 지금도 더없이 애틋한 관심을 두고 있다고 했다. 그의 결혼 생활에 온갖 행복이 깃들기를 빌며, 제인 부인께(온 세상이 그 인품을 일러 주더라고 했다) 자기 안부를 전하도록 허락해 주기를 청하면서, 언젠가 자기 어린 아들을 그 백부와 백모께 인사시킬 수 있기를 바라며, 그 아이를 위해 두 분의 호의와 보살핌을 미리 부탁드린다고 했다.\n\n피트 크롤리는 이 서신을 더없이 정중하게 받아들였다. 일찍이 크롤리 양이 로던의 필체로 적힌 레베카의 글들을 받아들이던 것보다 한결 정중하게 말이다. 그리고 제인 부인으로 말하면, 그 편지에 어찌나 마음이 끌렸던지, 남편이 당장 고모의 유산을 똑같이 둘로 나누어 그 절반을 파리의 동생에게 부쳐 줄 것이라 기대할 정도였다.\n\n그러나 부인이 놀랍게도, 피트는 동생에게 삼만 파운드짜리 수표를 끊어 주기를 사양했다. 다만 동생이 영국에 와서 받기를 원할 때면 언제든 손을 내밀겠다는 후한 제안을 했다. 그리고 자신과 제인 부인을 좋게 보아 준 데 대해 크롤리 부인에게 감사하면서, 그 어린 아들을 위해서라면 어떤 기회든 기꺼이 돕겠노라고 정중히 밝혔다.\n\n이렇게 하여 형제 사이에 거의 화해라 할 만한 것이 이루어졌다. 레베카가 런던에 왔을 때 피트와 그 아내는 런던에 없었다. 그녀는 그들이 거기 있는 크롤리 양의 집을 차지했는지 보려고, 파크 레인의 그 낡은 문 앞을 몇 번이나 마차로 지나다녔다. 그러나 새 식구는 모습을 드러내지 않았다. 그녀가 그들의 움직임을 전해 들은 것은 오로지 래글스를 통해서였다. 크롤리 양의 하인들이 점잖은 사례금과 함께 해고되었다는 것, 그리고 피트 씨가 런던에 모습을 보인 것은 단 한 번, 그 집에 며칠 묵으며 거기서 변호사들과 일을 보고 크롤리 양의 프랑스 소설을 죄다 본드 가의 어느 책방에 팔아넘긴 때뿐이었다는 것 말이다. 베키에게는 새 친척이 오기를 애타게 기다릴 만한 나름의 이유가 있었다. '제인 부인이 오면,' 그녀는 생각했다, '그 사람이 런던 사교계에서 내 후원자가 되어 줄 거야. 그리고 여자들로 말하면! 흥! 남자들이 나를 보고 싶어 한다는 걸 알게 되면 여자들이 알아서 나를 청할 테지.'\n\n이런 처지의 숙녀에게 마차나 꽃다발 못지않게 긴요한 물건이 바로 말동무다. 나는 동정 없이는 살아가지 못하는 그 가녀린 존재들이, 자기와 거의 떨어질 수 없는 지극히 평범한 동성 친구 하나를 들이는 그 방식에 늘 감탄해 왔다. 빛바랜 가운을 걸친 그 빠질 수 없는 여자가 오페라 특별석에서 사랑하는 벗 뒤에 앉아 있거나 사륜마차의 뒷자리를 차지하고 있는 광경은, 내게는 언제나 건전하고 교훈적인 것이다. 이집트의 미식가들이 잔치 때 끌어다 놓던 해골만큼이나 유쾌한 환기물이요, 허영의 시장을 향한 야릇하고 냉소적인 기념물인 것이다. 무어라고? 저 닳고 닳은, 뻔뻔하고, 아름답고, 양심도 없고, 인정머리도 없는 파이어브레이스 부인, 그 수치 때문에 아비가 죽었다는 그 여자조차도, 또 영국의 어느 남자든 넘을 만한 울타리라면 다 넘어 말을 달리고 공원에서 회색 말들을 손수 모는, 그 어미가 아직도 배스에서 행상 좌판을 보고 있는, 그 사랑스럽고 대담한 맨트랩 부인조차도, 그토록 대담하여 무엇이든 맞설 수 있을 성싶은 그런 여자들조차도, 여자 친구 하나 없이는 감히 세상과 맞서지 못하는 것이다. 그들에게는 매달릴 누군가가 있어야 하는 법이다, 그 정 많은 존재들에게는! 그러니 어느 공공장소에서든, 물들인 비단옷을 걸친 초라한 말동무 하나가 그들 바로 뒤 그늘 어딘가에 앉아 있지 않은 모습을 보기란 좀처럼 어려울 것이다.\n\n\"로던.\" 어느 날 밤, 신사들 한 무리가 그녀의 탁탁 타오르는 응접실 난롯가에 둘러앉아 있을 때 베키가 아주 늦은 시각에 입을 열었다(남자들은 밤을 마무리하러 그녀의 집으로 모여들었고, 그녀는 그들에게 얼음과 커피를 내놓았는데 런던에서 으뜸가는 것이었다). \"나 양치기 개가 한 마리 있어야겠어요.\"\n\n\"뭐가 있어야겠다고?\" 로던이 에카르테 판에서 고개를 들며 말했다.\n\n\"양치기 개라뇨!\" 젊은 사우스다운 경이 말했다. \"크롤리 부인, 별 희한한 생각을 다 하시는군요! 차라리 덴마크 개를 들이시지 그래요? 맹세코 기린만 한 놈을 하나 알고 있는데, 그놈이면 부인의 사륜마차도 끌 만하다오. 아니면 페르시아 그레이하운드는 어떻소, 응?(괜찮으시다면 패를 집겠소.) 아니면 스테인 경의 코담뱃갑에 쏙 들어갈 만한 자그마한 퍼그는요? 베이스워터에 사는 어떤 사람이 한 마리 길렀는데 그놈 코가 어찌나—내가 킹을 내고 칩니다—그 코에 모자를 걸어 둘 수 있을 정도라오.\"\n\n\"내가 그 패를 먹지.\" 로던이 점잖게 말했다. 그는 으레 제 노름에만 골몰할 뿐, 말이나 내기에 관한 게 아니면 대화에 좀처럼 끼어들지 않았다.\n\n\"대체 양치기 개로 뭘 하시려는 거요?\" 활달하고 자그마한 사우스다운이 말을 이었다.\n\n\"내 말은, 도덕적인 양치기 개요.\" 베키가 웃으며 스테인 경을 올려다보고 말했다.\n\n\"그건 또 뭐란 말이오?\" 경이 말했다.\n\n\"나한테서 늑대들을 쫓아 줄 개죠.\" 레베카가 말을 이었다. \"말동무 말이에요.\"\n\n\"가엾고도 천진한 어린 양 같으니, 정말 한 마리 들이셔야겠소.\" 후작이 말했다. 그러고는 턱을 쑥 내밀고 흉하게 이를 드러내며 웃기 시작했고, 자그마한 두 눈은 레베카 쪽으로 음흉하게 곁눈질을 보냈다.\n\n위대하신 스테인 경은 난롯가에 서서 커피를 홀짝이고 있었다. 불은 기분 좋게 탁탁거리며 활활 타올랐다. 벽난로 선반 둘레에는, 금박이며 청동이며 도자기로 된 갖가지 기묘한 촛대에 꽂힌 촛불 스무 개가 반짝였다. 그 불빛은 화사한 꽃무늬가 덮인 소파에 앉은 레베카의 자태를 더없이 곱게 비추었다. 그녀는 장미처럼 싱그러워 보이는 분홍빛 드레스를 입었고, 눈부시게 흰 두 팔과 어깨는 얇고 어렴풋한 스카프로 반쯤 가렸는데 그 너머로 살결이 반짝였다. 머리칼은 목덜미를 따라 곱슬곱슬 늘어졌고, 자그마한 한쪽 발이 싱그럽게 빳빳한 비단 주름 사이로 살짝 내비쳤다. 세상에서 가장 고운 비단 양말에, 가장 고운 자그마한 샌들을 신은, 가장 고운 자그마한 발이었다.\n\n촛불은 스테인 경의 번들거리는 대머리를 비추었는데, 그 둘레로는 붉은 머리칼이 술처럼 둘려 있었다. 그는 짙고 덥수룩한 눈썹에, 천 갈래 주름으로 둘러싸인, 핏발 서고 반짝이는 작은 눈을 지녔다. 턱은 아래로 튀어나와 있어서, 웃을 때면 하얀 토끼 같은 앞니 두 개가 비죽 나와 그 웃음 한가운데서 사납게 번득였다. 그는 왕족들과 만찬을 들고 온 터라 가터 훈장과 그 띠를 두르고 있었다. 경은 키가 작고, 가슴팍이 떡 벌어졌으며, 다리는 휘었으나, 제 발과 발목이 곱다는 것을 자랑스러워하여 늘 가터를 두른 무릎을 어루만지곤 했다.\n\n\"그래, 양치기로는 부족하다는 거요.\" 그가 말했다. \"제 어린 양을 지키기에 말이오?\"\n\n\"양치기가 카드놀이며 클럽 출입을 너무 좋아해서 탈이지요.\" 베키가 웃으며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"저런, 어찌 그리 방탕한 코리돈이람!\" 경이 말했다. \"피리나 불 입이로군!\"\n\n\"두 점 받고 석 점 부르겠소.\" 카드 탁자에서 로던이 끼어들었다.\n\n\"멜리보이우스 좀 보게나.\" 고귀하신 후작이 으르렁대듯 말했다. \"저 사람도 목가적인 일에 몰두하고 있군. 사우스다운의 털을 깎는 중이지. 어찌나 천진한 양인지, 응? 빌어먹을, 어찌나 새하얀 양털인지!\"\n\n* 코리돈, 멜리보이우스: 베르길리우스 목가시에 나오는 양치기들의 이름. 사우스다운은 양 품종 이름이기도 하여 말장난이 된다.\n레베카의 두 눈이 경멸 어린 익살의 광채를 쏘아 보냈다. \"각하.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"각하야말로 그 기사단의 기사이시지요.\" 과연 그는 목에 그 목걸이를 두르고 있었으니, 복위한 에스파냐 왕가가 내린 선물이었다.\n\n스테인 경은 젊은 시절 노름판에서의 대담함과 승운으로 악명이 자자했다. 그는 폭스 씨와 더불어 꼬박 이틀 밤낮을 주사위 노름에 매달려 앉아 있은 적도 있었다. 이 나라에서 가장 존엄한 인물들에게서 돈을 따냈고, 소문에 따르면 자신의 후작 작위마저 노름판에서 따냈다고 했다. 그러나 그는 그 옛 방종을 들먹이는 것을 좋아하지 않았다. 레베카는 그 묵직한 이마 위로 노여운 빛이 서리는 것을 보았다.\n* 프르데느: 젊은 시절의 방탕한 짓, 무모한 장난을 뜻하는 프랑스어.\n\n그녀는 소파에서 일어나 다가가서는, 가볍게 무릎을 굽혀 절하며 그의 손에서 커피잔을 받아 들었다. \"그래요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"나는 파수견을 한 마리 들여야겠어요. 하지만 그 개가 각하께 짖는 일은 없을 거예요.\" 그러고는 옆 응접실로 가 피아노 앞에 앉더니, 어찌나 매혹적이고 가슴 떨리는 목소리로 자그마한 프랑스 노래들을 부르기 시작하던지, 마음이 누그러진 귀족은 부랴부랴 그녀를 따라 그 방으로 들어갔고, 이내 그녀 위로 고개를 끄덕이며 박자에 맞춰 몸을 굽실대는 모습을 볼 수 있었다.\n\n그러는 동안 로던과 그 친구는 진력이 날 때까지 에카르테를 쳤다. 대령이 땄다. 그러나 그가 아무리 많이, 아무리 자주 딴다 한들, 일주일에도 몇 번씩 되풀이되는 이런 밤들이란—아내가 온갖 화제와 온갖 찬사를 독차지하고, 자기는 그 무리 바깥에 말없이 앉아 그 안에서 오가는 농담이며 빗댄 말이며 알 듯 모를 듯한 언사를 한마디도 알아듣지 못한 채 있어야 하는 이런 밤들이란—전직 용기병에게는 자못 따분한 노릇이었으리라.\n\n\"크롤리 부인의 바깥양반은 안녕하신가?\" 스테인 경은 그와 마주칠 때면 인사 삼아 이렇게 말하곤 했다. 그리고 사실 그것이 이제 그의 인생살이가 된 셈이었다. 그는 더 이상 크롤리 대령이 아니었다. 그는 크롤리 부인의 바깥양반이었다.\n\n어린 로던에 관해 여태 한마디도 없었다면, 그건 그 아이가 위층 어느 다락방엔가 숨겨져 있거나, 동무가 그리워 아래층 부엌으로 기어 내려가 있기 때문이다. 어머니는 그 아이에게 좀처럼 눈길을 주는 법이 없었다. 아이는 프랑스인 유모가 크롤리 씨 집에 머무는 동안에는 그 유모와 더불어 나날을 보냈고, 그 프랑스 여자가 떠나자, 밤의 외로움 속에서 울부짖던 그 어린것을 어느 하녀가 가엾이 여겨, 그 호젓한 육아방에서 데리고 나와 바로 곁 다락방 제 침대에 뉘고 달래 주었다.\n\n이 울음소리가 머리 위에서 들려왔을 때, 레베카와 스테인 경, 그리고 한두 사람이 더 오페라가 끝난 뒤 응접실에서 차를 들고 있었다. \"내 천사가 유모를 찾으며 우는 거예요.\" 그녀가 말했다. 그녀는 아이를 보러 가겠다고 선뜻 나서지 않았다. \"아이를 찾아간다고 공연히 마음 쓸 것 없소.\" 스테인 경이 냉소조로 말했다. \"쳇!\" 다른 쪽이 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 대꾸했다. \"울다가 제풀에 잠들 거예요.\" 그러고는 그들은 오페라 이야기로 빠져들었다.\n\n그러나 로던은 슬그머니 자리를 빠져나가 제 아들이자 후사를 살피러 갔다. 그리고 마음씨 고운 돌리가 아이를 달래고 있는 것을 보고서야 손님들 곁으로 돌아왔다. 대령의 화장방이 그 위층에 있었다. 그는 거기서 남몰래 아이를 보곤 했다. 그가 면도하는 아침마다 둘은 만남을 가졌다. 어린 로던은 아버지 곁 상자 위에 앉아 그 면도하는 모습을 끝없는 즐거움으로 지켜보았다. 그 아이와 아비는 둘도 없는 동무였다. 아버지는 후식 중에서 단것을 가져다가 낡은 견장 상자 속에 감춰 두곤 했고, 아이는 그것을 찾으러 가서 보물을 발견하고는 기뻐 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 웃되, 너무 큰 소리로는 웃지 않았다. 엄마가 아래층에서 자고 있으니 깨워서는 안 되었던 것이다. 엄마는 아주 늦어서야 잠자리에 들었고 정오가 지나서야 겨우 일어나는 일이 잦았다.\n\n로던은 아이에게 그림책을 잔뜩 사 주고 육아방을 장난감으로 가득 채웠다. 방의 벽은 아버지가 손수 풀로 붙인, 그가 현금으로 사들인 그림들로 뒤덮여 있었다. 로던 부인과 더불어 공원에 나가지 않는 비번일 때면, 그는 이 위층에 들어앉아 아이와 더불어 몇 시간씩 보냈다. 아이는 그의 가슴팍에 올라타고, 그의 커다란 콧수염을 마치 마차 고삐라도 되는 양 잡아당겼으며, 그와 함께 지칠 줄 모르고 뛰놀며 하루를 보냈다. 천장이 낮은 방이었던 터라, 아이가 채 다섯 살도 되기 전 어느 날, 아버지가 아이를 두 팔로 거칠게 위로 던져 올리다가 그 가엾은 어린것의 머리통을 천장에 어찌나 세게 부딪쳤던지, 그 변고에 어찌나 질겁했던지 하마터면 아이를 떨어뜨릴 뻔했다.\n\n어린 로던은 어마어마하게 울어 댈 양으로 얼굴을 잔뜩 찌푸렸다—사실 그만큼 세게 부딪쳤으니 그렇게 울어 댈 만도 했다. 그러나 막 울음을 터뜨리려는 찰나, 아버지가 가로막았다.\n\n\"제발, 로디, 엄마 깨우지 마라.\" 그가 외쳤다. 그러자 아이는 아주 모질고도 가련한 눈빛으로 아버지를 바라보더니, 입술을 깨물고 두 주먹을 꼭 쥔 채, 조금도 울지 않았다. 로던은 그 이야기를 클럽에서도, 장교 식당에서도, 온 동네 누구에게나 떠벌렸다. \"맹세코, 여보게.\" 그는 세상 사람들에게 두루 설명했다. \"우리 애가 어찌나 배짱 좋은 녀석인지—어찌나 대단한 놈인지! 맹세코, 그 머리통을 거의 천장에 처박을 뻔했는데도, 글쎄 제 어미 깨울까 봐 울지를 않더라니까.\"\n\n이따금—일주일에 한두 번쯤—그 부인이 아이가 사는 위층을 찾아왔다. 그녀는 마치 「유행 백화점」에서 막 살아 나온 인물처럼, 더없이 아름다운 새 옷에 자그마한 장갑과 부츠 차림으로 환하게 미소 지으며 나타났다. 놀라운 스카프와 레이스, 보석들이 그녀의 몸에 반짝였다. 늘 새 보닛을 쓰고 있었고, 거기에는 꽃이 끊임없이 피어 있거나, 아니면 동백처럼 보드랍고 새하얀, 으리으리하게 굽이치는 타조 깃털이 꽂혀 있었다. 그녀는 저녁밥을 먹다가, 혹은 군인 그림을 색칠하다가 고개를 든 어린 아들에게 짐짓 생색내듯 두세 번 고개를 끄덕여 보였다. 그녀가 방을 나가고 나면 장미 향이나 그 밖의 마법 같은 향내가 육아방에 감돌았다. 아이의 눈에 그녀는 이 세상 것이 아닌 존재였으니, 아버지보다도—온 세상 누구보다도 위에 있어, 멀찍이서 우러르고 흠모해야 할 존재였다. 그 부인과 마차를 타고 나가는 것은 엄숙한 의식이었다. 아이는 뒷자리에 꼿꼿이 앉아 감히 입도 떼지 못한 채, 맞은편의 아름답게 차려입은 공주님을 두 눈 가득 응시했다. 으리으리하게 껑충대는 말을 탄 신사들이 다가와 미소 지으며 그녀와 이야기를 나누었다. 그 신사들 모두에게 그녀의 눈은 어찌나 환히 빛나던지! 그들이 지나갈 때면 그녀의 손은 우아하게 떨리듯 나풀거렸다. 아이는 그녀와 함께 외출할 때면 새 빨간 옷을 입었다. 집에 있을 때는 낡은 갈색 무명옷으로 족했다. 이따금 그녀가 외출하고 없을 때, 하녀 돌리가 침대를 정리하노라면, 아이는 어머니 방으로 들어가 보았다. 아이에게 그곳은 요정의 거처와도 같았다—찬란함과 환희가 깃든 신비로운 방이었다. 그 옷장에는 그 놀라운 옷들이—분홍빛, 푸른빛, 갖가지 빛깔의 옷들이 걸려 있었다. 은 걸쇠가 달린 보석함도, 화장대 위에서 백 개의 반지로 온통 번쩍이는 그 신비로운 청동 손 모양 장식도 있었다. 그리고 그 예술의 기적이라 할 체경이 있어, 거기에 어리둥절해하는 제 머리통과, 침대 베개를 두드려 부풀리는 돌리의 모습(괴상하게 일그러진 채, 마치 천장 위에 있기라도 한 듯이)이 비치는 것을 아이는 겨우 들여다볼 수 있었다. 오, 가엾고 외롭고 앞 못 보는 어린것이여! 어린아이들의 입과 마음에서 어머니란 곧 하느님을 일컫는 이름이거늘, 여기 돌덩이를 떠받드는 한 아이가 있었구나!\n\n그런데 로던 크롤리는, 비록 대령이 불한당이긴 했어도, 마음속에 사내다운 정의 기미를 얼마간 지니고 있어, 아이 하나와 여자 하나만은 여전히 사랑할 줄 알았다. 그는 그 무렵 어린 로던에게 남모를 깊은 애정을 품고 있었는데, 비록 아내에게 그 이야기를 떠벌리지는 않았지만 레베카의 눈을 피하지는 못했다. 그래도 그녀는 언짢아하지 않았다. 그러기에는 천성이 너무 무던했다. 그 일은 그저 그에 대한 그녀의 경멸을 더할 뿐이었다. 그는 어쩐지 이 아비로서의 다정함이 부끄러워, 아내에게는 그것을 숨긴 채, 아이와 단둘이 있을 때만 마음껏 누렸다.\n\n그는 아침이면 아이를 데리고 나가 함께 마구간으로, 또 공원으로 가곤 했다. 사람 좋기로는 으뜸가는, 제 머리에 쓴 모자라도 선뜻 선물로 줄 위인이요, 평생의 주된 일이라곤 나중에 남에게 줄 자질구레한 물건들을 사들이는 것뿐인 자그마한 사우스다운 경은, 그 어린것에게 조랑말 한 마리를 사 주었는데, 주는 이의 말로는 큰 쥐만 한 크기밖에 안 되는 놈이었다. 어린 로던의 위대한 아버지는 이 자그마한 검은 셰틀랜드산 난쟁이 말에 아이를 기꺼이 태우고는, 공원에서 그 곁을 나란히 걸었다. 옛 주둔지를, 그리고 나이츠브리지의 옛 근위대 동료들을 보는 것이 그에게는 흐뭇했다. 그는 독신 시절을 얼마간 아쉬움 비슷한 마음으로 떠올리기 시작하던 참이었다. 옛 기병들은 자기네 옛 장교를 알아보고 그 어린 대령을 어르며 반가워했다. 크롤리 대령은 장교 식당에서, 또 동료 장교들과 더불어 식사하는 것이 무척 즐거웠다. \"젠장, 난 그 사람한테 어울릴 만큼 영리하질 못해—나도 알아. 그 사람은 내가 없어도 아쉬워하지 않을걸.\" 그는 이렇게 말하곤 했다. 그리고 그 말이 옳아서, 아내는 그가 없어도 아쉬워하지 않았다.\n\n레베카는 남편을 아꼈다. 그녀는 그에게 늘 더없이 무던하고 다정했다. 그에 대한 경멸조차 좀처럼 드러내지 않았다. 어쩌면 그가 바보였기에 그를 더 좋아했는지도 모른다. 그는 그녀의 우두머리 하인이요 집사장이었다. 그는 그녀의 심부름을 다녔고, 그녀의 분부에 두말없이 따랐으며, 군소리 없이 그녀와 더불어 마차를 몰아 승마장을 돌았고, 오페라 특별석까지 그녀를 데려다준 뒤 공연이 진행되는 동안에는 클럽에서 마음을 달래다가, 때가 되면 어김없이 그녀를 데리러 돌아왔다. 그는 아내가 아이를 조금만 더 사랑해 주기를 바랐지만, 그것마저도 체념하고 받아들였다. \"젠장, 알다시피 그 사람은 어찌나 영리한지.\" 그가 말했다. \"한데 나는 글재주도 그런 것도 없고 말이야, 알잖나.\" 앞서 말했듯, 카드와 당구에서 이기는 데는 대단한 지혜가 필요치 않거니와, 로던은 그 밖의 어떤 솜씨도 내세우는 법이 없었다.\n\n말동무가 들어오자 그의 집안일은 무척 가벼워졌다. 아내는 그가 밖에서 식사하도록 부추겼고, 오페라에서도 그의 임무를 면제해 주곤 했다. \"오늘 밤엔 집에 남아 멍청하게 굴 것 없어요, 여보.\" 그녀는 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"당신은 따분하기만 할 사람들이 올 거예요. 안 부르고 싶지만, 다 당신 잘되라고 그러는 거 알잖아요. 게다가 이제 나한테 양치기 개가 생겼으니, 혼자 있어도 무서울 게 없고요.\"\n\n'양치기 개라니—말동무라니! 베키 샤프한테 말동무가 다 있다니! 이거 참 우습지 않아?' 크롤리 부인은 속으로 생각했다. 그 발상이 그녀의 유머 감각을 무던히도 간질였다.\n\n어느 일요일 아침, 로던 크롤리와 그 어린 아들, 그리고 조랑말이 여느 때처럼 공원을 거닐다가, 대령의 옛 지인인 연대의 클링크 상병 곁을 지나게 되었다. 그는 한 친구, 곧 어린 로던과 비슷한 또래의 사내아이를 품에 안은 어느 노신사와 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 그 다른 어린것은 상병이 단 워털루 훈장을 움켜쥐고는 신이 나서 들여다보던 참이었다.\n\n\"안녕하십니까, 나리.\" 클링크가 대령의 \"잘 지냈나, 클링크?\"라는 인사에 답하여 말했다. \"이 어린 신사분이 우리 어린 대령님과 엇비슷한 또래입죠, 나리.\" 상병이 말을 이었다.\n\n\"이 애 아버지도 워털루에 참전한 사람이었지요.\" 아이를 안은 노신사가 말했다. \"그렇지, 조지?\"\n\n\"네.\" 조지가 말했다. 그 아이와 조랑말 위의 어린것은 서로를 있는 힘껏 바라보고 있었다—아이들이 으레 그러듯 진지하게 서로를 뜯어보면서.\n\n\"보병 연대였습죠.\" 클링크가 으스대는 투로 말했다.\n\n\"제 사위는 제××연대의 대위였소이다.\" 노신사가 다소 거드름을 피우며 말했다. \"조지 오스본 대위라는 사람이지요, 나리—혹 아셨을지도 모르겠소. 그 사람은 영웅답게 죽었소이다, 나리, 코르시카의 폭군에 맞서 싸우다 말이오.\" 크롤리 대령은 얼굴이 새빨개졌다. \"그 사람이라면 아주 잘 알았지요, 어르신.\" 그가 말했다. \"그리고 그 사람 아내, 그 사랑스럽고 자그마한 부인도요, 어르신—그분은 어떻게 지내십니까?\"\n\n\"그 애가 제 딸이올시다, 나리.\" 노신사가 아이를 내려놓고는 자못 엄숙하게 명함 한 장을 꺼내 대령에게 건네며 말했다. 거기에는 이렇게 적혀 있었다—\n\n\"세들리. 흑다이아몬드 무재석탄 협회 단독 대리인. 템스 가 벙커스 부두 및 풀럼 로드 웨스트 애나마리아 코티지.\"\n\n어린 조지가 다가가 셰틀랜드 조랑말을 들여다보았다.\n\n\"한번 타 볼래?\" 안장에 앉은 어린 로던이 말했다.\n\n\"응.\" 조지가 말했다. 얼마간 흥미롭게 그 아이를 바라보던 대령은 아이를 안아 올려 어린 로던 뒤에 태워 주었다.\n\n\"이 애를 꽉 붙들어, 조지.\" 그가 말했다—\"우리 어린 아들 허리를 잡으렴—얘 이름은 로던이란다.\" 그러자 두 아이가 다 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"이 여름날, 이보다 더 어여쁜 한 쌍은 못 보실 겁니다, 나리.\" 사람 좋은 상병이 말했다. 그리고 대령과 상병, 그리고 우산을 든 늙은 세들리 씨는 두 아이 곁을 나란히 걸어갔다.\n\n어린 조지 오즈번이 나이츠브리지에서 풀럼 쪽으로 말을 달려갔다고 치자. 그러면 그 마을에 잠시 멈춰, 우리가 거기 남겨 두고 온 몇몇 벗들의 소식을 물어보아야겠다. 워털루의 폭풍이 지나간 뒤 애밀리아 부인은 어떻게 지내고 있을까? 살아서 잘 지내고 있을까? 늘 그녀의 집 근처를 어슬렁거리던 그 마차의 주인, 도빈 소령은 어찌 되었을까? 그리고 보글리 월라의 징세관에 대한 소식은 없을까? 후자에 관한 사실은 간단히 이러하다.\n\n우리의 존경스러운 뚱뚱한 벗 조지프 세들리는 브뤼셀에서 빠져나온 지 얼마 지나지 않아 인도로 돌아갔다. 휴가가 끝났던 탓인지, 아니면 워털루에서 도망친 자신을 본 목격자들과 마주치기가 두려웠던 탓인지 모를 일이었다. 어쨌거나 그는 나폴레옹이 세인트헬레나에 거처를 정한 직후 벵골의 직무로 돌아갔는데, 그곳에서 조스는 그 폐위된 황제를 보았다. 세들리 씨가 배 위에서 떠드는 소리를 들었더라면, 그와 코르시카 사내가 만난 것이 이번이 처음이 아니며, 이 민간인이 몽생장에서 그 프랑스 장군에게 맞서기라도 한 줄로 알았을 것이다. 그는 그 유명한 전투들에 대해 천 가지나 되는 일화를 늘어놓았고, 모든 연대의 위치며 각 연대가 입은 손실까지 다 꿰고 있었다. 자신이 그 승리들에 관여했다는 것을, 군대와 함께 있으면서 웰링턴 공작의 급보를 전달했다는 것을 그는 부정하지 않았다. 그리고 워털루의 그날, 생각할 수 있는 모든 순간에 공작이 무엇을 하고 무슨 말을 했는지, 각하의 심정과 행적을 어찌나 정확히 아는 듯 묘사하는지, 그가 그날 종일 그 정복자의 곁에 있었던 게 틀림없어 보일 정도였다. 비록 비전투원인지라 그 전투에 관한 공식 문서에 그의 이름은 언급되지 않았지만 말이다. 어쩌면 그는 정말로 스스로를 부추겨 자신이 군대와 함께 싸웠다고 믿게 되었는지도 모른다. 분명한 것은, 그가 한동안 캘커타에서 굉장한 파란을 일으켰고, 그 후 벵골에 머무는 내내 워털루 세들리라고 불렸다는 사실이다.\n\n조스가 그 불운한 말들을 사들이면서 끊어 준 어음은 그와 그의 대리인들이 군말 없이 지불했다. 그가 그 거래를 입에 올렸다는 말은 한 번도 들린 적이 없었고, 그 말들이 어찌 되었는지, 그가 그것들을 어떻게 처분했는지, 또 그의 벨기에인 하인 이지도르가 어찌 되었는지 확실히 아는 이는 아무도 없었다. 그 이지도르라는 자는 1815년 가을 어느 무렵 발랑시엔에서, 조스가 타던 것과 아주 흡사한 회색 말 한 마리를 팔아 치웠던 것이다.\n\n조스의 런던 대리인들은 매년 백이십 파운드를 풀럼의 부모에게 지불하라는 지시를 받았다. 그것이 이 노부부의 주된 생계 수단이었다. 세들리 씨가 파산 이후로 인생에서 벌인 투기들은 그 영락한 노신사의 무너진 재산을 결코 되돌려 주지 못했기 때문이다. 그는 포도주 상인, 석탄 상인, 복권 위탁 판매원 따위가 되어 보려고 애썼다. 새 장사를 시작할 때마다 친구들에게 안내장을 돌렸고, 문에 달 새 놋쇠 명패를 주문했으며, 아직도 한밑천 잡겠노라고 거드름을 피우며 떠들었다. 그러나 행운은 이 쇠약하고 타격을 입은 노인에게 다시는 돌아오지 않았다. 친구들은 하나둘 떨어져 나갔고, 그에게서 비싼 석탄과 형편없는 포도주를 사는 데 진력이 났다. 그리고 아침이면 그가 비틀거리며 시내로 나설 때 그가 아직도 거기서 무슨 사업이라도 하고 있는 줄로 여기는 사람은 온 세상에 그의 아내뿐이었다. 저녁이면 그는 천천히 기다시피 돌아왔다. 그리고 밤이면 어느 선술집의 자그마한 클럽에 가곤 했는데, 거기서 그는 국가의 재정을 좌지우지했다. 그가 수백만이며, 환차익이며, 할인이며, 로스차일드가 무얼 하는지, 베어링 브라더스가 어떤지를 떠드는 소리를 듣자면 참으로 놀라울 따름이었다. 그가 어찌나 어마어마한 액수를 입에 올리던지, 클럽의 신사들은—약제사며, 장의사며, 큰 목수이자 건축업자며, 몰래 끼어들어 오는 게 허용된 교구 서기며, 우리의 옛 지인 클랩 씨까지—그 노신사를 깍듯이 받들었다. \"나도 한때는 살림이 더 나았소이다, 선생.\" 그는 \"그 방을 드나드는\" 누구에게나 어김없이 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"내 아들은 말이오, 선생, 지금 이 순간 벵골 관할구 람건지의 수석 행정관이라, 한 달에 사천 루피를 만지고 있다오. 내 딸은 마음만 먹으면 대령 부인이 될 수도 있었지요. 나는 내일이라도 당장 수석 행정관인 내 아들 앞으로 이천 파운드 어음을 끊을 수 있고, 그러면 알렉산더가 그 어음을 현금으로 바꿔 줄 게요, 선생, 두말없이, 그 자리에서 말이오, 선생. 하지만 세들리 집안은 본디 자존심 강한 가문이었소이다.\" 친애하는 독자여, 그대도 나도 언젠가 이런 처지에 빠질 수 있다. 우리의 벗들 중 여럿이 이미 그 지경에 이르지 않았던가? 우리의 운이 다할 수도, 우리의 능력이 우리를 저버릴 수도, 무대 위 우리의 자리를 더 낫고 더 젊은 배우들에게 빼앗길 수도 있다. 인생의 기회란 굴러가 버리고 우리를 산산이 부서져 좌초된 채 남겨 두는 법이다. 그러면 사람들은 그대를 마주치면 길을 건너가 버릴 것이다. 아니, 그보다 더 고약하게는, 손가락 두 개만 내밀며 가엾다는 듯 생색을 낼 것이다. 그때 그대는 알게 되리라, 그대가 등을 돌리기가 무섭게 그대의 벗이 \"딱한 친구로다, 어쩌면 그리 경솔한 짓들을 저질렀는지, 그 작자가 차 버린 기회들이 얼마나 많은지!\" 하고 입을 떼리라는 것을. 글쎄다, 글쎄다—마차 한 대와 연 삼천 파운드가 보상의 정점도 아니요, 인간에 대한 신의 심판의 끝도 아니다. 만약 돌팔이들이 망하는 만큼이나 자주 번창하고—만약 어릿광대들이 성공하고 악당들이 한밑천 잡으며, 또 그 반대로, 우리 가운데 가장 유능하고 정직한 자들과 똑같이 불운과 번영을 나누어 갖는다면—내 말하건대, 형제여, 허영의 시장이 주는 선물과 쾌락이란 결코 대단한 것으로 여길 수 없으며, 아마도…… 그러나 우리는 이야기의 영역에서 벗어나 헤매고 있다.\n\n세들리 부인이 기운찬 여자였더라면, 남편이 파멸한 뒤에 그 기운을 발휘하여 큰 집을 얻고 하숙인을 들였을 것이다. 영락한 세들리는 하숙집 안주인의 남편 노릇을 곧잘 해냈을 것이다. 사생활의 무뇨스라고나 할까. 명목상의 주인이자 가장, 고기를 썰어 나누는 자, 집안 살림을 거드는 자, 그 칙칙한 왕좌를 차지한 여인의 비천한 남편 말이다. 나는 한때 명석한 두뇌와 좋은 가문에, 좋은 전망과 활력을 지녔던 사내들, 젊은 시절 향사들을 접대하고 사냥말을 거느렸던 사내들이, 앙심 가득한 늙은 노파들을 위해 순하게 양 다리 고기를 썰어 주며 그 음울한 식탁을 주재하는 척하는 꼴을 본 적이 있다. 그러나 우리가 말하건대, 세들리 부인에게는 타임스에서 읽을 법한 \"유쾌한 음악 가정에 합류할 엄선된 입주자 몇 분\"을 구하러 분주히 돌아다닐 기운이 없었다. 그녀는 운명이 자신을 좌초시켜 놓은 그 물가에 누워 만족했다. 그러니 이 노부부의 인생 행로가 끝났음을 누구라도 알 수 있었던 것이다.\n\n나는 그들이 불행했다고는 생각하지 않는다. 어쩌면 그들은 번영하던 시절보다 영락한 처지에서 더 자존심이 셌는지도 모른다. 세들리 부인은 지하실이나 잘 꾸며진 부엌으로 내려가 집주인 클랩 부인과 여러 시간을 함께 보낼 때면, 늘 클랩 부인에게 대단한 인물로 행세했다. 아일랜드 출신 하녀 베티 플래너건의 보닛과 리본이며, 그 건방진 태도며, 게으름이며, 부엌 양초를 무모하게 펑펑 써 대는 버릇이며, 차와 설탕을 축내는 일 따위가, 옛날 자기 집안 식구들이 하던 일이나 진배없이 이 노부인을 사로잡고 즐겁게 해 주었다. 그 옛 살림이라면 샘보와 마부, 마구간지기, 사동, 그리고 한 무리의 여자 하인들을 거느린 가정부까지 있던 시절이었으니—이 마음씨 좋은 부인은 하루에도 백 번씩 그 옛 살림 이야기를 했다. 게다가 베티 플래너건 말고도, 세들리 부인은 그 거리의 잡일 하녀들을 죄다 감독했다. 그녀는 오두막의 세입자 하나하나가 그 자그마한 집세를 어떻게 내고 있는지, 또 얼마나 밀렸는지까지 다 알았다. 여배우 루즈몽 부인이 그 수상쩍은 식구들을 거느리고 지나가면 그녀는 옆으로 비켜섰다. 약제사 부인 페슬러 부인이 남편의 영업용 일두마차를 타고 지나가면 그녀는 고개를 빳빳이 쳐들었다. 세들리 씨가 좋아하는 한 푼어치 순무를 두고 채소 장수와 입씨름을 벌이는가 하면, 우유 장수와 빵집 사동을 감시하기도 했고, 정육점에 행차하기도 했다. 그 정육점 주인은 세들리 부인의 양 허릿살 한 덩이를 두고 벌어지는 소동보다 훨씬 덜한 수선으로 황소 수백 마리를 팔아 치웠을 게 분명했다. 또 그녀는 일요일이면 고깃덩이 아래 깔린 감자알을 세었는데, 그날이면 가장 좋은 옷을 차려입고 교회에 두 번 갔으며, 저녁에는 블레어의 설교집을 읽었다.\n\n그날이면—평일에는 \"사업\" 때문에 그런 즐거움을 누릴 수 없었으니—늙은 세들리는 어린 손자 조지를 데리고 근처 공원이나 켄싱턴 정원에 나가 군인들을 구경시키거나 오리에게 먹이를 주는 것을 큰 낙으로 삼았다. 조지는 붉은 군복을 좋아했고, 할아버지는 그 애 아버지가 이름난 군인이었다는 이야기를 들려주며, 가슴에 워털루 훈장을 단 여러 하사관들과 다른 이들에게 그 애를 소개했다. 늙은 할아버지는 거드름을 피우며 그 아이를, 저 영광스러운 십팔일에 영광스럽게 전사한 ××연대 오즈번 대위의 아들이라고 소개했던 것이다. 그가 이 하사관 양반들 몇몇에게 흑맥주 한 잔씩을 대접한 일도 알려져 있거니와, 사실 처음 몇 번의 일요일 산책에서는 어린 조지를 응석받이로 만들어, 그 애 건강을 해칠 만큼 사과며 박하 사탕을 잔뜩 먹였다. 마침내 애밀리아가, 할아버지가 그 아이에게 어떤 케이크도, 막대 사탕도, 노점에서 파는 어떤 군것질거리도 주지 않겠노라고 명예를 걸고 엄숙히 약속하지 않는 한, 조지를 결코 할아버지와 함께 내보내지 않겠다고 선언하기에 이르렀다.\n\n세들리 부인과 그녀의 딸 사이에는 이 아이를 두고 일종의 냉랭함과 은밀한 시샘이 감돌았다. 조지가 아주 어렸던 시절 어느 날 저녁, 작은 거실에 앉아 바느질을 하던 애밀리아가, 노부인이 방을 나간 줄도 미처 모르고 있다가, 그 순간까지 잠들어 있던 아이의 울음소리에 본능적으로 위층 육아실로 달려 올라간 일이 있었던 것이다. 그리고 거기서 그녀는 세들리 부인이 갓난아기에게 몰래 대피의 영약을 먹이고 있는 광경을 목격했다. 일상의 인간들 가운데 가장 온순하고 다정한 애밀리아는, 자신의 모성적 권위를 이렇게 침범당했음을 알고는 분노로 온몸을 부르르 떨었다. 여느 때는 창백하던 그녀의 두 볼이 이제 발갛게 달아올라, 열두 살 어린아이 시절 그랬던 것처럼 빨개졌다. 그녀는 어머니의 품에서 아기를 낚아챈 다음 병을 와락 붙잡았고, 노부인은 죄지은 듯한 찻숟가락을 든 채 격분하여 입을 딱 벌리고 그녀를 멍하니 바라보았다.\n\n애밀리아는 그 병을 벽난로 속으로 와장창 내던졌다. \"엄마, 우리 아기 독살되게 둘 수 없어요.\" 에미가 두 팔로 아기를 감싸 안고 거칠게 어르며, 번득이는 눈으로 어머니를 돌아보고 소리쳤다.\n\n\"독살이라니, 애밀리아!\" 노부인이 말했다. \"나한테 그런 말을 해?\"\n\n\"이 아이는 페슬러 씨가 보내 주는 약 말고는 어떤 약도 먹어선 안 돼요. 그분이 대피의 영약은 독이라고 하셨다고요.\"\n\n\"좋아, 그래, 그럼 넌 내가 살인자라고 생각하는 거구나.\" 세들리 부인이 대꾸했다. \"이게 네 어미한테 쓰는 말투냐. 내가 불운을 겪었지. 인생에서 밑바닥까지 떨어졌어. 마차를 굴리던 몸이 이제 두 발로 걸어 다니지. 그래도 내가 살인자인 줄은 여태 몰랐구나, 그 소식 일러 줘서 고맙다.\"\n\n\"엄마.\" 늘 눈물을 쏟을 준비가 되어 있는 이 가엾은 딸이 말했다. \"저한테 너무 모질게 굴지 마세요. 저는—저는 그런 뜻이 아니었어요—제 말은, 엄마가 이 소중한 아이한테 무슨 해를 끼친다는 뜻이 아니라, 다만—\"\n\n\"오, 아니지, 얘야—다만 내가 살인자라는 거지. 그렇다면 차라리 올드 베일리로 가는 게 낫겠구나. 하기야 난 네가 어릴 적에 독을 먹이기는커녕, 돈으로 구할 수 있는 가장 좋은 교육과 가장 비싼 선생들을 붙여 주었지만 말이다. 그래, 나는 다섯 아이를 길러 셋을 묻었어. 그리고 그중에서도 내가 가장 사랑했던 아이, 후두염이며 이앓이며 홍역이며 백일해를 다 거두며 보살폈고, 비용 따위 아랑곳없이 외국인 선생들을 붙이고, 미네르바 학원에서—내가 처녀 시절엔 받아 본 적도 없던 온갖 교양을 배우게 해 준 그 아이가—오래오래 이 땅에서 살고자 부모를 공경하고, 쓸모 있는 사람이 되려 하고, 종일 제 방에서 시무룩하니 귀부인 행세나 하지 않던 그 시절의 나로서는 그저 감지덕지였던 그 자식이—이제 와서 나더러 살인자라는구나. 아, 오즈번 부인! 부디 그대만은 가슴속에 독사를 품어 기르는 일이 없기를, 그게 내 기도라오.\"\n\n\"엄마, 엄마!\" 어쩔 줄 모르는 딸이 부르짖었다. 그녀의 품에 안긴 아이도 미친 듯이 합창하듯 울음을 터뜨렸다. \"살인자라니, 정말로! 무릎 꿇고 앉아, 그 사악하고 배은망덕한 마음을 깨끗이 씻어 달라고 하느님께 기도해라, 애밀리아. 그리고 내가 너를 용서하듯, 하느님께서도 너를 용서하시기를.\" 그러고는 세들리 부인이 다시 한번 독이라는 말을 쉭 내뱉으며 방에서 휙 나가 버렸으니, 이로써 자비로운 축복을 끝맺은 것이었다.\n\n세들리 부인이 천수를 다할 때까지, 그녀와 딸 사이의 이 균열은 끝내 온전히 메워지지 않았다. 이 다툼은 노부인에게 무수한 이점을 안겨 주었고, 그녀는 그것을 여자 특유의 영악함과 끈기로 빠짐없이 활용했다. 이를테면, 그 후로 그녀는 여러 주 동안 애밀리아에게 거의 말을 걸지 않았다. 그녀는 하인들에게, 오즈번 부인이 노여워할지 모르니 아이를 건드리지 말라고 일렀다. 그녀는 딸에게, 조지를 위해 매일 끓이는 그 자그마한 죽에 독이 들어 있지 않은지 직접 보고 확인하라고 청했다. 이웃들이 아이의 건강을 물으면, 그녀는 들으라는 듯이 오즈번 부인에게 물어보라고 했다. 그녀는 아기가 잘 있는지 어떤지 감히 묻는 법이 없었다. 그녀는 그 아이가 제 손자요 더없이 소중한 자기 핏줄인데도 손도 대려 하지 않았는데, 아이를 다루는 데 익숙하지 않아 자칫 죽일지도 모른다는 까닭에서였다. 그리고 페슬러 씨가 치료차 진찰하러 올 때면, 그녀는 어찌나 빈정대며 경멸하는 태도로 의사를 맞이하던지, 그 외과의가 말하기를, 자기가 직업상 모시는 영광을 누리는 시슬우드 귀부인 본인조차도 늙은 세들리 부인보다 더 거드름을 피우지는 못할 것이라고 할 정도였다—더구나 그는 그녀에게서 진료비 한 푼 받지 않으면서 말이다. 그리고 아마 에미도 제 나름대로 시샘을 했을 것이다. 어느 어머니인들, 제 자식을 대신 건사하려 들거나 그 애정의 으뜸 자리를 노리는 이들에게 시샘하지 않겠는가. 분명한 것은, 누가 그 아이를 돌보기라도 하면 그녀가 마음 졸였다는 사실이며, 또한 그녀가 클랩 부인이나 하녀에게 그 아이를 입히거나 보살피게 하느니, 차라리 자기 작은 침대 위에 걸린 남편의 세밀화를 그들에게 닦게 하는 편이 나을 만큼 그것을 허락하지 않았다는 사실이다—그 작은 침대란, 이 가엾은 딸이 남편에게로 떠나갈 때 일어났던 바로 그 침대요, 이제 그녀가 길고 고요하고 눈물겹지만 행복한 여러 해를 보내려 돌아온 그 침대였다.\n\n이 방에 애밀리아의 온 마음과 보물이 깃들어 있었다. 바로 이곳에서 그녀는 한결같은 사랑의 정열로 아들을 돌보고, 어린 시절의 그 숱한 병치레를 지켜보았다. 손위 조지가 어쩐지 이 아이 속에 되살아난 듯했는데, 다만 더 나아진 모습으로, 천국에서 돌아온 듯한 모습으로였다. 백 가지나 되는 자그마한 말투며 표정이며 몸짓에서 이 아이는 제 아버지를 어찌나 닮았던지, 미망인이 아이를 가슴에 안을 때면 그 가슴이 떨려 오곤 했다. 그러면 아이는 곧잘 어머니가 우는 까닭을 물었다. 네가 아버지를 닮아서 그렇단다, 하고 그녀는 거리낌 없이 말해 주었다. 그녀는 죽은 아버지에 대해 끊임없이 아이에게 이야기했고, 그 천진하고 의아해하는 아이에게 조지를 향한 자신의 사랑을 들려주었다—조지 본인에게나 처녀 시절 자신의 어떤 절친한 벗에게 했던 것보다 훨씬 더 많이. 그녀는 부모에게는 이 일을 결코 입에 올리지 않았으니, 그들에게 자기 마음을 드러내기를 꺼린 것이었다. 어린 조지가 그들보다 더 잘 알아들었을 리 없었지만, 그녀는 그 아이의 귀에다, 오직 그 아이의 귀에만 자신의 감상적인 비밀들을 거리낌 없이 쏟아부었다. 이 여인의 기쁨이라는 것 자체가 일종의 슬픔이었고, 적어도 어찌나 애틋했던지 그 표현이 눈물로 나타날 정도였다. 그녀의 감수성은 어찌나 여리고 떨림이 많은지, 어쩌면 책에서 이야기하지 않는 편이 나을지도 모르겠다. 페슬러 박사(이제는 더없이 번창하는 여성 전문의로, 호사스러운 짙은 초록색 마차를 굴리고, 머지않아 기사 작위를 받을 전망에다, 맨체스터 광장에 집을 두고 있다)에게 들은 바로는, 아이의 젖을 뗄 때 그녀가 보인 슬픔은 헤롯 왕마저도 무너뜨릴 만한 광경이었다고 한다. 그는 여러 해 전에는 무척 마음이 여린 사람이었고, 그의 아내는 그때나 그 후로도 오래도록 애밀리아 부인을 죽도록 시샘했다.\n\n어쩌면 그 의사 부인의 시샘에는 그럴 만한 까닭이 있었는지도 모른다. 애밀리아와 알고 지내던 그 자그마한 무리에 속한 여인들 대부분이 그 시샘을 함께 나누었으며, 이성들이 그녀를 우러러보는 그 열의에 대해 적잖이 분개했던 것이다. 그녀에게 가까이 다가오는 사내라면 거의 모두가 그녀를 사랑했기 때문이다. 비록 그 까닭을 대라면 다들 난감해했겠지만 말이다. 그녀는 빛나지도, 재치 있지도, 그리 지혜롭지도, 유난히 아름답지도 않았다. 그러나 그녀는 가는 곳마다 모든 남자의 마음을 건드리고 사로잡았는데, 그것이 자기 자매들의 경멸과 불신을 불러일으키는 것만큼이나 한결같았다. 내 생각에 그녀의 주된 매력은 바로 그 연약함이었다—일종의 달콤한 순종과 부드러움, 마주치는 사내마다 그 동정과 보호를 호소하는 듯한 그런 것 말이다. 우리는 앞서 보았다. 연대에서 그녀가 조지의 동료들 중 몇몇에게만 말을 건넸을 뿐인데도, 식탁에 둘러앉은 젊은이들의 칼이란 칼은 모두 칼집에서 뛰쳐나와 그녀를 둘러싸고 싸우려 들었을 것임을. 풀럼의 그 비좁은 하숙집과 작은 무리에서도 마찬가지였으니, 그녀는 누구에게나 흥미를 자아내고 호감을 샀다. 설령 그녀가 망고 부인 본인이었다 한들—크러치드 프라이어스 소재 망고·플랜틴 상회의 대단한 가문 출신이요, 풀럼의 파인어리스를 호화롭게 소유한 그 안주인, 공작이며 백작들이 드나드는 여름 오찬을 베풀고, 켄싱턴의 왕실 마구간에서조차 내놓지 못할 휘황한 노란 제복과 밤색 말들을 거느리고 교구를 돌아다니던 그 망고 부인 말이다—내 말하건대, 설령 그녀가 망고 부인 본인이었다 한들, 아니 그 아들의 아내 메리 망고 부인(상회의 우두머리와 결혼하기를 황송히 승낙한 캐슬몰디 백작의 딸)이었다 한들, 이 동네의 상인들이 그 온순한 젊은 미망인에게, 그녀가 자기네 가게 문 앞을 지나거나 변변찮은 물건을 사러 들를 때마다 한결같이 표하던 그 이상의 경의를 표하지는 못했을 것이다.\n\n이리하여 의사인 페슬러 씨뿐만 아니라, 하녀들과 영세 상인들을 진료하며 진료소에서 타임스를 읽는 모습을 언제든 볼 수 있던 젊은 조수 린턴 씨까지도 공공연히 오즈번 부인의 노예임을 자처했다. 그는 풍채 좋은 젊은 신사로, 세들리 부인의 하숙집에서는 그의 상관보다도 더 환영받았다. 조지에게 무슨 탈이라도 나면, 그는 하루에 두세 번씩 들러 그 꼬마를 살피곤 했는데, 진료비 따위는 생각조차 하지 않았다. 그는 어린 조지를 위해 진료소 서랍에서 마름모 사탕이며 타마린드며 그 밖의 것들을 슬쩍 빼내 왔고, 기적처럼 달콤한 물약이며 합제를 조제해 주었으니, 그 아이로서는 아픈 것이 차라리 즐거운 일이 될 지경이었다. 그와 그의 상관 페슬러는, 조지가 홍역을 앓던 그 중대하고도 무시무시한 한 주 동안 꼬박 이틀 밤을 그 아이 곁에서 지새웠다—그것도 어머니가 보인 그 공포로 미루어 보자면, 세상에 홍역이라는 것이 일찍이 없었던 양 호들갑을 떨던 그 시절에 말이다. 그들이 다른 사람들을 위해서도 그만큼 했을까? 파인어리스의 식구들을 위해, 랠프 플랜태저넷이며 그웬돌린이며 귀네버 망고가 똑같은 어린이 병을 앓을 때 밤을 지새웠을까? 집주인의 딸인 어린 메리 클랩이 실제로 어린 조지에게서 그 병을 옮았을 때, 그 아이를 위해 밤을 지새웠을까? 진실을 말하자면, 아니었다. 그들은 적어도 그 아이에 관한 한 아주 편안히 잠들었고—그 애 병은 가벼운 경우라 거의 저절로 나을 거라고 진단했으며, 물약 한두 첩을 보내 주고, 아이가 차도를 보이자 완전히 무심한 태도로, 그저 형식치레로 키니네를 처방해 주었을 뿐이다.\n\n또 길 건너편에는 자그마한 프랑스인 기사가 있어, 동네의 여러 학교에서 자기 모국어를 가르쳤는데, 밤이면 그의 거처에서 그가 쌕쌕거리는 낡은 바이올린으로 떨리는 옛 가보트며 미뉴에트를 켜는 소리를 들을 수 있었다. 분 바르고 정중한 이 노인은 해머스미스의 수녀원 예배당의 일요일 미사를 한 번도 거르지 않았고, 생각이며 행실이며 거동이며 모든 점에서, 영악한 앨비언을 저주하며 오늘날 콰드런트 아케이드에서 시가를 문 채 사람을 노려보는 그 수염투성이 동족 야만인들과는 전혀 딴판이었다—이 탈롱루주 노기사가 오즈번 부인 이야기를 할 때면, 그는 먼저 코담배 한 줌을 다 들이마신 다음, 손을 우아하게 휘저어 남은 가루를 털어 내고, 다시 손가락을 한데 모아 입가로 가져가서는 입맞춤으로 후 불어 펼치며 외치곤 했다. 아! 라 디빈 크레아튀르! (아, 저 거룩한 여인이여!) 그는 애밀리아가 브롬프턴 골목을 거닐면 그 발밑에서 꽃들이 흐드러지게 피어난다고 맹세코 단언했다. 그는 어린 조지를 큐피드라고 불렀고, 그 애 엄마인 비너스의 안부를 물었으며, 어리둥절해하는 베티 플래너건에게 그대는 삼미신 중 한 분이요, 사랑의 여왕을 모시는 총애받는 시녀라고 했다.\n\n이처럼 손쉽게 얻은, 본인은 의식조차 못 하는 인기의 사례들은 얼마든지 더 들 수 있다. 이 가족이 다니던 구역 예배당의 온화하고 점잖은 부목사 비니 씨가, 부지런히 미망인을 찾아와 어린 사내아이를 무릎에 앉혀 어르고, 그 애에게 라틴어를 가르쳐 주겠노라 제안했던 일도 있지 않은가—그의 살림을 맡아 하던 노처녀 누이의 노여움을 사면서까지 말이다. \"저 여자한테는 별것 없어요, 베일비.\" 그 누이는 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"여기 차 마시러 와서는 저녁 내내 한마디도 안 하잖아요. 그저 가련하고 맥없는 여자일 뿐이고, 내 보기엔 마음이라고는 아예 없는 것 같다니까. 댁들 신사분들이 그토록 탄복하는 건 그저 그 예쁘장한 얼굴뿐이라고요. 오천 파운드에다 그 밖의 기대까지 있는 그리츠 양이 그 여자보다 인품이 두 배는 낫고, 내 취향으론 천 배는 더 마음에 들어요. 게다가 그리츠 양이 미인이기까지 했다면, 오라버니도 그녀를 완벽하다 여겼으리란 걸 난 알아요.\"\n\n아마도 비니 양의 말은 상당 부분 옳았을 것이다. 사내들의 마음에, 그 사악한 악당들의 마음에 동정을 불러일으키는 것은 바로 예쁜 얼굴인 것이다. 한 여자가 미네르바의 지혜와 정결을 다 갖추었다 한들, 얼굴이 못생겼다면 우리는 그녀에게 눈길조차 주지 않는다. 한 쌍의 빛나는 눈이 어떤 어리석음인들 용서받게 하지 못하랴? 붉은 입술과 달콤한 말씨가 어떤 따분함인들 즐겁게 만들지 못하랴? 그리하여 숙녀들은 그 특유의 공정함으로, 여자가 아름다우니 그러므로 바보임에 틀림없다고 따지는 것이다. 오, 숙녀들이여, 숙녀들이여! 그대들 중에는 아름답지도 지혜롭지도 못한 이들이 더러 있도다.\n\n이런 것들은 우리 여주인공의 삶에서 들려줄 만한 자질구레한 사건들에 지나지 않는다. 그녀의 이야기는, 점잖은 독자께서 이미 의심할 바 없이 알아채셨듯, 경이로운 일들을 다루지 않는다. 그러니 그 아들이 태어난 후 칠 년 동안의 그녀의 행적을 일지로 적어 두었다 한들, 앞 쪽에 기록된 그 홍역 사건보다 더 눈에 띄는 일은 거의 찾아볼 수 없었을 것이다. 그렇다, 어느 날, 그녀로서는 무척이나 놀랍게도, 방금 언급한 비니 목사가 그녀에게 오즈번이라는 성을 자기 성으로 바꾸지 않겠느냐고 청했다. 그러자 그녀는 깊이 얼굴을 붉히고 눈에도 목소리에도 눈물을 머금은 채, 자신을 그토록 마음에 두어 준 데 대해 감사하고, 자신과 가엾은 어린 아들에게 베풀어 준 친절에 고마움을 표하면서도, 자신은 결코, 결코 다른 누구도 생각할 수 없으며 오직—오직 자신이 잃은 그 남편만을 생각할 수밖에 없노라고 말했다.\n\n사월 이십오일과 유월 십팔일, 결혼한 날과 미망인이 된 날이면, 그녀는 온종일 방에 틀어박혀, 그 떠나간 벗을 추억하는 데 그날들을 (그리고 어린 아들이 침대 곁 요람에서 잠든 가운데, 외로운 밤 생각에 잠겨 보낸 그 얼마나 많은 시간을—우리로서는 알 수 없는—) 오롯이 바쳤다. 낮 동안에는 좀 더 활기차게 지냈다. 그녀는 조지에게 읽기와 쓰기, 그리고 그림도 조금 가르쳐야 했다. 책에서 이야기를 들려주려고 그녀는 책을 읽었다. 그 아이의 눈이 뜨이고 주위를 둘러싼 바깥 자연의 영향 아래 그 마음이 자라남에 따라, 그녀는 자신의 변변찮은 능력을 다하여 그 아이에게 만물의 창조주를 우러르도록 가르쳤다. 그리하여 밤마다 아침마다 그 아이와 그녀는—(이 광경을 목격하거나 기억하는 사람이라면 누구나 그 가슴이 떨려 오리라 믿는 저 경외롭고 가슴 뭉클한 교감 속에서)—어머니와 어린 사내아이는 함께 하늘에 계신 우리 아버지께 기도드렸다. 어머니는 그 온유한 마음을 다해 간구하고, 아이는 어머니가 말하는 대로 더듬더듬 따라 외었다. 그리고 그때마다 그들은 사랑하는 아빠를 축복해 달라고 하느님께 기도했으니, 마치 그가 살아서 그들과 한방에 있기라도 한 듯이 말이다. 이 어린 신사를 씻기고 입히는 일—아침이면 식사 전에, 또 할아버지가 \"사업\" 보러 물러가기 전에 그 애를 데리고 한바탕 뛰어다니는 일—그 애를 위해 더없이 신기하고 기발한 옷들을 지어 주는 일, 이를 위해 알뜰한 미망인은 결혼 시절에 지녔던 옷장 속 쓸 만한 고운 옷가지란 옷가지는 죄다 잘라 고쳐 썼다—오즈번 부인 자신은 (특히 영락한 뒤로 고운 옷을 더 좋아하게 된 어머니로서는 무척 속상한 일이었지만) 늘 검은 드레스에 검은 리본을 단 밀짚 보닛을 쓰고 다녔으니—이런 일들이 그녀의 하루 많은 시간을 차지했다. 남는 시간은 어머니와 늙은 아버지를 돌보는 데 썼다. 그녀는 일부러 익혀 두었다가, 아버지가 클럽에 가지 않는 밤이면 그와 크리비지 카드놀이를 하곤 했다. 아버지가 그러고 싶어 할 때면 노래도 불러 드렸는데, 그것은 좋은 징조였으니, 그가 그 음악이 흐르는 동안 어김없이 편안한 잠에 빠져들었기 때문이다. 그녀는 아버지의 그 숱한 진정서며 편지며 안내장이며 사업 계획서를 대신 써 주었다. 노신사의 옛 지인들 대부분에게, 그가 흑금강석 무연탄 회사의 대리인이 되어 친구들과 일반에게 최상의 석탄을 한 차당 몇 실링에 공급할 수 있게 되었다고 알린 것도 바로 그녀의 필체였다. 노신사가 한 일이라고는 화려한 서명을 휘갈겨 회람장에 이름을 적고, 떨리는 사무원 같은 글씨로 수신처를 쓴 것뿐이었다. 이런 회람장 한 통이 콕스 앤드 그린우드 상회 편으로 ××연대 도빈 소령에게 부쳐졌으나, 당시 마드라스에 있던 소령으로서는 석탄이 딱히 필요할 일이 없었다. 그래도 그는 그 안내장을 쓴 필체가 누구의 것인지 알아보았다. 맙소사! 그것을 제 손에 쥐기 위해서라면 그가 무엇인들 내주지 않았으랴! 두 번째 안내장이 나와, J. 세들리 상회가 오포르토와 보르도, 세인트메리스에 대리점을 설치하여 친구들과 일반에게 가장 좋고 가장 이름난 포트와인과 셰리주와 클라레 와인을 합리적인 가격에, 더없이 유리한 조건으로 제공할 수 있게 되었음을 소령에게 알렸다. 이 언질을 받아들여 도빈은 총독이며, 총사령관이며, 판사들이며, 연대들이며, 그 관할구에서 자기가 아는 사람이란 사람은 죄다 미친 듯이 찾아다니며 권유했고, 세들리 상회 앞으로 와인 주문을 보냈으니, 세들리 씨와 그 사업의 \"상회\" 격인 클랩 씨가 그만 어안이 벙벙해질 정도였다. 그러나 그 첫 행운의 폭발 이후로는 더 이상 주문이 들어오지 않았다. 가엾은 늙은 세들리는 그 한 번의 행운에 기대어 시내에 사옥을 짓고, 한 무리의 사무원을 두고, 자기만의 부두를 갖추고, 전 세계에 거래처를 두려던 참이었건만. 노신사의 옛 와인 감식 솜씨는 사라지고 없었다. 식당의 동료 장교들이 퍼붓는 욕설이, 그 형편없는 술을 그곳에 들여놓은 장본인인 도빈 소령에게로 쏟아졌다. 그래서 그는 그 와인을 상당량 도로 사들여 공개 경매에 부쳐, 자신에게 막대한 손해를 보며 팔아 치웠다. 한편 이 무렵에는 캘커타의 세무국 위원 자리로 승진해 있던 조스로 말하면, 우편으로 이 술꾼 안내장 한 묶음이 배달되어 왔을 때 분통이 터져 펄펄 뛰었다. 거기에는 아버지가 따로 적은 쪽지가 들어 있었는데, 아비가 이 사업에서 너를 믿고 있으며, 송장에 적힌 대로 엄선한 와인을 얼마간 너에게 부쳤으니 그 대금만큼 너 앞으로 어음을 끊는다는 내용이었다. 조스는 자기 아버지, 그러니까 세무국 위원인 조스 세들리의 아버지가 주문이나 구걸하는 와인 장수라고 여겨지는 것을, 차라리 잭 케치 사형 집행인으로 여겨지는 것만큼이나 견딜 수 없었던지라, 그 어음을 경멸하며 거절하고는, 노신사에게 당신 일이나 신경 쓰라며 무례하기 짝이 없는 답장을 써 보냈다. 그리하여 부도난 어음이 되돌아오자, 세들리 상회는 마드라스 거래에서 거둔 이익과 에미가 모아 둔 돈의 얼마간을 보태어 그것을 메워야 했다.\n\n연 오십 파운드의 연금 외에도, 남편의 유언 집행인이 밝힌 바에 따르면, 오즈번이 세상을 떠날 당시 대리인의 수중에 오백 파운드가 남아 있었는데, 조지의 후견인인 도빈은 이 돈을 인도의 어느 대리점에 연 팔 푼 이자로 맡겨 두자고 제안했다. 소령이 그 돈에 대해 무슨 협잡 속셈을 품고 있다고 여긴 세들리 씨는 이 계획에 한사코 반대했다. 그래서 그는 문제의 그 돈을 그렇게 굴리는 데 직접 항의하려고 대리인을 찾아갔다가, 놀랍게도 그들 수중에는 그런 돈이 애당초 없었으며, 작고한 대위의 자산을 다 합쳐도 백 파운드에 미치지 못했고, 문제의 그 오백 파운드는 분명 별도의 금액으로 그 내막은 도빈 소령이 알고 있으리라는 사실을 알게 되었다. 무슨 협잡이 있다는 확신이 그 어느 때보다 굳어진 늙은 세들리는 소령을 추궁하고 나섰다. 그는 딸의 가장 가까운 친지로서, 고압적인 태도로 작고한 대위의 회계 내역을 내놓으라고 요구했다. 도빈이 말을 더듬고, 얼굴을 붉히고, 어쩔 줄 몰라 하는 꼴은, 자기가 상대하는 자가 협잡꾼이라는 세들리의 확신을 더욱 부채질했다. 그는 위풍당당한 어조로, 자기 말마따나 그 장교에게 속에 담아 둔 말을 한바탕 쏟아냈으니, 소령이 작고한 사위의 돈을 불법으로 가로채고 있는 것으로 믿는다고 단도직입적으로 말한 것이다.\n\n이에 도빈은 인내심을 모두 잃었고, 만약 그를 비난하는 자가 그토록 늙고 영락한 처지가 아니었다면, 슬로터스 커피하우스에서 두 사람 사이에 한바탕 싸움이 벌어졌을지도 모른다. 두 신사는 그 유흥 장소의 칸막이 좌석에서 이야기를 나누던 참이었다. \"위층으로 올라가시지요, 선생.\" 소령이 떠듬떠듬 내뱉었다. \"기어이 위층으로 올라가셔야겠습니다. 어느 쪽이 손해 본 당사자인지, 가엾은 조지인지 나인지, 내 보여 드리지요.\" 그러고는 노신사를 자기 침실까지 끌고 올라가 책상에서 오즈번의 회계 장부와, 오즈번이 끊어 준 차용증 한 묶음을 꺼냈다. 공정을 기하자면, 오즈번은 늘 차용증을 끊어 줄 준비가 되어 있는 사람이었다. \"그는 영국에서 진 빚은 다 갚았습니다.\" 도빈이 덧붙였다. \"하지만 전사했을 때 그에게는 이 세상에 백 파운드도 없었습니다. 나와 동료 장교 한둘이 그 얼마 안 되는 돈을 마련했는데, 그게 우리가 내놓을 수 있는 전부였습니다. 그런데도 당신은 감히, 우리가 미망인과 고아를 속여 먹으려 든다고 말씀하시는 겁니까.\" 세들리는 몹시 뉘우치며 풀이 죽었다—사실인즉 윌리엄 도빈이 노신사에게 새빨간 거짓말을 한 것이었지만 말이다. 그 돈은 한 푼까지 도빈 자신이 내놓은 것이었고, 벗을 손수 묻어 주었으며, 그 참변과 가엾은 애밀리아의 이주에 따르는 온갖 비용과 부담을 다 떠맡은 것도 그였기 때문이다.\n\n이런 비용에 대해 늙은 오즈번은 한 번도 골치 아프게 생각해 본 적이 없었고, 애밀리아의 다른 어떤 친지도, 사실 애밀리아 본인도 그러했다. 그녀는 회계 담당으로서 도빈 소령을 믿었고, 다소 뒤죽박죽인 그의 계산을 곧이곧대로 받아들였으며, 자신이 그에게 얼마나 신세를 지고 있는지는 단 한 번도 의심해 본 적이 없었다.\n\n약속대로 그녀는 일 년에 두세 번씩 마드라스로 그에게 편지를 썼는데, 죄다 어린 조지에 관한 편지였다. 그가 이 편지들을 얼마나 소중히 간직했던지! 애밀리아가 편지를 쓸 때마다 그는 답장을 했고, 그러기 전에는 결코 쓰지 않았다. 그러나 그는 자신을 기억해 달라는 뜻으로 끝없는 선물들을 대자와 그녀에게 보냈다. 그는 중국에서 스카프 한 상자와 훌륭한 상아 체스 한 벌을 주문해 보냈다. 졸은 초록색과 흰색의 작은 병사들로, 진짜 칼과 방패를 들고 있었다. 기사들은 말을 탔고, 성은 코끼리 등에 얹혀 있었다. \"파인어리스에 있는 망고 부인의 체스 한 벌도 이만큼 훌륭하지는 않더군요.\" 페슬러 씨가 한마디 했다. 이 체스 말들은 조지의 삶에 더없는 기쁨이었으니, 그 아이는 대부의 이 선물에 답례하느라 난생처음으로 글자를 또박또박 써 보냈다. 그는 잼이며 피클도 보내 주었는데, 피클이라는 것을 이 어린 신사가 찬장에서 몰래 맛보다가 너무 먹어 하마터면 죽을 뻔했다. 어찌나 매웠던지, 그 애는 그것이 몰래 훔쳐 먹은 데 대한 천벌이라고 여겼다. 에미는 이 작은 사고를 익살스럽게 적어 소령에게 편지했다. 그녀의 기운이 차츰 되살아나, 이제 가끔은 명랑해질 수도 있게 되었구나 싶어 그는 흐뭇했다. 그는 숄도 두 장 보냈는데, 그녀에게는 흰 것을, 그녀의 어머니에게는 종려잎 무늬가 든 검은 것을 보냈고, 늙은 세들리 씨와 조지에게는 겨울 목도리로 쓰라고 붉은 스카프 두 장을 보냈다. 세들리 부인이 아는 바로는, 그 숄은 적어도 한 장에 오십 기니는 족히 나가는 물건이었다. 그녀는 그것을 브롬프턴의 교회에 위풍당당하게 두르고 갔고, 그 멋들어진 새 물건을 두고 여자 친구들에게서 축하를 받았다. 에미의 것 역시 그녀의 수수한 검은 드레스에 곱게 어울렸다. \"저 애가 그분을 마음에 두지 않으려 하다니, 참으로 안타까운 일이지요!\" 세들리 부인은 클랩 부인에게며 브롬프턴의 친구들 모두에게 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"조스는 우리한테 이런 선물 한 번 보낸 적이 없고, 뭐든 인색하게 굴기만 한다니까요. 소령이 저 애한테 홀딱 빠져 있는 게 분명한데도, 내가 그 이야기를 조금이라도 꺼낼라치면 저 애는 얼굴이 빨개져서 울기 시작하더니 위층으로 올라가 그 세밀화를 끼고 앉아 있는다오. 난 그 세밀화라면 신물이 나요. 저 밉살스럽고 돈 자랑이나 하는 오즈번 집안 사람들을 아예 보지 않았더라면 좋았을 것을.\"\n\n이처럼 보잘것없는 정경과 사람들 틈에서 조지의 어린 시절이 흘러갔고, 그 아이는 섬세하고, 감수성 예민하고, 군림하려 들고, 여자 손에 길러진 아이로 자라났다—자기가 열렬한 애정으로 사랑하는 그 온순한 어머니를 휘어잡으면서 말이다. 그 아이는 주위의 작은 세상 모두를 다스렸다. 자라면서 그 아이가 보이는 거만한 태도와 한결같이 제 아버지를 빼닮은 모습에 어른들은 혀를 내둘렀다. 그 아이는 캐묻기 좋아하는 여느 아이들처럼 무엇에 대해서나 질문을 던졌다. 그 말과 물음의 깊이에 늙은 할아버지는 놀라 자빠질 지경이라, 선술집 클럽 사람들을 그 꼬마의 학식과 천재성에 관한 이야기로 진저리 나게 만들곤 했다. 그 아이는 할머니를 너그러운 무관심으로 견뎌 냈다. 그 아이를 둘러싼 작은 무리는 이 사내아이에 견줄 만한 아이가 이 세상에 없다고 믿었다. 조지는 제 아버지의 자존심을 물려받았으니, 어쩌면 그들의 생각이 틀리지 않다고 여겼는지도 모른다.\n\n그 아이가 여섯 살쯤 되었을 무렵, 도빈은 그 애에게 무척 자주 편지를 쓰기 시작했다. 소령은 조지가 학교에 다니게 되었다는 소식을 듣고 싶어 했고, 그곳에서 칭찬받을 만큼 잘 해내기를 바랐다. 아니면 집에서 좋은 가정교사를 두는 게 어떻겠느냐고 했다. 이제 그 애가 공부를 시작할 때가 되었다는 것이었다. 그리고 대부이자 후견인인 그는, 그 아이의 교육비를 자기가 부담할 수 있게 해 주었으면 한다는 뜻을 넌지시 비쳤다. 그 비용이 어머니의 빠듯한 수입에 큰 짐이 될 터였기 때문이다. 한마디로 소령은 늘 애밀리아와 그 어린 아들을 생각하고 있었고, 대리인들에게 지시하여 그 아이에게 그림책이며 물감 상자며 책상이며, 생각할 수 있는 온갖 놀이와 학습 도구를 빠짐없이 마련해 주었다. 조지의 여섯 번째 생일을 사흘 앞둔 어느 날, 하인을 대동한 한 신사가 이륜마차를 타고 세들리 씨의 집으로 들이닥쳐 조지 오즈번 도련님을 뵙겠다고 청했다. 그는 콘듀잇 거리의 군복 재단사 울지 씨로, 소령의 주문을 받아 그 어린 신사의 옷 한 벌 치수를 재러 온 것이었다. 그는 일찍이 그 어린 신사의 아버지인 대위의 옷을 짓는 영광을 누린 바 있었다. 또 때로는, 의심할 바 없이 소령의 부탁에 따라, 그의 누이들인 도빈 양들이 가족 마차를 타고 찾아와, 애밀리아와 어린 아들이 내키기만 하면 함께 드라이브를 나가자고 청하기도 했다. 이 숙녀들이 베푸는 후원과 친절은 애밀리아에게 무척 거북한 것이었으나, 그녀는 그것을 꽤나 온순히 견뎌 냈으니, 양보하는 것이 그녀의 천성이었기 때문이다. 게다가 그 마차와 그 호화로움이 어린 조지에게는 더없는 기쁨을 안겨 주었다. 숙녀들은 이따금 그 아이가 하루쯤 자기들과 함께 지낼 수 있게 해 달라고 청했고, 그 아이는 그들이 사는 덴마크힐의 그 멋진 정원 딸린 저택에 가는 것을 언제나 좋아했다. 그곳 온실에는 그토록 좋은 포도가, 담장에는 복숭아가 열려 있었던 것이다.\n\n어느 날 그들은 애밀리아가 들으면 틀림없이 기뻐하리라 확신하는 소식을 가지고 그녀를 친절히 찾아왔다—그들의 소중한 윌리엄에 관한, 무척이나 흥미로운 소식이라는 것이었다.\n\n\"무슨 소식인데요? 그분이 귀국하시나요?\" 그녀는 두 눈에 기쁨을 환히 빛내며 물었다.\n\n\"오, 아니에요—그건 전혀—다만 자기네 소중한 윌리엄이 곧 결혼할 모양이라고 믿을 만한 충분한 근거가 있다는 것이었다—그것도 애밀리아의 아주 절친한 벗의 친척과 말이다—마이클 오다우드 경의 누이인 글로비나 오다우드 양으로, 마드라스의 오다우드 부인에게 합류하러 떠난 처녀였는데—누구나 입을 모아 더없이 아름답고 교양 있는 아가씨라고 하더라는 것이었다.\"\n\n애밀리아는 \"오!\" 하고 말했다. 애밀리아는 정말로, 정말로 무척 행복했다. 그러나 그녀 생각에 글로비나가 더없이 친절한 그 옛 지인만 할 리는 없었지만—그래도—그래도 그녀는 정말로 무척 행복했다. 그러고는 그 의미를 나로서는 설명할 수 없는 어떤 충동에 사로잡혀, 그녀는 조지를 품에 끌어안고 유난스러운 애틋함으로 입을 맞추었다. 아이를 내려놓을 때 그녀의 두 눈은 흠뻑 젖어 있었다. 그리고 드라이브를 하는 내내 그녀는 거의 한마디도 하지 않았다—정말로 그토록 무척 행복했으면서도 말이다.\n\n이제 우리의 소임은 우리를 잠시 햄프셔의 옛 지인들에게로 다시 데려간다. 부유한 친척 노부인의 재산 처분을 둘러싼 그들의 기대가 그토록 처참하게 무너진 바로 그 사람들이다. 누이에게서 삼만 파운드를 받으리라 셈하고 있던 뷰트 크롤리에게, 고작 오천 파운드를 받는다는 것은 호된 타격이었다. 게다가 그 돈에서 자기 빚과 대학에 다니는 아들 짐의 빚까지 갚고 나니, 못생긴 네 딸을 시집보내는 데 쓸 몫으로는 그야말로 푼돈밖에 남지 않았다. 자신의 폭압적인 처신이 남편을 파멸시키는 데 얼마나 일조했는지를, 뷰트 부인은 끝내 알지 못했다. 적어도 인정하려 들지는 않았다. 여자로서 할 수 있는 일은 다 했노라고, 그녀는 맹세코 단언하며 우겼다. 위선자 조카 핏 크롤리가 부리는 그런 아첨의 재주를 자기가 갖지 못한 것이 어디 자기 잘못이란 말인가? 그녀는 부정하게 거머쥔 재산으로 그가 누릴 자격이 있는 만큼의 온갖 행복을 누리기를 빌어 주었다. \"적어도 그 돈은 집안에 남는 셈이지.\" 그녀가 너그러운 척 말했다. \"핏은 그 돈을 절대 쓰지 않을 거예요, 여보, 그건 아주 확실해요. 영국에 그보다 더한 구두쇠는 없으니까. 게다가 그 애는, 방식은 다를망정, 제 동생인 저 낭비벽 심한 탕아 로던 못지않게 밉살스러운 인간이거든요.\"\n\n그리하여 뷰트 부인은 처음의 격분과 실망이라는 충격이 가라앉자, 달라진 형편에 되도록 자신을 맞추고 온 힘을 다해 절약하고 긴축하기 시작했다. 그녀는 딸들에게 가난을 명랑하게 견디는 법을 가르쳤고, 그것을 감추거나 모면할 천 가지 그럴싸한 방법을 고안해 냈다. 그녀는 칭찬받아 마땅한 정력으로 딸들을 이끌고 이웃의 무도회며 공공장소를 누볐다. 아니, 그뿐 아니라 목사관에서 손님들을 후하고 안락하게 접대하기까지 했는데, 그것도 사랑하는 크롤리 양의 유산이 굴러들기 전보다 훨씬 잦게 그리하였다. 그녀의 겉으로 드러난 태도만 보아서는 이 집안이 기대를 저버린 일을 겪었으리라고 아무도 짐작하지 못했을 것이고, 그녀가 그토록 자주 사람들 앞에 모습을 드러내는 것을 보고서 집에서는 얼마나 옹색하게 굶주리며 사는지 알아챌 사람도 없었을 것이다. 딸들은 어느 때보다 많은 의상 장신구를 갖추었다. 그들은 끈질기게도 윈체스터와 사우샘프턴의 사교 모임에 모습을 비추었다. 카우스까지 파고들어 그곳의 경마 무도회와 보트 경기의 흥겨움을 즐겼다. 그리고 쟁기를 끌던 말을 떼어다 맨 그들의 마차는 쉴 새 없이 굴러다녔으니, 마침내는 그 네 자매가 고모에게서 재산을 물려받았다고 거의 믿어질 지경이 되었다. 그 고모의 이름을 그 집안은 사람들 앞에서 입에 올릴 때면 늘 더없이 애틋한 감사와 경의를 담아서만 언급했던 것이다. 나는 이런 종류의 거짓말보다 허영의 시장에서 더 흔한 거짓말을 알지 못한다. 그리고 이런 짓을 일삼는 사람들이 자신의 위선을 도리어 제 공으로 돌리며, 자기 형편의 실상에 관해 세상을 속일 수 있다는 이유만으로 스스로 더없이 덕스럽고 칭찬받을 만하다고 우쭐대는 모습은 눈여겨볼 만한 것이다.\n\n뷰트 부인은 분명 스스로를 영국에서 가장 덕망 높은 여인 가운데 하나로 여겼고, 그녀의 화목한 가족이라는 광경은 모르는 이들에게 교화적인 본보기였다. 그들은 그토록 명랑하고, 그토록 다정하고, 그토록 잘 교육받았으며, 그토록 꾸밈없었던 것이다! 마사는 꽃 그림을 절묘하게 그려서 그 주의 자선 바자회 절반을 채웠다. 에마는 어엿한 주(州)의 음유시인이어서, 그녀가 햄프셔 텔레그래프에 실은 시는 그 신문 시(詩) 난(欄)의 영광이었다. 패니와 마틸다는 함께 이중창을 불렀고, 그동안 어머니는 피아노를 쳤으며, 나머지 두 자매는 서로의 허리를 팔로 감싸고 앉아 애정 어린 표정으로 귀를 기울였다. 그 가엾은 딸들이 남몰래 그 이중창을 어설프게 두들기며 연습하는 모습은 아무도 보지 못했다. 어머니가 한 시간 또 한 시간 그들을 혹독하게 훈련시키는 모습도 아무도 보지 못했다. 한마디로, 뷰트 부인은 운명에 맞서 태연한 얼굴을 꾸미고 더없이 덕스러운 방식으로 체면을 지켰던 것이다.\n\n선량하고 점잖은 어머니가 할 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든 뷰트 부인은 했다. 그녀는 사우샘프턴에서 요트 타는 신사들을, 윈체스터 대성당 구역에서 목사들을, 그곳 병영에서 장교들을 끌어들였다. 순회재판이 열릴 때면 젊은 변호사들을 꾀어 보려 했고, 짐이 H. H. 사냥대와 함께 사냥을 다니며 사귄 친구들을 집으로 데려오도록 부추겼다. 사랑하는 자식들의 이익을 위해서라면 어머니가 못 할 일이 무엇이 있겠는가?\n\n이런 여인과, 저택에 사는 그 밉살스러운 준남작인 시동생 사이에 공통점이라곤 거의 있을 수 없었음은 분명한 일이다. 뷰트와 그의 형 핏 경 사이의 결렬은 완전한 것이었다. 아니, 사실은 핏 경과 그 주 전체 사이의 결렬이 그러했으니, 그 노인은 온 고을의 망신거리였던 것이다. 점잖은 사교계에 대한 그의 혐오는 나이가 들수록 깊어 갔고, 핏과 제인 부인이 결혼 후 의례적인 인사차 다녀간 이래로 저택 정문이 신사의 마차 바퀴를 향해 열린 적은 한 번도 없었다.\n\n그것은 끔찍하고도 불운한 방문이었으니, 그 집안 사람들이 진저리를 치지 않고서는 결코 떠올릴 수 없는 것이었다. 핏은 새파랗게 질린 얼굴로 아내에게 그 일은 절대 입 밖에 내지 말라고 사정했다. 그래서 핏 경이 아들과 며느리를 맞이한 정황이 그나마 세상에 알려진 것은 오로지, 여전히 저택에서 일어나는 모든 일을 죄다 꿰고 있던 뷰트 부인 자신을 통해서였다.\n\n말끔하고 잘 갖춰진 마차를 타고 공원 가로수 길을 올라가면서, 핏은 나무들 사이로 — 자기 나무들 사이로 — 군데군데 휑하게 뚫린 큰 구멍들을 보고 경악과 분노를 느꼈다. 늙은 준남작이 아무런 허락도 없이 죄다 베어 버린 자리였다. 공원은 황량하고 폐허 같은 모습을 띠고 있었다. 마찻길은 제대로 관리되지 않아, 그 말끔한 마차가 길을 따라 진흙 웅덩이에 흙탕물을 튀기며 허우적댔다. 테라스와 현관 계단 앞의 너른 진입로는 거무튀튀하니 이끼로 뒤덮였고, 한때 단정했던 화단은 무성하게 잡초가 우거져 있었다. 저택의 거의 전 면에 걸쳐 덧창이 닫혀 있었다. 종을 한참 울린 끝에야 커다란 현관문의 빗장이 풀렸다. 호록스가 마침내 퀸스크롤리의 상속자와 그 신부를 선조들의 저택 안으로 들였을 때, 리본을 두른 어떤 인물이 검은 떡갈나무 계단을 휙 올라가는 것이 눈에 띄었다. 그는 이른바 핏 경의 \"서재\"라는 곳으로 앞장서 안내했는데, 핏과 제인 부인이 그 방에 가까워질수록 담배 연기 냄새가 점점 짙어졌다. \"핏 경께서 그리 평안치 못하십니다.\" 호록스가 변명조로 말하며, 주인이 요통으로 고생하신다는 뜻을 넌지시 비쳤다.\n\n서재는 앞쪽 산책로와 공원이 내다보이는 방이었다. 핏 경은 창문 하나를 열어젖히고는, 짐을 막 내리려는 듯 보이는 역마차 마부와 핏의 하인을 향해 거기서 고래고래 소리를 지르고 있었다.\n\n\"그 트렁크들 하나도 내리지 마라.\" 그가 손에 쥔 담뱃대로 가리키며 외쳤다. \"잠깐 아침 인사나 하러 온 거다, 터커, 이 멍청아. 저런, 저 바깥쪽 말은 발굽 뒤가 어쩌면 저렇게 다 갈라졌누! 킹스헤드에 그놈 발굽 좀 문질러 줄 사람도 없단 말이냐? 잘 있었느냐, 핏? 잘 있었나, 얘야? 늙은이 보러 왔단 말이지, 응? 허, 너 얼굴도 곱구나. 네 그 늙어 빠진 말상 어미 같지가 않아. 이리 와서 이 늙은 핏한테 입 한 번 맞춰 다오, 착한 아가씨처럼.\"\n\n그 포옹은 며느리를 다소 당혹스럽게 했다. 면도도 하지 않고 담배 냄새를 풍기는 늙은 노인의 애무이고 보니 그럴 만도 했다. 그러나 그녀는 자기 오라비 사우스다운도 콧수염을 기르고 시가를 피운다는 사실을 떠올리며, 그런대로 우아하게 준남작에게 몸을 내맡겼다.\n\n\"핏이 살이 쪘구나.\" 이런 애정의 표시를 하고 나서 준남작이 말했다. \"이 녀석이 너한테 아주 긴 설교를 늘어놓더냐, 얘야? 백 번째 시편이니, 저녁 찬송이니, 그런 거 말이다, 응 핏? 호록스, 가서 제인 부인께 맘지 포도주 한 잔하고 케이크 좀 가져오너라, 이 덩치만 큰 멍청아. 살찐 돼지처럼 멀거니 서서 빤히 쳐다보지 말고. 머물다 가라고는 안 하마, 얘야. 너무 따분할 테니까. 나도 핏하고 함께 있자면 따분할 거다. 난 이제 늙은이라, 내 식대로 사는 게 좋아. 밤에는 내 담뱃대하고 주사위 놀이나 하면서 말이지.\"\n\n\"저도 주사위 놀이를 할 줄 알아요, 어르신.\" 제인 부인이 웃으며 말했다. \"아빠랑 크롤리 양하고 곧잘 했었거든요, 그렇죠, 크롤리 씨?\"\n\n\"제인 부인도 할 줄 압니다, 어르신. 그토록 즐기신다고 말씀하시는 그 놀이 말입니다.\" 핏이 거만하게 말했다.\n\n\"그래도 묵고 가진 않을 게다. 아냐, 아냐, 머드버리로 돌아가서 린서 부인 장사나 좀 거들어 주려무나. 아니면 목사관으로 내려가서 뷰티한테 저녁 한 끼 청하든지. 그 녀석은 너희를 보면 아주 반가워할 게다, 알지? 늙은 여편네 돈 손에 넣게 해 줬다고 너희한테 어찌나 고마워하는데. 하, 하! 그중 얼마는 내가 죽고 나면 이 저택 손보는 데 쓰이게 될 테고.\"\n\n\"보아하니, 어르신,\" 핏이 목소리를 높이며 말했다. \"어르신 사람들이 그 나무들을 베어 넘기는 모양입니다만.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 날씨 참 좋구나. 이맘때치고는 철에 맞는 날씨야.\" 핏 경이 대답했는데, 갑자기 귀가 먹은 척했다. \"하지만 나도 이젠 늙어 가는 거다, 핏. 원, 이런, 너도 쉰이 멀지 않았잖느냐. 그래도 이이는 정정하지요, 어여쁜 제인 부인, 안 그렇소? 다 경건함과 절제와 도덕적인 생활 덕분이지. 날 좀 보시오, 난 여든이 멀지 않았는데도 — 허, 허.\" 그러면서 그는 웃고, 코담배를 들이켜고, 그녀를 곁눈질하며 그녀의 손을 꼬집었다.\n\n핏은 다시 한 번 화제를 나무 쪽으로 돌렸지만, 준남작은 또 그 즉시 귀가 먹어 버렸다.\n\n\"난 아주 늙어 가는 데다, 올해는 요통으로 지독히도 고생했단다. 이제 여기 오래 있지는 못할 게다. 그래도 네가 와 주어서 기쁘구나, 며늘아기야. 난 네 얼굴이 마음에 든다, 제인 부인. 거기엔 그 빌어먹을 광대뼈 튀어나온 빙키 집안 꼴이 하나도 없거든. 그래서 너한테 예쁜 걸 하나 주마, 얘야, 궁정에 갈 때 차고 가게.\" 그러더니 그는 발을 질질 끌며 방을 가로질러 찬장으로 다가가, 거기서 제법 값나가는 보석이 든 작고 오래된 함을 하나 꺼냈다. \"이걸 받아라.\" 그가 말했다. \"얘야, 이건 내 어머니 것이었고, 그 후엔 첫 번째 빙키 부인 것이었지. 고운 진주란다. 그 철물장수 딸년한테는 절대 주지 않았어. 아무렴, 아니고말고. 받아서 얼른 챙겨 넣어라.\" 그가 함을 며느리의 손에 떠넘기며 말하고는, 호록스가 쟁반에 다과를 받쳐 들고 들어오는 것을 보고 찬장 문을 탁 닫았다.\n\n\"핏의 마누라한테 뭘 주신 거예요?\" 핏과 제인 부인이 노인에게 작별 인사를 하고 떠난 뒤, 그 리본을 두른 인물이 말했다. 집사의 딸인 호록스 양이었다. 온 고을에 퍼진 추문의 장본인이자, 이제 퀸스크롤리에서 거의 무소불위의 권세를 누리는 여인이었다.\n\n그 리본 양의 등장과 부상은 그 고을과 그 집안 사람들의 경악 속에 진행되었다. 리본 양은 머드버리 지점 저축은행에 계좌를 텄다. 리본 양은 저택 하인들이 쓰라고 있는 조랑말 마차를 독차지하고 그것을 몰아 교회에 갔다. 하인들은 그녀의 기분 내키는 대로 해고되었다. 아직 저택에 남아, 자기가 가꾼 담장과 온실에 자부심을 느끼며, 사실상 자기가 도지를 부쳐 사우샘프턴에 그 소출을 내다 팔아 제법 괜찮은 생계를 꾸리던 스코틀랜드인 정원사는, 어느 화창한 아침 리본 양이 남쪽 담장에서 복숭아를 따 먹고 있는 것을 보고는 자기 소유물에 대한 이 침범에 항의하다가 따귀를 얻어맞았다. 그와 그의 스코틀랜드인 아내, 그리고 그의 스코틀랜드인 아이들, 퀸스크롤리의 유일하게 점잖은 거주자였던 그들은 가재도구와 세간을 챙겨 떠나도록 내몰렸고, 위풍당당하고 안락하던 정원은 황폐해지도록, 화단은 멋대로 씨앗을 맺도록 버려졌다. 가엾은 크롤리 부인의 장미 정원은 더없이 황량한 황무지가 되었다. 음산한 옛 하인 식당에는 하인 두세 명만이 추위에 떨고 있었다. 마구간과 사무실은 텅 비고 닫혀 반쯤 허물어졌다. 핏 경은 은둔 생활을 했고, 밤마다 (이제 그렇게 불리기 시작한) 집사 겸 가령(家令) 호록스와, 그리고 그 타락한 리본 양과 어울려 술을 진탕 마셨다. 그녀가 짐마차를 몰고 머드버리로 나가 자잘한 장사꾼들을 \"나리\"라고 불러 주던 시절과는 사정이 많이도 달라진 것이다. 부끄러워서였는지, 아니면 이웃들이 싫어서였는지, 퀸스크롤리의 늙은 냉소가는 이제 공원 정문 밖으로 좀처럼 나서지 않았다. 그는 대리인들과 다투었고 소작인들을 편지로 닦달했다. 그의 나날은 직접 서신을 처리하며 흘러갔다. 그와 거래할 일이 있는 변호사며 농장 관리인들은 리본 양을 통하지 않고는 그에게 닿을 수 없었으니, 그녀는 자기들이 드나드는 뒷문을 굽어보는 가정부 방 문간에서 그들을 맞이했다. 그리하여 준남작의 나날의 골칫거리는 늘어 갔고, 곤경은 그의 주위에서 점점 쌓여만 갔다.\n\n아버지가 노망이 들었다는 이런 소식들이 더없이 모범적이고 단정한 신사인 핏 크롤리에게 닿았을 때 그가 느낀 경악은 능히 짐작할 만하다. 그는 행여 그 리본 양이 자신의 두 번째 법적 장모로 선포되었다는 소식을 듣게 될까 봐 날마다 떨었다. 그 처음이자 마지막 방문 이후로, 핏의 우아하고 점잖은 집안에서는 아버지의 이름이 결코 입에 오르지 않았다. 그것은 그 집안의 해골이었고, 온 식구가 두려움과 침묵 속에 그 곁을 지나다녔다. 사우스다운 백작 부인은 더없이 자극적인 종교 소책자를 — 머리카락이 쭈뼛 곤두설 만한 소책자를 — 계속 우편 마차 편으로 저택 정문에 떨어뜨렸다. 목사관의 뷰트 부인은 밤마다 밖을 내다보며, 저택이 가려 서 있는 느릅나무 뒤로 하늘이 벌겋게 물들어 그 저택에 불이 난 것은 아닌지 살폈다. 그 집안의 오랜 벗인 G. 왑샷 경과 H. 퍼들스톤 경은 사계 법원에서 핏 경과 한자리에 앉으려 하지 않았고, 사우샘프턴 큰길에서 그 파렴치한이 더럽고 늙은 손을 그들에게 내미는데도 본 척도 하지 않고 지나쳤다. 무엇 하나 그에게는 통하지 않았다. 그는 두 손을 호주머니에 찔러 넣은 채 너털웃음을 터뜨리며 사두마차에 기어올랐다. 그는 사우스다운 부인의 소책자를 보고도 너털웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그리고 그는 자기 아들들을 보고도, 세상을 보고도, 그리고 리본 양이 화를 낼 때면 — 그런 일이 드물지 않았다 — 그녀를 보고도 웃어 댔다.\n\n호록스 양은 퀸스크롤리의 가정부로 자리를 잡고 그곳의 모든 하인을 대단한 위엄과 엄격함으로 다스렸다. 모든 하인은 그녀를 \"마님\" 혹은 \"부인\"이라고 부르도록 지시받았다. 그리고 새로 진급한 어린 하녀 하나는 그녀를 \"마님\"이라 한사코 불러 댔는데, 가정부 쪽에서 이를 나무라는 일은 전혀 없었다. \"더 나은 마님도 있었고, 더 못한 마님도 있었지, 헤스터.\" 이것이 손아랫사람의 이 아부에 대한 호록스 양의 대답이었다. 그렇게 그녀는 자기 아버지를 빼고 모두 위에 군림하며 다스렸는데, 아버지에게조차 상당히 거만하게 굴며, \"장차 준남작의 마님이 될 사람\"에게 너무 허물없이 굴지 말라고 경고했다. 정말이지 그녀는 그 드높은 인생의 배역을 스스로 대단히 흡족해하며 연습했고, 늙은 핏 경은 이를 재미있어했다. 그는 그녀의 거드름과 점잔을 보고 낄낄거렸고, 그녀가 위엄을 떠는 시늉이며 상류 생활을 흉내 내는 꼴을 보며 한 시간이고 내리 웃어 댔다. 그는 그녀가 귀부인 역을 하는 모습을 보는 것이 연극 한 편 못지않다고 단언하며, 첫 번째 크롤리 부인의 궁정 예복 한 벌을 그녀에게 입혀 놓고는, 그 옷이 그녀에게 기막히게 잘 어울린다고 (전적으로 호록스 양 본인도 같은 생각이었거니와) 떠벌리며, 당장 이 순간 사두마차에 태워 궁정으로 데려가겠다고 으름장을 놓았다. 그녀는 작고한 두 부인의 옷장을 마음대로 뒤졌고, 그들이 남기고 간 화려한 옷가지들을 자기 취향과 몸매에 맞도록 자르고 마구 잘라 냈다. 그리고 그들의 보석과 장신구도 차지하고 싶었으나, 늙은 준남작이 그것들을 자기 개인 함 속에 넣어 잠가 두었던 데다, 아무리 구슬리고 어르고 해도 그에게서 열쇠를 빼낼 수가 없었다. 그리고 이건 엄연한 사실인데, 그녀가 퀸스크롤리를 떠나고 얼마 후 이 여인의 글씨 연습장이 하나 발견되었으니, 거기에는 그녀가 남몰래 글쓰기 전반을, 특히 자기 이름을 크롤리 부인, 베치 호록스 부인, 엘리자베스 크롤리 부인 따위로 쓰는 법을 익히느라 무던히도 공을 들였음이 드러나 있었다.\n\n목사관의 선량한 사람들은 저택에 결코 발걸음을 하지 않았고 그 주인인 끔찍한 늙은 노망난이를 멀리했으나, 그래도 거기서 일어나는 모든 일을 빈틈없이 파악하고 있었고, 호록스 양 역시 학수고대하던 그 파국을 날마다 기다리고 있었다. 그러나 운명이 시샘하듯 끼어들어, 그토록 티 없는 사랑과 미덕에 마땅히 돌아갈 보상을 그녀가 받지 못하도록 막아 버렸다.\n\n어느 날 준남작은, 그가 농조로 부르듯 \"마님\"께서 응접실의 그 낡고 음도 맞지 않는 피아노 앞에 — 베키 샤프가 카드리유 곡을 친 이래 거의 손도 댄 적 없던 그 피아노 앞에 — 앉아 있는 모습을 불시에 보게 되었다. 그녀는 더없이 진지한 표정으로 피아노 앞에 앉아, 언젠가 들은 적 있는 음악을 흉내 내며 있는 힘껏 빽빽거리고 있었다. 새로 진급한 어린 부엌 하녀는 그 작업이 진행되는 동안 자기 안주인 곁에 서서 자못 황홀해하며 고개를 위아래로 까딱거리고 외쳐 댔다. \"어머, 마님, 정말 아름다워요.\" — 마치 진짜 응접실에 있는 점잖은 아첨꾼이라도 된 양 말이다.\n\n이 사건에 늙은 준남작은 늘 그러하듯 와르르 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그는 그날 저녁 내내 그 정황을 호록스에게 열두 번도 더 떠벌렸는데, 호록스 양으로서는 여간 낭패스러운 노릇이 아니었다. 그는 식탁을 무슨 악기라도 되는 양 둥둥 두들기며, 그녀가 노래하던 식을 흉내 내어 빽빽거렸다. 그는 그렇게 아름다운 목소리는 갈고닦아야 마땅하다고 단언하며, 그녀에게 성악 선생을 붙여 줘야겠다고 선언했는데, 정작 그녀는 이런 제안에서 우스운 구석이라곤 조금도 보지 못했다. 그날 밤 그는 기분이 한껏 들떠서, 자기 벗이자 집사인 자와 더불어 럼주에 물 탄 술을 어마어마하게 마셔 댔다. 아주 늦은 시각에야 그 충실한 벗이자 하인은 주인을 침실로 모셔 갔다.\n\n삼십 분쯤 후, 저택 안이 한바탕 분주하게 들썩였다. 외따로 떨어진 황량한 옛 저택, 그중 평소 주인이 쓰는 방이라곤 두세 칸뿐인 그 저택의 창에서 창으로 불빛들이 오갔다. 이윽고 한 소년이 조랑말을 타고 머드버리에 있는 의사 집을 향해 쏜살같이 달려갔다. 그리고 다시 한 시간 후에 (이 사실로 미루어 그 훌륭한 뷰트 크롤리 부인이 늘 큰 저택과 얼마나 빈틈없이 내통하고 있었는지를 헤아릴 수 있거니와) 나막신을 신고 외출용 두건을 쓴 그 부인과, 뷰트 크롤리 목사와, 그녀의 아들 제임스 크롤리가 목사관에서 공원을 가로질러 걸어와, 열린 현관문으로 저택에 들어섰다.\n\n그들은 현관과 작은 떡갈나무 응접실을 지났는데, 그 응접실 탁자 위에는 핏 경의 술판에 쓰인 술잔 세 개와 빈 럼주 병이 놓여 있었다. 그리고 그 방을 지나 핏 경의 서재로 들어섰다. 거기서 그들은 그 죄 많은 리본을 두른 호록스 양이, 미친 듯한 기색으로 열쇠 꾸러미를 들고 장롱과 서랍장들을 열어 보려 애쓰고 있는 것을 발견했다. 검은 두건 아래로 자그마한 뷰트 부인의 두 눈이 번뜩이며 자신을 노려보자, 그녀는 공포의 비명을 지르며 열쇠 꾸러미를 떨어뜨렸다.\n\n\"저것 좀 봐라, 제임스, 그리고 크롤리 씨.\" 뷰트 부인이, 겁에 질린 그 검은 눈의 죄 많은 계집의 모습을 가리키며 외쳤다.\n\n\"나리께서 주신 거예요, 나리께서 주셨다고요!\" 그녀가 울부짖었다.\n\n\"네게 주셨다고, 이 타락한 것아!\" 뷰트 부인이 비명을 질렀다. \"증인이 되어 주세요, 크롤리 씨. 우리는 이 쓸모없는 여자가 당신 형님의 재산을 훔치는 현장을 잡은 겁니다. 그러니 이 여자는 교수형을 당할 거예요, 내가 늘 그렇게 될 거라고 말했듯이 말이지요.\"\n\n베치 호록스는 잔뜩 기가 죽어 무릎을 털썩 꿇고는 울음을 터뜨렸다. 그러나 진정으로 선량한 여인을 아는 이들은, 그런 여인이 용서를 서두르는 법이 없으며, 적이 굴욕당하는 것이 그 영혼에는 하나의 승리라는 것을 잘 안다.\n\n\"종을 울려라, 제임스.\" 뷰트 부인이 말했다. \"사람들이 올 때까지 계속 울려.\" 그 버려진 옛 저택에 살던 서너 명의 하인이 그 요란하게 거듭되는 종소리에 곧 모여들었다.\n\n\"저 여자를 광에 가둬라.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이 여자가 핏 경의 재산을 훔치는 현장을 잡았으니까. 크롤리 씨, 당신은 이 여자 구속영장을 작성해 주세요. 그리고 베도스, 자네는 아침에 짐마차로 이 여자를 사우샘프턴 감옥까지 몰고 가게.\"\n\n\"여보,\" 치안판사이자 목사인 그가 끼어들었다. \"이 여자는 그저—\"\n\n\"수갑은 없는 게냐?\" 뷰트 부인이 나막신을 신은 발을 쿵쿵 구르며 계속했다. \"예전엔 수갑이 있었는데. 이것의 그 망할 아비는 어디 있어?\"\n\n\"나리께서 진짜로 주셨다니까요.\" 가엾은 베치가 여전히 울부짖었다. \"안 그래, 헤스터? 네가 봤잖아 — 너도 알잖아 — 핏 경께서 나한테 주신 거 말이야, 한참 전에 — 머드버리 장날 다음 날에. 내가 이걸 갖고 싶어서 그런 게 아니야. 내 게 아니라고 생각하면 가져가라고.\" 그러면서 이 가련한 것은 호주머니에서 커다란 모조 보석 박힌 구두 버클 한 쌍을 꺼냈는데, 그것은 그녀의 탐심을 자극했던 물건으로, 서재 책장 하나에 놓여 있던 것을 방금 슬쩍 가로챈 것이었다.\n\n\"세상에, 베치, 어쩌면 그렇게 못된 거짓말을 할 수가 있어!\" 얼마 전에야 진급한 어린 부엌 하녀 헤스터가 말했다. \"그것도 마님 크롤리께, 저렇게 선량하고 친절하신 분께, 그리고 목사님께 (무릎을 굽혀 절하며) 말이야. 제 상자는 전부 다 뒤져 보셔도 좋아요, 마님, 정말이에요. 그리고 여기 제 열쇠도 있어요, 저는 정직한 아이거든요, 비록 가난한 부모 밑에서 태어나 구빈원에서 자라긴 했지만 말이에요. 그리고 만약 마님께서 골라 가지신 그 모든 옷가지 중에서 변변찮은 레이스 한 조각이나 비단 양말 한 짝이라도 제게서 나오거든, 다시는 교회에 안 가도 좋아요.\"\n\n\"네 열쇠나 내놔, 이 닳고 닳은 화냥년아.\" 두건을 쓴 그 덕망 높은 자그마한 부인이 쉭쉭거리며 내뱉었다.\n\n\"그리고 여기 촛불 있어요, 마님, 그리고 괜찮으시다면, 마님, 제가 저 여자 방을 보여 드릴게요, 마님, 그리고 가정부 방에 있는 장롱도요, 마님, 저 여자가 잔뜩 쌓아 둔 물건들이 거기 있거든요, 마님.\" 의욕에 찬 어린 헤스터가 연신 무릎을 굽혀 대며 외쳤다.\n\n\"제발 입 좀 다물려무나. 저것이 쓰는 방쯤은 나도 똑똑히 알고 있으니까. 브라운 부인, 미안하지만 나와 함께 가 주시오. 그리고 베도스, 자네는 저 여자에게서 눈을 떼지 말게.\" 뷰트 부인이 촛불을 집어 들며 말했다. \"크롤리 씨, 당신은 위층으로 올라가서 저들이 당신의 가엾은 형님을 죽이고 있는 건 아닌지 살펴보는 게 좋겠어요.\" — 그러고는 그 두건은 브라운 부인을 거느린 채, 그녀가 사실대로 말한 대로 자기가 똑똑히 알고 있는 그 방으로 걸어갔다.\n\n뷰트는 위층으로 올라갔다가, 머드버리에서 온 의사와, 의자에 앉은 주인 위로 굽어선 겁에 질린 호록스를 발견했다. 그들은 핏 크롤리 경의 피를 뽑으려 애쓰고 있었다.\n\n이른 아침에, 만사를 손수 지휘하며 밤새도록 늙은 준남작을 지켜본 목사 부인이 핏 크롤리 씨에게 급보를 띄웠다. 그는 일종의 소생 상태로 돌아와 있었다. 말은 못 했으나 사람을 알아보는 듯했다. 뷰트 부인은 결연히 그의 침상 곁을 지켰다. 그 자그마한 여인은 잠 따위는 조금도 원치 않는 듯, 의사가 안락의자에서 코를 고는 동안에도 그 불타는 검은 두 눈을 한 번도 감지 않았다. 호록스는 자기 권위를 내세우며 주인을 돌보려 몇 차례 거칠게 애써 보았으나, 뷰트 부인은 그를 술 취한 늙은 망나니라 부르며, 다시는 이 집에 얼굴을 비치지 말라고, 안 그러면 그 못된 딸년처럼 유형에 처해질 거라고 호통쳤다.\n\n그녀의 서슬에 겁먹은 그는 제임스 씨가 있는 떡갈나무 응접실로 슬그머니 내려갔다. 제임스 씨는 거기 놓인 술병을 기울여 보고 술이 한 방울도 없자, 호록스 씨에게 럼주 한 병을 더 가져오라 시켰다. 호록스가 깨끗한 잔과 함께 그것을 가져오자, 목사와 그 아들은 거기 자리를 잡고 앉아, 호록스에게 당장 이 순간 열쇠를 내려놓고 다시는 얼굴을 비치지 말라고 명했다.\n\n이런 처사에 기가 꺾인 호록스는 열쇠를 내놓았고, 그와 그의 딸은 밤을 틈타 소리 없이 슬그머니 빠져나가 퀸스크롤리 저택의 점유권을 포기했다.\n\n크롤리 가문의 후계자는 이 참변이 있은 뒤 머지않아 본가에 당도했고, 이제부터는 퀸스크롤리를 다스렸다고 해도 좋을 것이다. 늙은 준남작은 그 후로도 여러 달을 더 살아 있었지만 지력도 언어능력도 끝내 온전히 되찾지 못했으므로, 영지의 통치권은 그 장남에게로 넘어갔던 것이다. 피트가 마주한 영지의 형편은 참으로 기묘한 것이었다. 피트 준남작 어른은 늘 무언가를 사들이고 또 저당 잡히는 사람이었다. 거래하는 사무 대리인이 스무 명이나 되었고, 그 하나하나와 다투었다. 소작인 전부와도 다투며 그들과 소송을 벌였고, 변호사들과도 소송을 벌였으며, 자신이 지분을 가진 광산 회사며 부두 회사와도 소송을 벌였고, 사업으로 얽힌 모든 사람과 소송을 벌였다. 이 난마처럼 얽힌 곤경을 풀어내고 영지를 깨끗이 정리하는 일은 펌퍼니켈의 그 질서정연하고 끈기 있는 외교가에게나 어울릴 만한 과업이었고, 그는 어마어마한 근면함으로 그 일에 착수했다. 물론 그의 온 가족이 퀸스크롤리로 옮겨 왔고, 사우스다운 부인 역시 당연히 함께 따라왔다. 그녀는 목사 코앞에서 교구를 개종시키는 일에 착수했고, 자신을 따르는 비국교 성직자들까지 불러들여 성난 뷰트 부인을 경악하게 만들었다. 피트 준남작은 퀸스크롤리 목사직의 매각 계약을 아직 맺지 않은 터였다. 그 자리가 비게 되면 부인은 그 임명권을 자기 수중에 넣어 어느 젊은 피후견인을 목사관에 앉힐 작정이었는데, 이 문제에 대해서는 외교가다운 피트가 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n베치 호럭스 양에 관한 뷰트 부인의 의도는 끝내 실행에 옮겨지지 못했고, 그녀는 사우샘프턴 감옥을 찾아갈 일이 없었다. 베치와 그 아비는, 그 아비가 마을의 크롤리암스 여관을 피트 준남작에게서 빌려 차지하게 되자 본가를 떠났다. 그 전직 집사는 그곳에 자그마한 자유보유지까지 손에 넣었고, 그 덕분에 자치구 선거에서 투표권을 한 표 얻었다. 목사도 그런 투표권을 한 표 가지고 있었으니, 이 두 표와 다른 네 표가 퀸스크롤리에서 두 명의 의원을 선출하는 선거인단을 이루었다.\n\n목사관과 본가의 숙녀들 사이에는, 적어도 젊은 축들 사이에서는 겉으로나마 예의가 유지되고 있었다. 뷰트 부인과 사우스다운 부인은 만나기만 하면 한바탕 싸움이 벌어지지 않는 법이 없어서 차츰 서로 만나는 일을 그만두게 되었던 것이다. 목사관의 숙녀들이 본가의 사촌들을 찾아올 때면 사우스다운 부인은 자기 방에 틀어박혀 있었다. 아마 피트는 장모의 이런 이따금의 부재가 그리 못마땅하지만은 않았을 것이다. 그는 빙키 가문이 세상에서 가장 위대하고 가장 현명하며 가장 흥미로운 가문이라고 믿었고, 부인과 그의 고모는 오랫동안 그를 손아귀에 쥐고 있었다. 그러나 때로 그는 부인이 자신을 지나치게 좌지우지한다고 느끼는 것이었다. 젊다고 여겨지는 것이야 의심할 바 없이 듣기 좋은 말이지만, 마흔여섯 살이 되어서도 어린아이 취급을 당하는 일은 이따금 자존심 상하는 노릇이었다. 그렇지만 제인 부인은 모든 것을 어머니에게 양보했다. 그녀가 제 아이들을 어여삐 여기는 것은 오직 남몰래일 때뿐이었으니, 사우스다운 부인의 그 잡다한 일거리며, 장관들과의 회담이며, 아프리카와 아시아와 오스트랄라시아 등지의 온갖 선교사들과 주고받는 서신이 그 존엄하신 백작 부인을 워낙 바쁘게 만든 덕분에, 손녀인 어린 마틸다와 손자인 피트 크롤리 도련님에게 쏟을 시간이 거의 없었던 것은 그녀로서는 다행이었다. 손자는 허약한 아이여서, 사우스다운 부인이 엄청난 양의 감홍을 먹여야 겨우 목숨을 부지할 수 있을 정도였다.\n\n피트 준남작 어른으로 말하면, 그는 예전에 크롤리 부인이 숨을 거둔 바로 그 방으로 물러갔고, 그곳에서 출세할 기회를 잡은 처녀 헤스터 양의 한결같은 보살핌과 정성스러운 시중을 받았다. 보수를 두둑이 받는 간병인의 사랑과 충직함과 한결같음에 견줄 만한 것이 세상에 또 어디 있겠는가? 그들은 베개를 매만지고 칡뿌리 죽을 쑨다. 밤중에 일어나고, 환자의 불평과 짜증을 견딘다. 문밖에 해가 비치는 것을 보고도 나가고 싶어 하지 않는다. 안락의자에서 잠을 자고 끼니는 홀로 때운다. 기나긴 저녁 시간을 아무것도 하지 않고 잿불을 들여다보거나 단지 속에서 보글거리는 환자의 마실 거리를 지켜보며 보낸다. 주간 신문 한 부를 일주일 내내 읽고, 로의 「엄숙한 부르심」이나 「인간의 온전한 본분」이 일 년치 읽을거리로 충분하다. 그런데도 우리는 그들의 친척이 일주일에 한 번 찾아올 때 빨래 바구니에 진 한 병이 몰래 숨겨져 들어온다는 이유로 그들과 다투는 것이다. 숙녀 여러분, 사랑하는 이를 일 년 내내 병구완하며 버텨 낼 남자의 사랑이 어디 있단 말인가? 반면 간병인은 분기마다 십 파운드만 주면 곁을 지켜 줄 텐데, 우리는 그조차 너무 많이 쳐준다고 여기는 것이다. 적어도 크롤리는 자기 아버지인 준남작을 한결같이 시중든 헤스터 양에게 그 절반을 치르는 일을 두고도 어지간히 투덜거렸다.\n\n볕이 좋은 날이면 이 노인은 의자에 실려 테라스로 나가곤 했다. 그 의자란 다름 아닌 크롤리 양이 브라이턴에서 쓰던 것으로, 사우스다운 부인의 여러 세간과 함께 그곳에서 퀸스크롤리로 옮겨 온 것이었다. 제인 부인은 늘 노인 곁에서 함께 걸었고, 분명 노인이 아끼는 사람이었다. 노인은 그녀가 들어오면 몇 번이고 고개를 끄덕이며 미소 지었고, 그녀가 떠나려 하면 알아들을 수 없는 만류의 신음을 흘리는 것이었다. 문이 그녀의 뒤로 닫히면 그는 울며 흐느꼈다. 그러면 안주인이 곁에 있을 때면 늘 더없이 상냥하고 부드럽던 헤스터의 얼굴과 태도가 즉시 돌변하여, 그를 향해 인상을 쓰고 주먹을 불끈 쥐며 \"입 좀 다물어요, 이 멍청한 늙은 바보야\" 하고 소리치고는, 그가 그토록 바라보기 좋아하던 난롯불에서 그의 의자를 홱 돌려놓는 것이었다. 그러면 노인은 더욱 서럽게 울었다. 칠십 년이 넘는 세월의 교활함과 발버둥과 술과 모략과 죄와 이기심 끝에 남은 것이 이것뿐이었으니, 갓난아기처럼 침대에 누였다 일으켜졌다 하고, 씻기고 먹여지는, 훌쩍거리는 늙은 백치 하나였던 것이다.\n\n이윽고 간병인의 소임이 끝나는 날이 왔다. 어느 날 이른 아침, 피트 크롤리가 서재에서 집사며 영지 관리인의 장부를 들여다보고 있는데 문 두드리는 소리가 났다. 헤스터가 무릎을 굽혀 절을 하며 나타나더니 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"송구합니다만, 피트 준남작님, 어르신께서 오늘 아침에 돌아가셨습니다, 준남작님. 제가 어르신 죽에 넣어 드릴 토스트를 굽고 있었습죠, 준남작님, 매일 아침 여섯 시면 어김없이 드시던 그 죽 말입니다요, 준남작님, 그런데 신음 비슷한 소리가 들리지 뭡니까, 준남작님, 그래서, 그래서, 그래서요.\" 그녀는 다시 한 번 무릎을 굽혀 절했다.\n\n피트의 창백한 얼굴이 새빨갛게 달아오른 것은 무엇 때문이었을까? 그가 마침내 피트 준남작이 되어 의회에 자리를 얻고, 어쩌면 앞날의 영예까지 내다보게 되었기 때문이었을까? \"이제 가진 돈으로 영지를 깨끗이 정리해야지.\" 그는 그렇게 생각하며 영지에 걸린 부담과 자신이 해 나갈 개량 사업들을 재빨리 계산해 보았다. 그는 그 전까지는 고모의 돈에 손대지 않으려 했으니, 혹여 피트 준남작이 회복하기라도 하면 그 지출이 헛수고가 될까 염려해서였다.\n\n본가와 목사관의 차양은 모두 내려졌다. 교회 종이 조종을 울렸고, 성단소에는 검은 천이 드리워졌다. 뷰트 크롤리는 사냥 모임에 가는 대신 퍼들스턴으로 가서 조용히 식사를 했고, 거기서 사람들은 포도주를 기울이며 고인이 된 그의 형과 젊은 피트 준남작에 대해 이야기를 나누었다. 이 무렵 머드베리의 마구 장수에게 시집간 베치 양은 한참을 울었다. 집안 주치의가 말을 타고 찾아와 정중히 조의를 표하고 부인들의 안부를 여쭈었다. 머드베리에서도 크롤리암스 여관에서도 그 죽음이 입에 오르내렸는데, 여관 주인은 근래 들어 목사와 화해한 사이였고, 목사는 이따금 그 응접실에 들러 호럭스의 순한 맥주를 맛보는 것으로 알려져 있었다.\n\n\"제가 당신 동생에게 편지를 쓸까요, 아니면 당신이 쓰시겠어요?\" 제인 부인이 남편인 피트 준남작에게 물었다.\n\n\"당연히 내가 써야지.\" 피트 준남작이 말했다. \"장례식에 초대해야겠소. 그게 도리에 맞는 일이오.\"\n\n\"그리고, 그리고, 로던 부인도요.\" 제인 부인이 조심스레 말했다.\n\n\"제인!\" 사우스다운 부인이 말했다. \"어찌 그런 생각을 다 한단 말이냐?\"\n\n\"로던 부인도 당연히 청해야지요.\" 피트 준남작이 단호하게 말했다.\n\n\"내가 이 집에 있는 동안은 안 됩니다!\" 사우스다운 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"부인께서는 제가 이 집안의 가장이라는 점을 부디 기억해 주시기 바랍니다.\" 피트 준남작이 대꾸했다. \"제인, 미안하지만 로던 크롤리 부인에게 편지를 써서 이 슬픈 자리에 와 줄 것을 청해 주시오.\"\n\n\"제인, 펜에 손도 대지 못하게 하겠다!\" 백작 부인이 소리쳤다.\n\n\"제가 이 집안의 가장인 줄로 압니다.\" 피트 준남작이 거듭 말했다. \"그리고 부인께서 이 집을 떠나시게 될 어떤 사정이라도 생긴다면 제가 무척 유감스럽겠지만, 송구하나마 저는 제가 옳다고 보는 대로 이 집을 다스려 나가야겠습니다.\"\n\n사우스다운 부인은 「맥베스」의 맥베스 부인 역을 맡은 시던스 부인만큼이나 위풍당당하게 일어서서 자기 마차에 말을 매라고 분부했다. 아들과 딸이 제 집에서 자신을 내쫓는다면, 그녀는 어딘가 외로운 곳에 가서 슬픔을 감추고 그들이 더 나은 생각으로 회개하기를 기도하겠노라고 했다.\n\n\"저희는 어머니를 집에서 내쫓는 게 아니에요, 어머니.\" 소심한 제인 부인이 애원하듯 말했다.\n\n\"그리스도교도 숙녀라면 결코 마주해서는 안 될 그런 손님을 이 집에 부르겠다니, 나는 내일 아침 내 말을 부르겠다.\"\n\n\"미안하지만 받아 적어 주시오, 제인. 내가 부르는 대로요.\" 피트 준남작이 자리에서 일어나 전람회에 걸린 어느 신사의 초상화처럼 명령조의 자세를 취하며 말했다. \"자, 적으시오. '퀸스크롤리에서, 1822년 9월 14일. 친애하는 동생에게.'\"\n\n이 단호하고도 무시무시한 말을 듣자, 사위 쪽에서 행여 약해지거나 흔들리는 기색을 보일까 기다리고 있던 맥베스 부인은 자리에서 일어나 겁먹은 얼굴로 서재를 떠났다. 제인 부인은 어머니를 따라가 달래 주고 싶다는 듯 남편을 올려다보았으나, 피트는 아내에게 움직이지 말라고 일렀다.\n\n\"장모님은 떠나지 않으실 거요.\" 그가 말했다. \"브라이턴의 집을 세놓으셨고 지난 반년 치 배당금도 다 써 버리셨거든. 여관에 묵는 백작 부인이라니 망한 신세나 다름없지. 나는 오래전부터 이런 결정적인 한 걸음을 내딛을 기회를 기다려 왔다오, 여보. 당신도 알다시피 한 집안에 우두머리가 둘이나 있을 수는 없는 노릇이니까. 자, 미안하지만 받아쓰던 것을 마저 합시다. '친애하는 동생에게, 내가 우리 가족에게 전해야 할 의무를 진 이 슬픈 소식은 진작부터 예견되었을 줄로—'\" 운운.\n\n요컨대 피트는 자기 왕국에 올랐고, 그가 스스로 여기기로는 행운으로, 아니 차라리 그만한 자격으로, 다른 친척들이 기대했던 재산을 거의 모조리 거머쥐게 되었으니, 가족을 너그럽고 점잖게 대우하고 퀸스크롤리를 다시금 번듯한 집안으로 만들겠다고 작정했다. 그가 그 집안의 우두머리가 되리라는 생각은 그를 흐뭇하게 했다. 그는 자신의 뛰어난 재능과 지위가 머지않아 이 주에서 가져다줄 막대한 영향력을 활용하여 동생에게 자리를 마련해 주고 사촌들을 그럭저럭 살게 해 줄 작정이었는데, 그들이 바라던 모든 것의 소유주가 다름 아닌 자신이라는 생각에 어쩌면 양심의 가책이 살짝 따끔거렸는지도 모른다. 사나흘간 다스리는 동안 그의 태도는 달라졌고 그의 계획은 확고히 굳어졌다. 그는 공정하고 정직하게 다스리고, 사우스다운 부인을 끌어내리며, 피붙이인 모든 친척과 더없이 다정한 사이로 지내겠노라 마음먹었다.\n\n그리하여 그는 동생 로던에게 보낼 편지를 받아쓰게 했다. 더없이 심오한 견해를 담고 더없이 긴 낱말들로 짜인, 엄숙하고도 공들인 편지였으니, 남편의 분부를 받아 받아 적던 그 순박한 작은 비서는 경탄을 금치 못했다. \"이이가 하원에 들어가면 얼마나 굉장한 웅변가가 될까.\" 그녀는 생각했다(이 점에 대해서나 사우스다운 부인의 횡포에 대해서나, 피트는 이따금 잠자리에서 아내에게 슬쩍 운을 떼곤 했다). \"얼마나 현명하고 선량한지, 우리 남편은 정말 천재야! 좀 차가운 사람인 줄로만 알았는데, 이렇게나 선량하고 이렇게나 천재일 줄이야!\"\n\n사실인즉 피트 크롤리는 이 편지를 깜짝 놀랄 아내에게 들려주는 것이 적당하다고 여기기 한참 전에, 외교가다운 은밀함으로 한 자 한 자 깊고 완벽하게 익히고 또 연구하여 죄다 외워 두었던 것이다.\n\n이리하여 이 편지는, 검은 테두리와 봉인을 큼지막하게 두른 채, 피트 크롤리 준남작의 손을 떠나 런던에 있는 그 동생 대령에게로 부쳐졌다. 로던 크롤리는 그것을 받고도 그저 반쯤만 기뻐했다. \"그 따분한 동네까지 내려가서 뭐 한단 말이야?\" 그는 생각했다. \"식사 끝나고 피트와 단둘이 앉아 있는 건 견딜 수가 없고, 오가는 말 삯만 해도 이십 파운드는 들 텐데.\"\n\n그는 온갖 골치 아픈 일이 생길 때마다 그러했듯이, 이 편지를 들고 위층 침실로 베키를 찾아갔다. 매일 아침 손수 만들어 가져다주는 그 코코아와 함께였다.\n\n그는 아침 식사가 담긴 쟁반과 편지를 화장대 위에 내려놓았는데, 베키는 그 앞에 앉아 노란 머리칼을 빗고 있었다. 그녀는 검은 테를 두른 그 편지를 집어 들어 읽고 나더니, 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 \"만세!\" 하고 외치며 그 쪽지를 머리 위로 빙빙 휘둘렀다.\n\n\"만세라고?\" 로던이, 황갈색 머리칼을 헝클어뜨린 채 펄럭이는 플란넬 실내복 차림으로 깡충깡충 뛰어다니는 그 자그마한 사람을 의아하게 바라보며 말했다. \"우리한테 남긴 거라곤 아무것도 없잖아, 베키. 내 몫은 성년이 됐을 때 이미 받았는걸.\"\n\n\"당신은 영영 성년이 될 일이 없는 사람이에요, 이 어리석은 양반아.\" 베키가 대꾸했다. \"어서 브뤼노이 부인네로 달려가요. 상복이 있어야 하니까. 그리고 당신 모자에 검은 상장(喪章)을 두르고 검은 조끼도 마련해요. 아마 한 벌도 없을 거예요. 내일 집으로 가져오라고 시켜요. 그래야 목요일에 떠날 수 있으니까.\"\n\n\"갈 생각인 건 아니지?\" 로던이 끼어들었다.\n\n\"당연히 갈 생각이죠. 내년에 제인 부인이 나를 궁정에 소개시켜 줘야 하니까요. 당신 형이 당신에게 의회 자리 하나를 마련해 줘야 하니까요, 이 어리석은 양반아. 스테인 경이 당신 표와 형님 표를 얻도록 해야 하니까요, 여보. 그래서 당신이 아일랜드 차관이나 서인도 제도 총독이 되든가, 아니면 재무관이나 영사나 뭐 그런 자리에 오르도록요.\"\n\n\"역마 삯이 어마어마하게 들 텐데.\" 로던이 투덜거렸다.\n\n\"사우스다운의 마차를 빌릴 수도 있겠죠. 그분도 가문의 친척이니 장례식에 참석하는 게 마땅하니까. 하지만 아니에요, 우리는 역마차를 타고 가는 게 좋겠어요. 그쪽이 더 마음에 들 거예요. 더 겸손해 보이거든요.\"\n\n\"로디도 당연히 가는 거지?\" 대령이 물었다.\n\n\"그럴 리가요. 자리 하나를 더 사느라 돈을 왜 써요? 그 애는 너무 커서 당신과 나 사이에 끼여 갈 수도 없어요. 육아실에 그냥 두고, 브리그스더러 검은 옷이나 하나 지어 입히라고 해요. 당신이나 가서 내가 시키는 대로 하세요. 그리고 하인 스파크스한테는, 늙은 피트 준남작이 죽었으니 일이 정리되면 당신이 상당한 재산을 물려받게 될 거라고 일러두는 게 좋겠어요. 그러면 그가 래글스한테 그 말을 전할 텐데, 래글스가 돈을 갚으라고 졸라 대고 있으니, 가엾은 래글스에게 위안이 되겠죠.\" 그러고서 베키는 코코아를 홀짝이기 시작했다.\n\n저녁이 되어 충직한 스테인 경이 당도했을 때, 그는 베키와 그녀의 말동무가—다름 아닌 우리의 벗 브리그스였다—이 슬픈 자리에 쓸 만한 온갖 검은 천을 자르고 뜯고 가위질하고 찢느라 분주한 모습을 보았다.\n\n\"브리그스 양과 저는 우리 아버님의 죽음에 슬픔과 비탄에 잠겨 있답니다.\" 레베카가 말했다. \"피트 크롤리 준남작이 돌아가셨거든요, 경. 오전 내내 머리칼을 쥐어뜯다가, 이제는 낡은 옷가지를 쥐어뜯고 있지요.\"\n\n\"오, 레베카, 어쩌면 그렇게—\" 브리그스는 눈을 치켜뜨며 그렇게밖에 말하지 못했다.\n\n\"오, 레베카, 어쩌면 그렇게—\" 경도 따라 말했다. \"그래, 그 늙은 악당이 죽었단 말이지? 패만 좀 더 잘 썼더라면 귀족이 될 수도 있었을 텐데. 피트 씨가 거의 다 만들어 줄 뻔했는데, 늘 엉뚱한 때에 변절을 했단 말이야. 어쩜 그렇게 늙은 실레노스 같았는지!\"\n\n\"하마터면 내가 그 실레노스의 미망인이 될 뻔했지요.\" 레베카가 말했다. \"브리그스 양, 기억나지 않아요? 당신이 문틈으로 들여다봤을 때 늙은 피트 준남작이 내 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 있었던 거 말이에요.\" 우리의 옛 벗 브리그스 양은 이 회상에 얼굴이 몹시 붉어졌고, 스테인 경이 그녀더러 아래층에 내려가 차 한 잔을 끓여 오라고 분부했을 때 적이 안도했다.\n\n브리그스는 레베카가 자기 순결과 평판을 지킬 파수꾼으로 마련해 둔 집 지키는 개였다. 크롤리 양은 그녀에게 자그마한 연금을 남겼었다. 그녀로서는 자신에게도 모든 이에게도 다정한 제인 부인과 함께 크롤리 가문에 그대로 머무는 것으로 흡족했을 것이다. 그러나 사우스다운 부인은 예의가 허락하는 한 최대한 빠르게 가엾은 브리그스를 내보냈고, 피트는(이십 년 남짓 크롤리 양의 충직한 하인 노릇을 했을 뿐인 여자에게 고인이 된 친척이 청하지도 않은 후의를 베푼 탓에 자신이 큰 손해를 봤다고 여겼다) 그 노부인이 권한을 행사하는 데 아무 이의도 달지 않았다. 보울스와 퍼킨 역시 유산과 더불어 해고 통지를 받았고, 그런 부류의 관례에 따라 결혼하여 하숙집을 차렸다.\n\n브리그스는 시골에 사는 친척들과 함께 살아 보려 했으나, 익숙해진 더 나은 사교계를 겪은 뒤라 그 시도가 헛된 것임을 깨달았다. 어느 시골 읍내의 영세 상인인 브리그스의 친지들은, 크롤리 양의 친척들이 그 부인의 유산을 두고 다투었던 것 못지않게, 아니 그보다 더 노골적으로 브리그스 양의 연 사십 파운드를 두고 다투었던 것이다. 급진파 모자 장수 겸 식료품상인 브리그스의 오라비는, 누이가 제 가게에 물건을 채워 넣을 자본의 일부를 대주려 하지 않는다는 이유로 그녀를 돈푼깨나 있다고 거드름 피우는 귀족 나부랭이라고 불렀다. 사실 그녀는 그렇게 해 주었을 공산이 컸지만, 다른 예배당에 다니는 그 모자 장수 겸 식료품상과 사이가 틀어진 자매—비국교파 구두장이의 부인 되는 이가—오라비가 파산 직전에 몰려 있음을 일러 주고는 한동안 브리그스를 차지하고 말았다. 그 비국교파 구두장이는 브리그스 양이 자기 아들을 대학에 보내 신사로 만들어 주기를 바랐다. 두 집안은 작당하여 그녀의 사사로운 저축 가운데 상당 부분을 우려냈고, 마침내 그녀는 양쪽의 저주를 등에 진 채 런던으로 달아났으니, 하녀살이가 자유보다 한없이 덜 고된 것이라 여기고 다시금 그 일자리를 구하기로 마음먹은 것이었다. 그리하여 \"더없이 상냥한 몸가짐에 최상류 사교계에 익숙한 숙녀가 간절히 바라는 바는\" 운운하는 광고를 신문에 내고, 그녀는 하프문 거리의 보울스 씨 집에 거처를 정한 채 그 광고의 결과를 기다렸다.\n\n레베카와 마주친 것이 바로 그러한 연유에서였다. 어느 날 로던 부인의 맵시 좋은 작은 마차와 조랑말들이 거리를 휙 달려 내려가는데, 마침 브리그스 양이 광고를 여섯 번째로 내려고 시내의 타임스 사무소까지 고단하게 걸어갔다가 지친 몸으로 보울스 씨의 문 앞에 막 다다른 참이었다. 레베카가 고삐를 쥐고 있다가 단번에 그 상냥한 몸가짐의 숙녀를 알아보고는, 앞서 보았듯 더없이 쾌활한 여자인 데다 브리그스에게 호감을 품고 있던 터라, 문간 계단 앞에서 조랑말을 멈추고 고삐를 마부에게 넘긴 뒤 훌쩍 뛰어내려, 그 상냥한 몸가짐의 여인이 옛 벗을 본 충격에서 미처 헤어나기도 전에 브리그스의 두 손을 덥석 붙잡았다.\n\n브리그스는 울었고 베키는 한참을 웃었으며, 복도로 들어서자마자 그 숙녀에게 입을 맞추었다. 그러고는 거기서 보울스 부인의 앞쪽 응접실로 들어갔으니, 붉은 모린 천 커튼과 둥근 거울이 있고 그 위에 사슬에 묶인 독수리 장식이 얹혀 있어, 창가에 놓인 \"셋방 있음\"이라고 적힌 안내판의 뒷면을 내려다보는 방이었다.\n\n브리그스는 자신의 온 사연을 털어놓았는데, 그 사이사이 그처럼 여린 천성의 여인들이 옛 지인을 맞거나 길거리의 우연한 해후를 대할 때면 으레 그러하듯, 도무지 그럴 까닭이 없는 흐느낌과 놀라움의 감탄사가 섞여 들었다. 사람들이야 날마다 다른 사람들과 마주치는 법이건만, 그중에는 한사코 그것을 기적인 양 여기려 드는 이들이 있다. 게다가 여인들이란 서로 미워했던 사이라도 막상 만나면 울음부터 터뜨리며, 마지막으로 다투었던 그때를 한탄하고 떠올리는 것이다. 그리하여 한마디로 브리그스는 자신의 온 사연을 털어놓았고, 베키는 늘 그러하듯 천연덕스럽고 솔직한 태도로 제 인생 이야기를 들려주었다.\n\n전에 퍼킨이었던 보울스 부인이 복도로 나와, 앞쪽 응접실에서 벌어지는 그 신경질적인 훌쩍거림과 키득거림을 못마땅한 얼굴로 엿들었다. 베키는 한 번도 그녀의 마음에 든 적이 없었다. 이 부부가 런던에 자리를 잡은 뒤로 그들은 래글스 집안의 옛 친구들과 자주 어울렸는데, 대령네 살림에 대한 래글스의 이야기가 영 마음에 들지 않았던 것이다. \"난 그자를 못 믿겠네, 래그.\" 보울스가 한마디 했다. 그리하여 그의 아내는, 로던 부인이 응접실에서 나오자 그저 더없이 떨떠름한 절로 그 부인을 맞이했을 뿐이었다. 그 손가락은 마치 소시지를 여러 개 늘어놓은 듯 차갑고 생기 없었으니, 한사코 이 전직 하녀와 악수를 하려 드는 로던 부인을 향해 마지못해 손을 내밀었던 것이다. 부인은 광고판 바로 아래 창가에 매달려 고개를 끄덕이는 브리그스 양을 향해 더없이 다정한 미소로 고갯짓을 보내며 피커딜리 쪽으로 휙 내달렸고, 다음 순간 그녀는 대여섯의 멋쟁이들이 마차 뒤를 천천히 달려 따라오는 가운데 공원에 들어서 있었다.\n\n그녀는 옛 벗이 처한 형편을 알게 되었고, 크롤리 양에게서 받은 짭짤한 유산이 있어 우리의 그 숙녀에게 봉급 따위는 문제가 되지 않는다는 것을 알게 되자, 베키는 즉시 그녀와 관련하여 자비로운 가정사 계획을 몇 가지 세웠다. 그녀의 살림에 딱 어울릴 만한 말동무였던 것이다. 베키는 바로 그날 저녁 함께 식사하자며 브리그스를 초대했으니, 그 자리에서 베키의 사랑스러운 어린 로던도 볼 수 있다고 했다.\n\n보울스 부인은 자기 하숙인에게 사자 굴로 함부로 들어가지 말라고 경고했다. \"거기 들어가면 후회하실 거예요, 브리그스 양, 내 말을 잘 새겨들으세요. 내 이름이 보울스인 게 틀림없는 것만큼이나 분명한 일이라고요.\" 그러자 브리그스는 무척 조심하겠노라 다짐했다. 그 다짐의 결말인즉, 그녀는 이튿날 한 주가 지나 로던 부인과 함께 살러 들어갔고, 채 반년도 지나기 전에 로던 크롤리에게 육백 파운드를 연금 조건으로 빌려주었다는 것이다.\n\n그리하여 상복이 갖추어지고 피트 크롤리 경에게도 그들의 도착이 알려지자, 크롤리 대령과 그의 아내는 바로 그 낡은 하이플라이어 역마차에 두 자리를 잡았다. 레베카가 구 년쯤 전 처음 세상으로 나서던 그 길에, 지금은 고인이 된 준남작과 함께 타고 갔던 바로 그 마차였다. 그녀는 그 여관 마당이며, 돈을 거절했던 마부며, 길 위에서 자기 외투로 그녀를 감싸 주던 그 알랑거리던 케임브리지 청년을 어찌나 또렷이 기억하고 있던지! 로던은 바깥자리를 잡았고 직접 말을 몰고 싶었으나 슬픔이 그것을 허락지 않았다. 그는 마부 옆에 앉아 가는 내내 말이며 길에 대해, 그리고 누가 그 여관들을 운영하는지, 그와 피트가 소년 시절 이튼으로 가던 길에 숱하게 탔던 그 마차에 누가 말을 대 주는지 따위를 이야기했다. 머드베리에 이르자 검은 옷을 입은 마부가 모는 한 쌍의 말이 끄는 마차가 그들을 맞이했다. \"옛날 그 마차네요, 로던.\" 레베카가 올라타며 말했다. \"좀이 천을 꽤나 갉아먹었군요. 저기 얼룩 좀 봐요, 피트 경이, 아! 철물상 도슨이 덧문을 닫아 두었네요, 피트 경이 어찌나 야단법석을 떨었던지요. 우리가 당신 고모님 드린다고 사우샘프턴에서 가져오던 그 체리 브랜디 한 병을 깨뜨렸을 때 말이에요. 정말이지 세월 참 빠르기도 하지! 저기 오두막에서 어머니 곁에 서 있는 저 통통한 처녀, 설마 폴리 톨보이스는 아니겠지요. 정원에서 잡초나 뜯던 옴 오른 꼬마였던 게 기억나는데.\"\n\n\"고운 처녀로군.\" 로던이 그 오두막에서 보내온 인사에, 상장(喪章)을 두른 모자챙에 손가락 두 개를 갖다 대며 답례하고는 말했다. 베키는 고개 숙여 인사하며 여기저기서 사람들을 우아하게 알아보았다. 이런 알은체는 그녀에게 말할 수 없이 기분 좋은 일이었다. 이제 자신이 더는 사기꾼이 아니라, 조상 대대로 내려온 집안의 고향으로 돌아오고 있는 듯한 기분이었다. 반면 로던은 다소 멋쩍고 풀이 죽어 있었다. 소년 시절과 순수했던 날들의 어떤 기억이 그의 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔을까? 어렴풋한 회한과 의혹과 부끄러움의 어떤 아픔이 일었을까?\n\n\"당신 누이들도 이제 다 큰 처녀가 되었겠어요.\" 레베카가 말했다. 어쩌면 그 아이들 곁을 떠난 이래 처음으로 그들을 떠올린 것이었다.\n\n\"글쎄, 나야 모르지.\" 대령이 대답했다. \"어이! 저기 락 할멈이 있군. 어떻게 지내시오, 락 부인? 날 기억하지요, 안 그렇소? 로던 도련님이오, 응? 제기랄, 저 할멈들은 어찌 저리 오래 사는지. 내가 어릴 적에도 백 살은 돼 보였는데.\"\n\n그들은 락 할멈이 지키는 정문을 지나는 참이었다. 할멈이 삐걱대는 낡은 철문을 활짝 열어젖히자, 레베카는 굳이 그 손을 잡고 흔들어야겠다고 고집을 부렸고, 마차는 비둘기와 뱀 조각이 얹힌 이끼 낀 두 기둥 사이를 지나갔다.\n\n\"형님이 나무를 베어 냈군.\" 로던이 주위를 둘러보며 말하고는 입을 다물었다. 베키도 마찬가지였다. 둘 다 다소 마음이 동요되어 옛 시절을 떠올리고 있었다. 그는 이튼을, 그리고 차갑고 새침했던 여인으로 기억되는 자기 어머니를, 또 자기가 끔찍이도 아꼈으나 죽고 만 누이를 떠올렸다. 또 자기가 피트를 어떻게 두들겨 패곤 했는지를, 그리고 집에 있는 어린 로디를 떠올렸다. 레베카는 자기 자신의 어린 시절과 일찍부터 더럽혀진 그 나날의 어두운 비밀들을, 그리고 저 문을 지나 세상에 첫발을 들이던 일을, 그리고 핑커튼 양과 조와 애밀리아를 떠올렸다.\n\n자갈길과 테라스는 말끔히 쓸려 깨끗했다. 큰 현관 위에는 이미 화려하게 그려진 문장판이 걸려 있었고, 검은 옷을 입은 두 명의 더없이 근엄하고 키 큰 인물이 마차가 그 낯익은 계단 앞에 멈춰 서자 각기 문짝 한 짝씩을 활짝 열어젖혔다. 로던은 얼굴이 붉어졌고 베키는 다소 창백해진 채로, 둘은 팔짱을 끼고 그 옛 현관을 지나갔다. 떡갈나무 응접실로 들어설 때 그녀는 남편의 팔을 꼬집었다. 그곳에서는 피트 경과 그의 아내가 그들을 맞이할 채비를 하고 있었다. 검은 옷을 입은 피트 경, 검은 옷을 입은 제인 부인, 그리고 사우스다운 부인은 유리구슬과 깃털로 장식한 커다란 검은 머리 장식을 얹고 있었는데, 그것이 부인의 머리 위에서 마치 장의사의 운구 쟁반처럼 흔들거렸다.\n\n피트 경의 판단은 옳았다. 그녀는 이 저택을 떠나지 않을 작정이었다. 그녀는 피트와 그 반항적인 아내가 함께 있는 자리에서는 엄숙하고 돌처럼 굳은 침묵을 지키는 것으로, 또 그 음산하리만치 침울한 거동으로 육아실의 아이들을 겁주는 것으로 만족하고 있었다. 저 탕아들이 가족의 품으로 돌아왔건만, 로던과 그의 아내를 맞이한 것이라곤 그저 그 머리 장식과 깃털을 아주 희미하게 한 번 까딱이는 것뿐이었다.\n\n사실대로 말하자면, 두 사람은 이 냉랭함에 이러나저러나 크게 마음 쓰지 않았다. 그 순간 그들의 마음속에서 부인은 그저 부차적인 고려 대상에 지나지 않았던 것이다. 그들은 지금 이 집안을 다스리는 형과 형수가 자신들을 어떻게 맞아 줄지에만 온통 마음을 쏟고 있었다.\n\n피트는 다소 상기된 얼굴로 다가와 동생의 손을 잡고 흔들었고, 레베카에게는 악수와 함께 더없이 깊은 절로 인사했다. 그러나 제인 부인은 시동생의 아내의 두 손을 모두 잡고 다정하게 입을 맞추었다. 그 포옹은 어쩐 일인지 이 작은 모험가의 두 눈에 눈물을 자아냈는데, 우리가 알다시피 그녀가 좀처럼 두르지 않는 장신구였다. 그 꾸밈없는 친절과 신뢰의 표시가 그녀의 마음을 움직이고 기쁘게 했다. 그리고 로던은 누이가 보여 준 이런 살가운 표현에 용기를 얻어 콧수염을 비비 꼬며, 제인 부인에게 실례를 무릅쓰고 입맞춤으로 인사했는데, 이 때문에 부인은 얼굴이 몹시 붉어졌다.\n\n\"정말이지 괜찮은 작은 부인이야, 제인 부인 말이야.\" 그와 그의 아내가 다시 둘만 있게 되었을 때 그가 내린 평이었다. \"피트도 살이 올랐고, 일을 훌륭하게 처리하고 있더군.\" \"그럴 형편이 되니까요.\" 레베카가 말했고, 남편의 또 다른 견해, 즉 \"장모 되는 노친네는 어마어마한 늙은 허수아비고, 누이들은 제법 인물이 반반한 처녀들\"이라는 데에도 동의했다.\n\n그 처녀들 역시 장례식에 참석하라고 학교에서 불려 온 터였다. 보아하니 피트 크롤리 경은 가문과 집안의 위신을 위해, 가능한 한 많은 검은 옷 입은 사람들을 저택에 불러 모아 두는 것이 옳다고 여긴 모양이었다. 집안의 모든 하인과 하녀들, 선대 피트 경이 응당 받을 몫의 상당 부분을 가로채 속여 먹었던 양로원의 노파들, 교구 서기의 가족, 그리고 본가와 목사관 양쪽의 특별한 가신들이 모두 검은 상복을 입었다. 거기에 더해, 장의사가 부리는 일꾼들이 적어도 스무 명은 상장과 모자띠를 두르고 있었는데, 이들은 그 성대한 장례 행렬이 펼쳐질 때면 그럴듯한 볼거리가 되어 주었다. 그러나 이들은 우리 극에서 말 없는 인물들이니, 할 일도 할 말도 없는 만큼 여기서는 아주 작은 자리만 차지하면 될 것이다.\n\n시누이들에 관해서라면, 레베카는 예전 그들의 가정교사였던 자기 처지를 굳이 잊으려 들지 않았다. 오히려 그것을 솔직하고 다정하게 떠올리며, 더없이 진지한 태도로 그들의 공부에 대해 묻고, 숱한 나날 동안 그들을 생각했으며 그들이 잘 지내는지 무척 알고 싶었노라고 말했다. 사실 당신이라면 그녀가 그들 곁을 떠난 이래 줄곧 그들을 마음속 가장 윗자리에 두고 그들의 안녕에 더없이 애틋한 관심을 기울여 왔으리라 여겼을 것이다. 크롤리 부인 본인과 그 어린 시누이들도 그렇게 여겼다.\n\n\"팔 년이 지났는데도 거의 변하지 않았네.\" 로잘린드 양이 만찬 채비를 하면서 바이올렛 양에게 말했다.\n\n\"저런 빨간 머리 여자들은 놀랄 만큼 정정하게 보인다니까.\" 다른 쪽이 대답했다.\n\n\"언니 머리는 예전보다 훨씬 짙어졌어. 염색을 한 게 분명해.\" 로잘린드 양이 덧붙였다. \"몸집도 더 통통해졌고, 전체적으로 나아졌어.\" 살집이 꽤 있는 편인 로잘린드 양이 말을 이었다.\n\n\"적어도 거드름은 안 피우고, 한때 우리 가정교사였다는 걸 기억하고는 있더라.\" 바이올렛 양이 말했다. 모든 가정교사란 제 분수에 맞는 자리를 지켜야 마땅하다는 뜻을 내비친 것인데, 정작 자기 자신이 월폴 크롤리 경의 손녀일 뿐 아니라 머드베리의 도슨 씨의 손녀이기도 하다는 것, 그래서 제 문장(紋章) 속에 석탄통 하나가 들어 있다는 것은 까맣게 잊고 있었다. 허영의 시장에서 우리가 날마다 마주치는, 분명 이만큼이나 잘 잊어버리는 선량한 사람들이 또 있는 법이다.\n\n\"목사관 처녀들이 한 말이 사실일 리 없어. 저 언니 어머니가 오페라 무희였다던데.\"\n\n\"태생이야 어쩔 수 없는 일이지.\" 로잘린드가 무척 너그럽게 대답했다. \"그리고 나도 오빠 말에 동의해. 집안 식구가 된 이상, 당연히 우리도 알은체를 해야 하는 거야. 정말이지 뷰트 고모는 남 말 할 처지가 못 돼. 케이트를 술장수 후퍼 그 젊은이한테 시집보내려고 안달이 나서는, 글쎄 그 사람더러 목사관에 와서 주문받아 가라고까지 했다잖아.\"\n\n\"사우스다운 부인은 떠나실까 모르겠네. 로던 부인을 무척 못마땅한 눈으로 보시던데.\" 다른 쪽이 말했다.\n\n\"제발 좀 가셨으면. 나는 '핀칠리 광야의 세탁부' 따위 안 읽을 거야.\" 바이올렛이 단언했다. 그러고는 어떤 관(棺) 하나가 두 사람의 밤샘꾼과 함께 놓여 있고 닫힌 방 안에서 등불이 끊임없이 타고 있는 복도 끝을 피해, 두 젊은 처녀는 평소처럼 종이 울린 가족 만찬 자리로 내려갔다.\n\n그러나 그에 앞서, 제인 부인은 레베카를 그녀를 위해 마련된 방으로 안내했다. 그 방은 집안의 다른 곳과 마찬가지로 피트가 다스리는 동안 한결 정돈되고 안락한 모습으로 나아져 있었다. 그곳에서 로던 부인의 자그마하고 검소한 트렁크들이 이미 도착해 침실과 그에 딸린 화장실에 놓여 있는 것을 보고는, 부인은 그녀가 단정한 검은 보닛과 망토를 벗는 것을 거들어 주며, 시동생의 아내에게 더 도와줄 일이 없는지 물었다.\n\n\"제가 가장 하고 싶은 일은요,\" 레베카가 말했다. \"육아실에 가서 그 사랑스러운 아이들을 보는 거예요.\" 그 말에 두 부인은 더없이 다정한 눈으로 서로를 바라보고는, 손을 맞잡고 그 방으로 갔다.\n\n베키는 아직 네 살도 채 안 된 어린 마틸다를 세상에서 가장 사랑스러운 꼬마라며 칭찬했다. 그리고 두 살배기 사내아이는, 창백하고 눈이 처지고 머리가 큰 아이였는데, 크기로 보나 영민함으로 보나 아름다움으로 보나 완벽한 신동이라고 단언했다.\n\n\"어머니가 저 애한테 약을 그렇게 많이 먹이라고 고집만 안 부리셨으면 좋겠어요.\" 제인 부인이 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"약 없이도 우리 모두 더 잘 지낼 텐데, 하는 생각이 자주 들어요.\" 그러고는 제인 부인과 새로 사귄 벗은 아이들에 관한 그런 은밀한 의학 담소를 나누었으니, 내가 듣기로는 모든 어머니가, 또 대다수 여인들이 즐겨 하는 그런 이야기였다. 오십 년 전, 지금 이 글을 쓰는 사람이 사랑스러운 어린 소년이던 시절, 만찬이 끝나고 숙녀들과 함께 방에서 물러가라는 명을 받곤 했는데, 그때 그분들의 이야기가 주로 자기들 병치레에 관한 것이었음을 나는 똑똑히 기억한다. 그 뒤로 두어 사람에게 이 질문을 대놓고 던져 보았더니, 한결같이 세월이 달라진 게 없다는 시인을 얻어 냈다. 나의 어여쁜 여성 독자들도 바로 오늘 저녁, 디저트 식탁을 떠나 응접실의 비밀 의식을 거행하러 모일 때 스스로 한번 눈여겨보시기를. 아무튼, 반 시간 만에 베키와 제인 부인은 가깝고 친밀한 벗이 되었다. 그리고 그날 저녁이 다 가기도 전에, 부인은 피트 경에게 새로 맞은 시동생의 아내가 친절하고 솔직하고 꾸밈없고 다정한 젊은 여자인 것 같다고 말했다.\n\n이렇게 손쉽게 며느리의 호의를 얻어 낸 이 지칠 줄 모르는 작은 여인은, 위풍당당한 사우스다운 부인을 구슬리는 일에 착수했다. 부인이 혼자 있는 것을 발견하자마자, 레베카는 대뜸 육아 문제로 그녀를 공략하여, 파리의 모든 의사가 그 가엾은 아이를 포기했을 때 감홍(甘汞)을 넉넉히 먹여 자기 어린 아들을 살려 냈노라고, 실제로 살려 냈노라고 말했다. 그러고는 자기가 다니던 메이페어 예배당의 목사이신 저 훌륭한 분, 로런스 그릴스 목사님께 사우스다운 부인에 관한 이야기를 얼마나 자주 들었는지 모른다고, 또 자기 생각이 갖가지 사정과 불행으로 무척 달라졌노라고, 그리고 지난날을 세속과 과오 속에 보냈다 하여 앞으로 더 진지한 사색을 못 하게 되는 일은 없기를 바란다고 말했다. 그녀는 예전에 크롤리 씨에게 신앙의 가르침을 입었던 일을 들려주고, 더없이 큰 유익을 얻으며 읽었다는 '핀칠리 광야의 세탁부' 이야기를 슬쩍 꺼냈으며, 그 재능 있는 저자인 에밀리 부인, 이제는 케이프타운에서 에밀리 혼블로어 부인이 된 그분의 안부를 물었다. 그 남편은 그곳에서 카프라리아의 주교가 되리라는 큰 희망을 품고 있다고 했다.\n\n그러나 그녀가 모든 것에 화룡점정을 찍어 사우스다운 부인의 환심을 굳힌 것은, 장례를 치른 뒤 무척 마음이 동요되고 몸이 좋지 않다며 부인의 의학적 조언을 청한 일이었다. 노부인은 조언을 해 주었을 뿐 아니라, 잠옷을 두르고 그 어느 때보다 맥베스 부인을 닮은 모습으로, 밤중에 몰래 베키의 방으로 찾아왔다. 손에는 자기가 아끼는 소책자 꾸러미와 손수 조제한 약 한 가지를 들고서, 로던 부인이 그 약을 꼭 먹어야 한다고 우겼다.\n\n베키는 우선 소책자들을 받아 들고 무척 흥미로운 양 살펴보기 시작하면서, 그 책들과 자기 영혼의 안녕에 관한 이야기로 노부인을 붙들어 두었으니, 이로써 자기 몸뚱이만은 약을 면할 수 있기를 바란 것이었다. 그러나 종교적 화제가 바닥나고 나서도, 맥베스 부인은 그 밤 약 잔이 다 비워지기 전에는 베키의 방을 떠나려 하지 않았다. 그리하여 가엾은 로던 부인은 정말로 감사한 표정을 지어 보이고는, 한 치도 물러서지 않는 그 노부인의 코앞에서 약을 삼킬 수밖에 없었다. 노부인은 마침내 축복의 말을 남기고 제 희생양을 떠났다.\n\n그 일은 로던 부인에게 별 위안이 되지 못했다. 로던이 들어와 무슨 일이 있었는지 듣자 그녀의 표정은 무척이나 묘했다. 그리고 베키가, 비록 제 손해를 보는 이야기였음에도 차마 감출 수 없는 그 익살로 그 일을, 즉 자기가 사우스다운 부인에게 어떻게 희생당했는지를 묘사하자, 로던은 여느 때처럼 큰 소리로 폭소를 터뜨렸다. 로던과 그의 아내가 메이페어의 거처로 돌아온 뒤, 스타인 경과 런던에 있는 그녀의 아들도 이 이야기로 숱하게 웃었다. 베키는 그들 앞에서 그 장면 전체를 연기해 보였다. 그녀는 나이트캡을 쓰고 잠옷을 걸쳤다. 그러고는 진짜 엄숙한 투로 일장 설교를 늘어놓았다. 약의 효능에 관해 강론하며 그것을 먹이는 시늉을 하는데, 그 흉내가 어찌나 완벽하게 진지하던지, 그녀가 코로 흥흥거리는 것이 정말로 백작 부인 본인의 로마식 매부리코인 줄로만 알았을 것이다. \"사우스다운 부인이랑 그 검은 약 한 사발 좀 보여 줘요\"는 메이페어에 있는 베키의 자그마한 응접실에 모인 사람들 사이에 끊이지 않는 외침이었다. 그리하여 사우스다운 백작 미망인은 난생처음으로 사람들에게 웃음거리가 되었던 것이다.\n\n피트 경은 레베카가 지난날 자기에게 몸소 바쳤던 존경과 흠모의 표시들을 기억하고 있던 터라, 그녀에게 제법 호의적이었다. 그 결혼은 분별없는 짓이었음에도 로던을 무척이나 나아지게 만들었는데, 이는 대령의 달라진 습관과 거동으로 보아 분명한 일이었다. 게다가 피트 자신에게도 그것은 운 좋은 결합이 아니었던가? 이 교활한 책략가는 그 결혼 덕에 제 재산을 얻게 되었음을 속으로 인정하며 빙긋 웃었고, 적어도 자기만은 그 일을 두고 비난할 처지가 못 된다는 것을 시인했다. 그의 흡족함은 레베카 본인의 말과 행동과 대화로 인해 조금도 줄어들지 않았다.\n\n그녀는 전에 그를 매료시켰던 그 깍듯함을 곱절로 늘려, 그의 대화 능력을 끌어내되 피트 자신마저 깜짝 놀랄 정도로 그렇게 해 주었다. 늘 제 재능을 높이 사는 경향이 있던 그는, 레베카가 그것을 짚어 보여 주자 더욱더 그 재능을 흠모하게 된 것이다. 시누이를 상대로 레베카는, 자기가 나중에 그토록 헐뜯음을 당한 그 결혼을 실은 뷰트 크롤리 부인이 성사시킨 것임을, 또 크롤리 양의 재산을 몽땅 차지하고 로던에게서 고모의 총애를 빼앗으려던 뷰트 부인의 탐욕이야말로 레베카에 관한 그 온갖 못된 소문들을 지어내고 퍼뜨린 장본인임을 흡족하게 입증해 보일 수 있었다. \"그분은 우리를 가난뱅이로 만드는 데는 성공했지요.\" 레베카가 천사 같은 인내의 빛을 띠고 말했다. \"하지만 세상에서 가장 좋은 남편 중 하나를 제게 주신 분에게 어떻게 제가 화를 낼 수 있겠어요? 게다가 그분의 탐욕은, 제 희망이 무너지고 그토록 애지중지하던 재산을 잃은 것으로 충분히 벌을 받지 않았던가요? 가난이라뇨!\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"제인 부인, 가난 따위가 우리에게 무슨 대수겠어요? 저야 어릴 적부터 가난에 익숙한걸요. 그리고 저는 제가 그 일원임을 그토록 자랑스러워하는 이 유서 깊은 명문가의 영광을 되살리는 데 크롤리 양의 돈이 쓰이게 된 것이 도리어 고마운 때가 많답니다. 피트 경께서 로던보다 그 돈을 훨씬 더 잘 쓰시리라 믿어요.\"\n\n이 모든 말은 더없이 충실한 아내의 입을 통해 피트 경에게 고스란히 전해졌고, 레베카가 남긴 호의적인 인상을 한층 더 키워 주었다. 그리하여 장례를 치른 지 사흘째 되던 날, 식구들이 모여 만찬을 들고 있을 때, 피트 크롤리 경은 식탁 상석에서 새고기를 썰다가 로던 부인에게 실제로 이렇게 말했던 것이다. \"에헴! 레베카, 날개 살 한 점 들겠나?\" 이 한마디에 이 작은 여인의 두 눈은 기쁨으로 반짝였다.\n\n레베카가 앞서 말한 그 계략과 희망들을 추진하는 동안, 피트 크롤리는 장례 의식과 더불어 제 장래의 출세와 위신에 관한 여러 일들을 챙겼고, 제인 부인은 어머니가 허락하는 한도 내에서 육아실 일로 분주했으며, 해는 뜨고 또 졌고, 저택의 시계탑 종은 평소처럼 만찬과 기도 시간을 알리며 울렸다. 그러는 사이 퀸스크롤리의 고인이 된 옛 주인의 시신은, 그가 쓰던 방에 누워, 그 의식을 위해 고용된 전문 일꾼들의 끊임없는 지킴을 받고 있었다. 여인 한둘과 장의사가 부리는 사내 서넛, 사우샘프턴이 댈 수 있는 가장 솜씨 좋은 자들이 검은 옷을 입고 적절히 은밀하고 비극적인 거동으로, 번갈아 가며 유해를 지켰다. 그들은 비번일 때면 가정부 방을 만남의 장소로 삼아 그곳에서 은밀히 카드놀이를 하고 맥주를 마셨다.\n\n집안 식구들과 하인들은 그 음산한 자리를 멀리했다. 그곳에는 고대 기사와 신사들의 핏줄을 이어받은 한 사내의 뼈가, 가족 납골당으로 마지막 안치될 날을 기다리며 누워 있었다. 그를 애도하는 이는 아무도 없었으니, 다만 피트 경의 아내가, 또 그 미망인이 되기를 바라다가 거의 그 안주인이 될 뻔했던 저택에서 망신을 당하고 달아난 그 가엾은 여인의 슬픔만이 있을 뿐이었다. 그녀와, 그가 아끼던 늙은 포인터 한 마리—치매를 앓던 시절 그와 이 개 사이에는 어떤 정이 오갔다—를 빼면, 이 노인에게는 그를 애도해 줄 벗이 단 하나도 없었다. 실로 그는 평생토록 단 한 명의 벗을 얻으려는 수고조차 손톱만큼도 들인 적이 없었던 것이다. 이 땅을 떠나는 우리 가운데 가장 선하고 다정한 이라 할지라도, 만일 다시 이곳을 찾아올 기회가 주어진다면, 짐작하건대 그 또는 그녀는(우리가 향하는 그 세계에도 허영의 시장의 감정이라는 것이 조금이나마 남아 있다고 친다면) 우리 뒤에 남은 이들이 어쩌면 그리도 빨리 위로를 받는지를 알고는 굴욕의 아픔을 느낄 것이다. 그리하여 피트 경은, 우리 가운데 가장 선하고 다정한 이들과 마찬가지로, 잊혀졌다. 다만 몇 주쯤 더 빨랐을 뿐이다.\n\n원하는 이라면 그의 유해를 따라 무덤까지 가 보아도 좋다. 정해진 날, 더없이 격식에 맞는 모양새로 그것은 운구되었다. 가족은 검은 마차에 타고, 손수건을 코에 갖다 댄 채 끝내 나오지 않는 눈물에 대비하고 있었다. 장의사와 그 일꾼들은 깊은 비탄에 잠겼고, 선별된 소작인들은 새 지주에 대한 예우 차원에서 애도했으며, 이웃 신사들의 마차는 시속 삼 마일로, 텅 빈 채 깊은 슬픔에 잠겨 따라왔고, 목사는 \"세상을 떠난 우리 사랑하는 형제\"에 관한 정해진 문구를 읊었다. 우리는 사람의 몸뚱이가 남아 있는 한 그 위에서 우리의 허영을 연출하며, 그것을 허튼수작과 의식으로 둘러싸고, 정중히 안치하고, 금박 못과 벨벳으로 싸 둔다. 그리고 그 위에 거짓말로 빼곡히 새겨진 비석 하나를 얹는 것으로 우리의 의무를 끝낸다. 뷰트의 부목사, 옥스퍼드 출신의 영민한 젊은이가 피트 크롤리 경과 함께 고인이 된 준남작을 위해 적절한 라틴어 묘비명을 지었고, 그 부목사는 고전적인 설교 한 편을 읊어 살아남은 자들에게 슬픔에 잠기지 말 것을 권하면서, 더없이 정중한 말투로 그들 역시 언젠가는 방금 그들의 애도받는 형제의 유해 위로 닫힌 저 음울하고 신비로운 문을 지나게 되리라고 알려 주었다. 그러고 나서 소작인들은 다시 말에 올랐거나, 아니면 남아서 크롤리 암스 주막에서 요기를 했다. 그러고 나서 퀸스크롤리의 하인 식당에서 점심을 든 뒤, 신사들의 마차는 제각기 다른 목적지를 향해 바퀴를 굴려 떠났다. 그러고 나서 장의사 일꾼들은 밧줄과 관보와 벨벳과 타조 깃털과 그 밖의 장례 도구들을 챙겨 영구차 지붕 위로 기어올라 사우샘프턴으로 떠났다. 말들이 정문을 빠져나가 트인 길로 들어서며 더 활기찬 속보로 달리기 시작하자, 그들의 얼굴은 자연스러운 표정으로 되돌아왔다. 그리고 그들 한 무리가 술집 입구를 검게 점점이 물들이고 있는 광경을, 백랍 술잔이 햇살에 번뜩이는 모습을 볼 수 있었을 것이다. 피트 경의 환자용 의자는 정원의 연장 창고로 굴려 보내졌다. 늙은 포인터가 처음 얼마간은 이따금 울부짖곤 했으나, 피트 크롤리 준남작이 육십 년 가까이 주인 노릇을 했던 그 저택에서 들려온 슬픔의 가락이라곤 오직 그것뿐이었다.\n\n새가 제법 많은 데다 자고새 사냥이란 정치가다운 기질을 지닌 영국 신사의 의무나 다름없는 일이었으므로, 피트 크롤리 경은 슬픔의 첫 충격이 가시고 나자 상장을 두른 흰 모자를 쓰고 잠깐씩 나가 그 소일거리를 즐겼다. 이제는 제 것이 된 저 그루터기 밭과 순무밭을 바라보노라면 그는 남모를 기쁨이 솟곤 했다. 더러는, 더없이 절묘한 겸양을 부리며 총을 들지 않고 평화로운 대나무 지팡이만 짚고 나서기도 했는데, 형인 로던과 사냥지기들이 그 곁에서 총을 쏘아 댔다. 피트의 돈과 토지는 동생에게 큰 영향을 미쳤다. 무일푼인 대령은 제 집안의 가장에게 완전히 굽실거리며 깍듯해졌고, 더는 그 물러터진 피트를 업신여기지 않았다. 로던은 형이 펼쳐 보이는 식목과 배수 계획을 공감하며 귀 기울여 들었고, 마구간과 가축에 관해 조언했으며, 제인 부인이 타기에 좋겠다 싶은 암말 한 마리를 보러 머드베리까지 말을 달려갔다가 그 말을 길들여 주겠다고 나서기도 했다. 그 반항적이던 용기병은 완전히 고분고분해지고 누그러져, 더없이 떳떳한 동생이 되었다. 그는 런던에 남겨 둔 어린 로던에 관해 브리그스 양에게서 끊임없이 소식을 받았는데, 아이도 제 나름의 전갈을 보내왔다. \"저는 아주 잘 있어요.\" 아이는 이렇게 썼다. \"아빠도 아주 잘 계시기를 바라요. 엄마도 아주 잘 계시기를 바라요. 조랑말도 아주 잘 있어요. 그레이가 저를 공원에 데리고 가서 태워 줘요. 저는 가볍게 달릴 줄 알아요. 전에 앞서서 말 타던 그 꼬마를 만났어요. 그 애는 가볍게 달릴 때 울었어요. 저는 안 울어요.\" 로던은 이 편지들을 형과 제인 부인에게 읽어 주었고, 부인은 무척 기뻐했다. 준남작은 그 아이를 학교에 보내 돌봐 주겠다고 약속했고, 마음씨 고운 그의 아내는 레베카에게 은행권 한 장을 건네며 그 돈으로 어린 조카에게 줄 선물을 사 달라고 부탁했다.\n\n하루가 가고 또 하루가 갔고, 집안의 부인들은 시골 부인들을 만족시키는 그 잔잔한 일과와 놀이 속에서 나날을 보냈다. 종은 식사와 기도 시간을 알리며 울렸다. 젊은 처녀들은 아침마다 식후에 피아노로 연습을 했는데, 레베카가 그들에게 가르침을 베풀어 주었다. 그러고 나서 그들은 두꺼운 신을 신고 공원이나 관목 숲을 거닐거나, 울타리 너머 마을로 나가, 사우스다운 부인의 약과 소책자를 들고 그곳 병자들을 찾아 오두막마다 들렀다. 사우스다운 부인이 조랑말 마차를 타고 나설 때면, 레베카는 노부인 곁에 자리 잡고 앉아 그 엄숙한 이야기를 더없이 흥미진진하게 귀담아들었다. 그녀는 저녁이면 식구들에게 헨델과 하이든을 불러 주었고, 마치 그 일에 타고나기라도 한 듯, 또 이런 식의 삶이 그녀와 함께 죽 이어져 그녀가 점잖은 노년에 이르러 무덤으로 잠겨 들며 못다 한 아쉬움과 어마어마한 액수의 공채(公債)를 뒤에 남기게 되기라도 할 듯, 커다란 털실 자수 작업에 매달렸다. 마치 그녀가 다시 세상으로 나서면 공원 문밖에서 근심과 빚쟁이들, 갖은 계략과 임시변통과 가난이 도사리고 있다가 덮쳐 오기라도 할 것이 아니라는 듯이 말이다.\n\n\"시골 신사의 아내가 되는 건 어렵지 않아.\" 레베카는 생각했다. \"연 오천 파운드만 있으면 나도 착한 여자가 될 수 있을 것 같아. 육아실에서 빈둥거리며 담장에 열린 살구를 세고 있을 수도 있겠지. 온실에서 화초에 물을 주고 제라늄에서 시든 잎을 따 줄 수도 있고. 노파들에게 류머티즘이 어떠냐고 물어보고, 가난한 이들에게 반 크라운어치 수프를 주문해 줄 수도 있을 거야. 연 오천 파운드에서 그쯤이야 아쉬울 것도 없지. 십 마일쯤 마차를 몰고 가서 이웃집에서 만찬을 들 수도 있고, 재작년 유행을 따라 차려입을 수도 있어. 교회에 가서 큰 가족석에서 깨어 있거나, 아니면 익숙해지기만 한다면 베일을 내리고 커튼 뒤에서 잠들어 있을 수도 있지. 돈만 있으면 누구에게든 다 갚을 수 있어. 여기 이 요술쟁이들이 우쭐대는 게 바로 그것이지. 그들은 돈 한 푼 없는 우리 가엾은 죄인들을 가엾다는 듯 내려다보지. 우리 아이들에게 오 파운드짜리 지폐 한 장 쥐여 주면 자기들이 후하다고 여기고, 우리에게 그게 없으면 우리를 한심하다 여기지.\" 어쩌면 레베카의 이런 짐작이 옳았는지도, 그녀와 정숙한 여인 사이의 차이를 가른 것이 그저 돈과 재산의 문제였는지도 모를 일이다. 유혹이라는 것을 셈에 넣는다면, 누가 자기가 이웃보다 낫다고 말할 수 있겠는가? 안락한 번영의 삶이란, 설령 사람을 정직하게 만들지는 못할지언정, 적어도 정직한 채로 머물게는 해 준다. 자라 잔치에서 돌아오는 시참사회원이 양 다리 고기 한 덩이를 훔치려고 제 마차에서 내리는 일은 없을 것이다. 그러나 그를 굶겨 보라, 그가 빵 한 덩이를 슬쩍하지 않나 보라. 베키는 이렇게 세상의 가능성을 저울질하고 선과 악의 분배를 고르게 따져 봄으로써 스스로를 위로했다.\n\n옛날에 드나들던 곳들, 옛 들판과 숲, 잡목림과 연못과 정원들, 칠 년 전 그녀가 두어 해를 보냈던 그 낡은 저택의 방들을, 그녀는 모두 정성껏 다시 둘러보았다. 그곳에서 그녀는 젊었었다, 적어도 비교적 젊었었다. 사실 그녀는 자기가 한때 젊었던 시절이라는 것 자체를 잊고 살았으니 말이다. 그러나 그녀는 칠 년 전의 제 생각과 감정들을 기억해 냈고, 이제 세상을 두루 보고 지체 높은 이들과 더불어 살며 원래의 미천한 처지에서 까마득히 높이 올라선 지금의 생각과 감정에 견주어 보았다.\n\n\"내가 그 처지를 벗어난 건 머리가 있기 때문이야.\" 베키는 생각했다. \"그리고 세상 나머지 거의 모두는 바보들이지. 이제 와서 돌아가 우리 아버지 화실에서 마주치곤 하던 그런 사람들과 어울릴 수는 없어. 주머니에 담배 부스러기나 넣고 다니는 가난한 화가들 대신, 별과 가터 훈장을 단 귀족들이 내 집 문 앞을 찾아오지. 몇 해 전만 해도 내가 하녀나 다름없던 바로 그 집에서, 이제 신사를 남편으로, 백작의 딸을 시누이로 두고 있어. 하지만 내가 가난한 화가의 딸로 모퉁이 식료품상을 구슬려 설탕이며 차를 얻어 내던 그때보다, 지금 세상에서 형편이 그리 많이 나아진 걸까? 나를 그토록 좋아하던 프랜시스와 결혼했다고 쳐 봐. 지금보다 그리 가난할 것도 없었을 거야. 아이고! 사교계에서의 내 지위와 내 친척들을 죄다, 삼 푼 이자 공채에 든 아늑한 한밑천하고 바꿀 수 있다면 좋으련만.\" 베키가 인간사의 허영을 느낀 것이 바로 이런 식이었고, 그녀가 닻을 내리고 싶어 한 것이 바로 그런 유가증권들이었던 것이다.\n\n어쩌면 그녀의 머릿속에는 이런 생각이 스쳤을지도 모른다. 정직하고 겸손하게 살며 제 본분을 다하고 제 길을 곧장 걸어왔더라면, 지금 그녀가 행복에 닿으려 발버둥 치는 그 길만큼이나 그녀를 행복에 가까이 데려다주었으리라는 생각 말이다. 그러나, 마치 퀸스크롤리의 아이들이 제 아비의 시신이 누운 방을 빙 돌아 지나가듯, 설령 베키에게 이런 생각이 들었더라도 그녀는 으레 그 둘레를 빙 돌아 갈 뿐 안을 들여다보지는 않았다. 그녀는 그런 생각들을 슬쩍 피하고 멸시했다. 아니, 적어도 그녀는 이제 물러설 수 없는 다른 길에 발을 들여놓은 터였다. 그리고 내 생각으로는, 회한이란 인간의 모든 도덕적 감각 중에서 가장 활동성이 떨어지는 것이다. 일단 깨어났다가도 잠재우기가 더없이 쉬운 것이요, 어떤 이들에게서는 아예 깨어나는 법이 없는 것이다. 우리는 들통난 것을 두고, 또 수치나 벌을 받으리라는 생각에 슬퍼하지만, 그저 잘못을 저질렀다는 자각만으로 불행해지는 사람은 허영의 시장에 지극히 드문 법이다.\n\n그리하여 레베카는 퀸스크롤리에 머무는 동안, 자기가 손에 넣을 수 있는 한 많은 불의의 재물을 벗으로 삼았다. 제인 부인과 그 남편은 더없이 따뜻한 호의의 표시와 함께 그녀에게 작별을 고했다. 그들은 곤트 가의 가문 저택을 수리하고 아름답게 단장한 뒤 런던에서 다시 만나게 될 그때를 기쁜 마음으로 고대했다. 사우스다운 부인은 그녀에게 약 꾸러미 하나를 챙겨 주고, 로런스 그릴스 목사 앞으로 보내는 편지 한 통을 들려 보내, 이 편지를 \"영광되이 전하는\" 그 불씨를 불길에서 건져 내라고 그 신사를 권면했다. 피트는 미리 짐을 짐수레에 실어, 사냥감을 잔뜩 곁들여 먼저 보낸 뒤, 네 마리 말이 끄는 마차로 그들을 머드베리까지 배웅했다.\n\n\"사랑하는 아드님을 다시 만나면 얼마나 기쁘시겠어요!\" 크롤리 부인이 친척에게 작별을 고하며 말했다.\n\n\"아, 얼마나 기쁘겠어요!\" 레베카가 초록빛 눈을 치뜨며 말했다. 그녀는 이곳을 벗어나게 된 것이 더없이 기뻤지만, 그러면서도 떠나기가 못내 아쉬웠다. 퀸스크롤리는 지긋지긋하게 따분한 곳이었으나, 그곳의 공기는 어쩐지 그녀가 늘 들이마시던 공기보다 더 맑았다. 다들 따분한 사람들이긴 했어도, 저마다의 방식으로 친절했던 것이다. \"이게 다 삼 푼 이자 공채를 오래 끼고 산 영향이지.\" 베키는 속으로 중얼거렸고, 아마 그 말이 옳았을 것이다.\n\n그러나 역마차가 피커딜리로 굴러 들어설 때 런던의 등불들이 즐거이 번쩍였고, 브리그스는 커즌 가에 아름다운 불을 지펴 놓았으며, 어린 로던은 아빠와 엄마를 맞으려 잠도 자지 않고 기다리고 있었다.\n\n우리의 존경할 만한 벗, 러셀 광장의 오스본 노인을 마지막으로 본 지 어느덧 상당한 시간이 흘렀다. 마지막으로 만난 이래로 그는 인간들 가운데 가장 행복한 축에 들지 못했다. 그의 성미를 누그러뜨리는 데 아무런 도움이 안 되는 일들이 벌어졌고, 한두 번이 아니게 제 뜻대로 일이 풀리지 못했던 것이다. 이 합당한 욕망이 좌절되는 것은 이 노신사에게 언제나 몹시 해로운 일이었다. 게다가 통풍에다 나이, 외로움, 그리고 숱한 실망의 무게까지 한데 겹쳐 그를 짓누르니, 그 저항감은 곱절로 더 사람을 노엽게 만들었다. 빳빳하던 검은 머리는 아들이 죽은 직후부터 완연히 세기 시작했고, 얼굴은 더 벌게졌으며, 포트와인을 잔에 따를 때면 두 손이 점점 더 떨렸다. 시내에서 그는 점원들을 끔찍한 지경으로 부려 먹었다. 집안 식구들이라고 해서 더 행복할 것도 없었다. 우리가 콘솔 공채를 위해 경건히 기도하는 모습을 본 적 있는 그 리베카라 하더라도, 자신의 가난과 그 무모할 만큼 짜릿한 흥분과 인생의 모험을 오스본의 돈과 그를 휘감은 단조로운 우울과 맞바꾸려 했을지 나는 의심스럽다. 그는 스워츠 양에게 청혼했지만, 그녀를 스코틀랜드 귀족 가문의 풋내기 청년과 결혼시켜 버린 그 부인의 후원자들에게 경멸 어린 거절을 당하고 말았다. 그는 천한 집안의 여자를 아내로 맞아 그 뒤로 끔찍하게 들볶을 법한 사내였지만, 자기 취향에 맞는 여자가 나타나지 않는 통에, 그 대신 집에서 미혼인 딸을 폭군처럼 억눌렀다. 그녀에게는 훌륭한 마차와 훌륭한 말이 있었고, 더없이 호화로운 식기로 그득한 식탁의 상석에 앉았다. 수표책이 있었고, 외출할 때면 뒤따르는 일급 하인이 있었으며, 무제한의 신용과 상인들 모두의 절과 아첨, 그리고 상속녀에게 따르는 온갖 부속물을 거느렸다. 그러나 그녀는 비참한 나날을 보냈다. 고아원의 어린 자선 소녀들이며, 길모퉁이의 청소부 여인이며, 하인 방의 가장 미천한 보조 부엌데기조차도, 저 불운하고 이제는 중년에 접어든 처녀에 비하면 행복했다.\n\n불록·헐커·불록 상사의 프레더릭 불록 귀하는 마리아 오스본과 결혼했는데, 불록 측의 적잖은 어려움과 불평이 없지 않은 끝에 이루어진 일이었다. 조지가 죽고 아버지의 유언장에서 잘려 나간 마당이니, 프레더릭은 노신사 재산의 절반을 자기 마리아 앞으로 양도해야 한다고 우겼고, 실제로 오랫동안 그 밖의 어떤 조건으로도 \"결판 짓기를\"(프레더릭 본인의 표현이었다) 거부했다. 오스본은 프레드가 자기 딸을 이만 파운드와 함께 데려가기로 동의했으니, 그 이상은 한 푼도 묶어 두지 않겠노라고 했다. \"프레드가 그걸 받아들이겠다면 기꺼이 환영이고, 마다하겠다면 가서 목이나 매라지.\" 조지가 상속에서 제외되었을 때 잔뜩 부풀어 올랐던 기대를 품었던 프레드는 늙은 상인에게 파렴치하게 사기당했다고 여겨, 한동안 혼사를 아예 깨 버릴 듯이 굴었다. 오스본은 불록·헐커 상사에서 거래 계좌를 빼냈고, 이름은 밝히지 않겠으나 어느 악당의 등짝을 후려쳐 주겠노라 맹세하며 말채찍을 들고 거래소에 나갔으며, 평소의 그 난폭한 태도로 처신했다. 이 집안싸움이 벌어지는 동안 제인 오스본은 동생 마리아를 위로했다. \"내가 늘 말했잖니, 마리아, 그 사람이 사랑한 건 네가 아니라 네 돈이라고.\" 그녀가 달래듯 말했다.\n\n\"어쨌든 그 사람은 나와 내 돈을 골랐지, 언니와 언니 돈을 고르진 않았잖아.\" 마리아가 머리를 홱 치켜들며 대꾸했다.\n\n그러나 그 격분은 한때의 것일 뿐이었다. 프레드의 아버지와 선임 동업자들은 그에게, 이만 파운드를 양도하되 절반은 즉시 받고 나머지 절반은 오스본 씨가 죽을 때 받는 조건으로, 그리고 장차 재산이 더 나뉠 가능성까지 끼고서, 마리아를 받아들이라고 권했다. 그리하여 그는 다시 자기 표현을 빌리자면 \"굽히고\" 들어가, 늙은 헐커를 화해의 사절로 오스본에게 보냈다. 그가 말하기를, 혼사를 도무지 들으려 하지 않고 온갖 난관을 만든 것은 자기 아버지였으며, 자기로서는 약혼을 지키고 싶어 안달이 났다는 것이었다. 오스본 씨는 뚱한 얼굴로 그 변명을 받아들였다. 헐커와 불록은 시내 귀족계의 명문가로, 웨스트엔드의 \"거물들\"과도 연줄이 닿아 있었다. \"내 아들이 말이야, 헐커·불록 상사의 사람이지. 내 딸의 사촌은 메리 망고 부인이고, 캐슬몰디 백작 각하의 따님이라네.\" 이렇게 말할 수 있다는 것은 노인에게 자못 대단한 일이었다. 그는 상상 속에서 자기 집안이 \"거물들\"로 그득한 광경을 그려 보았다. 그리하여 그는 젊은 불록을 용서하고 혼례를 치르는 데 동의했다.\n\n그것은 성대한 행사였다. 신랑 측 친지들이 피로연을 베풀었으며, 그들의 거처가 행사가 거행될 하노버 광장의 세인트조지 교회 근처에 있었던 것이다. \"웨스트엔드의 거물들\"이 초대되었고, 그들 중 다수가 방명록에 서명했다. 망고 씨와 메리 망고 부인이 참석했고, 사랑스러운 젊은 그웬덜린 망고와 귀네버 망고가 들러리를 섰다. 신랑의 또 다른 사촌인 용기병 근위대의 블러디어 대령(민싱 레인의 블러디어 형제 상사 장남)과 그 부인 블러디어 귀부인이 왔고, 르밴트 경의 아들인 조지 볼터 귀하와 망고 양이었던 그 부인이, 캐슬토디 자작이, 제임스 맥멀 귀하와 (예전의 스워츠 양인) 맥멀 부인이, 그 밖에 롬바드 거리와 혼맥을 맺어 콘힐을 귀하게 빛내는 데 크게 이바지한 멋쟁이들이 한 무리 참석했다.\n\n이 젊은 부부는 버클리 광장 근처에 집을, 그리고 로햄프턴의 은행가 거주 단지에 자그마한 별장을 마련했다. 프레드는 자기 집안 부인네들이 보기에는 다소 격이 낮은 혼사를 한 셈이었는데, 그 부인네들의 할아버지는 자선 학교 출신인 데다 그 부인네들은 남편들을 통해 영국에서 가장 고귀한 혈통과 연이 닿아 있었던 것이다. 그래서 마리아는 빼어난 자존심과 방문객 명부를 꾸미는 데 들이는 각별한 정성으로 출생의 결함을 메워야만 했고, 아버지와 언니를 되도록 멀리하는 것이 자기 의무라고 여겼다.\n\n그녀가, 아직 수십만 파운드나 되는 돈을 나누어 줄 여력이 있는 그 노인과 완전히 인연을 끊으리라 짐작하는 것은 어불성설이다. 프레드 불록이 결코 그녀에게 그런 짓을 허락할 리 없었다. 그러나 그녀는 아직 젊었고 자기 감정을 감출 줄 몰랐다. 그래서 아버지와 언니를 자신의 삼류 연회에나 초대하고, 그들이 오면 몹시 쌀쌀맞게 대하며, 러셀 광장에는 발길을 끊고, 분별없이 아버지더러 그 꼴사납고 천박한 동네를 떠나라고 졸라 댐으로써, 프레더릭의 온갖 외교술로도 메우지 못할 만큼 일을 그르치고 말았으니, 분별없고 경솔한 인간이 다 그렇듯 그녀는 자기 상속 기회를 위태롭게 만들었던 것이다.\n\n\"그러니까 러셀 광장이 마리아 마님께는 성에 차지 않으신다 이거지, 응?\" 어느 날 밤, 프레더릭 불록 부인의 집에서 만찬을 마치고 딸과 함께 마차를 몰아 떠나면서, 노신사는 마차 창문을 덜컹덜컹 올리며 말했다. \"그래서 제 아비와 언니를 이튿날 다시 데운 만찬에나 초대하시고(그 곁들이 요리인지 앙트레인지 하는 것들이 어제 안 나온 거라면 내 손에 장을 지진다), 시내 사람들이며 글쟁이들이나 만나게 하고, 백작이며 귀부인이며 귀하들은 죄다 저 혼자 끼고 앉으셨다 이거지. 귀하라고? 빌어먹을 귀하 같으니. 나는 평범한 영국 상인이야, 나는. 그깟 거지 같은 작자들쯤은 통째로 사고도 남지. 영주라, 참! 어허, 그 연회인지 뭔지 하는 자리에서 내가 그중 하나가 빌어먹을 깽깽이쟁이한테 말 거는 꼴을 봤다니까, 내가 경멸하는 그런 작자한테 말이야. 그러고선 러셀 광장에는 안 오시겠다 이거지, 응? 어디, 내 목숨을 걸고 말하건대, 내가 더 좋은 와인을 갖고 있고, 그걸 더 비싼 값에 사고, 더 멋진 은식기 한 벌을 내보이고, 내 마호가니 식탁에 그자들이 제 식탁에서 차려 본 것보다 더 나은 만찬을 차릴 수 있어 — 그 굽실거리고 비굴하고 거드름 피우는 멍청이들 같으니. 어서 몰아라, 제임스. 어서 러셀 광장으로 돌아가자고 — 하, 하!\" 그러고는 사나운 웃음을 터뜨리며 구석에 털썩 기댔다. 노신사는 자신의 우월한 됨됨이를 두고 이렇게 곱씹으며 적잖이 스스로를 위로하곤 했던 것이다.\n\n제인 오스본도 동생의 처신을 두고 이런 견해에 동조하지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 프레더릭 부인의 맏아이인 프레더릭 오거스터스 하워드 스탠리 데버루 불록이 태어났을 때, 세례식에 대부로 초대받은 늙은 오스본은 갓난아이에게 금잔 하나를 보내고 그 안에 유모 몫으로 이십 기니를 넣어 주는 것으로 그쳤다. \"이만하면 네놈 영주들 누구도 못 내놓을 액수일 게다, 내 장담하지.\" 그는 그렇게 말하고는 식에 참석하기를 거절했다.\n\n그러나 그 선물의 호화로움은 불록 집안에 큰 만족을 안겼다. 마리아는 아버지가 자기를 무척 기특해한다고 생각했고, 프레더릭은 자기 어린 아들이자 후계자에게 더없이 좋은 앞날을 점쳤다.\n\n러셀 광장의 고독 속에서 오스본 양이 「모닝 포스트」를 읽으며 느꼈을 가슴앓이는 능히 짐작이 간다. 그 신문에는 \"사교계 모임\"이라는 표제 아래 기사가 실릴 때마다 동생의 이름이 심심찮게 등장했고, 그녀는 프레데리카 불록 귀부인의 손에 이끌려 응접실에 인사 들었을 적의 프레더릭 불록 부인의 의상에 관한 묘사까지 읽을 기회를 얻었던 것이다. 앞서 말했듯, 제인 자신의 삶에는 그런 호사라곤 없었다. 그것은 끔찍한 생활이었다. 그녀는 깜깜한 겨울 아침이면 일어나 인상을 잔뜩 찌푸린 늙은 아버지의 아침상을 차려야 했다. 여덟 시 반까지 차가 준비되어 있지 않으면 온 집안을 문밖으로 내쫓을 양반이었던 것이다. 그녀는 차 끓이는 항아리가 쉭쉭거리는 소리에 귀 기울이며 그 맞은편에 말없이 앉아, 아버지가 신문을 읽고 늘 먹던 양만큼의 머핀과 차를 비우는 동안 벌벌 떨며 자리를 지켰다. 아홉 시 반이 되면 아버지가 일어나 시내로 나가고, 그러면 그녀는 만찬 때까지 거의 자유로운 몸이 되어, 부엌을 순시하며 하인들을 꾸짖고, 마차로 외출해 굉장히 공손한 상인들을 덮치듯 찾아가고, 시내 친구들의 크고 우중충하고 점잖은 저택들에 자기 명함과 아버지 명함을 두고 오고, 아니면 손님을 기다리며 그 커다란 응접실에 홀로 앉아 있곤 했다. 벽난로 곁 소파에 앉아, 그 음울한 방 안에서 구슬프도록 큰 소리로 똑딱이며 시각을 알리는 커다란 이피게니아 시계 바로 옆에서, 거대한 털실 수예품을 짜면서 말이다. 벽난로 선반 위의 커다란 거울은 방 반대편 끝에 마주 놓인 또 하나의 커다란 콘솔 거울과 맞보며, 그 둘 사이에서 샹들리에를 씌운 갈색 무명 자루를 자꾸자꾸 늘려 비추어, 마침내 그 갈색 무명 자루들이 끝없는 원근 속으로 아득히 사라지는 광경이 보였으니, 오스본 양의 이 거실은 마치 연이은 응접실들로 이루어진 한 우주의 중심처럼 보였다. 그녀가 그랜드 피아노에서 코르도바 가죽 덮개를 걷어 내고 음 몇 개를 쳐 볼라치면, 그 소리는 구슬픈 서글픔을 띠고 울리며 집 안의 음산한 메아리를 깨우는 것이었다. 조지의 초상화는 사라져 다락방의 허섭스레기 광에 처박혀 있었다. 그를 의식하는 마음이 늘 있었고, 아버지와 딸이 서로 그를 생각하고 있음을 본능적으로 알아챌 때가 잦았지만, 그 용감하고 한때 그토록 귀애받던 아들의 이야기는 결코 입에 오르지 않았다.\n\n다섯 시가 되면 오스본 씨가 만찬을 들러 돌아왔다. 그는 딸과 함께 말없이 식사를 했고(요리가 마음에 들지 않으면 욕설을 퍼붓고 사납게 굴 때 말고는 그 침묵이 좀처럼 깨지지 않았다), 한 달에 두 번쯤은 오스본과 같은 신분에 같은 연배인 음울한 친구들 몇과 함께 식탁을 나누었다. 블룸즈버리 광장에서 온 늙은 걸프 박사와 그 부인, 베드퍼드 로에서 온 늙은 변호사 프라우저 씨—사업 덕분에 \"웨스트엔드의 거물들\"과 막역한 사이인 대단한 인물이었다—어퍼 베드퍼드 플레이스에서 온 봄베이 주둔군의 늙은 리버모어 대령과 리버모어 부인, 늙은 토피 상사와 토피 부인, 그리고 이따금 베드퍼드 광장에서 온 늙은 토머스 코핀 경과 코핀 부인이 있었다. 토머스 경은 사형을 즐겨 선고하는 판사로 유명했는데, 그가 오스본 씨와 만찬을 들 때면 그 특별한 황갈색 포트와인이 나왔다.\n\n이런 사람들과 그 부류는 거드름 떠는 그 러셀 광장 상인에게 거드름 떠는 만찬으로 답례하곤 했다. 그들은 술을 마신 뒤 위층으로 올라가 엄숙한 휘스트 게임을 벌였고, 열 시 반이면 마차를 불렀다. 우리네 가엾은 인간들이 곧잘 부러워하는 부자들 중 다수가 위에서 묘사한 것과 같은 생활을 흡족하게 이어 가는 것이다. 제인 오스본은 예순 살 아래의 남자를 만나는 일이 거의 없었고, 그들의 사교계에 모습을 보이는 거의 유일한 독신남이라곤 저 유명한 부인과 의사 스머크 씨뿐이었다.\n\n이 끔찍한 생활의 단조로움을 흔드는 일이 전혀 없었노라고는 말 못 하겠다. 실은 가엾은 제인의 인생에는 한 가지 비밀이 있었으니, 그것이 그 아버지를 천성과 자존심과 과식이 만들어 놓은 것보다도 더 사납고 침울하게 만들었던 것이다. 이 비밀은 워트 양과 얽혀 있었는데, 그녀에게는 화가인 사촌, 스미 씨가 있었다. 그는 훗날 초상화가이자 왕립 미술원 회원으로 자못 유명해졌지만, 한때는 유행을 좇는 부인네들에게 그림 수업을 해 주는 것을 기꺼워하던 인물이었다. 스미 씨는 이제 러셀 광장이 어디 붙어 있는지조차 잊어버렸지만, 오스본 양이 그에게 가르침을 받던 1818년에는 그곳을 기꺼이 드나들었던 것이다.\n\n스미는(전에 프리스 거리의 샤프 문하에 있던 자로, 방탕하고 종잡을 수 없고 변변찮은 사람이었으나 자기 예술에 대해서는 상당한 식견을 갖춘 사람이었다) 우리가 말했듯 워트 양의 사촌으로서 그녀의 소개로 오스본 양과 알게 되었는데, 그 무렵 오스본 양은 여러 차례 어설픈 연애 끝에 손도 마음도 아직 자유로운 처지였다. 그는 이 부인에게 깊은 애착을 느꼈고, 그녀의 가슴에도 그런 마음을 불러일으켰다고 전해진다. 워트 양은 이 밀애의 심복이었다. 그녀가 선생과 제자가 그림 그리는 방을 나가 주어, 제삼자가 있는 자리에서는 도무지 살갑게 주고받기 어려운 그 맹세와 정담을 나눌 기회를 마련해 주곤 했는지, 나는 알지 못한다. 자기 사촌이 그 부유한 상인의 딸을 채어 가는 데 성공하면, 그를 그렇게 만들어 준 대가로 그가 워트 양에게 그 재산의 일부를 떼어 주리라 기대했는지, 그것도 나는 알지 못한다. 다만 확실한 것은, 오스본 씨가 이 거래에 대한 낌새를 어렴풋이 챘다는 것이다. 그는 시내에서 느닷없이 돌아와 등나무 지팡이를 들고 응접실로 들어섰고, 거기서 화가와 제자와 말동무가 죄다 새파랗게 질려 있는 꼴을 발견했다. 그러고는 화가의 뼈란 뼈는 죄다 부러뜨려 주겠다고 으름장을 놓으며 그를 문밖으로 내쫓았고, 반 시간 뒤에는 워트 양마저 내보내며 그녀의 트렁크를 계단 아래로 걷어차고 모자 상자를 짓밟고, 그녀를 태우고 떠나는 삯마차를 향해 주먹을 휘둘렀다.\n\n제인 오스본은 여러 날 동안 침실에 틀어박혔다. 그 뒤로 그녀에게 말동무가 허락되는 일은 없었다. 아버지는 만약 그녀가 자기 동의 없이 어떤 혼사라도 맺는다면 자기 돈은 한 푼도 못 가져갈 줄 알라고 못 박아 맹세했다. 그리고 자기 집을 건사할 여자가 필요했기에, 그는 그녀가 결혼하는 것을 바라지 않았으니, 그녀는 큐피드와 조금이라도 관련된 온갖 계획을 단념할 수밖에 없었다. 그리하여 아버지가 살아 있는 동안 그녀는 여기 묘사한 그런 생활 방식에 자신을 내맡기고 노처녀로 사는 데 만족했다. 그러는 동안 그녀의 동생은 해마다 더 그럴듯한 이름을 지닌 아이들을 낳았고, 두 자매 사이의 왕래는 끊임없이 점점 더 뜸해져 갔다. \"제인과 나는 같은 생활권에서 움직이는 사이가 아니에요.\" 불록 부인은 말했다. \"물론 언니로 여기긴 하지요\"—이 말이 뜻하는 바는—어느 부인이 제인을 언니로 여긴다고 말할 때 그게 대체 무슨 뜻이란 말인가?\n\n도빈가의 아가씨들이 아버지와 함께 덴마크 힐의 멋진 별장에서 살았다는 것은 앞서 묘사한 바 있다. 그곳에는 어린 조지 오스본이 좋아하던 아름다운 포도밭과 복숭아나무가 있었다. 도빈가의 아가씨들은 우리의 사랑스러운 애밀리아를 보러 곧잘 브롬프턴으로 마차를 몰았는데, 이따금 러셀 광장에도 와서 옛 지인인 오스본 양을 방문하곤 했다. 그들이 조지 부인에게 마음을 쓴 것은, 내 생각에 인도에 있는 그들의 오라비 소령(그들의 아버지가 굉장히 존경하던 인물이었다)의 분부 때문이었던 듯하다. 애밀리아의 어린 아들의 대부이자 후견인인 소령은, 그 아이의 할아버지가 아들을 봐서라도 마음을 누그러뜨려 그 아이를 손자로 인정하게 될지도 모른다고 여전히 바라고 있었던 것이다. 도빈가의 아가씨들은 오스본 양에게 애밀리아의 형편을 소상히 알려 주었다. 그녀가 부모와 함께 살고 있다는 것, 그들이 얼마나 가난한지, 그들의 오라비와 사랑하는 오스본 대위 같은 사내들이 그토록 보잘것없는 자그마한 계집애에게서 대체 무엇을 보았던 건지 자기들로서는 도무지 의아하다는 것, 그녀가 여전히 예나 지금이나 물 탄 듯 밍밍하고 새침을 떠는 감상적인 여자라는 것—그러나 그 아이만큼은 정말이지 여태껏 본 중 가장 의젓한 사내아이라는 것을 말이다. 어린아이를 보면 모든 여인의 마음이 따뜻해지는 법이고, 아무리 심술궂은 노처녀라도 아이들에게는 다정해지기 마련이니까.\n\n어느 날, 도빈가 아가씨들의 거듭된 간청 끝에, 애밀리아는 어린 조지가 덴마크 힐에 가서 그들과 하루를 보내도록 허락해 주었다. 그날의 한나절을 그녀 자신은 인도에 있는 소령에게 편지를 쓰며 보냈다. 그녀는 그의 누이들이 방금 전해 준 기쁜 소식을 두고 그를 축하했다. 그의 번영과 그가 택한 신부의 번영을 빌었다. 자신이 고통에 빠져 있을 때 그가 베풀어 준 천만 가지 다정한 배려와 변함없는 우정의 증표에 감사했다. 어린 조지의 최근 소식을 들려주며, 그 아이가 바로 그날 그의 누이들과 함께 시골에서 하루를 보내러 갔다고 적었다. 그녀는 편지 곳곳에 잔뜩 밑줄을 그었고, 다정한 그대의 벗, 애밀리아 오스본이라고 서명했다. 그녀는 평소의 버릇과 달리 오다우드 부인에게 안부의 말을 전하는 것을 깜빡했고—글로비나의 이름은 거명하지 않았으며, 오직 이탤릭체로 소령의 신부라고만 적어, 그분에게 축복이 깃들기를 빌었다. 그러나 그 결혼 소식은 그녀가 그를 향해 지켜 온 거리감을 거두어 가게 했다. 그녀는 자신이 그를 얼마나 따뜻하고 고맙게 여기는지 인정하고 또 느낄 수 있게 된 것이 기뻤다—그리고 글로비나를 질투한다는 생각에 이르러서는(글로비나라니, 참!), 설령 하늘에서 내려온 천사가 그런 기미를 넌지시 비쳤더라도 애밀리아는 콧방귀를 뀌며 일축했을 것이다. 그날 밤, 조지가 자기가 무척 좋아하는, 윌리엄 도빈 경의 늙은 마부가 모는 조랑말 마차를 타고 돌아왔을 때, 그 아이의 목에는 멋진 금줄과 금시계가 걸려 있었다. 그는 어떤 노부인이, 예쁘지는 않은데, 그것을 주었다고 말했다. 자기한테 자꾸 울면서 입을 맞추더라고 했다. 하지만 자기는 그 부인이 마음에 안 든다고 했다. 자기는 포도가 무척 좋다고 했다. 그리고 자기는 엄마만 좋아한다고 했다. 애밀리아는 움찔하며 흠칫했다. 그 소심한 영혼은 아이 아버지의 친척들이 그 아이를 보았다는 말을 듣고 두려움의 예감에 사로잡혔던 것이다.\n\n오스본 양은 아버지의 만찬을 차려 드리러 돌아왔다. 그는 시내에서 투기로 재미를 본 터라 그날따라 기분이 제법 좋았고, 우연히 딸이 안절부절못하고 있는 것을 알아챘다. \"무슨 일이냐, 오스본 양?\" 그가 황송하게도 입을 열었다.\n\n여자는 울음을 터뜨렸다. \"아, 아버지.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"어린 조지를 봤어요. 천사처럼 어여쁜 데다—그 애를 어쩌면 그렇게 빼닮았는지!\" 그녀 맞은편의 노인은 한마디도 하지 않았으나, 얼굴이 시뻘게지더니 온몸을 떨기 시작했다.\n\n독자는 놀랍겠지만, 이제 일만 마일을 날아 우리 인도 제국의 마드라스 관구에 있는 번들건지 주둔지로 자리를 옮겨 달라는 부탁을 받아야겠다. 우리의 용맹한 옛 벗들, ××연대가 그 용감한 대령 마이클 오다우드 경의 지휘 아래 주둔하고 있는 곳이다. 세월은 이 건장한 장교에게 너그러웠으니, 위장이 튼튼하고 성품이 너그러우며 두뇌의 피로에 그리 시달리지 않는 사내들에게 흔히 그러하듯 말이다. 대령은 점심 식사 때 칼과 포크를 솜씨 좋게 놀리고, 저녁 식사 때 다시 그 무기들을 들고 대단한 성공을 거둔다. 두 끼의 식사 뒤에는 늘 물담배를 피우는데, 워털루에서 프랑스군의 포화 아래에서 그러했던 것만큼이나, 아내가 그를 나무라는 동안에도 조용히 연기를 뿜는다. 나이와 더위도 멀로니 가문과 몰로이 가문 후손의 활력이나 달변을 조금도 줄이지 못했다. 우리의 옛 지인인 부인께서는 브뤼셀에서나 마드라스에서나, 막사 안에서나 천막 아래에서나 똑같이 편안해하신다. 행군 중에는 왕실용 코끼리 위에 올라 연대 선두에 앉은 그녀를 볼 수 있었으니, 참으로 당당한 광경이었다. 그 짐승에 올라탄 채 그녀는 정글에서 호랑이를 상대로 전투에 나섰고, 토착 군주들의 영접을 받았으니, 그들은 그녀와 글로비나를 자기네 규방 깊숙이 맞아들이고 숄과 보석을 권했으며, 그녀로서는 그것을 거절하는 일이 가슴 아플 지경이었다. 어느 병과의 보초든 그녀가 나타나는 곳마다 경례를 올렸고, 그녀는 그 경례에 진중하게 모자에 손을 대어 답했다. 오다우드 부인은 마드라스 관구에서 가장 지체 높은 숙녀 가운데 하나이니, 배석 판사 마이노스 스미스 경의 부인 스미스 부인과 벌인 그녀의 다툼은 지금도 마드라스의 몇몇 사람이 기억하고 있다. 그때 대령 부인은 판사 부인의 면전에서 손가락을 딱 튕기며, 자기는 한낱 거지 같은 문관 따위의 뒤를 절대 따라 걷지 않겠노라고 말했던 것이다. 지금까지도, 그것이 이십오 년 전 일이건만, 사람들은 오다우드 부인이 총독 관저에서 지그 춤을 추던 모습을 기억한다. 그녀는 부관 두 명과 마드라스 기병대의 소령 한 명, 그리고 문관 두 명을 춤으로 거꾸러뜨렸으며, ××연대 부지휘관인 도빈 소령(바스 훈작사)이 야식방으로 물러가시라 권하자, 지쳤으나 아직 흡족하지 못한 채 물러갔던 것이다.\n\n* 라사타 논둠 사티아타 레케시트: \"지쳤으나 아직 흡족하지 못한 채 물러갔다\"는 뜻의 라틴어 구절.\n페기 오다우드는 참으로 변함이 없으니, 행동과 생각에서 다정하고, 성미는 불같으며, 명령하기를 좋아하고, 남편 마이클에게는 폭군이요, 연대의 모든 부인들 사이에서는 용 같은 존재이며, 젊은 사내들 모두에게는 어머니 같은 존재였다. 그들이 병들면 보살피고, 곤경에 처하면 두둔해 주니, 페기 부인은 그 청년들 사이에서 엄청난 인기를 누렸다. 그러나 소위와 대위의 부인들은(소령은 미혼이었다) 그녀를 두고 꽤나 작당하여 험담을 늘어놓았다. 그들 말로는 글로비나가 잘난 척한다느니, 페기 본인이 견딜 수 없을 만큼 위세를 부린다느니 하는 것이었다. 그녀는 커크 부인이 꾸린 자그마한 기도 모임에 끼어들어, 젊은 사내들을 비웃어 그 설교 자리에서 쫓아 버렸다. 군인의 아내가 목사 노릇을 할 일이 아니라느니, 커크 부인은 차라리 남편 옷이나 깁는 편이 훨씬 나을 거라느니 하면서, 연대에 설교가 필요하다면 자기에게 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 설교, 곧 자기 숙부인 수석 사제의 설교가 있다고 했다. 또한 그녀는 연대의 스터블 중위가 외과의의 아내와 시작한 시시덕거림을 단칼에 끝장내 버렸으니, 당장 그만두고 병가를 내어 케이프로 떠나지 않으면 그가 자기에게서 빌린 돈을 받아 내겠다고 으름장을 놓은 것이다(그 청년은 여전히 헤픈 성미였다). 다른 한편으로 그녀는 어느 밤 격분한 남편이 두 번째 브랜디 병을 휘두르며 뒤쫓는 가운데 자기 방갈로에서 도망쳐 나온 포스키 부인을 거두어 보살펴 주었고, 또 실제로 포스키를 진전섬망에서 끌어내어, 모든 악습이 사내에게 그러하듯 그 장교에게 점점 자라난 음주 습관을 끊게 만들었다. 한마디로, 역경에 처해서는 더없이 훌륭한 위로자였고, 행운이 따를 때는 더없이 성가신 벗이었으니, 늘 자기 자신에 대해 더없이 흡족한 견해를 품고, 무슨 일이든 제 뜻대로 하고야 말겠다는 꺾을 수 없는 결의를 지니고 있었다.\n\n여러 가지 가운데서도 그녀는 글로비나를 우리의 옛 벗 도빈과 짝지어야겠다고 마음을 굳혔다. 오다우드 부인은 소령의 재산 기대치를 알았고, 그의 훌륭한 자질과 그 직업에서 누리는 높은 명망을 높이 샀다. 글로비나는 더없이 잘생기고 혈색이 싱그러우며 검은 머리에 푸른 눈을 한 젊은 숙녀로, 콕 주의 어느 처녀 못지않게 말을 탈 줄도, 소나타를 연주할 줄도 알았으니, 도빈의 행복을 보장해 줄 운명을 타고난 바로 그 사람으로 보였다. 그가 늘 그토록 애를 태우던 저 가엾고 착하고 자그마하고 기개 없는 애밀리아보다 훨씬 더 어울렸던 것이다. \"글로비나가 방에 들어서는 걸 보세요.\" 오다우드 부인은 말하곤 했다. \"그러고는 그 가엾은 오스본 부인하고 비교해 보세요, 거위한테 훠이 소리 하나 못 지르는 그 여자 말이에요. 글로비나야말로 소령님께 어울리는 사람이에요. 소령님은 본디 말수가 적은 분이니, 대신 말해 줄 사람이 있어야지요. 그리고 비록 그 애가 멀로니 가문이나 몰로이 가문만큼 좋은 핏줄은 아니라 해도, 분명히 말씀드리지만 그 애는 어떤 귀족이라도 사돈으로 맺기를 자랑스러워할 만한 유서 깊은 집안 출신이랍니다.\"\n\n그러나 글로비나가 그런 결심에 이르러 자신의 애교로 도빈 소령을 굴복시키기로 작정하기 전에, 그녀가 다른 데서 그 애교를 꽤나 갈고닦았다는 사실은 인정해야겠다. 그녀는 더블린에서 한 사교철을 보냈고, 콕과 킬라니, 맬로에서는 또 몇 차례나 보냈는지 누가 알겠는가? 그녀는 자기 고장의 주둔지가 배출한 결혼 적령기의 장교들 모두와, 그리고 자격이 있어 보이는 독신 향사들 모두와 시시덕거렸다. 그녀를 그토록 모질게 대한 바스의 그 목사 말고도, 아일랜드에서 약혼한 것만 열 번이 넘었다. 람천더호 동인도 무역선의 선장과 일등 항해사를 상대로는 마드라스로 오는 내내 시시덕거렸고, 연대 소령이 주둔지 지휘를 맡는 동안 마침 그곳에 머물던 오빠와 오다우드 부인과 함께 관구에서 한 사교철을 보냈다. 그곳에서 다들 그녀를 흠모했고, 다들 그녀와 춤을 추었으나, 결혼할 만한 가치가 있는 자로서 청혼한 이는 아무도 없었다. 지극히 어린 소위 한둘이 그녀를 그리며 한숨을 쉬었고, 수염도 안 난 문관 한둘이 그러했으나, 그녀는 이런 자들을 제 격에 못 미친다 하여 물리쳤다. 그러는 사이 글로비나보다 더 어린 처녀들이 그녀보다 먼저 시집을 갔다. 인생에서 이런 팔자를 타고난 여자들이 있으니, 잘생긴 여자도 그러하다. 그들은 더없이 후하게 사랑에 빠지고, 마흔에 가까워지도록 육군 명부의 절반과 더불어 말을 타고 산책을 하건만, 그래도 오그래디 가의 처녀들은 여전히 오그래디 가의 처녀들로 남는 것이다. 글로비나는, 오다우드 부인이 판사 부인과 그 불운한 다툼만 벌이지 않았더라면 마드라스에서 좋은 혼처를 잡았을 거라고 우겼다. 문관의 우두머리였던 노신사 처트니 씨가(그는 나중에 막 유럽의 학교를 마치고 도착한 겨우 열세 살짜리 젊은 숙녀 돌비 양과 결혼했다) 그녀에게 막 청혼하려던 참이었다는 것이다.\n\n자, 비록 오다우드 부인과 글로비나가 날마다 수도 없이, 그것도 거의 생각할 수 있는 온갖 주제를 두고 다투기는 했지만(사실 믹 오다우드가 천사 같은 성품을 지니지 않았더라면, 그런 두 여자가 끊임없이 귓전에서 들볶아 댄 통에 정신이 나가고 말았을 것이다), 그래도 이 한 가지 점에서만큼은 둘이 뜻을 같이했으니, 글로비나가 도빈 소령과 결혼해야 한다는 것이었다. 그리고 그 일이 성사되기 전에는 소령이 절대 마음 편할 날이 없도록 하겠다고 작심한 것이었다. 마흔, 쉰 번에 이르는 앞선 패배에도 굴하지 않고 글로비나는 그에게 공성을 펼쳤다. 그녀는 끊임없이 그를 향해 아일랜드 가락을 불렀다. \"그대 정자로 오시려나요?\" 하고 어찌나 자주, 또 어찌나 애절하게 물었는지, 감정 있는 사내라면 그 초대를 어떻게 거역할 수 있었을지 신기할 따름이었다. \"슬픔의 젊은 날도 시들었던가요?\" 하고 묻기를 결코 지치는 법이 없었고, 그의 위험과 출정 이야기를 들으면 데스데모나처럼 귀 기울이며 눈물 흘릴 채비가 되어 있었다. 우리의 정직하고 다정한 옛 벗이 남몰래 플루트를 연주하곤 했다는 말은 앞서도 했거니와, 글로비나는 그와 이중주를 하겠다고 고집을 부렸고, 그 젊은 두 사람이 그러고 있을 때면 오다우드 부인은 자리에서 일어나 짐짓 자연스러운 척 방을 나가곤 했다. 글로비나는 아침마다 소령에게 자기와 함께 말을 타라고 강요했다. 주둔지 전체가 그들이 나섰다가 돌아오는 모습을 보았다. 그녀는 끊임없이 그의 집으로 쪽지를 써 보내고, 그의 책을 빌려 가서는 자기 마음에 와닿는 감상적이거나 익살스러운 구절마다 굵직한 연필 자국으로 줄을 그어 놓았다. 그의 말도, 하인도, 숟가락도, 가마도 빌렸으니, 세상 소문이 그녀를 그의 짝으로 점찍고, 영국에 있는 소령의 누이들이 곧 올케를 보게 되리라 짐작한 것도 당연한 일이었다.\n\n이렇게 맹렬한 공성에 시달리던 도빈은, 그러는 내내 더없이 얄미울 만큼 태평한 상태였다. 연대의 젊은 친구들이 글로비나가 그에게 베푸는 노골적인 관심을 두고 놀려 댈 때면 그는 웃어넘기곤 했다. \"흥!\" 그가 말했다. \"그저 솜씨를 녹슬지 않게 하는 거라고. 토저 부인의 피아노를 가지고 연습하듯이 날 가지고 연습하는 거야. 주둔지에서 가장 손쉬운 악기니까. 나는 너무 닳고 늙어서 글로비나 같은 어여쁜 젊은 숙녀에게는 어림없는 상대거든.\" 그러면서 그는 계속 그녀와 말을 타고, 그녀의 앨범에 악보와 시구를 베껴 적어 주고, 더없이 고분고분하게 그녀와 체스를 두었다. 인도에 주둔하는 어떤 장교들은 이런 소박한 놀이로 한가한 시간을 흘려보내는 데 익숙하기 때문이다. 반면 가정적인 기질이 덜한 다른 장교들은 멧돼지를 사냥하고 도요새를 쏘거나, 노름을 하고 여송연을 피우며 브랜디 물에 빠져 지냈다. 마이클 오다우드 경으로 말하자면, 부인과 그 처제가 둘 다 소령을 불러 분명히 입장을 밝히게 하고 그렇게 부끄러운 짓으로 가엾고 죄 없는 처녀를 계속 괴롭히지 못하게 하라고 그를 다그쳤건만, 노병은 그런 모의에 일절 끼어들기를 딱 잘라 거부했다. \"이런, 소령은 다 큰 사람이니 알아서 고를 거요.\" 마이클 경이 말했다. \"자네가 필요하면 자네한테 청할 거요.\" 그러지 않으면 그는 그 일을 농으로 돌려 버리며, \"도빈은 살림을 차리기엔 너무 어려서 집에다 엄마 허락을 받겠다고 편지를 써 보냈다네\"라고 단언하곤 했다. 아니, 그는 한 걸음 더 나아가, 소령과 사사로이 이야기를 나눌 때면 그를 조심시키고 놀려 대기까지 했다. \"정신 똑바로 차리게, 도브, 이 사람아. 그 처자들이 무슨 일을 벌이려고 단단히 작정했어. 우리 부인이 방금 유럽에서 옷 한 상자를 받았는데, 글로비나한테 줄 분홍 새틴 드레스가 들어 있다네. 여자나 새틴이 자넬 움직일 힘이 있다면, 그게 자넬 끝장내 버릴 거야, 도브.\"\n\n그러나 진실을 말하자면, 미모도 유행도 그를 정복하지 못했다. 우리의 정직한 벗은 머릿속에 여자에 대한 단 하나의 관념만을 품고 있었고, 그 하나는 분홍 새틴을 두른 글로비나 오다우드 양과는 조금도 닮지 않았다. 검은 옷을 입은 부드럽고 자그마한 여인, 큰 눈에 갈색 머리를 하고, 말을 거는 때 말고는 좀처럼 입을 열지 않으며, 그럴 때면 글로비나 양과는 조금도 닮지 않은 목소리로 말하는 여인. 갓난아기를 돌보며 미소로 소령을 불러 아이를 들여다보게 하는 부드러운 젊은 어머니. 러셀 광장의 방으로 노래하며 들어오거나, 행복하게 사랑에 겨워 조지 오스본의 팔에 매달려 있던 발그레한 볼의 아가씨. 우리의 정직한 소령의 마음을 밤낮으로 채우고 늘 그 마음을 지배한 것은 오직 이 모습뿐이었다. 어쩌면 애밀리아는 소령이 마음속에 빚어 둔 초상과 닮지 않았을지도 모른다. 영국에 있는 누이들이 가지고 있던 유행 화보집 속에 한 인물이 있었는데, 윌리엄은 그것을 남몰래 빼돌려 자기 책상 뚜껑 안쪽에 붙여 두고는, 그 그림이 오스본 부인과 어딘가 닮았다고 상상하곤 했다. 그러나 나는 그것을 보았고, 그것이 한낱 허리선이 높은 가운 그림에 불과하며 그 위로 도무지 있을 법하지 않은 인형 같은 얼굴이 새치름하게 웃고 있을 뿐임을 장담할 수 있다. 어쩌면 도빈 씨의 감상에 젖은 애밀리아도, 그가 소중히 간직한 이 우스꽝스러운 작은 그림이 실물과 닮지 않은 것 이상으로 실물과 닮지 않았을지 모른다. 그러나 우리 가운데 사랑에 빠진 사내치고 누가 그보다 더 잘 안단 말인가? 또 자기 환상을 똑똑히 보고 인정한들 그가 더 행복해지기나 하던가? 도빈은 이 마법에 걸려 있었다. 그는 자기 감정을 두고 벗들이나 세상 사람들을 그리 성가시게 하지 않았고, 그 때문에 타고난 잠이나 식욕을 잃지도 않았다. 우리가 그를 마지막으로 본 이래 그의 머리는 희끗희끗해졌고, 부드러운 갈색 머리칼 사이로도 은빛 줄기 한두 가닥이 보였다. 그러나 그의 감정은 조금도 변하거나 늙지 않았고, 그의 사랑은 사내가 소년 시절을 떠올릴 때만큼이나 싱그럽게 남아 있었다.\n\n소령의 유럽 쪽 서신 상대인 도빈 자매와 애밀리아가 그에게 영국에서 편지를 보냈다는 이야기는 앞서 했거니와, 오스본 부인은 그가 오다우드 양과 곧 혼인하게 된 것을 두고 더없이 솔직하고 다정하게 축하의 말을 전했다. \"댁의 누이께서 방금 친절하게도 저를 찾아 주셔서,\" 애밀리아는 편지에 이렇게 적었다. \"한 흥미로운 사건을 알려 주셨는데, 그에 대해 더없이 진심 어린 축하를 드리고자 합니다. 듣자 하니 당신과 맺어지실 그 젊은 숙녀가, 그 자신이 더없이 다정하고 선량한 분께 모든 면에서 걸맞은 사람으로 드러나기를 바랍니다. 가엾은 과부에게는 그저 기도를 올리고 당신의 번영을 진심으로, 진심으로 빌어 드리는 일밖에 없네요! 조지가 사랑하는 대부님께 안부를 전하며, 부디 자기를 잊지 말아 달라고 합니다. 저는 그 애에게, 당신께서 당신의 모든 애정을 받기에 마땅한 분과 새로운 인연을 맺으려 하신다고, 그리고 그런 인연이 물론 가장 강하고 신성한 것이어서 다른 모든 인연을 앞설 테지만, 그래도 당신께서 늘 지켜 주고 사랑해 주신 과부와 아이에게도 분명 당신 마음 한구석에 자리가 있을 거라고 일러 주었답니다.\" 앞서 언급한 그 편지는 이런 투로 이어지면서, 글쓴이가 더없이 흡족하다는 것을 처음부터 끝까지 거듭 주장하고 있었다.\n\n이 편지는 다름 아닌, 런던에서 오다우드 부인의 모자 상자를 실어 온 바로 그 배편으로 도착했는데(그리고 도빈이 우편으로 받은 다른 어떤 꾸러미보다 이것을 먼저 뜯어 보았으리라는 것은 굳이 말할 필요도 없다), 받은 이의 마음을 어찌나 어지럽혀 놓았던지 글로비나도, 그녀의 분홍 새틴도, 그녀에게 딸린 모든 것이 그에게는 완전히 역겨워졌다. 소령은 여자들의 수다를, 그리고 여성이라는 부류 전체를 저주했다. 그날은 모든 것이 그를 짜증나게 했으니, 사열은 견딜 수 없이 덥고 따분했다. 맙소사! 지성을 갖춘 사내가 날이면 날마다 가죽 멜빵이나 점검하고 멍청이들에게 제식 훈련이나 시키며 인생을 허비해야 한단 말인가? 식당에서 떠드는 젊은이들의 분별없는 잡담이 그 어느 때보다 거슬렸다. 마흔을 향해 한길로 달려가는 사내가, 스미스 중위가 도요새를 몇 마리나 잡았는지, 브라운 소위의 암말이 어떤 솜씨를 부렸는지 안들 무슨 상관이란 말인가? 식탁에서 오가는 농담은 그를 부끄러움으로 가득 채웠다. 그는 너무 늙어서 외과 보조의의 익살이나 젊은이들의 속어 따위를 듣고 앉아 있을 수가 없었다. 대머리에 붉은 얼굴을 한 늙은 오다우드는 그런 농담에도 아주 수월하게 웃어 댔다. 노인은 지난 삼십 년 내내 그 농담들을 들어 왔고, 도빈 자신도 십오 년째 그것들을 듣고 있었다. 게다가 식탁의 요란하고 따분한 분위기 뒤에는, 연대 부인들의 다툼과 험담이 있었으니! 견딜 수 없고 부끄러운 노릇이었다. \"오, 애밀리아, 애밀리아.\" 그는 생각했다. \"내가 그토록 변함없이 충실했던 그대가, 나를 나무라다니! 그대가 나를 헤아려 줄 수 없기에 내가 이 따분한 삶을 질질 끌고 가는 거요. 그런데 그대는 몇 해에 걸친 헌신의 대가로, 글쎄, 이 요란뻑적지근한 아일랜드 처녀와의 결혼을 축복해 주다니!\" 가엾은 윌리엄은 속이 상하고 서글펐고, 그 어느 때보다 비참하고 외로웠다. 그는 삶과 그 허영 전부와 영영 인연을 끊고 싶었으니, 그 몸부림이 그토록 헛되고 보람 없으며, 그 앞날이 그토록 황량하고 쓸쓸해 보였던 것이다. 그는 그날 밤 내내 한잠도 못 이루고 누워, 고향으로 돌아가기를 갈망했다. 애밀리아의 편지는 그에게 텅 빈 공백처럼 떨어졌다. 그 어떤 충절도, 변함없는 진심과 열정도 그녀를 따뜻하게 움직일 수 없었다. 그녀는 그가 자기를 사랑한다는 것을 보려 하지 않았다. 침대에서 뒤척이며 그는 그녀에게 소리 내어 말했다. \"맙소사, 애밀리아!\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 이 세상에서 오직 그대만을 사랑한다는 걸 모른단 말이오. 내게는 돌덩이 같은 그대를. 몇 달이고 몇 달이고 병과 슬픔에 잠긴 그대를 돌보았건만, 얼굴에 미소를 띤 채 내게 작별을 고하고는, 우리 사이에 문이 닫히기도 전에 나를 잊어버린 그대를!\" 그의 베란다 바깥에 누워 있던 토착 하인들은, 평소에는 그토록 냉정하고 조용하던 소령이 지금은 이토록 격정에 휩싸여 풀이 죽은 모습을 보며 의아해했다. 그녀가 그를 보았더라면 가엾이 여겼을까? 그는 그녀에게서 받은 모든 편지를 읽고 또 읽었다. 남편이 그녀에게 남긴 것이라 그가 믿게 만든 자그마한 재산에 관한 사무적인 편지들, 짤막한 초대 쪽지들, 그녀가 그에게 보낸 글이라면 글자 하나하나까지. 그 편지들이 얼마나 차갑고, 얼마나 다정하고, 얼마나 가망 없고, 얼마나 이기적이던가!\n\n이 말 없고 너그러운 마음을 읽고 헤아려 줄 따뜻하고 부드러운 영혼이 가까이 있었더라면, 어쩌면 애밀리아의 치세가 끝나고 우리 벗 윌리엄의 사랑이 더 다정한 물줄기로 흘러들었을지 누가 알겠는가? 그러나 그가 허물없이 사귀는 사람이라고는 칠흑 같은 곱슬머리의 글로비나뿐이었고, 이 늠름한 젊은 여자는 소령을 사랑하려는 게 아니라 오히려 소령이 자기를 흠모하게 만들려는 데 마음을 쏟고 있었다. 그것도 더없이 헛되고 가망 없는 일이었으니, 적어도 가엾은 처녀가 그 일을 해낼 만한 수단을 가진 것으로 보아 그러했다. 그녀는 머리를 곱슬곱슬 말고 그를 향해 어깨를 드러내 보였으니, 마치 이렇게 칠흑 같은 곱슬머리와 이런 살결을 본 적 있느냐고 말하는 듯했다. 그를 향해 활짝 웃어 보여 머릿속 이가 하나하나 다 성하다는 것을 보이려 했건만, 그는 이 모든 매력에 도무지 눈길도 주지 않았다. 모자 상자가 도착하고 얼마 지나지 않아, 아마도 정말로 그것을 기념하여, 오다우드 부인과 국왕 연대의 부인들은 주둔지의 회사 소속 연대와 문관들을 위해 무도회를 열었다. 글로비나는 그 사람 잡을 분홍 드레스를 뽐냈고, 무도회에 참석하여 더없이 처량하게 방 안을 오락가락하던 소령은 그 분홍 옷을 알아보기는커녕 눈길조차 주지 않았다. 글로비나가 주둔지의 젊은 소위들과 어울려 약이 오른 채 그의 곁을 스치며 춤추었건만, 소령은 그녀의 춤 솜씨를 조금도 시샘하지 않았고, 기병대의 뱅글스 대위가 그녀를 야식방으로 데려간다고 화를 내지도 않았다. 그를 움직일 수 있는 것은 질투도, 드레스도, 어깨도 아니었고, 글로비나에게는 그 이상 가진 것이 없었다.\n\n그리하여 이 두 사람은 각기 이 삶의 허영을 몸소 보여 주고 있었으니, 저마다 손에 넣을 수 없는 것을 갈망했던 것이다. 글로비나는 실패에 분이 받쳐 울었다. 그녀는 흐느끼며, 자기가 \"다른 누구보다도\" 소령에게 마음을 두었노라고 털어놓았다. \"그이가 내 가슴을 찢어 놓을 거야, 정말이야, 페기.\" 그녀는 올케와 사이가 좋을 때면 이렇게 칭얼대곤 했다. \"정말이지 내 드레스를 다 줄여야 한다니까. 내가 점점 해골처럼 되어 가고 있단 말이야.\" 살이 찌든 빠지든, 웃든 우울하든, 말을 타든 피아노 의자에 앉든, 소령에게는 매한가지였다. 그리고 대령은 파이프를 뻐끔거리며 이런 푸념을 듣다가, 글로리는 다음번 런던에서 오는 상자에 검은 드레스나 몇 벌 받는 게 좋겠다고 권하고는, 아일랜드의 어느 부인이 남편을 잃은 슬픔으로 죽었는데 그것도 남편을 채 하나 얻기도 전이었다는 알쏭달쏭한 이야기를 들려주는 것이었다.\n\n소령이 이렇게 사람 애간장 태우는 식으로, 청혼도 하지 않고 사랑에 빠지기도 거부하며 지내는 동안, 유럽에서 또 다른 배 한 척이 편지를 싣고 들어왔는데, 그중에는 이 무정한 사내에게 온 편지도 몇 통 있었다. 이것들은 앞선 꾸러미보다 더 이른 소인이 찍힌 고향 편지들이었다. 도빈 소령이 그 가운데서 누이의 필체를 알아보았으니—그녀는 오라비에게 편지를 쓸 때 늘 가로로 쓰고 그 위에 다시 세로로 겹쳐 썼고, 그러모을 수 있는 온갖 나쁜 소식이란 소식은 죄다 긁어모아, 누이다운 솔직함으로 그를 나무라고 훈계를 늘어놓았으며, \"더없이 사랑하는 윌리엄\"이 그녀의 서한 한 통을 다 읽고 나면 으레 하루 종일 비참한 기분에 빠뜨려 놓곤 했다—고로, 진실을 말하자면, 더없이 사랑받는 윌리엄은 도빈 양의 편지 봉인을 서둘러 뜯지 않고, 그러기에 유달리 좋은 날과 기분을 기다린 것이었다. 게다가 보름 전, 그는 그녀가 오스본 부인에게 그런 터무니없는 이야기를 전한 것을 꾸짖는 편지를 써 보냈고, 그 부인에게도 답장을 부쳐, 자기에 관한 소문에 대해 그 오해를 풀어 주면서 \"현재로서는 자기 처지를 바꿀 어떤 생각도 전혀 없다\"고 다짐해 둔 터였다.\n\n두 번째 편지 꾸러미가 도착하고 나서 이삼 일이 지난 어느 밤, 소령은 오다우드 부인의 집에서 꽤 유쾌하게 저녁을 보냈는데, 글로비나는 그가 평소보다 좀 더 주의 깊게 자기 노래에 귀 기울인다고 여겼다. \"강물의 만남\"이며 \"음유 소년\"이며, 그 밖에 한두 곡의 노래를 그에게 들려주었던 것이다(사실인즉 그는 바깥 달빛 아래에서 자칼이 울부짖는 소리에 귀 기울이지 않는 것만큼이나 글로비나의 노래에도 귀를 기울이지 않았으니, 그 착각은 늘 그렇듯 그녀의 몫이었다). 그러고는 그녀와 체스 한 판을 둔 뒤(외과의와 하는 크리비지가 오다우드 부인이 가장 좋아하는 저녁 소일거리였다), 도빈 소령은 늘 그러던 시각에 대령 가족에게 작별을 고하고 자기 집으로 물러갔다.\n\n거기, 그의 탁자 위에는 누이의 편지가 그를 나무라듯 놓여 있었다. 그는 그것을 그동안 소홀히 한 것이 다소 부끄러워 집어 들고는, 그 갈겨쓴 글씨의 멀리 있는 친지와 한 시간 남짓 불쾌한 교감을 나눌 채비를 했다…… 소령이 대령 집을 떠나고 한 시간쯤 지났을 무렵이었으리라. 마이클 경은 의인의 잠을 자고 있었고, 글로비나는 검은 곱슬머리를 평소처럼 그것을 단단히 마는 데 쓰는 수많은 자그마한 종잇조각으로 둘둘 말아 놓았으며, 오다우드 부인 역시 일층 신방의 침대에 들어 그 고운 몸뚱이에 모기장을 둘러친 참이었다. 그때 지휘관 관저 울안의 문을 지키던 경비병은 달빛 속에서 도빈 소령이 빠른 걸음에 몹시 동요한 낯빛으로 집을 향해 달려오는 모습을 보았고, 그가 보초를 지나쳐 대령 침실 창문 앞으로 다가가는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"오다우드—대령!\" 도빈이 외치며 큰 소리로 부르기를 그치지 않았다.\n\n\"어머나, 소령님!\" 머리에 종이를 만 글로비나도 자기 창문에서 머리를 내밀며 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 일인가, 도브, 이 사람아?\" 대령이 주둔지에 불이라도 났거나 본부에서 진군 명령이 내려온 줄 알고 말했다.\n\n\"저는—저는 휴가를 얻어야겠습니다. 영국으로 가야 해요—더없이 긴급한 사사로운 일로요.\" 도빈이 말했다.\n\n\"맙소사, 무슨 일이 일어난 거지!\" 글로비나는 종이 말이를 온통 떨면서 생각했다.\n\n\"저는 떠나야 합니다—지금—오늘 밤에요.\" 도빈이 말을 이었고, 대령은 자리에서 일어나 그와 이야기를 나누러 나왔다.\n\n도빈 양이 가로세로로 겹쳐 쓴 편지의 추신에서, 소령은 마침 다음과 같은 대목을 맞닥뜨린 참이었다. \"어제 마차를 타고 오라버니의 옛 지인인 오스본 부인을 보러 갔어요. 그들이 파산한 뒤로 사는 그 비참한 곳 말이에요, 아시잖아요. 세들리 씨는, 그 오두막(별로 나을 것도 없어요) 문에 붙은 놋쇠 명패로 미루어 보건대 석탄 장수더군요. 그 어린 사내아이, 오라버니의 대자는 분명 잘생긴 아이이긴 한데, 조숙하고 건방지고 고집스러운 구석이 있어요. 그래도 우리는 오라버니가 바라시는 대로 그 애를 눈여겨보아 왔고, 그 애 고모인 오스본 양에게 소개해 주었더니 고모도 그 애를 제법 마음에 들어 했어요. 어쩌면 그 애의 할아버지가, 거의 노망이 든 그 파산한 할아버지 말고 러셀 광장의 오스본 씨 말이에요, 자기의 그릇되고 고집 센 아들의 벗인 오라버니의 그 대자를 너그러이 받아들이도록 설득될 수도 있겠지요. 그리고 애밀리아도 그 애를 내주기를 그리 꺼리지 않을 거예요. 그 과부는 마음을 추슬렀고, 곧 어느 목사님과 결혼한다는군요. 브롬프턴의 부목사 가운데 한 분인 비니 목사라나요. 변변찮은 혼처죠. 하지만 오스본 부인도 점점 나이가 들어서, 그 머리에 흰 것이 잔뜩 보이더군요. 그래도 그녀는 아주 기분이 좋아 보였어요. 그리고 오라버니의 그 어린 대자는 우리 집에서 과식을 했답니다. 어머니가 사랑을 전하시고, 오라버니를 아끼는 누이 앤 도빈도 사랑을 전합니다.\"\n\n우리의 옛 벗 크롤리 집안의 그레이트곤트 거리 저택은, 피트 크롤리 경의 별세를 애도하는 표시로 내걸린 문장(紋章)을 아직도 정면에 달고 있었다. 그러나 이 문장 장식 자체가 실은 더없이 화려하고 요란한 장식물이었으니, 저택의 나머지 부분도 작고한 준남작이 다스리던 시절보다 한층 더 눈부시게 단장되어 갔다. 벽돌을 뒤덮고 있던 검은 외피가 벗겨지니, 벽돌들은 흰 줄무늬가 섞인 발그레하고 명랑한 얼굴을 드러냈다. 문고리에 달린 낡은 청동 사자들은 보기 좋게 도금되었고, 난간은 새로 칠해졌다. 그리하여 그레이트곤트 거리에서 가장 음울하던 집은, 늙은 피트 크롤리 경이 마지막으로 그 아래를 지나갈 때 퀸스크롤리 가로수 길의 나무들에 매달려 있던 누런 잎들이 햄프셔의 푸른 잎으로 갈리기도 전에, 그 일대에서 가장 말쑥한 집이 되어 있었다.\n\n이 저택 주위에는 거기에 걸맞은 마차를 거느린 자그마한 여인 하나가 끊임없이 모습을 드러냈다. 또한 어린 사내아이를 데리고 날마다 그곳을 찾아오는 나이 지긋한 노처녀의 모습도 눈에 띄곤 했다. 브리그스 양과 어린 로던이었다. 이들이 맡은 일이란 피트 경 저택의 내부 수리를 챙기는 것, 블라인드와 휘장을 꿰매는 여자 일꾼들을 감독하는 것, 두 세대에 걸친 크롤리 부인들의 더러운 유물과 한데 쌓인 잡동사니로 빼곡한 서랍과 찬장을 쑤시고 뒤지는 것, 그리고 벽장과 광에 든 도자기며 유리그릇이며 그 밖의 살림살이를 일일이 목록으로 작성하는 것이었다.\n\n로던 크롤리 부인은 이 모든 정비 작업의 총사령관이었으니, 피트 경으로부터 가구를 팔고, 바꾸고, 압류하고, 사들이라는 전권을 위임받은 터였다. 그녀는 자신의 안목과 재간을 마음껏 펼칠 수 있는 이 일에 적잖이 즐거워했다. 저택을 새로 단장하기로 결정된 것은, 피트 경이 십일월에 변호사들을 만나러 상경하여, 애정 깊은 아우 부부의 지붕 아래 커즌 거리에서 거의 일주일을 머물던 무렵이었다.\n\n피트 경은 처음에는 호텔에 묵었으나, 베키는 준남작이 도착했다는 소식을 듣자마자 혼자서 그를 맞으러 달려갔고, 한 시간 뒤에는 피트 경을 마차에 나란히 태우고 커즌 거리로 돌아왔다. 이 천진난만한 작은 인물의 환대란, 어찌나 다정하게 권하고 또 어찌나 솔직하고 살갑게 베풀어지는지 도무지 뿌리칠 수가 없을 때가 더러 있는 법이었다. 피트가 오겠노라 응낙하자, 베키는 감격에 겨워 그의 손을 덥석 잡았다. \"고마워요.\" 그녀는 그 손을 꼭 쥐고는, 얼굴이 벌게진 준남작의 눈을 들여다보며 말했다. \"로던이 얼마나 기뻐할까요!\" 그녀는 피트의 침실로 종종걸음 쳐 올라가며, 그의 트렁크를 그리로 나르는 하인들을 앞장서서 이끌었다. 이윽고 그녀 자신이 자기 방에서 석탄 통을 들고 웃으며 들어왔다.\n\n피트 경의 방에는 이미 불이 활활 타오르고 있었다(덧붙이자면, 그 방은 본래 브리그스 양의 방으로, 브리그스 양은 위층으로 보내져 하녀와 함께 자게 되었다). \"내가 기어이 모시고 올 줄 알았다니까요.\" 그녀가 두 눈에 기쁨을 반짝이며 말했다. 사실 그녀는 그를 손님으로 맞은 것이 진심으로 기뻤던 것이다.\n\n피트가 머무는 동안, 베키는 로던에게 한두 번 볼일을 핑계로 밖에서 저녁을 들게 했고, 그리하여 준남작은 그녀와 브리그스와 더불어 단란한 저녁을 보냈다. 그녀는 손수 부엌으로 내려가 정말로 그를 위해 자잘한 요리를 만들었다. \"이 살미* 맛있지 않아요?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"아주버님 드리려고 만든 거예요. 이보다 더 맛난 것도 해 드릴 수 있어요, 저를 보러 오시면 꼭 해 드릴게요.\"\n\n\"자네가 하는 일은 무엇이든 다 훌륭해.\" 준남작이 정중하게 말했다. \"이 살미도 정말이지 일품이로군.\"\n\n\"가난한 사내의 아내란,\" 리베카가 명랑하게 받았다. \"쓸모 있는 사람이 되어야 하잖아요.\" 그러자 시아주버니는 \"자네는 황제의 아내가 되어도 손색이 없네, 살림 솜씨가 능란하다는 것이야말로 여인의 가장 매혹적인 덕목 가운데 하나일 게 분명하지\"라고 단언했다. 그러면서 피트 경은 집에 있는 제인 부인을 떠올렸으니, 어딘가 분해지는 심정이었다. 그녀가 기어이 만들겠다고 우겨 저녁 식탁에 올렸던 어떤 파이, 그 더없이 끔찍한 파이를 떠올린 것이었다.\n\n살미 — 그것은 스테인 경이 자신의 스틸브룩 별장에서 보내온 꿩으로 만든 것이었다 — 말고도, 베키는 시아주버니에게 백포도주 한 병을 내놓았다. 로던이 프랑스에서 가져온 것으로, 거저나 다름없이 손에 넣은 것이라고 이 작은 이야기꾼은 둘러댔지만, 사실 그 술은 스테인 후작의 이름난 술 창고에서 나온 화이트 에르미타주였다. 그것이 준남작의 창백한 두 뺨에 불을 지피고 그 쇠약한 몸뚱이에 화색을 돌게 했다.\n\n이윽고 그가 그 백포도주 한 병을 다 비우자, 그녀는 그에게 손을 내밀어 응접실로 데려가서는 난롯가 소파에 아늑하게 자리 잡게 하고, 그가 이야기하도록 내버려 둔 채 더없이 자상하고 다정한 관심으로 귀 기울이며 그의 곁에 앉아, 사랑하는 어린 아들을 위해 셔츠 한 벌을 감치고 있었다. 로던 부인이 유독 겸손하고 정숙한 모습을 보이고 싶을 때면, 이 자그마한 셔츠가 으레 반짇고리에서 나오곤 했다. 그것은 다 짓기도 전에 진작에 로던에게는 너무 작아져 있었다.\n\n자, 리베카는 피트의 이야기에 귀 기울였고, 그에게 말을 건넸고, 그에게 노래를 불러 주었고, 그를 어르고 보듬었다. 그리하여 피트는 그레이스 인의 변호사 사무실에서 커즌 거리의 활활 타는 난롯가로 돌아오는 일이 날이 갈수록 점점 더 즐거워졌다. 이 즐거움은 법률가들도 함께 누리는 것이었으니, 피트의 일장 연설이란 더없이 긴 축에 들었기 때문이다. 그리하여 막상 떠날 때가 되자 그는 작별이 사뭇 가슴 아렸다. 우편 마차에 자리를 잡고 앉은 그를 향해, 그녀가 마차에서 손에 입 맞추어 보이고 손수건을 흔들던 그 모습이 어찌나 어여뻤던지! 그녀는 한 번 손수건을 눈가에 갖다 댔다. 마차가 떠나가자 그는 물개 가죽 모자를 눈 위까지 푹 눌러쓰고는, 등받이에 몸을 묻은 채 속으로 생각했다. 그녀가 나를 얼마나 존경하는지, 또 내가 그 존경을 받아 마땅한 사람인지, 로던이란 제 아내의 값어치를 절반도 못 알아보는 어리석고 둔한 사내라는 것을, 또 저 영민하고 작은 베키에 견주면 제 아내가 얼마나 말없고 멍청한 여자인지를 말이다. 어쩌면 이 모든 생각을 베키가 하나하나 넌지시 일러 준 것이었는지도 모른다. 다만 어찌나 섬세하고 부드럽게 흘렸던지 언제 어디서 그랬는지조차 알기 어려웠을 따름이다. 그리고 헤어지기 전에 두 사람은, 다음 사교철을 위해 런던 저택을 새로 단장하기로, 또 두 형제의 가족이 성탄절에 시골에서 다시 만나기로 합의를 보았다.\n\n\"당신이 그 양반한테서 돈푼이라도 좀 우려냈으면 좋았을 텐데.\" 준남작이 떠난 뒤 로던이 침울하게 아내에게 말했다. \"라글스 영감한테 뭐라도 좀 쥐여 주고 싶단 말이오, 정말이지 안 그러면 사람이 아니지. 그 영감이 받을 돈을 못 받고 있는 건 옳지 않은 일이오, 알잖소. 불편한 노릇일 테고, 또 우리 말고 딴 사람한테 세를 줘 버릴 수도 있는 거요, 알다시피.\"\n\n\"그 사람한테 이렇게 말해요.\" 베키가 말했다. \"피트 경의 재산 문제가 정리되는 대로 모두에게 빚을 갚을 거라고요. 그리고 우선 갚는 셈으로 얼마쯤 쥐여 줘요. 여기 피트가 아이 앞으로 두고 간 수표가 있어요.\" 그러면서 그녀는 가방에서 종이 한 장을 꺼내 남편에게 건넸다. 시아주버니가 크롤리 가문 작은집의 어린 아들이자 상속자를 위해 그녀에게 넘겨준 것이었다.\n\n사실 그녀는 남편이 한번 부딪쳐 보라고 바랐던 그 일을, 손수 떠보았던 것이다. 더없이 조심스럽게 떠보았으나, 안전하지 않다는 것을 알아차렸다. 곤궁하다는 기색을 슬쩍 비치기만 해도 피트 크롤리 경은 화들짝 놀라 꽁무니를 뺐다. 그는 일장 연설을 늘어놓기 시작했으니, 자기야말로 돈 문제로 얼마나 쪼들리는지, 소작인들이 어찌나 돈을 안 내는지, 부친의 재산 문제와 노인의 별세에 따른 비용 탓에 어떻게 얽매여 있는지, 저당 잡힌 빚을 갚아야 한다는 것, 은행과 대리인들에게 진 빚이 한도를 넘어섰다는 것 따위를 설명했다. 그리하여 피트 크롤리는 결국 제수와 적당히 타협하여, 그녀의 어린 아들을 위해 아주 적은 액수를 쥐여 주는 것으로 마무리 지었다.\n\n피트는 제 아우와 그 가족이 얼마나 가난한지 알고 있었다. 로던네 식구가 살아갈 거리라고는 아무것도 없다는 것, 그리고 집과 마차란 거저 굴러가는 게 아니라는 것이, 이토록 냉철하고 노련한 늙은 외교가의 눈을 피해 갈 리 없었다. 그는 자기가 모든 정당한 셈법대로라면 아우에게 돌아갔어야 할 돈을 차지한, 아니 가로챈 자라는 것을 잘 알고 있었다. 그러니 그의 가슴속에는 — 우리는 이를 확신해도 좋으리라 — 어떤 은밀한 양심의 가책이 있었으니, 이 낙담한 친지들에게 무언가 정의로운 행위를, 아니 보상이라 해 두자, 그런 것을 베풀어야 한다고 그를 일깨우는 가책이었다. 정의롭고 점잖으며 머리도 제법 돌아가는 사람, 기도를 올리고 교리문답을 외우며 평생 겉으로는 제 도리를 다해 온 사람인 그로서는, 제 손으로 아우에게 무언가 갚을 것이 있다는 것, 도덕적으로 자신이 로던에게 빚진 자라는 것을 모를 수가 없었던 것이다.\n\n그러나 이따금 타임스 신문 지면에서 읽게 되는, 재무장관이 내는 그 기묘한 공고를 보라. A. B.에게서 50파운드를, 혹은 W. T.에게서 10파운드를 양심 부담금으로 받았음을, 그 A. B.나 W. T.가 마땅히 내야 할 세금조로 받았음을 알리는 공고 말이다. 그 참회자들은 자기네가 낸 돈을 존경하는 장관께서 공공 언론을 통해 확인해 주기를 청하는 것이다. 그런데 재무장관도, 또한 독자도, 의심할 바 없이 늘 확신하기를, 앞서 말한 A. B.와 W. T.는 실제로 빚진 액수 가운데 아주 적은 할부금 한 몫을 치르는 것일 뿐이며, 20파운드 지폐 한 장을 부쳐 오는 자는 십중팔구 마땅히 셈해야 할 돈을 수백, 수천 파운드는 더 가지고 있으리라는 것이다. 적어도 A. B.나 W. T.의 그 미진한 참회 행위를 볼 때 내 심정은 그러하다. 그러니 피트 크롤리가 그토록 많은 이득을 본 아우를 향해 보인 그 뉘우침, 아니 호의라 해도 좋으나, 그것 역시 로던에게 진 빚의 원금에 대한 아주 작은 배당금 한 몫에 지나지 않았으리라는 것을 나는 의심치 않는다. 그나마 그만큼이라도 기꺼이 내놓는 이가 흔치 않다. 돈을 내놓는다는 것은, 질서 감각을 타고난 사람이라면 거의 누구에게나 감당하기 어려운 희생인 것이다. 이웃에게 5파운드를 베풀면서 제가 갸륵한 일을 했다고 여기지 않을 사람이란 세상에 거의 없다. 헤픈 자는 베푸는 자선의 기쁨에서가 아니라 흥청대며 쓰는 게으른 쾌락에서 돈을 내놓는다. 그는 자기 즐거움이라면 단 하나도 마다하지 않는다. 오페라 특별석도, 말도, 만찬도, 심지어 라자로에게 5파운드를 베푸는 그 즐거움조차 마다하지 않는다. 반면 아끼는 자는 선량하고 현명하고 정의로우며 누구에게도 단 한 푼 빚지지 않건만, 거지를 외면하고, 삯마차꾼과 흥정하며, 가난한 친척을 매정하게 내친다. 그러니 둘 중 어느 쪽이 더 이기적인지 나는 분간하기 어렵다. 돈이란 그저 저마다의 눈에 다른 값어치를 지닐 뿐이다.\n\n그리하여 한마디로, 피트 크롤리는 아우를 위해 무언가 해 주어야겠다고 생각했고, 이내 그 일은 언젠가 다른 때 다시 생각해 보자고 마음먹었다.\n\n베키로 말할 것 같으면, 이웃의 관대함에 너무 많은 것을 기대하는 여자가 아니었으니, 피트 크롤리가 그녀를 위해 해 준 일에 자못 만족했다. 그녀는 가문의 수장으로부터 인정을 받은 셈이었다. 설령 피트가 그녀에게 아무것도 내주지 않는다 해도, 언젠가는 그녀를 위해 무언가 마련해 줄 터였다. 시아주버니에게서 돈을 못 받았다 한들, 그녀는 돈이나 다름없는 것, 곧 신용을 얻었다. 라글스는 두 형제가 화목하게 지내는 광경에다 그 자리에서 받은 약간의 돈, 그리고 머지않아 훨씬 큰 액수를 떼어 주겠다는 약속에 마음이 제법 놓였다. 그리고 리베카는 브리그스 양에게 — 그녀가 빌려준 자그마한 돈에 대한 성탄절 배당금을, 베키는 마치 제 금고가 황금으로 넘쳐흐르기라도 하는 양 거리낌 없이 기쁜 얼굴로 치러 주었거니와 — 그러니까 리베카는 브리그스 양에게 은밀히 털어놓기를, 재정 전문가로 이름난 피트 경과 다른 누구도 아닌 브리그스를 위해 의논을 했노라고, 브리그스 양에게 남은 자본을 어떻게 가장 수익성 있게 굴릴지 상의했노라고 했다. 피트 경이 곰곰이 궁리한 끝에, 브리그스가 돈을 굴릴 더없이 안전하고 유리한 길을 생각해 냈다는 것이었다. 작고한 크롤리 양과 온 집안에 정성을 다한 벗인 그녀를 특별히 마음에 두고 있는 데다, 런던을 떠나기 한참 전에 이미 권하기를, 피트 경이 눈여겨보아 둔 주식을 가장 유리한 기회에 사들일 수 있도록 언제든 즉시 돈을 내놓을 채비를 해 두라 했다는 것이다. 가엾은 브리그스 양은 피트 경의 이런 배려에 무척이나 고마워했다. 청하지도 않았는데 베풀어 준 것이라며, 자기로서는 공채에서 돈을 빼낼 생각은 꿈에도 못 했을 거라고 했다. 그 섬세한 마음씀씀이가 호의를 한층 돋보이게 했다. 그녀는 곧장 자기 거래 담당자를 만나 적당한 때에 그 자그마한 현금을 내놓을 채비를 해 두겠노라 약속했다.\n\n이 갸륵한 여인은 이 일에서 베풀어진 리베카의 친절과, 그 너그러운 은인인 대령의 친절에 어찌나 고마워했던지, 밖에 나가 반년치 배당금의 상당 부분을 털어 어린 로던에게 줄 검정 벨벳 외투를 사 왔다. 그런데 덧붙이자면, 그 아이는 이제 검정 벨벳을 입기에는 몸집이 거의 너무 커져, 사내다운 재킷과 바지를 입혀도 좋을 만한 키와 나이에 이르러 있었다.\n\n아이는 얼굴이 훤하고 솔직한 사내아이로, 파란 눈에 물결치는 아마빛 머리를 하고, 팔다리는 단단했으나 마음씨는 너그럽고 여렸으며, 자기에게 잘해 주는 이라면 누구에게나 살갑게 정을 붙였다. 조랑말에게도, 그 말을 준 사우스다운 경에게도(그 다정한 젊은 귀족을 보면 아이는 온몸이 발그레 달아오르곤 했다), 조랑말을 돌보는 마부에게도, 밤이면 유령 이야기와 식탁에 오른 맛난 것들을 잔뜩 안겨 주는 요리사 몰리에게도, 늘 놀려 대고 비웃던 브리그스에게도, 그리고 특히 제 아버지에게도 그러했으니, 그 사내아이를 향한 아버지의 애정 또한 보기에 흥미로운 것이었다. 아이가 여덟 살쯤 자랄 무렵, 그가 정을 붙인 대상은 여기까지였다고 해도 좋으리라. 어머니라는 아름다운 환영은 얼마 못 가 빛이 바래 버렸다. 거의 두 해 가까이 그녀는 아이에게 거의 말을 건네지 않았다. 그녀는 아이를 싫어했다. 아이는 홍역에 걸렸고 백일해도 앓았다. 아이는 그녀를 따분하게 했다. 어느 날 아이가 어머니의 목소리에 이끌려 위층에서 몰래 기어 내려와 층계참에 서 있었으니, 어머니가 스테인 경에게 노래를 불러 주던 참이었다. 그때 응접실 문이 별안간 열리며, 바로 조금 전까지 황홀경에 잠겨 그 노랫소리에 귀 기울이던 이 작은 엿보는 아이가 들통이 났다.\n\n어머니가 나오더니 아이의 귀싸대기를 사납게 두어 대 후려쳤다. 아이는 안쪽 방에서 후작이 터뜨리는 웃음소리를 들었고(후작은 거리낌 없이 천연덕스럽게 드러난 베키의 성깔에 재미있어했다), 슬픔에 겨워 울음을 터뜨리며 아래층 부엌의 제 벗들에게로 달아났다.\n\n\"아파서 그러는 게 아니에요.\" 어린 로던이 헐떡이며 말했다. \"다만, 다만……\" 그러고는 흐느낌과 눈물이 한바탕 휘몰아치며 말끝을 삼켜 버렸다. 피 흘리는 것은 이 어린 사내아이의 마음이었다. \"왜 나는 엄마 노래를 들으면 안 되는 거예요? 왜 엄마는 나한텐 한 번도 노래를 안 불러 줘요, 이빨 큰 그 대머리 아저씨한테는 불러 주면서?\" 아이는 분노와 슬픔에 찬 이 외침들을 띄엄띄엄 토해 냈다. 요리사는 하녀를 쳐다보았고, 하녀는 하인을 의미심장하게 쳐다보았다. 집집마다 자리 잡고 앉아 심판을 내리며 모든 것을 다 아는, 저 무시무시한 부엌의 종교재판소가 그 순간 리베카를 판결대에 올린 것이었다.\n\n이 일이 있은 뒤로 어머니의 미움은 증오로 커져 갔다. 아이가 집 안에 있다는 의식 자체가 그녀에게는 책망이요 고통이었다. 그 모습만 보아도 짜증이 났다. 한편 아이의 가슴속에도 두려움과 의심과 반항심이 솟아났다. 귀싸대기를 맞던 그날 이후, 두 사람은 완전히 갈라서고 말았다.\n\n스테인 경 또한 그 사내아이를 진심으로 싫어했다. 어쩌다 마주치면 그는 아이에게 빈정대듯 인사를 하거나 말을 던졌고, 사납게 번뜩이는 눈으로 아이를 노려보았다. 로던은 그럴 때마다 그의 얼굴을 마주 노려보며 자그마한 두 주먹을 불끈 쥐어 보였다. 아이는 제 원수를 알아본 것이었으니, 이 집을 드나드는 모든 이들 가운데 아이를 가장 화나게 하는 이가 바로 그 신사였다. 어느 날 하인이, 아이가 현관에서 스테인 경의 모자를 향해 주먹을 들이대고 있는 것을 발견했다. 하인은 그 일을 우스운 농담거리 삼아 스테인 경의 마부에게 일러바쳤고, 그 마부는 다시 스테인 경의 시종에게, 또 하인방 전체에 두루 퍼뜨렸다. 그리고 머지않아, 로던 크롤리 부인이 곤트 하우스에 모습을 드러내자, 빗장을 풀어 문을 열어 주는 문지기며, 현관에 늘어선 갖가지 제복 차림의 하인들이며, 층계참마다 로던 크롤리 대령 부부의 이름을 외쳐 대는 흰 조끼 차림의 종복들이, 죄다 그녀에 대한 일을 알고 있거나, 알고 있다고 여겼다. 그녀에게 다과를 가져다주고 그 의자 뒤에 서 있던 사내는, 곁에 선 알록달록한 옷차림의 덩치 큰 사내와 더불어 그녀의 됨됨이를 입에 올린 적이 있었던 것이다. 맙소사! 끔찍한 노릇이다, 저 하인들의 종교재판소란! 어느 화려한 살롱의 성대한 연회에서, 충실한 흠모자들에 둘러싸여 반짝이는 눈길을 흩뿌리며, 더없이 완벽하게 차려입고, 머리를 곱슬곱슬 말고, 연지를 바르고, 미소 짓는 행복한 한 여인을 보라. 그때 정체의 폭로가 그녀에게 공손히 다가오니, 그것은 종아리가 굵고 얼음과자 쟁반을 든 분 바른 거구의 사내 형상이다. 그 뒤로는 비방이(이것은 진실만큼이나 치명적인 것이다) 따라오니, 곧 살구씨 비스킷을 나르는 그 우람한 녀석의 형상이다. 부인이여, 당신의 비밀은 오늘 밤 저 사내들이 술집의 자기네 모임에서 입방아에 올릴 것이다. 제임스는 담배 연기와 백랍 맥주잔을 사이에 두고 찰스에게 당신에 대한 제 견해를 늘어놓을 것이다. 허영의 시장에서는 더러 벙어리 하인을 두어야 할 사람도 있는 법이다, 글도 쓸 줄 모르는 벙어리 말이다. 죄가 있거든 떨지어다. 당신의 의자 뒤에 선 저 녀석이, 어쩌면 비단 반바지 호주머니에 활시위를 감춘 친위병일지도 모르니. 죄가 없거든 겉모습을 조심할지어다. 겉모습이란 죄과 못지않게 사람을 파멸로 몰아가는 것이니.\n\n\"리베카는 죄가 있었던가, 없었던가?\" 하인방의 비밀재판소는 이미 그녀에게 유죄를 선고한 터였다.\n\n그리고 부끄럽게도 한마디 덧붙이자면, 그들이 그녀를 죄 있는 여자로 믿지 않았더라면 그녀는 외상 신용조차 얻지 못했을 것이다. 그녀가 신용을 얻은 것은 무엇보다도, 라글스가 한밤중 칠흑 같은 어둠 속에서 그녀의 집 문 앞에 타오르는 스테인 후작의 마차 등불을 본 광경 덕분이었다. 그가 훗날 말한 대로 \"그게 사람을 버티게 해 준\" 것이었으니, 그것은 리베카의 온갖 수완과 아양보다도 한층 더한 것이었다.\n\n그리하여 — 십중팔구 죄 없는 몸으로 — 그녀는 사람들이 이른바 \"사교계의 지위\"라 부르는 것을 향해 몸부림치며 떠밀려 나아가고 있었고, 하인들은 그녀를 타락하고 파멸한 여자라 손가락질하고 있었다. 그러니 보라, 어느 아침 하녀 몰리가 문설주에서 거미 한 마리가 줄을 자아 힘겹게 기어오르는 것을 지켜보다가, 이내 그 장난에 싫증이 나 빗자루를 들어 그 줄과 그 솜씨꾼을 함께 쓸어 버리는 것을.\n\n성탄절을 하루 이틀 앞두고, 베키와 그녀의 남편과 아들은 채비를 갖추어 조상 대대로 내려오는 영지인 퀸스크롤리로 휴가를 보내러 떠났다. 베키는 그 어린 녀석을 두고 가고 싶어 했고, 실제로 그러했을 것이다. 다만 제인 부인이 그 아이를 한사코 청한 데다, 로던이 제 아들을 소홀히 대하는 아내에게 반항과 불만의 기색을 드러낸 탓에 그러지 못했다. \"이 아이는 영국에서 제일가는 사내아이요.\" 아버지는 나무라는 어조로 그녀에게 말했다. \"그런데 당신은 이 아이를 당신 애완견만큼도 아끼지 않는 것 같단 말이오, 베키. 이 아이가 당신을 그리 성가시게 하지도 않을 거요. 집에 가면 육아방에 있을 테니 당신 곁에서 떨어져 있을 거고, 나랑 같이 마차 바깥자리에 타고 가면 되니까.\"\n\n\"당신이야 그 더러운 시가를 피우고 싶어서 바깥자리에 타는 거잖아요.\" 로던 부인이 받아쳤다.\n\n\"하지만 당신도 한때는 그걸 좋아하지 않았소.\" 남편이 대꾸했다.\n\n베키는 웃었다. 그녀는 거의 언제나 기분이 좋았다. \"그건 내가 한창 출세하려고 애쓰던 시절 얘기죠, 멍청한 양반.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"로던을 바깥자리에 데리고 타요, 그러고 싶으면 그 애한테도 시가를 한 대 주든가요.\"\n\n로던은 겨울철 여행에 어린 아들을 그런 식으로 데우지는 않았으나, 그와 브리그스는 아이를 숄과 목도리로 둘둘 감쌌고, 아이는 어둑한 아침 화이트호스셀러의 등불 아래서 정중하게 마차 지붕 위로 올려졌다. 그리하여 아이는 적잖은 기쁨 속에 동이 트는 광경을 지켜보았고, 아버지가 여전히 집이라 부르는 그곳으로 처음 길을 떠났다. 그것은 아이에게 더없이 즐거운 여행이었으니, 길에서 마주치는 일들이 끝없는 흥미를 불러일으켰고, 아버지는 그에 얽힌 온갖 물음에 일일이 답해 주며, 오른편 저 커다란 흰 집에는 누가 사는지, 저 사슴 동산은 누구 것인지 일러 주었다. 한편 마차 안에 탄 어머니는 하녀와 모피며 무릎 덮개며 향수병을 잔뜩 늘어놓고 어찌나 부산을 떨던지, 마치 역마차라곤 난생처음 타 본 사람인 줄 알았을 것이다. 하물며 그녀가 십 년쯤 전 어느 길에서, 삯을 치르는 손님에게 자리를 내주려고 바로 이 마차에서 내쫓겼던 적이 있었다는 것은 더더욱 상상도 못 했으리라.\n\n어린 로던이 머드베리에서 큰아버지의 마차로 갈아타려고 잠에서 깨어났을 때는 다시 날이 어두워져 있었다. 아이는 자리에 앉아 마차 밖을 내다보며, 커다란 철문이 활짝 열리는 것을, 또 휙휙 스쳐 가는 보리수의 하얀 줄기들을 신기한 눈으로 바라보았다. 그러다 마침내 마차는, 성탄절을 맞아 환히 밝혀진 채 아늑하고 따스한 저택의 불 켜진 창문 앞에 멈추어 섰다. 현관문이 활짝 열렸다. 커다란 옛 벽난로에서는 큰 불이 타오르고 있었고, 흑백이 엇갈린 바닥돌 위로는 양탄자가 깔려 있었다. \"예전에 부인 회랑에 깔려 있던 그 낡은 터키 양탄자로구나.\" 리베카는 그렇게 생각하고는, 다음 순간 제인 부인에게 입을 맞추고 있었다.\n\n그녀와 피트 경도 더없이 엄숙하게 같은 인사를 나누었다. 그러나 로던은 시가를 피운 터라 제수에게서 다소 멀찍이 물러섰고, 그 사이 제수의 두 아이가 사촌에게 다가왔다. 마틸다가 손을 내밀어 아이에게 입을 맞추는 동안, 아들이자 상속자인 피트 빈키 사우스다운은 다소 떨어져 서서, 마치 작은 개가 큰 개를 살피듯 아이를 뜯어보았다.\n\n이윽고 다정한 안주인은 손님들을 명랑한 불길이 타오르는 아늑한 방으로 안내했다. 그러자 젊은 아가씨들이 로던 부인의 방문을 두드리고 찾아왔으니, 겉으로는 도울 일이 없을까 싶어서라는 핑계였으나, 실은 그녀의 끈 상자와 모자 상자에 든 것들을, 또 비록 검은색이긴 해도 런던의 최신 유행을 따른 그녀의 드레스들을 구경하는 즐거움을 누리기 위해서였다. 그들은 저택이 얼마나 좋게 달라졌는지, 늙은 사우스다운 부인이 어떻게 떠나갔는지, 피트가 어떻게 한 사람의 크롤리답게 이 고을에서 제 자리를 잡아 가고 있는지를 그녀에게 들려주었다. 이윽고 만찬을 알리는 큰 종이 울리자, 온 가족이 식탁에 둘러앉았다. 이 자리에서 어린 로던은 인심 좋은 안주인인 큰어머니 곁에 앉았고, 피트 경은 제 오른편의 제수에게 유난히 살뜰한 관심을 기울였다.\n\n어린 로던은 왕성한 식욕을 드러내면서도 신사다운 몸가짐을 보였다.\n\n\"여기서 밥 먹는 게 좋아요.\" 식사를 다 마친 아이가 큰어머니에게 말했다. 식사가 끝나고 피트 경이 점잖게 감사 기도를 올린 뒤, 작은아들이자 상속자가 불려 나와 준남작 곁의 높은 의자에 앉혀졌고, 딸아이는 어머니 옆에 마련된 제자리와 작은 포도주 잔을 차지했다. \"여기서 밥 먹는 게 좋아요.\" 작은 로던이 큰어머니의 다정한 얼굴을 올려다보며 말했다.\n\n\"왜 그러니?\" 마음씨 고운 제인 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"집에서는 부엌에서 밥을 먹거든요.\" 작은 로던이 대답했다. \"아니면 브리그스랑 같이 먹든가요.\" 그러나 베키는 집주인인 준남작을 상대하기에 여념이 없었다. 온갖 찬사와 기쁨과 감탄을 쏟아 내며, 어린 피트 빈키를 두고 세상에서 가장 어여쁘고 영민하며 기품 있게 생긴 작은 아이라고, 제 아버지를 쏙 빼닮았다고 추켜세우느라, 저 너머 넓고 반들거리는 식탁 끝자락에서 제 핏줄이 하는 말은 듣지도 못했다.\n\n손님인 데다 도착한 첫날 밤이었던 만큼, 둘째 로던은 차를 다 마실 때까지 자지 않고 앉아 있도록 허락받았다. 차가 끝나자 커다란 금박 책 한 권이 피트 경 앞 식탁에 놓였고, 집안의 하인들이 모두 줄지어 들어왔으며, 피트 경이 기도문을 읽었다. 이 가엾은 어린 사내아이가 그런 의식을 직접 보거나 들어 본 것은 그때가 처음이었다.\n\n저택은 준남작의 짧은 치세 이래로도 한층 더 손질이 되어 있었으니, 베키가 피트 경과 더불어 둘러보고는 완벽하다고, 매력적이라고, 더없이 흐뭇하다고 단언할 정도였다. 어린 로던으로 말하자면, 아이들을 길잡이 삼아 저택을 살펴본 그에게 그곳은 마법과 경이로 가득한 완벽한 궁전처럼 보였다. 기다란 회랑들이 있었고, 고풍스러운 손님용 침실들이 있었으며, 그림과 오래된 도자기와 갑옷이 있었다. 또 할아버지가 돌아가신 방들이 있었으니, 아이들은 겁먹은 얼굴로 그 곁을 지나다녔다. \"할아버지가 누군데?\" 아이가 묻자, 아이들은 할아버지가 무척 늙은 분이었다고, 정원용 바퀴 의자에 실려 이리저리 다니곤 했다고 일러 주었다. 그러고는 어느 날 그 정원용 의자를 보여 주었으니, 노인이 저 너머 교회로 — 그 첨탑이 사슴 동산의 느릅나무들 위로 반짝이고 있었다 — 실려 간 이래로 줄곧 처박혀 있던 헛간에서 썩어 가고 있었다.\n\n두 형제는 며칠 아침 동안, 피트 경의 재능과 절약으로 이루어 낸 개량의 성과들을 살펴보는 좋은 소일거리를 얻었다. 그렇게 거닐거나 말을 타고 다니며 그것들을 둘러보는 동안, 둘은 서로를 너무 지루하게 하지 않고도 이야기를 나눌 수 있었다. 그리고 피트는 이런 개량에 돈이 얼마나 많이 들었는지, 또 토지와 공채를 지닌 사람도 더러 단돈 20파운드에 쪼들릴 때가 있다는 것을 잊지 않고 로던에게 일러 주었다. \"저기 저 새 정문도 그렇다네.\" 피트가 대나무 지팡이로 겸손하게 그것을 가리키며 말했다. \"일월에 배당금이 나오기 전엔 저 값을 치르기란, 내가 하늘을 나는 것만큼이나 어림없는 일이야.\"\n\n\"그때까진 내가 빌려줄 수 있네, 피트.\" 로던이 다소 멋쩍게 대답했다. 그러고는 둘은 안으로 들어가 새로 고친 수위실을 둘러보았으니, 가문의 문장이 막 돌에 새로 새겨진 참이었고, 또 늙은 록 부인은 실로 오랜 세월 만에 처음으로 빈틈없는 문과 성한 지붕과 멀쩡한 창문을 갖게 된 터였다.\n* 살미: 들새 고기를 와인 소스에 익혀 만드는 프랑스식 스튜 요리.\n\n피트 크롤리 경은 퀸스크롤리 영지에서 울타리를 고치고 허물어진 별채를 손보는 일 이상의 것을 해냈다. 현명한 사람답게 그는 가문의 손상된 명망을 다시 일으켜 세우고, 평판 나쁘고 헤픈 노선대가 자기 이름에 남겨 놓은 틈과 폐허를 메우는 일에 착수했던 것이다. 그는 아버지가 세상을 뜨자 곧바로 그 선거구의 의원으로 선출되었다. 치안판사이자 하원의원이며 카운티의 유력 인사요 유서 깊은 가문의 대표자가 된 그는, 햄프셔 주민들 앞에 자기 모습을 드러내는 것을 의무로 삼아 카운티의 자선사업에 후하게 기부하고, 카운티의 인사들을 부지런히 찾아다녔으며, 한마디로 햄프셔에서, 그리고 나아가 제국 전체에서, 자신의 비범한 재능에 마땅히 걸맞다고 여기는 그 지위를 차지하기 위해 갖은 노력을 기울였다. 제인 부인은 이웃에 사는 퍼들스턴 일가며 왑샷 일가며 그 밖의 이름난 준남작들과 친하게 지내라는 지시를 받았다. 이제 퀸스크롤리의 가로수 길에서는 그들의 마차를 자주 볼 수 있었다. 그들은 저택에서 제법 자주 만찬을 들었고(요리가 어찌나 훌륭했던지 제인 부인이 손을 댄 적은 거의 없음이 분명했다), 그 답례로 피트와 그 아내는 날씨가 어떻든 거리가 얼마나 멀든 가리지 않고 열성껏 만찬에 나갔다. 피트는 본디 화롯불도 식욕도 변변찮은 냉랭한 사람이라 흥겨운 자리를 좋아하지 않았지만, 손님을 후하게 대접하고 사람들에게 너그럽게 처신하는 것이야말로 자기 지위에 따르는 책무라고 여겼던 것이다. 그래서 식후에 너무 오래 앉아 있다가 두통이 도질 때마다, 그는 자신이 의무에 순교하는 것이라고 느꼈다. 그는 카운티에서 가장 내로라하는 시골 신사들과 어울려 작황이며 곡물법이며 정치를 논했다. 또한 (전에는 이런 문제에서 한심한 자유사상가 노릇을 즐기던 그가) 밀렵 단속과 사냥감 보호에 열을 올렸다. 그는 사냥을 하지 않았다. 사냥하는 부류의 사람이 아니었던 것이다. 그는 책을 가까이하고 조용한 습성을 지닌 사람이었다. 그러나 시골에서는 말의 혈통을 잘 보존해야 하고 따라서 여우의 혈통도 보살펴야 한다고 생각했으며, 자기 친구인 허들스턴 퍼들스턴 경이 옛날 퍼들스턴 사냥개들이 퀸스크롤리에서 그러했듯 자기 영지로 사냥을 나오고 싶어 한다면 기꺼이 그를, 그리고 퍼들스턴 사냥대의 신사분들을 맞이하겠노라고 했다. 게다가 사우스다운 부인을 경악케 한 일이거니와, 그는 날이 갈수록 정통 신앙 쪽으로 기울어 갔다. 공개적으로 설교하거나 집회소에 다니는 일을 그만두고, 꿋꿋이 교회에 나갔으며, 윈체스터의 주교와 모든 성직자를 찾아가 인사했고, 트럼퍼 부주교 예하가 휘스트 한판을 청했을 때도 마다하지 않았다. 그토록 불경한 오락을 허락하다니, 사우스다운 부인은 얼마나 큰 마음의 고통을 겪었을 것이며, 또 사위를 얼마나 구제 불능의 타락한 인간으로 여겼겠는가! 그리고 일가족이 윈체스터의 오라토리오에서 돌아왔을 때, 준남작이 젊은 숙녀들에게 내년에는 아마도 \"카운티 무도회\"에 데려가 주겠노라고 알리자, 그들은 그 친절에 그를 떠받들었다. 제인 부인은 더없이 순종적이었고, 어쩌면 자신도 가고 싶었던 것인지도 모른다. 노부인은 케이프에 있는 「핀칠리 코먼의 세탁부 아낙」의 저자에게 딸의 세속적인 행실을 더없이 끔찍한 표현으로 적어 보냈다. 그리고 마침 이 무렵 브라이턴의 자기 집이 비게 되자, 그녀는 그 휴양지로 돌아갔는데, 자녀들은 그녀가 떠난 것을 그리 애석해하지 않았다. 또한 레베카가 두 번째로 퀸스크롤리를 찾았을 때, 그 약상자의 여주인이 없다는 사실을 그리 슬퍼하지 않았으리라고 짐작해도 좋을 것이다. 그러면서도 그녀는 사우스다운 부인에게 크리스마스 편지를 써 보내, 자신을 부디 기억해 달라며 정중히 안부를 전하고, 지난 방문 때 부인의 대화가 안겨 준 기쁨에 감사한다 말하고, 자신이 병중일 때 부인이 베풀어 준 친절을 길게 늘어놓고는, 퀸스크롤리의 모든 것이 멀리 떠난 그 벗을 떠올리게 한다고 했던 것이다.\n\n피트 크롤리 경의 달라진 태도와 높아진 인기의 상당 부분은, 커즌 거리의 그 약삭빠른 작은 부인이 일러 준 조언에서 비롯된 것이라 해도 무방했다. \"당신은 그저 준남작에 머무르려는 건가요. 한낱 시골 신사로 만족하시겠다고요.\" 그가 런던에서 그녀의 손님으로 머무를 때 그녀가 말했다. \"아니지요, 피트 크롤리 경, 저는 당신을 더 잘 알아요. 저는 당신의 재능과 야망을 알지요. 당신은 그 둘을 다 숨긴다고 여기시지만, 제게는 어느 것 하나 감출 수 없답니다. 저는 스타인 경에게 맥아에 관한 당신의 소책자를 보여 드렸어요. 그분은 이미 그것을 잘 알고 계셨고, 내각 전체의 의견으로도 그 주제에 관해 나온 글 가운데 가장 명석한 것이라고 하셨답니다. 정부가 당신을 주목하고 있어요. 그리고 저는 당신이 무엇을 원하는지 알지요. 당신은 의회에서 두각을 나타내고 싶으신 거예요. 다들 당신이 영국에서 가장 빼어난 연설가라고 하니까요(옥스퍼드에서 하신 연설들이 아직도 회자되고 있거든요). 당신은 카운티의 의원이 되고 싶으신 거예요. 거기서라면 당신 자신의 표와 당신을 뒷받침하는 선거구를 등에 업고 무엇이든 좌지우지할 수 있을 테니까요. 그리고 당신은 퀸스크롤리의 크롤리 남작이 되고 싶으신 거고, 죽기 전에 반드시 그리되실 거예요. 저는 그 모든 걸 다 알았답니다. 저는 당신의 마음을 읽을 수 있었어요, 피트 경. 만약 제게 당신의 이름뿐 아니라 당신의 지성까지 지닌 남편이 있었더라면, 저는 가끔 제가 그이에게 부끄럽지 않은 사람이 되지 않았을까 생각하곤 해요. 하지만, 하지만 이제 저는 당신의 친척이지요.\" 그녀가 웃으며 덧붙였다. \"한 푼 없는 가엾은 것이지만, 저도 약간의 연줄은 있답니다. 그러니 누가 알겠어요. 어쩌면 생쥐가 사자를 도울 수 있을지도요.\" 피트 크롤리는 그녀의 말에 깜짝 놀라며 황홀해했다. \"저 여자는 나를 어쩌면 그리도 잘 이해하는가!\" 그가 말했다. \"제인은 그 맥아 소책자를 세 쪽도 채 읽게 만들 수가 없었지. 그 사람은 내게 탁월한 재능이나 은밀한 야망이 있다는 걸 짐작도 못 해. 그래, 그자들이 옥스퍼드에서 하던 내 연설을 기억한다 이거지? 못된 것들 같으니! 이제 내가 선거구를 대표하고 카운티 의원으로 나설 수 있게 되니 나를 기억하기 시작한다 이 말이지! 아니, 스타인 경은 작년 알현식에서 나를 본 척도 안 하지 않았던가. 이제야 사람들이 피트 크롤리도 결국 어엿한 인물임을 알아차리기 시작하는군. 그래, 이 사람들이 무시하던 그 사람은 늘 한결같았어. 다만 기회가 없었을 뿐이지. 이제 내가 글뿐 아니라 말도 하고 행동도 할 수 있다는 걸 저들에게 보여 주리라. 아킬레우스도 그들이 칼을 쥐여 주기 전에는 자기 정체를 드러내지 않았지. 이제 내가 그 칼을 쥐었으니, 세상은 머지않아 피트 크롤리의 이름을 듣게 될 것이다.\"\n\n그리하여 이 교활한 책략가는 그토록 손님을 후하게 대접하게 되었고, 오라토리오와 병원에 그토록 정중해졌으며, 주임사제며 참사회에 그토록 친절해졌고, 만찬을 베풀고 받는 데 그토록 후해졌으며, 장날이면 농부들에게 유달리 상냥해졌고, 카운티의 사무에 그토록 관심을 쏟게 되었던 것이다. 그리하여 그해 저택의 크리스마스는 오랜 세월 동안 그곳에서 볼 수 없었던 가장 흥겨운 크리스마스가 되었다.\n\n크리스마스 날에는 성대한 가족 모임이 열렸다. 목사관의 크롤리 일가가 모두 만찬에 왔다. 레베카는 마치 상대가 한 번도 자기 적이었던 적이 없었다는 듯 뷰트 부인에게 솔직하고 다정하게 굴었다. 사랑스러운 딸들에게 애정 어린 관심을 보였고, 자기가 있던 때 이후로 그 애들의 음악 실력이 늘어난 데 놀라워했으며, 짐이 투덜대면서도 어쩔 수 없이 목사관에서 옆구리에 끼고 와야 했던 그 두꺼운 노래책 가운데 한 곡의 이중창을 기어이 한 번 더 부르게 했다. 뷰트 부인은 마지못해 이 어린 모험가에게 그럴듯한 태도를 취할 수밖에 없었다. 물론 나중에 딸들과 더불어, 피트 경이 제수에게 보이는 그 터무니없는 존중을 두고 마음껏 수군거릴 자유는 있었지만 말이다. 그러나 만찬에서 그녀 옆에 앉았던 짐은 그녀가 멋진 사람이라고 단언했고, 목사 일가는 너 나 할 것 없이 어린 로던이 잘생긴 사내아이라는 데 의견을 모았다. 그들은 그 아이에게서 장차 준남작이 될 가능성을 존중했던 것이다. 작위와 그 아이 사이를 가로막은 것이라곤 병약하고 창백한 자그마한 피트 빙키뿐이었으니까.\n\n아이들은 매우 사이가 좋았다. 피트 빙키는 로던처럼 덩치 큰 강아지가 함께 놀기에는 너무 작은 강아지였고, 마틸다는 한낱 계집아이일 뿐이니, 여덟 살이 다 되어 가고 머지않아 짧은 상의를 입게 될 어린 신사에게는 당연히 어울리는 동무가 못 되었다. 로던은 이 작은 무리의 지휘권을 단번에 거머쥐었다. 어린 계집아이와 어린 사내아이는 그가 황송하게도 함께 놀아 줄 때면 깊은 경의를 품고 그를 졸졸 따라다녔다. 시골에서 그가 누린 행복과 즐거움은 더할 나위 없었다. 채마밭이 그를 무척 흐뭇하게 했고, 꽃은 그저 그랬으나, 비둘기며 가금이며, 가도 좋다는 허락을 받을 때면 마구간이 그에게는 더없이 즐거운 구경거리였다. 그는 크롤리 양들이 입맞춤하려 들면 뿌리쳤지만, 제인 부인이 이따금 끌어안는 것은 허락했다. 그리고 응접실로 물러가라는 신호가 떨어져 숙녀들이 신사들을 클라레와 함께 남겨 두고 자리를 뜰 때면, 그는 제 어머니 곁보다 그 부인 곁에 앉기를 좋아했다. 어느 날 저녁, 다정함이 유행이라는 것을 알아챈 레베카가 모든 숙녀가 보는 앞에서 로던을 자기에게 불러 몸을 굽혀 입을 맞추었기 때문이다.\n\n그 의식이 끝난 뒤 아이는 그녀의 얼굴을 똑바로 쳐다보며, 마음이 동할 때면 늘 그러하듯 부들부들 떨면서 얼굴이 새빨개졌다. \"엄마는 집에선 한 번도 내게 입맞춰 준 적 없잖아요.\" 그가 말했다. 그러자 좌중에 일제히 침묵과 경악이 흘렀고, 베키의 두 눈에는 결코 유쾌하다 할 수 없는 빛이 어렸다.\n\n로던은 자기 형수가 아들을 아껴 주는 까닭에 그녀를 좋아했다. 제인 부인과 베키는, 대령의 아내가 환심을 사려 안달이던 지난번 방문 때만큼 이번에는 사이가 그리 좋지 않았다. 아이가 내뱉은 그 두 마디 말이 분위기에 적잖이 찬물을 끼얹었다. 어쩌면 피트 경이 그녀에게 지나치게 살가웠던 탓도 있었으리라.\n\n그러나 로던은 제 나이와 덩치에 걸맞게 여자들보다 남자들과 어울리기를 더 좋아해서, 대령이 시가를 피우러 물러가는 마구간으로 아비를 따라가는 일에 결코 싫증을 내는 법이 없었다. 목사의 아들 짐이 가끔 사촌과 함께 그런저런 놀이에 어울리기도 했다. 로던은 준남작의 사냥터지기와 매우 가까운 벗이 되었는데, \"개\"에 대한 서로의 취향이 그들을 자주 한데 어울리게 만들었던 것이다. 어느 날 제임스 군과 대령, 그리고 사냥터지기 혼이 어린 로던을 데리고 꿩 사냥을 나갔다. 또 어느 더없이 행복한 아침에는, 이 네 신사가 헛간에서 쥐 사냥의 재미를 함께 누렸는데, 로던은 그때껏 그보다 더 고귀한 놀이를 본 적이 없었다. 그들은 헛간에 난 어떤 배수구의 한쪽 끝을 막고, 다른 쪽 구멍으로 흰담비를 들이밀고는, 손에 막대기를 치켜든 채 조용히 한쪽에 떨어져 섰다. 그러는 동안 잔뜩 안달이 난 자그마한 테리어(다름 아닌 제임스 군의 그 유명한 \"개\" 포셉스였다)는 흥분으로 숨도 제대로 못 쉰 채, 세 발로 꼼짝 않고 서서 발밑에서 들려오는 쥐들의 희미한 찍찍 소리에 귀를 기울였다. 마침내 막다른 데 몰린 짐승들은 죽기 살기로 대담하게 땅 위로 뛰쳐나왔다. 한 마리는 테리어가, 또 한 마리는 사냥터지기가 처치했다. 로던은 허둥대고 흥분한 나머지 제 쥐는 놓쳤지만, 그 대신 흰담비 한 마리를 반쯤 죽여 놓았다.\n\n그러나 무엇보다 가장 큰 날은, 허들스턴 퍼들스턴 경의 사냥개들이 퀸스크롤리의 잔디밭에 모인 날이었다.\n\n그것은 어린 로던에게 굉장한 구경거리였다. 열 시 반에 허들스턴 퍼들스턴 경의 사냥꾼 톰 무디가 가도를 따라 잰걸음으로 올라오는 모습이 보였고, 그 뒤로 늠름한 사냥개 떼가 빽빽이 무리를 이루어 따라왔다. 행렬의 맨 뒤는 얼룩진 진홍빛 사냥복 차림의 몰이꾼 둘이 맡았다. 몸집이 가볍고 야무진 얼굴을 한 젊은이들로, 혈통 좋은 비쩍 마른 말에 올라타 있었으며, 무리에서 벗어나 어슬렁대거나, 코앞에서 튀어 오르는 토끼와 산토끼에게 조금이라도 눈길을 주거나 심지어 곁눈질이라도 하는 개가 있으면, 그 길고 묵직한 채찍 끝을 가장 살갗이 얇은 자리에 정확히 후려치는 놀라운 솜씨를 지니고 있었다.\n\n다음으로는 톰 무디의 아들 잭 소년이 왔는데, 무게는 다섯 스톤에 키는 마흔여덟 인치였고, 앞으로도 더 자라지는 못할 아이였다. 그는 큼직한 안장에 절반쯤 가려진, 뼈만 앙상한 커다란 사냥말 위에 올라타 있었다. 이 말은 허들스턴 퍼들스턴 경이 아끼는 말 노브였다. 다른 어린 소년들이 탄 다른 말들도 이따금 도착하여, 곧 천천히 말을 몰고 올 주인들을 기다렸다.\n\n톰 무디가 저택 문 앞까지 말을 몰고 오자, 집사가 나와 그를 맞이하며 마실 것을 권했으나 그는 사양했다. 그런 다음 그와 그의 개 떼는 잔디밭의 한적한 구석으로 물러났다. 거기서 개들은 풀밭을 뒹굴며 장난을 치거나 서로 으르렁댔고, 이따금 사납게 싸움이 붙기도 했지만, 으름장 놓는 데 둘째가라면 서러운 톰의 목소리나 채찍의 뱀 같은 가죽끈에 금세 제압되곤 했다.\n\n순종 승용마에 올라탄 젊은 신사들이 무릎까지 진흙을 튀긴 채 잇따라 천천히 다가와서는, 집 안으로 들어가 체리 브랜디를 마시고 숙녀들에게 인사를 올렸다. 더 겸손하고 사냥꾼다운 이들은 진흙 묻은 장화를 벗고 승용마를 사냥말로 바꾸어 탄 뒤, 잔디밭을 한 바퀴 미리 내달려 몸을 덥혔다. 그런 다음 그들은 구석에 모인 개 떼 주위로 모여들어 톰 무디와 지난 사냥 이야기며, 스니벨러와 다이아몬드의 기량이며, 고장의 형편이며, 형편없는 여우의 혈통에 대해 이야기를 나누었다.\n\n이윽고 허들스턴 경이 영리한 조랑말에 올라타 나타나서는 저택까지 말을 몰고 와 안으로 들어가 숙녀들에게 예의를 차렸고, 그런 뒤에는 본디 말수가 적은 사람답게 곧장 일에 착수했다. 사냥개들이 저택 문 앞으로 끌려오자, 어린 로던이 그 한가운데로 내려갔다. 개들이 자기에게 안겨 오는 어리광과, 흔들리는 꼬리에 얻어맞는 통과, 톰 무디의 목소리와 채찍에도 좀처럼 가라앉지 않는 그 개들끼리의 으르렁거림에 신이 나면서도 반쯤은 겁을 먹은 채였다.\n\n그러는 동안 허들스턴 경은 굼뜬 몸을 끌어 노브 위에 올라탔다. \"소스터의 잡목 숲을 한번 둘러보세, 톰.\" 준남작이 말했다. \"맹글 농부 말로는 거기에 여우가 두 마리 있다더군.\" 톰이 뿔나팔을 불며 잰걸음으로 떠나자, 그 뒤를 사냥개 떼가, 몰이꾼들이, 윈체스터에서 온 젊은 신사들이, 인근의 농부들이, 그리고 이날을 큰 축제로 여기는 교구의 일꾼들이 걸어서 뒤따랐다. 허들스턴 경은 크롤리 대령과 함께 행렬의 맨 뒤를 맡았고, 이 모든 행렬은 가도 저편으로 사라져 갔다.\n\n뷰트 크롤리 목사는(조카의 창문 앞에서 열리는 공개 집결에 모습을 드러내기에는 너무 겸손했다) 톰 무디가 사십 년 전을 기억하기로, 가장 사나운 말을 타고 가장 넓은 개울을 뛰어넘고 고장에서 가장 새로 세운 울타리들을 신나게 넘나들던 호리호리한 신학자였다. 우리가 이야기하는 그 예하께서는, 마침 허들스턴 경이 지나갈 무렵 그 힘센 검은 말에 올라타 목사관 골목에서 잰걸음으로 나오던 참이었다. 그는 그 훌륭한 준남작과 합류했다. 사냥개들과 기마 행렬이 사라지자, 어린 로던은 경이로움과 행복에 젖은 채 문간 계단에 남았다.\n\n이 기억할 만한 휴일이 이어지는 동안, 어린 로던은 늘 위압적이고 냉랭하며 서재에 틀어박혀 재판 업무에 파묻혀 집행관과 농부들에게 둘러싸여 있는 백부에게는 딱히 정을 붙이지 못했지만, 결혼한 숙모와 시집 안 간 숙모들의, 저택의 두 꼬마의, 그리고 목사관의 짐의 호감을 사는 데는 성공했다. 피트 경은 짐이 젊은 숙녀들 가운데 하나에게 구애하도록 부추기고 있었는데, 거기에는 두말할 것 없이 여우 사냥이나 즐기는 그의 늙은 아비가 비우게 될 성직록을 짐에게 물려주겠다는 양해가 깔려 있었다. 짐은 그 사냥을 이미 그만두고, 크리스마스 휴가 동안에는 그저 무해한 오리나 도요새 사냥, 혹은 쥐를 데리고 하는 조용한 소일거리에 만족하고 있었다. 그러고 나면 그는 대학으로 돌아가 다시 한번 낙제하지 않으려 애쓸 참이었다. 그는 이미 초록 상의며 빨간 목도리며 그 밖의 세속적 치장을 끊어 버리고, 처지의 변화에 대비해 자신을 가다듬고 있었다. 이런 값싸고 알뜰한 방식으로 피트 경은 가문에 진 빚을 갚으려 했던 것이다.\n\n또한 이 즐거운 크리스마스가 끝나기 전에, 준남작은 동생에게 거래 은행 앞으로 또 한 장의 어음을 끊어 줄 만큼 용기를 그러모았는데, 그 액수가 무려 백 파운드나 되었다. 이 일로 피트 경은 처음에는 살을 에는 듯한 고통을 느꼈으나, 나중에는 자신을 더없이 너그러운 사람 가운데 하나로 여기며 흐뭇해 마지않았다. 로던과 그 아들은 더없이 무거운 마음으로 떠났다. 그러나 베키와 숙녀들은 제법 후련하게 작별했다. 그리고 우리의 벗은 런던으로 돌아가, 이 장이 시작될 때 그녀가 몰두해 있던 그 소일거리에 다시 착수했다. 그녀의 보살핌 아래 그레이트곤트 거리의 크롤리 저택은 완전히 새 단장을 하여, 준남작이 의회의 직무를 수행하고 그 막대한 천재성에 걸맞은 지위를 이 나라에서 차지하고자 런던에 올라올 때, 피트 경과 그 가족을 맞이할 채비를 갖추었다.\n\n첫 회기 동안 이 깊숙한 위장의 명수는 자기 계획을 감추고, 머드버리의 청원을 제출할 때 말고는 입을 떼는 법이 없었다. 그러나 그는 자기 자리에 부지런히 출석하여 의회의 관례와 업무를 철저히 익혔다. 집에서는 청서 정독에 몰두했는데, 늦은 시각까지 일하고 지나치게 골몰하느라 제 몸을 상하게 하는 것이 아닌가 하여 제인 부인이 놀라워하며 걱정할 정도였다. 그리고 그는 장관들이며 당의 수뇌들과 친분을 맺어, 여러 해가 가기 전에 자신도 그들 가운데 하나로 손꼽히겠다고 단단히 마음먹었다.\n\n제인 부인의 상냥함과 친절함은 레베카에게 그 부인에 대한 경멸을 불러일으켰는데, 그 작은 부인은 그것을 감추기가 여간 어렵지 않았다. 제인 부인이 지닌 그런 종류의 선량함과 천진함이 우리의 벗 베키를 짜증나게 했고, 그녀로서는 이따금 그 경멸을 드러내거나 상대가 눈치채게 만들지 않을 수 없었던 것이다. 게다가 그녀가 곁에 있으면 제인 부인은 마음이 불편해졌다. 남편이 끊임없이 베키와 이야기를 나누었기 때문이다. 둘 사이에는 알아듣는다는 눈짓이 오가는 듯했고, 피트는 제인 부인과는 함께 논할 생각조차 하지 않던 화제들을 베키와는 논했다. 물론 제인 부인은 그런 이야기를 알아듣지 못했지만, 잠자코 앉아 있는 것은 굴욕적이었다. 더욱 굴욕적인 것은 자기가 할 말이 하나도 없음을 깨닫는 것이요, 그 자그마한 당돌한 로던 부인이 이 화제에서 저 화제로 거침없이 내달리며 모든 사람에게 한마디씩 건네고 늘 때맞춰 농담을 던지는 것을 듣는 것이요, 제 집 안에서 홀로 난롯가에 앉아 자기 연적 주위로 모여드는 남자들을 지켜보는 것이었다.\n\n시골에서 제인 부인이 무릎 둘레로 모여든 아이들에게(꼬마 로던도 한몫 끼었으니, 그 아이는 부인을 무척 따랐다) 이야기를 들려주고 있을 때, 베키가 초록빛 경멸의 눈을 번득이며 방으로 들어오면, 가엾은 제인 부인은 그 악의 어린 눈초리 아래 입을 다물어 버렸다. 그녀의 소박하고 자그마한 상상은, 동화책 속 요정들이 자기보다 우월한 사악한 천사 앞에서 그러하듯 부들부들 떨며 움츠러들었다. 레베카가 목소리에 아주 살짝 비꼼을 실어 그 매혹적인 이야기를 계속해 달라고 청해도, 그녀는 도무지 이어 갈 수가 없었다. 한편 베키 부인 쪽에서는, 다정한 마음씨와 소박한 즐거움이 그저 가증스러울 따름이었다. 그것들은 그녀와 어울리지 않았다. 그녀는 그런 것을 좋아하는 사람들을 미워했다. 그녀는 아이들도, 아이를 사랑하는 이들도 경멸했다. \"나는 버터 바른 빵 따위에는 취미가 없답니다.\" 그녀는 스타인 경에게 제인 부인과 그 행실을 우스꽝스럽게 흉내 내며 그렇게 말하곤 했다.\n\n\"성수에 취미가 없는 어떤 사람도 마찬가지지.\" 경께서는 절을 하고 히죽 웃으며 대꾸하더니, 이내 귀에 거슬리는 큰 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n그리하여 이 두 부인은, 동생의 아내가 형수에게서 얻어 낼 무언가가 있어 드나들 때를 제외하고는 서로 얼굴을 마주하는 일이 별로 없었다. 그들은 부지런히 \"내 사랑\" \"내 귀여운 이\"를 주고받았지만 대체로 멀찍이 떨어져 지냈다. 반면 피트 경은 그 갖가지 분주한 일거리 한복판에서도 날마다 제수를 만날 짬을 냈다.\n\n첫 의장 만찬이 있던 날, 피트 경은 제수 앞에 자기 정복을 차려입고 나설 기회를 잡았다. 그것은 그가 펌퍼니클 공사관의 수행원으로 있을 때 입었던 그 낡은 외교관 예복이었다.\n\n베키는 그 차림새를 칭찬하며, 그가 집을 나서기 전에 제 모습을 보여 준 그의 아내와 아이들 못지않게 그를 우러러보았다. 그녀는 궁정 예복을 멋들어지게 차려입을 수 있는 것은 오로지 순종 혈통의 신사뿐이며, 짧은 반바지가 어울리는 것은 오로지 유서 깊은 가문의 사내뿐이라고 했다. 피트는 흐뭇한 마음으로 자기 다리를 내려다보았는데, 사실 그 다리는 옆구리에 달랑거리는 가느다란 궁정용 검보다 균형도 볼록함도 그리 나을 게 없었다. 그래도 그는 제 다리를 내려다보며, 속으로 자신이 죽여주게 멋지다고 생각했다.\n\n그가 떠나자 베키 부인은 그의 모습을 우스꽝스럽게 그린 그림을 한 장 그려서, 스타인 경이 당도하자 그에게 보여 주었다. 경께서는 그 닮은 정도가 어찌나 정확한지 흡족해하며 그 그림을 가져갔다. 그는 베키 부인의 집에서 피트 크롤리 경을 만나는 영광을 베풀었고, 이 새 준남작이자 의원에게 더없이 정중하게 대했다. 피트 또한 그 위대한 귀족이 자기 제수를 대하는 정중함과, 대화에서 보이는 그녀의 여유롭고 발랄한 태도며, 자리에 함께한 다른 남자들이 그녀의 이야기에 귀 기울이는 그 즐거워하는 기색에 적잖이 놀랐다. 스타인 경은 준남작이 이제 막 공적 생활의 첫발을 내디뎠을 뿐임을 의심치 않았고, 다소 조바심을 내며 그가 연설가로 활약하는 모습을 보기를 고대했다. 그들은 이웃지간이었으므로(그레이트곤트 거리는 곤트 광장으로 이어지는데, 누구나 알다시피 곤트 저택은 그 광장의 한 면을 이루고 있다), 경께서는 스타인 부인이 런던에 도착하는 대로 크롤리 부인과 안면을 트는 영광을 누리기를 바랐다. 그는 하루 이틀 사이에 이웃에게 명함을 두고 갔는데, 전임자와는 거의 백 년 가까이 가까이 살았으면서도 한 번도 아는 체할 생각조차 하지 않았던 것이다.\n\n이런 책략과 화려한 연회와 현명하고 빛나는 인사들 한복판에서, 로던은 날이 갈수록 점점 더 고립되어 가는 자신을 느꼈다. 이제 그는 클럽에 더 자주 드나들고, 미혼인 벗들과 밖에서 만찬을 들고, 아무런 추궁도 받지 않은 채 마음대로 오가도록 허락받았다. 그는 어린 로던과 함께 곤트 거리로 걸어가, 피트 경이 의회로 가는 길에 혹은 의회에서 돌아오는 길에 레베카와 단둘이 틀어박혀 있는 동안, 거기서 그 부인과 아이들과 더불어 앉아 있곤 했다.\n\n전직 대령은 형의 집에서 입을 꾹 다문 채 몇 시간이고 앉아, 되도록 생각도 행동도 하지 않으려 했다. 그는 심부름이라도 시키면 반가워했다. 말이나 하인에 관해 알아보러 가거나, 아이들 저녁 식사로 양고기 구이를 썰어 주는 일 따위 말이다. 그는 두들겨 맞고 기가 꺾여 게으름과 순종에 길들었다. 델릴라가 그를 가두고 머리카락까지 잘라 버린 것이다. 십 년 전의 그 대담하고 무모한 젊은 혈기는 정복되어, 둔하고 고분고분한 중년의 뚱뚱한 신사로 변해 버렸다.\n\n그리고 가엾은 제인 부인은, 자기와 로던 부인이 만나는 날마다 \"내 귀여운 이\" \"내 사랑\" 하며 서로를 불러 댔건만, 레베카가 자기 남편을 사로잡았다는 것을 알고 있었다.\n\n고난과 시련 한편 브롬프턴에 있는 우리의 벗들은 그동안 그들 나름의 방식으로, 그리고 결코 그리 유쾌하다고는 할 수 없는 식으로 성탄절을 보내고 있었다.\n\n연간 백 파운드 남짓이 수입의 거의 전부였는데, 미망인이 된 오즈번 부인은 그 가운데 거의 사분의 삼을 자신과 어린 아들의 생활비 명목으로 친정 부모에게 내놓는 것이 버릇이 되어 있었다. 거기에 조스가 보태는 백이십 파운드를 더하면, 클랩 부부의 시중까지 함께 드는 아일랜드인 하녀 하나의 도움을 받아 가며 이 네 식구는 그럭저럭 한 해를 점잖고 안락하게 꾸려 나갈 수 있었고, 아직은 고개를 들고 살 수 있었으며, 젊은 날의 풍파와 좌절을 겪고도 여전히 벗에게 차 한 잔쯤은 대접할 수 있는 형편이었다. 세들리는 옛 사무원이던 클랩 씨의 가족에게 여전히 그 위세를 유지하고 있었다. 클랩은 러셀 광장에 자리한 그 상인의 풍성한 식탁에서 의자 끝에 걸터앉아 \"인도에 계신 세들리 마님, 에미 아가씨, 조지프 도련님\"의 건강을 위해 잔을 단숨에 비우던 시절을 기억했다. 세월은 이 정직한 사무원의 가슴속에서 그 추억의 광휘를 한층 더 부풀려 놓았다. 부엌 옆 거실에서 응접실로 올라와 세들리 씨와 차나 진을 탄 물을 함께 마실 때면, 그는 늘 \"이런 건 한때 나리께서 익숙하시던 게 아니지요\"라고 말하면서, 그 집안이 더없이 번성하던 시절에 그랬던 것처럼 엄숙하고 경건하게 숙녀분들의 건강을 위해 잔을 들었다. 그는 에밀리아 아가씨의 연주가 여태껏 연주된 그 어떤 것보다 신성한 음악이라 여겼고, 그녀를 더없이 고귀한 숙녀라 믿었다. 그는 클럽에서조차 세들리 앞에서는 결코 먼저 앉는 법이 없었고, 그 신사의 인품이 그 모임의 어느 회원에게라도 모욕당하는 것을 두고 보지 못했다. 그는 런던의 일류 인사들이 세들리 씨와 악수를 나누는 광경을 직접 목격한 사람이었다. 그는 \"로스차일드가 거래소에서 그분과 언제든 어울리던 시절부터 그분을 알아 왔고, 자기가 가진 모든 게 다 그분 덕\"이라고 말했다.\n\n클랩은 더없이 훌륭한 인품과 필체를 지닌 덕분에, 주인이 파산한 직후 곧바로 다른 일자리를 구할 수 있었다. \"나 같은 송사리는 어느 물통에서든 헤엄칠 수 있죠\"라고 그는 입버릇처럼 말하곤 했는데, 노년의 세들리가 떨어져 나온 그 상회의 한 회원이 클랩 씨의 일손을 기꺼이 빌려 쓰고 넉넉한 봉급으로 보답해 준 것이었다. 요컨대 세들리의 부유한 벗들은 하나둘 떨어져 나갔건만, 가난한 옛 식객인 이 사람만은 여전히 충직하게 그의 곁을 지킨 셈이었다.\n\n애밀리아가 자신을 위해 따로 떼어 둔 얼마 안 되는 수입의 나머지에서, 미망인은 사랑하는 아들에게 조지 오즈번의 아들에 걸맞은 옷차림을 시키고, 또 자신으로서는 숱한 망설임과 주저 끝에, 또 남몰래 가슴 졸이고 두려워한 끝에 마침내 아들을 보내기로 결심한 그 자그마한 학교의 비용을 대기 위해, 동원할 수 있는 모든 절약과 알뜰함이 필요했다. 그녀는 조지에게 가르쳐 주려고 밤을 새워 가며 수업 내용을 익히고, 까다로운 문법책과 지리책을 한 자 한 자 읽어 나갔다. 그 언어로 아이를 가르칠 수 있을지도 모른다는 가냘픈 희망을 품고, 라틴어 어형 변화까지 공부했다. 종일토록 아이와 떨어져 지내고, 교사의 회초리와 학교 친구들의 거친 손길에 아이를 내맡기는 일은, 떨림 많고 감수성 가득한 그 여린 어머니에게는 마치 아이를 한 번 더 젖떼는 일이나 다름없었다. 그러나 아이는 아이대로 더없이 행복해하며 학교로 달려갔다. 그 변화를 손꼽아 기다리던 터였다. 그 천진한 기쁨이 어머니에게는 상처가 되었으니, 정작 그녀 자신은 아이와 떨어지는 일이 그토록 서글펐던 것이다. 아이가 좀 더 슬퍼해 주었으면 싶다가도, 자기 아들이 불행하기를 바랄 만큼 이기적인 마음을 감히 품었다는 데에 속으로 깊이 뉘우치는 것이었다.\n\n조지는 그 학교에서 큰 진전을 보였는데, 그 학교는 그녀를 늘 흠모하는 비니 목사의 한 벗이 운영하는 곳이었다. 아이는 수도 없이 많은 상과 능력을 증명하는 표창장을 집으로 가져왔다. 매일 밤 학교 친구들 이야기를 어머니에게 끝도 없이 들려주었다. 라이언스는 얼마나 멋진 녀석인지, 스니핀은 또 얼마나 비열한 놈인지, 스틸의 아버지는 실제로 그 학교에 고기를 대 주는 사람이고, 골딩의 어머니는 매주 토요일마다 마차를 타고 아들을 데리러 온다는 것, 니트는 바지에 가죽끈을 달았다는데 자기도 가죽끈을 달면 안 되겠느냐는 것, 그리고 불 메이저는 (비록 에우트로피우스 책에서만 그렇긴 하나) 어찌나 힘이 센지 조교인 워드 선생까지도 때려눕힐 수 있을 거라고들 한다는 것이었다. 그리하여 애밀리아는 그 학교의 아이들을 하나하나, 조지 자신이 아는 만큼이나 속속들이 알게 되었고, 밤이면 아이의 숙제를 거들어 주면서, 마치 자기가 아침에 직접 선생 앞으로 나아가기라도 할 것처럼 그 작은 머리로 아이의 수업 내용을 골똘히 궁리하는 것이었다. 한번은 스미스라는 아이와 한판 붙고 나서, 조지가 눈에 멍이 든 채 어머니에게 돌아와서는, 그 싸움에서 보인 자기 용맹을 어머니와 기뻐 어쩔 줄 모르는 늙은 외할아버지에게 엄청나게 떠벌렸다. 사실대로 말하자면 그 싸움에서 아이가 특별히 영웅적으로 처신한 것도 아니었고, 오히려 확실히 더 호되게 당한 쪽이었지만 말이다. 그래도 애밀리아는 오늘날까지 그 스미스를 결코 용서하지 않았으니, 그 아이가 지금은 레스터 광장 근처에서 점잖게 약방을 꾸리고 있는데도 그러했다.\n\n이런 조용한 수고와 해될 것 없는 근심 속에서 이 온순한 미망인의 삶은 흘러가고 있었고, 은빛 머리카락이 한두 올 돋아 그 머리 위에 세월의 자취를 새겼으며, 그 고운 이마에는 주름 한 줄이 아주 조금씩 깊어 갔다. 그녀는 이런 세월의 흔적들을 보며 미소 짓곤 했다. \"이런 게 무슨 대수겠어요?\" 그녀는 물었다. \"나처럼 늙은 여자한테?\" 그녀가 바라는 것이라곤, 아들이 마땅히 받아야 할 만큼 위대하고, 유명하고, 영광스러운 사람이 되는 것을 살아서 보는 일뿐이었다. 그녀는 아이의 습자책과 그림과 작문을 고이 간직했다가, 마치 천재의 기적이라도 되는 양 자기 작은 모임 사람들에게 두루 보여 주었다. 그녀는 그런 표본 몇 가지를 도빈 양에게 맡겨, 조지의 고모인 오즈번 양에게 보여 주게 하고, 또 오즈번 씨 본인에게까지 보여 주어, 그 노인이 세상을 떠난 이에게 품었던 잔인함과 모진 감정을 뉘우치도록 만들고자 했다. 남편의 온갖 결점과 약점은 모조리 그와 함께 무덤에 묻어 버린 그녀였다. 오로지 온갖 희생을 무릅쓰고 자신과 결혼해 준 그 연인을, 그토록 용맹하고 아름답던 그 고귀한 남편을, 임금을 위해 싸우고 영광스럽게 죽으러 떠나던 그 아침에 자신이 매달려 안겼던 그 품을 기억할 뿐이었다. 천국에서 그 영웅은 분명, 그녀를 위로하고 다독이라고 남겨 두고 간 그 더없이 빼어난 아들을 내려다보며 미소 짓고 있으리라. 조지의 외할아버지 가운데 한 분(오즈번 씨)이 러셀 광장의 안락의자에 앉은 채 날이 갈수록 더욱 사납고 침울해져 갔다는 것, 그리고 그 딸이 으리으리한 마차와 으리으리한 말들을 거느리고 도시의 공공 자선 명부 절반에 제 이름을 올려 두고도 외롭고 비참하며 핍박받는 노처녀였다는 것은 우리가 이미 본 바와 같다. 그녀는 자기가 본 그 아름다운 어린 사내아이를, 오라비의 아들을 몇 번이고 거듭 떠올렸다. 그 으리으리한 마차를 타고 아이가 사는 집까지 가 보아도 좋다는 허락을 받기를 간절히 바랐고, 공원에서 홀로 마차를 몰 때면 행여 아이를 볼 수 있을까 하는 희망에 날이면 날마다 밖을 살피곤 했다. 은행가의 부인인 그녀의 동생은 이따금 러셀 광장의 옛 집과 옛 길동무를 찾아오는 황송한 너그러움을 베풀었다. 그녀는 깔끔한 척하는 유모가 시중드는 병약한 아이 둘을 데려와서는, 고상한 척 힘없이 키득거리는 어조로 자기의 멋진 교제권에 대해 언니에게 재잘거렸다. 자기네 어린 프레더릭이 클로드 롤리팝 경을 빼다 박았다느니, 자기네 사랑스러운 마리아가 로햄프턴에서 당나귀 수레를 타고 가는데 남작 부인이 눈여겨보았다느니 하는 식이었다. 그녀는 아빠한테 이 귀여운 아이들을 위해 뭐라도 해 주시도록 졸라 달라고 언니를 채근했다. 프레더릭은 근위대에 보내기로 작정했다는 것이었다. 그런데 만약 그 아이를 장남으로 삼는다면(불록 씨는 땅을 사들이느라 그야말로 자기 살을 깎고 졸라매며 죽을 지경이었다), 그 귀여운 딸아이는 무엇으로 살림 밑천을 마련해 주느냐는 것이었다. \"난 언니만 믿어요.\" 불록 부인은 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"당연히 아빠 재산에서 내 몫은 우리 집안의 가장한테 돌아가야 하잖아, 그렇지? 가엾고 사랑스러운 캐슬토디 경이 돌아가시면, 그분이 간질이 아주 심하시거든, 사랑하는 로다 맥멀이 캐슬토디 영지 전부를 풀어낼 거고, 어린 맥더프 맥멀이 캐슬토디 자작이 될 거예요. 민싱 레인의 블러디어 형제분도 둘 다 패니 블러디어의 어린 아들한테 재산을 물려주기로 했고요. 우리 사랑스러운 프레더릭은 무슨 일이 있어도 꼭 장남이 돼야 해요. 그리고, 그리고 아빠한테 롬바드 거리에 맡긴 계좌를 우리 쪽으로 도로 옮겨 달라고 부탁 좀 해 줘요, 언니. 아빠가 스텀피 앤드 라우디한테 거래를 맡기시는 게 영 보기 안 좋잖아.\" 유행과 잇속이 한데 뒤섞인 이런 따위의 연설을 늘어놓고는, 굴 껍데기에 닿는 듯한 입맞춤을 나눈 뒤, 프레더릭 불록 부인은 풀 먹인 옷을 입은 제 새끼들을 챙겨 거드름스럽게 다시 마차에 올라타는 것이었다.\n\n이 사교계의 선도자가 친정을 찾아올 때마다 그것은 그녀에게 더욱 불운한 일이 되었다. 아버지는 스텀피 앤드 라우디에 더 많은 돈을 맡겼다. 그녀의 거들먹거리는 시혜는 갈수록 더 견딜 수 없는 것이 되어 갔다. 브롬프턴의 작은 집에서 제 보물을 지키는 가난한 미망인은, 어떤 이들이 그것을 얼마나 간절히 탐내고 있는지 까맣게 몰랐던 것이다.\n\n제인 오즈번이 아버지에게 손자를 보았노라고 말한 그날 밤, 노인은 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았으나, 화를 내지도 않았다. 그리고 자기 방으로 들어가면서 제법 다정한 목소리로 잘 자라는 인사를 건넸다. 그는 분명 딸이 한 말을 곰곰이 곱씹고, 그 방문에 관해 도빈 가족에게 얼마간 알아보았던 듯했다. 그로부터 보름이 지난 어느 날, 그는 딸에게 평소 차고 다니던 그 작은 프랑스제 시계와 줄이 어디 있느냐고 물었던 것이다.\n\n\"제 돈으로 산 거예요, 아버지.\" 그녀가 잔뜩 겁에 질려 대답했다.\n\n\"가서 그것과 똑같은 걸로, 아니 더 좋은 걸 구할 수 있거든 더 좋은 걸로 하나 새로 맞추거라.\" 노신사는 이렇게 말하고는 다시 침묵에 잠겼다.\n\n근래 들어 도빈 자매는 한 번 이상, 조지가 자기네 집을 방문하게 허락해 달라고 애밀리아에게 거듭 간청했다. 아이의 고모가 그런 마음을 내비쳤다는 것이었다. 어쩌면 아이의 외할아버지 본인도 손자와 화해할 생각이 있을지 모른다고 그들은 넌지시 비쳤다. 애밀리아가 아이에게 이토록 유리한 기회를 차마 마다할 수는 없는 노릇이었다. 실제로 그녀도 마다하지 못했지만, 더없이 무겁고 미심쩍은 마음으로 그들의 제안을 받아들였고, 아이가 곁을 떠나 있는 동안에는 늘 안절부절못했으며, 마치 무슨 위험에서 구해 내기라도 한 듯 아이를 맞이하곤 했다. 아이는 돈과 장난감을 받아 왔는데, 미망인은 그것을 불안과 질투의 눈길로 바라보았다. 그녀는 늘 아이에게 어떤 신사를 만났느냐고 물었다. \"사륜마차에 태워 데리고 다니신 윌리엄 경 할아버지하고, 오후에 그 멋진 밤색 말을 타고 오신 도빈 아저씨밖에 없었어요. 초록색 외투에 분홍색 목도리를 두르고, 금 손잡이 채찍을 든 분 말이에요. 런던 탑을 구경시켜 주고 서리 사냥개들이랑 같이 사냥에 데려가 주겠다고 약속하셨어요.\" 그러더니 마침내 아이가 말했다. \"눈썹이 짙고, 챙 넓은 모자를 쓰고, 큼직한 줄에 도장을 단 할아버지가 한 분 계셨어요.\" 마부가 회색 조랑말에 조지를 태우고 잔디밭을 빙빙 돌리던 어느 날 그분이 오셨다는 것이었다. \"그 할아버지가 절 아주 빤히 쳐다보셨어요. 그리고 막 떠셨어요. 저녁 먹고 나서 제가 '내 이름은 노벌'을 외웠더니 고모가 울기 시작했어요. 고모는 늘 울어요.\" 그날 밤 조지가 전한 보고는 그러했다.\n\n그제야 애밀리아는 아이가 외할아버지를 보았다는 것을 알았고, 분명히 뒤따라올 것이라 확신한 어떤 제안을 안달하며 기다렸는데, 과연 며칠 뒤에 그것이 들이닥쳤다. 오즈번 씨는 아이를 데려가, 본래 그 아비에게 물려주려 했던 재산의 상속자로 삼겠노라고 정식으로 제의했다. 조지 오즈번 부인에게는 점잖게 살아갈 만한 생활비를 보장할 만큼의 수당을 주겠다는 것이었다. 만약 조지 오즈번 부인이 다시 결혼할 생각이라면 — 오즈번 씨가 듣기로는 그것이 그녀의 뜻이라 했다 — 그래도 그 수당을 거두지는 않겠다고 했다. 다만 아이는 전적으로 러셀 광장의 외할아버지와 함께 살거나, 오즈번 씨가 정하는 다른 어떤 곳에서 살아야 하며, 이따금 조지 오즈번 부인의 거처로 가서 그녀를 만나는 것은 허락하겠다는 것을 분명히 해 두어야 한다고 했다. 이 전갈은 어느 날 편지에 담겨 그녀에게 전해졌는데, 마침 어머니는 집을 비우고 아버지는 여느 때처럼 시내에 나가 있을 때였다.\n\n그녀가 평생 화를 내는 모습을 보인 것은 두세 번에 지나지 않았는데, 오즈번 씨의 변호사는 그런 분위기에 휩싸인 그녀를 목도하는 행운을 누렸다. 편지를 다 읽은 포 씨가 그것을 그녀에게 건네자마자, 그녀는 몸을 부들부들 떨며 얼굴이 시뻘게져서는 벌떡 일어나, 종이를 백 조각으로 찢어 발로 짓밟았다. \"내가 다시 결혼을 한다고요! 자식과 갈라서는 대가로 돈을 받는다고요! 누가 감히 이런 걸 제안해서 날 모욕하는 거예요? 오즈번 씨한테 가서 이건 비겁한 편지라고 전하세요. 비겁한 편지라고요. 답장 따위는 안 하겠어요. 좋은 아침 보내세요.\" \"그러더니 그녀는 비극 속의 여왕처럼 머리 숙여 인사하며 날 방에서 내보내더군요.\" 이 이야기를 들려준 그 변호사의 말이었다.\n\n그날 그녀의 부모는 그녀의 동요를 전혀 눈치채지 못했고, 그녀도 그 면담에 대해 그들에게 일언반구 하지 않았다. 그들에게는 따로 마음 쓸 일이 있었으니, 이 순진하고 아무것도 모르는 부인과도 깊이 얽힌 일이었다. 그녀의 아버지인 노신사는 늘 투기에 손을 댔다. 포도주 회사와 석탄 회사가 그를 어떻게 망쳐 놓았는지는 우리가 이미 본 바와 같다. 그러나 여전히 안달하며 한시도 가만있지 못하고 시내를 어슬렁대던 그는, 또 다른 사업 하나에 눈독을 들였고, 어찌나 마음에 들었던지 클랩 씨의 만류에도 아랑곳없이 거기에 발을 들였는데, 사실 클랩 씨에게는 자기가 거기에 얼마나 깊이 발을 담갔는지 차마 털어놓을 엄두를 내지 못했다. 게다가 여자들 앞에서는 돈 문제를 입에 올리지 않는 것이 세들리 씨의 변함없는 신조였던 터라, 가족들은 불행한 노신사가 어쩔 수 없이 조금씩 실토하기 전까지는 자기들에게 닥쳐올 불운을 까맣게 모르고 있었다.\n\n매주 정산하던 이 작은 살림의 청구서들이 먼저 밀리기 시작했다. 인도에서 송금이 도착하지 않았노라고, 세들리 씨는 근심 어린 얼굴로 아내에게 말했다. 여태껏 청구서를 꼬박꼬박 갚아 온 터라, 가엾은 부인이 일일이 찾아다니며 기한을 미뤄 달라고 사정해야 했던 상인들 가운데 한둘은, 형편이 더 들쭉날쭉한 손님들에게는 으레 익숙했을 그 지체를 두고 몹시 노여워했다. 그래도 에미가 아무 질문 없이 선뜻 내놓는 돈이 이 작은 식구를 절반쯤 굶주린 채나마 지탱해 주었다. 그리하여 처음 여섯 달은 제법 수월하게 흘러갔고, 노년의 세들리는 여전히 자기 주식이 틀림없이 오를 것이고 만사가 잘 풀리리라는 생각을 버리지 않았다.\n\n그러나 그 반년이 끝나도록 살림을 거들 육십 파운드는 들어오지 않았고, 형편은 점점 더 깊은 곤경 속으로 빠져들었다. 점점 쇠약해지고 몹시 충격을 받은 세들리 부인은 입을 다물고 있거나, 부엌에서 클랩 부인과 함께 하염없이 우는 일이 잦았다. 정육점 주인은 유독 무뚝뚝했고, 식료품 장수는 무례했다. 한두 번은 어린 조지가 저녁상에 대해 투덜거렸고, 자기 저녁이라면 빵 한 조각으로도 만족했을 애밀리아였지만, 아들이 푸대접받고 있음을 알아채지 않을 수 없어, 아이의 건강을 지키려고 자기 사사로운 지갑을 털어 자질구레한 것들을 사들이곤 했다.\n\n마침내 그들은 그녀에게 사정을 털어놓았다. 아니, 곤경에 빠진 사람들이 으레 그러듯 두루뭉술하게 얼버무린 이야기를 들려주었다. 어느 날, 자기 몫의 돈이 들어와서 애밀리아가 그것을 막 건네려던 참이었는데, 자신이 쓴 돈의 내역을 꼼꼼히 적어 두던 그녀가, 조지의 새 옷 한 벌을 맞추기로 약속한 일이 있어 자기 배당금에서 일정액을 떼어 두겠다고 했다.\n\n그러자 비로소 진상이 드러났다. 조스의 송금이 들어오지 않았고, 집안이 곤경에 처했다는 것, 애밀리아가 진작 알아챘어야 했건만 조지 말고는 아무것도 아무도 안중에 없었던 탓이라고 어머니가 말했다. 이 말에 그녀는 한마디 없이 제 돈을 모조리 탁자 너머로 어머니에게 밀어 넘기고는, 제 방으로 돌아가 눈이 빠지도록 울었다. 그날 그녀는 또 한 차례 격한 감정에 휩싸였으니, 그 옷을, 성탄절을 위해 애지중지 마음에 품어 두었던 그 사랑스러운 옷을, 그 마름질과 모양새를 작은 모자 가게 주인이자 벗인 이와 숱한 대화를 나누며 정해 두었던 그 옷을, 가서 취소해야만 했기 때문이다.\n\n무엇보다 견디기 힘들었던 것은 이 일을 조지에게 꺼내야 한다는 것이었는데, 아이는 큰 소리로 떼를 썼다. 성탄절에는 누구나 새 옷을 입는다는 것이었다. 다른 아이들이 자기를 비웃을 거라고 했다. 새 옷을 갖고 말겠다고 했다. 엄마가 약속하지 않았느냐고 했다. 가엾은 미망인이 아이에게 줄 수 있는 것이라곤 입맞춤뿐이었다. 그녀는 눈물을 흘리며 헌 옷을 기웠다. 그녀는 그토록 바라는 새것을 마련할 무엇이라도 팔 수 있을까 하여 자기의 자그마한 패물들을 이리저리 뒤졌다. 도빈이 보내 준 그 인도산 숄이 있었다. 그녀는 예전에 어머니와 함께 러드게이트 힐에 있는 어느 멋진 인도 물품점에 가던 일을 떠올렸다. 그곳에서 숙녀들은 그런 물건들을 두고 온갖 거래와 흥정을 벌이곤 했던 것이다. 이 방도를 떠올리자 그녀의 두 볼은 발갛게 달아오르고 두 눈은 기쁨으로 반짝였으며, 이튿날 아침 그녀는 조지에게 입을 맞춰 학교로 보내면서 그 뒤로 환히 미소 지었다. 아이는 어머니의 표정에 좋은 소식이 깃들어 있음을 알아챘다.\n\n손수건에 숄을 싸서 (이 또한 그 마음씨 고운 소령이 보내 준 선물이었다) 망토 밑에 감춘 채, 그녀는 상기되고 들뜬 얼굴로 러드게이트 힐까지 줄곧 걸어갔는데, 공원 담장을 따라 종종걸음 치고 건널목을 뛰어 건너는 통에, 그녀가 황급히 지나칠 때면 숱한 남자가 돌아서서 그 발그레하고 어여쁜 얼굴을 바라보았다. 그녀는 숄을 판 돈을 어떻게 쓸지 셈을 해 보았다. 옷 말고도 아이가 그토록 갖고 싶어 하던 책들을 사 주고, 반년 치 학비를 내고, 또 아버지에게는 그 낡은 외투 대신 새 망토를 사 드릴 작정이었다. 그 소령의 선물이 지닌 값어치를 그녀가 잘못 안 것은 아니었다. 그것은 더없이 곱고 아름다운 직물이었고, 상인이 숄값으로 이십 기니를 쳐준 것은 그로서는 아주 남는 흥정을 한 셈이었다.\n\n자기가 거머쥔 재물에 어리둥절하고 들떠서 그녀는 세인트폴 성당 묘지에 있는 다턴 서점으로 달려가, 조지가 그토록 갖고 싶어 하던 「부모의 조력자」와 「샌드퍼드와 머튼」을 사고는, 꾸러미를 안은 채 거기서 합승마차에 올라 의기양양하게 집으로 돌아왔다. 그러고는 더없이 정갈한 작은 글씨로 속표지에 \"조지 오즈번, 사랑하는 엄마가 주는 성탄절 선물\"이라고 적어 넣으며 흐뭇해했다. 그 책들은 그 곱고 섬세한 글귀와 함께 오늘날까지 전해 온다.\n\n그녀가 책을 손에 들고 자기 방에서 나와, 학교에서 돌아온 조지가 찾아볼 수 있도록 그것을 아이의 책상에 놓아 두려던 참에, 복도에서 그녀와 어머니가 마주쳤다. 그 잘생긴 작은 일곱 권의 금박 장정이 노부인의 눈에 띄었다.\n\n\"그게 뭐냐?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"조지한테 줄 책 몇 권이에요.\" 애밀리아가 대답했다. \"제가, 제가 성탄절에 사 주겠다고 약속했거든요.\"\n\n\"책이라고!\" 노부인이 분개하여 외쳤다. \"온 집안이 빵이 없어 굶을 판에 책이라니! 너랑 네 아들은 호강시키고 네 사랑하는 아버지는 감옥에 안 가시게 하려고, 내가 가진 패물이란 패물은 죄다 팔아 치우고, 내 등에 두른 인도 숄에서부터 숟가락 하나까지 다 팔아서, 우리 거래처들이 우리를 모욕하지 못하게 하고, 클랩 씨가, 인색한 집주인도 아니고 점잖은 사람이고 한 집안의 가장인 그 사람이, 받아 마땅한 집세나마 받을 수 있게 해 놨건만, 책이라니! 오, 애밀리아! 너는 그 책이며, 네가 망쳐 놓고 있으면서도 떼어 놓지는 못하는 그 아들놈이며로 내 가슴을 찢어 놓는구나. 오, 애밀리아, 부디 하느님께서 내가 둔 자식보다 더 효성스러운 자식을 네게 점지해 주시기를! 조스는 늙은 아비를 노년에 저버렸고, 살림 밑천을 마련받을 수도 있고 부자가 될 수도 있는 조지는, 목에 금시계와 금줄을 두르고 귀족 도련님처럼 학교에 다니는데, 정작 내 사랑하는, 사랑하는 늙은 양반은 단 한 푼, 단 한 푼도 없으니!\" 발작하듯 흐느끼고 부르짖는 소리로 세들리 부인의 연설은 끝이 났는데, 그 소리는 이 작은 집의 방마다 메아리쳐, 다른 여자 식구들은 그 대화를 한마디도 빠짐없이 들었다.\n\n\"오, 어머니, 어머니!\" 가엾은 애밀리아가 대답하여 외쳤다. \"어머니가 제게 아무 말씀도 안 하셨잖아요. 저는, 저는 아이한테 책을 사 주겠다고 약속했고요. 저는, 저는 오늘 아침에야 제 숄을 팔았을 뿐이에요. 돈을 가지세요. 다 가지세요.\" 그러면서 그녀는 떨리는 손으로 은화와 금화를, 자기의 그 소중한 금화 소버린을 꺼내 어머니의 손에 떠넘겼고, 돈은 그 손에서 흘러넘쳐 떨어지더니 계단을 따라 데구루루 굴러 내려갔다.\n\n그러고 나서 그녀는 자기 방으로 들어가, 절망과 더없는 비참 속에 주저앉았다. 이제 그녀는 모든 것을 깨달았다. 자기의 이기심이 아이를 희생시키고 있었던 것이다. 자기만 아니었다면 아이는 부와 지위와 교육을, 그리고 아비인 조지가 자신을 위해 잃어버린 그 자리를 누릴 수도 있었을 것이다. 그저 한마디 말만 하면 되었다. 그러면 아버지는 넉넉한 형편을 되찾고, 아이는 큰 재물의 주인이 될 터였다. 오, 그 여리고 상처 입은 가슴에 그것은 얼마나 사무치는 깨달음이었던가!\n\n세상이 다 알다시피, 스타인 경의 도시 저택은 곤트 광장에 자리하고 있다. 그곳에서 그레이트 곤트 거리가 뻗어 나가는데, 우리가 고인이 된 피트 크롤리 경의 시절에 레베카를 처음 데려갔던 바로 그 거리이다. 난간 너머로, 검은 나무들 사이로 광장의 정원을 들여다보면, 핼쑥한 얼굴의 학생 몇을 데리고 정원을 빙빙 도는 가엾은 가정교사 몇몇이 보인다. 그 한가운데에는 음울한 잔디밭이 펼쳐져 있고, 거기에는 민덴 전투에서 싸웠던 곤트 경의 동상이, 세 갈래 가발을 쓰고 그 밖의 차림은 로마 황제 같은 모습으로 솟아 있다. 곤트 저택은 광장의 거의 한 면을 통째로 차지하고 있다. 나머지 세 면은 과부살이로 영락한 저택들로 이루어져 있다. 돌로 된 창틀이나, 좀 더 밝은 붉은빛으로 가장자리를 두른 창틀이 달린, 높다랗고 어두침침한 집들 말이다. 이제 그 야위고 적막한 여닫이창 안쪽에는 불빛이라곤 거의 보이지 않고, 손님을 맞이하는 환대도 그 문에서는 사라져 버렸다. 옛 시절 레이스 두른 하인들과 횃불 든 소년들이 사라진 것이나 진배없이 말이다. 그 소년들은 계단 위 등불 양옆에 지금도 남아 있는, 텅 빈 쇠 소화기에 횃불을 끄곤 했던 것이다. 이제 광장에는 놋쇠 명패들이 파고들었다. 개업의들, 디들섹스 은행 서부 지점, 영국유럽연합회 따위가 말이다. 광장은 음울한 행색을 띠고 있다. 그렇다고 스타인 경의 저택이 덜 음울한 것도 아니다. 내가 그 저택에서 본 것이라곤 앞쪽의 거대한 담장, 큰 대문에 세워진 투박한 기둥들이 전부였다. 그 사이로 살지고 음침한 붉은 얼굴의 늙은 문지기가 이따금 빠끔히 내다보곤 했다. 그리고 담장 너머로는 다락방과 침실의 창문들, 그리고 이제는 좀처럼 연기가 피어오르지 않는 굴뚝들이 보일 뿐이었다. 지금의 스타인 경은 나폴리에 살고 있어서, 곤트 광장 담장의 음울한 모습보다는 만과 카프리섬과 베수비오 화산의 경치를 더 좋아하기 때문이다.\n\n뉴 곤트 거리를 따라 몇십 야드 내려가, 실제로 곤트 마구간으로 통하는 곳에는, 다른 어느 마구간 문과도 구별되지 않을 만큼 수수한 작은 뒷문 하나가 있다. 하지만 나에게 이 이야기를 들려준 사람(모르는 것이 없는 데다 이곳을 직접 보여 준 꼬마 톰 이브스)의 말로는, 작고 은밀한 마차들이 그 문 앞에 수도 없이 멈춰 섰다고 한다. \"왕자님과 퍼디타가 저 문을 들락거렸지요, 손님.\" 그는 종종 내게 말하곤 했다. \"마리안 클라크도 ××공작과 함께 저 문으로 들어갔답니다. 저 문은 스타인 경의 그 유명한 작은 별실로 통하지요. 손님, 한 방은 온통 상아와 흰 새틴으로, 또 한 방은 흑단과 검은 벨벳으로 꾸며져 있답니다. 폼페이의 살루스티우스 저택에서 본떠 코스웨이가 그림을 그린 작은 연회실도 있고요. 또 작은 전용 주방이 있는데, 거기엔 냄비란 냄비가 죄다 은이고 꼬챙이는 모조리 금이었답니다. 바로 거기서 오를레앙의 에갈리테가 자고새를 구웠지요. 그가 스타인 후작과 함께 옴브르 노름으로 어느 귀하신 분에게서 십만을 따낸 그날 밤에 말입니다. 그 돈의 절반은 프랑스 혁명에 들어갔고, 절반은 곤트 경의 후작 작위와 가터 훈장을 사는 데 쓰였지요. 그리고 그 나머지는……\" 하지만 그 나머지가 어찌 되었는지를 전하는 것은 우리 이야기의 범위에 들지 않는다. 모든 이의 사정을 다 아는 꼬마 톰 이브스라면, 그 한 푼 한 푼까지, 아니 그보다 훨씬 더 많은 것까지 기꺼이 설명할 준비가 되어 있겠지만 말이다.\n\n도시 저택 말고도, 후작은 삼 왕국의 곳곳에 성과 궁을 거느리고 있었다. 그 묘사는 여행 안내서에서 찾아볼 수 있다. 섀넌강 기슭의 숲을 거느린 스트롱보 성, 리처드 이세가 포로로 잡혔던 카마던셔의 곤트 성, 그리고 요크셔의 곤틀리 홀이 있는데, 내가 들은 바로는 그곳에는 저택 손님들의 아침 식사를 위한 은제 찻주전자가 이백 개나 있었고, 그 밖의 온갖 것도 그 호화로움에 걸맞았다고 한다. 또 햄프셔에는 후작의 농장인 스틸브룩이 있었는데, 소박한 거처이긴 했으나, 후작이 세상을 떠나면서 근래 이름난 경매인이 처분했던 그 놀라운 가구들을 우리 모두 기억하고 있다.\n\n스타인 후작 부인은 카멜롯의 후작인 케어라이언 가문의, 저 유명하고 유서 깊은 일족 출신이었다. 이 가문은 그들의 첫 조상인 존경하는 드루이드가 개종한 이래로 줄곧 옛 신앙을 지켜 왔고, 그 가계도는 브루투스 왕이 이 섬에 도래한 시점을 한참이나 거슬러 올라간다. 이 가문 장남의 칭호는 펜드래건이다. 그 아들들은 아득한 옛날부터 아서, 우서, 카라독이라 불려 왔다. 그 가문의 머리는 충성스러운 음모에 휘말려 숱하게 떨어져 나갔다. 엘리자베스 여왕은 당대의 아서, 그러니까 필립과 메리의 시종이었으며 스코틀랜드 여왕과 그 외삼촌인 기즈 가문 사이에 편지를 날랐던 그 아서의 목을 베어 버렸다. 가문의 차남 한 사람은 대공의 장교로서 저 유명한 성 바르톨로메오 음모에서 두각을 나타냈다. 메리가 유폐되어 있던 내내, 카멜롯 가문은 그녀를 위해 음모를 꾸몄다. 이 가문은 무적함대 시절 에스파냐에 맞서 군비를 갖추느라 들인 비용으로 큰 손해를 보았고, 그에 못지않게 사제를 숨겨 주고, 완강하게 국교를 거부하고, 교황파의 악행을 저질렀다 하여 엘리자베스에게 벌금과 몰수를 당해 손해를 보았다. 제임스 시절의 한 변절자는 저 위대한 신학자의 논변에 잠시 신앙을 저버렸고, 그 시의적절한 나약함 덕분에 가문의 재산은 다소 회복되었다. 그러나 찰스 치세의 카멜롯 백작은 가문의 옛 신조로 돌아갔고, 그들은 반란을 이끌거나 부추길 스튜어트 왕가의 후예가 한 사람이라도 남아 있는 한, 그 신앙을 위해 싸우고 그 신앙을 위해 스스로를 파멸시키기를 멈추지 않았다.\n\n메리 케어라이언 영애는 파리의 한 수녀원에서 자랐으며, 황태자비 마리 앙투아네트가 그녀의 대모였다. 그 미모가 한창 자랑스럽던 시절, 그녀는 당시 파리에 있던 곤트 경과 결혼했다. 사람들 말로는, 팔려 갔다고들 했다. 곤트 경은 오를레앙 공 필립의 연회 자리에서 그 영애의 오라비에게서 막대한 돈을 따낸 사람이었다. 곤트 백작이 회색 총사대의 라마르슈 백작과 벌인 그 유명한 결투는, 세간의 소문에 따르면 그 장교(한때 시동이었고 여전히 여왕의 총애를 받던 자)가 아름다운 메리 케어라이언 영애의 손을 넘본 데서 비롯된 것이라 했다. 그녀는 백작이 부상으로 앓아누워 있는 동안 곤트 경과 혼인하여, 곤트 저택에 거처를 정하고 잠시나마 웨일스 공의 화려한 궁정에서 빛을 발했다. 폭스는 그녀를 위해 축배를 들었다. 모리스와 셰리든은 그녀에 관한 노래를 지었다. 맘즈베리는 그녀에게 최고의 예를 갖추었고, 월폴은 그녀를 매혹적이라 평했으며, 데번셔는 그녀를 거의 질투할 지경이었다. 그러나 그녀는 자신이 내던져진 그 사교계의 거친 향락과 흥청거림에 겁을 먹었고, 아들 둘을 낳은 뒤로는 경건한 칩거의 삶 속으로 움츠러들고 말았다. 향락과 명랑함을 좋아하던 스타인 경이 결혼 후 이 떨고, 말없고, 미신에 사로잡히고, 불행한 부인 곁에 좀처럼 모습을 드러내지 않았던 것도 무리는 아니었다.\n\n앞서 언급한 톰 이브스(런던의 모든 명사를 알고 각 가문의 사연과 비밀을 죄다 알고 있다는 점 말고는 이 이야기에 아무런 몫도 없는 인물이다)는 스타인 부인에 관해 더 많은 정보를 가지고 있었는데, 사실인지 아닌지는 알 수 없다. \"그 여자가 제 집에서 감내해야 했던 모욕이란,\" 톰은 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"정말이지 끔찍한 것이었지요. 스타인 경은 그 부인을, 나 같으면 차라리 죽었으면 죽었지 내 아내 이브스 부인과는 어울리게 하지 않을 그런 여자들과 한 식탁에 앉게 했답니다. 크래큰베리 부인이라든가, 치펀햄 부인이라든가, 프랑스인 서기관의 아내인 드라크뤼슈카세 부인이라든가 하는 여자들과 말이지요(그 여자들을 알고 지내려고 제 아내라도 기꺼이 바쳤을 톰 이브스로 말하자면, 그 여자들 누구에게서든 인사 한번 받거나 만찬 한번 얻어먹으면 더없이 기뻐했겠지만요). 한마디로, 당대를 주름잡는 총애받는 여인과 한 식탁에 앉게 했던 것이지요. 그런데 부르봉 가문 못지않게 콧대 높은 그 가문 출신의 여자, 스타인 가문 따위는 한낱 하인이요 어제오늘 솟아난 버섯에 불과한 그런 가문 출신의 여자가(따지고 보면 그들은 옛 곤트 가문이 아니라 그 가문의 변변찮고 미심쩍은 방계 출신이거든요), 영국에서 가장 도도한 그 스타인 후작 부인이, 무슨 까닭도 없이 남편 앞에 그토록 고분고분 머리를 조아렸으리라고 생각하십니까(독자께서는 늘 톰 이브스가 하는 말이라는 것을 명심하시기 바란다)? 천만의 말씀! 내 말하건대, 거기엔 은밀한 사정이 있는 겁니다. 내 말하건대, 망명 시절에 이곳에 와서 퓌제, 탱트니악과 함께 키브롱 사건에 가담했던 라마르슈 신부, 그자가 바로 팔육년에 스타인이 결투를 벌였던 그 회색 총사대의 대령이었던 거지요. 그자와 후작 부인이 다시 만났고, 그 성직자가 된 대령이 브르타뉴에서 총에 맞아 죽은 다음부터, 스타인 부인이 지금까지 이어 오는 그 극단적인 신앙 행위에 빠져들었던 겁니다. 그녀는 날마다 고해 신부와 단둘이 틀어박혀 지내고, 아침마다 스패니시 플레이스에서 예배를 드리지요. 내가 거기서 그녀를 지켜봤으니까요. 아니, 어쩌다 그 앞을 지나가게 된 거지만요. 장담컨대, 그 여자한테는 무슨 비밀이 있는 겁니다. 뉘우칠 무언가가 있지 않고서야 사람이 그렇게까지 불행할 수는 없는 노릇이지요.\" 톰 이브스는 다 안다는 듯 고개를 까딱이며 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"그리고 장담컨대, 후작이 그 여자 머리 위에 들이밀 칼자루를 쥐고 있지 않았다면, 그 여자가 그렇게까지 고분고분하지는 않았을 겁니다.\"\n\n그러니, 이브스 씨의 정보가 정확하다면, 그토록 높은 지위에 오른 이 부인이 남몰래 숱한 수모를 감내하고 차분한 얼굴 뒤에 숱한 은밀한 슬픔을 감춰야 했을 가능성도 다분하다. 그러니 명사 인명록에 이름이 오르지 못한 우리 형제들이여, 우리네 윗사람들이 얼마나 비참할 수 있는지를 흐뭇하게 떠올리며 스스로를 위로하자. 새틴 방석에 앉아 금 접시로 시중을 받는 다모클레스의 머리 위에는, 집달리든, 유전병이든, 가문의 비밀이든 하는 모양을 한 무시무시한 칼이 매달려 있는 법이며, 그 비밀이라는 것은 수놓은 벽걸이 천 사이로 이따금 섬뜩하게 빠끔히 고개를 내밀다가, 언젠가는 반드시 떨어질 자리에 떨어지고야 마는 것이다.\n\n또한 가난한 자의 처지를 높으신 분의 처지와 견주어 볼 때, (역시 이브스 씨에 따르면) 가난한 자에게는 또 하나의 위안거리가 있다. 물려줄 가산도 물려받을 가산도 거의 없거나 아예 없는 그대는, 아버지나 아들과 사이좋게 지낼 수 있다. 반면 스타인 경 같은 대공의 후계자는, 제 왕국에서 밀려나 있다는 데 자연히 분개할 수밖에 없고, 그 자리를 차지하고 있는 자를 그리 곱지 않은 눈초리로 바라볼 수밖에 없는 것이다. \"이건 정해진 이치라고 보면 됩니다.\" 이 냉소적인 늙은 이브스는 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"온 명문가의 아비와 장남은 서로 미워하는 법이지요. 왕세자는 늘 왕관에 반기를 들거나 그걸 갈망하는 법이고요. 셰익스피어는 세상을 알았던 사람입니다, 손님. 그가 핼 왕자(곤트 가문이 그 후손이라 자처하지만, 따지고 보면 손님이 곤트의 존과 무관한 것만큼이나 그들도 무관하지요)가 제 아비의 왕관을 한번 써 보는 장면을 그릴 때, 그건 모든 추정 상속인의 본성을 있는 그대로 그려 낸 것이랍니다. 손님이 공작 작위와 하루 천 파운드의 상속인이라면, 그걸 손에 넣고 싶지 않을 거라고 말씀하실 텐가요? 천만의 말씀! 그러니 모든 위인이, 제 아비를 향해 이런 감정을 품어 봤으니, 제 아들도 자신을 향해 그 감정을 품고 있다는 걸 모를 리 없다는 게 당연한 이치지요. 그러니 그들은 서로 의심하고 적대할 수밖에 없는 겁니다.\n\n\"그리고 또 형이 동생들을 어떻게 보느냐 하는 문제도 그렇습니다. 손님, 아셔야 할 것은, 모든 맏형이 가문의 차남들을 타고난 적으로 본다는 점이에요. 마땅히 제 것이어야 할 현금을 그만큼 빼앗아 가는 자들이라 이 말입니다. 바자제트 경의 장남인 조지 맥 터크가 이런 말을 하는 걸 나는 종종 들었지요. 자기가 작위를 물려받으면 마음대로 할 수만 있다면 술탄들처럼 해서, 동생들 목을 한꺼번에 죄다 쳐 버려 영지를 깨끗이 정리하겠다고 말입니다. 정도의 차이는 있을지언정 그들 모두가 다 이런 식이지요. 내 말하건대, 그자들은 죄다 속으로는 투르크인입니다. 흥, 손님, 그자들은 세상을 알거든요.\" 그러다가 마침 위인 한 사람이 다가오기라도 하면, 톰 이브스의 모자는 머리에서 벗겨지고, 그는 절을 하며 히죽 웃는 얼굴로 달려 나갔는데, 그 모습은 그 역시 세상을 안다는 것을 보여 주는 것이었다. 그러니까, 톰 이브스 식으로 말이다. 그리고 자기 재산을 한 푼도 남김없이 연금에 쏟아부은 톰으로서는, 조카들에게 아무런 악감정도 품지 않을 만한 여유가 있었고, 윗사람들에 대해서는 그들과 함께 만찬을 들고 싶다는 변함없고 너그러운 소망 말고는 다른 감정을 품지 않을 여유가 있었다.\n\n후작 부인과, 자식을 향한 어미의 자연스럽고 애틋한 정 사이에는, 신앙의 차이라는 저 잔인한 장벽이 가로놓여 있었다. 그녀가 아들들에게 느낄 법한 바로 그 사랑이, 오히려 이 소심하고 경건한 부인을 더욱 두렵고 불행하게 만들 따름이었다. 그들을 갈라놓은 심연은 치명적이고 건널 수 없는 것이었다. 그녀는 그 위로 가냘픈 두 팔을 뻗을 수도, 자기 신앙이 일러 주는바 안전이라곤 없는 저쪽으로부터 자식들을 이쪽으로 끌어당길 수도 없었다. 아들들이 어리던 시절, 학식이 깊고 궤변에도 일가견이 있던 스타인 경은, 시골에서 만찬을 든 뒤의 저녁나절에 이보다 더 좋은 소일거리가 없었다. 와인을 앞에 두고, 아들들의 가정교사인 트레일 목사(지금의 일링 주교)와 부인의 고해 신부인 몰 신부를 맞붙이고, 옥스퍼드와 생타셸을 서로 겨루게 하는 일 말이다. 그는 번갈아 가며 \"잘한다, 래티머! 옳거니, 로욜라!\" 하고 외쳤다. 몰에게는 개종하면 주교 자리를 주겠노라 약속했고, 트레일에게는 탈교하면 추기경 모자를 얻어 주는 데 자기 영향력을 다 쓰겠노라 맹세했다. 두 성직자 어느 쪽도 굴복하려 들지 않았고, 자애로운 어미는 막내이자 가장 아끼는 아들이 제 교회, 그러니까 어미의 교회와 화해하기를 바랐건만, 이 경건한 부인을 기다리고 있던 것은 슬프고도 끔찍한 실망이었다. 그것은 마치 그녀의 결혼이라는 죄에 대한 심판처럼 보이는 실망이었다.\n\n귀족 명감을 들춰 본 사람이라면 누구나 알다시피, 곤트 경은 블랑슈 시슬우드 영애와 결혼했다. 이 진실한 이야기에 앞서 언급된 바 있는 명문 베어에이커스 가문의 딸이었다. 곤트 저택의 한쪽 별채가 이 부부에게 배정되었다. 가문의 수장이 친히 저택을 다스리기를 택했고, 다스리는 동안에는 절대적으로 군림하기를 택했기 때문이다. 그러나 그의 아들이자 후계자는 집에 거의 머물지 않았고, 아내와 사이가 틀어졌으며, 아버지가 내주려는 지극히 인색한 액수를 넘어서서 필요한 돈은 상속 담보 차용증으로 빌려다 썼다. 후작은 아들의 빚을 한 푼까지 죄다 알고 있었다. 애석한 그의 죽음 뒤에, 후작 자신이 후계자의 채권을 여럿 소유하고 있었음이 드러났는데, 그것들은 그들을 위한답시고 사들였던 것을, 후작이 막내아들의 자식들에게 유증한 것이었다.\n\n곤트 경에게는 낭패스럽게도, 또 그의 타고난 적이자 아비 되는 사람에게는 키득거릴 만큼 고소하게도, 곤트 부인에게는 자식이 없었다. 그리하여 빈에서 왈츠와 외교에 몰두하던 조지 곤트 경에게 돌아오라는 명이 떨어졌고, 명예로운 조앤 양과 혼인하라는 명이 떨어졌다. 그녀는 헬벨린의 초대 남작이자 스레드니들 거리의 은행가, 존스 브라운 로빈슨 상사의 수장인 존 존스의 외동딸이었다. 이 결합에서 여러 아들딸이 태어났으나, 그들의 행적은 이 이야기와 무관하다.\n\n이 결혼은 처음에는 행복하고 순탄했다. 조지 곤트 경은 글을 읽을 줄 알았을 뿐만 아니라 제법 정확하게 쓸 줄도 알았다. 프랑스어도 상당히 유창하게 구사했고, 유럽에서 손꼽히는 왈츠 명수이기도 했다. 이런 재능에다 본가의 후광까지 더해졌으니, 그가 외교관으로서 최고의 영예에 오르리라는 것은 의심할 여지가 거의 없었다. 그의 아내인 부인은 궁정이야말로 자기 무대라고 느꼈고, 그 재력 덕분에 남편의 외교 임무가 이끄는 대륙의 도시들에서 호화로운 접대를 베풀 수 있었다. 그를 공사로 임명한다는 말이 나돌았고, 트래블러스 클럽에서는 머지않아 그가 대사가 되리라는 데 내기가 걸렸다. 그런데 별안간, 그 서기관의 괴이한 행동에 관한 소문이 날아들었다. 그의 상관이 베푼 성대한 외교 만찬에서, 그가 벌떡 일어나 거위 간 파테에 독이 들었다고 외쳤다는 것이었다. 그는 바이에른 공사인 슈프링복호헨라우펜 백작의 저택에서 열린 무도회에, 머리를 빡빡 밀고 카푸친 수도사 차림을 하고 나타났다. 어떤 이들이 그대에게 믿게 하려던 것처럼 가장무도회였던 것도 아니었다. 뭔가 이상한 일이라고, 사람들은 수군거렸다. 그의 할아버지도 그랬다는 것이었다. 집안 내력이라는 것이었다.\n\n그의 아내와 자식들은 본국으로 돌아와 곤트 저택에 거처를 정했다. 조지 경은 유럽 대륙의 직책을 내놓았고, 브라질로 발령이 났다고 관보에 실렸다. 그러나 사람들은 더 잘 알고 있었다. 그는 그 브라질 원정에서 돌아온 적이 없었다. 그곳에서 죽은 적도, 그곳에서 산 적도, 애초에 그곳에 가 본 적도 없었다. 그는 아무 데도 없었다. 아예 사라져 버린 것이었다. \"브라질이라니,\" 한 입방아꾼이 다른 이에게 히죽거리며 말했다. \"브라질이란 세인트존스우드를 말하는 거지. 리우데자네이루란 사방이 담장으로 둘러싸인 별채 한 채를 말하는 거고, 조지 곤트는 그자를 구속복 훈장으로 치장해 준 어느 간수에게 파견된 셈이라네.\" 이런 것들이야말로 사람들이 허영의 시장에서 서로에게 바치는 부류의 묘비명인 것이다.\n\n일주일에 두세 번씩, 이른 새벽이면 가엾은 어미는 제 죄를 갚으러 그 가엾은 병자를 보러 갔다. 어떤 때는 그가 어미를 보고 웃었고(그 웃음은 우는 소리를 듣는 것보다 더 측은했다), 또 어떤 때는 빈 회의 시절의 그 눈부신 멋쟁이 외교관이 아이 장난감을 질질 끌고 다니거나 간수네 아기 인형을 어르고 있는 모습을 보기도 했다. 어떤 때는 그가 어미와, 어미의 고해 신부이자 길동무인 몰 신부를 알아보았으나, 더 자주 어미를 잊어버렸다. 아내며 자식이며 사랑이며 야망이며 허영을 잊어버린 것처럼 말이다. 그러나 그는 제 식사 시간만은 기억하고 있어서, 물 탄 와인이 충분히 진하지 않으면 울곤 했다.\n\n그것은 핏줄에 깃든 알 수 없는 오점이었다. 가엾은 어미가 제 유서 깊은 가문에서 그것을 가져온 것이었다. 그 화근은 스타인 부인의 죄가 시작되기도, 그 죄를 속죄하느라 그녀의 단식과 눈물과 고행이 바쳐지기도 한참 전에, 아비 쪽 집안에서도 한두 번 터져 나온 적이 있었다. 그 가문의 자랑거리는 파라오의 맏아들처럼 쓰러졌다. 운명과 파멸의 어두운 표지가 그 문지방에 새겨져 있었다. 작위 관과 새겨진 문장이 위에 얹힌, 높다랗고 오래된 그 문지방에 말이다.\n\n한편 그 부재중인 경의 자식들은, 자기들 머리 위에도 그 파멸이 드리워져 있다는 것을 까맣게 모른 채 재잘거리며 자라났다. 처음에 아이들은 아버지 이야기를 하며 그가 돌아오면 무얼 할지 궁리를 짜냈다. 그러다가 산 채로 죽은 그 사람의 이름이 그들 입에 오르는 일이 차츰 뜸해졌고, 마침내 아예 입에 오르지 않게 되었다. 그러나 충격에 잠긴 늙은 할머니는, 이 아이들 또한 제 아비의 명예뿐 아니라 그 수치까지 물려받은 후계자들이라는 생각에 몸을 떨었고, 저 무시무시한 조상 대대로의 저주가 이 아이들에게 내려올 그날을 병들 듯 노심초사하며 지켜보았다.\n\n이 어두운 예감은 스타인 경 또한 따라다니며 괴롭혔다. 그는 그 끔찍한 머리맡의 유령을 와인과 흥청거림이라는 홍해에 가라앉히려 애썼고, 향락의 무리와 소동 속에서 이따금 그것을 시야에서 놓치곤 했다. 그러나 그것은 혼자 있을 때면 늘 그에게 되돌아왔고, 세월이 흐를수록 더욱 위협적으로 자라나는 듯했다. \"네 아들을 데려갔거늘,\" 그것은 말했다. \"어찌 너라고 못 데려가랴? 언젠가 네 아들 조지처럼 너를 감방에 가둘 수도 있느니. 내일이라도 네 머리를 툭 칠 수 있느니. 그러면 향락도 명예도, 잔치도 미색도, 벗도 아첨꾼도, 프랑스 요리사도, 좋은 말과 집도 다 사라지고, 그 대신 감방과 간수와 조지 곤트의 것 같은 짚 깔개가 남으리라.\" 그러면 후작은 자기를 위협하는 그 유령에게 맞섰다. 적의 코를 납작하게 만들 수 있는 비책을 알고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n그리하여 곤트 저택의, 그을음 낀 작위 관과 머리글자가 새겨진 높다란 문 안쪽에는 화려함과 부가 있었으나, 어쩌면 그리 대단한 행복은 없었던 것이다. 그곳의 잔치는 런던에서 가장 성대한 축에 들었으나, 후작의 식탁에 앉은 손님들 사이를 빼고는 거기에 그리 큰 만족이라곤 없었다. 그가 그토록 대단한 대공이 아니었던들, 그를 찾아오는 이는 아마 몇 안 되었을 것이다. 그러나 허영의 시장에서는 아주 높으신 분들의 죄란 너그럽게 보아 넘겨지는 법이다. (어느 프랑스 부인이 말했듯이) \"누 르가르동 아 되 푸아\"—후작처럼 의심할 여지 없이 지체 높은 인물을 단죄하기에 앞서 우리는 \"두 번 들여다보는\" 것이다. 이름난 트집쟁이나 까다로운 도덕군자 몇몇이 스타인 경에게 뾰로통할 수도 있었지만, 그가 청하면 그들은 기꺼이 달려왔다.\n\n\"스타인 경은 정말이지 너무 못됐어요.\" 슬링스톤 부인은 말했다. \"하지만 다들 가니까, 나도 물론 우리 딸들이 해를 입지 않도록 잘 살펴야지요.\" \"그분으로 말하자면 내가 많은 신세를, 아니 인생의 모든 것을 빚진 분이지요.\" 트레일 주교는 이렇게 말했는데, 대주교 자리가 다소 위태롭다고 여기던 터였고, 트레일 부인과 그 댁 따님들은 교회에 빠지는 것이나 그분의 연회에 빠지는 것이나 똑같이 꺼릴 지경이었다. \"그분 품행은 나빠.\" 어린 사우스다운 경은, 곤트 저택에서 벌어지는 일들에 관해 어머니에게서 무시무시한 전설을 들은 터라 얌전히 항변하던 누이에게 이렇게 말했다. \"하지만 어쩌겠어, 그 댁엔 유럽에서 가장 좋은 드라이 실레리가 있는걸!\" 그리고 준남작 피트 크롤리 경으로 말하자면—저 예법의 본보기인 피트 경, 선교회 모임에서 앞장서 사회를 보던 피트 경으로 말하자면—그 역시 가지 않겠다는 생각은 한순간도 하지 않았다. \"일링 주교와 슬링스톤 백작 부인 같은 분들이 보이는 자리라면, 제인, 우리가 틀릴 리는 없다고 봐도 무방하지.\" 준남작은 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"스타인 경의 높은 지위와 신분으로 보아, 그분은 우리 같은 처지의 사람들을 좌지우지할 만한 위치에 계신 거요. 한 주의 부지사라면, 여보, 존경할 만한 인물 아니겠소. 게다가 조지 곤트와 나는 젊은 시절에 가깝게 지냈고, 우리가 품퍼니켈에서 함께 외교관 보좌로 있을 때 그는 내 후배였단 말이오.\"\n\n한마디로, 청을 받은 사람이라면 누구나 이 대단한 인물에게 문안을 드리러 갔다. 독자인 그대(아니라고 하지 마시라)나 이 글을 쓰는 나라도, 초대를 받았더라면 갔을 것처럼 말이다.\n\n# CHAPTER XLVIII\n\n마침내 베키가 남편 집안의 어른에게 베푼 친절과 정성은 더없이 큰 보상을 받게 될 운명이었다. 비록 그 보상이 다소 실속 없는 것이긴 했어도, 이 자그마한 여인은 그보다 더 확실한 이득보다도 그것을 한층 더 열렬히 갈망했다. 비록 그녀가 정숙한 삶을 살고 싶어 한 것은 아니었더라도, 적어도 정숙하다는 평판만큼은 누리고 싶어 했던 것이다. 그리고 우리가 알다시피, 상류 사회의 어떤 숙녀도 끌리는 옷자락과 깃 장식을 두르고 궁정에서 군주를 알현하기 전까지는 이 절실한 소망을 손에 넣을 수가 없는 법이다. 그 장엄한 알현을 마치고 나오면 그들은 정숙한 여인이라는 도장이 찍힌 셈이 된다. 시종장이 그들에게 정숙함의 증명서를 발급해 주는 것이다. 그리고 미심쩍은 물품이나 편지가 검역소에서 화덕을 거치고 향초를 뿌린 뒤에야 깨끗하다고 선언되듯이, 그러지 않았다면 평판이 미심쩍어 전염병을 옮길 우려가 있었을 숱한 숙녀들이, 왕실 알현이라는 이 위생적인 시련을 거치고 나면 온갖 오염에서 벗어난 몸으로 나오는 것이었다.\n\n베어에이커스 부인이나 터프토 부인, 시골의 뷰트 크롤리 부인, 그리고 로던 크롤리 부인과 마주친 적이 있는 다른 숙녀들로서야, 그 가증스러운 자그마한 모험가가 군주 앞에서 무릎을 굽혀 인사를 올린다는 발상에 흥하고 콧방귀를 뀌며, 만약 어질고 인자한 샬럿 왕비가 살아 계셨더라면 그토록 단정치 못한 인물을 그 정결한 응접실에 결코 들이지 않으셨으리라 단언했을 법도 하다. 그러나 로던 부인이 그 시험을 치르고, 말하자면 평판이라는 학위를 받은 자리가 다름 아닌 유럽 제일의 신사가 친히 임석한 자리였음을 헤아려 본다면, 그녀의 정숙함을 더 이상 의심한다는 것은 분명 노골적인 불충임이 틀림없을 것이다. 나로 말하자면, 역사 속의 그 위대한 인물을 사랑과 경외의 마음으로 돌이켜 본다. 아, 저 존경스럽고 장엄한 존재가 이 제국의 세련되고 교양 있는 부류의 만장일치 갈채에 힘입어 그 왕국의 으뜸가는 신사라는 칭호를 받았을 때, 허영의 시장에는 숙녀의 자질에 대한 얼마나 드높고 고결한 안목이 있었던 것인지! 친애하는 엠— 군, 오 내 젊은 날의 벗이여, 그대는 기억하는가, 스물다섯 해 전 어느 더없이 행복한 밤을, 「위선자」가 상연되고 엘리스턴이 극장 지배인이었으며 다우턴과 리스턴이 배우로 나왔던 그날, 슬로터하우스 학교에서 교육받던 두 소년이 충성스러운 선생들의 허락을 받아 학교를 빠져나와, 왕을 맞이하려 거기 모인 군중 틈에 끼어 드루리 레인 극장 무대에 모습을 드러냈던 일을 말이다. 왕이라고? 그분이 거기 계셨다. 그 장엄한 특별석 앞에는 근위 호위병들이 있었고, 화장방 시종장이신 스타인 후작과 다른 고관대작들이 그분이 앉으신 의자 뒤에 서 있었으며, 그분이 앉아 계셨다—불그레한 얼굴에 풍채는 당당하고, 온갖 훈장을 두르고, 풍성하게 곱슬거리는 머리채를 하신 채로. 우리가 그분을 위해 「하느님 폐하를 지키소서」를 어떻게 불렀던가! 그 장엄한 음악에 극장이 어떻게 흔들리며 함성으로 들끓었던가. 사람들이 어떻게 환호하고, 외치고, 손수건을 흔들었던가. 숙녀들은 울었고, 어머니들은 자식을 끌어안았으며, 더러는 감격에 겨워 정신을 잃기도 했다. 일층 관람석에서는 사람들이 숨이 막힐 지경이었고, 그분을 위해서라면 기꺼이 죽을 각오가 되어 있던—실로 거의 그래 보이기까지 했던—백성들이 몸부림치며 외쳐 대는 무리 속에서 비명과 신음이 솟아올랐다. 그렇다, 우리는 그분을 보았다. 운명도 그것만큼은 우리에게서 앗아 가지 못한다. 더러는 나폴레옹을 보았을 것이다. 프리드리히 대왕이며 존슨 박사며 마리 앙투아네트 따위를 직접 본 이도 아직 몇몇 살아 있을 것이다. 우리는 자손들에게 우리가 선량하고 장엄하며 위대하신 조지 임금을 뵈었노라고 자랑해도 마땅하리라.\n\n자, 이윽고 로던 크롤리 부인의 삶에 행복한 날이 찾아왔다. 그토록 갈망하던 궁정이라는 낙원에 이 천사가 받아들여지고, 손위 동서가 그녀의 후견인 노릇을 한 것이었다. 정해진 날이 되자, 피트 경과 그 부인이 큼직한 가족용 마차에 올라(이 마차는 그 준남작이 주의 주장관 직무를 맡게 된 것에 대비해 막 새로 지어진 것이었다) 커즌 거리의 자그마한 집으로 다가왔는데, 채소 가게에서 지켜보던 래글스에게는 그야말로 교화가 될 만한 광경이었다. 안에 꽂힌 고운 깃 장식이 보였고, 새로 지은 하인들의 제복 가슴팍에는 커다란 꽃다발이 달려 있었다.\n\n피트 경은 번쩍이는 예복 차림으로 마차에서 내려, 다리 사이에 검을 찬 채 커즌 거리로 들어갔다. 어린 로던은 응접실 창유리에 얼굴을 바짝 댄 채, 마차 안에 있는 숙모를 향해 온 힘을 다해 미소 짓고 고개를 끄덕였다. 이윽고 피트 경이 다시 집에서 나오면서 한 숙녀를 이끌고 나왔는데, 그녀는 화려한 깃 장식을 꽂고 흰 숄을 두른 채, 더없이 화려한 브로케이드 자락을 우아하게 추켜들고 있었다. 그녀는 마치 공주라도 된 양, 평생토록 궁정 출입을 해 온 사람인 양 마차에 올라타며, 문가의 하인에게도 자기를 따라 마차에 오른 피트 경에게도 우아하게 미소를 지어 보였다.\n\n뒤이어 로던이 옛 근위대 예복 차림으로 따라 올랐는데, 그 옷은 한심하리만큼 낡아빠진 데다 몸에 너무 끼었다. 본래 그는 행렬을 뒤따라 따로 삯마차를 타고 가서 군주를 알현하기로 되어 있었으나, 인심 좋은 손위 동서가 한 가족끼리 다 함께 가야 한다고 우긴 것이었다. 마차는 컸고 숙녀들은 그리 덩치가 크지 않았으니, 옷자락은 무릎 위에 받쳐 들면 될 일이었다—마침내 네 사람은 화목하게 함께 길을 나섰고, 그들의 마차는 이내 피커딜리와 세인트 제임스 거리를 따라, 브런즈윅의 별이 귀족과 양가의 사람들을 맞이하려 기다리고 있는 그 오래된 벽돌 궁전을 향해 내려가는 왕실 마차들의 행렬에 끼어들었다.\n\n베키는 어찌나 마음이 들떴던지, 또 마침내 인생에서 도달한 그 위엄 있는 지위가 어찌나 강하게 느껴졌던지, 마차 창밖으로 사람들에게 축복이라도 내려 주고 싶은 심정이었다. 우리의 베키마저도 약점이 있었던 것이다. 흔히 보다시피 사람들이란 남들은 좀처럼 알아주지 않는 장기를 두고 스스로 우쭐대는 법이어서—이를테면 코머스는 자기가 영국 제일의 비극 배우라고 굳게 믿고, 그 유명한 소설가 브라운은 천재로 여겨지기보다 멋쟁이로 여겨지기를 갈망하며, 위대한 법률가 로빈슨은 웨스트민스터 법정에서의 평판 따위에는 도통 관심이 없고 들판에서 다섯 단짜리 울타리를 뛰어넘는 솜씨에서만큼은 자기가 견줄 자 없다고 믿는 것이다—그와 마찬가지로 점잖은 여인이 되는 것, 그리고 점잖은 여인으로 여겨지는 것이 바로 베키의 인생 목표였고, 그녀는 놀라우리만치 부지런하고 능숙하게, 또 성공적으로 그 우아함을 익혀 나갔다. 앞서 말했듯이, 그녀는 이따금 자신이 정말로 귀부인이라고 믿으며, 집 안 금고에는 돈 한 푼 없다는 것도—문 앞에 빚쟁이들이 진을 치고 있고, 상인들을 어르고 구슬려야 하며, 한마디로 발붙일 땅조차 없다는 것도 잊어버리곤 했다. 그래서 마차를, 그러니까 그 가족용 마차를 타고 궁정으로 가면서, 그녀는 어찌나 거창하고 흡족하고 신중하고 위풍당당한 태도를 취했던지 제인 부인마저도 웃음을 터뜨릴 지경이었다. 그녀는 황후에게나 어울릴 법한 도도한 고갯짓으로 왕실 거실에 걸어 들어갔으니, 만약 그녀가 정말 황후였더라면 그 배역을 완벽하게 소화해 냈으리라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없다.\n\n로던 크롤리 부인이 군주를 알현하던 그날 차려입은 궁정 예복은 더없이 우아하고 눈부신 것이었음을 우리는 자신 있게 밝힐 수 있다. 별 모양 훈장과 띠를 두르고 세인트 제임스 궁의 모임에 참석하는 우리, 혹은 진흙투성이 장화를 신고 펠멜 거리를 어슬렁거리며 깃 장식을 단 귀인들을 태우고 다가오는 마차 안을 기웃거리는 우리라면, 알현일 오전 두 시쯤에 더러 멋쟁이 숙녀들을 본 적이 있을 것이다. 그 시각이면 끈으로 장식한 제복 차림의 근위 기병대 악단이 껑충대는 음악 의자 같은 크림색 군마에 올라앉아 개선 행진곡을 불어 대는데, 그 한낮 이른 시각에 그 숙녀들은 결코 사랑스럽거나 매혹적인 대상이 아니었다. 예순 살 먹은 뚱뚱한 백작 부인이 가슴을 깊이 드러내고, 분칠을 하고, 처진 눈꺼풀까지 연지가 주름 속에 끼어 있고, 가발에는 다이아몬드가 반짝이는 모습이란, 보기에 유익하고 교화가 될지언정 결코 즐거운 광경은 아니다. 그 모습은 세인트 제임스 거리의 불빛이 이른 아침에 절반쯤 꺼지고 나머지는 마치 새벽 앞에서 유령처럼 사라지려는 듯 희미하게 깜박이는 그 시들한 꼴과도 같다. 부인의 마차가 지나갈 때 우리가 언뜻 보게 되는 그런 매력이란 오직 밤에만 바깥에 나타나야 마땅한 것이다. 신시아조차도, 우리가 올겨울 철에 이따금 보듯, 하늘 맞은편에서 포이보스가 뚫어지게 쏘아보아 무안하게 만들 때면 오후엔 초췌해 보이거늘, 하물며 늙은 캐슬몰디 부인이 마차 창으로 햇빛이 정통으로 쏟아져 들어 세월이 그 얼굴에 새겨 놓은 온갖 금과 틈을 죄다 드러낼 때 어찌 고개를 들고 있을 수 있겠는가! 안 될 일이다. 알현은 십일월이나 첫 안개 끼는 날로 잡아야 하든가, 아니면 우리 허영의 시장의 늙은 여왕님들이 닫힌 가마를 타고 와서 가린 통로로 내려, 등불의 비호 아래 군주께 무릎을 굽혀 인사를 올려야 할 것이다.\n\n그러나 우리의 사랑스러운 레베카에게는 그 미모를 돋보이게 할 그런 다정한 후광 따위가 전혀 필요치 않았다. 그녀의 살결은 아직 어떤 햇살도 견딜 수 있었고, 그녀의 옷은—만약 지금 그것을 본다면 오늘날 허영의 시장의 어느 숙녀라도 여태 입어 본 가장 어리석고 터무니없는 차림새라고 단언하겠지만—이십오 년쯤 전 당시에는 그녀의 눈에도 세상 사람들의 눈에도, 이번 철 가장 이름난 미인의 가장 눈부신 의상만큼이나 멋들어진 것이었다. 이십 년쯤 지나면 그 의상 역시, 그 모자 가게 장인의 걸작 역시 이전의 모든 허영과 더불어 우스꽝스러움의 영역으로 넘어가 있을 것이다. 그러나 우리는 너무 곁길로 새고 말았다. 그녀가 알현하던 그 뜻깊은 날, 로던 부인의 옷차림은 매혹적이라는 평을 들었다. 마음씨 고운 자그마한 제인 부인조차도 그 친척을 바라보며 그 효과를 인정하지 않을 수 없었고, 자기는 베키 부인에 비하면 취향이 한참 떨어진다고 서글피 속으로 시인했다.\n\n제인 부인은 로던 부인이 그 옷에 얼마나 많은 정성과 궁리와 재간을 쏟았는지 알지 못했다. 레베카는 유럽의 어느 모자 가게 장인 못지않은 안목을 지녔고, 일을 처리하는 솜씨가 어찌나 영리했던지 제인 부인으로서는 도무지 짐작도 못 할 정도였다. 제인 부인은 곧 베키의 옷자락을 이룬 브로케이드의 화려함과 옷에 달린 레이스의 눈부심을 알아챘다.\n\n브로케이드는 오래된 자투리 천이라고 베키는 말했다. 레이스로 말하자면 아주 싸게 산 것이라고 했다. 백 년도 더 전부터 가지고 있던 것이라는 얘기였다.\n\n\"크롤리 부인, 분명 적잖은 값이 나갔을 거예요.\" 제인 부인은 그러면서 자기 레이스를 내려다보았는데, 그것은 베키 것에 비하면 한참 못한 것이었다. 그러고는 로던 부인의 궁정 예복 옷감인 그 오래된 브로케이드의 품질을 살펴보고는, 자기는 그런 고급 옷감은 엄두도 못 낸다고 말하고 싶은 마음이 들었으나, 친척에게 야박한 말이라 여겨 애써 그 말을 삼갔다.\n\n그러나 만약 제인 부인이 전말을 다 알았더라면, 그토록 인정 많은 성품마저도 무너지고 말았으리라 싶다. 사실인즉, 로던 부인은 피트 경의 집을 정돈하던 중에 그 집안의 옛 안주인들 소유였던 레이스와 브로케이드를 낡은 옷장에서 발견하고는, 슬그머니 그 물건들을 집으로 가져와 자기의 자그마한 몸에 맞게 손질한 것이었다. 브리그스는 그녀가 그것을 가져가는 것을 보았으나 아무것도 묻지 않았고 어디에도 발설하지 않았다. 다만 나는 브리그스가 이 문제에서 그녀에게 깊이 공감했으리라 믿으며, 정직한 다른 많은 여인들 또한 그러했을 것이다.\n\n그리고 다이아몬드는—\"대체 그 다이아몬드는 어디서 났소, 베키?\" 남편이 물었다. 그는 여태 한 번도 본 적 없는, 그녀의 귀와 목에서 눈부시고도 풍성하게 반짝이는 그 보석들을 감탄하며 바라보았다.\n\n베키는 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 잠시 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다. 피트 크롤리도 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 창밖으로 시선을 돌렸다. 사실인즉, 그 보석들 가운데 아주 일부를 그가 그녀에게 준 것이었다. 그녀가 두른 진주 목걸이를 여미는 예쁜 다이아몬드 잠금쇠 말이다—그런데 준남작은 그 사정을 자기 부인에게 미처 말해 두지 않았던 것이다.\n\n베키는 남편을 보고, 다음엔 피트 경을 보았다. 마치 \"제가 비밀을 폭로해 버릴까요?\" 하고 말하는 듯, 짓궂게 의기양양한 기색을 띤 채로.\n\n\"맞혀 보세요!\" 그녀가 남편에게 말했다. \"어머, 이 어리석은 양반아.\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"이걸 대체 어디서 났겠어요?—오래전에 어느 정다운 친구가 준 작은 잠금쇠 하나만 빼고 말이에요. 빌린 거랍니다, 그럼요. 코번트리 거리의 폴로니어스 씨 가게에서 빌렸어요. 궁정에 드나드는 다이아몬드가 죄다 그걸 두른 사람 것이라고 생각하시면 안 돼요. 제인 부인이 두른 저 아름다운 보석들처럼요. 저 보석들은 제가 가진 그 어떤 것보다도 훨씬 멋지답니다, 틀림없이요.\"\n\n\"저건 집안 대대로 내려온 보석이라오.\" 피트 경이 또다시 거북한 기색으로 말했다. 이렇게 집안끼리 담소를 나누는 사이에 마차는 거리를 굴러 내려갔고, 마침내 그 짐을 군주가 위풍당당하게 앉아 계신 궁전 문 앞에 부려 놓았다.\n\n로던의 감탄을 자아냈던 그 다이아몬드는 코번트리 거리의 폴로니어스 씨에게 결코 되돌아가지 않았고, 그 신사 또한 반환을 요구하는 일이 없었다. 다이아몬드는 애밀리아 세들리가 오래오래전에 그녀에게 준 낡은 책상 안 자그마한 비밀 보관함으로 들어갔는데, 베키는 거기에 쓸모 있고, 어쩌면 값나가기도 할 물건들을 잔뜩 간직해 두었으니, 남편은 그에 대해 아무것도 몰랐다. 아무것도, 혹은 거의 아무것도 모르는 것이 어떤 남편들의 천성인 것이다. 그리고 감추는 것은 얼마나 많은 여인들의 천성이던가? 오, 숙녀 여러분! 여러분 가운데 몇이나 몰래 쌓인 모자 가게 청구서를 두고 있는지요? 몇이나 차마 내보이지 못하거나, 입을 때마다 벌벌 떨게 되는 가운과 팔찌를 두고 있는지요?—벌벌 떨면서, 곁에 앉은 남편에게 미소로 알랑거리지요. 새 벨벳 가운을 헌 것과 구분하지도, 새 팔찌를 작년 것과 구별하지도 못하며, 그 너덜너덜해 보이는 노란 레이스 스카프가 사십 기니나 나갔고 보비노 부인이 그 돈을 받겠다고 매주 독촉 편지를 보내고 있다는 것은 짐작도 못 하는 그 남편에게 말이에요!\n\n이리하여 로던은 자기 부인의 고운 가슴을 장식한 눈부신 다이아몬드 귀고리며 화려한 보석 장신구에 대해 아무것도 몰랐다. 그러나 화장방 시종장으로서 궁정의 제자리를 지키고 있던 스타인 경, 영국 왕좌의 고관대작이요 빛나는 수호자 가운데 한 사람이며, 온갖 별 모양 훈장과 가터와 목걸이와 띠를 두르고 나와서 그 자그마한 여인에게 각별한 관심을 보인 그 사람은, 그 보석들이 어디서 났는지, 또 누가 그 값을 치렀는지를 알고 있었다.\n\n그는 그녀에게 허리를 굽히며 미소 짓고는, 「머리타래의 강탈」에서 벨린다의 다이아몬드를 두고 읊은, 진부하나 아름다운 그 시구를 인용했다. \"유대인도 입 맞추려 들고 이교도도 흠모하리라.\"\n\n\"하지만 각하께선 정통 신자이시기를 바라요.\" 그 자그마한 숙녀가 고갯짓을 하며 말했다. 그리고 그 거물 귀족이 그 자그마한 모험가에게 얼마나 두드러진 관심을 쏟는지 본 주변의 숱한 숙녀들이 수군거리며 이야기를 나누었고, 숱한 신사들이 고개를 끄덕이며 귀엣말을 주고받았다.\n\n처녀 적 성이 샤프였던 레베카 크롤리와 그 황제 같은 군주 사이의 알현이 어떠한 정황이었는지는, 나처럼 미약하고 미숙한 붓으로 감히 옮기려 들 일이 못 된다. 그 장엄한 관념 앞에서는 눈이 부셔 절로 감기는 법이다. 충성스러운 경의와 예의가, 상상력에게마저도 그 신성한 알현실을 너무 날카롭고 대담하게 휘둘러보지 말고, 그 장엄한 어전에서 깊이 허리 굽혀 절을 올리며 재빠르고 조용하고 공손하게 뒷걸음질로 물러나라 이르는 것이다.\n\n다만 이 정도는 말할 수 있겠다. 이 알현을 마친 뒤 온 런던을 통틀어 베키보다 더 충성스러운 마음을 지닌 이는 없었다는 것을 말이다. 그녀의 입에는 늘 임금의 이름이 오르내렸고, 그녀는 그분을 세상에서 가장 매력적인 분이라 떠받들었다. 그녀는 콜나기 가게로 가서 예술이 빚어낼 수 있고 외상이 허락하는 한도 안에서 가장 빼어난 그분의 초상화를 주문했다. 그녀가 고른 것은 저 유명한 초상, 곧 그 더없이 훌륭한 군주께서 모피 깃 달린 프록코트에 반바지와 비단 양말 차림으로, 곱슬거리는 갈색 가발 아래로 소파에 기댄 채 점잔 빼며 웃고 있는 그림이었다. 그녀는 그분을 브로치에 그려 넣게 하여 달고 다녔다—실로 그녀는 그분의 정중함과 미모에 대해 끊임없이 떠벌려 대며 지인들을 즐겁게도, 다소 성가시게도 했다. 누가 알랴! 어쩌면 이 자그마한 여인은 자기가 맹트농이나 퐁파두르 같은 역할을 해낼 수 있으리라 여겼는지도 모를 일이다.\n\n그러나 그녀가 알현을 마친 뒤로 무엇보다 가장 큰 구경거리는 그녀가 정숙한 척 떠들어 대는 소리를 듣는 일이었다. 그녀에게는 몇몇 여자 지인이 있었는데, 인정하건대 허영의 시장에서 그리 드높은 평판을 지닌 부류는 아니었다. 그러나 말하자면 정숙한 여인으로 거듭난 마당에 베키는 더 이상 이 미심쩍은 여인들과 어울리려 들지 않았다. 그래서 크래컨베리 부인이 자기 오페라 특별석에서 고개를 끄덕여 인사하자 못 본 척했고, 하이드 파크의 승마길에서 워싱턴 화이트 부인을 모른 척 지나쳐 버렸다. \"이봐요, 사람은 자기가 어엿한 신분이라는 걸 보여야 하는 법이에요.\" 그녀는 말했다. \"미심쩍은 사람들과 함께 있는 모습을 보여선 안 돼요. 크래컨베리 부인은 진심으로 가엾게 여겨요. 워싱턴 화이트 부인도 어쩌면 아주 좋은 사람일지 모르고요. 당신이야 카드놀이가 좋으면 가서 그들과 식사를 해도 좋아요. 하지만 나는 안 돼요, 절대 안 할 거예요. 그러니 둘 중 누가 찾아오거든 내가 집에 없다고 하라고 스미스에게 일러 줘요.\"\n\n베키의 옷차림은 세세한 부분까지 신문에 실렸다—깃 장식이며, 늘어뜨린 장식 천이며, 화려한 다이아몬드며, 그 밖의 모든 것이. 크래컨베리 부인은 그 기사를 비통한 심정으로 읽고서 자기 추종자들에게 저 여자가 부리는 도도한 거드름에 대해 늘어놓았다. 시골의 뷰트 크롤리 부인과 그 딸들도 도시에서 보내온 「모닝 포스트」 한 부를 받아 보고는 의분을 터뜨렸다. \"네가 만약 모래빛 머리에 초록 눈을 했고 프랑스 줄타기 광대의 딸로 태어났더라면,\" 뷰트 부인이 맏딸에게 말했는데(이 딸은 그와는 정반대로 살갗이 거뭇하고 키가 작고 들창코였다), \"암, 너도 화려한 다이아몬드쯤은 두를 수 있었을 테고, 네 사촌 제인 부인을 따라 궁정에 알현하러 갔을지도 모르지. 하지만 가엾은 우리 딸아, 너는 그저 양가의 규수일 뿐이란다. 네 핏줄에는 영국에서 가장 고귀한 피가 흐를 뿐이고, 곧은 도리와 신앙심이 네 몫이지. 준남작 동생의 아내인 나조차도 궁정에 간다는 따위는 꿈도 꿔 본 적이 없단다—만약 어진 샬럿 왕비가 살아 계셨더라면 다른 사람들도 그랬을 게야.\" 이런 식으로 그 갸륵한 목사 부인은 스스로를 위로했고, 딸들은 한숨을 내쉬며 밤새도록 「귀족 명감」을 들여다보고 앉아 있었다.\n\n그 이름난 알현이 있고 며칠 뒤, 정숙한 베키에게 또 한 번 크나큰 영광이 베풀어졌다. 스타인 부인의 마차가 로던 크롤리 씨의 집 문 앞으로 다가왔는데, 하인은 그 어마어마한 문 두드리는 소리로 미루어 집을 통째로 부숴 버릴 기세였으나, 마음을 누그러뜨려 그저 명함 두 장을 안에 들여보냈을 뿐이었다. 거기에는 스타인 후작 부인과 곤트 백작 부인의 이름이 새겨져 있었다. 만약 이 마분지 조각들이 아름다운 그림이었거나, 그 두 배의 기니에 맞먹는 백 야드짜리 말린 레이스로 감겨 있었더라도, 베키가 이보다 더 큰 기쁨으로 그것을 바라보지는 못했을 것이다. 짐작하다시피 그 명함들은 베키가 방문객들의 명함을 보관해 두는 응접실 탁자 위의 도자기 그릇 안에서 가장 눈에 띄는 자리를 차지했다. 아이고, 세상에! 가엾은 워싱턴 화이트 부인의 명함이며 크래컨베리 부인의 명함이—몇 달 전만 해도 우리의 자그마한 친구가 받고서 그리도 기뻐했고, 한때는 그 어리석은 자그마한 여인이 제법 우쭐대기까지 했던 그 명함들이—아이고, 세상에, 다시 말하지만, 이 거창한 궁정 카드들이 나타나자마자 그 가엾고 자그마한 찬밥 신세의 끗수 낮은 패들이 어찌나 순식간에 카드 뭉치 맨 밑바닥으로 가라앉아 버렸던지! 스타인! 베어에이커스, 헬벨린의 존스 가문! 그리고 카멜롯의 캐릴런 가문이라니! 분명 베키와 브리그스는 「귀족 명감」에서 그 장엄한 이름들을 찾아내어, 가문의 나뭇가지를 모든 갈래까지 거슬러 올라가며 그 고귀한 혈통을 좇아 보았으리라.\n\n두어 시간 뒤에 스타인 경이 찾아와서는, 늘 그러듯 주위를 둘러보고 모든 것을 눈여겨보다가, 자기 집 부인들의 명함이 이미 베키가 쥔 패의 으뜸패처럼 가지런히 놓여 있는 것을 발견하고는, 인간의 나약함이 순진하게 드러나는 광경을 볼 때마다 늘 그러듯 이 늙은 냉소가는 히죽 웃었다. 베키는 이내 그에게로 내려왔다. 이 다정한 아가씨는 각하가 오시리라 예상될 때면 으레 몸단장을 갖추어 두었으니, 머리는 흐트러짐 없이 가지런하고, 손수건이며 앞치마며 스카프며 자그마한 모로코가죽 슬리퍼며 그 밖의 여성용 자질구레한 장식들이 정돈되어 있었으며, 그를 맞이할 채비를 갖춘 채 무심한 듯 우아한 자세로 앉아 있는 것이었다—물론 불시에 놀랄 때면 자기 방으로 날듯이 달려가 거울로 매무새를 후딱 살피고는 다시 종종걸음으로 내려와 그 거물 귀족을 모셨다.\n\n그녀는 그가 도자기 그릇을 들여다보며 히죽거리고 있는 것을 발견했다. 들킨 셈이라 그녀는 살짝 얼굴을 붉혔다. \"고맙습니다, 각하.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"보시다시피 부인들께서 다녀가셨어요. 어찌나 친절하신지요! 진작 내려오질 못했어요—부엌에서 푸딩을 만들고 있었거든요.\"\n\n\"알고 있소, 마차를 타고 오면서 지하 부엌 난간 틈으로 당신을 보았으니.\" 그 노신사가 대꾸했다.\n\n\"각하께선 뭐든 다 보시는군요.\" 그녀가 받아넘겼다.\n\n\"몇 가지는 보지만, 그건 못 봤소, 어여쁜 부인.\" 그가 너그럽게 말했다. \"이 어리석은 자그마한 거짓말쟁이 같으니! 나는 당신이 위층 방에 있는 소리를 들었소. 거기서 분명 연지를 좀 바르고 있었을 테지—그 연지를 곤트 부인한테 좀 나눠 주구려. 그 사람 낯빛은 정말 영 형편없으니 말이오—그러고는 침실 문이 열리는 소리가 났고, 이내 당신이 아래층으로 내려왔지.\"\n\n\"각하께서 여기 오시는데 가장 곱게 보이려 애쓰는 게 죄인가요?\" 로던 부인이 가련한 목소리로 대꾸하며, 자기 경우엔 연지라곤 전혀 없고 오로지 진짜 홍조와 수줍음뿐이라는 것을 보이려는 듯 손수건으로 뺨을 문질렀다. 이것에 대해서야 누가 알 수 있으랴? 손수건에 묻어나지 않는 연지도 있고, 어찌나 좋은지 눈물로도 흐트러지지 않는 연지도 있다는 것을 나는 안다.\n\n\"흠.\" 그 노신사가 자기 아내의 명함을 빙글빙글 돌리며 말했다. \"당신은 기어이 귀부인이 되겠다는 작정이로군. 나를 사교계에 끼워 넣어 달라고 이 가엾고 늙은 목숨을 들들 볶아 대니 말이오. 거기서 당신은 제 몫을 지켜 내지 못할 거요, 이 어리석은 자그마한 바보 같으니. 당신한텐 돈이 없잖소.\"\n\n\"각하께서 우릴 위해 자리 하나 마련해 주실 거잖아요.\" 베키가 끼어들었다. \"되도록 빨리요.\"\n\n\"당신한테는 돈이 없는데, 돈 가진 사람들과 겨루겠다는 거요. 이 가엾은 자그마한 질그릇 단지 같으니, 거대한 구리 솥들과 함께 물살을 타고 떠내려가겠다는 거지. 여자들은 죄다 똑같다니까. 다들 가질 가치도 없는 것을 손에 넣겠다고 안달이지! 원, 어제는 임금님과 식사를 했는데 양고기 목살에 순무가 나왔소. 살진 소를 잡아 차린 잔칫상보다 푸성귀 한 접시가 더 나을 때가 허다하단 말이지. 당신은 곤트 하우스에 가게 될 거요. 거기 가기 전엔 이 늙은이를 도무지 가만 놔두질 않을 테니. 거기는 여기만큼 절반도 좋지 않소. 당신은 거기서 따분해질 거요. 나도 따분하니까. 내 아내는 맥베스 부인만큼이나 명랑하고, 내 딸들은 리건과 거너릴만큼이나 쾌활하지. 나는 내 침실이라고들 부르는 데서는 감히 잠도 못 잔다오. 침대는 성 베드로 대성당의 닫집 같고, 그림들은 나를 겁에 질리게 하니 말이오. 나는 화장실에 자그마한 놋쇠 침대 하나와 은수자 같은 자그마한 털 매트리스 하나를 두고 지낸다오. 나는 은수자라 그 말이지. 호! 호! 당신은 다음 주에 만찬 초대를 받을 거요. 그러니 여자들을 조심하시오, 단단히 정신 차리고 제 몫을 지켜요! 그 여자들이 당신을 얼마나 못살게 굴는지!\" 이것은 스타인 경처럼 말수 적은 사람에게는 무척이나 긴 연설이었다. 게다가 그날 그가 베키를 위해 늘어놓은 첫 연설도 아니었다.\n\n브리그스는 저쪽 방에서 자기가 앉아 있던 작업대에서 고개를 들었다가, 그 거물 후작이 자기네 여성을 그토록 가벼이 말하는 것을 듣고는 깊은 한숨을 내쉬었다.\n\n\"저 가증스러운 양치기 개를 내쫓지 않으면,\" 스타인 경이 어깨너머로 그녀를 흉포하게 노려보며 말했다. \"독을 먹여 죽여 버리겠소.\"\n\n\"저는 늘 제 접시에 든 걸로 우리 개한테 밥을 준답니다.\" 레베카가 짓궂게 웃으며 말했다. 그러고는 어여쁜 대령 부인과의 단둘이만의 자리를 방해한다는 이유로 가엾은 브리그스를 미워하는 각하의 낭패한 꼴을 한동안 즐긴 뒤, 마침내 로던 부인은 자기 숭배자에게 인정을 베풀어, 브리그스를 불러서는 날씨가 참 좋다고 추켜세우며 아이를 데리고 산책이나 다녀오라고 일렀다.\n\n\"저 사람을 내보낼 수가 없어요.\" 베키가 잠시 뜸을 들였다가, 무척 서글픈 목소리로 말했다. 그렇게 말하면서 그녀의 눈에는 눈물이 그렁그렁 차올랐고, 그녀는 고개를 돌려 외면했다.\n\n\"저 여자한테 품삯을 빚지고 있나 보군?\" 그 귀족이 말했다.\n\n\"그보다 더 나빠요.\" 베키가 여전히 눈을 내리깐 채 말했다. \"제가 저 사람을 파산시켰는걸요.\"\n\n\"파산시켰다고? 그렇다면 왜 내쫓지 않는 거요?\" 그 신사가 물었다.\n\n\"남자들이나 그러지요.\" 베키가 씁쓸하게 대답했다. \"여자들은 당신네만큼 모질지 않답니다. 작년에 우리가 마지막 한 기니까지 거덜 났을 때, 저 사람이 우리한테 가진 걸 죄다 내주었어요. 우리 형편이 아주 거덜 날 때까지는—그런데 그날이 멀지 않은 것 같지만요—아니면 제가 저 사람한테 마지막 한 푼까지 갚을 수 있을 때까지는, 저 사람은 결코 제 곁을 떠나지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"빌어먹을, 대체 얼마요?\" 그 귀족이 욕설을 내뱉으며 말했다. 그러자 베키는 그의 재력이 어마어마하다는 데에 생각이 미쳐, 브리그스 양에게서 빌린 금액뿐 아니라 그 거의 곱절에 달하는 액수를 일러 주었다.\n\n이 말에 스타인 경은 또 한 번 짧고도 격렬한 분노의 말을 터뜨렸고, 그러자 레베카는 고개를 더욱 푹 숙이며 비통하게 울었다. \"어쩔 도리가 없었어요. 그게 제 유일한 기회였는걸요. 감히 남편한테는 말도 못 해요. 제가 무슨 짓을 했는지 알면 그이는 절 죽이려 들 거예요. 각하 말고는 누구한테도 비밀로 해 왔어요—그런데 각하께서 억지로 캐내신 거고요. 아, 어쩌면 좋아요, 스타인 경? 저는 정말, 정말 불행한걸요!\"\n\n스타인 경은 손가락으로 악마의 북소리를 두드려 대고 손톱을 깨물 뿐 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다. 마침내 그는 모자를 머리에 탁 눌러쓰고는 방에서 휙 나가 버렸다. 레베카는 문이 그의 등 뒤로 쾅 닫히고 그의 마차가 휭 하니 떠나가 버릴 때까지 그 비탄에 잠긴 자세에서 일어나지 않았다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 더없이 기묘한, 의기양양하고 짓궂은 기색이 그 초록 눈에 반짝이는 채로 몸을 일으켰다. 그녀는 일감 앞에 앉아 한두 번 혼자 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 피아노 앞에 앉아 건반 위로 의기양양한 즉흥곡을 빠르게 두드려 댔으니, 그 눈부신 연주에 사람들이 그녀의 창문 아래 걸음을 멈추고 귀를 기울일 정도였다.\n\n그날 밤, 곤트 하우스에서 그 자그마한 여인 앞으로 쪽지 두 통이 왔다. 하나에는 다음 금요일 곤트 하우스에서 열리는 만찬에 스타인 경 부부가 초대한다는 초청장이 들어 있었고, 다른 하나에는 스타인 경의 서명과 롬바드 거리의 존스 브라운 로빈슨 상회 주소가 적힌 회색 종잇조각 한 장이 들어 있었다.\n\n로던은 그날 밤 베키가 한두 번 웃는 소리를 들었다. 자기를 그렇게 웃게 만든 건 그저 곤트 하우스에 가서 거기 숙녀들과 맞설 생각에 신이 나서 그런 것뿐이라고 그녀는 말했다. 그러나 사실인즉 그녀는 다른 숱한 생각들에 골몰해 있었던 것이다. 늙은 브리그스에게 빚을 갚고 떠나라고 해 버릴까? 래글스에게 외상값을 청산해 주어 깜짝 놀라게 해 줄까? 그녀는 베개를 베고 이 온갖 생각들을 이리저리 굴려 보았고, 이튿날 로던이 아침에 클럽으로 인사차 나가자, 크롤리 부인은(수수한 옷차림에 베일을 쓴 채) 삯마차를 타고 부리나케 도심으로 달려갔다. 그러고는 존스 로빈슨 은행 앞에 내려 창구의 담당자에게 서류 한 장을 내밀자, 그가 그 답으로 \"어떻게 받으시겠습니까?\" 하고 물었다.\n\n그녀는 \"백오십 파운드는 작은 지폐로 받고, 나머지는 한 장짜리 지폐로 받겠어요\" 하고 상냥하게 말했다. 그러고는 세인트 폴 대성당 묘지를 지나는 길에 그곳에 들러 돈으로 살 수 있는 가장 멋진 검은 비단 가운을 브리그스에게 줄 것으로 사서는, 입맞춤과 더없이 다정한 말과 함께 그 순박한 늙은 노처녀에게 선사했다.\n\n그런 다음 그녀는 래글스 씨에게 걸어가 그의 아이들 안부를 다정하게 물은 뒤 외상값으로 오십 파운드를 건넸다. 그러고는 자기가 마차를 빌려 쓰던 마차 임대업자에게 가서 그에게도 같은 액수를 쥐여 주어 흡족하게 했다. \"그리고 이게 자네한테 교훈이 되길 바라요, 스페이빈.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"다음 알현일에는 우리 시아주버니 피트 경께서 폐하를 알현하러 가시는데 내 마차가 준비되지 못한 탓에 우리 넷을 그분 마차에 다 태우셔야 하는 폐를 끼치는 일이 없도록 말이에요.\" 지난번 알현일에 의견 충돌이 있었던 모양이다. 그래서 대령은 삯마차를 타고 군주의 어전에 들어가야 하는 망신을 하마터면 당할 뻔했던 것이다.\n\n이런 일들을 다 마무리 짓고 나서, 베키는 위층으로 올라가 앞서 말한 그 책상을 찾았다. 애밀리아 세들리가 오래오래전에 그녀에게 준 것으로, 쓸모 있고 값나가는 자그마한 물건들이 잔뜩 들어 있는 그 책상 말이다—그녀는 그 개인 박물관에 존스 로빈슨 상회의 출납계원이 건네준 한 장짜리 지폐를 간직해 두었다.\n\n그날 아침 곤트 저택의 숙녀들이 아침 식사를 들고 있을 때, 스타인 경이(그는 초콜릿을 혼자 들었고 집안의 여자들을 거의 방해하는 법이 없었으며, 공식 행사가 있는 날이나 복도에서 마주칠 때, 혹은 오페라 극장의 일층 특별석에서 그랜드 티어에 자리한 그녀들의 박스석을 바라볼 때가 아니면 좀처럼 그들을 보지 않았다) — 그러니까 그분이, 차와 토스트를 앞에 두고 모여 앉은 숙녀들과 아이들 틈에 모습을 드러냈고, 레베카를 둘러싸고 한바탕 큰 싸움이 벌어졌다.\n\n\"스타인 부인.\" 그가 말했다. \"금요일 만찬의 손님 명단을 보고 싶소. 그리고 괜찮다면 크롤리 대령 부부 앞으로 초대장을 한 장 써 주었으면 하오.\"\n\n\"그건 블랜치가 씁니다.\" 스타인 부인이 안절부절못하며 말했다. \"곤트 부인이 쓰지요.\"\n\n\"저는 그런 사람한테는 쓰지 않겠어요.\" 곤트 부인이 말했다. 큰 키에 위엄 있는 부인이었는데, 그렇게 말하고는 잠깐 눈을 들었다가 이내 다시 내리깔았다. 스타인 경의 비위를 거스른 자들로서는 그의 눈을 마주하는 것이 결코 이로운 일이 아니었다.\n\n\"애들을 방에서 내보내. 나가!\" 그가 종 줄을 잡아당기며 말했다. 그 앞에서는 늘 겁에 질려 있던 어린것들이 물러갔다. 아이들의 어머니도 따라 나가려 했다. \"당신은 아니오.\" 그가 말했다. \"당신은 남아.\"\n\n\"스타인 부인.\" 그가 말했다. \"한 번 더 부탁하지. 책상으로 가서 금요일 만찬 초대장을 써 주는 친절을 베풀어 주겠소?\"\n\n\"각하, 저는 그 자리에 참석하지 않겠어요.\" 곤트 부인이 말했다. \"친정으로 가겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그래 주면 좋겠군, 가서 거기 눌러앉아 있어. 베리에이커스의 집달리들이 아주 유쾌한 길동무가 되어 줄 게야. 나는 자네 친정 식구들에게 돈을 꿔 주는 일에서도, 자네의 그 빌어먹을 비극 배우 같은 거드름에서도 풀려나겠지. 자네가 대체 누구라고 여기서 명령을 내려? 돈도 없잖아. 머리도 텅 비었고. 자네는 아이를 낳으라고 여기 들어온 건데, 하나도 낳지 못했어. 곤트는 자네라면 진절머리를 내고, 이 집안에서 자네가 죽기를 바라지 않는 사람이라곤 조지의 아내뿐이지. 자네만 죽으면 곤트는 다시 장가들 텐데.\"\n\n\"저도 죽었으면 좋겠어요.\" 부인이 눈에 눈물과 분노를 가득 담은 채 대답했다.\n\n\"새삼스레, 자네는 정숙한 척 거드름을 피워야 직성이 풀리는 모양이지. 누구나 알다시피 티 없는 성녀요 평생 그른 짓이라곤 한 적 없는 내 아내는 내 젊은 벗 크롤리 부인을 만나는 데 아무 거리낌이 없는데 말이야. 스타인 부인은 알지, 때로는 가장 훌륭한 여인일수록 겉모양이 불리하게 비치고, 가장 순결한 여인일수록 거짓말이 따라붙는다는 것을. 부인, 이왕이면 자네 어머니 베리에이커스 부인에 관한 자그마한 일화들을 몇 가지 들려줄까?\"\n\n\"때리고 싶으시면 때리세요, 각하. 어떤 모진 매질이라도 하셔도 좋아요.\" 곤트 부인이 말했다. 아내와 며느리가 괴로워하는 꼴을 보는 것은 늘 각하의 기분을 흐뭇하게 했다.\n\n\"내 어여쁜 블랜치.\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 신사라서 여자에게 손찌검을 하는 법이 없지, 친절을 베푸는 경우라면 모를까. 그저 자네 성품의 자그마한 흠들을 바로잡아 주고 싶을 따름이야. 자네들 여자들은 너무 거만해, 그리고 안타깝게도 겸손이 부족하지. 몰 신부님이 여기 계셨다면 분명 스타인 부인에게도 그렇게 일러 주셨을 게야. 거드름을 피워선 안 되지. 온순하고 겸허해야 해, 내 사랑스러운 사람들. 스타인 부인이 다 알 수야 없지만, 저 모함받는 순박하고 싹싹한 크롤리 부인은 어쩌면 아주 결백한 사람일지도 몰라 — 부인 자신보다 더 결백할지도 모르지. 그 남편의 평판이야 좋지 않지만, 자네가 받았던 단 하나의 유산을 사기로 가로채고 자네를 빈털터리로 만들어 내 손에 떠넘긴 베리에이커스 정도는 되지. 노름판에 좀 끼어들었다가 빚은 거의 갚지 않은 그 작자 말이야. 그리고 크롤리 부인의 가문이야 그리 대단치 않지만, 패니의 그 저명한 조상이신 초대 들라존 경보다 못할 것도 없지.\"\n\n\"제가 이 집안에 가지고 들어온 돈으로 말씀드리면, 각하.\" 조지 부인이 소리쳤다 —\n\n\"자네는 그걸로 불확실한 상속 차례나 사들인 셈이지.\" 후작이 음울하게 말했다. \"곤트가 죽으면 자네 남편이 그 작위를 물려받을지도 모르고, 자네 어린 아들들이 그것을 이어받을지도 모르지. 그 밖에 또 무엇이 있을지 누가 알겠나? 그러니 그동안에는, 부인들, 바깥에서야 마음껏 거만하고 정숙하게 굴어도 좋지만, 내게는 거드름 부리지 마. 크롤리 부인의 평판으로 말할 것 같으면, 그 변호가 필요하다는 암시조차 입에 담아 나 자신이나 그 더없이 티 없고 흠잡을 데 없는 부인을 욕보일 생각은 없네. 자네들은 그 부인을 더없이 살갑게 맞아 주는 것이 좋을 게야. 내가 이 집에 들이는 모든 사람을 그렇게 맞아 주듯이 말이지. 이 집이라고?\" 그가 웃음을 터뜨리며 말했다. \"이 집의 주인이 누구지? 이 집이 무엇인가? 이 미덕의 전당은 내 것이야. 그러니 내가 뉴게이트 감옥 전체나 베들럼 정신병원 전체를 이리로 부른다 해도, 빌어먹을, 그들은 환영받을 게야.\"\n\n집안에 불복종의 기미가 보일 때마다 스타인 경이 자신의 \"하렘\"에게 으레 베풀곤 하던 이런 종류의 일장 연설을 한 차례 듣고 나자, 풀이 죽은 여인들에게는 그저 순종하는 것 말고는 도리가 없었다. 곤트 부인은 각하가 요구한 초대장을 썼고, 시어머니와 함께 쓰라리고 모욕당한 마음을 안은 채 몸소 마차를 몰아 로던 부인의 집에 명함을 두고 왔다. 그리고 그 명함을 받아 든 일은 그 순진한 부인에게 더없는 기쁨을 안겨 주었다.\n\n런던에는 그 지체 높은 숙녀들의 손에서 그런 영예를 받기 위해서라면 일 년치 수입이라도 기꺼이 바쳤을 집안들이 있었다. 이를테면 프레더릭 불록 부인은, 만약 스타인 부인과 곤트 부인이 시티에서 기다리고 있다가 그녀를 일으켜 세우며 \"다음 금요일에 우리 집으로 오세요\"라고 말해 준다면, 메이페어에서 롬바드 거리까지 무릎으로 기어서라도 갔을 것이다 — 그것도 누구나 가는 곤트 저택의 그 떠들썩한 대연회나 성대한 무도회가 아니라, 그 신성하고 범접할 수 없으며 신비롭고 황홀한 연회, 그중 하나에 끼는 것만으로도 하나의 특권이요 영예요 참으로 축복이라 할 만한 그런 자리에 말이다.\n\n엄격하고, 티 없고, 아름다운 곤트 부인은 허영의 시장에서 그야말로 최고의 지위를 차지하고 있었다. 스타인 경이 그녀를 대하는 그 빼어난 예의는 그의 처신을 지켜본 모든 이를 매료시켰고, 가장 신랄한 비평가들마저 그가 얼마나 완벽한 신사인지 인정하게 했으며, 적어도 각하의 마음만큼은 제자리에 똑바로 놓여 있다는 것을 시인하게 만들었다.\n\n곤트 저택의 숙녀들은 공동의 적을 물리치기 위해 베리에이커스 부인을 원군으로 불러들였다. 곤트 부인의 마차 한 대가 그 어머니를 모시러 힐 거리로 갔다. 부인의 마차란 마차는 죄다 집달리들의 손아귀에 들어가 있었고, 그 보석과 의상마저도 저 무자비한 유대인들에게 압류당했다는 소문이었다. 베리에이커스 성도 그들 손에 넘어갔는데, 거기 딸린 값비싼 그림과 가구와 미술 골동품도 모조리 함께였다 — 그 웅장한 반다이크 작품들, 그 고귀한 레이놀즈의 그림들, 야하면서도 아름다워 삼십 년 전만 해도 진짜 천재의 작품 못지않게 귀히 여겨지던 로런스의 초상화들, 그리고 베리에이커스 부인이 젊은 시절 모델로 앉았던 카노바의 비할 데 없는 「춤추는 님프」상까지 — 그 시절 베리에이커스 부인은 눈부셨고, 재산과 지위와 미모로 광채를 발했건만, 이제는 이도 빠지고 머리도 벗어진 노파, 한때의 화려한 예복에서 떨어져 나온 한낱 걸레 조각에 지나지 않았다. 같은 시기에 로런스가 그린 그 남편의 초상화는 베리에이커스 성을 배경으로 군도를 휘두르며 시슬우드 의용기병대 대령 군복을 입은 모습이었지만, 실제로는 외투에 브루투스식 가발을 쓴 시들고 마른 늙은이로, 주로 아침나절이면 그레이스인 법학원 근처를 슬그머니 어슬렁거리고 클럽에서 혼자 식사를 했다. 그는 이제 스타인과 함께 식사하기를 좋아하지 않았다. 젊은 시절에는 둘이 향락의 경주를 함께 벌였고 그때는 베리에이커스가 승자였다. 그러나 스타인이 그보다 끈기가 있어 그를 끝까지 견뎌 냈다. 후작은 이제 1785년의 젊은 곤트 경보다 열 배는 더 대단한 인물이 되었고, 베리에이커스는 그 경주에서 한참 뒤처졌다 — 늙고, 패배하고, 파산하고, 무너져 내렸다. 스타인에게 너무 많은 돈을 빌린 터라 그 옛 동지를 자주 만나는 것이 즐거울 리 없었다. 후작은 흥겹게 굴고 싶을 때면 곤트 부인에게 어째서 아버지가 딸을 보러 오지 않느냐고 조롱하듯 묻곤 했다. \"넉 달째 발길을 끊었구먼.\" 스타인 경은 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"베리에이커스가 다녀가고 나면 나중에 내 수표책을 보면 늘 알 수 있지. 부인들, 이 얼마나 다행스러운 일이오. 나는 내 한 아들의 장인 은행에 돈을 맡기고, 또 다른 아들의 장인은 내 은행에 돈을 맡기니 말이오!\"\n\n베키가 이 첫 사교계 입성에서 만나는 영예를 누린 다른 저명인사들에 관해서는, 지금의 이 사가가 길게 늘어놓는 것이 어울리지 않을 것이다. 페테르바라딘 대공 각하가 대공비와 함께 와 있었다 — 허리를 잔뜩 졸라맨 귀족으로, 떡 벌어진 군인다운 가슴에는 훈장의 패가 찬란하게 빛났고, 목에는 황금양모기사단의 붉은 깃을 두르고 있었다. 그는 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 양 떼의 주인이었다. \"저 얼굴 좀 보세요. 양의 후손이 분명한 것 같아요.\" 베키가 스타인 경에게 속삭였다. 과연 각하의 용모는, 길쭉하고 근엄하며 허연 데다 목에 두른 그 장식까지 더해져, 어딘가 위풍당당한 늙은 길잡이 양을 닮은 데가 있었다.\n\n미국 대사관에 명목상 소속되어 있고 「뉴욕 데마고그」지의 통신원인 존 폴 제퍼슨 존스 씨도 와 있었는데, 그는 좌중의 환심을 사 보겠답시고, 만찬 중 대화가 잠시 끊긴 틈을 타 스타인 부인에게 그의 절친한 벗 조지 곤트가 브라질 생활을 마음에 들어 하더냐고 물었다. 그와 조지는 나폴리에서 더없이 절친한 사이였고 둘이 함께 베수비오 화산에 올랐다는 것이었다. 존스 씨는 그 만찬에 관해 빠짐없이 시시콜콜 적은 기사를 썼고, 그것은 어김없이 「데마고그」지에 실렸다. 그는 손님들의 이름과 작위를 일일이 거론하며 주요 인사들의 약전을 곁들였다. 숙녀들의 자태를 청산유수로 묘사하고, 식탁의 차림새며, 하인들의 키와 복장이며, 차려 나온 요리와 포도주를 낱낱이 열거하고, 찬장의 장식이며, 식기의 짐작 가는 값어치까지 적었다. 그는 이런 만찬이라면 일 인당 십오 달러 내지 십팔 달러는 들었으리라고 어림했다. 그리고 그는 바로 얼마 전까지만 해도, 현 스타인 후작에게 추천장을 들려 피보호자들을 보내곤 했는데, 절친했던 벗 곧 작고한 선대 후작과 가까이 지낸 사이라는 점에 고무되어 그래 온 것이었다. 그는 어리고 보잘것없는 귀족 사우스다운 백작이 식당으로 행진해 들어갈 때 자기 앞자리를 가로챈 데에 몹시 분개했다. \"내가 막 한 발 나서서 더없이 상냥하고 재기 넘치는 사교계의 명사, 저 눈부시고 도도한 로던 크롤리 부인에게 손을 내밀려던 참인데,\" 그는 이렇게 썼다. \"그 어린 귀족이 나와 부인 사이로 끼어들더니 한마디 사과도 없이 내 헬레네를 채 가 버렸다. 나는 하는 수 없이 그 부인의 남편인 대령과 함께 행렬의 후미를 맡아야 했는데, 이 건장하고 불그레한 얼굴의 무인은 워털루에서 공을 세운 자로, 그곳에서 그는 뉴올리언스에서 몇몇 동료 붉은 군복들에게 닥친 것보다 나은 운을 누린 인물이었다.\"\n\n이 점잖은 사교계에 들어선 대령의 얼굴은, 누이의 학교 친구들과 맞닥뜨린 열여섯 살 소년의 얼굴이 그러하듯, 온통 붉게 물들어 있었다. 정직한 로던이 평생 어느 시기에도 숙녀들과 어울리는 일에 그다지 익숙하지 못했다는 것은 앞서 이미 이야기한 바 있다. 클럽이나 장교 식당의 사내들과는 곧잘 어울렸다. 그중 가장 대담한 자들과 어깨를 나란히 하여 말도 타고, 내기도 걸고, 담배도 피우고, 당구도 칠 줄 알았다. 그에게도 여인들과 정을 나누던 시절이 있긴 했지만 그것은 이십 년 전 일이었고, 그 숙녀들이란 저 희극 속 청년 말로가 하드캐슬 양 앞에서 수줍어하기 전에 허물없이 지냈다는 부류의 여인들과 같은 신분의 여인들이었다. 시절이 이러하여, 허영의 시장의 수많은 우리 젊은이들이 날마다 드나드는, 밤마다 카지노와 무도장을 가득 채우는, 하이드 파크의 승마장이나 세인트제임스의 예배 모임만큼이나 분명히 존재한다고 알려진 — 그러나 가장 도덕적이라고는 못 해도 가장 까다로운 사교계가 짐짓 모른 체하기로 작정한 — 그런 부류의 동무들을 입에 올리기조차 좀처럼 엄두 내기 어렵다. 한마디로, 비록 크롤리 대령이 이제 마흔다섯 살이 되었지만, 모범 그 자체인 자기 아내 말고는 변변한 여인을 대여섯 명도 채 만나 보지 못한 것이 그의 인생 행로였다. 그 아내와, 부드러운 천성으로 그를 길들이고 사로잡은 다정한 누이 제인 부인을 빼면, 어떤 여인이든 이 훌륭한 대령을 주눅 들게 했고, 곤트 저택에서의 첫 만찬 자리에서도 그가 입을 떼는 소리라곤 날씨가 무척 덥다고 한 말 한마디뿐이었다. 사실 베키로서는 그를 집에 두고 오고 싶었지만, 사교계에 처음 모습을 드러내는 이 겁 많고 안절부절못하는 가녀린 여인을 지켜 줄 남편이 곁에 있어야 한다는 것이 정숙함의 법도였던 것이다.\n\n그녀가 처음 들어서자 스타인 경이 앞으로 나서서 그녀의 손을 잡고 더없이 정중하게 맞이한 뒤, 스타인 부인과 그 딸인 두 부인에게 그녀를 소개했다. 두 부인은 위엄 있는 절을 세 번 했고, 손위 부인은 과연 새로 온 손님에게 손을 내밀긴 했으나, 그 손은 대리석처럼 차갑고 생기가 없었다.\n\n그래도 베키는 고마운 듯 겸허하게 그 손을 잡고, 으뜸가는 무용 선생도 칭찬할 만한 절을 올리며, 이를테면 스타인 부인의 발치에 자신을 낮추어 이렇게 말했다. 각하께서는 제 아버지의 가장 오래된 벗이요 후원자이셨으며, 자기 베키는 어린 시절부터 스타인 가문을 우러르고 존경하도록 배워 왔노라고. 사실인즉 스타인 경이 일찍이 작고한 샤프에게서 그림 두어 점을 사들인 일이 있었고, 정 많은 그 고아 딸은 그 호의에 대한 고마움을 결코 잊을 수 없었던 것이다.\n\n이어 베리에이커스 부인이 베키의 눈에 들어왔다 — 대령 부인은 그녀에게도 더없이 공손하게 절을 올렸으나, 그 지체 높은 분께서는 서릿발 같은 위엄으로 그것을 받았다.\n\n\"십 년 전 브뤼셀에서 부인을 알게 되는 기쁨을 누렸지요.\" 베키가 더없이 사람의 마음을 끄는 태도로 말했다. \"워털루 전투 전날 밤, 리치먼드 공작부인의 무도회에서 베리에이커스 부인을 뵙는 행운을 누렸답니다. 그리고 부인과 따님이신 블랜치 부인께서 여관 정문 마차 통로에 세워 둔 마차 안에 앉아 말을 기다리시던 모습이 기억나는걸요. 부인의 다이아몬드가 무사하기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n좌중의 모든 눈이 서로 옆 사람의 눈치를 살폈다. 그 유명한 다이아몬드는 보아하니 그 유명한 압류를 당한 모양이었는데, 베키야 물론 그런 일을 까맣게 모르고 있었다. 로던 크롤리는 사우스다운 경과 함께 창가로 물러나 있었는데, 로던이 베리에이커스 부인이 말을 구하지 못해 크롤리 부인에게 \"맹세코, 무릎까지 꿇었다\"는 이야기를 들려주자 사우스다운 경이 주체 못 할 만큼 웃어 대는 소리가 들렸다. \"저 여자라면 무서워할 것 없겠어.\" 베키는 생각했다. 과연 베리에이커스 부인은 딸과 겁에 질린 성난 눈길을 주고받더니, 그림이 놓인 탁자 쪽으로 물러나 무척이나 열심히 그림을 들여다보기 시작했다.\n\n도나우 강가에서 온 그 군주가 모습을 드러내자 대화는 프랑스어로 이어졌고, 베리에이커스 부인과 젊은 숙녀들은 한층 더 분한 노릇으로, 크롤리 부인이 자기들보다 그 언어에 훨씬 능통하며 훨씬 좋은 억양으로 구사한다는 것을 알게 되었다. 베키는 1816년에서 17년 사이 프랑스에 주둔한 군대에서 다른 헝가리 귀족들을 만난 적이 있었다. 그녀는 깊은 관심을 보이며 그 벗들의 안부를 물었다. 그 외국 인사들은 그녀를 지체 높은 귀부인이라 여겼고, 대공과 대공비는 각각 만찬장으로 안내해 준 스타인 경과 후작 부인에게 저토록 말솜씨가 빼어난 저 자그마한 부인이 누구냐고 물었다.\n\n마침내 행렬이 저 미국 외교관이 묘사한 순서대로 갖추어지자, 그들은 연회가 차려진 그 방으로 줄지어 들어갔다. 그리고 그 방으로 말하자면, 독자께서 마음껏 즐기시도록 해 드리겠다고 약속한 바 있으니, 독자께서는 제 취향에 맞게 직접 주문하실 자유를 누리시면 되겠다.\n\n그러나 정작 한판 큰 싸움이 벌어지리라는 것을 베키가 안 것은 숙녀들만 남게 되었을 때였다. 그리고 과연 그제야 이 작은 여인은, 제 분수 위에 있는 숙녀들의 무리를 경계하라던 스타인 경의 충고가 옳았음을 인정하지 않을 수 없는 처지에 놓이게 되었다. 흔히들 아일랜드 사람을 가장 미워하는 자는 아일랜드 사람이라고들 하는데, 마찬가지로 여자에게 가장 모진 폭군은 분명 여자인 것이다. 가엾은 작은 베키가 숙녀들과만 남게 되어, 지체 높은 숙녀들이 모여 있는 벽난로 쪽으로 다가가자, 그 지체 높은 숙녀들은 자리를 떠 그림이 놓인 탁자를 차지해 버렸다. 베키가 그들을 따라 그림 탁자로 가자, 그들은 하나둘씩 다시 벽난로 쪽으로 흩어졌다. 그녀는 아이들 가운데 하나에게 말을 걸어 보려 했지만(그녀는 공공장소에서는 으레 아이들을 귀여워했다), 어린 조지 곤트 도련님은 제 어머니가 불러 데려가 버렸다. 그리하여 이 이방인은 끝내 어찌나 모진 대접을 받았던지, 스타인 부인마저 그녀를 가엾이 여겨 친구 하나 없는 그 작은 여인에게 말을 걸러 다가갔다.\n\n\"스타인 경 말씀이,\" 부인이 핼쑥한 두 볼을 발그레 붉히며 말했다. \"크롤리 부인께서 노래도 연주도 무척 아름답게 하신다더군요 — 제게 노래를 한 곡 들려주는 친절을 베풀어 주시면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"스타인 경 각하나 부인을 기쁘게 해 드릴 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든 하겠어요.\" 레베카가 진심으로 고마워하며 이렇게 말하고는 피아노 앞에 앉아 노래하기 시작했다.\n\n그녀는 스타인 부인이 어린 시절부터 즐겨 듣던 모차르트의 종교곡들을 어찌나 감미롭고 부드럽게 불렀던지, 피아노 곁을 서성이던 부인은 그 옆에 앉아 두 눈에 눈물이 흘러내릴 때까지 귀를 기울였다. 방 저편의 적대적인 숙녀들이 줄곧 시끄럽고 그칠 줄 모르게 웅성거리며 떠들어 댄 것은 사실이었지만, 스타인 부인의 귀에는 그 소란이 들리지 않았다. 그녀는 다시 어린아이가 되어, 사십 년의 황야를 거슬러 수녀원 정원으로 되돌아가 있었다. 예배당의 오르간이 바로 그 가락을 울려 퍼뜨렸고, 그 오르간 연주자, 곧 수도원에서 그녀가 가장 사랑하던 수녀가 그 행복하던 어린 시절에 이 곡들을 가르쳐 주었던 것이다. 그녀는 다시 한 번 소녀가 되었고, 그 짧았던 행복의 시절이 한 시간 동안 다시 꽃피어났다 — 삐걱거리는 문이 활짝 열어젖혀지고 스타인 경의 요란한 웃음소리와 함께 일행의 남자들이 흥에 겨워 들어서자 그녀는 흠칫 놀랐다.\n\n그는 자기가 없는 사이에 무슨 일이 있었는지 한눈에 알아채고는, 이번만큼은 아내에게 고마워했다. 그는 다가가 아내에게 말을 건네고 세례명으로 그녀를 불러, 다시금 그 창백한 얼굴에 홍조가 떠오르게 했다 — \"내 아내가 그러는데, 부인이 천사처럼 노래했다더군.\" 그가 베키에게 말했다. 그런데 천사에는 두 종류가 있고, 그 두 종류가 다 저마다의 방식으로 매혹적이라는 말이 있다.\n\n그날 저녁 앞부분이 어떠했든 간에, 그날 밤의 나머지는 베키에게 대단한 승리였다. 그녀는 그야말로 최선을 다해 노래했는데, 어찌나 훌륭했던지 남자들이 하나도 빠짐없이 다가와 피아노 둘레에 몰려들었다. 그녀의 적인 여자들은 완전히 외톨이가 되어 버렸다. 그리고 폴 제퍼슨 존스 씨는 곤트 부인에게 다가가 부인의 매력적인 벗이 일류로 노래한다고 칭찬함으로써 그 부인의 마음을 사로잡았노라 여겼다.\n\n이 희극적 이야기를 주관하는 뮤즈가 누구든 간에, 그 여신은 이제 자기가 높이 날아오르던 고상한 정점에서 내려와, 황송하게도 브롬프턴에 자리한 존 세들리의 누추한 지붕 위로 내려앉아, 그곳에서 어떤 일들이 벌어지고 있는지를 그려 보여 주어야 한다. 이 초라한 셋집에도 근심과 불신과 낙담이 깃들어 살고 있는 것이다. 부엌에서는 클랩 부인이 집세를 두고 남편에게 남몰래 투덜대며, 이 마음씨 좋은 사내더러 오랜 벗이자 후원자이며 지금은 세입자가 된 사람에게 들고 일어나라고 부추긴다. 세들리 부인은 이제 아래층 영역에 있는 집주인을 찾아가는 일도 그만두었고, 사실 더는 클랩 부인을 너그러이 보살펴 줄 처지가 못 되었다. 사십 파운드쯤 빚진 데다 줄곧 그 돈에 대한 암시를 흘려 대는 부인에게, 어떻게 거들먹거리며 너그러운 척할 수 있단 말인가? 아일랜드 출신 하녀의 친절하고 공손한 태도는 조금도 변함이 없었으나, 세들리 부인은 그 아이가 점점 무례하고 배은망덕해진다고 여겼고, 가는 곳마다 덤불마다 포졸로 보인다는 도둑이 제 발 저리듯, 그 아이의 모든 말과 대답에서 위협적인 빈정거림이며 붙잡으려는 기색을 읽어 냈다. 클랩 양은 이제 어엿한 처녀로 자랐는데, 심술궂어진 노부인은 그 아이를 참아 줄 수 없는 건방진 계집애라고 단언했다. 애밀리아가 어찌하여 그 아이를 그토록 좋아하는지, 어찌하여 제 방에 그토록 자주 들이는지, 어찌하여 그토록 늘 함께 산책을 나가는지, 세들리 부인으로서는 도무지 헤아릴 수가 없었다. 가난의 쓰라림이 한때 명랑하고 다정했던 부인의 삶을 독으로 물들여 버린 것이었다. 부인은 애밀리아가 자신에게 한결같이 부드럽게 대하는 데도 고마운 줄 몰랐고, 친절을 베풀거나 도우려는 노력을 트집 잡았으며, 제 자식을 자랑스러워하는 어리석은 자긍심과 부모를 소홀히 한다며 딸에게 욕설을 퍼부었다. 조스 외삼촌의 연금이 끊긴 뒤로 어린 식구가 거의 굶주리다시피 지내게 되면서, 조지의 집은 그리 활기찬 곳이 못 되었다.\n\n애밀리아는 생각하고 또 생각하며, 온 식구가 굶주리고 있는 그 적은 돈을 조금이라도 늘릴 방도를 찾으려고 머리를 쥐어짰다. 무언가를 가르쳐 볼 수 있을까? 카드꽂이에 그림을 그려 볼까? 고운 바느질을 해 볼까? 그녀는 여자들이 하루 이 펜스를 받고, 자기보다 더 솜씨 좋게, 그토록 고되게 일하고 있음을 알게 되었다. 그녀는 잡화 문구점에서 도금한 브리스틀 판지 두 장을 사다가 거기에 정성을 다해 그림을 그렸다. 한 장에는 빨간 조끼를 입고 연필로 그린 풍경 한가운데서 발그레한 얼굴로 미소 짓는 양치기를, 다른 한 장에는 작은 다리를 건너는 양치기 처녀와 음영을 곱게 넣은 작은 개를 그렸다. 잡화점 겸 브롬프턴 미술품 상회의 주인은(그녀는 그에게서 그 가리개를 샀고, 자기 손으로 장식을 하고 나면 그가 도로 사 주리라는 헛된 기대를 품었다) 이 보잘것없는 미술품을 살펴보면서 비웃음을 좀처럼 감추지 못했다. 그는 가게에서 기다리는 부인을 곁눈질하더니, 카드를 다시 누르스름한 갈색 종이 봉투에 싸서 가엾은 미망인과 클랩 양에게 건넸다. 클랩 양은 평생 그토록 아름다운 물건을 본 적이 없는지라, 그 주인이 가리개에 적어도 이 기니는 쳐 주리라고 철석같이 믿고 있던 터였다. 두 사람은 희미하고 메스꺼운 희망을 품은 채 런던 안쪽의 다른 가게들을 찾아다녔다. \"필요 없소이다.\" 한 가게에서는 이렇게 말했다. \"썩 꺼지슈.\" 다른 가게에서는 사납게 쏘아붙였다. 삼 실링 육 펜스만 헛되이 쓴 셈이었다. 가리개는 클랩 양의 침실로 물러갔고, 그녀는 그것이 사랑스럽다고 한사코 믿어 마지않았다.\n\n그녀는 가장 정갈한 글씨로 작은 카드 한 장을 적었다. 오랜 궁리와 문안 작성의 수고 끝에, 거기에는 사람들에게 이렇게 알리는 내용이 담겼다. \"다소의 여가가 있는 한 숙녀가 어린 소녀들의 교육을 맡고자 하오니, 영어와 프랑스어와 지리와 역사와 음악을 가르쳐 드리겠습니다. 브라운 씨 댁의 A. O. 앞으로 연락 주시기 바랍니다.\" 그녀는 그 카드를 미술품 상회 주인에게 맡겼고, 그는 그것을 계산대 위에 놓아 두기로 했다. 카드는 거기서 점점 거뭇거뭇 때가 끼고 파리똥이 슬었다. 애밀리아는 브라운 씨가 무슨 소식이라도 전해 주기를 바라며 그 가게 앞을 몇 번이고 애틋하게 지나쳤으나, 그는 한 번도 그녀를 안으로 손짓해 부르지 않았다. 그녀가 소소한 물건을 사러 들를 때에도, 그녀에게 들려줄 소식이라곤 없었다. 가엾고 순박한 부인, 여리고 약한 이여, 그대가 어찌 이 아등바등 사나운 세상과 맞서 싸운단 말인가?\n\n그녀는 나날이 더 수심에 잠기고 서글퍼졌으며, 자기 자식에게 불안한 눈길을 붙박았으나, 어린 소년은 그 표정의 뜻을 헤아리지 못했다. 그녀는 밤이면 화들짝 일어나 살그머니 아이의 방을 들여다보며, 아이가 잠들어 있는지, 누가 채 가지는 않았는지 살폈다. 그녀는 이제 잠을 거의 이루지 못했다. 끊임없는 생각과 공포가 그녀를 사로잡고 있었다. 그 길고 고요한 밤들에 그녀가 어찌나 울며 기도했던지, 자꾸만 되돌아오는 그 생각을—자기가 아이와 헤어져야 한다는 생각을, 자기야말로 아이와 그 영달 사이를 가로막는 유일한 장벽이라는 생각을—어떻게든 스스로에게서 감추려고 어찌나 애썼던지. 그녀는 차마, 차마 그럴 수 없었다. 적어도 지금은. 언젠가 다른 날에. 아아! 그것은 떠올리기도 견디기도 너무나 모진 일이었다.\n\n한 가지 생각이 그녀를 스쳐, 얼굴을 붉히며 스스로에게서 고개를 돌리게 했다. 부모님이 연금을 그대로 받게 할 수도 있다는 생각이었다. 그 부목사가 자기와 결혼해 자기와 아이에게 보금자리를 마련해 줄 것이었다. 그러나 거기에는 조지의 초상과 더없이 소중한 추억이 있어 그녀를 꾸짖었다. 수치심과 사랑이 그 희생에 안 된다고 말했다. 그녀는 무언가 부정한 것에서 움츠러들듯 그 생각에서 움츠러들었고, 그토록 순결하고 부드러운 가슴속에는 그런 생각이 결코 머물 자리를 찾지 못했다.\n\n우리가 한두 문장으로 그려 내는 이 싸움은, 가엾은 애밀리아의 가슴속에서 여러 주에 걸쳐 이어졌고, 그동안 그녀에게는 속내를 털어놓을 사람 하나 없었다. 사실 그녀는 그런 사람을 둘 수도 없었으니, 날마다 맞서 싸우는 적 앞에서 날마다 무너지면서도, 자기가 굴복할 가능성이 있다는 것 자체를 스스로 인정하려 들지 않았기 때문이다. 진실들이 하나씩 하나씩 소리 없이 그녀를 향해 도열하더니 그 자리를 지켰다. 모두에게 닥칠 가난과 비참, 부모님에게 닥칠 궁핍과 영락, 아이에게 저지르는 부당함—가엾은 영혼이 자기의 유일한 사랑이자 보물을 애틋하게 지키고 있던 그 작은 성채의 외곽 보루가 하나씩 하나씩 함락되었다.\n\n이 싸움이 시작될 무렵, 그녀는 캘커타에 있는 오라비에게 애틋한 간청의 편지 한 통을 써 보냈었다. 부모님께 베풀어 온 부양을 거두지 말아 달라고 애원하며, 두 분의 외롭고 가련한 처지를 꾸밈없는 비통함으로 그려 낸 편지였다. 그녀는 그 일의 진상을 알지 못했다. 조스의 연금은 여전히 어김없이 지급되고 있었으나, 그것을 받아 챙기는 사람은 시내의 한 고리대금업자였다. 늙은 세들리가 자기의 헛된 사업을 벌일 돈을 마련하려고 그 연금을 팔아넘긴 것이었다. 에미는 그 편지가 도착해 답장이 올 때까지 얼마나 걸릴지를 애타게 헤아렸다. 그녀는 편지를 부친 날짜를 수첩에 적어 두었다. 자기 아들의 후견인인, 마드라스의 그 선량한 소령에게는 자기의 슬픔과 곤경을 조금도 알리지 않았다. 그녀는 그의 결혼이 임박했음을 축하하는 편지를 쓴 뒤로는 그에게 한 번도 편지하지 않았다. 그녀는 메스꺼운 낙담에 잠겨, 그 벗이—유일한 벗, 자기에게 그토록 깊은 정을 품었던 그 사람이—이제는 멀어져 떠나 버렸다고 생각했다.\n\n어느 날, 사정이 매우 고약한 지경에 이르러—채권자들은 졸라 대고, 어머니는 발작적인 슬픔에 빠지고, 아버지는 평소보다 더 침울하고, 식구들은 저마다 남모르는 불행과 억울한 심사에 짓눌린 채 서로를 피하고 있던 그때—아버지와 딸이 우연히 단둘이 남게 되었고, 애밀리아는 자기가 한 일을 들려주어 아버지를 위로하고자 했다. 조지프에게 편지를 써 두었으니, 서너 달 안에는 답장이 올 것이라고 했다. 그 애는 늘 무심하긴 해도 너그러운 사람이었다. 부모님의 형편이 얼마나 군색한지 알게 되면 거절하지 못할 것이었다.\n\n그러자 가엾은 노신사는 딸에게 진실을 모조리 털어놓았다. 자기 아들은 여전히 연금을 보내고 있지만, 자신이 분별없이 그것을 내던져 버렸다는 것이었다. 그는 더 일찍 그 말을 꺼낼 엄두를 내지 못했다. 떨리고 비참한 목소리로 그 고백을 했을 때, 애밀리아의 핼쑥하고 겁에 질린 표정이 자기의 은폐를 책망하는 것이라고 그는 여겼다. \"아!\" 그가 입술을 떨며 고개를 돌리고 말했다. \"이제 너도 이 늙은 애비를 경멸하는 게로구나!\"\n\n\"오, 아버지! 그게 아니에요.\" 애밀리아가 부르짖으며 그의 목을 끌어안고 몇 번이고 입을 맞추었다. \"아버지는 언제나 선하고 자상하셨어요. 다 잘되라고 하신 일이잖아요. 돈 때문이 아니에요. 그건—오, 하느님! 하느님! 저를 가엾이 여기시어, 이 시련을 견딜 힘을 주소서.\" 그러고는 다시 미친 듯이 아버지에게 입을 맞추고는 자리를 떠났다.\n\n그래도 아버지는 그 말이 무슨 뜻인지, 가엾은 딸이 자기를 두고 떠나며 터뜨린 그 비통함이 무엇인지 알지 못했다. 그것은 그녀가 졌다는 뜻이었다. 선고가 내려진 것이었다. 아이는 그녀를 떠나야 했다. 다른 이들에게로—그녀를 잊으러. 그녀의 가슴이자 보물, 그녀의 기쁨이요 희망이요 사랑이요 경배의 대상, 거의 그녀의 하느님이나 다름없는 아이를! 그녀는 아이를 내주어야 했고, 그러고 나면—그러고 나면 조지에게로 가서, 둘이 함께 아이를 굽어보며 아이가 천국에서 자기들에게 올 때까지 기다리리라.\n\n그녀는 자기가 무얼 하는지도 거의 모르는 채 보닛을 쓰고, 조지가 학교에서 돌아오던 길, 그녀가 늘 아이를 마중하러 나가곤 하던 그 골목길로 걸어 나갔다. 때는 오월이었고, 반공일이었다. 잎이 죄다 돋아나고 날씨는 눈부셨다. 아이는 건강으로 발그레한 얼굴로 노래를 부르며, 가죽끈에 책 꾸러미를 매단 채 그녀에게 뛰어왔다. 거기 아이가 있었다. 그녀는 두 팔로 아이를 끌어안았다. 아니, 불가능한 일이었다. 둘은 헤어질 수 없었다. \"왜 그러세요, 엄마?\" 아이가 말했다. \"얼굴이 아주 창백하세요.\"\n\n\"아무것도 아니란다, 우리 아가.\" 그녀는 그렇게 말하고는 몸을 숙여 아이에게 입을 맞추었다.\n\n그날 밤 애밀리아는 아이에게 사무엘의 이야기를, 그리고 그의 어머니 한나가 그를 젖 떼게 한 뒤 대제사장 엘리에게 데려가 주님 앞에서 섬기게 한 대목을 읽게 했다. 아이는 한나가 부른 감사의 노래를 읽었으니, 거기에는 누가 사람을 가난하게도 부유하게도 하시며, 누가 낮추기도 높이기도 하시는지가 적혀 있었다. 가난한 자가 어떻게 흙먼지 속에서 일으켜 세워지는지, 그리고 어떤 사람도 제 힘으로는 강해질 수 없다는 것이. 그러고 나서 아이는 사무엘의 어머니가 그에게 작은 겉옷을 지어, 해마다 제사를 드리러 올라올 때마다 가져다주었다는 대목을 읽었다. 그러자 조지의 어머니는 다정하고 소박한 방식으로 이 가슴 저미는 이야기를 아이에게 풀이해 주었다. 한나가 아들을 그토록 사랑했으면서도 자기의 서원 때문에 어떻게 아들을 내주었는지를. 그리고 그녀가 멀리 떨어진 집에 앉아 그 작은 겉옷을 지으면서 늘 아이를 생각했음이 틀림없다고. 또 사무엘은 분명 어머니를 결코 잊지 않았으리라고. 그리고 자기 아이를 다시 보게 될 그때가(세월은 참 빨리도 흘러가는 법이다) 오면, 또 아이가 얼마나 착하고 지혜롭게 자랐는지를 보게 되면, 그녀가 얼마나 행복했겠느냐고. 이 작은 설교를, 그녀는 부드럽고 엄숙한 목소리로, 마른 눈으로 이어 갔다. 그러다 두 사람이 다시 만나는 대목에 이르자—이야기는 갑자기 끊기고, 그 애틋한 가슴이 북받쳐 넘쳐흘렀으며, 그녀는 아이를 가슴에 끌어안고 두 팔로 어르며 거룩한 눈물의 고뇌에 잠겨 소리 없이 아이 위로 울었다.\n\n마음을 굳힌 미망인은, 자기가 작정한 그 끝을 향해 나아가고자 스스로 옳다고 여겨지는 조처를 취하기 시작했다. 어느 날, 러셀 광장의 오스본 양은(애밀리아는 십 년 동안 그 집의 이름도 번지도 적어 본 적이 없었다—겉봉을 적으면서 그녀의 젊은 날이, 지난날의 이야기가 되살아났다) 어느 날 오스본 양은 애밀리아에게서 편지 한 통을 받았는데, 그 편지에 그녀는 얼굴을 몹시 붉히며 식탁 저편 제자리에 침울하게 앉아 있는 아버지 쪽을 바라보았다.\n\n애밀리아는 소박한 말로, 자기 아이를 두고 마음을 바꾸게 된 사연을 그녀에게 들려주었다. 아버지가 새로운 불운을 만나 완전히 파산하셨다는 것, 자기 몫의 푼돈이 너무도 적어 부모님을 겨우 봉양할 수 있을 뿐 조지에게 마땅히 누려야 할 혜택을 베풀어 줄 수는 없다는 것이었다. 아이와 헤어지는 고통이 아무리 크더라도, 하느님의 도우심으로, 아이를 위해 그것을 견뎌 내겠노라고 했다. 그녀는 아이가 가게 될 그곳 사람들이 아이를 행복하게 해 주려고 힘닿는 데까지 다 하리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 그녀는 자기가 짐작하는 대로 아이의 성정을 그려 보였다. 영민하나 구속이나 모진 대우를 견디지 못하고, 사랑과 친절에는 쉽게 마음이 움직인다고. 추신에서는 한 가지를 못 박아 두었으니, 자기가 원할 때면 언제든 아이를 볼 수 있다는 서면 합의가 있어야 한다는 것, 그 밖의 어떤 조건으로도 아이와 헤어질 수 없다는 것이었다.\n\n\"뭐라고? 그 도도하신 부인께서 굽히고 들어오셨다, 이거지?\" 오스본 양이 떨리고 다급한 목소리로 편지를 읽어 주자 늙은 오스본이 말했다. \"꼼짝없이 굶겨 내쫓겼다, 응? 하, 하! 그럴 줄 알았다니까.\" 그는 위엄을 지키며 여느 때처럼 신문을 읽으려 했으나, 도무지 글이 눈에 들어오지 않았다. 그는 신문지 뒤에서 혼자 낄낄거리며 욕설을 중얼거렸다.\n\n마침내 그는 신문을 내던지고, 평소 버릇대로 딸을 노려보고는, 방을 나가 옆에 붙은 서재로 들어갔다가 곧 열쇠 하나를 들고 돌아왔다. 그는 그것을 오스본 양에게 던졌다.\n\n\"내 방 위에 있는 방을—그 애 방이었던 데를—치워 두어라.\" 그가 말했다. \"네, 아버지.\" 딸이 떨면서 대답했다. 그것은 조지의 방이었다. 십 년이 넘도록 열린 적이 없는 방이었다. 그의 옷가지 몇 벌과 서류, 손수건, 채찍과 모자, 낚싯대와 사냥 도구가 아직도 그대로 있었다. 표지에 그의 이름이 적힌 1814년판 육군 명부, 그가 글을 쓸 때 즐겨 쓰던 작은 사전, 그리고 어머니가 그에게 준 성경이 박차 한 쌍과 십 년 묵은 먼지가 뽀얗게 앉은 마른 잉크병과 함께 벽난로 선반 위에 놓여 있었다. 아! 그 잉크가 채 마르지 않았던 그 시절 이래로, 얼마나 많은 날과 사람들이 스러져 갔는가! 아직도 탁자 위에 놓인 습자책에는 그의 손길이 남긴 얼룩이 묻어 있었다.\n\n오스본 양은 하인들을 거느리고 이 방에 처음 들어섰을 때 몹시 마음이 흔들렸다. 그녀는 핼쑥하게 질려 그 작은 침대에 주저앉았다. \"이건 복된 소식이에요, 아씨—정말이지요, 아씨.\" 가정부가 말했다. \"그 좋던 옛 시절이 돌아오는 거예요, 아씨. 그 귀여운 도련님이, 암요, 아씨, 얼마나 행복해하시겠어요! 하지만 메이페어의 어떤 사람들은, 아씨, 도련님을 두고 원한을 품을걸요, 아씨.\" 그러고는 창틀을 잡고 있던 빗장을 딸깍 젖혀, 방 안으로 바람이 들어오게 했다.\n\n\"그 여자한테 돈을 좀 보내 주는 게 좋겠다.\" 오스본 씨가 나가기 전에 말했다. \"부족한 게 없게 해 줘야지. 백 파운드를 보내라.\"\n\n\"그럼 제가 내일 그분을 뵈러 가도 될까요?\" 오스본 양이 물었다.\n\n\"그거야 네가 알아서 할 일이고. 그 여자는 여기 발을 들이지 못한다, 알겠느냐. 안 돼, 절대로—런던의 돈을 다 준대도 안 될 일이야. 하지만 이제 궁색하게 두어선 안 돼. 그러니 잘 챙겨서 모든 걸 제대로 해 두어라.\" 이런 짤막한 말로 오스본 씨는 딸과 작별하고는 여느 때처럼 시내로 향했다.\n\n\"여기, 아버지, 돈이에요.\" 그날 밤 애밀리아가 늙은 아버지에게 입을 맞추고 백 파운드짜리 어음을 그의 손에 쥐여 주며 말했다. \"그리고—그리고, 어머니, 조지에게 모질게 굴지 마세요. 그 애는—그 애는 이제 우리와 오래 머물지 못할 거예요.\" 그녀는 더는 아무 말도 하지 못하고, 말없이 자기 방으로 걸어갔다. 그녀의 기도와 슬픔 위로 방문을 닫아 두기로 하자. 이토록 큰 사랑과 비탄에 대해서는 말을 아끼는 편이 가장 나으리라 싶다.\n\n이튿날 오스본 양은 자기 편지에 담긴 약속대로 찾아와 애밀리아를 만났다. 두 사람의 만남은 다정했다. 오스본 양의 눈빛과 몇 마디 말은, 적어도 이 여자에 관한 한, 그녀가 자기 아들의 애정에서 으뜸가는 자리를 차지하지나 않을까 두려워할 까닭이 없음을 가엾은 미망인에게 일러 주었다. 그녀는 차갑고 분별 있었으나, 매정하지는 않았다. 어쩌면 이 연적이 더 잘생기고, 더 젊고, 더 다정하고, 더 따뜻한 마음씨를 지녔더라면, 어머니는 이토록 흡족하지 못했을 것이다. 한편 오스본 양은 지난날과 추억을 떠올리며, 이 가엾은 어머니의 애처로운 처지에 마음이 움직이지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀는 굴복했고, 말하자면 무기를 내려놓고서 겸허히 받아들였다. 그날 두 사람은 함께 항복 조약의 예비 조항들을 정해 두었다.\n\n이튿날 조지는 학교를 쉬고 고모를 만났다. 애밀리아는 두 사람을 단둘이 남겨 두고 자기 방으로 갔다. 그녀는 그 이별을 미리 견뎌 보고 있었다—저 가엾고 온순한 제인 그레이 부인이 곧 내리쳐 자기의 가녀린 목숨을 끊을 도끼의 날을 더듬어 보았듯이. 며칠은 협상과 방문과 준비로 지나갔다. 미망인은 조지에게 그 일을 무척 조심스레 꺼냈으니, 그 소식에 아이가 몹시 슬퍼하리라 여겼던 것이다. 그러나 아이는 오히려 들떠 했고, 가엾은 부인은 서글피 고개를 돌렸다. 그날 아이는 학교에서 친구들에게 그 소식을 자랑했다. 자기가 할아버지와, 그러니까 아버지의 아버지와—이따금 여기 오는 그 할아버지 말고—함께 살게 되었다고 일러 주었다. 자기는 아주 부자가 되어 마차와 조랑말을 갖게 되고, 훨씬 더 멋진 학교에 다니게 될 거라고, 부자가 되면 리더의 연필갑을 사고 파이 파는 아주머니에게 외상값도 갚아 주겠다고 했다. 다정한 어머니의 생각에, 아이는 제 아버지를 빼다 박은 모습이었다.\n\n사실 나는, 우리의 사랑하는 애밀리아를 생각하면, 조지가 집에서 보낸 마지막 날들의 이야기를 끝까지 들려줄 엄두가 나지 않는다.\n\n마침내 그날이 왔다. 마차가 다가와 멈춰 섰고, 사랑과 추억의 정표가 담긴 초라하고 작은 꾸러미들은 진작부터 채비를 갖춰 현관에 놓여 있었다. 조지는 새 옷을 입고 있었으니, 앞서 재단사가 와서 치수를 재 갔던 그 옷이었다. 아이는 해와 함께 벌떡 일어나 새 옷을 입었고, 그 소리를 바로 옆방에서 어머니가 들었다. 그녀는 말 못 할 슬픔에 잠긴 채 그 방에 누워 지켜보고 있었던 것이다. 며칠 전부터 그녀는 그 끝을 위해 채비를 하고 있었다. 아이가 쓸 자잘한 물건들을 사들이고, 아이의 책과 옷가지에 이름을 적어 넣고, 아이와 이야기를 나누며 그 변화를 맞을 마음의 준비를 시키고 있었다. 아이에게 마음의 준비가 필요하다고 애틋하게 여기면서.\n\n자기 처지에 변화만 생긴다면, 아이가 무엇을 마음 쓰겠는가? 아이는 그 변화를 간절히 바라고 있었다. 할아버지와 함께 살게 되면 무얼 하겠다는 갖가지 들뜬 다짐을 늘어놓음으로써, 아이는 이별이라는 생각에 자기가 얼마나 풀이 죽지 않았는지를 가엾은 미망인에게 고스란히 드러내 보였다. \"조랑말을 타고 엄마를 자주 보러 올게요.\" 아이가 말했다. \"마차를 타고 와서 엄마를 모셔 갈 거예요. 공원에서 함께 마차를 타고, 엄마가 원하는 건 뭐든 다 갖게 해 드릴게요.\" 가엾은 어머니는 이 이기적인 애정의 표시에나마 만족하려 애썼고, 제 아들이 얼마나 진심으로 자기를 사랑하는지를 스스로에게 납득시키려 했다. 아이는 자기를 사랑하는 게 틀림없었다. 아이들이란 다 그런 법이니까. 새로운 것에 조금 마음이 쏠리고, 또—아니, 이기적인 게 아니라, 제멋대로일 뿐이지. 내 아이는 세상에서 누릴 즐거움과 야망을 가져야 마땅하다. 자기야말로 자신의 이기심과 분별없는 사랑 때문에 지금껏 아이에게서 마땅한 권리와 즐거움을 빼앗아 온 것이라고, 그녀는 생각했다.\n\n여자의 그 소심한 자기 비하와 자기 낮춤보다 더 가슴 저미는 것을 나는 별로 알지 못한다. 죄지은 자가 남자가 아니라 자기라고 어떻게 인정하는지, 모든 허물을 어떻게 제 쪽으로 떠안는지, 저지르지도 않은 잘못에 대한 벌을 어떻게 자청하다시피 하며 진짜 죄인을 한사코 감싸려 드는지! 여자에게 상처 주는 자들이야말로 여자에게서 가장 큰 친절을 받는 법이다. 여자들은 본디 소심하게 태어나, 자기 앞에서 가장 비굴한 자들을 폭군처럼 짓밟고 학대한다.\n\n그리하여 가엾은 애밀리아는 말없는 비참 속에서 아들의 떠남을 준비해 오며, 그 끝을 위해 채비하는 데에 길고 긴 외로운 시간을 숱하게 보냈다. 조지는 어머니 곁에 서서, 조금의 근심도 없이 그 채비를 지켜보았다. 아이의 상자들 안으로는 눈물이 떨어졌고, 아이가 가장 좋아하던 책들에는 군데군데 줄이 그어졌으며, 낡은 장난감과 유품과 보물들은 아이를 위해 따로 간직되어 야릇하리만치 정갈하고 정성스럽게 꾸려졌다. 그러나 이 모든 것을 아이는 거들떠보지도 않았다. 어머니의 가슴이 미어지는 동안 아이는 웃으며 떠나간다. 맙소사, 허영의 시장에서 아이를 향한 여자들의 그 헛된 사랑이란 참으로 가련한 것이다.\n\n며칠이 지나고, 애밀리아의 생애에서 가장 큰 사건이 마침내 이루어졌다. 그 어떤 천사도 끼어들지 않았다. 아이는 운명에 제물로 바쳐졌고, 미망인은 이제 홀로 남았다.\n\n물론 아이는 그녀를 보러 자주 온다. 뒤에 마부를 거느린 채 조랑말을 타고 오는데, 그 곁에서 자랑스레 골목길을 걸어 내려오는 늙은 할아버지 세들리가 여간 기뻐하는 게 아니다. 그녀는 아이를 보지만, 아이는 이제 더는 그녀의 아이가 아니다. 게다가 아이는 그 작은 학교의 친구들을 보러, 또 그들 앞에서 자기의 새 부와 영화를 뽐내러 말을 타고 가기도 한다. 이틀 만에 아이는 다소 거만한 태도와 거들먹거리는 몸가짐을 익혔다. 제 아버지가 그러했듯, 이 아이도 남을 부리도록 타고났다고, 어머니는 생각한다.\n\n이제 날씨가 화창하다. 아이가 오지 않는 날 저녁이면, 그녀는 런던까지 먼 길을 걸어 나간다—그렇다, 러셀 광장까지, 그 먼 데까지 가서, 오스본 씨 집 맞은편 정원 난간 옆의 돌 위에 앉아 쉰다. 거기는 어찌나 쾌적하고 시원한지. 그녀는 고개를 들어, 불 밝힌 응접실 창문을, 그리고 아홉 시쯤이면 조지가 잠드는 위층의 방을 바라볼 수 있다. 그녀는 안다—아이가 일러 주었으니까. 불빛이 꺼지면 그녀는 거기서 기도한다. 겸허한 마음으로 기도하고는, 움츠러든 채 말없이 집으로 걸어온다. 집에 돌아올 때면 그녀는 몹시 지쳐 있다. 어쩌면 그 길고 고단한 걸음 덕분에 잠이 더 잘 들지도 모르고, 조지의 꿈을 꿀 수 있을지도 모른다.\n\n어느 일요일, 그녀는 마침 러셀 광장을, 오스본 씨 집에서 좀 떨어진 곳에서(그래도 멀리서 그 집이 보이긴 했다) 거닐고 있었는데, 그때 안식일을 알리는 종이 죄다 울려 퍼졌고, 조지와 그의 고모가 교회에 가려고 집을 나섰다. 어린 굴뚝 청소부 하나가 적선을 청하자, 책을 들고 있던 하인이 그 아이를 쫓아내려 했다. 그러나 조지가 걸음을 멈추고 그 아이에게 돈을 주었다. 부디 그 아이에게 하느님의 축복이 있기를! 에미는 광장을 빙 돌아 그 굴뚝 청소부에게 다가가 자기도 얼마 안 되는 돈을 쥐여 주었다. 안식일을 알리는 종이 죄다 울려 퍼지고 있었고, 그녀는 그 종소리를 따라가다 마침내 파운들링 교회에 이르러 안으로 들어갔다. 거기서 그녀는 아이의 머리가, 그 아버지의 묘비 아래로 보이는 자리에 앉았다. 그곳에서는 수백 명의 싱그러운 아이들 목소리가 솟아올라, 자비로우신 아버지께 찬송을 불렀고, 어린 조지의 영혼은 그 영광스러운 시편 노래의 물결에 기쁨으로 떨렸다. 어머니는 한동안, 두 눈을 흐리게 하는 안개 너머로 아이를 볼 수 없었다.\n\n# CHAPTER LI\n\n독자를 어리둥절하게 할 수도 있고 안 할 수도 있는 무언극이 펼쳐지는 장\n\n베키가 스테인 경의 사사롭고 엄선된 모임에 모습을 드러낸 뒤로, 이 가상한 여인이 사교계에서 차지하는 위상은 확고하게 자리 잡았고, 머지않아 수도에서 가장 으리으리하고 키 큰 문들이 몇 개씩 그녀 앞에 활짝 열렸다. 어찌나 으리으리하고 키 큰 문이었던지, 이 글을 사랑하는 독자도, 이 글을 쓰는 사람도 그 문으로 들어서기를 바라 봤자 헛일일 것이다. 친애하는 형제들이여, 저 위풍당당한 대문 앞에서 우리 모두 떨도록 하자. 내가 보기에 그 문들은 번쩍이는 은 쇠스랑을 든 시종들이 지키고 서서, 입장할 권리가 없는 자들을 죄다 그 쇠스랑으로 찔러 내쫓는 것만 같다. 사람들 말로는, 그 큰 잔치에 입장이 허락된 명사들의 이름을 받아 적느라 현관에 앉아 있는 저 정직한 신문 기자 양반은 얼마 못 가 죽고 만다고 한다. 그는 사교계의 광채를 오래 견디지 못하는 것이다. 그 광채가 그를 바싹 태워 버리고 마는 셈인데, 마치 정장을 갖춰 입고 나타난 유피테르가 저 가엾고 경솔한 세멜레를 태워 없앤 것과 같다. 자기 본래의 대기권 밖으로 무모하게 나섰다가 스스로를 파멸시킨, 경박한 나방 같은 존재 말이다. 타이번 사람들이며 벨그레이비어 사람들은 마땅히 그 신화를 가슴에 새겨야 할 것이다. 그 이야기를, 그리고 어쩌면 베키의 이야기까지도. 아, 숙녀분들이여! 투리퍼 목사에게 한번 물어보시라. 벨그레이비어가 울리는 꽹과리요 타이번이 소리 나는 징이 아닌지를. 이런 것들은 다 허영이다. 이런 것들조차 사라지고 말 것이다. 그리고 언젠가는(다행히도 그것은 우리 시대가 지난 뒤의 일이겠지만) 하이드 파크 가든스도 저 이름난 바빌론의 원예 교외만큼이나 알려지지 않은 곳이 될 것이며, 벨그레이브 광장도 베이커 거리나 광야의 타드모르만큼이나 황량해질 것이다.\n\n숙녀분들이여, 그 위대한 피트가 베이커 거리에 살았다는 사실을 알고 계신가? 지금은 영락한 그 저택에서 헤스터 부인의 모임에 초대받기 위해서라면, 그대들의 할머니들은 무엇인들 내놓지 않았겠는가? 나는 그곳에서 식사를 해 본 사람이다. 지금 여러분께 말씀드리는 이 몸이 말이다. 나는 그 방을 위대한 망자들의 유령으로 가득 채웠다. 우리가 오늘날의 사람들과 더불어 진지하게 클라레를 마시며 앉아 있노라니, 떠난 이들의 혼령이 들어와 그 어두컴컴한 식탁 둘레에 제자리를 잡았다. 폭풍을 헤쳐 나간 그 키잡이는 영혼의 포트와인을 큰 잔으로 죽 들이켰고, 던다스의 망령은 한 방울도 남기지 않았다. 애딩턴은 허리를 굽실거리며 섬뜩한 표정으로 히죽거리고 앉아, 소리 없는 술병이 돌아올 때면 결코 뒤지지 않으려 들었다. 스콧은 텁수룩한 눈썹 아래로 술잔 가장자리에 떠도는 거품의 허깨비를 향해 눈을 찡긋했다. 윌버포스는 두 눈을 천장으로 치켜올린 통에 자기 잔이 어떻게 가득 차서 입까지 올라갔다가 빈 채로 내려오는지도 모르는 듯했다. 바로 어제까지도 우리 머리 위에 있던 그 천장, 지난날의 위인들이 모두 우러러보던 그 천장으로 말이다. 지금은 그 집을 가구 딸린 셋집으로 내놓고 있다. 그렇다, 헤스터 부인은 한때 베이커 거리에 살았으나, 지금은 광야에 잠들어 있다. 이오텐이 거기서 그녀를 보았다. 베이커 거리가 아니라 또 다른 적막한 곳에서 말이다.\n\n확실히 이 모든 것이 허영이긴 하나, 그 허영을 조금쯤 좋아한다는 사실을 누가 인정하지 않겠는가? 한낱 덧없는 것이라는 이유만으로 구운 쇠고기를 싫어하는, 그런 제대로 된 정신의 소유자가 과연 어디 있는지 나는 알고 싶다. 그것도 허영이다. 그러나 이 글을 읽는 모든 이가 평생토록 그 허영을 넉넉히 누리기를 나는 비는 바이다. 그렇다, 설령 내 독자가 오십만 명이라 해도 말이다. 자, 신사 여러분, 자리에 앉아 왕성한 식욕으로 마음껏 드시라. 비계든 살코기든 고깃국물이든, 그리고 서양고추냉이는 입맛대로, 아끼지 말고 드시라. 와인 한 잔 더 하게, 존스, 이 사람아. 그 일요일 만찬용으로 남겨 둔 고기도 한 점 들게. 그렇다, 이 허영의 것을 배불리 먹고 그에 감사하도록 하자. 그리고 베키의 그 귀족적인 쾌락도 마찬가지로 우리가 한껏 즐기도록 하자. 이런 것들도 다른 온갖 인간사의 기쁨과 마찬가지로 한낱 덧없는 것에 지나지 않았으니 말이다.\n\n베키가 스테인 경을 방문한 결과는 이러했다. 페테르바라딘 대공 전하께서 이튿날 클럽에서 크롤리 대령을 만났을 때 그와의 친분을 새로이 다질 기회를 잡았고, 하이드 파크의 승마장에서는 크롤리 부인에게 모자를 깊숙이 벗어 들며 정중히 인사를 건넨 것이다. 그녀와 그녀의 남편은 곧바로 대공이 르방 저택에서 여는 소규모 모임 가운데 하나에 초대받았다. 르방 저택은 그 고귀한 소유주가 잠시 영국을 비운 사이 대공 전하께서 머무르고 계신 곳이었다. 그녀는 만찬 뒤에 아주 조촐한 모임 앞에서 노래를 불렀다. 스테인 후작이 그 자리에 함께하여, 제자가 발전해 가는 모습을 아비 같은 마음으로 지켜보았다.\n\n르방 저택에서 베키는 유럽이 배출한 가장 빼어난 신사이자 가장 위대한 대신 가운데 한 사람을 만났다. 다름 아닌 자보티에르 공작이었으니, 당시 가장 그리스도교적인 국왕이 보낸 대사였고 훗날 그 군주의 대신이 된 인물이었다. 이 위풍당당한 이름들을 적어 내려가노라니 정말이지 나 자신이 자랑스러워 가슴이 부풀어 오르는 것이다.\n\n베키는 차츰 사교계를 누비고 다녔다. 그녀는 프랑스 대사관의 단골손님이 되었으니, 그곳에서는 매혹적인 로던 크롤리 부인이 자리하지 않으면 어떤 모임도 완전하다고 여겨지지 않았다. 두 사람 다 대사관 부속 직원이던 트뤼피니 씨(페리고르 가문 출신이었다)와 샹피냐크 씨는 대번에 그 어여쁜 대령 부인의 매력에 사로잡혔고, 둘 다 자기 나라의 관습에 따라 선언하기를(영국에서 나온 프랑스인치고 적어도 대여섯 가문을 비참하게 만들어 놓고 그만큼의 마음을 자기 수첩에 챙겨 가지 않은 자를 여태 본 적이 있던가?), 거듭 말하거니와 둘 다 매혹적인 로던 부인과 더할 나위 없이 가까운 사이라고 선언했다.\n\n그러나 나는 그 주장이 옳은지 의심스럽다. 샹피냐크는 에카르테를 무척 좋아하여 저녁마다 대령과 여러 판을 벌이곤 했는데, 그동안 베키는 옆방에서 스테인 경에게 노래를 들려주고 있었다. 그리고 트뤼피니로 말할 것 같으면, 그가 트래블러스 클럽에는 감히 발걸음을 못 했다는 것이 익히 알려진 사실이다. 거기 급사들에게 진 빚이 있었던 까닭이다. 그러니 식사할 곳으로 대사관이 없었더라면 이 가상한 젊은 신사는 영락없이 굶주릴 판이었다. 거듭 말하거니와, 나는 베키가 이 두 젊은이 가운데 누구를 골라 각별한 정을 베풀었으리라고는 의심스럽게 여긴다. 그들은 그녀의 심부름을 다녀오고, 장갑과 꽃을 사다 바치고, 그녀를 위해 빚을 내어 오페라 박스석을 마련하는가 하면, 천 가지 방식으로 살갑게 굴었다. 그리고 그들은 사랑스러우리만치 어수룩한 영어를 구사했는데, 베키와 스테인 경은 그것을 두고 늘 즐거워했고, 베키는 그 둘 가운데 누구든 그 사람 면전에서 흉내를 내고는 영어 실력이 늘었다고 짐짓 점잖게 칭찬을 건넸으니, 그 진지한 태도가 그녀의 냉소적인 늙은 후원자인 후작을 어김없이 간질여 웃기는 것이었다. 트뤼피니는 베키의 심복인 브리그스의 마음을 사려고 그녀에게 숄을 선물하고는, 편지 한 통을 맡아 달라고 부탁했는데, 순진한 그 노처녀는 그 편지를 여러 사람이 보는 앞에서 받는 사람 본인에게 그대로 건네 버렸고, 그 편지의 문구는 그것을 읽은 모든 이를 크게 즐겁게 했다. 스테인 경도 그것을 읽었고, 정직한 로던만 빼고 모두가 읽었다. 메이 페어의 그 작은 집에서 벌어지는 일을 그에게 죄다 일러 줄 필요는 없었던 것이다.\n\n이곳에서 머지않아 베키는 \"최상류\"의 외국인들뿐 아니라(우리의 고상하고 감탄스러운 사교계 은어로 하면 그렇다는 말이다) 최상류의 영국인들 가운데 일부도 맞아들이게 되었다. 가장 덕망 높은 자들이라거나, 사실 가장 덕망 없는 자들이라거나, 가장 영리한 자들이라거나, 가장 어리석은 자들이라거나, 가장 부유한 자들이라거나, 가장 가문 좋은 자들을 말하는 것이 아니다. 그저 \"최상류\"라는 것이다. 한마디로, 이러쿵저러쿵 따질 여지가 없는 사람들 말이다. 이를테면 올맥의 수호성인이신 저 위대한 피츠윌리스 부인, 위대한 슬로보어 부인, 위대한 그리젤 맥베스 부인(이분은 글로리의 그레이 경의 따님인 그리젤 글로리 부인이었다) 같은 분들이다. 피츠윌리스 백작 부인(이 마님은 킹 스트리트 가문 출신이니, 데브렛과 버크를 보시라)이 한번 누군가를 받아들이면, 그 남자든 여자든 안전한 것이다. 더 이상 그들에 대해 왈가왈부할 것이 없어진다. 그렇다고 피츠윌리스 마님이 남들보다 더 나은 사람이라는 말은 아니다. 오히려 반대로, 쉰일곱 살 먹은 빛바랜 인물이요, 미인도 아니고 부유하지도 않고 재미있지도 않은 분이다. 그러나 그분이 \"최상류 인사\"에 속한다는 데에는 모두가 한목소리로 동의한다. 그분을 찾아가는 사람들은 다 최상류인 것이다. 그리고 아마도 스테인 부인에 대한 해묵은 원한 탓이었겠지만(당시 젊은 조지나 프레더리카, 즉 웨일스 공의 총신이던 포탠셰리 백작의 따님이던 이 마님이 한때 그 후작 부인 자리를 노린 적이 있었다), 이 위대하고 이름난 사교계의 선도자께서 로던 크롤리 부인을 인정하기로 마음먹은 것이다. 자신이 주관하던 그 모임에서 베키에게 누구보다 눈에 띄게 무릎을 굽혀 인사를 건넸고, 자기 아들 세인트키츠(이 도련님은 스테인 경의 주선으로 그 자리를 얻었다)에게 크롤리 부인의 집을 자주 드나들도록 부추겼을 뿐 아니라, 그녀를 자신의 저택으로 초대하기까지 했으며 만찬 자리에서 더없이 공개적이고 자상한 태도로 그녀에게 두 차례나 말을 건넸다. 그날 밤 이 중대한 사실이 런던 전역에 알려졌다. 크롤리 부인을 두고 망측하다며 험담하던 사람들이 입을 다물었다. 재사이자 변호사요 스테인 경의 오른팔인 웬엄은 곳곳을 돌아다니며 그녀를 칭송했다. 머뭇거리던 몇몇은 대번에 나서서 그녀를 환대했고, 사우스다운에게 그런 타락한 여자를 찾아가지 말라고 경고했던 꼬마 톰 토디는 이제 그녀에게 소개시켜 달라고 사정했다. 한마디로, 그녀는 \"최상류\" 인사 축에 들었음을 인정받은 것이다. 아, 사랑하는 독자들이여, 형제들이여, 가엾은 베키를 섣불리 부러워하지 마시라. 이런 영광이란 덧없는 것이라 하지 않는가. 흔히들 전하는 말로는, 그 가장 깊숙한 핵심 모임 안에서조차 그들이 그 권역 바깥을 떠도는 가엾은 방랑자들보다 더 행복할 것도 없다고 한다. 그리고 사교계의 한복판까지 꿰뚫고 들어가 저 위대한 조지 4세를 코앞에서 마주 본 베키도, 그곳에 역시 허영이 있더라고 그 뒤로 인정한 바 있다.\n\n그녀의 이 시절을 논함에 있어서는 우리도 간략히 해야겠다. 내가 프리메이슨의 비밀을 묘사할 수 없는 것은—그것이 허튼수작이라는 짐작이 제법 들기는 하지만—마찬가지로, 입문하지 않은 자가 상류 사회를 정확하게 그려 보이겠노라 나설 수는 없는 노릇이니, 그 견해가 무엇이든 간에 혼자 속으로 간직하는 편이 상책인 것이다.\n\n베키는 그 뒤로 여러 해 동안, 런던 사교계의 가장 위대한 모임을 누비고 다니던 이 시절의 이야기를 자주 입에 올리곤 했다. 그녀의 성공은 그녀를 들뜨게 하고, 우쭐하게 하고, 그러다 마침내 따분하게 만들었다. 처음에는 더없이 어여쁜 새 옷과 장신구를 고안하고 또 마련해 들이는 일만큼 즐거운 소일거리가 없었다(덧붙이자면, 후자, 즉 그것을 마련해 들이는 일은 로던 크롤리 부인처럼 형편이 빠듯한 사람에게는 적잖은 수고와 재간이 드는 일이었다). 멋진 만찬 모임으로 마차를 몰고 가면 명사들이 그녀를 반겨 주었고, 멋진 만찬 모임에서 다시 멋진 무도회로 옮겨 가면, 그곳에는 함께 식사한 바로 그 사람들, 전날 밤 만났고 이튿날 또 보게 될 그 사람들이 오는 것이었다. 흠잡을 데 없이 차려입고, 멋들어진 넥타이를 매고, 더없이 매끈하게 윤이 나는 부츠에 흰 장갑을 낀 젊은이들이며, 풍채 좋고 놋쇠 단추를 단, 고결해 보이고 정중하면서도 따분한 노인들이며, 금발에 수줍어하며 분홍 옷을 차려입은 젊은 숙녀들이며, 위풍당당하고 아름답고 화려하고 근엄하며 다이아몬드를 두른 그 어머니들이 말이다. 그들은 소설에서처럼 서툰 프랑스어가 아니라 영어로 이야기를 나누었다. 그들은 서로의 저택이며 사람됨이며 가문에 대해 떠들어 댔으니, 꼭 존스네가 스미스네 이야기를 하는 것과 다를 바 없었다. 베키의 옛 지인들은 그녀를 미워하고 시기했다. 그러나 가엾은 그녀 자신은 속으로 하품을 하고 있었다. \"이 바닥에서 벗어났으면 좋겠어.\" 그녀는 혼자 중얼거렸다. \"이러느니 차라리 목사 아내가 되어 주일학교를 가르치는 편이 낫겠어. 아니면 부사관 부인이 되어 연대 마차나 타고 다니거나. 아니, 그게 아니라, 차라리 번쩍이는 장식을 단 옷에 바지를 입고 장터 천막 앞에서 춤추는 편이 얼마나 더 흥겨울까.\"\n\n\"당신이라면 아주 잘 해낼 거요.\" 스테인 경이 웃으며 말했다. 그녀는 위대한 인물인 그에게 자신의 권태와 곤혹스러움을 천연덕스러운 투로 털어놓곤 했는데, 그것이 그를 즐겁게 했다.\n\n\"로던이라면 곡마단 단장 노릇을, 그러니까 의전관이라고 해야 하나, 뭐라고 부르더라, 그 커다란 부츠에 제복을 입고 채찍을 휘두르며 마장을 도는 그 사람 말이에요, 아주 훌륭하게 해낼 거예요. 그이는 덩치가 크고 묵직한 데다 군인다운 풍채가 있잖아요. 기억이 나요.\" 베키는 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 듯 말을 이었다. \"어릴 적에 아버지가 브룩그린 장터에 구경을 데려가 주셨는데, 집에 돌아와서는 죽마를 만들어 화실에서 춤을 추어 모든 제자들을 놀라게 한 적이 있어요.\"\n\n\"그걸 봤더라면 좋았을 것을.\" 스테인 경이 말했다.\n\n\"지금 한번 해 보고 싶어요.\" 베키가 말을 이었다. \"블린키 부인은 눈을 휘둥그렇게 뜰 거고, 그리젤 맥베스 부인은 빤히 쳐다보겠지요! 쉿! 조용히! 파스타가 노래를 시작하네요.\" 베키는 이런 귀족들의 모임에 불려 온 전문 가수며 연주자며 하는 분들에게는 늘 유난히 정중하게 구는 것을 잊지 않았다. 그들이 말없이 앉아 있는 구석 자리까지 따라가 손을 맞잡고, 모든 사람이 보는 앞에서 그들에게 미소를 지어 보이는 것이었다. 그녀가 참으로 옳게 말했듯, 그녀 자신도 예술가였다. 자신의 출신을 인정하는 그 솔직하고 겸허한 태도가, 보는 이에 따라 어떤 이의 부아를 돋우는가 하면, 어떤 이의 적의를 누그러뜨리고, 또 어떤 이를 즐겁게 했다. \"저 여자는 어쩌면 저렇게 태연할까.\" 누군가는 이렇게 말했다. \"가만히 앉아서 누가 말이라도 걸어 주면 감지덕지해야 할 처지에, 무슨 독립이라도 한 양 저런 허세를 부린담!\" \"참으로 정직하고 마음씨 고운 사람이로군!\" 또 다른 이는 이렇게 말했다. \"교활한 계집애 같으니!\" 세 번째 사람은 이렇게 말했다. 어쩌면 그들 모두 옳았을 것이다. 그러나 베키는 제 갈 길을 갔고, 그 전문가들을 어찌나 사로잡았던지 그들은 목이 아픈 것도 마다하고 그녀의 모임에서 노래를 불러 주고 거저 가르침까지 주는 것이었다.\n\n그렇다, 그녀는 커즌 거리의 그 작은 집에서 모임을 열었다. 등불을 환히 밝힌 마차 수십 대가 거리를 가로막아, 문 두드리는 소리가 천둥처럼 울려 도무지 쉴 수가 없던 100번지 집의 부아를 돋우고, 시기심에 잠을 못 이루던 102번지 집의 속을 뒤집어 놓았다. 그 마차들을 따라온 거구의 하인들은 너무 덩치가 커서 베키의 작은 현관에 다 들어가지를 못해 이웃 술집에 나누어 머물렀고, 필요할 때면 사환들이 그들을 맥주잔 앞에서 불러왔다. 런던의 멋쟁이 신사 수십 명이 그 작은 계단에서 서로 밀치고 발을 밟아 가며, 자신들이 그곳에 와 있다는 사실에 즐거워 웃어 댔다. 그리고 더없이 흠잡을 데 없고 근엄한 사교계의 숙녀들이 그 작은 응접실에 앉아, 전문 가수들의 노래에 귀를 기울였으니, 그 가수들은 으레 그러하듯, 꼭 창문이라도 날려 버릴 듯이 목청껏 노래를 불러 댔다. 그리고 이튿날이면 「모닝 포스트」의 사교계 동정란에 다음과 같은 취지의 기사 한 토막이 실리는 것이었다.\n\n\"어제 크롤리 대령 부부는 메이 페어의 저택에서 엄선된 손님들을 모시고 만찬을 베풀었다. 페테르바라딘 대공 및 대공비 전하, 터키 대사인 파푸시 파샤 각하(공사관 통역관인 키봅 베이 동행), 스테인 후작, 사우스다운 백작, 피트 크롤리 경 부부, 왜그 씨 등이 참석했다. 만찬 뒤에 크롤리 부인은 모임을 열었으니, 스틸턴 공작 미망인, 그뤼예르 공작, 체셔 후작 부인, 알레산드로 스트라키노 후작, 브리 백작, 샵추거 남작, 토스티 기사, 슬링스톤 백작 부인, 그리고 F. 매캐덤 부인, 매캐덤 소장 부부, 그리고 (두 분의) 매베스 양들, 패딩턴 자작, 호러스 포기 경, 샌즈 베드윈 각하, 보바치 바하더 등이 참석했다.\" 그리고 등등의 내역이 빽빽한 작은 활자로 열 몇 줄 더 이어졌으니, 독자께서는 마음껏 채워 넣으시기 바란다.\n\n명사들과 교제하는 자리에서도 우리의 사랑스러운 벗은, 신분 낮은 이들과 거래할 때 보여 주던 것과 똑같은 솔직함을 드러냈다. 한번은 아주 으리으리한 저택에 갔을 때, 리베카가 (어쩌면 좀 과시하는 투로) 그 나라의 이름난 테너 가수와 프랑스어로 대화를 나누고 있었는데, 그리젤 맥베스 부인이 그 어깨 너머로 두 사람을 노려보고 있었다.\n\n\"프랑스어를 참 잘하시는군요.\" 그리젤 부인이 말했다. 정작 그녀 자신은 그 언어를 듣기에 더없이 별난 에든버러 억양으로 구사했다.\n\n\"마땅히 알아야지요.\" 베키는 두 눈을 내리깔며 겸손하게 말했다. \"제가 학교에서 프랑스어를 가르쳤거든요. 게다가 어머니가 프랑스 분이셨고요.\"\n\n그리젤 부인은 그녀의 겸손함에 마음이 사로잡혀, 이 자그마한 여인에게 누그러졌다. 부인은 온갖 계층의 사람들을 윗사람들의 모임에 끼워 주는, 이 시대의 그 고약한 평준화 풍조를 개탄했지만, 그래도 적어도 이 여자만큼은 처신이 발라서 제 분수를 잊는 법이 없다고 마님은 인정했다. 그녀는 참으로 선량한 부인이었다. 가난한 이들에게 너그럽고, 어리석고, 흠잡을 데 없고, 남을 의심할 줄 모르는 분이었다. 자신이 그대나 나보다 더 낫다고 여기는 것이 그 마님의 잘못은 아니다. 그녀의 조상들이 입던 옷자락은 수백 년 동안 입맞춤을 받아 왔다. 사람들 말로는, 그 가문의 위대한 시조가 스코틀랜드의 왕이 되었을 때 작고한 던컨의 영주들과 고문들이 그 가문 수장의 타탄 격자무늬에 입을 맞춘 지가 천 년이나 되었다고 한다.\n\n음악 소동이 있고 난 뒤, 스테인 부인은 베키 앞에 무릎을 꿇었고, 어쩌면 그녀에게 호의가 없지도 않았다. 곤트 가문의 젊은 부인들도 어쩔 수 없이 굴복하고 말았다. 한두 번 사람을 시켜 그녀에게 시비를 걸어 보았으나 실패로 돌아갔다. 눈부신 스터닝턴 부인이 그녀와 한판 설전을 벌여 보았으나, 겁 없는 자그마한 베키에게 처참하게 패하고 말았다. 이따금 공격을 받을 때면, 베키는 새침하고 천진한 처녀의 태도를 취하는 재간이 있었는데, 바로 그 태도 아래에 있을 때 그녀가 가장 위험했다. 이런 기분일 때면 그녀는 더없이 단순하고 꾸밈없는 태도로 가장 짓궂은 말을 내뱉고는, 짐짓 천연덕스럽게 자기 실언을 사과하여 온 세상이 그녀가 그런 말을 했음을 알게 만드는 것이었다.\n\n이름난 재사이자 스테인 경에게 빌붙어 식객 노릇을 하는 왜그 씨가 숙녀들의 사주를 받아 그녀를 공격하게 되었다. 이 가상한 친구는 자기 후원자인 마님들을 곁눈질하며 \"자, 이제 재미난 구경거리를 기대하시라\"는 듯 한쪽 눈을 찡긋해 보이고는, 어느 날 저녁 아무것도 모른 채 식사를 하고 있던 베키를 향해 공격을 개시했다. 느닷없이 공격을 당하긴 했으나 결코 무방비 상태인 적이 없던 이 자그마한 여인은, 순식간에 눈을 번뜩이며 받아넘기더니, 정통으로 찌르는 한 방으로 되받아쳐 왜그의 얼굴을 수치심에 화끈거리게 만들었다. 그러고는 더없이 완벽하게 침착한 태도로, 얼굴에 잔잔한 미소를 띤 채 다시 수프로 돌아갔다. 왜그에게 만찬을 베풀고 이따금 약간의 돈도 꾸어 주며, 왜그가 선거며 신문이며 온갖 잡일을 도맡아 처리해 주던 그 위대한 후원자는, 그 가련한 친구에게 두 눈으로 어찌나 사나운 눈초리를 던졌던지 그가 식탁 아래로 꺼지듯 주저앉아 울음을 터뜨릴 지경이었다. 그는 가련한 눈길로 후작을 바라보았으나 후작은 만찬 내내 그에게 말 한마디 건네지 않았고, 숙녀들을 바라보았으나 그들도 그를 모르는 체했다. 마침내 베키 자신이 그를 가엾이 여겨 대화에 끌어들이려 애썼다. 그는 이후 여섯 주 동안 만찬에 다시 초대받지 못했다. 그리고 왜그가 자연히 온갖 비위를 맞추던 후작의 심복 피슈는, 만일 그가 다시 한번 감히 크롤리 부인에게 무례한 말을 하거나 그녀를 자신의 어리석은 농담거리로 삼는다면, 후작 나리께서 그의 약속어음을 죄다 변호사 손에 넘겨 인정사정없이 그를 파산시켜 버리겠노라고 전하라는 분부를 받았다. 왜그는 피슈 앞에서 눈물을 흘리며, 친애하는 벗이여 부디 자신을 위해 중재해 달라고 애원했다. 그는 R. C. 부인을 찬양하는 시 한 편을 지었고, 그것은 그가 주관하던 「허럼스캐럼 매거진」 바로 다음 호에 실렸다. 그는 그녀를 만나는 모임마다 그녀의 호의를 간청했다. 그는 클럽에서 로던에게 굽실거리며 비위를 맞추었다. 얼마 지나서 그는 곤트 저택에 다시 드나드는 것을 허락받았다. 베키는 늘 그에게 다정했고, 늘 즐거워했으며, 결코 화를 내는 법이 없었다.\n\n후작의 재상이자 으뜸가는 심복(국회와 만찬 식탁에 다 자리를 가진)인 웬엄 씨는 왜그 씨보다 처신도, 견해도 훨씬 더 신중했다. 그가 졸부라면 모조리 미워할 마음이 제아무리 굴뚝같았다 한들(웬엄 씨 자신은 골수 보수 토리당원이었고, 그의 아버지는 잉글랜드 북부의 자그마한 석탄 상인이었다), 이 후작의 부관은 새 총신에게 결코 어떤 적의도 드러내는 법이 없었으며, 오히려 은밀한 친절과, 어딘가 음흉하면서도 깍듯한 정중함으로 그녀를 대했으니, 그것이 다른 이들의 노골적인 적의보다 어쩐지 더 베키를 불안하게 만드는 것이었다.\n\n크롤리 부부가 상류 사회를 대접하던 그 향응에 쏟아부은 돈을 어디서 마련했는가 하는 것은 하나의 수수께끼였고, 당시 적잖은 입방아에 올라, 아마도 이 자그마한 잔치에 흥미를 한층 돋우어 주었을 것이다. 어떤 이들은 피트 크롤리 경이 동생에게 두둑한 생활비를 대 준다고 단언했다. 만일 그랬다면, 베키가 준남작에게 휘두른 위력은 정말이지 비상했을 것이고, 그의 성품도 노년에 이르러 크게 달라졌을 것이다. 또 다른 이들은 베키가 남편의 친구들 모두에게서 돈을 거두어들이는 버릇이 있다고 넌지시 일렀다. 이 사람에게는 눈물을 흘리며 집에 차압이 들어왔다고 둘러대며 찾아가고, 저 사람에게는 무릎을 꿇고서 이러이러한 어음을 갚지 못하면 온 가족이 감옥에 가거나 자살할 수밖에 없다고 하소연한다는 것이었다. 들리는 말로는 사우스다운 경이 이런 가련한 호소에 넘어가 수백 파운드를 내준 적이 한두 번이 아니라고 했다. 제××용기병대의 펠섬 도련님(군모와 군장을 만드는 타일러 앤드 펠섬 상회의 아들이었다)도, 크롤리 부부가 사교계에 끌어들인 인물인데, 금전 면에서 베키의 희생양 가운데 하나로 거론되었다. 사람들은 그녀가 정부의 비밀스러운 자리를 알선해 주겠다는 핑계로, 마음씨 단순한 여러 사람에게서 돈을 우려냈다고 단언했다. 우리의 사랑스럽고 순진한 벗에 대해 무슨 이야기가 떠돌았는지, 또 떠돌지 않았는지 누가 알겠는가? 다만 확실한 것은, 그녀가 구걸했다거나 빌렸다거나 훔쳤다고 일컬어지는 그 돈을 죄다 가지고 있었다면, 능히 그것을 밑천 삼아 평생 정직하게 살 수도 있었으리라는 점이다. 그런데도 실상은 그렇지 않았으니, 그러나 이는 이야기를 너무 앞질러 가는 것이다.\n\n진실인즉슨, 알뜰한 살림과 빈틈없는 운영으로, 즉 현금을 아껴 쓰고 누구에게도 좀처럼 갚지 않음으로써, 사람은 적어도 한동안은 쥐꼬리만 한 재산으로도 거창한 겉치레를 꾸려 갈 수 있는 법이다. 그리고 우리가 믿기로는, 그토록 입방아에 오르내린 베키의 모임도, 따지고 보면 그리 잦았던 것도 아니지만, 이 부인에게는 그 벽을 환히 밝힌 양초값보다 그리 많은 비용이 들지 않았다. 스틸브룩과 퀸스 크롤리에서는 그녀에게 사냥한 고기와 과일을 넉넉히 보내 주었다. 스테인 경의 술 곳간은 그녀가 마음대로 쓸 수 있었고, 그 훌륭한 귀족의 이름난 요리사들이 그녀의 작은 부엌을 주관하거나, 후작의 분부를 받아 자기네 부엌에서 진귀한 산해진미를 보내 주었다. 단언하건대 세상이 이렇듯 순박한 사람을 두고, 그 시절 사람들이 베키를 두고 그러하듯 헐뜯어 대는 것은 참으로 부끄러운 일이며, 나는 그녀에 대한 이야기를 십분의 일이라도 믿지 말라고 대중에게 경고하는 바이다. 만일 빚을 지고 갚지 못하는 사람을 죄다 사교계에서 추방해야 한다면, 만일 우리가 모든 사람의 사생활을 들여다보고 그들의 수입을 따져 보며 그들의 씀씀이가 마음에 들지 않으면 인연을 끊어야 한다면, 아니, 그렇다면 허영의 시장은 얼마나 울부짖는 황야요 견디기 힘든 거처가 되겠는가! 그렇게 된다면, 친애하는 선생, 너 나 할 것 없이 서로 이웃에게 손을 치켜들고 맞설 것이며, 문명의 혜택은 깡그리 사라지고 말 것이다. 우리는 서로 다투고, 헐뜯고, 피하게 될 것이다. 우리 집은 동굴이 될 것이고, 누구 하나 거들떠보지 않으니 우리는 누더기를 걸치고 다니게 될 것이다. 집세는 떨어질 것이다. 모임은 더 이상 열리지 않을 것이다. 온 도시의 상인들이 파산할 것이다. 와인이며, 양초며, 식료품이며, 연지며, 크리놀린 페티코트며, 다이아몬드며, 가발이며, 루이 14세풍 자질구레한 장식품이며, 골동 자기며, 공원에서 타는 승용마며, 멋들어지게 발을 높이 차올리는 마차용 말이며—거듭 말하건대 인생의 온갖 즐거움이 죄다 결딴나고 말 것이다. 만일 사람들이 정말로 그 어리석은 원칙대로 행동하여 자기가 싫어하고 헐뜯는 사람들을 피한다면 말이다. 반면에, 약간의 너그러움과 서로 간의 너그러운 인내만 있으면, 만사가 제법 즐겁게 굴러가는 법이다. 우리는 누군가를 얼마든지 헐뜯고, 교수대를 면한 가장 큰 악당이라 욕할 수도 있지만, 그렇다고 그를 목매달기를 바라는가? 천만에. 우리는 만나면 악수를 한다. 그 집 요리사 솜씨가 좋으면 우리는 그를 용서하고 그 집에 가서 식사를 하며, 그도 우리에게 똑같이 해 주리라 기대한다. 이렇게 장사가 번창하고, 문명이 발전하며, 평화가 유지되고, 매주 새 무도회를 위해 새 옷이 필요해지며, 작년에 빚은 라피트 포도주는 그것을 길러 낸 정직한 주인에게 보답을 안겨 주는 것이다.\n\n우리가 이 글을 쓰는 이 시절, 비록 위대한 조지가 왕좌에 앉아 있었고 숙녀들이 지금 실제로 유행하는 그 단순한 소매와 사랑스러운 화관 대신 양다리 모양 소매를 달고 머리에는 거북딱지로 만든 부삽 같은 큼직한 빗을 꽂고 다니던 시절이긴 했으나, 더없이 고상한 상류 사회의 풍속은, 내가 보기에 오늘날과 본질적으로 다르지 않았다. 그리고 그들의 오락도 자못 비슷했다. 바깥에서 경찰관의 어깨너머로, 궁정이나 무도회로 들어가는 그 황홀한 미인들을 바라보는 우리에게는, 그들이 이 세상 것이 아닌 광채를 두른 존재요, 우리로서는 다다를 수 없는 더없이 황홀한 행복을 누리는 존재로 보일지도 모른다. 바로 이렇듯 불만에 찬 몇몇 사람을 위로하고자, 우리는 우리의 사랑스러운 베키의 분투와 승리와 좌절을 이야기하는 것이니, 실로 그녀도 다른 모든 잘난 사람들과 마찬가지로 그 분투와 승리와 좌절을 제 몫만큼 겪었던 것이다.\n\n이즈음 무언극 놀이라는 이 사랑스러운 오락이 프랑스에서 우리에게 건너와, 이 나라에서 자못 크게 유행했다. 그 덕에 우리 가운데 미모를 갖춘 많은 숙녀들은 그 매력을 뽐낼 수 있었고, 재기를 갖춘 더 적은 수의 숙녀들은 그 기지를 펼쳐 보일 수 있었다. 스테인 경은 베키의 부추김을 받아—아마도 베키는 자신이 위의 두 자질을 모두 타고났다고 여겼던 모양이다—곤트 저택에서 이런 자그마한 촌극 몇 가지를 곁들인 여흥을 베풀게 되었으니, 우리는 외람되나마 독자를 이 화려한 모임으로 안내하고자 한다. 다만 서글픈 환영의 마음으로 안내하는 것인데, 이는 우리가 독자를 모시고 갈 행운을 누리게 될 마지막 사교계 여흥 가운데 하나가 될 터이기 때문이다.\n\n곤트 저택의 미술관이라는 그 으리으리한 방의 일부가 무언극 무대로 꾸며졌다. 조지 3세가 왕이던 시절에도 그렇게 쓰인 적이 있었으니, 머리에 분을 뿌리고 분홍 리본을 단 채 이른바 로마식 차림을 한 곤트 후작의 그림이 지금도 전해지는데, 애디슨 씨의 비극 「카토」에서 카토 역을 연기하는 모습이다. 그 연극은 웨일스 공 전하와 오스나브뤼흐 주교, 그리고 윌리엄 헨리 왕자 앞에서 공연되었는데, 그분들도 당시에는 그 배우와 마찬가지로 어린아이들이었다. 그 뒤로 줄곧 다락방에 처박혀 있던 한두 가지 옛 소도구를 끌어내어, 이번 잔치를 위해 새로 손질해 두었다.\n\n당시 우아한 멋쟁이이자 동방 여행가이던 젊은 베드윈 샌즈가 이 여흥의 진행을 맡았다. 그 시절 동방 여행가란 제법 대단한 인물이었으니, 사절판 책까지 펴내고 사막의 천막 아래서 몇 달을 지낸 적이 있는 이 모험심 강한 베드윈은 결코 만만찮은 거물이었다. 그의 책에는 갖가지 동방 의상을 차려입은 샌즈의 그림이 여러 장 실려 있었다. 그리고 그는 더없이 험상궂게 생긴 흑인 시종을 데리고 다녔으니, 꼭 또 한 명의 브라이언 드 부아길베르 같았다. 베드윈과 그의 의상과 흑인은 곤트 저택에서 더없이 값진 수확으로 환영받았다.\n\n그가 첫 번째 무언극의 막을 열었다. 깃털을 어마어마하게 단 터키 장교(예니체리가 아직 존속하는 것으로 여겨지던 시절이라, 타르부시가 아직 그 진실한 신자들의 유서 깊고 위풍당당한 머리 장식을 밀어내지 못한 때였다)가 긴 의자에 비스듬히 누워, 물담배를 빠는 시늉을 하고 있는 모습이 보였다. 다만 숙녀들을 위해 그 안에서는 향긋한 향환만 피우도록 허락되었다. 이 터키 고관이 하품을 하며 권태롭고 무료한 기색을 내비친다. 그가 손뼉을 치자 누비아인 메스루르가 등장하니, 맨팔에 팔찌를 끼고 언월도를 차고 온갖 동방 장신구를 두른, 깡마르고 키 크고 흉측한 모습이다. 그가 아가 나리 앞에 살람 인사를 올린다.\n\n전율과 환희가 좌중을 훑고 지나간다. 숙녀들은 서로 귓속말을 주고받는다. 저 흑인 노예는 어느 이집트 파샤가 마라스키노 술 서른여섯 병과 맞바꾸어 베드윈 샌즈에게 준 것이다. 그는 그동안 숱한 후궁들을 자루에 꿰매 넣어 나일강에 던져 버렸다.\n\n\"노예 상인을 들라 하라.\" 터키의 호색한이 손을 한 번 흔들며 말한다. 메스루르가 노예 상인을 후작 나리 앞으로 데려오는데, 그는 베일을 쓴 여인 하나를 함께 데려온다. 그가 베일을 벗긴다. 박수갈채의 전율이 온 좌중에 터져 나온다. 다름 아닌 윈크워스 부인(처녀 적 이름은 앱설롬 양이었다)으로, 그 아름다운 눈과 머리칼의 소유자였다. 그녀는 화려한 동방 의상을 차려입었으니, 까맣게 땋아 늘인 머리채에는 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 보석이 엮여 있고, 옷에는 금화가 온통 뒤덮여 있다. 저 가증스러운 이슬람교도가 그녀의 미모에 홀딱 반했음을 드러낸다. 그녀가 무릎을 꿇고서, 자신이 태어난 산악으로, 자신의 체르케스 연인이 여태 줄레이카가 없는 것을 한탄하고 있는 그곳으로 돌려보내 달라고 그에게 애원한다. 그러나 어떤 애원으로도 완고한 하산의 마음은 움직이지 않는다. 그는 체르케스 신랑이라는 생각에 코웃음을 친다. 줄레이카는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고는, 더없이 아름다운 절망의 자태로 무너져 내린다. 그녀에게는 도무지 희망이 없어 보이는데, 그때—그때 키슬라르 아가가 등장한다.\n\n키슬라르 아가가 술탄이 보낸 편지를 가져온다. 하산이 그 무시무시한 칙령을 받아 자기 머리에 얹는다. 섬뜩한 공포가 그를 사로잡는 한편, 흑인의 얼굴에는(또다시 다른 의상을 입은 메스루르다) 섬뜩한 기쁨이 떠오른다. \"자비를! 자비를 베푸소서!\" 파샤가 부르짖는다. 그동안 키슬라르 아가는 무섭게 이를 드러내며 활시위 하나를 꺼낸다.\n\n그가 그 무시무시한 무기를 막 쓰려는 찰나에 막이 내린다. 안에서 하산이 큰 소리로 외친다. \"첫 두 음절이오.\" 그러자 이 무언극에 출연하기로 되어 있는 로던 크롤리 부인이 앞으로 나와, 윈크워스 부인에게 그 의상의 빼어난 안목과 아름다움을 칭찬한다.\n\n무언극의 두 번째 부분이 시작된다. 여전히 동방의 한 장면이다. 다른 옷으로 갈아입은 하산이, 이제는 완전히 마음을 푼 줄레이카 곁에 멋진 자세로 서 있다. 키슬라르 아가는 온순한 흑인 노예가 되어 있다. 사막에 해가 떠오르고, 터키인들이 동쪽으로 고개를 돌려 모래에 절을 한다. 단봉낙타가 가까이에 없는 까닭에, 악단이 익살스럽게 「낙타가 온다」를 연주한다. 거대한 이집트인 두상이 그 장면에 등장한다. 그것은 노래하는 두상이라, 동방 여행가들이 놀라워하는 가운데, 왜그 씨가 지은 익살스러운 노래를 부른다. 동방의 나그네들이 「마술 피리」의 파파게노와 무어인 왕처럼 춤을 추며 퇴장한다. \"마지막 두 음절이오.\" 두상이 우렁차게 외친다.\n\n마지막 막이 열린다. 이번에는 그리스풍 천막이다. 키가 크고 건장한 사내가 그 안의 침상에 누워 있다. 그의 머리 위에는 투구와 방패가 걸려 있다. 이제는 그것들이 필요 없다. 일리움은 함락되었다. 이피게네이아는 살해되었다. 카산드라는 그의 바깥 홀에 포로로 잡혀 있다. 인간들의 왕(다름 아닌 크롤리 대령으로, 실은 일리움의 약탈에 대해서도 카산드라의 정복에 대해서도 아무것도 모른다), 그 아낙스 안드론이 아르고스의 침실에서 잠들어 있다. 등불 하나가 잠든 전사의 널따란 그림자를 벽 위에 일렁이게 비추고, 트로이의 검과 방패가 그 불빛에 번득인다. 악단이 석상이 들어오기 전 「돈 후안」의 그 무시무시한 곡을 연주한다.\n\n아이기스토스가 창백한 얼굴로 발끝을 세워 살그머니 들어온다. 휘장 뒤에서 음험하게 그의 뒤를 내다보는 저 섬뜩한 얼굴은 무엇인가? 그가 잠든 자를 찌르려고 단검을 치켜드는데, 잠자던 자가 침상에서 몸을 뒤척이며 마치 그 일격을 받겠다는 듯 떡 벌어진 가슴을 드러낸다. 그는 차마 고귀하게 잠든 그 족장을 찌르지 못한다. 클리타임네스트라가 유령처럼 재빨리 방 안으로 미끄러져 들어온다. 그녀의 두 팔은 맨살에 새하얗고, 적갈색 머리채가 어깨 위로 흘러내리고, 얼굴은 죽은 듯이 창백하며, 두 눈은 어찌나 섬뜩한 미소로 빛나는지 보는 이들이 부르르 떤다.\n\n전율이 온 좌중을 훑고 지나간다. \"맙소사!\" 누군가가 말했다. \"로던 크롤리 부인이잖아.\"\n\n그녀는 경멸하는 태도로 아이기스토스의 손에서 단검을 낚아채 침상으로 다가간다. 그 단검이 등불의 희미한 빛 속에서 그녀의 머리 위로 번득이는 것이 보이는데—그러다 등불이 신음 소리와 함께 꺼지고, 사방이 캄캄해진다.\n\n그 어둠과 그 장면이 사람들을 겁에 질리게 했다. 리베카가 자기 역을 어찌나 잘, 그리고 어찌나 섬뜩하리만치 실감 나게 해냈던지, 구경꾼들은 모두 말문이 막혀 버렸다. 그러다 와락 홀의 등불이 죄다 다시 환히 타오르자, 비로소 모두가 갈채를 보내기 시작했다. \"브라바! 브라바!\" 늙은 스테인의 새된 목소리가 다른 모든 소리를 누르고 우렁차게 울려 퍼졌다. \"맹세코, 저 여자라면 정말 해치우고도 남을걸.\" 그가 이를 악물고 중얼거렸다. 출연자들이 온 좌중의 부름을 받았으니, 홀에는 \"진행자! 클리타임네스트라!\" 하는 외침이 울려 퍼졌다. 아가멤논은 그 고전풍 짧은 옷차림으로 도무지 모습을 드러내려 하지 않고, 아이기스토스며 그 짧은 연극의 다른 출연자들과 함께 뒤편에 서 있었다. 베드윈 샌즈 씨가 줄레이카와 클리타임네스트라를 데리고 나왔다. 어느 고귀한 분이 매혹적인 클리타임네스트라에게 자신을 소개해 달라고 우겼다. \"어허? 칼로 푹 찔러 그 몸뚱이를 꿰뚫겠다는 거지. 그러고 나서 딴 남자랑 결혼하겠다, 응?\" 전하께서 이렇게 딱 들어맞는 말씀을 하셨다.\n\n\"로던 크롤리 부인은 그 역에 아주 기막히게 어울리더군요.\" 스테인 경이 말했다. 베키는 명랑하고 발랄한 표정으로 웃으며, 여태껏 본 적 없는 가장 어여쁜 무릎 인사로 좌중을 휩쓸었다.\n\n하인들이 갖가지 시원한 진미를 담은 큰 쟁반을 들여왔고, 출연자들은 두 번째 무언극 활인화를 준비하러 사라졌다.\n\n이 무언극의 세 음절은 무언의 몸짓으로 묘사하기로 되어 있었고, 공연은 다음과 같은 식으로 펼쳐졌다.\n\n첫째 음절. C.B. 훈장을 단 로던 크롤리 대령이 챙이 늘어진 모자를 쓰고 지팡이와 외투를 갖춘 채, 마구간에서 빌려 온 등불을 들고 무대를 가로질러 가며, 주민들에게 시각을 알리듯 큰 소리로 외친다. 아래층 창문 안으로는 행상인 둘이 크리비지 놀이를 하는 듯 보이는데, 그 위에서 연신 하품을 해 댄다. 그들에게 구두닦이 보츠처럼 보이는 자(G. 링우드 각하)가 들어오니, 이 도련님이 그 역할을 완벽하게 해내며, 그들의 아랫도리를 벗겨 간다. 이윽고 객실 하녀(사우스다운 경 각하)가 촛대 둘과 발 데우는 냄비를 들고 등장한다. 그녀가 위층 방으로 올라가 침대를 데운다. 그녀는 그 발 데우는 냄비를 무기 삼아 행상인들이 집적거리는 것을 막아 낸다. 그녀가 퇴장한다. 그들은 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자를 쓰고 차양을 내린다. 보츠가 나와 일 층 방의 덧문을 닫는다. 안에서 그가 문에 빗장을 지르고 쇠사슬을 거는 소리가 들린다. 모든 불이 꺼진다. 악단이 「잠드소서, 잠드소서, 사랑하는 이들이여」를 연주한다. 막 뒤에서 한 목소리가 말한다. \"첫째 음절이오.\"\n\n둘째 음절. 갑자기 등불이 모두 환하게 켜진다. 악단이 「파리의 요한」에 나오는 그 옛 가락 「아, 여행하는 즐거움이여」를 연주한다. 같은 장면이다. 그려진 그 집의 일 층과 이 층 사이에는, 스테인 가문의 문장이 그려진 간판이 보인다. 집 안 곳곳에서 종이 울려 댄다. 아래층 방에서는 한 남자가 긴 종잇조각을 또 다른 남자에게 내미는 것이 보이는데, 그 남자는 주먹을 흔들며 으르고 이건 말도 안 된다고 펄펄 뛴다. \"마부 양반, 내 이륜마차를 돌려 오게.\" 또 다른 남자가 문간에서 외친다. 그가 객실 하녀(사우스다운 경 각하)의 턱을 쓰다듬는다. 그녀는 칼립소가 또 다른 그 이름난 여행가 율리시스의 부재를 한탄했듯, 그가 떠나는 것을 애석해하는 듯하다. 보츠(G. 링우드 각하)가 은제 큰 술병이 든 나무 상자를 들고 지나가며 \"주전자요\" 하고 어찌나 절묘하게 익살스럽고 자연스럽게 외쳐 대는지, 온 좌중이 박수갈채로 울리고 그에게 꽃다발이 던져진다. 채찍이 짝, 짝, 짝 소리를 낸다. 주인장과 객실 하녀와 급사가 문간으로 달려가는데, 어느 귀하신 손님이 막 당도하려는 바로 그 순간 막이 닫히고, 보이지 않는 연극 진행자가 외친다. \"둘째 음절이오.\"\n\n\"내 생각엔 '호텔'인 것 같은데.\" 근위 기병대의 그리그 대위가 말한다. 대위의 영민함에 좌중에 한바탕 웃음이 인다. 그는 정답에서 그리 멀지 않다.\n\n셋째 음절이 준비되는 동안, 악단이 뱃노래 모음곡을 연주하기 시작한다. 「다운스의 모든 이」, 「거센 북풍이여 그쳐라」, 「룰 브리타니아」, 「오, 비스케이만에서!」—무언가 바다와 관련된 사건이 벌어지려는 참이다. 막이 옆으로 젖혀지면서 종소리가 들린다. \"자, 여러분, 뭍으로 갑시다!\" 한 목소리가 외친다. 사람들이 서로 작별 인사를 나눈다. 그들은 검은 막으로 표현된 구름을 향하듯 불안하게 손가락질을 하며, 두려운 듯 고개를 끄덕인다. 스퀴엄스 부인(사우스다운 경 각하)이 자기 애완견과 가방들과 손가방들과 남편과 함께 자리에 앉아, 밧줄을 붙들고 매달린다. 분명 배다.\n\n선장(C.B. 훈장의 크롤리 대령)이 삼각모를 쓰고 망원경을 든 채, 모자를 손으로 머리에 누르며 들어와 밖을 살핀다. 그의 외투 자락이 바람에 날리듯 펄럭인다. 그가 망원경을 쓰려고 모자에서 손을 떼자, 그의 모자가 날아가 버려 어마어마한 갈채가 쏟아진다. 바람이 거세게 분다. 음악이 점점 높아지며 점점 더 크게 휘파람 소리를 내고, 선원들이 마치 배가 심하게 요동치는 듯 비틀거리며 무대를 가로지른다. 사환(G. 링우드 각하)이 대야 여섯 개를 든 채 비틀비틀 지나간다. 그가 그중 하나를 잽싸게 스퀴엄스 경 곁에 놓는다. 스퀴엄스 부인은 자기 개를 꼬집어 그 개가 가련하게 울부짖기 시작하자, 손수건을 얼굴에 갖다 대고 선실로 가듯 황급히 달려 나간다. 음악이 폭풍처럼 격앙된 가장 격렬한 절정으로 치솟고, 셋째 음절이 막을 내린다.\n\n「르 로시뇰」이라는 짤막한 발레가 있었으니, 그 시절 몽테쉬와 노블레가 이것으로 이름을 떨치곤 했는데, 왜그 씨가 이를 영국 무대에 오페라로 옮겨 와, 솜씨 좋은 작사가이던 그가 그 발레의 어여쁜 가락에 자기 노랫말을 붙였다. 그것은 옛 프랑스풍 의상을 차려입고 공연되었으니, 이제 자그마한 사우스다운 경이 흠잡을 데 없이 구부정한 지팡이를 짚고 무대를 절뚝거리며 돌아다니는 노파로 감쪽같이 분장하여 등장했다.\n\n낭랑한 가락이 무대 뒤에서 들려오니, 장미와 격자 세공으로 뒤덮인 어여쁜 판지 오두막에서 흘러나온 것이었다. \"필로멜, 필로멜!\" 노파가 외치자, 필로멜이 나온다.\n\n또다시 갈채가 터진다. 다름 아닌 로던 크롤리 부인으로, 머리에 분을 뿌리고 애교점을 찍은, 이 세상에서 가장 매혹적인 자그마한 후작 부인이다.\n\n그녀는 웃으며 콧노래를 흥얼거리며 들어와, 연극 속 젊음의 온갖 천진함을 담아 무대 위를 깡충깡충 뛰어다닌다. 그녀가 무릎 인사를 한다. 엄마가 말한다. \"아니, 얘야, 너는 어쩌면 늘 웃고 노래만 부르는구나.\" 그러자 그녀가 이런 노래와 함께 뛰쳐나간다.\n\n# THE ROSE UPON MY BALCONY\n\n내 발코니의 장미\n내 발코니의 장미, 아침 공기를 향기롭게 적시는 그 꽃은 겨우내 잎 하나 없이 봄을 애타게 그리워했지요. 그대는 묻네요, 어찌하여 그 숨결이 달콤하고 그 뺨이 활짝 피었는지를. 그것은 해가 떠오르고 새들이 노래하기 시작한 까닭이랍니다.\n\n푸른 숲을 가로질러 그 가락이 울려 퍼지는 저 나이팅게일은 가지가 헐벗고 바람이 매섭게 불 적엔 말없이 잠잠했지요. 엄마, 그가 노래하는 까닭을 제게 물으신다면, 그것은 해가 떠오르고 온 잎새가 푸르른 까닭이랍니다.\n\n이렇듯 저마다 제 몫을 다하지요, 엄마, 새들은 제 목소리를 찾았고, 활짝 핀 장미는, 엄마, 그 고운 뺨을 물들일 홍조를 얻었답니다. 그리고 제 마음속에도, 엄마, 햇살이 있어 깨어나 기뻐하니, 그래서 저는 노래하고 얼굴을 붉히지요, 엄마, 바로 그것이 그 까닭이랍니다.\n\n이 노래의 절과 절 사이사이에, 노래하는 이에게서 엄마라고 불리는 그 마음씨 좋은 인물은—그 모자 아래로 커다란 구레나룻이 비어져 나와 있었는데—딸 역을 맡은 그 천진한 존재를 끌어안아 자기 모정을 한껏 드러내 보이고 싶어 안달이 난 듯했다. 그 다정한 손길 하나하나가 공감하는 관객들의 요란한 웃음 갈채로 맞아들여졌다. 노래가 끝나자(악단이 마치 무수한 새들이 지저귀듯 교향곡을 연주하는 동안) 온 좌중이 한목소리로 앙코르를 외쳤다. 그리고 그날 저녁의 나이팅게일에게는 갈채와 끝없는 꽃다발이 쏟아졌다. 스테인 경의 갈채하는 목소리가 그 모든 것 가운데 가장 우렁찼다. 나이팅게일인 베키는 그가 던져 준 꽃을 받아, 노련한 희극 배우다운 태도로 그것을 가슴에 꼭 껴안았다. 스테인 경은 기쁨에 미칠 지경이었다. 손님들의 열광이 그 자신의 열광과 어우러졌다. 첫 번째 무언극에서 그 등장만으로 그토록 큰 환희를 자아냈던, 그 아름답고 검은 눈의 천국 미녀는 어디로 갔는가? 그녀는 베키보다 갑절은 어여뻤건만, 베키의 광채가 그녀를 완전히 가려 버린 것이었다. 모든 목소리가 그녀를 향했다. 스티븐스, 카라도리, 론치 데 베니스—사람들은 그녀를 이 사람 저 사람에 견주어 보았고, 어쩌면 합당한 근거로, 그녀가 만일 여배우였다면 무대 위 그 누구도 그녀를 능가하지 못했으리라는 데 의견을 같이했다. 그녀는 자기 절정에 다다른 것이었다. 그녀의 목소리는 갈채의 폭풍 위로 낭랑하고 밝게 솟아올라, 그녀의 승리만큼이나 드높고 즐겁게 날아올랐다. 연극 여흥이 끝난 뒤에는 무도회가 있었고, 모두가 그날 저녁의 가장 큰 관심의 표적인 베키 주위로 몰려들었다. 그 왕족 인사는 그녀가 완벽 그 자체라고 맹세코 단언하며, 거듭거듭 그녀와 이야기를 나누었다. 이런 영예에 자그마한 베키의 영혼은 자긍심과 환희로 부풀어 올랐다. 그녀는 행운과 명성과 영화가 자기 앞에 펼쳐진 것을 보았다. 스테인 경은 그녀의 노예가 되어, 어디든 그녀를 따라다니며 그 방에서 그녀 말고는 거의 아무에게도 말을 건네지 않았고, 더없이 눈에 띄게 그녀에게 찬사와 관심을 바쳤다. 그녀는 여전히 후작 부인 의상 차림으로, 자보티에르 공작의 부속 직원인 트뤼피니 씨와 미뉴에트를 추었다. 그리고 옛 궁정의 온갖 전통을 다 익힌 그 공작은, 크롤리 부인이 베스트리스의 제자였어도 손색이 없을 만하며 베르사유에서 한자리를 차지했어도 좋았으리라고 단언했다. 다만 위엄을 지켜야 한다는 마음과 통풍, 그리고 더없이 강한 의무감과 자기희생의 정신만 아니었더라면, 각하께서는 친히 그녀와 춤을 추었을 것이다. 그리고 그는 크롤리 부인처럼 말하고 춤출 줄 아는 숙녀라면 유럽 어느 궁정의 대사 부인이 되어도 손색이 없으리라고 사람들 앞에서 단언했다. 그가 위안을 얻은 것은 그녀가 출생으로 보아 반은 프랑스 여인이라는 말을 듣고서야였다. \"동포가 아니고서야,\" 각하께서 단언하기를, \"그토록 위엄 있는 춤을 그런 식으로 추어 낼 수는 없는 노릇이지요.\"\n\n그러고 나서 그녀는 페테르바라딘 대공의 사촌이자 부속 직원인 클링겐슈포어 씨와 함께 왈츠를 추었다. 기뻐 어쩔 줄 모르던 대공은, 프랑스인 외교 동료보다 절제력이 부족했던지라, 그 매혹적인 여인과 한바탕 춤을 추겠다고 우기더니, 그녀와 함께 무도회장을 빙글빙글 돌며 부츠 술과 경기병 재킷에서 다이아몬드를 흩뿌려 댔으니, 마침내 전하께서는 그야말로 숨이 턱에 차고 말았다. 파푸시 파샤 본인도, 만일 춤이라는 것이 자기 나라의 풍습이었더라면 그녀와 춤추고 싶어 했을 것이다. 좌중이 그녀를 둘러싸고 원을 그리며, 마치 그녀가 노블레나 탈리오니라도 되는 양 미친 듯이 갈채를 보냈다. 모두가 황홀경에 빠졌으니, 베키 역시 그러했음은 더 말할 나위가 없다. 그녀는 경멸하는 눈길로 스터닝턴 부인 곁을 지나쳤다. 그녀는 곤트 부인과 그 어리둥절하고 굴욕에 찬 시누이에게 짐짓 너그러운 태도를 베풀었다. 그녀는 경쟁하는 모든 미녀들을 짓뭉개 버렸다. 가엾은 윈크워스 부인으로 말하자면, 그날 저녁 첫머리에 그토록 큰 반향을 일으켰던 그 긴 머리채와 큰 눈을 가진 그녀는 이제 어디 있는가? 이 경주에서는 어디에도 없었다. 그녀는 그 긴 머리채를 쥐어뜯고 그 큰 눈에서 눈물을 쏟을 수도 있었으련만, 그 낭패를 거들떠보거나 애석해할 사람은 하나도 없었다.\n\n무엇보다 가장 큰 승리는 야식 시간에 찾아왔다. 그녀는 앞서 말한 그 고귀한 인물인 왕족 전하와 그 밖의 귀한 손님들과 더불어, 더없이 특별한 상석에 자리를 잡았다. 그녀는 금접시에 음식을 받았다. 만일 그녀가 원했다면 또 한 명의 클레오파트라처럼 진주를 녹여 자기 샴페인에 타 마실 수도 있었을 것이며, 페테르바라딘의 그 군주는 저 눈부신 눈에서 다정한 눈길 한 번을 받기 위해서라면 자기 재킷에 달린 보석의 절반이라도 내주었을 것이다. 자보티에르는 본국 정부에 그녀에 대한 보고문을 써 보냈다. 다른 식탁에서 한낱 은접시에 야식을 들며 스테인 경이 그녀에게 끊임없이 관심을 쏟는 것을 지켜보던 숙녀들은, 그것이야말로 괴이쩍은 홀림이요, 지체 높은 숙녀들에 대한 심한 모욕이라고 입을 모았다. 만일 비아냥으로 사람을 죽일 수 있었다면, 스터닝턴 부인은 그 자리에서 당장 그녀를 죽여 버렸을 것이다.\n\n로던 크롤리는 이런 승리들에 겁이 났다. 그것들은 어쩐지 그 어느 때보다도 더 자기 아내를 자기에게서 멀리 떼어 놓는 것만 같았다. 그는 그녀가 자기보다 얼마나 헤아릴 수 없이 뛰어난 사람인지를 생각하니, 고통에 가까운 심정이 되었다.\n\n떠날 시간이 되자, 한 무리의 젊은이들이 그녀를 따라 그녀의 마차까지 갔으니, 그 마차를 두고 밖에서 사람들이 큰 소리로 불러 댔고, 그 외침을 곤트 저택의 키 높은 대문 밖에 진을 친 횃불 든 사내들이 받아 이어 갔다. 그들은 대문에서 나오는 사람마다 축하의 말을 건네며, 그 나리께서 이 고귀한 모임을 즐거이 누리셨기를 바란다고 했다.\n\n한바탕 큰 소리로 부른 끝에 대문으로 다가온 로던 크롤리 부인의 마차가 환히 밝혀진 안마당으로 덜컹거리며 들어와, 지붕 덮인 통로 앞에 와 멈춰 섰다. 로던이 아내를 마차에 태웠고, 마차는 떠나갔다. 웬엄 씨가 그에게 걸어서 집에 가자고 청하며, 대령에게 시가 한 대로 입가심을 하자고 권했다.\n\n그들은 밖에 늘어선 그 많은 횃불 든 소년들 가운데 하나의 등불에 시가를 붙였고, 로던은 벗인 웬엄과 더불어 걸어갔다. 두 사람이 무리에서 떨어져 나와 그 두 신사의 뒤를 따랐다. 그리고 그들이 곤트 광장을 몇십 걸음쯤 걸어 내려갔을 때, 그 사내들 가운데 하나가 다가와 로던의 어깨를 툭 치며 말했다. \"실례합니다, 대령님, 긴히 드릴 말씀이 있습니다요.\" 이 사내가 말하는 동안 그 일행이 큰 소리로 휘파람을 불자, 그 신호에 곤트 저택 대문 앞에 늘어서 있던 마차들 가운데 한 대가 덜컹거리며 달려왔다. 그러자 그 부관이 빙 돌아 크롤리 대령 앞을 가로막아 섰다.\n\n그 용감한 장교는 자신에게 무슨 일이 닥쳤는지 단번에 알아차렸다. 그는 집행관들의 손아귀에 들어간 것이었다. 그는 흠칫 물러서다가, 맨 처음 자기를 건드렸던 그 사내에게 부딪혔다.\n\n\"우리는 셋이올시다요, 달아나 봤자 소용없습니다요.\" 뒤에 있던 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"자네 모스로군, 그렇지?\" 대령이 말했다. 그는 상대를 아는 모양이었다. \"얼마인가?\"\n\n\"별것 아닙니다요.\" 챈서리 레인 커시터 거리에 사는, 미들섹스 주장관의 보조 집행관인 모스 씨가 속삭였다. \"백육십육 파운드 육 실링 팔 펜스, 네이선 씨가 거신 소송입니다요.\"\n\n\"백 파운드만 좀 빌려주게, 웬엄, 제발 부탁이네.\" 가엾은 로던이 말했다. \"집에 칠십 파운드가 있으니.\"\n\n\"내겐 이 세상에 십 파운드도 없다네.\" 가엾은 웬엄 씨가 말했다. \"잘 가게, 이 사람아.\"\n\n\"잘 가게.\" 로던이 침울하게 말했다. 그리고 웬엄은 걸어가 버렸다. 그리고 로던 크롤리는 마차가 템플 바 아래로 달려 들어가는 동안 시가를 마저 다 피웠다.\n\n스타인 경은 일단 자비를 베풀 마음이 들면 결코 어중간하게 하는 법이 없었으니, 크롤리 가족을 향한 그 친절은 그의 자비로운 안목에 더없는 영예를 안겨 주는 것이었다. 그분은 호의를 어린 로던에게까지 넓혔다. 그는 아이의 부모에게, 이 아이를 퍼블릭 스쿨에 보낼 필요가 있다고 일러 주었다. 이제 아이가, 경쟁심이며 라틴어의 기초며 권투 단련이며 또래 친구들과의 교제 따위가 더없이 큰 유익이 될 나이가 되었다는 것이었다. 아버지는 좋은 퍼블릭 스쿨에 아이를 보낼 만큼 넉넉하지 못하다고 반대했고, 어머니는 브리그스가 아이에게 더없이 훌륭한 가정교사라서 (실제로 그러했듯이) 영어와 라틴어 기초와 일반 교양에서 아이를 빼어나게 키워 왔다고 반대했다. 그러나 이 모든 반대도 스타인 후작의 너그러운 끈기 앞에서는 자취를 감추고 말았다. 그분은 화이트프라이어스라는 이름의, 저 유명하고 오래된 학료의 운영 이사 가운데 하나였던 것이다. 그곳은 옛날에는 시토 수도회의 수도원이었으니, 거기에 맞붙은 스미스필드가 마상 창 시합장이던 시절의 일이다. 완고한 이단자들이 바로 그 근처에서 화형당하기에 알맞도록 그곳으로 끌려오곤 했다. 신앙의 수호자 헨리 8세는 그 수도원과 그 재산을 차지하고, 자신의 개혁 보조에 발맞추지 못하는 수도사 몇몇을 목매달고 고문했다. 마침내 어느 큰 상인이 그 저택과 그에 인접한 땅을 사들였고, 그곳에서, 그리고 토지와 금전으로 이루어진 또 다른 부유한 기부의 도움을 받아, 노인과 아이들을 위한 유명한 자선 구호원을 세웠다. 거의 수도원이나 다름없던 그 오래된 시설 둘레로 통학생 학교가 자라났으니, 그곳은 중세풍의 복식과 관습을 그대로 간직한 채 지금도 존속하고 있다. 그리고 모든 시토회 수사들은 그곳이 오래도록 번창하기를 기도하는 것이다.\n\n이 유명한 학교의 운영 이사로는 영국에서 가장 지체 높은 귀족과 고위 성직자와 명사 들이 이름을 올리고 있다. 그리고 학생들은 더없이 안락하게 거처하고 먹고 교육받으며, 이후 대학의 좋은 장학금과 교회의 성직록까지 받게 되니, 어린 신사 여럿이 더없이 여린 시절부터 성직에 바쳐지고, 이 시설의 추천 자리를 따내려는 경쟁이 자못 치열한 것이다. 본래는 가난하나 그럴 만한 자격을 갖춘 성직자와 평신도의 아들들을 위한 것이었으나, 그 시설의 지체 높은 운영 이사들 가운데 다수는 폭넓고도 다분히 변덕스러운 자비심으로 온갖 부류를 골라 그 은혜의 대상으로 삼았다. 거저 교육을 받고, 장차의 생계와 직업까지 보장받는다는 것은 워낙 훌륭한 방책이라, 가장 부유한 이들조차 그것을 마다하지 않았다. 그리하여 지체 높은 이들의 친척뿐 아니라 지체 높은 이들 자신이 그 기회의 덕을 보라고 제 아들을 보냈으니, 존경하올 고위 성직자들은 자기 친족이나 휘하 성직자의 아들을 보냈고, 다른 한편으로는 어떤 큰 귀족들은 제 심복 하인의 자식을 후원하기를 마다하지 않았다. 그리하여 이 시설에 들어온 소년은 어울릴 만한 온갖 부류의 어린 동무를 만날 수 있었던 것이다.\n\n로던 크롤리는 비록 평생 공부한 책이라고는 경마 일정표뿐이었고, 고상한 학문에 관한 기억이라고 해 봐야 젊은 시절 이튼에서 받은 매질과 얽힌 것이 고작이었으나, 영국 신사라면 누구나 품는 그 고전 학문에 대한 점잖고 진솔한 경의만큼은 지니고 있었다. 그래서 제 아들이 어쩌면 평생의 살길과 학자가 될 만한 확실한 기회를 얻으리라 생각하니 기뻤다. 그리고 그 아이는 그에게 가장 큰 위안이자 동무였고, 아내에게는 굳이 말하고 싶지 않은 자잘한 인연 천 갈래로 그를 살뜰히 붙들어 매고 있었으나(아내는 처음부터 줄곧 제 아들에게 더없는 무관심을 보여 왔다), 로던은 곧바로 아이와 떨어지는 데, 어린아이의 안녕을 위해 제 가장 큰 안락과 즐거움을 포기하는 데 동의했다. 그는 아이를 떠나보내야 할 처지가 되고서야 비로소 자기가 그 아이를 얼마나 사랑하는지 깨달았다. 아이가 떠나고 나자 그는 차마 내색하고 싶지 않을 만큼 슬프고 풀이 죽었다. 새로운 인생을 시작하고 또래 동무들을 만나게 되어 마냥 행복해하던 아이 자신보다 훨씬 더 슬펐던 것이다. 대령이 어설프고 두서없는 말투로 아이의 떠남에 대한 감상적 슬픔을 표현해 보려 할 때마다, 베키는 한두 번 까르르 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 가엾은 사내는 제 더없는 즐거움이자 가장 가까운 벗을 빼앗겼다고 느꼈다. 그는 아이가 잠들곤 하던, 자기 화장방의 그 작고 텅 빈 침대를 자주, 그리고 애틋하게 바라보았다. 그는 아침마다 아이가 사무치게 그리웠고, 아이 없이 공원을 거닐어 보려 했으나 헛일이었다. 어린 로던이 떠나고 나서야 그는 자기가 얼마나 외로운지를 알았다. 그는 아이를 아껴 주는 사람들이 좋았고, 마음씨 고운 누이 제인 부인에게 가서 몇 시간이고 함께 앉아, 아이의 미덕이며 잘생긴 얼굴이며 백 가지 좋은 점들에 대해 이야기하곤 했다.\n\n젊은 로던의 큰어머니가 그를 무척 귀애했다는 것은 앞서 말한 바와 같으니, 그 어린 딸도 마찬가지여서 사촌이 떠날 때가 되자 펑펑 눈물을 쏟았다. 형뻘 되는 로던은 모녀의 그 다정함에 고마워했다. 그가 두 사람 앞에서 마음껏 쏟아 낸 그 꾸밈없는 부정(父情)의 토로 속에, 또 그들의 공감에 힘입어, 이 사내의 더없이 선량하고 진솔한 감정이 고스란히 드러났다. 그는 제 아내에게는 보여 줄 수 없었던 그 감정들을 드러냄으로써 제인 부인의 친절뿐 아니라 그 진심 어린 존경까지 얻어 냈다. 두 동서는 되도록 마주치는 일이 드물었다. 베키는 제인의 감상과 무름을 두고 신랄하게 비웃었고, 제인의 친절하고 온화한 천성은 동서의 그 냉담한 처신에 반발하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n이런 일은 로던 자신이 알거나 스스로 인정하는 것 이상으로 그를 아내에게서 멀어지게 했다. 아내는 그 소원해짐을 개의치 않았다. 사실 그녀는 그도, 다른 누구도 그리워하지 않았다. 그녀는 그를 제 심부름꾼이자 비천한 노예쯤으로 여겼다. 그가 아무리 풀이 죽어 있거나 시무룩해 있어도, 그녀는 그 기색을 알아채지 못했고, 알아챈대도 그저 비웃음으로 받아넘길 뿐이었다. 그녀는 제 지위며, 제 즐거움이며, 제 사교계에서의 출세를 궁리하느라 바빴다. 그녀가 그곳에서 대단한 자리를 차지하는 것이 마땅하다는 것은 분명한 일이었다.\n\n아이가 학교에 가져갈 자그마한 짐을 꾸려 준 것은 정직한 브리그스였다. 아이가 떠날 때 가정부 몰리는 복도에서 엉엉 울었으니, 밀린 품삯이 오래도록 쌓여 있는데도 친절하고 충실하기 그지없는 몰리였다. 베키 부인은 아이를 학교에 데려다주는 데 남편이 마차를 쓰도록 허락하지 않았다. 말들을 시내까지 끌고 가다니! 그런 일은 들어 본 적도 없었다. 삯마차나 불러 오라는 것이었다. 그녀는 아이가 떠날 때 입맞춤을 청하지도 않았고, 아이 역시 그녀를 끌어안으려 하지 않았다. 다만 늙은 브리그스에게는 입을 맞추었고(평소에는 그녀를 어루만지기를 몹시 수줍어하던 아이였다), 토요일마다 집에 돌아와 그녀가 자기를 볼 수 있게 되리라고 일러 주며 그녀를 달랬다. 삯마차가 시내를 향해 굴러갈 무렵, 베키의 마차는 덜컹대며 공원으로 달려가고 있었다. 아버지와 아들이 학교의 오래된 정문으로 들어설 때, 그녀는 서펜타인 못가에서 스무 명 남짓한 젊은 멋쟁이들과 재잘대며 웃고 있었다. 로던은 거기에 아이를 남겨 두고 돌아섰으니, 그 마음에는, 어쩌면 이 가엾고 닳고 닳은 사내가 자기 자신이 어린 시절을 벗어난 이래 한 번도 느껴 본 적 없을 만큼, 더 슬프고도 더 순수한 감정이 깃들어 있었다.\n\n그는 집까지 줄곧 더없이 침울하게 걸어와, 브리그스와 단둘이 저녁을 들었다. 그는 그녀에게 무척 다정했고, 아이를 향한 그녀의 사랑과 돌봄에 고마워했다. 그가 브리그스의 돈을 빌렸고 또 그녀를 속이는 데 한몫했다는 양심의 가책이 그를 찔러 댔다. 두 사람은 어린 로던에 대해 오래도록 이야기를 나누었으니, 베키는 그저 옷을 차려입고 만찬에 나가려고 집에 들렀을 뿐이었다. 그러고 나서 그는 마음이 편치 않은 채 제인 부인에게 차를 마시러 가서, 무슨 일이 있었는지를, 어린 로던이 얼마나 야무지게 떠났는지를, 어떻게 그 아이가 학생복과 짧은 반바지를 입게 되었는지를, 또 옛 연대 시절의 잭 블랙볼의 아들인 젊은 블랙볼이 어떻게 그 아이를 맡아 잘 돌봐 주겠노라 약속했는지를 이야기했다.\n\n일주일도 채 못 되어 젊은 블랙볼은 어린 로던을 제 잔심부름꾼이자 구두닦이이자 아침 토스트 굽는 시종으로 삼았고, 라틴어 문법의 신비를 일러 주었으며, 서너 차례 매질을 했으나 모질게는 아니었다. 그 꼬마의 선량하고 정직한 얼굴이 그를 위해 길을 터 주었던 것이다. 그가 받은 매질이라고는 분명 그에게 약이 될 만한 정도였을 따름이다. 그리고 구두를 닦고 빵을 굽고 자질구레한 심부름을 하는 것으로 말하자면, 그런 일들이야말로 영국의 어린 신사라면 누구나 받아야 할 교육의 필수 과정으로 여겨지지 않았던가?\n\n우리가 다룰 이야기는 그 둘째 세대며 로던 도련님의 학교생활에 있지 않으니, 그러지 않았다면 이 이야기는 한없이 길게 늘어졌을 것이다. 대령은 얼마 지나지 않아 아들을 보러 갔는데, 그 꼬마가 자그마한 검은 학생복과 자그마한 반바지 차림으로 싱글벙글 웃으며 더없이 건강하고 행복하게 지내는 것을 보았다.\n\n아버지는 약삭빠르게도 그 아이의 상급자인 블랙볼에게 1소버린을 쥐여 주고, 제 잔심부름꾼을 향한 그 젊은 신사의 호의를 확보해 두었다. 위대한 스타인 경의 비호를 받는 아이요, 어느 주(州) 하원의원의 조카요, 그 이름이 모닝 포스트의 가장 화려한 연회 기사 몇몇에 오르내리는 대령이자 바스 훈작사의 아들이었으니, 어쩌면 학교 당국도 이 아이를 박정하게 대할 마음은 들지 않았을 것이다. 그에게는 용돈이 넉넉했고, 그는 그 돈으로 동무들에게 산딸기 파이를 푸짐하게 한턱내며 썼으며, 토요일이면 종종 아버지에게 돌아오도록 허락받았는데, 아버지는 늘 그날을 잔칫날로 삼았다. 짬이 나면 로던은 아이를 연극 구경에 데려갔고, 그러지 못할 때는 하인을 딸려 보냈다. 일요일이면 아이는 브리그스와 제인 부인과 사촌들과 함께 교회에 갔다. 로던은 학교며 싸움이며 잔심부름에 관한 아이의 이야기에 경탄했다. 머지않아 그는 어린 로던 못지않게 모든 선생과 주요 학생들의 이름을 죄다 외게 되었다. 그는 학교에서 사귄 어린 로던의 단짝을 초대했고, 연극이 끝난 뒤 두 아이에게 파이와 굴과 흑맥주를 먹여 둘 다 탈이 나게 만들었다. 어린 로던이 라틴어 문법책의 어느 대목을 \"배우는 중\"인지 보여 주면, 그는 아는 체하는 표정을 지어 보이려 애썼다. \"꾸준히 하거라, 얘야.\" 그는 자못 엄숙하게 아이에게 말했다. \"훌륭한 고전 교육만 한 것이 없단다. 그만한 게 없어!\"\n\n남편을 향한 베키의 멸시는 나날이 더해 갔다. \"당신 하고 싶은 대로 해요. 좋을 대로 어디서든 저녁을 들고, 애슬리 곡마장에 가서 생강 맥주에 톱밥이나 먹든지, 제인 부인이랑 찬송가나 부르든지. 다만 내가 그 애한테 매달리기를 바라진 말아요. 난 당신 이익을 챙겨야 하니까. 당신은 스스로 그걸 챙기지도 못하잖아요. 만약 내가 당신을 보살피지 않았더라면 지금쯤 당신이 어디서, 사교계의 어떤 자리에 있을지 정말 알고 싶군요.\" 사실 베키가 드나들던 연회 자리에서 가엾은 늙은 로던을 찾는 사람은 아무도 없었다. 이제 그녀는 그 없이 초대받는 일이 잦았다. 그녀는 마치 메이페어를 송두리째 제 소유라도 한 양 지체 높은 이들에 대해 떠들어 댔고, 궁정이 상중에 들면 그녀도 늘 검은 옷을 입었다.\n\n어린 로던이 처리되고 나자, 이 사랑스러운 가난한 가족의 일에 그토록 아비다운 관심을 기울이던 스타인 경은, 브리그스 양을 내보내면 그들의 지출을 자못 유익하게 줄일 수 있겠다고, 또 베키가 제 살림을 손수 꾸려 갈 만큼 충분히 영리하다고 생각했다. 그 자비로운 귀족이 제 피후견인에게 브리그스 양에게 진 자그마한 빚을 갚으라고 돈을 주었던 일은 앞 장에서 이야기한 바 있는데, 그런데도 브리그스는 여전히 그 벗들 곁에 남아 있었다. 그리하여 경은, 크롤리 부인이 제 너그러운 후원자가 빌려준 돈을 그 본래 목적과는 다른 데 썼으리라는 괴로운 결론에 이르렀다. 그러나 스타인 경은 이 점에 관한 의심을 베키 부인에게 굳이 털어놓을 만큼 무례하지는 않았다. 돈 문제로 입씨름을 벌이면 그녀의 감정이 상할 수도 있었고, 또 그녀에게는 그분의 너그러운 차용금을 달리 처분할 만한 괴로운 사정이 천 가지나 있을지도 몰랐던 것이다. 그러나 그는 사정의 실상을 제 눈으로 확인해 두기로 마음먹고, 더없이 조심스럽고 은근한 방식으로 필요한 탐문에 나섰다.\n\n우선 그는 일찌감치 기회를 잡아 브리그스 양을 떠보았다. 그것은 어려운 일이 아니었다. 조금만 부추겨 주면 그 갸륵한 여인은 청산유수로 떠들며 제 속을 죄다 쏟아 놓곤 했던 것이다. 그러던 어느 날, 로던 부인이 마차를 타고 나간 사이(크롤리 부부가 마차와 말을 두던 대여 마구간에서, 아니 그보다는 마구간 주인이 크롤리 부부를 위해 마차와 말을 두고 있던 그곳에서, 경의 심복 하인 피시 씨가 수월히 알아낸 바였다), 경은 커즌 거리의 그 집에 불쑥 들렀다. 그는 브리그스에게 커피 한 잔을 청하고, 학교에 있는 그 어린 아들에 관해 좋은 소식을 들었다고 말했다. 그러고는 오 분 만에, 로던 부인이 검은 비단 드레스 한 벌 말고는 그녀에게 아무것도 주지 않았으며, 브리그스 양은 그 드레스를 더없이 고마워하고 있다는 사실을 알아냈다.\n\n그는 이 꾸밈없는 이야기에 속으로 웃었다. 사실인즉, 우리의 친애하는 벗 레베카는 그에게, 돈을—천백이십오 파운드를—받은 브리그스가 얼마나 기뻐했는지, 또 그 돈을 무슨 증권에 투자했는지를 더없이 소상하게 늘어놓았던 것이다. 게다가 그토록 흐뭇한 거금을 내놓아야 하는 데 베키 자신이 얼마나 가슴 아팠는지까지. \"누가 알겠어.\" 이 사랑스러운 여인은 속으로 이렇게 생각했을지도 모른다. \"어쩌면 조금 더 줄지도.\" 그러나 경은 이 자그마한 책략가에게 그런 제안을 하지 않았다. 십중팔구 자기가 이미 충분히 너그러웠노라 생각했을 것이다.\n\n이어 그는 브리그스 양에게 그녀 자신의 형편이 어떤지를 물어볼 호기심이 일었고, 그녀는 경에게 제 처지를 솔직하게 털어놓았다. 어떻게 크롤리 양이 자기에게 유산을 남겼는지, 어떻게 친척들이 그 일부를 가져갔는지, 어떻게 크롤리 대령이 또 다른 몫을 투자해 주었고 자기는 그 대가로 더없이 확실한 담보와 이자를 받고 있는지, 또 로던 부부가 친절하게도 피트 경에게 나머지를 부탁하느라 애써 주었고, 피트 경은 짬이 나면 그것을 자기에게 가장 유리하게 처분해 주기로 했는지를. 경이 대령이 이미 그녀를 위해 얼마를 투자했느냐고 묻자, 브리그스 양은 즉시, 그리고 사실대로 그 액수가 육백 몇 파운드라고 일러 주었다.\n\n그러나 이야기를 다 털어놓기가 무섭게, 수다스러운 브리그스는 제 솔직함을 후회하며, 자기가 한 그 고백을 크롤리 씨에게는 일러바치지 말아 달라고 경에게 간청했다. \"대령이 워낙 친절하셔서요. 크롤리 씨가 기분이 상하셔서 돈을 도로 갚으실지도 모르는데, 그러면 그만한 이자는 어디서도 받지 못할 테니까요.\" 스타인 경은 웃으며 두 사람의 대화를 결코 발설하지 않겠노라 약속했고, 브리그스 양과 헤어질 때는 한층 더 크게 웃었다.\n\n\"어쩌면 저토록 노련한 꼬마 악마람!\" 그는 생각했다. \"기막힌 여배우요, 수완가가 아닌가! 그 살살거리는 솜씨로 며칠 전엔 나한테서 두 번째 돈까지 거의 우려낼 뻔했지. 내가 잘 보낸 평생 동안 보아 온 모든 여자를 통틀어 단연 최고야. 그 여자들은 저 여자에 비하면 갓난애에 불과해. 나야말로 풋내기요, 저 여자 손아귀에서 노는 바보지. 늙은 바보 말이야. 거짓말에 있어선 누구도 따를 자가 없어.\" 그녀의 영리함을 보여 주는 이 증거 앞에서 베키를 향한 경의 감탄은 한량없이 치솟았다. 돈을 얻어 낸 것이야 아무것도 아니지만, 필요한 액수의 곱절을 얻어 내고도 아무에게도 갚지 않다니, 그것은 참으로 멋들어진 한 수였다. 그리고 크롤리로 말하자면, 하고 경은 생각했다. 크롤리는 겉보기처럼 그렇게 바보가 아니야. 제 쪽에서도 이 일을 제법 영리하게 꾸려 온 거지. 그 얼굴이며 태도만 보아선 이 돈 문제를 조금이라도 안다고는 누구도 짐작 못 할 텐데, 알고 보면 그자가 여자를 부추긴 거고, 그 돈도 분명 그자가 썼을 거야. 우리도 알다시피 이 견해에서 경은 틀렸으나, 그것은 크롤리 대령을 향한 그의 처신에 적잖이 영향을 미쳐, 그는 전에 그 신사에게 보이던 그 시늉뿐인 존경심마저 더욱 줄여 가며 대하기 시작했다. 그 자그마한 부인이 제 몫의 돈주머니를 챙기고 있을지도 모른다는 생각은 크롤리 부인의 후원자의 머릿속에 도무지 떠오르지 않았다. 그리고, 사실대로 말하자면, 그는 자기가 그 오랜 세월, 인류의 숱한 약점을 익히 알게 해 준 그 잘 보낸 평생 동안 알고 지낸 다른 남편들에 비추어 크롤리 대령을 가늠했던 것이다. 경은 평생토록 워낙 많은 사내를 사들여 왔으니, 이 사내의 값도 알아냈다고 짐작한 것쯤은 분명 너그러이 봐줄 만한 일이었다.\n\n그는 베키를 단둘이 만난 첫 자리에서 이 점을 두고 그녀를 추궁했고, 필요한 액수보다 더 많은 돈을 얻어 낸 그 영리함을 두고 사람 좋게 그녀를 칭찬했다. 베키는 그저 살짝 당황했을 뿐이다. 이 사랑스러운 여인은 어쩔 수 없이 떠밀릴 때를 빼고는 거짓말을 하는 버릇이 없었으나, 이런 다급한 고비에서는 거리낌 없이 거짓말을 늘어놓는 것이 그녀의 수법이었다. 그리하여 그녀는 순식간에, 또 하나의 그럴듯하고 정연하며 소상한 이야기를 지어내어 후원자에게 들이밀 채비를 갖추었다. 앞서 그에게 한 진술은 거짓이었다고, 못된 거짓말이었다고, 그녀는 인정했다. 그러나 누가 그녀로 하여금 그런 말을 하게 만들었던가? \"아, 경이시여.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제가 말없이 견디고 감내해야 하는 그 모든 것을 경께서는 모르세요. 경 앞에서는 명랑하고 행복한 제 모습만 보시지요. 곁에 지켜 줄 이 하나 없을 때 제가 무엇을 견뎌야 하는지는 거의 모르세요. 경을 속이고 청했던 그 돈은, 제 남편이 협박과 더없이 사나운 학대로 저를 몰아붙여 요구하게 만든 거예요. 돈의 처분에 관해 추궁이 있으리라 미리 내다보고, 제가 그런 식으로 둘러대도록 떠민 것도 바로 그 사람이고요. 돈은 그 사람이 가져갔어요. 그이는 브리그스 양에게 갚았다고 했어요. 저는 그 말을 의심하고 싶지도, 감히 의심할 수도 없었죠. 절박한 사내가 어쩔 수 없이 저지른 잘못을 부디 용서해 주시고, 가엾고 가엾은 이 여자를 가엾이 여겨 주세요.\" 그녀는 이렇게 말하며 와락 울음을 터뜨렸다. 박해받는 미덕이 이보다 더 매혹적으로 가련해 보인 적은 없었다.\n\n두 사람은 크롤리 부인의 마차를 함께 타고 리젠트 공원을 빙글빙글 돌면서 오랫동안 대화를 나누었으니, 그 자세한 내용을 여기 되풀이할 필요는 없겠으나, 그 결말인즉, 베키가 집에 돌아오자 미소 띤 얼굴로 사랑하는 브리그스에게 달려가, 그녀에게 들려줄 더없이 좋은 소식이 있노라 알렸다는 것이다. 스타인 경께서 더없이 고귀하고 너그러운 처신을 하셨다는 것이었다. 그분은 언제 어떻게 선행을 베풀 수 있을지 늘 궁리하시는 분이라고. 이제 어린 로던이 학교에 갔으니, 다정한 길동무이자 벗은 더는 필요하지 않다고. 브리그스와 헤어지는 것이 이루 말할 수 없이 슬프지만, 제 형편상 온갖 절약을 다 해야 하는 처지라고. 그래도 사랑하는 브리그스가 제 초라한 집에 있느니 그 너그러운 후원자 곁에서 훨씬 더 나은 살길을 얻으리라는 생각에 슬픔이 누그러진다고. 곤틀리 저택의 가정부 필킹턴 부인이 부쩍 늙고 쇠약해진 데다 류머티즘까지 앓고 있어서, 그 광대한 저택을 관리하는 일을 감당하지 못하니 후임을 찾아야 한다는 것이었다. 그것은 더없이 훌륭한 자리였다. 그 집안 사람들은 이 년에 한 번도 곤틀리에 가지 않았다. 그렇지 않은 때면 가정부가 그 으리으리한 저택의 안주인이었으니, 매일 제 식탁에 네 사람분 식기를 차렸고, 성직자들과 그 고을의 가장 점잖은 이들의 방문을 받았다. 말하자면 그녀가 곤틀리의 마님이었던 셈이다. 필킹턴 부인 이전의 두 가정부는 곤틀리의 교구 목사들과 혼인까지 했으나, 필킹턴 부인은 현재 그 목사의 고모인지라 그럴 수 없었다. 그 자리가 아직 브리그스의 차지가 된 것은 아니었으나, 그녀가 필킹턴 부인을 찾아가 머물면서 그 뒤를 잇고 싶은지 가늠해 볼 수는 있었다.\n\n브리그스의 그 황홀한 감격을 무슨 말로 그려 낼 수 있으랴! 그녀가 단 하나 조건으로 내건 것은, 어린 로던이 저택으로 내려와 자기를 보러 오게 해 달라는 것뿐이었다. 베키는 그러마고—무엇이든 약속했다. 그녀는 남편이 돌아오자 그에게 달려가 그 기쁜 소식을 전했다. 로던은 기뻤다, 지독히도 기뻤다. 가엾은 브리그스의 돈을 두고 마음을 짓누르던 짐이 덜어진 것이다. 어쨌거나 그녀의 살길은 마련된 셈이었다. 그러나—그러나 그의 마음은 편치 않았다. 어쩐지 무언가 미덥지가 않은 듯했다. 그는 어린 사우스다운에게 스타인 경이 한 일을 이야기했는데, 그 젊은이는 크롤리를 놀라게 할 만한 표정으로 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n그는 제인 부인에게 스타인의 너그러움을 보여 주는 이 두 번째 증거를 이야기했는데, 그녀 역시 묘하고 놀란 기색이었다. 피트 경도 마찬가지였다. \"그 사람은 너무 영리하고—또—명랑해서, 길동무 하나 없이 이 연회 저 연회를 떠돌게 둘 수가 없어요.\" 두 사람은 입을 모아 말했다. \"로던, 자네가 그 사람이 가는 데마다 함께 가야 하네. 그리고 누군가 곁에 두어야 해. 어쩌면 퀸스크롤리의 처녀들 가운데 하나라도 좋겠지. 하기야 그 애들은 그 사람을 지켜 줄 보호자로는 좀 들떠 있긴 하지만 말이야.\"\n\n베키에게는 누군가 곁에 두어야 했다. 그러나 그러는 사이에도, 정직한 브리그스가 평생 자리 잡을 기회를 놓쳐서는 안 된다는 것만큼은 분명했다. 그리하여 그녀와 그녀의 짐 보따리가 꾸려졌고, 그녀는 여정에 올랐다. 그렇게 해서 로던의 전초병 둘이 적의 손아귀에 떨어진 셈이었다.\n\n피트 경은 동서를 찾아가, 브리그스를 내보낸 일이며 그 밖에 집안의 민감한 일들을 두고 따져 물었다. 가엾은 제 남편에게 스타인 경의 비호가 얼마나 긴요한지, 또 브리그스에게 주어진 그 자리를 빼앗는 것이 자기들로서는 얼마나 모진 짓일지를 그녀가 아무리 일러 보아야 헛일이었다. 알랑거림도, 살살거림도, 미소도, 눈물도 피트 경을 만족시키지 못했고, 그는 한때 흠모하던 베키와 거의 다툼이나 다름없는 언쟁을 벌였다. 그는 집안의 명예를, 크롤리 가문의 티끌 하나 없는 명성을 입에 올렸다. 그녀가 그 젊은 프랑스인들을—그 들뜬 멋쟁이 젊은이들을, 또 늘 그녀의 문 앞에 마차를 대 놓고 매일같이 몇 시간씩 그녀와 함께 지내며 그 끊임없는 출입으로 세상이 그녀를 입에 올리게 만드는 스타인 경 자신을—받아들이는 데 대해 분개한 어조로 말했다. 집안의 가장으로서 그는 그녀에게 좀 더 신중하기를 간청했다. 사교계가 이미 그녀를 두고 가벼이 입방아를 찧고 있었던 것이다. 스타인 경은 비록 더없이 지체 높고 재능이 빼어난 귀족이긴 하나, 그 관심을 받았다 하면 어느 여인의 평판이든 더럽혀 놓고 마는 사내였다. 그는 동서에게 그 귀족과의 교제에 신중하기를 빌고, 간청하고, 명령했다.\n\n베키는 피트가 바라는 것이라면 무엇이든 다 약속했다. 그러나 스타인 경은 여전히 변함없이 그녀의 집을 드나들었고, 피트 경의 분노는 더해 갔다. 제 남편이 마침내 그가 총애하던 레베카의 흠을 잡았으니, 제인 부인은 화가 났을까, 아니면 기뻤을까? 스타인 경의 방문이 이어지자 피트 자신의 방문은 끊겼고, 그 아내는 그 귀족과의 모든 왕래를 거절하려 했으며 후작 부인이 보내온 패러디극 공연의 밤 초대마저 사양하려 했다. 그러나 피트 경은 황태자 전하께서 거기 납시므로 그것은 받아들여야 한다고 생각했다.\n\n그 연회에 가긴 했으나, 피트 경은 아주 일찌감치 자리를 떴고, 그 아내 역시 일어서게 되어 무척 기뻐했다. 베키는 그에게 말을 건네기는커녕 동서를 거들떠보지도 않다시피 했다. 피트 크롤리는 그녀의 행실이 터무니없이 무례하다고 단언했고, 연극을 흉내 내고 분장 차림을 하는 그 버릇이 영국 여성에게 도무지 어울리지 않는다고 강한 어조로 나무랐으며, 패러디극이 끝나자 동생 로던을 호되게 꾸짖었다. 제가 직접 무대에 나서고, 또 제 아내가 그런 부적절한 구경거리에 끼어들도록 내버려 두었다는 것이었다.\n\n로던은 그녀가 다시는 그런 놀음에 끼지 못하게 하겠노라고 말했다. 그런데 사실 그는, 어쩌면 형과 형수의 귀띔에서 비롯된 것이겠으나, 이미 더없이 빈틈없고 모범적인 가장이 되어 있었다. 그는 클럽도 당구도 그만두었다. 결코 집을 비우는 법이 없었다. 그는 베키를 마차에 태워 데리고 다녔고, 그녀의 모든 연회에 꾸역꾸역 따라다녔다. 스타인 경이 찾아올 때면 영락없이 대령이 그 자리에 있었다. 그리고 베키가 남편 없이 외출하려 들거나 저 혼자 초대를 받으면, 그는 단호하게 그녀에게 거절하라고 명했다. 그리고 이 신사의 태도에는 복종을 강제하는 무언가가 깃들어 있었다. 자그마한 베키는, 공평하게 말하자면, 로던의 그 의젓한 태도에 매료되었다. 그가 시무룩할 때도 그녀는 결코 그러지 않았다. 벗들이 있든 없든 그녀는 늘 그에게 다정한 미소를 지어 보였고, 그의 즐거움과 안락을 살뜰히 챙겼다. 그것은 결혼 초의 나날이 다시 돌아온 듯했다. 똑같은 명랑함, 살가운 배려, 즐거움, 그리고 꾸밈없는 믿음과 정이었다. \"마차에 그 어리석은 늙은 브리그스 대신 당신이 곁에 있으니 얼마나 더 즐거운지 몰라요!\" 그녀는 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"우리 늘 이렇게 지내요, 사랑하는 로던. 우리한테 돈만 있다면 얼마나 좋을까, 얼마나 늘 행복할까!\" 그는 저녁을 든 뒤 의자에 앉은 채 잠들곤 했다. 그는 맞은편의, 초췌하고 지치고 무서운 그 얼굴을 보지 못했다. 그가 깨어나면 그 얼굴은 새삼 천진한 미소로 환히 빛났다. 그 얼굴은 명랑하게 그에게 입을 맞추었다. 그는 어쩌다 자기가 의심 따위를 품었던가 의아해했다. 아니, 그는 한 번도 의심한 적 없었다. 그의 마음속에 쌓여 가던 그 모든 말 없는 의구심과 시무룩한 불안이라고는 그저 부질없는 질투에 지나지 않았다. 그녀는 그를 사랑했다. 늘 그래 왔다. 그녀가 사교계에서 빛나는 것으로 말하면, 그것은 그녀의 잘못이 아니었다. 그녀는 거기서 빛나도록 타고난 사람이었다. 그녀처럼 말하고, 노래하고, 무엇이든 해낼 수 있는 여인이 어디 또 있단 말인가? 그녀가 그 아이만 좋아해 준다면! 하고 로던은 생각했다. 그러나 어미와 아들은 도무지 화목하게 어울리지 못했다.\n\n그리고 로던의 마음이 이런 의심과 갈피 모를 혼란으로 어지럽던 바로 그 무렵, 앞 장에서 이야기한 그 사건이 벌어졌으니, 가엾은 대령은 집에서 멀리 떨어진 채 갇힌 몸이 되고 만 것이다.\n\n친구 로던은 그 길로 마차를 몰아 커시터 거리에 있는 모스 씨의 저택으로 향했고, 그 음울한 환대의 장소로 정식으로 안내되었다. 덜컹거리는 마차가 그곳의 메아리를 깨울 무렵, 챈서리 거리의 활기찬 지붕들 위로 아침이 밝아 오고 있었다. 떠오르는 아침처럼 발그레한 머리에 눈이 발그레한 자그마한 유대인 소년이 일행을 집 안으로 들였고, 로던은 길동무이자 주인인 모스 씨에게 일 층 방으로 환영받았다. 모스 씨는 마차를 타고 오느라 고생했으니 따뜻한 것을 한 잔 들겠느냐고 명랑하게 물었다.\n\n대저택과 사랑스러운 아내를 떠나 채무자 임시 구류소에 갇히는 신세가 되면 어떤 사람은 풀이 죽기 마련이지만, 대령은 그만큼 의기소침하지는 않았다. 사실대로 말하자면, 그는 전에도 한두 번 모스 씨의 시설에 묵은 적이 있었기 때문이다. 우리는 이 이야기를 풀어 오는 가운데 이런 사소하고 자질구레한 집안 사건들을 굳이 언급할 필요가 없다고 여겼다. 그러나 독자께서는 일 년에 한 푼도 안 들이고 살아가는 사람의 인생에서 이런 일이 심심찮게 일어날 수 있음을 분명히 믿으셔도 좋다.\n\n모스 씨를 처음 찾았을 때, 당시 독신이었던 대령은 고모의 너그러운 처사 덕에 풀려났다. 두 번째 불운 때는 어린 베키가 더없는 기백과 친절을 발휘해 사우스다운 경에게서 얼마간 돈을 빌렸고, 남편의 채권자(사실 이자는 그녀에게 숄이며 벨벳 가운이며 레이스 손수건이며 장신구며 자잘한 패물 따위를 대 주던 사람이었다)를 구슬려 청구액 가운데 일부와 나머지에 대한 로던의 약속어음을 받아 가게 했다. 그리하여 이 두 번의 경우 모두 체포와 석방이 사방의 더없는 호기로움 속에 이루어졌고, 그래서 모스와 대령은 더없이 좋은 사이였다.\n\n\"전에 쓰시던 침대를 그대로 쓰실 거예요, 대령님. 모든 게 다 편안하답니다.\" 그 신사가 말했다. \"정직하게 말씀드릴 수 있어요. 잘 환기해 두었으니 안심하셔도 되고, 게다가 최고급 손님들이 쓰던 거랍니다. 그저께 밤에는 제오십 용기병 연대 소속의 페미시 대위님이 주무셨지요. 그분 어머님이 보름 만에 데려가셨는데, 벌을 좀 줘야겠다고 하시더라고요. 그런데, 원, 정말이지, 그분은 제 샴페인한테 벌을 주셨고요, 밤마다 파티를 여셨죠. 클럽이며 웨스트엔드에서 내려온 어엿한 일류 멋쟁이들이었어요. 래그 대위님, 템플에 사시는 듀세이스 명예 귀하, 그리고 술맛 좀 아는 양반들이었다니까요, 장담합니다. 위층엔 신학 박사님 한 분, 휴게실엔 신사분이 다섯, 그리고 모스 부인이 다섯 시 반에 정식을 차립니다. 그 뒤엔 카드놀이나 음악도 좀 즐기는데, 그때 대령님을 뵙게 되면 더없이 기쁘겠습니다요.\"\n\n\"필요한 게 있으면 종을 울리지.\" 로던은 그렇게 말하고 조용히 자기 침실로 들어갔다. 앞서 말했듯 그는 노련한 군인이라 운명의 자잘한 충격쯤에 흔들릴 사람이 아니었다. 더 나약한 사람이었다면 잡혀 들어온 그 순간 곧장 아내에게 편지를 보냈으리라. \"그러나 굳이 밤잠을 깨워 봐야 무슨 소용인가?\" 로던은 생각했다. \"내가 방에 있는지 없는지도 모를 텐데. 그 사람이 푹 자고 나서, 나도 좀 자고 나서 편지를 써도 늦지 않아. 고작 백칠십 파운드인데, 그걸 못 마련할 리야 없지.\" 그래서 대령은, 자기가 이런 묘한 곳에 와 있다는 걸 알리고 싶지 않은 어린 로던을 떠올리며, 얼마 전 페미시 대위가 쓰던 그 침대로 들어가 잠이 들었다. 잠에서 깨니 열 시였고, 발그레한 머리의 소년이 자랑스러운 표정으로 면도를 할 수 있도록 멋진 은제 화장 도구함을 가져다주었다. 사실 모스 씨의 집은 다소 지저분하기는 해도 구석구석이 호화로웠다. 찬장에는 지저분한 쟁반과 포도주 냉각기가 늘 놓여 있었고, 커다랗고 지저분한 금박 처마 장식이 둘러져 있었으며, 커시터 거리가 내다보이는 창살 박힌 창문에는 칙칙한 노란 공단 휘장이 드리워 있었다. 거대하고 지저분한 금박 액자에 끼워진 사냥 그림과 종교화가 사방에 걸려 있었는데, 죄다 일류 거장들의 작품이라 어음 거래에서도 최고가에 팔렸으며, 그 거래 과정에서 몇 번이고 거듭 사고팔리는 것이었다. 대령의 아침 식사도 똑같이 칙칙하면서도 호화로운 도금 식기에 담겨 나왔다. 종이로 머리를 만 검은 눈동자의 하녀 모스 양이 찻주전자를 들고 나타나, 빙긋 웃으며 대령에게 잘 주무셨느냐고 물었다. 그리고 그녀는 전날 밤 스타인 경의 연회에서 활약한 온갖 고관대작의 이름이 실린 모닝 포스트를 가져다주었다. 거기에는 그 잔치에 대한 화려한 기사와, 아름답고 재능 넘치는 로던 크롤리 부인의 감탄스러운 연기에 대한 기사가 실려 있었다.\n\n이 숙녀(그녀는 편안한 자세로 아침 식탁 가장자리에 걸터앉아 스타킹의 주름과 뒤축이 닳아빠진 한물간 흰 공단 신발을 드러내고 있었다)와 활기찬 잡담을 나눈 뒤, 크롤리 대령은 펜과 잉크와 종이를 청했고, 몇 장이 필요하냐는 물음에 한 장만 골랐는데 그것은 모스 양이 직접 엄지와 검지로 집어 건네주었다. 검은 눈동자의 그 처녀가 그렇게 들여온 종이가 한두 장이 아니었다. 가엾은 사내들이 그 무시무시한 방을 서성이며 황급히 애원의 글을 갈겨쓰고 얼룩지게 한 끝에, 심부름꾼이 답장을 받아 오기를 기다린 일이 한두 번이 아니었다. 가난한 사람들은 늘 우편 대신 심부름꾼을 쓰는 법이다. 봉인 풀이 채 마르지도 않은 편지를 받아 본 적이 없는 이가 어디 있겠으며, 누가 현관에서 기다리고 있다는 전갈을 받아 본 적이 없는 이가 어디 있겠는가?\n\n그런데 자신의 청을 두고 로던은 별다른 걱정을 하지 않았다.\n\n베키에게 (로던은 이렇게 썼다)\n\n잘 잤기를 바라오. 내가 당신 커피를 갖다주지 못해도 놀라지 마시오. 어젯밤 담배를 피우며 집으로 오던 길에 사고를 당했소. 커시터 거리의 모스한테 붙잡혔는데, 지금 그자의 금박 입힌 으리으리한 응접실에서 이 편지를 쓰고 있소. 두 해 전 이맘때 나를 잡아갔던 바로 그자요. 모스 양이 차를 들여왔는데, 아주 살이 쪘고 여전히 스타킹 뒤축이 흘러내려 있더이다.\n\n네이선 일이오. 백오십 파운드인데, 비용까지 하면 백칠십이오. 부탁이니 내 책상과 옷가지 좀 보내 주오. 지금 무도화에 흰 넥타이 차림이오(모스 양 스타킹 꼴이랑 비슷하지). 책상 안에 칠십 파운드가 있소. 이 편지를 받는 즉시 네이선한테 마차를 몰고 가서, 칠십오 파운드를 당장 내겠다고 하고 갱신을 부탁하시오. 포도주는 받겠다고 하고, 저녁에 마실 셰리주쯤은 받아 두는 게 좋겠소. 다만 그림은 안 되오, 너무 비싸니까.\n\n그자가 안 들어주면, 내 회중시계와 당신 물건 중 떼어 줄 만한 걸 챙겨서 볼스한테 보내시오. 어쨌든 오늘 밤 안으로 그 돈을 마련해야 하오. 미루는 건 안 될 일이오, 내일이 일요일이니 말이오. 이곳 침대는 그리 깨끗하지 못하고, 나를 상대로 다른 청구가 또 들어와 있을지도 모르오. 로던이 집에 오는 토요일이 아니라 다행이오. 신의 가호가 있기를.\n\n바삐 쓰며, R. C. 추신. 서둘러 와 주오.\n\n봉인 풀로 봉한 이 편지는 모스 씨의 시설 근처를 늘 어슬렁거리는 심부름꾼 가운데 하나가 받아 갔고, 로던은 그가 떠나는 것을 지켜본 뒤 마당으로 나가 머리 위 창살에도 아랑곳없이 제법 편안한 마음으로 시가를 피웠다. 모스 씨의 마당은 새장처럼 철책으로 둘러싸여 있었는데, 그 집에 묵는 신사들이 주인의 환대에서 달아나고 싶은 마음이 들지 않도록 한 것이었다.\n\n베키가 도착해 자신의 감옥 문을 열어 주기까지는 길어야 세 시간이면 되리라고 그는 헤아렸다. 그리고 이 시간을 그는 담배를 피우고 신문을 읽으며 제법 명랑하게 보냈고, 마침 그곳에 있던 지인 워커 대위와 함께 휴게실에서 몇 시간 동안 육 펜스씩을 걸고 카드를 떼었는데, 양쪽 다 운이 엇비슷했다.\n\n그러나 하루가 다 지나가도록 심부름꾼은 돌아오지 않았다. 베키도 오지 않았다. 모스 씨의 정식은 정해진 시각인 다섯 시 반에 차려졌고, 그 잔치 값을 치를 형편이 되는 하숙객 신사들이 앞서 묘사한 그 으리으리한 앞쪽 응접실로 와서 함께 들었다. 크롤리 씨의 임시 처소는 그 응접실과 통해 있었다. 이때 모스 양(그녀의 아버지는 그녀를 헴 양이라 불렀다)이 아침의 종이 머리말이는 떼고 나타났고, 헴 부인은 일등급 삶은 양 다리와 순무 요리의 시중을 들었는데 대령은 영 입맛이 없는 채로 그것을 먹었다. 일행에게 샴페인 한 병을 \"쏘겠느냐\"는 물음에 그는 응했고, 숙녀들은 그의 \"건강\"을 빌며 마셨으며, 모스 씨는 더없이 정중한 태도로 \"그를 향해 잔을 들었다.\"\n\n그런데 이 식사가 한창일 때 초인종 소리가 들렸다. 발그레한 머리의 모스 청년이 열쇠를 들고 일어나 응대하더니, 돌아와서는 심부름꾼이 가방과 책상과 편지를 가지고 돌아왔다고 대령에게 알리며 편지를 건넸다. \"격식 차리지 마세요, 대령님, 부디.\" 모스 부인이 손을 내저으며 말했고, 그는 다소 떨리는 손으로 편지를 뜯었다. 향내가 짙게 밴 아름다운 편지로, 분홍색 종이에 연두색 봉인이 찍혀 있었다.\n\n가엾은 내 사랑 작은 양반에게 (크롤리 부인은 이렇게 썼다)\n\n밉살스럽고 늙은 내 괴물 양반한테 무슨 일이 생겼나 생각하느라 한숨도 못 잤어요. 아침이 되어서야 겨우 잠들었는데, (열이 펄펄 끓어서) 블렌치 씨를 불렀더니 진정제를 주고는 무슨 일이 있어도 절대 나를 깨우지 말라고 피네트에게 일러두고 갔지 뭐예요. 그래서 내 가엾은 양반의 심부름꾼은, 피네트 말로는 행색이 영 험악하고 진 냄새를 풍기더라는데, 내가 종을 울리기를 기다리며 몇 시간이나 현관에 죽치고 있었답니다. 가엾고 사랑스러운 당신의 철자투성이 편지를 읽었을 때 내 심정이 어땠을지 짐작이나 가요?\n\n몸이 그렇게 아픈데도 나는 곧장 마차를 부르라고 했고, 옷을 차려입자마자(초콜릿은 한 방울도 마실 수 없었어요. 정말이지 내 괴물 양반이 가져다주지 않으면 못 마신다니까요) 쏜살같이 네이선한테 달려갔어요. 그를 만나서, 울고, 부르짖고, 그 밉살스러운 무릎 앞에 엎드렸지요. 그래도 그 끔찍한 인간은 조금도 마음을 누그러뜨리지 않더군요. 돈을 다 받든가, 아니면 내 가엾은 괴물 양반을 감옥에 가둬 두겠다지 뭐예요. 나는 큰아버지 댁에 그 서글픈 방문을 할 작정으로 집에 돌아왔어요(내 장신구를 죄다 당신 마음대로 쓰셔도 좋아요. 다 합쳐도 백 파운드는 안 되겠지만요. 알다시피 몇 개는 이미 그 큰아버지한테 가 있으니까요). 그런데 집에 와 보니 그 양 같은 얼굴을 한 늙은 불가리아 괴물이랑 같이 백작님이 와 계셨어요. 어젯밤 공연에 대한 찬사를 전하러 오신 거였죠. 패딩턴도 와서 느릿느릿 혀짤배기소리를 내며 제 머리칼을 배배 꼬고 있었고, 샹피냐크도, 그의 요리장도 왔어요. 다들 찬사며 듣기 좋은 소리를 한 무더기씩 늘어놓으며, 그들을 어서 떼어 버리고 싶고 시시각각 내 가엾은 죄수 양반의 시간만 생각하던 가엾은 나를 들볶았답니다.\n\n그들이 다 가고 나서, 나는 백작님 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 우리가 모든 걸 전당포에 잡혀야 할 처지라고 말씀드리며 이백 파운드를 빌려 달라고 간청하고 또 애원했어요. 그분은 노발대발하며 흥, 쳇 하시더니, 그런 어리석은 짓 하지 말라고, 전당포에 잡히지 말라고 하셨어요. 그러고는 돈을 빌려줄 수 있을지 알아보겠다고 하시더군요. 마침내 그분은 아침에 보내 주겠노라 약속하며 떠나셨어요. 그러면 그 돈을 내 가엾고 늙은 괴물 양반한테, 그분을 사랑하는 베키의 입맞춤과 함께 가져다드리겠어요.\n\n베키\n침대에 누워서 쓰고 있어요. 아, 머리도 너무 아프고 가슴도 너무 아파요!\n\n로던이 이 편지를 다 읽고 나자 얼굴이 어찌나 붉어지고 표정이 어찌나 험악해졌던지, 정식 식탁에 앉은 일행은 그에게 나쁜 소식이 전해졌음을 쉽게 알아챘다. 떨쳐 내려 애써 오던 온갖 의심이 그에게 되살아났다. 그녀는 자기를 풀어 주려고 밖에 나가 장신구를 팔 생각조차 하지 않았다. 그가 감옥에 갇혀 있는 동안 그녀는 자기에게 쏟아진 찬사를 두고 웃고 떠들 수 있었던 것이다. 그를 거기 처넣은 자가 누구인가? 웬엄이 그와 함께 걸었었다. 혹시…… 그는 자신이 의심하는 바를 차마 생각조차 할 수가 없었다. 그는 황급히 방을 나와 자기 방으로 뛰어 들어가 책상을 열고 급히 두 줄을 적은 뒤, 그것을 피트 경 또는 크롤리 부인 앞으로 적었다. 그러고는 심부름꾼에게 마차를 잡아 타고 그것을 당장 곤트 거리로 가져가라 이르며, 한 시간 안에 돌아오면 일 기니를 주겠노라 약속했다.\n\n쪽지에서 그는 사랑하는 형과 형수에게, 하느님을 봐서, 사랑하는 자기 아이와 자기 명예를 봐서, 자기에게 와서 이 곤경에서 벗어나게 해 달라고 간청했다. 자기는 감옥에 갇혀 있고 풀려나려면 백 파운드가 필요하다고, 부디 와 달라고 애원했다.\n\n심부름꾼을 보낸 뒤 그는 식당으로 돌아와 포도주를 더 청했다. 그는 사람들이 보기에 묘하게 떠들썩한 태도로 웃고 떠들었다. 때로는 자기 자신의 두려움을 두고 미친 듯이 웃기도 하면서, 한 시간 동안 줄곧 자신의 운명을 실어 올 마차 소리에 귀를 기울이며 술을 들이켰다.\n\n그 시간이 다 지났을 무렵, 바퀴 소리가 문을 향해 굴러오는 것이 들렸다. 젊은 문지기가 문 열쇠를 들고 나갔다. 그가 채무 집행관용 문으로 들여보낸 것은 한 숙녀였다.\n\n\"크롤리 대령님을.\" 그녀가 몹시 떨면서 말했다. 그는 무언가 안다는 듯한 표정으로 그녀 뒤로 바깥문을 잠그고는, 안쪽 문을 풀어 열고 \"대령님, 손님이오.\" 하고 외친 뒤, 그녀를 자신이 쓰는 안쪽 응접실로 안내했다.\n\n로던은 그 모든 사람이 흥청대는 식당에서 자기 안쪽 방으로 들어왔다. 거친 빛 한 줄기가 그를 따라, 여전히 몹시 긴장한 채 숙녀가 서 있는 방으로 흘러들었다.\n\n\"저예요, 로던.\" 그녀가 애써 명랑한 척하며 겁먹은 목소리로 말했다. \"제인이에요.\" 로던은 그 다정한 목소리와 존재에 완전히 압도되었다. 그는 그녀에게 달려가, 두 팔로 끌어안고, 알아들을 수 없는 감사의 말을 헐떡이며 내뱉더니, 정말로 그녀의 어깨에 얼굴을 묻고 흐느꼈다. 그녀는 그가 그토록 격해진 까닭을 알지 못했다.\n\n모스 씨의 청구액은 곧 정산되었는데, 적어도 일요일까지는 대령을 손님으로 붙들어 둘 셈이던 그 신사에게는 어쩌면 실망스러운 일이었을 것이다. 환한 미소와 행복으로 눈을 반짝이며 제인은 채무 집행관의 집에서 로던을 데리고 나왔고, 두 사람은 그녀가 그를 풀어 주려고 황급히 타고 온 그 마차를 타고 집으로 향했다. \"로던의 쪽지가 왔을 때 피트는 의회 만찬에 가고 없었어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그래서, 로던, 제가, 제가 직접 온 거예요.\" 그러면서 그녀는 그의 손에 자신의 다정한 손을 포갰다. 어쩌면 피트가 그 만찬에 가 있었던 것이 로던 크롤리에게는 다행한 일이었으리라. 로던은 형수에게 백 번이고 고마워했는데, 그 감사의 열렬함이 그 마음씨 고운 여인을 감동시켰고 거의 놀라게 할 정도였다. \"아아.\" 그가 투박하고 꾸밈없는 태도로 말했다. \"형수님은, 형수님은 모르실 거예요, 제가 형수님을, 그리고 어린 로디를 알고부터 얼마나 달라졌는지를요. 저는, 저는 어떻게든 달라지고 싶어요. 보세요, 저는 되고 싶어요, 되고 싶다고요, 그러니까……\" 그는 말을 맺지 못했지만, 그녀는 그 뜻을 헤아릴 수 있었다. 그리고 그날 밤, 그가 떠난 뒤 자기 어린 아들의 침대맡에 앉아 그녀는 그 가엾고 세상에 지친 죄인을 위해 겸허히 기도했다.\n\n로던은 그녀와 헤어져 빠른 걸음으로 집을 향해 걸었다. 밤 아홉 시였다. 그는 허영의 시장의 거리와 큰 광장들을 가로질러 달려, 마침내 숨을 헐떡이며 자기 집 맞은편에 다다랐다. 그는 흠칫 물러서며 난간에 기대섰고, 위를 올려다보며 부들부들 떨었다. 응접실 창문들이 환한 불빛으로 타오르고 있었다. 그녀는 자기가 아파서 침대에 누워 있다고 말했었다. 그는 그 자리에 한동안 서 있었고, 방에서 새어 나온 불빛이 그의 창백한 얼굴 위로 떨어졌다.\n\n그는 현관 열쇠를 꺼내 집 안으로 들어섰다. 위층 방에서 웃음소리가 들려왔다. 그는 전날 밤 붙잡힐 때 입었던 무도복 차림 그대로였다. 그는 소리 없이 계단을 올라 계단 꼭대기에서 난간에 기대섰다. 집 안에 움직이는 사람이라곤 아무도 없었다. 하인들이 죄다 내보내진 것이었다. 로던은 안에서 들려오는 웃음소리를, 웃음소리와 노랫소리를 들었다. 베키가 전날 밤의 노래 한 자락을 부르고 있었다. 쉰 목소리가 \"브라보! 브라보!\" 하고 외쳤다. 스타인 경의 목소리였다.\n\n로던은 문을 열고 들어갔다. 저녁상이 차려진 자그마한 탁자가 놓여 있었다. 포도주와 식기도. 스타인은 베키가 앉아 있는 소파 위로 몸을 숙이고 있었다. 그 가련한 여인은 휘황찬란한 정장 차림으로, 두 팔과 손가락마다 팔찌와 반지를 반짝였고, 가슴에는 스타인이 그녀에게 준 보석이 빛나고 있었다. 그는 그녀의 손을 잡고 거기에 입 맞추려 몸을 숙이던 참이었는데, 그때 베키가 로던의 새하얀 얼굴을 알아채고는 가느다란 비명을 지르며 벌떡 일어섰다. 다음 순간 그녀는 남편을 반기려는 듯 미소를, 끔찍한 미소를 지어 보였다. 스타인은 이를 갈며, 창백한 얼굴에 분노를 띤 채 일어섰다.\n\n그 역시 웃어 보이려 애쓰며 손을 내밀고 앞으로 나섰다. \"아니, 돌아왔구려! 어떻게 지내시오, 크롤리?\" 그가 침입자에게 억지로 웃어 보이려 애쓰자 입가의 신경이 씰룩거렸다.\n\n로던의 얼굴에 어린 무언가에 베키는 그의 앞으로 몸을 던졌다. \"저는 결백해요, 로던.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"하느님 앞에 맹세코, 저는 결백해요.\" 그녀는 그의 외투에, 그의 두 손에 매달렸다. 그녀 자신의 손은 온통 뱀 모양 장신구며 반지며 패물로 뒤덮여 있었다. \"저는 결백해요. 제가 결백하다고 말해 주세요.\" 그녀가 스타인 경에게 말했다.\n\n그는 자기에게 덫이 놓인 것이라 여겼고, 남편에게만큼이나 아내에게도 노발대발했다. \"네가 결백하다고? 빌어먹을.\" 그가 비명처럼 소리쳤다. \"네가 결백하다고? 네 몸에 걸친 장신구란 장신구는 죄다 내가 값을 치른 것들이다. 나는 네게 수천 파운드를 주었고, 그걸 이자가 다 써 버렸고, 그 대가로 너를 팔아넘긴 거다. 결백하다고, 빌어먹을! 네가 결백하긴, 발레 무희였던 네 어미나 불한당인 네 서방만큼이나 결백하겠지. 다른 사람들한테 했듯이 나를 겁줄 생각일랑 마라. 비켜라, 이 사람아, 좀 지나가게.\" 그러고는 스타인 경이 모자를 집어 들고, 눈에 불을 켠 채 적수의 얼굴을 사납게 노려보며 그를 향해 다가섰다. 상대가 길을 비켜 주리라는 것을 한순간도 의심치 않은 채로.\n\n그러나 로던 크롤리가 펄쩍 뛰어나와 그의 넥타이를 움켜쥐었고, 스타인은 거의 목이 졸려 그의 팔 아래에서 몸부림치며 구부러졌다. \"거짓말 마라, 이 개 같은 놈아!\" 로던이 말했다. \"거짓말 마라, 이 비겁하고 악랄한 놈아!\" 그러고는 손바닥을 펴 그 귀족의 얼굴을 두 번 후려치고는, 피 흘리는 그를 바닥에 내동댕이쳤다. 레베카가 끼어들 틈도 없이 모든 일이 벌어졌다. 그녀는 그 앞에 서서 부들부들 떨었다. 그녀는 강하고 용감하고 승리한 자기 남편에게 감탄했다.\n\n\"이리 와.\" 그가 말했다. 그녀는 곧장 다가왔다.\n\n\"그것들을 벗어.\" 그녀는 떨면서 팔에서 보석을 빼고 떨리는 손가락에서 반지를 빼기 시작하더니, 그것들을 한 무더기로 그러쥔 채 부들부들 떨며 그를 올려다보았다. \"내려놔.\" 그가 말하자 그녀는 그것들을 떨어뜨렸다. 그는 그녀의 가슴에서 다이아몬드 장신구를 잡아 뜯어 스타인 경에게 던졌다. 그것이 그의 벗어진 이마를 베었다. 스타인은 죽는 날까지 그 흉터를 지니고 살았다.\n\n\"위층으로 올라가.\" 로던이 아내에게 말했다. \"죽이지 마세요, 로던.\" 그녀가 말했다. 그는 사납게 웃었다. \"저 인간이 돈에 대해서도 나에 대해서처럼 거짓말을 하는지 봐야겠어. 저자가 당신한테 돈을 준 게 있나?\"\n\n\"없어요.\" 레베카가 말했다. \"그러니까……\"\n\n\"열쇠를 내놔.\" 로던이 대답했고, 두 사람은 함께 나갔다.\n\n레베카는 열쇠 하나만 빼고 모두 그에게 내주었는데, 그 하나가 없는 것을 그가 알아채지 못하기를 바랐다. 그것은 애밀리아가 옛날에 그녀에게 준 자그마한 책상의 열쇠로, 그녀는 그것을 은밀한 곳에 보관해 두고 있었다. 그러나 로던은 상자며 옷장이며 마구 열어젖히고 그 안에 든 잡다한 허섭스레기들을 이리저리 내던지다가, 마침내 그 책상을 찾아냈다. 여자는 어쩔 수 없이 그것을 열었다. 그 안에는 서류며, 여러 해 묵은 연애편지며, 온갖 자잘한 장신구와 여자의 비망록이 들어 있었다. 그리고 지폐가 든 지갑 하나가 들어 있었다. 그중에는 십 년 전 날짜의 것도 있었고, 하나는 아주 새것으로, 스타인 경이 그녀에게 준 천 파운드짜리 어음이었다.\n\n\"이걸 저자가 당신한테 줬나?\" 로던이 말했다.\n\n\"네.\" 레베카가 대답했다.\n\n\"오늘 안으로 그자에게 돌려보내겠어.\" 로던이 말했다(다시 날이 밝아 있었고, 이렇게 뒤지느라 여러 시간이 지나간 것이었다). \"그리고 그 아이한테 잘해 준 브리그스에게 갚고, 빚도 좀 갚겠어. 나머지는 어디로 보내면 될지 알려 줘. 베키, 이 많은 것 중에서 나한테 백 파운드쯤은 떼어 줄 수도 있었잖아. 나는 늘 당신과 나눠 가졌는데.\"\n\n\"저는 결백해요.\" 베키가 말했다. 그리고 그는 더는 한마디도 없이 그녀를 떠났다.\n\n그가 떠났을 때 그녀는 무슨 생각을 했을까? 그가 가 버린 뒤로도 그녀는 몇 시간이나 그대로 있었고, 햇살이 방 안으로 쏟아져 들어오는데 레베카는 홀로 침대 가장자리에 앉아 있었다. 서랍이란 서랍은 죄다 열려 그 속의 것들이 사방에 흩어져 있었다. 드레스며 깃털 장식이며 스카프며 장신구며, 한 무더기의 어지러운 허영의 물건들이 폐허처럼 널브러져 있었다. 머리칼은 어깨 위로 흘러내렸고, 가운은 로던이 거기서 보석을 잡아 뜯은 자리가 찢겨 있었다. 그가 자기를 떠난 지 몇 분 만에 계단을 내려가는 소리가, 그리고 그의 등 뒤로 문이 쾅 닫히는 소리가 들렸다. 그녀는 그가 결코 돌아오지 않으리라는 것을 알았다. 그는 영영 가 버린 것이었다. 그가 자살할까? 그녀는 생각했다. 스타인 경을 만나기 전까지는 아니리라. 그녀는 자신의 길고도 지나온 삶을, 그 모든 음울한 사건들을 떠올렸다. 아아, 그것은 얼마나 황량하고, 얼마나 비참하고, 외롭고, 보람 없어 보였던가! 아편을 먹고 그것으로 끝내, 온갖 희망이며 계략이며 빚이며 승리며 죄다 끝내 버릴까? 프랑스 하녀가 이런 자세로 있는 그녀를 발견했다. 두 손을 맞잡고 메마른 눈을 한 채 자신의 비참한 잔해 한가운데에 앉아 있는 모습을. 그 여자는 그녀의 공범이자 스타인의 돈을 받는 끄나풀이었다. \"맙소사, 마님, 무슨 일이세요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n무슨 일이 있었던가? 그녀는 죄가 있는가, 없는가? 그녀는 없다고 했지만, 그 입술에서 나온 것이 진실인지, 또 그 타락한 마음이 이 경우에는 정녕 깨끗했는지를 누가 알 수 있겠는가?\n\n그녀의 온갖 거짓말과 계략, 이기심과 술수, 그 모든 기지와 재능이 이런 파산으로 귀결된 것이었다. 그 여자는 커튼을 치고는, 얼마간 사정하고 친절을 가장하며 여주인을 설득해 침대에 눕게 했다. 그러고는 아래층으로 내려가, 레베카가 남편의 명령에 따라 떨어뜨린 뒤로 바닥에 그대로 놓여 있던 장신구들을, 스타인 경이 떠난 뒤로 줄곧 거기 있던 그것들을 주워 모았다.\n\n피트 크롤리 경의 저택은 그레이트 곤트 거리에 자리 잡고 있었는데, 이제 막 하루를 맞을 채비를 시작하던 참이었다. 로던은 이틀째 그대로 걸치고 있는 야회복 차림으로, 현관 계단을 닦다가 화들짝 놀란 하녀를 지나쳐 형의 서재로 들어섰다. 제인 부인은 실내복 차림으로 이미 일어나 이층 육아실에 올라가 아이들의 몸단장을 챙기며, 어린것들이 그녀의 무릎 곁에서 올리는 아침 기도를 듣고 있었다. 그녀와 아이들은 매일 아침 이 의무를 단둘이 먼저 치렀고, 그런 뒤에야 피트 경이 주재하며 온 집안 식구가 모두 모이도록 되어 있는 공식 예배가 이어졌다. 로던은 서재 안 준남작의 책상 앞에 앉았다. 그 책상에는 가지런히 정리된 푸른 표지의 의회 보고서와 편지들, 깔끔하게 분류해 정리한 청구서들과 좌우 대칭으로 놓인 소책자들, 자물쇠를 채운 회계 장부들, 책상받침과 문서함들, 성경, 계간평론지, 궁정 인명록 따위가 늘어서 있었는데, 모두가 마치 사열이라도 받듯 그 주인의 점검을 기다리며 도열해 있는 듯했다.\n\n피트 경이 일요일 아침마다 식구들에게 한 편씩 들려주는 버릇이 있는 가족용 설교집 한 권이 서재 책상 위에 펼칠 채비를 갖춘 채, 그분의 사려 깊은 선택을 기다리고 있었다. 그리고 설교집 옆에는 옵서버 신문이 물기에 살짝 젖어 단정히 접힌 채, 피트 경 개인이 쓰도록 놓여 있었다. 그분의 시종만이 그것을 주인의 책상 옆에 놓아두기 전에 미리 읽어볼 기회를 누렸다. 그 시종은 그날 아침 신문을 서재로 들이기에 앞서, \"곤트 하우스의 향연\"에 관한 화려한 기사를 그 신문에서 이미 읽은 터였다. 거기에는 스타인 후작이 왕세자 전하를 모시고자 초대한 저명인사들의 이름이 죄다 실려 있었다. 시종은 가정부와 그 조카가 가정부의 방에서 이른 차와 버터를 바른 뜨거운 토스트를 들고 있을 적에 이 연회에 대해 한마디씩 평을 늘어놓았고, 로던 크롤리 부부가 대체 어떻게 살아가는지 궁금해하기도 하다가, 그러고 나서 신문을 다시 물기에 적셔 접어 두어, 집주인이 도착할 즈음에는 마치 갓 배달된 듯 말짱하고 손도 안 댄 것처럼 보이게 해 놓았던 것이다.\n\n가엾은 로던은 신문을 집어 들고 형이 올 때까지 어떻게든 읽어 보려 애썼다. 그러나 활자는 그의 눈에 그저 텅 비어 떨어질 뿐, 자기가 무엇을 읽고 있는지 조금도 알 수가 없었다. 정부 소식과 인사 발령(공인인 피트 경으로서는 반드시 정독해야 하는 것이었으니, 그렇지 않았던들 일요일 신문이 집 안에 들어오는 것을 결코 허락하지 않았을 터였다), 연극 비평, 바킹의 푸주한과 터트버리의 귀염둥이가 양편에 백 파운드씩 걸고 벌인 시합, 그리고 베키 부인이 여주인공으로 활약한 그 유명한 무언극을 더없이 찬사 어린, 그러나 조심스러운 어조로 전하는 곤트 하우스 소식 그 자체까지—이 모든 것이 가문의 어른이 도착하기를 앉아 기다리는 로던의 눈앞에서 안개에 싸인 듯 흘러갈 따름이었다.\n\n검은 대리석으로 만든 서재 시계의 날카로운 종소리가 아홉 점을 알리기 시작하자, 어김없이 피트 경이 모습을 드러냈다. 산뜻하고 단정하며 말끔히 면도를 한, 밀랍처럼 깨끗한 얼굴에 빳빳한 와이셔츠 깃을 세우고, 듬성듬성한 머리칼은 빗어 기름을 발랐으며, 풀 먹인 크라바트에 회색 플란넬 실내복을 걸친 채로 위풍당당하게 계단을 내려오면서 손톱을 다듬고 있었다. 한마디로 진정한 구식 영국 신사요, 단정함과 온갖 예법의 표본이었다. 그는 흐트러진 옷차림에 핏발 선 눈, 얼굴을 덮은 머리칼을 한 가엾은 로던이 서재에 있는 것을 보고 흠칫 놀랐다. 그는 동생이 술이 덜 깬 채로, 밤새 어디서 진탕 놀다 온 것이라고 여겼다. \"이런 세상에, 로던.\" 그가 어리둥절한 얼굴로 말했다. \"이 아침 시간에 여기는 웬일이냐? 어째서 집에 있지 않고?\"\n\n\"집이라뇨.\" 로던이 거친 웃음을 터뜨리며 말했다. \"놀라지 마세요, 피트 형. 저 취하지 않았어요. 문 좀 닫으세요. 형한테 할 얘기가 있어요.\"\n\n피트는 문을 닫고 책상으로 다가와, 다른 안락의자에 앉았다. 그것은 집사나 대리인, 혹은 준남작과 볼일을 보러 오는 막역한 손님을 맞기 위해 놓아둔 의자였다. 그러고는 그 어느 때보다 격렬하게 손톱을 다듬었다.\n\n\"피트 형, 저는 이제 끝장났어요.\" 잠시 침묵하던 끝에 대령이 말했다. \"저는 끝났어요.\"\n\n\"이렇게 될 줄 내가 늘 말했지.\" 준남작이 짜증스럽게 외치며, 말끔히 다듬은 손톱으로 박자를 두드렸다. \"내가 천 번도 더 경고했잖느냐. 더는 너를 도와줄 수가 없다. 내 돈은 한 푼까지 죄다 묶여 있어. 어젯밤 제인이 가져다준 그 백 파운드조차 내일 아침에 내 변호사한테 주기로 약속한 돈이라, 그게 없으면 나는 크나큰 곤란을 겪게 된다. 끝내 너를 돕지 않겠다는 말은 아니다. 하지만 네 빚쟁이들한테 빚을 전부 갚아 준다는 거라면, 차라리 국채를 다 갚겠다고 바라는 편이 낫겠다. 그런 걸 생각한다는 건 미친 짓이야, 순전히 미친 짓이지. 너는 화해 절충을 봐야 해. 집안으로서는 괴로운 일이다만, 다들 그렇게 하는 거다. 래글런드 경의 아들 조지 키틀리도 지난주에 파산 법정을 거쳤는데, 사람들 말로 이른바 깨끗이 면책을 받았다더구나. 래글런드 경은 그 애를 위해 한 푼도 내놓지 않았고, 그리고—\"\n\n\"제가 원하는 건 돈이 아니에요.\" 로던이 말을 끊었다. \"제 일로 형을 찾아온 게 아니라고요. 제게 무슨 일이 닥치든 그건 신경 쓰지 마세요.\"\n\n\"그럼 대체 무슨 일이냐?\" 피트가 다소 안도하며 물었다.\n\n\"애 때문이에요.\" 로던이 잠긴 목소리로 말했다. \"제가 떠나고 나면 그 애를 형이 맡아 주겠다고 약속해 주셨으면 해요. 그 착하고 다정한 형수님은 그 애한테 늘 잘해 주셨어요. 그 애도 제 어미보다 형수님을 더 따른다고요…—제기랄. 들어 보세요, 피트 형. 제가 크롤리 고모님 돈을 물려받기로 되어 있었던 거 형도 아시잖아요. 저는 차남처럼 길러진 게 아니라, 늘 헤프게 쓰고 빈둥거리도록 부추김을 받으며 자랐어요. 이것만 아니었던들 저도 아주 딴사람이 되었을지 몰라요. 연대에서도 제 임무를 그리 못하지는 않았어요. 제가 그 돈 문제로 어떻게 내쳐졌는지, 그걸 누가 차지했는지 형도 알잖아요.\"\n\n\"내가 치른 희생을 생각하면, 또 내가 너를 어떻게 감싸 왔는지를 생각하면, 이런 식의 책망은 부질없는 것 같구나.\" 피트 경이 말했다. \"네 결혼은 내가 한 게 아니라 네가 한 거다.\"\n\n\"그건 이제 끝났어요.\" 로던이 말했다. \"이제 끝났다고요.\" 그 말은 신음과 함께 그에게서 비틀려 나왔고, 그 소리에 형은 흠칫 놀랐다.\n\n\"맙소사! 그 사람이 죽기라도 했느냐?\" 피트 경이 진정 놀라고 측은해하는 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"차라리 제가 죽었으면 싶어요.\" 로던이 대답했다. \"어린 로던만 아니었던들 오늘 아침에 제 목을 베어 버렸을 거예요—그 빌어먹을 악당의 목도요.\"\n\n피트 경은 곧바로 진상을 알아채고, 로던이 목숨을 빼앗고자 하는 자가 스타인 경임을 짐작했다. 대령은 형에게 간략하게, 그리고 끊어지는 말투로 이 사건의 정황을 들려주었다. \"그 악당과 그 여자 사이에 짜고 친 계략이었어요.\" 그가 말했다. \"저한테 집달리들을 붙였죠. 저는 그자의 집에서 나오던 참에 붙잡혔어요. 제가 그 여자한테 돈을 보내 달라고 편지했더니, 자기는 아파서 누워 있다며 다음 날로 미루더군요. 그러다 제가 집에 와 보니, 그 여자가 다이아몬드를 두르고 그 악당과 단둘이 앉아 있는 거예요.\" 그러고 나서 그는 스타인 경과 몸으로 맞붙은 일을 황급히 설명했다. 물론 그런 성질의 일에는 한 가지 결말밖에 없으며, 형과 의논을 마치고 나면 뒤따를 수밖에 없는 결투를 위해 필요한 채비를 하러 떠날 작정이라고 그는 말했다. \"그리고 그게 저한테 치명적으로 끝날지도 모르니,\" 로던이 끊어지는 목소리로 말했다. \"그 애한테는 어미가 없으니, 그 애를 형과 형수님께 맡길 수밖에 없어요, 피트 형—다만 형이 그 애의 벗이 되어 주겠다고 약속만 해 주신다면 저한테는 그게 위안이 될 거예요.\"\n\n형은 크게 마음이 움직여, 좀처럼 보이지 않던 다정함으로 로던의 손을 잡고 흔들었다. 로던은 더부룩한 눈썹 위로 손을 쓸어내렸다. \"고마워요, 형.\" 그가 말했다. \"형의 약속은 믿을 수 있다는 거 압니다.\"\n\n\"내 명예를 걸고 그러마.\" 준남작이 말했다. 그리하여, 거의 말없이, 두 사람 사이에 이 약조가 맺어졌다.\n\n이윽고 로던은 베키의 책상에서 찾아낸 그 자그마한 수첩을 주머니에서 꺼내, 그 안에 든 지폐 한 묶음을 끄집어냈다. \"여기 육백 파운드예요.\" 그가 말했다. \"제가 이렇게 부자인 줄 형은 몰랐죠. 이 돈을 브리그스한테 줬으면 해요. 우리한테 빌려준 사람이거든요—게다가 그 애한테도 잘해 줬고요—저는 그 가엾은 노부인의 돈을 받은 게 늘 부끄러웠어요. 그리고 여기 좀 더 있어요—저는 몇 파운드밖에 안 챙겼어요—이건 베키가 살림을 꾸려 나가는 데 쓰도록 가지면 되겠죠.\" 그렇게 말하며 그는 형에게 건네주려고 나머지 지폐를 집어 들었으나, 손이 떨리고 어찌나 동요했던지 수첩이 그의 손에서 떨어졌고, 그 안에서 천 파운드짜리 지폐 한 장이 빠져나왔다. 그것은 불운한 베키가 마지막으로 딴 돈이었다.\n\n피트는 몸을 굽혀 그것들을 주워 들고는, 그 엄청난 액수에 깜짝 놀랐다. \"그건 아니에요.\" 로던이 말했다. \"저는 그게 임자인 그자한테 총알을 박아 넣을 작정이에요.\" 그는 그 지폐로 총알을 싸서 스타인을 죽인다면 멋진 복수가 되리라고 속으로 생각했던 것이다.\n\n이 대화를 마친 뒤 형제는 다시 한번 손을 맞잡고 헤어졌다. 제인 부인은 대령이 왔다는 소식을 듣고, 여자의 직감으로 불길한 예감에 사로잡힌 채 옆방 식당에서 남편을 기다리고 있었다. 식당 문이 마침 열려 있던 터라, 두 형제가 서재에서 나오는 참에 부인은 으레 그러하듯 그 문에서 나오고 있었다. 그녀는 로던에게 손을 내밀며 아침 식사를 하러 와서 반갑다고 말했지만, 그의 초췌하고 면도도 안 한 얼굴과 남편의 어두운 낯빛으로 미루어, 두 사람 사이에 아침 식사 따위는 문제가 되지 않는다는 것을 알아챌 수 있었다. 로던은 약속이 있다며 무어라 변명을 중얼거리고는, 형수가 내민 그 소심하고 자그마한 손을 꽉 쥐었다. 애원하는 그녀의 눈은 그의 얼굴에서 재앙 외에는 아무것도 읽어 낼 수 없었으나, 그는 더는 한마디도 없이 떠나갔다. 피트 경 역시 그녀에게 아무 설명도 해 주지 않았다. 아이들이 그에게 인사하러 다가오자, 그는 여느 때의 그 싸늘한 태도로 아이들에게 입을 맞추었다. 어머니는 두 아이를 자기 곁으로 바싹 끌어당겨, 아이들이 기도하러 무릎을 꿇을 때 저마다 한 손씩 잡아 주었다. 피트 경은 아이들에게, 그리고 쉭쉭 김을 뿜는 차 주전자 맞은편으로 의자에 줄지어 앉은, 일요일 정장이나 제복을 차려입은 하인들에게 기도문을 읽어 주었다. 그날은 지체된 일들 탓에 아침 식사가 어찌나 늦어졌는지, 그들이 식탁에 둘러앉아 있는 동안 벌써 교회 종이 울리기 시작했다. 그리고 제인 부인은 몸이 너무 좋지 않다며 교회에 가지 못하겠다고 했는데, 사실 그녀의 마음은 가족 예배가 진행되는 내내 온통 딴 데 가 있었던 것이다.\n\n한편 로던 크롤리는 그레이트 곤트 거리를 서둘러 빠져나가, 곤트 하우스 정문에 달린 커다란 청동 메두사 머리를 두드려, 붉은빛과 은빛의 조끼를 걸친, 그 궁전의 문지기 노릇을 하는 자줏빛 실레노스 같은 사내를 불러냈다. 이 사내도 대령의 헝클어진 행색에 화들짝 놀라, 마치 상대가 억지로 밀고 들어올까 봐 겁이라도 난 듯 길을 막아섰다. 그러나 크롤리 대령은 그저 명함 한 장을 꺼내, 그것을 스타인 경에게 꼭 전하라고, 그리고 거기 적힌 주소를 눈여겨보라고, 또 크롤리 대령은 한 시 이후로 종일 세인트제임스 거리의 리전트 클럽에 있을 것이며 집에는 없을 것이라고 전하라고 각별히 일렀다. 살집 있고 붉은 얼굴의 사내는 그가 성큼성큼 걸어가는 뒷모습을 놀란 눈으로 바라보았다. 그렇게 일찍 나와 있던, 일요일 옷차림을 한 사람들도, 얼굴이 반들반들 빛나는 자선 학교 아이들도, 가게 문간에 기대선 채소 장수도, 예배가 시작되기 전에 햇살 속에서 덧창을 닫던 선술집 주인도 마찬가지였다. 그가 그곳에서 마차를 잡아 마부에게 나이츠브리지 병영으로 가자고 이르자, 마차 대기소의 사람들이 그의 행색을 두고 농지거리를 했다.\n\n그가 그곳에 다다랐을 무렵 온 동네의 종이 일제히 뎅그렁뎅그렁 울려 대고 있었다. 만약 그가 살펴보고 있었더라면, 옛 지인 애밀리아가 브롬프턴에서 러셀 광장으로 가는 길에 있는 것을 보았을지도 모른다. 무리 지은 학교 아이들이 교회를 향해 행진하고 있었고, 교외의 번들거리는 포장도로며 마차 지붕 위까지 일요일 나들이에 나선 사람들로 붐볐다. 그러나 대령은 너무도 바쁜 나머지 이런 광경에는 조금도 눈길을 주지 않았고, 나이츠브리지에 도착하자마자 옛 친구이자 전우인 맥머도 대위의 방으로 곧장 올라갔다. 크롤리는 그가 병영에 있는 것을 보고 흡족해했다.\n\n맥머도 대위는 워털루를 겪은 노련한 장교로 연대에서 크게 사랑받는 인물이었으나, 오로지 돈이 없는 탓에 최고위직에 오르지 못한 사람이었다. 그는 침대에 누운 채 오전 한나절을 느긋하게 즐기고 있었다. 그는 전날 밤 명예로운 조지 싱크바스 대위가 브롬프턴 광장의 자택에서 연대의 젊은 장교 몇몇과 발레단 숙녀 여럿을 초대해 베푼 흥겨운 만찬 자리에 다녀온 터였다. 그리고 늙은 맥은 나이와 신분을 막론하고 누구와도 격의 없이 어울리는 사람이라, 장군들이며 개 애호가들이며 오페라 무용수들이며 권투 선수들이며 한마디로 온갖 부류의 사람들과 사귀는 터였으니, 밤새 수고한 뒤 휴식을 취하는 중이었고, 당번도 아니어서 침대에 누워 있었던 것이다.\n\n그의 방은 전우들이 연대를 떠나 결혼하고 조용한 삶에 정착하면서 선물한 권투며 사냥이며 무용 그림들로 빙 둘러 걸려 있었다. 그리고 그는 이제 나이가 쉰에 가까웠고 그중 스물네 해를 군단에서 보냈으니, 진기한 박물관을 하나 갖춘 셈이었다. 그는 영국에서 손꼽히는 명사수 가운데 하나였고, 덩치 큰 사람치고는 손꼽히는 명기수 가운데 하나이기도 했다. 사실 크롤리가 군에 있던 시절 그와 크롤리는 맞수였다. 요컨대 맥머도 씨는 침대에 누워, 앞서 언급된 바로 그 시합, 즉 터트버리의 귀염둥이와 바킹의 푸주한이 벌인 시합에 관한 기사를 벨스 라이프에서 읽고 있었던 것이다. 짧게 깎은 자그마한 잿빛 머리에 비단 잠자리 모자를 쓰고, 붉은 얼굴과 코에, 물들인 커다란 콧수염을 기른, 수염이 까칠한 노전사였다.\n\n로던이 대위에게 입회인이 한 사람 필요하다고 말하자, 맥머도는 자기가 어떤 우정의 임무를 맡으라는 부름을 받았는지 더없이 잘 알아챘으니, 실로 그는 지인들을 위해 그런 일을 수십 건이나 더없이 신중하고 능란하게 처리해 온 사람이었다. 고인이 되신 애도받는 총사령관 전하께서도 이 점 때문에 맥머도를 더없이 아끼셨고, 그는 곤경에 처한 신사들이 흔히 찾는 피난처였다.\n\n\"무슨 소동이냐, 크롤리, 이 사람아?\" 늙은 전사가 말했다. \"또 노름 문제는 아니겠지, 응? 우리가 마커 대위를 쐈을 때처럼 말이야.\"\n\n\"그게—제 아내에 관한 일이에요.\" 크롤리가 눈을 내리깔고 얼굴을 새빨갛게 붉히며 대답했다.\n\n상대는 휘파람을 불었다. \"내가 늘 말했잖아, 그 여자가 자넬 차 버릴 거라고.\" 그가 입을 열었다—실제로 연대에서나 클럽에서나 크롤리 대령에게 닥칠 법한 운명을 두고 내기가 오갈 만큼, 그의 아내의 평판은 전우들과 세상 사람들 사이에서 그토록 하찮게 여겨졌던 것이다. 그러나 로던이 이런 의견 표명에 답하는 그 사나운 눈빛을 보고는, 맥머도는 더 길게 늘어놓는 것이 마땅치 않다고 여겼다.\n\n\"빠져나갈 길이 없는 건가, 이 사람아?\" 대위가 진지한 어조로 이어 말했다. \"그저 의심에 불과한 건가, 아니면—아니면 대체 무슨 일인가? 편지라도 있나? 조용히 덮을 수는 없겠나? 그런 종류의 일은 어쩔 수 없는 게 아니라면 떠들어 대지 않는 게 상책이야.\" '이제야 그 여자 정체를 알아챘다니.' 대위는 속으로 생각하며, 식당 식탁에서 크롤리 부인의 평판이 갈가리 찢기던 그 숱한 대화들을 떠올렸다.\n\n\"빠져나갈 길은 단 하나뿐이에요.\" 로던이 대답했다. \"그리고 그 길은 우리 둘 중 한 사람한테만 열려 있어요, 맥—무슨 말인지 아시겠어요? 저는 길에서 치워졌어요—붙잡혔다고요—그러고는 두 사람이 단둘이 같이 있는 걸 봤죠. 그자더러 거짓말쟁이에 비겁한 놈이라고 말하고, 때려눕혀서 흠씬 두들겨 줬어요.\"\n\n\"당해도 싸지.\" 맥머도가 말했다. \"그게 누군가?\"\n\n로던은 스타인 경이라고 대답했다.\n\n\"이런 빌어먹을! 후작이라니! 사람들은 그자가—그러니까, 사람들은 자네가—\"\n\n\"대체 무슨 소릴 하는 거예요?\" 로던이 버럭 고함을 질렀다. \"그럼 어떤 작자가 내 아내를 두고 수군대는 걸 들으면서도 나한테 말 안 해 줬다는 거예요, 맥?\"\n\n\"세상은 입방아가 심한 법이야, 이 사람아.\" 상대가 대답했다. \"어떤 멍청이들이 떠들어 댄 소릴 자네한테 일러바쳐 봤자 무슨 소용이 있었겠나?\"\n\n\"그건 정말 우정 없는 짓이었어요, 맥.\" 로던이 완전히 무너진 채 말하고는, 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 북받치는 감정에 그대로 몸을 내맡겼다. 그 광경에 맞은편에 앉은 강인한 늙은 노병마저 측은한 마음에 움찔할 정도였다. \"기운 내게, 이 사람아.\" 그가 말했다. \"후작이든 뭐든, 우리가 그놈한테 총알을 박아 줄 테니까, 빌어먹을 놈. 여자들로 말하면, 죄다 그 모양이지.\"\n\n\"제가 그 여자를 얼마나 사랑했는지 형은 몰라요.\" 로던이 거의 알아들을 수 없게 더듬더듬 말했다. \"제기랄, 저는 하인처럼 그 여자를 졸졸 따라다녔어요. 제가 가진 걸 죄다 그 여자한테 내줬다고요. 그 여자와 결혼하겠다고 거지가 됐어요. 맹세컨대, 그 여자가 갖고 싶어 하는 거라면 뭐든 사 주려고 제 시계까지 전당포에 잡혔어요. 그런데 그 여자는—그 여자는 그동안 내내 제 주머니만 채우면서, 제가 감옥에 갇혔을 때 빼내 줄 백 파운드조차 아까워했다고요.\" 그러고 나서 그는 사납고 두서없이, 그리고 그의 조언자가 한 번도 본 적 없는 동요 속에서, 맥머도에게 사건의 정황을 들려주었다. 그의 조언자는 그 이야기 속에 흘러나온 몇몇 단서를 붙들었다. \"어쩌면 그래도 그 여자가 결백할지도 모르지.\" 그가 말했다. \"그 여자도 그렇게 말한다며. 스타인은 전에도 그 집에서 그 여자와 백 번도 더 단둘이 있었잖나.\"\n\n\"그럴지도 모르죠.\" 로던이 슬프게 대답했다. \"하지만 이건 그리 결백해 보이지가 않아요.\" 그러고는 베키의 수첩에서 찾아낸 그 천 파운드짜리 지폐를 대위에게 보여 주었다. \"이게 그자가 그 여자한테 준 거예요, 맥. 그런데 그 여자는 제 모르게 이걸 숨겨 뒀고요. 집 안에 이 돈을 두고도, 제가 갇혔을 때 절 편들기를 거부했단 말이에요.\" 대위도 그 돈을 숨겨 둔 것이 무척이나 보기 흉하다는 점은 인정할 수밖에 없었다.\n\n이렇게 두 사람이 의논을 나누는 동안, 로던은 맥머도 대위의 하인을 커즌 거리로 보내, 그 집 하인에게 대령이 몹시 필요로 하는 옷 가방 하나를 내주라는 지시를 전하게 했다. 그리고 그 하인이 자리를 비운 사이, 로던과 그의 입회인은 무척 애를 쓰며 존슨 사전의 큰 도움을 받아—그것이 그들에게 요긴하게 쓰였다—편지 한 통을 작성했는데, 입회인이 그것을 스타인 경에게 보내기로 했다. 맥머도 대위는 로던 크롤리 대령을 대리하여 스타인 후작을 방문하는 영광을 누리게 되었으며, 자신은 대령으로부터, 각하께서 요구하실 의향임이 분명한, 그리고 오늘 아침의 정황으로 말미암아 불가피해진 결투에 관하여 어떠한 합의든 맺을 권한을 위임받았음을 알리고자 한다는 내용이었다. 맥머도 대위는 더없이 정중한 태도로 스타인 경에게 입회인을 한 사람 지명하여 자신(맥머도 대위)이 그와 연락할 수 있도록 해 주기를 청하고, 결투가 가능한 한 지체 없이 이루어지기를 바란다고 했다.\n\n추신에서 대위는, 크롤리 대령이 스타인 후작의 소유물이라고 추정할 만한 거액의 은행권 한 장을 자신이 소지하고 있다고 적었다. 그리고 자신은 대령을 대신하여 그 지폐를 임자에게 돌려주기를 간절히 바란다고 했다.\n\n이 편지가 다 작성될 무렵, 대위의 하인이 커즌 거리에 있는 크롤리 대령의 집으로 갔던 심부름에서 돌아왔는데, 가지러 보냈던 융단 가방과 여행용 트렁크는 없이, 몹시 어리둥절하고 야릇한 얼굴로 돌아왔다.\n\n\"그것들을 안 내주는데요.\" 하인이 말했다. \"집 안이 온통 한바탕 난리가 났고, 모든 게 뒤죽박죽이에요. 집주인이 들어와서 다 차지해 버렸고요. 하인들은 응접실에서 술을 퍼마시고 있더라고요. 그자들 말로는—대령님이 은식기를 가지고 달아났다더군요.\" 하인이 잠시 멈췄다가 덧붙였다. \"하인 하나는 벌써 떠나 버렸고요. 그리고 심슨이라는 자는, 아주 시끄럽게 떠들고 곤드레만드레 취한 자인데, 자기 품삯을 다 받기 전에는 집에서 아무것도 못 나간다고 합니다.\"\n\n메이페어에서 벌어진 이 자그마한 변란에 관한 이야기는, 안 그래도 무척 침울하던 대화에 놀라움과 더불어 약간의 흥취를 더해 주었다. 두 장교는 로던의 낭패를 두고 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"꼬마가 집에 없어서 다행이에요.\" 로던이 손톱을 깨물며 말했다. \"그 애 기억하시죠, 맥? 승마 학교에서 말이에요. 그 펄쩍펄쩍 뛰는 말에 어찌나 잘 올라타 있던지! 안 그래요?\"\n\n\"그랬지, 이 사람아.\" 마음씨 좋은 대위가 말했다.\n\n그때 어린 로던은 화이트프라이어스 학교 예배당에서 쉰 명 남짓한 가운 입은 학생들 가운데 하나로 앉아, 설교가 아니라 다음 토요일에 집에 가는 일을 생각하고 있었다. 그날이면 아버지가 분명 용돈을 쥐여 줄 테고 어쩌면 연극 구경에 데려가 줄지도 몰랐다.\n\n\"그 녀석은 정말 야무진 아이예요.\" 아버지가 여전히 아들 생각에 잠긴 채 이어 말했다. \"이봐요, 맥, 만약 일이 잘못되면—제가 쓰러지면—형이 그 애를 가서 한번 봐 주셨으면 해요, 아시겠죠. 그래서 제가 그 애를 무척 사랑했다고, 그렇게 전해 주세요. 그리고—제기랄—이 사람아, 그 애한테 이 금 소맷단추를 주세요. 제가 가진 건 이게 전부예요.\" 그는 검은 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸고, 그 위로 눈물이 흘러내려 하얀 골을 냈다. 맥머도 씨도 비단 잠자리 모자를 벗어 두 눈을 문지를 일이 생겼다.\n\n\"내려가서 아침 식사 좀 시켜 와라.\" 그가 큰 소리로 명랑하게 하인에게 말했다. \"뭘 들겠나, 크롤리? 매콤하게 양념한 콩팥 요리하고 청어—그걸로 하지. 그리고 클레이, 대령님 입으실 옷 좀 챙겨 놔라. 우리는 늘 덩치가 거의 비슷했지, 로던, 이 사람아. 그리고 처음 군단에 들어왔을 때만큼 우리 둘 다 몸이 가볍지가 않으니 말이야.\" 그러고는 대령이 옷을 입도록 내버려 둔 채, 맥머도는 벽 쪽으로 돌아누워, 친구가 몸단장을 마치고 자기가 자유로이 단장을 시작할 수 있게 될 때까지 다시 벨스 라이프를 읽어 나갔다.\n\n이번에는 귀족을 만나러 가는 길이었으므로, 맥머도 대위는 이 단장을 각별히 공들여 해냈다. 그는 콧수염에 밀랍을 발라 번쩍번쩍 윤이 나게 다듬고, 꽉 죄는 크라바트에 말끔한 황갈색 조끼를 차려입었다. 그래서 크롤리가 친구보다 앞서 들어가 있던 식당의 젊은 장교들이 모두 아침 식사 자리에서 맥의 차림새를 칭찬하며, 이번 일요일에 결혼이라도 하느냐고 물을 정도였다.\n\n전날 밤의 사건들이 그 담대한 영혼을 빠뜨려 놓은 망연자실하고 혼란스러운 상태에서 베키가 정신을 차린 것은, 커즌 거리의 예배당 종들이 오후 예배를 알리며 울릴 무렵이었다. 그녀는 침대에서 일어나 자기 손으로 종을 당기기 시작했다. 몇 시간 전에 그녀 곁을 떠난 프랑스인 하녀를 부르기 위함이었다.\n\n로던 크롤리 부인은 여러 차례 종을 당겼으나 헛수고였다. 마지막에는 종 끈이 뜯겨 나갈 만큼 거세게 당겼건만, 피핀 양은 끝내 모습을 드러내지 않았다. 그렇다, 여주인이 잔뜩 약이 올라 종 끈을 손에 쥔 채, 머리카락을 어깨에 늘어뜨리고 층계참까지 나와 시중드는 하녀를 거듭 불러 댔어도 소용없었다.\n\n사실인즉, 그녀는 벌써 여러 시간 전에 그 집을 떠나 버린 것이었다. 우리네 사이에서 이른바 \"프랑스식 작별\"이라 부르는, 그런 식의 무단이탈이었다. 응접실에 떨어진 장신구들을 주워 챙긴 뒤, 마드무아젤은 자기 방으로 올라가 그곳에서 제 상자들을 꾸리고 끈으로 묶었으며, 종종걸음으로 빠져나가 손수 삯마차를 부르고, 제 손으로 트렁크들을 끌고 내려왔다. 다른 하인들에게는 도움을 청하지도 않았는데(어차피 그들은 그녀를 진심으로 미워했으니 거절했을 것이 뻔했다), 그 누구에게도 작별 인사 한마디 없이 커즌 거리를 떠나 버린 것이었다.\n\n그녀가 보기에, 그 자그마한 살림집에서는 이미 판이 끝난 셈이었다. 피핀은 삯마차를 타고 떠났다. 우리는 비슷한 처지에서 그녀와 같은 나라의 더 고귀한 인물들도 그렇게 떠나는 것을 알고 있다. 다만 그녀는 그런 이들보다 한결 빈틈없고 운이 좋아서, 제 물건뿐 아니라 여주인의 물건 일부까지(과연 그 부인에게 무슨 물건이라 할 것이 조금이라도 있었다면 말이지만) 잘 챙겨 두었다. 앞서 말한 그 장신구들과, 진작부터 눈독을 들여 온 마음에 드는 드레스 몇 벌만 가져간 것이 아니었다. 화려하게 도금한 루이 십사세풍 촛대 네 개, 금박 입힌 앨범과 기념첩과 미인 화보집 여섯 권, 한때 뒤바리 부인의 것이었던 금에 칠보를 입힌 코담뱃갑, 그리고 베키가 그 사랑스러운 분홍빛 짧은 편지를 쓸 때 쓰던 더없이 앙증맞은 잉크스탠드와 자개로 장식한 압지첩까지, 모조리 마드무아젤 피핀과 함께 커즌 거리의 그 집에서 자취를 감추었다. 로던이 들이닥쳐 망쳐 버린 그 자그마한 잔치를 위해 식탁에 차려 놓았던 은식기도 죄다 사라졌다. 마드무아젤이 남겨 둔 도금 식기들은 너무 거추장스러웠던 모양이고, 아마 그런 까닭에 부지깽이 따위 난로 도구들과 벽난로 거울, 그리고 자단으로 만든 오두막형 피아노도 함께 남겨 둔 것이 분명했다.\n\n훗날 그녀와 무척 닮은 한 부인이 파리 엘데 거리에서 모자 가게를 운영하며 신용을 두텁게 쌓고 스테인 경의 후원까지 누렸다. 이 인물은 늘 영국을 세상에서 가장 음흉한 나라라고 말하고 다녔으며, 어린 제자들에게 자신이 그 섬나라 토박이들에게 끔찍하게 도둑맞았노라고 이야기하곤 했다. 스테인 후작이 생타마랑트 부인에게 그토록 친절을 베푼 것은 의심할 바 없이 그녀의 불운에 대한 동정심 때문이었으리라. 부디 그녀가 받아 마땅한 만큼 번창하기를 빈다. 그녀는 우리 허영의 시장 한 모퉁이에는 더 이상 나타나지 않는다.\n\n아래층에서 웅성거리는 소리와 부산한 기척이 들려오자, 자신의 부름에 응하지 않는 하인들의 뻔뻔함에 분개한 크롤리 부인은 실내복을 휙 걸치고는, 그 소리가 새어 나오는 응접실로 위풍당당하게 내려갔다.\n\n거기에는 요리사가 시커멓게 그을린 얼굴로, 그 아름다운 사라사 무명 소파에 래글스 부인과 나란히 앉아 그녀에게 마라스키노 술을 따라 주고 있었다. 설탕 덩어리 모양 단추가 달린 옷을 입고, 베키의 분홍빛 짧은 편지를 들고 다니며 그녀의 작은 마차에 그토록 날렵하게 뛰어오르던 시동 녀석은, 지금 크림 접시에 손가락을 처박고 있었다. 하인은 래글스에게 말을 건네고 있었는데, 래글스의 얼굴은 당혹과 비탄으로 가득 차 있었다. 그런데도, 문이 열려 있고 베키가 불과 몇 발짝 떨어진 곳에서 대여섯 번이나 소리쳐 불렀건만, 시중드는 자들 가운데 그 누구도 그녀의 부름에 따르지 않았던 것이다. \"한 방울만 더 들어요, 자 어서요, 래글스 부인.\" 베키가 흰 캐시미어 실내복 자락을 펄럭이며 들어섰을 때 요리사가 그렇게 말하고 있었다.\n\n\"심프슨! 트로터!\" 이 집의 여주인이 노발대발하여 외쳤다. \"내가 부르는 소리를 들었으면서 어찌 감히 여기 머물러 있는 게냐? 어찌 감히 내 앞에서 앉아 있어? 내 하녀는 어디 있느냐?\" 시동은 순간적인 공포에 손가락을 입에서 빼냈으나, 요리사는 마라스키노 한 잔을 비웠다. 래글스 부인은 이미 충분히 마신 터였다. 요리사는 작은 도금 잔의 술을 들이켜며 그 너머로 베키를 빤히 노려보았다. 그 술이 이 가증스러운 반역자에게 용기를 불어넣은 모양이었다.\n\n\"당신 소파라니, 참!\" 요리사가 말했다. \"난 래글스 부인 소파에 앉아 있는 거예요. 일어나지 마세요, 래글스 부인 마님. 난 래글스 내외분 소파에 앉아 있는 거라고요. 두 분이 정직하게 번 돈으로 산 거고, 값도 어지간히 비싸게 치른 물건이지요. 그리고 내 생각엔, 내가 받을 품삯을 받을 때까지 여기 앉아 있으려면 한참을 앉아 있어야 할 것 같네요, 래글스 부인. 그래, 앉아 있고말고요. 하하!\" 그러고는 그 술을 한 잔 더 따라, 한결 더 흉측하고 비아냥거리는 태도로 들이켰다.\n\n\"트로터! 심프슨! 저 술 취한 망나니를 당장 내쫓아라.\" 크롤리 부인이 비명을 질렀다.\n\n\"안 할 거요.\" 하인 트로터가 말했다. \"직접 내쫓으시구려. 우리 품삯이나 내놓고, 나도 내쫓아 보슈. 그럼 우린 잽싸게 나가 줄 테니.\"\n\n\"너희 모두 나를 모욕하려고 여기 모여 있는 게냐?\" 베키가 격분하여 외쳤다. \"크롤리 대령이 집에 돌아오면 내가—\"\n\n이 말에 하인들이 거칠게 와하하 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 다만 여전히 더없이 침울한 낯빛을 짓고 있던 래글스만은 그 웃음에 끼지 않았다. \"그 양반은 안 돌아와요.\" 트로터가 말을 이었다. \"사람을 보내 짐을 가져가려고 했지요. 래글스 씨는 내주려 했지만 내가 안 내줬소. 게다가 그자가 대령이라는 것도 나만큼이나 못 믿겠더이다. 그 양반은 줄행랑쳤고, 당신도 그 뒤를 쫓아갈 셈인가 본데. 당신네 둘 다 사기꾼이나 진배없어. 나한테 으름장 놓을 생각일랑 마슈. 안 통하니까. 우리 품삯이나 내놓으라고. 품삯 말이오.\" 트로터의 벌게진 얼굴과 어눌한 말투로 보아, 그 역시 술기운을 빌린 것이 분명했다.\n\n\"래글스 씨.\" 베키가 분통이 치밀어 말했다. \"설마 저 술 취한 자에게 내가 모욕당하도록 가만 내버려 두지는 않으시겠지요?\" \"조용히 해요, 트로터. 자 어서요.\" 시동 심프슨이 말했다. 그는 여주인의 딱한 처지에 마음이 흔들린 터라, \"술 취한\"이라는 말에 하인이 무지막지하게 발끈하며 부인하려는 것을 가까스로 막아 냈다.\n\n\"오, 마님.\" 래글스가 말했다. \"이런 꼴을 보고 살게 될 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐습니다요. 저는 태어났을 때부터 크롤리 집안을 알고 지냈지요. 크롤리 아씨 댁에서 삼십 년이나 집사로 일했고요. 그 집안 사람이 저를 망하게 할 줄이야, 그래요, 저를 망하게 할 줄이야 어찌 알았겠습니까.\" 가엾은 사내는 눈물을 글썽이며 말했다. \"값을 치러 주시겠습니까요? 마님은 이 집에서 사 년을 사셨지요. 제 재산을, 제 식기며 침구를 다 쓰셨고요. 우유랑 버터 값만 이백 파운드 외상이 있습니다요. 오믈렛 만드신다고 갓 낳은 달걀이 있어야 했지요, 애완견한테 먹일 크림도 있어야 했고요.\"\n\n\"제 핏줄이 뭘 먹는지도 아랑곳하지 않으셨다니까요.\" 요리사가 끼어들었다. \"내가 없었으면 그 도련님은 여러 번 굶어 죽었을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그 도련님은 이제 자선 학교 학생이라우, 요리사 양반.\" 트로터가 술 취한 목소리로 \"하하!\" 웃으며 말했다. 그리고 정직한 래글스는 비통한 어조로 자기 한탄을 줄줄이 늘어놓았다. 그가 한 말은 모두 사실이었다. 베키와 그 남편이 그를 망쳐 놓은 것이다. 다음 주에 만기가 돌아오는 어음들이 있는데 갚을 길이 없었다. 크롤리 집안을 믿었던 탓에, 그는 가게와 집을 차압당하고 쫓겨날 판이었다. 그의 눈물과 한탄에 베키는 그 어느 때보다 짜증이 났다.\n\n\"다들 나에게 등을 돌린 모양이로구나.\" 그녀가 매섭게 말했다. \"무엇들을 바라느냐? 일요일에는 너희에게 돈을 줄 수가 없다. 내일 다시 오면 죄다 갚아 주마. 나는 크롤리 대령이 너희와 셈을 마친 줄 알았다. 내일 그가 갚아 줄 게다. 내 명예를 걸고 너희에게 말하건대, 그이는 오늘 아침에 천오백 파운드를 수첩에 넣고 집을 나갔다. 나에게는 한 푼도 안 남겼어. 그이에게 청구해라. 모자와 숄을 다오, 내가 나가서 그이를 찾아오마. 오늘 아침에 우리 사이에 다툼이 있었다. 너희 모두 그걸 아는 모양이니. 내 맹세코 너희 모두에게 돈을 치러 주마. 그이는 좋은 자리를 얻었어. 내가 나가서 그이를 찾게 해 다오.\"\n\n이 대담무쌍한 말에 래글스를 비롯해 거기 있던 다른 자들은 어안이 벙벙하여 서로를 마주 보았고, 레베카는 그 틈에 그들을 떠났다. 그녀는 위층으로 올라가, 이번에는 프랑스인 하녀의 도움 없이 직접 옷을 차려입었다. 로던의 방에 들어가 보니, 트렁크 하나와 가방 하나가 옮겨 갈 채비를 마치고 꾸려져 있었고, 연필로 적은 쪽지가 붙어 있어 찾으러 오면 내주라고 일러두고 있었다. 이어 프랑스인 하녀의 다락방에 들어가 보니 모든 것이 말끔히 치워져 있었고, 그곳 서랍들도 죄다 비어 있었다. 그녀는 바닥에 떨어져 있던 장신구들이 떠올랐고, 그 여자가 달아났음을 확신했다. \"맙소사! 나 같은 불운이 또 어디 있단 말인가?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이토록 가까이 와서, 모든 걸 잃다니. 정녕 다 너무 늦은 걸까?\" 아니다. 아직 한 번의 기회가 더 남아 있었다.\n\n그녀는 옷을 차려입고, 이번에는 아무에게도 방해받지 않고 길을 나섰으나, 혼자였다. 네 시였다. 그녀는 거리들을 빠르게 걸어 내려갔고(마차 삯을 치를 돈이 없었던 것이다), 그레이트 곤트 거리에 있는 피트 크롤리 경의 집 문 앞에 다다를 때까지 한 번도 멈추지 않았다. 제인 크롤리 부인은 어디 있는가? 그녀는 교회에 가 있었다. 베키로서는 다행스러운 일이었다. 피트 경은 서재에 있었고 방해하지 말라고 일러 둔 터였으나, 그녀는 그를 꼭 만나야 했다. 그녀는 제복 입은 문지기를 단숨에 지나쳐, 놀란 준남작이 미처 신문을 내려놓기도 전에 피트 경의 방 안에 들어서 있었다.\n\n그는 얼굴이 벌게져, 크게 놀라고 두려워하는 기색으로 그녀에게서 움찔 물러섰다.\n\n\"그런 눈으로 보지 마세요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저는 죄가 없어요, 피트, 다정한 피트. 당신은 한때 제 벗이었잖아요. 하느님 앞에 맹세코 저는 죄가 없어요. 그렇게 보일 뿐이에요. 모든 것이 저에게 불리하지요. 아, 게다가 이런 순간에! 제 모든 희망이 막 이루어지려던 바로 그때에, 우리에게 행복이 마련되어 있던 바로 그때에 말이에요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 내가 신문에서 본 게 사실이란 말이오?\" 피트 경이 말했다. 거기에 실린 한 단락이 그를 몹시 놀라게 했던 것이다.\n\n\"사실이에요. 스테인 경이 금요일 밤에, 그 끔찍한 무도회가 있던 밤에 제게 말해 주었어요. 그이는 이 여섯 달 사이 언제든 자리를 주겠노라는 약속을 받아 두었지요. 식민지 담당 장관인 마터 씨가 어제 그분께, 발령이 났다고 말했대요. 그런데 그 운 나쁜 체포 소동이 벌어졌고, 그 끔찍한 마주침이 일어난 거예요. 제가 지은 죄라고는 로던을 위해 너무 헌신했다는 것뿐이에요. 저는 전에도 백 번이나 스테인 경을 혼자 만났어요. 고백하건대, 저에게는 로던이 모르는 돈이 좀 있었어요. 그이가 돈을 얼마나 함부로 쓰는지 모르세요? 그런 그이에게 제가 감히 그 돈을 맡길 수 있었겠어요?\" 그렇게 그녀는 완벽하게 앞뒤가 맞는 이야기를 줄줄 풀어, 어리둥절해하는 시숙의 귀에 쏟아부었다.\n\n그 내용인즉 다음과 같았다. 베키는 더없이 솔직하게, 그러나 깊이 뉘우치는 태도로 털어놓았다. 스테인 경이 자신에게 호의를 보이는 것을 알아채고(이 대목에서 피트는 얼굴을 붉혔다), 또 제 정조에는 자신이 있었기에, 그 위대한 귀족의 애착을 자신과 가족의 이익을 위해 돌려보기로 마음먹었노라고. \"저는 당신을 위해 귀족 작위를 얻어 보려고 했어요, 피트.\" 그녀가 말했다(시숙은 또다시 얼굴이 벌게졌다). \"우린 그 얘기를 나눴잖아요. 당신의 재능과 스테인 경의 영향력이면, 이 끔찍한 재앙이 닥쳐 우리 모든 희망을 끝장내지만 않았더라도 충분히 가망이 있었지요. 하지만 무엇보다 먼저, 제 목적은 사랑하는 남편을, 그이가 저를 그토록 푸대접하고 의심했음에도 제가 사랑하는 그 사람을 구해 내는 것이었어요. 우리 머리 위로 들이닥치던 가난과 파멸에서 그이를 건져 내는 것 말이에요. 저는 스테인 경이 저에게 호의를 보이는 것을 알았어요.\" 그녀가 눈을 내리깔며 말했다. \"고백하건대, 저는 그분 마음에 들기 위해, 정숙한 여인으로서 할 수 있는 한도 안에서, 그분의—그분의 존중을 얻기 위해 제가 할 수 있는 모든 것을 했어요. 코번트리 섬 총독이 죽었다는 소식이 온 것이 바로 금요일 아침이었는데, 그분께서 즉시 사랑하는 제 남편을 위해 그 자리를 확보해 주셨지요. 그건 그이를 위한 깜짝 선물로 마련된 것이었어요. 그이가 오늘 신문에서 보게 될 참이었지요. 그 끔찍한 체포 소동이 벌어진 뒤에도(그 비용은 스테인 경이 너그럽게 치러 주겠다고 하셔서, 저는 어떤 의미로는 남편을 도우러 갈 수가 없게 되었지요), 그분은 저와 함께 웃으시며, 사랑하는 로던이 그 끔찍한 빚쟁이 집에서 자기 발령 소식을 신문에서 읽으면 위로받을 거라고 말씀하셨어요. 그러고 나서—그러고 나서 그이가 집에 돌아온 거예요. 그이의 의심에 불이 붙었지요. 그 끔찍한 장면이 그분과, 잔인하고 잔인한 제 로던 사이에 벌어졌어요. 아, 하느님, 이제 무슨 일이 벌어질까요? 피트, 다정한 피트! 저를 가엾이 여겨 주시고, 우리를 화해시켜 주세요!\" 그렇게 말하며 그녀는 무릎을 꿇고 엎드려, 울음을 터뜨리며 피트의 손을 붙잡고는 격정적으로 그 손에 입을 맞췄다.\n\n바로 이 자세를 하고 있을 때, 교회에서 돌아온 제인 부인이 로던 크롤리 부인이 그 안에 들어앉아 있다는 말을 듣자마자 남편의 방으로 달려와, 준남작과 그 시숙의 아내를 발견한 것이었다.\n\n\"저 여자가 뻔뻔하게 이 집에 들어왔다니 기가 막히는군요.\" 제인 부인이 온몸을 부들부들 떨며, 얼굴이 새파랗게 질려 말했다(부인은 아침 식사 직후에 하녀를 내보냈는데, 그 하녀가 래글스 및 로던 크롤리 댁 식솔들과 이야기를 나누고 왔으며, 그들은 자기들이 아는 것뿐 아니라 그 이상의 것까지 그 이야기를, 그리고 그 밖의 숱한 이야기들까지 하녀에게 죄다 일러바친 터였다). \"어찌 감히 크롤리 부인이—정직한 집안의—집에 발을 들인단 말입니까?\"\n\n피트 경은 아내가 이토록 기세를 부리는 데 놀라 움찔 물러섰다. 베키는 여전히 무릎 꿇은 자세로 피트 경의 손에 매달려 있었다.\n\n\"제가 다 알지는 못한다고 부인께 말해 주세요. 제가 결백하다고 말해 주세요, 다정한 피트.\" 그녀가 훌쩍이며 말했다.\n\n\"정말이지, 여보, 당신이 크롤리 부인에게 부당하게 구는 것 같소.\" 피트 경이 말했다. 이 말에 레베카는 크게 안도했다. \"정말로 나는 이 사람이—\"\n\n\"이 사람이 뭐라고요?\" 제인 부인이 외쳤다. 그녀의 맑은 목소리가 떨려 나왔고, 말하는 동안 가슴이 격렬하게 뛰었다. \"사악한 여자라고요? 매정한 어미요, 부정한 아내라고요? 저 여자는 제 어린 아들을 사랑한 적이 한 번도 없어요. 그 아이는 이리로 도망쳐 와서, 어미가 자기에게 얼마나 모질게 구는지 제게 이야기하곤 했지요. 저 여자는 어느 집안에 들어가든 불행을 끌고 들어와서, 그 사악한 아첨과 거짓말로 가장 거룩한 정마저 약하게 만들려고 안달이었어요. 저 여자는 남편을 속였어요, 모든 이를 속였듯이 말이에요. 그 영혼은 허영과 속물근성과 온갖 죄악으로 새카맣지요. 저 여자에게 손이 닿기만 해도 몸서리가 쳐져요. 제 아이들은 저 여자 눈에 띄지 않게 해 둔답니다.\"\n\n\"제인!\" 피트 경이 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다. \"이건 정말이지 말이 지나친—\" \"저는 당신께 진실하고 충실한 아내였어요, 피트.\" 제인 부인이 굴하지 않고 말을 이었다. \"저는 하느님 앞에 세운 혼인 서약을 지켜 왔고, 아내로서 마땅히 그래야 하듯 순종하고 다정하게 굴어 왔어요. 하지만 올바른 순종에도 한계가 있는 법이에요. 그러니 분명히 말씀드리는데, 저 여자가 다시 우리 지붕 아래 들어오는 것을 저는 결코 참지 못하겠어요. 저 여자가 들어온다면, 저와 제 아이들은 이 집을 나가겠어요. 저 여자는 그리스도교도와 한자리에 앉을 자격이 없어요. 당신은—당신은 저 여자와 저 가운데 하나를 택하셔야 해요.\" 그러고는 부인은 제 대담함에 스스로 떨면서 방을 휙 나가 버렸고, 레베카와 피트 경은 적잖이 놀란 채 남겨졌다.\n\n베키로 말하자면, 상처받기는커녕 오히려 흐뭇해했다. \"다이아몬드 걸쇠는 당신이 주신 거였어요.\" 그녀가 피트 경에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. 그리고 그녀가 그곳을 떠나기 전에(이 장면을 제인 부인이 위층 화장실 창문으로 내다보고 있었으리라는 것은 틀림없는 일이다), 준남작은 가서 동생을 찾아 화해를 주선해 보겠노라고 약속했다.\n\n로던은 연대의 젊은 친구들 몇이 회식실에 앉아 아침을 먹고 있는 것을 발견했고, 별 어려움 없이 그 식사 자리에, 그리고 이 젊은 신사들이 기운을 차리고자 들고 있던 매운 양념 닭다리 구이며 소다수에 끼게 되었다. 이어 그들은 그 요일과 제 나이에 어울리는 대화를 나누었다. 배터시에서 열릴 다음 비둘기 사격 시합과, 거기에 로스와 오스발디스턴을 두고 건 내기 이야기, 프랑스 오페라단의 아리안 양과, 누가 그녀를 버렸는지, 그리고 팬서 카가 어떻게 그녀를 위로해 주었는지에 대한 이야기, 또 도살자와 애완동물 사이의 시합이며 그것이 짜고 친 판일 가능성에 대한 이야기 따위였다. 콧수염 한 쌍을 기르려고 안간힘을 쓰고 있는 열일곱 살의 영웅 탠디먼 청년은 그 시합을 직접 보고 왔고, 더없이 전문가다운 태도로 그 경기와 선수들의 상태에 대해 떠들어 댔다. 그가 바로 자기 사두마차에 도살자를 태워 경기장까지 데려다주고, 전날 밤 내내 그와 함께 지낸 장본인이었다. 부정만 없었더라면 도살자가 틀림없이 이겼으리라는 것이었다. 권투계의 그 노련한 패거리가 죄다 끼어 있었고, 탠디먼은 돈을 못 내겠다는 것이었다. 그래, 빌어먹을, 못 내겠다는 것이었다. 지금은 크리브의 술집에서 제법 빠삭한 솜씨를 자랑하는 이 젊은 기수가, 토피 사탕을 여전히 좋아하고 이튼에서 회초리를 맞던 것이 불과 일 년 전 일이었다.\n\n그렇게 그들은 무희며 시합이며 술이며 화류계 여자들 이야기를 이어 갔고, 이윽고 맥머도가 내려와 청년들과 그 대화에 합류했다. 그는 그들의 풋내기 시절에 무슨 각별한 존중을 보여야 한다고는 여기지 않는 듯했다. 그 노인은 자기 이야기를 척척 끼워 넣었는데, 그것은 거기 있던 가장 어린 난봉꾼이 들려줄 수 있는 그 어떤 이야기 못지않게 진한 것이었다. 자기 머리가 희끗희끗하다거나 그들 얼굴이 매끈하다거나 하는 것도 그를 멈추게 하지 못했다. 늙은 맥은 그 걸쭉한 이야기 솜씨로 유명했다. 그는 딱히 부인들에게 인기 있는 부류는 아니었다. 그러니까, 사람들이 그를 식사에 초대할 때는 어머니 댁보다는 정부의 집으로 부르는 쪽이었다는 말이다. 아마 그의 삶보다 더 낮은 삶이란 거의 없을 테지만, 그는 그런대로 제 삶에 흡족해했고, 더없이 선량하고 소박하며 겸손한 태도로 그 삶을 살아갔다.\n\n맥이 푸짐한 아침 식사를 끝마칠 무렵에는 다른 이들 대부분이 식사를 마친 뒤였다. 젊은 바리나스 경은 어마어마하게 큰 해포석 파이프를 피우고 있었고, 위그 대위는 시가를 즐기고 있었다. 저 사납고 자그마한 악동 탠디먼은 두 다리 사이에 자기 작은 불테리어를 끼고서, 듀시스 대위를 상대로 동전 던지기에 온 힘을 쏟고 있었다(그 친구는 언제나 무슨 노름이든 하고 있었다). 그리고 맥과 로던은 함께 클럽으로 발길을 옮겼는데, 물론 두 사람 다 자기들 머릿속을 차지하고 있는 그 용건에 대해서는 한마디도 내비치지 않았다. 도리어 두 사람 다 그 대화에 제법 명랑하게 어울렸으니, 굳이 흥을 깰 까닭이 있겠는가? 허영의 시장에서는 잔치며 음주며 음담패설이며 웃음이, 온갖 다른 일들과 나란히 굴러가는 법이다. 로던과 그의 벗이 세인트제임스 거리를 내려가 클럽에 들어설 무렵, 사람들이 교회에서 쏟아져 나오고 있었다.\n\n평소 클럽의 큰 정면 창문 밖을 멍하니 입 벌리고 히죽거리며 내다보곤 하는 늙은 멋쟁이들과 단골들은 아직 제자리에 당도하지 않은 터였다. 신문 열람실은 거의 텅 비어 있었다. 한 사람은 로던이 모르는 자였고, 또 한 사람은 휘스트 노름으로 그에게 약간의 빚이 있어 그런 까닭에 그가 마주치고 싶지 않은 자였다. 세 번째 사람은 탁자에서 일요판 〈로열리스트〉(추문 보도와 교회 및 왕실에 대한 충성으로 유명한 정기 간행물이었다)를 읽고 있었는데, 자못 관심 어린 눈으로 크롤리를 올려다보며 말했다. \"크롤리, 축하하오.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이오?\" 대령이 말했다.\n\n\"〈옵서버〉에도 〈로열리스트〉에도 났던데.\" 스미스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"뭐가 말이오?\" 로던이 얼굴이 새빨개지며 외쳤다. 그는 스테인 경과의 그 사건이 벌써 인쇄물에 났구나 싶었던 것이다. 스미스는 대령이 신문을 집어 들고 떨면서 읽기 시작하는 모습에 드러난 그 동요를, 의아한 눈빛으로 미소 지으며 올려다보았다.\n\n스미스 씨와 브라운 씨(로던과 휘스트 빚을 셈해야 할 사이인 신사였다)는 대령이 들어오기 직전까지 그에 대해 이야기를 나누던 참이었다.\n\n\"마침 딱 좋은 때에 들어왔구먼.\" 스미스가 말했다. \"내 짐작에 크롤리는 한 푼도 없는 신세였을 거요.\"\n\n\"바람이 누구에게나 좋게 부는 격이지.\" 브라운 씨가 말했다. \"이제 그자가 나한테 진 빚 이십오 파운드를 안 갚고는 떠나지 못할 테니.\"\n\n\"봉급이 얼마요?\" 스미스가 물었다.\n\n\"이삼천 파운드쯤.\" 상대가 대답했다. \"하지만 기후가 어찌나 지독한지, 오래 누리지를 못한다오. 리버시지는 십팔 개월 만에 죽었고, 그 전 사람은 육 주 만에 갔다더군.\"\n\n\"어떤 이들은 그자의 형이 아주 영리한 사람이라고들 하더이다. 난 늘 그 인간이 지독하게 따분하기만 하던데.\" 스미스가 내뱉었다. \"그래도 든든한 연줄이 있는 게 분명해. 그가 대령에게 그 자리를 얻어 준 게 틀림없으니까.\"\n\n\"그가?\" 브라운이 코웃음을 치며 말했다. \"쳇. 그 자리를 얻어 준 건 스테인 경이라오.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 말이오?\"\n\n\"정숙한 아내는 남편의 면류관이라지 않소.\" 상대가 알쏭달쏭하게 대답하고는, 신문을 마저 읽으러 가 버렸다.\n\n한편 로던은 〈로열리스트〉에서 다음과 같은 놀라운 단락을 읽었다.\n\n코번트리 섬 총독직.—국왕 폐하의 군함 옐로잭 호(함장 존더스)가 코번트리 섬에서 서신과 서류를 가져왔다. 총독 토머스 리버시지 경 각하께서 스왐프턴에 창궐한 열병의 희생양이 되셨다. 번창하던 식민지에서 그의 죽음을 깊이 애도하는 분위기다. 듣건대 그 총독직이 워털루 전투의 공로로 이름난 장교, 무공훈장 수훈자 로던 크롤리 대령에게 제안되었다고 한다. 우리 식민지의 사무를 관장하는 데에는 공인된 용맹을 갖춘 인물뿐 아니라 행정적 재능을 갖춘 인물도 필요한 법인데, 코번트리 섬에서 일어난 이 애통한 공석을 메우고자 식민지청이 선발한 신사라면 그가 곧 맡게 될 그 직책에 더없이 걸맞은 인물임이 분명하리라 의심치 않는다.\n\n\"코번트리 섬이라! 그게 어디였더라? 누가 자네를 그 총독 자리에 임명했단 말인가? 자넨 나를 비서관으로 데려가야 하네, 친구.\" 맥머도 대위가 웃으며 말했다. 크롤리와 그의 벗이 그 발표를 두고 어리둥절하고 당혹스러워하며 앉아 있는데, 클럽 종업원이 대령에게 명함 한 장을 가져왔다. 거기에는 웬햄 씨라는 이름이 새겨져 있었고, 크롤리 대령을 뵙기를 청한다고 적혀 있었다.\n\n대령과 그의 부관은 그 신사를 맞으러 나갔는데, 그가 스테인 경의 사자(使者)이리라고 옳게 짐작한 터였다. \"잘 지내시오, 크롤리? 만나서 반갑소이다.\" 웬햄 씨가 부드러운 미소를 지으며, 더없이 다정하게 크롤리의 손을 잡고 말했다.\n\n\"당신은 짐작건대, 어느 분의—\"\n\n\"바로 그렇소.\" 웬햄 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 이쪽은 내 벗인 근위 기병 녹색 부대의 맥머도 대위요.\"\n\n\"맥머도 대위를 알게 되어 진심으로 기쁘오.\" 웬햄 씨가 말하며, 앞서 주인에게 그랬듯 부관에게도 또 한 번 미소와 악수를 건넸다. 맥은 사슴 가죽 장갑을 낀 손가락 하나만 내밀고는, 빳빳한 넥타이 위로 웬햄 씨에게 더없이 싸늘한 인사를 보냈다. 그는 아마도 민간인 나부랭이와 상대하게 된 것이 못마땅했던 모양이고, 스테인 경이라면 적어도 대령 정도는 보냈어야 했다고 여기는 듯했다.\n\n\"맥머도가 나를 대신하여 행동하고, 내 뜻을 잘 아는 터이니.\" 크롤리가 말했다. \"내가 물러나서 두 분이 함께 있게 하는 편이 낫겠소.\"\n\n\"물론이지.\" 맥머도가 말했다.\n\n\"천만의 말씀이오, 대령.\" 웬햄 씨가 말했다. \"내가 영광스럽게도 청한 이 면담은 당신 개인과의 자리였소. 물론 맥머도 대위가 함께 계시는 것 또한 더없이 흐뭇한 일임에 틀림없지만 말이오. 사실, 대위, 나는 우리 대화가 더없이 흡족한 결과만을 낳기를 바라는 바요. 내 벗 크롤리 대령이 예상하는 듯한 그런 결과와는 사뭇 다른 것 말이오.\"\n\n\"흠!\" 맥머도 대위가 말했다. 이 민간인 나부랭이들이란, 그는 속으로 생각했다, 늘 일을 조정하니 연설을 늘어놓느니 한단 말이야. 웬햄 씨는 권하지도 않은 의자를 끌어다 앉아, 주머니에서 종이 한 장을 꺼내고는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"오늘 아침 신문에서 이 기쁜 발표를 보셨겠지요, 대령? 정부는 더없이 값진 일꾼을 확보했고, 당신은 그 직책을 받아들이신다면, 짐작건대 받아들이실 테지만, 훌륭한 자리를 얻으신 셈이오. 연봉 삼천 파운드에, 쾌적한 기후, 더할 나위 없는 총독 관저, 식민지에서 모든 일을 마음대로 할 수 있는 권한, 그리고 확실한 승진까지. 진심으로 축하드리오. 두 분께서는 짐작건대, 내 벗이 누구의 후원으로 이 자리를 얻게 되었는지 아시겠지요?\"\n\n\"내가 알 게 뭐람.\" 대위가 말했다. 그의 주인은 얼굴이 새빨개졌다.\n\n\"세상에서 가장 너그럽고 친절한 분 가운데 하나이자, 또한 가장 위대한 분 가운데 하나인 분—내 훌륭한 벗, 스테인 후작 덕분이오.\"\n\n\"그의 자리를 받느니 차라리 그자가 지옥에 떨어지는 꼴을 보겠소.\" 로던이 으르렁거렸다.\n\n\"당신은 내 고귀한 벗에게 노여움을 품고 있구려.\" 웬햄 씨가 침착하게 말을 이었다. \"자, 그렇다면 상식과 정의의 이름으로, 어째서 그런지 말해 보시오.\"\n\n\"어째서라니?\" 로던이 놀라 외쳤다.\n\n\"어째서냐고? 빌어먹을!\" 대위가 지팡이로 바닥을 쾅 치며 말했다.\n\n\"빌어먹을, 그래요.\" 웬햄 씨가 더없이 상냥한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"그래도, 세상 물정 아는 사람으로서—정직한 사람으로서—이 일을 한번 보시오. 그리고 당신이 그릇된 것은 아니었는지 따져 보시오. 당신은 여행에서 돌아와 무엇을 보았소? 스테인 경께서 커즌 거리의 당신 댁에서 크롤리 부인과 저녁을 들고 계신 광경 아니오. 그게 무슨 괴이하거나 새삼스러운 일이오? 그분이 전에도 백 번이나 똑같은 상황에 계시지 않았소? 신사로서 내 명예와 말을 걸고서\"—웬햄 씨는 여기서 의회 연설하듯 한 손을 조끼에 얹었다—\"단언컨대 당신의 의심은 터무니없고 전혀 근거가 없으며, 그 의심이 당신에게 천 가지 은혜를 베풀어 호의를 입증한 명예로운 신사 한 분과, 더없이 흠 없고 결백한 부인 한 분을 해치고 있다고 나는 보는 바요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면—크롤리가 잘못 짚었다는 말씀이오?\" 맥머도 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"나는 크롤리 부인이 내 아내 웬햄 부인만큼이나 결백하다고 믿소.\" 웬햄 씨가 힘주어 말했다. \"내가 믿기로는, 여기 내 벗이 못된 질투에 사로잡혀 그만, 높은 지위에 있는 노쇠한 노인이자 한결같은 벗이요 은인인 분을 칠 뿐 아니라, 제 아내와 자신의 더없이 소중한 명예와 아들의 장래 평판과 제 자신의 앞날까지 함께 치고 있는 것이오.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이 있었는지 말씀드리리다.\" 웬햄 씨가 자못 엄숙하게 말을 이었다. \"오늘 아침 스테인 경께서 나를 부르셨는데, 가 보니 그분은 가련한 상태에 계셨소. 당신처럼 힘센 자와 몸싸움을 벌인 뒤라면 나이 들고 노쇠한 누구든 그러할 것임은 크롤리 대령께 굳이 말씀드릴 필요도 없겠지요. 면전에 대고 말하건대, 당신은 그 힘을 비겁하게 이용한 것이오, 크롤리 대령. 내 고귀하고 훌륭한 벗은 몸만 다친 것이 아니오. 그 마음이, 그래요, 그 마음이 피 흘리고 있었소. 그분이 온갖 은혜를 베풀고 애정으로 대하던 자가, 그분에게 더없이 추악한 모욕을 안긴 것이오. 오늘 신문에 난 바로 이 발령이야말로, 그분이 당신에게 베푼 친절의 증거가 아니고 무엇이겠소? 오늘 아침 내가 뵌 그분은 참으로 보기에 가련한 상태였고, 자신이 당한 그 모욕을 피로써 갚으려고 당신 못지않게 안달하고 계셨소. 짐작건대, 그분이 그런 일에 용기를 입증하셨다는 것은 알고 계시겠지요, 크롤리 대령?\"\n\n\"그자는 배짱 하나는 두둑하지.\" 대령이 말했다. \"그 점을 부인한 사람은 아무도 없었소.\"\n\n\"그분이 제게 내리신 첫 명령은, 결투장을 써서 그것을 크롤리 대령에게 전하라는 것이었소. 그분은 '우리 둘 중 하나는,' 이렇게 말씀하셨소. '간밤의 그 모욕을 겪고서 살아남아서는 안 된다'고 말이오.\"\n\n크롤리가 고개를 끄덕였다. \"본론으로 들어오는구려, 웬햄.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"나는 스테인 경을 진정시키려고 온 힘을 다했소. '맙소사, 경이시여,' 내가 말했지요. '웬햄 부인과 제가 크롤리 부인의 저녁 초대를 받아들이지 않은 것이 어찌나 후회스러운지요!'\"\n\n\"부인이 당신더러 저녁을 함께 들자고 청했소?\" 맥머도 대위가 말했다.\n\n\"오페라가 끝난 뒤에 말이오. 여기 그 초대장이—잠깐만—아니, 이건 다른 종이로군—분명 가지고 있었는데, 뭐 그건 중요한 게 아니고, 그 사실에 대해서는 내 말을 걸고 보증하리다. 우리가 갔더라면—웬햄 부인의 두통만 아니었어도 갔을 텐데, 그 사람이 두통을 꽤나 앓는다오, 특히 봄철에는 더하지요—우리가 가고, 당신이 집에 돌아왔더라면, 다툼도 모욕도 의심도 없었을 거요. 그러니 가엾은 내 아내가 두통을 앓았다는 바로 그 이유로, 당신은 명예로운 두 사내에게 죽음을 끌어내리고, 이 왕국에서 가장 훌륭하고 유서 깊은 두 가문을 치욕과 비탄에 빠뜨리려 하는 셈이오.\"\n\n맥머도 씨는 몹시 어리둥절한 사람의 표정으로 자기 주인을 바라보았고, 로던은 일종의 분노 속에서 자기 먹잇감이 빠져나가고 있음을 느꼈다. 그는 그 이야기를 한마디도 믿지 않았지만, 그렇다고 어찌 그것을 헐뜯거나 반박한단 말인가?\n\n웬햄 씨는, 의회의 자기 의석에서 그토록 자주 갈고닦은 그 유창한 웅변을 그대로 이어 갔다. \"나는 한 시간 남짓 스테인 경의 머리맡에 앉아, 결투를 청하려는 뜻을 거두어 달라고 그분께 간청하고 애원했소. 나는 그 정황이 결국 의심을 살 만했다고 그분께 지적했소—과연 의심스러운 상황이었지요. 나는 그것을 인정하오—당신 처지에 있는 사람이라면 누구든 속아 넘어갔을 거요—나는, 질투에 미친 사람은 사실상 미치광이나 다름없으니 그렇게 여겨야 마땅하다고, 두 분의 결투는 관련된 모든 이의 치욕으로 이어질 수밖에 없다고, 그분처럼 드높은 지위에 계신 분이, 더없이 흉악한 혁명 사상과 더없이 위험한 평등 교리가 천한 무리 사이에 설파되는 요즘 같은 시절에 공공연한 추문을 일으킬 권리는 없다고, 그리고 아무리 결백하다 한들 평민들은 한사코 그분을 죄인으로 몰 거라고 말씀드렸소. 요컨대 나는 그분께 결투장을 보내지 말아 달라고 애원했소.\"\n\n\"나는 그 이야기를 한마디도 믿지 않소.\" 로던이 이를 갈며 말했다. \"그건 빌어먹을 거짓말이고, 당신도 거기 한패라고 믿소, 웬햄 씨. 그자에게서 도전장이 오지 않는다면, 맹세코 내 쪽에서 보내고 말 거요.\"\n\n웬햄 씨는 대령의 이 사나운 가로막음에 새파랗게 질려 문 쪽을 바라보았다.\n\n그러나 그는 맥머도 대위라는 옹호자를 만났다. 그 신사는 욕설을 내뱉으며 일어서더니, 로던의 말투를 나무랐다. \"자네가 이 일을 내 손에 맡겼으니, 맹세코 자네 뜻대로가 아니라 내가 옳다고 여기는 대로 처리해야 하네. 자네에게 이런 식의 말로 웬햄 씨를 모욕할 권리는 없어. 그리고 빌어먹을, 웬햄 씨, 당신은 사과를 받아 마땅하오. 또 스테인 경에게 보낼 도전장으로 말하자면, 그건 다른 사람을 시켜 전하시게. 나는 안 하겠네. 흠씬 두들겨 맞고도 그분이 가만히 있기로 한다면, 빌어먹을, 그러시라고 하지. 그리고 크롤리 부인과의—그 일로 말할 것 같으면, 내 생각엔 입증된 게 아무것도 없네. 자네 아내는 결백해, 웬햄 씨 말마따나 결백하다고. 그러니 아무튼 자네가 그 자리를 받아들이고 입을 다물지 않는다면 그거야말로 빌어먹을 바보짓이지.\"\n\n\"맥머도 대위, 당신은 분별 있는 사람답게 말씀하시는구려.\" 웬햄 씨가 더없이 안도하여 외쳤다. \"크롤리 대령이 순간의 격앙 속에서 쓰신 말은 모두 잊겠소이다.\"\n\n\"그럴 줄 알았소.\" 로던이 코웃음을 치며 말했다.\n\n\"입 다물게, 이 고집불통 늙은이야.\" 대위가 선량하게 말했다. \"웬햄 씨는 싸움을 할 사람이 아니야. 그리고 그게 지극히 옳고.\"\n\n\"이 일은, 내 생각엔,\" 스테인 경의 사자가 외쳤다. \"더없이 깊은 망각 속에 묻혀야 마땅하오. 이에 관한 말은 한마디도 이 문밖으로 새어 나가서는 안 되오. 나는 내 벗을 위해서, 또한 한사코 나를 적으로 여기시는 크롤리 대령을 위해서도 하는 말이오.\"\n\n\"스테인 경께서야 이 일을 그리 떠벌리지 않으실 테고,\" 맥머도 대위가 말했다. \"우리 쪽에서도 그래야 할 까닭이 없겠지요. 어느 모로 보든 그리 보기 좋은 일이 아니니, 말을 적게 할수록 좋소. 두들겨 맞은 건 그쪽이지 우리가 아니오. 그러니 그쪽이 만족한다면, 글쎄, 우리도 만족해야겠지요.\"\n\n이 말에 웬햄 씨는 모자를 집어 들었고, 맥머도 대위가 그를 문까지 배웅하여 자기와 스테인 경의 대리인을 안에 남겨진 로던과 떼어 놓듯 문을 닫았다. 두 사람이 문 저편으로 나서자, 맥머도는 그 둥글고 쾌활한 얼굴에 도무지 존경이라고는 할 수 없는 표정을 지으며 또 한 명의 사절을 빤히 노려보았다.\n\n\"당신은 사소한 일쯤은 개의치 않는구려, 웬햄 씨.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"과찬이시오, 맥머도 대위.\" 상대가 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"이제 내 명예와 양심을 걸고 말하건대, 크롤리 부인은 정말로 오페라가 끝난 뒤 우리에게 저녁을 함께 들자고 청했소.\"\n\n\"물론 그랬겠지요. 그리고 웬햄 부인은 두통이 도지셨고. 이보시오, 내게 천 파운드짜리 어음이 한 장 있는데, 영수증을 써 주신다면 이걸 당신께 드리리다. 그러면 그 어음을 봉투에 넣어 스테인 경께 보내겠소. 우리 사람은 그분과 싸우지 않을 거요. 하지만 우리는 그분 돈을 받지 않는 편이 낫겠소.\"\n\n\"모두 오해였소—다 오해였던 거요, 친애하는 선생.\" 상대가 더없이 천진한 태도로 말했다. 그리고 맥머도 대위가 그를 클럽 계단 아래로 인사하며 배웅할 때, 마침 피트 크롤리 경이 그 계단을 올라오고 있었다. 이 두 신사는 안면이 약간 있는 사이였다. 대위는 준남작과 함께 그의 동생이 있는 방으로 돌아가며, 스테인 경과 대령 사이의 일을 자기가 잘 마무리했노라고 피트 경에게 은밀히 일러 주었다.\n\n피트 경은 물론 이 소식에 무척 흐뭇해하며, 일이 평화롭게 매듭지어진 것을 두고 동생에게 따뜻이 축하의 말을 건넸고, 결투의 폐해와 그런 식의 분쟁 해결이 지닌 불만족스러운 성격에 대해 적절한 도덕적 논평을 곁들였다.\n\n그리고 이 서두를 늘어놓은 뒤, 그는 온갖 능변을 동원하여 로던과 그 아내 사이의 화해를 성사시키려 애썼다. 그는 베키가 한 진술들을 다시 간추려 들려주고, 그것이 참일 가능성을 짚어 주었으며, 자신은 그녀의 결백을 굳게 믿는다고 단언했다.\n\n그러나 로던은 그 말을 들으려 하지 않았다. \"그 사람은 이 십 년 동안 나한테 돈을 감춰 왔소.\" 그가 말했다. \"바로 간밤에도 스테인한테서 받은 돈은 없다고 맹세했지요. 내가 알아챈 순간, 그 사람은 다 끝장났다는 걸 알았소. 결백하지 않다면, 형님, 그 사람은 죄지은 거나 다름없이 못된 거고, 나는 다시는 그 사람을 보지 않겠소—절대로.\" 그 말을 하면서 그의 고개가 가슴팍으로 푹 떨어졌고, 그는 완전히 무너져 슬픔에 잠긴 듯이 보였다.\n\n\"가엾은 친구.\" 맥머도가 고개를 저으며 말했다.\n\n로던 크롤리는 그토록 가증스러운 후원자가 마련해 준 자리를 받아들인다는 생각에 한동안 저항했고, 또한 스테인 경의 영향력으로 들어가게 된 그 학교에서 아들을 빼내려고도 했다. 그러나 그는 형과 맥머도의 간청에, 특히 맥머도가, 자기 원수의 출세가 제 손으로 이루어진 것임을 알면 스테인이 얼마나 길길이 날뛰겠느냐고 짚어 준 데에 마음이 움직여, 결국 그 혜택들을 받아들이는 데 동의했다.\n\n스테인 후작이 그 사고에서 회복하여 바깥출입을 하게 되자, 식민지 담당 장관이 그에게 다가가 인사하며, 그토록 훌륭한 인사 발령을 해낸 것을 두고 자신과 관청을 추어올렸다. 이 축하의 말을 스테인 경이 어느 정도의 고마움으로 받아들였을지는 능히 짐작이 갈 것이다.\n\n그와 크롤리 대령 사이에 벌어진 그 충돌의 비밀은, 웬햄의 말마따나 더없이 깊은 망각 속에 묻혔다. 그러니까, 입회인들과 당사자들에 의해서 말이다. 그러나 그날 저녁이 다 가기도 전에, 그 일은 허영의 시장의 쉰 군데 만찬 식탁에서 입에 오르내렸다. 캐클비라는 자는 직접 저녁 모임 일곱 군데에 가서, 그때마다 논평과 윤색을 곁들여 그 이야기를 들려주었다. 워싱턴 화이트 부인이 그것을 얼마나 흥겹게 떠벌렸던가! 일링의 주교 부인은 이루 말할 수 없을 만큼 충격을 받았고, 주교는 바로 그날 곤트 하우스에 가서 방문록에 제 이름을 적어 두었다. 어린 사우스다운은 안타까워했고, 그의 누이 제인 부인 역시 몹시 안타까워했음은 틀림없는 일이다. 사우스다운 부인은 그 소식을, 희망봉에 가 있는 다른 딸에게 적어 보냈다. 그것은 적어도 사흘간 장안의 화젯거리가 되었고, 웬햄 씨의 귀띔에 따라 움직인 왜그 씨의 분투 덕분에 가까스로 신문에는 실리지 않았다.\n\n집행관들과 중개인들이 커즌 거리의 가엾은 래글스에게 들이닥쳤고, 그 가련하고 자그마한 저택의 한때 어여뻤던 세입자는 그동안—어디에 있었던가? 누가 알 바였으랴! 하루 이틀이 지나고서 누가 그녀의 안부를 물었던가? 그녀는 죄가 있었던가, 없었던가? 우리는 세상이 얼마나 너그러운지, 그리고 의심이 있을 때 허영의 시장의 평결이 어디로 기우는지를 익히 알고 있다. 어떤 이들은 그녀가 스테인 경을 쫓아 나폴리로 갔다고 했고, 또 어떤 이들은 그분이 베키가 도착했다는 소식을 듣고 그 도시를 떠나 팔레르모로 달아났다고 단언했다. 더러는 그녀가 비르슈타트에 살면서 불가리아 왕비의 여관(女官)이 되었다고 했고, 더러는 불로뉴에 있다고, 또 다른 이들은 첼트넘의 하숙집에 있다고 했다.\n\n로던은 그녀에게 그런대로 괜찮은 연금을 마련해 주었는데, 흔히들 하는 말마따나 그녀가 적은 돈을 야무지게 늘려 쓸 줄 아는 여자임은 틀림없다. 그는 자기 생명을 받아 줄 보험 회사를 구할 수만 있었더라면 영국을 떠나면서 빚을 갚았을 테지만, 코번트리 섬의 기후가 어찌나 고약한지 봉급을 담보로는 한 푼도 빌릴 수가 없었다. 그래도 그는 형에게는 꼬박꼬박 송금했고, 어린 아들에게는 우편선이 뜰 때마다 어김없이 편지를 썼다. 그는 맥머도에게 시가가 떨어지지 않게 보내 주었고, 제인 부인에게는 조개껍데기며 카옌 고춧가루며 매운 절임이며 구아바 젤리며 식민지 산물을 잔뜩 보냈다. 형에게는 〈스왐프 타운 가제트〉를 보내 주었는데, 거기서는 새 총독을 어마어마한 열의로 칭송하고 있었다. 반면 그 부인이 총독 관저에 초대받지 못한 〈스왐프 타운 센티넬〉은, 그 총독 각하야말로 폭군이며 네로조차 그에 비하면 깨인 박애주의자라고 단언했다. 어린 로던은 그 신문들을 받아 각하에 관한 기사를 읽기를 좋아하곤 했다.\n\n그의 어머니는 그 아이를 만나려는 어떤 움직임도 보이지 않았다. 아이는 일요일과 휴일이면 고모 집으로 갔다. 그는 곧 퀸스크롤리 인근의 새 둥지란 둥지는 죄다 꿰게 되었고, 햄프셔를 처음 찾았던 그 잊지 못할 방문 때 그토록 감탄했던 허들스턴 경의 사냥개들을 따라 말을 달리곤 했다.\n\n조지 오스본은 이제 러셀 광장에 자리한 외할아버지의 저택에 어엿하게 자리를 잡았고, 그 집에서 아버지가 쓰던 방을 차지했으며, 그곳의 온갖 호사를 물려받을 후계자가 되었다. 잘생긴 외모와 늠름한 거동, 신사다운 풍채를 지닌 그 소년은 외할아버지의 마음을 단번에 사로잡았다. 오스본 씨는 일찍이 형뻘인 조지를 자랑스러워했던 것 못지않게 이 아이를 자랑스러워했다.\n\n이 아이는 제 아버지가 누렸던 것보다 훨씬 많은 사치와 응석을 누렸다. 오스본의 사업은 근래 몇 해 동안 크게 번창했다. 그가 런던 상업계에서 차지하는 부와 비중도 한층 커진 터였다. 지난날 그는 형뻘 조지를 괜찮은 사립학교에 보낼 수 있었던 것만으로도 충분히 흐뭇해했고, 아들에게 군 장교 임관 자리를 사 준 일도 그에게는 적잖은 자랑거리였다. 그러나 어린 조지와 그 장래에 대해서라면 이 노인은 훨씬 더 높은 곳을 바라보았다. 이 꼬마를 어엿한 신사로 만들겠다는 것이 어린 조지를 두고 오스본 씨가 입버릇처럼 하는 말이었다. 그는 마음속 눈으로 손자가 대학생이 되고, 국회의원이 되고, 어쩌면 준남작이 되는 모습까지 그려 보았다. 손자가 그런 영예로 나아가는 길에 들어선 것을 볼 수만 있다면 흡족하게 눈감을 수 있으리라고 노인은 생각했다. 그 애를 가르칠 사람으로는 오직 최고 명문 대학 출신만을 두려 했다. 그런 돌팔이며 사기꾼 따위는 어림도 없었다. 절대로. 몇 해 전만 해도 그는 사납게 굴며 목사며 학자며 그런 부류를 죄다 싸잡아 욕하곤 했다. 그자들은 한낱 사기꾼이며 돌팔이 떼거리로, 라틴어와 그리스어나 갈아 대는 것 말고는 제 밥벌이도 못 할 위인들이며, 자기네라면 한꺼번에 쉰 명쯤은 사 버릴 수도 있는 영국 상인과 신사들을 깔보는 척하는 거만한 개 같은 자들이라는 것이었다. 그러던 그가 이제는 자신이 제대로 교육받지 못한 것을 자못 엄숙하게 한탄하며, 조지를 앞에 두고 거창한 일장 연설을 늘어놓으면서 고전 교양의 필요성과 탁월함을 거듭 일러 주는 것이었다.\n\n식사 자리에서 만나면 외할아버지는 그 아이에게 하루 동안 무엇을 읽었는지 묻곤 했고, 아이가 제 공부에 관해 들려주는 보고에 크나큰 흥미를 보였으며, 어린 조지가 그 이야기를 할 때면 알아듣는 척했다. 그는 백 번도 더 헛다리를 짚었고, 여러 차례 제 무지를 드러냈다. 그 일은 아이가 윗사람에게 품은 존경심을 키워 주지 못했다. 영민한 머리와 다른 데서 받은 더 나은 교육 덕분에, 아이는 이내 제 외할아버지가 둔재임을 알아차렸고, 따라서 그를 부리고 깔보기 시작했다. 비록 보잘것없고 옹색했을지언정, 그가 앞서 받은 교육이 외할아버지의 그 어떤 계획보다도 조지를 훨씬 더 나은 신사로 만들어 놓았기 때문이다. 그는 마음씨 곱고 여리고 다정한 한 여인의 손에서 자랐다. 그녀는 오직 아들에 대해서만 자부심을 가졌을 뿐 다른 무엇에도 으스대는 법이 없었고, 그 마음은 더없이 순결하고 그 몸가짐은 더없이 온순하고 겸손하여, 누가 뭐래도 진정한 숙녀일 수밖에 없었다. 그녀는 다정한 소임과 조용한 의무에 늘 분주했다. 결코 번지르르한 말을 하는 법은 없었으나, 모진 말을 입에 담거나 모진 생각을 품는 법도 없었다. 꾸밈없고 천진하고, 사랑이 넘치고 순결한 우리의 가엾은 어린 애밀리아가 어찌 진정한 숙녀가 아닐 수 있겠는가!\n\n어린 조지는 이 부드럽고 순한 천성 위에 군림했다. 그리고 그것의 소박함과 섬세함이, 그다음으로 마주하게 된 둔한 노인의 거칠고 거만한 태도와 대비를 이루는 바람에, 조지는 그 노인 위에도 군림하게 되었다. 설령 그가 왕세자였다 한들, 제 잘난 줄 알도록 이보다 더 잘 키워질 수는 없었으리라.\n\n그의 어머니가 집에서 그를 그리워하며, 내가 진정 믿건대 하루의 매시간을, 그리고 서글프고 외로운 밤의 거의 모든 시간을 그를 생각하며 보내는 동안, 이 어린 신사에게는 여러 가지 즐거움과 위안이 베풀어졌고, 그래서 그로서는 애밀리아와의 이별을 아주 가뿐하게 견뎌 냈다. 학교에 가면서 우는 어린 사내아이들은 더없이 불편한 곳으로 가게 되어 우는 것이다. 순전히 애정에서 우는 아이는 극소수에 불과하다. 당신의 어린 시절 그 눈이 생강 과자 한 조각만 보면 마르고, 자두 케이크 한 조각이 엄마와 누이들과 헤어지는 고통의 보상이 되었음을 떠올려 본다면, 오, 나의 벗이요 형제여, 당신은 제 고운 감정을 너무 자신해서는 안 될 것이다.\n\n자, 그러니 조지 오스본 도련님은 부유하고 손이 큰 늙은 외할아버지가 마련해 줌 직하다고 여긴 온갖 안락과 사치를 다 누렸다. 마부는 돈으로 살 수 있는 가장 멋진 조랑말을 그에게 사 주라는 지시를 받았고, 조지는 그 말에 타는 법을 배웠다. 처음에는 승마 학교에서였는데, 그곳에서 등자 없이, 또 도약대를 넘으며 만족스럽게 솜씨를 보인 뒤에는, 뉴 로드를 거쳐 리젠트 공원으로, 다시 하이드 공원으로 인도되었고, 거기서 마부 마틴을 뒤에 거느린 채 위풍당당하게 말을 달렸다. 이제는 런던 상업계의 일들을 한결 느긋하게 처리하며 사업을 젊은 동업자들에게 맡겨 둔 늙은 오스본도, 오스본 양과 함께 같은 멋쟁이들의 거리로 자주 마차를 몰고 나가곤 했다. 어린 조지가 멋을 한껏 부린 자태로, 발뒤꿈치를 아래로 내린 채 가볍게 말을 달려 다가오면, 외할아버지는 아이의 고모를 슬쩍 찌르며 말하는 것이었다. \"저것 좀 봐, 오스본 양.\" 그러고는 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 창밖으로 아이에게 고개를 끄덕이며, 마부가 마차에 경례하고 하인이 조지 도련님에게 경례하는 사이, 그 얼굴은 흐뭇함에 발갛게 달아오르는 것이었다. 여기에는 또한 그의 고모인 프레더릭 불록 부인도 있었으니(그 부인의 마차는 날마다 승마장에서 볼 수 있었는데, 차문 판자며 마구에 황소가, 아니 문장이 새겨져 있었고, 모표와 깃털을 단 창백한 얼굴의 어린 불록 셋이 창밖을 빤히 내다보고 있었다), 거듭 말하건대 프레더릭 불록 부인은, 옆구리에 손을 얹고 모자를 한쪽 귀에 걸친 채 영주처럼 거만하게 말을 달려 지나가는 이 어린 벼락출세자를 향해 더없이 쓰디쓴 증오의 눈길을 쏘아 댔다.\n\n아직 열한 살도 채 못 되었건만, 조지 도련님은 어른처럼 바짓단 끈을 매고 더없이 아름다운 작은 부츠를 신었다. 도금한 박차에, 금 손잡이가 달린 채찍에, 손수건에 꽂는 멋진 핀에, 램스 컨듀잇 거리에서 구할 수 있는 가장 말쑥한 작은 새끼염소 가죽 장갑까지 갖추었다. 어머니는 그에게 목도리 두 장을 주었고, 작은 셔츠 몇 벌을 정성껏 단을 박아 지어 주었다. 그러나 그녀의 엘리가 미망인을 보러 왔을 무렵에는 그것들이 훨씬 고운 아마포로 바뀌어 있었다. 고운 무명 셔츠 가슴팍에는 보석 박힌 작은 단추들이 달려 있었다. 그녀의 소박한 선물들은 한쪽으로 치워진 채였다. 짐작건대 오스본 양이 그것들을 마부의 아들에게 주어 버린 것이리라. 애밀리아는 이 변화가 흐뭇하다고 생각하려 애썼다. 사실 그녀는 아이가 그토록 아름답게 차려입은 모습을 보며 행복하고 흐뭇했다.\n\n그녀는 일 실링을 주고 아이의 옆얼굴을 검은 윤곽으로 그린 작은 그림을 하나 마련해 두었는데, 이것을 침대 위 또 다른 초상화 옆에 걸어 두었다. 어느 날 그 아이가 으레 하던 대로 찾아왔다. 브롬프턴의 그 작은 거리를 따라 말을 달려 내려오자, 여느 때처럼 거리의 주민들이 죄다 창가로 몰려 나와 그 휘황함에 감탄했다. 아이는 잔뜩 들뜬 기색에 의기양양한 얼굴로 외투에서 상자 하나를 꺼냈다. 그것은 망토가 달리고 벨벳 깃을 단 맵시 있는 하얀 외투였는데, 그 안에서 붉은 모로코가죽 상자를 꺼내 어머니에게 건넸다.\n\n\"제 돈으로 산 거예요, 엄마.\" 그가 말했다. \"엄마가 좋아하실 것 같아서요.\"\n\n애밀리아는 상자를 열고는, 기쁨에 겨운 애정의 작은 탄성을 지르며 아이를 와락 끌어안고 백 번도 더 입을 맞추었다. 그것은 그 아이 자신의 세밀 초상이었는데, 아주 예쁘게 그려져 있었다(물론 미망인의 생각에는 실제 잘생긴 모습의 절반에도 못 미쳤겠지만, 그건 분명한 일이다). 그의 외할아버지가 한 화가에게 손자의 초상을 그리게 하고 싶어 했는데, 사우샘프턴 로의 어느 상점 진열창에 걸린 그 화가의 작품이 노신사의 눈길을 끌었던 것이다. 돈이 넉넉했던 조지는 문득 그 화가에게 작은 초상화 사본 하나가 얼마나 드는지 물어볼 생각이 떠올랐고, 제 돈으로 값을 치를 테니 그것을 어머니에게 드리고 싶다고 했다. 흐뭇해진 화가는 헐값에 그것을 그려 주었고, 이 일을 전해 들은 늙은 오스본은 만족스러운 듯 으르렁대며 그 세밀 초상화 값의 곱절이나 되는 금화를 아이에게 주었다.\n\n그러나 외할아버지의 흐뭇함이 애밀리아의 황홀함에 어찌 견주랴? 아이의 애정을 보여 주는 그 증거가 그녀를 어찌나 매료시켰던지, 그녀는 세상 어느 아이도 제 아이만큼 착하지는 못하리라 여겼다. 그 후로 여러 주 동안, 아이의 사랑을 떠올리는 것만으로도 그녀는 행복했다. 그 초상화를 베개 밑에 두고서 그녀는 한결 잘 잤고, 또 얼마나 자주, 얼마나 여러 번 그것에 입 맞추며 울고 그것을 두고 기도했던가! 사랑하는 이들이 베푸는 작은 친절 하나가 그 소심한 마음을 감사로 가득 채웠다. 조지와 헤어진 이래로 그녀에게는 그런 기쁨도 위안도 없었던 것이다.\n\n새 보금자리에서 조지 도련님은 영주처럼 군림했다. 식사 자리에서는 더없이 태연하게 숙녀분들에게 함께 와인을 들자고 청했고, 샴페인을 들이켜는 그 모습이 늙은 외할아버지를 매료시켰다. \"저 녀석 좀 보게.\" 노인은 흐뭇함에 자줏빛으로 물든 얼굴로 옆 사람을 슬쩍 찌르며 말하곤 했다. \"저런 녀석 본 적 있나? 원, 세상에! 다음번엔 화장 도구함이며 면도할 면도칼까지 주문하겠어. 정말이지 안 그럴 리가 없다니까.\"\n\n그러나 그 아이의 별난 짓거리가 노신사를 흐뭇하게 한 만큼 오스본 씨의 벗들까지 즐겁게 한 것은 아니었다. 코핀 판사로서는 조지가 대화에 끼어들어 제 이야기를 망쳐 놓는 것이 영 즐겁지 않았다. 포기 대령도 그 어린 사내아이가 반쯤 거나해진 꼴을 보는 데 흥미를 느끼지 못했다. 토피 변호사 부인은, 아이가 팔꿈치를 휙 꺾는 바람에 포도주 한 잔이 자기 노란 새틴 위로 엎질러지고 그 낭패를 두고 아이가 깔깔대며 웃었을 때 별달리 고마운 마음이 들지 않았다. 또한 늙은 오스본은 더없이 흐뭇해했지만, 부인 쪽으로 말하자면, 조지가 러셀 광장에서 자기 셋째 아들을(조지보다 한 살 위인 어린 신사로, 마침 일링 학교의 티클리어스 박사 댁에서 방학을 맞아 집에 와 있던 참이었다) \"한 대 갈겼을\" 때도 그리 흡족해하지 않았다. 조지의 외할아버지는 그 무용담을 두고 아이에게 금화 두 닢을 주었고, 앞으로도 제 덩치와 나이보다 위인 아이를 그런 식으로 갈겨 줄 때마다 더 상을 내리겠노라고 약속했다. 노인이 이런 싸움질에서 대체 무슨 좋은 점을 보았는지는 말하기 어렵다. 다만 그는 다투는 것이 사내아이를 단단하게 만들고, 남을 윽박지르는 것이 그들이 익혀 둘 만한 쓸모 있는 재간이라는 막연한 생각을 품고 있었다. 영국의 젊은이들은 아주 오래전부터 그런 식으로 길러져 왔고, 우리에게는 아이들 사이에 자행되는 불의와 비참함과 잔혹함을 변호하고 떠받드는 자가 수십만이나 있는 것이다. 토피 도련님을 이기고 칭찬에 우쭐해진 조지는 당연하게도 그 정복을 더 밀고 나가고 싶어 했다. 그러던 어느 날, 굉장히 멋을 부린 새 옷을 차려입고 세인트팽크러스 근처를 으스대며 거닐다가, 어린 빵집 사환 녀석이 그 행색을 두고 빈정대자, 이 젊은 귀족 나리는 패기 넘치게 그 멋쟁이 윗도리를 벗어, 동행한 친구에게(러셀 광장 그레이트 코럼 거리에 사는 토드 도련님으로, 오스본 상회의 젊은 동업자의 아들이었다) 맡기고는, 그 어린 빵집 녀석을 갈겨 주려 했다. 그러나 이번에는 전세가 불리하여, 어린 빵집 녀석이 조지를 갈겨 주었다. 조지는 가엾게도 눈에 시퍼런 멍이 들고, 자기 코에서 흘러나온 붉은 피로 그 고운 셔츠 주름 장식을 온통 적신 채 집에 돌아왔다. 그는 외할아버지에게 거인과 싸웠노라 둘러댔고, 브롬프턴의 가엾은 어머니에게는 그 싸움에 관해 장황하고 결코 사실과는 거리가 먼 이야기를 늘어놓아 어머니를 겁에 질리게 했다.\n\n러셀 광장 코럼 거리에 사는 이 어린 토드는 조지 도련님의 둘도 없는 친구이자 숭배자였다. 둘은 다 같이 연극 인물들을 그려 넣는 취미를 즐겼고, 딱딱한 사탕과 라즈베리 타르트를 좋아했으며, 날씨가 허락할 때면 리젠트 공원과 서펜타인 호수에서 미끄럼을 타고 스케이트를 지치는 것을 즐겼고, 또 연극 구경을 좋아했다. 연극을 보러 갈 때는 오스본 씨의 분부에 따라 조지 도련님 전속으로 정해진 시종 로슨이 종종 데리고 갔는데, 셋이서 일등석에 아주 편안히 자리를 잡고 앉았다.\n\n이 신사를 동무 삼아 그들은 수도의 주요 극장이란 극장은 다 다녔고, 드루리 레인에서 새들러스 웰스에 이르기까지 모든 배우의 이름을 꿰뚫었다. 또한 실제로 웨스트의 그 유명한 인물 종이 인형들로, 자기네 판지 무대에서 토드네 식구들과 그 어린 동무들을 앞에 두고 숱한 연극을 공연하기도 했다. 하인 로슨은 너그러운 성품이라, 주머니에 돈이 있을 때면 연극이 끝난 뒤 어린 주인에게 굴 요리를 자주 사 주었고, 잠자리에 들기 전 마시는 럼주 한 잔도 한턱냈다. 그러나 로슨 씨 또한 그가 어린 주인을 그 즐거움으로 인도해 준 데 대한 어린 주인의 후한 인심과 감사에서 제 나름대로 한몫 톡톡히 챙겼으리라는 것은 우리도 거의 확신할 만하다.\n\n런던 서쪽 끝의 한 유명한 재단사가―오스본 씨는 이 아이를 위해서라면 그런 런던 상업지구나 홀번의 서툰 작자들은 절대 안 된다고 했다(정작 자기에게는 런던 상업지구 재단사로도 충분했지만)―어린 조지를 치장하라는 부름을 받았고, 그 일에 비용을 아끼지 말라는 분부를 받았다. 그리하여 컨듀잇 거리의 울지 씨는 상상력을 한껏 펼쳐, 어린 멋쟁이들로 학교 하나를 꾸리고도 남을 만큼 멋진 바지며 멋진 조끼며 멋진 윗도리를 잔뜩 지어 보냈다. 조지는 저녁 모임용으로 흰 작은 조끼들을 갖추었고, 만찬용으로는 무늬를 넣어 자른 벨벳 조끼들을 갖추었으며, 영락없이 작은 어른 같은 사랑스러운 작은 숄 실내복도 한 벌 가졌다. 그는 외할아버지의 말마따나 \"영락없는 서쪽 끝의 멋쟁이처럼\" 날마다 만찬을 위해 차려입었다. 하인 하나가 그의 전속으로 배정되어 몸단장 시중을 들고, 종소리에 응답하고, 늘 은쟁반에 받쳐 편지를 가져다주었다.\n\n조지는 아침을 먹고 나면 식당의 안락의자에 앉아, 영락없는 어른처럼 「모닝 포스트」를 읽곤 했다. \"저 어린 게 어찌나 욕지거리를 해 대고 빌어먹을 소리를 하던지.\" 하인들은 그 조숙함에 흐뭇해하며 떠들어 댔다. 그의 아버지인 대위를 기억하는 이들은 조지 도련님이 머리끝부터 발끝까지 그 아버지 빼다박았다고 단언했다. 그는 활기와 거만함과 잔소리와 사람 좋은 성미로 집안에 생기를 불어넣었다.\n\n조지의 교육은 이웃에 사는 한 학자이자 사숙 훈장에게 맡겨졌으니, 그는 \"젊은 귀족과 신사들을 대학과 의회와 학식을 요하는 직업으로 나아가도록 준비시키며, 그의 방식은 유서 깊은 교육 기관에서 여태 행해지는 저 품위를 떨어뜨리는 체벌을 포함하지 아니하고, 그의 집안에서 문하생들은 세련된 사교계의 우아함과 더불어 한 가정의 신뢰와 애정을 누리게 될 것\"이라고 광고했다. 블룸즈버리 하트 거리에 사는 베어에이커스 백작의 전속 사제 로런스 빌 목사가 그의 아내인 빌 부인과 더불어 문하생을 꾀어들이려 애쓴 방법이 바로 이러했다.\n\n이렇듯 부지런히 광고를 내고 밀어붙인 덕에, 이 전속 사제와 그 부인은 대체로 한두 명의 문하생을 곁에 두는 데 성공했다. 그들은 높은 수업료를 냈고, 유달리 안락한 거처에 머무는 것으로 여겨졌다. 마호가니빛 안색에 곱슬머리, 지독히 멋을 부린 행색의 서인도제도 출신 학생이 하나 있었는데, 그를 찾아오는 이는 아무도 없었다. 또 스물세 살이나 된 우람한 사내아이가 하나 있었는데, 교육이 소홀했던 그를 빌 씨 부부가 상류 사교계에 소개해 줄 참이었다. 그리고 동인도회사 소속 뱅글스 대령의 두 아들이 있었다. 조지가 빌 부인의 학원에 들어왔을 때, 이 넷이 빌 부인의 품격 있는 식탁에 둘러앉았다.\n\n조지는 다른 열두어 명의 문하생과 마찬가지로 통학생일 뿐이었다. 그는 아침이면 친구 로슨 씨의 보호를 받으며 도착했고, 날이 좋으면 오후에는 마부를 뒤에 거느리고 조랑말을 타고 떠나곤 했다. 그 외할아버지의 재산이 어마어마하다는 소문이 학원에 자자했다. 빌 목사는 조지를 면전에서 그 점을 두고 추켜세우곤 했는데, 그가 높은 지위에 오를 운명을 타고났으며, 따라서 젊을 때 근면과 순종으로써 장성한 뒤 맡게 될 높은 소임에 대비하는 것이 마땅하고, 아이 적의 순종이야말로 어른이 되어 명령을 내리는 데 가장 좋은 준비라고 일러 주었다. 그러니 조지더러 학원에 토피 사탕을 가져와, 빌 부인의 우아하고 풍성한 식탁에서 원하는 것은 무엇이든 다 갖춘 뱅글스 도련님들의 건강을 망쳐 놓는 일이 없도록 해 달라고 당부하는 것이었다.\n\n학문에 관해 말하자면, 빌 씨가 즐겨 부르던 대로 \"교과 과정\"이란 그 폭이 어마어마하여, 하트 거리의 어린 신사들은 알려진 온갖 학문을 조금씩이나마 배울 수 있었다. 빌 목사는 태양계 의(儀)며, 발전기며, 녹로며, (세탁실에 차린) 극장이며, 화학 실험 기구며, 그리고 그가 고대와 현대의 온갖 언어로 된 최고 저자들의 모든 저작을 가려 뽑은 장서라고 부르는 것을 갖추고 있었다. 그는 아이들을 대영박물관에 데려가 그곳의 고대 유물과 자연사 표본들을 논했는데, 어찌나 청산유수였던지 그가 이야기를 하면 사람들이 그 주위로 몰려들었고, 블룸즈버리 사람들 모두가 그를 어마어마하게 박식한 사람이라며 떠받들었다. 그리고 그는 입을 열 때마다(거의 늘 입을 열고 있었지만) 어휘가 허락하는 가장 곱고 가장 긴 낱말을 골라 쓰는 데 신경을 썼으니, 곱고 큼직하고 낭랑하게 울리는 형용사를 쓰는 것이나 인색하게 작은 낱말을 쓰는 것이나 드는 품은 매한가지라고 옳게 판단했던 것이다.\n\n이를테면 그는 학원에서 조지에게 이렇게 말하는 것이었다. \"내가 나의 훌륭한 벗 불더스 박사와―제군, 그분은 참된 고고학자이시네, 참된 고고학자이시지―더불어 어느 저녁나절 학문적 담소의 즐거움을 누리고 집으로 돌아오는 길에 보니, 러셀 광장에 있는 자네의 존경스러운 외할아버님의 거의 궁궐과도 같은 저택의 창문들이 마치 잔치를 벌이려는 듯 환히 밝혀져 있더군. 오스본 씨께서 어젯밤 그 호사스러운 식탁에 가려 뽑은 명사들의 모임을 베푸셨으리라는 내 짐작이 옳은가?\"\n\n상당한 익살을 지닌 어린 조지는 빌 씨를 면전에서 굉장한 패기와 솜씨로 흉내 내곤 했는데, 빌 선생님의 짐작이 지극히 옳다고 대답하는 것이었다.\n\n\"그렇다면 제군, 오스본 씨의 환대를 함께 누리는 영예를 입은 그 벗들은, 내 무엇이든 걸어도 좋으니, 그 식사를 두고 불평할 이유가 없었을 게야. 나 자신도 한 번 이상 그런 은혜를 입은 바 있으니까.(그런데 오스본 도련님, 자네 오늘 아침 좀 늦게 왔더군. 이 점에서 자네가 규칙을 어긴 게 한두 번이 아니야.) 거듭 말하건대 제군, 나 자신은 비록 미천하나, 오스본 씨의 우아한 환대를 함께 누릴 만한 자격이 없지 않다고 인정받았네. 그리고 내 비록 세상의 위대하고 고귀한 분들과 더불어 향연을 베푼 적이 있으되―나의 훌륭한 벗이자 후원자이신 베어에이커스 백작 조지 각하를 그 한 분으로 꼽아도 좋으리라 여기네만―그래도 단언하건대, 영국 상인의 식탁도 그에 못지않게 푸짐하게 차려져 있었고, 그 접대 또한 그만큼 흡족하고 고귀하였네. 블럭 군, 괜찮다면 오스본 도련님의 지각으로 중단되었던 에우트로피우스의 그 대목을 다시 시작하기로 하지.\"\n\n이 위대한 인물에게 조지의 교육이 한동안 맡겨졌다. 애밀리아는 그의 어투에 어리둥절했으나, 그를 학문의 경이로 여겼다. 그 가엾은 미망인은 제 나름의 이유에서 빌 부인과 가까이 지냈다. 그녀는 그 집에 머물며 조지가 그곳으로 학교에 오는 것을 보는 게 좋았다. 그녀는 한 달에 한 번 열리는 빌 부인의 담화회에 초대받는 것이 좋았는데(분홍색 초대장에 「아테나」라고 새겨진 것으로 알려 주었다), 그 자리에서 교수는 자기 문하생들과 그 벗들을 맞이하여 멀건 차와 학문적 담소를 베풀었다. 가엾은 어린 애밀리아는 이런 모임에 한 번도 빠지지 않았고, 조지를 곁에 앉힐 수만 있다면 그 모임이 더없이 즐겁다고 여겼다. 그녀는 날씨가 어떻든 브롬프턴에서부터 걸어왔고, 모임이 파하고 조지가 시종 로슨 씨와 함께 떠나 버린 뒤, 가엾은 오스본 부인이 집으로 걸어 돌아갈 채비로 외투와 숄을 걸칠 때면, 즐거운 저녁을 보냈노라며 눈물 어린 감사로 빌 부인을 끌어안는 것이었다.\n* 아테나: 그리스 여신 아테네의 이름. 원문에는 그리스 문자로 새겨져 있다.\n\n조지가 백 가지 학문에 통달한 이 귀한 스승 밑에서 흡수한 학식으로 말하자면, 아이가 외할아버지에게 가져가는 주간 성적표로 판단하건대 그 진척이 놀라웠다. 바람직한 지식의 분야 스무남은 개의 이름이 표에 인쇄되어 있었고, 그 각각에서 문하생이 거둔 진척을 교수가 표시해 주었다. 그리스어에서 조지는 아리스토스, 라틴어에서는 옵티무스, 프랑스어에서는 트레비앵 등의 평가를 받았다. 그리고 연말에는 모두가 무엇으로든 상을 탔다. 곱슬머리의 어린 신사이자 존경하는 맥멀 부인의 이복형제인 스워츠 군조차, 또 농촌 출신으로 교육이 소홀했던 스물세 살의 블럭 군조차, 또 앞서 말한 게으르고 망나니 같은 토드 도련님조차, \"아테네\"라는 글자가 새겨지고 교수가 어린 벗들에게 보내는 거창한 라틴어 헌사가 적힌 십팔 펜스짜리 작은 책들을 받았다. * 아리스토스, 옵티무스, 트레비앵: 각각 그리스어, 라틴어, 프랑스어로 '최우수'를 뜻하는 평가어.\n\n이 토드 도련님의 집안은 오스본 가문에 빌붙어 사는 식객들이었다. 노신사는 토드를 일개 사무원에서 자기 회사의 젊은 동업자로 끌어올려 주었던 것이다.\n\n오스본 씨는 어린 토드 도련님의 대부였고(그는 훗날 명함에 '오스본 토드'라고 써넣었으며, 영락없는 멋쟁이가 되었다), 오스본 양은 마리아 토드 양과 함께 세례식에 참석했으며, 자기가 후원하는 그 아이에게 해마다 기도서며, 소책자 모음이며, 더없이 저교회파다운 시집이며, 그 비슷한 자선의 기념품을 주었다. 오스본 양은 이따금 토드네 식구들을 제 마차에 태워 바람을 쐬게 해 주었다. 그들이 앓아누우면, 큼직한 플러시 천 반바지에 조끼를 입은 그녀의 하인이 러셀 광장에서 코럼 거리로 젤리며 별미를 날라 갔다. 과연 코럼 거리는 러셀 광장 앞에서 벌벌 떨며 우러러보았고, 양고기 다리에 두를 종이 장식 오리는 솜씨가 제법이고 순무와 당근으로 꽃이며 오리 따위를 그럴듯하게 빚어낼 줄 알던 토드 부인은, 사람들이 \"광장\"이라 부르던 그곳으로 가서 큰 만찬에 따르는 준비를 거들면서도, 정작 그 잔칫상에 앉을 생각은 꿈에도 하지 않았다. 막판에 손님 하나가 빠지기라도 하면 그제야 토드가 만찬에 청해졌다. 토드 부인과 마리아는 저녁에 건너와, 소리 죽인 노크와 함께 슬그머니 들어와서는, 오스본 양과 그녀가 거느린 숙녀들이 응접실에 다다를 무렵이면 이미 그곳에 자리를 잡고 앉아, 신사들이 올라올 때까지 이중창을 펼치고 노래할 채비를 갖추고 있었다. 가엾은 마리아 토드, 가엾은 아가씨! 이 이중창과 소나타를 \"광장\"에서 공개로 선보이기 전에, 그녀는 그 \"거리\"에서 그것들을 얼마나 연습하고 또 줄을 뜯어 대야 했던가!\n\n이렇듯 조지가 마주치는 모든 이를 깔아뭉개며 군림하고, 벗이며 친척이며 하인이 죄다 그 어린 녀석 앞에 무릎을 꿇어야 한다는 것이 운명으로 정해진 듯했다. 그가 이런 처지에 기꺼이 순순히 적응했다는 것은 인정해야겠다. 대부분의 사람들이 그러는 법이다. 그리고 조지는 주인 노릇 하기를 좋아했고, 아마도 거기에 타고난 소질이 있었으리라.\n\n러셀 광장에서는 모두가 오스본 씨를 두려워했고, 오스본 씨는 조지를 두려워했다. 아이의 활달한 거동과, 책이며 학문에 관해 거침없이 떠벌리는 모습, 그리고 (저 멀리 브뤼셀에서 화해하지 못한 채 죽어 간) 제 아버지를 빼닮은 점이 노신사를 주눅 들게 했고, 어린아이에게 주도권을 쥐여 주었다. 노인은 어린 녀석이 무심코 내비치는 어떤 집안 내력의 이목구비나 말투에 흠칫 놀라며, 조지의 아버지가 다시 눈앞에 서 있는 듯 여기곤 했다. 그는 손자에게 너그럽게 굴어, 형뻘 조지에게 모질게 대했던 것을 만회해 보려 했다. 사람들은 그가 그 아이에게 다정한 데 놀랐다. 그는 여느 때처럼 오스본 양에게는 으르렁대며 욕을 퍼부었지만, 조지가 아침 식사에 늦게 내려오면 미소를 지었다.\n\n조지의 고모인 오스본 양은 사십 년이 넘는 따분함과 거친 대우에 짓눌려 시들어 버린 늙은 노처녀였다. 패기 있는 사내아이가 그녀를 휘어잡기란 쉬운 일이었다. 조지는 그녀의 찬장에 든 잼 단지에서부터 물감 상자에 든 갈라지고 말라붙은 낡은 물감에 이르기까지(그것은 그녀가 스미 씨의 문하생이던 시절, 그러니까 아직 거의 젊고 한창 꽃피던 시절에 쓰던 낡은 물감 상자였다) 그녀에게서 무언가를 원할 때면, 제가 탐내는 물건을 차지해 버렸고, 일단 손에 넣고 나면 더는 고모에게 눈길도 주지 않았다.\n\n그의 벗이자 단짝으로는, 그를 추켜세워 주는 거만한 늙은 훈장과, 그가 마구 두들겨 줄 수 있는 자기보다 나이 많은 아첨꾼 하나가 있었다. 사랑하는 토드 부인의 더없는 기쁨은 그를 자기 막내딸인 로자 제미마와 함께 두는 것이었으니, 그 아이는 여덟 살 난 귀여운 꼬마였다. 그 어린 한 쌍이 어찌나 잘 어울리던지, 그녀는(물론 \"광장\" 사람들에게는 절대 그러지 않았으리라는 게 분명하지만) 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"누가 알겠어요, 무슨 일이 생길지? 정말 예쁜 한 쌍 아닌가요?\" 그 다정한 어머니는 그렇게 생각하는 것이었다.\n\n기가 꺾인 늙은 외할아버지 또한 이 어린 폭군에게 휘둘렸다. 그는 그토록 고운 옷을 입고 마부를 뒤에 거느린 채 말을 타는 아이를 존경하지 않을 수 없었다. 조지 쪽에서는, 무자비한 옛 원수인 오스본 씨가 존 세들리에게 퍼붓는 거친 욕설과 천박한 비아냥을 늘 듣곤 했다. 오스본은 상대편을 늙은 거지, 늙은 석탄 장수, 늙은 파산자, 그 밖의 잔인하고 모욕적인 온갖 이름으로 불렀다. 그토록 짓밟힌 사람을 어린 조지가 어찌 존경하겠는가? 친할아버지 댁에 온 지 몇 달 뒤, 세들리 부인이 세상을 떠났다. 그녀와 아이 사이에는 애정이라 할 것이 별로 없었다. 그는 별다른 슬픔을 내보이려 들지 않았다. 그는 멋진 새 상복 차림으로 어머니를 보러 내려왔으나, 잔뜩 벼르던 연극을 보러 갈 수 없게 되었다고 몹시 화를 냈다.\n\n그 노부인의 병환은 애밀리아에게 하나의 소일거리였고, 어쩌면 하나의 보호막이기도 했다. 사내들이 여인의 순교에 대해 무엇을 알겠는가? 우리라면 숱한 여인들이 온순하게 견뎌 내는 그 나날의 고통의 백분의 일이라도 감내해야 한다면 미쳐 버리고 말 것이다. 아무 보답도 없는 끝없는 종살이, 한결같은 잔인함으로 되돌아오는 한결같은 온유와 친절, 따뜻한 말 한마디 인정받지 못하는 사랑과 노고와 인내와 보살핌. 이 모든 것을 그 얼마나 많은 여인들이 잠자코 견디며, 마치 아무것도 느끼지 못하는 양 밝은 얼굴로 세상에 나서야 하는 것인가. 다정한 노예인 그들은 위선자도 약자도 될 수밖에 없는 것이다.\n\n애밀리아의 어머니는 의자에서 자리보전을 하게 되었고, 다시는 그 자리를 뜨지 못했다. 오스본 부인 자신도 조지를 보러 달려갈 때를 빼고는 결코 그 곁을 떠나지 않았다. 노부인은 그 드문 나들이조차 못마땅해했다. 한때, 그러니까 형편이 넉넉하던 시절에는 다정하고 잘 웃고 사람 좋은 어머니였으나, 가난과 병약함이 그녀를 무너뜨려 놓았던 것이다. 그녀의 병환이나 소원해진 마음이 애밀리아를 괴롭히지는 않았다. 오히려 그 덕분에 그녀는 자신이 시달리던 또 다른 재앙을 견뎌 낼 수 있었으니, 환자의 끊임없는 부름이 그 재앙에 대한 생각에서 그녀를 떼어 놓아 주었기 때문이다. 애밀리아는 어머니의 모진 태도를 더없이 온순하게 견뎌 냈다. 불편한 베개를 고쳐 주었고, 살피듯 칭얼대는 그 목소리에 늘 부드러운 대답을 준비해 두었으며, 그 신실하고 소박한 마음이 가장 절절히 느끼고 입에 올릴 수 있는 희망의 말들로 병자를 달랬고, 한때 그토록 다정하게 자신을 바라보던 그 두 눈을 감겨 드렸다.\n\n그러고 나서 그녀의 모든 시간과 다정함은, 닥쳐온 일격에 망연자실하여 세상에 외따로 남겨진 채 서 있던 늙은 아버지를 위로하고 보살피는 데 바쳐졌다. 아내며, 명예며, 재산이며, 그가 가장 사랑하던 모든 것이 그에게서 떨어져 나가 버렸다. 비틀거리며 가슴 무너진 그 노인을 곁에서 그 다정한 팔로 떠받쳐 줄 사람은 오직 애밀리아뿐이었다. 우리는 그 사연을 적지는 않으련다. 너무도 음울하고 따분할 테니. 허영의 시장이 그것을 앞두고 미리 하품을 하는 모습이 눈에 선하다.\n\n어느 날, 빌 목사 댁 서재에 어린 신사들이 모여 있고, 베어에이커스 백작 각하의 전속 사제가 여느 때처럼 청산유수로 떠벌리고 있을 때, 아테네 여신상으로 장식된 문 앞으로 멋들어진 마차 한 대가 다가와 멈춰 섰고, 두 신사가 내렸다. 어린 뱅글스 도련님들은 어쩌면 아버지가 봄베이에서 돌아오셨나 하는 막연한 생각에 창가로 달려갔다. 에우트로피우스의 한 대목을 두고 남몰래 울고 있던 그 우람한 스물세 살 학생은, 마차의 마부석에서 안내인이 뛰어내려 마차 안의 사람들을 내려 주는 사이, 자기의 푸대접받은 코를 유리창에 납작 들이대고 그 사두마차를 바라보았다.\n\n\"한 명은 뚱뚱하고 한 명은 말랐네.\" 블럭 군이 말했을 때, 문에서 우렁찬 노크 소리가 울렸다.\n\n모두가 흥미를 보였으니, 미래의 문하생들의 아버지를 보는 것이기를 바라던 그 전속 사제 본인부터, 책을 내려놓을 핑계라면 무엇이든 반가웠던 어린 조지 도련님에 이르기까지 다 그러했다.\n\n빛바랜 구리 단추가 달린 초라한 제복을 입은 사환 아이가―늘 문을 열어 주려고 그 꽉 끼는 윗도리 속에 제 몸을 욱여넣곤 했다―서재로 들어와 말했다. \"두 신사분이 오스본 도련님을 뵙겠다는데요.\" 교수는 그날 아침 폭죽을 학원에 들여온 일을 두고 의견 차이가 생겨 그 어린 신사와 사소한 언쟁을 벌인 터였으나, 이제 그 얼굴에 평소의 부드럽고 정중한 표정을 되찾으며 말했다. \"오스본 도련님, 마차로 찾아온 자네 벗들을 만나러 가도 좋다고 전적으로 허락하네. 그분들께 나와 빌 부인의 정중한 안부를 전해 주기 바라네.\"\n\n조지는 응접실로 들어가, 여느 때처럼 거만한 태도로 고개를 빳빳이 든 채 두 낯선 사람을 바라보았다. 한 사람은 뚱뚱하고 콧수염을 길렀으며, 다른 한 사람은 마르고 키가 컸는데, 푸른 프록코트 차림에 갈색 얼굴과 희끗희끗한 머리를 하고 있었다.\n\n\"맙소사, 어쩌면 이리도 닮았는지!\" 키 큰 신사가 흠칫 놀라며 말했다. \"우리가 누구인지 알아맞히겠니, 조지?\"\n\n아이의 얼굴이 발갛게 달아올랐다. 마음이 동요할 때면 늘 그러했는데, 두 눈도 밝게 빛났다. \"다른 분은 모르겠지만,\" 그가 말했다. \"이쪽은 도빈 소령님이 틀림없으실 것 같은데요.\"\n\n과연 그는 우리의 옛 친구였다. 그는 그 아이에게 인사를 건네며 기쁨에 목소리가 떨렸고, 아이의 두 손을 제 두 손에 마주 쥐고는 그 녀석을 제 쪽으로 끌어당겼다.\n\n\"네 어머니가 내 이야기를 너에게 해 주었구나, 그렇지?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼요.\" 조지가 대답했다. \"수백 번 수천 번도 더요.\"\n\n늙은 오스본이 즐겨 스스로를 위로하던 여러 개인적 자부심의 근거들 가운데 하나는, 그의 오랜 맞수이자 적이며 은인이기도 했던 세들리가 말년에 이르러 어찌나 철저히 패배하고 굴욕을 당했던지, 자기에게 가장 큰 해를 끼치고 모욕을 안긴 바로 그 사내의 손에서 금전적 신세를 지지 않을 수 없게 되었다는 사실이었다. 세상에서 성공을 거둔 그 사내는 늙은 빈민을 저주하면서도 때때로 그를 구제해 주었다. 그는 조지에게 어머니에게 전할 돈을 마련해 주면서, 특유의 거칠고 야비한 방식으로 넌지시 흘리는 말로, 조지의 외조부란 그저 비참한 늙은 파산자이자 식객에 불과하며, 존 세들리로 말하자면 이미 그토록 많은 돈을 빚진 사내가 지금 너그러이 베푸는 그 도움에 감사할 줄 알아야 한다는 것을 어린 소년에게 일깨워 주었다. 조지는 그 거드름 어린 돈을 어머니에게, 그리고 이제 그녀의 삶의 가장 중요한 소임이 보살피고 위로하는 일이 되어 버린 그 무너진 늙은 홀아비에게 날라다 주었다. 어린 녀석은 그 쇠약하고 실의에 빠진 노인에게 마치 시혜라도 베풀듯 굴었다.\n\n애밀리아가 아버지의 적의 손에서 이런 금전적 혜택을 받아들이기로 한 것은, 어쩌면 \"마땅한 자존심\"이 결여된 처사로 보일지도 모른다. 그러나 마땅한 자존심이라는 것과 이 가엾은 부인은 애초에 그리 친분이 두텁지 못했다. 본디 단순하여 누군가의 보호를 필요로 하는 천성, 그리고 거의 처녀 시절부터, 아니 조지 오스본과의 그 불운한 결혼 이후로 줄곧 이어진 가난과 굴종, 나날의 결핍과 모진 말, 베풀고도 보답받지 못한 친절의 기나긴 세월이 그녀에게 주어진 몫이었다. 매일같이 자기보다 나은 이들이 이런 수치를 묵묵히 견디며, 운명의 푸대접 아래 온순하게 시달리고, 가엾이 여겨지지도 못한 채 점잖고, 가난하며, 그 가난 탓에 도리어 멸시받는 모습을 보는 당신은, 과연 자신의 번영에서 한 걸음 내려와 그 가엾고 지친 거지들의 발을 씻겨 준 적이 있는가? 그들을 떠올리는 것조차 가증스럽고 천박한 일이렷다. \"계급이라는 건 있어야 마땅하지—부자도 있고 가난한 자도 있어야 하는 법이야.\" 부자가 클라레를 들이켜며 그렇게 말한다(창문 아래 앉은 나사로에게 먹다 남은 고기 부스러기라도 내보낸다면 그나마 다행이다). 지당한 말씀이다. 그러나 생각해 보라. 어떤 이에게는 자줏빛 옷과 고운 아마포를 안기고, 다른 이에게는 누더기를 옷으로, 개를 위로꾼으로 보내는 저 인생의 추첨이라는 것이 얼마나 신비롭고, 또 얼마나 헤아릴 수 없는 것인지를.\n\n그러니 나는 인정하지 않을 수 없다. 애밀리아는 별다른 원망 없이, 아니 도리어 감사에 가까운 마음으로, 시아버지가 이따금 떨어뜨려 주는 부스러기를 받아, 그것으로 제 친정아버지를 봉양했던 것이다. 그것이 자신의 의무임을 깨닫는 순간, 자기 자신을 희생하고 가진 모든 것을 사랑하는 대상의 발치에 내던지는 것이 이 젊은 여인의 천성이었다(숙녀 여러분, 그녀는 아직 서른밖에 되지 않았으니, 우리는 그 나이에도 그녀를 젊은 여인이라 부르기로 한다). 그러니까 그것이 그녀의 천성이었던 것이다. 어린 조지가 집에 함께 있던 시절, 그 아이를 위해 손가락이 닳도록 일하며 보낸 그 길고 보답 없는 밤들은 또 얼마였던가. 아버지와 어머니를 위해 견뎌 낸 그 숱한 매질과 멸시와 결핍과 가난은 또 어떠했던가! 그리고 이 모든 외로운 체념과 남모를 희생의 한가운데서도, 그녀는 세상이 그녀를 존중하지 않은 만큼이나 자기 자신도 존중하지 않았으니, 도리어 마음속으로는 자신이 기개 없고 비루한 보잘것없는 존재이며, 그 삶의 행운조차 제 분수에는 과분한 것이라고 여겼던 것이리라. 오, 가엾은 여인들이여! 오, 삶 자체가 고문이며, 침실에서는 형틀에 묶이고, 매일같이 응접실 식탁에서 단두대에 머리를 얹는 그대들, 가엾고 은밀한 순교자이자 희생자들이여. 그대들의 고통을 지켜보거나, 그대들에게 그 고문이 가해지는 어두운 곳을 들여다보는 사내라면 누구든 그대들을 가엾이 여기지 않을 수 없으리라—그리고—그리고 제게 수염이 있음을 하느님께 감사하지 않을 수 없으리라. 여러 해 전, 파리 인근 비세트르의 백치와 광인을 가두는 감옥에서, 나는 갇힌 몸의 속박과 제 몸의 병약함에 짓눌려 굽은 한 가엾은 자를 본 기억이 있다. 우리 일행 중 한 사람이 그에게 종이를 비비 꼬아 만든 봉지에 반 페니어치의 코담배를 담아 건넸다. 그 친절은 그 가엾은 간질 환자에게는 너무도 벅찬 것이었다. 그는 환희와 감사에 못 이겨 괴로움에 사무친 듯 울었던 것이다. 만약 누군가가 그대나 나에게 연 천 파운드를 주거나 목숨을 구해 준다 한들, 우리는 그토록 감격하지는 못하리라. 그러니 여자를 제대로 짓밟아 본다면, 반 페니어치의 친절만으로도 그녀의 마음을 움직여, 마치 그대가 그녀에게 은혜를 베푸는 천사라도 되는 양 두 눈에 눈물이 고이게 할 수 있음을 알게 될 것이다.\n\n운명이 가엾은 어린 애밀리아에게 허락한 최선의 은혜라는 것이 바로 이런 종류의 것이었다. 그리 불운하지 않게 시작된 그녀의 삶이 이 지경에까지 떨어진 것이었다—초라한 감옥과, 길고도 비천한 속박으로. 어린 조지가 이따금 갇힌 어미를 찾아와, 희미하게 깜박이는 격려의 빛으로 그 처지를 달래 주었다. 러셀 광장이 그녀가 갇힌 감옥의 경계였다. 그녀는 가끔 그쪽으로 걸음을 옮길 수도 있었으나, 밤이면 언제나 제 감방으로 돌아와 잠들고, 즐거움이라곤 없는 의무를 다하고, 보답 없는 병상을 지키고, 투정 부리는 실의에 찬 노년의 시달림과 횡포를 견뎌야 했다. 이 기나긴 노예살이를 견디도록 운명 지어진 사람들이, 그것도 대부분 여자들이, 도대체 몇천 명이나 될 것인가?—삯도 받지 못하는 병원 간병인들, 굳이 말하자면 희생의 낭만도 감상도 없는 자선 수녀들, 가엾이 여겨지지도 못한 채 애쓰고, 굶고, 지켜보고, 고통받다가, 비천하게 알려지지도 못한 채 시들어 가는 그 여인들이 말이다.\n\n인류의 운명을 나누어 주는 저 은밀하고 두려운 지혜는, 다정하고 선량하며 지혜로운 이들을 그토록 굴욕에 빠뜨려 내치고, 이기적이고 어리석거나 사악한 이들을 떠받들기를 즐긴다. 오, 형제여, 그대의 번영 속에서 부디 겸손하라! 그대보다 덜 운이 좋은 이들에게, 비록 더 합당한 자격을 갖추지는 못했더라도 부드럽게 대하라. 생각해 보라. 그대의 미덕이라는 것이 그저 유혹이 없었던 탓일 뿐이요, 그대의 성공이라는 것이 어쩌면 한낱 우연일 뿐이며, 그대의 신분이라는 것이 조상이 거머쥔 우연의 산물일 뿐이고, 그대의 번영이라는 것이 십중팔구 하나의 풍자에 지나지 않을진대, 무슨 권리로 그대가 남을 멸시한단 말인가.\n\n사람들은 애밀리아의 어머니를 브롬프턴 교회 묘지에 묻었다. 그날은 마침, 애밀리아가 조지와 결혼하기 위해 처음 그곳에 갔던 날 기억나던 바로 그런, 비 내리고 어두운 날이었다. 어린 아들은 으리으리한 새 상복을 입고 그녀 곁에 앉아 있었다. 그녀는 그 늙은 좌석 안내 여자와 서기를 떠올렸다. 목사가 성경을 읽는 동안 그녀의 생각은 다른 시절로 멀리 달아나 있었다. 만약 조지의 손을 제 손에 쥐고 있지 않았더라면, 어쩌면 그녀는 그 자리를 바꾸고 싶었을지도…. 그러다 그녀는 늘 그렇듯 제 이기적인 생각이 부끄러워져, 의무를 다할 힘을 달라고 속으로 기도했다.\n\n그리하여 그녀는 온 힘과 정성을 다해 늙은 아버지를 행복하게 해 드리려 애쓰기로 마음먹었다. 그녀는 종처럼 일하고, 고되게 애쓰고, 깁고 꿰매고, 노래하고 주사위 놀이를 함께 하고, 신문을 소리 내어 읽어 주고, 늙은 세들리를 위해 요리를 하고, 그를 켄싱턴 정원이나 브롬프턴의 오솔길로 부지런히 데리고 나가 산책시키고, 지칠 줄 모르는 미소와 애정 어린 위선으로 그의 이야기에 귀 기울이거나, 쇠약하고 투정 많은 노인이 정원 벤치에서 볕을 쬐며 제 억울함이나 슬픔을 늘어놓는 동안 그 곁에 앉아 생각에 잠긴 채 자신의 상념과 추억과 마주하곤 했다. 이 과부의 생각이란 얼마나 서글프고 채워지지 않는 것이었던지! 정원의 비탈과 넓은 길을 오르내리며 뛰노는 아이들은 그녀에게서 빼앗겨 간 조지를 떠올리게 했다. 첫 번째 조지도 그녀에게서 빼앗겨 갔던 것이다. 두 경우 모두 그녀의 이기적이고 죄 많은 사랑은 질책당했고 혹독하게 징벌당했다. 그녀는 자신이 그토록 벌받는 것이 마땅하다고 생각하려 애썼다. 자신은 그토록 비참하고 사악한 죄인이라고. 자신은 이 세상에 철저히 홀로라고.\n\n나도 안다. 이런 종류의 외로운 유폐 이야기란, 그것을 활기차게 만들어 줄 명랑하거나 익살스러운 사건—이를테면 마음 따뜻한 간수라든가, 익살스러운 요새 사령관이라든가, 라튀드의 턱수염과 구레나룻 둘레를 기어 나와 노니는 생쥐라든가, 트렌크가 손톱과 이쑤시개로 파낸 성 밑의 지하 통로 같은 것—이 곁들여지지 않는 한, 견딜 수 없이 지루하다는 것을. 그러나 역사가에게는 애밀리아의 유폐를 서술하면서 들려줄 그런 흥겨운 사건이 하나도 없다. 부디 상상해 보시라. 이 시기 동안 그녀가 몹시 슬프면서도, 누가 말을 걸면 언제나 기꺼이 미소 지을 채비가 되어 있고, 천박하다고까지는 말하지 않더라도 몹시 초라하고 가난한 처지에서, 늙은 아버지를 위해 노래를 부르고, 푸딩을 만들고, 카드놀이를 하고, 양말을 깁고 있는 모습을. 그러니 그녀가 여주인공이든 아니든 마음 쓸 것 없다. 또한 그대나 나도, 아무리 늙고 잔소리 심하고 파산했다 한들—부디 우리의 말년에, 기댈 수 있는 부드럽고 다정한 어깨 하나와, 통풍으로 쑤시는 늙은 베개를 어루만져 줄 너그러운 손길 하나만은 있기를.\n\n늙은 세들리는 아내가 죽은 뒤로 딸을 무척 아끼게 되었고, 애밀리아는 그 노인에게 자신의 의무를 다하는 데서 위안을 얻었다.\n\n그러나 우리는 이 두 사람을 이토록 비천하고 품위 없는 처지에 오래 내버려 둘 생각은 없다. 세속적인 번영으로 말하자면, 두 사람 모두에게 더 나은 날들이 예비되어 있었다. 어쩌면 눈치 빠른 독자라면, 우리의 오랜 벗 도빈 소령과 함께 조지를 학교로 찾아온 그 뚱뚱한 신사가 누구였는지 이미 짐작했을지도 모른다. 그는 영국으로 돌아온 또 한 사람의 옛 지인이었으며, 그것도 그가 나타난 것이 그곳에 있던 친척들에게 큰 위안이 될 만한 시기에 돌아온 것이었다.\n\n도빈 소령은 마음씨 좋은 사령관에게서 긴급한 사적인 용무로 마드라스로, 그리고 거기서 다시 아마도 유럽으로 떠나도 좋다는 허락을 어렵잖이 얻어 내고는, 목적지에 다다를 때까지 밤낮으로 쉼 없이 여행했다. 어찌나 급히 행로를 재촉했던지 마드라스에 도착했을 때는 고열에 시달리고 있었다. 그를 수행한 하인들은 그를, 유럽으로 떠날 때까지 머물기로 마음먹었던 친구의 집으로 인사불성의 상태로 데려왔다. 여러 날 동안 사람들은, 그가 성 조지 교회의 묘지보다 더 멀리는 결코 가지 못하리라고, 그곳에서 군대가 그의 무덤 위로 조총을 쏘아 올리고, 수많은 용맹한 장교들이 고향에서 멀리 떨어진 곳에 누워 있는 바로 그 자리에 그도 묻히게 되리라고 여겼다.\n\n그 가엾은 사내가 고열에 몸부림치며 누워 있는 동안, 그를 지켜보던 사람들은 그가 애밀리아의 이름을 부르며 헛소리하는 것을 들었을 법하다. 그녀를 다시는 보지 못하리라는 생각이, 정신이 맑은 순간이면 그를 침울하게 했다. 그는 자신의 마지막 날이 왔다고 여기고, 엄숙하게 떠날 채비를 갖추어, 이 세상에서의 신변을 정리하고, 자신이 가진 보잘것없는 재산을 가장 혜택을 베풀고 싶은 이들에게 남겼다. 그가 머물던 집의 친구가 그 유언의 증인이 되어 주었다. 그는 자신이 목에 두르고 다니던 작은 갈색 머리카락 사슬과 함께 묻히기를 바랐는데, 굳이 진실을 밝히자면, 그것은 그가 브뤼셀에서 애밀리아의 하녀에게서 얻은 것이었다. 젊은 과부가 성 장 산 고원에서 조지 오스본이 전사한 뒤 그 충격으로 앓아누웠을 때, 열병을 앓는 동안 잘라 낸 그녀의 머리카락이었던 것이다.\n\n그는 차도를 보였다가, 기력을 회복했다가, 다시 도졌으니, 그 과정에서 받은 사혈과 감홍 투약이 어찌나 혹독했던지 그의 타고난 체질의 강건함이 도리어 드러날 지경이었다. 캘커타에서 출발해 마드라스에 기항한 동인도 회사 선박 람춘더호—브래그 선장의 배였다—에 그를 태웠을 때, 그는 거의 해골과 다름없었고, 어찌나 쇠약하고 기진해 있던지, 병중 내내 그를 보살펴 준 친구는 이 정직한 소령이 항해에서 결코 살아남지 못하리라고, 어느 날 아침이면 깃발과 해먹에 싸인 채 뱃전 너머로 던져져, 가슴에 품고 있던 그 유품과 함께 바닷속으로 가라앉으리라고 예언했다. 그러나 바닷바람 덕분이었는지, 아니면 배가 돛을 펴고 정박지를 벗어나 고향을 향해 나아가던 날부터 그의 안에서 새로이 솟아오른 희망 덕분이었는지, 우리의 벗은 차도를 보이기 시작하더니, 희망봉에 닿기도 전에 (비록 사냥개처럼 비쩍 야위긴 했으나) 완전히 건강을 되찾았다. \"이번에는 커크가 소령 진급에서 물 먹게 생겼군.\" 그가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"연대가 본국에 도착할 즈음이면 자기 진급이 관보에 실리리라 잔뜩 기대하고 있을 텐데 말이야.\" 사정인즉슨 이러했다. 소령이 그토록 엄청난 속도로 마드라스로 달려갔다가 그곳에서 병들어 누워 있는 동안, 여러 해 해외에서 복무했던 그 용맹한 ××연대—서인도제도에서 돌아온 뒤로 워털루 전역 때문에 본국에 머물 기회를 빼앗기고, 플랑드르에서 인도로 파견되었던 그 연대가 본국 귀환 명령을 받았던 것이다. 그러니 만약 소령이 동료들이 마드라스에 도착하기를 기다리기로 했더라면, 그들과 함께 돌아갈 수도 있었을 터였다.\n\n어쩌면 그는 기진맥진한 처지에 다시 글로비나의 보호 아래 자신을 맡기고 싶지 않았는지도 모른다. \"오다우드 양이 배에 함께 탔더라면 나는 끝장났을 거요.\" 그가 동승객에게 웃으며 말했다. \"그리고 나를 침몰시키고 나면, 그다음엔 분명히 당신을 덮쳤을 거요, 틀림없이. 그러고는 당신을 전리품 삼아 사우샘프턴까지 끌고 갔을 테지, 조스, 이 사람아.\"\n\n실로 그 동승객이란 바로 다름 아닌, 람춘더호에 같이 타고 있던 우리의 뚱뚱한 벗이었던 것이다. 그는 십 년 세월을 벵골에서 보냈다. 끊임없이 이어지는 만찬과 점심 연회, 페일에일과 클라레, 법정 사무의 엄청난 노고, 그리고 그곳에서 어쩔 수 없이 들이켜야 했던 브랜디 물의 자양분이 워털루의 영웅 세들리에게 그 나름의 영향을 미쳤다. 그에게는 유럽으로의 항해가 필요하다는 진단이 내려졌다. 인도에서 복무 기간을 꽉 채웠고, 상당한 액수의 돈을 모을 수 있게 해 준 좋은 직책들을 거친 덕분에, 그는 후한 연금을 받으며 본국에 돌아와 머물 수도 있었고, 아니면 다시 돌아가 연공과 비범한 재능에 걸맞은 그 직위를 이어받을 수도 있는 자유로운 몸이었다.\n\n그는 우리가 마지막으로 보았을 때보다 다소 야위었으나, 거동의 위엄과 근엄함만은 더 늘어 있었다. 그는 워털루에서의 공로로 기를 자격을 얻은 콧수염을 다시 길렀고, 금띠를 두른 화려한 벨벳 모자를 쓴 채, 온몸에 핀과 보석붙이를 잔뜩 치장하고서 갑판 위를 거들먹거리며 돌아다녔다. 그는 선실에서 아침을 들었고, 마치 본드 거리나 캘커타의 경마장에라도 나서려는 양 근엄하게 차려입고서 후갑판에 모습을 드러냈다. 그는 원주민 하인 하나를 데려왔는데, 그자는 시종 겸 담뱃대 담당으로, 터번에 세들리 가문의 문장을 은빛으로 달고 있었다. 그 동방의 하인은 조스 세들리의 횡포 아래 비참한 삶을 살았다. 조스는 여자처럼 제 외모에 허영을 부렸고, 한물간 미인 못지않게 몸치장에 긴 시간을 들였다. 승객 중 젊은 축들, 150연대의 젊은 채퍼스와, 세 번째 열병을 앓고서 귀향길에 오른 가엾은 어린 리케츠는 식당 식탁에서 곧잘 세들리의 말문을 트게 하여, 그가 자기 자신과 호랑이 및 나폴레옹을 상대로 한 무용담에 대해 어마어마한 이야기를 늘어놓게 만들곤 했다. 그는 롱우드에 있는 황제의 무덤을 찾았을 때 한껏 거창해졌으니, 도빈 소령이 곁에 없을 때면 이 신사들과 배의 젊은 장교들에게 워털루 전투의 전모를 묘사하며, 자기 조스 세들리가 아니었더라면 나폴레옹은 애당초 세인트헬레나에 가지도 않았으리라고 거의 떠벌리다시피 했다.\n\n세인트헬레나를 떠난 뒤로 그는 무척 후해져서, 자신의 개인적 향락을 위해 가져왔던 엄청난 양의 선상 비축품, 클라레, 저장 고기, 소다수를 가득 채운 커다란 통들을 아낌없이 나누어 주었다. 배에는 숙녀가 한 명도 없었다. 소령이 민간인에게 우선권을 양보한 터라, 그는 식탁에서 가장 높은 자리에 앉는 인물이 되었고, 브래그 선장과 람춘더호의 장교들에게서 그 지위에 걸맞은 대우를 받았다. 그는 이틀간 이어진 폭풍 동안 다소 공황에 빠져 자취를 감추었으니, 선실의 현창들을 단단히 틀어막게 하고는 침상에 누워 「핀칠리 공유지의 빨래하는 여인」을 읽었다. 그 책은 사일러스 혼블로어 목사의 부인이자 명예로운 에밀리 혼블로어 부인이, 그 목사가 선교사로 일하게 될 희망봉으로 가는 항해 중에 람춘더호에 남기고 간 것이었다. 그러나 평소의 읽을거리로는 소설과 희곡을 잔뜩 가져와 배 안의 다른 이들에게 빌려주었고, 그 친절과 너그러운 겸양으로 모두에게 호감을 샀다.\n\n밤이면 밤마다, 배가 으르렁대는 어두운 바다를 가르고 나아가고, 머리 위로는 달과 별이 빛나며, 종이 당직 시각을 알리는 동안, 세들리 씨와 소령은 배의 후갑판에 앉아 고향 이야기를 나누곤 했다. 소령은 엽궐련을 피웠고, 민간인은 하인이 준비해 준 물담배를 뻐끔거렸다.\n\n이런 대화 중에 도빈 소령이 어쩌면 그토록 끈질기고 교묘하게 이야기를 애밀리아와 그녀의 어린 아들 쪽으로 끌고 갈 수 있었는지는 참으로 놀라운 일이었다. 조스는 제 아버지의 불행과, 자기에게 무람없이 손을 벌리는 행태에 다소 부아가 나 있었으나, 노인의 불운과 노령을 짚어 주는 소령의 말에 누그러졌다. 어쩌면 조스로서는 그 노부부와 함께 사는 것이 내키지 않을 수도 있었으니, 다른 부류의 교제에 익숙한 젊은이에게 그들의 생활 방식이나 생활 시간이 맞지 않을 수도 있었기 때문이다(조스는 이 찬사에 고개를 까딱였다). 그러나 소령은, 조스 세들리가 예전처럼 한낱 독신 남자의 살림이 아니라, 런던에 자기 소유의 집을 갖는 것이 얼마나 이로운 일인지, 그 집을 다스릴 적임자로 누이 애밀리아만 한 사람이 어디 있는지, 그녀가 얼마나 우아하고 온화하며 또 얼마나 세련되고 고상한 몸가짐을 지녔는지를 짚어 주었다. 그는 조지 오스본 부인이 지난날 브뤼셀에서, 그리고 런던에서 거둔 성공담을 들려주었으니, 그곳에서 그녀가 대단히 지체 높은 멋쟁이들에게서 많은 흠모를 받았다는 것이었다. 그러고는 조지를 좋은 학교에 보내 사내대장부로 키우는 것이 조스에게 얼마나 어울리는 일인지를 넌지시 일렀으니, 어미와 그 외조부모로 말하자면 틀림없이 아이를 응석받이로 망쳐 놓을 것이기 때문이었다. 한마디로, 이 교활한 소령은 민간인에게서 애밀리아와 그 의지가지없는 아이를 돌보겠다는 약속을 받아 냈던 것이다. 그는 그 작은 세들리 일가에 어떤 일들이 벌어졌는지, 또 어떻게 죽음이 어머니를 데려갔고 부유함이 조지를 애밀리아에게서 앗아 갔는지는 아직 알지 못했다. 그러나 사실인즉, 사랑에 빠진 이 중년의 신사는 날마다, 그리고 언제나 오스본 부인을 생각하고 있었고, 그의 온 마음은 그녀에게 좋은 일을 해 주는 데 쏠려 있었던 것이다. 그는 조스 세들리를 어르고, 구슬리고, 달래고, 추켜세웠으니, 그 끈기와 정성은 어쩌면 본인조차 미처 자각하지 못할 정도였다. 그러나 미혼의 누이나 심지어 딸을 둔 어떤 사내들이라면, 여자에게 구애하는 신사가 그 남자 친척들에게는 얼마나 유난히도 상냥하게 구는지를 기억할 것이다. 그러니 이 능청맞은 도빈 또한 어쩌면 그와 비슷한 위선에 떠밀린 것이었으리라.\n\n진실인즉 이러했다. 도빈 소령이 몹시 앓는 몸으로 람춘더호에 올랐을 때, 그리고 배가 마드라스 정박지에 머물던 사흘 동안, 그는 차도를 보이지 않았고, 옛 지인인 세들리 씨가 배에 타고 있는 것을 보고 알아본 것조차 그를 그리 기운 나게 하지 못했다. 어느 날, 소령이 갑판 위에 맥없이 누워 있을 때 두 사람이 나눈 대화가 있고 나서야 비로소 달라졌던 것이다. 그때 그는 자신의 운이 다한 것 같다고 말했다. 유언으로 대자에게 약간의 것을 남겼고, 오스본 부인이 자기를 다정히 기억해 주기를, 그리고 그녀가 곧 맺으려는 결혼에서 행복하기를 바란다고 했다. \"결혼이라니? 천만에.\" 조스가 대답했다. \"나는 그 애한테서 소식을 들었는데, 결혼 얘기는 한마디도 없었어. 그리고 말이 나왔으니 말인데, 참 묘하지, 그 애가 편지에 쓰기를 도빈 소령이 결혼할 거라며, 그가 행복하기를 바란다고 했단 말이지.\" 세들리가 유럽에서 받은 편지들의 날짜는 언제였던가? 민간인이 그것들을 가져왔다. 그 편지들은 소령이 받은 것보다 두 달이나 뒤의 것이었다. 그러자 배의 군의관은, 마드라스의 의사가 거의 희망이 없다며 선상으로 떠넘기다시피 한 새 환자에게 자신이 취한 치료법을 두고 흐뭇해했다. 왜냐하면 그날부터, 바로 그가 물약을 바꾼 그날부터 도빈 소령이 차도를 보이기 시작했기 때문이다. 그리하여 그 자격 있는 장교 커크 대위는 소령 진급에서 물을 먹게 되었던 것이다.\n\n세인트헬레나를 지난 뒤로 도빈 소령의 쾌활함과 기력은 동승객 모두를 놀라게 할 정도였다. 그는 사관후보생들과 장난을 치고, 항해사들과 막대기 검술을 겨루고, 소년처럼 삭구를 타고 오르고, 어느 날 밤에는 저녁 식사 후 그로그주를 들고 모여든 일행 전부를 즐겁게 하려고 익살스러운 노래를 부르는가 하면, 어찌나 명랑하고 활기차고 상냥하게 굴었던지, 처음에는 자기 승객에게서 별것 없다고 여기며 기개 없는 사내라고 생각했던 브래그 선장조차 그 소령이 말수는 적으나 박식하고 훌륭한 장교임을 인정하지 않을 수 없었다. \"그자한텐 '세련된' 매너라곤 없단 말이지, 젠장.\" 브래그가 일등 항해사에게 말했다. \"총독 관저에서는 어림도 없을 거요, 로퍼. 거기서 각하랑 윌리엄 부인은 나한테 어찌나 친절하던지, 좌중 전부가 보는 앞에서 나랑 악수를 하고, 총사령관 본인이 보는 앞에서 나더러 만찬에서 맥주를 같이 들자고까지 했단 말이오. 그자한텐 매너는 없지만, 뭔가가 있긴 해—\" 이렇게 브래그 선장은 자신이 지휘관으로서의 능력뿐 아니라 사람을 보는 안목까지 갖추고 있음을 보여 주었다.\n\n그러나 람춘더호가 영국까지 열흘 항해 거리에 들었을 때 바다가 잔잔해지자, 도빈은 어찌나 안달하며 언짢아하던지, 전에는 그의 활기와 좋은 성정에 감탄했던 동료들마저 놀라게 했다. 그는 다시 미풍이 불어올 때까지 평정을 되찾지 못했고, 수로 안내인이 배에 올랐을 때는 몹시 흥분한 상태였다. 맙소사, 정겨운 사우샘프턴의 두 첨탑이 시야에 들어왔을 때 그의 심장은 어찌나 두근거렸던가.\n\n우리의 소령은 램천더 호 선상에서 어찌나 사람들의 사랑을 받았던지, 그와 세들리 씨가 배에서 내려 자기들을 뭍으로 데려다줄 반가운 거룻배에 올라타자 선원이며 사관이며 할 것 없이 온 승무원이, 위대한 브래그 선장이 손수 앞장서서 도빈 소령을 위해 세 번 만세를 불렀고, 소령은 몹시 얼굴을 붉히며 고개를 숙여 감사의 뜻을 표했다. 그 만세가 십중팔구 제 몫이라 여겼을 조스는 금몰을 두른 모자를 벗어 들고 위풍당당하게 친구들을 향해 흔들어 보였으며, 그렇게 두 사람은 뭍으로 끌려가 더없이 위엄 있게 부두에 내려선 다음, 그곳에서 로열조지 호텔로 향했다.\n\n조지 호텔의 커피룸에 들어서는, 이국땅에서 돌아온 여행객의 눈을 사철 맞이하는 저 으리으리한 통구이 쇠고기 한 덩이며, 진짜 영국산 가양주 에일과 포터를 떠올리게 하는 은빛 술잔의 광경이란, 너무도 기운을 북돋우고 흐뭇하여, 이토록 아늑하고 푸근하고 정겨운 영국식 여관에 들어선 사람이라면 며칠쯤 거기 머물고 싶어 할 법도 하건만, 도빈은 곧장 역마차 이야기를 꺼냈고, 사우샘프턴에 도착하기가 무섭게 런던으로 가는 길에 오르고 싶어 했다. 그러나 조스는 그날 저녁에 움직인다는 말은 들으려고도 하지 않았다. 어찌하여 그가, 항해 내내 저 풍채 좋은 벵골 신사를 가둬 두었던 끔찍하게 비좁은 침상을 대신해 줄, 거기 떡하니 마련된 크고 넓은 깃털 침대의 출렁이는 보드라움을 마다하고, 역마차 안에서 밤을 지새워야 한단 말인가? 짐이 통관을 마치기 전에는 움직일 생각이 없었고, 물담뱃대를 곁에 두고 갈 수 있게 되기 전에는 길을 떠날 생각도 없었다. 그리하여 소령은 하는 수 없이 그날 밤을 묵어 가야 했고, 자기 가족에게 도착을 알리는 편지를 부치면서, 조스에게도 제 친지들에게 편지하겠다는 약속을 받아 냈다. 조스는 약속했으나 그 약속을 지키지 않았다. 선장과 선의, 그리고 한두 명의 승객이 여관으로 와서 우리 두 신사와 함께 저녁을 들었는데, 조스는 호화롭게 저녁상을 차리느라 한껏 솜씨를 부렸고 이튿날 소령과 함께 도시로 가겠노라 약속했다. 여관 주인은 세들리 씨가 첫 잔의 포터를 비우는 모습을 보니 눈이 다 시원해진다고 말했다. 내게 시간이 있고 또 곁길로 새는 일을 무릅쓸 용기가 있다면, 영국 땅에서 들이켜는 그 첫 잔의 포터에 관해 한 장을 통째로 쓰고 싶을 지경이다. 아, 그것이 얼마나 좋은지! 그 한 모금을 즐기기 위해서라도 일 년쯤 집을 떠나 있을 만한 가치가 있는 것이다.\n\n도빈 소령은 이튿날 아침 여느 때 버릇대로 말끔히 면도하고 옷을 차려입은 채 모습을 드러냈다. 사실 너무도 이른 아침이라, 집 안에 일어나 있는 사람이라고는 도무지 잠이라곤 필요 없어 보이는 저 신통한 여관 구두닦이뿐이었다. 소령은 그 어둑한 복도를 삐걱대며 돌아다니는 동안, 집 안 여기저기 묵는 손님들의 코 고는 소리가 복도를 따라 우렁차게 울려 퍼지는 것을 들을 수 있었다. 이윽고 잠 없는 구두닦이가 이 문 저 문을 살금살금 돌아다니며, 문밖에 내놓인 블루처 구두며 웰링턴 부츠며 옥스퍼드화를 하나하나 거두어 갔다. 그다음에는 조스의 인도인 하인이 일어나 주인의 묵직한 세면 도구를 챙기고 물담뱃대를 준비하기 시작했고, 뒤이어 하녀들이 일어났다가 복도에서 그 까무잡잡한 사내와 마주치고는 비명을 지르며 그를 악마로 착각했다. 그 하인과 도빈은 하녀들이 로열조지 호의 갑판을 닦느라 복도에 내놓은 물통에 발이 걸려 비틀거렸다. 면도도 하지 않은 첫 급사가 나타나 여관 문빗장을 풀자, 소령은 떠날 때가 왔다 여기고 당장 역마차를 불러오라 일러, 길을 나설 채비를 했다.\n\n그러고 나서 그는 세들리 씨의 방으로 걸음을 옮겨, 조스 씨가 코를 골고 있는 그 크고 넓은 가족용 침대의 휘장을 걷었다. \"자, 일어나요! 세들리,\" 소령이 말했다. \"떠날 시간이오. 삼십 분 뒤면 마차가 문 앞에 와 있을 거요.\"\n\n조스는 이불 밑에서 으르렁대며 몇 시냐고 물었다. 그러나 마침내 얼굴을 붉히는 소령에게서(아무리 제게 득이 될 일이라 해도 결코 거짓말이라곤 하지 못하는 사람이었다) 아침의 진짜 시각을 알아내자, 그는 차마 여기에 옮겨 적지 못할 험한 말을 한바탕 쏟아 냈는데, 그 욕설을 통해 도빈에게 알린 바인즉, 이런 시각에 일어났다가는 제 영혼을 위태롭게 할 것이며, 소령이야 가서 목이라도 매달리든 말든 알 바 아니고, 자기는 도빈과 함께 길을 떠나지 않을 것이며, 잠든 사람을 그런 식으로 깨우는 것이야말로 더없이 야박하고 신사답지 못한 짓이라는 것이었다. 그리하여 무안해진 소령은 물러날 수밖에 없었고, 조스가 중단된 잠을 다시 청하도록 내버려 둘 수밖에 없었다.\n\n이윽고 마차가 당도하자 소령은 더 이상 기다리려 하지 않았다.\n\n설령 그가 유람길에 오른 영국 귀족이거나 급보를 전하는 신문사 급사였다 해도(정부의 전갈은 대개 훨씬 조용히 운반되는 법이다), 그보다 더 빨리 달리지는 못했을 것이다. 역마차 마부들은 그가 자기들에게 던져 주는 삯에 어리둥절해했다. 마차가 이정표에서 이정표로 쏜살같이 굴러갈 때, 그 시골 풍경은 어찌나 행복하고 푸르러 보이던지. 여관 주인들이 미소와 절로 그를 맞으러 나오는 아담한 시골 읍내를 지나고, 느릅나무에 간판이 걸리고 말과 짐마차꾼들이 얼룩덜룩한 나무 그늘 아래서 목을 축이는 어여쁜 길가 여관을 지나고, 오래된 저택과 정원을 지나고, 고색창연한 잿빛 교회 주위로 옹기종기 모여든 시골 마을을 지나고 — 그렇게 매혹적이고 정겨운 영국의 풍경 속을 지나갔다. 세상에 이만한 곳이 또 있을까? 고향으로 돌아오는 나그네에게 그 풍경은 어찌나 다정해 보이는지 — 지나가는 동안 마치 당신과 악수를 청하는 듯한 것이다. 그렇게 도빈 소령은 사우샘프턴에서 런던에 이르도록 이 모든 풍경을 지나쳤으나, 길가의 이정표 너머로는 별달리 눈여겨보지 않았다. 보다시피 그는 캠버웰의 부모님을 어서 뵙고 싶은 마음에 그토록 조급했던 것이다.\n\n그는 피카딜리에서, 충실히 마차를 몰고 찾아간 옛 단골집 슬로터스 사이에서 허비한 시간을 아까워했다. 그곳을 마지막으로 본 지, 그러니까 그와 조지가 젊은 시절 그곳에서 숱하게 잔치를 벌이고 숱하게 흥청거리던 그 시절 이후로 긴 세월이 흘렀다. 이제 그는 노년의 문턱에 접어든 처지였다. 머리는 희끗희끗해졌고, 젊은 날의 숱한 열정과 감정도 그사이 잿빛으로 시들어 버렸다. 그러나 거기 문간에는 그 옛날의 늙은 급사가, 그 기름때 전 검은 옷차림 그대로, 그 이중 턱과 축 늘어진 얼굴 그대로, 회중시계 주머니에 그 큼직한 인장 꾸러미를 매단 그대로, 예전처럼 주머니 속 동전을 짤랑이며 서 있다가, 마치 소령이 떠난 지 일주일밖에 안 된 양 그를 맞이했다. \"소령님 짐은 이십삼호실에 들여놓게, 거기가 소령님 방이니까.\" 존이 조금도 놀라는 기색 없이 말했다. \"저녁으로는 통구이 닭이겠지요. 결혼은 안 하셨고요? 결혼하셨다고들 하던데 — 그 댁 스코틀랜드 군의관이 여기 왔었거든요. 아니지, 인도에서 모 연대랑 같이 주둔했던 삼십삼 연대 험비 대위였구먼. 더운물 좀 드릴까요? 그런데 어쩌자고 역마차를 타고 오셨대요 — 합승마차로는 부족하셨나?\" 그러고는 이 충실한 급사는, 이 집을 드나든 사관이라면 누구든 다 알고 다 기억하며, 십 년 세월쯤은 어제 일과 다름없는 사람이라, 앞장서서 도빈을 그의 옛 방으로 안내했다. 거기에는 그 커다란 모린 천 침대와, 한층 더 거무죽죽해진 듯한 낡은 양탄자, 그리고 빛바랜 사라사 천을 씌운 그 검은 가구들이, 소령이 젊은 시절에 기억하던 그대로 놓여 있었다.\n\n그는 조지가 결혼 전날 그 방 안을 서성이며 손톱을 물어뜯고, 아버지가 분명 마음을 돌릴 거라고, 만약 안 돌린다 해도 자기는 눈곱만큼도 상관없다고 호언하던 모습을 떠올렸다. 그는 조지가 도빈의 방문과, 바로 옆에 붙은 제 방 문을 쾅쾅 닫으며 걸어 들어오는 모습을 눈앞에 그려 볼 수 있었다 —\n\n\"젊어지진 않으셨네요.\" 존이 옛 시절의 벗을 차분히 훑어보며 말했다.\n\n도빈은 웃었다. \"십 년 세월에 열병까지 앓고 나면 사람이 젊어질 리가 있나, 존.\" 그가 말했다. \"늘 젊은 건 자네지 — 아니, 자네는 늘 늙어 있구먼.\"\n\n\"오스본 대위님 미망인은 어찌 됐답니까?\" 존이 말했다. \"그 양반 참 훤칠한 젊은이였는데. 세상에, 돈을 어찌나 펑펑 쓰시던지. 여기서 행군해 나가시던 그날 이후로 영영 안 돌아오셨지요. 지금 이 순간까지도 제게 삼 파운드를 빚지셨다오. 자 보세요, 제 장부에 적혀 있어요. '1815년 4월 10일, 오스본 대위: 3파운드.' 그 양반 부친이 갚아 주려나 모르겠네요.\" 그러면서 슬로터스의 존은, 대위에게 빌려준 돈을 적어 둔 바로 그 모로코가죽 수첩을 꺼내 보였는데, 그 기름때 전 빛바랜 책장은 아직도 멀쩡히 남아 있었고, 거기에는 이 집을 드나들던 지난날의 단골들에 관한 다른 갈겨쓴 비망록도 잔뜩 적혀 있었다.\n\n손님을 방에 들여놓은 뒤, 존은 더없이 태연하게 물러갔다. 도빈 소령은 제 우스꽝스러움에 얼굴을 붉히고 픽 웃지 않을 수 없으면서도, 행장 중에서 자기가 가진 가장 맵시 있고 가장 잘 어울리는 평상복을 골라냈고, 화장대 위 음울하고 자그마한 거울에 비친 제 햇볕에 그을린 얼굴과 희끗한 머리를 들여다보며 스스로를 비웃었다.\n\n\"늙은 존이 날 잊지 않았다니 다행이군.\" 그는 생각했다. \"그녀도 날 알아봐 주면 좋으련만.\" 그러고는 여관을 나서서, 다시 한번 브롬프턴 쪽으로 걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n그 한결같은 사내가 그녀의 집을 향해 걸어가는 동안, 애밀리아와의 마지막 만남의 사소한 장면 하나하나가 그의 마음속에 또렷이 떠올랐다. 그가 마지막으로 피카딜리에 와 본 이후로 개선문과 아킬레우스 동상이 세워졌고, 그의 눈과 마음이 어렴풋이 알아챈 변화가 백 가지는 일어나 있었다. 그는 브롬프턴에서 골목길을 올라가는 동안, 그녀가 사는 거리로 이어지는 그 또렷이 기억에 남은 골목길을 오르는 동안 떨리기 시작했다. 그녀는 결혼하게 될 것인가, 아닌가? 만약 그녀를 어린 아들과 함께 만난다면 — 맙소사, 그러면 어떻게 해야 한단 말인가? 그는 다섯 살쯤 된 아이를 데리고 자기 쪽으로 다가오는 한 여자를 보았다 — 저게 그녀일까? 그는 그 가능성만으로도 떨리기 시작했다. 마침내 그녀가 사는 그 줄지어 선 집들에 이르러 대문 앞에 다다르자, 그는 대문을 붙잡고 멈춰 섰다. 제 심장이 쿵쾅거리는 소리가 들릴 지경이었다. \"무슨 일이 있었든, 전능하신 하느님께서 그녀에게 축복을 내리시기를.\" 그는 속으로 생각했다. \"쳇! 그녀가 여기서 떠나 버렸을지도 모르지.\" 그렇게 말하고 그는 대문을 지나 안으로 들어섰다.\n\n예전에 그녀가 쓰던 응접실 창문은 열려 있었으나 방 안에는 아무도 없었다. 그래도 소령은 그 위에 걸린 그림과 함께, 옛날 그대로 놓여 있는 그 피아노를 알아본 듯했고, 그의 불안이 다시 일었다. 문에는 여전히 클랩 씨의 놋쇠 명패가 붙어 있었고, 도빈은 그 문고리로 사람을 불렀다.\n\n열여섯쯤 된, 통통하고 두 눈이 반짝이며 두 볼이 발그레한 처녀가 노크에 답하러 나와서는, 자그마한 현관에 기대선 소령을 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n그는 유령처럼 창백해진 얼굴로 가까스로 더듬더듬 말을 꺼냈다. \"여기 오스본 부인이 사십니까?\"\n\n처녀는 잠시 그의 얼굴을 빤히 들여다보더니 — 이내 자기도 새하얗게 질려서는 — 말했다. \"어머나 세상에 — 도빈 소령님이시네.\" 그녀는 떨리는 두 손을 내밀었다. \"절 기억 못 하세요?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제가 소령님을 사탕과자 소령님이라고 부르곤 했잖아요.\" 그러자, 평생 처음 있는 일이었으리라 싶은데, 소령은 그 처녀를 두 팔로 끌어안고 입을 맞추었다. 처녀는 발작하듯 웃다가 울다가 하더니, 있는 힘껏 \"엄마, 아빠!\" 하고 소리쳐 그 착한 양주를 불러왔다. 두 사람은 이미 운치 있는 부엌의 여닫이창으로 소령을 살피던 참이었는데, 좁은 복도에서 제 딸이 푸른 프록코트에 흰 무명 바지 차림의 키 큰 사내 품에 안겨 있는 것을 보고는 깜짝 놀랐다.\n\n\"전 옛 친구랍니다.\" 그가 말했다 — 그러면서도 얼굴을 붉히긴 했지만. \"절 기억 못 하시겠어요, 클랩 부인, 차 마실 때 만들어 주시던 그 맛난 케이크 말입니다? 절 못 알아보겠소, 클랩? 난 조지의 대부요, 인도에서 막 돌아온 길이라오.\" 한바탕 악수가 오갔다 — 클랩 부인은 몹시 감격하고 또 기뻐하며, 그 복도에서 수도 없이 하늘에 가호를 빌었다.\n\n이 집의 주인 내외는 사람 좋은 소령을 세들리네 방으로 안내했는데(그는 그 방의 가구라면, 한때 스토서드 사가 만든 산뜻하고 자그마한 악기였던 그 놋쇠 장식 낡은 피아노에서부터, 가리개며 설화석고로 만든 자그마한 묘비 모양 장식품 — 그 한가운데서 세들리 씨의 금시계가 째깍대고 있었다 — 에 이르기까지 하나하나 다 기억하고 있었다), 그가 그 빈 셋방살이용 안락의자에 앉자, 아버지와 어머니와 딸이 이야기 중간중간 수도 없이 감탄사를 터뜨려 가며, 우리가 이미 아는 바를 소령에게 일러 주었다. 다만 애밀리아의 사연 가운데 그가 미처 모르던 자세한 부분들 — 즉 세들리 부인의 죽음, 조지가 할아버지 오스본과 화해한 일, 미망인이 아들을 떠나보내며 얼마나 가슴 아파했는지, 그리고 그녀의 삶에 관한 다른 세세한 일들도 함께 일러 주었다. 두세 번 그는 결혼 문제를 물어보려 했으나 그때마다 용기가 나지 않았다. 그는 이런 사람들 앞에 그 속내를 드러내고 싶지 않았다. 마침내 그는, 오스본 부인이 화창한 오후 식사를 마친 뒤면 늘 노신사와 함께 그러하듯, 켄싱턴 공원으로 아버지와 산책을 나갔다는 말을 들었다(노인은 이제 몹시 쇠약하고 짜증이 심해 그녀를 고달프게 했으나, 그녀는 정말이지 천사처럼 그를 대했다).\n\n\"제가 시간이 몹시 촉박해서요.\" 소령이 말했다. \"오늘 밤에 중요한 볼일이 있거든요. 그래도 오스본 부인은 좀 뵙고 싶군요. 폴리 양이 저와 함께 가서 길을 안내해 주시면 어떨까요?\"\n\n폴리 양은 이 제안에 매료되었고 또 놀랐다. 그녀는 길을 알았다. 그녀가 도빈 소령에게 길을 안내해 드리겠노라 했다. 오스본 부인이 외출했을 때면 — 러셀 광장 쪽으로 갔을 때면 — 자기가 세들리 씨를 모시고 자주 갔었기에, 노인이 즐겨 앉는 그 벤치도 알고 있었다. 그녀는 폴짝폴짝 제 방으로 뛰어가더니, 이내 가장 좋은 보닛에다, 소령에게 어울리는 길동무가 되겠다며 빌려 걸친 어머니의 노란 숄과 커다란 마노 브로치까지 두르고 나타났다.\n\n그리하여 그 사관은, 푸른 프록코트에 사슴가죽 장갑 차림으로, 젊은 아가씨에게 팔을 내주었고, 두 사람은 더없이 흥겹게 걸어갔다. 그는 어쩐지 두려운 그 장면을 앞두고 곁에 동무가 있어 다행이라 여겼다. 그는 동행에게 애밀리아에 관해 천 가지도 더 되는 질문을 던졌다. 그 다정한 마음은, 그녀가 아들과 헤어져야 했다는 생각에 가슴 아파했다. 그녀는 그 일을 어떻게 견뎌 냈을까? 자주 아들을 보러 갈까? 세들리 씨는 이제 세속적인 형편으로 보아 그런대로 편안한 처지일까? 폴리는 사탕과자 소령님의 이 모든 질문에 제가 할 수 있는 한 최선을 다해 대답했다.\n\n그렇게 걸어가던 도중에 한 가지 일이 일어났는데, 그 성격이야 지극히 단순했으나 도빈 소령에게는 더없이 큰 기쁨을 안겨 준 일이었다. 성긴 구레나룻에 빳빳한 흰 목도리를 두른 한 창백한 젊은이가, 양팔에 숙녀를 하나씩 낀 샌드위치 꼴로 골목길을 걸어 내려오고 있었다. 한 사람은 키가 크고 위풍당당한 중년 여자로, 그 옆을 나란히 걷는 영국 국교회 목사와 이목구비며 안색이 비슷했고, 다른 한 사람은 거무튀튀한 얼굴에 키가 자그마한 여자로, 산뜻한 새 보닛에 흰 리본으로 멋을 내고 맵시 있는 외투를 걸쳤으며, 몸 한가운데에는 값나가는 금시계를 차고 있었다. 이 두 숙녀에게 양팔이 죄다 붙들린 신사는 게다가 양산과 숄과 바구니까지 들고 있어서 두 팔이 완전히 묶여 버린 터라, 메리 클랩 양이 무릎을 굽혀 인사하는 데도 모자에 손을 대어 답례할 수가 없었던 것이다.\n\n그는 그저 고개만 까닥여 그녀의 인사에 답했고, 두 숙녀는 생색내는 태도로 그 인사를 받으면서, 동시에 폴리 양과 동행한 푸른 코트에 등나무 지팡이를 든 사내를 매섭게 뜯어보았다.\n\n\"저 사람들은 누구죠?\" 소령이, 그 일행이 골목길을 올라가도록 길을 비켜 준 뒤, 그 무리가 재미있다는 듯 물었다. 메리는 다소 장난스럽게 그를 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"저분이 우리 부목사님이세요, 비니 목사님요(도빈 소령의 얼굴이 움찔했다). 그리고 저 누님 되시는 비니 양요. 어휴 세상에, 주일 학교에서 우릴 어찌나 들볶으셨는지. 그리고 다른 부인, 저 사팔뜨기에 멋진 시계 찬 자그마한 분은 비니 부인이세요 — 처녀 적엔 그리츠 양이었지요. 친정아버지가 식료품상이셨는데, 켄싱턴 그래블핏츠에서 '리틀 오리지널 골드 티 포트'를 하셨대요. 지난달에 결혼해서 마게이트에서 막 돌아왔답니다. 재산이 오천 파운드나 된대요. 그런데 그 부인이랑, 이 혼사를 맺어 준 비니 양이 벌써 사이가 틀어졌다지 뭐예요.\"\n\n소령은 아까도 얼굴이 움찔했지만, 이번에는 화들짝 놀라며 등나무 지팡이로 어찌나 힘껏 땅을 내리쳤던지 클랩 양이 \"아이고머니나\" 하고 외치며 깔깔 웃었다. 그는 잠시 입을 벌린 채 말없이 서서, 멀어져 가는 그 젊은 부부의 뒷모습을 바라보았다. 메리 양이 그들의 내력을 이야기하는 동안에도, 그는 그 목사가 결혼했다는 말 이후로는 아무것도 귀에 들어오지 않았다. 그의 머릿속은 행복으로 빙빙 돌고 있었다. 이 마주침 이후로 그는 목적지를 향해 곱절로 빨리 걷기 시작했다 — 그런데도 너무 일찍 당도하고 말았다(지난 십 년 동안 줄곧 갈망해 온 그 만남을 앞두고 그는 몹시 떨고 있었으니까) — 브롬프턴의 골목길을 지나, 켄싱턴 공원 담벼락의 자그마한 옛 문으로 들어섰다.\n\n\"저기 계시네요.\" 폴리 양이 말하자, 그녀는 소령이 또 한 번 제 팔에 매달린 채 흠칫 뒤로 물러서는 것을 느꼈다. 그녀는 어느새 이 일의 전모를 다 아는 심복이 되어 있었다. 그녀는 제가 좋아하는 소설책 — 『아비 없는 패니』나 『스코틀랜드의 족장들』 — 에서 읽기라도 한 듯 그 사연을 훤히 알고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"아가씨가 먼저 달려가서 부인께 알려 드리는 게 어떻겠소.\" 소령이 말했다. 폴리는 노란 숄을 산들바람에 나부끼며 앞으로 달려 나갔다.\n\n세들리 노인은 벤치에 앉아 무릎 위에 손수건을 펼쳐 놓은 채, 여느 때 버릇대로 옛 시절에 관한 어떤 오래된 이야기를 주절주절 늘어놓고 있었다. 애밀리아가 전에도 숱하게 귀 기울이며 참을성 있는 미소로 받아 주던 그런 이야기였다. 요즈음 그녀는 아버지의 이야기에 미소를 짓거나 그 밖의 알아듣는 시늉을 하면서도, 노인의 옛이야기는 한마디도 거의 귀에 담지 않은 채, 제 자신의 일을 골똘히 생각할 수 있게 되었다. 메리가 폴짝폴짝 달려오고 애밀리아가 그 모습을 알아보자, 그녀는 벤치에서 벌떡 일어났다. 첫 번째 든 생각은 조지에게 무슨 일이 생겼나 하는 것이었으나, 그 전령의 들뜨고 행복한 얼굴을 보자 겁 많은 어미의 가슴속 그 두려움은 이내 사라졌다.\n\n\"소식이에요! 소식! \" 도빈 소령의 사자가 외쳤다. \"그분이 오셨어요! 오셨다고요!\"\n\n\"누가 왔다는 거니?\" 에미가 여전히 아들 생각을 하며 말했다.\n\n\"저기 보세요.\" 클랩 양이 몸을 돌려 손가락으로 가리키며 대답했다. 애밀리아가 그쪽을 바라보니, 도빈의 야윈 몸과 긴 그림자가 잔디밭을 가로질러 성큼성큼 다가오고 있었다. 이번에는 애밀리아가 흠칫하더니, 얼굴이 발그레 달아오르며, 그러고는 으레 그렇듯 울기 시작했다. 이 순박한 자그마한 사람의 온갖 행사에는 늘 그 큰 분수의 물줄기가 터지곤 했던 것이다. 그녀가 그를 향해, 두 손을 앞으로 내밀어 그에게 건넬 채비를 하고 달려오는 동안, 그는 그녀를 바라보았다 — 아, 얼마나 애틋한 눈길로. 그녀는 변하지 않았다. 조금 창백하고, 몸매가 조금 더 통통해졌을 뿐. 두 눈은 여전했다, 그 다정하고 믿음직한 두 눈은. 그 부드러운 갈색 머리카락에 은빛 머리칼이라곤 채 세 가닥도 되지 않았다. 그녀는 눈물 너머로 발그레한 얼굴에 미소를 띤 채, 그의 정직하고 수수한 얼굴을 올려다보며 두 손을 다 그에게 내밀었다. 그는 그 자그마한 두 손을 자기 두 손 사이에 감싸 쥐고 그대로 잡고 있었다. 그는 잠시 말을 잇지 못했다. 어찌하여 그는 그녀를 두 팔로 끌어안고 결코 그녀를 떠나지 않겠노라 맹세하지 않았던가? 그녀는 분명 응했을 텐데. 그의 말에 따르지 않을 수 없었을 텐데.\n\n\"또 — 또 한 사람의 도착을 알려 드릴 게 있습니다.\" 그가 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 말했다.\n\n\"도빈 부인이요?\" 애밀리아가 뒤로 한 걸음 물러서며 말했다 — 어째서 그는 말을 않는 걸까?\n\n\"아니요.\" 그가 그녀의 두 손을 놓으며 말했다. \"누가 그런 거짓말을 했답니까? 제 말은, 부인의 오라버니 조스가 저와 같은 배로 와서, 여러분 모두를 행복하게 해 드리려고 고향에 돌아왔다는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"아버지, 아버지!\" 에미가 외쳤다. \"소식이에요! 오라버니가 영국에 왔어요. 아버지를 보살펴 드리려고요. 여기 도빈 소령님도 오셨고요.\"\n\n세들리 씨는 몹시 떨면서 생각을 가다듬으며 벌떡 일어났다. 그러고는 앞으로 나아가, 그를 도빈 씨라 부르며 구식의 절을 하고는, 그의 훌륭하신 부친 윌리엄 경께서 평안하시냐고 안부를 물었다. 그는 얼마 전 자기를 찾아 주시는 영예를 베푸신 윌리엄 경께 답방을 가겠노라 했다. 윌리엄 경이 이 노신사를 찾아온 일은 팔 년 전의 일이었으니 — 노인은 바로 그 방문에 답례할 생각을 하고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"아버지가 많이 쇠약해지셨어요.\" 도빈이 다가가 다정하게 노인과 악수를 나누는 동안 에미가 속삭였다.\n\n소령은 그날 저녁 런던에서 그토록 긴한 볼일이 있었음에도, 집으로 가서 차를 함께 들자는 세들리 씨의 권유에 그 볼일을 미루기로 했다. 애밀리아는 노란 숄을 두른 어린 동무의 팔에 제 팔을 끼고 집으로 돌아가는 일행의 앞장을 섰고, 그리하여 세들리 씨는 도빈의 몫으로 떨어졌다. 노인은 아주 느릿느릿 걸으며, 자기 자신과 가엾은 베시에 관한, 한때의 번영과 파산에 관한 케케묵은 옛이야기를 잔뜩 늘어놓았다. 쇠약해진 노인들이 흔히 그러하듯, 그의 생각은 온통 지난 시절에 가 있었다. 그가 절감하는 그 한 가지 재앙만 빼면, 그는 현재에 관해서는 아는 바가 거의 없었다. 소령은 노인이 마음껏 이야기하도록 내버려 두어 기뻤다. 그의 두 눈은 앞서가는 그 모습에 — 그의 상상 속에, 그의 기도 속에 늘 함께하고, 깨어 있든 잠들어 있든 그의 꿈을 찾아오던 그 사랑스럽고 자그마한 모습에 — 못 박혀 있었다.\n\n애밀리아는 그날 저녁 내내 더없이 행복하게, 미소를 머금고 분주히 움직이며, 도빈이 보기에 더할 나위 없는 우아함과 예의로 그 자그마한 다과회의 안주인 노릇을 해냈다. 그들이 땅거미 속에 앉아 있는 동안, 그의 두 눈은 그녀를 따라 이리저리 움직였다. 그가 얼마나 숱하게 그 순간을 갈망했던가. 뜨거운 바람 아래서, 고단한 행군 속에서, 멀리 떨어진 그녀를 — 지금 그가 보고 있는 그대로, 온화하고 행복하게, 노년의 필요를 다정히 돌보며, 다디단 순종으로 가난을 곱게 단장하는 그녀를 떠올렸던가. 그의 취향이 가장 고상했다거나, 우리의 순박한 옛 벗에게 흡족했던 것과 같은 빵과 버터의 낙원에 만족하는 것이 위대한 지성의 의무라고 말하려는 것은 아니다. 그러나 좋든 나쁘든 그의 소망은 이런 부류의 것이었고, 애밀리아가 곁에서 거들어 준다면야, 그는 존슨 박사에 못지않게 몇 잔이고 차를 들이켤 준비가 되어 있었다.\n\n애밀리아는 이런 성향을 알아채고 웃으며 부추겼고, 그에게 차를 한 잔 또 한 잔 따라 주면서 무척이나 장난스러운 표정을 지었다. 사실 그녀는 소령이 저녁을 거른 줄도, 슬로터스에 그를 위한 식탁보가 깔리고 그 위에 자리를 맡아 두었다는 표시로 접시 하나가 놓여 있는 줄도 알지 못했다. 바로 그 칸막이 자리, 그녀가 핑커튼 양의 학교에서 막 돌아온 어린아이였던 시절에 소령과 조지가 숱하게 마주 앉아 흥청대던 그 자리에 말이다.\n\n오스본 부인이 소령에게 맨 먼저 보여 준 것은 조지의 초상화였는데, 집에 도착하자마자 그것을 가지러 그녀는 위층으로 뛰어 올라갔다. 물론 그 그림은 그 아이의 절반만큼도 잘생기게 그려지지 못했지만, 그래도 그것을 어머니에게 가져다줄 생각을 했다니 얼마나 기특한 일인가? 아버지가 깨어 있는 동안에는 그녀가 조지 이야기를 그리 많이 하지 않았다. 오스본 씨와 러셀 광장에 관한 이야기는 노인이 듣기 좋아하지 않았으니, 노인은 지난 몇 달 동안 주로 그 더 부유한 연적의 적선으로 살아온 줄도 아마 의식하지 못한 채, 그쪽을 입에 올리기만 해도 성을 내곤 했던 것이다.\n\n도빈은 램천더 호 선상에서 있었던 일을 노인에게 죄다, 어쩌면 죄다보다 조금 더 보태어 들려주었고, 아버지를 향한 조스의 자비로운 마음과 노년을 편안히 모시겠다는 결심을 부풀려 이야기했다. 사실인즉 항해 동안 소령은 이 의무를 동행자에게 더없이 강력하게 새겨 넣고, 누이와 그 아이를 책임지겠다는 약속까지 받아 냈던 것이다. 그는 노인이 자기 앞으로 발행한 어음 문제로 조스가 부아를 내는 것을 달래 주었고, 같은 일로 자기가 겪은 고초와 노인이 자기에게 보내 준 그 유명한 포도주 위탁 건을 웃음거리 삼아 들려주면서, 기분이 좋고 적당히 추켜세워 주면 결코 심성이 고약하지 않은 조스 씨를, 유럽에 있는 친지들에 대해 아주 흐뭇한 심정에 이르게 만들었다.\n\n그리고 끝내, 부끄러운 말이지만, 소령은 진실을 한껏 늘여, 조스가 다시 유럽으로 돌아온 것은 주로 부친을 뵙고 싶은 마음 때문이었노라고 세들리 노인에게 일러 주었다.\n\n여느 때의 시각이 되자 세들리 씨는 의자에 앉은 채 꾸벅꾸벅 졸기 시작했고, 그제야 비로소 애밀리아는 이야기를 시작할 기회를 얻어, 더없이 간절한 마음으로 입을 열었다 — 그것은 오로지 조지에 관한 이야기였다. 그녀는 아들과 헤어질 때 제가 겪은 고통에 관해서는 일절 입에 올리지 않았다. 실로 이 갸륵한 여인은, 아이와의 이별로 반쯤 죽을 지경이었으면서도, 아들을 잃었다고 한탄하는 것은 제가 몹시 못된 짓을 저지르는 거라 여겼던 것이다. 다만 그 아이에 관한 것이라면 무엇이든 — 그 미덕이며 재주며 앞날이며 — 죄다 쏟아 냈다. 그녀는 그 아이의 천사 같은 아름다움을 그려 보였고, 함께 사는 동안 그 아이가 보여 준 너그러움과 큰 도량의 사례를 백 가지나 늘어놓았다. 어느 왕실 공작부인이 켄싱턴 공원에서 걸음을 멈추고 그 아이에게 감탄했다는 이야기며, 지금은 얼마나 융숭하게 보살핌을 받고 있으며, 마부와 조랑말까지 거느리고 있다는 이야기, 그 아이가 얼마나 영민하고 똑똑한지, 그리고 조지의 스승인 로런스 빌 목사가 얼마나 놀랄 만큼 박식하고 유쾌한 분인지 하는 이야기까지. \"그분은 모르는 게 없으세요.\" 애밀리아가 말했다. \"더없이 유쾌한 모임을 여신답니다. 소령님처럼 그토록 학식 있고, 그토록 책을 많이 읽으셨고, 그토록 영리하고 교양 있는 분이라면 — 고개를 저으며 아니라고 하지 마세요 — 그분이 늘 소령님더러 그렇다고 하셨거든요 — 빌 씨의 모임에 분명 반하실 거예요. 매달 마지막 화요일에 열린답니다. 그분 말씀이, 조지가 법조계든 의회든 못 오를 자리가 없다지 뭐예요. 자 보세요.\" 그러고 그녀는 피아노 서랍으로 가서 조지가 쓴 작문 한 편을 꺼냈다. 아직도 조지의 어머니가 간직하고 있는 이 위대한 천재의 역작은 다음과 같다.\n\n이기심에 관하여 — 인간의 품성을 떨어뜨리는 온갖 악덕 가운데, 이기심은 가장 가증스럽고 경멸할 만한 것이다. 자기 자신에 대한 지나친 사랑은 가장 끔찍한 죄악으로 이어지며, 국가에든 가정에든 가장 큰 불행을 불러온다. 이기적인 사람이 제 집안을 가난하게 만들고 흔히 파멸로 몰아넣듯이, 이기적인 임금은 제 백성에게 파멸을 가져오고 흔히 그들을 전쟁으로 몰아넣는다.\n\n예: 시인 호메로스가 지적한 바, 아킬레우스의 이기심은 그리스인들에게 천 가지 재앙을 안겨 주었다 — 무리 아카이오이스 알게 에테케 —(호메로스 『일리아스』 제1권 2행). 고(故) 나폴레옹 보나파르트의 이기심은 유럽에 헤아릴 수 없는 전쟁을 일으켰고, 그 자신을 비참한 섬 — 대서양의 세인트헬레나 섬 — 에서 죽게 만들었다.\n\n우리는 이 사례들을 통해, 우리가 우리 자신의 이익과 야심만을 좇아서는 안 되며, 우리 자신의 이익과 더불어 남의 이익도 헤아려야 함을 알 수 있다.\n\n조지 S. 오스본 아테네 학사, 1827년 4월 24일\n\n\"이 나이에 이런 글씨를 쓰고, 게다가 그리스어까지 인용한다고 생각해 보세요.\" 흐뭇해하는 어머니가 말했다. \"오, 윌리엄,\" 그녀가 소령에게 손을 내밀며 덧붙였다. \"하늘이 저 아이로 제게 얼마나 큰 보물을 주셨는지요! 그 아이는 제 삶의 위안이에요 — 그리고 그 아이는 떠나가신 — 그분을 꼭 빼닮았답니다!\"\n\n\"그녀가 그에게 한결같다고 해서 내가 화를 내야 옳단 말인가?\" 윌리엄은 생각했다. \"무덤 속의 내 벗을 질투해야 옳단 말인가, 아니면 애밀리아 같은 마음이 단 한 번 영원토록만 사랑할 수 있다 하여 상처받아야 옳단 말인가? 오, 조지, 조지, 그러면서도 자네는 자네가 얻은 보물이 얼마나 값진 줄 어찌 그리 몰랐던가.\" 이 감회가 윌리엄의 마음을 빠르게 스쳐 지나가는 동안, 그는 애밀리아의 손을 잡고 있었고, 그녀는 손수건으로 두 눈을 가리고 있었다.\n\n\"고마운 친구분,\" 그녀가 제 손을 잡은 그 손을 꼭 쥐며 말했다. \"소령님은 늘 제게 얼마나 다정하고, 얼마나 친절하셨는지요! 저것 봐요! 아버지가 깨시려나 봐요. 내일 조지를 보러 가실 거죠, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"내일은 안 됩니다.\" 가엾은 늙은 도빈이 말했다. \"볼일이 있어서요.\" 그는 아직 부모님과 사랑하는 누이 앤에게조차 들르지 못했다는 사실을 차마 털어놓고 싶지 않았다 — 이 태만함을 두고 분별 있는 사람이라면 누구나 소령을 나무라리라고 나는 확신한다. 이윽고 그는 작별을 고하면서, 조스가 당도하거든 전하라며 제 주소를 남겨 두고 떠났다. 그리하여 첫째 날이 저물었고, 그는 그녀를 본 것이다.\n\n그가 슬로터스로 돌아왔을 때 통구이 닭은 물론 식어 있었고, 그는 그 식은 그대로를 저녁으로 먹었다. 그리고 제 가족이 얼마나 일찍 잠자리에 드는지 잘 알고 있었기에, 이토록 늦은 시각에 그들의 잠을 굳이 깨울 필요가 없다 여기고서, 도빈 소령은 그날 저녁 헤이마켓 극장에서 반값 입장으로 호사를 누렸다고 기록에 전한다. 부디 그가 거기서 즐거운 시간을 보냈기를 바라 마지않는다.\n\n소령의 방문은 늙은 존 세들리를 큰 동요와 흥분 상태에 빠뜨렸다. 그날 밤 딸은 아무리 애써도 그를 평소의 일거리나 소일거리에 진득이 붙어 있게 만들 수 없었다. 그는 저녁 내내 상자며 책상을 뒤적거렸고, 떨리는 손으로 서류 뭉치를 풀어 헤쳐, 조스가 도착할 때를 대비해 그것들을 분류하고 정리했다. 그는 모든 것을 더없이 가지런하게 갖추어 두었다. 끈이며 서류철이며, 영수증이며, 변호사며 거래처와 주고받은 편지며, 와인 사업과 관련된 서류들이며(이 사업은 더없이 찬란한 전망으로 시작했으나 도무지 설명할 수 없는 사고로 실패하고 말았다), 석탄 사업에 관한 것들이며(이것은 자본 부족이 없었더라면 일찍이 세상에 선보인 가장 성공적인 사업이 되었을 것이다), 특허받은 제재소와 톱밥 압축 사업에 관한 것들이며 등등을. 그는 밤새도록, 아주 늦은 시각까지 이 서류들을 정리하느라, 가물거리는 촛불과 떨리는 손을 들고 이 방 저 방을 비척비척 옮겨 다녔다. 여기는 와인 서류, 여기는 톱밥, 여기는 석탄. 여기는 내가 캘커타와 마드라스로 보낸 편지들, 그리고 그에 대한 도빈 소령 무공훈장 수훈자와 조지프 세들리 씨의 답장들. \"이 아비한테서는 흐트러진 구석 하나 못 찾을 게다, 에미.\" 노신사가 말했다.\n\n에미는 미소 지었다. \"조스가 그 서류들을 보고 싶어 할 것 같진 않은데요, 아버지.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"넌 사업이라는 걸 통 모르는구나, 얘야.\" 아비가 자못 의젓한 태도로 고개를 가로저으며 대답했다. 그리고 이 점에서 에미가 몹시 무지했음은 인정하지 않을 수 없거니와, 더러 그토록 사정에 밝은 사람들이 있다는 것이 안타까운 일이기도 한 것이다. 이 모든 하찮은 서류들을 곁탁자 위에 정리해 둔 다음, 늙은 세들리는 깨끗한 인도식 손수건 한 장으로(도빈 소령에게서 받은 것 가운데 하나였다) 그것들을 정성껏 덮어 두었다. 그러고는 하녀와 집주인 여자에게 더없이 엄숙한 태도로, 이튿날 아침 조지프 세들리 씨, \"명예로운 동인도회사 벵골 행정청 소속 조지프 세들리 씨\"의 도착을 위해 정리해 둔 그 서류들을 절대 건드리지 말라고 단단히 일러두었다.\n\n이튿날 아침 애밀리아가 보니 그는 어느 때보다도 일찍 일어나, 더욱 들떠 있고, 더욱 상기되어 있고, 더욱 떨고 있었다. \"잠을 통 못 잤다, 에미야, 얘야.\" 그가 말했다. \"가엾은 베시 생각을 했단다. 그 사람이 살아서 조스의 마차를 한 번 더 타 볼 수 있다면 얼마나 좋을까. 제 마차를 가지고 다녔는데, 참 잘 어울렸지.\" 그의 두 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁 차오르더니 주름진 늙은 얼굴을 타고 흘러내렸다. 애밀리아는 그 눈물을 닦아 주고, 미소 지으며 입을 맞추고, 노인의 목수건을 맵시 있게 나비 모양으로 매어 주고, 그의 가장 좋은 셔츠 가슴 장식에 브로치를 꽂아 주었다. 그렇게 차려입은 그는 일요일에 입는 상복 차림으로 아침 여섯 시부터 앉아 아들이 도착하기를 기다렸다.\n\n그러나 우편배달부가 모습을 드러내자, 이 작은 식구는 조스가 누이에게 보낸 편지를 받고 마음 졸이던 상태에서 풀려났다. 편지에서 그는 항해 끝에 조금 피곤하여 그날은 움직일 수 없을 것 같으나, 이튿날 아침 일찍 사우샘프턴을 떠나 저녁이면 아버지와 어머니 곁에 닿겠노라고 알렸다. 애밀리아는 아버지에게 편지를 소리 내어 읽어 주다가 그 끝 대목에서 잠시 멈칫했다. 오라버니가 집안에 무슨 일이 일어났는지 모르고 있는 것이 분명했다. 알 리가 없었다. 사실 소령은 자기 길동무가 스물네 시간이라는 그토록 짧은 시간 안에 도무지 몸을 일으키지 못하고 무슨 핑계든 대어 미적거리리라 정확히 짐작하기는 했으나, 우편 시간이 한참 지나도록 애밀리아와 이야기 나누느라 정신이 팔려, 세들리 집안에 닥친 불행을 알리는 편지를 조스에게 써 보내지 못했던 것이다.\n\n사우샘프턴의 번화가에는 멋들어진 양복점 몇이 있는데, 그 고운 판유리 창에는 비단과 벨벳, 금빛과 진홍빛으로 된 온갖 화려한 조끼가 걸려 있고, 최신 유행을 그린 그림들이 걸려 있다. 그 그림 속에서는 외알 안경을 든 멋쟁이 신사들이, 눈이 유난히 크고 머리가 곱슬곱슬한 어린 사내아이들의 손을 잡은 채, 애플리 하우스의 아킬레스 동상 곁을 말 타고 사뿐사뿐 지나가는 승마복 차림의 숙녀들에게 추파를 던지고 있다. 조스는 캘커타에서 마련할 수 있는 가장 화려한 조끼들을 갖추고 있었음에도, 이런 옷 한두 벌을 갖추기 전에는 도시로 갈 수 없다고 여겼다. 그리하여 금빛 나비를 수놓은 진홍 새틴 조끼 하나와, 흰 줄무늬에 둥글게 접히는 깃이 달린 붉고 검은 벨벳 타탄 조끼 하나를 골랐다. 거기에 고급 푸른 새틴 목장식과, 분홍 에나멜을 입힌 기마인이 다섯 단짜리 울타리 문을 뛰어넘는 모양의 금 핀까지 갖추면, 어느 정도 위엄을 차리고 런던에 입성할 수 있으리라 생각했다. 예전에 조스가 보이던 수줍음, 어물거리며 얼굴을 붉히던 소심함은 이제 자기 값어치에 대한 한결 솔직하고 당당한 자기주장으로 바뀌어 있었다. \"나는 굳이 부인하지 않겠소.\" 워털루 세들리는 벗들에게 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"나는 멋 부리기를 좋아하는 사람이오.\" 그러면서도 그는 관저 무도회에서 숙녀들이 자기를 쳐다보면 다소 불편해했고, 그들의 눈길을 받으면 얼굴을 붉히며 놀라 외면했는데, 이는 주로 그들이 자기에게 연정을 품을까 봐 두려워서 피한 것이었으니, 결혼이라는 것 자체를 질색하던 사람이었다. 그러나 캘커타에 워털루 세들리만 한 멋쟁이는 없었다고들 한다. 그는 가장 멋진 마차를 갖추었고, 가장 훌륭한 독신자 만찬을 베풀었으며, 그 일대에서 가장 좋은 은식기를 소유하고 있었다.\n\n이 조끼들을 그의 몸집과 위엄에 걸맞게 짓는 데는 적어도 하루가 걸렸는데, 그 시간의 일부를 그는 자기와 자기 인도인 하인의 시중을 들 하인 하나를 고용하는 데, 그리고 자기 짐과 상자, 한 번도 읽지 않는 책들, 망고와 처트니와 카레 가루가 든 궤짝들, 아직 알지도 못하는 사람들에게 선물할 숄들, 그 밖의 페르시아풍 호사스러운 물품 일체를 통관시킬 대리인에게 지시하는 데 썼다.\n\n* 페르시아풍 호사: 호라티우스의 시구 \"페르시아풍 사치를 나는 싫어하노라\"에서 따온 표현으로, 동방의 호화로운 물품을 가리킨다.\n마침내 사흘째 되는 날 그는 새 조끼를 차려입고 느긋이 마차를 몰아 런던으로 향했다. 인도인 하인은 마부석에서 새 유럽인 하인 곁에 앉아 숄을 두른 채 이를 딱딱 마주치며 떨고 있었고, 조스는 마차 안에서 이따금 파이프를 뻐끔거리며 어찌나 위풍당당한 모습이던지, 어린 사내아이들이 만세를 외쳤고 많은 사람이 그를 무슨 총독쯤으로 여겼을 정도였다. 장담하건대, 그는 깔끔한 시골 마을마다 여관 주인들이 내려서 요기하고 가시라고 굽실거리며 청하는 것을 마다하지 않았다. 사우샘프턴에서 생선과 밥과 삶은 달걀을 곁들인 푸짐한 아침을 든 그는, 윈체스터에 이르러서는 어지간히 기운을 차린지라 셰리주 한 잔이 필요하겠다고 여겼다. 올턴에서는 하인의 청에 따라 마차에서 내려, 그 고장에서 유명한 에일을 들이켰다. 판햄에서는 주교의 성을 구경하느라 멈추고, 스튜로 끓인 장어와 송아지 고기 커틀릿과 강낭콩에 클라레 한 병을 곁들인 가벼운 식사를 들었다. 배그숏 들판을 지날 무렵에는 추위가 닥쳐, 인도인 하인은 점점 더 이를 떨었고 조스 사힙은 브랜디에 물을 타 마셨다. 사실 그가 도시로 마차를 몰고 들어왔을 때는, 와인이며 맥주며 고기며 절임이며 체리브랜디며 담배로, 증기선 사무장 선실만큼이나 가득 차 있었다. 그의 마차가 브롬프턴의 작은 문 앞으로 요란하게 들이닥쳤을 때는 저녁 무렵이었다. 그 다정한 사내는 도빈 씨가 슬로터스 여관에 잡아 둔 숙소로 가기에 앞서, 먼저 이곳으로 마차를 몰았던 것이다.\n\n거리의 온갖 얼굴들이 창가에 모여들었다. 어린 하녀는 쪽문으로 날아갔고, 클랩 부인 모녀는 장식 달린 부엌 여닫이창으로 내다보았다. 에미는 잔뜩 들떠서, 모자며 외투가 걸린 복도에 나와 있었고, 안쪽 거실의 늙은 세들리는 온몸을 떨고 있었다. 조스는 사우샘프턴에서 데려온 새 시종과, 갈색 얼굴이 이제는 추위로 시퍼레져 칠면조 모래주머니 빛깔이 된 채 덜덜 떠는 인도인 하인의 부축을 받으며, 위풍당당한 모습으로 역마차에서 내려 삐걱대며 흔들리는 발판을 밟고 내려섰다. 이윽고 그는 복도에서 엄청난 소동을 일으켰다. 거실 문에 귀를 대고 엿들으려고 나온 듯한 클랩 부인과 그 딸은, 외투들 아래 현관 의자에서 롤 주왑이 덜덜 떨며 묘하게 가련한 소리로 신음하고, 누런 눈알과 흰 이를 드러내고 있는 것을 발견했다.\n\n까닭인즉, 보다시피, 우리가 조스와 늙은 아버지, 그리고 안에 있던 가엾고 작고 온순한 누이의 상봉을 향해 슬며시 문을 닫아 버렸기 때문이다. 노인은 몹시 감격했다. 물론 그의 딸도 그러했고, 조스 또한 감정이 북받치지 않은 것은 아니었다. 십 년이라는 그 긴 부재 동안에는, 아무리 이기적인 사람이라도 고향과 어린 시절의 인연을 떠올리는 법이다. 거리는 그 둘을 모두 거룩하게 만든다. 잃어버린 그 즐거움들을 오래도록 곱씹다 보면 그 매력과 달콤함은 부풀려지기 마련이다. 조스는 그동안 서먹한 사이였던 아버지를 보고 그 손을 잡으며 꾸밈없이 기뻐했고, 그토록 예쁘고 잘 웃던 모습으로 기억하는 누이동생을 보고 기뻐했으며, 세월과 슬픔과 불운이 저 망가진 노인에게 빚어 놓은 변화에 마음 아파했다. 에미는 검은 옷차림으로 문간에 나와, 어머니가 돌아가셨다는 것을 그에게 속삭이며 아버지 앞에서는 그 일을 입에 올리지 말라고 일렀다. 그러나 그런 당부는 필요하지 않았으니, 늙은 세들리 본인이 곧장 그 일을 입에 올리기 시작하여, 재잘재잘 이야기하고, 펑펑 눈물을 쏟았기 때문이다. 그것은 인도에서 온 사람을 적잖이 충격에 빠뜨렸고, 그 가엾은 사내로 하여금 평소 버릇과는 달리 제 생각만 하지는 못하게 만들었다.\n\n그 만남의 결과는 무척 흡족한 것이었음이 틀림없다. 조스가 다시 역마차에 올라 자기 호텔로 떠나고 나자, 에미는 의기양양한 기색으로 아버지에게 호소하듯 다정하게 끌어안으며, 오라버니의 마음씨가 곱다고 자기가 늘 말하지 않았더냐고 노인에게 물었던 것이다.\n\n과연 조지프 세들리는, 자기 식구들이 처한 초라한 형편에 마음이 움직이고, 첫 상봉이 불러일으킨 벅차오르고 넘쳐흐르는 감정에 젖어, 이제 다시는 식구들이 궁핍이나 불편을 겪는 일은 없을 것이며, 자기가 어쨌든 한동안은 본국에 머물 터이니 그동안 자기 집과 가진 것 모두를 식구들의 것으로 삼겠노라고, 그리고 애밀리아가 제 살림을 차리기 전까지는 자기 식탁의 안주인 노릇을 하면 무척 예뻐 보일 것이라고 선언했다.\n\n그녀는 서글피 고개를 가로젓고, 늘 그렇듯 또 눈물 보따리를 풀었다. 그녀는 오라버니의 말뜻을 알아챘던 것이다. 그녀와 어린 속내 친구 메리 양은 바로 소령이 방문하던 그날 밤, 이 문제를 아주 속속들이 이야기 나누었다. 충동적인 폴리는 그날 밤이 지나기까지 자기가 알아낸 사실을 입에 담지 않고는 배기지 못했으니, 비니 씨가 신부를 데리고 지나가고 소령이 이제 두려워할 연적이 없음을 알게 되었을 때 도빈 소령이 깜짝 놀라며 기쁨에 떨던 모습을 본 그대로 그려 보였던 것이다. \"글쎄, 아씨가 그분께 결혼하셨냐고 여쭈었을 때 그분이 온몸을 떨면서 '누가 그런 거짓말을 합디까?' 하시던 거 못 보셨어요? 아유, 아씨.\" 폴리가 말했다. \"그분은 아씨한테서 눈을 떼질 못하셨다니까요. 분명 아씨 생각에 머리가 다 세도록 애태우신 거예요.\"\n\n그러나 애밀리아는, 남편과 아들의 초상화가 걸려 있는 침대를 올려다보며, 그 어린 보호 대상에게 다시는, 두 번 다시는 그 이야기를 입에 올리지 말라고 일렀다. 도빈 소령은 자기 남편의 가장 소중한 벗이자 자기와 조지에게 더없이 다정하고 자애로운 보호자였으며, 자기는 그를 오라비처럼 사랑한다고. 그러나 저런 천사 같은 사람과 결혼했던 여자가—그러면서 그녀는 벽을 가리켰다—다른 그 누구와 다시 맺어지는 일을 생각이나 할 수 있겠느냐고. 가엾은 폴리는 한숨을 쉬었다. 그녀는 진찰소의 그 젊은 톰킨스 씨가 죽기라도 하면 자기는 어찌해야 하나 생각했다. 교회에서 늘 자기를 그렇게 바라보고, 그저 그런 도발적인 눈길만으로도 겁 많은 자기 작은 가슴을 어찌나 두근거리게 하던지 당장이라도 항복하고 싶게 만들던 그 사람 말이다. 그가 죽으면 어쩌나? 그녀는 그가 폐병을 앓고 있음을 알고 있었으니, 두 볼이 어찌나 붉고 허리가 어찌나 유난히 가늘던지.\n\n그렇다고 에미가 그 정직한 소령의 연정을 알게 되었다 하여 어떤 식으로든 그를 매정하게 물리쳤다거나 그가 못마땅했던 것은 아니다. 그토록 진실하고 의리 있는 신사가 보내는 그런 사모의 정에 화를 낼 여자는 없는 법이다. 데스데모나도 캐시오에게 화를 내지 않았으니, 그녀가 그 부관이 자기에게 호감을 품은 것을 알아챘으리라는 데는 의심의 여지가 거의 없다(또한 나로서는 저 가련한 사건에서, 그 훌륭한 무어인 장교가 결코 알지 못한 일들이 훨씬 더 많이 벌어졌으리라 믿는다). 미란다조차 칼리반에게 무척 다정하지 않았던가, 그리고 우리는 그것이 같은 까닭에서였음을 거의 확신할 수 있다. 그렇다고 그녀가 그를—저 가엾고 흉측한 괴물을—조금이라도 부추겼다는 말은 물론 아니다. 마찬가지로 에미도 결코 자기를 흠모하는 소령을 무슨 식으로든 부추길 마음은 없었다. 그녀는 그토록 빼어난 인품과 충심에 합당한 다정한 호의를 그에게 베풀 것이요, 그가 청혼하기 전까지는 더없이 진심 어린 솔직한 태도로 그를 대할 것이며, 그때가 되면 비로소 입을 열어, 결코 이루어질 수 없는 그 희망을 끝맺을 시간도 넉넉하리라고 생각했다.\n\n그리하여 그녀는 그날 밤 폴리 양과의 대화를 마치고는 아주 곤히 잠들었고, 조스가 도착을 미룬 데도 아랑곳없이 여느 때보다 더 행복했다. \"그이가 그 오다우드 양과 결혼하지 않게 되어 다행이야.\" 그녀는 생각했다. \"오다우드 대령에게 윌리엄 소령처럼 교양 있는 사람에게 어울릴 누이가 있을 리 없지.\" 자기의 좁은 교제권 안에 그에게 좋은 아내가 되어 줄 사람이 누가 있을까? 비니 양은 아니지, 너무 나이 들고 성미가 까다로우니까. 오스본 양? 그 사람도 너무 나이 들었고. 어린 폴리는 너무 어렸다. 오스본 부인은 잠들기 전까지 소령에게 어울릴 사람을 끝내 찾아내지 못했다.\n\n같은 날 아침, 도빈 소령에게도 사우샘프턴의 친구가 슬로터스 커피점으로 보낸 편지 한 통이 도착했다. 다정한 도브, 전날 잠에서 깨워졌을 때 화를 낸 것을 부디 너그러이 봐 달라며(지독한 두통이 있던 데다 막 첫잠이 든 참이었다는 것이다), 슬로터스에 세들리 씨와 그 하인들이 묵을 편안한 방을 잡아 달라고 도브에게 청하는 내용이었다. 항해하는 동안 소령은 조스에게 없어서는 안 될 사람이 되어 있었다. 조스는 그에게 정이 들었고 그에게 기댔다. 다른 승객들은 모두 런던으로 떠나 버렸다. 젊은 리케츠와 어린 채퍼스는 그날 역마차로 떠났는데, 리케츠는 마부석에 올라 보틀리에게서 고삐를 넘겨받았다. 의사는 포트시의 식구들에게로 떠났고, 브래그는 동업자들에게로 도시로 갔으며, 일등 항해사는 램춘더호의 짐을 부리느라 바빴다. 조 씨는 사우샘프턴에서 몹시 외로워, 그날 조지 여관 주인을 붙잡고 함께 와인을 한 잔 들었으니, 바로 그 시각에 도빈 소령은 아버지 윌리엄 경의 식탁에 앉아 있었고, 거기서 그의 누이는 그가 조지 오스본 부인을 만나고 왔다는 것을 알아냈던 것이다(소령으로서는 거짓말을 하기가 도무지 불가능했으므로).\n\n조스는 세인트마틴스 레인에 어찌나 안락하게 자리 잡았던지, 그곳에서 더없이 편안하게 후카를 즐길 수 있었고, 마음 내키면 어찌나 기분 좋게 어슬렁어슬렁 극장으로 내려갈 수 있었던지, 만일 친구인 소령이 곁에서 떠밀지 않았더라면 아예 슬로터스에 눌러앉아 버렸을지도 모른다. 그 신사는 조스가 애밀리아와 아버지를 위해 집을 마련하겠다던 약속을 지킬 때까지 그 벵골 사람을 가만 놓아두지 않았다. 조스는 누구의 손아귀에서든 물러 터진 사람이었고, 도빈은 제 일만 빼고는 누구의 일에든 가장 적극적인 사람이었다. 그리하여 이 행정관은 마음씨 좋은 이 술책가의 천연덕스러운 수완에 손쉬운 먹잇감이 되어, 친구가 마땅하다고 여기는 것이라면 무엇이든 하고, 사고, 빌리고, 내놓을 준비가 되어 있었다. 세인트마틴스 레인 일대의 사내아이들이 거무튀튀한 낯짝을 거리에 내밀 때마다 짓궂게 놀려 대던 롤 주왑은, 캘커타로 돌아가는 동인도회사 선박 레이디 키클베리호에 태워 돌려보내졌으니, 윌리엄 도빈 경이 그 배에 지분을 가지고 있던 터였다. 그 전에 그는 조스의 유럽인 하인에게 카레와 필래프와 파이프 만드는 법을 가르쳐 두었다. 조스에게는 자기와 소령이 이웃한 롱에이커에 주문해 둔 맵시 있는 사륜마차의 제작을 감독하는 일이 더없는 즐거움이자 소일거리였다. 잘생긴 말 두 마리도 삯을 주고 빌려 와서, 조스는 그것을 몰고 위풍당당하게 공원을 누비거나 인도 시절 친구들을 찾아다녔다. 이런 나들이에는 애밀리아가 그의 곁에 함께 타는 일도 드물지 않았고, 그럴 때면 도빈 소령이 마차 뒷자리에 앉아 있는 모습도 볼 수 있었다. 또 어떤 때는 늙은 세들리와 그 딸이 마차를 이용했는데, 자주 자기 벗을 따라나서던 클랩 양은, 그 유명한 노란 숄을 두르고 마차에 앉아 진찰소의 젊은 신사에게 알아봄을 받는 것에 큰 기쁨을 느꼈다. 그녀가 지나갈 때면 그 젊은이의 얼굴이 흔히 창의 차양 너머로 보이곤 했던 것이다.\n\n조스가 브롬프턴에 처음 모습을 드러내고 얼마 지나지 않아, 세들리 일가가 지난 십 년의 삶을 보낸 그 초라한 오두막에서는 참으로 서글픈 한 장면이 펼쳐졌다. 어느 날 조스의 마차가(만들고 있던 사륜마차가 아니라 임시로 빌린 마차였다) 도착하여, 늙은 세들리와 그 딸을 태우고 떠나갔으니—다시는 돌아오지 않을 길이었다. 그 일을 두고 집주인 여자와 그 딸이 흘린 눈물은, 이 이야기를 거쳐 오는 동안 쏟아진 그 어떤 눈물 못지않게 진정 슬픔에서 우러난 눈물이었다. 그 오랜 사귐과 친분 속에서도 그들은 애밀리아가 모진 말 한마디 내뱉은 것을 떠올릴 수 없었다. 그녀는 그저 더없이 상냥하고 친절했으며, 클랩 부인이 성을 내며 집세를 독촉할 때조차도 늘 고마워하고 늘 온순했다. 그 다정한 사람이 영영 떠나가게 되자, 집주인 여자는 자기가 일찍이 그녀에게 거친 말을 했던 것을 두고 쓰라리게 자책했다. 그토록 오래 쓰이던 작은 방들을 세놓는다는 종이를 창에 풀로 붙여 두면서, 그 여자가 얼마나 울었던가! 다시는 그런 세입자들을 두지 못하리라는 것은 너무도 분명했다. 훗날의 삶이 이 우울한 예언이 옳았음을 증명해 주었으니, 클랩 부인은 인류가 변변치 못해진 데 대한 분풀이로, 세든 이들의 차통과 양 다리 고기에 더없이 사납게 분담금을 매겨 댔다. 그들 대부분은 잔소리하고 투덜거렸고, 더러는 돈을 내지 않았으며, 누구도 오래 머물지 않았다. 그 집주인 여자가 자기를 떠나 버린 그 오래되고 오래된 벗들을 그리워한 것도 무리는 아니었다.\n\n메리 양으로 말하자면, 애밀리아가 떠나는 데 대한 그 슬픔이 어떠했는지는 내가 굳이 그려 보려 들지 않으련다. 어릴 적부터 그녀는 날마다 애밀리아와 함께 지냈고, 그 다정하고 선량한 부인에게 어찌나 열렬히 마음을 붙였던지, 호화로운 사륜마차가 그녀를 화려한 곳으로 데려가려고 왔을 때, 그녀는 친구의 품에 안겨 까무러쳤다. 사실 그 친구 또한 마음씨 고운 처녀 못지않게 감정이 북받쳤던 것이다. 애밀리아는 그녀를 딸처럼 사랑했다. 십일 년 동안 그 처녀는 그녀의 변함없는 벗이자 길동무였다. 그러니 이별은 그녀에게 참으로 가슴 아픈 것이었다. 그러나 물론, 메리가 오스본 부인이 옮겨 가는 그 으리으리한 새집에 자주 와서 머물기로 약속이 되었거니와, 그 집에서는 메리가 이 초라한 오두막에서—클랩 양이 자기가 좋아하는 소설의 말투로 부르던 표현을 빌리자면—누렸던 만큼 결코 행복하지는 못하리라고 그녀는 확신했다.\n\n부디 그녀의 판단이 틀렸기를 바라자. 가엾은 에미가 그 초라한 오두막에서 행복했던 날들은 참으로 적었다. 그곳에서는 음울한 운명이 그녀를 짓눌렀다. 그녀는 그 집을 떠난 뒤로는 다시 그리로 돌아가는 것을, 또 자기를 횡포하게 굴던 그 집주인 여자와 마주하는 것을 결코 좋아하지 않았다. 그 여자는 기분이 언짢고 집세를 못 받았을 때면 그녀에게 폭군처럼 굴었고, 기분이 좋을 때면 그에 못지않게 역겨운 천박한 허물없음으로 그녀를 대했다. 에미가 형편이 폈을 때 보인 그 비굴함과 낯간지러운 아첨도 그 부인의 마음에 들기는 마찬가지였다. 그 여자는 새집을 온통 돌아다니며 감탄사를 쏟아 내고, 가구며 장식품 하나하나를 추켜세웠다. 그녀는 오스본 부인의 옷들을 만지작거리며 그 값을 가늠해 보았다. 저 사랑스러운 부인에게는 무엇이든 아무리 좋아도 지나치지 않다고, 그 여자는 맹세하고 또 다짐했다. 그러나 이제 자기에게 알랑거리는 이 천박한 아첨꾼에게서, 에미는 늘 자기를 숱하게 비참하게 만들었던 그 거친 폭군을 떠올렸다. 집세가 밀렸을 때 어쩔 수 없이 말미를 달라고 사정해야 했던 상대, 병든 어머니나 아버지를 위해 별미라도 사면 사치를 부린다고 호통치던 상대, 그녀가 비굴하게 구는 것을 보고 짓밟던 그 상대를 말이다.\n\n이 슬픔들에 대해서는 아무도 전해 듣지 못했으니, 그것은 우리의 가엾은 작은 부인이 살면서 짊어진 몫의 일부였다. 그녀는 그것을 아버지에게 숨겼는데, 그 아버지의 무분별함이야말로 그녀가 겪은 비참함의 많은 부분의 원인이었다. 그녀는 아버지가 저지른 일에 대한 비난을 죄다 떠안아야 했고, 사실 어찌나 더없이 온순하고 겸손했던지 천성적으로 희생양이 되도록 태어난 사람이었다.\n\n그녀가 그런 모진 대접을 더는 많이 겪지 않기를 바란다. 그리고 모든 슬픔에는 어떤 위안이 깃들어 있다고들 하니, 한마디 덧붙여 두자면, 가엾은 메리는 벗이 떠나간 뒤 발작 상태에 빠져 있다가 진찰소의 그 젊은이에게 진료를 받게 되었고, 그의 보살핌 아래 얼마 지나지 않아 기운을 되찾았다. 에미는 브롬프턴을 떠날 때, 집 안에 있던 가구를 하나도 빠짐없이 메리에게 다 주고, 다만 자기 그림들(침대 위에 걸린 두 점의 그림)과 피아노만은 가져갔다. 그 작고 오래된 피아노는 이제 구슬프게 댕그랑거리는 노년에 접어들어 있었으나, 그녀는 그것을 자기만의 까닭으로 사랑했다. 그녀가 그것을 처음 친 것은 어린아이 적이었고, 부모가 그것을 그녀에게 선물해 주었다. 그리고 독자가 기억할지 모르나, 그 뒤 아버지의 집이 풍비박산 나고 그 악기가 그 잔해 속에서 되찾아졌을 때, 그것은 그녀에게 다시 한번 주어진 것이었다.\n\n도빈 소령은—소령은 조스의 새집이 아주 멋지고 안락해야 한다고 우겼는데—그 집 단장을 감독하던 차에, 브롬프턴에서 손수레가 도착하여 그 마을을 떠나온 이들의 트렁크와 모자 상자들을, 그리고 그것들과 함께 그 오래된 피아노를 싣고 오자 무척 기뻐했다. 애밀리아는 그것을 자기 거실에 두려 했으니, 그곳은 이층에 있는 아담하고 작은 방으로 아버지 방과 붙어 있었고, 노신사가 저녁이면 흔히 앉아 있던 곳이었다.\n\n이윽고 일꾼들이 이 오래된 악기 상자를 메고 나타나 애밀리아가 그것을 앞서 말한 그 방에 들이라고 분부하자, 도빈은 무척 들떴다. \"이걸 간직하고 계셨다니 기쁩니다.\" 그가 자못 감상에 젖은 태도로 말했다. \"마음에 두지 않으셨을까 봐 걱정했었지요.\"\n\n\"저는 이걸 이 세상에 가진 그 무엇보다 소중히 여겨요.\" 애밀리아가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇습니까, 애밀리아?\" 소령이 외쳤다. 사실인즉, 그것을 직접 산 사람은 그 자신이었으나, 그는 그에 대해 한 번도 입을 연 적이 없었으므로, 에미가 그것을 다른 누군가 산 것으로 여기리라고는 그의 머릿속에 떠오른 적조차 없었다. 그래서 그는 으레 그녀가 그 선물이 자기에게서 온 것임을 알고 있으리라 짐작했던 것이다. \"그렇습니까, 애밀리아?\" 그가 말했다. 그리고 그 물음, 무엇보다 중대한 그 물음이 그의 입술에 떨려 나오려는 찰나, 에미가 대답했다—\n\n\"어찌 그러지 않겠어요?—그이가 제게 준 것인데요?\"\n\n\"저는 몰랐습니다.\" 가엾은 늙은 도브가 말했고, 그의 얼굴빛이 어두워졌다.\n\n에미는 그때는 그 일을 알아차리지 못했고, 정직한 도빈의 얼굴에 떠오른 그 몹시 침울한 표정도 곧바로 마음에 두지 않았으나, 나중에 가서 그것을 떠올렸다. 그리고 그때, 형언할 수 없는 아픔과 또한 굴욕감과 함께, 그 피아노를 준 사람이 자기가 여겨 왔던 조지가 아니라 윌리엄이었다는 사실이 그녀의 가슴을 쳤다. 그것은 조지의 선물이 아니었다. 그녀가 생각하기에 자기가 연인에게서 받은 유일한 선물—다른 그 무엇보다 애지중지하던 것—가장 소중한 유품이자 보물이었던 것인데. 그녀는 그것을 향해 조지 이야기를 했고, 그것으로 그가 좋아하던 곡들을 쳤으며, 긴 저녁 시간을 앉아서 자기의 서툰 솜씨껏 그 건반으로 구슬픈 화음을 짚으며, 말없이 그 위에 눈물을 흘리곤 했었다. 그것은 조지의 유품이 아니었다. 이제 그것은 아무 값어치도 없었다. 다음번에 늙은 세들리가 한 곡 쳐 보라고 청하자, 그녀는 음이 형편없이 어긋났다느니, 머리가 아프다느니, 칠 수가 없다느니 했다.\n\n이윽고 그녀는 늘 그러던 버릇대로, 자기의 토라짐과 배은망덕을 꾸짖고, 그 피아노를 두고 정직한 윌리엄에게—입 밖에 내지는 않았으나 마음으로 느꼈던—그 푸대접을 보상하기로 마음먹었다. 며칠 뒤, 조스가 저녁을 먹고 더없이 편안히 잠들어 버린 응접실에 그들이 앉아 있을 때, 애밀리아가 자못 떨리는 목소리로 도빈 소령에게 말했다—\n\n\"한 가지 용서를 빌 일이 있어요.\"\n\n\"무엇 말입니까?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"저—저 작은 사각 피아노 말이에요. 아주아주 오래전, 제가 결혼하기 전에 그걸 주셨을 때 저는 한 번도 고맙다는 말을 못 드렸어요. 다른 사람이 준 거라고 여겼거든요. 고마워요, 윌리엄.\" 그녀가 손을 내밀었으나, 그 가엾은 작은 부인의 가슴은 피 흘리고 있었다. 그리고 그 두 눈으로 말하면, 물론 또 제 할 일을 하고 있었다.\n\n그러나 윌리엄은 더는 참을 수가 없었다. \"애밀리아, 애밀리아.\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 당신을 위해 그것을 산 게 맞습니다. 그때도 지금처럼 당신을 사랑했어요. 말해야겠습니다. 나는 당신을 처음 본 그 순간부터 사랑했던 것 같습니다. 조지가 자기와 약혼한 애밀리아를 보여 주겠다고 나를 당신 집으로 데려갔을 때 말입니다. 당신은 아직 어린 처녀였고, 흰 옷을 입고 큼직한 곱슬머리를 늘어뜨린 채, 노래를 부르며 내려왔지요—기억하세요?—그리고 우리는 복스홀에 갔었지요. 그 뒤로 나는 이 세상에서 단 한 여인만을 생각해 왔으니, 그게 바로 당신이었습니다. 지난 십이 년 동안 하루도, 당신을 생각하지 않고 지나간 시간은 없었던 것 같습니다. 인도로 떠나기 전에 이 말을 하려고 당신을 찾아갔었지만, 당신은 마음에 두지 않았고, 나는 차마 입을 뗄 용기가 없었지요. 당신은 내가 머물든 떠나든 아랑곳하지 않았으니까요.\"\n\n\"제가 참 배은망덕했어요.\" 애밀리아가 말했다.\n\n\"아니요, 그저 무심했을 뿐입니다.\" 도빈이 자포자기한 듯 말을 이었다. \"내게는 여자가 달리 마음먹게 만들 만한 구석이 없으니까요. 지금 당신이 무엇을 느끼고 있는지 압니다. 그 피아노를 두고 알게 된 사실에, 그게 조지가 아니라 나에게서 온 것이라는 데에 마음이 상해 있는 거지요. 내가 깜빡했지, 그렇지 않았다면 결코 그런 식으로 그 이야기를 꺼내지 않았을 겁니다. 잠시나마 어리석은 사람이 되어, 여러 해의 한결같음과 헌신이 당신의 마음을 움직였을지도 모른다고 여긴 것에 대해 오히려 내가 용서를 빌어야겠군요.\"\n\n\"이제 잔인하신 건 당신이에요.\" 애밀리아가 다소 발끈하여 말했다. \"조지는 이 땅에서도 하늘에서도 제 남편이에요. 어찌 제가 그이 아닌 다른 누구를 사랑할 수 있겠어요? 저는 당신이 저를 처음 보았던 그때와 똑같이 지금도 그이의 사람이에요, 윌리엄. 당신이 얼마나 선량하고 너그러운 사람인지 제게 일러 준 것도 그이였고, 당신을 오라비처럼 사랑하라고 가르쳐 준 것도 그이였어요. 당신은 저와 제 아이에게 모든 것이 되어 주지 않았나요? 우리의 가장 소중하고, 가장 진실하고, 가장 다정한 벗이자 보호자가 아니었나요? 당신이 몇 달만 더 일찍 와 주었더라면 어쩌면 제게 그—그 끔찍한 이별을 면하게 해 주었을지도 모르는데. 아, 그것은 저를 거의 죽일 뻔했어요, 윌리엄—하지만 당신은 오지 않았어요, 제가 와 달라고 그토록 바라고 기도했는데도. 그리고 그들은 그 애마저 제게서 데려가 버렸어요. 그 애는 고귀한 아이지요, 윌리엄? 부디 변함없이 그 애의, 그리고 저의 벗으로 남아 주세요\"—여기서 그녀의 목소리가 메었고, 그녀는 그의 어깨에 얼굴을 묻었다.\n\n소령은 두 팔로 그녀를 감싸, 마치 그녀가 어린아이라도 되는 양 자기 품에 끌어안고, 그 머리에 입을 맞추었다. \"변치 않겠습니다, 사랑하는 애밀리아.\" 그가 말했다. \"당신의 사랑보다 더 많은 것은 바라지 않습니다. 그 이상은 바라지 않는 편이 차라리 나을 것 같습니다. 그저 당신 곁에 머물며 자주 당신을 볼 수 있게만 해 주세요.\"\n\n\"네, 자주요.\" 애밀리아가 말했다. 그리하여 윌리엄은 마음껏 바라보고 마음껏 그리워해도 좋게 되었으니—돈 한 푼 없는 가엾은 학교 소년이 파이 장수 쟁반에 담긴 것들을 두고 한숨짓는 것이나 다를 바 없는 일이었다.\n\n이제 행운이 애밀리아에게 미소 짓기 시작했다. 우리는 그녀를 여태껏 기어다니던 그 비천한 세계에서 끌어내어 점잖은 사교계로 안내하게 되어 기쁘다. 우리의 또 다른 여자 친구 베키 부인이 모습을 드러냈던 그곳만큼 으리으리하고 세련된 곳은 아니지만, 그래도 품위와 유행을 자처하기에 결코 손색없는 그런 곳이었다. 조스의 친구들은 모두 인도의 세 관구 출신이었고, 그의 새집은 모이라 플레이스를 중심으로 한 그 안락한 영국계 인도인 지구에 있었다. 민토 광장, 그레이트 클라이브 거리, 워런 거리, 헤이스팅스 거리, 옥털로니 플레이스, 플래시 광장, 어세이 테라스(\"가든\"이라는 운치 있는 말이, 앞쪽에 아스팔트 테라스를 깐 회벽칠한 집들에까지 붙은 것은 1827년 무렵엔 아직 아니었다)—이 은퇴한 인도 귀족들의 점잖은 거처들을, 그리고 한마디로 웨넘 씨가 블랙홀이라 부르는 그 동네를 누가 모르겠는가? 조스의 사회적 지위는 모이라 플레이스에 집을 가질 만큼 대단하지는 않았다. 그곳에는 은퇴한 참사회 의원이나 인도 상사의 동업자들(아내 앞으로 십만 파운드를 떼어 놓은 뒤 파산하고, 연 사천 파운드 수입에 시골집 하나를 두고 상대적인 빈곤 속으로 은퇴하는 부류)밖에 살 수 없었기 때문이다. 그는 길레스피 거리에 자리한, 이류나 삼류쯤 되는 안락한 집 한 채를 빌렸고, 카펫과 값비싼 거울들과 세든스가 만든 보기 좋고 알맞게 설계된 가구들을 스케이프 씨의 재산 관리인들에게서 사들였다. 스케이프 씨로 말하자면, 얼마 전 캘커타의 대상사인 포글, 페이크 앤드 크랙스맨 상회의 동업자로 받아들여진 사람이었는데, 가엾은 스케이프는 거기에 자신의 길고 떳떳한 평생의 벌이인 칠만 파운드를 쏟아부었고, 서식스의 어느 으리으리한 사유지로 은퇴한 페이크의 자리를 대신한 것이었다(포글 집안은 오래전에 그 상사에서 손을 뗐고, 호러스 포글 경은 곧 밴대너 남작으로 작위를 받아 귀족이 될 참이다)—그러니까 내 말은, 포글 앤드 페이크라는 그 대규모 대리상회의 동업자로 받아들여졌는데, 그 상사가 백만 파운드의 빚을 지고 무너지며 인도 대중의 절반을 비참과 파멸 속으로 몰아넣기 꼭 이 년 전의 일이었다.\n\n스케이프는 파산하고, 정직하고, 예순다섯의 나이에 상심에 잠긴 채 상사의 일을 정리하러 캘커타로 떠났다. 월터 스케이프는 이튼에서 자퇴하고 어느 상인의 집에 들어갔다. 플로런스 스케이프와 패니 스케이프, 그리고 그들의 어머니는 불로뉴로 스러져 갔고, 다시는 그들의 소식을 듣지 못할 것이다. 간단히 말해, 조스가 끼어들어 그들의 카펫과 찬장을 사들였고, 그들의 다정하고 잘생긴 얼굴을 비추던 거울 앞에서 제 모습을 흐뭇하게 들여다본 것이다. 스케이프네 상인들은 모두 떳떳이 대금을 받은 터라 명함을 두고 갔고, 새 살림에 물건을 대고 싶어 안달이었다. 스케이프네 만찬 때 시중들던 흰 조끼 차림의 덩치 큰 사내들, 즉 사적인 신분으로는 채소 장수요 은행 짐꾼이요 우유 장수인 자들이 저마다 주소를 남기고 집사의 환심을 사려 들었다. 마지막 세 가문의 굴뚝을 청소해 온 굴뚝 청소부 처미 씨는, 집사와 그 아래 소년의 비위를 맞추려 애썼는데, 그 소년의 임무란 애밀리아 부인이 바깥나들이를 할 때면 그분을 호위하기 위해 단추가 줄줄이 달리고 바짓가랑이에 줄무늬가 들어간 옷을 입고 나서는 것이었다.\n\n소박한 살림이었다. 집사는 조스의 시종까지 겸했고, 주인의 와인을 마땅히 아낄 줄 아는, 작은 집안의 집사가 으레 취하는 정도 이상으로는 결코 취하지 않았다. 에미에게는 윌리엄 도빈 경의 교외 영지에서 자란 하녀 하나가 붙여졌는데, 착한 처녀였다. 그 친절함과 겸손함이 오즈번 부인의 경계심을 풀어 주었으니, 부인은 처음엔 자기 시중을 들어 줄 하인을 둔다는 생각에 겁부터 났고, 하인을 어떻게 부려야 하는지 도무지 몰랐으며, 늘 하인들에게 더없이 공손하고 정중하게 말을 건넸던 것이다. 그러나 이 하녀는 세들리 노인의 시중을 솜씨 좋게 드는 일로 집안에 큰 보탬이 되었다. 노인은 집의 자기 구역에만 거의 틀어박혀 지냈고, 그곳에서 벌어지는 그 어떤 흥겨운 일에도 끼지 않았다.\n\n수많은 사람이 오즈번 부인을 보러 찾아왔다. 도빈 부인과 딸들은 그녀의 달라진 운수에 기뻐하며 그녀를 방문했다. 러셀 광장의 오즈번 양은 리즈 가문의 문장을 화려하게 새긴 마부석 덮개가 번쩍이는 으리으리한 사륜마차를 타고 왔다. 조스는 어마어마한 부자라고 소문이 났다. 오즈번 노인은 조지가 제 재산뿐 아니라 외삼촌의 재산까지 물려받는 데 이의가 없었다. \"젠장, 우리가 그 녀석을 어엿한 사내로 만들어 줘야지.\" 그가 말했다. \"내 죽기 전에 그 녀석이 의회에 들어가는 걸 봐야겠어. 네가 가서 그 애 어미를 만나도 좋다, 오 양아. 나는 그 여자를 두 번 다시 안 볼 테지만 말이다.\" 그리하여 오즈번 양이 찾아왔다. 짐작하다시피 에미는 그녀를 만나 더없이 기뻤으니, 그로써 조지에게 한 발짝 더 가까워졌기 때문이다. 그 어린 친구는 전보다 훨씬 자주 어머니를 보러 오도록 허락받았다. 그는 일주일에 한두 번씩 길레스피 거리에서 저녁을 먹었고, 러셀 광장에서 그러듯 거기서도 하인들과 친척들을 윽박질렀다.\n\n그러나 그는 도빈 소령에게만은 늘 깍듯했고, 그 신사가 자리에 있을 때면 처신도 한결 얌전해졌다. 그는 영리한 소년이었고 소령을 두려워했다. 조지는 친구의 소박함과 좋은 성품, 조용히 나누어 주는 해박한 학식, 진실과 정의를 두루 사랑하는 마음을 흠모하지 않을 수 없었다. 그는 여태껏 살아오며 그런 사람을 만난 적이 없었고, 본능적으로 신사를 좋아했던 것이다. 그는 대부 곁에 다정하게 붙어 다녔고, 공원을 거닐며 도빈의 이야기를 듣는 것을 더없는 낙으로 삼았다. 윌리엄은 조지에게 그의 아버지 이야기며 인도와 워털루 이야기를 들려주었는데, 자기 자신에 관한 것만 빼고 무엇이든 다 들려주었다. 조지가 유난히 건방지고 우쭐댈 때면 소령은 그를 두고 농담을 던지곤 했는데, 오즈번 부인은 그것을 몹시 야박하다고 여겼다. 어느 날, 그를 데리고 연극을 보러 갔는데 소년이 일반석이 천박하다며 들어가기를 마다하자, 소령은 그를 특별석으로 데려가 거기 남겨 두고 자기는 일반석으로 내려갔다. 그가 거기 앉은 지 얼마 안 되어, 누군가 자기 팔 밑으로 팔을 끼우고 새끼염소 가죽 장갑을 낀 멋쟁이 같은 작은 손이 제 팔을 꼭 쥐는 것을 느꼈다. 조지가 제 행동이 우스꽝스러움을 깨닫고 위층 자리에서 내려온 것이었다. 뉘우치는 그 어린 탕아를 바라보는 도빈 노인의 얼굴과 두 눈에는 인자하고 부드러운 웃음이 환히 피어올랐다. 그는 애밀리아에게 속한 모든 것을 사랑하듯 그 소년을 사랑했다. 조지의 이 착한 행실에 관한 이야기를 들었을 때 그녀가 얼마나 기뻐했던가! 그녀의 두 눈이 그 어느 때보다 다정하게 도빈을 바라보았다. 그렇게 그를 바라본 뒤 그녀가 얼굴을 붉혔다고, 그는 생각했다.\n\n조지는 어머니에게 소령을 칭찬하는 데 도무지 싫증을 내지 않았다. \"난 그 아저씨가 좋아요, 엄마. 워낙 아는 게 많거든요. 그리고 늘 으스대면서 그렇게 긴 단어만 써 대는 늙은 빌 선생하곤 영 딴판이에요, 아시겠어요? 학교 애들은 그 선생을 '꼬랑지'라고 불러요. 내가 붙여 준 별명인데, 기막히지 않아요? 그런데 도브 아저씨는 라틴어를 영어처럼 술술 읽고, 프랑스어도 그렇고, 그런 걸 다 한다니까요. 그리고 우리가 같이 나가면 우리 아빠 이야기를 들려주지, 자기 얘긴 절대 안 해요. 그래도 난 할아버지 댁에서 버클러 대령님이 그러는 걸 들었는걸요. 도브 아저씨가 군대에서 가장 용감한 장교 중 하나였고 무척이나 큰 공을 세웠다고요. 할아버지는 깜짝 놀라서 그러셨어요. '그 녀석이! 아니, 난 그자가 거위 한 마리 앞에서도 찍소리 못 낼 줄 알았다'고요. 하지만 난 아저씨가 그럴 수 있다는 걸 알아요, 그렇죠 엄마?\"\n\n에미는 웃었다. 소령이 그 정도는 충분히 할 수 있으리라 생각한 것이다.\n\n조지와 소령 사이에 진실한 호감이 있었던 것이라면, 그 소년과 외삼촌 사이에는 그다지 큰 정이 없었다는 것을 인정해야겠다. 조지는 양 볼을 부풀리고 두 손을 조끼 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채 \"맙소사, 설마 그럴 리가\"라고 말하는 버릇이 있었는데, 그것이 조스 노인의 투를 어찌나 똑같이 흉내 낸 것인지 도저히 웃음을 참을 수가 없었다. 식탁에 없는 무언가를 달라고 하면서 그 소년이 그런 표정을 짓고 그 애용하는 말투를 쓰면, 하인들은 만찬 자리에서 그만 웃음을 터뜨리고 마는 것이었다. 도빈조차 소년의 흉내에 별안간 웃음을 한바탕 터뜨리곤 했다. 조지가 외삼촌의 면전에서 그를 흉내 내지 않은 것은 오로지 도빈의 꾸지람과 애밀리아의 겁에 질린 간청 덕분이었으니, 그 작은 말썽꾼은 그제야 마지못해 그만두는 것이었다. 그리고 이 점잖은 문관은 그 소년이 자기를 멍청이로 여기고 우스갯거리로 삼으려 든다는 어렴풋한 의식에 사로잡혀, 어린 조지 도련님 앞에서는 몹시 주눅이 들었고, 당연히 그만큼 곱절로 거드름을 피우며 위엄을 부리곤 했다. 그 젊은 도련님이 어머니와 저녁을 먹으러 길레스피 거리에 오기로 했다는 전갈이 오면, 조스 씨는 으레 클럽에 약속이 있다는 것을 발견하곤 했다. 아마 그의 부재를 그리 서운해한 이는 아무도 없었을 것이다. 그런 날이면 세들리 씨는 으레 위층의 은신처에서 나오도록 권유받았고, 그리하여 단출한 가족 모임이 이루어졌는데, 도빈 소령은 거의 빠짐없이 그 일원이 되었다. 그는 이 집안의 벗이었으니—세들리 노인의 벗이요, 에미의 벗이요, 조지의 벗이요, 조스의 자문이자 조언자였다. \"우리가 그분을 보는 걸로 치면 마드라스에 가 계신 거나 마찬가지지.\" 캠버웰에서 앤 도빈 양이 말했다. 아! 앤 양, 소령이 결혼하고 싶어 한 사람이 당신은 아니라는 생각이 들지 않던가요?\n\n그리하여 조지프 세들리는 그 지체 높은 인물에게 걸맞은, 위엄 있는 무위도식의 나날을 보냈다. 물론 그가 가장 먼저 한 일은 오리엔탈 클럽의 회원이 되는 것이었으니, 그곳에서 그는 같은 인도 출신 동료들과 어울려 오전을 보냈고, 그곳에서 저녁을 먹거나 그곳에서 사람들을 집으로 데려와 저녁을 대접했다.\n\n애밀리아는 이 신사들과 그 부인들을 맞이하고 대접해야 했다. 그녀는 이들에게서 듣게 되었다. 스미스가 머지않아 참사회 의원이 되리라는 것, 존스가 얼마나 많은 락의 돈을 챙겨 왔는지, 런던의 톰슨 상회가 봄베이의 톰슨, 키봅지 앤드 코 상회가 발행한 어음을 부도 처리했다는 것, 그리고 캘커타 상회 또한 무너질 것이라 여겨진다는 것, 적어도 점잖게 말해도 너무나 경솔했던 브라운 부인(아메드누거 비정규군 브라운의 아내)의 처신, 즉 친위대의 젊은 스왱키와 밤늦도록 갑판에 앉아 있었고 케이프에서 함께 말을 타고 나갔다가 길을 잃었다는 것, 하디먼 부인이 시골 부목사인 펠릭스 래비츠 목사의 딸들인 자기 자매 열셋을 데려와 그중 열하나를 시집보냈고 일곱은 관직 높은 자리에 시집보냈다는 것, 혼비가 아내가 유럽에 눌러앉으려 해서 길길이 날뛰었다는 것, 트로터가 우메라푸라의 징세관으로 임명되었다는 것을 말이다. 이런저런 비슷한 이야기들이 사방의 성대한 만찬에서 오갔다. 다들 똑같은 대화를 나누었고, 똑같은 은접시에, 똑같은 양고기 안심 구이며 삶은 칠면조며 앙트레가 나왔다. 후식이 끝나고 얼마 지나지 않아 정치 이야기가 시작되면, 부인들은 위층으로 물러가 자기네 병치레와 아이들 이야기를 나누었다.\n\n이름만 바꾸면 다 매한가지인 것이다. 변호사 부인들은 순회재판 이야기를 하지 않던가? 군인 부인들은 연대 이야기를 수다 떨지 않던가? 목사 부인들은 주일학교며 누가 누구 일을 대신 맡는지를 논하지 않던가? 가장 지체 높은 귀부인들조차 자기들이 속한 그 작은 패거리 이야기를 하지 않던가? 그런데 어찌하여 우리 인도 친구들만은 자기네 이야기를 하면 안 된단 말인가?—다만 곁에 앉아 들어야 하는 신세가 더러 되는 문외한에게는 그것이 따분한 일임을 나도 인정한다.\n\n머지않아 에미는 방문 명부를 갖추고 정기적으로 마차를 타고 돌아다니며 사람들을 찾았다. 블러디어 부인(벵골군 소장 로저 블러디어 경, 바스 훈작사의 아내), 허프 부인(봄베이군의 G. 허프 경의 아내), 파이스 부인(중역 파이스의 부인) 등을 방문한 것이다. 우리는 인생의 변화에 익숙해지는 데 오래 걸리지 않는 법이다. 그 마차는 날마다 길레스피 거리로 돌아왔고, 그 단추 달린 소년은 에미와 조스의 명함을 들고 마부석에서 폴짝폴짝 오르내렸으며, 정해진 시각이면 에미는 마차를 타고 클럽으로 조스를 데리러 가 그에게 바람을 쐬어 주었다. 또는 세들리 노인을 마차에 태우고 리젠트 파크를 한 바퀴 돌게 해 드렸다. 하녀와 사륜마차, 방문 명부와 단추 달린 사동은, 곧 브롬프턴에서의 소박한 일상만큼이나 애밀리아에게 친숙한 것이 되었다. 그녀는 이쪽에 적응했듯 저쪽에도 적응했다. 만약 운명이 그녀를 공작부인으로 점지했더라면, 그녀는 그 소임마저 능히 해냈을 것이다. 조스의 여성 사교계에서 그녀는 제법 호감 가는 젊은 여자라는 평을 들었다—대단할 건 없지만 호감이 가고, 뭐 그런 부류라는 것이었다.\n\n남자들은 으레 그렇듯 그녀의 꾸밈없는 친절함과 소박하고 세련된 처신을 좋아했다. 휴가를 받아 본국에 돌아온 멋쟁이 인도 청년들—실로 대단한 멋쟁이들이었으니—목줄 같은 장식에 콧수염을 기르고, 요란한 이륜마차를 몰며, 극장의 기둥서방 노릇을 하고, 웨스트엔드의 호텔에 묵는 그자들조차 오즈번 부인을 흠모하여, 공원에서 그녀의 마차에 인사하기를 좋아했고, 오전에 그녀를 방문하는 영예를 누리게 되기를 좋아했다. 그 위험한 청년이자 지금 휴가를 받아 와 있는 인도군 전체에서 가장 멋쟁이인 친위대의 스왱키 본인도, 어느 날 도빈 소령에게 애밀리아와 단둘이 마주 앉아 있는 모습을 들켰는데, 그는 그녀에게 멧돼지 사냥의 묘미를 무척이나 유쾌하고 능변으로 늘어놓고 있었다. 그러고는 나중에 이렇게 말했다. 이 집에 늘 빌붙어 있는 빌어먹을 국왕 직속 장교 하나가 있는데—길쭉하고 비쩍 마르고 묘하게 생긴 늙수그레한 자라며—그래도 만만찮은 자라서, 말솜씨로는 사람을 영 무색하게 만든다는 것이었다.\n\n소령이 개인적인 허영심을 조금만 더 가졌더라면, 저 매혹적인 벵골 대위처럼 위험한 멋쟁이 청년을 질투했을 것이다. 그러나 도빈은 애밀리아에 대해 추호의 의심도 품기에는 너무나 소박하고 너그러운 천성이었다. 그는 젊은 남자들이 그녀에게 경의를 표하고 다른 이들이 그녀를 흠모하는 것이 기뻤다. 그녀가 여인이 된 이래로 거의 줄곧, 박해받고 푸대접받아 오지 않았던가? 그는 친절함이 그녀의 좋은 자질들을 끌어내고, 형편이 나아짐에 따라 그녀의 기운이 은은히 살아나는 것을 보는 게 흐뭇했다. 그녀를 알아주는 사람은 누구든 소령의 안목을 칭찬하는 셈이었다—말하자면, 사랑의 미혹에 빠진 사내에게 안목이 있다고 할 수 있다면 말이다.\n\n조스가 궁정 알현을 하러 간 뒤로—군주의 충성스러운 신하로서 그가 그리했으리라는 것은 우리도 익히 짐작할 수 있는데(궁정 정장을 완전히 차려입고 클럽에 나타났고, 도빈은 무척 낡고 초라한 옛 군복 차림으로 그를 데리러 왔다)—늘 확고한 왕당파요 조지 4세의 숭배자였던 그는, 이제 어마어마한 토리당원이자 국가의 기둥이 되어, 애밀리아도 궁정 접견식에 데려가야 한다고 했다. 그는 어쩐 일인지, 자신이 공공의 안녕을 지키는 데 한몫 끼어 있으며, 조스 세들리와 그 가족이 세인트제임스 궁에 나타나 군주를 옹위하지 않으면 그분이 흡족해하지 못하리라고 스스로 믿어 버린 것이었다.\n\n에미는 웃었다. \"집안 다이아몬드라도 걸고 갈까요, 조스 오빠?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 좀 사 줄 수 있게 해 주면 좋으련만.\" 소령은 생각했다. \"당신한테 과분한 물건이 어디 있을지 어디 한번 보고 싶군.\"\n\n칠십일장.\n\n* 칠십일장: 원문의 챕터 번호는 LXI(61)이나, 본문 첫머리 제목은 그대로 옮긴다.\n세상의 대부분 집에서 일어나는 그런 일이 닥쳐, 조스 세들리 씨 가족이 빠져 있던 점잖은 즐거움과 엄숙한 흥겨움의 순환이 중단되는 날이 찾아왔다. 응접실 층에서 침실 층으로 집 안 계단을 올라가다 보면, 바로 눈앞 벽에 자그마한 아치가 나 있는 것을 눈여겨본 적이 있을 것이다. 그것은 이 층에서 (대개 아이 방과 하인 방이 있는) 삼 층으로 통하는 계단에 빛을 들이는 동시에, 또 다른 쓸모를 위해 있는 것이기도 한데, 그 쓸모란 장의사 일꾼들이 일러 줄 만한 것이다. 그들은 그 아치 위에 관을 받쳐 두거나, 그 사이로 관을 통과시키는데, 검은 방주 안에서 잠든 차디찬 입주자를 행여 점잖지 못하게 흔들어 깨우지 않으려는 것이다.\n\n런던 집의 그 이 층 아치, 계단 통로의 위아래를 굽어보며, 사는 이들이 오가는 중심 통로를 한눈에 거느린 그 아치. 날이 밝기도 전에 요리사가 부엌에서 냄비와 솥을 닦으려고 그 아래로 살그머니 내려가고, 젊은 주인이 현관에 장화를 벗어 두고 클럽에서의 흥겨운 밤을 보낸 뒤 동틀 무렵 몰래 들어와 그 위로 슬그머니 올라가며, 아가씨가 산뜻한 리본을 두르고 모슬린 자락을 펼치며 눈부시고 아름답게, 정복과 무도회를 채비하고 사락사락 그 아래로 내려오고, 토미 도련님이 위험도 계단도 아랑곳 않고 난간을 운반 수단 삼아 그 아래로 미끄러져 내려오며, 의사 선생이 그 매력적인 환자가 이제 아래층으로 내려가도 좋다고 선언한 날, 한 걸음 한 걸음 묵직하게 내딛는 건장한 남편의 두 팔에 안겨 산후 간호사를 거느린 채 어머니가 미소 지으며 사랑스레 그 아래로 옮겨지고, 존이 지지직거리는 수지 양초를 들고 하품을 하며 그 위로 자러 슬그머니 올라갔다가 해 뜨기 전에 복도에서 자기를 기다리는 장화를 거두어 가는 그 통로. 그 위로, 혹은 그 아래로 갓난아기가 안겨 오르내리고, 노인이 부축을 받으며, 손님이 무도회로 인도되고, 목사가 세례식을 위해 걸음을 옮기고, 의사가 병실로, 장의사 일꾼들이 위층으로 향하는 그 계단. 그것은 — 그 아치와 계단은 — 그대가 굳이 헤아려 보고자 한다면, 계단참에 앉아 통로의 위아래를 바라본다면, 삶과 죽음과 허영의 얼마나 절절한 기념물이란 말인가! 알록달록한 옷을 입은 나의 벗이여, 그 의사 선생은 마지막으로 그곳에서 우리에게도 올라올 것이다. 간호사가 휘장 사이로 들여다봐도 그대는 아랑곳하지 않을 테고, 그러면 그녀는 잠시 창문을 활짝 열어젖혀 공기를 들일 것이다. 그런 다음 사람들은 집의 정면 차양을 모두 끌어내리고 뒷방에서 지낼 것이며, 그러고는 변호사며 검은 옷을 입은 다른 사람들 따위를 부르러 보낼 것이다. 그대의 희극도 나의 희극도 그때면 다 끝났을 테고, 우리는 나팔 소리와 환호와 거드름 피우는 몸짓에서, 오, 얼마나 멀리, 치워져 있을 것인가. 우리가 양반붙이라면 사람들은 우리가 살던 거처 위에 도금한 천사상과 \"천국에는 안식이 있도다\"라고 적힌 문구를 곁들인 상장(喪章)을 걸어 둘 것이다. 그대의 아들은 집을 새로 단장하거나, 어쩌면 세를 놓고 더 현대적인 동네로 옮겨 갈 것이며, 그대의 이름은 이듬해 그대가 속한 클럽의 명부에서 \"사망한 회원\" 가운데 오를 것이다. 그대가 아무리 애도를 받는다 한들, 그대의 미망인은 자기 상복이 맵시 있게 지어지기를 바랄 것이고, 요리사는 만찬에 관해 묻고자 사람을 보내거나 올라올 것이며, 살아남은 자는 머잖아 벽난로 위에 걸린 그대의 초상화를 견딜 만하게 바라보게 될 것이다. 그 초상화는 이윽고 그 영예로운 자리에서 끌어내려져, 이제 군림하는 아들의 초상화에 자리를 내어 줄 것이다.\n\n죽은 이들 가운데 누가 가장 애틋하고 격렬하게 애도받는가? 살아남은 이들을 가장 덜 사랑한 이들이라고, 나는 생각한다. 자식의 죽음은 격정적인 슬픔과 미친 듯한 눈물을 자아내는데, 형제 같은 독자여, 그대의 임종은 결코 그런 것을 불러일으키지 못할 것이다. 그대를 거의 알지도 못했고, 그대와 일주일만 떨어져 있어도 그대를 잊었을 갓난아기의 죽음은, 그대의 가장 가까운 벗이나 맏아들 — 자기 자식까지 거느린, 그대와 같은 어엿한 성인 — 을 잃는 것보다 더 그대를 무너뜨릴 것이다. 우리는 유다와 시므온에게는 모질고 준엄할지 모르나, 우리의 사랑과 연민은 막내인 어린 베냐민에게로 솟구쳐 흐른다. 그리고 그대가 늙었다면 — 이 글을 읽는 누군가는 이미 늙고 부유하거나, 늙고 가난하거나, 혹은 장차 그리될지도 모르니 — 그대는 어느 날 혼자 이런 생각을 할지도 모른다. \"내 주위의 이 사람들은 참 착하지만, 내가 떠나도 그리 깊이 슬퍼하지는 않을 게야. 나는 무척 부유하고, 저들은 내 유산을 원하지. 아니면 내가 무척 가난해서, 저들은 나를 부양하는 데 지쳤거나.\"\n\n세들리 부인의 죽음에 대한 애도 기간이 막 끝나고, 조스가 미처 상복을 벗어 던지고 그가 그토록 좋아하던 화려한 조끼 차림으로 나설 새도 없을 무렵, 세들리 씨 곁의 사람들에게는 또 다른 사건이 임박했다는 것이, 그리고 노인이 자기보다 먼저 떠난 아내를 찾아 그 어두운 나라로 가려 한다는 것이 분명해졌다. \"아버님 건강 상태가,\" 조스 세들리는 클럽에서 엄숙하게 말하곤 했다. \"이번 철에는 도무지 큰 연회를 열 수가 없다네. 하지만 처트니, 이 사람아, 자네가 여섯 시 반에 조용히 와서 옛 친구 한둘과 함께 소박한 저녁을 들어 준다면, 나야 언제든 반갑게 맞이하지.\" 그리하여 조스와 그의 지인들은 위층에서 노인의 생명의 모래시계가 다해 가는 동안, 자기들끼리 말없이 만찬을 들고 클라레를 마셨다. 벨벳처럼 발소리 없는 집사가 그들에게 포도주를 가져다주었고, 그들은 만찬 뒤 휘스트 한 판으로 자리를 가다듬었는데, 거기에는 이따금 도빈 소령이 와서 한 손 끼기도 했다. 그리고 오스본 부인도 이따금 내려왔으니, 위층의 그녀 환자가 잠자리에 들고 노년의 베개를 찾아드는, 그 가볍게 뒤척이는 선잠 가운데 하나에 빠져들었을 때였다.\n\n노인은 이 병중에 딸에게 매달렸다. 거의 다른 어떤 손으로도 미음이며 약을 받으려 들지 않았다. 그를 돌보는 일이 거의 그녀 삶의 유일한 일이 되었다. 그녀의 침상은 그의 방으로 통하는 문 바로 곁에 놓였고, 그녀는 그 까탈스러운 병자의 침상에서 들려오는 아주 작은 소리나 기척에도 깨어났다. 하지만 노인을 위해 공정히 말하자면, 그는 친절하고 빈틈없는 자기 간병인을 깨우고 싶지 않아, 말없이 꼼짝도 않고 여러 시간 깨어 누워 있곤 했다.\n\n그는 이제, 어쩌면 그녀의 어린 시절 이래로 그 어느 때보다 더 애틋하게 딸을 사랑했다. 다정한 시중과 자식 된 자의 갸륵한 도리를 다하는 데서, 이 소박한 사람은 그 무엇보다 빛났다. \"햇살처럼 소리 없이 방으로 걸어 들어오는군.\" 도빈 씨는 그녀가 아버지의 방을 드나드는 모습을, 우아하고 소리 없이 이리저리 움직일 때 그 얼굴을 환히 밝히는 명랑한 다정함을 바라보며 그렇게 생각했다. 여인들이 제 아이를 어르고 굽어볼 때나 병실에서 분주할 때, 그 얼굴에 어린 그 달콤하고 천사 같은 사랑과 연민의 광채를 보지 못한 이가 어디 있겠는가?\n\n이렇게 하여 몇 해 묵은 은밀한 반목이 치유되었으니, 암묵적인 화해와 더불어서였다. 이 마지막 시간에, 그녀의 사랑과 선량함에 마음이 움직여, 노인은 그녀에게 품었던 온갖 서운함과, 그가 아내와 더불어 숱한 긴 밤을 두고 따지던 잘못들을 모두 잊었다. 그녀가 어떻게 자기 아들을 위해 모든 것을 내던졌는지, 부모가 노년의 불운에 처했을 때 어떻게 그들을 등한히 하고 오로지 그 아이만 생각했는지, 조지가 그녀에게서 떼어져 나갔을 때 어떻게 그토록 터무니없고 어리석게, 참으로 불경스럽게 슬퍼했는지. 늙은 세들리는 자기 마지막 셈을 치르면서 이런 책망들을 잊고, 그 온화하고 불평 없는 작은 순교자에게 정당한 평가를 베풀었다. 어느 밤 그녀가 그의 방으로 살그머니 들어가니, 그가 깨어 있는 것을 보았고, 그때 그 쇠잔한 노인은 자기 고백을 했다. \"오, 에미야, 우리가 너에게 참 매정하고 부당했다는 생각이 드는구나.\" 그가 말하며 차갑고 힘없는 손을 그녀에게 내밀었다. 그녀는 그의 침상 곁에 무릎 꿇고 기도했고, 그도 여전히 그녀의 손을 잡은 채 함께 기도했다. 벗이여, 우리 차례가 올 때, 우리도 기도 가운데 그런 길동무를 곁에 두기를!\n\n어쩌면 그때 그가 깨어 누워 있는 동안, 그의 삶이 눈앞을 스쳐 지나갔을지도 모른다. 그 젊은 날의 희망에 찬 분투, 사내다운 성공과 번영, 노쇠한 만년의 몰락, 그리고 지금의 속수무책인 처지 — 자기를 이긴 운명에 복수할 기회도 없고, 물려줄 이름도 돈도 없으며, 패배와 실망으로 다 타 버린 부질없는 삶, 그리고 그 끝이 여기로다! 형제 같은 독자여, 어느 쪽이 나은 운명일지 나는 궁금하다. 번영하고 명망 있게 죽는 것일까, 아니면 가난하고 실망한 채 죽는 것일까? 가진 채로 그것을 내놓도록 강요당하는 것과, 승부를 벌여 패한 채로 삶에서 가라앉는 것 가운데? 우리 생의 어느 하루가 닥쳐와 이렇게 말할 때면, 그것은 분명 야릇한 느낌일 것이다. \"내일이면 성공도 실패도 별로 중요치 않을 것이고, 해는 떠오를 것이며, 무수한 인류는 여느 때처럼 제 일과 제 즐거움을 찾아갈 테지만, 나는 그 소란 바깥에 있겠지.\"\n\n그리하여 온 세상이 일어나 저마다의 갖가지 일과 즐거움에 나서는 어느 아침과 해돋이가 찾아왔으니, 다만 늙은 존 세들리만은 예외였다. 그는 더는 운명과 싸우지도, 바라지도, 궁리하지도 않고, 다만 브롬튼의 한 묘지로 가서, 옛 아내 곁에 고요하고 더없이 알려지지 않은 거처를 마련하게 되었다.\n\n도빈 소령과 조스와 조지가 검은 천을 두른 마차를 타고 그의 유해를 무덤까지 따라갔다. 조스는 그 비통한 사건 뒤로 물러나 있던 리치먼드의 스타 앤드 가터에서 일부러 왔다. 그는 그 집에 머물고 싶어 하지 않았으니 — 그 정황으로 보아, 무슨 말인지 짐작이 갈 것이다. 그러나 에미는 남아서 여느 때처럼 제 도리를 다했다. 그녀는 어떤 각별한 슬픔에 짓눌리지도 않았고, 비통하다기보다는 오히려 엄숙했다. 그녀는 자신의 임종도 그처럼 평온하고 고통 없기를 기도했으며, 아버지가 병중에 자신에게 들려준 말들, 그의 믿음과 체념과 미래의 소망을 드러내던 그 말들을 신뢰와 경외로 되새겼다.\n\n그렇다, 따지고 보면 둘 중 그쪽이 더 나은 끝맺음이라고 나는 생각한다. 그대가 유달리 부유하고 넉넉하여 그 마지막 날에 이렇게 말한다고 치자. \"나는 무척 부유하다. 나는 제법 이름이 알려져 있다. 나는 평생 최상류 사교계에서 살아왔고, 하늘에 감사하게도 더없이 점잖은 가문 출신이다. 나는 명예롭게 임금과 나라를 섬겼다. 나는 여러 해 의회에 몸담았고, 거기서 내 연설이 경청되고 제법 호평을 받았다고 말할 수 있다. 나는 누구에게도 단 한 푼 빚지지 않았다. 외려 옛 대학 동무 잭 라자루스에게 오십 파운드를 빌려주었는데, 내 유언 집행자들도 그에게 갚으라 다그치지 않을 것이다. 나는 두 딸에게 각기 만 파운드씩을 남기니 — 처녀들에게는 아주 훌륭한 몫이다. 나의 식기와 가구, 베이커 거리의 내 집은, 넉넉한 과부 급여와 더불어 내 미망인에게 평생 누리도록 물려준다. 그리고 나의 토지와, 공채에 들어 있는 돈과, 베이커 거리의 잘 골라 채운 포도주 저장고는 내 아들에게 물려준다. 나는 내 시종에게 연 이십 파운드를 남기며, 내가 떠난 뒤 누구든 내 인품에서 흠 하나라도 잡아낼 수 있거든 어디 한번 잡아 보라.\" 아니면 그 반대로, 그대의 백조가 사뭇 다른 가락의 만가(輓歌)를 부르며 이렇게 말한다고 치자. \"나는 시들고 실망한 가련한 늙은이로, 평생 철저히 실패하고 말았다. 나는 두뇌도 행운도 타고나지 못했고, 백 가지 실수와 실책을 저질렀음을 고백한다. 나는 숱하게 내 도리를 잊었음을 인정한다. 나는 진 빚도 갚을 수 없다. 나는 마지막 자리에 속수무책으로, 비천하게 누워, 내 약함에 대한 용서를 빌며, 뉘우치는 마음으로 신의 자비 발치에 나를 내던진다.\" 이 두 연설 가운데 어느 쪽이 그대 자신의 장례에 가장 어울리는 식사(式辭)일 것 같은가? 늙은 세들리는 후자를 택했다. 그리고 그 겸허한 마음가짐으로, 딸의 손을 잡은 채, 삶과 실망과 허영이 그의 발밑에서 스러져 갔다.\n\n\"보아라,\" 늙은 오스본이 조지에게 말했다. \"공덕과 근면과 분별 있는 투자며 그런 것에서 무엇이 나오는지. 나와 내 은행 계좌를 보아라. 네 가엾은 외할아버지 세들리와 그의 파산을 보아라. 그래도 이십 년 전 오늘날에는 그가 나보다 나은 사람이었지 — 그래, 만 파운드만큼은 나은 사람이었다고 해 두자.\"\n\n이 사람들과, 조의를 표하러 브롬튼에서 건너온 클랩 씨네 식구 말고는, 살아 있는 어느 누구도 늙은 존 세들리를 단 한 푼어치도 마음에 두거나, 그런 사람이 존재했다는 것을 기억하지 않았다.\n\n늙은 오스본이 (어린 조지가 이미 우리에게 일러 준 대로) 자기 벗 버클러 대령에게서 도빈 소령이 얼마나 뛰어난 장교인지 처음 들었을 때, 그는 한껏 비웃음 섞인 의혹을 드러내며 그깟 사내 따위가 어떻게 두뇌든 명성이든 갖추었겠느냐고 놀라움을 표했다. 그러나 그는 자기 사교계의 여러 사람에게서 소령의 명성을 거듭 들었다. 윌리엄 도빈 경은 자기 아들을 대단히 높이 평가하며, 소령의 학식과 무용과 세평을 보여 주는 이야기를 숱하게 들려주었다. 마침내 소령의 이름이 귀족들의 큰 연회 한두 곳의 명단에 올랐고, 이 사실은 러셀 광장의 그 늙은 귀족 행세꾼에게 엄청난 영향을 미쳤다.\n\n조지의 후견인으로서 — 조지의 보호권은 외할아버지에게 양도된 터였다 — 소령의 처지로 인해 두 신사 사이의 몇 차례 만남은 불가피했다. 그리고 그 만남 가운데 한 번, 빈틈없는 사업가인 늙은 오스본이 소령과 자기 피후견인 및 그 아이 어머니에 관한 회계를 들여다보다가, 그를 몹시 놀라게 하고 동시에 가슴 아프게도 또 흐뭇하게도 하는 단서를 잡아냈으니, 그 가련한 미망인과 아이가 연명해 온 그 자금의 일부가 다름 아닌 윌리엄 도빈 자신의 호주머니에서 나왔다는 것이었다.\n\n그 점에 대해 추궁받자, 거짓말을 할 줄 모르는 도빈은 얼굴을 붉히고 한참 더듬거리다가 마침내 털어놓았다. \"그 결혼은,\" 그가 말하자 상대의 낯빛이 어두워졌다. \"거의 제가 한 일이나 다름없었습니다. 제 가엾은 벗이 너무 멀리 와 버려서, 약혼에서 발을 빼는 것은 그에게는 불명예요 오스본 부인에게는 죽음이나 마찬가지라고 생각했습니다. 그리고 그녀가 아무런 재력도 없이 남겨졌을 때, 제가 떼어 둘 수 있는 돈을 그녀의 생계를 위해 내어 주는 것, 그 정도는 해야 한다고 여겼지요.\"\n\n\"도빈 소령,\" 오스본 씨가 그를 뚫어지게 바라보며, 자기도 얼굴이 시뻘게진 채 말했다. \"자네는 나에게 큰 해를 끼쳤네. 하지만, 이 말은 해야겠네, 자네는 정직한 사람일세. 자, 내 손을 잡게. 내 핏줄이 자네에게 얹혀살았으리라고는 꿈에도 생각 못 했네만 —\" 그렇게 두 사람은 악수했는데, 이렇게 자선의 위선 행각을 들켜 버린 도빈 소령 쪽이 무척 당혹스러워했다.\n\n그는 노인의 마음을 누그러뜨려 아들의 추억과 화해시키려 애썼다. \"그는 얼마나 고귀한 사내였는지요.\" 그가 말했다. \"우리 모두 그를 사랑했고, 그를 위해서라면 무엇이든 했을 겁니다. 그 시절 젊은이였던 저는 그가 저를 각별히 아껴 준 데에 더없이 우쭐했고, 총사령관과 함께 있는 것보다 그와 함께 있는 모습을 남들에게 보이는 게 더 기뻤습니다. 저는 담력과 대담함, 그리고 군인다운 온갖 자질에서 그에게 견줄 사람을 본 적이 없습니다.\" 그러고는 도빈은 그 늙은 아버지에게 자기 아들의 용맹과 무공에 관해 기억나는 대로 숱한 이야기를 들려주었다. \"그리고 조지는 그를 어찌나 닮았는지요.\" 소령이 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그 애가 어찌나 그를 닮았는지 가끔은 내가 다 몸이 떨린다네.\" 외할아버지가 말했다.\n\n한두 차례 저녁에 소령은 오스본 씨와 만찬을 들러 왔고 (세들리 씨가 병중이던 무렵이었다), 두 사람이 만찬 뒤 저녁에 함께 앉아 있노라면 그들의 이야기는 온통 그 떠난 영웅에 관한 것이었다. 아버지는 늘 하던 버릇대로 그를 자랑했고, 아들의 위업과 용맹을 늘어놓으며 스스로 우쭐해했지만, 어쨌든 그의 심사는 지금껏 그 가련한 사내를 두고 품어 온 것보다 한결 누그러지고 너그러워졌다. 그리고 친절한 소령의 그리스도교적인 마음은 평화와 선의가 되돌아오는 이런 조짐들에 흐뭇해졌다. 둘째 날 저녁, 늙은 오스본은 도빈과 조지가 함께 소년이던 시절에 그러던 것처럼 도빈을 윌리엄이라 불렀고, 그 정직한 신사는 이 화해의 표시에 흐뭇해했다.\n\n이튿날 아침 식사 자리에서, 오스본 양이 제 나이와 성격의 모진 데를 드러내며 소령의 외모나 거동을 깎아내리는 한마디를 감히 던지자, 이 집 주인이 그녀의 말을 가로막았다. \"너야말로 그 사람을 차지하고 싶었을 게다, 오스본 양. 하지만 그건 신 포도지. 하! 하! 윌리엄 소령은 훌륭한 사내야.\"\n\n\"맞아요, 할아버지.\" 조지가 맞장구치며 말했다. 그러고는 늙은 신사 곁으로 바짝 다가가 그 커다란 잿빛 구레나룻을 한 줌 거머쥐고는 그 얼굴에 대고 흐뭇하게 웃으며 입을 맞추었다. 그리고 그 애는 밤에 어머니에게 그 이야기를 들려주었고, 어머니는 아이의 말에 전적으로 동의했다. \"정말 그렇단다.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"네 사랑하는 아버지도 늘 그렇게 말씀하셨지. 그분은 더없이 훌륭하고 올곧은 분들 가운데 한 분이란다.\" 마침 이 대화가 있고 얼마 지나지 않아 도빈이 불쑥 들렀는데, 그 때문에 어쩌면 애밀리아의 얼굴이 붉어졌을 것이고, 그 어린 개구쟁이는 이야기의 나머지 대목을 도빈에게 들려주어 그 당혹스러움을 한층 더했다. \"있잖아요, 돕 아저씨.\" 그가 말했다. \"아저씨랑 결혼하고 싶어 하는 굉장히 멋진 아가씨가 있어요. 돈도 잔뜩 있고, 가발도 쓰고, 아침부터 밤까지 하인들을 닦달하지요.\" \"그게 누군데?\" 도빈이 물었다. \"오스본 고모예요.\" 아이가 대답했다. \"할아버지가 그러셨어요. 그리고 있잖아요, 돕 아저씨, 아저씨가 우리 외삼촌이 되면 얼마나 근사할까요.\" 바로 이때 옆방에서 늙은 세들리의 떨리는 목소리가 힘없이 애밀리아를 불렀고, 웃음은 끝이 났다.\n\n늙은 오스본의 마음이 변해 가고 있다는 것은 꽤 분명했다. 그는 이따금 조지에게 그 외삼촌에 관해 물었고, 조스가 \"맙소사\" 하고 내뱉으며 수프를 게걸스레 들이켜던 모양을 아이가 흉내 내는 것을 보고 웃었다. 그러고는 이렇게 말했다. \"너희 젊은것들이 친척을 흉내 내다니 점잖지 못한 짓이다. 오스본 양, 오늘 마차 타고 나가거든 세들리 씨 댁에 내 명함을 두고 오너라, 알겠느냐? 어쨌거나 나와 그 사람 사이에 다툴 일은 없으니 말이다.\"\n\n명함은 답례로 돌아왔고, 조스와 소령은 만찬에 초대받았다. 아마도 오스본 씨가 베푼 만찬 가운데 가장 호사스럽고 가장 따분한 만찬이었으리라. 집안의 식기란 식기는 한 점도 빠짐없이 진열되었고, 가장 훌륭한 손님들이 초대되었다. 세들리 씨가 오스본 양을 만찬석으로 이끌었고, 그녀는 그에게 무척 상냥했다. 반면 소령에게는 거의 말도 걸지 않았으니, 소령은 그녀에게서 떨어져 오스본 씨 곁에 앉아 무척 주눅 들어 있었다. 조스는 자못 엄숙하게, 평생 맛본 가운데 가장 훌륭한 거북 수프라고 말하며, 오스본 씨에게 그 마데이라 포도주를 어디서 구했는지 물었다.\n\n\"세들리 씨네 포도주 가운데 하나입니다요.\" 집사가 주인에게 속삭였다. \"그건 오래전부터 갖고 있던 거라네. 값도 두둑이 치렀고 말이야.\" 오스본 씨가 손님에게 소리 내어 말하고는, 오른편 자리에 앉은 이에게 그 \"영감 경매에서\" 그걸 손에 넣었다고 속삭였다.\n\n그는 한 번 이상 소령에게 — 조지 오스본 부인에 관해 물었는데, 소령이 마음만 먹으면 무척 능변을 토할 수 있는 화제였다. 그는 오스본 씨에게 그녀가 겪은 고초를 — 지금도 그 기억을 흠모하는 남편을 향한 그녀의 격정적인 애착을, 그리고 자기 도리라고 여겨졌을 때 부모를 봉양하고 제 아들을 내어 준 그 다정하고 갸륵한 처신을 들려주었다. \"그녀가 무엇을 견뎌 냈는지 어르신은 모르십니다.\" 정직한 도빈이 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"그리고 부디 어르신께서 그녀와 화해하시기를 저는 바라고 또 믿습니다. 그녀가 어르신께 아드님을 빼앗아 갔다면, 자기 아들을 어르신께 내어 드린 것이니까요. 그리고 어르신이 조지를 아무리 사랑하셨다 한들, 정녕 그녀는 자기 아들을 그 열 배는 더 사랑했습니다.\"\n\n\"맙소사, 자네는 좋은 사람일세.\" 오스본 씨가 한 말은 그것뿐이었다. 그 미망인이 아이와 헤어지는 데에 무슨 고통을 느끼리라거나, 아이가 큰 재산을 갖게 된 것이 그녀를 슬프게 할 수도 있다는 것은 그의 머릿속에 한 번도 떠오른 적이 없었다. 화해는 곧, 또 피할 수 없이 이루어질 것으로 알려졌고, 애밀리아의 가슴은 조지의 아버지와 그 두려운 만남을 가질 생각에 벌써부터 두근거리기 시작했다.\n\n그러나 그 만남은 끝내 이루어질 운명이 아니었다. 늙은 세들리의 길어진 병환과 죽음이 닥쳐왔고, 그 뒤로 한동안 만남은 불가능했다. 그 참변과 다른 사건들이 오스본 씨의 마음에 작용했는지도 모른다. 그는 요즘 들어 몹시 흔들렸고, 부쩍 늙었으며, 그의 마음은 안으로 곱씹고 있었다. 그는 변호사들을 불러들였고, 아마도 유언장에서 무언가를 고쳤을 것이다. 들여다보러 온 의사는 그가 기력이 쇠하고 동요한다고 진단하며 피를 좀 뽑고 바닷가에 가 보라고 했지만, 그는 그 어느 처방도 따르지 않았다.\n\n어느 날, 그가 아침 식사 자리에 내려왔어야 할 시각에 그가 보이지 않자, 그의 하인이 화장실로 들어가 보니 그가 발작을 일으킨 채 화장대 발치에 쓰러져 있었다. 오스본 양에게 기별이 갔고, 의사들이 불려 왔으며, 조지는 학교에 가지 않았고, 사혈사와 부항사가 왔다. 오스본은 잠시 의식을 되찾았으나 두 번 다시 말은 하지 못했으니, 한두 차례 안간힘을 써 보았지만 끝내 그러했고, 나흘 만에 그는 숨졌다. 의사들은 아래층으로 내려갔고, 장의사 일꾼들은 위층으로 올라갔으며, 러셀 광장 정원 쪽 덧창은 모조리 닫혔다. 불럭이 황급히 시내에서 달려왔다. \"그가 그 애한테 돈을 얼마나 남겼지? 설마 절반은 아니겠지? 분명히 세 사람이 똑같이 나눠 갖는 거겠지?\" 마음 졸이게 하는 순간이었다.\n\n그 가련한 노인이 한두 번 헛되이 말하려 했던 것은 무엇이었을까? 나는 그것이, 그가 세상을 떠나기 전에 애밀리아를 보고 자기 아들의 사랑스럽고 충직한 아내와 화해하고 싶었던 것이기를 바란다. 십중팔구 그러했을 것이니, 그가 그토록 오래 품어 온 증오가 그의 가슴에서 사라졌음을 그의 유언장이 보여 주었기 때문이다.\n\n사람들은 그의 실내복 호주머니에서, 조지가 워털루에서 그에게 써 보낸, 커다란 붉은 봉인이 찍힌 그 편지를 찾아냈다. 그는 아들에 관한 다른 서류들도 들여다본 터였으니, 그것들을 넣어 두던 상자의 열쇠 역시 그의 호주머니에 있었고, 봉인과 봉투가 뜯겨 있는 것이 발견되었다. 아마도 발작이 일어나기 전날 밤, 집사가 서재로 차를 가져갔다가 그가 커다란 붉은 가족용 성경을 읽고 있는 것을 본 그때였을 것이다.\n\n유언장이 개봉되자, 재산의 절반은 조지에게, 나머지는 두 자매에게 나누어 남겨졌음이 드러났다. 불럭 씨는 적절하다고 판단하는 대로, 그 공동의 이익을 위해 그 상사(商社)의 일을 계속하든지 손을 떼든지 하도록 했다. 조지의 재산에 부담 지우는 연 오백 파운드의 연금이 그의 어머니, \"내 사랑하는 아들 조지 오스본의 미망인\"에게 남겨졌으며, 그녀가 아이의 후견을 다시 맡도록 했다.\n\n\"내 사랑하는 아들의 벗, 윌리엄 도빈 소령\"이 유언 집행자로 지명되었다. \"그리고 그가 친절과 후의로, 또 자신의 사사로운 자금으로, 달리 생계를 꾸릴 길이 없던 내 손자와 내 아들의 미망인을 부양해 주었기에 (유언자는 이렇게 이어 갔다), 나는 그들을 향한 그의 사랑과 배려에 진심으로 감사하며, 그가 중령 임관을 사들이기에 넉넉한 금액을, 혹은 그가 적절하다고 여기는 어떤 방식으로든 처분할 만한 금액을 받아 주기를 간곡히 청하는 바이다.\"\n\n애밀리아는 시아버지가 자기와 화해했다는 것을 들었을 때, 마음이 녹아내렸고, 자기에게 남겨진 재산에 감사했다. 그러나 조지가 어떻게 자기에게 되돌아왔는지를 듣고, 그것이 어떻게, 또 누구에 의해서였는지를 알고, 가난 속에서 자기를 떠받쳐 준 것이 윌리엄의 후의였음을, 자기에게 남편을, 또 아들을 안겨 준 것이 윌리엄이었음을 알았을 때 — 오, 그때 그녀는 무릎 꿇고 그 한결같고 친절한 마음에 축복을 빌었다. 그녀는 몸을 숙여 자신을 낮추고, 그 아름답고 너그러운 애정의 발에 입맞춤하다시피 했다.\n\n그리고 그토록 갸륵한 헌신과 은혜에 그녀가 갚을 수 있는 것이라고는 감사뿐이었다 — 오로지 감사뿐! 만일 그녀가 그 밖의 다른 보답을 생각하기라도 하면, 조지의 형상이 무덤에서 일어나 이렇게 말했다. \"당신은 내 것이오, 오로지 내 것, 지금도 영원토록.\"\n\n윌리엄은 그녀의 심정을 알았다. 그가 평생을 그것을 헤아리는 데 바쳐 오지 않았던가?\n\n오스본 씨 유언장의 내막이 세상에 알려지자, 조지 오스본 부인이 자기 교제권을 이루는 사람들의 평가 속에서 얼마나 격상되는지를 지켜보는 것은 자못 교훈적이었다. 조스네 살림을 꾸리던 하인들은, 그녀의 겸손한 분부에 토를 달고 따라야 할지 말아야 할지 \"주인 나리께 여쭤보겠다\"고 말하곤 했지만, 이제는 그런 식의 호소 따위는 떠올리지도 않았다. 요리사는 그녀의 초라한 낡은 드레스를 비웃던 일을 잊었고 (사실 그 드레스는 그녀가 일요일 저녁 예배에 가려고 차려입었을 때 그 부인의 호사스러운 차림에 완전히 빛이 바래는 것이었다), 다른 이들도 더는 그녀의 종소리에 투덜대거나 그 부름에 굼뜨게 응하지 않았다. 자기 말들을 끌어내야 하고 자기 마차가 그 늙은 영감과 오스본 부인을 위한 병원 노릇이나 한다고 투덜대던 마부는, 이제 그녀를 더없이 부지런히 모셨고, 행여 오스본 씨의 마부에게 밀려날까 벌벌 떨면서, \"그 러셀 광장 마부 녀석들이 도시에 대해 뭘 안다고, 숙녀분 앞에서 마부석에 앉을 자격이나 되겠느냐\"고 물었다. 조스의 친구들은 남녀를 막론하고 갑자기 에미에게 관심을 갖게 되었고, 그녀의 현관 탁자에는 조의를 표하는 명함이 늘어 갔다. 그녀를 그저 사람 좋고 해 끼치지 않는 빈털터리쯤으로, 먹을 것과 거처를 마련해 주는 것이 자기 도리라 여기던 조스 본인도, 이제 그녀와 그 부유한 어린 사내아이, 자기 조카에게 더없는 경의를 표했고, \"가엾은 우리 애\"가 그 온갖 고초와 시련을 겪고 나서 기분 전환과 여흥을 누리기를 바랐으며, 아침 식사 자리에 모습을 드러내기 시작하더니, 특히나 그날 하루를 어떻게 보내고 싶은지를 꼬박꼬박 물었다.\n\n조지의 후견인 자격으로, 그녀는 공동 수탁자인 소령의 동의를 얻어, 오스본 양에게 원하는 한 얼마든지 러셀 광장 저택에서 살아 달라고 청했다. 그러나 그 부인은 감사를 표하면서도, 그 음울한 저택에서 혼자 지낼 생각은 도무지 할 수 없노라 말하고, 옛 하인 두엇을 거느린 채 깊은 상복 차림으로 첼튼엄으로 떠났다. 나머지 하인들에게는 후하게 보수를 치르고 해고했는데, 오스본 부인이 계속 두고자 했던 그 충직한 늙은 집사는 사직하고 제 저축을 술집에 투자하는 편을 택했으니, 거기서 그가 그럭저럭 번창했기를 바라 본다. 오스본 양이 러셀 광장에서 살기를 마다하니, 오스본 부인도 의논 끝에 그 음침한 옛 저택에 들어가 사는 것을 사양했다. 집은 해체되었고, 호화로운 가구와 세간, 그 으리으리한 샹들리에와 음산하게 텅 빈 거울들은 꾸려져 치워졌으며, 값진 자단(紫檀) 응접실 세트는 짚으로 감싸였고, 양탄자들은 둘둘 말려 노끈으로 묶였으며, 단단히 제본된 책들로 알차게 추린 작은 서가는 포도주 상자 두 개에 챙겨 넣어졌고, 그 일습은 거대한 짐마차 여러 대에 실려 팬테크니콘으로 옮겨져, 조지가 성년이 될 때까지 거기 놓이게 되었다. 그리고 그 크고 묵직한 검은 식기 상자들은 스텀피 앤드 라우디 상회로 보내져, 같은 시기가 올 때까지 그 명망 높은 은행가들의 지하 금고에 놓이게 되었다.\n\n어느 날 에미는 조지의 손을 잡고 깊은 상복을 두른 채, 처녀 시절 이후로 들어가 본 적 없던 그 버려진 저택을 찾아갔다. 집 앞은 짐마차에 짐을 싣고 굴려 보낸 자리에 짚이 어지러이 널려 있었다. 그들은 텅 빈 큰 방들로 들어갔는데, 그 벽에는 그림과 거울이 걸려 있던 자국이 남아 있었다. 그런 다음 텅 빈 큰 돌계단을 올라 위층 방들로, 조지가 속삭이듯 말한 대로 할아버지가 돌아가신 그 방으로 들어갔다가, 더 위로 올라가 조지 자신의 방으로 갔다. 아이는 여전히 그녀 곁에 매달려 있었지만, 그녀는 그 아이 말고 또 다른 사람을 생각하고 있었다. 그녀는 그 방이 아이의 방인 동시에 아이 아버지의 방이기도 했음을 알고 있었다.\n\n그녀는 열린 창문 가운데 하나로 (아이가 처음 그녀에게서 떼어졌을 때, 가슴 아파하며 내다보곤 하던 바로 그런 창문 가운데 하나였다) 다가갔고, 거기서 밖을 내다보니 러셀 광장의 나무들 너머로, 자기가 태어난 옛집, 신성한 청춘의 그토록 많은 행복한 나날을 보낸 그 집이 보였다. 그 모든 것이 그녀에게 되돌아왔다. 그 즐거운 휴일들, 다정한 얼굴들, 근심 없고 기쁘던 지난날, 그리고 그 뒤로 그녀를 무너뜨린 길고 긴 고통과 시련. 그녀는 이런 것들을, 그리고 자기의 한결같은 보호자였고, 자기의 선한 수호신이었으며, 자기의 유일한 은인이자, 다정하고 너그러운 벗이었던 그 사람을 떠올렸다.\n\n\"이것 봐요, 엄마.\" 조지가 말했다. \"여기 유리에 다이아몬드로 G.O.라고 긁혀 있어요. 한 번도 본 적 없는데, 내가 한 게 아니에요.\"\n\n\"그건 네가 태어나기 훨씬 전부터 네 아버지 방이었단다, 조지.\" 그녀는 그렇게 말하며, 아이에게 입맞추면서 얼굴을 붉혔다.\n\n그들이 임시로 집을 얻어 둔 리치먼드로 마차를 타고 돌아오는 동안 그녀는 무척 말이 없었다. 그곳으로는 싱글거리는 변호사들이 부산스레 그녀를 만나러 건너오곤 했고 (그 방문을 청구서에 적어 넣었으리라는 것은 우리가 능히 짐작할 만하다), 그곳에는 물론 도빈 소령을 위한 방도 마련되어 있었으니, 그는 자기 어린 피후견인을 위해 처리할 일이 많아 자주 말을 달려 건너왔다.\n\n이 무렵 조지는 빌 씨네에서 데려와 기한 없는 방학을 누리게 되었고, 그 신사는 파운들링 안에 조지 오스본 대위의 기념비 아래 세울 고운 대리석 판에 새길 비문을 짓는 일을 맡았다.\n\n조지의 고모인 불럭 부인은, 비록 그 어린 괴물에게 아버지에게서 받으려던 액수의 절반을 빼앗기긴 했어도, 어머니와 아이에게 화해의 손을 내밀어 그 너그러운 심성을 내보였다. 로햄프턴은 리치먼드에서 멀지 않았고, 어느 날 차체 옆면에 황금빛 황소 문장이 새겨진, 축 늘어진 아이들을 태운 마차가 리치먼드의 애밀리아 집으로 달려왔다. 그리고 불럭 가족은 정원으로 들이닥쳤으니, 거기서는 애밀리아가 책을 읽고 있었고, 조스는 정자에서 느긋하게 딸기를 포도주에 적시고 있었으며, 인도풍 웃옷을 입은 소령은 조지에게 등을 내주어 조지가 그 위로 뛰어넘게 하고 있었다. 조지는 소령의 머리 위를 뛰어넘어, 모자에 커다란 검은 리본을 달고 허리에 큼직한 검은 띠를 두른 채 상복 차림의 엄마를 뒤따르던 어린 불럭 무리 한가운데로 폴짝 뛰어들었다.\n\n\"저 애가 딱 로자 또래로구나.\" 그 자식 사랑 지극한 어머니는 그렇게 생각하며, 일곱 살배기 병약한 어린 아가씨인 그 사랑스러운 아이 쪽을 흘끗 바라보았다.\n\n\"로자야, 가서 네 사랑하는 사촌에게 입맞춤하렴.\" 프레더릭 부인이 말했다. \"조지, 나를 모르겠니? 내가 네 고모란다.\"\n\n\"잘 알아요.\" 조지가 말했다. \"하지만 전 입맞춤은 싫은데요.\" 그러고는 고분고분한 사촌이 어르듯 다가오는 것을 피해 물러섰다.\n\n\"네 사랑하는 엄마한테 데려다주렴, 이 익살맞은 녀석아.\" 프레더릭 부인이 말했고, 그리하여 그 두 부인은 십오 년이 넘는 절연 끝에 만나게 되었다. 에미가 시름과 가난에 잠겨 있던 동안 다른 한쪽은 단 한 번도 그녀를 보러 올 생각을 하지 않았지만, 이제 그녀가 세상에서 그럭저럭 번창하게 되자, 그 손위 동서는 당연하다는 듯 그녀를 찾아왔다.\n\n그렇게 찾아온 이들이 더 숱하게 많았다. 우리의 옛 벗 스워츠 양도 남편과 더불어 불타는 듯한 노란색 제복을 두르고 햄프턴 코트에서 요란하게 달려왔으며, 여전히 변함없이 격하게 애밀리아를 좋아했다. 스워츠 양은 볼 수만 있었더라면 늘 그녀를 좋아했을 것이다. 그 점만은 그녀를 위해 공정히 인정해 주어야 한다. 하지만 어쩌겠는가? 이 광대한 도시에서는 누구도 벗을 찾아 나설 겨를이 없는 것을. 벗들이 대열에서 떨어져 나가면 사라져 버리고, 우리는 그들 없이 행진을 이어 간다. 허영의 시장에서 그리워지는 이가 도대체 누구란 말인가?\n\n그러나 한마디로, 오스본 씨의 죽음에 대한 애도의 기간이 채 가시기도 전에, 에미는 어느새 자못 점잖은 한 교제권의 한복판에 놓인 자신을 발견했으니, 그 일원들은 거기 속한 누군가가 무척 운이 좋지 않을 수 있다는 것을 도무지 상상조차 못 하는 이들이었다. 그 부인들 가운데 남편이 시내의 건어물상일지언정 친척 중에 귀족 한 명쯤 두지 않은 이가 거의 없었다. 더러는 무척 학식 높은 식자(識者)로, 서머빌 부인의 책을 읽고 왕립 연구소를 드나들었으며, 더러는 준엄한 복음주의자로 엑서터 홀을 지지했다. 솔직히 말하건대, 에미는 그들의 수다 한복판에서 도무지 갈피를 못 잡았고, 프레더릭 불럭 부인의 환대를 받아들이지 않을 수 없었던 한두 번의 자리에서는 비참하리만치 곤욕을 치렀다. 그 부인은 한사코 그녀를 후원하려 들었고, 더없이 자애롭게 그녀를 길들이기로 작정했다. 그녀는 애밀리아에게 모자 장수를 구해 주고 그녀의 살림과 몸가짐을 단속했다. 그녀는 로햄프턴에서 끊임없이 마차를 몰고 건너와, 시들한 사교계 시시콜콜 잡담과 맥 빠진 궁정 객설로 자기 벗을 즐겁게 해 주었다. 조스는 그것을 듣기 좋아했지만, 소령은 그 이 푼짜리 점잔을 빼는 여자가 나타나면 으레 투덜대며 자리를 떴다. 그는 그 은행가의 가장 성대한 연회 중 한 자리에서, 만찬 뒤 프레더릭 불럭의 벗어진 머리 아래에서 잠이 들었고 (프레드는 여전히 오스본 재산의 잔액이 스텀피 앤드 라우디 상회에서 자기네 쪽으로 옮겨지기를 안달했다), 한편 라틴어도 모르고, 에든버러 리뷰에 실린 최신 명문(名文)을 누가 썼는지도 모르며, 저 운명적인 가톨릭 구제 법안에 관한 필 씨의 근래의 그 놀라운 변절을 조금도 개탄하거나 어쩌거나 하지 않는 애밀리아는, 으리으리한 응접실에서 부인들 가운데 말없이 앉아, 벨벳 같은 잔디밭과 말끔히 손질된 자갈길과 반짝이는 온실들을 내다보고 있었다.\n\n\"사람은 좋아 보이는데 어딘가 밋밋하군요.\" 라우디 부인이 말했다. \"저 소령은 단단히 반한 모양이고요.\"\n\n\"세련된 데가 영 모자라요.\" 홀리오크 부인이 말했다. \"이봐요, 당신은 절대로 저 여자를 길들이지 못할 거예요.\"\n\n\"끔찍하게 무식하거나 무관심하더군요.\" 글로리 부인이 무덤에서 울려 나오는 듯한 목소리로, 머리와 터번을 슬프게 가로저으며 말했다. \"내가 그 여자에게, 졸스 씨 말대로 1836년에, 아니면 왭샷 씨 말대로 1839년에 교황이 몰락할 것 같으냐고 물었더니, 그 여자가 하는 말이, '가엾은 교황님! 그러지 않기를 바라요. 그분이 무슨 잘못을 하셨길래요?'라지 뭐예요.\"\n\n\"그 여자는 제 오라비의 미망인이에요, 여러분.\" 프레더릭 부인이 대답했다. \"그러니만큼 그 여자가 세상에 발을 들이는 데에 우리 모두가 온갖 보살핌과 가르침을 베풀 의무가 있다고 생각해요. 다들 잘 아시다시피 실망스러운 처지에 놓인 사람을 대하는 데에야 무슨 잇속을 차리는 속셈 따위가 있을 리 없잖아요.\"\n\n\"그 가엾은 불럭 부인 말이에요.\" 라우디가 홀리오크에게 함께 마차를 몰고 떠나며 말했다. \"늘 무슨 꿍꿍이를 꾸미고 주무르려 든다니까요. 오스본 부인의 거래를 우리 은행에서 자기네 쪽으로 옮기려는 거예요. 그리고 그 사내아이를 어르고 달래서 눈곱 낀 그 어린 로자 옆에 앉히는 꼴이라니, 정말 가관이지 뭐예요.\"\n\n\"글로리는 자기가 떠받드는 그 죄악의 사람이며 아마겟돈 전투에 목이나 콱 막혀 버렸으면 좋겠어요.\" 다른 한 사람이 외쳤고, 마차는 퍼트니 다리 위로 굴러갔다.\n\n그러나 이런 부류의 사교계는 에미에게는 너무나 모질도록 점잔을 빼는 것이었고, 그래서 해외 여행이 제안되자 다들 기뻐 날뛰었다.\n\n앞서 말한 그런 일상사들이 다 벌어지고 몇 주가 흐른 뒤, 화창한 어느 아침이었다. 의회 회기가 끝나고 여름이 무르익어, 런던의 상류 인사들이 해마다 즐기는 쾌락이나 건강을 좇는 유람을 위해 막 그 도시를 떠나려던 무렵, 바타비르 증기선 한 척이 한 무리의 잉글랜드 도망객들을 가득 싣고 타워 부두 계단을 떠났다. 뒷갑판에는 차양이 쳐졌고, 벤치며 통로마다 발그레한 아이들이 떼를 지어, 분주한 유모들이, 더없이 어여쁜 분홍 보닛에 여름옷을 차려입은 숙녀들이, 여행용 모자에 아마포 재킷을 걸친, 이번 유람을 앞두고 막 콧수염이 돋기 시작한 신사들이, 그리고 풀 먹인 넥타이에 말끔히 솔질한 모자를 쓴 다부지고 단정한 노병들이 빼곡했으니, 이들은 전쟁이 끝난 이래 줄곧 유럽으로 쳐들어가 대륙의 도시마다 영국식 욕설을 퍼뜨리고 다닌 그런 부류였다. 모자 상자며 브래마 책상이며 화장 도구함이 산더미처럼 쌓여 있었다. 가정교사를 대동하고 노넨베르트나 쾨니히스빈터로 독서 유람을 떠나는 멋쟁이 케임브리지 청년들이 있었고, 더없이 화려한 구레나룻과 보석으로 치장한 채 쉴 새 없이 말 이야기만 늘어놓으며 배에 탄 젊은 숙녀들에게는 지나치도록 정중하게 구는 아일랜드 신사들도 있었는데, 정작 케임브리지 청년들과 그 창백한 얼굴의 가정교사들은 처녀처럼 수줍어하며 그 숙녀들을 피해 다녔다. 또 엠스며 비스바덴으로 가서, 한 시즌의 만찬으로 더부룩해진 속을 씻어 내리려 온천 요법을 받고, 또 흥을 돋우려 룰렛과 트랑테카랑트 노름도 좀 즐기려는, 폴몰 거리의 늙은 한량들도 있었다. 근위대의 파피용 대위에게 자기 양산과 안내책자를 들리고 다니는, 젊은 아내를 얻은 늙은 므두셀라도 있었고, 신부를 데리고 신혼 유람을 떠나는 젊은 메이도 있었으니(이 신부는 본디 윈터 부인이라 불리던 이로, 메이의 할머니와 학교를 같이 다닌 사이였다), 자식 열둘에 그에 걸맞은 수의 유모를 거느린 존 경 부부도 있었으며, 바퀴 근처에 자기들끼리만 따로 앉아 누구든 빤히 쳐다보면서도 아무에게도 말을 걸지 않는 저 거물 베어에이커스 일가도 있었다. 그들의 마차는 작은 관 문장이 새겨지고 번쩍이는 짐 상자들이 잔뜩 실린 채 앞갑판에 놓여, 그와 같은 마차 열두어 대와 함께 단단히 묶여 있었다. 그 사이를 비집고 드나들기란 여간 어려운 일이 아니었고, 앞 선실의 가엾은 승객들은 움직일 자리조차 거의 없었다. 그 승객들이란 자기네 먹을 것을 직접 싸 들고 와서는 대형 살롱에 탄 화려한 사람들 절반쯤은 너끈히 사 버릴 수 있을 법한, 하운즈디치 출신의 호화롭게 차려입은 신사 몇몇과, 콧수염을 기르고 화첩을 든 채 배에 탄 지 반 시간도 안 되어 그림을 그려 대기 시작한 정직한 사내 몇몇과, 배가 그리니치를 지날 무렵에는 벌써 끔찍하게 멀미를 앓기 시작한 프랑스인 하녀 한둘과, 자기들이 맡은 마구간 칸 근처를 어슬렁거리거나 외륜 옆 뱃전에 기대어 누가 레저 경마에서 유망한지, 굿우드컵으로 얼마를 따거나 잃을지를 떠들어 대는 마부 한둘이었다.\n\n수행 안내인들은 다들 배 안을 여기저기 헤집고 다니다가 저마다의 주인을 선실이나 갑판에 자리 잡게 해 준 뒤 한데 모여 수다를 떨고 담배를 피우기 시작했다. 히브리 신사들도 거기 끼어들어 마차들을 살펴보았다. 열세 사람은 너끈히 태울 만한 존 경의 거대한 마차가 있었고, 므두셀라 경의 마차, 베어에이커스 경의 사륜마차며 브리츠카며 짐마차도 있었는데, 마음 있는 자라면 누구든 그 값을 치러도 좋을 것이었다. 베어에이커스 경이 이 여행 경비를 댈 현금을 어디서 구했는지는 참으로 알 수 없는 노릇이었다. 하지만 히브리 신사들은 그가 어떻게 구했는지를 알고 있었다. 그분의 호주머니에 바로 그 순간 얼마가 들어 있는지, 그가 거기에 무슨 이자를 치르는지, 누가 그 돈을 댔는지를 그들은 알았다. 끝으로, 더없이 말끔하고 멋들어진 여행용 마차 한 대가 있었으니, 신사들은 그 마차를 두고 이런저런 추측을 늘어놓았다.\n\n\"저 마차는 누구 거요?\" 큼직한 모로코가죽 돈주머니에 귀고리를 단 한 신사 안내인이, 귀고리에 큼직한 모로코가죽 돈주머니를 찬 다른 안내인에게 물었다.\n\n\"내 생각엔 키르슈 거요. 아까 보니까 마차 안에서 샌드위치를 꺼내고 있더군요.\" 안내인이 그럴듯한 독일식 프랑스어로 대답했다.\n\n키르슈는 이내 짐칸 근처에서 모습을 드러냈는데, 거기서 그는 승객들의 짐을 챙겨 넣는 선원들에게 갖은 나라말로 욕설을 섞어 가며 고래고래 지시를 내리던 참이었다. 그는 동료 통역들에게 제 사정을 일러 주러 왔다. 그러고는 그 마차가 캘커타와 자메이카에서 온 어마어마한 부자 나리, 자기가 모시고 여행하기로 계약한 그 나리의 것이라고 알려 주었다. 바로 그때 한 젊은 신사가, 외륜 칸 사이의 다리에 올라가지 말라는 주의를 받고는 거기서 므두셀라 경의 마차 지붕으로 뛰어내려, 또 거기서 다른 마차들과 짐 상자들을 타고 넘어 자기네 마차에까지 기어오르더니, 거기서 다시 내려와 창문을 통해 마차 안으로 들어갔다. 지켜보던 안내인들의 갈채가 쏟아졌다.\n\n\"오늘 항해는 아주 순조롭겠습니다, 조지 도련님.\" 안내인이 금실로 장식된 모자를 들어 올리며 히죽 웃고 말했다.\n\n\"네 프랑스어 따위 집어치워.\" 젊은 신사가 말했다. \"비스킷은 어디 있어, 응?\" 그러자 키르슈는 영어로, 아니 그가 구사할 수 있는 영어 비슷한 것으로 대답했으니, 키르슈 씨는 온갖 언어에 익숙하긴 했어도 어느 한 언어도 제대로 아는 게 없어서, 어느 말이든 그저 어지간히 술술, 또 어지간히 틀리게 늘어놓을 뿐이었다.\n\n비스킷을 게걸스레 먹어 치우던 그 거만한 젊은 신사는(사실 요기를 할 때도 되긴 했으니, 그는 꼬박 세 시간 전 리치먼드에서 아침을 먹은 터였다) 다름 아닌 우리의 젊은 벗 조지 오즈번이었다. 조스 외삼촌과 그의 어머니는 뒷갑판에서, 평소에 자주 만나 보던 한 신사와 함께 있었으니, 이 네 사람이 막 여름 유람을 떠나려는 참이었다.\n\n조스는 그 순간 갑판 차양 아래에, 베어에이커스 백작 부부와 그 가족의 거의 정면에 앉아 있었는데, 그 일가의 일거수일투족이 이 벵골 사람의 마음을 송두리째 사로잡았다. 그 고귀한 부부는 둘 다, 사건 많던 1815년에 조스가 브뤼셀에서 보았던(사실 그는 인도에서 늘 그들과 막역한 사이라고 떠벌리고 다녔다) 그때보다 오히려 젊어 보였다. 그때만 해도 검던 베어에이커스 부인의 머리칼은 이제 고운 황금빛 적갈색이 되었고, 한편 예전엔 붉던 베어에이커스 경의 구레나룻은 이제 짙은 검은빛으로, 빛을 받으면 보랏빛과 초록빛이 어른거렸다. 그러나 그렇게 달라졌어도, 이 고귀한 부부의 거동은 조스의 마음을 완전히 사로잡았다. 귀족의 존재가 그를 매혹시켜, 그는 다른 어떤 것도 눈에 들어오지 않았다.\n\n\"저 사람들한테 꽤나 마음이 끌리시나 봅니다.\" 도빈이 웃으며 그를 지켜보다가 말했다. 애밀리아도 웃었다. 그녀는 검은 리본을 단 밀짚 보닛을 쓰고, 그 밖에는 상복 차림이었지만, 여행의 자그마한 분주함과 들뜬 기분이 그녀를 즐겁고 설레게 하여, 유난히 행복해 보였다.\n\n\"정말 더없이 좋은 날이에요!\" 에미가 말하더니, 더없이 독창적이게도 한마디 덧붙였다. \"잔잔한 항해가 됐으면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n조스는 손을 내저으면서, 그러는 동시에 눈을 내리뜬 채 맞은편의 귀하신 분들을 곁눈질했다. \"자네가 우리처럼 항해를 해 봤다면,\" 그가 말했다. \"날씨 따위는 그리 신경 쓰지 않을 걸세.\" 그러나 그런 말을 해도, 그 자칭 여행통은 그날 밤 자기 마차 안에서 끔찍하게 멀미를 앓으며 보냈고, 안내인이 브랜디에 물을 탄 음료며 온갖 호사스러운 것들로 그를 보살펴 주었다.\n\n이윽고 이 행복한 일행은 로테르담 부두에 내렸고, 거기서 또 다른 증기선으로 옮겨 타 쾰른 시까지 갔다. 여기서 마차와 일가족은 뭍에 올랐는데, 조스는 자기 도착 소식이 쾰른 신문에 \"런던에서 온 폰 세들리 백작 경 일행\"이라고 실린 것을 보고 적잖이 흐뭇해했다. 그는 궁정 예복을 챙겨 왔고, 도빈에게도 연대 정장 일습을 가져오라고 우겼다. 그는 몇몇 외국 궁정에 알현하여, 자기가 방문의 영광을 베푸는 나라들의 군주들께 경의를 표할 작정이라고 공언했다.\n\n일행이 어디에 머물든, 기회만 생기면 조스 씨는 \"우리 공사 각하\" 댁에 자기 명함과 소령의 명함을 두고 왔다. 유덴슈타트 자유시의 영국 영사가 그 손님 후한 관리로서 우리 여행자들을 만찬에 초대했을 때, 조스가 삼각모를 쓰고 꽉 끼는 바지를 입고서 그를 찾아가겠다는 것을 말리기란 여간 어려운 일이 아니었다. 그는 항해 일지를 꼬박꼬박 적어, 자기가 묵은 여러 여관의 흠이나 장점을, 또 자기가 맛본 포도주와 요리를 일일이 공들여 기록했다.\n\n에미로 말하자면, 그녀는 더없이 행복하고 즐거웠다. 도빈은 늘 그녀를 위해 접의자와 스케치북을 들고 다녔고, 마음씨 고운 그 자그마한 화가의 그림을 일찍이 어느 누구도 그래 본 적 없을 만큼 감탄하며 칭찬했다. 그녀는 증기선 갑판에 앉아 절벽이며 성채를 그렸고, 당나귀에 올라타 옛 도적들의 탑까지 올라가기도 했는데, 그럴 때면 두 부관, 즉 조지와 도빈이 그녀를 따랐다. 그녀는 당나귀 등에 올라탄 도빈의 우스꽝스러운 모습을, 긴 다리가 땅에 닿는 그 꼴을 보고 웃었고, 소령 자신도 함께 웃었다. 그는 일행의 통역이었으니, 군대에서 익힌 독일어 실력이 제법이었기 때문이다. 그래서 그와 신이 난 조지는 라인란트와 팔츠 지방의 전투들을 함께 누볐다. 조지는 몇 주 만에, 마부석에 앉은 키르슈 씨와 부지런히 떠들어 댄 덕에 고지 독일어 실력이 놀랍도록 늘어, 호텔 종업원이며 마부에게 말을 걸어 제 어머니를 흐뭇하게 하고 제 후견인을 즐겁게 했다.\n\n조스 씨는 동행들의 오후 나들이에는 별로 끼지 않았다. 그는 만찬을 들고 나면 한참 잠을 자거나, 운치 있는 여관 정원의 정자에서 볕을 쬐었다. 정겨운 라인 강가의 정원들이여! 평화와 햇살의 고운 풍경이여, 그 봉우리가 장엄한 강물에 비치는 저 고귀한 자줏빛 산들이여, 그대들을 한 번이라도 본 사람이라면 그 정답고 아름다운 안식의 풍경을 어찌 고마운 마음으로 기억하지 않으랴? 펜을 내려놓고 그저 그 아름다운 라인란트를 떠올리는 것만으로도 마음이 행복해진다. 이런 여름 저녁 무렵이면, 소들이 음매 울며 방울을 딸랑이며 언덕에서 줄지어 내려와, 옛 해자와 성문과 첨탑과 밤나무가 있는, 풀밭 위로 길게 푸른 그림자가 드리운 옛 고을로 향한다. 하늘과 그 아래 강물은 진홍과 황금빛으로 불타오르고, 달은 벌써 떠올라 지는 해 쪽을 향해 창백하게 걸려 있다. 해가 거대한 성채 얹힌 산봉우리 뒤로 가라앉으면 밤이 문득 내려앉고, 강물은 점점 더 어두워지며, 옛 성벽의 창문에서 불빛이 강물에 일렁이고, 건너편 기슭 언덕 아래 마을들에서 평화롭게 반짝인다.\n\n그리하여 조스는 반다나 손수건을 얼굴에 덮고 한참 잠을 자며 더없이 편안하게 지냈고, 영국 소식을 죄다 읽었으며, 갈리냐니의 그 훌륭한 신문은 한 자도 빠짐없이 읽었다(일찍이 외국에 나가 본 적 있는 모든 영국인의 축복이, 그 해적판 신문을 세운 이들과 그 소유주들에게 깃들기를!). 그가 깨어 있든 잠들어 있든, 그의 벗들은 그를 별로 아쉬워하지 않았다. 그렇다, 그들은 더없이 행복했다. 그들은 저녁이면 자주 오페라를 보러 갔다. 독일 고을들의 그 아늑하고 수수하고 정겨운 옛 오페라 극장으로 말이다. 거기서는 한쪽에 귀족들이 앉아 눈물을 흘리며 양말을 뜨개질하고, 맞은편에는 부르주아들이 앉으며, 다들 몹시 살지고 인심 좋은, 더없이 투명하신 공작 전하와 그 투명하신 가족이 한가운데 큰 특별석을 차지하러 온다. 그리고 일층 좌석은 더없이 우아하게 허리가 잘록한, 짚빛 콧수염을 기른 장교들로 가득한데, 이들은 정식 봉급이래야 하루 이 펜스를 받는 자들이었다. 바로 이곳에서 에미는 기쁨을 찾았고, 모차르트와 치마로사의 경이를 난생처음 접했다. 소령의 음악적 취향에 대해서는 앞서 언급한 바 있고, 그의 플루트 연주가 칭찬받았다는 것도 말한 바 있다. 그러나 이 오페라들에서 그가 누린 가장 큰 즐거움은, 아마도 그것을 듣는 동안 황홀경에 빠진 에미의 얼굴을 지켜보는 데 있었을 것이다. 저 거룩한 곡들을 처음 접했을 때, 그녀에게는 사랑과 아름다움의 새로운 세계가 활짝 열렸다. 이 부인은 더없이 예민하고 섬세한 감수성을 지녔으니, 모차르트를 들으면서 어찌 무덤덤할 수 있었으랴? \"돈 후안\"의 애틋한 대목들은 그녀의 마음에 너무도 황홀한 환희를 일깨워, 밤에 기도를 드리러 갈 때면 \"베드라이 카리노\"와 \"바티 바티\"가 그 다정하고 자그마한 가슴을 가득 채운 그토록 큰 기쁨을 느끼는 것이 죄가 아닌지 스스로 묻곤 했다. 그러나 그녀가 이 문제를 두고 신학 자문역으로 의논한 소령은(그 자신도 경건하고 신실한 영혼을 지닌 이였다) 자기로 말하자면 예술이든 자연이든 온갖 아름다움이 자기를 행복하게 할 뿐 아니라 감사하게 만든다고, 또 훌륭한 음악을 듣는 데서 얻는 기쁨이란 하늘의 별이나 아름다운 풍경이나 그림을 바라보는 것과 마찬가지로, 다른 어떤 세속의 축복 못지않게 진심으로 하늘에 감사할 만한 은혜라고 말했다. 그리고 애밀리아 부인이 미약하게나마 이의를 제기하자(이는 핀칠리 코먼의 빨래하는 여인 따위, 오즈번 부인이 브롬프턴에서 지내는 동안 받아 본 그런 부류의 어떤 신학서들에서 따온 것이었다) 그는 그녀에게, 햇빛은 눈에 견딜 수 없는 것이며 나이팅게일은 더없이 과대평가된 새라고 여기던 부엉이에 관한 동방의 우화 하나를 들려주었다. \"노래하는 게 한쪽의 본성이라면 부엉부엉 우는 게 다른 쪽의 본성이지요.\" 그가 웃으며 말했다. \"그리고 부인처럼 그토록 고운 목소리를 지녔으니, 부인은 응당 나이팅게일 편에 속하는 사람입니다.\"\n\n나는 그녀의 인생에서 이 시절을 즐겨 곱씹으며, 그녀가 명랑하고 행복했다고 생각하기를 좋아한다. 보다시피 그녀는 아직 그런 부류의 삶을 그리 많이 누려 보지 못했고, 자기 취향이나 지성을 갈고닦을 길도 만나 보지 못했다. 그녀는 지금껏 천박한 지성들에게 짓눌려 지내 왔다. 이는 숱한 여인의 운명이다. 그리고 저 사랑스러운 여성이라는 종족은 하나하나가 모두 제 동족 나머지의 경쟁자인지라, 그들의 너그러운 판단 속에서 수줍음은 어리석음으로 통하고, 온순함은 둔함으로 통하며, 침묵은 — 그것이란 지배하는 자들의 달갑잖은 주장에 대한 소심한 부정이요 말 없는 항변일 뿐인데 — 무엇보다도 저 여성 종교재판소의 손에서 자비라곤 얻지 못한다. 그러니 친애하고 교양 있는 독자여, 만약 그대와 내가 오늘 저녁 이를테면 채소 장수들의 모임 한가운데 끼게 된다면, 우리의 대화가 빛나지는 못할 공산이 크다. 반대로, 채소 장수 하나가, 누구나 재치 있는 말을 주고받고, 잘난 체하며 평판 있는 부인네들이 더없이 흐뭇한 솜씨로 제 친구들을 갈가리 헐뜯어 대는, 그대의 세련되고 정중한 다과상 자리에 끼게 된다면, 그 낯선 손님은 그리 말수도 많지 않을 것이요, 결코 흥미롭지도 흥미를 느끼지도 못할 것이다.\n\n그리고 잊지 말아야 할 것은, 이 가엾은 부인이 바로 이 순간에 이르기까지 평생 단 한 번도 진정한 신사를 만나 본 적이 없다는 사실이다. 어쩌면 그런 인물은 우리들 중 더러가 생각하는 것보다 더 드문지도 모른다. 우리 가운데 누가 제 주위에서 그런 이를 여럿 가리켜 보일 수 있겠는가? 그 뜻이 너그럽고, 그 진실이 한결같으며, 한결같을 뿐 아니라 그 정도가 고결하고, 비루함이 없는지라 도리어 소박하며, 크고 작은 모든 이를 한결같이 의젓하게 헤아리는 마음으로 세상을 정직하게 마주 볼 수 있는 사내 말이다. 옷을 아주 잘 빼입은 자라면 우리는 백 명도 알고, 매너가 빼어난 자라면 스무 명도 알며, 이른바 상류 사교계의 일원이 되어 유행의 한복판, 그 과녁의 정중앙에 명중한 행복한 자도 한둘은 안다. 하지만 진정한 신사로 말하면 과연 몇이나 되겠는가? 우리 모두 종이 한 조각을 꺼내 저마다 그 명단을 적어 보자.\n\n내 벗인 소령을, 나는 한 점 의심 없이 내 명단에 적어 넣는다. 그는 다리가 무척 길었고, 누런 얼굴에, 처음엔 다소 우스꽝스러웠던 가벼운 혀짤배기소리를 냈다. 그러나 그 생각은 올발랐고, 머리는 제법 좋았으며, 삶은 정직하고 순결했고, 마음은 따뜻하고 겸손했다. 그에게 분명 무척 큰 손과 발이 있긴 했으니, 두 조지 오즈번이 그것을 곧잘 흉내 내며 비웃곤 했다. 어쩌면 그들의 조롱과 웃음이 가엾은 어린 에미로 하여금 그의 진가를 그르쳐 보게 만들었는지도 모른다. 그러나 우리 모두 우리의 영웅들에 대해 잘못 알았다가 백 번도 더 생각을 바꾼 적이 있지 않은가? 에미는 이 행복한 시절에, 소령의 진가에 대한 제 생각이 몹시도 크게 달라졌음을 깨달았다.\n\n어쩌면 이때가 두 사람 모두의 인생에서 가장 행복한 시절이었는지도 모른다. 정말로, 그들이 그것을 알기만 했더라면 — 한데 누가 그것을 알겠는가? 우리 가운데 누가, 저것이 절정이었노라, 저것이 인간 기쁨의 정점이었노라 하고 가리켜 말할 수 있겠는가? 어쨌든 이 두 사람은 제법 흡족하게 만족하여, 그해 잉글랜드를 떠난 그 어느 짝 못지않게 즐거운 여름 유람을 누렸다. 조지는 늘 연극을 보러 따라왔지만, 공연이 끝나고 에미의 숄을 어깨에 걸쳐 주는 이는 소령이었다. 그리고 산책이나 나들이를 할 때면 그 소년은 앞장서 탑 계단이나 나무 위로 올라가 있었고, 그동안 좀 더 진중한 두 사람은 아래에서, 소령은 더없이 차분하고 한결같이 시가를 피우고 에미는 그 자리며 폐허를 스케치하곤 했다. 한 마디 한 마디가 모두 진실인 이 이야기를 지금 적고 있는 나 자신이, 그들을 처음 보고 알게 되는 기쁨을 누린 것도 바로 이 유람에서였다.\n\n내가 도빈 대령과 그 일행을 처음 본 것은, 아담하고 안락한 공국의 고을 품퍼니켈에서였다(다름 아닌, 피트 크롤리 경이 외교관 수습으로 그토록 두각을 나타냈던 바로 그곳인데, 그건 아주 아주 옛날, 아우스터리츠 전투 소식이 독일에 있던 잉글랜드 외교관들을 죄다 우향우시켜 돌려보내기 이전의 일이었다). 그들은 마차와 안내인을 거느리고 그 고을 최고의 여관인 에르프린츠 호텔에 도착했고, 일행 모두 정찬에 함께 자리했다. 누구나 조스의 위풍당당함과, 그가 정찬용으로 시킨 요하니스베르거 포도주를 홀짝이는, 아니 빨아 마시는 그 능청스러운 솜씨를 눈여겨보았다. 우리는 그 어린 소년에게도 굉장한 식욕이 있어서, 햄이며 구운 고기며 감자며 크랜베리 잼이며 샐러드며 푸딩이며 통닭 구이며 과자며를, 제 나라의 명예에 누가 되지 않을 만큼 늠름하게 먹어 치우는 것을 보았다. 그는 열다섯 접시쯤 비운 뒤 후식으로 식사를 마무리했는데, 그 후식 중 일부는 아예 바깥으로 들고 나가기까지 했다. 식탁에 앉아 있던 젊은 신사 몇몇이 그의 능청스러움과 의젓하고 거리낌 없는 태도에 재미를 느껴, 마카롱 한 줌을 호주머니에 챙기라고 부추겼던 것이다. 그는 명랑하고 사람 좋은 그 자그마한 독일 고을에서 다들 향하는 극장으로 가는 길에 그 마카롱을 처치했다. 검은 옷을 입은 부인, 그 소년의 어머니는 정찬이 이어지는 내내, 또 제 아들이 부린 갖가지 묘기며 장난스러운 짓을 보며 웃고 얼굴을 붉히고, 더없이 흐뭇하면서도 수줍어하는 기색이었다. 대령은 — 그는 그 뒤로 곧 대령이 되었으니 — 내 기억으로는 자못 진지한 익살을 부려 그 소년을 놀려 댔으니, 아직 먹어 보지 않은 요리를 일러 주며, 식욕을 꺾지 말고 이것저것 한 번 더 청해 먹으라고 권하는 것이었다.\n\n그날 밤은 품퍼니켈 대공 전하의 궁정 극장에서 이른바 객원 출연의 밤이라, 당시 그 미모와 천재성이 한창 무르익은 슈뢰더 데브리엔트 여사가 저 경이로운 오페라 \"피델리오\"에서 여주인공 역을 맡았다. 우리는 일층 좌석에서, 에르프린츠 호텔의 슈벤들러가 제일 귀한 손님들을 위해 잡아 두는 그 특별석에 앉은, 정찬 자리에서 본 우리의 네 친구를 바라볼 수 있었다. 그리고 나는 그 빼어난 여배우와 음악이 오즈번 부인에게 — 콧수염 기른 그 뚱뚱한 신사가 그녀를 그렇게 부르는 것을 들었던 터라 — 자아내는 효과를 눈여겨보지 않을 수 없었다. 그 여배우의 황홀한 목소리가 더없이 매혹적인 화음으로 솟구쳐 오르던 저 놀라운 죄수들의 합창 대목에서, 그 잉글랜드 부인의 얼굴에는 어찌나 경이와 환희에 찬 표정이 어렸던지, 시들해진 외교관 어린 핍스마저도 거기에 마음이 동해, 그녀에게 안경을 들이대며 느릿느릿 내뱉었다. \"허 참, 저토록 감동할 줄 아는 여자를 보니 정말 마음이 흐뭇해지는걸.\" 그리고 피델리오가 제 남편에게 달려가며 \"안 돼요, 안 돼요, 내 플로레스탄\"이라고 외치는 감옥 장면에서, 그녀는 완전히 정신을 잃고 손수건으로 얼굴을 가렸다. 그때 극장 안의 여인이란 여인은 죄다 훌쩍거리고 있었지만, 내가 유독 그녀를 눈여겨본 것은 아마도 바로 이 부인의 회고록을 쓰도록 미리 정해진 운명이었기 때문이리라.\n\n이튿날에는 베토벤의 또 다른 곡, \"비토리아 전투\"를 연주했다. 공연 첫머리에는 프랑스군의 기세 좋은 진격을 나타내는 표지로 \"말브룩\"이 도입되었다. 이어 북소리, 나팔 소리, 우레 같은 포성, 죽어 가는 자들의 신음이 잇따랐고, 마침내 장엄하게 한껏 부풀어 오르며 \"신이여 임금을 지키소서\"가 연주되었다.\n\n극장 안에 잉글랜드 사람이라곤 스무 명쯤밖에 없었을 것이다. 그러나 그 정겹고 익숙한 곡조가 터져 나오자, 그 한 사람 한 사람이, 일층 좌석에 앉은 우리 젊은 패거리며, (아홉 자식을 교육시키려 품퍼니켈에 집을 얻은) 존 불민스터 경 부부며, 콧수염 기른 뚱뚱한 신사며, 흰 무명 바지를 입은 키 큰 소령이며, 그가 그토록 살뜰히 챙기던 어린 소년을 데리고 온 부인이며, 심지어 위층 관람석에 있던 안내인 키르슈까지, 다들 제자리에서 꼿꼿이 일어나 자기들이 정겨운 옛 영국 민족의 일원임을 천명했다. 대리공사 테이프웜으로 말하면, 그는 마치 제국 전체를 대표하기라도 하는 양 제 특별석에서 일어나 절을 하고 알랑거리는 미소를 지었다. 테이프웜은 늙은 팁토프 원수의 조카이자 상속자였는데, 이 원수는 이 이야기에서 워털루 직전에 팁토프 장군으로 소개된 바 있는 인물로, 도빈 소령이 복무하던 ××연대의 연대장이었으며, 올해 온갖 영예를 누리다가 물떼새 알 젤리를 먹고 세상을 떠났다. 그 뒤 폐하께서는 황공하게도 그 연대를, 숱한 영광스러운 전장에서 그것을 지휘했던 바스 훈위 받은 마이클 오다우드 대령 경에게 하사하셨다.\n\n테이프웜은 분명 그 대령의 연대장인 원수의 댁에서 도빈 대령을 만난 적이 있을 것이다. 그날 밤 극장에서 그를 알아보았으니 말이다. 그리고 폐하의 공사는 더없이 황송한 겸양을 베풀어, 제 특별석에서 건너와 새로 찾은 벗과 공공연히 악수를 나누었다.\n\n\"저 흉악한 능구렁이 테이프웜 좀 보게나.\" 핍스가 일층 좌석에서 제 상관을 살피며 속삭였다. \"어디든 예쁜 여자만 있으면 어김없이 비집고 들어간다니까.\" 한데 외교관이란 본디 그러라고 만들어진 게 아니라면 도대체 무엇하러 있단 말인가?\n\n\"도빈 부인을 뵙는 영광을 누리는 것이 맞습니까?\" 그 서기관이 더없이 알랑거리는 웃음을 지으며 물었다.\n\n조지가 웃음을 터뜨리며 말했다. \"거참, 그거 한 방 먹였네.\" 에미와 소령은 얼굴을 붉혔다. 우리는 일층 좌석에서 그것을 보았다.\n\n\"이 부인은 조지 오즈번 부인입니다.\" 소령이 말했다. \"그리고 이쪽은 그 오라버니 되시는 세들리 씨로, 벵골 문관청의 저명한 관리이십니다. 각하께 소개하는 영광을 베풀어 주십시오.\"\n\n각하께서는 더없이 매혹적인 미소로 조스가 거의 다리에 힘이 풀리게 만들었다. \"품퍼니켈에 머무르실 작정입니까?\" 그가 말했다. \"따분한 곳이긴 합니다만, 우리에겐 멋진 분들이 좀 필요하니, 여러분께 이곳을 더없이 즐거운 곳으로 만들어 드리도록 애써 보지요. 어 — 음 — 그러니까 — 오호. 내일 여관으로 찾아뵙는 영광을 누리겠습니다.\" 그러고는 오즈번 부인을 완전히 끝장내 버리리라 자신하는 작별의 능청스러운 미소와 눈길을 던지며 자리를 떴다.\n\n공연이 끝나자, 우리 젊은 패거리는 로비를 어슬렁거렸고, 우리는 그 사교계 인사들이 떠나는 것을 지켜보았다. 노공작 미망인은 충직하고 늙어 시든 시녀 둘과, 갈색 가발에 갖가지 훈장으로 뒤덮인 초록 외투를 걸친, 코담배에 절은 비쩍 마른 다리의 자그마한 시종 하나를 거느리고 짤랑거리는 낡은 마차에 올라 떠나갔는데, 그 훈장 가운데서도 품퍼니켈 성 미카엘 훈장의 별과 큼직한 노란 띠가 가장 눈에 띄었다. 북소리가 울리고, 근위병들이 받들어총을 하자, 그 낡은 마차가 떠나갔다.\n\n이어서 더없이 투명하신 공작 전하와 그 투명하신 가족이, 국정과 궁정의 고관대작들을 거느리고 나왔다. 그는 누구에게나 잔잔하게 절을 했다. 그리고 근위병들의 받들어총과 진홍색 옷을 입은 횃불잡이 시종들의 활활 타오르는 횃불 속에서, 그 투명하신 마차들은 성채 언덕 위에 탑과 첨봉을 우뚝 세운 옛 공작 성을 향해 달려갔다. 품퍼니켈에서는 누구나 서로를 다 알았다. 외지인이 그곳에 나타나기가 무섭게 외무 장관이나, 아니면 다른 크고 작은 관리 하나가 에르프린츠 호텔로 돌아다니며 그 새 손님의 이름을 알아냈다.\n\n우리도 그들이 극장에서 나오는 것을 지켜보았다. 테이프웜은 이미 망토를 두르고 걸어 나간 뒤였는데, 그 망토는 언제나 그의 거구의 사냥꾼 하인이 들고 시중을 들었고, 그 모습은 영락없이 돈 후안 같았다. 총리의 부인은 막 자기 가마에 몸을 욱여넣은 참이었고, 그 딸인 매력적인 이다는 두건 모자를 쓰고 나막신을 신은 차림이었다. 그때 잉글랜드 일행이 나왔으니, 소년은 따분한 듯 하품을 하고, 소령은 오즈번 부인의 머리 위로 숄을 잘 여며 주느라 무던히도 애를 쓰고, 세들리 씨는 접이식 오페라 모자를 머리 한쪽에 비스듬히 얹고, 폭 넉넉한 흰 조끼의 배 부분에 한 손을 찔러 넣은 채 위풍당당하게 보였다. 우리는 정찬 자리에서 알게 된 이들에게 모자를 벗어 인사했고, 그 부인은 답례로 자그마한 미소와 무릎 굽혀 인사를 건넸으니, 누구라도 고마워할 만한 것이었다.\n\n분주한 키르슈 씨의 지휘 아래, 여관에서 온 마차가 일행을 모시려 대기하고 있었다. 그러나 그 뚱뚱한 남자는 집으로 가는 길에 걸어가며 시가를 피우겠다고 하여, 나머지 셋은 우리에게 고갯짓과 미소를 보내며 세들리 씨 없이 떠나갔고, 키르슈는 시가 상자를 들고 제 주인의 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n우리는 다 함께 걸으며 그 뚱뚱한 신사에게 그곳의 즐길 거리에 대해 이야기를 나누었다. 잉글랜드 사람들에게는 더없이 쾌적한 곳이었다. 사냥 모임이며 몰이사냥이 있었고, 손님 후한 궁정에서는 무도회와 연회가 풍성했으며, 사교계도 대체로 훌륭했고, 극장은 더없이 빼어났으며, 생활비는 저렴했다.\n\n\"그리고 우리 공사 각하께서도 더없이 유쾌하고 상냥한 분이신 것 같더군요.\" 우리의 새 친구가 말했다. \"그런 분이 우리를 대표하고, 또 — 또 솜씨 좋은 의사 양반까지 있다면, 이곳이 더없이 살 만한 곳이라 여겨집니다그려. 그럼 안녕히들 주무십시오, 신사 여러분.\" 그러고는 조스가 삐걱삐걱 계단을 올라 잠자리로 향했고, 키르슈가 횃불을 들고 그 뒤를 따랐다. 우리는 그 어여쁜 부인이 이 고을에 한동안 머물도록 이끌리기를 자못 바랐다.\n\n오랜 지인을 만나다\n\n테이프웜 경의 그토록 정중한 처신은 세들리 씨의 마음에 더없이 호의적인 영향을 미치지 않을 수 없었다. 바로 이튿날 아침 식사 자리에서 그는 펌퍼니클이야말로 이번 여행에서 들른 그 어느 곳보다 가장 유쾌한 작은 고장이라는 의견을 내놓았던 것이다. 조스의 속셈과 술수란 그리 알아채기 어려운 것이 아니었으니, 도빈은 위선자답게 — 실제로 그는 위선자였다 — 속으로 웃었다. 조스가 그 아침에 벌써 일어나 휴대용 귀족 명감을 들춰 보았다는 사실을, 이 인도 관리의 짐짓 아는 체하는 태도와 테이프웜 성이며 그 가문의 다른 일원들을 거침없이 입에 올리는 무심한 말투에서 알아차렸기 때문이다. 그렇다, 그는 그분의 부친이신 배그위그 백작 각하를 뵌 적이 있었다. 분명히 뵈었고, 그분을 — 그 알현식에서 만났는데 — 도빈은 기억나지 않느냐는 것이었다. 그리고 그 외교관이 약속을 지켜 일행을 찾아오자, 조스는 그 자그마한 공사에게 좀처럼 베풀어지지 않는 경례와 예우로 그를 맞이했다. 그는 각하가 도착하자 키르슈에게 눈짓을 보냈고, 미리 지시받은 그 심부름꾼은 밖으로 나가 쟁반에 담겨 들어오는 냉육이며 젤리며 그 밖의 진미들로 차린 다과상을 지휘 감독했으니, 조스 씨는 자신의 고귀한 손님이 반드시 그것을 들어야 한다고 한사코 우겼던 것이다.\n\n테이프웜은 오스본 부인의 빛나는 두 눈을 — 그 산뜻한 안색은 대낮에도 놀랄 만큼 잘 견뎌 냈다 — 흠모할 기회를 누릴 수 있는 한, 세들리 씨의 거처에 머물러 달라는 그 어떤 초대도 기꺼이 받아들였다. 그는 세들리 씨에게 인도며 그곳의 무희들에 관해 한두 가지 능란한 질문을 던졌고, 애밀리아에게는 그녀와 함께 있던 그 아름다운 소년에 대해 물었으며, 그녀가 이 집안에 일으킨 어마어마한 반향을 두고 어리둥절해하는 이 자그마한 부인에게 찬사를 보냈다. 또한 지난 전쟁과, 지금은 펌퍼니클 공작이 된 당시의 세습 공자 휘하에서 펌퍼니클 파견 부대가 세운 무공을 화제 삼아 도빈을 매료시키려 들었다.\n\n테이프웜 경은 그 가문 특유의 여성 편력 기질을 적잖이 물려받은 사람이었고, 자신이 친근한 눈길을 던지는 여인이라면 거의 누구나 자기에게 반했다고 믿는 행복한 신념의 소유자였다. 그는 에미를 두고 자신의 재치와 매력에 그녀가 넘어갔다고 굳게 믿으며 자리를 떴고, 거처로 돌아가서는 그녀에게 보낼 예쁘장한 짧은 쪽지를 썼다. 그러나 그녀는 그의 히죽거리는 웃음과 새치름한 미소, 향내 나는 고급 무명 손수건, 굽 높은 칠피 부츠에 매료되기는커녕 그저 어리둥절할 따름이었다. 그녀는 그가 늘어놓는 찬사의 절반도 알아듣지 못했다. 사람을 겪어 본 그 짧은 경험 안에서 그녀는 직업적인 여성 편력가를 아직 한 번도 만나 본 적이 없었던 것이다. 그래서 그녀는 이 나리를 유쾌하다기보다 신기한 존재쯤으로 여겼고, 흠모하지는 않았더라도 신기해한 것만은 분명했다. 반면 조스는 더없이 기뻐했다. \"각하께서는 어찌나 상냥하신지.\" 그가 말했다. \"의사 양반을 보내 주시겠다니, 각하께서 어찌나 친절하신지! 키르슈, 자네는 당장 슐뤼셀바흐 백작께 우리 명함을 전하게. 소령과 나는 되도록 빨리 궁정에 문안을 올리는 더없는 영광을 누리겠네. 내 예복을 꺼내 두게, 키르슈 — 우리 둘 예복 다 말일세. 영국 신사라면 누구나, 자신이 방문하는 나라의 군주들께 자기 나라 대표를 대하듯 문안을 올리는 것이 마땅히 보여야 할 예의의 표시지.\"\n\n테이프웜의 주치의가 왔으니, 공작 전하의 시의인 폰 글라우버 박사였다. 그는 펌퍼니클의 광천수와 자신의 특별 치료법이라면 이 벵골 신사를 틀림없이 젊음과 날씬함으로 되돌려 놓으리라고 조스를 금세 납득시켰다. \"작년에 여기 오신 분이 있디오.\" 그가 말했다. \"벌클리 장군이라고, 영국 장군인데, 선생보다 두 배는 뚱뚱했디오. 석 달 만에 아주 홀쭉하게 만들어 돌려보냈고, 두 달 만에 그분은 글라우버 남작 부인과 춤을 추었디오.\"\n\n조스의 마음은 정해졌다. 광천수며 박사며 궁정이며 대리 공사가 그를 설득해 냈으니, 그는 이 유쾌한 거처에서 가을을 보내기로 작정했다. 그리고 약속에 어김없이, 이튿날 대리 공사는 궁정 의전관인 슐뤼셀바흐 백작의 안내를 받아 조스와 소령을 빅토르 아우렐리우스 17세께 알현시켰다.\n\n그들은 곧바로 궁정 만찬에 초대받았고, 이 고장에 머물 작정이라는 소식이 알려지자 온 도시에서 가장 정중한 부인들이 즉각 오스본 부인을 예방했다. 그리고 이들 중에는 아무리 가난한 이라도 남작 부인보다 신분이 낮은 자가 하나도 없었으니, 조스의 기쁨은 이루 말할 수가 없었다. 그는 클럽의 처트니에게 편지를 부쳐, 독일에서는 인도 근무 경력이 대단히 높이 평가받고 있다는 것, 자신이 벗인 슐뤼셀바흐 백작에게 인도식으로 멧돼지를 창으로 찌르는 법을 가르쳐 줄 작정이라는 것, 그리고 자신의 존귀한 벗인 공작 부처가 더없이 친절하고 정중하더라는 것을 알렸다.\n\n에미 또한 그 존귀한 가문에 소개되었으니, 특정한 날에는 궁정에서 상복이 허용되지 않으므로, 그녀는 오라비가 선물한 다이아몬드 장식을 가슴께에 단 분홍 크레이프 드레스 차림으로 나섰다. 이 의상을 걸친 그녀가 어찌나 어여뻤던지, 공작과 궁정 사람들은 — 그녀가 야회복을 입은 모습을 거의 본 적이 없어 도무지 스물다섯 살로는 보이지 않는다고 장담한 소령은 논외로 치더라도 — 모두 그녀를 지나치게 흠모했다.\n\n이 드레스 차림으로 그녀는 궁정 무도회에서 도빈 소령과 폴로네즈를 추었는데, 이 느긋한 춤에서 조스 씨는 슐뤼셀바흐 백작 부인을 인도하는 영광을 누렸다. 등이 굽은 노부인이었지만 열여섯 대를 거슬러 올라가는 어엿한 귀족 혈통을 지녔고, 독일 왕가의 절반과 연이 닿은 분이었다.\n\n펌퍼니클은 어느 행복한 골짜기 한복판에 자리하고 있는데, 그 골짜기를 가로질러 비옥한 펌프 강 줄기가 반짝이며 흐른다 — 어딘가에서 라인 강과 합류하긴 하지만, 지금 지도가 손에 없어 정확히 어느 지점인지는 말할 수 없다. 어떤 곳에서 이 강은 나룻배 한 척을 띄울 만큼 넓고, 또 어떤 곳에서는 물방앗간 하나를 돌릴 만하다. 펌퍼니클 시 자체에는, 지금으로부터 네 분 전의 전하이신 위대하고 명성 높은 빅토르 아우렐리우스 14세께서 웅장한 다리 하나를 세우셨으니, 그 위에는 물의 요정들과 승리, 평화, 풍요의 상징들에 둘러싸인 그분 자신의 조각상이 솟아 있다. 그분은 엎드린 터키인의 목을 발로 밟고 있는데 — 역사에 따르면 소비에스키의 빈 구원전 당시 그분이 한 예니체리 병사와 맞붙어 그 몸을 창으로 꿰뚫었다고 한다 — 그러나 발치에서 더없이 끔찍한 모습으로 몸부림치는 그 엎드린 마호메트교도의 고통 따위에는 전혀 아랑곳없이, 공자는 부드럽게 미소 지으며 지휘봉으로 아우렐리우스 광장 쪽을 가리키고 있다. 바로 그곳에 그분이 새 궁전을 짓기 시작했으니, 만약 이 도량 넓은 공자께 그것을 완성할 자금만 있었더라면 당대의 경이가 되었을 터였다. 그러나 몽플레지르(정직한 독일 사람들은 몽블레지르라 부른다)의 완공은 현금 부족으로 중단되었고, 그것과 그 정원이며 뜰은 이제 다소 빛바랜 상태에 놓여, 현 군주의 궁정을 수용하기에 열 배 이상은 너끈할 정도밖에 안 되게 되었다.\n\n정원은 베르사유의 그것을 본떠 꾸며졌으니, 테라스와 숲 사이로는 거대한 우의적 분수 장치 몇 점이 아직도 남아 있어, 축제일이면 어마어마하게 물을 뿜고 거품을 일으키며 그 엄청난 물의 봉기로 사람을 놀라게 한다. 트로포니우스의 동굴이 있는데, 거기서는 어떤 장치로 인해 납으로 된 트리톤들이 물을 뿜을 뿐 아니라 자신들의 납 소라고둥에서 더없이 끔찍한 신음을 토해 내도록 되어 있다. 또 요정의 목욕탕과 나이아가라 폭포가 있으니, 인근 주민들은 의회가 열리는 시기의 연례 장날에 오거나, 이 행복한 작은 나라가 여전히 자기네 공자 통치자들의 생일과 결혼일을 기념하는 축제 때 와서 이것들을 이루 말할 수 없이 흠모하는 것이다.\n\n그러면 거의 십 마일에 걸쳐 뻗어 있는 이 공국의 온 도시에서 — 프로이센에 도전하듯 서쪽 변경에 자리한 볼쿰에서, 공자가 사냥용 별장을 둔 그로크비츠에서, 그곳에서는 펌프 강이 그의 영토를 이웃 포첸탈 공의 영토와 갈라놓는데 — 이 세 대도시 말고도 이 행복한 공국에 점점이 흩어진 온갖 자그마한 마을들에서, 펌프 강을 따라 늘어선 농가와 물방앗간에서, 빨간 페티코트에 벨벳 머리쓰개를 한 사람들이며, 삼각모를 쓰고 입에 파이프를 문 사람들이 무리 지어, 수도로 몰려와 그곳의 장날과 축제의 즐거움을 함께 나눈다. 그러면 극장은 무료로 개방되고, 몽블레지르의 분수가 물을 뿜기 시작하며(그것을 구경할 일행이 있다는 건 다행스러운 일이다, 혼자 본다면 무서울 테니까), 그러면 약장수들과 곡마단이 찾아온다(전하께서 그 곡마 단원 하나에게 매혹되신 사연은 널리 알려져 있고, 사람들이 라 프티트 비방디에르라 부르던 그 여자가 프랑스 측 첩자였다는 설이 있다). 그러면 들뜬 사람들은 대공 궁전의 이 방 저 방을 줄지어 행진하며 미끄러운 마룻바닥이며 화려한 휘장이며 그 수많은 방마다 문가에 놓인 타구를 흠모하도록 허락받는다. 몽블레지르에는 아우렐리우스 빅토르 15세께서 — 위대한 공자였으나 향락을 지나치게 좋아하셨던 분이다 — 꾸미신 별관이 하나 있는데, 내가 듣기로는 음탕한 우아함의 완벽한 경이라 한다. 그곳은 바쿠스와 아리아드네의 이야기로 그려져 있고, 식탁은 권양기를 이용해 방 안팎으로 드나들게 되어 있어, 일행은 하인의 시중을 전혀 받지 않고도 식사 대접을 받을 수 있었다. 그러나 이곳은 아우렐리우스 15세의 미망인 바르바라에 의해 폐쇄되었으니, 볼쿰 가문 출신의 엄격하고 독실한 공녀이자, 향락의 절정에서 꺾인 남편이 세상을 떠난 뒤 아들의 영광스러운 미성년기 동안 공국을 다스린 섭정이었다.\n\n펌퍼니클의 극장은 독일의 그 지방에서 알려지고 또 유명한 곳이다. 현 공작이 젊은 시절 자신이 작곡한 오페라를 그곳에서 상연하라고 우긴 탓에 한동안 침체되기도 했다. 들리는 말로는 어느 날 그가 연습을 참관하다가 오케스트라석에서 격분하여, 너무 느리게 지휘하던 악장의 머리를 바순으로 내리쳐 부러뜨렸다고 한다. 그동안 소피아 공작 부인은 가정극을 썼는데, 그것을 지켜보기란 무척이나 따분한 노릇이었을 게 틀림없다. 그러나 공자는 이제 자신의 음악을 사사로이 연주하고, 공작 부인은 그저 자신의 친절한 작은 궁정을 찾는 명망 높은 외국인들에게 자기 희곡을 나눠 줄 뿐이다.\n\n이 궁정은 적잖은 안락과 화려함으로 운영된다. 무도회가 열리면 만찬에 사백 명이 모인다 해도 네 사람마다 진홍색 정장에 레이스를 두른 하인 하나가 시중을 들고, 모두 은식기로 대접받는다. 축제와 연회가 끊임없이 이어지고, 공작에게는 시종들과 마부장들이 있으며 공작 부인에게는 의상 담당 상궁과 시녀들이 있으니, 다른 더 강성한 군주들과 다를 바가 없다.\n\n이 나라의 헌정 체제는 — 지금도 그렇거니와 — 의회로 다소 완화된 온건한 전제정이었는데, 그 의회란 선출될 수도 있고 안 될 수도 있는 것이었다. 적어도 내가 펌퍼니클에 머무는 동안 그 의회가 열렸다는 소리는 한 번도 듣지 못한 게 확실하다. 총리는 어느 건물 삼 층에 거처를 두었고, 외무 장관은 츠비바크 제과점 위층의 안락한 거처에 묵었다. 군대라곤 무대에서도 임무를 수행하는 멋들어진 악단 하나뿐이어서, 그 훌륭한 친구들이 연지를 바르고 나무 언월도를 차고 터키 복장으로 행진하는 모습이나, 오피클라이드와 트롬본을 든 로마 전사 차림으로 나오는 모습을 보는 것은 자못 유쾌한 노릇이었다 — 다시 말하건대, 아침 내내 아우렐리우스 광장에서 그들의 연주를 들은 뒤에 — 그곳에서 그들은 우리가 아침을 먹던 카페 맞은편에서 연주했다 — 밤에 다시 그들을 보는 것 말이다. 악단 말고도 풍성하고 수많은 장교진이 있었고, 내 생각엔 사병도 몇 명 있었던 것 같다. 정규 보초들 말고도, 후사르 차림을 한 서너 명이 궁전에서 근무하곤 했으나, 나는 그들이 말 탄 모습을 한 번도 본 적이 없다. 하긴, 깊은 평화의 시절에 기병이 무슨 소용이며 — 도대체 후사르들이 어디로 말을 달려간단 말인가?\n\n누구나 — 물론 귀족 신분인 이는 누구나, 부르주아로 말하자면 우리가 그들에게까지 관심을 둘 수야 없는 노릇이니 — 이웃을 방문했다. 폰 부르스트 부인 각하는 일주일에 한 번 손님을 맞았고, 폰 슈누르바르트 부인 각하는 자기 야회 날이 따로 있었다. 극장은 일주일에 두 번 열렸고, 궁정은 너그럽게도 일주일에 한 번 손님을 맞았으니, 실로 사람이 이 허세 없는 펌퍼니클식 방식대로 완벽하게 즐거움이 끊이지 않는 삶을 누릴 수 있는 셈이었다.\n\n이곳에 반목이 없었다고는 누구도 부인할 수 없을 것이다. 펌퍼니클에서는 정치가 대단히 격렬했고, 당파 간의 알력도 몹시 신랄했다. 슈트룸프 파와 레더룽 파가 있었으니, 전자는 우리 공사가 후원하고 후자는 프랑스 대리 공사 마카보 씨가 후원했다. 사실 우리 공사로서는, 둘 중 분명 더 뛰어난 가수이고 경쟁자인 레더룽 부인보다 음역이 세 음이나 높은 슈트룸프 부인의 편을 들기만 하면 그만이었다 — 다시 말하건대, 우리 공사가 무슨 의견을 내놓기만 하면 그 프랑스 외교관이 즉각 반박하기에 충분했다는 말이다.\n\n도시의 모든 이가 이 두 파 가운데 어느 한쪽에 줄을 섰다. 레더룽은 확실히 제법 어여쁘고 자그마한 여자였고, 그 목소리는(있는 만큼은) 무척 감미로웠다. 그리고 슈트룸프가 첫 젊음과 미모의 시절을 지난 데다 분명히 너무 뚱뚱했다는 것도 의심할 여지가 없다. 가령 그녀가 「몽유병의 여인」 마지막 장면에서 잠옷 차림으로 손에 등잔을 들고 나와 창문을 넘어 물방앗간 널빤지를 건너야 할 때면, 창문으로 몸을 비집고 나오는 것만도 그녀로선 힘에 부쳤고, 널빤지는 그 무게에 휘어지며 다시금 삐걱대곤 했다 — 그러나 그녀가 오페라의 피날레를 얼마나 쏟아 내듯 불렀던가! 또 얼마나 북받치는 감정으로 엘비노의 품에 뛰어들었던가 — 그를 거의 질식시킬 지경이었다! 반면 자그마한 레더룽으로 말하자면 — 그러나 이런 잡담은 이쯤 해 두자 — 사실인즉 이 두 여자는 펌퍼니클에서 프랑스 파와 영국 파의 두 깃발이었고, 사교계는 이 두 위대한 나라에 대한 충성을 두고 둘로 갈라져 있었던 것이다.\n\n우리 편에는 내무 장관, 마사관, 공작의 비서, 공자의 가정교사가 있었던 반면, 프랑스 파에는 외무 장관과, 나폴레옹 휘하에서 복무했던 총사령관의 부인, 그리고 궁정 의전관과 그의 아내가 있었으니, 이 아내는 파리에서 유행을 들여오는 것을 기뻐하여 늘 마카보 씨의 급사를 통해 유행 물품과 모자를 받아 보았다. 그 공사관 비서는 자그마한 그리냐크라는 젊은이로, 사탄처럼 짓궂어서 이 고장의 온갖 앨범마다 테이프웜의 풍자화를 그려 넣곤 했다.\n\n그들의 본거지와 정식 회식 장소는 이 도시의 다른 여관인 파리저 호프에 자리하고 있었다. 그리고 물론 이 신사들은 공석에서야 정중하게 굴 수밖에 없었지만, 마치 데번셔에서 한 쌍의 씨름꾼이 서로의 정강이를 후려치면서도 얼굴 근육 하나 까딱 않고 고통을 감추는 것을 내가 본 것처럼, 면도날같이 예리한 경구로 서로를 베어 댔다. 테이프웜도 마카보도 자기 정부에 공문을 보낼 때면 경쟁자에 대한 더없이 잔혹한 일련의 공격을 빼놓는 법이 없었다. 가령 우리 쪽에서는 이렇게 쓰는 것이었다. \"이곳, 그리고 독일 전역에서 영국의 이익은 현 프랑스 공사의 유임으로 위태로워지고 있습니다. 이자는 그 성품이 어찌나 파렴치한지, 제 목적을 이루기 위해서라면 어떤 거짓말도 마다하지 않고 어떤 죄악도 서슴지 않을 위인입니다. 그는 궁정의 마음에 영국 공사에 대한 독을 풀어 넣고, 영국의 처신을 더없이 가증스럽고 흉악한 것으로 그려 보이는데, 불행히도 그 무지와 궁핍이 그 영향력만큼이나 치명적이고 또 악명 높은 한 장관의 비호를 받고 있습니다.\" 그쪽 편에서는 또 이렇게 쓰는 것이었다. \"테이프웜 씨는 세계 최대의 국민을 상대로 멍청한 섬나라식 오만과 천박한 거짓말의 행태를 계속하고 있다. 어제 그는 베리 공작 부인 전하를 가볍게 입에 올리는 것이 목격되었고, 이전에는 영웅적인 앙굴렘 공작을 모욕했으며 오를레앙 공작 전하께서 백합의 존엄한 왕좌에 맞서 음모를 꾸미고 있다고 감히 넌지시 비추었다. 그의 황금은 그 멍청한 위협이 겁주는 데 실패한 모든 방면에 헤프게 뿌려진다. 이런저런 수단으로 그는 이곳 궁정의 앞잡이들을 매수해 왔으니 — 요컨대 이 독사를 발뒤꿈치로 짓밟아 버리기 전에는 펌퍼니클도 잠잠해지지 않고, 독일도 평온해지지 않으며, 프랑스도 존중받지 못하고, 유럽도 만족하지 못할 것이다.\" 등등. 어느 한쪽이 유달리 자극적인 공문을 쓰면, 그 소식은 으레 새어 나가게 마련이었다.\n\n겨울이 미처 깊어지기도 전에, 에미가 자기 야회 날을 정해 더없이 단정하고 겸손하게 손님을 맞았다는 것이 실제로 기록에 남아 있다. 그녀에게는 프랑스어 선생이 있었는데, 그는 그녀의 정확한 발음과 빠른 습득력을 칭찬했다. 사실 그녀는 오래전에 이미 배웠고, 그 후 조지를 가르칠 수 있도록 문법을 스스로 다져 두었던 것이다. 그리고 슈트룸프 부인이 와서 노래 수업을 해 주었는데, 그녀가 어찌나 노래를 잘하고 또 그 목소리가 어찌나 진실했던지, 총리 댁 아래층 맞은편에 거처를 둔 소령의 창문은 그 수업을 들으려 늘 열려 있었다. 취향이 무척 감상적이고 소박한 독일 부인들 몇몇은 그녀에게 반해서 대뜸 그녀를 너라고 친근하게 부르기 시작했다. 이런 것들은 사소한 자질구레한 이야기지만, 행복한 시절에 얽힌 것들이다. 소령은 스스로 조지의 가정교사가 되어 그와 함께 카이사르와 수학을 읽었고, 그들에게는 독일어 선생도 있었으며, 저녁이면 에미의 마차 곁에서 말을 타고 나갔다 — 그녀는 늘 너무 겁이 많아서 말 위에서 조금만 무슨 일이 생겨도 끔찍하게 비명을 질러 댔던 것이다. 그래서 그녀는 정다운 독일 친구 하나를 데리고 마차로 돌아다녔고, 조스는 사륜마차 뒷자리에서 잠들어 있었다.\n\n그는 그라핀 파니 데 부터브로트에게 무척 다정해지고 있었으니, 더없이 온순하고 마음씨 곱고 가식 없는 젊은 처자로, 어엿한 본인 자격의 수녀회원이자 백작 영애였지만 재산이라곤 일 년에 십 파운드도 채 안 되는 사람이었다. 그리고 파니는 파니대로 애밀리아의 자매가 되는 것이야말로 하늘이 자기에게 내려 줄 수 있는 가장 큰 기쁨이라고 단언했다. 조스로서는 자기 마차와 포크에 새겨진 자기 문장 옆에 백작 영애의 방패꼴 문장과 관 문장을 나란히 새겨 넣어도 좋았을 것이다 — 그런데 — 그런데 이런저런 사건이 일어났고, 펌퍼니클의 세습 공자와 사랑스러운 훔부르크슐리펜슐로펜의 애밀리아 공주의 결혼을 기념하는 그 성대한 축제가 거행되었던 것이다.\n\n이 축제에서 펼쳐진 화려함이란 저 낭비벽 심하던 빅토르 14세의 시절 이래로 이 작은 독일 고장에서 일찍이 본 적이 없는 것이었다. 인근의 모든 공자와 공녀와 귀족이 이 잔치에 초대되었다. 펌퍼니클에서는 잠자리 값이 하룻밤에 반 크라운까지 치솟았고, 사방에서 도착하는 전하며 각하며 나리들을 위해 의장대를 마련하느라 군대가 동났다. 공주는 부친의 거처에서 슐뤼셀바흐 백작을 통해 대리 혼례를 올렸다. 코담뱃갑이 아낌없이 하사되었고(이 사실은 그것을 팔았다가 나중에 도로 사들인 궁정 보석상에게서 우리가 알게 되었다), 펌퍼니클의 성 미카엘 훈장이 궁정 귀족들에게 수두룩이 보내졌으며, 우리 쪽으로는 슐리펜슐로펜의 성 카타리나 수레바퀴 훈장의 약장과 장식이 광주리째 들어왔다. 프랑스 공사는 둘 다 받았다. \"저자는 우승한 짐말처럼 리본으로 뒤덮였군.\" 테이프웜이 말했으니, 그는 직무 규정상 어떤 훈장도 받는 것이 허용되지 않는 사람이었다. \"약장이야 받으라지. 하지만 승리는 누구의 것인가?\" 사실인즉 이것은 영국 외교의 승리였으니, 프랑스 파가 포츠타우젠트도너베터 가문의 공녀와의 혼인을 제안하고 그것을 성사시키려 안간힘을 썼으나, 당연히 우리가 그것을 좌절시켰던 것이다.\n\n누구나 이 결혼 축제에 초대받았다. 어린 신부를 맞이하기 위해 길을 가로질러 화환과 개선문이 내걸렸다. 거대한 성 미카엘 분수에서는 유달리 신 포도주가 흘렀고, 포병 광장의 분수에서는 맥주가 거품을 일으켰다. 거대한 분수들이 물을 뿜었다. 또 행복한 농민들을 위해 공원과 정원에 장대가 세워졌으니, 그들이 마음껏 그것을 기어올라 꼭대기에 매단 시계며 은포크며 분홍 리본을 단 상품 소시지 따위를 떼어 갈 수 있게 한 것이었다. 조지도 하나를 얻었는데, 구경꾼들이 즐거워하는 가운데 장대를 기어올라 그것을 비틀어 떼어 낸 다음, 폭포수처럼 빠르게 미끄러져 내려왔다. 그러나 그것은 그저 영광을 위해서였다. 소년은 그 소시지를, 자기가 거의 다 잡을 뻔하다가 놓쳐 장대 밑에서 엉엉 울고 있던 한 농부에게 주어 버렸다.\n\n프랑스 공사관에서는 우리보다 등불을 여섯 개 더 달아 야간 조명을 밝혔다. 그러나 우리 쪽의 투명화는 젊은 부부가 앞으로 나아오고 '불화'가 달아나는 모습을 그린 것이었는데, 그 '불화'가 프랑스 대사를 더없이 우스꽝스럽게 빼닮은 까닭에 프랑스 쪽 그림을 완전히 압도했다. 그리고 나는 그것이 테이프웜에게, 그가 나중에 얻은 승진과 배스 훈장을 안겨 주었으리라 믿어 의심치 않는다.\n\n축제를 보러 외국인 무리가 몰려들었고, 물론 영국인도 몰려들었다. 궁정 무도회 말고도 시청과 레두트에서 공개 무도회가 열렸는데, 시청 쪽에는 트랑테카랑트와 룰렛을 할 수 있는 방이 마련되었으니, 오로지 이 축제 주간 동안만, 엠스나 엑스라샤펠에서 온 독일의 큰 회사 하나가 운영하는 것이었다. 이 고장의 장교나 주민은 이런 도박을 하는 것이 허락되지 않았지만, 외지인이며 농민이며 부인들은 입장할 수 있었고, 돈을 잃든 따든 마음 내키는 사람은 누구나 끼어들 수 있었다.\n\n그 가운데 저 자그마한 망나니 조지 오스본도 있었으니, 주머니에는 늘 달러가 가득했고 그 친척들은 궁정의 성대한 축제에 가 있던 차였다. 그는 외삼촌의 급사인 키르슈 씨를 따라 시청 무도회에 왔다. 그는 도빈의 팔에 매달려 바덴바덴의 도박장을 빠끔히 들여다본 적이 한 번 있었을 뿐이고, 거기서야 물론 노름이 허락되지 않았던 터라, 이 연회의 이 대목으로 신이 나서 달려와 노름 진행자들과 노름꾼들이 손을 놀리는 탁자 주위를 어슬렁거리며 군침을 흘렸다. 여자들도 노름을 하고 있었다. 그중 몇몇은 가면을 쓰고 있었으니, 이 광란의 사육제 시절에는 그런 방종이 허용되었던 것이다.\n\n밝은색 머리에, 이전만큼은 결코 산뜻하지 않은 가슴이 파인 드레스를 입고, 검은 가면을 쓴 한 여자가 — 그 가면의 눈구멍 사이로 그녀의 두 눈이 야릇하게 반짝였다 — 룰렛 탁자 하나에 앉아, 앞에 카드 한 장과 핀 하나와 플로린 두 닢을 놓아두고 있었다. 노름 진행자가 색깔과 숫자를 외칠 때마다 그녀는 그것을 무척 조심스럽고 규칙적으로 카드에 핀으로 콕콕 찍었고, 빨강이나 검정이 일정한 횟수만큼 나온 뒤에야 비로소 색깔에 돈을 걸어 보는 것이었다. 그녀를 바라보는 것은 야릇한 노릇이었다.\n\n그러나 그 조심성과 끈기에도 불구하고 그녀는 잘못 짚었으니, 마지막 두 플로린마저 노름 진행자의 갈퀴 밑으로 잇따라 끌려 들어갔고, 그는 그 가차 없는 목소리로 딴 색깔과 숫자를 외쳤다. 그녀는 한숨을 내쉬며, 이미 드레스 밖으로 너무 많이 드러난 어깨를 한 번 으쓱하더니, 카드 위에 핀을 푹 찔러 탁자에 꽂고는 한동안 그것을 손가락으로 퉁기며 앉아 있었다. 그러다 주위를 둘러보다가, 조지가 그 정직한 얼굴로 이 광경을 빤히 쳐다보고 있는 것을 보았다. 그 자그마한 개구쟁이! 녀석이 거기서 무얼 하고 있단 말인가?\n\n그 소년을 보자 그녀는, 반짝이는 두 눈과 가면 너머로 그 얼굴을 뚫어지게 바라보며 말했다. \"므슈는 노름꾼이 아니신가요?\"\n\n\"네, 마담.\" 소년이 말했다. 그러나 그녀는 그의 억양에서 그가 어느 나라 사람인지 분명 알아챘을 것이니, 약간 외국 말씨가 섞인 어조로 그에게 답했던 것이다. \"한 번도 노름을 해 보신 적이 없으시군요 — 제게 자그마한 부탁 하나 들어주시겠어요?\"\n\n\"뭔데요?\" 조지가 다시 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. 키르슈 씨는 자기대로 루주에누아르에 열중하느라 어린 주인을 보지 못했다.\n\n\"부디 이걸 저 대신 걸어 주세요. 아무 숫자에나, 아무 숫자에나요.\" 그러고는 가슴께에서 지갑을 꺼내, 그 안에서 거기 든 유일한 동전인 금화 한 닢을 꺼내어 조지의 손에 쥐여 주었다. 소년은 웃으며 시키는 대로 했다.\n\n그 숫자가 정말로 나왔다. 사람들 말로는 초심자를 위해 그런 일을 마련해 주는 어떤 힘이 있다고 한다.\n\n\"고마워요.\" 그녀가 그 돈을 자기 쪽으로 끌어당기며 말했다. \"고마워요. 이름이 뭐죠?\"\n\n\"제 이름은 오스본이에요.\" 조지가 말하며 자기 주머니에서 달러를 만지작거리다가 막 한 번 시험 삼아 걸어 보려던 참이었는데, 그때 예복 차림의 소령과, 후작 차림으로 궁정 무도회에서 온 조스가 모습을 드러냈다. 다른 사람들도 그 연회가 따분하다고 여기고 시청 쪽의 흥겨움을 더 좋아한 나머지 궁정 무도회를 일찌감치 떠난 터였다. 그러나 짐작건대 소령과 조스는 집에 돌아갔다가 소년이 없는 것을 알아챈 모양이었으니, 소령이 즉각 그에게 다가가 어깨를 붙잡고는 유혹의 장소에서 그를 휙 끌어냈던 것이다. 그러고는 방 안을 둘러보다가, 앞서 말한 대로 키르슈가 노름에 빠져 있는 것을 보고 그에게 다가가, 어찌 감히 조지 도련님을 이런 곳에 데려왔느냐고 따져 물었다.\n\n\"날 좀 가만 내버려 두쇼.\" 키르슈 씨가 노름과 술에 잔뜩 취해 말했다. \"즐겨야 할 거 아니오, 젠장. 난 나리 부하가 아니란 말이오.\"\n\n그의 상태를 본 소령은 그 사내와 다툴 마음이 내키지 않아, 그저 조지를 데려가는 것으로 만족하고는 조스에게 같이 가겠느냐고 물었다. 조스는 가면 쓴 여자 바로 곁에 서 있었는데, 그 여자는 이제 제법 운이 좋게 노름을 하고 있었고, 조스는 그 게임을 무척 흥미롭게 지켜보고 있었다.\n\n\"같이 가는 게 낫지 않겠소, 조스.\" 소령이 말했다. \"조지하고 나하고 말이오.\"\n\n\"난 남았다가 저 망나니 키르슈하고 같이 들어가겠소.\" 조스가 말했다. 그리고 소년 앞에서는 마땅히 지켜야 한다고 여긴 그 똑같은 체면 때문에, 도빈은 조스에게 굳이 잔소리할 마음이 내키지 않아, 그를 두고 조지와 함께 걸어서 집으로 갔다.\n\n\"너 노름했니?\" 두 사람이 밖으로 나와 집으로 가는 길에 소령이 물었다.\n\n소년은 \"아니요\"라고 말했다.\n\n\"신사로서 명예를 걸고 절대 하지 않겠다고 약속하렴.\"\n\n\"왜요?\" 소년이 말했다. \"무척 재미있어 보이던데요.\" 그러자 소령은 무척이나 설득력 있고 인상적인 태도로 그가 왜 노름을 해서는 안 되는지 일러 주었으니, 만약 조지의 친아버지에 대한 기억을 깎아내릴 만한 말을 하고 싶었더라면 바로 그 아비를 본보기로 들어 자신의 훈계를 못 박았을 것이다. 그는 소년을 집에 들여보낸 뒤 잠자리에 들었고, 애밀리아 방 바깥의 그 작은 방에서 새어 나오던 소년의 불빛이 이윽고 사라지는 것을 보았다. 애밀리아의 불빛은 그로부터 반 시간 뒤에 따라 꺼졌다. 무엇이 소령으로 하여금 그것을 그토록 정확히 눈여겨보게 했는지 나는 알지 못한다.\n\n그러나 조스는 노름 탁자에 남았다. 그는 노름꾼은 아니었으나 이따금 노름의 자그마한 흥분을 마다하지는 않는 편이었고, 궁정 조끼의 수놓인 주머니에는 나폴레옹 금화 몇 닢이 짤랑이고 있었다. 그는 앞에 앉은 그 자그마한 노름꾼 여자의 어여쁜 어깨너머로 한 닢을 걸었고, 그들은 땄다. 그녀는 자기 곁에 그가 앉을 자리를 마련해 주려고 살짝 몸을 움직이며, 거기 빈 의자에서 자기 드레스 자락을 막 거두어들였다.\n\n\"이리 오셔서 제게 행운을 가져다주세요.\" 그녀가 여전히 외국 말씨로 말했으니, 조지의 횡재를 두고 인사하며 건넸던 그 솔직하고 완벽하게 영국적인 \"고마워요\"와는 사뭇 다른 어조였다. 그 풍채 좋은 신사는 자기를 알아볼 만한 신분의 사람이 있는지 둘러보고는 자리에 앉았다. 그는 중얼거렸다 — \"아, 이거 참, 그러니까, 맙소사. 제가 운이 무척 좋군요. 분명 제가 부인께 행운을 가져다드릴 겁니다.\" 그러고는 또 다른 찬사와 횡설수설을 늘어놓았다. \"노름을 많이 하시나요?\" 그 가면 쓴 외국 여자가 말했다.\n\n\"한두 닢 걸어 봅니다.\" 조스가 거만한 태도로 말하며 금화 한 닢을 던졌다.\n\n\"그렇군요. 식후의 한두 닢이로군요.\" 가면이 짓궂게 말했다. 그러나 조스가 겁먹은 기색을 보이자 그녀는 그 어여쁜 프랑스 말씨로 말을 이었다. \"당신은 따려고 노름하시는 게 아니군요. 저도 그래요. 저는 잊으려고 노름하지만, 잊을 수가 없어요. 저는 옛 시절을 잊을 수가 없답니다, 므슈. 당신의 그 어린 조카는 그 아버지를 빼닮았더군요. 그리고 당신은 — 당신은 변하지 않으셨어요 — 아니, 변하셨네요. 누구나 변하고, 누구나 잊지요. 누구에게도 마음이라곤 없어요.\"\n\n\"맙소사, 대체 누구시오?\" 조스가 가슴을 떨며 물었다.\n\n\"못 알아보시겠어요, 조지프 세들리?\" 그 자그마한 여자가 서글픈 목소리로 말하며 가면을 벗고 그를 바라보았다. \"저를 잊으셨군요.\"\n\n\"세상에! 크롤리 부인!\" 조스가 숨을 헐떡이며 내뱉었다.\n\n\"리베카예요.\" 그 여자가 그의 손에 자기 손을 얹으며 말했다. 그러나 그녀는 그를 바라보는 내내 여전히 노름을 좇고 있었다.\n\n\"저는 엘레팡 여관에 묵고 있어요.\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"로던 부인을 찾으시면 돼요. 오늘 제 사랑하는 애밀리아를 봤어요. 어찌나 어여쁘고, 어찌나 행복해 보이던지! 당신도 그렇고요! 비참한 저만 빼고 다들 그렇네요, 조지프 세들리.\" 그러고는, 마치 손이 우연히 움직이기라도 한 듯 빨강에서 검정으로 돈을 옮겨 놓더니, 찢어진 레이스가 가장자리에 달린 손수건으로 눈가를 훔쳤다.\n\n빨강이 다시 나왔고, 그녀는 그 판돈 전부를 잃었다. \"이리 나와요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"잠깐 저와 함께 가요 — 우리는 오랜 친구잖아요, 그렇죠, 다정한 세들리 씨?\"\n\n그리고 키르슈 씨는 이때쯤 자기 돈을 몽땅 잃은 터라, 주인을 따라 달빛 속으로 나섰으니, 그곳에서는 조명들이 깜박이며 꺼져 가고 우리 공사관 위의 투명화도 거의 보이지 않았다.\n\n우리는 레베카 크롤리 부인의 전기 가운데 한 부분을, 세상이 요구하는 그 가벼움과 섬세함으로 건너뛰어야겠다. 여기서 세상이란 도덕의 세계를 말하는데, 그 세계는 악덕 자체에는 별다른 이의가 없을지 몰라도, 악덕을 제 이름 그대로 부르는 것을 듣는 일에는 이겨낼 수 없는 거부감을 품는다. 허영의 시장에는 우리가 행하고 또 빤히 알고 있으면서도 결코 입에 올리지 않는 일들이 있는 법이다. 마치 아리만교도가 악마를 숭배하되 그 이름을 입에 담지 않듯이 말이다. 점잖은 대중이란 악덕에 대한 진솔한 묘사를 차마 읽지 못하는 것이, 진정으로 세련된 영국이나 미국 여성이 그 정숙한 귀에 \"바지\"라는 낱말이 발음되는 것을 허락하지 못하는 것과 같다. 그런데 부인, 그 둘은 매일같이 우리 눈앞에서 세상을 활보하면서도 우리를 그다지 충격에 빠뜨리지 않는다. 만약 그것들이 지나갈 때마다 부인께서 얼굴을 붉히신다면, 그 안색이 대체 어찌 되겠는가! 오로지 그 못된 이름들이 소리 내어 불릴 때라야 비로소 부인의 정숙함이 놀라움이나 모욕감을 드러낼 까닭이 생기는 것이다. 그리하여 본 작가는 이 이야기를 쓰는 내내, 지금 널리 통용되는 그 유행에 공손히 따르고자, 그저 가볍고 편안하고 듣기 좋은 방식으로 사악함의 존재를 넌지시 암시하기만 하여, 그 누구의 고상한 감정도 거스르지 않으려 애써 왔다. 분명 얼마간 악덕을 지닌 우리의 베키가 더없이 품위 있고 거슬리지 않는 방식으로 대중에게 소개되지 않았다고 감히 누가 말할 수 있겠는가. 노래하고 미소 짓고, 어르고 구슬리는 이 세이렌을 묘사하면서, 작가는 겸허한 자부심으로 사방의 독자들에게 묻노니, 단 한 번이라도 예의의 법도를 잊고 그 괴물의 흉측한 꼬리를 수면 위로 드러낸 적이 있었던가? 천만에! 보고 싶은 이들은 제법 투명한 물결 아래를 들여다보고서, 그것이 악마처럼 흉측하고 끈적거리는 모습으로 몸부림치며 뒤틀리는 것을, 뼈들 사이에서 펄떡이거나 시체를 휘감는 것을 보아도 좋다. 그러나 수면 위로는, 묻노니, 모든 것이 점잖고 듣기 좋고 단정하지 않았던가, 그리고 허영의 시장에서 제아무리 까다로운 비도덕가라 한들 그것을 두고 망측하다 외칠 권리가 있겠는가? 그러나 세이렌이 사라져 물밑으로, 죽은 사내들 사이로 잠겨들면, 물론 그 위로 물이 탁해지는 법이니, 아무리 호기심에 차서 그 속을 들여다보아도 헛수고일 따름이다. 그것들은 바위 위에 앉아 하프를 뜯고 머리를 빗으며 노래하고, 어서 와서 거울을 들어 달라 손짓할 때는 제법 예뻐 보인다. 그러나 그것들이 제 본래의 터전으로 가라앉을 때면, 장담하건대 저 인어들은 결코 좋은 짓을 꾸미고 있는 게 아니니, 우리는 그 악마 같은 바다의 식인귀들이 가엾은 절임 신세가 된 제물들을 두고 흥청대며 잔치를 벌이는 꼴을 살펴보지 않는 편이 상책이다. 그러니 베키가 눈앞에서 사라졌을 때면, 그녀가 딱히 떳떳한 일에 종사하고 있지 않다는 것, 그리고 그녀의 행적에 관해서는 적게 이야기할수록 오히려 나으리라는 것을 명심하기 바란다.\n\n만약 우리가 커즌 거리의 그 파국에 뒤이은 이태 동안의 그녀의 행적을 낱낱이 늘어놓는다면, 사람들이 이 책더러 점잖지 못하다고 말할 만한 까닭이 생길지도 모른다. 몹시 허영에 차고, 무정하고, 쾌락만 좇는 사람들의 행동이란 대개 점잖지 못한 법이며(근엄한 얼굴에 흠 없는 평판을 지닌 나의 벗이여, 그대의 행동도 상당수가 그러하다네 — 그러나 이건 그저 지나가는 말일 뿐이다), 하물며 신앙도 사랑도 인품도 없는 여인의 행동이야 어떠하겠는가? 그리고 내 생각에는, 베키 부인의 생애에 어느 한 시기가 있었으니, 그때 그녀는 회한이 아니라 일종의 절망에 사로잡혀, 제 몸가짐을 완전히 내팽개치고 제 평판조차 아랑곳하지 않았던 것이다.\n\n이러한 의기소침과 타락은 단번에 닥쳐온 것이 아니었다. 그것은 그녀의 재앙 이후, 또 버텨 보려는 숱한 몸부림 끝에, 차츰차츰 이루어진 것이었다. 마치 배 밖으로 떨어진 사람이 조금이라도 희망이 남아 있는 동안에는 부유물에 매달려 있다가, 발버둥쳐 봐야 헛일임을 깨닫고는 그것을 내던지고 가라앉아 버리는 것처럼 말이다.\n\n그녀는 남편이 부임지로 떠날 채비를 하는 동안 런던 언저리에서 머뭇거렸고, 시아주버니인 피트 크롤리 경을 만나 그의 감정에 호소해 보려는 시도를 한두 번 했던 것으로 여겨진다. 사실 그녀는 거의 그의 마음을 자기편으로 끌어들였던 터였다. 피트 경과 웬햄 씨가 하원으로 걸어 내려가던 중에, 웬햄이 검은 베일을 쓴 로던 부인이 입법부 건물 가까이에서 어슬렁거리는 것을 발견했다. 그녀는 웬햄의 눈과 마주치자 슬그머니 자취를 감추었고, 결국 준남작에 대한 그 계획은 끝내 성공하지 못했다.\n\n아마도 제인 부인이 끼어든 것이리라. 듣자 하니 그녀는 이 싸움에서 보여 준 기개로, 그리고 베키 부인과의 인연을 끊겠다는 결연한 의지로 남편을 적잖이 놀라게 했다고 한다. 그녀는 자진하여 로던에게, 코번트리 섬으로 떠나기 전까지 곤트 거리에 와서 머물라고 청했는데, 그가 곁에서 지키고 있으면 베키 부인이 감히 문을 밀치고 들어오려 하지 않으리라는 것을 알았기 때문이다. 또한 그녀는 피트 경 앞으로 도착하는 모든 편지의 겉봉을 유심히 살폈으니, 그와 시누이가 서로 편지를 주고받지나 않을까 염려해서였다. 레베카가 마음만 먹었다면 편지를 쓸 수도 있었겠지만, 그녀는 피트의 집으로 그를 찾아가거나 편지를 보내려 하지 않았고, 한두 번 시도한 끝에는 부부간의 불화에 관한 연락은 오로지 변호사들을 통해서만 주고받자는 그의 요구에 동의했다.\n\n사실인즉 피트의 마음은 그녀에 대한 독으로 물들어 있었다. 스타인 경의 사고가 있은 지 얼마 지나지 않아 웬햄이 준남작을 찾아와, 퀸스크롤리의 그 의원을 깜짝 놀라게 할 만한 베키 부인의 전기를 들려주었던 것이다. 그는 그녀에 관한 모든 것을 알고 있었다. 그녀의 아버지가 누구였는지, 그녀의 어머니가 몇 년도에 오페라 무대에서 춤을 추었는지, 그녀의 지난 내력은 어떠했는지, 그리고 결혼 생활 동안의 처신은 또 어떠했는지까지. 이 이야기의 태반이 거짓이며 사사로운 악의에서 비롯된 것임을 나는 의심치 않으니, 여기서 되풀이하지는 않겠다. 그러나 베키는, 한때 그녀에게 제법 호의를 품었던 어느 시골 신사이자 친척의 마음속에 더없이 슬프고도 슬픈 평판만을 남기고 만 것이었다.\n\n코번트리 섬 총독의 수입은 많지 않았다. 그 일부는 각하께서 미결제로 남은 몇몇 빚과 채무를 갚는 데 떼어 두었고, 그 높은 지위에 따르는 비용도 상당히 들었다. 결국 따져 보니 그가 아내에게 떼어 줄 수 있는 돈은 연 삼백 파운드를 넘지 못했고, 그는 그녀가 다시는 자기를 괴롭히지 않겠다는 약속을 조건으로 그것을 지급하겠다고 제안했다. 그러지 않으면 추문이요, 별거요, 종교 법정 행이 뒤따를 터였다. 그러나 이 더없이 불쾌한 사건을 무마하고 그녀를 나라 밖으로 내보내는 일은 웬햄 씨의 일이요, 스타인 경의 일이요, 로던의 일이요, 그러니까 모두의 일이었던 것이다.\n\n그녀는 아마도 남편의 변호사들과 이러한 사무를 처리하는 데 너무 골몰한 나머지, 제 아들인 어린 로던에 관해서는 그 어떤 조치도 취하기를 잊었고, 한 번도 그를 보러 가겠다는 말조차 꺼내지 않았다. 그 어린 신사는 고모와 고모부의 온전한 후견에 맡겨졌는데, 그중 고모는 늘 그 아이의 애정을 가장 많이 차지해 온 터였다. 그의 어머니는 영국을 떠날 무렵 불로뉴에서 그에게 단정한 편지 한 통을 써 보내, 학업에 힘쓰라 당부하고는 자기는 대륙 여행을 떠날 참이며 그동안 다시 편지 쓰는 기쁨을 누리겠노라고 적었다. 그러나 그녀는 그 후로 일 년 동안 한 번도 편지하지 않았고, 늘 병약하던 피트 경의 외아들이 백일해와 홍역으로 세상을 떠나고 나서야 비로소 — 그제야 로던의 어머니는 사랑하는 아들에게 더없이 다정한 글을 써 보냈다. 이 불행한 사고로 아들은 퀸스크롤리의 상속자가 되었고, 이미 그 다정한 마음으로 아이를 거두어들였던 그 인자한 부인에게 그 어느 때보다 가까이 끌려 들어갔다. 어느덧 훤칠하고 잘생긴 소년으로 자란 로던 크롤리는 그 편지를 받고 얼굴을 붉혔다. \"오, 제인 고모, 고모가 제 어머니예요!\" 그는 말했다. \"저 사람이 — 저 사람이 아니라요.\" 그러나 그는 당시 피렌체의 하숙집에 살고 있던 레베카 부인에게 다정하고 정중한 답장을 써 보냈다. 그러나 우리는 이야기를 앞질러 가고 있다.\n\n우리의 사랑스러운 베키가 처음 날아간 거리는 그리 멀지 않았다. 그녀는 그토록 많은 추방당한 영국식 순결이 깃드는 피난처, 불로뉴의 프랑스 해안에 내려앉아, 거기서 어느 호텔에 하녀 한 명과 방 두 칸을 두고 제법 점잖은 미망인 행세를 하며 살았다. 그녀는 정식 식탁에서 식사를 했는데, 사람들은 그녀를 무척 유쾌한 사람이라 여겼고, 그녀는 자기 오라버니인 피트 경과 런던의 쟁쟁한 지인들에 관한 이야기로 옆 사람들을 즐겁게 해 주며, 교양이 변변치 못한 어떤 부류의 사람들에게 그토록 잘 먹히는 그 편안하고 세련된 수다를 떨었다. 그녀는 그들 가운데 많은 이에게 대단한 인물로 통했다. 그녀는 자기 방에서 자그마한 다과회를 열었고, 해수욕이며 무개마차 나들이며 모래밭 산책이며 연극 구경이며 하는 그 고장의 천진한 오락에 끼어들었다. 인쇄업자의 부인인 버조이스 부인은 여름 동안 식구들과 함께 그 호텔에 묵고 있었고, 그녀의 버조이스 씨는 토요일과 일요일에만 찾아오곤 했는데, 부인은 그 망나니 같은 버조이스가 그녀에게 지나치게 관심을 쏟기 시작하기 전까지는 그녀를 매력적이라 여겼다. 그러나 그 일에는 별다른 사연이 있었던 게 아니라, 그저 베키가 늘 상냥하고 편안하고 사람 좋게 굴었던 것뿐이었다 — 특히 남자들에게 그러했다.\n\n으레 그러하듯 사교철이 끝날 무렵이면 숱한 사람들이 외국으로 나가는 터라, 베키는 런던 상류사회의 지인들이 보이는 태도를 통해, 자기 행실에 대해 \"사교계\"가 품은 견해를 알아낼 기회를 얼마든지 얻었다. 어느 날 베키는 불로뉴 부두를 얌전히 거닐던 참에 파틀릿 부인과 그 딸들과 맞닥뜨렸는데, 깊고 푸른 바다 너머 저 멀리에서는 알비온의 절벽이 빛나고 있었다. 파틀릿 부인은 양산을 한 번 휙 휘둘러 딸들을 죄다 자기 둘레로 불러 모으더니, 거기 홀로 서 있는 가엾은 작은 베키에게 사나운 눈길을 쏘아붙이며 부두에서 물러갔다.\n\n또 어느 날에는 연락선이 들어왔다. 바람이 제법 세게 불었는데, 사람들이 배에서 내려올 때 그 우스꽝스럽게 초췌한 얼굴들을 구경하는 일은 늘 베키의 기분에 맞았다. 마침 그날 슬링스톤 부인이 배에 타고 있었다. 부인은 마차 안에서 몹시 멀미를 한 데다 기진맥진하여, 배에서 부두로 이어진 널다리를 오르기조차 버거운 형편이었다. 그러나 분홍색 보닛 아래 장난스레 미소 짓는 베키를 본 순간 그녀의 기력은 죄다 되살아나, 웬만한 여자라면 오그라들고 말 멸시의 눈길을 그녀에게 한 번 던지고는, 아무런 부축도 받지 않고 세관 건물 안으로 걸어 들어갔다. 베키는 그저 웃기만 했다. 그러나 그녀가 그것을 마음에 들어 했으리라고는 생각지 않는다. 그녀는 자기가 외롭다고, 아주 외롭다고 느꼈고, 저 멀리 빛나는 영국의 절벽은 그녀로서는 결코 다다를 수 없는 곳이었다.\n\n남자들의 태도 역시 어찌 된 영문인지 달라져 있었다. 그린스톤은 이를 드러내고 보기 싫게 허물없는 태도로 그녀의 면전에서 웃어댔다. 석 달 전만 해도 그녀에게 모자를 벗어 들고 굽실거리며, 곤트 하우스 앞에 줄지어 선 마차들 속에서 그녀의 마차를 찾아 주려고 빗속을 일 마일이나 걸어 주던 꼬마 밥 서클링은, 어느 날 베키가 산책하러 나간 그 방파제에서 근위대의 피츠우프(히호 경의 아들)와 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 꼬마 보비는 모자에 손도 대지 않은 채 어깨너머로 그녀에게 까딱 고갯짓만 하고는, 히호 가의 그 상속자와 하던 대화를 계속했다. 톰 레이크스는 입에 시가를 문 채 여관의 그녀 거실로 걸어 들어오려 했으나, 그녀는 그의 면전에서 문을 닫았고, 그의 손가락이 끼어 있지만 않았더라면 자물쇠까지 채웠을 것이다. 그녀는 정말이지 자기가 몹시 외롭다는 것을 느끼기 시작했다. \"그이가 여기 있었더라면,\" 그녀는 말했다. \"저 비겁한 자들이 감히 나를 모욕할 엄두도 못 냈을 텐데.\" 그녀는 \"그이\"를 두고 깊은 슬픔에 잠겨, 어쩌면 그리움에 잠겨 생각했다 — 그 정직하고, 우둔하고, 한결같은 친절과 충실함을, 그 그칠 줄 모르던 순종을, 그 너그러운 성품을, 그 용맹함과 담대함을. 십중팔구 그녀는 울었을 것이다. 저녁 식사 자리에 내려왔을 때 그녀는 유난히 명랑했고, 평소보다 연지를 조금 더 발랐던 것이다.\n\n이제 그녀는 으레 연지를 발랐다. 그리고 — 그리고 그녀의 하녀는 호텔 계산서에 올라간 것 말고도 따로 코냑을 구해다 주었다.\n\n그러나 남자들의 모욕이라 한들, 어떤 여자들의 동정만큼 그녀에게 견디기 힘든 것은 아니었으리라. 크래컨버리 부인과 워싱턴 화이트 부인이 스위스로 가는 길에 불로뉴를 지나갔다. 일행은 호너 대령과 젊은 보모리스, 그리고 물론 늙은 크래컨버리와 화이트 부인의 어린 딸의 호위를 받고 있었다. 그들은 그녀를 피하지 않았다. 그들은 키득거리고, 재잘대고, 수군대고, 위로하고, 달래고, 생색을 내며 그녀를 거의 격분에 미칠 지경으로 몰아갔다. 저따위 것들에게 동정을 받다니! 그들이 입을 맞추고 나서 헤벌쭉 웃으며 떠나갈 때 그녀는 그렇게 생각했다. 그리고 그녀는 계단에서 울려 퍼지는 보모리스의 웃음소리를 들었고, 그 흥겨운 웃음을 어떻게 해석해야 할지 너무도 잘 알았다.\n\n베키가 — 매주 청구서를 꼬박꼬박 치르고, 그 집안의 모든 사람에게 상냥하게 굴고, 안주인에게 미소 짓고, 종업원들을 \"므슈\"라 부르고, 객실 하녀들에게는 예의와 사과로 보답하여, 돈 문제에서 조금 인색한 점(베키는 결코 이 점에서 자유롭지 못했다)을 너끈히 메우고도 남던 그 베키가 — 그렇다, 우리가 말하건대, 바로 그 베키가 이 방문이 있은 뒤 주인으로부터 나가 달라는 통고를 받은 것이다. 누군가 주인에게 그녀가 자기 호텔에 둘 만한 인물이 결코 못 되며, 영국 숙녀들이 그녀와 한 식탁에 앉으려 들지 않으리라고 일러바친 것이었다. 그리하여 그녀는 그 따분함과 적막함이 더없이 진력나는 셋방으로 도망치듯 옮겨갈 수밖에 없었다.\n\n그래도 그녀는 이런 냉대에도 굴하지 않고 버티며, 스스로 떳떳한 인품을 세우고 추문을 물리치려 애썼다. 그녀는 더없이 꼬박꼬박 예배에 나가, 거기 누구보다도 큰 소리로 찬송을 불렀다. 그녀는 난파한 어부들의 미망인을 위한 사업을 떠맡았고, 콰시부 선교회에 일감과 그림을 내놓았다. 그녀는 무도회비를 냈으면서도 왈츠는 추지 않으려 했다. 한마디로 그녀는 점잖다고 할 만한 일은 죄다 했으니, 우리가 그녀 생애의 이 대목을 두고, 그다지 유쾌하지 못한 이후의 행적보다 더 애정을 담아 오래 머무는 것도 그 때문이다. 그녀는 사람들이 자기를 피하는 것을 보면서도 여전히 안간힘을 다해 그들에게 미소를 지어 보였다. 그 얼굴만 보아서는 그녀가 속으로 얼마나 굴욕의 고통을 견디고 있는지 결코 짐작할 수 없었으리라.\n\n그녀의 내력은 따지고 보면 하나의 수수께끼였다. 그녀를 두고 사람들의 의견은 갈렸다. 굳이 수고를 들여 그 일에 끼어든 어떤 이들은 그녀가 죄인이라고 했고, 또 어떤 이들은 그녀가 어린양처럼 결백하며 그 가증스러운 남편이 잘못이라고 장담했다. 그녀는 제 아들 이야기에 눈물을 터뜨리고, 그 이름이 입에 오르거나 그와 닮은 누군가를 보면 더없이 미친 듯한 슬픔을 드러내 보임으로써 적잖은 사람들을 자기편으로 끌어들였다. 그녀는 그런 식으로 마음씨 고운 올더니 부인의 마음을 얻었는데, 이 부인으로 말하면 영국인이 모인 불로뉴에서는 거의 여왕 격으로 그곳 거주자들 중 가장 많은 만찬과 무도회를 베푸는 사람이었다. 올더니 도련님이 방학을 어머니와 함께 보내려고 스위시테일 박사의 학원에서 돌아왔을 때, 베키는 눈물을 흘려 보였다. \"그 애와 우리 로던이 동갑이고, 또 어찌나 닮았는지요.\" 베키는 고통으로 목이 메는 목소리로 말했다. 그러나 사실 두 소년의 나이는 다섯 살이나 차이가 났고, 둘 사이의 닮은 점이라곤 존경하는 독자 여러분과 그 보잘것없는 종복 사이의 닮은 점만큼이나 없었다. 웬햄은 스타인 경과 합류하러 키싱겐으로 가는 길에 외국으로 나가던 중 이 점에 관해 올더니 부인의 눈을 틔워 주었으니, 어린 로던을 묘사하는 일이라면 그 어미보다 자기가 훨씬 잘할 수 있다고 — 그 어미는 소문난 대로 아이를 미워하여 한 번도 보지 않았으니 — 일러 주었다. 그 애는 열세 살인데 어린 올더니는 겨우 아홉 살이요, 그 애는 살결이 검은데 이 귀여운 도련님은 살결이 희다는 것 따위를 — 한마디로 그는 문제의 부인이 제 호의를 후회하게 만들고 만 것이었다.\n\n베키가 믿을 수 없는 노고와 수고를 들여 제 둘레에 자그마한 무리를 만들어 놓을 때마다, 누군가가 나타나 그것을 매정하게 쓸어 무너뜨렸고, 그녀는 모든 일을 다시 처음부터 시작해야 했다. 그것은 몹시 고된 일이었다. 정말이지 고되고, 외롭고, 맥 빠지는 일이었다.\n\n뉴브라이트 부인이라는 이가 있어 한동안 그녀를 거두어 주었으니, 예배 때 부르는 그녀의 감미로운 노래와, 지난날 퀸스크롤리에서 베키 부인이 적잖이 가르침을 받은 바 있는 경건한 문제들에 관한 그녀의 올바른 견해에 마음이 끌린 것이었다. 그러니, 그녀는 전도지를 받아 들기만 한 게 아니라 그것을 읽기까지 했다. 그녀는 콰시부족을 위해 플란넬 속치마를 짓고 — 코코넛 인디언을 위해 무명 잠자리 모자를 만들고 — 교황과 유대인의 개종을 위해 손부채에 그림을 그리고 — 수요일에는 롤스 씨, 목요일에는 허글턴 씨의 설교 아래 앉았으며, 일요일에는 두 차례의 예배에 참석하고도 저녁이면 더비파인 볼러 씨의 설교까지 들었으나, 죄다 헛일이었다. 뉴브라이트 부인은 피지 섬 주민을 위한 보온 침대 기금 문제로 사우스다운 백작부인과 편지를 주고받을 일이 있었는데(이 훌륭한 자선사업의 운영에 두 부인 모두 여성 위원회의 일원으로 참여하고 있었다), 자기의 \"사랑스러운 벗\" 로던 크롤리 부인을 언급하자, 노백작부인은 베키에 관해 갖가지 자세한 내막과 암시와 사실과 거짓과 두루뭉술한 저주를 곁들인 답장을 써 보냈고, 그리하여 뉴브라이트 부인과 크롤리 부인의 친분은 그 즉시 끝장나고 말았다. 이 불행이 일어난 투르의 경건한 무리 전체도 그 천하의 죄인과 곧장 절교해 버렸다. 외국에 나가 있는 영국인 거류지를 아는 이라면, 우리가 어디에 자리를 잡든 우리의 자존심과 환약과 편견과 하비 소스와 카옌 후추, 그 밖의 온갖 수호신을 함께 짊어지고 다니며 거기에 작은 영국을 만들어 낸다는 것을 알 것이다.\n\n베키는 이 거류지에서 저 거류지로 불안하게 도망쳐 다녔다. 불로뉴에서 디에프로, 디에프에서 캉으로, 캉에서 투르로 — 안간힘을 다해 점잖아지려 애썼건만, 아아! 언제나 어느 날엔가는 정체가 드러나, 진짜 갈가마귀들에게 쪼여 새장 밖으로 내쫓기고 마는 것이었다.\n\n이런 곳들 가운데 한 곳에서 후크 이글스 부인이 그녀를 거두어 주었으니, 흠 하나 없는 인품에 포트먼 광장에 저택을 둔 여인이었다. 그녀는 베키가 도망쳐 간 디에프의 그 호텔에 묵고 있었고, 두 사람은 함께 헤엄을 치던 바다에서 처음 사귀게 되었으며, 그 뒤로는 그 호텔의 정식 식탁에서 안면을 텄다. 이글스 부인은 스타인 사건의 추문을 — 하기야 그것을 듣지 못한 사람이 어디 있었겠는가? — 얼마간 들어 알고 있었다. 그러나 베키와 한 번 이야기를 나눈 뒤 그녀는 단언하기를, 크롤리 부인은 천사요, 그 남편은 악당이요, 스타인 경은 누구나 다 알듯이 파렴치한 인간이요, 크롤리 부인에 대한 사건 전체는 저 악당 웬햄이 꾸민 더없이 파렴치하고 사악한 음모라는 것이었다. \"당신이 조금이라도 기개 있는 사내라면, 이글스 씨, 다음번에 클럽에서 그 못된 자를 보거든 따귀라도 한 대 갈겨야 마땅해요.\" 그녀는 남편에게 말했다. 그러나 이글스는 그저 조용한 노신사로, 이글스 부인의 남편이요, 지질학에 취미가 있을 뿐, 누구의 따귀에 손이 닿을 만큼 키가 크지도 못했다.\n\n그리하여 이글스 부부는 로던 부인을 비호하여, 파리의 제 집으로 데려가 함께 살게 했고, 자기가 돌보는 사람을 받아들이려 하지 않는다 하여 대사 부인과 다투었으며, 베키를 미덕과 명예의 길에서 벗어나지 않게 하려고 한 여인이 할 수 있는 일은 죄다 했다.\n\n베키는 처음에는 무척 점잖고 단정했으나, 단조롭게 덕을 지키는 생활이 머지않아 그녀에게는 도무지 견딜 수 없이 지루해졌다. 날마다 똑같은 일과요, 똑같은 따분함과 안락이요, 똑같이 멍청한 불로뉴 숲을 가로지르는 똑같은 마차 산책이요, 저녁이면 똑같은 사람들이요, 일요일 밤이면 똑같은 블레어의 설교집이요 — 똑같은 오페라가 늘 거듭거듭 상연되는 격이었다. 베키가 권태로 죽을 지경이 되었을 무렵, 다행스럽게도 젊은 이글스 군이 케임브리지에서 돌아왔고, 그의 어머니는 자기의 작은 벗이 아들에게 어떤 인상을 심어 주는지를 알아채고는 그 즉시 베키에게 나가 달라는 통고를 했다.\n\n그러고 나서 그녀는 어떤 여자 친구와 함께 살림을 차려 보았는데, 이내 그 두 살림은 다투기 시작했고 빚을 지게 되었다. 그러자 그녀는 하숙 생활을 하기로 마음먹고, 파리의 루아얄 거리에서 생타무르 부인이 꾸리는 그 유명한 저택에서 한동안 살았는데, 거기서 그녀는 안주인의 살롱을 드나드는 초라한 멋쟁이들과 한물간 미인들을 상대로 제 매력과 교태를 부려 보기 시작했다. 베키는 사교계를 사랑했으니, 아니 정말이지 아편쟁이가 한 모금 없이는 못 살 듯 사교계 없이는 살 수 없었으며, 하숙 생활을 하던 그 시절에는 제법 행복했다. \"여기 여자들도 메이페어 여자들만큼이나 재미있어.\" 그녀는 우연히 마주친 옛 런던 친구에게 말했다. \"다만 옷차림이 그만큼 산뜻하지 못할 뿐이지. 남자들은 빤 장갑을 끼고, 확실히 형편없는 악당들이긴 하지만, 그래도 잭 아무개니 톰 아무개니 하는 자들보다 나쁘진 않아. 이 집 안주인은 좀 천박하긴 한데, 그래도 그 레이디 ◯◯◯◯만큼 천박하진 않은 것 같아.\" 그러고는 그녀는 어느 대단한 유행계의 우두머리 이름을 댔는데, 나는 그 이름을 밝히느니 차라리 죽고 말리라. 사실 밤이면 생타무르 부인의 방들이 환히 밝혀지고, 가슴에 훈장과 수장을 단 사내들이 에카르테 탁자에 둘러앉고, 여자들이 그 조금 떨어진 곳에 자리한 광경을 보노라면, 한동안은 자기가 상류사회에 와 있는 듯, 그리고 그 부인이 진짜 백작부인인 듯 여겨질 법도 했다. 실제로 그렇게 여기는 사람들이 많았고, 베키는 한동안 그 백작부인의 살롱에서 가장 멋들어진 부인들 가운데 하나였다.\n\n그러나 십중팔구 1815년 무렵의 옛 채권자들이 그녀를 찾아내어 파리를 떠나게 만든 모양이니, 가엾은 작은 여인은 제법 갑작스레 그 도시를 떠나야 했고, 거기서 브뤼셀로 옮겨갔다.\n\n그녀는 그곳을 어찌나 또렷이 기억하던지! 그녀는 자기가 묵었던 그 자그마한 중이층을 올려다보며 히죽 웃었고, 호텔 정문 차도에 마차를 세워 둔 채 말을 대령하라, 어서 도망치자, 고래고래 외쳐대던 베어에이커스 일가를 떠올렸다. 그녀는 워털루로, 또 라컨으로 갔는데, 거기 있는 조지 오스본의 기념비가 그녀에게 적잖은 감회를 주었다. 그녀는 그것을 가볍게 그려 두었다. \"그 가엾은 큐피드 같으니!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"어찌나 끔찍하게 나한테 빠져 있었던지, 또 어찌나 바보 같았던지! 어린 에미가 살아 있을지 모르겠네. 마음씨 고운 작은 사람이었지. 그 뚱뚱한 오라비도. 그 우스꽝스럽게 뚱뚱한 초상화를 아직도 내 서류들 사이에 갖고 있는걸. 친절하고 순박한 사람들이었어.\"\n\n베키는 생타무르 부인이 자기 친구인 보로디노 백작부인에게 그녀를 천거하여 브뤼셀에 당도했다. 보로디노 백작부인은 나폴레옹 휘하의 장군이자 그 유명한 보로디노 백작의 미망인이었는데, 세상을 떠난 그 영웅이 남겨 준 살림 밑천이라곤 정식 식탁과 에카르테 탁자가 전부였다. 이류 멋쟁이들과 난봉꾼들, 언제나 소송 한 건쯤은 끼고 사는 미망인들, 그리고 이런 집들에서 \"대륙 사교계\"를 구경하는 줄로 여기는 더없이 순진한 영국 사람들이 보로디노 부인의 식탁에 돈을 내려놓거나 식사를 했다. 의기양양한 젊은 친구들은 정식 식탁에 둘러앉은 사람들에게 샴페인을 한턱냈고, 여자들과 말을 타고 나가거나 시골 나들이를 위해 말을 빌렸으며, 돈을 추렴해 연극이나 오페라의 특별석을 잡았고, 에카르테 탁자에서는 부인들의 고운 어깨너머로 돈을 걸었으며, 데번셔에 있는 부모에게는 외국 사교계에 운 좋게 발을 들였노라 편지를 써 보냈다.\n\n파리에서 그러했듯 여기서도 베키는 하숙집의 여왕이었고, 엄선된 하숙집들을 호령했다. 그녀는 샴페인도, 꽃다발도, 시골 나들이도, 특별석도 결코 마다하지 않았다. 그러나 그녀가 가장 좋아한 것은 밤에 하는 에카르테였으니 — 그녀는 대담하게 도박을 벌였다. 처음에는 그저 푼돈으로 시작하더니, 이윽고 오 프랑짜리 동전으로, 그다음엔 나폴레옹 금화로, 그다음엔 지폐로 판을 키웠다. 그러다 한 달치 하숙비를 치를 수 없게 되었다. 그러면 젊은 신사들에게 돈을 빌렸다. 그러다 다시 현금이 생기면, 전에 어르고 구슬려대던 보로디노 부인을 윽박질렀다. 그러다가 한 번에 십 수씩만 걸 처지가 되어, 끔찍한 가난에 빠졌다. 그러다 분기별 수당이 들어오면 보로디노 부인에게 진 빚을 갚고는, 다시 한번 로시뇰 씨나 라프 기사를 상대로 카드를 잡는 것이었다.\n\n베키가 브뤼셀을 떠날 무렵의 서글픈 진실인즉, 그녀는 보로디노 부인에게 석 달치 하숙비를 빚지고 있었다. 이 사실이며, 도박이며, 음주며, 영국 국교회 목사인 머프 목사 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 돈을 빌린 일이며, 머프 목사의 제자이자 누들 경의 아들인 누들 도련님을 어르고 꼬드겨 제 방으로 끌어들여 에카르테로 거액을 따낸 일이며 — 그러니까 이 사실이며 그 밖의 백 가지 못된 짓거리를, 보로디노 백작부인은 자기 집에 묵는 영국 사람마다 붙들고 일러바치면서, 로던 부인은 한낱 독사나 다름없는 여자였노라고 떠벌리는 것이다.\n\n그리하여 우리의 작은 방랑자는 율리시스나 뱀필드 무어 캐루처럼 잠시도 가만있지 못한 채, 유럽의 여러 도시를 옮겨 다니며 천막을 쳤다. 점잖지 못함을 좇는 그녀의 취향은 갈수록 더 두드러졌다. 그녀는 머지않아 완벽한 보헤미안이 되어, 마주치면 머리카락이 곤두설 만한 사람들과 어울려 지냈다.\n\n유럽에서 이름깨나 있는 도시치고 영국인 부랑자들의 작은 거류지가 없는 곳은 없으니 — 헴프 집행관이 보안관 법정에서 그 이름을 정기적으로 읽어 올리는 사내들이요, 흔히 제법 양가의 출신이지만 그 집안이 인연을 끊어 버린 젊은 신사들이요, 당구장과 카페의 단골들이요, 외국 경마와 도박판의 후원자들이다. 그들은 채무자 감옥을 가득 메운다 — 술을 마시고 으스댄다 — 싸우고 주먹다짐을 벌인다 — 돈도 안 내고 달아난다 — 프랑스와 독일 장교들과 결투를 벌인다 — 에카르테로 스푸니 씨를 속여먹는다 — 돈을 손에 쥐면 으리으리한 사륜마차를 몰고 바덴으로 내뺀다 — 백전백승의 도박술을 시험해 보고는, 빈 주머니로, 초라한 협잡꾼이요 빈털터리 멋쟁이가 되어 도박 탁자 언저리를 어슬렁거리다가, 가짜 환어음으로 유대인 은행가를 등쳐먹거나 또 다른 스푸니 씨를 찾아내어 털어먹는 것이다. 이런 사람들이 겪는 영화와 비참의 교차란 참으로 기묘한 구경거리다. 그들의 삶이란 필시 굉장한 흥분의 연속일 것이다. 베키도 — 이것을 인정해야만 할까? — 이런 삶에 빠져들었고, 그것도 그리 싫지 않은 마음으로 빠져들었다. 그녀는 이 보헤미안들 틈에 끼어 이 도시 저 도시를 떠돌았다. 운 좋은 로던 부인은 독일의 도박 탁자란 도박 탁자에서는 죄다 이름이 알려져 있었다. 그녀는 크뤼슈카세 부인과 피렌체에서 함께 살림을 차렸다. 듣자 하니 그녀는 뮌헨에서 추방당했다 하고, 내 벗 프레더릭 피전 씨가 단언하기로는, 자기가 로잔에 있는 그녀의 집에서 밤참 자리에 약을 먹고 정신을 잃은 채 로더 소령과 듀세이스 귀공자에게 팔백 파운드를 잃었다는 것이다. 보다시피 우리는 베키의 전기에 대해 어느 정도 이야기를 늘어놓을 의무가 있지만, 이 대목에 관해서는 아마도 적게 이야기할수록 나을 것이다.\n\n듣자 하니, 크롤리 부인은 운수가 유난히 사나울 때면 여기저기서 음악회를 열고 음악 교습을 했다고 한다. 라우든 부인이라는 사람이 있어, 확실히 빌트바트에서 발라키아 영주의 수석 피아니스트인 헤르 스포프의 반주로 음악 오찬회를 열었다고 한다. 또 모르는 이가 없고 안 가 본 곳이 없는 내 작은 벗 이브스 씨는, 1830년에 자기가 스트라스부르에 있었는데, 그때 어느 레베크 부인이라는 사람이 오페라 「하얀 옷의 여인」에 출연하여 그곳 극장에서 한바탕 굉장한 소동을 일으켰다고 늘 단언하곤 했다. 그녀는 관객의 야유를 받으며 무대에서 쫓겨났는데, 한편으로는 그녀 자신의 무능 때문이었지만, 주로는 일층 객석(주둔지 장교들이 입장권을 받는 곳)에 있던 몇몇 사람들의 경솔한 성원 탓이었다. 이브스는 문제의 그 불운한 신인이 다름 아닌 로던 크롤리 부인이었다고 확신했다.\n\n사실 그녀는 이 땅 위의 한낱 부랑자나 다름없었다. 돈이 생기면 도박을 했고, 도박으로 그것을 날리면 입에 풀칠하느라 갖은 수를 다 썼다. 그녀가 어떻게, 무슨 수로 그것을 해냈는지 누가 알겠는가? 듣자 하니 그녀가 한번은 상트페테르부르크에서 목격되었다고 하는데, 경찰에 의해 그 수도에서 즉각 쫓겨났다 하니, 그 뒤로 그녀가 토플리츠와 빈에서 러시아의 첩자 노릇을 했다는 소문에는 진실의 가능성이 조금도 있을 수 없다. 심지어 나는 이런 이야기까지 들었으니, 그녀가 파리에서 제 친척을 한 사람 찾아냈는데, 그가 다름 아닌 외할머니였다는 것이다. 그 외할머니로 말하면 결코 몽모랑시 가의 사람 따위가 아니라, 큰길가의 어느 극장에서 일하는 흉측한 늙은 박스 안내원이었다. 다른 데서 넌지시 비쳤듯 다른 사람들도 알고 있었던 듯한 그 두 사람의 만남은, 필시 무척이나 감동적인 상봉이었으리라. 본 사가는 그 사건에 관해 확실한 세부 내막은 알려 줄 수가 없다.\n\n언젠가 로마에서 데 로던 부인의 반년치 봉급이 마침 그곳의 으뜸가는 은행가 앞으로 막 입금된 일이 있었다. 그런데 잔고가 오백 스쿠도를 넘는 사람이라면 누구나 이 상인들의 군주가 겨울 동안 베푸는 무도회에 초대받는 터라, 베키도 초대장을 받는 영예를 누려, 폴로니아 공작 부부가 베푸는 화려한 야회들 가운데 하나에 모습을 드러냈다. 공작 부인은 폼필리 가문 출신으로, 로마의 두 번째 왕과 올림포스 가문의 에게리아의 직계 후손이었다. 한편 공작의 조부 알레산드로 폴로니아는 비누 방울이며 향수며 담배며 손수건 따위를 팔고, 신사들의 심부름을 다니고, 자그마하게 돈놀이를 하던 사람이었다. 로마의 쟁쟁한 사람들이 죄다 그의 응접실로 몰려들었으니 — 공작이며 대공이며 대사며 예술가며 바이올린 연주자며 고위 성직자며, 인도자를 거느린 풋내기 곰 같은 자들이며 — 온갖 신분과 처지의 사람이 다 모였다. 그의 홀들은 불빛과 호화로움으로 번쩍였고, (그림이 든) 금빛 액자들과 진위가 의심스러운 골동품들로 눈부셨다. 그리고 그 군주다운 주인의 거대한 금빛 왕관과 문장이 — 진홍빛 바탕(그가 팔던 손수건의 색깔이다)에 황금 버섯이 새겨진 그 문장과, 폼필리 가문의 은빛 분수가 — 저택의 천장이며 문이며 벽판마다, 또 교황과 황제를 모시려고 마련된 그 웅장한 벨벳 천개마다 온통 번쩍이고 있었다.\n\n그리하여 피렌체에서 역마차를 타고 당도하여 더없이 검소하게 어느 여관에 묵고 있던 베키는 폴로니아 공작의 야회 초대장을 손에 넣었고, 그녀의 하녀는 평소와 달리 정성껏 그녀를 단장해 주었다. 그녀는 마침 그 무렵 함께 여행 중이던 로더 소령의 팔에 기대어 이 성대한 무도회에 갔다(이 소령으로 말하면 이듬해 나폴리에서 라볼리 공작을 쏘아 죽이고, 에카르테를 할 때 쓰는 것 말고도 모자 속에 킹 카드를 넉 장이나 숨겨 두었다가 존 벅스킨 경에게 지팡이로 두들겨 맞은 바로 그 사내다). 이 한 쌍은 함께 방으로 들어섰고, 베키는 자기가 결백하지는 않았으되 정체가 들통나지도 않았던 더 행복한 시절에 알았던 낯익은 얼굴들을 여럿 보았다. 로더 소령은 단춧구멍에 더러운 줄무늬 리본을 꽂고 셔츠는 거의 드러내지 않은, 수염 기른 매서운 눈초리의 외국인들을 숱하게 알고 있었다. 그러나 정작 그의 동포들은, 누가 보더라도, 그 소령을 피했다. 베키 역시 여기저기서 부인 몇몇을 알아보았으니 — 프랑스인 미망인들이며, 남편에게 학대받은 진위가 의심스러운 이탈리아 백작부인들이었다 — 에잇, 허영의 시장에서 제법 빼어난 사람들 틈에 섞여 본 우리가, 이 악당들의 찌꺼기요 침전물을 두고 뭐라 말할 수 있겠는가? 노름을 할 바엔 깨끗한 카드로 할 일이지, 이런 더러운 패로 할 일은 아니다. 그러나 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 여행자 무리의 일원이 되어 본 사람이라면 누구나, 이 약탈자 같은 비정규병들이 — 마치 님과 피스톨처럼 — 본대에 들러붙어, 임금의 군기를 두르고 그의 위임장을 떠벌리면서도 제 잇속만 차려 약탈을 일삼다가, 이따금 길가에서 교수대에 매달리는 꼴을 본 적이 있을 것이다.\n\n자, 그리하여 그녀는 로더 소령의 팔에 매달려 그와 함께 방들을 누비며, 간이식당에서 샴페인을 잔뜩 마셨다. 거기서는 사람들이, 특히 소령의 비정규 부대가 다과를 차지하려고 사납게 다투고 있었다. 두 사람은 실컷 마시고 나자, 응접실들이 줄지어 늘어선 그 끝에 있는 공작부인의 분홍빛 벨벳 응접실(비너스 조각상과 은테를 두른 커다란 베네치아 거울들이 있는 방)에 이를 때까지 인파를 헤치고 나아갔다. 거기서는 군주 일가가 둥근 식탁에 둘러앉아 가장 존귀한 손님들을 만찬으로 대접하고 있었다. 그것은 베키가 스타인 경의 집에서 함께 한 적 있다고 기억하는 바로 그런 종류의 자그마하고 엄선된 연회였다 — 그런데 폴로니아의 식탁에 그가 앉아 있었으니, 그녀는 그를 본 것이다. 그의 희고 벗어진 번들거리는 이마에 다이아몬드가 그어 놓은 흉터가 벌겋게 타오르는 자국을 이루고 있었다. 그의 붉은 구레나룻은 자줏빛으로 물들여져 있어, 그러잖아도 창백한 그의 얼굴을 더욱 창백해 보이게 했다. 그는 깃 장식과 훈장을, 청색 수장과 가터를 차고 있었다. 거기에는 군림하는 대공 한 사람과 그 공주들을 거느린 왕족 전하 한 사람도 있었으나, 그는 그 누구보다도 위대한 군주였다. 그리고 그 경 곁에는 아름다운 벨라도나 백작부인이, 처녀 적 이름은 드 글랑디에였던 그 부인이 앉아 있었으니, 그녀의 남편(빼어난 곤충 표본 수집으로 그토록 이름난 파올로 델라 벨라도나 백작)은 모로코 황제에게 파견된 사절단의 임무로 오래도록 자리를 비운 터였다.\n\n베키가 그 낯익고 쟁쟁한 얼굴을 마주한 순간, 로더 소령이 별안간 얼마나 천박해 보이던지, 또 저 가증스러운 룩 대위에게서는 어쩌면 그리도 담배 냄새가 풍기던지! 그녀는 한순간에 다시 우아한 귀부인의 자태를 되찾고, 마치 다시 한번 메이페어에 와 있는 양 보이고 또 느끼려 애썼다. \"저 여자는 멍청하고 심술궂어 보이는걸.\" 그녀는 생각했다. \"분명 저런 여자가 그이를 즐겁게 해 줄 리 없어. 그래, 그이는 저 여자한테 따분해하고 있을 거야 — 나한텐 한 번도 그런 적 없었지.\" 그녀가 더없이 반짝이는 눈으로(눈꺼풀까지 바른 연지가 그 눈을 반짝이게 했다) 그 위대한 귀족을 바라보는 동안, 이렇듯 가슴 저미는 백 가지 희망과 두려움과 추억이 그녀의 작은 가슴속에서 두근거렸다. 스타와 가터를 차는 밤이면 스타인 경은 으레 더없이 거창한 태도를 갖추고, 본래 그러했듯 위대한 군주처럼 보이고 또 말하곤 했다. 베키는 그가 호화롭게, 편안하게, 고고하고 위엄 있게 미소 짓는 모습을 우러러보았다. 아아, 맙소사, 그는 얼마나 유쾌한 길동무였던가, 얼마나 눈부신 재담가였던가, 얼마나 풍성한 이야깃거리를 가졌던가, 얼마나 거창한 풍채였던가! — 그런데 그녀는 이런 사람을, 시가와 브랜디 탄 물 냄새가 진동하는 로더 소령과, 마부 같은 농지거리와 권투판 은어를 늘어놓는 룩 대위와, 그 따위 작자들과 맞바꾼 것이었다. \"그이가 나를 알아볼까.\" 그녀는 생각했다. 스타인 경은 곁에 앉은 어느 위대하고 쟁쟁한 부인과 이야기를 나누며 웃다가, 문득 고개를 들어 베키를 보았다.\n\n두 사람의 눈이 마주치자 그녀는 온몸이 떨려 왔고, 그녀는 짜낼 수 있는 가장 멋진 미소를 지으며 그에게 작고 수줍은, 애원하는 듯한 인사를 살포시 건넸다. 그는 한순간 그녀를 보고 아연실색하여 멍하니 바라보았는데, 마치 맥베스가 제 무도회 만찬 자리에 뱅쿼가 불쑥 나타난 것을 보았을 때처럼, 입을 떡 벌린 채 그녀를 바라보고 있었다. 바로 그때 저 끔찍한 로더 소령이 그녀를 끌어당겼다.\n\n\"이리 와서 식당 쪽으로 갑시다, R 부인.\" 이것이 그 신사의 말이었다. \"이 나리들이 먹어대는 걸 보고 있자니 나도 출출해졌단 말이오. 가서 영감님네 샴페인이나 좀 맛봅시다.\" 베키가 보기에 소령은 이미 너무 많이 마신 터였다.\n\n이튿날 그녀는 핀치오 언덕 — 로마의 한량들에게는 하이드 파크 격인 곳 — 으로 산책을 나갔는데, 어쩌면 스타인 경을 다시 한번 보게 되지 않을까 하는 기대에서였으리라. 그러나 그녀가 거기서 마주친 것은 다른 지인이었다. 다름 아닌 그 경의 심복인 피시 씨로, 그는 제법 허물없이 그녀에게 고갯짓을 하고 모자에 손가락을 갖다 대며 다가왔다. \"마담께서 여기 계신 줄 알고 있었습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"호텔에서부터 뒤따라왔지요. 마담께 드릴 충고가 있습니다.\"\n\n\"스타인 후작에게서 온 건가요?\" 베키는 짜낼 수 있는 만큼 위엄을 다시 갖추며 물었으나, 희망과 기대에 적잖이 마음이 흔들리고 있었다.\n\n\"아닙니다.\" 그 시종이 말했다. \"제가 드리는 겁니다. 로마는 무척 몸에 해롭지요.\"\n\n\"이 철에는 아니잖아요, 므슈 피시 — 부활절이 지나기 전에는 아니지요.\"\n\n\"마담께 말씀드리건대, 지금이 해롭습니다. 어떤 사람들에게는 늘 말라리아가 도사리고 있지요. 그 저주받은 늪지 바람이 철을 가리지 않고 많은 이를 죽음으로 몰아갑니다. 보십시오, 크롤리 부인, 부인께서는 늘 마음씨 고운 분이셨고, 저는 부인께 마음을 쓰고 있습니다, 명예를 걸고 말씀드립니다. 부디 경고를 새겨들으십시오. 로마를 떠나십시오, 분명히 말씀드립니다 — 그러지 않으면 부인께서는 병들어 죽게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n베키는 분노와 격분에 휩싸이면서도 웃었다. \"어머나! 이 가엾고 작은 나를 암살이라도 한다는 건가요?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"어쩌면 그리 낭만적인지! 우리 나리께서는 자객을 심부름꾼으로 데리고 다니시고, 짐마차에 단검이라도 싣고 다니신답니까? 흥! 차라리 그분을 괴롭히기 위해서라도 머물러 있겠어요. 여기 있는 동안 나를 지켜 줄 사람들이 있으니까요.\"\n\n이번에는 므슈 피시가 웃을 차례였다. \"부인을 지켜 준다고요.\" 그가 말했다. \"대체 누가 말입니까? 그 소령이요, 그 대위요, 마담께서 보시는 그 도박꾼들 가운데 누구든, 백 루이만 주면 부인의 목숨을 거둬 갈 작자들입니다. 우리는 로더 소령에 관해 이런저런 일들을 알고 있는데(그자가 소령인 것은 제가 후작 나리인 것이나 매한가지지요), 그걸 알면 그자를 갤리선이나 그보다 더한 곳으로 보낼 수 있을 겁니다. 우리는 모든 것을 알고 있고, 어디에나 친구가 있습니다. 우리는 부인께서 파리에서 누구를 만나셨는지, 거기서 어떤 친척을 찾아내셨는지도 알고 있지요. 그래요, 마담께서 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨셔도, 우리는 알고 있습니다. 대륙의 어느 장관도 마담을 받아들이려 하지 않았던 게 어찌 된 영문이겠습니까? 부인께서는 누군가의 비위를 거스르신 겁니다. 결코 용서하는 법이 없는 분, 부인을 보고 그 분노가 곱절이 된 분의 비위를 말입니다. 어젯밤 댁으로 돌아오셨을 때 그분은 미친 사람 같았습니다. 벨라도나 부인이 부인 일로 한바탕 소동을 벌이며 발작을 일으키셨거든요.\"\n\n\"오, 그게 벨라도나 부인이었군요?\" 베키는 조금 마음을 놓으며 말했다. 방금 들은 정보가 그녀를 겁먹게 했던 것이다.\n\n\"아니지요 — 그 부인은 중요하지 않습니다 — 그 부인이야 늘 질투를 하니까요. 제가 말씀드리는 건 후작 나리입니다. 부인께서 그분 앞에 모습을 드러내신 건 잘못이었어요. 그러니 여기 머무신다면 후회하시게 될 겁니다. 제 말을 새겨들으십시오. 떠나십시오. 저기 나리의 마차가 옵니다.\" 그러고는 베키의 팔을 붙잡고 정원의 한 샛길로 황급히 끌고 들어갔다. 마침 그때 문장 장식이 번쩍이는 스타인 경의 사륜마차가 거의 값을 매길 수 없는 명마들에 이끌려 가로수 길을 따라 휘몰아치듯 달려왔다. 그 안에는 벨라도나 부인이 방석에 비스듬히 기대어 있었으니, 검은 머리에 시무룩한 얼굴이었으나 화사하게 피어 있었고, 무릎에는 킹 찰스 종 강아지를 앉히고 머리 위로는 흰 양산을 살랑이고 있었다. 그 곁에는 늙은 스타인이 납빛 얼굴에 섬뜩한 눈을 하고 길게 뻗어 누워 있었다. 증오나 분노나 욕정이 이따금 아직도 그 눈을 번득이게 했으나, 평소에는 아무런 빛도 없이, 거의 모든 쾌락과 더없이 빼어난 아름다움마저 진력나 버린, 이 지치고 사악한 늙은이의 눈으로 바라보는 세상에 그저 싫증이 난 듯했다.\n\n\"후작 나리께서는 그날 밤의 충격에서 끝내 회복하지 못하셨습니다, 끝끝내요.\" 마차가 휙 지나가는 동안 므슈 피시가 크롤리 부인에게 속삭였고, 그녀는 자기를 가려 주는 관목 뒤에서 그것을 빼꼼히 내다보았다. \"어쨌든 그것만은 위안이 되는걸.\" 베키는 생각했다.\n\n므슈 피시의 말대로 그 경이 정말로 베키 부인에게 살의를 품고 있었던 것인지(후작 나리가 세상을 떠난 뒤 그는 제 고국으로 돌아가 거기서 무척 존경받으며 살고 있으며, 제 군주로부터 피치 남작이라는 작위를 사들였다), 그래서 그 집사가 암살에 가담하기를 꺼린 것이었는지, 아니면 그저 그 경이 겨울을 나려고 정한 도시에서 — 그녀를 보는 것이 그 위대한 귀족에게는 더없이 불쾌한 일일 터이므로 — 크롤리 부인을 겁주어 쫓아내라는 임무를 받았을 뿐이었는지, 그것은 끝내 밝혀지지 않은 문제다. 그러나 그 협박은 그 작은 여인에게 효력을 발휘했고, 그녀는 두 번 다시 옛 후원자 앞에 끼어들려 하지 않았다.\n\n그 귀족의 서글픈 최후는 누구나 다 알고 있으니, 그것은 1830년의 프랑스 혁명이 일어난 지 두 달 뒤 나폴리에서 닥쳐왔다. 그때 더없이 존귀하신 조지 거스타버스, 스타인 후작이요, 아일랜드 귀족 명부에 오른 곤트 백작이자 곤트 캐슬 백작이요, 헬버러 자작이요, 피칠리 및 그릴스비 남작이요, 더없이 고귀한 가터 기사단과 에스파냐의 황금 양털 기사단과 러시아의 일급 성 니콜라우스 기사단과 터키의 초승달 기사단의 기사요, 화장실 시종장이자 뒤층계 시종이요, 곤트 또는 섭정 친위 민병 연대의 대령이요, 대영박물관 평의원이요, 트리니티 하우스의 원로 형제요, 화이트 프라이어스의 이사요, 민법학 박사이던 그분이 — 일련의 발작 끝에 세상을 떠난 것인데, 신문들이 전하기로는 유서 깊은 프랑스 왕정의 몰락이 그 경의 예민한 감수성에 입힌 충격이 그 원인이었다고 한다.\n\n어느 주간지에 그의 미덕과 호화로움과 재능과 선행을 기리는 유려한 송덕문이 실렸다. 그의 감수성과, 그가 인척 관계라 주장하던 저 쟁쟁한 부르봉 가문에 대한 애착은 어찌나 깊었던지, 그는 자기의 존귀한 친족들이 겪은 불행을 견디고 살아남을 수가 없었다는 것이다. 그의 시신은 나폴리에 묻혔고, 그의 심장은 — 언제나 온갖 고결하고 고귀한 감정으로 고동치던 그 심장은 — 은빛 항아리에 담겨 곤트 캐슬로 옮겨졌다. 와그 씨는 이렇게 말했다. \"그분의 죽음으로 가난한 이들과 예술은 인자한 후원자를 잃었고, 사교계는 가장 눈부신 장식 가운데 하나를 잃었으며, 영국은 가장 드높은 애국자이자 정치가 가운데 한 사람을 잃었다\" 운운.\n\n그의 유언장을 두고 적잖은 다툼이 일었고, 그 경이 늘 집게손가락에 끼고 다니던 \"유대인의 눈\"이라 불리는 그 유명한 다이아몬드를 벨라도나 부인에게서 빼앗아 내려는 시도가 있었으니, 듣자 하니 그녀가 그 애통한 죽음 직후에 그 반지에서 그것을 빼내었다는 것이었다. 그러나 그의 심복이자 시종이던 므슈 피시가 입증하기를, 그 반지는 후작이 세상을 떠나기 이틀 전에 문제의 벨라도나 부인에게 선물로 주어진 것이며, 그 경의 비밀 책상에서 발견되어 그 상속자들이 그 가엾은 여인에게서 내놓으라 요구한 지폐와 보석과 나폴리 및 프랑스 채권 따위도 마찬가지였다는 것이다.\n\n연극 도박판에서 만난 다음 날, 조스는 평소와 달리 유난히 공들여 화려하게 차려입었다. 그러고는 전날 밤에 일어난 일을 가족 누구에게도 굳이 한마디 비칠 필요를 느끼지 않은 채, 또 산책에 누구도 동행을 청하지 않고, 이른 시각에 길을 나섰다. 그리고 이내 코끼리 호텔 문 앞에서 무언가를 묻고 있는 모습이 눈에 띄었다. 축제 덕분에 호텔은 손님으로 가득 차 있었고, 거리에 놓인 탁자들은 벌써 담배를 피우며 그 나라 특유의 약한 맥주를 들이켜는 사람들로 둘러싸여 있었으며, 공용 객실들은 담배 연기로 자욱했다. 조스 씨는 특유의 거들먹거리는 태도와 어설픈 독일어로 찾는 사람에 대해 묻고는, 호텔 꼭대기 층으로 안내받았다. 어느 떠돌이 행상들이 묵으며 보석과 비단을 펼쳐 놓고 파는 이 층 객실을 지나, 도박 회사의 간부진이 차지한 삼 층 객실을 지나, 그 위로 이름난 보헤미아 곡예단과 재주꾼들이 묵는 사 층 객실을 지나, 그렇게 지붕 밑의 자그마한 골방까지 올라가는 것이었다. 그곳에는 학생들과 행상꾼들과 소상인들과 축제를 보러 시골에서 올라온 사람들 틈에, 베키가 작은 둥지를 틀고 있었다. 미인이 몸을 숨기기에는 더없이 더럽고 누추한 은신처였다.\n\n베키는 그런 생활이 마음에 들었다. 그녀는 행상이든, 도박꾼이든, 재주꾼이든, 학생이든, 그곳의 누구와도 격의 없이 어울렸다. 그녀는 취향으로 보나 처지로 보나 둘 다 보헤미안이었던 아버지와 어머니에게서 물려받은, 거칠고 떠돌아다니기 좋아하는 천성을 지니고 있었다. 곁에 귀족이 없으면 그 귀족의 시종과도 더없이 즐겁게 수다를 떨었던 것이다. 떠들썩한 소음, 부산스러움, 술, 담배 연기, 유대인 행상들의 잡담, 가난한 재주꾼들의 점잔 빼는 허풍, 도박판 직원들의 음흉한 말투, 학생들의 노래와 으스대는 꼴, 그리고 그곳 전체에서 웅웅거리며 들끓는 소란이, 운이 따르지 않아 셈도 치를 수 없을 때조차 이 자그마한 여인을 즐겁게 하고 간질였다. 그러니 전날 밤 어린 조지가 따 준 돈으로 지갑이 두둑해진 지금, 그 모든 부산스러움이 그녀에게 얼마나 유쾌했겠는가!\n\n조스가 마지막 계단을 끽끽거리며 헐떡헐떡 올라와, 층계참에 다다라서는 말도 못 한 채 얼굴을 닦기 시작했고, 이윽고 찾는 사람을 찾으라 일러 받은 방인 92호를 두리번거리고 있을 때, 맞은편 방인 90호의 문이 열려 있었다. 그 안에서는 승마 장화에 더러운 실내복 차림의 한 학생이 침대에 누워 긴 담뱃대를 빨고 있었고, 또 다른 학생은 긴 노란 머리에 장식 끈이 달린 외투를 걸치고서—역시나 몹시 멋을 부리고 또 몹시 더러웠는데—실제로 92호 앞에 무릎을 꿇은 채 열쇠 구멍에 대고 안에 있는 사람에게 애원을 늘어놓고 있었다.\n\n\"저리 가요.\" 어디선가 들어 본 목소리가 들려 조스를 전율케 했다. \"사람을 기다리고 있어요. 우리 할아버지를 기다린다고요. 그분이 거기서 당신을 보면 안 된단 말이에요.\"\n\n\"천사 같은 영국 아가씨여!\" 희끄무레한 갈색 곱슬머리에 큼지막한 반지를 낀, 무릎 꿇은 학생이 고래고래 외쳤다. \"부디 우리를 가엾이 여겨 주시오. 약속 하나만 잡읍시다. 나랑 프리츠랑 공원 안 여관에서 함께 식사합시다. 꿩 구이에 흑맥주, 자두 푸딩에 프랑스 와인을 차립시다. 안 와 주면 우린 죽고 말 거요.\"\n\n\"정말 죽고 말 거요.\" 침대에 누운 젊은 귀족이 거들었다. 조스는 이 주고받는 말을 다 엿들었지만, 그 말이 오가는 언어를 한 번도 배운 적이 없었던 까닭에 무슨 뜻인지 알아듣지 못했다.\n\n\"누메로 카테르방 두즈, 실 부 플레.\" 조스가 비로소 말이 나오자 더없이 거창한 투로 말했다.\n\n\"콰테르 팡 투스!\" 학생이 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다. 그러고는 제 방으로 후다닥 뛰어 들어가 문을 잠갔고, 조스는 그가 침대에 누운 동무와 함께 깔깔대는 소리를 들었다.\n\n벵골에서 온 신사가 이 일에 당황하여 우두커니 서 있는데, 92호의 문이 저절로 열리더니 베키의 자그마한 머리가, 짓궂고 장난기 가득한 표정으로 빠끔히 내다보았다. 그녀의 눈이 조스에게 닿았다. \"당신이군요.\" 그녀가 나오며 말했다. \"내가 얼마나 기다렸는지 몰라요! 잠깐만요! 아직요—잠시만 있으면 들어오셔도 돼요.\" 그 순간 그녀는 연지통 하나와 브랜디 병 하나, 그리고 먹다 남은 고기 한 접시를 침대 속으로 밀어 넣고, 머리를 한 번 매만진 다음, 마침내 손님을 안으로 들였다.\n\n그녀는 실내복 삼아 분홍색 도미노 가운을 걸치고 있었는데, 조금 빛이 바래고 더러웠으며 군데군데 포마드 자국이 묻어 있었다. 그러나 그 헐렁한 소맷자락 밖으로 드러난 두 팔은 더없이 희고 고왔고, 가운은 그 자그마한 허리에 묶여 있어 입은 이의 맵시 있는 작은 몸매를 그런대로 잘 살려 주고 있었다. 그녀는 조스의 손을 잡고 그를 자기 다락방으로 이끌었다. \"들어오세요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"들어와서 저랑 이야기해요. 저쪽 의자에 앉으세요.\" 그러고는 그 문관 양반의 손을 살짝 꼭 쥐고 웃으면서 그를 의자에 앉혔다. 자기는 침대에 자리를 잡았는데—물론 병과 접시 위는 아니었다—조스가 그 자리를 택했더라면 그가 거기 기대앉을 수도 있었던 것이다. 그렇게 그녀는 거기 앉아 옛 흠모자와 이야기를 나누었다. \"세월이 당신을 어쩜 이렇게 조금밖에 바꾸지 못했을까요.\" 그녀가 다정하고 살뜰한 눈빛으로 말했다. \"어디서 봤어도 당신을 알아봤을 거예요. 낯선 사람들 틈에서 오랜 친구의 꾸밈없고 정직한 얼굴을 다시 보는 게 얼마나 큰 위안인지 몰라요!\"\n\n그 꾸밈없고 정직한 얼굴은, 사실대로 말하자면 이 순간 솔직함이나 정직함과는 거리가 먼 온갖 표정을 띠고 있었다. 도리어 몹시 당혹스럽고 어리둥절한 기색이었다. 조스는 옛 연인이 머무는 이 기묘한 자그마한 방을 둘러보고 있었던 것이다. 그녀의 가운 하나가 침대 위에 걸쳐져 있었고, 또 하나는 문에 박힌 고리에 매달려 있었다. 보닛은 거울의 절반을 가리고 있었고, 그 거울 위에는 더없이 예쁘장한 청동빛 부츠 한 켤레가 놓여 있었다. 침대 머리맡 탁자에는 프랑스 소설 한 권과 양초가—밀랍은 아닌 양초가—놓여 있었다. 베키는 그것마저 침대 속에 처박을까 생각했지만, 잠자리에 들 때 촛불을 끄는 데 쓰던 자그마한 종이 촛불 끄개만 안에 넣어 두었다.\n\n\"어디서 봤어도 당신을 알아봤을 거예요.\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"여자는 어떤 것들은 결코 잊지 못하는 법이에요. 그리고 당신은 내가 난생처음—난생처음 본 남자였답니다.\"\n\n\"내가 정말로요?\" 조스가 말했다. \"이런 세상에, 당신이—당신이 설마 그럴 리가요.\"\n\n\"당신 누이동생과 함께 치즈윅에서 나왔을 때, 나는 거의 어린애나 다름없었어요.\" 베키가 말했다. \"그 사랑스러운 분은 어떻게 지내요? 아, 그분의 남편은 슬프게도 못된 사람이었어요. 그러니 그 가엾은 분이 질투한 게 다 나 때문이었지요. 마치 내가 그이한테 마음이라도 있었던 것처럼요, 휴! 하지만 그때 다른 누군가가—아니에요—옛날 얘긴 그만둬요.\" 그러고는 너덜너덜한 레이스가 달린 손수건으로 두 눈가를 훔쳤다.\n\n\"여기는 참 이상한 곳 아닌가요.\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"그것도 전혀 다른 세계에서 살아온 여자가 머물기에는 말이에요. 나는 너무도 많은 슬픔과 부당한 일을 겪었어요, 조지프 세들리. 너무도 모질게 고통받아서 때로는 거의 미칠 지경이랍니다. 어디 한 곳에 가만히 머물지 못하고, 늘 불안하고 불행한 채로 떠돌기만 해요. 친구들이 다 나를 배신했어요—전부요. 이 세상에 정직한 남자란 없어요. 나는 세상 누구보다 진실한 아내였어요. 비록 다른 누군가 때문에 홧김에 남편과 결혼했지만—그 얘긴 그만두죠. 나는 진실했는데, 그이는 나를 짓밟고 버렸어요. 나는 더없이 다정한 어머니였어요. 내게는 단 하나의 아이, 단 하나의 귀염둥이, 단 하나의 희망, 단 하나의 기쁨이 있었어요. 어미의 사랑으로 내 가슴에 품었던, 내 목숨이요, 내 기도요, 내—내 축복이었던 아이를요. 그런데 그자들이—그자들이 내게서 그 아이를 빼앗아 갔어요—내게서 빼앗아 갔다고요.\" 그러고는 절망에 찬 격정적인 몸짓으로 가슴에 손을 얹으며, 잠시 침대에 얼굴을 파묻었다.\n\n침대 속의 브랜디 병이 식은 소시지가 담긴 접시에 부딪쳐 쨍그랑 소리를 냈다. 두 물건 모두 그토록 큰 슬픔의 토로에 마음이 움직였던 것이 분명하다. 막스와 프리츠는 문가에서 베키 부인의 흐느낌과 울부짖음에 놀라며 귀를 기울이고 있었다. 조스 역시 옛 연인이 이런 처지에 놓인 것을 보고 적잖이 겁이 나고 마음이 흔들렸다. 그러자 그녀는 곧장 자기 사연을 풀어놓기 시작했다—어찌나 깔끔하고 단순하고 꾸밈없는 이야기였던지, 그것을 듣노라면 이 지상에서 마귀들의 흉악한 음모와 악행에 시달리도록 천국에서 도망쳐 나온 흰옷 입은 천사가 정말로 있었다면, 그 티 없는 존재가—그 비참하고 더럽혀지지 않은 순교자가, 지금 조스 앞 침대 위에—브랜디 병 위에 앉아 있다는 것이 더없이 분명해 보일 지경이었다.\n\n거기서 두 사람은 더없이 길고도 화기애애하고 은밀한 이야기를 나누었다. 그러는 사이에 조스 세들리는 (그러나 조금도 그를 겁먹게 하거나 불쾌하게 하지 않는 방식으로) 어쩐지 이런 사실들을 알게 되었다. 베키의 심장이 두근거리기를 처음 배운 것은 매혹적인 조스의 존재 앞에서였다는 것, 조지 오즈번이 분명 그녀에게 부당한 구애를 했고 그것으로 애밀리아의 질투와 두 사람 사이의 작은 불화가 설명될 법하다는 것, 그러나 베키는 그 불운한 장교에게 추호도 빌미를 준 적이 없으며, 처음 그를 본 바로 그날부터 단 한 번도 조스를 생각하지 않은 적이 없었다는 것을 말이다. 물론 결혼한 여자로서의 의무가 무엇보다 우선이었지만—그 의무를 그녀는 늘 지켜 왔고, 죽는 날까지, 아니면 크롤리 대령이 살고 있는 저 속담으로도 악명 높은 험한 기후가 그의 잔인함으로 진저리 나게 된 그 멍에에서 그녀를 풀어 줄 때까지 지켜 갈 작정이라는 것이었다.\n\n조스는 그녀가 더없이 매혹적인 여인들 가운데 하나일 뿐 아니라 더없이 정숙한 여인이라고 확신하며 자리를 떴다. 그러면서 그녀의 안녕을 위한 온갖 자비로운 계획을 마음속으로 굴렸다. 그녀에 대한 박해는 끝나야 마땅했다. 그녀는 자신이 그 장식과도 같았던 사교계로 돌아가야 마땅했다. 무엇을 해야 할지 알아보리라. 그녀는 저곳을 떠나 조용한 하숙으로 옮겨야 했다. 애밀리아가 와서 그녀를 만나고 벗이 되어 주어야 했다. 자기가 가서 그 일을 처리하고 소령과 의논하리라. 그녀는 그와 헤어지면서 진심에서 우러난 감사의 눈물을 흘렸고, 그 늠름하고 살진 신사가 허리를 숙여 그녀의 손에 입 맞추려 하자 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었다.\n\n그리하여 베키는 마치 자기가 안주인 노릇을 하는 궁전이라도 되는 양 우아하게 조스를 자기 다락방에서 배웅했다. 그 묵직한 신사가 계단 아래로 사라지자, 막스와 프리츠가 담뱃대를 입에 문 채 자기네 굴에서 나왔고, 그녀는 식은 빵과 소시지를 우물거리고 애용하는 브랜디 물을 들이켜면서 그들에게 조스 흉내를 내며 즐거워했다.\n\n조스는 더없이 엄숙한 걸음으로 도빈의 하숙으로 걸어가, 방금 알게 된 가슴 아픈 사연을 그에게 전했다. 그러나 전날 밤의 도박 이야기만은 입에 올리지 않았다. 그리고 두 신사는 머리를 맞대고 베키 부인을 도울 가장 좋은 방도를 의논했다. 그러는 동안 그녀는 중간에 끊겼던 포크 식사를 마저 끝내고 있었다.\n\n그녀는 어쩌다 그 작은 도시로 흘러들게 되었을까? 어쩌다 친구 하나 없이 홀로 떠돌게 되었을까? 학교에 다니는 사내아이들은 가장 처음 배우는 라틴어 교본에서 아베르누스로 내려가는 길은 무척 쉽다는 것을 배운다. 그녀의 추락 과정에 얽힌 사연의 그 사이 대목은 건너뛰기로 하자. 그녀는 지금 형편이 좋던 시절에 비해 더 나빠진 것은 아니었다—그저 운이 조금 따르지 않을 뿐이었다.\n\n* 아베르누스: 고대 로마 신화에서 저승으로 통하는 입구로 여겨진 호수. \"쉽게 내려간다\"는 것은 베르길리우스의 시구에서 온 표현이다.\n한편 애밀리아 부인으로 말하자면, 천성이 어찌나 무르고 어리석은지 누가 불행하다는 소리만 들으면 그 고통받는 이를 향해 곧장 마음이 녹아내리는 여인이었다. 게다가 그녀는 스스로 죽을죄가 될 만한 일을 생각하거나 저지른 적이 한 번도 없었던 까닭에, 훨씬 더 사리에 밝은 도덕가들이 보이는 그런 악에 대한 혐오를 지니지 못했다. 그녀가 곁에 다가오는 모든 이를 친절과 칭찬으로 버릇없게 만들었다 한들, 종을 울려 대답하게 하느라 번거롭게 했다고 하인들 모두에게 사과를 했다 한들, 비단 한 필을 보여 준 점원 아이에게 미안하다고 했다 한들, 길 쓰는 청소부에게 무릎을 굽혀 인사하며 그가 쓸어 놓은 길목이 참 깔끔하다고 한마디 추어올렸다 한들—그리고 그녀는 이 온갖 어리석은 짓들을 거의 하나도 빠짐없이 저지를 만한 사람이었으니—옛 지인이 비참한 처지라는 생각이 그녀의 마음을 누그러뜨릴 것은 틀림없는 일이었다. 또한 그녀는 누가 마땅히 벌받아 불행하다는 말 따위는 들으려고도 하지 않았다. 그녀와 같은 식의 법으로 다스려지는 세상이라면 그리 질서 정연한 거처는 못 될 것이다. 그러나 그녀 같은 부류의 여인은, 적어도 다스리는 자리에 있는 여인 중에는 그리 많지 않다. 이 부인으로 말하면, 내 생각에는 세상의 모든 감옥과 형벌과 수갑과 채찍질과 가난과 질병과 굶주림을 다 없애 버렸을 사람이며, 어찌나 줏대 없는 사람인지—우리는 이 점을 인정하지 않을 수 없는데—치명적인 모욕조차 잊어버릴 수 있는 사람이었다.\n\n소령이 조스에게서 방금 그가 겪은 감상적인 모험담을 전해 들었을 때, 그는 솔직히 말해 벵골에서 온 신사만큼 흥미를 느끼지는 않았다. 도리어 그의 흥분은 결코 유쾌한 종류의 것이 아니었다. 그는 고난에 처한 가엾은 여인을 두고 짧지만 점잖지 못한 표현을 입에 올렸는데, 사실인즉 이렇게 말한 것이다. \"그 앙큼한 계집이 또 모습을 드러냈단 말이오?\" 그는 그녀에게 추호도 호감을 가져 본 적이 없었고, 그녀의 초록빛 눈이 자기 눈을 한 번 바라보았다가 돌려 버린 바로 그 순간부터 진심으로 그녀를 미덥지 못하게 여겨 왔던 것이다.\n\n\"그 어린 악녀는 가는 곳마다 화근을 몰고 다니지.\" 소령이 무례하게 말했다. \"그동안 어떤 식으로 살아왔는지 누가 알겠소? 게다가 그 여자가 무슨 볼일로 여기 타국에 그것도 홀로 와 있단 말이오? 박해니 원수니 하는 소리는 내게 하지 마시오. 정직한 여자한테는 늘 친구가 있고, 결코 가족과 떨어지는 법이 없소. 어째서 남편을 떠났단 말이오? 당신 말마따나 그자가 평판 나쁘고 못된 인간이었을 수는 있소. 늘 그랬으니까. 나는 그 망할 사기 도박꾼을, 그자가 가엾은 조지를 속이고 등쳐 먹던 수법을 똑똑히 기억하오. 그 두 사람이 갈라설 때 무슨 추문이 있지 않았소? 뭔가 들은 것 같은데.\" 도빈 소령이 외쳤다. 그는 뒷공론에는 별 관심이 없는 사람이었고, 조스는 베키 부인이 모든 면에서 더없이 피해를 입은 정숙한 여인이라고 그를 납득시키려 애썼으나 헛수고였다.\n\n\"좋아요, 좋아. 조지 부인한테 물어봅시다.\" 그 빈틈없는 외교관 같은 소령이 말했다. \"그저 가서 그 부인과 의논해 봅시다. 적어도 그 부인이 훌륭한 판단을 내릴 줄 알고, 이런 일에서 무엇이 옳은지 안다는 데에는 당신도 동의할 테지요.\"\n\n\"흠! 에미는 아주 괜찮은 사람이지요.\" 마침 누이동생에게 푹 빠져 있지는 않았던 조스가 말했다.\n\n\"아주 괜찮다고요? 맹세코, 선생, 그분은 내가 평생 만나 본 가장 훌륭한 숙녀요.\" 소령이 불쑥 외쳤다. \"당장 가서 이 여자를 만나야 할지 말아야 할지 그분께 여쭤 봅시다—나는 그분의 판결에 만족하겠소.\" 그런데 이 얄미울 만치 교활한 능구렁이 같은 소령은 속으로 자기 쪽이 이긴 게 틀림없다고 생각하고 있었다. 그가 기억하기로 에미는 한때 레베카를 모질게, 그러나 마땅한 까닭으로 질투했고, 그 이름을 입에 올릴 때면 늘 움츠러들고 진저리를 쳤던 것이다—질투하는 여자는 결코 용서하지 않는 법이지, 도빈은 그렇게 생각했다. 그리하여 두 사람은 길 건너 조지 부인의 집으로 갔는데, 거기서 그녀는 슈트룸프 부인과의 음악 수업 중에 흐뭇하게 노래를 흥얼거리고 있었다.\n\n그 부인이 자리를 뜨자, 조스는 평소의 거창한 말투로 본론을 꺼냈다. \"애밀리아, 얘야.\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 방금 더없이 기이한—그래—이런 세상에! 더없이 기이한 일을 겪었단다—옛 친구를—그래, 너의 더없이 흥미로운 옛 친구를, 옛날로 치자면 말이다, 방금 여기서 만났는데, 네가 그 사람을 좀 만나 봤으면 한다.\"\n\n\"그 사람이요!\" 애밀리아가 말했다. \"누구예요? 도빈 소령님, 제발 제 가위 좀 망가뜨리지 마세요.\" 소령은 가위가 이따금 그 주인의 허리께에 매달려 있곤 하는 작은 사슬을 잡고 빙빙 돌리고 있어서, 자기 눈을 위태롭게 하던 참이었다.\n\n\"내가 몹시 싫어하는 여자요.\" 소령이 고집스럽게 말했다. \"그리고 당신도 사랑할 까닭이 없는 여자고요.\"\n\n\"레베카예요, 분명 레베카예요.\" 애밀리아가 얼굴을 붉히고 몹시 동요하며 말했다.\n\n\"맞소. 당신은 늘 맞는 말만 하지.\" 도빈이 대답했다. 브뤼셀, 워털루, 아득히 먼 옛 시절, 슬픔, 아픔, 추억이 한꺼번에 애밀리아의 여린 가슴속으로 밀려들어 거기에 모진 동요를 일으켰다.\n\n\"그 사람을 만나게 하지 마세요.\" 에미가 말을 이었다. \"난 그 사람을 만날 수 없어요.\"\n\n\"내가 그럴 거라 하지 않았소.\" 도빈이 조스에게 말했다.\n\n\"그 사람은 아주 불행해요, 그리고—뭐 그런 처지란다.\" 조스가 졸랐다. \"아주 가난하고 의지할 데 없고, 게다가 아팠단다—몹시 심하게 아팠어—그 남편이라는 못된 자가 그 사람을 버렸지.\"\n\n\"아!\" 애밀리아가 말했다.\n\n\"세상에 친구 하나 없단다.\" 조스가 제법 능란하게 말을 이어 갔다. \"그러면서 그 사람이 말하길, 너한테는 기댈 수 있을 것 같다더구나. 그 사람은 너무 비참해, 에미. 슬픔에 거의 미칠 지경이었어. 그 사연을 들으니 나도 정말 마음이 움직이더구나—맹세코, 정말 그랬다—그토록 모진 박해를 그토록 천사같이 견뎌 낸 사람은 일찍이 없었다고 할 만하지. 그 가족이 그 사람한테 더없이 모질게 굴었더구나.\"\n\n\"가엾은 사람!\" 애밀리아가 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 친구를 못 구하면 죽을 것 같다더구나.\" 조스가 낮고 떨리는 목소리로 말을 이었다. \"이런 세상에! 글쎄 그 사람이 스스로 목숨을 끊으려 했다지 뭐냐? 아편 정기를 늘 지니고 다닌단다—그 병을 그 방에서 봤어—그 비참하고 자그마한 방에서—삼류 호텔인 코끼리 호텔, 그것도 지붕 밑 맨 꼭대기에서 말이다. 내가 거기까지 갔단다.\"\n\n이 말은 에미에게 별 감흥을 주지 못하는 듯했다. 그녀는 심지어 살짝 미소를 짓기까지 했다. 아마도 조스가 헐떡거리며 계단을 오르는 모습이 떠올랐던 모양이다.\n\n\"그 사람은 슬픔에 제정신이 아니야.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"그 여자가 견뎌 낸 고통이란 듣기만 해도 끔찍할 지경이지. 그 사람한테 어린 사내아이가 하나 있었는데, 조지하고 동갑이었단다.\"\n\n\"네, 네, 기억나는 것 같아요.\" 에미가 한마디 했다. \"그래서요?\"\n\n\"여태껏 본 가장 아름다운 아이였지.\" 워낙 살집이 많고 쉽게 마음이 동하는 데다 베키가 들려준 이야기에 가슴이 뭉클해졌던 조스가 말했다. \"완벽한 천사였어, 제 어미를 끔찍이 따르던 아이였지. 그 악당들이 비명을 지르는 아이를 그 사람 품에서 떼어 냈고, 두 번 다시 그 사람을 못 보게 했다는구나.\"\n\n\"조지프 오라버니.\" 에미가 그 자리에서 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다. \"당장 그 사람을 만나러 가요.\" 그러고는 옆에 딸린 침실로 달려 들어가, 허둥지둥 보닛을 묶고는, 숄을 팔에 걸친 채 나와서 도빈에게 따라오라고 일렀다.\n\n그는 가서 그녀의 숄을—그것은 흰 캐시미어 숄로, 소령 본인이 인도에서 보내 준 것이었다—그녀의 어깨에 둘러 주었다. 그는 따르는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없음을 알았고, 그녀는 그의 팔에 손을 얹었으며, 그렇게 두 사람은 길을 나섰다.\n\n\"92호란다, 계단을 네 층 올라가야 해.\" 조스가 말했는데, 아마도 그 계단을 다시 오르고 싶지 않았던 모양이다. 다만 그는 코끼리 호텔이 서 있는 광장이 내려다보이는 자기 응접실 창가에 자리를 잡고, 두 사람이 시장을 가로질러 행진해 가는 모습을 지켜보았다.\n\n베키도 자기 다락방에서 그들을 본 것이 다행이었다. 그녀와 두 학생이 거기서 재잘대며 웃고 있었던 까닭이다. 그들은 베키의 할아버지가 왔다 간 일을—그 도착과 떠남을 직접 목격했던 것이다—두고 농담을 주고받던 참이었다. 그러나 그녀에게는 그들을 내보내고 자기 작은 방을 치워 둘 시간이 있었다. 오즈번 부인이 군주의 궁정에서 큰 총애를 받는 사람임을 알고 그만큼 그녀를 깍듯이 대하던 코끼리 호텔 주인이, 미라디와 헤어 소령이 계단을 다 오르도록 격려하며 지붕 밑 층으로 가는 길을 앞장서 안내했을 때 말이다.\n\n\"존귀하신 부인, 존귀하신 부인!\" 호텔 주인이 베키의 방문을 두드리며 말했다. 그는 전날만 해도 그녀를 그저 마담이라 불렀고 결코 정중하게 대하지 않았던 것이다.\n\n\"누구세요?\" 베키가 머리를 내밀며 말하더니, 작게 비명을 질렀다. 거기에 에미가 떨면서 서 있었고, 그 곁에는 키 큰 소령 도빈이 지팡이를 든 채 서 있었다.\n\n그는 가만히 서서 지켜보았고, 그 장면에 무척 흥미를 느끼고 있었다. 그러나 에미는 두 팔을 활짝 벌리고 레베카를 향해 달려들었고, 그 순간 그녀를 용서했으며, 진심을 다해 그녀를 끌어안고 입을 맞추었다. 아, 가엾은 것, 네 입술이 이토록 순결한 입맞춤에 닿아 본 적이 마지막으로 언제였더냐?\n\n『아만티움 이라이』\n\n애밀리아 같은 솔직함과 다정함은 베키처럼 굳어진 작은 망나니의 마음마저 움직일 만했다. 베키는 에미의 다정한 손길과 따뜻한 말에, 거의 감사라 할 만한 무언가로, 또한 비록 오래가지는 않더라도 한순간만큼은 거의 진심에 가까운 어떤 감정으로 화답했다. \"비명을 지르며 품에서 떼어 내진\" 아이 이야기는 그녀의 행운의 한 수였다. 그 가슴 찢어지는 불행으로 베키는 친구의 마음을 도로 얻은 것이며, 우리의 가엾고 순박한 어린 에미가 새로 만난 옛 지인에게 말을 건넨 맨 처음 화두 가운데 하나가 바로 그것이었으리라는 점은 충분히 짐작할 만하다.\n\n\"그래서 네 소중한 아이를 너한테서 데려갔단 말이지?\" 우리의 순진한 사람이 외쳤다. \"오, 레베카, 가엾고 고통받는 내 친구야, 나도 사내아이를 잃는다는 게 어떤 건지, 또 아이를 잃은 사람의 마음이 어떤지 알아. 하지만 자비로우신 하느님께서 내게 내 아이를 돌려주셨듯이, 부디 네게도 네 아이를 돌려주시기를.\"\n\n\"그 아이, 내 아이 말이지? 오, 그래, 내 고통은 끔찍했어.\" 베키는 그렇게 인정했는데, 어쩌면 양심의 가책이 전혀 없지는 않았을 것이다. 이토록 큰 믿음과 순진함에 답하느라 당장 거짓말부터 늘어놓아야 한다는 것이 그녀의 마음을 거슬리게 했다. 그러나 이런 종류의 위조를 시작하는 자가 겪는 불행이 바로 그것이다. 거짓말 하나가, 말하자면 만기가 돌아오면, 그 옛 어음을 막기 위해 또 다른 거짓말을 위조해야 하는 법이다. 그리하여 시중에 유통되는 거짓말의 재고는 필연적으로 불어나고, 들통날 위험은 날마다 커져 가는 것이다.\n\n\"내 고통은 말이야,\" 베키가 말을 이었다. \"그 애를 내게서 데려갔을 때(저 여자가 술병 위에 앉지 않아야 할 텐데) 견딜 수 없을 만큼 끔찍했어. 죽는 줄로만 알았지. 하지만 다행히도 뇌염에 걸려서, 의사도 가망이 없다며 손을 놓았는데, 그래도, 그래도 나는 회복했고, 그래서, 그래서 이렇게 가난뱅이에 외톨이 신세로 여기 있는 거야.\"\n\n\"그 애는 몇 살이지?\" 에미가 물었다.\n\n\"열한 살이야.\" 베키가 말했다.\n\n\"열한 살!\" 에미가 외쳤다. \"아니, 그럼 우리 조지랑 같은 해에 태어났네. 조지가 지금―\"\n\n\"알아, 알아.\" 베키가 외쳤는데, 사실 그녀는 어린 로던의 나이를 까맣게 잊고 있었던 것이다. \"슬픔 때문에 너무 많은 걸 잊어버렸어, 사랑하는 애밀리아. 나는 아주 많이 변했어. 가끔은 반쯤 미친 사람 같지. 그 애가 열한 살 때 내게서 데려갔어. 그 사랑스러운 얼굴에 축복이 있기를. 그 후로 다시는 못 봤어.\"\n\n\"그 애는 금발이었어, 아니면 검은 머리였어?\" 그 어처구니없을 만큼 순진한 에미가 말을 이었다. \"그 애 머리카락 좀 보여 줘.\"\n\n베키는 그녀의 순박함에 하마터면 웃음을 터뜨릴 뻔했다. \"오늘은 안 되겠어, 얘. 다음에, 내가 라이프치히에서―거기서 이곳으로 왔거든―트렁크가 도착하면. 그리고 행복했던 시절에 내가 그 애를 그린 자그마한 그림도 있어.\"\n\n\"가엾은 베키, 가엾은 베키!\" 에미가 말했다. \"나는 얼마나, 얼마나 감사해야 하는지\" (다만 우리가 어린 시절 집안 여인들에게서 주입받은 그 신심의 실천, 즉 우리가 다른 누군가보다 처지가 낫다 하여 감사해야 한다는 것이, 과연 매우 이치에 맞는 종교적 수행인지는 의문이지만) 그러고 나서 그녀는 늘 그렇듯, 자기 아들이야말로 온 세상에서 가장 잘생기고, 가장 착하고, 가장 영리한 아이라는 생각에 잠기기 시작했다.\n\n\"우리 조지를 보게 될 거야.\" 이것이 베키를 위로하려고 에미가 떠올릴 수 있는 최선이었다. 무엇이든 그녀를 편안하게 해 줄 수 있다면, 바로 그것이리라.\n\n그리하여 두 여자는 한 시간 남짓 계속 이야기를 나누었고, 그동안 베키는 새 친구에게 자신의 사적인 내력을 빠짐없이 온전히 들려줄 기회를 얻었다. 그녀는 로던 크롤리와의 결혼이 가족에게 어떻게 늘 더없는 적의로 받아들여졌는지, 시누이(교활한 여자)가 어떻게 남편의 마음에 그녀에 대한 독을 풀어 놓았는지, 그가 어떻게 가증스러운 교제 관계를 맺어 그녀에게서 애정을 거두어 갔는지를 늘어놓았다. 또 자신이 가장 사랑하는 사람에게서 받은 가난과 무관심과 냉대를 어떻게 다 견뎌 냈는지, 그 모든 것이 오로지 자기 아이를 위해서였는지를, 그리고 마침내 더없이 파렴치한 폭거로 인해 어떻게 남편과의 별거를 요구하지 않을 수 없는 지경에 내몰렸는지를 이야기했다. 그 비열한 인간이, 자기가 어느 대단하고 막강하지만 파렴치한 인물―바로 스타인 후작 말이다―의 힘을 빌려 출세를 도모할 수 있도록 그녀가 제 정조를 희생하라고 요구하기를 서슴지 않았다는 것이다. 그 잔악무도한 괴물 같으니!\n\n자신의 파란만장한 내력 가운데 이 대목을 베키는 더없이 여성스러운 섬세함과 더없이 분개에 찬 정숙함으로 들려주었다. 이 모욕 때문에 남편의 지붕 아래서 도망치지 않을 수 없게 되자, 그 비겁한 자는 그녀의 아이를 빼앗아 가는 것으로 복수를 밀어붙였다는 것이다. 그리하여 자신은 가난하고, 보호받지 못하고, 친구도 없고, 비참한 떠돌이 신세가 되었다고 베키는 말했다.\n\n에미는 제법 길게 이어진 이 이야기를, 그녀의 성품을 아는 이들이라면 능히 짐작할 만한 모습으로 받아들였다. 그녀는 그 비참한 로던과 파렴치한 스타인의 행실에 관한 이야기에 분개하여 몸을 떨었다. 베키가 자기 귀족 친척들의 박해와 남편의 변심을 묘사하는 문장 하나하나마다, 에미의 두 눈은 감탄의 부호를 찍었다. (베키는 남편을 욕하지 않았다. 분노라기보다는 차라리 슬픔에 찬 어조로 말했다. 그녀는 그를 너무도 애틋하게 사랑했을 뿐이며, 게다가 그가 자기 아들의 아버지가 아니던가?) 그리고 아이와 헤어지던 장면으로 말하자면, 베키가 그것을 읊는 동안 에미는 손수건 뒤로 아예 자취를 감춰 버렸으니, 그 빼어난 어린 비극 배우는 자기 연기가 관객에게 자아낸 효과를 보며 분명 흐뭇했을 것이다.\n\n숙녀들이 대화를 이어 가는 동안, 애밀리아의 변함없는 호위자인 소령은(물론 그는 그들의 회담을 방해하고 싶지 않았고, 게다가 천장이 모자의 보풀을 스칠 만큼 좁은 계단 통로를 삐걱대며 오르내리는 데 다소 진력이 나 있었다) 집 일층으로 내려가, 계단이 통하는 그 큰 방, 곧 엘리펀트를 드나드는 온갖 사람들이 함께 쓰는 공동 방으로 들어섰다. 이 방은 늘 담배 연기가 자욱하고 맥주가 흥건히 흩뿌려져 있다. 더러운 탁자 위에는 투숙객들을 위한 우지 양초와 그에 짝을 이루는 놋쇠 촛대가 수십 개씩 늘어서 있고, 그 양초들 위로는 객실 열쇠들이 줄지어 걸려 있다. 에미는 조금 전 얼굴을 붉히며 이 방을 지나갔는데, 그곳에는 온갖 부류의 사람들이 모여 있었다. 짐짝을 짊어진 티롤 출신 장갑 장수들과 도나우 강변에서 온 아마포 상인들, 버터 바른 빵과 고기로 기운을 채우는 학생들, 끈적끈적하고 맥주에 전 탁자에서 카드놀이나 도미노를 하는 한량들, 공연이 잠시 멈춘 틈에 원기를 돋우는 곡예사들―한마디로, 장날의 독일 여관이 풍기는 온갖 연기와 소음이 거기 있었다. 종업원이 으레 그렇듯 소령에게 맥주 한 잔을 가져다주었고, 그는 시가 한 대를 꺼내 들고, 자기가 맡은 사람이 내려와 그를 찾을 때까지 그 해로운 풀잎과 신문으로 시간을 때웠다.\n\n이윽고 막스와 프리츠가 모자를 한쪽으로 비뚜름히 쓰고, 박차를 짤랑이며, 문장이 화려하게 새겨지고 술이 탐스럽게 달린 파이프를 물고 아래층으로 내려와, 90호 열쇠를 걸이에 걸고 버터 바른 빵과 맥주를 한 차례 주문했다. 두 사람은 소령 옆에 앉아 대화에 빠져들었는데, 그는 어쩔 수 없이 그 일부를 듣게 되었다. 대화는 주로 \"여우\"와 \"속물\"에 관한 것, 그리고 인근 쇼펜하우젠 대학에서 벌어진 결투며 술판에 관한 것이었다. 보아하니 그들은 그 이름난 학문의 전당에서 베키를 곁에 태우고 막 급행 마차를 타고 와, 품퍼니켈의 결혼 축제에 참석하려는 참이었다.\n\n\"저 영국 여자라는 칭호는 아무래도 안면이 있는 사이인 것 같아.\" 프랑스어를 아는 막스가 동료 프리츠에게 말했다. \"저 뚱뚱한 할아버지가 가고 나니까 예쁘장한 동포 아가씨가 하나 왔거든. 그 자그마한 여자 방에서 둘이 재잘거리고 훌쩍거리는 걸 들었지.\"\n\n\"그 여자 음악회 표를 사 줘야겠어.\" 프리츠가 말했다. \"돈 있어, 막스?\"\n\n\"흥.\" 다른 쪽이 말했다. \"그 음악회는 구름 속의 음악회야. 한스 말로는 그 여자가 라이프치히에서도 음악회를 광고했고 대학생들이 표를 많이 샀다더군. 그런데 노래도 안 부르고 떠나 버렸대. 어제 마차에서는 자기 피아노 반주자가 드레스덴에서 병이 났다고 하더라고. 노래를 못하는 게 분명해. 목소리가 너처럼 갈라졌거든, 이 맥주에 절은 허풍쟁이 양반아!\"\n\n\"갈라졌고말고. 창문 너머로 그 여자가 '발코니 위의 장미'라는 끔찍한 영국 발라드를 연습하는 걸 들었어.\"\n\n\"술 마시기랑 노래 부르기는 같이 안 가는 법이지.\" 코가 빨간 프리츠가 말했는데, 그는 분명 전자의 오락을 더 좋아했다. \"안 돼, 너는 그 여자 표를 한 장도 사면 안 돼. 그 여자 어젯밤에 트랑테 에 카랑트로 돈을 땄거든. 내가 봤어. 어린 영국 사내아이한테 대신 패를 잡게 하더군. 네 돈은 거기서나 극장에서 쓰자고. 아니면 아우렐리우스 정원에서 그 여자한테 프랑스 와인이나 코냑을 한턱내든가. 하지만 표는 사지 말자. 어떻게 생각해? 자, 맥주 한 잔 더?\" 그리하여 둘은 차례로 금빛 구레나룻을 그 느끼한 술잔에 파묻었다가, 구레나룻을 비비 꼬며 거들먹거리는 걸음으로 장터로 나갔다.\n\n90호 열쇠가 걸이에 걸리는 것을 보았고 또 두 젊은 대학 멋쟁이의 대화를 들은 소령은, 그들의 이야기가 베키에 관한 것임을 어렵잖이 알아차렸다. \"저 작은 악녀가 옛날 수법을 또 부리는군.\" 그는 그렇게 생각하며, 조스를 상대로 한 그 필사적인 수작질과 그 모험의 우스꽝스러운 결말을 목격했던 옛날을 떠올리고 미소 지었다. 그와 조지는 그 후로도 그 일을 두고 자주 함께 웃곤 했는데, 조지가 결혼하고 몇 주 지났을 무렵, 그 역시 이 작은 키르케의 그물에 걸려들어, 동료가 분명 의심하면서도 모른 척하는 편을 택한 그런 사이를 그녀와 맺기 전까지는 그랬다. 윌리엄은 너무나 상심했거나 부끄러웠던 탓에 그 수치스러운 비밀의 진상을 캐물을 엄두를 내지 못했지만, 한 번은 조지가, 분명 마음에 회한을 품은 채 그 일을 넌지시 입에 올린 적이 있었다. 워털루 전투가 벌어지던 날 아침, 두 젊은이가 자기네 전열 앞에 나란히 서서 맞은편 고지를 뒤덮은 프랑스군의 검은 무리를 바라보고 있을 때, 그리고 비가 내리고 있을 때의 일이었다. \"어떤 여자랑 어리석은 정사에 얽혀 들었어.\" 조지가 말했다. \"우리가 이렇게 진군해 떠나게 돼서 다행이야. 내가 쓰러지면, 부디 에미가 그 일을 영영 모르기를 바라. 아예 시작도 안 했더라면 좋았을걸, 하느님 맙소사!\" 그래서 윌리엄은, 오스본이 아내 곁을 떠난 뒤, 그리고 첫날 카트르브라 전투를 치른 뒤에, 동료에게 자기 아버지와 아내 이야기를 진지하고 애정 어린 어조로 했다는 사실을 떠올리며 흐뭇해했고, 또 한 번 이상 그 이야기로 가엾은 조지의 미망인을 달래 주기도 했다. 윌리엄은 늙은 오스본과의 대화에서도 이 사실들을 무척 강하게 역설했고, 그리하여 그 노신사의 생애가 막 저물어 갈 무렵, 그가 아들의 기억과 화해하도록 이끄는 다리 노릇을 했던 것이다.\n\n\"그래, 이 악녀가 아직도 정사질을 계속하고 있군.\" 윌리엄은 생각했다. \"여기서 백 마일은 떨어진 데 있으면 좋으련만. 가는 곳마다 화근을 몰고 다닌다니까.\" 그리하여 그는 두 손에 머리를 파묻은 채, 코앞에 지난주 품퍼니켈 신문을 읽지도 않고 펼쳐 놓은 채로 이런 불길한 예감과 이 불편한 상념의 갈래를 좇고 있었는데, 그때 누군가 양산으로 그의 어깨를 톡톡 쳤고, 고개를 들어 보니 애밀리아 부인이었다.\n\n이 여자는 도빈 소령을 마음대로 부리는 버릇이 있었으니(가장 나약한 사람일수록 누군가에게는 군림하려 드는 법이다), 그녀는 그에게 이래라저래라 명령하고, 토닥거리고, 마치 그가 커다란 뉴펀들랜드 개라도 되는 양 물건을 가져오라 들고 오라 시켰다. 그도 말하자면 그녀가 \"올라가, 도빈!\" 하고 외치면 물속으로 뛰어들고, 입에 그녀의 손가방을 문 채 그녀 뒤를 종종거리며 따라가는 것을 좋아했다. 이 이야기에서 독자가 소령이 푹 빠진 얼간이라는 사실을 알아차리지 못했다면, 이 글은 거의 헛수고로 쓰인 셈이다.\n\n\"왜 저를 데리고 내려가시려고 기다리지 않으셨어요, 소령님?\" 그녀는 고개를 살짝 까딱이고 더없이 비꼬는 투로 무릎 굽혀 인사하며 말했다.\n\n\"통로에서는 똑바로 설 수가 없었거든요.\" 그는 우스꽝스럽게 미안해하는 표정으로 대답했다. 그러고는 그녀에게 팔을 내주고 그 끔찍하게 연기 자욱한 곳에서 데리고 나오게 된 것이 기뻐, 종업원 생각은 까맣게 잊은 채 그냥 걸어 나가려 했는데, 그 젊은이가 뒤쫓아 와 엘리펀트 문턱에서 그를 붙잡고는 마시지도 않은 맥주값을 치르게 했다. 에미는 웃었다. 그녀는 그를 외상값을 떼먹고 달아나려는 짓궂은 사람이라 부르며, 사실 그 상황과 그 묽은 맥주에 어울리는 농담 몇 마디를 던졌다. 그녀는 기분이 한껏 들떠 명랑한 채로, 매우 경쾌하게 장터를 가로질러 종종걸음을 쳤다. 그녀는 당장 조스를 보고 싶어 했다. 소령은 애밀리아 부인이 보이는 그 성마른 애정에 웃음을 지었으니, 실은 그녀가 오빠를 \"당장\" 보고 싶어 하는 일이 그리 흔치는 않았기 때문이다. 두 사람은 그 민간인을 이층 응접실에서 발견했다. 에미가 다락방에서 친구와 단둘이 틀어박혀 있고 소령이 아래층 공동 방의 끈적이는 탁자를 두드리며 시간을 보내던 지난 한 시간 동안, 그는 방 안을 서성이고, 손톱을 물어뜯고, 적어도 백 번은 장터 너머 엘리펀트 쪽을 내다보았던 것이다. 그 역시 오스본 부인이 무척 보고 싶었던 것이다.\n\n\"그래?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"가엾고 가엾은 사람, 얼마나 고생을 했는지!\" 에미가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 하느님 맙소사, 그렇지.\" 조스가 고개를 끄덕이며 말하니, 그 두 볼이 젤리처럼 출렁였다.\n\n\"페인 방을 그 친구한테 쓰게 하면 되겠어. 페인은 위층으로 올라가면 되고.\" 에미가 말을 이었다. 페인은 오스본 부인의 차분한 영국인 하녀이자 시중을 드는 사람으로, 안내인이 으레 그렇듯 그녀에게 알랑거렸고, 조지는 독일 강도와 유령 이야기를 늘어놓으며 그녀를 무던히도 놀려 대곤 했다. 그녀는 주로 투덜거리고, 안주인에게 이래라저래라 명령하고, 다음 날 아침이면 고향 마을 클래펌으로 돌아가겠다는 뜻을 밝히며 시간을 보냈다. \"페인 방을 쓰게 하면 돼.\" 에미가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 설마 그 여자를 이 집에 들이겠다는 건 아니겠지?\" 소령이 벌떡 일어서며 불쑥 내뱉었다.\n\n\"당연히 들여야지요.\" 애밀리아가 세상에 둘도 없이 천진한 투로 말했다. \"화내면서 가구 부수지 마세요, 도빈 소령님. 당연히 그 친구를 여기 들일 거예요.\"\n\n\"당연하지, 얘야.\" 조스가 말했다.\n\n\"그 가엾은 사람이, 그 온갖 고생을 하고 나서.\" 에미가 말을 이었다. \"그 끔찍한 은행가는 파산해서 달아나 버렸고, 남편이라는 인간은―그 사악한 작자는―그 친구를 버리고 아이까지 빼앗아 갔는데\" (여기서 그녀는 자그마한 두 주먹을 꽉 쥐고 더없이 위협적인 자세로 앞에 들이밀었으니, 소령은 그토록 겁 없는 여장부를 보고 흐뭇해졌다) \"그 가엾은 사람을! 그렇게 외톨이로, 입에 풀칠하려고 별수 없이 노래 교습까지 해야 하는데, 그런데도 여기 안 들인다고요!\"\n\n\"교습은 받으세요, 조지 부인.\" 소령이 외쳤다. \"하지만 그 여자를 집에 들이지는 마세요. 제발 부탁입니다, 그러지 마세요.\"\n\n\"쳇.\" 조스가 말했다.\n\n\"늘 선량하고 다정하셨던 분이―적어도 예전엔 그러셨잖아요―저는 소령님이 놀랍네요, 윌리엄 소령님.\" 애밀리아가 외쳤다. \"아니, 그 친구가 이토록 비참할 때가 아니면 대체 언제 도와줘요? 지금이야말로 그 친구한테 도움이 되어 줄 때라고요. 제가 사귄 가장 오래된 친구인데, 그런데도―\"\n\n\"그 여자가 늘 당신 친구였던 건 아니지요, 애밀리아.\" 소령이 말했으니, 그는 단단히 화가 나 있었던 것이다. 이 말은 에미에게 너무 지나쳤다. 그녀는 거의 사납다 할 만큼 소령의 얼굴을 똑바로 노려보며 말했다. \"부끄러운 줄 아세요, 도빈 소령님!\" 그러고는 이 한 발을 쏘아붙인 뒤, 더없이 위엄 있는 태도로 방을 나가, 모욕당한 자존심과 함께 자기 방문을 거세게 닫아 버렸다.\n\n\"하필 그 일을 들먹이다니!\" 문이 닫히자 그녀가 말했다. \"오, 그걸 내게 상기시키다니 너무 잔인해.\" 그러고는 늘 그렇듯 그곳에 걸려 있는 조지의 초상화를, 그리고 그 아래 걸린 아들의 초상화를 올려다보았다. \"잔인한 사람. 내가 그 일을 용서했는데, 그가 그걸 입에 올려야 했을까? 아니야. 게다가 내 질투가 얼마나 사악하고 근거 없는 것이었는지 바로 그 사람 입으로 들어서 알게 됐잖아. 당신은 결백했어요. 오, 그래요, 당신은 결백했어요, 하늘에 계신 나의 성인이여!\"\n\n그녀는 부들부들 떨고 분개하며 방 안을 서성였다. 그녀는 초상화가 걸린 서랍장으로 가 거기에 기대어, 그것을 보고 또 보았다. 그 두 눈은 그녀를 내려다보며 나무라는 듯했고, 그 책망은 그녀가 바라볼수록 더욱 깊어지는 것만 같았다. 그 짧았던 사랑의 절정기에 깃든, 일찍이 소중하고 소중했던 추억들이 그녀에게 와락 되밀려 왔다. 세월이 겨우 아물게 했던 상처가 새로이 피를 흘렸으니, 오, 얼마나 쓰라리게 흘렸던가! 그녀는 자기 앞에 있는 남편의 책망을 견딜 수 없었다. 있을 수 없는 일이었다. 결코, 결코.\n\n가엾은 도빈, 가엾은 늙은 윌리엄! 그 불운한 한마디가 여러 해 공들인 일을―사랑과 변함없음으로 쌓아 올린 한평생의 길고 고된 건축물을, 그것도 묻혀 버린 정열과 헤아릴 수 없는 분투와 알려지지 않은 희생이 깃든 은밀하고 숨겨진 토대 위에 세워 올린 그 건축물을 허물어뜨렸다. 작은 한마디가 내뱉어졌고, 그 아름다운 희망의 궁전이 와르르 무너져 내렸다. 한마디 말에, 그가 평생토록 꾀어 보려 애써 온 그 새가 훌쩍 날아가 버렸다!\n\n윌리엄은 애밀리아의 표정을 보고 큰 위기가 닥쳤음을 알았지만, 그래도 더없이 힘주어 세들리에게 레베카를 조심하라고 거듭 간청했다. 그는 간절히, 거의 미친 듯이 조스에게 그녀를 받아들이지 말라고 당부했다. 그는 세들리 씨에게 적어도 그녀에 관해 알아보기라도 하라고 청하며, 자기가 듣기로 그녀가 노름꾼과 평판 나쁜 자들과 어울려 다닌다고 일러 주었다. 그녀가 지난날 어떤 못된 짓을 저질렀는지, 그녀와 크롤리가 어떻게 가엾은 조지를 잘못 이끌어 파멸로 몰아넣었는지, 그녀가 이제 자기 입으로 고백했듯이 어떻게 남편과 갈라섰는지, 어쩌면 그럴 만한 까닭이 있어서였는지를 지적했다. 세상 물정이라고는 아무것도 모르는 그의 누이에게 그녀가 얼마나 위험한 동무가 되겠는가! 윌리엄은 자기가 끌어낼 수 있는 온갖 웅변을 다해, 그리고 이 조용한 신사가 평소 보이던 것보다 훨씬 더 큰 열의를 다해 조스에게 레베카를 그의 집안에 들이지 말라고 간청했다.\n\n만약 그가 덜 격렬했거나 더 노련했더라면, 조스를 향한 그 간청에 성공했을지도 모른다. 그러나 그 민간인은 소령이 자기를 향해 늘 드러내 보인다고 여기는(실제로 그는 이 견해를 안내인 키르슈 씨에게 털어놓았는데, 도빈 소령은 이번 여행에서 그의 계산서를 일일이 따졌고, 그래서 그는 주인 편을 들었다) 그 잘난 척하는 태도를 적잖이 못마땅해하고 있었다. 그리하여 그는 자기 명예쯤은 스스로 지킬 능력이 있다느니, 제 일에 누가 간섭하는 것을 원치 않는다느니, 한마디로 소령에게 반기를 들겠다느니 하며 큰소리치는 연설을 시작했는데, 그때 다소 길고 험악하게 이어지던 이 언쟁이 더없이 간단한 방식으로, 곧 베키 부인이 엘리펀트 호텔의 짐꾼에게 자기의 더없이 초라한 짐을 맡겨 들고 도착하는 바람에 끝이 났다.\n\n그녀는 애정 어린 공경의 태도로 주인에게 인사했고, 도빈 소령에게는 움츠러들면서도 우호적인 인사를 건넸다. 그녀의 직감은, 그가 자기의 적이며 줄곧 자기를 헐뜯어 왔음을 단박에 일러 주었던 것이다. 그녀가 도착하며 일으킨 부산함과 소란에 애밀리아가 방에서 나왔다. 에미는 다가가 더없이 따뜻하게 손님을 끌어안았고, 소령에게는 화난 눈길을 한 번 던질 뿐 아무 관심도 보이지 않았으니―그것은 이 가엾고 자그마한 여인이 태어난 이래 그 얼굴에 떠오른 가장 부당하고 경멸에 찬 눈빛이었을 것이다. 그러나 그녀에게는 그녀 나름의 사사로운 이유가 있었고, 기어이 그에게 화를 내기로 작정한 터였다. 그리고 도빈은 패배가 아니라 그 부당함에 분개하며, 그 자그마한 여인이 굳이 작별 인사로 보낸 그 사람 죽이는 듯한 무릎 인사 못지않게 거만한 절을 그녀에게 하고는 떠나 버렸다.\n\n그가 가고 나자 에미는 레베카에게 유난히 활기차고 다정하게 굴며, 방마다 부산을 떨고 손님을 자기 방에 들이는 데 우리의 차분한 어린 친구가 좀처럼 보이지 않던 열의와 활기를 쏟았다. 그러나 부당한 일을 저지를 때는, 특히 나약한 사람일수록, 그것을 재빨리 해치우는 것이 상책이다. 그리고 에미는 지금의 처신으로 자기가 대단한 결단력과 올바른 감정을, 그리고 고인이 된 오스본 대위에 대한 깊은 존경을 드러내 보이고 있다고 여겼다.\n\n조지가 축제에서 돌아와 저녁 식사 시간에 와 보니, 식탁에는 늘 그렇듯 네 사람 분의 그릇이 차려져 있었다. 그런데 그 자리 중 하나는 도빈 소령 대신 한 숙녀가 차지하고 있었다. \"어이! 도브는 어디 갔어요?\" 어린 신사가 늘 그렇듯 거침없는 말투로 물었다. \"도빈 소령님은 밖에서 식사하시나 보다.\" 어머니가 그렇게 말하고는, 아이를 끌어당겨 마구 입을 맞추고, 이마에서 머리카락을 쓸어 넘긴 뒤 크롤리 부인에게 소개했다. \"내 아들이에요, 레베카.\" 오스본 부인이 말했는데―마치 이렇게 말하는 듯했다―세상에 이런 아이가 또 있을까요? 베키는 황홀한 눈길로 아이를 바라보며 그 손을 애틋하게 꼭 쥐었다. \"귀여운 아이!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"꼭 우리―같구나.\" 북받치는 감정에 더는 말을 잇지 못했지만, 애밀리아는 베키가 마치 입 밖에 낸 것처럼 또렷이 알아들었다. 그녀가 제 사랑스러운 아이를 생각하고 있다는 것을. 어쨌든 친구와 함께 있다는 사실에 크롤리 부인은 위로를 받았고, 저녁을 아주 잘 먹었다.\n\n식사하는 동안 그녀는 여러 차례 말할 기회가 있었고, 그럴 때면 조지가 그녀를 빤히 쳐다보며 그 말에 귀를 기울였다. 후식이 나올 무렵 에미는 다른 집안일을 챙기러 나가 있었고, 조스는 큰 안락의자에 앉아 갈리냐니 신문을 보며 꾸벅꾸벅 졸고 있었다. 조지와 새로 온 손님은 바싹 붙어 앉아 있었는데―아이는 아까부터 한 번 이상 무언가 안다는 듯한 눈길로 그녀를 보고 있더니, 마침내 호두까기를 내려놓았다.\n\n\"저기요.\" 조지가 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 말을 하려고?\" 베키가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"아줌마는 루즈 에 누아르에서 가면 쓰고 있던 그 부인이잖아요.\"\n\n\"쉿! 이 작은 능청꾼아.\" 베키가 그의 손을 잡아 입을 맞추며 말했다. \"네 외삼촌도 거기 계셨단다. 그러니 엄마가 알면 안 돼.\"\n\n\"아, 네―절대로요.\" 어린 녀석이 대답했다.\n\n\"보세요, 우린 벌써 아주 좋은 친구가 됐어요.\" 이제 막 다시 들어온 에미에게 베키가 말했다. 그리고 인정하지 않을 수 없는 것은, 오스본 부인이 더없이 분별 있고 상냥한 동무를 자기 집에 들여놓았다는 점이다.\n\n윌리엄은 자신을 기다리고 있는 온갖 배신은 아직 까맣게 모른 채로, 잔뜩 분개하여 미친 듯이 거리를 돌아다니다가 공사관 서기관 테이프웜과 마주쳤고, 그는 윌리엄을 저녁 식사에 초대했다. 식사를 하면서 윌리엄은 기회를 보아 서기관에게, 자기가 알기로 런던에서 제법 떠들썩하게 굴었던 어느 로던 크롤리 부인이라는 사람에 대해 아는 게 있느냐고 물었다. 그러자 물론 런던의 온갖 소문을 다 꿰고 있는 데다 가운트 부인의 친척이기도 한 테이프웜은, 깜짝 놀란 소령의 귀에 베키와 그 남편에 관한 이야기를 어찌나 줄줄이 쏟아 냈던지 질문한 사람을 아연실색하게 만들었으니, 이 이야기의 모든 대목을 그가 채워 준 셈이다. 실은 바로 그 식탁에서, 여러 해 전에, 지금 이 글을 쓰는 사람이 그 이야기를 들을 기쁨을 누렸던 것이다. 터프토, 스타인, 크롤리 일가, 그리고 그들의 내력―베키와 그녀의 지난 삶에 얽힌 모든 것이 그 신랄한 외교관의 입을 거쳐 낱낱이 까발려졌다. 그는 모든 것을, 그리고 온 세상 사람들에 관해 그 밖에도 숱한 것을 알고 있었다. 한마디로, 그는 순박한 마음씨의 소령에게 더없이 놀라운 폭로를 쏟아부었다. 도빈이 오스본 부인과 세들리 씨가 그녀를 집에 들였다고 하자, 테이프웜은 소령을 질겁하게 할 만큼 한바탕 폭소를 터뜨리고는, 차라리 감옥에 사람을 보내, 머리를 빡빡 깎고 노란 죄수복을 입은 채 둘씩 쇠사슬에 묶여 품퍼니켈 거리를 쓰는 신사 한둘을 데려다가 먹이고 재우면서 그 작은 망나니 조지의 가정 교사로 삼는 편이 낫지 않겠느냐고 물었다.\n\n이 정보는 소령을 적잖이 놀라게 하고 또 소름 끼치게 했다. 그날 아침에(레베카와 마주치기 전에) 그날 밤 애밀리아가 궁정 무도회에 가기로 약속이 되어 있었다. 거기가 바로 그가 그녀에게 이야기를 꺼낼 자리였다. 소령은 집으로 가 군복을 차려입고, 오스본 부인을 보리라는 희망을 품고 궁정으로 향했다. 그녀는 끝내 오지 않았다. 그가 숙소로 돌아왔을 때 세들리네 거처의 불은 모두 꺼져 있었다. 그녀를 아침까지 볼 수 없었던 것이다. 이 끔찍한 비밀을 안고 잠자리에 든 그가 그날 밤 어떤 잠을 잤을지는 나도 알 수 없다.\n\n이튿날 아침, 적당한 가장 이른 시각에 그는 하인을 길 건너로 보내, 그녀와 꼭 긴히 이야기를 나누고 싶다는 쪽지를 전했다. 오스본 부인이 몹시 몸이 좋지 않아 방에서 나오지 않고 있다는 전갈이 돌아왔다.\n\n그녀 역시 그날 밤 내내 깨어 있었다. 그녀는 전에도 백 번은 마음을 뒤흔들었던 어떤 일을 골똘히 생각하고 있었다. 백 번이나 마음을 굽히기 직전까지 갔다가도, 그녀는 자기에게 너무 벅차다고 느껴지는 그 희생 앞에서 번번이 움츠러들었다. 그의 사랑과 변함없음에도, 그리고 자신도 인정하는 호감과 존경과 감사에도 불구하고, 그녀는 그럴 수가 없었다. 은혜가 무엇이고, 변함없음이나 공덕이 다 무엇이란 말인가? 한 처녀의 곱슬머리 한 가닥, 구레나룻의 터럭 한 올이면 그 모든 것을 한순간에 저울 반대편으로 기울게 하는 법이다. 그런 것들은 다른 여자들에게나 마찬가지로 에미에게도 무게가 나가지 않았다. 그녀는 그것들을 저울에 올려 보았고, 그것들이 통과되기를 바랐지만, 그럴 수 없었다. 그리하여 그 무자비한 자그마한 여인은 구실 하나를 찾아냈고, 자유로워지기로 작정했던 것이다.\n\n마침내 오후가 되어 소령이 애밀리아에게 들어가도 좋다는 허락을 얻었을 때, 그는 이제 오랜 세월 익숙해진 그 다정하고 따뜻한 환대 대신, 무릎 굽혀 절하는 인사와, 장갑 낀 자그마한 손 하나를―그것도 내밀자마자 곧바로 거두어들이는 손을 맞이했다.\n\n레베카 역시 그 방에 있었고, 미소를 띤 채 손을 내밀며 그를 맞으러 다가왔다. 도빈은 다소 어쩔 줄 모르며 뒷걸음질 쳤다. \"저, 죄송합니다, 부인.\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 제가 지금 이곳에 온 건 당신의 친구로서가 아니라는 걸 분명히 말씀드려야겠군요.\"\n\n\"흥! 제기랄, 이런 일은 그만둡시다!\" 조스가 외쳤으니, 그는 겁에 질려 이 소동에서 벗어나고 싶어 안달이 났던 것이다.\n\n\"도빈 소령님이 레베카에 대해 무슨 험담을 하려는 건지 궁금하네요.\" 애밀리아가 약간 떨리는, 낮고 또렷한 목소리로, 두 눈에 자못 단호한 빛을 띠고 말했다.\n\n\"내 집에서 이런 일은 절대 용납 못 해.\" 조스가 다시 끼어들었다. \"절대 안 된다니까. 그리고 도빈, 제발 부탁이네, 그만두게.\" 그러고는 부들부들 떨며 얼굴이 새빨개진 채 주위를 둘러보더니, 크게 한숨을 한 번 내쉬고는 문 쪽으로 향했다.\n\n\"소중한 친구분!\" 레베카가 천사처럼 다정하게 말했다. \"도빈 소령님이 저에 대해 무슨 말을 하시려는지 꼭 들어 보세요.\"\n\n\"안 들어, 안 듣는다니까.\" 조스가 목청껏 꽥 소리를 지르고는, 실내복 자락을 그러모아 쥐고 사라져 버렸다.\n\n\"우린 여자 둘뿐이에요.\" 애밀리아가 말했다. \"이제 말씀하셔도 돼요, 소령님.\"\n\n\"저를 대하는 이런 태도는 당신답지 않은 것이군요, 애밀리아.\" 소령이 거만하게 대답했다. \"그리고 제가 여자들에게 늘 모질게 구는 사람도 아니라고 믿습니다. 제가 하러 온 이 의무를 다하는 것이 제게 기쁜 일은 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"부디 그 일을 빨리 해치우세요, 괜찮으시다면요, 도빈 소령님.\" 점점 더 심통이 난 애밀리아가 말했다. 그녀가 이토록 위압적인 투로 말할 때 도빈의 얼굴에 떠오른 표정은 보기 좋은 것이 아니었다.\n\n\"제가 하러 온 말은―크롤리 부인, 당신이 자리에 있으니 당신 앞에서 말해야겠군요―당신은, 당신은 제 친구들의 가족 일원이 되어서는 안 된다고 생각한다는 것입니다. 남편과 갈라서고, 자기 이름으로 다니지도 않고, 공공 도박장을 드나드는 여자는―\"\n\n\"무도회에 갔던 거예요.\" 베키가 외쳤다.\n\n\"―오스본 부인과 그 아들에게 어울리는 동무가 아닙니다.\" 도빈이 말을 이었다. \"그리고 한마디 덧붙이자면, 이곳에는 당신을 아는 사람들이 있고, 그들은 당신의 행실에 관해, 제가 오스본 부인 앞에서는 입에 올리고 싶지조차 않은 그 일에 관해 안다고 공언하고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"참으로 점잖고도 편리한 종류의 중상모략이군요, 도빈 소령님.\" 레베카가 말했다. \"결국 입 밖에도 내지 않은 비난의 짐을 제게 지워 놓으시다니. 그게 뭔가요? 남편에 대한 부정인가요? 저는 그런 건 경멸하며, 누구든 그걸 증명해 보라고 당당히 맞섭니다―당신에게도 맞서겠어요. 제 명예는 일찍이 저를 가장 모질게 헐뜯은 원수의 명예 못지않게 깨끗합니다. 제가 가난하고, 버림받고, 비참하다고 비난하시는 건가요? 그래요, 저는 그런 잘못이라면 지었고, 그 죄로 날마다 벌을 받고 있지요. 가게 해 주렴, 에미. 그냥 내가 너를 만나지 않았다고만 치면 돼. 그럼 나는 어제보다 오늘이 더 나쁠 것도 없어. 그저 밤이 지나가고 가엾은 떠돌이가 다시 제 길을 간다고만 여기면 돼. 옛날, 그 소중한 옛날에 우리가 함께 부르던 노래를 기억하니? 나는 그때 이후로 줄곧 떠돌아다녔어. 비참하다는 이유로 멸시받고, 혼자라는 이유로 모욕당하는 가엾은 표류자로 말이야. 가게 해 줘. 내가 여기 머무는 게 이 신사분의 계획을 방해하니까.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇습니다, 부인.\" 소령이 말했다. \"제게 이 집에서 무슨 권한이라도 있다면―\"\n\n\"권한이라니, 전혀 없어요!\" 애밀리아가 발끈했다. \"레베카, 넌 나와 함께 있어. 네가 박해받았다고 해서 널 버리지 않을 거고, 도빈 소령님이 그렇게 하기로 마음먹었다고 해서―그렇다고 해서 널 모욕하지도 않을 거야. 가자, 얘.\" 그러고는 두 여자가 문 쪽으로 향했다.\n\n윌리엄이 문을 열어 주었다. 그러나 그들이 나가려는 참에, 그는 애밀리아의 손을 잡고 말했다. \"잠깐 남아서 저와 이야기 좀 하시겠어요?\"\n\n\"나 없는 데서 너한테 할 말이 있나 봐.\" 베키가 순교자 같은 표정을 지으며 말했다. 애밀리아는 그 대답으로 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥐었다.\n\n\"맹세컨대 제가 하려는 말은 당신에 관한 게 아닙니다.\" 도빈이 말했다. \"돌아오세요, 애밀리아.\" 그러자 그녀가 돌아왔다. 도빈은 크롤리 부인을 향해 절을 하고 그녀 뒤로 문을 닫았다. 애밀리아는 거울에 기댄 채 그를 바라보았다. 그녀의 얼굴과 입술은 새하얗게 질려 있었다.\n\n\"방금은 제가 흥분해서 말이 헝클어졌습니다.\" 소령이 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 말했다. \"그리고 권한이라는 말을 잘못 썼군요.\"\n\n\"그러셨죠.\" 애밀리아가 이를 딱딱 부딪치며 말했다.\n\n\"그래도 제 말을 들어 주실 자격은 제게 있습니다.\" 도빈이 말을 이었다.\n\n\"당신께 진 신세를 제게 상기시키시다니 참 관대하시네요.\" 그 여인이 대답했다.\n\n\"제가 말하는 자격이란, 조지의 아버님께서 제게 남겨 주신 것입니다.\" 윌리엄이 말했다.\n\n\"그래요, 그런데 당신은 그분의 기억을 모욕했어요. 어제 그랬잖아요. 당신도 알잖아요. 그리고 난 절대 당신을 용서하지 않을 거예요. 절대로!\" 애밀리아가 말했다. 그녀는 분노와 격정에 떨며 짧은 문장 하나하나를 쏘아붙였다.\n\n\"진심은 아니지요, 애밀리아?\" 윌리엄이 슬프게 말했다. \"다급한 한순간에 내뱉은 그 말들이, 한평생을 바친 헌신보다 더 무겁게 저울에 달릴 거라는 게 진심은 아니겠지요? 제가 조지의 기억을 다루어 온 방식 때문에 그 기억이 손상되지는 않았다고 봅니다. 그리고 우리가 서로 책망을 주고받는 지경에 이르렀다 해도, 적어도 저는 그의 미망인이자 그의 아들의 어머니에게서 책망받을 일은 없습니다. 나중에, 당신이 한가해질 때 다시 생각해 보세요. 그러면 당신의 양심이 이 비난을 거두어들일 겁니다. 지금도 이미 거두고 있고요.\" 애밀리아는 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"당신을 움직이는 건 어제의 그 말이 아닙니다.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"그건 한낱 구실일 뿐이에요, 애밀리아. 아니라면, 제가 십오 년 동안 당신을 사랑하고 지켜본 것이 헛일이었던 거지요. 제가 그 세월 동안 당신의 온갖 감정을 읽고 당신의 생각을 들여다보는 법을 배우지 못했겠습니까? 저는 당신의 마음이 무엇을 할 수 있는지 압니다. 그 마음은 어떤 추억에 충실히 매달리고 한 환상을 소중히 품을 수는 있지만, 제 사랑이 짝이 될 만한 그런 애정은, 그리고 제가 당신보다 더 너그러운 여인에게서라면 얻어 냈을 그런 애정은 느끼지 못합니다. 아니요, 당신은 제가 바친 사랑을 받을 자격이 없습니다. 제가 평생을 걸었던 그 상이 얻을 만한 가치가 없다는 걸, 그리고 제가 어리석은 자라서, 그것도 부질없는 환상에 빠진 채, 제 진실과 열정의 전부를 당신의 그 가냘프게 남은 사랑 한 줌과 맞바꿔 왔다는 걸 저는 줄곧 알고 있었습니다. 더는 흥정하지 않겠습니다. 물러나겠어요. 당신을 탓하지는 않습니다. 당신은 무척 마음씨가 곱고, 최선을 다했지요. 하지만 당신은 끝내, 끝내 제가 당신에게 품었던 그 애정의 높이까지는, 당신보다 더 고결한 영혼이라면 함께 나눈 것을 자랑스러워했을 그 애정의 높이까지는 닿지 못했습니다. 잘 있어요, 애밀리아! 저는 당신의 갈등을 지켜봐 왔습니다. 이제 그만 끝냅시다. 우리 둘 다 그것에 지쳤으니까요.\"\n\n윌리엄이 이렇게 별안간, 그녀가 그를 붙들어 매고 있던 사슬을 끊어 버리고 자신의 독립과 우위를 선언하자, 애밀리아는 겁에 질려 말없이 서 있었다. 그가 너무도 오랫동안 그녀의 발치에 엎드려 있었던 탓에, 그 가엾은 자그마한 여인은 그를 짓밟는 데 익숙해져 있었던 것이다. 그녀는 그와 결혼하기를 바라지는 않았지만, 그를 곁에 붙들어 두기는 바랐다. 그녀는 그에게 아무것도 주지 않으면서, 그는 자기에게 모든 것을 주기를 바랐다. 사랑에서 드물지 않게 매겨지는 거래다.\n\n윌리엄의 일격은 그녀를 완전히 무너뜨리고 짓눌러 버렸다. 그녀의 공세는 진작에 끝나 버렸고 격퇴당한 터였다.\n\n\"그럼 당신이 떠난다는, 멀리 가 버린다는 말로 알아들으면 되나요, 윌리엄?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n그는 서글픈 웃음을 지었다. \"전에 한 번 떠났다가,\" 그가 말했다. \"십이 년 만에 돌아왔지요. 그때 우린 젊었어요, 애밀리아. 잘 있어요. 이 연극에 제 인생을 충분히 써 버렸으니까요.\"\n\n그들이 이야기를 나누는 동안, 오스본 부인 방으로 통하는 문이 아주 조금 열려 있었다. 사실 베키는 손잡이를 잡고 있다가 도빈이 그 방을 나서는 순간 그것을 돌렸던 것이고, 그래서 두 사람 사이에 오간 대화를 한마디도 빠짐없이 들었다. \"저 남자는 어쩌면 저토록 고결한 마음을 지녔을까.\" 그녀는 생각했다. \"그런데 저 여자는 어찌 저토록 부끄러운 줄도 모르고 그 마음을 가지고 노는 걸까!\" 그녀는 도빈에게 감탄했다. 그가 자기를 거스르는 편에 섰다고 해서 그에게 앙심을 품지는 않았다. 그것은 게임에서 드러내 놓고 둔 한 수였고, 정정당당하게 둔 것이었다. \"아!\" 그녀는 생각했다. \"내가 저런 남편을 가질 수 있었더라면―가슴도 있고 머리도 있는 남자를! 발이 좀 크다 한들 개의치 않았을 텐데.\" 그러고는 자기 방으로 달려 들어가, 정말로 무언가를 떠올리고는 그에게 쪽지를 써서, 며칠만 머물러 달라고―떠날 생각일랑 말아 달라고―그리고 자기가 A에 관해 그를 도울 수 있다고 간청했다.\n\n작별은 끝났다. 또 한 번 가엾은 윌리엄은 문 쪽으로 걸어가 사라졌다. 그리고 이 모든 일을 꾸민 장본인인 자그마한 미망인은 제 뜻을 이루었고, 승리를 거두었으며, 그것을 제멋대로 누리도록 남겨졌다. 부디 숙녀들이여, 그녀의 승리를 부러워하시라.\n\n저녁 식사라는 낭만적인 시각에 조지 도련님이 모습을 드러내고는, 또다시 \"늙은 도브\"가 보이지 않음을 알아차렸다. 식사는 일행이 말없이 했다. 조스의 식욕은 줄지 않았지만, 에미는 아무것도 입에 대지 않았다.\n\n식사가 끝난 뒤, 조지는 오래된 창가의 방석에 비스듬히 기대 있었는데―그 큰 창은 박공에서 튀어나온 세 면이 유리로 된 것으로, 한쪽으로는 엘리펀트가 있는 장터가 내려다보였다―어머니가 바로 옆에서 부산을 떨고 있을 때, 길 건너편 소령의 집에 무언가 움직이는 기미가 있음을 알아차렸다.\n\n\"어!\" 그가 말했다. \"도브의 마차네―마당에서 끌어내고 있어요.\" 문제의 그 \"마차\"는 소령이 육 파운드에 산 것으로, 다들 그것을 두고 그를 곧잘 놀려 대곤 했다.\n\n에미는 흠칫 놀랐지만 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"어!\" 조지가 말을 이었다. \"프랜시스가 여행 가방을 들고 나오고, 외눈박이 역마차 마부 쿤츠가 백마 세 마리를 끌고 장터를 내려오네. 저 장화랑 노란 윗도리 좀 봐요. 진짜 별난 사람 아니에요? 어라―도브의 마차에 말을 매고 있어요. 어디 가시려나?\"\n\n\"그래.\" 에미가 말했다. \"여행을 떠나신단다.\"\n\n\"여행을 떠나신다고요. 그럼 언제 돌아오시는데요?\"\n\n\"그분은―돌아오지 않으셔.\" 에미가 대답했다.\n\n\"돌아오지 않으신다고요!\" 조지가 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다. \"거기 가만있어.\" 조스가 버럭 소리쳤다. \"가만있어, 조지.\" 어머니가 몹시 슬픈 얼굴로 말했다. 아이는 멈춰 서서 방 안을 발로 차며 돌아다니다가, 무릎으로 창가 자리에서 폴짝폴짝 오르내렸고, 온갖 불안과 호기심의 기색을 다 드러내 보였다.\n\n말이 매어졌다. 짐도 끈으로 단단히 동여매졌다. 프랜시스가 주인의 검과 지팡이와 우산을 한데 묶어 들고 나와 마차 바닥에 놓고, 그의 책상과 낡은 양철 삼각모 상자를 좌석 밑에 넣었다. 프랜시스는 붉은 낙타 모직으로 안을 댄, 얼룩진 낡은 푸른색 외투를 들고 나왔는데, 그것은 지난 십오 년 내내 그 주인을 감싸 주었고, 그 시절 즐겨 부르던 어느 노래의 가사처럼 \"숱한 풍파를 겪어 낸\" 것이었다. 그 외투는 워털루 원정 때 새것이었고, 카트르브라의 밤이 지난 뒤 조지와 윌리엄을 덮어 주었던 것이다.\n\n숙소 주인인 늙은 부르케가 나오고, 이어서 프랜시스가 짐을 더 들고―마지막 짐이었다―나오고, 그다음에 윌리엄 소령이 나왔다. 부르케는 그에게 입을 맞추고 싶어 했다. 소령은 그와 상대한 모든 이에게 흠모를 받았다. 이런 애정의 표시에서 그가 벗어나기란 여간 어려운 일이 아니었다.\n\n\"맙소사, 나 갈래요!\" 조지가 비명을 질렀다. \"이걸 그분께 드리렴.\" 베키가 자못 관심 어린 표정으로 말하며, 아이의 손에 종이 한 장을 쥐여 주었다. 아이는 순식간에 계단을 뛰어 내려가 길을 가로질러 달려갔다. 노란 옷의 마부는 채찍을 살살 휘두르고 있었다.\n\n윌리엄은 숙소 주인의 포옹에서 풀려나 마차에 올라타 있었다. 조지는 뒤따라 풀쩍 뛰어올라 소령의 목을 두 팔로 끌어안고는(창문에서 그들이 보았듯이), 온갖 질문을 쏟아 내기 시작했다. 그러고는 조끼 주머니를 더듬어 그에게 쪽지 하나를 건넸다. 윌리엄은 다소 다급하게 그것을 낚아채 떨리는 손으로 펼쳤지만, 이내 그 얼굴빛이 변하더니, 종이를 둘로 찢어 마차 밖으로 떨어뜨렸다. 그는 조지의 머리에 입을 맞추었고, 아이는 두 주먹으로 눈을 비비며 프랜시스의 도움을 받아 마차에서 내렸다. 아이는 마차 옆판에 손을 얹은 채 머뭇거렸다. 출발이오, 형씨! 노란 옷의 마부가 채찍을 어마어마하게 휘둘렀고, 프랜시스가 마부석으로 훌쩍 뛰어올랐으며, 백마들이 내달리기 시작했고, 도빈은 고개를 가슴에 묻고 있었다. 마차가 애밀리아의 창문 아래를 지날 때 그는 끝내 고개를 들지 않았다. 그리고 거리에 홀로 남겨진 조지는 모여든 군중이 다 보는 앞에서 그만 울음을 터뜨리고 말았다.\n\n에미의 하녀는 밤중에도 그가 다시 흐느끼는 소리를 듣고는, 그를 달래 주려고 설탕에 절인 살구를 가져다주었다. 그녀는 자신의 탄식을 그의 탄식에 보탰다. 그를 아는 가난한 이들, 미천한 이들, 정직한 이들, 그리고 모든 선량한 사람들이 그 마음씨 곱고 소박한 신사를 사랑했다.\n\n에미로 말할 것 같으면, 그녀는 자기 의무를 다하지 않았던가? 그녀에게는 위안으로 삼을 조지의 초상화가 있었다.\n\n도빈의 진실한 사랑이 마침내 성취의 영광을 누리게 될, 베키 나름의 은밀한 계획이 무엇이었든 간에, 이 자그마한 여인은 그 비밀을 당분간 묻어 두어도 좋다고 여겼다. 게다가 다른 누구의 안녕에도 자기 자신의 안녕만큼 관심을 둘 리 없는 그녀였으니, 자신과 관련된 따져 볼 일이 수두룩했고, 그것들은 이승에서 도빈 소령이 누릴 행복 따위보다 그녀에게 훨씬 더 절실한 문제였던 것이다.\n\n그녀는 뜻밖에도 별안간 아늑하고 편안한 거처에 들어앉은 자신을 발견했다. 오래도록 만나 보지 못한 부류의 벗들, 다정함, 그리고 선량하고 꾸밈없는 사람들에 둘러싸인 것이다. 천성으로 보나 처지로 보나 방랑자였던 그녀였지만, 휴식이 달콤하게 느껴지는 순간들이 있었다. 사막을 가로질러 단봉낙타의 혹 위에 올라타고 내달리던 가장 모진 아랍인조차도 이따금 물가의 대추야자나무 아래서 쉬어 가기를, 혹은 도시로 들어가 시장통을 거닐고 목욕탕에서 몸을 풀며 모스크에서 기도를 올린 뒤에야 다시 약탈하러 나서기를 좋아하는 법이다. 그와 마찬가지로 조스의 천막과 필라프는 이 자그마한 이스마엘의 후예에게 더없이 흐뭇한 것이었다. 그녀는 말을 매어 두고 무기를 걸어 놓고는 그의 화롯가에서 편안히 몸을 녹였다. 그 떠돌이의, 안식 없는 삶에서 맞은 그 멈춤이 그녀에게는 말할 수 없이 위안이 되고 흐뭇한 것이었다.\n\n그리하여 스스로 흡족해진 그녀는, 온 힘을 다해 모두를 기쁘게 하려 애썼다. 그리고 우리가 알다시피, 그녀는 즐거움을 베푸는 기술에 관한 한 발군의 성공한 명인이었다. 조스로 말할 것 같으면, 코끼리 여관 다락방에서의 그 짧은 만남에서조차 그녀는 이미 그의 호의를 상당 부분 되찾을 방도를 찾아낸 터였다. 일주일이 지나자 이 문관 나리는 그녀의 충실한 노예이자 열렬한 숭배자가 되었다. 그는 애밀리아와 함께하던 훨씬 덜 활기찬 자리에서 늘 그러했듯 식후에 잠들어 버리는 법이 없었다. 그는 베키를 태우고 자신의 무개 마차를 몰고 나갔다. 그녀를 기리는 자그마한 모임을 청하고 온갖 여흥을 궁리해 냈다.\n\n그토록 모질게 그녀를 헐뜯었던 대리공사 테이프웜은 조스와 식사를 함께하러 왔고, 그 뒤로는 날마다 베키에게 경의를 표하러 찾아왔다. 본디 말수가 적었던 데다 도빈이 떠난 뒤로 더더욱 시무룩하고 말이 없어진 가엾은 에미는, 이 빼어난 재주꾼이 등장하자 까맣게 잊힌 신세가 되었다. 프랑스 공사도 영국인 적수 못지않게 그녀에게 매료되었다. 풍속에 관한 한, 특히 영국인의 풍속에 관한 한 결코 까다롭지 않았던 독일 부인들은 오스본 부인의 이 매력적인 벗이 보여 주는 영민함과 재치에 흠뻑 빠졌다. 그리고 그녀가 궁정 출입을 청한 것도 아니었건만, 그곳의 더없이 존엄하고 고귀하신 분들까지도 그녀의 매혹에 관한 이야기를 전해 듣고는 그녀를 알고 싶어 자못 궁금해했다. 그녀가 명문 출신의 귀족이며, 그 남편이 근위대 대령이요 각하이자 어느 섬의 총독이며, 다만 베르테르가 여전히 읽히고 괴테의 친화력이 교훈적인 도덕서로 여겨지는 나라에서는 대수롭지 않게 여겨지는 그런 사소한 불화 하나로 부인과 떨어져 지낼 뿐이라는 사실이 알려지자, 이 자그마한 공국의 더없이 상류한 사교계에서 그녀를 맞아들이기를 마다할 이는 아무도 없었다. 부인들은 애밀리아에게 그 더없이 값진 은혜를 베풀던 때보다도 한층 더 기꺼이 그녀를 '너'라고 부르고 영원한 우정을 맹세하려 들었다. 사랑과 자유라는 것을, 저 꾸밈없는 독일인들은 요크셔나 서머싯셔의 정직한 사람들로서는 도무지 이해할 수 없는 방식으로 해석했으니, 어떤 철학적이고 개화된 도시에서는 부인이 제 남편들과 몇 번이고 이혼을 거듭하고도 사교계에서의 평판을 고스란히 지킬 수 있는 것이었다. 조스의 집은 그가 제 집을 갖게 된 이래로, 레베카가 만들어 낸 만큼 즐거웠던 적이 없었다. 그녀는 노래하고, 연주하고, 웃고, 두세 개 나라말로 이야기하고, 온갖 사람을 그 집으로 불러들였으며, 그러면서도 그 고장의 사교계를 그의 주위로 끌어모은 것이 다름 아닌 조스 자신의 빼어난 사교적 재능과 재치 덕분이라고 그가 믿게끔 만들었다.\n\n청구서를 치러야 할 때를 빼고는 제 집의 안주인 노릇이라곤 조금도 하지 못하던 에미로 말하면, 베키는 곧 그녀를 달래고 기쁘게 할 방도를 찾아냈다. 그녀는 쫓겨나다시피 떠나간 도빈 소령 이야기를 줄곧 에미에게 들려주었고, 그 훌륭하고 고결한 신사에 대한 자신의 흠모를 거리낌 없이 드러내며, 에미가 그를 대한 처사가 더없이 모질었다고 말하기를 서슴지 않았다. 에미는 자신의 행동을 변호하며, 그것은 오직 가장 순수한 종교적 원칙에서 비롯된 것임을, 여자란 한번 어쩌고저쩌고하면, 더구나 자신이 운 좋게 결혼할 수 있었던 그이 같은 천사에게는, 영원히 결혼한 몸이라는 것을 역설했다. 그러나 베키가 아무리 그 소령을 칭송한다 해도 그것을 듣는 데에는 조금도 거리낌이 없었고, 오히려 하루에도 스무 번씩 화제를 도빈 쪽으로 끌고 오는 것이었다.\n\n조지와 하인들의 호감을 사는 방도는 손쉽게 찾아냈다. 애밀리아의 하녀가 그 너그러운 소령의 더없이 열렬한 편이었다는 것은 앞서 말한 바 있다. 처음에는 소령을 안주인 곁에서 내쫓는 빌미가 되었다 하여 베키를 못마땅해했으나, 나중에는 크롤리 부인과 화해했으니, 베키가 윌리엄의 더없이 열렬한 숭배자이자 옹호자가 되었기 때문이다. 그리하여 모임을 마친 뒤 두 부인이 즐기던 밤마다의 밀담에서, 페인 양이 한 사람의 노란 머리채와 또 한 사람의 보드라운 갈색 머리채를 두고 자기 말로 \"머리 빗질\"을 해 주는 동안, 이 하녀는 늘 그 다정하고 선량한 신사 도빈 소령을 위해 한마디 거들었다. 그녀의 두둔이 애밀리아를 화나게 하지 않은 것은, 레베카가 그를 흠모하는 것이 화나게 하지 않은 것과 마찬가지였다. 그녀는 조지에게 끊임없이 소령에게 편지를 쓰게 했고, 추신으로 \"엄마의 다정한 사랑\"을 전하는 것을 잊지 않았다. 그리고 밤마다 남편의 초상을 바라볼 때면, 그것은 더 이상 그녀를 책망하지 않았다. 어쩌면 이제 윌리엄이 떠나 버린 지금, 그녀가 그것을 책망하고 있었는지도 모른다.\n\n에미는 그 영웅적인 희생을 치르고도 그다지 행복하지 않았다. 그녀는 몹시 넋이 나가 있었고, 신경이 곤두서고, 말이 없고, 비위 맞추기 어려운 사람이 되었다. 가족들도 그녀가 그토록 까탈스러운 것은 일찍이 본 적이 없었다. 그녀는 창백해지고 병들어 갔다. 그녀는 어떤 노래들을 (\"Einsam bin ich nicht alleine\"도 그중 하나였는데, 이는 베버의 그 애틋한 사랑 노래로, 옛 시절에—젊은 숙녀 여러분, 여러분이 채 태어나기도 전에—여러분보다 먼저 살았던 이들 역시 사랑할 줄도 노래할 줄도 알았음을 보여 주는 곡이었다), 그러니까 소령이 즐겨 듣던 어떤 노래들을 부르려 애쓰곤 했다. 그리고 응접실에서 땅거미 지는 가운데 그 노래들을 흥얼거리다가는, 노래 한가운데서 뚝 그치고 옆방으로 걸어 들어가서는, 거기서 의심할 바 없이 남편의 작은 초상화 속으로 피신하는 것이었다.\n\n도빈이 떠난 뒤에도, 그의 이름이 적힌 책 몇 권이 아직 남아 있었다. 이를테면 면지에 \"윌리엄 도빈, 제××연대\"라고 적힌 독일어 사전 한 권, 그의 머리글자가 새겨진 안내서 한 권, 그리고 소령의 것이었던 한두 권의 책이 더 있었다. 에미는 이것들을 치워 서랍장 위에 얹어 두었는데, 거기에는 두 조지의 초상화 아래로 자신의 반짇고리, 문갑, 성경, 기도서를 놓아둔 터였다. 그리고 소령이 떠나면서 장갑을 두고 갔는데, 한참 뒤에 조지가 어머니의 문갑을 뒤지다가 그 장갑이 가지런히 접힌 채 이른바 문갑의 비밀 서랍에 넣어져 있는 것을 발견했으니, 이것은 엄연한 사실이다.\n\n사교 모임에는 관심이 없어 그곳에서 무던히도 시름에 잠겨 지내던 에미에게, 여름 저녁의 가장 큰 낙은 조지와 함께 긴 산책을 나가는 것이었다(그동안 레베카는 조지프 씨의 말동무로 남겨졌다). 그러고는 모자와 아들이 소령 이야기를 나누었는데, 그 방식이 어찌나 극진했던지 소년조차 미소를 지을 정도였다. 그녀는 아들에게 윌리엄 소령이야말로 온 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 사람이라고, 가장 온화하고 가장 다정하며, 가장 용감하고 가장 겸손한 사람이라고 말했다. 그녀는 자기네가 이 세상에서 가진 모든 것이 그 다정한 벗의 자애로운 보살핌 덕분임을, 그가 가난과 불운의 세월 내내 자기네를 도와주었음을, 아무도 자기네를 거들떠보지 않을 때 자기네를 지켜 주었음을, 그가 결코 제 용맹한 행적을 입에 올리지 않았는데도 동료들이 모두 그를 우러러보았음을, 조지의 아버지가 다른 어느 누구보다도 그를 신뢰했고 늘 그 선량한 윌리엄의 도움을 받았음을, 거듭하고 또 거듭하여 들려주었다. \"그래, 네 아빠가 어린 소년이었을 때,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"아빠는 둘이 함께 다니던 학교에서 어떤 폭군 같은 아이에게서 자기를 지켜 준 게 윌리엄이었다고 종종 내게 이야기했단다. 그리고 두 사람의 우정은 그날부터 네 사랑하는 아버지가 전사한 마지막 그날까지 결코 끊이지 않았지.\"\n\n\"도빈 아저씨가 아빠를 죽인 사람을 죽였나요?\" 조지가 말했다. \"틀림없이 죽였을 거예요, 아니면 잡을 수만 있었다면 죽였을 거예요, 그렇죠, 엄마? 제가 군대에 가면 프랑스 놈들을 미워하지 않고 배기겠어요? 그뿐이에요.\"\n\n이런 대화로 모자는 함께하는 많은 시간을 보냈다. 그 천진한 여인은 소년을 마음을 터놓는 벗으로 삼은 것이다. 그를 잘 아는 다른 모든 이들과 마찬가지로, 소년 또한 윌리엄의 벗이었다.\n\n그런데 말이 나온 김에 덧붙이자면, 감상에서 뒤지지 않으려는 베키 부인 또한 자기 방에 작은 초상화 하나를 걸어 두었으니, 대부분의 사람들은 놀라워하며 재미있어했고, 그 주인공은 더없이 기뻐했다. 그 주인공이란 다름 아닌 우리의 벗 조스였다. 처음 세들리 일가를 찾아와 신세를 지게 되었을 때, 유난히 자그맣고 초라한 짐을 들고 온 이 자그마한 여인은 어쩌면 자기 트렁크며 모자 상자가 보잘것없는 것이 부끄러웠던지, 라이프치히에 두고 와 그 도시에서 가져와야 한다는 짐 이야기를 자못 거창하게 늘어놓곤 했다. 어느 여행자가 정작 지금 몸에 지니고 있지도 않은 제 짐의 휘황함을 그대에게 줄곧 떠벌리거든, 내 아들아, 그 여행자를 경계하라! 십중팔구 그는 사기꾼이다.\n\n조스도 에미도 이 중요한 격언을 알지 못했다. 두 사람에게는 베키가 보이지 않는 트렁크 속에 아주 고운 옷을 잔뜩 지녔든 말든 아무 상관이 없는 듯했다. 다만 그녀가 지금 갖춘 것이 너무도 초라했으므로, 에미는 자기 옷가지를 내어 그녀를 입혔고, 혹은 그녀를 데리고 그 고장에서 가장 좋은 모자 가게로 가서 거기서 그녀를 차려입혔다. 이제는, 내 장담하건대, 찢어진 깃이며 어깨에서 빛바랜 채 늘어진 비단 따위는 더 이상 없었다. 베키는 처지가 바뀜에 따라 습관도 바꾸었으니, 연지 단지는 손을 떼게 되었고, 또 하나 그녀가 길들여 온 자극거리 역시 제쳐 두었다. 적어도 남몰래 즐길 때 말고는 그러했으니, 이를테면 에미와 소년이 산책 나가 자리를 비운 여름 저녁, 조스의 권유에 못 이겨 독한 술에 물을 탄 것을 조금 마실 때 정도였다. 그러나 그녀는 절제했을지언정, 안내인은 그러지 못했다. 저 망나니 키르슈는 도무지 술병에서 떼어 놓을 수가 없었고, 술병에 손을 댈 때면 제가 얼마나 마셨는지도 가늠하지 못했다. 그는 세들리 씨의 코냑이 줄어드는 모양새에 이따금 제 스스로도 놀라곤 했다. 자, 자, 이건 괴로운 화제다. 베키는 아마도 이 단정한 가정에 들어오기 전만큼 그리 흥청대지는 않았을 것이다.\n\n마침내 그토록 떠벌려 온 상자들이 라이프치히에서 도착했다. 세 개였는데, 결코 크지도 휘황하지도 않았다. 게다가 막상 도착한 그 상자들에서 베키는 옷가지든 장신구든 무엇 하나 꺼내는 기색이 없었다. 다만 그중 하나, 자기 서류 뭉치가 가득 담긴 상자에서—그것은 바로 로던 크롤리가 베키가 숨겨 둔 돈을 미친 듯이 뒤지며 샅샅이 헤집었던 바로 그 상자였다—그녀는 더없이 신이 나서 그림 한 점을 꺼냈고, 그것을 자기 방에 핀으로 꽂아 두고는 조스에게 보여 주었다. 그것은 어느 신사를 연필로 그린 초상이었는데, 그 얼굴은 분홍빛으로 덧칠해진 덕을 보고 있었다. 그 신사는 코끼리에 올라타 야자나무 몇 그루와 탑 하나를 등지고 멀어져 가는 모습이었으니, 동방의 풍경이었다.\n\n\"이런 맙소사, 내 초상이잖소!\" 조스가 외쳤다. 과연 그것은 1804년식 재단의 능직 무명 윗옷을 걸치고 젊음과 아름다움이 만개한 그 자신이었다. 러셀 광장에 걸려 있던 바로 그 오래된 그림이었다.\n\n\"제가 샀어요.\" 베키가 감정에 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"혹시라도 제 다정한 벗들께 무슨 도움이 될까 싶어 가 보았던 거예요. 저는 그 그림을 한 번도 떼어 놓지 않았어요. 앞으로도 결코 떼어 놓지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"정말이오?\" 조스가 이루 말할 수 없는 환희와 만족이 어린 표정으로 외쳤다. \"정말로 나를 생각해서 그걸 그리 소중히 여겼단 말이오?\"\n\n\"제가 그랬다는 걸 잘 아시잖아요.\" 베키가 말했다. \"하지만 무엇하러 말하고, 무엇하러 생각하고, 무엇하러 뒤를 돌아보겠어요! 이젠 너무 늦었는걸요!\"\n\n그날 저녁의 대화는 조스에게 더없이 감미로운 것이었다. 에미는 그저 몹시 지치고 몸이 편치 않아 잠자리에 들려고 잠깐 들어왔을 뿐이었다. 조스와 그의 어여쁜 손님은 매혹적인 단둘만의 자리를 가졌고, 옆방에 누워 잠 못 이루던 누이는 레베카가 조스에게 1815년의 옛 노래들을 거듭 불러 주는 것을 들을 수 있었다. 신기하게도 그는 그날 밤 애밀리아 못지않게 도무지 잠들지 못했다.\n\n때는 유월이었고, 따라서 런던은 한창 사교철이었다. 매일같이 비할 데 없는 갈리냐니지(망명객의 가장 좋은 벗이다)를 통독하던 조스는, 아침 식사 자리에서 부인들에게 그 신문에서 추린 기사들을 들려주곤 했다. 이 신문에는 매주 군대의 이동에 관한 상세한 기사가 실렸는데, 한때 군에 몸담았던 사람으로서 조스는 거기에 각별한 관심을 두었다. 한번은 그가 이런 기사를 읽어 주었다. \"제××연대의 귀환. 그레이브젠드, 유월 이십일. 동인도 회사 소속 선박 람천더호가 오늘 아침 강으로 들어왔다. 이 용맹한 부대의 장교 십사 명과 병졸 백삼십이 명을 태우고 온 것이다. 이들은 워털루 전투 이듬해에 출항한 이래 십사 년 동안 영국을 떠나 있었으니, 그 영광스러운 전투에서 적극적으로 활약했으며, 이후 버마 전쟁에서도 빼어난 공을 세웠다. 노련한 연대장 마이클 오다우드 경(바스 훈작사)이 부인 및 누이와 함께 어제 이곳에 상륙했고, 포스키·스터블·매크로·맬로니 대위, 스미스·존스·톰슨·F. 톰슨 중위, 힉스·그레이디 소위가 함께였다. 부두에서는 악대가 국가를 연주했고, 군중은 이 용맹한 노병들이 웨이트 호텔로 들어설 때 우렁차게 환호했으니, 그곳에는 노쇠한 영국을 지킨 이들을 위한 성대한 연회가 마련되어 있었다. 새삼 말할 것도 없이 웨이트 호텔 특유의 솜씨로 차려진 그 향연이 진행되는 동안 환호성이 어찌나 열렬히 이어졌던지, 오다우드 부인과 연대장이 발코니로 나와 웨이트 호텔 최상품 클라레 한 잔을 가득 채워 동포들의 건강을 위해 들이켰다.\"\n\n또 한번은 조스가 짤막한 소식을 읽어 주었다. 도빈 소령이 채텀에서 제××연대에 합류했다는 것이었다. 그 뒤로 그는 마이클 오다우드 경(바스 훈작사) 연대장과, 오다우드 부인(밸리맬로니의 맬로이 맬로니 부인이 소개), 그리고 글로비나 오다우드 양(오다우드 부인이 소개)이 궁정 알현실에 소개되었다는 소식을 전했다. 그 직후라 해도 좋을 만큼 곧이어, 도빈의 이름이 중령들의 명단에 올랐다. 늙은 팁토프 원수가 제××연대의 마드라스 항해 도중에 세상을 떠났던 까닭에, 군주께서는 마이클 오다우드 경 연대장이 영국으로 돌아오자 그를 소장으로 진급시키시는 한편, 그가 그토록 오래 지휘해 온 그 빼어난 연대의 연대장 직을 맡으라는 뜻을 내비치셨던 것이다.\n\n애밀리아는 이러한 이동의 일부를 전해 듣고 있었다. 조지와 그 후견인 사이의 서신 왕래는 결코 끊긴 적이 없었다. 윌리엄은 떠난 뒤로 그녀에게도 한두 차례 편지를 쓰기까지 했으나, 그 어조가 어찌나 거리낌 없이 싸늘했던지, 가엾은 여인은 이번에는 자기 차례로 그에 대한 자신의 힘을 잃었음을, 그리고 그가 말했던 대로 그가 자유로운 몸이 되었음을 느꼈다. 그가 그녀를 떠났고, 그녀는 비참했다. 그가 베푼 헤아릴 수 없는 봉사들, 그 고결하고도 애정 어린 마음이 이제 그녀의 뇌리에 떠올라 밤낮없이 그녀를 책망했다. 그녀는 늘 그러했듯 그 추억들을 곱씹으며, 자신이 하찮게 여겨 가지고 놀았던 그 애정의 순수함과 아름다움을 깨닫고, 그런 보물을 내던져 버린 자신을 책망했다.\n\n그것은 정녕 가 버린 것이었다. 윌리엄은 그것을 모조리 다 써 버린 것이다. 그는 자신이 더는 예전처럼 그녀를 사랑하지 않는다고 생각했다. 다시는 그럴 수 없었다. 그가 그토록 오랜 세월 변함없이 그녀에게 바쳐 온 그런 마음이란, 한번 내던져져 산산조각 난 뒤에 다시 이어 붙인다고 해서 흉터 하나 없이 멀쩡할 수는 없는 법이다. 그 분별없는 자그마한 폭군이 그것을 그렇게 부수어 놓은 것이다. 아니, 윌리엄은 거듭하고 또 거듭하여 생각했다. \"내가 속인 것은 나 자신이었고, 내가 끈질기게 어르고 달랜 것도 나 자신이었어. 내가 바친 사랑을 받을 만한 여자였다면 진작에 그 사랑에 응답했겠지. 그건 어리석은 착각이었어. 인생의 모든 행로가 이런 착각들로 이루어진 것 아니던가? 그리고 설령 내가 그녀를 얻었다 한들, 승리한 이튿날이면 환상이 깨지지 않았겠는가? 무엇하러 애태우고, 무엇하러 내 패배를 부끄러워하겠는가?\" 그는 자기 인생의 이 기나긴 한 토막을 생각하면 할수록, 자신의 착각이 더욱 또렷이 보였다. \"다시 멍에를 메야지.\" 그가 말했다. \"그리고 하늘이 나를 두기로 정하신 그 처지에서 내 본분을 다해야지. 신병들의 단추가 제대로 반들거리는지, 하사관들이 회계에서 실수하지 않는지 살피겠어. 장교 식당에서 식사를 하고, 스코틀랜드 군의관이 늘어놓는 이야기에 귀를 기울이겠어. 늙고 쇠약해지면 반액 봉급을 받고 물러나, 늙은 누이들의 잔소리나 들으며 지내야지. 나는 '발렌슈타인'의 처녀가 말하듯 사랑했고 또 살았노라(geliebt und gelebet). 나는 끝났어. 청구서들을 치르고 시가나 한 대 가져오게. 오늘 밤 극장에서 무얼 하는지 알아봐 주게, 프랜시스. 내일 우리는 바타비르호로 건너가네.\" 그는 로테르담의 봄피스 강변을 거닐며 위와 같은 말을 했는데, 프랜시스가 알아들은 것은 마지막 두 마디뿐이었다. 바타비르호는 선착장에 정박해 있었다. 그는 갑판 후미, 그 행복했던 출항길에 그와 에미가 함께 앉아 있던 자리를 바라볼 수 있었다. 그 자그마한 크롤리 부인이 그에게 무슨 할 말이 있단 말인가? 흥, 내일이면 우리는 바다로 나아가 영국으로, 집으로, 그리고 본분으로 돌아가리라!\n\n유월이 지나면 펌퍼니클의 자그마한 궁정 사교계는 으레 독일식으로 뿔뿔이 흩어져, 수백 곳의 온천지로 향하곤 했다. 거기서 그들은 샘물을 마시고, 당나귀를 타고, 돈과 마음이 동하면 도박장에서 노름을 하고, 같은 부류의 수백 명과 더불어 정식 식탁으로 우르르 몰려가 먹어 대고, 여름을 빈둥거리며 흘려보냈다. 영국 외교관들은 테플리츠와 키싱겐으로 떠났고, 그들의 프랑스 적수들은 공관 문을 닫아걸고 그들이 애지중지하는 강 대로로 휙 사라졌다. 고귀하신 통치 가문도 온천으로 가거나 사냥용 별장으로 물러갔다. 점잔깨나 빼는 이라면 누구나 떠나갔으니, 물론 그 무리에는 궁정 시의 폰 글라우버 박사와 그 남작 부인도 끼어 있었다. 온천철은 그 박사에게 가장 벌이가 짭짤한 시기였으니, 그는 일과 즐거움을 한데 겸했고, 그가 즐겨 찾는 곳은 독일인이 즐겨 드나드는 오스텐더였다. 그곳에서 박사는 자신과 그 아내에게 제 말로 바닷물에 \"몸 한번 담그기\"라는 호사를 베풀었다.\n\n그의 흥미로운 환자 조스는 박사에게 그야말로 젖소나 다름없었으니, 박사는 조스 본인의 건강을 위해서뿐 아니라 실로 몹시 쇠약해진 그 매력적인 누이의 건강을 위해서라며, 그 흉측한 바닷가 도시에서 여름을 나도록 이 문관을 어렵잖게 구슬렸다. 에미는 어디로 가든 그다지 개의치 않았다. 조지는 거처를 옮긴다는 생각에 펄쩍 뛰며 좋아했다. 베키로 말하면, 조스 씨가 사들인 그 멋진 사륜마차 안 네 번째 자리에 당연하다는 듯 따라 탔으니, 하인 둘은 앞쪽 마부석에 앉았다. 오스텐더에서 마주칠 법한 지인들, 흉한 소문을 퍼뜨릴지도 모를 그런 이들이 마음에 걸리기야 했겠지만, 흥! 그녀는 제 앞가림쯤은 너끈히 할 만큼 단단한 여자였다. 이제 그녀는 조스라는 닻을 단단히 내려 두었으니, 그것을 흔들어 놓으려면 어지간한 폭풍으로는 어림없을 터였다. 그 초상화 사건이 그를 완전히 끝장내 버린 것이다. 베키는 코끼리 그림을 떼어 내, 아주 오래전 애밀리아에게서 받은 그 작은 상자에 넣었다. 에미 역시 자신의 수호신들을—두 점의 초상화를—챙겨 들고 떠났고, 마침내 일행은 오스텐더의 더없이 비싸고 불편한 집에 거처를 잡았다.\n\n그곳에서 애밀리아는 해수욕을 시작하여 거기서 얻을 수 있는 효험을 보려 했다. 그리고 베키와 안면이 있는 숱한 사람들이 그녀 곁을 스쳐 가며 그녀를 못 본 체했건만, 그녀와 함께 거닐면서도 아는 이 하나 없던 오스본 부인은, 자신이 그토록 분별 있게 골랐다는 그 길동무가 어떤 대접을 받고 있는지 알지 못했다. 실로 베키는 그 천진한 눈앞에서 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 그녀에게 털어놓는 것이 마땅하다고 여기지 않았던 것이다.\n\n그러나 로던 크롤리 부인과 안면이 있는 이들 가운데 몇몇은 선뜻 그녀를 아는 체했으니, 어쩌면 그녀가 바랐을 법한 것보다 더 선뜻 그러했다. 그 가운데에는 (무소속의) 로더 소령과 (전직 소총부대) 룩 대위가 있었으니, 이들은 둑 위에서 담배를 피우며 여자들을 빤히 쳐다보는 모습을 날마다 볼 수 있었고, 이내 조지프 세들리 씨의 후한 식탁과 까다로운 모임에 끼어들 소개를 얻어 냈다. 사실 이들은 거절이라는 것을 받아들이지 않았다. 베키가 집에 있든 없든 막무가내로 집 안으로 쳐들어와, 오스본 부인의 응접실로 걸어 들어가 그들의 외투와 콧수염 냄새로 방을 진동시켰고, 조스를 \"이 양반\"이라 부르며 그의 식탁을 점령하고는, 그곳에서 오래도록 웃고 마셔 댔다.\n\n\"저 사람들 도대체 무슨 속셈일까요?\" 이 신사들을 좋아하지 않던 조지가 물었다. \"어제 소령이 크롤리 부인한테 이렇게 말하는 걸 들었어요. '안 되지, 안 돼, 베키, 저 양반을 혼자 독차지하려 들면 못쓰지. 우리도 한몫 끼워 줘야지, 안 그러면 젠장, 내가 다 까발릴 테니.' 소령이 무슨 뜻으로 그런 말을 한 걸까요, 엄마?\"\n\n\"소령이라니! 그 사람을 소령이라 부르지 마라!\" 에미가 말했다. \"정말이지 그 사람이 무슨 뜻으로 그랬는지는 나도 모르겠구나.\" 그와 그의 벗이 곁에 있다는 것만으로도 이 자그마한 여인은 견딜 수 없는 공포와 혐오에 사로잡혔다. 그들은 술 취한 입으로 그녀에게 찬사를 늘어놓았고, 식탁 너머로 그녀를 곁눈질했다. 그리고 대위가 그녀에게 수작을 걸어 와 그녀를 메스껍도록 질겁하게 했으니, 그녀는 조지가 곁에 있지 않으면 결코 그를 만나려 하지 않았다.\n\n레베카는, 그녀의 공정을 기해 말하건대, 이 두 사내 가운데 누구도 애밀리아와 단둘이 있도록 결코 내버려 두지 않았다. 소령 역시 임자 없는 몸이라, 자신이 그녀를 차지할 거라며 큰소리를 쳤다. 두 명의 망나니가 이 순진한 여인을 두고 다투며, 그녀의 식탁에서 그녀를 걸고 노름을 벌인 것이다. 그리고 그녀는 비록 그 악당들이 자신을 노린 속셈을 알지는 못했어도, 그들이 곁에 있으면 공포와 불안을 느꼈고 어디로든 달아나기를 갈망했다.\n\n그녀는 조스에게 떠나자고 애원하고 또 간청했다. 그는 들으려 하지 않았다. 그는 거동이 굼떴고, 그 박사에게, 또 어쩌면 다른 어떤 끈에도 매여 있었던 것이다. 적어도 베키만큼은 영국으로 갈 마음이 없었다.\n\n마침내 그녀는 큰 결심을 했다. 큰 모험에 뛰어든 것이다. 그녀는 바다 건너편에 있는 어느 벗에게 편지 한 통을 써 보냈는데, 그 편지에 관해서는 아무에게도 한마디 비치지 않았고, 숄 아래 감춘 채 손수 우체국으로 가져갔다. 그것을 두고 누가 무어라 말한 바도 없었으니, 다만 조지와 마주쳤을 때 그녀가 몹시 상기되고 동요한 듯 보였고, 그날 밤 소년에게 입 맞추며 한참을 그러안고 있었을 뿐이다. 산책에서 돌아온 뒤로 그녀는 제 방에서 나오지 않았다. 베키는 그녀를 겁먹게 한 것이 로더 소령과 대위라고 여겼다.\n\n\"여기 머물러선 안 돼.\" 베키는 혼자 따져 보았다. \"저 어리석고 자그마한 바보는 떠나야 해. 아직도 그 얼간이 같은 남편을 못 잊어 훌쩍거리고 있으니—죽은 지(그것도 당연한 죗값이었지!) 벌써 십오 년이나 된 사람을 말이야. 저 여자가 저 두 사내 중 누구와도 결혼하게 둬선 안 돼. 로더라니 너무 못됐어. 안 되지, 저 여잔 그 대나무 막대기 같은 사내와 결혼시켜야지, 바로 오늘 밤 매듭을 지어야겠어.\"\n\n그리하여 베키는 차 한 잔을 들고 애밀리아의 사실(私室)로 들어갔다가, 이 부인이 자기 초상화들을 벗 삼아 더없이 우울하고 신경이 곤두선 채 있는 것을 보았다. 베키는 찻잔을 내려놓았다.\n\n\"고마워.\" 애밀리아가 말했다.\n\n\"내 말 들어, 애밀리아.\" 베키가 그녀 앞에서 방 안을 왔다 갔다 거닐며, 일종의 경멸 어린 다정함으로 그녀를 훑어보면서 말했다. \"너한테 할 말이 있어. 너는 여기를, 그리고 저 사내들의 무례한 짓거리를 떠나야 해. 저들이 너를 들볶도록 두고 보지 않겠어. 네가 머무르면 저들은 너를 모욕할 거야. 내 말하건대 저들은 망나니야. 감옥선에나 처넣어 마땅한 작자들이지. 내가 저들을 어떻게 아는지는 묻지 마. 나는 누구든 다 알아. 조스는 너를 지켜 줄 수 없어. 그 사람은 너무 약해 빠져서 제 몸을 지켜 줄 보호자가 필요한 처지야. 너는 갓난아기나 다름없이 세상을 살아갈 깜냥이 못 돼. 너는 결혼을 해야 해, 안 그러면 너도 네 그 귀한 아들도 다 파멸하고 말 거야. 너는 남편이 있어야 한다고, 이 바보야. 그런데 내 평생 본 가장 훌륭한 신사 가운데 한 사람이 네게 수백 번이나 청혼했는데, 너는 그를 거절했어, 이 어리석고 매정하고 은혜도 모르는 자그마한 것아!\"\n\n\"애써 봤어, 정말 최선을 다해 애써 봤다고, 레베카.\" 애밀리아가 자기를 변명하듯 말했다. \"하지만 잊을 수가 없었어.\" 그러고는 초상화를 올려다보는 것으로 말끝을 맺었다.\n\n\"그 사람을 못 잊겠다고!\" 베키가 외쳤다. \"그 이기적인 사기꾼, 그 천박한 런던내기 멋쟁이, 속에 솜이나 채운 그 멍청이를! 재치도, 예의도, 마음씨도 없던 작자, 네가 엘리자베스 여왕에 견줄 수 없는 만큼이나 그 대나무 막대기 같은 네 벗에게는 견줄 수도 없던 작자를! 아니, 그 사내는 너한테 진력이 나서 너를 차 버리려 했는데, 도빈이 약속을 지키라고 다그치는 바람에 못 그런 거야. 그 사람이 내게 직접 털어놓았어. 그는 너를 조금도 사랑하지 않았어. 나한테 너를 두고 비웃기를 몇 번이고 거듭했고, 너와 결혼한 그 이듬 주에 나한테 수작을 걸었다고.\"\n\n\"거짓말이야! 거짓말이야, 레베카!\" 애밀리아가 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다.\n\n\"이걸 보라고, 이 바보야.\" 베키가 여전히 약 올리는 듯한 명랑함으로, 허리띠에서 작은 종이쪽지 하나를 꺼내더니, 그것을 펼쳐 에미의 무릎 위로 휙 던지며 말했다. \"너도 그 사람 필체는 알아보겠지. 그 사람이 이걸 내게 썼어. 나더러 자기랑 달아나자고 했지. 네 코앞에서 내게 건넨 거야, 총에 맞기 바로 전날 말이야. 그것도 다 죗값이었지!\" 베키가 거듭 말했다.\n\n에미는 그녀의 말이 들리지 않았다. 그녀는 그 편지를 들여다보고 있었다. 그것은 리치먼드 공작 부인의 무도회가 있던 밤, 조지가 부케 속에 끼워 베키에게 건넸던 바로 그 쪽지였다. 그녀의 말 그대로였다. 그 어리석은 젊은이가 그녀에게 함께 달아나자고 청했던 것이다.\n\n에미의 고개가 떨궈졌고, 이 이야기에서 그녀가 눈물 흘리도록 청해질 거의 마지막 순간에 그녀는 그 일에 착수했다. 고개가 가슴께로 떨어지고 두 손이 눈으로 올라갔다. 그렇게 그녀는 한동안 자기 감정에 몸을 내맡겼고, 베키는 가만히 서서 그 모습을 지켜보았다. 누가 그 눈물을 헤아려, 그것이 달콤한 것인지 쓰디쓴 것인지 말할 수 있으랴? 그녀가 그토록 슬퍼한 것은 평생의 우상이 무너져 제 발치에서 산산이 부서진 까닭이었을까, 아니면 자신의 사랑이 그토록 멸시당했음에 분개한 까닭이었을까, 아니면 정숙함이 그녀와 새롭고도 참된 애정 사이에 세워 두었던 장벽이 걷힌 것이 기뻤던 까닭이었을까? \"이제 나를 가로막을 것은 아무것도 없어.\" 그녀는 생각했다. \"이제 나는 그이를 온 마음 다해 사랑해도 돼. 오, 그럴 거야, 그럴 거야, 그이가 허락만 해 준다면, 나를 용서만 해 준다면.\" 그 자그맣고 부드러운 가슴을 뒤흔든 다른 모든 감정을 압도하고 솟구쳐 오른 것은 바로 이 마음이었으리라.\n\n실로 그녀는 베키가 예상한 만큼 그리 많이 울지는 않았다. 베키가 그녀를 달래고 입 맞추어 주었으니, 베키 부인에게서는 보기 드문 공감의 표시였다. 그녀는 에미를 어린아이처럼 대하며 그 머리를 토닥였다. \"자, 이제 펜과 잉크를 가져와서 당장 이리로 와 달라고 그이에게 편지를 쓰자.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"나, 나는 오늘 아침에 이미 편지를 썼어.\" 에미가 얼굴을 새빨갛게 붉히며 말했다. 베키는 깔깔거리며 폭소를 터뜨렸다. \"쪽지 한 장(Un biglietto),\" 그녀가 로지나처럼 노래하듯 외쳤다. \"여기 있다네(eccolo qua)!\" 온 집 안이 그 날카로운 노랫소리로 울렸다.\n\n이 자그마한 장면이 있은 지 이틀째 아침, 비바람이 몰아치는 날씨였고, 애밀리아는 밤새 포효하는 바람 소리에 귀를 기울이며 뭍으로든 물로든 길 떠난 모든 이를 가엾이 여기느라 도무지 잠을 이루지 못한 터였지만, 그래도 그녀는 일찍 일어나 조지를 데리고 둑으로 산책을 나가겠다고 고집했다. 그리하여 그녀는 비가 얼굴에 들이치는 가운데 거기를 거닐며, 어둑한 수평선 너머 서쪽을 바라보았고, 부풀어 올라 굽이치며 거품을 일으키며 기슭으로 밀려드는 물결 너머를 내다보았다. 두 사람은 별말이 없었으니, 다만 이따금 소년이 겁먹은 동행에게 공감과 보호의 뜻을 담은 몇 마디를 건넬 뿐이었다.\n\n\"이런 날씨에는 그분이 바다를 건너지 않으셨으면 좋겠구나.\" 에미가 말했다.\n\n\"십중팔구 건너실걸요.\" 소년이 대답했다. \"보세요, 엄마, 저기 증기선 연기가 있어요.\" 과연 그것은 바로 그 신호였다.\n\n그러나 증기선이 출항했다 한들, 그가 배에 타고 있지 않을 수도 있었다. 그가 편지를 받지 못했을 수도, 오기를 마다했을 수도 있었다. 수백 가지 두려움이, 둑으로 밀려드는 파도만큼이나 빠르게 그 자그마한 가슴속으로 꼬리에 꼬리를 물고 쏟아져 들어왔다.\n\n배가 연기를 뒤따라 시야에 들어왔다. 조지에게는 멋들어진 망원경이 있어, 더없이 능란한 솜씨로 그 배를 시야에 잡아넣었다. 그리고 증기선이 물 위에서 곤두박질치고 솟구치며 점점 더 가까이 다가오는 동안, 그는 그 다가오는 모양새를 두고 그럴싸한 항해 용어로 논평을 늘어놓았다. 영국 증기선이 시야에 들어왔다는 신호가 부두의 돛대 위로 펄럭이며 올라갔다. 짐작건대 애밀리아 부인의 가슴도 그와 비슷하게 펄럭이고 있었으리라.\n\n에미는 조지의 어깨너머로 망원경을 들여다보려 했으나, 도무지 아무것도 분간할 수가 없었다. 그저 검은 그림자 하나가 눈앞에서 까딱까딱 오르내리는 것만 보일 뿐이었다.\n\n조지가 다시 망원경을 잡고 배를 샅샅이 훑었다. \"야, 정말 곤두박질치네요!\" 그가 말했다. \"저기 파도가 뱃머리를 철썩 덮치는 것 좀 봐요. 갑판에는 키잡이 말고 사람이 둘밖에 없어요. 한 사람은 누워 있고, 또 한—사람은 외투를 걸치고 있는데—와! 도빈 아저씨예요, 틀림없어요!\" 그는 망원경을 탁 접고는 어머니에게 두 팔을 둘렀다. 그 부인으로 말하면, 어느 즐겨 읽는 시인의 말을 빌려 그녀가 어찌했는지 말해 두자. \"웃으며 눈물 흘렸노라(Dakruoen gelasasa).\" 그녀는 그것이 윌리엄임을 확신했다. 다른 누구일 리 없었다. 그가 오지 않기를 바란다고 했던 말은 죄다 위선이었다. 물론 그는 올 것이었다. 온다는 것 말고 그가 달리 어찌하겠는가? 그녀는 그가 오리라는 것을 알고 있었던 것이다.\n\n배는 빠르게 점점 더 가까이 다가왔다. 부두의 선착장으로 배를 맞으러 나아가는 동안, 에미는 무릎이 어찌나 떨리던지 좀처럼 뛸 수가 없었다. 그녀는 거기서 무릎을 꿇고 감사의 기도를 올리고 싶은 심정이었다. 오, 그녀는 평생토록 그 기도를 올리리라고 생각했다!\n\n날씨가 어찌나 사나웠던지 배가 부두에 옆으로 들어설 무렵에는 바깥에 빈둥거리는 이 하나 없었고, 증기선의 몇 안 되는 승객을 기다리는 짐꾼조차 좀처럼 보이지 않았다. 저 젊은 개구쟁이 조지마저 달아나 버린 터라, 붉은 천을 덧댄 낡은 외투를 걸친 그 신사가 뭍으로 발을 내디뎠을 때, 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 지켜보는 이는 거의 아무도 없었다. 그 일이란 간추리자면 이러했다.\n\n물에 흠뻑 젖은 흰 보닛과 숄 차림의 한 부인이 자그마한 두 손을 앞으로 내민 채 그에게로 다가갔고, 다음 순간 그녀는 그 낡은 외투 자락 아래로 완전히 자취를 감추고는, 온 힘을 다해 그의 한 손에 입 맞추고 있었다. 한편 그의 다른 한 손은, 짐작건대 그녀를 제 가슴께로(그녀의 머리가 겨우 그쯤에 닿았다) 끌어안고 그녀가 쓰러지지 않도록 붙드는 데 쓰이고 있었으리라. 그녀는 무언가를 중얼거리고 있었으니, 용서해 주세요, 사랑하는 윌리엄, 사랑하는, 사랑하는, 더없이 사랑하는 벗이여, 입맞춤, 입맞춤, 입맞춤, 그런 따위의 말들이었다. 그리고 실로 그녀는 외투 자락 아래서 우스꽝스러운 모양으로 그 짓을 이어 갔다.\n\n에미가 그 안에서 빠져나왔을 때에도, 그녀는 여전히 윌리엄의 한 손을 꼭 붙든 채 그의 얼굴을 올려다보고 있었다. 그 얼굴에는 슬픔과 다정한 사랑과 연민이 가득했다. 그녀는 그 책망의 빛을 알아차리고 고개를 떨궜다.\n\n\"진작 나를 불렀어야 했소, 사랑하는 애밀리아.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"다신 떠나지 않으실 거죠, 윌리엄?\"\n\n\"그럼, 다시는.\" 그가 대답하고는, 그 사랑스럽고 자그마한 영혼을 다시 한번 제 가슴에 끌어안았다.\n\n두 사람이 세관 구내에서 빠져나오는데, 조지가 망원경을 눈에 댄 채 우렁찬 환영의 웃음을 터뜨리며 그들에게 달려들었다. 그는 두 사람 주위를 빙빙 돌며 갖가지 우스꽝스러운 익살을 부리면서 그들을 집으로 이끌었다. 조스는 아직 일어나지 않았고, 베키는 모습을 보이지 않았다(그래도 그녀는 블라인드 너머로 그들을 내다보고 있었다). 조지는 아침 식사를 챙기러 달려갔다. 통로에서 페인 부인의 손에 숄과 보닛을 벗어 맡긴 에미는, 이제 윌리엄의 외투 걸쇠를 끄르러 다가갔는데—우리는, 괜찮으시다면, 조지와 함께 가서 연대장의 아침 식사나 챙겨 보기로 하자. 배는 항구에 들었다. 그는 평생토록 좇아 온 그 상을 마침내 손에 넣은 것이다. 새가 마침내 품에 날아든 것이다. 보라, 그 새가 머리를 그의 어깨에 기댄 채, 그의 가슴 바로 곁에서 부리를 비비며 구구거리고, 보드라운 날개를 펼쳐 파닥이고 있지 않은가. 이것이야말로 그가 십팔 년 동안 날마다 시각마다 청해 온 것이다. 이것이야말로 그가 애태우며 그리던 것이다. 자, 여기 있다. 정점이요, 끝이다. 제3권 마지막 페이지다. 잘 가오, 연대장. 신의 가호가 있기를, 정직한 윌리엄이여! 잘 가오, 사랑하는 애밀리아여. 그대가 매달려 온 그 우툴두툴한 늙은 떡갈나무를 휘감고, 부드러운 자그마한 덩굴이여, 다시 푸르게 자라나기를!\n\n어쩌면 그것은 인생에서 맨 처음으로 자기를 감싸 준 그 다정하고 꾸밈없는 사람에 대한 양심의 가책이었을지도 모르고, 어쩌면 그 모든 감상적인 장면에 대한 거부감이었을지도 모른다. 그러나 레베카는 이 일에서 자신이 맡은 역할에 만족하여, 도빈 연대장과 그가 결혼한 부인 앞에는 끝내 모습을 드러내지 않았다. \"긴한 볼일\"이 있어 브뤼주에 가야 한다며 그녀는 그리로 떠났고, 결혼식 자리에는 조지와 그 외삼촌만이 참석했다. 식이 끝나고 조지가 부모에게로 돌아가자, 베키 부인은 (그저 며칠간) 돌아와 홀로 남은 독신자 조지프 세들리를 위로했다. 그는 대륙에서의 삶이 더 좋다며, 누이며 그 남편과 한 살림을 차리기를 마다했던 것이다.\n\n에미는 자신이 조지의 그 편지를 읽거나 알게 되기 전에 이미 남편에게 편지를 썼다는 사실을 마음속으로 무척 다행스러워했다. \"나는 처음부터 다 알고 있었소.\" 윌리엄이 말했다. \"하지만 내가 그 가엾은 친구에 대한 추억을 두고 그런 무기를 쓸 수 있었겠소? 당신이 그때 그랬을 때 내가 그토록 괴로웠던 것도 바로 그것 때문이었소.\"\n\n\"그날 이야기는 다신 꺼내지 말아요.\" 에미가 어찌나 뉘우치며 겸허하게 외치던지, 윌리엄은 화제를 돌려, 소환 편지가 당도했을 때 자기가 함께 앉아 있던 글로비나와 그리운 페기 오다우드 이야기를 들려주었다. \"당신이 나를 부르지 않았더라면,\" 그가 웃으며 덧붙였다. \"지금쯤 글로비나의 성이 무엇이 되어 있을지 누가 알겠소?\"\n\n지금 그녀의 이름은 글로비나 포스키(이제 포스키 소령 부인)다. 그녀는 결코 연대 바깥의 사람과는 결혼하지 않겠다고 작정한 터라, 포스키의 첫 부인이 죽자 그를 차지한 것이다. 오다우드 부인 역시 연대에 어찌나 애착이 깊던지, 만일 믹에게 무슨 일이라도 생기면 맹세코 돌아와 연대원 가운데 누군가와 결혼하겠노라고 말한다. 그러나 그 소장은 더없이 정정하여 오다우드스타운에서 비글 사냥개 한 무리를 거느리고 대단히 호화롭게 지내고 있으니, (어쩌면 그들의 이웃인 캐슬호가티의 호가티를 빼고는) 그 고을에서 으뜸가는 인물이다. 부인은 여전히 지그 춤을 추며, 부총독의 지난번 무도회에서는 기마관과 짝을 이루어 춤을 추겠다고 우겨 댔다. 그녀와 글로비나 모두 도빈이 글로비나를 \"수치스럽게(SHEAMFULLY)\" 대했노라고 단언했으나, 포스키가 끼어드는 바람에 글로비나는 위안을 얻었고, 파리에서 온 아름다운 터번 하나가 오다우드 부인의 노여움을 가라앉혔다.\n\n도빈 연대장은 결혼하자마자 곧 군에서 물러났는데, 그러고는 햄프셔에 있는 자그마하고 어여쁜 시골 거처 하나를 세냈으니, 퀸스크롤리에서 그리 멀지 않은 곳이었다. 그곳에는 선거법 개정안이 통과된 뒤로 이제 핏 경과 그 가족이 늘 머물고 있었다. 작위 따위는 어림도 없는 이야기가 되었으니, 준남작이 의회에 가지고 있던 두 의석을 잃었기 때문이다. 그는 그 재앙으로 주머니도 비고 의기도 꺾여, 건강이 상한 데다 머잖아 제국이 멸망하리라는 예언까지 늘어놓았다.\n\n제인 영부인과 도빈 부인은 둘도 없는 벗이 되었다. 본관과, 연대장의 거처인 에버그린스 사이에는 끊임없이 조랑말 마차가 오갔다(이 거처는 연대장이, 가족과 함께 외국에 나가 있던 벗 폰토 소령에게서 세낸 것이었다). 영부인은 도빈 부인의 아이의 대모가 되어, 아이는 그녀의 이름을 받았고, 아버지의 뒤를 이어 그 성직록을 물려받은 제임스 크롤리 목사에게서 세례를 받았다. 그리고 두 소년, 조지와 로던 사이에는 제법 두터운 우정이 자리 잡았으니, 둘은 방학이면 함께 사냥과 총질을 다녔고, 케임브리지의 같은 학료에 나란히 입학했으며, 제인 영부인의 딸을 두고 서로 다투었다. 물론 둘 다 그 아가씨에게 반해 있었던 것이다. 조지와 그 아가씨를 짝지으려는 것은 오래도록 두 안주인이 즐겨 품어 온 계획이었으나, 정작 크롤리 양 본인은 사촌 쪽으로 마음이 기울었다고 나는 들었다.\n\n로던 크롤리 부인의 이름은 어느 집안에서도 입에 오르는 법이 없었다. 그녀에 관해서라면 모두가 입을 다물 만한 이유들이 있었던 것이다. 조지프 세들리 씨가 가는 곳마다 그녀 역시 함께 다녔으니, 그 정신 나간 사내는 완전히 그녀의 노예가 된 듯 보였다. 연대장의 변호사들은 그에게, 처남이 자기 생명에 거액의 보험을 들어 두었다고 알려 왔으니, 이로 미루어 그가 빚을 갚느라 돈을 끌어다 쓴 모양이라고 했다. 그는 동인도 회사로부터 장기 휴가를 얻어 냈는데, 실로 그의 병세는 날로 깊어 가고 있었다.\n\n보험에 관한 소식을 듣자, 애밀리아는 적잖이 놀라, 남편에게 당시 조스가 있던 브뤼셀로 가서 그의 형편이 어떠한지 알아봐 달라고 간청했다. 연대장은 마지못해 집을 나섰으니(그는 지금도 여전히 매달려 있는 펀자브 역사 집필에 깊이 빠져 있던 데다, 자기가 그토록 끔찍이 아끼는, 마침 수두에서 막 회복되어 가던 어린 딸 때문에 몹시 마음을 졸이던 터였다), 브뤼셀로 가서 보니 조스는 그 도시의 한 거대한 호텔에서 지내고 있었다. 자기 마차를 두고 손님을 청해 대접하며 자못 고상하게 살던 크롤리 부인은, 같은 호텔의 다른 객실을 쓰고 있었다.\n\n연대장은 물론 그 부인을 만날 마음이 없었고, 자기가 브뤼셀에 도착했다는 것조차도 시종을 통해 조스에게만 은밀히 알리는 것이 마땅하다고 여겼다. 조스는 크롤리 부인이 야회에 나가 둘이 단둘이 만날 수 있는 그날 밤에 와 달라고 연대장에게 청했다. 그는 가엾으리만치 쇠약해진 처남을 보았는데, 처남은 레베카를 입에 침이 마르게 칭찬하면서도 그녀를 끔찍이 두려워하고 있었다. 그녀는 듣도 보도 못한 온갖 병을 앓는 그를 더없이 갸륵한 정성으로 간병했다는 것이다. 그녀는 그에게 친딸이나 다름없었다고 했다. \"하지만, 하지만, 오, 제발 와서 내 곁에서 살아 주게, 그리고, 그리고 이따금 나를 좀 보러 와 주게.\" 이 가엾은 사내가 흐느끼듯 말했다.\n\n이 말에 연대장의 이마가 어두워졌다. \"그럴 순 없네, 조스.\" 그가 말했다. \"사정이 사정인지라, 애밀리아는 자네를 찾아올 수 없네.\"\n\n\"맹세코, 성경에 두고 맹세코,\" 조지프가 성경에 입 맞추려 들며 헐떡이듯 말했다. \"그 사람은 어린아이처럼 결백하고, 자네 아내만큼이나 한 점 티가 없는 사람이야.\"\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지.\" 연대장이 침울하게 말했다. \"하지만 에미는 자네에게 올 수 없네. 사내답게 굴게, 조스. 이 추잡한 관계를 끊어 버리게. 자네 가족에게로 돌아오게. 자네 형편이 얽혀 있다고들 하더군.\"\n\n\"얽혀 있다고!\" 조스가 외쳤다. \"누가 그런 모함을 한단 말인가? 내 돈은 죄다 더없이 유리하게 굴려지고 있어. 크롤리 부인이, 그러니까, 내 말은, 가장 이문이 많이 남도록 굴려 두었다고.\"\n\n\"그럼 빚은 없다는 건가? 그런데 어째서 생명 보험을 들었나?\"\n\n\"그건 생각해 보니, 그 사람에게 작은 선물이라도, 혹시 무슨 일이 생길 경우에 말이야. 게다가 자네도 알다시피 내 건강이 워낙 부실하니, 그저 흔한 도리상의 고마움이라고나 할까. 그리고 내 돈은 죄다 자네에게 남길 작정이야. 내 수입에서 그 정도는 떼어 둘 여유가 있어, 정말이라니까.\" 윌리엄의 그 나약한 처남이 외쳤다.\n\n연대장은 조스에게 당장 달아나라고, 크롤리 부인이 따라올 수 없는 인도로 돌아가라고, 무슨 수를 써서라도 그에게 더없이 치명적인 결과를 가져올지 모를 그 관계를 끊으라고 간곡히 권했다.\n\n조스는 두 손을 맞잡고 부르짖었다. 인도로 돌아가겠노라고. 무엇이든 하겠으나 다만 시간이 좀 필요하다고. 크롤리 부인에게는 아무 말도 해선 안 된다고. 그녀가, 그녀가 이 일을 알면 자기를 죽일 거라고. \"자넨 그 사람이 얼마나 무서운 여자인지 몰라.\" 가엾은 가련한 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면, 어째서 나와 함께 떠나지 않나?\" 도빈이 대답으로 말했다. 그러나 조스에게는 그럴 용기가 없었다. 아침에 도빈을 다시 만나겠노라고, 자기가 여기 다녀갔다는 말은 절대 해선 안 된다고, 이제 그만 가야 한다고, 베키가 들어올지도 모른다고. 그리하여 도빈은 온갖 불길한 예감에 사로잡힌 채 그의 곁을 떠났다.\n\n그는 다시는 조스를 보지 못했다. 석 달 뒤 조지프 세들리는 엑스라샤펠에서 죽었다. 알고 보니 그의 재산은 죄다 투기로 흐지부지 날아가, 갖가지 거품 회사의 휴지나 다름없는 주식으로 남아 있었다. 그가 처분할 수 있는 자산이라곤 자기 생명에 든 보험금 이천 파운드뿐이었으니, 그것은 그가 사랑하는 \"누이 애밀리아, 즉 누구누구의 아내와, 병중에 둘도 없이 귀한 간병인이자 벗이었던 레베카, 즉 로던 크롤리 중령(바스 훈작사)의 아내\" 사이에 똑같이 나뉘었고, 후자가 유산 관리인으로 지명되었다.\n\n보험 회사의 사무 변호사는 이것이 일찍이 자기가 다룬 사건 가운데 가장 시커먼 사건이라고 단언하며, 그 죽음을 조사하기 위해 엑스로 조사단을 보내겠다고 말했고, 회사는 보험금 지급을 거부했다. 그러나 크롤리 부인은, 혹은 스스로 일컫던 대로 크롤리 영부인은, 즉시 (테이비스 인의 사무 변호사 버크·서텔·헤이스 씨를 대동하고) 런던으로 와서 회사가 지급을 거부할 테면 거부해 보라고 으름장을 놓았다. 그들은 조사를 청했고, 그녀야말로 평생토록 자신을 따라다닌 파렴치한 음모의 표적이라고 단언하며, 마침내 승리를 거두었다. 보험금은 지급되었고 그녀의 평판도 바로 섰으나, 도빈 연대장은 유산 가운데 자기 몫을 보험 회사로 돌려보내고, 레베카와는 어떠한 왕래도 하지 않겠다고 단호히 거절했다.\n\n그녀는 스스로 그리 일컫기를 그치지 않았으나 결코 크롤리 영부인이었던 적은 없었다. 로던 크롤리 중령 각하는 코번트리 섬에서 황열병으로 죽었으니, 그의 형 핏 경이 세상을 뜨기 여섯 주 전의 일이었고, 더없이 깊은 사랑과 애도를 받았다. 그리하여 영지는 현재의 로던 크롤리 준남작 경에게로 넘어갔다.\n\n그 역시 자기 어머니를 만나기를 거절했는데, 그는 어머니에게 후한 생활비를 보내고 있거니와, 게다가 그녀는 형편이 매우 넉넉해 보이기까지 한다. 준남작은 줄곧 퀸스크롤리에서 제인 영부인과 그 딸과 함께 지내고, 한편 레베카, 즉 크롤리 영부인은 주로 배스와 첼트넘 언저리를 맴도는데, 그곳에서는 더없이 훌륭한 사람들로 이루어진 막강한 무리가 그녀를 천하에 둘도 없이 억울하게 누명을 쓴 여인으로 여기고 있다. 그녀에게는 적들이 있다. 적 없는 이가 어디 있으랴? 그녀의 삶이 곧 그들에게 보내는 대답이다. 그녀는 경건한 일에 분주히 매달린다. 교회에 나가는데, 결코 하인을 거느리지 않는 법이 없다. 그녀의 이름은 온갖 자선 명부에 올라 있다. 헐벗은 오렌지 파는 소녀, 외면당한 빨래하는 아낙, 곤궁한 머핀 장수가 그녀에게서 변함없고 너그러운 벗을 만난다. 그녀는 이 가엾은 이들을 위해 늘 자선 시장에 좌판을 벌인다. 얼마 전 런던에 온 에미와 그 아이들과 연대장은, 그런 한 자선 시장에서 별안간 그녀와 마주쳤다. 그녀는 다소곳이 눈을 내리깔고 미소 지었으나, 그들은 그녀를 피해 화들짝 물러났다. 에미는 (이제 늠름한 젊은 신사로 자란) 조지의 팔에 매달려 종종걸음으로 달아났고, 연대장은 세상 그 무엇보다도—심지어 자기 펀자브 역사보다도—더 끔찍이 아끼는 어린 제이니를 와락 안아 들었다.\n\n\"나보다 더 끔찍이 아끼지.\" 에미는 한숨지으며 생각한다. 그러나 그는 애밀리아에게 다정하고 부드럽지 않은 말이라곤 한마디도 한 적이 없으며, 그녀의 바람이라면 무엇이든 채워 주려 애쓰지 않은 적이 없다.\n\n아! 헛되고 헛되도다(Vanitas Vanitatum)! 우리 가운데 누가 이 세상에서 행복한가? 누가 제 소원을 이루었는가? 혹은 이루고도 만족하는가? 자, 얘들아, 이제 상자와 꼭두각시들을 챙겨 넣자꾸나. 우리 연극은 다 끝났으니.";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-599"]=[[["While the present century was in its teens, and on one sunshiny morning in June, there drove up to the great iron gate of Miss Pinkerton's academy for young ladies, on Chiswick Mall, a large family coach, with two fat horses in blazing harness, driven by a fat coachman in a three-cornered hat and wig, at the rate of four miles an hour.", "금세기가 아직 십 대였던 어느 시절, 유월의 어느 햇살 따스한 아침이었다. 치즈윅 거리에 자리한 핑커튼 여사의 숙녀 교육원, 그 커다란 철문 앞으로 큼직한 가족용 마차 한 대가 다가와 멈춰 섰다."], ["A black servant, who reposed on the box beside the fat coachman, uncurled his bandy legs as soon as the equipage drew up opposite Miss Pinkerton's shining brass plate, and as he pulled the bell at least a score of young heads were seen peering out of the narrow windows of the stately old brick house.", "번쩍이는 마구를 두른 살진 말 두 마리가 끄는 마차였고, 삼각모를 쓰고 가발을 얹은 살진 마부가 시속 사 마일의 속도로 몰고 온 것이었다. 살진 마부 옆 마부석에 늘어져 있던 흑인 하인은, 마차가 핑커튼 여사의 반짝이는 놋쇠 명패 맞은편에 멈추자마자 휘어진 다리를 펴고 일어나 종을 당겼다."], ["Nay, the acute observer might have recognized the little red nose of good-natured Miss Jemima Pinkerton herself, rising over some geranium pots in the window of that lady's own drawing-room.", "그러자 위풍당당한 낡은 벽돌 저택의 좁은 창문마다 적어도 스무 개는 됨직한 어린 머리들이 빼꼼히 내다보았다. 아니, 눈썰미 있는 관찰자라면 인자한 제미마 핑커튼 양 본인의 자그마한 빨간 코가, 응접실 창가의 제라늄 화분들 너머로 솟아오르는 것까지 알아보았을지도 모른다."]], [["\"It is Mrs. Sedley's coach, sister,\"", "\"세들리 부인의 마차예요, 언니.\""], ["said Miss Jemima.", "제미마 양이 말했다."], ["\"Sambo, the black servant, has just rung the bell; and the coachman has a new red waistcoat.\"", "\"흑인 하인 샘보가 방금 종을 울렸어요. 마부는 빨간 조끼를 새로 입었네요.\""]], [["\"Have you completed all the necessary preparations incident to Miss Sedley's departure, Miss Jemima?\"", "\"세들리 양의 출발에 따르는 필요한 준비는 모두 마쳤느냐, 제미마?\" 핑커튼 여사 본인이 물었다."], ["asked Miss Pinkerton herself, that majestic lady; the Semiramis of Hammersmith, the friend of Doctor Johnson, the correspondent of Mrs. Chapone herself.", "저 위엄 넘치는 부인, 해머스미스의 세미라미스, 존슨 박사의 벗이자 채폰 부인 본인과 서신을 주고받는 사이인 그분이었다."]], [["\"The girls were up at four this morning, packing her trunks, sister,\"", "\"여자아이들이 오늘 아침 네 시에 일어나서 트렁크를 쌌어요, 언니.\""], ["replied Miss Jemima; \"we have made her a bow-pot.\"", "제미마 양이 대답했다. \"꽃다발도 만들어 주었고요.\""]], [["\"Say a bouquet, sister Jemima, 'tis more genteel.\"", "\"부케라고 하렴, 제미마. 그게 더 품위 있는 표현이지.\""]], [["\"Well, a booky as big almost as a haystack; I have put up two bottles of the gillyflower water for Mrs.", "\"네, 거의 건초더미만 한 부케예요."], ["Sedley, and the receipt for making it, in Amelia's box.\"", "세들리 부인을 위해 향꽃무 화장수 두 병이랑, 그 만드는 법까지 애밀리아의 상자에 넣어 두었어요.\""]], [["\"And I trust, Miss Jemima, you have made a copy of Miss Sedley's account.", "\"그리고 제미마, 세들리 양의 계산서 사본을 만들어 두었으리라 믿는다."], ["This is it, is it?", "이게 그것이냐?"], ["Very good--ninety-three pounds, four shillings.", "그렇구나, 아주 좋아. 구십삼 파운드 사 실링이라."], ["Be kind enough to address it to John Sedley, Esquire, and to seal this billet which I have written to his lady.\"", "수고스럽겠지만 존 세들리 귀하 앞으로 주소를 적어 두고, 내가 그 부인께 쓴 이 편지에 봉인을 해 다오.\""]], [["In Miss Jemima's eyes an autograph letter of her sister, Miss Pinkerton, was an object of as deep veneration as would have been a letter from a sovereign.", "제미마 양의 눈에는 언니인 핑커튼 여사가 친필로 쓴 편지란, 군주에게서 온 편지나 다름없이 깊은 경외의 대상이었다."], ["Only when her pupils quitted the establishment, or when they were about to be married, and once, when poor Miss Birch died of the scarlet fever, was Miss Pinkerton known to write personally to the parents of her pupils; and it was Jemima's opinion that if anything could console Mrs.", "핑커튼 여사가 제자들의 부모에게 손수 편지를 쓰는 일이라고는, 제자가 학원을 떠날 때나 결혼을 앞두었을 때, 그리고 딱 한 번, 가엾은 버치 양이 성홍열로 세상을 떠났을 때뿐이었다."], ["Birch for her daughter's loss, it would be that pious and eloquent composition in which Miss Pinkerton announced the event.", "그러니 만약 버치 부인이 딸을 잃은 슬픔을 무엇으로든 달랠 수 있다면, 그것은 핑커튼 여사가 그 비보를 알리며 써 보낸 경건하고 유려한 문장일 것이라는 게 제미마의 생각이었다."]], [["In the present instance Miss Pinkerton's \"billet\" was to the following effect:--", "이번 경우, 핑커튼 여사의 \"편지\"는 다음과 같은 내용이었다."]], [["The Mall, Chiswick, June 15, 18", "치즈윅 거리에서, 18××년 6월 15일"]], [["MADAM,--After her six years'", "부인께."], ["residence at the Mall, I have the honour and happiness of presenting Miss Amelia Sedley to her parents, as a young lady not unworthy to occupy a fitting position in their polished and refined circle.", "따님께서 본원에 육 년간 머무신 끝에, 저는 애밀리아 세들리 양을 그 부모님께 돌려보내는 영광과 기쁨을 누리게 되었습니다. 세들리 양은 부모님의 세련되고 고상한 교제권에서 어울리는 자리를 차지하기에 부족함이 없는 숙녀입니다."], ["Those virtues which characterize the young English gentlewoman, those accomplishments which become her birth and station, will not be found wanting in the amiable Miss Sedley, whose INDUSTRY and OBEDIENCE have endeared her to her instructors, and whose delightful sweetness of temper has charmed her AGED and her YOUTHFUL companions.", "영국 양가의 젊은 숙녀를 특징짓는 미덕들, 그 출생과 신분에 걸맞은 교양들을 이 사랑스러운 세들리 양에게서 찾아보지 못하는 일은 없을 것입니다. 그 근면함과 순종이 교사들의 사랑을 한 몸에 받았고, 그 더없이 사랑스러운 온화한 성정이 나이 든 동무들과 어린 동무들 모두를 매료시켰습니다."]], [["In music, in dancing, in orthography, in every variety of embroidery and needlework, she will be found to have realized her friends' fondest wishes.", "음악, 무용, 철자법, 그리고 온갖 종류의 자수와 바느질에서 세들리 양은 벗들의 가장 흐뭇한 기대를 모두 실현했음이 드러날 것입니다."], ["In geography there is still much to be desired; and a careful and undeviating use of the backboard, for four hours daily during the next three years, is recommended as necessary to the acquirement of that dignified DEPORTMENT AND CARRIAGE, so requisite for every young lady of FASHION.", "다만 지리 과목에서는 아직 바라는 바가 많이 남아 있습니다. 또한 모든 유행을 좇는 숙녀에게 그토록 긴요한, 저 기품 있는 몸가짐과 자태를 익히기 위해서는, 앞으로 삼 년간 매일 네 시간씩 자세 교정판을 한결같이 어김없이 사용하기를 권하는 바입니다."]], [["In the principles of religion and morality, Miss Sedley will be found worthy of an establishment which has been honoured by the presence of THE GREAT LEXICOGRAPHER, and the patronage of the admirable Mrs. Chapone.", "종교와 도덕의 원리에서도 세들리 양은, 저 위대한 사전 편찬자가 친히 왕림하시는 영광을 누렸고 또한 흠모할 만한 채폰 부인의 후원을 받아 온 본원에 부끄럽지 않은 인물임이 드러날 것입니다."], ["In leaving the Mall, Miss Amelia carries with her the hearts of her companions, and the affectionate regards of her mistress, who has the honour to subscribe herself,", "이 거리를 떠나면서 애밀리아 양은 동무들의 마음과, 이 글을 적어 올리는 영광을 누리는 그 스승의 애정 어린 마음을 함께 가져갈 것입니다."]], [["Madam, Your most obliged humble servant, BARBARA PINKERTON", "부인께, 부인의 더없이 황송한 종복 바버라 핑커튼 올림."]], [["P.S.--Miss Sharp accompanies Miss Sedley.", "추신. 샤프 양이 세들리 양과 동행합니다."], ["It is particularly requested that Miss Sharp's stay in Russell Square may not exceed ten days.", "샤프 양이 러셀 광장에 머무는 기간이 열흘을 넘지 않도록 각별히 부탁드립니다."], ["The family of distinction with whom she is engaged, desire to avail themselves of her services as soon as possible.", "샤프 양을 고용한 명문가에서 가능한 한 빨리 그녀의 봉사를 받고자 하기 때문입니다."]], [["This letter completed, Miss Pinkerton proceeded to write her own name, and Miss Sedley's, in the fly-leaf of a Johnson's Dictionary--the interesting work which she invariably presented to her scholars, on their departure from the Mall.", "이 편지를 다 쓰고 나서, 핑커튼 여사는 존슨 사전의 속표지에 자신의 이름과 세들리 양의 이름을 적었다. 이 흥미로운 저작은 제자들이 이 거리를 떠날 때마다 그녀가 반드시 선사하는 것이었다."], ["On the cover was inserted a copy of \"Lines addressed to a young lady on quitting Miss Pinkerton's school, at the Mall; by the late revered Doctor Samuel Johnson.\"", "그 표지에는 \"치즈윅 거리, 핑커튼 여사의 학원을 떠나는 어느 젊은 숙녀에게 바치는 시. 고인이 되신 존경하는 새뮤얼 존슨 박사 작\"이라는 시구의 사본이 끼워져 있었다."], ["In fact, the Lexicographer's name was always on the lips of this majestic woman, and a visit he had paid to her was the cause of her reputation and her fortune.", "사실 이 위엄 넘치는 부인의 입에는 늘 그 사전 편찬자의 이름이 오르내렸고, 그분이 한 번 찾아왔던 일이 그녀의 명성과 재산의 밑천이 된 것이었다."]], [["Being commanded by her elder sister to get \"the Dictionary\" from the cupboard, Miss Jemima had extracted two copies of the book from the receptacle in question.", "언니에게서 찬장에서 \"사전\"을 꺼내 오라는 명을 받자, 제미마 양은 문제의 그 보관함에서 책 두 권을 꺼냈다."], ["When Miss Pinkerton had finished the inscription in the first, Jemima, with rather a dubious and timid air, handed her the second.", "핑커튼 여사가 첫 권에 적어 넣기를 마치자, 제미마는 다소 미심쩍고 머뭇거리는 기색으로 두 번째 권을 건넸다."]], [["\"For whom is this, Miss Jemima?\"", "\"이건 누구 것이냐, 제미마?\""], ["said Miss Pinkerton, with awful coldness.", "핑커튼 여사가 소름 끼치도록 싸늘하게 말했다."]], [["\"For Becky Sharp,\"", "\"베키 샤프 거예요.\""], ["answered Jemima, trembling very much, and blushing over her withered face and neck, as she turned her back on her sister.", "제미마가 몹시 떨면서, 시든 얼굴과 목까지 붉히며, 언니에게 등을 돌린 채 대답했다. \"베키 샤프요."], ["\"For Becky Sharp: she's going too.\"", "그 애도 떠나잖아요.\""]], [["\"MISS JEMIMA!\"", "\"제미마!\""], ["exclaimed Miss Pinkerton, in the largest capitals.", "핑커튼 여사가 가장 큰 대문자로 외쳤다."], ["\"Are you in your senses?", "\"제정신이냐?"], ["Replace the Dixonary in the closet, and never venture to take such a liberty in future.\"", "그 사전을 도로 찬장에 넣어라. 앞으로 다시는 그런 방자한 짓을 저지를 엄두도 내지 말아라.\""]], [["\"Well, sister, it's only two-and-ninepence, and poor Becky will be miserable if she don't get one.\"", "\"하지만 언니, 겨우 이 실링 구 펜스인걸요. 가엾은 베키가 못 받으면 비참해할 거예요.\""]], [["\"Send Miss Sedley instantly to me,\"", "\"세들리 양을 당장 내게 보내라.\""], ["said Miss Pinkerton.", "핑커튼 여사가 말했다."], ["And so venturing not to say another word, poor Jemima trotted off, exceedingly flurried and nervous.", "그래서 가엾은 제미마는 감히 한마디도 더 못 하고, 몹시 허둥대며 안절부절못한 채 종종걸음으로 물러갔다."]], [["Miss Sedley's papa was a merchant in London, and a man of some wealth; whereas Miss Sharp was an articled pupil, for whom Miss Pinkerton had done, as she thought, quite enough, without conferring upon her at parting the high honour of the Dixonary.", "세들리 양의 아버지는 런던의 상인으로, 제법 재산이 있는 사람이었다. 반면 샤프 양은 계약을 맺고 들어온 장학생이었다. 핑커튼 여사는 작별의 자리에서 사전이라는 높은 영예까지 베풀지 않더라도, 자기 딴에는 그녀에게 충분히 해 줄 만큼 해 주었다고 여기는 터였다."]], [["Although schoolmistresses'", "여교장의 편지란 묘지의 비문 이상으로도 이하로도 믿을 게 못 되는 법이다. 그러나 때로는 정말로 비석에 새겨진 온갖 찬사를 받아 마땅한 사람이 세상을 떠나는 일도 있는 법이다."], ["letters are to be trusted no more nor less than churchyard epitaphs; yet, as it sometimes happens that a person departs this life who is really deserving of all the praises the stone cutter carves over his bones; who IS a good Christian, a good parent, child, wife, or husband; who actually DOES leave a disconsolate family to mourn his loss; so in academies of the male and female sex it occurs every now and then that the pupil is fully worthy of the praises bestowed by the disinterested instructor.", "참으로 훌륭한 그리스도교도였고, 훌륭한 부모요 자식이요 아내요 남편이었으며, 실제로 슬픔에 잠긴 유족을 남기고 떠난 사람 말이다. 그와 마찬가지로, 남녀를 막론하고 교육원에서도 이따금, 사심 없는 교사가 베푼 찬사를 받기에 온전히 합당한 제자가 나오는 법이다."], ["Now, Miss Amelia Sedley was a young lady of this singular species; and deserved not only all that Miss Pinkerton said in her praise, but had many charming qualities which that pompous old Minerva of a woman could not see, from the differences of rank and age between her pupil and herself.", "그런데 애밀리아 세들리 양이야말로 이 보기 드문 부류의 젊은 숙녀였다. 핑커튼 여사가 칭찬한 모든 것을 받아 마땅했을 뿐 아니라, 저 거드름 피우는 늙은 미네르바 같은 부인이 제자와 자신 사이의 신분과 나이 차이 때문에 미처 보지 못한 매력적인 자질들도 숱하게 지니고 있었다."]], [["For she could not only sing like a lark, or a Mrs.", "그녀는 종달새처럼, 혹은 빌링턴 부인처럼 노래할 줄 알았고, 힐리스버그나 파리소처럼 춤출 줄 알았으며, 자수도 아름답게 놓고, 철자법은 사전 그 자체만큼이나 정확했다."], ["Billington, and dance like Hillisberg or Parisot; and embroider beautifully; and spell as well as a Dixonary itself; but she had such a kindly, smiling, tender, gentle, generous heart of her own, as won the love of everybody who came near her, from Minerva herself down to the poor girl in the scullery, and the one-eyed tart-woman's daughter, who was permitted to vend her wares once a week to the young ladies in the Mall.", "그뿐 아니라 그녀에게는 다정하고, 잘 웃고, 부드럽고, 온화하고, 너그러운 마음씨가 있어서, 미네르바 본인부터 부엌데기 가엾은 하녀에 이르기까지, 또 일주일에 한 번 이 거리의 숙녀들에게 물건을 팔도록 허락받은 외눈박이 파이 장수의 딸에 이르기까지, 곁에 다가오는 모든 이의 사랑을 얻었다."], ["She had twelve intimate and bosom friends out of the twenty-four young ladies.", "스물네 명의 젊은 숙녀 가운데 열두 명이 그녀의 마음을 터놓는 절친한 벗이었다."], ["Even envious Miss Briggs never spoke ill of her; high and mighty Miss Saltire (Lord Dexter's granddaughter) allowed that her figure was genteel; and as for Miss Swartz, the rich woolly-haired mulatto from St.", "시샘 많은 브리그스 양조차 그녀를 험담하는 법이 없었고, 도도하고 콧대 높은 솔타이어 양(덱스터 경의 손녀)도 그녀의 자태만큼은 기품 있다고 인정했다."], ["Kitt's, on the day Amelia went away, she was in such a passion of tears that they were obliged to send for Dr. Floss, and half tipsify her with salvolatile.", "그리고 세인트키츠 출신의 부유한 곱슬머리 혼혈인 스워츠 양으로 말하자면, 애밀리아가 떠나던 날 어찌나 격렬하게 눈물을 쏟던지 플로스 박사를 부르고 각성제로 반쯤 취하게 만들어야 할 지경이었다."], ["Miss Pinkerton's attachment was, as may be supposed from the high position and eminent virtues of that lady, calm and dignified; but Miss Jemima had already whimpered several times at the idea of Amelia's departure; and, but for fear of her sister, would have gone off in downright hysterics, like the heiress (who paid double) of St.", "핑커튼 여사의 애정으로 말하면, 그분의 높은 지위와 빼어난 덕망으로 미루어 짐작할 수 있듯, 차분하고 위엄 있는 것이었다. 그러나 제미마 양은 애밀리아가 떠난다는 생각에 벌써 몇 번이나 훌쩍거렸고, 언니가 무섭지만 않았더라면 세인트키츠 출신의 그 (수업료를 곱절로 내는) 상속녀처럼 그야말로 발작이라도 일으켰을 것이다."], ["Kitt's. Such luxury of grief, however, is only allowed to parlour-boarders.", "하지만 그런 사치스러운 슬픔이란 응접실에 묵는 특별 기숙생에게나 허락되는 법이었다."], ["Honest Jemima had all the bills, and the washing, and the mending, and the puddings, and the plate and crockery, and the servants to superintend.", "정직한 제미마는 온갖 계산서며 빨래며 수선이며 푸딩이며 식기며 그릇이며 하인들까지 죄다 도맡아 관리해야 했다. 그런데 그녀 이야기를 해서 무엇하겠는가?"], ["But why speak about her? It is probable that we shall not hear of her again from this moment to the end of time, and that when the great filigree iron gates are once closed on her, she and her awful sister will never issue therefrom into this little world of history.", "아마도 우리는 이 순간부터 시간의 끝에 이르도록 다시는 그녀의 소식을 듣지 못할 것이고, 저 커다란 세공 철문이 그녀를 향해 한 번 닫히고 나면, 그녀와 그 무시무시한 언니는 이 자그마한 이야기의 세계로 다시는 나오지 못할 것이다."]], [["But as we are to see a great deal of Amelia, there is no harm in saying, at the outset of our acquaintance, that she was a dear little creature; and a great mercy it is, both in life and in novels, which (and the latter especially) abound in villains of the most sombre sort, that we are to have for a constant companion so guileless and good-natured a person. As she is not a heroine, there is no need to describe her person; indeed I am afraid that her nose was rather short than otherwise, and her cheeks a great deal too round and red for a heroine; but her face blushed with rosy health, and her lips with the freshest of smiles, and she had a pair of eyes which sparkled with the brightest and honestest good-humour, except indeed when they filled with tears, and that was a great deal too often; for the silly thing would cry over a dead canary-bird; or over a mouse, that the cat haply had seized upon; or over the end of a novel, were it ever so stupid; and as for saying an unkind word to her, were any persons hard-hearted enough to do so--why, so much the worse for them. Even Miss Pinkerton, that austere and godlike woman, ceased scolding her after the first time, and though she no more comprehended sensibility than she did Algebra, gave all masters and teachers particular orders to treat Miss Sedley with the utmost gentleness, as harsh treatment was injurious to her.", "그러나 우리가 앞으로 애밀리아를 자주 보게 될 터이니, 알게 된 첫머리에서 그녀가 사랑스러운 작은 사람이었다고 말해 두어도 해될 것은 없겠다. 인생에서나 소설에서나 가장 음울한 부류의 악당들이 넘쳐나는데(특히 소설이 그렇다), 이토록 천진하고 선량한 사람을 변함없는 길동무로 삼게 된 것은 참으로 큰 자비이다. 그녀는 여주인공이 아니니 그 용모를 묘사할 필요는 없겠다. 사실 그녀의 코는 오히려 짧은 편이었고, 양 볼은 여주인공치고는 너무 둥글고 발그레했던 것 같아 걱정이긴 하다. 그러나 그 얼굴은 장밋빛 건강으로 발그레했고, 입술에는 더없이 싱그러운 미소가 어렸으며, 더없이 밝고 정직한 명랑함으로 반짝이는 두 눈을 지니고 있었다. 그 눈이 눈물로 가득 차는 때만 빼면 말이다. 그런데 그런 때가 너무도 잦았다. 이 어리석은 아이는 죽은 카나리아를 두고도 울었고, 어쩌다 고양이에게 잡힌 생쥐를 두고도 울었으며, 소설의 결말을 두고도, 그게 아무리 시시한 것이라도 울었기 때문이다. 그리고 그녀에게 모진 말을 한다는 것으로 말할 것 같으면, 어느 누가 그토록 모진 마음을 먹고 그런 짓을 한다면 그건 그 사람만 더 손해 볼 일이었다. 저 준엄하고 신과도 같은 부인 핑커튼 여사조차 처음 한 번 꾸짖은 뒤로는 다시 야단치지 않았고, 비록 감수성이라는 것을 대수롭게 여기는 정도가 대수학을 여기는 정도와 다를 바 없었지만, 모진 대우는 그녀에게 해롭다 하여 모든 교사와 선생에게 세들리 양을 더없이 부드럽게 대하라는 특별한 지시를 내렸던 것이다."]], [["So that when the day of departure came, between her two customs of laughing and crying, Miss Sedley was greatly puzzled how to act.", "그리하여 떠나는 날이 오자, 세들리 양은 웃는 버릇과 우는 버릇 사이에서 어떻게 처신해야 할지 몹시 갈피를 잡지 못했다."], ["She was glad to go home, and yet most woefully sad at leaving school.", "집에 가게 되어 기쁘면서도, 학교를 떠나는 일이 더없이 서글펐던 것이다."], ["For three days before, little Laura Martin, the orphan, followed her about like a little dog.", "사흘 전부터 고아 소녀인 어린 로라 마틴은 강아지처럼 그녀를 졸졸 따라다녔다."], ["She had to make and receive at least fourteen presents--to make fourteen solemn promises of writing every week: \"Send my letters under cover to my grandpapa, the Earl of Dexter,\"", "그녀는 적어도 열네 개의 선물을 만들어 주고 또 받아야 했으며, 매주 편지하겠다는 엄숙한 약속을 열네 번이나 해야 했다. \"내 편지는 우리 할아버지 덱스터 백작 앞으로 봉투에 싸서 보내.\""], ["said Miss Saltire (who, by the way, was rather shabby).", "솔타이어 양이 말했다(덧붙이자면, 이 아이는 행색이 다소 초라했다)."], ["\"Never mind the postage, but write every day, you dear darling,\"", "\"우편 요금일랑 신경 쓰지 말고 매일 써 보내, 우리 귀여운 애야.\""], ["said the impetuous and woolly-headed, but generous and affectionate Miss Swartz; and the orphan little Laura Martin (who was just in round-hand), took her friend's hand and said, looking up in her face wistfully, \"Amelia, when I write to you I shall call you Mamma.\"", "충동적이고 곱슬머리지만 너그럽고 정 많은 스워츠 양이 말했다. 그리고 고아인 어린 로라 마틴은(이제 막 정자체를 배우는 참이었다) 친구의 손을 잡고 애틋하게 그 얼굴을 올려다보며 말했다."], ["All which details, I have no doubt, JONES, who reads this book at his Club, will pronounce to be excessively foolish, trivial, twaddling, and ultra-sentimental.", "\"애밀리아, 내가 너한테 편지 쓸 땐 너를 엄마라고 부를래.\" 이 모든 자질구레한 이야기를, 자기 클럽에서 이 책을 읽고 있는 존스라면 분명 지나치게 어리석고, 하찮고, 시답잖고, 극도로 감상적이라고 단언하리라는 것을 나는 의심치 않는다."], ["Yes; I can see Jones at this minute (rather flushed with his joint of mutton and half pint of wine), taking out his pencil and scoring under the words \"foolish, twaddling,\"", "그렇다, 나는 지금 이 순간 존스가(양고기 한 점과 와인 반 파인트로 얼굴이 다소 불콰해진 채) 연필을 꺼내 들고 \"어리석은, 시답잖은\""], ["&c., and adding to them his own remark of \"QUITE TRUE.\"", "따위의 낱말에 밑줄을 그으며 거기에 자기 나름의 평인 \"정말 옳은 말이로다\"를 적어 넣는 모습이 눈에 선하다."], ["Well, he is a lofty man of genius, and admires the great and heroic in life and novels; and so had better take warning and go elsewhere.", "그래, 그는 고상한 천재라서 인생에서나 소설에서나 위대하고 영웅적인 것을 흠모하는 사람이니, 부디 경고를 새겨듣고 딴 데로 가는 편이 나을 것이다."]], [["Well, then.", "자, 그럼 이야기를 이어 가자."], ["The flowers, and the presents, and the trunks, and bonnet-boxes of Miss Sedley having been arranged by Mr.", "세들리 양의 꽃이며 선물이며 트렁크며 모자 상자가 샘보의 손에 마차에 실리고 나서, 거기에 더해 샤프 양의 명함이 단정하게 못으로 박힌, 비바람에 닳은 자그마한 쇠가죽 트렁크 하나가 실렸다. 샘보는 그것을 히죽거리며 건넸고 마부도 거기에 걸맞은 비웃음을 지으며 실었다."], ["Sambo in the carriage, together with a very small and weather-beaten old cow's-skin trunk with Miss Sharp's card neatly nailed upon it, which was delivered by Sambo with a grin, and packed by the coachman with a corresponding sneer--the hour for parting came; and the grief of that moment was considerably lessened by the admirable discourse which Miss Pinkerton addressed to her pupil.", "이윽고 작별의 시간이 왔다. 그러나 그 순간의 슬픔은 핑커튼 여사가 제자에게 들려준 훌륭한 일장 연설 덕분에 상당히 누그러졌다."], ["Not that the parting speech caused Amelia to philosophise, or that it armed her in any way with a calmness, the result of argument; but it was intolerably dull, pompous, and tedious; and having the fear of her schoolmistress greatly before her eyes, Miss Sedley did not venture, in her presence, to give way to any ebullitions of private grief.", "그 작별 연설이 애밀리아를 사색에 잠기게 했다거나, 논리에서 우러난 평정심으로 어떤 식으로든 그녀를 무장시켰다는 말은 아니다. 다만 그 연설이 견딜 수 없이 따분하고, 거드름 가득하고, 지루했던 데다, 여교장에 대한 두려움이 눈앞에 크게 어른거린 탓에, 세들리 양은 그분 면전에서 감히 사사로운 슬픔을 터뜨릴 엄두를 내지 못했던 것이다."], ["A seed-cake and a bottle of wine were produced in the drawing-room, as on the solemn occasions of the visits of parents, and these refreshments being partaken of, Miss Sedley was at liberty to depart.", "응접실에는 부모님이 방문하는 엄숙한 행사 때처럼 씨앗 케이크 한 조각과 와인 한 병이 나왔고, 이 다과를 나눈 뒤에야 세들리 양은 비로소 떠나도 좋다는 허락을 받았다."]], [["\"You'll go in and say good-by to Miss Pinkerton, Becky!\"", "\"들어가서 핑커튼 여사님께 작별 인사 드려야지, 베키!\""], ["said Miss Jemima to a young lady of whom nobody took any notice, and who was coming downstairs with her own bandbox.", "제미마 양이, 아무도 눈여겨보지 않는 한 젊은 숙녀에게 말했다. 그녀는 자기 모자 상자를 직접 들고 계단을 내려오던 참이었다."]], [["\"I suppose I must,\"", "\"그래야겠지요.\" 샤프 양이 차분하게 대답하니, 제미마 양은 적잖이 놀랐다."], ["said Miss Sharp calmly, and much to the wonder of Miss Jemima; and the latter having knocked at the door, and receiving permission to come in, Miss Sharp advanced in a very unconcerned manner, and said in French, and with a perfect accent, \"Mademoiselle, je viens vous faire mes adieux.\"", "제미마가 문을 두드리고 들어와도 좋다는 허락을 받자, 샤프 양은 더없이 무심한 태도로 나아가 완벽한 억양의 프랑스어로 말했다. \"마드무아젤, 작별 인사를 드리러 왔습니다.\""]], [["Miss Pinkerton did not understand French; she only directed those who did: but biting her lips and throwing up her venerable and Roman-nosed head (on the top of which figured a large and solemn turban), she said, \"Miss Sharp, I wish you a good morning.\" As the Hammersmith Semiramis spoke, she waved one hand, both by way of adieu, and to give Miss Sharp an opportunity of shaking one of the fingers of the hand which was left out for that purpose.", "핑커튼 여사는 프랑스어를 알아듣지 못했다. 그저 그것을 아는 이들을 부릴 따름이었다. 그러나 그녀는 입술을 깨물고, 위풍당당한 로마식 매부리코가 달린 머리를(그 위에는 크고 근엄한 터번이 얹혀 있었다) 한껏 치켜들며 말했다. \"샤프 양, 좋은 아침 보내기를 바라네.\" 해머스미스의 세미라미스는 그렇게 말하면서, 작별의 뜻을 표하는 동시에 샤프 양이 그 목적으로 내민 손가락 하나를 잡고 흔들 기회를 주려고 한 손을 흔들어 보였다."]], [["Miss Sharp only folded her own hands with a very frigid smile and bow, and quite declined to accept the proffered honour; on which Semiramis tossed up her turban more indignantly than ever.", "샤프 양은 그저 더없이 싸늘한 미소와 인사로 자기 두 손을 마주 모을 뿐, 베풀어진 그 영예를 받아들이기를 한사코 거절했다. 그러자 세미라미스는 그 어느 때보다 분개하여 터번을 치켜 흔들었다."], ["In fact, it was a little battle between the young lady and the old one, and the latter was worsted.", "사실 이것은 젊은 숙녀와 늙은 숙녀 사이의 자그마한 한판 싸움이었고, 패한 쪽은 늙은 숙녀였다."], ["\"Heaven bless you, my child,\"", "\"하느님의 가호가 있기를, 얘야.\""], ["said she, embracing Amelia, and scowling the while over the girl's shoulder at Miss Sharp.", "그녀가 애밀리아를 끌어안으며, 그러는 내내 소녀의 어깨너머로 샤프 양을 노려보면서 말했다."], ["\"Come away, Becky,\"", "\"이리 와, 베키.\""], ["said Miss Jemima, pulling the young woman away in great alarm, and the drawing-room door closed upon them for ever.", "제미마 양이 잔뜩 겁에 질려 그 젊은 여자를 끌어당기며 말했고, 응접실 문은 그들 뒤로 영영 닫혔다."]], [["Then came the struggle and parting below.", "이윽고 아래층에서는 부둥켜안고 헤어지는 한바탕 소동이 벌어졌다."], ["Words refuse to tell it.", "말로는 다 전할 수가 없다."], ["All the servants were there in the hall--all the dear friends--all the young ladies--the dancing-master who had just arrived; and there was such a scuffling, and hugging, and kissing, and crying, with the hysterical YOOPS of Miss Swartz, the parlour-boarder, from her room, as no pen can depict, and as the tender heart would fain pass over.", "하인들이 모두 현관에 나와 있었고, 정다운 벗들도, 젊은 숙녀들도 다 모여 있었으며, 막 도착한 무용 선생도 있었다. 그리하여 서로 밀치고, 부둥켜안고, 입 맞추고, 우는 통에, 거기다 자기 방에서 들려오는 응접실 기숙생 스워츠 양의 발작적인 흐느낌까지 더해져, 그 어떤 붓으로도 그릴 수 없고 다정한 마음이라면 차마 그냥 지나치고 싶을 광경이 펼쳐졌다."], ["The embracing was over; they parted--that is, Miss Sedley parted from her friends.", "포옹이 끝나자 그들은 헤어졌다. 그러니까, 세들리 양이 벗들과 헤어졌다는 말이다."], ["Miss Sharp had demurely entered the carriage some minutes before.", "샤프 양은 몇 분 전에 이미 새침하게 마차에 올라타 있었다."], ["Nobody cried for leaving HER.", "그녀가 떠난다고 우는 이는 아무도 없었다."]], [["Sambo of the bandy legs slammed the carriage door on his young weeping mistress.", "휘어진 다리의 샘보가 눈물짓는 어린 여주인을 향해 마차 문을 쾅 닫았다."], ["He sprang up behind the carriage.", "그러고는 마차 뒤로 훌쩍 올라탔다."], ["\"Stop!\"", "\"잠깐만요!\""], ["cried Miss Jemima, rushing to the gate with a parcel.", "제미마 양이 꾸러미 하나를 들고 철문으로 달려 나오며 외쳤다."]], [["\"It's some sandwiches, my dear,\"", "\"샌드위치란다, 얘야.\" 그녀가 애밀리아에게 말했다."], ["said she to Amelia.", "\"가다 보면 배가 고플지도 모르니까."], ["\"You may be hungry, you know; and Becky, Becky Sharp, here's a book for you that my sister--that is, I--Johnson's Dixonary, you know; you mustn't leave us without that.", "그리고 베키, 베키 샤프, 여기 네게 줄 책이야. 우리 언니가, 아니 내가, 그러니까 존슨 사전 말이다."], ["Good-by.", "이건 꼭 챙겨 가야지."], ["Drive on, coachman.", "잘 가렴. 어서 모세요, 마부 양반."], ["God bless you!\"", "하느님의 가호가 있기를!\""]], [["And the kind creature retreated into the garden, overcome with emotion.", "그러고는 마음씨 고운 그녀는 북받치는 감정에 못 이겨 정원 안으로 물러갔다."]], [["But, lo!", "그런데, 이게 웬일인가!"], ["and just as the coach drove off, Miss Sharp put her pale face out of the window and actually flung the book back into the garden.", "마차가 막 출발하려는 찰나, 샤프 양이 창밖으로 핼쑥한 얼굴을 내밀더니 정말로 그 책을 정원 안으로 도로 내던지는 것이 아닌가."]], [["This almost caused Jemima to faint with terror.", "이 일로 제미마는 하마터면 공포에 질려 기절할 뻔했다. \"어머나, 세상에.\""], ["\"Well, I never\"--said she--\"what an audacious\"--Emotion prevented her from completing either sentence.", "그녀가 말했다. \"어쩜 저렇게 뻔뻔한——\""], ["The carriage rolled away; the great gates were closed; the bell rang for the dancing lesson.", "북받치는 감정이 두 문장 다 끝맺지 못하게 가로막았다. 마차는 굴러갔고, 커다란 철문은 닫혔으며, 무용 수업을 알리는 종이 울렸다."], ["The world is before the two young ladies; and so, farewell to Chiswick Mall.", "이제 두 젊은 숙녀 앞에는 온 세상이 펼쳐져 있었다. 그러니, 치즈윅 거리여, 안녕히."]], [["# CHAPTER II", "# CHAPTER II"]], [["In Which Miss Sharp and Miss Sedley Prepare to Open the Campaign\nWhen Miss Sharp had performed the heroical act mentioned in the last chapter, and had seen the Dixonary, flying over the pavement of the little garden, fall at length at the feet of the astonished Miss Jemima, the young lady's countenance, which had before worn an almost livid look of hatred, assumed a smile that perhaps was scarcely more agreeable, and she sank back in the carriage in an easy frame of mind, saying--\"So much for the Dixonary; and, thank God, I'm out of Chiswick.\"", "샤프 양이 앞 장에서 언급한 그 영웅적인 행위를 해치우고, 도서 사전이 작은 정원의 포장길 위를 날아가 마침내 어안이 벙벙해진 제미마 양의 발치에 떨어지는 것을 지켜보았을 때, 조금 전까지 거의 시퍼렇게 질린 증오의 빛을 띠고 있던 그 젊은 숙녀의 얼굴은 어쩌면 그보다 별로 더 보기 좋다고 할 것도 없는 미소로 바뀌었고, 그녀는 홀가분한 심정으로 마차 좌석에 등을 기대며 말했다. \"사전은 이걸로 끝, 그리고 다행스럽게도, 나는 치즈윅을 벗어났어.\""]], [["Miss Sedley was almost as flurried at the act of defiance as Miss Jemima had been; for, consider, it was but one minute that she had left school, and the impressions of six years are not got over in that space of time.", "세들리 양은 이 반항적인 행동에 제미마 양 못지않게 당황했다. 생각해 보라, 그녀가 학교를 떠난 지는 겨우 일 분밖에 되지 않았고, 육 년간 쌓인 인상이 그 짧은 시간 안에 사라질 리는 없는 것이다."], ["Nay, with some persons those awes and terrors of youth last for ever and ever.", "아니, 어떤 사람들에게는 젊은 시절의 그 두려움과 공포가 영영 사라지지 않고 남기도 한다."], ["I know, for instance, an old gentleman of sixty-eight, who said to me one morning at breakfast, with a very agitated countenance, \"I dreamed last night that I was flogged by Dr.", "이를테면 나는 예순여덟 살 된 어느 노신사를 아는데, 그는 어느 날 아침 식사 자리에서 몹시 동요된 얼굴로 내게 이렇게 말했다. \"어젯밤 꿈에 레인 박사한테 매를 맞았지 뭔가.\""], ["Raine.\" Fancy had carried him back five-and-fifty years in the course of that evening.", "그날 저녁 동안 환상이 그를 오십오 년 전으로 되돌려 놓았던 것이다."], ["Dr. Raine and his rod were just as awful to him in his heart, then, at sixty-eight, as they had been at thirteen.", "레인 박사와 그의 회초리는 예순여덟이 된 지금도 그의 마음속에서, 열세 살 때와 똑같이 무시무시했던 것이다."], ["If the Doctor, with a large birch, had appeared bodily to him, even at the age of threescore and eight, and had said in awful voice, \"Boy, take down your pant--\"?", "만일 그 박사가 큼직한 자작나무 회초리를 들고, 예순여덟의 나이에 이른 그의 앞에 몸소 나타나, 무시무시한 목소리로 \"이놈, 바지를 내려라—\" 하고 말하기라도 한다면?"], ["Well, well, Miss Sedley was exceedingly alarmed at this act of insubordination.", "그래, 그래, 어쨌든 세들리 양은 이 항명 행위에 몹시 놀란 것이었다."]], [["\"How could you do so, Rebecca?\"", "\"어쩌면 그럴 수가 있어, 레베카?\""], ["at last she said, after a pause.", "한참 뜸을 들인 끝에 마침내 그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"Why, do you think Miss Pinkerton will come out and order me back to the black-hole?\"", "\"왜, 핑커튼 여사가 쫓아 나와서 나더러 어둠의 골방으로 돌아가라고 명령이라도 할 것 같아?\""], ["said Rebecca, laughing.", "레베카가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["\"No: but--\"", "\"아니, 하지만—\""]], [["\"I hate the whole house,\"", "\"난 그 집 전체가 다 싫어.\""], ["continued Miss Sharp in a fury.", "샤프 양이 격분하여 말을 이었다."], ["\"I hope I may never set eyes on it again.", "\"다시는 그 꼴을 안 봤으면 좋겠어."], ["I wish it were in the bottom of the Thames, I do; and if Miss Pinkerton were there, I wouldn't pick her out, that I wouldn't.", "그게 템스강 바닥에 가라앉아 버렸으면 좋겠다, 정말로. 그리고 핑커튼 여사가 거기 빠져 있다면, 난 그 여자를 건져 내지 않을 거야, 정말로 안 건져."], ["O how I should like to see her floating in the water yonder, turban and all, with her train streaming after her, and her nose like the beak of a wherry.\"", "아, 그 여자가 저 물 위에 둥둥 떠 있는 꼴을 보면 얼마나 좋을까, 터번이고 뭐고 다 쓴 채로, 옷자락은 뒤로 길게 끌리고, 코는 거룻배 뱃머리처럼 삐죽 솟아서 말이야.\""]], [["\"Hush!\"", "\"쉿!\""], ["cried Miss Sedley.", "세들리 양이 외쳤다."]], [["\"Why, will the black footman tell tales?\"", "\"왜, 저 흑인 하인이 일러바치기라도 할까 봐?\""], ["cried Miss Rebecca, laughing.", "레베카 양이 웃으며 외쳤다."], ["\"He may go back and tell Miss Pinkerton that I hate her with all my soul; and I wish he would; and I wish I had a means of proving it, too.", "\"돌아가서 핑커튼 여사한테 내가 온 영혼을 다해 그 여자를 미워한다고 일러도 돼. 차라리 그래 줬으면 좋겠어."], ["For two years I have only had insults and outrage from her.", "그리고 그걸 증명할 방법이 있었으면 좋겠다고. 이태 동안 난 그 여자한테서 모욕과 학대만 받았어."], ["I have been treated worse than any servant in the kitchen.", "부엌의 어떤 하인보다도 못한 대접을 받았다고."], ["I have never had a friend or a kind word, except from you.", "너 말고는 친구 한 명, 다정한 말 한마디 들어 본 적이 없어."], ["I have been made to tend the little girls in the lower schoolroom, and to talk French to the Misses, until I grew sick of my mother tongue.", "아래층 교실에서 어린애들 시중을 들어야 했고, 그 아가씨들이랑 프랑스어로 떠들어야 해서 결국 내 모국어가 지긋지긋해질 지경이었어."], ["But that talking French to Miss Pinkerton was capital fun, wasn't it?", "그래도 핑커튼 여사한테 프랑스어로 말한 건 정말 신나는 일이었지, 안 그래?"], ["She doesn't know a word of French, and was too proud to confess it.", "그 여자는 프랑스어라곤 한마디도 모르는데, 그걸 자존심 때문에 인정하지 못했거든."], ["I believe it was that which made her part with me; and so thank Heaven for French.", "그것 때문에 그 여자가 날 떠나보낸 거라고 생각해. 그러니 프랑스어에 감사할 따름이지."], ["Vive la France!", "프랑스 만세!"], ["Vive l'Empereur!", "황제 만세!"], ["Vive Bonaparte!\"", "보나파르트 만세!\""]], [["\"O Rebecca, Rebecca, for shame!\"", "\"아 레베카, 레베카, 부끄러운 줄 알아!\" 세들리 양이 외쳤다."], ["cried Miss Sedley; for this was the greatest blasphemy Rebecca had as yet uttered; and in those days, in England, to say, \"Long live Bonaparte!\"", "이것은 레베카가 그때껏 입에 담은 가장 불경한 말이었기 때문이다. 그 시절 영국에서 \"보나파르트 만세!\"라고 말하는 것은 \"루시퍼 만세!\"라고 말하는 것이나 다름없었던 것이다."], ["was as much as to say, \"Long live Lucifer!\" \"How can you--how dare you have such wicked, revengeful thoughts?\"", "\"어떻게 너는—어떻게 감히 그런 사악하고 앙심에 찬 생각을 할 수가 있어?\""]], [["\"Revenge may be wicked, but it's natural,\"", "\"복수가 사악할지는 몰라도, 그건 자연스러운 거야.\""], ["answered Miss Rebecca.", "레베카 양이 대답했다."], ["\"I'm no angel.\"", "\"난 천사가 아니거든.\""], ["And, to say the truth, she certainly was not.", "그리고, 사실대로 말하자면, 그녀는 분명 천사가 아니었다."]], [["For it may be remarked in the course of this little conversation (which took place as the coach rolled along lazily by the river side) that though Miss Rebecca Sharp has twice had occasion to thank Heaven, it has been, in the first place, for ridding her of some person whom she hated, and secondly, for enabling her to bring her enemies to some sort of perplexity or confusion; neither of which are very amiable motives for religious gratitude, or such as would be put forward by persons of a kind and placable disposition.", "이 짧은 대화가 오가는 동안(마차가 강가를 따라 느릿느릿 굴러가는 사이에 벌어진 일이었다) 한 가지 짚어 둘 만한 점이 있으니, 레베카 샤프 양이 두 번이나 하늘에 감사할 일이 있었는데, 그 첫째는 자신이 미워하던 어떤 사람에게서 벗어나게 해 준 것에 대해서였고, 둘째는 자신의 적들을 어떤 식으로든 곤혹스럽고 낭패한 처지에 몰아넣을 수 있게 해 준 것에 대해서였다는 사실이다. 그 어느 쪽도 종교적 감사를 바칠 만한 그리 갸륵한 동기는 아니며, 다정하고 너그러운 성품을 지닌 사람이라면 내세울 법한 것도 아니었다."], ["Miss Rebecca was not, then, in the least kind or placable.", "그러니 레베카 양은 조금도 다정하거나 너그러운 사람이 아니었던 것이다."], ["All the world used her ill, said this young misanthropist, and we may be pretty certain that persons whom all the world treats ill, deserve entirely the treatment they get.", "온 세상이 자기를 모질게 대한다고 이 젊은 염세가는 말했지만, 온 세상이 모질게 대하는 사람이라면 그가 받는 대접을 받아 마땅하다고 우리는 제법 확신해도 좋을 것이다."], ["The world is a looking-glass, and gives back to every man the reflection of his own face.", "세상은 거울이어서, 누구에게나 제 얼굴의 반영을 되비쳐 주는 법이다."], ["Frown at it, and it will in turn look sourly upon you; laugh at it and with it, and it is a jolly kind companion; and so let all young persons take their choice.", "거울을 향해 찡그리면 거울도 시큰둥한 얼굴로 너를 마주 볼 것이요, 거울을 보고 웃으면 또 거울과 함께 웃으면, 거울은 유쾌하고 다정한 길동무가 되어 주는 것이다. 그러니 모든 젊은이들이여, 알아서들 고를 일이다."], ["This is certain, that if the world neglected Miss Sharp, she never was known to have done a good action in behalf of anybody; nor can it be expected that twenty-four young ladies should all be as amiable as the heroine of this work, Miss Sedley (whom we have selected for the very reason that she was the best-natured of all, otherwise what on earth was to have prevented us from putting up Miss Swartz, or Miss Crump, or Miss Hopkins, as heroine in her place!) it could not be expected that every one should be of the humble and gentle temper of Miss Amelia Sedley; should take every opportunity to vanquish Rebecca's hard-heartedness and ill-humour; and, by a thousand kind words and offices, overcome, for once at least, her hostility to her kind.", "한 가지는 분명하니, 세상이 샤프 양을 푸대접했다 하더라도, 그녀가 그 누구를 위해 착한 일 한 번 했다는 소문은 들린 적이 없다는 사실이다. 또한 스물네 명의 젊은 숙녀가 모조리 이 작품의 여주인공인 세들리 양처럼 사랑스러우리라 기대할 수도 없는 노릇이며(우리가 그녀를 고른 것은 다름 아니라 그녀가 누구보다 천성이 착했기 때문이지, 그렇지 않았다면 도대체 무엇이 우리를 가로막아 스워츠 양이나 크럼프 양이나 홉킨스 양을 그 자리에 여주인공으로 내세우지 못하게 했겠는가!), 모든 사람이 애밀리아 세들리 양처럼 겸손하고 온화한 성정을 지니리라 기대할 수도, 기회가 닿을 때마다 레베카의 모진 마음과 심술을 누그러뜨리려 들고, 천 마디의 다정한 말과 보살핌으로 적어도 한 번만이라도 동족을 향한 그녀의 적의를 이겨 내리라 기대할 수도 없는 것이다."]], [["Miss Sharp's father was an artist, and in that quality had given lessons of drawing at Miss Pinkerton's school.", "샤프 양의 아버지는 화가였고, 그 자격으로 핑커튼 여사의 학원에서 그림 수업을 맡았던 사람이다."], ["He was a clever man; a pleasant companion; a careless student; with a great propensity for running into debt, and a partiality for the tavern.", "그는 영리한 사람이었고, 유쾌한 길동무였으며, 게으른 학도였고, 빚을 지는 데 큰 소질이 있었으며, 술집을 각별히 좋아했다."], ["When he was drunk, he used to beat his wife and daughter; and the next morning, with a headache, he would rail at the world for its neglect of his genius, and abuse, with a good deal of cleverness, and sometimes with perfect reason, the fools, his brother painters.", "술에 취하면 아내와 딸을 때리곤 했고, 이튿날 아침이면 두통에 시달리며 자기 천재성을 알아주지 않는 세상을 욕하고, 어리석은 자들이라며 동료 화가들을 상당히 영리하게, 때로는 더없이 지당한 논리로 비방하곤 했다."], ["As it was with the utmost difficulty that he could keep himself, and as he owed money for a mile round Soho, where he lived, he thought to better his circumstances by marrying a young woman of the French nation, who was by profession an opera-girl.", "그는 제 한 몸 건사하기조차 무척 어려웠던 데다, 자기가 살던 소호 일대 사방 일 마일 안에 빚을 지고 있던 터라, 직업이 오페라 무용수인 프랑스 출신의 젊은 여자와 결혼해서 형편을 좀 펴 볼 궁리를 했다."], ["The humble calling of her female parent Miss Sharp never alluded to, but used to state subsequently that the Entrechats were a noble family of Gascony, and took great pride in her descent from them.", "그 어머니의 보잘것없는 직업에 대해서는 샤프 양은 한 번도 언급한 적이 없었고, 다만 그 후로는 앙트르샤 가문이 가스코뉴의 명문가였다고 말하면서 그 가문의 후손이라는 것을 무척 자랑스러워하곤 했다."], ["And curious it is that as she advanced in life this young lady's ancestors increased in rank and splendour.", "그리고 신기한 일은, 이 젊은 숙녀가 인생을 살아갈수록 그녀의 조상들의 지위와 광휘가 점점 높아졌다는 사실이다."]], [["Rebecca's mother had had some education somewhere, and her daughter spoke French with purity and a Parisian accent.", "레베카의 어머니는 어딘가에서 약간의 교육을 받은 적이 있어서, 그 딸은 순수한 파리식 억양의 프랑스어를 구사했다."], ["It was in those days rather a rare accomplishment, and led to her engagement with the orthodox Miss Pinkerton.", "그 시절에는 이것이 제법 드문 재주였고, 덕분에 그녀는 격식을 따지는 핑커튼 여사에게 채용될 수 있었던 것이다."], ["For her mother being dead, her father, finding himself not likely to recover, after his third attack of delirium tremens, wrote a manly and pathetic letter to Miss Pinkerton, recommending the orphan child to her protection, and so descended to the grave, after two bailiffs had quarrelled over his corpse.", "어머니가 세상을 떠난 데다, 아버지가 세 번째 진전섬망 발작 끝에 자신이 회복할 가망이 없음을 깨닫고, 핑커튼 여사에게 그 고아가 된 자식을 보살펴 달라 부탁하는 사내답고도 애절한 편지를 써 보낸 뒤, 집행관 두 명이 그 시신을 두고 다툰 끝에 무덤으로 내려갔던 것이다."], ["Rebecca was seventeen when she came to Chiswick, and was bound over as an articled pupil; her duties being to talk French, as we have seen; and her privileges to live cost free, and, with a few guineas a year, to gather scraps of knowledge from the professors who attended the school.", "레베카는 치즈윅에 왔을 때 열일곱 살이었고, 계약 장학생 신분으로 묶여 있었다. 앞서 보았다시피 그녀의 임무는 프랑스어로 말하는 것이었고, 그 특전이란 비용 없이 지내면서 일 년에 몇 기니씩 받으며 학원에 출강하는 교수들에게서 토막 지식을 주워 모으는 것이었다."]], [["She was small and slight in person; pale, sandy-haired, and with eyes habitually cast down: when they looked up they were very large, odd, and attractive; so attractive that the Reverend Mr.", "그녀는 몸집이 작고 가냘팠으며, 안색은 창백하고 머리카락은 모래빛이었고, 두 눈은 늘 내리깔고 있었다."], ["Crisp, fresh from Oxford, and curate to the Vicar of Chiswick, the Reverend Mr.", "그러나 그 눈을 들어 올리면 무척 크고 묘하고 매혹적이었다."], ["Flowerdew, fell in love with Miss Sharp; being shot dead by a glance of her eyes which was fired all the way across Chiswick Church from the school-pew to the reading-desk.", "어찌나 매혹적이었던지 옥스퍼드를 갓 나와 치즈윅 교구목사 플라워듀 목사 밑에서 부목사로 있던 크리스프 목사가 샤프 양에게 반해 버렸을 정도다. 학교 전용석에서 성서대까지 치즈윅 교회를 가로질러 쏘아 보낸 그녀의 눈길 한 방에 그는 그 자리에서 거꾸러진 것이었다."], ["This infatuated young man used sometimes to take tea with Miss Pinkerton, to whom he had been presented by his mamma, and actually proposed something like marriage in an intercepted note, which the one-eyed apple-woman was charged to deliver. Mrs.", "이 넋이 나간 청년은 이따금 핑커튼 여사와 차를 마시곤 했는데(그의 어머니가 그를 핑커튼 여사에게 소개해 준 사이였다), 급기야 외눈박이 사과 장수에게 전해 달라고 맡긴 편지에서 결혼 비슷한 것을 제안하기에 이르렀는데, 그 편지가 도중에 가로채진 것이다."], ["Crisp was summoned from Buxton, and abruptly carried off her darling boy; but the idea, even, of such an eagle in the Chiswick dovecot caused a great flutter in the breast of Miss Pinkerton, who would have sent away Miss Sharp but that she was bound to her under a forfeit, and who never could thoroughly believe the young lady's protestations that she had never exchanged a single word with Mr.", "크리스프 부인이 벅스턴에서 급히 불려 와 사랑하는 아들을 황급히 데려가 버렸다. 그러나 치즈윅이라는 비둘기장에 그런 독수리 한 마리가 들어왔다는 생각만으로도 핑커튼 여사의 가슴은 크게 술렁였고, 그녀는 위약금 때문에 샤프 양을 묶어 두고 있지만 않았더라면 당장 내쫓았을 것이다."], ["Crisp, except under her own eyes on the two occasions when she had met him at tea.", "그리고 차를 마시며 두 차례 그를 만났을 때 자기가 보는 앞에서가 아니고는 크리스프 씨와 단 한마디도 주고받은 적이 없다는 그 젊은 숙녀의 항변을, 핑커튼 여사는 끝내 온전히 믿지 못했던 것이다."]], [["By the side of many tall and bouncing young ladies in the establishment, Rebecca Sharp looked like a child.", "그 학원의 키 크고 발랄한 젊은 숙녀들 곁에 서면 레베카 샤프는 어린아이처럼 보였다."], ["But she had the dismal precocity of poverty.", "그러나 그녀에게는 가난이 길러 낸 음울한 조숙함이 있었다."], ["Many a dun had she talked to, and turned away from her father's door; many a tradesman had she coaxed and wheedled into good-humour, and into the granting of one meal more.", "그녀는 빚쟁이를 숱하게 상대하여 아버지의 집 문전에서 돌려보냈고, 장사치들을 어르고 구슬려 기분 좋게 만들어 한 끼 식사를 더 외상으로 얻어 내기도 한두 번이 아니었다."], ["She sate commonly with her father, who was very proud of her wit, and heard the talk of many of his wild companions--often but ill-suited for a girl to hear.", "그녀는 흔히 아버지 곁에 앉아 있었는데, 아버지는 딸의 재치를 무척 자랑스러워했고, 그녀는 그 막돼먹은 동료들이 나누는 이야기를—소녀가 듣기에는 어울리지 않을 때가 많은 이야기를—들었다."], ["But she never had been a girl, she said; she had been a woman since she was eight years old.", "그러나 자기는 한 번도 소녀였던 적이 없었다고, 여덟 살 때부터 이미 여자였다고 그녀는 말했다."], ["Oh, why did Miss Pinkerton let such a dangerous bird into her cage?", "아, 어찌하여 핑커튼 여사는 그토록 위험한 새를 자기 새장 안에 들였단 말인가?"]], [["The fact is, the old lady believed Rebecca to be the meekest creature in the world, so admirably, on the occasions when her father brought her to Chiswick, used Rebecca to perform the part of the ingenue; and only a year before the arrangement by which Rebecca had been admitted into her house, and when Rebecca was sixteen years old, Miss Pinkerton majestically, and with a little speech, made her a present of a doll--which was, by the way, the confiscated property of Miss Swindle, discovered surreptitiously nursing it in school-hours.", "사실인즉, 이 노부인은 레베카가 세상에서 가장 온순한 사람이라고 믿었던 것이다. 아버지가 그녀를 치즈윅에 데려왔을 때 레베카가 어찌나 감쪽같이 순진한 처녀 역할을 해냈던지 말이다."], ["How the father and daughter laughed as they trudged home together after the evening party (it was on the occasion of the speeches, when all the professors were invited) and how Miss Pinkerton would have raged had she seen the caricature of herself which the little mimic, Rebecca, managed to make out of her doll.", "레베카가 그 집에 들어오게 한 약조가 맺어지기 겨우 일 년 전, 레베카가 열여섯 살이었을 때, 핑커튼 여사는 위풍당당하게 짤막한 연설까지 곁들이며 그녀에게 인형 하나를 선물한 적이 있었다. 그런데 그 인형은 사실 스윈들 양이 수업 시간에 몰래 어르고 있는 것이 발각되어 압수당한 물건이었다."], ["Becky used to go through dialogues with it; it formed the delight of Newman Street, Gerrard Street, and the Artists'", "그날 저녁 모임이 끝난 뒤(교수들이 죄다 초대받았던 연설 행사 때의 일이었다) 그 부녀가 함께 터덜터덜 집으로 걸어가며 얼마나 웃어 댔던가."], ["quarter: and the young painters, when they came to take their gin-and-water with their lazy, dissolute, clever, jovial senior, used regularly to ask Rebecca if Miss Pinkerton was at home: she was as well known to them, poor soul!", "그리고 그 작은 흉내쟁이 레베카가 그 인형으로 만들어 낸 자신의 풍자 인형을 핑커튼 여사가 보았더라면 얼마나 펄펄 뛰었을 것인가. 베키는 그 인형을 데리고 대화 장면을 연기하곤 했다."], ["as Mr.", "그것은 뉴먼 거리, 제라드 거리, 그리고 화가촌의 즐길 거리였다."], ["Lawrence or President West.", "젊은 화가들이 그 게으르고 방탕하고 영리하고 유쾌한 선배 화가와 진토닉을 마시러 올 때면 으레 레베카에게 핑커튼 여사가 댁에 계시냐고 물어보곤 했다. 가엾게도, 그 여사는 화가들에게 로런스 씨나 웨스트 원장만큼이나 잘 알려진 사람이 되어 있었던 것이다!"], ["Once Rebecca had the honour to pass a few days at Chiswick; after which she brought back Jemima, and erected another doll as Miss Jemmy: for though that honest creature had made and given her jelly and cake enough for three children, and a seven-shilling piece at parting, the girl's sense of ridicule was far stronger than her gratitude, and she sacrificed Miss Jemmy quite as pitilessly as her sister.", "한번은 레베카가 치즈윅에서 며칠을 보내는 영광을 누렸는데, 그 후 그녀는 제미마를 데려와 또 다른 인형을 제미 양이라며 세워 놓았다. 그 정직한 여인이 그녀에게 아이 셋이 먹어도 남을 만큼 젤리와 케이크를 만들어 주었고 헤어질 때 칠 실링짜리 동전까지 쥐여 주었건만, 이 소녀의 비웃는 감각은 고마워하는 마음보다 훨씬 더 강했던지라, 그녀는 그 언니 못지않게 가차 없이 제미 양을 제물로 삼았던 것이다."]], [["The catastrophe came, and she was brought to the Mall as to her home.", "급기야 파국이 닥쳤고, 그녀는 자기 집으로 가듯이 그 거리로 보내졌다. 그곳의 엄격한 격식은 그녀를 숨 막히게 했다."], ["The rigid formality of the place suffocated her: the prayers and the meals, the lessons and the walks, which were arranged with a conventual regularity, oppressed her almost beyond endurance; and she looked back to the freedom and the beggary of the old studio in Soho with so much regret, that everybody, herself included, fancied she was consumed with grief for her father.", "수도원 같은 규칙성으로 짜인 기도며 식사며 수업이며 산책이 그녀를 거의 견딜 수 없을 만큼 짓눌렀다. 그래서 그녀는 소호의 그 낡은 작업실에서 누리던 자유와 가난을 어찌나 애틋하게 돌아보았던지, 그녀 자신을 포함해 모두가 그녀가 아버지를 잃은 슬픔에 사무쳐 있다고 여길 정도였다."], ["She had a little room in the garret, where the maids heard her walking and sobbing at night; but it was with rage, and not with grief.", "그녀는 다락방의 작은 방을 썼는데, 하녀들은 밤이면 그녀가 서성이며 흐느끼는 소리를 들었다. 그러나 그것은 슬픔이 아니라 분노에서 나온 것이었다."], ["She had not been much of a dissembler, until now her loneliness taught her to feign.", "그녀는 그때까지는 그리 능숙한 위선자가 아니었으나, 이제는 외로움이 그녀에게 가식을 가르쳤던 것이다. 그녀는 여자들의 사교에 끼어 본 적이 없었다."], ["She had never mingled in the society of women: her father, reprobate as he was, was a man of talent; his conversation was a thousand times more agreeable to her than the talk of such of her own sex as she now encountered.", "그녀의 아버지는 비록 막돼먹은 인간이었으나 재능 있는 사람이었고, 그의 대화는 이제 그녀가 마주하게 된 같은 성의 여자들이 나누는 이야기보다 천 배는 더 흥미로웠다. 거드름 피우는 늙은 여교장의 허영, 그 동생의 어리석은 사람 좋음, 나이 많은 여학생들의 시시한 잡담과 험담, 가정교사들의 싸늘한 모범 의식이 한결같이 그녀의 신경을 거슬렀다."], ["The pompous vanity of the old schoolmistress, the foolish good-humour of her sister, the silly chat and scandal of the elder girls, and the frigid correctness of the governesses equally annoyed her; and she had no soft maternal heart, this unlucky girl, otherwise the prattle and talk of the younger children, with whose care she was chiefly intrusted, might have soothed and interested her; but she lived among them two years, and not one was sorry that she went away.", "그리고 이 불운한 소녀에게는 부드러운 모성애라곤 없었으니, 그렇지만 않았더라면 주로 그녀가 돌보도록 맡겨진 어린아이들의 재잘거림과 수다가 그녀를 달래 주고 즐겁게 해 주었을지도 모른다. 그러나 그녀는 이 년이나 그들 틈에서 살았건만, 그녀가 떠난다고 아쉬워하는 아이는 하나도 없었다."], ["The gentle tender-hearted Amelia Sedley was the only person to whom she could attach herself in the least; and who could help attaching herself to Amelia?", "온화하고 마음씨 다정한 애밀리아 세들리만이 그녀가 조금이나마 정을 붙일 수 있는 유일한 사람이었다. 하기야 애밀리아에게 정을 붙이지 않을 사람이 어디 있겠는가?"]], [["The happiness--the superior advantages of the young women round about her, gave Rebecca inexpressible pangs of envy.", "주변 젊은 여자들의 행복이—그 우월한 처지가—레베카에게 이루 말할 수 없는 질투의 고통을 안겼다."], ["\"What airs that girl gives herself, because she is an Earl's grand-daughter,\"", "\"저 계집애가 백작의 손녀라고 어찌나 거드름을 피우는지.\" 그녀는 한 아이를 두고 그렇게 말했다."], ["she said of one. \"How they cringe and bow to that Creole, because of her hundred thousand pounds!", "\"저 크리올 계집애 앞에서 십만 파운드 때문에 다들 어찌나 굽실거리며 알랑대는지!"], ["I am a thousand times cleverer and more charming than that creature, for all her wealth.", "난 저따위 것보다 천 배는 더 영리하고 매력적이야, 그 재산이 다 무슨 소용이람."], ["I am as well bred as the Earl's grand-daughter, for all her fine pedigree; and yet every one passes me by here.", "난 저 백작의 손녀 못지않게 잘 자랐어, 그 잘난 혈통이 다 무슨 소용이람. 그런데도 여기서는 다들 날 거들떠보지도 않아."], ["And yet, when I was at my father's, did not the men give up their gayest balls and parties in order to pass the evening with me?\"", "그렇지만 내가 아버지 집에 있을 때는, 남자들이 제일 화려한 무도회며 파티를 마다하고 나하고 저녁을 보내려 하지 않았던가?\""], ["She determined at any rate to get free from the prison in which she found herself, and now began to act for herself, and for the first time to make connected plans for the future.", "그녀는 어찌 됐든 자기가 갇혀 있는 이 감옥에서 벗어나기로 작정하고, 이제 스스로를 위해 행동하기 시작했으며, 난생처음으로 앞날을 위한 짜임새 있는 계획을 세우기 시작했다."]], [["She took advantage, therefore, of the means of study the place offered her; and as she was already a musician and a good linguist, she speedily went through the little course of study which was considered necessary for ladies in those days.", "그리하여 그녀는 그곳이 제공하는 학습의 기회를 이용했다. 그녀는 이미 음악가이자 뛰어난 어학자였던지라, 그 시절 숙녀들에게 필요하다고 여겨지던 그 자그마한 학습 과정을 금세 마쳐 버렸다."], ["Her music she practised incessantly, and one day, when the girls were out, and she had remained at home, she was overheard to play a piece so well that Minerva thought, wisely, she could spare herself the expense of a master for the juniors, and intimated to Miss Sharp that she was to instruct them in music for the future.", "음악은 쉴 새 없이 연습했는데, 어느 날 여자아이들이 외출하고 그녀 혼자 집에 남아 있던 차에 한 곡을 어찌나 잘 연주하던지 그 소리를 엿들은 미네르바가 현명하게도 어린 학생들을 위한 교사 비용을 아낄 수 있겠다 싶어, 앞으로 그 아이들에게 음악을 가르치라고 샤프 양에게 일렀다."]], [["The girl refused; and for the first time, and to the astonishment of the majestic mistress of the school.", "소녀는 거절했다. 난생처음으로, 그것도 그 위풍당당한 여교장을 깜짝 놀라게 하면서 말이다."], ["\"I am here to speak French with the children,\"", "\"저는 여기 아이들과 프랑스어로 말하러 온 거지,\""], ["Rebecca said abruptly, \"not to teach them music, and save money for you.", "레베카가 퉁명스레 말했다. \"음악을 가르쳐서 당신 돈을 아껴 주러 온 게 아니에요."], ["Give me money, and I will teach them.\"", "돈을 주시면 가르치죠.\""]], [["Minerva was obliged to yield, and, of course, disliked her from that day.", "미네르바는 양보할 수밖에 없었고, 물론 그날부터 그녀를 미워했다."], ["\"For five-and-thirty years,\"", "\"삼십오 년 동안,\""], ["she said, and with great justice, \"I never have seen the individual who has dared in my own house to question my authority.", "그녀는 말했는데, 참으로 지당한 말이었다. \"내 집에서 감히 내 권위에 의문을 제기한 인간은 본 적이 없어."], ["I have nourished a viper in my bosom.\"", "내가 품 안에 독사를 길렀구나.\""]], [["\"A viper--a fiddlestick,\"", "\"독사라니—말도 안 되는 소리.\""], ["said Miss Sharp to the old lady, almost fainting with astonishment.", "샤프 양이 놀라 거의 까무러칠 지경인 노부인에게 말했다."], ["\"You took me because I was useful.", "\"당신은 내가 쓸모 있어서 받아들인 거예요."], ["There is no question of gratitude between us.", "우리 사이에 감사 따위는 없어요."], ["I hate this place, and want to leave it.", "난 이곳이 싫고, 떠나고 싶어요."], ["I will do nothing here but what I am obliged to do.\"", "여기서는 꼭 해야 하는 일 말고는 아무것도 안 할 거예요.\""]], [["It was in vain that the old lady asked her if she was aware she was speaking to Miss Pinkerton?", "지금 핑커튼 여사에게 말하고 있다는 걸 아느냐고 노부인이 물어봐도 소용없었다. 레베카는 그 얼굴에 대고, 여교장이 거의 발작을 일으킬 만큼 끔찍하고 빈정대는 악마 같은 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["Rebecca laughed in her face, with a horrid sarcastic demoniacal laughter, that almost sent the schoolmistress into fits.", "\"목돈을 주고,\" 소녀가 말했다."], ["\"Give me a sum of money,\"", "\"저를 떼어 내세요. 아니면, 그게 더 좋으시다면, 귀족 가문에 가정교사 좋은 자리를 구해 주시든가요."], ["said the girl, \"and get rid of me--or, if you like better, get me a good place as governess in a nobleman's family--you can do so if you please.\"", "마음만 먹으면 하실 수 있잖아요.\" 그리고 그 후의 입씨름에서도 그녀는 늘 이 점으로 돌아왔다."], ["And in their further disputes she always returned to this point, \"Get me a situation--we hate each other, and I am ready to go.\"", "\"자리를 구해 줘요. 우린 서로 미워하고, 난 떠날 준비가 됐어요.\""]], [["Worthy Miss Pinkerton, although she had a Roman nose and a turban, and was as tall as a grenadier, and had been up to this time an irresistible princess, had no will or strength like that of her little apprentice, and in vain did battle against her, and tried to overawe her.", "훌륭한 핑커튼 여사는 비록 로마식 코에 터번을 둘렀고 척탄병만큼 키가 컸으며 그때까지는 거역할 수 없는 공주님이었으나, 그 자그마한 견습생만 한 의지나 기력이 없었으니, 맞서 싸워도, 위세로 윽박지르려 해도 헛수고였다."], ["Attempting once to scold her in public, Rebecca hit upon the before-mentioned plan of answering her in French, which quite routed the old woman.", "한번은 사람들 앞에서 그녀를 꾸짖으려 했다가, 레베카가 앞서 말한 그 수법대로 프랑스어로 대꾸하는 바람에 노부인은 완전히 무너지고 말았다."], ["In order to maintain authority in her school, it became necessary to remove this rebel, this monster, this serpent, this firebrand; and hearing about this time that Sir Pitt Crawley's family was in want of a governess, she actually recommended Miss Sharp for the situation, firebrand and serpent as she was.", "자기 학원의 권위를 지키려면 이 반역자, 이 괴물, 이 뱀, 이 화근을 치워 버리는 수밖에 없었다. 그러던 차에 그 무렵 핏 크롤리 경의 집안에서 가정교사를 구한다는 소식을 듣자, 그녀는 화근이자 뱀인 샤프 양을 그 자리에 정말로 추천했던 것이다."], ["\"I cannot, certainly,\"", "\"분명히,\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, \"find fault with Miss Sharp's conduct, except to myself; and must allow that her talents and accomplishments are of a high order.", "\"나에게 한 짓 말고는 샤프 양의 처신에서 흠잡을 데를 찾을 수가 없고, 그 재능과 교양이 높은 수준임은 인정해야겠지."], ["As far as the head goes, at least, she does credit to the educational system pursued at my establishment.\"", "적어도 머리로 따지자면, 그 애는 우리 학원에서 추구하는 교육 방식에 영예를 안겨 주는 인물이야.\""]], [["And so the schoolmistress reconciled the recommendation to her conscience, and the indentures were cancelled, and the apprentice was free.", "그리하여 여교장은 그 추천을 자기 양심과 화해시켰고, 계약서는 파기되었으며, 견습생은 자유로워졌다."], ["The battle here described in a few lines, of course, lasted for some months.", "여기 몇 줄로 적어 놓은 이 싸움은 물론 몇 달에 걸쳐 이어진 것이었다."], ["And as Miss Sedley, being now in her seventeenth year, was about to leave school, and had a friendship for Miss Sharp (\"'tis the only point in Amelia's behaviour,\"", "그리고 세들리 양이 이제 열일곱 살이 되어 학교를 떠나려던 참이었고, 또 샤프 양과 우정을 나누고 있던 터라(\"그 점이,\" 미네르바는 말했다."], ["said Minerva, \"which has not been satisfactory to her mistress\"), Miss Sharp was invited by her friend to pass a week with her at home, before she entered upon her duties as governess in a private family.", "\"애밀리아의 처신 가운데 그 스승의 마음에 차지 않는 유일한 대목이야\"), 샤프 양은 어느 개인 가정에 가정교사로 들어가는 임무를 시작하기 전에 친구의 초대를 받아 그 집에서 일주일을 보내게 되었다."]], [["Thus the world began for these two young ladies.", "이렇게 하여 이 두 젊은 숙녀에게 세상이 열리기 시작했다."], ["For Amelia it was quite a new, fresh, brilliant world, with all the bloom upon it.", "애밀리아에게 그것은 온통 꽃이 만발한, 더없이 새롭고 싱그럽고 찬란한 세상이었다."], ["It was not quite a new one for Rebecca--(indeed, if the truth must be told with respect to the Crisp affair, the tart-woman hinted to somebody, who took an affidavit of the fact to somebody else, that there was a great deal more than was made public regarding Mr. Crisp and Miss Sharp, and that his letter was in answer to another letter).", "레베카에게는 그다지 새로운 세상이 아니었으나—(사실 크리스프 사건에 관해 굳이 진실을 밝히자면, 그 파이 장수 여인이 누군가에게 귀띔하기를, 또 그 누군가가 그 사실을 다른 누군가에게 진술서까지 만들어 전하기를, 크리스프 씨와 샤프 양에 관해 세상에 알려진 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 일이 있었으며, 그의 편지는 또 다른 편지에 대한 답장이었다고 했다)."], ["But who can tell you the real truth of the matter?", "하지만 이 일의 진짜 진실을 누가 말해 줄 수 있겠는가?"], ["At all events, if Rebecca was not beginning the world, she was beginning it over again.", "어쨌든 레베카가 세상을 처음 시작하는 것은 아니었다 해도, 다시 시작하고 있는 것만은 분명했다."]], [["By the time the young ladies reached Kensington turnpike, Amelia had not forgotten her companions, but had dried her tears, and had blushed very much and been delighted at a young officer of the Life Guards, who spied her as he was riding by, and said, \"A dem fine gal, egad!\"", "두 젊은 숙녀가 켄싱턴 통행료 징수소에 이르렀을 무렵, 애밀리아는 동무들을 잊지는 않았으나 눈물은 이미 거두었고, 마차를 타고 지나가다 그녀를 흘끗 본 어느 젊은 근위 기병 장교가 \"이런, 정말 멋진 아가씨로군!\" 하고 말하는 통에 얼굴을 잔뜩 붉히면서도 기뻐하고 있었다."], ["and before the carriage arrived in Russell Square, a great deal of conversation had taken place about the Drawing-room, and whether or not young ladies wore powder as well as hoops when presented, and whether she was to have that honour: to the Lord Mayor's ball she knew she was to go.", "그리고 마차가 러셀 광장에 닿기도 전에, 알현실에 관해, 또 알현할 때 젊은 숙녀들이 후프 치마와 함께 머리에 분도 뿌리는지, 자기에게도 그런 영광이 주어질지에 관해 한참 이야기가 오갔다. 시장 주최 무도회에 가게 되리라는 것만은 그녀도 알고 있었다."], ["And when at length home was reached, Miss Amelia Sedley skipped out on Sambo's arm, as happy and as handsome a girl as any in the whole big city of London.", "그리고 마침내 집에 다다르자, 애밀리아 세들리 양은 샘보의 팔에 의지해 폴짝 뛰어내렸으니, 런던이라는 큰 도시 안의 그 어느 아가씨 못지않게 행복하고 어여쁜 아가씨였다."], ["Both he and coachman agreed on this point, and so did her father and mother, and so did every one of the servants in the house, as they stood bobbing, and curtseying, and smiling, in the hall to welcome their young mistress.", "샘보도 마부도 이 점에는 의견이 일치했고, 그녀의 부모도, 또 어린 여주인을 맞으러 현관에 늘어서서 고개를 까딱이고 무릎을 굽혀 인사하며 웃음 짓던 집안 하인들 한 명 한 명도 다 그렇게 여겼다."]], [["You may be sure that she showed Rebecca over every room of the house, and everything in every one of her drawers; and her books, and her piano, and her dresses, and all her necklaces, brooches, laces, and gimcracks.", "그녀가 레베카에게 집안의 방이란 방을, 또 자기 서랍이란 서랍 속에 든 것이란 것은 죄다 구경시켜 주었으리라는 것은 짐작하고도 남으리라. 책이며 피아노며 드레스며, 그 온갖 목걸이며 브로치며 레이스며 자질구레한 장신구까지 말이다."], ["She insisted upon Rebecca accepting the white cornelian and the turquoise rings, and a sweet sprigged muslin, which was too small for her now, though it would fit her friend to a nicety; and she determined in her heart to ask her mother's permission to present her white Cashmere shawl to her friend.", "그녀는 레베카에게 흰 홍옥수 반지와 터키석 반지를, 또 이제는 자기에게는 작지만 친구에게는 꼭 맞을 어여쁜 잔무늬 모슬린 드레스를 한사코 받게 했다. 그리고 마음속으로는 어머니의 허락을 받아 자기 흰 캐시미어 숄을 친구에게 선물하기로 작정했다."], ["Could she not spare it?", "그것 하나쯤 못 내줄 것도 없지 않은가?"], ["and had not her brother Joseph just brought her two from India?", "게다가 오라버니 조지프가 인도에서 그녀에게 막 두 장이나 가져다주지 않았던가?"]], [["When Rebecca saw the two magnificent Cashmere shawls which Joseph Sedley had brought home to his sister, she said, with perfect truth, \"that it must be delightful to have a brother,\" and easily got the pity of the tender-hearted Amelia for being alone in the world, an orphan without friends or kindred.", "조지프 세들리가 누이에게 가져다준 그 두 장의 으리으리한 캐시미어 숄을 보았을 때, 레베카는 더없이 진심으로 \"오라버니가 있다는 건 정말 좋은 일이겠다\"고 말하고는, 친구도 친지도 없이 세상에 홀로 남은 고아라는 점을 내세워 마음씨 다정한 애밀리아의 동정을 쉽사리 얻어 냈다."]], [["\"Not alone,\"", "\"혼자가 아니야.\" 애밀리아가 말했다."], ["said Amelia; \"you know, Rebecca, I shall always be your friend, and love you as a sister--indeed I will.\"", "\"있잖아, 레베카, 나는 언제까지나 네 친구가 되어 줄 거고, 자매처럼 너를 사랑할 거야. 정말 그럴 거야.\""]], [["\"Ah, but to have parents, as you have--kind, rich, affectionate parents, who give you everything you ask for; and their love, which is more precious than all!", "\"아, 하지만 너처럼 부모님이 계시다면—다정하고, 부유하고, 자애로운 부모님이, 네가 달라는 건 뭐든 다 주시고, 게다가 그 무엇보다도 소중한 사랑까지 주시잖아!"], ["My poor papa could give me nothing, and I had but two frocks in all the world!", "가엾은 우리 아빠는 내게 아무것도 못 주셨고, 난 세상에 옷이라곤 두 벌뿐이었어!"], ["And then, to have a brother, a dear brother!", "게다가 오라버니가, 사랑하는 오라버니가 있다니!"], ["Oh, how you must love him!\"", "아, 넌 그분을 얼마나 사랑하겠니!\""]], [["Amelia laughed.", "애밀리아가 웃었다."]], [["\"What!", "\"아니!"], ["don't you love him?", "넌 오라버니를 사랑하지 않아?"], ["you, who say you love everybody?\"", "누구든 다 사랑한다는 네가?\""]], [["\"Yes, of course, I do--only--\"", "\"응, 당연히 사랑하지—다만—\""]], [["\"Only what?\"", "\"다만 뭐?\""]], [["\"Only Joseph doesn't seem to care much whether I love him or not.", "\"다만 조지프 오라버니는 내가 자기를 사랑하든 말든 그다지 신경 쓰지 않는 것 같아. 십 년이나 떨어져 있다가 돌아와서는 악수하라고 손가락 두 개만 내미는걸!"], ["He gave me two fingers to shake when he arrived after ten years' absence!", "무척 친절하고 좋은 분이지만, 나한테는 거의 말을 안 걸어."], ["He is very kind and good, but he scarcely ever speaks to me; I think he loves his pipe a great deal better than his\"--but here Amelia checked herself, for why should she speak ill of her brother?", "내 생각엔 자기—보다 담뱃대를 훨씬 더 사랑하는 것 같아.\" 그러나 여기서 애밀리아는 입을 다물었다."], ["\"He was very kind to me as a child,\"", "무엇 하러 오라버니를 험담하겠는가? \"어렸을 때는 나한테 무척 잘해 주셨어.\""], ["she added; \"I was but five years old when he went away.\"", "그녀가 덧붙였다. \"그분이 떠나실 때 나는 겨우 다섯 살이었거든.\""]], [["\"Isn't he very rich?\"", "\"오라버니가 무척 부자 아니야?\""], ["said Rebecca.", "레베카가 말했다."], ["\"They say all Indian nabobs are enormously rich.\"", "\"인도의 나리들은 다들 엄청난 부자라던데.\""]], [["\"I believe he has a very large income.\"", "\"수입이 아주 많은 것 같긴 해.\""]], [["\"And is your sister-in-law a nice pretty woman?\"", "\"그럼 올케언니는 착하고 예쁜 분이야?\""]], [["\"La!", "\"어머!"], ["Joseph is not married,\"", "조지프 오라버니는 결혼 안 했어.\""], ["said Amelia, laughing again.", "애밀리아가 다시 웃으며 말했다."]], [["Perhaps she had mentioned the fact already to Rebecca, but that young lady did not appear to have remembered it; indeed, vowed and protested that she expected to see a number of Amelia's nephews and nieces.", "어쩌면 그녀는 이미 그 사실을 레베카에게 말했는지도 모르나, 그 젊은 숙녀는 그걸 기억하는 기색이 전혀 없었다. 아니, 애밀리아의 조카들을 잔뜩 보게 되리라 기대하고 있었노라고 맹세하고 우겨 댔다."], ["She was quite disappointed that Mr.", "세들리 씨가 결혼하지 않았다니 무척 실망스럽다는 것이었다."], ["Sedley was not married; she was sure Amelia had said he was, and she doted so on little children.", "분명 애밀리아가 그가 결혼했다고 말한 줄 알았다면서, 자기는 어린아이들을 그렇게도 좋아한다는 것이었다."]], [["\"I think you must have had enough of them at Chiswick,\"", "\"치즈윅에서 애들이라면 실컷 겪지 않았어?\""], ["said Amelia, rather wondering at the sudden tenderness on her friend's part; and indeed in later days Miss Sharp would never have committed herself so far as to advance opinions, the untruth of which would have been so easily detected.", "애밀리아가 친구의 그 느닷없는 다정함에 다소 의아해하며 말했다. 사실 훗날의 샤프 양이었다면 그 거짓이 그토록 쉽게 들통날 의견을 함부로 내세우는 따위의 실수는 결코 저지르지 않았을 것이다."], ["But we must remember that she is but nineteen as yet, unused to the art of deceiving, poor innocent creature! and making her own experience in her own person.", "하지만 그녀가 아직 열아홉 살에 지나지 않으며, 속이는 기술에 서투른, 가엾고 순진한 사람이며, 제 몸으로 직접 경험을 쌓아 가는 중임을 우리는 기억해야 한다!"], ["The meaning of the above series of queries, as translated in the heart of this ingenious young woman, was simply this: \"If Mr.", "앞서 늘어놓은 일련의 물음이 이 영리한 젊은 여인의 마음속에서 번역된 뜻은 그저 이런 것이었다."], ["Joseph Sedley is rich and unmarried, why should I not marry him?", "\"조지프 세들리 씨가 부자에 미혼이라면, 내가 그와 결혼하지 못할 이유가 어디 있담?"], ["I have only a fortnight, to be sure, but there is no harm in trying.\"", "시간이 고작 이 주뿐이긴 하지만, 한번 시도해 본다고 해될 것도 없지.\""], ["And she determined within herself to make this laudable attempt.", "그리고 그녀는 마음속으로 이 갸륵한 시도를 해 보기로 작정했다."], ["She redoubled her caresses to Amelia; she kissed the white cornelian necklace as she put it on; and vowed she would never, never part with it.", "그녀는 애밀리아를 더욱 살갑게 어루만지고, 흰 홍옥수 목걸이를 목에 걸면서 거기에 입을 맞추었으며, 결코, 결코 이것을 손에서 놓지 않겠노라고 맹세했다."], ["When the dinner-bell rang she went downstairs with her arm round her friend's waist, as is the habit of young ladies.", "저녁 식사를 알리는 종이 울리자 그녀는 젊은 숙녀들의 버릇대로 친구의 허리에 팔을 두르고 아래층으로 내려갔다."], ["She was so agitated at the drawing-room door, that she could hardly find courage to enter.", "응접실 문 앞에서 어찌나 떨리던지 들어갈 용기를 좀처럼 낼 수 없었다."], ["\"Feel my heart, how it beats, dear!\"", "\"얘, 내 심장 좀 만져 봐, 어떻게 뛰는지!\""], ["said she to her friend.", "그녀가 친구에게 말했다."]], [["\"No, it doesn't,\"", "\"아니, 안 뛰는데.\""], ["said Amelia.", "애밀리아가 말했다."], ["\"Come in, don't be frightened.", "\"들어가, 무서워하지 말고."], ["Papa won't do you any harm.\"", "우리 아빠는 너를 어떻게 하지 않으셔.\""]], [["Rebecca Is in Presence of the Enemy", "리베카, 적과 마주하다"]], [["A VERY stout, puffy man, in buckskins and Hessian boots, with several immense neckcloths that rose almost to his nose, with a red striped waistcoat and an apple green coat with steel buttons almost as large as crown pieces (it was the morning costume of a dandy or blood of those days) was reading the paper by the fire when the two girls entered, and bounced off his arm-chair, and blushed excessively, and hid his entire face almost in his neckcloths at this apparition.", "두 소녀가 들어섰을 때, 몹시 살집 좋고 부석부석한 한 남자가 난롯가에서 신문을 읽고 있었다. 사슴 가죽 바지에 헤시안 부츠를 신고, 거의 코까지 치솟은 여러 겹의 큼직한 목도리를 두르고, 붉은 줄무늬 조끼에 왕관 동전만큼이나 큼지막한 강철 단추가 달린 사과 빛 초록색 외투를 걸치고 있었으니(이는 그 시절 멋쟁이나 한량의 아침 차림새였다), 그는 이 출현에 화들짝 안락의자에서 튀어 일어나 얼굴이 새빨개지더니 거의 온 얼굴을 목도리 속에 파묻어 버렸다."]], [["\"It's only your sister, Joseph,\"", "\"오라버니 동생이에요, 조지프.\""], ["said Amelia, laughing and shaking the two fingers which he held out.", "애밀리아가 웃으며, 그가 내민 두 손가락을 흔들며 말했다."], ["\"I've come home FOR GOOD, you know; and this is my friend, Miss Sharp, whom you have heard me mention.\"", "\"이제 아주 돌아왔답니다. 그리고 이쪽은 제 친구 샤프 양인데, 제가 말씀드린 적 있죠.\""]], [["\"No, never, upon my word,\"", "\"아니, 들은 적 없는데, 정말이야.\" 목도리 속의 머리통이 몹시 떨면서 말했다."], ["said the head under the neckcloth, shaking very much--\"that is, yes--what abominably cold weather, Miss\"--and herewith he fell to poking the fire with all his might, although it was in the middle of June.", "\"그러니까, 그래, 날씨가 어찌나 지긋지긋하게 추운지요, 그—\" 그러더니 그는 유월 한복판인데도 온 힘을 다해 부지깽이로 불을 쑤셔 대기 시작했다."]], [["\"He's very handsome,\"", "\"정말 미남이시구나.\""], ["whispered Rebecca to Amelia, rather loud.", "리베카가 애밀리아에게 제법 큰 소리로 속삭였다."]], [["\"Do you think so?\"", "\"그렇게 생각해?\""], ["said the latter.", "애밀리아가 말했다."], ["\"I'll tell him.\"", "\"그럼 오라버니한테 일러 줄게.\""]], [["\"Darling!", "\"얘!"], ["not for worlds,\"", "무슨 일이 있어도 안 돼.\""], ["said Miss Sharp, starting back as timid as a fawn.", "샤프 양이 새끼 사슴처럼 수줍게 흠칫 물러서며 말했다."], ["She had previously made a respectful virgin-like curtsey to the gentleman, and her modest eyes gazed so perseveringly on the carpet that it was a wonder how she should have found an opportunity to see him.", "그녀는 앞서 그 신사에게 처녀답고 정중한 인사를 올렸고, 정숙한 두 눈이 어찌나 한결같이 양탄자만 응시하던지, 대체 어느 틈에 그를 볼 기회가 있었는지 신기할 따름이었다."]], [["\"Thank you for the beautiful shawls, brother,\"", "\"아름다운 숄, 고마워요, 오라버니.\""], ["said Amelia to the fire poker.", "애밀리아가 부지깽이를 향해 말했다."], ["\"Are they not beautiful, Rebecca?\"", "\"정말 아름답지 않니, 리베카?\""]], [["\"O heavenly!\"", "\"오, 천상의 것이네!\""], ["said Miss Sharp, and her eyes went from the carpet straight to the chandelier.", "샤프 양이 말하니, 그 두 눈이 양탄자에서 곧장 샹들리에로 올라갔다."]], [["Joseph still continued a huge clattering at the poker and tongs, puffing and blowing the while, and turning as red as his yellow face would allow him.", "조지프는 여전히 부지깽이와 부집게를 요란하게 달그락거리며 숨을 헐떡이고 씩씩거렸고, 누런 얼굴이 허락하는 한껏 새빨개졌다."], ["\"I can't make you such handsome presents, Joseph,\"", "\"난 너한테 이런 멋진 선물은 못 해 준단다, 조지프.\""], ["continued his sister, \"but while I was at school, I have embroidered for you a very beautiful pair of braces.\"", "동생이 말을 이었다. \"하지만 학교에 있는 동안 오라버니 드리려고 아주 아름다운 멜빵 한 쌍을 수놓았어요.\""]], [["\"Good Gad!", "\"맙소사!"], ["Amelia,\"", "애밀리아.\""], ["cried the brother, in serious alarm, \"what do you mean?\"", "오라버니가 정말로 기겁하며 외쳤다. \"그게 무슨 소리냐?\""], ["and plunging with all his might at the bell-rope, that article of furniture came away in his hand, and increased the honest fellow's confusion.", "그러고는 온 힘을 다해 종 끈을 잡아당기는 바람에 그 가구 부속이 손에 쑥 빠져나와, 이 정직한 친구의 당혹스러움만 더하고 말았다."], ["\"For heaven's sake see if my buggy's at the door.", "\"제발 내 마차가 문 앞에 와 있는지 좀 봐 다오."], ["I CAN'T wait.", "못 기다린다."], ["I must go.", "가 봐야 해."], ["D---- that groom of mine.", "빌어먹을 마부 녀석 같으니."], ["I must go.\"", "가 봐야 해.\""]], [["At this minute the father of the family walked in, rattling his seals like a true British merchant.", "바로 이 순간 이 집안의 가장이, 진정한 영국 상인답게 회중시계 줄 장식을 짤랑거리며 들어왔다."], ["\"What's the matter, Emmy?\"", "\"무슨 일이냐, 에미?\""], ["says he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"Joseph wants me to see if his--his buggy is at the door.", "\"조지프 오라버니가 자기—자기 버기가 문 앞에 와 있는지 봐 달래요."], ["What is a buggy, Papa?\"", "버기가 뭐예요, 아버지?\""]], [["\"It is a one-horse palanquin,\"", "\"말 한 필이 끄는 가마 같은 거란다.\""], ["said the old gentleman, who was a wag in his way.", "노신사가 말했는데, 그는 그 나름대로 익살꾼이었다."]], [["Joseph at this burst out into a wild fit of laughter; in which, encountering the eye of Miss Sharp, he stopped all of a sudden, as if he had been shot.", "이에 조지프가 와락 미친 듯한 웃음을 터뜨렸으나, 샤프 양의 시선과 마주치자 마치 총에라도 맞은 듯 뚝 멈췄다."]], [["\"This young lady is your friend?", "\"이 젊은 숙녀가 네 친구냐?"], ["Miss Sharp, I am very happy to see you.", "샤프 양, 만나서 정말 반갑소."], ["Have you and Emmy been quarrelling already with Joseph, that he wants to be off?\"", "그래, 너랑 에미가 벌써 조지프와 다투기라도 했길래 이 녀석이 나가려는 거냐?\""]], [["\"I promised Bonamy of our service, sir,\"", "\"제가 우리 부서의 보너미와 함께 식사하기로 약속했거든요, 아버지.\""], ["said Joseph, \"to dine with him.\"", "조지프가 말했다."]], [["\"O fie!", "\"이런!"], ["didn't you tell your mother you would dine here?\"", "어머니께 여기서 저녁을 들겠다고 하지 않았느냐?\""]], [["\"But in this dress it's impossible.\"", "\"하지만 이런 차림으로는 어림없어요.\""]], [["\"Look at him, isn't he handsome enough to dine anywhere, Miss Sharp?\"", "\"이 녀석 좀 보시오, 어디 가서 식사한들 손색없을 만큼 미남이지 않소, 샤프 양?\""]], [["On which, of course, Miss Sharp looked at her friend, and they both set off in a fit of laughter, highly agreeable to the old gentleman.", "이에 샤프 양은 당연히 친구를 쳐다보았고, 두 사람은 와락 웃음을 터뜨렸으니, 노신사에게는 더할 나위 없이 흐뭇한 일이었다."]], [["\"Did you ever see a pair of buckskins like those at Miss Pinkerton's?\"", "\"핑커튼 여사 댁에서 저런 사슴 가죽 바지를 본 적이 있소?\""], ["continued he, following up his advantage.", "그가 유리한 고지를 놓치지 않고 밀어붙였다."]], [["\"Gracious heavens!", "\"하늘에 맙소사!"], ["Father,\"", "아버지.\""], ["cried Joseph.", "조지프가 외쳤다."]], [["\"There now, I have hurt his feelings.", "\"그래, 이 녀석 기분을 상하게 했군."], ["Mrs. Sedley, my dear, I have hurt your son's feelings.", "여보, 부인, 내가 당신 아들 기분을 상하게 했소."], ["I have alluded to his buckskins.", "사슴 가죽 바지 얘기를 꺼냈으니 말이오."], ["Ask Miss Sharp if I haven't?", "안 그런지 샤프 양한테 물어보구려."], ["Come, Joseph, be friends with Miss Sharp, and let us all go to dinner.\"", "자, 조지프, 샤프 양과 화해하고 다 같이 저녁이나 들러 가자.\""]], [["\"There's a pillau, Joseph, just as you like it, and Papa has brought home the best turbot in Billingsgate.\"", "\"필라우가 있단다, 조지프, 네가 좋아하는 그대로 말이다. 게다가 아버지가 빌링즈게이트에서 제일 좋은 가자미를 사 오셨어.\""]], [["\"Come, come, sir, walk downstairs with Miss Sharp, and I will follow with these two young women,\"", "\"자, 자, 어서, 샤프 양과 함께 아래층으로 내려가게나. 나는 이 두 부인을 데리고 뒤따라가겠네.\""], ["said the father, and he took an arm of wife and daughter and walked merrily off.", "가장이 이렇게 말하고는 아내와 딸의 팔을 한쪽씩 끼고 흥겹게 걸어갔다."]], [["If Miss Rebecca Sharp had determined in her heart upon making the conquest of this big beau, I don't think, ladies, we have any right to blame her; for though the task of husband-hunting is generally, and with becoming modesty, entrusted by young persons to their mammas, recollect that Miss Sharp had no kind parent to arrange these delicate matters for her, and that if she did not get a husband for herself, there was no one else in the wide world who would take the trouble off her hands.", "만약 리베카 샤프 양이 이 덩치 큰 멋쟁이를 정복하기로 마음먹었다 한들, 숙녀 여러분, 우리에게 그녀를 탓할 권리가 있다고는 생각지 않는다. 남편 사냥이라는 일은 대개 적당한 겸양과 함께 젊은 사람들이 자기 어머니에게 맡기는 법이지만, 샤프 양에게는 이 미묘한 일을 주선해 줄 다정한 부모가 없었음을, 그리고 그녀가 스스로 남편을 구하지 못한다면 이 넓은 세상천지에 그 수고를 대신 떠맡아 줄 사람이 아무도 없었음을 기억하라."], ["What causes young people to \"come out,\" but the noble ambition of matrimony?", "젊은 사람들이 사교계에 \"데뷔\"하게 만드는 것이 결혼이라는 고귀한 야망 말고 무엇이겠는가?"], ["What sends them trooping to watering-places?", "그들을 떼 지어 온천지로 몰려가게 하는 것은 무엇인가?"], ["What keeps them dancing till five o'clock in the morning through a whole mortal season?", "한 시즌 내내 죽을 둥 살 둥 새벽 다섯 시까지 춤추게 하는 것은 무엇인가?"], ["What causes them to labour at pianoforte sonatas, and to learn four songs from a fashionable master at a guinea a lesson, and to play the harp if they have handsome arms and neat elbows, and to wear Lincoln Green toxophilite hats and feathers, but that they may bring down some \"desirable\"", "그들로 하여금 피아노포르테 소나타를 익히느라 애쓰게 하고, 한 번 수업에 기니씩이나 받는 유행하는 선생에게서 노래 네 곡을 배우게 하고, 팔이 곱고 팔꿈치가 매끈하면 하프를 켜게 하고, 링컨 그린의 궁사 모자에 깃털을 꽂게 하는 것이 무엇이겠는가? 그 죽여주는 활과 화살로 어떤 \"탐나는\""], ["young man with those killing bows and arrows of theirs?", "젊은 남자를 쏘아 떨어뜨리려는 속셈 말고 무엇이겠는가?"], ["What causes respectable parents to take up their carpets, set their houses topsy-turvy, and spend a fifth of their year's income in ball suppers and iced champagne?", "점잖은 부모들로 하여금 양탄자를 걷어 내고 집안을 발칵 뒤집으며 한 해 수입의 오분의 일을 무도회 만찬과 얼음 채운 샴페인에 쏟아붓게 하는 것은 무엇인가?"], ["Is it sheer love of their species, and an unadulterated wish to see young people happy and dancing?", "그게 순전히 같은 인류에 대한 사랑이며, 젊은이들이 행복하게 춤추는 모습을 보고 싶은 순수한 마음에서일까?"], ["Psha!", "흥!"], ["they want to marry their daughters; and, as honest Mrs.", "그들은 딸을 시집보내고 싶은 것이다."], ["Sedley has, in the depths of her kind heart, already arranged a score of little schemes for the settlement of her Amelia, so also had our beloved but unprotected Rebecca determined to do her very best to secure the husband, who was even more necessary for her than for her friend.", "정직한 세들리 부인이 그 다정한 마음 깊은 곳에서 이미 자기 애밀리아의 안착을 위해 자잘한 계략을 스무 가지나 짜 두었듯이, 우리의 사랑스럽지만 보호자 없는 리베카 역시 친구에게보다도 자신에게 더 절실히 필요한 그 남편을 손에 넣으려고 최선을 다하기로 작정한 것이었다. 그녀에게는 생생한 상상력이 있었다."], ["She had a vivid imagination; she had, besides, read the Arabian Nights and Guthrie's Geography; and it is a fact that while she was dressing for dinner, and after she had asked Amelia whether her brother was very rich, she had built for herself a most magnificent castle in the air, of which she was mistress, with a husband somewhere in the background (she had not seen him as yet, and his figure would not therefore be very distinct); she had arrayed herself in an infinity of shawls, turbans, and diamond necklaces, and had mounted upon an elephant to the sound of the march in Bluebeard, in order to pay a visit of ceremony to the Grand Mogul.", "게다가 그녀는 「아라비안나이트」와 거스리의 「지리서」를 읽은 터였다. 그리하여 그녀가 저녁 식사를 위해 옷을 차려입는 동안, 그리고 애밀리아에게 오라버니가 아주 부자냐고 물어본 뒤에, 그녀는 스스로가 안주인이 되는 더없이 으리으리한 공중누각을 지어 올렸으니, 어딘가 배경에는 남편이 하나 있고(아직 그를 본 적이 없으니 그 모습이 그리 또렷하지는 않았다), 그녀는 숄과 터번과 다이아몬드 목걸이를 무수히 두르고 「푸른 수염」의 행진곡 가락에 맞춰 코끼리에 올라타고 대(大)무굴 황제에게 의례적 방문을 다니는 것이었다."], ["Charming Alnaschar visions!", "매혹적인 알나샤르의 환상이여!"], ["it is the happy privilege of youth to construct you, and many a fanciful young creature besides Rebecca Sharp has indulged in these delightful day-dreams ere now!", "그대를 지어 올리는 것은 젊음의 행복한 특권이니, 리베카 샤프 말고도 공상에 잠긴 숱한 젊은이들이 지금껏 이 즐거운 백일몽에 빠져들어 왔으리라!"]], [["Joseph Sedley was twelve years older than his sister Amelia.", "조지프 세들리는 누이동생 애밀리아보다 열두 살 위였다."], ["He was in the East India Company's Civil Service, and his name appeared, at the period of which we write, in the Bengal division of the East India Register, as collector of Boggley Wollah, an honourable and lucrative post, as everybody knows: in order to know to what higher posts Joseph rose in the service, the reader is referred to the same periodical.", "그는 동인도회사의 문관(文官)이었고, 우리가 글을 쓰는 이 시기에 그의 이름은 동인도 명부의 벵골 지부에 보글리 월라의 징세관으로 올라 있었으니, 누구나 알다시피 명예롭고 잇속 좋은 자리였다. 조지프가 그 후 이 회사에서 어떤 더 높은 자리에 올랐는지 알고 싶은 독자는 같은 간행물을 참고하시라."]], [["Boggley Wollah is situated in a fine, lonely, marshy, jungly district, famous for snipe-shooting, and where not unfrequently you may flush a tiger.", "보글리 월라는 도요새 사냥으로 유명하고 심심찮게 호랑이를 몰아낼 수 있는, 멋지고 외지고 늪지투성이에 정글이 우거진 지역에 자리하고 있었다."], ["Ramgunge, where there is a magistrate, is only forty miles off, and there is a cavalry station about thirty miles farther; so Joseph wrote home to his parents, when he took possession of his collectorship.", "치안판사가 있는 람겅게가 겨우 사십 마일 떨어져 있고, 거기서 한 삼십 마일을 더 가면 기병대 주둔지가 있었다. 그가 징세관 자리를 떠맡았을 때 조지프는 부모에게 보낸 편지에 그렇게 적었다."], ["He had lived for about eight years of his life, quite alone, at this charming place, scarcely seeing a Christian face except twice a year, when the detachment arrived to carry off the revenues which he had collected, to Calcutta.", "그는 일 년에 두 번, 그가 거둬들인 세금을 캘커타로 실어 가려고 파견대가 올 때를 빼고는 기독교도의 얼굴이라곤 거의 구경도 못 한 채, 이 매력적인 곳에서 팔 년 가까운 세월을 그야말로 홀로 살았다."]], [["Luckily, at this time he caught a liver complaint, for the cure of which he returned to Europe, and which was the source of great comfort and amusement to him in his native country.", "다행히 이 무렵 그는 간장병에 걸렸고, 그것을 고치려고 유럽으로 돌아왔으니, 이 병은 고국에서 그에게 더없는 위안과 즐거움의 원천이 되었다."], ["He did not live with his family while in London, but had lodgings of his own, like a gay young bachelor.", "그는 런던에 있는 동안 가족과 함께 살지 않고, 명랑한 젊은 독신 남자답게 자기만의 숙소를 따로 두었다."], ["Before he went to India he was too young to partake of the delightful pleasures of a man about town, and plunged into them on his return with considerable assiduity.", "인도로 가기 전에는 도회지 한량의 즐거운 쾌락을 누리기에 너무 어렸으나, 돌아온 뒤로는 상당한 열성으로 거기에 푹 빠져들었다."], ["He drove his horses in the Park; he dined at the fashionable taverns (for the Oriental Club was not as yet invented); he frequented the theatres, as the mode was in those days, or made his appearance at the opera, laboriously attired in tights and a cocked hat.", "그는 공원에서 말을 몰았고, 유행하는 선술집에서 식사했으며(오리엔탈 클럽이 아직 생기기 전이었다), 그 시절 유행대로 극장을 들락거리거나, 몸에 딱 붙는 타이츠에 삼각모를 갖춰 입고 애써 오페라에 모습을 드러내곤 했다."]], [["On returning to India, and ever after, he used to talk of the pleasure of this period of his existence with great enthusiasm, and give you to understand that he and Brummel were the leading bucks of the day.", "인도로 돌아간 뒤로도, 그리고 그 후로도 줄곧 그는 인생의 이 시기에 누린 쾌락을 무척 열을 올려 떠벌렸고, 자기와 브러멜이 당대의 으뜸가는 멋쟁이였다는 식으로 떠들어 댔다."], ["But he was as lonely here as in his jungle at Boggley Wollah.", "그러나 그는 여기 런던에서도 보글리 월라의 정글에서만큼이나 외로웠다."], ["He scarcely knew a single soul in the metropolis: and were it not for his doctor, and the society of his blue-pill, and his liver complaint, he must have died of loneliness.", "이 대도시에서 그가 아는 사람이라곤 거의 한 명도 없었으니, 의사와 청환약 약병의 벗과 간장병이 없었더라면 그는 외로움에 죽고 말았을 것이다."], ["He was lazy, peevish, and a bon-vivant; the appearance of a lady frightened him beyond measure; hence it was but seldom that he joined the paternal circle in Russell Square, where there was plenty of gaiety, and where the jokes of his good-natured old father frightened his amour-propre.", "그는 게으르고, 까탈스럽고, 미식가였다. 숙녀의 출현은 그를 한없이 겁먹게 했으니, 그래서 그는 흥겨운 일이 가득하고 마음씨 좋은 늙은 아버지의 농담이 그의 자존심을 겁박하는 러셀 광장의 가족 모임에 좀처럼 끼지 않았던 것이다."], ["His bulk caused Joseph much anxious thought and alarm; now and then he would make a desperate attempt to get rid of his superabundant fat; but his indolence and love of good living speedily got the better of these endeavours at reform, and he found himself again at his three meals a day.", "그의 비대한 몸집은 조지프에게 적잖은 근심과 불안을 안겨 주었다. 이따금 그는 넘쳐 나는 군살을 빼 보려고 필사적인 시도를 하곤 했으나, 그의 게으름과 잘 먹는 데 대한 사랑이 이내 그 개혁의 노력을 이기고 말아, 그는 어느새 다시 하루 세 끼를 챙겨 먹고 있는 자신을 발견했다."], ["He never was well dressed; but he took the hugest pains to adorn his big person, and passed many hours daily in that occupation.", "그는 결코 옷을 잘 차려입은 적이 없었으나, 그 거대한 몸뚱이를 꾸미느라 엄청난 공을 들였고, 매일 그 일에 여러 시간을 쏟았다."], ["His valet made a fortune out of his wardrobe: his toilet-table was covered with as many pomatums and essences as ever were employed by an old beauty: he had tried, in order to give himself a waist, every girth, stay, and waistband then invented.", "그의 시종은 그 의상실 덕에 한밑천 잡았고, 그의 화장대는 늙은 미인이 쓰던 만큼이나 많은 머릿기름과 향수로 뒤덮여 있었다. 그는 허리를 만들어 보려고 그 시절 발명된 온갖 복대며 코르셋이며 허리띠를 죄다 시험해 보았다."], ["Like most fat men, he would have his clothes made too tight, and took care they should be of the most brilliant colours and youthful cut.", "대부분의 뚱뚱한 남자가 그러하듯, 그는 옷을 너무 꽉 끼게 지어 입었고, 옷이 더없이 화사한 빛깔에 젊은이다운 재단이 되도록 신경을 썼다."], ["When dressed at length, in the afternoon, he would issue forth to take a drive with nobody in the Park; and then would come back in order to dress again and go and dine with nobody at the Piazza Coffee-House.", "마침내 차려입고 나면, 오후에 그는 공원으로 아무도 없이 홀로 마차 산책을 나섰고, 그러고는 다시 차려입으러 돌아왔다가 피아자 커피하우스로 가서 아무하고도 없이 홀로 저녁을 들었다."], ["He was as vain as a girl; and perhaps his extreme shyness was one of the results of his extreme vanity.", "그는 처녀처럼 허영이 심했다. 어쩌면 그의 극심한 수줍음도 극심한 허영의 한 결과였는지 모른다."], ["If Miss Rebecca can get the better of him, and at her first entrance into life, she is a young person of no ordinary cleverness.", "만약 리베카 양이 사교계에 첫발을 들이자마자 이 남자를 손에 넣을 수 있다면, 그녀는 예사롭지 않은 영리함을 지닌 젊은이일 것이다."]], [["The first move showed considerable skill.", "첫수부터 그녀는 상당한 솜씨를 보였다."], ["When she called Sedley a very handsome man, she knew that Amelia would tell her mother, who would probably tell Joseph, or who, at any rate, would be pleased by the compliment paid to her son. All mothers are.", "세들리를 아주 잘생긴 남자라고 말했을 때, 그녀는 애밀리아가 그 말을 어머니에게 전하고, 어머니는 아마 조지프에게 전하리라는 것을, 아니 적어도 아들에게 바쳐진 그 찬사에 어머니가 기뻐하리라는 것을 알았던 것이다."], ["If you had told Sycorax that her son Caliban was as handsome as Apollo, she would have been pleased, witch as she was.", "어머니란 다 그런 법이다. 시코락스에게 그녀의 아들 캘리밴이 아폴로만큼 잘생겼다고 말했다면, 마녀인 그녀조차 기뻐했을 것이다."], ["Perhaps, too, Joseph Sedley would overhear the compliment--Rebecca spoke loud enough--and he did hear, and (thinking in his heart that he was a very fine man) the praise thrilled through every fibre of his big body, and made it tingle with pleasure.", "게다가 어쩌면 조지프 세들리가 그 칭찬을 엿들었을지도 모르고—리베카는 들릴 만큼 충분히 큰 소리로 말했다—실제로 그는 들었다. 그리하여 (속으로 자기가 아주 근사한 남자라고 여기던) 그의 거대한 몸뚱이는 그 칭찬에 온 신경이 짜릿하게 떨려 와 쾌감에 저렸다."], ["Then, however, came a recoil.", "그러나 곧이어 반동이 왔다."], ["\"Is the girl making fun of me?\"", "\"이 처녀가 날 놀리는 건가?\""], ["he thought, and straightway he bounced towards the bell, and was for retreating, as we have seen, when his father's jokes and his mother's entreaties caused him to pause and stay where he was.", "그는 생각했고, 곧장 종 쪽으로 튀어 가서, 앞서 본 대로 물러나려 했으나, 아버지의 농담과 어머니의 간청에 멈칫하여 그 자리에 머물게 되었던 것이다."], ["He conducted the young lady down to dinner in a dubious and agitated frame of mind.", "그는 미심쩍고 동요하는 마음으로 그 젊은 숙녀를 데리고 아래층 식사 자리로 내려갔다."], ["\"Does she really think I am handsome?\"", "\"이 처녀가 정말 날 잘생겼다고 여기는 걸까,\""], ["thought he, \"or is she only making game of me?\"", "그는 생각했다. \"아니면 그저 날 놀리는 건가?\""], ["We have talked of Joseph Sedley being as vain as a girl.", "우리는 조지프 세들리가 처녀처럼 허영이 심하다고 말한 바 있다."], ["Heaven help us!", "맙소사!"], ["the girls have only to turn the tables, and say of one of their own sex, \"She is as vain as a man,\" and they will have perfect reason.", "처녀들이 입장을 뒤집어 자기네 동성 가운데 누군가를 두고 \"쟤는 남자처럼 허영이 심해\"라고 말하기만 한다면, 그 말은 더없이 지당할 것이다."], ["The bearded creatures are quite as eager for praise, quite as finikin over their toilettes, quite as proud of their personal advantages, quite as conscious of their powers of fascination, as any coquette in the world.", "턱수염 난 족속들도 세상 어느 요염한 여자 못지않게 칭찬에 안달이고, 못지않게 몸치장에 까탈스럽고, 못지않게 제 외모를 뽐내며, 못지않게 제 매혹의 힘을 의식하는 것이다."]], [["Downstairs, then, they went, Joseph very red and blushing, Rebecca very modest, and holding her green eyes downwards.", "그리하여 두 사람은 아래층으로 내려갔다. 조지프는 얼굴이 새빨개져 홍조를 띠었고, 리베카는 더없이 정숙하게 그 초록빛 두 눈을 내리뜨고 있었다."], ["She was dressed in white, with bare shoulders as white as snow--the picture of youth, unprotected innocence, and humble virgin simplicity.", "그녀는 흰옷을 입었고, 드러난 두 어깨는 눈처럼 희었으니—젊음과, 보호받지 못하는 순결과, 겸손한 처녀의 순박함 그 자체였다."], ["\"I must be very quiet,\"", "\"아주 얌전하게 굴어야지,\""], ["thought Rebecca, \"and very much interested about India.\"", "리베카는 생각했다. \"그리고 인도 얘기에 무척 관심 있는 척해야지.\""]], [["Now we have heard how Mrs. Sedley had prepared a fine curry for her son, just as he liked it, and in the course of dinner a portion of this dish was offered to Rebecca.", "자, 우리는 세들리 부인이 아들이 좋아하는 그대로 멋진 카레를 그를 위해 마련해 두었다는 이야기를 들은 바 있는데, 저녁 식사 도중에 이 요리 한 접시가 리베카에게도 권해졌다."], ["\"What is it?\"", "\"이게 뭔가요?\""], ["said she, turning an appealing look to Mr. Joseph.", "그녀가 애원하는 눈빛을 조지프 쪽으로 돌리며 말했다."]], [["\"Capital,\"", "\"기막히지요.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["His mouth was full of it: his face quite red with the delightful exercise of gobbling.", "입에는 카레가 가득했고, 얼굴은 게걸스레 먹어 대는 그 즐거운 운동으로 한껏 붉어져 있었다."], ["\"Mother, it's as good as my own curries in India.\"", "\"어머니, 인도에서 제가 직접 만들던 카레만큼이나 맛있는데요.\""]], [["\"Oh, I must try some, if it is an Indian dish,\"", "\"오, 인도 요리라면 저도 좀 먹어 봐야겠어요.\""], ["said Miss Rebecca.", "리베카 양이 말했다."], ["\"I am sure everything must be good that comes from there.\"", "\"거기서 온 거라면 무엇이든 분명 좋을 거예요.\""]], [["\"Give Miss Sharp some curry, my dear,\"", "\"샤프 양한테 카레 좀 드리렴, 얘야.\""], ["said Mr. Sedley, laughing.", "세들리 씨가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["Rebecca had never tasted the dish before.", "리베카는 그 음식을 한 번도 맛본 적이 없었다."]], [["\"Do you find it as good as everything else from India?\"", "\"인도에서 온 다른 모든 것만큼이나 좋소?\""], ["said Mr. Sedley.", "세들리 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, excellent!\"", "\"오, 일품이에요!\""], ["said Rebecca, who was suffering tortures with the cayenne pepper.", "카옌 고추 때문에 고문 같은 고통을 겪으며 리베카가 말했다."]], [["\"Try a chili with it, Miss Sharp,\"", "\"고추를 곁들여 드셔 보세요, 샤프 양.\""], ["said Joseph, really interested.", "정말로 관심을 보이며 조지프가 말했다."]], [["\"A chili,\"", "\"고추요.\""], ["said Rebecca, gasping.", "리베카가 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다."], ["\"Oh yes!\"", "\"오, 좋아요!\""], ["She thought a chili was something cool, as its name imported, and was served with some. \"How fresh and green they look,\"", "그녀는 그 이름이 시사하듯 고추가 뭔가 시원한 것이리라 여겼고, 그래서 고추 몇 개를 받았다.* \"어쩜 이렇게 싱싱하고 푸르네요.\""], ["she said, and put one into her mouth.", "그녀가 그렇게 말하며 하나를 입에 넣었다."], ["It was hotter than the curry; flesh and blood could bear it no longer.", "그것은 카레보다 더 매웠다. 살과 피로 된 인간으로서는 더 이상 견딜 수가 없었다."], ["She laid down her fork.", "그녀는 포크를 내려놓았다."], ["\"Water, for Heaven's sake, water!\"", "\"물 좀, 제발, 물 좀!\""], ["she cried. Mr.", "그녀가 외쳤다."], ["Sedley burst out laughing (he was a coarse man, from the Stock Exchange, where they love all sorts of practical jokes).", "세들리 씨가 와락 웃음을 터뜨렸다(그는 증권거래소 출신의 거친 남자였는데, 그곳 사람들은 온갖 종류의 짓궂은 장난을 좋아한다)."], ["\"They are real Indian, I assure you,\"", "\"정말 인도 본토 것이오, 장담하리다.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"Sambo, give Miss Sharp some water.\"", "\"샘보, 샤프 양께 물 좀 갖다 드려라.\""]], [["The paternal laugh was echoed by Joseph, who thought the joke capital.", "아버지의 웃음에 조지프도 메아리쳤으니, 그는 그 장난이 기막히다고 여겼다."], ["The ladies only smiled a little.", "부인들은 그저 살짝 미소 지을 뿐이었다."], ["They thought poor Rebecca suffered too much.", "그들은 가엾은 리베카가 너무 심한 고생을 한다고 여겼다."], ["She would have liked to choke old Sedley, but she swallowed her mortification as well as she had the abominable curry before it, and as soon as she could speak, said, with a comical, good-humoured air, \"I ought to have remembered the pepper which the Princess of Persia puts in the cream-tarts in the Arabian Nights.", "그녀는 늙은 세들리의 목이라도 조르고 싶은 심정이었으나, 앞서 그 끔찍한 카레를 삼켰던 만큼이나 잘도 그 굴욕을 삼켰고, 입을 뗄 수 있게 되자마자 익살스럽고 너그러운 태도로 말했다. \"「아라비안나이트」에서 페르시아 공주가 크림 타르트에 넣는 후추를 기억했어야 했는데."], ["Do you put cayenne into your cream-tarts in India, sir?\"", "어르신, 인도에서는 크림 타르트에 카옌을 넣나요?\""]], [["Old Sedley began to laugh, and thought Rebecca was a good-humoured girl.", "늙은 세들리는 웃기 시작했고, 리베카가 너그러운 처녀라고 여겼다."], ["Joseph simply said, \"Cream-tarts, Miss?", "조지프는 그저 이렇게 말했다. \"크림 타르트요, 양?"], ["Our cream is very bad in Bengal.", "벵골에서는 크림이 아주 형편없습니다."], ["We generally use goats' milk; and, 'gad, do you know, I've got to prefer it!\"", "우리는 대개 염소젖을 쓰는데, 이거 참, 그거 아세요, 제가 그쪽을 더 좋아하게 됐지 뭡니까!\""]], [["\"You won't like EVERYTHING from India now, Miss Sharp,\" said the old gentleman; but when the ladies had retired after dinner, the wily old fellow said to his son, \"Have a care, Joe; that girl is setting her cap at you.\"", "\"이제는 인도에서 온 거라고 죄다 좋아할 수는 없겠지요, 샤프 양.\" 노신사가 말했다. 그러나 저녁 식사 후 부인들이 물러가자, 이 능청맞은 노인은 아들에게 말했다. \"조심해라, 조. 저 처녀가 너한테 눈독을 들이고 있어.\""]], [["\"Pooh!", "\"흥!"], ["nonsense!\"", "말도 안 돼요!\""], ["said Joe, highly flattered.", "조가 한껏 우쭐해져서 말했다. \"기억하시는지요, 아버지, 덤덤에 한 처녀가 있었지요."], ["\"I recollect, sir, there was a girl at Dumdum, a daughter of Cutler of the Artillery, and afterwards married to Lance, the surgeon, who made a dead set at me in the year '4--at me and Mulligatawney, whom I mentioned to you before dinner--a devilish good fellow Mulligatawney--he's a magistrate at Budgebudge, and sure to be in council in five years.", "포병대 커틀러의 딸인데, 나중에 외과의 랜스와 결혼했지요. 그 처녀가 4년에 저한테 작정하고 덤벼들었지 뭡니까—저랑 멀리거토니한테요, 저녁 식사 전에 말씀드린 그 친구 말이에요—멀리거토니는 정말 끝내주게 좋은 친구지요—버지버지의 치안판사인데, 오 년 안에 틀림없이 참사회에 들어갈 겁니다."], ["Well, sir, the Artillery gave a ball, and Quintin, of the King's 14th, said to me, 'Sedley,'", "그래, 아버지, 포병대가 무도회를 열었는데, 국왕 직속 14연대의 퀸틴이 저한테 그러더군요. '세들리,'"], ["said he, 'I bet you thirteen to ten that Sophy Cutler hooks either you or Mulligatawney before the rains.'", "그러는 거예요, '우기 오기 전에 소피 커틀러가 자네나 멀리거토니 둘 중 하나를 낚는다는 데 십 대 십삼으로 걸지.'"], ["'Done,'", "'좋아,'"], ["says I; and egad, sir--this claret's very good.", "제가 그랬지요. 그래서, 이런 참—이 클라레, 아주 좋은데요."], ["Adamson's or Carbonell's?\"", "애덤슨 거예요, 아니면 카보넬 거예요?\""]], [["A slight snore was the only reply: the honest stockbroker was asleep, and so the rest of Joseph's story was lost for that day.", "가벼운 코 고는 소리만이 유일한 대답이었다. 정직한 주식 중개인은 잠들어 있었으니, 그리하여 조지프의 나머지 이야기는 그날은 묻혀 버리고 말았다."], ["But he was always exceedingly communicative in a man's party, and has told this delightful tale many scores of times to his apothecary, Dr. Gollop, when he came to inquire about the liver and the blue-pill.", "그러나 그는 남자들끼리의 술자리에서는 늘 입이 무척 헤펐으니, 약제사 골롭 박사가 간장과 청환약에 관해 물으러 올 때마다 이 즐거운 이야기를 수십 번이고 들려주곤 했다."]], [["Being an invalid, Joseph Sedley contented himself with a bottle of claret besides his Madeira at dinner, and he managed a couple of plates full of strawberries and cream, and twenty-four little rout cakes that were lying neglected in a plate near him, and certainly (for novelists have the privilege of knowing everything) he thought a great deal about the girl upstairs.", "병자인지라 조지프 세들리는 저녁 식사 때 마데이라주(酒) 말고는 클라레 한 병으로 만족했고, 거기에 딸기와 크림을 두 접시나 가득 비우고, 곁의 접시에 손도 안 댄 채 놓여 있던 자그마한 라우트 케이크 스물네 개를 해치웠다. 그리고 (소설가에게는 모든 것을 알 특권이 있으니) 그가 위층의 그 처녀를 두고 무던히도 생각에 잠겼던 것은 분명하다."], ["\"A nice, gay, merry young creature,\"", "\"참하고, 발랄하고, 명랑한 처녀로군,\""], ["thought he to himself.", "그는 속으로 생각했다."], ["\"How she looked at me when I picked up her handkerchief at dinner!", "\"식사 때 내가 손수건을 주워 줄 때 날 어찌나 쳐다보던지!"], ["She dropped it twice.", "두 번이나 떨어뜨렸지."], ["Who's that singing in the drawing-room?", "응접실에서 노래하는 게 누구지?"], ["'Gad!", "이런!"], ["shall I go up and see?\"", "올라가서 봐 볼까?\""]], [["But his modesty came rushing upon him with uncontrollable force.", "그러나 그의 수줍음이 걷잡을 수 없는 기세로 그에게 밀려들었다."], ["His father was asleep: his hat was in the hall: there was a hackney-coach standing hard by in Southampton Row.", "아버지는 잠들어 있고, 모자는 현관에 있고, 사우샘프턴 거리 바로 옆에는 삯마차 한 대가 서 있었다."], ["\"I'll go and see the Forty Thieves,\"", "\"「사십인의 도둑」이나 보러 가야지,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, \"and Miss Decamp's dance\"; and he slipped away gently on the pointed toes of his boots, and disappeared, without waking his worthy parent.", "\"그리고 디캠프 양의 춤도.\" 그러고는 그는 부츠의 뾰족한 발끝으로 살그머니 빠져나가, 훌륭한 부모님을 깨우지 않고 사라져 버렸다."]], [["\"There goes Joseph,\"", "\"저기 조지프가 가네.\""], ["said Amelia, who was looking from the open windows of the drawing-room, while Rebecca was singing at the piano.", "애밀리아가 응접실의 열린 창밖을 내다보며 말했다. 그동안 리베카는 피아노 앞에서 노래를 부르고 있었다."]], [["\"Miss Sharp has frightened him away,\"", "\"샤프 양이 그 애를 겁줘서 쫓아버렸군요.\""], ["said Mrs. Sedley.", "세들리 부인이 말했다."], ["\"Poor Joe, why WILL he be so shy?\"", "\"가엾은 조, 어쩜 저렇게 수줍음이 많을까요?\""]], [["The Green Silk Purse\nPoor Joe's panic lasted for two or three days; during which he did not visit the house, nor during that period did Miss Rebecca ever mention his name.", "가엾은 조의 공황 상태는 이삼 일 동안 이어졌다. 그동안 그는 그 집에 발걸음을 하지 않았고, 그 기간 내내 레베카 양도 그의 이름을 입에 올리지 않았다."], ["She was all respectful gratitude to Mrs. Sedley; delighted beyond measure at the Bazaars; and in a whirl of wonder at the theatre, whither the good-natured lady took her.", "그녀는 세들리 부인에게 더없이 깍듯한 감사를 표했고, 바자회에서는 이루 말할 수 없이 즐거워했으며, 마음씨 고운 부인이 데려가 준 극장에서는 경이로움의 소용돌이에 휩싸였다."], ["One day, Amelia had a headache, and could not go upon some party of pleasure to which the two young people were invited: nothing could induce her friend to go without her.", "어느 날 애밀리아가 두통이 나서, 두 젊은이가 초대받은 어느 유흥 모임에 갈 수 없게 되었다. 그러자 무슨 수를 써도 그녀의 친구는 애밀리아 없이 가려 하지 않았다."], ["\"What!", "\"뭐라고요!"], ["you who have shown the poor orphan what happiness and love are for the first time in her life--quit YOU?", "이 가엾은 고아에게 생애 처음으로 행복과 사랑이 무엇인지 알려 준 당신을 두고 가라고요? 절대로요!\""], ["Never!\" and the green eyes looked up to Heaven and filled with tears; and Mrs.", "그러면서 초록빛 눈동자가 하늘을 우러러보며 눈물로 가득 찼다."], ["Sedley could not but own that her daughter's friend had a charming kind heart of her own.", "세들리 부인은 딸의 친구가 정말이지 사랑스럽고 다정한 마음씨를 지녔음을 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["As for Mr. Sedley's jokes, Rebecca laughed at them with a cordiality and perseverance which not a little pleased and softened that good-natured gentleman. Nor was it with the chiefs of the family alone that Miss Sharp found favour. She interested Mrs. Blenkinsop by evincing the deepest sympathy in the raspberry-jam preserving, which operation was then going on in the Housekeeper's room; she persisted in calling Sambo \"Sir,\" and \"Mr. Sambo,\" to the delight of that attendant; and she apologised to the lady's maid for giving her trouble in venturing to ring the bell, with such sweetness and humility, that the Servants' Hall was almost as charmed with her as the Drawing Room.", "세들리 씨의 농담으로 말하자면, 레베카는 거기에 어찌나 진심 어린 웃음을 거듭 터뜨리던지, 그 마음씨 좋은 신사를 적잖이 기쁘게 하고 마음을 누그러뜨렸다. 샤프 양이 호감을 얻은 것은 비단 그 집안의 가장들에게만이 아니었다. 그녀는 가정부의 방에서 한창 진행 중이던 산딸기 잼 졸이는 일에 더없이 깊은 관심을 보여 블렌킨솝 부인의 환심을 샀고, 흑인 하인 샘보를 한사코 \"나리\", \"샘보 나리\"라고 불러 그 하인을 흐뭇하게 했으며, 감히 종을 울려 수고를 끼쳐 미안하다고 시녀에게 어찌나 상냥하고 겸손하게 사과를 하던지, 하인들 방까지도 응접실 못지않게 그녀에게 매료되었다."]], [["Once, in looking over some drawings which Amelia had sent from school, Rebecca suddenly came upon one which caused her to burst into tears and leave the room.", "한번은 애밀리아가 학교에서 보내온 그림 몇 점을 들춰 보다가, 레베카가 느닷없이 어떤 그림 한 점을 발견하고는 눈물을 왈칵 쏟으며 방을 뛰쳐나간 적이 있었다."], ["It was on the day when Joe Sedley made his second appearance.", "그날은 조 세들리가 두 번째로 모습을 드러낸 날이었다."]], [["Amelia hastened after her friend to know the cause of this display of feeling, and the good-natured girl came back without her companion, rather affected too.", "애밀리아는 이 감정의 표출이 무슨 까닭인지 알아보려고 친구를 뒤쫓아 갔고, 그 마음씨 고운 소녀는 친구를 데려오지 못한 채 자기도 다소 감정이 북받친 채로 돌아왔다."], ["\"You know, her father was our drawing-master, Mamma, at Chiswick, and used to do all the best parts of our drawings.\"", "\"엄마, 있잖아요, 그 애 아버지가 치즈윅에서 우리 그림 선생님이셨잖아요. 늘 우리 그림에서 제일 잘된 부분들을 손봐 주셨고요.\""]], [["\"My love!", "\"얘야!"], ["I'm sure I always heard Miss Pinkerton say that he did not touch them--he only mounted them.\"", "핑커튼 여사님 말씀으로는 분명 그분이 손을 댄 게 아니라 그저 표구만 해 주셨다고 들었던 것 같은데.\""], ["\"It was called mounting, Mamma.", "\"표구라고 했어요, 엄마."], ["Rebecca remembers the drawing, and her father working at it, and the thought of it came upon her rather suddenly--and so, you know, she--\"", "레베카는 그 그림이며 자기 아버지가 거기 매달려 작업하시던 게 기억나는데, 그 생각이 그만 불쑥 떠오르는 바람에, 그러니까, 그 애가……\""]], [["\"The poor child is all heart,\"", "\"가엾은 것, 정이 많은 아이로구나.\""], ["said Mrs. Sedley.", "세들리 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"I wish she could stay with us another week,\"", "\"그 애가 우리랑 한 주만 더 있을 수 있으면 좋겠어요.\""], ["said Amelia.", "애밀리아가 말했다."]], [["\"She's devilish like Miss Cutler that I used to meet at Dumdum, only fairer.", "\"내가 덤덤에서 만나곤 했던 커틀러 양이랑 어쩌면 그렇게 똑 닮았는지, 다만 그 애가 더 살결이 흴 뿐이야."], ["She's married now to Lance, the Artillery Surgeon.", "그 양반은 지금 포병대 군의관 랜스랑 결혼했지."], ["Do you know, Ma'am, that once Quintin, of the 14th, bet me--\"", "그런데 어머니, 그거 아세요? 한번은 14연대의 퀸틴이 나한테 내기를 걸었는데……\""]], [["\"O Joseph, we know that story,\"", "\"아유, 조지프, 그 이야기는 우리도 알아요.\""], ["said Amelia, laughing.", "애밀리아가 웃으며 말했다."], ["\"Never mind about telling that; but persuade Mamma to write to Sir Something Crawley for leave of absence for poor dear Rebecca: here she comes, her eyes red with weeping.\"", "\"그 얘기는 그만하시고, 그것보다 엄마를 설득해서 가엾은 레베카가 좀 더 머물 수 있게 그 무슨 크롤리 경한테 휴가 연장을 부탁하는 편지를 써 주세요. 저기 오네요, 눈이 울어서 빨개진 채로요.\""]], [["\"I'm better, now,\"", "\"이제 좀 괜찮아졌어요.\""], ["said the girl, with the sweetest smile possible, taking good-natured Mrs.", "그 소녀가 더없이 사랑스러운 미소를 지으며, 마음씨 고운 세들리 부인이 내민 손을 잡고 공손히 입을 맞추며 말했다."], ["Sedley's extended hand and kissing it respectfully.", "\"다들 어쩜 이렇게 저한테 잘해 주시는지! 다들요.\""], ["\"How kind you all are to me! All,\"", "그녀가 웃으며 덧붙였다."], ["she added, with a laugh, \"except you, Mr. Joseph.\"", "\"조지프 씨, 당신만 빼고요.\""]], [["\"Me!\"", "\"나라고요!\""], ["said Joseph, meditating an instant departure.", "조지프가 당장이라도 자리를 뜰 궁리를 하며 말했다."], ["\"Gracious Heavens!", "\"맙소사!"], ["Good Gad!", "이런!"], ["Miss Sharp!'", "샤프 양!\""]], [["\"Yes; how could you be so cruel as to make me eat that horrid pepper-dish at dinner, the first day I ever saw you?", "\"네. 어쩌면 그렇게 모질게도 우리가 처음 만난 그날 저녁 식사에서 저더러 그 끔찍한 후추 요리를 먹게 만드실 수가 있었어요?"], ["You are not so good to me as dear Amelia.\"", "당신은 사랑스러운 애밀리아만큼 저한테 잘해 주지 않는걸요.\""]], [["\"He doesn't know you so well,\"", "\"오빠는 너를 그만큼 잘 몰라서 그래.\""], ["cried Amelia.", "애밀리아가 외쳤다."]], [["\"I defy anybody not to be good to you, my dear,\"", "\"누구든 너한테 잘해 주지 않을 사람은 없을 거다, 얘야.\""], ["said her mother.", "그녀의 어머니가 말했다."]], [["\"The curry was capital; indeed it was,\"", "\"그 카레는 일품이었어요. 정말이지 그랬다고요.\""], ["said Joe, quite gravely.", "조가 자못 진지하게 말했다."], ["\"Perhaps there was NOT enough citron juice in it--no, there was NOT.\"", "\"어쩌면 레몬즙이 좀 모자랐는지도—아니, 모자라지 않았어요.\""]], [["\"And the chilis?\"", "\"그럼 고추는요?\""]], [["\"By Jove, how they made you cry out!\"", "\"이런, 그것 때문에 당신이 어찌나 비명을 질러 댔는지!\""], ["said Joe, caught by the ridicule of the circumstance, and exploding in a fit of laughter which ended quite suddenly, as usual.", "그 상황의 우스꽝스러움에 사로잡힌 조가 웃음을 터뜨리며 말하더니, 늘 그렇듯 이내 뚝 그쳐 버렸다."]], [["\"I shall take care how I let YOU choose for me another time,\"", "\"다음번엔 절대로 당신더러 제 음식을 골라 달라고 하지 않게 조심해야겠어요.\""], ["said Rebecca, as they went down again to dinner.", "그들이 다시 저녁을 먹으러 내려가며 레베카가 말했다."], ["\"I didn't think men were fond of putting poor harmless girls to pain.\"", "\"남자들이 가엾고 죄 없는 여자애를 괴롭히는 걸 좋아하는 줄은 미처 몰랐네요.\""]], [["\"By Gad, Miss Rebecca, I wouldn't hurt you for the world.\"", "\"맹세코, 레베카 양, 난 무슨 일이 있어도 당신을 다치게 하진 않을 거요.\""]], [["\"No,\" said she, \"I KNOW you wouldn't\"; and then she gave him ever so gentle a pressure with her little hand, and drew it back quite frightened, and looked first for one instant in his face, and then down at the carpet-rods; and I am not prepared to say that Joe's heart did not thump at this little involuntary, timid, gentle motion of regard on the part of the simple girl.", "\"네.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그러지 않으실 줄 알아요.\" 그러고는 그 작은 손으로 그에게 더없이 부드럽게 힘을 주어 누르더니, 화들짝 놀란 듯 손을 거두고는, 먼저 한순간 그의 얼굴을 올려다보았다가 이내 카펫 고정봉으로 눈길을 떨어뜨렸다. 그러니 이 순박한 소녀가 보인 이 작고 무심결의, 수줍고도 다정한 호의의 몸짓에 조의 심장이 쿵쾅거리지 않았다고는 나로서는 단언할 수 없는 노릇이다."]], [["It was an advance, and as such, perhaps, some ladies of indisputable correctness and gentility will condemn the action as immodest; but, you see, poor dear Rebecca had all this work to do for herself.", "그것은 한 걸음 다가선 것이었고, 그런 까닭에 더없이 올바르고 품위 있다고 자부하는 어떤 숙녀들은 이 행동을 정숙하지 못하다고 비난할지도 모른다. 그러나 보다시피 가엾은 레베카는 이 모든 일을 스스로 해내야 하는 처지였다."], ["If a person is too poor to keep a servant, though ever so elegant, he must sweep his own rooms: if a dear girl has no dear Mamma to settle matters with the young man, she must do it for herself.", "아무리 우아한들 너무 가난해서 하인을 둘 수 없는 사람은 제 방을 손수 쓸어야 하는 법이다. 사랑스러운 아가씨에게 젊은 남자와 일을 매듭지어 줄 사랑스러운 엄마가 없다면, 그 아가씨는 스스로 그 일을 해야 하는 법이다."], ["And oh, what a mercy it is that these women do not exercise their powers oftener!", "그러니 아, 이 여인들이 그 힘을 더 자주 휘두르지 않는다는 것은 얼마나 다행한 일인가!"], ["We can't resist them, if they do.", "만일 그들이 그 힘을 쓴다면 우리는 도무지 당해 낼 재간이 없으니 말이다."], ["Let them show ever so little inclination, and men go down on their knees at once: old or ugly, it is all the same.", "그들이 아주 조금이라도 끌리는 기색을 내비치기만 하면 남자들은 당장 무릎을 꿇는다. 늙었든 못생겼든 매한가지다."], ["And this I set down as a positive truth.", "그리고 나는 이것을 명백한 진리로 적어 둔다."], ["A woman with fair opportunities, and without an absolute hump, may marry WHOM SHE LIKES.", "웬만한 기회를 갖춘 여자라면, 그것도 등에 두드러진 혹만 없다면, 자기가 원하는 남자라면 누구든 결혼할 수 있는 것이다."], ["Only let us be thankful that the darlings are like the beasts of the field, and don't know their own power.", "다만 이 사랑스러운 존재들이 들판의 짐승들처럼 제 힘을 알지 못한다는 것에 감사할 따름이다."], ["They would overcome us entirely if they did.", "만일 그들이 그것을 안다면 우리를 완전히 정복하고 말 것이다."]], [["\"Egad!\"", "\"세상에!\""], ["thought Joseph, entering the dining-room, \"I exactly begin to feel as I did at Dumdum with Miss Cutler.\"", "조지프가 식당으로 들어서며 생각했다. \"딱 덤덤에서 커틀러 양과 있을 때 같은 기분이 들기 시작하는걸.\""], ["Many sweet little appeals, half tender, half jocular, did Miss Sharp make to him about the dishes at dinner; for by this time she was on a footing of considerable familiarity with the family, and as for the girls, they loved each other like sisters.", "샤프 양은 저녁 식탁에서 음식을 두고 절반은 다정하고 절반은 익살스러운 사랑스러운 응석을 그에게 숱하게 부렸다. 이즈음 그녀는 이미 그 집안과 상당히 허물없는 사이가 되어 있었고, 두 아가씨로 말하자면 자매처럼 서로를 아꼈다."], ["Young unmarried girls always do, if they are in a house together for ten days.", "결혼 안 한 젊은 처녀들이란 열흘만 한집에 함께 지내면 으레 그렇게 되는 법이다."]], [["As if bent upon advancing Rebecca's plans in every way--what must Amelia do, but remind her brother of a promise made last Easter holidays--\"When I was a girl at school,\"", "마치 무슨 수를 써서라도 레베카의 계획을 진척시키려고 작정이라도 한 듯이—애밀리아가 한 일이라곤 다름 아니라 지난 부활절 휴가 때 오빠가 한 약속을 일깨워 준 것이었으니, \"내가 학교 다니는 계집애였을 때 말이에요.\" 그녀가 웃으며 말했다."], ["said she, laughing--a promise that he, Joseph, would take her to Vauxhall. \"Now,\"", "다름 아니라 그, 조지프가 자기를 보크홀에 데려가 주겠다고 한 약속이었다."], ["she said, \"that Rebecca is with us, will be the very time.\"", "\"이제 레베카가 우리와 함께 있으니, 지금이 바로 딱 좋은 때예요.\" 그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"O, delightful!\"", "\"어머, 신난다!\""], ["said Rebecca, going to clap her hands; but she recollected herself, and paused, like a modest creature, as she was.", "레베카가 손뼉을 치려다가, 정숙한 여인답게 제풀에 정신을 차리고 멈칫했다."]], [["\"To-night is not the night,\"", "\"오늘 밤은 갈 때가 아니지.\""], ["said Joe.", "조가 말했다."]], [["\"Well, to-morrow.\"", "\"그럼 내일이요.\""]], [["\"To-morrow your Papa and I dine out,\"", "\"내일은 네 아버지하고 내가 밖에서 저녁을 먹는단다.\""], ["said Mrs. Sedley.", "세들리 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"You don't suppose that I'm going, Mrs. Sed?\"", "\"설마 내가 간다고 생각하는 건 아니겠지, 세들리 부인?\""], ["said her husband, \"and that a woman of your years and size is to catch cold, in such an abominable damp place?\"", "그녀의 남편이 말했다. \"당신 나이에 그 덩치로, 그렇게 끔찍하게 눅눅한 곳에서 감기에 걸리란 말이오?\""]], [["\"The children must have someone with them,\"", "\"애들한테는 누가 같이 있어 줘야지요.\""], ["cried Mrs. Sedley.", "세들리 부인이 외쳤다."]], [["\"Let Joe go,\"", "\"조더러 가라지.\""], ["said-his father, laughing.", "그녀의 남편이 웃으며 말했다."], ["\"He's big enough.\"", "\"충분히 컸으니까.\""], ["At which speech even Mr. Sambo at the sideboard burst out laughing, and poor fat Joe felt inclined to become a parricide almost.", "이 말에 식기대 앞에 서 있던 샘보까지 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 가엾은 뚱뚱보 조는 그야말로 아비를 죽이고 싶은 심정이 되었다."]], [["\"Undo his stays!\"", "\"코르셋을 풀어 줘라!\""], ["continued the pitiless old gentleman.", "인정사정없는 노신사가 계속 말했다."], ["\"Fling some water in his face, Miss Sharp, or carry him upstairs: the dear creature's fainting.", "\"샤프 양, 얼굴에 물이나 좀 끼얹어 주든지, 아니면 위층으로 데리고 올라가구려. 이 사랑스러운 양반이 기절하시는구먼."], ["Poor victim!", "가엾은 희생양 같으니!"], ["carry him up; he's as light as a feather!\"", "위층으로 모셔 가요. 깃털처럼 가벼우니까!\""]], [["\"If I stand this, sir, I'm d------!\"", "\"이걸 참으면, 제가, 제가 사람이 아닙니다!\""], ["roared Joseph.", "조지프가 고함을 질렀다."]], [["\"Order Mr. Jos's elephant, Sambo!\"", "\"샘보, 조스 도련님 코끼리를 대령하렷다!\""], ["cried the father.", "아버지가 외쳤다."], ["\"Send to Exeter 'Change, Sambo\"; but seeing Jos ready almost to cry with vexation, the old joker stopped his laughter, and said, holding out his hand to his son, \"It's all fair on the Stock Exchange, Jos--and, Sambo, never mind the elephant, but give me and Mr.", "\"엑서터 체인지로 사람을 보내라, 샘보.\" 그러나 화가 치밀어 거의 울 지경이 된 조스를 보더니, 이 늙은 익살꾼은 웃음을 멈추고 아들에게 손을 내밀며 말했다."], ["Jos a glass of Champagne.", "\"증권 거래소에서야 다 정당한 거래지, 조스."], ["Boney himself hasn't got such in his cellar, my boy!\"", "그리고 샘보, 코끼리는 됐고, 나하고 조스 도련님한테 샴페인이나 한 잔씩 다오. 보니 그 작자라도 제 술 창고에 이런 건 없을 게다, 얘야!\""]], [["A goblet of Champagne restored Joseph's equanimity, and before the bottle was emptied, of which as an invalid he took two-thirds, he had agreed to take the young ladies to Vauxhall.", "샴페인 한 잔이 조지프의 평정을 되찾아 주었고, 환자랍시고 그가 삼분의 이를 마셔 버린 그 병이 비기도 전에, 그는 두 아가씨를 보크홀에 데려가기로 합의했다."]], [["\"The girls must have a gentleman apiece,\"", "\"여자애들한테는 각각 신사가 하나씩 붙어야지.\""], ["said the old gentleman.", "노신사가 말했다."], ["\"Jos will be sure to leave Emmy in the crowd, he will be so taken up with Miss Sharp here.", "\"조스는 여기 샤프 양한테 정신이 팔려서 분명 사람들 틈에 에미를 내버려 둘 게 뻔하니까."], ["Send to 96, and ask George Osborne if he'll come.\"", "96번지로 사람을 보내서 조지 오스본더러 와 줄 수 있는지 물어보거라.\""]], [["At this, I don't know in the least for what reason, Mrs.", "이 말에, 무슨 영문인지 나로서는 도무지 알 수 없지만, 세들리 부인이 남편을 바라보며 웃었다."], ["Sedley looked at her husband and laughed. Mr.", "세들리 씨의 두 눈은 형언할 수 없으리만치 짓궂게 반짝였고, 그는 애밀리아를 바라보았다."], ["Sedley's eyes twinkled in a manner indescribably roguish, and he looked at Amelia; and Amelia, hanging down her head, blushed as only young ladies of seventeen know how to blush, and as Miss Rebecca Sharp never blushed in her life--at least not since she was eight years old, and when she was caught stealing jam out of a cupboard by her godmother.", "그러자 애밀리아는 고개를 떨군 채, 열일곱 살 아가씨만이 짓는 법을 아는 그런 식으로 얼굴을 붉혔으니, 레베카 샤프 양은 평생 그렇게 얼굴을 붉혀 본 적이 없었다—적어도 여덟 살 적, 대모에게 찬장에서 잼을 훔쳐 먹다 들켰던 그 이후로는 말이다."], ["\"Amelia had better write a note,\"", "\"애밀리아가 쪽지를 쓰는 게 좋겠구나.\""], ["said her father; \"and let George Osborne see what a beautiful handwriting we have brought back from Miss Pinkerton's.", "그녀의 아버지가 말했다. \"우리가 핑커튼 여사님 학원에서 얼마나 아름다운 필체를 배워 왔는지 조지 오스본한테 보여 주려무나."], ["Do you remember when you wrote to him to come on Twelfth-night, Emmy, and spelt twelfth without the f?\"", "에미, 그 애한테 십이야에 오라고 편지 쓰면서 'twelfth'를 f 없이 적었던 거 기억하니?\""]], [["\"That was years ago,\"", "\"그건 한참 전 일이잖아요.\""], ["said Amelia.", "애밀리아가 말했다."]], [["\"It seems like yesterday, don't it, John?\" said Mrs.", "\"꼭 어제 일 같지 않소, 존?\""], ["Sedley to her husband; and that night in a conversation which took place in a front room in the second floor, in a sort of tent, hung round with chintz of a rich and fantastic India pattern, and double with calico of a tender rose-colour; in the interior of which species of marquee was a featherbed, on which were two pillows, on which were two round red faces, one in a laced nightcap, and one in a simple cotton one, ending in a tassel--in a CURTAIN LECTURE, I say, Mrs.", "세들리 부인이 남편에게 말했다. 그리고 그날 밤, 이층 앞방에서 나눈 한 대화 속에서—그 방은 화려하고 기묘한 인도풍 무늬의 사라사 천을 두르고 그 안쪽으로 연한 장밋빛 옥양목을 덧대어 일종의 천막처럼 꾸며져 있었고, 그 천막 같은 것 안에는 깃털 침대가 있었으며, 그 위에는 베개 두 개가, 그 위에는 둥글고 불그레한 두 얼굴이 놓여 있었으니, 하나는 레이스 달린 나이트캡을, 다른 하나는 끝에 술이 달린 수수한 무명 모자를 쓰고 있었다—그러니까 이 '잠자리 잔소리'"], ["Sedley took her husband to task for his cruel conduct to poor Joe.", "속에서, 세들리 부인은 가엾은 조에게 모질게 군 일을 두고 남편을 나무랐다."]], [["\"It was quite wicked of you, Mr.", "\"정말이지 당신도 너무하셨어요, 세들리 씨.\""], ["Sedley,\"", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, \"to torment the poor boy so.\"", "\"그 가엾은 애를 그렇게나 들들 볶다니요.\""]], [["\"My dear,\"", "\"여보.\""], ["said the cotton-tassel in defence of his conduct, \"Jos is a great deal vainer than you ever were in your life, and that's saying a good deal.", "무명 술 모자가 자기 행동을 변호하며 말했다. \"조스는 당신이 평생 그랬던 것보다 훨씬 더 허영이 심한 녀석이오."], ["Though, some thirty years ago, in the year seventeen hundred and eighty--what was it?--perhaps you had a right to be vain--I don't say no.", "이건 보통 말이 아니지. 하긴 한 삼십 년 전, 천칠백팔십—그게 몇 년이었더라?—그 무렵에야 당신도 우쭐할 만했을지 모르겠소."], ["But I've no patience with Jos and his dandified modesty.", "아니라고는 안 하겠소. 하지만 조스와 그 멋쟁이 티 내는 수줍음에는 도무지 참을 수가 없단 말이오."], ["It is out-Josephing Joseph, my dear, and all the while the boy is only thinking of himself, and what a fine fellow he is.", "그건 요셉보다 더 요셉다운 척하는 짓이오, 여보. 그러면서 내내 그 녀석은 제 생각만 하면서, 자기가 얼마나 잘난 사내인지에만 빠져 있다오."], ["I doubt, Ma'am, we shall have some trouble with him yet.", "여보, 우리가 그 애 때문에 골치깨나 썩게 될 것 같소."], ["Here is Emmy's little friend making love to him as hard as she can; that's quite clear; and if she does not catch him some other will.", "보시오, 에미의 그 어린 친구가 죽기 살기로 그 녀석한테 추파를 던지고 있잖소. 그건 너무도 빤한 일이지."], ["That man is destined to be a prey to woman, as I am to go on 'Change every day.", "저 애가 그 녀석을 낚지 못하면 다른 누군가가 낚을 거요. 내가 날마다 증권 거래소에 나가게 되어 있듯이, 그 사내는 여자의 먹잇감이 될 운명이라오."], ["It's a mercy he did not bring us over a black daughter-in-law, my dear.", "그 녀석이 우리한테 흑인 며느리를 데려오지 않은 것만도 다행이오, 여보. 하지만 내 말 새겨들으시오."], ["But, mark my words, the first woman who fishes for him, hooks him.\"", "그 녀석한테 처음 낚싯대를 던지는 여자가 그 녀석을 낚고 말 거요.\""]], [["\"She shall go off to-morrow, the little artful creature,\"", "\"내일로 당장 내보내야겠어요, 그 음흉한 계집애를.\""], ["said Mrs. Sedley, with great energy.", "세들리 부인이 잔뜩 기를 쓰며 말했다."]], [["\"Why not she as well as another, Mrs. Sedley?", "\"다른 누구라고 안 될 거 뭐 있소, 세들리 부인?"], ["The girl's a white face at any rate.", "그 애가 그래도 살결은 흰 얼굴이잖소."], ["I don't care who marries him.", "누가 그 녀석과 결혼하든 난 상관없소."], ["Let Joe please himself.\"", "조더러 제 좋을 대로 하라지.\""]], [["And presently the voices of the two speakers were hushed, or were replaced by the gentle but unromantic music of the nose; and save when the church bells tolled the hour and the watchman called it, all was silent at the house of John Sedley, Esquire, of Russell Square, and the Stock Exchange.", "이윽고 두 사람의 목소리가 잦아들거나, 부드럽지만 결코 낭만적이지 않은 코의 음악으로 바뀌었다. 그리하여 교회 종이 시간을 알리고 야경꾼이 그것을 외칠 때를 빼면, 러셀 광장과 증권 거래소의 존 세들리 귀하 댁은 온통 고요했다."]], [["When morning came, the good-natured Mrs.", "아침이 오자, 마음씨 고운 세들리 부인은 더 이상 샤프 양에 관한 자신의 으름장을 실행에 옮길 생각을 하지 않았다."], ["Sedley no longer thought of executing her threats with regard to Miss Sharp; for though nothing is more keen, nor more common, nor more justifiable, than maternal jealousy, yet she could not bring herself to suppose that the little, humble, grateful, gentle governess would dare to look up to such a magnificent personage as the Collector of Boggley Wollah.", "어머니의 질투란 더없이 날카롭고, 더없이 흔하며, 더없이 정당한 것이긴 하나, 그토록 작고 비천하고 고분고분하고 온순한 가정교사가 보글리 월라의 징세관이라는 그토록 으리으리한 인물을 감히 우러러볼 엄두를 내리라고는 도무지 상상할 수가 없었던 것이다."], ["The petition, too, for an extension of the young lady's leave of absence had already been despatched, and it would be difficult to find a pretext for abruptly dismissing her.", "게다가 그 아가씨의 휴가를 연장해 달라는 청원서도 이미 발송된 터라, 그녀를 느닷없이 내쫓을 구실을 찾기란 어려운 노릇이었다."]], [["And as if all things conspired in favour of the gentle Rebecca, the very elements (although she was not inclined at first to acknowledge their action in her behalf) interposed to aid her.", "그리고 마치 만물이 온순한 레베카의 편을 들기로 모의라도 한 듯이, 자연의 요소들마저(처음에 그녀는 그것들이 자기를 위해 나섰음을 인정하려 들지 않았지만) 끼어들어 그녀를 거들었다."], ["For on the evening appointed for the Vauxhall party, George Osborne having come to dinner, and the elders of the house having departed, according to invitation, to dine with Alderman Balls at Highbury Barn, there came on such a thunder-storm as only happens on Vauxhall nights, and as obliged the young people, perforce, to remain at home.", "보크홀 모임으로 정해진 날 저녁, 조지 오스본이 저녁을 먹으러 왔고, 그 집의 어른들은 초대받은 대로 하이버리 반에서 볼스 시의원과 저녁을 먹으러 떠난 뒤였는데, 오로지 보크홀에 가는 날 밤에만 일어날 법한 그런 천둥 번개를 동반한 폭풍우가 닥쳐와 젊은이들이 어쩔 수 없이 집에 머물러야 했던 것이다. 오스본 씨는 이 일에 조금도 실망한 기색이 아니었다."], ["Mr. Osborne did not seem in the least disappointed at this occurrence.", "그와 조지프 세들리는 식당에서 단둘이 적당한 양의 포도주를 마셨고, 그 술을 마시는 동안 세들리는 자기의 가장 멋진 인도 이야기들을 한 보따리 풀어놓았다."], ["He and Joseph Sedley drank a fitting quantity of port-wine, tete-a-tete, in the dining-room, during the drinking of which Sedley told a number of his best Indian stories; for he was extremely talkative in man's society; and afterwards Miss Amelia Sedley did the honours of the drawing-room; and these four young persons passed such a comfortable evening together, that they declared they were rather glad of the thunder-storm than otherwise, which had caused them to put off their visit to Vauxhall.", "그는 남자들끼리 있을 때면 더없이 말이 많았기 때문이다. 그 뒤에는 애밀리아 세들리 양이 응접실에서 손님을 접대했고, 이 네 젊은이는 함께 어찌나 아늑한 저녁을 보냈던지, 보크홀 나들이를 미루게 만든 그 천둥 폭풍우가 차라리 다행스럽다고들 입을 모았다."]], [["Osborne was Sedley's godson, and had been one of the family any time these three-and-twenty years.", "오스본은 세들리의 대자였고, 지난 스물세 해 동안 줄곧 이 집안의 일원이나 다름없었다."], ["At six weeks old, he had received from John Sedley a present of a silver cup; at six months old, a coral with gold whistle and bells; from his youth upwards he was \"tipped\"", "생후 육 주에 그는 존 세들리에게서 은잔을 선물로 받았고, 생후 육 개월에는 금 호루라기와 방울이 달린 산호를 받았다. 어린 시절 내내 크리스마스 때면 노신사에게서 어김없이 용돈을 \"받았다.\""], ["regularly by the old gentleman at Christmas: and on going back to school, he remembered perfectly well being thrashed by Joseph Sedley, when the latter was a big, swaggering hobbadyhoy, and George an impudent urchin of ten years old.", "그리고 학교로 돌아갈 때면, 조지프 세들리가 덩치 크고 으스대는 멋대가리 없는 풋내기였고 조지가 열 살배기 건방진 개구쟁이였던 시절, 그에게 두들겨 맞았던 일을 그는 똑똑히 기억했다."], ["In a word, George was as familiar with the family as such daily acts of kindness and intercourse could make him.", "한마디로, 조지는 그런 날마다의 친절과 왕래로 빚어질 수 있는 만큼 그 집안과 허물없는 사이였다."]], [["\"Do you remember, Sedley, what a fury you were in, when I cut off the tassels of your Hessian boots, and how Miss--hem!--how Amelia rescued me from a beating, by falling down on her knees and crying out to her brother Jos, not to beat little George?\"", "\"세들리, 기억나요? 내가 당신 헤시안 부츠의 술을 잘라 버렸을 때 당신이 얼마나 펄펄 뛰었는지, 그리고 그—흠!—애밀리아가 무릎을 꿇고 오빠 조스한테 어린 조지를 때리지 말라고 울며 빌어서 날 매질에서 구해 줬던 거?\""]], [["Jos remembered this remarkable circumstance perfectly well, but vowed that he had totally forgotten it.", "조스는 이 잊지 못할 사건을 똑똑히 기억했지만, 까맣게 잊어버렸노라고 우겨 댔다."]], [["\"Well, do you remember coming down in a gig to Dr. Swishtail's to see me, before you went to India, and giving me half a guinea and a pat on the head?", "\"그럼, 당신이 인도로 떠나기 전에 이륜마차를 타고 스위시테일 박사님 학교로 날 보러 와서, 반 기니랑 머리 한 번 쓰다듬어 주고 갔던 건 기억나요?"], ["I always had an idea that you were at least seven feet high, and was quite astonished at your return from India to find you no taller than myself.\"", "난 늘 당신이 적어도 칠 피트는 될 거라고 생각했는데, 인도에서 돌아온 당신이 나보다 키가 크지도 않은 걸 보고 어찌나 놀랐는지.\""]], [["\"How good of Mr. Sedley to go to your school and give you the money!\"", "\"세들리 씨가 당신 학교에까지 찾아가서 용돈을 주시다니 어쩜 그렇게 자상하실까!\""], ["exclaimed Rebecca, in accents of extreme delight.", "레베카가 더없이 기쁜 어조로 외쳤다."]], [["\"Yes, and after I had cut the tassels of his boots too.", "\"그래요, 그것도 내가 그 양반 부츠 술을 잘라 버린 뒤에 말이오."], ["Boys never forget those tips at school, nor the givers.\"", "사내애들은 학교에서 받은 그런 용돈도, 준 사람도 결코 잊지 않는 법이지.\""]], [["\"I delight in Hessian boots,\"", "\"전 헤시안 부츠가 참 좋더라고요.\""], ["said Rebecca.", "레베카가 말했다."], ["Jos Sedley, who admired his own legs prodigiously, and always wore this ornamental chaussure, was extremely pleased at this remark, though he drew his legs under his chair as it was made.", "제 다리를 엄청나게 자랑스러워해서 늘 이 멋들어진 신발을 신고 다니던 조스 세들리는, 비록 그 신발을 신은 채 다리를 의자 밑으로 끌어들이긴 했어도, 이 말에 더없이 흐뭇해했다."]], [["\"Miss Sharp!\"", "\"샤프 양!\""], ["said George Osborne, \"you who are so clever an artist, you must make a grand historical picture of the scene of the boots.", "조지 오스본이 말했다. \"당신처럼 그토록 솜씨 좋은 화가시라면, 그 부츠 장면을 멋진 역사화로 그려야 해요."], ["Sedley shall be represented in buckskins, and holding one of the injured boots in one hand; by the other he shall have hold of my shirt-frill.", "세들리는 사슴 가죽 바지를 입고 한 손에는 그 수난당한 부츠 한 짝을 들고 있는 모습으로 그리고, 다른 한 손으로는 내 셔츠 주름 장식을 움켜쥐고 있게 하는 거죠."], ["Amelia shall be kneeling near him, with her little hands up; and the picture shall have a grand allegorical title, as the frontispieces have in the Medulla and the spelling-book.\"", "애밀리아는 그 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 작은 두 손을 치켜든 채로 그리고요. 그리고 『메둘라』나 철자 교본의 권두 삽화처럼, 그 그림엔 거창한 우의적 제목을 붙이는 거예요.\""]], [["\"I shan't have time to do it here,\"", "\"여기서는 그릴 시간이 없을 거예요.\""], ["said Rebecca.", "레베카가 말했다."], ["\"I'll do it when--when I'm gone.\"", "\"제가, 제가 떠나고 나면 그리지요.\""], ["And she dropped her voice, and looked so sad and piteous, that everybody felt how cruel her lot was, and how sorry they would be to part with her.", "그러고는 목소리를 낮추며 어찌나 슬프고 애처로운 표정을 짓던지, 모두가 그녀의 처지가 얼마나 모진지, 또 그녀와 헤어지는 일이 얼마나 서글플지를 절감했다."]], [["\"O that you could stay longer, dear Rebecca,\"", "\"네가 좀 더 머물 수 있으면 좋겠다, 레베카.\""], ["said Amelia.", "애밀리아가 말했다."]], [["\"Why?\"", "\"왜?\""], ["answered the other, still more sadly.", "다른 쪽이 한층 더 서글프게 대답했다."], ["\"That I may be only the more unhap--unwilling to lose you?\"", "\"그러면 그저 더 불행—너와 헤어지기가 더 싫어지기밖에 더 하겠어?\""], ["And she turned away her head.", "그러고는 고개를 돌렸다."], ["Amelia began to give way to that natural infirmity of tears which, we have said, was one of the defects of this silly little thing.", "애밀리아는, 우리가 말했다시피 이 어리석은 어린 것의 결함 중 하나인 그 타고난 눈물병에 굴복하기 시작했다."], ["George Osborne looked at the two young women with a touched curiosity; and Joseph Sedley heaved something very like a sigh out of his big chest, as he cast his eyes down towards his favourite Hessian boots.", "조지 오스본은 마음이 움직인 듯한 호기심으로 두 젊은 여인을 바라보았고, 조지프 세들리는 자기가 가장 아끼는 헤시안 부츠로 눈길을 떨구며 그 널따란 가슴에서 한숨과도 같은 무언가를 토해 냈다."]], [["\"Let us have some music, Miss Sedley--Amelia,\"", "\"음악이나 좀 들읍시다, 세들리 양—애밀리아.\" 조지가 말했다."], ["said George, who felt at that moment an extraordinary, almost irresistible impulse to seize the above-mentioned young woman in his arms, and to kiss her in the face of the company; and she looked at him for a moment, and if I should say that they fell in love with each other at that single instant of time, I should perhaps be telling an untruth, for the fact is that these two young people had been bred up by their parents for this very purpose, and their banns had, as it were, been read in their respective families any time these ten years.", "그는 그 순간 앞서 말한 그 젊은 여인을 두 팔로 끌어안고 좌중이 보는 앞에서 그 얼굴에 입을 맞추고 싶은, 거의 거부할 수 없는 유별난 충동을 느꼈던 것이다. 그녀가 한순간 그를 바라보았는데, 만일 내가 두 사람이 바로 그 찰나에 서로 사랑에 빠졌다고 말한다면 아마도 거짓을 고하는 셈이 될 것이다."], ["They went off to the piano, which was situated, as pianos usually are, in the back drawing-room; and as it was rather dark, Miss Amelia, in the most unaffected way in the world, put her hand into Mr.", "사실인즉 이 두 젊은이는 다름 아닌 바로 이 목적을 위해 부모 손에 길러졌고, 말하자면 그들의 결혼 예고는 지난 십 년간 줄곧 양가에서 낭독되어 온 것이나 다름없었으니 말이다. 그들은 피아노로 갔는데, 그것은 피아노가 으레 그렇듯 안쪽 응접실에 놓여 있었다."], ["Osborne's, who, of course, could see the way among the chairs and ottomans a great deal better than she could. But this arrangement left Mr.", "그리고 안이 다소 어두웠던 터라, 애밀리아 양은 세상에 둘도 없이 천연덕스러운 태도로 오스본 씨의 손에 제 손을 맡겼는데, 그야 물론 그가 그녀보다 의자며 긴 의자 사이의 길을 훨씬 더 잘 분간했기 때문이다."], ["Joseph Sedley tete-a-tete with Rebecca, at the drawing-room table, where the latter was occupied in knitting a green silk purse.", "그러나 이 배치 덕분에 조지프 세들리 씨는 응접실 탁자 곁에서 레베카와 단둘이 마주 앉게 되었고, 그녀는 거기서 초록빛 비단 지갑을 뜨고 있었다."]], [["\"There is no need to ask family secrets,\"", "\"집안 비밀이야 물어볼 것도 없네요.\""], ["said Miss Sharp.", "샤프 양이 말했다."], ["\"Those two have told theirs.\"", "\"저 두 분은 자기들 비밀을 다 털어놓았으니까요.\""]], [["\"As soon as he gets his company,\"", "\"저 친구가 중대장만 되면,\""], ["said Joseph, \"I believe the affair is settled.", "조지프가 말했다. \"그 일은 정해진 거나 다름없지요."], ["George Osborne is a capital fellow.\"", "조지 오스본은 더할 나위 없이 멋진 사내요.\""]], [["\"And your sister the dearest creature in the world,\"", "\"그리고 당신 누이동생은 세상에 둘도 없이 사랑스러운 사람이지요.\""], ["said Rebecca.", "레베카가 말했다."], ["\"Happy the man who wins her!\"", "\"그녀를 얻는 사람은 행복할 거예요!\""], ["With this, Miss Sharp gave a great sigh.", "이 말과 함께 샤프 양은 깊은 한숨을 내쉬었다."]], [["When two unmarried persons get together, and talk upon such delicate subjects as the present, a great deal of confidence and intimacy is presently established between them.", "미혼의 두 사람이 한자리에 모여 지금처럼 그런 미묘한 화제를 두고 이야기를 나누다 보면, 이내 둘 사이에는 상당한 신뢰와 친밀감이 자리 잡기 마련이다. 이제 세들리 씨와 그 젊은 아가씨 사이에 오간 대화를 굳이 특별히 보고할 필요는 없겠다."], ["There is no need of giving a special report of the conversation which now took place between Mr.", "앞의 본보기에서 짐작할 수 있듯, 그 대화는 딱히 재치 있다거나 능변이라 할 만한 것이 아니었으니 말이다."], ["Sedley and the young lady; for the conversation, as may be judged from the foregoing specimen, was not especially witty or eloquent; it seldom is in private societies, or anywhere except in very high-flown and ingenious novels.", "사사로운 자리에서나, 아니 매우 과장되고 기발한 소설을 제외한 그 어디에서도, 대화란 좀처럼 그렇지 못한 법이다. 옆방에서 음악이 흐르고 있던 터라, 두 사람의 이야기는 물론 낮고 알맞은 어조로 이어졌다."], ["As there was music in the next room, the talk was carried on, of course, in a low and becoming tone, though, for the matter of that, the couple in the next apartment would not have been disturbed had the talking been ever so loud, so occupied were they with their own pursuits.", "하지만 그건 그렇다 치고, 옆방의 한 쌍은 아무리 큰 소리로 떠들었어도 방해받지 않았을 것이다. 그만큼 그들은 제 일에 빠져 있었으니 말이다."]], [["Almost for the first time in his life, Mr. Sedley found himself talking, without the least timidity or hesitation, to a person of the other sex.", "세들리 씨는 평생 거의 처음으로, 조금의 수줍음이나 머뭇거림도 없이 이성과 이야기를 나누는 자신을 발견했다."], ["Miss Rebecca asked him a great number of questions about India, which gave him an opportunity of narrating many interesting anecdotes about that country and himself.", "레베카 양은 인도에 관해 그에게 숱하게 질문을 던졌고, 덕분에 그는 그 나라와 자신에 관한 흥미로운 일화들을 여럿 늘어놓을 기회를 얻었다."], ["He described the balls at Government House, and the manner in which they kept themselves cool in the hot weather, with punkahs, tatties, and other contrivances; and he was very witty regarding the number of Scotchmen whom Lord Minto, the Governor-General, patronised; and then he described a tiger-hunt; and the manner in which the mahout of his elephant had been pulled off his seat by one of the infuriated animals.", "그는 총독 관저의 무도회며, 부채와 갈대 발과 그 밖의 갖가지 장치로 더운 날씨에 몸을 시원하게 유지하던 방식을 묘사했다. 또한 총독인 민토 경이 비호한 스코틀랜드인의 수에 관해 자못 재치 있게 떠들어 댔고, 그러고는 호랑이 사냥과, 자기가 탄 코끼리의 몰이꾼이 성난 짐승 한 마리에게 자리에서 끌려 떨어진 광경을 묘사했다."], ["How delighted Miss Rebecca was at the Government balls, and how she laughed at the stories of the Scotch aides-de-camp, and called Mr. Sedley a sad wicked satirical creature; and how frightened she was at the story of the elephant!", "레베카 양은 그 총독 관저 무도회 이야기에 어찌나 흐뭇해하고, 스코틀랜드인 부관들 이야기에 어찌나 깔깔거리며 세들리 씨를 짓궂고 못된 풍자꾼이라고 불러 댔던지, 또 코끼리 이야기에는 어찌나 겁을 집어먹었던지!"], ["\"For your mother's sake, dear Mr. Sedley,\"", "\"어머님을 봐서라도, 세들리 씨,\""], ["she said, \"for the sake of all your friends, promise NEVER to go on one of those horrid expeditions.\"", "그녀가 말했다. \"당신의 모든 벗들을 봐서라도, 그런 끔찍한 원정에는 다시는 절대 나서지 않겠다고 약속해 주세요.\""]], [["\"Pooh, pooh, Miss Sharp,\"", "\"쳇, 쳇, 샤프 양.\""], ["said he, pulling up his shirt-collars; \"the danger makes the sport only the pleasanter.\"", "그가 셔츠 깃을 추켜올리며 말했다. \"위험이 있어야 사냥이 그만큼 더 재미있는 법이오.\""], ["He had never been but once at a tiger-hunt, when the accident in question occurred, and when he was half killed--not by the tiger, but by the fright.", "그는 평생 호랑이 사냥에 딱 한 번 나가 보았을 뿐인데, 바로 그때 문제의 그 사고가 일어났고, 그는 거의 죽을 뻔했다—호랑이 때문이 아니라 겁에 질려서 말이다."], ["And as he talked on, he grew quite bold, and actually had the audacity to ask Miss Rebecca for whom she was knitting the green silk purse?", "그리고 이야기를 이어 가는 동안 그는 자못 대담해져서, 급기야 레베카 양에게 그 초록빛 비단 지갑은 누구를 위해 뜨고 있느냐고 물어볼 만큼 뻔뻔해졌다."], ["He was quite surprised and delighted at his own graceful familiar manner.", "그는 자신의 그 우아하고 허물없는 태도에 스스로도 적잖이 놀라고 흐뭇해했다."]], [["\"For any one who wants a purse,\"", "\"지갑이 필요한 누군가를 위해서요.\""], ["replied Miss Rebecca, looking at him in the most gentle winning way.", "레베카 양이 더없이 부드럽고 마음을 끄는 태도로 그를 바라보며 대답했다."], ["Sedley was going to make one of the most eloquent speeches possible, and had begun--\"O Miss Sharp, how--\"", "세들리는 더없이 능란한 연설을 한바탕 늘어놓으려고 \"아, 샤프 양, 어쩌면……\""], ["when some song which was performed in the other room came to an end, and caused him to hear his own voice so distinctly that he stopped, blushed, and blew his nose in great agitation.", "하고 막 입을 떼었는데, 그때 옆방에서 부르던 노래가 끝나는 바람에 제 목소리가 너무도 또렷이 들려, 그는 그만 말을 멈추고 얼굴을 붉히더니 잔뜩 당황한 채 코를 풀었다."]], [["\"Did you ever hear anything like your brother's eloquence?\"", "\"당신 오빠의 능변 같은 거 들어 본 적 있어요?\""], ["whispered Mr. Osborne to Amelia.", "오스본 씨가 애밀리아에게 속삭였다."], ["\"Why, your friend has worked miracles.\"", "\"당신 친구가 기적을 일으켰군요.\""]], [["\"The more the better,\"", "\"기적은 많을수록 좋지요.\""], ["said Miss Amelia; who, like almost all women who are worth a pin, was a match-maker in her heart, and would have been delighted that Joseph should carry back a wife to India.", "애밀리아 양이 말했다. 그녀는 핀만 한 값어치라도 있는 거의 모든 여자가 그렇듯 마음속으로는 중매쟁이였고, 조지프가 인도로 아내를 데려간다면 더없이 기뻤을 것이다."], ["She had, too, in the course of this few days' constant intercourse, warmed into a most tender friendship for Rebecca, and discovered a million of virtues and amiable qualities in her which she had not perceived when they were at Chiswick together.", "게다가 그녀는 이 며칠간의 끊임없는 왕래 속에서 레베카를 향한 더없이 다정한 우정에 흠뻑 젖어들었고, 함께 치즈윅에 있을 때는 미처 알아보지 못했던 백만 가지 미덕과 사랑스러운 자질들을 그녀에게서 발견했다."], ["For the affection of young ladies is of as rapid growth as Jack's bean-stalk, and reaches up to the sky in a night.", "젊은 아가씨들의 애정이란 잭의 콩나무처럼 빠르게 자라나 하룻밤 새 하늘까지 닿는 법이니까."], ["It is no blame to them that after marriage this Sehnsucht nach der Liebe subsides.", "결혼 후에 이 '사랑을 향한 그리움'이 가라앉는다 해도 그들을 탓할 일은 아니다."], ["It is what sentimentalists, who deal in very big words, call a yearning after the Ideal, and simply means that women are commonly not satisfied until they have husbands and children on whom they may centre affections, which are spent elsewhere, as it were, in small change.", "그것은 대단히 거창한 낱말을 즐겨 쓰는 감상주의자들이 이상을 향한 갈망이라 일컫는 것이며, 쉽게 말하자면 여자들이란 대개 자신의 애정을—말하자면 다른 데서 잔돈처럼 쓰던 그 애정을—쏟아부을 남편과 아이가 생기기 전에는 좀처럼 만족하지 못한다는 뜻일 따름이다."]], [["Having expended her little store of songs, or having stayed long enough in the back drawing-room, it now appeared proper to Miss Amelia to ask her friend to sing.", "자기가 가진 얼마 안 되는 노래 밑천을 다 써 버렸든, 아니면 안쪽 응접실에 충분히 오래 머물렀든, 이제 애밀리아 양에게는 친구에게 노래를 청하는 것이 마땅해 보였다."], ["\"You would not have listened to me,\" she said to Mr.", "\"당신은 제 노래엔 귀 기울이지 않으셨을 거예요.\""], ["Osborne (though she knew she was telling a fib), \"had you heard Rebecca first.\"", "그녀가 오스본 씨에게 말했다(그게 거짓말인 줄 그녀도 알면서 말이다). \"레베카 노래를 먼저 들으셨더라면요.\""]], [["\"I give Miss Sharp warning, though,\"", "\"하지만 샤프 양께 미리 일러두겠는데,\""], ["said Osborne, \"that, right or wrong, I consider Miss Amelia Sedley the first singer in the world.\"", "오스본이 말했다. \"옳든 그르든, 나는 애밀리아 세들리 양이 세상에서 으뜸가는 가수라고 여깁니다.\""]], [["\"You shall hear,\"", "\"들어 보세요.\""], ["said Amelia; and Joseph Sedley was actually polite enough to carry the candles to the piano.", "애밀리아가 말했고, 조지프 세들리는 실제로 촛대를 피아노까지 날라다 줄 만큼 정중하게 굴었다."], ["Osborne hinted that he should like quite as well to sit in the dark; but Miss Sedley, laughing, declined to bear him company any farther, and the two accordingly followed Mr. Joseph.", "오스본은 어둠 속에 앉아 있어도 그에 못지않게 좋겠다고 넌지시 비쳤지만, 세들리 양은 웃으며 더 이상 그와 함께 있어 주기를 마다했고, 그리하여 두 사람은 조지프 씨를 따라갔다."], ["Rebecca sang far better than her friend (though of course Osborne was free to keep his opinion), and exerted herself to the utmost, and, indeed, to the wonder of Amelia, who had never known her perform so well.", "레베카는 친구보다 훨씬 더 노래를 잘했고(물론 오스본은 제 견해를 고수할 자유가 있었지만), 있는 힘을 다해 부른 데다, 정말이지 그녀가 그토록 잘 부르는 걸 한 번도 본 적 없던 애밀리아를 놀라게 했다."], ["She sang a French song, which Joseph did not understand in the least, and which George confessed he did not understand, and then a number of those simple ballads which were the fashion forty years ago, and in which British tars, our King, poor Susan, blue-eyed Mary, and the like, were the principal themes.", "그녀는 조지프가 한마디도 알아듣지 못하고 조지마저 못 알아듣겠다고 실토한 프랑스 노래를 한 곡 부른 다음, 사십 년 전 유행하던 그 소박한 발라드 몇 곡을 불렀다. 그 노래들에서는 영국 수병이며, 우리 임금이며, 가엾은 수전이며, 푸른 눈의 메리며 하는 것들이 주된 주제였다."], ["They are not, it is said, very brilliant, in a musical point of view, but contain numberless good-natured, simple appeals to the affections, which people understood better than the milk-and-water lagrime, sospiri, and felicita of the eternal Donizettian music with which we are favoured now-a-days.", "그것들은 음악적인 관점에서야 그다지 빼어나지 않다고들 하지만, 마음을 향한 한없이 따뜻하고 소박한 호소를 담고 있어, 요즘 우리에게 베풀어지는 저 영원한 도니체티풍 음악의 밍밍한 '눈물'이며 '한숨'이며 '행복' 따위보다 사람들이 한결 잘 이해했던 것이다."]], [["Conversation of a sentimental sort, befitting the subject, was carried on between the songs, to which Sambo, after he had brought the tea, the delighted cook, and even Mrs. Blenkinsop, the housekeeper, condescended to listen on the landing-place.", "노래와 노래 사이에는 그 주제에 걸맞은 감상적인 대화가 오갔는데, 샘보는 차를 내온 뒤에, 그리고 그 흐뭇해하는 요리사며, 심지어 가정부 블렌킨솝 부인까지도 황송하게 층계참에서 그것에 귀를 기울였다."]], [["Among these ditties was one, the last of the concert, and to the following effect:", "이 노래들 가운데, 그 음악회의 마지막을 장식한 한 곡은 다음과 같은 내용이었다."]], [["Ah!", "아!"], ["bleak and barren was the moor, Ah!", "황량하고 메마른 황야여, 아!"], ["loud and piercing was the storm, The cottage roof was shelter'd sure, The cottage hearth was bright and warm--An orphan boy the lattice pass'd, And, as he mark'd its cheerful glow, Felt doubly keen the midnight blast, And doubly cold the fallen snow.", "거세고 살을 에는 폭풍이여, 오두막 지붕은 든든히 막아 주고, 오두막 화롯불은 밝고도 따스했네—한 고아 소년이 창살 곁을 지나며, 그 정겨운 불빛을 바라보자니, 한밤의 칼바람은 곱절로 매섭고, 내려앉은 눈은 곱절로 시렸어라."]], [["They mark'd him as he onward prest, With fainting heart and weary limb; Kind voices bade him turn and rest, And gentle faces welcomed him.", "그들은 가냘픈 마음과 지친 다리로 앞으로 나아가는 그를 알아보았네. 다정한 목소리들이 돌아와 쉬어 가라 하고, 부드러운 얼굴들이 그를 맞아 주었네."], ["The dawn is up--the guest is gone, The cottage hearth is blazing still; Heaven pity all poor wanderers lone!", "동이 트고—손님은 떠났건만, 오두막 화롯불은 여전히 타오르네. 하늘이여 외로이 떠도는 가엾은 이들을 모두 굽어살피소서!"], ["Hark to the wind upon the hill!", "언덕 위로 부는 저 바람 소리를 들으라!"]], [["It was the sentiment of the before-mentioned words, \"When I'm gone,\" over again.", "그것은 앞서 말한 그 \"제가 떠나고 나면\"이라는 말의 정조를 거듭하는 것이었다."], ["As she came to the last words, Miss Sharp's \"deep-toned voice faltered.\"", "그녀가 마지막 구절에 이르자, 샤프 양의 \"낮게 울리는 목소리가 떨렸다.\""], ["Everybody felt the allusion to her departure, and to her hapless orphan state.", "모두가 그녀의 떠남과, 의지할 데 없는 고아의 처지를 빗댄 것임을 알아챘다."], ["Joseph Sedley, who was fond of music, and soft-hearted, was in a state of ravishment during the performance of the song, and profoundly touched at its conclusion.", "음악을 좋아하고 마음이 여린 조지프 세들리는 그 노래가 흐르는 동안 황홀경에 빠져 있었고, 끝날 무렵에는 깊이 마음이 움직였다."], ["If he had had the courage; if George and Miss Sedley had remained, according to the former's proposal, in the farther room, Joseph Sedley's bachelorhood would have been at an end, and this work would never have been written.", "만일 그에게 용기가 있었더라면, 만일 조지와 세들리 양이 앞서 조지가 제안한 대로 안쪽 방에 그대로 머물러 있었더라면, 조지프 세들리의 독신 생활은 그것으로 끝장났을 것이고, 이 작품은 영영 쓰이지 않았을 것이다."], ["But at the close of the ditty, Rebecca quitted the piano, and giving her hand to Amelia, walked away into the front drawing-room twilight; and, at this moment, Mr.", "그러나 그 노래가 끝나자 레베카는 피아노 곁을 떠나 애밀리아에게 손을 내밀고는 어슴푸레한 앞쪽 응접실로 걸어 들어갔다."], ["Sambo made his appearance with a tray, containing sandwiches, jellies, and some glittering glasses and decanters, on which Joseph Sedley's attention was immediately fixed.", "그리고 바로 그 순간, 샘보 씨가 샌드위치며 젤리며, 번쩍이는 잔 몇 개와 술병을 담은 쟁반을 들고 모습을 드러냈으니, 조지프 세들리의 시선은 즉각 거기에 붙박이고 말았다."], ["When the parents of the house of Sedley returned from their dinner-party, they found the young people so busy in talking, that they had not heard the arrival of the carriage, and Mr.", "세들리 집안의 부모가 만찬 모임에서 돌아왔을 때, 젊은이들은 마차가 도착한 소리도 듣지 못할 만큼 이야기에 푹 빠져 있었고, 조지프 씨는 마침 이렇게 말하던 참이었다."], ["Joseph was in the act of saying, \"My dear Miss Sharp, one little teaspoonful of jelly to recruit you after your immense--your--your delightful exertions.\"", "\"친애하는 샤프 양, 그 엄청난—당신의—당신의 즐거운 수고 끝에 기운을 차리시라고, 젤리 한 작은술만 들어 보시지요.\""]], [["\"Bravo, Jos!\" said Mr.", "\"잘한다, 조스!\""], ["Sedley; on hearing the bantering of which well-known voice, Jos instantly relapsed into an alarmed silence, and quickly took his departure.", "세들리 씨가 말했다. 그 익숙한 목소리의 놀림조를 듣자마자, 조스는 즉각 화들짝 놀라 입을 다물고는 황급히 자리를 떴다."], ["He did not lie awake all night thinking whether or not he was in love with Miss Sharp; the passion of love never interfered with the appetite or the slumber of Mr.", "그는 밤새 자기가 샤프 양을 사랑하는지 아닌지를 곱씹으며 잠 못 이루지는 않았다. 사랑이라는 열정이 조지프 세들리 씨의 식욕이나 잠을 방해하는 법은 결코 없었으니까."], ["Joseph Sedley; but he thought to himself how delightful it would be to hear such songs as those after Cutcherry--what a distinguee girl she was--how she could speak French better than the Governor-General's lady herself--and what a sensation she would make at the Calcutta balls.", "다만 그는 재판소 일을 마친 뒤에 그런 노래를 듣는다면 얼마나 즐거울까—그 아가씨는 얼마나 기품 있는 여자인가—총독 부인보다도 프랑스어를 더 잘하지 않던가—그리고 캘커타 무도회에서 얼마나 사람들의 이목을 끌까 하는 생각에 잠겼다."], ["\"It's evident the poor devil's in love with me,\"", "\"저 가엾은 것이 나한테 반한 게 분명해.\""], ["thought he.", "그가 생각했다."], ["\"She is just as rich as most of the girls who come out to India.", "\"인도로 건너오는 여자애들 대부분만큼이나 재산도 넉넉하고."], ["I might go farther, and fare worse, egad!\"", "더 멀리 가 봤자 더 못한 짝이나 만날지도 모르지, 암!\""], ["And in these meditations he fell asleep.", "그리고 이런 상념에 잠긴 채 그는 잠이 들었다."]], [["How Miss Sharp lay awake, thinking, will he come or not to-morrow? need not be told here.", "샤프 양이 내일 그가 올까 안 올까 생각하며 어떻게 잠 못 이루고 누워 있었는지는, 여기서 구태여 이야기할 필요가 없겠다."], ["To-morrow came, and, as sure as fate, Mr. Joseph Sedley made his appearance before luncheon.", "내일이 왔고, 운명이라도 정한 듯 어김없이, 조지프 세들리 씨는 점심 식사 전에 모습을 드러냈다."], ["He had never been known before to confer such an honour on Russell Square.", "그가 러셀 광장에 그런 영예를 베푼 적은 전에 한 번도 없었다."], ["George Osborne was somehow there already (sadly \"putting out\" Amelia, who was writing to her twelve dearest friends at Chiswick Mall), and Rebecca was employed upon her yesterday's work.", "어찌 된 영문인지 조지 오스본은 이미 와 있었고(치즈윅 거리의 열두 절친한 벗들에게 편지를 쓰던 애밀리아를 딱하게도 \"방해하면서\""], ["As Joe's buggy drove up, and while, after his usual thundering knock and pompous bustle at the door, the ex-Collector of Boggley Wollah laboured up stairs to the drawing-room, knowing glances were telegraphed between Osborne and Miss Sedley, and the pair, smiling archly, looked at Rebecca, who actually blushed as she bent her fair ringlets over her knitting.", "말이다), 레베카는 어제 하던 일에 매달려 있었다. 조의 이륜마차가 다가왔을 때, 그리고 보글리 월라의 전직 징세관이 여느 때처럼 천둥 같은 노크 소리와 거드름 가득한 부산을 떨며 응접실로 끙끙대고 올라오는 동안, 오스본과 세들리 양 사이에는 뜻이 통하는 눈짓이 오갔고, 그 한 쌍은 짓궂게 미소 지으며 레베카를 바라보았으니, 그녀는 정말로 얼굴을 붉히며 뜨개질 위로 고운 곱슬머리를 숙였다."], ["How her heart beat as Joseph appeared--Joseph, puffing from the staircase in shining creaking boots--Joseph, in a new waistcoat, red with heat and nervousness, and blushing behind his wadded neckcloth.", "조지프가 나타나자 그녀의 가슴은 얼마나 두근거렸던지—번쩍이며 삐걱거리는 부츠를 신고 계단에서 헐떡이며 올라오는 조지프—새 조끼를 걸치고, 더위와 긴장으로 얼굴이 벌게진 채, 속을 댄 목도리 뒤에서 얼굴을 붉히는 조지프."], ["It was a nervous moment for all; and as for Amelia, I think she was more frightened than even the people most concerned.", "모두에게 조마조마한 순간이었다. 그리고 애밀리아로 말하자면, 정작 가장 관계된 당사자들보다도 그녀가 더 겁을 집어먹었던 것 같다."]], [["Sambo, who flung open the door and announced Mr.", "문을 활짝 열어젖히고 조지프 씨를 알린 샘보는, 히죽거리며 징세관의 뒤를 따라 들어왔는데, 손에는 멋들어진 꽃다발 두 개를 받쳐 들고 있었다."], ["Joseph, followed grinning, in the Collector's rear, and bearing two handsome nosegays of flowers, which the monster had actually had the gallantry to purchase in Covent Garden Market that morning--they were not as big as the haystacks which ladies carry about with them now-a-days, in cones of filigree paper; but the young women were delighted with the gift, as Joseph presented one to each, with an exceedingly solemn bow.", "그 괴물 같은 양반이 정말로 그날 아침 코번트 가든 시장에서 그것을 사 오는 호기를 부렸던 것이다—요즘 숙녀들이 가는 종이를 원뿔 모양으로 말아 들고 다니는 그 건초더미만 한 것들만큼 크지는 않았지만, 조지프가 더없이 엄숙하게 절을 하며 두 아가씨에게 하나씩 건네자, 그들은 그 선물에 더없이 기뻐했다."]], [["\"Bravo, Jos!\"", "\"잘한다, 조스!\""], ["cried Osborne.", "오스본이 외쳤다."]], [["\"Thank you, dear Joseph,\" said Amelia, quite ready to kiss her brother, if he were so minded.", "\"고마워요, 조지프 오빠.\""], ["(And I think for a kiss from such a dear creature as Amelia, I would purchase all Mr. Lee's conservatories out of hand.)", "애밀리아가, 오빠가 원하기만 한다면 입맞춤이라도 해 줄 양으로 말했다.(그리고 내 생각에, 애밀리아처럼 사랑스러운 존재의 입맞춤 한 번을 위해서라면, 나는 리 씨의 온실을 전부 당장 사들이고도 남을 것이다.)"]], [["\"O heavenly, heavenly flowers!\"", "\"어머나, 천상의, 천상의 꽃이네요!\""], ["exclaimed Miss Sharp, and smelt them delicately, and held them to her bosom, and cast up her eyes to the ceiling, in an ecstasy of admiration.", "샤프 양이 탄성을 지르며, 더없이 우아하게 꽃향기를 맡고는, 그것을 가슴에 끌어안고 두 눈을 천장으로 치켜뜨며 황홀한 감탄에 젖었다."], ["Perhaps she just looked first into the bouquet, to see whether there was a billet-doux hidden among the flowers; but there was no letter.", "어쩌면 그녀는 꽃 사이에 연애편지가 숨겨져 있지는 않은지 보려고 먼저 그 부케 안부터 들여다보았는지도 모른다. 그러나 편지는 없었다."]], [["\"Do they talk the language of flowers at Boggley Wollah, Sedley?\"", "\"보글리 월라에서는 꽃말을 쓰나요, 세들리?\""], ["asked Osborne, laughing.", "오스본이 웃으며 물었다."]], [["\"Pooh, nonsense!\"", "\"쳇, 무슨 말도 안 되는 소리.\""], ["replied the sentimental youth.", "그 감상적인 청년이 대꾸했다. \"네이선 가게에서 산 거요."], ["\"Bought 'em at Nathan's; very glad you like 'em; and eh, Amelia, my dear, I bought a pine-apple at the same time, which I gave to Sambo.", "마음에 든다니 기쁘구려. 그리고 어, 애밀리아, 얘야, 그때 파인애플도 하나 샀는데, 그건 샘보한테 줬다."], ["Let's have it for tiffin; very cool and nice this hot weather.\"", "점심에 그걸 먹자꾸나. 이 더운 날씨에 아주 시원하고 좋을 거야.\""], ["Rebecca said she had never tasted a pine, and longed beyond everything to taste one.", "레베카는 파인애플은 한 번도 맛본 적이 없다며, 무엇보다도 그것을 맛보고 싶어 못 견디겠다고 말했다."]], [["So the conversation went on.", "그렇게 대화가 이어졌다."], ["I don't know on what pretext Osborne left the room, or why, presently, Amelia went away, perhaps to superintend the slicing of the pine-apple; but Jos was left alone with Rebecca, who had resumed her work, and the green silk and the shining needles were quivering rapidly under her white slender fingers.", "무슨 핑계로 오스본이 방을 나갔는지, 또 어째서 이내 애밀리아까지 자리를 떴는지—아마 파인애플 써는 일을 챙기러 갔으리라—나로서는 알 길이 없다. 어쨌든 조스는 레베카와 단둘이 남게 되었고, 그녀는 다시 일감을 손에 잡았으니, 초록빛 비단실과 번쩍이는 바늘이 그녀의 희고 가느다란 손가락 아래에서 빠르게 떨리고 있었다."]], [["\"What a beautiful, BYOO-OOTIFUL song that was you sang last night, dear Miss Sharp,\"", "\"어젯밤에 당신이 부른 그 노래, 정말이지 아름답고, 아—름다운 노래였소, 친애하는 샤프 양.\""], ["said the Collector.", "징세관이 말했다."], ["\"It made me cry almost; 'pon my honour it did.\"", "\"거의 울 뻔했다오. 정말이지 그랬소, 명예를 걸고.\""]], [["\"Because you have a kind heart, Mr.", "\"당신이 다정한 마음씨를 지녔으니 그렇지요, 조지프 씨."], ["Joseph; all the Sedleys have, I think.\"", "세들리 집안 분들은 다들 그런 것 같아요.\""]], [["\"It kept me awake last night, and I was trying to hum it this morning, in bed; I was, upon my honour.", "\"그 노래 때문에 어젯밤 한숨도 못 잤다오. 오늘 아침엔 침대에서 그걸 흥얼거려 보기까지 했지."], ["Gollop, my doctor, came in at eleven (for I'm a sad invalid, you know, and see Gollop every day), and, 'gad!", "정말이라오, 명예를 걸고."], ["there I was, singing away like--a robin.\"", "우리 의사 골롭이 열한 시에 왔는데(난 가엾게도 환자라서, 알다시피 날마다 골롭을 본다오), 아 글쎄, 그때 내가 거기서 노래를 불러 대고 있더란 말이오—꼭 울새처럼.\""]], [["\"O you droll creature!", "\"어머, 익살꾼 같으니라고!"], ["Do let me hear you sing it.\"", "어디 한번 불러 보세요.\""]], [["\"Me?", "\"내가?"], ["No, you, Miss Sharp; my dear Miss Sharp, do sing it.\"", "안 되오, 당신이, 샤프 양. 친애하는 샤프 양, 어서 불러 봐요.\""], ["\"Not now, Mr. Sedley,\"", "\"지금은 안 돼요, 세들리 씨.\""], ["said Rebecca, with a sigh.", "레베카가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."], ["\"My spirits are not equal to it; besides, I must finish the purse.", "\"그럴 만한 기분이 못 되네요."], ["Will you help me, Mr. Sedley?\"", "게다가 이 지갑도 마저 떠야 하고요."], ["And before he had time to ask how, Mr.", "좀 도와주시겠어요, 세들리 씨?\""], ["Joseph Sedley, of the East India Company's service, was actually seated tete-a-tete with a young lady, looking at her with a most killing expression; his arms stretched out before her in an imploring attitude, and his hands bound in a web of green silk, which she was unwinding.", "그리고 그가 어떻게 도와야 하는지 물어볼 새도 없이, 동인도 회사에 봉직하는 조지프 세들리 씨는 실제로 한 젊은 아가씨와 단둘이 마주 앉아, 사람 잡을 듯한 표정으로 그녀를 바라보고 있었다. 두 팔은 애원하는 자세로 그녀 앞에 쭉 뻗은 채, 두 손은 그녀가 풀어내고 있는 초록빛 비단실 그물에 묶인 채로 말이다."]], [["In this romantic position Osborne and Amelia found the interesting pair, when they entered to announce that tiffin was ready.", "이 낭만적인 자세를 취한 흥미로운 한 쌍을, 오스본과 애밀리아가 점심이 준비되었음을 알리러 들어왔을 때 발견했다."], ["The skein of silk was just wound round the card; but Mr. Jos had never spoken.", "비단실 타래는 마침 카드에 다 감겼지만, 조스 씨는 끝내 입을 떼지 못한 터였다."]], [["\"I am sure he will to-night, dear,\"", "\"오늘 밤엔 분명 그럴 거야, 얘.\" 애밀리아가 레베카의 손을 꼭 쥐며 말했다."], ["Amelia said, as she pressed Rebecca's hand; and Sedley, too, had communed with his soul, and said to himself, \"'Gad, I'll pop the question at Vauxhall.\"", "그리고 세들리 역시 제 영혼과 이야기를 나누고는, 스스로에게 이렇게 다짐했다. \"암, 보크홀에서 청혼을 해야겠어.\""]], [["Dobbin of Ours\nCuff's fight with Dobbin, and the unexpected issue of that contest, will long be remembered by every man who was educated at Dr.", "스위시테일 박사의 그 유명한 학교에서 교육받은 사람이라면 누구나, 커프와 도빈의 그 싸움과 그 시합의 뜻밖의 결말을 두고두고 기억할 것이다."], ["Swishtail's famous school. The latter Youth (who used to be called Heigh-ho Dobbin, Gee-ho Dobbin, and by many other names indicative of puerile contempt) was the quietest, the clumsiest, and, as it seemed, the dullest of all Dr.", "이 후자의 소년(흔히들 워워 도빈이니, 이랴이랴 도빈이니, 그 밖에도 유치한 경멸이 담긴 온갖 별명으로 불리던 아이)은 스위시테일 박사의 그 모든 도련님들 가운데 가장 말수가 적고, 가장 어설프며, 또 보기에 가장 우둔한 아이였다."], ["Swishtail's young gentlemen.", "그의 아버지는 도심의 식료품상이었다."], ["His parent was a grocer in the city: and it was bruited abroad that he was admitted into Dr.", "그리고 그가 이른바 \"상호주의 원칙\"에 따라 스위시테일 박사의 학원에 입학을 허락받았다는 소문이 자자했다. 다시 말해 그의 기숙비와 수업료가 돈이 아니라 물품으로 그 아버지에 의해 치러졌다는 것이다."], ["Swishtail's academy upon what are called \"mutual principles\"--that is to say, the expenses of his board and schooling were defrayed by his father in goods, not money; and he stood there--most at the bottom of the school--in his scraggy corduroys and jacket, through the seams of which his great big bones were bursting--as the representative of so many pounds of tea, candles, sugar, mottled-soap, plums (of which a very mild proportion was supplied for the puddings of the establishment), and other commodities.", "그래서 그는 거기에, 학교에서도 가장 밑바닥에, 깡마른 코듀로이 바지와 재킷을 걸친 채 서 있었다. 그 솔기 사이로 큼직한 뼈마디가 비어져 나올 지경이었으니, 그는 차며 양초며 설탕이며 얼룩 비누며 (그 학원의 푸딩용으로는 지극히 인색한 분량만 공급되던) 자두며, 그 밖의 온갖 상품을 몇 파운드어치씩 대표하는 존재로 거기 서 있는 셈이었다."], ["A dreadful day it was for young Dobbin when one of the youngsters of the school, having run into the town upon a poaching excursion for hardbake and polonies, espied the cart of Dobbin & Rudge, Grocers and Oilmen, Thames Street, London, at the Doctor's door, discharging a cargo of the wares in which the firm dealt.", "어린 도빈에게는 끔찍한 날이었다. 학교의 한 아이가 단단한 캐러멜과 폴로니 소시지를 노리고 마을로 몰래 빠져나갔다가, 박사의 문 앞에서 \"런던 템스 거리, 식료품 및 기름 상회, 도빈 앤드 러지\"라고 적힌 짐수레가 그 상회에서 취급하는 물품을 부려 놓는 광경을 목격한 그날 말이다."]], [["Young Dobbin had no peace after that.", "그 후로 어린 도빈에게는 평온이라곤 없었다."], ["The jokes were frightful, and merciless against him.", "그를 향한 농담들은 무시무시했고 또 무자비했다."], ["\"Hullo, Dobbin,\"", "\"이봐, 도빈.\""], ["one wag would say, \"here's good news in the paper.", "한 익살꾼이 말하곤 했다. \"신문에 좋은 소식이 났네."], ["Sugars is ris', my boy.\"", "설탕값이 올랐대, 얘.\""], ["Another would set a sum--\"If a pound of mutton-candles cost sevenpence-halfpenny, how much must Dobbin cost?\"", "또 다른 아이는 산수 문제를 내곤 했다. \"양 기름 양초 한 파운드가 칠 펜스 반이라면, 도빈은 얼마쯤 나가야 할까?\""], ["and a roar would follow from all the circle of young knaves, usher and all, who rightly considered that the selling of goods by retail is a shameful and infamous practice, meriting the contempt and scorn of all real gentlemen.", "그러면 그 어린 악동들의 무리에서, 사감까지 포함해서, 한바탕 폭소가 터져 나오는 것이었다. 그들은 물건을 소매로 파는 것이야말로 부끄럽고 수치스러운 짓이며, 진짜 신사라면 누구나 경멸하고 비웃어 마땅한 행위라고 옳게 여겼던 것이다."]], [["\"Your father's only a merchant, Osborne,\"", "\"네 아버진 그저 장사꾼일 뿐이잖아, 오스본.\""], ["Dobbin said in private to the little boy who had brought down the storm upon him.", "도빈은 자기 머리 위로 그 폭풍을 불러온 그 꼬마에게 단둘이 있을 때 말했다."], ["At which the latter replied haughtily, \"My father's a gentleman, and keeps his carriage\"; and Mr.", "그러자 그 아이는 거만하게 대꾸했다. \"우리 아버진 신사고, 마차도 두신다고.\""], ["William Dobbin retreated to a remote outhouse in the playground, where he passed a half-holiday in the bitterest sadness and woe.", "그러자 윌리엄 도빈은 운동장 한구석의 외딴 헛간으로 물러나, 더없이 쓰라린 슬픔과 비탄 속에서 반공일을 보냈다."], ["Who amongst us is there that does not recollect similar hours of bitter, bitter childish grief?", "우리 가운데 누가 이와 비슷한, 쓰라리고 또 쓰라린 어린 시절의 슬픔의 시간을 기억하지 못하겠는가?"], ["Who feels injustice; who shrinks before a slight; who has a sense of wrong so acute, and so glowing a gratitude for kindness, as a generous boy?", "너그러운 소년만큼 부당함을 절감하고, 모욕 앞에 움츠러들며, 자신이 받은 부당한 처사를 그토록 예민하게 느끼고, 또 친절에는 그토록 뜨거운 감사를 품는 이가 어디 있겠는가?"], ["and how many of those gentle souls do you degrade, estrange, torture, for the sake of a little loose arithmetic, and miserable dog-latin?", "그런데도 너희는 그 가냘픈 영혼들 가운데 얼마나 많은 수를, 한 줌의 엉성한 산수와 비참한 엉터리 라틴어 따위를 위해 깎아내리고, 멀리하고, 괴롭히는 것이냐?"]], [["Now, William Dobbin, from an incapacity to acquire the rudiments of the above language, as they are propounded in that wonderful book the Eton Latin Grammar, was compelled to remain among the very last of Doctor Swishtail's scholars, and was \"taken down\"", "자, 윌리엄 도빈은 저 놀라운 책, 이튼 라틴어 문법서에 제시된 그 언어의 기초조차 익히지 못하는 탓에, 스위시테일 박사의 학생들 가운데 맨 꼴찌 축에 머물 수밖에 없었다."], ["continually by little fellows with pink faces and pinafores when he marched up with the lower form, a giant amongst them, with his downcast, stupefied look, his dog's-eared primer, and his tight corduroys.", "그가 거인처럼 우뚝하니, 풀 죽고 멍한 표정에, 귀퉁이가 접힌 입문서를 들고 꼭 끼는 코듀로이 바지를 입은 채 하급반과 함께 진급해 올라가면, 발그레한 얼굴에 앞치마를 두른 꼬마들에게 끊임없이 \"강등당하기\" 일쑤였다."], ["High and low, all made fun of him.", "위아래 할 것 없이 모두가 그를 놀려 댔다."], ["They sewed up those corduroys, tight as they were.", "그들은 그 꼭 끼는 코듀로이 바지를, 그렇게 끼는데도 더 꿰매 붙여 놓았다."], ["They cut his bed-strings.", "그의 침대 끈을 잘라 놓았다."], ["They upset buckets and benches, so that he might break his shins over them, which he never failed to do.", "양동이며 벤치를 엎어 놓아 그가 거기에 정강이를 부딪쳐 까지게 만들었는데, 그는 어김없이 매번 그렇게 까이고 말았다."], ["They sent him parcels, which, when opened, were found to contain the paternal soap and candles.", "그들은 그에게 소포를 보냈는데, 뜯어 보면 그 안에는 아버지가 만든 비누와 양초가 들어 있었다."], ["There was no little fellow but had his jeer and joke at Dobbin; and he bore everything quite patiently, and was entirely dumb and miserable.", "도빈을 향해 야유와 농을 던지지 않는 꼬마가 하나도 없었다. 그리고 그는 모든 것을 잠자코 견디며, 그저 입을 다문 채 비참하게 지냈다."]], [["Cuff, on the contrary, was the great chief and dandy of the Swishtail Seminary.", "반면 커프는 스위시테일 학원의 위대한 우두머리이자 멋쟁이였다."], ["He smuggled wine in.", "그는 술을 몰래 들여왔다."], ["He fought the town-boys.", "마을 아이들과 싸웠다."], ["Ponies used to come for him to ride home on Saturdays.", "토요일이면 그를 집으로 태우고 갈 조랑말이 오곤 했다."], ["He had his top-boots in his room, in which he used to hunt in the holidays.", "그는 자기 방에 사냥용 장화를 두고, 방학이면 그것을 신고 사냥을 다녔다."], ["He had a gold repeater: and took snuff like the Doctor.", "금딱지 회중시계도 차고 있었고, 박사처럼 코담배도 피웠다."], ["He had been to the Opera, and knew the merits of the principal actors, preferring Mr. Kean to Mr.", "오페라에도 가 본 적이 있어 주연 배우들의 우열을 가릴 줄 알았는데, 켐블 씨보다 킨 씨를 더 높이 쳤다."], ["Kemble. He could knock you off forty Latin verses in an hour.", "한 시간에 라틴어 시 마흔 줄을 척척 지어낼 수 있었다."], ["He could make French poetry.", "프랑스어 시도 지을 줄 알았다."], ["What else didn't he know, or couldn't he do?", "그가 모르는 것, 그가 못 하는 것이 그 밖에 또 무엇이 있었겠는가?"], ["They said even the Doctor himself was afraid of him.", "심지어 박사 본인조차 그를 두려워한다는 말까지 나돌았다."]], [["Cuff, the unquestioned king of the school, ruled over his subjects, and bullied them, with splendid superiority.", "학교의 명실상부한 왕인 커프는 신하들 위에 군림하며, 빼어난 우월감으로 그들을 부려 먹었다. 이 아이는 그의 구두를 닦았다."], ["This one blacked his shoes: that toasted his bread, others would fag out, and give him balls at cricket during whole summer afternoons. \"Figs\"", "저 아이는 그의 빵을 구웠고, 또 다른 아이들은 잔심부름을 하거나 여름 오후 내내 그에게 크리켓 공을 던져 주곤 했다."], ["was the fellow whom he despised most, and with whom, though always abusing him, and sneering at him, he scarcely ever condescended to hold personal communication.", "\"무화과\"는 그가 가장 멸시하는 녀석이었고, 늘 욕하고 비웃으면서도 도무지 몸소 말을 섞으려 들지 않는 상대였다."]], [["One day in private, the two young gentlemen had had a difference.", "어느 날, 단둘이 있을 때 두 도련님 사이에 다툼이 벌어졌다."], ["Figs, alone in the schoolroom, was blundering over a home letter; when Cuff, entering, bade him go upon some message, of which tarts were probably the subject.", "무화과가 교실에 혼자 남아 집으로 보낼 편지를 끙끙대며 쓰고 있는데, 커프가 들어오더니 무슨 심부름을 가라고 시켰다. 아마도 타르트가 그 주제였을 것이다."]], [["\"I can't,\"", "\"못 가.\""], ["says Dobbin; \"I want to finish my letter.\"", "도빈이 말했다. \"편지를 마저 써야 해.\""]], [["\"You CAN'T?\" says Mr.", "\"못 간다고?\""], ["Cuff, laying hold of that document (in which many words were scratched out, many were mis-spelt, on which had been spent I don't know how much thought, and labour, and tears; for the poor fellow was writing to his mother, who was fond of him, although she was a grocer's wife, and lived in a back parlour in Thames Street).", "커프 씨가 말하며 그 문서를 움켜쥐었다(거기에는 지운 단어도 많고 틀리게 쓴 단어도 많았으며, 그 위에 얼마나 많은 고민과 수고와 눈물이 쏟아부어졌는지 알 수 없었다. 그 가엾은 녀석은 어머니에게 편지를 쓰고 있었던 것이다."], ["\"You CAN'T?\" says Mr.", "식료품상의 아내로 템스 거리 뒷방에 사는 처지였지만, 그를 끔찍이 아끼는 어머니에게 말이다)."], ["Cuff: \"I should like to know why, pray?", "\"못 간다고?\" 커프 씨가 말했다."], ["Can't you write to old Mother Figs to-morrow?\"", "\"그 까닭이 뭔지 좀 알았으면 싶은데? 무화과 노친네한테는 내일 편지하면 될 것 아니냐?\""]], [["\"Don't call names,\"", "\"이름 부르지 마.\""], ["Dobbin said, getting off the bench very nervous.", "도빈이 잔뜩 긴장한 채 벤치에서 일어나며 말했다."]], [["\"Well, sir, will you go?\"", "\"그래서, 갈 거야 말 거야?\""], ["crowed the cock of the school.", "학교의 수탉이 의기양양하게 외쳤다."]], [["\"Put down the letter,\"", "\"편지 내려놔.\""], ["Dobbin replied; \"no gentleman readth letterth.\"", "도빈이 대꾸했다. \"신사라면 남의 편지를 읽지 않는 법이야.\""]], [["\"Well, NOW will you go?\"", "\"그래, 이제 갈 거냐고?\""], ["says the other.", "상대가 말했다."]], [["\"No, I won't.", "\"안 가."], ["Don't strike, or I'll THMASH you,\"", "때리지 마, 안 그럼 박살을 내 버릴 테니까.\""], ["roars out Dobbin, springing to a leaden inkstand, and looking so wicked, that Mr.", "도빈이 납으로 된 잉크병으로 달려들며, 어찌나 험악한 표정을 짓던지, 커프 씨는 멈칫하더니 다시 코트 소매를 내려 접고 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고는 비웃음을 지으며 가 버렸다."], ["Cuff paused, turned down his coat sleeves again, put his hands into his pockets, and walked away with a sneer.", "그러나 그 후로 그는 그 식료품상 아들에게 몸소 시비를 걸지는 않았다."], ["But he never meddled personally with the grocer's boy after that; though we must do him the justice to say he always spoke of Mr. Dobbin with contempt behind his back.", "다만 그의 명예를 위해 한마디 보태자면, 그는 등 뒤에서는 늘 도빈 씨를 경멸하며 입에 올리곤 했다."]], [["Some time after this interview, it happened that Mr.", "이 면담이 있고 얼마 뒤, 어느 화창한 오후에 커프 씨가 가엾은 윌리엄 도빈의 근처에 있게 된 일이 있었다."], ["Cuff, on a sunshiny afternoon, was in the neighbourhood of poor William Dobbin, who was lying under a tree in the playground, spelling over a favourite copy of the Arabian Nights which he had apart from the rest of the school, who were pursuing their various sports--quite lonely, and almost happy.", "도빈은 운동장 나무 아래 누워, 다른 아이들과 떨어진 곳에서 자기가 아끼는 『아라비안나이트』 한 권을 한 자 한 자 더듬어 읽고 있었다. 다른 아이들이 저마다 온갖 놀이에 빠져 있는 동안, 그는 외톨이로, 그러나 거의 행복하게 있었던 것이다."], ["If people would but leave children to themselves; if teachers would cease to bully them; if parents would not insist upon directing their thoughts, and dominating their feelings--those feelings and thoughts which are a mystery to all (for how much do you and I know of each other, of our children, of our fathers, of our neighbour, and how far more beautiful and sacred are the thoughts of the poor lad or girl whom you govern likely to be, than those of the dull and world-corrupted person who rules him?)--if, I say, parents and masters would leave their children alone a little more, small harm would accrue, although a less quantity of as in praesenti might be acquired.", "사람들이 아이들을 그저 제멋대로 내버려 두기만 한다면, 교사들이 아이들을 들볶기를 그만두기만 한다면, 부모들이 아이들의 생각을 끝끝내 지도하려 들고 그 감정을 지배하려 들지만 않는다면—누구에게나 신비로 남아 있는 그 감정과 생각을(우리가 서로를, 우리 아이들을, 우리 아버지들을, 우리 이웃을 대체 얼마나 알겠는가, 또 너희가 다스리는 그 가엾은 소년이나 소녀의 생각이, 그를 지배하는 저 우둔하고 세속에 찌든 인간의 생각보다 얼마나 더 아름답고 거룩할 법한가?)—내 말은, 부모와 스승이 아이들을 조금만 더 내버려 둔다면, 비록 현재형 라틴어 어미를 익히는 양은 조금 줄어들지언정, 해될 일은 거의 없으리라는 것이다."]], [["Well, William Dobbin had for once forgotten the world, and was away with Sindbad the Sailor in the Valley of Diamonds, or with Prince Ahmed and the Fairy Peribanou in that delightful cavern where the Prince found her, and whither we should all like to make a tour; when shrill cries, as of a little fellow weeping, woke up his pleasant reverie; and looking up, he saw Cuff before him, belabouring a little boy.", "자, 윌리엄 도빈은 모처럼 세상을 잊고서, 다이아몬드 골짜기의 뱃사람 신드바드와 함께, 혹은 아메드 왕자가 페리바누 요정을 만난 그 황홀한 동굴—우리 모두 한 번쯤 가 보고 싶은 그곳—에서 왕자와 요정과 함께 멀리 떠나 있었다. 그런데 어린아이가 우는 듯한 날카로운 울음소리가 그 즐거운 공상을 깨뜨렸다. 고개를 들어 보니, 커프가 그의 앞에서 한 꼬마를 두들겨 패고 있었다."]], [["It was the lad who had peached upon him about the grocer's cart; but he bore little malice, not at least towards the young and small.", "식료품상 짐수레 일을 일러바친 바로 그 아이였다. 그러나 그는 별로 앙심을 품지 않았다."], ["\"How dare you, sir, break the bottle?\"", "적어도 어리고 작은 아이에게는 그랬다."], ["says Cuff to the little urchin, swinging a yellow cricket-stump over him.", "\"이놈, 어디 감히 병을 깨뜨려?\" 커프가 노란 크리켓 막대기를 그 꼬마 위로 휘두르며 말했다."]], [["The boy had been instructed to get over the playground wall (at a selected spot where the broken glass had been removed from the top, and niches made convenient in the brick); to run a quarter of a mile; to purchase a pint of rum-shrub on credit; to brave all the Doctor's outlying spies, and to clamber back into the playground again; during the performance of which feat, his foot had slipt, and the bottle was broken, and the shrub had been spilt, and his pantaloons had been damaged, and he appeared before his employer a perfectly guilty and trembling, though harmless, wretch.", "그 아이는 운동장 담을 넘어가라는 지시를 받았었다(깨진 유리가 위쪽에서 치워지고 벽돌에 발 디딜 홈이 편리하게 파인, 미리 골라 둔 지점에서 말이다). 사 분의 일 마일을 달려가, 외상으로 럼주 시럽 한 파인트를 사 오고, 학교 밖에 깔린 박사의 첩자들을 모두 무릅쓰고, 다시 운동장 안으로 기어 올라오라는 것이었다. 그런데 이 묘기를 부리는 동안 발이 미끄러져 병이 깨졌고, 시럽이 쏟아졌으며, 바지까지 못 쓰게 되어, 그는 비록 해를 끼친 것은 없으나 완전히 죄지은 듯 벌벌 떨며 자기 고용주 앞에 나타나게 된 것이었다."]], [["\"How dare you, sir, break it?\"", "\"이놈, 어디 감히 그걸 깨뜨려?\""], ["says Cuff; \"you blundering little thief.", "커프가 말했다. \"이 얼간이 좀도둑 같으니."], ["You drank the shrub, and now you pretend to have broken the bottle.", "시럽을 네가 마셔 놓고 이제 와서 병을 깨뜨린 척하는 게로구나."], ["Hold out your hand, sir.\"", "손 내밀어, 이놈.\""]], [["Down came the stump with a great heavy thump on the child's hand.", "막대기가 그 아이의 손바닥 위로 묵직하게 쾅 내리쳐졌다. 신음 소리가 뒤따랐다."], ["A moan followed.", "도빈이 고개를 들었다."], ["Dobbin looked up.", "페리바누 요정은 아메드 왕자와 함께 동굴 가장 깊은 곳으로 달아나 버렸다. 로크 새는 뱃사람 신드바드를 다이아몬드 골짜기에서 휙 낚아채 시야 밖, 저 멀리 구름 속으로 데려가 버렸다."], ["The Fairy Peribanou had fled into the inmost cavern with Prince Ahmed: the Roc had whisked away Sindbad the Sailor out of the Valley of Diamonds out of sight, far into the clouds: and there was everyday life before honest William; and a big boy beating a little one without cause.", "그리고 정직한 윌리엄의 눈앞에는 일상의 현실이 펼쳐져 있었다. 큰 아이 하나가 까닭 없이 작은 아이를 때리고 있는 광경 말이다."]], [["\"Hold out your other hand, sir,\"", "\"다른 손 내밀어, 이놈.\""], ["roars Cuff to his little schoolfellow, whose face was distorted with pain.", "커프가 그 어린 학우에게 호통쳤고, 그 아이의 얼굴은 고통으로 일그러져 있었다."], ["Dobbin quivered, and gathered himself up in his narrow old clothes.", "도빈은 부르르 떨더니, 작고 낡은 옷을 추슬러 몸을 일으켰다."]], [["\"Take that, you little devil!\" cried Mr.", "\"이거나 받아라, 이 꼬마 악마야!\""], ["Cuff, and down came the wicket again on the child's hand.--Don't be horrified, ladies, every boy at a public school has done it.", "커프 씨가 외쳤고, 막대기가 다시금 그 아이의 손 위로 내리쳐졌다. 부디 질겁하지 마시라, 숙녀분들이여."], ["Your children will so do and be done by, in all probability.", "공립학교에 다니는 사내아이라면 누구나 한 번쯤 해 본 짓이니까. 당신네 자녀들도 십중팔구 그런 짓을 하고 또 당할 것이다."], ["Down came the wicket again; and Dobbin started up.", "막대기가 다시 내리쳐졌다. 그러자 도빈이 벌떡 일어섰다."]], [["I can't tell what his motive was.", "그의 동기가 무엇이었는지 나로서는 알 수 없다."], ["Torture in a public school is as much licensed as the knout in Russia.", "공립학교에서의 가혹 행위란 러시아의 채찍형만큼이나 공인된 것이다. 그것에 저항한다는 것은 (어떤 의미로는) 신사답지 못한 일이었다."], ["It would be ungentlemanlike (in a manner) to resist it.", "어쩌면 도빈의 어리석은 영혼이 그 폭정의 행사에 반기를 들었는지도 모른다. 아니면 마음속에 복수심이 꿈틀거려, 영광과 자긍심과 위세와 위엄을 한 몸에 두르고, 깃발이 나부끼고 북이 울리고 호위병이 경례하는 가운데 그 자리에 군림하던 저 눈부신 폭군이자 골목대장과 한번 겨뤄 보고 싶었는지도 모른다."], ["Perhaps Dobbin's foolish soul revolted against that exercise of tyranny; or perhaps he had a hankering feeling of revenge in his mind, and longed to measure himself against that splendid bully and tyrant, who had all the glory, pride, pomp, circumstance, banners flying, drums beating, guards saluting, in the place.", "그러나 그 동기가 무엇이었든 간에, 그는 벌떡 일어서더니 외쳤다. \"그만둬, 커프."], ["Whatever may have been his incentive, however, up he sprang, and screamed out, \"Hold off, Cuff; don't bully that child any more; or I'll--\"", "그 애 더는 괴롭히지 마. 안 그러면 내가—\""]], [["\"Or you'll what?\"", "\"안 그러면 네가 뭘 어쩐다고?\""], ["Cuff asked in amazement at this interruption.", "이 훼방에 어안이 벙벙해진 커프가 물었다."], ["\"Hold out your hand, you little beast.\"", "\"손 내밀어, 이 짐승만도 못한 놈아.\""]], [["\"I'll give you the worst thrashing you ever had in your life,\"", "\"네가 평생 당해 본 적 없는 가장 호된 매질을 안겨 주겠어.\" 도빈이 커프의 말 앞부분에 대꾸하며 말했다."], ["Dobbin said, in reply to the first part of Cuff's sentence; and little Osborne, gasping and in tears, looked up with wonder and incredulity at seeing this amazing champion put up suddenly to defend him: while Cuff's astonishment was scarcely less.", "그러자 헐떡이며 눈물 흘리던 어린 오스본은, 이 놀라운 투사가 느닷없이 자기를 지키러 나선 것을 보고는 경이와 의심이 뒤섞인 눈으로 올려다보았다. 한편 커프의 경악도 그에 못지않았다."], ["Fancy our late monarch George III when he heard of the revolt of the North American colonies: fancy brazen Goliath when little David stepped forward and claimed a meeting; and you have the feelings of Mr.", "우리의 작고하신 군주 조지 3세께서 북아메리카 식민지의 반란 소식을 들었을 때를 떠올려 보시라. 작은 다윗이 앞으로 나서서 결투를 청했을 때 그 뻔뻔한 골리앗을 떠올려 보시라."], ["Reginald Cuff when this rencontre was proposed to him.", "그러면 이 한판 시합을 제안받은 레지널드 커프 씨의 심정을 짐작할 수 있으리라."]], [["\"After school,\"", "\"수업 끝나고.\" 그가 말했다."], ["says he, of course; after a pause and a look, as much as to say, \"Make your will, and communicate your last wishes to your friends between this time and that.\"", "물론, 잠시 뜸을 들이고 한 번 흘겨본 뒤였는데, 그 표정인즉슨 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"지금부터 그때까지 유언장이나 작성하고, 마지막 소원이나 친구들에게 전해 두시지.\""]], [["\"As you please,\"", "\"좋을 대로.\""], ["Dobbin said.", "도빈이 말했다."], ["\"You must be my bottle holder, Osborne.\"", "\"네가 내 세컨드를 봐 줘야겠어, 오스본.\""]], [["\"Well, if you like,\"", "\"뭐, 정 그렇다면.\""], ["little Osborne replied; for you see his papa kept a carriage, and he was rather ashamed of his champion.", "어린 오스본이 대꾸했다. 그도 그럴 것이, 아시다시피 그의 아버지는 마차를 두는 사람이었고, 그는 자기 편을 들어 줄 이 투사가 어쩐지 좀 부끄러웠던 것이다."]], [["Yes, when the hour of battle came, he was almost ashamed to say, \"Go it, Figs\"; and not a single other boy in the place uttered that cry for the first two or three rounds of this famous combat; at the commencement of which the scientific Cuff, with a contemptuous smile on his face, and as light and as gay as if he was at a ball, planted his blows upon his adversary, and floored that unlucky champion three times running.", "그렇다, 결투의 시간이 닥쳐오자, 그는 \"잘해라, 무화과\"라고 말하기조차 거의 부끄러울 지경이었다. 그리고 이 유명한 시합의 처음 두세 회 동안에는 그 자리의 그 어느 아이도 그런 응원의 함성을 내지르지 않았다."], ["At each fall there was a cheer; and everybody was anxious to have the honour of offering the conqueror a knee.", "시합 초반, 기술이 능숙한 커프는 얼굴에 경멸의 미소를 띤 채, 마치 무도회에라도 온 듯 가뿐하고 명랑하게 상대에게 주먹을 꽂아 넣어, 그 운 없는 투사를 내리 세 번이나 바닥에 쓰러뜨렸다. 쓰러질 때마다 환호성이 터졌고, 다들 그 승자에게 무릎을 받쳐 주는 영예를 누리고 싶어 안달이었다."]], [["\"What a licking I shall get when it's over,\"", "\"이게 끝나면 난 또 얼마나 두들겨 맞을까.\""], ["young Osborne thought, picking up his man.", "어린 오스본은 자기 편을 일으켜 세우며 생각했다."], ["\"You'd best give in,\"", "\"그냥 항복하는 게 낫겠어.\""], ["he said to Dobbin; \"it's only a thrashing, Figs, and you know I'm used to it.\"", "그가 도빈에게 말했다. \"그래 봤자 매질 한 번이잖아, 무화과."], ["But Figs, all whose limbs were in a quiver, and whose nostrils were breathing rage, put his little bottle-holder aside, and went in for a fourth time.", "너도 알다시피 난 익숙하니까.\" 그러나 온 사지를 부들부들 떨고 콧구멍으로 분노를 뿜어내던 무화과는, 그 작은 세컨드를 옆으로 밀쳐 두고 네 번째로 맞붙으러 들어갔다."]], [["As he did not in the least know how to parry the blows that were aimed at himself, and Cuff had begun the attack on the three preceding occasions, without ever allowing his enemy to strike, Figs now determined that he would commence the engagement by a charge on his own part; and accordingly, being a left-handed man, brought that arm into action, and hit out a couple of times with all his might--once at Mr.", "자기를 향해 날아드는 주먹을 막아 낼 줄은 전혀 몰랐던 데다, 앞선 세 차례 모두 커프가 적에게 손쓸 틈도 주지 않고 먼저 공격을 시작했던 터라, 무화과는 이번엔 자기 쪽에서 먼저 돌격해 시합을 개시하기로 마음먹었다. 그리하여 왼손잡이였던 그는 그 팔을 휘둘러, 있는 힘을 다해 두 번을 내질렀다."], ["Cuff's left eye, and once on his beautiful Roman nose.", "한 번은 커프 씨의 왼쪽 눈에, 또 한 번은 그 멋진 로마식 코에 말이다."]], [["Cuff went down this time, to the astonishment of the assembly. \"Well hit, by Jove,\"", "이번에는 커프가 쓰러졌고, 모여 있던 아이들은 깜짝 놀랐다."], ["says little Osborne, with the air of a connoisseur, clapping his man on the back.", "\"야, 멋지게 한 방 먹였다, 세상에.\" 어린 오스본이 감식가다운 태도로 자기 편의 등을 두드리며 말했다."], ["\"Give it him with the left, Figs my boy.\"", "\"왼손으로 먹여, 무화과, 이 친구야.\""]], [["Figs's left made terrific play during all the rest of the combat.", "남은 시합 내내 무화과의 왼손은 무시무시한 활약을 펼쳤다."], ["Cuff went down every time.", "커프는 번번이 나가떨어졌다."], ["At the sixth round, there were almost as many fellows shouting out, \"Go it, Figs,\" as there were youths exclaiming, \"Go it, Cuff.\"", "여섯 번째 회에 이르자 \"잘한다, 커프\"를 외치는 아이들만큼이나 \"잘한다, 무화과\"를 외치는 아이들이 거의 같은 수로 늘어났다."], ["At the twelfth round the latter champion was all abroad, as the saying is, and had lost all presence of mind and power of attack or defence.", "열두 번째 회에 이르자 후자의 투사는, 흔히들 하는 말로 정신을 놓아 버려서, 침착함도 공격력도 방어력도 모조리 잃은 상태였다."], ["Figs, on the contrary, was as calm as a quaker.", "반면 무화과는 퀘이커 교도처럼 차분했다."], ["His face being quite pale, his eyes shining open, and a great cut on his underlip bleeding profusely, gave this young fellow a fierce and ghastly air, which perhaps struck terror into many spectators.", "얼굴은 새파랗게 질리고, 두 눈은 부릅뜬 채 번득이며, 아랫입술에 난 큼직한 상처에서 피가 줄줄 흐르는 그 모습은 이 젊은 친구에게 사납고 섬뜩한 기운을 더해 주어, 아마도 적잖은 구경꾼들의 가슴에 공포를 새겨 넣었을 것이다."], ["Nevertheless, his intrepid adversary prepared to close for the thirteenth time.", "그럼에도 그 겁 없는 적수는 열세 번째로 맞붙을 채비를 했다."]], [["If I had the pen of a Napier, or a Bell's Life, I should like to describe this combat properly. It was the last charge of the Guard--(that is, it would have been, only Waterloo had not yet taken place)--it was Ney's column breasting the hill of La Haye Sainte, bristling with ten thousand bayonets, and crowned with twenty eagles--it was the shout of the beef-eating British, as leaping down the hill they rushed to hug the enemy in the savage arms of battle--in other words, Cuff coming up full of pluck, but quite reeling and groggy, the Fig-merchant put in his left as usual on his adversary's nose, and sent him down for the last time.", "내게 네이피어나 『벨스 라이프』 같은 필력이 있다면, 이 시합을 제대로 묘사해 보고 싶다. 그것은 근위대의 마지막 돌격이었다(라고 해야겠지만, 다만 워털루 전투는 아직 벌어지지 않았으니). 그것은 일만 자루의 총검으로 곤두서고 스무 개의 독수리 군기로 빛나며 라에생트 언덕을 밀고 올라가는 네 장군의 종대였다. 그것은 언덕을 뛰어 내려가 야만적인 전투의 품으로 적을 끌어안으러 돌진하는, 쇠고기 먹는 영국 병사들의 함성이었다. 다시 말해, 커프가 기개를 잔뜩 끌어모아 다가왔으나 완전히 비틀비틀 휘청거리는 가운데, 그 무화과 상인은 여느 때처럼 적의 코에 왼손을 꽂아 넣어 마지막으로 그를 쓰러뜨린 것이다."]], [["\"I think that will do for him,\"", "\"이걸로 끝장이 났을걸.\" 무화과가 말했다."], ["Figs said, as his opponent dropped as neatly on the green as I have seen Jack Spot's ball plump into the pocket at billiards; and the fact is, when time was called, Mr.", "그의 상대가 풀밭 위로 어찌나 깔끔하게 곤두박질치던지, 마치 잭 스폿의 공이 당구대 포켓으로 쏙 떨어지는 것을 내가 본 그 모양 그대로였다."], ["Reginald Cuff was not able, or did not choose, to stand up again.", "그리고 사실인즉, 시작 신호가 떨어졌을 때 레지널드 커프 씨는 다시 일어설 수 없었거나, 일어서지 않기로 한 것이었다."]], [["And now all the boys set up such a shout for Figs as would have made you think he had been their darling champion through the whole battle; and as absolutely brought Dr.", "그러자 이제 아이들이 모두 무화과를 향해 어찌나 큰 함성을 질러 대던지, 마치 그가 시합 내내 자기네의 사랑받는 투사였던 것처럼 여겨질 지경이었다."], ["Swishtail out of his study, curious to know the cause of the uproar.", "그리고 그 함성이 어찌나 컸던지, 그 소동의 까닭이 궁금해진 스위시테일 박사가 서재에서 영락없이 뛰쳐나오고 말았다."], ["He threatened to flog Figs violently, of course; but Cuff, who had come to himself by this time, and was washing his wounds, stood up and said, \"It's my fault, sir--not Figs'--not Dobbin's.", "물론 그는 무화과를 호되게 매질하겠다고 으름장을 놓았다. 그러나 그새 정신을 차리고 상처를 씻고 있던 커프가 일어서서 말했다."], ["I was bullying a little boy; and he served me right.\"", "\"제 잘못입니다, 선생님. 무화과 잘못도, 도빈 잘못도 아닙니다."], ["By which magnanimous speech he not only saved his conqueror a whipping, but got back all his ascendancy over the boys which his defeat had nearly cost him.", "제가 어린아이를 괴롭히고 있었고, 그래서 당해도 싼 거였습니다.\" 이 도량 넓은 한마디로 그는 자기를 이긴 자가 매질당하는 것을 막아 주었을 뿐 아니라, 패배로 하마터면 잃을 뻔했던 아이들에 대한 자신의 위세를 고스란히 되찾았다."]], [["Young Osborne wrote home to his parents an account of the transaction.", "어린 오스본은 부모에게 이 사건의 전말을 적어 집으로 부쳤다."]], [["Sugarcane House, Richmond, March, 18--", "리치먼드, 슈거케인 하우스, 18××년 3월"]], [["DEAR MAMA,--I hope you are quite well.", "엄마께,—건강히 잘 지내시기를 바라요."], ["I should be much obliged to you to send me a cake and five shillings.", "케이크 하나랑 오 실링을 보내 주시면 정말 고맙겠어요. 여기서 커프랑 도빈이 싸웠어요."], ["There has been a fight here between Cuff & Dobbin.", "아시다시피 커프는 학교의 수탉이었거든요."], ["Cuff, you know, was the Cock of the School.", "두 사람이 열세 번을 맞붙었는데, 도빈이 이겼어요."], ["They fought thirteen rounds, and Dobbin Licked.", "그래서 커프는 이제 둘째 수탉밖에 안 돼요."], ["So Cuff is now Only Second Cock.", "이 싸움은 저 때문에 난 거예요."], ["The fight was about me. Cuff was licking me for breaking a bottle of milk, and Figs wouldn't stand it.", "제가 우유병을 깨뜨려서 커프가 저를 두들겨 패고 있었는데, 무화과가 그걸 못 참았거든요."], ["We call him Figs because his father is a Grocer--Figs & Rudge, Thames St., City--I think as he fought for me you ought to buy your Tea & Sugar at his father's.", "우리는 그 애를 무화과라고 불러요, 아버지가 식료품상이라서요—무화과 앤드 러지, 도심 템스 거리. 그 애가 저를 위해 싸워 줬으니, 엄마도 차랑 설탕은 그 애 아버지네서 사 주시면 좋겠어요."], ["Cuff goes home every Saturday, but can't this, because he has 2 Black Eyes.", "커프는 토요일마다 집에 가는데, 이번엔 눈이 두 개나 멍들어서 못 가요."], ["He has a white Pony to come and fetch him, and a groom in livery on a bay mare.", "그 애는 데리러 오는 흰 조랑말이 있고, 제복 입은 마부가 적갈색 암말을 타고 와요."], ["I wish my Papa would let me have a Pony, and I am", "우리 아빠도 조랑말을 갖게 해 주시면 좋겠어요. 저는"]], [["Your dutiful Son, GEORGE SEDLEY OSBORNE", "엄마의 효성스러운 아들, 조지 세들리 오스본 올림"]], [["P.S.--Give my love to little Emmy.", "추신.—꼬마 에미한테 사랑한다고 전해 주세요."], ["I am cutting her out a Coach in cardboard.", "그 애한테 줄 마차를 마분지로 오려서 만들고 있어요."], ["Please not a seed-cake, but a plum-cake.", "씨앗 케이크 말고 자두 케이크로 보내 주세요."]], [["In consequence of Dobbin's victory, his character rose prodigiously in the estimation of all his schoolfellows, and the name of Figs, which had been a byword of reproach, became as respectable and popular a nickname as any other in use in the school.", "도빈의 승리 덕분에 모든 학우들의 평가 속에서 그의 평판은 엄청나게 치솟았고, 비난의 대명사였던 무화과라는 이름은 학교에서 통용되는 그 어떤 별명 못지않게 점잖고 인기 있는 별명이 되었다."], ["\"After all, it's not his fault that his father's a grocer,\"", "\"결국, 아버지가 식료품상인 게 그 애 잘못은 아니잖아.\""], ["George Osborne said, who, though a little chap, had a very high popularity among the Swishtail youth; and his opinion was received with great applause.", "조지 오스본이 말했다. 그는 비록 꼬맹이였지만 스위시테일의 아이들 사이에서 대단한 인기를 누리고 있었던지라, 그의 의견은 우레와 같은 박수로 받아들여졌다."], ["It was voted low to sneer at Dobbin about this accident of birth. \"Old Figs\"", "도빈을 두고 그 출생의 우연을 비웃는 것은 천박한 짓이라고 결의되었다."], ["grew to be a name of kindness and endearment; and the sneak of an usher jeered at him no longer.", "\"늙은 무화과\"는 다정하고 정겨운 이름이 되었고, 그 비열한 사감조차 더는 그를 야유하지 않았다."]], [["And Dobbin's spirit rose with his altered circumstances.", "그리고 도빈의 기개도 달라진 처지와 함께 솟아올랐다."], ["He made wonderful advances in scholastic learning.", "그는 학업에서도 놀라운 진전을 보였다."], ["The superb Cuff himself, at whose condescension Dobbin could only blush and wonder, helped him on with his Latin verses; \"coached\"", "도빈으로서는 그 황송함에 그저 얼굴을 붉히며 놀랄 따름이던 저 빼어난 커프가, 몸소 그의 라틴어 시 작문을 도와주었다."], ["him in play-hours: carried him triumphantly out of the little-boy class into the middle-sized form; and even there got a fair place for him.", "놀이 시간에 그를 \"지도해\" 주었고, 그를 의기양양하게 꼬맹이 반에서 중간 반으로 끌어올려 주었으며, 거기서도 그에게 그럴듯한 자리를 마련해 주었다."], ["It was discovered, that although dull at classical learning, at mathematics he was uncommonly quick.", "그가 비록 고전 학문에는 둔하지만 수학에는 유달리 빠르다는 사실이 밝혀졌다."], ["To the contentment of all he passed third in algebra, and got a French prize-book at the public Midsummer examination.", "모두가 흐뭇해하는 가운데 그는 대수학에서 삼등을 했고, 한여름 공개 시험에서는 프랑스어 상으로 책을 받았다."], ["You should have seen his mother's face when Telemaque (that delicious romance) was presented to him by the Doctor in the face of the whole school and the parents and company, with an inscription to Gulielmo Dobbin.", "박사가 온 학교 학생들과 부모들, 그리고 손님들이 보는 앞에서 \"굴리엘무스 도빈에게\"라는 헌사를 적은 『텔레마크』(그 감미로운 로맨스)를 그에게 수여하던 그때 그 어머니의 표정을 보셨어야 하는 건데 말이다."], ["All the boys clapped hands in token of applause and sympathy.", "아이들이 모두 박수를 쳐 갈채와 공감을 표했다."], ["His blushes, his stumbles, his awkwardness, and the number of feet which he crushed as he went back to his place, who shall describe or calculate?", "얼굴을 붉히고, 발을 헛디디고, 어색해하고, 또 자기 자리로 돌아가는 길에 짓밟은 발이 도대체 몇 개였는지, 그 누가 묘사하거나 헤아릴 수 있겠는가?"], ["Old Dobbin, his father, who now respected him for the first time, gave him two guineas publicly; most of which he spent in a general tuck-out for the school: and he came back in a tail-coat after the holidays.", "이때 처음으로 그를 자랑스러워하게 된 그의 아버지 늙은 도빈은 여러 사람 앞에서 그에게 이 기니를 주었는데, 그는 그 대부분을 학교 아이들 모두에게 잔치를 베푸는 데 써 버렸다. 그리고 그는 방학이 끝난 뒤 연미복을 입고 돌아왔다."]], [["Dobbin was much too modest a young fellow to suppose that this happy change in all his circumstances arose from his own generous and manly disposition: he chose, from some perverseness, to attribute his good fortune to the sole agency and benevolence of little George Osborne, to whom henceforth he vowed such a love and affection as is only felt by children--such an affection, as we read in the charming fairy-book, uncouth Orson had for splendid young Valentine his conqueror.", "도빈은 너무도 겸손한 젊은이여서, 자기 처지의 이 행복한 변화가 자신의 너그럽고 사내다운 기질에서 비롯되었다고는 생각조차 하지 못했다. 그는 어떤 삐딱한 심사에서, 자신의 행운을 오로지 어린 조지 오스본의 주선과 호의 덕으로 돌리기를 택했고, 그 후로 그에게 오직 아이들만이 느낄 수 있는 그런 사랑과 애정을 바치기로 마음먹었다."], ["He flung himself down at little Osborne's feet, and loved him.", "저 매혹적인 동화책에서 읽듯, 거친 오르송이 자기를 이긴 눈부신 젊은이 발렌타인에게 품었던 바로 그런 애정 말이다."], ["Even before they were acquainted, he had admired Osborne in secret.", "그는 어린 오스본의 발치에 몸을 던지며 그를 사랑했다."], ["Now he was his valet, his dog, his man Friday.", "서로 알기도 전부터 그는 남몰래 오스본을 흠모했었다."], ["He believed Osborne to be the possessor of every perfection, to be the handsomest, the bravest, the most active, the cleverest, the most generous of created boys.", "이제 그는 그의 시종이요, 그의 개요, 그의 충복 프라이데이였다. 그는 오스본이야말로 온갖 완벽함을 다 갖춘 존재이며, 세상에 태어난 소년들 가운데 가장 잘생기고, 가장 용감하고, 가장 활동적이고, 가장 영리하고, 가장 너그러운 아이라고 믿었다."], ["He shared his money with him: bought him uncountable presents of knives, pencil-cases, gold seals, toffee, Little Warblers, and romantic books, with large coloured pictures of knights and robbers, in many of which latter you might read inscriptions to George Sedley Osborne, Esquire, from his attached friend William Dobbin--the which tokens of homage George received very graciously, as became his superior merit.", "그는 자기 돈을 그와 나누었고, 칼이며 필통이며 금딱지 도장이며 토피며 『리틀 워블러』며, 기사와 도적들의 큼직한 채색 삽화가 든 모험 소설들이며, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 선물을 그에게 사 주었다. 그 후자의 책들 가운데 여러 권에서는 \"조지 세들리 오스본 귀하께, 다정한 벗 윌리엄 도빈으로부터\"라는 헌사를 읽을 수 있었는데, 이 충정의 표시를 조지는 자기의 빼어난 자질에 걸맞게 더없이 너그럽게 받아들였다."]], [["So that Lieutenant Osborne, when coming to Russell Square on the day of the Vauxhall party, said to the ladies, \"Mrs.", "그리하여 오스본 중위는 복스홀 나들이가 있던 날 러셀 광장으로 와서 숙녀들에게 이렇게 말했던 것이다."], ["Sedley, Ma'am, I hope you have room; I've asked Dobbin of ours to come and dine here, and go with us to Vauxhall.", "\"세들리 부인, 자리가 있었으면 합니다만. 저희 연대의 도빈한테 여기 와서 같이 저녁을 먹고 복스홀에 함께 가자고 청해 두었거든요."], ["He's almost as modest as Jos.\"", "그 친구는 조스만큼이나 숫기가 없는 친구입니다.\""]], [["\"Modesty! pooh,\"", "\"숫기가 없다니!"], ["said the stout gentleman, casting a vainqueur look at Miss Sharp.", "흥.\" 그 뚱뚱한 신사가 샤프 양에게 정복자 같은 눈길을 던지며 말했다."]], [["\"He is--but you are incomparably more graceful, Sedley,\"", "\"정말 그렇다니까요—하지만 자네야 비할 데 없이 더 우아하지, 세들리.\""], ["Osborne added, laughing.", "오스본이 웃으며 덧붙였다."], ["\"I met him at the Bedford, when I went to look for you; and I told him that Miss Amelia was come home, and that we were all bent on going out for a night's pleasuring; and that Mrs.", "\"자네를 찾으러 갔다가 베드퍼드에서 그 친구를 만났어요. 그래서 그 친구한테 애밀리아 양이 집에 돌아왔고, 저희가 다들 하룻밤 즐기러 나가기로 했다고, 그리고 세들리 부인께서 그 친구가 아이들 잔치 때 펀치 사발을 깨뜨린 일을 용서해 주셨다고 말해 줬지요."], ["Sedley had forgiven his breaking the punch-bowl at the child's party. Don't you remember the catastrophe, Ma'am, seven years ago?\"", "칠 년 전의 그 참사 기억 안 나세요, 부인?\""]], [["\"Over Mrs. Flamingo's crimson silk gown,\"", "\"플라밍고 부인의 진홍색 비단 가운 위에다 엎질렀잖아요.\""], ["said good-natured Mrs. Sedley.", "마음씨 좋은 세들리 부인이 말했다."], ["\"What a gawky it was!", "\"어찌나 어설픈 녀석이던지!"], ["And his sisters are not much more graceful.", "게다가 그 누이들도 별로 더 우아하지가 않아요."], ["Lady Dobbin was at Highbury last night with three of them.", "어젯밤 도빈 부인이 그중 셋을 데리고 하이베리에 왔던걸요."], ["Such figures! my dears.\"", "어찌나 볼품없던지, 얘들아.\""]], [["\"The Alderman's very rich, isn't he?\"", "\"그 시의원 양반이 아주 부자라면서요, 안 그래요?\""], ["Osborne said archly.", "오스본이 짓궂게 말했다."], ["\"Don't you think one of the daughters would be a good spec for me, Ma'am?\"", "\"그 딸들 중 하나가 저한테 괜찮은 투자가 되지 않을까요, 부인?\""]], [["\"You foolish creature!", "\"이 어리석은 녀석 같으니!"], ["Who would take you, I should like to know, with your yellow face?\"", "그 누런 얼굴을 하고서 도대체 누가 너를 데려가겠니, 내가 좀 알았으면 좋겠구나?\""]], [["\"Mine a yellow face?", "\"제 얼굴이 누렇다고요?"], ["Stop till you see Dobbin.", "도빈을 보실 때까지 기다려 보세요. 아니, 그 친구는 황열병을 세 번이나 앓았다고요."], ["Why, he had the yellow fever three times; twice at Nassau, and once at St. Kitts.\"", "나소에서 두 번, 세인트키츠에서 한 번.\""]], [["\"Well, well; yours is quite yellow enough for us.", "\"그래그래, 우리가 보기엔 네 얼굴도 누렇기로는 충분하단다."], ["Isn't it, Emmy?\" Mrs.", "안 그러니, 에미?\""], ["Sedley said: at which speech Miss Amelia only made a smile and a blush; and looking at Mr.", "세들리 부인이 말했다. 이 말에 애밀리아 양은 그저 미소를 짓고 얼굴을 붉힐 뿐이었다."], ["George Osborne's pale interesting countenance, and those beautiful black, curling, shining whiskers, which the young gentleman himself regarded with no ordinary complacency, she thought in her little heart that in His Majesty's army, or in the wide world, there never was such a face or such a hero.", "조지 오스본 씨의 창백하고도 흥미로운 얼굴과, 그 젊은이 자신이 적잖은 만족감으로 바라보던 저 아름답고 검은, 곱슬곱슬 윤기 나는 구레나룻을 바라보면서, 그녀는 작은 가슴속으로 폐하의 군대에도, 아니 이 넓은 세상천지에도 저런 얼굴이나 저런 영웅은 다시없으리라고 생각했다."], ["\"I don't care about Captain Dobbin's complexion,\"", "\"도빈 대위의 얼굴빛이야 난 상관없어요.\""], ["she said, \"or about his awkwardness.", "그녀가 말했다. \"그 어색한 거동도요."], ["I shall always like him, I know,\"", "난 그분을 늘 좋아할 거예요, 그럼요.\""], ["her little reason being, that he was the friend and champion of George.", "그 작은 이유인즉, 그가 조지의 벗이자 그를 지켜 준 투사였기 때문이다."]], [["\"There's not a finer fellow in the service,\"", "\"군에 그 친구보다 더 훌륭한 사내는 없습니다.\""], ["Osborne said, \"nor a better officer, though he is not an Adonis, certainly.\"", "오스본이 말했다. \"또 그보다 더 나은 장교도 없고요, 물론 미남은 아니지만 말입니다.\""], ["And he looked towards the glass himself with much naivete; and in so doing, caught Miss Sharp's eye fixed keenly upon him, at which he blushed a little, and Rebecca thought in her heart, \"Ah, mon beau Monsieur!", "그러면서 그는 사뭇 천연덕스럽게 거울 쪽으로 눈길을 던졌는데, 그러다가 자기에게 날카롭게 고정된 샤프 양의 시선과 마주치고는 살짝 얼굴을 붉혔다. 그리고 레베카는 속으로 생각했다."], ["I think I have YOUR gauge\"--the little artful minx!", "\"아, 나의 잘생긴 신사 양반! 당신 속내쯤은 내가 다 꿰뚫어 본 것 같은데\"—이 약삭빠른 작은 여우 같으니!"]], [["That evening, when Amelia came tripping into the drawing-room in a white muslin frock, prepared for conquest at Vauxhall, singing like a lark, and as fresh as a rose--a very tall ungainly gentleman, with large hands and feet, and large ears, set off by a closely cropped head of black hair, and in the hideous military frogged coat and cocked hat of those times, advanced to meet her, and made her one of the clumsiest bows that was ever performed by a mortal.", "그날 저녁, 복스홀에서 한껏 사람들을 사로잡을 채비를 하고 흰 모슬린 드레스를 차려입은 애밀리아가, 종달새처럼 노래하며 장미처럼 싱그러운 모습으로 사뿐사뿐 응접실로 들어서는데—손과 발이 큼직하고 귀도 큼직한, 거기에 검은 머리를 바짝 깎아 그것이 더욱 도드라지는, 게다가 그 시절의 흉측한 장식 끈 달린 군복과 삼각모를 걸친 대단히 키 크고 볼품없는 신사 하나가 그녀를 맞으러 나아오더니, 인간이 일찍이 해 본 적 없을 만큼 어설픈 인사를 한 번 꾸벅했다."]], [["This was no other than Captain William Dobbin, of His Majesty's Regiment of Foot, returned from yellow fever, in the West Indies, to which the fortune of the service had ordered his regiment, whilst so many of his gallant comrades were reaping glory in the Peninsula.", "이는 다름 아닌 폐하의 보병 연대 소속 윌리엄 도빈 대위였다. 그 많은 용감한 전우들이 이베리아반도에서 영광을 거두고 있는 동안, 그의 연대는 군 복무의 운명에 따라 서인도제도로 보내졌고, 그는 거기서 황열병을 앓고 막 돌아온 참이었다."]], [["He had arrived with a knock so very timid and quiet that it was inaudible to the ladies upstairs: otherwise, you may be sure Miss Amelia would never have been so bold as to come singing into the room.", "그가 도착해 문을 두드린 소리가 어찌나 소심하고 조용하던지 위층의 숙녀들에게는 들리지 않을 정도였다. 그렇지 않았더라면, 장담컨대 애밀리아 양이 그토록 대담하게 노래를 부르며 방으로 들어섰을 리 없다."], ["As it was, the sweet fresh little voice went right into the Captain's heart, and nestled there.", "어쨌거나 그 달콤하고 싱그러운 작은 목소리는 곧장 대위의 가슴속으로 파고들어 거기 둥지를 틀었다."], ["When she held out her hand for him to shake, before he enveloped it in his own, he paused, and thought--\"Well, is it possible--are you the little maid I remember in the pink frock, such a short time ago--the night I upset the punch-bowl, just after I was gazetted?", "그녀가 악수를 청하며 손을 내밀었을 때, 그는 그 손을 자기 손으로 감싸 쥐기에 앞서 잠시 멈추고 생각했다—\"아니, 이게 정말 가능한 일인가—그대가 정말, 그토록 얼마 전에 분홍 드레스를 입고 있던, 내가 기억하는 그 어린 아가씨란 말인가—내가 임관 발령을 받고 나서 막 그 펀치 사발을 엎질렀던 그 밤의?"], ["Are you the little girl that George Osborne said should marry him?", "그대가 조지 오스본이 자기랑 결혼할 거라고 말하던 그 어린 소녀란 말인가?"], ["What a blooming young creature you seem, and what a prize the rogue has got!\"", "그대는 어쩜 이리도 활짝 피어난 젊은이로 보이는지, 또 그 악동 녀석은 어쩜 이런 보물을 거머쥐었는지!\""], ["All this he thought, before he took Amelia's hand into his own, and as he let his cocked hat fall.", "애밀리아의 손을 자기 손에 쥐기 전에, 삼각모를 떨어뜨리면서, 그는 이 모든 것을 생각했던 것이다."]], [["His history since he left school, until the very moment when we have the pleasure of meeting him again, although not fully narrated, has yet, I think, been indicated sufficiently for an ingenious reader by the conversation in the last page.", "학교를 떠난 뒤로 우리가 그를 다시 만나는 기쁨을 누리는 바로 이 순간까지의 그의 내력은, 비록 온전히 다 이야기되지는 않았으나, 앞 페이지의 대화를 통해 영리한 독자에게는 충분히 암시되었으리라고 나는 생각한다."], ["Dobbin, the despised grocer, was Alderman Dobbin--Alderman Dobbin was Colonel of the City Light Horse, then burning with military ardour to resist the French Invasion.", "멸시받던 식료품상 도빈은 도빈 시의원이 되었고—도빈 시의원은 도심 경기병대의 대령이 되었으니, 당시 그 부대는 프랑스의 침공에 맞서겠다는 군사적 열정으로 불타고 있었다."], ["Colonel Dobbin's corps, in which old Mr. Osborne himself was but an indifferent corporal, had been reviewed by the Sovereign and the Duke of York; and the colonel and alderman had been knighted.", "늙은 오스본 씨 본인은 보잘것없는 상병에 지나지 않았던 그 도빈 대령의 부대는 군주와 요크 공의 사열을 받았고, 그리하여 그 대령이자 시의원은 기사 작위를 받았다."], ["His son had entered the army: and young Osborne followed presently in the same regiment.", "그의 아들은 군에 입대했다. 그리고 젊은 오스본도 이내 같은 연대를 따라 들어갔다."], ["They had served in the West Indies and in Canada.", "그들은 서인도제도와 캐나다에서 복무했다."], ["Their regiment had just come home, and the attachment of Dobbin to George Osborne was as warm and generous now as it had been when the two were schoolboys.", "그들의 연대는 이제 막 본국으로 돌아온 참이었고, 도빈이 조지 오스본에게 품은 애정은 두 사람이 학생이던 시절 그대로 지금도 따뜻하고 너그러웠다."]], [["So these worthy people sat down to dinner presently.", "그리하여 이 훌륭한 사람들은 이내 저녁 식탁에 둘러앉았다."], ["They talked about war and glory, and Boney and Lord Wellington, and the last Gazette.", "그들은 전쟁과 영광에 대해, 그리고 보니와 웰링턴 경에 대해, 또 최근의 관보에 대해 이야기했다."], ["In those famous days every gazette had a victory in it, and the two gallant young men longed to see their own names in the glorious list, and cursed their unlucky fate to belong to a regiment which had been away from the chances of honour.", "그 유명한 시절에는 관보마다 승전보가 실려 있었고, 이 두 늠름한 젊은이는 그 영광스러운 명단에서 제 이름을 보고 싶어 애가 탔으며, 명예의 기회에서 멀리 떨어져 있던 연대에 속한 자신들의 불운한 팔자를 저주했다."], ["Miss Sharp kindled with this exciting talk, but Miss Sedley trembled and grew quite faint as she heard it.", "샤프 양은 이 흥미진진한 이야기에 신이 났으나, 세들리 양은 그것을 들으며 부르르 떨더니 아주 기진맥진해졌다."], ["Mr. Jos told several of his tiger-hunting stories, finished the one about Miss Cutler and Lance the surgeon; helped Rebecca to everything on the table, and himself gobbled and drank a great deal.", "조스 씨는 자기의 호랑이 사냥 이야기를 여러 편 늘어놓았고, 커틀러 양과 군의관 랜스에 관한 그 이야기를 마저 끝냈으며, 식탁 위의 온갖 음식을 레베카에게 권하면서, 자기 자신도 잔뜩 먹어 치우고 마구 들이켰다."]], [["He sprang to open the door for the ladies, when they retired, with the most killing grace--and coming back to the table, filled himself bumper after bumper of claret, which he swallowed with nervous rapidity.", "숙녀들이 자리를 뜰 때, 그는 더없이 매혹적인 우아함으로 벌떡 일어나 문을 열어 주었고—식탁으로 돌아와서는 클라레를 잔 가득 따라 연거푸 들이켰는데, 신경질적으로 다급하게 꿀꺽꿀꺽 삼키는 것이었다."]], [["\"He's priming himself,\"", "\"저 친구가 미리 기운을 돋우고 있는 거예요.\""], ["Osborne whispered to Dobbin, and at length the hour and the carriage arrived for Vauxhall.", "오스본이 도빈에게 속삭였고, 이윽고 복스홀로 떠날 시간과 함께 마차가 당도했다."]], [["Vauxhall\nI know that the tune I am piping is a very mild one (although there are some terrific chapters coming presently), and must beg the good-natured reader to remember that we are only discoursing at present about a stockbroker's family in Russell Square, who are taking walks, or luncheon, or dinner, or talking and making love as people do in common life, and without a single passionate and wonderful incident to mark the progress of their loves.", "내가 지금 연주하는 곡조가 매우 잔잔한 것임을 나도 안다(물론 머지않아 가공할 만한 장들이 다가오고 있긴 하지만). 그러니 마음씨 너그러운 독자께서는, 지금 우리가 다루고 있는 것이 그저 러셀 광장에 사는 어느 주식 중개인 집안의 이야기일 뿐이라는 점을 기억해 주시기를 청한다."], ["The argument stands thus--Osborne, in love with Amelia, has asked an old friend to dinner and to Vauxhall--Jos Sedley is in love with Rebecca.", "그들은 평범한 사람들이 흔히 그러하듯 산책을 하거나, 점심을 들거나, 저녁을 먹거나, 담소를 나누고 사랑을 속삭일 따름이며, 그들의 연애 과정을 수놓을 만한 격정적이고 놀라운 사건이라곤 단 하나도 없는 것이다. 사정은 이러하다."], ["Will he marry her?", "애밀리아에게 반한 오스본이 옛 친구를 저녁 식사와 보크스홀에 초대했고, 조스 세들리는 레베카에게 반해 있다."], ["That is the great subject now in hand.", "과연 그가 그녀와 결혼할 것인가? 이것이 지금 우리 손에 놓인 중대한 주제인 것이다."]], [["We might have treated this subject in the genteel, or in the romantic, or in the facetious manner.", "우리는 이 주제를 점잖은 방식으로, 또는 낭만적인 방식으로, 또는 익살스러운 방식으로 다룰 수도 있었을 것이다."], ["Suppose we had laid the scene in Grosvenor Square, with the very same adventures--would not some people have listened?", "만약 우리가 똑같은 사건들을 그로스브너 광장을 무대로 펼쳐 놓았다고 가정해 보자. 그랬다면 귀 기울여 들을 사람도 더러 있지 않았겠는가?"], ["Suppose we had shown how Lord Joseph Sedley fell in love, and the Marquis of Osborne became attached to Lady Amelia, with the full consent of the Duke, her noble father: or instead of the supremely genteel, suppose we had resorted to the entirely low, and described what was going on in Mr.", "조지프 세들리 경이 어떻게 사랑에 빠졌는지, 그리고 오스본 후작이 그 고귀한 아버지 공작의 흔쾌한 승낙을 받아 애밀리아 영애에게 어떻게 마음을 주게 되었는지를 보여 주었다고 가정해 보자. 혹은 그토록 더없이 점잖은 방식 대신, 정반대로 완전히 비천한 쪽으로 눈을 돌려, 세들리 씨 댁 부엌에서 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있었는지를 묘사했다고 가정해 보자."], ["Sedley's kitchen--how black Sambo was in love with the cook (as indeed he was), and how he fought a battle with the coachman in her behalf; how the knife-boy was caught stealing a cold shoulder of mutton, and Miss Sedley's new femme de chambre refused to go to bed without a wax candle; such incidents might be made to provoke much delightful laughter, and be supposed to represent scenes of \"life.\"", "흑인 샘보가 요리사에게 어떻게 반했는지(실제로 그랬다), 그리고 그녀를 두고 마부와 어떻게 한바탕 싸움을 벌였는지, 칼 닦는 소년이 식어 버린 양 어깨살을 훔치다 어떻게 들켰는지, 그리고 세들리 양의 새 몸종이 밀랍 양초가 없으면 잠자리에 들기를 거부했다는 이야기 말이다. 그런 사건들이라면 흥겨운 웃음을 자아내게 꾸밀 수도 있을 것이고, \"인생\"의 한 장면을 보여 주는 것으로 여겨질 수도 있을 것이다."], ["Or if, on the contrary, we had taken a fancy for the terrible, and made the lover of the new femme de chambre a professional burglar, who bursts into the house with his band, slaughters black Sambo at the feet of his master, and carries off Amelia in her night-dress, not to be let loose again till the third volume, we should easily have constructed a tale of thrilling interest, through the fiery chapters of which the reader should hurry, panting.", "혹은 이와 반대로, 우리가 무시무시한 것을 좋아하는 취향이 동하여, 새 몸종의 연인을 직업적인 강도로 만들고, 그가 패거리를 이끌고 집에 들이닥쳐 주인의 발치에서 흑인 샘보를 살육하고 애밀리아를 잠옷 바람으로 납치해 가서는 제3권에 이르도록 풀어 주지 않는 식으로 그렸다고 가정해 보자. 그랬다면 우리는 손쉽게 손에 땀을 쥐게 하는 흥미진진한 이야기를 지어냈을 것이고, 그 불타는 장들을 독자는 숨을 헐떡이며 허겁지겁 읽어 나갔을 것이다."], ["But my readers must hope for no such romance, only a homely story, and must be content with a chapter about Vauxhall, which is so short that it scarce deserves to be called a chapter at all.", "그러나 내 독자께서는 그런 로맨스를 바라서는 안 된다. 그저 소박한 이야기뿐이니, 보크스홀에 관한 한 장으로 만족하셔야 한다."], ["And yet it is a chapter, and a very important one too.", "그 장이 너무나 짧아서 장이라고 부르기조차 민망할 지경이긴 하다."], ["Are not there little chapters in everybody's life, that seem to be nothing, and yet affect all the rest of the history?", "그럼에도 그것은 분명 하나의 장이며, 그것도 매우 중요한 장인 것이다. 누구의 인생에나, 아무것도 아닌 듯 보이지만 그 나머지 이야기 전부에 영향을 미치는 자그마한 장들이 있지 않은가?"]], [["Let us then step into the coach with the Russell Square party, and be off to the Gardens.", "그러면 우리도 러셀 광장 일행과 함께 마차에 올라타 정원으로 떠나 보자."], ["There is barely room between Jos and Miss Sharp, who are on the front seat.", "앞자리에 앉은 조스와 샤프 양 사이에는 비집고 앉을 자리가 겨우 있을 뿐이다."], ["Mr. Osborne sitting bodkin opposite, between Captain Dobbin and Amelia.", "오스본 씨는 그 맞은편에서 도빈 대위와 애밀리아 사이에 바늘처럼 끼어 앉아 있다."]], [["Every soul in the coach agreed that on that night Jos would propose to make Rebecca Sharp Mrs. Sedley.", "마차에 탄 사람이라면 한 사람도 빠짐없이, 그날 밤 조스가 레베카 샤프를 세들리 부인으로 만들겠노라 청혼하리라는 데 의견이 일치했다."], ["The parents at home had acquiesced in the arrangement, though, between ourselves, old Mr.", "집에 있는 부모도 이 일을 묵인했다. 다만 우리끼리 이야기지만, 늙은 세들리 씨는 자기 아들에게 경멸에 가까운 감정을 품고 있었다."], ["Sedley had a feeling very much akin to contempt for his son.", "그는 아들이 허영심 많고, 이기적이고, 게으르고, 사내답지 못하다고 했다."], ["He said he was vain, selfish, lazy, and effeminate. He could not endure his airs as a man of fashion, and laughed heartily at his pompous braggadocio stories.", "그는 멋쟁이 행세를 하는 아들의 거드름을 견디지 못했고, 거들먹거리며 늘어놓는 허풍 섞인 이야기들을 듣고는 실컷 비웃었다."], ["\"I shall leave the fellow half my property,\"", "\"내 재산의 절반은 저 녀석한테 물려줄 작정이야.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said; \"and he will have, besides, plenty of his own; but as I am perfectly sure that if you, and I, and his sister were to die to-morrow, he would say 'Good Gad!'", "\"게다가 제 몫의 재산도 넉넉히 가지게 될 테고. 하지만 만약 너랑 나랑 제 누이까지 내일 당장 죽는다 해도, 저 녀석은 '저런!'"], ["and eat his dinner just as well as usual, I am not going to make myself anxious about him.", "한마디 내뱉고는 평소와 다름없이 저녁밥을 잘만 먹을 게 분명해. 그러니 나는 저 녀석 일로 마음 졸일 생각이 없네."], ["Let him marry whom he likes.", "마음에 드는 여자랑 결혼하라지."], ["It's no affair of mine.\"", "나하고는 상관없는 일이야.\""]], [["Amelia, on the other hand, as became a young woman of her prudence and temperament, was quite enthusiastic for the match.", "반면에 애밀리아는, 그 신중함과 성품을 지닌 젊은 여자답게, 이 혼사에 자못 열의를 보였다."], ["Once or twice Jos had been on the point of saying something very important to her, to which she was most willing to lend an ear, but the fat fellow could not be brought to unbosom himself of his great secret, and very much to his sister's disappointment he only rid himself of a large sigh and turned away.", "한두 번쯤 조스가 그녀에게 무언가 매우 중요한 말을 꺼내려 한 적이 있었고, 그녀도 기꺼이 귀 기울일 참이었다. 그러나 그 뚱뚱한 사내는 끝내 그 엄청난 비밀을 가슴에서 털어놓지 못했고, 누이를 몹시 실망시키며 그저 큰 한숨 한 번을 토해 내고는 고개를 돌려 버릴 뿐이었다."]], [["This mystery served to keep Amelia's gentle bosom in a perpetual flutter of excitement.", "이 수수께끼는 애밀리아의 여린 가슴을 끊임없는 흥분으로 두근거리게 만들었다."], ["If she did not speak with Rebecca on the tender subject, she compensated herself with long and intimate conversations with Mrs.", "그녀는 레베카와 그 애틋한 주제를 두고 이야기를 나누지는 못한 대신, 가정부 블렌킨솝 부인과 길고 은밀한 대화를 나누며 마음을 달랬다."], ["Blenkinsop, the housekeeper, who dropped some hints to the lady's-maid, who may have cursorily mentioned the matter to the cook, who carried the news, I have no doubt, to all the tradesmen, so that Mr.", "블렌킨솝 부인은 그 이야기를 몸종에게 슬쩍 흘렸고, 몸종은 그것을 지나가는 말처럼 요리사에게 언급했을 것이며, 요리사는 그 소식을 모든 상인에게 전했을 것이 분명하다."], ["Jos's marriage was now talked of by a very considerable number of persons in the Russell Square world.", "그리하여 조스 씨의 결혼은 이제 러셀 광장 세계의 상당히 많은 사람들 입에 오르내리게 된 것이었다."]], [["It was, of course, Mrs. Sedley's opinion that her son would demean himself by a marriage with an artist's daughter.", "물론 세들리 부인의 생각으로는, 아들이 화가의 딸과 결혼하는 것은 스스로 격을 떨어뜨리는 일이었다."], ["\"But, lor', Ma'am,\"", "\"하지만 어머나, 마님.\""], ["ejaculated Mrs.", "블렌킨솝 부인이 외쳤다."], ["Blenkinsop, \"we was only grocers when we married Mr.", "\"저희가 세들리 나리와 혼인했을 적엔 한낱 식료품 장수였습죠."], ["S., who was a stock-broker's clerk, and we hadn't five hundred pounds among us, and we're rich enough now.\"", "나리도 그때는 주식 중개인 밑에서 일하는 서기에 불과하셨고, 둘이 합쳐 오백 파운드도 없었습니다요. 그래도 지금은 이렇게 부유하지 않습니까.\""], ["And Amelia was entirely of this opinion, to which, gradually, the good-natured Mrs. Sedley was brought.", "애밀리아도 전적으로 같은 생각이었고, 마음씨 고운 세들리 부인도 차츰 그 의견 쪽으로 기울게 되었다."]], [["Mr. Sedley was neutral.", "세들리 씨는 중립이었다."], ["\"Let Jos marry whom he likes,\"", "\"조스가 좋아하는 여자랑 결혼하라지.\""], ["he said; \"it's no affair of mine.", "그가 말했다. \"나하고는 상관없는 일이야."], ["This girl has no fortune; no more had Mrs.", "이 처녀는 재산이 없지. 하지만 세들리 부인도 마찬가지였어."], ["Sedley. She seems good-humoured and clever, and will keep him in order, perhaps.", "성격도 쾌활하고 영리해 보이니, 어쩌면 저 녀석을 잘 다스릴지도 모르지."], ["Better she, my dear, than a black Mrs. Sedley, and a dozen of mahogany grandchildren.\"", "흑인 세들리 부인에다 마호가니 빛깔 손주 열두 명을 보느니, 여보, 차라리 저 처녀가 낫지.\""]], [["So that everything seemed to smile upon Rebecca's fortunes.", "그리하여 모든 일이 레베카의 운수를 향해 미소 짓는 듯 보였다."], ["She took Jos's arm, as a matter of course, on going to dinner; she had sate by him on the box of his open carriage (a most tremendous \"buck\"", "그녀는 저녁 식사 자리에 갈 때면 당연한 듯 조스의 팔짱을 꼈고, 그의 무개마차 마부석에 그와 나란히 앉기도 했다(그는 회색 말들을 몰며 그 자리에 의젓하고 위풍당당하게 앉아 있을 때면 그야말로 굉장한 \"멋쟁이\"였다)."], ["he was, as he sat there, serene, in state, driving his greys), and though nobody said a word on the subject of the marriage, everybody seemed to understand it.", "그리고 비록 누구 하나 결혼 이야기를 입 밖에 내지는 않았지만, 모두가 그 일을 이해하고 있는 듯했다."], ["All she wanted was the proposal, and ah!", "그녀에게 필요한 것은 청혼뿐이었다."], ["how Rebecca now felt the want of a mother!--a dear, tender mother, who would have managed the business in ten minutes, and, in the course of a little delicate confidential conversation, would have extracted the interesting avowal from the bashful lips of the young man!", "아, 이때 레베카는 어머니가 없다는 사실을 얼마나 절감했던가! 다정하고 자상한 어머니라면 단 십 분 만에 그 일을 처리하고, 은밀하고 섬세한 대화를 조금 나누는 사이에, 그 수줍은 청년의 입술에서 흥미로운 고백을 끄집어냈을 텐데 말이다!"]], [["Such was the state of affairs as the carriage crossed Westminster bridge.", "마차가 웨스트민스터 다리를 건널 무렵의 사정이 이러했다."]], [["The party was landed at the Royal Gardens in due time.", "일행은 때맞춰 왕립 정원에 도착했다."], ["As the majestic Jos stepped out of the creaking vehicle the crowd gave a cheer for the fat gentleman, who blushed and looked very big and mighty, as he walked away with Rebecca under his arm.", "위풍당당한 조스가 삐걱대는 마차에서 내리자 군중이 그 뚱뚱한 신사를 향해 환호를 보냈고, 그는 레베카를 팔에 낀 채 걸어가면서 얼굴을 붉히면서도 자못 거창하고 위풍당당해 보였다."], ["George, of course, took charge of Amelia.", "조지는 물론 애밀리아를 맡았다."], ["She looked as happy as a rose-tree in sunshine.", "그녀는 햇살을 받은 장미나무처럼 행복해 보였다."]], [["\"I say, Dobbin,\"", "\"이봐, 도빈.\""], ["says George, \"just look to the shawls and things, there's a good fellow.\"", "조지가 말했다. \"숄이랑 물건들 좀 맡아 주게나."], ["And so while he paired off with Miss Sedley, and Jos squeezed through the gate into the gardens with Rebecca at his side, honest Dobbin contented himself by giving an arm to the shawls, and by paying at the door for the whole party.", "자네는 참 좋은 친구야.\" 그리하여 그가 세들리 양과 짝을 이루고, 조스가 레베카를 곁에 낀 채 문을 비집고 정원 안으로 들어가는 동안, 정직한 도빈은 숄에 팔을 내어 주고 일행 전체의 입장료를 문간에서 치르는 것으로 만족했다."]], [["He walked very modestly behind them.", "그는 무척이나 겸손하게 그들 뒤를 따라 걸었다."], ["He was not willing to spoil sport.", "흥을 깨고 싶지 않았던 것이다."], ["About Rebecca and Jos he did not care a fig.", "레베카와 조스에 대해서는 손톱만큼도 관심이 없었다."], ["But he thought Amelia worthy even of the brilliant George Osborne, and as he saw that good-looking couple threading the walks to the girl's delight and wonder, he watched her artless happiness with a sort of fatherly pleasure.", "다만 그는 애밀리아라면 저 빛나는 조지 오스본에게도 부족함이 없는 여자라고 여겼고, 그 잘 어울리는 한 쌍이 산책로를 누비며 소녀를 기쁘고 황홀하게 만드는 모습을 지켜보면서, 그녀의 천진한 행복을 일종의 아버지 같은 흐뭇함으로 바라보았다."], ["Perhaps he felt that he would have liked to have something on his own arm besides a shawl (the people laughed at seeing the gawky young officer carrying this female burthen); but William Dobbin was very little addicted to selfish calculation at all; and so long as his friend was enjoying himself, how should he be discontented?", "어쩌면 그는 자기 팔에도 숄 말고 다른 무언가를 걸쳐 봤으면 좋겠다고 느꼈는지도 모른다(사람들은 그 꺽다리 젊은 장교가 여자의 짐을 들고 다니는 꼴을 보며 웃었다). 그러나 윌리엄 도빈은 이기적인 계산이라는 것에는 도무지 빠지는 일이 거의 없는 사람이었다."], ["And the truth is, that of all the delights of the Gardens; of the hundred thousand extra lamps, which were always lighted; the fiddlers in cocked hats, who played ravishing melodies under the gilded cockle-shell in the midst of the gardens; the singers, both of comic and sentimental ballads, who charmed the ears there; the country dances, formed by bouncing cockneys and cockneyesses, and executed amidst jumping, thumping and laughter; the signal which announced that Madame Saqui was about to mount skyward on a slack-rope ascending to the stars; the hermit that always sat in the illuminated hermitage; the dark walks, so favourable to the interviews of young lovers; the pots of stout handed about by the people in the shabby old liveries; and the twinkling boxes, in which the happy feasters made-believe to eat slices of almost invisible ham--of all these things, and of the gentle Simpson, that kind smiling idiot, who, I daresay, presided even then over the place--Captain William Dobbin did not take the slightest notice.", "그러니 친구가 즐거운 시간을 보내고 있는 한, 그가 어찌 불만스러울 수 있었겠는가? 그리고 실은, 그 정원의 온갖 즐길 거리들, 그러니까 늘 환히 밝혀 두는 십만 개의 여분 등불이며, 정원 한복판 금빛 조가비 차양 아래서 황홀한 가락을 연주하던 삼각모 쓴 바이올린 연주자들이며, 희극적인 노래든 감상적인 노래든 그곳에서 사람들의 귀를 사로잡던 가수들이며, 펄쩍거리는 런던내기 남녀들이 짝을 지어 펄쩍거리고 쿵쾅대고 웃어 가며 추던 시골 춤이며, 사키 부인이 별까지 닿는 느슨한 밧줄을 타고 하늘로 솟구쳐 오를 참이라고 알리던 신호며, 늘 등불 밝힌 은둔자 오두막에 앉아 있던 은둔자며, 젊은 연인들의 밀회에 그토록 안성맞춤이던 어두운 산책로며, 허름하고 낡은 제복 차림의 사람들이 돌리던 흑맥주 잔이며, 행복한 만찬객들이 거의 보이지도 않는 햄 조각을 먹는 시늉을 하던 반짝이는 칸막이 좌석이며, 그리고 그 마음씨 좋고 미소 짓는 얼간이 같은, 짐작건대 그 시절에도 그곳을 관장하고 있었을 점잖은 심프슨이며, 이 모든 것에 대해 윌리엄 도빈 대위는 눈곱만치의 관심도 두지 않았다."]], [["He carried about Amelia's white cashmere shawl, and having attended under the gilt cockle-shell, while Mrs. Salmon performed the Battle of Borodino (a savage cantata against the Corsican upstart, who had lately met with his Russian reverses)--Mr.", "그는 애밀리아의 흰 캐시미어 숄을 들고 다녔고, 금빛 조가비 차양 아래서 새먼 부인이 \"보로디노 전투\"(최근 러시아에서 참패를 맛본 그 코르시카 출신 벼락출세자를 겨냥한 사나운 합창곡)를 노래하는 동안 그 옆을 지키고 서 있었다."], ["Dobbin tried to hum it as he walked away, and found he was humming--the tune which Amelia Sedley sang on the stairs, as she came down to dinner.", "그러다 도빈 씨는 자리를 뜨면서 그 곡을 흥얼거려 보려 했는데, 정작 자기가 흥얼거리고 있는 것이 애밀리아 세들리가 저녁 식사를 하러 계단을 내려오며 부르던 그 곡조임을 깨달았다."]], [["He burst out laughing at himself; for the truth is, he could sing no better than an owl.", "그는 자기 자신에게 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 실은 그가 올빼미보다 나을 것 없는 노래 솜씨를 가졌기 때문이다."]], [["It is to be understood, as a matter of course, that our young people, being in parties of two and two, made the most solemn promises to keep together during the evening, and separated in ten minutes afterwards.", "물론 두 명씩 짝을 이룬 우리의 젊은이들이, 저녁 내내 함께 붙어 다니자고 더없이 엄숙한 약속을 했다가 그러고는 십 분 만에 뿔뿔이 흩어졌다는 것은 두말할 나위 없는 일이다."], ["Parties at Vauxhall always did separate, but 'twas only to meet again at supper-time, when they could talk of their mutual adventures in the interval.", "보크스홀에서의 일행이란 늘 흩어지기 마련이었으나, 그것은 그저 저녁 식사 때 다시 모여 그동안 각자 겪은 모험을 이야기하기 위함일 뿐이었다."]], [["What were the adventures of Mr.", "오스본 씨와 애밀리아 양의 모험은 어떠했던가?"], ["Osborne and Miss Amelia?", "그것은 비밀이다."], ["That is a secret.", "그러나 이것만은 분명히 해 두자."], ["But be sure of this--they were perfectly happy, and correct in their behaviour; and as they had been in the habit of being together any time these fifteen years, their tete-a-tete offered no particular novelty.", "그들은 더없이 행복했고, 행실은 나무랄 데 없었다. 그리고 그들은 지난 십오 년 동안 줄곧 함께 지내 온 사이였으므로, 그 단둘만의 시간이라고 해서 딱히 새로울 것은 없었다."]], [["But when Miss Rebecca Sharp and her stout companion lost themselves in a solitary walk, in which there were not above five score more of couples similarly straying, they both felt that the situation was extremely tender and critical, and now or never was the moment Miss Sharp thought, to provoke that declaration which was trembling on the timid lips of Mr.", "그러나 레베카 샤프 양과 그 뚱뚱한 동행이 호젓한 산책로로 접어들어 둘만의 길을 잃었을 때(물론 거기에도 비슷하게 거니는 한 백 쌍쯤은 더 있었지만), 두 사람은 그 상황이 더없이 애틋하고 결정적이라는 것을 느꼈다. 그리고 샤프 양은 지금이 아니면 영영 안 될 순간이라고, 세들리 씨의 그 소심한 입술에서 떨고 있는 고백을 끌어낼 순간이라고 생각했다."], ["Sedley. They had previously been to the panorama of Moscow, where a rude fellow, treading on Miss Sharp's foot, caused her to fall back with a little shriek into the arms of Mr.", "두 사람은 앞서 모스크바 파노라마를 구경했는데, 거기서 어떤 무례한 사내가 샤프 양의 발을 밟는 바람에 그녀가 작은 비명을 지르며 세들리 씨의 품으로 넘어진 일이 있었다."], ["Sedley, and this little incident increased the tenderness and confidence of that gentleman to such a degree, that he told her several of his favourite Indian stories over again for, at least, the sixth time.", "그리고 이 자그마한 사건은 그 신사의 애틋함과 자신감을 어찌나 북돋아 놓았던지, 그는 자기가 좋아하는 인도 이야기 몇 토막을 적어도 여섯 번째로 그녀에게 또다시 늘어놓았던 것이다."]], [["\"How I should like to see India!\"", "\"저도 인도를 한번 보고 싶어요!\""], ["said Rebecca.", "레베카가 말했다."]], [["\"SHOULD you?\"", "\"그래요?\""], ["said Joseph, with a most killing tenderness; and was no doubt about to follow up this artful interrogatory by a question still more tender (for he puffed and panted a great deal, and Rebecca's hand, which was placed near his heart, could count the feverish pulsations of that organ), when, oh, provoking!", "조지프가 더없이 애간장을 녹이는 다정함으로 말했다. 그러고는 이 교묘한 질문에 뒤이어 한층 더 다정한 물음을 던지려던 참이 분명했다(그는 연신 헐떡이며 숨을 몰아쉬고 있었고, 그의 심장께에 놓인 레베카의 손은 그 기관이 열에 들떠 두근거리는 박동을 헤아릴 수 있을 정도였으니까)."], ["the bell rang for the fireworks, and, a great scuffling and running taking place, these interesting lovers were obliged to follow in the stream of people.", "그런데, 아, 얄궂게도! 불꽃놀이를 알리는 종이 울렸고, 한바탕 부산하게 떠밀고 내달리는 소동이 벌어지는 통에, 이 흥미로운 연인들도 사람들의 물결을 따라가지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["Captain Dobbin had some thoughts of joining the party at supper: as, in truth, he found the Vauxhall amusements not particularly lively--but he paraded twice before the box where the now united couples were met, and nobody took any notice of him.", "도빈 대위는 저녁 식사 자리에 합석할까 하는 생각을 잠시 품었다. 사실 그는 보크스홀의 오락거리가 딱히 흥겹지 않다고 느꼈던 것이다."], ["Covers were laid for four.", "그러나 그는 이제 다시 모인 두 쌍이 마주 앉은 칸막이 좌석 앞을 두 번이나 어슬렁거렸건만, 아무도 그에게 눈길 한번 주지 않았다."], ["The mated pairs were prattling away quite happily, and Dobbin knew he was as clean forgotten as if he had never existed in this world.", "식기는 네 사람 분이 차려져 있었다. 짝지은 두 쌍은 더없이 행복하게 재잘거리고 있었고, 도빈은 마치 자기가 이 세상에 존재한 적조차 없는 양 깨끗이 잊혔다는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["\"I should only be de trop,\"", "\"내가 끼면 거추장스럽기만 하겠군.\""], ["said the Captain, looking at them rather wistfully.", "대위가 다소 쓸쓸하게 그들을 바라보며 말했다."], ["\"I'd best go and talk to the hermit,\"--and so he strolled off out of the hum of men, and noise, and clatter of the banquet, into the dark walk, at the end of which lived that well-known pasteboard Solitary.", "\"차라리 은둔자한테 가서 이야기나 나누는 게 낫겠어.\" 그러고는 그는 사람들의 웅성거림과 떠들썩한 소리, 잔치의 달그락거리는 소음에서 벗어나, 그 끝에 저 유명한 판지로 만든 은둔자가 살고 있는 어두운 산책로로 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 들어갔다."], ["It wasn't very good fun for Dobbin--and, indeed, to be alone at Vauxhall, I have found, from my own experience, to be one of the most dismal sports ever entered into by a bachelor.", "도빈에게는 그다지 즐거운 일이 아니었다. 그리고 사실 보크스홀에서 혼자가 된다는 것은, 내 경험에 비추어 보건대, 독신 남자가 빠져들 수 있는 가장 처량한 소일거리 중 하나인 것이다."]], [["The two couples were perfectly happy then in their box: where the most delightful and intimate conversation took place.", "그러는 동안 두 쌍은 칸막이 좌석에서 더없이 행복했고, 그곳에서는 가장 유쾌하고 다정한 대화가 오갔다."], ["Jos was in his glory, ordering about the waiters with great majesty.", "조스는 한껏 신이 나서 위풍당당하게 종업원들을 이리저리 부려 먹었다."], ["He made the salad; and uncorked the Champagne; and carved the chickens; and ate and drank the greater part of the refreshments on the tables.", "그는 샐러드를 만들고, 샴페인 코르크를 뽑고, 닭고기를 썰고, 식탁에 차려진 음식 대부분을 먹고 마셨다."], ["Finally, he insisted upon having a bowl of rack punch; everybody had rack punch at Vauxhall.", "마침내 그는 럭 펀치를 한 사발 가져오라고 우겼다. 보크스홀에서는 다들 럭 펀치를 마셨던 것이다."], ["\"Waiter, rack punch.\"", "\"여보게, 럭 펀치.\""]], [["That bowl of rack punch was the cause of all this history.", "바로 그 럭 펀치 한 사발이 이 모든 이야기의 원인이 되었다."], ["And why not a bowl of rack punch as well as any other cause?", "그리고 다른 어떤 것 못지않게, 럭 펀치 한 사발이라고 해서 원인이 되지 못할 까닭이 무엇이란 말인가?"], ["Was not a bowl of prussic acid the cause of Fair Rosamond's retiring from the world?", "청산가리 한 사발이 아름다운 로저먼드가 세상을 등진 원인이 아니었던가?"], ["Was not a bowl of wine the cause of the demise of Alexander the Great, or, at least, does not Dr.", "포도주 한 사발이 알렉산드로스 대왕이 세상을 떠난 원인이 아니었던가? 적어도 렘프리에르 박사는 그렇게 말하지 않던가?"], ["Lempriere say so?--so did this bowl of rack punch influence the fates of all the principal characters in this \"Novel without a Hero,\"", "그와 마찬가지로, 이 럭 펀치 한 사발이 우리가 지금 들려주고 있는 이 \"영웅 없는 소설\"의 주요 등장인물 모두의 운명에 영향을 미쳤던 것이다."], ["which we are now relating. It influenced their life, although most of them did not taste a drop of it.", "비록 그들 대부분은 그 펀치를 한 방울도 입에 대지 않았건만, 그것은 그들의 삶에 영향을 미쳤다."]], [["The young ladies did not drink it; Osborne did not like it; and the consequence was that Jos, that fat gourmand, drank up the whole contents of the bowl; and the consequence of his drinking up the whole contents of the bowl was a liveliness which at first was astonishing, and then became almost painful; for he talked and laughed so loud as to bring scores of listeners round the box, much to the confusion of the innocent party within it; and, volunteering to sing a song (which he did in that maudlin high key peculiar to gentlemen in an inebriated state), he almost drew away the audience who were gathered round the musicians in the gilt scollop-shell, and received from his hearers a great deal of applause.", "젊은 숙녀들은 그것을 마시지 않았다. 오스본도 그것을 좋아하지 않았다. 그 결과 그 뚱뚱한 식도락가 조스가 사발에 담긴 것을 죄다 마셔 버렸고, 그가 사발을 통째로 비운 결과는, 처음에는 놀랍기 그지없다가 이내 거의 보기 딱할 지경에 이른 흥겨움이었다. 그는 어찌나 큰 소리로 떠들고 웃어 댔던지 수십 명의 구경꾼이 좌석 주위로 몰려들었고, 그 안에 있던 죄 없는 일행은 몹시 난처해졌다. 게다가 그는 자청해서 노래까지 불렀는데(술 취한 신사들 특유의 청승맞게 높은 가락으로 불러 댔다), 금빛 조가비 차양 아래 악사들 주위에 모여 있던 청중까지 거의 빼앗다시피 끌어모았고, 듣는 이들로부터 대단한 갈채를 받았다."]], [["\"Brayvo, Fat un!\"", "\"잘한다, 뚱보!\""], ["said one; \"Angcore, Daniel Lambert!\"", "누군가 외쳤다. \"한 번 더, 대니얼 램버트!\""], ["said another; \"What a figure for the tight-rope!\"", "또 다른 사람이 외쳤다. \"줄타기에 딱 어울리는 몸매로구먼!\""], ["exclaimed another wag, to the inexpressible alarm of the ladies, and the great anger of Mr. Osborne.", "또 다른 익살꾼이 소리치자, 숙녀들은 이루 말할 수 없이 놀랐고 오스본 씨는 크게 노했다."]], [["\"For Heaven's sake, Jos, let us get up and go,\"", "\"제발, 조스, 우리 일어나서 갑시다.\""], ["cried that gentleman, and the young women rose.", "그 신사가 외쳤고, 젊은 여자들도 자리에서 일어섰다."]], [["\"Stop, my dearest diddle-diddle-darling,\"", "\"잠깐, 내 사랑스러운 둥기둥기 아가씨.\""], ["shouted Jos, now as bold as a lion, and clasping Miss Rebecca round the waist.", "이제 사자처럼 대담해진 조스가 외치며 레베카 양의 허리를 끌어안았다."], ["Rebecca started, but she could not get away her hand.", "레베카는 흠칫했지만, 손을 빼낼 수가 없었다."], ["The laughter outside redoubled.", "바깥의 웃음소리가 곱절로 커졌다."], ["Jos continued to drink, to make love, and to sing; and, winking and waving his glass gracefully to his audience, challenged all or any to come in and take a share of his punch.", "조스는 계속 마시고, 사랑을 속삭이고, 노래를 불렀다. 그러고는 눈을 찡긋거리며 청중을 향해 잔을 우아하게 흔들어 보이고는, 너 나 할 것 없이 누구든 들어와서 자기 펀치를 함께 들자고 청했다."]], [["Mr. Osborne was just on the point of knocking down a gentleman in top-boots, who proposed to take advantage of this invitation, and a commotion seemed to be inevitable, when by the greatest good luck a gentleman of the name of Dobbin, who had been walking about the gardens, stepped up to the box.", "오스본 씨가 이 초대를 틈타 한몫 끼려는 장화 신은 한 신사를 막 때려눕히려던 참이었고, 한바탕 소동이 피할 수 없어 보였다."], ["\"Be off, you fools!\"", "그런데 천만다행으로, 정원을 거닐고 있던 도빈이라는 이름의 한 신사가 그 좌석으로 다가왔다. \"꺼져, 이 멍청이들아!\""], ["said this gentleman--shouldering off a great number of the crowd, who vanished presently before his cocked hat and fierce appearance--and he entered the box in a most agitated state.", "이 신사는 그렇게 말하며 군중을 어깨로 우르르 밀쳐 냈고, 군중은 이내 그의 삼각모와 험상궂은 기색 앞에서 사라져 버렸다. 그러고는 그가 몹시 격앙된 상태로 좌석 안으로 들어섰다."]], [["\"Good Heavens!", "\"맙소사!"], ["Dobbin, where have you been?\"", "도빈, 자네 어디 있었던 거야?\" 오스본이 친구의 팔에서 흰 캐시미어 숄을 낚아채 애밀리아를 둘둘 감싸며 말했다."], ["Osborne said, seizing the white cashmere shawl from his friend's arm, and huddling up Amelia in it.--\"Make yourself useful, and take charge of Jos here, whilst I take the ladies to the carriage.\"", "\"쓸모 있게 좀 굴어서 여기 조스 좀 맡아 주게. 그동안 내가 숙녀들을 마차로 모실 테니.\""]], [["Jos was for rising to interfere--but a single push from Osborne's finger sent him puffing back into his seat again, and the lieutenant was enabled to remove the ladies in safety.", "조스가 끼어들겠다고 일어서려 했으나, 오스본의 손가락 한 번 미는 힘에 그는 헐떡이며 도로 자리에 주저앉았고, 덕분에 중위는 무사히 숙녀들을 데리고 나갈 수 있었다."], ["Jos kissed his hand to them as they retreated, and hiccupped out \"Bless you!", "조스는 그들이 물러가는 동안 손에 입을 맞춰 작별 인사를 보내며 \"잘 가요!"], ["Bless you!\"", "잘 가요!\""], ["Then, seizing Captain Dobbin's hand, and weeping in the most pitiful way, he confided to that gentleman the secret of his loves.", "하고 딸꾹질 섞인 소리를 내뱉었다. 그러더니 도빈 대위의 손을 붙들고 더없이 가련하게 흐느끼며, 그 신사에게 자기 사랑의 비밀을 털어놓았다."], ["He adored that girl who had just gone out; he had broken her heart, he knew he had, by his conduct; he would marry her next morning at St.", "그는 방금 나간 그 처녀를 흠모한다고, 자기 행실 때문에 그녀의 마음을 아프게 했음을 안다고, 분명히 그랬다고 했다."], ["George's, Hanover Square; he'd knock up the Archbishop of Canterbury at Lambeth: he would, by Jove!", "그는 내일 아침 하노버 광장의 세인트 조지 교회에서 그녀와 결혼하겠노라고, 램버스에 있는 캔터베리 대주교를 깨워서라도 그렇게 하겠노라고, 정말이지 맹세코 그러겠노라고, 대주교를 대기시켜 두겠노라고 했다."], ["and have him in readiness; and, acting on this hint, Captain Dobbin shrewdly induced him to leave the gardens and hasten to Lambeth Palace, and, when once out of the gates, easily conveyed Mr. Jos Sedley into a hackney-coach, which deposited him safely at his lodgings.", "그러자 도빈 대위는 이 말을 빌미 삼아, 정원을 나가 어서 램버스 궁으로 가자고 그를 교묘하게 구슬렸고, 일단 문밖으로 나오자 손쉽게 조스 세들리 씨를 삯마차에 태워 무사히 그의 숙소까지 데려다 놓았다."]], [["George Osborne conducted the girls home in safety: and when the door was closed upon them, and as he walked across Russell Square, laughed so as to astonish the watchman.", "조지 오스본은 처녀들을 무사히 집까지 바래다주었다. 그러고는 그들 뒤로 문이 닫히자, 러셀 광장을 가로질러 걸어가면서 야경꾼이 깜짝 놀랄 만큼 큰 소리로 웃어 댔다."], ["Amelia looked very ruefully at her friend, as they went up stairs, and kissed her, and went to bed without any more talking.", "애밀리아는 두 사람이 위층으로 올라가는 동안 친구를 몹시 안쓰러운 눈빛으로 바라보더니, 그녀에게 입을 맞추고는 더 이상 아무 말도 없이 잠자리에 들었다."]], [["\"He must propose to-morrow,\"", "\"그이는 내일 청혼할 거야.\""], ["thought Rebecca.", "레베카는 생각했다."], ["\"He called me his soul's darling, four times; he squeezed my hand in Amelia's presence.", "\"그이는 나를 네 번이나 마음속 연인이라고 불렀어. 애밀리아가 보는 앞에서 내 손을 꼭 쥐었지."], ["He must propose to-morrow.\"", "그이는 분명 내일 청혼할 거야.\""], ["And so thought Amelia, too.", "애밀리아도 똑같이 생각했다."], ["And I dare say she thought of the dress she was to wear as bridesmaid, and of the presents which she should make to her nice little sister-in-law, and of a subsequent ceremony in which she herself might play a principal part, &c., and &c., and &c., and &c.", "그리고 짐작건대 그녀는 자기가 신부 들러리로 입을 옷이며, 그 사랑스러운 새 올케에게 해 줄 선물이며, 머지않아 자기 자신이 주인공 노릇을 하게 될지도 모를 또 다른 예식 따위를 생각했을 것이다. 등등, 등등, 등등."]], [["Oh, ignorant young creatures!", "오, 철없는 젊은이들이여!"], ["How little do you know the effect of rack punch!", "그대들은 럭 펀치의 위력을 어찌 그리도 모르는가!"], ["What is the rack in the punch, at night, to the rack in the head of a morning?", "밤에 펀치 속에 든 럭이, 아침에 머릿속에 든 고통(rack)에 견주면 무엇이란 말인가?"], ["To this truth I can vouch as a man; there is no headache in the world like that caused by Vauxhall punch.", "이 진실에 대해서라면 나는 한 사람의 남자로서 보증할 수 있다. 세상에 보크스홀 펀치가 일으키는 두통만 한 두통은 없다."], ["Through the lapse of twenty years, I can remember the consequence of two glasses!", "이십 년의 세월이 흘렀건만, 나는 단 두 잔이 가져온 결과를 아직도 기억한다."], ["two wine-glasses!", "두 잔!"], ["but two, upon the honour of a gentleman; and Joseph Sedley, who had a liver complaint, had swallowed at least a quart of the abominable mixture.", "그것도 와인 잔으로 겨우 두 잔이었으니, 신사의 명예를 걸고 하는 말이다. 그런데 간이 좋지 않던 조지프 세들리는 그 끔찍한 혼합물을 적어도 한 쿼트는 들이켰던 것이다."]], [["That next morning, which Rebecca thought was to dawn upon her fortune, found Sedley groaning in agonies which the pen refuses to describe.", "레베카가 자기 운수가 환히 밝아 오리라 여겼던 그 이튿날 아침, 세들리는 붓으로는 차마 묘사할 수 없는 고통 속에서 신음하고 있었다."], ["Soda-water was not invented yet.", "소다수는 아직 발명되기 전이었다."], ["Small beer--will it be believed!--was the only drink with which unhappy gentlemen soothed the fever of their previous night's potation.", "믿어지겠는가만, 묽은 맥주가 전날 밤의 폭음으로 생긴 열기를 불행한 신사들이 달랠 수 있는 유일한 음료였다."], ["With this mild beverage before him, George Osborne found the ex-Collector of Boggley Wollah groaning on the sofa at his lodgings.", "그 순한 음료를 앞에 놓고 신음하고 있는 보글리 월라의 전직 징세관을, 조지 오스본은 그의 숙소 소파에서 발견했다."], ["Dobbin was already in the room, good-naturedly tending his patient of the night before.", "도빈은 이미 그 방에 와서, 마음씨 좋게 전날 밤의 환자를 돌보고 있었다."], ["The two officers, looking at the prostrate Bacchanalian, and askance at each other, exchanged the most frightful sympathetic grins.", "두 장교는 뻗어 있는 그 주신(酒神)의 신도를 바라보다가 서로를 곁눈질하며, 더없이 끔찍한 동정의 웃음을 주고받았다."], ["Even Sedley's valet, the most solemn and correct of gentlemen, with the muteness and gravity of an undertaker, could hardly keep his countenance in order, as he looked at his unfortunate master.", "신사 중에서도 가장 근엄하고 빈틈없는 사람인 세들리의 시종조차, 장의사 같은 침묵과 엄숙함을 지녔건만, 그 가엾은 주인을 바라보며 표정을 가다듬기가 여간 힘들지 않았다."]], [["\"Mr. Sedley was uncommon wild last night, sir,\"", "\"세들리 나리께서 어젯밤에 어찌나 사납게 구셨는지 모릅니다요, 나리.\""], ["he whispered in confidence to Osborne, as the latter mounted the stair.", "오스본이 계단을 올라올 때 그가 은밀히 귓속말로 속삭였다."], ["\"He wanted to fight the 'ackney-coachman, sir.", "\"삯마차 마부와 한판 붙으려 하셨답니다요, 나리."], ["The Capting was obliged to bring him upstairs in his harms like a babby.\"", "대위 나리께서 어쩔 수 없이 어린애처럼 두 팔로 안아서 위층까지 모셔 왔습죠.\""], ["A momentary smile flickered over Mr.", "브러시 씨가 말하는 동안 그 얼굴에 순간적으로 미소가 어른거렸다."], ["Brush's features as he spoke; instantly, however, they relapsed into their usual unfathomable calm, as he flung open the drawing-room door, and announced \"Mr. Hosbin.\"", "그러나 그것도 잠시, 그는 응접실 문을 활짝 열어젖히고 \"호스빈 나리\"라고 알리면서, 이내 평소의 헤아릴 길 없는 평정으로 돌아갔다."]], [["\"How are you, Sedley?\"", "\"좀 어떤가, 세들리?\""], ["that young wag began, after surveying his victim.", "그 젊은 익살꾼은 자기 희생양을 한번 훑어보고는 말문을 열었다."], ["\"No bones broke?", "\"부러진 데는 없고?"], ["There's a hackney-coachman downstairs with a black eye, and a tied-up head, vowing he'll have the law of you.\"", "아래층에 눈이 시퍼렇게 멍들고 머리에 붕대 감은 삯마차 마부가 와서는, 자네를 법대로 처리하겠다고 별러 대던데.\""]], [["\"What do you mean--law?\"", "\"무슨 소린가, 법이라니?\""], ["Sedley faintly asked.", "세들리가 힘없이 물었다."]], [["\"For thrashing him last night--didn't he, Dobbin?", "\"어젯밤에 그자를 흠씬 두들겨 팼잖나. 안 그런가, 도빈?"], ["You hit out, sir, like Molyneux.", "자네 그 몰리뉴처럼 주먹을 휘둘러 댔다고."], ["The watchman says he never saw a fellow go down so straight.", "야경꾼 말로는 사내가 그렇게 곧장 나가떨어지는 건 처음 봤다더군."], ["Ask Dobbin.\"", "도빈한테 물어보게.\""]], [["\"You DID have a round with the coachman,\"", "\"자네가 마부랑 한판 붙은 건 맞아.\""], ["Captain Dobbin said, \"and showed plenty of fight too.\"", "도빈 대위가 말했다. \"게다가 제법 사납게 싸우기도 했고.\""]], [["\"And that fellow with the white coat at Vauxhall!", "\"그리고 보크스홀에서 그 흰 코트 입은 친구는 또 어땠고!"], ["How Jos drove at him!", "조스가 어찌나 달려들던지!"], ["How the women screamed!", "여자들은 또 어찌나 비명을 질러 댔는지!"], ["By Jove, sir, it did my heart good to see you.", "정말이지, 자네 그 모습을 보니 내 속이 다 시원하더군."], ["I thought you civilians had no pluck; but I'll never get in your way when you are in your cups, Jos.\"", "난 자네 같은 민간인들은 배짱이 없는 줄 알았는데. 하지만 조스, 자네가 술이 거나하게 올랐을 땐 절대로 자네 앞길을 막지 않겠네.\""]], [["\"I believe I'm very terrible, when I'm roused,\"", "\"내가 화가 나면 꽤나 무서운 모양이지.\""], ["ejaculated Jos from the sofa, and made a grimace so dreary and ludicrous, that the Captain's politeness could restrain him no longer, and he and Osborne fired off a ringing volley of laughter.", "조스가 소파에서 내뱉으며, 어찌나 처량하고도 우스꽝스러운 표정을 지었던지, 대위의 예의범절도 더는 그를 억누르지 못했고, 그와 오스본은 한바탕 쩌렁쩌렁한 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["Osborne pursued his advantage pitilessly.", "오스본은 인정사정없이 우세를 몰아붙였다."], ["He thought Jos a milksop.", "그는 조스를 겁쟁이로 여겼다."], ["He had been revolving in his mind the marriage question pending between Jos and Rebecca, and was not over well pleased that a member of a family into which he, George Osborne, of the --th, was going to marry, should make a mesalliance with a little nobody--a little upstart governess.", "그는 조스와 레베카 사이에 걸려 있는 결혼 문제를 마음속으로 곱씹어 보고 있던 터였고, 자기, 그러니까 ××연대의 조지 오스본이 장차 장가들 집안의 일원이 한낱 보잘것없는 계집, 한낱 벼락 출세한 가정교사 따위와 격에 안 맞는 혼인을 한다는 것이 영 마뜩잖았던 것이다."], ["\"You hit, you poor old fellow!\"", "\"자네가 주먹질을 했다고, 이 가엾은 친구야!\""], ["said Osborne.", "오스본이 말했다."], ["\"You terrible!", "\"자네가 무섭다고?"], ["Why, man, you couldn't stand--you made everybody laugh in the Gardens, though you were crying yourself.", "이런, 이 사람아, 자네는 제대로 서 있지도 못했어. 자네 자신은 울고 있으면서도 정원에 있는 사람들을 죄다 웃겼다고."], ["You were maudlin, Jos.", "자네 그렇게 청승맞게 굴었어, 조스."], ["Don't you remember singing a song?\"", "자네가 노래 부른 거 기억 안 나나?\""]], [["\"A what?\"", "\"뭘 불렀다고?\""], ["Jos asked.", "조스가 물었다."]], [["\"A sentimental song, and calling Rosa, Rebecca, what's her name, Amelia's little friend--your dearest diddle-diddle-darling?\"", "\"감상적인 노래 말이야. 그리고 로자인지, 레베카인지, 그 이름이 뭐더라, 애밀리아의 그 자그마한 친구를 '내 사랑스러운 둥기둥기 아가씨'라고 부른 거 말이야.\""], ["And this ruthless young fellow, seizing hold of Dobbin's hand, acted over the scene, to the horror of the original performer, and in spite of Dobbin's good-natured entreaties to him to have mercy.", "그러더니 이 인정머리 없는 젊은이는 도빈의 손을 덥석 붙들고 그 장면을 그대로 재연해 보였고, 그 원조 연기자는 기겁을 했으며, 도빈이 마음씨 좋게 제발 좀 봐주라고 사정했건만 막무가내였다."]], [["\"Why should I spare him?\"", "\"내가 저 친구를 봐줘야 할 까닭이 뭔가?\""], ["Osborne said to his friend's remonstrances, when they quitted the invalid, leaving him under the hands of Doctor Gollop.", "친구가 만류하자 오스본이 말했다. 그들이 골롭 박사에게 환자를 맡겨 두고 자리를 뜬 뒤였다."], ["\"What the deuce right has he to give himself his patronizing airs, and make fools of us at Vauxhall?", "\"도대체 무슨 권리로 저 친구가 잘난 척 거들먹거리며 우리를 보크스홀에서 바보로 만든단 말인가?"], ["Who's this little schoolgirl that is ogling and making love to him?", "저 친구한테 추파를 던지며 사랑을 속삭이는 그 자그마한 여학생은 또 뭐고?"], ["Hang it, the family's low enough already, without HER.", "젠장, 그 여자가 끼어들지 않아도 저 집안은 이미 충분히 격이 떨어진다고."], ["A governess is all very well, but I'd rather have a lady for my sister-in-law.", "가정교사야 뭐 그럭저럭 괜찮지만, 난 내 처남댁만큼은 숙녀이길 바라네. 난 마음이 너그러운 사람이야."], ["I'm a liberal man; but I've proper pride, and know my own station: let her know hers.", "하지만 나름의 자존심이 있고 내 분수를 알지. 그 여자도 제 분수를 알게 해야 해."], ["And I'll take down that great hectoring Nabob, and prevent him from being made a greater fool than he is.", "그리고 난 저 거들먹대는 인도 부자 나리를 한 수 꺾어서, 지금보다 더한 바보가 되는 걸 막을 작정이야."], ["That's why I told him to look out, lest she brought an action against him.\"", "그래서 그 여자가 저 친구를 상대로 소송이라도 걸까 조심하라고 일러둔 거라네.\""]], [["\"I suppose you know best,\"", "\"자네가 잘 알아서 하겠지.\""], ["Dobbin said, though rather dubiously.", "도빈이 자못 미심쩍은 투로 말했다."], ["\"You always were a Tory, and your family's one of the oldest in England.", "\"자네야 늘 토리당원이었고, 자네 집안은 영국에서 가장 오래된 가문 중 하나니까."], ["But--\"", "하지만……\""]], [["\"Come and see the girls, and make love to Miss Sharp yourself,\"", "\"가서 처녀들이나 보세. 그리고 자네는 자네대로 샤프 양한테 사랑을 속삭여 보든가.\""], ["the lieutenant here interrupted his friend; but Captain Dobbin declined to join Osborne in his daily visit to the young ladies in Russell Square.", "여기서 중위가 친구의 말을 가로막았다. 그러나 도빈 대위는 러셀 광장의 젊은 숙녀들을 날마다 찾아가는 오스본의 방문길에 동참하기를 사양했다."]], [["As George walked down Southampton Row, from Holborn, he laughed as he saw, at the Sedley Mansion, in two different stories two heads on the look-out.", "조지가 홀번에서 사우샘프턴 거리로 내려가다가, 세들리 저택의 서로 다른 두 층에서 망을 보고 있는 두 개의 머리를 보고는 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["The fact is, Miss Amelia, in the drawing-room balcony, was looking very eagerly towards the opposite side of the Square, where Mr. Osborne dwelt, on the watch for the lieutenant himself; and Miss Sharp, from her little bed-room on the second floor, was in observation until Mr. Joseph's great form should heave in sight.", "사실인즉, 응접실 발코니에 나와 있던 애밀리아 양은 오스본 씨가 사는 광장 건너편을 향해 무척이나 간절하게 시선을 던지며 바로 그 중위를 기다리고 있었고, 삼 층 자기 자그마한 침실에 있던 샤프 양은 조지프 씨의 그 거대한 형체가 시야에 나타날 때까지 망을 보고 있었던 것이다."]], [["\"Sister Anne is on the watch-tower,\"", "\"앤 언니가 망루에서 망을 보고 있군그래.\" 그가 애밀리아에게 말했다."], ["said he to Amelia, \"but there's nobody coming\"; and laughing and enjoying the joke hugely, he described in the most ludicrous terms to Miss Sedley, the dismal condition of her brother.", "\"하지만 오는 사람은 아무도 없어.\" 그러고는 웃음을 터뜨리며 이 농담을 한껏 즐기면서, 세들리 양에게 그 오라비의 비참한 처지를 더없이 우스꽝스러운 말로 묘사해 보였다."]], [["\"I think it's very cruel of you to laugh, George,\"", "\"그렇게 웃다니 정말 너무 매정해요, 조지.\""], ["she said, looking particularly unhappy; but George only laughed the more at her piteous and discomfited mien, persisted in thinking the joke a most diverting one, and when Miss Sharp came downstairs, bantered her with a great deal of liveliness upon the effect of her charms on the fat civilian.", "그녀가 유난히 시무룩한 표정으로 말했다. 그러나 조지는 그 가련하고 풀 죽은 기색에 한층 더 웃어 댈 뿐, 이 농담이 더없이 재미나다고 고집하면서, 샤프 양이 아래층으로 내려오자 그 매력이 그 뚱뚱한 민간인에게 미친 영향을 두고 자못 흥겹게 그녀를 놀려 댔다."]], [["\"O Miss Sharp!", "\"오, 샤프 양!"], ["if you could but see him this morning,\"", "당신이 오늘 아침 그 사람 꼴을 봤어야 하는 건데.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said--\"moaning in his flowered dressing-gown--writhing on his sofa; if you could but have seen him lolling out his tongue to Gollop the apothecary.\"", "\"꽃무늬 실내복을 입고 끙끙 앓는 꼴이며, 소파에서 몸부림치는 꼴이라니. 약제사 골롭한테 혀를 쑥 빼물어 보이는 꼴을 봤어야 하는 건데 말이오.\""]], [["\"See whom?\"", "\"누구를요?\""], ["said Miss Sharp.", "샤프 양이 말했다."]], [["\"Whom?", "\"누구라니?"], ["O whom?", "오, 누구긴요?"], ["Captain Dobbin, of course, to whom we were all so attentive, by the way, last night.\"", "물론 도빈 대위지요. 그러고 보니 어젯밤에 우리 모두가 그렇게나 살뜰히 챙겨 드린 그 양반 말입니다.\""]], [["\"We were very unkind to him,\"", "\"우리가 그분께 너무 박정했어요.\""], ["Emmy said, blushing very much.", "에미가 얼굴을 몹시 붉히며 말했다."], ["\"I--I quite forgot him.\"", "\"전, 전 그분을 까맣게 잊고 있었어요.\""]], [["\"Of course you did,\"", "\"그야 물론 그랬겠지요.\""], ["cried Osborne, still on the laugh.", "오스본이 여전히 웃음을 머금은 채 외쳤다."]], [["\"One can't be ALWAYS thinking about Dobbin, you know, Amelia.", "\"늘 도빈만 생각하고 있을 수야 없지 않소, 애밀리아."], ["Can one, Miss Sharp?\"", "안 그렇소, 샤프 양?\""]], [["\"Except when he overset the glass of wine at dinner,\"", "\"저녁 식사 때 그분이 와인 잔을 엎질렀던 일 말고는,\""], ["Miss Sharp said, with a haughty air and a toss of the head, \"I never gave the existence of Captain Dobbin one single moment's consideration.\"", "샤프 양이 도도한 기색으로 고개를 홱 젖히며 말했다. \"전 도빈 대위라는 사람이 이 세상에 있다는 사실에 단 한순간도 마음을 쓴 적이 없답니다.\""]], [["\"Very good, Miss Sharp, I'll tell him,\"", "\"좋아요, 샤프 양, 그 사람한테 그대로 전하지요.\""], ["Osborne said; and as he spoke Miss Sharp began to have a feeling of distrust and hatred towards this young officer, which he was quite unconscious of having inspired.", "오스본이 말했다. 그가 이렇게 말하는 사이, 샤프 양은 이 젊은 장교에게 불신과 증오의 감정을 품기 시작했는데, 정작 그는 자기가 그런 감정을 불러일으켰다는 것을 전혀 알아채지 못했다."], ["\"He is to make fun of me, is he?\"", "\"이 사람이 나를 놀림감으로 삼겠다 이거지?\""], ["thought Rebecca.", "레베카는 생각했다."], ["\"Has he been laughing about me to Joseph?", "\"이 사람이 조지프한테 내 험담을 늘어놓은 걸까?"], ["Has he frightened him?", "그래서 그이한테 겁을 준 걸까?"], ["Perhaps he won't come.\"--A film passed over her eyes, and her heart beat quite quick.", "어쩌면 그이는 오지 않을지도 몰라.\" 그녀의 눈앞에 막이 한 겹 드리워졌고, 가슴이 몹시 빠르게 뛰었다."]], [["\"You're always joking,\"", "\"당신은 늘 농담만 하시는군요.\""], ["said she, smiling as innocently as she could.", "그녀가 최대한 천진하게 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["\"Joke away, Mr.", "\"마음껏 농담하세요, 조지 씨."], ["George; there's nobody to defend ME.\"", "저를 두둔해 줄 사람은 아무도 없으니까요.\""], ["And George Osborne, as she walked away--and Amelia looked reprovingly at him--felt some little manly compunction for having inflicted any unnecessary unkindness upon this helpless creature.", "그리고 그녀가 걸어 나가는 동안 애밀리아가 자기를 나무라는 눈빛으로 바라보자, 조지 오스본은 이 가련하고 가냘픈 사람에게 공연한 박정함을 베푼 데 대해 사내로서 약간의 양심의 가책을 느꼈다. \"사랑하는 애밀리아.\""], ["\"My dearest Amelia,\"", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, \"you are too good--too kind.", "\"당신은 너무 착하고, 너무 마음씨가 고와요."], ["You don't know the world.", "당신은 세상을 모르오. 나는 알지."], ["I do. And your little friend Miss Sharp must learn her station.\"", "그리고 당신의 그 자그마한 친구 샤프 양도 제 분수를 깨우쳐야 해요.\""]], [["\"Don't you think Jos will--\"", "\"당신은 조스가 정말……\""]], [["\"Upon my word, my dear, I don't know.", "\"솔직히 말해서, 여보, 나도 모르겠소."], ["He may, or may not.", "그럴 수도 있고, 안 그럴 수도 있고."], ["I'm not his master.", "내가 그 사람 주인은 아니니까."], ["I only know he is a very foolish vain fellow, and put my dear little girl into a very painful and awkward position last night.", "다만 내가 아는 건, 그 사람이 아주 어리석고 허영심 많은 친구라는 것, 그리고 어젯밤에 내 사랑스러운 아가씨를 아주 곤혹스럽고 난처한 처지에 몰아넣었다는 것뿐이오."], ["My dearest diddle-diddle-darling!\"", "내 사랑스러운 둥기둥기 아가씨!\""], ["He was off laughing again, and he did it so drolly that Emmy laughed too.", "그는 또다시 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 어찌나 익살스럽게 굴던지 에미도 따라 웃고 말았다."]], [["All that day Jos never came.", "그날 하루 종일 조스는 끝내 오지 않았다."], ["But Amelia had no fear about this; for the little schemer had actually sent away the page, Mr.", "그러나 애밀리아는 이 일로 걱정하지 않았다."], ["Sambo's aide-de-camp, to Mr. Joseph's lodgings, to ask for some book he had promised, and how he was; and the reply through Jos's man, Mr.", "그 자그마한 책략가가 실은 샘보 씨의 부관 격인 사환을 조지프 씨의 숙소로 보내, 그가 약속했던 책을 가져오게 하고 또 그가 어떤지 안부를 물어 오게 했던 것이다."], ["Brush, was, that his master was ill in bed, and had just had the doctor with him.", "조스의 하인 브러시 씨를 통해 돌아온 대답은, 주인이 앓아누워 있으며 방금 의사가 다녀갔다는 것이었다."], ["He must come to-morrow, she thought, but she never had the courage to speak a word on the subject to Rebecca; nor did that young woman herself allude to it in any way during the whole evening after the night at Vauxhall.", "그이는 내일은 올 거야, 그녀는 생각했지만, 이 일에 대해서는 레베카에게 한마디도 꺼낼 용기가 차마 나지 않았다. 그리고 그 젊은 여자 자신도 보크스홀에서의 그날 밤 이후 저녁 내내 어떤 식으로도 그 일을 입에 올리지 않았다."]], [["The next day, however, as the two young ladies sate on the sofa, pretending to work, or to write letters, or to read novels, Sambo came into the room with his usual engaging grin, with a packet under his arm, and a note on a tray.", "그러나 그 이튿날, 두 젊은 숙녀가 소파에 앉아 바느질을 하는 척, 편지를 쓰는 척, 혹은 소설을 읽는 척하고 있을 때, 샘보가 여느 때처럼 사람 좋은 웃음을 띠고 겨드랑이에 꾸러미 하나를 끼고 쟁반에 쪽지 하나를 받쳐 든 채 방으로 들어왔다."], ["\"Note from Mr. Jos, Miss,\"", "\"조스 나리께서 보내신 쪽지입니다요, 아가씨.\""], ["says Sambo.", "샘보가 말했다."]], [["How Amelia trembled as she opened it!", "애밀리아는 그것을 펼치며 얼마나 떨었던가!"]], [["Dear Amelia,--I send you the \"Orphan of the Forest.\"", "쪽지의 내용은 이러했다. 사랑하는 애밀리아."], ["I was too ill to come yesterday.", "「숲속의 고아」를 보낸다."], ["I leave town to-day for Cheltenham.", "어제는 너무 아파서 못 갔구나. 나는 오늘 첼트넘으로 떠난다."], ["Pray excuse me, if you can, to the amiable Miss Sharp, for my conduct at Vauxhall, and entreat her to pardon and forget every word I may have uttered when excited by that fatal supper.", "보크스홀에서 한 내 행실에 대해, 가능하다면 그 상냥한 샤프 양에게 부디 사과를 전해 다오. 그리고 그 끔찍한 저녁 식사에 들떠서 내가 내뱉었을지 모를 말들을 모두 용서하고 잊어 달라고 그분께 간청해 다오."], ["As soon as I have recovered, for my health is very much shaken, I shall go to Scotland for some months, and am", "건강이 몹시 상한 터라, 회복되는 대로 나는 몇 달간 스코틀랜드로 떠날 생각이다. 그럼 이만."]], [["Truly yours, Jos Sedley", "너의 진실한 조스 세들리로부터."]], [["It was the death-warrant.", "그것은 사형 선고였다."], ["All was over.", "모든 것이 끝나 버렸다."], ["Amelia did not dare to look at Rebecca's pale face and burning eyes, but she dropt the letter into her friend's lap; and got up, and went upstairs to her room, and cried her little heart out.", "애밀리아는 차마 레베카의 창백한 얼굴과 불타는 눈을 바라볼 엄두가 나지 않아, 친구의 무릎 위에 그 편지를 떨어뜨리고는 자리에서 일어나 위층 자기 방으로 올라가, 그 가냘픈 가슴이 미어지도록 울었다."]], [["Blenkinsop, the housekeeper, there sought her presently with consolation, on whose shoulder Amelia wept confidentially, and relieved herself a good deal.", "이윽고 가정부 블렌킨솝이 위로의 말을 들고 그녀를 찾아왔고, 애밀리아는 그 어깨에 기대어 마음을 터놓고 울며 한결 시름을 덜었다."], ["\"Don't take on, Miss.", "\"너무 상심 마세요, 아가씨."], ["I didn't like to tell you. But none of us in the house have liked her except at fust.", "말씀드리기가 뭣했습니다만, 이 집 식구들 가운데 처음 말고는 그 아가씨를 좋아한 사람이 아무도 없답니다."], ["I sor her with my own eyes reading your Ma's letters.", "제 두 눈으로 그 아가씨가 마님 편지를 읽는 걸 똑똑히 봤어요."], ["Pinner says she's always about your trinket-box and drawers, and everybody's drawers, and she's sure she's put your white ribbing into her box.\"", "피너 말로는, 그 아가씨가 늘 아가씨 패물 상자며 서랍이며, 또 누구 할 것 없이 다들 서랍을 뒤지고 다닌다는데요, 아가씨 그 흰 리본도 자기 상자에 넣었을 게 분명하다지 뭡니까.\""]], [["\"I gave it her, I gave it her,\"", "\"그건 내가 준 거야, 내가 줬다고.\""], ["Amelia said.", "애밀리아가 말했다."]], [["But this did not alter Mrs. Blenkinsop's opinion of Miss Sharp.", "그러나 이 말도 블렌킨솝 부인이 샤프 양에 대해 품은 생각을 바꾸지는 못했다."], ["\"I don't trust them governesses, Pinner,\"", "\"난 그런 가정교사들은 못 믿어, 피너.\""], ["she remarked to the maid.", "그녀가 하녀에게 말했다."], ["\"They give themselves the hairs and hupstarts of ladies, and their wages is no better than you nor me.\"", "\"숙녀랍시고 거드름을 피우고 벼락출세한 티를 내지만, 받는 삯이라곤 너나 나보다 나을 게 없는 것들이야.\""]], [["It now became clear to every soul in the house, except poor Amelia, that Rebecca should take her departure, and high and low (always with the one exception) agreed that that event should take place as speedily as possible.", "이제 가엾은 애밀리아만 빼고 이 집안의 모든 사람에게, 레베카가 떠나야 한다는 것이 분명해졌고, 위아래 할 것 없이(언제나 그 한 사람만은 예외였다) 그 일이 되도록 빨리 이루어져야 한다는 데 의견을 같이했다."], ["Our good child ransacked all her drawers, cupboards, reticules, and gimcrack boxes--passed in review all her gowns, fichus, tags, bobbins, laces, silk stockings, and fallals--selecting this thing and that and the other, to make a little heap for Rebecca.", "우리의 착한 아이는 자기 서랍이며 찬장이며 손가방이며 자질구레한 상자들을 죄다 뒤져, 자기 가운이며 목도리며 장식끈이며 실패며 레이스며 비단 양말이며 온갖 자잘한 장신구를 하나하나 살펴보고는, 이것저것을 골라내어 레베카에게 줄 자그마한 꾸러미를 만들었다."], ["And going to her Papa, that generous British merchant, who had promised to give her as many guineas as she was years old--she begged the old gentleman to give the money to dear Rebecca, who must want it, while she lacked for nothing.", "그리고 자기 나이만큼의 기니를 주겠노라 약속했던 그 너그러운 영국 상인 아버지를 찾아가서는, 자기는 아쉬울 게 하나도 없지만 분명 그 돈이 필요할 사랑하는 레베카에게 그 돈을 주시라고 노신사에게 졸랐다."]], [["She even made George Osborne contribute, and nothing loth (for he was as free-handed a young fellow as any in the army), he went to Bond Street, and bought the best hat and spenser that money could buy.", "그녀는 심지어 조지 오스본에게도 한몫 보태게 했고, 조지도 기꺼이(그는 군대를 통틀어 둘째가라면 서러울 만큼 손이 큰 젊은이였으니까) 본드 거리로 가서 돈으로 살 수 있는 가장 좋은 모자와 짧은 외투를 사 왔다."]], [["\"That's George's present to you, Rebecca, dear,\"", "\"이건 조지가 너한테 주는 선물이야, 사랑하는 레베카.\""], ["said Amelia, quite proud of the bandbox conveying these gifts.", "애밀리아가 이 선물들이 담긴 상자를 자못 자랑스러워하며 말했다."], ["\"What a taste he has!", "\"그이는 정말 안목이 뛰어나지!"], ["There's nobody like him.\"", "그이 같은 사람은 또 없을 거야.\""]], [["\"Nobody,\"", "\"그래, 또 없겠지.\""], ["Rebecca answered.", "레베카가 대답했다."], ["\"How thankful I am to him!\"", "\"그분께 얼마나 감사한지 몰라!\" 그러나 그녀는 속으로 이렇게 생각하고 있었다."], ["She was thinking in her heart, \"It was George Osborne who prevented my marriage.\"--And she loved George Osborne accordingly.", "\"내 결혼을 막은 건 바로 그 조지 오스본이야.\" 그리고 그녀는 그만큼 조지 오스본을 사랑했다."]], [["She made her preparations for departure with great equanimity; and accepted all the kind little Amelia's presents, after just the proper degree of hesitation and reluctance.", "그녀는 더없이 평온한 마음으로 떠날 채비를 했고, 착한 애밀리아가 주는 온갖 선물을 꼭 알맞을 만큼만 망설이고 사양하는 시늉을 한 끝에 받아들였다. 그녀는 물론 세들리 부인에게 영원한 감사를 맹세했다."], ["She vowed eternal gratitude to Mrs.", "그러나 그 마음씨 좋은 부인에게 지나치게 들러붙지는 않았는데, 부인 쪽에서 거북해하며 자기를 피하고 싶어 하는 기색이 역력했기 때문이다."], ["Sedley, of course; but did not intrude herself upon that good lady too much, who was embarrassed, and evidently wishing to avoid her.", "세들리 씨가 돈주머니를 건네자 그녀는 그의 손에 입을 맞추었고, 앞으로 그를 자기의 더없이 자상한 벗이자 보호자로 여기게 해 달라고 청했다. 그녀의 태도가 어찌나 감동적이었던지 그는 이십 파운드짜리 수표를 한 장 더 써 줄 뻔했다."], ["She kissed Mr.", "그러나 그는 감정을 억눌렀다."], ["Sedley's hand, when he presented her with the purse; and asked permission to consider him for the future as her kind, kind friend and protector.", "그를 저녁 식사 자리로 데려갈 마차가 기다리고 있었으므로, 그는 이렇게 말하며 총총히 자리를 떴다. \"잘 가게, 얘야."], ["Her behaviour was so affecting that he was going to write her a cheque for twenty pounds more; but he restrained his feelings: the carriage was in waiting to take him to dinner, so he tripped away with a \"God bless you, my dear, always come here when you come to town, you know.--Drive to the Mansion House, James.\"", "런던에 올 때면 언제든 이리로 오너라, 알겠지? 제임스, 맨션 하우스로 가세.\""]], [["Finally came the parting with Miss Amelia, over which picture I intend to throw a veil.", "마침내 애밀리아 양과의 작별이 다가왔는데, 나는 이 장면 위로는 장막을 드리울 작정이다."], ["But after a scene in which one person was in earnest and the other a perfect performer--after the tenderest caresses, the most pathetic tears, the smelling-bottle, and some of the very best feelings of the heart, had been called into requisition--Rebecca and Amelia parted, the former vowing to love her friend for ever and ever and ever.", "그러나 한 사람은 진심이고 다른 한 사람은 완벽한 연기자였던 한바탕 장면이 지나가고, 더없이 다정한 포옹과 더없이 애절한 눈물과 각성제 병과 마음속 가장 진실한 감정의 일부까지 죄다 동원된 끝에, 레베카와 애밀리아는 헤어졌다. 전자는 영원토록, 영원토록, 또 영원토록 친구를 사랑하겠노라 맹세하면서."]], [["Crawley of Queen's Crawley\nAmong the most respected of the names beginning in C which the Court-Guide contained, in the year 18--, was that of Crawley, Sir Pitt, Baronet, Great Gaunt Street, and Queen's Crawley, Hants.", "18××년, 궁정 인명록에 실린 C로 시작하는 이름들 가운데 가장 존경받는 축에 드는 것이 바로 크롤리라는 이름이었다. 햄프셔주 그레이트곤트가와 퀸스크롤리에 적을 둔 준남작 핏 크롤리 경의 이름 말이다."], ["This honourable name had figured constantly also in the Parliamentary list for many years, in conjunction with that of a number of other worthy gentlemen who sat in turns for the borough.", "이 명예로운 이름은 또한 여러 해에 걸쳐 의회 명부에도 변함없이 등장했는데, 그 선거구를 번갈아 대표해 온 여러 다른 훌륭한 신사들의 이름과 나란히 올라 있었다."]], [["It is related, with regard to the borough of Queen's Crawley, that Queen Elizabeth in one of her progresses, stopping at Crawley to breakfast, was so delighted with some remarkably fine Hampshire beer which was then presented to her by the Crawley of the day (a handsome gentleman with a trim beard and a good leg), that she forthwith erected Crawley into a borough to send two members to Parliament; and the place, from the day of that illustrious visit, took the name of Queen's Crawley, which it holds up to the present moment. And though, by the lapse of time, and those mutations which age produces in empires, cities, and boroughs, Queen's Crawley was no longer so populous a place as it had been in Queen Bess's time--nay, was come down to that condition of borough which used to be denominated rotten--yet, as Sir Pitt Crawley would say with perfect justice in his elegant way, \"Rotten! be hanged--it produces me a good fifteen hundred a year.\"", "퀸스크롤리 선거구와 관련해서는 이런 이야기가 전한다. 엘리자베스 여왕이 순행 중에 크롤리에 들러 아침 식사를 하게 되었는데, 그때 그 시절의 크롤리(말끔하게 다듬은 수염에 잘빠진 다리를 지닌 잘생긴 신사였다)가 진상한 유난히 빼어난 햄프셔 맥주에 어찌나 흡족했던지, 즉석에서 크롤리를 의원 두 사람을 의회에 보낼 수 있는 선거구로 승격시켰다는 것이다. 그리고 이 고장은 그 영광스러운 행차의 날 이래로 퀸스크롤리라는 이름을 얻게 되었고, 그 이름을 지금 이 순간까지 지니고 있는 것이다. 그러나 세월이 흐르고, 제국이며 도시며 선거구에 나이가 가져오는 그 온갖 변천을 겪으면서, 퀸스크롤리는 엘리자베스 여왕 시절만큼 인구가 많은 고장이 더는 아니게 되었다. 아니, 이른바 부패 선거구라 일컬어지던 그런 처지로까지 영락하고 말았다. 그래도 핏 크롤리 경이 특유의 우아한 말투로 지극히 정당하게 내뱉곤 했듯이, \"부패라니, 그까짓 거 집어치우게나. 그 덕분에 내가 일 년에 천오백 파운드는 너끈히 벌어들이고 있잖나.\""]], [["Sir Pitt Crawley (named after the great Commoner) was the son of Walpole Crawley, first Baronet, of the Tape and Sealing-Wax Office in the reign of George II., when he was impeached for peculation, as were a great number of other honest gentlemen of those days; and Walpole Crawley was, as need scarcely be said, son of John Churchill Crawley, named after the celebrated military commander of the reign of Queen Anne.", "핏 크롤리 경(저 위대한 평민 정치가의 이름을 딴 것이었다)은 초대 준남작 월폴 크롤리의 아들이었는데, 월폴 크롤리는 조지 2세 치세에 봉인과 봉랍 관리국에 몸담았다가, 그 시절의 다른 정직한 신사들 상당수가 그러했듯, 공금 횡령 혐의로 탄핵당한 인물이었다. 그리고 월폴 크롤리는 굳이 말할 것도 없이 존 처칠 크롤리의 아들이었으니, 이 이름은 앤 여왕 치세의 그 저명한 군사령관의 이름을 딴 것이었다."], ["The family tree (which hangs up at Queen's Crawley) furthermore mentions Charles Stuart, afterwards called Barebones Crawley, son of the Crawley of James the First's time; and finally, Queen Elizabeth's Crawley, who is represented as the foreground of the picture in his forked beard and armour.", "그 가계도(퀸스크롤리에 걸려 있다)에는 그 밖에도 찰스 스튜어트 크롤리, 훗날 베어본즈 크롤리라 불린 인물이 언급되어 있는데, 그는 제임스 1세 시절 크롤리의 아들이었다. 그리고 마지막으로 엘리자베스 여왕 시절의 크롤리가 있었으니, 그는 갈라진 수염에 갑옷을 두른 모습으로 그림의 맨 앞에 그려져 있었다."], ["Out of his waistcoat, as usual, grows a tree, on the main branches of which the above illustrious names are inscribed.", "그의 조끼에서는 으레 그러하듯 나무 한 그루가 자라나고, 그 큰 가지마다 위에서 말한 그 영예로운 이름들이 새겨져 있었다."], ["Close by the name of Sir Pitt Crawley, Baronet (the subject of the present memoir), are written that of his brother, the Reverend Bute Crawley (the great Commoner was in disgrace when the reverend gentleman was born), rector of Crawley-cum-Snailby, and of various other male and female members of the Crawley family.", "준남작 핏 크롤리 경(이 전기의 주인공이다)의 이름 바로 곁에는 그의 동생인 목사 뷰트 크롤리(이 목사 양반이 태어났을 때 그 위대한 평민 정치가는 실각해 있었다)의 이름이 적혀 있었는데, 그는 크롤리컴스네일비의 교구 목사였다. 그 밖에도 크롤리 가문의 여러 남녀 일족의 이름이 적혀 있었다."]], [["Sir Pitt was first married to Grizzel, sixth daughter of Mungo Binkie, Lord Binkie, and cousin, in consequence, of Mr. Dundas.", "핏 경은 처음에 그리젤과 결혼했는데, 그녀는 빙키 경 멍고 빙키의 여섯째 딸이었고, 따라서 던다스 씨의 사촌이 되는 셈이었다."], ["She brought him two sons: Pitt, named not so much after his father as after the heaven-born minister; and Rawdon Crawley, from the Prince of Wales's friend, whom his Majesty George IV forgot so completely.", "그녀는 그에게 두 아들을 낳아 주었다. 핏은 아버지를 따랐다기보다는 저 하늘이 점지한 재상의 이름을 딴 것이었고, 로던 크롤리는 황태자의 벗이었던 인물의 이름을 딴 것이었으니, 그 인물을 국왕 조지 4세는 어찌나 깡그리 잊어버렸던지."], ["Many years after her ladyship's demise, Sir Pitt led to the altar Rosa, daughter of Mr. G.", "부인이 세상을 떠난 지 여러 해 뒤에 핏 경은 로자를 제단으로 이끌었으니, 그녀는 머드베리의 G."], ["Dawson, of Mudbury, by whom he had two daughters, for whose benefit Miss Rebecca Sharp was now engaged as governess.", "도슨 씨의 딸이었다. 그는 그녀에게서 두 딸을 얻었는데, 이제 그 딸들을 위하여 레베카 샤프 양이 가정교사로 고용된 것이었다."], ["It will be seen that the young lady was come into a family of very genteel connexions, and was about to move in a much more distinguished circle than that humble one which she had just quitted in Russell Square.", "보다시피 이 젊은 숙녀는 매우 품위 있는 연줄을 지닌 집안으로 들어가게 되었고, 방금 러셀 광장에서 떠나온 그 보잘것없는 무리보다 훨씬 더 격조 높은 사교계에서 활동하게 될 참이었다."]], [["She had received her orders to join her pupils, in a note which was written upon an old envelope, and which contained the following words:", "그녀가 제자들에게 합류하라는 명을 받은 것은 헌 봉투에 적힌 쪽지를 통해서였는데, 그 내용은 다음과 같았다."]], [["Sir Pitt Crawley begs Miss Sharp and baggidge may be hear on Tuesday, as I leaf for Queen's Crawley to-morrow morning ERLY.", "핏 크롤리 경은 샤프 양과 짐이 화요일까지 이리로 오기를 바라오. 나는 내일 아침 일찍 퀸스크롤리로 떠나기 때문이오."]], [["Great Gaunt Street.", "그레이트곤트가에서."]], [["Rebecca had never seen a Baronet, as far as she knew, and as soon as she had taken leave of Amelia, and counted the guineas which good-natured Mr.", "레베카는 자기가 아는 한 준남작이라는 사람을 본 적이 한 번도 없었다."], ["Sedley had put into a purse for her, and as soon as she had done wiping her eyes with her handkerchief (which operation she concluded the very moment the carriage had turned the corner of the street), she began to depict in her own mind what a Baronet must be.", "그래서 애밀리아와 작별을 고하고, 마음씨 고운 세들리 씨가 지갑에 넣어 준 기니를 헤아려 본 다음, 손수건으로 눈물을 닦는 일을 마치고 나자(이 일은 마차가 거리 모퉁이를 돌자마자 곧바로 끝내 버렸다), 그녀는 준남작이라는 사람이 대체 어떤 인물일지 마음속으로 그려 보기 시작했다."], ["\"I wonder, does he wear a star?\"", "\"별 모양 훈장을 달고 다닐까?\""], ["thought she, \"or is it only lords that wear stars?", "그녀는 생각했다. \"아니면 별 훈장은 귀족들만 다는 건가?"], ["But he will be very handsomely dressed in a court suit, with ruffles, and his hair a little powdered, like Mr. Wroughton at Covent Garden.", "어쨌든 그는 주름 장식이 달린 궁정 예복을 더없이 멋들어지게 차려입고, 코번트가든의 로턴 씨처럼 머리에는 분을 살짝 뿌렸겠지."], ["I suppose he will be awfully proud, and that I shall be treated most contemptuously.", "분명 무섭도록 거만할 테고, 나는 더없이 업신여기는 대접을 받겠지. 그래도 내 고된 운명은 되도록 잘 견뎌 내야 해."], ["Still I must bear my hard lot as well as I can--at least, I shall be amongst GENTLEFOLKS, and not with vulgar city people\": and she fell to thinking of her Russell Square friends with that very same philosophical bitterness with which, in a certain apologue, the fox is represented as speaking of the grapes.", "적어도 나는 점잖은 양반들 틈에 있게 될 테고, 천박한 도회지 사람들과 함께 있지는 않을 테니까.\" 그러고는 어느 우화에서 여우가 포도를 두고 말하는 그 모습 그대로의, 바로 그 철학적인 쓰라림을 품고서, 러셀 광장의 벗들을 떠올리기 시작했다."]], [["Having passed through Gaunt Square into Great Gaunt Street, the carriage at length stopped at a tall gloomy house between two other tall gloomy houses, each with a hatchment over the middle drawing-room window; as is the custom of houses in Great Gaunt Street, in which gloomy locality death seems to reign perpetual.", "곤트 광장을 지나 그레이트곤트가로 접어든 마차는 마침내 키 큰 음침한 두 집 사이에 끼인 키 큰 음침한 집 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 그 집들은 저마다 이층 응접실 한가운데 창문 위에 상문장이 걸려 있었는데, 그레이트곤트가의 집들 풍습이 다 그러했고, 이 음울한 동네에는 죽음이 영영 군림하는 듯했다."], ["The shutters of the first-floor windows of Sir Pitt's mansion were closed--those of the dining-room were partially open, and the blinds neatly covered up in old newspapers.", "핏 경 저택의 이층 창문 덧문은 닫혀 있었고, 식당 덧문은 반쯤 열려 있었으며, 차양은 헌 신문지로 말끔하게 덮여 있었다."]], [["John, the groom, who had driven the carriage alone, did not care to descend to ring the bell; and so prayed a passing milk-boy to perform that office for him. When the bell was rung, a head appeared between the interstices of the dining-room shutters, and the door was opened by a man in drab breeches and gaiters, with a dirty old coat, a foul old neckcloth lashed round his bristly neck, a shining bald head, a leering red face, a pair of twinkling grey eyes, and a mouth perpetually on the grin.", "마차를 혼자 몰고 온 마부 존은 굳이 내려서 종을 울리고 싶지 않았다. 그래서 지나가던 우유 배달 소년에게 그 일을 좀 해 달라고 부탁했다. 종이 울리자 식당 덧문 틈새로 머리 하나가 나타났고, 이윽고 문이 열리더니 한 남자가 모습을 드러냈다. 칙칙한 황갈색 반바지에 각반을 차고, 더러운 헌 외투를 걸쳤으며, 뻣뻣한 수염이 돋은 목에는 추레한 헌 목도리를 칭칭 동여매고 있었다. 번들거리는 대머리에, 곁눈질하는 벌건 얼굴, 반짝거리는 잿빛 눈 한 쌍, 그리고 줄곧 히죽거리는 입을 한 남자였다."]], [["\"This Sir Pitt Crawley's?\"", "\"여기가 핏 크롤리 경 댁이오?\""], ["says John, from the box.", "마부석에서 존이 물었다."]], [["\"Ees,\"", "\"그렇소.\""], ["says the man at the door, with a nod.", "문가의 남자가 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."]], [["\"Hand down these 'ere trunks then,\"", "\"그럼 여기 이 트렁크들 좀 내려 받으쇼.\""], ["said John.", "존이 말했다."]], [["\"Hand 'n down yourself,\"", "\"댁이 직접 내리시구려.\""], ["said the porter.", "문지기가 말했다."]], [["\"Don't you see I can't leave my hosses?", "\"내가 말들을 떠날 수 없는 거 안 보이오?"], ["Come, bear a hand, my fine feller, and Miss will give you some beer,\"", "자, 손 좀 거들어 주쇼, 양반. 그럼 아가씨가 맥주값이라도 줄 거요.\""], ["said John, with a horse-laugh, for he was no longer respectful to Miss Sharp, as her connexion with the family was broken off, and as she had given nothing to the servants on coming away.", "존이 너털웃음을 터뜨리며 말했으니, 그는 이제 더 이상 샤프 양에게 공손하게 굴지 않았던 것이다. 그녀와 그 집안의 연이 끊긴 데다, 떠나오면서 하인들에게 아무것도 쥐여 주지 않았기 때문이었다."]], [["The bald-headed man, taking his hands out of his breeches pockets, advanced on this summons, and throwing Miss Sharp's trunk over his shoulder, carried it into the house.", "대머리 남자는 반바지 주머니에서 두 손을 빼내더니 이 부름에 응해 다가왔고, 샤프 양의 트렁크를 어깨에 둘러메고 집 안으로 날랐다."]], [["\"Take this basket and shawl, if you please, and open the door,\"", "\"미안하지만 이 바구니랑 숄도 좀 받아 주고, 문도 열어 주세요.\""], ["said Miss Sharp, and descended from the carriage in much indignation.", "샤프 양이 말하고는, 잔뜩 분개한 채 마차에서 내렸다."], ["\"I shall write to Mr. Sedley and inform him of your conduct,\"", "\"세들리 씨께 편지를 써서 당신이 어떻게 처신했는지 알려 드리겠어요.\""], ["said she to the groom.", "그녀가 마부에게 말했다."]], [["\"Don't,\"", "\"그러지 마쇼.\""], ["replied that functionary.", "그 직책의 사내가 대꾸했다."], ["\"I hope you've forgot nothink?", "\"아무것도 안 빠뜨렸길 바라오."], ["Miss 'Melia's gownds--have you got them--as the lady's maid was to have 'ad?", "애밀리아 아가씨 드레스들 말이오, 챙겼소? 본래 몸종이 받기로 돼 있던 거 말이오."], ["I hope they'll fit you.", "댁한테 잘 맞기를 바라오."], ["Shut the door, Jim, you'll get no good out of 'ER,\"", "문 닫게, 짐. 저 여자한테선 콩고물 하나 안 떨어질 테니까.\""], ["continued John, pointing with his thumb towards Miss Sharp: \"a bad lot, I tell you, a bad lot,\"", "존은 엄지손가락으로 샤프 양을 가리키며 계속 지껄였다. \"못된 인간이야, 내 말 믿게, 못된 인간이라고.\""], ["and so saying, Mr. Sedley's groom drove away.", "그러고는 세들리 씨의 마부는 마차를 몰고 떠나가 버렸다."], ["The truth is, he was attached to the lady's maid in question, and indignant that she should have been robbed of her perquisites.", "사실인즉 그는 문제의 그 몸종에게 마음을 두고 있었고, 그녀가 응당 받을 부수입을 빼앗겼다는 데에 분개했던 것이다."]], [["On entering the dining-room, by the orders of the individual in gaiters, Rebecca found that apartment not more cheerful than such rooms usually are, when genteel families are out of town. The faithful chambers seem, as it were, to mourn the absence of their masters. The turkey carpet has rolled itself up, and retired sulkily under the sideboard: the pictures have hidden their faces behind old sheets of brown paper: the ceiling lamp is muffled up in a dismal sack of brown holland: the window-curtains have disappeared under all sorts of shabby envelopes: the marble bust of Sir Walpole Crawley is looking from its black corner at the bare boards and the oiled fire-irons, and the empty card-racks over the mantelpiece: the cellaret has lurked away behind the carpet: the chairs are turned up heads and tails along the walls: and in the dark corner opposite the statue, is an old-fashioned crabbed knife-box, locked and sitting on a dumb waiter.", "각반을 찬 그 사내의 안내에 따라 식당에 들어선 레베카는, 점잖은 집안 식구들이 시골에 가 있을 때면 으레 그러하듯, 그 방이 그런 방들이 흔히 그렇듯 조금도 화사하지 않다는 것을 알아차렸다. 충직한 방들은 마치 주인들의 부재를 애도하는 듯했다. 터키산 양탄자는 둘둘 말려 시무룩하게 찬장 밑으로 물러나 있었다. 그림들은 헌 갈색 종이 뒤에 얼굴을 감추고 있었다. 천장의 등은 음울한 갈색 삼베 자루에 싸여 있었다. 창문 커튼은 온갖 추레한 덮개 밑으로 자취를 감추었다. 월폴 크롤리 경의 대리석 흉상은 그 시커먼 구석에서 맨바닥과 기름칠한 부지깽이 한 벌, 그리고 벽난로 선반 위 텅 빈 명함꽂이를 바라보고 있었다. 술 보관함은 양탄자 뒤로 숨어들어 있었다. 의자들은 벽을 따라 위아래로 뒤집힌 채 포개져 있었다. 그리고 그 조각상 맞은편 어두운 구석에는 자물쇠가 채워진 구식 칼 보관함 하나가 까다로운 표정으로 식기 받침대 위에 놓여 있었다."]], [["Two kitchen chairs, and a round table, and an attenuated old poker and tongs were, however, gathered round the fire-place, as was a saucepan over a feeble sputtering fire.", "그래도 부엌 의자 두 개와 둥근 탁자 하나, 그리고 여위어 빠진 헌 부지깽이와 부젓가락은 벽난로 둘레에 모여 있었고, 약하게 지글거리는 불 위에는 냄비 하나가 걸려 있었다."], ["There was a bit of cheese and bread, and a tin candlestick on the table, and a little black porter in a pint-pot.", "탁자 위에는 치즈 한 조각과 빵, 양철 촛대 하나가 놓여 있었고, 한 파인트들이 단지에는 검은 흑맥주가 조금 담겨 있었다."]], [["\"Had your dinner, I suppose?", "\"저녁은 드셨겠지?"], ["It is not too warm for you?", "너무 덥진 않소?"], ["Like a drop of beer?\"", "맥주 한 잔 어떻소?\""]], [["\"Where is Sir Pitt Crawley?\"", "\"핏 크롤리 경은 어디 계신가요?\""], ["said Miss Sharp majestically.", "샤프 양이 위엄 있게 말했다."]], [["\"He, he!", "\"히, 히!"], ["I'm Sir Pitt Crawley.", "내가 핏 크롤리 경이오."], ["Reklect you owe me a pint for bringing down your luggage.", "댁 짐을 내려 줬으니 맥주 한 파인트 빚진 거 잊지 마쇼."], ["He, he!", "히, 히!"], ["Ask Tinker if I aynt. Mrs.", "정 못 믿겠거든 팅커한테 물어보쇼."], ["Tinker, Miss Sharp; Miss Governess, Mrs.", "팅커 부인, 이쪽은 샤프 양이오. 가정교사 아가씨, 이쪽은 잡일 하는 아주머니요."], ["Charwoman. Ho, ho!\"", "호, 호!\""]], [["The lady addressed as Mrs.", "이때 팅커 부인이라 불린 그 여인이 파이프 하나와 담배 한 봉지를 들고 나타났는데, 그것은 샤프 양이 도착하기 일 분 전에 그녀를 시켜 사 오게 한 것이었다."], ["Tinker at this moment made her appearance with a pipe and a paper of tobacco, for which she had been despatched a minute before Miss Sharp's arrival; and she handed the articles over to Sir Pitt, who had taken his seat by the fire.", "그녀는 그 물건들을 벽난로 옆에 자리를 잡고 앉은 핏 경에게 건넸다."]], [["\"Where's the farden?\"", "\"잔돈은 어디 있나?\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"I gave you three halfpence.", "\"내가 일 페니 반을 줬는데."], ["Where's the change, old Tinker?\"", "거스름돈은 어디 갔어, 늙은 팅커?\""]], [["\"There!\" replied Mrs.", "\"여기요!\""], ["Tinker, flinging down the coin; \"it's only baronets as cares about farthings.\"", "팅커 부인이 동전을 탁 내던지며 대꾸했다. \"파딩에 신경 쓰는 건 준남작이나 하는 짓이지.\""]], [["\"A farthing a day is seven shillings a year,\"", "\"하루에 일 파딩이면 일 년이면 칠 실링이야.\""], ["answered the M.P.; \"seven shillings a year is the interest of seven guineas.", "그 하원의원이 대꾸했다. \"칠 실링이면 칠 기니에 붙는 이자란 말이지."], ["Take care of your farthings, old Tinker, and your guineas will come quite nat'ral.\"", "파딩을 잘 간수해, 늙은 팅커, 그럼 기니는 절로 굴러들어 올 테니까.\""]], [["\"You may be sure it's Sir Pitt Crawley, young woman,\" said Mrs.", "\"이분이 핏 크롤리 경이 틀림없다는 건, 아가씨, 안심하셔도 돼요.\""], ["Tinker, surlily; \"because he looks to his farthings.", "팅커 부인이 퉁명스럽게 말했다. \"파딩까지 챙기시는 분이니 말이에요."], ["You'll know him better afore long.\"", "머지않아 이분을 더 잘 알게 되실 거예요.\""]], [["\"And like me none the worse, Miss Sharp,\"", "\"그래도 나를 조금도 덜 좋아하진 않을 거요, 샤프 양.\""], ["said the old gentleman, with an air almost of politeness.", "그 노신사가 거의 정중하다 할 만한 태도로 말했다."], ["\"I must be just before I'm generous.\"", "\"후하게 베풀기 전에 우선 공정해야 하는 법이니까.\""]], [["\"He never gave away a farthing in his life,\"", "\"이분은 평생 단 한 푼도 거저 내준 적이 없으세요.\""], ["growled Tinker.", "팅커가 으르렁대듯 말했다."]], [["\"Never, and never will: it's against my principle.", "\"그렇지, 앞으로도 그럴 거고. 그건 내 신조에 어긋나거든."], ["Go and get another chair from the kitchen, Tinker, if you want to sit down; and then we'll have a bit of supper.\"", "앉고 싶거든 부엌에 가서 의자 하나 더 가져와, 팅커. 그럼 우리도 저녁 좀 먹어 보세.\""]], [["Presently the baronet plunged a fork into the saucepan on the fire, and withdrew from the pot a piece of tripe and an onion, which he divided into pretty equal portions, and of which he partook with Mrs.", "이윽고 준남작은 불 위의 냄비에 포크를 푹 찔러 넣더니, 단지에서 양 곱창 한 점과 양파 한 개를 건져 냈다. 그는 그것을 거의 똑같은 몫으로 나누어, 팅커 부인과 함께 나눠 먹었다."], ["Tinker. \"You see, Miss Sharp, when I'm not here Tinker's on board wages: when I'm in town she dines with the family.", "\"보다시피 샤프 양, 내가 여기 없을 때면 팅커는 식대만 받고 알아서 끼니를 해결하지."], ["Haw! haw!", "내가 시내에 와 있을 땐 식구들과 함께 먹고."], ["I'm glad Miss Sharp's not hungry, ain't you, Tink?\"", "하! 하!"], ["And they fell to upon their frugal supper.", "샤프 양이 시장하지 않다니 다행이군, 안 그래, 팅크?\" 그러고는 그들은 그 소박한 저녁상에 달려들었다."]], [["After supper Sir Pitt Crawley began to smoke his pipe; and when it became quite dark, he lighted the rushlight in the tin candlestick, and producing from an interminable pocket a huge mass of papers, began reading them, and putting them in order.", "저녁을 먹고 나서 핏 크롤리 경은 파이프를 피우기 시작했다. 그리고 완전히 어두워지자, 그는 양철 촛대에 꽂힌 골풀 양초에 불을 붙이고는, 끝도 없이 깊은 주머니에서 어마어마한 서류 뭉치를 꺼내 그것을 읽으며 정리하기 시작했다."]], [["\"I'm here on law business, my dear, and that's how it happens that I shall have the pleasure of such a pretty travelling companion to-morrow.\"", "\"내가 여기 온 건 소송 일 때문이라오, 아가씨. 그러니 내일 이렇게 예쁜 길동무와 함께 가는 즐거움을 누리게 된 거지.\""]], [["\"He's always at law business,\" said Mrs.", "\"이분은 늘 소송 일에 매달려 계세요.\""], ["Tinker, taking up the pot of porter.", "팅커 부인이 흑맥주 단지를 집어 들며 말했다."]], [["\"Drink and drink about,\"", "\"한 모금씩 돌려 마시지.\""], ["said the Baronet.", "준남작이 말했다."], ["\"Yes; my dear, Tinker is quite right: I've lost and won more lawsuits than any man in England.", "\"그래, 아가씨, 팅커 말이 아주 옳아. 나는 영국의 그 누구보다도 많은 소송에서 지기도 하고 이기기도 했지."], ["Look here at Crawley, Bart. v.", "여기 이걸 보라고, 준남작 크롤리 대 스내플."], ["Snaffle. I'll throw him over, or my name's not Pitt Crawley.", "이 자를 보기 좋게 뒤엎어 줄 테야, 안 그러면 내 이름이 핏 크롤리가 아니지."], ["Podder and another versus Crawley, Bart.", "포더 외 일인 대 준남작 크롤리."], ["Overseers of Snaily parish against Crawley, Bart.", "스네일리 교구 감독관들 대 준남작 크롤리."], ["They can't prove it's common: I'll defy 'em; the land's mine.", "그자들은 그게 공유지라는 걸 증명할 수가 없어. 어디 한번 해 보라지."], ["It no more belongs to the parish than it does to you or Tinker here.", "그 땅은 내 거야. 그게 교구 것이 아닌 건 댁이나 여기 팅커 것이 아닌 거나 마찬가지지."], ["I'll beat 'em, if it cost me a thousand guineas.", "천 기니가 들더라도 그자들을 박살 내 줄 거요."], ["Look over the papers; you may if you like, my dear.", "이 서류들 한번 훑어보구려. 보고 싶으면 봐도 좋소, 아가씨."], ["Do you write a good hand?", "글씨는 좀 잘 쓰오?"], ["I'll make you useful when we're at Queen's Crawley, depend on it, Miss Sharp.", "퀸스크롤리에 가면 댁을 요긴하게 부려 먹어야겠어, 두고 보쇼, 샤프 양."], ["Now the dowager's dead I want some one.\"", "이제 노부인이 죽고 없으니 누가 좀 있어야겠단 말이지.\""]], [["\"She was as bad as he,\"", "\"그 부인도 이분 못지않게 지독했어요.\""], ["said Tinker.", "팅커가 말했다."], ["\"She took the law of every one of her tradesmen; and turned away forty-eight footmen in four year.\"", "\"거래하는 상인이란 상인은 죄다 상대로 소송을 걸어 댔고, 사 년 동안 하인을 마흔여덟 명이나 내쫓았으니까요.\""]], [["\"She was close--very close,\"", "\"그 사람 인색했지, 아주 인색했어.\""], ["said the Baronet, simply; \"but she was a valyble woman to me, and saved me a steward.\"--And in this confidential strain, and much to the amusement of the new-comer, the conversation continued for a considerable time.", "준남작이 담담하게 말했다. \"하지만 내겐 값진 여자였지."], ["Whatever Sir Pitt Crawley's qualities might be, good or bad, he did not make the least disguise of them.", "집사 하나 둘 돈을 아껴 줬으니까.\" 그리고 이런 흉허물 없는 어조로, 새로 온 손님을 무던히도 재미나게 해 주며 대화는 꽤 오랫동안 이어졌다."], ["He talked of himself incessantly, sometimes in the coarsest and vulgarest Hampshire accent; sometimes adopting the tone of a man of the world.", "핏 크롤리 경의 자질이 좋든 나쁘든, 그게 무엇이건 간에, 그는 그것을 조금도 감추려 들지 않았다. 그는 쉴 새 없이 자기 이야기를 늘어놓았는데, 때로는 더없이 거칠고 천박한 햄프셔 사투리로, 때로는 세상 물정에 밝은 사람의 말투를 빌려서였다."], ["And so, with injunctions to Miss Sharp to be ready at five in the morning, he bade her good night.", "그렇게, 그는 샤프 양에게 아침 다섯 시까지 채비를 마치라고 분부한 뒤 작별 인사를 건넸다."], ["\"You'll sleep with Tinker to-night,\"", "\"오늘 밤은 팅커와 함께 자도록 하시오.\""], ["he said; \"it's a big bed, and there's room for two.", "그가 말했다. \"큰 침대니까 둘이 자도 자리가 넉넉하지."], ["Lady Crawley died in it.", "크롤리 부인이 그 침대에서 죽었다오."], ["Good night.\"", "잘 자시구려.\""]], [["Sir Pitt went off after this benediction, and the solemn Tinker, rushlight in hand, led the way up the great bleak stone stairs, past the great dreary drawing-room doors, with the handles muffled up in paper, into the great front bedroom, where Lady Crawley had slept her last.", "이 축복의 인사를 건넨 뒤 핏 경은 가 버렸고, 근엄한 팅커는 골풀 양초를 손에 들고 앞장서서 그 크고 황량한 돌계단을 올라, 손잡이가 종이로 둘둘 싸인 그 크고 음산한 응접실 문들을 지나, 크롤리 부인이 마지막 잠을 잤던 그 커다란 앞쪽 침실로 들어갔다."], ["The bed and chamber were so funereal and gloomy, you might have fancied, not only that Lady Crawley died in the room, but that her ghost inhabited it.", "그 침대와 방은 어찌나 장례식장 같고 음울하던지, 크롤리 부인이 그 방에서 죽었을 뿐 아니라 그 유령이 거기 깃들어 있다고 여겨도 무방할 정도였다."], ["Rebecca sprang about the apartment, however, with the greatest liveliness, and had peeped into the huge wardrobes, and the closets, and the cupboards, and tried the drawers which were locked, and examined the dreary pictures and toilette appointments, while the old charwoman was saying her prayers.", "그러나 레베카는 더없이 발랄하게 방 안을 이리저리 돌아다니며, 늙은 잡역부가 기도를 올리는 동안, 그 거대한 옷장들과 벽장들과 찬장들을 들여다보고, 잠긴 서랍들을 당겨 보고, 그 음산한 그림들과 화장대 비품들을 살펴보았다."], ["\"I shouldn't like to sleep in this yeer bed without a good conscience, Miss,\"", "\"저 같으면 떳떳한 양심 없이는 이 침대에서 자고 싶지 않을 거예요, 아가씨.\""], ["said the old woman.", "늙은 여인이 말했다."], ["\"There's room for us and a half-dozen of ghosts in it,\"", "\"우리 둘에다 유령 여섯쯤 더 들어와도 자리가 넉넉하겠는걸요.\""], ["says Rebecca.", "레베카가 말했다."], ["\"Tell me all about Lady Crawley and Sir Pitt Crawley, and everybody, my DEAR Mrs. Tinker.\"", "\"크롤리 부인이랑 핏 크롤리 경이랑, 다들 어떤 사람들인지 죄다 들려주세요, 친애하는 팅커 부인.\""]], [["But old Tinker was not to be pumped by this little cross-questioner; and signifying to her that bed was a place for sleeping, not conversation, set up in her corner of the bed such a snore as only the nose of innocence can produce.", "그러나 늙은 팅커는 이 어린 캐묻기 선수에게 호락호락 속을 내줄 사람이 아니었다. 그녀는 침대란 잠을 자는 곳이지 이야기를 나누는 곳이 아니라는 뜻을 내비치고는, 침대 자기 쪽 구석에서, 오직 순결한 자의 코만이 낼 수 있는 그런 코골이를 시작했다."], ["Rebecca lay awake for a long, long time, thinking of the morrow, and of the new world into which she was going, and of her chances of success there.", "레베카는 오래오래 잠 못 이룬 채 누워, 내일 일을, 자기가 들어가게 될 그 새로운 세계를, 그리고 거기서 성공할 가능성을 곰곰이 헤아렸다."], ["The rushlight flickered in the basin.", "골풀 양초는 받침 접시 안에서 가물거렸다."], ["The mantelpiece cast up a great black shadow, over half of a mouldy old sampler, which her defunct ladyship had worked, no doubt, and over two little family pictures of young lads, one in a college gown, and the other in a red jacket like a soldier.", "벽난로 선반은 커다란 검은 그림자를 드리워, 곰팡이 핀 헌 자수본 절반을 뒤덮었는데, 그것은 분명 고인이 된 그 부인이 손수 놓은 것이리라. 그리고 그 그림자는 어린 두 소년을 그린 자그마한 가족 초상화 두 점도 덮었으니, 하나는 대학 가운을 입은 모습이었고, 다른 하나는 군인처럼 붉은 상의를 걸친 모습이었다."], ["When she went to sleep, Rebecca chose that one to dream about.", "잠이 들 때, 레베카는 꿈에 그릴 대상으로 그 후자를 골랐다."]], [["At four o'clock, on such a roseate summer's morning as even made Great Gaunt Street look cheerful, the faithful Tinker, having wakened her bedfellow, and bid her prepare for departure, unbarred and unbolted the great hall door (the clanging and clapping whereof startled the sleeping echoes in the street), and taking her way into Oxford Street, summoned a coach from a stand there.", "그레이트곤트가마저 화사해 보이게 만들 만큼 그토록 장밋빛으로 물든 어느 여름날 아침 네 시, 충직한 팅커는 잠동무를 깨워 떠날 채비를 하라 이른 다음, 큰 현관문의 빗장과 자물쇠를 끌렀고(그 철컹거리고 쾅 닫히는 소리가 거리에서 잠들어 있던 메아리들을 놀래켰다), 옥스퍼드가로 접어들어 거기 마차 대기소에서 삯마차 하나를 불러 세웠다."], ["It is needless to particularize the number of the vehicle, or to state that the driver was stationed thus early in the neighbourhood of Swallow Street, in hopes that some young buck, reeling homeward from the tavern, might need the aid of his vehicle, and pay him with the generosity of intoxication.", "그 마차의 번호가 무엇이었는지 시시콜콜 밝힐 것도 없고, 그 마부가 이렇게 이른 시각에 스왈로가 근처에 진을 치고 있었던 것은 어느 술집에서 비틀비틀 집으로 돌아가던 젊은 멋쟁이가 제 마차의 신세를 필요로 하여, 취기의 호기로 후한 삯을 쳐주지 않을까 하는 바람에서였다고 굳이 일러둘 것도 없다."]], [["It is likewise needless to say that the driver, if he had any such hopes as those above stated, was grossly disappointed; and that the worthy Baronet whom he drove to the City did not give him one single penny more than his fare. It was in vain that Jehu appealed and stormed; that he flung down Miss Sharp's bandboxes in the gutter at the 'Necks, and swore he would take the law of his fare.", "마찬가지로 굳이 말할 것도 없이, 그 마부가 위에서 말한 그런 바람을 품었더라면 그는 톡톡히 실망했을 터였다. 그가 시내까지 태워다 준 그 훌륭한 준남작은 그에게 정해진 삯에서 단 한 푼도 더 얹어 주지 않았으니 말이다. 그 마부가 사정하고 으름장을 놓아 봐야 다 헛수고였다. 그는 '넥스' 앞에서 샤프 양의 모자 상자들을 도랑에 내동댕이치며, 손님을 상대로 소송을 걸겠다고 욕설을 퍼부었다."]], [["\"You'd better not,\"", "\"그러지 않는 게 좋을 거요.\""], ["said one of the ostlers; \"it's Sir Pitt Crawley.\"", "마구간 일꾼 하나가 말했다. \"저분이 핏 크롤리 경이라고.\""]], [["\"So it is, Joe,\"", "\"그렇고말고, 조.\""], ["cried the Baronet, approvingly; \"and I'd like to see the man can do me.\"", "준남작이 흡족한 듯 외쳤다. \"나를 어떻게 해 볼 수 있는 자가 있거든 어디 한번 봤으면 좋겠군.\""]], [["\"So should oi,\"", "\"저도 봤으면 좋겠네요.\""], ["said Joe, grinning sulkily, and mounting the Baronet's baggage on the roof of the coach.", "조가 시무룩하게 히죽거리며 말하고는, 준남작의 짐을 마차 지붕 위에 올려 실었다."]], [["\"Keep the box for me, Leader,\"", "\"그 마부석은 내 자리로 비워 두게, 리더.\""], ["exclaims the Member of Parliament to the coachman; who replied, \"Yes, Sir Pitt,\"", "그 하원의원이 마부에게 외치자, 마부는 모자에 손을 살짝 갖다 대며 \"예, 핏 경\""], ["with a touch of his hat, and rage in his soul (for he had promised the box to a young gentleman from Cambridge, who would have given a crown to a certainty), and Miss Sharp was accommodated with a back seat inside the carriage, which might be said to be carrying her into the wide world.", "하고 대꾸했지만, 속으로는 분이 끓어올랐다(그는 그 자리를 케임브리지에서 온 어느 젊은 신사에게 약속해 둔 터였는데, 그 신사라면 영락없이 일 크라운은 쥐여 주었을 것이었다). 그리고 샤프 양은 마차 안쪽 뒷좌석에 자리를 잡게 되었으니, 그 마차가 그녀를 드넓은 세상 속으로 실어 나르는 셈이라 해도 좋을 것이었다."]], [["How the young man from Cambridge sulkily put his five great-coats in front; but was reconciled when little Miss Sharp was made to quit the carriage, and mount up beside him--when he covered her up in one of his Benjamins, and became perfectly good-humoured--how the asthmatic gentleman, the prim lady, who declared upon her sacred honour she had never travelled in a public carriage before (there is always such a lady in a coach--Alas!", "케임브리지에서 온 그 젊은이가 시무룩하게 제 외투 다섯 벌을 앞쪽에 쌓아 두었다가, 어린 샤프 양이 마차에서 내려 자기 곁에 올라앉게 되자 마음이 풀렸다는 것, 그리고 자기 두툼한 외투 한 벌로 그녀를 폭 덮어 주고는 더없이 기분이 좋아졌다는 것, 천식을 앓는 신사 하나와, 명예를 걸고 맹세컨대 대중 마차란 걸 평생 한 번도 타 본 적이 없다고 우기는 새침한 부인 하나(마차에는 늘 그런 부인이 한 명씩 있는 법이다. 아, 있었던 법이다."], ["was; for the coaches, where are they?), and the fat widow with the brandy-bottle, took their places inside--how the porter asked them all for money, and got sixpence from the gentleman and five greasy halfpence from the fat widow--and how the carriage at length drove away--now threading the dark lanes of Aldersgate, anon clattering by the Blue Cupola of St. Paul's, jingling rapidly by the strangers'", "그 마차들은, 지금 다 어디로 갔단 말인가?), 그리고 브랜디 병을 든 뚱뚱한 과부 하나가 안쪽에 자리를 잡았다는 것, 짐꾼이 그들 모두에게 돈을 청해 그 신사에게서는 육 펜스를, 뚱뚱한 과부에게서는 기름때 묻은 반 페니짜리 다섯 닢을 받아 냈다는 것, 그리고 마차가 마침내 출발하여, 어느새 올더스게이트의 어두운 골목들을 누비는가 하면, 이내 세인트폴 대성당의 푸른 둥근 지붕 곁을 덜컹거리며 지나고, 플리트 시장의 객 출입구를 딸랑딸랑 내달려 지났다는 것 그 출입구는 이제 엑서터 체인지와 함께 그림자의 세계로 떠나가 버렸다 그리고 그들이 피커딜리의 화이트베어 앞을 지나, 나이츠브리지의 채소밭에서 이슬이 피어오르는 광경을 보았다는 것, 그리고 턴햄그린과 브렌트우드와 백쇼트를 지났다는 것은 여기서 굳이 이야기할 필요가 없다."], ["entry of Fleet-Market, which, with Exeter 'Change, has now departed to the world of shadows--how they passed the White Bear in Piccadilly, and saw the dew rising up from the market-gardens of Knightsbridge--how Turnhamgreen, Brentwood, Bagshot, were passed--need not be told here.", "그러나 이 글을 쓰는 사람은, 지난날, 똑같이 화창한 날씨에, 바로 그 잊지 못할 여정을 따라가 본 적이 있는지라, 달콤하고도 애틋한 회한 없이는 그 길을 떠올릴 수가 없다."], ["But the writer of these pages, who has pursued in former days, and in the same bright weather, the same remarkable journey, cannot but think of it with a sweet and tender regret. Where is the road now, and its merry incidents of life?", "지금 그 길은 어디에 있으며, 그 길에 깃들었던 인생의 흥겨운 일화들은 다 어디로 갔는가?"], ["Is there no Chelsea or Greenwich for the old honest pimple-nosed coachmen?", "정직하고 코에 뾰루지가 돋은 그 늙은 마부들에게는 첼시도 그리니치도 없단 말인가?"], ["I wonder where are they, those good fellows?", "그 좋은 친구들, 다들 어디로 갔는지 궁금하구나."], ["Is old Weller alive or dead?", "늙은 웰러는 살았는가 죽었는가?"], ["and the waiters, yea, and the inns at which they waited, and the cold rounds of beef inside, and the stunted ostler, with his blue nose and clinking pail, where is he, and where is his generation?", "그리고 그 종업원들, 그래, 그들이 시중들던 그 여관들, 그 안에 차려져 있던 차가운 쇠고기 덩어리, 그리고 파란 코에 양동이를 절그럭거리던 그 땅딸막한 마구간 일꾼은, 그는 어디에 있으며 그의 후예들은 또 어디에 있는가?"], ["To those great geniuses now in petticoats, who shall write novels for the beloved reader's children, these men and things will be as much legend and history as Nineveh, or Coeur de Lion, or Jack Sheppard.", "지금은 치마를 입은 어린 나이인 그 위대한 천재들, 장차 사랑하는 독자의 자녀들을 위해 소설을 쓰게 될 그들에게, 이 사람들과 사물들은 니네베나 사자심왕이나 잭 셰퍼드 못지않은 전설이요 역사가 될 것이다. 그들에게 역마차란 낭만의 이야기가 되어 있을 것이요, 네 마리 밤색 말 한 조는 부케팔로스나 블랙 베스만큼이나 신화 속 존재가 되어 있을 것이다."], ["For them stage-coaches will have become romances--a team of four bays as fabulous as Bucephalus or Black Bess.", "아, 마구간지기들이 덮개 옷을 벗겨 내면 그 말들의 털가죽은 어찌나 윤이 났던지, 그러고는 쏜살같이 내달려 가던 모습이라니."], ["Ah, how their coats shone, as the stable-men pulled their clothes off, and away they went--ah, how their tails shook, as with smoking sides at the stage's end they demurely walked away into the inn-yard.", "아, 여정 끝에 옆구리에서 김을 내뿜으며 얌전히 여관 마당으로 걸어 들어갈 때, 그 꼬리들은 또 어찌나 흔들거렸던지. 아, 슬프도다!"], ["Alas! we shall never hear the horn sing at midnight, or see the pike-gates fly open any more.", "우리는 이제 다시는 한밤중에 울리는 그 나팔 소리를 듣지 못할 것이요, 통행세 관문이 활짝 열리는 광경도 두 번 다시 보지 못할 것이다."], ["Whither, however, is the light four-inside Trafalgar coach carrying us?", "그건 그렇고, 안에 네 사람을 태운 그 가벼운 트라팔가르 마차는 우리를 대체 어디로 실어 가고 있는가?"], ["Let us be set down at Queen's Crawley without further divagation, and see how Miss Rebecca Sharp speeds there.", "더는 옆길로 새지 말고 퀸스크롤리에 내려서, 레베카 샤프 양이 거기서 어떻게 일이 풀려 나가는지 지켜보기로 하자."]], [["Miss Rebecca Sharp to Miss Amelia Sedley, Russell Square, London.", "런던 러셀 광장의 애밀리아 세들리 양에게, 리베카 샤프 양으로부터."], ["(Free.--Pitt Crawley.)", "(무료 배송. — 핏 크롤리.)"]], [["# MY DEAREST, SWEETEST AMELIA,", "# MY DEAREST, SWEETEST AMELIA,"]], [["With what mingled joy and sorrow do I take up the pen to write to my dearest friend!", "더없이 사랑하고 더없이 다정한 애밀리아에게,\n이토록 기쁨과 슬픔이 뒤섞인 마음으로 더없이 소중한 벗에게 편지를 쓰려 펜을 드는구나!"], ["Oh, what a change between to-day and yesterday!", "아, 어제와 오늘이 이렇게나 다를 수가!"], ["Now I am friendless and alone; yesterday I was at home, in the sweet company of a sister, whom I shall ever, ever cherish!", "지금 나는 벗도 없이 외톨이가 되었는데, 어제까지만 해도 나는 집에서, 언제까지나 영원히 아끼고 또 아낄 한 자매의 다정한 벗 노릇을 하고 있었지!"]], [["I will not tell you in what tears and sadness I passed the fatal night in which I separated from you.", "너와 헤어진 그 운명의 밤을 내가 얼마나 눈물과 슬픔 속에 보냈는지는 말하지 않으련다."], ["YOU went on Tuesday to joy and happiness, with your mother and YOUR DEVOTED YOUNG SOLDIER by your side; and I thought of you all night, dancing at the Perkins's, the prettiest, I am sure, of all the young ladies at the Ball.", "너는 화요일에 어머니와, 또 네 곁에 있는 너의 헌신적인 젊은 군인과 함께 즐거움과 행복을 향해 떠났지. 나는 밤새도록 퍼킨스 댁에서 춤추는 네 모습을, 분명 무도회의 그 어느 젊은 숙녀보다도 아름다웠을 네 모습을 떠올렸단다."], ["I was brought by the groom in the old carriage to Sir Pitt Crawley's town house, where, after John the groom had behaved most rudely and insolently to me (alas!", "나는 마부의 손에 이끌려 낡은 마차를 타고 핏 크롤리 경의 시내 저택으로 보내졌어. 그곳에서 마부 존이 나를 더없이 무례하고 거만하게 대한 뒤에(아아!"], ["'twas safe to insult poverty and misfortune!), I was given over to Sir P.'s care, and made to pass the night in an old gloomy bed, and by the side of a horrid gloomy old charwoman, who keeps the house.", "가난과 불운을 모욕하는 일이란 참으로 안전한 일이지!), 나는 핏 경의 보살핌에 맡겨졌고, 음침한 낡은 침대에서, 이 집을 지키는 끔찍하고 음침한 늙은 잡일꾼 노파 곁에서 밤을 지새워야 했단다."], ["I did not sleep one single wink the whole night.", "나는 밤새 단 한순간도 눈을 붙이지 못했어."]], [["Sir Pitt is not what we silly girls, when we used to read Cecilia at Chiswick, imagined a baronet must have been.", "핏 경은 우리 어리석은 계집애들이 치즈윅에서 「세실리아」를 읽으며 준남작이라면 응당 이러이러하리라 상상하던 그런 사람이 아니야."], ["Anything, indeed, less like Lord Orville cannot be imagined.", "정말이지, 오빌 경과 이보다 더 닮지 않은 사람은 상상조차 할 수 없을 거야."], ["Fancy an old, stumpy, short, vulgar, and very dirty man, in old clothes and shabby old gaiters, who smokes a horrid pipe, and cooks his own horrid supper in a saucepan.", "늙고, 땅딸막하고, 키 작고, 천박하고, 몹시 지저분한 사내가 낡은 옷과 해진 낡은 각반 차림으로 끔찍한 파이프 담배를 피우며, 소스 냄비에 자기가 먹을 끔찍한 저녁밥을 손수 끓여 먹는 모습을 한번 떠올려 봐."], ["He speaks with a country accent, and swore a great deal at the old charwoman, at the hackney coachman who drove us to the inn where the coach went from, and on which I made the journey OUTSIDE FOR THE GREATER PART OF THE WAY.", "그는 촌스러운 사투리로 말하고, 늙은 노파에게며, 우리를 역마차가 출발하는 여관까지 데려다준 삯마차 마부에게며 욕설을 끝없이 퍼부어 댔어. 게다가 그 역마차 여정의 대부분을 나는 바깥자리에서 가야 했단다."]], [["I was awakened at daybreak by the charwoman, and having arrived at the inn, was at first placed inside the coach. But, when we got to a place called Leakington, where the rain began to fall very heavily--will you believe it?--I was forced to come outside; for Sir Pitt is a proprietor of the coach, and as a passenger came at Mudbury, who wanted an inside place, I was obliged to go outside in the rain, where, however, a young gentleman from Cambridge College sheltered me very kindly in one of his several great coats.", "나는 동틀 녘 그 노파에게 잠을 깼고, 여관에 도착해서는 처음엔 마차 안자리에 앉혀졌어. 그런데 리킹턴이라는 곳에 이르러 비가 몹시 쏟아지기 시작하자 — 너 이걸 믿을 수 있겠니? — 나는 어쩔 수 없이 바깥자리로 나가야 했단다. 핏 경이 그 마차의 소유주인 데다, 머드베리에서 안자리를 원하는 승객이 탔기 때문에, 나는 비를 맞으며 바깥으로 나가야만 했어. 그래도 케임브리지 칼리지에서 온 한 젊은 신사가 자기가 가진 여러 외투 가운데 하나로 나를 친절히 감싸 주었단다."]], [["This gentleman and the guard seemed to know Sir Pitt very well, and laughed at him a great deal.", "이 신사와 마차 차장은 핏 경을 아주 잘 아는 듯, 그를 두고 한참을 비웃더구나."], ["They both agreed in calling him an old screw; which means a very stingy, avaricious person.", "둘 다 입을 모아 그를 늙은 구두쇠라고 불렀어. 그건 몹시 인색하고 탐욕스러운 사람이란 뜻이야."], ["He never gives any money to anybody, they said (and this meanness I hate); and the young gentleman made me remark that we drove very slow for the last two stages on the road, because Sir Pitt was on the box, and because he is proprietor of the horses for this part of the journey.", "그는 누구에게도 한 푼 주는 법이 없다더구나(이런 비열함이 나는 정말 싫어). 그리고 그 젊은 신사는 마지막 두 구간을 우리가 아주 느릿느릿 달린 까닭을 내게 일깨워 주었는데, 그건 핏 경이 마부석에 앉아 있어서이고, 또 그 구간의 말들이 그의 소유였기 때문이래."], ["\"But won't I flog 'em on to Squashmore, when I take the ribbons?\"", "\"내가 고삐를 잡으면 스쿼시모어까지 저놈들을 채찍질로 몰아붙이지 않겠어?\""], ["said the young Cantab.", "그 젊은 케임브리지 학생이 말했어."], ["\"And sarve 'em right, Master Jack,\"", "\"그래야 싸지요, 잭 도련님.\""], ["said the guard.", "차장이 말했지."], ["When I comprehended the meaning of this phrase, and that Master Jack intended to drive the rest of the way, and revenge himself on Sir Pitt's horses, of course I laughed too.", "이 말의 뜻을, 그러니까 잭 도련님이 남은 길을 직접 몰아 핏 경의 말들에게 분풀이할 작정이라는 걸 알아듣고는, 나도 물론 웃고 말았단다."]], [["A carriage and four splendid horses, covered with armorial bearings, however, awaited us at Mudbury, four miles from Queen's Crawley, and we made our entrance to the baronet's park in state.", "하지만 머드베리에서, 퀸스 크롤리에서 사 마일 떨어진 그곳에서, 문장을 두른 훌륭한 말 네 필이 끄는 마차가 우리를 기다리고 있었고, 우리는 위풍당당하게 준남작의 영지로 들어섰어."], ["There is a fine avenue of a mile long leading to the house, and the woman at the lodge-gate (over the pillars of which are a serpent and a dove, the supporters of the Crawley arms), made us a number of curtsies as she flung open the old iron carved doors, which are something like those at odious Chiswick.", "저택으로 이어지는 일 마일 길이의 멋진 가로수길이 있었는데, 문지기 집 문(그 기둥들 위에는 크롤리 가문 문장을 받치는 뱀과 비둘기가 얹혀 있었어) 앞의 여인이 그 낡은 무쇠 세공 문을 활짝 열어젖히며 우리에게 연거푸 절을 했단다. 그 문은 그 지긋지긋한 치즈윅의 문과 어딘가 비슷하더구나."]], [["\"There's an avenue,\"", "\"여기 가로수길은,\""], ["said Sir Pitt, \"a mile long.", "핏 경이 말했어. \"일 마일이나 되지."], ["There's six thousand pound of timber in them there trees.", "저 나무들에 육천 파운드어치 목재가 들어 있어."], ["Do you call that nothing?\"", "그래도 그게 아무것도 아니라고 하겠나?\""], ["He pronounced avenue--EVENUE, and nothing--NOTHINK, so droll; and he had a Mr.", "그는 가로수길을 '가루수길'이라고, 아무것도를 '아무거또'라고 발음했는데, 어찌나 우습던지."], ["Hodson, his hind from Mudbury, into the carriage with him, and they talked about distraining, and selling up, and draining and subsoiling, and a great deal about tenants and farming--much more than I could understand.", "그리고 그는 머드베리에서 온 농장 일꾼 호드슨 씨를 마차에 함께 태웠는데, 둘은 압류며, 재산 처분이며, 배수며, 심토 갈이며, 소작인이며 농사 이야기를 끝없이 나누었어 — 내가 알아듣기엔 너무 많은 이야기였지."], ["Sam Miles had been caught poaching, and Peter Bailey had gone to the workhouse at last.", "샘 마일스가 밀렵을 하다 잡혔다고 하고, 피터 베일리는 결국 구빈원에 들어갔다더라."], ["\"Serve him right,\"", "\"그래 싸지.\""], ["said Sir Pitt; \"him and his family has been cheating me on that farm these hundred and fifty years.\"", "핏 경이 말했어. \"그자와 그자 집안이 백오십 년 동안 그 농장에서 날 속여 먹었으니까.\""], ["Some old tenant, I suppose, who could not pay his rent.", "짐작건대 소작료를 못 내는 어느 늙은 소작인인 듯해."], ["Sir Pitt might have said \"he and his family,\" to be sure; but rich baronets do not need to be careful about grammar, as poor governesses must be.", "핏 경은 물론 문법에 맞게 말할 수도 있었겠지만, 가난한 가정교사와 달리 부유한 준남작은 문법 따위를 신경 쓸 필요가 없는 법이지."]], [["As we passed, I remarked a beautiful church-spire rising above some old elms in the park; and before them, in the midst of a lawn, and some outhouses, an old red house with tall chimneys covered with ivy, and the windows shining in the sun.", "지나가는 길에 나는 영지 안의 오래된 느릅나무들 위로 솟아오른 아름다운 교회 첨탑을 보았어. 그리고 그 앞쪽, 잔디밭 한가운데, 몇몇 별채들 곁에는, 담쟁이덩굴로 뒤덮인 높은 굴뚝이 달린 낡은 붉은 벽돌집이 있고, 창문들이 햇빛에 반짝이고 있더구나."], ["\"Is that your church, sir?\"", "\"저게 어르신 교회인가요?\""], ["I said.", "내가 물었어."]], [["\"Yes, hang it,\"", "\"그래, 빌어먹을.\""], ["(said Sir Pitt, only he used, dear, A MUCH WICKEDER WORD); \"how's Buty, Hodson?", "(핏 경이 말했지. 다만, 얘야, 훨씬 더 고약한 말을 썼단다.) \"버티는 어떤가, 호드슨?"], ["Buty's my brother Bute, my dear--my brother the parson.", "버티는 내 동생 뷰트야, 얘야 — 목사인 내 동생 말이지."], ["Buty and the Beast I call him, ha, ha!\"", "나는 그놈을 버티와 야수라고 부른단다, 하하!\""]], [["Hodson laughed too, and then looking more grave and nodding his head, said, \"I'm afraid he's better, Sir Pitt.", "호드슨도 따라 웃더니, 이내 좀 더 진지한 표정으로 고개를 끄덕이며 말했어. \"유감스럽지만 좀 나아지신 모양입니다, 핏 경."], ["He was out on his pony yesterday, looking at our corn.\"", "어제 망아지를 타고 나오셔서 저희 밀밭을 둘러보셨어요.\""]], [["\"Looking after his tithes, hang'un (only he used the same wicked word).", "\"십일조를 챙기러 나왔겠지, 빌어먹을 놈.\""], ["Will brandy and water never kill him?", "(다만 그 똑같은 고약한 말을 썼어.) \"브랜디에 물 탄 것도 그놈을 못 죽이나?"], ["He's as tough as old whatdyecallum--old Methusalem.\"", "그 늙은 뭐냐그거 — 늙은 므두셀라처럼 질긴 놈이라니까.\""]], [["Mr. Hodson laughed again.", "호드슨 씨는 또 웃었어."], ["\"The young men is home from college.", "\"젊은 도련님들이 대학에서 돌아오셨습니다."], ["They've whopped John Scroggins till he's well nigh dead.\"", "존 스크로긴스를 거의 죽도록 두들겨 팼지 뭡니까.\""]], [["\"Whop my second keeper!\"", "\"내 둘째 사냥터지기를 두들겨 패!\""], ["roared out Sir Pitt.", "핏 경이 고함을 질렀어."]], [["\"He was on the parson's ground, sir,\"", "\"목사님 땅에 들어가 있었답니다, 어르신.\""], ["replied Mr.", "호드슨 씨가 대답했지."], ["Hodson; and Sir Pitt in a fury swore that if he ever caught 'em poaching on his ground, he'd transport 'em, by the lord he would.", "그러자 핏 경은 노발대발하며, 만약 그놈들이 자기 땅에서 밀렵하는 걸 한 번이라도 잡으면 유배 보내 버리겠다고, 맹세코 그러겠다고 욕설을 퍼부었어. 그러면서도 그는 이렇게 말했어."], ["However, he said, \"I've sold the presentation of the living, Hodson; none of that breed shall get it, I war'nt\"; and Mr.", "\"성직록 추천권은 내가 팔아 버렸네, 호드슨. 그 핏줄에겐 절대 안 넘어가게 해 놨지, 장담하네.\""], ["Hodson said he was quite right: and I have no doubt from this that the two brothers are at variance--as brothers often are, and sisters too.", "그러자 호드슨 씨는 지당하신 말씀이라고 했어. 이것만 봐도 나는 그 두 형제가 사이가 틀어졌다는 걸 의심치 않아 — 형제끼리, 또 자매끼리 흔히 그렇듯이 말이야."], ["Don't you remember the two Miss Scratchleys at Chiswick, how they used always to fight and quarrel--and Mary Box, how she was always thumping Louisa?", "치즈윅의 스크래츨리 자매 둘 기억나니? 어떻게 늘 다투고 으르렁대던지 — 그리고 메리 박스가 어떻게 늘 루이자를 두들겨 패던지 말이야."]], [["Presently, seeing two little boys gathering sticks in the wood, Mr. Hodson jumped out of the carriage, at Sir Pitt's order, and rushed upon them with his whip.", "이윽고 숲에서 막대기를 줍고 있는 두 어린 사내아이를 보더니, 호드슨 씨가 핏 경의 명령에 따라 마차에서 뛰어내려 채찍을 들고 그 애들에게 달려들었어."], ["\"Pitch into 'em, Hodson,\"", "\"혼쭐을 내, 호드슨.\" 준남작이 고함쳤어."], ["roared the baronet; \"flog their little souls out, and bring 'em up to the house, the vagabonds; I'll commit 'em as sure as my name's Pitt.\"", "\"고 조그만 것들 영혼이 빠지도록 두들겨 패서 집으로 끌고 와, 저 부랑자 놈들을. 내 이름이 핏인 게 분명하듯이 저놈들을 영락없이 잡아넣고 말 테니.\""], ["And presently we heard Mr. Hodson's whip cracking on the shoulders of the poor little blubbering wretches, and Sir Pitt, seeing that the malefactors were in custody, drove on to the hall.", "그러더니 이내 호드슨 씨의 채찍이 가엾게 훌쩍이는 그 조그만 것들의 어깨를 후려치는 소리가 들렸고, 핏 경은 그 범죄자들이 붙잡힌 걸 확인하고는 저택을 향해 마차를 몰았단다."]], [["All the servants were ready to meet us, and . . .", "하인들이 모두 우리를 맞으러 나와 있었고, 그리고…"]], [["Here, my dear, I was interrupted last night by a dreadful thumping at my door: and who do you think it was?", "얘야, 여기서 어젯밤 나는 문을 무섭게 두드리는 소리에 글쓰기를 멈췄단다. 그게 누구였을 것 같니?"], ["Sir Pitt Crawley in his night-cap and dressing-gown, such a figure!", "잠자리 모자를 쓰고 실내복을 걸친 핏 크롤리 경이었어, 그 꼴이라니!"], ["As I shrank away from such a visitor, he came forward and seized my candle.", "내가 그런 방문객을 보고 움찔 물러서자, 그는 앞으로 다가와 내 촛불을 낚아챘어."], ["\"No candles after eleven o'clock, Miss Becky,\"", "\"열한 시 넘어서는 촛불 안 돼, 베키 양.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했어."], ["\"Go to bed in the dark, you pretty little hussy\"", "\"어둠 속에서 자라고, 요 예쁘장한 말괄량이야\"(그가 나를 그렇게 불렀어)."], ["(that is what he called me), \"and unless you wish me to come for the candle every night, mind and be in bed at eleven.\"", "\"그리고 내가 매일 밤 촛불 가지러 오게 만들고 싶지 않거든, 명심하고 열한 시엔 잠자리에 들어.\" 그러고는 집사 호록스 씨와 함께 웃으며 가 버렸어."], ["And with this, he and Mr. Horrocks the butler went off laughing.", "내가 다시는 그들의 방문을 부추기지 않으리란 건 너도 분명히 알 거야."], ["You may be sure I shall not encourage any more of their visits.", "그들은 밤이면 거대한 사냥개 두 마리를 풀어놓는데, 어젯밤 내내 그놈들이 달을 향해 짖고 울부짖었단다. \"이 개는 고러라고 부르지.\""], ["They let loose two immense bloodhounds at night, which all last night were yelling and howling at the moon.", "핏 경이 말했어. \"사람을 한 명 죽인 놈이야, 이 개 말이야."], ["\"I call the dog Gorer,\"", "황소도 거뜬히 잡는 놈이지."], ["said Sir Pitt; \"he's killed a man that dog has, and is master of a bull, and the mother I used to call Flora; but now I calls her Aroarer, for she's too old to bite.", "어미는 플로라라고 부르곤 했는데, 지금은 어로어러라고 부른단다. 이젠 너무 늙어서 물지도 못하거든."], ["Haw, haw!\"", "하, 하!\""]], [["Before the house of Queen's Crawley, which is an odious old-fashioned red brick mansion, with tall chimneys and gables of the style of Queen Bess, there is a terrace flanked by the family dove and serpent, and on which the great hall-door opens.", "퀸스 크롤리 저택은 엘리자베스 여왕 시대 양식의 높은 굴뚝과 박공이 달린, 지긋지긋한 구식 붉은 벽돌 저택인데, 그 앞에는 가문의 비둘기와 뱀이 양옆에 늘어선 테라스가 있고, 거기로 커다란 현관 대문이 열린단다."], ["And oh, my dear, the great hall I am sure is as big and as glum as the great hall in the dear castle of Udolpho.", "그리고 아, 얘야, 그 커다란 홀은 분명 그 우돌포 성의 커다란 홀만큼이나 크고 음침할 거야."], ["It has a large fireplace, in which we might put half Miss Pinkerton's school, and the grate is big enough to roast an ox at the very least.", "거기엔 핑커튼 여사 학원 절반은 들어갈 만한 커다란 벽난로가 있고, 쇠살대는 적어도 황소 한 마리는 구울 만큼 크단다."], ["Round the room hang I don't know how many generations of Crawleys, some with beards and ruffs, some with huge wigs and toes turned out, some dressed in long straight stays and gowns that look as stiff as towers, and some with long ringlets, and oh, my dear!", "방 둘레에는 몇 대인지 모를 크롤리 가문 사람들의 초상화가 걸려 있는데, 더러는 턱수염에 주름 깃을 두른 모습이고, 더러는 거대한 가발을 쓰고 발끝을 바깥으로 벌린 모습이고, 더러는 탑처럼 빳빳해 보이는 길고 곧은 코르셋과 가운 차림이고, 더러는 긴 곱슬머리를 늘어뜨린 채, 아, 얘야! 코르셋이라곤 거의 걸치지 않은 모습이란다."], ["scarcely any stays at all. At one end of the hall is the great staircase all in black oak, as dismal as may be, and on either side are tall doors with stags'", "홀 한쪽 끝에는 더할 나위 없이 음울한 까만 떡갈나무 대계단이 있고, 양옆에는 위에 사슴 머리가 걸린 높다란 문들이 있어서 당구실과 서재로, 또 커다란 노란색 응접실과 모닝룸으로 이어진단다."], ["heads over them, leading to the billiard-room and the library, and the great yellow saloon and the morning-rooms.", "이 층에는 침실이 적어도 스무 개는 있는 것 같아. 그 가운데 하나에는 엘리자베스 여왕이 주무셨다는 침대가 있어."], ["I think there are at least twenty bedrooms on the first floor; one of them has the bed in which Queen Elizabeth slept; and I have been taken by my new pupils through all these fine apartments this morning.", "오늘 아침 나는 새 제자들에게 이끌려 이 멋진 방들을 죄다 구경했단다."], ["They are not rendered less gloomy, I promise you, by having the shutters always shut; and there is scarce one of the apartments, but when the light was let into it, I expected to see a ghost in the room.", "정말이지, 덧문을 늘 닫아 두는 통에 그 방들이 조금이라도 덜 음침해 보이는 건 아니야. 어느 방이든 빛이 들어올 때면 나는 그 안에서 유령이 보이지나 않을까 했을 정도란다."], ["We have a schoolroom on the second floor, with my bedroom leading into it on one side, and that of the young ladies on the other. Then there are Mr.", "우리 공부방은 삼 층에 있는데, 한쪽으로는 내 침실이, 다른 쪽으로는 아가씨들의 침실이 이어져 있어."], ["Pitt's apartments--Mr. Crawley, he is called--the eldest son, and Mr.", "그다음엔 핏 씨의 방들이 있어 — 그는 크롤리 씨라고 불리는데 — 맏아들이지."], ["Rawdon Crawley's rooms--he is an officer like SOMEBODY, and away with his regiment.", "그리고 로던 크롤리 씨의 방들도 있는데 — 그는 누구처럼 장교라서 연대를 따라 멀리 떠나 있단다."], ["There is no want of room I assure you.", "정말이지 방이 부족할 일은 없어."], ["You might lodge all the people in Russell Square in the house, I think, and have space to spare.", "내 생각엔 러셀 광장 사람들을 죄다 이 집에 들여도 자리가 남을 거야."]], [["Half an hour after our arrival, the great dinner-bell was rung, and I came down with my two pupils (they are very thin insignificant little chits of ten and eight years old).", "우리가 도착한 지 삼십 분 만에 커다란 만찬 종이 울렸고, 나는 두 제자(열 살, 여덟 살짜리, 비쩍 마르고 보잘것없는 꼬마들이야)를 데리고 내려갔어. 나는 네가 준 그 소중한 모슬린 가운을 입고 내려갔단다(네가 그걸 내게 주었다고 그 지긋지긋한 피너 부인이 어찌나 무례하게 굴었던지)."], ["I came down in your dear muslin gown (about which that odious Mrs.", "손님 오는 날만 빼면 나는 식구 가운데 한 사람으로 대접받기로 되어 있거든."], ["Pinner was so rude, because you gave it me); for I am to be treated as one of the family, except on company days, when the young ladies and I are to dine upstairs.", "손님 오는 날엔 아가씨들과 나는 위층에서 식사를 해야 한단다."]], [["Well, the great dinner-bell rang, and we all assembled in the little drawing-room where my Lady Crawley sits.", "자, 그 커다란 만찬 종이 울리자, 우리는 모두 크롤리 마님이 계시는 작은 응접실에 모였어."], ["She is the second Lady Crawley, and mother of the young ladies.", "그분은 두 번째 크롤리 마님이고, 아가씨들의 어머니야."], ["She was an ironmonger's daughter, and her marriage was thought a great match.", "철물상의 딸이었는데, 그 결혼은 굉장한 출세로 여겨졌다더라."], ["She looks as if she had been handsome once, and her eyes are always weeping for the loss of her beauty.", "한때는 미인이었던 듯한 모습인데, 그 눈은 잃어버린 미모를 슬퍼하며 늘 눈물에 젖어 있어."], ["She is pale and meagre and high-shouldered, and has not a word to say for herself, evidently.", "창백하고, 여위고, 어깨가 솟았고, 자기 의견이라곤 한마디도 못 하는 게 분명해."], ["Her stepson Mr. Crawley, was likewise in the room.", "의붓아들인 크롤리 씨도 방에 함께 있었어."], ["He was in full dress, as pompous as an undertaker.", "정장 차림인데, 장의사처럼 거드름을 피우더구나."], ["He is pale, thin, ugly, silent; he has thin legs, no chest, hay-coloured whiskers, and straw-coloured hair.", "창백하고, 마르고, 못생기고, 말이 없어. 다리는 가늘고, 가슴팍은 빈약하고, 건초빛 구레나룻에 지푸라기빛 머리칼을 하고 있어."], ["He is the very picture of his sainted mother over the mantelpiece--Griselda of the noble house of Binkie.", "벽난로 위에 걸린 성녀 같은 자기 어머니의 모습 그대로야 — 빙키 명문가의 그리젤다 말이야."]], [["\"This is the new governess, Mr. Crawley,\"", "\"이 사람이 새 가정교사예요, 크롤리 씨.\""], ["said Lady Crawley, coming forward and taking my hand.", "크롤리 마님이 앞으로 나와 내 손을 잡으며 말했어."], ["\"Miss Sharp.\"", "\"샤프 양이에요.\""]], [["\"O!\" said Mr.", "\"오!\""], ["Crawley, and pushed his head once forward and began again to read a great pamphlet with which he was busy.", "크롤리 씨는 그렇게 말하고는 고개를 한 번 앞으로 까딱하더니, 다시 자기가 열중하던 두꺼운 소책자를 읽기 시작했어."]], [["\"I hope you will be kind to my girls,\"", "\"제 딸들에게 친절히 대해 주시기를 바라요.\""], ["said Lady Crawley, with her pink eyes always full of tears.", "크롤리 마님이, 늘 눈물에 그렁그렁한 발그레한 눈으로 말했어."]], [["\"Law, Ma, of course she will,\"", "\"어머, 엄마, 당연히 그러시겠죠.\""], ["said the eldest: and I saw at a glance that I need not be afraid of THAT woman.", "맏이가 말했어. 나는 한눈에 저 여자는 두려워할 필요가 없다는 걸 알아챘단다."], ["\"My lady is served,\"", "\"마님, 식사 준비되었습니다.\""], ["says the butler in black, in an immense white shirt-frill, that looked as if it had been one of the Queen Elizabeth's ruffs depicted in the hall; and so, taking Mr.", "검은 옷을 입고, 마치 홀에 그려진 엘리자베스 여왕의 주름 깃 가운데 하나 같은 거대한 흰 셔츠 깃을 두른 집사가 말했어."], ["Crawley's arm, she led the way to the dining-room, whither I followed with my little pupils in each hand.", "그러자 마님은 크롤리 씨의 팔을 잡고 식당으로 앞장섰고, 나는 양손에 두 어린 제자를 하나씩 잡고 그 뒤를 따랐단다."]], [["Sir Pitt was already in the room with a silver jug.", "핏 경은 은 주전자를 들고 이미 방에 와 있었어."], ["He had just been to the cellar, and was in full dress too; that is, he had taken his gaiters off, and showed his little dumpy legs in black worsted stockings.", "막 지하 저장고에 다녀온 참이었고, 그도 정장 차림이었어. 그러니까, 각반을 벗고서 까만 양모 양말 신은 짤막하고 땅딸한 다리를 드러내고 있었단다."], ["The sideboard was covered with glistening old plate--old cups, both gold and silver; old salvers and cruet-stands, like Rundell and Bridge's shop.", "식기대에는 번쩍이는 낡은 은식기가 가득했어 — 금이며 은이며 낡은 잔들, 낡은 쟁반과 양념병 받침대까지, 마치 런들 앤드 브리지 가게 같았어."], ["Everything on the table was in silver too, and two footmen, with red hair and canary-coloured liveries, stood on either side of the sideboard.", "식탁 위 모든 것도 은제였고, 빨간 머리에 카나리아빛 제복을 입은 하인 둘이 식기대 양옆에 서 있었어."]], [["Mr. Crawley said a long grace, and Sir Pitt said amen, and the great silver dish-covers were removed.", "크롤리 씨가 긴 식전 기도를 올렸고, 핏 경이 아멘 하자, 커다란 은제 접시 덮개들이 치워졌어."]], [["\"What have we for dinner, Betsy?\"", "\"오늘 저녁은 뭔가, 베치?\""], ["said the Baronet.", "준남작이 말했어."]], [["\"Mutton broth, I believe, Sir Pitt,\"", "\"양고기 수프인 것 같아요, 핏 경.\""], ["answered Lady Crawley.", "크롤리 마님이 대답했어."]], [["\"Mouton aux navets,\"", "\"무통 오 나베입니다.\""], ["added the butler gravely (pronounce, if you please, moutongonavvy); \"and the soup is potage de mouton a l'Ecossaise.", "집사가 근엄하게 덧붙였어(부디 '무통고나비'라고 발음해 주렴). \"그리고 수프는 포타주 드 무통 아 레코세즈입니다."], ["The side-dishes contain pommes de terre au naturel, and choufleur a l'eau.\"", "곁들이 요리로는 폼 드 테르 오 나튀렐과 슈플뢰르 아 로가 있습니다.\""]], [["\"Mutton's mutton,\"", "\"양고기는 양고기지.\""], ["said the Baronet, \"and a devilish good thing.", "준남작이 말했어. \"지독하게 맛 좋은 것이고."], ["What SHIP was it, Horrocks, and when did you kill?\"", "무슨 양이었나, 호록스, 언제 잡았지?\""], ["\"One of the black-faced Scotch, Sir Pitt: we killed on Thursday.\"", "\"검은 얼굴 스코틀랜드 양 가운데 하나입니다, 핏 경. 목요일에 잡았습니다.\""]], [["\"Who took any?\"", "\"누가 좀 가져갔나?\""]], [["\"Steel, of Mudbury, took the saddle and two legs, Sir Pitt; but he says the last was too young and confounded woolly, Sir Pitt.\"", "\"머드베리의 스틸이 등심과 다리 두 짝을 가져갔습니다, 핏 경. 다만 마지막 것은 너무 어리고 지독하게 양털이 많았다고 합니다, 핏 경.\""]], [["\"Will you take some potage, Miss ah--Miss Blunt?", "\"수프 좀 드시겠습니까, 어 — 블런트 양?\""], ["said Mr. Crawley.", "크롤리 씨가 말했어."]], [["\"Capital Scotch broth, my dear,\"", "\"훌륭한 스코틀랜드 수프야, 아가씨.\""], ["said Sir Pitt, \"though they call it by a French name.\"", "핏 경이 말했어. \"저들이 프랑스 이름으로 부르긴 하지만 말이야.\""]], [["\"I believe it is the custom, sir, in decent society,\"", "\"제대로 된 사교계에서는,\" 크롤리 씨가 도도하게 말했어."], ["said Mr. Crawley, haughtily, \"to call the dish as I have called it\"; and it was served to us on silver soup plates by the footmen in the canary coats, with the mouton aux navets.", "\"이 요리를 제가 부른 대로 부르는 게 관례인 줄로 압니다.\""], ["Then \"ale and water\" were brought, and served to us young ladies in wine-glasses.", "그러고 나서 그것은 카나리아빛 옷을 입은 하인들이 은제 수프 접시에 담아 무통 오 나베와 함께 우리에게 내왔어."], ["I am not a judge of ale, but I can say with a clear conscience I prefer water.", "그다음엔 '에일에 물 탄 것'이 나와서, 우리 어린 아가씨들에겐 포도주 잔에 따라 주었어. 나는 에일을 가릴 줄 모르지만, 양심에 거리낌 없이 말하건대 나는 물이 더 좋아."]], [["While we were enjoying our repast, Sir Pitt took occasion to ask what had become of the shoulders of the mutton.", "우리가 식사를 즐기는 동안, 핏 경이 양고기 어깨살은 어떻게 되었느냐고 물을 기회를 잡았어."]], [["\"I believe they were eaten in the servants'", "\"하인들 방에서 먹은 것 같아요.\""], ["hall,\" said my lady, humbly.", "마님이 겸손하게 말했어."]], [["\"They was, my lady,\"", "\"먹었지요, 마님.\""], ["said Horrocks, \"and precious little else we get there neither.\"", "호록스가 말했어. \"거기서야 그것 말고는 변변히 얻어먹는 게 거의 없으니까요.\""]], [["Sir Pitt burst into a horse-laugh, and continued his conversation with Mr. Horrocks.", "핏 경은 말처럼 껄껄대며 웃음을 터뜨리더니, 호록스 씨와 다시 이야기를 이어 갔어."], ["\"That there little black pig of the Kent sow's breed must be uncommon fat now.\"", "\"그 켄트 암퇘지 새끼인 까만 새끼돼지가 이제 어지간히 살이 올랐겠는데.\""]], [["\"It's not quite busting, Sir Pitt,\"", "\"아직 터질 정도는 아닙니다, 핏 경.\""], ["said the butler with the gravest air, at which Sir Pitt, and with him the young ladies, this time, began to laugh violently.", "집사가 더없이 근엄한 표정으로 말했어. 그러자 핏 경이, 이번엔 아가씨들까지 함께, 격렬하게 웃어 대기 시작했어."]], [["\"Miss Crawley, Miss Rose Crawley,\" said Mr.", "\"크롤리 양, 로즈 크롤리 양.\""], ["Crawley, \"your laughter strikes me as being exceedingly out of place.\"", "크롤리 씨가 말했어. \"너희 웃음소리가 내겐 몹시 부적절하게 들리는구나.\""]], [["\"Never mind, my lord,\"", "\"신경 쓰지 마라, 도련님.\""], ["said the Baronet, \"we'll try the porker on Saturday.", "준남작이 말했어. \"토요일에 그 돼지를 한번 잡아 보자고."], ["Kill un on Saturday morning, John Horrocks.", "토요일 아침에 잡아라, 존 호록스."], ["Miss Sharp adores pork, don't you, Miss Sharp?\"", "샤프 양은 돼지고기를 무척 좋아하지요, 안 그렇소, 샤프 양?\""]], [["And I think this is all the conversation that I remember at dinner.", "만찬에서 내가 기억하는 대화는 이게 다인 것 같아."], ["When the repast was concluded a jug of hot water was placed before Sir Pitt, with a case-bottle containing, I believe, rum.", "식사가 끝나자 핏 경 앞에 뜨거운 물 주전자가 놓였고, 럼주가 든 듯한 각진 병이 함께 놓였어."], ["Mr. Horrocks served myself and my pupils with three little glasses of wine, and a bumper was poured out for my lady.", "호록스 씨가 나와 제자들에게 포도주 작은 잔 세 개를 따라 주었고, 마님에게는 가득 찬 잔이 따라졌어."], ["When we retired, she took from her work-drawer an enormous interminable piece of knitting; the young ladies began to play at cribbage with a dirty pack of cards.", "우리가 물러 나오자, 마님은 일감 서랍에서 어마어마하게 끝도 없는 뜨개질감을 꺼냈고, 아가씨들은 꾀죄죄한 카드 한 벌로 크리비지 놀이를 시작했어. 촛불이라곤 하나만 켜져 있었는데, 그래도 그건 으리으리한 낡은 은촛대에 꽂혀 있었단다."], ["We had but one candle lighted, but it was in a magnificent old silver candlestick, and after a very few questions from my lady, I had my choice of amusement between a volume of sermons, and a pamphlet on the corn-laws, which Mr. Crawley had been reading before dinner.", "마님이 몇 마디 물어본 뒤로, 나는 설교집 한 권과, 크롤리 씨가 저녁 식사 전에 읽던 곡물법 소책자 가운데에서 마음껏 골라 소일거리로 삼을 수 있었어."]], [["So we sat for an hour until steps were heard.", "그렇게 우리는 발소리가 들릴 때까지 한 시간을 앉아 있었어."]], [["\"Put away the cards, girls,\"", "\"카드 치워라, 얘들아.\""], ["cried my lady, in a great tremor; \"put down Mr.", "마님이 몹시 떨면서 외쳤어. \"크롤리 씨 책 내려놓으세요, 샤프 양.\""], ["Crawley's books, Miss Sharp\"; and these orders had been scarcely obeyed, when Mr. Crawley entered the room.", "그리고 이 명령을 미처 다 따르기도 전에 크롤리 씨가 방으로 들어왔어."]], [["\"We will resume yesterday's discourse, young ladies,\"", "\"어제 하던 설교를 마저 보자, 아가씨들.\" 그가 말했어."], ["said he, \"and you shall each read a page by turns; so that Miss a--Miss Short may have an opportunity of hearing you\"; and the poor girls began to spell a long dismal sermon delivered at Bethesda Chapel, Liverpool, on behalf of the mission for the Chickasaw Indians.", "\"한 사람씩 번갈아 한 쪽씩 읽거라. 그래야 어 — 쇼트 양도 들을 기회가 있을 테니.\""], ["Was it not a charming evening?", "그러자 가엾은 아가씨들은 리버풀 베데스다 예배당에서 치카소 인디언 선교를 위해 행해진, 길고 음울한 설교문을 더듬더듬 읽기 시작했어. 정말 멋진 저녁이지 않니?"]], [["At ten the servants were told to call Sir Pitt and the household to prayers.", "열 시가 되자 하인들에게 핏 경과 온 식구를 기도에 불러 모으라는 명이 내려졌어."], ["Sir Pitt came in first, very much flushed, and rather unsteady in his gait; and after him the butler, the canaries, Mr.", "핏 경이 맨 먼저 들어왔는데, 얼굴이 잔뜩 붉어진 채 걸음걸이가 다소 비틀거리더구나."], ["Crawley's man, three other men, smelling very much of the stable, and four women, one of whom, I remarked, was very much overdressed, and who flung me a look of great scorn as she plumped down on her knees.", "그 뒤로 집사와, 카나리아빛 옷을 입은 하인들과, 크롤리 씨의 시종과, 마구간 냄새가 물씬 풍기는 다른 사내 셋과, 여자 넷이 들어왔는데, 그 가운데 한 여자는 보아하니 몹시 지나치게 차려입었고, 무릎을 털썩 꿇으면서 내게 몹시 경멸 어린 눈길을 던지더라."]], [["After Mr.", "크롤리 씨가 일장 연설과 설교를 마치고 나자, 우리는 촛불을 받아 들고 잠자리에 들었어."], ["Crawley had done haranguing and expounding, we received our candles, and then we went to bed; and then I was disturbed in my writing, as I have described to my dearest sweetest Amelia.", "그러고 나서 내가 더없이 사랑하고 다정한 애밀리아 너에게 이야기한 대로 글쓰기를 방해받았던 거란다."]], [["Good night.", "잘 자렴."], ["A thousand, thousand, thousand kisses!", "천 번, 천 번, 천 번의 입맞춤을 보낸다!"]], [["Saturday.--This morning, at five, I heard the shrieking of the little black pig.", "토요일. — 오늘 아침 다섯 시에 나는 그 까만 새끼돼지의 비명을 들었어."], ["Rose and Violet introduced me to it yesterday; and to the stables, and to the kennel, and to the gardener, who was picking fruit to send to market, and from whom they begged hard a bunch of hot-house grapes; but he said that Sir Pitt had numbered every \"Man Jack\"", "로즈와 바이올렛이 어제 내게 그 돼지를 소개해 주었거든. 그리고 마구간이며, 개집이며, 시장에 보낼 과일을 따고 있던 정원사도 소개해 주었어."], ["of them, and it would be as much as his place was worth to give any away.", "아가씨들이 온실 포도 한 송이를 달라고 그에게 한참 졸라 댔지만, 그는 핏 경이 그 포도알 하나하나까지 다 세어 두었기 때문에, 하나라도 내주었다간 자기 일자리가 날아갈 거라고 했어."], ["The darling girls caught a colt in a paddock, and asked me if I would ride, and began to ride themselves, when the groom, coming with horrid oaths, drove them away.", "사랑스러운 아가씨들은 울안에서 망아지 한 마리를 붙잡고는 내게 말을 타겠느냐고 물었고, 자기들이 타기 시작했는데, 마부가 끔찍한 욕설을 퍼부으며 다가와 그 애들을 쫓아 버렸어."]], [["Lady Crawley is always knitting the worsted.", "크롤리 마님은 늘 양모 뜨개질을 하고 있어."], ["Sir Pitt is always tipsy, every night; and, I believe, sits with Horrocks, the butler. Mr.", "핏 경은 늘 밤마다 거나하게 취해서, 내 생각엔 집사 호록스와 함께 앉아 있는 것 같아."], ["Crawley always reads sermons in the evening, and in the morning is locked up in his study, or else rides to Mudbury, on county business, or to Squashmore, where he preaches, on Wednesdays and Fridays, to the tenants there.", "크롤리 씨는 저녁이면 늘 설교집을 읽고, 아침이면 서재에 틀어박혀 있거나, 아니면 군의 공무로 머드베리로 말을 달리거나, 수요일과 금요일마다 스쿼시모어로 가서 그곳 소작인들에게 설교를 한단다."]], [["A hundred thousand grateful loves to your dear papa and mamma.", "네 사랑하는 아버님과 어머님께 십만 번의 다정한 안부를 전해 줘."], ["Is your poor brother recovered of his rack-punch?", "네 가엾은 오빠는 그 럼펀치 후유증에서 회복되었니?"], ["Oh, dear!", "아아, 저런!"], ["Oh, dear!", "아아, 저런!"], ["How men should beware of wicked punch!", "사내들이란 그 고약한 펀치를 어찌나 조심해야 하는지!"]], [["Ever and ever thine own REBECCA", "언제까지나 영원히 너의 리베카로부터."]], [["Everything considered, I think it is quite as well for our dear Amelia Sedley, in Russell Square, that Miss Sharp and she are parted.", "모든 걸 고려해 볼 때, 러셀 광장의 우리 사랑스러운 애밀리아 세들리에게는 샤프 양과 헤어진 것이 차라리 잘된 일이라고 나는 생각한다."], ["Rebecca is a droll funny creature, to be sure; and those descriptions of the poor lady weeping for the loss of her beauty, and the gentleman \"with hay-coloured whiskers and straw-coloured hair,\"", "리베카가 익살맞고 우스운 인물인 것은 분명한 사실이다. 또한 잃어버린 미모를 슬퍼하며 우는 가엾은 마님이라든가, '건초빛 구레나룻에 지푸라기빛 머리칼'을 한 신사를 묘사한 대목은 의심할 바 없이 무척 재기가 넘치며, 세상물정에 밝다는 것을 보여 준다."], ["are very smart, doubtless, and show a great knowledge of the world. That she might, when on her knees, have been thinking of something better than Miss Horrocks's ribbons, has possibly struck both of us.", "다만 그녀가 무릎을 꿇고 있을 때 호록스 양의 리본보다 더 나은 무언가를 생각하고 있었을 법도 하다는 점은, 아마 우리 둘 다 떠올렸을 것이다."], ["But my kind reader will please to remember that this history has \"Vanity Fair\" for a title, and that Vanity Fair is a very vain, wicked, foolish place, full of all sorts of humbugs and falsenesses and pretensions.", "그러나 나의 다정한 독자께서는 이 이야기의 제목이 '허영의 시장'이라는 것을, 그리고 허영의 시장이란 온갖 허풍과 거짓과 허세로 가득한, 몹시 허황되고 사악하고 어리석은 곳이라는 것을 부디 기억해 주시기 바란다."], ["And while the moralist, who is holding forth on the cover ( an accurate portrait of your humble servant), professes to wear neither gown nor bands, but only the very same long-eared livery in which his congregation is arrayed: yet, look you, one is bound to speak the truth as far as one knows it, whether one mounts a cap and bells or a shovel hat; and a deal of disagreeable matter must come out in the course of such an undertaking.", "그리고 표지에서 일장 설교를 늘어놓는 그 도덕가는(이 보잘것없는 종복의 정확한 초상이다) 목사 가운도 띠도 두르지 않고, 다만 자기 신도들이 걸치고 있는 그 똑같은 당나귀 귀 달린 어릿광대 옷을 입었노라 공언하는 바이지만, 자, 보시라, 사람이란 어릿광대 모자를 쓰든 삽 모양 모자를 쓰든, 자기가 아는 한 진실을 말해야 하는 법이며, 이런 일을 해 나가는 과정에서는 적잖이 불쾌한 사실들이 드러날 수밖에 없는 것이다."]], [["I have heard a brother of the story-telling trade, at Naples, preaching to a pack of good-for-nothing honest lazy fellows by the sea-shore, work himself up into such a rage and passion with some of the villains whose wicked deeds he was describing and inventing, that the audience could not resist it; and they and the poet together would burst out into a roar of oaths and execrations against the fictitious monster of the tale, so that the hat went round, and the bajocchi tumbled into it, in the midst of a perfect storm of sympathy.", "나는 나폴리에서, 이야기꾼 동업자 한 사람이 바닷가에서 쓸모없이 게으르기만 한 정직한 무리에게 설교하는 것을 들은 적이 있다. 그는 자기가 묘사하고 지어내는 악당들의 못된 행실에 어찌나 격분하고 열을 올리던지, 청중들이 도저히 견디지 못할 지경이었다. 그리하여 그들과 그 시인이 한데 어우러져, 이야기 속의 가공의 괴물을 향해 욕설과 저주를 한바탕 퍼부어 댔고, 그 통에 모자가 한 바퀴 돌면서, 완벽한 공감의 폭풍 한가운데서 동전들이 그 안으로 쏟아져 들어갔다."]], [["At the little Paris theatres, on the other hand, you will not only hear the people yelling out \"Ah gredin!", "한편 파리의 작은 극장들에서는, 관객들이 박스석에서 \"아, 악당! 아, 괴물 같으니!\""], ["Ah monstre:\" and cursing the tyrant of the play from the boxes; but the actors themselves positively refuse to play the wicked parts, such as those of infames Anglais, brutal Cossacks, and what not, and prefer to appear at a smaller salary, in their real characters as loyal Frenchmen.", "하고 고래고래 소리치며 극 중 폭군을 저주할 뿐 아니라, 배우들 자신이 그 악역, 이를테면 비열한 영국인이라든가 야만적인 코사크 따위의 배역을 맡기를 한사코 거부하고, 더 적은 봉급을 받더라도 충직한 프랑스인이라는 자기 본래의 모습으로 무대에 서기를 더 좋아하는 것을 보게 될 것이다."], ["I set the two stories one against the other, so that you may see that it is not from mere mercenary motives that the present performer is desirous to show up and trounce his villains; but because he has a sincere hatred of them, which he cannot keep down, and which must find a vent in suitable abuse and bad language.", "내가 이 두 이야기를 나란히 견주어 놓는 것은, 지금 이 공연자가 자기 악당들을 까발리고 혼내 주고자 하는 것이 단지 돈 때문이 아님을, 다만 그가 그들을 진심으로 미워하여 도저히 억누를 수 없는 마음이 마땅한 욕설과 험한 말로 분출구를 찾을 수밖에 없기 때문임을 그대들이 알아주기를 바라서다."]], [["I warn my \"kyind friends,\" then, that I am going to tell a story of harrowing villainy and complicated--but, as I trust, intensely interesting--crime.", "그러므로 나의 '다정한 벗들'에게 미리 일러두건대, 나는 앞으로 가슴을 도려내는 악행과, 복잡하게 얽힌 — 그러나 내가 믿건대 더없이 흥미진진할 — 범죄의 이야기를 들려줄 작정이다."], ["My rascals are no milk-and-water rascals, I promise you.", "장담컨대 내 악당들은 맹물에 물 탄 듯 밍밍한 악당들이 아니다."], ["When we come to the proper places we won't spare fine language--No, no!", "알맞은 대목에 이르면 우리는 험한 말을 아끼지 않을 것이다 — 결코, 결코!"], ["But when we are going over the quiet country we must perforce be calm.", "그러나 조용한 시골을 지나는 동안에는 어쩔 수 없이 차분해야만 한다."], ["A tempest in a slop-basin is absurd.", "죽사발 속에서 일으키는 폭풍이란 우스꽝스러운 법이다."], ["We will reserve that sort of thing for the mighty ocean and the lonely midnight.", "그런 일은 거대한 대양과 외로운 한밤을 위해 아껴 두기로 하자."], ["The present Chapter is very mild.", "지금 이 장은 무척 점잖다."], ["Others--But we will not anticipate THOSE.", "다른 장들은 — 하지만 그것은 미리 내다보지 말기로 하자."]], [["And, as we bring our characters forward, I will ask leave, as a man and a brother, not only to introduce them, but occasionally to step down from the platform, and talk about them: if they are good and kindly, to love them and shake them by the hand: if they are silly, to laugh at them confidentially in the reader's sleeve: if they are wicked and heartless, to abuse them in the strongest terms which politeness admits of.", "그리고 우리의 등장인물들을 차례로 내세우는 동안, 나는 한 사람으로서, 또 한 형제로서, 그들을 소개할 뿐 아니라 이따금 단상에서 내려와 그들에 관해 이야기할 수 있도록 양해를 구하고자 한다. 그들이 선량하고 다정하면 그들을 사랑하고 손을 맞잡을 것이며, 그들이 어리석으면 독자의 소매 뒤에서 은근히 그들을 비웃을 것이고, 그들이 사악하고 무정하면 예의가 허락하는 가장 강한 말로 그들을 매도할 것이다."]], [["Otherwise you might fancy it was I who was sneering at the practice of devotion, which Miss Sharp finds so ridiculous; that it was I who laughed good-humouredly at the reeling old Silenus of a baronet--whereas the laughter comes from one who has no reverence except for prosperity, and no eye for anything beyond success.", "그러지 않으면, 샤프 양이 그토록 우스꽝스럽게 여기는 신앙의 관행을 비웃는 것이 바로 나인 줄로, 또 비틀거리는 늙은 실레노스 같은 준남작을 너그러이 비웃은 것이 바로 나인 줄로 그대들은 여길지도 모른다. 하지만 그 웃음은, 번영 말고는 아무것도 떠받들 줄 모르고 성공 너머의 것은 아무것도 볼 줄 모르는 자에게서 나온 것이다."], ["Such people there are living and flourishing in the world--Faithless, Hopeless, Charityless: let us have at them, dear friends, with might and main.", "그런 자들이 세상에 살아 번성하고 있다 — 믿음도 없고, 희망도 없고, 사랑도 없는 자들이. 자, 다정한 벗들이여, 온 힘을 다해 그들에게 맞서자."], ["Some there are, and very successful too, mere quacks and fools: and it was to combat and expose such as those, no doubt, that Laughter was made.", "또 어떤 자들은, 그것도 아주 성공한 자들은, 한낱 돌팔이요 바보일 뿐이다. 의심할 바 없이, 바로 그런 자들과 맞서 싸우고 그들을 까발리라고 웃음이라는 것이 생겨난 것이다."]], [["Family Portraits\nSir Pitt Crawley was a philosopher with a taste for what is called low life.", "피트 크롤리 경은 흔히 천박한 삶이라 일컫는 것에 취미를 둔 철학자였다. 명문 빙키 가문의 딸과 한 그의 첫 결혼은 양친의 주선으로 이루어진 것이었다."], ["His first marriage with the daughter of the noble Binkie had been made under the auspices of his parents; and as he often told Lady Crawley in her lifetime she was such a confounded quarrelsome high-bred jade that when she died he was hanged if he would ever take another of her sort, at her ladyship's demise he kept his promise, and selected for a second wife Miss Rose Dawson, daughter of Mr.", "그래서 그는 크롤리 부인이 살아 있는 동안 곧잘 그녀에게 말하기를, 당신이란 사람은 빌어먹게 싸움질이나 좋아하는 도도한 잡것이라, 만일 당신이 죽거든 두 번 다시 그런 부류를 아내로 들이는 일이 있다면 내 목을 매달아도 좋다고 했다. 그리고 그 부인이 세상을 떠나자 그는 약속을 지켜, 두 번째 아내로 머드베리의 철물상 존 토머스 도슨 씨의 딸 로즈 도슨 양을 택했다."], ["John Thomas Dawson, ironmonger, of Mudbury. What a happy woman was Rose to be my Lady Crawley!", "크롤리 부인이 된 로즈는 얼마나 행복한 여인이었던가!"]], [["Let us set down the items of her happiness.", "자, 그녀가 누린 행복의 항목들을 하나하나 적어 보기로 하자."], ["In the first place, she gave up Peter Butt, a young man who kept company with her, and in consequence of his disappointment in love, took to smuggling, poaching, and a thousand other bad courses.", "우선 그녀는 자기와 사귀던 청년 피터 버트를 버렸고, 그는 실연의 상심 끝에 밀수와 밀렵을 비롯한 천 가지 못된 길로 빠져들었다."], ["Then she quarrelled, as in duty bound, with all the friends and intimates of her youth, who, of course, could not be received by my Lady at Queen's Crawley--nor did she find in her new rank and abode any persons who were willing to welcome her.", "다음으로 그녀는 마땅한 도리로서 젊은 날의 벗과 가까운 이들 모두와 절교했는데, 물론 그들은 퀸스크롤리에서 크롤리 부인의 접대를 받을 만한 처지가 못 되었다. 그렇다고 그녀가 새로 얻은 신분과 거처에서 자기를 기꺼이 맞아 줄 사람들을 찾은 것도 아니었다."], ["Who ever did?", "대체 누가 그러겠는가?"], ["Sir Huddleston Fuddleston had three daughters who all hoped to be Lady Crawley.", "허들스턴 퍼들스턴 경에게는 딸이 셋 있었는데 하나같이 크롤리 부인이 되기를 바라던 터였다."], ["Sir Giles Wapshot's family were insulted that one of the Wapshot girls had not the preference in the marriage, and the remaining baronets of the county were indignant at their comrade's misalliance.", "자일스 왑샷 경의 가문은 왑샷 가의 딸들 가운데 하나가 그 혼인에서 우선권을 얻지 못했다 하여 모욕으로 받아들였고, 그 고장에 남은 준남작들도 동료의 격에 맞지 않는 혼인에 분개했다."], ["Never mind the commoners, whom we will leave to grumble anonymously.", "평민들이야 굳이 신경 쓸 것 없으니, 익명으로 투덜대도록 내버려 두기로 하자."]], [["Sir Pitt did not care, as he said, a brass farden for any one of them.", "피트 경은 자기 말마따나 그들 가운데 누구에게도 놋쇠 동전 한 닢만큼의 관심도 없었다."], ["He had his pretty Rose, and what more need a man require than to please himself?", "그에게는 어여쁜 로즈가 있었으니, 사내가 제 마음에 흡족한 것 말고 더 무엇을 바라겠는가?"], ["So he used to get drunk every night: to beat his pretty Rose sometimes: to leave her in Hampshire when he went to London for the parliamentary session, without a single friend in the wide world. Even Mrs.", "그리하여 그는 밤마다 곤드레만드레 취하곤 했고, 이따금 그 어여쁜 로즈를 두들겨 패기도 했으며, 의회 회기로 런던에 갈 때면 이 너른 세상에 친구라곤 단 한 사람도 없는 그녀를 햄프셔에 홀로 남겨 두곤 했다."], ["Bute Crawley, the Rector's wife, refused to visit her, as she said she would never give the pas to a tradesman's daughter.", "목사의 아내인 뷰트 크롤리 부인조차 그녀를 방문하기를 거절했으니, 자기는 장사치의 딸에게 결코 윗자리를 내주지 않겠노라는 것이었다."]], [["As the only endowments with which Nature had gifted Lady Crawley were those of pink cheeks and a white skin, and as she had no sort of character, nor talents, nor opinions, nor occupations, nor amusements, nor that vigour of soul and ferocity of temper which often falls to the lot of entirely foolish women, her hold upon Sir Pitt's affections was not very great.", "* 윗자리: 원문 'le pas'로, 사교 자리에서 신분에 따라 앞서 나가는 우선권을 뜻한다. 자연이 크롤리 부인에게 베푼 천부의 자질이라곤 발그레한 두 볼과 흰 살결뿐이었고, 이렇다 할 성격도, 재능도, 견해도, 일거리도, 소일거리도, 또 어리석기 짝이 없는 여인들에게 흔히 주어지는 저 영혼의 기개나 사나운 기질조차 없었던 까닭에, 피트 경의 애정을 붙들어 두는 그녀의 힘은 그리 대단하지 못했다."], ["Her roses faded out of her cheeks, and the pretty freshness left her figure after the birth of a couple of children, and she became a mere machine in her husband's house of no more use than the late Lady Crawley's grand piano.", "두 볼에서 장미빛이 시들었고, 아이를 둘 낳은 뒤로는 자태에서 그 어여쁜 싱그러움도 사라져, 그녀는 남편의 집에서 한낱 기계로 전락하고 말았으니, 작고한 전 크롤리 부인의 그랜드 피아노보다 나을 게 없는 존재였다. 살빛이 옅은 여자였던 그녀는 대개의 금발 여인이 그러하듯 옅은 빛깔의 옷을 입었고, 무엇보다 칙칙하게 끌리는 바다빛 초록이나 단정치 못한 하늘빛 옷차림으로 나타나곤 했다."], ["Being a light-complexioned woman, she wore light clothes, as most blondes will, and appeared, in preference, in draggled sea-green, or slatternly sky-blue.", "그녀는 그 털실 자수를 밤낮없이 떴고, 그와 비슷한 다른 것들도 떴다. 몇 해가 지나는 동안 그녀는 크롤리의 모든 침대에 깔 침대보를 마련했다."], ["She worked that worsted day and night, or other pieces like it.", "그녀에게는 자그마한 꽃밭이 하나 있었는데 그것만은 제법 애착을 두었으나, 그 밖에 좋아하거나 싫어하는 것이라곤 없었다."], ["She had counterpanes in the course of a few years to all the beds in Crawley.", "남편이 그녀에게 무례하게 굴면 그녀는 무덤덤했고, 그가 손찌검을 할 때면 울었다."], ["She had a small flower-garden, for which she had rather an affection; but beyond this no other like or disliking.", "술에 빠질 만한 기개조차 없었던 그녀는 종일 신을 질질 끌고 머리에 종이를 만 채로 신음하듯 돌아다녔다."], ["When her husband was rude to her she was apathetic: whenever he struck her she cried.", "오, 허영의 시장이여, 허영의 시장이여! 그대만 아니었던들 이 여인은 명랑한 처녀였으리라."], ["She had not character enough to take to drinking, and moaned about, slipshod and in curl-papers all day. O Vanity Fair--Vanity Fair!", "피터 버트와 로즈는 단란한 농가에서 화목한 식구들을 거느린 행복한 부부가 되어, 기쁨과 근심과 희망과 분투의 정직한 몫을 누렸으리라."], ["This might have been, but for you, a cheery lass--Peter Butt and Rose a happy man and wife, in a snug farm, with a hearty family; and an honest portion of pleasures, cares, hopes and struggles--but a title and a coach and four are toys more precious than happiness in Vanity Fair: and if Harry the Eighth or Bluebeard were alive now, and wanted a tenth wife, do you suppose he could not get the prettiest girl that shall be presented this season?", "그러나 작위와 사두마차란 허영의 시장에서는 행복보다 더 값진 장난감인 것이다. 그리고 만일 헨리 8세나 푸른 수염이 지금 살아 있어 열 번째 아내를 구한다면, 이번 시즌에 사교계에 나올 가장 어여쁜 처녀를 손에 넣지 못하리라고 그대는 생각하는가?"]], [["The languid dulness of their mamma did not, as it may be supposed, awaken much affection in her little daughters, but they were very happy in the servants'", "으레 짐작할 수 있듯이, 어머니의 맥없는 둔함은 두 어린 딸의 마음에 그다지 애정을 불러일으키지 못했다. 그러나 아이들은 하인 방과 마구간에서 매우 즐겁게 지냈다."], ["hall and in the stables; and the Scotch gardener having luckily a good wife and some good children, they got a little wholesome society and instruction in his lodge, which was the only education bestowed upon them until Miss Sharp came.", "그리고 스코틀랜드인 정원사가 다행히 착한 아내와 착한 아이들을 두고 있었던 덕분에, 아이들은 그의 오두막에서 조금이나마 유익한 교제와 가르침을 얻었으니, 이것이 샤프 양이 오기 전까지 아이들에게 베풀어진 유일한 교육이었다."]], [["Her engagement was owing to the remonstrances of Mr.", "그녀를 고용하게 된 것은 피트 크롤리 씨의 간청 덕분이었다."], ["Pitt Crawley, the only friend or protector Lady Crawley ever had, and the only person, besides her children, for whom she entertained a little feeble attachment.", "그는 크롤리 부인이 평생 가졌던 유일한 벗이자 보호자였고, 자식들 말고 그녀가 가냘프게나마 애정을 품은 유일한 사람이었다. 피트 씨는 자기가 그 핏줄을 이어받은 명문 빙키 가문을 닮아, 매우 예의 바르고 반듯한 신사였다."], ["Mr. Pitt took after the noble Binkies, from whom he was descended, and was a very polite and proper gentleman.", "어른이 되어 크라이스트처치에서 돌아온 그는, 자기를 두려워하던 아버지의 반대에도 아랑곳없이 느슨해진 저택의 기강을 바로잡기 시작했다."], ["When he grew to man's estate, and came back from Christchurch, he began to reform the slackened discipline of the hall, in spite of his father, who stood in awe of him.", "그는 어찌나 엄격하게 세련을 따지는 사람이었던지, 흰 넥타이 없이 식사하느니 차라리 굶기를 택할 위인이었다. 한번은 그가 막 대학에서 돌아왔을 무렵, 집사 호록스가 편지를 쟁반에 받쳐 들지 않고 그냥 가져온 일이 있었는데, 그는 그 하인에게 한 번 눈길을 던지고는 어찌나 신랄한 말을 퍼부었던지, 그 뒤로 호록스는 그의 앞에서 늘 벌벌 떨었다."], ["He was a man of such rigid refinement, that he would have starved rather than have dined without a white neckcloth.", "온 집안이 그에게 머리를 조아렸다. 그가 집에 와 있을 때면 크롤리 부인은 일찌감치 머리의 종이를 풀었고, 피트 경의 진흙투성이 각반도 자취를 감췄다."], ["Once, when just from college, and when Horrocks the butler brought him a letter without placing it previously on a tray, he gave that domestic a look, and administered to him a speech so cutting, that Horrocks ever after trembled before him; the whole household bowed to him: Lady Crawley's curl-papers came off earlier when he was at home: Sir Pitt's muddy gaiters disappeared; and if that incorrigible old man still adhered to other old habits, he never fuddled himself with rum-and-water in his son's presence, and only talked to his servants in a very reserved and polite manner; and those persons remarked that Sir Pitt never swore at Lady Crawley while his son was in the room.", "그리고 그 구제 불능의 늙은이가 여전히 다른 옛 버릇은 못 버렸을지언정, 아들이 보는 앞에서는 럼과 물을 들이켜 곤드레가 되는 법이 없었고, 하인들에게도 그저 매우 점잖고 예의 바르게 말할 따름이었다. 또 사람들의 말로는, 아들이 방에 있는 동안에는 피트 경이 크롤리 부인에게 결코 욕설을 퍼붓지 않았다고 한다."]], [["It was he who taught the butler to say, \"My lady is served,\" and who insisted on handing her ladyship in to dinner.", "집사에게 \"마님의 식사가 준비되었습니다\"라고 아뢰도록 가르친 것도 그였고, 부인을 식탁으로 모시고 들어가기를 한사코 고집한 것도 그였다."], ["He seldom spoke to her, but when he did it was with the most powerful respect; and he never let her quit the apartment without rising in the most stately manner to open the door, and making an elegant bow at her egress.", "그는 부인에게 좀처럼 말을 걸지 않았으나, 말을 할 때면 더없이 깍듯한 존경을 담았다. 그리고 부인이 방을 나설 때면 언제나 더없이 위엄 있는 자세로 일어나 문을 열어 주고, 부인이 나가는 길에 우아하게 절을 하지 않는 법이 없었다."]], [["At Eton he was called Miss Crawley; and there, I am sorry to say, his younger brother Rawdon used to lick him violently.", "이튼에서 그는 크롤리 양이라 불렸다. 그리고 거기서, 말하기 안타깝지만, 그의 동생 로던이 곧잘 그를 사납게 두들겨 패곤 했다."], ["But though his parts were not brilliant, he made up for his lack of talent by meritorious industry, and was never known, during eight years at school, to be subject to that punishment which it is generally thought none but a cherub can escape.", "그러나 비록 그의 재주는 빼어나지 못했어도, 그는 재능의 부족을 가상한 근면으로 메웠고, 학교에서 보낸 팔 년 동안 천사 말고는 그 누구도 면치 못한다고 흔히들 여기는 그 체벌을 한 번도 받은 적이 없었다."]], [["At college his career was of course highly creditable.", "대학에서의 그의 이력은 물론 더없이 훌륭한 것이었다."], ["And here he prepared himself for public life, into which he was to be introduced by the patronage of his grandfather, Lord Binkie, by studying the ancient and modern orators with great assiduity, and by speaking unceasingly at the debating societies.", "그리고 그는 이곳에서 공적인 삶을 준비했는데, 그 길로 들어서는 데는 외조부인 빈키 경의 후원이 있을 터였다. 그는 고금의 웅변가들을 더없이 부지런히 연구하고, 토론회에서 쉴 새 없이 발언함으로써 그 준비에 임했다."], ["But though he had a fine flux of words, and delivered his little voice with great pomposity and pleasure to himself, and never advanced any sentiment or opinion which was not perfectly trite and stale, and supported by a Latin quotation; yet he failed somehow, in spite of a mediocrity which ought to have insured any man a success.", "그러나 비록 그가 막힘없이 술술 말을 쏟아 냈고, 그 가느다란 목소리를 한껏 거드름을 피우며 스스로 흡족해 가며 내질렀으며, 라틴어 인용으로 뒷받침되지 않은, 그리고 완벽하게 진부하고 케케묵지 않은 감상이나 의견이란 단 한 번도 내놓은 적이 없었음에도, 그는 어찌 된 일인지 실패하고 말았다. 누구에게나 성공을 보장해 주었어야 마땅할 그 범용함에도 불구하고 말이다."], ["He did not even get the prize poem, which all his friends said he was sure of.", "그는 친구들이 하나같이 따 놓은 당상이라 장담하던 시 부문 상조차 받지 못했다."]], [["After leaving college he became Private Secretary to Lord Binkie, and was then appointed Attache to the Legation at Pumpernickel, which post he filled with perfect honour, and brought home despatches, consisting of Strasburg pie, to the Foreign Minister of the day.", "대학을 떠난 뒤 그는 빈키 경의 개인 비서가 되었고, 이어 품퍼니켈 공사관의 수습 외교관으로 임명되었다. 그는 더없이 명예롭게 그 직책을 수행했으며, 스트라스부르 파이로 이루어진 외교 문서를 당대 외무 장관에게 가지고 돌아왔다."], ["After remaining ten years Attache (several years after the lamented Lord Binkie's demise), and finding the advancement slow, he at length gave up the diplomatic service in some disgust, and began to turn country gentleman.", "십 년간 수습 외교관으로 머문(애도받아 마땅한 빈키 경이 서거한 뒤로도 몇 해가 더 지난) 끝에, 승진이 더디다는 것을 깨닫고는 마침내 다소 환멸을 느끼며 외교 업무를 그만두고 시골 신사로 처신하기 시작했다."]], [["He wrote a pamphlet on Malt on returning to England (for he was an ambitious man, and always liked to be before the public), and took a strong part in the Negro Emancipation question.", "그는 영국으로 돌아오면서 맥아에 관한 소책자를 한 편 썼고(그는 야심 찬 사람이라 늘 대중 앞에 나서기를 좋아했다), 흑인 노예 해방 문제에 강력히 가담했다."], ["Then he became a friend of Mr. Wilberforce's, whose politics he admired, and had that famous correspondence with the Reverend Silas Hornblower, on the Ashantee Mission.", "그러고는 윌버포스 씨의 벗이 되어 그의 정치를 흠모했으며, 아샨티 선교 문제를 두고 사일러스 혼블로어 목사와 그 유명한 서신 왕래를 나누었다."], ["He was in London, if not for the Parliament session, at least in May, for the religious meetings.", "그는 의회 회기 때는 아닐지언정, 적어도 종교 모임이 열리는 오월이면 런던에 있었다."], ["In the country he was a magistrate, and an active visitor and speaker among those destitute of religious instruction.", "시골에서는 치안 판사였고, 종교적 가르침을 받지 못한 이들 사이를 부지런히 방문하고 연설하는 사람이었다."], ["He was said to be paying his addresses to Lady Jane Sheepshanks, Lord Southdown's third daughter, and whose sister, Lady Emily, wrote those sweet tracts, \"The Sailor's True Binnacle,\" and \"The Applewoman of Finchley Common.\"", "그는 사우스다운 경의 셋째 딸인 제인 시프섕크스 부인에게 구애 중이라는 말이 돌았는데, 그 부인의 언니 에밀리 부인은 \"선원의 진정한 나침반함\"과 \"핀칠리 공유지의 사과 파는 여인\"이라는 그 감미로운 소책자들을 쓴 이였다."]], [["Miss Sharp's accounts of his employment at Queen's Crawley were not caricatures.", "샤프 양이 전한 그가 퀸스크롤리에서 하는 일들에 대한 묘사는 결코 과장이 아니었다."], ["He subjected the servants there to the devotional exercises before mentioned, in which (and so much the better) he brought his father to join.", "그는 그곳의 하인들에게 앞서 언급한 신앙 예배를 강요했고, 거기에(그러는 편이 차라리 나았다) 자기 아버지까지 끌어들여 동참하게 만들었다."], ["He patronised an Independent meeting-house in Crawley parish, much to the indignation of his uncle the Rector, and to the consequent delight of Sir Pitt, who was induced to go himself once or twice, which occasioned some violent sermons at Crawley parish church, directed point-blank at the Baronet's old Gothic pew there.", "그는 크롤리 교구의 한 독립 교회를 후원했는데, 이는 목사인 숙부의 분노를 크게 샀고, 그 결과 피트 경을 기쁘게 했다. 피트 경은 한두 번 손수 그곳에 나가도록 부추겨졌고, 이 일은 크롤리 교구 교회에서 격렬한 설교 몇 차례를 불러일으켰으니, 그 설교들은 거기 자리한 준남작의 낡은 고딕식 좌석을 정면으로 겨냥한 것이었다."], ["Honest Sir Pitt, however, did not feel the force of these discourses, as he always took his nap during sermon-time.", "그러나 정직한 피트 경은 설교 시간 내내 늘 낮잠을 잤던 탓에, 그 일장 설교의 위력을 조금도 느끼지 못했다."]], [["Mr. Crawley was very earnest, for the good of the nation and of the Christian world, that the old gentleman should yield him up his place in Parliament; but this the elder constantly refused to do.", "크롤리 씨는 나라와 그리스도교 세계의 이익을 위해 그 노신사가 자신에게 의회 의석을 양보해 주기를 더없이 간절히 바랐다."], ["Both were of course too prudent to give up the fifteen hundred a year which was brought in by the second seat (at this period filled by Mr.", "그러나 아버지는 한결같이 이를 거절했다. 둘 다 물론 두 번째 의석에서 나오는 연 천오백 파운드를 포기하기에는 너무 신중했다(당시 이 의석은 콰드룬 씨가 차지하고 있었는데, 그는 노예 문제에 관해 백지위임을 받은 처지였다)."], ["Quadroon, with carte blanche on the Slave question); indeed the family estate was much embarrassed, and the income drawn from the borough was of great use to the house of Queen's Crawley.", "사실 가문의 영지는 빚에 크게 옭매여 있었고, 그 자치구에서 끌어오는 수입은 퀸스크롤리 가문에 큰 보탬이 되었다."]], [["It had never recovered the heavy fine imposed upon Walpole Crawley, first baronet, for peculation in the Tape and Sealing Wax Office.", "그 가문은 초대 준남작 월폴 크롤리가 노끈 봉랍 관리국에서 공금을 횡령한 죄로 부과된 막대한 벌금에서 끝내 회복하지 못했다."], ["Sir Walpole was a jolly fellow, eager to seize and to spend money (alieni appetens, sui profusus, as Mr.", "월폴 경은 유쾌한 사람으로, 돈을 거머쥐고 또 쓰는 데 열을 올렸으며(남의 것은 탐하고 제 것은 흥청망청 써 댔다고, 크롤리 씨라면 한숨지으며 말했을 것이다), 그 시절 퀸스크롤리에서 끊임없이 이어진 폭음과 환대 덕분에 온 고을의 사랑을 받았다."], ["Crawley would remark with a sigh), and in his day beloved by all the county for the constant drunkenness and hospitality which was maintained at Queen's Crawley.", "그때는 술 창고에 부르고뉴 포도주가 가득했고, 개집에는 사냥개가, 마구간에는 늠름한 사냥말이 가득했다. 그런데 지금은, 퀸스크롤리가 가진 말이라고 해 봐야 밭을 갈거나 트라팔가르 역마차를 끄는 처지였다."], ["The cellars were filled with burgundy then, the kennels with hounds, and the stables with gallant hunters; now, such horses as Queen's Crawley possessed went to plough, or ran in the Trafalgar Coach; and it was with a team of these very horses, on an off-day, that Miss Sharp was brought to the Hall; for boor as he was, Sir Pitt was a stickler for his dignity while at home, and seldom drove out but with four horses, and though he dined off boiled mutton, had always three footmen to serve it.", "샤프 양이 바로 이런 말들로 이루어진 한 무리에 실려, 휴마일에 저택으로 끌려온 것이었다. 비록 촌뜨기였으나, 피트 경은 집에 있을 때만큼은 자기 체면에 깐깐한 사람이라 좀처럼 네 마리 말이 아니고서는 외출하는 법이 없었고, 비록 삶은 양고기로 끼니를 때울지언정 그것을 내오는 데는 늘 하인 셋을 두었다."]], [["If mere parsimony could have made a man rich, Sir Pitt Crawley might have become very wealthy--if he had been an attorney in a country town, with no capital but his brains, it is very possible that he would have turned them to good account, and might have achieved for himself a very considerable influence and competency.", "단순한 인색함만으로 사람이 부자가 될 수 있었다면, 피트 크롤리 경은 어마어마한 부자가 되었을지도 모른다. 만일 그가 밑천이라고는 제 머리밖에 없는 시골 마을의 변호사였다면, 그 머리를 잘 굴려서 자기 앞으로 상당한 영향력과 재산을 거머쥐었을 공산이 매우 크다."], ["But he was unluckily endowed with a good name and a large though encumbered estate, both of which went rather to injure than to advance him.", "그러나 그는 불운하게도 훌륭한 이름과, 빚에 옭매였을지언정 큰 영지를 타고났는데, 이 둘은 모두 그를 키우기보다는 해치는 쪽으로 작용했다."], ["He had a taste for law, which cost him many thousands yearly; and being a great deal too clever to be robbed, as he said, by any single agent, allowed his affairs to be mismanaged by a dozen, whom he all equally mistrusted.", "그는 소송을 즐기는 취향이 있었고, 이는 해마다 그에게 수천 파운드를 치르게 했다. 그리고 그가 말하듯 어느 한 대리인 손에 등쳐먹히기에는 자기가 너무 영리한 탓에, 그는 자기 일을 열두 명에게 맡겨 엉망으로 처리되게 내버려 두었으니, 그 열두 명을 하나같이 똑같이 불신했다."], ["He was such a sharp landlord, that he could hardly find any but bankrupt tenants; and such a close farmer, as to grudge almost the seed to the ground, whereupon revengeful Nature grudged him the crops which she granted to more liberal husbandmen.", "그는 어찌나 야박한 지주였던지 파산한 소작인밖에는 도무지 구할 수가 없었고, 어찌나 인색한 농사꾼이었던지 땅에 뿌릴 씨앗마저 아까워했다. 그러자 앙심을 품은 자연이, 더 너그러운 농부에게는 베푸는 수확을 그에게는 인색하게 굴었다."], ["He speculated in every possible way; he worked mines; bought canal-shares; horsed coaches; took government contracts, and was the busiest man and magistrate of his county.", "그는 가능한 온갖 방식으로 투기에 손을 댔다. 광산을 파고, 운하 주식을 사고, 역마차에 말을 대고, 정부와 계약을 맺었으며, 그 고을에서 가장 분주한 사람이자 치안 판사였다."], ["As he would not pay honest agents at his granite quarry, he had the satisfaction of finding that four overseers ran away, and took fortunes with them to America.", "그가 화강암 채석장의 정직한 일꾼들에게 품삯을 치르지 않으려 한 탓에, 그는 감독관 넷이 달아나면서 재산을 챙겨 미국으로 가 버리는 흡족한 꼴을 맛보았다."], ["For want of proper precautions, his coal-mines filled with water: the government flung his contract of damaged beef upon his hands: and for his coach-horses, every mail proprietor in the kingdom knew that he lost more horses than any man in the country, from underfeeding and buying cheap.", "제대로 된 대비책을 마련하지 않은 탓에 그의 탄광은 물로 가득 찼고, 정부는 상한 쇠고기 납품 계약을 그에게 도로 떠안겼다. 또 그의 역마차용 말로 말하자면, 왕국의 우편마차 주인이라면 누구나, 사료를 박하게 먹이고 싸구려로 사들이는 탓에 그가 이 고을의 그 누구보다 많은 말을 잃는다는 것을 알았다."], ["In disposition he was sociable, and far from being proud; nay, he rather preferred the society of a farmer or a horse-dealer to that of a gentleman, like my lord, his son: he was fond of drink, of swearing, of joking with the farmers'", "기질로 말하면 그는 사교적이었고 거만함과는 거리가 멀었다. 아니, 그는 자기 아들인 나리 같은 신사보다 차라리 농부나 말 거간꾼과 어울리는 편을 더 좋아했다."], ["daughters: he was never known to give away a shilling or to do a good action, but was of a pleasant, sly, laughing mood, and would cut his joke and drink his glass with a tenant and sell him up the next day; or have his laugh with the poacher he was transporting with equal good humour.", "그는 술을 좋아하고, 욕설을 좋아하고, 농부의 딸들과 시시덕거리기를 좋아했다. 그가 일 실링이라도 베풀거나 선행을 했다는 말은 한 번도 들리지 않았으나, 그는 유쾌하고, 능청스럽고, 잘 웃는 기질이라, 소작인과 농담을 주고받고 술잔을 기울이고는 이튿날 그를 알거지로 만들어 쫓아내는가 하면, 똑같이 흐뭇한 기분으로 자기가 유배 보내는 밀렵꾼과 한바탕 웃음을 나누곤 했다."], ["His politeness for the fair sex has already been hinted at by Miss Rebecca Sharp--in a word, the whole baronetage, peerage, commonage of England, did not contain a more cunning, mean, selfish, foolish, disreputable old man.", "여성에 대한 그의 정중함은 이미 리베카 샤프 양이 넌지시 비친 바 있거니와, 한마디로 영국의 준남작이며 귀족이며 평민을 통틀어, 이보다 더 교활하고, 비열하고, 이기적이고, 어리석고, 평판 나쁜 늙은이는 없었다."], ["That blood-red hand of Sir Pitt Crawley's would be in anybody's pocket except his own; and it is with grief and pain, that, as admirers of the British aristocracy, we find ourselves obliged to admit the existence of so many ill qualities in a person whose name is in Debrett.", "피트 크롤리 경의 그 핏빛 손은 제 주머니만 빼고 누구의 주머니든 넘나들었다. 영국 귀족을 흠모하는 사람으로서, 데브렛 명감에 이름이 오른 인물에게 그토록 많은 못된 자질이 깃들어 있음을 인정하지 않을 수 없으니, 그것이 슬프고도 가슴 아픈 일이다."]], [["One great cause why Mr. Crawley had such a hold over the affections of his father, resulted from money arrangements.", "크롤리 씨가 아버지의 애정을 그토록 단단히 거머쥔 큰 까닭 하나는 금전 관계에서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["The Baronet owed his son a sum of money out of the jointure of his mother, which he did not find it convenient to pay; indeed he had an almost invincible repugnance to paying anybody, and could only be brought by force to discharge his debts.", "준남작은 아들의 모친이 남긴 과부 재산 가운데 일부를 아들에게 빚지고 있었으나, 그것을 갚는 것이 편치 않았다. 사실 그는 누구에게든 돈을 치른다는 데 거의 이겨 낼 수 없는 거부감을 품고 있어서, 빚을 청산하게 만들려면 억지로 강제하는 수밖에 없었다."], ["Miss Sharp calculated (for she became, as we shall hear speedily, inducted into most of the secrets of the family) that the mere payment of his creditors cost the honourable Baronet several hundreds yearly; but this was a delight he could not forego; he had a savage pleasure in making the poor wretches wait, and in shifting from court to court and from term to term the period of satisfaction.", "샤프 양은(우리가 곧 듣게 되겠지만, 그녀는 이 집안의 거의 모든 비밀을 꿰뚫게 되었다) 그 명예로운 준남작이 단지 채권자들에게 돈을 치르는 데만도 해마다 수백 파운드를 쓴다고 계산해 냈다. 그러나 이것이야말로 그가 결코 포기할 수 없는 낙이었다."], ["What's the good of being in Parliament, he said, if you must pay your debts?", "그는 그 가엾은 작자들을 기다리게 하고, 변제 시기를 이 법정에서 저 법정으로, 이 개정기에서 저 개정기로 미루는 데서 야만적인 즐거움을 느꼈다. 빚을 갚아야 한다면 의회에 들어가는 게 무슨 소용이냐고, 그는 말했다."], ["Hence, indeed, his position as a senator was not a little useful to him.", "그러니 사실 상원 의원으로서의 그의 지위는 그에게 적잖이 쓸모가 있었던 것이다."]], [["Vanity Fair--Vanity Fair!", "허영의 시장이여, 허영의 시장이여!"], ["Here was a man, who could not spell, and did not care to read--who had the habits and the cunning of a boor: whose aim in life was pettifogging: who never had a taste, or emotion, or enjoyment, but what was sordid and foul; and yet he had rank, and honours, and power, somehow: and was a dignitary of the land, and a pillar of the state.", "여기, 철자도 못 쓰고 읽을 마음도 없는 한 사내가 있었다. 촌뜨기의 습성과 교활함을 지녔고, 인생의 목표라고는 좀스러운 송사뿐이며, 추잡하고 더러운 것이 아니고서는 취향도 감흥도 즐거움도 가져 본 적 없는 자였다."], ["He was high sheriff, and rode in a golden coach.", "그런데도 그는 어찌 된 셈인지 지위와 명예와 권력을 지녔고, 이 나라의 고관이자 국가의 기둥이었다."], ["Great ministers and statesmen courted him; and in Vanity Fair he had a higher place than the most brilliant genius or spotless virtue.", "그는 주의 장관이었고 황금 마차를 타고 다녔다. 위대한 장관과 정치가 들이 그에게 아첨했으며, 허영의 시장에서 그는 가장 눈부신 천재나 한 점 티 없는 미덕보다 더 높은 자리를 차지했다."]], [["Sir Pitt had an unmarried half-sister who inherited her mother's large fortune, and though the Baronet proposed to borrow this money of her on mortgage, Miss Crawley declined the offer, and preferred the security of the funds.", "피트 경에게는 미혼인 이복 누이가 하나 있었는데, 그녀는 자기 어머니의 막대한 재산을 물려받았다. 준남작은 이 돈을 담보 잡고 그녀에게서 빌리려고 제안했으나, 크롤리 양은 그 제안을 거절하고 공채의 안전함을 선호했다."], ["She had signified, however, her intention of leaving her inheritance between Sir Pitt's second son and the family at the Rectory, and had once or twice paid the debts of Rawdon Crawley in his career at college and in the army.", "그러나 그녀는 자기 유산을 피트 경의 둘째 아들과 목사관 식구들에게 나누어 주겠다는 뜻을 내비친 바 있었고, 로던 크롤리가 대학과 군대에서 지내는 동안 한두 번 그의 빚을 갚아 준 적도 있었다."], ["Miss Crawley was, in consequence, an object of great respect when she came to Queen's Crawley, for she had a balance at her banker's which would have made her beloved anywhere.", "그 결과 크롤리 양은 퀸스크롤리에 올 때마다 깊은 존경의 대상이 되었으니, 어디에서든 사랑받게 해 줄 만한 잔액이 그녀의 은행에 있었기 때문이다."]], [["What a dignity it gives an old lady, that balance at the banker's!", "은행 잔액이 늙은 부인에게 부여하는 위엄이라니!"], ["How tenderly we look at her faults if she is a relative (and may every reader have a score of such), what a kind good-natured old creature we find her!", "그녀가 친척이라면 우리는 그 결점들을 어찌나 너그럽게 봐주는지(부디 모든 독자께 그런 친척이 한 다스씩 있기를), 어찌나 친절하고 마음씨 고운 늙은이로 여기는지!"], ["How the junior partner of Hobbs and Dobbs leads her smiling to the carriage with the lozenge upon it, and the fat wheezy coachman!", "홉스 앤드 돕스 상회의 후배 동업자는 그녀를, 마름모꼴 가문 문장이 박히고 살지고 씩씩대는 마부가 모는 마차로 어찌나 웃는 낯으로 모셔다 드리는지!"], ["How, when she comes to pay us a visit, we generally find an opportunity to let our friends know her station in the world!", "그녀가 우리를 방문하러 오면, 우리는 대개 어떻게든 기회를 잡아 그녀가 세상에서 차지하는 지위를 친구들에게 알리는지!"], ["We say (and with perfect truth) I wish I had Miss MacWhirter's signature to a cheque for five thousand pounds.", "우리는(그리고 더없이 진실되게) 맥휘터 양의 서명이 든 오천 파운드짜리 수표가 있었으면 좋겠다고 말한다."], ["She wouldn't miss it, says your wife.", "그 정도야 그분께 아쉬울 것도 없죠, 하고 아내가 말한다."], ["She is my aunt, say you, in an easy careless way, when your friend asks if Miss MacWhirter is any relative.", "친구가 맥휘터 양이 친척이냐고 물으면 자네는 짐짓 태평하고 무심한 투로 우리 고모님이시라네, 하고 말한다."], ["Your wife is perpetually sending her little testimonies of affection, your little girls work endless worsted baskets, cushions, and footstools for her.", "자네 아내는 끊임없이 그녀에게 자그마한 애정의 표시를 보내고, 자네의 어린 딸들은 끝도 없이 털실로 바구니와 방석과 발받침을 짠다."], ["What a good fire there is in her room when she comes to pay you a visit, although your wife laces her stays without one!", "그녀가 자네를 방문하러 오면 그녀의 방에는 어찌나 따뜻한 불이 활활 타는지, 정작 자네 아내는 불도 없이 코르셋 끈을 조이는데 말이다!"], ["The house during her stay assumes a festive, neat, warm, jovial, snug appearance not visible at other seasons.", "그녀가 머무는 동안 집은 다른 철에는 볼 수 없는 흥겹고, 말끔하고, 따스하고, 유쾌하고, 아늑한 모습을 띤다."], ["You yourself, dear sir, forget to go to sleep after dinner, and find yourself all of a sudden (though you invariably lose) very fond of a rubber.", "친애하는 선생, 자네 자신부터 저녁 식사 뒤에 깜빡 잠드는 것을 잊고, 어느새 카드 한판을(자네는 늘 지면서도) 무척 좋아하는 자신을 발견하게 된다."], ["What good dinners you have--game every day, Malmsey-Madeira, and no end of fish from London.", "자네는 또 어찌나 잘 먹는지, 날마다 사냥한 고기에, 맘지 마데이라 포도주에, 런던에서 끝도 없이 올라오는 생선이라니."], ["Even the servants in the kitchen share in the general prosperity; and, somehow, during the stay of Miss MacWhirter's fat coachman, the beer is grown much stronger, and the consumption of tea and sugar in the nursery (where her maid takes her meals) is not regarded in the least.", "부엌의 하인들마저 이 전반적인 번영을 함께 누린다. 그리고 어찌 된 셈인지, 맥휘터 양의 살진 마부가 머무는 동안에는 맥주가 한결 독해지고, 아이 방에서(그녀의 하녀가 거기서 끼니를 든다) 차와 설탕을 소비하는 것쯤은 조금도 따지지 않게 된다."], ["Is it so, or is it not so?", "그러한가, 그렇지 아니한가?"], ["I appeal to the middle classes.", "나는 중산층에게 묻겠다. 아, 자비로운 신이여!"], ["Ah, gracious powers!", "부디 제게 늙은 고모 한 분을 보내 주소서."], ["I wish you would send me an old aunt--a maiden aunt--an aunt with a lozenge on her carriage, and a front of light coffee-coloured hair--how my children should work workbags for her, and my Julia and I would make her comfortable!", "미혼의 고모, 마차에 가문 문장을 박고, 연한 커피색 앞머리를 얹은 고모를 보내 주소서. 그러면 우리 아이들이 그분을 위해 어찌나 손가방을 짜 댈 것이며, 우리 줄리아와 나는 그분을 어찌나 편안히 모실 것인가!"], ["Sweet--sweet vision!", "감미로운, 감미로운 환상이여!"], ["Foolish--foolish dream!", "어리석은, 어리석은 꿈이여!"]], [["Miss Sharp Begins to Make Friends\nAnd now, being received as a member of the amiable family whose portraits we have sketched in the foregoing pages, it became naturally Rebecca's duty to make herself, as she said, agreeable to her benefactors, and to gain their confidence to the utmost of her power.", "이제 앞선 페이지들에서 그 초상을 그려 보인 그 사랑스러운 가족의 일원으로 받아들여졌으니, 레베카로서는 본인의 표현대로 은인들의 마음에 들도록 처신하고, 제 능력이 닿는 데까지 그들의 신임을 얻는 것이 자연스러운 의무가 되었다. 보호자 없는 고아가 지닌 이 감사라는 미덕을 어찌 감탄하지 않을 수 있겠는가."], ["Who can but admire this quality of gratitude in an unprotected orphan; and, if there entered some degree of selfishness into her calculations, who can say but that her prudence was perfectly justifiable?", "그리고 만약 그녀의 계산에 얼마간의 이기심이 섞여 들었다 한들, 그 신중함이 더없이 정당한 것이 아니라고 누가 단언할 수 있겠는가? \"나는 이 세상에 혼자야.\""], ["\"I am alone in the world,\"", "의지가지없는 그 소녀는 말했다."], ["said the friendless girl.", "\"내 손으로 벌어들이는 것 말고는 기댈 데가 없어."], ["\"I have nothing to look for but what my own labour can bring me; and while that little pink-faced chit Amelia, with not half my sense, has ten thousand pounds and an establishment secure, poor Rebecca (and my figure is far better than hers) has only herself and her own wits to trust to.", "게다가 나보다 분별력이 절반도 안 되는 저 발그레한 얼굴의 꼬맹이 애밀리아는 만 파운드에다 탄탄한 살림까지 가졌는데, 가엾은 레베카는(게다가 내 몸매가 그 애보다 훨씬 낫지) 오직 제 자신과 제 재치만 믿을 수밖에 없는 신세지. 자, 내 재치가 떳떳한 생계를 마련해 줄 수 있을지, 그리고 언젠가는 내가 애밀리아 양에게 그 애보다 내가 진정 우월하다는 걸 보여 줄 수 있을지 어디 두고 보자고."], ["Well, let us see if my wits cannot provide me with an honourable maintenance, and if some day or the other I cannot show Miss Amelia my real superiority over her.", "가엾은 애밀리아가 싫다는 건 아니야. 그토록 악의 없고 선량한 사람을 누가 싫어할 수 있겠어?"], ["Not that I dislike poor Amelia: who can dislike such a harmless, good-natured creature?--only it will be a fine day when I can take my place above her in the world, as why, indeed, should I not?\"", "다만 내가 세상에서 그 애 위에 자리 잡는 날이 온다면 그건 참 멋진 날일 거야. 그러지 못할 까닭이 대체 어디 있겠어?\""], ["Thus it was that our little romantic friend formed visions of the future for herself--nor must we be scandalised that, in all her castles in the air, a husband was the principal inhabitant.", "우리의 작고 낭만적인 벗은 그렇게 제 앞날에 대한 환상을 빚어 갔다. 그녀가 쌓아 올린 모든 공중누각에 남편이 주요 거주자로 자리 잡고 있다 하여 우리가 충격받을 필요는 없다."], ["Of what else have young ladies to think, but husbands?", "젊은 숙녀들이 남편 말고 무슨 생각을 하겠는가?"], ["Of what else do their dear mammas think?", "그들의 사랑스러운 어머니들 또한 그것 말고 무슨 생각을 하겠는가?"], ["\"I must be my own mamma,\"", "\"내 어머니 노릇은 내가 해야지.\""], ["said Rebecca; not without a tingling consciousness of defeat, as she thought over her little misadventure with Jos Sedley.", "레베카는 그렇게 말했다. 조스 세들리와의 그 자그마한 낭패를 떠올리며, 패배감이 짜릿하게 스쳐 가는 것을 느끼면서."]], [["So she wisely determined to render her position with the Queen's Crawley family comfortable and secure, and to this end resolved to make friends of every one around her who could at all interfere with her comfort.", "그리하여 그녀는 퀸스크롤리 가에서 자신의 처지를 편안하고 안전하게 만들겠노라 현명하게 마음먹었고, 그 목적을 위해 조금이라도 자기 안락에 영향을 끼칠 만한 주위 모든 이들을 벗으로 삼기로 결심했다."]], [["As my Lady Crawley was not one of these personages, and a woman, moreover, so indolent and void of character as not to be of the least consequence in her own house, Rebecca soon found that it was not at all necessary to cultivate her good will--indeed, impossible to gain it.", "크롤리 부인은 그런 인물 축에 들지 못했을 뿐 아니라, 워낙 게으르고 줏대가 없어 제 집안에서조차 털끝만큼의 비중도 없는 여자였으므로, 레베카는 곧 그 호의를 가꿀 필요가 전혀 없다는 것을, 아니 얻는 것 자체가 불가능하다는 것을 깨달았다. 그녀는 제자들에게 그들의 \"가엾은 어머니\""], ["She used to talk to her pupils about their \"poor mamma\"; and, though she treated that lady with every demonstration of cool respect, it was to the rest of the family that she wisely directed the chief part of her attentions.", "이야기를 하곤 했다. 그리고 그 부인을 향해서는 온갖 냉랭한 존경의 표시로 대하긴 했으나, 정작 정성의 큰 몫은 현명하게도 가족의 나머지 사람들에게 쏟았다."]], [["With the young people, whose applause she thoroughly gained, her method was pretty simple.", "마음껏 갈채를 얻어 낸 아이들을 다루는 그녀의 방법은 꽤나 단순했다."], ["She did not pester their young brains with too much learning, but, on the contrary, let them have their own way in regard to educating themselves; for what instruction is more effectual than self-instruction?", "그녀는 아이들의 어린 머릿속을 지나친 학식으로 들볶지 않았고, 오히려 스스로 깨치도록 제멋대로 내버려 두었다. 자습보다 더 효과적인 가르침이 어디 있겠는가?"], ["The eldest was rather fond of books, and as there was in the old library at Queen's Crawley a considerable provision of works of light literature of the last century, both in the French and English languages (they had been purchased by the Secretary of the Tape and Sealing Wax Office at the period of his disgrace), and as nobody ever troubled the bookshelves but herself, Rebecca was enabled agreeably, and, as it were, in playing, to impart a great deal of instruction to Miss Rose Crawley.", "큰딸은 책을 제법 좋아했는데, 퀸스크롤리의 낡은 서재에는 지난 세기의 가벼운 문학 작품들이 프랑스어와 영어로 상당량 갖춰져 있었고(끈과 봉랍 관리국 장관이 실각하던 무렵에 사들인 것들이었다), 그 책꽂이를 건드리는 이라곤 그녀밖에 없었으므로, 레베카는 즐겁게, 말하자면 놀이를 하듯이, 로즈 크롤리 양에게 많은 것을 가르칠 수 있었다."]], [["She and Miss Rose thus read together many delightful French and English works, among which may be mentioned those of the learned Dr. Smollett, of the ingenious Mr.", "그리하여 그녀와 로즈 양은 함께 수많은 유쾌한 프랑스어와 영어 작품을 읽었는데, 그중에는 박식한 스몰릿 박사, 재기 넘치는 헨리 필딩 씨, 우리의 불멸의 시인 그레이가 그토록 흠모했던 우아하고 환상적인 크레비용 2세, 그리고 만물에 통달한 볼테르 씨의 작품도 끼어 있었다."], ["Henry Fielding, of the graceful and fantastic Monsieur Crebillon the younger, whom our immortal poet Gray so much admired, and of the universal Monsieur de Voltaire. Once, when Mr.", "한번은 크롤리 씨가 아이들이 무엇을 읽고 있느냐고 묻자 가정교사는 \"스몰릿입니다\"라고 대답했다."], ["Crawley asked what the young people were reading, the governess replied \"Smollett.\" \"Oh, Smollett,\"", "\"오, 스몰릿이라.\""], ["said Mr. Crawley, quite satisfied.", "크롤리 씨는 자못 흡족해하며 말했다."], ["\"His history is more dull, but by no means so dangerous as that of Mr. Hume.", "\"그의 역사서는 흄 씨의 것보다 더 따분하긴 해도, 결코 그만큼 위험하지는 않지."], ["It is history you are reading?\"", "자네가 읽는 게 역사서인가?\""], ["\"Yes,\"", "\"네.\""], ["said Miss Rose; without, however, adding that it was the history of Mr.", "로즈 양이 대답했다. 다만 그것이 험프리 클링커 씨의 사연이라는 말은 덧붙이지 않았다."], ["Humphrey Clinker. On another occasion he was rather scandalised at finding his sister with a book of French plays; but as the governess remarked that it was for the purpose of acquiring the French idiom in conversation, he was fain to be content.", "또 한번은 누이가 프랑스 희곡집을 들고 있는 것을 보고 크롤리 씨가 다소 못마땅해했으나, 가정교사가 회화에서 프랑스어 관용 표현을 익히기 위함이라고 일러 주자 그도 마지못해 흡족해하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Mr. Crawley, as a diplomatist, was exceedingly proud of his own skill in speaking the French language (for he was of the world still), and not a little pleased with the compliments which the governess continually paid him upon his proficiency.", "크롤리 씨는 외교관답게 자신의 프랑스어 구사 솜씨를 무척 자랑스러워했고(아직은 세속에 발을 담그고 있는 사람이었으니까), 그 능숙함을 두고 가정교사가 끊임없이 바치는 찬사에 적잖이 우쭐해했다."]], [["Miss Violet's tastes were, on the contrary, more rude and boisterous than those of her sister.", "이와는 반대로 바이올렛 양의 취향은 언니보다 거칠고 왁자했다."], ["She knew the sequestered spots where the hens laid their eggs.", "그녀는 암탉들이 알을 낳는 외진 자리들을 죄다 알고 있었다."], ["She could climb a tree to rob the nests of the feathered songsters of their speckled spoils.", "나무에 기어올라 깃털 가진 가수들의 둥지를 털어 알록달록한 전리품을 빼앗을 줄도 알았다."], ["And her pleasure was to ride the young colts, and to scour the plains like Camilla.", "또 어린 망아지를 타고서 카밀라처럼 들판을 내달리는 것을 낙으로 삼았다."], ["She was the favourite of her father and of the stablemen.", "그녀는 아버지와 마구간 일꾼들의 귀염둥이였다."], ["She was the darling, and withal the terror of the cook; for she discovered the haunts of the jam-pots, and would attack them when they were within her reach.", "요리사에게는 사랑스러운 존재인 동시에 골칫거리였는데, 잼 단지들이 숨겨진 곳을 기어이 찾아내어 손에 닿기만 하면 들이쳤기 때문이다."], ["She and her sister were engaged in constant battles.", "그녀와 언니는 끊임없이 싸움을 벌였다."], ["Any of which peccadilloes, if Miss Sharp discovered, she did not tell them to Lady Crawley; who would have told them to the father, or worse, to Mr.", "이런 자잘한 비행들을 샤프 양은 설령 알아챈다 해도 크롤리 부인에게는 일러바치지 않았다. 그랬다가는 부인이 아버지에게, 더 나쁘게는 크롤리 씨에게 일러바칠 터였으니까."], ["Crawley; but promised not to tell if Miss Violet would be a good girl and love her governess.", "대신 그녀는 바이올렛 양이 착하게 굴고 가정교사를 사랑하기만 한다면 말하지 않겠노라 약속했다."]], [["With Mr. Crawley Miss Sharp was respectful and obedient.", "크롤리 씨에게 샤프 양은 공손하고 순종적이었다."], ["She used to consult him on passages of French which she could not understand, though her mother was a Frenchwoman, and which he would construe to her satisfaction: and, besides giving her his aid in profane literature, he was kind enough to select for her books of a more serious tendency, and address to her much of his conversation.", "어머니가 프랑스 여인이었음에도 도무지 이해할 수 없는 프랑스어 구절이 나오면 그에게 여쭙곤 했고, 그러면 그는 그녀가 흡족하도록 풀이해 주었다. 게다가 그는 속된 문학에서 그녀를 도와줄 뿐 아니라, 친절하게도 좀 더 진중한 경향의 책들을 골라 주고, 자기 이야기의 많은 부분을 그녀에게 들려주었다."], ["She admired, beyond measure, his speech at the Quashimaboo-Aid Society; took an interest in his pamphlet on malt: was often affected, even to tears, by his discourses of an evening, and would say--\"Oh, thank you, sir,\"", "그녀는 그가 콰시마부 구호 협회에서 한 연설을 한없이 우러러보았고, 맥아에 관한 그의 소책자에 관심을 보였으며, 저녁이면 그가 늘어놓는 설교에 눈물까지 흘릴 만큼 자주 감동하여 한숨과 함께 하늘을 올려다보며 \"오, 감사합니다, 선생님\""], ["with a sigh, and a look up to heaven, that made him occasionally condescend to shake hands with her.", "하고 말하곤 했는데, 그러면 그는 이따금 황송하게도 그녀와 악수까지 해 주었다."], ["\"Blood is everything, after all,\"", "\"역시 핏줄이 모든 걸 결정하는 법이지.\""], ["would that aristocratic religionist say.", "이 귀족적인 신앙가는 그렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["\"How Miss Sharp is awakened by my words, when not one of the people here is touched.", "\"이곳 사람들은 하나도 감동받지 못하는데, 샤프 양은 내 말에 어쩌면 저토록 일깨워지는지."], ["I am too fine for them--too delicate.", "내가 저들에게는 너무 고상한 거야. 너무 섬세한 거지."], ["I must familiarise my style--but she understands it.", "내 화법을 좀 더 평이하게 길들여야겠어. 하지만 저 사람은 알아들어."], ["Her mother was a Montmorency.\"", "그 어머니가 몽모랑시 가문 출신이거든.\""]], [["Indeed it was from this famous family, as it appears, that Miss Sharp, by the mother's side, was descended.", "알려진 바로는, 샤프 양은 모친 쪽으로 바로 이 유서 깊은 가문의 후손이었다. 물론 그녀는 어머니가 무대에 섰었다는 말은 하지 않았다."], ["Of course she did not say that her mother had been on the stage; it would have shocked Mr. Crawley's religious scruples.", "그랬다가는 크롤리 씨의 신앙적 양심에 충격을 주었을 테니까."], ["How many noble emigres had this horrid revolution plunged in poverty!", "저 끔찍한 혁명이 얼마나 많은 고귀한 망명객을 가난 속에 빠뜨렸던가!"], ["She had several stories about her ancestors ere she had been many months in the house; some of which Mr. Crawley happened to find in D'Hozier's dictionary, which was in the library, and which strengthened his belief in their truth, and in the high-breeding of Rebecca.", "그 집에 들어온 지 몇 달 되지도 않아 그녀는 자기 조상들에 관한 이야기를 여럿 풀어놓았고, 그중 몇몇을 크롤리 씨는 마침 서재에 있던 도지에의 인명사전에서 찾아냈는데, 이것이 그 이야기들의 진실성과 레베카의 고귀한 혈통에 대한 그의 믿음을 한층 굳건히 해 주었다."], ["Are we to suppose from this curiosity and prying into dictionaries, could our heroine suppose that Mr.", "사전을 뒤지며 캐고 다닌 이 호기심에서 우리는 무엇을 짐작해야 할까. 우리의 여주인공은 크롤리 씨가 자기에게 마음이 있다고 짐작할 수 있었을까?"], ["Crawley was interested in her?--no, only in a friendly way.", "아니, 그저 우정 어린 관심일 뿐이었다."], ["Have we not stated that he was attached to Lady Jane Sheepshanks?", "그가 제인 시프섕크스 영애에게 마음을 두고 있다고 이미 말하지 않았던가?"]], [["He took Rebecca to task once or twice about the propriety of playing at backgammon with Sir Pitt, saying that it was a godless amusement, and that she would be much better engaged in reading \"Thrump's Legacy,\" or \"The Blind Washerwoman of Moorfields,\"", "그는 한두 번 레베카에게 피트 경과 주사위놀이를 하는 것이 온당한지를 두고 따져 물으며, 그것이 신을 모르는 오락이니 차라리 「스럼프의 유산」이나 「무어필즈의 눈먼 빨래꾼」, 아니면 좀 더 진중한 성격의 책을 읽는 편이 훨씬 나을 거라고 했다."], ["or any work of a more serious nature; but Miss Sharp said her dear mother used often to play the same game with the old Count de Trictrac and the venerable Abbe du Cornet, and so found an excuse for this and other worldly amusements.", "그러나 샤프 양은 사랑하는 어머니가 늙은 트릭트랙 백작이며 점잖은 뒤코르네 신부와 곧잘 똑같은 놀이를 하시곤 했다고 말하며, 이 오락을 비롯한 여러 세속적 즐길거리에 대한 핑계를 마련했다."]], [["But it was not only by playing at backgammon with the Baronet, that the little governess rendered herself agreeable to her employer.", "그런데 이 작은 가정교사가 고용주의 마음에 든 것은 비단 준남작과 주사위놀이를 해 준 덕분만은 아니었다."], ["She found many different ways of being useful to him.", "그녀는 그에게 쓸모 있는 사람이 될 갖가지 방법을 찾아냈다."], ["She read over, with indefatigable patience, all those law papers, with which, before she came to Queen's Crawley, he had promised to entertain her.", "그녀가 퀸스크롤리에 오기 전에 그가 즐겁게 해 주겠노라 호언했던 그 모든 법률 서류를, 그녀는 지칠 줄 모르는 인내심으로 끝까지 읽어 냈다."], ["She volunteered to copy many of his letters, and adroitly altered the spelling of them so as to suit the usages of the present day.", "그의 편지를 여러 통 베껴 쓰는 일도 자청했고, 그 철자법을 솜씨 좋게 고쳐 오늘날의 관용에 맞도록 손보았다."], ["She became interested in everything appertaining to the estate, to the farm, the park, the garden, and the stables; and so delightful a companion was she, that the Baronet would seldom take his after-breakfast walk without her (and the children of course), when she would give her advice as to the trees which were to be lopped in the shrubberies, the garden-beds to be dug, the crops which were to be cut, the horses which were to go to cart or plough.", "그녀는 영지며 농장이며 정원이며 화원이며 마구간에 이르기까지 관련된 모든 일에 관심을 기울이게 되었다. 그리하여 어찌나 유쾌한 동반자였던지, 준남작은 아침 식사 후 산책을 그녀(물론 아이들도 함께였다) 없이는 좀처럼 나서지 않게 되었으니, 그럴 때면 그녀는 관목 숲에서 쳐 내야 할 나무며, 갈아엎어야 할 화단이며, 베어야 할 작물이며, 수레나 쟁기에 매야 할 말들에 관해 조언을 건넸다."], ["Before she had been a year at Queen's Crawley she had quite won the Baronet's confidence; and the conversation at the dinner-table, which before used to be held between him and Mr.", "퀸스크롤리에 온 지 일 년도 채 되기 전에 그녀는 준남작의 신임을 완전히 얻어 냈다."], ["Horrocks the butler, was now almost exclusively between Sir Pitt and Miss Sharp. She was almost mistress of the house when Mr.", "식탁에서의 대화도 예전에는 그와 집사 호록스 씨 사이에서 오가던 것이, 이제는 거의 전적으로 피트 경과 샤프 양 사이에서만 오갔다."], ["Crawley was absent, but conducted herself in her new and exalted situation with such circumspection and modesty as not to offend the authorities of the kitchen and stable, among whom her behaviour was always exceedingly modest and affable.", "크롤리 씨가 자리를 비울 때면 그녀는 사실상 이 집의 안주인이나 다름없었으나, 새로이 격상된 자기 처지에서도 어찌나 신중하고 겸손하게 처신했던지 부엌과 마구간의 실권자들의 비위를 거스르는 법이 없었으니, 그들 사이에서 그녀의 태도는 언제나 더없이 겸손하고 상냥했다."], ["She was quite a different person from the haughty, shy, dissatisfied little girl whom we have known previously, and this change of temper proved great prudence, a sincere desire of amendment, or at any rate great moral courage on her part.", "그녀는 우리가 앞서 알던, 오만하고 수줍고 불평 많던 그 작은 소녀와는 사뭇 딴사람이었고, 이런 성정의 변화는 그녀 편에서 대단한 신중함이나, 고치려는 진실한 열망, 적어도 대단한 도덕적 용기를 입증하는 것이었다."], ["Whether it was the heart which dictated this new system of complaisance and humility adopted by our Rebecca, is to be proved by her after-history.", "우리의 레베카가 새로이 채택한 이 친절과 겸손의 방침을 명령한 것이 과연 그 마음이었는지는, 앞으로 펼쳐질 그녀의 행적이 증명해 줄 것이다."], ["A system of hypocrisy, which lasts through whole years, is one seldom satisfactorily practised by a person of one-and-twenty; however, our readers will recollect, that, though young in years, our heroine was old in life and experience, and we have written to no purpose if they have not discovered that she was a very clever woman.", "몇 해를 통째로 이어 가는 위선의 방침이란 스물한 살 먹은 사람이 흡족하게 실행하기에는 좀처럼 어려운 노릇이다. 그러나 독자들은 우리의 여주인공이 나이는 어려도 인생과 경험에서는 노숙했음을 기억할 것이며, 만약 그녀가 무척 영리한 여자임을 독자들이 진작 알아채지 못했다면 우리가 헛글을 써 온 셈이리라."]], [["The elder and younger son of the house of Crawley were, like the gentleman and lady in the weather-box, never at home together--they hated each other cordially: indeed, Rawdon Crawley, the dragoon, had a great contempt for the establishment altogether, and seldom came thither except when his aunt paid her annual visit.", "크롤리 가의 장남과 차남은 날씨 상자 속의 신사와 숙녀처럼 결코 한집에 함께 머무는 법이 없었다. 둘은 진심으로 서로를 미워했다. 실제로 용기병인 로던 크롤리는 이 집안 전체를 몹시 경멸하여, 고모가 해마다 방문할 때를 빼고는 좀처럼 이곳에 발걸음하지 않았다."]], [["The great good quality of this old lady has been mentioned.", "이 노부인의 빼어난 장점은 앞서 언급한 바 있다."], ["She possessed seventy thousand pounds, and had almost adopted Rawdon.", "그녀는 칠만 파운드를 지녔고, 로던을 거의 양자나 다름없이 거두었다."], ["She disliked her elder nephew exceedingly, and despised him as a milksop.", "그녀는 큰조카를 몹시 싫어하여 물러 터진 겁쟁이라고 깔보았다."], ["In return he did not hesitate to state that her soul was irretrievably lost, and was of opinion that his brother's chance in the next world was not a whit better.", "그 답례로 그는 서슴없이 고모의 영혼이 돌이킬 수 없이 타락했다고 단언했고, 동생이 내세에서 누릴 가망 또한 털끝만큼도 나을 게 없다는 견해를 품었다."], ["\"She is a godless woman of the world,\" would Mr.", "\"그 양반은 신을 모르는 세속의 여인이야.\""], ["Crawley say; \"she lives with atheists and Frenchmen.", "크롤리 씨는 그렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["My mind shudders when I think of her awful, awful situation, and that, near as she is to the grave, she should be so given up to vanity, licentiousness, profaneness, and folly.\"", "\"무신론자들이며 프랑스인들과 어울려 사니까. 그 끔찍하고도 끔찍한 처지를, 무덤이 코앞에 닥쳤는데도 허영과 방종과 불경과 어리석음에 그토록 빠져 있는 꼴을 생각하면 내 마음이 다 떨려.\""], ["In fact, the old lady declined altogether to hear his hour's lecture of an evening; and when she came to Queen's Crawley alone, he was obliged to pretermit his usual devotional exercises.", "사실 노부인은 저녁마다 그가 한 시간씩 늘어놓는 설교를 듣기를 아예 거부했다. 그래서 그녀가 혼자 퀸스크롤리에 올 때면 그는 평소의 신앙 의식을 부득이 중단해야 했다."]], [["\"Shut up your sarmons, Pitt, when Miss Crawley comes down,\"", "\"크롤리 양이 내려오거든 설교일랑 집어치워라, 피트.\""], ["said his father; \"she has written to say that she won't stand the preachifying.\"", "아버지가 말했다. \"설교는 못 참겠다고 편지에 써 보냈으니 말이다.\""]], [["\"O, sir! consider the servants.\"", "\"하지만 아버지, 하인들을 생각하셔야지요.\""]], [["\"The servants be hanged,\"", "\"하인들이야 어찌 되든 알 게 뭐냐.\""], ["said Sir Pitt; and his son thought even worse would happen were they deprived of the benefit of his instruction.", "피트 경이 말했다. 아들은 만약 하인들이 자신의 가르침이라는 은혜를 박탈당한다면 그보다 더 고약한 일이 벌어지리라 생각했다."]], [["\"Why, hang it, Pitt!\"", "\"이런 제기랄, 피트!\""], ["said the father to his remonstrance.", "아들의 항변에 아버지가 말했다."], ["\"You wouldn't be such a flat as to let three thousand a year go out of the family?\"", "\"설마 연 삼천 파운드를 집안 밖으로 흘려보낼 만큼 멍청이는 아니겠지?\""]], [["\"What is money compared to our souls, sir?\"", "\"우리 영혼에 비하면 돈이 다 무엇입니까, 아버지?\""], ["continued Mr. Crawley.", "크롤리 씨가 말을 이었다."]], [["\"You mean that the old lady won't leave the money to you?\"--and who knows but it was Mr.", "\"그러니까 노부인이 너한테 그 돈을 안 남길까 봐 그러는 게냐?\""], ["Crawley's meaning?", "그런데 그것이 크롤리 씨의 본심이었는지 누가 알겠는가?"]], [["Old Miss Crawley was certainly one of the reprobate.", "크롤리 노양은 분명 타락한 무리의 하나였다."], ["She had a snug little house in Park Lane, and, as she ate and drank a great deal too much during the season in London, she went to Harrowgate or Cheltenham for the summer.", "그녀는 파크 레인에 아담하고 안락한 집을 한 채 가지고 있었는데, 런던 사교철 동안 너무 과하게 먹고 마신 탓에 여름이면 해러게이트나 첼트넘으로 떠났다."], ["She was the most hospitable and jovial of old vestals, and had been a beauty in her day, she said. (All old women were beauties once, we very well know.) She was a bel esprit, and a dreadful Radical for those days.", "그녀는 늙은 베스타 처녀들 가운데 가장 손님 대접을 잘하고 가장 쾌활한 축이었으며, 자기 말로는 한창때 미인이었다고 했다.(늙은 여인은 누구나 한때 미인이었다는 것을 우리는 익히 알고 있다.) 그녀는 재사였고, 그 시절치고는 무시무시한 급진파였다."], ["She had been in France (where St. Just, they say, inspired her with an unfortunate passion), and loved, ever after, French novels, French cookery, and French wines.", "프랑스에 다녀온 적이 있었고(그곳에서 생쥐스트가 그녀에게 불운한 연정을 불러일으켰다고들 한다), 그 후로 줄곧 프랑스 소설과 프랑스 요리와 프랑스 포도주를 사랑했다."], ["She read Voltaire, and had Rousseau by heart; talked very lightly about divorce, and most energetically of the rights of women.", "그녀는 볼테르를 읽었고 루소를 줄줄 외웠으며, 이혼에 관해서는 무척 가볍게, 여성의 권리에 관해서는 무척 열성적으로 떠들어 댔다."], ["She had pictures of Mr.", "집안 방마다 폭스 씨의 초상이 걸려 있었다."], ["Fox in every room in the house: when that statesman was in opposition, I am not sure that she had not flung a main with him; and when he came into office, she took great credit for bringing over to him Sir Pitt and his colleague for Queen's Crawley, although Sir Pitt would have come over himself, without any trouble on the honest lady's part.", "그 정치가가 야당에 있던 시절에 그녀가 그와 한판 도박을 벌이지 않았다고는 장담 못 하겠다. 그리고 그가 입각하자, 그녀는 피트 경과 그의 퀸스크롤리 동료를 그쪽으로 끌어왔노라며 큰 공을 내세웠다."], ["It is needless to say that Sir Pitt was brought to change his views after the death of the great Whig statesman.", "사실 이 정직한 부인이 수고를 들이지 않았어도 피트 경은 제 발로 넘어갔을 테지만 말이다. 그 위대한 휘그당 정치가가 죽은 뒤 피트 경이 견해를 바꾸었다는 것은 굳이 말할 필요도 없겠다."]], [["This worthy old lady took a fancy to Rawdon Crawley when a boy, sent him to Cambridge (in opposition to his brother at Oxford), and, when the young man was requested by the authorities of the first-named University to quit after a residence of two years, she bought him his commission in the Life Guards Green.", "이 갸륵한 노부인은 로던 크롤리가 소년이던 시절부터 그를 어여삐 여겨 케임브리지로 보냈고(옥스퍼드에 다니던 형에 맞서서였다), 그 대학 당국이 이 년간 머문 그를 떠나 달라고 요청하자, 녹색 근위 기병대의 장교직을 사 주었다."]], [["A perfect and celebrated \"blood,\" or dandy about town, was this young officer.", "이 젊은 장교는 완벽하고도 이름난 \"혈기 넘치는 멋쟁이\", 곧 도회의 한량이었다."], ["Boxing, rat-hunting, the fives court, and four-in-hand driving were then the fashion of our British aristocracy; and he was an adept in all these noble sciences.", "권투며 쥐 사냥이며 핸드볼 경기며 사두마차 몰이가 그 무렵 우리 영국 귀족 사회의 유행이었는데, 그는 이 모든 고귀한 기예에 통달한 명수였다."], ["And though he belonged to the household troops, who, as it was their duty to rally round the Prince Regent, had not shown their valour in foreign service yet, Rawdon Crawley had already (apropos of play, of which he was immoderately fond) fought three bloody duels, in which he gave ample proofs of his contempt for death.", "그리고 비록 그가 섭정 왕세자 주위에 결집하는 것이 본분이라 아직 외국에서의 복무로 무용을 떨치지는 못한 근위 부대 소속이었으나, 로던 크롤리는 이미(그가 분에 넘치게 좋아하던 도박과 관련하여) 피비린내 나는 결투를 세 차례나 벌여, 죽음을 두려워하지 않는다는 충분한 증거를 보여 준 터였다."]], [["\"And for what follows after death,\" would Mr.", "\"그리고 죽음 다음에 오는 것도 두려워하지 않지.\""], ["Crawley observe, throwing his gooseberry-coloured eyes up to the ceiling.", "크롤리 씨는 그렇게 말하며 구스베리빛 두 눈을 천장으로 치떴다."], ["He was always thinking of his brother's soul, or of the souls of those who differed with him in opinion: it is a sort of comfort which many of the serious give themselves.", "그는 늘 동생의 영혼을, 아니면 자기와 견해가 다른 이들의 영혼을 염려했다. 이것은 신앙심 깊은 많은 이들이 스스로에게 베푸는 일종의 위안이다."]], [["Silly, romantic Miss Crawley, far from being horrified at the courage of her favourite, always used to pay his debts after his duels; and would not listen to a word that was whispered against his morality.", "어리석고 낭만적인 크롤리 양은 자기가 아끼는 조카의 무용에 질겁하기는커녕, 결투를 치를 때마다 늘 그의 빚을 갚아 주었고, 그의 품행에 대해 수군대는 말이라곤 한마디도 들으려 하지 않았다. \"젊은 혈기야 풀고 다니는 거지.\""], ["\"He will sow his wild oats,\"", "그녀는 그렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["she would say, \"and is worth far more than that puling hypocrite of a brother of his.\"", "\"그래도 저 칭얼대는 위선자 같은 제 형보다는 훨씬 값진 아이야.\""]], [["Arcadian Simplicity\nBesides these honest folks at the Hall (whose simplicity and sweet rural purity surely show the advantage of a country life over a town one), we must introduce the reader to their relatives and neighbours at the Rectory, Bute Crawley and his wife.", "홀에 사는 이 정직한 사람들 외에도(그들의 소박함과 감미로운 시골다운 순수함이야말로 도회 생활에 비한 전원 생활의 이점을 분명히 보여 주는 것이렷다), 우리는 독자에게 목사관에 사는 그들의 친척이자 이웃인 뷰트 크롤리와 그 아내를 소개해야겠다."]], [["The Reverend Bute Crawley was a tall, stately, jolly, shovel-hatted man, far more popular in his county than the Baronet his brother.", "뷰트 크롤리 목사는 키가 크고 위풍당당하며 쾌활한, 사제용 챙 넓은 모자를 쓴 사람으로, 준남작인 형보다 이 고장에서 훨씬 더 인기가 있었다."], ["At college he pulled stroke-oar in the Christchurch boat, and had thrashed all the best bruisers of the \"town.\"", "대학 시절 그는 크라이스트처치 보트의 선두 노잡이를 맡았고, \"읍내\"의 내로라하는 권투꾼들을 죄다 두들겨 패 본 사람이었다."], ["He carried his taste for boxing and athletic exercises into private life; there was not a fight within twenty miles at which he was not present, nor a race, nor a coursing match, nor a regatta, nor a ball, nor an election, nor a visitation dinner, nor indeed a good dinner in the whole county, but he found means to attend it.", "그는 권투며 운동에 대한 취미를 사생활에까지 끌고 들어왔다. 이십 마일 안에서 벌어지는 싸움이라면 그가 빠지는 법이 없었고, 경마든 사냥개 시합이든 보트 경주든 무도회든 선거든 시찰 만찬이든, 아니 사실 온 고장의 어떤 훌륭한 만찬이든 그가 참석할 방도를 찾아내지 못하는 자리란 없었다."], ["You might see his bay mare and gig-lamps a score of miles away from his Rectory House, whenever there was any dinner-party at Fuddleston, or at Roxby, or at Wapshot Hall, or at the great lords of the county, with all of whom he was intimate.", "퍼들스턴이나 록스비, 와프숏 홀, 혹은 그가 두루 친분을 맺고 있던 고장의 대귀족들 댁에서 만찬회라도 열리는 날이면, 목사관에서 이십 마일이나 떨어진 곳에서도 그의 밤색 암말과 마차 등불을 볼 수 있었다."], ["He had a fine voice; sang \"A southerly wind and a cloudy sky\"; and gave the \"whoop\"", "그는 좋은 목소리를 지녀서 \"남풍에 흐린 하늘\"을 불렀고, 합창에서 \"와!\""], ["in chorus with general applause.", "하는 추임새를 넣어 만장의 갈채를 받았다."], ["He rode to hounds in a pepper-and-salt frock, and was one of the best fishermen in the county.", "그는 후추와 소금을 섞은 듯한 잿빛 프록코트 차림으로 사냥개를 따라 말을 달렸고, 고장에서 손꼽히는 낚시꾼이기도 했다."]], [["Mrs. Crawley, the rector's wife, was a smart little body, who wrote this worthy divine's sermons.", "목사의 아내 크롤리 부인은 영리한 자그마한 여자로, 이 훌륭한 성직자의 설교문을 대신 써 주는 사람이었다."], ["Being of a domestic turn, and keeping the house a great deal with her daughters, she ruled absolutely within the Rectory, wisely giving her husband full liberty without.", "살림에 능하고 딸들과 함께 집에서 지내는 일이 많았던 터라, 그녀는 목사관 안을 절대적으로 다스리는 한편 남편에게는 바깥에서의 완전한 자유를 현명하게 허락했다."], ["He was welcome to come and go, and dine abroad as many days as his fancy dictated, for Mrs.", "남편은 마음 내키는 대로 오가도, 또 변덕이 시키는 만큼 며칠이고 바깥에서 식사를 하고 와도 좋았다."], ["Crawley was a saving woman and knew the price of port wine. Ever since Mrs.", "크롤리 부인은 알뜰한 여자였고 포트와인 값이 얼마인지 잘 알았기 때문이다."], ["Bute carried off the young Rector of Queen's Crawley (she was of a good family, daughter of the late Lieut.-Colonel Hector McTavish, and she and her mother played for Bute and won him at Harrowgate), she had been a prudent and thrifty wife to him.", "뷰트 부인이 퀸스 크롤리의 젊은 목사를 낚아챈 이래로(그녀는 좋은 집안 출신으로, 고인이 된 헥터 맥태비시 중령의 딸이었으며, 그녀와 그 어머니가 해러게이트에서 뷰트를 걸고 한판 벌여 그를 따낸 것이었다) 그녀는 그에게 신중하고 검소한 아내가 되어 주었다."], ["In spite of her care, however, he was always in debt.", "그러나 그녀의 알뜰함에도 불구하고 그는 늘 빚에 쪼들렸다."], ["It took him at least ten years to pay off his college bills contracted during his father's lifetime.", "아버지가 살아 있을 적에 진 대학 빚을 갚는 데만도 적어도 십 년이 걸렸다."], ["In the year 179-, when he was just clear of these incumbrances, he gave the odds of 100 to 1 (in twenties) against Kangaroo, who won the Derby.", "179×년, 이 빚더미에서 막 벗어났을 때 그는 더비 경마에서 우승한 캥거루를 상대로 일대 백(이십 파운드씩)의 내기를 걸었다."], ["The Rector was obliged to take up the money at a ruinous interest, and had been struggling ever since.", "목사는 파멸적인 이자로 돈을 끌어다 써야 했고, 그 후로 줄곧 허덕여 왔다."], ["His sister helped him with a hundred now and then, but of course his great hope was in her death--when \"hang it\"", "누이가 이따금 백 파운드씩 도와주긴 했지만, 물론 그의 가장 큰 희망은 누이가 죽는 데 있었다."], ["(as he would say), \"Matilda must leave me half her money.\"", "그때가 되면 \"젠장\"(그의 말버릇대로) \"마틸다도 내게 재산의 절반은 남겨 줄 수밖에 없을 테니\" 말이다."]], [["So that the Baronet and his brother had every reason which two brothers possibly can have for being by the ears.", "그리하여 준남작과 그 아우는 두 형제가 도무지 사이가 틀어질 만한 온갖 이유를 다 갖추고 있었던 셈이다."], ["Sir Pitt had had the better of Bute in innumerable family transactions.", "핏 경은 무수한 집안 거래에서 뷰트보다 유리한 고지를 차지해 왔다."], ["Young Pitt not only did not hunt, but set up a meeting house under his uncle's very nose.", "젊은 핏은 사냥을 안 할 뿐 아니라 숙부의 코앞에 예배소를 세워 놓기까지 했다."], ["Rawdon, it was known, was to come in for the bulk of Miss Crawley's property.", "로던은, 알려진 바로는, 크롤리 양 재산의 대부분을 물려받게 되어 있었다."], ["These money transactions--these speculations in life and death--these silent battles for reversionary spoil--make brothers very loving towards each other in Vanity Fair.", "이런 돈 거래, 이런 생사를 건 투기, 상속될 전리품을 둘러싼 이런 소리 없는 다툼이야말로 허영의 시장에서 형제들을 서로 더없이 살갑게 만드는 법이다."], ["I, for my part, have known a five-pound note to interpose and knock up a half century's attachment between two brethren; and can't but admire, as I think what a fine and durable thing Love is among worldly people.", "나로 말하자면, 오 파운드짜리 지폐 한 장이 끼어들어 형제간의 반세기에 걸친 우애를 단번에 깨뜨려 버린 경우를 알고 있다. 그러니 세속의 사람들 사이에서 사랑이란 얼마나 곱고도 질긴 것인가를 떠올리면 그저 감탄하지 않을 수 없는 것이다."]], [["It cannot be supposed that the arrival of such a personage as Rebecca at Queen's Crawley, and her gradual establishment in the good graces of all people there, could be unremarked by Mrs. Bute Crawley.", "레베카 같은 인물이 퀸스 크롤리에 도착하고, 그곳 모든 사람의 환심을 차차 사들였던 일이 뷰트 크롤리 부인의 눈에 띄지 않았으리라고는 생각할 수 없다."], ["Mrs. Bute, who knew how many days the sirloin of beef lasted at the Hall; how much linen was got ready at the great wash; how many peaches were on the south wall; how many doses her ladyship took when she was ill--for such points are matters of intense interest to certain persons in the country--Mrs.", "홀에서 소 등심 한 덩이가 며칠을 가는지, 큰 빨래 때 아마포가 얼마나 마련되는지, 남쪽 담장에 복숭아가 몇 개나 열렸는지, 마님이 앓을 때 약을 몇 첩이나 드시는지까지 훤히 꿰고 있던 뷰트 부인이었으니(시골의 어떤 사람들에게는 이런 사소한 일들이 더없이 큰 관심사인 법이다), 그런 뷰트 부인이, 다시 말해, 홀에 새로 온 가정교사의 내력과 됨됨이에 관해 온갖 것을 캐묻지 않고 그냥 넘어갈 리 없었던 것이다."], ["Bute, I say, could not pass over the Hall governess without making every inquiry respecting her history and character.", "목사관과 홀의 하인들 사이에는 늘 더없이 좋은 양해가 오갔다. 목사관 부엌에는 늘 홀 사람들을 위한 좋은 맥주 한 잔이 마련되어 있었으니, 그도 그럴 것이 홀에서 평소 마시는 술은 워낙 묽었기 때문이다."], ["There was always the best understanding between the servants at the Rectory and the Hall.", "사실 목사 부인은 홀의 맥주 한 통에 맥아가 얼마나 들어가는지까지 정확히 알고 있었다."], ["There was always a good glass of ale in the kitchen of the former place for the Hall people, whose ordinary drink was very small--and, indeed, the Rector's lady knew exactly how much malt went to every barrel of Hall beer--ties of relationship existed between the Hall and Rectory domestics, as between their masters; and through these channels each family was perfectly well acquainted with the doings of the other.", "주인들 사이에 친척의 연이 닿아 있듯, 홀과 목사관의 하인들 사이에도 그러한 연이 얽혀 있었다. 그리고 이런 통로를 거쳐 양가는 서로의 동정을 완벽하게 알고 지냈다."], ["That, by the way, may be set down as a general remark.", "덧붙여 말하건대, 이것은 일반적인 진리로 적어 둘 만하다."], ["When you and your brother are friends, his doings are indifferent to you.", "당신과 당신 형제가 사이가 좋을 때는 그가 무엇을 하든 당신은 무심하다."], ["When you have quarrelled, all his outgoings and incomings you know, as if you were his spy.", "그러나 다투고 나면 그가 드나드는 일거수일투족을, 마치 당신이 그의 첩자라도 된 양 죄다 알게 되는 것이다."]], [["Very soon then after her arrival, Rebecca began to take a regular place in Mrs.", "그리하여 레베카가 도착하고 얼마 지나지 않아, 홀에서 크롤리 부인에게 올라오는 소식 보고에는 그녀가 으레 한자리를 차지하게 되었다."], ["Crawley's bulletin from the Hall.", "그 보고란 이런 식이었다."], ["It was to this effect: \"The black porker's killed--weighed x stone--salted the sides--pig's pudding and leg of pork for dinner.", "\"검은 돼지를 잡았는데 무게가 몇 스톤이고, 옆구리 살은 소금에 절였으며, 저녁상에는 돼지 순대와 다리 고기가 올랐다. 머드베리의 크램프 씨가 존 블랙모어를 감옥에 처넣는 문제로 핏 경을 찾아왔다."], ["Mr. Cramp from Mudbury, over with Sir Pitt about putting John Blackmore in gaol--Mr.", "핏 씨는 예배소에 갔다(참석한 사람들의 이름을 죄다 곁들여서)."], ["Pitt at meeting (with all the names of the people who attended)--my lady as usual--the young ladies with the governess.\"", "마님은 여느 때와 다름없으시다. 아가씨들은 가정교사와 함께 있다.\""]], [["Then the report would come--the new governess be a rare manager--Sir Pitt be very sweet on her--Mr.", "이윽고 이런 보고가 들어왔다. 새 가정교사는 보기 드문 살림꾼이라더라, 핏 경이 그녀에게 무척 살갑게 굴더라, 크롤리 씨도 그렇더라, 그녀에게 종교 소책자를 읽어 주더라."], ["Crawley too--He be reading tracts to her--\"What an abandoned wretch!\"", "\"저런 막돼먹은 여편네 같으니라고!\""], ["said little, eager, active, black-faced Mrs. Bute Crawley.", "자그맣고 성마르며 부산스러운, 거무튀튀한 얼굴의 뷰트 크롤리 부인이 말했다."]], [["Finally, the reports were that the governess had \"come round\"", "마침내 들어온 보고에 따르면, 그 가정교사가 모든 이를 \"구워삶아\""], ["everybody, wrote Sir Pitt's letters, did his business, managed his accounts--had the upper hand of the whole house, my lady, Mr. Crawley, the girls and all--at which Mrs.", "핏 경의 편지를 대신 쓰고, 그의 사무를 처리하고, 그의 회계를 관리하더라는 것이었다."], ["Crawley declared she was an artful hussy, and had some dreadful designs in view.", "마님이며 크롤리 씨며 딸들이며 할 것 없이 온 집안을 손아귀에 쥐고 있다는 것이었다."], ["Thus the doings at the Hall were the great food for conversation at the Rectory, and Mrs.", "이에 크롤리 부인은 그녀가 교활한 계집이며 뭔가 무시무시한 속셈을 품고 있는 게 분명하다고 단언했다."], ["Bute's bright eyes spied out everything that took place in the enemy's camp--everything and a great deal besides.", "이렇듯 홀에서 벌어지는 일들은 목사관에서 더없이 좋은 이야깃거리가 되었고, 뷰트 부인의 반짝이는 두 눈은 적진에서 일어나는 모든 일을 염탐해 냈다. 모든 일을, 그리고 그 밖의 숱한 일까지 말이다."]], [["Mrs. Bute Crawley to Miss Pinkerton, The Mall, Chiswick.", "뷰트 크롤리 부인이 치즈윅 거리의 핑커튼 여사에게."]], [["Rectory, Queen's Crawley, December--.", "퀸스 크롤리 목사관에서, 십이월—."]], [["My Dear Madam,--Although it is so many years since I profited by your delightful and invaluable instructions, yet I have ever retained the FONDEST and most reverential regard for Miss Pinkerton, and DEAR Chiswick.", "친애하는 부인께. 부인의 그 즐겁고도 더없이 값진 가르침을 받은 지 어느덧 여러 해가 흘렀지만, 저는 핑커튼 여사님과 그리운 치즈윅을 향해 늘 더없이 깊고 더없이 경건한 애정을 간직해 왔습니다."], ["I hope your health is GOOD.", "부디 건강하시기를 바랍니다."], ["The world and the cause of education cannot afford to lose Miss Pinkerton for MANY MANY YEARS.", "세상도, 교육이라는 대의도 핑커튼 여사님을 오래오래 잃어서는 안 될 것입니다."], ["When my friend, Lady Fuddleston, mentioned that her dear girls required an instructress (I am too poor to engage a governess for mine, but was I not educated at Chiswick?)--\"Who,\"", "제 벗인 퍼들스턴 부인이 그 사랑스러운 딸들에게 여교사가 필요하다고 말했을 때(저는 너무 가난해서 제 딸들을 위해서는 가정교사를 둘 수 없으나, 저 자신이 치즈윅에서 교육받지 않았던가요?), 저는 외쳤습니다."], ["I exclaimed, \"can we consult but the excellent, the incomparable Miss Pinkerton?\"", "\"그 탁월하시고 비길 데 없는 핑커튼 여사님 말고 누구와 의논하겠어요?\""], ["In a word, have you, dear madam, any ladies on your list, whose services might be made available to my kind friend and neighbour?", "한마디로, 친애하는 부인께서 명단에 올려 두신 숙녀들 가운데, 저의 친절한 벗이자 이웃에게 그 봉사를 보내 줄 만한 분이 혹 있으신지요?"], ["I assure you she will take no governess BUT OF YOUR CHOOSING.", "분명히 말씀드리지만, 그 부인은 오직 부인께서 골라 주시는 가정교사만을 받아들일 것입니다."]], [["My dear husband is pleased to say that he likes EVERYTHING WHICH COMES FROM MISS PINKERTON'S SCHOOL.", "제 사랑하는 남편은 핑커튼 여사님의 학원에서 나오는 것이라면 무엇이든 마음에 든다고 흔쾌히 말합니다."], ["How I wish I could present him and my beloved girls to the friend of my youth, and the ADMIRED of the great lexicographer of our country!", "그이와 제 사랑하는 딸들을, 제 젊은 날의 벗이자 우리나라 저 위대한 사전 편찬자께서 흠모하시는 그분께 소개할 수 있다면 얼마나 좋을까요!"], ["If you ever travel into Hampshire, Mr. Crawley begs me to say, he hopes you will adorn our RURAL RECTORY with your presence.", "행여 햄프셔로 여행하시는 일이 있거든, 부디 부인의 왕림으로 저희의 시골 목사관을 빛내 주시기를 바란다고, 크롤리 씨가 제게 꼭 전해 달라 합니다."], ["'Tis the humble but happy home of", "비록 누추하나 행복한 보금자리인 이곳을 말입니다."]], [["Your affectionate Martha Crawley", "부인을 사랑하는 마사 크롤리 올림."]], [["P.S.", "추신."], ["Mr. Crawley's brother, the baronet, with whom we are not, alas!", "크롤리 씨의 형님이자 준남작 되시는 분과 저희는, 아아, 형제가 마땅히 누려야 할 그런 화목한 사이가 못 됩니다만, 그분이 어린 딸들을 위해 가정교사를 두고 있는데, 듣자 하니 그 여자가 다행히도 치즈윅에서 교육받은 행운을 누렸다 합니다."], ["upon those terms of UNITY in which it BECOMES BRETHREN TO DWELL, has a governess for his little girls, who, I am told, had the good fortune to be educated at Chiswick.", "그녀에 관해 이런저런 소문이 들려옵니다. 그런데 저는 제 더없이 사랑스러운 어린 조카딸들에게 더없이 애틋한 관심을 품고 있어, 집안 간의 불화에도 불구하고 그 아이들을 제 자식들 틈에 보고 싶을 뿐 아니라, 부인의 제자라면 누구에게든 마음을 쓰고 싶은 터이니, 친애하는 핑커튼 여사님, 부디 이 젊은 숙녀의 내력을 좀 알려 주십시오."], ["I hear various reports of her; and as I have the tenderest interest in my dearest little nieces, whom I wish, in spite of family differences, to see among my own children--and as I long to be attentive to ANY PUPIL OF YOURS--do, my dear Miss Pinkerton, tell me the history of this young lady, whom, for YOUR SAKE, I am most anxious to befriend.--M.", "부인을 봐서라도 저는 그녀와 벗이 되어 주고 싶은 마음이 간절합니다. ㅡM."], ["C.", "C."]], [["Miss Pinkerton to Mrs. Bute Crawley.", "핑커튼 여사가 뷰트 크롤리 부인에게."]], [["Johnson House, Chiswick, Dec. 18--.", "치즈윅, 존슨관에서, 십이월 18××년."]], [["Dear Madam,--I have the honour to acknowledge your polite communication, to which I promptly reply.", "친애하는 부인께. 부인의 정중한 서신을 받게 되어 영광이며, 이에 곧바로 답신을 올립니다."], ["'Tis most gratifying to one in my most arduous position to find that my maternal cares have elicited a responsive affection; and to recognize in the amiable Mrs. Bute Crawley my excellent pupil of former years, the sprightly and accomplished Miss Martha MacTavish.", "저처럼 더없이 고된 자리에 있는 사람으로서, 저의 어머니 같은 보살핌이 화답하는 애정을 이끌어 냈음을 알게 되고, 또 저 상냥한 뷰트 크롤리 부인에게서 지난날 저의 빼어난 제자, 명랑하고 재주 많던 마사 맥태비시 양을 알아보게 되니 더없이 흐뭇합니다."], ["I am happy to have under my charge now the daughters of many of those who were your contemporaries at my establishment--what pleasure it would give me if your own beloved young ladies had need of my instructive superintendence!", "지금 제 보살핌 아래에는 본원에서 부인과 동기였던 많은 분들의 따님들이 있으니, 만약 부인의 사랑스러운 따님들이 저의 가르치는 보살핌을 필요로 한다면 제게 얼마나 큰 기쁨이 될까요!"]], [["Presenting my respectful compliments to Lady Fuddleston, I have the honour (epistolarily) to introduce to her ladyship my two friends, Miss Tuffin and Miss Hawky.", "퍼들스턴 부인께 정중한 인사를 전하며, 저는 (서신으로나마) 부인께 저의 두 벗, 터핀 양과 호키 양을 소개하는 영광을 누립니다."]], [["Either of these young ladies is PERFECTLY QUALIFIED to instruct in Greek, Latin, and the rudiments of Hebrew; in mathematics and history; in Spanish, French, Italian, and geography; in music, vocal and instrumental; in dancing, without the aid of a master; and in the elements of natural sciences.", "이 두 젊은 숙녀는 그리스어, 라틴어, 히브리어 기초를, 수학과 역사를, 스페인어, 프랑스어, 이탈리아어와 지리를, 성악과 기악을, 선생의 도움 없이 무용을, 그리고 자연과학의 기초를 가르치기에 완벽하게 자격을 갖추고 있습니다."], ["In the use of the globes both are proficients.", "지구의 사용에서도 둘 다 능숙합니다."], ["In addition to these Miss Tuffin, who is daughter of the late Reverend Thomas Tuffin (Fellow of Corpus College, Cambridge), can instruct in the Syriac language, and the elements of Constitutional law.", "이에 더하여 터핀 양은, 고인이 된 토머스 터핀 목사(케임브리지 코퍼스 칼리지의 특별연구원)의 따님으로서, 시리아어와 헌법학 기초까지 가르칠 수 있습니다."], ["But as she is only eighteen years of age, and of exceedingly pleasing personal appearance, perhaps this young lady may be objectionable in Sir Huddleston Fuddleston's family.", "다만 이 젊은 숙녀는 이제 겨우 열여덟 살이고 용모가 대단히 빼어난지라, 어쩌면 허들스턴 퍼들스턴 경의 가정에는 다소 거슬릴는지도 모르겠습니다."]], [["Miss Letitia Hawky, on the other hand, is not personally well-favoured.", "반면에 러티시아 호키 양은 용모가 빼어나지 못합니다."], ["She is twenty-nine; her face is much pitted with the small-pox.", "나이는 스물아홉이며, 얼굴은 천연두 자국으로 몹시 얽었습니다."], ["She has a halt in her gait, red hair, and a trifling obliquity of vision.", "걸음걸이에 절뚝거림이 있고, 머리는 붉으며, 사시기가 약간 있습니다."], ["Both ladies are endowed with EVERY MORAL AND RELIGIOUS VIRTUE.", "두 숙녀 모두 온갖 도덕적, 종교적 미덕을 갖추고 있습니다."], ["Their terms, of course, are such as their accomplishments merit.", "그들의 보수는 물론 그 재능에 걸맞은 수준입니다."], ["With my most grateful respects to the Reverend Bute Crawley, I have the honour to be,", "뷰트 크롤리 목사님께 더없이 감사한 경의를 전하며, 영광스럽게 인사 올립니다."]], [["Dear Madam,", "친애하는 부인께,"]], [["Your most faithful and obedient servant, Barbara Pinkerton.", "부인의 더없이 충실하고 순종하는 종복 바버라 핑커튼 올림."]], [["P.S.", "추신."], ["The Miss Sharp, whom you mention as governess to Sir Pitt Crawley, Bart., M.P., was a pupil of mine, and I have nothing to say in her disfavour.", "부인께서 준남작이자 하원 의원이신 핏 크롤리 경의 가정교사로 언급하신 샤프 양은 저의 제자였으며, 그녀에게 불리한 말은 저로서는 한마디도 할 게 없습니다."], ["Though her appearance is disagreeable, we cannot control the operations of nature: and though her parents were disreputable (her father being a painter, several times bankrupt, and her mother, as I have since learned, with horror, a dancer at the Opera); yet her talents are considerable, and I cannot regret that I received her OUT OF CHARITY.", "비록 그 외양이 호감을 주지는 못하나, 우리가 자연의 조화를 어찌할 수는 없는 노릇이지요. 또한 그녀의 부모가 변변치 못했으나(그 아버지는 화가로 여러 차례 파산했고, 그 어머니는 제가 나중에 소름 끼치는 심정으로 알게 된 바로는 오페라극장의 무희였습니다), 그래도 그녀의 재능만은 상당하니, 제가 자선을 베풀어 그녀를 거두어들인 일을 후회할 수는 없습니다."], ["My dread is, lest the principles of the mother--who was represented to me as a French Countess, forced to emigrate in the late revolutionary horrors; but who, as I have since found, was a person of the very lowest order and morals--should at any time prove to be HEREDITARY in the unhappy young woman whom I took as AN OUTCAST.", "다만 제가 두려워하는 것은, 그 어미의 성향이 ㅡ제게는 그녀가 지난날 혁명의 참화 속에 어쩔 수 없이 망명한 프랑스 백작 부인이라 소개되었으나, 나중에 알고 보니 그녀는 더없이 천한 신분과 품행의 인물이었습니다ㅡ 제가 버려진 아이로 거두었던 그 가엾은 젊은 여자에게서 언젠가 유전으로 드러나지나 않을까 하는 것입니다."], ["But her principles have hitherto been correct (I believe), and I am sure nothing will occur to injure them in the elegant and refined circle of the eminent Sir Pitt Crawley.", "그러나 지금까지 그녀의 성향은 (제가 믿기로) 올발랐으며, 저 저명하신 핏 크롤리 경의 우아하고 세련된 교제권 안에서 그것이 해를 입을 일은 결코 없으리라 확신합니다."]], [["Miss Rebecca Sharp to Miss Amelia Sedley.", "레베카 샤프 양이 애밀리아 세들리 양에게."]], [["I have not written to my beloved Amelia for these many weeks past, for what news was there to tell of the sayings and doings at Humdrum Hall, as I have christened it; and what do you care whether the turnip crop is good or bad; whether the fat pig weighed thirteen stone or fourteen; and whether the beasts thrive well upon mangelwurzel?", "지난 여러 주 동안 나의 사랑하는 애밀리아에게 편지를 쓰지 못했어. 내가 따분한 홀이라 이름 붙인 이곳에서 벌어지는 일이며 오가는 말 가운데 전할 만한 소식이 뭐가 있었겠니."], ["Every day since I last wrote has been like its neighbour.", "또 순무 농사가 잘됐는지 못됐는지, 살진 돼지가 열세 스톤이나 나갔는지 열네 스톤이나 나갔는지, 짐승들이 사료용 무를 잘 먹고 토실토실해지는지 따위가 네게 무슨 상관이 있겠어?"], ["Before breakfast, a walk with Sir Pitt and his spud; after breakfast studies (such as they are) in the schoolroom; after schoolroom, reading and writing about lawyers, leases, coal-mines, canals, with Sir Pitt (whose secretary I am become); after dinner, Mr.", "지난번 편지를 쓴 이래로 하루하루가 그저 그 옆날과 다를 바 없었단다. 아침을 먹기 전에는 핏 경과 그의 잡초 뽑는 괭이를 따라 산책을 하고, 아침을 먹은 뒤에는 교실에서 공부랍시고 (그게 공부라면 말이지만) 하고, 교실 일이 끝나면 핏 경과 함께 변호사, 임대차, 탄광, 운하에 관한 글을 읽고 쓰는 거야(나는 그분의 비서가 되어 버렸거든)."], ["Crawley's discourses on the baronet's backgammon; during both of which amusements my lady looks on with equal placidity.", "저녁을 먹은 뒤에는 준남작의 주사위 놀이를 두고 크롤리 씨가 일장 연설을 늘어놓고. 그 두 가지 소일거리 내내 마님은 한결같이 평온한 얼굴로 지켜보고 계시지."], ["She has become rather more interesting by being ailing of late, which has brought a new visitor to the Hall, in the person of a young doctor.", "마님은 근래 들어 몸이 편찮으셔서 다소 흥미로운 분이 되셨는데, 그 덕에 젊은 의사라는 새 손님이 홀을 드나들게 되었단다. 자, 얘야, 젊은 여자란 결코 절망할 필요가 없는 법이야."], ["Well, my dear, young women need never despair. The young doctor gave a certain friend of yours to understand that, if she chose to be Mrs.", "그 젊은 의사가 너의 어떤 벗에게, 만약 그녀가 글라우버 부인이 되기로 마음먹기만 한다면 기꺼이 그 진료소를 빛내 주어도 좋다는 뜻을 넌지시 비치지 뭐니!"], ["Glauber, she was welcome to ornament the surgery!", "나는 그 뻔뻔한 양반에게, 금박을 입힌 절구공이와 절구만으로도 장식이라면 차고 넘친다고 대꾸해 주었지."], ["I told his impudence that the gilt pestle and mortar was quite ornament enough; as if I was born, indeed, to be a country surgeon's wife!", "내가 무슨 시골 의사의 아내가 되려고 태어나기라도 한 듯이 말이야! 글라우버 씨는 그 퇴짜에 단단히 풀이 죽어 집으로 돌아갔고, 열을 내리는 약을 들이켰으며, 이제는 말끔히 나았단다."], ["Mr. Glauber went home seriously indisposed at his rebuff, took a cooling draught, and is now quite cured.", "핏 경은 내 결단을 크게 칭찬하셨어."], ["Sir Pitt applauded my resolution highly; he would be sorry to lose his little secretary, I think; and I believe the old wretch likes me as much as it is in his nature to like any one.", "그분은 당신의 작은 비서를 잃기가 아쉬우신 거겠지. 그리고 내 생각엔 그 늙은 양반이, 그의 천성으로 누군가를 좋아할 수 있는 만큼은 나를 좋아하시는 것 같아."], ["Marry, indeed!", "결혼이라니, 흥!"], ["and with a country apothecary, after-- No, no, one cannot so soon forget old associations, about which I will talk no more.", "게다가 시골 약제사하고, 그것도 ㅡ 아니, 아니, 사람이란 옛정을 그리 쉽게 잊을 수 있는 게 아니지. 그 이야기는 더 하지 않으련다."], ["Let us return to Humdrum Hall.", "따분한 홀로 되돌아가자꾸나."]], [["For some time past it is Humdrum Hall no longer.", "얼마 전부터 이곳은 더 이상 따분한 홀이 아니란다."], ["My dear, Miss Crawley has arrived with her fat horses, fat servants, fat spaniel--the great rich Miss Crawley, with seventy thousand pounds in the five per cents., whom, or I had better say WHICH, her two brothers adore.", "얘야, 크롤리 양이 살진 말이며 살진 하인들이며 살진 스패니얼을 거느리고 도착하셨거든. 오 퍼센트 공채로 칠만 파운드를 가진 그 어마어마한 부자 크롤리 양 말이야."], ["She looks very apoplectic, the dear soul; no wonder her brothers are anxious about her.", "두 형제가 그분을, 아니 그 재산을 떠받든다고 하는 편이 낫겠구나. 그 어른은 무척 졸도하실 것처럼 보이는데, 가엾기도 하시지."], ["You should see them struggling to settle her cushions, or to hand her coffee!", "두 형제가 그분 걱정에 안달하는 것도 무리는 아니야. 두 분이 그분의 방석을 매만지고 커피를 받쳐 올리려 다투는 꼴을 너도 봤어야 하는 건데!"], ["\"When I come into the country,\"", "\"내가 시골에 올 때면\""], ["she says (for she has a great deal of humour), \"I leave my toady, Miss Briggs, at home.", "그분이 말씀하시지(워낙 익살이 넘치는 분이거든). \"내 아첨꾼 브리그스 양은 집에 두고 온단다."], ["My brothers are my toadies here, my dear, and a pretty pair they are!\"", "여기서는 내 두 형제가 내 아첨꾼이지, 얘야. 어찌나 잘 어울리는 한 쌍인지!\""]], [["When she comes into the country our hall is thrown open, and for a month, at least, you would fancy old Sir Walpole was come to life again.", "그분이 시골에 오시면 우리 홀은 활짝 문을 열고, 적어도 한 달 동안은 늙은 월폴 경이 되살아나기라도 한 게 아닌가 싶을 정도란다."], ["We have dinner-parties, and drive out in the coach-and-four--the footmen put on their newest canary-coloured liveries; we drink claret and champagne as if we were accustomed to it every day.", "만찬회를 열고, 사두마차를 타고 나들이를 하지. 하인들은 가장 새것인 카나리아색 제복을 차려입고, 우리는 마치 날마다 그래 온 사람들처럼 클라레와 샴페인을 마신단다."], ["We have wax candles in the schoolroom, and fires to warm ourselves with.", "교실에는 밀랍 양초가 켜지고, 몸을 녹일 난롯불도 지펴지지."], ["Lady Crawley is made to put on the brightest pea-green in her wardrobe, and my pupils leave off their thick shoes and tight old tartan pelisses, and wear silk stockings and muslin frocks, as fashionable baronets'", "크롤리 부인은 옷장에서 가장 화사한 완두콩빛 초록 드레스를 꺼내 입게 되고, 내 학생들은 두꺼운 신발과 꽉 끼는 낡은 격자무늬 외투를 벗어 던지고, 유행하는 준남작의 딸들답게 비단 양말과 모슬린 드레스를 입는단다."], ["daughters should.", "어제 로즈가 가엾은 꼴로 들어왔어."], ["Rose came in yesterday in a sad plight--the Wiltshire sow (an enormous pet of hers) ran her down, and destroyed a most lovely flowered lilac silk dress by dancing over it--had this happened a week ago, Sir Pitt would have sworn frightfully, have boxed the poor wretch's ears, and put her upon bread and water for a month.", "윌트셔 암퇘지가(그 애가 끔찍이도 아끼는 놈이지) 그 애를 들이받고는 더없이 사랑스러운 라일락빛 꽃무늬 비단 드레스 위를 마구 짓밟아 망쳐 놓았거든. 이런 일이 일주일 전에 일어났더라면 핏 경은 무시무시하게 욕을 퍼붓고 그 가엾은 것의 따귀를 올려붙인 다음 한 달 동안 빵과 물만 먹게 했을 거야."], ["All he said was, \"I'll serve you out, Miss, when your aunt's gone,\"", "그런데 그분은 고작 이렇게 말씀하셨을 뿐이야. \"이놈아, 네 고모 떠나고 나면 두고 보자.\""], ["and laughed off the accident as quite trivial.", "그러고는 그 사고를 아주 사소한 일인 양 웃어넘기셨지."], ["Let us hope his wrath will have passed away before Miss Crawley's departure.", "크롤리 양이 떠나기 전에 그분의 노여움이 가라앉기를 바라야겠어."], ["I hope so, for Miss Rose's sake, I am sure.", "정말이지 로즈 양을 위해서 그러기를 바란다."], ["What a charming reconciler and peacemaker money is!", "돈이란 얼마나 멋진 화해자요 중재자인지!"]], [["Another admirable effect of Miss Crawley and her seventy thousand pounds is to be seen in the conduct of the two brothers Crawley.", "크롤리 양과 그 칠만 파운드가 빚어내는 또 하나의 놀라운 효과는 두 크롤리 형제의 처신에서 볼 수 있단다."], ["I mean the baronet and the rector, not OUR brothers--but the former, who hate each other all the year round, become quite loving at Christmas.", "준남작과 목사 말이야. 우리 형제가 아니라ㅡ아무튼 일 년 내내 서로 미워하던 그 두 사람이 크리스마스만 되면 아주 살가워지지 뭐니."], ["I wrote to you last year how the abominable horse-racing rector was in the habit of preaching clumsy sermons at us at church, and how Sir Pitt snored in answer.", "그 가증스러운 경마꾼 목사가 교회에서 우리에게 어설픈 설교를 늘어놓곤 하고, 그러면 핏 경이 화답으로 코를 골곤 하던 일을 작년에 네게 써 보냈었지. 그런데 크롤리 양이 오시면 다투는 소리란 도무지 들리지 않는단다."], ["When Miss Crawley arrives there is no such thing as quarrelling heard of--the Hall visits the Rectory, and vice versa--the parson and the Baronet talk about the pigs and the poachers, and the county business, in the most affable manner, and without quarrelling in their cups, I believe--indeed Miss Crawley won't hear of their quarrelling, and vows that she will leave her money to the Shropshire Crawleys if they offend her.", "홀이 목사관을 찾고 그 반대도 마찬가지고, 목사와 준남작은 돼지며 밀렵꾼이며 고장 일에 관해 더없이 정답게 이야기를 나누고, 술기운에 다투는 법도 없는 듯해. 사실 크롤리 양은 두 분이 다투는 꼴을 못 견뎌 하시고, 자기 비위를 거스르면 재산을 슈롭셔 크롤리 가에 남기겠다고 으름장을 놓으시거든."], ["If they were clever people, those Shropshire Crawleys, they might have it all, I think; but the Shropshire Crawley is a clergyman like his Hampshire cousin, and mortally offended Miss Crawley (who had fled thither in a fit of rage against her impracticable brethren) by some strait-laced notions of morality.", "만약 그 슈롭셔 크롤리 가가 영리한 사람들이라면 그 재산을 죄다 차지할 수도 있을 텐데. 하지만 슈롭셔 크롤리도 그 햄프셔 사촌과 마찬가지로 성직자여서, 도덕에 관한 어떤 고지식한 생각들로 크롤리 양의 비위를 단단히 거슬러 놓았다는구나(그분은 다루기 힘든 형제들에게 화가 치밀어 그리로 도망쳐 갔던 거였지)."], ["He would have prayers in the house, I believe.", "내가 듣기로 그 사람은 집 안에서 기도를 올리려 했다나 봐."]], [["Our sermon books are shut up when Miss Crawley arrives, and Mr.", "크롤리 양이 오시면 우리 설교집은 덮여 버리고, 그분이 질색하는 핏 씨는 슬그머니 런던으로 가 버리는 게 편리하다고 여기지."], ["Pitt, whom she abominates, finds it convenient to go to town. On the other hand, the young dandy--\"blood,\"", "그 대신에 젊은 멋쟁이가, 아마 \"혈기 왕성한 한량\"이라고들 하는 것 같은데, 그 크롤리 대위가 나타난단다."], ["I believe, is the term--Captain Crawley makes his appearance, and I suppose you will like to know what sort of a person he is.", "그가 어떤 사람인지 너도 궁금할 테지."]], [["Well, he is a very large young dandy.", "자, 그는 무척 덩치 큰 젊은 멋쟁이야. 키는 육 피트에 목소리가 우렁차고, 욕을 입에 달고 살며, 하인들을 마구 부려 먹는단다."], ["He is six feet high, and speaks with a great voice; and swears a great deal; and orders about the servants, who all adore him nevertheless; for he is very generous of his money, and the domestics will do anything for him.", "그런데도 하인들은 다들 그를 떠받들지. 돈을 후하게 쓰는 사람이라, 하인들은 그를 위해서라면 무엇이든 하거든."], ["Last week the keepers almost killed a bailiff and his man who came down from London to arrest the Captain, and who were found lurking about the Park wall--they beat them, ducked them, and were going to shoot them for poachers, but the baronet interfered.", "지난주에는 그 대위를 체포하러 런던에서 내려온 집행관과 그 수하가 공원 담장 근처를 어슬렁거리다 발각됐는데, 사냥터지기들이 그들을 거의 죽일 뻔했단다. 그들을 두들겨 패고 물에 처넣고는 밀렵꾼이라며 총으로 쏘아 버리려 했는데, 준남작이 끼어들어 말렸지."]], [["The Captain has a hearty contempt for his father, I can see, and calls him an old PUT, an old SNOB, an old CHAW-BACON, and numberless other pretty names.", "그 대위는 자기 아버지를 진심으로 경멸하는 게 내 눈에도 보여. 그를 늙다리, 늙은 속물, 늙은 촌뜨기, 그 밖에 무수히 듣기 좋은 이름으로 부르거든."], ["He has a DREADFUL REPUTATION among the ladies.", "그는 숙녀들 사이에서 무시무시한 평판을 얻고 있어."], ["He brings his hunters home with him, lives with the Squires of the county, asks whom he pleases to dinner, and Sir Pitt dares not say no, for fear of offending Miss Crawley, and missing his legacy when she dies of her apoplexy.", "자기 사냥말들을 끌고 와서는 고장의 향사들과 어울려 지내고, 제멋대로 아무나 저녁 식사에 부르는데, 핏 경은 감히 안 된다는 말을 못 해. 크롤리 양의 비위를 거슬렀다가 그분이 졸도로 돌아가실 때 유산을 놓치게 될까 봐 두려운 거지."], ["Shall I tell you a compliment the Captain paid me?", "그 대위가 내게 건넨 찬사 하나를 너에게 들려줄까?"], ["I must, it is so pretty.", "들려줘야겠어. 어찌나 듣기 좋던지."], ["One evening we actually had a dance; there was Sir Huddleston Fuddleston and his family, Sir Giles Wapshot and his young ladies, and I don't know how many more.", "어느 저녁 우리가 정말로 춤판을 벌였는데, 허들스턴 퍼들스턴 경과 그 식구들, 자일스 와프숏 경과 그 딸들, 그리고 몇 사람이나 더 왔는지 모르겠어."], ["Well, I heard him say--\"By Jove, she's a neat little filly!\"", "그런데 그가 이렇게 말하는 걸 들었지 뭐니. \"이런, 참 맵시 있는 망아지로구먼!\""], ["meaning your humble servant; and he did me the honour to dance two country-dances with me.", "다름 아닌 너의 변변찮은 벗을 두고 한 말이었어. 그러고는 영광스럽게도 나와 컨트리댄스를 두 곡이나 추어 주었단다."], ["He gets on pretty gaily with the young Squires, with whom he drinks, bets, rides, and talks about hunting and shooting; but he says the country girls are BORES; indeed, I don't think he is far wrong.", "그는 함께 술 마시고 내기하고 말을 달리고 사냥이며 사격 이야기를 나누는 젊은 향사들과는 꽤 즐겁게 어울리지만, 시골 처녀들은 따분하다고 한단다. 사실 나도 그 말이 그리 틀렸다고는 보지 않아."], ["You should see the contempt with which they look down on poor me!", "그 처녀들이 가엾은 나를 얼마나 깔보는 눈으로 내려다보는지 너도 봤어야 하는 건데!"], ["When they dance I sit and play the piano very demurely; but the other night, coming in rather flushed from the dining-room, and seeing me employed in this way, he swore out loud that I was the best dancer in the room, and took a great oath that he would have the fiddlers from Mudbury.", "그들이 춤출 때 나는 한쪽에 앉아 얌전히 피아노를 친단다. 그런데 며칠 전 밤에 그가 식당에서 다소 불콰해진 얼굴로 들어오다가 내가 그렇게 피아노 치는 모습을 보고는, 내가 그 방에서 가장 춤을 잘 추는 사람이라고 큰 소리로 떠들어 댔고, 머드베리에서 바이올린 악사들을 불러오겠다고 큰소리쳤단다."]], [["\"I'll go and play a country-dance,\" said Mrs.", "\"내가 가서 컨트리댄스 곡을 쳐 주마.\""], ["Bute Crawley, very readily (she is a little, black-faced old woman in a turban, rather crooked, and with very twinkling eyes); and after the Captain and your poor little Rebecca had performed a dance together, do you know she actually did me the honour to compliment me upon my steps!", "뷰트 크롤리 부인이 선뜻 나서며 말했어(그 부인은 거무튀튀한 얼굴에 터번을 쓴 자그마한 노부인인데, 허리가 좀 굽었고 두 눈은 무척 반짝거리지). 그러고는 그 대위와 너의 가엾은 작은 레베카가 함께 춤을 추고 나자, 글쎄, 그분이 정말로 영광스럽게도 내 스텝을 칭찬해 주지 뭐니!"], ["Such a thing was never heard of before; the proud Mrs.", "이런 일은 일찍이 들어 본 적이 없어."], ["Bute Crawley, first cousin to the Earl of Tiptoff, who won't condescend to visit Lady Crawley, except when her sister is in the country.", "티프토프 백작의 사촌 누이이면서, 자기 언니가 시골에 와 있을 때가 아니면 크롤리 부인을 찾아보는 일조차 황송해 마지않는 그 콧대 높은 뷰트 크롤리 부인이 말이야."], ["Poor Lady Crawley!", "가엾은 크롤리 부인!"], ["during most part of these gaieties, she is upstairs taking pills.", "이 흥겨운 자리 내내 그분은 거의 위층에서 알약이나 삼키고 계신단다."]], [["Mrs. Bute has all of a sudden taken a great fancy to me.", "뷰트 부인은 별안간 내게 푹 빠지셨어."], ["\"My dear Miss Sharp,\"", "\"친애하는 샤프 양.\" 그분이 말씀하시지."], ["she says, \"why not bring over your girls to the Rectory?--their cousins will be so happy to see them.\"", "\"학생들을 목사관으로 데려오는 게 어때요? 사촌들이 그 애들을 보면 무척 기뻐할 텐데.\""], ["I know what she means.", "나는 그분의 속셈을 안단다."], ["Signor Clementi did not teach us the piano for nothing; at which price Mrs.", "클레멘티 선생이 공짜로 우리에게 피아노를 가르쳐 준 게 아니었으니, 그분은 그 비용으로 제 자식들을 위한 교사를 얻으려는 거지."], ["Bute hopes to get a professor for her children.", "그 속셈을, 마치 그분이 내게 직접 털어놓기라도 한 듯 훤히 꿰뚫어 보고 있어. 하지만 나는 가야겠어."], ["I can see through her schemes, as though she told them to me; but I shall go, as I am determined to make myself agreeable--is it not a poor governess's duty, who has not a friend or protector in the world?", "상냥하게 처신하기로 마음먹었거든. 세상에 벗 하나, 보호자 하나 없는 가엾은 가정교사의 의무가 아니겠니?"], ["The Rector's wife paid me a score of compliments about the progress my pupils made, and thought, no doubt, to touch my heart--poor, simple, country soul!--as if I cared a fig about my pupils!", "목사 부인은 내 학생들이 거둔 진보에 관해 수십 번이나 칭찬을 늘어놓으며, 분명 내 마음을 사로잡을 수 있으리라 여겼겠지ㅡ가엾고 순진한 시골 사람 같으니!ㅡ내가 마치 내 학생들을 손톱만큼이라도 마음에 두고 있기라도 한 듯이 말이야!"]], [["Your India muslin and your pink silk, dearest Amelia, are said to become me very well.", "가장 사랑하는 애밀리아, 너의 인도산 모슬린과 분홍빛 비단이 내게 무척 잘 어울린다더라."], ["They are a good deal worn now; but, you know, we poor girls can't afford des fraiches toilettes.", "이젠 꽤 낡았지만, 너도 알다시피 우리 같은 가난한 처녀들은 새 옷을 마련할 형편이 못 되잖니."], ["Happy, happy you!", "행복한, 행복한 너!"], ["who have but to drive to St. James's Street, and a dear mother who will give you any thing you ask.", "너는 그저 세인트제임스 거리로 마차를 몰고 가기만 하면 되고, 무엇이든 달라는 대로 주시는 다정한 어머니가 계시니 말이야."], ["Farewell, dearest girl,", "잘 있어, 가장 사랑하는 친구야."]], [["Your affectionate Rebecca.", "너를 사랑하는 레베카가."]], [["P.S.--I wish you could have seen the faces of the Miss Blackbrooks (Admiral Blackbrook's daughters, my dear), fine young ladies, with dresses from London, when Captain Rawdon selected poor me for a partner!", "추신. 로던 대위가 가엾은 나를 짝으로 골랐을 때, 블랙브룩 양들의(블랙브룩 제독의 딸들 말이야, 얘야) 얼굴을 네가 봤어야 하는 건데. 런던에서 마련한 드레스를 차려입은 그 잘난 젊은 숙녀들 말이야!"]], [["When Mrs. Bute Crawley (whose artifices our ingenious Rebecca had so soon discovered) had procured from Miss Sharp the promise of a visit, she induced the all-powerful Miss Crawley to make the necessary application to Sir Pitt, and the good-natured old lady, who loved to be gay herself, and to see every one gay and happy round about her, was quite charmed, and ready to establish a reconciliation and intimacy between her two brothers.", "뷰트 크롤리 부인이(영리한 우리 레베카가 일찌감치 그 속셈을 간파한 그 부인 말이다) 샤프 양에게서 방문하겠다는 약속을 받아 내자, 그녀는 막강한 크롤리 양을 부추겨 핏 경에게 필요한 청을 넣게 했다."], ["It was therefore agreed that the young people of both families should visit each other frequently for the future, and the friendship of course lasted as long as the jovial old mediatrix was there to keep the peace.", "그러자 본인부터 흥겨운 것을 좋아하고 주위 사람 모두가 흥겹고 행복한 꼴을 보기 좋아하던 그 마음씨 좋은 노부인은 무척 흡족해하며, 기꺼이 두 형제 사이의 화해와 친교를 주선하려 들었다. 그리하여 앞으로 양가의 젊은이들이 서로 자주 왕래하기로 합의가 이루어졌고, 물론 그 우정은 그 흥겨운 늙은 중재자가 평화를 지키며 그곳에 머무는 동안만 이어졌다."]], [["\"Why did you ask that scoundrel, Rawdon Crawley, to dine?\"", "\"왜 그 망나니 로던 크롤리를 저녁 식사에 불렀소?\""], ["said the Rector to his lady, as they were walking home through the park.", "공원을 가로질러 집으로 걸어가며 목사가 아내에게 말했다."], ["\"I don't want the fellow.", "\"난 그자가 싫소."], ["He looks down upon us country people as so many blackamoors.", "그자는 우리 시골 사람들을 새까만 깜둥이쯤으로 깔보는 작자란 말이오."], ["He's never content unless he gets my yellow-sealed wine, which costs me ten shillings a bottle, hang him!", "한 병에 십 실링이나 하는 내 노란 봉인 와인을 얻어 마시지 않고는 결코 만족하는 법이 없으니, 빌어먹을!"], ["Besides, he's such an infernal character--he's a gambler--he's a drunkard--he's a profligate in every way.", "게다가 그자는 그야말로 고약한 인간이오ㅡ노름꾼에다 술꾼에다 모든 면에서 방탕한 작자라고. 결투에서 사람을 쏘아 죽였고, 빚이 목까지 차 있는 데다, 나와 내 식구들에게 돌아왔어야 할 크롤리 양 재산의 알짜를 가로채 갔단 말이오."], ["He shot a man in a duel--he's over head and ears in debt, and he's robbed me and mine of the best part of Miss Crawley's fortune.", "왁시 말로는 누님이 그자에게ㅡ\" 여기서 목사는 욕설에 가까운 무언가를 내뱉으며 달을 향해 주먹을 흔들고는, 침울한 어조로 덧붙였다."], ["Waxy says she has him\"--here the Rector shook his fist at the moon, with something very like an oath, and added, in a melancholious tone, \"--down in her will for fifty thousand; and there won't be above thirty to divide.\"", "\"ㅡ유언장에 오만 파운드를 정해 놓았다더군. 그러면 나눠 가질 게 삼만 파운드밖에 안 남는단 말이오.\""]], [["\"I think she's going,\"", "\"누님은 가실 때가 가까운 것 같아요.\""], ["said the Rector's wife.", "목사 부인이 말했다."], ["\"She was very red in the face when we left dinner.", "\"저녁상에서 일어날 때 얼굴이 몹시 붉으시던걸요."], ["I was obliged to unlace her.\"", "코르셋 끈을 풀어 드려야 했다니까요.\""]], [["\"She drank seven glasses of champagne,\"", "\"누님은 샴페인을 일곱 잔이나 드셨소.\""], ["said the reverend gentleman, in a low voice; \"and filthy champagne it is, too, that my brother poisons us with--but you women never know what's what.\"", "목사가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"게다가 그것도 우리 형이 우리한테 먹이는 그 형편없는 샴페인이지ㅡ허나 당신네 여자들은 도무지 뭐가 뭔지를 모른다니까.\""]], [["\"We know nothing,\"", "\"우린 아무것도 모르지요.\""], ["said Mrs. Bute Crawley.", "뷰트 크롤리 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"She drank cherry-brandy after dinner,\"", "\"누님은 저녁 식사 뒤에 체리브랜디를 드셨고,\""], ["continued his Reverence, \"and took curacao with her coffee.", "목사가 말을 이었다. \"커피에는 큐라소까지 타서 드셨소."], ["I wouldn't take a glass for a five-pound note: it kills me with heartburn.", "나라면 오 파운드를 준다 해도 그건 한 잔도 입에 못 댈 거요. 속이 쓰려서 죽을 지경이거든."], ["She can't stand it, Mrs. Crawley--she must go--flesh and blood won't bear it!", "누님 몸이 그걸 견뎌 낼 리가 없소, 크롤리 부인ㅡ가실 수밖에 없다고ㅡ피와 살로 된 몸이 그걸 어찌 버티겠소!"], ["and I lay five to two, Matilda drops in a year.\"", "내 오 대 이로 걸겠는데, 마틸다는 일 년 안에 쓰러질 거요.\""]], [["Indulging in these solemn speculations, and thinking about his debts, and his son Jim at College, and Frank at Woolwich, and the four girls, who were no beauties, poor things, and would not have a penny but what they got from the aunt's expected legacy, the Rector and his lady walked on for a while.", "이런 엄숙한 추측에 빠져 자신의 빚이며, 대학에 다니는 아들 짐이며, 울위치에 있는 프랭크며, 가엾게도 미색이라곤 없어 고모가 남길 유산이 아니고서는 한 푼도 가질 게 없는 네 딸 생각에 잠긴 채, 목사와 그 아내는 한동안 말없이 걸었다."]], [["\"Pitt can't be such an infernal villain as to sell the reversion of the living.", "\"핏이 아무리 그래도 성직록의 상속권을 팔아 치울 만큼 악독한 놈은 아닐 테지."], ["And that Methodist milksop of an eldest son looks to Parliament,\"", "게다가 그 감리교쟁이 겁쟁이 맏아들 놈은 의회만 바라보고 있으니.\""], ["continued Mr. Crawley, after a pause.", "잠시 뒤 크롤리 씨가 말을 이었다."]], [["\"Sir Pitt Crawley will do anything,\"", "\"핏 크롤리 경은 무슨 짓이든 할 사람이에요.\""], ["said the Rector's wife.", "목사 부인이 말했다."], ["\"We must get Miss Crawley to make him promise it to James.\"", "\"크롤리 양을 시켜서 그 자리를 제임스에게 약속하게 만들어야 해요.\""]], [["\"Pitt will promise anything,\"", "\"핏은 무슨 약속이든 다 할 거요.\""], ["replied the brother.", "아우가 대꾸했다."], ["\"He promised he'd pay my college bills, when my father died; he promised he'd build the new wing to the Rectory; he promised he'd let me have Jibb's field and the Six-acre Meadow--and much he executed his promises!", "\"아버지가 돌아가셨을 때 내 대학 빚을 갚아 주겠다고 약속했지. 목사관에 새 별채를 지어 주겠다고 약속했고, 지브의 밭과 육 에이커 목초지를 내게 주겠다고 약속했어ㅡ그런데 그 약속을 얼마나 잘도 지켰는지!"], ["And it's to this man's son--this scoundrel, gambler, swindler, murderer of a Rawdon Crawley, that Matilda leaves the bulk of her money.", "그런데 바로 그 작자의 아들, 그 망나니에 노름꾼에 사기꾼에 살인자인 로던 크롤리에게 마틸다가 재산의 알짜를 남긴다 이거지."], ["I say it's un-Christian.", "이건 그리스도교인답지 못한 짓이오."], ["By Jove, it is.", "정말이지 그렇소."], ["The infamous dog has got every vice except hypocrisy, and that belongs to his brother.\"", "그 파렴치한 개자식은 위선만 빼고 온갖 악덕을 다 갖췄어. 그 위선이라는 건 제 형 몫이고.\""]], [["\"Hush, my dearest love!", "\"쉿, 여보!"], ["we're in Sir Pitt's grounds,\"", "우린 지금 핏 경 영지 안에 있어요.\""], ["interposed his wife.", "아내가 끼어들었다."]], [["\"I say he has got every vice, Mrs. Crawley.", "\"내 말은 그자가 온갖 악덕을 다 갖췄다는 거요, 크롤리 부인."], ["Don't Ma'am, bully me.", "부인, 날 윽박지르지 마시오."], ["Didn't he shoot Captain Marker?", "그자가 마커 대위를 쏘지 않았소?"], ["Didn't he rob young Lord Dovedale at the Cocoa-Tree?", "코코아트리에서 젊은 도브데일 경의 돈을 털지 않았소?"], ["Didn't he cross the fight between Bill Soames and the Cheshire Trump, by which I lost forty pound?", "빌 솜스와 체셔 트럼프의 시합을 농간 부려서 내가 사십 파운드를 날리게 하지 않았느냐 말이오? 당신도 그자가 그랬다는 걸 알잖소."], ["You know he did; and as for the women, why, you heard that before me, in my own magistrate's room.\"", "그리고 여자들로 말할 것 같으면, 아니, 당신은 내 치안판사실에서 바로 내 앞에서 그 얘길 듣지 않았소.\""]], [["\"For heaven's sake, Mr.", "\"제발 부탁이에요, 크롤리 씨.\""], ["Crawley,\"", "부인이 말했다."], ["said the lady, \"spare me the details.\"", "\"그 자질구레한 이야기는 그만하세요.\""]], [["\"And you ask this villain into your house!\"", "\"그런 악당을 당신 집에 부르다니!\""], ["continued the exasperated Rector.", "격분한 목사가 말을 이었다."], ["\"You, the mother of a young family--the wife of a clergyman of the Church of England.", "\"당신, 어린 자식들을 둔 어미요ㅡ잉글랜드 국교회 성직자의 아내가 말이오."], ["By Jove!\"", "맙소사!\""]], [["\"Bute Crawley, you are a fool,\"", "\"뷰트 크롤리, 당신은 바보예요.\""], ["said the Rector's wife scornfully.", "목사 부인이 경멸하듯 말했다."]], [["\"Well, Ma'am, fool or not--and I don't say, Martha, I'm so clever as you are, I never did.", "\"흥, 부인, 바보든 아니든ㅡ게다가 마사, 나는 내가 당신만큼 영리하다고 한 적도 없고, 단 한 번도 그런 적 없소."], ["But I won't meet Rawdon Crawley, that's flat.", "하지만 난 로던 크롤리하고는 절대 안 만날 거요, 그건 분명하오."], ["I'll go over to Huddleston, that I will, and see his black greyhound, Mrs.", "허들스턴 댁에나 다녀와야겠소, 정말 그래야겠어. 그 댁 검은 그레이하운드를 보러 가서 말이오, 크롤리 부인."], ["Crawley; and I'll run Lancelot against him for fifty.", "그리고 오십 파운드를 걸고 우리 랜슬롯을 그놈하고 붙여 봐야겠소."], ["By Jove, I will; or against any dog in England.", "맙소사, 정말 그러겠소. 잉글랜드의 어떤 개를 상대로든 말이오."], ["But I won't meet that beast Rawdon Crawley.\"", "하지만 그 짐승 같은 로던 크롤리하고는 절대 안 만날 거요.\""]], [["\"Mr. Crawley, you are intoxicated, as usual,\"", "\"크롤리 씨, 당신은 늘 그렇듯 또 취하셨군요.\""], ["replied his wife.", "아내가 대꾸했다."], ["And the next morning, when the Rector woke, and called for small beer, she put him in mind of his promise to visit Sir Huddleston Fuddleston on Saturday, and as he knew he should have a wet night, it was agreed that he might gallop back again in time for church on Sunday morning.", "그리고 이튿날 아침 목사가 잠에서 깨어 묽은 맥주를 찾자, 부인은 토요일에 허들스턴 퍼들스턴 경을 찾아가기로 한 그의 약속을 일깨워 주었다. 그날 밤 진탕 마시게 되리라는 것을 그도 알았으므로, 일요일 아침 예배 시간에 맞춰 다시 말을 달려 돌아오기로 합의가 이루어졌다."], ["Thus it will be seen that the parishioners of Crawley were equally happy in their Squire and in their Rector.", "이로써 알 수 있듯, 크롤리의 교구민들은 그들의 향사로 보나 그들의 목사로 보나 그저 행복하기 그지없었던 셈이다."]], [["Miss Crawley had not long been established at the Hall before Rebecca's fascinations had won the heart of that good-natured London rake, as they had of the country innocents whom we have been describing.", "크롤리 양이 홀에 자리 잡은 지 얼마 되지 않아, 레베카의 매력은 우리가 묘사해 온 시골의 순박한 이들의 마음을 사로잡았듯, 그 마음씨 좋은 런던 난봉꾼의 마음마저 사로잡아 버렸다."], ["Taking her accustomed drive, one day, she thought fit to order that \"that little governess\" should accompany her to Mudbury.", "어느 날 평소처럼 마차를 타고 나서면서, 크롤리 양은 \"그 작은 가정교사\"가 머드베리까지 자기와 동행하도록 분부를 내리는 게 좋겠다고 여겼다."], ["Before they had returned Rebecca had made a conquest of her; having made her laugh four times, and amused her during the whole of the little journey.", "그들이 돌아오기도 전에 레베카는 이미 그분을 완전히 정복해 버렸다. 그분을 네 번이나 웃게 만들었고, 그 짧은 나들이 내내 즐겁게 해 드렸던 것이다."]], [["\"Not let Miss Sharp dine at table!\"", "\"샤프 양을 식탁에서 식사 못 하게 한다고요!\""], ["said she to Sir Pitt, who had arranged a dinner of ceremony, and asked all the neighbouring baronets.", "그분은, 격식 차린 만찬을 마련하고 인근의 준남작들을 죄다 불러 놓은 핏 경에게 말했다."], ["\"My dear creature, do you suppose I can talk about the nursery with Lady Fuddleston, or discuss justices' business with that goose, old Sir Giles Wapshot?", "\"이런 딱한 양반 같으니, 내가 퍼들스턴 부인하고 육아 이야기나 하고, 저 멍청한 늙은 자일스 와프숏 경하고 치안판사 일이나 논하고 있어야 한다고 생각하는 거예요?"], ["I insist upon Miss Sharp appearing.", "나는 샤프 양이 꼭 나와야겠어요."], ["Let Lady Crawley remain upstairs, if there is no room.", "자리가 없으면 크롤리 부인더러 위층에 그냥 있으라고 해요."], ["But little Miss Sharp!", "하지만 우리 작은 샤프 양은!"], ["Why, she's the only person fit to talk to in the county!\"", "아니, 이 고장에서 말 상대가 될 만한 사람은 그 애뿐이라고요!\""]], [["Of course, after such a peremptory order as this, Miss Sharp, the governess, received commands to dine with the illustrious company below stairs.", "물론 이렇게 단호한 분부가 떨어진 뒤로, 가정교사 샤프 양은 아래층에서 그 쟁쟁한 손님들과 함께 식사하라는 명을 받게 되었다."], ["And when Sir Huddleston had, with great pomp and ceremony, handed Miss Crawley in to dinner, and was preparing to take his place by her side, the old lady cried out, in a shrill voice, \"Becky Sharp!", "그리고 허들스턴 경이 한껏 위세를 부리며 크롤리 양을 식탁으로 모시고 들어와 그 곁자리에 막 앉으려 하자, 노부인이 날카로운 목소리로 외쳤다. \"베키 샤프!"], ["Miss Sharp!", "샤프 양!"], ["Come you and sit by me and amuse me; and let Sir Huddleston sit by Lady Wapshot.\"", "이리 와서 내 옆에 앉아 날 즐겁게 해 주렴. 허들스턴 경은 와프숏 부인 옆에 앉으시고.\""]], [["When the parties were over, and the carriages had rolled away, the insatiable Miss Crawley would say, \"Come to my dressing room, Becky, and let us abuse the company\"--which, between them, this pair of friends did perfectly. Old Sir Huddleston wheezed a great deal at dinner; Sir Giles Wapshot had a particularly noisy manner of imbibing his soup, and her ladyship a wink of the left eye; all of which Becky caricatured to admiration; as well as the particulars of the night's conversation; the politics; the war; the quarter-sessions; the famous run with the H.H., and those heavy and dreary themes, about which country gentlemen converse. As for the Misses Wapshot's toilettes and Lady Fuddleston's famous yellow hat, Miss Sharp tore them to tatters, to the infinite amusement of her audience.", "연회가 끝나고 마차들이 줄지어 떠나가고 나면, 도무지 만족을 모르는 크롤리 양은 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"내 화장실로 오렴, 베키. 우리 손님들 흉이나 보자꾸나.\" 그리하여 이 한 쌍의 벗은 둘이서 그 일을 더없이 완벽하게 해냈다. 늙은 허들스턴 경은 저녁 식사 내내 쌕쌕거리며 숨을 몰아쉬었고, 자일스 와프숏 경은 유난히 소리를 내며 수프를 들이켰으며, 그 부인은 왼쪽 눈을 찡긋거리는 버릇이 있었다. 베키는 이 모든 것을 기막히게 흉내 냈다. 그뿐 아니라 그날 밤 오간 대화의 시시콜콜한 내용, 정치, 전쟁, 분기 순회재판, 그 유명한 H.H. 사냥 이야기며, 시골 신사들이 입에 올리는 그 묵직하고 따분한 화제들까지 죄다 흉내 냈다. 와프숏 양들의 차림새와 퍼들스턴 부인의 그 유명한 노란 모자로 말하자면, 샤프 양은 그것들을 갈가리 찢어발겨 청중에게 무한한 즐거움을 안겨 주었다."]], [["\"My dear, you are a perfect trouvaille,\"", "\"얘야, 너는 그야말로 보물이로구나.\" 크롤리 양은 말하곤 했다."], ["Miss Crawley would say.", "\"네가 런던에 있는 내게 와 줄 수 있다면 좋으련만."], ["\"I wish you could come to me in London, but I couldn't make a butt of you as I do of poor Briggs no, no, you little sly creature; you are too clever--Isn't she, Firkin?\"", "하지만 나는 가엾은 브리그스를 놀려 먹듯 너를 놀림감으로 삼을 수는 없겠지ㅡ아니, 아니, 너 같은 작고 능청스러운 것을. 너는 너무 영리하거든ㅡ안 그래, 퍼킨?\""]], [["Mrs. Firkin (who was dressing the very small remnant of hair which remained on Miss Crawley's pate), flung up her head and said, \"I think Miss is very clever,\"", "(크롤리 양의 정수리에 얼마 남지 않은 머리카락을 매만지고 있던) 퍼킨 부인은 고개를 홱 쳐들며, 더없이 사람 잡을 듯한 비꼬는 투로 말했다."], ["with the most killing sarcastic air.", "\"제 생각에도 아가씨는 무척 영리하지요.\""], ["In fact, Mrs. Firkin had that natural jealousy which is one of the main principles of every honest woman.", "사실 퍼킨 부인에게는, 정직한 여자라면 누구나 지니는 주요 원리 가운데 하나인 그 타고난 질투심이 있었던 것이다."]], [["After rebuffing Sir Huddleston Fuddleston, Miss Crawley ordered that Rawdon Crawley should lead her in to dinner every day, and that Becky should follow with her cushion--or else she would have Becky's arm and Rawdon with the pillow.", "허들스턴 퍼들스턴 경을 퇴짜 놓은 뒤로, 크롤리 양은 날마다 로던 크롤리가 자기를 식탁으로 모시고 들어가고, 베키가 방석을 들고 그 뒤를 따르도록 분부했다. 아니면 베키의 팔에 의지하고 로던에게 방석을 들게 했다."], ["\"We must sit together,\"", "\"우린 함께 앉아야 해.\""], ["she said.", "그분은 말했다."], ["\"We're the only three Christians in the county, my love\"--in which case, it must be confessed, that religion was at a very low ebb in the county of Hants.", "\"이 고장에서 우리 셋만이 유일한 그리스도교인이거든, 얘야.\" 이 말대로라면 햄프셔 지방의 신앙심이란 그야말로 바닥까지 떨어져 있었다고 인정할 수밖에 없겠다."]], [["Besides being such a fine religionist, Miss Crawley was, as we have said, an Ultra-liberal in opinions, and always took occasion to express these in the most candid manner.", "이렇듯 신앙심이 깊었을 뿐 아니라, 앞서 말했듯 크롤리 양은 사상에서 극단적 자유주의자였으며, 늘 기회만 닿으면 그 사상을 더없이 솔직하게 표명하곤 했다."]], [["\"What is birth, my dear!\" she would say to Rebecca--\"Look at my brother Pitt; look at the Huddlestons, who have been here since Henry II; look at poor Bute at the parsonage--is any one of them equal to you in intelligence or breeding? Equal to you--they are not even equal to poor dear Briggs, my companion, or Bowls, my butler. You, my love, are a little paragon--positively a little jewel--You have more brains than half the shire--if merit had its reward you ought to be a Duchess--no, there ought to be no duchesses at all--but you ought to have no superior, and I consider you, my love, as my equal in every respect; and--will you put some coals on the fire, my dear; and will you pick this dress of mine, and alter it, you who can do it so well?\" So this old philanthropist used to make her equal run of her errands, execute her millinery, and read her to sleep with French novels, every night.", "\"가문이 다 무어냐, 얘야!\" 그분은 레베카에게 말하곤 했다. \"우리 오라버니 핏을 보렴. 헨리 2세 이래로 이 고장에 살아온 허들스턴 가를 보렴. 목사관에 있는 가엾은 뷰트를 보렴ㅡ그중 누가 지성에서나 교양에서 너와 견줄 만하더냐? 너와 견주기는커녕, 내 말동무 가엾은 브리그스나 내 집사 보울스에도 못 미친단다. 얘야, 너는 작은 본보기 같은 아이야ㅡ정말이지 작은 보석이지ㅡ너는 이 고장 사람 절반보다 더 영리한 머리를 가졌어ㅡ공덕에 마땅한 보답이 따른다면 너는 공작부인이 되어야 마땅하지ㅡ아니, 공작부인이라는 것 자체가 아예 없어야 옳아ㅡ하지만 너는 누구의 아래에도 있어선 안 돼. 그리고 얘야, 나는 너를 모든 면에서 나와 동등한 사람으로 여긴단다. 그러니ㅡ얘야, 난로에 석탄 좀 더 넣어 주겠니? 그리고 이 내 드레스 좀 뜯어서 고쳐 주련? 너는 그 일을 어찌나 잘하는지.\" 이렇듯 이 늙은 박애주의자는 자기가 동등하다고 한 그 아이에게 심부름을 시키고, 바느질을 맡기며, 밤마다 프랑스 소설을 읽어 주어 자기를 잠들게 했던 것이다."]], [["At this time, as some old readers may recollect, the genteel world had been thrown into a considerable state of excitement by two events, which, as the papers say, might give employment to the gentlemen of the long robe.", "이 무렵, 어떤 나이 든 독자들은 기억할지도 모르겠지만, 상류 사회는 두 사건으로 적잖이 들썩이고 있었다. 신문들이 흔히 말하듯, 법조계 양반들에게 일거리를 안겨 줄 만한 사건이었다."], ["Ensign Shafton had run away with Lady Barbara Fitzurse, the Earl of Bruin's daughter and heiress; and poor Vere Vane, a gentleman who, up to forty, had maintained a most respectable character and reared a numerous family, suddenly and outrageously left his home, for the sake of Mrs.", "샤프턴 소위가 브루인 백작의 딸이자 상속녀인 바버라 피츠어스 영애를 데리고 달아났던 것이다."], ["Rougemont, the actress, who was sixty-five years of age.", "그리고 가엾은 비어 베인은, 마흔이 되도록 더없이 존경할 만한 인품을 유지하고 자식도 여럿 키운 신사였건만, 별안간 터무니없이도 예순다섯 살 먹은 여배우 루즈몽 부인을 위해 제 집을 버렸던 것이다."]], [["\"That was the most beautiful part of dear Lord Nelson's character,\"", "\"그것이야말로 그리운 넬슨 경의 인품에서 가장 아름다운 대목이지.\""], ["Miss Crawley said.", "크롤리 양이 말했다."], ["\"He went to the deuce for a woman.", "\"그분은 한 여자를 위해 파멸의 길로 가셨거든."], ["There must be good in a man who will do that.", "그렇게 할 수 있는 남자에게는 분명 무언가 좋은 점이 있는 거야."], ["I adore all imprudent matches.-- What I like best, is for a nobleman to marry a miller's daughter, as Lord Flowerdale did--it makes all the women so angry--I wish some great man would run away with you, my dear; I'm sure you're pretty enough.\"", "나는 무모한 결혼이라면 다 좋아한단다.ㅡ내가 가장 좋아하는 건, 플라워데일 경이 그랬듯 귀족이 방앗간 집 딸과 결혼하는 거야ㅡ그러면 온 여자들이 어찌나 분개하는지ㅡ어떤 대단한 양반이 너를 데리고 달아나 주면 좋으련만, 얘야. 너는 분명 그러기에 충분히 예쁘니까.\""]], [["\"Two post-boys!--Oh, it would be delightful!\"", "\"우편마차 마부가 둘이나!ㅡ아, 정말 멋질 거예요!\""], ["Rebecca owned.", "레베카는 그렇다고 맞장구쳤다."]], [["\"And what I like next best, is for a poor fellow to run away with a rich girl.", "\"그다음으로 내가 좋아하는 건, 가난한 사내가 부잣집 처녀를 데리고 달아나는 거야."], ["I have set my heart on Rawdon running away with some one.\"", "나는 로던이 누군가를 데리고 달아나면 좋겠다고 마음먹고 있단다.\""]], [["\"A rich some one, or a poor some one?\"", "\"부잣집 처녀요, 아니면 가난한 집 처녀요?\""]], [["\"Why, you goose!", "\"아니, 이런 멍청한 것!"], ["Rawdon has not a shilling but what I give him.", "로던은 내가 주는 것 말고는 단돈 한 푼도 없는 처지야."], ["He is crible de dettes--he must repair his fortunes, and succeed in the world.\"", "그 애는 빚에 짓눌려 있단다ㅡ재산을 다시 일으켜 세상에서 성공해야 한다고.\""]], [["\"Is he very clever?\"", "\"그분은 무척 영리한가요?\""], ["Rebecca asked.", "레베카가 물었다."]], [["\"Clever, my love?--not an idea in the world beyond his horses, and his regiment, and his hunting, and his play; but he must succeed--he's so delightfully wicked.", "\"영리하냐고, 얘야?ㅡ자기 말이며 연대며 사냥이며 노름 말고는 머릿속에 든 생각이 하나도 없는 애야. 하지만 그 앤 성공해야 해ㅡ어찌나 사랑스럽도록 못된 녀석인지."], ["Don't you know he has hit a man, and shot an injured father through the hat only?", "그 애가 사람을 한 명 때렸고, 분기탱천한 어떤 아버지를 쏘아 모자만 꿰뚫어 놓은 거 모르니?"], ["He's adored in his regiment; and all the young men at Wattier's and the Cocoa-Tree swear by him.\"", "그 앤 자기 연대에서 떠받들어지고 있단다. 워티어스며 코코아트리에 드나드는 젊은이들도 다 그 애라면 사족을 못 쓰지.\""]], [["When Miss Rebecca Sharp wrote to her beloved friend the account of the little ball at Queen's Crawley, and the manner in which, for the first time, Captain Crawley had distinguished her, she did not, strange to relate, give an altogether accurate account of the transaction.", "레베카 샤프 양이 사랑하는 벗에게 퀸스 크롤리에서 열린 그 작은 무도회와, 크롤리 대위가 처음으로 자기를 각별히 대해 준 정황을 적어 보냈을 때, 이상하게도 그녀는 그 일을 온전히 정확하게 적지는 않았다."], ["The Captain had distinguished her a great number of times before.", "그 대위가 그녀를 각별히 대한 건 그전에도 여러 번이었다."], ["The Captain had met her in a half-score of walks.", "그 대위는 산책길에서 십여 차례 그녀와 마주쳤다."], ["The Captain had lighted upon her in a half-hundred of corridors and passages.", "그 대위는 복도며 통로에서 쉰 번도 넘게 그녀와 맞닥뜨렸다."], ["The Captain had hung over her piano twenty times of an evening (my Lady was now upstairs, being ill, and nobody heeded her) as Miss Sharp sang.", "그 대위는 샤프 양이 노래할 때면(마님은 이제 위층에서 앓고 있었고 아무도 그녀를 거들떠보지 않았다) 어느 저녁이고 스무 번씩 그녀의 피아노에 몸을 기울였다."], ["The Captain had written her notes (the best that the great blundering dragoon could devise and spell; but dulness gets on as well as any other quality with women).", "그 대위는 그녀에게 쪽지도 써 보냈다(덩치만 크고 어수룩한 기병이 궁리해 짜내고 철자를 더듬어 쓴, 그나마 최선의 쪽지였다. 하지만 여자에게는 우둔함도 다른 어떤 자질 못지않게 잘 통하는 법이다)."], ["But when he put the first of the notes into the leaves of the song she was singing, the little governess, rising and looking him steadily in the face, took up the triangular missive daintily, and waved it about as if it were a cocked hat, and she, advancing to the enemy, popped the note into the fire, and made him a very low curtsey, and went back to her place, and began to sing away again more merrily than ever.", "그러나 그가 첫 번째 쪽지를 그녀가 부르던 노래의 악보 갈피에 끼워 넣자, 그 작은 가정교사는 일어서서 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 쳐다보며 그 세모진 편지를 곱게 집어 들더니, 그것을 마치 삼각모라도 되는 양 흔들어 보였다. 그러고는 적을 향해 나아가듯 그것을 난롯불 속에 톡 던져 넣고, 그에게 더없이 깊숙이 무릎을 굽혀 인사한 뒤, 제자리로 돌아가 그 어느 때보다 흥겹게 다시 노래하기 시작했다."]], [["\"What's that?\"", "\"무슨 일이냐?\""], ["said Miss Crawley, interrupted in her after-dinner doze by the stoppage of the music.", "음악이 멎는 바람에 저녁 식사 뒤의 선잠에서 깨어난 크롤리 양이 물었다."]], [["\"It's a false note,\"", "\"틀린 음이 나서요.\""], ["Miss Sharp said with a laugh; and Rawdon Crawley fumed with rage and mortification.", "샤프 양이 웃으며 말했다. 그러는 동안 로던 크롤리는 분과 굴욕으로 부글부글 끓고 있었다."]], [["Seeing the evident partiality of Miss Crawley for the new governess, how good it was of Mrs.", "크롤리 양이 새 가정교사를 그토록 노골적으로 편애하는 것을 보면서도, 뷰트 크롤리 부인은 질투하지 않고 그 젊은 숙녀를 목사관에 맞아들였으니 어찌나 마음씨가 고운지!"], ["Bute Crawley not to be jealous, and to welcome the young lady to the Rectory, and not only her, but Rawdon Crawley, her husband's rival in the Old Maid's five per cents!", "그녀뿐 아니라, 그 노처녀의 오 퍼센트 공채를 두고 자기 남편의 경쟁자인 로던 크롤리까지 맞아들였으니 말이다! 크롤리 부인과 그 조카는 서로의 벗을 무척 좋아하게 되었다."], ["They became very fond of each other's society, Mrs. Crawley and her nephew.", "그는 사냥을 그만두었고, 퍼들스턴 댁의 연회도 마다했으며, 머드베리 주둔지의 장교 식당에서 식사하는 것도 싫다 했다."], ["He gave up hunting; he declined entertainments at Fuddleston: he would not dine with the mess of the depot at Mudbury: his great pleasure was to stroll over to Crawley parsonage--whither Miss Crawley came too; and as their mamma was ill, why not the children with Miss Sharp?", "그의 가장 큰 즐거움은 크롤리 목사관까지 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어가는 것이었다. 그곳에는 크롤리 양도 왔으니, 그 애들 어머니가 앓고 있으니 아이들이 샤프 양과 함께 못 갈 까닭이 있겠는가?"], ["So the children (little dears!) came with Miss Sharp; and of an evening some of the party would walk back together.", "그리하여 아이들은(귀여운 것들!) 샤프 양과 함께 왔고, 저녁이면 일행 중 몇몇이 함께 걸어 돌아오곤 했다. 크롤리 양은 아니었다."], ["Not Miss Crawley--she preferred her carriage--but the walk over the Rectory fields, and in at the little park wicket, and through the dark plantation, and up the checkered avenue to Queen's Crawley, was charming in the moonlight to two such lovers of the picturesque as the Captain and Miss Rebecca.", "그분은 마차를 더 좋아했으니까. 그러나 목사관 들판을 가로질러, 자그마한 공원 쪽문을 지나, 어두운 농원을 지나, 얼룩진 가로수 길을 올라 퀸스 크롤리로 가는 그 길은, 달빛 아래에서 그 대위와 레베카 양처럼 풍경을 사랑하는 두 사람에게는 더없이 매혹적이었다."]], [["\"O those stars, those stars!\"", "\"아, 저 별들, 저 별들!\""], ["Miss Rebecca would say, turning her twinkling green eyes up towards them.", "레베카 양은 그 반짝이는 초록 눈을 별들을 향해 치켜올리며 말하곤 했다."], ["\"I feel myself almost a spirit when I gaze upon them.\"", "\"저것들을 바라보고 있노라면 나 자신이 거의 영혼이 된 것만 같아요.\""]], [["\"O--ah--Gad--yes, so do I exactly, Miss Sharp,\"", "\"오ㅡ아ㅡ저런ㅡ그렇소, 나도 꼭 그렇소, 샤프 양.\""], ["the other enthusiast replied.", "또 한 사람의 열광자가 대꾸했다."], ["\"You don't mind my cigar, do you, Miss Sharp?\"", "\"내 시가 연기가 거슬리지는 않겠지요, 샤프 양?\" 샤프 양은 세상 그 무엇보다도 야외에서 맡는 시가 냄새를 좋아했다."], ["Miss Sharp loved the smell of a cigar out of doors beyond everything in the world--and she just tasted one too, in the prettiest way possible, and gave a little puff, and a little scream, and a little giggle, and restored the delicacy to the Captain, who twirled his moustache, and straightway puffed it into a blaze that glowed quite red in the dark plantation, and swore--\"Jove--aw--Gad--aw--it's the finest segaw I ever smoked in the world aw,\"", "그뿐 아니라 그녀는 더없이 어여쁜 자태로 시가를 한 모금 맛보기까지 하더니, 살짝 연기를 내뿜고는 작게 비명을 지르고 작게 키득거리고 나서, 그 진귀한 물건을 대위에게 돌려주었다. 대위는 콧수염을 배배 꼬며 그것을 단번에 피워 올려 어두운 농원에서 시뻘겋게 타오르게 하고는 이렇게 내뱉었다."], ["for his intellect and conversation were alike brilliant and becoming to a heavy young dragoon.", "\"저런ㅡ어ㅡ맙소사ㅡ어ㅡ이건 내 평생 피워 본 중에 가장 좋은 시가요, 어.\" 그의 지성과 화술은 둘 다, 둔중한 젊은 기병에게 어울릴 만큼 똑같이 빛나는 것이었던 셈이다."]], [["Old Sir Pitt, who was taking his pipe and beer, and talking to John Horrocks about a \"ship\"", "파이프 담배를 피우며 맥주를 들이켜고는 잡을 \"양\""], ["that was to be killed, espied the pair so occupied from his study-window, and with dreadful oaths swore that if it wasn't for Miss Crawley, he'd take Rawdon and bundle un out of doors, like a rogue as he was.", "한 마리에 관해 존 호럭스와 이야기를 나누던 늙은 핏 경이, 서재 창문으로 그렇게 시시덕거리는 두 사람을 발견하고는, 만약 크롤리 양만 아니라면 로던을 잡아다 망나니 같은 놈을 내쫓듯 문밖으로 내던져 버리겠다며 무시무시하게 욕을 퍼부었다."]], [["\"He be a bad'n, sure enough,\" Mr.", "\"그자가 못된 놈인 건 분명합지요.\""], ["Horrocks remarked; \"and his man Flethers is wuss, and have made such a row in the housekeeper's room about the dinners and hale, as no lord would make--but I think Miss Sharp's a match for'n, Sir Pitt,\"", "호럭스 씨가 말했다. \"그 하인 플레더스란 작자는 한술 더 떠서, 저녁이며 맥주를 두고 가정부 방에서 어찌나 한바탕 소란을 피우는지, 어느 귀족 나리도 그렇게는 안 하실 겁니다요ㅡ허나 제 생각엔 샤프 양이 그자한테는 만만찮은 상대일 듯합니다요, 핏 경.\""], ["he added, after a pause.", "그는 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 덧붙였다."]], [["And so, in truth, she was--for father and son too.", "그리고 사실 그녀는 그러했다ㅡ아버지에게도, 아들에게도 만만찮은 상대였던 것이다."]], [["Quite a Sentimental Chapter\nWe must now take leave of Arcadia, and those amiable people practising the rural virtues there, and travel back to London, to inquire what has become of Miss Amelia.", "이제 우리는 아르카디아와 그곳에서 시골의 미덕을 실천하는 그 사랑스러운 사람들에게 작별을 고하고, 런던으로 되돌아가 애밀리아 양이 어찌 되었는지를 살펴보아야겠다."]], [["\"We don't care a fig for her,\"", "\"우리는 그 애 따위 조금도 신경 쓰지 않아요.\""], ["writes some unknown correspondent with a pretty little handwriting and a pink seal to her note.", "어느 이름 모를 여인이 예쁘장한 필체로, 분홍빛 봉인을 찍은 편지에 이렇게 적어 보낸다."], ["\"She is fade and insipid,\"", "\"그 애는 김빠지고 밍밍해요.\""], ["and adds some more kind remarks in this strain, which I should never have repeated at all, but that they are in truth prodigiously complimentary to the young lady whom they concern.", "그러고는 이런 투의 친절한 평을 몇 마디 더 덧붙이는데, 그 말들이 사실은 거기 거론된 그 젊은 숙녀에게 엄청난 찬사가 된다는 점이 아니었더라면 나는 그것들을 도무지 옮겨 적지 않았을 것이다."]], [["Has the beloved reader, in his experience of society, never heard similar remarks by good-natured female friends; who always wonder what you CAN see in Miss Smith that is so fascinating; or what COULD induce Major Jones to propose for that silly insignificant simpering Miss Thompson, who has nothing but her wax-doll face to recommend her?", "사랑하는 독자께서는, 사교계 경험 가운데 마음씨 고운 여자 친구들에게서 이런 비슷한 평을 들어 보신 적이 한 번도 없으신가? 그들은 늘 의아해한다."], ["What is there in a pair of pink cheeks and blue eyes forsooth?", "도대체 스미스 양의 무엇이 그토록 매혹적이라는 건지, 혹은 도대체 무엇이 존스 소령으로 하여금 저 어리석고 하찮고 실실 웃기만 하는 톰슨 양에게 청혼하게 만들었다는 건지 말이다. 그 여자라곤 밀랍 인형 같은 얼굴 말고는 내세울 게 하나도 없는데!"], ["these dear Moralists ask, and hint wisely that the gifts of genius, the accomplishments of the mind, the mastery of Mangnall's Questions, and a ladylike knowledge of botany and geology, the knack of making poetry, the power of rattling sonatas in the Herz-manner, and so forth, are far more valuable endowments for a female, than those fugitive charms which a few years will inevitably tarnish.", "분홍빛 두 볼과 파란 두 눈에 대체 뭐가 있다는 거예요? 이 다정한 도덕가들은 이렇게 묻고는, 천재적 재능이라든가, 정신의 교양이라든가, 망널 문답서의 통달이라든가, 식물학과 지질학에 관한 숙녀다운 지식이라든가, 시를 짓는 솜씨라든가, 헤르츠 풍으로 소나타를 좔좔 쳐 내는 능력 따위가, 몇 해만 지나면 어쩔 수 없이 빛바래고 마는 저 덧없는 매력보다 여자에게는 훨씬 더 값진 자질이라고 슬며시 지혜로운 척 일러 준다."], ["It is quite edifying to hear women speculate upon the worthlessness and the duration of beauty.", "여자들이 아름다움의 무가치함과 그 수명에 대해 이러쿵저러쿵 따지는 것을 듣고 있노라면 참으로 교화가 되는 일이다. * 망널 문답서: 19세기 영국에서 널리 쓰인 여학생용 일반상식 학습서."]], [["But though virtue is a much finer thing, and those hapless creatures who suffer under the misfortune of good looks ought to be continually put in mind of the fate which awaits them; and though, very likely, the heroic female character which ladies admire is a more glorious and beautiful object than the kind, fresh, smiling, artless, tender little domestic goddess, whom men are inclined to worship--yet the latter and inferior sort of women must have this consolation--that the men do admire them after all; and that, in spite of all our kind friends'", "그러나 미덕이 훨씬 더 훌륭한 것이고, 미모라는 불운에 시달리는 저 가엾은 피조물들에게는 그들을 기다리고 있는 운명을 끊임없이 일깨워 주어야 마땅하며, 또한 십중팔구 숙녀들이 흠모하는 저 영웅적 여성상이, 남자들이 떠받들고 싶어 하는 친절하고 싱그럽고 미소 짓고 꾸밈없고 다정한 자그마한 가정의 여신보다 더 영광스럽고 아름다운 대상이라 하더라도, 후자에 속하는 그 열등한 부류의 여자들에게는 이런 위안이 남아 있는 법이다. 즉, 남자들은 결국 그런 여자들을 흠모한다는 것, 그리고 우리의 그 다정한 친구들이 아무리 경고하고 항변해도 우리는 한사코 그 절망적인 오류와 어리석음을 고집할 것이며, 이야기가 끝나는 그날까지 그러리라는 것이다."], ["warnings and protests, we go on in our desperate error and folly, and shall to the end of the chapter. Indeed, for my own part, though I have been repeatedly told by persons for whom I have the greatest respect, that Miss Brown is an insignificant chit, and Mrs.", "사실 나로 말하자면, 내가 더없이 존경하는 사람들에게서 브라운 양은 하찮은 계집애에 불과하고, 화이트 부인은 자그마한 흐트러진 얼굴 말고는 아무것도 없으며, 블랙 부인은 자기 입으로 할 말 한마디 없는 사람이라는 말을 거듭 들었음에도, 나는 블랙 부인과 더없이 즐거운 대화를 나누어 왔음을 안다(물론 친애하는 부인, 그 대화들은 결코 발설치 않을 것입니다)."], ["White has nothing but her petit minois chiffonne, and Mrs. Black has not a word to say for herself; yet I know that I have had the most delightful conversations with Mrs.", "또한 모든 남자가 화이트 부인의 의자 둘레에 떼 지어 모여 있는 것을, 모든 젊은이가 브라운 양과 춤추려고 다투는 것을 나는 본다."], ["Black (of course, my dear Madam, they are inviolable): I see all the men in a cluster round Mrs. White's chair: all the young fellows battling to dance with Miss Brown; and so I am tempted to think that to be despised by her sex is a very great compliment to a woman.", "그래서 나는 같은 여자들에게 멸시받는 것이야말로 한 여자에게는 대단한 찬사라고 여기고 싶어지는 것이다."]], [["The young ladies in Amelia's society did this for her very satisfactorily.", "애밀리아의 교제권에 있는 젊은 숙녀들이야말로 그녀를 위해 이 일을 더없이 흡족하게 해 주었다."], ["For instance, there was scarcely any point upon which the Misses Osborne, George's sisters, and the Mesdemoiselles Dobbin agreed so well as in their estimate of her very trifling merits: and their wonder that their brothers could find any charms in her.", "이를테면 조지의 누이들인 오스본 자매와 도빈 자매가 그토록 의견 일치를 본 점이라곤, 그녀의 보잘것없는 장점에 대한 평가와, 자기네 오라비들이 도대체 그녀에게서 무슨 매력을 찾아낼 수 있단 말인가 하는 의아함 말고는 거의 없을 정도였다."], ["\"We are kind to her,\"", "\"우리는 그 애한테 친절하게 대해 줘요.\""], ["the Misses Osborne said, a pair of fine black-browed young ladies who had had the best of governesses, masters, and milliners; and they treated her with such extreme kindness and condescension, and patronised her so insufferably, that the poor little thing was in fact perfectly dumb in their presence, and to all outward appearance as stupid as they thought her.", "오스본 자매는 이렇게 말했는데, 검은 눈썹의 잘난 두 젊은 숙녀로 최고의 가정교사와 선생들과 모자 장수의 보살핌을 받아 온 처지였다. 그리고 그들은 그녀를 어찌나 극진히 친절하고 생색내며 대해 주는지, 또 어찌나 못 견디게 후원해 주는 척하는지, 그 가엾은 작은 사람은 사실 그들 앞에만 서면 완전히 입이 굳어 버려서, 겉으로 보기에는 영락없이 그들이 여기는 만큼 멍청해 보였다."], ["She made efforts to like them, as in duty bound, and as sisters of her future husband.", "그녀는 도리상 마땅한 일로, 또 장차 남편이 될 사람의 누이들이라는 이유로 그들을 좋아하려고 애를 썼다."], ["She passed \"long mornings\" with them--the most dreary and serious of forenoons.", "그녀는 그들과 함께 \"긴 오전\"을 보냈는데, 그것은 더없이 음울하고 엄숙한 오전 시간이었다."], ["She drove out solemnly in their great family coach with them, and Miss Wirt their governess, that raw-boned Vestal.", "그녀는 그들과, 그리고 그들의 가정교사인 저 깡마른 노처녀 위트 양과 함께 그 커다란 가족용 마차를 타고 점잖게 드라이브를 나갔다."], ["They took her to the ancient concerts by way of a treat, and to the oratorio, and to St. Paul's to see the charity children, where in such terror was she of her friends, she almost did not dare be affected by the hymn the children sang.", "그들은 호의랍시고 그녀를 고전 음악회며 오라토리오며, 또 자선 학교 아이들을 구경하는 세인트폴 대성당까지 데려갔는데, 그곳에서 그녀는 친구들이 어찌나 무서웠던지 아이들이 부르는 찬송가에 감동하는 것조차 거의 감히 하지 못할 지경이었다."], ["Their house was comfortable; their papa's table rich and handsome; their society solemn and genteel; their self-respect prodigious; they had the best pew at the Foundling: all their habits were pompous and orderly, and all their amusements intolerably dull and decorous.", "그들의 집은 안락했고, 아버지의 식탁은 풍성하고 훌륭했으며, 그들의 교제권은 엄숙하고 점잖았고, 그들의 자존심은 엄청났으며, 고아원 예배당에서도 가장 좋은 자리를 차지하고 있었다. 그들의 온갖 습관은 거드름 가득하고 질서 정연했으며, 그들의 온갖 오락은 견딜 수 없이 따분하고 점잔을 뺐다."], ["After every one of her visits (and oh how glad she was when they were over!) Miss Osborne and Miss Maria Osborne, and Miss Wirt, the vestal governess, asked each other with increased wonder, \"What could George find in that creature?\"", "그녀가 방문할 때마다(아, 그것이 끝났을 때 그녀가 얼마나 기뻐했던지!) 오스본 양과 마리아 오스본 양과 노처녀 가정교사 위트 양은 갈수록 더 의아해하며 서로에게 물었다. \"조지는 도대체 저 애한테서 무얼 보는 걸까?\""]], [["How is this? some carping reader exclaims.", "어째서 이렇단 말인가?"], ["How is it that Amelia, who had such a number of friends at school, and was so beloved there, comes out into the world and is spurned by her discriminating sex?", "트집 잡기 좋아하는 어느 독자가 이렇게 외친다. 학교에서는 그토록 많은 친구를 거느렸고 그토록 사랑받던 애밀리아가, 어째서 세상에 나오자마자 안목 높은 같은 여자들에게 퇴짜를 맞는단 말인가?"], ["My dear sir, there were no men at Miss Pinkerton's establishment except the old dancing-master; and you would not have had the girls fall out about HIM?", "친애하는 선생, 핑커튼 여사의 학원에는 늙은 무용 선생 말고는 남자라곤 없었다오. 설마 여자아이들이 그 사람을 두고 다투기를 바라셨던 건 아니겠지요?"], ["When George, their handsome brother, ran off directly after breakfast, and dined from home half-a-dozen times a week, no wonder the neglected sisters felt a little vexation.", "잘생긴 오라비 조지가 아침 식사가 끝나기 무섭게 곧장 빠져나가 일주일에 대여섯 번씩 밖에서 저녁을 먹는데, 버림받은 누이들이 다소 분이 났던 것도 무리는 아니다."], ["When young Bullock (of the firm of Hulker, Bullock & Co., Bankers, Lombard Street), who had been making up to Miss Maria the last two seasons, actually asked Amelia to dance the cotillon, could you expect that the former young lady should be pleased?", "지난 두 사교철 동안 마리아 양에게 구애해 오던 젊은 불록(롬바드 거리의 헐커 불록 상회 은행가)이 정작 애밀리아에게 코티용을 추자고 청했을 때, 앞서의 그 젊은 숙녀가 기뻐하리라 기대할 수 있었겠는가? 그런데도 그녀는, 꾸밈없고 너그러운 사람인 양 기쁘다고 말했다."], ["And yet she said she was, like an artless forgiving creature.", "\"오빠가 우리 애밀리아를 좋아해 주시니 정말 기뻐요.\""], ["\"I'm so delighted you like dear Amelia,\"", "그녀는 춤이 끝난 뒤 불록 씨에게 자못 열을 올리며 말했다."], ["she said quite eagerly to Mr. Bullock after the dance.", "\"그 애는 우리 오빠 조지와 약혼한 사이예요."], ["\"She's engaged to my brother George; there's not much in her, but she's the best-natured and most unaffected young creature: at home we're all so fond of her.\"", "별것 없는 애지만 성품 하나는 더없이 착하고 꾸밈이 없답니다. 우리 집에서는 다들 그 애를 무척 아껴요.\""], ["Dear girl!", "사랑스러운 아가씨!"], ["who can calculate the depth of affection expressed in that enthusiastic SO?", "그 열렬한 \"정말\" 속에 담긴 애정의 깊이를 누가 가늠할 수 있으랴?"]], [["Miss Wirt and these two affectionate young women so earnestly and frequently impressed upon George Osborne's mind the enormity of the sacrifice he was making, and his romantic generosity in throwing himself away upon Amelia, that I'm not sure but that he really thought he was one of the most deserving characters in the British army, and gave himself up to be loved with a good deal of easy resignation.", "위트 양과 이 다정한 두 젊은 여자는 조지 오스본의 마음에, 그가 치르고 있는 희생이 얼마나 엄청난 것인지, 또 애밀리아 같은 여자에게 자신을 내던지는 그의 낭만적인 도량이 얼마나 대단한 것인지를 어찌나 열심히 또 빈번히 새겨 넣었던지, 나는 그가 정말로 자신을 영국군 안에서 가장 자격 있는 인물 가운데 하나로 여겼고, 또 사랑받는 일에 상당히 느긋한 체념의 자세로 자신을 내맡겼던 것이 아닐까 싶다."]], [["Somehow, although he left home every morning, as was stated, and dined abroad six days in the week, when his sisters believed the infatuated youth to be at Miss Sedley's apron-strings: he was NOT always with Amelia, whilst the world supposed him at her feet.", "어찌 된 일인지, 앞서 말한 대로 그는 아침마다 집을 나섰고 일주일에 엿새를 밖에서 저녁을 먹었지만, 그 정신 나간 청년이 세들리 양의 치맛자락에 매달려 있으리라 누이들이 믿고 있을 때에도, 또 세상이 그가 그녀의 발치에 엎드려 있으리라 여기고 있을 때에도, 그는 늘 애밀리아와 함께 있는 것은 아니었다."], ["Certain it is that on more occasions than one, when Captain Dobbin called to look for his friend, Miss Osborne (who was very attentive to the Captain, and anxious to hear his military stories, and to know about the health of his dear Mamma), would laughingly point to the opposite side of the square, and say, \"Oh, you must go to the Sedleys'", "한 번이 아니라 여러 차례, 도빈 대위가 친구를 찾아왔을 때, 오스본 양은(대위에게 무척 살갑게 굴며 그의 군대 이야기를 듣고 싶어 했고 그의 사랑하는 어머니의 안부를 알고 싶어 했다) 웃으면서 광장 맞은편을 가리키며 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"어머, 조지를 찾으시려면 세들리 댁으로 가 보셔야 해요."], ["to ask for George; WE never see him from morning till night.\"", "우리는 아침부터 밤까지 그 애 얼굴 한 번 못 본답니다.\""], ["At which kind of speech the Captain would laugh in rather an absurd constrained manner, and turn off the conversation, like a consummate man of the world, to some topic of general interest, such as the Opera, the Prince's last ball at Carlton House, or the weather--that blessing to society.", "이런 말을 들으면 대위는 다소 어색하고 억지스러운 태도로 웃어넘기고는, 노련한 세상 물정 밝은 사내답게 오페라라든가, 카를턴 하우스에서 열린 황태자의 지난번 무도회라든가, 날씨라든가 하는, 사교계의 축복인 그런 일반적인 화제로 이야기를 돌리는 것이었다."]], [["\"What an innocent it is, that pet of yours,\"", "\"댁의 그 귀여운 분은 어쩌면 저렇게 순진할까.\""], ["Miss Maria would then say to Miss Jane, upon the Captain's departure.", "그러면 마리아 양은 대위가 떠난 뒤 제인 양에게 이렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["\"Did you see how he blushed at the mention of poor George on duty?\"", "\"근무 중인 가엾은 조지 이야기가 나오니까 얼굴이 발그레해지는 거 봤니?\""]], [["\"It's a pity Frederick Bullock hadn't some of his modesty, Maria,\"", "\"프레더릭 불록한테 저 사람 겸손함이 조금이라도 있었으면 좋으련만, 마리아.\""], ["replies the elder sister, with a toss of he head.", "언니가 머리를 한 번 까딱하며 대꾸했다."]], [["\"Modesty!", "\"겸손이라니!"], ["Awkwardness you mean, Jane.", "어색한 거겠지, 제인 언니."], ["I don't want Frederick to trample a hole in my muslin frock, as Captain Dobbin did in yours at Mrs. Perkins'.\"", "난 프레더릭이 퍼킨스 부인 댁에서 도빈 대위가 언니 모슬린 드레스에 그랬듯이 내 드레스를 밟아 구멍 내는 건 싫어.\""]], [["\"In YOUR frock, he, he!", "\"내 드레스에, 호호!"], ["How could he?", "그 사람이 어떻게 그래?"], ["Wasn't he dancing with Amelia?\"", "그 사람 애밀리아랑 춤추고 있지 않았어?\""]], [["The fact is, when Captain Dobbin blushed so, and looked so awkward, he remembered a circumstance of which he did not think it was necessary to inform the young ladies, viz., that he had been calling at Mr.", "사실인즉, 도빈 대위가 그토록 얼굴을 붉히고 그토록 어색해 보였던 것은, 굳이 젊은 숙녀들에게 알릴 필요는 없다고 여긴 어떤 사정을 떠올렸기 때문이었다. 즉, 그는 물론 조지를 본다는 핑계로 이미 세들리 씨 댁에 들렀었는데, 조지는 거기 없었고, 다만 응접실 창가에 다소 슬프고 애틋한 얼굴로 앉아 있는 가엾은 작은 애밀리아만 있었다는 것이다."], ["Sedley's house already, on the pretence of seeing George, of course, and George wasn't there, only poor little Amelia, with rather a sad wistful face, seated near the drawing-room window, who, after some very trifling stupid talk, ventured to ask, was there any truth in the report that the regiment was soon to be ordered abroad; and had Captain Dobbin seen Mr. Osborne that day?", "그녀는 몇 마디 시답잖고 어색한 이야기를 나눈 끝에, 연대가 곧 해외로 파병될 것이라는 소문이 사실이냐고, 또 그날 오스본 씨를 보셨느냐고 용기를 내어 물었던 것이다."]], [["The regiment was not ordered abroad as yet; and Captain Dobbin had not seen George.", "연대는 아직 해외로 파병 명령을 받지 않았고, 도빈 대위는 조지를 보지 못했다. \"아마 누이들과 함께 있을 겁니다.\""], ["\"He was with his sister, most likely,\"", "대위가 말했다. \"그 도망자를 데리러 가 볼까요?\""], ["the Captain said. \"Should he go and fetch the truant?\"", "그러자 그녀는 친절하고 고마운 마음으로 그에게 손을 내밀었다."], ["So she gave him her hand kindly and gratefully: and he crossed the square; and she waited and waited, but George never came.", "그리고 그는 광장을 가로질러 갔다. 그녀는 기다리고 또 기다렸으나, 조지는 끝내 오지 않았다."]], [["Poor little tender heart!", "가엾은 작고 여린 마음이여!"], ["and so it goes on hoping and beating, and longing and trusting.", "그렇게 그것은 끊임없이 바라고 두근거리고 갈망하고 믿는다."], ["You see it is not much of a life to describe.", "보다시피 묘사할 만한 인생이랄 것도 못 된다."], ["There is not much of what you call incident in it.", "거기에는 사람들이 사건이라 부를 만한 것도 별로 없다."], ["Only one feeling all day--when will he come?", "온종일 단 하나의 감정뿐이다. 언제쯤 그가 올까?"], ["only one thought to sleep and wake upon.", "잠들 때나 깨어날 때나 단 하나의 생각뿐이다."], ["I believe George was playing billiards with Captain Cannon in Swallow Street at the time when Amelia was asking Captain Dobbin about him; for George was a jolly sociable fellow, and excellent in all games of skill.", "짐작건대 애밀리아가 도빈 대위에게 그에 대해 묻고 있던 바로 그 시각, 조지는 스왈로 거리에서 캐넌 대위와 당구를 치고 있었을 것이다. 조지는 쾌활하고 붙임성 좋은 사내였고, 온갖 기량을 겨루는 놀이마다 뛰어났기 때문이다."]], [["Once, after three days of absence, Miss Amelia put on her bonnet, and actually invaded the Osborne house.", "한번은 사흘이나 그의 발길이 끊긴 끝에, 애밀리아 양이 모자를 쓰고 정말로 오스본 댁에 쳐들어갔다."], ["\"What!", "\"어머!"], ["leave our brother to come to us?\"", "오빠를 내버려 두고 우리한테 오다니?\""], ["said the young ladies.", "젊은 숙녀들이 말했다."], ["\"Have you had a quarrel, Amelia?", "\"둘이 다퉜니, 애밀리아?"], ["Do tell us!\"", "어서 말해 봐!\""], ["No, indeed, there had been no quarrel.", "아니, 정말이지 다툰 적 없었다."], ["\"Who could quarrel with him?\"", "\"그이와 어떻게 다툴 수가 있겠어요?\""], ["says she, with her eyes filled with tears.", "그녀가 두 눈에 눈물을 가득 머금고 말했다."], ["She only came over to--to see her dear friends; they had not met for so long.", "그저 사랑하는 친구들을 보러 왔을 뿐이었다. 너무 오래 못 만났으니까."], ["And this day she was so perfectly stupid and awkward, that the Misses Osborne and their governess, who stared after her as she went sadly away, wondered more than ever what George could see in poor little Amelia.", "그런데 이날 그녀가 어찌나 완전히 멍청하고 어색했던지, 그녀가 슬프게 떠나는 뒷모습을 빤히 바라보던 오스본 자매와 그 가정교사는 그 어느 때보다 더 의아해하며, 조지가 도대체 가엾은 작은 애밀리아에게서 무얼 본다는 걸까 궁금해했다."]], [["Of course they did.", "물론 그들은 의아해할 만했다."], ["How was she to bare that timid little heart for the inspection of those young ladies with their bold black eyes?", "그녀가 어찌 그 소심하고 작은 마음을, 대담한 검은 눈을 가진 그 젊은 숙녀들의 검열 앞에 발가벗겨 보일 수 있었겠는가?"], ["It was best that it should shrink and hide itself.", "그것은 움츠러들어 숨어 버리는 편이 나았다."], ["I know the Misses Osborne were excellent critics of a Cashmere shawl, or a pink satin slip; and when Miss Turner had hers dyed purple, and made into a spencer; and when Miss Pickford had her ermine tippet twisted into a muff and trimmings, I warrant you the changes did not escape the two intelligent young women before mentioned.", "나는 오스본 자매가 캐시미어 숄이나 분홍빛 새틴 속치마에 대해서는 빼어난 감식가였음을 안다. 터너 양이 자기 것을 자줏빛으로 물들여 짧은 재킷으로 고쳤을 때, 또 픽퍼드 양이 어민 목도리를 비틀어 토시와 장식으로 만들었을 때, 장담컨대 그 변화는 앞서 말한 두 영리한 젊은 여자의 눈을 결코 피해 가지 못했다."], ["But there are things, look you, of a finer texture than fur or satin, and all Solomon's glories, and all the wardrobe of the Queen of Sheba--things whereof the beauty escapes the eyes of many connoisseurs.", "그러나, 보시라, 모피나 새틴보다, 또 솔로몬의 온갖 영화와 시바 여왕의 온갖 옷장보다 더 고운 결을 지닌 것들이 있는 법이다. 그 아름다움이 숱한 감식가의 눈을 비껴가는 것들 말이다."], ["And there are sweet modest little souls on which you light, fragrant and blooming tenderly in quiet shady places; and there are garden-ornaments, as big as brass warming-pans, that are fit to stare the sun itself out of countenance.", "그리고 그대가 우연히 마주치는, 그윽하고 그늘진 곳에서 향기를 풍기며 다정스레 피어나는 사랑스럽고 수수한 작은 영혼들도 있는가 하면, 놋쇠 보온기만큼이나 커다래서 태양마저 무색하게 만들기에 알맞은 정원 장식물 같은 것들도 있다."], ["Miss Sedley was not of the sunflower sort; and I say it is out of the rules of all proportion to draw a violet of the size of a double dahlia.", "세들리 양은 해바라기 부류가 아니었다. 그리고 나는 겹달리아만 한 크기의 제비꽃을 그린다는 것은 온갖 균형의 법칙에 어긋나는 일이라고 말하겠다."]], [["No, indeed; the life of a good young girl who is in the paternal nest as yet, can't have many of those thrilling incidents to which the heroine of romance commonly lays claim.", "그렇다, 정말이지, 아직 부모의 둥지 안에 있는 착한 처녀의 삶에는, 소설 속 여주인공이 으레 주장하는 그런 짜릿한 사건들이 많을 수가 없다."], ["Snares or shot may take off the old birds foraging without--hawks may be abroad, from which they escape or by whom they suffer; but the young ones in the nest have a pretty comfortable unromantic sort of existence in the down and the straw, till it comes to their turn, too, to get on the wing.", "밖에서 먹이를 구하는 어미 새들은 덫이나 총탄에 목숨을 잃을 수도 있고, 그들이 피하거나 혹은 그들에게 화를 입히는 매가 하늘을 떠돌 수도 있겠지만, 둥지 안의 어린 새들은 솜털과 지푸라기 속에서 제법 안락하고 낭만이라곤 없는 삶을 누리다가, 마침내 자기네도 날개를 펼 차례가 오는 법이다."], ["While Becky Sharp was on her own wing in the country, hopping on all sorts of twigs, and amid a multiplicity of traps, and pecking up her food quite harmless and successful, Amelia lay snug in her home of Russell Square; if she went into the world, it was under the guidance of the elders; nor did it seem that any evil could befall her or that opulent cheery comfortable home in which she was affectionately sheltered.", "베키 샤프가 시골에서 제 날개로 날아다니며 온갖 잔가지 위를 폴짝거리고, 수없이 많은 덫 사이를 오가며, 멀쩡하고 솜씨 좋게 제 먹이를 쪼아 먹고 있는 동안, 애밀리아는 러셀 광장의 제 집에 아늑하게 들어앉아 있었다. 그녀가 세상에 나갈 때면 어른들의 인도를 받았고, 그녀에게도, 또 그녀가 다정스레 보호받고 있는 그 풍족하고 명랑하고 안락한 집에도 어떤 화가 닥칠 것 같지는 않았다."], ["Mamma had her morning duties, and her daily drive, and the delightful round of visits and shopping which forms the amusement, or the profession as you may call it, of the rich London lady.", "어머니에게는 아침나절의 일과가 있었고, 매일의 드라이브가 있었으며, 부유한 런던 부인의 오락 — 혹은 직업이라 불러도 좋을 — 을 이루는 저 즐거운 방문과 쇼핑의 순행이 있었다. 아버지는 시티에서 자신의 알 수 없는 사업을 꾸려 나갔다."], ["Papa conducted his mysterious operations in the City--a stirring place in those days, when war was raging all over Europe, and empires were being staked; when the \"Courier\"", "그곳은 그 시절 들끓던 곳이었으니, 전쟁이 유럽 전역에 휘몰아치고 제국들의 운명이 걸려 있던 때였다. 그때 \"쿠리에\""], ["newspaper had tens of thousands of subscribers; when one day brought you a battle of Vittoria, another a burning of Moscow, or a newsman's horn blowing down Russell Square about dinner-time, announced such a fact as--\"Battle of Leipsic--six hundred thousand men engaged--total defeat of the French--two hundred thousand killed.\"", "신문은 수만 명의 구독자를 거느렸고, 어느 날은 비토리아 전투 소식을, 또 어느 날은 모스크바 화재 소식을 전해 주었으며, 저녁 무렵이면 신문팔이의 나팔이 러셀 광장을 따라 울려 퍼지며 이런 사실을 알리는 것이었다. \"라이프치히 전투 — 육십만 병력 교전 — 프랑스군 완패 — 이십만 전사.\""], ["Old Sedley once or twice came home with a very grave face; and no wonder, when such news as this was agitating all the hearts and all the Stocks of Europe.", "노 세들리는 한두 번 매우 심각한 얼굴로 귀가했는데, 이런 소식이 유럽의 온 인심과 온 주식 시장을 뒤흔들고 있었으니 그도 무리는 아니었다."]], [["Meanwhile matters went on in Russell Square, Bloomsbury, just as if matters in Europe were not in the least disorganised.", "그러는 동안 블룸즈버리의 러셀 광장에서는, 마치 유럽의 사정이 조금도 어지럽지 않은 것처럼 일상이 흘러갔다."], ["The retreat from Leipsic made no difference in the number of meals Mr. Sambo took in the servants'", "라이프치히에서의 퇴각도 하인 방에서 샘보 씨가 먹는 끼니 수에는 아무런 차이를 내지 않았다."], ["hall; the allies poured into France, and the dinner-bell rang at five o'clock just as usual.", "동맹군이 프랑스로 쏟아져 들어가도, 저녁 식사를 알리는 종은 여느 때와 다름없이 다섯 시에 울렸다."], ["I don't think poor Amelia cared anything about Brienne and Montmirail, or was fairly interested in the war until the abdication of the Emperor; when she clapped her hands and said prayers--oh, how grateful!", "짐작건대 가엾은 애밀리아는 브리엔이나 몽미라유 따위에는 아무 관심도 없었고, 황제의 퇴위가 있기 전까지는 그 전쟁에 도무지 흥미를 느끼지 못했던 것 같다. 황제가 퇴위하자 그녀는 두 손을 마주치며 기도를 올렸다."], ["and flung herself into George Osborne's arms with all her soul, to the astonishment of everybody who witnessed that ebullition of sentiment.", "아, 어찌나 감격에 겨워했던지! 그러고는 그 감정의 격발을 목격한 모든 이가 깜짝 놀라도록, 온 영혼을 다해 조지 오스본의 품에 몸을 던졌다."], ["The fact is, peace was declared, Europe was going to be at rest; the Corsican was overthrown, and Lieutenant Osborne's regiment would not be ordered on service.", "사실인즉, 평화가 선포되었고, 유럽은 안정을 찾으려는 참이었다. 저 코르시카 사내는 타도되었고, 오스본 중위의 연대는 출정 명령을 받지 않게 될 터였다."], ["That was the way in which Miss Amelia reasoned.", "애밀리아 양이 따져 본 방식은 바로 이러했다."], ["The fate of Europe was Lieutenant George Osborne to her.", "그녀에게 유럽의 운명이란 곧 조지 오스본 중위였던 것이다."], ["His dangers being over, she sang Te Deum.", "그의 위험이 사라졌으니 그녀는 감사의 찬가를 불렀다."], ["He was her Europe: her emperor: her allied monarchs and august prince regent.", "그가 곧 그녀의 유럽이요, 그녀의 황제요, 그녀의 동맹 군주들이요, 위엄 넘치는 황태자였다."], ["He was her sun and moon; and I believe she thought the grand illumination and ball at the Mansion House, given to the sovereigns, were especially in honour of George Osborne.", "그가 그녀의 해요 달이었다. 그리고 짐작건대 그녀는, 여러 군주를 위해 맨션 하우스에서 베풀어진 그 성대한 불꽃놀이와 무도회가 특별히 조지 오스본을 기리는 것이라고 여겼던 것 같다."]], [["We have talked of shift, self, and poverty, as those dismal instructors under whom poor Miss Becky Sharp got her education.", "우리는 가엾은 베키 샤프 양이 그 아래에서 교육을 받은 저 음울한 스승들로 임기응변과 자아와 가난을 이야기한 바 있다."], ["Now, love was Miss Amelia Sedley's last tutoress, and it was amazing what progress our young lady made under that popular teacher. In the course of fifteen or eighteen months'", "그런데 애밀리아 세들리 양의 마지막 가정교사는 사랑이었고, 우리의 젊은 숙녀가 그 인기 높은 선생 아래에서 이룬 진보는 참으로 놀라운 것이었다."], ["daily and constant attention to this eminent finishing governess, what a deal of secrets Amelia learned, which Miss Wirt and the black-eyed young ladies over the way, which old Miss Pinkerton of Chiswick herself, had no cognizance of!", "이 빼어난 교양 가정교사에게 열다섯 달이며 열여덟 달이 넘도록 날마다 한결같이 마음을 기울인 끝에, 애밀리아는 위트 양도, 또 길 건너편의 그 검은 눈을 가진 젊은 숙녀들도, 또 치즈윅의 늙은 핑커튼 여사 본인도 도무지 알지 못하는 그 얼마나 많은 비밀을 깨우쳤던가! 하기야 그 새침하고 평판 좋은 처녀들 가운데 누가 그런 것을 알 수 있었겠는가?"], ["As, indeed, how should any of those prim and reputable virgins? With Misses P.", "핑커튼 양과 위트 양으로 말하자면, 그 다정한 정념이란 애초에 논외였다."], ["and W. the tender passion is out of the question: I would not dare to breathe such an idea regarding them.", "나는 감히 그들에 대해 그런 생각을 입에 담을 엄두조차 내지 못하겠다."], ["Miss Maria Osborne, it is true, was \"attached\" to Mr.", "마리아 오스본 양이 헐커 불록 앤드 불록 상회의 프레더릭 오거스터스 불록 씨에게 \"마음을 두고\""], ["Frederick Augustus Bullock, of the firm of Hulker, Bullock & Bullock; but hers was a most respectable attachment, and she would have taken Bullock Senior just the same, her mind being fixed--as that of a well-bred young woman should be--upon a house in Park Lane, a country house at Wimbledon, a handsome chariot, and two prodigious tall horses and footmen, and a fourth of the annual profits of the eminent firm of Hulker & Bullock, all of which advantages were represented in the person of Frederick Augustus.", "있었던 것은 사실이지만, 그녀의 것은 더없이 존경할 만한 종류의 애정이어서, 만약 그녀의 마음이 — 교양 있는 젊은 여자의 마음이 으레 그래야 하듯 — 파크 레인의 저택, 윔블던의 시골 별장, 멋진 사륜마차, 엄청나게 키 큰 말 두 필과 하인들, 그리고 명망 높은 헐커 앤드 불록 상회의 연간 수익 사분의 일에 단단히 고정되어 있는 한, 그녀는 늙은 불록을 데려가도 똑같이 좋아했을 것이다. 그 온갖 이점들이 프레더릭 오거스터스라는 사람으로 대표되었던 것이다."], ["Had orange blossoms been invented then (those touching emblems of female purity imported by us from France, where people's daughters are universally sold in marriage), Miss Maria, I say, would have assumed the spotless wreath, and stepped into the travelling carriage by the side of gouty, old, bald-headed, bottle-nosed Bullock Senior; and devoted her beautiful existence to his happiness with perfect modesty--only the old gentleman was married already; so she bestowed her young affections on the junior partner.", "만약 그 시절에 오렌지 꽃이 발명되어 있었더라면(처녀의 순결을 나타내는 저 가슴 뭉클한 상징물로, 딸들이 너 나 할 것 없이 혼인으로 팔려 가는 프랑스에서 우리가 수입해 온 것이다), 단언컨대 마리아 양은 그 티 없는 화관을 쓰고, 통풍에 걸린 늙고 대머리에 코끝이 붉은 늙은 불록 곁에 나란히 여행용 마차에 올랐을 것이며, 더없이 정숙한 자태로 그 아름다운 평생을 그의 행복에 바쳤을 것이다. 다만 그 노신사는 이미 기혼이었던 까닭에, 그녀는 자기 젊은 애정을 젊은 동업자에게 베풀었던 것이다."], ["Sweet, blooming, orange flowers!", "사랑스럽고 활짝 핀 오렌지 꽃이여!"], ["The other day I saw Miss Trotter (that was), arrayed in them, trip into the travelling carriage at St. George's, Hanover Square, and Lord Methuselah hobbled in after.", "며칠 전에 나는 (한때) 트로터 양이었던 분이 그 꽃을 두르고 하노버 광장의 세인트조지 교회에서 여행용 마차로 사뿐히 올라타는 것을 보았는데, 그 뒤로 므두셀라 경이 절뚝거리며 따라 들어갔다."], ["With what an engaging modesty she pulled down the blinds of the chariot--the dear innocent!", "그녀가 어찌나 사람 마음을 끄는 정숙한 태도로 마차의 차양을 끌어 내리던지 — 그 사랑스러운 순진한 분!"], ["There were half the carriages of Vanity Fair at the wedding.", "그 결혼식에는 허영의 시장 마차의 절반은 모여 있었다."]], [["This was not the sort of love that finished Amelia's education; and in the course of a year turned a good young girl into a good young woman--to be a good wife presently, when the happy time should come.", "이런 종류의 사랑이 애밀리아의 교육을 완성하고, 일 년 사이에 착한 처녀를 착한 젊은 여인으로 — 머지않아 행복한 시절이 오면 착한 아내가 될 사람으로 — 탈바꿈시킨 것은 아니었다."], ["This young person (perhaps it was very imprudent in her parents to encourage her, and abet her in such idolatry and silly romantic ideas) loved, with all her heart, the young officer in His Majesty's service with whom we have made a brief acquaintance.", "이 젊은 사람은(어쩌면 그녀의 부모가 그런 우상 숭배와 어리석고 낭만적인 생각을 부추기고 거든 것은 매우 경솔한 일이었는지도 모른다) 우리가 잠시 알게 된 그 폐하의 군대에 복무하는 젊은 장교를 온 마음을 다해 사랑했다."], ["She thought about him the very first moment on waking; and his was the very last name mentioned in her prayers.", "그녀는 잠에서 깨는 바로 그 첫 순간 그를 생각했고, 기도 가운데 가장 마지막으로 부르는 이름도 그의 것이었다."], ["She never had seen a man so beautiful or so clever: such a figure on horseback: such a dancer: such a hero in general.", "그녀는 그토록 잘생기고 그토록 영리한 남자를 본 적이 없었다. 말 위에서의 그토록 멋진 자태, 그토록 빼어난 춤꾼, 한마디로 그토록 대단한 영웅을 말이다."], ["Talk of the Prince's bow!", "황태자의 인사 솜씨를 말한들 무엇하랴!"], ["what was it to George's? She had seen Mr.", "그것이 조지의 것에 비하면 무엇이란 말인가?"], ["Brummell, whom everybody praised so.", "그녀는 다들 그토록 칭송하는 브러멜 씨도 본 적이 있었다."], ["Compare such a person as that to her George!", "그런 사람을 그녀의 조지에 견주다니!"], ["Not amongst all the beaux at the Opera (and there were beaux in those days with actual opera hats) was there any one to equal him.", "오페라의 모든 멋쟁이들 가운데에서도(그 시절에는 진짜 오페라 모자를 쓴 멋쟁이들이 있었다) 그에 견줄 만한 이는 하나도 없었다."], ["He was only good enough to be a fairy prince; and oh, what magnanimity to stoop to such a humble Cinderella!", "그는 동화 속 왕자가 되기에 딱 알맞을 만큼만 훌륭했다. 그러니 오, 그토록 미천한 신데렐라에게 몸을 굽히다니, 그 얼마나 큰 도량인가!"], ["Miss Pinkerton would have tried to check this blind devotion very likely, had she been Amelia's confidante; but not with much success, depend upon it.", "만약 핑커튼 여사가 애밀리아의 속내를 털어놓는 상대였더라면, 십중팔구 이 맹목적인 헌신을 막아 보려 했을 것이다. 그러나, 장담컨대, 별 성과는 없었을 것이다."], ["It is in the nature and instinct of some women.", "그것은 어떤 여자들의 천성이요 본능이기 때문이다."], ["Some are made to scheme, and some to love; and I wish any respected bachelor that reads this may take the sort that best likes him.", "어떤 여자들은 책략을 꾸미도록 만들어졌고, 어떤 여자들은 사랑하도록 만들어졌다. 그러니 이 글을 읽는 존경하는 독신 신사가 있다면, 부디 자기에게 가장 잘 맞는 쪽을 고르시기를 바란다."]], [["While under this overpowering impression, Miss Amelia neglected her twelve dear friends at Chiswick most cruelly, as such selfish people commonly will do.", "이 압도적인 감흥에 사로잡혀 있는 동안, 애밀리아 양은 그런 이기적인 사람들이 으레 그러하듯, 치즈윅의 그 사랑하는 열두 친구를 더없이 매정하게 소홀히 했다. 물론 그녀에게는 생각할 거리라곤 이 한 가지 주제뿐이었다."], ["She had but this subject, of course, to think about; and Miss Saltire was too cold for a confidante, and she couldn't bring her mind to tell Miss Swartz, the woolly-haired young heiress from St.", "그런데 솔타이어 양은 속내를 털어놓기에는 너무 차가웠고, 세인트키츠 출신의 곱슬머리 젊은 상속녀 스워츠 양에게는 도무지 마음이 내키지 않았다. 그녀는 방학 동안 어린 로라 마틴을 집에 데리고 있었다."], ["Kitt's. She had little Laura Martin home for the holidays; and my belief is, she made a confidante of her, and promised that Laura should come and live with her when she was married, and gave Laura a great deal of information regarding the passion of love, which must have been singularly useful and novel to that little person.", "그래서 내 생각에 그녀는 로라를 속내 털어놓는 상대로 삼아, 자기가 결혼하면 로라가 와서 함께 살게 해 주겠다고 약속하고, 사랑이라는 정념에 관해 로라에게 숱한 정보를 일러 주었던 것 같다."], ["Alas, alas!", "그것은 그 어린 사람에게 유달리 유익하고 신기한 일이었음에 틀림없다."], ["I fear poor Emmy had not a well-regulated mind.", "아아, 슬프도다! 가엾은 에미는 정돈된 마음을 지니지 못했던 것 같아 걱정스럽다."]], [["What were her parents doing, not to keep this little heart from beating so fast?", "이 작은 가슴이 그토록 빠르게 두근거리지 않도록 단속하기는커녕, 그녀의 부모는 대체 무얼 하고 있었단 말인가? 노 세들리는 사정을 그다지 눈여겨보지 않는 듯했다."], ["Old Sedley did not seem much to notice matters.", "그는 근래 들어 더 심각해졌고, 시티의 사업에 정신이 팔려 있었다."], ["He was graver of late, and his City affairs absorbed him. Mrs.", "세들리 부인은 워낙 태평하고 캐묻기 싫어하는 성미여서 질투조차 하지 않았다."], ["Sedley was of so easy and uninquisitive a nature that she wasn't even jealous. Mr.", "조스 씨는 첼튼엄에서 어느 아일랜드 과부에게 포위 공격을 당하며 집을 비우고 있었다."], ["Jos was away, being besieged by an Irish widow at Cheltenham.", "애밀리아는 집을 독차지하다시피 했다. 아, 때로는 너무 독차지했다!"], ["Amelia had the house to herself--ah!", "그렇다고 그녀가 의심을 품었다는 말은 아니다."], ["too much to herself sometimes--not that she ever doubted; for, to be sure, George must be at the Horse Guards; and he can't always get leave from Chatham; and he must see his friends and sisters, and mingle in society when in town (he, such an ornament to every society!); and when he is with the regiment, he is too tired to write long letters.", "왜냐하면, 그야 물론, 조지는 근위 사령부에 가 있을 게 분명하고, 채텀에서 늘 휴가를 얻을 수 있는 것도 아니며, 시내에 있을 때면 친구들과 누이들을 만나고 사교계에 어울려야 마땅하니까(그토록 어느 모임에서나 빛나는 사람이!). 또 연대에 있을 때면 너무 지쳐서 긴 편지를 쓸 수 없는 것이니까."], ["I know where she kept that packet she had--and can steal in and out of her chamber like Iachimo--like Iachimo?", "나는 그녀가 가지고 있던 그 편지 꾸러미를 어디에 간직했는지 안다. 그리고 야키모처럼 그녀의 침실을 몰래 드나들 수도 있다."], ["No--that is a bad part.", "야키모처럼? 아니, 그건 못된 역할이다."], ["I will only act Moonshine, and peep harmless into the bed where faith and beauty and innocence lie dreaming.", "나는 그저 달빛 역할만 맡아, 신의와 아름다움과 순결이 누워 꿈꾸고 있는 그 침상을 해롭잖게 들여다보기만 하겠다."]], [["But if Osborne's were short and soldierlike letters, it must be confessed, that were Miss Sedley's letters to Mr.", "그러나 오스본의 편지가 짧고 군인다운 것이었다 해도, 이것만은 인정해야겠다."], ["Osborne to be published, we should have to extend this novel to such a multiplicity of volumes as not the most sentimental reader could support; that she not only filled sheets of large paper, but crossed them with the most astonishing perverseness; that she wrote whole pages out of poetry-books without the least pity; that she underlined words and passages with quite a frantic emphasis; and, in fine, gave the usual tokens of her condition.", "만약 세들리 양이 오스본 씨에게 보낸 편지들을 출판한다면, 우리는 이 소설을 어찌나 많은 권수로 늘려야 할지, 아무리 감상적인 독자라도 견디지 못할 지경이 될 것이다. 그녀는 큼직한 종이를 빼곡히 채웠을 뿐 아니라, 더없이 놀라운 고집으로 그 위에 가로로 겹쳐 썼고, 시집에서 통째로 몇 쪽씩이나 조금도 가차 없이 베껴 적었으며, 거의 광적인 강조로 낱말과 구절에 밑줄을 그었고, 한마디로 자기 처지를 드러내는 온갖 징표를 다 보여 주었다."], ["She wasn't a heroine.", "그녀는 여주인공이 아니었다."], ["Her letters were full of repetition.", "그녀의 편지는 같은 말의 반복으로 가득했다."], ["She wrote rather doubtful grammar sometimes, and in her verses took all sorts of liberties with the metre.", "때로는 다소 미심쩍은 문법으로 글을 썼고, 시구에서는 운율을 두고 온갖 자유를 누렸다."], ["But oh, mesdames, if you are not allowed to touch the heart sometimes in spite of syntax, and are not to be loved until you all know the difference between trimeter and tetrameter, may all Poetry go to the deuce, and every schoolmaster perish miserably!", "그러나 오, 부인들이여, 만약 그대들이 통사론에도 불구하고 이따금 사람의 마음을 울리는 것이 허락되지 않는다면, 또 그대들 모두가 삼음보와 사음보의 차이를 알기 전에는 사랑받지 못한다면, 모든 시 따위는 악마에게나 가 버리고, 모든 학교 선생은 비참하게 스러져 버려라!"]], [["Sentimental and Otherwise\nI fear the gentleman to whom Miss Amelia's letters were addressed was rather an obdurate critic.", "애밀리아 양의 편지를 받는 그 신사는, 안타깝게도 다소 무정한 비평가였던 모양이다."], ["Such a number of notes followed Lieutenant Osborne about the country, that he became almost ashamed of the jokes of his mess-room companions regarding them, and ordered his servant never to deliver them except at his private apartment.", "어찌나 많은 편지가 시골 곳곳으로 오스본 중위를 따라다녔던지, 그는 그 편지들을 두고 떠들어대는 식당 동료들의 농담이 거의 부끄러울 지경이 되어, 하인에게 절대로 자기 사실(私室) 말고는 편지를 전하지 말라고 일렀다."], ["He was seen lighting his cigar with one, to the horror of Captain Dobbin, who, it is my belief, would have given a bank-note for the document.", "그가 그중 한 통으로 시가에 불을 붙이는 것이 목격되어 도빈 대위를 경악하게 했는데, 내 생각에 도빈은 그 편지 한 장을 손에 넣을 수만 있다면 지폐 한 장이라도 내놓았을 것이다."]], [["For some time George strove to keep the liaison a secret.", "한동안 조지는 그 관계를 비밀로 하려 애썼다."], ["There was a woman in the case, that he admitted.", "한 여자가 얽혀 있다는 것만은 그도 인정했다."], ["\"And not the first either,\"", "\"그것도 처음이 아니라니까.\""], ["said Ensign Spooney to Ensign Stubble.", "소위 스푸니가 소위 스터블에게 말했다."], ["\"That Osborne's a devil of a fellow.", "\"그 오스본이란 작자, 보통내기가 아니야."], ["There was a judge's daughter at Demerara went almost mad about him; then there was that beautiful quadroon girl, Miss Pye, at St.", "데메라라에서는 어떤 판사 딸이 그놈한테 빠져서 거의 미칠 지경이 됐다더군. 그리고 너도 알다시피 세인트빈센트에는 그 아름다운 혼혈 아가씨 파이 양이 있었고."], ["Vincent's, you know; and since he's been home, they say he's a regular Don Giovanni, by Jove.\"", "게다가 본국에 돌아온 뒤로는, 글쎄 영락없는 돈 조반니라잖아, 정말이지.\""]], [["Stubble and Spooney thought that to be a \"regular Don Giovanni, by Jove\" was one of the finest qualities a man could possess, and Osborne's reputation was prodigious amongst the young men of the regiment.", "스터블과 스푸니는 \"정말이지 영락없는 돈 조반니\"라는 것이야말로 사내가 가질 수 있는 가장 멋진 자질 가운데 하나라고 여겼고, 그래서 연대의 젊은 친구들 사이에서 오스본의 명성은 대단한 것이었다."], ["He was famous in field-sports, famous at a song, famous on parade; free with his money, which was bountifully supplied by his father.", "그는 야외 경기로 이름났고, 노래로 이름났으며, 사열장에서도 이름났다. 게다가 아버지가 넉넉하게 대주는 돈을 아낌없이 썼다."], ["His coats were better made than any man's in the regiment, and he had more of them.", "그의 코트는 연대의 누구 것보다도 잘 지어졌고, 또 누구보다도 많았다."], ["He was adored by the men.", "그는 부하들에게 흠모의 대상이었다."], ["He could drink more than any officer of the whole mess, including old Heavytop, the colonel.", "연대 식당의 어떤 장교보다도, 늙은 대령 헤비탑까지 포함해서도, 그가 술을 더 많이 마실 수 있었다."], ["He could spar better than Knuckles, the private (who would have been a corporal but for his drunkenness, and who had been in the prize-ring); and was the best batter and bowler, out and out, of the regimental club.", "권투 시합 무대에 섰던 적도 있고 술버릇만 아니었으면 하사가 됐을 사병 너클스보다도 권투를 더 잘했다. 연대 클럽에서는 단연 최고의 타자요 투수였다."], ["He rode his own horse, Greased Lightning, and won the Garrison cup at Quebec races.", "그리고 자기 말 '기름칠한 번개'를 타고 퀘벡 경마에서 수비대 컵을 따냈다."], ["There were other people besides Amelia who worshipped him.", "애밀리아 말고도 그를 숭배하는 사람은 또 있었다."], ["Stubble and Spooney thought him a sort of Apollo; Dobbin took him to be an Admirable Crichton; and Mrs. Major O'Dowd acknowledged he was an elegant young fellow, and put her in mind of Fitzjurld Fogarty, Lord Castlefogarty's second son.", "스터블과 스푸니는 그를 일종의 아폴론으로 여겼고, 도빈은 그를 흠잡을 데 없는 만능인 크라이턴으로 생각했으며, 오다우드 소령 부인은 그가 우아한 청년이라고 인정하며 캐슬포가티 경의 차남 피츠제럴드 포가티를 떠올리게 한다고 말했다."]], [["Well, Stubble and Spooney and the rest indulged in most romantic conjectures regarding this female correspondent of Osborne's--opining that it was a Duchess in London who was in love with him--or that it was a General's daughter, who was engaged to somebody else, and madly attached to him--or that it was a Member of Parliament's lady, who proposed four horses and an elopement--or that it was some other victim of a passion delightfully exciting, romantic, and disgraceful to all parties, on none of which conjectures would Osborne throw the least light, leaving his young admirers and friends to invent and arrange their whole history.", "자, 스터블과 스푸니와 그 나머지 패거리는 오스본의 그 여자 편지 상대를 두고 더없이 낭만적인 억측을 즐겼다. 그를 사랑하는 런던의 어느 공작부인이라거나, 다른 누군가와 약혼한 처지이면서 그에게 미친 듯이 빠진 어느 장군의 딸이라거나, 말 네 필과 야반도주를 제안한 어느 의원의 부인이라거나, 아니면 더없이 짜릿하고 낭만적이며 모든 당사자에게 수치스러운 그 어떤 격정의 또 다른 희생자라거나 하는 것이었다. 그러나 오스본은 그 어느 억측에도 일말의 단서조차 흘리지 않았고, 그리하여 그의 젊은 숭배자들과 친구들은 그 사연 전부를 제멋대로 지어내고 꾸며 맞춰야 했다."]], [["And the real state of the case would never have been known at all in the regiment but for Captain Dobbin's indiscretion.", "그리고 도빈 대위의 경솔한 입놀림만 아니었다면, 일의 실상은 연대 안에서 끝내 알려지지 않았을 것이다."], ["The Captain was eating his breakfast one day in the mess-room, while Cackle, the assistant-surgeon, and the two above-named worthies were speculating upon Osborne's intrigue--Stubble holding out that the lady was a Duchess about Queen Charlotte's court, and Cackle vowing she was an opera-singer of the worst reputation.", "어느 날 대위가 식당에서 아침을 먹고 있는데, 군의관 보조 캐클과 앞서 말한 두 호걸이 오스본의 밀애를 두고 추측을 늘어놓고 있었다. 스터블은 그 부인이 샬럿 왕비 궁정의 어느 공작부인이라고 우겼고, 캐클은 그녀가 최악의 평판을 지닌 오페라 가수라고 장담했다."], ["At this idea Dobbin became so moved, that though his mouth was full of eggs and bread-and-butter at the time, and though he ought not to have spoken at all, yet he couldn't help blurting out, \"Cackle, you're a stupid fool.", "이 말에 도빈은 어찌나 동요했던지, 마침 입안에 달걀과 버터 바른 빵이 가득해 도무지 입을 열 처지가 아니었는데도, 그만 불쑥 내뱉지 않을 수 없었다. \"캐클, 자네 멍청한 바보로군."], ["You're always talking nonsense and scandal.", "늘 헛소리에 험담만 늘어놓는다니까."], ["Osborne is not going to run off with a Duchess or ruin a milliner.", "오스본은 공작부인과 달아날 작자도, 모자 가게 아가씨를 망칠 작자도 아니야."], ["Miss Sedley is one of the most charming young women that ever lived.", "세들리 양은 이제껏 살았던 가장 매력적인 젊은 여성 가운데 한 분일세."], ["He's been engaged to her ever so long; and the man who calls her names had better not do so in my hearing.\"", "오래전부터 그분과 약혼한 사이라고. 그러니 그분을 헐뜯는 자는 내 귀에 들리는 데서는 그러지 않는 게 좋을 거야.\""], ["With which, turning exceedingly red, Dobbin ceased speaking, and almost choked himself with a cup of tea.", "그 말과 함께 도빈은 얼굴이 새빨개져서 입을 다물었고, 차 한 잔에 거의 목이 멜 뻔했다."], ["The story was over the regiment in half-an-hour; and that very evening Mrs.", "그 이야기는 삼십 분 만에 온 연대에 쫙 퍼졌다."], ["Major O'Dowd wrote off to her sister Glorvina at O'Dowdstown not to hurry from Dublin--young Osborne being prematurely engaged already.", "그리고 바로 그날 저녁, 오다우드 소령 부인은 오다우즈타운에 있는 동생 글로비나에게 더블린에서 서둘러 올 것 없다고 편지를 써 보냈다. 젊은 오스본이 이미 진작에 약혼한 몸이라는 것이었다."]], [["She complimented the Lieutenant in an appropriate speech over a glass of whisky-toddy that evening, and he went home perfectly furious to quarrel with Dobbin (who had declined Mrs.", "그날 저녁 부인은 위스키 토디 한 잔을 들며 적절한 인사말로 중위를 추켜세웠고, 그는 자기 비밀을 누설한 죄로 도빈과 한판 다투려고 잔뜩 화가 나서 집으로 돌아갔다(도빈은 오다우드 소령 부인의 파티를 사양하고 제 방에 앉아 플루트를 불고 있었는데, 내가 알기로는 자못 우울한 투로 시까지 짓고 있었다)."], ["Major O'Dowd's party, and sat in his own room playing the flute, and, I believe, writing poetry in a very melancholy manner)--to quarrel with Dobbin for betraying his secret.", "도빈이 자기 비밀을 발설한 데 따지러 간 것이었다."]], [["\"Who the deuce asked you to talk about my affairs?\"", "\"대체 누가 자네더러 내 일을 떠들어대라고 했나?\""], ["Osborne shouted indignantly.", "오스본이 분개하여 소리쳤다."], ["\"Why the devil is all the regiment to know that I am going to be married?", "\"내가 결혼할 거라는 걸 온 연대가 다 알아야 할 까닭이 뭐냐고?"], ["Why is that tattling old harridan, Peggy O'Dowd, to make free with my name at her d--d supper-table, and advertise my engagement over the three kingdoms?", "그 수다쟁이 늙은 마귀할멈 페기 오다우드가 그 빌어먹을 만찬 식탁에서 내 이름을 함부로 입에 올리고, 세 왕국 천지에 내 약혼을 광고하고 다니는 게 말이 되나?"], ["After all, what right have you to say I am engaged, or to meddle in my business at all, Dobbin?\"", "따지고 보면, 자네가 무슨 권리로 내가 약혼했다고 떠들고, 도무지 무슨 권리로 내 일에 참견하나, 도빈?\""]], [["\"It seems to me,\"", "\"내가 보기엔 말일세.\""], ["Captain Dobbin began.", "도빈 대위가 입을 떼었다."]], [["\"Seems be hanged, Dobbin,\"", "\"보기엔이고 뭐고, 집어치우게, 도빈.\""], ["his junior interrupted him.", "후배가 그의 말을 가로막았다."], ["\"I am under obligations to you, I know it, a d--d deal too well too; but I won't be always sermonised by you because you're five years my senior.", "\"내가 자네한테 신세진 거 알아. 그것도 빌어먹을 정도로 잘 알지."], ["I'm hanged if I'll stand your airs of superiority and infernal pity and patronage.", "하지만 자네가 나보다 다섯 살 위라고 해서 노상 설교나 듣고 있지는 않겠네. 자네의 그 잘난 척이며 빌어먹을 동정이며 생색이며, 더는 못 참아."], ["Pity and patronage!", "동정이라니, 생색이라니!"], ["I should like to know in what I'm your inferior?\"", "내가 자네보다 못한 게 대체 뭔지 알고 싶군.\""]], [["\"Are you engaged?\"", "\"자네 약혼했나?\""], ["Captain Dobbin interposed.", "도빈 대위가 끼어들었다."]], [["\"What the devil's that to you or any one here if I am?\"", "\"내가 약혼했든 말든 그게 자네나 여기 누구하고 무슨 상관인가?\""]], [["\"Are you ashamed of it?\"", "\"부끄러운가?\""], ["Dobbin resumed.", "도빈이 다시 물었다."]], [["\"What right have you to ask me that question, sir?", "\"무슨 권리로 그런 걸 묻나, 응?"], ["I should like to know,\"", "정말이지 알고 싶군.\""], ["George said.", "조지가 말했다."]], [["\"Good God, you don't mean to say you want to break off?\"", "\"맙소사, 설마 파혼하고 싶다는 말은 아니겠지?\""], ["asked Dobbin, starting up.", "도빈이 벌떡 일어서며 물었다."]], [["\"In other words, you ask me if I'm a man of honour,\"", "\"바꿔 말하면, 내가 명예를 아는 사내냐고 묻는 거로군.\""], ["said Osborne, fiercely; \"is that what you mean?", "오스본이 사납게 말했다. \"그게 자네 말뜻인가?"], ["You've adopted such a tone regarding me lately that I'm ------ if I'll bear it any more.\"", "요즘 자네는 나를 대하는 투가 영 그래서, 더는 못 참겠네.\""]], [["\"What have I done?", "\"내가 뭘 어쨌다고?"], ["I've told you you were neglecting a sweet girl, George.", "자네가 어여쁜 아가씨를 소홀히 하고 있다고 했을 뿐이야, 조지."], ["I've told you that when you go to town you ought to go to her, and not to the gambling-houses about St. James's.\"", "런던에 가거든 세인트제임스 근처 도박장이 아니라 그 아가씨한테 가야 한다고 했을 뿐이라고.\""]], [["\"You want your money back, I suppose,\"", "\"돈을 돌려받고 싶은 모양이지.\""], ["said George, with a sneer.", "조지가 비웃으며 말했다."]], [["\"Of course I do--I always did, didn't I?\"", "\"물론 돌려받고 싶지. 늘 그랬지, 안 그런가?\""], ["says Dobbin.", "도빈이 말했다."], ["\"You speak like a generous fellow.\"", "\"참 너그러운 친구로구먼.\""]], [["\"No, hang it, William, I beg your pardon\"--here George interposed in a fit of remorse; \"you have been my friend in a hundred ways, Heaven knows. You've got me out of a score of scrapes. When Crawley of the Guards won that sum of money of me I should have been done but for you: I know I should. But you shouldn't deal so hardly with me; you shouldn't be always catechising me. I am very fond of Amelia; I adore her, and that sort of thing. Don't look angry. She's faultless; I know she is. But you see there's no fun in winning a thing unless you play for it. Hang it: the regiment's just back from the West Indies, I must have a little fling, and then when I'm married I'll reform; I will upon my honour, now. And--I say--Dob--don't be angry with me, and I'll give you a hundred next month, when I know my father will stand something handsome; and I'll ask Heavytop for leave, and I'll go to town, and see Amelia to-morrow--there now, will that satisfy you?\"", "\"아니, 젠장, 윌리엄, 내가 잘못했네.\" 여기서 조지가 후회에 사로잡혀 끼어들었다. \"자넨 백 가지로 내 친구였어, 하늘이 알지. 숱한 곤경에서 날 건져줬고. 근위대 크롤리가 나한테서 그 돈을 따갔을 때, 자네 아니었으면 난 끝장났을 거야. 그건 나도 알아. 정말이라고. 하지만 자네도 날 그렇게 모질게 다루면 안 돼. 노상 나를 캐묻고 따지면 안 된다고. 난 애밀리아가 무척 좋아. 그녀를 흠모해, 그런 거란 말이야. 그렇게 화난 얼굴 하지 말게. 그녀는 흠잡을 데 없어. 나도 알아. 하지만 알다시피, 따려고 겨뤄보지 않으면 뭔가를 손에 넣어도 재미가 없는 법이거든. 젠장, 연대가 이제 막 서인도제도에서 돌아왔으니, 나도 좀 놀아야겠네. 그러고 나서 결혼하면 마음을 고쳐먹지. 명예를 걸고 그러겠네, 정말로. 그리고 말이야, 도브, 나한테 화내지 말게. 다음 달에 백 파운드 줄게. 아버지가 후하게 한몫 떼주실 게 분명하니까. 헤비탑한테 휴가를 청해서 런던에 가, 내일 애밀리아를 보러 가겠네. 자, 그러면 됐나?\""]], [["\"It is impossible to be long angry with you, George,\"", "\"자네한테는 오래 화내고 있을 수가 없다니까, 조지.\""], ["said the good-natured Captain; \"and as for the money, old boy, you know if I wanted it you'd share your last shilling with me.\"", "사람 좋은 대위가 말했다. \"그리고 돈으로 말하면, 이 친구야, 내가 만약 돈이 궁하면 자네가 마지막 한 푼까지 나눠주리란 걸 알지 않나.\""]], [["\"That I would, by Jove, Dobbin,\"", "\"암, 그렇고말고, 도빈.\""], ["George said, with the greatest generosity, though by the way he never had any money to spare.", "조지가 더없이 너그러운 태도로 말했다. 하긴 그에게는 남에게 떼줄 만한 돈이 있어본 적이 없었지만 말이다."]], [["\"Only I wish you had sown those wild oats of yours, George.", "\"다만 자네가 그 방종한 짓들을 어서 끝냈으면 하는 마음뿐일세, 조지."], ["If you could have seen poor little Miss Emmy's face when she asked me about you the other day, you would have pitched those billiard-balls to the deuce.", "며칠 전 가엾은 어린 에미 양이 자네 안부를 물을 때 그 얼굴을 자네가 봤더라면, 그 당구공들일랑 진작에 내던졌을 걸세. 어서 가서 그녀를 위로해주게, 이 못된 친구야."], ["Go and comfort her, you rascal.", "가서 긴 편지를 써."], ["Go and write her a long letter.", "그녀를 행복하게 해줄 무언가를 하라고."], ["Do something to make her happy; a very little will.\"", "아주 사소한 것으로도 충분할 테니.\""]], [["\"I believe she's d--d fond of me,\"", "\"내 보기에 그녀는 날 끔찍이도 좋아하지.\""], ["the Lieutenant said, with a self-satisfied air; and went off to finish the evening with some jolly fellows in the mess-room.", "중위가 흡족한 표정으로 말했다. 그러고는 식당의 흥겨운 친구들과 어울려 저녁을 마저 보내려고 자리를 떴다."]], [["Amelia meanwhile, in Russell Square, was looking at the moon, which was shining upon that peaceful spot, as well as upon the square of the Chatham barracks, where Lieutenant Osborne was quartered, and thinking to herself how her hero was employed.", "한편 그 무렵 애밀리아는 러셀 광장에서 달을 바라보고 있었다. 그 평화로운 곳을 비추는 달은, 오스본 중위가 주둔한 채텀 병영의 연병장에도 똑같이 비치고 있었다."], ["Perhaps he is visiting the sentries, thought she; perhaps he is bivouacking; perhaps he is attending the couch of a wounded comrade, or studying the art of war up in his own desolate chamber.", "그녀는 자기 영웅이 지금 무엇을 하고 있을까 홀로 생각에 잠겼다. 보초들을 둘러보고 있을지도 몰라, 그녀는 생각했다."], ["And her kind thoughts sped away as if they were angels and had wings, and flying down the river to Chatham and Rochester, strove to peep into the barracks where George was.", "야영하고 있을지도, 다친 전우의 자리를 지켜주고 있을지도, 아니면 자기 쓸쓸한 방에서 병법을 연구하고 있을지도 몰라. 그러자 그녀의 다정한 상념은 천사라도 되어 날개를 단 듯 멀리 날아가, 강을 따라 채텀과 로체스터로 내려가 조지가 있는 병영을 들여다보려 안간힘을 썼다……"], [". .", "하지만 이런저런 사정을 헤아려보건대, 문이 닫혀 있고 보초가 아무도 들이지 않은 것이 오히려 다행이었지 싶다."], [". All things considered, I think it was as well the gates were shut, and the sentry allowed no one to pass; so that the poor little white-robed angel could not hear the songs those young fellows were roaring over the whisky-punch.", "덕분에 그 가엾고 자그마한 흰옷 입은 천사는, 그 젊은 친구들이 위스키 펀치를 들이켜며 고래고래 부르던 노래를 듣지 않을 수 있었으니 말이다."]], [["The day after the little conversation at Chatham barracks, young Osborne, to show that he would be as good as his word, prepared to go to town, thereby incurring Captain Dobbin's applause.", "채텀 병영에서 그 짤막한 대화가 오간 다음 날, 젊은 오스본은 자기가 한 말을 지키겠다는 것을 보여주려고 런던에 갈 채비를 했고, 그리하여 도빈 대위의 갈채를 받았다."], ["\"I should have liked to make her a little present,\"", "\"그녀한테 자그마한 선물이라도 해주고 싶은데 말이야.\""], ["Osborne said to his friend in confidence, \"only I am quite out of cash until my father tips up.\"", "오스본이 친구에게 은근히 털어놓았다. \"아버지가 한몫 챙겨주실 때까지는 완전히 빈털터리라서.\""], ["But Dobbin would not allow this good nature and generosity to be balked, and so accommodated Mr.", "그러나 도빈은 이 선량함과 너그러움이 좌절되도록 내버려두지 않고, 오스본에게 지폐 몇 장을 마련해주었다."], ["Osborne with a few pound notes, which the latter took after a little faint scruple.", "오스본은 짐짓 한번 망설이는 시늉을 한 뒤 그것을 받았다."]], [["And I dare say he would have bought something very handsome for Amelia; only, getting off the coach in Fleet Street, he was attracted by a handsome shirt-pin in a jeweller's window, which he could not resist; and having paid for that, had very little money to spare for indulging in any further exercise of kindness.", "내 짐작에 그는 애밀리아에게 무척 근사한 것을 사주었을 법도 하다. 다만 플리트 거리에서 마차를 내리다가 어느 보석상 진열창의 멋진 셔츠핀에 마음을 빼앗겨 도무지 뿌리칠 수가 없었고, 그 값을 치르고 나니 더 이상의 친절을 베풀 만한 돈이 거의 남지 않았던 것이다."], ["Never mind: you may be sure it was not his presents Amelia wanted.", "하지만 상관없다. 애밀리아가 원한 것이 그의 선물이 아니었음은 틀림없으니 말이다."], ["When he came to Russell Square, her face lighted up as if he had been sunshine.", "그가 러셀 광장에 이르자, 그녀의 얼굴은 그가 햇살이라도 되는 양 환히 밝아졌다."], ["The little cares, fears, tears, timid misgivings, sleepless fancies of I don't know how many days and nights, were forgotten, under one moment's influence of that familiar, irresistible smile.", "며칠 밤낮인지 모를 자그마한 근심들, 두려움, 눈물, 소심한 의구심, 잠 못 이루던 망상들이, 그 낯익고 거역할 수 없는 미소가 한순간 미친 영향 아래 모조리 잊혔다. 그는 응접실 문간에서 그녀를 향해 환히 빛났다."], ["He beamed on her from the drawing-room door--magnificent, with ambrosial whiskers, like a god.", "신들의 양식 같은 구레나룻을 두른, 한 분의 신처럼 늠름한 모습이었다. 삼보는 그 젊은 장교에게 멋대로 임시 계급을 한 단계 올려 \"오스본 대위\"라고 알리면서, 동조하는 듯한 함박웃음으로 얼굴이 환했는데, 그 어린 아가씨가 창가 망보던 자리에서 화들짝 놀라 얼굴을 붉히며 벌떡 일어서는 것을 보았다."], ["Sambo, whose face as he announced Captain Osbin (having conferred a brevet rank on that young officer) blazed with a sympathetic grin, saw the little girl start, and flush, and jump up from her watching-place in the window; and Sambo retreated: and as soon as the door was shut, she went fluttering to Lieutenant George Osborne's heart as if it was the only natural home for her to nestle in.", "그리고 삼보는 물러갔다. 문이 닫히기가 무섭게 그녀는, 마치 깃들기에 그곳만이 유일하게 자연스러운 보금자리라는 듯, 파닥파닥 조지 오스본 중위의 가슴으로 날아들었다."], ["Oh, thou poor panting little soul!", "오, 가엾고 가쁜 숨 몰아쉬는 작은 영혼이여!"], ["The very finest tree in the whole forest, with the straightest stem, and the strongest arms, and the thickest foliage, wherein you choose to build and coo, may be marked, for what you know, and may be down with a crash ere long.", "숲에서 가장 좋은 나무, 줄기가 가장 곧고 가지가 가장 튼튼하며 잎이 가장 무성하여 그대가 그 위에 둥지를 틀고 구구거리려는 그 나무가, 그대도 알지 못하는 새 표시가 찍혀 머지않아 우지끈 쓰러질지도 모르는 일이다."], ["What an old, old simile that is, between man and timber!", "사람과 나무를 견주는 이 비유라니, 어찌나 오래고도 오랜 것인지!"]], [["In the meanwhile, George kissed her very kindly on her forehead and glistening eyes, and was very gracious and good; and she thought his diamond shirt-pin (which she had not known him to wear before) the prettiest ornament ever seen.", "그러는 사이 조지는 그녀의 이마와 반짝이는 두 눈에 무척 다정하게 입을 맞추었고, 더없이 자상하고 살가웠다. 그녀는 그의 다이아몬드 셔츠핀이(전에는 그가 그것을 단 것을 본 적이 없었다) 여태껏 본 가장 예쁜 장신구라고 생각했다."]], [["The observant reader, who has marked our young Lieutenant's previous behaviour, and has preserved our report of the brief conversation which he has just had with Captain Dobbin, has possibly come to certain conclusions regarding the character of Mr.", "우리의 젊은 중위가 앞서 보인 행실을 눈여겨보고, 또 그가 방금 도빈 대위와 나눈 그 짤막한 대화의 보고를 기억해둔 세심한 독자라면, 어쩌면 오스본의 인품에 대해 어떤 결론에 다다랐을지도 모른다."], ["Osborne. Some cynical Frenchman has said that there are two parties to a love-transaction: the one who loves and the other who condescends to be so treated.", "어느 냉소적인 프랑스인이 말하기를, 연애라는 거래에는 두 당사자가 있는데, 하나는 사랑하는 쪽이요 다른 하나는 그렇게 대접받기를 황송히 허락해주는 쪽이라고 했다."], ["Perhaps the love is occasionally on the man's side; perhaps on the lady's.", "어쩌면 사랑은 때로 남자 쪽에 있고, 어쩌면 여자 쪽에 있다."], ["Perhaps some infatuated swain has ere this mistaken insensibility for modesty, dulness for maiden reserve, mere vacuity for sweet bashfulness, and a goose, in a word, for a swan.", "어쩌면 어떤 홀딱 반한 시골 총각은 진작에 무신경을 정숙함으로, 둔함을 처녀다운 수줍음으로, 그저 텅 빈 머릿속을 사랑스러운 부끄럼으로, 한마디로 거위를 백조로 오해했을지도 모른다."], ["Perhaps some beloved female subscriber has arrayed an ass in the splendour and glory of her imagination; admired his dulness as manly simplicity; worshipped his selfishness as manly superiority; treated his stupidity as majestic gravity, and used him as the brilliant fairy Titania did a certain weaver at Athens.", "어쩌면 사랑받는 어느 여성 독자께서는 한 마리 당나귀를 자기 상상의 광채와 영광으로 치장해, 그 둔함을 사내다운 소박함으로 흠모하고, 그 이기심을 사내다운 우월함으로 숭배하며, 그 어리석음을 위엄 있는 진중함으로 떠받들고, 저 눈부신 요정 티타니아가 아테네의 어느 직조공을 부린 것처럼 그를 부렸을지도 모른다."], ["I think I have seen such comedies of errors going on in the world.", "내 생각에 나는 세상에서 이런 착각의 희극들이 벌어지는 것을 본 적이 있다. 그러나 한 가지만은 분명하다."], ["But this is certain, that Amelia believed her lover to be one of the most gallant and brilliant men in the empire: and it is possible Lieutenant Osborne thought so too.", "애밀리아는 자기 연인을 이 제국에서 가장 늠름하고 빛나는 사내 가운데 하나라고 믿었다는 것이다. 그리고 어쩌면 오스본 중위도 스스로 그렇게 생각했을 것이다."]], [["He was a little wild: how many young men are; and don't girls like a rake better than a milksop?", "그는 약간 방종했다. 하지만 젊은 사내들 가운데 그렇지 않은 이가 얼마나 되겠는가."], ["He hadn't sown his wild oats as yet, but he would soon: and quit the army now that peace was proclaimed; the Corsican monster locked up at Elba; promotion by consequence over; and no chance left for the display of his undoubted military talents and valour: and his allowance, with Amelia's settlement, would enable them to take a snug place in the country somewhere, in a good sporting neighbourhood; and he would hunt a little, and farm a little; and they would be very happy.", "그리고 아가씨들이란 겁쟁이보다 난봉꾼을 더 좋아하지 않던가? 그는 아직 그 방종한 짓을 다 끝내지 못했지만, 머지않아 끝낼 참이었다."], ["As for remaining in the army as a married man, that was impossible. Fancy Mrs.", "그리고 이제 평화가 선포되었으니 군대를 떠날 작정이었다."], ["George Osborne in lodgings in a county town; or, worse still, in the East or West Indies, with a society of officers, and patronized by Mrs.", "코르시카의 괴물은 엘바에 갇혔고, 따라서 진급도 끝났으며, 그의 의심할 바 없는 군사적 재능과 용맹을 펼칠 기회도 남지 않았다. 게다가 그의 봉급에 애밀리아의 지참금까지 보태면, 어딘가 사냥하기 좋은 시골에 아늑한 거처를 마련할 수 있을 터였다."], ["Major O'Dowd!", "거기서 사냥도 좀 하고 농사도 좀 지으며, 둘이서 무척 행복하게 살 것이었다. 결혼한 몸으로 군대에 남는다는 것은 어림없는 일이었다."], ["Amelia died with laughing at Osborne's stories about Mrs.", "조지 오스본 부인이 어느 지방 소도시의 셋방에 사는 꼴이라니, 아니 더 끔찍하게는 장교 패거리 틈에 끼어 오다우드 소령 부인의 비호를 받으며 동인도나 서인도제도에 사는 꼴이라니, 어디 상상이나 되는가! 애밀리아는 오다우드 소령 부인을 두고 오스본이 들려주는 이야기에 자지러지게 웃어댔다."], ["Major O'Dowd.", "그는 그녀를 너무도 애틋이 사랑한 나머지, 그 끔찍한 여자와 그 천박함에, 군인 아내의 거친 처지에 그녀를 내맡길 수가 없었다. 그는 자기 자신은 아무래도 좋았다."], ["He loved her much too fondly to subject her to that horrid woman and her vulgarities, and the rough treatment of a soldier's wife.", "정말로 그랬다. 다만 그의 사랑스러운 어린 아가씨만큼은, 자기 아내로서 마땅히 누려야 할 사회적 자리를 차지하게 해주고 싶었다."], ["He didn't care for himself--not he; but his dear little girl should take the place in society to which, as his wife, she was entitled: and to these proposals you may be sure she acceded, as she would to any other from the same author.", "그리고 이런 제안들에 그녀가 동의했으리란 것은 여러분도 틀림없이 짐작할 것이다. 같은 사람에게서 나온 그 어떤 제안에도 그녀는 그렇게 동의했을 테니 말이다."]], [["Holding this kind of conversation, and building numberless castles in the air (which Amelia adorned with all sorts of flower-gardens, rustic walks, country churches, Sunday schools, and the like; while George had his mind's eye directed to the stables, the kennel, and the cellar), this young pair passed away a couple of hours very pleasantly; and as the Lieutenant had only that single day in town, and a great deal of most important business to transact, it was proposed that Miss Emmy should dine with her future sisters-in-law.", "이런 식의 이야기를 나누며 수없이 많은 공중누각을 쌓아 올리면서(애밀리아는 거기에 온갖 꽃밭이며 시골 오솔길이며 시골 교회며 주일학교 따위로 단장을 했고, 그동안 조지의 마음의 눈은 마구간과 개집과 술 저장고를 향해 있었다), 이 젊은 한 쌍은 두어 시간을 무척 즐겁게 흘려보냈다. 그런데 중위는 런던에 머물 날이 그 하루뿐인 데다 처리해야 할 무척 중대한 일이 산더미였으므로, 에미 양이 장차 시누이가 될 사람들과 함께 저녁을 들면 어떻겠느냐는 제안이 나왔다."], ["This invitation was accepted joyfully.", "이 초대는 기쁘게 받아들여졌다."], ["He conducted her to his sisters; where he left her talking and prattling in a way that astonished those ladies, who thought that George might make something of her; and he then went off to transact his business.", "그는 그녀를 누이들에게 데려갔고, 그녀가 떠들고 재잘대는 모양이 그 숙녀들을 어찌나 놀라게 했던지, 그들은 조지가 그녀를 제법 그럴듯하게 키워낼 수 있겠다고 생각했다. 그런 뒤 그는 자기 볼일을 보러 떠났다."]], [["In a word, he went out and ate ices at a pastry-cook's shop in Charing Cross; tried a new coat in Pall Mall; dropped in at the Old Slaughters', and called for Captain Cannon; played eleven games at billiards with the Captain, of which he won eight, and returned to Russell Square half an hour late for dinner, but in very good humour.", "한마디로, 그는 밖으로 나가 채링크로스의 어느 제과점에서 아이스크림을 먹고, 폴몰에서 새 코트를 입어보고, 올드슬로터스에 들러 캐넌 대위를 찾았으며, 대위와 당구를 열한 판 쳐서 그중 여덟 판을 이기고는, 저녁 식사 시간에 삼십 분 늦게, 그러나 무척 기분 좋게 러셀 광장으로 돌아왔다."]], [["It was not so with old Mr. Osborne.", "늙은 오스본 씨는 사정이 그렇지 못했다."], ["When that gentleman came from the City, and was welcomed in the drawing-room by his daughters and the elegant Miss Wirt, they saw at once by his face--which was puffy, solemn, and yellow at the best of times--and by the scowl and twitching of his black eyebrows, that the heart within his large white waistcoat was disturbed and uneasy.", "그 신사가 시티에서 돌아와 응접실에서 딸들과 우아한 워트 양의 영접을 받았을 때, 그들은 그의 얼굴만 보고도 — 평소 좋을 때조차 부석부석하고 근엄하며 누렇던 얼굴이었다 — 또 그 검은 눈썹의 찌푸림과 꿈틀거림만 보고도, 그 커다란 흰 조끼 속의 심장이 어지럽고 불편하다는 것을 단박에 알아차렸다. 애밀리아가 늘 그러듯 잔뜩 떨고 주눅이 들어 그에게 인사하러 앞으로 나서자, 그는 알은체로 무뚝뚝하게 한 번 끙 소리를 내고는, 그 크고 텁수룩한 손아귀에 잡은 작은 손을, 거기 붙잡아두려는 기색 하나 없이 그냥 떨어뜨려버렸다."], ["When Amelia stepped forward to salute him, which she always did with great trembling and timidity, he gave a surly grunt of recognition, and dropped the little hand out of his great hirsute paw without any attempt to hold it there.", "그는 음울하게 맏딸을 둘러보았다. 그 눈빛이 분명 \"대체 저 애가 왜 여기 있는 거냐?\""], ["He looked round gloomily at his eldest daughter; who, comprehending the meaning of his look, which asked unmistakably, \"Why the devil is she here?\" said at once:", "하고 묻는 뜻임을 알아챈 맏딸이 곧바로 말했다."]], [["\"George is in town, Papa; and has gone to the Horse Guards, and will be back to dinner.\"", "\"조지가 런던에 왔어요, 아버지. 호스가즈에 갔다가 저녁 시간에 맞춰 돌아올 거예요.\""]], [["\"O he is, is he?", "\"오 그래? 그 녀석 때문에 저녁을 미뤄둘 생각은 없다, 제인.\""], ["I won't have the dinner kept waiting for him, Jane\"; with which this worthy man lapsed into his particular chair, and then the utter silence in his genteel, well-furnished drawing-room was only interrupted by the alarmed ticking of the great French clock.", "그 점잖은 사내는 이 말과 함께 자기 전용 의자에 털썩 주저앉았다. 그러자 품위 있고 가구가 잘 갖춰진 응접실에는 적막만이 감돌았고, 그 적막을 깨는 것이라곤 커다란 프랑스제 시계의 겁먹은 듯한 똑딱거림뿐이었다."]], [["When that chronometer, which was surmounted by a cheerful brass group of the sacrifice of Iphigenia, tolled five in a heavy cathedral tone, Mr. Osborne pulled the bell at his right hand--violently, and the butler rushed up.", "이피게네이아의 희생을 묘사한 명랑한 황동 군상이 얹힌 그 시계가 무거운 대성당 종소리 같은 가락으로 다섯 시를 알리자, 오스본 씨는 오른편의 종 줄을 사납게 잡아당겼고, 집사가 부랴부랴 올라왔다."]], [["\"Dinner!\"", "\"저녁!\""], ["roared Mr. Osborne.", "오스본 씨가 으르렁거렸다."]], [["\"Mr. George isn't come in, sir,\"", "\"조지 도련님이 아직 안 들어오셨습니다, 나리.\""], ["interposed the man.", "집사가 끼어들었다."]], [["\"Damn Mr. George, sir.", "\"조지 따위 알 게 뭐냐."], ["Am I master of the house?", "이 집 주인이 나냐 아니냐?"], ["DINNER!\" Mr.", "저녁 내와!\""], ["Osborne scowled. Amelia trembled.", "오스본 씨가 얼굴을 찌푸렸다."], ["A telegraphic communication of eyes passed between the other three ladies.", "애밀리아는 떨었다. 나머지 세 숙녀 사이에 눈짓으로 전보 같은 신호가 오갔다."], ["The obedient bell in the lower regions began ringing the announcement of the meal.", "아래층의 고분고분한 종이 식사를 알리는 소리를 울리기 시작했다."], ["The tolling over, the head of the family thrust his hands into the great tail-pockets of his great blue coat with brass buttons, and without waiting for a further announcement strode downstairs alone, scowling over his shoulder at the four females.", "종소리가 그치자, 이 집안의 가장은 황동 단추가 달린 커다란 파란 코트의 그 큼직한 뒷주머니에 두 손을 찔러 넣고는, 더 이상의 통보를 기다리지도 않고 어깨너머로 네 여인을 노려보며 혼자 성큼성큼 아래층으로 내려갔다."]], [["\"What's the matter now, my dear?\"", "\"이번엔 또 무슨 일이야?\""], ["asked one of the other, as they rose and tripped gingerly behind the sire.", "그들이 자리에서 일어나 아버지 뒤를 조심조심 종종걸음으로 따르며 서로에게 물었다."], ["\"I suppose the funds are falling,\"", "\"공채 시세가 떨어지는 모양이지.\""], ["whispered Miss Wirt; and so, trembling and in silence, this hushed female company followed their dark leader.", "워트 양이 속삭였다. 그렇게, 떨면서 말없이, 이 숨죽인 여인들은 자신들의 어두운 인도자를 뒤따랐다."], ["They took their places in silence.", "그들은 말없이 제자리에 앉았다."], ["He growled out a blessing, which sounded as gruffly as a curse.", "그가 으르렁거리듯 식전 기도를 읊었는데, 그 소리가 저주만큼이나 무뚝뚝하게 들렸다."], ["The great silver dish-covers were removed.", "커다란 은제 음식 덮개들이 벗겨졌다."], ["Amelia trembled in her place, for she was next to the awful Osborne, and alone on her side of the table--the gap being occasioned by the absence of George.", "애밀리아는 자기 자리에서 떨었다. 그도 그럴 것이, 그녀는 그 무시무시한 오스본 씨 곁에, 게다가 식탁의 자기 쪽에 혼자 앉아 있었는데, 그 빈자리는 조지가 없는 탓에 생긴 것이었다."]], [["\"Soup?\" says Mr.", "\"수프?\""], ["Osborne, clutching the ladle, fixing his eyes on her, in a sepulchral tone; and having helped her and the rest, did not speak for a while.", "오스본 씨가 국자를 움켜쥐고 그녀에게 눈을 고정한 채 무덤 속에서 울리는 듯한 목소리로 말했다. 그러고는 그녀와 나머지에게 떠주고 나서, 한동안 입을 열지 않았다."]], [["\"Take Miss Sedley's plate away,\"", "\"세들리 양 접시 치워라.\""], ["at last he said.", "마침내 그가 말했다."], ["\"She can't eat the soup--no more can I.", "\"이 애는 수프를 못 먹어. 나도 못 먹겠다."], ["It's beastly.", "형편없으니까."], ["Take away the soup, Hicks, and to-morrow turn the cook out of the house, Jane.\"", "수프 치워, 힉스. 그리고 제인, 내일 요리사를 이 집에서 내쫓아라.\""]], [["Having concluded his observations upon the soup, Mr. Osborne made a few curt remarks respecting the fish, also of a savage and satirical tendency, and cursed Billingsgate with an emphasis quite worthy of the place.", "수프에 관한 소견을 마친 오스본 씨는 생선에 대해서도 몇 마디 짤막하게 평했는데, 이 또한 사납고 빈정대는 투였으며, 그 어시장 이름값을 톡톡히 하리만치 힘주어 빌링스게이트를 저주했다."], ["Then he lapsed into silence, and swallowed sundry glasses of wine, looking more and more terrible, till a brisk knock at the door told of George's arrival when everybody began to rally.", "그러고는 다시 침묵에 빠져 와인을 몇 잔이나 들이켰고, 점점 더 무시무시한 표정이 되어갔다. 그러다 문에서 경쾌한 노크 소리가 나며 조지가 도착했음을 알리자, 그제야 모두가 기운을 차리기 시작했다."]], [["\"He could not come before.", "더 일찍은 올 수가 없었다는 것이었다."], ["General Daguilet had kept him waiting at the Horse Guards.", "다길레 장군이 호스가즈에서 그를 기다리게 했다나."], ["Never mind soup or fish.", "수프든 생선이든 상관없다."], ["Give him anything--he didn't care what.", "아무거나 달라, 뭐든 괜찮다."], ["Capital mutton--capital everything.\"", "양고기 훌륭하군, 죄다 훌륭해."], ["His good humour contrasted with his father's severity; and he rattled on unceasingly during dinner, to the delight of all--of one especially, who need not be mentioned.", "그의 좋은 기분은 아버지의 험상궂음과 대조를 이루었고, 그는 저녁 내내 쉴 새 없이 재잘거려 모두를, 특히 굳이 이름을 댈 것도 없는 한 사람을 기쁘게 했다."]], [["As soon as the young ladies had discussed the orange and the glass of wine which formed the ordinary conclusion of the dismal banquets at Mr. Osborne's house, the signal to make sail for the drawing-room was given, and they all arose and departed.", "오스본 씨 댁의 그 음울한 만찬을 으레 마무리 짓는 오렌지 한 알과 와인 한 잔을 젊은 숙녀들이 비우기가 무섭게, 응접실로 출항하라는 신호가 떨어졌고, 그들은 모두 일어나 자리를 떴다."], ["Amelia hoped George would soon join them there.", "애밀리아는 조지가 곧 그리로 합류하기를 바랐다."], ["She began playing some of his favourite waltzes (then newly imported) at the great carved-legged, leather-cased grand piano in the drawing-room overhead.", "그녀는 위층 응접실의, 다리에 큼직한 조각을 새기고 가죽 덮개를 씌운 그랜드피아노 앞에서 그가 좋아하는 왈츠 몇 곡을(당시 막 들여온 곡들이었다) 치기 시작했다. 그러나 이 자그마한 술책도 그를 불러오지 못했다."], ["This little artifice did not bring him.", "그는 왈츠 소리에 귀를 막은 듯했다."], ["He was deaf to the waltzes; they grew fainter and fainter; the discomfited performer left the huge instrument presently; and though her three friends performed some of the loudest and most brilliant new pieces of their repertoire, she did not hear a single note, but sate thinking, and boding evil.", "곡조는 점점 희미해졌고, 의기소침해진 연주자는 이윽고 그 커다란 악기를 떠났다. 그리고 세 친구가 자신들의 연주 목록 가운데 가장 우렁차고 가장 화려한 신곡 몇 곡을 연주했지만, 그녀는 단 한 음도 듣지 못한 채, 그저 앉아서 생각에 잠겨 불길한 예감에 사로잡혀 있었다."], ["Old Osborne's scowl, terrific always, had never before looked so deadly to her.", "늙은 오스본의 찌푸린 얼굴은 언제나 무시무시했지만, 그날만큼 죽일 듯이 보인 적은 없었다."], ["His eyes followed her out of the room, as if she had been guilty of something.", "그가 방을 나설 때 그의 눈은, 마치 그녀가 무슨 죄라도 지은 양 그녀를 좇았다."], ["When they brought her coffee, she started as though it were a cup of poison which Mr. Hicks, the butler, wished to propose to her.", "그들이 커피를 내오자, 그녀는 마치 집사 힉스 씨가 권하려는 독배라도 되는 양 화들짝 놀랐다."], ["What mystery was there lurking?", "어떤 비밀이 도사리고 있는 것일까?"], ["Oh, those women!", "오, 저 여인들이란!"], ["They nurse and cuddle their presentiments, and make darlings of their ugliest thoughts, as they do of their deformed children.", "그들은 자신의 예감을 보듬고 어르며, 마치 기형의 자식을 그러듯 자신의 가장 추한 생각을 애지중지하는 것이다."]], [["The gloom on the paternal countenance had also impressed George Osborne with anxiety.", "아버지의 침울한 안색은 조지 오스본에게도 불안감을 심어주었다."], ["With such eyebrows, and a look so decidedly bilious, how was he to extract that money from the governor, of which George was consumedly in want?", "저런 눈썹에, 저토록 결정적으로 심사 뒤틀린 표정을 하고 있으니, 조지가 그토록 절실히 원하는 그 돈을 어떻게 아버지한테서 우려낸단 말인가?"], ["He began praising his father's wine.", "그는 아버지의 와인을 칭찬하기 시작했다."], ["That was generally a successful means of cajoling the old gentleman.", "그것은 대개 그 노신사를 구슬리는 데 성공적인 수단이었다."]], [["\"We never got such Madeira in the West Indies, sir, as yours.", "\"서인도제도에서는 아버지 것 같은 마데이라는 도무지 구경도 못 했답니다, 아버지."], ["Colonel Heavytop took off three bottles of that you sent me down, under his belt the other day.\"", "며칠 전에 아버지가 보내주신 그 와인을 헤비탑 대령이 세 병이나 단숨에 비워버렸지 뭡니까.\""]], [["\"Did he?\"", "\"그랬어?\""], ["said the old gentleman.", "노신사가 말했다."], ["\"It stands me in eight shillings a bottle.\"", "\"그게 한 병에 팔 실링이나 든단다.\""]], [["\"Will you take six guineas a dozen for it, sir?\"", "\"한 다스에 육 기니에 파시지 않겠습니까, 아버지?\""], ["said George, with a laugh.", "조지가 웃으며 말했다."], ["\"There's one of the greatest men in the kingdom wants some.\"", "\"이 왕국에서 가장 높은 분 중 한 분이 좀 구하고 싶어 하시거든요.\""]], [["\"Does he?\"", "\"그래?\""], ["growled the senior.", "노인이 으르렁거렸다."], ["\"Wish he may get it.\"", "\"구할 수 있으면 좋겠구먼.\""]], [["\"When General Daguilet was at Chatham, sir, Heavytop gave him a breakfast, and asked me for some of the wine.", "\"다길레 장군이 채텀에 오셨을 때요, 아버지, 헤비탑이 장군께 아침을 대접하면서 저더러 그 와인을 좀 달라고 했지요."], ["The General liked it just as well--wanted a pipe for the Commander-in-Chief.", "장군도 어찌나 맘에 들어 하시던지, 총사령관께 드릴 큰 통으로 하나 원하셨답니다."], ["He's his Royal Highness's right-hand man.\"", "그분이 전하의 오른팔이시거든요.\""]], [["\"It is devilish fine wine,\"", "\"기막히게 좋은 와인이지.\""], ["said the Eyebrows, and they looked more good-humoured; and George was going to take advantage of this complacency, and bring the supply question on the mahogany, when the father, relapsing into solemnity, though rather cordial in manner, bade him ring the bell for claret.", "그 눈썹이 말했고, 표정이 한결 누그러진 듯했다. 조지는 이 흡족함을 틈타 이 마호가니 식탁 위로 자금 문제를 꺼내려던 참이었는데, 아버지가 다시 근엄함으로 돌아가, 그러나 태도는 제법 다정하게, 그에게 클라레를 가져오게 종을 울리라고 일렀다."], ["\"And we'll see if that's as good as the Madeira, George, to which his Royal Highness is welcome, I'm sure.", "\"그 클라레가 마데이라만큼 좋은지 어디 보자꾸나, 조지. 그 마데이라야 물론 전하께서 기꺼이 가져가셔도 좋고."], ["And as we are drinking it, I'll talk to you about a matter of importance.\"", "그리고 그걸 마시면서, 네게 중요한 일을 한 가지 이야기하마.\""]], [["Amelia heard the claret bell ringing as she sat nervously upstairs.", "애밀리아는 위층에 불안하게 앉아 있다가 클라레 종이 울리는 소리를 들었다."], ["She thought, somehow, it was a mysterious and presentimental bell.", "그녀는 어쩐지 그것이 알 수 없는, 예감을 품은 종소리라고 생각했다."], ["Of the presentiments which some people are always having, some surely must come right.", "어떤 이들이 노상 품는 그 예감들 가운데, 분명 더러는 들어맞기 마련인 법이다."]], [["\"What I want to know, George,\"", "\"내가 알고 싶은 건 말이다, 조지.\""], ["the old gentleman said, after slowly smacking his first bumper--\"what I want to know is, how you and--ah--that little thing upstairs, are carrying on?\"", "노신사가 첫 잔을 천천히 쩝쩝 비우고 나서 말했다. \"내가 알고 싶은 건, 너와 — 어 — 저 위층의 그 자그마한 것이, 어떻게 지내고 있느냐 하는 거다.\""]], [["\"I think, sir, it is not hard to see,\"", "\"제 생각엔 말입니다, 아버지, 보기 어려운 일은 아니지요.\""], ["George said, with a self-satisfied grin.", "조지가 흡족한 미소를 띠고 말했다."], ["\"Pretty clear, sir.--What capital wine!\"", "\"꽤 분명하지 않습니까, 아버지. 이 와인 정말 기막히군요!\""]], [["\"What d'you mean, pretty clear, sir?\"", "\"꽤 분명하다니, 그게 무슨 소리냐?\""]], [["\"Why, hang it, sir, don't push me too hard.", "\"아니, 젠장, 아버지, 저를 너무 몰아붙이진 마세요."], ["I'm a modest man.", "저는 겸손한 사내랍니다."], ["I--ah--I don't set up to be a lady-killer; but I do own that she's as devilish fond of me as she can be.", "저는 — 어 — 여자깨나 후린다고 으스댈 생각은 없어요. 하지만 그녀가 저를 더할 나위 없이 끔찍이 좋아한다는 것만은 인정합니다."], ["Anybody can see that with half an eye.\"", "누가 봐도 한눈에 알 수 있는 일이지요.\""]], [["\"And you yourself?\"", "\"그럼 너 자신은?\""]], [["\"Why, sir, didn't you order me to marry her, and ain't I a good boy?", "\"아니, 아버지, 그녀와 결혼하라고 명하신 게 아버지 아니십니까. 그래서 제가 착한 아들 노릇 하고 있는 거고요."], ["Haven't our Papas settled it ever so long?\"", "우리 아버님들이 진작에 정해두신 일 아닙니까?\""]], [["\"A pretty boy, indeed.", "\"참 착한 아들이로구나, 그래."], ["Haven't I heard of your doings, sir, with Lord Tarquin, Captain Crawley of the Guards, the Honourable Mr. Deuceace and that set.", "근위대 크롤리 대위며 명예직의 듀세이스 씨며 타퀸 경이며 그 패거리와 어울려 네가 벌인 짓들을 내가 못 들은 줄 아느냐."], ["Have a care sir, have a care.\"", "조심해라, 조심해.\""]], [["The old gentleman pronounced these aristocratic names with the greatest gusto.", "노신사는 이 귀족들의 이름을 더없이 흐뭇한 투로 발음했다."], ["Whenever he met a great man he grovelled before him, and my-lorded him as only a free-born Briton can do.", "그는 지체 높은 인사를 만나면 그 앞에서 굽실거리며, 자유민으로 태어난 영국인만이 할 수 있는 식으로 \"각하, 각하\""], ["He came home and looked out his history in the Peerage: he introduced his name into his daily conversation; he bragged about his Lordship to his daughters.", "하고 떠받들었다. 그는 집에 돌아와 귀족 명감에서 그 가문 내력을 찾아보았고, 일상 대화에 그 이름을 슬쩍 끼워 넣었으며, 딸들에게 그 각하 자랑을 늘어놓았다."], ["He fell down prostrate and basked in him as a Neapolitan beggar does in the sun.", "그는 나폴리의 거지가 햇볕을 쬐듯, 그 앞에 넙죽 엎드려 그 빛을 쬐었다."], ["George was alarmed when he heard the names.", "조지는 그 이름들을 듣고 흠칫했다."], ["He feared his father might have been informed of certain transactions at play.", "아버지가 도박판에서 벌어진 어떤 거래들에 대해 전해 들었을까 봐 겁이 났던 것이다."], ["But the old moralist eased him by saying serenely:", "그러나 그 늙은 도덕가는 태연히 말하며 그를 안심시켰다."]], [["\"Well, well, young men will be young men.", "\"뭐, 뭐, 젊은 사내들이야 젊은 사내들답기 마련이지. 그리고 내게 위안이 되는 건 말이다, 조지, 네가 영국 최상류 사회에서 어울려 지내고 있다는 거다."], ["And the comfort to me is, George, that living in the best society in England, as I hope you do; as I think you do; as my means will allow you to do--\"", "그러길 바라고, 또 그러고 있다고 믿는다. 내 재력이 너에게 허락하는 만큼은 말이야.\""]], [["\"Thank you, sir,\"", "\"감사합니다, 아버지.\""], ["says George, making his point at once.", "조지가 곧바로 자기 요점을 짚으며 말했다. \"그런 지체 높은 분들과 어울리려면 공짜로는 안 되지요."], ["\"One can't live with these great folks for nothing; and my purse, sir, look at it\"; and he held up a little token which had been netted by Amelia, and contained the very last of Dobbin's pound notes.", "그리고 제 지갑이라고는, 아버지, 이걸 좀 보세요.\" 그러면서 그는 애밀리아가 그물뜨기로 짠 자그마한 패물 같은 지갑을 들어 보였는데, 그 안에는 도빈의 지폐 가운데 정말 마지막 한 장이 들어 있었다."]], [["\"You shan't want, sir.", "\"부족함이 없게 해주마."], ["The British merchant's son shan't want, sir.", "영국 상인의 아들이 궁색해서야 쓰겠느냐."], ["My guineas are as good as theirs, George, my boy; and I don't grudge 'em.", "내 기니도 그자들 것 못지않게 좋은 거다, 조지, 이 녀석아."], ["Call on Mr.", "나는 그걸 아끼지 않아."], ["Chopper as you go through the City to-morrow; he'll have something for you.", "내일 시티를 지나거든 초퍼 씨한테 들러라. 네게 줄 게 있을 테니."], ["I don't grudge money when I know you're in good society, because I know that good society can never go wrong.", "네가 좋은 사회에 있다는 걸 아는 한, 나는 돈을 아끼지 않는다. 좋은 사회는 결코 그릇될 리 없다는 걸 아니까 말이야."], ["There's no pride in me.", "내게는 오만이라곤 없다."], ["I was a humbly born man--but you have had advantages.", "나야 비천하게 태어난 몸이지만, 너는 좋은 여건을 누려왔어."], ["Make a good use of 'em.", "그걸 잘 활용해라."], ["Mix with the young nobility.", "젊은 귀족들과 어울리거라."], ["There's many of 'em who can't spend a dollar to your guinea, my boy.", "그자들 중에는 네 기니에 맞먹는 일 달러도 못 쓰는 자들이 수두룩하다, 이 녀석아."], ["And as for the pink bonnets (here from under the heavy eyebrows there came a knowing and not very pleasing leer)--why boys will be boys.", "그리고 분홍 보닛으로 말하면(여기서 그 짙은 눈썹 밑으로 무언가 알겠다는 듯한, 그다지 유쾌하지 못한 곁눈질이 나왔다) — 뭐, 사내아이들은 사내아이들답기 마련이니까. 다만 한 가지, 내가 네게 피하라고 명하는 게 있다."], ["Only there's one thing I order you to avoid, which, if you do not, I'll cut you off with a shilling, by Jove; and that's gambling.\"", "그걸 어기면 정말이지 한 푼도 안 남기고 너를 의절해버릴 테다. 그건 바로 도박이야.\""]], [["\"Oh, of course, sir,\"", "\"아, 물론이지요, 아버지.\""], ["said George.", "조지가 말했다."]], [["\"But to return to the other business about Amelia: why shouldn't you marry higher than a stockbroker's daughter, George--that's what I want to know?\"", "\"하지만 애밀리아에 관한 그 다른 일로 돌아가서 말이다. 어째서 너는 주식 중개인의 딸보다 더 높은 데로 장가들면 안 되는 거냐, 조지? 그게 내가 알고 싶은 거다.\""]], [["\"It's a family business, sir,\"", "\"그건 집안끼리의 일이지 않습니까, 아버지.\""], ["says George, cracking filberts.", "조지가 개암을 까며 말했다."], ["\"You and Mr. Sedley made the match a hundred years ago.\"", "\"아버지와 세들리 씨가 백 년 전에 맺어주신 혼사인데요.\""]], [["\"I don't deny it; but people's positions alter, sir.", "\"그걸 부정하지는 않는다. 하지만 사람의 처지란 변하는 법이다, 얘야."], ["I don't deny that Sedley made my fortune, or rather put me in the way of acquiring, by my own talents and genius, that proud position, which, I may say, I occupy in the tallow trade and the City of London.", "세들리가 내 재산의 밑천이 됐다는 것도, 아니 정확히 말하면 내 재능과 천재로 그 떳떳한 자리, 그러니까 수지 장사와 런던 시티에서 내가 차지하고 있다 할 만한 그 자리를 일굴 길을 열어줬다는 것도 부정하지 않아. 나는 세들리에게 그 은혜를 갚아왔다."], ["I've shown my gratitude to Sedley; and he's tried it of late, sir, as my cheque-book can show.", "그런데 그 사람이 요즘 들어 그 은혜를 시험하고 있어, 얘야. 내 수표책이 그 증거다."], ["George!", "조지!"], ["I tell you in confidence I don't like the looks of Mr. Sedley's affairs.", "너한테만 은밀히 하는 말이다만, 나는 세들리 씨의 사업 형편이 어째 미덥지가 않아."], ["My chief clerk, Mr. Chopper, does not like the looks of 'em, and he's an old file, and knows 'Change as well as any man in London.", "내 수석 서기 초퍼 씨도 그 형편을 영 못 미더워하는데, 그 사람은 닳고 닳은 인물이라 런던의 누구 못지않게 거래소 사정을 훤히 안다."], ["Hulker & Bullock are looking shy at him.", "헐커 앤드 불록도 그 사람을 미심쩍게 보고 있어."], ["He's been dabbling on his own account I fear.", "내 짐작에 그 사람이 제 잇속으로 투기에 손을 댄 모양이다."], ["They say the Jeune Amelie was his, which was taken by the Yankee privateer Molasses.", "양키 사략선 몰래시스호에 나포된 죈아멜리호가 그 사람 것이었다는 말도 있어. 그러니 분명히 말해두마."], ["And that's flat--unless I see Amelia's ten thousand down you don't marry her.", "애밀리아의 만 파운드를 내 눈앞에 현금으로 내놓지 않는 한 너는 그 애와 결혼 못 한다."], ["I'll have no lame duck's daughter in my family.", "절름발이 오리 같은 자의 딸을 내 집안에 들이진 않을 테니까."], ["Pass the wine, sir--or ring for coffee.\"", "와인 이리 주거라, 얘야. 아니면 종을 울려 커피를 가져오게 하든지.\""]], [["With which Mr. Osborne spread out the evening paper, and George knew from this signal that the colloquy was ended, and that his papa was about to take a nap.", "이 말과 함께 오스본 씨는 석간신문을 펼쳐 들었고, 조지는 이 신호에서 대화가 끝났으며 아버지가 이제 한숨 졸려 한다는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["He hurried upstairs to Amelia in the highest spirits.", "그는 더없이 들뜬 기분으로 애밀리아에게로 서둘러 올라갔다."], ["What was it that made him more attentive to her on that night than he had been for a long time--more eager to amuse her, more tender, more brilliant in talk?", "그날 밤 그가 오랜만에 그녀에게 그토록 다정해진 것은, 그녀를 즐겁게 해주려 그토록 애쓰고, 그토록 부드러워지고, 그토록 말솜씨가 빛난 것은 대체 무엇 때문이었을까?"], ["Was it that his generous heart warmed to her at the prospect of misfortune; or that the idea of losing the dear little prize made him value it more?", "불행이 닥쳐올 전망 앞에서 그의 너그러운 가슴이 그녀에게 따뜻해진 것이었을까, 아니면 그 사랑스러운 어린 상품을 잃을지도 모른다는 생각이 그것을 더 귀하게 여기게 만든 것이었을까?"]], [["She lived upon the recollections of that happy evening for many days afterwards, remembering his words; his looks; the song he sang; his attitude, as he leant over her or looked at her from a distance.", "그녀는 그 후로 여러 날 동안 그 행복한 저녁의 추억에 기대어 살았다. 그의 말, 그의 눈빛, 그가 부른 노래, 그가 자기 위로 몸을 기울이거나 멀리서 자기를 바라보던 그 자태를 떠올리며 말이다."], ["As it seemed to her, no night ever passed so quickly at Mr.", "그녀가 느끼기에, 오스본 씨 댁에서 그토록 빨리 지나간 밤은 일찍이 없었다."], ["Osborne's house before; and for once this young person was almost provoked to be angry by the premature arrival of Mr. Sambo with her shawl.", "그리고 이 젊은 사람은 단 한 번, 삼보 씨가 숄을 들고 너무 일찍 나타난 통에 하마터면 화가 날 뻔했다."]], [["George came and took a tender leave of her the next morning; and then hurried off to the City, where he visited Mr.", "조지는 다음 날 아침 와서 그녀에게 다정하게 작별 인사를 한 뒤, 시티로 서둘러 갔다."], ["Chopper, his father's head man, and received from that gentleman a document which he exchanged at Hulker & Bullock's for a whole pocketful of money.", "거기서 그는 아버지의 수석 서기 초퍼 씨를 찾아가 그에게서 서류 한 장을 받았고, 그것을 헐커 앤드 불록에서 주머니 가득한 돈으로 바꾸었다."], ["As George entered the house, old John Sedley was passing out of the banker's parlour, looking very dismal.", "조지가 그 건물에 들어설 때, 늙은 존 세들리가 은행가의 응접실에서 무척 침울한 얼굴로 나오던 참이었다."], ["But his godson was much too elated to mark the worthy stockbroker's depression, or the dreary eyes which the kind old gentleman cast upon him.", "그러나 그의 대자는 너무도 의기양양한 나머지, 그 점잖은 주식 중개인의 풀 죽은 모습도, 그 인자한 노신사가 자기에게 던진 쓸쓸한 눈길도 알아채지 못했다."], ["Young Bullock did not come grinning out of the parlour with him as had been his wont in former years.", "젊은 불록도 예년처럼 그와 함께 히죽거리며 응접실에서 나오지 않았다."]], [["And as the swinging doors of Hulker, Bullock & Co. closed upon Mr. Sedley, Mr. Quill, the cashier (whose benevolent occupation it is to hand out crisp bank-notes from a drawer and dispense sovereigns out of a copper shovel), winked at Mr. Driver, the clerk at the desk on his right. Mr. Driver winked again.", "그리고 헐커 앤드 불록 상회의 여닫이문이 세들리 씨의 등 뒤로 닫히자, 출납계 퀼 씨가(서랍에서 빳빳한 지폐를 꺼내주고 구리 삽으로 금화를 떠 나눠주는 것이 그의 자비로운 소임이었다) 오른편 책상에 앉은 서기 드라이버 씨에게 윙크를 보냈다. 드라이버 씨도 마주 윙크했다."]], [["\"No go,\" Mr.", "\"가망 없어.\""], ["D. whispered.", "드라이버 씨가 속삭였다."]], [["\"Not at no price,\" Mr.", "\"어떤 값에도 안 돼.\""], ["Q. said.", "퀼 씨가 말했다."], ["\"Mr. George Osborne, sir, how will you take it?\"", "\"조지 오스본 씨, 어떻게 받으시겠습니까?\""], ["George crammed eagerly a quantity of notes into his pockets, and paid Dobbin fifty pounds that very evening at mess.", "조지는 지폐 한 뭉치를 게걸스레 주머니에 쑤셔 넣고는, 바로 그날 저녁 식당에서 도빈에게 오십 파운드를 갚았다."]], [["That very evening Amelia wrote him the tenderest of long letters.", "바로 그날 저녁 애밀리아는 그에게 더없이 다정한 긴 편지를 썼다."], ["Her heart was overflowing with tenderness, but it still foreboded evil.", "그녀의 마음은 애정으로 넘쳐흘렀으나, 여전히 불행을 예감하고 있었다."], ["What was the cause of Mr. Osborne's dark looks?", "오스본 씨의 어두운 표정은 무엇 때문이었을까?"], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."], ["Had any difference arisen between him and her papa?", "그분과 자기 아버지 사이에 무슨 갈등이라도 생긴 것일까?"], ["Her poor papa returned so melancholy from the City, that all were alarmed about him at home--in fine, there were four pages of loves and fears and hopes and forebodings.", "가엾은 아버지는 시티에서 어찌나 침울하게 돌아오셨던지 집안의 모두가 그분을 걱정한다는 것이었다. 요컨대, 사랑과 두려움과 희망과 불길한 예감으로 채워진 넉 장이었다."]], [["\"Poor little Emmy--dear little Emmy.", "\"가엾은 어린 에미, 사랑스러운 어린 에미."], ["How fond she is of me,\"", "어쩜 이리도 날 좋아할까.\""], ["George said, as he perused the missive--\"and Gad, what a headache that mixed punch has given me!\"", "조지가 그 편지를 읽으며 말했다. \"그나저나 젠장, 그 섞어 만든 펀치 때문에 머리가 어찌나 지끈거리는지!\""], ["Poor little Emmy, indeed.", "가엾은 어린 에미, 정말이지."]], [["Miss Crawley at Home\nAbout this time there drove up to an exceedingly snug and well-appointed house in Park Lane, a travelling chariot with a lozenge on the panels, a discontented female in a green veil and crimped curls on the rumble, and a large and confidential man on the box.", "그 무렵, 파크 레인의 더없이 아늑하고 잘 꾸며진 저택 앞으로 여행용 마차 한 대가 다가와 멈춰 섰다. 마차 옆면에는 마름모꼴 문장이 그려져 있었고, 뒷자리에는 초록색 베일을 쓰고 곱슬머리를 지진 시무룩한 여자가, 마부석에는 덩치 크고 믿음직한 사내가 앉아 있었다."], ["It was the equipage of our friend Miss Crawley, returning from Hants.", "그것은 햄프셔에서 돌아오는 우리의 벗 크롤리 양의 마차였다."], ["The carriage windows were shut; the fat spaniel, whose head and tongue ordinarily lolled out of one of them, reposed on the lap of the discontented female.", "마차의 창문들은 모두 닫혀 있었고, 평소라면 그중 한 창으로 머리와 혀를 늘어뜨리고 있을 살진 스패니얼은 시무룩한 여자의 무릎 위에 얌전히 엎드려 있었다."], ["When the vehicle stopped, a large round bundle of shawls was taken out of the carriage by the aid of various domestics and a young lady who accompanied the heap of cloaks.", "마차가 멈추자 여러 하인들과, 그 외투 더미를 따라온 한 젊은 숙녀의 도움으로 숄로 둘둘 감싼 커다랗고 둥근 꾸러미가 마차에서 내려졌다."], ["That bundle contained Miss Crawley, who was conveyed upstairs forthwith, and put into a bed and chamber warmed properly as for the reception of an invalid.", "그 꾸러미 안에 들어 있던 이가 바로 크롤리 양이었으니, 그녀는 즉시 위층으로 옮겨져, 환자를 맞이하기에 알맞게 데워진 방의 침대에 뉘어졌다."], ["Messengers went off for her physician and medical man.", "그녀의 주치의와 의사를 부르러 심부름꾼들이 떠났다."], ["They came, consulted, prescribed, vanished.", "그들은 와서, 의논하고, 처방을 내리고, 사라졌다."], ["The young companion of Miss Crawley, at the conclusion of their interview, came in to receive their instructions, and administered those antiphlogistic medicines which the eminent men ordered.", "크롤리 양의 젊은 말동무는 그 진찰이 끝나자 들어와 지시를 받았고, 그 명망 높은 분들이 처방한 소염제를 직접 복용시켰다."]], [["Captain Crawley of the Life Guards rode up from Knightsbridge Barracks the next day; his black charger pawed the straw before his invalid aunt's door.", "이튿날 근위 기병대 소속의 크롤리 대위가 나이츠브리지 병영에서 말을 달려왔다. 그의 검은 군마는 병석에 누운 고모의 문 앞에서 깔린 지푸라기를 앞발로 긁었다."], ["He was most affectionate in his inquiries regarding that amiable relative.", "그는 그 인자한 친척의 안부를 더없이 다정하게 물었다."], ["There seemed to be much source of apprehension.", "걱정거리가 한둘이 아닌 듯했다."], ["He found Miss Crawley's maid (the discontented female) unusually sulky and despondent; he found Miss Briggs, her dame de compagnie, in tears alone in the drawing-room.", "그는 크롤리 양의 하녀(그 시무룩한 여자)가 유난히 뚱하고 풀이 죽어 있는 것을 보았다. 또한 그녀의 말동무인 브리그스 양이 응접실에 홀로 앉아 눈물짓는 것도 보았다."], ["She had hastened home, hearing of her beloved friend's illness.", "그녀는 사랑하는 벗이 병들었다는 소식을 듣고 부랴부랴 집으로 달려온 것이었다."], ["She wished to fly to her couch, that couch which she, Briggs, had so often smoothed in the hour of sickness.", "그녀는 자신이, 즉 브리그스가 병상의 시간마다 그토록 자주 매만져 주던 그 침상으로 날아가고 싶었다."], ["She was denied admission to Miss Crawley's apartment.", "그러나 그녀는 크롤리 양의 방에 들어가는 것을 거절당했다."], ["A stranger was administering her medicines--a stranger from the country--an odious Miss ...", "어떤 낯선 여자가, 시골에서 온 낯선 여자가, 그 가증스러운 샤프라는 양이 그녀의 약을 챙기고 있었던 것이다."], ["--tears choked the utterance of the dame de compagnie, and she buried her crushed affections and her poor old red nose in her pocket handkerchief.", "그 말동무는 눈물에 목이 메어 말을 잇지 못했고, 짓밟힌 애정과 가엾고 늙어 빨개진 코를 손수건에 파묻었다."]], [["Rawdon Crawley sent up his name by the sulky femme de chambre, and Miss Crawley's new companion, coming tripping down from the sick-room, put a little hand into his as he stepped forward eagerly to meet her, gave a glance of great scorn at the bewildered Briggs, and beckoning the young Guardsman out of the back drawing-room, led him downstairs into that now desolate dining-parlour, where so many a good dinner had been celebrated.", "로던 크롤리는 그 뚱한 하녀를 시켜 자기 이름을 위층에 전했고, 크롤리 양의 새 말동무가 병실에서 사뿐사뿐 내려와, 그가 반갑게 맞으러 나서자 작은 손을 그의 손에 얹고는, 어리둥절해하는 브리그스를 향해 더없이 경멸 어린 눈길을 던졌다. 그러고는 그 젊은 근위병에게 안쪽 응접실 밖으로 나오라고 손짓하며, 그를 아래층의 이제는 적막해진 식당으로 이끌었다. 한때 그토록 숱한 훌륭한 만찬이 베풀어졌던 그 방으로 말이다."]], [["Here these two talked for ten minutes, discussing, no doubt, the symptoms of the old invalid above stairs; at the end of which period the parlour bell was rung briskly, and answered on that instant by Mr.", "여기서 이 두 사람은 십 분간 이야기를 나누었는데, 의심할 바 없이 위층에 누운 늙은 환자의 병세를 논하던 것이었다. 그 시간이 끝날 무렵 식당의 종이 힘차게 울렸고, 즉시 크롤리 양의 덩치 큰 믿음직한 집사 보울스 씨가 그 부름에 응했다(사실 그는 면담 내내 거의 줄곧 열쇠 구멍에 붙어 있었던 것이다)."], ["Bowls, Miss Crawley's large confidential butler (who, indeed, happened to be at the keyhole during the most part of the interview); and the Captain coming out, curling his mustachios, mounted the black charger pawing among the straw, to the admiration of the little blackguard boys collected in the street.", "이윽고 대위는 콧수염을 비비 꼬며 나와, 지푸라기 사이에서 발을 구르던 검은 군마에 올라탔는데, 거리에 모여든 자그마한 부랑아 녀석들의 감탄을 자아냈다. 그는 말을 다루며 식당 창문 안을 들여다보았고, 말은 아름답게 껑충거리며 뛰놀았다."], ["He looked in at the dining-room window, managing his horse, which curvetted and capered beautifully--for one instant the young person might be seen at the window, when her figure vanished, and, doubtless, she went upstairs again to resume the affecting duties of benevolence.", "한순간 그 젊은 여자가 창가에 보이는가 싶더니, 이내 그 모습이 사라졌다. 필시 그녀는 다시 위층으로 올라가 그 가슴 뭉클한 자비의 의무를 계속하러 간 것이리라."]], [["Who could this young woman be, I wonder?", "대체 이 젊은 여자가 누구인지, 나는 궁금하다."], ["That evening a little dinner for two persons was laid in the dining-room--when Mrs.", "그날 저녁 식당에는 두 사람을 위한 자그마한 만찬이 차려졌다."], ["Firkin, the lady's maid, pushed into her mistress's apartment, and bustled about there during the vacancy occasioned by the departure of the new nurse--and the latter and Miss Briggs sat down to the neat little meal.", "마침 하녀 퍼킨 부인이 새 간병인이 자리를 비운 틈을 타 안주인의 방으로 비집고 들어가 그 안을 부산스레 오가던 차였고, 그 새 간병인과 브리그스 양은 그 단출한 식탁에 마주 앉았다."]], [["Briggs was so much choked by emotion that she could hardly take a morsel of meat.", "브리그스는 북받치는 감정에 목이 너무 메어 고기 한 점도 제대로 넘기지 못했다."], ["The young person carved a fowl with the utmost delicacy, and asked so distinctly for egg-sauce, that poor Briggs, before whom that delicious condiment was placed, started, made a great clattering with the ladle, and once more fell back in the most gushing hysterical state.", "젊은 여자는 더없이 우아하게 닭고기를 발라 냈고, 어찌나 또렷한 목소리로 달걀 소스를 청하던지, 그 맛깔스러운 양념이 앞에 놓인 가엾은 브리그스는 흠칫 놀라 국자를 요란하게 달그락거리더니, 다시 한번 걷잡을 수 없이 흐느끼는 발작 상태에 빠지고 말았다."]], [["\"Had you not better give Miss Briggs a glass of wine?\"", "\"브리그스 양에게 포도주 한 잔 드리는 게 낫지 않겠어요?\""], ["said the person to Mr. Bowls, the large confidential man.", "그 여자가 덩치 큰 믿음직한 사내 보울스 씨에게 말했다."], ["He did so.", "그는 그대로 했다."], ["Briggs seized it mechanically, gasped it down convulsively, moaned a little, and began to play with the chicken on her plate.", "브리그스는 기계적으로 잔을 움켜쥐고 경련하듯 단숨에 들이켜더니, 잠시 신음하다가 접시 위의 닭고기를 깨작거리기 시작했다."]], [["\"I think we shall be able to help each other,\"", "\"우리 둘이서 서로 도울 수 있을 것 같네요.\""], ["said the person with great suavity: \"and shall have no need of Mr.", "여자가 더없이 상냥하게 말했다."], ["Bowls's kind services. Mr.", "\"보울스 씨의 친절한 수고는 필요 없을 거예요."], ["Bowls, if you please, we will ring when we want you.\"", "보울스 씨, 괜찮으시다면 필요할 때 종을 울리겠어요.\""], ["He went downstairs, where, by the way, he vented the most horrid curses upon the unoffending footman, his subordinate.", "그는 아래층으로 내려갔는데, 가는 길에 아무 잘못도 없는 자기 부하 하인에게 차마 입에 담지 못할 욕설을 퍼부었다."]], [["\"It is a pity you take on so, Miss Briggs,\"", "\"그렇게 마음 졸이시다니 딱하네요, 브리그스 양.\""], ["the young lady said, with a cool, slightly sarcastic, air.", "젊은 숙녀가 차갑고 살짝 빈정대는 투로 말했다."]], [["\"My dearest friend is so ill, and wo-o-on't see me,\"", "\"내 더없이 소중한 벗이 저토록 아픈데, 나를 보려고도, 안 하-하-하잖아요.\""], ["gurgled out Briggs in an agony of renewed grief.", "브리그스가 새삼 북받친 슬픔에 겨워 꺽꺽거리며 내뱉었다."]], [["\"She's not very ill any more.", "\"이제 그리 많이 편찮으신 것도 아니에요."], ["Console yourself, dear Miss Briggs.", "마음 푸세요, 브리그스 양."], ["She has only overeaten herself--that is all.", "그저 과식하셨을 뿐이에요. 그게 다예요."], ["She is greatly better.", "많이 나아지셨어요."], ["She will soon be quite restored again.", "곧 완전히 회복되실 거예요."], ["She is weak from being cupped and from medical treatment, but she will rally immediately.", "부항을 뜨고 치료를 받느라 기운이 빠지셨을 뿐, 금세 기력을 되찾으실 거예요."], ["Pray console yourself, and take a little more wine.\"", "부디 마음 푸시고, 포도주 좀 더 드세요.\""]], [["\"But why, why won't she see me again?\"", "\"하지만 왜, 왜 나를 다시 안 보려 하실까요?\""], ["Miss Briggs bleated out.", "브리그스가 가냘프게 울먹였다."], ["\"Oh, Matilda, Matilda, after three-and-twenty years'", "\"오, 머틸다, 머틸다, 스물세 해나 정을 나누었는데!"], ["tenderness! is this the return to your poor, poor Arabella?\"", "이게 그대의 가엾고 가엾은 아라벨라에게 돌아오는 보답인가요?\""]], [["\"Don't cry too much, poor Arabella,\"", "\"너무 울지 마세요, 가엾은 아라벨라.\""], ["the other said (with ever so little of a grin); \"she only won't see you, because she says you don't nurse her as well as I do.", "상대가 (아주 살짝 비웃음을 띠며) 말했다. \"그분이 당신을 안 보려 하는 건 그저, 당신이 나만큼 간병을 잘 못한다고 하시기 때문이에요."], ["It's no pleasure to me to sit up all night.", "밤새 곁을 지키는 게 나라고 즐거울 리 있겠어요."], ["I wish you might do it instead.\"", "차라리 당신이 대신해 주면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["\"Have I not tended that dear couch for years?\"", "\"내가 그 소중한 침상을 여러 해 동안 돌보지 않았던가요?\""], ["Arabella said, \"and now--\"", "아라벨라가 말했다. \"그런데 이제는…\""]], [["\"Now she prefers somebody else.", "\"이제는 다른 사람을 더 좋아하시는 거죠."], ["Well, sick people have these fancies, and must be humoured.", "뭐, 병든 사람들은 이런 변덕을 부리기 마련이고, 비위를 맞춰 드려야 하잖아요."], ["When she's well I shall go.\"", "그분이 나으시면 나는 떠날 거예요.\""]], [["\"Never, never,\"", "\"안 돼요, 절대 안 돼요.\""], ["Arabella exclaimed, madly inhaling her salts-bottle.", "아라벨라가 미친 듯이 각성제 병을 들이마시며 외쳤다."]], [["\"Never be well or never go, Miss Briggs?\" the other said, with the same provoking good-nature.", "\"절대로 안 나으신다는 거예요, 아니면 내가 절대 안 떠난다는 거예요, 브리그스 양?\""], ["\"Pooh--she will be well in a fortnight, when I shall go back to my little pupils at Queen's Crawley, and to their mother, who is a great deal more sick than our friend.", "상대가 똑같이 약 올리는 듯한 너그러운 투로 말했다. \"쳇, 그분은 보름이면 나으실 테고, 그러면 나는 퀸스크롤리의 어린 제자들에게로, 그리고 우리 벗보다 훨씬 더 편찮으신 그 애들 어머니에게로 돌아갈 거예요."], ["You need not be jealous about me, my dear Miss Briggs.", "나 때문에 질투하실 필요 없어요, 브리그스 양."], ["I am a poor little girl without any friends, or any harm in me.", "나는 벗도 없고 악의도 없는 가엾은 계집애일 뿐이에요."], ["I don't want to supplant you in Miss Crawley's good graces.", "크롤리 양의 총애를 두고 당신 자리를 빼앗을 생각은 없어요."], ["She will forget me a week after I am gone: and her affection for you has been the work of years.", "내가 떠나면 그분은 일주일 만에 나를 잊으실 거예요. 그런데 당신을 향한 애정은 여러 해에 걸쳐 쌓인 것이잖아요."], ["Give me a little wine if you please, my dear Miss Briggs, and let us be friends.", "괜찮으시면 포도주 좀 따라 주세요, 브리그스 양, 그리고 우리 벗이 되어요."], ["I'm sure I want friends.\"", "나야말로 정말 벗이 필요하거든요.\""]], [["The placable and soft-hearted Briggs speechlessly pushed out her hand at this appeal; but she felt the desertion most keenly for all that, and bitterly, bitterly moaned the fickleness of her Matilda.", "마음 약하고 무던한 브리그스는 이 호소에 말없이 손을 내밀었다. 그러나 그러면서도 자신이 버림받았다는 사실을 뼈저리게 느꼈고, 머틸다의 변덕을 두고 쓰디쓰게, 쓰디쓰게 한탄했다."], ["At the end of half an hour, the meal over, Miss Rebecca Sharp (for such, astonishing to state, is the name of her who has been described ingeniously as \"the person\"", "반 시간이 지나 식사가 끝나자, 리베카 샤프 양은(놀랍게도 이것이 바로 지금껏 교묘하게 \"그 여자\"라고 묘사되어 온 이의 이름이다) 다시 환자의 방으로 올라갔고, 더없이 상냥한 예의를 갖추어 가엾은 퍼킨을 그 방에서 몰아냈다."], ["hitherto), went upstairs again to her patient's rooms, from which, with the most engaging politeness, she eliminated poor Firkin. \"Thank you, Mrs.", "\"고마워요, 퍼킨 부인, 그만하면 됐어요."], ["Firkin, that will quite do; how nicely you make it!", "어쩜 이리 잘 만드세요!"], ["I will ring when anything is wanted.\"", "필요한 게 있으면 종을 울릴게요."], ["\"Thank you\"; and Firkin came downstairs in a tempest of jealousy, only the more dangerous because she was forced to confine it in her own bosom.", "고마워요.\" 그러자 퍼킨은 질투의 폭풍에 휩싸여 아래층으로 내려갔는데, 그 폭풍을 제 가슴속에 가둬 둘 수밖에 없는 처지인 만큼 더더욱 위험한 것이었다."]], [["Could it be the tempest which, as she passed the landing of the first floor, blew open the drawing-room door?", "이층 층계참을 지날 때 응접실 문이 활짝 열린 것은 그 폭풍 때문이었을까?"], ["No; it was stealthily opened by the hand of Briggs.", "아니었다. 그것은 브리그스의 손이 살그머니 연 것이었다."], ["Briggs had been on the watch.", "브리그스는 줄곧 망을 보고 있었던 것이다."], ["Briggs too well heard the creaking Firkin descend the stairs, and the clink of the spoon and gruel-basin the neglected female carried.", "브리그스는 삐걱대는 발소리와 함께 퍼킨이 계단을 내려가는 소리며, 그 푸대접받은 여자가 들고 가는 숟가락과 죽 그릇이 달그락대는 소리를 너무도 똑똑히 들었던 것이다."]], [["\"Well, Firkin?\"", "\"어때요, 퍼킨?\""], ["says she, as the other entered the apartment.", "그녀가 상대가 방에 들어서자 말했다."], ["\"Well, Jane?\"", "\"어때요, 제인?\""]], [["\"Wuss and wuss, Miss B.,\"", "\"점점 더 안 좋아져요, 브리그스 양.\""], ["Firkin said, wagging her head.", "퍼킨이 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다."]], [["\"Is she not better then?\"", "\"그럼 차도가 없으신 거예요?\""]], [["\"She never spoke but once, and I asked her if she felt a little more easy, and she told me to hold my stupid tongue.", "\"딱 한 번 말씀하셨는데, 제가 좀 편안해지셨냐고 여쭈니까 그 멍청한 혀 좀 닥치라고 하시잖아요."], ["Oh, Miss B., I never thought to have seen this day!\"", "오, 브리그스 양, 이런 날을 보게 될 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐어요!\""], ["And the water-works again began to play.", "그러더니 그 분수 같은 눈물이 다시 솟구치기 시작했다."]], [["\"What sort of a person is this Miss Sharp, Firkin?", "\"이 샤프 양이라는 사람은 대체 어떤 사람이에요, 퍼킨?"], ["I little thought, while enjoying my Christmas revels in the elegant home of my firm friends, the Reverend Lionel Delamere and his amiable lady, to find a stranger had taken my place in the affections of my dearest, my still dearest Matilda!\"", "나의 굳건한 벗인 라이오넬 들라미어 목사님과 그 인자한 사모님의 우아한 댁에서 성탄절 흥겨운 잔치를 즐기는 동안, 낯선 여자가 나의 더없이 소중한, 지금도 더없이 소중한 머틸다의 애정 속에서 내 자리를 차지해 버릴 줄은 정말 꿈에도 몰랐어요!\""], ["Miss Briggs, it will be seen by her language, was of a literary and sentimental turn, and had once published a volume of poems--\"Trills of the Nightingale\"--by subscription.", "그 말투에서 알 수 있듯, 브리그스 양은 문학적이고 감상적인 기질의 소유자로, 예전에 시집 한 권을 「나이팅게일의 떨림」이라는 제목으로 예약 출판한 적이 있었다."]], [["\"Miss B., they are all infatyated about that young woman,\"", "\"브리그스 양, 다들 그 젊은 여자한테 홀딱 빠져 버렸어요.\""], ["Firkin replied.", "퍼킨이 대답했다."], ["\"Sir Pitt wouldn't have let her go, but he daredn't refuse Miss Crawley anything.", "\"피트 경께서도 그 여자를 보내려 하지 않으셨지만, 크롤리 양 말씀이라면 감히 거절을 못 하시잖아요."], ["Mrs. Bute at the Rectory jist as bad--never happy out of her sight.", "목사관의 뷰트 부인도 어찌나 똑같은지, 그 여자가 눈앞에 없으면 한시도 마음을 못 놓으세요."], ["The Capting quite wild about her. Mr.", "대위님은 아주 그 여자한테 미쳐 계시고요."], ["Crawley mortial jealous. Since Miss C.", "크롤리 씨는 죽도록 질투하시고요."], ["was took ill, she won't have nobody near her but Miss Sharp, I can't tell for where nor for why; and I think somethink has bewidged everybody.\"", "크롤리 양께서 병이 나신 뒤로는 샤프 양 말고는 아무도 곁에 못 오게 하시는데, 어디서 왜 그러시는지 도무지 알 수가 없어요. 제 생각엔 뭔가가 모두를 홀려 버린 것 같아요.\""]], [["Rebecca passed that night in constant watching upon Miss Crawley; the next night the old lady slept so comfortably, that Rebecca had time for several hours'", "리베카는 그날 밤을 꼬박 크롤리 양 곁에서 지새우며 간호했다. 이튿날 밤에는 노부인이 어찌나 편안히 잠을 자던지, 리베카도 환자의 침대 발치 소파에서 몇 시간 단잠을 잘 수 있었다."], ["comfortable repose herself on the sofa, at the foot of her patroness's bed; very soon, Miss Crawley was so well that she sat up and laughed heartily at a perfect imitation of Miss Briggs and her grief, which Rebecca described to her. Briggs'", "머지않아 크롤리 양은 상태가 좋아져 자리에 일어나 앉았고, 리베카가 들려준 브리그스 양과 그 슬픔에 대한 완벽한 흉내에 깔깔거리며 웃었다."], ["weeping snuffle, and her manner of using the handkerchief, were so completely rendered that Miss Crawley became quite cheerful, to the admiration of the doctors when they visited her, who usually found this worthy woman of the world, when the least sickness attacked her, under the most abject depression and terror of death.", "브리그스의 훌쩍이는 콧소리며 손수건 쓰는 모양새까지 어찌나 똑같이 재현하던지, 크롤리 양은 자못 유쾌해졌고, 이는 진찰하러 온 의사들의 감탄을 자아냈다. 평소에 이 세상 물정에 밝은 부인은 아주 사소한 병에만 걸려도 더없이 비참하게 풀이 죽어 죽음을 두려워하기 일쑤였기 때문이다."]], [["Captain Crawley came every day, and received bulletins from Miss Rebecca respecting his aunt's health.", "크롤리 대위는 날마다 찾아와 리베카 양에게서 고모의 건강에 관한 소식을 들었다."], ["This improved so rapidly, that poor Briggs was allowed to see her patroness; and persons with tender hearts may imagine the smothered emotions of that sentimental female, and the affecting nature of the interview.", "병세가 어찌나 빨리 호전되던지, 가엾은 브리그스도 마침내 자신이 모시는 부인을 만나도록 허락되었다. 다정한 마음을 지닌 이들이라면 그 감상적인 여자가 짓눌렀던 감정과, 그 만남의 가슴 뭉클한 광경을 능히 상상할 수 있으리라."]], [["Miss Crawley liked to have Briggs in a good deal soon.", "이내 크롤리 양은 브리그스를 자주 곁에 두고 싶어 했다."], ["Rebecca used to mimic her to her face with the most admirable gravity, thereby rendering the imitation doubly piquant to her worthy patroness.", "리베카는 더없이 진지한 표정으로 그녀의 면전에서 그녀를 흉내 내곤 했는데, 덕분에 그 흉내는 점잖은 부인에게 갑절로 짜릿한 재미를 안겨 주는 것이었다."]], [["The causes which had led to the deplorable illness of Miss Crawley, and her departure from her brother's house in the country, were of such an unromantic nature that they are hardly fit to be explained in this genteel and sentimental novel.", "크롤리 양을 그토록 한심한 병으로 몰아넣고, 시골에 있는 동생의 집을 떠나게 만든 원인이라는 것은 어찌나 낭만과는 거리가 먼 것이던지, 이 점잖고 감상적인 소설에서 차마 설명하기에 적당치가 않다."], ["For how is it possible to hint of a delicate female, living in good society, that she ate and drank too much, and that a hot supper of lobsters profusely enjoyed at the Rectory was the reason of an indisposition which Miss Crawley herself persisted was solely attributable to the dampness of the weather?", "좋은 사회에 몸담은 어느 우아한 부인이 너무 먹고 너무 마셨다느니, 목사관에서 거하게 즐긴 따끈한 바닷가재 야식이 병의 원인이었다느니 하는 것을 어찌 입에 담을 수 있겠는가."], ["The attack was so sharp that Matilda--as his Reverence expressed it--was very nearly \"off the hooks\"; all the family were in a fever of expectation regarding the will, and Rawdon Crawley was making sure of at least forty thousand pounds before the commencement of the London season.", "정작 크롤리 양 본인은 그 병이 오로지 궂은 날씨 탓이라고 우겨 댔는데 말이다. 발작이 어찌나 심했던지, 목사님의 표현을 빌리자면 머틸다는 하마터면 \"황천길에 오를\""], ["Mr.", "뻔했다."], ["Crawley sent over a choice parcel of tracts, to prepare her for the change from Vanity Fair and Park Lane for another world; but a good doctor from Southampton being called in in time, vanquished the lobster which was so nearly fatal to her, and gave her sufficient strength to enable her to return to London.", "온 집안이 유언장을 두고 들뜬 기대에 들떠 있었고, 로던 크롤리는 런던 사교철이 시작되기 전에 적어도 사만 파운드는 확실히 손에 넣게 되었다고 믿어 의심치 않았다. 크롤리 씨는 그녀가 허영의 시장과 파크 레인을 떠나 저세상으로 옮겨 갈 채비를 하도록 엄선한 전도서 한 꾸러미를 보내왔다."], ["The Baronet did not disguise his exceeding mortification at the turn which affairs took.", "그러나 사우샘프턴에서 온 솜씨 좋은 의사가 때맞춰 불려 와, 그녀를 거의 죽음으로 몰아넣을 뻔한 바닷가재를 물리치고, 그녀가 런던으로 돌아갈 수 있을 만큼 기력을 회복시켜 주었다. 준남작은 일이 이렇게 돌아간 데 대한 극심한 분함을 굳이 감추려 하지 않았다."]], [["While everybody was attending on Miss Crawley, and messengers every hour from the Rectory were carrying news of her health to the affectionate folks there, there was a lady in another part of the house, being exceedingly ill, of whom no one took any notice at all; and this was the lady of Crawley herself.", "모두가 크롤리 양을 시중들고, 목사관의 다정한 식구들에게 그녀의 건강 소식을 전하느라 한 시간마다 심부름꾼이 오가는 동안, 집의 다른 한편에는 몹시 앓고 있으면서도 아무도 거들떠보지 않는 부인이 한 사람 있었으니, 바로 크롤리 부인 본인이었다. 그 솜씨 좋은 의사는 그녀를 보고 나서 고개를 가로저었다."], ["The good doctor shook his head after seeing her; to which visit Sir Pitt consented, as it could be paid without a fee; and she was left fading away in her lonely chamber, with no more heed paid to her than to a weed in the park.", "진찰료가 들지 않는다는 이유로 피트 경이 그 왕진을 허락했던 것이다. 그리하여 그녀는 외딴 방에서, 정원의 잡초만큼도 관심을 받지 못한 채 시들어 가도록 방치되었다."]], [["The young ladies, too, lost much of the inestimable benefit of their governess's instruction, So affectionate a nurse was Miss Sharp, that Miss Crawley would take her medicines from no other hand.", "젊은 숙녀들 또한 그 가정교사가 베푸는 더없이 값진 가르침의 혜택을 크게 잃고 말았다. 샤프 양은 어찌나 정성스러운 간병인이던지, 크롤리 양은 다른 사람의 손으로는 약을 받으려 하지 않았다."], ["Firkin had been deposed long before her mistress's departure from the country.", "퍼킨은 안주인이 시골을 떠나기 한참 전에 이미 그 자리에서 밀려난 처지였다."], ["That faithful attendant found a gloomy consolation on returning to London, in seeing Miss Briggs suffer the same pangs of jealousy and undergo the same faithless treatment to which she herself had been subject.", "그 충직한 시중꾼은 런던으로 돌아와, 브리그스 양이 자신이 겪었던 것과 똑같은 질투의 고통을 겪고 똑같은 배신을 당하는 꼴을 보며 울적한 위안을 얻었다."]], [["Captain Rawdon got an extension of leave on his aunt's illness, and remained dutifully at home.", "로던 대위는 고모가 병든 것을 핑계로 휴가를 연장하고는 효성스럽게 집에 머물렀다. 그는 늘 고모의 대기실에 붙어 있었다(고모는 작은 푸른색 응접실을 지나야 들어갈 수 있는 안방에 누워 앓고 있었다)."], ["He was always in her antechamber.", "그의 아버지는 늘 거기서 그와 마주쳤다."], ["(She lay sick in the state bedroom, into which you entered by the little blue saloon.) His father was always meeting him there; or if he came down the corridor ever so quietly, his father's door was sure to open, and the hyena face of the old gentleman to glare out.", "혹은 그가 아무리 조용히 복도를 내려와도 어김없이 아버지의 방문이 열리며 그 노신사의 하이에나 같은 얼굴이 빠끔 내다보는 것이었다. 무엇이 한 사람으로 하여금 다른 사람을 그토록 감시하게 만들었을까?"], ["What was it set one to watch the other so? A generous rivalry, no doubt, as to which should be most attentive to the dear sufferer in the state bedroom.", "안방에 누운 그 소중한 환자에게 누가 더 정성을 쏟느냐를 두고 벌어진, 의심할 바 없이 너그러운 경쟁이었다."], ["Rebecca used to come out and comfort both of them; or one or the other of them rather.", "리베카는 나와서 두 사람을 다 위로하곤 했다. 아니, 정확히는 둘 중 어느 한쪽을 말이다."], ["Both of these worthy gentlemen were most anxious to have news of the invalid from her little confidential messenger.", "이 점잖은 두 신사는 그 작은 심복 전령으로부터 환자의 소식을 듣고 싶어 안달이 나 있었던 것이다."]], [["At dinner--to which meal she descended for half an hour--she kept the peace between them: after which she disappeared for the night; when Rawdon would ride over to the depot of the 150th at Mudbury, leaving his papa to the society of Mr.", "만찬 자리에서, 그녀는 반 시간 정도 아래층에 내려와 두 사람 사이의 평화를 지켰다. 그 후 그녀는 밤을 보내러 사라졌고, 그러면 로던은 머드베리에 있는 백오십 연대의 주둔지로 말을 달려갔으며, 그의 아버지는 호록스 씨와 럼주를 탄 물을 벗 삼아 남았다."], ["Horrocks and his rum and water.", "그녀는 누구든 크롤리 양의 병실에서 보낸 적이 없을 만큼 진을 빼는 보름을 보냈다."], ["She passed as weary a fortnight as ever mortal spent in Miss Crawley's sick-room; but her little nerves seemed to be of iron, as she was quite unshaken by the duty and the tedium of the sick-chamber.", "그러나 그녀의 가냘픈 신경은 쇠로 되어 있는 듯, 병실의 의무와 따분함에도 조금도 흔들리지 않았다."]], [["She never told until long afterwards how painful that duty was; how peevish a patient was the jovial old lady; how angry; how sleepless; in what horrors of death; during what long nights she lay moaning, and in almost delirious agonies respecting that future world which she quite ignored when she was in good health.--Picture to yourself, oh fair young reader, a worldly, selfish, graceless, thankless, religionless old woman, writhing in pain and fear, and without her wig.", "그 의무가 얼마나 고통스러웠는지, 그 유쾌하던 노부인이 환자로서 얼마나 짜증스러웠는지, 얼마나 화를 잘 냈는지, 얼마나 잠을 못 이루었는지, 얼마나 죽음의 공포에 시달렸는지, 또 얼마나 긴 밤들을 신음하며, 건강할 때는 까맣게 무시하던 저세상을 두고 거의 헛소리에 가까운 고뇌에 빠져 누워 있었는지를, 그녀는 한참 뒤에야 비로소 털어놓았다. 오, 어여쁜 젊은 독자여, 속물적이고 이기적이고 천박하고 은혜를 모르고 신앙심도 없는 노파가 고통과 두려움에 몸부림치며, 그것도 가발마저 벗겨진 채 누운 모습을 한번 상상해 보라."], ["Picture her to yourself, and ere you be old, learn to love and pray!", "그 모습을 그려 보고, 그대 늙기 전에 사랑하고 기도하는 법을 배우라!"]], [["Sharp watched this graceless bedside with indomitable patience.", "샤프는 그 천박한 병상을 불굴의 인내로 지켜보았다."], ["Nothing escaped her; and, like a prudent steward, she found a use for everything.", "그 무엇도 그녀의 눈을 벗어나지 못했고, 그녀는 분별 있는 집사처럼 무엇이든 쓸모를 찾아냈다."], ["She told many a good story about Miss Crawley's illness in after days--stories which made the lady blush through her artificial carnations.", "훗날 그녀는 크롤리 양의 병에 관한 그럴듯한 이야기를 숱하게 들려주었는데, 그것은 그 부인이 인공적인 분홍 화장 너머로 얼굴을 붉히게 만드는 이야기들이었다."], ["During the illness she was never out of temper; always alert; she slept light, having a perfectly clear conscience; and could take that refreshment at almost any minute's warning.", "병간호를 하는 동안 그녀는 결코 신경질을 부리지 않았고, 늘 기민했다. 양심이 더없이 깨끗한 덕에 잠도 얕게 들었고, 거의 어느 순간에든 한마디만 일러 주면 그 휴식을 취할 수 있었다."], ["And so you saw very few traces of fatigue in her appearance.", "그래서 그녀의 모습에서는 피로의 흔적을 좀처럼 찾아볼 수 없었다."], ["Her face might be a trifle paler, and the circles round her eyes a little blacker than usual; but whenever she came out from the sick-room she was always smiling, fresh, and neat, and looked as trim in her little dressing-gown and cap, as in her smartest evening suit.", "얼굴빛이 살짝 더 창백하고 눈가의 그늘이 평소보다 조금 더 거뭇할 뿐, 병실에서 나올 때면 그녀는 언제나 미소를 띠고, 산뜻하고, 단정했으며, 자그마한 실내복과 모자 차림이 가장 멋들어진 야회복 차림 못지않게 맵시 있었다."]], [["The Captain thought so, and raved about her in uncouth convulsions.", "대위도 그렇게 여겼고, 어쭙잖은 발작을 일으키며 그녀에게 열을 올렸다. 사랑의 미늘 박힌 화살이 그의 둔한 가죽을 꿰뚫었던 것이다."], ["The barbed shaft of love had penetrated his dull hide.", "여섯 주, 가까이 지냄, 기회, 이것들이 그를 완전히 사로잡고 말았다."], ["Six weeks--appropinquity--opportunity--had victimised him completely.", "그는 하필이면 이 세상 모든 사람 중에서 목사관에 있는 고모를 속내를 털어놓는 상대로 삼았다."], ["He made a confidante of his aunt at the Rectory, of all persons in the world.", "그녀는 그 일로 그를 놀려 댔다. 그녀는 그의 어리석음을 진작 알아챘던 것이다."], ["She rallied him about it; she had perceived his folly; she warned him; she finished by owning that little Sharp was the most clever, droll, odd, good-natured, simple, kindly creature in England.", "그녀는 그에게 경고했다. 그러고는 마침내, 샤프야말로 영국에서 가장 영리하고 익살맞고 별나고 마음씨 곱고 꾸밈없고 다정한 사람이라고 인정했다."], ["Rawdon must not trifle with her affections, though--dear Miss Crawley would never pardon him for that; for she, too, was quite overcome by the little governess, and loved Sharp like a daughter.", "하지만 로던은 그녀의 마음을 가지고 놀아서는 안 된다, 소중한 크롤리 양께서 그것만큼은 결코 용서하지 않으실 테니까. 크롤리 양 역시 그 자그마한 가정교사에게 홀딱 반해 샤프를 친딸처럼 사랑하셨기 때문이다."], ["Rawdon must go away--go back to his regiment and naughty London, and not play with a poor artless girl's feelings.", "로던은 떠나야 한다, 자기 연대로, 또 그 못된 런던으로 돌아가야지, 가엾고 순진한 처녀의 감정을 가지고 놀아서는 안 된다는 것이었다."]], [["Many and many a time this good-natured lady, compassionating the forlorn life-guardsman's condition, gave him an opportunity of seeing Miss Sharp at the Rectory, and of walking home with her, as we have seen.", "이 마음씨 좋은 부인은 그 가련한 근위병의 처지를 가엾이 여겨, 우리가 앞서 본 대로 그가 목사관에서 샤프 양을 만나고 또 함께 집까지 걸어올 기회를 몇 번이고, 몇 번이고 만들어 주었다."], ["When men of a certain sort, ladies, are in love, though they see the hook and the string, and the whole apparatus with which they are to be taken, they gorge the bait nevertheless--they must come to it--they must swallow it--and are presently struck and landed gasping.", "숙녀 여러분, 어떤 부류의 남자들은 사랑에 빠지면, 자기를 낚으려는 그 낚싯바늘과 낚싯줄과 온갖 장치를 빤히 보면서도 기어이 미끼를 덥석 무는 법이다. 그들은 다가올 수밖에 없고, 삼킬 수밖에 없으며, 이내 낚여 올라와 가쁜 숨을 헐떡이게 마련이다."], ["Rawdon saw there was a manifest intention on Mrs. Bute's part to captivate him with Rebecca.", "로던은 뷰트 부인이 리베카로 자신을 사로잡으려는 속셈이 빤히 드러나는 것을 보았다."], ["He was not very wise; but he was a man about town, and had seen several seasons.", "그는 그리 영리하지는 않았으나, 도시 물을 먹은 사내였고 사교철도 여러 번 겪어 본 터였다."], ["A light dawned upon his dusky soul, as he thought, through a speech of Mrs. Bute's.", "뷰트 부인의 어떤 말 한마디를 통해, 그의 둔한 영혼에 한 줄기 빛이 비쳤다고 그는 생각했다."]], [["\"Mark my words, Rawdon,\"", "\"내 말 명심해, 로던.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"You will have Miss Sharp one day for your relation.\"", "\"자네는 언젠가 샤프 양을 친척으로 두게 될 거야.\""]], [["\"What relation--my cousin, hey, Mrs.", "\"무슨 친척요? 제 사촌형수요, 뷰트 부인?"], ["Bute? James sweet on her, hey?\"", "제임스가 그 여자한테 반했나요?\""], ["inquired the waggish officer.", "익살맞은 장교가 물었다."]], [["\"More than that,\"", "\"그 이상이지.\""], ["Mrs. Bute said, with a flash from her black eyes.", "뷰트 부인이 검은 눈을 번뜩이며 말했다."]], [["\"Not Pitt?", "\"피트 형은 아니죠?"], ["He sha'n't have her.", "형은 그 여자를 가질 수 없어요."], ["The sneak a'n't worthy of her.", "그 비열한 작자한테는 과분한 여자라고요."], ["He's booked to Lady Jane Sheepshanks.\"", "형은 제인 십섕크스 아가씨와 약혼이 정해져 있잖아요.\""]], [["\"You men perceive nothing.", "\"자네들 남자들은 아무것도 못 알아챈다니까. 이 어리석고 눈먼 양반아."], ["You silly, blind creature--if anything happens to Lady Crawley, Miss Sharp will be your mother-in-law; and that's what will happen.\"", "크롤리 부인에게 무슨 일이라도 생기면, 샤프 양은 자네 계모가 될 거야. 바로 그렇게 될 거라고.\""]], [["Rawdon Crawley, Esquire, gave vent to a prodigious whistle, in token of astonishment at this announcement.", "로던 크롤리 귀하는 이 소식에 놀랐다는 표시로 어마어마한 휘파람을 길게 내뿜었다. 그는 그것을 부인할 수 없었다."], ["He couldn't deny it.", "아버지가 샤프 양을 노골적으로 마음에 들어 하는 것을 그도 진작 눈치챘던 것이다."], ["His father's evident liking for Miss Sharp had not escaped him.", "그는 그 노신사의 성품을 잘 알았다. 그토록 파렴치한 늙은…"], ["He knew the old gentleman's character well; and a more unscrupulous old--whyou--he did not conclude the sentence, but walked home, curling his mustachios, and convinced he had found a clue to Mrs.", "빌어먹을… 그는 말을 끝맺지 않고, 콧수염을 꼬며 집으로 걸어갔다."], ["Bute's mystery.", "뷰트 부인의 수수께끼를 풀 실마리를 찾았다고 확신하면서 말이다."]], [["\"By Jove, it's too bad,\"", "\"이런, 너무하잖아.\""], ["thought Rawdon, \"too bad, by Jove!", "로던은 생각했다. \"정말 너무해!"], ["I do believe the woman wants the poor girl to be ruined, in order that she shouldn't come into the family as Lady Crawley.\"", "저 여자는 그 가엾은 처녀가 크롤리 부인으로 집안에 들어오지 못하게 하려고, 차라리 그 애를 망쳐 버리고 싶은 거야.\""]], [["When he saw Rebecca alone, he rallied her about his father's attachment in his graceful way.", "그는 리베카와 단둘이 있을 때, 특유의 멋들어진 태도로 아버지가 그녀에게 빠진 일을 두고 놀려 댔다."], ["She flung up her head scornfully, looked him full in the face, and said,", "그녀는 도도하게 고개를 쳐들고 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 쳐다보며 말했다."]], [["\"Well, suppose he is fond of me.", "\"그래요, 그분이 나를 좋아한다고 칩시다."], ["I know he is, and others too.", "그러시는 걸 나도 알아요, 다른 분들도요."], ["You don't think I am afraid of him, Captain Crawley?", "내가 그분을 무서워하는 줄 아세요, 크롤리 대위님?"], ["You don't suppose I can't defend my own honour,\"", "내가 내 명예 하나 못 지킬 줄 아세요?\""], ["said the little woman, looking as stately as a queen.", "그 작은 여자가 여왕처럼 위엄 있는 모습으로 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, ah, why--give you fair warning--look out, you know--that's all,\"", "\"오, 아, 글쎄, 그저 미리 일러두는 거예요. 조심하라고요, 그뿐이에요.\""], ["said the mustachio-twiddler.", "콧수염을 만지작거리는 사내가 말했다."]], [["\"You hint at something not honourable, then?\"", "\"그럼 떳떳하지 못한 무언가를 넌지시 비추시는 거군요?\""], ["said she, flashing out.", "그녀가 발끈하며 말했다."]], [["\"O Gad--really--Miss Rebecca,\"", "\"오, 이런, 정말이지, 리베카 양.\""], ["the heavy dragoon interposed.", "그 둔한 용기병이 끼어들었다."]], [["\"Do you suppose I have no feeling of self-respect, because I am poor and friendless, and because rich people have none?", "\"내가 가난하고 의지할 데 없다고 해서, 또 부자들에게 그런 게 없다고 해서, 나에게도 자존심이라는 게 없는 줄 아세요?"], ["Do you think, because I am a governess, I have not as much sense, and feeling, and good breeding as you gentlefolks in Hampshire?", "내가 가정교사라고 해서, 햄프셔의 점잖으신 양반들만큼 분별도, 감정도, 교양도 없는 줄 아세요? 나는 몽모랑시 가문 사람이에요."], ["I'm a Montmorency. Do you suppose a Montmorency is not as good as a Crawley?\"", "몽모랑시 가문이 크롤리 가문만 못한 줄 아세요?\""]], [["When Miss Sharp was agitated, and alluded to her maternal relatives, she spoke with ever so slight a foreign accent, which gave a great charm to her clear ringing voice. \"No,\" she continued, kindling as she spoke to the Captain; \"I can endure poverty, but not shame--neglect, but not insult; and insult from--from you.\"", "샤프 양은 흥분해서 외가 친척들을 들먹일 때면, 아주 살짝 이국적인 억양으로 말했는데, 그것이 그녀의 또랑또랑하고 낭랑한 목소리에 큰 매력을 더해 주었다. \"아니요.\" 그녀가 대위를 향해 점점 더 격해지며 말을 이었다. \"나는 가난은 견딜 수 있어도 치욕은 못 견뎌요. 무시는 견뎌도 모욕은 못 견뎌요. 게다가 다른 누구도 아닌… 당신에게서 받는 모욕은요.\""]], [["Her feelings gave way, and she burst into tears.", "복받친 감정에 그녀는 무너져 내려 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["\"Hang it, Miss Sharp--Rebecca--by Jove--upon my soul, I wouldn't for a thousand pounds.", "\"제기랄, 샤프 양, 리베카, 맹세코, 정말이지, 천 파운드를 준대도 그런 뜻은 아니었어요."], ["Stop, Rebecca!\"", "그만, 리베카!\""]], [["She was gone.", "그녀는 가 버렸다."], ["She drove out with Miss Crawley that day.", "그날 그녀는 크롤리 양과 함께 마차를 타고 외출했다."], ["It was before the latter's illness.", "크롤리 양이 병에 걸리기 전의 일이었다."], ["At dinner she was unusually brilliant and lively; but she would take no notice of the hints, or the nods, or the clumsy expostulations of the humiliated, infatuated guardsman.", "만찬 자리에서 그녀는 유난히 눈부시고 활기찼으나, 그 굴욕당한, 정신 못 차리는 근위병이 보내는 암시며, 끄덕임이며, 어설픈 항변에는 전혀 아랑곳하지 않았다."], ["Skirmishes of this sort passed perpetually during the little campaign--tedious to relate, and similar in result.", "이런 식의 자그마한 접전이 그 짤막한 전투 내내 끊임없이 이어졌는데, 일일이 늘어놓기에는 따분하고 결과도 비슷했다."], ["The Crawley heavy cavalry was maddened by defeat, and routed every day.", "크롤리 가의 중기병은 패배에 미쳐 날뛰었고, 날마다 패주했다."]], [["If the Baronet of Queen's Crawley had not had the fear of losing his sister's legacy before his eyes, he never would have permitted his dear girls to lose the educational blessings which their invaluable governess was conferring upon them.", "퀸스크롤리의 준남작이 누이의 유산을 잃을지 모른다는 두려움을 눈앞에 두지 않았더라면, 그는 결코 사랑하는 딸들이 그 더없이 값진 가정교사가 베푸는 교육의 축복을 잃도록 내버려 두지 않았으리라."], ["The old house at home seemed a desert without her, so useful and pleasant had Rebecca made herself there.", "리베카가 그곳에서 그토록 쓸모 있고 즐거운 존재가 되어 준 덕에, 그녀가 없는 시골의 낡은 저택은 황량한 사막 같았다."], ["Sir Pitt's letters were not copied and corrected; his books not made up; his household business and manifold schemes neglected, now that his little secretary was away.", "그 작은 비서가 떠나고 나니 피트 경의 편지는 정서되지도 교정되지도 않았고, 장부는 정리되지 않았으며, 집안일과 갖가지 계획도 등한시되었다."], ["And it was easy to see how necessary such an amanuensis was to him, by the tenor and spelling of the numerous letters which he sent to her, entreating her and commanding her to return.", "그녀에게 보낸 수많은 편지의 내용과 철자만 보아도, 그런 필경사가 그에게 얼마나 긴요한 존재였는지 쉽게 알 수 있었다. 그 편지들은 그녀에게 돌아와 달라고 애원하고 또 명령하는 것이었다."], ["Almost every day brought a frank from the Baronet, enclosing the most urgent prayers to Becky for her return, or conveying pathetic statements to Miss Crawley, regarding the neglected state of his daughters'", "거의 날마다 준남작의 무료 우편이 도착했는데, 베키에게 돌아와 달라는 더없이 절박한 간청을 담고 있거나, 방치된 딸들의 교육 상태를 두고 크롤리 양에게 호소하는 애절한 사연을 담고 있었다."], ["education; of which documents Miss Crawley took very little heed.", "그런 문서에 크롤리 양은 거의 신경을 쓰지 않았다."]], [["Miss Briggs was not formally dismissed, but her place as companion was a sinecure and a derision; and her company was the fat spaniel in the drawing-room, or occasionally the discontented Firkin in the housekeeper's closet.", "브리그스 양이 정식으로 해고된 것은 아니었으나, 말동무로서의 그녀의 자리는 한직이자 웃음거리로 전락했다. 그녀의 벗이라고는 응접실의 살진 스패니얼이거나, 이따금 가정부 방에서 만나는 시무룩한 퍼킨뿐이었다."], ["Nor though the old lady would by no means hear of Rebecca's departure, was the latter regularly installed in office in Park Lane.", "또한 노부인이 리베카가 떠난다는 말은 결코 들으려 하지 않았으면서도, 리베카가 파크 레인에 정식으로 자리를 잡은 것도 아니었다."], ["Like many wealthy people, it was Miss Crawley's habit to accept as much service as she could get from her inferiors; and good-naturedly to take leave of them when she no longer found them useful.", "부유한 사람들이 흔히 그렇듯, 크롤리 양은 아랫사람들에게서 받아 낼 수 있는 만큼 시중을 받아 내다가, 더 이상 쓸모가 없어지면 너그러운 척 작별을 고하는 것이 버릇이었다."], ["Gratitude among certain rich folks is scarcely natural or to be thought of.", "어떤 부류의 부자들에게 감사라는 것은 좀처럼 자연스럽지도, 기대할 만하지도 않은 것이다."], ["They take needy people's services as their due.", "그들은 가난한 이들의 시중을 당연한 자기 몫으로 받아들인다."], ["Nor have you, O poor parasite and humble hanger-on, much reason to complain!", "그렇다고 오, 가엾은 식객이요 비천한 빌붙이여, 그대 또한 그리 불평할 처지는 못 된다!"], ["Your friendship for Dives is about as sincere as the return which it usually gets.", "부자에 대한 그대의 우정이란 으레 그것이 받는 보답만큼이나 진실한 것이니 말이다."], ["It is money you love, and not the man; and were Croesus and his footman to change places you know, you poor rogue, who would have the benefit of your allegiance.", "그대가 사랑하는 것은 돈이지 사람이 아니다. 그러니 크로이소스와 그의 하인이 자리를 바꾼다면, 이 가엾은 악당아, 그대도 알다시피 누가 그대의 충성에서 덕을 볼지는 뻔한 노릇이다."]], [["And I am not sure that, in spite of Rebecca's simplicity and activity, and gentleness and untiring good humour, the shrewd old London lady, upon whom these treasures of friendship were lavished, had not a lurking suspicion all the while of her affectionate nurse and friend.", "그리고 나는, 리베카의 꾸밈없음과 부지런함과 상냥함과 지칠 줄 모르는 명랑함에도 불구하고, 그 우정의 보물을 듬뿍 받은 그 약빠른 런던 노부인이 줄곧 자신의 다정한 간병인이자 벗을 어렴풋이 의심하고 있지 않았다고는 장담하지 못하겠다."], ["It must have often crossed Miss Crawley's mind that nobody does anything for nothing.", "세상에 거저 무언가를 해 주는 사람은 없다는 생각이 크롤리 양의 머릿속을 자주 스쳤음이 분명하다."], ["If she measured her own feeling towards the world, she must have been pretty well able to gauge those of the world towards herself; and perhaps she reflected that it is the ordinary lot of people to have no friends if they themselves care for nobody.", "그녀가 세상을 향한 자신의 감정을 가늠해 본다면, 세상이 자신을 향해 품은 감정도 제법 잘 헤아릴 수 있었을 터이다. 또한 어쩌면 그녀는, 스스로 아무도 아끼지 않는 사람에게는 벗이 없는 것이 인지상정임을 떠올렸을지도 모른다."]], [["Well, meanwhile Becky was the greatest comfort and convenience to her, and she gave her a couple of new gowns, and an old necklace and shawl, and showed her friendship by abusing all her intimate acquaintances to her new confidante (than which there can't be a more touching proof of regard), and meditated vaguely some great future benefit--to marry her perhaps to Clump, the apothecary, or to settle her in some advantageous way of life; or at any rate, to send her back to Queen's Crawley when she had done with her, and the full London season had begun.", "어쨌든 그러는 동안 베키는 그녀에게 더없는 위안이자 편의가 되어 주었고, 그래서 그녀는 베키에게 새 드레스 두 벌과 낡은 목걸이와 숄을 주었으며, 새로 마음을 터놓는 그 상대에게 자신의 모든 절친한 지인들을 헐뜯음으로써 우정을 표시했고(이보다 더 가슴 뭉클한 호의의 증거는 없는 법이다), 막연하나마 장차 베키에게 큰 은혜를 베풀 궁리를 했다. 이를테면 약제사 클럼프와 짝지어 주거나, 무언가 유리한 살길에 정착시켜 주거나, 아니면 적어도 그녀가 더 이상 필요 없어지고 런던 사교철이 본격적으로 시작되면 퀸스크롤리로 돌려보내는 것이었다."]], [["When Miss Crawley was convalescent and descended to the drawing-room, Becky sang to her, and otherwise amused her; when she was well enough to drive out, Becky accompanied her.", "크롤리 양이 회복기에 접어들어 응접실로 내려오게 되자, 베키는 그녀에게 노래를 불러 주고 이런저런 방법으로 그녀를 즐겁게 해 주었다. 그녀가 마차로 외출할 만큼 건강해지자 베키도 동행했다."], ["And amongst the drives which they took, whither, of all places in the world, did Miss Crawley's admirable good-nature and friendship actually induce her to penetrate, but to Russell Square, Bloomsbury, and the house of John Sedley, Esquire.", "그런데 그 숱한 외출 가운데, 크롤리 양의 그 갸륵한 너그러움과 우정이 그녀를 이끌어 기어이 발을 들여놓게 한 곳이, 이 세상 그 많은 곳 중에 하필 어디였겠는가. 바로 블룸즈버리의 러셀 광장, 존 세들리 귀하의 집이었다."]], [["Ere that event, many notes had passed, as may be imagined, between the two dear friends.", "그 일이 있기에 앞서, 능히 짐작할 수 있듯, 두 절친한 벗 사이에 숱한 편지가 오갔다."], ["During the months of Rebecca's stay in Hampshire, the eternal friendship had (must it be owned?) suffered considerable diminution, and grown so decrepit and feeble with old age as to threaten demise altogether.", "리베카가 햄프셔에 머무는 몇 달 동안, 그 영원한 우정은(인정해야 할까?) 상당히 시들어, 노쇠한 나머지 늙고 쇠약해져 아예 숨을 거둘 지경에 이르렀던 것이다. 사실인즉 두 처녀 모두 골몰할 자기만의 진짜 관심사가 있었다."], ["The fact is, both girls had their own real affairs to think of: Rebecca her advance with her employers--Amelia her own absorbing topic.", "리베카에게는 고용주들 사이에서 자기 입지를 다지는 일이, 애밀리아에게는 그녀 자신을 온통 사로잡은 화제가 있었다."], ["When the two girls met, and flew into each other's arms with that impetuosity which distinguishes the behaviour of young ladies towards each other, Rebecca performed her part of the embrace with the most perfect briskness and energy.", "두 처녀가 만나 젊은 숙녀들이 서로를 대할 때 특유의 그 격정으로 와락 부둥켜안았을 때, 리베카는 더없이 활기차고 힘차게 자기 몫의 포옹을 해냈다."], ["Poor little Amelia blushed as she kissed her friend, and thought she had been guilty of something very like coldness towards her.", "가엾은 어린 애밀리아는 벗에게 입맞추며 얼굴을 붉혔는데, 자신이 그녀에게 어딘가 냉담함에 가까운 잘못을 저질러 온 것은 아닐까 싶어서였다."]], [["Their first interview was but a very short one.", "두 사람의 첫 만남은 그저 아주 짧은 것이었다."], ["Amelia was just ready to go out for a walk.", "애밀리아는 막 산책을 나서려던 참이었다."], ["Miss Crawley was waiting in her carriage below, her people wondering at the locality in which they found themselves, and gazing upon honest Sambo, the black footman of Bloomsbury, as one of the queer natives of the place.", "크롤리 양은 아래층에서 마차에 앉아 기다리고 있었는데, 그녀의 하인들은 자기들이 와 있는 동네에 어리둥절해하며, 블룸즈버리의 흑인 하인인 정직한 샘보를 마치 이 지방의 진기한 토박이라도 되는 양 빤히 쳐다보았다. 그러나 애밀리아가 그 다정한 미소 띤 얼굴로 내려왔을 때(리베카가 그녀를 자기 벗에게 소개해야 했다."], ["But when Amelia came down with her kind smiling looks (Rebecca must introduce her to her friend, Miss Crawley was longing to see her, and was too ill to leave her carriage)--when, I say, Amelia came down, the Park Lane shoulder-knot aristocracy wondered more and more that such a thing could come out of Bloomsbury; and Miss Crawley was fairly captivated by the sweet blushing face of the young lady who came forward so timidly and so gracefully to pay her respects to the protector of her friend.", "크롤리 양은 그녀를 몹시 보고 싶어 했으나, 너무 편찮아 마차에서 내릴 수가 없었다), 그러니까 내 말은, 애밀리아가 내려왔을 때, 파크 레인의 견장 단 귀족 하인 무리는 이런 사람이 블룸즈버리에서 나올 수 있다는 데에 점점 더 놀라워했다. 그리고 크롤리 양은 자기 벗의 보호자에게 인사를 드리러 그토록 수줍고도 우아하게 다가오는 그 젊은 숙녀의, 발그레 물든 사랑스러운 얼굴에 그야말로 마음을 빼앗기고 말았다."]], [["\"What a complexion, my dear!", "\"어쩜 저런 살결이람, 얘야!"], ["What a sweet voice!\"", "어쩜 저리 고운 목소리람!\""], ["Miss Crawley said, as they drove away westward after the little interview.", "그 짤막한 만남이 끝난 뒤 일행이 서쪽으로 마차를 달릴 때 크롤리 양이 말했다."], ["\"My dear Sharp, your young friend is charming.", "\"샤프 양, 네 어린 벗은 정말 매력적이구나."], ["Send for her to Park Lane, do you hear?\"", "그 애를 파크 레인으로 부르렴, 알겠지?\""], ["Miss Crawley had a good taste.", "크롤리 양은 안목이 있었다."], ["She liked natural manners--a little timidity only set them off.", "그녀는 자연스러운 몸가짐을 좋아했고, 약간의 수줍음은 그 몸가짐을 한층 돋보이게 할 뿐이었다."], ["She liked pretty faces near her; as she liked pretty pictures and nice china.", "그녀는 예쁜 그림과 고운 도자기를 좋아하듯, 자기 곁에 예쁜 얼굴을 두기를 좋아했다."], ["She talked of Amelia with rapture half a dozen times that day.", "그날 그녀는 애밀리아 이야기를 예닐곱 번이나 황홀하게 늘어놓았다."], ["She mentioned her to Rawdon Crawley, who came dutifully to partake of his aunt's chicken.", "고모의 닭고기를 함께 들겠다고 효성스레 찾아온 로던 크롤리에게도 그녀 이야기를 꺼냈다."]], [["Of course, on this Rebecca instantly stated that Amelia was engaged to be married--to a Lieutenant Osborne--a very old flame.", "물론 이 말에 리베카는 즉시, 애밀리아가 어느 중위와, 오스본 중위라는 아주 오래된 옛 연인과 약혼한 사이라고 일러 주었다."]], [["\"Is he a man in a line-regiment?\"", "\"전열 보병 연대 소속의 사내인가?\""], ["Captain Crawley asked, remembering after an effort, as became a guardsman, the number of the regiment, the --th.", "크롤리 대위가, 근위병답게 한참 애를 쓴 끝에 그 연대 번호 제몇 연대를 떠올리며 물었다."]], [["Rebecca thought that was the regiment.", "리베카는 그 연대가 맞는 것 같다고 했다."], ["\"The Captain's name,\"", "\"그 대위 이름은,\""], ["she said, \"was Captain Dobbin.\"", "그녀가 말했다. \"도빈 대위였어요.\""]], [["\"A lanky gawky fellow,\"", "\"껑충하고 어설픈 친구지.\""], ["said Crawley, \"tumbles over everybody.", "크롤리가 말했다. \"아무한테나 부딪치고 다니는."], ["I know him; and Osborne's a goodish-looking fellow, with large black whiskers?\"", "내가 그 친구를 알아. 그리고 오스본은 검은 구레나룻이 무성한, 제법 잘생긴 친구지?\""]], [["\"Enormous,\"", "\"엄청나죠.\""], ["Miss Rebecca Sharp said, \"and enormously proud of them, I assure you.\"", "리베카 샤프 양이 말했다. \"그리고 장담컨대, 그 구레나룻을 엄청나게 자랑스러워하는걸요.\""]], [["Captain Rawdon Crawley burst into a horse-laugh by way of reply; and being pressed by the ladies to explain, did so when the explosion of hilarity was over.", "크롤리 대위는 대답 삼아 말처럼 껄껄 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 숙녀들이 설명해 보라고 다그치자, 그 웃음의 폭발이 가라앉은 뒤에야 입을 열었다."], ["\"He fancies he can play at billiards,\"", "\"그 친구는 자기가 당구를 칠 줄 안다고 여기더라고요.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"I won two hundred of him at the Cocoa-Tree.", "\"내가 코코아트리에서 그 친구한테 이백 파운드를 땄어요."], ["HE play, the young flat!", "그 친구가 당구를 친다니, 그 풋내기가 말이죠!"], ["He'd have played for anything that day, but his friend Captain Dobbin carried him off, hang him!\"", "그날 무엇을 걸고든 칠 기세였는데, 그 친구 도빈 대위가 데려가 버렸지 뭐예요, 빌어먹을 작자 같으니!\""]], [["\"Rawdon, Rawdon, don't be so wicked,\"", "\"로던, 로던, 그렇게 못되게 굴지 마라.\""], ["Miss Crawley remarked, highly pleased.", "크롤리 양이 무척 흐뭇해하며 한마디 했다."]], [["\"Why, ma'am, of all the young fellows I've seen out of the line, I think this fellow's the greenest.", "\"아니, 고모, 근위대 밖에서 본 젊은 친구들 중에서 이 친구가 제일 풋내기인 것 같아요."], ["Tarquin and Deuceace get what money they like out of him.", "타퀸이랑 듀세이스가 마음껏 돈을 우려내고 있죠."], ["He'd go to the deuce to be seen with a lord.", "그 친구는 귀족과 함께 있는 모습을 보이려고 지옥에라도 갈 위인이에요."], ["He pays their dinners at Greenwich, and they invite the company.\"", "그리니치에서 그자들 만찬값을 다 치르는데, 정작 손님은 그자들이 부른다니까요.\""]], [["\"And very pretty company too, I dare say.\"", "\"손님들도 아주 훌륭하겠구나, 짐작건대.\""]], [["\"Quite right, Miss Sharp.", "\"바로 맞혔어요, 샤프 양."], ["Right, as usual, Miss Sharp.", "늘 그렇듯 정확해요, 샤프 양."], ["Uncommon pretty company--haw, haw!\"", "더없이 훌륭한 손님들이죠, 하, 하!\""], ["and the Captain laughed more and more, thinking he had made a good joke.", "대위는 자기가 그럴듯한 농담을 했다고 여기며 점점 더 크게 웃어 댔다."]], [["\"Rawdon, don't be naughty!\"", "\"로던, 짓궂게 굴지 말렴!\""], ["his aunt exclaimed.", "고모가 외쳤다."]], [["\"Well, his father's a City man--immensely rich, they say.", "\"뭐, 그 친구 아버지가 시내 상인인데, 어마어마한 부자라더군요."], ["Hang those City fellows, they must bleed; and I've not done with him yet, I can tell you.", "그 시내 작자들이란, 빌어먹을, 단단히 피를 봐야 마땅하죠. 그리고 그 친구는 아직 끝난 게 아니에요, 장담하죠."], ["Haw, haw!\"", "하, 하!\""]], [["\"Fie, Captain Crawley; I shall warn Amelia.", "\"저런, 크롤리 대위. 내가 애밀리아에게 일러 줘야겠구나."], ["A gambling husband!\"", "노름꾼 남편이라니!\""]], [["\"Horrid, ain't he, hey?\"", "\"끔찍하죠, 안 그래요?\""], ["the Captain said with great solemnity; and then added, a sudden thought having struck him: \"Gad, I say, ma'am, we'll have him here.\"", "대위가 자못 엄숙하게 말하더니, 문득 어떤 생각이 떠올라 덧붙였다. \"이런, 그렇지, 고모, 그 친구를 여기로 부릅시다.\""]], [["\"Is he a presentable sort of a person?\"", "\"점잖은 자리에 내놓을 만한 사람이냐?\""], ["the aunt inquired.", "고모가 물었다."]], [["\"Presentable?--oh, very well.", "\"내놓을 만하냐고요? 오, 더없이요."], ["You wouldn't see any difference,\"", "차이를 못 느끼실걸요.\" 크롤리 대위가 대답했다."], ["Captain Crawley answered.", "\"고모가 손님을 몇 분 맞기 시작하면 꼭 부릅시다. 그리고 그 친구 뭐라더라, 그 친구의 정인 말이죠."], ["\"Do let's have him, when you begin to see a few people; and his whatdyecallem--his inamorato--eh, Miss Sharp; that's what you call it--comes.", "안 그래요, 샤프 양? 그렇게 부른다죠?"], ["Gad, I'll write him a note, and have him; and I'll try if he can play piquet as well as billiards.", "그 아가씨도 오라고 하고요. 이런, 내가 그 친구한테 편지를 써서 부를게요."], ["Where does he live, Miss Sharp?\"", "그리고 그 친구가 당구만큼 피켓도 잘 치는지 한번 시험해 봐야지. 그 친구가 어디 산다고 했죠, 샤프 양?\""]], [["Miss Sharp told Crawley the Lieutenant's town address; and a few days after this conversation, Lieutenant Osborne received a letter, in Captain Rawdon's schoolboy hand, and enclosing a note of invitation from Miss Crawley.", "샤프 양은 크롤리에게 그 중위의 도시 주소를 일러 주었다. 이 대화가 있은 지 며칠 뒤, 오스본 중위는 로던 대위의 학생 같은 필체로 쓰인, 크롤리 양의 초대장을 동봉한 편지를 받았다."]], [["Rebecca despatched also an invitation to her darling Amelia, who, you may be sure, was ready enough to accept it when she heard that George was to be of the party.", "리베카는 또한 자신의 사랑하는 애밀리아에게도 초대장을 띄웠는데, 조지가 그 모임에 끼게 된다는 말을 들은 그녀가 흔쾌히 그것을 받아들였으리라는 것은 능히 짐작할 수 있다."], ["It was arranged that Amelia was to spend the morning with the ladies of Park Lane, where all were very kind to her.", "애밀리아는 아침나절을 파크 레인의 숙녀들과 보내기로 정해졌고, 그곳에서 다들 그녀에게 무척 친절했다."], ["Rebecca patronised her with calm superiority: she was so much the cleverer of the two, and her friend so gentle and unassuming, that she always yielded when anybody chose to command, and so took Rebecca's orders with perfect meekness and good humour.", "리베카는 침착한 우월감으로 그녀를 보살피듯 대했다. 둘 중에 그녀가 훨씬 영리했고, 그녀의 벗은 어찌나 온순하고 겸손한지 누구든 명령하려 들면 늘 따랐던 터라, 리베카의 분부도 더없이 순순하고 기분 좋게 받아들였다."], ["Miss Crawley's graciousness was also remarkable.", "크롤리 양의 자상함 또한 눈여겨볼 만했다."], ["She continued her raptures about little Amelia, talked about her before her face as if she were a doll, or a servant, or a picture, and admired her with the most benevolent wonder possible.", "그녀는 어린 애밀리아를 두고 황홀해하기를 그치지 않았고, 그녀 면전에서 마치 인형이나 하인이나 그림 이야기라도 하듯 그녀에 대해 떠들어 대며, 더없이 자애로운 경탄으로 그녀를 감탄해 마지않았다."], ["I admire that admiration which the genteel world sometimes extends to the commonalty.", "나는 점잖은 세상이 이따금 평민에게 베푸는 그런 감탄을 흠모해 마지않는다."], ["There is no more agreeable object in life than to see Mayfair folks condescending.", "인생에서 메이페어 사람들이 황송하게 몸을 낮추는 광경만큼 흐뭇한 구경거리도 없는 법이다."], ["Miss Crawley's prodigious benevolence rather fatigued poor little Amelia, and I am not sure that of the three ladies in Park Lane she did not find honest Miss Briggs the most agreeable.", "크롤리 양의 어마어마한 자애로움은 가엾은 어린 애밀리아를 차라리 지치게 만들었으니, 나는 파크 레인의 세 숙녀 중에서 그녀가 정직한 브리그스 양을 가장 마음에 들어 한 것은 아닐까 싶다."], ["She sympathised with Briggs as with all neglected or gentle people: she wasn't what you call a woman of spirit.", "그녀는 푸대접받거나 온순한 모든 이들에게 그러하듯 브리그스에게도 공감했던 것이다. 그녀는 이른바 기개 있는 여자가 못 되었다."]], [["George came to dinner--a repast en garcon with Captain Crawley.", "조지가 만찬에 왔는데, 크롤리 대위와 함께하는 남자들만의 식사였다."]], [["The great family coach of the Osbornes transported him to Park Lane from Russell Square; where the young ladies, who were not themselves invited, and professed the greatest indifference at that slight, nevertheless looked at Sir Pitt Crawley's name in the baronetage; and learned everything which that work had to teach about the Crawley family and their pedigree, and the Binkies, their relatives, &c., &c.", "오스본가의 큼직한 가족용 마차가 그를 러셀 광장에서 파크 레인까지 실어 날랐다. 정작 자신들은 초대받지 못해 그 푸대접에 짐짓 더없이 무관심한 척하던 그 집 젊은 숙녀들도, 준남작 명감에서 피트 크롤리 경의 이름을 들여다보고는, 그 책이 가르쳐 줄 수 있는 크롤리 가문과 그 혈통, 그들의 친척인 빙키 가문 따위에 관한 모든 것을 죄다 익혔다."], ["Rawdon Crawley received George Osborne with great frankness and graciousness: praised his play at billiards: asked him when he would have his revenge: was interested about Osborne's regiment: and would have proposed piquet to him that very evening, but Miss Crawley absolutely forbade any gambling in her house; so that the young Lieutenant's purse was not lightened by his gallant patron, for that day at least.", "로던 크롤리는 조지 오스본을 더없이 솔직하고 자상하게 맞이했다. 그의 당구 솜씨를 칭찬하고, 언제 설욕전을 치를 거냐고 물었으며, 오스본의 연대에 관심을 보였고, 바로 그날 저녁에라도 피켓을 한판 청할 참이었다."], ["However, they made an engagement for the next, somewhere: to look at a horse that Crawley had to sell, and to try him in the Park; and to dine together, and to pass the evening with some jolly fellows.", "그러나 크롤리 양이 자기 집에서의 도박은 일절 금했던 터라, 적어도 그날만큼은 젊은 중위의 지갑이 그 호기로운 후원자의 손에 가벼워지지는 않았다. 그래도 두 사람은 이튿날 어디선가 만나기로 약속을 잡았다."], ["\"That is, if you're not on duty to that pretty Miss Sedley,\"", "크롤리가 팔려고 내놓은 말을 보고, 그것을 공원에서 한번 몰아 보고, 함께 만찬을 들고, 유쾌한 친구들과 저녁을 보내자는 것이었다. \"그러니까, 자네가 그 예쁜 세들리 양에게 매여 있지만 않다면 말이지.\""], ["Crawley said, with a knowing wink.", "크롤리가 의미심장하게 윙크하며 말했다."], ["\"Monstrous nice girl, 'pon my honour, though, Osborne,\"", "\"그래도 정말 굉장히 예쁜 아가씨야, 명예를 걸고, 오스본.\""], ["he was good enough to add.", "그는 친절하게도 이렇게 덧붙였다."], ["\"Lots of tin, I suppose, eh?\"", "\"돈도 두둑하겠지, 어때?\""]], [["Osborne wasn't on duty; he would join Crawley with pleasure: and the latter, when they met the next day, praised his new friend's horsemanship--as he might with perfect honesty--and introduced him to three or four young men of the first fashion, whose acquaintance immensely elated the simple young officer.", "오스본은 매여 있지 않았다. 그는 기꺼이 크롤리와 어울리겠다고 했다. 이튿날 두 사람이 만나자 크롤리는 새 벗의 승마 솜씨를 칭찬했는데, 이는 완벽히 정직하게 할 수 있는 칭찬이었다. 그러고는 그를 최신 유행의 멋쟁이 젊은이 서너 명에게 소개했는데, 그들과 알게 된 일에 그 순박한 젊은 장교는 한껏 우쭐해졌다."]], [["\"How's little Miss Sharp, by-the-bye?\"", "\"그나저나 그 어린 샤프 양은 어떻게 지내나?\""], ["Osborne inquired of his friend over their wine, with a dandified air.", "오스본이 포도주를 들며 멋쟁이인 척하는 태도로 벗에게 물었다."], ["\"Good-natured little girl that.", "\"마음씨 고운 아가씨지."], ["Does she suit you well at Queen's Crawley?", "퀸스크롤리에서 자네한테 잘 맞나?"], ["Miss Sedley liked her a good deal last year.\"", "세들리 양이 작년에 그 아가씨를 무척 좋아했지.\""]], [["Captain Crawley looked savagely at the Lieutenant out of his little blue eyes, and watched him when he went up to resume his acquaintance with the fair governess.", "크롤리 대위는 그 작은 푸른 눈으로 중위를 사납게 쏘아보더니, 그가 일어나 그 어여쁜 가정교사와 다시 인사를 나누러 다가가는 동안 그를 지켜보았다."], ["Her conduct must have relieved Crawley if there was any jealousy in the bosom of that life-guardsman.", "만일 그 근위병의 가슴속에 조금이라도 질투심이 있었다면, 그녀의 처신은 분명 크롤리를 안심시켰을 것이다."]], [["When the young men went upstairs, and after Osborne's introduction to Miss Crawley, he walked up to Rebecca with a patronising, easy swagger.", "두 젊은이가 위층으로 올라가, 오스본이 크롤리 양에게 인사를 드린 뒤, 그는 보살피는 듯한 느긋한 거드름을 부리며 리베카에게 다가갔다."], ["He was going to be kind to her and protect her.", "그는 그녀에게 친절을 베풀고 그녀를 감싸 줄 작정이었다."], ["He would even shake hands with her, as a friend of Amelia's; and saying, \"Ah, Miss Sharp!", "심지어 애밀리아의 벗이라는 명목으로 그녀와 악수까지 할 생각이었다. 그래서 \"아, 샤프 양!"], ["how-dy-doo?\"", "어떻게 지내요?\""], ["held out his left hand towards her, expecting that she would be quite confounded at the honour.", "하고 말하며, 그녀가 그 영광에 어쩔 줄 몰라 하리라 기대하면서 왼손을 그녀에게 내밀었다."]], [["Miss Sharp put out her right forefinger, and gave him a little nod, so cool and killing, that Rawdon Crawley, watching the operations from the other room, could hardly restrain his laughter as he saw the Lieutenant's entire discomfiture; the start he gave, the pause, and the perfect clumsiness with which he at length condescended to take the finger which was offered for his embrace.", "샤프 양은 오른손 집게손가락을 내밀더니, 어찌나 싸늘하고 사람 잡는 듯한 가벼운 목례를 보내던지, 옆방에서 이 광경을 지켜보던 로던 크롤리는 중위가 완전히 당황하는 꼴을 보며 터져 나오는 웃음을 참기 어려웠다. 그가 움찔하는 모양새며, 멈칫하는 모양새며, 마침내 황송하게도 그 포옹을 위해 내밀어진 손가락 하나를 잡을 때의 그 완벽한 어색함이라니."]], [["\"She'd beat the devil, by Jove!\"", "\"맹세코, 저 여자라면 악마도 이기겠어!\""], ["the Captain said, in a rapture; and the Lieutenant, by way of beginning the conversation, agreeably asked Rebecca how she liked her new place.", "대위가 황홀해하며 말했다. 한편 중위는 대화의 물꼬를 트느라, 리베카에게 새 일자리가 마음에 드느냐고 상냥하게 물었다."]], [["\"My place?\"", "\"제 일자리요?\" 샤프 양이 싸늘하게 말했다."], ["said Miss Sharp, coolly, \"how kind of you to remind me of it!", "\"그걸 일깨워 주시다니 참 친절도 하셔라! 그런대로 괜찮은 자리예요."], ["It's a tolerably good place: the wages are pretty good--not so good as Miss Wirt's, I believe, with your sisters in Russell Square.", "급료도 제법 좋고요. 러셀 광장 댁 누이분들을 가르치는 워트 양만큼은 못 되는 것 같지만요."], ["How are those young ladies?--not that I ought to ask.\"", "그 젊은 숙녀분들은 어떻게 지내세요? 제가 여쭐 처지는 아니지만요.\""]], [["\"Why not?\" Mr.", "\"왜 그런 말씀을?\""], ["Osborne said, amazed.", "오스본 씨가 어리둥절해하며 말했다."]], [["\"Why, they never condescended to speak to me, or to ask me into their house, whilst I was staying with Amelia; but we poor governesses, you know, are used to slights of this sort.\"", "\"아, 그분들은 제가 애밀리아네 묵는 동안 제게 말을 거시거나 댁으로 청하시는 일이 한 번도 없으셨거든요. 하지만 우리 같은 가난한 가정교사들은, 아시다시피, 이런 푸대접에 익숙하답니다.\""]], [["\"My dear Miss Sharp!\"", "\"이런, 샤프 양!\""], ["Osborne ejaculated.", "오스본이 탄식하듯 내뱉었다."]], [["\"At least in some families,\"", "\"적어도 어떤 집안에서는 그렇다는 거예요.\""], ["Rebecca continued.", "리베카가 말을 이었다."], ["\"You can't think what a difference there is though.", "\"그래도 그 차이가 얼마나 큰지 모르실 거예요."], ["We are not so wealthy in Hampshire as you lucky folks of the City.", "우리 햄프셔 사람들은 당신 같은 운 좋은 시내 분들만큼 부유하진 않아요."], ["But then I am in a gentleman's family--good old English stock.", "하지만 그래도 저는 신사의 집안에, 유서 깊은 옛 영국 가문에 있는걸요."], ["I suppose you know Sir Pitt's father refused a peerage.", "피트 경의 부친께서 작위를 사양하셨다는 건 아실 테죠."], ["And you see how I am treated.", "그러니 제가 어떻게 대접받는지 보세요."], ["I am pretty comfortable.", "저는 꽤 편안하답니다."], ["Indeed it is rather a good place.", "정말이지 제법 좋은 자리예요."], ["But how very good of you to inquire!\"", "그나저나 이렇게 물어봐 주시다니 어쩜 그리 친절하셔라!\""]], [["Osborne was quite savage.", "오스본은 그야말로 약이 올랐다."], ["The little governess patronised him and persiffled him until this young British Lion felt quite uneasy; nor could he muster sufficient presence of mind to find a pretext for backing out of this most delectable conversation.", "그 자그마한 가정교사가 그를 깔보듯 대하고 비아냥거리는 통에, 이 젊은 영국 사자는 자못 거북해졌다. 게다가 그는 이 더없이 유쾌한 대화에서 빠져나올 핑계를 찾을 만큼의 침착함조차 끌어모으지 못했다."]], [["\"I thought you liked the City families pretty well,\"", "\"나는 당신이 시내 집안들을 제법 좋아하는 줄 알았는데요.\""], ["he said, haughtily.", "그가 거만하게 말했다."]], [["\"Last year you mean, when I was fresh from that horrid vulgar school?", "\"작년 말씀이시군요, 제가 그 끔찍하고 천박한 학교에서 갓 나왔을 때요?"], ["Of course I did.", "물론 좋아했죠."], ["Doesn't every girl like to come home for the holidays?", "어느 처녀가 방학을 맞아 집에 돌아가는 걸 안 좋아하겠어요?"], ["And how was I to know any better? But oh, Mr.", "그리고 제가 어떻게 그보다 나은 걸 알았겠어요?"], ["Osborne, what a difference eighteen months' experience makes!", "하지만 오, 오스본 씨, 일 년 반의 경험이 얼마나 큰 차이를 만드는지 몰라요!"], ["eighteen months spent, pardon me for saying so, with gentlemen.", "그 일 년 반을, 이런 말씀 드려 죄송하지만, 신사분들과 함께 보냈으니까요."], ["As for dear Amelia, she, I grant you, is a pearl, and would be charming anywhere.", "사랑하는 애밀리아로 말하면, 그 애야말로 진주 같은 사람이고 어디에 있든 매력적일 거라는 점은 인정해요. 자, 이제 보니 기분이 좀 풀리기 시작하시네요."], ["There now, I see you are beginning to be in a good humour; but oh these queer odd City people! And Mr.", "하지만 오, 그 별나고 괴상한 시내 사람들이라니!"], ["Jos--how is that wonderful Mr. Joseph?\"", "그리고 조스 씨는요, 그 굉장하신 조지프 씨는 어떻게 지내세요?\""]], [["\"It seems to me you didn't dislike that wonderful Mr. Joseph last year,\"", "\"제가 보기엔 당신도 작년에는 그 굉장하신 조지프 씨를 싫어하지 않으셨던 것 같은데요.\""], ["Osborne said kindly.", "오스본이 상냥하게 말했다."]], [["\"How severe of you!", "\"어쩜 그리 짓궂으세요!"], ["Well, entre nous, I didn't break my heart about him; yet if he had asked me to do what you mean by your looks (and very expressive and kind they are, too), I wouldn't have said no.\"", "뭐, 우리끼리 이야기지만, 그분 때문에 제 가슴이 찢어지진 않았어요. 하지만 만약 그분이 당신 그 눈빛이 뜻하는 바를(아주 의미심장하고 친절한 눈빛이기도 하죠) 제게 청했더라면, 저는 싫다고 하진 않았을 거예요.\""]], [["Mr.", "오스본 씨는 \"거참, 어찌나 고마우신지!\""], ["Osborne gave a look as much as to say, \"Indeed, how very obliging!\"", "하고 말하는 듯한 표정을 지었다."]], [["\"What an honour to have had you for a brother-in-law, you are thinking?", "\"저를 처제로 두었더라면 얼마나 영광이었을까, 그렇게 생각하시는 거죠?"], ["To be sister-in-law to George Osborne, Esquire, son of John Osborne, Esquire, son of--what was your grandpapa, Mr. Osborne?", "조지 오스본 귀하의 처제가 된다니, 존 오스본 귀하의 아드님이자, 또 누구의 아드님이시더라, 오스본 씨 할아버님은 무엇 하시던 분이셨죠?"], ["Well, don't be angry.", "자, 화내지 마세요."], ["You can't help your pedigree, and I quite agree with you that I would have married Mr.", "자기 혈통이야 어쩔 수 없는 노릇이고, 저도 제가 조 세들리 씨와 결혼했더라면 좋았으리라는 데에 전적으로 동감이에요."], ["Joe Sedley; for could a poor penniless girl do better?", "가난하고 한 푼 없는 처녀가 그보다 더 잘할 수 있었겠어요?"], ["Now you know the whole secret.", "자, 이제 비밀을 죄다 아셨네요."], ["I'm frank and open; considering all things, it was very kind of you to allude to the circumstance--very kind and polite.", "저는 솔직하고 거리낌이 없는 사람이에요. 모든 걸 따져 볼 때, 그 일을 들먹여 주시다니 참 친절하셨어요."], ["Amelia dear, Mr.", "참 친절하고 정중하셨죠."], ["Osborne and I were talking about your poor brother Joseph.", "애밀리아, 얘, 오스본 씨하고 내가 마침 네 가엾은 오라버니 조지프 이야기를 하던 참이야."], ["How is he?\"", "그분은 어떻게 지내시니?\""]], [["Thus was George utterly routed.", "이리하여 조지는 완전히 패퇴하고 말았다."], ["Not that Rebecca was in the right; but she had managed most successfully to put him in the wrong.", "리베카가 옳았다는 게 아니라, 그녀가 그를 그른 처지로 몰아넣는 데 더없이 성공적으로 수완을 발휘했다는 말이다."], ["And he now shamefully fled, feeling, if he stayed another minute, that he would have been made to look foolish in the presence of Amelia.", "이제 그는 부끄럽게도 달아나고 말았으니, 일 분만 더 머물렀다가는 애밀리아 앞에서 바보 꼴이 되고 말리라는 느낌이 들었기 때문이다."]], [["Though Rebecca had had the better of him, George was above the meanness of talebearing or revenge upon a lady--only he could not help cleverly confiding to Captain Crawley, next day, some notions of his regarding Miss Rebecca--that she was a sharp one, a dangerous one, a desperate flirt, &c.; in all of which opinions Crawley agreed laughingly, and with every one of which Miss Rebecca was made acquainted before twenty-four hours were over.", "리베카가 자기보다 한 수 위였음에도, 조지는 숙녀를 두고 고자질을 하거나 보복을 할 만큼 비열한 위인은 아니었다. 다만 그는 이튿날 크롤리 대위에게 리베카 양에 관한 자기 나름의 견해를 슬쩍 털어놓지 않을 수 없었을 뿐이다."], ["They added to her original regard for Mr.", "그녀가 만만치 않은 여자요, 위험한 여자요, 지독한 바람둥이라는 둥 말이다. 크롤리는 그 모든 견해에 웃으며 동의했고, 그 견해 하나하나는 스물네 시간이 채 지나기도 전에 리베카 양의 귀에 죄다 들어갔다."], ["Osborne.", "그 견해들은 오스본 씨에 대한 그녀의 본래 감정에 한층 무게를 더했다."], ["Her woman's instinct had told her that it was George who had interrupted the success of her first love-passage, and she esteemed him accordingly.", "자신의 첫 연애 행각의 성공을 가로막은 이가 바로 조지였다고 그녀의 여자다운 직감이 일러 주었던 터라, 그녀는 그에 걸맞게 그를 높이 평가했던 것이다."]], [["\"I only just warn you,\" he said to Rawdon Crawley, with a knowing look--he had bought the horse, and lost some score of guineas after dinner, \"I just warn you--I know women, and counsel you to be on the look-out.\"", "\"내 그저 미리 일러두는 건데,\" 그가 의미심장한 눈빛으로 로던 크롤리에게 말했다. 그는 말을 사들였고 만찬 후에 몇십 기니를 잃은 참이었다. \"내 그저 일러두지. 난 여자들을 잘 알거든. 그러니 자네는 조심하는 게 좋을 거야.\""]], [["\"Thank you, my boy,\"", "\"고맙네, 이 친구야.\""], ["said Crawley, with a look of peculiar gratitude.", "크롤리가 유난히 고마워하는 표정으로 말했다."], ["\"You're wide awake, I see.\"", "\"보아하니 자네는 빈틈이 없구먼.\""], ["And George went off, thinking Crawley was quite right.", "그러자 조지는 크롤리가 정말 옳다고 여기며 자리를 떴다."]], [["He told Amelia of what he had done, and how he had counselled Rawdon Crawley--a devilish good, straightforward fellow--to be on his guard against that little sly, scheming Rebecca.", "그는 애밀리아에게 자기가 한 일을 들려주었다. 자기가 그 더없이 솔직하고 시원시원한 친구 로던 크롤리에게, 그 음흉하고 잔꾀 부리는 어린 리베카를 경계하라고 일러 주었노라고 말이다."]], [["\"Against whom?\"", "\"누구를요?\""], ["Amelia cried.", "애밀리아가 외쳤다."]], [["\"Your friend the governess.--Don't look so astonished.\"", "\"네 친구 그 가정교사 말이야. 그렇게 놀란 표정 짓지 마.\""]], [["\"O George, what have you done?\" Amelia said.", "\"오, 조지, 무슨 짓을 하신 거예요?\""], ["For her woman's eyes, which Love had made sharp-sighted, had in one instant discovered a secret which was invisible to Miss Crawley, to poor virgin Briggs, and above all, to the stupid peepers of that young whiskered prig, Lieutenant Osborne.", "애밀리아가 말했다. 사랑이 예리하게 만들어 준 그녀의 여자다운 눈은, 크롤리 양에게도, 가엾은 노처녀 브리그스에게도, 무엇보다 저 구레나룻 기른 젊은 잘난 척쟁이 오스본 중위의 멍청한 눈깔에게도 보이지 않던 비밀을 한순간에 알아채고 만 것이다."]], [["For as Rebecca was shawling her in an upper apartment, where these two friends had an opportunity for a little of that secret talking and conspiring which form the delight of female life, Amelia, coming up to Rebecca, and taking her two little hands in hers, said, \"Rebecca, I see it all.\"", "리베카가 위층 어느 방에서 그녀에게 숄을 둘러 주던 차에, 그 두 벗은 여자들의 삶에 더없는 즐거움이 되어 주는 그 은밀한 속닥임과 모의를 나눌 기회를 얻었는데, 그때 애밀리아가 리베카에게 다가가 그녀의 작은 두 손을 자기 손에 감싸 쥐고 말했다. \"리베카, 나 다 알아.\""]], [["Rebecca kissed her.", "리베카는 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다."]], [["And regarding this delightful secret, not one syllable more was said by either of the young women.", "그리고 이 즐거운 비밀에 대해서는, 두 젊은 여자 중 누구도 더 이상 단 한마디도 입에 올리지 않았다."], ["But it was destined to come out before long.", "그러나 그것은 머지않아 드러날 운명이었다."]], [["Some short period after the above events, and Miss Rebecca Sharp still remaining at her patroness's house in Park Lane, one more hatchment might have been seen in Great Gaunt Street, figuring amongst the many which usually ornament that dismal quarter.", "위의 사건들이 있은 지 얼마 지나지 않아, 리베카 샤프 양이 여전히 자신이 모시는 부인의 파크 레인 저택에 머물고 있을 무렵, 그레이트곤트 거리에서는 그 음울한 동네를 으레 장식하곤 하는 숱한 상문 가운데 또 하나의 상문이 내걸린 것을 볼 수 있었다."], ["It was over Sir Pitt Crawley's house; but it did not indicate the worthy baronet's demise.", "그것은 피트 크롤리 경의 저택 위에 걸려 있었으나, 그 갸륵한 준남작의 별세를 알리는 것은 아니었다."], ["It was a feminine hatchment, and indeed a few years back had served as a funeral compliment to Sir Pitt's old mother, the late dowager Lady Crawley.", "그것은 여인의 상문이었고, 실은 몇 해 전에 피트 경의 노모인 고 크롤리 노부인을 위한 장례의 예로 쓰였던 것이었다."], ["Its period of service over, the hatchment had come down from the front of the house, and lived in retirement somewhere in the back premises of Sir Pitt's mansion.", "그 쓰임의 기간이 끝나자 상문은 저택 정면에서 내려져, 피트 경 저택의 어딘가 뒤편 살림 공간에 들어앉아 은퇴 생활을 하고 있었다."], ["It reappeared now for poor Rose Dawson.", "그것이 이제 가엾은 로즈 도슨을 위해 다시 모습을 드러낸 것이다."], ["Sir Pitt was a widower again.", "피트 경은 다시 홀아비가 되었다."], ["The arms quartered on the shield along with his own were not, to be sure, poor Rose's.", "방패 위에 그의 문장과 나란히 사등분되어 새겨진 문장은, 물론 가엾은 로즈의 것이 아니었다."], ["She had no arms.", "그녀에게는 문장이 없었다."], ["But the cherubs painted on the scutcheon answered as well for her as for Sir Pitt's mother, and Resurgam was written under the coat, flanked by the Crawley Dove and Serpent.", "그러나 그 문장 방패에 그려진 천사들은 피트 경의 모친에게나 그녀에게나 무난히 들어맞았고, 문장 아래에는 크롤리 가의 비둘기와 뱀을 양옆에 거느린 채 \"레수르감\"이 적혀 있었다."], ["Arms and Hatchments, Resurgam.--Here is an opportunity for moralising!", "문장과 상문, 그리고 레수르감이라니. 여기 교훈을 새길 만한 기회가 있도다!"]], [["Mr. Crawley had tended that otherwise friendless bedside.", "크롤리 씨는 그 외로운 병상을 지켜 주었다."], ["She went out of the world strengthened by such words and comfort as he could give her.", "그녀는 그가 베풀 수 있는 그런 말과 위로에 힘을 얻어 세상을 떠났다."], ["For many years his was the only kindness she ever knew; the only friendship that solaced in any way that feeble, lonely soul.", "여러 해 동안 그가 베푼 친절만이 그녀가 아는 유일한 친절이었고, 그 가냘프고 외로운 영혼을 그나마 달래 준 유일한 우정이었다."], ["Her heart was dead long before her body.", "그녀의 마음은 육신이 죽기 한참 전에 이미 죽어 있었다."], ["She had sold it to become Sir Pitt Crawley's wife.", "그녀는 피트 크롤리 경의 아내가 되려고 그것을 팔아 버렸던 것이다."], ["Mothers and daughters are making the same bargain every day in Vanity Fair.", "허영의 시장에서는 날마다 어머니들과 딸들이 똑같은 거래를 하고 있다."]], [["When the demise took place, her husband was in London attending to some of his innumerable schemes, and busy with his endless lawyers.", "별세가 일어났을 때, 그녀의 남편은 런던에서 그 수없이 많은 계획 가운데 몇 가지를 챙기느라, 끝도 없는 변호사들과 씨름하느라 바빴다."], ["He had found time, nevertheless, to call often in Park Lane, and to despatch many notes to Rebecca, entreating her, enjoining her, commanding her to return to her young pupils in the country, who were now utterly without companionship during their mother's illness.", "그래도 그는 파크 레인에 자주 들르고, 리베카에게 숱한 편지를 띄울 짬은 냈으니, 어머니가 병든 동안 이제 동무 하나 없이 지내게 된 시골의 어린 제자들에게 돌아와 달라고 그녀에게 애원하고, 당부하고, 명령하는 내용이었다."], ["But Miss Crawley would not hear of her departure; for though there was no lady of fashion in London who would desert her friends more complacently as soon as she was tired of their society, and though few tired of them sooner, yet as long as her engoument lasted her attachment was prodigious, and she clung still with the greatest energy to Rebecca.", "그러나 크롤리 양은 그녀가 떠난다는 말은 들으려고도 하지 않았다. 런던의 어느 사교계 부인도, 일단 누군가와 어울리는 데 싫증이 나면 크롤리 양만큼 태연히 벗을 저버리는 이가 없었고, 또 그토록 빨리 싫증을 내는 이도 드물었으나, 그 열중이 지속되는 동안만큼은 그녀의 애착이 어마어마했던 터라, 그녀는 여전히 더없는 열의로 리베카에게 매달려 있었던 것이다."]], [["The news of Lady Crawley's death provoked no more grief or comment than might have been expected in Miss Crawley's family circle.", "크롤리 부인의 별세 소식은 크롤리 양의 집안에서 예상할 수 있는 정도 이상의 슬픔이나 논평을 불러일으키지 않았다."], ["\"I suppose I must put off my party for the 3rd,\"", "\"삼일에 열기로 한 모임을 미뤄야겠구나.\""], ["Miss Crawley said; and added, after a pause, \"I hope my brother will have the decency not to marry again.\"", "크롤리 양이 말하더니, 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 덧붙였다. \"내 동생이 염치가 있다면 다시 결혼하진 않겠지.\""], ["\"What a confounded rage Pitt will be in if he does,\"", "\"형이 만약 그러면 무지하게 길길이 날뛸 거예요.\""], ["Rawdon remarked, with his usual regard for his elder brother.", "로던이 형에 대한 평소의 애정을 담아 한마디 했다."], ["Rebecca said nothing.", "리베카는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["She seemed by far the gravest and most impressed of the family.", "그녀는 집안에서 단연 가장 침통하고 가장 깊이 마음을 쓰는 듯이 보였다."], ["She left the room before Rawdon went away that day; but they met by chance below, as he was going away after taking leave, and had a parley together.", "그날 로던이 떠나기 전에 그녀는 방을 나섰으나, 작별 인사를 마친 그가 막 떠나려던 차에 두 사람은 아래층에서 우연히 마주쳤고, 함께 잠시 이야기를 나누었다."]], [["On the morrow, as Rebecca was gazing from the window, she startled Miss Crawley, who was placidly occupied with a French novel, by crying out in an alarmed tone, \"Here's Sir Pitt, Ma'am!\"", "이튿날, 리베카가 창밖을 바라보다가, 프랑스 소설을 차분히 읽고 있던 크롤리 양을 깜짝 놀라게 했다. 그녀가 다급한 목소리로 외친 것이다."], ["and the Baronet's knock followed this announcement.", "\"피트 경께서 오셨어요, 마님!\" 그리고 이 알림에 뒤이어 준남작의 문 두드리는 소리가 들렸다."]], [["\"My dear, I can't see him.", "\"얘야, 난 그를 못 만나겠다."], ["I won't see him.", "안 만날 거야."], ["Tell Bowls not at home, or go downstairs and say I'm too ill to receive any one.", "보울스에게 출타 중이라고 하렴. 아니면 아래층으로 내려가서 내가 너무 아파서 아무도 못 맞겠다고 하든지."], ["My nerves really won't bear my brother at this moment,\"", "지금 이 순간엔 내 신경이 도저히 내 동생을 견뎌 내질 못하겠구나.\""], ["cried out Miss Crawley, and resumed the novel.", "크롤리 양이 이렇게 외치고는 다시 소설책을 집어 들었다."]], [["\"She's too ill to see you, sir,\"", "\"부인께서 너무 편찮으셔서 어른을 못 뵙겠답니다.\""], ["Rebecca said, tripping down to Sir Pitt, who was preparing to ascend.", "리베카가 막 계단을 오르려던 피트 경에게 사뿐사뿐 내려가며 말했다."]], [["\"So much the better,\"", "\"그게 더 잘됐군.\""], ["Sir Pitt answered.", "피트 경이 대답했다."], ["\"I want to see YOU, Miss Becky.", "\"내가 보고 싶은 건 바로 자네야, 베키 양."], ["Come along a me into the parlour,\"", "나하고 응접실로 들어가지.\""], ["and they entered that apartment together.", "그리하여 두 사람은 함께 그 방으로 들어갔다."]], [["\"I wawnt you back at Queen's Crawley, Miss,\"", "\"자네가 퀸스크롤리로 돌아와 줬으면 하네, 양.\""], ["the baronet said, fixing his eyes upon her, and taking off his black gloves and his hat with its great crape hat-band.", "준남작이 그녀에게 시선을 고정한 채, 검은 장갑과 커다란 검은 상장(喪章)을 두른 모자를 벗으며 말했다."], ["His eyes had such a strange look, and fixed upon her so steadfastly, that Rebecca Sharp began almost to tremble.", "그의 눈에는 어찌나 묘한 빛이 어려 있던지, 그녀를 어찌나 한결같이 빤히 응시하던지, 리베카 샤프는 그만 떨릴 지경이었다."]], [["\"I hope to come soon,\"", "\"곧 돌아가고 싶어요.\" 그녀가 낮은 목소리로 말했다."], ["she said in a low voice, \"as soon as Miss Crawley is better--and return to--to the dear children.\"", "\"크롤리 양께서 좀 나아지시는 대로요. 그리고 그 사랑스러운 아이들에게로 돌아갈게요.\""]], [["\"You've said so these three months, Becky,\"", "\"자네는 이 석 달 내내 그렇게 말해 왔지, 베키.\" 피트 경이 대답했다."], ["replied Sir Pitt, \"and still you go hanging on to my sister, who'll fling you off like an old shoe, when she's wore you out.", "\"그런데도 여태 내 누이한테 매달려 있잖나. 누이는 자네를 다 부려먹고 나면 헌 신짝처럼 내팽개칠 텐데."], ["I tell you I want you.", "내 말하지만 난 자네가 필요해."], ["I'm going back to the Vuneral.", "난 장례식 치르러 돌아가는 길이야."], ["Will you come back?", "돌아오겠나?"], ["Yes or no?\"", "예야, 아니야?\""]], [["\"I daren't--I don't think--it would be right--to be alone--with you, sir,\" Becky said, seemingly in great agitation.", "\"감히… 그럴 수는… 옳지 않을 것… 같아요… 어른과 단둘이 있는 건요.\" 베키가 자못 몹시 동요한 듯이 말했다."]], [["\"I say agin, I want you,\"", "\"다시 말하지만, 난 자네가 필요해.\""], ["Sir Pitt said, thumping the table.", "피트 경이 탁자를 쾅 내리치며 말했다."], ["\"I can't git on without you.", "\"자네 없이는 일이 안 돌아가."], ["I didn't see what it was till you went away.", "자네가 떠나기 전까진 그게 뭔지 몰랐어."], ["The house all goes wrong.", "집안이 죄다 엉망이야."], ["It's not the same place.", "예전 같지가 않아."], ["All my accounts has got muddled agin.", "내 장부도 죄다 또 뒤죽박죽이 됐어."], ["You MUST come back.", "자넨 꼭 돌아와야 해."], ["Do come back.", "제발 돌아와."], ["Dear Becky, do come.\"", "베키, 제발 돌아와 줘.\""]], [["\"Come--as what, sir?\"", "\"돌아가서… 무엇이 되는 건가요, 어른?\""], ["Rebecca gasped out.", "리베카가 숨을 몰아쉬며 내뱉었다."]], [["\"Come as Lady Crawley, if you like,\"", "\"원한다면 크롤리 부인이 되어 돌아오게.\""], ["the Baronet said, grasping his crape hat.", "준남작이 상장 두른 모자를 움켜쥐며 말했다."], ["\"There!", "\"자!"], ["will that zatusfy you?", "그럼 만족하겠나?"], ["Come back and be my wife.", "돌아와서 내 아내가 되어 줘."], ["Your vit vor't.", "자넨 그럴 자격이 있어."], ["Birth be hanged.", "가문 따위 집어치워."], ["You're as good a lady as ever I see.", "자넨 내가 본 그 어떤 숙녀 못지않은 숙녀야."], ["You've got more brains in your little vinger than any baronet's wife in the county.", "자네 그 작은 손가락 하나에 든 머리가 이 고을 어느 준남작 부인의 머리보다 낫다고."], ["Will you come?", "돌아오겠나?"], ["Yes or no?\"", "예야, 아니야?\""]], [["\"Oh, Sir Pitt!\"", "\"오, 피트 경!\""], ["Rebecca said, very much moved.", "리베카가 몹시 마음이 흔들려 말했다."]], [["\"Say yes, Becky,\"", "\"예라고 해, 베키.\""], ["Sir Pitt continued.", "피트 경이 말을 이었다."], ["\"I'm an old man, but a good'n.", "\"난 늙은이지만 좋은 사람이야."], ["I'm good for twenty years.", "앞으로 이십 년은 끄떡없어."], ["I'll make you happy, zee if I don't.", "자네를 행복하게 해 주겠네, 두고 보라고."], ["You shall do what you like; spend what you like; and 'ave it all your own way.", "자넨 하고 싶은 대로 하면 돼. 쓰고 싶은 대로 쓰고."], ["I'll make you a zettlement.", "죄다 자네 마음대로 해. 재산도 떼어 주겠어."], ["I'll do everything reglar.", "모든 걸 격식대로 해 주겠네."], ["Look year!\"", "자, 보라고!\""], ["and the old man fell down on his knees and leered at her like a satyr.", "그러더니 노인은 무릎을 꿇고 엎드려, 사티로스처럼 음흉하게 그녀를 곁눈질했다."]], [["Rebecca started back a picture of consternation.", "리베카는 기겁한 모습으로 뒷걸음질 쳤다."], ["In the course of this history we have never seen her lose her presence of mind; but she did now, and wept some of the most genuine tears that ever fell from her eyes.", "이 이야기를 거쳐 오는 동안 우리는 그녀가 침착성을 잃는 모습을 한 번도 본 적이 없다. 그러나 이번에는 그러고 말았으니, 그녀의 두 눈에서 일찍이 흘러내린 적 없는 더없이 진솔한 눈물을 몇 방울 떨구었던 것이다."]], [["\"Oh, Sir Pitt!\"", "\"오, 피트 경!\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said.", "\"오, 어른…"], ["\"Oh, sir--I--I'm married ALREADY.\"", "저는… 저는 이미 결혼한 몸이에요.\""]], [["Every reader of a sentimental turn (and we desire no other) must have been pleased with the tableau with which the last act of our little drama concluded; for what can be prettier than an image of Love on his knees before Beauty?", "어떤 감상적 기질을 지닌 독자라면(우리는 다른 부류의 독자는 바라지도 않거니와), 우리의 자그마한 연극의 마지막 막을 매듭짓던 그 활인화 같은 장면에 분명 흐뭇해했을 것이다. 미인 앞에 무릎 꿇은 사랑의 모습보다 더 어여쁜 그림이 어디 있겠는가?"]], [["But when Love heard that awful confession from Beauty that she was married already, he bounced up from his attitude of humility on the carpet, uttering exclamations which caused poor little Beauty to be more frightened than she was when she made her avowal.", "그러나 사랑은 미인의 입에서 자기가 이미 결혼한 몸이라는 그 끔찍한 고백을 듣자, 융단 위에 무릎 꿇었던 겸손한 자세를 박차고 벌떡 일어나며 탄성을 내질렀는데, 그 바람에 가엾은 작은 미인은 고백을 했을 때보다 한층 더 겁에 질리고 말았다."], ["\"Married; you're joking,\"", "\"결혼이라니, 농담이겠지.\""], ["the Baronet cried, after the first explosion of rage and wonder.", "분노와 경악이 한바탕 터져 나온 끝에 준남작이 외쳤다."], ["\"You're making vun of me, Becky.", "\"나를 놀리는 거지, 베키."], ["Who'd ever go to marry you without a shilling to your vortune?\"", "땡전 한 푼 없는 너 같은 애를 누가 결혼 상대로 거두겠어?\""]], [["\"Married!", "\"결혼했어요!"], ["married!\"", "결혼했다고요!\""], ["Rebecca said, in an agony of tears--her voice choking with emotion, her handkerchief up to her ready eyes, fainting against the mantelpiece a figure of woe fit to melt the most obdurate heart.", "리베카가 고통스러운 눈물을 흘리며 말했다. 감정에 북받쳐 목이 메고, 어느새 그렁그렁한 두 눈에 손수건을 갖다 대며, 벽난로 선반에 기대 정신을 잃을 듯한 그 비탄의 자태는 더없이 모진 마음마저 녹여 버릴 만한 것이었다."], ["\"O Sir Pitt, dear Sir Pitt, do not think me ungrateful for all your goodness to me.", "\"오, 피트 경, 피트 경, 저에게 베풀어 주신 그 모든 호의를 제가 고마워하지 않는다고 여기지 말아 주세요."], ["It is only your generosity that has extorted my secret.\"", "오로지 경의 너그러우심이 제 비밀을 끌어낸 것이랍니다.\""]], [["\"Generosity be hanged!\"", "\"너그러움은 무슨 빌어먹을!\""], ["Sir Pitt roared out.", "피트 경이 버럭 소리쳤다."], ["\"Who is it tu, then, you're married?", "\"그럼 도대체 누구하고 결혼한 거야?"], ["Where was it?\"", "어디서 했어?\""]], [["\"Let me come back with you to the country, sir!", "\"저를 시골로 다시 데려가 주세요, 경!"], ["Let me watch over you as faithfully as ever!", "예전처럼 한결같이 충실하게 경을 보살피게 해 주세요!"], ["Don't, don't separate me from dear Queen's Crawley!\"", "제발, 제발 저를 그리운 퀸스크롤리에서 떼어 놓지 말아 주세요!\""]], [["\"The feller has left you, has he?\"", "\"그놈이 너를 버린 거로구나, 그렇지?\""], ["the Baronet said, beginning, as he fancied, to comprehend.", "준남작이 자기 딴에는 이제 사정을 알아챘다는 듯 말했다."], ["\"Well, Becky--come back if you like.", "\"그럼 됐다, 베키, 원한다면 돌아오너라."], ["You can't eat your cake and have it.", "한 입으로 두말은 못 하는 법이지."], ["Any ways I made you a vair offer.", "어쨌거나 나는 너한테 정당한 제안을 했어."], ["Coom back as governess--you shall have it all your own way.\"", "가정교사로 돌아오면 돼, 다 네 마음대로 하게 해 주마.\""], ["She held out one hand.", "그녀는 한 손을 내밀었다."], ["She cried fit to break her heart; her ringlets fell over her face, and over the marble mantelpiece where she laid it.", "가슴이 찢어지도록 울었다. 곱슬머리가 얼굴 위로, 또 그녀가 얼굴을 묻은 대리석 벽난로 선반 위로 흘러내렸다."]], [["\"So the rascal ran off, eh?\"", "\"그래서 그 못된 놈이 달아났다는 거지, 응?\""], ["Sir Pitt said, with a hideous attempt at consolation.", "피트 경이 끔찍할 만큼 어설프게 위로하려 들며 말했다."], ["\"Never mind, Becky, I'LL take care of 'ee.\"", "\"괜찮다, 베키, 내가 너를 돌봐 주마.\""]], [["\"Oh, sir!", "\"오, 경!"], ["it would be the pride of my life to go back to Queen's Crawley, and take care of the children, and of you as formerly, when you said you were pleased with the services of your little Rebecca.", "퀸스크롤리로 돌아가, 예전처럼 아이들과 경을 보살피는 것이야말로 제 평생의 자랑이 될 거예요. 경께서 이 작은 리베카의 봉사가 마음에 든다고 말씀하시던 그때처럼요."], ["When I think of what you have just offered me, my heart fills with gratitude indeed it does.", "방금 저에게 베풀어 주신 그 제안을 생각하면, 정말이지 제 가슴은 감사로 가득 찬답니다."], ["I can't be your wife, sir; let me--let me be your daughter.\"", "저는 경의 아내가 될 수는 없어요. 그저, 그저 경의 딸이 되게 해 주세요.\""], ["Saying which, Rebecca went down on HER knees in a most tragical way, and, taking Sir Pitt's horny black hand between her own two (which were very pretty and white, and as soft as satin), looked up in his face with an expression of exquisite pathos and confidence, when--when the door opened, and Miss Crawley sailed in.", "이렇게 말하며 리베카는 더없이 비극적인 몸짓으로 무릎을 꿇고는, 피트 경의 거칠고 검은 손을 자신의 두 손(아주 곱고 희며 공단처럼 부드러운 손이었다) 사이에 감싸 쥐고, 더없이 절절한 애원과 신뢰의 표정으로 그 얼굴을 올려다보았다. 그런데 바로 그때, 문이 열리고 크롤리 양이 미끄러지듯 들어왔다."]], [["Mrs. Firkin and Miss Briggs, who happened by chance to be at the parlour door soon after the Baronet and Rebecca entered the apartment, had also seen accidentally, through the keyhole, the old gentleman prostrate before the governess, and had heard the generous proposal which he made her.", "준남작과 리베카가 방에 들어선 직후 우연히 응접실 문가에 있던 퍼킨 부인과 브리그스 양은, 열쇠 구멍을 통해 노신사가 가정교사 앞에 엎드린 광경을 우연히 보았고, 그가 그녀에게 한 그 너그러운 제안도 들었던 것이다."], ["It was scarcely out of his mouth when Mrs. Firkin and Miss Briggs had streamed up the stairs, had rushed into the drawing-room where Miss Crawley was reading the French novel, and had given that old lady the astounding intelligence that Sir Pitt was on his knees, proposing to Miss Sharp.", "그 말이 채 그의 입에서 떨어지기도 전에 퍼킨 부인과 브리그스 양은 계단을 줄달음쳐 올라가, 크롤리 양이 프랑스 소설을 읽고 있던 응접실로 뛰어들어, 피트 경이 무릎을 꿇고 샤프 양에게 청혼하고 있다는 그 기절초풍할 소식을 노부인에게 전했다."], ["And if you calculate the time for the above dialogue to take place--the time for Briggs and Firkin to fly to the drawing-room--the time for Miss Crawley to be astonished, and to drop her volume of Pigault le Brun--and the time for her to come downstairs--you will see how exactly accurate this history is, and how Miss Crawley must have appeared at the very instant when Rebecca had assumed the attitude of humility.", "그리고 위의 대화가 오가는 데 걸린 시간이며, 브리그스와 퍼킨이 응접실로 날듯이 달려간 시간이며, 크롤리 양이 깜짝 놀라 피고 르브룅의 책을 떨어뜨린 시간이며, 그녀가 계단을 내려온 시간까지 헤아려 본다면, 이 이야기가 얼마나 정확한지, 또 어찌하여 크롤리 양이 리베카가 막 겸손한 자세를 취한 바로 그 순간에 나타날 수밖에 없었는지 알 수 있을 것이다."]], [["\"It is the lady on the ground, and not the gentleman,\"", "\"무릎을 꿇은 건 신사가 아니라 바닥에 엎드린 숙녀로군요.\""], ["Miss Crawley said, with a look and voice of great scorn.", "크롤리 양이 잔뜩 경멸하는 표정과 목소리로 말했다. \"사람들이 그러길 당신이 무릎을 꿇고 있다더군요, 피트 경."], ["\"They told me that YOU were on your knees, Sir Pitt: do kneel once more, and let me see this pretty couple!\"", "어디 한 번 더 꿇어 보시지요. 이 어여쁜 한 쌍을 좀 구경하게요!\""]], [["\"I have thanked Sir Pitt Crawley, Ma'am,\"", "\"피트 크롤리 경께 감사를 드렸어요, 부인.\""], ["Rebecca said, rising, \"and have told him that--that I never can become Lady Crawley.\"", "리베카가 일어서며 말했다. \"그리고 말씀드렸지요, 저는, 저는 결코 크롤리 부인이 될 수 없다고요.\""]], [["\"Refused him!\"", "\"거절했다고!\""], ["Miss Crawley said, more bewildered than ever.", "크롤리 양이 그 어느 때보다 어리둥절해하며 말했다."], ["Briggs and Firkin at the door opened the eyes of astonishment and the lips of wonder.", "문가의 브리그스와 퍼킨은 경악으로 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨고 입을 떡 벌렸다."]], [["\"Yes--refused,\"", "\"네, 거절했어요.\""], ["Rebecca continued, with a sad, tearful voice.", "리베카가 슬프고 눈물 어린 목소리로 이었다."]], [["\"And am I to credit my ears that you absolutely proposed to her, Sir Pitt?\"", "\"정말로 자네가 그녀에게 청혼을 했단 말인가, 피트 경? 내 귀를 믿어도 되는 건가요?\""], ["the old lady asked.", "노부인이 물었다."]], [["\"Ees,\"", "\"그려.\""], ["said the Baronet, \"I did.\"", "준남작이 말했다. \"내가 했지.\""]], [["\"And she refused you as she says?\"", "\"그런데 이 애 말대로 거절을 했고요?\""]], [["\"Ees,\"", "\"그려.\""], ["Sir Pitt said, his features on a broad grin.", "피트 경이 입이 함박만 하게 벌어지도록 웃으며 말했다."]], [["\"It does not seem to break your heart at any rate,\"", "\"어쨌거나 가슴이 미어지는 표정은 아니로군요.\""], ["Miss Crawley remarked.", "크롤리 양이 말했다."]], [["\"Nawt a bit,\"", "\"눈곱만큼도 아니지.\""], ["answered Sir Pitt, with a coolness and good-humour which set Miss Crawley almost mad with bewilderment.", "피트 경이 어찌나 태연하고 유쾌하게 대답하던지, 크롤리 양은 어리둥절한 나머지 거의 미칠 지경이 되었다."], ["That an old gentleman of station should fall on his knees to a penniless governess, and burst out laughing because she refused to marry him--that a penniless governess should refuse a Baronet with four thousand a year--these were mysteries which Miss Crawley could never comprehend.", "지체 높은 노신사가 한 푼 없는 가정교사 앞에 무릎을 꿇고, 게다가 그녀가 결혼을 거절했다고 너털웃음을 터뜨리다니, 또 한 푼 없는 가정교사가 연 사천 파운드를 가진 준남작을 거절하다니, 이는 크롤리 양으로서는 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 수수께끼였다."], ["It surpassed any complications of intrigue in her favourite Pigault le Brun.", "그녀가 애독하는 피고 르브룅의 그 어떤 복잡한 음모 줄거리보다도 더한 일이었다."]], [["\"I'm glad you think it good sport, brother,\"", "\"오라버니가 이걸 재미난 놀음으로 여기니 다행이군요.\""], ["she continued, groping wildly through this amazement.", "크롤리 양이 이 경악 속을 마구잡이로 더듬거리며 말을 이었다."]], [["\"Vamous,\"", "\"굉장하지.\""], ["said Sir Pitt.", "피트 경이 말했다."], ["\"Who'd ha' thought it!", "\"누가 짐작이나 했겠어!"], ["what a sly little devil!", "어찌나 앙큼한 계집인지!"], ["what a little fox it waws!\"", "어찌나 여우 같은 것인지!\""], ["he muttered to himself, chuckling with pleasure.", "그는 흐뭇한 듯 낄낄대며 혼자 중얼거렸다."]], [["\"Who'd have thought what?\"", "\"무엇을 누가 짐작했겠다는 거예요?\""], ["cries Miss Crawley, stamping with her foot.", "크롤리 양이 발을 구르며 외쳤다."], ["\"Pray, Miss Sharp, are you waiting for the Prince Regent's divorce, that you don't think our family good enough for you?\"", "\"여보세요, 샤프 양, 혹시 섭정 왕세자의 이혼이라도 기다리느라 우리 집안이 자네한테는 성에 차지 않는다는 건가?\""]], [["\"My attitude,\"", "\"부인께서 들어오셨을 때 제 자세는,\""], ["Rebecca said, \"when you came in, ma'am, did not look as if I despised such an honour as this good--this noble man has deigned to offer me.", "리베카가 말했다, \"이 선량한, 이 고결한 분께서 황송하게도 베풀어 주신 그런 영예를 제가 업신여기는 것처럼 보이지는 않았을 거예요."], ["Do you think I have no heart?", "제게 마음이 없다고 생각하세요?"], ["Have you all loved me, and been so kind to the poor orphan--deserted--girl, and am I to feel nothing?", "여러분 모두가 저를 사랑해 주시고, 의지할 데 없는 가엾은 고아 소녀에게 그토록 친절히 대해 주셨는데, 제가 아무것도 느끼지 말아야 하나요?"], ["O my friends!", "오, 나의 벗들이여!"], ["O my benefactors!", "오, 나의 은인들이여!"], ["may not my love, my life, my duty, try to repay the confidence you have shown me?", "제 사랑과 제 삶과 제 본분으로, 여러분이 베풀어 주신 그 신뢰에 보답해 보려 하면 안 되는 건가요?"], ["Do you grudge me even gratitude, Miss Crawley?", "크롤리 양, 저에게 감사하는 마음마저도 아까워하시는 건가요?"], ["It is too much--my heart is too full\"; and she sank down in a chair so pathetically, that most of the audience present were perfectly melted with her sadness.", "너무하세요, 제 가슴이 벅차서 견딜 수가 없어요.\" 그러고는 어찌나 애처롭게 의자에 풀썩 주저앉던지, 그 자리에 있던 청중 대부분이 그녀의 슬픔에 완전히 녹아 버렸다."]], [["\"Whether you marry me or not, you're a good little girl, Becky, and I'm your vriend, mind,\"", "\"나하고 결혼을 하든 안 하든, 너는 착한 아이야, 베키. 그리고 나는 네 편이라는 걸 잊지 마라.\""], ["said Sir Pitt, and putting on his crape-bound hat, he walked away--greatly to Rebecca's relief; for it was evident that her secret was unrevealed to Miss Crawley, and she had the advantage of a brief reprieve.", "피트 경은 이렇게 말하고는 검은 띠를 두른 모자를 쓰고 걸어 나갔다. 리베카로서는 무척이나 마음이 놓이는 일이었으니, 그녀의 비밀이 크롤리 양에게 아직 드러나지 않았음이 분명했고, 그리하여 잠시나마 유예를 얻은 셈이었기 때문이다."]], [["Putting her handkerchief to her eyes, and nodding away honest Briggs, who would have followed her upstairs, she went up to her apartment; while Briggs and Miss Crawley, in a high state of excitement, remained to discuss the strange event, and Firkin, not less moved, dived down into the kitchen regions, and talked of it with all the male and female company there.", "손수건을 두 눈에 갖다 대고, 따라 올라오려는 착한 브리그스를 고갯짓으로 물리치며 그녀는 자기 방으로 올라갔다."], ["And so impressed was Mrs. Firkin with the news, that she thought proper to write off by that very night's post, \"with her humble duty to Mrs.", "그동안 브리그스와 크롤리 양은 잔뜩 흥분한 상태로 남아 이 기묘한 사건을 논했고, 못지않게 들뜬 퍼킨은 부엌 영역으로 내려가 거기 있는 남녀 하인들과 그 이야기를 나누었다."], ["Bute Crawley and the family at the Rectory, and Sir Pitt has been and proposed for to marry Miss Sharp, wherein she has refused him, to the wonder of all.\"", "그리고 퍼킨 부인은 이 소식에 어찌나 감동을 받았던지, 바로 그날 밤 우편으로 편지를 부치는 것이 마땅하다 여겨, \"목사관의 뷰트 크롤리 부인과 가족 여러분께 삼가 문안 올리오며, 피트 경께서 샤프 양에게 청혼을 하셨는데, 모두가 놀랍게도 샤프 양이 거절하였다\"고 적어 보냈다."]], [["The two ladies in the dining-room (where worthy Miss Briggs was delighted to be admitted once more to confidential conversation with her patroness) wondered to their hearts'", "식당에 있던 두 숙녀(여기서 착한 브리그스 양은 다시금 후원자와 은밀한 대화를 나눌 자격을 얻은 것이 기뻐 어쩔 줄 몰랐다)는 피트 경의 청혼과 리베카의 거절을 두고 마음껏 의아해했다."], ["content at Sir Pitt's offer, and Rebecca's refusal; Briggs very acutely suggesting that there must have been some obstacle in the shape of a previous attachment, otherwise no young woman in her senses would ever have refused so advantageous a proposal.", "브리그스는 무척 예리하게도, 무언가 먼저 정을 둔 상대라는 형태의 장애물이 있는 게 틀림없다고, 그렇지 않고서야 제정신인 젊은 여자가 그토록 유리한 청혼을 거절했을 리 없다고 넌지시 짚었다."]], [["\"You would have accepted it yourself, wouldn't you, Briggs?\"", "\"브리그스, 당신 같으면 받아들였겠지요, 안 그래요?\""], ["Miss Crawley said, kindly.", "크롤리 양이 다정하게 말했다."]], [["\"Would it not be a privilege to be Miss Crawley's sister?\"", "\"크롤리 양의 올케가 된다면 그야말로 영광이 아니겠어요?\""], ["Briggs replied, with meek evasion.", "브리그스가 얌전하게 둘러대며 대답했다."]], [["\"Well, Becky would have made a good Lady Crawley, after all,\"", "\"하긴, 베키라면 그래도 훌륭한 크롤리 부인이 되었을 거예요.\""], ["Miss Crawley remarked (who was mollified by the girl's refusal, and very liberal and generous now there was no call for her sacrifices).", "크롤리 양이 말했다(그 아이가 거절한 덕분에 마음이 누그러졌고, 이제 자기가 희생을 치를 일이 없어지자 한결 너그럽고 후해진 것이다)."], ["\"She has brains in plenty (much more wit in her little finger than you have, my poor dear Briggs, in all your head).", "\"머리가 비상한 아이지요(가엾은 브리그스, 당신 머릿속 전부보다 그 애 새끼손가락 하나에 든 재치가 훨씬 많다오). 게다가 내가 가르쳐 놓았으니 몸가짐도 더없이 훌륭하고요."], ["Her manners are excellent, now I have formed her.", "그 애는 몽모랑시 가문 사람이에요, 브리그스."], ["She is a Montmorency, Briggs, and blood is something, though I despise it for my part; and she would have held her own amongst those pompous stupid Hampshire people much better than that unfortunate ironmonger's daughter.\"", "핏줄이란 게 무언가 있긴 한 법이지요, 나야 그런 걸 경멸하지만 말이에요. 게다가 그 애라면 그 거드름이나 피우는 멍청한 햄프셔 사람들 틈에서, 그 불운한 철물상 딸보다 훨씬 잘 제 몫을 해냈을 거예요.\""]], [["Briggs coincided as usual, and the \"previous attachment\"", "브리그스는 늘 그렇듯 맞장구를 쳤고, 이어 \"먼저 정을 둔 상대\"가 누구일지를 두고 갖가지 억측이 오갔다. \"당신처럼 의지할 데 없는 가엾은 사람들은 늘 어리석은 연정 같은 걸 품기 마련이지요.\""], ["was then discussed in conjectures.", "크롤리 양이 말했다."], ["\"You poor friendless creatures are always having some foolish tendre,\" Miss Crawley said.", "\"당신만 해도 그렇잖아요, 글씨 선생을 사모했었잖아요(울지 말아요, 브리그스, 당신은 늘 운다니까, 운다고 그 사람이 다시 살아나는 것도 아닌데)."], ["\"You yourself, you know, were in love with a writing-master (don't cry, Briggs--you're always crying, and it won't bring him to life again), and I suppose this unfortunate Becky has been silly and sentimental too--some apothecary, or house-steward, or painter, or young curate, or something of that sort.\"", "이 가엾은 베키도 짐작건대 어리석고 감상적이었던 게지요. 약제사라든가, 집사라든가, 화가라든가, 젊은 부목사라든가, 뭐 그런 부류겠지요.\""]], [["\"Poor thing!", "\"가엾어라!"], ["poor thing!\"", "가엾어라!\""], ["says Briggs (who was thinking of twenty-four years back, and that hectic young writing-master whose lock of yellow hair, and whose letters, beautiful in their illegibility, she cherished in her old desk upstairs).", "브리그스가 말했다(그녀는 이십사 년 전을, 그리고 그 병약했던 젊은 글씨 선생을 떠올리고 있었다. 그의 노란 머리카락 한 타래와, 알아볼 수 없을 만큼 휘갈겨 쓴 것이 도리어 아름답던 그의 편지들을, 그녀는 위층 낡은 책상 속에 고이 간직하고 있었다)."], ["\"Poor thing, poor thing!\"", "\"가엾어라, 가엾어라!\""], ["says Briggs.", "브리그스가 말했다."], ["Once more she was a fresh-cheeked lass of eighteen; she was at evening church, and the hectic writing-master and she were quavering out of the same psalm-book.", "그 순간 그녀는 다시 한 번 발그레한 볼을 가진 열여덟 살 처녀가 되었다. 저녁 예배에 나가, 병약한 글씨 선생과 한 권의 찬송가책을 함께 들여다보며 떨리는 목소리로 노래하고 있었다."]], [["\"After such conduct on Rebecca's part,\"", "\"리베카가 그렇게 처신했으니,\""], ["Miss Crawley said enthusiastically, \"our family should do something.", "크롤리 양이 열의를 담아 말했다, \"우리 집안이 무언가 해 주어야지요. 그 정인이 누구인지 알아봐 줘요, 브리그스."], ["Find out who is the objet, Briggs.", "내가 그 사람한테 가게를 차려 주든가, 알다시피 그 사람한테 내 초상화를 주문하든가, 아니면 내 사촌인 주교한테 말을 넣든가 하겠어요."], ["I'll set him up in a shop; or order my portrait of him, you know; or speak to my cousin, the Bishop and I'll doter Becky, and we'll have a wedding, Briggs, and you shall make the breakfast, and be a bridesmaid.\"", "그리고 베키한테 지참금을 마련해 주고, 결혼식을 올립시다, 브리그스. 당신이 피로연 음식을 차리고 신부 들러리도 서요.\""]], [["Briggs declared that it would be delightful, and vowed that her dear Miss Crawley was always kind and generous, and went up to Rebecca's bedroom to console her and prattle about the offer, and the refusal, and the cause thereof; and to hint at the generous intentions of Miss Crawley, and to find out who was the gentleman that had the mastery of Miss Sharp's heart.", "브리그스는 그거 참 즐거운 일이겠다고 단언하고는, 친애하는 크롤리 양은 언제나 다정하고 너그럽다고 거듭 칭송하며, 리베카의 침실로 올라갔다. 그녀를 위로하고, 청혼이며 거절이며 그 까닭에 대해 재잘대고, 크롤리 양의 너그러운 의향을 넌지시 비치고, 또 샤프 양의 마음을 사로잡은 신사가 누구인지 알아내기 위해서였다."]], [["Rebecca was very kind, very affectionate and affected--responded to Briggs's offer of tenderness with grateful fervour--owned there was a secret attachment--a delicious mystery--what a pity Miss Briggs had not remained half a minute longer at the keyhole! Rebecca might, perhaps, have told more: but five minutes after Miss Briggs's arrival in Rebecca's apartment, Miss Crawley actually made her appearance there--an unheard-of honour--her impatience had overcome her; she could not wait for the tardy operations of her ambassadress: so she came in person, and ordered Briggs out of the room. And expressing her approval of Rebecca's conduct, she asked particulars of the interview, and the previous transactions which had brought about the astonishing offer of Sir Pitt.", "리베카는 무척 다정하고, 무척 살갑고, 또 무척이나 짐짓 꾸민 태도였다. 브리그스가 베푸는 다정함에 감사 어린 열의로 화답했고, 은밀히 정을 둔 상대가 있음을, 달콤한 비밀이 있음을 인정했다. 브리그스 양이 열쇠 구멍에 반 분만 더 붙어 있었더라면 얼마나 좋았으랴! 리베카는 어쩌면 더 많은 이야기를 들려주었을지도 모른다. 그러나 브리그스 양이 리베카의 방에 들어온 지 오 분 만에, 크롤리 양이 친히 그곳에 모습을 드러냈다. 전례 없는 영광이었다. 조바심을 이기지 못한 것이다. 사절로 보낸 이가 더디게 일을 처리하는 꼴을 차마 기다릴 수 없어, 몸소 찾아와 브리그스에게 방에서 나가라 명한 것이었다. 그리고 리베카의 처신을 칭찬하면서, 면담의 자초지종과 피트 경의 그 놀라운 청혼에 이르게 된 그간의 일들을 자세히 캐물었다."]], [["Rebecca said she had long had some notion of the partiality with which Sir Pitt honoured her (for he was in the habit of making his feelings known in a very frank and unreserved manner) but, not to mention private reasons with which she would not for the present trouble Miss Crawley, Sir Pitt's age, station, and habits were such as to render a marriage quite impossible; and could a woman with any feeling of self-respect and any decency listen to proposals at such a moment, when the funeral of the lover's deceased wife had not actually taken place?", "리베카가 말하기를, 피트 경이 자기를 각별히 어여삐 여긴다는 것은 진작부터 어렴풋이 알고 있었다고 했다(그가 워낙 자기 감정을 거리낌 없이 솔직하게 드러내는 버릇이 있었기 때문이다). 그러나 지금으로서는 크롤리 양을 번거롭게 하고 싶지 않은 사적인 이유들은 차치하더라도, 피트 경의 나이와 신분과 생활 습관으로 미루어 결혼은 도저히 불가능한 일이며, 더구나 정인의 죽은 아내 장례조차 아직 치르지 않은 그런 마당에, 조금이라도 자존심과 염치가 있는 여자라면 어찌 청혼 따위에 귀를 기울일 수 있겠느냐고 했다."]], [["\"Nonsense, my dear, you would never have refused him had there not been some one else in the case,\"", "\"말도 안 돼, 얘야. 다른 누군가가 끼어 있지 않고서야 네가 그를 거절했을 리 없지.\""], ["Miss Crawley said, coming to her point at once.", "크롤리 양이 단번에 본론으로 들어가며 말했다."], ["\"Tell me the private reasons; what are the private reasons?", "\"사적인 이유를 말해 봐. 그 사적인 이유가 뭐냐?"], ["There is some one; who is it that has touched your heart?\"", "누군가 있구나. 네 마음을 사로잡은 게 누구냐?\""]], [["Rebecca cast down her eyes, and owned there was.", "리베카는 눈을 내리깔고, 그렇다고 인정했다."], ["\"You have guessed right, dear lady,\"", "\"맞히셨어요, 다정한 부인.\""], ["she said, with a sweet simple faltering voice.", "그녀가 달콤하고 순박하게 더듬거리는 목소리로 말했다."], ["\"You wonder at one so poor and friendless having an attachment, don't you?", "\"이렇게 가난하고 의지할 데 없는 애가 사랑에 빠진 게 의아하시겠지요?"], ["I have never heard that poverty was any safeguard against it.", "하지만 가난이 사랑을 막아 준다는 말은 들어 본 적이 없는걸요."], ["I wish it were.\"", "그랬으면 좋으련만요.\""]], [["\"My poor dear child,\"", "\"가엾은 내 아가야,\""], ["cried Miss Crawley, who was always quite ready to be sentimental, \"is our passion unrequited, then?", "언제든 감상에 젖을 채비가 되어 있던 크롤리 양이 외쳤다, \"그럼 우리의 연정이 보답을 못 받고 있는 거냐?"], ["Are we pining in secret?", "우리가 남몰래 애를 태우고 있는 거야?"], ["Tell me all, and let me console you.\"", "다 털어놓으렴, 내가 위로해 줄 테니.\""]], [["\"I wish you could, dear Madam,\"", "\"부인께서 그래 주실 수 있다면 좋겠어요.\""], ["Rebecca said in the same tearful tone.", "리베카가 똑같이 눈물 어린 어조로 말했다."], ["\"Indeed, indeed, I need it.\"", "\"정말이지, 정말이지 위로가 필요하답니다.\""], ["And she laid her head upon Miss Crawley's shoulder and wept there so naturally that the old lady, surprised into sympathy, embraced her with an almost maternal kindness, uttered many soothing protests of regard and affection for her, vowed that she loved her as a daughter, and would do everything in her power to serve her.", "그러고는 크롤리 양의 어깨에 머리를 기대고 어찌나 자연스럽게 울던지, 얼결에 연민에 사로잡힌 노부인은 거의 어머니 같은 다정함으로 그녀를 끌어안고, 애정과 보살핌을 약속하는 위로의 말을 숱하게 늘어놓으며, 그녀를 친딸처럼 사랑하노라고, 그녀를 위해서라면 할 수 있는 일은 무엇이든 다 하겠노라고 다짐했다."], ["\"And now who is it, my dear?", "\"자, 그래 그게 누구냐, 얘야?"], ["Is it that pretty Miss Sedley's brother?", "그 어여쁜 세들리 양의 오라버니냐?"], ["You said something about an affair with him.", "그 사람과 무슨 일이 있다는 듯이 말했었지."], ["I'll ask him here, my dear.", "그 사람을 이리로 부르마, 얘야."], ["And you shall have him: indeed you shall.\"", "그래서 네가 그 사람을 갖게 해 주마. 정말이지 그래 주마.\""]], [["\"Don't ask me now,\"", "\"지금은 묻지 말아 주세요.\""], ["Rebecca said.", "리베카가 말했다."], ["\"You shall know all soon.", "\"곧 다 알게 되실 거예요."], ["Indeed you shall.", "정말 그러실 거예요."], ["Dear kind Miss Crawley--dear friend, may I say so?\"", "다정하고 친절한 크롤리 양, 그리운 벗이라고 불러도 될까요?\""]], [["\"That you may, my child,\"", "\"그럼, 되고말고, 얘야.\""], ["the old lady replied, kissing her.", "노부인이 그녀에게 입을 맞추며 대답했다."]], [["\"I can't tell you now,\"", "\"지금은 말씀드릴 수가 없어요.\""], ["sobbed out Rebecca, \"I am very miserable.", "리베카가 흐느끼며 토해 냈다. \"저는 너무나 비참해요."], ["But O!", "하지만 오!"], ["love me always--promise you will love me always.\"", "언제까지나 저를 사랑해 주세요, 언제까지나 사랑하시겠다고 약속해 주세요.\""], ["And in the midst of mutual tears--for the emotions of the younger woman had awakened the sympathies of the elder--this promise was solemnly given by Miss Crawley, who left her little protege, blessing and admiring her as a dear, artless, tender-hearted, affectionate, incomprehensible creature.", "그리고 서로 눈물을 흘리는 가운데(젊은 여자의 격정이 나이 든 여자의 연민을 불러일으켰던 것이다) 크롤리 양은 이 약속을 엄숙히 해 주었고, 자기의 작은 피후견인을 그리운, 천진한, 마음 여린, 정 많은, 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 아이라며 축복하고 감탄하면서 그 곁을 떠났다."]], [["And now she was left alone to think over the sudden and wonderful events of the day, and of what had been and what might have been.", "이제 그녀는 홀로 남아, 그날의 갑작스럽고 놀라운 사건들을, 또 있었던 일과 있을 수도 있었던 일들을 곰곰이 되새기게 되었다."], ["What think you were the private feelings of Miss, no (begging her pardon) of Mrs. Rebecca?", "샤프 양, 아니(실례를 무릅쓰자면) 리베카 부인의 속마음이 어떠했으리라고 생각하는가?"], ["If, a few pages back, the present writer claimed the privilege of peeping into Miss Amelia Sedley's bedroom, and understanding with the omniscience of the novelist all the gentle pains and passions which were tossing upon that innocent pillow, why should he not declare himself to be Rebecca's confidante too, master of her secrets, and seal-keeper of that young woman's conscience?", "몇 쪽 앞에서, 이 글을 쓰는 사람이 애밀리아 세들리 양의 침실을 엿보고, 그 순결한 베개 위에서 일렁이던 온갖 부드러운 아픔과 격정을 소설가의 전지함으로 헤아릴 특권을 주장했다면, 어찌하여 리베카의 속내를 털어놓는 벗이요, 그 비밀의 주인이요, 이 젊은 여자의 양심을 봉인해 두는 자라고 자처하지 못하겠는가?"]], [["Well, then, in the first place, Rebecca gave way to some very sincere and touching regrets that a piece of marvellous good fortune should have been so near her, and she actually obliged to decline it.", "자, 그렇다면 우선 리베카는, 그토록 굉장한 행운이 코앞까지 와 있었건만 그것을 사양할 수밖에 없었다는 데 대해 자못 진실하고 가슴 저린 회한에 빠져들었다."], ["In this natural emotion every properly regulated mind will certainly share.", "올바르게 다스려진 마음을 지닌 사람이라면 누구든 분명 이 자연스러운 심정에 공감할 것이다."], ["What good mother is there that would not commiserate a penniless spinster, who might have been my lady, and have shared four thousand a year?", "귀부인이 되어 연 사천 파운드를 함께 누릴 수도 있었던, 한 푼 없는 노처녀를 가엾이 여기지 않을 어진 어머니가 어디 있겠는가?"], ["What well-bred young person is there in all Vanity Fair, who will not feel for a hard-working, ingenious, meritorious girl, who gets such an honourable, advantageous, provoking offer, just at the very moment when it is out of her power to accept it?", "그토록 명예롭고 유리하고 애간장 타는 청혼을, 하필이면 그것을 받아들일 도리가 없는 바로 그 순간에 받게 된, 부지런하고 영리하고 갸륵한 아가씨를 두고 동정심을 느끼지 않을 교양 있는 젊은이가 이 허영의 시장 어디에 있겠는가?"], ["I am sure our friend Becky's disappointment deserves and will command every sympathy.", "우리의 벗 베키의 낙담은 마땅히 온갖 동정을 받을 만하며, 또 반드시 받게 될 것이다."]], [["I remember one night being in the Fair myself, at an evening party.", "언젠가 밤에 나 자신이 이 시장의 어느 야회에 가 있었던 일이 떠오른다."], ["I observed old Miss Toady there also present, single out for her special attentions and flattery little Mrs.", "그 자리에 늙은 토디 양도 와 있는 것을 보았는데, 그녀가 특별한 관심과 아첨의 대상으로 콕 집어낸 이는 변호사의 아내인 자그마한 브리플리스 부인이었다."], ["Briefless, the barrister's wife, who is of a good family certainly, but, as we all know, is as poor as poor can be.", "분명 그 부인은 양가 출신이긴 하나, 우리 모두가 알다시피 더없이 가난한 처지였다."]], [["What, I asked in my own mind, can cause this obsequiousness on the part of Miss Toady; has Briefless got a county court, or has his wife had a fortune left her?", "토디 양 쪽에서 이런 굽실거림을 보이는 까닭이 무엇일까, 나는 속으로 자문했다. 브리플리스가 무슨 주 법원 자리라도 얻었나, 아니면 그 아내한테 무슨 유산이라도 굴러들어 왔나?"], ["Miss Toady explained presently, with that simplicity which distinguishes all her conduct.", "토디 양은 곧, 그녀의 온갖 처신에서 한결같이 드러나는 그 천연덕스러움으로 까닭을 설명해 주었다."], ["\"You know,\"", "\"있잖아요,\""], ["she said, \"Mrs Briefless is granddaughter of Sir John Redhand, who is so ill at Cheltenham that he can't last six months.", "그녀가 말했다, \"브리플리스 부인이 존 레드핸드 경의 손녀딸이에요. 그분이 첼튼햄에서 어찌나 위중한지 반년도 못 버틸 거래요."], ["Mrs. Briefless's papa succeeds; so you see she will be a baronet's daughter.\"", "그러면 브리플리스 부인의 아버지가 작위를 잇게 되니, 보다시피 그 부인은 준남작의 딸이 되는 거지요.\""], ["And Toady asked Briefless and his wife to dinner the very next week.", "그러고서 토디는 바로 다음 주에 브리플리스 부부를 만찬에 초대했다."]], [["If the mere chance of becoming a baronet's daughter can procure a lady such homage in the world, surely, surely we may respect the agonies of a young woman who has lost the opportunity of becoming a baronet's wife.", "준남작의 딸이 될 가망만으로도 어떤 부인이 세상에서 그런 떠받듦을 받을 수 있다면, 준남작의 아내가 될 기회를 놓친 젊은 여인의 고뇌야말로 분명히, 분명히 우리가 존중해 마땅하지 않겠는가?"], ["Who would have dreamed of Lady Crawley dying so soon?", "크롤리 부인이 그토록 빨리 세상을 뜰 줄 누가 꿈엔들 알았겠는가?"], ["She was one of those sickly women that might have lasted these ten years--Rebecca thought to herself, in all the woes of repentance--and I might have been my lady!", "그 사람은 앞으로 십 년은 더 버틸 법한 그런 병약한 여자였는데, 하고 리베카는 후회의 온갖 비통함 속에서 속으로 생각했다."], ["I might have led that old man whither I would.", "그랬다면 나는 귀부인이 되었을 텐데!"], ["I might have thanked Mrs. Bute for her patronage, and Mr.", "그 늙은 양반을 내 마음대로 어디로든 끌고 다닐 수 있었을 텐데."], ["Pitt for his insufferable condescension. I would have had the town-house newly furnished and decorated.", "뷰트 부인의 후원에도 감사 인사를 해 주고, 피트 씨의 그 견딜 수 없는 거드름에도 고개를 숙여 줄 수 있었을 텐데."], ["I would have had the handsomest carriage in London, and a box at the opera; and I would have been presented next season.", "런던 저택은 가구며 장식을 새로 들였을 테고. 런던에서 제일 멋진 마차에, 오페라 극장에 전용석도 가졌을 테고, 다음 사교철에는 궁정에 알현도 했을 텐데."], ["All this might have been; and now--now all was doubt and mystery.", "이 모든 것이 가능했으련만. 그런데 이제, 이제는 모든 것이 불안과 수수께끼뿐이었다."]], [["But Rebecca was a young lady of too much resolution and energy of character to permit herself much useless and unseemly sorrow for the irrevocable past; so, having devoted only the proper portion of regret to it, she wisely turned her whole attention towards the future, which was now vastly more important to her.", "그러나 리베카는, 돌이킬 수 없는 과거를 두고 부질없고 꼴사납게 오래 슬퍼하기에는 너무나 결단력 있고 기개 넘치는 성격의 젊은 숙녀였다. 그리하여 거기에 딱 알맞은 몫의 회한만을 바친 다음, 그녀는 현명하게도 온 정신을 미래로 돌렸으니, 이제 그것이야말로 그녀에게 비할 데 없이 더 중요한 일이었다."], ["And she surveyed her position, and its hopes, doubts, and chances.", "그리고 자신의 처지와, 그 처지에 깃든 희망과 불안과 가능성을 두루 살폈다."]], [["In the first place, she was MARRIED--that was a great fact.", "우선 첫째로, 그녀는 결혼한 몸이었다. 그것은 엄연한 사실이었다."], ["Sir Pitt knew it.", "피트 경이 그 사실을 알았다."], ["She was not so much surprised into the avowal, as induced to make it by a sudden calculation.", "그녀가 그 고백을 한 것은 얼결에 몰려서라기보다, 순간적인 계산에 이끌린 것이었다."], ["It must have come some day: and why not now as at a later period?", "언젠가는 밝혀질 수밖에 없는 일이었다. 그러니 나중이 아니라 지금인들 어떠랴?"], ["He who would have married her himself must at least be silent with regard to her marriage.", "자기가 직접 그녀와 결혼하려 했던 사람이니, 적어도 그녀의 결혼에 대해서는 입을 다물 수밖에 없을 터였다. 크롤리 양이 그 소식을 어떻게 받아들일까, 그것이 가장 큰 문제였다."], ["How Miss Crawley would bear the news--was the great question.", "리베카에게 불안이 없지는 않았다. 그러나 그녀는 크롤리 양이 한 말을 죄다 떠올렸다."], ["Misgivings Rebecca had; but she remembered all Miss Crawley had said; the old lady's avowed contempt for birth; her daring liberal opinions; her general romantic propensities; her almost doting attachment to her nephew, and her repeatedly expressed fondness for Rebecca herself.", "노부인이 공공연히 드러내는 가문에 대한 경멸, 그 대담하고 진보적인 견해, 전반적으로 낭만을 좇는 기질, 조카를 향한 거의 맹목적인 애착, 그리고 리베카 자신에게 거듭 표한 다정함을. 이모님은 그 사람을 어찌나 끔찍이 위하는지 무슨 짓이든 용서하고 말 거야, 하고 리베카는 생각했다."], ["She is so fond of him, Rebecca thought, that she will forgive him anything: she is so used to me that I don't think she could be comfortable without me: when the eclaircissement comes there will be a scene, and hysterics, and a great quarrel, and then a great reconciliation.", "또 나한테도 어찌나 익숙해졌는지, 내가 없으면 영 마음이 편치 않으실걸. 모든 것이 밝혀지고 나면 한바탕 소동이 일고, 히스테리가 터지고, 큰 다툼이 벌어졌다가, 이윽고 큰 화해가 찾아오겠지."], ["At all events, what use was there in delaying?", "어차피 미룬다고 무슨 소용이 있겠는가?"], ["the die was thrown, and now or to-morrow the issue must be the same.", "주사위는 던져졌고, 결과란 지금이든 내일이든 매한가지일 터였다."], ["And so, resolved that Miss Crawley should have the news, the young person debated in her mind as to the best means of conveying it to her; and whether she should face the storm that must come, or fly and avoid it until its first fury was blown over.", "그리하여 크롤리 양에게 이 소식을 알리기로 마음을 굳힌 이 젊은 여인은, 그것을 그녀에게 전할 가장 좋은 방도가 무엇일지를 마음속으로 따져 보았다. 닥쳐올 게 뻔한 그 폭풍을 정면으로 맞을 것인가, 아니면 첫 분노가 가라앉을 때까지 달아나 피해 있을 것인가."], ["In this state of meditation she wrote the following letter:", "이런 궁리에 잠긴 채 그녀는 다음과 같은 편지를 썼다."]], [["Dearest Friend,", "더없이 그리운 벗에게."]], [["The great crisis which we have debated about so often is COME.", "우리가 그토록 자주 의논했던 그 큰 위기가 닥쳤어요."], ["Half of my secret is known, and I have thought and thought, until I am quite sure that now is the time to reveal THE WHOLE OF THE MYSTERY.", "제 비밀의 절반이 알려지고 말았어요. 그래서 생각하고 또 생각한 끝에, 이제야말로 그 수수께끼 전부를 밝힐 때라고 확신하게 되었답니다."], ["Sir Pitt came to me this morning, and made--what do you think?--A DECLARATION IN FORM.", "오늘 아침 피트 경이 저를 찾아와서, 무엇을 했는지 아세요? 정식으로 청혼을 했답니다."], ["Think of that!", "생각 좀 해 보세요!"], ["Poor little me. I might have been Lady Crawley.", "가엾은 이 몸이 크롤리 부인이 될 수도 있었던 거예요."], ["How pleased Mrs.", "뷰트 부인이 얼마나 흐뭇해했을까요."], ["Bute would have been: and ma tante if I had taken precedence of her!", "그리고 제가 이모님(ma tante)을 제치고 윗자리를 차지했다면 이모님은 또 어땠을까요!"], ["I might have been somebody's mamma, instead of--O, I tremble, I tremble, when I think how soon we must tell all!", "저는 누군가의 어머니가 될 수도 있었던 거예요. 그런데 그 대신에, 오, 우리가 머잖아 모든 걸 털어놓아야 한다는 걸 생각하면, 저는 떨려요, 정말 떨려요!"]], [["Sir Pitt knows I am married, and not knowing to whom, is not very much displeased as yet.", "피트 경은 제가 결혼한 몸이라는 걸 알아요. 다만 상대가 누구인지는 모르니, 아직은 그리 언짢아하지 않아요."], ["Ma tante is ACTUALLY ANGRY that I should have refused him.", "이모님은 제가 그를 거절했다고 정말로 화를 내신답니다."], ["But she is all kindness and graciousness.", "그래도 더없이 다정하고 너그러우세요."], ["She condescends to say I would have made him a good wife; and vows that she will be a mother to your little Rebecca.", "황송하게도 제가 그분의 좋은 아내가 되었을 거라고 말씀하시고, 당신의 이 작은 리베카에게 어머니가 되어 주겠다고 다짐하세요."], ["She will be shaken when she first hears the news.", "처음 그 소식을 들으면 이모님도 충격을 받으실 테지요."], ["But need we fear anything beyond a momentary anger?", "하지만 한순간의 노여움 너머의 무언가를 우리가 두려워할 필요가 있을까요?"], ["I think not: I AM SURE not.", "저는 그렇지 않다고 생각해요. 그렇지 않다고 확신해요."], ["She dotes upon you so (you naughty, good-for-nothing man), that she would pardon you ANYTHING: and, indeed, I believe, the next place in her heart is mine: and that she would be miserable without me.", "이모님은 당신을 어찌나 끔찍이 위하시는지(이 짓궂고 쓸모없는 양반아), 당신이라면 무슨 짓이든 용서하실 거예요. 그리고 정말이지, 그분 마음속에서 그다음 자리는 제 차지라고 믿어요."], ["Dearest!", "제가 없으면 비참해하실 거예요."], ["something TELLS ME we shall conquer.", "더없이 그리운 이여! 무언가가 제게 일러 주네요, 우리가 이겨 내리라고."], ["You shall leave that odious regiment: quit gaming, racing, and BE A GOOD BOY; and we shall all live in Park Lane, and ma tante shall leave us all her money.", "당신은 그 지긋지긋한 연대를 떠나, 노름이며 경마를 끊고 착한 사람이 되세요. 그러면 우리 모두 파크 레인에 살게 될 테고, 이모님은 우리에게 그 재산을 전부 물려주실 거예요."]], [["I shall try and walk to-morrow at 3 in the usual place.", "내일 세 시에 여느 때 그 자리로 산책 나가 보려고 해요."], ["If Miss B. accompanies me, you must come to dinner, and bring an answer, and put it in the third volume of Porteus's Sermons.", "만약 B 양이 저와 동행하거든, 당신은 만찬에 와서 답장을 가져와 포티어스 설교집 셋째 권에 끼워 두세요."], ["But, at all events, come to your own", "하지만 어떻든, 당신의 사람인 이 몸에게로 와 주세요."]], [["R.", "R."]], [["To Miss Eliza Styles, At Mr. Barnet's, Saddler, Knightsbridge.", "엘리자 스타일스 양 앞, 나이츠브리지 마구상 바넷 씨 댁 전교."]], [["And I trust there is no reader of this little story who has not discernment enough to perceive that the Miss Eliza Styles (an old schoolfellow, Rebecca said, with whom she had resumed an active correspondence of late, and who used to fetch these letters from the saddler's), wore brass spurs, and large curling mustachios, and was indeed no other than Captain Rawdon Crawley.", "그리고 나는 이 자그마한 이야기의 독자들 가운데, 그 엘리자 스타일스 양이란(리베카의 말로는 옛 학교 동무로, 요즘 들어 다시 부지런히 편지를 주고받게 되었으며, 마구상에게서 이 편지들을 받아 오곤 했다는 그 사람이) 놋쇠 박차를 차고 크게 말아 올린 콧수염을 기른, 실은 다름 아닌 로던 크롤리 대위였음을 알아챌 만한 분별력조차 없는 이는 한 사람도 없으리라 믿는다."]], [["The Letter on the Pincushion\nHow they were married is not of the slightest consequence to anybody.", "그들이 어떻게 결혼했는가 하는 문제는 누구에게도 털끝만큼도 중요하지 않다."], ["What is to hinder a Captain who is a major, and a young lady who is of age, from purchasing a licence, and uniting themselves at any church in this town?", "소령이기도 한 대위와 성년에 이른 젊은 숙녀가 혼인 허가서를 사들여 이 도시의 어느 교회에서든 백년가약을 맺는 것을 무엇이 막을 수 있단 말인가? 여자에게 의지가 있다면 반드시 방도를 찾아내고야 만다는 사실을 굳이 누구에게 일러 줄 필요가 있겠는가?"], ["Who needs to be told, that if a woman has a will she will assuredly find a way?--My belief is that one day, when Miss Sharp had gone to pass the forenoon with her dear friend Miss Amelia Sedley in Russell Square, a lady very like her might have been seen entering a church in the City, in company with a gentleman with dyed mustachios, who, after a quarter of an hour's interval, escorted her back to the hackney-coach in waiting, and that this was a quiet bridal party.", "내 짐작으로는, 어느 날 샤프 양이 러셀 광장에 사는 그 다정한 벗 애밀리아 세들리 양과 오전을 보내러 갔다던 그날, 그녀와 무척 닮은 한 숙녀가 도심의 어느 교회로 들어서는 모습을 본 사람이 있을 법하다. 그녀는 콧수염을 물들인 한 신사와 동행이었으며, 그 신사는 십오 분쯤 지난 뒤 그녀를 대기하고 있던 삯마차까지 바래다주었으니, 이것이 바로 어느 조촐한 혼례의 일행이었던 것이다."]], [["And who on earth, after the daily experience we have, can question the probability of a gentleman marrying anybody?", "게다가, 우리가 날마다 겪는 경험에 비추어 볼 때, 신사가 누군가와 결혼할 개연성을 도대체 누가 의심할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["How many of the wise and learned have married their cooks?", "현명하고 학식 있는 이들 가운데 자기 집 요리사와 결혼한 자가 또 얼마나 많은가?"], ["Did not Lord Eldon himself, the most prudent of men, make a runaway match?", "더없이 신중한 인물인 엘던 경 본인조차 야반도주식 혼인을 하지 않았던가?"], ["Were not Achilles and Ajax both in love with their servant maids?", "아킬레우스와 아이아스 또한 모두 자기네 하녀를 사랑하지 않았던가?"], ["And are we to expect a heavy dragoon with strong desires and small brains, who had never controlled a passion in his life, to become prudent all of a sudden, and to refuse to pay any price for an indulgence to which he had a mind?", "그런데 욕망은 거센데 두뇌는 빈약하고, 평생 단 한 번도 격정을 다스려 본 적이 없는 우람한 용기병이 어느 날 갑자기 신중해져서, 마음에 둔 향락에 대가 치르기를 거부하리라고 기대해야 한단 말인가?"], ["If people only made prudent marriages, what a stop to population there would be!", "사람들이 신중한 결혼만 한다면, 인구 증가에 그 얼마나 큰 제동이 걸리겠는가!"]], [["It seems to me, for my part, that Mr. Rawdon's marriage was one of the honestest actions which we shall have to record in any portion of that gentleman's biography which has to do with the present history.", "내 생각에는, 로던 씨의 결혼이야말로 이번 이야기와 관련된 그 신사의 전기 어느 대목에서든 우리가 기록하게 될 가장 정직한 행동의 하나였던 셈이다."], ["No one will say it is unmanly to be captivated by a woman, or, being captivated, to marry her; and the admiration, the delight, the passion, the wonder, the unbounded confidence, and frantic adoration with which, by degrees, this big warrior got to regard the little Rebecca, were feelings which the ladies at least will pronounce were not altogether discreditable to him.", "한 여인에게 사로잡히는 것이, 혹은 사로잡힌 끝에 그 여인과 결혼하는 것이 사내답지 못한 일이라고 말할 사람은 아무도 없으리라. 그리고 이 거구의 전사가 그 자그마한 레베카를 차츰 바라보게 된 그 흠모며 환희며 격정이며 경탄이며 그 무한한 신뢰와 미칠 듯한 숭배는, 적어도 숙녀분들이라면 그에게 그다지 불명예스러운 감정만은 아니라고 단언할 그런 감정들이었다."], ["When she sang, every note thrilled in his dull soul, and tingled through his huge frame.", "그녀가 노래하면 음표 하나하나가 그의 둔한 영혼을 전율케 했고 그 거대한 몸뚱이를 짜릿하게 관통했다."], ["When she spoke, he brought all the force of his brains to listen and wonder.", "그녀가 말하면 그는 온 두뇌의 힘을 그러모아 귀 기울이며 경탄했다."], ["If she was jocular, he used to revolve her jokes in his mind, and explode over them half an hour afterwards in the street, to the surprise of the groom in the tilbury by his side, or the comrade riding with him in Rotten Row.", "그녀가 농을 던지면 그는 그 농담을 머릿속에서 곱씹다가 반 시간 뒤 거리에서 별안간 웃음을 터뜨려, 곁에 탄 이륜마차의 마부나 로튼 거리를 함께 말 타고 가던 동료를 놀라게 하곤 했다."], ["Her words were oracles to him, her smallest actions marked by an infallible grace and wisdom.", "그녀의 말은 그에게 신탁이었고, 그녀의 사소한 행동 하나하나도 빈틈없는 우아함과 지혜의 표시였다."], ["\"How she sings,--how she paints,\"", "\"저렇게 노래를 잘하다니, 그림도 어찌나 잘 그리는지.\""], ["thought he.", "그는 생각했다."], ["\"How she rode that kicking mare at Queen's Crawley!\"", "\"퀸스크롤리에서 그 발길질해 대는 암말을 타던 모습이라니!\""], ["And he would say to her in confidential moments, \"By Jove, Beck, you're fit to be Commander-in-Chief, or Archbishop of Canterbury, by Jove.\"", "그러고는 은밀한 순간이면 그녀에게 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"이런, 베키, 당신은 총사령관이라도, 아니 캔터베리 대주교라도 될 만한 사람이야, 정말로.\""], ["Is his case a rare one?", "그의 경우가 과연 드문 일이던가?"], ["and don't we see every day in the world many an honest Hercules at the apron-strings of Omphale, and great whiskered Samsons prostrate in Delilah's lap?", "우리는 옴팔레의 앞치마 끈에 매여 사는 정직한 헤라클레스며, 데릴라의 무릎에 엎드린 구레나룻 무성한 삼손을 세상에서 날마다 보고 있지 않은가?"]], [["When, then, Becky told him that the great crisis was near, and the time for action had arrived, Rawdon expressed himself as ready to act under her orders, as he would be to charge with his troop at the command of his colonel.", "그리하여 베키가 그에게 중대한 고비가 가까웠고 행동에 나설 때가 왔다고 일러 주자, 로던은 마치 대령의 명령에 따라 부대를 이끌고 돌격할 때처럼 그녀의 명령에 따라 행동할 준비가 되어 있노라 밝혔다."], ["There was no need for him to put his letter into the third volume of Porteus.", "그가 자기 편지를 포티어스 설교집 제3권 사이에 끼워 둘 필요도 없었다."], ["Rebecca easily found a means to get rid of Briggs, her companion, and met her faithful friend in \"the usual place\" on the next day.", "레베카는 손쉽게 방도를 찾아 자기 말동무인 브리그스를 떼어 내고는, 이튿날 \"늘 만나던 곳\"에서 그 충직한 벗과 만났다."], ["She had thought over matters at night, and communicated to Rawdon the result of her determinations.", "그녀는 밤새 일을 곰곰이 따져 보았고, 그 결심의 결과를 로던에게 알렸다. 그는 물론 모든 일에 동의했다."], ["He agreed, of course, to everything; was quite sure that it was all right: that what she proposed was best; that Miss Crawley would infallibly relent, or \"come round,\"", "다 잘될 것이 틀림없으며, 그녀가 제안한 것이 최선이며, 크롤리 양도 시간이 좀 지나면 어김없이 마음을 누그러뜨릴 것이라고, 그의 표현대로라면 \"돌아설\""], ["as he said, after a time.", "것이라고 굳게 믿었다."], ["Had Rebecca's resolutions been entirely different, he would have followed them as implicitly.", "설령 레베카의 결심이 전혀 딴판이었다 한들, 그는 똑같이 고분고분 그것을 따랐을 것이다."], ["\"You have head enough for both of us, Beck,\"", "\"당신 머리 하나면 우리 둘 몫은 충분해, 베키.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"You're sure to get us out of the scrape.", "\"당신이라면 이 곤경에서 우리를 틀림없이 빼내 줄 거야."], ["I never saw your equal, and I've met with some clippers in my time too.\"", "나는 당신 같은 사람을 본 적이 없어. 그래도 내 평생 제법 똑 부러진 사람들을 만나 봤는데도 말이야.\""], ["And with this simple confession of faith, the love-stricken dragoon left her to execute his part of the project which she had formed for the pair.", "그러고는 이 소박한 신앙 고백과 함께, 사랑에 빠진 그 용기병은 그녀가 두 사람을 위해 짜 놓은 계획에서 자기 몫을 실행하러 그녀 곁을 떠났다."]], [["It consisted simply in the hiring of quiet lodgings at Brompton, or in the neighbourhood of the barracks, for Captain and Mrs.", "그 몫이란 그저 브롬프턴, 혹은 병영 근처에 크롤리 대위 부부가 묵을 조용한 셋방을 구하는 일이었다."], ["Crawley. For Rebecca had determined, and very prudently, we think, to fly.", "레베카가, 우리 보기에 매우 신중하게도, 달아나기로 결심했기 때문이다."], ["Rawdon was only too happy at her resolve; he had been entreating her to take this measure any time for weeks past.", "로던은 그녀의 결심에 더없이 기뻐했다. 그는 지난 몇 주 내내 그녀에게 이렇게 하자고 졸라 오던 터였다."], ["He pranced off to engage the lodgings with all the impetuosity of love.", "그는 사랑의 온갖 조급함을 안고 의기양양하게 셋방을 구하러 나섰다."], ["He agreed to pay two guineas a week so readily, that the landlady regretted she had asked him so little.", "그가 일주일에 이 기니를 내겠노라 어찌나 선뜻 응하던지, 집주인 여자는 자기가 너무 적게 불렀다고 후회할 지경이었다."], ["He ordered in a piano, and half a nursery-house full of flowers: and a heap of good things.", "그는 피아노 한 대를 주문해 들이고, 묘목원 절반을 비울 만큼 꽃을 들였으며, 온갖 좋은 물건을 산더미처럼 사들였다."], ["As for shawls, kid gloves, silk stockings, gold French watches, bracelets and perfumery, he sent them in with the profusion of blind love and unbounded credit.", "숄이며 새끼 염소 가죽 장갑이며 비단 양말이며 프랑스산 금시계며 팔찌며 향수로 말하자면, 그는 눈먼 사랑과 무한한 외상의 아낌없는 씀씀이로 그것들을 보내 댔다."], ["And having relieved his mind by this outpouring of generosity, he went and dined nervously at the club, waiting until the great moment of his life should come.", "그렇게 이 너그러움을 쏟아 내어 마음의 짐을 던 그는, 자기 인생의 위대한 순간이 오기를 기다리며 클럽에 가서 안절부절못하며 저녁을 들었다."]], [["The occurrences of the previous day; the admirable conduct of Rebecca in refusing an offer so advantageous to her, the secret unhappiness preying upon her, the sweetness and silence with which she bore her affliction, made Miss Crawley much more tender than usual.", "전날의 사건들, 그러니까 자신에게 그토록 유리한 청혼을 거절한 레베카의 갸륵한 처신과, 그녀를 좀먹는 남모를 불행과, 그 고통을 견디는 다소곳하고 그윽한 침묵은, 크롤리 양을 여느 때보다 한결 다정하게 만들었다."], ["An event of this nature, a marriage, or a refusal, or a proposal, thrills through a whole household of women, and sets all their hysterical sympathies at work. As an observer of human nature, I regularly frequent St.", "이런 종류의 사건, 즉 결혼이며 거절이며 청혼이라는 것은 한집안 여자들 전체를 전율케 하고 그들의 발작적인 동정심을 죄다 발동시키는 법이다."], ["George's, Hanover Square, during the genteel marriage season; and though I have never seen the bridegroom's male friends give way to tears, or the beadles and officiating clergy any way affected, yet it is not at all uncommon to see women who are not in the least concerned in the operations going on--old ladies who are long past marrying, stout middle-aged females with plenty of sons and daughters, let alone pretty young creatures in pink bonnets, who are on their promotion, and may naturally take an interest in the ceremony--I say it is quite common to see the women present piping, sobbing, sniffling; hiding their little faces in their little useless pocket-handkerchiefs; and heaving, old and young, with emotion.", "인간 본성의 관찰자로서, 나는 점잖은 결혼철이면 어김없이 하노버 광장의 세인트조지 교회를 드나든다. 비록 신랑의 남자 친구들이 눈물에 무너지는 모습이나, 교회 안내인이며 식을 집전하는 성직자가 조금이라도 마음 흔들리는 모습은 한 번도 본 적이 없지만, 그 진행되는 일과 손톱만큼도 관계없는 여자들이, 그러니까 혼인할 나이가 한참 지난 노부인들이며, 아들딸을 잔뜩 둔 살집 좋은 중년 여인들이, 게다가 이제 한창 시집갈 나이라 자연히 그 예식에 관심을 가질 법한 분홍 모자 쓴 어여쁜 젊은 처자들은 말할 것도 없고, 하여튼 그런 여자들이 훌쩍이고, 흐느끼고, 코를 훌쩍이며, 그 자그마한 얼굴을 쓸모도 없는 자그마한 손수건에 파묻은 채, 늙은이고 젊은이고 할 것 없이 감정에 들썩이는 모습을 보는 일은 결코 드물지 않다."], ["When my friend, the fashionable John Pimlico, married the lovely Lady Belgravia Green Parker, the excitement was so general that even the little snuffy old pew-opener who let me into the seat was in tears.", "내 친구인 멋쟁이 존 핌리코가 어여쁜 벨그레이비어 그린파커 영애와 결혼하던 날에는, 그 흥분이 어찌나 두루 번졌던지 나를 자리로 안내한 코담배 냄새 풍기는 자그마한 늙은 좌석 안내원 노파마저 눈물을 글썽였다."], ["And wherefore?", "한데 그건 어째서인가?"], ["I inquired of my own soul: she was not going to be married.", "나는 내 영혼에게 물었다. 그 노파가 결혼하는 것도 아니었거늘."]], [["Miss Crawley and Briggs in a word, after the affair of Sir Pitt, indulged in the utmost luxury of sentiment, and Rebecca became an object of the most tender interest to them.", "요컨대 크롤리 양과 브리그스는 피트 경의 사건이 있은 뒤로 더없이 호사스러운 감상에 흠뻑 빠져들었고, 레베카는 그들에게 더없이 애틋한 관심의 대상이 되었다."], ["In her absence Miss Crawley solaced herself with the most sentimental of the novels in her library.", "그녀가 없는 동안 크롤리 양은 서재에 있는 가장 감상적인 소설들로 마음을 달랬다."], ["Little Sharp, with her secret griefs, was the heroine of the day.", "남모를 비탄을 품은 자그마한 샤프야말로 그 시절의 여주인공이었던 것이다."]], [["That night Rebecca sang more sweetly and talked more pleasantly than she had ever been heard to do in Park Lane.", "그날 밤 레베카는 파크 레인에서 일찍이 들려준 적 없을 만큼 감미롭게 노래하고 즐겁게 이야기했다."], ["She twined herself round the heart of Miss Crawley.", "그녀는 크롤리 양의 마음을 칭칭 휘감았다."], ["She spoke lightly and laughingly of Sir Pitt's proposal, ridiculed it as the foolish fancy of an old man; and her eyes filled with tears, and Briggs's heart with unutterable pangs of defeat, as she said she desired no other lot than to remain for ever with her dear benefactress.", "그녀는 피트 경의 청혼을 가볍고 우스개 삼아 이야기하며, 늙은이의 어리석은 망상이라고 비웃었다. 그러고는 사랑하는 은인 곁에 영영 머무는 것 말고는 다른 운명을 바라지 않는다고 말할 때, 그녀의 두 눈은 눈물로 가득 찼고 브리그스의 가슴은 말로 다 할 수 없는 패배의 고통으로 가득 찼다."], ["\"My dear little creature,\"", "\"내 귀여운 것아.\""], ["the old lady said, \"I don't intend to let you stir for years, that you may depend upon it.", "노부인이 말했다. \"앞으로 몇 해든 너를 곁에서 한 발짝도 못 떠나게 할 작정이다, 그건 믿어도 좋아."], ["As for going back to that odious brother of mine after what has passed, it is out of the question.", "이런 일이 있고 난 마당에 그 밉살스러운 내 동생한테 돌아간다는 건 어림도 없는 소리지."], ["Here you stay with me and Briggs.", "너는 여기서 나와 브리그스랑 함께 지내는 거야."], ["Briggs wants to go to see her relations very often. Briggs, you may go when you like.", "브리그스는 친척들 보러 가고 싶어 자주 그러니까, 브리그스, 너야 가고 싶을 때 가도 좋다."], ["But as for you, my dear, you must stay and take care of the old woman.\"", "하지만 너만은, 얘야, 여기 남아서 이 늙은이를 돌봐 줘야 한다.\""]], [["If Rawdon Crawley had been then and there present, instead of being at the club nervously drinking claret, the pair might have gone down on their knees before the old spinster, avowed all, and been forgiven in a twinkling.", "만약 로던 크롤리가 클럽에서 안절부절못하며 적포도주나 들이켜는 대신 그때 그 자리에 있었더라면, 두 사람은 그 늙은 노처녀 앞에 무릎 꿇고 죄다 털어놓아 눈 깜짝할 새에 용서받았을지도 모른다."], ["But that good chance was denied to the young couple, doubtless in order that this story might be written, in which numbers of their wonderful adventures are narrated--adventures which could never have occurred to them if they had been housed and sheltered under the comfortable uninteresting forgiveness of Miss Crawley.", "그러나 그 좋은 기회는 이 젊은 부부에게 허락되지 않았으니, 이는 분명 이 이야기가 쓰이게 하기 위함이었던 것이다. 이 이야기에는 그들의 놀라운 모험이 숱하게 펼쳐지거니와, 만약 그들이 크롤리 양의 안락하고 따분한 용서 아래 깃들어 보살핌을 받았더라면 결코 그들에게 일어날 수 없었을 모험들인 것이다."]], [["Under Mrs. Firkin's orders, in the Park Lane establishment, was a young woman from Hampshire, whose business it was, among other duties, to knock at Miss Sharp's door with that jug of hot water which Firkin would rather have perished than have presented to the intruder.", "파크 레인 저택에서 퍼킨 부인의 지휘를 받는 사람들 가운데, 햄프셔 출신의 젊은 여자가 하나 있었다."], ["This girl, bred on the family estate, had a brother in Captain Crawley's troop, and if the truth were known, I daresay it would come out that she was aware of certain arrangements, which have a great deal to do with this history.", "그녀가 맡은 여러 일 가운데 하나는, 퍼킨이라면 차라리 죽어 버릴지언정 그 굴러든 식객에게는 결코 들여놓지 않았을 그 뜨거운 물 주전자를 들고 샤프 양의 방문을 두드리는 것이었다. 가문의 영지에서 자란 이 여자에게는 크롤리 대위의 부대에 든 오라비가 있었으니, 사실인즉 이 이야기와 깊이 관련된 어떤 모의를 그녀가 알고 있었다는 것이 드러나리라 나는 감히 짐작한다."], ["At any rate she purchased a yellow shawl, a pair of green boots, and a light blue hat with a red feather with three guineas which Rebecca gave her, and as little Sharp was by no means too liberal with her money, no doubt it was for services rendered that Betty Martin was so bribed.", "어쨌거나 그녀는 레베카가 준 삼 기니로 노란 숄 하나와 초록색 부츠 한 켤레와 빨간 깃털 꽂힌 연한 파란 모자 하나를 사들였다. 자그마한 샤프가 결코 제 돈을 헤프게 쓰는 사람이 아니었던 만큼, 베티 마틴이 그렇게 매수당한 것은 의심할 바 없이 그녀가 제공한 어떤 봉사에 대한 대가였던 것이다."]], [["On the second day after Sir Pitt Crawley's offer to Miss Sharp, the sun rose as usual, and at the usual hour Betty Martin, the upstairs maid, knocked at the door of the governess's bedchamber.", "피트 크롤리 경이 샤프 양에게 청혼한 지 이틀째 되던 날, 해는 여느 때처럼 떠올랐고, 여느 때와 같은 시각에 이층 담당 하녀 베티 마틴이 가정교사 침실의 방문을 두드렸다."]], [["No answer was returned, and she knocked again.", "아무런 응답이 없자 그녀는 다시 두드렸다."], ["Silence was still uninterrupted; and Betty, with the hot water, opened the door and entered the chamber.", "침묵은 여전히 깨지지 않았다. 베티는 뜨거운 물을 든 채 문을 열고 방 안으로 들어섰다."]], [["The little white dimity bed was as smooth and trim as on the day previous, when Betty's own hands had helped to make it.", "자그마한 흰 무명 침대는 어제, 베티 자신의 손으로 정돈을 거들었던 그날과 다름없이 매끈하고 가지런했다."], ["Two little trunks were corded in one end of the room; and on the table before the window--on the pincushion--the great fat pincushion lined with pink inside, and twilled like a lady's nightcap--lay a letter.", "자그마한 트렁크 두 개가 방 한쪽 끝에 끈으로 묶여 있었고, 창가의 탁자 위에는, 그러니까 바늘꽂이 위에는 — 안에 분홍색 천을 댄, 숙녀의 나이트캡처럼 골이 진 그 크고 통통한 바늘꽂이 위에는 — 편지 한 통이 놓여 있었다."], ["It had been reposing there probably all night.", "아마 밤새 그 자리에 놓여 있었던 모양이었다."]], [["Betty advanced towards it on tiptoe, as if she were afraid to awake it--looked at it, and round the room, with an air of great wonder and satisfaction; took up the letter, and grinned intensely as she turned it round and over, and finally carried it into Miss Briggs's room below.", "베티는 그것을 깨우기라도 할까 두려운 듯 발끝으로 살금살금 다가가서는, 자못 놀랍고도 흡족한 기색으로 편지를 보고 또 방 안을 둘러보았다. 그러고는 편지를 집어 들어 이리저리 뒤집어 보며 함박 히죽거리더니, 마침내 그것을 아래층 브리그스 양의 방으로 가지고 갔다."]], [["How could Betty tell that the letter was for Miss Briggs, I should like to know?", "대체 베티가 그 편지가 브리그스 양에게 온 것인 줄 어떻게 알았는지, 나는 정말 알고 싶다."], ["All the schooling Betty had had was at Mrs. Bute Crawley's Sunday school, and she could no more read writing than Hebrew.", "베티가 받은 교육이라고는 죄다 뷰트 크롤리 부인의 주일학교에서 배운 것이 전부였고, 그녀로서는 글씨를 읽는 것이 히브리어를 읽는 것이나 진배없었던 것이다."]], [["\"La, Miss Briggs,\" the girl exclaimed, \"O, Miss, something must have happened--there's nobody in Miss Sharp's room; the bed ain't been slep in, and she've run away, and left this letter for you, Miss.\"", "\"어머나, 브리그스 아씨.\" 처녀가 외쳤다. \"아씨, 무슨 일이 났나 봐요. 샤프 아씨 방에 아무도 없어요. 침대에선 잔 흔적도 없고요, 도망쳐 버린 거예요, 아씨한테 이 편지를 남겨 놓고요.\""]], [["\"WHAT!\"", "\"뭐라고!\""], ["cries Briggs, dropping her comb, the thin wisp of faded hair falling over her shoulders; \"an elopement!", "브리그스가 빗을 떨어뜨리며 외치니, 빛바랜 머리카락 한 가닥이 어깨 위로 흘러내렸다. \"야반도주라니!"], ["Miss Sharp a fugitive!", "샤프 양이 달아나다니!"], ["What, what is this?\"", "이게, 이게 무슨 일이람?\""], ["and she eagerly broke the neat seal, and, as they say, \"devoured the contents\" of the letter addressed to her.", "그러고는 그녀는 다급하게 그 단정한 봉인을 뜯고는, 말마따나 자신 앞으로 온 그 편지의 \"내용을 게걸스레 삼켰다.\""]], [["Dear Miss Briggs [the refugee wrote], the kindest heart in the world, as yours is, will pity and sympathise with me and excuse me.", "친애하는 브리그스 양 [도망친 여인은 이렇게 적었다], 당신처럼 세상에서 가장 어진 마음을 지닌 분이라면 저를 가엾이 여겨 동정하고 또 용서해 주시겠지요."], ["With tears, and prayers, and blessings, I leave the home where the poor orphan has ever met with kindness and affection.", "눈물과 기도와 축복으로, 이 가엾은 고아가 한결같이 친절과 애정을 받아 온 그 보금자리를 저는 떠납니다."], ["Claims even superior to those of my benefactress call me hence.", "저의 은인보다도 더 우선하는 도리가 저를 이곳에서 불러내는 것입니다."], ["I go to my duty--to my HUSBAND.", "저는 제 도리를 다하러 — 제 남편에게로 갑니다."], ["Yes, I am married.", "그렇습니다, 저는 결혼했습니다."], ["My husband COMMANDS me to seek the HUMBLE HOME which we call ours.", "제 남편이 우리의 것이라 부르는 그 초라한 보금자리를 찾아오라 명하는 것입니다."], ["Dearest Miss Briggs, break the news as your delicate sympathy will know how to do it--to my dear, my beloved friend and benefactress.", "더없이 친애하는 브리그스 양, 부디 그 섬세한 동정심으로 잘 아실 그 방법대로, 저의 사랑하고 흠모하는 벗이자 은인께 이 소식을 전해 주세요."], ["Tell her, ere I went, I shed tears on her dear pillow--that pillow that I have so often soothed in sickness--that I long AGAIN to watch--Oh, with what joy shall I return to dear Park Lane!", "제가 떠나기 전, 그분의 다정한 베개에 — 병석에 누우셨을 때 제가 그토록 자주 매만져 드리던 그 베개에, 다시금 곁에서 지켜보기를 간절히 바라는 그 베개에 눈물을 떨구었노라고 전해 주세요. 아, 그리운 파크 레인으로 돌아갈 때 저는 얼마나 큰 기쁨에 젖을는지요!"], ["How I tremble for the answer which is to SEAL MY FATE!", "제 운명을 결정지을 그 답을 생각하면 어찌나 떨리는지요!"], ["When Sir Pitt deigned to offer me his hand, an honour of which my beloved Miss Crawley said I was DESERVING (my blessings go with her for judging the poor orphan worthy to be HER SISTER!) I told Sir Pitt that I was already A WIFE.", "피트 경께서 황송하게도 제게 손을 내미셨을 때, 사랑하는 크롤리 양께서 제가 그럴 자격이 있다고 말씀하셨던 그 영광을 (이 가엾은 고아를 당신의 동생감으로 합당하게 보아 주신 그분께 축복이 함께하기를!) 받았을 때, 저는 피트 경께 이미 한 사람의 아내라고 말씀드렸습니다."], ["Even he forgave me.", "그분조차 저를 용서하셨지요."], ["But my courage failed me, when I should have told him all--that I could not be his wife, for I WAS HIS DAUGHTER!", "그러나 모든 것을 말씀드려야 했을 때 — 그분의 아내가 될 수 없는 까닭이 제가 바로 그분의 딸이기 때문이라는 것을 말씀드려야 했을 때, 제겐 용기가 나지 않았습니다!"], ["I am wedded to the best and most generous of men--Miss Crawley's Rawdon is MY Rawdon.", "저는 가장 선량하고 가장 너그러운 사내와 부부의 연을 맺었습니다 — 크롤리 양의 로던이 바로 저의 로던입니다."], ["At his COMMAND I open my lips, and follow him to our humble home, as I would THROUGH THE WORLD.", "그이의 명에 따라 저는 입을 열어, 온 세상을 헤쳐서라도 따르듯 그 초라한 보금자리로 그이를 따라갑니다."], ["O, my excellent and kind friend, intercede with my Rawdon's beloved aunt for him and the poor girl to whom all HIS NOBLE RACE have shown such UNPARALLELED AFFECTION.", "아, 더없이 갸륵하고 어진 벗이여, 그이와, 그이의 그 고귀한 가문 모두가 이토록 견줄 데 없는 애정을 베풀어 주신 이 가엾은 처자를 위하여, 부디 제 로던의 사랑하는 고모님께 잘 말씀드려 주세요."], ["Ask Miss Crawley to receive HER CHILDREN.", "부디 크롤리 양께서 자기 자식들을 거두어 주십사 청해 주세요."], ["I can say no more, but blessings, blessings on all in the dear house I leave, prays", "더는 드릴 말씀이 없습니다. 다만 제가 떠나는 그 정든 집의 모든 분께 축복이, 축복이 함께하기를 비는 사람,"]], [["Your affectionate and GRATEFUL Rebecca Crawley.", "당신을 아끼고 또 고마워하는 레베카 크롤리 올림."], ["Midnight.", "한밤중에."]], [["Just as Briggs had finished reading this affecting and interesting document, which reinstated her in her position as first confidante of Miss Crawley, Mrs.", "브리그스가 이 가슴 저미고 흥미로운 문서를 — 자신을 크롤리 양의 으뜸가는 심복 자리로 되돌려 놓은 그 문서를 — 막 다 읽고 났을 때, 퍼킨 부인이 방으로 들어왔다."], ["Firkin entered the room. \"Here's Mrs.", "\"방금 뷰트 크롤리 부인께서 햄프셔에서 우편마차로 도착하셨는데, 차를 좀 드시겠대요."], ["Bute Crawley just arrived by the mail from Hampshire, and wants some tea; will you come down and make breakfast, Miss?\"", "내려오셔서 아침상 좀 봐 주시겠어요, 아씨?\""]], [["And to the surprise of Firkin, clasping her dressing-gown around her, the wisp of hair floating dishevelled behind her, the little curl-papers still sticking in bunches round her forehead, Briggs sailed down to Mrs. Bute with the letter in her hand containing the wonderful news.", "그러자 퍼킨이 놀랍게도, 브리그스는 실내복을 몸에 단단히 여민 채, 흐트러진 머리카락 한 가닥을 뒤로 풀어 헤치고, 컬을 만 종이 뭉치들을 아직 이마 둘레에 다발다발 매단 그대로, 그 놀라운 소식이 담긴 편지를 손에 쥐고 미끄러지듯 뷰트 부인에게 내려갔다."]], [["\"Oh, Mrs. Firkin,\"", "\"아유, 퍼킨 부인.\""], ["gasped Betty, \"sech a business.", "베티가 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다."], ["Miss Sharp have a gone and run away with the Capting, and they're off to Gretney Green!\"", "\"이런 난리가 다 있어요. 샤프 아씨가 대위님이랑 달아나 버렸지 뭐예요, 둘이서 그레트나그린으로 줄행랑을 쳤대요!\""], ["We would devote a chapter to describe the emotions of Mrs.", "우리는 퍼킨 부인의 격정을 묘사하는 데 한 장을 통째로 바쳤을 것이다."], ["Firkin, did not the passions of her mistresses occupy our genteeler muse.", "다만 우리의 한층 점잖은 시신(詩神)이 그 여주인들의 격정에 매여 있지 않았더라면 말이다."]], [["When Mrs. Bute Crawley, numbed with midnight travelling, and warming herself at the newly crackling parlour fire, heard from Miss Briggs the intelligence of the clandestine marriage, she declared it was quite providential that she should have arrived at such a time to assist poor dear Miss Crawley in supporting the shock--that Rebecca was an artful little hussy of whom she had always had her suspicions; and that as for Rawdon Crawley, she never could account for his aunt's infatuation regarding him, and had long considered him a profligate, lost, and abandoned being.", "뷰트 크롤리 부인은 한밤중의 여행으로 몸이 곱은 채 새로 타닥타닥 타오르는 응접실 난롯불에 몸을 녹이다가, 브리그스 양에게서 그 은밀한 결혼 소식을 전해 듣자, 가엾고 사랑하는 크롤리 양이 그 충격을 견디도록 도우러 마침 이런 때에 자신이 당도한 것은 참으로 하늘의 뜻이라고 단언했다."], ["And this awful conduct, Mrs.", "그러면서 레베카란 진작부터 자신이 의심을 품어 온 교활한 계집이며, 로던 크롤리로 말하자면 그 고모가 그자한테 그토록 홀딱 빠진 까닭을 도무지 헤아릴 수 없었거니와 오래전부터 그자를 방탕하고 타락한 폐인으로 여겨 왔노라 했다."], ["Bute said, will have at least this good effect, it will open poor dear Miss Crawley's eyes to the real character of this wicked man. Then Mrs.", "그러고는 이 끔찍한 행실에도 적어도 한 가지 좋은 점은 있을 것이라고, 즉 가엾고 사랑하는 크롤리 양이 이 사악한 사내의 본색에 눈을 뜨게 될 것이라고 뷰트 부인은 말했다."], ["Bute had a comfortable hot toast and tea; and as there was a vacant room in the house now, there was no need for her to remain at the Gloster Coffee House where the Portsmouth mail had set her down, and whence she ordered Mr.", "그러고 나서 뷰트 부인은 흐뭇하게 따끈한 토스트와 차를 들었다. 그리고 이제 집 안에 빈방이 하나 생겼으니, 포츠머스 우편마차가 자신을 내려놓은 글로스터 커피하우스에 더 머물 필요가 없었다."], ["Bowls's aide-de-camp the footman to bring away her trunks.", "그래서 그녀는 보울스 씨의 부관 격인 하인을 시켜 그곳에서 자기 트렁크들을 가져오게 했다."]], [["Miss Crawley, be it known, did not leave her room until near noon--taking chocolate in bed in the morning, while Becky Sharp read the Morning Post to her, or otherwise amusing herself or dawdling.", "밝혀 두자면, 크롤리 양은 정오 무렵까지는 방에서 나오지 않았다. 아침이면 침대에서 초콜릿을 들었고, 그동안 베키 샤프가 그녀에게 모닝 포스트를 읽어 주었으며, 그러지 않을 때는 무언가로 소일하거나 빈둥거리며 시간을 보냈다."], ["The conspirators below agreed that they would spare the dear lady's feelings until she appeared in her drawing-room: meanwhile it was announced to her that Mrs.", "아래층의 모의자들은 그 다정한 부인이 응접실에 나타날 때까지는 그 마음을 다치게 하지 않기로 합의를 보았다."], ["Bute Crawley had come up from Hampshire by the mail, was staying at the Gloster, sent her love to Miss Crawley, and asked for breakfast with Miss Briggs. The arrival of Mrs.", "그동안 뷰트 크롤리 부인이 햄프셔에서 우편마차로 올라와 글로스터에 묵고 있으며, 크롤리 양에게 안부를 전하고 브리그스 양과 아침을 들겠다 청한다는 전갈이 그녀에게 전해졌다."], ["Bute, which would not have caused any extreme delight at another period, was hailed with pleasure now; Miss Crawley being pleased at the notion of a gossip with her sister-in-law regarding the late Lady Crawley, the funeral arrangements pending, and Sir Pitt's abrupt proposal to Rebecca.", "다른 때 같으면 그다지 큰 반가움을 자아내지 못했을 뷰트 부인의 도착이 이번에는 기쁘게 환영받았다. 크롤리 양으로서는 고인이 된 크롤리 부인이며, 미루어진 장례 준비며, 피트 경이 레베카에게 별안간 청혼한 일을 두고 그 올케와 한바탕 수다를 떨 생각에 흐뭇했던 것이다."]], [["It was not until the old lady was fairly ensconced in her usual arm-chair in the drawing-room, and the preliminary embraces and inquiries had taken place between the ladies, that the conspirators thought it advisable to submit her to the operation.", "노부인이 응접실의 늘 앉는 안락의자에 제대로 자리 잡고, 두 부인 사이에 첫 인사와 안부 묻기가 오간 뒤에야, 모의자들은 그녀에게 그 시술을 가하는 것이 좋겠다고 판단했다. 여자들이 벗들에게 나쁜 소식을 받아들일 \"채비를 시키는\""], ["Who has not admired the artifices and delicate approaches with which women \"prepare\"", "그 술책과 섬세한 접근을 누군들 감탄하지 않으랴?"], ["their friends for bad news? Miss Crawley's two friends made such an apparatus of mystery before they broke the intelligence to her, that they worked her up to the necessary degree of doubt and alarm.", "크롤리 양의 두 벗은 그 소식을 꺼내기에 앞서 어찌나 신비스러운 분위기를 잔뜩 차려 놓던지, 그녀를 필요한 만큼의 의심과 경악으로 몰아넣고야 말았다."]], [["\"And she refused Sir Pitt, my dear, dear Miss Crawley, prepare yourself for it,\" Mrs.", "\"그래서 그 애가 피트 경을 거절했지 뭐예요, 친애하는, 친애하는 크롤리 양, 마음 단단히 잡수세요.\""], ["Bute said, \"because--because she couldn't help herself.\"", "뷰트 부인이 말했다. \"왜냐하면 — 왜냐하면 그 애로서는 어쩔 도리가 없었던 거예요.\""]], [["\"Of course there was a reason,\"", "\"물론 무슨 까닭이 있었겠지.\""], ["Miss Crawley answered.", "크롤리 양이 대답했다."], ["\"She liked somebody else.", "\"다른 누군가를 좋아한 게야."], ["I told Briggs so yesterday.\"", "내가 어제 브리그스한테도 그렇게 말했잖아.\""]], [["\"LIKES somebody else!\"", "\"다른 누군가를 좋아하다뇨!\""], ["Briggs gasped.", "브리그스가 숨을 헐떡였다."], ["\"O my dear friend, she is married already.\"", "\"아, 친애하는 마님, 그 애는 벌써 결혼했답니다.\""]], [["\"Married already,\" Mrs.", "\"벌써 결혼했어요.\""], ["Bute chimed in; and both sate with clasped hands looking from each other at their victim.", "뷰트 부인이 맞장구를 쳤다. 그러고는 두 사람 다 두 손을 맞잡은 채 서로를 번갈아 보고 또 그 희생자를 바라보며 앉아 있었다."]], [["\"Send her to me, the instant she comes in.", "\"그 애가 들어오는 즉시 내게 보내라."], ["The little sly wretch: how dared she not tell me?\"", "그 교활한 계집 같으니, 어떻게 감히 내게 말도 안 했단 말이냐?\""], ["cried out Miss Crawley.", "크롤리 양이 소리쳤다."]], [["\"She won't come in soon.", "\"그 애는 곧 들어오지 않을 거예요."], ["Prepare yourself, dear friend--she's gone out for a long time--she's--she's gone altogether.\"", "마음 단단히 잡수세요, 친애하는 마님 — 그 애는 한동안 나가 버렸어요 — 그 애는, 그 애는 아예 떠나 버렸어요.\""]], [["\"Gracious goodness, and who's to make my chocolate?", "\"아이고 맙소사, 그럼 누가 내 초콜릿을 만든단 말이냐?"], ["Send for her and have her back; I desire that she come back,\"", "사람을 보내서 그 애를 도로 데려와라. 그 애가 돌아오기를 명한다.\""], ["the old lady said.", "노부인이 말했다."]], [["\"She decamped last night, Ma'am,\"", "\"그 애는 간밤에 야반도주를 했답니다, 마님.\""], ["cried Mrs. Bute.", "뷰트 부인이 외쳤다."]], [["\"She left a letter for me,\"", "\"그 애가 제게 편지를 남겼어요.\""], ["Briggs exclaimed.", "브리그스가 외쳤다."], ["\"She's married to--\"", "\"그 애는 결혼했어요, 상대는—\""]], [["\"Prepare her, for heaven's sake.", "\"제발 마님 마음 다잡게 해 드려요."], ["Don't torture her, my dear Miss Briggs.\"", "그렇게 마님을 괴롭히지 말아요, 친애하는 브리그스 양.\""]], [["\"She's married to whom?\"", "\"그 애가 누구랑 결혼했단 말이냐?\""], ["cries the spinster in a nervous fury.", "노처녀가 안절부절못하며 격노하여 외쳤다."]], [["\"To--to a relation of--\"", "\"그게 — 그게 어느 친척과—\""]], [["\"She refused Sir Pitt,\"", "\"그 애가 피트 경을 거절했다고.\""], ["cried the victim.", "희생자가 외쳤다."], ["\"Speak at once.", "\"당장 말해라."], ["Don't drive me mad.\"", "날 미치게 만들지 말고.\""]], [["\"O Ma'am--prepare her, Miss Briggs--she's married to Rawdon Crawley.\"", "\"아, 마님 — 마님 마음 단단히 잡수시게 해 드려요, 브리그스 양 — 그 애는 로던 크롤리와 결혼했어요.\""]], [["\"Rawdon married Rebecca--governess--nobod-- Get out of my house, you fool, you idiot--you stupid old Briggs--how dare you?", "\"로던이 레베카랑 결혼을 해 — 가정교사 따위 — 근본도 없는 — 내 집에서 썩 나가라, 이 멍청아, 이 얼간아 — 이 어리석은 늙은 브리그스 같으니 — 어떻게 감히?"], ["You're in the plot--you made him marry, thinking that I'd leave my money from him--you did, Martha,\"", "너도 한패로구나 — 내가 그 애한테서 재산을 떼어 놓을 줄 알고 네가 그 애를 부추겨 결혼시킨 게야 — 네가 그랬지, 마사.\""], ["the poor old lady screamed in hysteric sentences.", "가엾은 노부인이 발작하듯 토막 난 말로 비명을 질러 댔다."]], [["\"I, Ma'am, ask a member of this family to marry a drawing-master's daughter?\"", "\"제가요, 마님, 이 집안 사람더러 그림 선생 딸과 결혼하라고 부추겼다고요?\""]], [["\"Her mother was a Montmorency,\"", "\"그 애 어미는 몽모랑시 가문 사람이었어.\""], ["cried out the old lady, pulling at the bell with all her might.", "노부인이 온 힘을 다해 종을 잡아당기며 외쳤다."]], [["\"Her mother was an opera girl, and she has been on the stage or worse herself,\"", "\"그 애 어미는 오페라 무대의 계집이었고, 그 애 자신도 무대에 섰거나 아니면 그보다 더한 짓을 했답니다.\""], ["said Mrs. Bute.", "뷰트 부인이 말했다."]], [["Miss Crawley gave a final scream, and fell back in a faint.", "크롤리 양은 마지막으로 한 번 비명을 지르더니 까무러쳐 뒤로 넘어갔다."], ["They were forced to take her back to the room which she had just quitted.", "사람들은 어쩔 수 없이 그녀를 방금 나온 그 방으로 도로 데려가야 했다."], ["One fit of hysterics succeeded another.", "발작이 한 차례 또 한 차례 잇따랐다."], ["The doctor was sent for--the apothecary arrived.", "사람을 보내 의사를 불렀고, 약제사가 당도했다."], ["Mrs. Bute took up the post of nurse by her bedside.", "뷰트 부인은 그 머리맡에서 간병인 자리를 차지했다."], ["\"Her relations ought to be round about her,\"", "\"친척들이 곁에서 둘러앉아 있어야지.\""], ["that amiable woman said.", "그 어진 여인은 말했다."]], [["She had scarcely been carried up to her room, when a new person arrived to whom it was also necessary to break the news.", "그녀가 위층 방으로 옮겨지자마자, 또 한 사람이 당도했으니 그에게도 이 소식을 전해야 했다."], ["This was Sir Pitt.", "바로 피트 경이었다."], ["\"Where's Becky?\"", "\"베키는 어디 있느냐?\""], ["he said, coming in.", "그가 들어서며 말했다."], ["\"Where's her traps?", "\"그 애 짐은 어디 있고?"], ["She's coming with me to Queen's Crawley.\"", "그 애는 나랑 퀸스크롤리로 가기로 했단 말이다.\""]], [["\"Have you not heard the astonishing intelligence regarding her surreptitious union?\"", "\"그 애가 몰래 혼인한 그 놀라운 소식을 못 들으셨나요?\""], ["Briggs asked.", "브리그스가 물었다."]], [["\"What's that to me?\"", "\"그게 나랑 무슨 상관이냐?\""], ["Sir Pitt asked.", "피트 경이 물었다."], ["\"I know she's married.", "\"그 애가 결혼했다는 건 나도 안다."], ["That makes no odds.", "그건 아무 차이도 없어."], ["Tell her to come down at once, and not keep me.\"", "당장 내려오라고 일러라, 날 기다리게 하지 말고.\""]], [["\"Are you not aware, sir,\"", "\"나리, 모르셨나요.\""], ["Miss Briggs asked, \"that she has left our roof, to the dismay of Miss Crawley, who is nearly killed by the intelligence of Captain Rawdon's union with her?\"", "브리그스 양이 물었다. \"그 애가 이 집을 떠나 버려서 크롤리 양께서 망연자실하셨답니다, 로던 대위가 그 애와 혼인했다는 소식에 거의 돌아가실 지경이 되셨다고요?\""]], [["When Sir Pitt Crawley heard that Rebecca was married to his son, he broke out into a fury of language, which it would do no good to repeat in this place, as indeed it sent poor Briggs shuddering out of the room; and with her we will shut the door upon the figure of the frenzied old man, wild with hatred and insane with baffled desire.", "피트 크롤리 경은 레베카가 자기 아들과 결혼했다는 말을 듣자, 차마 이 자리에서 옮겨 봐야 아무 득 될 것 없는 험한 말을 한바탕 욕설로 쏟아 냈다. 실제로 그 욕설에 가엾은 브리그스는 부들부들 떨며 방에서 빠져나갔다. 그리하여 우리는 그녀와 함께, 증오로 광란하고 좌절된 욕망으로 실성한 그 미친 늙은이의 모습 위로 문을 닫아 두기로 하자."]], [["One day after he went to Queen's Crawley, he burst like a madman into the room she had used when there--dashed open her boxes with his foot, and flung about her papers, clothes, and other relics.", "그가 퀸스크롤리로 내려간 다음 날, 그는 미치광이처럼 그녀가 거기 있을 적에 쓰던 방으로 뛰어 들어가서는 — 발길질로 그녀의 상자들을 박살 내고 — 그녀의 서류며 옷가지며 그 밖의 유품들을 마구 내던졌다."], ["Miss Horrocks, the butler's daughter, took some of them.", "집사의 딸 호럭스 양이 그 가운데 몇 가지를 챙겼다."], ["The children dressed themselves and acted plays in the others.", "아이들은 나머지를 걸치고 연극 놀이를 했다."], ["It was but a few days after the poor mother had gone to her lonely burying-place; and was laid, unwept and disregarded, in a vault full of strangers.", "그것은 가엾은 어머니가 외로운 무덤으로 떠난 지 겨우 며칠 뒤의 일이었다. 그녀는 아무도 울어 주지 않고 거들떠보지도 않는 가운데, 낯선 이들로 가득한 납골당에 안치되었던 것이다."]], [["\"Suppose the old lady doesn't come to,\"", "\"그 늙은 부인이 마음을 안 돌리면 어쩐다.\""], ["Rawdon said to his little wife, as they sate together in the snug little Brompton lodgings.", "로던이 아늑하고 자그마한 브롬프턴 셋방에 자기 어린 아내와 함께 앉아서 말했다."], ["She had been trying the new piano all the morning.", "그녀는 아침 내내 새 피아노를 만지작거려 보던 참이었다."], ["The new gloves fitted her to a nicety; the new shawls became her wonderfully; the new rings glittered on her little hands, and the new watch ticked at her waist; \"suppose she don't come round, eh, Becky?\"", "새 장갑은 그녀에게 딱 맞아떨어졌고, 새 숄은 놀랍도록 잘 어울렸으며, 새 반지들은 그 자그마한 손에서 반짝였고, 새 시계는 허리춤에서 똑딱거렸다. \"마음을 안 돌리면, 응, 베키?\""]], [["\"I'LL make your fortune,\"", "\"내가 당신 한밑천 잡게 해 줄게요.\""], ["she said; and Delilah patted Samson's cheek.", "그녀가 말했다. 그러고는 데릴라가 삼손의 뺨을 토닥였다."]], [["\"You can do anything,\"", "\"당신이라면 무엇이든 할 수 있어.\""], ["he said, kissing the little hand.", "그가 그 자그마한 손에 입 맞추며 말했다."], ["\"By Jove you can; and we'll drive down to the Star and Garter, and dine, by Jove.\"", "\"정말이지 당신은 할 수 있어. 그러니 우리 스타 앤드 가터로 마차나 몰고 가서, 한 끼 거하게 먹자고, 정말로.\""]], [["How Captain Dobbin Bought a Piano\nIf there is any exhibition in all Vanity Fair which Satire and Sentiment can visit arm in arm together; where you light on the strangest contrasts laughable and tearful: where you may be gentle and pathetic, or savage and cynical with perfect propriety: it is at one of those public assemblies, a crowd of which are advertised every day in the last page of the Times newspaper, and over which the late Mr.", "이 허영의 시장 전체에서, 풍자와 감상이 서로 팔짱을 끼고 함께 거닐 수 있는 구경거리가 있다면, 우습기도 하고 눈물겹기도 한 더없이 기묘한 대조와 마주치는 곳, 점잖고 애처롭게 굴어도 좋고 사납고 냉소적으로 굴어도 더없이 어울리는 곳이 있다면, 그것은 바로 저 공개 경매장들 가운데 하나일 것이다."], ["George Robins used to preside with so much dignity.", "그런 경매는 날마다 무더기로 「타임스」"], ["There are very few London people, as I fancy, who have not attended at these meetings, and all with a taste for moralizing must have thought, with a sensation and interest not a little startling and queer, of the day when their turn shall come too, and Mr.", "신문 마지막 면에 광고가 실리며, 고인이 된 조지 로빈스 씨가 그토록 위엄 있게 진행을 맡곤 하던 바로 그것이다. 내 짐작으로는, 이런 모임에 한 번도 참석해 보지 않은 런던 사람은 극히 드물 것이며, 무릇 도덕적 사색을 즐기는 이라면 누구나 적잖이 놀랍고도 야릇한 어떤 느낌과 흥미를 품은 채, 언젠가 자기 차례도 돌아오리라는 것을 떠올려 보았음이 틀림없다."], ["Hammerdown will sell by the orders of Diogenes' assignees, or will be instructed by the executors, to offer to public competition, the library, furniture, plate, wardrobe, and choice cellar of wines of Epicurus deceased.", "그날이 오면 해머다운 씨가 디오게네스의 파산 관재인의 명을 받들어, 혹은 유언 집행인의 위촉을 받아, 고인이 된 에피쿠로스의 장서며 가구며 식기며 의복이며, 그리고 엄선된 포도주 저장고를 공개 경쟁에 부쳐 처분할 것이다."]], [["Even with the most selfish disposition, the Vanity Fairian, as he witnesses this sordid part of the obsequies of a departed friend, can't but feel some sympathies and regret.", "아무리 이기적인 천성을 지닌 허영의 시장 사람이라 해도, 떠나간 벗의 장례식 가운데 이토록 추레한 대목을 목도하노라면 얼마간의 연민과 회한을 느끼지 않을 수 없는 법이다."], ["My Lord Dives's remains are in the family vault: the statuaries are cutting an inscription veraciously commemorating his virtues, and the sorrows of his heir, who is disposing of his goods.", "다이브스 경의 유해는 가족 납골당에 안치되어 있다. 조각가들은 그의 미덕과, 그의 유산을 처분하고 있는 상속인의 슬픔을 진실되게 기리는 비문을 새기고 있는 중이다."], ["What guest at Dives's table can pass the familiar house without a sigh?--the familiar house of which the lights used to shine so cheerfully at seven o'clock, of which the hall-doors opened so readily, of which the obsequious servants, as you passed up the comfortable stair, sounded your name from landing to landing, until it reached the apartment where jolly old Dives welcomed his friends!", "다이브스의 식탁에 둘러앉았던 손님이라면 그 누구인들 이 낯익은 집을 한숨 한 번 없이 지나칠 수 있겠는가. 일곱 시면 불빛이 그토록 흥겹게 빛나던 낯익은 집, 현관문이 그토록 기꺼이 열리던 집, 그 안락한 계단을 올라가노라면 굽실거리는 하인들이 층계참마다 당신의 이름을 외쳐 전하여 마침내 명랑한 노신사 다이브스가 벗들을 맞이하던 그 방에 이르게 하던 집을 말이다!"], ["What a number of them he had; and what a noble way of entertaining them.", "그에게는 또 얼마나 많은 벗이 있었으며, 그들을 대접하는 솜씨는 또 얼마나 고상했던가."], ["How witty people used to be here who were morose when they got out of the door; and how courteous and friendly men who slandered and hated each other everywhere else!", "문밖에 나서면 시무룩하던 사람들이 이곳에서는 또 얼마나 재치가 넘쳤으며, 다른 데서는 서로 헐뜯고 미워하던 사내들이 여기서는 또 얼마나 정중하고 다정했던가! 그는 거드름을 피우는 사람이었으나, 그런 요리사를 둔 마당에 무엇인들 못 삼키겠는가."], ["He was pompous, but with such a cook what would one not swallow?", "어쩌면 좀 따분한 사람이었는지도 모르나, 그런 포도주라면 무슨 대화인들 즐겁게 만들지 않겠는가."], ["he was rather dull, perhaps, but would not such wine make any conversation pleasant?", "\"값이 얼마가 들든 그의 부르고뉴 포도주는 꼭 좀 손에 넣어야겠어.\" 조문객들이 클럽에서 부르짖는다."], ["We must get some of his Burgundy at any price, the mourners cry at his club.", "\"이 담뱃갑은 다이브스 영감 경매에서 산 거라네.\" 핀처가 그것을 돌려 보이며 말한다."], ["\"I got this box at old Dives's sale,\"", "\"루이 십오세의 정부 가운데 하나라지."], ["Pincher says, handing it round, \"one of Louis XV's mistresses--pretty thing, is it not?--sweet miniature,\"", "예쁘지 않은가? 아주 고운 세밀화일세.\""], ["and they talk of the way in which young Dives is dissipating his fortune.", "그러고는 젊은 다이브스가 어떤 식으로 재산을 탕진하고 있는지 이야기꽃을 피운다."]], [["How changed the house is, though!", "그런데 그 집은 또 얼마나 변해 버렸는가!"], ["The front is patched over with bills, setting forth the particulars of the furniture in staring capitals.", "정면은 가구의 명세를 큼직한 대문자로 떡하니 박아 넣은 광고지들로 온통 도배되어 있다."], ["They have hung a shred of carpet out of an upstairs window--a half dozen of porters are lounging on the dirty steps--the hall swarms with dingy guests of oriental countenance, who thrust printed cards into your hand, and offer to bid.", "위층 창밖으로는 양탄자 한 조각이 늘어져 나와 있고, 더러운 계단에는 짐꾼 대여섯이 빈둥거리고 있으며, 현관에는 동방인 같은 얼굴빛을 한 꾀죄죄한 손님들이 우글거리며 당신 손에 인쇄된 명함을 떠안기고는 응찰을 청한다."], ["Old women and amateurs have invaded the upper apartments, pinching the bed-curtains, poking into the feathers, shampooing the mattresses, and clapping the wardrobe drawers to and fro.", "위층 방들은 노파들과 호사가들이 점령하여, 침대 휘장을 만지작거리고, 깃털 이불을 쿡쿡 찔러 보고, 매트리스를 주물러 보고, 옷장 서랍을 이리저리 여닫는다."], ["Enterprising young housekeepers are measuring the looking-glasses and hangings to see if they will suit the new menage (Snob will brag for years that he has purchased this or that at Dives's sale), and Mr.", "진취적인 젊은 살림꾼들은 새 살림에 맞을지 알아보려고 거울이며 휘장이며 치수를 재고 있다(속물들은 이것저것을 다이브스 경 경매에서 샀노라고 몇 해를 두고 자랑할 것이다)."], ["Hammerdown is sitting on the great mahogany dining-tables, in the dining-room below, waving the ivory hammer, and employing all the artifices of eloquence, enthusiasm, entreaty, reason, despair; shouting to his people; satirizing Mr.", "그리고 아래층 식당에서는 해머다운 씨가 커다란 마호가니 식탁 위에 걸터앉아 상아 망치를 흔들어 대며, 웅변과 열정과 간청과 논리와 절망의 온갖 술수를 부린다."], ["Davids for his sluggishness; inspiriting Mr. Moss into action; imploring, commanding, bellowing, until down comes the hammer like fate, and we pass to the next lot.", "부하들에게 소리치고, 데이비즈 씨의 굼뜬 동작을 비꼬고, 모스 씨를 부추겨 움직이게 하고, 애원하고 명령하고 고함치다가, 마침내 운명처럼 망치가 떨어지면 우리는 다음 물건으로 넘어가는 것이다."], ["O Dives, who would ever have thought, as we sat round the broad table sparkling with plate and spotless linen, to have seen such a dish at the head of it as that roaring auctioneer?", "오 다이브스여, 식기와 티 한 점 없는 식탁보로 반짝이던 그 너른 식탁에 둘러앉았을 적에, 그 상석에 저 고래고래 소리치는 경매인 같은 요리가 놓이게 되리라고 그 누가 생각이나 했겠는가?"]], [["It was rather late in the sale.", "경매도 제법 막바지에 이른 무렵이었다."], ["The excellent drawing-room furniture by the best makers; the rare and famous wines selected, regardless of cost, and with the well-known taste of the purchaser; the rich and complete set of family plate had been sold on the previous days.", "일류 장인들이 만든 빼어난 응접실 가구며, 비용을 따지지 않고 구매자의 정평 있는 안목으로 엄선해 둔 진귀하고 이름난 포도주며, 호화롭고 빠짐없이 갖춰진 가족용 은식기 한 벌은 지난 며칠 동안 이미 다 팔린 뒤였다."], ["Certain of the best wines (which all had a great character among amateurs in the neighbourhood) had been purchased for his master, who knew them very well, by the butler of our friend John Osborne, Esquire, of Russell Square.", "그 가운데 으뜸가는 포도주 몇 가지(이웃의 호사가들 사이에서 두루 명성이 자자한 것들이었다)는 러셀 광장에 사는 우리의 벗 존 오스본 귀하의 집사가, 그것들을 훤히 꿰고 있는 자기 주인을 위해 사들였다."], ["A small portion of the most useful articles of the plate had been bought by some young stockbrokers from the City.", "가장 요긴한 은식기들 가운데 일부는 시티에서 온 어느 젊은 주식 중개인들이 사 갔다."], ["And now the public being invited to the purchase of minor objects, it happened that the orator on the table was expatiating on the merits of a picture, which he sought to recommend to his audience: it was by no means so select or numerous a company as had attended the previous days of the auction.", "그리고 이제 자질구레한 물건들을 사라고 대중에게 권하는 차례가 되어, 마침 식탁 위의 그 웅변가는 청중에게 추천하려는 어느 그림 한 점의 장점을 늘어놓고 있었다. 경매가 진행되던 지난 며칠에 비하면, 오늘 모인 무리는 그리 고상하지도 많지도 않았다."]], [["\"No. 369,\"", "\"삼백육십구 번이오.\""], ["roared Mr. Hammerdown.", "해머다운 씨가 고함쳤다."], ["\"Portrait of a gentleman on an elephant.", "\"코끼리에 탄 신사의 초상화올시다."], ["Who'll bid for the gentleman on the elephant?", "코끼리에 탄 신사에 응찰하실 분 안 계시오?"], ["Lift up the picture, Blowman, and let the company examine this lot.\"", "블로먼, 그림을 들어 올려서 여러분이 이 물건을 살펴보시게 하게.\""], ["A long, pale, military-looking gentleman, seated demurely at the mahogany table, could not help grinning as this valuable lot was shown by Mr.", "마호가니 식탁에 점잖게 앉아 있던, 키가 크고 핼쑥하며 군인 같은 풍채의 한 신사는, 블로먼 씨가 이 진귀한 물건을 들어 보이자 씩 웃지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Blowman. \"Turn the elephant to the Captain, Blowman.", "\"블로먼, 그 코끼리를 대위 양반 쪽으로 돌려 보이게."], ["What shall we say, sir, for the elephant?\"", "자, 신사 양반, 이 코끼리에 얼마를 부르시겠소?\""], ["but the Captain, blushing in a very hurried and discomfited manner, turned away his head.", "그러나 대위는 몹시 황급하고 당황한 기색으로 얼굴을 붉히며 고개를 돌려 버렸다."]], [["\"Shall we say twenty guineas for this work of art?--fifteen, five, name your own price.", "\"이 예술 작품에 이십 기니를 부를까요? 십오 기니, 오 기니, 부르고 싶은 값을 말씀하시오."], ["The gentleman without the elephant is worth five pound.\"", "코끼리를 빼고 신사만 쳐도 오 파운드는 나갈 물건이오.\""]], [["\"I wonder it ain't come down with him,\"", "\"신사가 그 위에서 안 굴러떨어진 게 신기할 노릇이오.\" 직업적인 익살꾼 하나가 말했다."], ["said a professional wag, \"he's anyhow a precious big one\"; at which (for the elephant-rider was represented as of a very stout figure) there was a general giggle in the room.", "\"어쨌든 무지하게 큰 분이시니 말이지.\" 이 말에(코끼리에 탄 사람이 자못 뚱뚱한 풍채로 그려져 있던 터라) 장내에 한바탕 키득거림이 일었다."]], [["\"Don't be trying to deprecate the value of the lot, Mr.", "\"이 물건의 값어치를 깎아내리려 들지 마시오, 모스 씨.\""], ["Moss,\" Mr.", "해머다운 씨가 말했다."], ["Hammerdown said; \"let the company examine it as a work of art--the attitude of the gallant animal quite according to natur'; the gentleman in a nankeen jacket, his gun in his hand, is going to the chase; in the distance a banyhann tree and a pagody, most likely resemblances of some interesting spot in our famous Eastern possessions.", "\"여러분, 예술 작품으로서 이것을 살펴보시오. 이 늠름한 짐승의 자세는 그야말로 자연 그대로올시다."], ["How much for this lot?", "면직 재킷을 입고 총을 손에 쥔 신사는 사냥을 나가는 길이지요. 멀리 보이는 저 반얀나무며 탑은 십중팔구 우리의 저 이름난 동방 영토 어느 흥미로운 명소를 본뜬 것이올시다."], ["Come, gentlemen, don't keep me here all day.\"", "이 물건에 얼마를 부르시겠소? 자, 신사 여러분, 나를 여기 온종일 붙들어 두지 마시고.\""]], [["Some one bid five shillings, at which the military gentleman looked towards the quarter from which this splendid offer had come, and there saw another officer with a young lady on his arm, who both appeared to be highly amused with the scene, and to whom, finally, this lot was knocked down for half a guinea.", "누군가 오 실링을 불렀다. 그러자 군인 신사는 이 훌륭한 제안이 날아온 쪽으로 시선을 돌렸고, 거기서 젊은 숙녀 한 사람을 팔에 낀 또 다른 장교를 보았다."], ["He at the table looked more surprised and discomposed than ever when he spied this pair, and his head sank into his military collar, and he turned his back upon them, so as to avoid them altogether.", "두 사람 다 이 광경을 자못 재미있어하는 듯했고, 결국 이 물건은 반 기니에 그들에게 낙찰되었다. 식탁에 앉아 있던 그 사내는 이 한 쌍을 알아보고 어느 때보다 놀라고 당황한 표정이 되더니, 고개를 군복 옷깃 속으로 푹 파묻고는 그들을 아예 외면하려는 듯 등을 돌렸다."]], [["Of all the other articles which Mr.", "그날 해머다운 씨가 공개 경쟁에 부치는 영광을 누린 다른 온갖 물건들에 대해서는 일일이 언급할 생각이 없으나, 딱 하나만은 예외로 하자."], ["Hammerdown had the honour to offer for public competition that day it is not our purpose to make mention, save of one only, a little square piano, which came down from the upper regions of the house (the state grand piano having been disposed of previously); this the young lady tried with a rapid and skilful hand (making the officer blush and start again), and for it, when its turn came, her agent began to bid.", "그것은 작은 사각형 피아노로, 집 위층에서 내려온 것이었다(예식용 그랜드피아노는 진작에 처분된 뒤였다). 젊은 숙녀는 빠르고 능숙한 손놀림으로 그것을 한번 쳐 보았고(그 바람에 장교는 다시 얼굴을 붉히고 흠칫했다), 그 차례가 돌아오자 그녀의 대리인이 응찰을 시작했다."]], [["But there was an opposition here.", "그런데 여기서 경쟁이 붙었다."], ["The Hebrew aide-de-camp in the service of the officer at the table bid against the Hebrew gentleman employed by the elephant purchasers, and a brisk battle ensued over this little piano, the combatants being greatly encouraged by Mr.", "식탁에 앉은 장교를 모시는 히브리인 부관이, 코끼리 그림을 산 이들이 고용한 히브리인 신사를 상대로 응찰에 나섰고, 이 작은 피아노를 두고 한바탕 치열한 싸움이 벌어졌다."], ["Hammerdown.", "경쟁자들은 해머다운 씨의 부추김에 한껏 기세가 올랐다."]], [["At last, when the competition had been prolonged for some time, the elephant captain and lady desisted from the race; and the hammer coming down, the auctioneer said:--\"Mr.", "마침내 경쟁이 한동안 길어진 끝에, 코끼리 대위와 숙녀는 다툼에서 물러났다. 그리고 망치가 떨어지자 경매인이 말했다."], ["Lewis, twenty-five,\" and Mr.", "\"루이스 씨, 이십오 기니.\""], ["Lewis's chief thus became the proprietor of the little square piano.", "이리하여 루이스 씨의 주인이 이 작은 사각형 피아노의 임자가 되었다."], ["Having effected the purchase, he sate up as if he was greatly relieved, and the unsuccessful competitors catching a glimpse of him at this moment, the lady said to her friend,", "그 사내는 구매를 마치자 크게 안도한 듯 몸을 곧추세웠고, 입찰에 실패한 경쟁자들이 마침 그 순간 그를 얼핏 알아보았다. 숙녀가 자기 벗에게 말했다."]], [["\"Why, Rawdon, it's Captain Dobbin.\"", "\"어머나, 로던, 저분 도빈 대위잖아요.\""]], [["I suppose Becky was discontented with the new piano her husband had hired for her, or perhaps the proprietors of that instrument had fetched it away, declining farther credit, or perhaps she had a particular attachment for the one which she had just tried to purchase, recollecting it in old days, when she used to play upon it, in the little sitting-room of our dear Amelia Sedley.", "내 짐작으로는, 베키가 남편이 빌려다 준 새 피아노에 만족하지 못했거나, 아니면 그 악기 주인들이 더는 외상을 주기 싫다며 그것을 도로 가져가 버렸거나, 그도 아니면 그녀가 방금 사려고 했던 그 피아노에 각별한 애착을 품고 있었던 것이리라. 옛날에 우리의 사랑스러운 애밀리아 세들리의 그 자그마한 거실에서 그것을 치곤 하던 시절을 떠올리면서 말이다."]], [["The sale was at the old house in Russell Square, where we passed some evenings together at the beginning of this story.", "경매는 러셀 광장의 그 오래된 집에서 열리고 있었는데, 이 이야기의 첫머리에서 우리가 함께 몇 차례 저녁나절을 보냈던 바로 그 집이다."], ["Good old John Sedley was a ruined man.", "선량한 노인 존 세들리는 영락한 사람이 되어 있었다."], ["His name had been proclaimed as a defaulter on the Stock Exchange, and his bankruptcy and commercial extermination had followed. Mr.", "그의 이름이 증권 거래소에서 채무 불이행자로 공표되었고, 곧이어 파산과 상업적 파멸이 뒤따랐다."], ["Osborne's butler came to buy some of the famous port wine to transfer to the cellars over the way.", "오스본 씨의 집사는 그 이름난 포트와인을 사다가 길 건너 저장고로 옮기려고 찾아왔다."], ["As for one dozen well-manufactured silver spoons and forks at per oz., and one dozen dessert ditto ditto, there were three young stockbrokers (Messrs. Dale, Spiggot, and Dale, of Threadneedle Street, indeed), who, having had dealings with the old man, and kindnesses from him in days when he was kind to everybody with whom he dealt, sent this little spar out of the wreck with their love to good Mrs.", "온스 단위로 정교하게 만들어진 은수저와 은포크 열두 벌, 그리고 후식용 작은 것들 또한 열두 벌로 말하면, 세 사람의 젊은 주식 중개인이 있었는데(실은 스레드니들 거리의 데일·스피곳·데일 상회였다), 그들은 이 노인과 거래해 온 사이로, 그가 거래하는 모든 이에게 친절을 베풀던 시절에 그의 호의를 입은 적이 있었다."], ["Sedley; and with respect to the piano, as it had been Amelia's, and as she might miss it and want one now, and as Captain William Dobbin could no more play upon it than he could dance on the tight rope, it is probable that he did not purchase the instrument for his own use.", "그래서 이들은 난파선에서 건져 낸 이 자그마한 널빤지 한 조각을, 선량한 세들리 부인에게 자기들의 애정을 담아 보냈다. 그리고 피아노로 말하자면, 그것이 애밀리아의 것이었던 데다, 그녀가 그것을 그리워하며 이제 한 대 필요로 할지도 모르는 데다, 윌리엄 도빈 대위로 말하면 줄타기를 못하는 것만큼이나 피아노를 칠 줄 모르는 사람이었으니, 그가 자기 쓰려고 그 악기를 산 것은 아닐 공산이 크다."]], [["In a word, it arrived that evening at a wonderful small cottage in a street leading from the Fulham Road--one of those streets which have the finest romantic names--(this was called St.", "한마디로, 그것은 그날 저녁 풀럼 거리에서 갈라져 나온 어느 길에 자리한, 놀랄 만큼 자그마한 집에 도착했다."], ["Adelaide Villas, Anna-Maria Road West), where the houses look like baby-houses; where the people, looking out of the first-floor windows, must infallibly, as you think, sit with their feet in the parlours; where the shrubs in the little gardens in front bloom with a perennial display of little children's pinafores, little red socks, caps, &c. (polyandria polygynia); whence you hear the sound of jingling spinets and women singing; where little porter pots hang on the railings sunning themselves; whither of evenings you see City clerks padding wearily: here it was that Mr.", "그 길은 더없이 낭만적인 이름을 단 거리들 가운데 하나였는데(이름이 성 아델라이드 빌라, 애나마리아 로드 웨스트였다), 집들이 죄다 인형의 집처럼 생긴 곳이요, 이층 창밖을 내다보는 사람들은 당신이 보기에 영락없이 두 발을 응접실에 둔 채 앉아 있을 수밖에 없겠다 싶은 곳이며, 앞쪽 작은 정원의 관목들에는 어린아이들의 턱받이며, 작은 빨간 양말이며, 모자 따위(다우男 다우女 한해살이)가 사철 내내 피어나는 곳이요, 어디선가 딸랑거리는 스피넷 소리와 여자들의 노랫소리가 들려오는 곳이며, 작은 흑맥주 단지들이 난간에 걸려 볕을 쬐는 곳이요, 저녁이면 시티의 사무원들이 지친 걸음으로 터덜터덜 돌아오는 모습이 보이는 곳이었다."], ["Clapp, the clerk of Mr. Sedley, had his domicile, and in this asylum the good old gentleman hid his head with his wife and daughter when the crash came.", "바로 이곳에 세들리 씨의 사무원 클랩 씨가 거처를 두고 있었고, 파국이 닥치자 선량한 노신사는 이 피난처에 아내와 딸과 함께 몸을 숨겼던 것이다."]], [["Jos Sedley had acted as a man of his disposition would, when the announcement of the family misfortune reached him.", "조스 세들리는 집안의 불행을 알리는 소식이 당도하자, 자기 같은 천성을 지닌 사람이 으레 할 법한 대로 처신했다."], ["He did not come to London, but he wrote to his mother to draw upon his agents for whatever money was wanted, so that his kind broken-spirited old parents had no present poverty to fear.", "그는 런던에 오지는 않았으나, 어머니에게 편지를 써서 필요한 돈은 얼마든 자기 대리인에게서 받아 쓰라고 했고, 그리하여 마음씨 곱고 풀 죽은 노부모는 당장의 가난을 두려워할 일은 없게 되었다."], ["This done, Jos went on at the boarding-house at Cheltenham pretty much as before.", "이렇게 해 놓고 조스는 첼트넘의 하숙집에서 거의 예전과 다름없이 지냈다."], ["He drove his curricle; he drank his claret; he played his rubber; he told his Indian stories, and the Irish widow consoled and flattered him as usual.", "이륜마차를 몰고, 클라레를 마시고, 카드놀이를 하고, 인도 시절 이야기를 늘어놓았으며, 아일랜드 출신 과부는 여느 때처럼 그를 위로하고 추어올렸다."], ["His present of money, needful as it was, made little impression on his parents; and I have heard Amelia say that the first day on which she saw her father lift up his head after the failure was on the receipt of the packet of forks and spoons with the young stockbrokers'", "그가 보낸 돈은 절실하기는 했으나 부모에게 별다른 감흥을 주지 못했다. 애밀리아의 말로는, 파산 이후 아버지가 처음으로 고개를 든 날은 바로 그 젊은 주식 중개인들의 애정이 담긴 포크와 수저 꾸러미를 받은 날이었다고 한다."], ["love, over which he burst out crying like a child, being greatly more affected than even his wife, to whom the present was addressed.", "아버지는 그것을 받고 어린아이처럼 울음을 터뜨렸으니, 정작 그 선물을 받은 당사자인 아내보다도 훨씬 더 깊이 감동했던 것이다."], ["Edward Dale, the junior of the house, who purchased the spoons for the firm, was, in fact, very sweet upon Amelia, and offered for her in spite of all.", "상회를 대신해 그 수저를 사들인, 상회의 막내 에드워드 데일은 사실 애밀리아에게 자못 마음을 두고 있어서, 그 모든 사정에도 불구하고 그녀에게 청혼했다."], ["He married Miss Louisa Cutts (daughter of Higham and Cutts, the eminent cornfactors) with a handsome fortune in 1820; and is now living in splendour, and with a numerous family, at his elegant villa, Muswell Hill.", "그는 1820년에 상당한 재산을 지닌 루이자 커츠 양(이름난 곡물상 하이엄·커츠 상회의 딸)과 결혼했고, 지금은 머스웰 힐의 우아한 별장에서 식구를 줄줄이 거느린 채 호화롭게 살고 있다."], ["But we must not let the recollections of this good fellow cause us to diverge from the principal history.", "그러나 이 선량한 친구에 대한 추억 때문에 본 줄거리에서 벗어나서는 안 되겠다."]], [["I hope the reader has much too good an opinion of Captain and Mrs. Crawley to suppose that they ever would have dreamed of paying a visit to so remote a district as Bloomsbury, if they thought the family whom they proposed to honour with a visit were not merely out of fashion, but out of money, and could be serviceable to them in no possible manner.", "독자가 크롤리 대위 부부를 너무도 좋게 보아 준 나머지, 그들이 블룸즈베리처럼 외진 동네에까지 방문할 꿈을 꾸었으리라 여기지는 않으리라 믿는다."], ["Rebecca was entirely surprised at the sight of the comfortable old house where she had met with no small kindness, ransacked by brokers and bargainers, and its quiet family treasures given up to public desecration and plunder.", "방문하는 영예를 베풀려는 그 집안이 단지 유행에 뒤떨어졌을 뿐 아니라 돈마저 없어서 그들에게 어떤 식으로도 쓸모가 없으리라고 생각했다면 말이다. 레베카는 적잖은 친절을 입었던 그 안락한 옛집이 중개인들과 흥정꾼들에게 샅샅이 뒤집어지고, 그 조용한 집안의 보물들이 공공연한 모독과 약탈에 내맡겨진 광경을 보고 그야말로 깜짝 놀랐다."], ["A month after her flight, she had bethought her of Amelia, and Rawdon, with a horse-laugh, had expressed a perfect willingness to see young George Osborne again.", "도망친 지 한 달이 지나서야 그녀는 애밀리아를 떠올렸고, 로던은 껄껄 웃으며 다시 젊은 조지 오스본을 만나고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같다고 했다."], ["\"He's a very agreeable acquaintance, Beck,\"", "\"그 친구 정말 마음에 드는 지인이지, 벡.\""], ["the wag added.", "익살꾼이 덧붙였다."], ["\"I'd like to sell him another horse, Beck.", "\"그 친구한테 말을 한 마리 더 팔고 싶단 말이야, 벡."], ["I'd like to play a few more games at billiards with him.", "그 친구랑 당구나 몇 판 더 치고 싶고."], ["He'd be what I call useful just now, Mrs. C.--ha, ha!\"", "지금 같은 때 그 친구야말로 이를테면 요긴한 사람이지, 부인."], ["by which sort of speech it is not to be supposed that Rawdon Crawley had a deliberate desire to cheat Mr.", "하하!\" 그러나 이런 식의 말이라 해서 로던 크롤리가 노름판에서 오스본 씨를 작정하고 속여먹을 마음을 품었다고 여겨서는 안 된다."], ["Osborne at play, but only wished to take that fair advantage of him which almost every sporting gentleman in Vanity Fair considers to be his due from his neighbour.", "다만 허영의 시장의 거의 모든 노름 좋아하는 신사가 자기 이웃에게서 마땅히 누려야 할 몫이라고 여기는, 그 정도의 유리한 이득을 챙기고 싶었을 따름이다."]], [["The old aunt was long in \"coming-to.\"", "노숙모는 좀처럼 \"마음을 풀 줄\" 몰랐다."], ["A month had elapsed.", "한 달이 흘렀다."], ["Rawdon was denied the door by Mr. Bowls; his servants could not get a lodgment in the house at Park Lane; his letters were sent back unopened.", "로던은 보울스 씨에게 문전박대당했고, 그의 하인들은 파크 레인의 집에 발붙일 데가 없었으며, 그의 편지는 뜯기지도 않은 채 되돌아왔다."], ["Miss Crawley never stirred out--she was unwell--and Mrs.", "크롤리 양은 한 번도 바깥출입을 하지 않았으니, 몸이 편치 않았던 것이다."], ["Bute remained still and never left her.", "그리고 뷰트 부인은 줄곧 자리를 지키며 곁을 떠나지 않았다."], ["Crawley and his wife both of them augured evil from the continued presence of Mrs. Bute.", "크롤리와 그의 아내는 둘 다 뷰트 부인의 이 끈질긴 체류에서 불길한 조짐을 점쳤다."]], [["\"Gad, I begin to perceive now why she was always bringing us together at Queen's Crawley,\"", "\"이런, 이제야 그 여자가 왜 줄곧 퀸스 크롤리에서 우리 둘을 붙여 놓으려 했는지 알 것 같군.\""], ["Rawdon said.", "로던이 말했다."]], [["\"What an artful little woman!\"", "\"어쩌면 그렇게 교활한 여자가 다 있을까!\""], ["ejaculated Rebecca.", "레베카가 내뱉었다."]], [["\"Well, I don't regret it, if you don't,\"", "\"흠, 당신이 후회 안 한다면 나도 후회 안 해.\" 대위가 외쳤다."], ["the Captain cried, still in an amorous rapture with his wife, who rewarded him with a kiss by way of reply, and was indeed not a little gratified by the generous confidence of her husband.", "그는 여전히 아내에게 푹 빠진 사랑의 황홀경에 젖어 있었고, 아내는 답례로 입맞춤을 선사했다. 그녀는 사실 남편의 그 너그러운 신뢰에 적잖이 흐뭇해하고 있었던 것이다."]], [["\"If he had but a little more brains,\"", "\"이이가 머리만 조금 더 좋았더라면,\" 그녀는 속으로 생각했다."], ["she thought to herself, \"I might make something of him\"; but she never let him perceive the opinion she had of him; listened with indefatigable complacency to his stories of the stable and the mess; laughed at all his jokes; felt the greatest interest in Jack Spatterdash, whose cab-horse had come down, and Bob Martingale, who had been taken up in a gambling-house, and Tom Cinqbars, who was going to ride the steeplechase.", "\"뭔가 큰일을 시켜 볼 수도 있을 텐데.\" 그러나 그녀는 자기가 남편을 어떻게 여기는지 결코 내색하지 않았다."], ["When he came home she was alert and happy: when he went out she pressed him to go: when he stayed at home, she played and sang for him, made him good drinks, superintended his dinner, warmed his slippers, and steeped his soul in comfort.", "마구간과 장교 식당 이야기를 지칠 줄 모르는 흐뭇한 표정으로 들어 주었고, 그의 농담에는 죄다 웃어 주었으며, 마차용 말이 쓰러진 잭 스패터대시며, 도박장에서 붙들려 간 밥 마틴게일이며, 장애물 경마에 나갈 톰 생크바스에게 더없이 큰 관심을 보였다. 그가 집에 돌아오면 그녀는 생기 있고 행복해했으며, 그가 나갈 때면 어서 다녀오라고 떠밀었고, 그가 집에 머물면 그를 위해 피아노를 치고 노래를 불렀으며, 맛있는 음료를 만들어 주고, 저녁 식사를 챙기고, 슬리퍼를 데워 주고, 그의 영혼을 안락 속에 푹 잠기게 했다."], ["The best of women (I have heard my grandmother say) are hypocrites.", "여자 중에서도 으뜸가는 여자가(우리 할머니가 하시던 말씀이다) 위선자라는 것이다."], ["We don't know how much they hide from us: how watchful they are when they seem most artless and confidential: how often those frank smiles which they wear so easily, are traps to cajole or elude or disarm--I don't mean in your mere coquettes, but your domestic models, and paragons of female virtue.", "우리는 그네들이 얼마나 많은 것을 우리에게 숨기는지, 더없이 천진하고 흉허물 없어 보일 때 얼마나 빈틈없이 경계하는지, 그토록 수월하게 짓는 그 솔직한 미소가 어르고 구슬리거나 따돌리거나 무장해제시키려는 덫인 경우가 얼마나 잦은지 알지 못한다. 단순한 바람둥이 여자들 이야기가 아니라, 가정의 귀감이요 여성 미덕의 본보기 같은 여자들을 두고 하는 말이다."], ["Who has not seen a woman hide the dulness of a stupid husband, or coax the fury of a savage one?", "어리석은 남편의 둔함을 가려 주거나, 사나운 남편의 노여움을 어르고 달래는 여자를 보지 못한 이가 어디 있겠는가?"], ["We accept this amiable slavishness, and praise a woman for it: we call this pretty treachery truth.", "우리는 이 사랑스러운 종복 같은 태도를 받아들이고, 그래서 여자를 칭송한다. 우리는 이 어여쁜 배신을 진실이라 부른다."], ["A good housewife is of necessity a humbug; and Cornelia's husband was hoodwinked, as Potiphar was--only in a different way.", "훌륭한 살림꾼이란 어쩔 수 없이 사기꾼인 법이요, 코르넬리아의 남편 또한 보디발이 그랬듯 속아 넘어갔던 것이다. 다만 그 방식이 달랐을 뿐."]], [["By these attentions, that veteran rake, Rawdon Crawley, found himself converted into a very happy and submissive married man.", "이런 정성스러운 보살핌 덕분에, 저 산전수전 다 겪은 난봉꾼 로던 크롤리는 어느덧 더없이 행복하고 고분고분한 유부남으로 탈바꿈해 있었다."], ["His former haunts knew him not.", "그가 옛날에 들락거리던 곳들은 이제 그를 알아보지 못했다."], ["They asked about him once or twice at his clubs, but did not miss him much: in those booths of Vanity Fair people seldom do miss each other.", "클럽에서 한두 번 그의 안부를 묻기는 했으나, 딱히 그를 그리워하지는 않았다. 허영의 시장의 그 노점들에서는 사람들이 좀처럼 서로를 그리워하는 법이 없다."], ["His secluded wife ever smiling and cheerful, his little comfortable lodgings, snug meals, and homely evenings, had all the charms of novelty and secrecy.", "늘 미소 짓고 명랑한, 세상과 떨어져 사는 아내, 자그마하고 안락한 거처, 아늑한 식사, 정겨운 저녁나절은 죄다 새로움과 은밀함의 매력을 지니고 있었다."], ["The marriage was not yet declared to the world, or published in the Morning Post.", "결혼은 아직 세상에 공표되지도, 「모닝 포스트」에 발표되지도 않은 상태였다."], ["All his creditors would have come rushing on him in a body, had they known that he was united to a woman without fortune.", "그가 재산 없는 여자와 맺어졌다는 것을 알았더라면, 그의 채권자들이 죄다 떼를 지어 그에게 몰려들었을 것이다."], ["\"My relations won't cry fie upon me,\"", "\"우리 친척들이야 나한테 손가락질이야 안 하겠지.\""], ["Becky said, with rather a bitter laugh; and she was quite contented to wait until the old aunt should be reconciled, before she claimed her place in society.", "베키가 다소 씁쓸한 웃음을 지으며 말했다. 그래서 그녀는 사교계에서 자기 자리를 주장하기에 앞서, 노숙모가 화해할 마음이 들 때까지 기꺼이 기다리기로 했다."], ["So she lived at Brompton, and meanwhile saw no one, or only those few of her husband's male companions who were admitted into her little dining-room.", "그리하여 그녀는 브롬프턴에서 살았고, 그동안 아무도 만나지 않거나, 만나더라도 그녀의 자그마한 식당에 출입이 허락된 남편의 남자 친구 몇몇만을 만났다."], ["These were all charmed with her.", "이들은 죄다 그녀에게 매료되었다."], ["The little dinners, the laughing and chatting, the music afterwards, delighted all who participated in these enjoyments.", "그 자그마한 만찬이며, 웃고 떠드는 일이며, 식사 뒤의 음악은 이 즐거움에 끼어든 모든 이를 기쁘게 했다."], ["Major Martingale never thought about asking to see the marriage licence, Captain Cinqbars was perfectly enchanted with her skill in making punch.", "마틴게일 소령은 결혼 증명서를 보여 달라고 청할 생각조차 하지 않았고, 생크바스 대위는 그녀의 펀치 만드는 솜씨에 그야말로 홀딱 반했다."], ["And young Lieutenant Spatterdash (who was fond of piquet, and whom Crawley would often invite) was evidently and quickly smitten by Mrs.", "그리고 젊은 스패터대시 중위로 말하면(피케 놀이를 좋아해서 크롤리가 곧잘 초대하던 사람이었는데) 크롤리 부인에게 보란 듯이 또 순식간에 마음을 빼앗겼다."], ["Crawley; but her own circumspection and modesty never forsook her for a moment, and Crawley's reputation as a fire-eating and jealous warrior was a further and complete defence to his little wife.", "그러나 그녀 자신의 신중함과 정숙함은 한순간도 그녀를 저버리지 않았고, 불을 뿜듯 사납고 질투심 많은 무사라는 크롤리의 평판은 그 자그마한 아내에게 한층 더 완벽한 방패가 되어 주었다."]], [["There are gentlemen of very good blood and fashion in this city, who never have entered a lady's drawing-room; so that though Rawdon Crawley's marriage might be talked about in his county, where, of course, Mrs.", "이 도시에는 혈통도 좋고 행세깨나 하면서도 숙녀의 응접실에 단 한 번도 발을 들여 본 적 없는 신사들이 있다."], ["Bute had spread the news, in London it was doubted, or not heeded, or not talked about at all.", "그러니 로던 크롤리의 결혼이 그의 고향 지방에서는, 물론 뷰트 부인이 소식을 퍼뜨려 놓은 터라, 입에 오르내렸을지언정, 런던에서는 의심을 받거나, 아무도 거들떠보지 않거나, 아예 입에 오르지조차 않았다."], ["He lived comfortably on credit.", "그는 외상으로 안락하게 살았다."], ["He had a large capital of debts, which laid out judiciously, will carry a man along for many years, and on which certain men about town contrive to live a hundred times better than even men with ready money can do.", "그에게는 거액의 빚이라는 밑천이 있었는데, 빚이란 영리하게 부려 쓰면 사람을 여러 해 동안 너끈히 지탱해 주는 법이요, 도시의 어떤 사내들은 그 빚으로 현금을 가진 자들보다도 백 배는 더 떵떵거리며 사는 재간을 부린다."], ["Indeed who is there that walks London streets, but can point out a half-dozen of men riding by him splendidly, while he is on foot, courted by fashion, bowed into their carriages by tradesmen, denying themselves nothing, and living on who knows what?", "실로, 런던 거리를 걷는 이라면 누구나 자기는 걸어가는데 자기 옆을 으리으리하게 말을 타고 지나가는 사내 대여섯쯤은 짚어 낼 수 있지 않겠는가. 유행의 총아로 떠받들리고, 상인들이 굽실거리며 마차에 태워 올리고, 무엇 하나 마다하는 법 없이, 대체 무슨 돈으로 사는지 알 수 없는 그런 자들 말이다."], ["We see Jack Thriftless prancing in the park, or darting in his brougham down Pall Mall: we eat his dinners served on his miraculous plate.", "우리는 잭 스리프트리스가 공원에서 뽐내며 말을 달리거나, 사륜마차를 타고 펠 멜 거리를 쏜살같이 내려가는 모습을 본다. 그의 그 기적 같은 식기에 차려진 만찬을 먹는다."], ["\"How did this begin,\"", "\"이게 대체 어떻게 시작된 거지,\""], ["we say, \"or where will it end?\"", "우리는 말한다. \"또 어떻게 끝나려나?\""], ["\"My dear fellow,\"", "\"이보게,\""], ["I heard Jack once say, \"I owe money in every capital in Europe.\"", "언젠가 잭이 하는 말을 들었다. \"난 유럽의 수도란 수도에는 죄다 빚을 지고 있다네.\""], ["The end must come some day, but in the meantime Jack thrives as much as ever; people are glad enough to shake him by the hand, ignore the little dark stories that are whispered every now and then against him, and pronounce him a good-natured, jovial, reckless fellow.", "끝장은 언젠가 반드시 오고야 말 테지만, 그러는 동안에도 잭은 여전히 잘만 살아간다. 사람들은 그와 기꺼이 악수를 나누고, 이따금 그를 두고 수군거리는 자잘하고 음침한 소문들은 못 들은 척하며, 그를 두고 마음씨 좋고 유쾌하고 거침없는 친구라고 일컫는다."]], [["Truth obliges us to confess that Rebecca had married a gentleman of this order.", "레베카가 바로 이런 부류의 신사와 결혼했음을 우리는 진실의 명령에 따라 고백하지 않을 수 없다."], ["Everything was plentiful in his house but ready money, of which their menage pretty early felt the want; and reading the Gazette one day, and coming upon the announcement of \"Lieutenant G.", "그의 집에는 무엇이든 풍족했으나 오직 현금만은 없었고, 그들의 살림은 일찌감치 그 부족함을 느꼈다. 그러던 어느 날 「관보」를 읽다가 \"G."], ["Osborne to be Captain by purchase, vice Smith, who exchanges,\" Rawdon uttered that sentiment regarding Amelia's lover, which ended in the visit to Russell Square.", "오스본 중위, 전출하는 스미스의 후임으로 대위직을 매수함\"이라는 발표와 마주치자, 로던은 애밀리아의 연인에 관한 그 소회를 입에 올렸고, 그것이 결국 러셀 광장 방문으로 이어졌던 것이다."]], [["When Rawdon and his wife wished to communicate with Captain Dobbin at the sale, and to know particulars of the catastrophe which had befallen Rebecca's old acquaintances, the Captain had vanished; and such information as they got was from a stray porter or broker at the auction.", "로던과 그의 아내가 경매장에서 도빈 대위에게 말을 붙여 레베카의 옛 지인들에게 닥친 그 변고의 내막을 알아보려 했을 때, 대위는 이미 자취를 감춘 뒤였다. 그래서 그들이 알아낸 정보라고는 경매장의 떠돌이 짐꾼이나 중개인에게서 주워들은 것이 전부였다."]], [["\"Look at them with their hooked beaks,\"", "\"저 갈고리 같은 부리들 좀 봐요.\""], ["Becky said, getting into the buggy, her picture under her arm, in great glee.", "베키가 그림을 옆구리에 낀 채 신이 나서 이륜마차에 오르며 말했다."], ["\"They're like vultures after a battle.\"", "\"꼭 전투 끝난 뒤에 몰려드는 독수리 떼 같다니까요.\""]], [["\"Don't know.", "\"글쎄."], ["Never was in action, my dear.", "난 전투에 나가 본 적이 없어서 말이야, 여보."], ["Ask Martingale; he was in Spain, aide-de-camp to General Blazes.\"", "마틴게일한테 물어봐. 그 친구는 스페인에서 블레이즈 장군의 부관으로 있었으니까.\""]], [["\"He was a very kind old man, Mr. Sedley,\"", "\"세들리 씨는 참 친절한 노인이셨는데.\""], ["Rebecca said; \"I'm really sorry he's gone wrong.\"", "레베카가 말했다. \"그분이 영락하셨다니 정말 안됐어요.\""]], [["\"O stockbrokers--bankrupts--used to it, you know,\"", "\"아, 주식 중개인들이야 파산이 다반사지. 흔한 일이라고, 알잖아.\""], ["Rawdon replied, cutting a fly off the horse's ear.", "로던이 말의 귀에 앉은 파리를 쳐 내며 대꾸했다."]], [["\"I wish we could have afforded some of the plate, Rawdon,\"", "\"우리가 그 은식기를 좀 살 형편이 됐더라면 좋았을 텐데요, 로던.\""], ["the wife continued sentimentally.", "아내가 감상에 젖어 말을 이었다."], ["\"Five-and-twenty guineas was monstrously dear for that little piano.", "\"스물다섯 기니는 그 작은 피아노 값치고는 어처구니없이 비쌌어요."], ["We chose it at Broadwood's for Amelia, when she came from school.", "애밀리아가 학교를 마치고 나왔을 때, 우리가 브로드우드 가게에서 그 애 주려고 골라 준 거였거든요."], ["It only cost five-and-thirty then.\"", "그때는 겨우 서른다섯 기니밖에 안 들었는데.\""]], [["\"What-d'-ye-call'em--'Osborne,'", "\"그 뭐냐, 그 '오스본'인가 하는 친구는 이제 발을 빼겠지, 아마."], ["will cry off now, I suppose, since the family is smashed.", "집안이 거덜났으니 말이야."], ["How cut up your pretty little friend will be; hey, Becky?\"", "당신의 그 예쁘장한 친구가 얼마나 풀이 죽겠어. 안 그래, 베키?\""]], [["\"I daresay she'll recover it,\"", "\"그 앤 잘 추스를 거예요.\""], ["Becky said with a smile--and they drove on and talked about something else.", "베키가 미소 지으며 말했다. 그러고는 두 사람은 마차를 몰아 가며 다른 이야기로 화제를 돌렸다."]], [["Who Played on the Piano Captain Dobbin Bought\nOur surprised story now finds itself for a moment among very famous events and personages, and hanging on to the skirts of history.", "이 어리둥절한 이야기는 이제 잠시 더없이 유명한 사건들과 인물들 틈에 끼어들어, 역사의 옷자락에 매달리게 되었다."], ["When the eagles of Napoleon Bonaparte, the Corsican upstart, were flying from Provence, where they had perched after a brief sojourn in Elba, and from steeple to steeple until they reached the towers of Notre Dame, I wonder whether the Imperial birds had any eye for a little corner of the parish of Bloomsbury, London, which you might have thought so quiet, that even the whirring and flapping of those mighty wings would pass unobserved there?", "코르시카 출신의 벼락출세자 나폴레옹 보나파르트의 독수리들이, 엘바섬에 잠시 머문 뒤 둥지를 틀었던 프로방스에서 날아올라, 첨탑에서 첨탑으로 옮겨 가며 마침내 노트르담의 탑들에 이르렀을 때, 저 황제의 새들이 과연 런던 블룸즈버리 교구의 어느 자그마한 한구석에까지 눈길을 주었을지 나는 자못 궁금하다. 그토록 고요하여, 저 거대한 날개가 윙윙거리며 퍼덕이는 소리마저 거기서는 누구의 주목도 받지 못하고 지나갔으리라 여길 법한 그곳 말이다."]], [["\"Napoleon has landed at Cannes.\"", "\"나폴레옹이 칸에 상륙했다.\""], ["Such news might create a panic at Vienna, and cause Russia to drop his cards, and take Prussia into a corner, and Talleyrand and Metternich to wag their heads together, while Prince Hardenberg, and even the present Marquis of Londonderry, were puzzled; but how was this intelligence to affect a young lady in Russell Square, before whose door the watchman sang the hours when she was asleep: who, if she strolled in the square, was guarded there by the railings and the beadle: who, if she walked ever so short a distance to buy a ribbon in Southampton Row, was followed by Black Sambo with an enormous cane: who was always cared for, dressed, put to bed, and watched over by ever so many guardian angels, with and without wages?", "이런 소식은 빈에서는 공황을 불러일으키고, 러시아로 하여금 패를 내려놓게 하고, 프로이센을 한구석으로 끌고 가게 하고, 탈레랑과 메테르니히로 하여금 머리를 맞대고 끄덕이게 하며, 하르덴베르크 공작은 물론 지금의 런던데리 후작까지도 당혹게 했을지 모른다. 하지만 이 소식이 러셀 광장의 한 젊은 숙녀에게 무슨 영향을 끼친단 말인가."], ["Bon Dieu, I say, is it not hard that the fateful rush of the great Imperial struggle can't take place without affecting a poor little harmless girl of eighteen, who is occupied in billing and cooing, or working muslin collars in Russell Square?", "그녀가 잠들어 있는 동안 그 집 문 앞에서는 야경꾼이 시각을 알리며 노래를 불렀고, 그녀가 광장을 거닐기라도 하면 그곳의 철책과 교구 관리가 그녀를 지켜 주었으며, 사우샘프턴 가에 리본을 사러 그 짧디짧은 거리를 걸어갈 때조차 흑인 하인 샘보가 커다란 지팡이를 들고 뒤를 따랐고, 봉급을 받는 자든 받지 않는 자든 수많은 수호천사가 늘 그녀를 보살피고, 입혀 주고, 잠자리에 들여 주고, 지켜 주지 않았던가. 봉 디외, 내 말하건대, 저 위대한 황제의 투쟁이 운명적으로 휘몰아치는 일이, 러셀 광장에서 사랑을 속삭이거나 모슬린 옷깃에 수를 놓는 일에 몰두해 있는 가엾고 작고 무해한 열여덟 소녀에게 영향을 끼치지 않고서는 일어날 수 없다니, 이 어찌 가혹한 일이 아니겠는가?"], ["You too, kindly, homely flower!--is the great roaring war tempest coming to sweep you down, here, although cowering under the shelter of Holborn?", "너 또한, 다정하고 소박한 꽃이여! 비록 홀번의 그늘 아래 웅크리고 있다 한들, 저 거대하게 포효하는 전쟁의 폭풍이 여기까지 와서 너를 쓸어 가려는 것이냐?"], ["Yes; Napoleon is flinging his last stake, and poor little Emmy Sedley's happiness forms, somehow, part of it.", "그렇다. 나폴레옹은 마지막 판돈을 던지고 있고, 가엾고 작은 에미 세들리의 행복도 어찌어찌 그 일부를 이루고 있는 것이다."]], [["In the first place, her father's fortune was swept down with that fatal news.", "* 봉 디외: 프랑스어로 '맙소사' 정도의 감탄사."], ["All his speculations had of late gone wrong with the luckless old gentleman.", "우선, 그 운명적인 소식과 함께 그녀 아버지의 재산이 휩쓸려 내려갔다."], ["Ventures had failed; merchants had broken; funds had risen when he calculated they would fall.", "운 없는 이 노신사의 투기는 근래 들어 죄다 어긋나기만 했다. 투자가 실패하고, 거래처가 파산하고, 그가 떨어지리라 계산한 채권은 도리어 올랐다."], ["What need to particularize?", "굳이 일일이 따져 무엇하겠는가?"], ["If success is rare and slow, everybody knows how quick and easy ruin is.", "성공이란 드물고 더디지만, 파멸이 얼마나 빠르고 손쉬운지는 누구나 아는 법이다."], ["Old Sedley had kept his own sad counsel.", "세들리 노인은 그 서글픈 속사정을 혼자만 끌어안고 있었다."], ["Everything seemed to go on as usual in the quiet, opulent house; the good-natured mistress pursuing, quite unsuspiciously, her bustling idleness, and daily easy avocations; the daughter absorbed still in one selfish, tender thought, and quite regardless of all the world besides, when that final crash came, under which the worthy family fell.", "그 조용하고 풍족한 집 안에서는 만사가 여느 때와 다름없이 굴러가는 듯했다. 사람 좋은 안주인은 추호의 의심도 없이 분주한 듯한 게으름과 나날의 한가로운 소일거리를 좇았고, 딸은 여전히 이기적이고도 애틋한 한 가지 생각에 빠져 그 밖의 온 세상에는 도무지 무심했으니, 그러던 차에 마침내 그 마지막 파국이 들이닥쳐, 이 훌륭한 가족이 그 아래 쓰러지고 만 것이다."]], [["One night Mrs.", "어느 날 밤 세들리 부인은 파티 초대장을 쓰고 있었다."], ["Sedley was writing cards for a party; the Osbornes had given one, and she must not be behindhand; John Sedley, who had come home very late from the City, sate silent at the chimney side, while his wife was prattling to him; Emmy had gone up to her room ailing and low-spirited.", "오스본 집안에서 파티를 열었으니 그녀도 뒤질 수는 없는 노릇이었다. 시내에서 몹시 늦게 돌아온 존 세들리는 벽난로 옆에 말없이 앉아 있었고, 그 사이 아내는 그에게 재잘거리고 있었다."], ["\"She's not happy,\" the mother went on.", "에미는 몸이 좋지 않고 풀이 죽어 제 방으로 올라간 뒤였다."], ["\"George Osborne neglects her.", "\"그 애가 행복하질 않아요.\""], ["I've no patience with the airs of those people.", "어머니가 말을 이었다. \"조지 오스본이 그 애를 소홀히 한다고요."], ["The girls have not been in the house these three weeks; and George has been twice in town without coming.", "난 그 사람들 거드름에 진절머리가 나요. 그 집 딸들은 이 삼 주째 우리 집엔 코빼기도 안 비치고, 조지는 시내에 두 번이나 왔다 가면서 들르지도 않았어요."], ["Edward Dale saw him at the Opera.", "에드워드 데일이 오페라에서 그 애를 봤대요."], ["Edward would marry her I'm sure: and there's Captain Dobbin who, I think, would--only I hate all army men.", "에드워드라면 분명 그 애와 결혼할 거예요. 게다가 도빈 대위라는 사람도, 내 보기엔 그럴 것 같고요."], ["Such a dandy as George has become.", "다만 난 군인이라면 죄다 싫지만요."], ["With his military airs, indeed!", "조지가 어쩜 그리 멋쟁이가 됐는지."], ["We must show some folks that we're as good as they.", "그 군인 티 내는 꼴이라니, 정말! 우리도 그 사람들 못지않다는 걸 어떤 사람들한텐 보여 줘야 해요."], ["Only give Edward Dale any encouragement, and you'll see.", "에드워드 데일한테 조금만 부추겨 주면, 두고 보세요."], ["We must have a party, Mr. S.", "우리도 파티를 열어야겠어요, 여보."], ["Why don't you speak, John?", "왜 말이 없어요, 존?"], ["Shall I say Tuesday fortnight?", "다음다음 주 화요일이라고 할까요?"], ["Why don't you answer?", "왜 대답이 없어요?"], ["Good God, John, what has happened?\"", "맙소사, 존, 무슨 일이 있었어요?\""]], [["John Sedley sprang up out of his chair to meet his wife, who ran to him.", "존 세들리는 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 자기에게 달려오는 아내를 맞았다."], ["He seized her in his arms, and said with a hasty voice, \"We're ruined, Mary.", "그는 아내를 두 팔로 끌어안고 다급한 목소리로 말했다. \"우린 망했소, 메리."], ["We've got the world to begin over again, dear.", "처음부터 다시 시작해야 하오, 여보."], ["It's best that you should know all, and at once.\"", "당신이 다 알아 두는 게, 그것도 당장 아는 게 좋겠소.\""], ["As he spoke, he trembled in every limb, and almost fell.", "이렇게 말하면서 그는 온몸을 부들부들 떨었고, 거의 쓰러질 지경이었다."], ["He thought the news would have overpowered his wife--his wife, to whom he had never said a hard word.", "그는 이 소식이 아내를 짓눌러 버리리라 생각했다. 단 한 번도 모진 말을 건넨 적 없는 그 아내를 말이다."], ["But it was he that was the most moved, sudden as the shock was to her.", "그러나 그녀에게 그 충격이 아무리 갑작스러웠다 해도, 정작 가장 크게 흔들린 사람은 그 자신이었다. 그가 다시 의자에 주저앉자, 위로하는 역할을 맡은 것은 아내였다."], ["When he sank back into his seat, it was the wife that took the office of consoler.", "그녀는 떨리는 그의 손을 잡아 입을 맞추고는 제 목에 둘렀다. 그녀는 그를 자기 존이라고, 사랑하는 존이라고, 자기 영감이라고, 자상한 영감이라고 불렀다."], ["She took his trembling hand, and kissed it, and put it round her neck: she called him her John--her dear John--her old man--her kind old man; she poured out a hundred words of incoherent love and tenderness; her faithful voice and simple caresses wrought this sad heart up to an inexpressible delight and anguish, and cheered and solaced his over-burdened soul.", "그녀는 두서없는 사랑과 다정함의 말을 수없이 쏟아 냈다. 그 한결같은 목소리와 꾸밈없는 손길은 이 서글픈 마음을 형언할 수 없는 기쁨과 고뇌로 끌어올렸고, 무겁게 짓눌린 그의 영혼을 북돋우고 어루만져 주었다."]], [["Only once in the course of the long night as they sate together, and poor Sedley opened his pent-up soul, and told the story of his losses and embarrassments--the treason of some of his oldest friends, the manly kindness of some, from whom he never could have expected it--in a general confession--only once did the faithful wife give way to emotion.", "긴 밤이 흐르는 동안 둘이 함께 앉아 있었고, 가엾은 세들리가 꽉 막혔던 속을 털어놓으며 자신의 손실과 곤경의 사연을 — 가장 오랜 벗 몇몇의 배신과, 도무지 기대조차 하지 못했던 이들의 사내다운 친절을 — 온전히 고백하던 그 긴 밤에, 단 한 번, 오직 단 한 번만 그 한결같은 아내가 감정에 무너졌다."]], [["\"My God, my God, it will break Emmy's heart,\"", "\"맙소사, 맙소사, 이건 에미의 가슴을 찢어 놓을 거예요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["The father had forgotten the poor girl.", "아버지는 그 가엾은 딸을 잊고 있었다."], ["She was lying, awake and unhappy, overhead.", "그 애는 머리 위층에서 잠들지 못한 채 불행하게 누워 있었다."], ["In the midst of friends, home, and kind parents, she was alone.", "벗들과 집과 자상한 부모에 둘러싸여 있으면서도, 그녀는 혼자였다."], ["To how many people can any one tell all?", "사람이 누군가에게 모든 것을 털어놓을 수 있는 상대가 과연 몇이나 되겠는가?"], ["Who will be open where there is no sympathy, or has call to speak to those who never can understand?", "공감해 주는 이가 없는데 누가 마음을 열겠으며, 도무지 이해하지 못할 이들에게 말할 까닭이 어디 있겠는가?"], ["Our gentle Amelia was thus solitary.", "우리의 온순한 애밀리아가 바로 그렇게 외로웠다."], ["She had no confidante, so to speak, ever since she had anything to confide.", "털어놓을 무언가가 생긴 그 순간부터, 말하자면 그녀에게는 속을 터놓을 상대가 없었던 것이다."], ["She could not tell the old mother her doubts and cares; the would-be sisters seemed every day more strange to her.", "그녀는 늙은 어머니에게 자신의 의혹과 근심을 말할 수 없었고, 언니가 되어 줄 뻔했던 그 여자들은 날이 갈수록 더 서먹하게만 느껴졌다."], ["And she had misgivings and fears which she dared not acknowledge to herself, though she was always secretly brooding over them.", "그리고 그녀에게는 차마 스스로에게조차 인정할 수 없는 불안과 두려움이 있었지만, 늘 남몰래 그것을 곱씹고 있었다."]], [["Her heart tried to persist in asserting that George Osborne was worthy and faithful to her, though she knew otherwise.", "그녀의 마음은 조지 오스본이 자기에게 합당하고 충실한 사람이라고 우겨 보려 안간힘을 썼지만, 실은 그렇지 않다는 걸 그녀도 알고 있었다."], ["How many a thing had she said, and got no echo from him.", "그녀가 얼마나 많은 말을 건넸으되, 그에게서 아무런 메아리도 얻지 못했던가."], ["How many suspicions of selfishness and indifference had she to encounter and obstinately overcome.", "이기심과 무관심을 얼마나 많이 짐작하고 마주하면서, 또 얼마나 끈질기게 떨쳐 내야 했던가."], ["To whom could the poor little martyr tell these daily struggles and tortures?", "이 가엾고 작은 순교자가 나날의 이 싸움과 고통을 대체 누구에게 털어놓을 수 있었겠는가?"], ["Her hero himself only half understood her.", "그녀의 영웅조차 그녀를 절반밖에 이해하지 못했다."], ["She did not dare to own that the man she loved was her inferior; or to feel that she had given her heart away too soon.", "그녀는 자기가 사랑하는 그 남자가 자기보다 못한 사람임을 차마 인정할 수 없었고, 너무 일찍 마음을 줘 버렸다고 느낄 수도 없었다."], ["Given once, the pure bashful maiden was too modest, too tender, too trustful, too weak, too much woman to recall it.", "한번 내준 이상, 그 순결하고 수줍은 처녀는 너무도 정숙하고, 너무도 다정하고, 너무도 잘 믿고, 너무도 여리고, 너무나 여자였기에 그것을 도로 거두어들이지 못했다."], ["We are Turks with the affections of our women; and have made them subscribe to our doctrine too.", "우리는 여자들의 애정 앞에서 터키 사람들이나 다름없고, 게다가 그녀들로 하여금 우리의 교리에 따르도록 만들어 놓았다."], ["We let their bodies go abroad liberally enough, with smiles and ringlets and pink bonnets to disguise them instead of veils and yakmaks.", "우리는 그녀들의 몸뚱이가 바깥나들이하는 것은 제법 너그럽게 허락하여, 베일과 야크마크 대신 미소와 곱슬머리와 분홍 보닛으로 가리게 한다."], ["But their souls must be seen by only one man, and they obey not unwillingly, and consent to remain at home as our slaves--ministering to us and doing drudgery for us.", "그러나 그녀들의 영혼만큼은 오직 한 남자에게만 보여야 하는 법이고, 그녀들은 마지못한 기색도 없이 순순히 따르며, 우리의 노예로서 집 안에 머무는 데 — 우리를 섬기고 우리를 위해 궂은일을 하는 데 동의하는 것이다."]], [["So imprisoned and tortured was this gentle little heart, when in the month of March, Anno Domini 1815, Napoleon landed at Cannes, and Louis XVIII fled, and all Europe was in alarm, and the funds fell, and old John Sedley was ruined.", "* 야크마크: 이슬람 여성이 얼굴을 가리는 데 쓰던 천. 이 온순하고 작은 마음이 그토록 갇히고 시달리고 있던 차에, 서기 1815년 3월, 나폴레옹이 칸에 상륙하고, 루이 18세가 달아나고, 온 유럽이 경악에 빠지고, 채권이 폭락하고, 늙은 존 세들리가 파산했던 것이다."]], [["We are not going to follow the worthy old stockbroker through those last pangs and agonies of ruin through which he passed before his commercial demise befell.", "우리는 이 훌륭한 노 주식 중개인이 상업적 죽음을 맞기에 앞서 겪어야 했던 그 파멸의 마지막 고통과 단말마를 일일이 뒤쫓을 생각은 없다."], ["They declared him at the Stock Exchange; he was absent from his house of business: his bills were protested: his act of bankruptcy formal.", "사람들은 그를 증권 거래소에서 파산자로 선언했고, 그는 자신의 사업장에 나타나지 않았으며, 그의 어음은 부도 처리되었고, 그의 파산 행위는 공식화되었다."], ["The house and furniture of Russell Square were seized and sold up, and he and his family were thrust away, as we have seen, to hide their heads where they might.", "러셀 광장의 집과 가구는 압류되어 경매에 부쳐졌고, 우리가 본 대로 그와 그 가족은 어디든 머리를 숨길 만한 곳으로 내쫓겼다."]], [["John Sedley had not the heart to review the domestic establishment who have appeared now and anon in our pages and of whom he was now forced by poverty to take leave.", "존 세들리는, 그동안 우리 지면에 이따금 등장했던, 그리고 이제 가난 탓에 어쩔 수 없이 작별해야 하는 그 집안의 하인들을 차마 돌아볼 마음이 나지 않았다."], ["The wages of those worthy people were discharged with that punctuality which men frequently show who only owe in great sums--they were sorry to leave good places--but they did not break their hearts at parting from their adored master and mistress.", "그 훌륭한 사람들의 봉급은, 큰 액수의 빚만 지는 이들이 흔히 보이는 그 정확함으로 깔끔하게 청산되었다. 그들은 좋은 일자리를 떠나는 것을 아쉬워했으나, 흠모하던 주인 내외와 헤어진다고 가슴이 미어지지는 않았다."], ["Amelia's maid was profuse in condolences, but went off quite resigned to better herself in a genteeler quarter of the town.", "애밀리아의 하녀는 위로의 말을 아낌없이 늘어놓았지만, 시내의 좀 더 점잖은 동네에서 제 신세를 고쳐 보겠다며 아주 체념한 듯 떠나갔다. 흑인 하인 샘보는 제 직종에 대한 묘한 집착으로 술집을 차리기로 마음먹었다."], ["Black Sambo, with the infatuation of his profession, determined on setting up a public-house. Honest old Mrs.", "정직한 노파 블렌킨솝만은 정말로, 조스와 애밀리아가 태어나는 것을 보았고 존 세들리와 그 아내가 연애하던 시절까지 지켜본 터라, 봉급도 없이 그들 곁에 남으려 했다."], ["Blenkinsop indeed, who had seen the birth of Jos and Amelia, and the wooing of John Sedley and his wife, was for staying by them without wages, having amassed a considerable sum in their service: and she accompanied the fallen people into their new and humble place of refuge, where she tended them and grumbled against them for a while.", "그동안 그 집에서 일하며 제법 큰돈을 모아 두었던 것이다. 그녀는 영락한 사람들을 따라 그들의 새롭고 초라한 피난처까지 동행하여, 한동안 그들을 보살피기도 하고 그들에게 투덜대기도 했다."]], [["Of all Sedley's opponents in his debates with his creditors which now ensued, and harassed the feelings of the humiliated old gentleman so severely, that in six weeks he oldened more than he had done for fifteen years before--the most determined and obstinate seemed to be John Osborne, his old friend and neighbour--John Osborne, whom he had set up in life--who was under a hundred obligations to him--and whose son was to marry Sedley's daughter.", "뒤이어 벌어진 채권자들과의 분쟁에서, 모욕당한 이 노신사의 심사를 어찌나 모질게 들쑤셔 놓던지 그가 여섯 주 만에 지난 십오 년보다 더 늙어 버릴 지경이었는데, 세들리의 그 모든 적수 가운데 가장 단호하고 완강했던 이는 다름 아닌 그의 오랜 벗이자 이웃인 존 오스본이었던 듯하다. 세들리가 인생을 일으켜 세워 준 사람, 세들리에게 백 가지 은혜를 입은 사람, 그 아들이 세들리의 딸과 결혼하기로 되어 있던 바로 그 사람 말이다."], ["Any one of these circumstances would account for the bitterness of Osborne's opposition.", "이 사정 가운데 어느 하나만으로도 오스본의 반목이 그토록 쓰라렸던 까닭을 설명하기에는 충분할 것이다."]], [["When one man has been under very remarkable obligations to another, with whom he subsequently quarrels, a common sense of decency, as it were, makes of the former a much severer enemy than a mere stranger would be.", "어떤 사람이 다른 이에게 매우 두드러진 은혜를 입고 나서 훗날 그와 다투게 되면, 이른바 일반적인 염치의 감각이 그 전자를, 한낱 낯선 사람보다 훨씬 더 가혹한 원수로 만들어 버린다."], ["To account for your own hard-heartedness and ingratitude in such a case, you are bound to prove the other party's crime.", "그런 경우 자기 자신의 모진 심보와 배은망덕을 변명하자면, 상대편의 죄를 입증하지 않을 수 없는 법이다."], ["It is not that you are selfish, brutal, and angry at the failure of a speculation--no, no--it is that your partner has led you into it by the basest treachery and with the most sinister motives.", "당신이 이기적이고 잔인하며 투기 실패에 화가 난 것이 아니라 — 아니, 아니다 — 당신의 동업자가 더없이 비열한 배신과 더없이 음흉한 속셈으로 당신을 그 일에 끌어들였다는 것이다."], ["From a mere sense of consistency, a persecutor is bound to show that the fallen man is a villain--otherwise he, the persecutor, is a wretch himself.", "단지 앞뒤를 맞추려는 일념에서, 박해자는 영락한 자가 악당임을 보여야만 하는 법이다. 그러지 않으면 박해자인 자기 자신이 비열한 인간이 되어 버리니까."]], [["And as a general rule, which may make all creditors who are inclined to be severe pretty comfortable in their minds, no men embarrassed are altogether honest, very likely.", "그리고 일반적인 규칙으로 말하건대 — 이는 모질게 굴려는 마음을 먹은 모든 채권자의 마음을 제법 편안하게 해 줄 만한데 — 곤경에 처한 사람치고 완전히 정직한 자란 아마 없을 것이다. 그들은 무언가를 숨긴다."], ["They conceal something; they exaggerate chances of good luck; hide away the real state of affairs; say that things are flourishing when they are hopeless, keep a smiling face (a dreary smile it is) upon the verge of bankruptcy--are ready to lay hold of any pretext for delay or of any money, so as to stave off the inevitable ruin a few days longer.", "행운의 가능성을 부풀린다. 일의 실상을 감춘다."], ["\"Down with such dishonesty,\" says the creditor in triumph, and reviles his sinking enemy.", "가망이 없는데도 잘되어 간다고 말하고, 파산의 벼랑 끝에서도 미소 띤 얼굴을(그것은 음울한 미소다) 유지하며, 피할 수 없는 파멸을 며칠이라도 더 미루려고 무슨 핑계든 무슨 돈이든 붙잡으려 든다."], ["\"You fool, why do you catch at a straw?\"", "\"그런 부정직함은 끌어내려야지.\""], ["calm good sense says to the man that is drowning.", "채권자가 의기양양하게 말하며 가라앉아 가는 적을 욕한다."], ["\"You villain, why do you shrink from plunging into the irretrievable Gazette?\"", "\"이 바보야, 왜 지푸라기를 붙잡느냐?\" 차분한 양식이 물에 빠진 사람에게 이렇게 말한다."], ["says prosperity to the poor devil battling in that black gulf.", "\"이 악당아, 돌이킬 수 없는 파산 공고에 뛰어들기를 왜 꺼리느냐?\""], ["Who has not remarked the readiness with which the closest of friends and honestest of men suspect and accuse each other of cheating when they fall out on money matters?", "번영이, 저 검은 심연에서 허우적대는 가엾은 자에게 이렇게 말한다. 더없이 가까운 벗들, 더없이 정직한 사람들이 돈 문제로 틀어지면 서로를 사기꾼이라 의심하고 비난하기를 얼마나 선뜻 해치우는지, 이를 눈여겨보지 않은 이가 어디 있겠는가?"], ["Everybody does it.", "누구나 그렇게 한다."], ["Everybody is right, I suppose, and the world is a rogue.", "누구나 다 옳은 것이리라. 그리고 세상은 악당인 것이다."]], [["Then Osborne had the intolerable sense of former benefits to goad and irritate him: these are always a cause of hostility aggravated.", "게다가 오스본에게는 지난날 베푼 은혜에 대한 견딜 수 없는 의식이 있어 그를 부추기고 들쑤셨으니, 이런 것은 늘 적의를 더 키우는 원인이 되는 법이다."], ["Finally, he had to break off the match between Sedley's daughter and his son; and as it had gone very far indeed, and as the poor girl's happiness and perhaps character were compromised, it was necessary to show the strongest reasons for the rupture, and for John Osborne to prove John Sedley to be a very bad character indeed.", "끝으로, 그는 세들리의 딸과 자기 아들 사이의 혼약을 깨뜨려야 했다. 그런데 그 일이 이미 상당히 깊이 진척된 데다, 그 가엾은 처녀의 행복과 어쩌면 평판까지도 위태로워진 터라, 파혼의 더없이 강력한 사유를 내세울 필요가 있었고, 그러자면 존 오스본으로서는 존 세들리가 정말이지 아주 못된 인간임을 입증해야만 했다."]], [["At the meetings of creditors, then, he comported himself with a savageness and scorn towards Sedley, which almost succeeded in breaking the heart of that ruined bankrupt man.", "그리하여 채권자 모임에서 그는 세들리를 향해 어찌나 사나운 멸시를 드러냈던지, 그 영락한 파산자의 가슴을 거의 찢어 놓을 뻔했다."], ["On George's intercourse with Amelia he put an instant veto--menacing the youth with maledictions if he broke his commands, and vilipending the poor innocent girl as the basest and most artful of vixens.", "그는 조지가 애밀리아와 교제하는 것을 즉각 금지하면서, 만약 그 명령을 어긴다면 저주를 퍼붓겠다고 젊은 아들을 위협했고, 그 가엾고 죄 없는 처녀를 더없이 비열하고 더없이 교활한 여우라고 헐뜯었다."], ["One of the great conditions of anger and hatred is, that you must tell and believe lies against the hated object, in order, as we said, to be consistent.", "분노와 증오의 큰 조건 가운데 하나는, 앞서 말했듯 앞뒤를 맞추기 위해, 미워하는 대상을 두고 거짓말을 지어내고 또 그것을 믿어야 한다는 점이다."]], [["When the great crash came--the announcement of ruin, and the departure from Russell Square, and the declaration that all was over between her and George--all over between her and love, her and happiness, her and faith in the world--a brutal letter from John Osborne told her in a few curt lines that her father's conduct had been of such a nature that all engagements between the families were at an end--when the final award came, it did not shock her so much as her parents, as her mother rather expected (for John Sedley himself was entirely prostrate in the ruins of his own affairs and shattered honour).", "저 큰 파국이 닥쳤을 때 — 파멸의 통보와, 러셀 광장에서의 퇴거와, 그녀와 조지 사이의 모든 것이 끝났다는 선언이, 다시 말해 그녀와 사랑 사이가, 그녀와 행복 사이가, 그녀와 세상에 대한 믿음 사이가 다 끝났다는 선언이 닥쳤을 때 — 존 오스본의 모진 편지가 몇 줄 무뚝뚝한 문장으로 그녀에게 알리기를, 그녀 아버지의 행실이 그러한 것이었으니 두 집안 사이의 모든 약속은 끝났다고 했을 때 — 그 마지막 판결이 내려졌을 때, 그것은 그녀의 부모만큼, 또 그녀의 어머니가 짐작했던 만큼 그녀에게 큰 충격을 주지는 않았다(존 세들리 본인은 자기 사업과 산산조각 난 명예의 폐허 속에서 완전히 무너져 있었으니까)."], ["Amelia took the news very palely and calmly.", "애밀리아는 그 소식을 몹시 창백하고 차분하게 받아들였다."], ["It was only the confirmation of the dark presages which had long gone before.", "그것은 오래전부터 앞서갔던 어두운 예감을 그저 확인해 주는 것일 뿐이었다."], ["It was the mere reading of the sentence--of the crime she had long ago been guilty--the crime of loving wrongly, too violently, against reason.", "그것은 한낱 판결문의 낭독, 그러니까 그녀가 오래전에 저지른 죄 — 잘못 사랑한 죄, 이성을 거슬러 너무도 격렬하게 사랑한 죄에 대한 판결문의 낭독일 뿐이었다."], ["She told no more of her thoughts now than she had before.", "그녀는 이제 자기 생각을 전보다 더 털어놓지도 않았다."], ["She seemed scarcely more unhappy now when convinced all hope was over, than before when she felt but dared not confess that it was gone.", "모든 희망이 끝났음을 확신하게 된 지금이, 희망이 사라졌음을 느끼면서도 차마 고백하지 못하던 이전보다 그다지 더 불행해 보이지도 않았다."], ["So she changed from the large house to the small one without any mark or difference; remained in her little room for the most part; pined silently; and died away day by day.", "그렇게 그녀는 아무런 표시도 변화도 없이 큰 집에서 작은 집으로 옮겨 갔다. 거의 내내 자기 작은 방에 틀어박혀, 말없이 야위어 가며, 하루하루 시들어 갔다."], ["I do not mean to say that all females are so.", "모든 여자가 다 그렇다는 말은 아니다."], ["My dear Miss Bullock, I do not think your heart would break in this way.", "사랑하는 불록 양, 나는 당신의 가슴은 이런 식으로 무너지지 않으리라 생각한다."], ["You are a strong-minded young woman with proper principles.", "당신은 올바른 신념을 지닌, 의지가 굳센 젊은 여인이니까."], ["I do not venture to say that mine would; it has suffered, and, it must be confessed, survived.", "감히 내 가슴이 그러리라고도 말하지 않겠다. 그것도 괴로움을 겪긴 했으나, 고백건대 살아남았으니 말이다."], ["But there are some souls thus gently constituted, thus frail, and delicate, and tender.", "하지만 그렇게도 곱게 빚어진, 그렇게도 여리고, 섬세하고, 다정한 영혼들이 더러 있는 법이다."]], [["Whenever old John Sedley thought of the affair between George and Amelia, or alluded to it, it was with bitterness almost as great as Mr.", "늙은 존 세들리는 조지와 애밀리아 사이의 일을 떠올리거나 입에 올릴 때마다, 오스본 씨가 보였던 것에 거의 맞먹는 쓰라림을 드러냈다."], ["Osborne himself had shown. He cursed Osborne and his family as heartless, wicked, and ungrateful.", "그는 오스본과 그 가족을 매정하고, 사악하고, 배은망덕한 자들이라며 저주했다."], ["No power on earth, he swore, would induce him to marry his daughter to the son of such a villain, and he ordered Emmy to banish George from her mind, and to return all the presents and letters which she had ever had from him.", "이 땅의 어떤 힘으로도 자기 딸을 그런 악당의 아들에게 시집보내도록 자기를 부추길 수는 없으리라고 그는 맹세했고, 에미에게 조지를 마음에서 몰아내고 그에게서 받은 선물과 편지를 죄다 돌려보내라고 명령했다."]], [["She promised acquiescence, and tried to obey.", "그녀는 따르겠노라 약속하고 순종하려 애썼다."], ["She put up the two or three trinkets: and, as for the letters, she drew them out of the place where she kept them; and read them over--as if she did not know them by heart already: but she could not part with them.", "그녀는 두세 가지 패물을 챙겨 두었다. 그리고 편지들은, 보관해 두었던 곳에서 꺼내 — 이미 줄줄 외우고 있지 않기라도 한 듯 — 다시 읽고 또 읽었지만, 차마 그것들과는 헤어질 수 없었다."], ["That effort was too much for her; she placed them back in her bosom again--as you have seen a woman nurse a child that is dead.", "그 일만큼은 그녀에게 너무 벅찼다. 그녀는 그것들을 다시 가슴에 품었다 — 죽은 아이를 품에 안고 어르는 여인을 당신도 본 적이 있으리라, 꼭 그처럼."], ["Young Amelia felt that she would die or lose her senses outright, if torn away from this last consolation.", "어린 애밀리아는 이 마지막 위안마저 빼앗긴다면 자기가 죽거나 아예 정신을 놓아 버리리라 느꼈다."], ["How she used to blush and lighten up when those letters came!", "그 편지들이 오면 그녀는 얼마나 얼굴을 붉히며 환해지곤 했던가!"], ["How she used to trip away with a beating heart, so that she might read unseen!", "남몰래 읽으려고 가슴을 두근거리며 얼마나 종종걸음으로 빠져나가곤 했던가!"], ["If they were cold, yet how perversely this fond little soul interpreted them into warmth.", "편지가 냉랭할 때조차, 이 애틋하고 작은 영혼은 그것을 얼마나 비뚤어지게 따스함으로 해석해 냈던가."], ["If they were short or selfish, what excuses she found for the writer!", "편지가 짧거나 이기적일 때면, 쓴 사람을 위해 또 얼마나 많은 변명을 찾아냈던가!"]], [["It was over these few worthless papers that she brooded and brooded.", "그녀가 곱씹고 또 곱씹은 것은 바로 이 몇 장 안 되는 하찮은 종이들이었다."], ["She lived in her past life--every letter seemed to recall some circumstance of it.", "그녀는 지난 삶 속에서 살았다. 편지 한 장 한 장이 그 시절의 어떤 정황을 되살려 내는 듯했다."], ["How well she remembered them all!", "그녀는 그 모든 것을 얼마나 또렷이 기억하고 있었던가!"], ["His looks and tones, his dress, what he said and how--these relics and remembrances of dead affection were all that were left her in the world.", "그의 표정과 말투, 그의 옷차림, 그가 한 말과 그 말투 — 죽어 버린 애정의 이 유물과 추억들이 이 세상에서 그녀에게 남은 전부였다."], ["And the business of her life, was--to watch the corpse of Love.", "그리고 그녀 삶의 일과란, 바로 사랑의 시신을 지키는 일이었다."]], [["To death she looked with inexpressible longing.", "그녀는 형언할 수 없는 그리움으로 죽음을 바라보았다."], ["Then, she thought, I shall always be able to follow him.", "그러면, 하고 그녀는 생각했다, 나는 언제까지나 그분을 따라갈 수 있을 거야."], ["I am not praising her conduct or setting her up as a model for Miss Bullock to imitate. Miss B.", "나는 그녀의 행실을 칭찬하거나, 불록 양이 본받을 본보기로 떠받드는 것이 아니다."], ["knows how to regulate her feelings better than this poor little creature. Miss B.", "불록 양은 이 가엾고 작은 사람보다 자기 감정을 다스리는 법을 더 잘 안다."], ["would never have committed herself as that imprudent Amelia had done; pledged her love irretrievably; confessed her heart away, and got back nothing--only a brittle promise which was snapt and worthless in a moment.", "불록 양이라면 저 경솔한 애밀리아처럼 자신을 내맡기는 짓은 결코 하지 않았으리라. 돌이킬 수 없이 사랑을 맹세하고, 마음을 죄다 털어 고백해 버리고도, 얻은 것이라곤 아무것도 없으니 — 그저 한순간에 뚝 부러져 버리는 하찮은 약속 하나뿐이었다."], ["A long engagement is a partnership which one party is free to keep or to break, but which involves all the capital of the other.", "긴 약혼이란, 한쪽은 마음대로 지키거나 깨뜨릴 수 있으면서 다른 쪽의 모든 밑천은 죄다 걸리게 만드는 동업이다."]], [["Be cautious then, young ladies; be wary how you engage.", "그러니 조심하라, 젊은 숙녀들이여. 약혼에는 신중하라."], ["Be shy of loving frankly; never tell all you feel, or (a better way still), feel very little.", "솔직하게 사랑하기를 꺼려라. 느끼는 바를 결코 다 말하지 말라."], ["See the consequences of being prematurely honest and confiding, and mistrust yourselves and everybody.", "아니, (더 나은 방법은) 아주 조금만 느껴라. 너무 일찍 정직하고 마음을 다 내준 결과가 어떠한지를 보라."], ["Get yourselves married as they do in France, where the lawyers are the bridesmaids and confidantes.", "그리고 너 자신도 모든 이도 다 믿지 말라. 변호사가 들러리와 속내를 터놓는 친구 노릇을 하는 프랑스에서 하듯, 그렇게 결혼을 성사시켜라."], ["At any rate, never have any feelings which may make you uncomfortable, or make any promises which you cannot at any required moment command and withdraw.", "어쨌든, 너를 불편하게 만들 만한 감정일랑 결코 품지 말고, 필요한 순간에 언제든 거두어들일 수 없는 약속일랑 결코 하지 말라."], ["That is the way to get on, and be respected, and have a virtuous character in Vanity Fair.", "그것이 허영의 시장에서 출세하고, 존경받고, 정숙한 평판을 얻는 길이다."]], [["If Amelia could have heard the comments regarding her which were made in the circle from which her father's ruin had just driven her, she would have seen what her own crimes were, and how entirely her character was jeopardised.", "만약 애밀리아가, 아버지의 파멸로 자신이 막 쫓겨난 그 사교계에서 자기를 두고 오가는 평을 들을 수 있었다면, 자신의 죄가 무엇인지, 자신의 평판이 얼마나 완전히 위태로워졌는지 알았을 것이다."], ["Such criminal imprudence Mrs. Smith never knew of; such horrid familiarities Mrs.", "그토록 죄스러운 경솔함이라니, 스미스 부인은 들어 본 적도 없었다."], ["Brown had always condemned, and the end might be a warning to HER daughters.", "그토록 흉측한 허물없음이라니, 브라운 부인은 늘 비난해 오던 바였고, 그 결말은 자기 딸들에게 좋은 경고가 될 만했다."], ["\"Captain Osborne, of course, could not marry a bankrupt's daughter,\"", "\"오스본 대위가 파산자의 딸과 결혼할 수야 없는 노릇이지요.\""], ["the Misses Dobbin said.", "도빈 댁 따님들이 말했다."], ["\"It was quite enough to have been swindled by the father.", "\"그 아비한테 사기당한 것만 해도 진절머리가 나는데."], ["As for that little Amelia, her folly had really passed all--\"", "그리고 그 애밀리아라는 애로 말하면, 그 어리석음이란 정말이지 도를 넘어서—\""]], [["\"All what?\" Captain Dobbin roared out.", "\"도를 넘다니 뭐가 도를 넘었다는 거요?\""], ["\"Haven't they been engaged ever since they were children?", "도빈 대위가 버럭 소리쳤다. \"그 둘이 어릴 적부터 약혼한 사이가 아니었소?"], ["Wasn't it as good as a marriage?", "그게 결혼이나 다름없지 않았소?"], ["Dare any soul on earth breathe a word against the sweetest, the purest, the tenderest, the most angelical of young women?\"", "이 땅의 어느 누가, 저토록 더없이 사랑스럽고, 더없이 순결하고, 더없이 다정하고, 더없이 천사 같은 처녀를 두고 감히 한마디라도 입에 올린단 말이오?\""]], [["\"La, William, don't be so highty-tighty with US.", "\"어머, 윌리엄, 우리한테까지 그렇게 거만하게 굴진 마. 우린 남자가 아니잖아."], ["We're not men.", "너랑 싸울 수도 없는걸.\" 제인 양이 말했다."], ["We can't fight you,\" Miss Jane said.", "\"우린 세들리 양을 두고 아무 험담도 안 했어."], ["\"We've said nothing against Miss Sedley: but that her conduct throughout was MOST IMPRUDENT, not to call it by any worse name; and that her parents are people who certainly merit their misfortunes.\"", "다만 그 애 행실이 처음부터 끝까지 더없이 경솔했다는 거지, 그보다 더한 말은 안 하더라도. 그리고 그 부모는 분명 그런 불운을 당해도 싼 사람들이라는 거고.\""]], [["\"Hadn't you better, now that Miss Sedley is free, propose for her yourself, William?\"", "\"이제 세들리 양도 자유로운 몸이 됐으니, 네가 직접 청혼하는 게 어때, 윌리엄?\""], ["Miss Ann asked sarcastically.", "앤 양이 비꼬듯 물었다."], ["\"It would be a most eligible family connection.", "\"더없이 어울리는 집안끼리의 인연이 될 텐데."], ["He!", "호!"], ["he!\"", "호!\""]], [["\"I marry her!\"", "\"내가 그 여자와 결혼한다고!\""], ["Dobbin said, blushing very much, and talking quick.", "도빈이 얼굴을 잔뜩 붉히며 빠르게 말했다."], ["\"If you are so ready, young ladies, to chop and change, do you suppose that she is?", "\"당신들이야 그렇게 손바닥 뒤집듯 마음을 바꿔 대니, 그 여자도 그러리라 여기는 거요?"], ["Laugh and sneer at that angel.", "그 천사를 비웃고 빈정대 보시오."], ["She can't hear it; and she's miserable and unfortunate, and deserves to be laughed at.", "그녀는 듣지도 못하니까. 게다가 그녀는 비참하고 불운하니, 비웃음을 받아 마땅하겠지."], ["Go on joking, Ann.", "계속 농담이나 하시오, 앤."], ["You're the wit of the family, and the others like to hear it.\"", "당신은 이 집안의 익살꾼이고, 다른 사람들도 그걸 듣길 좋아하니까.\""]], [["\"I must tell you again we're not in a barrack, William,\"", "\"다시 말하지만 우린 병영에 있는 게 아니야, 윌리엄.\""], ["Miss Ann remarked.", "앤 양이 한마디 했다."]], [["\"In a barrack, by Jove--I wish anybody in a barrack would say what you do,\"", "\"병영이라, 맙소사 — 병영에서 누가 당신들처럼 지껄여 보라지.\""], ["cried out this uproused British lion.", "발끈한 이 영국 사자가 소리쳤다."], ["\"I should like to hear a man breathe a word against her, by Jupiter.", "\"주피터께 맹세코, 어떤 사내가 그녀를 두고 한마디라도 입에 올리는 걸 한번 들어 봤으면 좋겠소."], ["But men don't talk in this way, Ann: it's only women, who get together and hiss, and shriek, and cackle.", "하지만 사내들은 이런 식으로 떠들지 않아, 앤. 떼 지어 모여서 쉭쉭대고, 빽빽거리고, 꽥꽥대는 건 여자들뿐이지."], ["There, get away--don't begin to cry.", "자, 저리 가시오 — 울기는 또 왜 우는 거요."], ["I only said you were a couple of geese,\"", "난 그저 당신들이 거위 한 쌍이라고 했을 뿐이오.\""], ["Will Dobbin said, perceiving Miss Ann's pink eyes were beginning to moisten as usual.", "앤 양의 발그레한 눈이 여느 때처럼 촉촉해지기 시작하는 것을 알아채고 윌 도빈이 말했다."], ["\"Well, you're not geese, you're swans--anything you like, only do, do leave Miss Sedley alone.\"", "\"그래, 거위가 아니라 백조요 — 뭐든 좋을 대로 하시오, 다만 제발, 제발 세들리 양만은 좀 내버려 두시오.\""]], [["Anything like William's infatuation about that silly little flirting, ogling thing was never known, the mamma and sisters agreed together in thinking: and they trembled lest, her engagement being off with Osborne, she should take up immediately her other admirer and Captain.", "저 어리석고, 사내들에게 추파나 던지고 곁눈질이나 해 대는 것에게 윌리엄이 보이는 그런 맹목적인 빠짐이란 일찍이 들어 본 적이 없다고, 어머니와 누이들은 한데 입을 모았다. 그리고 그 여자가 오스본과의 약혼이 깨졌으니 곧장 또 다른 숭배자이자 대위인 그 사람을 잡아채지나 않을까 전전긍긍했다."], ["In which forebodings these worthy young women no doubt judged according to the best of their experience; or rather (for as yet they had had no opportunities of marrying or of jilting) according to their own notions of right and wrong.", "이런 우려 속에서, 이 훌륭한 젊은 여인들은 분명 자기네 경험에 비추어, 아니 (아직은 결혼하거나 약혼자를 차 버릴 기회조차 없었으니) 차라리 옳고 그름에 대한 자기네 나름의 관념에 비추어 판단했던 것이다."]], [["\"It is a mercy, Mamma, that the regiment is ordered abroad,\"", "\"연대가 해외로 파견 명령을 받았으니 천만다행이에요, 어머니.\""], ["the girls said.", "딸들이 말했다."], ["\"THIS danger, at any rate, is spared our brother.\"", "\"이 위험만큼은 적어도 우리 오빠가 면하게 됐으니까요.\""]], [["Such, indeed, was the fact; and so it is that the French Emperor comes in to perform a part in this domestic comedy of Vanity Fair which we are now playing, and which would never have been enacted without the intervention of this august mute personage.", "과연 사실이 그러했다. 그리하여, 우리가 지금 펼치고 있는 이 허영의 시장의 가정 희극에 한 배역을 맡아, 프랑스 황제가 등장하게 되는 것이다."], ["It was he that ruined the Bourbons and Mr. John Sedley.", "이 위풍당당한 무언의 인물이 끼어들지 않았던들 이 희극은 결코 무대에 오르지 못했으리라."], ["It was he whose arrival in his capital called up all France in arms to defend him there; and all Europe to oust him.", "부르봉 왕가와 존 세들리 씨를 망가뜨린 것이 바로 그였다. 그가 수도에 당도하자 그를 거기서 지켜 내려고 온 프랑스가 무장하고 일어섰으며, 그를 몰아내려고 온 유럽이 일어선 것이다."], ["While the French nation and army were swearing fidelity round the eagles in the Champ de Mars, four mighty European hosts were getting in motion for the great chasse a l'aigle; and one of these was a British army, of which two heroes of ours, Captain Dobbin and Captain Osborne, formed a portion.", "프랑스 국민과 군대가 샹드마르스에서 독수리 깃발을 둘러싸고 충성을 맹세하는 동안, 네 갈래의 막강한 유럽 대군이 저 거대한 '독수리 사냥'을 위해 움직이기 시작했다. 그 가운데 하나가 영국군이었으니, 우리의 두 영웅 도빈 대위와 오스본 대위도 그 일부를 이루고 있었다."]], [["The news of Napoleon's escape and landing was received by the gallant --th with a fiery delight and enthusiasm, which everybody can understand who knows that famous corps.", "나폴레옹의 탈출과 상륙 소식은 용맹한 ○○연대에 불같은 환희와 열광으로 받아들여졌는데, 그 유명한 부대를 아는 이라면 누구든 이해할 만한 일이다."], ["From the colonel to the smallest drummer in the regiment, all were filled with hope and ambition and patriotic fury; and thanked the French Emperor as for a personal kindness in coming to disturb the peace of Europe.", "연대장에서부터 부대 안의 가장 어린 고수에 이르기까지, 모두가 희망과 야망과 애국적 열기로 가득 차서, 유럽의 평화를 흩뜨려 놓으러 와 준 것을 마치 개인적 호의라도 되는 양 프랑스 황제에게 감사했다."], ["Now was the time the --th had so long panted for, to show their comrades in arms that they could fight as well as the Peninsular veterans, and that all the pluck and valour of the --th had not been killed by the West Indies and the yellow fever. Stubble and Spooney looked to get their companies without purchase.", "이제야말로 ○○연대가 그토록 오래 갈망해 온 때, 곧 자기네도 이베리아 반도의 노병들 못지않게 싸울 수 있음을, 또 ○○연대의 그 모든 기개와 용맹이 서인도 제도와 황열병에 죽어 없어지지 않았음을 전우들에게 보여 줄 때가 온 것이었다."], ["Before the end of the campaign (which she resolved to share), Mrs. Major O'Dowd hoped to write herself Mrs.", "스터블과 스푸니는 돈을 들이지 않고도 자기 중대를 얻기를 기대했다."], ["Colonel O'Dowd, C.B. Our two friends (Dobbin and Osborne) were quite as much excited as the rest: and each in his way--Mr.", "이번 출정이 끝나기 전에(그녀는 그 출정에 함께하기로 작정했다), 소령 부인 오다우드는 자신을 '오다우드 대령 부인, 바스 훈장 수훈자'라고 서명하게 되기를 바랐다."], ["Dobbin very quietly, Mr. Osborne very loudly and energetically--was bent upon doing his duty, and gaining his share of honour and distinction.", "우리의 두 친구(도빈과 오스본)도 다른 이들 못지않게 들떠 있었으니, 저마다 제 방식대로 — 도빈 씨는 아주 조용하게, 오스본 씨는 아주 떠들썩하고 활기차게 — 제 임무를 다하고 제 몫의 명예와 영광을 거머쥐겠다고 단단히 벼르고 있었다."]], [["The agitation thrilling through the country and army in consequence of this news was so great, that private matters were little heeded: and hence probably George Osborne, just gazetted to his company, busy with preparations for the march, which must come inevitably, and panting for further promotion--was not so much affected by other incidents which would have interested him at a more quiet period.", "이 소식의 여파로 나라와 군대를 휩쓴 동요가 어찌나 컸던지 사사로운 일은 거의 안중에도 없었다. 그러니 아마도 조지 오스본은, 막 자기 중대 지휘관으로 임명되어 머지않아 반드시 닥칠 행군 채비에 분주하고 더 높은 진급을 갈망하던 터라, 좀 더 평온한 시절이었다면 관심을 가졌을 다른 일들에는 그다지 마음이 흔들리지 않았던 것이다."], ["He was not, it must be confessed, very much cast down by good old Mr. Sedley's catastrophe.", "고백건대, 그는 사람 좋은 늙은 세들리 씨의 파국에도 그리 크게 풀이 죽지는 않았다."], ["He tried his new uniform, which became him very handsomely, on the day when the first meeting of the creditors of the unfortunate gentleman took place.", "그 불운한 신사의 채권자들이 처음 모임을 갖던 날, 그는 자기에게 아주 잘 어울리는 새 군복을 입어 보고 있었다."], ["His father told him of the wicked, rascally, shameful conduct of the bankrupt, reminded him of what he had said about Amelia, and that their connection was broken off for ever; and gave him that evening a good sum of money to pay for the new clothes and epaulets in which he looked so well.", "아버지는 그에게 그 파산자의 사악하고, 비열하고, 수치스러운 행실을 일러 주었고, 애밀리아에 관해 자기가 했던 말을 상기시키며 두 집안의 인연이 영영 끊겼음을 알렸다. 그리고 그날 저녁 그에게 두둑한 돈을 주어, 그가 그토록 멋지게 차려입은 새 옷과 견장 값을 치르게 했다."], ["Money was always useful to this free-handed young fellow, and he took it without many words.", "돈이란 이 씀씀이 헤픈 젊은이에게 늘 요긴한 것이었으니, 그는 별말 없이 그것을 받아 챙겼다."], ["The bills were up in the Sedley house, where he had passed so many, many happy hours.", "그가 그토록 많고도 많은 행복한 시간을 보냈던 세들리 댁에는 압류 딱지가 붙어 있었다."], ["He could see them as he walked from home that night (to the Old Slaughters', where he put up when in town) shining white in the moon.", "그날 밤 집을 나서며(그는 시내에 있을 때면 묵던 올드슬로터스로 향하던 참이었다) 그는 달빛 아래 하얗게 빛나는 그 딱지들을 볼 수 있었다."], ["That comfortable home was shut, then, upon Amelia and her parents: where had they taken refuge?", "그렇다면 그 아늑한 집은 애밀리아와 그 부모를 안에 가둔 채 닫혀 버린 것이었다. 그들은 어디로 피신한 것일까?"], ["The thought of their ruin affected him not a little.", "그들의 파멸을 생각하니 그도 적잖이 마음이 아렸다."], ["He was very melancholy that night in the coffee-room at the Slaughters'; and drank a good deal, as his comrades remarked there.", "그날 밤 그는 슬로터스의 커피룸에서 몹시 우울해했고, 거기 있던 전우들이 한마디 했을 만큼 술을 꽤나 마셨다."]], [["Dobbin came in presently, cautioned him about the drink, which he only took, he said, because he was deuced low; but when his friend began to put to him clumsy inquiries, and asked him for news in a significant manner, Osborne declined entering into conversation with him, avowing, however, that he was devilish disturbed and unhappy.", "이윽고 도빈이 들어와 술을 삼가라고 일렀으나, 오스본은 그저 기분이 지독히 가라앉아서 마셨을 뿐이라고 했다. 하지만 친구가 어설프게 이것저것 캐묻기 시작하며 의미심장한 투로 무슨 소식이 없느냐고 묻자, 오스본은 그와 대화하기를 거절하면서도 자기가 지독히 심란하고 불행하다는 것만은 털어놓았다."]], [["Three days afterwards, Dobbin found Osborne in his room at the barracks--his head on the table, a number of papers about, the young Captain evidently in a state of great despondency.", "사흘 뒤, 도빈은 병영의 제 방에 있는 오스본을 발견했다. 그는 탁자에 머리를 박은 채였고, 주위에는 종이 몇 장이 흩어져 있었으며, 이 젊은 대위는 누가 봐도 깊은 실의에 빠진 모습이었다."], ["\"She--she's sent me back some things I gave her--some damned trinkets.", "\"그녀가 — 그녀가 내가 줬던 물건들을 돌려보냈어 — 그 빌어먹을 패물들 말이야."], ["Look here!\"", "여기 좀 봐!\""], ["There was a little packet directed in the well-known hand to Captain George Osborne, and some things lying about--a ring, a silver knife he had bought, as a boy, for her at a fair; a gold chain, and a locket with hair in it.", "낯익은 필체로 '조지 오스본 대위께'라고 적힌 자그마한 꾸러미가 있었고, 그 주위에 물건들이 흩어져 있었다. 반지 하나, 그가 어릴 적 장에서 그녀에게 사 주었던 은제 칼 한 자루, 금줄 하나, 그리고 머리카락이 들어 있는 로켓 하나였다."], ["\"It's all over,\"", "\"다 끝났어.\""], ["said he, with a groan of sickening remorse.", "그가 메스꺼운 회한의 신음을 토하며 말했다."], ["\"Look, Will, you may read it if you like.\"", "\"봐, 윌, 원하면 읽어 봐도 돼.\""]], [["There was a little letter of a few lines, to which he pointed, which said:", "그가 가리키는 곳에 몇 줄짜리 짤막한 편지가 있었고, 거기엔 이렇게 적혀 있었다."]], [["My papa has ordered me to return to you these presents, which you made in happier days to me; and I am to write to you for the last time.", "저의 아빠가 제게 명하시기를, 더 행복했던 시절에 당신이 제게 주신 이 선물들을 당신께 돌려보내라 하셨습니다. 그래서 마지막으로 당신께 편지를 씁니다."], ["I think, I know you feel as much as I do the blow which has come upon us.", "우리에게 닥친 이 타격을 당신도 저만큼 느끼시리라 저는 생각합니다, 아니 확신합니다."], ["It is I that absolve you from an engagement which is impossible in our present misery.", "지금 우리의 이 비참한 처지에서는 도저히 지킬 수 없는 그 약혼에서, 제가 당신을 풀어 드립니다."], ["I am sure you had no share in it, or in the cruel suspicions of Mr. Osborne, which are the hardest of all our griefs to bear.", "당신은 그 일에도, 또 우리의 온갖 슬픔 가운데 가장 견디기 힘든 저 오스본 씨의 매정한 의심에도 아무런 몫이 없었음을 저는 확신합니다."], ["Farewell.", "안녕히 계세요."], ["Farewell.", "안녕히."], ["I pray God to strengthen me to bear this and other calamities, and to bless you always.", "이 일과 또 다른 재난들을 견딜 수 있도록 하느님께서 저에게 힘을 주시기를, 그리고 언제까지나 당신을 축복해 주시기를 기도합니다."], ["A.", "A."]], [["I shall often play upon the piano--your piano.", "저는 자주 피아노를 칠 거예요 — 당신의 피아노를요."], ["It was like you to send it.", "그걸 보내 주시다니 정말 당신다웠어요."]], [["Dobbin was very soft-hearted.", "도빈은 마음이 몹시 여린 사람이었다."], ["The sight of women and children in pain always used to melt him.", "고통받는 여인들과 아이들의 모습은 늘 그의 마음을 녹이곤 했다."], ["The idea of Amelia broken-hearted and lonely tore that good-natured soul with anguish.", "애밀리아가 가슴이 찢긴 채 외로이 있다는 생각은 그 마음씨 고운 영혼을 고뇌로 후벼 팠다."], ["And he broke out into an emotion, which anybody who likes may consider unmanly.", "그리하여 그는 한바탕 감정을 터뜨렸는데, 누구든 좋을 대로 사내답지 못한 짓이라 여겨도 좋다."], ["He swore that Amelia was an angel, to which Osborne said aye with all his heart.", "그는 애밀리아가 천사라고 맹세했고, 오스본도 진심으로 거기에 그렇다고 맞장구쳤다."], ["He, too, had been reviewing the history of their lives--and had seen her from her childhood to her present age, so sweet, so innocent, so charmingly simple, and artlessly fond and tender.", "그 역시 두 사람의 삶의 역사를 되짚어 보던 참이었다. 그래서 어린 시절부터 지금의 나이에 이르기까지, 그토록 사랑스럽고, 그토록 순진하고, 그토록 매혹적으로 꾸밈없으며, 천진하게 다정하고 살가운 그녀를 떠올려 보고 있었던 것이다."]], [["What a pang it was to lose all that: to have had it and not prized it!", "그 모든 것을 잃다니, 또 그것을 가졌으면서도 소중히 여기지 않았다니, 이 얼마나 가슴 아픈 일인가!"], ["A thousand homely scenes and recollections crowded on him--in which he always saw her good and beautiful.", "천 가지의 소박한 정경과 추억이 그에게 밀려들었고, 그 속에서 그는 언제나 선량하고 아름다운 그녀를 보았다."], ["And for himself, he blushed with remorse and shame, as the remembrance of his own selfishness and indifference contrasted with that perfect purity.", "그리고 자기 자신으로 말하면, 자신의 이기심과 무관심에 대한 기억이 그 완벽한 순결함과 대비되니 그는 회한과 수치로 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["For a while, glory, war, everything was forgotten, and the pair of friends talked about her only.", "한동안 영광이며 전쟁이며 모든 것이 잊혔고, 두 친구는 오직 그녀에 대해서만 이야기했다."]], [["\"Where are they?\"", "\"그들은 어디 있지?\""], ["Osborne asked, after a long talk, and a long pause--and, in truth, with no little shame at thinking that he had taken no steps to follow her.", "오랜 이야기 끝에 한참을 침묵하다가 오스본이 물었다. 사실 그녀를 뒤쫓을 아무런 조치도 취하지 않았다는 생각에 적잖이 부끄러워하면서."], ["\"Where are they?", "\"그들이 어디 있느냐고?"], ["There's no address to the note.\"", "이 편지엔 주소가 없잖아.\""]], [["Dobbin knew.", "도빈은 알고 있었다."], ["He had not merely sent the piano; but had written a note to Mrs.", "그는 그저 피아노만 보낸 것이 아니라, 세들리 부인에게 편지를 써서 찾아뵙고 싶다고 청을 넣었던 것이다."], ["Sedley, and asked permission to come and see her--and he had seen her, and Amelia too, yesterday, before he came down to Chatham; and, what is more, he had brought that farewell letter and packet which had so moved them.", "그리고 그는 채텀으로 내려오기 전인 어제 그녀를, 또한 애밀리아까지 만났다. 더욱이 그토록 그들의 마음을 흔들어 놓은 그 작별 편지와 꾸러미를 가져온 것도 그였다."]], [["The good-natured fellow had found Mrs. Sedley only too willing to receive him, and greatly agitated by the arrival of the piano, which, as she conjectured, MUST have come from George, and was a signal of amity on his part.", "이 마음씨 고운 친구는 세들리 부인이 자기를 너무도 기꺼이 맞아 줄 뿐 아니라, 피아노가 도착한 일로 무척 들떠 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Captain Dobbin did not correct this error of the worthy lady, but listened to all her story of complaints and misfortunes with great sympathy--condoled with her losses and privations, and agreed in reprehending the cruel conduct of Mr.", "그녀의 짐작으로는 그것이 분명 조지에게서 온 것이며, 그의 화해 신호임에 틀림없다는 것이었다. 도빈 대위는 이 선량한 부인의 오해를 바로잡지 않고, 그녀가 늘어놓는 온갖 하소연과 불행의 사연을 깊은 공감으로 다 들어 주었다."], ["Osborne towards his first benefactor.", "그녀의 손실과 궁핍을 위로하고, 자신의 첫 은인을 향한 오스본 씨의 매정한 처사를 함께 나무랐다."], ["When she had eased her overflowing bosom somewhat, and poured forth many of her sorrows, he had the courage to ask actually to see Amelia, who was above in her room as usual, and whom her mother led trembling downstairs.", "그녀가 벅차오르던 가슴을 어느 정도 가라앉히고 슬픔을 한껏 쏟아 낸 뒤, 그는 용기를 내어 정말로 애밀리아를 만나게 해 달라고 청했다. 그녀는 여느 때처럼 위층 제 방에 있었고, 어머니가 떨고 있는 그녀를 데리고 아래층으로 내려왔다."]], [["Her appearance was so ghastly, and her look of despair so pathetic, that honest William Dobbin was frightened as he beheld it; and read the most fatal forebodings in that pale fixed face.", "그녀의 행색이 어찌나 처참하고, 그 절망의 빛이 어찌나 애처롭던지, 그것을 본 순간 정직한 윌리엄 도빈은 겁이 났다. 그 창백하게 굳은 얼굴에서 그는 더없이 불길한 예감을 읽었다."], ["After sitting in his company a minute or two, she put the packet into his hand, and said, \"Take this to Captain Osborne, if you please, and--and I hope he's quite well--and it was very kind of you to come and see us--and we like our new house very much.", "그녀는 그와 한두 마디 함께 앉아 있다가, 꾸러미를 그의 손에 쥐여 주며 말했다. \"이걸 오스본 대위께 전해 주세요, 부디."], ["And I--I think I'll go upstairs, Mamma, for I'm not very strong.\"", "그리고 — 그리고 그분이 안녕하시길 바라요 — 그리고 이렇게 찾아와 주시다니 정말 친절하셔요 — 우린 새집이 무척 마음에 들어요."], ["And with this, and a curtsey and a smile, the poor child went her way.", "그리고 저는 — 저는 위층으로 올라가는 게 좋겠어요, 어머니. 제가 그리 튼튼하질 못해서요.\""], ["The mother, as she led her up, cast back looks of anguish towards Dobbin.", "이 말과 함께, 무릎을 살짝 굽히는 인사와 미소를 지으며 그 가엾은 아이는 제 갈 길로 갔다."], ["The good fellow wanted no such appeal.", "어머니는 그녀를 데리고 올라가면서 고뇌에 찬 눈길을 도빈 쪽으로 거듭 던졌다."], ["He loved her himself too fondly for that.", "마음씨 고운 그 친구에게는 그런 호소가 필요치도 않았다."], ["Inexpressible grief, and pity, and terror pursued him, and he came away as if he was a criminal after seeing her.", "그는 그러기에는 그녀를 너무도 애틋하게 사랑하고 있었으니까. 형언할 수 없는 슬픔과 연민과 두려움이 그를 따라붙었고, 그는 마치 죄인이라도 된 양 그녀를 보고 나서 그곳을 떠나왔다."]], [["When Osborne heard that his friend had found her, he made hot and anxious inquiries regarding the poor child.", "친구가 그녀를 만났다는 말을 듣자, 오스본은 그 가엾은 아이에 관해 다급하고 안타까운 물음을 쏟아 냈다."], ["How was she?", "그녀는 어떻던가?"], ["How did she look?", "그녀의 안색은 어땠나?"], ["What did she say?", "그녀가 뭐라고 했나?"], ["His comrade took his hand, and looked him in the face.", "그의 전우는 그의 손을 잡고 그의 얼굴을 가만히 들여다보았다."]], [["\"George, she's dying,\"", "\"조지, 그녀는 죽어 가고 있어.\""], ["William Dobbin said--and could speak no more.", "윌리엄 도빈이 말했다. 그리고 그는 더 말을 잇지 못했다."]], [["There was a buxom Irish servant-girl, who performed all the duties of the little house where the Sedley family had found refuge: and this girl had in vain, on many previous days, striven to give Amelia aid or consolation.", "세들리 가족이 피신한 그 작은 집의 온갖 살림을 도맡아 하는 통통한 아일랜드 출신 하녀가 하나 있었다. 이 처녀는 앞서 여러 날 동안 애밀리아에게 도움이나 위안을 주어 보려 했으나 헛수고였다."], ["Emmy was much too sad to answer, or even to be aware of the attempts the other was making in her favour.", "에미는 너무도 슬픔에 잠긴 나머지 대답조차 하지 못했고, 심지어 그녀가 자기를 위해 애쓰고 있다는 것조차 알아채지 못했다."]], [["Four hours after the talk between Dobbin and Osborne, this servant-maid came into Amelia's room, where she sate as usual, brooding silently over her letters--her little treasures.", "도빈과 오스본이 이야기를 나눈 지 네 시간쯤 뒤, 이 하녀가 애밀리아의 방으로 들어왔다. 애밀리아는 여느 때처럼 거기 앉아 자기 편지들을, 그 작은 보물들을 말없이 곱씹고 있었다."], ["The girl, smiling, and looking arch and happy, made many trials to attract poor Emmy's attention, who, however, took no heed of her.", "처녀는 미소를 지으며, 짐짓 장난스럽고 행복한 표정으로 가엾은 에미의 주의를 끌어 보려 갖은 애를 썼지만, 에미는 그녀에게 도통 눈길도 주지 않았다."]], [["\"Miss Emmy,\"", "\"에미 아가씨.\""], ["said the girl.", "처녀가 말했다."]], [["\"I'm coming,\"", "\"가요.\""], ["Emmy said, not looking round.", "에미가 돌아보지도 않고 말했다."]], [["\"There's a message,\"", "\"전갈이 왔어요.\""], ["the maid went on.", "하녀가 말을 이었다."], ["\"There's something--somebody--sure, here's a new letter for you--don't be reading them old ones any more.\"", "\"뭔가가 — 누군가가 — 이거 봐요, 아가씨한테 새 편지가 왔다니까요 — 그 헌 편지들은 이제 그만 좀 읽으시고요.\""], ["And she gave her a letter, which Emmy took, and read.", "그러고는 그녀에게 편지 한 통을 건넸고, 에미는 그것을 받아 읽었다."]], [["\"I must see you,\"", "\"당신을 봐야겠소.\""], ["the letter said.", "편지에는 그렇게 적혀 있었다."], ["\"Dearest Emmy--dearest love--dearest wife, come to me.\"", "\"더없이 사랑하는 에미 — 더없이 사랑하는 이여 — 더없이 사랑하는 나의 아내여, 내게로 와요.\""]], [["George and her mother were outside, waiting until she had read the letter.", "조지와 그녀의 어머니가 그녀가 편지를 다 읽기를 기다리며 밖에 서 있었다."]], [["Miss Crawley at Nurse\nWe have seen how Mrs. Firkin, the lady's maid, as soon as any event of importance to the Crawley family came to her knowledge, felt bound to communicate it to Mrs.", "우리는 앞서 보았듯, 시녀인 퍼킨 부인은 크롤리 가문에 중대한 일이 생겼다는 것을 알게 되면 으레 목사관에 있는 뷰트 크롤리 부인에게 그것을 전해야 한다는 의무감을 느꼈다."], ["Bute Crawley, at the Rectory; and have before mentioned how particularly kind and attentive that good-natured lady was to Miss Crawley's confidential servant.", "그리고 그 마음씨 좋은 부인이 크롤리 양의 심복 하녀에게 얼마나 각별히 친절하고 살뜰했는지도 앞서 언급한 바 있다."], ["She had been a gracious friend to Miss Briggs, the companion, also; and had secured the latter's good-will by a number of those attentions and promises, which cost so little in the making, and are yet so valuable and agreeable to the recipient.", "그녀는 말동무인 브리그스 양에게도 자상한 벗이었으며, 베푸는 데는 거의 비용이 들지 않으면서도 받는 이에게는 그토록 값지고 흐뭇한 그 숱한 호의와 약속으로 그녀의 호감을 단단히 사 두었다. 사실 알뜰한 살림꾼이자 능숙한 가정 관리자라면 누구나 알 것이다."], ["Indeed every good economist and manager of a household must know how cheap and yet how amiable these professions are, and what a flavour they give to the most homely dish in life.", "이런 식의 입에 발린 말들이 얼마나 값싸면서도 또 얼마나 사랑스럽게 들리는지를, 그리고 그것이 세상에서 가장 변변찮은 음식에마저 얼마나 맛깔스러운 풍미를 더해 주는지를 말이다."], ["Who was the blundering idiot who said that \"fine words butter no parsnips\"?", "\"고운 말로는 무 한 쪽도 못 버무린다\"고 지껄인 멍청이는 대체 누구였던가?"], ["Half the parsnips of society are served and rendered palatable with no other sauce.", "이 세상에 차려지는 무 요리의 절반은 다름 아닌 그 양념으로만 그럴듯하게 입맛을 돋우는 것이다."], ["As the immortal Alexis Soyer can make more delicious soup for a half-penny than an ignorant cook can concoct with pounds of vegetables and meat, so a skilful artist will make a few simple and pleasing phrases go farther than ever so much substantial benefit-stock in the hands of a mere bungler.", "불멸의 알렉시스 소이어가 반 페니어치로 무지한 요리사가 몇 파운드어치의 채소와 고기로 끓여 내는 것보다 더 맛난 수프를 만들어 내듯, 솜씨 좋은 명인은 어설픈 풋내기가 산더미 같은 실질적 은혜의 밑천을 쥐고도 못 해내는 일을, 간소하고 듣기 좋은 몇 마디 말로 훨씬 멀리까지 해낸다. 아니, 우리는 안다."], ["Nay, we know that substantial benefits often sicken some stomachs; whereas, most will digest any amount of fine words, and be always eager for more of the same food. Mrs.", "실질적 은혜란 더러 어떤 위장에는 거북함을 안기지만, 입에 발린 고운 말이라면 대개 얼마든지 소화해 내고, 늘 그 같은 음식을 더 달라며 안달한다는 것을."], ["Bute had told Briggs and Firkin so often of the depth of her affection for them; and what she would do, if she had Miss Crawley's fortune, for friends so excellent and attached, that the ladies in question had the deepest regard for her; and felt as much gratitude and confidence as if Mrs. Bute had loaded them with the most expensive favours.", "뷰트 부인은 브리그스와 퍼킨에게 자신이 그들을 얼마나 깊이 아끼는지, 또 만약 크롤리 양의 재산이 자기 것이 된다면 이토록 훌륭하고 충직한 벗들을 위해 무엇을 해 줄 것인지 어찌나 자주 일러 두었던지, 그 두 숙녀는 그녀에게 더없이 깊은 경의를 품게 되었고, 마치 뷰트 부인이 더없이 값진 은혜를 그득히 베풀기라도 한 양 고마움과 신뢰를 느꼈던 것이다."]], [["Rawdon Crawley, on the other hand, like a selfish heavy dragoon as he was, never took the least trouble to conciliate his aunt's aides-de-camp, showed his contempt for the pair with entire frankness--made Firkin pull off his boots on one occasion--sent her out in the rain on ignominious messages--and if he gave her a guinea, flung it to her as if it were a box on the ear.", "반면 로던 크롤리는 이기적이고 둔중한 용기병답게, 고모의 부관들의 환심을 사려는 노력 따위는 손톱만큼도 기울이지 않았다. 그는 그 두 사람에 대한 경멸을 거리낌 없이 고스란히 드러냈다."], ["As his aunt, too, made a butt of Briggs, the Captain followed the example, and levelled his jokes at her--jokes about as delicate as a kick from his charger.", "한번은 퍼킨에게 자기 장화를 벗기게 했고, 빗속에 치욕스러운 심부름을 보냈으며, 행여 일 기니를 줄 때에도 마치 귀싸대기라도 갈기듯 그녀에게 휙 던져 주었던 것이다. 게다가 고모가 브리그스를 놀림감으로 삼았으니, 대위 역시 그 본을 따라 그녀에게 농지거리를 퍼부었다."], ["Whereas, Mrs.", "그 농담이란 그의 군마에게 한 방 걷어차이는 것만큼이나 섬세한 것이었다."], ["Bute consulted her in matters of taste or difficulty, admired her poetry, and by a thousand acts of kindness and politeness, showed her appreciation of Briggs; and if she made Firkin a twopenny-halfpenny present, accompanied it with so many compliments, that the twopence-half-penny was transmuted into gold in the heart of the grateful waiting-maid, who, besides, was looking forwards quite contentedly to some prodigious benefit which must happen to her on the day when Mrs.", "그러나 뷰트 부인은 취향이 걸리거나 까다로운 일이 있으면 그녀에게 의견을 구했고, 그녀의 시를 칭송했으며, 수천 가지 친절과 정중한 행동으로 브리그스를 높이 사고 있음을 보여 주었다. 그리고 행여 퍼킨에게 이 펜스 반페니짜리 선물을 줄 때에도 어찌나 많은 찬사를 곁들였던지, 그 이 펜스 반페니는 고마워하는 하녀의 가슴속에서 금덩이로 변모하는 것이었다."], ["Bute came into her fortune.", "게다가 그 하녀는 뷰트 부인이 재산을 물려받는 그날 자기에게 떨어질 어마어마한 은혜를 자못 흐뭇하게 고대하고 있었던 것이다."]], [["The different conduct of these two people is pointed out respectfully to the attention of persons commencing the world.", "이 두 사람의 상반된 처신은, 막 세상에 발을 들이는 이들의 주의를 끌도록 삼가 권해 두는 바이다."], ["Praise everybody, I say to such: never be squeamish, but speak out your compliment both point-blank in a man's face, and behind his back, when you know there is a reasonable chance of his hearing it again.", "누구든 칭찬하라, 나는 그런 이들에게 이렇게 말한다. 결코 까다롭게 굴지 말고, 상대의 면전에 대고 곧장 찬사를 늘어놓되, 그가 다시 전해 들을 만한 합당한 기회가 있다 싶으면 등 뒤에서도 그리하라."], ["Never lose a chance of saying a kind word.", "다정한 말 한마디 건넬 기회를 결코 놓치지 말라."], ["As Collingwood never saw a vacant place in his estate but he took an acorn out of his pocket and popped it in; so deal with your compliments through life.", "콜링우드는 자기 영지에 빈자리가 보이면 으레 주머니에서 도토리 한 알을 꺼내 슬쩍 심었으니, 평생 그대의 찬사도 그렇게 다루라."], ["An acorn costs nothing; but it may sprout into a prodigious bit of timber.", "도토리 한 알은 거저나 다름없지만, 자라나면 엄청난 재목이 될지도 모르는 것이다."]], [["In a word, during Rawdon Crawley's prosperity, he was only obeyed with sulky acquiescence; when his disgrace came, there was nobody to help or pity him. Whereas, when Mrs.", "요컨대 로던 크롤리가 잘나가던 시절에도 사람들은 그저 시무룩하게 마지못해 그를 따랐을 뿐이고, 그가 낯을 못 들게 되자 그를 도와주거나 가엾이 여기는 이는 아무도 없었다."], ["Bute took the command at Miss Crawley's house, the garrison there were charmed to act under such a leader, expecting all sorts of promotion from her promises, her generosity, and her kind words.", "그에 반해 뷰트 부인이 크롤리 양의 집에서 지휘권을 쥐자, 그곳 수비대는 그런 지휘관 아래에서 일하게 된 것을 더없이 기뻐하며, 그녀의 약속과 너그러움과 다정한 말에서 온갖 영전을 기대했던 것이다."]], [["That he would consider himself beaten, after one defeat, and make no attempt to regain the position he had lost, Mrs. Bute Crawley never allowed herself to suppose.", "뷰트 크롤리 부인은 로던이 한 번 패했다고 스스로 졌다 여기며, 잃어버린 자리를 되찾으려는 시도조차 하지 않으리라고는 결코 생각하지 않았다."], ["She knew Rebecca to be too clever and spirited and desperate a woman to submit without a struggle; and felt that she must prepare for that combat, and be incessantly watchful against assault; or mine, or surprise.", "그녀는 레베카가 너무도 영리하고 기개 있으며 또 절박한 여자라서, 한바탕 싸워 보지도 않고 순순히 굴복할 리 없다는 것을 알았다. 그리하여 그 싸움에 대비해야 하고, 기습이든 매복이든 불시의 공격이든 끊임없이 경계해야 한다고 느꼈다."]], [["In the first place, though she held the town, was she sure of the principal inhabitant?", "우선, 비록 그녀가 이 도성을 장악했다 한들 그곳의 주된 주민까지 확실히 제 편으로 두었다고 할 수 있겠는가?"], ["Would Miss Crawley herself hold out; and had she not a secret longing to welcome back the ousted adversary?", "크롤리 양 본인이 과연 버텨 줄 것인가? 그리고 쫓겨난 적을 다시 맞아들이고 싶은 은밀한 바람을 품고 있지는 않을까?"], ["The old lady liked Rawdon, and Rebecca, who amused her. Mrs.", "그 노부인은 자신을 즐겁게 해 주던 로던과 레베카를 마음에 들어 했다."], ["Bute could not disguise from herself the fact that none of her party could so contribute to the pleasures of the town-bred lady.", "뷰트 부인은 자기 편의 그 누구도 이 도회풍 숙녀를 그만큼 즐겁게 해 줄 수 없다는 사실을 스스로 감출 수 없었다."], ["\"My girls' singing, after that little odious governess's, I know is unbearable,\"", "\"우리 애들 노래는, 그 가증스러운 가정교사 계집애 노래를 들은 다음이니, 영 못 들어 줄 지경이라는 걸 나도 안다니까.\""], ["the candid Rector's wife owned to herself.", "솔직한 목사 부인은 속으로 인정했다."], ["\"She always used to go to sleep when Martha and Louisa played their duets.", "\"마사랑 루이자가 이중주를 켤 때면 형님은 늘 잠이 들곤 했지."], ["Jim's stiff college manners and poor dear Bute's talk about his dogs and horses always annoyed her.", "짐의 뻣뻣한 대학물 든 태도며, 가엾은 뷰트가 개니 말이니 늘어놓는 이야기에 형님은 늘 진저리를 냈고."], ["If I took her to the Rectory, she would grow angry with us all, and fly, I know she would; and might fall into that horrid Rawdon's clutches again, and be the victim of that little viper of a Sharp.", "내가 형님을 목사관으로 모셔 간다면 우리 모두에게 화를 내고 달아나 버릴 테지, 나는 알아. 그러고는 다시 그 끔찍한 로던의 손아귀에 떨어져 그 살무사 같은 샤프 계집애의 먹잇감이 되고 말 거야."], ["Meanwhile, it is clear to me that she is exceedingly unwell, and cannot move for some weeks, at any rate; during which we must think of some plan to protect her from the arts of those unprincipled people.\"", "어쨌든 형님이 몹시 편찮으시고, 적어도 몇 주는 꼼짝 못 하시리라는 건 분명해. 그동안 우리는 그 파렴치한 자들의 농간에서 형님을 지킬 무슨 방도를 궁리해 두어야 해.\""]], [["In the very best of moments, if anybody told Miss Crawley that she was, or looked ill, the trembling old lady sent off for her doctor; and I daresay she was very unwell after the sudden family event, which might serve to shake stronger nerves than hers. At least, Mrs.", "크롤리 양은 더없이 기분 좋은 순간에도, 누군가 그녀더러 편찮으시다거나 안색이 안 좋아 보인다고 말하기만 하면, 벌벌 떨며 의사를 부르러 사람을 보내는 노부인이었다."], ["Bute thought it was her duty to inform the physician, and the apothecary, and the dame-de-compagnie, and the domestics, that Miss Crawley was in a most critical state, and that they were to act accordingly.", "그러니 단언컨대 그 같은 갑작스러운 집안 사건 뒤에 그녀는 몹시 편찮았을 것이다. 하물며 그녀보다 더 단단한 신경의 소유자라도 흔들어 놓았을 만한 일이었으니 말이다."], ["She had the street laid knee-deep with straw; and the knocker put by with Mr. Bowls's plate.", "적어도 뷰트 부인은 크롤리 양이 더없이 위중한 상태이며, 그러니 그에 맞추어 처신하라고 의사며 약제사며 말동무며 하인들에게 알리는 것이 자기 의무라 여겼다."], ["She insisted that the Doctor should call twice a day; and deluged her patient with draughts every two hours.", "그녀는 거리에 짚을 무릎 높이까지 깔게 했고, 문에 달린 노커는 보울스 씨의 식기와 함께 치워 두게 했다."], ["When anybody entered the room, she uttered a shshshsh so sibilant and ominous, that it frightened the poor old lady in her bed, from which she could not look without seeing Mrs.", "의사가 하루 두 번씩 들르도록 못을 박았고, 두 시간마다 환자에게 물약을 들이부었다. 누군가 방에 들어오기라도 하면 그녀는 \"쉬이이\""], ["Bute's beady eyes eagerly fixed on her, as the latter sate steadfast in the arm-chair by the bedside.", "하고 어찌나 새된 소리로, 또 어찌나 불길하게 입을 막던지, 침대에 누운 가엾은 노부인을 겁먹게 했다."], ["They seemed to lighten in the dark (for she kept the curtains closed) as she moved about the room on velvet paws like a cat. There Miss Crawley lay for days--ever so many days--Mr.", "노부인은 누운 채로 눈만 뜨면, 침대 곁 안락의자에 꼼짝 않고 앉은 뷰트 부인의 구슬 같은 눈이 자신에게 간절히 박혀 있는 것을 보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Bute reading books of devotion to her: for nights, long nights, during which she had to hear the watchman sing, the night-light sputter; visited at midnight, the last thing, by the stealthy apothecary; and then left to look at Mrs.", "그녀가 고양이처럼 벨벳 같은 발로 방 안을 돌아다닐 때면, 그 눈은 어둠 속에서 번득이는 듯했다(그녀가 커튼을 닫아 두었기 때문이다). 크롤리 양은 그렇게 며칠을, 그것도 한없이 여러 날을, 뷰트 씨가 신앙 서적을 읽어 주는 가운데 누워 있었다."], ["Bute's twinkling eyes, or the flicks of yellow that the rushlight threw on the dreary darkened ceiling.", "밤이면, 길고 긴 밤이면, 그녀는 야경꾼이 외치는 소리와 등잔 심지가 타닥거리는 소리를 들어야 했고, 한밤중 마지막 순서로 약제사가 발소리를 죽이고 다녀가면, 그저 뷰트 부인의 반짝이는 눈이나, 등심초 촛불이 음산하게 어두워진 천장에 던지는 노란 불빛의 가물거림을 바라보도록 남겨졌다."], ["Hygeia herself would have fallen sick under such a regimen; and how much more this poor old nervous victim?", "건강의 여신 히게이아 본인이라도 이런 양생법 아래에서라면 병이 났을 터인데, 하물며 이 가엾고 늙고 신경질적인 희생자야 오죽했겠는가?"], ["It has been said that when she was in health and good spirits, this venerable inhabitant of Vanity Fair had as free notions about religion and morals as Monsieur de Voltaire himself could desire, but when illness overtook her, it was aggravated by the most dreadful terrors of death, and an utter cowardice took possession of the prostrate old sinner.", "일찍이 이런 말이 있었다. 건강하고 기분이 좋을 때면 이 유서 깊은 허영의 시장 주민은 볼테르 씨조차 흡족해할 만큼 종교와 도덕에 대해 자유분방한 생각을 품었으나, 일단 병이 덮치면 죽음에 대한 더없이 끔찍한 공포가 그것을 한층 부채질했고, 드러누운 늙은 죄인은 완전히 겁쟁이가 되어 버렸다는 것이다."]], [["Sick-bed homilies and pious reflections are, to be sure, out of place in mere story-books, and we are not going (after the fashion of some novelists of the present day) to cajole the public into a sermon, when it is only a comedy that the reader pays his money to witness.", "병상에서의 설교와 경건한 성찰이란 한낱 이야기책에는 어울리지 않는 법이고, 우리는(요즘 어떤 소설가들이 하는 식으로) 독자가 그저 희극 한 편을 구경하려고 돈을 낸 마당에 설교를 들이밀어 대중을 어르려는 것이 아니다."], ["But, without preaching, the truth may surely be borne in mind, that the bustle, and triumph, and laughter, and gaiety which Vanity Fair exhibits in public, do not always pursue the performer into private life, and that the most dreary depression of spirits and dismal repentances sometimes overcome him.", "그러나 설교를 늘어놓지 않더라도, 이 진실만큼은 분명 마음에 새겨 둘 만하다. 허영의 시장이 사람들 앞에 펼쳐 보이는 그 부산함과 승리와 웃음과 흥겨움이, 언제나 그 연기자를 사사로운 삶에까지 따라가 주는 것은 아니며, 때로는 더없이 음울한 의기소침과 참담한 회한이 그를 덮치기도 한다는 것이다."], ["Recollection of the best ordained banquets will scarcely cheer sick epicures.", "더없이 잘 차려졌던 연회를 회상한들 병든 미식가의 기운을 북돋우기는 어려울 것이다."], ["Reminiscences of the most becoming dresses and brilliant ball triumphs will go very little way to console faded beauties.", "더없이 잘 어울리던 드레스며 무도회에서 거둔 눈부신 승리를 떠올린들 시든 미인을 위로하는 데는 거의 아무 소용이 없을 것이다."], ["Perhaps statesmen, at a particular period of existence, are not much gratified at thinking over the most triumphant divisions; and the success or the pleasure of yesterday becomes of very small account when a certain (albeit uncertain) morrow is in view, about which all of us must some day or other be speculating.", "어쩌면 정치가들도 인생의 어느 시기에 이르면, 가장 통쾌했던 표결을 곱씹어 본들 그다지 흐뭇하지 않을 것이다. 그리고 어떤 (비록 불확실하긴 해도) 확실한 내일이 눈앞에 보일 때면 어제의 성공이나 쾌락은 지극히 하찮은 것이 되고 마는 법이니, 그 내일에 대해서는 우리 모두가 언젠가는 헤아려 보아야 하는 것이다."], ["O brother wearers of motley!", "오, 어릿광대 옷을 함께 걸친 형제들이여!"], ["Are there not moments when one grows sick of grinning and tumbling, and the jingling of cap and bells?", "히죽거리고 재주넘는 일에, 또 광대 모자에 달린 방울 소리에 진력이 나는 순간이 어찌 없겠는가?"], ["This, dear friends and companions, is my amiable object--to walk with you through the Fair, to examine the shops and the shows there; and that we should all come home after the flare, and the noise, and the gaiety, and be perfectly miserable in private.", "사랑하는 벗이자 길동무들이여, 이것이 바로 나의 정겨운 목적이라네. 그대들과 함께 이 시장을 거닐며 그곳의 가게와 구경거리를 들여다보고, 그러다 그 번쩍임과 소란과 흥겨움이 다 지나고 나면 다 함께 집으로 돌아와, 저마다 홀로 더없이 비참해지자는 것 말일세."]], [["\"If that poor man of mine had a head on his shoulders,\"", "\"내 남편이라는 그 가엾은 사람이 어깨 위에 머리라는 걸 제대로 얹고만 있었어도,\""], ["Mrs.", "뷰트 크롤리 부인은 혼자 생각했다."], ["Bute Crawley thought to herself, \"how useful he might be, under present circumstances, to this unhappy old lady!", "\"지금 같은 상황에서 이 가엾은 노부인께 얼마나 쓸모가 있었을까! 그이라면 형님이 그 충격적인 자유사상의 버릇을 뉘우치게 만들 수도 있었을 테고, 의무를 다하시라고, 그래서 제 몸과 제 집안을 망신시킨 그 가증스러운 망나니를 내치시라고 다그칠 수도 있었을 거야."], ["He might make her repent of her shocking free-thinking ways; he might urge her to do her duty, and cast off that odious reprobate who has disgraced himself and his family; and he might induce her to do justice to my dear girls and the two boys, who require and deserve, I am sure, every assistance which their relatives can give them.\"", "그리고 우리 사랑스러운 딸들과 두 아들에게 마땅히 베푸셔야 할 정당한 몫을 베풀게 할 수도 있었을 테지. 그 애들이야말로 친척이 베풀 수 있는 온갖 도움을 필요로 하고, 또 분명 받을 자격이 있는 애들이니까.\""]], [["And, as the hatred of vice is always a progress towards virtue, Mrs.", "그리고 악덕을 미워하는 것은 언제나 미덕으로 나아가는 한 걸음이므로, 뷰트 크롤리 부인은 시누이에게 로던 크롤리의 그 갖가지 죄악에 대한 마땅한 혐오를 불어넣으려 애썼다."], ["Bute Crawley endeavoured to instil her sister-in-law a proper abhorrence for all Rawdon Crawley's manifold sins: of which his uncle's wife brought forward such a catalogue as indeed would have served to condemn a whole regiment of young officers.", "그 죄목으로 말하면 작은어머니 되는 이가 어찌나 줄줄이 늘어놓던지, 실로 젊은 장교 한 연대를 통째로 단죄하고도 남을 정도였다. 사람이 살면서 잘못을 저질렀을 때, 그 잘못을 세상에 일러바치는 데 자기 친척만큼 안달하는 도덕가를 나는 달리 알지 못한다."], ["If a man has committed wrong in life, I don't know any moralist more anxious to point his errors out to the world than his own relations; so Mrs.", "그리하여 뷰트 부인은 로던의 내력에 대해 완벽한 집안 식구다운 관심과 해박함을 드러내 보였다."], ["Bute showed a perfect family interest and knowledge of Rawdon's history.", "그녀는 마커 대위와 벌인 그 추악한 다툼의 전말을 죄다 꿰고 있었다."], ["She had all the particulars of that ugly quarrel with Captain Marker, in which Rawdon, wrong from the beginning, ended in shooting the Captain.", "처음부터 잘못은 로던에게 있었으나 끝내 그가 대위를 쏘아 버리고 만 사건 말이다. 또한 그녀는 가엾은 도브데일 경이 어떻게 망가졌는지도 알고 있었다."], ["She knew how the unhappy Lord Dovedale, whose mamma had taken a house at Oxford, so that he might be educated there, and who had never touched a card in his life till he came to London, was perverted by Rawdon at the Cocoa-Tree, made helplessly tipsy by this abominable seducer and perverter of youth, and fleeced of four thousand pounds.", "그의 어머니가 아들을 거기서 교육시키려고 옥스퍼드에 집을 한 채 얻었고, 그래서 런던에 오기 전까지는 평생 카드 한 장 만져 본 적 없던 그가, 코코아트리 클럽에서 로던에게 타락하여, 이 가증스러운 유혹자이자 청년을 망치는 자의 손에 어쩔 도리 없이 곤드레만드레 취해서는 사천 파운드를 뜯기고 만 것이다. 그녀는 그가 파멸시킨 시골 양가들의 고통을 더없이 생생하고 세세하게 그려 보였다."], ["She described with the most vivid minuteness the agonies of the country families whom he had ruined--the sons whom he had plunged into dishonour and poverty--the daughters whom he had inveigled into perdition.", "그가 불명예와 가난 속으로 처박은 아들들, 그가 꾀어 파멸로 몰아넣은 딸들을. 그녀는 그의 낭비로 파산한 가엾은 상인들도, 그가 그 낭비를 메우려 부린 치사한 술수와 사기 행각도, 더없이 너그러운 고모를 속여 넘긴 어처구니없는 거짓말도, 또 고모의 희생을 배은망덕과 조롱으로 갚은 일도 죄다 알고 있었다."], ["She knew the poor tradesmen who were bankrupt by his extravagance--the mean shifts and rogueries with which he had ministered to it--the astounding falsehoods by which he had imposed upon the most generous of aunts, and the ingratitude and ridicule by which he had repaid her sacrifices.", "그녀는 이런 이야기들을 조금씩 조금씩 크롤리 양에게 풀어놓았다. 그것을 빠짐없이 들려주었고, 그리스도교도이자 한 집안의 어머니로서 그리하는 것이 자신의 마땅한 의무라 여겼다."], ["She imparted these stories gradually to Miss Crawley; gave her the whole benefit of them; felt it to be her bounden duty as a Christian woman and mother of a family to do so; had not the smallest remorse or compunction for the victim whom her tongue was immolating; nay, very likely thought her act was quite meritorious, and plumed herself upon her resolute manner of performing it.", "자기 혀가 제물로 바치고 있는 그 희생자에 대해서는 손톱만큼의 가책이나 거리낌도 없었다. 아니, 십중팔구는 자기 행위가 자못 가상한 일이라 여기며, 그것을 단호하게 수행하는 자신의 처신에 우쭐했던 것이다."], ["Yes, if a man's character is to be abused, say what you will, there's nobody like a relation to do the business.", "그렇다, 누군가의 인격을 헐뜯으려 들 때면, 뭐라 해도 그 일에는 친척만 한 적임자가 없다. 그리고 이 가엾은 한심한 자 로던 크롤리에 관해서는 한 가지 인정하지 않을 수 없다."], ["And one is bound to own, regarding this unfortunate wretch of a Rawdon Crawley, that the mere truth was enough to condemn him, and that all inventions of scandal were quite superfluous pains on his friends' parts.", "곧 한낱 진실만으로도 그를 단죄하기에 충분했고, 온갖 추문을 꾸며내는 것은 그 친지들로서는 영 부질없는 수고였다는 점이다."]], [["Rebecca, too, being now a relative, came in for the fullest share of Mrs. Bute's kind inquiries.", "이제 친척이 된 레베카 역시 뷰트 부인의 자상한 탐문의 몫을 가장 톡톡히 차지했다."], ["This indefatigable pursuer of truth (having given strict orders that the door was to be denied to all emissaries or letters from Rawdon), took Miss Crawley's carriage, and drove to her old friend Miss Pinkerton, at Minerva House, Chiswick Mall, to whom she announced the dreadful intelligence of Captain Rawdon's seduction by Miss Sharp, and from whom she got sundry strange particulars regarding the ex-governess's birth and early history.", "이 지칠 줄 모르는 진실 추적자는(로던이 보내는 모든 밀사나 편지에 대해 문을 걸어 잠그라고 엄명을 내려 둔 뒤) 크롤리 양의 마차를 타고, 치즈윅 거리 미네르바 하우스에 있는 오랜 벗 핑커튼 양에게로 달려갔다. 그녀에게 로던 대위가 샤프 양에게 꾀임을 당했다는 끔찍한 소식을 알리는 동시에, 그 전직 가정교사의 출생과 어린 시절 내력에 관한 갖가지 기이한 사연을 캐냈다."], ["The friend of the Lexicographer had plenty of information to give.", "그 사전 편찬자의 벗에게는 들려줄 정보가 차고 넘쳤다."], ["Miss Jemima was made to fetch the drawing-master's receipts and letters.", "제미마 양은 그림 선생의 영수증과 편지를 가져오라는 분부를 받았다."], ["This one was from a spunging-house: that entreated an advance: another was full of gratitude for Rebecca's reception by the ladies of Chiswick: and the last document from the unlucky artist's pen was that in which, from his dying bed, he recommended his orphan child to Miss Pinkerton's protection.", "그중 하나는 채무자 임시 구류소에서 온 것이었고, 다른 하나는 돈을 좀 미리 달라고 사정하는 것이었으며, 또 하나는 레베카가 치즈윅 숙녀들에게 받아들여진 데 대한 감사로 가득했다. 그리고 그 불운한 화가의 펜에서 나온 마지막 서한은, 임종의 자리에서 자기 고아 딸을 핑커튼 양의 보호에 부탁한다는 것이었다."], ["There were juvenile letters and petitions from Rebecca, too, in the collection, imploring aid for her father or declaring her own gratitude.", "그 묶음 속에는 레베카가 어린 시절에 쓴 편지와 청원서도 있었는데, 아버지를 도와 달라 애원하거나 제 고마움을 토로하는 것들이었다."], ["Perhaps in Vanity Fair there are no better satires than letters.", "어쩌면 허영의 시장에서 편지만큼 훌륭한 풍자도 없을 것이다."], ["Take a bundle of your dear friend's of ten years back--your dear friend whom you hate now.", "십 년 전 그대의 소중한 벗이 보낸 편지 한 묶음을 꺼내 보라. 지금은 그대가 미워하는 그 소중한 벗 말이다."], ["Look at a file of your sister's!", "그대 누이의 편지철을 들여다보라."], ["how you clung to each other till you quarrelled about the twenty-pound legacy!", "그대들은 이십 파운드짜리 유산을 두고 다투기 전까지 어찌나 서로 매달려 살았던가!"], ["Get down the round-hand scrawls of your son who has half broken your heart with selfish undutifulness since; or a parcel of your own, breathing endless ardour and love eternal, which were sent back by your mistress when she married the Nabob--your mistress for whom you now care no more than for Queen Elizabeth.", "그 뒤로 이기적인 불효로 그대 가슴을 반쯤 찢어 놓은 아들이 또박또박 갈겨쓴 글씨를 꺼내 보라. 아니면 끝없는 열정과 영원한 사랑을 토로하는, 그대 자신이 보냈던 편지 꾸러미를 보라."], ["Vows, love, promises, confidences, gratitude, how queerly they read after a while!", "그것은 그대의 연인이 그 인도 갑부와 결혼하면서 그대에게 되돌려 보낸 것이다."], ["There ought to be a law in Vanity Fair ordering the destruction of every written document (except receipted tradesmen's bills) after a certain brief and proper interval.", "이제 와서는 엘리자베스 여왕만큼이나 아무런 마음도 가지 않는 그 연인 말이다. 맹세며 사랑이며 약속이며 속내며 감사며, 한참 지나고 나서 다시 읽으면 그 모든 것이 어찌나 기묘하게 읽히는가!"], ["Those quacks and misanthropes who advertise indelible Japan ink should be made to perish along with their wicked discoveries.", "허영의 시장에는, 적당히 짧고 합당한 기간이 지나면 (영수 처리된 상인의 청구서를 빼고) 모든 문서를 파기하라고 명하는 법이 하나쯤 있어야 마땅하다."], ["The best ink for Vanity Fair use would be one that faded utterly in a couple of days, and left the paper clean and blank, so that you might write on it to somebody else.", "지워지지 않는 일본제 먹물을 광고하는 그 돌팔이며 인간 혐오자들은 그 사악한 발명품과 함께 진작에 사라져야 할 것이다. 허영의 시장에서 쓰기에 가장 좋은 먹물은, 이틀쯤 지나면 흔적도 없이 바래어 종이를 깨끗하고 텅 빈 백지로 되돌려 줌으로써, 그 위에 다른 누군가에게 또 글을 쓸 수 있게 해 주는 그런 먹물일 것이다."]], [["From Miss Pinkerton's the indefatigable Mrs. Bute followed the track of Sharp and his daughter back to the lodgings in Greek Street, which the defunct painter had occupied; and where portraits of the landlady in white satin, and of the husband in brass buttons, done by Sharp in lieu of a quarter's rent, still decorated the parlour walls.", "핑커튼 양에게서 나온 지칠 줄 모르는 뷰트 부인은 샤프와 그 딸의 자취를 좇아, 그 작고한 화가가 살던 그리스 거리의 셋집까지 거슬러 올라갔다."], ["Mrs. Stokes was a communicative person, and quickly told all she knew about Mr.", "그곳 응접실 벽에는 샤프가 한 분기치 집세 대신 그려 준, 흰 새틴 차림의 안주인과 놋쇠 단추를 단 그 남편의 초상화가 여태 걸려 있었다."], ["Sharp; how dissolute and poor he was; how good-natured and amusing; how he was always hunted by bailiffs and duns; how, to the landlady's horror, though she never could abide the woman, he did not marry his wife till a short time before her death; and what a queer little wild vixen his daughter was; how she kept them all laughing with her fun and mimicry; how she used to fetch the gin from the public-house, and was known in all the studios in the quarter--in brief, Mrs.", "스토크스 부인은 말이 많은 사람이라, 샤프 씨에 관해 자기가 아는 것을 죄다 술술 털어놓았다. 그가 얼마나 방탕하고 가난했는지, 또 얼마나 사람 좋고 익살스러웠는지, 늘 집달리며 빚쟁이들에게 쫓겨 다녔는지, 또 안주인의 경악스럽게도 — 비록 그 여자라면 도무지 좋게 볼 수가 없었지만 — 죽기 얼마 전에야 비로소 제 아내와 정식으로 혼인을 했는지, 그리고 그 딸이 또 얼마나 기묘하고 자그마한 들고양이 같은 말괄량이였는지를 말이다."], ["Bute got such a full account of her new niece's parentage, education, and behaviour as would scarcely have pleased Rebecca, had the latter known that such inquiries were being made concerning her.", "그 애가 어찌나 재담과 흉내로 다들 웃겨 댔는지, 어떻게 술집에서 진을 받아 오곤 했으며, 동네 화실마다 모르는 이가 없었는지 — 요컨대 뷰트 부인은 새로 생긴 조카며느리의 가문과 교육과 행실에 관해 더없이 소상한 이야기를 얻어 냈으니, 만약 레베카가 자기에 관해 이런 탐문이 벌어지고 있음을 알았더라면 결코 달가워하지 않았을 그런 이야기였다."]], [["Of all these industrious researches Miss Crawley had the full benefit. Mrs.", "이 모든 부지런한 조사의 혜택은 고스란히 크롤리 양에게 돌아갔다."], ["Rawdon Crawley was the daughter of an opera-girl.", "로던 크롤리 부인은 오페라 무용수의 딸이었다."], ["She had danced herself.", "그녀 자신도 춤을 추었다."], ["She had been a model to the painters.", "화가들의 모델 노릇도 했다."], ["She was brought up as became her mother's daughter.", "그녀는 그 어미의 딸답게 자라났다."], ["She drank gin with her father, &c. &c.", "아비와 함께 진을 마셨다, 운운."], ["It was a lost woman who was married to a lost man; and the moral to be inferred from Mrs.", "그것은 타락한 남자와 결혼한 타락한 여자였다."], ["Bute's tale was, that the knavery of the pair was irremediable, and that no properly conducted person should ever notice them again.", "그리고 뷰트 부인의 이야기에서 끌어낼 교훈인즉, 이 한 쌍의 악행은 도저히 구제할 길이 없으며, 점잖게 처신하는 사람이라면 결코 다시는 그들을 아는 체해서는 안 된다는 것이었다."]], [["These were the materials which prudent Mrs. Bute gathered together in Park Lane, the provisions and ammunition as it were with which she fortified the house against the siege which she knew that Rawdon and his wife would lay to Miss Crawley.", "이것들이 바로 신중한 뷰트 부인이 파크 레인에서 그러모은 재료, 말하자면 로던 부부가 크롤리 양에게 가하리라 짐작되는 공성전에 대비해 집을 요새화하는 데 쓴 군량이자 탄약이었다."]], [["But if a fault may be found with her arrangements, it is this, that she was too eager: she managed rather too well; undoubtedly she made Miss Crawley more ill than was necessary; and though the old invalid succumbed to her authority, it was so harassing and severe, that the victim would be inclined to escape at the very first chance which fell in her way. Managing women, the ornaments of their sex--women who order everything for everybody, and know so much better than any person concerned what is good for their neighbours, don't sometimes speculate upon the possibility of a domestic revolt, or upon other extreme consequences resulting from their overstrained authority.", "그러나 그녀의 작전에 흠을 하나 잡을 수 있다면 바로 이것이다. 곧 그녀가 지나치게 서둘렀다는 점, 일을 좀 지나치게 잘 꾸려 나갔다는 점이다. 그녀는 의심할 여지 없이 크롤리 양을 필요 이상으로 더 앓게 만들었다. 그리고 그 늙은 환자가 그녀의 권위에 굴복하긴 했으나, 그 권위가 어찌나 괴롭히고 가혹했던지, 희생자는 처음 걸려드는 기회만 생기면 당장 달아나고 싶어졌던 것이다. 살림을 휘어잡는 여인들, 곧 제 성(性)의 자랑거리들 — 만사를 만인을 위해 척척 지시하고, 이웃에게 무엇이 좋은지를 정작 당사자보다도 훨씬 잘 안다는 그 여인들은, 더러 집안의 반란이 일어날 가능성이나, 도를 넘은 권위에서 비롯되는 그 밖의 극단적 결과에 대해서는 미처 헤아리지 못하는 법이다."]], [["Thus, for instance, Mrs. Bute, with the best intentions no doubt in the world, and wearing herself to death as she did by foregoing sleep, dinner, fresh air, for the sake of her invalid sister-in-law, carried her conviction of the old lady's illness so far that she almost managed her into her coffin.", "가령 뷰트 부인은, 두말할 것 없이 세상에서 가장 갸륵한 뜻을 품고서, 또 병든 시누이를 위해 잠도 끼니도 바깥바람도 마다하며 제 몸이 망가지도록 애쓰면서도, 노부인이 병중이라는 자신의 확신을 어찌나 멀리까지 밀어붙였던지, 하마터면 그녀를 관 속으로 떠밀어 넣을 뻔했다."], ["She pointed out her sacrifices and their results one day to the constant apothecary, Mr. Clump.", "어느 날 그녀는 한결같이 드나드는 약제사 클럼프 씨에게 자신의 희생과 그 성과를 일러 보였다."]], [["\"I am sure, my dear Mr. Clump,\"", "\"분명 말씀드리는데요, 클럼프 선생,\""], ["she said, \"no efforts of mine have been wanting to restore our dear invalid, whom the ingratitude of her nephew has laid on the bed of sickness.", "그녀가 말했다. \"조카의 배은망덕 탓에 병석에 누우신 우리 가엾은 환자분을 일으켜 세우려고, 저는 한순간도 정성을 게을리하지 않았답니다."], ["I never shrink from personal discomfort: I never refuse to sacrifice myself.\"", "저는 제 몸 불편한 것 따위 결코 마다하지 않아요. 결코 제 한 몸 바치기를 거절하지 않지요.\""]], [["\"Your devotion, it must be confessed, is admirable,\" Mr.", "\"부인의 헌신은, 두말할 것 없이 감탄할 만합니다.\""], ["Clump says, with a low bow; \"but--\"", "클럼프 씨가 깊이 허리를 숙이며 말했다. \"그러나—\""]], [["\"I have scarcely closed my eyes since my arrival: I give up sleep, health, every comfort, to my sense of duty.", "\"이곳에 온 뒤로 저는 거의 눈도 붙이지 못했어요. 의무감을 위해 잠도, 건강도, 온갖 안락도 다 내려놓았답니다."], ["When my poor James was in the smallpox, did I allow any hireling to nurse him?", "우리 가엾은 제임스가 천연두를 앓을 때, 제가 삯꾼 따위에게 그 애 간병을 맡기던가요?"], ["No.\"", "천만에요.\""]], [["\"You did what became an excellent mother, my dear Madam--the best of mothers; but--\"", "\"훌륭한 어머니라면, 그것도 더없이 훌륭한 어머니라면 마땅히 하실 일을 하신 거지요, 부인. 그러나—\""]], [["\"As the mother of a family and the wife of an English clergyman, I humbly trust that my principles are good,\"", "\"한 집안의 어미이자 영국 성직자의 아내로서, 제 신조만큼은 옳다고 감히 믿습니다.\""], ["Mrs. Bute said, with a happy solemnity of conviction; \"and, as long as Nature supports me, never, never, Mr.", "뷰트 부인이 확신에 찬 흐뭇하고 엄숙한 어조로 말했다."], ["Clump, will I desert the post of duty. Others may bring that grey head with sorrow to the bed of sickness (here Mrs.", "\"그러니 클럼프 선생, 하늘이 저를 지탱해 주는 한, 저는 결코, 결코 이 의무의 자리를 저버리지 않을 거예요."], ["Bute, waving her hand, pointed to one of old Miss Crawley's coffee-coloured fronts, which was perched on a stand in the dressing-room), but I will never quit it.", "다른 이들이야 저 잿빛 머리를 슬픔과 함께 병상으로 내몰지도 모르지만(여기서 뷰트 부인은 손을 내저으며, 화장실 받침대 위에 얹혀 있던 늙은 크롤리 양의 커피색 가발 한 점을 가리켰다), 저는 결코 이 자리를 뜨지 않겠어요."], ["Ah, Mr. Clump!", "아아, 클럼프 선생!"], ["I fear, I know, that the couch needs spiritual as well as medical consolation.\"", "저 병상에는 의술의 위로뿐 아니라 영혼의 위로도 필요하다는 것을, 저는 두렵지만 잘 알고 있답니다.\""]], [["\"What I was going to observe, my dear Madam,\"--here the resolute Clump once more interposed with a bland air--\"what I was going to observe when you gave utterance to sentiments which do you so much honour, was that I think you alarm yourself needlessly about our kind friend, and sacrifice your own health too prodigally in her favour.\"", "\"제가 말씀드리려던 것은요, 부인,\" 단호한 클럼프가 다시금 부드러운 태도로 끼어들었다. \"그토록 부인의 명예를 드높이는 심정을 토로하셨을 때 제가 말씀드리려던 것은, 부인께서 우리 자상한 벗에 대해 공연히 스스로를 놀라게 하시고, 또 그분을 위해 부인 자신의 건강을 너무 아낌없이 희생하고 계신다는 겁니다.\""]], [["\"I would lay down my life for my duty, or for any member of my husband's family,\"", "\"저는 제 의무를 위해서라면, 또 제 남편 집안의 어느 분을 위해서라면 목숨이라도 내놓겠어요.\""], ["Mrs. Bute interposed.", "뷰트 부인이 가로막으며 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, Madam, if need were; but we don't want Mrs.", "\"그렇지요, 부인, 정 그래야 한다면요."], ["Bute Crawley to be a martyr,\"", "하지만 우리는 뷰트 크롤리 부인께서 순교자가 되시기를 바라지 않습니다.\""], ["Clump said gallantly.", "클럼프가 정중하게 말했다."], ["\"Dr. Squills and myself have both considered Miss Crawley's case with every anxiety and care, as you may suppose.", "\"스퀼스 박사와 저는, 짐작하시다시피 온갖 걱정과 정성을 기울여 크롤리 양의 병세를 살펴보았습니다."], ["We see her low-spirited and nervous; family events have agitated her.\"", "보아하니 그분은 의기소침하고 신경이 예민해져 있어요. 집안일로 마음이 흔들리신 거지요.\""]], [["\"Her nephew will come to perdition,\"", "\"그 조카 녀석은 끝내 파멸하고 말 거예요.\""], ["Mrs. Crawley cried.", "크롤리 부인이 외쳤다."]], [["\"Have agitated her: and you arrived like a guardian angel, my dear Madam, a positive guardian angel, I assure you, to soothe her under the pressure of calamity.", "\"마음이 흔들리신 겁니다. 그리고 부인께서는 마치 수호천사처럼, 정말이지 더없는 수호천사처럼 오셔서, 재난의 압박 속에 계신 그분을 다독여 드린 거예요."], ["But Dr. Squills and I were thinking that our amiable friend is not in such a state as renders confinement to her bed necessary.", "그러나 스퀼스 박사와 저는, 우리 사랑스러운 벗께서 침상에만 누워 계셔야 할 만한 상태는 아니라고 생각하고 있습니다."], ["She is depressed, but this confinement perhaps adds to her depression.", "그분은 가라앉아 계시지만, 어쩌면 이렇게 갇혀 지내시는 것이 그 침울함을 더하는지도 모릅니다."], ["She should have change, fresh air, gaiety; the most delightful remedies in the pharmacopoeia,\"", "변화가, 바깥바람이, 즐거움이 필요해요. 약전(藥典)을 통틀어 가장 흐뭇한 처방이지요.\""], ["Mr. Clump said, grinning and showing his handsome teeth.", "클럼프 씨가 히죽 웃으며 그 잘생긴 이를 드러냈다."], ["\"Persuade her to rise, dear Madam; drag her from her couch and her low spirits; insist upon her taking little drives.", "\"일어나시라고 권해 보세요, 부인. 침상과 그 침울함에서 그분을 끌어내세요."], ["They will restore the roses too to your cheeks, if I may so speak to Mrs.", "잠깐씩이라도 마차를 타고 나가시라고 단단히 일러 두세요. 그러면 부인의 두 뺨에도 다시 장밋빛이 돌아올 겁니다."], ["Bute Crawley.\"", "뷰트 크롤리 부인께 이런 말씀을 드려도 된다면 말이지요.\""]], [["\"The sight of her horrid nephew casually in the Park, where I am told the wretch drives with the brazen partner of his crimes,\" Mrs.", "\"공원에서 우연히 그 끔찍한 조카 녀석과 마주치기라도 하면요."], ["Bute said (letting the cat of selfishness out of the bag of secrecy), \"would cause her such a shock, that we should have to bring her back to bed again.", "듣자 하니 그 망나니가 제 죄악의 뻔뻔스러운 동반자를 태우고 거길 다닌다던데.\" 뷰트 부인이 말했다(비밀의 자루 속에서 이기심이라는 고양이를 슬쩍 내보이며)."], ["She must not go out, Mr. Clump.", "\"그 충격에 그분은 다시 자리보전을 하셔야 할 거예요."], ["She shall not go out as long as I remain to watch over her; And as for my health, what matters it?", "그분은 바깥출입을 하시면 안 돼요, 클럼프 선생. 제가 곁에서 지켜드리는 한, 절대 못 나가십니다."], ["I give it cheerfully, sir.", "그리고 제 건강이야 무슨 대수겠어요?"], ["I sacrifice it at the altar of my duty.\"", "저는 기꺼이 그것을 바치겠어요, 선생. 의무의 제단에 그것을 제물로 올리겠습니다.\""]], [["\"Upon my word, Madam,\" Mr.", "\"솔직히 말씀드리면, 부인,\""], ["Clump now said bluntly, \"I won't answer for her life if she remains locked up in that dark room.", "이제 클럼프 씨가 단도직입으로 말했다. \"그분이 저 어두운 방 안에 갇혀 계신다면, 저는 그분의 목숨을 장담하지 못합니다."], ["She is so nervous that we may lose her any day; and if you wish Captain Crawley to be her heir, I warn you frankly, Madam, that you are doing your very best to serve him.\"", "그분은 신경이 어찌나 예민한지, 언제 어느 날 우리가 그분을 잃을지 모릅니다. 그리고 만약 부인께서 크롤리 대위가 상속자가 되기를 바라신다면, 솔직히 경고드리는데, 부인께서는 그 대위를 위해 지금 최선을 다하고 계신 셈입니다.\""]], [["\"Gracious mercy!", "\"맙소사!"], ["is her life in danger?\"", "그분 목숨이 위태롭다는 건가요?\""], ["Mrs. Bute cried.", "뷰트 부인이 외쳤다."], ["\"Why, why, Mr. Clump, did you not inform me sooner?\"", "\"어쩌자고, 어쩌자고 클럼프 선생, 더 일찍 제게 알려 주지 않으셨어요?\""]], [["The night before, Mr. Clump and Dr. Squills had had a consultation (over a bottle of wine at the house of Sir Lapin Warren, whose lady was about to present him with a thirteenth blessing), regarding Miss Crawley and her case.", "그 전날 밤, 클럼프 씨와 스퀼스 박사는 (래핀 워런 경의 집에서 와인 한 병을 기울이며 — 그 댁 부인이 마침 그에게 열세 번째 경사를 안겨 드리려던 참이었다) 크롤리 양과 그 병세에 관해 의논을 나눈 터였다."]], [["\"What a little harpy that woman from Hampshire is, Clump,\"", "\"늙은 틸리 크롤리를 단단히 거머쥔 그 햄프셔 여자 말이야, 클럼프, 어찌나 자그마한 독수리 같은지.\""], ["Squills remarked, \"that has seized upon old Tilly Crawley.", "스퀼스가 한마디 했다."], ["Devilish good Madeira.\"", "\"이 마데이라 와인 한번 끝내주는군.\""]], [["\"What a fool Rawdon Crawley has been,\"", "\"로던 크롤리도 참 멍청하지.\""], ["Clump replied, \"to go and marry a governess!", "클럼프가 받았다. \"가정교사 따위와 결혼을 하다니!"], ["There was something about the girl, too.\"", "하기야 그 계집애한테도 뭔가가 있긴 했지.\""]], [["\"Green eyes, fair skin, pretty figure, famous frontal development,\"", "\"초록빛 눈에, 흰 살결에, 어여쁜 몸매에, 게다가 그 유명한 앞가슴의 발육이라니.\""], ["Squills remarked.", "스퀼스가 한마디 했다."], ["\"There is something about her; and Crawley was a fool, Squills.\"", "\"확실히 그 계집애한텐 뭔가가 있어. 그리고 크롤리는 멍청이였지, 스퀼스.\""]], [["\"A d---- fool--always was,\"", "\"빌어먹을 멍청이지, 늘 그랬고말고.\""], ["the apothecary replied.", "약제사가 받았다."]], [["\"Of course the old girl will fling him over,\"", "\"당연히 그 노친네는 그자를 내칠 거야.\""], ["said the physician, and after a pause added, \"She'll cut up well, I suppose.\"", "의사가 말했고, 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 덧붙였다. \"그 양반, 죽고 나면 제법 알차게 나눠질 텐데.\""]], [["\"Cut up,\"", "\"나눠지긴.\""], ["says Clump with a grin; \"I wouldn't have her cut up for two hundred a year.\"", "클럼프가 히죽 웃으며 말했다. \"나라면 연 이백 파운드를 준대도 그 양반이 나눠지는 꼴은 보고 싶지 않은데.\""]], [["\"That Hampshire woman will kill her in two months, Clump, my boy, if she stops about her,\"", "\"그 햄프셔 여자가 곁에 붙어 있다간 두 달 안에 그 양반을 죽이고 말 걸세, 클럼프 군.\""], ["Dr. Squills said.", "스퀼스 박사가 말했다."], ["\"Old woman; full feeder; nervous subject; palpitation of the heart; pressure on the brain; apoplexy; off she goes.", "\"늙은 데다, 잘 먹고, 신경은 예민하고, 심장은 두근거리고, 뇌에는 압박이 가고, 그러다 졸중이 오면, 그걸로 끝장이지."], ["Get her up, Clump; get her out: or I wouldn't give many weeks'", "어서 일으켜 세우게, 클럼프. 바깥으로 내보내게."], ["purchase for your two hundred a year.\"", "안 그러면 자네 그 연 이백 파운드, 앞으로 몇 주치 값어치도 못 쳐줄 테니.\""], ["And it was acting upon this hint that the worthy apothecary spoke with so much candour to Mrs. Bute Crawley.", "그리고 바로 이 귀띔에 따라, 이 갸륵한 약제사는 뷰트 크롤리 부인에게 그토록 솔직하게 말을 건넸던 것이다."]], [["Having the old lady under her hand: in bed: with nobody near, Mrs. Bute had made more than one assault upon her, to induce her to alter her will.", "침대에 누워 곁에 아무도 없는 노부인을 제 손아귀에 쥔 채로, 뷰트 부인은 그녀에게 유언장을 고치도록 부추기려고 한두 차례가 아니게 공세를 펼쳤다."], ["But Miss Crawley's usual terrors regarding death increased greatly when such dismal propositions were made to her, and Mrs.", "그러나 그 음울한 제안이 나올 때마다 죽음에 대한 크롤리 양의 평소 공포는 한층 더 커지기만 했다."], ["Bute saw that she must get her patient into cheerful spirits and health before she could hope to attain the pious object which she had in view.", "그래서 뷰트 부인은 자신이 마음에 품은 그 경건한 목적을 이루려면, 먼저 환자를 명랑한 기분과 건강한 상태로 만들어 놓아야겠다는 것을 깨달았다."], ["Whither to take her was the next puzzle.", "다음 골칫거리는 그녀를 어디로 데려가느냐 하는 것이었다."], ["The only place where she is not likely to meet those odious Rawdons is at church, and that won't amuse her, Mrs.", "그 가증스러운 로던 부부와 마주칠 염려가 없는 곳이라고는 교회뿐인데, 거기서는 흥이 날 리 없다고 뷰트 부인은 옳게 판단했다. \"런던의 그 아름다운 교외로 나가 봐야겠어.\""], ["Bute justly felt.", "그녀는 그렇게 생각했다."], ["\"We must go and visit our beautiful suburbs of London,\" she then thought.", "\"거기가 세상에서 가장 그림 같은 곳이라던데.\""], ["\"I hear they are the most picturesque in the world\"; and so she had a sudden interest for Hampstead, and Hornsey, and found that Dulwich had great charms for her, and getting her victim into her carriage, drove her to those rustic spots, beguiling the little journeys with conversations about Rawdon and his wife, and telling every story to the old lady which could add to her indignation against this pair of reprobates.", "그리하여 그녀는 별안간 햄스테드와 혼지에 흥미를 갖게 되었고, 덜위치가 자기에게 큰 매력으로 다가오는 것을 발견했다. 그러고는 희생자를 마차에 태워 그 한적한 곳들로 몰고 다녔는데, 그 짧은 여정마다 로던과 그 아내에 관한 이야기로 시간을 때웠고, 이 한 쌍의 망나니에 대한 노부인의 분노를 한층 돋울 만한 이야기란 이야기는 죄다 들려주었다."]], [["Perhaps Mrs. Bute pulled the string unnecessarily tight.", "어쩌면 뷰트 부인은 고삐를 공연히 너무 바짝 당겼는지도 모른다."], ["For though she worked up Miss Crawley to a proper dislike of her disobedient nephew, the invalid had a great hatred and secret terror of her victimizer, and panted to escape from her.", "그녀가 크롤리 양을 부추겨 그 말 안 듣는 조카를 마땅히 싫어하게 만들긴 했으나, 정작 그 환자는 자신을 괴롭히는 자에게 깊은 증오와 은밀한 공포를 품은 채 그녀에게서 벗어나기를 갈망하고 있었던 것이다."], ["After a brief space, she rebelled against Highgate and Hornsey utterly.", "얼마 지나지 않아 노부인은 하이게이트며 혼지에 완전히 반기를 들었다."], ["She would go into the Park. Mrs.", "그녀는 공원으로 가겠다고 했다."], ["Bute knew they would meet the abominable Rawdon there, and she was right.", "뷰트 부인은 거기서 그 가증스러운 로던과 마주치게 되리라는 것을 알았는데, 과연 그녀의 짐작이 맞았다."], ["One day in the ring, Rawdon's stanhope came in sight; Rebecca was seated by him.", "어느 날 마차길에서 로던의 이륜마차가 시야에 들어왔다. 레베카가 그 곁에 앉아 있었다."], ["In the enemy's equipage Miss Crawley occupied her usual place, with Mrs.", "적의 마차에는 크롤리 양이 여느 때의 자리를 차지하고 있었고, 왼편에는 뷰트 부인이, 뒷좌석에는 푸들과 브리그스 양이 있었다."], ["Bute on her left, the poodle and Miss Briggs on the back seat.", "마음 졸이는 순간이었다. 레베카는 그 마차를 알아보자 가슴이 빠르게 뛰었다."], ["It was a nervous moment, and Rebecca's heart beat quick as she recognized the carriage; and as the two vehicles crossed each other in a line, she clasped her hands, and looked towards the spinster with a face of agonized attachment and devotion.", "그리고 두 마차가 한 줄로 엇갈리는 순간, 그녀는 두 손을 마주 잡고서, 애절한 애정과 헌신이 어린 얼굴로 그 노처녀 쪽을 바라보았다."], ["Rawdon himself trembled, and his face grew purple behind his dyed mustachios.", "로던 본인도 부르르 떨었고, 물들인 콧수염 위로 얼굴이 검붉게 달아올랐다."], ["Only old Briggs was moved in the other carriage, and cast her great eyes nervously towards her old friends.", "저쪽 마차에서 마음이 움직인 사람이라고는 늙은 브리그스뿐이어서, 그녀는 그 큰 눈을 불안하게 옛 벗들 쪽으로 던졌다."], ["Miss Crawley's bonnet was resolutely turned towards the Serpentine.", "크롤리 양의 보닛은 단호하게 서펜타인 호수 쪽으로 돌려져 있었다."], ["Mrs. Bute happened to be in ecstasies with the poodle, and was calling him a little darling, and a sweet little zoggy, and a pretty pet.", "뷰트 부인은 마침 푸들에게 푹 빠져, 그놈을 작은 귀염둥이라느니, 어여쁜 멍멍이라느니, 예쁜 강아지라느니 불러 대고 있었다."], ["The carriages moved on, each in his line.", "마차는 저마다 제 줄을 따라 계속 나아갔다."]], [["\"Done, by Jove,\"", "\"틀렸어, 젠장.\""], ["Rawdon said to his wife.", "로던이 아내에게 말했다."]], [["\"Try once more, Rawdon,\"", "\"한 번만 더 해 봐요, 로던.\""], ["Rebecca answered.", "레베카가 대답했다."], ["\"Could not you lock your wheels into theirs, dearest?\"", "\"여보, 우리 마차 바퀴를 저쪽 바퀴에 걸어 버릴 순 없어요?\""]], [["Rawdon had not the heart for that manoeuvre.", "로던은 그런 술책을 부릴 만한 배짱이 없었다."], ["When the carriages met again, he stood up in his stanhope; he raised his hand ready to doff his hat; he looked with all his eyes.", "두 마차가 다시 마주쳤을 때, 그는 이륜마차 안에서 벌떡 일어섰다. 모자를 벗어 들 채비로 손을 들어 올렸다."], ["But this time Miss Crawley's face was not turned away; she and Mrs.", "두 눈을 부릅뜨고 바라보았다. 그러나 이번에는 크롤리 양의 얼굴이 돌려져 있지 않았다."], ["Bute looked him full in the face, and cut their nephew pitilessly.", "그녀와 뷰트 부인은 그의 얼굴을 정면으로 빤히 쳐다보면서, 인정사정없이 조카를 모른 체했다."], ["He sank back in his seat with an oath, and striking out of the ring, dashed away desperately homewards.", "그는 욕설을 내뱉으며 자리에 털썩 주저앉았고, 마차길에서 벗어나더니 절망에 차 집을 향해 내달렸다."]], [["It was a gallant and decided triumph for Mrs. Bute.", "그것은 뷰트 부인으로서는 늠름하고도 결정적인 승리였다."], ["But she felt the danger of many such meetings, as she saw the evident nervousness of Miss Crawley; and she determined that it was most necessary for her dear friend's health, that they should leave town for a while, and recommended Brighton very strongly.", "그러나 그녀는 크롤리 양의 역력한 불안을 보며 그런 마주침이 거듭되는 데 따르는 위험을 느꼈다. 그리하여 사랑하는 벗의 건강을 위해서는 한동안 도성을 떠나는 것이 무엇보다 긴요하다고 마음먹고는, 브라이턴을 아주 강력히 권했다."]], [["In Which Captain Dobbin Acts as the Messenger of Hymen\nWithout knowing how, Captain William Dobbin found himself the great promoter, arranger, and manager of the match between George Osborne and Amelia.", "윌리엄 도빈 대위는 자기도 어찌 된 영문인지 모르는 사이에, 조지 오스본과 애밀리아의 혼인을 추진하고 주선하고 관리하는 막중한 역할을 떠맡고 있는 자신을 발견했다."], ["But for him it never would have taken place: he could not but confess as much to himself, and smiled rather bitterly as he thought that he of all men in the world should be the person upon whom the care of this marriage had fallen.", "그가 아니었다면 이 일은 결코 성사되지 않았을 것이다. 그 점만큼은 스스로도 인정하지 않을 수 없었고, 세상 모든 남자들 가운데 하필 자기에게 이 혼사의 책임이 떨어졌다는 생각에 그는 다소 씁쓸하게 웃었다."], ["But though indeed the conducting of this negotiation was about as painful a task as could be set to him, yet when he had a duty to perform, Captain Dobbin was accustomed to go through it without many words or much hesitation: and, having made up his mind completely, that if Miss Sedley was balked of her husband she would die of the disappointment, he was determined to use all his best endeavours to keep her alive.", "그러나 이 협상을 끌고 나가는 일이 그에게 주어질 수 있는 임무 중 가장 고통스러운 것이긴 했어도, 도빈 대위는 일단 수행해야 할 의무가 있으면 말을 많이 하거나 망설이는 일 없이 끝까지 밀고 나가는 사람이었다. 그리고 만약 세들리 양이 남편을 빼앗긴다면 그 실망감에 죽고 말리라고 완전히 마음을 굳힌 그는, 그녀를 살려 두기 위해 자신이 가진 온 힘을 다하기로 작정했던 것이다."]], [["I forbear to enter into minute particulars of the interview between George and Amelia, when the former was brought back to the feet (or should we venture to say the arms?) of his young mistress by the intervention of his friend honest William.", "정직한 윌리엄, 그 벗의 중재로 조지가 다시 어린 연인의 발치(아니, 차라리 품이라 해야 할까?)로 돌아왔을 때, 조지와 애밀리아 사이에 오간 면담의 시시콜콜한 내막까지는 적지 않으련다."], ["A much harder heart than George's would have melted at the sight of that sweet face so sadly ravaged by grief and despair, and at the simple tender accents in which she told her little broken-hearted story: but as she did not faint when her mother, trembling, brought Osborne to her; and as she only gave relief to her overcharged grief, by laying her head on her lover's shoulder and there weeping for a while the most tender, copious, and refreshing tears--old Mrs.", "조지보다 훨씬 모진 마음이라도, 슬픔과 절망으로 처연하게 망가진 그 고운 얼굴을 보고, 또 가슴이 미어지는 자신의 작은 이야기를 들려주는 그 소박하고 애틋한 음성을 들었다면 녹아내리고 말았을 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 어머니가 떨면서 오스본을 데려왔을 때 기절하지 않았고, 그저 연인의 어깨에 머리를 기댄 채 한동안 더없이 애틋하고, 흠뻑 쏟아지고, 마음을 씻어 주는 눈물을 흘리며 북받친 슬픔을 달랬을 뿐이었다."], ["Sedley, too greatly relieved, thought it was best to leave the young persons to themselves; and so quitted Emmy crying over George's hand, and kissing it humbly, as if he were her supreme chief and master, and as if she were quite a guilty and unworthy person needing every favour and grace from him.", "그래서 늙은 세들리 부인은 너무도 안도한 나머지 젊은 두 사람만 남겨 두는 것이 상책이라 여겼다. 에미가 조지의 손에 엎드려 울며, 마치 그가 자기 지존의 주인이기라도 한 듯, 또 자기는 그에게서 온갖 은혜와 자비를 구걸해야 할 죄 많고 보잘것없는 사람이기라도 한 듯 겸손하게 그 손에 입 맞추는 모습을 뒤로하고 부인은 자리를 떴다."]], [["This prostration and sweet unrepining obedience exquisitely touched and flattered George Osborne.", "이 엎드린 자세, 그리고 불평 한마디 없는 이 달콤한 순종은 조지 오스본의 마음을 더없이 절묘하게 건드리고 흐뭇하게 했다."], ["He saw a slave before him in that simple yielding faithful creature, and his soul within him thrilled secretly somehow at the knowledge of his power.", "그는 저 소박하고 고분고분하고 충직한 존재 앞에서 한 노예를 보았고, 자신의 권능을 깨닫는 순간 그의 영혼은 왠지 은밀하게 짜릿한 전율에 휩싸였다."], ["He would be generous-minded, Sultan as he was, and raise up this kneeling Esther and make a queen of her: besides, her sadness and beauty touched him as much as her submission, and so he cheered her, and raised her up and forgave her, so to speak.", "그는 비록 군주이긴 하나 너그러운 마음을 베풀어, 무릎 꿇은 이 에스더를 일으켜 세워 그녀를 왕비로 만들어 주리라. 게다가 그녀의 슬픔과 아름다움도 그 순종 못지않게 그의 마음을 움직였으니, 그리하여 그는 그녀를 다독이고, 일으켜 세우고, 말하자면 용서해 주었던 것이다."], ["All her hopes and feelings, which were dying and withering, this her sun having been removed from her, bloomed again and at once, its light being restored.", "자신의 태양이 사라진 탓에 시들어 죽어 가던 그녀의 온갖 희망과 감정은, 그 빛이 되돌아오자 일시에 다시 활짝 피어났다."], ["You would scarcely have recognised the beaming little face upon Amelia's pillow that night as the one that was laid there the night before, so wan, so lifeless, so careless of all round about.", "그날 밤 애밀리아의 베개에 누인, 환하게 빛나는 그 작은 얼굴을, 그토록 핼쑥하고, 그토록 생기 없고, 그토록 만사에 무심한 채 전날 밤 그 자리에 뉘었던 얼굴과 같은 것이라고는 차마 알아보기 어려웠을 것이다."], ["The honest Irish maid-servant, delighted with the change, asked leave to kiss the face that had grown all of a sudden so rosy.", "그 변화를 보고 기뻐한 정직한 아일랜드인 하녀는 갑자기 그토록 발그레해진 그 얼굴에 입 맞추게 해 달라고 청했다."], ["Amelia put her arms round the girl's neck and kissed her with all her heart, like a child.", "애밀리아는 두 팔로 그 처녀의 목을 감싸 안고 마치 어린아이처럼 온 마음을 다해 입 맞추었다."], ["She was little more.", "사실 그녀는 어린아이나 다름없었다."], ["She had that night a sweet refreshing sleep, like one--and what a spring of inexpressible happiness as she woke in the morning sunshine!", "그날 밤 그녀는 달게 잠들어 한껏 원기를 되찾았고, 아침 햇살 속에서 깨어났을 때 그 가슴에 차오른 형언할 수 없는 행복의 샘물이란!"]], [["\"He will be here again to-day,\"", "\"오늘 또 오실 거야.\""], ["Amelia thought.", "애밀리아는 생각했다."], ["\"He is the greatest and best of men.\"", "\"그분은 세상에서 가장 위대하고 가장 훌륭한 분이야.\""], ["And the fact is, that George thought he was one of the generousest creatures alive: and that he was making a tremendous sacrifice in marrying this young creature.", "그리고 사실인즉, 조지는 자기야말로 살아 있는 가장 너그러운 사람 가운데 하나이며, 이 어린 처녀와 결혼함으로써 어마어마한 희생을 치르고 있는 셈이라고 생각하는 것이었다."]], [["While she and Osborne were having their delightful tete-a-tete above stairs, old Mrs. Sedley and Captain Dobbin were conversing below upon the state of the affairs, and the chances and future arrangements of the young people.", "그녀와 오스본이 위층에서 즐거운 단둘만의 시간을 보내는 동안, 늙은 세들리 부인과 도빈 대위는 아래층에서 일의 형편이며, 젊은 두 사람의 앞날의 가능성이며 향후 준비에 관해 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["Mrs. Sedley having brought the two lovers together and left them embracing each other with all their might, like a true woman, was of opinion that no power on earth would induce Mr.", "두 연인을 한자리에 데려다 놓고는 힘껏 서로 부둥켜안도록 내버려 두고 나온 세들리 부인은, 영락없는 여인답게, 세상 그 어떤 힘으로도 세들리 씨를 설득해 딸과, 자신을 그토록 수치스럽고, 사악하고, 흉악하게 대한 사내의 아들 사이의 혼인에 동의하게 만들 수는 없으리라는 의견이었다."], ["Sedley to consent to the match between his daughter and the son of a man who had so shamefully, wickedly, and monstrously treated him. And she told a long story about happier days and their earlier splendours, when Osborne lived in a very humble way in the New Road, and his wife was too glad to receive some of Jos's little baby things, with which Mrs.", "그러면서 부인은 더 행복했던 시절과 지난날의 영화에 대해 길게 이야기를 늘어놓았는데, 그 시절 오스본은 뉴로드에서 아주 초라하게 살았고, 그의 아내는 세들리 부인이 오스본네 아이 하나가 태어났을 때 마련해 준 조스의 자그마한 배냇저고리 따위를 기꺼이 받아 들고 좋아했다는 것이었다."], ["Sedley accommodated her at the birth of one of Osborne's own children.", "그 사내의 악마 같은 배은망덕이 세들리 씨의 가슴을 찢어 놓은 게 분명하다고 부인은 확신했다."], ["The fiendish ingratitude of that man, she was sure, had broken Mr. S.'s heart: and as for a marriage, he would never, never, never, never consent.", "그리고 혼인으로 말할 것 같으면, 남편은 결단코, 결단코, 결단코, 결단코 동의하지 않으리라는 것이었다."]], [["\"They must run away together, Ma'am,\"", "\"두 사람이 같이 도망쳐야겠군요, 부인.\""], ["Dobbin said, laughing, \"and follow the example of Captain Rawdon Crawley, and Miss Emmy's friend the little governess.\"", "도빈이 웃으며 말했다. \"로던 크롤리 대위하고, 에미 양의 벗인 그 자그마한 가정교사 본을 따라서 말이지요.\""], ["Was it possible?", "그게 정말일까?"], ["Well she never!", "세상에, 어쩜!"], ["Mrs. Sedley was all excitement about this news.", "세들리 부인은 이 소식에 잔뜩 흥분했다."], ["She wished that Blenkinsop were here to hear it: Blenkinsop always mistrusted that Miss Sharp.-- What an escape Jos had had!", "부인은 블렌킨숍이 여기 있어 이 이야기를 들었으면 했다. 블렌킨숍은 늘 그 샤프 양을 미심쩍게 여겼던 것이다."], ["and she described the already well-known love-passages between Rebecca and the Collector of Boggley Wollah.", "조스가 얼마나 다행스럽게 위기를 모면했는지! 그러면서 부인은 이미 다 알려진 레베카와 보글리 월라 징세관 사이의 연애담을 늘어놓았다."]], [["It was not, however, Mr. Sedley's wrath which Dobbin feared, so much as that of the other parent concerned, and he owned that he had a very considerable doubt and anxiety respecting the behaviour of the black-browed old tyrant of a Russia merchant in Russell Square.", "그러나 도빈이 두려워한 것은 세들리 씨의 노여움이라기보다, 이 일에 얽힌 다른 한쪽 아버지의 노여움이었으니, 그는 러셀 광장에 사는, 눈썹 검은 늙은 폭군 같은 러시아 무역상이 어떻게 나올지에 대해 적잖은 의심과 불안을 품고 있음을 스스로 인정했다."], ["He has forbidden the match peremptorily, Dobbin thought.", "그는 이 혼사를 단호히 금지했어, 도빈은 생각했다."], ["He knew what a savage determined man Osborne was, and how he stuck by his word.", "그는 오스본이 얼마나 야만스럽고 고집스러운 사람인지, 또 한번 내뱉은 말을 얼마나 끝까지 고수하는지 잘 알고 있었다."], ["\"The only chance George has of reconcilement,\"", "\"조지가 화해할 수 있는 유일한 길은,\""], ["argued his friend, \"is by distinguishing himself in the coming campaign.", "그의 벗은 따져 보았다, \"이번 출정에서 두각을 나타내는 거야."], ["If he dies they both go together.", "만일 그가 죽으면 둘은 함께 가는 셈이고."], ["If he fails in distinction--what then?", "만일 두각을 나타내는 데 실패하면, 그땐 어찌 될까?"], ["He has some money from his mother, I have heard enough to purchase his majority--or he must sell out and go and dig in Canada, or rough it in a cottage in the country.\"", "그 어머니에게서 받은 돈이 좀 있으니, 듣자 하니 소령직을 사기엔 충분하겠지. 아니면 군적을 팔고 캐나다로 가서 땅을 일구거나, 시골 오두막에서 고생스럽게 살아야겠지.\""], ["With such a partner Dobbin thought he would not mind Siberia--and, strange to say, this absurd and utterly imprudent young fellow never for a moment considered that the want of means to keep a nice carriage and horses, and of an income which should enable its possessors to entertain their friends genteelly, ought to operate as bars to the union of George and Miss Sedley.", "그런 반려와 함께라면 시베리아라도 마다하지 않으리라고 도빈은 생각했다. 그리고 이상한 말이지만, 이 터무니없고 전혀 분별없는 젊은 친구는, 번듯한 마차와 말을 건사할 재력이 없다는 것, 또 그 소유주가 벗들을 품위 있게 대접할 만한 수입이 없다는 것이 조지와 세들리 양의 결합을 가로막는 장애가 되어야 마땅하다는 생각은 단 한순간도 하지 않았던 것이다."]], [["It was these weighty considerations which made him think too that the marriage should take place as quickly as possible.", "혼인이 되도록 빨리 이루어져야 한다고 그가 생각하게 된 것도 바로 이런 무게 있는 고려들 때문이었다."], ["Was he anxious himself, I wonder, to have it over?--as people, when death has occurred, like to press forward the funeral, or when a parting is resolved upon, hasten it.", "그 자신도 이 일을 어서 끝내 버리고 싶어 안달했던 것일까, 나는 궁금하다. 사람들이 누가 죽으면 장례를 어서 치르려 하고, 이별이 정해지면 그것을 서두르듯이 말이다."], ["It is certain that Mr. Dobbin, having taken the matter in hand, was most extraordinarily eager in the conduct of it.", "어쨌든 도빈 씨가 일단 이 일을 손에 쥐고 나서는 그 처리에 유난히도 열을 올렸음은 분명하다."], ["He urged on George the necessity of immediate action: he showed the chances of reconciliation with his father, which a favourable mention of his name in the Gazette must bring about.", "그는 조지에게 즉각 행동에 나설 필요가 있음을 역설했다. 관보에 그의 이름이 좋게 거론되기만 하면 아버지와 화해할 가능성이 생긴다는 점을 일깨워 주었다."], ["If need were he would go himself and brave both the fathers in the business.", "필요하다면 자기가 직접 가서 이 일로 양쪽 아버지 모두에게 맞서겠노라 했다."], ["At all events, he besought George to go through with it before the orders came, which everybody expected, for the departure of the regiment from England on foreign service.", "어쨌든 그는, 누구나 예상하고 있던, 연대가 영국을 떠나 해외 근무에 나서라는 명령이 떨어지기 전에 이 일을 매듭짓기를 조지에게 간곡히 청했다."]], [["Bent upon these hymeneal projects, and with the applause and consent of Mrs. Sedley, who did not care to break the matter personally to her husband, Mr.", "이 혼인 계획에 골몰한 채, 그리고 남편에게 직접 이 일을 꺼낼 엄두가 나지 않았던 세들리 부인의 찬동과 동의를 얻은 도빈 씨는, 시내에 있는 존 세들리의 단골 출입처인 타피오카 커피하우스로 그를 찾아 나섰다."], ["Dobbin went to seek John Sedley at his house of call in the City, the Tapioca Coffee-house, where, since his own offices were shut up, and fate had overtaken him, the poor broken-down old gentleman used to betake himself daily, and write letters and receive them, and tie them up into mysterious bundles, several of which he carried in the flaps of his coat.", "자기 사무소가 문을 닫고 운명의 손길이 그를 덮친 이래로, 그 가엾고 영락한 노신사는 매일 그곳으로 향하곤 했으니, 편지를 쓰고 또 받아서는 그것을 수상쩍은 꾸러미로 묶어 두었고, 그 가운데 여러 묶음을 외투 자락 안에 넣고 다녔다."], ["I don't know anything more dismal than that business and bustle and mystery of a ruined man: those letters from the wealthy which he shows you: those worn greasy documents promising support and offering condolence which he places wistfully before you, and on which he builds his hopes of restoration and future fortune.", "나는 파산한 사람의 그 분주함이며 부산함이며 비밀스러움보다 더 음울한 것을 알지 못한다. 그가 보여 주는 부유한 이들의 편지들, 후원을 약속하고 위로를 건네는 그 닳고 기름때 묻은 서류들을, 그는 애처롭게 당신 앞에 펼쳐 놓고는 그 위에 재기와 미래의 부에 대한 희망을 쌓아 올리는 것이다."], ["My beloved reader has no doubt in the course of his experience been waylaid by many such a luckless companion.", "나의 사랑하는 독자께서도 살아오면서 분명 이런 운수 사나운 동무에게 숱하게 붙들려 보았을 것이다."], ["He takes you into the corner; he has his bundle of papers out of his gaping coat pocket; and the tape off, and the string in his mouth, and the favourite letters selected and laid before you; and who does not know the sad eager half-crazy look which he fixes on you with his hopeless eyes?", "그는 당신을 구석으로 끌고 가서는, 헤벌어진 외투 주머니에서 서류 뭉치를 꺼내고, 묶은 끈을 풀어 입에 문 채, 아끼는 편지들을 골라 당신 앞에 펼쳐 놓는다. 그 절망에 찬 눈으로 당신을 응시하는 그 서글프고 간절하고 반쯤 미친 듯한 표정을, 모르는 이가 어디 있겠는가?"]], [["Changed into a man of this sort, Dobbin found the once florid, jovial, and prosperous John Sedley.", "도빈이 마주한 이는, 한때 혈색 좋고, 쾌활하고, 번창했던 존 세들리가 바로 이런 부류의 사내로 변해 버린 모습이었다."], ["His coat, that used to be so glossy and trim, was white at the seams, and the buttons showed the copper.", "그토록 윤기 흐르고 단정하던 외투는 솔기마다 허옇게 닳았고, 단추는 구릿빛 바탕이 드러나 있었다."], ["His face had fallen in, and was unshorn; his frill and neckcloth hung limp under his bagging waistcoat.", "얼굴은 홀쭉하게 꺼졌고 수염도 깎지 않은 채였으며, 가슴 주름 장식과 목도리는 헐렁해진 조끼 아래로 축 늘어져 있었다."], ["When he used to treat the boys in old days at a coffee-house, he would shout and laugh louder than anybody there, and have all the waiters skipping round him; it was quite painful to see how humble and civil he was to John of the Tapioca, a blear-eyed old attendant in dingy stockings and cracked pumps, whose business it was to serve glasses of wafers, and bumpers of ink in pewter, and slices of paper to the frequenters of this dreary house of entertainment, where nothing else seemed to be consumed.", "지난날 커피하우스에서 청년들에게 한턱낼 때면 그는 거기 있는 누구보다 크게 외치고 크게 웃었으며, 종업원들이 그의 주위를 종종걸음으로 분주히 돌게 만들었다. 그런 그가 타피오카의 존에게, 침침한 눈에 칙칙한 양말과 갈라진 단화를 신은 그 늙은 종업원에게 그토록 비굴하고 굽실거리는 모습을 보는 것은 참으로 가슴 아픈 일이었다."], ["As for William Dobbin, whom he had tipped repeatedly in his youth, and who had been the old gentleman's butt on a thousand occasions, old Sedley gave his hand to him in a very hesitating humble manner now, and called him \"Sir.\"", "그 종업원의 일이라곤 이 음산한 유흥소를 드나드는 손님들에게 웨이퍼 과자가 담긴 잔이며, 백랍 잔에 가득 담긴 잉크며, 종이 몇 장을 내다 주는 것뿐이었는데, 이곳에서는 그 밖에 달리 소비되는 것이라곤 없어 보였다. 윌리엄 도빈으로 말하자면, 노신사가 그의 젊은 시절에 거듭 용돈을 쥐여 주었고 또 천 번도 넘게 놀림감으로 삼았던 사람이건만, 이제 늙은 세들리는 몹시 머뭇거리며 비굴한 태도로 그에게 손을 내밀고는 그를 \"선생\"이라 불렀다."], ["A feeling of shame and remorse took possession of William Dobbin as the broken old man so received and addressed him, as if he himself had been somehow guilty of the misfortunes which had brought Sedley so low.", "그 영락한 노인이 자기를 그렇게 맞이하고 그렇게 부르는 것을 보자, 윌리엄 도빈은 마치 세들리를 이토록 낮은 처지로 떨어뜨린 그 불운에 자신이 어떤 식으로든 책임이라도 있는 것처럼, 수치심과 회한에 사로잡혔다."]], [["\"I am very glad to see you, Captain Dobbin, sir,\"", "\"이렇게 만나니 정말 반갑소이다, 도빈 대위 선생.\""], ["says he, after a skulking look or two at his visitor (whose lanky figure and military appearance caused some excitement likewise to twinkle in the blear eyes of the waiter in the cracked dancing pumps, and awakened the old lady in black, who dozed among the mouldy old coffee-cups in the bar).", "그는 방문객을 한두 번 슬그머니 곁눈질하고 나서 말했다(도빈의 호리호리한 체구와 군인다운 풍모는, 갈라진 무도화를 신은 그 종업원의 침침한 눈에도 얼마간 흥미의 빛을 반짝이게 했고, 곰팡내 나는 낡은 커피 잔들 틈에서 졸고 있던 검은 옷의 노부인까지 깨워 놓았다)."], ["\"How is the worthy alderman, and my lady, your excellent mother, sir?\"", "\"그 훌륭하신 시참사회 의원께서는 안녕하시고, 마님이신 자네의 그 인자하신 모친께서도 평안하신가, 선생?\""], ["He looked round at the waiter as he said, \"My lady,\" as much as to say, \"Hark ye, John, I have friends still, and persons of rank and reputation, too.\"", "그는 \"마님\"이라고 말하면서 종업원을 둘러보았는데, 그 모양새가 마치 \"이보게, 존, 내게도 아직 벗들이 있다네, 그것도 지체 높고 명망 있는 분들이 말이야\""], ["\"Are you come to do anything in my way, sir?", "하고 말하는 듯했다. \"내 쪽 일로 무슨 볼일이라도 보러 온 겐가, 선생?"], ["My young friends Dale and Spiggot do all my business for me now, until my new offices are ready; for I'm only here temporarily, you know, Captain.", "새 사무소가 준비될 때까지는 내 젊은 친구들인 데일과 스피곳이 내 모든 업무를 대신 봐주고 있다네. 알다시피 나는 여기 잠시 머물 뿐이라서 말일세, 대위."], ["What can we do for you, sir?", "무엇을 도와드리면 되겠나, 선생?"], ["Will you like to take anything?\"", "뭐라도 좀 드시겠는가?\""]], [["Dobbin, with a great deal of hesitation and stuttering, protested that he was not in the least hungry or thirsty; that he had no business to transact; that he only came to ask if Mr.", "도빈은 잔뜩 머뭇거리며 더듬더듬, 자기는 조금도 시장하거나 목마르지 않으며, 처리할 볼일이 있는 것도 아니고, 그저 세들리 씨가 평안하신지 여쭙고 옛 친구와 악수나 나누려고 들렀을 뿐이라고 둘러댔다. 그러고는 진실을 절박하게 비틀어 가며 이렇게 덧붙였다."], ["Sedley was well, and to shake hands with an old friend; and, he added, with a desperate perversion of truth, \"My mother is very well--that is, she's been very unwell, and is only waiting for the first fine day to go out and call upon Mrs.", "\"제 어머니께서는 아주 평안하십니다. 그러니까, 한동안 몹시 편찮으셨는데, 이제 날이 처음 좋아지는 대로 나가셔서 세들리 부인을 방문하시려고 기다리고 계십니다."], ["Sedley. How is Mrs.", "부인께서는 어떠십니까, 선생?"], ["Sedley, sir? I hope she's quite well.\"", "부디 아주 평안하시기를 바랍니다.\""], ["And here he paused, reflecting on his own consummate hypocrisy; for the day was as fine, and the sunshine as bright as it ever is in Coffin Court, where the Tapioca Coffee-house is situated: and Mr.", "그러고는 그는 말을 멈추고 자신의 완벽한 위선을 곱씹었다. 그날은 타피오카 커피하우스가 자리한 코핀 코트에서도 더없이 화창했고 햇살도 더없이 밝게 비치고 있었던 것이다."], ["Dobbin remembered that he had seen Mrs. Sedley himself only an hour before, having driven Osborne down to Fulham in his gig, and left him there tete-a-tete with Miss Amelia.", "게다가 도빈 씨는 바로 한 시간 전에 자기 이륜마차로 오스본을 풀럼까지 데려다주고 그를 애밀리아 양과 단둘이 남겨 두고 오면서 세들리 부인을 직접 보았던 터였다."]], [["\"My wife will be very happy to see her ladyship,\"", "\"제 아내가 마님을 뵈면 무척 기뻐할 겁니다.\" 세들리는 서류를 꺼내며 대답했다."], ["Sedley replied, pulling out his papers.", "\"여기 자네 부친께서 보내신 아주 친절한 편지가 있는데, 부디 그분께 내 정중한 인사를 전해 주게."], ["\"I've a very kind letter here from your father, sir, and beg my respectful compliments to him.", "디 마님께서는 우리가 옛날 벗들을 맞이하던 데보다 다소 작은 집에 사는 우리를 보시게 될 걸세. 하지만 아늑하다네."], ["Lady D.", "게다가 공기가 바뀌니 내 딸한테도 좋아."], ["will find us in rather a smaller house than we were accustomed to receive our friends in; but it's snug, and the change of air does good to my daughter, who was suffering in town rather--you remember little Emmy, sir?--yes, suffering a good deal.\"", "그 애가 시내에서는 좀 시달렸거든. 어린 에미를 기억하지, 선생?"], ["The old gentleman's eyes were wandering as he spoke, and he was thinking of something else, as he sate thrumming on his papers and fumbling at the worn red tape.", "그래, 꽤나 많이 시달렸다네.\" 노신사는 이렇게 말하는 동안 눈길이 이리저리 방황했고, 서류를 손가락으로 두드리며 닳은 붉은 끈을 만지작거리는 그의 머릿속은 딴생각에 빠져 있었다."]], [["\"You're a military man,\"", "\"자네는 군인이지.\""], ["he went on; \"I ask you, Bill Dobbin, could any man ever have speculated upon the return of that Corsican scoundrel from Elba?", "그가 말을 이었다. \"내 묻겠네, 빌 도빈, 그 코르시카 출신 악당이 엘바에서 돌아오리라고 누가 짐작이나 할 수 있었겠나?"], ["When the allied sovereigns were here last year, and we gave 'em that dinner in the City, sir, and we saw the Temple of Concord, and the fireworks, and the Chinese bridge in St. James's Park, could any sensible man suppose that peace wasn't really concluded, after we'd actually sung Te Deum for it, sir?", "작년에 연합국 군주들이 여기 왔을 때, 우리가 시내에서 그분들께 그 만찬을 베풀고, 화합의 신전이며, 불꽃놀이며, 세인트제임스 공원의 중국식 다리를 구경했을 때, 우리가 실제로 그 평화를 위해 테 데움까지 불렀는데, 분별 있는 사람이라면 그 누가 평화가 정말로 체결된 게 아니라고 생각이나 할 수 있었겠나, 선생?"], ["I ask you, William, could I suppose that the Emperor of Austria was a damned traitor--a traitor, and nothing more?", "내 묻겠네, 윌리엄, 내가 어찌 오스트리아 황제가 빌어먹을 배신자라고, 배신자에 지나지 않는다고 짐작이나 할 수 있었겠나?"], ["I don't mince words--a double-faced infernal traitor and schemer, who meant to have his son-in-law back all along.", "나는 말을 에두르지 않네. 처음부터 제 사위를 되돌려 놓으려 작정한, 두 얼굴의 흉악한 배신자요 음모꾼이었던 거야."], ["And I say that the escape of Boney from Elba was a damned imposition and plot, sir, in which half the powers of Europe were concerned, to bring the funds down, and to ruin this country.", "그리고 단언하건대, 보니가 엘바에서 탈출한 건 빌어먹을 사기요 음모였네, 선생. 공채 시세를 떨어뜨려 이 나라를 결딴내려고 유럽 열강의 절반이 가담한 음모였단 말이야."], ["That's why I'm here, William.", "그래서 내가 이 꼴이 된 걸세, 윌리엄."], ["That's why my name's in the Gazette.", "그래서 내 이름이 관보에 오른 거야."], ["Why, sir?--because I trusted the Emperor of Russia and the Prince Regent.", "어째서겠나, 선생? 내가 러시아 황제와 섭정공을 믿었기 때문이지."], ["Look here.", "자, 보게."], ["Look at my papers.", "내 서류를 보란 말이야."], ["Look what the funds were on the 1st of March--what the French fives were when I bought for the count.", "3월 1일에 공채가 어땠는지 보게. 내가 거래처를 위해 사들였을 때 프랑스 오 푼리 공채가 얼마였는지."], ["And what they're at now.", "그리고 지금은 얼마인지."], ["There was collusion, sir, or that villain never would have escaped.", "결탁이 있었던 거야, 선생. 그렇지 않고서야 그 악당이 결코 탈출할 수 없었지."], ["Where was the English Commissioner who allowed him to get away?", "그를 놓아준 영국 측 위원은 대체 어디 있었나?"], ["He ought to be shot, sir--brought to a court-martial, and shot, by Jove.\"", "그자는 총살당해 마땅하네, 선생. 군법회의에 회부해 총살시켜야 마땅하다고, 맹세코.\""]], [["\"We're going to hunt Boney out, sir,\"", "\"저희가 보니를 몰아내러 갈 겁니다, 선생.\""], ["Dobbin said, rather alarmed at the fury of the old man, the veins of whose forehead began to swell, and who sate drumming his papers with his clenched fist.", "도빈은 노인의 분노에 다소 놀라 말했다. 노인의 이마에 핏줄이 부풀어 오르기 시작했고, 그는 꽉 쥔 주먹으로 서류를 두드리며 앉아 있었다."], ["\"We are going to hunt him out, sir--the Duke's in Belgium already, and we expect marching orders every day.\"", "\"저희가 그를 몰아내러 갈 겁니다, 선생. 공작께서는 이미 벨기에에 가 계시고, 저희도 진군 명령이 날마다 떨어지기를 기다리고 있습니다.\""]], [["\"Give him no quarter.", "\"그놈에게 자비를 베풀지 말게."], ["Bring back the villain's head, sir.", "그 악당의 목을 베어 오게, 선생. 그 비겁자를 쏘아 죽이게, 선생.\""], ["Shoot the coward down, sir,\"", "세들리가 고함쳤다. \"나도 입대했을 걸세, 정말이지."], ["Sedley roared.", "하지만 나는 영락한 늙은이라네."], ["\"I'd enlist myself, by--; but I'm a broken old man--ruined by that damned scoundrel--and by a parcel of swindling thieves in this country whom I made, sir, and who are rolling in their carriages now,\"", "그 빌어먹을 악당에게 결딴난 신세야. 그리고 이 나라의 사기꾼 도둑놈 한 무리에게 결딴이 났지."], ["he added, with a break in his voice.", "내가 키워 준 놈들인데, 선생, 이제 와선 제 마차를 굴리며 떵떵거리고들 있다네.\" 그가 목멘 소리로 덧붙였다."]], [["Dobbin was not a little affected by the sight of this once kind old friend, crazed almost with misfortune and raving with senile anger.", "도빈은 한때 인자했던 이 옛 친구가, 불운으로 거의 실성하다시피 하여 노망 든 노여움에 길길이 날뛰는 모습을 보고 적잖이 마음이 흔들렸다."], ["Pity the fallen gentleman: you to whom money and fair repute are the chiefest good; and so, surely, are they in Vanity Fair.", "돈과 좋은 평판을 으뜸가는 선으로 여기는 그대여, 이 영락한 신사를 가엾이 여기라. 그리고 허영의 시장에서야 분명 그것들이 으뜸가는 선이렷다."]], [["\"Yes,\"", "\"그래.\""], ["he continued, \"there are some vipers that you warm, and they sting you afterwards.", "그가 말을 이었다. \"품어 따뜻이 데워 주면 나중에 도리어 무는 독사들이 있지."], ["There are some beggars that you put on horseback, and they're the first to ride you down.", "말 등에 태워 주면 제일 먼저 그 위로 자네를 짓밟고 지나가는 거지들이 있단 말이야."], ["You know whom I mean, William Dobbin, my boy.", "내가 누구 얘기를 하는지 알 걸세, 윌리엄 도빈, 이 사람아."], ["I mean a purse-proud villain in Russell Square, whom I knew without a shilling, and whom I pray and hope to see a beggar as he was when I befriended him.\"", "한 푼 없을 적부터 내가 알고 지낸, 러셀 광장의 그 돈 자랑이나 일삼는 악당 얘기야. 내가 도와주기 전 그 거지 신세로 다시 돌아가는 꼴을 보게 해 달라고 기도하고 또 바라는 그 작자 말일세.\""]], [["\"I have heard something of this, sir, from my friend George,\"", "\"제 친구 조지에게서 그 일에 관해 좀 들었습니다, 선생.\""], ["Dobbin said, anxious to come to his point.", "도빈은 본론으로 들어가고 싶어 안달하며 말했다."], ["\"The quarrel between you and his father has cut him up a great deal, sir.", "\"선생과 그의 부친 사이의 다툼이 그를 무척 괴롭게 하고 있습니다, 선생."], ["Indeed, I'm the bearer of a message from him.\"", "실은, 제가 그에게서 전할 말을 가지고 왔습니다.\""]], [["\"O, THAT'S your errand, is it?\"", "\"오, 그게 자네 심부름이로군, 그렇지?\""], ["cried the old man, jumping up.", "노인이 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다."], ["\"What!", "\"뭐라고!"], ["perhaps he condoles with me, does he?", "어쩌면 그가 내게 위로의 말이라도 전하던가, 응?"], ["Very kind of him, the stiff-backed prig, with his dandified airs and West End swagger.", "참으로 친절하기도 하지, 그 뻣뻣하고 잘난 척하는 멋쟁이 녀석이, 그 맵시 부리는 거드름과 웨스트엔드식 허세를 부려 가면서 말이야."], ["He's hankering about my house, is he still?", "그래, 아직도 내 집 주위를 기웃거리고 있나?"], ["If my son had the courage of a man, he'd shoot him.", "내 아들놈이 사내다운 용기가 있었다면 그를 쏘아 버렸을 텐데."], ["He's as big a villain as his father.", "그놈은 제 애비 못지않은 악당이야."], ["I won't have his name mentioned in my house.", "내 집에서 그놈 이름이 입에 오르내리는 건 용납 못 하네."], ["I curse the day that ever I let him into it; and I'd rather see my daughter dead at my feet than married to him.\"", "그놈을 내 집에 들인 그날을 저주하네. 내 딸이 그놈과 결혼하는 꼴을 보느니 차라리 그 애가 내 발치에 죽어 있는 꼴을 보겠어.\""]], [["\"His father's harshness is not George's fault, sir.", "\"그 부친의 모진 처사가 조지의 잘못은 아닙니다, 선생."], ["Your daughter's love for him is as much your doing as his.", "따님이 그를 사랑하게 된 것도 그 못지않게 선생께서 하신 일입니다."], ["Who are you, that you are to play with two young people's affections and break their hearts at your will?\"", "선생이 대체 누구시기에, 젊은 두 사람의 애정을 가지고 놀며 멋대로 그들의 가슴을 찢어 놓으시는 겁니까?\""]], [["\"Recollect it's not his father that breaks the match off,\"", "\"이 혼사를 깨는 게 그놈 애비가 아니라는 걸 명심하게.\""], ["old Sedley cried out.", "늙은 세들리가 소리쳤다."], ["\"It's I that forbid it.", "\"그걸 금하는 건 바로 날세."], ["That family and mine are separated for ever.", "그 집안과 우리 집안은 영영 갈라섰어."], ["I'm fallen low, but not so low as that: no, no.", "내가 영락하긴 했네만, 그 정도까지 떨어지진 않았네. 아니고말고."], ["And so you may tell the whole race--son, and father and sisters, and all.\"", "그러니 자네는 그 집안 전부에게 그렇게 전하게. 아들이고, 애비고, 누이들이고, 죄다 말일세.\""]], [["\"It's my belief, sir, that you have not the power or the right to separate those two,\"", "\"제 생각에는, 선생, 선생께 그 두 사람을 갈라놓을 권한도 권리도 없습니다.\""], ["Dobbin answered in a low voice; \"and that if you don't give your daughter your consent it will be her duty to marry without it.", "도빈이 낮은 목소리로 대답했다. \"그리고 선생께서 따님께 동의해 주지 않으신다면, 그 동의 없이라도 결혼하는 것이 따님의 도리가 될 겁니다."], ["There's no reason she should die or live miserably because you are wrong-headed.", "선생이 고집을 부리신다고 해서 따님이 죽거나 비참하게 살아야 할 이유는 없습니다."], ["To my thinking, she's just as much married as if the banns had been read in all the churches in London.", "제가 보기에 따님은 런던의 모든 교회에서 결혼 예고가 낭독된 것이나 다름없을 만큼 이미 결혼한 셈입니다."], ["And what better answer can there be to Osborne's charges against you, as charges there are, than that his son claims to enter your family and marry your daughter?\"", "게다가 오스본이 선생께 퍼붓는 비난에 대해, 비난이 있다면 말이지요, 그 아들이 선생의 집안에 들어와 따님과 혼인하기를 청한다는 것보다 더 나은 대답이 또 어디 있겠습니까?\""]], [["A light of something like satisfaction seemed to break over old Sedley as this point was put to him: but he still persisted that with his consent the marriage between Amelia and George should never take place.", "이 점을 들이대자 늙은 세들리에게 만족 비슷한 기색이 언뜻 떠오르는 듯했다. 그러나 그는 자기 동의를 얻어서는 애밀리아와 조지의 혼인이 결코 이루어질 수 없다고 여전히 고집했다."]], [["\"We must do it without,\" Dobbin said, smiling, and told Mr.", "\"그렇다면 동의 없이 해치우는 수밖에요.\""], ["Sedley, as he had told Mrs. Sedley in the day, before, the story of Rebecca's elopement with Captain Crawley.", "도빈이 빙긋 웃으며 말하고는, 전날 세들리 부인에게 들려주었던 대로 레베카가 크롤리 대위와 사랑의 도피를 한 이야기를 세들리 씨에게 들려주었다."], ["It evidently amused the old gentleman.", "그것은 분명 노신사를 즐겁게 했다."], ["\"You're terrible fellows, you Captains,\"", "\"자네들은 정말 무서운 작자들이야, 자네들 대위라는 족속들은.\""], ["said he, tying up his papers; and his face wore something like a smile upon it, to the astonishment of the blear-eyed waiter who now entered, and had never seen such an expression upon Sedley's countenance since he had used the dismal coffee-house.", "그가 서류를 묶으며 말했다. 그 얼굴에는 미소 비슷한 것이 어렸는데, 마침 들어서던 침침한 눈의 종업원은 세들리가 이 음울한 커피하우스를 드나든 이래 그 얼굴에서 그런 표정을 한 번도 본 적이 없었던지라 깜짝 놀랐다."]], [["The idea of hitting his enemy Osborne such a blow soothed, perhaps, the old gentleman: and, their colloquy presently ending, he and Dobbin parted pretty good friends.", "자기 원수 오스본에게 그런 한 방을 먹인다는 생각이 어쩌면 노신사의 마음을 달래 주었는지도 모른다. 그리고 이내 그들의 담화가 끝나자, 그와 도빈은 꽤 좋은 친구로 헤어졌다."]], [["\"My sisters say she has diamonds as big as pigeons' eggs,\"", "\"우리 누이들 말로는 그 여자가 비둘기 알만 한 다이아몬드를 가지고 있다더군.\""], ["George said, laughing.", "조지가 웃으며 말했다."], ["\"How they must set off her complexion!", "\"그게 그 살빛을 얼마나 돋보이게 하겠어!"], ["A perfect illumination it must be when her jewels are on her neck.", "그 보석들을 목에 걸면 그야말로 휘황찬란하겠지."], ["Her jet-black hair is as curly as Sambo's.", "그 새까만 머리는 샘보 머리만큼이나 곱슬곱슬하고."], ["I dare say she wore a nose ring when she went to court; and with a plume of feathers in her top-knot she would look a perfect Belle Sauvage.\"", "장담컨대 궁정에 들 땐 코걸이를 했을 거야. 머리 꼭대기에 깃털 장식까지 꽂으면 영락없는 미개의 미녀로 보이겠지.\""]], [["George, in conversation with Amelia, was rallying the appearance of a young lady of whom his father and sisters had lately made the acquaintance, and who was an object of vast respect to the Russell Square family.", "조지는 애밀리아와 이야기를 나누며, 얼마 전 그의 부친과 누이들이 새로 알게 된, 러셀 광장 집안의 지극한 존경을 한 몸에 받는 한 젊은 숙녀의 외모를 놀려 대고 있었다."], ["She was reported to have I don't know how many plantations in the West Indies; a deal of money in the funds; and three stars to her name in the East India stockholders'", "소문에 따르면 그녀는 서인도제도에 농장을 몇 개나 가지고 있는지 모르고, 공채에도 막대한 돈을 넣어 두었으며, 동인도회사 주주 명부에 그 이름 옆에 별이 세 개나 붙어 있다고 했다."], ["list. She had a mansion in Surrey, and a house in Portland Place.", "그녀는 서리에 대저택이 있고, 포틀랜드 플레이스에도 집이 한 채 있었다."], ["The name of the rich West India heiress had been mentioned with applause in the Morning Post. Mrs.", "이 부유한 서인도제도 상속녀의 이름은 모닝 포스트지에 찬사와 함께 거론된 바 있었다."], ["Haggistoun, Colonel Haggistoun's widow, her relative, \"chaperoned\" her, and kept her house.", "해기스턴 대령의 미망인이자 그녀의 친척인 해기스턴 부인이 그녀의 보호자 노릇을 하며 살림을 맡고 있었다."], ["She was just from school, where she had completed her education, and George and his sisters had met her at an evening party at old Hulker's house, Devonshire Place (Hulker, Bullock, and Co.", "그녀는 학업을 마친 학교에서 갓 나온 참이었는데, 조지와 그의 누이들은 데번셔 플레이스의 늙은 헐커네 집 야회에서 그녀와 마주쳤고(헐커, 불록 상회는 오랫동안 그녀 집안의 서인도제도 거래처였다), 자매는 그녀에게 더없이 다정하게 접근했으며 그 상속녀도 이를 무척 흔쾌히 받아들였다. 저런 처지의 고아가, 저만한 돈을 가지고, 어쩜 저리도 흥미로울까!"], ["were long the correspondents of her house in the West Indies), and the girls had made the most cordial advances to her, which the heiress had received with great good humour.", "오스본 자매는 그렇게들 말했다. 그들은 헐커네 무도회에서 돌아와 말동무인 워트 양에게 자기들의 새 친구 이야기로 입에 침이 마를 줄 몰랐다."], ["An orphan in her position--with her money--so interesting! the Misses Osborne said.", "계속 만나기로 약속을 잡았고, 바로 그 이튿날 마차를 몰아 그녀를 만나러 갔다."], ["They were full of their new friend when they returned from the Hulker ball to Miss Wirt, their companion; they had made arrangements for continually meeting, and had the carriage and drove to see her the very next day. Mrs.", "빙키 경의 친척으로 늘 그분 이야기를 입에 달고 다니는 해기스턴 대령의 미망인 해기스턴 부인은, 이 순진하고 세상 물정 모르는 아가씨들에게는 다소 거만하고 자기 지체 높은 친척들 이야기를 너무 좋아하는 사람으로 비쳤다."], ["Haggistoun, Colonel Haggistoun's widow, a relation of Lord Binkie, and always talking of him, struck the dear unsophisticated girls as rather haughty, and too much inclined to talk about her great relations: but Rhoda was everything they could wish--the frankest, kindest, most agreeable creature--wanting a little polish, but so good-natured.", "그러나 로다는 그들이 바랄 수 있는 모든 것을 갖추고 있었으니, 더없이 솔직하고, 더없이 다정하고, 더없이 상냥한 사람이었다. 세련미가 조금 부족하긴 해도 그토록 마음씨가 곱지 않은가."], ["The girls Christian-named each other at once.", "아가씨들은 당장에 서로 이름을 부르는 사이가 되었다."]], [["\"You should have seen her dress for court, Emmy,\"", "\"그 여자가 궁정에 들려고 차려입은 모습을 봤어야 하는 건데, 에미.\""], ["Osborne cried, laughing.", "오스본이 웃으며 외쳤다."], ["\"She came to my sisters to show it off, before she was presented in state by my Lady Binkie, the Haggistoun's kinswoman.", "\"해기스턴의 친척인 빙키 마님의 안내로 정식으로 입궐하기 전에, 우리 누이들한테 그 옷을 자랑하러 왔더라고."], ["She's related to every one, that Haggistoun.", "그 해기스턴이라는 여자는 안 닿는 데가 없는 친척이야."], ["Her diamonds blazed out like Vauxhall on the night we were there.", "그 여자 다이아몬드는 우리가 갔던 날 밤 복스홀처럼 번쩍번쩍하더군. (복스홀 기억해, 에미?"], ["(Do you remember Vauxhall, Emmy, and Jos singing to his dearest diddle diddle darling?) Diamonds and mahogany, my dear!", "그리고 조스가 자기 더없이 사랑스러운 딩들딩들 자기야 어쩌고 노래 부르던 것도?) 다이아몬드에 마호가니 빛깔이라니, 이봐! 얼마나 유리한 대조인지 생각해 봐."], ["think what an advantageous contrast--and the white feathers in her hair--I mean in her wool.", "게다가 머리에 꽂은 하얀 깃털이라니, 아니 양털 같은 머리에 말이야. 귀걸이는 샹들리에만 했어."], ["She had earrings like chandeliers; you might have lighted 'em up, by Jove--and a yellow satin train that streeled after her like the tail of a comet.\"", "거기다 불을 켜도 됐을 정도지, 정말이지. 그리고 노란 새틴 자락을 혜성 꼬리처럼 질질 끌고 다니더라고.\""]], [["\"How old is she?\"", "\"그 여자 몇 살인데?\""], ["asked Emmy, to whom George was rattling away regarding this dark paragon, on the morning of their reunion--rattling away as no other man in the world surely could.", "에미가 물었다. 조지는 재회한 그날 아침 이 검은 피부의 모범적 인물에 관해 그녀에게 쉴 새 없이 떠들어 대고 있었으니, 세상 그 누구도 흉내 낼 수 없을 만큼 쉴 새 없이 떠들어 댔다."]], [["\"Why the Black Princess, though she has only just left school, must be two or three and twenty.", "\"글쎄, 그 흑인 공주님은 이제 막 학교를 나왔다곤 해도 스물둘이나 셋은 됐을 거야."], ["And you should see the hand she writes! Mrs.", "그리고 그 여자 글씨를 봐야 하는 건데!"], ["Colonel Haggistoun usually writes her letters, but in a moment of confidence, she put pen to paper for my sisters; she spelt satin satting, and Saint James's, Saint Jams.\"", "보통은 해기스턴 대령 부인이 편지를 대신 써 주는데, 한번은 친밀감이 솟구쳤는지 우리 누이들한테 직접 펜을 들었더라고. 새틴(satin)을 새팅(satting)이라고 쓰고, 세인트제임스(Saint James's)를 세인트잼스(Saint Jams)라고 썼지 뭐야.\""]], [["\"Why, surely it must be Miss Swartz, the parlour boarder,\"", "\"어머, 그럼 분명 응접실 기숙생인 스워츠 양이겠네.\""], ["Emmy said, remembering that good-natured young mulatto girl, who had been so hysterically affected when Amelia left Miss Pinkerton's academy.", "에미가 말했다. 애밀리아가 핑커튼 양의 학원을 떠날 때 발작이라도 일으킬 듯 격하게 슬퍼하던, 그 마음씨 고운 젊은 혼혈 아가씨가 떠올랐던 것이다."]], [["\"The very name,\"", "\"바로 그 이름이야.\""], ["George said.", "조지가 말했다."], ["\"Her father was a German Jew--a slave-owner they say--connected with the Cannibal Islands in some way or other.", "\"그 여자 아버지는 독일계 유대인이었어. 노예주였다더군."], ["He died last year, and Miss Pinkerton has finished her education.", "어떤 식으로든 식인종 섬과 연이 닿아 있었나 봐. 작년에 죽었고, 핑커튼 양이 그 여자 교육을 마무리해 줬지."], ["She can play two pieces on the piano; she knows three songs; she can write when Mrs.", "피아노로 두 곡 칠 줄 알고, 노래 세 곡 알고, 해기스턴 부인이 곁에서 철자를 일러 주면 글도 쓸 줄 알아."], ["Haggistoun is by to spell for her; and Jane and Maria already have got to love her as a sister.\"", "그리고 우리 제인이랑 마리아는 벌써 그 여자를 친자매처럼 사랑하게 됐고.\""]], [["\"I wish they would have loved me,\"", "\"누이들이 나도 사랑해 줬으면 좋았을 텐데.\""], ["said Emmy, wistfully.", "에미가 애틋하게 말했다."], ["\"They were always very cold to me.\"", "\"나한텐 늘 무척 쌀쌀맞았잖아.\""]], [["\"My dear child, they would have loved you if you had had two hundred thousand pounds,\"", "\"이봐 자기, 자기가 이십만 파운드를 가지고 있었으면 누이들도 자길 사랑했을 거야.\""], ["George replied.", "조지가 대답했다."], ["\"That is the way in which they have been brought up.", "\"그렇게들 자라 왔으니까."], ["Ours is a ready-money society.", "우리 사회는 현찰이 곧 전부인 사회야."], ["We live among bankers and City big-wigs, and be hanged to them, and every man, as he talks to you, is jingling his guineas in his pocket.", "은행가들이며 시내 거물들 틈에서 사는데, 그까짓 것들, 누구든 자네한테 말을 걸 때면 주머니 속 금화를 짤랑거리고 있다고."], ["There is that jackass Fred Bullock is going to marry Maria--there's Goldmore, the East India Director, there's Dipley, in the tallow trade--OUR trade,\"", "저 멍청이 프레드 불록은 마리아랑 결혼할 거고, 동인도회사 중역인 골드모어가 있고, 수지 장사를 하는 디플리도 있지. 우리 장사 말이야.\""], ["George said, with an uneasy laugh and a blush.", "조지가 불편한 웃음을 지으며 얼굴을 붉히고 말했다."], ["\"Curse the whole pack of money-grubbing vulgarians!", "\"돈만 긁어모으는 그 천박한 족속들을 전부 저주하라지!"], ["I fall asleep at their great heavy dinners.", "난 그자들의 거하고 묵직한 만찬 자리에선 졸음이 쏟아진다고."], ["I feel ashamed in my father's great stupid parties.", "우리 아버지의 그 거창하고 멍청한 파티 자리에선 부끄럽기만 하고."], ["I've been accustomed to live with gentlemen, and men of the world and fashion, Emmy, not with a parcel of turtle-fed tradesmen.", "난 신사들하고, 세상 물정에 밝고 멋을 아는 사람들하고 어울려 사는 데 익숙해, 에미. 거북 요리나 처먹는 장사꾼 무리하고가 아니라."], ["Dear little woman, you are the only person of our set who ever looked, or thought, or spoke like a lady: and you do it because you're an angel and can't help it.", "사랑스러운 자기, 우리 패거리 중에 숙녀처럼 보이고, 숙녀처럼 생각하고, 숙녀처럼 말하는 사람은 자기뿐이야. 그것도 자기가 천사라서, 어쩔 수 없이 그러는 거지."], ["Don't remonstrate.", "따지지 마."], ["You are the only lady.", "자기야말로 유일한 숙녀라니까."], ["Didn't Miss Crawley remark it, who has lived in the best company in Europe?", "유럽 최고의 사교계에서 살아온 크롤리 양이 그걸 알아보지 않았겠어?"], ["And as for Crawley, of the Life Guards, hang it, he's a fine fellow: and I like him for marrying the girl he had chosen.\"", "그리고 근위 기병대의 크롤리로 말하면, 이런, 멋진 친구야. 자기가 고른 여자랑 결혼했다는 점에서 난 그 친구가 마음에 들어.\""]], [["Amelia admired Mr.", "애밀리아도 그 점에서 크롤리 씨에게 무척 감탄했다."], ["Crawley very much, too, for this; and trusted Rebecca would be happy with him, and hoped (with a laugh) Jos would be consoled.", "레베카가 그와 함께 행복하기를 빌었고, (웃으며) 조스도 위로를 받기를 바랐다."], ["And so the pair went on prattling, as in quite early days.", "그렇게 두 사람은 아주 옛날처럼 도란도란 수다를 떨었다."], ["Amelia's confidence being perfectly restored to her, though she expressed a great deal of pretty jealousy about Miss Swartz, and professed to be dreadfully frightened--like a hypocrite as she was--lest George should forget her for the heiress and her money and her estates in Saint Kitt's.", "애밀리아의 자신감이 완벽하게 되돌아온 것이다. 비록 그녀는 스워츠 양을 두고 어여쁜 질투를 잔뜩 부리며, 위선자답게도, 조지가 그 상속녀와 그 돈과 세인트키츠의 영지에 혹해 자기를 잊어버릴까 봐 끔찍이도 무섭다고 엄살을 떨긴 했지만 말이다."], ["But the fact is, she was a great deal too happy to have fears or doubts or misgivings of any sort: and having George at her side again, was not afraid of any heiress or beauty, or indeed of any sort of danger.", "그러나 사실인즉 그녀는 너무도 행복해서 그 어떤 두려움이나 의심이나 불안도 품을 수가 없었다. 다시 조지가 곁에 있으니, 그 어떤 상속녀도 미녀도, 아니 그 어떤 종류의 위험도 두렵지 않았던 것이다."]], [["When Captain Dobbin came back in the afternoon to these people--which he did with a great deal of sympathy for them--it did his heart good to see how Amelia had grown young again--how she laughed, and chirped, and sang familiar old songs at the piano, which were only interrupted by the bell from without proclaiming Mr.", "오후에 도빈 대위가 이 사람들에게로 돌아왔을 때, 그는 그들을 향한 깊은 연민을 품고 돌아온 것이었는데, 애밀리아가 어찌나 다시 젊어졌는지를 보니 절로 마음이 흐뭇해졌다. 그녀가 웃고, 재잘대고, 피아노 앞에서 정겨운 옛 노래들을 부르는 모습이라니."], ["Sedley's return from the City, before whom George received a signal to retreat.", "그 노래는 바깥에서 세들리 씨가 시내에서 돌아왔음을 알리는 종소리가 울려서야 비로소 중단되었고, 그 소리에 조지는 물러가라는 신호를 받았다."]], [["Beyond the first smile of recognition--and even that was an hypocrisy, for she thought his arrival rather provoking--Miss Sedley did not once notice Dobbin during his visit.", "세들리 양은 처음에 알아보는 미소를 한 번 지어 보인 것을 빼면, 그 미소조차 위선이었으니 그가 온 것을 그녀는 도리어 성가시게 여겼던 것이다, 그가 머무는 내내 도빈에게 단 한 번도 눈길을 주지 않았다."], ["But he was content, so that he saw her happy; and thankful to have been the means of making her so.", "그러나 그는 만족했다. 그녀가 행복한 모습을 보았으니 그것으로 족했고, 그녀를 그렇게 만든 수단이 되었다는 데 감사할 따름이었다."]], [["A Quarrel About an Heiress\nLove may be felt for any young lady endowed with such qualities as Miss Swartz possessed; and a great dream of ambition entered into old Mr.", "미스 스워츠가 지닌 것과 같은 자질을 갖춘 젊은 숙녀라면 그 누구라도 사랑받을 수 있는 법이다."], ["Osborne's soul, which she was to realize. He encouraged, with the utmost enthusiasm and friendliness, his daughters'", "그리하여 늙은 오스본 씨의 영혼에는 야심의 원대한 꿈이 자리 잡았으니, 그 꿈을 실현해 줄 사람이 바로 그녀였던 것이다."], ["amiable attachment to the young heiress, and protested that it gave him the sincerest pleasure as a father to see the love of his girls so well disposed.", "그는 더없는 열정과 우애로 두 딸이 이 젊은 상속녀에게 품은 살가운 애정을 부추겼고, 자기 딸들의 사랑이 이토록 합당한 곳에 쏠리는 것을 보는 일이 아버지로서 더없이 진실한 기쁨이라고 떠벌렸다."]], [["\"You won't find,\"", "\"우리 누추한 러셀 광장 저택에서는 말입니다, 아가씨.\" 그는 로다 양에게 이렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["he would say to Miss Rhoda, \"that splendour and rank to which you are accustomed at the West End, my dear Miss, at our humble mansion in Russell Square.", "\"웨스트엔드에서 익숙하시던 그런 화려함이나 신분은 찾아보실 수 없을 겁니다. 제 딸들은 수수하고 사심 없는 아이들이지요."], ["My daughters are plain, disinterested girls, but their hearts are in the right place, and they've conceived an attachment for you which does them honour--I say, which does them honour.", "하지만 그 마음만은 제자리에 똑바로 박혀 있어서, 아가씨께 애정을 품게 되었으니, 그건 그 애들에게 명예로운 일입니다. 명예로운 일이고말고요."], ["I'm a plain, simple, humble British merchant--an honest one, as my respected friends Hulker and Bullock will vouch, who were the correspondents of your late lamented father.", "저는 수수하고 단순하고 보잘것없는 영국 상인입니다. 정직한 상인이지요."], ["You'll find us a united, simple, happy, and I think I may say respected, family--a plain table, a plain people, but a warm welcome, my dear Miss Rhoda--Rhoda, let me say, for my heart warms to you, it does really.", "작고하신 아가씨의 선친과 거래하던 제 존경하는 벗 헐커와 불록이 이를 보증해 줄 겁니다. 우리는 화목하고, 단순하고, 행복하고, 또 감히 말씀드리건대 존경받는 가족임을 알게 되실 겁니다."], ["I'm a frank man, and I like you.", "소박한 밥상에, 소박한 사람들이지만, 따뜻한 환대만큼은 보장합니다, 사랑하는 로다 양. 로다라고 부르게 해 주십시오."], ["A glass of Champagne!", "제 마음이 아가씨께 따뜻하게 끌리는 것이, 정말 그렇답니다. 저는 솔직한 사람이고, 아가씨가 마음에 듭니다."], ["Hicks, Champagne to Miss Swartz.\"", "샴페인 한 잔! 힉스, 미스 스워츠께 샴페인을 올려라.\""]], [["There is little doubt that old Osborne believed all he said, and that the girls were quite earnest in their protestations of affection for Miss Swartz.", "늙은 오스본이 자기가 한 말을 모두 진심으로 믿었으리라는 것, 그리고 딸들이 미스 스워츠에 대한 애정의 맹세를 자못 진지하게 했으리라는 것은 의심할 여지가 거의 없다."], ["People in Vanity Fair fasten on to rich folks quite naturally.", "허영의 시장 사람들은 부자에게 들러붙는 일을 지극히 자연스러운 것으로 여긴다."], ["If the simplest people are disposed to look not a little kindly on great Prosperity (for I defy any member of the British public to say that the notion of Wealth has not something awful and pleasing to him; and you, if you are told that the man next you at dinner has got half a million, not to look at him with a certain interest)--if the simple look benevolently on money, how much more do your old worldlings regard it!", "가장 순박한 사람들조차 위대한 번영을 적잖이 흐뭇한 눈길로 바라보기 마련이거늘(부의 관념에 무언가 경외롭고도 흐뭇한 구석이 없다고 단언할 수 있는 영국 국민이 한 사람이라도 있다면 어디 나와 보라. 또한 만찬 자리에서 옆자리 사람이 오십만 파운드를 가졌다는 말을 듣고서도 어떤 흥미도 없이 그를 바라볼 수 있다면 어디 나와 보라), 순박한 이들조차 돈을 그토록 자애로운 눈길로 바라본다면, 하물며 산전수전 다 겪은 늙은 속물들이야 돈을 어찌 여기겠는가!"], ["Their affections rush out to meet and welcome money.", "그들의 애정은 앞다투어 달려 나가 돈을 맞이하고 환영한다."], ["Their kind sentiments awaken spontaneously towards the interesting possessors of it.", "그 흥미로운 돈의 임자를 향해 그들의 따뜻한 정감이 절로 깨어나는 것이다."], ["I know some respectable people who don't consider themselves at liberty to indulge in friendship for any individual who has not a certain competency, or place in society.", "나는 일정한 재산이나 사회적 지위가 없는 사람과는 친분을 나눌 자유가 자기에게 없다고 여기는 점잖은 사람들 몇몇을 알고 있다."], ["They give a loose to their feelings on proper occasions.", "그들은 적절한 경우가 오면 그 감정에 고삐를 풀어 준다."], ["And the proof is, that the major part of the Osborne family, who had not, in fifteen years, been able to get up a hearty regard for Amelia Sedley, became as fond of Miss Swartz in the course of a single evening as the most romantic advocate of friendship at first sight could desire.", "그 증거로, 십오 년 동안 애밀리아 세들리에게 진심 어린 호의 한 자락 품지 못했던 오스본 가문 사람들 대다수가, 단 하룻저녁 만에 미스 스워츠에게 흠뻑 빠져들어, 첫눈에 반하는 우정을 가장 낭만적으로 옹호하는 자라도 흡족해할 정도가 된 것이다."]], [["What a match for George she'd be (the sisters and Miss Wirt agreed), and how much better than that insignificant little Amelia!", "그녀야말로 조지에게 얼마나 어울리는 짝인가(자매와 워트 양은 의견을 같이했다). 게다가 저 보잘것없고 하찮은 애밀리아보다 얼마나 더 나은가!"], ["Such a dashing young fellow as he is, with his good looks, rank, and accomplishments, would be the very husband for her.", "저토록 늠름한 청년이, 그 잘난 용모와 신분과 교양을 두루 갖춘 그가 그녀에게 더없이 알맞은 남편이 될 게 아닌가."], ["Visions of balls in Portland Place, presentations at Court, and introductions to half the peerage, filled the minds of the young ladies; who talked of nothing but George and his grand acquaintances to their beloved new friend.", "포틀랜드 광장에서 열릴 무도회며, 궁정 알현이며, 귀족 절반과의 소개 자리가 어른어른 떠올라 젊은 숙녀들의 마음을 채웠고, 그들은 사랑하는 새 친구에게 조지와 그의 으리으리한 인맥 이야기밖에는 다른 말을 하지 않았다."]], [["Old Osborne thought she would be a great match, too, for his son.", "늙은 오스본 역시 그녀가 아들에게 더없이 좋은 짝이 되리라고 생각했다."], ["He should leave the army; he should go into Parliament; he should cut a figure in the fashion and in the state.", "아들은 군대를 떠나 의회에 들어가고, 사교계와 정계에서 두각을 나타내야 마땅했다."], ["His blood boiled with honest British exultation, as he saw the name of Osborne ennobled in the person of his son, and thought that he might be the progenitor of a glorious line of baronets.", "오스본이라는 이름이 아들을 통해 작위를 얻는 광경을 떠올리며, 자신이 영광스러운 준남작 가문의 시조가 될지도 모른다고 생각하니, 그의 피는 정직한 영국식 환희로 끓어올랐다. 그는 상속녀의 재산에 관한 모든 것, 즉 그 돈이 어디에 투자되어 있고 영지가 어디에 자리 잡고 있는지까지 죄다 알아낼 때까지 시내며 증권거래소에서 부지런히 발품을 팔았다."], ["He worked in the City and on 'Change, until he knew everything relating to the fortune of the heiress, how her money was placed, and where her estates lay.", "그의 주요 정보통 가운데 하나인 젊은 프레드 불록은 자기가 직접 그녀에게 값을 매겨 보고 싶기도 했지만(젊은 은행가는 그렇게 표현했다), 이미 마리아 오스본에게 예약된 몸이었다."], ["Young Fred Bullock, one of his chief informants, would have liked to make a bid for her himself (it was so the young banker expressed it), only he was booked to Maria Osborne.", "그러나 그녀를 아내로 차지할 수 없게 된 사심 없는 프레드는, 그녀를 동서로 맞이하는 데는 전적으로 찬성이었다. \"조지더러 당장 끼어들어서 차지하라고 하세요.\""], ["But not being able to secure her as a wife, the disinterested Fred quite approved of her as a sister-in-law.", "이것이 그의 충고였다. \"쇠는 달궈졌을 때 두드려야지요."], ["\"Let George cut in directly and win her,\" was his advice.", "그녀가 아직 사교계에 갓 나온 풋풋한 동안에 말입니다."], ["\"Strike while the iron's hot, you know--while she's fresh to the town: in a few weeks some d---- fellow from the West End will come in with a title and a rotten rent-roll and cut all us City men out, as Lord Fitzrufus did last year with Miss Grogram, who was actually engaged to Podder, of Podder & Brown's.", "몇 주만 지나면 웨스트엔드에서 작위에 거덜 난 영지 따위나 가진 어떤 빌어먹을 작자가 끼어들어서 우리 시내 사람들을 죄다 밀어낼 겁니다. 작년에 피츠루퍼스 경이 그로그램 양을 가로챈 것처럼 말이지요."], ["The sooner it is done the better, Mr.", "그 아가씨는 사실 포더 앤드 브라운 상회의 포더와 약혼한 사이였는데도요."], ["Osborne; them's my sentiments,\"", "빠르면 빠를수록 좋습니다, 오스본 씨."], ["the wag said; though, when Osborne had left the bank parlour, Mr.", "그게 제 생각입니다.\" 이 익살꾼은 그렇게 말했다."], ["Bullock remembered Amelia, and what a pretty girl she was, and how attached to George Osborne; and he gave up at least ten seconds of his valuable time to regretting the misfortune which had befallen that unlucky young woman.", "다만 오스본이 은행 응접실을 떠나고 나자, 불록 씨는 애밀리아를 떠올리고는, 그 아이가 얼마나 예쁜 처녀였으며 조지 오스본에게 얼마나 깊이 정을 주었던가를 생각했다. 그리하여 그는 자기 귀한 시간 가운데 적어도 십 초가량을, 그 불운한 젊은 여인에게 닥친 불행을 안타까워하는 데 바쳤던 것이다."]], [["While thus George Osborne's good feelings, and his good friend and genius, Dobbin, were carrying back the truant to Amelia's feet, George's parent and sisters were arranging this splendid match for him, which they never dreamed he would resist.", "이렇듯 조지 오스본의 선량한 감정과, 그 선량한 벗이자 수호신인 도빈이 길 잃은 그를 다시 애밀리아의 발치로 데려가고 있는 동안, 조지의 아버지와 누이들은 그가 거부하리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못한 채 그를 위해 이 으리으리한 혼사를 꾸미고 있었다."]], [["When the elder Osborne gave what he called \"a hint,\" there was no possibility for the most obtuse to mistake his meaning.", "연장자 오스본이 이른바 \"암시\"라는 것을 줄 때면, 아무리 둔한 자라도 그 뜻을 오해할 가능성은 없었다."], ["He called kicking a footman downstairs a hint to the latter to leave his service. With his usual frankness and delicacy he told Mrs.", "그는 하인을 계단 아래로 걷어차는 것을 두고 그 하인더러 일을 그만두라는 암시라고 불렀다."], ["Haggistoun that he would give her a cheque for five thousand pounds on the day his son was married to her ward; and called that proposal a hint, and considered it a very dexterous piece of diplomacy.", "그는 여느 때의 그 솔직함과 섬세함으로 해기스턴 부인에게, 아들이 그녀가 후견하는 처녀와 혼인하는 날 오천 파운드짜리 수표를 끊어 주겠다고 말했다. 그러고는 그 제안을 암시라 일컬으며, 자못 능란한 외교적 수완이라 여겼다."], ["He gave George finally such another hint regarding the heiress; and ordered him to marry her out of hand, as he would have ordered his butler to draw a cork, or his clerk to write a letter.", "마침내 그는 상속녀에 관해서도 조지에게 그런 식의 암시를 하나 던졌으니, 집사에게 와인 병마개를 뽑으라 명하거나 서기에게 편지를 쓰라 명하듯, 두말 말고 그녀와 혼인하라고 명령했던 것이다."]], [["This imperative hint disturbed George a good deal.", "이 단호한 암시는 조지를 적잖이 동요케 했다."], ["He was in the very first enthusiasm and delight of his second courtship of Amelia, which was inexpressibly sweet to him.", "그는 애밀리아를 향한 두 번째 구애의 더없이 싱그러운 열정과 기쁨에 막 젖어들던 참이었고, 그 기쁨은 그에게 말할 수 없이 달콤했다."], ["The contrast of her manners and appearance with those of the heiress, made the idea of a union with the latter appear doubly ludicrous and odious.", "그녀의 몸가짐과 용모를 상속녀의 그것과 견주어 보면, 상속녀와 맺어진다는 생각이 갑절로 우스꽝스럽고 역겹게 느껴지는 것이었다."], ["Carriages and opera-boxes, thought he; fancy being seen in them by the side of such a mahogany charmer as that!", "마차며 오페라 특별석이라니, 하고 그는 생각했다. 저런 마호가니빛 미녀를 곁에 두고 그런 데서 사람들 눈에 띄는 꼴이라니!"], ["Add to all that the junior Osborne was quite as obstinate as the senior: when he wanted a thing, quite as firm in his resolution to get it; and quite as violent when angered, as his father in his most stern moments.", "게다가 젊은 오스본은 늙은 오스본 못지않게 고집이 셌다. 무언가를 원할 때면 그것을 손에 넣으려는 결심이 아비만큼이나 굳었고, 화가 났을 때면 아비가 가장 준엄하던 순간 못지않게 사나웠다."]], [["On the first day when his father formally gave him the hint that he was to place his affections at Miss Swartz's feet, George temporised with the old gentleman.", "아버지가 미스 스워츠의 발치에 애정을 바치라고 정식으로 암시를 던진 첫날, 조지는 그 노신사에게 시간을 끌었다. \"더 일찍 생각하셨어야지요, 아버지.\""], ["\"You should have thought of the matter sooner, sir,\"", "그가 말했다. \"지금은 안 됩니다."], ["he said.", "날마다 외국 파병을 기다리는 처지인걸요. 제가 돌아올 때까지 기다리세요."], ["\"It can't be done now, when we're expecting every day to go on foreign service.", "돌아온다면 말이지만요.\" 그러고는 연대가 영국을 곧 떠나리라고 날마다 대기하는 이런 때야말로 더없이 부적절한 시기라고 둘러댔다."], ["Wait till my return, if I do return\"; and then he represented, that the time when the regiment was daily expecting to quit England, was exceedingly ill-chosen: that the few days or weeks during which they were still to remain at home, must be devoted to business and not to love-making: time enough for that when he came home with his majority; \"for, I promise you,\"", "아직 본국에 머무를 며칠 혹은 몇 주는 연애가 아니라 일에 바쳐야 마땅하며, 그런 일이야 자기가 소령으로 진급해 돌아온 뒤에 해도 늦지 않다는 것이었다. \"약속드리지요.\""], ["said he, with a satisfied air, \"that one way or other you shall read the name of George Osborne in the Gazette.\"", "그는 흡족한 기색으로 말했다. \"어떻게든 조지 오스본이라는 이름을 관보에서 읽으시게 해 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["The father's reply to this was founded upon the information which he had got in the City: that the West End chaps would infallibly catch hold of the heiress if any delay took place: that if he didn't marry Miss S., he might at least have an engagement in writing, to come into effect when he returned to England; and that a man who could get ten thousand a year by staying at home, was a fool to risk his life abroad.", "이에 대한 아버지의 대답은 그가 시내에서 입수한 정보에 근거한 것이었다. 즉 조금이라도 지체했다가는 웨스트엔드의 작자들이 영락없이 상속녀를 낚아챌 것이며, 미스 스워츠와 혼인하지 못한다 해도 적어도 영국에 돌아오면 효력이 발생하는 서면 약혼이라도 맺어 둬야 하고, 집에 가만히 앉아 연 만 파운드를 거머쥘 수 있는 사내가 외국에서 목숨을 거는 짓은 바보나 할 짓이라는 것이었다."]], [["\"So that you would have me shown up as a coward, sir, and our name dishonoured for the sake of Miss Swartz's money,\"", "\"그러니까 저더러 겁쟁이로 손가락질받게 하고, 미스 스워츠의 돈을 위해 우리 가문의 이름에 먹칠을 하라는 말씀이로군요.\""], ["George interposed.", "조지가 끼어들었다."]], [["This remark staggered the old gentleman; but as he had to reply to it, and as his mind was nevertheless made up, he said, \"You will dine here to-morrow, sir, and every day Miss Swartz comes, you will be here to pay your respects to her.", "이 말에 노신사는 휘청했지만, 그래도 대꾸는 해야 했고 또 마음만은 이미 굳혀 둔 터라 이렇게 말했다. \"내일 여기서 저녁을 먹어라."], ["If you want for money, call upon Mr. Chopper.\"", "그리고 미스 스워츠가 오는 날이면 날마다 여기 와서 그녀에게 인사를 올려라."], ["Thus a new obstacle was in George's way, to interfere with his plans regarding Amelia; and about which he and Dobbin had more than one confidential consultation.", "돈이 궁하거든 초퍼 씨를 찾아가고.\" 이렇게 하여 조지가 애밀리아를 두고 세운 계획을 방해하는 새로운 장애물이 그의 앞길에 놓이게 되었으니, 그와 도빈은 이 일을 두고 한두 번이 아니게 은밀한 의논을 나누었다."], ["His friend's opinion respecting the line of conduct which he ought to pursue, we know already.", "그가 취해야 할 처신에 관한 친구의 의견이야 우리는 이미 알고 있다."], ["And as for Osborne, when he was once bent on a thing, a fresh obstacle or two only rendered him the more resolute.", "그리고 오스본으로 말하자면, 일단 무언가에 마음을 굳히고 나면 한두 개쯤 새로운 장애물이 생긴들 그를 한층 더 결연하게 만들 뿐이었다."]], [["The dark object of the conspiracy into which the chiefs of the Osborne family had entered, was quite ignorant of all their plans regarding her (which, strange to say, her friend and chaperon did not divulge), and, taking all the young ladies'", "오스본 가문의 수뇌들이 꾸민 이 음모의 가무잡잡한 표적은, 자기에 관한 그들의 온갖 계획을 까맣게 모르고 있었다(이상한 일이지만, 그녀의 친구이자 보호자도 이를 발설하지 않았다)."], ["flattery for genuine sentiment, and being, as we have before had occasion to show, of a very warm and impetuous nature, responded to their affection with quite a tropical ardour.", "그리하여 젊은 숙녀들의 온갖 아첨을 진심에서 우러난 감정으로 받아들였고, 앞서 보일 기회가 있었듯이 본디 자못 다정하고 격정적인 천성인지라, 그 애정에 자못 열대 지방다운 열정으로 화답했다."], ["And if the truth may be told, I dare say that she too had some selfish attraction in the Russell Square house; and in a word, thought George Osborne a very nice young man.", "그리고 사실대로 말하자면, 그녀 역시 러셀 광장 저택에 무언가 이기적인 끌림이 있었으리라고 나는 감히 짐작한다. 한마디로, 그녀는 조지 오스본을 자못 멋진 청년이라고 여겼던 것이다."], ["His whiskers had made an impression upon her, on the very first night she beheld them at the ball at Messrs.", "헐커 상회의 무도회에서 그를 처음 본 바로 그날 밤부터, 그의 구레나룻은 그녀에게 깊은 인상을 남겼다."], ["Hulkers; and, as we know, she was not the first woman who had been charmed by them.", "그리고 우리도 알다시피, 그 구레나룻에 매혹된 여인이 그녀가 처음은 아니었다."], ["George had an air at once swaggering and melancholy, languid and fierce.", "조지에게는 거들먹거리면서도 우수에 잠긴 듯한, 나른하면서도 사나운 분위기가 한꺼번에 어려 있었다."], ["He looked like a man who had passions, secrets, and private harrowing griefs and adventures.", "그는 격정과 비밀과, 남에게 말 못 할 가슴 저미는 슬픔과 모험을 간직한 사내처럼 보였다."], ["His voice was rich and deep.", "그의 목소리는 풍부하고 깊었다."], ["He would say it was a warm evening, or ask his partner to take an ice, with a tone as sad and confidential as if he were breaking her mother's death to her, or preluding a declaration of love.", "그가 날이 따뜻하다고 말하거나 상대에게 아이스크림을 권할 때면, 마치 어머니의 부음을 전하거나 사랑 고백의 서두를 떼는 사람처럼 슬프고도 은밀한 어조였다."], ["He trampled over all the young bucks of his father's circle, and was the hero among those third-rate men.", "그는 아버지 사교계의 젊은 멋쟁이들을 죄다 짓밟았고, 그 삼류들 사이에서 영웅이었다."], ["Some few sneered at him and hated him.", "더러는 그를 비웃고 미워했다."], ["Some, like Dobbin, fanatically admired him.", "더러는 도빈처럼 그를 광적으로 흠모했다."], ["And his whiskers had begun to do their work, and to curl themselves round the affections of Miss Swartz.", "그리고 그의 구레나룻은 이미 제 몫을 하기 시작하여, 미스 스워츠의 애정을 휘감아 들어가고 있었다."]], [["Whenever there was a chance of meeting him in Russell Square, that simple and good-natured young woman was quite in a flurry to see her dear Misses Osborne.", "러셀 광장에서 그를 만날 가망이 보일 때면, 그 순박하고 마음씨 고운 젊은 여인은 사랑하는 오스본 자매를 만나러 갈 생각에 안절부절못했다."], ["She went to great expenses in new gowns, and bracelets, and bonnets, and in prodigious feathers.", "그녀는 새 가운이며 팔찌며 보닛이며 엄청난 깃털 장식에 막대한 돈을 쏟아부었다."], ["She adorned her person with her utmost skill to please the Conqueror, and exhibited all her simple accomplishments to win his favour.", "정복자의 마음에 들고자 온갖 솜씨를 다해 몸치장을 했고, 그의 환심을 사고자 자기의 소박한 재주를 죄다 펼쳐 보였다."], ["The girls would ask her, with the greatest gravity, for a little music, and she would sing her three songs and play her two little pieces as often as ever they asked, and with an always increasing pleasure to herself.", "자매가 더없이 진지한 얼굴로 음악을 좀 들려 달라 청하면, 그녀는 청할 때마다 노래 세 곡을 부르고 짧은 곡 두 가지를 연주했으니, 그럴수록 스스로 느끼는 기쁨도 점점 커져 갔다."], ["During these delectable entertainments, Miss Wirt and the chaperon sate by, and conned over the peerage, and talked about the nobility.", "이 즐거운 여흥이 펼쳐지는 동안, 워트 양과 보호자는 곁에 앉아 귀족 명감을 들춰 보며 귀족 이야기를 나누었다."]], [["The day after George had his hint from his father, and a short time before the hour of dinner, he was lolling upon a sofa in the drawing-room in a very becoming and perfectly natural attitude of melancholy.", "조지가 아버지에게서 암시를 받은 이튿날, 저녁 식사 시간을 조금 앞두고, 그는 응접실 소파에 늘어진 채로 더없이 어울리면서도 지극히 자연스러운 우수의 자세를 취하고 있었다."], ["He had been, at his father's request, to Mr. Chopper in the City (the old gentleman, though he gave great sums to his son, would never specify any fixed allowance for him, and rewarded him only as he was in the humour).", "그는 아버지의 분부대로 시내의 초퍼 씨에게 다녀온 참이었다(노신사는 아들에게 큰돈을 주면서도 일정한 용돈을 정해 주는 법이 결코 없었고, 그저 기분 내키는 대로 상을 내릴 따름이었다)."], ["He had then been to pass three hours with Amelia, his dear little Amelia, at Fulham; and he came home to find his sisters spread in starched muslin in the drawing-room, the dowagers cackling in the background, and honest Swartz in her favourite amber-coloured satin, with turquoise bracelets, countless rings, flowers, feathers, and all sorts of tags and gimcracks, about as elegantly decorated as a she chimney-sweep on May-day.", "그러고 나서 풀럼에 가 사랑하는 자기 어린 애밀리아와 세 시간을 보냈고, 집에 돌아와 보니 누이들은 풀 먹인 모슬린을 펼쳐 입고 응접실에 앉아 있었으며, 노부인들은 뒤편에서 재잘대고 있었고, 정직한 스워츠는 그 좋아하는 호박색 새틴 드레스에 터키석 팔찌, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 반지며 꽃이며 깃털이며 온갖 끈과 싸구려 장신구를 주렁주렁 매단 채, 오월제의 여자 굴뚝 청소부만큼이나 우아하게 차려입고 있었다."]], [["The girls, after vain attempts to engage him in conversation, talked about fashions and the last drawing-room until he was perfectly sick of their chatter.", "자매는 그를 대화에 끌어들이려다 번번이 실패하자, 그가 그 수다에 진력이 날 때까지 유행이며 지난번 궁정 알현 이야기를 늘어놓았다."], ["He contrasted their behaviour with little Emmy's--their shrill voices with her tender ringing tones; their attitudes and their elbows and their starch, with her humble soft movements and modest graces.", "그는 그들의 행동거지를 어린 에미의 그것과 견주었다. 그들의 새된 목소리를 그녀의 부드럽고 낭랑한 음성과, 그들의 자세며 팔꿈치며 풀 먹인 옷차림을 그녀의 다소곳하고 부드러운 몸짓이며 단아한 기품과 견주어 본 것이다."], ["Poor Swartz was seated in a place where Emmy had been accustomed to sit.", "가엾은 스워츠는 에미가 늘 앉던 자리에 앉아 있었다."], ["Her bejewelled hands lay sprawling in her amber satin lap.", "보석을 주렁주렁 단 손은 호박색 새틴 무릎 위에 아무렇게나 늘어져 있었다."], ["Her tags and ear-rings twinkled, and her big eyes rolled about.", "그녀의 장식 끈과 귀고리는 반짝였고, 큰 눈은 두리번두리번 굴러다녔다."], ["She was doing nothing with perfect contentment, and thinking herself charming.", "그녀는 더없이 만족스러운 얼굴로 아무것도 하지 않고 있었고, 자기가 매력적이라고 여기고 있었다."], ["Anything so becoming as the satin the sisters had never seen.", "누이들은 그 새틴만큼 어울리는 옷을 본 적이 없었다."]], [["\"Dammy,\"", "\"제기랄.\""], ["George said to a confidential friend, \"she looked like a China doll, which has nothing to do all day but to grin and wag its head.", "조지가 절친한 벗에게 말했다. \"하루 종일 히죽거리며 고개나 까딱대는 것밖에는 할 일이 없는 도자기 인형 같더라니까."], ["By Jove, Will, it was all I I could do to prevent myself from throwing the sofa-cushion at her.\"", "맙소사, 윌, 소파 쿠션을 그 여자한테 집어 던지지 않으려고 안간힘을 다 썼다네.\""], ["He restrained that exhibition of sentiment, however.", "그렇긴 해도 그는 그런 감정 표출만큼은 억눌렀다."]], [["The sisters began to play the Battle of Prague.", "자매는 「프라하 전투」를 연주하기 시작했다."], ["\"Stop that d---- thing,\"", "\"그 빌어먹을 거 좀 그만둬.\""], ["George howled out in a fury from the sofa.", "조지가 소파에서 노발대발 고함쳤다."], ["\"It makes me mad.", "\"미쳐 버리겠으니."], ["You play us something, Miss Swartz, do.", "미스 스워츠, 당신이 뭐든 좀 연주해 주시오."], ["Sing something, anything but the Battle of Prague.\"", "뭐든 좋으니 노래를 불러요. 「프라하 전투」만 빼고요.\""]], [["\"Shall I sing 'Blue Eyed Mary' or the air from the Cabinet?\"", "\"「푸른 눈의 메리」를 부를까요, 아니면 「캐비닛」에 나오는 노래를 부를까요?\""], ["Miss Swartz asked.", "미스 스워츠가 물었다."]], [["\"That sweet thing from the Cabinet,\"", "\"「캐비닛」에 나오는 그 감미로운 곡이요.\""], ["the sisters said.", "자매가 말했다."]], [["\"We've had that,\"", "\"그건 이미 들었잖소.\""], ["replied the misanthrope on the sofa.", "소파에 앉은 인간 혐오자가 대꾸했다."]], [["\"I can sing 'Fluvy du Tajy,'\"", "\"「플루비 뒤 타지」를 부를 수 있어요.\""], ["Swartz said, in a meek voice, \"if I had the words.\"", "스워츠가 다소곳한 목소리로 말했다. \"가사만 있다면요.\""], ["It was the last of the worthy young woman's collection.", "이것이 이 갸륵한 젊은 여인의 보유 목록 가운데 마지막 곡이었다."]], [["\"O, 'Fleuve du Tage,'\"", "\"아, 「플뢰브 뒤 타주」 말이군요.\""], ["Miss Maria cried; \"we have the song,\"", "마리아 양이 외쳤다. \"그 노래라면 우리한테 있어요.\""], ["and went off to fetch the book in which it was.", "그러고는 그 곡이 실린 악보집을 가지러 갔다."]], [["Now it happened that this song, then in the height of the fashion, had been given to the young ladies by a young friend of theirs, whose name was on the title, and Miss Swartz, having concluded the ditty with George's applause (for he remembered that it was a favourite of Amelia's), was hoping for an encore perhaps, and fiddling with the leaves of the music, when her eye fell upon the title, and she saw \"Amelia Sedley\"", "그런데 마침 그 무렵 한창 유행하던 이 노래는, 표지에 이름이 적힌 그들의 어느 젊은 친구가 선물한 것이었다."], ["written in the comer.", "미스 스워츠가 조지의 박수를 받으며 그 노래를 끝마치고는(그가 그 곡을 애밀리아가 좋아하던 것으로 기억해 두었기 때문이다), 어쩌면 앙코르를 바라며 악보 책장을 만지작거리고 있을 때, 그녀의 눈이 표지에 가닿았고, 구석에 \"애밀리아 세들리\"라고 적힌 것을 발견했다."]], [["\"Lor!\"", "\"어머나!\""], ["cried Miss Swartz, spinning swiftly round on the music-stool, \"is it my Amelia?", "미스 스워츠가 피아노 의자 위에서 휙 몸을 돌리며 외쳤다. \"이거 우리 애밀리아 아니에요?"], ["Amelia that was at Miss P.'s at Hammersmith?", "해머스미스의 핑커튼 여사님 학원에 있던 그 애밀리아 말이에요? 그 애가 틀림없어요."], ["I know it is.", "그 애예요. 그리고……"], ["It's her, and-- Tell me about her--where is she?\"", "그 애 얘기 좀 해 주세요. 지금 어디 있어요?\""]], [["\"Don't mention her,\"", "\"그 애 얘긴 꺼내지 마세요.\""], ["Miss Maria Osborne said hastily.", "마리아 오스본 양이 황급히 말했다."], ["\"Her family has disgraced itself.", "\"그 집안은 망신을 자초했어요."], ["Her father cheated Papa, and as for her, she is never to be mentioned HERE.\"", "그 애 아버지가 우리 아빠를 속였고, 그 애로 말하자면 이 집에서는 절대 입에 올려서는 안 되는 사람이에요.\""], ["This was Miss Maria's return for George's rudeness about the Battle of Prague.", "이것은 「프라하 전투」를 두고 조지가 무례하게 군 데 대한 마리아 양의 앙갚음이었다."]], [["\"Are you a friend of Amelia's?\"", "\"당신이 애밀리아의 친구요?\""], ["George said, bouncing up.", "조지가 벌떡 일어서며 말했다."], ["\"God bless you for it, Miss Swartz.", "\"그것만으로도 하느님의 축복이 있기를 빌겠소, 미스 스워츠."], ["Don't believe what the girls say.", "누이들 말은 믿지 마시오."], ["SHE'S not to blame at any rate.", "어쨌든 그녀에게는 아무 잘못도 없으니까."], ["She's the best--\"", "그녀는 세상에서 가장……\""]], [["\"You know you're not to speak about her, George,\"", "\"오라버니, 그 애 얘긴 하면 안 되는 거 알잖아요.\""], ["cried Jane.", "제인이 외쳤다."], ["\"Papa forbids it.\"", "\"아빠가 금하셨다고요.\""]], [["\"Who's to prevent me?\"", "\"누가 날 막는단 말이냐?\""], ["George cried out.", "조지가 소리쳤다."], ["\"I will speak of her.", "\"난 그녀 얘기를 하겠다."], ["I say she's the best, the kindest, the gentlest, the sweetest girl in England; and that, bankrupt or no, my sisters are not fit to hold candles to her.", "그녀는 영국에서 가장 착하고, 가장 다정하고, 가장 온화하고, 가장 사랑스러운 처녀라고 말하겠다. 그리고 파산을 했건 안 했건, 내 누이들은 그녀 앞에서 촛불을 들 자격조차 없다."], ["If you like her, go and see her, Miss Swartz; she wants friends now; and I say, God bless everybody who befriends her.", "당신이 그녀를 좋아한다면, 가서 만나 보시오, 미스 스워츠. 지금 그녀에겐 친구가 필요하오."], ["Anybody who speaks kindly of her is my friend; anybody who speaks against her is my enemy.", "그리고 나는 말하겠소, 그녀를 도와주는 모든 이에게 하느님의 축복이 있기를. 그녀를 다정하게 말하는 사람은 누구든 내 친구요, 그녀를 헐뜯는 사람은 누구든 내 적이오."], ["Thank you, Miss Swartz\"; and he went up and wrung her hand.", "고맙소, 미스 스워츠.\" 그러고는 그녀에게 다가가 그 손을 힘껏 쥐었다."]], [["\"George!", "\"오라버니!"], ["George!\"", "오라버니!\""], ["one of the sisters cried imploringly.", "자매 가운데 하나가 애원하듯 외쳤다."]], [["\"I say,\"", "\"내 말하겠다.\""], ["George said fiercely, \"I thank everybody who loves Amelia Sed--\"", "조지가 사납게 말했다. \"애밀리아 세들리를 사랑하는 사람이라면 누구에게나 감사를……\""], ["He stopped.", "그는 말을 멈췄다."], ["Old Osborne was in the room with a face livid with rage, and eyes like hot coals.", "늙은 오스본이 분노로 새파랗게 질린 얼굴에 시뻘건 숯덩이 같은 눈을 하고 방 안에 들어와 있었다."]], [["Though George had stopped in his sentence, yet, his blood being up, he was not to be cowed by all the generations of Osborne; rallying instantly, he replied to the bullying look of his father, with another so indicative of resolution and defiance that the elder man quailed in his turn, and looked away.", "조지는 말을 멈추긴 했어도, 일단 피가 끓어오른 터라 오스본 가문 전 세대가 다 덤벼든들 주눅 들 사람이 아니었다. 그는 즉시 기운을 추슬러, 으름장 놓는 아버지의 눈길에 또 다른 눈길로 맞섰으니, 그 눈빛에 어린 결연함과 반항의 기색이 어찌나 또렷했던지 이번엔 연장자 쪽이 움찔하며 시선을 돌려 버렸다."], ["He felt that the tussle was coming. \"Mrs.", "그는 한판 싸움이 다가오고 있음을 느꼈다."], ["Haggistoun, let me take you down to dinner,\"", "\"해기스턴 부인, 제가 식당까지 모시겠습니다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"Give your arm to Miss Swartz, George,\"", "\"조지, 미스 스워츠께 팔을 내드려라.\""], ["and they marched.", "그리하여 그들은 줄지어 내려갔다."]], [["\"Miss Swartz, I love Amelia, and we've been engaged almost all our lives,\"", "\"미스 스워츠, 나는 애밀리아를 사랑하오. 우리는 거의 평생 동안 약혼한 사이였소.\""], ["Osborne said to his partner; and during all the dinner, George rattled on with a volubility which surprised himself, and made his father doubly nervous for the fight which was to take place as soon as the ladies were gone.", "오스본이 옆자리 짝에게 말했다. 그리고 만찬 내내 조지는 자기 스스로도 놀랄 만큼 청산유수로 떠들어 댔으니, 그 통에 아버지는 숙녀들이 자리를 뜨는 즉시 벌어질 싸움을 두고 갑절로 안절부절못했다."]], [["The difference between the pair was, that while the father was violent and a bully, the son had thrice the nerve and courage of the parent, and could not merely make an attack, but resist it; and finding that the moment was now come when the contest between him and his father was to be decided, he took his dinner with perfect coolness and appetite before the engagement began.", "이 부자 사이의 차이라면, 아버지는 난폭하고 으름장을 잘 놓는 자였던 반면, 아들은 부모보다 세 곱절은 더 담대하고 용기가 있어서, 단지 공격을 가할 뿐 아니라 그것을 막아 낼 줄도 안다는 점이었다. 그리하여 아버지와 자기 사이의 결판이 이제 곧 날 순간이 왔음을 알아채고도, 그는 전투가 시작되기 전까지 더없이 침착하게, 또 입맛 좋게 저녁을 먹었다."], ["Old Osborne, on the contrary, was nervous, and drank much.", "반면 늙은 오스본은 안절부절못하며 술을 많이 마셨다."], ["He floundered in his conversation with the ladies, his neighbours: George's coolness only rendering him more angry.", "그는 옆자리 숙녀들과의 대화에서 갈팡질팡했고, 조지의 침착함은 그를 한층 더 화나게 할 뿐이었다."], ["It made him half mad to see the calm way in which George, flapping his napkin, and with a swaggering bow, opened the door for the ladies to leave the room; and filling himself a glass of wine, smacked it, and looked his father full in the face, as if to say, \"Gentlemen of the Guard, fire first.\"", "조지가 냅킨을 탁탁 털며 거들먹거리는 자세로 허리를 굽혀 숙녀들이 방을 나가도록 문을 열어 주고는, 자기 잔에 술을 가득 따라 입맛을 다시며, 마치 \"근위대 신사 여러분, 먼저 쏘시지요\" 하고 말하기라도 하듯 아버지의 얼굴을 똑바로 쳐다보는 그 태연한 꼴을 보고 있자니, 늙은이는 거의 미칠 지경이었다."], ["The old man also took a supply of ammunition, but his decanter clinked against the glass as he tried to fill it.", "노인도 탄약을 채워 넣었지만, 술을 따르려는 그의 손에 술병이 잔에 부딪쳐 쨍그랑 소리를 냈다."]], [["After giving a great heave, and with a purple choking face, he then began.", "이윽고 한 번 크게 숨을 몰아쉬더니, 자줏빛으로 숨이 막힐 듯한 얼굴로 그는 입을 열었다."], ["\"How dare you, sir, mention that person's name before Miss Swartz to-day, in my drawing-room?", "\"이놈, 네가 어찌 감히 오늘 내 응접실에서, 미스 스워츠 앞에서 그 계집의 이름을 입에 올렸느냐?"], ["I ask you, sir, how dare you do it?\"", "묻겠다, 이놈아, 네가 어찌 감히 그런 짓을 했느냔 말이다.\""]], [["\"Stop, sir,\"", "\"그만하세요, 아버지.\""], ["says George, \"don't say dare, sir.", "조지가 말했다. \"감히라는 말은 쓰지 마십시오."], ["Dare isn't a word to be used to a Captain in the British Army.\"", "감히라는 말은 영국 육군 대위에게 쓸 말이 못 됩니다.\""]], [["\"I shall say what I like to my son, sir.", "\"난 내 아들한테 하고 싶은 말은 뭐든 할 테다, 이놈아."], ["I can cut him off with a shilling if I like.", "내키면 한 푼만 쥐여 주고 의절할 수도 있어."], ["I can make him a beggar if I like.", "내키면 거지로 만들어 버릴 수도 있고."], ["I WILL say what I like,\"", "난 하고 싶은 말은 하고야 말 테다.\""], ["the elder said.", "연장자가 말했다."]], [["\"I'm a gentleman though I AM your son, sir,\" George answered haughtily.", "\"제가 아버지의 아들이긴 해도 신사입니다, 아버지.\""], ["\"Any communications which you have to make to me, or any orders which you may please to give, I beg may be couched in that kind of language which I am accustomed to hear.\"", "조지가 거만하게 대꾸했다. \"저에게 하실 말씀이나 내리실 분부가 있다면, 제가 익히 들어 온 그런 종류의 언사로 표현해 주시기를 청합니다.\""]], [["Whenever the lad assumed his haughty manner, it always created either great awe or great irritation in the parent.", "이 젊은이가 거만한 태도를 취할 때면, 그것은 어김없이 아비에게 커다란 경외감이나 커다란 분노를 불러일으켰다."], ["Old Osborne stood in secret terror of his son as a better gentleman than himself; and perhaps my readers may have remarked in their experience of this Vanity Fair of ours, that there is no character which a low-minded man so much mistrusts as that of a gentleman.", "늙은 오스본은 아들이 자기보다 더 나은 신사라는 점에서 남몰래 그를 두려워했다. 그리고 우리 독자 여러분께서도 우리네 이 허영의 시장에서 겪어 본 바, 비루한 인간이 가장 미더워하지 못하는 인물이 바로 신사라는 사실을 더러 눈여겨보았을 것이다."]], [["\"My father didn't give me the education you have had, nor the advantages you have had, nor the money you have had.", "\"우리 아버진 너처럼 교육을 시켜 주지도 않았고, 너처럼 혜택을 베풀어 주지도 않았고, 너처럼 돈을 주지도 않았다."], ["If I had kept the company SOME FOLKS have had through MY MEANS, perhaps my son wouldn't have any reason to brag, sir, of his SUPERIORITY and WEST END AIRS (these words were uttered in the elder Osborne's most sarcastic tones).", "만일 내가 어떤 사람들이 내 덕분에 어울리고 다닌 그런 패거리들과 어울렸더라면, 아마 내 자식이 제 잘난 우월함이니 웨스트엔드의 거드름이니 하는 걸 떠벌릴 거리도 없었을 게다(이 말은 연장자 오스본 특유의 더없이 빈정대는 어조로 흘러나왔다)."], ["But it wasn't considered the part of a gentleman, in MY time, for a man to insult his father.", "하지만 내가 젊었던 시절엔, 사내가 제 아비를 모욕하는 게 신사가 할 짓으로 여겨지지 않았어."], ["If I'd done any such thing, mine would have kicked me downstairs, sir.\"", "내가 그런 짓을 했더라면 우리 아버지가 날 계단 아래로 걷어차 버렸을 게다, 이놈아.\""]], [["\"I never insulted you, sir.", "\"전 아버지를 모욕한 적이 없습니다."], ["I said I begged you to remember your son was a gentleman as well as yourself.", "다만 아버지의 아들도 아버지 못지않은 신사라는 점을 기억해 주십사 청했을 따름입니다."], ["I know very well that you give me plenty of money,\"", "아버지가 제게 돈을 넉넉히 주신다는 건 저도 잘 압니다.\""], ["said George (fingering a bundle of notes which he had got in the morning from Mr.", "조지가 (그날 아침 초퍼 씨에게서 받은 지폐 뭉치를 만지작거리며) 말했다."], ["Chopper). \"You tell it me often enough, sir.", "\"그 말씀은 충분히 자주 하시니까요, 아버지."], ["There's no fear of my forgetting it.\"", "잊어버릴 염려는 없습니다.\""]], [["\"I wish you'd remember other things as well, sir,\"", "\"다른 일들도 그만큼 잘 기억해 주면 좋겠구나, 이놈아.\" 아비가 대꾸했다."], ["the sire answered.", "\"이 집에서는 말이다, 네놈이 그 잘난 행차로 이 집을 빛내 주시는 동안에는 말이지, 대위 나리, 주인은 나란 걸 기억해 주면 좋겠다. 그리고 그 이름은, 그 이름은……"], ["\"I wish you'd remember that in this house--so long as you choose to HONOUR it with your COMPANY, Captain--I'm the master, and that name, and that that--that you--that I say--\"", "네놈이…… 내 말은……\""]], [["\"That what, sir?\"", "\"그 이름이 뭐 어떻다는 겁니까, 아버지?\""], ["George asked, with scarcely a sneer, filling another glass of claret.", "조지가 거의 비웃음에 가까운 기색으로 클라레를 한 잔 더 따르며 물었다."]], [["\"----!\"", "\"……!\" 아버지가 째지는 소리로 욕설을 터뜨리며 폭발했다."], ["burst out his father with a screaming oath--\"that the name of those Sedleys never be mentioned here, sir--not one of the whole damned lot of 'em, sir.\"", "\"그 세들리네 이름은 이 집에서 두 번 다시 입에 올리지 말란 말이다, 이놈아. 그 빌어먹을 족속 중에 단 한 놈도, 이놈아.\""]], [["\"It wasn't I, sir, that introduced Miss Sedley's name.", "\"세들리 양의 이름을 꺼낸 건 제가 아닙니다, 아버지."], ["It was my sisters who spoke ill of her to Miss Swartz; and by Jove I'll defend her wherever I go.", "미스 스워츠에게 그녀를 험담한 건 제 누이들이었어요. 그리고 맹세코 저는 어딜 가든 그녀를 옹호하겠습니다."], ["Nobody shall speak lightly of that name in my presence.", "제 앞에서 그 이름을 함부로 말하게 두지 않겠어요."], ["Our family has done her quite enough injury already, I think, and may leave off reviling her now she's down.", "우리 집안은 그녀에게 이미 충분히 못할 짓을 했다고 봅니다. 이제 그녀가 영락했으니 헐뜯는 짓은 그만둬도 좋을 텐데요."], ["I'll shoot any man but you who says a word against her.\"", "그녀를 두고 한마디라도 험담하는 자가 있다면, 아버지만 빼고 누구든 총으로 쏘아 버리겠습니다.\""]], [["\"Go on, sir, go on,\"", "\"계속해 봐, 이놈, 계속해 봐.\""], ["the old gentleman said, his eyes starting out of his head.", "노신사가 눈알이 튀어나올 듯한 얼굴로 말했다."]], [["\"Go on about what, sir?", "\"뭘 계속하라는 겁니까, 아버지?"], ["about the way in which we've treated that angel of a girl?", "우리가 그 천사 같은 처녀를 대접한 꼴 말입니까?"], ["Who told me to love her?", "누가 저더러 그녀를 사랑하라 했습니까?"], ["It was your doing.", "아버지가 그러셨잖아요."], ["I might have chosen elsewhere, and looked higher, perhaps, than your society: but I obeyed you.", "다른 데서 짝을 고를 수도 있었고, 어쩌면 아버지 사교계보다 더 높은 데를 넘볼 수도 있었지만, 저는 아버지께 순종했습니다. 그런데 이제 와 그녀의 마음이 제 것이 되자, 그걸 내던지고 그녀를 벌하라고, 어쩌면 죽이라고 명령하시는군요."], ["And now that her heart's mine you give me orders to fling it away, and punish her, kill her perhaps--for the faults of other people.", "그것도 남들의 잘못 때문에 말입니다. 맹세코 이건 부끄러운 짓입니다.\""], ["It's a shame, by Heavens,\"", "조지는 말을 이어 갈수록 격정과 흥분에 점점 더 사로잡혀 갔다. \"어린 처녀의 애정을 가지고 이랬다저랬다 농락하다니요."], ["said George, working himself up into passion and enthusiasm as he proceeded, \"to play at fast and loose with a young girl's affections--and with such an angel as that--one so superior to the people amongst whom she lived, that she might have excited envy, only she was so good and gentle, that it's a wonder anybody dared to hate her.", "그것도 그런 천사를요. 함께 살던 사람들보다 어찌나 빼어났던지 시샘을 살 만도 했건만, 워낙 착하고 온화한 사람이라 감히 그녀를 미워할 엄두를 낸 자가 있었다는 게 신기할 지경이었지요."], ["If I desert her, sir, do you suppose she forgets me?\"", "제가 그녀를 버린다면, 아버지, 그녀가 절 잊을 것 같습니까?\""]], [["\"I ain't going to have any of this dam sentimental nonsense and humbug here, sir,\"", "\"이 집에서 그따위 빌어먹을 감상적인 헛소리며 허튼수작은 더는 못 듣겠다, 이놈아.\""], ["the father cried out.", "아비가 고래고래 소리쳤다."], ["\"There shall be no beggar-marriages in my family.", "\"내 집안엔 거지와의 혼인 따윈 없을 게다."], ["If you choose to fling away eight thousand a year, which you may have for the asking, you may do it: but by Jove you take your pack and walk out of this house, sir.", "청하기만 하면 굴러들어 올 연 팔천 파운드를 내던지고 싶거든, 그래 던져 버려라. 하지만 맹세코 네 보따리나 챙겨서 이 집을 나가거라, 이놈아."], ["Will you do as I tell you, once for all, sir, or will you not?\"", "내 말대로 하겠느냐, 이번 한 번으로 끝장을 보자, 하겠느냐 말겠느냐?\""]], [["\"Marry that mulatto woman?\"", "\"그 물라토 여자랑 혼인하라고요?\""], ["George said, pulling up his shirt-collars.", "조지가 셔츠 깃을 추켜올리며 말했다."], ["\"I don't like the colour, sir.", "\"전 그 색깔이 마음에 안 듭니다, 아버지."], ["Ask the black that sweeps opposite Fleet Market, sir.", "플리트 시장 맞은편을 쓰는 흑인한테나 청해 보시지요."], ["I'm not going to marry a Hottentot Venus.\"", "전 호텐토트의 비너스랑은 혼인하지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["Mr. Osborne pulled frantically at the cord by which he was accustomed to summon the butler when he wanted wine--and almost black in the face, ordered that functionary to call a coach for Captain Osborne.", "오스본 씨는 와인이 필요할 때 집사를 부르려고 늘 잡아당기던 그 줄을 미친 듯이 잡아챘고, 얼굴이 거의 시커멓게 변한 채로 그 하인에게 오스본 대위를 위해 마차를 불러오라 명했다."]], [["\"I've done it,\"", "\"끝장을 봤다네.\""], ["said George, coming into the Slaughters' an hour afterwards, looking very pale.", "한 시간 뒤 슬로터스 식당으로 들어서며 조지가 새파랗게 질린 얼굴로 말했다."]], [["\"What, my boy?\"", "\"뭘 말인가, 이 친구야?\""], ["says Dobbin.", "도빈이 물었다."]], [["George told what had passed between his father and himself.", "조지는 아버지와 자기 사이에 오간 이야기를 들려주었다."]], [["\"I'll marry her to-morrow,\"", "\"난 내일 그녀와 혼인하겠네.\""], ["he said with an oath.", "그가 맹세코 다짐하듯 말했다."], ["\"I love her more every day, Dobbin.\"", "\"날이 갈수록 그녀가 더 사랑스러워, 도빈.\""]], [["A Marriage and Part of a Honeymoon\nEnemies the most obstinate and courageous can't hold out against starvation; so the elder Osborne felt himself pretty easy about his adversary in the encounter we have just described; and as soon as George's supplies fell short, confidently expected his unconditional submission.", "아무리 완강하고 용맹한 적이라 해도 굶주림 앞에서는 버틸 재간이 없는 법이다. 그러니 앞서 묘사한 그 대결에서 늙은 오즈본은 자기 적수에 대해 꽤나 마음을 놓고 있었다."], ["It was unlucky, to be sure, that the lad should have secured a stock of provisions on the very day when the first encounter took place; but this relief was only temporary, old Osborne thought, and would but delay George's surrender.", "조지의 보급이 바닥나기만 하면 무조건 항복해 올 것이라고 자신만만하게 기대했던 것이다. 물론 첫 대결이 벌어진 바로 그날 그 녀석이 식량을 한 무더기 확보해 두었다는 것은 유감스러운 일이었다."], ["No communication passed between father and son for some days.", "하지만 늙은 오즈본의 생각으로는 그런 구원이란 일시적인 것에 지나지 않으며, 조지의 항복을 잠시 늦출 뿐이었다. 며칠 동안 부자 사이에는 아무런 연락도 오가지 않았다."], ["The former was sulky at this silence, but not disquieted; for, as he said, he knew where he could put the screw upon George, and only waited the result of that operation.", "아버지는 이 침묵에 부루퉁했지만 불안해하지는 않았다. 자기 말마따나, 조지에게 어디다 죔쇠를 들이댈 수 있는지 잘 알고 있었고, 그저 그 작전의 결과를 기다릴 뿐이었기 때문이다."], ["He told the sisters the upshot of the dispute between them, but ordered them to take no notice of the matter, and welcome George on his return as if nothing had happened.", "그는 딸들에게 둘 사이에 벌어진 다툼의 자초지종을 들려주면서도, 그 일에 대해서는 일절 내색하지 말고 조지가 돌아오면 아무 일도 없었다는 듯이 맞아 주라고 일러두었다."], ["His cover was laid as usual every day, and perhaps the old gentleman rather anxiously expected him; but he never came.", "그의 식사 자리는 날마다 여느 때처럼 차려졌고, 어쩌면 이 노신사는 다소 애타게 아들을 기다렸는지도 모른다."], ["Some one inquired at the Slaughters'", "그러나 조지는 끝내 오지 않았다."], ["regarding him, where it was said that he and his friend Captain Dobbin had left town.", "누군가가 슬로터스에 들러 그의 소식을 묻자, 그가 친구인 도빈 대위와 함께 런던을 떠났다는 대답이 돌아왔다."]], [["One gusty, raw day at the end of April--the rain whipping the pavement of that ancient street where the old Slaughters'", "사월 말의 어느 거센 바람이 부는 을씨년스러운 날이었다. 옛 슬로터스 커피하우스가 자리했던 그 오래된 거리의 포도를 빗줄기가 후려치고 있었다."], ["Coffee-house was once situated--George Osborne came into the coffee-room, looking very haggard and pale; although dressed rather smartly in a blue coat and brass buttons, and a neat buff waistcoat of the fashion of those days.", "조지 오즈본은 커피하우스 안으로 들어섰는데, 몹시 초췌하고 핼쑥해 보였다. 그래도 차림새는 제법 말쑥해서, 놋쇠 단추가 달린 파란 외투에 그 시절 유행하던 단정한 담황색 조끼를 입고 있었다."], ["Here was his friend Captain Dobbin, in blue and brass too, having abandoned the military frock and French-grey trousers, which were the usual coverings of his lanky person.", "그곳에는 그의 친구 도빈 대위도 역시 파란 옷에 놋쇠 단추 차림으로 와 있었는데, 그의 호리호리한 몸을 늘 감싸던 군용 프록코트와 회청색 바지는 벗어 둔 모습이었다."]], [["Dobbin had been in the coffee-room for an hour or more.", "도빈은 한 시간 남짓 커피하우스에 있었다. 신문이란 신문은 죄다 들춰 보았으나 도무지 읽히지가 않았다."], ["He had tried all the papers, but could not read them.", "시계를 수십 번도 더 쳐다보았고, 빗방울이 후드득 떨어지는 거리도 내다보았다. 사람들이 나막신을 신고 또각또각 지나가며 번들거리는 돌바닥 위에 긴 그림자를 남기고 있었다."], ["He had looked at the clock many scores of times; and at the street, where the rain was pattering down, and the people as they clinked by in pattens, left long reflections on the shining stone: he tattooed at the table: he bit his nails most completely, and nearly to the quick (he was accustomed to ornament his great big hands in this way): he balanced the tea-spoon dexterously on the milk jug: upset it, &c., &c.; and in fact showed those signs of disquietude, and practised those desperate attempts at amusement, which men are accustomed to employ when very anxious, and expectant, and perturbed in mind.", "그는 탁자를 손가락으로 두드렸고, 손톱을 거의 속살이 드러나도록 깡그리 물어뜯었으며(원래 자기 그 커다란 두 손을 이런 식으로 꾸미는 버릇이 있었다), 찻숟가락을 우유 단지 위에 능숙하게 올려 균형을 잡다가 엎질러 버리는 등등의 짓을 했다. 요컨대 그는 마음이 몹시 초조하고 무언가를 기다리며 안절부절못할 때 사람들이 으레 드러내는 그런 불안의 징후를 내보였고, 또 그런 때 으레 시도하는 그 필사적인 소일거리들을 부려 보고 있었던 것이다."]], [["Some of his comrades, gentlemen who used the room, joked him about the splendour of his costume and his agitation of manner.", "이 방을 드나드는 신사인 그의 동료들 가운데 몇몇이, 그 화려한 차림새와 들떠 있는 거동을 두고 그를 놀려 댔다."], ["One asked him if he was going to be married?", "한 사람은 그에게 결혼이라도 하느냐고 물었다."], ["Dobbin laughed, and said he would send his acquaintance (Major Wagstaff of the Engineers) a piece of cake when that event took place.", "도빈은 웃으며, 그런 일이 벌어지면 그 지인(공병대의 왜그스태프 소령)에게 케이크 한 조각을 보내 주마고 대답했다."], ["At length Captain Osborne made his appearance, very smartly dressed, but very pale and agitated as we have said.", "마침내 오즈본 대위가 모습을 드러냈는데, 차림은 무척 말쑥했으나 앞서 말했듯이 몹시 핼쑥하고 동요한 기색이었다."], ["He wiped his pale face with a large yellow bandanna pocket-handkerchief that was prodigiously scented.", "그는 향수를 엄청나게 뿌린 커다란 노란색 반다나 손수건으로 핼쑥한 얼굴을 닦았다."], ["He shook hands with Dobbin, looked at the clock, and told John, the waiter, to bring him some curacao.", "그는 도빈과 악수를 나누고, 시계를 본 뒤, 웨이터 존에게 퀴라소를 가져오라고 일렀다."], ["Of this cordial he swallowed off a couple of glasses with nervous eagerness.", "그러고는 이 술을 두어 잔, 신경질적인 조급함으로 단숨에 들이켰다."], ["His friend asked with some interest about his health.", "그의 친구는 다소 걱정스레 그의 건강을 물었다."]], [["\"Couldn't get a wink of sleep till daylight, Dob,\"", "\"날이 샐 때까지 한숨도 못 잤어, 도브.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he. \"Infernal headache and fever.", "\"지독한 두통에 열까지 나서 말이야."], ["Got up at nine, and went down to the Hummums for a bath.", "아홉 시에 일어나서 허멈스로 목욕하러 갔지."], ["I say, Dob, I feel just as I did on the morning I went out with Rocket at Quebec.\"", "이봐, 도브, 꼭 퀘벡에서 로켓을 타고 출진하던 그날 아침 같은 기분이야.\""]], [["\"So do I,\"", "\"나도 그래.\""], ["William responded.", "윌리엄이 대답했다."], ["\"I was a deuced deal more nervous than you were that morning.", "\"그날 아침엔 너보다 내가 훨씬 더 떨었지."], ["You made a famous breakfast, I remember.", "너는 아침을 푸짐하게 먹었던 게 기억나는걸."], ["Eat something now.\"", "자, 지금도 뭐 좀 들어.\""]], [["\"You're a good old fellow, Will.", "\"넌 참 좋은 친구야, 윌."], ["I'll drink your health, old boy, and farewell to--\"", "네 건강을 위해 한잔 들겠어, 친구. 그리고 작별을 고하지, 저—\""]], [["\"No, no; two glasses are enough,\"", "\"안 돼, 안 돼. 두 잔이면 충분해.\""], ["Dobbin interrupted him.", "도빈이 그의 말을 가로막았다."], ["\"Here, take away the liqueurs, John.", "\"이봐, 존, 술은 치워 주게."], ["Have some cayenne-pepper with your fowl.", "닭 요리에는 카옌 후추를 좀 쳐서 먹어."], ["Make haste though, for it is time we were there.\"", "그래도 서둘러야 해. 이제 그곳에 가 있어야 할 시간이니까.\""]], [["It was about half an hour from twelve when this brief meeting and colloquy took place between the two captains.", "이 두 대위 사이에 이 짧은 만남과 대화가 오간 것은 열두 시가 되기 삼십 분쯤 전이었다."], ["A coach, into which Captain Osborne's servant put his master's desk and dressing-case, had been in waiting for some time; and into this the two gentlemen hurried under an umbrella, and the valet mounted on the box, cursing the rain and the dampness of the coachman who was steaming beside him.", "오즈본 대위의 하인이 주인의 책상과 화장 도구함을 실어 둔 마차가 한동안 대기하고 있었다. 두 신사는 우산 하나를 받쳐 들고 그 마차 안으로 황급히 올라탔고, 시종은 빗줄기와 옆자리에서 김을 피워 올리고 있는 마부의 축축함을 욕하며 마부석에 올라앉았다."], ["\"We shall find a better trap than this at the church-door,\"", "\"교회 앞에 가면 이것보다 나은 마차가 있을 거야.\" 그가 말했다."], ["says he; \"that's a comfort.\"", "\"그게 그나마 위안이지.\""], ["And the carriage drove on, taking the road down Piccadilly, where Apsley House and St.", "마차는 피커딜리 길을 따라 달려 내려갔다."], ["George's Hospital wore red jackets still; where there were oil-lamps; where Achilles was not yet born; nor the Pimlico arch raised; nor the hideous equestrian monster which pervades it and the neighbourhood; and so they drove down by Brompton to a certain chapel near the Fulham Road there.", "그곳은 앱슬리 하우스와 세인트조지 병원이 아직 붉은 외투를 걸치고 있던 시절이요, 기름등이 켜져 있고, 아킬레우스 상은 아직 태어나지도 않았으며, 핌리코 아치도 세워지지 않았고, 그 일대를 온통 뒤덮은 흉측한 기마상 괴물도 없던 시절이었다. 그리하여 그들은 브롬프턴을 지나 풀럼 로드 근처의 어느 예배당으로 마차를 몰아갔다."]], [["A chariot was in waiting with four horses; likewise a coach of the kind called glass coaches.", "말 네 마리가 끄는 사륜마차 한 대가 대기하고 있었고, 유리 마차라 불리는 종류의 마차도 한 대 있었다."], ["Only a very few idlers were collected on account of the dismal rain.", "음울한 비 탓에 한가한 구경꾼은 아주 적게 모여 있었다."]], [["\"Hang it!\"", "\"제기랄!\""], ["said George, \"I said only a pair.\"", "조지가 말했다. \"난 두 마리면 된다고 했는데.\""]], [["\"My master would have four,\" said Mr.", "\"저희 주인 나리께서 네 마리를 원하셨습죠.\""], ["Joseph Sedley's servant, who was in waiting; and he and Mr.", "대기하고 있던 조지프 세들리 씨의 하인이 말했다."], ["Osborne's man agreed as they followed George and William into the church, that it was a \"reg'lar shabby turn hout; and with scarce so much as a breakfast or a wedding faviour.\"", "그와 오즈본 씨의 하인은 조지와 윌리엄을 따라 교회로 들어가면서, 이건 \"영 초라한 행차다, 아침 식사 한 끼는커녕 결혼 장식 리본 하나 변변히 없으니\" 하고 의견을 모았다."]], [["\"Here you are,\"", "\"여기 왔네그려.\""], ["said our old friend, Jos Sedley, coming forward.", "우리의 옛 친구 조스 세들리가 앞으로 나서며 말했다."], ["\"You're five minutes late, George, my boy. What a day, eh?", "\"오 분 늦었어, 조지, 이 친구야."], ["Demmy, it's like the commencement of the rainy season in Bengal.", "날씨 한번 고약하군, 응? 제기랄, 벵골의 우기가 시작될 때랑 똑같구먼."], ["But you'll find my carriage is watertight.", "그래도 내 마차는 물 한 방울 안 새니까 걱정 말게."], ["Come along, my mother and Emmy are in the vestry.\"", "자, 어서 오게. 어머니와 에미는 제의실에 계시네.\""]], [["Jos Sedley was splendid.", "조스 세들리는 휘황찬란했다."], ["He was fatter than ever.", "어느 때보다도 더 살이 쪄 있었다."], ["His shirt collars were higher; his face was redder; his shirt-frill flaunted gorgeously out of his variegated waistcoat.", "셔츠 칼라는 더 높아졌고, 얼굴은 더 붉어졌으며, 셔츠 주름 장식이 알록달록한 조끼 사이로 화려하게 펄럭였다."], ["Varnished boots were not invented as yet; but the Hessians on his beautiful legs shone so, that they must have been the identical pair in which the gentleman in the old picture used to shave himself; and on his light green coat there bloomed a fine wedding favour, like a great white spreading magnolia.", "에나멜 부츠란 아직 발명되기 전이었으나, 그의 멋진 다리를 감싼 헤시안 부츠가 어찌나 반들거리던지, 저 옛 그림 속 신사가 면도할 때 거울 삼았다는 바로 그 부츠임에 틀림없어 보일 지경이었다. 그리고 그의 연녹색 외투에는, 활짝 핀 커다란 흰 목련처럼 멋들어진 결혼 장식 리본이 피어 있었다."]], [["In a word, George had thrown the great cast.", "한마디로, 조지는 운명의 큰 주사위를 던진 것이었다."], ["He was going to be married.", "그는 결혼하려던 참이었다."], ["Hence his pallor and nervousness--his sleepless night and agitation in the morning.", "그의 창백함과 초조함, 잠 못 이룬 밤과 아침의 동요는 다 거기서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["I have heard people who have gone through the same thing own to the same emotion.", "나는 같은 일을 겪어 본 사람들이 똑같은 심경을 토로하는 것을 들은 적이 있다."], ["After three or four ceremonies, you get accustomed to it, no doubt; but the first dip, everybody allows, is awful.", "서너 번쯤 그 의식을 치르고 나면 분명 익숙해지겠지만, 첫 번째 입수만큼은 누구나 끔찍하다고 인정하는 것이다."]], [["The bride was dressed in a brown silk pelisse (as Captain Dobbin has since informed me), and wore a straw bonnet with a pink ribbon; over the bonnet she had a veil of white Chantilly lace, a gift from Mr. Joseph Sedley, her brother.", "신부는 갈색 비단 펠리스를 입고(이 점은 그 뒤 도빈 대위가 내게 일러 준 바이다), 분홍 리본을 단 밀짚 보닛을 쓰고 있었다."], ["Captain Dobbin himself had asked leave to present her with a gold chain and watch, which she sported on this occasion; and her mother gave her her diamond brooch--almost the only trinket which was left to the old lady.", "보닛 위로는 흰 샹티이 레이스 베일을 드리웠는데, 이는 그녀의 오라비 조지프 세들리 씨가 준 선물이었다. 도빈 대위 본인은 그녀에게 금줄 시계를 선물하게 해 달라고 청한 터였고, 그녀는 이날 그것을 차고 있었다."], ["As the service went on, Mrs. Sedley sat and whimpered a great deal in a pew, consoled by the Irish maid-servant and Mrs.", "그녀의 어머니는 다이아몬드 브로치를 주었는데, 이 노부인에게 남은 거의 유일한 패물이었다."], ["Clapp from the lodgings. Old Sedley would not be present.", "예식이 진행되는 동안 세들리 부인은 신도석에 앉아 줄곧 훌쩍였고, 아일랜드인 하녀와 하숙집의 클랩 부인이 그녀를 달랬다."], ["Jos acted for his father, giving away the bride, whilst Captain Dobbin stepped up as groomsman to his friend George.", "늙은 세들리는 참석하려 들지 않았다. 조스가 아버지를 대신해 신부를 인도했고, 도빈 대위는 친구 조지의 들러리로 나섰다."]], [["There was nobody in the church besides the officiating persons and the small marriage party and their attendants.", "교회 안에는 예식을 집전하는 이들과 조촐한 결혼식 일행, 그리고 그들의 시중꾼 말고는 아무도 없었다."], ["The two valets sat aloof superciliously.", "두 하인은 거만하게 떨어져 앉아 있었다."], ["The rain came rattling down on the windows.", "빗줄기가 창문을 후드득 때렸다."], ["In the intervals of the service you heard it, and the sobbing of old Mrs. Sedley in the pew.", "예식이 잠시 끊길 때면 그 빗소리와, 신도석에서 흐느끼는 늙은 세들리 부인의 울음소리가 들렸다."], ["The parson's tones echoed sadly through the empty walls.", "목사의 음성이 텅 빈 벽을 따라 구슬프게 울렸다."], ["Osborne's \"I will\" was sounded in very deep bass.", "오즈본의 \"맹세합니다\"는 아주 깊은 저음으로 울려 나왔다."], ["Emmy's response came fluttering up to her lips from her heart, but was scarcely heard by anybody except Captain Dobbin.", "에미의 응답은 마음속에서 떨리며 입술로 솟아올랐으나, 도빈 대위 말고는 누구의 귀에도 거의 닿지 않았다."]], [["When the service was completed, Jos Sedley came forward and kissed his sister, the bride, for the first time for many months--George's look of gloom had gone, and he seemed quite proud and radiant.", "예식이 끝나자 조스 세들리가 앞으로 나서서, 여러 달 만에 처음으로 신부가 된 누이에게 입을 맞추었다. 조지의 침울한 기색은 사라지고, 그는 자못 자랑스럽고 환한 표정이었다."], ["\"It's your turn, William,\"", "\"이제 네 차례야, 윌리엄.\""], ["says he, putting his hand fondly upon Dobbin's shoulder; and Dobbin went up and touched Amelia on the cheek.", "그가 도빈의 어깨에 다정하게 손을 얹으며 말했다. 도빈은 다가가 애밀리아의 뺨에 가볍게 입을 맞추었다."]], [["Then they went into the vestry and signed the register.", "그러고 나서 그들은 제의실로 들어가 혼인부에 서명했다."], ["\"God bless you, Old Dobbin,\"", "\"하느님의 가호가 있기를, 도빈 친구.\""], ["George said, grasping him by the hand, with something very like moisture glistening in his eyes.", "조지가 그의 손을 꽉 움켜쥐며 말했는데, 그 두 눈에는 물기 같은 것이 어른거렸다."], ["William replied only by nodding his head.", "윌리엄은 그저 고개만 끄덕여 답했다."], ["His heart was too full to say much.", "가슴이 너무 벅차서 많은 말을 할 수 없었던 것이다."]], [["\"Write directly, and come down as soon as you can, you know,\"", "\"바로 편지해, 그리고 되도록 빨리 내려오라고, 알지?\""], ["Osborne said. After Mrs.", "오즈본이 말했다."], ["Sedley had taken an hysterical adieu of her daughter, the pair went off to the carriage.", "세들리 부인이 발작하듯 딸과 작별 인사를 나눈 뒤, 신랑 신부는 마차로 향했다."], ["\"Get out of the way, you little devils,\"", "\"비켜라, 이 작은 악동들아.\""], ["George cried to a small crowd of damp urchins, that were hanging about the chapel-door.", "조지가 예배당 문간에 몰려 있는 비에 젖은 부랑아 떼를 향해 소리쳤다."], ["The rain drove into the bride and bridegroom's faces as they passed to the chariot.", "신랑 신부가 사륜마차로 걸어가는 동안 빗줄기가 그들의 얼굴을 후려쳤다."], ["The postilions' favours draggled on their dripping jackets.", "마부들의 장식 리본이 흠뻑 젖은 외투 위로 축 늘어졌다."], ["The few children made a dismal cheer, as the carriage, splashing mud, drove away.", "마차가 진흙을 튀기며 떠나가자, 몇 안 되는 아이들이 음울한 환호를 보냈다."]], [["William Dobbin stood in the church-porch, looking at it, a queer figure.", "윌리엄 도빈은 교회 현관에 서서 그 광경을 바라보았는데, 묘한 행색이었다."], ["The small crew of spectators jeered him.", "조무래기 구경꾼 무리가 그를 비웃었다."], ["He was not thinking about them or their laughter.", "그는 그들도, 그들의 비웃음도 안중에 없었다."]], [["\"Come home and have some tiffin, Dobbin,\"", "\"집에 가서 점심이나 좀 들세, 도빈.\""], ["a voice cried behind him; as a pudgy hand was laid on his shoulder, and the honest fellow's reverie was interrupted.", "그의 등 뒤에서 한 목소리가 외쳤고, 통통한 손 하나가 그의 어깨에 얹히면서 이 정직한 사내의 상념이 깨졌다."], ["But the Captain had no heart to go a-feasting with Jos Sedley.", "그러나 대위는 조스 세들리와 어울려 잔치를 즐길 마음이 없었다."], ["He put the weeping old lady and her attendants into the carriage along with Jos, and left them without any farther words passing.", "그는 우는 노부인과 그 시중꾼들을 조스와 함께 마차에 태우고는, 더 이상 아무 말도 나누지 않고 그들과 헤어졌다."], ["This carriage, too, drove away, and the urchins gave another sarcastical cheer.", "이 마차도 떠나갔고, 부랑아들이 또 한 번 빈정대는 환호를 보냈다."]], [["\"Here, you little beggars,\"", "\"자, 받아라, 이 거지 녀석들아.\""], ["Dobbin said, giving some sixpences amongst them, and then went off by himself through the rain.", "도빈이 그들에게 육 펜스짜리 동전을 몇 닢 나눠 주며 말한 뒤, 빗속을 홀로 걸어갔다."], ["It was all over.", "모든 게 끝났다."], ["They were married, and happy, he prayed God.", "그들은 결혼했고, 부디 행복하기를 그는 하느님께 빌었다."], ["Never since he was a boy had he felt so miserable and so lonely.", "소년 시절 이후로 그가 이토록 비참하고 외롭게 느낀 적은 없었다."], ["He longed with a heart-sick yearning for the first few days to be over, that he might see her again.", "그는 가슴앓이하는 듯한 그리움으로, 그녀를 다시 볼 수 있도록 이 처음 며칠이 어서 지나가기를 간절히 바랐다."]], [["Some ten days after the above ceremony, three young men of our acquaintance were enjoying that beautiful prospect of bow windows on the one side and blue sea on the other, which Brighton affords to the traveller.", "위의 예식이 있은 지 열흘쯤 지나서, 우리가 아는 세 젊은이가 브라이턴이 나그네에게 선사하는 그 아름다운 풍경, 즉 한쪽으로는 활처럼 휜 출창들, 다른 쪽으로는 푸른 바다가 펼쳐지는 그 경치를 즐기고 있었다. 런던에서 온 사람은 때로는 바다 쪽을 황홀하게 바라본다."], ["Sometimes it is towards the ocean--smiling with countless dimples, speckled with white sails, with a hundred bathing-machines kissing the skirt of his blue garment--that the Londoner looks enraptured: sometimes, on the contrary, a lover of human nature rather than of prospects of any kind, it is towards the bow windows that he turns, and that swarm of human life which they exhibit.", "수없는 보조개로 미소 짓고, 흰 돛들로 점점이 수놓이고, 수백 대의 해수욕 탈의 마차가 그 푸른 옷자락에 입 맞추는 바다 쪽을. 그런가 하면 때로는, 어떤 종류의 경치보다도 인간 본성을 더 사랑하는 이라면 도리어 그 출창들 쪽으로, 그리고 그 안에 들끓는 인간 군상 쪽으로 눈길을 돌린다."], ["From one issue the notes of a piano, which a young lady in ringlets practises six hours daily, to the delight of the fellow-lodgers: at another, lovely Polly, the nurse-maid, may be seen dandling Master Omnium in her arms: whilst Jacob, his papa, is beheld eating prawns, and devouring the Times for breakfast, at the window below.", "어느 창에서는 피아노 소리가 흘러나오는데, 곱슬머리 아가씨가 같은 하숙인들을 기쁘게 하느라 날마다 여섯 시간씩 연습하는 것이다. 또 다른 창에서는 사랑스러운 보모 폴리가 옴니엄 도련님을 품에 안고 어르는 모습이 보이고, 아래쪽 창에서는 그 도련님의 아빠 제이컵이 새우를 까먹으며 아침 식사 삼아 타임스지를 게걸스레 읽고 있는 모습이 보인다."], ["Yonder are the Misses Leery, who are looking out for the young officers of the Heavies, who are pretty sure to be pacing the cliff; or again it is a City man, with a nautical turn, and a telescope, the size of a six-pounder, who has his instrument pointed seawards, so as to command every pleasure-boat, herring-boat, or bathing-machine that comes to, or quits, the shore, &c., &c.", "저쪽엔 리어리 자매가 있어, 분명 절벽 위를 거닐고 있을 중기병대 젊은 장교들을 내다보고 있다. 또는 항해에 취미가 있는 어느 시티 사람이 육 파운드 포만 한 망원경을 들고서, 해안에 닿거나 떠나는 유람선이며 청어잡이 배며 해수욕 탈의 마차를 죄다 한눈에 살피려고 그 기구를 바다 쪽으로 겨누고 있다, 운운."], ["But have we any leisure for a description of Brighton?--for Brighton, a clean Naples with genteel lazzaroni--for Brighton, that always looks brisk, gay, and gaudy, like a harlequin's jacket--for Brighton, which used to be seven hours distant from London at the time of our story; which is now only a hundred minutes off; and which may approach who knows how much nearer, unless Joinville comes and untimely bombards it?", "그런데 우리에게 브라이턴을 묘사할 겨를이 있던가? 점잖은 부랑자들이 사는 깨끗한 나폴리 같은 브라이턴, 어릿광대의 저고리처럼 늘 활기차고 흥겹고 야단스러워 보이는 브라이턴, 우리 이야기 무렵에는 런던에서 일곱 시간 거리였으나 지금은 백 분밖에 안 걸리는 브라이턴, 그리고 주앵빌이 와서 때아니게 포격이라도 하지 않는 한 앞으로 또 얼마나 더 가까워질지 모를 브라이턴을 말이다."]], [["\"What a monstrous fine girl that is in the lodgings over the milliner's,\"", "\"모자 가게 위층 셋방에 사는 저 아가씨는 어찌나 끝내주게 예쁜지.\""], ["one of these three promenaders remarked to the other; \"Gad, Crawley, did you see what a wink she gave me as I passed?\"", "이 세 산책자 가운데 하나가 다른 이에게 한마디 했다. \"이런, 크롤리, 내가 지나갈 때 저 여자가 나한테 윙크하는 거 봤나?\""]], [["\"Don't break her heart, Jos, you rascal,\"", "\"그 여자 마음을 아프게 하지 말게, 조스, 이 악당아.\""], ["said another.", "또 한 사람이 말했다."], ["\"Don't trifle with her affections, you Don Juan!\"", "\"그 여자의 애정을 가지고 장난치지 말라고, 이 돈 후안 같으니!\""]], [["\"Get away,\"", "\"이 사람 참.\""], ["said Jos Sedley, quite pleased, and leering up at the maid-servant in question with a most killing ogle.", "조스 세들리가 자못 흐뭇해하며, 문제의 그 하녀를 향해 더없이 살인적인 추파를 흘끔 던지며 말했다."], ["Jos was even more splendid at Brighton than he had been at his sister's marriage.", "조스는 누이의 결혼식 때보다도 브라이턴에서 한층 더 휘황찬란했다."], ["He had brilliant under-waistcoats, any one of which would have set up a moderate buck.", "그가 입은 화려한 속조끼는 어느 것 하나만으로도 어지간한 멋쟁이를 차려 줄 만했다."], ["He sported a military frock-coat, ornamented with frogs, knobs, black buttons, and meandering embroidery.", "그는 장식 끈과 단추, 검은 단추, 구불구불한 자수로 꾸민 군용 프록코트를 떨쳐입고 있었다."], ["He had affected a military appearance and habits of late; and he walked with his two friends, who were of that profession, clinking his boot-spurs, swaggering prodigiously, and shooting death-glances at all the servant girls who were worthy to be slain.", "그는 근래 군인 같은 풍채와 습성을 흉내 내고 있었다. 그래서 그 직업에 종사하는 두 친구와 함께 부츠 박차를 절그럭거리고 어마어마하게 으스대며 걸으면서, 죽임을 당해 마땅한 모든 하녀들에게 치명적인 추파를 쏘아 댔다."]], [["\"What shall we do, boys, till the ladies return?\"", "\"이보게들, 숙녀들이 돌아올 때까지 뭘 하지?\""], ["the buck asked.", "멋쟁이가 물었다."], ["The ladies were out to Rottingdean in his carriage on a drive.", "숙녀들은 그의 마차를 타고 로팅딘으로 드라이브를 나간 참이었다."]], [["\"Let's have a game at billiards,\"", "\"당구나 한판 칠까.\""], ["one of his friends said--the tall one, with lacquered mustachios.", "그의 친구 중 하나가 말했다. 옻칠한 듯한 콧수염을 기른 키 큰 사내였다."]], [["\"No, dammy; no, Captain,\"", "\"안 돼, 제기랄. 안 되네, 대위.\""], ["Jos replied, rather alarmed.", "조스가 다소 질겁하며 대답했다."], ["\"No billiards to-day, Crawley, my boy; yesterday was enough.\"", "\"오늘은 당구 안 되네, 크롤리, 이 친구야. 어제로 충분해.\""]], [["\"You play very well,\"", "\"자네 당구 솜씨가 아주 좋잖나.\""], ["said Crawley, laughing.", "크롤리가 웃으며 말했다."], ["\"Don't he, Osborne?", "\"안 그런가, 오즈본?"], ["How well he made that five stroke, eh?\"", "어제 그 다섯 점짜리 한 방, 어찌나 멋지게 치던지, 응?\""]], [["\"Famous,\"", "\"기막혔지.\""], ["Osborne said.", "오즈본이 말했다."], ["\"Jos is a devil of a fellow at billiards, and at everything else, too.", "\"조스는 당구에 귀신이야, 다른 것도 다 마찬가지고."], ["I wish there were any tiger-hunting about here!", "이 근처에 호랑이 사냥이라도 있으면 좋으련만!"], ["we might go and kill a few before dinner.", "저녁 먹기 전에 몇 마리 잡으러 갈 수 있을 텐데."], ["(There goes a fine girl!", "(저기 예쁜 아가씨가 지나가네!"], ["what an ankle, eh, Jos?) Tell us that story about the tiger-hunt, and the way you did for him in the jungle--it's a wonderful story that, Crawley.\"", "발목 좀 보게, 응, 조스?) 그 호랑이 사냥 이야기 좀 들려주게, 정글에서 그놈을 어떻게 해치웠는지 말이야. 그거 참 굉장한 이야기지, 크롤리.\""], ["Here George Osborne gave a yawn.", "여기서 조지 오즈본은 하품을 했다."], ["\"It's rather slow work,\"", "\"여긴 영 따분하단 말이지.\""], ["said he, \"down here; what shall we do?\"", "그가 말했다. \"뭘 하면 좋을까?\""]], [["\"Shall we go and look at some horses that Snaffler's just brought from Lewes fair?\"", "\"스내플러가 루이스 장에서 막 끌고 온 말들이나 구경하러 갈까?\""], ["Crawley said.", "크롤리가 말했다."]], [["\"Suppose we go and have some jellies at Dutton's,\"", "\"더턴 가게에 가서 젤리나 좀 먹는 게 어때.\""], ["and the rogue Jos, willing to kill two birds with one stone.", "능청맞은 조스가, 일석이조를 노리고 말했다."], ["\"Devilish fine gal at Dutton's.\"", "\"더턴 가게에 끝내주는 아가씨가 있거든.\""]], [["\"Suppose we go and see the Lightning come in, it's just about time?\"", "\"라이트닝호가 들어오는 거나 보러 가는 게 어떤가, 마침 그럴 때가 됐는데?\""], ["George said.", "조지가 말했다."], ["This advice prevailing over the stables and the jelly, they turned towards the coach-office to witness the Lightning's arrival.", "이 제안이 마구간과 젤리를 누르고 채택되어, 그들은 라이트닝호가 도착하는 것을 구경하려고 역마차 사무소 쪽으로 향했다."]], [["As they passed, they met the carriage--Jos Sedley's open carriage, with its magnificent armorial bearings--that splendid conveyance in which he used to drive, about at Cheltenham, majestic and solitary, with his arms folded, and his hat cocked; or, more happy, with ladies by his side.", "그렇게 지나가던 길에 그들은 마차 한 대와 마주쳤다. 조스 세들리의 무개 마차로, 그 위에 화려한 문장이 새겨진 그 으리으리한 탈것이었다. 그가 첼튼엄에서 팔짱을 끼고 모자를 비스듬히 쓴 채 위풍당당하게 홀로, 혹은 좀 더 흐뭇하게는 숙녀들을 곁에 태우고 몰고 다니던 그 마차였다."]], [["Two were in the carriage now: one a little person, with light hair, and dressed in the height of the fashion; the other in a brown silk pelisse, and a straw bonnet with pink ribbons, with a rosy, round, happy face, that did you good to behold.", "지금 마차 안에는 둘이 타고 있었다. 하나는 자그마한 사람으로, 머리칼이 옅은 빛깔이고 최신 유행에 따라 차려입고 있었다."], ["She checked the carriage as it neared the three gentlemen, after which exercise of authority she looked rather nervous, and then began to blush most absurdly.", "다른 하나는 갈색 비단 펠리스에 분홍 리본을 단 밀짚 보닛 차림이었으며, 보고만 있어도 마음이 흐뭇해지는 발그레하고 둥글고 행복한 얼굴이었다. 그녀는 마차가 세 신사에게 다가가자 마차를 멈춰 세웠는데, 그렇게 권위를 한 번 행사하고 나더니 자못 긴장한 기색으로 어처구니없을 만큼 얼굴을 붉히기 시작했다."], ["\"We have had a delightful drive, George,\"", "\"정말 즐거운 드라이브였어요, 조지.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, \"and--and we're so glad to come back; and, Joseph, don't let him be late.\"", "\"그리고—그리고 돌아오니 너무 기뻐요. 그리고 조지프, 이이가 늦지 않게 해 주세요.\""]], [["\"Don't be leading our husbands into mischief, Mr. Sedley, you wicked, wicked man you,\"", "\"우리 남편들을 못된 길로 끌어들이지 마세요, 세들리 씨, 이 못되고 못된 양반 같으니.\""], ["Rebecca said, shaking at Jos a pretty little finger covered with the neatest French kid glove.", "레베카가 말하며, 더없이 단정한 프랑스 새끼염소 가죽 장갑을 낀 예쁜 새끼손가락을 조스를 향해 까딱거렸다."], ["\"No billiards, no smoking, no naughtiness!\"", "\"당구도 안 돼요, 담배도 안 되고, 어떤 못된 짓도 안 돼요!\""]], [["\"My dear Mrs. Crawley--Ah now!", "\"친애하는 크롤리 부인—아, 이거 참!"], ["upon my honour!\"", "명예를 걸고 맹세컨대!\""], ["was all Jos could ejaculate by way of reply; but he managed to fall into a tolerable attitude, with his head lying on his shoulder, grinning upwards at his victim, with one hand at his back, which he supported on his cane, and the other hand (the one with the diamond ring) fumbling in his shirt-frill and among his under-waistcoats.", "조스가 답이랍시고 더듬거린 말은 그것이 전부였다. 그러나 그는 그럭저럭 봐줄 만한 자세를 취하는 데에는 성공했으니, 머리를 한쪽 어깨에 기댄 채 자기 희생자를 향해 위로 히죽거리고, 한 손은 등 뒤로 돌려 지팡이에 기대고, 다른 한 손(다이아몬드 반지를 낀 손)은 셔츠 주름 장식과 속조끼들 사이를 만지작거렸다."], ["As the carriage drove off he kissed the diamond hand to the fair ladies within.", "마차가 떠나가자 그는 그 다이아몬드 손에 입 맞춰 안에 있는 어여쁜 숙녀들에게 키스를 날렸다."], ["He wished all Cheltenham, all Chowringhee, all Calcutta, could see him in that position, waving his hand to such a beauty, and in company with such a famous buck as Rawdon Crawley of the Guards.", "그는 첼튼엄 사람들 모두가, 차우링기 사람들 모두가, 캘커타 사람들 모두가, 그런 미인에게 손을 흔들며, 게다가 근위대의 로던 크롤리 같은 이름난 멋쟁이와 어울려 있는 그 모습을 봐 주었으면 싶었다."]], [["Our young bride and bridegroom had chosen Brighton as the place where they would pass the first few days after their marriage; and having engaged apartments at the Ship Inn, enjoyed themselves there in great comfort and quietude, until Jos presently joined them.", "우리의 젊은 신랑 신부는 결혼 후 첫 며칠을 보낼 곳으로 브라이턴을 골랐다. 그들은 십 인 여관에 방을 잡고, 조스가 곧 합류하기 전까지 그곳에서 더없이 편안하고 조용하게 지냈다."], ["Nor was he the only companion they found there.", "게다가 거기서 만난 길동무가 조스 하나만은 아니었다."], ["As they were coming into the hotel from a sea-side walk one afternoon, on whom should they light but Rebecca and her husband.", "어느 날 오후 그들이 바닷가 산책을 마치고 호텔로 들어오는데, 마주친 이가 다름 아닌 레베카와 그녀의 남편이었던 것이다."], ["The recognition was immediate.", "둘은 곧바로 서로를 알아보았다."], ["Rebecca flew into the arms of her dearest friend.", "레베카는 그토록 사랑하는 벗의 품으로 날아들었다."], ["Crawley and Osborne shook hands together cordially enough: and Becky, in the course of a very few hours, found means to make the latter forget that little unpleasant passage of words which had happened between them.", "크롤리와 오즈본도 제법 다정하게 악수를 나누었다. 그리고 베키는 불과 몇 시간 만에, 전에 둘 사이에 오갔던 그 자그마한 불쾌한 말다툼을 오즈본이 잊게 만들 방도를 찾아냈다."], ["\"Do you remember the last time we met at Miss Crawley's, when I was so rude to you, dear Captain Osborne?", "\"지난번 크롤리 양 댁에서 우리가 만났을 때, 제가 그토록 무례하게 굴었던 거 기억하세요, 친애하는 오즈본 대위님?"], ["I thought you seemed careless about dear Amelia.", "그때 전 대위님이 사랑스러운 애밀리아에게 무심한 것 같다고 생각했어요."], ["It was that made me angry: and so pert: and so unkind: and so ungrateful.", "그래서 화가 났고, 그래서 그렇게 건방지게, 그렇게 매정하게, 그렇게 배은망덕하게 굴었던 거예요."], ["Do forgive me!\"", "부디 용서해 주세요!\""], ["Rebecca said, and she held out her hand with so frank and winning a grace, that Osborne could not but take it.", "레베카가 이렇게 말하며, 어찌나 솔직하고 마음을 끄는 우아함으로 손을 내밀던지, 오즈본은 그 손을 잡지 않을 수 없었다."], ["By humbly and frankly acknowledging yourself to be in the wrong, there is no knowing, my son, what good you may do.", "겸손하고 솔직하게 자기 잘못을 인정하면, 그것이 어떤 좋은 결과를 가져올지 모르는 법이다, 내 아들아."], ["I knew once a gentleman and very worthy practitioner in Vanity Fair, who used to do little wrongs to his neighbours on purpose, and in order to apologise for them in an open and manly way afterwards--and what ensued?", "나는 일찍이 허영의 시장에서 아주 능란한 술수가요 신사인 어떤 이를 알았는데, 그는 일부러 이웃들에게 자잘한 잘못을 저질러 놓고, 나중에 사내답게 떳떳이 사과하곤 했다. 그래서 결과가 어떻게 되었느냐?"], ["My friend Crocky Doyle was liked everywhere, and deemed to be rather impetuous--but the honestest fellow.", "내 벗 크로키 도일은 어딜 가나 사랑을 받았고, 다소 다혈질이긴 해도 더없이 정직한 사내라는 평을 들었던 것이다."], ["Becky's humility passed for sincerity with George Osborne.", "베키의 겸손은 조지 오즈본에게는 진심으로 통했다."]], [["These two young couples had plenty of tales to relate to each other.", "이 두 젊은 부부에게는 서로에게 들려줄 이야기가 넘쳐 났다."], ["The marriages of either were discussed; and their prospects in life canvassed with the greatest frankness and interest on both sides.", "양쪽의 결혼이 화제에 올랐고, 인생에서의 전망도 양편 다 더없는 솔직함과 흥미로 헤아려졌다."], ["George's marriage was to be made known to his father by his friend Captain Dobbin; and young Osborne trembled rather for the result of that communication.", "조지의 결혼은 그의 친구 도빈 대위가 그 아버지에게 알리기로 되어 있었는데, 젊은 오즈본은 그 통보의 결과를 두고 다소 떨고 있었다."], ["Miss Crawley, on whom all Rawdon's hopes depended, still held out.", "로던의 모든 희망이 걸려 있는 크롤리 양은 여전히 마음을 풀지 않고 있었다."], ["Unable to make an entry into her house in Park Lane, her affectionate nephew and niece had followed her to Brighton, where they had emissaries continually planted at her door.", "파크 레인의 그 집에 발도 들이지 못하게 된 다정한 조카 부부는 그녀를 따라 브라이턴까지 왔고, 그곳에서 끊임없이 그녀의 집 문 앞에 밀사를 심어 두고 있었다."]], [["\"I wish you could see some of Rawdon's friends who are always about our door,\"", "\"우리 집 문간을 늘 어슬렁거리는 로던의 친구들을 좀 보여 드리고 싶네요.\""], ["Rebecca said, laughing.", "레베카가 웃으며 말했다."], ["\"Did you ever see a dun, my dear; or a bailiff and his man?", "\"빚 독촉꾼을 본 적 있으세요, 자기? 아니면 집행관이랑 그 수하 말이에요."], ["Two of the abominable wretches watched all last week at the greengrocer's opposite, and we could not get away until Sunday.", "지난주 내내 그 흉측한 작자들 둘이 길 건너 채소 가게에서 망을 보는 통에, 우린 일요일이 되어서야 겨우 나올 수 있었다니까요."], ["If Aunty does not relent, what shall we do?\"", "고모님이 마음을 풀지 않으시면, 우린 어쩌면 좋죠?\""]], [["Rawdon, with roars of laughter, related a dozen amusing anecdotes of his duns, and Rebecca's adroit treatment of them.", "로던은 껄껄 웃음을 터뜨리며, 자기 빚쟁이들과 그들을 능숙하게 다루는 레베카의 수완에 관한 재미난 일화를 열두 가지나 늘어놓았다."], ["He vowed with a great oath that there was no woman in Europe who could talk a creditor over as she could.", "그는 거창한 맹세까지 곁들이며, 그녀처럼 채권자를 구워삶을 줄 아는 여자는 유럽 천지에 없다고 단언했다."], ["Almost immediately after their marriage, her practice had begun, and her husband found the immense value of such a wife.", "결혼하고 거의 곧바로 그녀의 솜씨가 발휘되기 시작했고, 남편은 그런 아내의 어마어마한 가치를 알게 되었다."], ["They had credit in plenty, but they had bills also in abundance, and laboured under a scarcity of ready money.", "그들은 외상은 넉넉했으나 청구서 또한 차고 넘쳤고, 현금이 모자라 쪼들렸다."], ["Did these debt-difficulties affect Rawdon's good spirits?", "그런데 이런 빚 문제가 로던의 좋은 기분을 망쳤을까? 천만에."], ["No. Everybody in Vanity Fair must have remarked how well those live who are comfortably and thoroughly in debt: how they deny themselves nothing; how jolly and easy they are in their minds.", "허영의 시장에 사는 누구나 보았을 테지만, 편안하고 철저하게 빚에 잠긴 이들이 얼마나 잘 사는지, 얼마나 무엇 하나 마다하지 않는지, 마음이 얼마나 유쾌하고 태평한지 모를 일이다."], ["Rawdon and his wife had the very best apartments at the inn at Brighton; the landlord, as he brought in the first dish, bowed before them as to his greatest customers: and Rawdon abused the dinners and wine with an audacity which no grandee in the land could surpass.", "로던과 그의 아내는 브라이턴 여관에서 가장 좋은 방을 차지하고 있었다. 여관 주인은 첫 요리를 내올 때 마치 자기 최고의 단골에게 하듯 그들 앞에서 머리를 조아렸고, 로던은 이 나라 어떤 귀족도 능가하지 못할 대담함으로 음식과 와인에 트집을 잡았다."], ["Long custom, a manly appearance, faultless boots and clothes, and a happy fierceness of manner, will often help a man as much as a great balance at the banker's.", "오랜 관록과 사내다운 풍채, 흠잡을 데 없는 부츠와 옷차림, 그리고 행복에 겨운 그 거만한 태도란 종종 은행에 거액의 잔고가 있는 것 못지않게 사람을 도와주는 법이다."]], [["The two wedding parties met constantly in each other's apartments.", "두 결혼식 패거리는 끊임없이 서로의 방을 드나들며 만났다."], ["After two or three nights the gentlemen of an evening had a little piquet, as their wives sate and chatted apart.", "두세 밤이 지나자 신사들은 저녁이면 피케 놀이를 조금씩 하게 되었고, 그동안 그들의 아내들은 따로 앉아 수다를 떨었다."], ["This pastime, and the arrival of Jos Sedley, who made his appearance in his grand open carriage, and who played a few games at billiards with Captain Crawley, replenished Rawdon's purse somewhat, and gave him the benefit of that ready money for which the greatest spirits are sometimes at a stand-still.", "이 소일거리에, 그리고 으리으리한 무개 마차를 타고 나타나 크롤리 대위와 당구를 몇 판 친 조스 세들리의 등장까지 더해져, 로던의 지갑은 다소 두둑해졌고, 아무리 호기로운 이라 해도 때로는 그것이 없어 옴짝달싹 못 하는 그 현금의 덕을 그도 누리게 되었다."]], [["So the three gentlemen walked down to see the Lightning coach come in.", "그렇게 세 신사는 라이트닝호 마차가 들어오는 것을 보러 걸어 내려갔다."], ["Punctual to the minute, the coach crowded inside and out, the guard blowing his accustomed tune on the horn--the Lightning came tearing down the street, and pulled up at the coach-office.", "일분도 어김없이, 안팎으로 손님을 빼곡히 태우고, 차장이 늘 부는 곡조를 나팔로 불어 대며, 라이트닝호가 거리를 질주해 내려와 역마차 사무소 앞에 멈춰 섰다."]], [["\"Hullo!", "\"이런!"], ["there's old Dobbin,\"", "저기 도빈 친구가 있네.\""], ["George cried, quite delighted to see his old friend perched on the roof; and whose promised visit to Brighton had been delayed until now.", "조지가 옛 친구가 지붕 위에 올라앉아 있는 것을 보고 무척 반가워하며 외쳤다. 도빈이 브라이턴에 오마던 약속은 이제껏 미뤄져 오던 터였다."], ["\"How are you, old fellow?", "\"잘 지냈나, 이 친구야?"], ["Glad you're come down.", "와 줘서 고맙네."], ["Emmy'll be delighted to see you,\"", "에미가 자네를 보면 무척 기뻐할 거야.\""], ["Osborne said, shaking his comrade warmly by the hand as soon as his descent from the vehicle was effected--and then he added, in a lower and agitated voice, \"What's the news?", "오즈본이 친구가 마차에서 내려서자마자 그 손을 따뜻하게 움켜잡으며 말했다. 그러고는 한층 낮고 동요한 목소리로 덧붙였다."], ["Have you been in Russell Square?", "\"무슨 소식이라도 있나?"], ["What does the governor say?", "러셀 광장에 다녀왔어? 아버지가 뭐라셔?"], ["Tell me everything.\"", "죄다 얘기해 봐.\""]], [["Dobbin looked very pale and grave.", "도빈은 몹시 핼쑥하고 심각한 얼굴이었다."], ["\"I've seen your father,\"", "\"자네 아버지를 뵙고 왔네.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"How's Amelia--Mrs.", "\"애밀리아는—조지 부인은 어떤가?"], ["George?", "소식은 이따 다 얘기하지."], ["I'll tell you all the news presently: but I've brought the great news of all: and that is--\"", "하지만 무엇보다 큰 소식을 가져왔어. 그건 바로—\""]], [["\"Out with it, old fellow,\"", "\"어서 말해 보게, 이 친구야.\""], ["George said.", "조지가 말했다."]], [["\"We're ordered to Belgium.", "\"우리 벨기에로 출동 명령이 떨어졌어."], ["All the army goes--guards and all.", "군 전체가 다 가네. 근위대까지 몽땅."], ["Heavytop's got the gout, and is mad at not being able to move.", "헤비톱은 통풍에 걸려서 움직이지도 못하게 됐다고 펄펄 뛰고 있어."], ["O'Dowd goes in command, and we embark from Chatham next week.\"", "오다우드가 지휘를 맡고, 우린 다음 주에 채텀에서 승선한다네.\""], ["This news of war could not but come with a shock upon our lovers, and caused all these gentlemen to look very serious.", "이 전쟁 소식은 우리 연인들에게 충격을 안기지 않을 수 없었고, 이 신사들 모두를 자못 심각한 표정으로 만들었다."]], [["Captain Dobbin Proceeds on His Canvass\nWhat is the secret mesmerism which friendship possesses, and under the operation of which a person ordinarily sluggish, or cold, or timid, becomes wise, active, and resolute, in another's behalf?", "우정이 지닌 그 은밀한 최면술이란 대체 무엇인가? 평소 굼뜨거나 차갑거나 소심한 사람이, 그 작용을 받으면 남을 위해 슬기롭고 민첩하고 단호해지니 말이다."], ["As Alexis, after a few passes from Dr. Elliotson, despises pain, reads with the back of his head, sees miles off, looks into next week, and performs other wonders, of which, in his own private normal condition, he is quite incapable; so you see, in the affairs of the world and under the magnetism of friendships, the modest man becomes bold, the shy confident, the lazy active, or the impetuous prudent and peaceful.", "알렉시스가 엘리엇슨 박사의 손짓 몇 번에 통증을 멸시하고 뒤통수로 글을 읽으며 몇 마일 밖을 내다보고 다음 주를 꿰뚫어 보며, 평소 멀쩡한 제 상태에서는 도저히 해낼 수 없는 온갖 신기를 부리는 것처럼, 세상사에 있어서도 우정이라는 자력에 끌리면 겸손한 사람이 대담해지고, 수줍은 사람이 자신만만해지고, 게으른 사람이 부지런해지고, 성급한 사람이 신중하고 평온해지는 것을 우리는 볼 수 있다."], ["What is it, on the other hand, that makes the lawyer eschew his own cause, and call in his learned brother as an adviser?", "반면에 변호사로 하여금 자기 사건을 회피하고 학식 깊은 동료를 자문으로 불러들이게 만드는 것은 무엇인가?"], ["And what causes the doctor, when ailing, to send for his rival, and not sit down and examine his own tongue in the chimney glass, or write his own prescription at his study-table?", "또 의사가 병이 나면 굳이 경쟁자를 부르고, 정작 벽난로 거울 앞에 앉아 제 혀를 들여다보거나 서재 책상에서 제 처방전을 쓰지 않게 만드는 것은 무엇인가? 나는 이런 의문들을 던져 영민한 독자들이 답하도록 맡기련다."], ["I throw out these queries for intelligent readers to answer, who know, at once, how credulous we are, and how sceptical, how soft and how obstinate, how firm for others and how diffident about ourselves: meanwhile, it is certain that our friend William Dobbin, who was personally of so complying a disposition that if his parents had pressed him much, it is probable he would have stepped down into the kitchen and married the cook, and who, to further his own interests, would have found the most insuperable difficulty in walking across the street, found himself as busy and eager in the conduct of George Osborne's affairs, as the most selfish tactician could be in the pursuit of his own.", "우리가 얼마나 잘 속으면서도 얼마나 회의적이며, 얼마나 무르면서도 얼마나 고집스럽고, 남을 위해서는 얼마나 굳건하면서도 자신에 대해서는 얼마나 자신 없는지를 단번에 아는 그분들에게 말이다. 어쨌든 분명한 것은, 우리의 벗 윌리엄 도빈으로 말하자면 본디 그 성정이 어찌나 순응적이었던지, 만약 부모가 다그쳤더라면 필시 부엌으로 내려가 요리사와 결혼했을 법한 위인이요, 제 잇속을 챙기는 일이라면 길 하나 건너는 것조차 도저히 넘기 어려운 난관으로 여겼을 사람이건만, 막상 조지 오스본의 일을 처리하는 데에는 가장 이기적인 책략가가 제 잇속을 좇을 때만큼이나 분주하고 열성적이었다는 사실이다."]], [["Whilst our friend George and his young wife were enjoying the first blushing days of the honeymoon at Brighton, honest William was left as George's plenipotentiary in London, to transact all the business part of the marriage.", "우리의 벗 조지와 그의 젊은 아내가 브라이튼에서 신혼의 발그레한 첫 나날을 즐기는 동안, 정직한 윌리엄은 런던에 남아 조지의 전권 대사로서 결혼에 따르는 온갖 사무를 도맡아 처리했다."], ["His duty it was to call upon old Sedley and his wife, and to keep the former in good humour: to draw Jos and his brother-in-law nearer together, so that Jos's position and dignity, as collector of Boggley Wollah, might compensate for his father's loss of station, and tend to reconcile old Osborne to the alliance: and finally, to communicate it to the latter in such a way as should least irritate the old gentleman.", "그가 맡은 임무는 늙은 세들리 부부를 찾아뵙고 노인의 기분을 풀어 드리는 일, 조스와 그 매부를 더욱 가까이 묶어 놓아, 보글리 월라의 징세관이라는 조스의 지위와 위신이 그 아버지의 신분 추락을 메우고 늙은 오스본이 이 혼사를 받아들이도록 이끄는 일, 그리고 마지막으로 늙은 신사의 노여움을 가장 덜 사는 방식으로 그에게 소식을 전하는 일이었다."]], [["Now, before he faced the head of the Osborne house with the news which it was his duty to tell, Dobbin bethought him that it would be politic to make friends of the rest of the family, and, if possible, have the ladies on his side.", "이제 마땅히 알려야 할 소식을 들고 오스본 가의 가장을 마주하기에 앞서, 도빈은 나머지 가족과 친분을 맺어 두는 것이 지혜로우며, 가능하다면 그 집 숙녀들을 제 편으로 끌어들이는 것이 상책이라는 생각이 들었다."], ["They can't be angry in their hearts, thought he.", "마음속으로야 화를 낼 리 없지, 하고 그는 생각했다."], ["No woman ever was really angry at a romantic marriage.", "낭만적인 결혼에 진심으로 화를 내는 여자란 일찍이 없었다."], ["A little crying out, and they must come round to their brother; when the three of us will lay siege to old Mr. Osborne.", "조금 소리 높여 떠들고 나면 결국 오라비 편을 들게 마련이고, 그러면 우리 셋이서 늙은 오스본 씨를 포위 공격할 수 있겠지."], ["So this Machiavellian captain of infantry cast about him for some happy means or stratagem by which he could gently and gradually bring the Misses Osborne to a knowledge of their brother's secret.", "그리하여 이 마키아벨리 같은 보병 대위는 오스본 아가씨들에게 오라비의 비밀을 부드럽고도 차근차근 알려 줄 어떤 묘책이나 책략은 없을까 하고 궁리에 궁리를 거듭했다."]], [["By a little inquiry regarding his mother's engagements, he was pretty soon able to find out by whom of her ladyship's friends parties were given at that season; where he would be likely to meet Osborne's sisters; and, though he had that abhorrence of routs and evening parties which many sensible men, alas!", "어머니인 부인의 약속들을 조금 캐물어 본 끝에, 그는 그 계절에 부인의 친구들 가운데 누가 모임을 여는지, 어디 가면 오스본의 누이들을 만날 수 있을지를 머잖아 알아낼 수 있었다. 그리고 분별 있는 사내들이 흔히—아아!—품기 마련인 그 떠들썩한 사교 모임과 야회에 대한 혐오를 그도 지니고 있었으나, 이내 오스본 아가씨들이 참석할 모임 하나를 찾아냈다."], ["entertain, he soon found one where the Misses Osborne were to be present.", "무도회에 모습을 드러낸 그는 두 아가씨와 두어 차례 짝을 지어 춤을 추었고 더없이 정중하게 처신했다."], ["Making his appearance at the ball, where he danced a couple of sets with both of them, and was prodigiously polite, he actually had the courage to ask Miss Osborne for a few minutes' conversation at an early hour the next day, when he had, he said, to communicate to her news of the very greatest interest.", "그러더니 정말로 용기를 내어, 오스본 양에게 다음 날 이른 시각에 몇 분만 이야기를 나눌 수 있겠느냐고, 더없이 중대한 소식을 전할 것이 있노라고 청하기까지 한 것이다."]], [["What was it that made her start back, and gaze upon him for a moment, and then on the ground at her feet, and make as if she would faint on his arm, had he not by opportunely treading on her toes, brought the young lady back to self-control?", "그녀가 흠칫 뒤로 물러나 잠시 그를 응시하다가 이내 제 발치 바닥을 내려다보고, 마치 그의 팔에 쓰러져 기절할 것처럼 굴게 만든 것은 대체 무엇이었을까? 만약 그가 때맞춰 그녀의 발가락을 밟아 이 젊은 숙녀를 정신 차리게 해 주지 않았더라면 말이다."], ["Why was she so violently agitated at Dobbin's request?", "어째서 그녀는 도빈의 청에 그토록 격렬하게 동요했을까?"], ["This can never be known.", "이는 결코 알 수 없는 노릇이다."], ["But when he came the next day, Maria was not in the drawing-room with her sister, and Miss Wirt went off for the purpose of fetching the latter, and the Captain and Miss Osborne were left together.", "어쨌든 그가 이튿날 찾아왔을 때, 마리아는 언니와 함께 응접실에 있지 않았고, 워트 양이 그녀를 데리러 자리를 떴기에 대위와 오스본 양만 남게 되었다."], ["They were both so silent that the ticktock of the Sacrifice of Iphigenia clock on the mantelpiece became quite rudely audible.", "둘 다 어찌나 말이 없던지, 벽난로 위에 놓인 '이피게네이아의 희생' 시계의 째깍거리는 소리가 무례할 정도로 또렷하게 들려왔다."]], [["\"What a nice party it was last night,\"", "\"어젯밤 모임은 참 즐거웠어요.\" 마침내 오스본 양이 격려하듯 말문을 열었다."], ["Miss Osborne at length began, encouragingly; \"and--and how you're improved in your dancing, Captain Dobbin.", "\"그리고… 그리고 도빈 대위님은 춤 솜씨가 정말 느셨더군요."], ["Surely somebody has taught you,\"", "분명 누군가가 가르쳐 드린 거예요.\""], ["she added, with amiable archness.", "그녀가 상냥한 짓궂음을 담아 덧붙였다."]], [["\"You should see me dance a reel with Mrs.", "\"우리 부대 오다우드 소령 부인하고 릴을 추는 걸 한번 보셔야 하는 건데요."], ["Major O'Dowd of ours; and a jig--did you ever see a jig?", "그리고 지그도요. 지그 춤 보신 적 있으세요?"], ["But I think anybody could dance with you, Miss Osborne, who dance so well.\"", "하지만 이렇게 춤을 잘 추시는 오스본 양하고라면 누구든 잘 출 수 있을 것 같습니다.\""]], [["\"Is the Major's lady young and beautiful, Captain?\"", "\"그 소령 부인은 젊고 아름다운가요, 대위님?\""], ["the fair questioner continued.", "어여쁜 질문자가 말을 이었다."], ["\"Ah, what a terrible thing it must be to be a soldier's wife!", "\"아, 군인의 아내가 된다는 건 얼마나 끔찍한 일일까요!"], ["I wonder they have any spirits to dance, and in these dreadful times of war, too!", "어떻게 춤출 기운이 나는지 모르겠어요, 게다가 이런 무시무시한 전쟁의 시국에 말이에요!"], ["O Captain Dobbin, I tremble sometimes when I think of our dearest George, and the dangers of the poor soldier.", "오, 도빈 대위님, 저는 우리 가장 소중한 조지와 가엾은 군인들이 처한 위험을 떠올리면 이따금 몸이 떨려요."], ["Are there many married officers of the --th, Captain Dobbin?\"", "××연대에는 결혼한 장교가 많은가요, 도빈 대위님?\""]], [["\"Upon my word, she's playing her hand rather too openly,\"", "\"이런, 패를 너무 대놓고 까는구나.\" 워트 양은 생각했다."], ["Miss Wirt thought; but this observation is merely parenthetic, and was not heard through the crevice of the door at which the governess uttered it.", "그러나 이 논평은 그저 곁다리로 흘린 말이었을 뿐, 가정교사가 문틈에 대고 중얼거린 것이라 그 사이로 들리지는 않았다."]], [["\"One of our young men is just married,\"", "\"우리 부대 젊은이 하나가 막 결혼했답니다.\""], ["Dobbin said, now coming to the point.", "이제 본론으로 들어서며 도빈이 말했다."], ["\"It was a very old attachment, and the young couple are as poor as church mice.\"", "\"아주 오래된 인연이었지요. 그런데 그 젊은 부부는 교회 생쥐처럼 가난하답니다.\""], ["\"O, how delightful!", "\"오, 정말 멋져요!"], ["O, how romantic!\"", "오, 정말 낭만적이에요!\""], ["Miss Osborne cried, as the Captain said \"old attachment\" and \"poor.\"", "대위가 \"오래된 인연\"이며 \"가난한\"이라는 말을 하자 오스본 양이 외쳤다."], ["Her sympathy encouraged him.", "그녀의 공감에 그는 용기를 얻었다."]], [["\"The finest young fellow in the regiment,\"", "\"연대에서 가장 훌륭한 젊은이지요.\""], ["he continued.", "그가 말을 이었다."], ["\"Not a braver or handsomer officer in the army; and such a charming wife!", "\"군대를 통틀어도 그보다 용감하거나 잘생긴 장교는 없을 겁니다. 게다가 그 아내가 어찌나 사랑스러운지요!"], ["How you would like her!", "얼마나 그 사람을 좋아하시게 될지!"], ["how you will like her when you know her, Miss Osborne.\"", "알게 되시면 분명 좋아하실 거예요, 오스본 양.\""], ["The young lady thought the actual moment had arrived, and that Dobbin's nervousness which now came on and was visible in many twitchings of his face, in his manner of beating the ground with his great feet, in the rapid buttoning and unbuttoning of his frock-coat, &c.--Miss Osborne, I say, thought that when he had given himself a little air, he would unbosom himself entirely, and prepared eagerly to listen.", "젊은 숙녀는 드디어 결정적인 순간이 왔다고 여겼다. 그리고 이제 막 도빈에게 밀려든 그 긴장, 그의 얼굴이 여러 번 씰룩거리는 데서, 그 커다란 발로 바닥을 구르는 모양새에서, 프록코트 단추를 잽싸게 채웠다 풀었다 하는 데서 역력히 드러나던 그 긴장—요컨대 오스본 양은, 그가 한숨 돌리고 나면 마음속을 죄다 털어놓으리라 여기고 열심히 귀 기울일 채비를 했다."], ["And the clock, in the altar on which Iphigenia was situated, beginning, after a preparatory convulsion, to toll twelve, the mere tolling seemed as if it would last until one--so prolonged was the knell to the anxious spinster.", "그리고 이피게네이아가 놓인 그 제단 안의 시계가, 한 차례 예비 경련을 거친 뒤 열두 시를 알리기 시작했는데, 그 종소리가 어찌나 길게 늘어지던지 이 애타는 노처녀에게는 마치 한 시까지 이어질 것만 같았다."]], [["\"But it's not about marriage that I came to speak--that is that marriage--that is--no, I mean--my dear Miss Osborne, it's about our dear friend George,\"", "\"하지만 제가 말씀드리려고 온 건 그 결혼에 관한 게 아니라—그러니까 그 결혼이—그게—아니, 제 말은—친애하는 오스본 양, 우리의 소중한 벗 조지에 관한 거예요.\""], ["Dobbin said.", "도빈이 말했다."]], [["\"About George?\"", "\"조지에 관해서요?\""], ["she said in a tone so discomfited that Maria and Miss Wirt laughed at the other side of the door, and even that abandoned wretch of a Dobbin felt inclined to smile himself; for he was not altogether unconscious of the state of affairs: George having often bantered him gracefully and said, \"Hang it, Will, why don't you take old Jane?", "그녀가 어찌나 낙담한 어조로 말하던지, 문 저편에서 마리아와 워트 양이 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 저 파렴치한 도빈마저 절로 미소가 나오려 했다. 사실 그도 사태의 정황을 아주 모르는 바는 아니었던 것이다."], ["She'll have you if you ask her.", "조지가 종종 그를 점잖게 놀리며 이렇게 말하곤 했으니 말이다. \"에이, 윌, 자네 늙은 제인이나 데려가지 그래?"], ["I'll bet you five to two she will.\"", "청하면 분명 받아 줄 텐데. 받아 준다는 데 오 대 이로 걸지.\""]], [["\"Yes, about George, then,\"", "\"네, 그러니까 조지에 관한 거예요.\""], ["he continued.", "그가 말을 이었다."], ["\"There has been a difference between him and Mr.", "\"그 친구와 오스본 씨 사이에 불화가 생겼답니다."], ["Osborne. And I regard him so much--for you know we have been like brothers--that I hope and pray the quarrel may be settled.", "그런데 저는 그 친구를 무척이나 아끼는 터라—아시다시피 우리는 형제나 다름없었으니까요—이 다툼이 부디 가라앉기를 바라고 또 기도하는 마음입니다."], ["We must go abroad, Miss Osborne.", "우리는 외국으로 떠나야 합니다, 오스본 양."], ["We may be ordered off at a day's warning.", "하루 전 통보만으로 출정 명령이 떨어질지도 몰라요."], ["Who knows what may happen in the campaign?", "전투에서 무슨 일이 일어날지 누가 알겠습니까?"], ["Don't be agitated, dear Miss Osborne; and those two at least should part friends.\"", "동요하지 마세요, 친애하는 오스본 양. 그래도 적어도 그 두 분만은 벗으로서 헤어져야 하지 않겠습니까.\""]], [["\"There has been no quarrel, Captain Dobbin, except a little usual scene with Papa,\"", "\"다툼은 없었어요, 도빈 대위님. 아버지하고 늘 있던 그저 사소한 한바탕 소동이 있었을 뿐이에요.\""], ["the lady said.", "숙녀가 말했다."], ["\"We are expecting George back daily.", "\"우리는 날마다 조지가 돌아오기를 기다리고 있어요."], ["What Papa wanted was only for his good.", "아버지가 바라셨던 건 오직 그 애를 위한 것이었을 뿐이에요."], ["He has but to come back, and I'm sure all will be well; and dear Rhoda, who went away from here in sad sad anger, I know will forgive him.", "그저 돌아오기만 하면, 다 잘될 거라고 확신해요. 그리고 여기서 몹시도, 몹시도 화가 나서 떠나간 그 다정한 로다도, 분명 그 애를 용서할 거예요."], ["Woman forgives but too readily, Captain.\"", "여자는 너무도 쉽게 용서하는 법이니까요, 대위님.\""]], [["\"Such an angel as YOU I am sure would,\"", "\"오스본 양 같은 천사라면 분명 그러시겠지요.\""], ["Mr. Dobbin said, with atrocious astuteness.", "도빈 씨가 무시무시한 능청을 떨며 말했다."], ["\"And no man can pardon himself for giving a woman pain.", "\"여자에게 아픔을 준 사내라면 결코 스스로를 용서할 수 없는 법이고요."], ["What would you feel, if a man were faithless to you?\"", "만약 어떤 사내가 오스본 양께 신의를 저버린다면 기분이 어떠시겠습니까?\""]], [["\"I should perish--I should throw myself out of window--I should take poison--I should pine and die.", "\"저는 죽어 버릴 거예요—창밖으로 몸을 던질 거예요—독약을 마실 거예요—애태우다 시들어 죽을 거예요."], ["I know I should,\"", "분명 그럴 거예요.\""], ["Miss cried, who had nevertheless gone through one or two affairs of the heart without any idea of suicide.", "아가씨가 외쳤다. 그렇지만 그녀는 그동안 한두 차례 연애를 겪으면서도 자살이라고는 추호도 떠올린 적이 없는 터였다."]], [["\"And there are others,\"", "\"그런데 다른 여인들 중에도 있답니다.\""], ["Dobbin continued, \"as true and as kind-hearted as yourself.", "도빈이 말을 이었다. \"오스본 양만큼이나 진실하고 마음 고운 여인이 말이에요."], ["I'm not speaking about the West Indian heiress, Miss Osborne, but about a poor girl whom George once loved, and who was bred from her childhood to think of nobody but him.", "저는 그 서인도 제도의 상속녀를 두고 하는 말이 아니에요, 오스본 양. 조지가 한때 사랑했던, 그리고 어릴 적부터 오직 그 사람만을 생각하도록 자라난 가엾은 처녀를 두고 하는 말입니다."], ["I've seen her in her poverty uncomplaining, broken-hearted, without a fault.", "저는 그 처녀가 가난 속에서도 불평 없이, 가슴이 미어지면서도 아무런 흠 없이 지내는 것을 보았습니다."], ["It is of Miss Sedley I speak.", "제가 말하는 건 세들리 양입니다."], ["Dear Miss Osborne, can your generous heart quarrel with your brother for being faithful to her?", "친애하는 오스본 양, 오라버니가 그녀에게 신의를 지킨다고 해서 그 너그러운 마음으로 오라버니를 탓하실 수 있겠습니까?"], ["Could his own conscience ever forgive him if he deserted her?", "만약 그녀를 버린다면 오라버니가 제 양심에 비추어 스스로를 용서할 수 있겠습니까?"], ["Be her friend--she always loved you--and--and I am come here charged by George to tell you that he holds his engagement to her as the most sacred duty he has; and to entreat you, at least, to be on his side.\"", "부디 그녀의 벗이 되어 주세요—그녀는 늘 오스본 양을 사랑했으니까요—그리고—그리고 저는 조지의 부탁을 받고 이 자리에 왔습니다. 그가 그녀와의 약혼을 자신이 짊어진 가장 신성한 의무로 여기고 있음을 전하고, 적어도 오스본 양만은 그의 편이 되어 달라고 간청하기 위해서요.\""]], [["When any strong emotion took possession of Mr.", "도빈 씨는 어떤 강렬한 감정에 사로잡히면, 처음 한두 마디를 머뭇거린 뒤에는 완벽하게 막힘없이 말할 수 있었다."], ["Dobbin, and after the first word or two of hesitation, he could speak with perfect fluency, and it was evident that his eloquence on this occasion made some impression upon the lady whom he addressed.", "그리고 이번 경우 그의 웅변이 그가 말을 건넨 숙녀에게 어느 정도 감명을 주었음이 역력했다."]], [["\"Well,\"", "\"글쎄요.\""], ["said she, \"this is--most surprising--most painful--most extraordinary--what will Papa say?--that George should fling away such a superb establishment as was offered to him but at any rate he has found a very brave champion in you, Captain Dobbin.", "그녀가 말했다. \"이건—정말 놀라운 일이군요—정말 가슴 아프고—정말 기이한 일이에요—아버지가 뭐라고 하실까요?—조지가 그 애한테 주어졌던 그토록 훌륭한 혼처를 내던지다니, 하지만 어쨌든 그 애는 대위님이라는 아주 용감한 옹호자를 얻은 셈이군요, 도빈 대위님."], ["It is of no use, however,\"", "하지만 다 소용없는 일이에요.\""], ["she continued, after a pause; \"I feel for poor Miss Sedley, most certainly--most sincerely, you know.", "그녀가 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 말을 이었다. \"저는 가엾은 세들리 양이 정말 안쓰러워요—정말로 진심으로요, 아시다시피."], ["We never thought the match a good one, though we were always very kind to her here--very.", "우리는 그 혼사를 한 번도 좋게 여긴 적이 없었어요. 여기서는 늘 그녀에게 무척 친절하게 대했지만요—정말 무척요."], ["But Papa will never consent, I am sure.", "하지만 아버지는 절대 허락하지 않으실 거예요, 분명히요."], ["And a well brought up young woman, you know--with a well-regulated mind, must--George must give her up, dear Captain Dobbin, indeed he must.\"", "그리고 잘 자란 처녀라면, 아시다시피—절제된 마음을 지닌 처녀라면—조지는 그녀를 단념해야 해요, 친애하는 도빈 대위님, 정말로 그래야만 해요.\""]], [["\"Ought a man to give up the woman he loved, just when misfortune befell her?\"", "\"한 사내가 사랑하는 여인을, 하필 그 여인에게 불운이 닥친 바로 그 순간에 단념해야 마땅합니까?\""], ["Dobbin said, holding out his hand.", "도빈이 손을 내밀며 말했다."], ["\"Dear Miss Osborne, is this the counsel I hear from you?", "\"친애하는 오스본 양, 제가 지금 오스본 양께 듣고 있는 충고가 이런 것입니까?"], ["My dear young lady!", "친애하는 젊은 숙녀시여!"], ["you must befriend her. He can't give her up.", "오스본 양은 반드시 그녀의 벗이 되어 주셔야 합니다."], ["He must not give her up.", "그는 그녀를 단념할 수 없어요."], ["Would a man, think you, give YOU up if you were poor?\"", "단념해서도 안 됩니다. 한번 생각해 보세요, 만약 오스본 양이 가난해진다면 어떤 사내가 오스본 양을 단념하겠습니까?\""]], [["This adroit question touched the heart of Miss Jane Osborne not a little.", "이 능란한 질문은 제인 오스본 양의 마음을 적잖이 건드렸다."], ["\"I don't know whether we poor girls ought to believe what you men say, Captain,\" she said.", "\"우리 같은 가난한 처녀들이 대위님 같은 사내들의 말을 믿어야 하는 건지 모르겠어요, 대위님.\""], ["\"There is that in woman's tenderness which induces her to believe too easily.", "그녀가 말했다. \"여자의 다정함에는 너무도 쉽게 믿어 버리게 만드는 무언가가 있지요."], ["I'm afraid you are cruel, cruel deceivers,\"--and Dobbin certainly thought he felt a pressure of the hand which Miss Osborne had extended to him.", "아무래도 대위님들은 잔인하고도 잔인한 사기꾼들 같아요.\" 그러는데 도빈은 분명, 오스본 양이 자신에게 내밀었던 그 손이 슬며시 죄어 오는 것을 느꼈다고 여겼다."]], [["He dropped it in some alarm.", "그는 다소 놀라 그 손을 놓아 버렸다."], ["\"Deceivers!\"", "\"사기꾼이라니요!\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"No, dear Miss Osborne, all men are not; your brother is not; George has loved Amelia Sedley ever since they were children; no wealth would make him marry any but her.", "\"아니에요, 친애하는 오스본 양, 모든 사내가 그렇진 않습니다. 오스본 양의 오라버니는 그렇지 않아요."], ["Ought he to forsake her?", "조지는 어린 시절부터 줄곧 애밀리아 세들리를 사랑해 왔어요. 그 어떤 재산으로도 그녀가 아닌 다른 여자와 결혼하게 만들 수는 없을 겁니다."], ["Would you counsel him to do so?\"", "그가 그녀를 저버려야 마땅합니까? 오스본 양은 그에게 그러라고 권하시겠습니까?\""]], [["What could Miss Jane say to such a question, and with her own peculiar views?", "이런 질문에, 그것도 자기만의 독특한 견해를 지닌 제인 오스본 양이 무어라 답할 수 있었겠는가? 그녀는 그에 답할 수 없었기에 이렇게 받아넘겼다."], ["She could not answer it, so she parried it by saying, \"Well, if you are not a deceiver, at least you are very romantic\"; and Captain William let this observation pass without challenge.", "\"글쎄요, 대위님이 사기꾼이 아니라면, 적어도 무척 낭만적인 분이긴 하시군요.\" 그리고 윌리엄 대위는 이 논평을 굳이 따지지 않고 그냥 넘겼다."]], [["At length when, by the help of farther polite speeches, he deemed that Miss Osborne was sufficiently prepared to receive the whole news, he poured it into her ear.", "마침내 그가 한층 더 정중한 말들을 거든 끝에, 오스본 양이 이제 모든 소식을 받아들일 만큼 충분히 채비가 되었다고 여겨졌을 때, 그는 그것을 그녀의 귀에 쏟아부었다."], ["\"George could not give up Amelia--George was married to her\"--and then he related the circumstances of the marriage as we know them already: how the poor girl would have died had not her lover kept his faith: how Old Sedley had refused all consent to the match, and a licence had been got: and Jos Sedley had come from Cheltenham to give away the bride: how they had gone to Brighton in Jos's chariot-and-four to pass the honeymoon: and how George counted on his dear kind sisters to befriend him with their father, as women--so true and tender as they were--assuredly would do.", "\"조지는 애밀리아를 단념할 수 없었습니다—조지는 이미 그녀와 결혼했답니다\"—그러고는 우리가 이미 아는 그 결혼의 정황을 들려주었다. 그 가엾은 처녀가 연인이 신의를 지키지 않았더라면 죽고 말았으리라는 것, 늙은 세들리가 이 혼사에 일절 동의를 거부했으나 결혼 허가증을 얻었다는 것, 조스 세들리가 신부를 인도하러 첼튼엄에서 올라왔다는 것, 그들이 조스의 사두마차를 타고 신혼을 보내러 브라이튼으로 떠났다는 것, 그리고 조지가, 그토록 진실하고 다정한 여인들이라면 분명 그래 주리라 믿으며, 사랑하는 다정한 누이들이 아버지 앞에서 자기 편이 되어 주기를 기대하고 있다는 것까지 죄다 이야기했다."], ["And so, asking permission (readily granted) to see her again, and rightly conjecturing that the news he had brought would be told in the next five minutes to the other ladies, Captain Dobbin made his bow and took his leave.", "그러고는 다시 한번 만나 뵙기를 청하여(흔쾌히 허락받았다), 또한 자신이 가져온 소식이 앞으로 오 분 안에 다른 숙녀들에게도 전해지리라 정확히 짐작하면서, 도빈 대위는 고개를 숙여 인사하고 작별을 고했다."]], [["He was scarcely out of the house, when Miss Maria and Miss Wirt rushed in to Miss Osborne, and the whole wonderful secret was imparted to them by that lady.", "그가 채 집을 벗어나기도 전에, 마리아 양과 워트 양이 오스본 양에게 달려들었고, 그 경이로운 비밀의 전모가 그녀의 입을 통해 두 사람에게 전해졌다."], ["To do them justice, neither of the sisters was very much displeased.", "공평하게 말하자면, 두 자매 중 누구도 그리 언짢아하지는 않았다."], ["There is something about a runaway match with which few ladies can be seriously angry, and Amelia rather rose in their estimation, from the spirit which she had displayed in consenting to the union.", "달아나서 한 결혼이란 어지간한 숙녀라면 진심으로 화내기 어려운 무언가가 있는 법이고, 애밀리아는 그 결혼에 동의하면서 보여 준 기개 덕분에 오히려 그들의 평가에서 한 단계 올라간 셈이었다."], ["As they debated the story, and prattled about it, and wondered what Papa would do and say, came a loud knock, as of an avenging thunder-clap, at the door, which made these conspirators start.", "그들이 그 이야기를 두고 갑론을박하고, 재잘거리고, 아버지가 어떻게 하고 무어라 하실지 궁금해하고 있는데, 마치 복수의 천둥소리 같은 요란한 노크 소리가 문에서 울려 이 음모자들을 화들짝 놀라게 했다."], ["It must be Papa, they thought. But it was not he.", "아버지가 분명해, 하고 그들은 생각했다."], ["It was only Mr.", "그러나 그는 아니었다."], ["Frederick Bullock, who had come from the City according to appointment, to conduct the ladies to a flower-show.", "그저 약속대로 숙녀들을 화훼 전시회에 데려가려고 시티에서 온 프레더릭 불록 씨일 뿐이었다."]], [["This gentleman, as may be imagined, was not kept long in ignorance of the secret.", "짐작하다시피, 이 신사 역시 그 비밀을 오래 모르고 있지는 않았다."], ["But his face, when he heard it, showed an amazement which was very different to that look of sentimental wonder which the countenances of the sisters wore.", "그러나 그것을 들었을 때 그의 얼굴에 떠오른 것은, 자매들의 얼굴에 어렸던 그 감상적인 경이의 빛과는 사뭇 다른 경악이었다. 불록 씨는 세상 물정에 밝은 사내요, 어느 부유한 상회의 젊은 동업자였다."], ["Mr. Bullock was a man of the world, and a junior partner of a wealthy firm.", "그는 돈이 무엇인지, 그 값어치가 얼마인지 알았다."], ["He knew what money was, and the value of it: and a delightful throb of expectation lighted up his little eyes, and caused him to smile on his Maria, as he thought that by this piece of folly of Mr. George's she might be worth thirty thousand pounds more than he had ever hoped to get with her.", "그래서 흐뭇한 기대감의 전율이 그 작은 두 눈에 불을 밝혔고, 조지 씨의 이 어리석은 짓 덕분에 마리아가 자신이 일찍이 그녀와 함께 얻기를 바랐던 것보다 삼만 파운드는 더 값나가는 신붓감이 될지도 모른다는 생각에, 그는 자기 마리아를 향해 미소 짓게 되었다."]], [["\"Gad!", "\"이런!"], ["Jane,\"", "제인.\""], ["said he, surveying even the elder sister with some interest, \"Eels will be sorry he cried off.", "그가 손위 누이까지도 제법 흥미롭게 뜯어보며 말했다. \"일스는 발을 뺀 걸 후회하겠는걸요."], ["You may be a fifty thousand pounder yet.\"", "당신은 어쩌면 오만 파운드짜리가 될지도 모르겠어요.\""]], [["The sisters had never thought of the money question up to that moment, but Fred Bullock bantered them with graceful gaiety about it during their forenoon's excursion; and they had risen not a little in their own esteem by the time when, the morning amusement over, they drove back to dinner.", "자매는 그 순간까지 돈 문제는 생각조차 해 본 적이 없었으나, 프레드 불록은 오전 나들이 내내 우아하고 명랑하게 그것을 두고 둘을 놀려 댔다. 그리하여 오전의 여흥이 끝나고 만찬을 들러 마차를 몰아 돌아올 즈음에는, 두 사람의 자존감이 적잖이 높아져 있었다."], ["And do not let my respected reader exclaim against this selfishness as unnatural.", "그러니 나의 존경하는 독자께서는 이 이기심을 부자연스럽다 하여 부디 나무라지 마시기를."], ["It was but this present morning, as he rode on the omnibus from Richmond; while it changed horses, this present chronicler, being on the roof, marked three little children playing in a puddle below, very dirty, and friendly, and happy.", "바로 오늘 아침, 이 글을 적는 자가 리치먼드에서 합승마차를 타고 오는 길에, 말을 바꾸어 매는 동안 마차 지붕 위에 앉아 있던 나는, 아래쪽 웅덩이에서 노는 어린아이 셋을 눈여겨보았다. 잔뜩 더럽고, 사이좋고, 행복한 아이들이었다."], ["To these three presently came another little one.", "그 셋에게 이내 또 다른 꼬마 하나가 다가왔다."], ["\"POLLY,\"", "\"폴리.\""], ["says she, \"YOUR SISTER'S GOT A PENNY.\"", "그 아이가 말했다. \"너네 언니가 일 페니 생겼대.\""], ["At which the children got up from the puddle instantly, and ran off to pay their court to Peggy.", "그러자 아이들은 즉각 웅덩이에서 일어나, 페기에게 알랑거리러 달려가는 것이었다."], ["And as the omnibus drove off I saw Peggy with the infantine procession at her tail, marching with great dignity towards the stall of a neighbouring lollipop-woman.", "그리고 합승마차가 떠날 무렵, 나는 페기가 그 어린 행렬을 꽁무니에 거느린 채, 더없이 위엄 있게 이웃 사탕 장수 아낙의 좌판을 향해 행진해 가는 모습을 보았다."]], [["In Which Mr. Osborne Takes Down the Family Bible\nSo having prepared the sisters, Dobbin hastened away to the City to perform the rest and more difficult part of the task which he had undertaken.", "그리하여 자매들을 미리 대비시켜 둔 다음, 도빈은 자신이 떠맡은 임무 가운데 나머지이자 더 까다로운 부분을 처리하기 위해 서둘러 시내로 향했다."], ["The idea of facing old Osborne rendered him not a little nervous, and more than once he thought of leaving the young ladies to communicate the secret, which, as he was aware, they could not long retain.", "늙은 오스본을 마주할 생각을 하니 적잖이 마음이 졸아들어서, 그는 한 번 이상이나 젊은 숙녀들에게 비밀을 전하는 일을 맡겨 버릴까 하고 생각했다."], ["But he had promised to report to George upon the manner in which the elder Osborne bore the intelligence; so going into the City to the paternal counting-house in Thames Street, he despatched thence a note to Mr.", "그가 알기로 그들은 그 비밀을 오래 간직하지 못할 것이었기 때문이다. 그러나 그는 큰오스본이 그 소식을 어떻게 받아들이는지 조지에게 보고하기로 약속한 터였다."], ["Osborne begging for a half-hour's conversation relative to the affairs of his son George. Dobbin's messenger returned from Mr.", "그래서 시내에 있는 템스 거리의 부친 회계 사무소로 들어가서, 그곳에서 오스본 씨에게 아들 조지의 일과 관련하여 삼십 분만 이야기를 나누었으면 한다는 쪽지를 띄웠다."], ["Osborne's house of business, with the compliments of the latter, who would be very happy to see the Captain immediately, and away accordingly Dobbin went to confront him.", "도빈의 심부름꾼은 오스본 씨의 사업소에서 돌아왔는데, 그 신사가 대위를 즉시 만나면 더없이 기쁘겠다는 인사를 전해 왔다. 그리하여 도빈은 그를 마주하러 길을 나섰다."]], [["The Captain, with a half-guilty secret to confess, and with the prospect of a painful and stormy interview before him, entered Mr. Osborne's offices with a most dismal countenance and abashed gait, and, passing through the outer room where Mr.", "대위는 절반쯤 죄책감이 서린 비밀을 털어놓아야 하는 데다, 앞으로 고통스럽고 험악한 면담이 펼쳐질 것을 내다보면서, 더없이 침울한 낯빛과 주눅 든 걸음걸이로 오스본 씨의 사무실에 들어섰다."], ["Chopper presided, was greeted by that functionary from his desk with a waggish air which farther discomfited him. Mr.", "그러고는 초퍼 씨가 자리를 지키는 바깥방을 지나는데, 그 사무원이 책상에서 익살스러운 기색으로 그를 맞이하니 그는 한층 더 면구스러워졌다."], ["Chopper winked and nodded and pointed his pen towards his patron's door, and said, \"You'll find the governor all right,\"", "초퍼 씨는 눈을 찡긋하고 고개를 끄덕이더니, 펜으로 자기 주인의 방문을 가리키며 더없이 약 올리는 듯한 쾌활한 어투로 말했다."], ["with the most provoking good humour.", "\"주인 나리는 아주 흡족해하고 계실 겁니다요.\""]], [["Osborne rose too, and shook him heartily by the hand, and said, \"How do, my dear boy?\"", "오스본 역시 일어나 진심으로 그의 손을 잡고 흔들며 말했다. \"잘 지냈는가, 이 사람아?\""], ["with a cordiality that made poor George's ambassador feel doubly guilty.", "그 살가운 인사에 가엾은 조지의 사절은 곱절로 죄스러운 기분이 들었다."], ["His hand lay as if dead in the old gentleman's grasp.", "도빈의 손은 그 노신사의 손아귀 안에서 마치 죽은 듯이 늘어져 있었다."], ["He felt that he, Dobbin, was more or less the cause of all that had happened.", "그는 자신이, 다시 말해 도빈 자신이 어느 정도는 벌어진 모든 일의 원인이라고 느꼈다."], ["It was he had brought back George to Amelia: it was he had applauded, encouraged, transacted almost the marriage which he was come to reveal to George's father: and the latter was receiving him with smiles of welcome; patting him on the shoulder, and calling him \"Dobbin, my dear boy.\"", "조지를 애밀리아에게 다시 데려간 것도 그였고, 이제 조지의 아버지에게 밝히러 온 그 결혼을 갈채하고 부추기고 거의 직접 성사시키다시피 한 것도 그였다. 그런데 그 아버지는 환영의 미소로 그를 맞이하며, 어깨를 토닥이고 \"도빈, 이 사람아\"라고 부르고 있었다."], ["The envoy had indeed good reason to hang his head.", "그 사절이 고개를 떨굴 만한 충분한 이유가 있었던 것이다."]], [["Osborne fully believed that Dobbin had come to announce his son's surrender.", "오스본은 도빈이 아들의 항복을 알리러 온 것이라고 철석같이 믿고 있었다."], ["Mr. Chopper and his principal were talking over the matter between George and his father, at the very moment when Dobbin's messenger arrived.", "도빈의 심부름꾼이 막 도착한 바로 그 순간에, 초퍼 씨와 그 주인은 조지와 그 아버지 사이의 문제를 두고 이야기를 나누던 참이었다."], ["Both agreed that George was sending in his submission.", "두 사람은 조지가 굴복을 청해 왔다는 데 의견을 같이했다."], ["Both had been expecting it for some days--and \"Lord!", "둘 다 며칠 전부터 그것을 기다리고 있었던 것이다."], ["Chopper, what a marriage we'll have!\" Mr.", "\"이런, 초퍼, 우리가 얼마나 굉장한 혼사를 치르게 될지!\""], ["Osborne said to his clerk, snapping his big fingers, and jingling all the guineas and shillings in his great pockets as he eyed his subordinate with a look of triumph.", "오스본 씨는 사무원에게 그렇게 말하며, 굵직한 손가락을 딱 소리 나게 튕기고는, 큼직한 주머니 속의 기니와 실링을 짤랑짤랑 흔들며 의기양양한 눈길로 부하를 바라보았다."]], [["With similar operations conducted in both pockets, and a knowing jolly air, Osborne from his chair regarded Dobbin seated blank and silent opposite to him.", "양쪽 주머니에서 똑같은 동작을 해 가며, 다 안다는 듯 유쾌한 기색으로, 오스본은 의자에 앉은 채 맞은편에 멍하니 입을 다물고 앉아 있는 도빈을 바라보았다."], ["\"What a bumpkin he is for a Captain in the army,\"", "'군대 대위라는 자가 어찌 저리 촌뜨기 같담.'"], ["old Osborne thought.", "늙은 오스본은 생각했다."], ["\"I wonder George hasn't taught him better manners.\"", "'조지가 어째서 저자한테 좀 더 나은 예법을 가르치지 않았을꼬.'"]], [["At last Dobbin summoned courage to begin.", "마침내 도빈이 용기를 그러모아 입을 열었다."], ["\"Sir,\"", "\"어르신,\""], ["said he, \"I've brought you some very grave news.", "그가 말했다. \"아주 엄중한 소식을 가져왔습니다."], ["I have been at the Horse Guards this morning, and there's no doubt that our regiment will be ordered abroad, and on its way to Belgium before the week is over.", "오늘 아침 호스 가즈에 다녀왔는데, 우리 연대가 해외로 파병 명령을 받게 될 것이 분명합니다. 이번 주가 가기 전에 벨기에로 향할 겁니다."], ["And you know, sir, that we shan't be home again before a tussle which may be fatal to many of us.\"", "그리고 어르신께서도 아시다시피, 우리 가운데 많은 이에게 치명적일 수 있는 한바탕 격전을 치르기 전에는 다시 고국으로 돌아오지 못할 것입니다.\""], ["Osborne looked grave.", "오스본의 낯빛이 굳어졌다."], ["\"My s--, the regiment will do its duty, sir, I daresay,\"", "\"내 아들은, 아니, 연대는 제 본분을 다할 걸세, 그건 틀림없겠지.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"The French are very strong, sir,\"", "\"프랑스군은 아주 강력합니다, 어르신.\""], ["Dobbin went on.", "도빈이 말을 이었다."], ["\"The Russians and Austrians will be a long time before they can bring their troops down.", "\"러시아군과 오스트리아군이 병력을 끌어내리기까지는 오랜 시간이 걸릴 겁니다."], ["We shall have the first of the fight, sir; and depend on it Boney will take care that it shall be a hard one.\"", "첫 전투는 우리 몫이 될 겁니다, 어르신. 그리고 보니가 그 싸움을 호되게 만들 것은 분명합니다.\""]], [["\"What are you driving at, Dobbin?\"", "\"대체 무슨 말을 하려는 건가, 도빈?\""], ["his interlocutor said, uneasy and with a scowl.", "그의 상대가 불안한 기색으로 인상을 찌푸리며 말했다."], ["\"I suppose no Briton's afraid of any d---- Frenchman, hey?\"", "\"설마 영국 사람 가운데 빌어먹을 프랑스 놈을 두려워할 자가 있다는 건 아니겠지, 응?\""]], [["\"I only mean, that before we go, and considering the great and certain risk that hangs over every one of us--if there are any differences between you and George--it would be as well, sir, that--that you should shake hands: wouldn't it?", "\"제 말씀은 그저, 우리가 떠나기 전에, 그리고 우리 한 사람 한 사람에게 닥쳐 있는 그 크고 분명한 위험을 생각할 때, 만일 어르신과 조지 사이에 어떤 불화가 있다면, 두 분이 화해의 악수를 나누시는 편이 좋지 않겠습니까?"], ["Should anything happen to him, I think you would never forgive yourself if you hadn't parted in charity.\"", "만에 하나 그에게 무슨 일이라도 생긴다면, 너그러이 작별하지 못한 것을 어르신께서 평생 자책하시게 될 것 같습니다.\""]], [["As he said this, poor William Dobbin blushed crimson, and felt and owned that he himself was a traitor.", "그렇게 말하는 동안, 가엾은 윌리엄 도빈은 시뻘겋게 얼굴을 붉히며, 자기 자신이야말로 배신자라고 느끼고 또 그것을 인정했다."], ["But for him, perhaps, this severance need never have taken place.", "그가 아니었더라면 어쩌면 이런 단절은 일어날 필요조차 없었을지 모른다."], ["Why had not George's marriage been delayed?", "어째서 조지의 결혼을 미루지 않았던가?"], ["What call was there to press it on so eagerly?", "무엇 때문에 그토록 열렬하게 그것을 밀어붙였단 말인가?"], ["He felt that George would have parted from Amelia at any rate without a mortal pang.", "조지였더라면 어떻게든 죽을 만큼 괴로워하는 일 없이 애밀리아와 헤어졌으리라는 것을 그는 느꼈다."], ["Amelia, too, MIGHT have recovered the shock of losing him.", "애밀리아 역시 그를 잃은 충격에서 회복했을지도 모른다."], ["It was his counsel had brought about this marriage, and all that was to ensue from it.", "이 결혼과 거기서 비롯될 모든 일을 불러온 것은 그의 조언이었다."], ["And why was it?", "그런데 어째서였던가?"], ["Because he loved her so much that he could not bear to see her unhappy: or because his own sufferings of suspense were so unendurable that he was glad to crush them at once--as we hasten a funeral after a death, or, when a separation from those we love is imminent, cannot rest until the parting be over.", "그가 그녀를 너무도 사랑한 나머지 그녀가 불행해하는 꼴을 차마 볼 수 없었기 때문이거나, 아니면 마음 졸이며 견디는 자신의 고통이 너무도 참기 힘들어서 단번에 그것을 짓눌러 버리는 편이 차라리 반가웠기 때문일 것이다. 마치 우리가 죽음 뒤에 장례를 서둘러 치르듯, 혹은 사랑하는 이들과의 이별이 임박했을 때 그 작별이 끝나기 전에는 마음을 놓지 못하듯이 말이다."]], [["\"You are a good fellow, William,\" said Mr.", "\"자네는 좋은 사람일세, 윌리엄.\""], ["Osborne in a softened voice; \"and me and George shouldn't part in anger, that is true.", "오스본 씨가 누그러진 목소리로 말했다. \"나와 조지가 노여움을 품고 헤어져서야 안 될 일이지, 그건 옳은 말이야."], ["Look here.", "이보게."], ["I've done for him as much as any father ever did.", "나는 그 아이를 위해 어느 아비 못지않게 해 주었네."], ["He's had three times as much money from me, as I warrant your father ever gave you.", "그 아이는 내게서, 분명 자네 아버지가 자네한테 준 것의 세 곱절은 되는 돈을 받았어."], ["But I don't brag about that.", "하지만 그걸 자랑하려는 건 아니야."], ["How I've toiled for him, and worked and employed my talents and energy, I won't say.", "내가 그 아이를 위해 얼마나 애쓰고, 일하고, 재능과 기운을 쏟아부었는지는 말하지 않겠네."], ["Ask Chopper.", "초퍼한테 물어보게."], ["Ask himself.", "그 아이한테 물어봐."], ["Ask the City of London.", "런던 시내 사람들한테 물어보라고."], ["Well, I propose to him such a marriage as any nobleman in the land might be proud of--the only thing in life I ever asked him--and he refuses me.", "자, 나는 그 아이한테 이 나라 어느 귀족이라도 자랑스러워할 만한 혼처를 마련해 주었네. 내가 평생 그 아이한테 청한 단 한 가지가 그것이었는데, 그 아이가 나를 거절한 거야."], ["Am I wrong?", "내가 잘못인가?"], ["Is the quarrel of MY making?", "이 다툼이 내가 만든 것인가?"], ["What do I seek but his good, for which I've been toiling like a convict ever since he was born?", "그 아이가 태어난 이래로 죄수처럼 고되게 일해 온 게 다 그 아이를 잘되게 하려는 것 말고 무엇이었나?"], ["Nobody can say there's anything selfish in me.", "나한테 사사로운 욕심이 있다고는 아무도 말 못 해."], ["Let him come back.", "그 아이더러 돌아오라고 하게."], ["I say, here's my hand.", "자, 여기 내 손이 있네."], ["I say, forget and forgive.", "잊고 용서하겠다 이 말이야."], ["As for marrying now, it's out of the question. Let him and Miss S.", "지금 당장 결혼하는 건 어림없는 일이지만 말일세."], ["make it up, and make out the marriage afterwards, when he comes back a Colonel; for he shall be a Colonel, by G-- he shall, if money can do it.", "그 아이하고 S 양이 화해하고, 나중에 그 아이가 대령이 되어 돌아오면 그때 혼사를 치르게 하면 돼. 맹세코 그 아이는 대령이 될 거야, 돈으로 될 일이라면 반드시 그렇게 만들지."], ["I'm glad you've brought him round.", "자네가 그 아이를 돌려놓아 주어서 기쁘네."], ["I know it's you, Dobbin.", "자네 솜씨라는 걸 내가 알아, 도빈."], ["You've took him out of many a scrape before.", "전에도 자네가 그 아이를 여러 곤경에서 빼내 주었지."], ["Let him come.", "그 아이더러 오라고 하게."], ["I shan't be hard.", "나도 모질게 굴지 않겠네."], ["Come along, and dine in Russell Square to-day: both of you.", "자, 오늘 러셀 광장에 와서 함께 저녁이나 들지, 자네들 둘 다 말이야."], ["The old shop, the old hour.", "늘 가던 그 집에서, 늘 그 시각에."], ["You'll find a neck of venison, and no questions asked.\"", "사슴 목살 고기가 있을 테니, 아무것도 캐묻지 않고 말일세.\""]], [["This praise and confidence smote Dobbin's heart very keenly.", "이 칭찬과 신뢰가 도빈의 마음을 몹시도 매섭게 후벼 팠다."], ["Every moment the colloquy continued in this tone, he felt more and more guilty.", "대화가 이런 어조로 이어지는 매 순간, 그는 점점 더 죄스러워졌다. \"어르신,\""], ["\"Sir,\"", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, \"I fear you deceive yourself.", "\"어르신께서 스스로를 속이고 계신 게 아닌가 합니다."], ["I am sure you do.", "분명 그러십니다."], ["George is much too high-minded a man ever to marry for money.", "조지는 돈을 보고 결혼할 만큼 그릇이 작은 사람이 결코 아닙니다."], ["A threat on your part that you would disinherit him in case of disobedience would only be followed by resistance on his.\"", "어르신께서 말을 듣지 않으면 상속에서 제하겠다고 으름장을 놓으셔 봐야, 그 아이로서는 저항으로 맞설 뿐일 겁니다.\""]], [["\"Why, hang it, man, you don't call offering him eight or ten thousand a year threatening him?\"", "\"이런, 빌어먹을, 이 사람아, 그 아이한테 연간 팔구천 파운드를 주겠다는 게 으름장이란 말인가?\""], ["Mr. Osborne said, with still provoking good humour.", "오스본 씨가 여전히 약 올리는 듯한 쾌활한 어투로 말했다."], ["\"'Gad, if Miss S. will have me, I'm her man.", "\"원, S 양이 나를 받아 준다면야 나라도 그 사람이 되겠네."], ["I ain't particular about a shade or so of tawny.\"", "살결이 좀 거무튀튀하다 한들 나는 까다롭게 굴지 않아.\""], ["And the old gentleman gave his knowing grin and coarse laugh.", "그러고는 노신사는 다 안다는 듯한 능글맞은 웃음과 상스러운 너털웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["\"You forget, sir, previous engagements into which Captain Osborne had entered,\"", "\"어르신께서는 조지 대위가 이미 맺어 놓은 약속을 잊고 계십니다.\""], ["the ambassador said, gravely.", "사절이 엄숙하게 말했다."]], [["\"What engagements?", "\"무슨 약속?"], ["What the devil do you mean?", "대체 무슨 소리를 하는 거야?"], ["You don't mean,\" Mr.", "설마,\""], ["Osborne continued, gathering wrath and astonishment as the thought now first came upon him; \"you don't mean that he's such a d---- fool as to be still hankering after that swindling old bankrupt's daughter?", "오스본 씨가 이제야 그 생각이 처음 떠오르는 듯 노여움과 경악을 한껏 끌어올리며 말을 이었다. \"설마 그 아이가 그 사기꾼 같은 늙은 파산자 딸한테 아직도 미련을 두는, 그런 망할 바보 천치란 말은 아니겠지?"], ["You've not come here for to make me suppose that he wants to marry HER?", "자네 설마 그 아이가 그 계집애와 결혼하고 싶어 한다고 내가 믿게 하려고 여기 온 건 아니겠지?"], ["Marry HER, that IS a good one.", "그 계집애와 결혼이라니, 그것참 가관이로군."], ["My son and heir marry a beggar's girl out of a gutter.", "내 아들이자 상속자가 시궁창 출신 거지 계집애와 결혼을 해?"], ["D---- him, if he does, let him buy a broom and sweep a crossing.", "빌어먹을, 정 그렇게 한다면 빗자루나 사서 길거리나 쓸라고 하게."], ["She was always dangling and ogling after him, I recollect now; and I've no doubt she was put on by her old sharper of a father.\"", "이제 와 생각해 보니 그 계집애가 늘 그 아이한테 들러붙어 추파를 던지더군. 그 늙은 사기꾼 아비가 시킨 게 분명해.\""]], [["\"Mr. Sedley was your very good friend, sir,\"", "\"세들리 씨는 어르신의 더없이 절친한 벗이었습니다, 어르신.\""], ["Dobbin interposed, almost pleased at finding himself growing angry.", "도빈이 끼어들었는데, 자기 안에서 노여움이 솟구치는 것을 깨닫자 거의 흐뭇할 지경이었다."], ["\"Time was you called him better names than rogue and swindler.", "\"한때 어르신께서는 그분을 악당이니 사기꾼이니 하는 것보다 더 좋은 이름으로 부르셨지요."], ["The match was of your making.", "그 혼사는 어르신께서 만드신 것입니다."], ["George had no right to play fast and loose--\"", "조지는 함부로 변덕을 부릴 권리가 없었—\""]], [["\"Fast and loose!\"", "\"변덕이라니!\""], ["howled out old Osborne.", "늙은 오스본이 고래고래 소리쳤다."], ["\"Fast and loose!", "\"함부로 변덕이라니!"], ["Why, hang me, those are the very words my gentleman used himself when he gave himself airs, last Thursday was a fortnight, and talked about the British army to his father who made him.", "이런, 빌어먹을, 그건 바로 우리 도련님께서 두 주 전 목요일에 거드름을 피우면서 직접 입에 담은 말이 아닌가. 자기를 만들어 준 아비한테 영국 군대 운운하면서 말이야."], ["What, it's you who have been a setting of him up--is it?", "뭐, 그 아이를 그렇게 부추긴 게 바로 자네란 말이지?"], ["and my service to you, CAPTAIN.", "정말 고맙구먼, 대위 나리."], ["It's you who want to introduce beggars into my family.", "우리 집안에 거지들을 들이려는 게 바로 자네였어. 천만에, 사양하네, 대위."], ["Thank you for nothing, Captain.", "그 계집애와 결혼이라니, 원, 히히!"], ["Marry HER indeed--he, he!", "어째서 그래야 하지?"], ["why should he? I warrant you she'd go to him fast enough without.\"", "장담컨대 그 계집애는 결혼하지 않고도 그 아이한테 냉큼 달려갈 텐데.\""]], [["\"Sir,\"", "\"어르신,\" 도빈이 노여움을 감추지 않고 벌떡 일어서며 말했다."], ["said Dobbin, starting up in undisguised anger; \"no man shall abuse that lady in my hearing, and you least of all.\"", "\"그 누구도 제가 듣는 데서 그 숙녀를 욕보일 수는 없습니다. 더구나 어르신은 더더욱 안 됩니다.\""]], [["\"O, you're a-going to call me out, are you?", "\"오, 그래서 나한테 결투라도 신청하겠다 이건가?"], ["Stop, let me ring the bell for pistols for two.", "잠깐, 두 사람 몫의 권총을 가져오라고 종을 울려야겠군."], ["Mr. George sent you here to insult his father, did he?\"", "조지 도련님이 제 아비를 모욕하라고 자네를 여기 보낸 모양이지?\""], ["Osborne said, pulling at the bell-cord.", "오스본이 종 줄을 잡아당기며 말했다."]], [["\"Mr. Osborne,\"", "\"오스본 어르신,\""], ["said Dobbin, with a faltering voice, \"it's you who are insulting the best creature in the world.", "도빈이 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"이 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 사람을 모욕하고 계신 건 바로 어르신이십니다."], ["You had best spare her, sir, for she's your son's wife.\"", "그 사람을 너그러이 봐주시는 게 좋을 겁니다, 어르신. 그 사람은 어르신 아드님의 아내니까요.\""]], [["And with this, feeling that he could say no more, Dobbin went away, Osborne sinking back in his chair, and looking wildly after him.", "그러고는 더는 할 말이 없다고 느낀 도빈은 자리를 떠났고, 오스본은 의자에 풀썩 주저앉아 그의 뒷모습을 망연히 바라보았다."], ["A clerk came in, obedient to the bell; and the Captain was scarcely out of the court where Mr.", "종소리에 따라 사무원이 들어왔다."], ["Osborne's offices were, when Mr. Chopper the chief clerk came rushing hatless after him.", "그런데 대위가 오스본 씨의 사무실이 있는 안마당을 미처 빠져나오기도 전에, 수석 사무원 초퍼 씨가 모자도 쓰지 않은 채 그를 뒤쫓아 부랴부랴 달려 나왔다."]], [["\"For God's sake, what is it?\"", "\"제발, 대체 무슨 일입니까?\""], ["Mr. Chopper said, catching the Captain by the skirt.", "초퍼 씨가 대위의 외투 자락을 붙잡으며 말했다."], ["\"The governor's in a fit.", "\"주인 나리가 발작을 일으키셨습니다."], ["What has Mr. George been doing?\"", "조지 도련님이 무슨 일을 저지른 겁니까?\""]], [["\"He married Miss Sedley five days ago,\"", "\"닷새 전에 세들리 양과 결혼했소.\""], ["Dobbin replied.", "도빈이 대답했다."], ["\"I was his groomsman, Mr.", "\"내가 그 신랑 들러리였소, 초퍼 씨."], ["Chopper, and you must stand his friend.\"", "그러니 당신이 그의 편이 되어 주어야 하오.\""]], [["The old clerk shook his head.", "늙은 사무원은 고개를 가로저었다."], ["\"If that's your news, Captain, it's bad.", "\"그게 전하시려는 소식이라면, 대위님, 그건 나쁜 소식입니다."], ["The governor will never forgive him.\"", "주인 나리는 결코 도련님을 용서하지 않으실 겁니다.\""]], [["Dobbin begged Chopper to report progress to him at the hotel where he was stopping, and walked off moodily westwards, greatly perturbed as to the past and the future.", "도빈은 초퍼에게 자신이 묵고 있는 호텔로 진행 상황을 알려 달라고 청한 뒤, 과거와 미래에 대한 생각으로 몹시 심란한 채 침울하게 서쪽으로 발걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["When the Russell Square family came to dinner that evening, they found the father of the house seated in his usual place, but with that air of gloom on his face, which, whenever it appeared there, kept the whole circle silent.", "그날 저녁 러셀 광장의 가족이 저녁 식사 자리에 모였을 때, 그들은 가장이 늘 앉던 자리에 앉아 있는 것을 보았다. 다만 그 얼굴에는 침울한 기색이 서려 있었는데, 그것이 떠오를 때면 언제나 온 식구가 입을 다물게 마련이었다."], ["The ladies, and Mr. Bullock who dined with them, felt that the news had been communicated to Mr.", "숙녀들과 함께 식사하던 불럭 씨는, 그 소식이 오스본 씨에게 전해졌음을 직감했다."], ["Osborne. His dark looks affected Mr.", "그의 어두운 낯빛은 불럭 씨까지 잠잠하고 조용하게 만들 만큼 영향을 미쳤다."], ["Bullock so far as to render him still and quiet: but he was unusually bland and attentive to Miss Maria, by whom he sat, and to her sister presiding at the head of the table.", "그러나 그는 옆에 앉은 마리아 양과, 식탁 상석을 맡은 그 언니에게는 평소답지 않게 상냥하고 살뜰하게 굴었다."]], [["Miss Wirt, by consequence, was alone on her side of the board, a gap being left between her and Miss Jane Osborne.", "그러다 보니 워트 양은 자기 쪽 식탁에 혼자 덩그러니 앉게 되었고, 그녀와 제인 오스본 양 사이에는 빈자리가 하나 남았다."], ["Now this was George's place when he dined at home; and his cover, as we said, was laid for him in expectation of that truant's return.", "그런데 이 자리는 조지가 집에서 식사할 때 앉던 자리였다. 그리고 앞서 말했듯, 그 가출한 아들이 돌아오리라는 기대 속에 그를 위한 식기까지 차려져 있었다."], ["Nothing occurred during dinner-time except smiling Mr. Frederick's flagging confidential whispers, and the clinking of plate and china, to interrupt the silence of the repast.", "식사 시간 내내, 미소 짓는 프레더릭 씨의 점점 힘 빠지는 은밀한 속삭임과 접시며 도자기가 부딪치는 달그락 소리 말고는, 그 식사의 침묵을 깨뜨리는 것이라곤 아무것도 없었다."], ["The servants went about stealthily doing their duty.", "하인들은 살금살금 돌아다니며 제 일을 했다. 장례식의 곡꾼이라도 오스본 씨네 하인들보다 더 침울해 보이지는 못할 것이었다."], ["Mutes at funerals could not look more glum than the domestics of Mr.", "그가 도빈에게 함께 들자고 청했던 그 사슴 목살 고기를 그는 더없이 침묵한 채로 손수 썰어 나누었다."], ["Osborne The neck of venison of which he had invited Dobbin to partake, was carved by him in perfect silence; but his own share went away almost untasted, though he drank much, and the butler assiduously filled his glass.", "그러나 그의 몫은 거의 입에도 대지 않은 채 물려졌다. 술은 많이 마셨고, 집사는 부지런히 그의 잔을 채워 댔지만 말이다."]], [["At last, just at the end of the dinner, his eyes, which had been staring at everybody in turn, fixed themselves for a while upon the plate laid for George.", "마침내 식사가 막 끝나갈 무렵, 차례로 모든 이를 노려보던 그의 두 눈이 한동안 조지를 위해 차려진 식기 위에 멎었다. 이윽고 그는 왼손으로 그것을 가리켰다."], ["He pointed to it presently with his left hand.", "딸들은 그를 바라보았으나 그 신호를 알아차리지 못했다."], ["His daughters looked at him and did not comprehend, or choose to comprehend, the signal; nor did the servants at first understand it.", "아니면 알아차리고 싶지 않았던 것이리라. 하인들도 처음에는 그 뜻을 이해하지 못했다."]], [["\"Take that plate away,\"", "\"저 식기를 치워라.\""], ["at last he said, getting up with an oath--and with this pushing his chair back, he walked into his own room.", "마침내 그가 욕설을 내뱉으며 일어나 말했다. 그러면서 의자를 뒤로 밀치고는 자기 방으로 걸어 들어갔다."]], [["Behind Mr. Osborne's dining-room was the usual apartment which went in his house by the name of the study; and was sacred to the master of the house.", "오스본 씨의 식당 뒤편에는, 그 집에서 서재라는 이름으로 통하는 으레 있는 방이 하나 있었는데, 그 집 주인만의 신성한 공간이었다."], ["Hither Mr. Osborne would retire of a Sunday forenoon when not minded to go to church; and here pass the morning in his crimson leather chair, reading the paper.", "오스본 씨는 일요일 오전에 교회에 갈 마음이 내키지 않을 때면 이곳으로 물러나, 진홍빛 가죽 의자에 앉아 신문을 읽으며 오전을 보내곤 했다."], ["A couple of glazed book-cases were here, containing standard works in stout gilt bindings. The \"Annual Register,\"", "이곳에는 유리문이 달린 책장 두 개가 있었는데, 묵직한 금박 장정의 표준 저작들이 담겨 있었다."], ["the \"Gentleman's Magazine,\" \"Blair's Sermons,\"", "「연감」, 「젠틀맨스 매거진」, 「블레어의 설교집」, 그리고 「흄과 스몰릿의 영국사」"], ["and \"Hume and Smollett.\"", "같은 것들이었다."], ["From year's end to year's end he never took one of these volumes from the shelf; but there was no member of the family that would dare for his life to touch one of the books, except upon those rare Sunday evenings when there was no dinner-party, and when the great scarlet Bible and Prayer-book were taken out from the corner where they stood beside his copy of the Peerage, and the servants being rung up to the dining parlour, Osborne read the evening service to his family in a loud grating pompous voice.", "그는 한 해가 다 가도록 이 책들 가운데 단 한 권도 선반에서 꺼내는 법이 없었다. 그러나 식구들 가운데 누구도 목숨을 걸고 그 책 한 권에 손댈 엄두를 내지 못했다."], ["No member of the household, child, or domestic, ever entered that room without a certain terror.", "단, 만찬 손님이 없는 그 드문 일요일 저녁만은 예외였다. 그런 날이면, 「귀족 명감」"], ["Here he checked the housekeeper's accounts, and overhauled the butler's cellar-book.", "옆 구석에 꽂혀 있던 그 큼직한 진홍빛 성서와 기도서를 꺼내고, 하인들을 식당으로 불러 모은 뒤, 오스본이 크고 거슬리는 거드름 가득한 목소리로 식구들에게 저녁 기도를 읽어 주는 것이었다. 식구든, 아이든, 하인이든 그 누구도 일말의 두려움 없이는 그 방에 들어서지 못했다."], ["Hence he could command, across the clean gravel court-yard, the back entrance of the stables with which one of his bells communicated, and into this yard the coachman issued from his premises as into a dock, and Osborne swore at him from the study window.", "그는 이곳에서 가정부의 장부를 점검했고, 집사의 술 창고 장부를 샅샅이 살폈다. 또한 이곳에서는 말끔하게 자갈을 깐 안마당 너머로, 그의 종 하나와 연결되어 있는 마구간 뒷문을 한눈에 굽어볼 수 있었다."], ["Four times a year Miss Wirt entered this apartment to get her salary; and his daughters to receive their quarterly allowance.", "마부는 그 안마당으로, 마치 피고석에 들어서듯 자기 처소에서 나왔고, 오스본은 서재 창문에서 그에게 욕설을 퍼붓곤 했다."], ["George as a boy had been horsewhipped in this room many times; his mother sitting sick on the stair listening to the cuts of the whip.", "일 년에 네 번, 워트 양은 봉급을 받으러 이 방에 들어왔고, 딸들은 분기별 용돈을 받으러 들어왔다. 조지는 어릴 적 이 방에서 여러 번 채찍질을 당했고, 그 어머니는 계단에 앓는 듯 앉아 채찍이 가르는 소리에 귀를 기울이곤 했다."], ["The boy was scarcely ever known to cry under the punishment; the poor woman used to fondle and kiss him secretly, and give him money to soothe him when he came out.", "그 아이가 그 벌을 받으면서 우는 일은 좀처럼 없었다. 가엾은 그 여인은 아이가 나오면 몰래 어루만지고 입 맞추며, 달래느라 돈을 쥐여 주곤 했다."]], [["There was a picture of the family over the mantelpiece, removed thither from the front room after Mrs.", "벽난로 선반 위에는 가족 초상화가 한 점 걸려 있었는데, 오스본 부인이 죽은 뒤 앞방에서 이리로 옮겨 온 것이었다."], ["Osborne's death--George was on a pony, the elder sister holding him up a bunch of flowers; the younger led by her mother's hand; all with red cheeks and large red mouths, simpering on each other in the approved family-portrait manner.", "조지는 조랑말을 타고 있고, 큰언니가 그에게 꽃다발을 들어 올려 주고 있으며, 작은딸은 어머니의 손에 이끌리고 있었다. 모두 발그레한 볼과 큼직하고 붉은 입을 하고서, 으레 가족 초상화에서 그러듯 서로를 향해 야릇하게 미소 짓고 있었다."], ["The mother lay underground now, long since forgotten--the sisters and brother had a hundred different interests of their own, and, familiar still, were utterly estranged from each other.", "이제 어머니는 땅속에 묻혀 오래전에 잊혔고, 자매와 형제는 저마다 백 가지 다른 관심사를 좇으며, 여전히 가까운 사이이면서도 서로에게서 완전히 멀어져 있었다."], ["Some few score of years afterwards, when all the parties represented are grown old, what bitter satire there is in those flaunting childish family-portraits, with their farce of sentiment and smiling lies, and innocence so self-conscious and self-satisfied.", "그로부터 몇십 년이 지나, 거기 그려진 모든 이가 늙어 버렸을 때, 저 으스대는 어린 시절 가족 초상화들이란 얼마나 쓰디쓴 풍자란 말인가. 그 감상의 익살과 미소 짓는 거짓, 그리고 그토록 자의식 강하고 자족적인 천진함이라니."], ["Osborne's own state portrait, with that of his great silver inkstand and arm-chair, had taken the place of honour in the dining-room, vacated by the family-piece.", "오스본 자신의 위풍당당한 초상화는, 그 큼직한 은 잉크병과 안락의자가 함께 그려진 채로, 가족 초상화가 비운 자리, 곧 식당의 영예로운 자리를 차지하게 되었다."]], [["To this study old Osborne retired then, greatly to the relief of the small party whom he left.", "그리하여 늙은 오스본은 이 서재로 물러갔고, 뒤에 남겨진 그 자그마한 식구들은 크게 안도했다."], ["When the servants had withdrawn, they began to talk for a while volubly but very low; then they went upstairs quietly, Mr.", "하인들이 물러가자, 그들은 잠시 동안 말은 많되 아주 나직하게 이야기를 나누었다."], ["Bullock accompanying them stealthily on his creaking shoes.", "그러고는 조용히 위층으로 올라갔는데, 불럭 씨도 삐걱대는 구두를 신은 채 살금살금 그들을 뒤따랐다."], ["He had no heart to sit alone drinking wine, and so close to the terrible old gentleman in the study hard at hand.", "그는 가까이에 있는 서재 안의 그 무서운 노신사 곁에서, 혼자 앉아 술을 마실 엄두가 나지 않았던 것이다."]], [["An hour at least after dark, the butler, not having received any summons, ventured to tap at his door and take him in wax candles and tea.", "날이 어두워지고 적어도 한 시간이 지나도록 아무런 부름이 없자, 집사는 큰맘 먹고 그 방문을 두드려 밀랍 양초와 차를 들여놓았다."], ["The master of the house sate in his chair, pretending to read the paper, and when the servant, placing the lights and refreshment on the table by him, retired, Mr.", "집주인은 의자에 앉아 신문을 읽는 척하고 있었는데, 하인이 등불과 다과를 그 옆 탁자에 놓고 물러가자, 오스본 씨는 일어나 그 뒤로 문을 잠갔다."], ["Osborne got up and locked the door after him.", "이번에는 사정을 잘못 짚을 여지가 없었다."], ["This time there was no mistaking the matter; all the household knew that some great catastrophe was going to happen which was likely direly to affect Master George.", "온 식구가, 조지 도련님에게 끔찍한 영향을 미칠 법한 어떤 큰 재앙이 닥쳐오고 있음을 알아차렸다."]], [["In the large shining mahogany escritoire Mr. Osborne had a drawer especially devoted to his son's affairs and papers.", "번쩍이는 큼직한 마호가니 사무용 책상 안에는, 오스본 씨가 특별히 아들의 일과 서류만을 위해 마련해 둔 서랍이 하나 있었다."], ["Here he kept all the documents relating to him ever since he had been a boy: here were his prize copy-books and drawing-books, all bearing George's hand, and that of the master: here were his first letters in large round-hand sending his love to papa and mamma, and conveying his petitions for a cake.", "그는 아들이 어린아이였을 적부터 그와 관련된 모든 서류를 이곳에 보관해 왔다. 이곳에는 조지의 필체와 선생의 필체가 함께 담긴 상장이며 습자 공책이며 그림 공책이 있었고, 또박또박한 큰 글씨로 아빠와 엄마에게 사랑을 전하고 케이크를 사 달라고 조르던 그의 첫 편지들도 있었다."], ["His dear godpapa Sedley was more than once mentioned in them.", "그 편지들에는 그의 사랑하는 대부 세들리가 한 번 이상 언급되어 있었다."], ["Curses quivered on old Osborne's livid lips, and horrid hatred and disappointment writhed in his heart, as looking through some of these papers he came on that name.", "그 서류들을 들춰 보다가 그 이름을 마주치자, 늙은 오스본의 핏기 가신 입술에 저주가 떨리며 새어 나왔고, 끔찍한 증오와 실망이 그 가슴속에서 꿈틀거렸다."], ["They were all marked and docketed, and tied with red tape.", "그것들은 하나하나 표시되고 정리되어 붉은 끈으로 묶여 있었다."], ["It was--\"From Georgy, requesting 5s., April 23, 18--; answered, April 25\"--or \"Georgy about a pony, October 13\"--and so forth.", "\"조지가 5실링을 청함, 18××년 4월 23일, 4월 25일에 답함\"이라거나 \"조지가 조랑말에 관하여, 10월 13일\" 따위로 적혀 있었다."], ["In another packet were \"Dr. S.'s accounts\"--\"G.'s tailor's bills and outfits, drafts on me by G.", "또 다른 묶음에는 \"S 박사의 청구서\", \"조지의 양복점 청구서 및 의복비, 주니어 조지 오스본이 본인에게 발행한 어음\""], ["Osborne, jun.,\"", "등이 들어 있었다."], ["&c.--his letters from the West Indies--his agent's letters, and the newspapers containing his commissions: here was a whip he had when a boy, and in a paper a locket containing his hair, which his mother used to wear.", "서인도제도에서 온 그의 편지들, 그의 대리인이 보낸 편지들, 그리고 그의 임관 소식이 실린 신문들도 있었다. 이곳에는 그가 어릴 적 쓰던 채찍 한 자루도 있었고, 종이 속에는 그의 어머니가 늘 지니고 다니던, 그의 머리카락이 담긴 작은 로켓도 있었다."]], [["Turning one over after another, and musing over these memorials, the unhappy man passed many hours.", "하나씩 차례로 뒤집어 보며 이 추억의 물건들을 곱씹는 사이, 이 불행한 사내는 여러 시간을 흘려보냈다."], ["His dearest vanities, ambitious hopes, had all been here.", "그의 가장 소중한 허영과 야심 찬 희망이 모두 이곳에 있었던 것이다."], ["What pride he had in his boy!", "그는 자기 아들을 얼마나 자랑스러워했던가!"], ["He was the handsomest child ever seen.", "그 아이는 일찍이 본 적 없을 만큼 잘생긴 아이였다."], ["Everybody said he was like a nobleman's son.", "다들 그 아이가 귀족의 아들 같다고 했다."], ["A royal princess had remarked him, and kissed him, and asked his name in Kew Gardens.", "왕가의 어느 공주가 큐 정원에서 그 아이를 눈여겨보고는 입을 맞추며 이름을 물어보기까지 했다."], ["What City man could show such another?", "시내의 어느 사업가가 이만한 아들을 또 내보일 수 있겠는가?"], ["Could a prince have been better cared for?", "왕자라 한들 이보다 더 잘 보살핌을 받을 수 있었겠는가?"], ["Anything that money could buy had been his son's.", "돈으로 살 수 있는 것이라면 무엇이든 그의 아들 차지였다."], ["He used to go down on speech-days with four horses and new liveries, and scatter new shillings among the boys at the school where George was: when he went with George to the depot of his regiment, before the boy embarked for Canada, he gave the officers such a dinner as the Duke of York might have sat down to.", "그는 학예 발표회 날이면 말 네 필을 거느리고 새 제복을 입혀 내려가, 조지가 다니던 학교의 소년들에게 새 실링 동전을 뿌리곤 했다. 아이가 캐나다로 떠나기 전, 그가 조지를 데리고 연대 주둔지로 갔을 때는, 요크 공작이라도 자리에 앉을 만한 만찬을 장교들에게 대접했다."], ["Had he ever refused a bill when George drew one?", "조지가 어음을 발행했을 때 그가 한 번이라도 그것을 거절한 적이 있었던가?"], ["There they were--paid without a word.", "그 어음들이 여기 있었다. 군말 한마디 없이 지불된 채로."], ["Many a general in the army couldn't ride the horses he had!", "군대의 숱한 장군들도 그가 가진 말들을 타지 못했을 것이다!"], ["He had the child before his eyes, on a hundred different days when he remembered George after dinner, when he used to come in as bold as a lord and drink off his glass by his father's side, at the head of the table--on the pony at Brighton, when he cleared the hedge and kept up with the huntsman--on the day when he was presented to the Prince Regent at the levee, when all Saint James's couldn't produce a finer young fellow.", "그는 백 가지 다른 날들의 모습으로 그 아이를 눈앞에 떠올렸다. 저녁 식사 뒤 조지를 떠올릴 때면, 식탁 상석 아버지 곁에서 귀족처럼 당당하게 들어와 잔을 단숨에 비우던 모습이며, 브라이턴에서 조랑말을 타고 산울타리를 훌쩍 뛰어넘어 사냥꾼들과 나란히 내달리던 모습이며, 궁중 알현식에서 섭정 왕세자를 알현하던 그날, 온 세인트제임스를 통틀어도 그보다 더 멋진 젊은이를 내놓을 수 없었던 그 모습이 떠올랐다."], ["And this, this was the end of all!--to marry a bankrupt and fly in the face of duty and fortune!", "그런데 이것이, 이것이 그 모든 것의 끝이라니! 파산자의 딸과 결혼하여 본분과 행운을 정면으로 거스르다니!"], ["What humiliation and fury: what pangs of sickening rage, balked ambition and love; what wounds of outraged vanity, tenderness even, had this old worldling now to suffer under!", "이 늙은 속물이 이제 견뎌야 하는 것은 그 얼마나 큰 굴욕과 격분이었던가. 속이 메스꺼울 만큼 들끓는 분노와, 좌절된 야심과 사랑의 고통과, 짓밟힌 허영과 심지어 부정(父情)에 입은 상처는 또 어떠했던가!"]], [["Having examined these papers, and pondered over this one and the other, in that bitterest of all helpless woe, with which miserable men think of happy past times--George's father took the whole of the documents out of the drawer in which he had kept them so long, and locked them into a writing-box, which he tied, and sealed with his seal.", "이 서류들을 살펴보고, 비참한 사내가 행복했던 지난날을 떠올릴 때 으레 겪는 그 어떤 무력한 비애보다도 쓰라린 심정으로 이것저것을 곱씹은 뒤, 조지의 아버지는 그토록 오래 간직해 온 그 서랍에서 서류 일체를 꺼내, 필기함 안에 넣고 끈으로 묶은 다음 자신의 인장으로 봉인했다."], ["Then he opened the book-case, and took down the great red Bible we have spoken of a pompous book, seldom looked at, and shining all over with gold.", "그러고는 책장을 열어, 앞서 말한 그 큼직한 붉은 성서를 꺼냈다. 좀처럼 펼쳐 보는 일 없는, 겉면이 온통 금빛으로 번쩍이는 거창한 책이었다."], ["There was a frontispiece to the volume, representing Abraham sacrificing Isaac.", "그 책의 권두 삽화에는 아브라함이 이삭을 제물로 바치는 장면이 그려져 있었다."], ["Here, according to custom, Osborne had recorded on the fly-leaf, and in his large clerk-like hand, the dates of his marriage and his wife's death, and the births and Christian names of his children.", "이곳, 관례에 따라 오스본은 그 면지에 자기 특유의 사무원 같은 큰 글씨로 자신의 결혼 날짜와 아내의 사망 날짜, 그리고 자녀들의 출생일과 세례명을 적어 두었다."], ["Jane came first, then George Sedley Osborne, then Maria Frances, and the days of the christening of each.", "제인이 맨 처음이고, 그다음이 조지 세들리 오스본, 그다음이 마리아 프랜시스였으며, 저마다의 세례일도 함께 적혀 있었다."], ["Taking a pen, he carefully obliterated George's names from the page; and when the leaf was quite dry, restored the volume to the place from which he had moved it.", "그는 펜을 들어, 조지의 이름을 그 면지에서 정성스레 지워 버렸다. 그리고 그 면지가 완전히 마르자, 책을 옮겨 두었던 자리에 도로 꽂아 두었다."], ["Then he took a document out of another drawer, where his own private papers were kept; and having read it, crumpled it up and lighted it at one of the candles, and saw it burn entirely away in the grate.", "그러고는 자신의 사사로운 서류들을 보관하는 또 다른 서랍에서 문서 한 장을 꺼내, 그것을 읽은 뒤 구겨서 촛불 하나에 불을 붙여, 벽난로 안에서 그것이 완전히 타 없어지는 것을 지켜보았다."], ["It was his will; which being burned, he sate down and wrote off a letter, and rang for his servant, whom he charged to deliver it in the morning.", "그것은 그의 유언장이었다. 그것을 태워 버린 뒤, 그는 자리에 앉아 편지 한 통을 써 내려갔고, 하인을 불러 아침에 그것을 전하라고 일렀다."], ["It was morning already: as he went up to bed, the whole house was alight with the sunshine; and the birds were singing among the fresh green leaves in Russell Square.", "이미 아침이 밝아 있었다. 그가 잠자리에 들러 올라갈 무렵, 온 집안은 햇살로 환했고, 새들은 러셀 광장의 싱그러운 푸른 잎새 사이에서 지저귀고 있었다."]], [["Anxious to keep all Mr. Osborne's family and dependants in good humour, and to make as many friends as possible for George in his hour of adversity, William Dobbin, who knew the effect which good dinners and good wines have upon the soul of man, wrote off immediately on his return to his inn the most hospitable of invitations to Thomas Chopper, Esquire, begging that gentleman to dine with him at the Slaughters'", "오스본 씨의 온 식구와 부하들의 비위를 맞추고, 역경에 처한 조지를 위해 되도록 많은 벗을 만들어 두고 싶었던 윌리엄 도빈은, 좋은 만찬과 좋은 술이 사람의 영혼에 미치는 효과를 잘 알고 있었던 터라, 자기 숙소로 돌아오자마자 즉시 토머스 초퍼 귀하에게 더없이 후한 초대장을 써 보냈다."], ["next day. The note reached Mr.", "이튿날 슬로터스 식당에서 함께 저녁을 들자고 그 신사에게 청하는 내용이었다."], ["Chopper before he left the City, and the instant reply was, that \"Mr. Chopper presents his respectful compliments, and will have the honour and pleasure of waiting on Captain D.\"", "그 쪽지는 초퍼 씨가 시내를 떠나기 전에 그에게 닿았고, 즉각 돌아온 답은 \"초퍼 씨가 정중히 인사를 올리며, D 대위님을 모시는 영광과 기쁨을 누리겠습니다\"라는 것이었다."], ["The invitation and the rough draft of the answer were shown to Mrs. Chopper and her daughters on his return to Somers'", "그날 저녁 서머스 타운으로 돌아온 그는 초퍼 부인과 딸들에게 그 초대장과 답장 초안을 보여 주었고, 식구들이 둘러앉아 차를 마시는 동안 그들은 군인 양반들이며 웨스트엔드 신사들에 관한 이야기를 한껏 들떠 나누었다."], ["Town that evening, and they talked about military gents and West End men with great exultation as the family sate and partook of tea.", "딸들이 잠자리에 들고 나자, 초퍼 부부는 주인 나리네 집안에서 벌어지고 있는 기이한 사건들에 관해 이야기를 주고받았다."], ["When the girls had gone to rest, Mr. and Mrs.", "사무원은 자기 주인이 그토록 격앙된 모습을 일찍이 본 적이 없었다."], ["C. discoursed upon the strange events which were occurring in the governor's family.", "도빈 대위가 떠난 뒤 오스본 씨에게 들어갔을 때, 초퍼 씨는 자기 상관의 얼굴이 시퍼렇게 질려 거의 발작을 일으킬 지경인 것을 보았다."], ["Never had the clerk seen his principal so moved.", "오스본 씨와 젊은 대위 사이에 어떤 끔찍한 다툼이 벌어졌음이 분명하다고 그는 확신했다."], ["When he went in to Mr. Osborne, after Captain Dobbin's departure, Mr.", "초퍼는 지난 삼 년 동안 오스본 대위에게 지급된 금액 일체의 명세서를 작성하라는 지시를 받았다."], ["Chopper found his chief black in the face, and all but in a fit: some dreadful quarrel, he was certain, had occurred between Mr. O.", "\"게다가 어지간히도 많은 돈을 받았더란 말입니다.\""], ["and the young Captain. Chopper had been instructed to make out an account of all sums paid to Captain Osborne within the last three years.", "수석 사무원은 그렇게 말하면서, 그토록 후하게 기니를 뿌려 댄 것을 두고 그 늙은 주인과 젊은 주인을 한층 더 존경했다."], ["\"And a precious lot of money he has had too,\"", "그 다툼은 세들리 양에 관한 무언가였다."], ["the chief clerk said, and respected his old and young master the more, for the liberal way in which the guineas had been flung about. The dispute was something about Miss Sedley.", "초퍼 부인은 그 대위처럼 잘생긴 젊은이를 놓치게 된 그 가엾은 아가씨가 딱하다며 입에 침이 마르도록 말했다."], ["Mrs. Chopper vowed and declared she pitied that poor young lady to lose such a handsome young fellow as the Capting.", "변변찮은 배당금을 지불한 불운한 투기꾼의 딸이라는 점에서, 초퍼 씨는 세들리 양에게 그다지 큰 호감이 없었다."], ["As the daughter of an unlucky speculator, who had paid a very shabby dividend, Mr. Chopper had no great regard for Miss Sedley.", "그는 런던 시내의 그 무엇보다도 오스본 집안을 존경했다."], ["He respected the house of Osborne before all others in the City of London: and his hope and wish was that Captain George should marry a nobleman's daughter.", "그래서 그의 바람이자 소망은 조지 대위가 귀족의 딸과 결혼하는 것이었다. 그날 밤 사무원은 그 주인보다 훨씬 더 깊이 잠들었다."], ["The clerk slept a great deal sounder than his principal that night; and, cuddling his children after breakfast (of which he partook with a very hearty appetite, though his modest cup of life was only sweetened with brown sugar), he set off in his best Sunday suit and frilled shirt for business, promising his admiring wife not to punish Captain D.'s port too severely that evening.", "그리고 아침 식사를 마친 뒤(비록 그의 검소한 인생의 잔은 흑설탕으로만 달큼해질 뿐이었지만, 그는 아주 왕성한 식욕으로 그것을 들었다) 아이들을 끌어안고는, 그날 저녁 D 대위의 포도주를 너무 가혹하게 축내지는 않겠다고 흠모하는 아내에게 약속하며, 가장 좋은 나들이옷에 주름 잡힌 셔츠 차림으로 일하러 길을 나섰다."]], [["Mr. Osborne's countenance, when he arrived in the City at his usual time, struck those dependants who were accustomed, for good reasons, to watch its expression, as peculiarly ghastly and worn.", "오스본 씨가 평소 시각에 시내에 도착했을 때, 그 얼굴은—나름의 이유로 그 표정을 살피는 데 익숙한 부하들의 눈에—유난히 송장 같고 초췌해 보였다."], ["At twelve o'clock Mr. Higgs (of the firm of Higgs & Blatherwick, solicitors, Bedford Row) called by appointment, and was ushered into the governor's private room, and closeted there for more than an hour.", "열두 시에 히그스 씨(베드퍼드 거리의 변호사 사무소 히그스 앤드 블래더윅의 일원이었다)가 약속대로 찾아와, 주인의 개인 방으로 안내되어 한 시간 넘게 그 안에 틀어박혀 있었다."], ["At about one Mr. Chopper received a note brought by Captain Dobbin's man, and containing an inclosure for Mr.", "한 시쯤 초퍼 씨는 도빈 대위의 하인이 가져온 쪽지를 받았는데, 그 안에는 오스본 씨에게 전할 봉투가 동봉되어 있어 사무원이 들어가 그것을 전달했다."], ["Osborne, which the clerk went in and delivered. A short time afterwards Mr.", "얼마 지나지 않아 초퍼 씨와 그다음 사무원인 버치 씨가 불려 들어가, 문서 한 장에 증인으로 서명해 달라는 요청을 받았다."], ["Chopper and Mr.", "\"새 유언장을 작성했네.\""], ["Birch, the next clerk, were summoned, and requested to witness a paper.", "오스본 씨가 말했고, 이에 두 사람은 각자 거기에 이름을 적어 넣었다."], ["\"I've been making a new will,\"", "아무런 대화도 오가지 않았다."], ["Mr. Osborne said, to which these gentlemen appended their names accordingly.", "히그스 씨는 바깥방으로 나오면서 몹시 엄숙한 표정을 지었고, 초퍼 씨의 얼굴을 아주 뚫어지게 바라보았다."], ["No conversation passed. Mr.", "그러나 아무런 설명도 없었다."], ["Higgs looked exceedingly grave as he came into the outer rooms, and very hard in Mr. Chopper's face; but there were not any explanations.", "사람들은 오스본 씨가 그날 하루 종일 유난히 조용하고 온화하다는 것을 알아챘는데, 그 어두운 거동을 보고 불길한 일을 점쳤던 이들에게는 놀라운 노릇이었다."], ["It was remarked that Mr. Osborne was particularly quiet and gentle all day, to the surprise of those who had augured ill from his darkling demeanour.", "그는 그날 누구에게도 험한 말을 하지 않았고, 욕설을 내뱉는 것도 단 한 번 들리지 않았다."], ["He called no man names that day, and was not heard to swear once.", "그는 일찍 일을 파했다."], ["He left business early; and before going away, summoned his chief clerk once more, and having given him general instructions, asked him, after some seeming hesitation and reluctance to speak, if he knew whether Captain Dobbin was in town?", "그리고 자리를 뜨기 전에 수석 사무원을 다시 한번 불러, 전반적인 지시를 내린 뒤, 말을 꺼내기를 망설이고 꺼리는 듯하다가, 도빈 대위가 시내에 있는지 아느냐고 물었다."]], [["Chopper said he believed he was.", "초퍼는 그런 것 같다고 대답했다."], ["Indeed both of them knew the fact perfectly.", "사실 두 사람 다 그 사실을 빤히 알고 있었다."]], [["Osborne took a letter directed to that officer, and giving it to the clerk, requested the latter to deliver it into Dobbin's own hands immediately.", "오스본은 그 장교 앞으로 된 편지 한 통을 집어 사무원에게 건네며, 그것을 즉시 도빈 본인의 손에 직접 전하라고 청했다."]], [["\"And now, Chopper,\" says he, taking his hat, and with a strange look, \"my mind will be easy.\"", "\"자, 이제 됐네, 초퍼.\""], ["Exactly as the clock struck two (there was no doubt an appointment between the pair) Mr.", "그가 모자를 집어 들며, 묘한 표정으로 말했다. \"내 마음이 편안해지겠군.\""], ["Frederick Bullock called, and he and Mr. Osborne walked away together.", "시계가 두 시를 치는 바로 그 순간(둘 사이에 약속이 있었음이 분명했다) 프레더릭 불럭 씨가 찾아왔고, 그와 오스본 씨는 함께 걸어 나갔다."]], [["The Colonel of the --th regiment, in which Messieurs Dobbin and Osborne had companies, was an old General who had made his first campaign under Wolfe at Quebec, and was long since quite too old and feeble for command; but he took some interest in the regiment of which he was the nominal head, and made certain of his young officers welcome at his table, a kind of hospitality which I believe is not now common amongst his brethren.", "도빈과 오스본 두 사람이 각각 중대를 맡고 있는 ××연대의 대령은, 퀘벡에서 울프 장군 휘하로 첫 출정을 치른 늙은 장군이었다. 지휘를 맡기에는 이미 오래전에 너무 늙고 노쇠해진 인물이었으나, 그는 자신이 명목상의 수장으로 있는 그 연대에 어느 정도 관심을 두고 있어, 젊은 장교 몇몇을 자기 식탁에 기꺼이 맞아들이곤 했다."], ["Captain Dobbin was an especial favourite of this old General.", "그런 환대란 요즘 그의 동료들 사이에서는 흔치 않은 일이지 싶다."], ["Dobbin was versed in the literature of his profession, and could talk about the great Frederick, and the Empress Queen, and their wars, almost as well as the General himself, who was indifferent to the triumphs of the present day, and whose heart was with the tacticians of fifty years back.", "도빈 대위는 이 늙은 장군이 특히 아끼는 사람이었다. 도빈은 자기 직업의 문헌에 정통해서, 위대한 프리드리히와 여제이자 왕비였던 분, 그리고 그들의 전쟁에 관하여 장군 본인 못지않게 능숙하게 이야기를 나눌 수 있었다."], ["This officer sent a summons to Dobbin to come and breakfast with him, on the morning when Mr.", "장군은 오늘날의 승리 따위에는 무심했고, 그 마음은 오십 년 전의 전술가들에게 가 있었다."], ["Osborne altered his will and Mr. Chopper put on his best shirt frill, and then informed his young favourite, a couple of days in advance, of that which they were all expecting--a marching order to go to Belgium.", "이 장교는 오스본 씨가 유언장을 고치고 초퍼 씨가 가장 좋은 셔츠 주름을 차려입던 그날 아침, 도빈에게 함께 아침 식사를 들자고 청했고, 그러고는 그 젊은 총신에게 모두가 예상하고 있던 일을 이틀 앞서 알려 주었다."], ["The order for the regiment to hold itself in readiness would leave the Horse Guards in a day or two; and as transports were in plenty, they would get their route before the week was over.", "바로 벨기에로 진군하라는 명령이었다. 연대더러 대기 태세를 갖추라는 명령이 하루 이틀 안에 호스 가즈에서 떨어질 것이고, 수송선이 넉넉하니 이번 주가 가기 전에 진군로를 받게 되리라는 것이었다."], ["Recruits had come in during the stay of the regiment at Chatham; and the old General hoped that the regiment which had helped to beat Montcalm in Canada, and to rout Mr.", "채텀에 연대가 머무는 동안 신병들이 들어와 있었다."], ["Washington on Long Island, would prove itself worthy of its historical reputation on the oft-trodden battle-grounds of the Low Countries.", "늙은 장군은, 캐나다에서 몽칼름을 무찌르고 롱아일랜드에서 워싱턴 씨를 패주시키는 데 한몫했던 그 연대가, 숱하게 짓밟힌 저지대의 전장에서 그 역사적 명성에 부끄럽지 않은 모습을 보여 주기를 바랐다."], ["\"And so, my good friend, if you have any affaire la,\"", "\"그러니, 이보게, 만약 자네한테 무슨 볼일이라도 있다면,\""], ["said the old General, taking a pinch of snuff with his trembling white old hand, and then pointing to the spot of his robe de chambre under which his heart was still feebly beating, \"if you have any Phillis to console, or to bid farewell to papa and mamma, or any will to make, I recommend you to set about your business without delay.\"", "늙은 장군은 떨리는 희고 늙은 손으로 코담배를 한 줌 집으며, 이어서 아직 가냘프게 뛰고 있는 심장이 자리한 실내복 자락을 가리키며 말했다. \"달래 줄 처녀라도 있다거나, 아버지 어머니께 작별 인사를 드린다거나, 작성해 둘 유언이라도 있다면, 지체 없이 그 일에 착수하기를 권하네.\""], ["With which the General gave his young friend a finger to shake, and a good-natured nod of his powdered and pigtailed head; and the door being closed upon Dobbin, sate down to pen a poulet (he was exceedingly vain of his French) to Mademoiselle Amenaide of His Majesty's Theatre.", "그러면서 장군은 젊은 벗에게 손가락 하나를 내밀어 흔들게 하고는, 분칠하고 변발한 머리를 사람 좋게 끄덕여 주었다. 그리고 도빈을 향해 문이 닫히자, 자리에 앉아 국립 극장의 마드무아젤 아메나이드에게 연애편지(그는 자신의 프랑스어 솜씨를 몹시도 우쭐해했다)를 끄적이기 시작했다."]], [["This news made Dobbin grave, and he thought of our friends at Brighton, and then he was ashamed of himself that Amelia was always the first thing in his thoughts (always before anybody--before father and mother, sisters and duty--always at waking and sleeping indeed, and all day long); and returning to his hotel, he sent off a brief note to Mr. Osborne acquainting him with the information which he had received, and which might tend farther, he hoped, to bring about a reconciliation with George.", "이 소식은 도빈을 침울하게 만들었고, 그는 브라이턴에 있는 우리 벗들을 떠올렸다. 그러다가 그는 자신의 생각 속에서 늘 애밀리아가 가장 먼저라는 것이(늘 누구보다도 먼저였다. 아버지와 어머니, 누이들과 본분보다도 먼저였고, 실은 잠에서 깰 때나 잠들 때나, 또 온종일 그러했다) 부끄러워졌다. 그래서 호텔로 돌아온 그는 오스본 씨에게 짤막한 쪽지를 띄워, 자신이 들은 소식을 알렸다. 그것이 조지와의 화해를 한층 더 이끌어 내는 데 보탬이 되기를 바라면서 말이다."]], [["This note, despatched by the same messenger who had carried the invitation to Chopper on the previous day, alarmed the worthy clerk not a little.", "전날 초퍼에게 초대장을 전했던 바로 그 심부름꾼 편에 보내진 이 쪽지는, 그 어진 사무원을 적잖이 놀라게 했다."], ["It was inclosed to him, and as he opened the letter he trembled lest the dinner should be put off on which he was calculating.", "그 쪽지는 그 앞으로 봉해져 있었는데, 그는 편지를 뜯으면서 자기가 잔뜩 기대하고 있던 그 만찬이 미뤄지는 것은 아닐까 하여 벌벌 떨었다."], ["His mind was inexpressibly relieved when he found that the envelope was only a reminder for himself.", "봉투 안에 든 것이 그저 그 자신을 위한 알림에 지나지 않음을 알았을 때, 그의 마음은 이루 말할 수 없이 놓였다."], ["(\"I shall expect you at half-past five,\" Captain Dobbin wrote.) He was very much interested about his employer's family; but, que voulez-vous?", "(\"다섯 시 반에 기다리겠소\"라고 도빈 대위는 적어 두었다.) 그는 자기 고용주의 집안일에 무척 관심이 많았으나, 어쩌겠는가?"], ["a grand dinner was of more concern to him than the affairs of any other mortal.", "거창한 만찬이 그에게는 다른 어떤 사람의 일보다도 더 큰 관심사였던 것이다."]], [["Dobbin was quite justified in repeating the General's information to any officers of the regiment whom he should see in the course of his peregrinations; accordingly he imparted it to Ensign Stubble, whom he met at the agent's, and who--such was his military ardour--went off instantly to purchase a new sword at the accoutrement-maker's.", "도빈은 자신이 여기저기 돌아다니는 길에 만나는 연대 장교라면 누구에게든 장군의 소식을 전해도 무방한 처지였다. 그리하여 그는 대리인의 사무소에서 마주친 스터블 소위에게 그것을 알렸는데, 군인다운 열정이 어찌나 대단하던지 그 소위는 곧장 군장 제작자에게 새 검을 사러 달려갔다."], ["Here this young fellow, who, though only seventeen years of age, and about sixty-five inches high, with a constitution naturally rickety and much impaired by premature brandy and water, had an undoubted courage and a lion's heart, poised, tried, bent, and balanced a weapon such as he thought would do execution amongst Frenchmen.", "이 젊은이는, 비록 겨우 열일곱 살에 키도 예순다섯 인치 남짓이고, 타고나기를 곱사등이 기질에 때 이른 브랜디와 물 탓에 몸이 많이 상해 있었지만, 의심할 바 없는 용기와 사자의 심장을 지니고 있었다. 이곳에서 그는 프랑스 놈들 사이에서 한몫 단단히 해낼 만하다 싶은 무기 하나를, 손에 쥐고 가늠하고 휘둘러 보고 굽혀 보고 무게를 재 보았다."], ["Shouting \"Ha, ha!\"", "\"하, 하!\""], ["and stamping his little feet with tremendous energy, he delivered the point twice or thrice at Captain Dobbin, who parried the thrust laughingly with his bamboo walking-stick.", "하고 외치며 작은 발을 어마어마한 기세로 굴러 대고는, 도빈 대위를 향해 두세 번 찌르기 동작을 날렸는데, 대위는 웃으며 자신의 대나무 지팡이로 그 찌르기를 막아 냈다."]], [["Mr. Stubble, as may be supposed from his size and slenderness, was of the Light Bobs.", "스터블 씨는, 그 체구와 호리호리함으로 짐작할 수 있듯 경보병 소속이었다."], ["Ensign Spooney, on the contrary, was a tall youth, and belonged to (Captain Dobbin's) the Grenadier Company, and he tried on a new bearskin cap, under which he looked savage beyond his years.", "반면 스푸니 소위는 키가 큰 젊은이로 (도빈 대위가 속한) 척탄병 중대 소속이었는데, 새로 받은 곰가죽 모자를 써 보니 나이답지 않게 사납게 보였다."], ["Then these two lads went off to the Slaughters', and having ordered a famous dinner, sate down and wrote off letters to the kind anxious parents at home--letters full of love and heartiness, and pluck and bad spelling.", "그러고는 이 두 젊은이는 슬로터스 식당으로 가서 근사한 만찬을 주문해 놓고 자리에 앉아, 고국의 다정하고 마음 졸이는 부모에게 편지를 써 내려갔다. 사랑과 진정과 패기, 그리고 엉터리 철자가 가득한 편지였다."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["there were many anxious hearts beating through England at that time; and mothers'", "그 무렵 영국 곳곳에서는 마음 졸이는 숱한 가슴이 두근거리고 있었다."], ["prayers and tears flowing in many homesteads.", "그리고 어머니들의 기도와 눈물이 숱한 가정에서 흘러내리고 있었다."]], [["Seeing young Stubble engaged in composition at one of the coffee-room tables at the Slaughters', and the tears trickling down his nose on to the paper (for the youngster was thinking of his mamma, and that he might never see her again), Dobbin, who was going to write off a letter to George Osborne, relented, and locked up his desk.", "슬로터스 식당의 다방 탁자 하나에서 어린 스터블이 글짓기에 골몰해 있고, 그 콧등을 타고 눈물이 종이 위로 뚝뚝 떨어지는 것을 보자(그 젊은이는 자기 엄마를, 그리고 어쩌면 다시는 엄마를 보지 못할지도 모른다는 것을 생각하고 있었다), 막 조지 오스본에게 편지를 써 보내려던 도빈은 마음이 누그러져 자기 책상을 도로 닫았다. \"내가 왜 굳이?\""], ["\"Why should I?\"", "그는 말했다."], ["said he. \"Let her have this night happy.", "\"그녀에게 오늘 밤만은 행복하게 보내게 하자."], ["I'll go and see my parents early in the morning, and go down to Brighton myself to-morrow.\"", "내일 아침 일찍 부모님을 찾아뵙고, 직접 브라이턴으로 내려가야겠다.\""]], [["So he went up and laid his big hand on young Stubble's shoulder, and backed up that young champion, and told him if he would leave off brandy and water he would be a good soldier, as he always was a gentlemanly good-hearted fellow.", "그래서 그는 다가가 어린 스터블의 어깨에 자기 큼직한 손을 얹고는, 그 젊은 용사를 격려해 주었다. 그리고 브랜디와 물을 끊기만 하면, 늘 그래 왔듯 신사답고 마음씨 고운 친구이니 훌륭한 군인이 될 거라고 일러 주었다."], ["Young Stubble's eyes brightened up at this, for Dobbin was greatly respected in the regiment, as the best officer and the cleverest man in it.", "이 말에 어린 스터블의 두 눈이 환해졌다. 도빈은 연대 안에서 가장 훌륭한 장교이자 가장 영리한 사람으로 크게 존경받고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["\"Thank you, Dobbin,\"", "\"고맙습니다, 도빈.\""], ["he said, rubbing his eyes with his knuckles, \"I was just--just telling her I would.", "그가 주먹으로 눈을 비비며 말했다. \"마침 그냥, 그냥 엄마한테 그러겠다고 쓰고 있던 참이에요."], ["And, O Sir, she's so dam kind to me.\"", "그리고, 아, 대위님, 엄마가 저한테 어찌나 끔찍이도 잘해 주시는지요.\""], ["The water pumps were at work again, and I am not sure that the soft-hearted Captain's eyes did not also twinkle.", "물 펌프가 다시 작동하기 시작했고, 마음 여린 대위의 두 눈에도 물기가 어렸던 게 아닐까 싶다."]], [["The two ensigns, the Captain, and Mr. Chopper, dined together in the same box.", "두 소위와 대위, 그리고 초퍼 씨는 같은 칸막이 자리에서 함께 저녁을 들었다."], ["Chopper brought the letter from Mr. Osborne, in which the latter briefly presented his compliments to Captain Dobbin, and requested him to forward the inclosed to Captain George Osborne.", "초퍼는 오스본 씨가 보낸 편지를 가져왔는데, 거기서 오스본 씨는 도빈 대위에게 짤막하게 인사를 전하며 동봉한 것을 조지 오스본 대위에게 전해 달라고 청하고 있었다."], ["Chopper knew nothing further; he described Mr.", "초퍼는 그 이상은 아무것도 몰랐다."], ["Osborne's appearance, it is true, and his interview with his lawyer, wondered how the governor had sworn at nobody, and--especially as the wine circled round--abounded in speculations and conjectures.", "사실 그는 오스본 씨의 모습이며 변호사와의 면담을 묘사하고, 주인 나리가 어째서 아무한테도 욕을 하지 않았는지 의아해하며, 특히 술이 돌아갈수록 온갖 추측과 억측을 늘어놓았다."], ["But these grew more vague with every glass, and at length became perfectly unintelligible.", "그러나 그 추측은 잔을 거듭할 때마다 점점 더 막연해지더니, 마침내는 도무지 알아들을 수 없는 지경이 되었다."], ["At a late hour Captain Dobbin put his guest into a hackney coach, in a hiccupping state, and swearing that he would be the kick--the kick--Captain's friend for ever and ever.", "늦은 시각에 도빈 대위는 자기 손님을, 딸꾹질을 해 대는 상태로 삯마차에 태워 보냈는데, 그는 영원무궁토록 그 끝내주는, 끝내주는 대위님의 벗이 되겠노라고 맹세하고 있었다."]], [["When Captain Dobbin took leave of Miss Osborne we have said that he asked leave to come and pay her another visit, and the spinster expected him for some hours the next day, when, perhaps, had he come, and had he asked her that question which she was prepared to answer, she would have declared herself as her brother's friend, and a reconciliation might have been effected between George and his angry father.", "앞서 말했듯, 도빈 대위는 오스본 양에게 작별을 고할 때 다시 한번 찾아뵙겠노라고 청을 넣었고, 그 노처녀는 이튿날 몇 시간이나 그를 기다렸다. 어쩌면 그가 찾아와, 그녀가 답할 준비를 해 두었던 그 질문을 던졌더라면, 그녀는 자신이 오빠의 편이라고 밝혔을 것이고, 그리하여 조지와 노여워하는 그 아버지 사이에 화해가 이루어졌을지도 모른다."], ["But though she waited at home the Captain never came.", "그러나 그녀가 집에서 기다렸음에도 대위는 끝내 오지 않았다."], ["He had his own affairs to pursue; his own parents to visit and console; and at an early hour of the day to take his place on the Lightning coach, and go down to his friends at Brighton.", "그에게는 좇아야 할 자기 일이 있었고, 찾아뵙고 위로해 드려야 할 부모가 있었으며, 그날 이른 시각에 라이트닝 마차에 자리를 잡고 브라이턴의 벗들에게로 내려가야 했다."], ["In the course of the day Miss Osborne heard her father give orders that that meddling scoundrel, Captain Dobbin, should never be admitted within his doors again, and any hopes in which she may have indulged privately were thus abruptly brought to an end. Mr.", "그날 하루가 지나는 동안 오스본 양은 아버지가, 그 참견 잘하는 악당 도빈 대위를 두 번 다시 자기 집 문 안에 들이지 말라고 명을 내리는 것을 들었고, 그리하여 그녀가 남몰래 품었을지도 모를 어떤 희망은 이렇게 돌연 끝장이 나고 말았다."], ["Frederick Bullock came, and was particularly affectionate to Maria, and attentive to the broken-spirited old gentleman.", "프레더릭 불럭 씨가 찾아와, 마리아에게 유난히 다정하게 굴고, 기가 꺾인 노신사에게 살뜰히 마음을 썼다."], ["For though he said his mind would be easy, the means which he had taken to secure quiet did not seem to have succeeded as yet, and the events of the past two days had visibly shattered him.", "비록 마음이 편안해지겠다고 말은 했으나, 그가 평온을 얻고자 취한 방편들은 아직 성공한 것 같지 않았고, 지난 이틀간의 일들은 눈에 띄게 그를 무너뜨려 놓았던 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXV", "# 제3장"]], [["In Which All the Principal Personages Think Fit to Leave Brighton\nConducted to the ladies, at the Ship Inn, Dobbin assumed a jovial and rattling manner, which proved that this young officer was becoming a more consummate hypocrite every day of his life.", "쉽 여관에 있는 숙녀들에게로 안내되어 간 도빈은 쾌활하고 수다스러운 태도를 꾸며 보였는데, 이는 이 젊은 장교가 날이 갈수록 더할 나위 없는 위선의 명수가 되어 가고 있음을 입증하는 것이었다."], ["He was trying to hide his own private feelings, first upon seeing Mrs. George Osborne in her new condition, and secondly to mask the apprehensions he entertained as to the effect which the dismal news brought down by him would certainly have upon her.", "그는 자신의 사사로운 감정을 감추려 애쓰고 있었으니, 첫째는 새로운 처지에 놓인 조지 오스본 부인을 보았을 때의 감정이었고, 둘째는 자신이 가지고 내려온 음울한 소식이 그녀에게 틀림없이 미칠 영향에 대한 우려를 감추려는 것이었다."]], [["\"It is my opinion, George,\"", "\"내 생각엔 말이야, 조지.\""], ["he said, \"that the French Emperor will be upon us, horse and foot, before three weeks are over, and will give the Duke such a dance as shall make the Peninsula appear mere child's play.", "그가 말했다. \"프랑스 황제가 삼 주 안에 기병이고 보병이고 죄다 끌고 우리한테 밀고 들어와서, 공작 각하한테 한바탕 호된 춤을 추게 해서 반도 전쟁쯤은 애들 장난처럼 보이게 만들 거라고."], ["But you need not say that to Mrs. Osborne, you know.", "하지만 오스본 부인한테는 그런 얘기 할 필요 없어, 알지."], ["There mayn't be any fighting on our side after all, and our business in Belgium may turn out to be a mere military occupation.", "따지고 보면 우리 쪽에선 전투가 전혀 없을 수도 있고, 벨기에에서 우리가 할 일이 그저 군사 점령에 그칠지도 모르니까."], ["Many persons think so; and Brussels is full of fine people and ladies of fashion.\"", "많은 사람들이 그렇게 생각해. 게다가 브뤼셀에는 멋진 사람들이며 유행을 좇는 숙녀들이 넘쳐 난다고.\""], ["So it was agreed to represent the duty of the British army in Belgium in this harmless light to Amelia.", "그리하여 벨기에에서 영국군이 맡은 임무를 애밀리아에게는 이처럼 무해한 빛깔로 그려 보이기로 합의가 이루어졌다."]], [["This plot being arranged, the hypocritical Dobbin saluted Mrs.", "이렇게 모의가 짜여진 뒤, 위선자 도빈은 조지 오스본 부인에게 사뭇 명랑하게 인사를 건네고, 신부라는 그녀의 새 처지에 관해 한두 마디 찬사를 건네려 애썼는데(고백하건대 그 찬사라는 것이 지독히 어설펐고 비참하리만치 어색하게 머뭇거렸다), 그러고는 브라이턴이며, 바닷바람이며, 그곳의 흥겨운 풍경이며, 길의 아름다움이며, 라이트닝 역마차와 말들의 장점에 대해 떠들기 시작했다."], ["George Osborne quite gaily, tried to pay her one or two compliments relative to her new position as a bride (which compliments, it must be confessed, were exceedingly clumsy and hung fire woefully), and then fell to talking about Brighton, and the sea-air, and the gaieties of the place, and the beauties of the road and the merits of the Lightning coach and horses--all in a manner quite incomprehensible to Amelia, and very amusing to Rebecca, who was watching the Captain, as indeed she watched every one near whom she came.", "그 모두가 애밀리아로서는 도무지 알아들을 수 없는 식이었으나, 도빈 대위를 지켜보고 있던 레베카에게는 무척이나 흥미로웠다. 사실 그녀는 자기 곁에 다가오는 사람이라면 누구든 지켜보았던 것이다."]], [["Little Amelia, it must be owned, had rather a mean opinion of her husband's friend, Captain Dobbin.", "고백하건대 어린 애밀리아는 남편의 친구인 도빈 대위를 다소 낮잡아 보고 있었다."], ["He lisped--he was very plain and homely-looking: and exceedingly awkward and ungainly.", "그는 혀짤배기소리를 냈고, 생김새가 매우 평범하고 볼품없었으며, 몹시 어색하고 굼떴다."], ["She liked him for his attachment to her husband (to be sure there was very little merit in that), and she thought George was most generous and kind in extending his friendship to his brother officer.", "그녀가 그를 좋아한 것은 남편을 향한 그의 충심 때문이었으나(물론 그 점에는 칭찬할 만한 구석이 거의 없었다), 동료 장교에게까지 우정을 베푸는 조지야말로 더없이 너그럽고 친절하다고 그녀는 생각했다."], ["George had mimicked Dobbin's lisp and queer manners many times to her, though to do him justice, he always spoke most highly of his friend's good qualities.", "조지는 도빈의 혀짤배기소리와 괴상한 거동을 그녀 앞에서 여러 번 흉내 내곤 했는데, 그를 위해 한마디 변호하자면, 친구의 훌륭한 자질에 대해서는 늘 더없이 높이 칭송하기는 했다."], ["In her little day of triumph, and not knowing him intimately as yet, she made light of honest William--and he knew her opinions of him quite well, and acquiesced in them very humbly.", "자신의 짧은 승리의 나날 속에서, 그리고 아직 그를 친밀하게 알지 못한 까닭에, 그녀는 정직한 윌리엄을 가볍게 여겼다. 그리고 그는 자신에 대한 그녀의 생각을 아주 잘 알면서도 더없이 겸허하게 거기에 따랐다."], ["A time came when she knew him better, and changed her notions regarding him; but that was distant as yet.", "훗날 그녀가 그를 더 잘 알게 되어 그에 대한 생각을 바꾸는 때가 오기는 했으나, 그것은 아직 먼 훗날의 일이었다."]], [["As for Rebecca, Captain Dobbin had not been two hours in the ladies' company before she understood his secret perfectly.", "레베카로 말할 것 같으면, 도빈 대위가 숙녀들과 함께한 지 두 시간도 채 되기 전에 그녀는 그의 비밀을 완벽하게 꿰뚫어 보았다."], ["She did not like him, and feared him privately; nor was he very much prepossessed in her favour.", "그녀는 그를 좋아하지 않았고 속으로 두려워했다. 그 또한 그녀에게 그다지 호감을 품지 않았다."], ["He was so honest, that her arts and cajoleries did not affect him, and he shrank from her with instinctive repulsion.", "그가 어찌나 정직한지 그녀의 술수와 아양이 그에게는 통하지 않았고, 그는 본능적인 거부감으로 그녀에게서 움츠러들었다."], ["And, as she was by no means so far superior to her sex as to be above jealousy, she disliked him the more for his adoration of Amelia.", "게다가 그녀 역시 시샘을 초월할 만큼 자기 성별을 한참 뛰어넘은 사람은 결코 아니었던지라, 그가 애밀리아를 흠모하는 것을 보고 더욱 그를 싫어했다."], ["Nevertheless, she was very respectful and cordial in her manner towards him.", "그럼에도 그녀는 그를 대하는 태도에서 매우 깍듯하고 다정했다."], ["A friend to the Osbornes!", "오스본 부부의 친구이지 않은가!"], ["a friend to her dearest benefactors!", "자기의 더없이 소중한 은인들의 친구이지 않은가!"], ["She vowed she should always love him sincerely: she remembered him quite well on the Vauxhall night, as she told Amelia archly, and she made a little fun of him when the two ladies went to dress for dinner.", "그녀는 언제까지나 그를 진심으로 사랑하겠노라 다짐했다. 그녀는 그를 복스홀에서 보냈던 그날 밤 또렷이 기억한다고 애밀리아에게 짓궂게 말했고, 두 숙녀가 저녁 식사 채비를 하러 들어갔을 때는 그를 두고 조금 우스갯소리를 했다."], ["Rawdon Crawley paid scarcely any attention to Dobbin, looking upon him as a good-natured nincompoop and under-bred City man.", "로던 크롤리는 도빈에게 거의 눈길도 주지 않았으니, 그를 마음씨 좋은 멍청이요 교양 없는 런던 장사치쯤으로 여겼던 것이다."], ["Jos patronised him with much dignity.", "조스는 자못 위엄을 부리며 그를 너그러이 대했다."]], [["When George and Dobbin were alone in the latter's room, to which George had followed him, Dobbin took from his desk the letter which he had been charged by Mr.", "조지와 도빈이, 조지가 따라 들어간 도빈의 방에 단둘이 있게 되자, 도빈은 오스본 씨에게서 그 아들에게 전하라는 부탁을 받은 편지를 책상에서 꺼냈다."], ["Osborne to deliver to his son.", "\"아버지 필체가 아닌데.\""], ["\"It's not in my father's handwriting,\"", "조지가 다소 놀란 기색으로 말했다. 과연 그러했다."], ["said George, looking rather alarmed; nor was it: the letter was from Mr. Osborne's lawyer, and to the following effect:", "그 편지는 오스본 씨의 변호사가 보낸 것으로, 내용은 다음과 같았다."]], [["\"Bedford Row, May 7, 1815.", "\"베드퍼드 로에서, 1815년 5월 7일."]], [["\"I am commissioned by Mr.", "귀하."], ["Osborne to inform you, that he abides by the determination which he before expressed to you, and that in consequence of the marriage which you have been pleased to contract, he ceases to consider you henceforth as a member of his family.", "본인은 오스본 씨로부터 위임을 받아 다음을 알려 드립니다. 그분께서는 이전에 귀하에게 표명하신 결심을 그대로 고수하시며, 귀하가 기어이 맺으신 혼인의 결과로 이후로는 귀하를 가족의 일원으로 여기지 않으십니다."], ["This determination is final and irrevocable.", "이 결심은 최종적이며 번복할 수 없습니다."]], [["\"Although the monies expended upon you in your minority, and the bills which you have drawn upon him so unsparingly of late years, far exceed in amount the sum to which you are entitled in your own right (being the third part of the fortune of your mother, the late Mrs. Osborne and which reverted to you at her decease, and to Miss Jane Osborne and Miss Maria Frances Osborne); yet I am instructed by Mr.", "귀하의 미성년기에 귀하에게 지출된 금액과, 근년에 귀하가 그분 앞으로 그토록 아낌없이 발행한 어음들은, 귀하가 응당 자기 몫으로 받을 권리가 있는 금액(작고하신 모친 오스본 부인의 재산 가운데 삼분의 일로, 부인이 별세하시면서 귀하와 제인 오스본 양, 마리아 프랜시스 오스본 양에게 돌아간 것입니다)을 그 액수에서 훨씬 웃돕니다."], ["Osborne to say, that he waives all claim upon your estate, and that the sum of 2,000 pounds, 4 per cent. annuities, at the value of the day (being your one-third share of the sum of 6,000 pounds), shall be paid over to yourself or your agents upon your receipt for the same, by", "그럼에도 본인은 오스본 씨의 지시에 따라, 그분께서 귀하의 상속분에 대한 모든 청구권을 포기하시며, 당일 시세로 환산한 사 푼 이자의 연금 이천 파운드(육천 파운드 가운데 귀하 몫인 삼분의 일에 해당합니다)를, 귀하가 수령증을 제출하시는 대로 귀하 본인 또는 귀하의 대리인에게 지불하리라는 것을 전합니다."]], [["\"Your obedient Servt., \"S.", "귀하의 충실한 종복, S."], ["HIGGS.", "힉스 올림."]], [["\"P.S.--Mr.", "추신."], ["Osborne desires me to say, once for all, that he declines to receive any messages, letters, or communications from you on this or any other subject.", "오스본 씨께서는 이번 일이든 다른 어떤 일이든, 귀하로부터 어떠한 전갈이나 편지나 연락도 받기를 거부하신다는 것을 단번에 분명히 밝히라 하셨습니다.\""]], [["\"A pretty way you have managed the affair,\"", "\"네가 일을 참 멋들어지게도 처리해 놨구나.\""], ["said George, looking savagely at William Dobbin.", "조지가 윌리엄 도빈을 사납게 노려보며 말했다."], ["\"Look there, Dobbin,\"", "\"이것 봐, 도빈.\""], ["and he flung over to the latter his parent's letter.", "그러고는 부친의 편지를 그에게 휙 던졌다."], ["\"A beggar, by Jove, and all in consequence of my d--d sentimentality.", "\"거지가 됐어, 맙소사, 그게 다 빌어먹을 내 감상벽 때문이라고."], ["Why couldn't we have waited?", "왜 우리가 기다릴 수 없었던 거지?"], ["A ball might have done for me in the course of the war, and may still, and how will Emmy be bettered by being left a beggar's widow?", "전쟁 중에 총알 한 방이면 내 목숨이 끝장날 수도 있고, 지금도 여전히 그럴 수 있는데, 에미가 거지의 미망인으로 남는다고 무슨 형편이 나아진단 말이야?"], ["It was all your doing.", "다 네가 한 짓이야."], ["You were never easy until you had got me married and ruined.", "너는 나를 결혼시키고 파멸시켜 놓기 전엔 도무지 마음을 못 놓았지."], ["What the deuce am I to do with two thousand pounds?", "이천 파운드 가지고 대체 나더러 어쩌라는 거냐?"], ["Such a sum won't last two years.", "그만한 돈은 이 년도 못 버텨."], ["I've lost a hundred and forty to Crawley at cards and billiards since I've been down here.", "여기 내려와서 카드며 당구로 크롤리한테 백사십 파운드나 잃었다고."], ["A pretty manager of a man's matters YOU are, forsooth.\"", "정말이지 너야말로 남의 살림을 잘도 꾸려 주는 사람이로구나.\""]], [["\"There's no denying that the position is a hard one,\"", "\"이 처지가 고된 것이야 부인할 수 없지.\""], ["Dobbin replied, after reading over the letter with a blank countenance; \"and as you say, it is partly of my making.", "멍한 표정으로 편지를 다 읽고 나서 도빈이 대답했다. \"그리고 네 말대로, 절반은 내 탓이고."], ["There are some men who wouldn't mind changing with you,\"", "하지만 너랑 처지를 바꾸고 싶어 할 사람도 더러 있을걸.\""], ["he added, with a bitter smile.", "그가 씁쓸한 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다."], ["\"How many captains in the regiment have two thousand pounds to the fore, think you?", "\"연대에서 이천 파운드를 쥐고 있는 대위가 몇이나 될 것 같아?"], ["You must live on your pay till your father relents, and if you die, you leave your wife a hundred a year.\"", "아버님 노여움이 풀리실 때까진 봉급으로 살아야지. 그리고 만약 네가 죽으면, 아내한테 연 백 파운드는 남겨 줄 수 있고.\""]], [["\"Do you suppose a man of my habits can live on his pay and a hundred a year?\"", "\"나 같은 생활 습관을 가진 사람이 봉급에 연 백 파운드로 살 수 있을 거라고 생각하는 거냐?\""], ["George cried out in great anger.", "조지가 잔뜩 성을 내며 소리쳤다."], ["\"You must be a fool to talk so, Dobbin.", "\"그런 소릴 하다니 넌 바보가 틀림없어, 도빈."], ["How the deuce am I to keep up my position in the world upon such a pitiful pittance?", "그 쥐꼬리만 한 푼돈으로 대체 내가 세상에서 내 지위를 어떻게 지킨단 말이야?"], ["I can't change my habits.", "난 습관을 바꿀 수 없어."], ["I must have my comforts.", "누릴 건 누려야 한다고."], ["I wasn't brought up on porridge, like MacWhirter, or on potatoes, like old O'Dowd.", "난 맥워터처럼 죽으로 자란 것도 아니고, 늙은 오다우드처럼 감자로 자란 것도 아니야."], ["Do you expect my wife to take in soldiers' washing, or ride after the regiment in a baggage waggon?\"", "내 아내더러 병사들 빨래를 받아서 하거나, 짐마차에 실려 연대를 따라다니라는 거냐?\""]], [["\"Well, well,\"", "\"자, 자.\""], ["said Dobbin, still good-naturedly, \"we'll get her a better conveyance.", "도빈이 여전히 너그럽게 말했다. \"부인한테는 더 나은 탈것을 마련해 드리자."], ["But try and remember that you are only a dethroned prince now, George, my boy; and be quiet whilst the tempest lasts.", "다만 너는 이제 폐위된 왕자에 불과하다는 걸 잊지 말려무나, 친구야. 그리고 폭풍이 몰아치는 동안에는 가만히 있어."], ["It won't be for long.", "오래가진 않을 테니."], ["Let your name be mentioned in the Gazette, and I'll engage the old father relents towards you:\"", "네 이름이 관보에 한번 오르기만 하면, 노아버님이 너한테 마음을 푸시리라고 내가 장담하지.\""]], [["\"Mentioned in the Gazette!\"", "\"관보에 오른다고!\""], ["George answered.", "조지가 대꾸했다."], ["\"And in what part of it?", "\"그것도 어느 난에 말이야?"], ["Among the killed and wounded returns, and at the top of the list, very likely.\"", "전사자며 부상자 명단에, 그것도 십중팔구 맨 윗줄에 오르겠지.\""]], [["\"Psha!", "\"체!"], ["It will be time enough to cry out when we are hurt,\"", "다치고 나서 비명을 질러도 늦지 않아.\""], ["Dobbin said.", "도빈이 말했다."], ["\"And if anything happens, you know, George, I have got a little, and I am not a marrying man, and I shall not forget my godson in my will,\"", "\"그리고 무슨 일이 생기면 말이지, 조지, 내가 가진 게 좀 있고, 난 결혼할 사람도 아니니, 유언장에서 내 대자를 잊지 않으마.\""], ["he added, with a smile.", "그가 미소 지으며 덧붙였다."], ["Whereupon the dispute ended--as many scores of such conversations between Osborne and his friend had concluded previously--by the former declaring there was no possibility of being angry with Dobbin long, and forgiving him very generously after abusing him without cause.", "이로써 다툼은 끝이 났으니, 오스본과 그 친구 사이에서 이런 대화가 수십 번이나 이전에도 그러했듯, 전자가 도빈에게 오래 화를 낼 도리가 없다고 선언하며, 까닭 없이 욕을 퍼붓고는 더없이 너그럽게 그를 용서하는 것으로 매듭지어진 것이다."]], [["\"I say, Becky,\"", "\"이봐, 베키.\""], ["cried Rawdon Crawley out of his dressing-room, to his lady, who was attiring herself for dinner in her own chamber.", "로던 크롤리가 자기 화장방에서 아내를 향해 소리쳤다. 아내는 제 방에서 저녁 식사를 위해 단장하는 중이었다."]], [["\"What?\"", "\"왜요?\""], ["said Becky's shrill voice.", "베키의 날카로운 목소리가 들렸다."], ["She was looking over her shoulder in the glass.", "그녀는 거울 속으로 어깨너머를 살펴보고 있었다."], ["She had put on the neatest and freshest white frock imaginable, and with bare shoulders and a little necklace, and a light blue sash, she looked the image of youthful innocence and girlish happiness.", "상상할 수 있는 가장 정갈하고 산뜻한 흰 드레스를 차려입고, 드러난 어깨에 자그마한 목걸이를 두르고, 연푸른 띠를 맨 그녀는 그야말로 젊디젊은 순진무구함과 소녀다운 행복의 화신처럼 보였다."]], [["\"I say, what'll Mrs. O. do, when O. goes out with the regiment?\" Crawley said coming into the room, performing a duet on his head with two huge hair-brushes, and looking out from under his hair with admiration on his pretty little wife.", "\"이봐, 오스본 부인은 어쩐대, 오스본이 연대 따라 출정하고 나면?\" 크롤리가 커다란 머리빗 두 개로 자기 머리에 이중주를 연주하면서, 머리카락 사이로 어여쁜 어린 아내를 흠모하듯 내다보며 방으로 들어와 말했다."]], [["\"I suppose she'll cry her eyes out,\"", "\"눈이 빠지도록 울겠죠 뭐.\""], ["Becky answered.", "베키가 대답했다."], ["\"She has been whimpering half a dozen times, at the very notion of it, already to me.\"", "\"벌써 그 생각만으로도 나한테 대여섯 번은 훌쩍거렸는걸요.\""]], [["\"YOU don't care, I suppose?\"", "\"당신은 신경도 안 쓰나 보군, 아마.\""], ["Rawdon said, half angry at his wife's want of feeling.", "로던이 아내의 무정함에 반쯤 성이 나서 말했다."]], [["\"You wretch!", "\"이 못된 양반!"], ["don't you know that I intend to go with you,\"", "내가 당신을 따라갈 작정인 걸 모르세요?\""], ["Becky replied.", "베키가 대꾸했다."], ["\"Besides, you're different.", "\"게다가 당신은 경우가 다르잖아요."], ["You go as General Tufto's aide-de-camp.", "당신은 터프토 장군의 부관으로 가는 거고."], ["We don't belong to the line,\" Mrs.", "우린 전열 부대 소속이 아니잖아요.\""], ["Crawley said, throwing up her head with an air that so enchanted her husband that he stooped down and kissed it.", "크롤리 부인이 그렇게 말하며 고개를 한껏 치켜드니, 그 모습에 남편이 어찌나 황홀해졌던지 몸을 숙여 그 머리에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["\"Rawdon dear--don't you think--you'd better get that--money from Cupid, before he goes?\"", "\"로던, 여보, 그 돈 말이에요, 큐피드한테서, 그가 떠나기 전에, 받아 두는 게 낫지 않겠어요?\""], ["Becky continued, fixing on a killing bow.", "베키가 사람 잡는 매듭리본을 매만지며 말을 이었다."], ["She called George Osborne, Cupid.", "그녀는 조지 오스본을 큐피드라고 불렀다."], ["She had flattered him about his good looks a score of times already.", "그녀는 벌써 스무 번도 넘게 그의 잘생긴 외모를 추켜세웠던 것이다."], ["She watched over him kindly at ecarte of a night when he would drop in to Rawdon's quarters for a half-hour before bed-time.", "그가 밤마다 잠자리에 들기 전 삼십 분쯤 로던의 처소에 들르면, 그녀는 에카르테 놀이를 하는 그를 다정하게 살펴 주곤 했다."]], [["She had often called him a horrid dissipated wretch, and threatened to tell Emmy of his wicked ways and naughty extravagant habits.", "그녀는 그를 끔찍하게 방탕한 못된 사람이라고 곧잘 불렀고, 그의 못된 행실과 짓궂은 낭비벽을 에미에게 일러바치겠다고 으름장을 놓곤 했다."], ["She brought his cigar and lighted it for him; she knew the effect of that manoeuvre, having practised it in former days upon Rawdon Crawley.", "그녀는 그의 시가를 가져다 불을 붙여 주었다. 그녀는 그 수법의 효력을 알고 있었으니, 지난날 로던 크롤리에게 써 본 적이 있었기 때문이다."], ["He thought her gay, brisk, arch, distinguee, delightful.", "그는 그녀가 명랑하고, 발랄하고, 짓궂고, 기품 있고, 즐거운 사람이라고 생각했다."], ["In their little drives and dinners, Becky, of course, quite outshone poor Emmy, who remained very mute and timid while Mrs.", "함께한 자그마한 드라이브며 만찬에서 베키는 물론 가엾은 에미를 한참 무색하게 만들었다."], ["Crawley and her husband rattled away together, and Captain Crawley (and Jos after he joined the young married people) gobbled in silence.", "크롤리 부인과 그 남편이 함께 떠들썩하게 수다를 떠는 동안 에미는 매우 말이 없고 주눅 든 채로 있었고, 크롤리 대위는(그리고 이 신혼부부와 합류한 뒤로는 조스도) 말없이 게걸스레 먹어 댔다."]], [["Emmy's mind somehow misgave her about her friend.", "에미는 어쩐 일인지 친구에 대해 마음이 자꾸 불안했다."], ["Rebecca's wit, spirits, and accomplishments troubled her with a rueful disquiet.", "레베카의 재치며 활기며 재주가 그녀를 서글픈 불안으로 어지럽혔다."], ["They were only a week married, and here was George already suffering ennui, and eager for others'", "결혼한 지 겨우 일주일인데, 벌써 조지는 권태로워하며 다른 이들과 어울리기를 갈망하고 있지 않은가! 그녀는 앞날이 두려워 떨었다."], ["society! She trembled for the future.", "어떻게 하면 내가 저이의 길동무가 될 수 있을까, 그녀는 생각했다."], ["How shall I be a companion for him, she thought--so clever and so brilliant, and I such a humble foolish creature?", "저토록 영리하고 빛나는 사람인데, 나는 이토록 보잘것없고 어리석은 존재이니. 나와 결혼해 주다니, 모든 걸 버리고 내게로 몸을 굽혀 주다니, 저이는 얼마나 고귀한가!"], ["How noble it was of him to marry me--to give up everything and stoop down to me!", "나는 저이를 거절했어야 했어, 다만 그럴 용기가 없었을 뿐. 집에 남아 가엾은 아버지를 돌봤어야 했어."], ["I ought to have refused him, only I had not the heart.", "그리고 부모를 소홀히 한 일이(사실 이 가엾은 아이의 불안한 양심이 스스로에게 들이댄 그 자책에는 어느 정도 근거가 있었다) 이제야 처음으로 떠올라, 그녀는 부끄러움에 얼굴을 붉혔다. 아아!"], ["I ought to have stopped at home and taken care of poor Papa.", "그녀는 생각했다. 나는 참으로 못되고 이기적이었어."], ["And her neglect of her parents (and indeed there was some foundation for this charge which the poor child's uneasy conscience brought against her) was now remembered for the first time, and caused her to blush with humiliation.", "부모님이 슬픔에 잠겨 계실 때 잊고 있었으니 이기적이었고, 조지더러 억지로 나와 결혼하게 했으니 이기적이었어."], ["Oh! thought she, I have been very wicked and selfish--selfish in forgetting them in their sorrows--selfish in forcing George to marry me.", "나는 저이에게 걸맞은 사람이 아니야."], ["I know I'm not worthy of him--I know he would have been happy without me--and yet--I tried, I tried to give him up.", "나 없이도 저이는 행복했으리란 걸 알아. 그런데도, 그래도, 나는 애썼어, 저이를 단념하려고 애썼어."]], [["It is hard when, before seven days of marriage are over, such thoughts and confessions as these force themselves on a little bride's mind.", "결혼한 지 이레도 채 지나기 전에 이런 생각과 고백이 어린 신부의 마음속으로 밀려든다는 것은 가혹한 일이다. 그러나 사정은 그러했다."], ["But so it was, and the night before Dobbin came to join these young people--on a fine brilliant moonlight night of May--so warm and balmy that the windows were flung open to the balcony, from which George and Mrs.", "그리고 도빈이 이 젊은이들과 합류하러 오기 전날 밤, 오월의 어느 환하게 빛나는 달밤이었다."], ["Crawley were gazing upon the calm ocean spread shining before them, while Rawdon and Jos were engaged at backgammon within--Amelia couched in a great chair quite neglected, and watching both these parties, felt a despair and remorse such as were bitter companions for that tender lonely soul.", "어찌나 따스하고 부드러운 밤이었던지 발코니로 난 창문을 활짝 열어 두었고, 거기서 조지와 크롤리 부인이 눈앞에 반짝이며 펼쳐진 고요한 대양을 바라보고 있었으며, 그러는 동안 로던과 조스는 안에서 주사위놀이에 빠져 있었다. 애밀리아는 큼직한 안락의자에 완전히 외면당한 채 파묻혀, 이 두 무리를 모두 지켜보며, 그 여리고 외로운 영혼에게는 쓰디쓴 길동무인 절망과 회한을 느꼈다."], ["Scarce a week was past, and it was come to this!", "채 일주일도 지나지 않았는데, 일이 이 지경에 이른 것이다!"], ["The future, had she regarded it, offered a dismal prospect; but Emmy was too shy, so to speak, to look to that, and embark alone on that wide sea, and unfit to navigate it without a guide and protector.", "앞날은, 만약 그녀가 그것을 들여다보았다면, 음울한 전망을 펼쳐 보였을 것이다. 그러나 에미는, 말하자면 너무도 수줍은 나머지 그 앞날을 바라보고 홀로 그 망망대해로 배를 띄울 엄두를 내지 못했고, 길잡이와 보호자 없이 그 바다를 항해할 만한 사람도 못 되었다."], ["I know Miss Smith has a mean opinion of her.", "스미스 양이 그녀를 낮잡아 본다는 것을 나는 안다."], ["But how many, my dear Madam, are endowed with your prodigious strength of mind?", "그러나 친애하는 부인, 그대처럼 어마어마한 정신력을 타고난 이가 과연 몇이나 되겠는가?"]], [["\"Gad, what a fine night, and how bright the moon is!\"", "\"이런, 정말 멋진 밤이군, 달도 참 밝고!\""], ["George said, with a puff of his cigar, which went soaring up skywards.", "조지가 시가를 한 모금 빨며 말하니, 연기가 하늘을 향해 솟구쳐 올랐다."]], [["\"How delicious they smell in the open air! I adore them.", "\"탁 트인 데서 피우니 냄새가 어찌나 향긋한지!"], ["Who'd think the moon was two hundred and thirty-six thousand eight hundred and forty-seven miles off?\"", "난 시가가 너무 좋아요. 달이 이십삼만 육천팔백사십칠 마일이나 떨어져 있다고 누가 생각이나 하겠어요?\""], ["Becky added, gazing at that orb with a smile.", "베키가 그 천체를 미소 띤 얼굴로 올려다보며 덧붙였다."], ["\"Isn't it clever of me to remember that?", "\"내가 그걸 다 기억하다니 영리하지 않아요?"], ["Pooh!", "흥!"], ["we learned it all at Miss Pinkerton's!", "그것도 다 핑커튼 여사님 학원에서 배운 거예요!"], ["How calm the sea is, and how clear everything.", "바다가 어쩌면 이렇게 잔잔하고, 모든 게 이리도 또렷할까."], ["I declare I can almost see the coast of France!\"", "정말이지 프랑스 해안이 거의 다 보일 것 같아!\""], ["and her bright green eyes streamed out, and shot into the night as if they could see through it.", "그러면서 그녀의 밝은 초록빛 눈이 흘러넘치듯 어둠 속으로 쏘아져, 마치 어둠을 꿰뚫어 보기라도 할 듯했다."]], [["\"Do you know what I intend to do one morning?\"", "\"내가 어느 날 아침에 무슨 일을 할 작정인지 아세요?\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said; \"I find I can swim beautifully, and some day, when my Aunt Crawley's companion--old Briggs, you know--you remember her--that hook-nosed woman, with the long wisps of hair--when Briggs goes out to bathe, I intend to dive under her awning, and insist on a reconciliation in the water.", "\"내가 수영을 기막히게 잘한다는 걸 알았거든요. 그래서 언젠가, 크롤리 고모님의 말동무가, 그러니까 늙은 브리그스 말이에요, 당신도 기억하죠, 그 매부리코에 머리카락을 길게 늘어뜨린 여자요, 그 브리그스가 멱을 감으러 나오면, 내가 그 차일 밑으로 잠수해 들어가서 물속에서 기어이 화해를 하자고 우길 작정이에요."], ["Isn't that a stratagem?\"", "멋진 계략 아니에요?\""]], [["George burst out laughing at the idea of this aquatic meeting.", "조지는 이 물속 만남을 상상하며 폭소를 터뜨렸다."], ["\"What's the row there, you two?\"", "\"거기 둘이서 무슨 소동이야?\""], ["Rawdon shouted out, rattling the box.", "로던이 주사위통을 달그락거리며 외쳤다."], ["Amelia was making a fool of herself in an absurd hysterical manner, and retired to her own room to whimper in private.", "애밀리아는 어처구니없게도 발작적으로 흐느끼며 바보 같은 꼴을 하고는, 남몰래 훌쩍이려고 제 방으로 물러갔다."]], [["Our history is destined in this chapter to go backwards and forwards in a very irresolute manner seemingly, and having conducted our story to to-morrow presently, we shall immediately again have occasion to step back to yesterday, so that the whole of the tale may get a hearing.", "이 장에서 우리의 이야기는 겉보기에 매우 우유부단한 방식으로 앞으로 갔다 뒤로 갔다 할 운명이니, 이제 곧 이야기를 내일로 끌고 갔다가도 곧장 다시 어제로 되돌아가야 할 일이 생길 것이고, 그리하여 이야기 전체가 빠짐없이 귀에 들어오게 될 것이다."], ["As you behold at her Majesty's drawing-room, the ambassadors' and high dignitaries'", "여왕 폐하의 알현실에서 보다시피, 대사들과 고관대작들의 마차는 전용 문으로 휙휙 빠져나가는데, 그동안 존스 대위의 부인들은 자기네 삯마차를 기다리고 있다."], ["carriages whisk off from a private door, while Captain Jones's ladies are waiting for their fly: as you see in the Secretary of the Treasury's antechamber, a half-dozen of petitioners waiting patiently for their audience, and called out one by one, when suddenly an Irish member or some eminent personage enters the apartment, and instantly walks into Mr.", "또 재무성 장관의 대기실에서 보다시피, 대여섯 명의 청원자들이 알현을 기다리며 끈기 있게 앉아 한 명씩 호명되는데, 그때 느닷없이 어느 아일랜드 의원이나 어느 저명한 인사가 방에 들어와 곧장 그 자리에 있는 모든 이들의 머리 위로 차관 나리에게로 곧장 걸어 들어가 버린다."], ["Under-Secretary over the heads of all the people present: so in the conduct of a tale, the romancer is obliged to exercise this most partial sort of justice.", "이야기를 이끌어 나가는 데서도 이와 마찬가지로, 소설가는 이런 더없이 편파적인 종류의 정의를 행사하지 않을 수 없다."], ["Although all the little incidents must be heard, yet they must be put off when the great events make their appearance; and surely such a circumstance as that which brought Dobbin to Brighton, viz., the ordering out of the Guards and the line to Belgium, and the mustering of the allied armies in that country under the command of his Grace the Duke of Wellington--such a dignified circumstance as that, I say, was entitled to the pas over all minor occurrences whereof this history is composed mainly, and hence a little trifling disarrangement and disorder was excusable and becoming.", "온갖 사소한 사건들도 모두 들려주어야 마땅하지만, 그래도 위대한 사건들이 등장할 때는 뒤로 미루어 두어야 한다. 그리고 도빈을 브라이턴으로 데려온 정황, 즉 근위대와 전열 부대의 벨기에 출동 명령, 그리고 그 나라에서 웰링턴 공작 각하의 지휘 아래 연합군이 집결한 일, 분명히 말하건대 그처럼 위엄 있는 정황이야말로, 이 이야기를 주로 이루는 온갖 사소한 사건들보다 앞자리를 차지할 자격이 있는 것이며, 따라서 약간의 사소한 뒤죽박죽과 무질서쯤은 너그러이 보아 넘길 만하고 또 마땅한 일이었다."], ["We have only now advanced in time so far beyond Chapter XXII as to have got our various characters up into their dressing-rooms before the dinner, which took place as usual on the day of Dobbin's arrival.", "우리는 지금 겨우 제22장보다 시간을 조금 앞질러 왔을 뿐이니, 도빈이 도착한 그날 여느 때처럼 차려진 저녁 식사를 앞두고 여러 등장인물들을 저마다의 화장방으로 들여보낸 참이다."]], [["George was too humane or too much occupied with the tie of his neckcloth to convey at once all the news to Amelia which his comrade had brought with him from London.", "조지는 너무 인정이 많았던지, 아니면 넥타이 매듭에 너무 정신이 팔려 있었던지, 동료가 런던에서 가지고 온 소식을 한꺼번에 애밀리아에게 전하지 못했다."], ["He came into her room, however, holding the attorney's letter in his hand, and with so solemn and important an air that his wife, always ingeniously on the watch for calamity, thought the worst was about to befall, and running up to her husband, besought her dearest George to tell her everything--he was ordered abroad; there would be a battle next week--she knew there would.", "그러나 그는 변호사의 편지를 손에 들고, 어찌나 엄숙하고 비장한 기색이었던지, 늘 재앙을 기민하게 살피고 있던 그의 아내는 최악의 사태가 닥치려는 줄로 여기고, 남편에게 달려가 더없이 소중한 조지에게 모든 걸 말해 달라고 애원했다. 저이가 해외로 출정 명령을 받았구나, 다음 주에 전투가 있겠구나, 그녀는 분명 그러리라 여겼다."]], [["Dearest George parried the question about foreign service, and with a melancholy shake of the head said, \"No, Emmy; it isn't that: it's not myself I care about: it's you.", "더없이 소중한 조지는 해외 출정에 관한 물음을 슬쩍 받아넘기고는, 우울하게 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"아니, 에미, 그게 아니야."], ["I have had bad news from my father.", "나 자신이 걱정되는 게 아니라 당신이 걱정되는 거야."], ["He refuses any communication with me; he has flung us off; and leaves us to poverty.", "아버지한테서 나쁜 소식이 왔어. 아버지가 나와 어떤 연락도 거부하셔."], ["I can rough it well enough; but you, my dear, how will you bear it?", "우리를 내치고, 가난 속에 내버려 두셨어. 나야 그럭저럭 견딜 수 있지만, 당신은, 여보, 그걸 어떻게 견디겠어?"], ["read here.\"", "여기 읽어 봐.\""], ["And he handed her over the letter.", "그러고는 그녀에게 편지를 건넸다."]], [["Amelia, with a look of tender alarm in her eyes, listened to her noble hero as he uttered the above generous sentiments, and sitting down on the bed, read the letter which George gave her with such a pompous martyr-like air.", "애밀리아는 다정한 놀라움이 어린 눈빛으로, 위와 같은 너그러운 심정을 토로하는 자기의 고귀한 영웅의 말에 귀를 기울이고는, 침대에 걸터앉아 조지가 그토록 거창하고 순교자 같은 기색으로 건넨 편지를 읽었다."], ["Her face cleared up as she read the document, however.", "그러나 그녀의 얼굴은 그 문서를 읽어 내려가는 동안 도리어 환해졌다."], ["The idea of sharing poverty and privation in company with the beloved object is, as we have before said, far from being disagreeable to a warm-hearted woman.", "사랑하는 사람과 함께 가난과 궁핍을 나눈다는 생각은, 앞서도 말했듯이, 따뜻한 마음을 지닌 여인에게는 결코 싫은 일이 아닌 것이다."], ["The notion was actually pleasant to little Amelia.", "그 생각은 어린 애밀리아에게 실로 즐거운 것이었다."], ["Then, as usual, she was ashamed of herself for feeling happy at such an indecorous moment, and checked her pleasure, saying demurely, \"O, George, how your poor heart must bleed at the idea of being separated from your papa!\"", "그러다가 그녀는 여느 때처럼, 이런 부적절한 순간에 행복을 느낀 자신이 부끄러워져, 기쁨을 억누르며 새침하게 말했다. \"오, 조지, 아버님과 헤어진다는 생각에 당신의 가엾은 가슴이 얼마나 미어지겠어요!\""]], [["\"It does,\"", "\"그래, 미어진다오.\""], ["said George, with an agonised countenance.", "조지가 고뇌에 찬 표정으로 말했다."]], [["\"But he can't be angry with you long,\"", "\"하지만 아버님도 오래 화내실 순 없을 거예요.\""], ["she continued.", "그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["\"Nobody could, I'm sure.", "\"누구라도 그럴 순 없을 거예요, 분명히."], ["He must forgive you, my dearest, kindest husband.", "아버님은 당신을 용서하실 거예요, 더없이 소중하고 더없이 다정한 내 남편이여."], ["O, I shall never forgive myself if he does not.\"", "오, 만약 그러지 않으신다면 난 결코 나 자신을 용서할 수 없을 거예요.\""]], [["\"What vexes me, my poor Emmy, is not my misfortune, but yours,\"", "\"내 가엾은 에미, 나를 괴롭히는 건 내 불행이 아니라 당신의 불행이야.\""], ["George said.", "조지가 말했다."], ["\"I don't care for a little poverty; and I think, without vanity, I've talents enough to make my own way.\"", "\"나야 약간의 가난쯤은 개의치 않아. 그리고 자만이 아니라, 내겐 스스로 길을 헤쳐 나갈 만한 재능이 충분하다고 생각해.\""]], [["\"That you have,\"", "\"맞아요, 당신은 그래요.\""], ["interposed his wife, who thought that war should cease, and her husband should be made a general instantly.", "아내가 끼어들었다. 그녀는 전쟁이 당장 멈추고 남편이 즉시 장군이 되어야 마땅하다고 생각했다."]], [["\"Yes, I shall make my way as well as another,\"", "\"그래, 나도 남들 못지않게 잘 헤쳐 나갈 거야.\""], ["Osborne went on; \"but you, my dear girl, how can I bear your being deprived of the comforts and station in society which my wife had a right to expect?", "오스본이 말을 이었다. \"하지만 당신은, 사랑스러운 그대여, 내 아내라면 당연히 누려야 할 안락함과 사회적 지위를 빼앗긴다니, 내가 그걸 어떻게 견딘단 말이야?"], ["My dearest girl in barracks; the wife of a soldier in a marching regiment; subject to all sorts of annoyance and privation!", "더없이 소중한 내 사람이 병영에 살고, 행군하는 연대 군인의 아내가 되어, 온갖 성가심과 궁핍에 시달리다니!"], ["It makes me miserable.\"", "그 생각만 하면 비참해져.\""]], [["Emmy, quite at ease, as this was her husband's only cause of disquiet, took his hand, and with a radiant face and smile began to warble that stanza from the favourite song of \"Wapping Old Stairs,\"", "에미는, 이것이 남편의 유일한 근심거리였던지라 아주 마음이 놓여, 그의 손을 잡고 환한 얼굴로 미소 지으며 즐겨 부르던 노래 「와핑 올드 스테어스」의 한 소절을 흥얼거리기 시작했다."], ["in which the heroine, after rebuking her Tom for inattention, promises \"his trousers to mend, and his grog too to make,\" if he will be constant and kind, and not forsake her.", "그 노래에서 여주인공은 무심하다고 톰을 나무란 끝에, 그가 변함없이 다정하게 자기를 저버리지만 않는다면 \"그의 바지를 기워 주고, 그의 그로그주도 타 주겠노라\"고 약속한다."], ["\"Besides,\"", "\"게다가,\""], ["she said, after a pause, during which she looked as pretty and happy as any young woman need, \"isn't two thousand pounds an immense deal of money, George?\"", "그녀가 잠시 말을 멈춘 사이, 어느 젊은 여인 못지않게 어여쁘고 행복해 보이는 얼굴로 말했다. \"이천 파운드면 어마어마하게 큰돈 아니에요, 조지?\""]], [["George laughed at her naivete; and finally they went down to dinner, Amelia clinging to George's arm, still warbling the tune of \"Wapping Old Stairs,\"", "조지는 그녀의 순진함에 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["and more pleased and light of mind than she had been for some days past.", "그리하여 마침내 두 사람은 저녁을 먹으러 아래층으로 내려갔는데, 애밀리아는 여전히 「와핑 올드 스테어스」의 가락을 흥얼거리며 조지의 팔에 매달려, 지난 며칠 동안보다 한결 기쁘고 홀가분한 마음이었다."]], [["Thus the repast, which at length came off, instead of being dismal, was an exceedingly brisk and merry one.", "그리하여 마침내 차려진 식사는, 음울하기는커녕 더없이 활기차고 흥겨운 자리가 되었다."], ["The excitement of the campaign counteracted in George's mind the depression occasioned by the disinheriting letter.", "출정의 흥분이 조지의 마음속에서 상속권 박탈 편지가 불러온 침울함을 상쇄했던 것이다."], ["Dobbin still kept up his character of rattle.", "도빈은 여전히 수다스러운 역할을 이어 갔다."], ["He amused the company with accounts of the army in Belgium; where nothing but fetes and gaiety and fashion were going on.", "그는 벨기에 주둔군에 관한 이야기로 좌중을 즐겁게 했는데, 그곳에서는 잔치며 흥겨움이며 유행이 끊일 새 없이 펼쳐지고 있다고 했다."], ["Then, having a particular end in view, this dexterous captain proceeded to describe Mrs.", "그러고는 한 가지 특정한 목적을 염두에 두고, 이 능란한 대위는 오다우드 소령 부인이 자기 옷가지와 소령의 옷가지를 꾸리던 모습을 묘사해 보였다."], ["Major O'Dowd packing her own and her Major's wardrobe, and how his best epaulets had been stowed into a tea canister, whilst her own famous yellow turban, with the bird of paradise wrapped in brown paper, was locked up in the Major's tin cocked-hat case, and wondered what effect it would have at the French king's court at Ghent, or the great military balls at Brussels.", "소령의 가장 좋은 견장이 차통에 처박힌 사연이며, 갈색 종이에 싼 극락조 깃털을 단 부인의 그 유명한 노란 터번이 소령의 양철 삼각모 상자 속에 들어가 잠겨 있던 일을 이야기하면서, 그것이 헨트에 있는 프랑스 왕의 궁정이나 브뤼셀의 성대한 군대 무도회에서 과연 어떤 효과를 낼지 궁금하다고 했다."]], [["\"Ghent!", "\"헨트라니!"], ["Brussels!\"", "브뤼셀이라니!\""], ["cried out Amelia with a sudden shock and start.", "애밀리아가 별안간 충격을 받고 흠칫 놀라며 소리쳤다."], ["\"Is the regiment ordered away, George--is it ordered away?\"", "\"연대가 출동 명령을 받았어요, 조지, 출동 명령을 받은 거예요?\""], ["A look of terror came over the sweet smiling face, and she clung to George as by an instinct.", "그 다정하고 미소 짓던 얼굴에 공포의 빛이 어렸고, 그녀는 본능에 이끌리듯 조지에게 매달렸다."]], [["\"Don't be afraid, dear,\"", "\"두려워 말아요, 여보.\""], ["he said good-naturedly; \"it is but a twelve hours'", "그가 너그럽게 말했다. \"고작 열두 시간 항해일 뿐이야."], ["passage. It won't hurt you.", "당신을 해치지 않아."], ["You shall go, too, Emmy.\"", "당신도 가게 될 거야, 에미.\""]], [["\"I intend to go,\"", "\"저는 갈 작정이에요.\""], ["said Becky.", "베키가 말했다."], ["\"I'm on the staff.", "\"전 참모부 소속이거든요."], ["General Tufto is a great flirt of mine.", "터프토 장군이 나한테 홀딱 빠져 있어요."], ["Isn't he, Rawdon?\"", "안 그래요, 로던?\""], ["Rawdon laughed out with his usual roar.", "로던이 여느 때처럼 우렁차게 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["William Dobbin flushed up quite red.", "윌리엄 도빈은 새빨갛게 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["\"She can't go,\"", "\"부인은 갈 수 없습니다.\""], ["he said; \"think of the--of the danger,\"", "그가 말했다. \"위험을, 그 위험을 생각해 보십시오.\""], ["he was going to add; but had not all his conversation during dinner-time tended to prove there was none?", "그는 그렇게 덧붙이려다가, 식사 내내 그가 한 이야기가 죄다 위험이란 없다는 것을 입증하려는 게 아니었던가?"], ["He became very confused and silent.", "그는 몹시 당황하여 입을 다물었다."]], [["\"I must and will go,\"", "\"저는 가야 하고, 또 갈 거예요.\""], ["Amelia cried with the greatest spirit; and George, applauding her resolution, patted her under the chin, and asked all the persons present if they ever saw such a termagant of a wife, and agreed that the lady should bear him company.", "애밀리아가 더없이 기운차게 외쳤다. 그러자 조지가 그녀의 결심을 칭찬하며 턱을 다독여 주고, 그 자리에 있는 모든 이들에게 이런 표독스러운 아내를 본 적이 있느냐고 물었고, 다들 부인이 그와 동행해야 마땅하다는 데 동의했다."], ["\"We'll have Mrs. O'Dowd to chaperon you,\"", "\"오다우드 부인을 당신 보호자로 모셔야겠군.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["What cared she so long as her husband was near her?", "남편이 곁에 있는 한 그녀가 무엇을 개의하겠는가?"], ["Thus somehow the bitterness of a parting was juggled away.", "그리하여 어쩐 일인지 이별의 쓰라림은 슬쩍 따돌려졌다."], ["Though war and danger were in store, war and danger might not befall for months to come.", "비록 전쟁과 위험이 앞에 도사리고 있었지만, 전쟁과 위험은 앞으로 몇 달간은 닥치지 않을지도 몰랐다."], ["There was a respite at any rate, which made the timid little Amelia almost as happy as a full reprieve would have done, and which even Dobbin owned in his heart was very welcome.", "어쨌거나 한숨 돌릴 유예가 생긴 것이고, 그것이 겁 많은 어린 애밀리아를 완전한 사면을 받았을 때나 다름없을 만큼 행복하게 만들었으며, 도빈조차 속으로 그것이 무척 반갑다고 인정하는 바였다."], ["For, to be permitted to see her was now the greatest privilege and hope of his life, and he thought with himself secretly how he would watch and protect her.", "이제 그녀를 볼 수 있다는 것이 그의 인생에서 가장 큰 특권이요 희망이었기에, 그는 남몰래 어떻게 그녀를 지켜보고 보호할지 마음속으로 헤아렸다."], ["I wouldn't have let her go if I had been married to her, he thought.", "내가 만약 그녀와 결혼한 사이라면 그녀를 보내지 않았을 텐데, 그는 생각했다."], ["But George was the master, and his friend did not think fit to remonstrate.", "그러나 조지가 주인이었고, 그 친구는 굳이 간언할 일이 못 된다고 여겼다."]], [["Putting her arm round her friend's waist, Rebecca at length carried Amelia off from the dinner-table where so much business of importance had been discussed, and left the gentlemen in a highly exhilarated state, drinking and talking very gaily.", "레베카는 마침내 친구의 허리에 팔을 두르고 애밀리아를 그토록 중대한 일들이 논의된 식탁에서 데리고 나갔고, 잔뜩 들뜬 채로 매우 흥겹게 술을 마시며 떠드는 신사들을 남겨 두었다."]], [["In the course of the evening Rawdon got a little family-note from his wife, which, although he crumpled it up and burnt it instantly in the candle, we had the good luck to read over Rebecca's shoulder.", "저녁이 무르익는 동안 로던은 아내에게서 자그마한 집안 쪽지 하나를 받았는데, 비록 그가 그것을 구겨서 촛불에 곧장 태워 버렸지만, 우리는 운 좋게도 레베카의 어깨너머로 그것을 읽을 수 있었다. \"굉장한 소식.\""], ["\"Great news,\" she wrote.", "그녀는 그렇게 적었다."], ["\"Mrs. Bute is gone.", "\"뷰트 부인이 떠났어요."], ["Get the money from Cupid tonight, as he'll be off to-morrow most likely.", "큐피드한테서 오늘 밤 돈을 받아 둬요. 그가 십중팔구 내일 떠날 테니까."], ["Mind this.--R.\"", "명심해요.—R.\""], ["So when the little company was about adjourning to coffee in the women's apartment, Rawdon touched Osborne on the elbow, and said gracefully, \"I say, Osborne, my boy, if quite convenient, I'll trouble you for that 'ere small trifle.\"", "그리하여 조촐한 일행이 여인들의 거처로 자리를 옮겨 커피를 마시려 할 즈음, 로던이 오스본의 팔꿈치를 툭 건드리며 우아하게 말했다. \"이봐, 오스본, 친구, 만약 별 어려움이 없거든, 그 자그마한 돈 좀 신세 좀 지세나.\""], ["It was not quite convenient, but nevertheless George gave him a considerable present instalment in bank-notes from his pocket-book, and a bill on his agents at a week's date, for the remaining sum.", "별 어려움이 없는 것은 아니었으나, 그럼에도 조지는 지갑에서 상당한 액수의 일부를 은행권으로 그에게 건네주고, 나머지 금액에 대해서는 일주일 후 만기로 자기 대리인 앞으로 어음을 끊어 주었다."]], [["This matter arranged, George, and Jos, and Dobbin, held a council of war over their cigars, and agreed that a general move should be made for London in Jos's open carriage the next day.", "이 일이 처리되자, 조지와 조스와 도빈은 시가를 피우며 작전 회의를 열고, 이튿날 조스의 무개마차를 타고 다 함께 런던으로 떠나기로 합의했다."], ["Jos, I think, would have preferred staying until Rawdon Crawley quitted Brighton, but Dobbin and George overruled him, and he agreed to carry the party to town, and ordered four horses, as became his dignity.", "조스는, 내 생각에, 로던 크롤리가 브라이턴을 떠날 때까지 머물기를 더 바랐을 테지만, 도빈과 조지가 그의 뜻을 눌렀고, 그리하여 그는 일행을 도시로 실어 나르기로 동의하고는, 자기 체면에 걸맞게 말 네 필을 주문했다."], ["With these they set off in state, after breakfast, the next day.", "이튿날 아침 식사를 마친 뒤, 그들은 그 말들과 함께 위풍당당하게 출발했다."], ["Amelia had risen very early in the morning, and packed her little trunks with the greatest alacrity, while Osborne lay in bed deploring that she had not a maid to help her.", "애밀리아는 아침 일찍 일어나 더없이 부지런하게 자기 자그마한 트렁크들을 꾸렸고, 그동안 오스본은 침대에 누운 채 도와줄 하녀가 그녀에게 없음을 한탄했다."], ["She was only too glad, however, to perform this office for herself.", "그러나 그녀는 이 일을 손수 하게 되어 그저 기쁘기만 했다."], ["A dim uneasy sentiment about Rebecca filled her mind already; and although they kissed each other most tenderly at parting, yet we know what jealousy is; and Mrs.", "레베카에 대한 어렴풋하고 불안한 감정이 벌써 그녀의 마음을 채우고 있었다. 그리고 비록 두 사람이 헤어지면서 더없이 다정하게 입을 맞추기는 했으나, 우리는 질투라는 것이 어떤 것인지 알지 않는가."], ["Amelia possessed that among other virtues of her sex.", "그리고 애밀리아 부인 역시 자기 성별의 다른 미덕들과 더불어 그 질투라는 것을 지니고 있었다."]], [["Besides these characters who are coming and going away, we must remember that there were some other old friends of ours at Brighton; Miss Crawley, namely, and the suite in attendance upon her.", "이렇게 오고 가는 인물들 외에도, 우리는 브라이턴에 우리의 오랜 벗 몇몇이 더 있었음을 잊어서는 안 된다."], ["Now, although Rebecca and her husband were but at a few stones'", "다름 아니라 크롤리 양과, 그녀를 시중드는 수행원들 말이다."], ["throw of the lodgings which the invalid Miss Crawley occupied, the old lady's door remained as pitilessly closed to them as it had been heretofore in London. As long as she remained by the side of her sister-in-law, Mrs.", "그런데 레베카와 그녀의 남편이 병약한 크롤리 양이 묵고 있는 숙소에서 돌멩이 몇 개 던질 거리밖에 떨어져 있지 않았건만, 그 노부인의 문은 일찍이 런던에서 그러했듯 그들에게 가차 없이 닫혀 있었다."], ["Bute Crawley took care that her beloved Matilda should not be agitated by a meeting with her nephew.", "올케 곁에 머무는 동안, 뷰트 크롤리 부인은 사랑하는 마틸다가 조카와 마주쳐 동요하는 일이 없도록 단단히 단속했다."], ["When the spinster took her drive, the faithful Mrs. Bute sate beside her in the carriage.", "노처녀가 드라이브를 나갈 때면 충직한 뷰트 부인이 그녀 곁에 앉아 마차에 동승했다."], ["When Miss Crawley took the air in a chair, Mrs. Bute marched on one side of the vehicle, whilst honest Briggs occupied the other wing.", "크롤리 양이 가마를 타고 바람을 쐬러 나갈 때면, 뷰트 부인이 가마 한쪽에서 행진하듯 걸었고, 정직한 브리그스가 다른 쪽 날개를 맡았다."], ["And if they met Rawdon and his wife by chance--although the former constantly and obsequiously took off his hat, the Miss-Crawley party passed him by with such a frigid and killing indifference, that Rawdon began to despair.", "그리고 어쩌다 로던과 그 아내를 마주치기라도 하면, 전자가 노상 굽실거리며 모자를 벗었음에도, 크롤리 양 일행은 어찌나 싸늘하고 사람 잡는 무관심으로 그를 지나쳐 버리던지, 로던은 절망하기 시작했다."]], [["\"We might as well be in London as here,\"", "\"여기 있으나 런던에 있으나 매한가지로군.\""], ["Captain Rawdon often said, with a downcast air.", "로던 대위는 풀 죽은 기색으로 곧잘 말했다."]], [["\"A comfortable inn in Brighton is better than a spunging-house in Chancery Lane,\"", "\"브라이턴의 안락한 여관이 챈서리 레인의 빚쟁이 유치소보다는 낫잖아요.\""], ["his wife answered, who was of a more cheerful temperament. \"Think of those two aides-de-camp of Mr.", "한결 명랑한 기질을 지닌 그의 아내가 대꾸했다."], ["Moses, the sheriff's-officer, who watched our lodging for a week. Our friends here are very stupid, but Mr.", "\"그 집행관 모지스 씨의 부관 둘이 일주일 내내 우리 숙소를 지키고 있던 걸 생각해 봐요."], ["Jos and Captain Cupid are better companions than Mr. Moses's men, Rawdon, my love.\"", "여기 우리 친구들이 아주 어리석긴 해도, 조스 씨와 큐피드 대위가 모지스 씨의 부하들보다는 나은 길동무잖아요, 로던, 내 사랑.\""]], [["\"I wonder the writs haven't followed me down here,\"", "\"여기까지 영장이 따라 내려오지 않은 게 이상하단 말이야.\""], ["Rawdon continued, still desponding.", "로던이 여전히 풀 죽어 말을 이었다."]], [["\"When they do, we'll find means to give them the slip,\"", "\"따라오면, 그때 가서 따돌릴 방도를 찾으면 돼요.\""], ["said dauntless little Becky, and further pointed out to her husband the great comfort and advantage of meeting Jos and Osborne, whose acquaintance had brought to Rawdon Crawley a most timely little supply of ready money.", "겁 없는 어린 베키가 말하고는, 조스와 오스본을 만난 일이 얼마나 큰 위안이자 이득인지를 남편에게 거듭 일깨워 주었다. 그들과의 친분이 로던 크롤리에게 더없이 때맞춰 자그마한 현금을 마련해 주었던 것이다."]], [["\"It will hardly be enough to pay the inn bill,\"", "\"여관 계산서 치를 만큼도 안 될 텐데.\""], ["grumbled the Guardsman.", "근위병이 투덜거렸다."]], [["\"Why need we pay it?\"", "\"우리가 그걸 왜 치러요?\""], ["said the lady, who had an answer for everything.", "무엇에든 답을 가진 부인이 말했다."]], [["Through Rawdon's valet, who still kept up a trifling acquaintance with the male inhabitants of Miss Crawley's servants'", "크롤리 양의 하인들과 여전히 사소한 친분을 유지하며, 마주칠 때마다 마부에게 술을 사라는 지시를 받은 로던의 시종을 통해, 늙은 크롤리 양의 동정은 우리 젊은 부부에게 제법 소상히 알려졌다."], ["hall, and was instructed to treat the coachman to drink whenever they met, old Miss Crawley's movements were pretty well known by our young couple; and Rebecca luckily bethought herself of being unwell, and of calling in the same apothecary who was in attendance upon the spinster, so that their information was on the whole tolerably complete.", "그리고 레베카는 다행스럽게도 자기가 몸이 안 좋은 척하면서, 노처녀를 진료하는 바로 그 약제사를 불러들일 생각을 해냈고, 그리하여 그들의 정보는 대체로 그런대로 빈틈없었다."], ["Nor was Miss Briggs, although forced to adopt a hostile attitude, secretly inimical to Rawdon and his wife.", "브리그스 양 역시 비록 적대적인 태도를 취하지 않을 수 없었으나, 속으로는 로던과 그 아내에게 적의를 품지 않았다."], ["She was naturally of a kindly and forgiving disposition.", "그녀는 천성이 다정하고 너그러운 기질이었다."], ["Now that the cause of jealousy was removed, her dislike for Rebecca disappeared also, and she remembered the latter's invariable good words and good humour. And, indeed, she and Mrs.", "이제 질투의 빌미가 사라지자 레베카에 대한 반감도 함께 사라졌고, 그녀는 레베카가 한결같이 건네던 다정한 말과 명랑한 기분을 떠올렸다."], ["Firkin, the lady's-maid, and the whole of Miss Crawley's household, groaned under the tyranny of the triumphant Mrs. Bute.", "그리고 실로 그녀와, 시녀인 퍼킨 부인, 그리고 크롤리 양 집안 전체가 의기양양한 뷰트 부인의 폭정 아래 신음하고 있었다."]], [["As often will be the case, that good but imperious woman pushed her advantages too far, and her successes quite unmercifully.", "흔히 그러하듯, 그 선량하나 위압적인 여인은 자기 잇속을 너무 멀리까지 밀어붙였고, 그 승리를 가차 없이 누렸다."], ["She had in the course of a few weeks brought the invalid to such a state of helpless docility, that the poor soul yielded herself entirely to her sister's orders, and did not even dare to complain of her slavery to Briggs or Firkin. Mrs.", "그녀는 몇 주 만에 그 병약한 부인을 어찌나 속수무책의 고분고분한 상태로 만들어 놓았던지, 가엾은 이는 올케의 명령에 전적으로 자신을 내맡기고, 브리그스나 퍼킨에게 자기의 노예 신세를 하소연할 엄두조차 내지 못했다."], ["Bute measured out the glasses of wine which Miss Crawley was daily allowed to take, with irresistible accuracy, greatly to the annoyance of Firkin and the butler, who found themselves deprived of control over even the sherry-bottle.", "뷰트 부인은 크롤리 양이 날마다 마실 수 있도록 허락된 포도주 잔을 거역할 수 없는 정확함으로 일일이 헤아려 따랐는데, 이는 셰리주 병에 대한 통제권마저 빼앗긴 퍼킨과 집사에게 큰 분통의 씨앗이었다."], ["She apportioned the sweetbreads, jellies, chickens; their quantity and order.", "그녀는 흉선 요리며 젤리며 닭고기의 양과 순서를 배정했다."], ["Night and noon and morning she brought the abominable drinks ordained by the Doctor, and made her patient swallow them with so affecting an obedience that Firkin said \"my poor Missus du take her physic like a lamb.\"", "밤이고 낮이고 아침이고, 그녀는 의사가 정해 준 그 끔찍한 약을 가져와 환자에게 어찌나 애처로운 순종으로 삼키게 했던지, 퍼킨은 \"우리 가엾은 마님이 양처럼 약을 잘도 잡수신다니까\"라고 말했다."], ["She prescribed the drive in the carriage or the ride in the chair, and, in a word, ground down the old lady in her convalescence in such a way as only belongs to your proper-managing, motherly moral woman.", "그녀는 마차로 드라이브를 하라느니 가마로 산책을 나가라느니 지시했고, 한마디로 그 노부인의 회복기를, 오로지 살림 잘하고 모성 넘치는 도덕적인 여인만이 할 법한 방식으로 짓눌렀다."], ["If ever the patient faintly resisted, and pleaded for a little bit more dinner or a little drop less medicine, the nurse threatened her with instantaneous death, when Miss Crawley instantly gave in. \"She's no spirit left in her,\"", "환자가 어쩌다 가냘프게 저항하며 식사를 조금만 더 하게 해 달라거나 약을 조금만 덜 먹게 해 달라고 사정하기라도 하면, 간병인은 당장 죽을 수도 있다고 으름장을 놓았고, 그러면 크롤리 양은 즉시 굴복했다."], ["Firkin remarked to Briggs; \"she ain't ave called me a fool these three weeks.\"", "\"마님한테 기운이라곤 안 남았다니까.\" 퍼킨이 브리그스에게 말했다."], ["Finally, Mrs. Bute had made up her mind to dismiss the aforesaid honest lady's-maid, Mr.", "\"삼 주째 나더러 바보라는 소릴 안 하셨거든.\""], ["Bowls the large confidential man, and Briggs herself, and to send for her daughters from the Rectory, previous to removing the dear invalid bodily to Queen's Crawley, when an odious accident happened which called her away from duties so pleasing.", "마침내 뷰트 부인은, 사랑하는 병약한 부인을 통째로 퀸스 크롤리로 옮기기에 앞서, 앞서 말한 정직한 시녀와 덩치 크고 믿음직한 사내 볼스 씨, 그리고 브리그스 본인까지 내보내고, 목사관에서 자기 딸들을 불러올 작정이었다. 그런데 그 흐뭇한 임무로부터 그녀를 불러내는 고약한 사고가 일어났다."], ["The Reverend Bute Crawley, her husband, riding home one night, fell with his horse and broke his collar-bone.", "그녀의 남편인 뷰트 크롤리 목사가 어느 날 밤 말을 타고 집으로 돌아오다가 말과 함께 넘어져 쇄골이 부러진 것이다."], ["Fever and inflammatory symptoms set in, and Mrs. Bute was forced to leave Sussex for Hampshire.", "열이 오르고 염증 증세가 나타나자, 뷰트 부인은 부득이 서식스를 떠나 햄프셔로 가야 했다."], ["As soon as ever Bute was restored, she promised to return to her dearest friend, and departed, leaving the strongest injunctions with the household regarding their behaviour to their mistress; and as soon as she got into the Southampton coach, there was such a jubilee and sense of relief in all Miss Crawley's house, as the company of persons assembled there had not experienced for many a week before.", "그녀는 뷰트가 회복하는 대로 더없이 소중한 벗에게 돌아오겠노라 약속하고, 안주인을 대하는 처신에 관해 집안사람들에게 더없이 단단히 분부를 내린 뒤 떠났다. 그리고 그녀가 사우샘프턴행 마차에 오르자마자, 크롤리 양의 집 안 전체에는 거기 모인 사람들이 지난 몇 주 동안 맛보지 못한 환희와 안도감이 감돌았다."], ["That very day Miss Crawley left off her afternoon dose of medicine: that afternoon Bowls opened an independent bottle of sherry for himself and Mrs. Firkin: that night Miss Crawley and Miss Briggs indulged in a game of piquet instead of one of Porteus's sermons.", "바로 그날 크롤리 양은 오후의 약 복용을 그만두었고, 그날 오후 볼스는 자신과 퍼킨 부인을 위해 셰리주 한 병을 따로 땄으며, 그날 밤 크롤리 양과 브리그스 양은 포티어스의 설교집 대신 피케 한 판을 즐겼다."], ["It was as in the old nursery-story, when the stick forgot to beat the dog, and the whole course of events underwent a peaceful and happy revolution.", "그것은 마치 옛 동화에서, 막대기가 개를 때리기를 잊자 사건의 온 흐름이 평화롭고 행복한 혁명을 겪던 것과 같았다."]], [["At a very early hour in the morning, twice or thrice a week, Miss Briggs used to betake herself to a bathing-machine, and disport in the water in a flannel gown and an oilskin cap.", "일주일에 두세 번, 아침 아주 이른 시각에 브리그스 양은 해수욕용 마차로 가서, 플란넬 가운에 기름 먹인 모자를 쓰고 물놀이를 하곤 했다."], ["Rebecca, as we have seen, was aware of this circumstance, and though she did not attempt to storm Briggs as she had threatened, and actually dive into that lady's presence and surprise her under the sacredness of the awning, Mrs. Rawdon determined to attack Briggs as she came away from her bath, refreshed and invigorated by her dip, and likely to be in good humour.", "우리가 보았듯이 레베카는 이 사정을 알고 있었고, 비록 으름장 놓았던 대로 브리그스에게 들이닥치거나 실제로 그 부인의 면전으로 잠수해 들어가 차일의 신성함 속에서 그녀를 놀라게 하려 들지는 않았으나, 로던 부인은 브리그스가 물놀이로 상쾌하고 기운이 나서 기분이 좋을 법한, 목욕을 마치고 나오는 길에 그녀를 공략하기로 마음먹었다."]], [["So getting up very early the next morning, Becky brought the telescope in their sitting-room, which faced the sea, to bear upon the bathing-machines on the beach; saw Briggs arrive, enter her box; and put out to sea; and was on the shore just as the nymph of whom she came in quest stepped out of the little caravan on to the shingles.", "그리하여 이튿날 아침 아주 일찍 일어난 베키는, 바다를 마주한 거실로 망원경을 가져와 해변의 해수욕용 마차들에 초점을 맞추었다. 그녀는 브리그스가 도착해 자기 칸으로 들어가 바다로 나아가는 것을 보았고, 자기가 찾아온 그 물의 요정이 자그마한 이동 칸에서 자갈밭으로 막 내려설 무렵 해변에 가 있었다."], ["It was a pretty picture: the beach; the bathing-women's faces; the long line of rocks and building were blushing and bright in the sunshine.", "그것은 아름다운 그림이었다. 해변이며, 해수욕하는 여인들의 얼굴이며, 길게 늘어선 바위와 건물들이 햇살 속에서 발갛게 물들어 반짝였다."], ["Rebecca wore a kind, tender smile on her face, and was holding out her pretty white hand as Briggs emerged from the box.", "레베카는 얼굴에 다정하고 부드러운 미소를 띠고, 브리그스가 칸에서 나오는 순간 어여쁜 흰 손을 내밀고 있었다."], ["What could Briggs do but accept the salutation?", "브리그스가 그 인사를 받는 것 말고 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["\"Miss Sh--Mrs. Crawley,\"", "\"샤 양, 아니 크롤리 부인.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["Mrs. Crawley seized her hand, pressed it to her heart, and with a sudden impulse, flinging her arms round Briggs, kissed her affectionately.", "크롤리 부인은 그녀의 손을 붙잡아 가슴에 꼭 갖다 대더니, 별안간 충동에 휩싸여 브리그스에게 두 팔을 두르고 다정하게 입을 맞추었다."], ["\"Dear, dear friend!\"", "\"사랑하는, 사랑하는 벗이여!\""], ["she said, with a touch of such natural feeling, that Miss Briggs of course at once began to melt, and even the bathing-woman was mollified.", "그녀가 어찌나 자연스러운 감정의 빛을 띠며 말했던지, 브리그스 양은 물론 즉시 마음이 녹기 시작했고, 해수욕하던 여인마저 누그러졌다."]], [["Rebecca found no difficulty in engaging Briggs in a long, intimate, and delightful conversation.", "레베카는 브리그스를 길고도 친밀하며 유쾌한 대화로 끌어들이는 데 아무 어려움이 없었다."], ["Everything that had passed since the morning of Becky's sudden departure from Miss Crawley's house in Park Lane up to the present day, and Mrs. Bute's happy retreat, was discussed and described by Briggs.", "베키가 파크 레인의 크롤리 양 집을 별안간 떠나던 그 아침부터 오늘에 이르기까지 일어난 모든 일과, 뷰트 부인의 다행스러운 퇴장에 이르기까지, 브리그스가 논하고 묘사했다."], ["All Miss Crawley's symptoms, and the particulars of her illness and medical treatment, were narrated by the confidante with that fulness and accuracy which women delight in.", "크롤리 양의 온갖 증세며, 병세와 의료 처치의 시시콜콜한 내막을, 그 심복은 여인들이 즐기는 그 충만함과 정확함으로 풀어놓았다. 자기네 병이며 의사 이야기를 두고 숙녀들이 서로 떠드는 데 지치는 법이 있던가?"], ["About their complaints and their doctors do ladies ever tire of talking to each other?", "브리그스는 이번에도 지치지 않았고, 레베카 또한 듣는 데 싫증 내지 않았다."], ["Briggs did not on this occasion; nor did Rebecca weary of listening.", "그녀는 정말로, 진심으로 고마워했다."], ["She was thankful, truly thankful, that the dear kind Briggs, that the faithful, the invaluable Firkin, had been permitted to remain with their benefactress through her illness.", "그 다정한 브리그스가, 그 충직하고 더없이 소중한 퍼킨이, 그 은인이 앓는 동안 곁에 머물도록 허락받았다는 것이. 부디 그분께 하늘의 축복이 있기를!"], ["Heaven bless her! though she, Rebecca, had seemed to act undutifully towards Miss Crawley; yet was not her fault a natural and excusable one?", "비록 그녀, 레베카가 크롤리 양에게 도리에 어긋나게 행동한 듯 보였을지언정, 그 잘못이란 자연스럽고 너그러이 보아 넘길 만한 것이 아니었던가?"], ["Could she help giving her hand to the man who had won her heart?", "자기 마음을 사로잡은 남자에게 손을 내미는 것을 어찌 마다할 수 있었겠는가?"], ["Briggs, the sentimental, could only turn up her eyes to heaven at this appeal, and heave a sympathetic sigh, and think that she, too, had given away her affections long years ago, and own that Rebecca was no very great criminal.", "감상적인 브리그스는 이 호소에 그저 눈을 들어 하늘을 우러르고 공감의 한숨을 내쉬며, 자기 또한 오래전에 마음을 내준 적이 있음을 떠올리고는, 레베카가 그리 대단한 죄인은 아니라고 인정할 수밖에 없었다."]], [["\"Can I ever forget her who so befriended the friendless orphan?", "\"의지가지없는 고아에게 그토록 벗이 되어 주신 분을 제가 어찌 잊을 수 있겠어요?"], ["No, though she has cast me off,\"", "아니에요, 비록 그분이 저를 내치셨다 해도,\""], ["the latter said, \"I shall never cease to love her, and I would devote my life to her service.", "레베카가 말했다. \"저는 결코 그분을 사랑하기를 멈추지 않을 것이고, 제 목숨을 바쳐 그분을 섬길 거예요."], ["As my own benefactress, as my beloved Rawdon's adored relative, I love and admire Miss Crawley, dear Miss Briggs, beyond any woman in the world, and next to her I love all those who are faithful to her.", "저의 은인이시고, 제 사랑하는 로던이 흠모하는 친척이시니, 사랑하는 브리그스 양, 저는 이 세상 어느 여인보다도 크롤리 양을 사랑하고 흠모하며, 그분 다음으로는 그분께 충실한 모든 분들을 사랑해요."], ["I would never have treated Miss Crawley's faithful friends as that odious designing Mrs.", "저라면 결코 저 고약하고 음흉한 뷰트 부인처럼 크롤리 양의 충직한 벗들을 대하지 않았을 거예요."], ["Bute has done.", "온통 마음뿐인 로던은,\""], ["Rawdon, who was all heart,\"", "레베카가 말을 이었다."], ["Rebecca continued, \"although his outward manners might seem rough and careless, had said a hundred times, with tears in his eyes, that he blessed Heaven for sending his dearest Aunty two such admirable nurses as her attached Firkin and her admirable Miss Briggs.", "\"비록 겉으로는 거칠고 무심해 보일지 몰라도, 눈물을 글썽이며 백 번도 더 말했답니다. 더없이 소중한 고모님께 충성스러운 퍼킨과 훌륭한 브리그스 양 같은 그토록 훌륭한 간병인 두 사람을 보내 주신 하늘에 감사한다고요.\""], ["Should the machinations of the horrible Mrs. Bute end, as she too much feared they would, in banishing everybody that Miss Crawley loved from her side, and leaving that poor lady a victim to those harpies at the Rectory, Rebecca besought her (Miss Briggs) to remember that her own home, humble as it was, was always open to receive Briggs.", "만약 그 끔찍한 뷰트 부인의 음모가, 그녀가 너무도 두려워하던 대로, 크롤리 양이 사랑하는 모든 이를 그 곁에서 내쫓고 그 가엾은 부인을 목사관의 그 독수리들에게 제물로 내버려 두는 것으로 끝난다면, 레베카는 그녀(브리그스 양)에게 부디 기억해 달라고 간청했다."], ["Dear friend,\" she exclaimed, in a transport of enthusiasm, \"some hearts can never forget benefits; all women are not Bute Crawleys!", "비록 누추할지언정 자기 집은 언제나 브리그스를 맞이할 수 있도록 열려 있다는 것을."], ["Though why should I complain of her,\"", "\"사랑하는 벗이여,\" 그녀가 열정에 북받쳐 외쳤다."], ["Rebecca added; \"though I have been her tool and the victim to her arts, do I not owe my dearest Rawdon to her?\"", "\"어떤 마음은 결코 은혜를 잊지 못하는 법이에요. 모든 여자가 다 뷰트 크롤리 같지는 않으니까요!"], ["And Rebecca unfolded to Briggs all Mrs.", "하기야 내가 왜 그 여자를 탓하겠어요,\" 레베카가 덧붙였다."], ["Bute's conduct at Queen's Crawley, which, though unintelligible to her then, was clearly enough explained by the events now--now that the attachment had sprung up which Mrs.", "\"비록 내가 그 여자의 도구요 술책의 희생물이 되었다 해도, 더없이 소중한 내 로던을 그 여자 덕에 얻은 게 아니던가요?\" 그러고는 레베카가 퀸스 크롤리에서 뷰트 부인이 한 모든 행동을 브리그스에게 낱낱이 펼쳐 보였는데, 그 행동이란 당시에는 그녀로서도 알 수 없는 것이었으나, 이제 와서는 그 사건들로 충분히 명백하게 설명되었다."], ["Bute had encouraged by a thousand artifices--now that two innocent people had fallen into the snares which she had laid for them, and loved and married and been ruined through her schemes.", "이제는, 뷰트 부인이 온갖 술수로 부추긴 그 애정이 싹튼 지금에 와서는, 두 순진한 사람이 그 여자가 놓은 덫에 빠져 사랑하고 결혼하고 그 여자의 책략 탓에 파멸하고 만 지금에 와서는 말이다."]], [["It was all very true.", "그것은 모두 사실이었다."], ["Briggs saw the stratagems as clearly as possible. Mrs.", "브리그스는 그 계략들을 더없이 또렷이 알아보았다."], ["Bute had made the match between Rawdon and Rebecca.", "뷰트 부인이 로던과 레베카의 혼사를 꾸민 것이었다."], ["Yet, though the latter was a perfectly innocent victim, Miss Briggs could not disguise from her friend her fear that Miss Crawley's affections were hopelessly estranged from Rebecca, and that the old lady would never forgive her nephew for making so imprudent a marriage.", "그러나 후자가 더없이 죄 없는 희생물임에도, 브리그스 양은 친구에게 한 가지 두려움을 감추지 못했다. 크롤리 양의 애정이 레베카에게서 가망 없이 멀어져 버렸고, 그 노부인이 그토록 무분별한 혼인을 한 조카를 결코 용서하지 않으리라는 두려움이었다."]], [["On this point Rebecca had her own opinion, and still kept up a good heart.", "이 점에 관해서 레베카는 나름의 견해가 있었고, 여전히 마음을 굳게 다잡았다."], ["If Miss Crawley did not forgive them at present, she might at least relent on a future day.", "크롤리 양이 지금은 그들을 용서하지 않는다 해도, 적어도 훗날 어느 날엔가 마음을 누그러뜨릴지도 몰랐다."], ["Even now, there was only that puling, sickly Pitt Crawley between Rawdon and a baronetcy; and should anything happen to the former, all would be well. At all events, to have Mrs.", "지금조차도 로던과 준남작 작위 사이를 가로막는 것이라곤 그 칭얼대는 병약한 핏 크롤리뿐이었으니, 그 전자에게 무슨 일이 생긴다면 만사가 잘 풀릴 터였다."], ["Bute's designs exposed, and herself well abused, was a satisfaction, and might be advantageous to Rawdon's interest; and Rebecca, after an hour's chat with her recovered friend, left her with the most tender demonstrations of regard, and quite assured that the conversation they had had together would be reported to Miss Crawley before many hours were over.", "어쨌거나 뷰트 부인의 계략이 폭로되고 그 여자가 톡톡히 욕을 먹게 된 것은 흐뭇한 일이었고, 로던의 잇속에 이로울 수도 있었다. 그리하여 레베카는 되찾은 친구와 한 시간 동안 담소를 나눈 뒤, 더없이 다정한 존경의 표시를 남기고 그녀와 헤어졌으니, 두 사람이 함께 나눈 대화가 몇 시간도 지나기 전에 크롤리 양에게 고스란히 전해지리라 확신하면서였다."]], [["This interview ended, it became full time for Rebecca to return to her inn, where all the party of the previous day were assembled at a farewell breakfast.", "이 만남이 끝나자, 레베카로서는 자기 여관으로 돌아가야 할 때가 무르익었다. 그곳에서는 전날의 일행이 모두 작별의 아침 식사 자리에 모여 있었다."], ["Rebecca took such a tender leave of Amelia as became two women who loved each other as sisters; and having used her handkerchief plentifully, and hung on her friend's neck as if they were parting for ever, and waved the handkerchief (which was quite dry, by the way) out of window, as the carriage drove off, she came back to the breakfast table, and ate some prawns with a good deal of appetite, considering her emotion; and while she was munching these delicacies, explained to Rawdon what had occurred in her morning walk between herself and Briggs.", "레베카는 자매처럼 서로 사랑하는 두 여인에게 어울리는 더없이 다정한 작별을 애밀리아에게 고했다. 손수건을 아낌없이 적시고, 마치 영영 헤어지기라도 하듯 친구의 목에 매달리고, 마차가 떠날 때 (그나저나 바싹 마른) 손수건을 창밖으로 흔들고 나서, 그녀는 아침 식탁으로 돌아와, 그 감정의 격앙에 비추어 보자면 자못 왕성한 식욕으로 새우를 좀 먹었다."], ["Her hopes were very high: she made her husband share them.", "그리고 이 별미들을 우물거리는 동안, 아침 산책길에 자신과 브리그스 사이에 있었던 일을 로던에게 설명해 주었다."], ["She generally succeeded in making her husband share all her opinions, whether melancholy or cheerful.", "그녀의 기대는 매우 컸고, 그녀는 남편도 그 기대를 나누게 했다. 그녀는 대체로 침울한 것이든 명랑한 것이든 자기 의견을 남편이 함께 나누게 하는 데 성공하곤 했다."]], [["\"You will now, if you please, my dear, sit down at the writing-table and pen me a pretty little letter to Miss Crawley, in which you'll say that you are a good boy, and that sort of thing.\"", "\"여보, 이제 괜찮다면, 책상 앞에 앉아 크롤리 양에게 보낼 어여쁜 편지를 한 통 써 줘요."], ["So Rawdon sate down, and wrote off, \"Brighton, Thursday,\"", "당신이 착한 아이라느니 뭐니 하는 그런 식으로 말이에요.\""], ["and \"My dear Aunt,\" with great rapidity: but there the gallant officer's imagination failed him.", "그리하여 로던은 자리에 앉아 \"브라이턴에서, 목요일\"과 \"사랑하는 고모님께\"라고 매우 빠르게 써 내려갔으나, 거기서 그 용맹한 장교의 상상력이 바닥나고 말았다."], ["He mumbled the end of his pen, and looked up in his wife's face.", "그는 펜 끝을 우물거리며 아내의 얼굴을 올려다보았다."], ["She could not help laughing at his rueful countenance, and marching up and down the room with her hands behind her, the little woman began to dictate a letter, which he took down.", "아내는 그 처량한 표정에 웃음을 참지 못하고, 두 손을 등 뒤로 하고 방 안을 오락가락하며 편지를 받아쓰게 하기 시작했고, 그는 그것을 받아 적었다."]], [["\"Before quitting the country and commencing a campaign, which very possibly may be fatal.\"", "\"이 나라를 떠나, 십중팔구 목숨을 앗아 갈지도 모르는 출정을 앞두고.\""]], [["\"What?\"", "\"뭐라고?\""], ["said Rawdon, rather surprised, but took the humour of the phrase, and presently wrote it down with a grin.", "로던이 다소 놀라 말했으나, 그 표현의 우스움을 알아채고는 이내 씩 웃으며 받아 적었다."]], [["\"Which very possibly may be fatal, I have come hither--\"", "\"십중팔구 목숨을 앗아 갈지도 모르는 출정을 앞두고, 저는 이리로 왔습니다.\""]], [["\"Why not say come here, Becky?", "\"그냥 여기로 왔다고 하면 안 돼, 베키?"], ["Come here's grammar,\"", "여기로 왔다가 문법에 맞잖아.\""], ["the dragoon interposed.", "용기병이 끼어들었다."]], [["\"I have come hither,\"", "\"저는 이리로 왔습니다.\""], ["Rebecca insisted, with a stamp of her foot, \"to say farewell to my dearest and earliest friend.", "레베카가 발을 한 번 구르며 고집했다. \"더없이 소중하고 더없이 오래된 벗에게 작별을 고하고자."], ["I beseech you before I go, not perhaps to return, once more to let me press the hand from which I have received nothing but kindnesses all my life.\"", "떠나기 전에, 어쩌면 다시 돌아오지 못할 길을 떠나기 전에, 평생토록 친절만을 베풀어 주신 그 손을 한 번만 더 잡게 해 주시기를 간청드립니다.\""]], [["\"Kindnesses all my life,\"", "\"평생토록 친절만을.\""], ["echoed Rawdon, scratching down the words, and quite amazed at his own facility of composition.", "로던이 그 말을 휘갈겨 적으며 따라 읊고는, 자기 자신의 문장 짓는 재주에 적잖이 놀랐다."]], [["\"I ask nothing from you but that we should part not in anger.", "\"제가 고모님께 바라는 것은 다른 것이 아니라, 우리가 노여움 속에서 헤어지지 않는 것뿐입니다."], ["I have the pride of my family on some points, though not on all.", "저는 어떤 점에서는 가문의 자존심을 지니고 있으나, 모든 점에서 그런 것은 아닙니다."], ["I married a painter's daughter, and am not ashamed of the union.\"", "저는 화가의 딸과 결혼했으며, 그 결합을 부끄러워하지 않습니다.\""]], [["\"No, run me through the body if I am!\"", "\"그래, 부끄러워하면 내 몸을 칼로 꿰뚫어도 좋아!\""], ["Rawdon ejaculated.", "로던이 외쳤다."]], [["\"You old booby,\"", "\"이런 멍청이 같으니.\""], ["Rebecca said, pinching his ear and looking over to see that he made no mistakes in spelling--\"beseech is not spelt with an a, and earliest is.\"", "레베카가 그의 귀를 꼬집으며, 철자에 틀린 데가 없는지 넘겨다보며 말했다. \"간청드립니다(beseech)에는 a가 안 들어가고, 오래된(earliest)에는 들어가요.\""], ["So he altered these words, bowing to the superior knowledge of his little Missis.", "그리하여 그는 이 낱말들을 고쳤으니, 자기의 어린 마님이 지닌 우월한 지식에 머리를 조아린 셈이었다."]], [["\"I thought that you were aware of the progress of my attachment,\"", "\"저는 고모님께서 제 애정의 진척을 알고 계신 줄로 여겼습니다.\" 레베카가 말을 이었다."], ["Rebecca continued: \"I knew that Mrs. Bute Crawley confirmed and encouraged it.", "\"저는 뷰트 크롤리 부인이 그것을 다지고 부추겼다는 것을 알고 있었습니다."], ["But I make no reproaches.", "그러나 저는 누구도 책망하지 않습니다."], ["I married a poor woman, and am content to abide by what I have done.", "저는 가난한 여인과 결혼했으며, 제가 한 일을 기꺼이 감내하고자 합니다."], ["Leave your property, dear Aunt, as you will.", "사랑하는 고모님, 고모님의 재산은 뜻대로 처분하십시오."], ["I shall never complain of the way in which you dispose of it.", "저는 고모님께서 그것을 어떻게 처분하시든 결코 불평하지 않겠습니다."], ["I would have you believe that I love you for yourself, and not for money's sake.", "부디 제가 돈 때문이 아니라 고모님 그 자체를 사랑한다는 것을 믿어 주시기를 바랍니다."], ["I want to be reconciled to you ere I leave England.", "저는 영국을 떠나기 전에 고모님과 화해하고 싶습니다."], ["Let me, let me see you before I go.", "떠나기 전에, 부디, 부디 고모님을 뵙게 해 주십시오."], ["A few weeks or months hence it may be too late, and I cannot bear the notion of quitting the country without a kind word of farewell from you.\"", "몇 주, 혹은 몇 달 뒤면 너무 늦을지도 모르며, 저는 고모님의 다정한 작별 인사 한마디 없이 이 나라를 떠난다는 생각을 견딜 수가 없습니다.\""]], [["\"She won't recognise my style in that,\"", "\"고모님은 이걸 내 문체로 알아보지 못할 거예요.\""], ["said Becky.", "베키가 말했다."], ["\"I made the sentences short and brisk on purpose.\"", "\"일부러 문장을 짧고 시원시원하게 만들었거든요.\""], ["And this authentic missive was despatched under cover to Miss Briggs.", "그리하여 이 진짜 같은 서신은 브리그스 양 앞으로 봉해져 발송되었다."]], [["Old Miss Crawley laughed when Briggs, with great mystery, handed her over this candid and simple statement.", "브리그스가 잔뜩 비밀스러운 기색으로 이 솔직하고 단순한 진술을 건네자, 늙은 크롤리 양은 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["\"We may read it now Mrs. Bute is away,\"", "\"뷰트 부인이 떠났으니 이제 읽어 봐도 되겠지.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"Read it to me, Briggs.\"", "\"읽어 봐라, 브리그스.\""]], [["When Briggs had read the epistle out, her patroness laughed more.", "브리그스가 그 편지를 소리 내어 다 읽고 나자, 그 후원자는 한층 더 웃었다."], ["\"Don't you see, you goose,\"", "\"모르겠느냐, 이 멍청아.\""], ["she said to Briggs, who professed to be much touched by the honest affection which pervaded the composition, \"don't you see that Rawdon never wrote a word of it.", "그녀가 그 글에 깃든 정직한 애정에 무척 감동했다고 공언하는 브리그스에게 말했다. \"이걸 로던이 단 한 자도 쓰지 않았다는 게 안 보이느냐?"], ["He never wrote to me without asking for money in his life, and all his letters are full of bad spelling, and dashes, and bad grammar.", "그 애는 평생 돈을 달라는 말 없이 나한테 편지한 적이 없고, 그 애 편지란 죄다 틀린 철자며 줄표며 엉터리 문법투성이야."], ["It is that little serpent of a governess who rules him.\"", "그 애를 쥐고 흔드는 건 그 가정교사라는 자그마한 독사라고.\""], ["They are all alike, Miss Crawley thought in her heart.", "다들 한통속이지, 크롤리 양은 속으로 생각했다."], ["They all want me dead, and are hankering for my money.", "죄다 내가 죽기만을 바라며 내 돈을 노리고 안달이라니까."]], [["\"I don't mind seeing Rawdon,\"", "\"로던을 만나는 거야 상관없다.\""], ["she added, after a pause, and in a tone of perfect indifference.", "그녀가 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤, 더없이 무심한 어조로 덧붙였다. \"그 애랑 악수하는 거야 안 할 것도 없고."], ["\"I had just as soon shake hands with him as not.", "한바탕 소동만 없다면, 만나지 못할 게 뭐 있겠느냐? 난 상관없다."], ["Provided there is no scene, why shouldn't we meet? I don't mind.", "하지만 사람의 인내심에도 한계가 있는 법이야."], ["But human patience has its limits; and mind, my dear, I respectfully decline to receive Mrs.", "그러니 명심해라, 얘야, 나는 정중히 로던 부인 맞이하기를 사절하겠다. 그것까지는 도저히 못 견디겠어.\""], ["Rawdon--I can't support that quite\"--and Miss Briggs was fain to be content with this half-message of conciliation; and thought that the best method of bringing the old lady and her nephew together, was to warn Rawdon to be in waiting on the Cliff, when Miss Crawley went out for her air in her chair.", "그리하여 브리그스 양은 이 절반의 화해 전갈에 만족하지 않을 수 없었다. 그러고는 그 노부인과 그 조카를 만나게 할 최선의 방법이란, 크롤리 양이 가마를 타고 바람을 쐬러 나갈 때 로던더러 절벽 위에서 대기하고 있으라 일러 두는 것이라고 생각했다."], ["There they met.", "거기서 그들은 만났다."], ["I don't know whether Miss Crawley had any private feeling of regard or emotion upon seeing her old favourite; but she held out a couple of fingers to him with as smiling and good-humoured an air, as if they had met only the day before.", "크롤리 양이 옛 총아를 보고 어떤 사사로운 정이나 감정을 느꼈는지는 나도 모르겠으나, 그녀는 마치 바로 어제 만나기라도 한 듯 더없이 미소 짓고 더없이 기분 좋은 기색으로 그에게 손가락 두 개를 내밀었다."], ["And as for Rawdon, he turned as red as scarlet, and wrung off Briggs's hand, so great was his rapture and his confusion at the meeting.", "그리고 로던으로 말하면, 그는 새빨갛게 얼굴을 붉히고는 브리그스의 손을 으스러져라 잡아 흔들었으니, 이 만남에 대한 그의 환희와 당혹감이 그토록 컸던 것이다."], ["Perhaps it was interest that moved him: or perhaps affection: perhaps he was touched by the change which the illness of the last weeks had wrought in his aunt.", "어쩌면 그를 움직인 것은 잇속이었는지도 모르고, 어쩌면 애정이었는지도 모르며, 어쩌면 그는 지난 몇 주의 병환이 고모에게 일으킨 변화에 마음이 동했는지도 몰랐다."]], [["\"The old girl has always acted like a trump to me,\"", "\"노부인이 늘 나한테 더없이 잘해 주셨거든.\""], ["he said to his wife, as he narrated the interview, \"and I felt, you know, rather queer, and that sort of thing.", "그가 그 만남을 들려주며 아내에게 말했다. \"그래서, 그게 말이지, 어쩐지 좀 묘한 기분이 들고, 뭐 그런 거였어."], ["I walked by the side of the what-dy'e-call-'em, you know, and to her own door, where Bowls came to help her in.", "나는 그 뭐냐 하는 거 옆에서 같이 걸었지, 알지, 그 집 문 앞까지 말이야. 거기서 볼스가 나와서 고모님을 부축해 들였고."], ["And I wanted to go in very much, only--\"", "나도 정말 들어가고 싶었는데, 다만—\""]], [["\"YOU DIDN'T GO IN, Rawdon!\"", "\"안 들어갔단 말이에요, 로던!\""], ["screamed his wife.", "그의 아내가 비명을 질렀다."]], [["\"No, my dear; I'm hanged if I wasn't afraid when it came to the point.\"", "\"그래, 여보. 막상 그 순간이 되니까 겁이 나더라고, 빌어먹을.\""]], [["\"You fool!", "\"이 바보!"], ["you ought to have gone in, and never come out again,\"", "들어가서 다시는 안 나왔어야죠.\""], ["Rebecca said.", "레베카가 말했다."]], [["\"Don't call me names,\"", "\"나한테 욕하지 마.\""], ["said the big Guardsman, sulkily.", "덩치 큰 근위병이 시무룩하게 말했다."], ["\"Perhaps I WAS a fool, Becky, but you shouldn't say so\"; and he gave his wife a look, such as his countenance could wear when angered, and such as was not pleasant to face.", "\"어쩌면 내가 바보였는지도 모르지, 베키, 하지만 당신이 그렇게 말하면 안 되지.\" 그러고는 그가 화났을 때 지을 법한, 마주하기에 그리 유쾌하지 않은 그런 눈초리로 아내를 쏘아보았다."]], [["\"Well, dearest, to-morrow you must be on the look-out, and go and see her, mind, whether she asks you or no,\"", "\"자, 여보, 내일은 잘 살피고 있다가 가서 고모님을 뵈요. 그분이 부르든 안 부르든, 명심해요.\""], ["Rebecca said, trying to soothe her angry yoke-mate.", "레베카가 성난 멍에짝을 달래려 애쓰며 말했다."], ["On which he replied, that he would do exactly as he liked, and would just thank her to keep a civil tongue in her head--and the wounded husband went away, and passed the forenoon at the billiard-room, sulky, silent, and suspicious.", "그러자 그는 자기 좋을 대로 하겠노라고, 그리고 입조심이나 좀 해 주면 고맙겠다고 대꾸했다. 상처 입은 남편은 자리를 떠나, 시무룩하고 말없이 의심에 잠긴 채 당구장에서 오전을 보냈다."]], [["But before the night was over he was compelled to give in, and own, as usual, to his wife's superior prudence and foresight, by the most melancholy confirmation of the presentiments which she had regarding the consequences of the mistake which he had made.", "그러나 밤이 다 가기도 전에 그는 굴복하지 않을 수 없었고, 여느 때처럼 아내의 우월한 분별과 선견지명을 인정하지 않을 수 없었으니, 자기가 저지른 실수의 결과에 관해 그녀가 품었던 예감이 더없이 음울하게 확인되었기 때문이다."], ["Miss Crawley must have had some emotion upon seeing him and shaking hands with him after so long a rupture.", "크롤리 양은 그토록 오랜 절교 끝에 그를 보고 악수를 하면서 어떤 감정을 느꼈음에 틀림없었다."], ["She mused upon the meeting a considerable time.", "그녀는 그 만남을 한참 곱씹었다."], ["\"Rawdon is getting very fat and old, Briggs,\"", "\"로던이 아주 살이 찌고 늙었더구나, 브리그스.\""], ["she said to her companion.", "그녀가 말동무에게 말했다."], ["\"His nose has become red, and he is exceedingly coarse in appearance.", "\"코는 빨개졌고, 외모가 지독히 천박해졌어."], ["His marriage to that woman has hopelessly vulgarised him.", "그 여자와 결혼하더니 가망 없이 상스러워졌어."], ["Mrs. Bute always said they drank together; and I have no doubt they do.", "뷰트 부인이 늘 그 둘이 함께 술을 마신다고 했는데, 정말 그러는 게 틀림없어."], ["Yes: he smelt of gin abominably.", "그래, 그 애한테서 진 냄새가 지독하게 났어."], ["I remarked it.", "내가 알아챘다."], ["Didn't you?\"", "너도 못 맡았느냐?\""]], [["In vain Briggs interposed that Mrs. Bute spoke ill of everybody: and, as far as a person in her humble position could judge, was an--", "뷰트 부인은 누구에 대해서나 나쁘게 말한다고, 그리고 자기처럼 미천한 처지의 사람이 판단할 수 있는 한 그 부인이야말로—이라고 브리그스가 끼어들었으나 헛수고였다."]], [["\"An artful designing woman?", "\"음흉하고 책략을 꾸미는 여자라고?"], ["Yes, so she is, and she does speak ill of every one--but I am certain that woman has made Rawdon drink.", "그래, 그 여자가 그렇지, 누구에 대해서나 나쁘게 말하고. 하지만 그 여자가 로던을 술꾼으로 만들었다는 건 내가 확신해."], ["All those low people do--\"", "그런 천한 사람들은 죄다—\""]], [["\"He was very much affected at seeing you, ma'am,\"", "\"그분은 마님을 뵙고 무척 감동하셨어요, 마님.\""], ["the companion said; \"and I am sure, when you remember that he is going to the field of danger--\"", "말동무가 말했다. \"그리고 그분이 위험천만한 전쟁터로 떠나신다는 걸 떠올리시면, 정말이지—\""]], [["\"How much money has he promised you, Briggs?\"", "\"그 애가 너한테 돈을 얼마나 약속하더냐, 브리그스?\""], ["the old spinster cried out, working herself into a nervous rage--\"there now, of course you begin to cry.", "늙은 노처녀가 신경질적인 노여움에 휩싸여 외쳤다. \"거봐라, 또 울기 시작하는구나."], ["I hate scenes.", "난 소동이 질색이야."], ["Why am I always to be worried?", "어째서 난 늘 이렇게 시달려야 하는 거지?"], ["Go and cry up in your own room, and send Firkin to me--no, stop, sit down and blow your nose, and leave off crying, and write a letter to Captain Crawley.\"", "네 방에 올라가서 울고, 퍼킨을 보내라. 아니, 잠깐, 앉아서 코를 풀고, 그만 울고, 크롤리 대위한테 편지나 써라.\""], ["Poor Briggs went and placed herself obediently at the writing-book.", "가엾은 브리그스는 가서 고분고분 필기책 앞에 자리를 잡았다."], ["Its leaves were blotted all over with relics of the firm, strong, rapid handwriting of the spinster's late amanuensis, Mrs. Bute Crawley.", "그 낱장들은 온통, 노처녀의 작고한 필경사였던 뷰트 크롤리 부인의 굳세고 힘차고 날쌘 필체의 흔적으로 얼룩져 있었다."]], [["\"Begin 'My dear sir,' or 'Dear sir,'", "\"'친애하는 귀하'라고 시작하거나, 아니 '귀하'가 낫겠다, 그렇게 시작해서, 크롤리 양께서, 아니, 크롤리 양의 주치의이신 크리머 씨께서 분부하시기를, 내 건강이 이러이러하여 지금의 이 미묘한 상태에서는 어떤 격한 감정도 위험할 수 있으니, 어떠한 집안 논의나 면담도 사절할 수밖에 없다고 적어라."], ["that will be better, and say you are desired by Miss Crawley--no, by Miss Crawley's medical man, by Mr.", "그리고 브라이턴까지 와 준 데 대해 감사하고, 어쩌고저쩌고, 나 때문에 더 이상 머물지 말아 달라고 청해라."], ["Creamer, to state that my health is such that all strong emotions would be dangerous in my present delicate condition--and that I must decline any family discussions or interviews whatever.", "그리고 브리그스, 그가 부디 좋은 여행 되기를 바라며, 만약 수고스럽게 그레이즈 인 광장에 있는 내 변호사를 찾아간다면 거기서 그에게 전할 연락이 있을 것이라고 덧붙여도 좋다."], ["And thank him for coming to Brighton, and so forth, and beg him not to stay any longer on my account. And, Miss Briggs, you may add that I wish him a bon voyage, and that if he will take the trouble to call upon my lawyer's in Gray's Inn Square, he will find there a communication for him.", "그래, 그러면 되겠다."], ["Yes, that will do; and that will make him leave Brighton.\"", "그러면 그 애가 브라이턴을 떠날 게야.\""], ["The benevolent Briggs penned this sentence with the utmost satisfaction.", "인정 많은 브리그스는 이 문장을 더없이 흐뭇한 마음으로 적어 넣었다."]], [["\"To seize upon me the very day after Mrs. Bute was gone,\"", "\"뷰트 부인이 떠난 바로 그 이튿날 나를 덮치다니,\""], ["the old lady prattled on; \"it was too indecent.", "노부인이 계속 재잘거렸다. \"너무 염치없는 짓이었어."], ["Briggs, my dear, write to Mrs. Crawley, and say SHE needn't come back.", "브리그스, 얘야, 크롤리 부인한테 편지를 써서, 그 여자는 돌아올 필요 없다고 일러라."], ["No--she needn't--and she shan't--and I won't be a slave in my own house--and I won't be starved and choked with poison.", "아니, 돌아올 필요 없어, 돌아와서도 안 되고, 나는 내 집에서 노예 노릇은 하지 않을 거야, 굶주리고 독약에 목이 메는 일도 없을 거야."], ["They all want to kill me--all--all\"--and with this the lonely old woman burst into a scream of hysterical tears.", "다들 나를 죽이고 싶어 해, 다들, 다들.\" 그러더니 이 외로운 노파는 발작적인 눈물의 비명을 터뜨렸다."]], [["The last scene of her dismal Vanity Fair comedy was fast approaching; the tawdry lamps were going out one by one; and the dark curtain was almost ready to descend.", "그녀의 음울한 허영의 시장 희극의 마지막 장면이 빠르게 다가오고 있었다. 번지르르한 등불이 하나둘 꺼져 가고 있었고, 어두운 막이 이제 막 내려올 채비를 하고 있었다."]], [["That final paragraph, which referred Rawdon to Miss Crawley's solicitor in London, and which Briggs had written so good-naturedly, consoled the dragoon and his wife somewhat, after their first blank disappointment, on reading the spinster's refusal of a reconciliation.", "로던을 런던의 크롤리 양 변호사에게 보내라고 일러 둔, 브리그스가 그토록 인정스럽게 적어 넣은 그 마지막 단락은, 노처녀가 화해를 거절했다는 것을 읽고 처음의 막막한 실망에 빠진 용기병과 그 아내를 다소나마 위로해 주었다."], ["And it effected the purpose for which the old lady had caused it to be written, by making Rawdon very eager to get to London.", "그리고 그것은 노부인이 그것을 쓰게 한 본디 목적을 달성했으니, 로던으로 하여금 런던에 가고 싶어 안달하게 만들었던 것이다."]], [["Out of Jos's losings and George Osborne's bank-notes, he paid his bill at the inn, the landlord whereof does not probably know to this day how doubtfully his account once stood.", "조스가 잃은 돈과 조지 오스본의 은행권으로, 그는 여관 계산서를 치렀다. 그 여관 주인은 십중팔구 자기 외상값이 한때 얼마나 위태로웠는지를 오늘날까지도 모를 것이다."], ["For, as a general sends his baggage to the rear before an action, Rebecca had wisely packed up all their chief valuables and sent them off under care of George's servant, who went in charge of the trunks on the coach back to London.", "장군이 전투에 앞서 짐을 후방으로 보내듯, 레베카는 현명하게도 그들의 주요 귀중품을 죄다 꾸려 조지의 하인에게 맡겨 보냈고, 그 하인은 트렁크들을 책임지고 마차에 실어 런던으로 돌아갔다."], ["Rawdon and his wife returned by the same conveyance next day.", "로던과 그 아내는 이튿날 같은 탈것을 타고 돌아왔다."]], [["\"I should have liked to see the old girl before we went,\"", "\"떠나기 전에 그 노부인을 한번 봤으면 좋았을 텐데.\""], ["Rawdon said.", "로던이 말했다."], ["\"She looks so cut up and altered that I'm sure she can't last long.", "\"어찌나 수척하고 변하셨는지, 분명 오래 못 사실 거야."], ["I wonder what sort of a cheque I shall have at Waxy's.", "왁시한테서 어떤 수표를 받게 될지 궁금하군."], ["Two hundred--it can't be less than two hundred--hey, Becky?\"", "이백 파운드, 이백보다 적지는 않겠지, 응, 베키?\""]], [["In consequence of the repeated visits of the aides-de-camp of the Sheriff of Middlesex, Rawdon and his wife did not go back to their lodgings at Brompton, but put up at an inn.", "미들섹스 집행관의 부관들이 거듭 찾아온 탓에, 로던과 그 아내는 브롬프턴의 숙소로 돌아가지 않고 여관에 묵었다."], ["Early the next morning, Rebecca had an opportunity of seeing them as she skirted that suburb on her road to old Mrs. Sedley's house at Fulham, whither she went to look for her dear Amelia and her Brighton friends.", "이튿날 아침 일찍, 레베카는 풀럼에 있는 늙은 세들리 부인의 집으로 가는 길에 그 변두리를 지나치다가 그들을 볼 기회가 있었는데, 그녀는 더없이 소중한 애밀리아와 브라이턴 친구들을 찾아가던 참이었다."], ["They were all off to Chatham, thence to Harwich, to take shipping for Belgium with the regiment--kind old Mrs.", "그들은 모두 채텀으로, 거기서 다시 하리치로 떠나, 연대와 함께 벨기에로 가는 배를 타려는 참이었다."], ["Sedley very much depressed and tearful, solitary.", "마음씨 고운 늙은 세들리 부인은 무척 풀이 죽고 눈물에 젖은 채 외로이 남아 있었다."], ["Returning from this visit, Rebecca found her husband, who had been off to Gray's Inn, and learnt his fate.", "이 방문에서 돌아온 레베카는, 그레이즈 인에 다녀와 자기 운명을 알게 된 남편을 발견했다."], ["He came back furious.", "그는 잔뜩 성이 나서 돌아와 있었다."]], [["\"By Jove, Becky,\"", "\"맙소사, 베키.\""], ["says he, \"she's only given me twenty pound!\"", "그가 말했다. \"고모님이 나한테 겨우 이십 파운드를 주셨어!\""]], [["Though it told against themselves, the joke was too good, and Becky burst out laughing at Rawdon's discomfiture.", "그것이 자기네에게 불리한 일이긴 했으나, 그 농담이 너무도 절묘했던지라, 베키는 로던의 낭패에 그만 폭소를 터뜨리고 말았다."]], [["Between London and Chatham\nOn quitting Brighton, our friend George, as became a person of rank and fashion travelling in a barouche with four horses, drove in state to a fine hotel in Cavendish Square, where a suite of splendid rooms, and a table magnificently furnished with plate and surrounded by a half-dozen of black and silent waiters, was ready to receive the young gentleman and his bride.", "브라이턴을 떠나면서, 우리의 친구 조지는 네 마리 말이 끄는 사륜마차로 여행하는 지위와 풍채를 갖춘 인물답게 위풍당당하게 캐번디시 광장의 어느 멋진 호텔로 향했다. 그곳에는 화려한 객실 한 채와, 은식기가 그득하고 말없이 시중드는 흑인 급사 대여섯이 둘러선 호화로운 식탁이, 그 젊은 신사와 신부를 맞이할 채비를 갖추고 있었다."], ["George did the honours of the place with a princely air to Jos and Dobbin; and Amelia, for the first time, and with exceeding shyness and timidity, presided at what George called her own table.", "조지는 조스와 도빈에게 마치 군주와도 같은 태도로 이 자리의 주인 노릇을 했고, 애밀리아는 처음으로, 그것도 더없는 수줍음과 소심함을 내비치며 조지가 \"그녀 자신의 식탁\"이라 부른 자리의 안주인 노릇을 했다."]], [["George pooh-poohed the wine and bullied the waiters royally, and Jos gobbled the turtle with immense satisfaction.", "조지는 와인을 두고 흠을 잡으며 급사들을 임금님처럼 들들 볶았고, 조스는 거북 요리를 한껏 흡족하게 게걸스레 먹어 치웠다. 도빈이 그에게 음식을 덜어 주었다."], ["Dobbin helped him to it; for the lady of the house, before whom the tureen was placed, was so ignorant of the contents, that she was going to help Mr. Sedley without bestowing upon him either calipash or calipee.", "수프 그릇이 놓인 앞자리의 안주인이 그 안에 무엇이 들었는지 도통 몰라서, 세들리 씨에게 등딱지 살도 배딱지 살도 떠 주지 않은 채 그냥 덜어 주려 했기 때문이다."]], [["The splendour of the entertainment, and the apartments in which it was given, alarmed Mr.", "* 등딱지 살(calipash)과 배딱지 살(calipee): 거북 요리에서 별미로 치는 부위."], ["Dobbin, who remonstrated after dinner, when Jos was asleep in the great chair.", "이 향응의 호사스러움과 그것이 베풀어진 객실의 화려함에 도빈 씨는 마음이 불안해져서, 식사 후 조스가 큼직한 안락의자에서 잠든 사이에 한마디 충고를 했다."], ["But in vain he cried out against the enormity of turtle and champagne that was fit for an archbishop.", "그러나 대주교에게나 어울릴 법한 거북 요리와 샴페인의 그 엄청남에 대고 항의해 본들 소용이 없었다."], ["\"I've always been accustomed to travel like a gentleman,\"", "\"난 늘 신사답게 여행하는 데 익숙했고,\" 조지가 말했다."], ["George said, \"and, damme, my wife shall travel like a lady.", "\"제기랄, 내 아내는 숙녀답게 여행할 거란 말이야."], ["As long as there's a shot in the locker, she shall want for nothing,\"", "주머니에 한 푼이라도 남아 있는 한, 내 아내한테는 아쉬운 게 하나도 없게 할 거라고.\""], ["said the generous fellow, quite pleased with himself for his magnificence of spirit.", "그 너그러운 친구는 자신의 통 큰 기개에 스스로 무척 흐뭇해하며 그렇게 말했다."], ["Nor did Dobbin try and convince him that Amelia's happiness was not centred in turtle-soup.", "도빈도 애밀리아의 행복이 거북 수프에 달려 있는 것은 아니라고 그를 설득하려 들지는 않았다."]], [["A while after dinner, Amelia timidly expressed a wish to go and see her mamma, at Fulham: which permission George granted her with some grumbling.", "식사가 끝나고 얼마쯤 지나, 애밀리아는 풀럼에 계신 어머니를 뵈러 가고 싶다고 수줍게 청을 했다. 조지는 다소 투덜대면서도 그 허락을 내어 주었다."], ["And she tripped away to her enormous bedroom, in the centre of which stood the enormous funereal bed, \"that the Emperor Halixander's sister slep in when the allied sufferings was here,\"", "그러자 그녀는 자신의 그 어마어마한 침실로 사뿐사뿐 달려갔다. 방 한가운데에는 어마어마한 영구대 같은 침대가 놓여 있었는데, \"황제 핼릭샌더 폐하의 누이께서 연합한 고난자들께서 여기 머무실 적에 주무신\""], ["and put on her little bonnet and shawl with the utmost eagerness and pleasure.", "침대라는 것이었다. 그녀는 더없는 열의와 기쁨으로 자그마한 보닛과 숄을 둘렀다."], ["George was still drinking claret when she returned to the dining-room, and made no signs of moving.", "그녀가 식당으로 돌아왔을 때 조지는 여전히 클라레를 마시며 일어설 기색도 보이지 않았다."], ["\"Ar'n't you coming with me, dearest?\"", "\"같이 안 가시는 거예요, 여보?\""], ["she asked him. No; the \"dearest\"", "그녀가 그에게 물었다."], ["had \"business\"", "안 갈 거였다."], ["that night. His man should get her a coach and go with her.", "\"여보\"께서는 그날 밤 \"볼일\"이 있으셨다."], ["And the coach being at the door of the hotel, Amelia made George a little disappointed curtsey after looking vainly into his face once or twice, and went sadly down the great staircase, Captain Dobbin after, who handed her into the vehicle, and saw it drive away to its destination.", "그의 하인이 마차를 잡아 그녀와 동행할 것이었다. 마차가 호텔 문 앞에 대령하자, 애밀리아는 그의 얼굴을 한두 번 부질없이 들여다본 뒤 다소 실망한 기색으로 조지에게 살짝 무릎을 굽혀 인사하고는 서글피 그 커다란 계단을 내려갔다."], ["The very valet was ashamed of mentioning the address to the hackney-coachman before the hotel waiters, and promised to instruct him when they got further on.", "도빈 대위가 그 뒤를 따라 그녀를 마차에 태워 주고, 마차가 목적지를 향해 떠나가는 것을 지켜보았다. 그 하인조차 호텔 급사들 앞에서 삯마차 마부에게 주소를 말하기가 부끄러워, 좀 더 가서 일러 주겠노라고 했다."]], [["Dobbin walked home to his old quarters and the Slaughters', thinking very likely that it would be delightful to be in that hackney-coach, along with Mrs.", "* 핼릭샌더 폐하, 연합한 고난자들: 알렉산드르 황제(러시아)와 동맹국(연합군)을 무식한 하녀가 잘못 발음한 것."], ["Osborne. George was evidently of quite a different taste; for when he had taken wine enough, he went off to half-price at the play, to see Mr.", "도빈은 옛 숙소인 슬로터스로 걸어서 돌아가면서, 오스본 부인과 함께 저 삯마차에 타고 있다면 얼마나 즐거울까, 하는 생각을 했을 법하다."], ["Kean perform in Shylock.", "조지는 분명 취향이 사뭇 달랐다."], ["Captain Osborne was a great lover of the drama, and had himself performed high-comedy characters with great distinction in several garrison theatrical entertainments.", "와인을 충분히 마시고 나자, 그는 케인 씨가 샤일록을 연기하는 것을 보러 반값 입장 시간에 맞춰 극장으로 떠나 버렸으니 말이다. 오스본 대위는 연극을 무척 좋아하는 사람이었고, 본인도 수비대의 여러 연극 공연에서 고급 희극의 배역을 빼어나게 연기한 적이 있었다."], ["Jos slept on until long after dark, when he woke up with a start at the motions of his servant, who was removing and emptying the decanters on the table; and the hackney-coach stand was again put into requisition for a carriage to convey this stout hero to his lodgings and bed.", "조스는 날이 한참 어두워진 뒤까지 계속 잠을 잤고, 식탁 위 유리병들을 치우고 비우던 하인의 손놀림에 화들짝 놀라 깨어났다. 그리하여 이 건장한 영웅을 숙소와 잠자리로 모셔 갈 마차를 부르느라 삯마차 정거장이 또 한 번 동원되었다."]], [["Mrs. Sedley, you may be sure, clasped her daughter to her heart with all maternal eagerness and affection, running out of the door as the carriage drew up before the little garden-gate, to welcome the weeping, trembling, young bride.", "세들리 부인이 온갖 모성의 열렬함과 애정으로 딸을 가슴에 끌어안았으리라는 것은 능히 짐작할 만하다."], ["Old Mr. Clapp, who was in his shirt-sleeves, trimming the garden-plot, shrank back alarmed.", "마차가 자그마한 정원 문 앞에 멈춰 서자 그녀는 문밖으로 달려 나와, 울먹이며 떨고 있는 젊은 신부를 맞이했다."], ["The Irish servant-lass rushed up from the kitchen and smiled a \"God bless you.\"", "셔츠 바람으로 정원 화단을 다듬고 있던 늙은 클랩 씨는 깜짝 놀라 뒤로 물러섰다. 아일랜드인 하녀는 부엌에서 뛰쳐나와 \"하느님의 가호가 있기를\""], ["Amelia could hardly walk along the flags and up the steps into the parlour.", "하고 미소 지었다. 애밀리아는 포석을 따라 걸어 계단을 올라 거실로 들어서기조차 힘에 부쳤다."]], [["How the floodgates were opened, and mother and daughter wept, when they were together embracing each other in this sanctuary, may readily be imagined by every reader who possesses the least sentimental turn.", "이 성소 같은 곳에서 함께 부둥켜안았을 때, 어머니와 딸의 눈물의 봇물이 어떻게 터졌는지는, 조금이라도 감상적인 기질을 지닌 독자라면 누구나 쉽게 그려 볼 수 있을 것이다."], ["When don't ladies weep?", "숙녀들이 울지 않는 때가 언제 있던가?"], ["At what occasion of joy, sorrow, or other business of life, and, after such an event as a marriage, mother and daughter were surely at liberty to give way to a sensibility which is as tender as it is refreshing.", "기쁨이든, 슬픔이든, 그 밖의 인생사 어느 대목에서든 말이다. 더구나 결혼 같은 사건을 치른 뒤라면, 어머니와 딸이 그 부드럽고도 후련한 감성에 마음껏 자신을 내맡길 자유가 분명 있는 법이다."], ["About a question of marriage I have seen women who hate each other kiss and cry together quite fondly.", "나는 서로를 미워하는 여자들이 결혼 문제를 두고는 무척이나 다정하게 입 맞추고 함께 우는 것을 본 적이 있다."], ["How much more do they feel when they love!", "하물며 서로 사랑하는 사이라면 얼마나 더 깊이 느끼겠는가!"], ["Good mothers are married over again at their daughters'", "착한 어머니들은 딸의 결혼식에서 자신이 다시 결혼하는 셈이다."], ["weddings: and as for subsequent events, who does not know how ultra-maternal grandmothers are?--in fact a woman, until she is a grandmother, does not often really know what to be a mother is.", "그리고 그 뒤에 일어나는 일들로 말하자면, 할머니들이 얼마나 극성스러운 모성을 발휘하는지 모르는 이가 어디 있겠는가? 사실 여자란 할머니가 되기 전까지는 어머니가 된다는 것이 무엇인지를 정말로는 잘 모르는 법이다."], ["Let us respect Amelia and her mamma whispering and whimpering and laughing and crying in the parlour and the twilight. Old Mr.", "거실에서, 황혼녘에 소곤거리고 훌쩍이고 웃고 우는 애밀리아와 그 어머니를 우리도 존중해 주기로 하자."], ["Sedley did. HE had not divined who was in the carriage when it drove up.", "늙은 세들리 씨가 그러했던 것처럼."], ["He had not flown out to meet his daughter, though he kissed her very warmly when she entered the room (where he was occupied, as usual, with his papers and tapes and statements of accounts), and after sitting with the mother and daughter for a short time, he very wisely left the little apartment in their possession.", "마차가 멈춰 섰을 때 그는 그 안에 누가 타고 있는지 미처 알아채지 못했다. 그는 딸을 맞으러 뛰쳐나가지는 않았으나, 딸이 방에 들어서자(그는 여느 때처럼 서류며 끈이며 계산서를 만지작거리고 있었다) 더없이 따뜻하게 입을 맞추었고, 잠시 모녀와 함께 앉아 있다가 매우 현명하게도 그 자그마한 방을 두 사람에게 내어 주고 자리를 떴다."]], [["George's valet was looking on in a very supercilious manner at Mr. Clapp in his shirt-sleeves, watering his rose-bushes.", "조지의 하인은 셔츠 바람으로 장미 덤불에 물을 주는 클랩 씨를 자못 거만한 태도로 지켜보고 있었다."], ["He took off his hat, however, with much condescension to Mr.", "그래도 그는 세들리 씨에게는 무척 황송해하며 모자를 벗어 인사했다."], ["Sedley, who asked news about his son-in-law, and about Jos's carriage, and whether his horses had been down to Brighton, and about that infernal traitor Bonaparty, and the war; until the Irish maid-servant came with a plate and a bottle of wine, from which the old gentleman insisted upon helping the valet.", "세들리 씨는 사위에 대해, 또 조스의 마차에 대해, 그의 말들이 브라이턴까지 내려갔는지, 또 저 빌어먹을 반역자 보나파르티와 전쟁에 대해 이런저런 소식을 물었다. 그러다 아일랜드인 하녀가 접시 하나와 와인 한 병을 들고 나오자, 노신사는 굳이 그 하인에게 술을 따라 주겠다고 고집을 부렸다."], ["He gave him a half-guinea too, which the servant pocketed with a mixture of wonder and contempt.", "그는 하인에게 반 기니까지 쥐여 주었고, 하인은 그것을 놀라움 반 경멸 반으로 주머니에 챙겨 넣었다."], ["\"To the health of your master and mistress, Trotter,\"", "\"자네 주인 내외의 건강을 위하여, 트로터.\" 세들리 씨가 말했다."], ["Mr. Sedley said, \"and here's something to drink your health when you get home, Trotter.\"", "\"그리고 이건 집에 돌아가거든 자네 건강을 위해 한잔하라고 주는 것일세, 트로터.\""]], [["There were but nine days past since Amelia had left that little cottage and home--and yet how far off the time seemed since she had bidden it farewell.", "애밀리아가 그 자그마한 오두막집을 떠난 지 겨우 아흐레가 지났을 뿐이건만, 그곳에 작별을 고하던 때가 어쩌면 이리도 까마득하게 느껴지던지."], ["What a gulf lay between her and that past life.", "그녀와 그 지난날 사이에 얼마나 깊은 심연이 가로놓여 있던가."], ["She could look back to it from her present standing-place, and contemplate, almost as another being, the young unmarried girl absorbed in her love, having no eyes but for one special object, receiving parental affection if not ungratefully, at least indifferently, and as if it were her due--her whole heart and thoughts bent on the accomplishment of one desire.", "그녀는 지금 서 있는 자리에서 그 시절을 돌아보며, 사랑에 푹 빠진 채 단 하나의 특별한 대상밖에 보이지 않던, 부모의 애정을 배은망덕하게는 아닐지언정 적어도 무심하게, 마치 당연한 제 몫인 양 받아들이던, 온 마음과 생각이 오로지 하나의 소망을 이루는 데 쏠려 있던 그 결혼 전의 처녀를 거의 딴사람을 보듯 바라볼 수 있었다."], ["The review of those days, so lately gone yet so far away, touched her with shame; and the aspect of the kind parents filled her with tender remorse.", "그토록 가까운 과거인데도 그토록 멀어진 그 시절을 돌이켜보니 부끄러움이 밀려왔고, 다정한 부모님의 모습에 애틋한 회한이 가득 차올랐다."], ["Was the prize gained--the heaven of life--and the winner still doubtful and unsatisfied?", "상은 손에 넣었건만, 그 인생의 천국을 거머쥐었건만, 그것을 차지한 이는 여전히 미심쩍어하고 만족하지 못하는 것이던가?"], ["As his hero and heroine pass the matrimonial barrier, the novelist generally drops the curtain, as if the drama were over then: the doubts and struggles of life ended: as if, once landed in the marriage country, all were green and pleasant there: and wife and husband had nothing to do but to link each other's arms together, and wander gently downwards towards old age in happy and perfect fruition.", "주인공 남녀가 결혼이라는 관문을 통과하면, 소설가는 으레 막을 내려 버리고 만다. 마치 그것으로 극이 끝나 버린 양, 인생의 의심과 투쟁이 막을 내린 양, 일단 결혼이라는 나라에 발을 들이고 나면 그곳은 온통 푸르고 즐겁기만 한 양, 아내와 남편은 그저 서로 팔짱을 끼고 행복하고 완벽한 결실 속에서 노년을 향해 다정히 내리막길을 거닐기만 하면 되는 양 말이다."], ["But our little Amelia was just on the bank of her new country, and was already looking anxiously back towards the sad friendly figures waving farewell to her across the stream, from the other distant shore.", "그러나 우리의 가엾은 애밀리아는 이제 막 그 새 나라의 강기슭에 발을 디뎠을 뿐이건만, 벌써부터 강 건너 저 멀리 다른 기슭에서 자신을 향해 작별의 손을 흔드는 슬프고 다정한 형상들을 불안하게 돌아보고 있었다."]], [["In honour of the young bride's arrival, her mother thought it necessary to prepare I don't know what festive entertainment, and after the first ebullition of talk, took leave of Mrs. George Osborne for a while, and dived down to the lower regions of the house to a sort of kitchen-parlour (occupied by Mr.", "젊은 신부의 도착을 축하하는 뜻에서, 그녀의 어머니는 무슨 잔치 같은 향응을 차려야겠다고 마음먹고는, 한바탕 이야기보따리를 풀어 놓은 뒤 조지 오스본 부인에게 잠시 양해를 구하고 집의 아래층 구역으로, 일종의 부엌 겸 거실로 내려갔다(그곳은 클랩 부부가 쓰는 방이었고, 저녁이면 설거지를 마치고 머리 마는 종이를 떼어 낸 아일랜드인 하녀 플래니건 양도 함께 썼다)."], ["and Mrs. Clapp, and in the evening, when her dishes were washed and her curl-papers removed, by Miss Flannigan, the Irish servant), there to take measures for the preparing of a magnificent ornamented tea.", "거기서 그녀는 멋들어지게 차린 차 자리를 준비할 방도를 강구하려는 것이었다."], ["All people have their ways of expressing kindness, and it seemed to Mrs. Sedley that a muffin and a quantity of orange marmalade spread out in a little cut-glass saucer would be peculiarly agreeable refreshments to Amelia in her most interesting situation.", "사람마다 다정함을 표현하는 저마다의 방식이 있는 법인데, 세들리 부인이 보기에는 머핀과, 자그마한 컷글라스 받침 접시에 듬뿍 펴 바른 오렌지 마멀레이드야말로, 더없이 흥미로운 처지에 놓인 애밀리아에게 각별히 흐뭇한 다과가 되리라 여겨졌던 것이다."]], [["While these delicacies were being transacted below, Amelia, leaving the drawing-room, walked upstairs and found herself, she scarce knew how, in the little room which she had occupied before her marriage, and in that very chair in which she had passed so many bitter hours.", "이런 진미들이 아래층에서 한창 마련되는 동안, 애밀리아는 거실을 빠져나와 위층으로 올라갔고, 어찌 된 영문인지도 모르게 결혼 전에 쓰던 그 자그마한 방, 그토록 숱한 쓰라린 시간을 보냈던 바로 그 의자에 와 있는 자신을 발견했다."], ["She sank back in its arms as if it were an old friend; and fell to thinking over the past week, and the life beyond it.", "그녀는 오랜 벗이라도 되는 양 그 의자의 팔걸이에 몸을 묻고, 지난 한 주와 그 너머의 인생을 곰곰이 되짚어 보기 시작했다."], ["Already to be looking sadly and vaguely back: always to be pining for something which, when obtained, brought doubt and sadness rather than pleasure; here was the lot of our poor little creature and harmless lost wanderer in the great struggling crowds of Vanity Fair.", "벌써부터 슬프고도 막연하게 과거를 돌아보고, 늘 무언가를 애타게 그리워하되, 막상 손에 넣고 보면 기쁨보다는 의심과 슬픔만 안겨 주는 것을 그리워하는 신세. 이것이 바로 허영의 시장의 그 거대하고 아등바등하는 군중 속에서 길 잃은, 죄 없는 방랑자, 우리의 가엾은 작은 존재의 운명이었던 것이다."]], [["Here she sate, and recalled to herself fondly that image of George to which she had knelt before marriage.", "그녀는 거기 앉아, 결혼 전에 무릎 꿇고 우러르던 조지의 그 모습을 애틋하게 떠올렸다."], ["Did she own to herself how different the real man was from that superb young hero whom she had worshipped?", "실제의 그 남자가, 자신이 숭배하던 그 늠름한 젊은 영웅과 얼마나 다른지를 그녀는 스스로 인정했을까?"], ["It requires many, many years--and a man must be very bad indeed--before a woman's pride and vanity will let her own to such a confession.", "여자의 자존심과 허영심이 그런 고백을 스스로에게 허락하기까지는 아주, 아주 오랜 세월이 걸리는 법이고, 또 남자가 정말이지 형편없는 작자여야만 하는 법이다."], ["Then Rebecca's twinkling green eyes and baleful smile lighted upon her, and filled her with dismay.", "이윽고 리베카의 반짝이는 초록빛 눈동자와 표독스러운 미소가 그녀의 머릿속에 떠올라 그녀를 낙담으로 가득 채웠다."], ["And so she sate for awhile indulging in her usual mood of selfish brooding, in that very listless melancholy attitude in which the honest maid-servant had found her, on the day when she brought up the letter in which George renewed his offer of marriage.", "그리하여 그녀는 한동안 평소의 이기적인 골똘함에 잠겨 거기 앉아 있었다. 조지가 청혼을 새로이 해 온 편지를 정직한 하녀가 들고 올라왔던 그날, 그 하녀가 그녀를 발견했을 때와 똑같은, 무기력하고 우울하기 짝이 없는 그 자세 그대로."]], [["She looked at the little white bed, which had been hers a few days before, and thought she would like to sleep in it that night, and wake, as formerly, with her mother smiling over her in the morning: Then she thought with terror of the great funereal damask pavilion in the vast and dingy state bedroom, which was awaiting her at the grand hotel in Cavendish Square.", "그녀는 며칠 전까지 자기 것이었던 그 자그마한 흰 침대를 바라보며, 오늘 밤은 저기서 자고, 예전처럼 아침에 어머니가 자기를 굽어보며 미소 짓는 가운데 깨어나면 좋겠다고 생각했다. 그러다 캐번디시 광장의 그 으리으리한 호텔에서 자기를 기다리고 있는, 그 광막하고 칙칙한 특실 침실의, 영구대 같은 커다란 다마스크 천막 침대를 떠올리고는 몸서리를 쳤다."], ["Dear little white bed!", "정다운 작은 흰 침대!"], ["how many a long night had she wept on its pillow!", "저 베개에 엎드려 얼마나 숱한 긴 밤을 울었던가!"], ["How she had despaired and hoped to die there; and now were not all her wishes accomplished, and the lover of whom she had despaired her own for ever?", "저기서 얼마나 절망하고, 또 차라리 죽어 버리기를 바랐던가. 그런데 이제 자신의 소망은 죄다 이루어지지 않았는가, 절망하며 단념했던 그 연인이 영영 자기 것이 되지 않았는가?"], ["Kind mother!", "자상한 어머니!"], ["how patiently and tenderly she had watched round that bed!", "그 침대맡을 얼마나 참을성 있게, 또 다정하게 지켜 주셨던가!"], ["She went and knelt down by the bedside; and there this wounded and timorous, but gentle and loving soul, sought for consolation, where as yet, it must be owned, our little girl had but seldom looked for it.", "그녀는 침대 곁으로 가 무릎을 꿇었다. 그리고 거기서, 상처 입고 겁에 질렸으나 온순하고 사랑스러운 이 영혼은, 솔직히 말해 우리의 어린 소녀가 여태껏 좀처럼 찾지 않았던 그곳에서 위안을 구했다."], ["Love had been her faith hitherto; and the sad, bleeding disappointed heart began to feel the want of another consoler.", "사랑이 지금껏 그녀의 신앙이었으나, 슬프고, 피 흘리고, 환멸에 빠진 마음은 비로소 또 다른 위로자가 절실함을 느끼기 시작했던 것이다."]], [["Have we a right to repeat or to overhear her prayers?", "우리에게 그녀의 기도를 옮겨 적거나 엿들을 권리가 있을까?"], ["These, brother, are secrets, and out of the domain of Vanity Fair, in which our story lies.", "형제여, 이것들은 비밀이며, 우리 이야기가 놓여 있는 허영의 시장의 영역 밖에 있는 것이다."]], [["But this may be said, that when the tea was finally announced, our young lady came downstairs a great deal more cheerful; that she did not despond, or deplore her fate, or think about George's coldness, or Rebecca's eyes, as she had been wont to do of late.", "그러나 이 말만은 해 둘 수 있겠다. 마침내 차가 준비되었다는 전갈이 오자, 우리의 젊은 숙녀는 한결 명랑해진 모습으로 아래층에 내려왔다는 것을."], ["She went downstairs, and kissed her father and mother, and talked to the old gentleman, and made him more merry than he had been for many a day.", "요즈음 곧잘 그러던 것처럼 풀이 죽거나, 자신의 신세를 한탄하거나, 조지의 냉담함이나 리베카의 눈동자를 떠올리지 않았다는 것을."], ["She sate down at the piano which Dobbin had bought for her, and sang over all her father's favourite old songs.", "그녀는 아래층으로 내려가 아버지와 어머니에게 입을 맞추고, 노신사와 이야기를 나누며 그를 여러 날 만에 처음으로 흥겹게 해 드렸다."], ["She pronounced the tea to be excellent, and praised the exquisite taste in which the marmalade was arranged in the saucers.", "그녀는 도빈이 사 준 피아노 앞에 앉아 아버지가 좋아하는 옛 노래들을 죄다 불러 드렸다. 그녀는 차가 일품이라고 단언하고, 마멀레이드를 받침 접시에 담아낸 그 빼어난 솜씨를 칭찬했다."], ["And in determining to make everybody else happy, she found herself so; and was sound asleep in the great funereal pavilion, and only woke up with a smile when George arrived from the theatre.", "그리하여 다른 사람들을 모두 행복하게 해 주리라 마음먹는 가운데, 그녀 자신이 그렇게 행복해진 것을 깨달았다. 그러고는 그 영구대 같은 커다란 천막 침대에서 곤히 잠들었다가, 조지가 극장에서 돌아왔을 때에야 비로소 미소를 지으며 잠에서 깨어났다."]], [["For the next day, George had more important \"business\" to transact than that which took him to see Mr.", "이튿날, 조지에게는 케인 씨가 샤일록을 연기하는 것을 보러 가는 것보다 더 중대한 \"볼일\"이 있었다."], ["Kean in Shylock. Immediately on his arrival in London he had written off to his father's solicitors, signifying his royal pleasure that an interview should take place between them on the morrow.", "런던에 도착하자마자 그는 부친의 변호사들에게 편지를 띄워, 이튿날 그들 사이에 면담이 이루어지기를 바란다는 자신의 지엄하신 뜻을 전했던 것이다."], ["His hotel bill, losses at billiards and cards to Captain Crawley had almost drained the young man's purse, which wanted replenishing before he set out on his travels, and he had no resource but to infringe upon the two thousand pounds which the attorneys were commissioned to pay over to him.", "호텔 숙박비에다, 당구와 카드로 크롤리 대위에게 잃은 돈으로 그 젊은이의 지갑은 거의 바닥이 난 터라, 여행길에 오르기 전에 채워 넣어야 했고, 변호사들이 그에게 지급하도록 위임받은 이천 파운드에 손을 대는 것 말고는 달리 방도가 없었다."], ["He had a perfect belief in his own mind that his father would relent before very long.", "그는 마음속으로 머지않아 아버지가 누그러지리라고 철석같이 믿고 있었다."], ["How could any parent be obdurate for a length of time against such a paragon as he was?", "자기처럼 더없이 훌륭한 본보기 같은 자식에게 어느 부모가 오래도록 모질게 굴 수 있단 말인가?"], ["If his mere past and personal merits did not succeed in mollifying his father, George determined that he would distinguish himself so prodigiously in the ensuing campaign that the old gentleman must give in to him.", "만약 그의 과거 행적과 개인적 미덕만으로 아버지의 마음을 누그러뜨리지 못한다면, 다가올 전쟁에서 어찌나 굉장하게 공을 세울 작정인지, 그러면 노신사도 그에게 굴복하고 말 것이었다."], ["And if not?", "그래도 안 되면?"], ["Bah!", "흥!"], ["the world was before him.", "세상은 그의 앞에 활짝 펼쳐져 있었다."], ["His luck might change at cards, and there was a deal of spending in two thousand pounds.", "카드 운이 트일 수도 있었고, 이천 파운드면 쓸 만큼 쓸 수 있는 큰돈이었다."]], [["So he sent off Amelia once more in a carriage to her mamma, with strict orders and carte blanche to the two ladies to purchase everything requisite for a lady of Mrs. George Osborne's fashion, who was going on a foreign tour.", "그리하여 그는 애밀리아를 다시 한번 마차에 태워 어머니에게로 보내면서, 두 부인에게 외국 여행을 떠나는 조지 오스본 부인 정도 되는 숙녀에게 필요한 것은 무엇이든 다 사 오라고 엄히 일러 백지 위임장을 내주었다."], ["They had but one day to complete the outfit, and it may be imagined that their business therefore occupied them pretty fully.", "채비를 갖출 시간이라고는 단 하루뿐이었으니, 따라서 두 사람이 그 일에 꽤나 바쁘게 매달렸으리라는 것은 능히 짐작이 갈 것이다."], ["In a carriage once more, bustling about from milliner to linen-draper, escorted back to the carriage by obsequious shopmen or polite owners, Mrs. Sedley was herself again almost, and sincerely happy for the first time since their misfortunes.", "다시 한번 마차에 올라, 모자 가게에서 포목점으로 분주히 돌아다니며, 굽실거리는 점원이나 정중한 주인들의 배웅을 받으며 마차로 돌아오니, 세들리 부인은 거의 예전의 자기 모습으로 돌아와, 가세가 기운 이래 처음으로 진심으로 행복해했다."], ["Nor was Mrs. Amelia at all above the pleasure of shopping, and bargaining, and seeing and buying pretty things.", "그렇다고 애밀리아 부인이 물건을 사고, 값을 흥정하고, 예쁜 것들을 구경하고 사들이는 즐거움을 마다할 만큼 도도한 것도 아니었다(아무리 철학자연하는 사내라도, 그런 것을 마다하는 여자에게 단돈 이 펜스라도 내려 하겠는가?)."], ["(Would any man, the most philosophic, give twopence for a woman who was?) She gave herself a little treat, obedient to her husband's orders, and purchased a quantity of lady's gear, showing a great deal of taste and elegant discernment, as all the shopfolks said.", "그녀는 남편의 분부에 따라 자신에게 자그마한 호사를 베풀어, 숱한 숙녀용 물품을 사들였는데, 모든 점원의 말마따나 빼어난 취향과 우아한 안목을 한껏 드러냈다."]], [["And about the war that was ensuing, Mrs.", "그리고 막 닥쳐올 전쟁에 대해서는, 오스본 부인은 그다지 걱정하지 않았다."], ["Osborne was not much alarmed; Bonaparty was to be crushed almost without a struggle.", "보나파르티는 거의 싸움다운 싸움도 없이 분쇄되고 말 것이었다."], ["Margate packets were sailing every day, filled with men of fashion and ladies of note, on their way to Brussels and Ghent.", "마게이트행 정기선들은 날마다 출항했고, 브뤼셀과 헨트로 향하는 멋쟁이 신사들과 명망 있는 숙녀들로 가득했다."], ["People were going not so much to a war as to a fashionable tour.", "사람들이 가는 것은 전쟁터라기보다 유행을 좇는 관광 여행이라 해야 옳았다."], ["The newspapers laughed the wretched upstart and swindler to scorn.", "신문들은 그 가련한 벼락출세자 사기꾼을 비웃으며 조롱했다."], ["Such a Corsican wretch as that withstand the armies of Europe and the genius of the immortal Wellington!", "저따위 코르시카 출신 비렁뱅이가 유럽의 군대들과 불멸의 웰링턴의 천재성에 맞선다니!"], ["Amelia held him in utter contempt; for it needs not to be said that this soft and gentle creature took her opinions from those people who surrounded her, such fidelity being much too humble-minded to think for itself.", "애밀리아는 그를 철저히 경멸했다. 굳이 말할 것도 없이, 이 여리고 온순한 존재는 자기를 둘러싼 사람들에게서 의견을 그대로 받아들였으니, 그 충실함이란 스스로 사고하기에는 너무나 겸손한 것이었다."], ["Well, in a word, she and her mother performed a great day's shopping, and she acquitted herself with considerable liveliness and credit on this her first appearance in the genteel world of London.", "어쨌든, 한마디로 그녀는 어머니와 함께 하루 종일 굉장한 쇼핑을 해냈고, 런던의 고상한 사교계에 처음 모습을 드러내는 이 자리를 상당한 활기와 평판 속에 무사히 치러 냈다."]], [["George meanwhile, with his hat on one side, his elbows squared, and his swaggering martial air, made for Bedford Row, and stalked into the attorney's offices as if he was lord of every pale-faced clerk who was scribbling there. He ordered somebody to inform Mr.", "그러는 동안 조지는 모자를 비스듬히 눌러쓰고, 두 팔꿈치를 네모지게 벌린 채, 으스대는 군인다운 풍채로 베드퍼드 거리를 향해, 거기서 끼적거리고 있는 핏기 없는 서기들 하나하나가 모두 제 종이라도 되는 양 변호사 사무실로 거들먹거리며 들어섰다."], ["Higgs that Captain Osborne was waiting, in a fierce and patronizing way, as if the pekin of an attorney, who had thrice his brains, fifty times his money, and a thousand times his experience, was a wretched underling who should instantly leave all his business in life to attend on the Captain's pleasure.", "그는 누군가에게 오스본 대위가 기다린다고 히그스 씨에게 전하라고, 사납고도 거들먹거리는 태도로 명령했다. 마치 그 자기보다 머리가 세 배는 좋고, 돈은 쉰 배는 많고, 경험은 천 배는 많은 한낱 변호사 따위가, 당장 제 모든 일을 팽개치고 대위의 비위나 맞춰야 할 가련한 아랫것이라도 되는 양 말이다."], ["He did not see the sneer of contempt which passed all round the room, from the first clerk to the articled gents, from the articled gents to the ragged writers and white-faced runners, in clothes too tight for them, as he sate there tapping his boot with his cane, and thinking what a parcel of miserable poor devils these were.", "그가 거기 앉아 지팡이로 장화를 톡톡 두드리며 이자들은 참으로 비참하고 가련한 위인들이로구나, 하고 생각하는 사이, 수석 서기에서 견습 사무원에게로, 견습 사무원에서 몸에 너무 끼는 옷을 걸친 누더기 차림의 필경사들과 핏기 없는 잔심부름꾼들에게로, 방 안을 두루 돌며 번지던 그 경멸의 비웃음을 그는 알아차리지 못했다."], ["The miserable poor devils knew all about his affairs.", "그 비참하고 가련한 위인들은 그의 사정을 죄다 알고 있었다."], ["They talked about them over their pints of beer at their public-house clubs to other clerks of a night.", "그들은 밤이면 단골 술집 모임에서 맥주잔을 기울이며 다른 사무실 서기들에게 그 이야기를 떠벌렸다."], ["Ye gods, what do not attorneys and attorneys' clerks know in London!", "오, 신들이시여, 런던의 변호사들과 변호사 사무실 서기들이 모르는 것이 무엇이 있단 말인가!"], ["Nothing is hidden from their inquisition, and their families mutely rule our city.", "그들의 캐묻는 눈을 피할 수 있는 것은 아무것도 없으며, 그들의 식솔들이 말없이 이 도시를 지배하고 있는 것이다."]], [["Perhaps George expected, when he entered Mr.", "어쩌면 조지는 히그스 씨의 방에 들어서면서, 그 신사가 아버지로부터 무슨 타협이나 화해의 전갈을 전하라는 부탁이라도 받았으리라 기대했는지도 모른다."], ["Higgs's apartment, to find that gentleman commissioned to give him some message of compromise or conciliation from his father; perhaps his haughty and cold demeanour was adopted as a sign of his spirit and resolution: but if so, his fierceness was met by a chilling coolness and indifference on the attorney's part, that rendered swaggering absurd.", "어쩌면 그의 그 오만하고 차가운 태도는 자신의 기개와 결의를 내보이려는 표시였는지도 모른다. 그러나 설사 그렇다 한들, 그의 그 사나운 기세는 변호사 쪽의 싸늘한 냉정함과 무관심에 부딪혀 으스대는 짓이 무색해지고 말았다."], ["He pretended to be writing at a paper, when the Captain entered.", "대위가 들어서자 그는 무슨 서류에 글을 쓰는 척했다. \"앉으시지요, 선생.\""], ["\"Pray, sit down, sir,\"", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, \"and I will attend to your little affair in a moment. Mr.", "\"선생의 그 자그마한 용건은 곧 처리해 드리겠습니다."], ["Poe, get the release papers, if you please\"; and then he fell to writing again.", "포 군, 미안하지만 해제 서류를 가져오게.\" 그러고는 다시 글쓰기에 몰두했다."]], [["Poe having produced those papers, his chief calculated the amount of two thousand pounds stock at the rate of the day; and asked Captain Osborne whether he would take the sum in a cheque upon the bankers, or whether he should direct the latter to purchase stock to that amount.", "포가 그 서류들을 가져오자, 그 우두머리는 이천 파운드어치 증권을 그날 시세로 계산해 보고는, 오스본 대위에게 그 금액을 은행 앞으로 발행한 수표로 받겠느냐, 아니면 은행에 그 액수만큼 증권을 매입하라고 지시할까 하고 물었다."], ["\"One of the late Mrs. Osborne's trustees is out of town,\"", "\"고인이 되신 오스본 부인의 신탁 관리인 한 분이 지금 출타 중입니다만,\""], ["he said indifferently, \"but my client wishes to meet your wishes, and have done with the business as quick as possible.\"", "그는 무심하게 말했다. \"제 의뢰인께서는 선생의 뜻에 따라 이 일을 되도록 빨리 마무리 짓기를 바라십니다.\""]], [["\"Give me a cheque, sir,\"", "\"수표로 주시오, 선생.\""], ["said the Captain very surlily.", "대위가 무척 퉁명스럽게 말했다."], ["\"Damn the shillings and halfpence, sir,\"", "\"실링이고 반 페니고 다 집어치우시오, 선생.\""], ["he added, as the lawyer was making out the amount of the draft; and, flattering himself that by this stroke of magnanimity he had put the old quiz to the blush, he stalked out of the office with the paper in his pocket.", "변호사가 어음 액수를 적어 내려가던 참에 그가 덧붙였다. 그리고 이 한 번의 통 큰 처사로 저 늙은 익살꾼을 무안하게 만들었다고 혼자 우쭐해하며, 그는 수표를 주머니에 챙겨 넣고 사무실을 거들먹거리며 나섰다."]], [["\"That chap will be in gaol in two years,\" Mr.", "\"저 친구는 이 년 안에 감옥에 가 있을 걸세.\""], ["Higgs said to Mr. Poe.", "히그스 씨가 포 씨에게 말했다."]], [["\"Won't O. come round, sir, don't you think?\"", "\"오스본 씨가 마음을 돌리지 않을까요, 선생, 그렇게 생각하지 않으세요?\""]], [["\"Won't the monument come round,\"", "\"기념비가 걸어 돌아오겠는가.\""], ["Mr. Higgs replied.", "히그스 씨가 대꾸했다."]], [["\"He's going it pretty fast,\"", "\"꽤나 빨리 거덜 내고 있네요.\""], ["said the clerk.", "서기가 말했다."], ["\"He's only married a week, and I saw him and some other military chaps handing Mrs. Highflyer to her carriage after the play.\"", "\"결혼한 지 겨우 일주일인데, 며칠 전엔 그자가 다른 군인 몇이랑 같이 연극이 끝난 뒤 하이플라이어 부인을 마차로 모시는 걸 봤다니까요.\""], ["And then another case was called, and Mr. George Osborne thenceforth dismissed from these worthy gentlemen's memory.", "그러고 나서 또 다른 사건이 호명되었고, 조지 오스본 씨는 그 길로 이 훌륭한 신사분들의 기억에서 깨끗이 잊혔다."]], [["The draft was upon our friends Hulker and Bullock of Lombard Street, to whose house, still thinking he was doing business, George bent his way, and from whom he received his money.", "그 수표는 우리의 친구 롬바드 거리의 헐커 앤드 불록 은행 앞으로 발행된 것이었으니, 조지는 여전히 사업이랍시고 그 집으로 발길을 옮겼고, 거기서 돈을 받았다."], ["Frederick Bullock, Esq., whose yellow face was over a ledger, at which sate a demure clerk, happened to be in the banking-room when George entered.", "누런 얼굴을 장부 위에 박고 있던 프레더릭 불록 씨는—그 곁에는 얌전한 서기 하나가 앉아 있었다—마침 조지가 들어섰을 때 영업실에 있었다."], ["His yellow face turned to a more deadly colour when he saw the Captain, and he slunk back guiltily into the inmost parlour.", "대위를 보자 그의 누런 얼굴은 더욱 송장 같은 빛으로 변했고, 그는 죄지은 사람처럼 안쪽 응접실로 슬그머니 몸을 숨겼다."], ["George was too busy gloating over the money (for he had never had such a sum before), to mark the countenance or flight of the cadaverous suitor of his sister.", "조지는 그 돈을 흐뭇하게 들여다보느라(그토록 큰돈은 여태 가져 본 적이 없었으므로) 너무 바빠서, 제 누이를 노리던 그 송장 같은 구혼자의 표정도, 줄행랑도 미처 알아채지 못했다."]], [["Fred Bullock told old Osborne of his son's appearance and conduct.", "프레드 불록은 늙은 오스본에게 그 아들의 행색과 행실을 일러바쳤다."], ["\"He came in as bold as brass,\"", "\"어찌나 뻔뻔하게 들어오던지요.\""], ["said Frederick.", "프레더릭이 말했다."], ["\"He has drawn out every shilling.", "\"한 푼도 안 남기고 죄다 인출해 갔습니다."], ["How long will a few hundred pounds last such a chap as that?\"", "저런 작자한테 몇백 파운드가 얼마나 가겠습니까?\""], ["Osborne swore with a great oath that he little cared when or how soon he spent it.", "오스본은 그자가 그 돈을 언제 어떻게 빨리 써 버리든 알 바 아니라고 험한 욕을 섞어 가며 맹세하듯 내뱉었다."], ["Fred dined every day in Russell Square now.", "프레드는 이제 날마다 러셀 광장에서 식사를 했다."], ["But altogether, George was highly pleased with his day's business.", "어쨌든 통틀어 조지는 그날의 볼일에 무척 만족했다."], ["All his own baggage and outfit was put into a state of speedy preparation, and he paid Amelia's purchases with cheques on his agents, and with the splendour of a lord.", "그는 자기 짐과 여행 채비를 죄다 부랴부랴 갖추도록 했고, 애밀리아가 사들인 물건들의 값은 자기 대리인 앞으로 발행한 수표로, 그것도 귀족 같은 호기를 부리며 치러 주었다."]], [["In Which Amelia Joins Her Regiment\nWhen Jos's fine carriage drove up to the inn door at Chatham, the first face which Amelia recognized was the friendly countenance of Captain Dobbin, who had been pacing the street for an hour past in expectation of his friends'", "조스의 멋들어진 마차가 채텀의 여관 문 앞으로 들어섰을 때, 애밀리아가 가장 먼저 알아본 얼굴은 다정한 도빈 대위의 모습이었다."], ["arrival.", "그는 벗들이 도착하기를 기다리며 지난 한 시간 동안 거리를 서성이고 있던 참이었다."], ["The Captain, with shells on his frockcoat, and a crimson sash and sabre, presented a military appearance, which made Jos quite proud to be able to claim such an acquaintance, and the stout civilian hailed him with a cordiality very different from the reception which Jos vouchsafed to his friend in Brighton and Bond Street.", "프록코트에 견장을 달고 진홍색 장식띠를 두르고 군도를 찬 대위는 자못 군인다운 풍채를 풍겼고, 그 덕분에 조스는 이런 사람을 아는 사이라고 내세울 수 있다는 사실에 적잖이 우쭐해졌다. 그리하여 이 살집 좋은 민간인은, 브라이튼과 본드 거리에서 자기 친구를 맞이하던 때와는 사뭇 다른 정겨움으로 그를 반겼다."]], [["Along with the Captain was Ensign Stubble; who, as the barouche neared the inn, burst out with an exclamation of \"By Jove!", "대위와 함께 있던 사람은 소위 스터블이었다. 사륜마차가 여관에 가까워지자 그는 \"이런 세상에!"], ["what a pretty girl\"; highly applauding Osborne's choice.", "어쩌면 저렇게 예쁜 아가씨가!\" 하고 탄성을 터뜨리며 오스본의 안목을 한껏 칭송했다."], ["Indeed, Amelia dressed in her wedding-pelisse and pink ribbons, with a flush in her face, occasioned by rapid travel through the open air, looked so fresh and pretty, as fully to justify the Ensign's compliment.", "사실 애밀리아는 혼인 예복용 외투에 분홍 리본을 두르고, 탁 트인 공기를 가르며 빠르게 달려온 탓에 두 볼이 발그레 상기되어, 그토록 싱그럽고 어여뻐 보였으니 소위의 찬사도 충분히 받아 마땅했다."], ["Dobbin liked him for making it.", "도빈은 그가 그런 말을 해 준 것이 기뻤다."], ["As he stepped forward to help the lady out of the carriage, Stubble saw what a pretty little hand she gave him, and what a sweet pretty little foot came tripping down the step.", "그가 숙녀를 마차에서 내리도록 돕고자 앞으로 나서던 차에, 스터블은 그녀가 자기에게 내미는 작고 예쁜 손이며, 발판을 사뿐히 디디고 내려오는 더없이 곱고 작은 발을 보았다."], ["He blushed profusely, and made the very best bow of which he was capable; to which Amelia, seeing the number of the the regiment embroidered on the Ensign's cap, replied with a blushing smile, and a curtsey on her part; which finished the young Ensign on the spot.", "그는 얼굴이 온통 새빨개져서, 할 수 있는 한 최고의 인사를 올렸다. 그러자 애밀리아는 소위의 모자에 수놓인 연대 번호를 보고는, 발그레한 미소와 함께 답례로 무릎을 살짝 굽혀 인사를 했고, 그 한 방에 이 젊은 소위는 그 자리에서 완전히 넘어가 버렸다."], ["Dobbin took most kindly to Mr. Stubble from that day, and encouraged him to talk about Amelia in their private walks, and at each other's quarters.", "그날 이후로 도빈은 스터블 군을 무척이나 아끼게 되어, 단둘이 산책할 때나 서로의 숙소에서 애밀리아 이야기를 마음껏 하도록 부추겼다."], ["It became the fashion, indeed, among all the honest young fellows of the --th to adore and admire Mrs. Osborne.", "사실 ××연대의 정직한 젊은이들 사이에서는 오스본 부인을 흠모하고 떠받드는 것이 하나의 유행처럼 되었다."], ["Her simple artless behaviour, and modest kindness of demeanour, won all their unsophisticated hearts; all which simplicity and sweetness are quite impossible to describe in print.", "그녀의 꾸밈없고 천진한 행동거지와 겸손하고 다정한 태도가 그들의 순박한 마음을 모조리 사로잡았던 것이다. 그 모든 소박함과 사랑스러움이란 글로는 도무지 그려 낼 수 없는 것이다."], ["But who has not beheld these among women, and recognised the presence of all sorts of qualities in them, even though they say no more to you than that they are engaged to dance the next quadrille, or that it is very hot weather?", "그러나 여인들에게서 이런 모습을 보지 못한 이가 어디 있으며, 그 안에 온갖 미덕이 깃들어 있음을 알아보지 못한 이가 어디 있겠는가. 설령 그녀들이 당신에게 건넨 말이라곤 다음 카드리유 춤은 약속이 있다거나, 날씨가 무척 덥다는 것뿐이라 해도 말이다."], ["George, always the champion of his regiment, rose immensely in the opinion of the youth of the corps, by his gallantry in marrying this portionless young creature, and by his choice of such a pretty kind partner.", "늘 자기 연대의 자랑거리였던 조지는, 이 한 푼 없는 어린 처녀와 혼인하는 의협심을 발휘하고 또 이토록 어여쁘고 다정한 짝을 고른 덕분에, 부대 젊은이들 사이에서 평판이 한껏 치솟았다."]], [["In the sitting-room which was awaiting the travellers, Amelia, to her surprise, found a letter addressed to Mrs. Captain Osborne.", "길손들을 기다리고 있던 응접실에서, 애밀리아는 뜻밖에도 오스본 대위 부인 앞으로 온 편지 한 통을 발견했다."], ["It was a triangular billet, on pink paper, and sealed with a dove and an olive branch, and a profusion of light blue sealing wax, and it was written in a very large, though undecided female hand.", "분홍색 종이에 쓴 삼각형 모양의 짧은 편지로, 비둘기와 올리브 가지가 새겨진 봉인에 옅은 파란 봉랍을 잔뜩 발랐고, 매우 큼직하면서도 어딘가 미덥지 못한 여자 필체로 적혀 있었다."]], [["\"It's Peggy O'Dowd's fist,\"", "\"페기 오다우드의 글씨로군.\""], ["said George, laughing.", "조지가 웃으며 말했다."], ["\"I know it by the kisses on the seal.\" And in fact, it was a note from Mrs.", "\"봉인에 찍힌 입맞춤 자국을 보면 알지.\""], ["Major O'Dowd, requesting the pleasure of Mrs. Osborne's company that very evening to a small friendly party.", "과연 그것은 오다우드 소령 부인이 보낸 짧은 편지로, 바로 그날 저녁 자그마한 친목 모임에 오스본 부인이 함께해 주는 기쁨을 누리게 해 달라는 청이었다."], ["\"You must go,\" George said.", "\"당신은 꼭 가야 해.\""], ["\"You will make acquaintance with the regiment there.", "조지가 말했다. \"거기서 연대 사람들과 인사를 트게 될 테니까."], ["O'Dowd goes in command of the regiment, and Peggy goes in command.\"", "오다우드가 연대를 지휘하고, 페기가 그 오다우드를 지휘하지.\""]], [["But they had not been for many minutes in the enjoyment of Mrs. O'Dowd's letter, when the door was flung open, and a stout jolly lady, in a riding-habit, followed by a couple of officers of Ours, entered the room.", "그러나 그들이 오다우드 부인의 편지를 즐긴 지 몇 분도 채 되지 않아, 문이 벌컥 열리더니 승마복 차림의 통통하고 쾌활한 부인이 우리 연대 장교 두 사람을 거느리고 방 안으로 들어왔다."]], [["\"Sure, I couldn't stop till tay-time.", "\"글쎄, 차 마실 시간까지 도저히 못 기다리겠지 뭐예요."], ["Present me, Garge, my dear fellow, to your lady.", "가지, 이봐요, 날 부인께 소개해 줘요. 부인, 만나 뵙게 되어 정말 기뻐요."], ["Madam, I'm deloighted to see ye; and to present to you me husband, Meejor O'Dowd\"; and with this, the jolly lady in the riding-habit grasped Amelia's hand very warmly, and the latter knew at once that the lady was before her whom her husband had so often laughed at.", "그리고 제 남편 오다우드 소령을 소개할게요.\" 이렇게 말하며 승마복 차림의 쾌활한 부인은 애밀리아의 손을 더없이 따뜻하게 부여잡았고, 애밀리아는 이 부인이 바로 자기 남편이 그토록 자주 웃음거리로 삼던 그 사람임을 단박에 알아차렸다."], ["\"You've often heard of me from that husband of yours,\"", "\"댁의 그 남편한테서 제 이야기를 자주 들으셨을 거예요.\""], ["said the lady, with great vivacity.", "부인이 무척 활기차게 말했다."]], [["\"You've often heard of her,\"", "\"이 사람 이야기를 자주 들으셨을 거요.\""], ["echoed her husband, the Major.", "그녀의 남편인 소령이 따라서 말했다."]], [["Amelia answered, smiling, \"that she had.\"", "애밀리아는 미소 지으며 \"그렇다\"고 대답했다."]], [["\"And small good he's told you of me,\" Mrs.", "\"그런데 그이가 저에 대해 좋은 말이라곤 거의 안 했겠지요.\""], ["O'Dowd replied; adding that \"George was a wicked divvle.\"", "오다우드 부인이 대꾸하더니, \"가지가 못된 악당\"이라고 덧붙였다."]], [["\"That I'll go bail for,\"", "\"그건 내가 보증하지요.\""], ["said the Major, trying to look knowing, at which George laughed; and Mrs.", "소령이 뭔가 아는 체하는 표정을 지으려 애쓰며 말하자 조지가 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["O'Dowd, with a tap of her whip, told the Major to be quiet; and then requested to be presented in form to Mrs. Captain Osborne.", "오다우드 부인은 채찍으로 톡 치며 소령에게 조용히 하라고 이른 뒤, 오스본 대위 부인께 격식을 갖추어 자신을 소개해 달라고 청했다."]], [["\"This, my dear,\"", "\"여보, 이분으로 말할 것 같으면,\""], ["said George with great gravity, \"is my very good, kind, and excellent friend, Auralia Margaretta, otherwise called Peggy.\"", "조지가 자못 엄숙하게 말했다. \"나의 더없이 훌륭하고, 다정하고, 빼어난 벗, 오릴리아 마거리타, 다른 이름으로는 페기라 불리는 분이오.\""]], [["\"Faith, you're right,\"", "\"맞아, 옳은 말이오.\""], ["interposed the Major.", "소령이 끼어들었다."]], [["\"Otherwise called Peggy, lady of Major Michael O'Dowd, of our regiment, and daughter of Fitzjurld Ber'sford de Burgo Malony of Glenmalony, County Kildare.\"", "\"다른 이름으로는 페기, 우리 연대 마이클 오다우드 소령의 부인이자, 킬데어 주 글렌맬로니의 피츠제럴드 베리스퍼드 디 버고 맬로니의 따님이시지.\""]], [["\"And Muryan Squeer, Doblin,\"", "\"그리고 더블린 머라이언 광장 출신이고요.\""], ["said the lady with calm superiority.", "부인이 차분하게 우쭐대며 말했다."]], [["\"And Muryan Square, sure enough,\"", "\"그렇지, 머라이언 광장이고말고.\""], ["the Major whispered.", "소령이 속삭이듯 말했다."]], [["\"'Twas there ye coorted me, Meejor dear,\"", "\"바로 거기서 당신이 날 쫓아다니며 구애했잖아요, 여보.\""], ["the lady said; and the Major assented to this as to every other proposition which was made generally in company.", "부인이 말했고, 소령은 사람들 앞에서 으레 나오는 다른 모든 말과 마찬가지로 이 말에도 고개를 끄덕여 동의했다."]], [["Major O'Dowd, who had served his sovereign in every quarter of the world, and had paid for every step in his profession by some more than equivalent act of daring and gallantry, was the most modest, silent, sheep-faced and meek of little men, and as obedient to his wife as if he had been her tay-boy.", "세계 방방곡곡에서 군주를 섬겨 왔고, 그 직업의 한 계단 한 계단을 그에 넘치고도 남을 만한 담대함과 무용으로 사들여 온 오다우드 소령이었지만, 정작 그는 세상에서 가장 겸손하고, 말수 적고, 양처럼 순하고, 온순한 자그마한 사내였으며, 마치 아내의 차 시중꾼이라도 되는 양 그녀에게 고분고분했다."], ["At the mess-table he sat silently, and drank a great deal.", "회식 자리에서 그는 말없이 앉아 술만 잔뜩 들이켰다."], ["When full of liquor, he reeled silently home.", "술이 잔뜩 오르면 말없이 비틀거리며 집으로 돌아갔다."], ["When he spoke, it was to agree with everybody on every conceivable point; and he passed through life in perfect ease and good-humour.", "입을 열 때라곤 무슨 일에서든 누구에게나 맞장구를 칠 때뿐이었고, 그렇게 더없는 평온과 호인다운 기질로 삶을 헤쳐 나갔다."], ["The hottest suns of India never heated his temper; and the Walcheren ague never shook it.", "인도의 그 뜨거운 태양도 그의 성미를 달군 적이 없고, 발헤런의 학질도 그것을 떨게 한 적이 없었다."], ["He walked up to a battery with just as much indifference as to a dinner-table; had dined on horse-flesh and turtle with equal relish and appetite; and had an old mother, Mrs.", "그는 만찬 식탁으로 가듯 태연히 포대를 향해 걸어 올라갔고, 말고기든 거북이 요리든 똑같이 흡족하게 식욕을 돋우며 먹어 치웠다."], ["O'Dowd of O'Dowdstown indeed, whom he had never disobeyed but when he ran away and enlisted, and when he persisted in marrying that odious Peggy Malony.", "그에게는 오다우드스타운의 오다우드 노부인이라는 노모가 있었는데, 그가 노모의 뜻을 거스른 적이라곤 집을 뛰쳐나가 입대했을 때와, 저 가증스러운 페기 맬로니와 기어이 혼인하겠다고 고집을 부렸을 때뿐이었다."]], [["Peggy was one of five sisters, and eleven children of the noble house of Glenmalony; but her husband, though her own cousin, was of the mother's side, and so had not the inestimable advantage of being allied to the Malonys, whom she believed to be the most famous family in the world.", "페기는 명문가 글렌맬로니의 다섯 자매 중 하나이자 열한 남매 중 하나였다. 그러나 그녀의 남편은 사촌지간이긴 했어도 어머니 쪽 사촌이라, 그녀가 세상에서 가장 명망 높은 가문이라 믿어 의심치 않는 맬로니가와 혈연으로 맺어지는 그 더없이 값진 이점은 누리지 못했다."], ["Having tried nine seasons at Dublin and two at Bath and Cheltenham, and not finding a partner for life, Miss Malony ordered her cousin Mick to marry her when she was about thirty-three years of age; and the honest fellow obeying, carried her off to the West Indies, to preside over the ladies of the --th regiment, into which he had just exchanged.", "더블린에서 아홉 시즌, 배스와 첼트넘에서 두 시즌을 보내고도 평생의 짝을 찾지 못하자, 맬로니 양은 서른세 살쯤 되었을 때 사촌 미크에게 자기와 혼인하라 명했다. 정직한 사내는 그 말에 따라 그녀를 서인도제도로 데려갔고, 그가 막 전입해 온 ××연대 부인네들을 다스리는 안주인 자리에 앉혔다."]], [["Before Mrs. O'Dowd was half an hour in Amelia's (or indeed in anybody else's) company, this amiable lady told all her birth and pedigree to her new friend.", "오다우드 부인은 애밀리아와(아니, 실은 그 누구와든) 함께 있은 지 반 시간도 채 되기 전에, 이 상냥한 부인은 자기 출생과 가문 내력을 새 친구에게 죄다 늘어놓았다."], ["\"My dear,\"", "\"이봐요,\""], ["said she, good-naturedly, \"it was my intention that Garge should be a brother of my own, and my sister Glorvina would have suited him entirely.", "그녀가 사람 좋게 말했다. \"나는 본래 가지를 내 친동생으로 삼을 작정이었어요."], ["But as bygones are bygones, and he was engaged to yourself, why, I'm determined to take you as a sister instead, and to look upon you as such, and to love you as one of the family.", "내 여동생 글로비나가 그이한테 더없이 잘 어울렸을 텐데 말이지요. 하지만 지난 일은 지난 일이고, 그이가 댁과 약혼을 했으니, 그렇담 나는 그 대신 댁을 동생으로 삼기로 단단히 마음먹었어요."], ["Faith, you've got such a nice good-natured face and way widg you, that I'm sure we'll agree; and that you'll be an addition to our family anyway.\"", "댁을 동생으로 여기고, 한 식구처럼 사랑할 거예요. 정말이지 댁은 얼굴도 곱고 마음씨도 좋아 보이고 몸가짐도 어찌나 사랑스러운지, 우리는 분명 잘 지낼 거고, 어쨌거나 댁은 우리 집안에 보탬이 될 거예요.\""]], [["\"'Deed and she will,\"", "\"암, 그렇고말고.\""], ["said O'Dowd, with an approving air, and Amelia felt herself not a little amused and grateful to be thus suddenly introduced to so large a party of relations.", "오다우드가 흡족한 기색으로 말했고, 애밀리아는 이렇게 느닷없이 이토록 많은 친척 무리에 소개받게 된 것이 적잖이 재미있으면서도 고맙게 느껴졌다."]], [["\"We're all good fellows here,\"", "\"여기 사람들은 다 좋은 이들이에요.\""], ["the Major's lady continued.", "소령 부인이 말을 이었다."], ["\"There's not a regiment in the service where you'll find a more united society nor a more agreeable mess-room.", "\"군대를 통틀어 우리만큼 화목한 사회도, 우리 회식실만큼 유쾌한 곳도 없을걸요."], ["There's no quarrelling, bickering, slandthering, nor small talk amongst us.", "우리 사이엔 다툼도, 입씨름도, 험담도, 쓸데없는 수다도 없어요."], ["We all love each other.\"", "우리는 다 서로 사랑한답니다.\""]], [["\"Especially Mrs. Magenis,\"", "\"특히 머제니스 부인을 말이지요.\""], ["said George, laughing.", "조지가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["\"Mrs.", "\"머제니스 대위 부인하고는 화해했어요."], ["Captain Magenis and me has made up, though her treatment of me would bring me gray hairs with sorrow to the grave.\"", "하긴 그 여자가 나한테 한 짓을 생각하면 슬픔에 백발이 되어 무덤까지 갈 지경이지만요.\""]], [["\"And you with such a beautiful front of black, Peggy, my dear,\"", "\"당신은 그렇게 새카만 머리가 멋들어진데 말이오, 페기, 여보.\""], ["the Major cried.", "소령이 외쳤다."]], [["\"Hould your tongue, Mick, you booby.", "\"입 좀 다물어요, 미크, 이 멍청한 양반아."], ["Them husbands are always in the way, Mrs.", "남편이란 작자들은 늘 거치적거린다니까요, 오스본 부인."], ["Osborne, my dear; and as for my Mick, I often tell him he should never open his mouth but to give the word of command, or to put meat and drink into it.", "우리 미크로 말할 것 같으면, 나는 늘 이이한테 명령을 내릴 때나 입에 음식이며 술을 넣을 때 말고는 절대 입을 벌리지 말라고 한답니다."], ["I'll tell you about the regiment, and warn you when we're alone.", "연대 이야기는 우리 둘만 있을 때 내가 다 들려주고 일러 줄게요."], ["Introduce me to your brother now; sure he's a mighty fine man, and reminds me of me cousin, Dan Malony (Malony of Ballymalony, my dear, you know who mar'ied Ophalia Scully, of Oystherstown, own cousin to Lord Poldoody).", "자, 이제 댁의 오라버니를 소개해 줘요. 정말 훤칠한 분이시네, 내 사촌 댄 맬로니가 떠오르는걸요(밸리맬로니의 맬로니 말이에요, 알잖아요, 오이스터스타운의 오필리아 스컬리와 혼인한 사람, 폴두디 경의 친사촌인 그 여자 말이에요)."], ["Mr. Sedley, sir, I'm deloighted to be made known te ye.", "세들리 씨, 만나 뵙게 되어 정말 기뻐요."], ["I suppose you'll dine at the mess to-day.", "오늘 회식 자리에 함께하시겠지요."], ["(Mind that divvle of a docther, Mick, and whatever ye du, keep yourself sober for me party this evening.)\"", "(저 망할 군의관 좀 잘 살펴요, 미크, 그리고 무슨 일이 있어도 오늘 저녁 내 모임을 위해 정신만은 멀쩡하게 차리고 있어요.)\""]], [["\"It's the 150th gives us a farewell dinner, my love,\"", "\"여보, 오늘은 150연대가 우리한테 송별 만찬을 베푸는 날이오.\" 소령이 끼어들었다."], ["interposed the Major, \"but we'll easy get a card for Mr. Sedley.\"", "\"하지만 세들리 씨 자리야 손쉽게 마련할 수 있을 거요.\""]], [["\"Run Simple (Ensign Simple, of Ours, my dear Amelia.", "\"심플더러 뛰어가라고 해요(우리 연대 심플 소위 말이에요, 애밀리아. 깜빡하고 소개를 못 했네요)."], ["I forgot to introjuice him to ye). Run in a hurry, with Mrs.", "어서 뛰어가서, 오다우드 소령 부인의 인사와 함께 테이비시 대령께 전하라고 해요."], ["Major O'Dowd's compliments to Colonel Tavish, and Captain Osborne has brought his brothernlaw down, and will bring him to the 150th mess at five o'clock sharp--when you and I, my dear, will take a snack here, if you like.\"", "오스본 대위가 처남을 데려왔으니 다섯 시 정각에 150연대 회식 자리로 모셔 가겠다고요. 그동안 우리 둘은, 댁만 좋다면, 여기서 간단히 요기나 하자고요.\""], ["Before Mrs. O'Dowd's speech was concluded, the young Ensign was trotting downstairs on his commission.", "오다우드 부인의 말이 채 끝나기도 전에, 젊은 소위는 그 임무를 받들어 계단을 종종걸음으로 내려가고 있었다."]], [["\"Obedience is the soul of the army.", "\"복종이야말로 군대의 혼이지."], ["We will go to our duty while Mrs. O'Dowd will stay and enlighten you, Emmy,\"", "오다우드 부인이 남아서 당신을 가르쳐 주는 동안 우리는 우리 임무를 보러 가야겠소, 에미.\""], ["Captain Osborne said; and the two gentlemen, taking each a wing of the Major, walked out with that officer, grinning at each other over his head.", "오스본 대위가 말했다. 그러고는 두 신사가 소령의 양옆을 한 사람씩 붙잡고, 그의 머리 너머로 서로 히죽거리며 그 장교와 함께 걸어 나갔다."]], [["And, now having her new friend to herself, the impetuous Mrs. O'Dowd proceeded to pour out such a quantity of information as no poor little woman's memory could ever tax itself to bear.", "이제 새 친구를 독차지하게 된 성마른 오다우드 부인은, 그 가엾고 자그마한 여인의 기억력으로는 도무지 감당할 수 없을 만큼 어마어마한 정보를 쏟아 내기 시작했다."], ["She told Amelia a thousand particulars relative to the very numerous family of which the amazed young lady found herself a member. \"Mrs.", "그녀는 어리둥절해진 이 젊은 숙녀가 일원이 되었음을 깨닫게 된 그 어마어마하게 대식구인 집안에 관해, 애밀리아에게 천 가지나 되는 시시콜콜한 사정을 들려주었다."], ["Heavytop, the Colonel's wife, died in Jamaica of the yellow faver and a broken heart comboined, for the horrud old Colonel, with a head as bald as a cannon-ball, was making sheep's eyes at a half-caste girl there.", "\"대령 부인인 헤비탑 부인은 자메이카에서 황열병에다 상심까지 겹쳐 세상을 떴어요. 그 끔찍한 늙은 대령이, 머리통이 대포알처럼 반들반들 벗어진 양반이, 거기서 어떤 혼혈 처녀한테 추파를 던지고 있었거든요."], ["Mrs. Magenis, though without education, was a good woman, but she had the divvle's tongue, and would cheat her own mother at whist.", "머제니스 부인은 배운 건 없어도 좋은 여자이긴 한데, 혀가 어찌나 독한지 휘스트 칠 땐 제 친어미라도 속여 먹을 사람이에요."], ["Mrs. Captain Kirk must turn up her lobster eyes forsooth at the idea of an honest round game (wherein me fawther, as pious a man as ever went to church, me uncle Dane Malony, and our cousin the Bishop, took a hand at loo, or whist, every night of their lives).", "커크 대위 부인으로 말하자면, 글쎄, 점잖은 카드놀이 이야기만 나와도 그 새우 눈을 치뜨고 본단 말이에요(우리 아버지로 말하면 교회 다니는 그 누구보다 경건한 분이었고, 우리 삼촌 댄 맬로니랑 우리 사촌 주교님까지도 평생 매일 밤 루나 휘스트를 한 판씩 즐기셨는데 말이지요)."], ["Nayther of 'em's goin' with the regiment this time,\"", "이번엔 두 사람 다 연대를 따라가지 않는답니다.\""], ["Mrs. O'Dowd added.", "오다우드 부인이 덧붙였다."], ["\"Fanny Magenis stops with her mother, who sells small coal and potatoes, most likely, in Islington-town, hard by London, though she's always bragging of her father's ships, and pointing them out to us as they go up the river: and Mrs.", "\"패니 머제니스는 제 어미한테 가 있어요, 런던 바로 옆 이즐링턴 마을에서 십중팔구 석탄 부스러기랑 감자 따위를 파는 여자한테요. 그러면서도 늘 제 아비 배 자랑을 늘어놓고, 강을 거슬러 올라가는 배들을 우리한테 가리켜 보이곤 하지요."], ["Kirk and her children will stop here in Bethesda Place, to be nigh to her favourite preacher, Dr. Ramshorn.", "그리고 커크 부인이랑 그 애들은 여기 베세다 플레이스에 머무를 거예요, 제가 떠받드는 설교자 램스혼 박사 가까이 있으려고요."], ["Mrs. Bunny's in an interesting situation--faith, and she always is, then--and has given the Lieutenant seven already.", "버니 부인은 흥미로운 처지에 놓여 있는데, 원, 그 여자야 늘 그 모양이지만요, 벌써 중위한테 일곱을 안겨 주었답니다."], ["And Ensign Posky's wife, who joined two months before you, my dear, has quarl'd with Tom Posky a score of times, till you can hear'm all over the bar'ck (they say they're come to broken pleets, and Tom never accounted for his black oi), and she'll go back to her mother, who keeps a ladies'", "그리고 포스키 소위 부인은, 댁보다 두 달 먼저 들어온 사람인데, 톰 포스키하고 스무 번도 넘게 싸워 댔어요. 어찌나 시끄러운지 막사 전체에 다 들릴 정도였지요(접시까지 깨부수는 지경에 이르렀다나요, 그리고 톰은 그 시퍼렇게 멍든 눈을 끝내 해명하지 못했고요)."], ["siminary at Richmond--bad luck to her for running away from it!", "그러다 결국 제 어미한테 돌아갈 거예요. 리치먼드에서 숙녀 교육원을 하는 여자한테요."], ["Where did ye get your finishing, my dear?", "거기서 도망쳐 나온 게 그 여자한텐 불운이었지요! 댁은 어디서 마무리 교육을 받았나요?"], ["I had moin, and no expince spared, at Madame Flanahan's, at Ilyssus Grove, Booterstown, near Dublin, wid a Marchioness to teach us the true Parisian pronunciation, and a retired Mejor-General of the French service to put us through the exercise.\"", "나는 더블린 근처 부터스타운, 일리수스 그로브에 있는 플래너핸 부인 학원에서 받았는데, 돈 한 푼 아끼지 않았답니다. 후작 부인이 진짜 파리식 발음을 가르쳐 주었고, 프랑스 군대에서 퇴역한 소장 한 분이 우리한테 훈련을 시켜 주었지요.\""]], [["Of this incongruous family our astonished Amelia found herself all of a sudden a member: with Mrs.", "이 뒤죽박죽인 집안에, 우리의 어리둥절한 애밀리아는 느닷없이 일원이 되어 버렸다."], ["O'Dowd as an elder sister.", "오다우드 부인을 손위 언니로 둔 채로 말이다."], ["She was presented to her other female relations at tea-time, on whom, as she was quiet, good-natured, and not too handsome, she made rather an agreeable impression until the arrival of the gentlemen from the mess of the 150th, who all admired her so, that her sisters began, of course, to find fault with her.", "그녀는 차 마실 시간에 다른 여자 친척들에게도 소개되었는데, 조용하고, 마음씨 곱고, 그리 빼어난 미인도 아니었던 까닭에, 150연대 회식 자리에서 신사들이 도착하기 전까지는 제법 호감을 사고 있었다. 그러나 그 신사들이 하나같이 그녀를 어찌나 흠모하던지, 자매들은 당연하다는 듯 그녀의 흠을 잡기 시작했다."]], [["\"I hope Osborne has sown his wild oats,\"", "\"오스본이 이제 바람기는 다 잡았으면 좋겠네요.\""], ["said Mrs. Magenis to Mrs.", "머제니스 부인이 버니 부인에게 말했다."], ["Bunny. \"If a reformed rake makes a good husband, sure it's she will have the fine chance with Garge,\"", "\"개과천선한 난봉꾼이 좋은 남편이 된다는 게 사실이라면야, 가지를 만난 저 여자는 정말 운수 대통한 셈이지요.\""], ["Mrs. O'Dowd remarked to Posky, who had lost her position as bride in the regiment, and was quite angry with the usurper.", "오다우드 부인이 포스키 부인에게 한마디 했다."], ["And as for Mrs. Kirk: that disciple of Dr.", "연대의 새색시 자리를 빼앗긴 포스키 부인은 그 찬탈자에게 잔뜩 화가 나 있던 터였다."], ["Ramshorn put one or two leading professional questions to Amelia, to see whether she was awakened, whether she was a professing Christian and so forth, and finding from the simplicity of Mrs.", "커크 부인으로 말하자면, 램스혼 박사의 그 제자는 애밀리아에게 신앙에 관한 핵심적인 물음을 한두 가지 던져 보았다. 그녀가 영적으로 깨어 있는지, 신앙을 고백하는 그리스도교도인지 따위를 알아보려는 것이었다."], ["Osborne's replies that she was yet in utter darkness, put into her hands three little penny books with pictures, viz., the \"Howling Wilderness,\"", "그러다 오스본 부인의 대답이 너무도 순진한 것을 보고 그녀가 아직 칠흑 같은 어둠 속에 있음을 알아채자, 그림이 든 한 푼짜리 작은 책자 세 권을 그녀의 손에 쥐여 주었다."], ["the \"Washerwoman of Wandsworth Common,\" and the \"British Soldier's best Bayonet,\"", "곧 「울부짖는 광야」, 「완즈워스 공유지의 빨래하는 여인」, 「영국 병사의 가장 훌륭한 총검」이라는 것이었다."], ["which, bent upon awakening her before she slept, Mrs. Kirk begged Amelia to read that night ere she went to bed.", "그녀가 잠들기 전에 그녀를 깨우치겠다는 일념으로, 커크 부인은 애밀리아에게 그날 밤 잠자리에 들기 전에 이것을 읽어 보라고 신신당부했다."]], [["But all the men, like good fellows as they were, rallied round their comrade's pretty wife, and paid her their court with soldierly gallantry.", "그러나 남자들은 하나같이 호인답게 동료의 어여쁜 아내 곁으로 모여들어, 군인다운 의협심으로 그녀에게 정중히 구애를 펼쳤다."], ["She had a little triumph, which flushed her spirits and made her eyes sparkle.", "그녀는 자그마한 승리를 거두었고, 그 덕분에 기분이 한껏 들떠 두 눈이 반짝거렸다."], ["George was proud of her popularity, and pleased with the manner (which was very gay and graceful, though naive and a little timid) with which she received the gentlemen's attentions, and answered their compliments.", "조지는 아내의 인기가 자랑스러웠고, 그녀가 신사들의 관심을 받아들이고 그 찬사에 답하는 태도(무척 명랑하고 우아하면서도 천진하고 조금은 수줍은 태도)가 흐뭇했다."], ["And he in his uniform--how much handsomer he was than any man in the room!", "그리고 군복을 입은 그는, 그 방 안 어느 누구보다 훨씬 더 늠름했다!"], ["She felt that he was affectionately watching her, and glowed with pleasure at his kindness.", "그녀는 그가 다정하게 자신을 지켜보고 있음을 느끼며, 그 따스함에 기쁨으로 발그레 달아올랐다."], ["\"I will make all his friends welcome,\"", "\"이이의 벗들을 모두 반갑게 맞이하리라.\""], ["she resolved in her heart.", "그녀는 마음속으로 다짐했다."], ["\"I will love all as I love him.", "\"이이를 사랑하듯 모두를 사랑하리라."], ["I will always try and be gay and good-humoured and make his home happy.\"", "언제나 명랑하고 쾌활하게 지내며 이이의 가정을 행복하게 만들리라.\""]], [["The regiment indeed adopted her with acclamation.", "과연 연대는 환호로 그녀를 받아들였다."], ["The Captains approved, the Lieutenants applauded, the Ensigns admired.", "대위들은 흡족해했고, 중위들은 갈채를 보냈으며, 소위들은 흠모했다."], ["Old Cutler, the Doctor, made one or two jokes, which, being professional, need not be repeated; and Cackle, the Assistant M.D.", "늙은 군의관 커틀러는 농담을 한두 마디 했는데, 직업과 관련된 것이라 여기 옮길 필요는 없겠다."], ["of Edinburgh, condescended to examine her upon leeterature, and tried her with his three best French quotations.", "그리고 에든버러 출신의 군의관 보조 캐클은 황송하게도 그녀에게 문학에 관한 질문을 던지고, 자기가 아는 가장 그럴듯한 프랑스어 인용구 세 가지로 그녀를 시험해 보았다."], ["Young Stubble went about from man to man whispering, \"Jove, isn't she a pretty gal?\"", "젊은 스터블은 이 사람 저 사람에게 돌아다니며 \"이런, 정말 예쁜 아가씨 아닌가?\""], ["and never took his eyes off her except when the negus came in.", "하고 속삭였고, 니거스 술이 들어올 때 말고는 그녀에게서 한시도 눈을 떼지 않았다."]], [["As for Captain Dobbin, he never so much as spoke to her during the whole evening.", "도빈 대위로 말하자면, 그는 저녁 내내 그녀에게 말 한마디 건네지 않았다."], ["But he and Captain Porter of the 150th took home Jos to the hotel, who was in a very maudlin state, and had told his tiger-hunt story with great effect, both at the mess-table and at the soiree, to Mrs.", "다만 그와 150연대의 포터 대위가 조스를 호텔까지 데려다주었다. 조스는 잔뜩 취해 청승맞은 꼴이 되어, 회식 식탁에서나 야회에서나 자기 호랑이 사냥 이야기를 자못 그럴듯하게 늘어놓았던 터였다."], ["O'Dowd in her turban and bird of paradise.", "터번에 극락조 깃털을 꽂은 오다우드 부인을 상대로 말이다."], ["Having put the Collector into the hands of his servant, Dobbin loitered about, smoking his cigar before the inn door.", "도빈은 이 징세관을 그의 하인에게 맡긴 뒤, 여관 문 앞에서 시가를 피우며 어슬렁거렸다."], ["George had meanwhile very carefully shawled his wife, and brought her away from Mrs.", "그러는 동안 조지는 무척 정성스레 아내에게 숄을 둘러 주고, 젊은 장교들이 다 함께 악수를 청한 뒤 오다우드 부인의 집에서 그녀를 데리고 나왔다."], ["O'Dowd's after a general handshaking from the young officers, who accompanied her to the fly, and cheered that vehicle as it drove off.", "장교들은 그녀를 마차까지 배웅하고는, 마차가 떠나갈 때 그것을 향해 환호를 보냈다."], ["So Amelia gave Dobbin her little hand as she got out of the carriage, and rebuked him smilingly for not having taken any notice of her all night.", "그리하여 애밀리아는 마차에서 내리며 도빈에게 자그마한 손을 내밀었고, 저녁 내내 자기한테 도통 눈길 한 번 주지 않았다며 미소 띤 얼굴로 그를 나무랐다."]], [["The Captain continued that deleterious amusement of smoking, long after the inn and the street were gone to bed.", "대위는 여관도 거리도 모두 잠든 뒤로도 한참 동안 그 해로운 흡연의 즐거움을 이어 갔다."], ["He watched the lights vanish from George's sitting-room windows, and shine out in the bedroom close at hand.", "그는 조지의 응접실 창에서 불빛이 사라지고, 바로 곁의 침실에서 불빛이 새어 나오는 것을 지켜보았다."], ["It was almost morning when he returned to his own quarters.", "그가 자기 숙소로 돌아온 것은 거의 동틀 무렵이었다."], ["He could hear the cheering from the ships in the river, where the transports were already taking in their cargoes preparatory to dropping down the Thames.", "강에 떠 있는 배들에서 환호 소리가 들려왔다. 수송선들이 벌써 화물을 싣고 템스강을 따라 내려갈 채비를 하고 있던 것이다."]], [["In Which Amelia Invades the Low Countries\nThe regiment with its officers was to be transported in ships provided by His Majesty's government for the occasion: and in two days after the festive assembly at Mrs.", "연대는 장교들과 함께, 이번 일을 위해 폐하의 정부가 마련한 배편으로 수송될 예정이었다."], ["O'Dowd's apartments, in the midst of cheering from all the East India ships in the river, and the military on shore, the band playing \"God Save the King,\" the officers waving their hats, and the crews hurrahing gallantly, the transports went down the river and proceeded under convoy to Ostend.", "오다우드 부인의 거처에서 그 흥겨운 연회가 열린 지 이틀 후, 강에 떠 있는 모든 동인도 선박과 뭍에 늘어선 군대의 환호 속에서, 군악대가 \"신이여 국왕을 지키소서\"를 연주하고 장교들이 모자를 흔들며 선원들이 호기롭게 만세를 외치는 가운데, 수송선들은 강을 따라 내려가 호위를 받으며 오스텐드로 향했다."], ["Meanwhile the gallant Jos had agreed to escort his sister and the Major's wife, the bulk of whose goods and chattels, including the famous bird of paradise and turban, were with the regimental baggage: so that our two heroines drove pretty much unencumbered to Ramsgate, where there were plenty of packets plying, in one of which they had a speedy passage to Ostend.", "한편 용맹한 조스는 누이동생과 소령 부인을 호위하기로 약속한 터였고, 그 유명한 극락조 깃털과 터번을 비롯한 부인의 살림살이 대부분은 연대 화물과 함께 부쳐졌다. 그리하여 우리의 두 여주인공은 별다른 짐 없이 홀가분하게 마차를 타고 램즈게이트까지 갔는데, 그곳에는 정기선이 숱하게 오가고 있어서 그중 한 척에 올라 오스텐드까지 빠르게 건너갔다."]], [["That period of Jos's life which now ensued was so full of incident, that it served him for conversation for many years after, and even the tiger-hunt story was put aside for more stirring narratives which he had to tell about the great campaign of Waterloo.", "이제부터 펼쳐질 조스의 인생 한 토막은 사건으로 가득 차서, 이후로 여러 해 동안 그의 입담거리가 되어 주었으니, 워털루 대전에 관해 들려줄 더 손에 땀을 쥐게 하는 이야기들 앞에서는 호랑이 사냥 이야기조차 뒷전으로 밀려나는 형편이었다."], ["As soon as he had agreed to escort his sister abroad, it was remarked that he ceased shaving his upper lip.", "누이동생을 외국까지 호위하기로 약속하자마자, 그는 윗입술 면도를 그만두었다는 소문이 돌았다."], ["At Chatham he followed the parades and drills with great assiduity.", "채텀에서 그는 더없이 부지런히 사열과 훈련을 따라다녔다."], ["He listened with the utmost attention to the conversation of his brother officers (as he called them in after days sometimes), and learned as many military names as he could. In these studies the excellent Mrs.", "그는 (훗날 그가 이따금 일컫던 대로) 자기 \"동료 장교들\"의 대화에 지극한 주의를 기울여 귀담아들었고, 군대 용어를 닥치는 대로 익혔다."], ["O'Dowd was of great assistance to him; and on the day finally when they embarked on board the Lovely Rose, which was to carry them to their destination, he made his appearance in a braided frock-coat and duck trousers, with a foraging cap ornamented with a smart gold band.", "이런 학습에서 훌륭한 오다우드 부인이 그에게 큰 도움이 되어 주었다. 그리고 마침내 그들을 목적지로 실어다 줄 러블리 로즈 호에 오르던 날, 그는 가두리 장식이 달린 프록코트에 흰 무명 바지를 입고, 멋들어진 금색 띠로 장식한 군용 약모를 쓰고 나타났다."], ["Having his carriage with him, and informing everybody on board confidentially that he was going to join the Duke of Wellington's army, folks mistook him for a great personage, a commissary-general, or a government courier at the very least.", "자기 마차를 가지고 온 데다 배에 탄 모든 이에게 자기가 웰링턴 공작의 군대에 합류하러 가는 길이라고 은밀히 일러두니, 사람들은 그를 대단한 인물, 적어도 병참감이나 정부 특사쯤으로 오해했다."]], [["He suffered hugely on the voyage, during which the ladies were likewise prostrate; but Amelia was brought to life again as the packet made Ostend, by the sight of the transports conveying her regiment, which entered the harbour almost at the same time with the Lovely Rose.", "항해 동안 그는 끔찍이 고생했고, 그동안 두 숙녀 역시 뱃멀미로 늘어져 있었다. 그러나 애밀리아는 정기선이 오스텐드에 다다르자, 러블리 로즈 호와 거의 동시에 항구로 들어서는 자기 연대의 수송선들을 보고는 다시 생기를 되찾았다."], ["Jos went in a collapsed state to an inn, while Captain Dobbin escorted the ladies, and then busied himself in freeing Jos's carriage and luggage from the ship and the custom-house, for Mr.", "조스는 기진맥진한 채로 여관으로 갔고, 그동안 도빈 대위는 두 숙녀를 호위한 뒤, 조스의 마차와 짐을 배와 세관에서 빼내는 일에 분주히 매달렸다."], ["Jos was at present without a servant, Osborne's man and his own pampered menial having conspired together at Chatham, and refused point-blank to cross the water.", "지금 조스에게는 하인이 없었기 때문이다. 오즈번의 하인과 그 자신의 어리광받이 하인이 채텀에서 작당하여, 바다를 건너기를 딱 잘라 거부한 것이다."], ["This revolt, which came very suddenly, and on the last day, so alarmed Mr.", "마지막 날에 느닷없이 터진 이 반란에 어찌나 놀랐던지, 세들리 가의 아들은 이 원정을 단념할 뻔했다."], ["Sedley, junior, that he was on the point of giving up the expedition, but Captain Dobbin (who made himself immensely officious in the business, Jos said), rated him and laughed at him soundly: the mustachios were grown in advance, and Jos finally was persuaded to embark.", "그러나 도빈 대위가 (조스의 말에 따르면 이 일에 엄청나게 주제넘게 나섰다) 그를 호되게 나무라고 실컷 비웃었다. 콧수염은 이미 미리 길러 놓은 터라, 조스도 결국 배에 오르도록 설득당했다."], ["In place of the well-bred and well-fed London domestics, who could only speak English, Dobbin procured for Jos's party a swarthy little Belgian servant who could speak no language at all; but who, by his bustling behaviour, and by invariably addressing Mr.", "영어밖에 할 줄 모르는, 교양 있고 잘 먹은 런던 하인들 대신, 도빈은 조스 일행을 위해 어느 나라 말도 할 줄 모르는 거무튀튀한 자그마한 벨기에 하인을 구해 주었다. 그러나 이자는 부산스러운 처신과, 세들리 씨를 한결같이 \"나리\"라고 부르는 덕분에 금세 그분의 환심을 샀다."], ["Sedley as \"My lord,\" speedily acquired that gentleman's favour.", "오스텐드는 이제 시절이 달라졌다."], ["Times are altered at Ostend now; of the Britons who go thither, very few look like lords, or act like those members of our hereditary aristocracy.", "그곳을 찾는 영국인 가운데 나리처럼 보이거나, 우리네 세습 귀족답게 처신하는 이는 거의 없다."], ["They seem for the most part shabby in attire, dingy of linen, lovers of billiards and brandy, and cigars and greasy ordinaries.", "그들 대부분은 행색이 초라하고, 셔츠가 꾀죄죄하며, 당구와 브랜디와 엽궐련과 기름진 정식을 좋아하는 부류로 보인다."]], [["But it may be said as a rule, that every Englishman in the Duke of Wellington's army paid his way.", "그러나 한 가지 일반론으로 말하건대, 웰링턴 공작의 군대에 속한 영국인이라면 누구나 제 셈은 제가 치렀다."], ["The remembrance of such a fact surely becomes a nation of shopkeepers.", "이런 사실을 떠올리면 분명 가게 주인들의 나라다워진다."], ["It was a blessing for a commerce-loving country to be overrun by such an army of customers: and to have such creditable warriors to feed.", "상업을 사랑하는 나라로서는, 이만큼 손님 떼 같은 군대가 몰려오고, 이토록 신용 있는 전사들을 먹이게 된 것은 큰 복이었던 것이다."], ["And the country which they came to protect is not military.", "그리고 그들이 지키러 온 나라는 군사적인 나라가 아니다."], ["For a long period of history they have let other people fight there.", "역사상 오랜 기간 그들은 다른 사람들이 그곳에서 싸우도록 내버려 두었다."], ["When the present writer went to survey with eagle glance the field of Waterloo, we asked the conductor of the diligence, a portly warlike-looking veteran, whether he had been at the battle.", "지금 이 글을 쓰는 이가 매서운 눈초리로 워털루 전장을 둘러보러 갔을 때, 우리는 역마차의 마부에게 — 풍채 좋고 전사처럼 생긴 노병이었다 — 그 전투에 참가한 적이 있느냐고 물었다. \"그렇게 멍청하진 않았지요.\""], ["\"Pas si bete\"--such an answer and sentiment as no Frenchman would own to--was his reply.", "그 어떤 프랑스인도 인정하려 들지 않을 그런 대답과 그런 정서가 그의 답이었다."], ["But, on the other hand, the postilion who drove us was a Viscount, a son of some bankrupt Imperial General, who accepted a pennyworth of beer on the road.", "그러나 다른 한편, 우리를 태운 역마차 마부는 자작이었으니, 파산한 어느 제정 시대 장군의 아들로서 길에서 일 페니어치 맥주를 받아 마시는 자였다."], ["The moral is surely a good one.", "그 교훈인즉 분명 좋은 것이렷다."]], [["This flat, flourishing, easy country never could have looked more rich and prosperous than in that opening summer of 1815, when its green fields and quiet cities were enlivened by multiplied red-coats: when its wide chaussees swarmed with brilliant English equipages: when its great canal-boats, gliding by rich pastures and pleasant quaint old villages, by old chateaux lying amongst old trees, were all crowded with well-to-do English travellers: when the soldier who drank at the village inn, not only drank, but paid his score; and Donald, the Highlander, billeted in the Flemish farm-house, rocked the baby's cradle, while Jean and Jeannette were out getting in the hay.", "이 평평하고 풍요롭고 살기 편한 나라가 1815년 그해 초여름만큼 부유하고 번창해 보인 적은 없었으리라. 그때 그곳의 푸른 들판과 고요한 도시들은 곳곳에 늘어난 붉은 군복으로 활기를 띠었고, 너른 큰길은 화려한 영국 마차들로 북적였으며, 기름진 목초지와 정겹고 예스러운 옛 마을들을 지나, 고목 사이에 자리한 오래된 성관들 곁을 미끄러져 가는 커다란 운하 배들은 죄다 부유한 영국 여행객들로 가득했다."], ["As our painters are bent on military subjects just now, I throw out this as a good subject for the pencil, to illustrate the principle of an honest English war.", "그때 시골 여관에서 술을 마시는 병사는 마시기만 하는 게 아니라 셈도 치렀으며, 플랑드르 농가에 숙영하게 된 고지인 도널드는 장과 자네트가 건초를 거두러 나간 동안 아기 요람을 흔들어 주었다. 요즘 우리네 화가들이 군사적 소재에 열을 올리고 있으니, 나는 이것을 정직한 영국식 전쟁의 원리를 그려 낼 좋은 화제로 던져 두는 바이다."], ["All looked as brilliant and harmless as a Hyde Park review.", "모든 것이 하이드 파크의 사열식만큼이나 눈부시고 해롭지 않아 보였다."], ["Meanwhile, Napoleon screened behind his curtain of frontier-fortresses, was preparing for the outbreak which was to drive all these orderly people into fury and blood; and lay so many of them low.", "그동안 국경 요새의 장막 뒤에 몸을 숨긴 나폴레옹은, 이 모든 질서 정연한 사람들을 광기와 피바다로 몰아넣고 그토록 많은 이들을 쓰러뜨릴 그 발발을 준비하고 있었던 것이다."]], [["Everybody had such a perfect feeling of confidence in the leader (for the resolute faith which the Duke of Wellington had inspired in the whole English nation was as intense as that more frantic enthusiasm with which at one time the French regarded Napoleon), the country seemed in so perfect a state of orderly defence, and the help at hand in case of need so near and overwhelming, that alarm was unknown, and our travellers, among whom two were naturally of a very timid sort, were, like all the other multiplied English tourists, entirely at ease.", "모두가 그 지휘관에게 더없는 신뢰를 품고 있었고(웰링턴 공작이 영국 국민 전체에게 불어넣은 그 굳건한 믿음은, 한때 프랑스인들이 나폴레옹을 우러러보던 그 광적인 열광 못지않게 강렬한 것이었다), 나라는 더없이 완벽한 방어 태세를 갖춘 듯 보였으며, 필요할 때 닥쳐올 원군은 그토록 가깝고 압도적이어서, 불안이라는 것은 알려진 바가 없었다. 그리하여 우리 여행객들은 — 그중 둘은 천성이 무척 겁이 많은 부류였지만 — 그 밖의 숱하게 늘어난 영국인 관광객들과 마찬가지로 더할 나위 없이 마음이 편했다."], ["The famous regiment, with so many of whose officers we have made acquaintance, was drafted in canal boats to Bruges and Ghent, thence to march to Brussels.", "우리가 그 장교들 가운데 여럿과 안면을 튼 그 유명한 연대는 운하 배편으로 브뤼주와 헨트로 보내졌고, 거기서 브뤼셀까지 행군할 예정이었다."], ["Jos accompanied the ladies in the public boats; the which all old travellers in Flanders must remember for the luxury and accommodation they afforded.", "조스는 공용 배편으로 두 숙녀와 동행했는데, 플랑드르를 여행해 본 옛 여행객이라면 누구나 그 배들이 베푼 호사와 안락함을 기억할 것이 분명하다."], ["So prodigiously good was the eating and drinking on board these sluggish but most comfortable vessels, that there are legends extant of an English traveller, who, coming to Belgium for a week, and travelling in one of these boats, was so delighted with the fare there that he went backwards and forwards from Ghent to Bruges perpetually until the railroads were invented, when he drowned himself on the last trip of the passage-boat.", "이 굼뜨지만 더없이 안락한 배에서의 먹을거리와 마실 거리가 어찌나 엄청나게 좋았던지, 일주일 일정으로 벨기에에 왔다가 이런 배 한 척에 올라 그곳 음식에 어찌나 흡족했던지 철도가 발명될 때까지 헨트와 브뤼주 사이를 끝없이 오갔고, 그 정기선의 마지막 운항 길에서 스스로 몸을 던져 익사했다는 어느 영국인 여행객의 전설이 전해 내려온다."], ["Jos's death was not to be of this sort, but his comfort was exceeding, and Mrs. O'Dowd insisted that he only wanted her sister Glorvina to make his happiness complete.", "조스의 죽음은 이런 종류는 아닐 터였으나, 그의 안락함은 그야말로 지극했고, 오다우드 부인은 그의 행복을 온전케 하는 데 모자란 것이라곤 제 누이동생 글로비나뿐이라고 우겨 댔다."], ["He sate on the roof of the cabin all day drinking Flemish beer, shouting for Isidor, his servant, and talking gallantly to the ladies.", "그는 온종일 선실 지붕 위에 앉아 플랑드르 맥주를 마시고, 제 하인 이지도르를 큰 소리로 불러 대며, 두 숙녀에게 호기롭게 말을 건넸다."]], [["His courage was prodigious.", "그의 용기는 가히 굉장했다."], ["\"Boney attack us!\" he cried.", "\"보니가 우리를 공격한다고!\""], ["\"My dear creature, my poor Emmy, don't be frightened.", "그가 외쳤다. \"내 사랑하는 누이야, 가엾은 우리 에미, 겁먹을 것 없다."], ["There's no danger.", "위험 따윈 없어."], ["The allies will be in Paris in two months, I tell you; when I'll take you to dine in the Palais Royal, by Jove!", "내 장담하건대 두 달 안에 동맹군이 파리에 입성할 거다. 그러면 내가 너를 팔레 루아얄로 데려가 식사를 시켜 주마, 맹세코!"], ["There are three hundred thousand Rooshians, I tell you, now entering France by Mayence and the Rhine--three hundred thousand under Wittgenstein and Barclay de Tolly, my poor love.", "내 너한테 말하지만, 지금 삼십만 명의 러시아군이 마인츠와 라인강을 거쳐 프랑스로 들어서고 있어. 비트겐슈타인과 바클라이 드 톨리 휘하의 삼십만이란 말이다, 가엾은 내 누이야."], ["You don't know military affairs, my dear.", "너는 군사 일을 모르지, 얘야. 나는 알아."], ["I do, and I tell you there's no infantry in France can stand against Rooshian infantry, and no general of Boney's that's fit to hold a candle to Wittgenstein.", "내 단언컨대 프랑스에는 러시아 보병에 맞설 보병이 없고, 보니의 장군들 가운데 비트겐슈타인의 발끝에라도 미칠 자가 없다."], ["Then there are the Austrians, they are five hundred thousand if a man, and they are within ten marches of the frontier by this time, under Schwartzenberg and Prince Charles.", "그다음엔 오스트리아군이 있지, 어림잡아도 오십만은 되는데, 지금쯤이면 슈바르첸베르크와 카를 대공 휘하로 국경에서 열흘 행군 거리 안에 들어와 있을 거다."], ["Then there are the Prooshians under the gallant Prince Marshal.", "또 용맹한 원수 공작 휘하의 프로이센군도 있지."], ["Show me a cavalry chief like him now that Murat is gone.", "뮈라가 사라진 지금, 그만한 기병 지휘관을 어디 한번 대 보라고."], ["Hey, Mrs. O'Dowd?", "어때요, 오다우드 부인?"], ["Do you think our little girl here need be afraid?", "여기 우리 작은 아가씨가 겁낼 필요가 있다고 생각하시오?"], ["Is there any cause for fear, Isidor?", "무슨 두려워할 까닭이라도 있느냐, 이지도르?"], ["Hey, sir?", "어떠냐, 이놈아?"], ["Get some more beer.\"", "맥주 좀 더 가져와라.\""]], [["Mrs. O'Dowd said that her \"Glorvina was not afraid of any man alive, let alone a Frenchman,\" and tossed off a glass of beer with a wink which expressed her liking for the beverage.", "오다우드 부인은 자기 \"글로비나는 프랑스인은 말할 것도 없고 살아 있는 그 어떤 사내도 두려워하지 않는다\"고 말하더니, 그 음료에 대한 애정을 드러내는 눈짓과 함께 맥주 한 잔을 쭉 들이켰다."]], [["Having frequently been in presence of the enemy, or, in other words, faced the ladies at Cheltenham and Bath, our friend, the Collector, had lost a great deal of his pristine timidity, and was now, especially when fortified with liquor, as talkative as might be.", "여러 차례 적과 마주한 적이 있는, 다시 말해 첼트넘과 바스에서 숙녀들과 맞선 적이 있는 우리의 벗 징세관은, 본래의 소심함을 상당 부분 잃어버린 터라, 이제는 특히 술기운으로 무장했을 때면 더없이 입담 좋게 떠들어 댔다."], ["He was rather a favourite with the regiment, treating the young officers with sumptuosity, and amusing them by his military airs.", "그는 연대 안에서 제법 인기를 누렸으니, 젊은 장교들을 푸짐하게 대접하고 군인 흉내로 그들을 즐겁게 해 준 덕이었다."], ["And as there is one well-known regiment of the army which travels with a goat heading the column, whilst another is led by a deer, George said with respect to his brother-in-law, that his regiment marched with an elephant.", "그리고 군대 안에 잘 알려진 한 연대가 염소를 행렬 선두에 세우고 행군하고, 또 다른 연대는 사슴을 앞세워 행군하듯이, 조지는 제 처남에 관해 그의 연대는 코끼리를 앞세우고 행군한다고 말했다."]], [["Since Amelia's introduction to the regiment, George began to be rather ashamed of some of the company to which he had been forced to present her; and determined, as he told Dobbin (with what satisfaction to the latter it need not be said), to exchange into some better regiment soon, and to get his wife away from those damned vulgar women.", "애밀리아가 연대에 소개된 이래로, 조지는 어쩔 수 없이 그녀를 인사시켜야 했던 몇몇 사람들이 다소 부끄러워지기 시작했다. 그래서 그는 도빈에게 말한 대로(상대방이 이를 얼마나 흡족하게 여겼는지는 말할 것도 없다), 머지않아 좀 더 나은 연대로 전속하여, 그 빌어먹을 천박한 여자들에게서 제 아내를 떼어 내기로 마음먹었다."], ["But this vulgarity of being ashamed of one's society is much more common among men than women (except very great ladies of fashion, who, to be sure, indulge in it); and Mrs.", "그러나 제가 어울리는 무리를 부끄러워하는 이 천박함은 여자들보다 남자들에게 훨씬 흔한 것이며(물론 더없이 대단한 유행계의 귀부인들은 예외로 이런 짓을 즐기지만), 천성이 꾸밈없고 가식 없는 애밀리아 부인에게는 남편이 제 나름의 섬세함으로 착각한 그런 인위적인 수치심이라곤 없었다."], ["Amelia, a natural and unaffected person, had none of that artificial shamefacedness which her husband mistook for delicacy on his own part.", "그리하여 오다우드 부인은 모자에 수탉 깃털을 꽂고, 배 위에는 아주 커다란 \"회중시게\"를 늘어뜨려 두고는 무슨 일이 있을 때마다 그것을 울려 대며, 그것이 \"혼인\""], ["Thus Mrs. O'Dowd had a cock's plume in her hat, and a very large \"repayther\"", "후 \"마차\"에 오를 적에 자기 \"아부지\"가 선물해 준 것이라고 이야기하곤 했다."], ["on her stomach, which she used to ring on all occasions, narrating how it had been presented to her by her fawther, as she stipt into the car'ge after her mar'ge; and these ornaments, with other outward peculiarities of the Major's wife, gave excruciating agonies to Captain Osborne, when his wife and the Major's came in contact; whereas Amelia was only amused by the honest lady's eccentricities, and not in the least ashamed of her company.", "이런 장신구들이며 소령 부인의 다른 외양상의 별난 점들은, 제 아내와 소령 부인이 한자리에 마주칠 때면 오즈번 대위에게 견딜 수 없는 고통을 안겨 주었다. 반면 애밀리아는 그 정직한 부인의 별난 점들에 그저 즐거워할 뿐, 그녀와 함께 있는 것을 조금도 부끄러워하지 않았다."]], [["As they made that well-known journey, which almost every Englishman of middle rank has travelled since, there might have been more instructive, but few more entertaining, companions than Mrs. Major O'Dowd.", "그 후로 거의 모든 중간 계층 영국인이 다녀온 그 유명한 여정을 그들이 밟아 가는 동안, 더 유익한 길동무는 더러 있었을지 몰라도 소령 부인 오다우드보다 더 즐거운 길동무는 좀처럼 없었으리라."], ["\"Talk about kenal boats; my dear!", "\"운하 배 얘기를 하시는데, 글쎄요!"], ["Ye should see the kenal boats between Dublin and Ballinasloe.", "더블린과 밸리나슬로 사이를 오가는 운하 배를 보셔야 하는데 말이에요."], ["It's there the rapid travelling is; and the beautiful cattle.", "거기야말로 쾌속 여행이라는 게 있고, 또 그 멋진 가축들이라니."], ["Sure me fawther got a goold medal (and his Excellency himself eat a slice of it, and said never was finer mate in his loif) for a four-year-old heifer, the like of which ye never saw in this country any day.\"", "우리 아부지가 글쎄 금메달을 타셨다니까요(각하께서 친히 그 고기 한 점을 잡수시고는 평생 그보다 좋은 고기는 없었다고 하셨대요), 네 살배기 암송아지로 말이에요. 이 나라에선 죽었다 깨어나도 못 볼 그런 놈이지요.\""], ["And Jos owned with a sigh, \"that for good streaky beef, really mingled with fat and lean, there was no country like England.\"", "그러자 조스가 한숨을 쉬며 인정하기를, \"기름기와 살코기가 정말로 알맞게 섞인 좋은 줄무늬 쇠고기로 말할 것 같으면, 영국만 한 나라가 없지요.\""]], [["\"Except Ireland, where all your best mate comes from,\"", "\"당신네 최상품 고기가 죄다 나오는 아일랜드만 빼면 그렇지요.\""], ["said the Major's lady; proceeding, as is not unusual with patriots of her nation, to make comparisons greatly in favour of her own country.", "소령 부인은 그렇게 말하고는, 자기 나라의 애국자들에게 드물지 않은 일이듯, 제 나라에 크게 유리하도록 비교를 늘어놓기 시작했다. 브뤼주의 시장을 더블린의 시장과 비교한다는 발상은, 비록 본인이 먼저 꺼낸 것이긴 했으나, 그녀로서는 엄청난 경멸과 조롱을 자아내는 것이었다."], ["The idea of comparing the market at Bruges with those of Dublin, although she had suggested it herself, caused immense scorn and derision on her part.", "\"저 시장 꼭대기에 있는 저 낡아 빠진 물건은 대체 무슨 뜻인지 좀 일러 주시면 고맙겠수다.\" 그녀가 그 낡은 탑이라도 무너뜨릴 만한 조롱을 터뜨리며 말했다."], ["\"I'll thank ye tell me what they mean by that old gazabo on the top of the market-place,\"", "그들이 지나가는 동안 그 도시는 영국 군인들로 가득했다. 아침이면 영국 나팔 소리가 그들을 깨웠고, 해 질 무렵이면 영국 횡적과 북소리에 맞춰 잠자리에 들었다."], ["said she, in a burst of ridicule fit to have brought the old tower down.", "온 나라와 유럽이 무장을 갖추고 있었고, 역사상 가장 위대한 사건이 임박해 있었다."], ["The place was full of English soldiery as they passed.", "그런데도 그 못지않게 이 일과 관계가 있는 정직한 페기 오다우드는, 밸리나패드며, 글렌멀로니의 마구간에 있는 말들이며, 거기서 마시던 \"포두주\""], ["English bugles woke them in the morning; at nightfall they went to bed to the note of the British fife and drum: all the country and Europe was in arms, and the greatest event of history pending: and honest Peggy O'Dowd, whom it concerned as well as another, went on prattling about Ballinafad, and the horses in the stables at Glenmalony, and the clar't drunk there; and Jos Sedley interposed about curry and rice at Dumdum; and Amelia thought about her husband, and how best she should show her love for him; as if these were the great topics of the world.", "이야기를 줄줄이 늘어놓았고, 조스 세들리는 덤덤에서 먹던 카레와 쌀밥 이야기로 끼어들었으며, 애밀리아는 제 남편 생각과, 그를 향한 사랑을 어떻게 가장 잘 보여 줄까 하는 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 마치 이런 것들이야말로 세상의 중대한 화제인 양 말이다."]], [["Those who like to lay down the History-book, and to speculate upon what MIGHT have happened in the world, but for the fatal occurrence of what actually did take place (a most puzzling, amusing, ingenious, and profitable kind of meditation), have no doubt often thought to themselves what a specially bad time Napoleon took to come back from Elba, and to let loose his eagle from Gulf San Juan to Notre Dame.", "역사책을 내려놓고서, 실제로 일어난 일이라는 운명적인 사태만 없었더라면 세상에 무슨 일이 일어날 수 있었을까를 추측해 보기를 좋아하는 이들이라면(더없이 골치 아프면서도 재미있고, 기발하고, 유익한 종류의 사색이다), 분명 나폴레옹이 엘바섬에서 돌아와 그 독수리를 산후안만에서 노트르담까지 풀어놓기에 유독 고약한 시기를 골랐구나 하고 속으로 생각해 본 적이 있을 것이다."], ["The historians on our side tell us that the armies of the allied powers were all providentially on a war-footing, and ready to bear down at a moment's notice upon the Elban Emperor.", "우리 편 사가들은 동맹 열강의 군대가 하나같이 천우신조로 전투태세를 갖추고서, 한순간의 통보만으로도 엘바섬의 황제에게 들이닥칠 채비가 되어 있었다고 일러 준다."], ["The august jobbers assembled at Vienna, and carving out the kingdoms of Europe according to their wisdom, had such causes of quarrel among themselves as might have set the armies which had overcome Napoleon to fight against each other, but for the return of the object of unanimous hatred and fear.", "빈에 모여서 제 나름의 지혜에 따라 유럽의 왕국들을 나누어 먹던 그 존엄한 협잡꾼들은, 만장일치로 미움받고 두려움받던 그 대상이 돌아오지만 않았더라면, 나폴레옹을 무찔렀던 그 군대들끼리 서로 싸움 붙일 만한 다툼거리를 저희들 사이에 안고 있었던 것이다."], ["This monarch had an army in full force because he had jobbed to himself Poland, and was determined to keep it: another had robbed half Saxony, and was bent upon maintaining his acquisition: Italy was the object of a third's solicitude.", "어느 군주는 폴란드를 제 몫으로 가로채고 그것을 지키기로 작정한 터라 전력을 갖춘 군대를 거느리고 있었고, 또 다른 군주는 작센의 절반을 약탈해 놓고는 그 획득물을 지키려 안달이었으며, 이탈리아는 세 번째 군주의 관심사였다."], ["Each was protesting against the rapacity of the other; and could the Corsican but have waited in prison until all these parties were by the ears, he might have returned and reigned unmolested.", "저마다 상대방의 탐욕에 항의를 늘어놓고 있었으니, 만일 그 코르시카 사람이 이 모든 무리가 서로 멱살을 잡을 때까지 감옥에서 기다리기만 했더라면, 돌아와 아무 방해도 받지 않고 군림할 수 있었으리라."], ["But what would have become of our story and all our friends, then?", "그러나 그랬다면 우리 이야기와 우리의 모든 벗들은 어찌 되었겠는가?"], ["If all the drops in it were dried up, what would become of the sea?", "그 안의 물방울이 죄다 말라 버린다면, 바다는 어찌 되겠는가?"]], [["In the meanwhile the business of life and living, and the pursuits of pleasure, especially, went on as if no end were to be expected to them, and no enemy in front.", "그동안에도 삶을 꾸리고 살아가는 일이며, 특히 즐거움을 좇는 일은, 마치 거기에 끝이라곤 기대할 수 없고 눈앞에 적이라곤 없다는 듯이 이어졌다."], ["When our travellers arrived at Brussels, in which their regiment was quartered, a great piece of good fortune, as all said, they found themselves in one of the gayest and most brilliant little capitals in Europe, and where all the Vanity Fair booths were laid out with the most tempting liveliness and splendour.", "우리 여행객들이 자기네 연대가 주둔하고 있는 브뤼셀에 도착했을 때, 모두가 입을 모아 큰 행운이라 했듯이, 그들은 유럽에서 가장 흥겹고 가장 눈부신 작은 수도 가운데 하나에 발을 들였음을 알게 되었으니, 그곳에는 허영의 시장의 온갖 노점이 더없이 유혹적인 활기와 화려함으로 펼쳐져 있었다."], ["Gambling was here in profusion, and dancing in plenty: feasting was there to fill with delight that great gourmand of a Jos: there was a theatre where a miraculous Catalani was delighting all hearers: beautiful rides, all enlivened with martial splendour; a rare old city, with strange costumes and wonderful architecture, to delight the eyes of little Amelia, who had never before seen a foreign country, and fill her with charming surprises: so that now and for a few weeks'", "이곳에는 도박이 흘러넘쳤고 춤판도 무성했으며, 저 대식가 조스를 환희로 가득 채워 줄 잔치가 있었고, 신기에 가까운 카탈라니가 모든 청중을 매혹시키는 극장이 있었으며, 군사적 화려함으로 활기를 띤 멋진 승마길이 있었다. 외국이라곤 한 번도 본 적이 없는 어린 애밀리아의 눈을 즐겁게 하고 매혹적인 놀라움으로 채워 줄, 진기한 옷차림과 경이로운 건축을 자랑하는 보기 드문 옛 도시가 있었다."], ["space in a fine handsome lodging, whereof the expenses were borne by Jos and Osborne, who was flush of money and full of kind attentions to his wife--for about a fortnight, I say, during which her honeymoon ended, Mrs. Amelia was as pleased and happy as any little bride out of England.", "그리하여 지금부터 몇 주 동안, 조스와 오즈번이 비용을 댄 멋지고 훌륭한 숙소에서 — 오즈번은 돈주머니가 두둑했고 제 아내에게 다정한 배려를 아끼지 않았다 — 그러니까 그녀의 신혼 기간이 끝나기까지 약 보름 동안, 애밀리아 부인은 영국에서 나온 그 어떤 어린 신부 못지않게 기뻐하고 행복해했다."]], [["Every day during this happy time there was novelty and amusement for all parties.", "이 행복한 시절 내내 날마다 모두에게 새로움과 즐길 거리가 있었다."], ["There was a church to see, or a picture-gallery--there was a ride, or an opera.", "구경할 교회가 있는가 하면 화랑이 있었고, 승마가 있는가 하면 오페라가 있었다."], ["The bands of the regiments were making music at all hours.", "연대의 군악대는 시도 때도 없이 음악을 연주했다."], ["The greatest folks of England walked in the Park--there was a perpetual military festival.", "영국에서 가장 대단한 사람들이 공원을 거닐었으니, 끝없는 군사 축제였던 것이다."], ["George, taking out his wife to a new jaunt or junket every night, was quite pleased with himself as usual, and swore he was becoming quite a domestic character.", "조지는 밤마다 아내를 데리고 새로운 나들이며 잔치에 나서면서, 여느 때처럼 제 자신에게 무척 흡족해하며, 자기가 아주 가정적인 사람이 되어 가고 있노라 큰소리쳤다."], ["And a jaunt or a junket with HIM!", "게다가 그와 함께하는 나들이나 잔치라니!"], ["Was it not enough to set this little heart beating with joy?", "이 작은 가슴을 기쁨으로 뛰게 하기에 그것으로 충분하지 않았겠는가?"], ["Her letters home to her mother were filled with delight and gratitude at this season.", "이 무렵 그녀가 친정어머니에게 보낸 편지들은 환희와 감사로 가득했다."], ["Her husband bade her buy laces, millinery, jewels, and gimcracks of all sorts.", "남편은 그녀에게 레이스며, 모자며, 보석이며, 온갖 자질구레한 장신구를 사라고 일렀다."], ["Oh, he was the kindest, best, and most generous of men!", "오, 그는 세상에서 가장 다정하고, 가장 훌륭하고, 가장 너그러운 사내였던 것이다!"]], [["The sight of the very great company of lords and ladies and fashionable persons who thronged the town, and appeared in every public place, filled George's truly British soul with intense delight.", "이 도시로 몰려들어 모든 공공장소에 나타나는, 그 숱한 귀족 나리들과 귀부인들과 유행계 인사들을 보는 것은 조지의 더없이 영국인다운 영혼을 강렬한 환희로 채워 주었다."], ["They flung off that happy frigidity and insolence of demeanour which occasionally characterises the great at home, and appearing in numberless public places, condescended to mingle with the rest of the company whom they met there.", "그들은 본국에서는 이따금 대단한 이들의 특징이 되는 그 잘난 척하는 냉담함과 거만한 태도를 벗어던지고, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 공공장소에 모습을 드러내며, 거기서 마주치는 나머지 사람들과 황송하게도 섞여 어울렸다."], ["One night at a party given by the general of the division to which George's regiment belonged, he had the honour of dancing with Lady Blanche Thistlewood, Lord Bareacres'", "어느 밤, 조지의 연대가 속한 사단의 장군이 베푼 연회에서, 그는 베리에이커스 경의 딸인 블랜치 시슬우드 영애와 춤추는 영광을 누렸다."], ["daughter; he bustled for ices and refreshments for the two noble ladies; he pushed and squeezed for Lady Bareacres' carriage; he bragged about the Countess when he got home, in a way which his own father could not have surpassed.", "그는 그 두 고귀한 숙녀를 위해 아이스크림과 다과를 가져오려 부산을 떨었고, 베리에이커스 부인의 마차를 잡으려 밀치고 비집고 다녔으며, 집에 돌아와서는 제 아버지조차 능가하지 못할 만큼 그 백작 부인 자랑을 늘어놓았다."], ["He called upon the ladies the next day; he rode by their side in the Park; he asked their party to a great dinner at a restaurateur's, and was quite wild with exultation when they agreed to come.", "이튿날 그는 그 숙녀들을 방문했고, 공원에서 그들 곁에서 말을 달렸으며, 어느 요릿집의 성대한 만찬에 그 일행을 초대하고는, 그들이 오겠노라 응하자 기뻐 어쩔 줄을 몰라 했다."], ["Old Bareacres, who had not much pride and a large appetite, would go for a dinner anywhere.", "자존심이라곤 별로 없고 식욕만 왕성한 늙은 베리에이커스는 만찬이라면 어디든 갈 위인이었다."]], [["\"I hope there will be no women besides our own party,\"", "\"우리 일행 말고 다른 여자들은 없으면 좋겠구나.\""], ["Lady Bareacres said, after reflecting upon the invitation which had been made, and accepted with too much precipitancy.", "베리에이커스 부인은 너무 성급하게 응낙해 버린 그 초대를 곱씹어 본 뒤 말했다."]], [["\"Gracious Heaven, Mamma--you don't suppose the man would bring his wife,\"", "\"어머나 맙소사, 어머니, 그 사람이 설마 제 아내를 데려오기야 하겠어요.\""], ["shrieked Lady Blanche, who had been languishing in George's arms in the newly imported waltz for hours the night before.", "블랜치 영애가 새된 소리로 말했다. 그녀는 바로 전날 밤, 갓 들여온 왈츠를 추며 몇 시간이나 조지의 품에서 나른하게 몸을 맡겼던 터였다."], ["\"The men are bearable, but their women--\"", "\"남자들은 봐줄 만하지만, 그 여자들이란—\""]], [["\"Wife, just married, dev'lish pretty woman, I hear,\"", "\"아내가, 갓 결혼했다는데, 듣자 하니 지독하게 예쁜 여자라더라.\""], ["the old Earl said.", "늙은 백작이 말했다."]], [["\"Well, my dear Blanche,\"", "\"그래, 사랑하는 블랜치야,\""], ["said the mother, \"I suppose, as Papa wants to go, we must go; but we needn't know them in England, you know.\"", "어머니가 말했다. \"아무래도 네 아버지가 가고 싶어 하시니 우리도 가야겠지."], ["And so, determined to cut their new acquaintance in Bond Street, these great folks went to eat his dinner at Brussels, and condescending to make him pay for their pleasure, showed their dignity by making his wife uncomfortable, and carefully excluding her from the conversation.", "하지만 영국에선 그 사람들과 모르는 척하면 그만 아니겠니.\" 그리하여 본드 거리에서는 그 새 친지들을 모른 척하기로 작정한 채, 이 대단한 사람들은 브뤼셀에서 그의 만찬을 먹으러 갔고, 황송하게도 제 즐거움의 값을 그가 치르게 하면서, 그의 아내를 거북하게 만들고 대화에서 그녀를 용의주도하게 따돌리는 것으로 제 위엄을 과시했다."], ["This is a species of dignity in which the high-bred British female reigns supreme.", "이것은 가문 좋은 영국 여성이 단연 으뜸으로 군림하는 종류의 위엄이다."], ["To watch the behaviour of a fine lady to other and humbler women, is a very good sport for a philosophical frequenter of Vanity Fair.", "우아한 귀부인이 자기보다 못하고 미천한 여자들을 대하는 태도를 지켜보는 것은, 허영의 시장을 철학적으로 드나드는 이에게는 더없이 좋은 구경거리다."]], [["This festival, on which honest George spent a great deal of money, was the very dismallest of all the entertainments which Amelia had in her honeymoon.", "정직한 조지가 큰돈을 쓴 이 잔치는, 애밀리아가 신혼 동안 누린 모든 여흥 가운데 가장 음울하기 짝이 없는 것이었다. 그녀는 그 잔치에 관해 더없이 가련한 내용을 친정어머니에게 적어 보냈다."], ["She wrote the most piteous accounts of the feast home to her mamma: how the Countess of Bareacres would not answer when spoken to; how Lady Blanche stared at her with her eye-glass; and what a rage Captain Dobbin was in at their behaviour; and how my lord, as they came away from the feast, asked to see the bill, and pronounced it a d---- bad dinner, and d---- dear. But though Amelia told all these stories, and wrote home regarding her guests'", "베리에이커스 백작 부인이 말을 걸어도 대꾸하지 않더라는 것, 블랜치 영애가 외알 안경으로 자기를 빤히 쳐다보더라는 것, 그들의 행태에 도빈 대위가 어찌나 화를 냈는지, 또 백작 나리는 잔치가 파해 자리를 뜨면서 계산서를 보자고 하더니 빌어먹게 형편없는 만찬에 빌어먹게 비싸다고 내뱉었다는 것 따위를 말이다."], ["rudeness, and her own discomfiture, old Mrs. Sedley was mightily pleased nevertheless, and talked about Emmy's friend, the Countess of Bareacres, with such assiduity that the news how his son was entertaining peers and peeresses actually came to Osborne's ears in the City.", "그러나 애밀리아가 이 모든 이야기를 전하며 손님들의 무례함과 자신의 당혹스러움에 관해 적어 보냈는데도, 늙은 세들리 부인은 그럼에도 한껏 기뻐하며, 에미의 친구인 베리에이커스 백작 부인 이야기를 어찌나 부지런히 떠벌렸던지, 제 아들이 귀족 나리들과 귀부인들을 대접하고 있다는 소식이 시내에 있는 오즈번의 귀에까지 들어가고 말았다."]], [["Those who know the present Lieutenant-General Sir George Tufto, K.C.B., and have seen him, as they may on most days in the season, padded and in stays, strutting down Pall Mall with a rickety swagger on his high-heeled lacquered boots, leering under the bonnets of passers-by, or riding a showy chestnut, and ogling broughams in the Parks--those who know the present Sir George Tufto would hardly recognise the daring Peninsular and Waterloo officer.", "현재의 중장 조지 터프토 경, 바스 훈장 이등 기사를 아는 이들, 그리고 사교철이면 거의 매일 볼 수 있듯이, 속을 채워 넣고 코르셋으로 조인 채, 굽 높은 칠피 장화를 신고 비틀거리는 거드름을 떨며 폴 몰 거리를 활보하면서, 지나가는 여인들의 보닛 아래를 곁눈질하거나, 화려한 밤색 말을 타고 공원의 사륜마차들에 추파를 던지는 그를 본 적이 있는 이들이라면 — 현재의 터프토 경을 아는 이들이라면, 저 대담했던 이베리아 반도와 워털루의 장교를 좀처럼 알아보지 못할 것이다."], ["He has thick curling brown hair and black eyebrows now, and his whiskers are of the deepest purple.", "그는 지금은 곱슬곱슬한 짙은 갈색 머리에 검은 눈썹을 하고 있고, 구레나룻은 더없이 짙은 자줏빛이다."], ["He was light-haired and bald in 1815, and stouter in the person and in the limbs, which especially have shrunk very much of late.", "1815년에 그는 머리숱이 옅고 대머리였으며, 몸피와 팔다리도 더 굵었는데, 특히 팔다리는 근래에 들어 무척 쪼그라들었다."], ["When he was about seventy years of age (he is now nearly eighty), his hair, which was very scarce and quite white, suddenly grew thick, and brown, and curly, and his whiskers and eyebrows took their present colour.", "그가 일흔 살쯤 되었을 때(지금은 여든에 가깝다), 무척 성기고 새하얗던 그의 머리카락이 별안간 숱 많고 갈색이며 곱슬곱슬하게 자라났고, 구레나룻과 눈썹도 지금의 색을 띠게 되었다."], ["Ill-natured people say that his chest is all wool, and that his hair, because it never grows, is a wig.", "심술궂은 사람들은 그의 가슴팍이 죄다 양털이고, 그의 머리카락은 도무지 자라는 법이 없으니 가발이라고들 한다."], ["Tom Tufto, with whose father he quarrelled ever so many years ago, declares that Mademoiselle de Jaisey, of the French theatre, pulled his grandpapa's hair off in the green-room; but Tom is notoriously spiteful and jealous; and the General's wig has nothing to do with our story.", "여러 해 전에 그 아비와 다툰 적이 있는 톰 터프토는, 프랑스 극장의 마드무아젤 드 제지가 분장실에서 자기 할아버지의 머리카락을 잡아 뽑았다고 단언한다. 그러나 톰은 악명 높게 심술궂고 시샘이 많은 자이니, 그 장군의 가발은 우리 이야기와는 아무 상관이 없다."]], [["One day, as some of our friends of the --th were sauntering in the flower-market of Brussels, having been to see the Hotel de Ville, which Mrs.", "어느 날, 우리 ××연대의 벗들 몇몇이 브뤼셀의 꽃시장을 거닐고 있었다."], ["Major O'Dowd declared was not near so large or handsome as her fawther's mansion of Glenmalony, an officer of rank, with an orderly behind him, rode up to the market, and descending from his horse, came amongst the flowers, and selected the very finest bouquet which money could buy.", "시청을 구경하고 난 참이었는데, 소령 부인 오다우드는 그것이 자기 \"아부지\"의 글렌멀로니 저택만큼 크지도 멋지지도 않다고 단언했다. 그때 뒤에 전령을 거느린 고위 장교 한 사람이 시장으로 말을 몰고 와 말에서 내리더니, 꽃들 사이로 들어와 돈으로 살 수 있는 가장 멋진 꽃다발을 골랐다."], ["The beautiful bundle being tied up in a paper, the officer remounted, giving the nosegay into the charge of his military groom, who carried it with a grin, following his chief, who rode away in great state and self-satisfaction.", "그 아름다운 꽃다발을 종이에 싸자, 장교는 다시 말에 올라 그 꽃다발을 자기 군속 마부에게 맡겼고, 마부는 그것을 히죽거리며 받아 들고, 한껏 위풍당당하게 자기만족에 차서 말을 몰아 떠나는 상관을 뒤따랐다."]], [["\"You should see the flowers at Glenmalony,\"", "\"글렌멀로니의 꽃들을 보셔야 하는데 말이에요.\""], ["Mrs. O'Dowd was remarking.", "오다우드 부인이 말하고 있었다."], ["\"Me fawther has three Scotch garners with nine helpers.", "\"우리 아부지는 스코틀랜드 정원사를 셋이나 두셨는데, 보조 일꾼이 아홉이라니까요."], ["We have an acre of hot-houses, and pines as common as pays in the sayson.", "우리 집엔 온실이 일 에이커나 되고, 파인애플이 제철엔 완두콩만큼이나 흔하답니다."], ["Our greeps weighs six pounds every bunch of 'em, and upon me honour and conscience I think our magnolias is as big as taykettles.\"", "우리 집 포도는 한 송이가 죄다 육 파운드씩 나가고, 제 명예와 양심을 걸고 말씀드리는데 우리 집 목련은 주전자만 한 것 같아요.\""]], [["Dobbin, who never used to \"draw out\" Mrs.", "저 못된 오즈번이 즐겨 하듯 오다우드 부인을 \"부추겨\""], ["O'Dowd as that wicked Osborne delighted in doing (much to Amelia's terror, who implored him to spare her), fell back in the crowd, crowing and sputtering until he reached a safe distance, when he exploded amongst the astonished market-people with shrieks of yelling laughter.", "떠벌리게 만드는 짓이라곤 결코 하지 않던(애밀리아는 그럴 때마다 질겁하며 제발 그러지 말라고 오즈번에게 애원했다) 도빈은, 안전한 거리에 이를 때까지 컥컥거리고 푸푸대며 사람들 틈으로 물러나더니, 마침내 그 거리에 다다르자 놀란 시장 사람들 한복판에서 째지는 듯한 웃음을 폭발시켰다."]], [["\"Hwhat's that gawky guggling about?\" said Mrs.", "\"저 멀대 같은 사람은 무얼 저리 컬컬대는 거예요?\""], ["O'Dowd. \"Is it his nose bleedn?", "오다우드 부인이 말했다."], ["He always used to say 'twas his nose bleedn, till he must have pomped all the blood out of 'um.", "\"코피라도 터졌나? 늘 코피가 났다고 우기더니, 이젠 몸속 피를 죄다 펌프질해 뺐을 텐데 말이지."], ["An't the magnolias at Glenmalony as big as taykettles, O'Dowd?\"", "글렌멀로니의 목련이 주전자만큼 크지 않수, 오다우드?\""]], [["\"'Deed then they are, and bigger, Peggy,\" the Major said.", "\"암, 그렇고말고, 그보다 더 크지, 페기.\""], ["When the conversation was interrupted in the manner stated by the arrival of the officer who purchased the bouquet.", "소령이 말했다. 그때 대화는 앞서 말한 대로 그 꽃다발을 산 장교의 등장으로 끊겼다."]], [["\"Devlish fine horse--who is it?\"", "\"기막히게 멋진 말인데, 누구지요?\""], ["George asked.", "조지가 물었다."]], [["\"You should see me brother Molloy Malony's horse, Molasses, that won the cop at the Curragh,\"", "\"우리 오빠 멀로이 멀로니의 말 몰래시스를 보셔야 하는데, 커러에서 우승컵을 탔다니까요.\""], ["the Major's wife was exclaiming, and was continuing the family history, when her husband interrupted her by saying--", "소령 부인이 외치면서 집안 내력을 계속 늘어놓으려 하는데, 남편이 이렇게 말하며 그녀를 가로막았다."]], [["\"It's General Tufto, who commands the ---- cavalry division\"; adding quietly, \"he and I were both shot in the same leg at Talavera.\"", "\"저분은 ××기병 사단을 지휘하시는 터프토 장군이오.\" 그러더니 조용히 덧붙였다. \"그분과 나는 탈라베라에서 같은 다리에 총을 맞았지.\""]], [["\"Where you got your step,\"", "\"그 덕에 당신이 진급했지요.\""], ["said George with a laugh.", "조지가 웃으며 말했다."], ["\"General Tufto!", "\"터프토 장군이라!"], ["Then, my dear, the Crawleys are come.\"", "그렇다면, 여보, 크롤리 부부가 온 거로군.\""]], [["Amelia's heart fell--she knew not why.", "애밀리아는 가슴이 내려앉았다 — 왜 그런지는 알 수 없었다."], ["The sun did not seem to shine so bright.", "햇살이 그리 밝게 빛나는 것 같지 않았다."], ["The tall old roofs and gables looked less picturesque all of a sudden, though it was a brilliant sunset, and one of the brightest and most beautiful days at the end of May.", "비록 눈부신 일몰이요, 오월 끝자락의 가장 밝고 아름다운 날들 가운데 하루였는데도, 높다란 옛 지붕들과 박공들이 별안간 한결 운치 없어 보였다."]], [["Brussels\nMr. Jos had hired a pair of horses for his open carriage, with which cattle, and the smart London vehicle, he made a very tolerable figure in the drives about Brussels.", "조스는 자신의 무개마차를 끌 말 두 마리를 빌렸는데, 그 말과 런던에서 가져온 맵시 좋은 마차 덕에 브뤼셀 인근을 드라이브할 때면 제법 그럴듯한 행색이 되었다."], ["George purchased a horse for his private riding, and he and Captain Dobbin would often accompany the carriage in which Jos and his sister took daily excursions of pleasure.", "조지는 자신이 직접 탈 말 한 필을 샀고, 그와 도빈 대위는 조스와 그 누이가 날마다 즐기는 유람에 나설 때 그 마차를 따라나서곤 했다."], ["They went out that day in the park for their accustomed diversion, and there, sure enough, George's remark with regard to the arrival of Rawdon Crawley and his wife proved to be correct.", "그날 그들은 늘 하던 대로 공원으로 기분 전환을 나갔는데, 거기서 로던 크롤리 부부의 도착에 관한 조지의 말이 어김없이 사실로 드러났다. 브뤼셀에서도 가장 지체 높은 인사들로 이루어진 한 무리의 기마행렬 한가운데, 더없이 어여쁘고 몸에 꼭 맞는 승마복 차림의 레베카가 보였다."], ["In the midst of a little troop of horsemen, consisting of some of the very greatest persons in Brussels, Rebecca was seen in the prettiest and tightest of riding-habits, mounted on a beautiful little Arab, which she rode to perfection (having acquired the art at Queen's Crawley, where the Baronet, Mr. Pitt, and Rawdon himself had given her many lessons), and by the side of the gallant General Tufto.", "그녀는 아름다운 작은 아라비아 말에 올라 더할 나위 없이 능숙하게 말을 몰았는데(이 재주는 퀸스크롤리에서 익힌 것으로, 그곳에서 준남작과 피트 씨, 그리고 로던 본인까지 그녀에게 숱한 가르침을 베풀었던 것이다), 그 옆에는 늠름한 터프토 장군이 있었다."]], [["\"Sure it's the Juke himself,\" cried Mrs.", "\"세상에, 저분이 바로 공작님이세요.\""], ["Major O'Dowd to Jos, who began to blush violently; \"and that's Lord Uxbridge on the bay.", "오다우드 소령 부인이 조스에게 외치자, 조스는 격하게 얼굴을 붉히기 시작했다. \"저기 밤색 말 위에 계신 분이 억스브리지 경이고요."], ["How elegant he looks!", "어쩜 저리 우아하실까!"], ["Me brother, Molloy Malony, is as like him as two pays.\"", "우리 오라버니 몰로이 말로니가 저분을 두 콩쪽처럼 빼닮았답니다.\""]], [["Rebecca did not make for the carriage; but as soon as she perceived her old acquaintance Amelia seated in it, acknowledged her presence by a gracious nod and smile, and by kissing and shaking her fingers playfully in the direction of the vehicle.", "레베카는 마차 쪽으로 다가오지는 않았다. 그러나 거기에 옛 지인인 애밀리아가 앉아 있는 것을 알아보자마자, 우아하게 고개를 끄덕이고 미소 지으며, 마차 쪽으로 손끝에 입을 맞춰 장난스럽게 흔들어 보이는 것으로 그 존재를 알은체했다."], ["Then she resumed her conversation with General Tufto, who asked \"who the fat officer was in the gold-laced cap?\" on which Becky replied, \"that he was an officer in the East Indian service.\"", "그러고는 다시 터프토 장군과 이야기를 이어갔는데, 장군이 \"저 금줄 두른 모자를 쓴 뚱뚱한 장교는 누구냐?\"고 묻자, 베키는 \"동인도 회사에 복무하는 장교\"라고 대답했다."], ["But Rawdon Crawley rode out of the ranks of his company, and came up and shook hands heartily with Amelia, and said to Jos, \"Well, old boy, how are you?\"", "그런데 로던 크롤리가 자기 일행의 대열에서 빠져나와 다가오더니 애밀리아와 다정하게 악수를 나누고는, 조스에게 \"어이, 친구, 잘 지냈나?\""], ["and stared in Mrs.", "하고 말했다."], ["O'Dowd's face and at the black cock's feathers until she began to think she had made a conquest of him.", "그러고는 오다우드 부인의 얼굴과 검은 수탉 깃털을 어찌나 빤히 쳐다보던지, 부인은 자기가 그를 사로잡았다고 생각하기 시작했다."]], [["George, who had been delayed behind, rode up almost immediately with Dobbin, and they touched their caps to the august personages, among whom Osborne at once perceived Mrs. Crawley.", "뒤처져 있던 조지는 도빈과 함께 거의 곧바로 말을 몰고 다가와, 그 지체 높은 인사들에게 모자를 들어 인사했는데, 오즈번은 그 무리 가운데서 단번에 크롤리 부인을 알아보았다."], ["He was delighted to see Rawdon leaning over his carriage familiarly and talking to Amelia, and met the aide-de-camp's cordial greeting with more than corresponding warmth.", "그는 로던이 자기 마차에 친근하게 기대어 애밀리아와 이야기하는 모습을 보고 기뻐했고, 부관의 정겨운 인사에 그에 못지않은, 아니 그 이상의 온정으로 응했다."], ["The nods between Rawdon and Dobbin were of the very faintest specimens of politeness.", "로던과 도빈이 주고받은 목례는 예의의 표본 가운데서도 더없이 희미한 축에 드는 것이었다."]], [["Crawley told George where they were stopping with General Tufto at the Hotel du Parc, and George made his friend promise to come speedily to Osborne's own residence.", "크롤리는 자기들이 터프토 장군과 함께 파르크 호텔에 묵고 있다고 조지에게 일러주었고, 조지는 친구더러 어서 오즈번 본인의 거처로 와달라고 약조를 받아냈다."], ["\"Sorry I hadn't seen you three days ago,\"", "\"사흘 전에 자넬 못 만난 게 아쉽군.\""], ["George said.", "조지가 말했다."], ["\"Had a dinner at the Restaurateur's--rather a nice thing.", "\"식당에서 만찬을 했거든. 제법 괜찮은 자리였어."], ["Lord Bareacres, and the Countess, and Lady Blanche, were good enough to dine with us--wish we'd had you.\"", "베어에이커스 경과 백작부인, 블랜치 영애까지 황송하게도 우리와 함께 식사해주셨지. 자네도 있었더라면 좋았을 텐데.\""], ["Having thus let his friend know his claims to be a man of fashion, Osborne parted from Rawdon, who followed the august squadron down an alley into which they cantered, while George and Dobbin resumed their places, one on each side of Amelia's carriage.", "이렇게 자기가 사교계 인사임을 친구에게 알린 다음, 오즈번은 로던과 헤어졌다. 로던은 그 지체 높은 기마대를 따라 어느 샛길로 빠르게 말을 몰아 사라졌고, 한편 조지와 도빈은 다시 애밀리아의 마차 양옆에 한 사람씩 자리를 잡았다."]], [["\"How well the Juke looked,\"", "\"공작님이 어쩜 그리 멋져 보이시던지.\""], ["Mrs. O'Dowd remarked.", "오다우드 부인이 한마디 했다."], ["\"The Wellesleys and Malonys are related; but, of course, poor I would never dream of introjuicing myself unless his Grace thought proper to remember our family-tie.\"", "\"웰즐리 가문과 말로니 가문은 친척간이랍니다. 하지만 물론 보잘것없는 저야, 공작 각하께서 우리 집안의 인연을 굳이 기억해주시는 게 마땅하다 여기지 않으시는 한, 감히 제 소개를 들이밀 꿈도 못 꾸지요.\""]], [["\"He's a great soldier,\"", "\"대단한 군인이지요.\""], ["Jos said, much more at ease now the great man was gone.", "조스가 말했다. 그 위대한 인물이 떠나고 나니 한결 마음이 편해진 것이었다."], ["\"Was there ever a battle won like Salamanca?", "\"살라망카 같은 승전이 또 어디 있었겠소?"], ["Hey, Dobbin?", "어떻소, 도빈?"], ["But where was it he learnt his art?", "그런데 그분이 그 병법을 어디서 익혔겠소?"], ["In India, my boy!", "인도라네, 이 사람아!"], ["The jungle's the school for a general, mark me that.", "정글이야말로 장군을 길러내는 학교지, 내 말 명심하시오."], ["I knew him myself, too, Mrs.", "나도 그분을 직접 알았다오, 오다우드 부인."], ["O'Dowd: we both of us danced the same evening with Miss Cutler, daughter of Cutler of the Artillery, and a devilish fine girl, at Dumdum.\"", "우리 둘이 같은 날 저녁에 컷틀러 양과 춤을 췄지요. 포병대 컷틀러의 딸인데, 덤덤에서였소, 기막히게 예쁜 아가씨였지요.\""]], [["The apparition of the great personages held them all in talk during the drive; and at dinner; and until the hour came when they were all to go to the Opera.", "그 지체 높은 인사들이 나타난 일은 드라이브 내내, 또 만찬 자리에서, 그리고 다들 오페라 극장으로 떠날 시간이 될 때까지 그들의 이야깃거리가 되었다."]], [["It was almost like Old England.", "거의 옛 잉글랜드에 온 것이나 다름없었다."], ["The house was filled with familiar British faces, and those toilettes for which the British female has long been celebrated.", "극장은 낯익은 영국인 얼굴들로 가득했고, 영국 여인들이 오래도록 명성을 떨쳐온 그 차림새들로 그득했다."], ["Mrs. O'Dowd's was not the least splendid amongst these, and she had a curl on her forehead, and a set of Irish diamonds and Cairngorms, which outshone all the decorations in the house, in her notion.", "오다우드 부인의 차림 또한 그 가운데서 결코 덜 화려한 축이 아니었으니, 이마에는 곱슬머리 한 가닥을 늘어뜨리고, 아일랜드 다이아몬드며 케언곰 보석을 한 벌 갖춰 둘렀는데, 그녀의 생각으로는 극장 안 그 어떤 장신구도 빛이 바랠 지경이었다."], ["Her presence used to excruciate Osborne; but go she would upon all parties of pleasure on which she heard her young friends were bent.", "그녀가 동석하면 오즈번은 늘 진땀을 흘렸지만, 젊은 벗들이 마음먹은 유흥거리라는 소문만 들으면 부인은 어디든 따라나서고야 말았다."], ["It never entered into her thought but that they must be charmed with her company.", "그들이 자신과 동행하면서 황홀해하지 않을 수도 있다는 생각은 그녀의 머릿속에 떠오른 적조차 없었던 것이다."]], [["\"She's been useful to you, my dear,\"", "\"저 여자가 자네한테 쓸모는 있었지.\""], ["George said to his wife, whom he could leave alone with less scruple when she had this society.", "조지가 아내에게 말했다. 그는 아내에게 이런 동무가 있으면 한결 거리낌 없이 혼자 둘 수 있었던 것이다."], ["\"But what a comfort it is that Rebecca's come: you will have her for a friend, and we may get rid now of this damn'd Irishwoman.\"", "\"하지만 레베카가 와줘서 얼마나 다행인지 몰라. 자네한테 벗이 생긴 거니, 이젠 저 빌어먹을 아일랜드 여편네를 떼어버릴 수 있겠어.\""], ["To this Amelia did not answer, yes or no: and how do we know what her thoughts were?", "이 말에 애밀리아는 그렇다고도 아니라고도 대답하지 않았다. 그녀가 무슨 생각을 하고 있었는지를 우리가 어찌 알겠는가?"]], [["The coup d'oeil of the Brussels opera-house did not strike Mrs. O'Dowd as being so fine as the theatre in Fishamble Street, Dublin, nor was French music at all equal, in her opinion, to the melodies of her native country.", "브뤼셀 오페라 극장의 전경은 오다우드 부인이 보기에 더블린 피섐블 거리의 극장만큼 훌륭하지는 않았고, 프랑스 음악 역시 그녀의 생각으로는 제 고향의 가락에 결코 견줄 바가 못 되었다."], ["She favoured her friends with these and other opinions in a very loud tone of voice, and tossed about a great clattering fan she sported, with the most splendid complacency.", "그녀는 이런저런 견해를 더없이 큰 목소리로 벗들에게 들려주었고, 자기가 자랑삼아 든 요란하게 달그락거리는 부채를 더없이 만족스러운 기색으로 휘둘러댔다."]], [["\"Who is that wonderful woman with Amelia, Rawdon, love?\"", "\"애밀리아 곁에 있는 저 굉장한 여자는 누구예요, 로던, 여보?\""], ["said a lady in an opposite box (who, almost always civil to her husband in private, was more fond than ever of him in company).", "맞은편 칸막이석에 앉은 한 부인이 말했다(이 부인은 단둘이 있을 때는 거의 늘 남편에게 깍듯하더니, 사람들 앞에서는 어느 때보다도 그에게 살가웠다)."]], [["\"Don't you see that creature with a yellow thing in her turban, and a red satin gown, and a great watch?\"", "\"터번에 노란 것을 달고, 빨간 새틴 가운을 입고, 커다란 회중시계를 찬 저 여자 안 보이세요?\""]], [["\"Near the pretty little woman in white?\"", "\"흰옷 입은 어여쁜 자그마한 여인 옆에 있는 사람 말이오?\""], ["asked a middle-aged gentleman seated by the querist's side, with orders in his button, and several under-waistcoats, and a great, choky, white stock.", "묻는 부인 곁에 앉은 중년의 신사가 물었다. 단춧구멍에는 훈장을 달고, 조끼를 여러 겹 껴입고, 숨이 막힐 듯 큼직한 흰 스톡을 두른 사람이었다."]], [["\"That pretty woman in white is Amelia, General: you are remarking all the pretty women, you naughty man.\"", "\"흰옷 입은 저 어여쁜 여인이 애밀리아예요, 장군님. 장군님은 어여쁜 여자들을 죄다 눈여겨보시는군요, 이 짓궂은 양반 같으니.\""]], [["\"Only one, begad, in the world!\"", "\"맹세컨대, 세상에 오직 한 사람뿐이라오!\""], ["said the General, delighted, and the lady gave him a tap with a large bouquet which she had.", "장군이 흐뭇해하며 말하자, 부인은 손에 든 커다란 꽃다발로 그를 톡 쳤다."]], [["\"Bedad it's him,\" said Mrs.", "\"세상에, 저 사람이 맞네요.\""], ["O'Dowd; \"and that's the very bokay he bought in the Marshy aux Flures!\"", "오다우드 부인이 말했다. \"그리고 저게 바로 그 사람이 마르셰 오 플뢰르에서 산 그 꽃다발이고요!\""], ["and when Rebecca, having caught her friend's eye, performed the little hand-kissing operation once more, Mrs. Major O'D., taking the compliment to herself, returned the salute with a gracious smile, which sent that unfortunate Dobbin shrieking out of the box again.", "그러고는 레베카가 벗의 눈길을 알아채고 다시 한 번 손에 입을 맞추는 그 자그마한 동작을 해 보이자, 오다우드 소령 부인은 그 인사가 자기에게 보낸 것이라 여기고 우아한 미소로 답례를 했는데, 그 바람에 가엾은 도빈은 또 한 번 비명을 지르며 칸막이석에서 달아나버렸다."]], [["At the end of the act, George was out of the box in a moment, and he was even going to pay his respects to Rebecca in her loge.", "막이 끝나자 조지는 순식간에 칸막이석을 빠져나가, 레베카의 좌석으로 찾아가 인사를 올릴 참이었다."], ["He met Crawley in the lobby, however, where they exchanged a few sentences upon the occurrences of the last fortnight.", "그러나 로비에서 크롤리와 마주쳤고, 거기서 두 사람은 지난 보름간의 일들에 관해 몇 마디를 주고받았다."]], [["\"You found my cheque all right at the agent's?", "\"대리인한테서 내 수표 제대로 받았겠지?\""], ["George said, with a knowing air.", "조지가 짐짓 아는 체하는 투로 말했다."]], [["\"All right, my boy,\"", "\"제대로 받았네, 이 사람아.\""], ["Rawdon answered.", "로던이 대답했다."], ["\"Happy to give you your revenge.", "\"기꺼이 자네한테 설욕할 기회를 주지."], ["Governor come round?\"", "영감은 마음 좀 돌렸나?\""]], [["\"Not yet,\"", "\"아직은.\" 조지가 말했다."], ["said George, \"but he will; and you know I've some private fortune through my mother.", "\"하지만 곧 그러실 거야. 그리고 알다시피 어머니 쪽으로 물려받은 내 개인 재산이 좀 있으니까."], ["Has Aunty relented?\"", "고모님은 누그러지셨나?\""]], [["\"Sent me twenty pound, damned old screw.", "\"이십 파운드 부쳐주셨지, 빌어먹을 노랭이 같으니."], ["When shall we have a meet?", "언제 한판 붙을까?"], ["The General dines out on Tuesday.", "장군이 화요일에 밖에서 식사하거든."], ["Can't you come Tuesday?", "화요일에 못 오나?"], ["I say, make Sedley cut off his moustache.", "참, 세들리한테 콧수염 좀 깎으라고 하게."], ["What the devil does a civilian mean with a moustache and those infernal frogs to his coat!", "도대체 민간인이 콧수염에다 그 빌어먹을 장식 단추까지 달고 무슨 짓이람!"], ["By-bye.", "잘 가게."], ["Try and come on Tuesday\"; and Rawdon was going-off with two brilliant young gentlemen of fashion, who were, like himself, on the staff of a general officer.", "화요일에 꼭 와보게.\" 그러고는 로던은 자기처럼 어느 장군의 참모로 있는 두 명의 화려한 젊은 멋쟁이 신사들과 함께 자리를 떴다."]], [["George was only half pleased to be asked to dinner on that particular day when the General was not to dine.", "조지는 하필 장군이 식사 자리에 없는 그날 만찬에 초대받은 것이 절반밖에 달갑지 않았다."], ["\"I will go in and pay my respects to your wife,\"", "\"들어가서 자네 부인께 인사 좀 드려야겠네.\""], ["said he; at which Rawdon said, \"Hm, as you please,\"", "그가 말하자, 로던은 \"흠, 좋을 대로 하게\""], ["looking very glum, and at which the two young officers exchanged knowing glances.", "하고 무척 시무룩한 표정으로 대답했고, 그 말에 두 젊은 장교는 의미심장한 눈길을 주고받았다."], ["George parted from them and strutted down the lobby to the General's box, the number of which he had carefully counted.", "조지는 그들과 헤어져 로비를 따라 으스대며 걸어가 장군의 칸막이석으로 향했는데, 그 번호는 미리 꼼꼼히 세어둔 터였다."]], [["\"Entrez,\"", "\"들어오세요.\""], ["said a clear little voice, and our friend found himself in Rebecca's presence; who jumped up, clapped her hands together, and held out both of them to George, so charmed was she to see him.", "맑고 자그마한 목소리가 들렸고, 우리의 친구는 어느새 레베카 앞에 서 있었다. 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 두 손을 마주치고는, 조지를 보고 어찌나 반가워하던지 두 손을 다 내밀었다."], ["The General, with the orders in his button, stared at the newcomer with a sulky scowl, as much as to say, who the devil are you?", "단춧구멍에 훈장을 단 장군은 새로 온 사람을 시무룩하게 노려보았는데, 그 표정이 마치 \"넌 도대체 누구냐?\" 하고 묻는 듯했다."]], [["\"My dear Captain George!\"", "\"나의 조지 대위님!\""], ["cried little Rebecca in an ecstasy.", "작은 레베카가 황홀해하며 외쳤다."], ["\"How good of you to come.", "\"와주시다니 얼마나 친절하신지."], ["The General and I were moping together tete-a-tete.", "장군님하고 저는 단둘이 마주 앉아 따분해하던 참이었어요."], ["General, this is my Captain George of whom you heard me talk.\"", "장군님, 이분이 제가 말씀드렸던 그 조지 대위님이세요.\""]], [["\"Indeed,\"", "\"그렇소이까.\""], ["said the General, with a very small bow; \"of what regiment is Captain George?\"", "장군이 아주 짧게 고개를 까딱하며 말했다. \"조지 대위는 어느 연대 소속이오?\""]], [["George mentioned the --th: how he wished he could have said it was a crack cavalry corps.", "조지는 ××연대라고 말했다. 정예 기병대라고 말할 수 있었더라면 얼마나 좋았을까 싶었다."]], [["\"Come home lately from the West Indies, I believe.", "\"근래에 서인도제도에서 돌아왔다지요, 아마."], ["Not seen much service in the late war.", "지난 전쟁에선 실전 경험을 별로 못 했고."], ["Quartered here, Captain George?\"--the General went on with killing haughtiness.", "여기 주둔 중이오, 조지 대위?\" 장군은 사람 죽일 듯한 거만함으로 말을 이어갔다."]], [["\"Not Captain George, you stupid man; Captain Osborne,\"", "\"조지 대위가 아니에요, 이 멍청한 양반아. 오즈번 대위지요.\""], ["Rebecca said.", "레베카가 말했다."], ["The General all the while was looking savagely from one to the other.", "그러는 내내 장군은 두 사람을 험상궂게 번갈아 노려보고 있었다."]], [["\"Captain Osborne, indeed!", "\"오즈번 대위라, 그렇군!"], ["Any relation to the L------ Osbornes?\"", "혹시 L가의 오즈번 집안과 친척이오?\""]], [["\"We bear the same arms,\"", "\"같은 문장(紋章)을 쓰지요.\""], ["George said, as indeed was the fact; Mr.", "조지가 말했는데, 실제로도 그러했다."], ["Osborne having consulted with a herald in Long Acre, and picked the L------ arms out of the peerage, when he set up his carriage fifteen years before.", "오즈번 씨가 십오 년 전 자기 마차를 처음 갖출 때 롱에이커의 문장관과 상의하여 귀족 명감에서 L가의 문장을 골라냈던 것이다."], ["The General made no reply to this announcement; but took up his opera-glass--the double-barrelled lorgnon was not invented in those days--and pretended to examine the house; but Rebecca saw that his disengaged eye was working round in her direction, and shooting out bloodshot glances at her and George.", "장군은 이 발언에 아무 대꾸도 하지 않고, 오페라글라스를 집어들더니(겹렌즈 오페라 안경은 그 시절엔 아직 발명되지 않았다) 극장 안을 살피는 척했다. 그러나 레베카는 그의 한쪽 자유로운 눈이 자기 쪽을 향해 돌아오며, 자기와 조지를 향해 핏발 선 눈길을 쏘아대는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["She redoubled in cordiality.", "그녀는 갑절로 살갑게 굴었다."], ["\"How is dearest Amelia?", "\"사랑스러운 애밀리아는 어떻게 지내나요?"], ["But I needn't ask: how pretty she looks!", "굳이 물을 필요도 없겠네요, 어쩜 저리 어여쁠까!"], ["And who is that nice good-natured looking creature with her--a flame of yours?", "그리고 애밀리아 옆에 있는 저 마음씨 좋아 보이는 멋진 분은 누구예요?"], ["O, you wicked men!", "대위님의 연인인가요?"], ["And there is Mr.", "아유, 이 못된 양반들 같으니!"], ["Sedley eating ice, I declare: how he seems to enjoy it!", "어머, 정말로 저기 세들리 씨가 아이스크림을 드시고 있네요. 어찌나 맛있게 드시는지!"], ["General, why have we not had any ices?\"", "장군님, 우리는 어째서 아이스크림이 없는 거죠?\""]], [["\"Shall I go and fetch you some?\"", "\"내가 가서 좀 가져다드릴까?\""], ["said the General, bursting with wrath.", "장군이 화를 못 이겨 터질 듯한 기색으로 말했다."]], [["\"Let ME go, I entreat you,\"", "\"제가 가겠습니다, 부디.\""], ["George said.", "조지가 말했다."]], [["\"No, I will go to Amelia's box.", "\"아니요, 제가 애밀리아의 칸막이석으로 갈래요."], ["Dear, sweet girl!", "사랑스럽고 어여쁜 애밀리아!"], ["Give me your arm, Captain George\"; and so saying, and with a nod to the General, she tripped into the lobby.", "팔을 좀 빌려주세요, 조지 대위님.\" 이렇게 말하고 장군에게 고개를 까딱한 다음, 그녀는 사뿐사뿐 로비로 나섰다."], ["She gave George the queerest, knowingest look, when they were together, a look which might have been interpreted, \"Don't you see the state of affairs, and what a fool I'm making of him?\"", "둘만 남게 되자 그녀는 조지에게 더없이 묘하고 의미심장한 눈길을 던졌는데, 그 눈길은 \"이 판국이 어떻게 돌아가는지, 내가 저 사람을 어떻게 바보로 만들고 있는지 안 보이세요?\" 하는 뜻으로 풀이될 만한 것이었다."], ["But he did not perceive it.", "그러나 그는 그것을 알아채지 못했다."], ["He was thinking of his own plans, and lost in pompous admiration of his own irresistible powers of pleasing.", "그는 자기 자신의 속셈에 골몰해 있었고, 거역할 수 없는 자신의 매력에 도취되어 우쭐한 자아도취에 빠져 있었다."]], [["The curses to which the General gave a low utterance, as soon as Rebecca and her conqueror had quitted him, were so deep, that I am sure no compositor would venture to print them were they written down.", "레베카와 그녀의 정복자가 자리를 뜨자마자 장군이 낮은 소리로 내뱉은 저주들은 어찌나 지독했던지, 그것을 글로 옮겨놓는다 한들 어떤 식자공도 감히 활자로 박으려 들지 않을 것이라 나는 확신한다."], ["They came from the General's heart; and a wonderful thing it is to think that the human heart is capable of generating such produce, and can throw out, as occasion demands, such a supply of lust and fury, rage and hatred.", "그것은 장군의 가슴 밑바닥에서 우러나온 것이었으니, 인간의 마음이 그런 산물을 빚어낼 수 있다는 것, 또 필요에 따라 그토록 많은 욕정과 분노, 격노와 증오를 쏟아낼 수 있다는 것은 참으로 놀라운 일이다."]], [["Amelia's gentle eyes, too, had been fixed anxiously on the pair, whose conduct had so chafed the jealous General; but when Rebecca entered her box, she flew to her friend with an affectionate rapture which showed itself, in spite of the publicity of the place; for she embraced her dearest friend in the presence of the whole house, at least in full view of the General's glass, now brought to bear upon the Osborne party.", "애밀리아의 온순한 두 눈 또한 그 한 쌍을 근심스레 좇고 있었으니, 그들의 행동거지가 그 질투 어린 장군의 속을 그토록 뒤집어놓았던 것이다. 그러나 레베카가 칸막이석으로 들어서자, 애밀리아는 벗에게 달려가 더없이 다정한 환희를 터뜨렸는데, 그 환희는 장소가 공공연한 곳임에도 불구하고 고스란히 드러나고 말았다."], ["Mrs. Rawdon saluted Jos, too, with the kindliest greeting: she admired Mrs.", "극장 전체가 보는 앞에서, 적어도 이제 오즈번 일행 쪽을 겨누고 있던 장군의 안경에 환히 보이는 데서, 그녀가 더없이 사랑하는 벗을 끌어안았던 것이다."], ["O'Dowd's large Cairngorm brooch and superb Irish diamonds, and wouldn't believe that they were not from Golconda direct.", "로던 부인 또한 더없이 다정한 인사로 조스에게도 알은체를 했다. 그녀는 오다우드 부인의 커다란 케언곰 브로치와 빼어난 아일랜드 다이아몬드를 찬탄하며, 그것들이 골콘다에서 곧장 온 것이 아니라고는 도무지 믿지 못하겠다고 했다."], ["She bustled, she chattered, she turned and twisted, and smiled upon one, and smirked on another, all in full view of the jealous opera-glass opposite.", "그녀는 부산스레 움직이고, 재잘대고, 몸을 돌렸다 비틀었다 하며, 이 사람에게 미소 짓고 저 사람에게 생긋거렸는데, 이 모든 것이 맞은편의 질투 어린 오페라글라스가 환히 보는 앞에서 벌어진 일이었다."], ["And when the time for the ballet came (in which there was no dancer that went through her grimaces or performed her comedy of action better), she skipped back to her own box, leaning on Captain Dobbin's arm this time.", "그리고 발레가 시작될 시간이 되자(그 발레에 등장하는 어떤 무용수도 그녀만큼 능숙하게 표정 연기를 해내거나 몸짓의 희극을 펼쳐 보이지는 못했다), 그녀는 자기 칸막이석으로 깡충깡충 돌아갔는데, 이번에는 도빈 대위의 팔에 기댄 채였다. 아니, 그녀는 조지의 팔은 마다했다."], ["No, she would not have George's: he must stay and talk to his dearest, best, little Amelia.", "그는 남아서 더없이 사랑스럽고 가장 좋은 작은 애밀리아와 이야기를 나누어야 한다는 것이었다."]], [["\"What a humbug that woman is!\"", "\"저 여자는 어찌 저리 사기꾼 같은지!\""], ["honest old Dobbin mumbled to George, when he came back from Rebecca's box, whither he had conducted her in perfect silence, and with a countenance as glum as an undertaker's.", "정직한 늙은 도빈이 레베카의 칸막이석에서 돌아와 조지에게 중얼거렸다. 그는 더없이 침묵한 채, 장의사처럼 음울한 표정으로 그녀를 그곳까지 데려다준 참이었다."], ["\"She writhes and twists about like a snake.", "\"꼭 뱀처럼 몸을 비틀고 뒤트는 꼴이라니."], ["All the time she was here, didn't you see, George, how she was acting at the General over the way?\"", "여기 있는 내내, 못 봤나, 조지, 저 여자가 어떻게 저 건너편 장군을 상대로 연기를 하고 있었는지?\""]], [["\"Humbug--acting!", "\"사기꾼이라니, 연기라니!"], ["Hang it, she's the nicest little woman in England,\"", "제기랄, 저 여자는 잉글랜드에서 제일 사랑스러운 작은 여인이야.\""], ["George replied, showing his white teeth, and giving his ambrosial whiskers a twirl.", "조지가 하얀 이를 드러내며, 향기로운 구레나룻을 한 번 꼬며 대꾸했다."], ["\"You ain't a man of the world, Dobbin.", "\"자넨 세상 물정을 모르는 사람이야, 도빈."], ["Dammy, look at her now, she's talked over Tufto in no time.", "빌어먹을, 지금 저 여자를 좀 보게, 순식간에 터프토를 구워삶았잖아."], ["Look how he's laughing!", "저 사람 웃는 꼴 좀 보라고!"], ["Gad, what a shoulder she has!", "세상에, 저 어깨선 좀 봐!"], ["Emmy, why didn't you have a bouquet?", "에미, 자넨 왜 꽃다발이 없나?"], ["Everybody has a bouquet.\"", "다들 꽃다발을 들었잖아.\""]], [["\"Faith, then, why didn't you BOY one?\" Mrs.", "\"어머나, 그럼 왜 직접 하나 사주지 않으셨나요?\""], ["O'Dowd said; and both Amelia and William Dobbin thanked her for this timely observation.", "오다우드 부인이 말했고, 애밀리아와 윌리엄 도빈은 둘 다 그 시의적절한 한마디에 그녀에게 고마워했다."], ["But beyond this neither of the ladies rallied.", "그러나 그 이상으로는 두 숙녀 가운데 누구도 기운을 차리지 못했다."], ["Amelia was overpowered by the flash and the dazzle and the fashionable talk of her worldly rival.", "애밀리아는 세속적인 연적의 화려함과 눈부심, 그리고 사교계의 언변에 짓눌리고 말았다."], ["Even the O'Dowd was silent and subdued after Becky's brilliant apparition, and scarcely said a word more about Glenmalony all the evening.", "오다우드 부인조차 베키의 눈부신 출현 이후로는 입을 다물고 풀이 죽어, 저녁 내내 글렌말로니에 관해서는 더 이상 한마디도 거의 하지 않았다."]], [["\"When do you intend to give up play, George, as you have promised me, any time these hundred years?\"", "\"백 년 동안이나 입버릇처럼 약속했으면서, 자넨 대체 언제 노름을 그만둘 셈인가, 조지?\""], ["Dobbin said to his friend a few days after the night at the Opera.", "오페라를 보던 그날 밤이 지나고 며칠 뒤, 도빈이 친구에게 말했다."], ["\"When do you intend to give up sermonising?\"", "\"자넨 대체 언제 설교질을 그만둘 셈인가?\""], ["was the other's reply.", "상대방의 대답은 그러했다."], ["\"What the deuce, man, are you alarmed about?", "\"도대체 이 사람아, 뭐가 그리 걱정인가?"], ["We play low; I won last night.", "우린 판돈 적게 친다고."], ["You don't suppose Crawley cheats?", "어젯밤엔 내가 땄어."], ["With fair play it comes to pretty much the same thing at the year's end.\"", "설마 크롤리가 속임수를 쓴다고 생각하는 건 아니겠지? 정정당당히 치면 한 해 끝에 가선 거기서 거기인 거야.\""]], [["\"But I don't think he could pay if he lost,\"", "\"하지만 그자가 잃으면 갚을 수나 있을지 모르겠군.\""], ["Dobbin said; and his advice met with the success which advice usually commands.", "도빈이 말했다. 그리고 그의 충고는 충고라는 것이 으레 거두는 만큼의 성공을 거두었다."], ["Osborne and Crawley were repeatedly together now.", "이제 오즈번과 크롤리는 거듭 어울려 다녔다."], ["General Tufto dined abroad almost constantly.", "터프토 장군은 거의 늘 밖에서 식사를 했다."], ["George was always welcome in the apartments (very close indeed to those of the General) which the aide-de-camp and his wife occupied in the hotel.", "부관 부부가 호텔에서 쓰는 거처(실은 장군의 거처와 아주 가까웠다)에서 조지는 언제나 환영받았다."]], [["Amelia's manners were such when she and George visited Crawley and his wife at these quarters, that they had very nearly come to their first quarrel; that is, George scolded his wife violently for her evident unwillingness to go, and the high and mighty manner in which she comported herself towards Mrs.", "조지와 애밀리아가 그 거처로 크롤리 부부를 찾아갔을 때 애밀리아의 태도가 어찌나 거북했던지, 두 사람은 하마터면 첫 부부싸움을 벌일 뻔했다."], ["Crawley, her old friend; and Amelia did not say one single word in reply; but with her husband's eye upon her, and Rebecca scanning her as she felt, was, if possible, more bashful and awkward on the second visit which she paid to Mrs.", "그러니까 조지가, 가기 싫어하는 기색이 역력한 데다 옛 벗인 크롤리 부인을 대하는 도도하고 거만한 태도를 두고 아내를 격하게 나무란 것이었다. 한데 애밀리아는 단 한마디도 대꾸하지 않았다."], ["Rawdon, than on her first call.", "그러나 남편의 시선이 자기에게 꽂혀 있고, 레베카가 자기를 빤히 살피고 있다고 느껴지는 가운데, 애밀리아는 로던 부인을 두 번째로 방문했을 때 처음 찾아갔을 때보다도, 가능하다면, 한층 더 수줍어하고 어색해했다."]], [["Rebecca was doubly affectionate, of course, and would not take notice, in the least, of her friend's coolness.", "물론 레베카는 갑절로 다정했고, 벗의 냉랭함을 조금도 알은체하지 않았다. \"에미는 아버지 이름이 그…"], ["\"I think Emmy has become prouder since her father's name was in the--since Mr.", "세들리 씨의 불행 이후로 더 도도해진 것 같아요.\""], ["Sedley's MISFORTUNES,\" Rebecca said, softening the phrase charitably for George's ear.", "레베카가 조지의 귀를 의식해 그 표현을 자비롭게 누그러뜨리며 말했다."]], [["\"Upon my word, I thought when we were at Brighton she was doing me the honour to be jealous of me; and now I suppose she is scandalised because Rawdon, and I, and the General live together.", "\"맹세컨대, 우리가 브라이턴에 있을 땐 저 사람이 황송하게도 나를 질투하는구나 싶었어요. 그런데 지금은 로던과 나, 그리고 장군이 함께 지낸다고 못마땅해하는 모양이지요."], ["Why, my dear creature, how could we, with our means, live at all, but for a friend to share expenses?", "아니, 이 사람아, 우리 형편에 비용을 나눠 낼 벗이 없으면 도대체 무슨 수로 살아가겠어요?"], ["And do you suppose that Rawdon is not big enough to take care of my honour?", "게다가 로던이 내 명예 하나 지킬 만큼의 위인이 못 된다고 생각하는 거예요?"], ["But I'm very much obliged to Emmy, very,\"", "하지만 에미한테 정말 고맙군요, 정말로.\""], ["Mrs. Rawdon said.", "로던 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Pooh, jealousy!\"", "\"흥, 질투라니!\""], ["answered George, \"all women are jealous.\"", "조지가 대꾸했다. \"여자들은 죄다 질투쟁이라니까.\""]], [["\"And all men too.", "\"남자들도 다 그렇죠."], ["Weren't you jealous of General Tufto, and the General of you, on the night of the Opera?", "오페라를 보던 그날 밤, 대위님은 터프토 장군을 질투하고, 장군은 대위님을 질투하지 않으셨던가요?"], ["Why, he was ready to eat me for going with you to visit that foolish little wife of yours; as if I care a pin for either of you,\"", "아니, 그 양반은 내가 대위님을 따라 그 바보 같은 작은 부인을 찾아갔다고 나를 잡아먹을 기세였다고요. 마치 내가 당신네 둘 중 누구라도 손톱만큼 신경 쓴다는 듯이 말이에요.\""], ["Crawley's wife said, with a pert toss of her head.", "크롤리의 아내가 톡 쏘듯 고개를 까딱하며 말했다."], ["\"Will you dine here?", "\"여기서 식사하실래요?"], ["The dragon dines with the Commander-in-Chief.", "그 용 같은 양반은 총사령관님과 식사한답니다."], ["Great news is stirring.", "큰 소식이 돌고 있어요."], ["They say the French have crossed the frontier.", "프랑스군이 국경을 넘었다더군요."], ["We shall have a quiet dinner.\"", "우린 조용히 식사할 수 있을 거예요.\""]], [["George accepted the invitation, although his wife was a little ailing.", "조지는 아내가 몸이 좀 좋지 않은데도 그 초대를 받아들였다."], ["They were now not quite six weeks married.", "이제 두 사람은 결혼한 지 채 여섯 주도 되지 않은 터였다."], ["Another woman was laughing or sneering at her expense, and he not angry.", "다른 여자가 아내를 두고 비웃거나 조롱하고 있는데도, 그는 화를 내지 않았다."], ["He was not even angry with himself, this good-natured fellow.", "이 마음씨 좋은 사내는 자기 자신에게조차 화를 내지 않았다."], ["It is a shame, he owned to himself; but hang it, if a pretty woman WILL throw herself in your way, why, what can a fellow do, you know?", "부끄러운 일이지, 하고 그는 속으로 인정했다. 하지만 빌어먹을, 어여쁜 여자가 제 발로 다가오는데, 사내가 어쩌란 말인가?"], ["I AM rather free about women, he had often said, smiling and nodding knowingly to Stubble and Spooney, and other comrades of the mess-table; and they rather respected him than otherwise for this prowess.", "나는 여자 문제에선 좀 자유로운 편이지, 하고 그는 종종 스터블이나 스푸니를 비롯한 식탁의 다른 전우들에게 미소 지으며 의미심장하게 고개를 끄덕이곤 했다. 그러면 그들은 이 무용담 때문에 그를 깔보기는커녕 오히려 그를 우러러보았다."], ["Next to conquering in war, conquering in love has been a source of pride, time out of mind, amongst men in Vanity Fair, or how should schoolboys brag of their amours, or Don Juan be popular?", "전쟁에서 승리하는 것 다음으로, 사랑에서 정복하는 것은 아득한 옛날부터 허영의 시장에 사는 사내들 사이에서 자랑거리의 원천이 되어왔다. 그렇지 않고서야 학교 다니는 사내아이들이 어찌 제 연애담을 떠벌리며, 돈 후안이 어찌 인기를 끌겠는가?"]], [["So Mr. Osborne, having a firm conviction in his own mind that he was a woman-killer and destined to conquer, did not run counter to his fate, but yielded himself up to it quite complacently.", "그리하여 오즈번 씨는 스스로가 여자 사냥꾼이며 정복할 운명을 타고났다는 굳은 신념을 품은 채, 제 운명을 거스르기는커녕 더없이 흐뭇한 마음으로 거기에 몸을 내맡겼다."], ["And as Emmy did not say much or plague him with her jealousy, but merely became unhappy and pined over it miserably in secret, he chose to fancy that she was not suspicious of what all his acquaintance were perfectly aware--namely, that he was carrying on a desperate flirtation with Mrs.", "그리고 에미가 말수가 적은 데다 질투로 그를 들볶지도 않고, 그저 남몰래 불행해하며 비참하게 속을 끓일 뿐이었으므로, 그는 모든 지인이 빤히 알고 있는 사실을, 즉 자기가 크롤리 부인과 필사적으로 시시덕거리고 있다는 사실을 아내가 눈치채지 못한다고 멋대로 믿어버렸다."], ["Crawley. He rode with her whenever she was free.", "그는 그녀가 시간이 날 때면 언제든 함께 말을 탔다."], ["He pretended regimental business to Amelia (by which falsehood she was not in the least deceived), and consigning his wife to solitude or her brother's society, passed his evenings in the Crawleys'", "애밀리아에게는 연대 업무라는 핑계를 댔고(이 거짓말에 그녀가 조금이라도 속아 넘어간 것은 아니었다), 아내를 고독에, 혹은 오라비의 동무에게 맡겨둔 채 저녁이면 크롤리 부부와 어울리며 시간을 보냈다."], ["company; losing money to the husband and flattering himself that the wife was dying of love for him.", "남편에게 돈을 잃으면서도, 그 아내가 자기에게 반해 애가 타고 있다고 우쭐대면서 말이다."], ["It is very likely that this worthy couple never absolutely conspired and agreed together in so many words: the one to cajole the young gentleman, whilst the other won his money at cards: but they understood each other perfectly well, and Rawdon let Osborne come and go with entire good humour.", "이 어엿한 부부가 한쪽은 젊은 신사를 구슬리고 한쪽은 카드로 그의 돈을 따먹자고 말 한마디 한마디로 작당하여 합의했을 리는 십중팔구 없을 것이다. 그러나 그들은 서로를 완벽하게 이해하고 있었으니, 로던은 오즈번이 드나드는 것을 더없이 너그러운 기분으로 내버려두었다."]], [["George was so occupied with his new acquaintances that he and William Dobbin were by no means so much together as formerly.", "조지는 새로 사귄 지인들에게 어찌나 빠져 있던지, 그와 윌리엄 도빈은 예전만큼 함께 어울리는 일이 결코 없었다."], ["George avoided him in public and in the regiment, and, as we see, did not like those sermons which his senior was disposed to inflict upon him.", "조지는 사람들 앞에서나 연대에서나 그를 피했고, 우리가 보다시피, 윗사람이 자기에게 기어이 늘어놓으려 드는 그 설교를 좋아하지 않았다."], ["If some parts of his conduct made Captain Dobbin exceedingly grave and cool; of what use was it to tell George that, though his whiskers were large, and his own opinion of his knowingness great, he was as green as a schoolboy? that Rawdon was making a victim of him as he had done of many before, and as soon as he had used him would fling him off with scorn?", "조지의 행실 가운데 어떤 부분이 도빈 대위를 몹시 진지하고 냉랭하게 만들었다 한들, 구레나룻은 큼직하고 자기 영민함에 대한 자부심도 대단하지만 실은 학생만큼이나 풋내기라고, 로던이 전에 숱한 이들을 그랬듯 그를 제물 삼고 있으며 일단 부려먹고 나면 경멸하며 내팽개칠 것이라고 조지에게 말해준들 무슨 소용이 있었겠는가?"], ["He would not listen: and so, as Dobbin, upon those days when he visited the Osborne house, seldom had the advantage of meeting his old friend, much painful and unavailing talk between them was spared.", "그는 들으려 하지 않았다. 그래서 도빈은 오즈번의 집을 찾는 날에도 옛 벗을 만날 기회가 좀처럼 없었으니, 두 사람 사이의 고통스럽고도 부질없는 대화가 그만큼 덜어진 셈이었다."], ["Our friend George was in the full career of the pleasures of Vanity Fair.", "우리의 친구 조지는 허영의 시장의 쾌락을 한껏 누리는 중이었다."]], [["There never was, since the days of Darius, such a brilliant train of camp-followers as hung round the Duke of Wellington's army in the Low Countries, in 1815; and led it dancing and feasting, as it were, up to the very brink of battle.", "다리우스 시절 이래로, 1815년 저지대 나라들에서 웰링턴 공작의 군대를 에워싸고, 그것을 춤추고 잔치를 벌이게 하며, 이를테면 전투의 벼랑 끝까지 이끌고 간 그토록 화려한 종군자 행렬은 일찍이 없었다."], ["A certain ball which a noble Duchess gave at Brussels on the 15th of June in the above-named year is historical.", "위에서 말한 그해 6월 15일에 어느 고귀한 공작부인이 브뤼셀에서 연 어떤 무도회는 역사에 남은 일이다."], ["All Brussels had been in a state of excitement about it, and I have heard from ladies who were in that town at the period, that the talk and interest of persons of their own sex regarding the ball was much greater even than in respect of the enemy in their front.", "브뤼셀 전체가 그 일로 들떠 있었으니, 그 무렵 그 도시에 있었던 부인들에게서 내가 들은 바로는, 무도회를 둘러싼 자기네 여성들의 이야기와 관심은 코앞에 닥친 적군에 대한 것보다도 한층 더 컸다고 한다."], ["The struggles, intrigues, and prayers to get tickets were such as only English ladies will employ, in order to gain admission to the society of the great of their own nation.", "입장권을 얻으려는 그 분투며 책략이며 애원은, 자기 나라 지체 높은 인사들의 사교계에 끼어들고자 할 때 오직 영국 부인들만이 부릴 법한 것이었다."]], [["Jos and Mrs. O'Dowd, who were panting to be asked, strove in vain to procure tickets; but others of our friends were more lucky.", "초대받기를 애타게 바라던 조스와 오다우드 부인은 입장권을 구하려 애썼지만 허사였다."], ["For instance, through the interest of my Lord Bareacres, and as a set-off for the dinner at the restaurateur's, George got a card for Captain and Mrs.", "그러나 우리의 다른 벗들은 한결 운이 좋았다."], ["Osborne; which circumstance greatly elated him. Dobbin, who was a friend of the General commanding the division in which their regiment was, came laughing one day to Mrs.", "이를테면 베어에이커스 경의 호의를 통해, 또 식당에서의 그 만찬에 대한 보답으로, 조지는 오즈번 대위 부부 앞으로 된 초대장을 받았고, 그 일로 그는 크게 우쭐해졌다."], ["Osborne, and displayed a similar invitation, which made Jos envious, and George wonder how the deuce he should be getting into society. Mr.", "도빈은 그들의 연대가 속한 사단을 지휘하는 장군의 벗이었던지라, 어느 날 웃으며 오즈번 부인을 찾아와 비슷한 초대장을 내보였는데, 그 일로 조스는 부러워했고 조지는 도대체 저 친구가 어떻게 사교계에 끼어드는 건지 의아해했다."], ["and Mrs. Rawdon, finally, were of course invited; as became the friends of a General commanding a cavalry brigade.", "마지막으로 로던 부부도 물론 초대받았으니, 어느 기병 여단을 지휘하는 장군의 벗다운 처사였다."]], [["On the appointed night, George, having commanded new dresses and ornaments of all sorts for Amelia, drove to the famous ball, where his wife did not know a single soul.", "약속된 그날 밤, 조지는 애밀리아를 위해 새 드레스며 온갖 장신구를 마련해주고는, 그 유명한 무도회로 마차를 몰았는데, 정작 거기서 그의 아내는 아는 사람이 단 한 명도 없었다."], ["After looking about for Lady Bareacres, who cut him, thinking the card was quite enough--and after placing Amelia on a bench, he left her to her own cogitations there, thinking, on his own part, that he had behaved very handsomely in getting her new clothes, and bringing her to the ball, where she was free to amuse herself as she liked.", "베어에이커스 부인을 두리번거리며 찾았으나, 부인은 초대장 하나면 충분하다 여기고 그를 본 척도 하지 않았다. 조지는 애밀리아를 긴 의자에 앉혀두고는, 자기 딴에는 새 옷을 마련해주고 무도회에 데려와 마음껏 즐기도록 해주었으니 참으로 훌륭하게 처신했다고 여기며, 그녀를 거기 혼자만의 상념에 내버려두었다."], ["Her thoughts were not of the pleasantest, and nobody except honest Dobbin came to disturb them.", "그녀의 생각은 그리 유쾌한 것이 못 되었고, 정직한 도빈 말고는 아무도 그 상념을 방해하러 오지 않았다."]], [["Whilst her appearance was an utter failure (as her husband felt with a sort of rage), Mrs. Rawdon Crawley's debut was, on the contrary, very brilliant.", "그녀의 출현이 완전한 실패였던(이는 그녀의 남편이 일종의 분노 어린 심정으로 느낀 바였다) 반면, 로던 크롤리 부인의 첫 등장은 그와 정반대로 무척이나 눈부셨다."], ["She arrived very late.", "그녀는 아주 늦게 도착했다."], ["Her face was radiant; her dress perfection.", "얼굴은 환히 빛났고, 옷차림은 완벽했다."], ["In the midst of the great persons assembled, and the eye-glasses directed to her, Rebecca seemed to be as cool and collected as when she used to marshal Miss Pinkerton's little girls to church.", "모여든 지체 높은 인사들 한가운데서, 오페라글라스들이 일제히 자기에게 향하는 가운데, 레베카는 핑커튼 여사의 어린 소녀들을 줄 세워 교회로 데려가던 시절만큼이나 침착하고 차분해 보였다."], ["Numbers of the men she knew already, and the dandies thronged round her.", "사내들 가운데 다수가 이미 그녀와 안면이 있었고, 멋쟁이들이 그녀 주위로 몰려들었다."], ["As for the ladies, it was whispered among them that Rawdon had run away with her from out of a convent, and that she was a relation of the Montmorency family.", "부인들로 말할 것 같으면, 로던이 수녀원에서 그녀를 데리고 달아났다느니, 그녀가 몽모랑시 가문의 친척이라느니 하는 말이 그들 사이에서 수군수군 오갔다."], ["She spoke French so perfectly that there might be some truth in this report, and it was agreed that her manners were fine, and her air distingue.", "그녀가 프랑스어를 어찌나 완벽하게 구사하던지 이 소문에는 얼마간 진실이 담겼을지도 모를 일이었고, 그녀의 몸가짐이 우아하고 그 분위기가 기품 있다는 데에는 다들 의견이 일치했다."], ["Fifty would-be partners thronged round her at once, and pressed to have the honour to dance with her.", "한꺼번에 쉰 명은 됨직한 춤 상대 지망자들이 그녀 주위로 몰려들어 그녀와 춤추는 영광을 누리게 해달라고 졸랐다."], ["But she said she was engaged, and only going to dance very little; and made her way at once to the place where Emmy sate quite unnoticed, and dismally unhappy.", "그러나 그녀는 이미 약속이 있는 데다 춤은 아주 조금만 출 작정이라고 말하고는, 곧장 에미가 아무도 거들떠보지 않는 채 처량하게 불행해하며 앉아 있는 곳으로 향했다."], ["And so, to finish the poor child at once, Mrs. Rawdon ran and greeted affectionately her dearest Amelia, and began forthwith to patronise her.", "그리하여 그 가엾은 아이의 숨통을 단번에 끊어놓으려는 듯, 로던 부인은 달려가 더없이 사랑하는 애밀리아에게 다정하게 인사하고는 곧장 그녀를 깔보듯 챙기기 시작했다."], ["She found fault with her friend's dress, and her hairdresser, and wondered how she could be so chaussee, and vowed that she must send her corsetiere the next morning.", "그녀는 벗의 드레스며 미용사를 흠잡고, 어쩌면 이런 신발을 신었느냐며 의아해하더니, 다음 날 아침 자기 코르셋 재봉사를 꼭 보내주겠노라 다짐했다."], ["She vowed that it was a delightful ball; that there was everybody that every one knew, and only a VERY few nobodies in the whole room.", "그녀는 정말 즐거운 무도회라고, 누구나 아는 사람은 다 와 있고 방 안 전체에 이름 없는 사람이라곤 아주 극소수뿐이라고 호언했다."], ["It is a fact, that in a fortnight, and after three dinners in general society, this young woman had got up the genteel jargon so well, that a native could not speak it better; and it was only from her French being so good, that you could know she was not a born woman of fashion.", "사실 이 젊은 여자는 단 보름 만에, 그것도 사교계에서 만찬을 세 번 한 끝에 그 우아한 은어를 어찌나 잘 익혔던지, 그곳 토박이라도 그보다 더 잘 구사하지는 못할 정도였다. 다만 그녀의 프랑스어가 너무도 훌륭한 탓에, 그녀가 사교계에서 나고 자란 여인이 아니라는 것을 겨우 알아챌 수 있을 뿐이었다."]], [["George, who had left Emmy on her bench on entering the ball-room, very soon found his way back when Rebecca was by her dear friend's side.", "무도회장에 들어서며 에미를 긴 의자에 두고 갔던 조지는, 레베카가 사랑하는 벗 곁에 다가서자 이내 그쪽으로 돌아오는 길을 찾아냈다."], ["Becky was just lecturing Mrs. Osborne upon the follies which her husband was committing.", "베키는 마침 오즈번 부인에게 그 남편이 저지르고 있는 어리석은 짓들에 관해 한바탕 일장 훈계를 늘어놓던 참이었다."], ["\"For God's sake, stop him from gambling, my dear,\"", "\"제발 부탁이니 저 양반 노름 좀 말려요, 이 사람아.\""], ["she said, \"or he will ruin himself.", "그녀가 말했다. \"안 그러면 신세 망친다고요."], ["He and Rawdon are playing at cards every night, and you know he is very poor, and Rawdon will win every shilling from him if he does not take care.", "저이하고 로던이 밤마다 카드를 친단 말이에요. 게다가 알다시피 저이는 형편이 무척 빈한하니, 정신 안 차리면 로던이 저이한테서 한 푼도 남김없이 따먹을 거예요."], ["Why don't you prevent him, you little careless creature?", "어째서 못 말리는 거예요, 이 무심한 사람아?"], ["Why don't you come to us of an evening, instead of moping at home with that Captain Dobbin?", "어째서 저녁이면 그 도빈 대위와 집에 처박혀 시무룩하게 있지 말고 우리한테 안 오는 거죠?"], ["I dare say he is tres aimable; but how could one love a man with feet of such size?", "그 양반이 무척 다정한 분이라는 건 인정해요. 하지만 발이 그렇게 큰 남자를 어떻게 사랑할 수 있겠어요?"], ["Your husband's feet are darlings--Here he comes.", "당신 남편 발은 어찌나 사랑스러운지. 마침 저기 오네요."], ["Where have you been, wretch?", "어디 있었어요, 못된 양반?"], ["Here is Emmy crying her eyes out for you.", "여기 에미가 당신 보고 싶어서 눈이 빠지게 울고 있었어요."], ["Are you coming to fetch me for the quadrille?\"", "카드리유 추러 나 데리러 오는 거예요?\""], ["And she left her bouquet and shawl by Amelia's side, and tripped off with George to dance.", "그러고는 꽃다발과 숄을 애밀리아 곁에 내려놓고, 조지와 함께 사뿐사뿐 춤추러 가버렸다."], ["Women only know how to wound so.", "여자만이 이토록 사람의 가슴을 후벼낼 줄 안다."], ["There is a poison on the tips of their little shafts, which stings a thousand times more than a man's blunter weapon.", "그들의 자그마한 화살촉 끝에는 독이 묻어 있어, 사내의 무딘 무기보다 천 배는 더 따끔하게 쏘는 법이다."], ["Our poor Emmy, who had never hated, never sneered all her life, was powerless in the hands of her remorseless little enemy.", "평생 누구를 미워해본 적도, 비웃어본 적도 없는 우리의 가엾은 에미는 무자비한 작은 적의 손아귀에서 속수무책이었다."]], [["George danced with Rebecca twice or thrice--how many times Amelia scarcely knew.", "조지는 레베카와 두세 번 춤을 추었다. 몇 번이나 추었는지 애밀리아는 거의 헤아리지도 못했다."], ["She sat quite unnoticed in her corner, except when Rawdon came up with some words of clumsy conversation: and later in the evening, when Captain Dobbin made so bold as to bring her refreshments and sit beside her.", "그녀는 자기 구석 자리에 아무도 거들떠보지 않는 채 앉아 있었는데, 다만 로던이 다가와 어설픈 말 몇 마디를 건넬 때나, 저녁이 깊어 도빈 대위가 용기를 내어 다과를 가져다주고 곁에 앉아줄 때만은 예외였다. 그는 그녀에게 어째서 그리 서글퍼하느냐고 차마 묻지 못했다."], ["He did not like to ask her why she was so sad; but as a pretext for the tears which were filling in her eyes, she told him that Mrs. Crawley had alarmed her by telling her that George would go on playing.", "그러나 그녀는 눈에 차오르는 눈물의 핑계로, 조지가 노름을 계속할 거라는 크롤리 부인의 말에 겁이 났다고 그에게 말했다."]], [["\"It is curious, when a man is bent upon play, by what clumsy rogues he will allow himself to be cheated,\"", "\"한 사내가 노름에 빠지면, 어찌나 어설픈 악당들한테까지 제 발로 속아 넘어가주는지 참 묘한 일이지요.\""], ["Dobbin said; and Emmy said, \"Indeed.\"", "도빈이 말했고, 에미는 \"정말 그러네요\" 하고 대답했다."], ["She was thinking of something else.", "그녀는 다른 무언가를 생각하고 있었다."], ["It was not the loss of the money that grieved her.", "그녀를 슬프게 한 것은 돈을 잃는 일이 아니었다."]], [["At last George came back for Rebecca's shawl and flowers.", "마침내 조지가 레베카의 숄과 꽃을 가지러 돌아왔다."], ["She was going away.", "그녀는 떠나려던 참이었다."], ["She did not even condescend to come back and say good-bye to Amelia.", "그녀는 다시 와서 애밀리아에게 작별 인사를 건네는 수고조차 베풀지 않았다."], ["The poor girl let her husband come and go without saying a word, and her head fell on her breast.", "그 가엾은 아이는 남편이 오가는 것을 한마디 말도 없이 내버려둔 채, 고개를 가슴께로 떨구었다."], ["Dobbin had been called away, and was whispering deep in conversation with the General of the division, his friend, and had not seen this last parting.", "도빈은 어디론가 불려가, 벗인 사단장 장군과 깊숙이 머리를 맞대고 속삭이듯 이야기를 나누느라 이 마지막 작별을 보지 못했다."], ["George went away then with the bouquet; but when he gave it to the owner, there lay a note, coiled like a snake among the flowers.", "그리하여 조지는 꽃다발을 들고 떠났다. 그러나 그 주인에게 그것을 건넬 때, 꽃 사이에 뱀처럼 똬리를 튼 쪽지 한 장이 놓여 있었다."], ["Rebecca's eye caught it at once.", "레베카의 눈은 단번에 그것을 알아챘다."], ["She had been used to deal with notes in early life.", "그녀는 어린 시절부터 쪽지를 다루는 데 이골이 난 사람이었다."], ["She put out her hand and took the nosegay.", "그녀는 손을 내밀어 꽃다발을 받아 들었다."], ["He saw by her eyes as they met, that she was aware what she should find there.", "두 사람의 눈길이 마주치는 순간, 그는 그녀가 거기서 무엇을 찾게 될지 알고 있음을 그 눈빛으로 알아챘다."], ["Her husband hurried her away, still too intent upon his own thoughts, seemingly, to take note of any marks of recognition which might pass between his friend and his wife.", "그녀의 남편은 그녀를 서둘러 데리고 갔는데, 여전히 제 생각에 너무 골몰한 나머지 자기 벗과 자기 아내 사이에 오갔을 법한 어떤 알은체의 기색도 알아차리지 못하는 듯했다."], ["These were, however, but trifling.", "하지만 그것은 그저 사소한 것들뿐이었다."], ["Rebecca gave George her hand with one of her usual quick knowing glances, and made a curtsey and walked away.", "레베카는 늘 짓던 그 빠르고 의미심장한 눈길 가운데 하나를 던지며 조지에게 손을 내밀었고, 무릎을 굽혀 인사한 다음 걸어가버렸다."], ["George bowed over the hand, said nothing in reply to a remark of Crawley's, did not hear it even, his brain was so throbbing with triumph and excitement, and allowed them to go away without a word.", "조지는 그 손에 머리를 숙였고, 크롤리의 말에 아무 대답도 하지 않았으며, 실은 듣지조차 못했으니, 그의 머릿속은 승리감과 흥분으로 어찌나 욱신거리던지, 그들이 한마디 말도 없이 떠나가도록 내버려두고 말았다."]], [["His wife saw the one part at least of the bouquet-scene.", "그의 아내는 적어도 그 꽃다발 장면의 한 대목만큼은 보았다."], ["It was quite natural that George should come at Rebecca's request to get her her scarf and flowers: it was no more than he had done twenty times before in the course of the last few days; but now it was too much for her.", "레베카의 청을 받고 조지가 그녀의 스카프와 꽃을 가지러 가는 것은 지극히 자연스러운 일이었으니, 지난 며칠 사이에 그가 스무 번은 했던 일에 지나지 않았다. 그러나 이제 그것은 그녀에게 너무도 벅찬 일이었다."], ["\"William,\"", "\"윌리엄.\""], ["she said, suddenly clinging to Dobbin, who was near her, \"you've always been very kind to me--I'm--I'm not well.", "그녀가 가까이 있던 도빈에게 불쑥 매달리며 말했다. \"당신은 늘 제게 무척 친절하셨어요."], ["Take me home.\"", "저… 저는 몸이 좋지 않아요."], ["She did not know she called him by his Christian name, as George was accustomed to do.", "집에 데려다주세요.\" 그녀는 자기가 그를 세례명으로 부른 줄도 몰랐으니, 조지가 늘 그렇게 부르던 터였다."], ["He went away with her quickly.", "그는 그녀와 함께 서둘러 자리를 떴다."], ["Her lodgings were hard by; and they threaded through the crowd without, where everything seemed to be more astir than even in the ball-room within.", "그녀의 숙소는 바로 근처였다. 두 사람은 바깥 인파를 헤치고 나아갔는데, 그곳은 무도회장 안보다도 모든 것이 한층 더 술렁이는 듯했다."]], [["George had been angry twice or thrice at finding his wife up on his return from the parties which he frequented: so she went straight to bed now; but although she did not sleep, and although the din and clatter, and the galloping of horsemen were incessant, she never heard any of these noises, having quite other disturbances to keep her awake.", "조지는 자기가 자주 드나드는 모임에서 돌아왔을 때 아내가 아직 깨어 있는 것을 보고 두세 번 화를 낸 적이 있었다. 그래서 그녀는 이제 곧장 잠자리에 들었다. 그러나 잠은 오지 않았고, 소란과 덜컹거림과 기마병들이 질주하는 소리가 끊이지 않았는데도, 그녀는 그 어떤 소음도 듣지 못했으니, 잠을 앗아갈 전혀 다른 동요가 그녀에게 있었기 때문이다."]], [["Osborne meanwhile, wild with elation, went off to a play-table, and began to bet frantically.", "한편 오즈번은 의기양양함에 들떠 노름판으로 가서 미친 듯이 판돈을 걸기 시작했다."], ["He won repeatedly.", "그는 거듭 돈을 땄다."], ["\"Everything succeeds with me to-night,\"", "\"오늘 밤엔 뭐든 다 잘 풀리는군.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["But his luck at play even did not cure him of his restlessness, and he started up after awhile, pocketing his winnings, and went to a buffet, where he drank off many bumpers of wine.", "그러나 노름판에서의 그 행운조차 그의 들뜬 마음을 가라앉혀주지는 못해, 얼마 뒤 그는 딴 돈을 주머니에 챙겨 넣고 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 간이주점으로 가서 포도주를 몇 잔이나 들이켰다."]], [["Here, as he was rattling away to the people around, laughing loudly and wild with spirits, Dobbin found him.", "거기서 그가 주위 사람들에게 떠벌리며 큰 소리로 웃고 잔뜩 들떠 있을 때, 도빈이 그를 찾아냈다."], ["He had been to the card-tables to look there for his friend.", "그는 친구를 찾으러 카드 탁자들을 둘러보고 온 참이었다."], ["Dobbin looked as pale and grave as his comrade was flushed and jovial.", "그의 동무가 얼굴이 불콰하고 흥겨운 만큼이나, 도빈은 창백하고 침통해 보였다."]], [["\"Hullo, Dob!", "\"어이, 도브!"], ["Come and drink, old Dob!", "와서 한잔하게, 우리 도브!"], ["The Duke's wine is famous.", "공작의 포도주는 일품이라네."], ["Give me some more, you sir\"; and he held out a trembling glass for the liquor.", "더 따라주게, 자네.\" 그러고는 떨리는 잔을 내밀어 술을 청했다."]], [["\"Come out, George,\"", "\"나오게, 조지.\""], ["said Dobbin, still gravely; \"don't drink.\"", "도빈이 여전히 침통하게 말했다. \"마시지 말게.\""]], [["\"Drink!", "\"마시라니!"], ["there's nothing like it.", "이만한 게 없는데."], ["Drink yourself, and light up your lantern jaws, old boy.", "자네도 한잔하고 그 홀쭉한 턱에 불 좀 밝히게, 이 사람아."], ["Here's to you.\"", "자네를 위하여.\""]], [["Dobbin went up and whispered something to him, at which George, giving a start and a wild hurray, tossed off his glass, clapped it on the table, and walked away speedily on his friend's arm.", "도빈이 다가가 그에게 무어라 속삭이자, 조지는 흠칫 놀라더니 거칠게 만세를 외치며 잔을 단숨에 비워 탁자에 탁 내려놓고는 친구의 팔에 의지해 황급히 걸어 나갔다."], ["\"The enemy has passed the Sambre,\"", "\"적군이 상브르강을 건넜네.\""], ["William said, \"and our left is already engaged.", "윌리엄이 말했다. \"그리고 우리 좌익은 이미 교전 중이야."], ["Come away.", "어서 나오게."], ["We are to march in three hours.\"", "우린 세 시간 안에 진군해야 해.\""]], [["Away went George, his nerves quivering with excitement at the news so long looked for, so sudden when it came.", "조지는 자리를 떴다. 그토록 오래 기다려왔으나 막상 닥치니 그토록 갑작스러운 그 소식에, 그의 신경은 흥분으로 떨렸다."], ["What were love and intrigue now?", "이제 사랑이며 밀회 따위가 다 무엇이란 말인가?"], ["He thought about a thousand things but these in his rapid walk to his quarters--his past life and future chances--the fate which might be before him--the wife, the child perhaps, from whom unseen he might be about to part.", "그는 거처로 빠르게 걸어가는 동안 그것 말고 천 가지 일들을 떠올렸다. 지나온 삶과 앞날의 가망, 자기 앞에 놓였을지 모를 운명, 그리고 그가 보지도 못한 채 영영 헤어지게 될지 모를 아내와, 어쩌면 아이까지."], ["Oh, how he wished that night's work undone!", "아, 그는 그날 밤에 저지른 일을 되돌릴 수만 있다면 얼마나 좋을까 싶었다!"], ["and that with a clear conscience at least he might say farewell to the tender and guileless being by whose love he had set such little store!", "그래서 적어도 떳떳한 양심으로, 그토록 하찮게 여겼던 그 사랑을 품은 다정하고 순진한 사람에게 작별을 고할 수만 있다면 얼마나 좋을까!"]], [["He thought over his brief married life.", "그는 짧았던 결혼 생활을 되짚어보았다."], ["In those few weeks he had frightfully dissipated his little capital.", "그 몇 주 사이에 그는 자신의 보잘것없는 밑천을 끔찍하게 탕진해버렸다."], ["How wild and reckless he had been!", "얼마나 무모하고 분별없이 굴었던가!"], ["Should any mischance befall him: what was then left for her?", "자기에게 무슨 변고라도 닥친다면, 그땐 그녀에게 무엇이 남을 것인가?"], ["How unworthy he was of her.", "자기는 얼마나 그녀에게 어울리지 않는 사내인가."], ["Why had he married her?", "어째서 그녀와 결혼했단 말인가?"], ["He was not fit for marriage.", "그는 결혼할 깜냥이 못 되는 사람이었다."], ["Why had he disobeyed his father, who had been always so generous to him?", "어째서 그는, 늘 그토록 자기에게 너그러웠던 아버지의 뜻을 거역했단 말인가?"], ["Hope, remorse, ambition, tenderness, and selfish regret filled his heart.", "희망과 회한, 야망과 다정함, 그리고 이기적인 후회가 그의 가슴을 가득 채웠다."], ["He sate down and wrote to his father, remembering what he had said once before, when he was engaged to fight a duel.", "그는 자리에 앉아 아버지에게 편지를 썼는데, 언젠가 결투를 앞두었던 시절 자기가 했던 말을 떠올리면서였다."], ["Dawn faintly streaked the sky as he closed this farewell letter.", "이 작별의 편지를 다 썼을 무렵, 동이 희미하게 하늘에 줄을 긋고 있었다."], ["He sealed it, and kissed the superscription.", "그는 봉인을 하고 겉봉에 입을 맞추었다."], ["He thought how he had deserted that generous father, and of the thousand kindnesses which the stern old man had done him.", "그는 자기가 그 너그러운 아버지를 저버렸던 일을, 또 그 준엄한 노인이 자기에게 베풀었던 수천 가지 친절을 떠올렸다."]], [["He had looked into Amelia's bedroom when he entered; she lay quiet, and her eyes seemed closed, and he was glad that she was asleep.", "그는 들어올 때 애밀리아의 침실을 들여다보았다. 그녀는 고요히 누워 있었고 눈은 감긴 듯했으니, 그는 그녀가 잠들어 있어 다행이라 여겼다."], ["On arriving at his quarters from the ball, he had found his regimental servant already making preparations for his departure: the man had understood his signal to be still, and these arrangements were very quickly and silently made.", "무도회에서 거처로 돌아왔을 때, 그는 연대의 종졸이 벌써 자기 출발 채비를 하고 있는 것을 발견했다. 그 사내는 조용히 하라는 그의 신호를 알아챘고, 그리하여 이 준비는 무척 빠르고 소리 없이 이루어졌다."], ["Should he go in and wake Amelia, he thought, or leave a note for her brother to break the news of departure to her?", "들어가서 애밀리아를 깨워야 할까, 아니면 그녀의 오라비에게 쪽지를 남겨 출발 소식을 그녀에게 전하게 할까, 하고 그는 생각했다."], ["He went in to look at her once again.", "그는 그녀를 다시 한 번 보려고 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["She had been awake when he first entered her room, but had kept her eyes closed, so that even her wakefulness should not seem to reproach him.", "그가 처음 방에 들어왔을 때 그녀는 깨어 있었으나 눈을 감은 채로 있었으니, 깨어 있다는 사실조차 그를 책망하는 것처럼 보이지 않게 하려는 것이었다."], ["But when he had returned, so soon after herself, too, this timid little heart had felt more at ease, and turning towards him as he stept softly out of the room, she had fallen into a light sleep.", "그러나 그가, 그것도 자기 뒤를 이어 그토록 빨리 돌아오자, 이 겁 많은 작은 가슴은 한결 마음이 놓였고, 그가 발소리를 죽여 방을 나설 때 그를 향해 돌아누우며 그녀는 얕은 잠에 빠졌던 것이다."], ["George came in and looked at her again, entering still more softly.", "조지는 한층 더 발소리를 죽이며 들어와 그녀를 다시 한 번 바라보았다."], ["By the pale night-lamp he could see her sweet, pale face--the purple eyelids were fringed and closed, and one round arm, smooth and white, lay outside of the coverlet.", "창백한 야등 불빛 아래 그는 그녀의 사랑스럽고 창백한 얼굴을 볼 수 있었다. 보랏빛 도는 눈꺼풀은 속눈썹을 드리운 채 감겨 있었고, 매끄럽고 흰 둥근 팔 하나가 이불 밖으로 나와 있었다."], ["Good God!", "맙소사!"], ["how pure she was; how gentle, how tender, and how friendless!", "그녀는 얼마나 순결한가. 얼마나 온순하고, 얼마나 다정하며, 또 얼마나 의지가지없는가!"], ["and he, how selfish, brutal, and black with crime!", "그런데 자기는 얼마나 이기적이고, 잔인하며, 죄로 새카만 사내인가!"], ["Heart-stained, and shame-stricken, he stood at the bed's foot, and looked at the sleeping girl.", "마음이 더럽혀지고 수치심에 사로잡힌 채, 그는 침대 발치에 서서 잠든 소녀를 바라보았다."], ["How dared he--who was he, to pray for one so spotless!", "그가 감히 어찌, 자기 같은 위인이 무엇이라고, 이토록 티 없는 사람을 위해 기도한단 말인가!"], ["God bless her!", "그녀에게 신의 가호가 있기를!"], ["God bless her!", "신의 가호가 있기를!"], ["He came to the bedside, and looked at the hand, the little soft hand, lying asleep; and he bent over the pillow noiselessly towards the gentle pale face.", "그는 침대맡으로 다가가, 잠든 채 놓인 그 손을, 그 자그럽고 보드라운 손을 바라보았다. 그러고는 소리 없이 베개 위로 몸을 숙여 그 온순하고 창백한 얼굴 쪽으로 다가갔다."]], [["Two fair arms closed tenderly round his neck as he stooped down.", "그가 몸을 숙이자 어여쁜 두 팔이 다정하게 그의 목을 감아 안았다."], ["\"I am awake, George,\"", "\"저 깨어 있어요, 조지.\""], ["the poor child said, with a sob fit to break the little heart that nestled so closely by his own.", "그 가엾은 아이가 말했다. 그토록 가까이 그의 가슴에 안긴 그 작은 가슴이 찢어질 듯한 흐느낌과 함께였다."], ["She was awake, poor soul, and to what?", "그녀는 깨어 있었다, 가엾은 영혼, 그런데 무엇 때문에 깨어 있었던가?"], ["At that moment a bugle from the Place of Arms began sounding clearly, and was taken up through the town; and amidst the drums of the infantry, and the shrill pipes of the Scotch, the whole city awoke.", "바로 그 순간, 광장 쪽에서 나팔 소리가 맑게 울리기 시작해 온 도시로 퍼져나갔다. 그리고 보병대의 북소리와 스코틀랜드 부대의 째지는 피리 소리 가운데, 도시 전체가 잠에서 깨어났다."]], [["\"The Girl I Left Behind Me\" We do not claim to rank among the military novelists.", "우리는 군대 소설가의 반열에 끼기를 자처하지 않는다."], ["Our place is with the non-combatants.", "우리의 자리는 비전투원들 틈이다."], ["When the decks are cleared for action we go below and wait meekly.", "갑판이 전투 채비로 비워지면 우리는 아래로 내려가 얌전히 기다린다."], ["We should only be in the way of the manoeuvres that the gallant fellows are performing overhead.", "머리 위에서 용감한 사내들이 펼치는 작전에 방해만 될 뿐이니까."], ["We shall go no farther with the --th than to the city gate: and leaving Major O'Dowd to his duty, come back to the Major's wife, and the ladies and the baggage.", "우리는 ××연대를 따라 시 성문까지만 갈 것이다. 그러고는 오다우드 소령은 그 임무에 맡겨 둔 채, 소령의 부인과 숙녀들과 짐꾸러미에게로 되돌아오기로 한다."]], [["Now the Major and his lady, who had not been invited to the ball at which in our last chapter other of our friends figured, had much more time to take their wholesome natural rest in bed, than was accorded to people who wished to enjoy pleasure as well as to do duty.", "자, 지난 장에서 다른 벗들이 등장했던 그 무도회에 초대받지 못했던 소령과 그 부인은, 의무를 다하면서 동시에 즐거움도 누리고자 했던 이들보다 침대에서 건강하고 자연스러운 휴식을 취할 시간이 훨씬 넉넉했다."], ["\"It's my belief, Peggy, my dear,\"", "\"내 생각엔 말이오, 페기, 여보.\""], ["said he, as he placidly pulled his nightcap over his ears, \"that there will be such a ball danced in a day or two as some of 'em has never heard the chune of\"; and he was much more happy to retire to rest after partaking of a quiet tumbler, than to figure at any other sort of amusement.", "그가 잠자리 모자를 두 귀까지 차분히 끌어내리며 말했다. \"하루 이틀 안에 어떤 작자들은 그 가락도 들어 본 적 없는 그런 무도회가 한바탕 벌어질 것 같소.\""], ["Peggy, for her part, would have liked to have shown her turban and bird of paradise at the ball, but for the information which her husband had given her, and which made her very grave.", "그러고는 그는 다른 어떤 종류의 여흥에 모습을 드러내느니, 조용히 한잔 걸친 뒤 잠자리에 드는 편이 훨씬 행복했다. 페기로 말하자면, 그 무도회에서 터번과 극락조 깃털을 뽐내고 싶었겠지만, 남편이 들려준 소식 때문에 마음이 몹시 무거워졌다."]], [["\"I'd like ye wake me about half an hour before the assembly beats,\"", "\"집합 나팔이 울리기 한 삼십 분 전쯤에 날 깨워 주면 좋겠소.\""], ["the Major said to his lady.", "소령이 부인에게 말했다."], ["\"Call me at half-past one, Peggy dear, and see me things is ready.", "\"한 시 반에 깨워 주오, 페기, 그리고 내 물건들이 준비됐는지 살펴봐 주고."], ["May be I'll not come back to breakfast, Mrs. O'D.\"", "어쩌면 아침은 먹으러 못 돌아올지도 모르겠소, 오다우드 부인.\""], ["With which words, which signified his opinion that the regiment would march the next morning, the Major ceased talking, and fell asleep.", "이 말은 곧 연대가 이튿날 아침 행군하리라는 그의 견해를 뜻하는 것이었고, 그 말과 함께 소령은 말을 그치고 잠이 들었다."]], [["Mrs. O'Dowd, the good housewife, arrayed in curl papers and a camisole, felt that her duty was to act, and not to sleep, at this juncture.", "알뜰한 살림꾼 오다우드 부인은, 종이로 만 컬에 속옷 차림으로, 이 중대한 고비에 자신의 의무란 잠자는 것이 아니라 움직이는 것임을 절감했다."], ["\"Time enough for that,\"", "\"그건 믹이 떠나고 나서 해도 늦지 않아.\""], ["she said, \"when Mick's gone\"; and so she packed his travelling valise ready for the march, brushed his cloak, his cap, and other warlike habiliments, set them out in order for him; and stowed away in the cloak pockets a light package of portable refreshments, and a wicker-covered flask or pocket-pistol, containing near a pint of a remarkably sound Cognac brandy, of which she and the Major approved very much; and as soon as the hands of the \"repayther\"", "그녀는 그렇게 말하고는, 행군에 대비해 그의 여행 가방을 꾸리고, 그의 외투와 군모와 그 밖의 군장을 솔질하여 차곡차곡 챙겨 두었으며, 외투 주머니에는 가볍게 들고 다닐 만한 간식 꾸러미와, 등나무로 감싼 휴대용 술병 하나를 넣어 두었다. 그 병에는 그녀와 소령이 무척 흡족해하는, 한 파인트 가까이 되는 빼어나게 좋은 코냑 브랜디가 담겨 있었다."], ["pointed to half-past one, and its interior arrangements (it had a tone quite equal to a cathaydral, its fair owner considered) knelled forth that fatal hour, Mrs. O'Dowd woke up her Major, and had as comfortable a cup of coffee prepared for him as any made that morning in Brussels.", "그리고 \"기보종\"의 바늘이 한 시 반을 가리키고, 그 내부 장치가(그 가락이 대성당 종소리에 견줄 만하다고 어여쁜 주인은 여겼다) 그 운명의 시각을 울려 알리자마자, 오다우드 부인은 소령을 깨우고, 그날 아침 브뤼셀에서 끓인 그 어떤 것 못지않게 안락한 커피 한 잔을 그를 위해 마련해 두었다."], ["And who is there will deny that this worthy lady's preparations betokened affection as much as the fits of tears and hysterics by which more sensitive females exhibited their love, and that their partaking of this coffee, which they drank together while the bugles were sounding the turn-out and the drums beating in the various quarters of the town, was not more useful and to the purpose than the outpouring of any mere sentiment could be?", "그러니 이 갸륵한 부인의 채비가, 더 예민한 여인네들이 눈물과 발작으로 제 사랑을 드러내는 것 못지않게 애정을 담은 것이었음을, 또한 도시 곳곳에서 출동 나팔이 울리고 북이 울리는 동안 둘이 함께 마신 그 커피가, 한낱 감상의 토로 따위보다 훨씬 쓸모 있고 요긴한 것이었음을, 누가 부인하겠는가?"], ["The consequence was, that the Major appeared on parade quite trim, fresh, and alert, his well-shaved rosy countenance, as he sate on horseback, giving cheerfulness and confidence to the whole corps.", "그 결과 소령은 사열장에 더없이 단정하고, 생기 넘치고, 기민한 모습으로 나타났으니, 말 위에 앉은 그의 깔끔히 면도한 발그레한 얼굴은 부대 전체에 쾌활함과 자신감을 안겨 주었다."], ["All the officers saluted her when the regiment marched by the balcony on which this brave woman stood, and waved them a cheer as they passed; and I daresay it was not from want of courage, but from a sense of female delicacy and propriety, that she refrained from leading the gallant--th personally into action.", "이 용감한 여인이 서 있던 발코니 앞으로 연대가 행군해 지날 때 모든 장교가 그녀에게 경례했고, 지나가면서 그녀에게 환호를 보냈다. 그리고 그녀가 그 용맹한 ××연대를 몸소 전투로 이끌지 않은 것은, 단언컨대 용기가 부족해서가 아니라 여성으로서의 섬세함과 분별 때문이었으리라."]], [["On Sundays, and at periods of a solemn nature, Mrs. O'Dowd used to read with great gravity out of a large volume of her uncle the Dean's sermons.", "일요일이나 엄숙한 성격의 시기에는, 오다우드 부인은 숙부인 사제께서 쓰신 설교집 큰 책을 무척 근엄하게 읽곤 했다."], ["It had been of great comfort to her on board the transport as they were coming home, and were very nearly wrecked, on their return from the West Indies.", "그 책은 서인도제도에서 돌아오던 길에 수송선이 거의 난파될 뻔했을 때 그녀에게 큰 위안이 되어 주었다. 연대가 떠난 뒤 그녀는 묵상을 위해 이 책을 펼쳐 들었다."], ["After the regiment's departure she betook herself to this volume for meditation; perhaps she did not understand much of what she was reading, and her thoughts were elsewhere: but the sleep project, with poor Mick's nightcap there on the pillow, was quite a vain one.", "어쩌면 읽고 있는 내용을 그리 많이 이해하지 못했고, 마음은 딴 데 가 있었을지도 모른다. 그러나 가엾은 믹의 잠자리 모자가 저기 베개 위에 놓여 있는 마당에, 잠을 자겠다는 계획은 부질없는 것이었다."], ["So it is in the world.", "세상사가 다 그런 법이다."], ["Jack or Donald marches away to glory with his knapsack on his shoulder, stepping out briskly to the tune of \"The Girl I Left Behind Me.\"", "잭이든 도널드든 배낭을 어깨에 메고 \"내가 두고 온 여인\"의 가락에 맞추어 경쾌하게 발을 내디디며 영광을 향해 행군해 간다."], ["It is she who remains and suffers--and has the leisure to think, and brood, and remember.", "남아서 고통받는 이는 그녀다. 그리고 생각하고, 곱씹고, 추억할 여유를 가진 이도 그녀다."]], [["Knowing how useless regrets are, and how the indulgence of sentiment only serves to make people more miserable, Mrs. Rebecca wisely determined to give way to no vain feelings of sorrow, and bore the parting from her husband with quite a Spartan equanimity.", "후회란 얼마나 부질없는 것이며, 감상에 빠지는 일이 사람을 더 비참하게 만들 뿐임을 잘 아는 레베카 부인은, 슬픔이라는 헛된 감정에 결코 굴하지 않기로 현명하게 결심하고, 남편과의 작별을 그야말로 스파르타식 평정으로 견뎌 냈다."], ["Indeed Captain Rawdon himself was much more affected at the leave-taking than the resolute little woman to whom he bade farewell.", "사실 로던 대위 본인이 작별의 순간에, 그가 작별을 고하는 그 단호한 작은 여인보다 훨씬 더 감정에 북받쳐 있었다."], ["She had mastered this rude coarse nature; and he loved and worshipped her with all his faculties of regard and admiration.", "그녀는 이 거칠고 투박한 사내를 완전히 휘어잡았던 것이다. 그리고 그는 자신이 지닌 온갖 애모와 흠모의 능력을 다해 그녀를 사랑하고 떠받들었다."], ["In all his life he had never been so happy, as, during the past few months, his wife had made him.", "평생토록 그는, 지난 몇 달 동안 아내가 그를 행복하게 해 준 만큼 행복했던 적이 없었다."], ["All former delights of turf, mess, hunting-field, and gambling-table; all previous loves and courtships of milliners, opera-dancers, and the like easy triumphs of the clumsy military Adonis, were quite insipid when compared to the lawful matrimonial pleasures which of late he had enjoyed.", "경마장이며, 장교 식당이며, 사냥터며, 도박판에서 누렸던 지난날의 온갖 즐거움, 모자 가게 처녀들이며 오페라 무희들이며 하는, 저 어설픈 군인 아도니스가 거두던 손쉬운 승리 따위의 지난 연애와 구애가, 근래 그가 누린 합법적인 부부의 기쁨에 견주면 죄다 시들하기 짝이 없었다."], ["She had known perpetually how to divert him; and he had found his house and her society a thousand times more pleasant than any place or company which he had ever frequented from his childhood until now.", "그녀는 끊임없이 그를 즐겁게 해 줄 줄 알았다. 그래서 그는 어릴 적부터 지금까지 드나든 그 어떤 장소나 모임보다도 제 집과 그녀와 함께하는 시간이 천배는 더 즐겁다는 것을 깨달았다."], ["And he cursed his past follies and extravagances, and bemoaned his vast outlying debts above all, which must remain for ever as obstacles to prevent his wife's advancement in the world.", "그리고 그는 지난날의 어리석음과 방탕을 저주했고, 무엇보다 산더미 같은 미결제 빚을 한탄했으니, 그것이 아내가 세상에서 출세하는 것을 가로막는 영원한 걸림돌로 남으리라는 것이었다."], ["He had often groaned over these in midnight conversations with Rebecca, although as a bachelor they had never given him any disquiet.", "그는 레베카와 한밤중에 이야기를 나누다가 이 빚들을 두고 자주 신음했다. 총각 시절에는 그것이 한 번도 그를 불안하게 만든 적이 없었는데도 말이다."], ["He himself was struck with this phenomenon.", "그 자신도 이 현상에 적잖이 놀랐다."], ["\"Hang it,\"", "\"제기랄,\""], ["he would say (or perhaps use a still stronger expression out of his simple vocabulary), \"before I was married I didn't care what bills I put my name to, and so long as Moses would wait or Levy would renew for three months, I kept on never minding.", "그는 말하곤 했다(어쩌면 그 소박한 어휘에서 더 험한 표현을 골라 썼을지도 모른다). \"결혼하기 전엔 무슨 어음에 내 이름을 적든 신경도 안 썼지."], ["But since I'm married, except renewing, of course, I give you my honour I've not touched a bit of stamped paper.\"", "모지스가 좀 기다려 주거나 레비가 석 달 연장만 해 주면, 난 아무 걱정 없이 계속 그러고 살았소. 그런데 결혼하고 나선, 물론 연장하는 거야 빼고, 맹세코 인지 붙은 종이엔 손도 안 댔다오.\""]], [["Rebecca always knew how to conjure away these moods of melancholy.", "레베카는 이런 우울한 기분을 마법처럼 떨쳐 버리는 법을 늘 알고 있었다."], ["\"Why, my stupid love,\"", "\"어머, 우리 멍청한 양반.\""], ["she would say, \"we have not done with your aunt yet.", "그녀는 말하곤 했다. \"아직 당신 고모님 일이 끝난 게 아니잖아요."], ["If she fails us, isn't there what you call the Gazette?", "그분이 우릴 저버린다 해도, 당신이 말하던 그 관보라는 게 있지 않아요?"], ["or, stop, when your uncle Bute's life drops, I have another scheme.", "아니, 가만, 당신 숙부 뷰트 씨가 돌아가시면 내게 또 다른 묘안이 있어요."], ["The living has always belonged to the younger brother, and why shouldn't you sell out and go into the Church?\"", "그 목사 자리는 늘 동생 쪽에 돌아갔으니, 당신이 군적을 팔고 성직에 들면 안 될 게 뭐예요?\""], ["The idea of this conversion set Rawdon into roars of laughter: you might have heard the explosion through the hotel at midnight, and the haw-haws of the great dragoon's voice.", "이렇게 개종한다는 발상에 로던은 폭소를 터뜨렸다. 한밤중에 호텔 전체에 그 폭소가 울려 퍼지고, 이 덩치 큰 용기병의 우렁찬 너털웃음 소리가 들렸을 정도였다."], ["General Tufto heard him from his quarters on the first floor above them; and Rebecca acted the scene with great spirit, and preached Rawdon's first sermon, to the immense delight of the General at breakfast.", "터프토 장군은 그들 위층인 이 층 자기 숙소에서 그 소리를 들었다. 그리고 레베카는 그 장면을 아주 신명 나게 연기하며, 로던의 첫 설교를 늘어놓아, 아침 식사 자리에서 장군을 더할 나위 없이 즐겁게 했다."]], [["But these were mere by-gone days and talk.", "그러나 이것은 한낱 지나간 날들이며 지나간 이야기였다."], ["When the final news arrived that the campaign was opened, and the troops were to march, Rawdon's gravity became such that Becky rallied him about it in a manner which rather hurt the feelings of the Guardsman.", "전역이 개시되어 부대가 행군하게 되었다는 최종 소식이 전해지자, 로던의 표정이 어찌나 심각해졌던지, 베키가 그 일로 그를 놀려 댔는데, 그것이 이 근위병의 마음을 다소 상하게 했다."], ["\"You don't suppose I'm afraid, Becky, I should think,\"", "\"내가 겁먹었다고 생각하는 건 아니겠지, 베키, 그건 아닐 테고.\""], ["he said, with a tremor in his voice.", "그가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다."], ["\"But I'm a pretty good mark for a shot, and you see if it brings me down, why I leave one and perhaps two behind me whom I should wish to provide for, as I brought 'em into the scrape.", "\"하지만 난 총알 맞기 딱 좋은 표적이거든. 만약 그게 날 쓰러뜨리면, 봐, 난 내가 끌어들여 이 곤경에 빠뜨린, 내가 보살펴 주고 싶은 사람을 하나, 어쩌면 둘이나 남기고 가는 거잖아."], ["It is no laughing matter that, Mrs. C., anyways.\"", "그건 어찌 됐든 웃을 일이 아니야, 크롤리 부인.\""]], [["Rebecca by a hundred caresses and kind words tried to soothe the feelings of the wounded lover.", "레베카는 갖은 애무와 다정한 말로 상처받은 연인의 마음을 달래려 했다."], ["It was only when her vivacity and sense of humour got the better of this sprightly creature (as they would do under most circumstances of life indeed) that she would break out with her satire, but she could soon put on a demure face.", "이 발랄한 사람은 발랄함과 익살이 도를 넘칠 때라야(사실 인생의 거의 모든 상황에서 그렇기 마련이지만) 풍자를 터뜨렸으나, 이내 다소곳한 표정을 지을 줄도 알았다. \"더없이 사랑하는 양반.\""], ["\"Dearest love,\"", "그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, \"do you suppose I feel nothing?\"", "\"내가 아무것도 못 느낄 것 같아요?\""], ["and hastily dashing something from her eyes, she looked up in her husband's face with a smile.", "그러고는 황급히 눈가에서 무언가를 훔쳐 내며, 미소 띤 얼굴로 남편의 얼굴을 올려다보았다."]], [["\"Look here,\"", "\"이거 봐.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"If I drop, let us see what there is for you.", "\"만약 내가 쓰러지면, 당신한테 뭐가 남는지 한번 따져 보자고."], ["I have had a pretty good run of luck here, and here's two hundred and thirty pounds.", "여기서 운이 제법 좋았어. 여기 이백삼십 파운드가 있고."], ["I have got ten Napoleons in my pocket.", "주머니엔 나폴레옹 금화 열 닢이 있어. 그 정도면 내겐 충분하지."], ["That is as much as I shall want; for the General pays everything like a prince; and if I'm hit, why you know I cost nothing.", "장군이 뭐든 왕자처럼 다 내주니까. 그리고 내가 총에 맞으면, 알다시피 난 돈 한 푼 안 드는 셈이고."], ["Don't cry, little woman; I may live to vex you yet.", "울지 마, 작은 여인, 살아 돌아와서 또 당신 속을 썩일지도 모르잖아."], ["Well, I shan't take either of my horses, but shall ride the General's grey charger: it's cheaper, and I told him mine was lame.", "그래, 내 말 두 마리는 둘 다 안 데려가고 장군의 회색 군마를 탈 거야. 그게 더 싸게 먹히고, 게다가 장군한테는 내 말이 절름발이라고 해 뒀거든."], ["If I'm done, those two ought to fetch you something.", "내가 잘못되면, 그 두 마리가 당신한테 돈푼깨나 만들어 줄 거야."], ["Grigg offered ninety for the mare yesterday, before this confounded news came, and like a fool I wouldn't let her go under the two o's.", "어제 이 빌어먹을 소식이 오기 전에 그리그가 암말한테 구십을 부르던데, 멍청하게도 난 이백 아래로는 못 넘긴다고 했지."], ["Bullfinch will fetch his price any day, only you'd better sell him in this country, because the dealers have so many bills of mine, and so I'd rather he shouldn't go back to England.", "불핀치는 언제 팔아도 제값을 받을 거야. 다만 이 나라에서 파는 게 나아, 거래상들이 내 어음을 잔뜩 쥐고 있으니까 영국으로 도로 가지 않는 편이 낫거든."], ["Your little mare the General gave you will fetch something, and there's no d--d livery stable bills here as there are in London,\"", "장군이 당신한테 준 작은 암말도 얼마간 값이 나갈 거고, 여긴 런던처럼 빌어먹을 마구간 외상값 같은 것도 없으니까.\""], ["Rawdon added, with a laugh.", "로던이 웃으며 덧붙였다."], ["\"There's that dressing-case cost me two hundred--that is, I owe two for it; and the gold tops and bottles must be worth thirty or forty.", "\"저 화장 도구 상자는 이백을 줬어. 아니, 그거 값으로 이백 빚을 졌단 말이지."], ["Please to put THAT up the spout, ma'am, with my pins, and rings, and watch and chain, and things.", "금 뚜껑이랑 병들은 삼사십은 나갈 거야. 그건 전당포에 맡겨요, 부인, 내 핀이랑 반지랑 시계랑 시곗줄이랑 다 같이."], ["They cost a precious lot of money.", "그것들 값이 어마어마하게 나갔거든."], ["Miss Crawley, I know, paid a hundred down for the chain and ticker.", "크롤리 고모가 그 줄이랑 회중시계에 현금으로 백을 냈다는 거 내가 알지."], ["Gold tops and bottles, indeed!", "금 뚜껑에 병이라니, 참!"], ["dammy, I'm sorry I didn't take more now.", "빌어먹을, 이젠 그때 더 안 챙긴 게 후회되네."], ["Edwards pressed on me a silver-gilt boot-jack, and I might have had a dressing-case fitted up with a silver warming-pan, and a service of plate.", "에드워즈가 은도금 장화 벗개를 억지로 떠안겼는데, 은 탕파에다 식기 한 벌까지 갖춘 화장 도구 상자도 받아 둘 수 있었을 텐데."], ["But we must make the best of what we've got, Becky, you know.\"", "하지만 가진 걸로 최선을 다해야지, 베키, 알잖아.\""]], [["And so, making his last dispositions, Captain Crawley, who had seldom thought about anything but himself, until the last few months of his life, when Love had obtained the mastery over the dragoon, went through the various items of his little catalogue of effects, striving to see how they might be turned into money for his wife's benefit, in case any accident should befall him.", "그리하여 인생의 마지막 몇 달, 사랑이 이 용기병을 완전히 사로잡기 전까지는 자기 자신 말고는 좀처럼 무엇도 생각해 본 적 없던 크롤리 대위는, 마지막 처분을 매기며, 혹여 자신에게 무슨 변고가 닥칠 경우 그것들을 어떻게 아내에게 득이 되도록 돈으로 바꿀 수 있을지 궁리하면서, 자신의 자그마한 재산 목록을 항목별로 하나하나 짚어 나갔다."], ["He pleased himself by noting down with a pencil, in his big schoolboy handwriting, the various items of his portable property which might be sold for his widow's advantage as, for example, \"My double-barril by Manton, say 40 guineas; my driving cloak, lined with sable fur, 50 pounds; my duelling pistols in rosewood case (same which I shot Captain Marker), 20 pounds; my regulation saddle-holsters and housings; my Laurie ditto,\"", "그는 그 큼직한 학동 같은 필체로, 미망인이 된 아내에게 득이 되도록 팔 수 있을 만한 휴대 재산의 갖가지 항목을 연필로 적어 내려가며 스스로 흐뭇해했다. 가령 \"맨턴제 쌍열총, 사십 기니쯤; 검은담비 모피 안감을 댄 내 마차용 외투, 오십 파운드; 자단 케이스에 든 내 결투용 권총(마커 대위를 쏜 바로 그것), 이십 파운드; 내 규격 안장 권총집과 마구 덮개; 내 로리제 동품\""], ["and so forth, over all of which articles he made Rebecca the mistress.", "따위로, 이 모든 물품의 주인을 레베카로 정했다."]], [["Faithful to his plan of economy, the Captain dressed himself in his oldest and shabbiest uniform and epaulets, leaving the newest behind, under his wife's (or it might be his widow's) guardianship.", "절약 계획에 충실하게도, 대위는 가장 낡고 허름한 군복과 견장을 걸치고, 가장 새것은 아내(혹은 미망인이 될지도 모를 사람)의 보관에 맡겨 두고 떠났다."], ["And this famous dandy of Windsor and Hyde Park went off on his campaign with a kit as modest as that of a sergeant, and with something like a prayer on his lips for the woman he was leaving.", "그리하여 윈저와 하이드파크에서 이름난 이 멋쟁이는, 하사관 못지않게 수수한 군장을 갖추고, 두고 떠나는 여인을 위한 기도 비슷한 것을 입가에 머금은 채 전역에 나섰다."], ["He took her up from the ground, and held her in his arms for a minute, tight pressed against his strong-beating heart.", "그는 그녀를 바닥에서 들어 올려, 세차게 뛰는 자신의 강인한 가슴에 꼭 끌어안은 채 잠시 품에 안고 있었다."], ["His face was purple and his eyes dim, as he put her down and left her.", "그녀를 내려놓고 떠날 때 그의 얼굴은 검붉었고 두 눈은 흐릿했다."], ["He rode by his General's side, and smoked his cigar in silence as they hastened after the troops of the General's brigade, which preceded them; and it was not until they were some miles on their way that he left off twirling his moustache and broke silence.", "그는 장군의 곁에서 말을 몰며, 그들에 앞서 가는 장군 여단의 부대를 서둘러 뒤쫓는 동안 말없이 시가를 피웠다. 그리고 길을 따라 몇 마일을 간 뒤에야 콧수염을 비비 꼬던 손을 멈추고 침묵을 깼다."]], [["And Rebecca, as we have said, wisely determined not to give way to unavailing sentimentality on her husband's departure.", "그리고 레베카는, 앞서 말했듯이, 남편의 출발에 부질없는 감상에 빠지지 않기로 현명하게 결심했다."], ["She waved him an adieu from the window, and stood there for a moment looking out after he was gone.", "그녀는 창가에서 그에게 작별의 손짓을 보내고, 그가 떠난 뒤에도 잠시 그 자리에 서서 그가 간 쪽을 바라보았다."], ["The cathedral towers and the full gables of the quaint old houses were just beginning to blush in the sunrise.", "대성당의 탑들과 고풍스러운 옛집들의 둥근 박공이 막 해돋이에 발그레 물들기 시작하고 있었다."], ["There had been no rest for her that night.", "그날 밤 그녀에게는 휴식이라곤 없었다."], ["She was still in her pretty ball-dress, her fair hair hanging somewhat out of curl on her neck, and the circles round her eyes dark with watching.", "그녀는 아직도 그 어여쁜 무도회복 차림이었고, 금빛 머리칼은 컬이 다소 풀린 채 목덜미로 흘러내렸으며, 밤을 새운 탓에 두 눈가에는 거뭇한 그늘이 드리워 있었다."], ["\"What a fright I seem,\"", "\"내 꼴이 영 흉측하잖아.\""], ["she said, examining herself in the glass, \"and how pale this pink makes one look!\"", "그녀가 거울에 자신을 비추어 보며 말했다. \"그리고 이 분홍색 옷은 사람을 어찌나 창백해 보이게 하는지!\""], ["So she divested herself of this pink raiment; in doing which a note fell out from her corsage, which she picked up with a smile, and locked into her dressing-box.", "그래서 그녀는 이 분홍빛 의상을 벗었는데, 그러는 사이 보디스에서 쪽지 하나가 떨어졌다. 그녀는 미소 지으며 그것을 주워 화장 상자에 넣고 잠가 두었다."], ["And then she put her bouquet of the ball into a glass of water, and went to bed, and slept very comfortably.", "그러고는 무도회에서 받은 꽃다발을 물 잔에 꽂아 두고, 잠자리에 들어 아주 편안하게 잤다."]], [["The town was quite quiet when she woke up at ten o'clock, and partook of coffee, very requisite and comforting after the exhaustion and grief of the morning's occurrences.", "그녀가 열 시에 깨어나, 그날 아침 사건의 피로와 슬픔 뒤에 더없이 요긴하고 위안이 되는 커피를 마실 때, 도시는 완전히 잠잠했다."]], [["This meal over, she resumed honest Rawdon's calculations of the night previous, and surveyed her position.", "이 식사를 마치고 나서, 그녀는 정직한 로던이 전날 밤에 한 계산을 다시 이어, 자신의 처지를 점검했다."], ["Should the worst befall, all things considered, she was pretty well to do.", "최악의 사태가 닥친다 해도, 이모저모 따져 보니 그녀의 형편은 꽤 괜찮았다."], ["There were her own trinkets and trousseau, in addition to those which her husband had left behind.", "남편이 남기고 간 것들에 더해, 그녀 자신의 패물과 혼수도 있었다."], ["Rawdon's generosity, when they were first married, has already been described and lauded.", "처음 결혼했을 때 로던이 보인 너그러움은 이미 묘사하고 칭송한 바 있다."], ["Besides these, and the little mare, the General, her slave and worshipper, had made her many very handsome presents, in the shape of cashmere shawls bought at the auction of a bankrupt French general's lady, and numerous tributes from the jewellers' shops, all of which betokened her admirer's taste and wealth.", "이것들 말고도, 또 작은 암말 말고도, 그녀의 노예이자 숭배자인 장군이 그녀에게 아주 값진 선물을 숱하게 안겨 주었으니, 파산한 어느 프랑스 장군 부인의 경매에서 사들인 캐시미어 숄이며, 보석상에서 바쳐 온 무수한 공물 따위로, 이 모두가 이 흠모자의 안목과 재력을 말해 주는 것이었다."], ["As for \"tickers,\" as poor Rawdon called watches, her apartments were alive with their clicking.", "가엾은 로던이 회중시계를 부르던 말마따나 \"딸깍이\"로 말하자면, 그녀의 거처는 그것들의 딸깍거리는 소리로 생기가 넘쳤다."], ["For, happening to mention one night that hers, which Rawdon had given to her, was of English workmanship, and went ill, on the very next morning there came to her a little bijou marked Leroy, with a chain and cover charmingly set with turquoises, and another signed Brequet, which was covered with pearls, and yet scarcely bigger than a half-crown.", "어느 날 밤 그녀가 무심코, 로던이 준 자기 시계가 영국제라 잘 안 맞는다고 말했더니, 바로 이튿날 아침 르루아라는 상표가 박힌 자그마한 보석 하나가, 터키석으로 매혹적으로 장식한 줄과 뚜껑이 달린 채로 그녀에게 왔고, 또 브레게라는 서명이 든 다른 하나는, 진주로 뒤덮였으면서도 반 크라운 동전보다 겨우 큰 정도였다."], ["General Tufto had bought one, and Captain Osborne had gallantly presented the other. Mrs.", "터프토 장군이 하나를 샀고, 오즈번 대위가 다른 하나를 늠름하게 선사한 것이었다."], ["Osborne had no watch, though, to do George justice, she might have had one for the asking, and the Honourable Mrs.", "그런데 오즈번 부인에게는 시계가 없었다. 조지의 명예를 위해 덧붙이자면, 그녀가 달라고만 했으면 하나쯤 가질 수도 있었을 텐데 말이다."], ["Tufto in England had an old instrument of her mother's that might have served for the plate-warming pan which Rawdon talked about. If Messrs.", "그리고 영국에 있는 명예로운 터프토 부인에게는 어머니에게서 물려받은 낡은 물건이 하나 있었는데, 로던이 말하던 그 탕파 노릇이나 할 만한 것이었다."], ["Howell and James were to publish a list of the purchasers of all the trinkets which they sell, how surprised would some families be: and if all these ornaments went to gentlemen's lawful wives and daughters, what a profusion of jewellery there would be exhibited in the genteelest homes of Vanity Fair!", "만약 하월 앤드 제임스 상회가 자기네가 파는 온갖 패물의 구매자 명단을 공표한다면, 몇몇 가문이 얼마나 놀랄 것인가. 그리고 이 모든 장신구가 신사들의 합법적인 아내와 딸에게로 갔다면, 허영의 시장의 가장 점잖은 가정마다 얼마나 휘황한 보석이 진열되어 있을 것인가!"]], [["Every calculation made of these valuables Mrs. Rebecca found, not without a pungent feeling of triumph and self-satisfaction, that should circumstances occur, she might reckon on six or seven hundred pounds at the very least, to begin the world with; and she passed the morning disposing, ordering, looking out, and locking up her properties in the most agreeable manner.", "이 귀중품들을 일일이 계산하고 나서, 레베카 부인은, 짜릿한 승리감과 자족감이 없지 않은 채로, 만약 그런 사태가 닥친다면 새 인생을 시작하는 데 적어도 육칠백 파운드는 셈할 수 있겠다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["Among the notes in Rawdon's pocket-book was a draft for twenty pounds on Osborne's banker.", "그리고 그녀는 더없이 흐뭇하게 자기 재산을 처분하고, 정리하고, 골라내고, 챙겨 넣으며 오전을 보냈다. 로던의 수첩 속 쪽지들 가운데에는 오즈번의 거래 은행 앞으로 발행된 이십 파운드짜리 어음이 한 장 있었다."], ["This made her think about Mrs. Osborne.", "이 때문에 그녀는 오즈번 부인을 떠올렸다."], ["\"I will go and get the draft cashed,\"", "\"가서 이 어음을 현금으로 바꿔야지.\""], ["she said, \"and pay a visit afterwards to poor little Emmy.\"", "그녀는 말했다. \"그러고 나서 가엾은 어린 에미를 한번 찾아가 봐야겠어.\""], ["If this is a novel without a hero, at least let us lay claim to a heroine.", "이것이 영웅 없는 소설이라면, 적어도 여주인공만큼은 있다고 주장하자."], ["No man in the British army which has marched away, not the great Duke himself, could be more cool or collected in the presence of doubts and difficulties, than the indomitable little aide-de-camp's wife.", "행군해 떠난 영국군 가운데 그 누구도, 그 위대한 공작 본인조차도, 의심과 곤경 앞에서 이 불굴의 작은 부관 아내보다 더 냉정하고 침착할 수는 없었을 것이다."]], [["And there was another of our acquaintances who was also to be left behind, a non-combatant, and whose emotions and behaviour we have therefore a right to know. This was our friend the ex-collector of Boggley Wollah, whose rest was broken, like other people's, by the sounding of the bugles in the early morning. Being a great sleeper, and fond of his bed, it is possible he would have snoozed on until his usual hour of rising in the forenoon, in spite of all the drums, bugles, and bagpipes in the British army, but for an interruption, which did not come from George Osborne, who shared Jos's quarters with him, and was as usual occupied too much with his own affairs or with grief at parting with his wife, to think of taking leave of his slumbering brother-in-law--it was not George, we say, who interposed between Jos Sedley and sleep, but Captain Dobbin, who came and roused him up, insisting on shaking hands with him before his departure.", "그리고 뒤에 남겨지게 된, 비전투원인 우리 지인이 또 한 사람 있었으니, 그러므로 우리는 그의 감정과 행동을 알 권리가 있다. 바로 보글리 월라의 전직 징세관인 우리 벗이었으니, 그 역시 다른 이들과 마찬가지로 이른 아침 나팔 소리에 휴식이 깨졌다. 잠이 많고 침대를 좋아하는지라, 영국군의 그 모든 북과 나팔과 백파이프에도 아랑곳없이, 평소 늦은 오전에 일어나는 시각까지 계속 졸고 있었을지도 모른다. 한 가지 방해만 없었더라면 말이다. 그런데 그 방해는 조스와 숙소를 함께 쓰던 조지 오즈번에게서 온 것이 아니었으니, 그는 늘 그렇듯 제 일에 너무 골몰해 있거나 아내와 헤어지는 슬픔에 잠겨, 잠든 처남에게 작별을 고할 생각 따위는 하지 못했다. 조스 세들리와 잠 사이에 끼어든 이는, 그러니까 조지가 아니라 도빈 대위였다. 그가 와서 그를 깨우고는, 떠나기 전에 기어이 악수를 하겠다고 고집을 부렸던 것이다."]], [["\"Very kind of you,\"", "\"참 친절하시구려.\""], ["said Jos, yawning, and wishing the Captain at the deuce.", "조스가 하품을 하며 말했는데, 속으로는 대위가 지옥에나 가 버렸으면 싶었다."]], [["\"I--I didn't like to go off without saying good-bye, you know,\"", "\"나, 나는 작별 인사도 없이 떠나기가 좀 그래서요, 아시잖소.\""], ["Dobbin said in a very incoherent manner; \"because you know some of us mayn't come back again, and I like to see you all well, and--and that sort of thing, you know.\"", "도빈이 몹시 두서없는 투로 말했다. \"왜냐하면 아시다시피 우리 중 누구는 다시 못 돌아올 수도 있고, 난 여러분 다 잘 지내는 모습을 보고 싶고, 그, 그런 거 말이오, 아시잖소.\""]], [["\"What do you mean?\"", "\"무슨 말씀이신지?\""], ["Jos asked, rubbing his eyes.", "조스가 눈을 비비며 물었다."], ["The Captain did not in the least hear him or look at the stout gentleman in the nightcap, about whom he professed to have such a tender interest.", "대위는 그의 말을 조금도 듣지 못했고, 그토록 애틋한 관심을 보인다던, 잠자리 모자를 쓴 그 뚱뚱한 신사를 쳐다보지도 않았다."], ["The hypocrite was looking and listening with all his might in the direction of George's apartments, striding about the room, upsetting the chairs, beating the tattoo, biting his nails, and showing other signs of great inward emotion.", "이 위선자는 조지의 방 쪽을 향해 온 힘을 다해 보고 듣느라, 방 안을 성큼성큼 거닐고, 의자들을 넘어뜨리고, 손가락으로 북 치듯 두드리고, 손톱을 물어뜯으며, 속으로 큰 동요를 일으키고 있다는 온갖 기색을 내비치고 있었다."]], [["Jos had always had rather a mean opinion of the Captain, and now began to think his courage was somewhat equivocal.", "조스는 늘 대위를 다소 하찮게 여겨 왔는데, 이제 그의 용기마저 어딘가 미심쩍다고 생각하기 시작했다."], ["\"What is it I can do for you, Dobbin?\"", "\"내가 당신을 위해 뭘 해 드리면 되겠소, 도빈?\""], ["he said, in a sarcastic tone.", "그가 빈정대는 투로 말했다."]], [["\"I tell you what you can do,\"", "\"내 말해 드리리다, 무얼 해 주면 되는지.\""], ["the Captain replied, coming up to the bed; \"we march in a quarter of an hour, Sedley, and neither George nor I may ever come back.", "대위가 침대로 다가오며 대답했다. \"우린 십오 분 후에 행군하오, 세들리, 그리고 조지든 나든 영영 못 돌아올지도 모르오."], ["Mind you, you are not to stir from this town until you ascertain how things go.", "명심하시오, 당신은 일이 어찌 돌아가는지 확실히 알기 전까지는 이 도시를 떠나면 안 되오."], ["You are to stay here and watch over your sister, and comfort her, and see that no harm comes to her.", "여기 남아 누이를 지키고, 위로하고, 누이에게 아무 해도 미치지 않도록 살펴야 하오."], ["If anything happens to George, remember she has no one but you in the world to look to.", "조지에게 무슨 일이 생기면, 그녀가 이 세상에서 기댈 사람은 당신밖에 없다는 걸 기억하시오."], ["If it goes wrong with the army, you'll see her safe back to England; and you will promise me on your word that you will never desert her.", "군에 일이 잘못되면, 당신이 누이를 무사히 영국으로 데려가야 하오. 그리고 결코 누이를 저버리지 않겠다고 명예를 걸고 내게 약속해 주시오."], ["I know you won't: as far as money goes, you were always free enough with that.", "당신이 그러지 않으리란 걸 나도 아오. 돈에 관해서라면, 당신은 늘 후한 편이었으니까."], ["Do you want any?", "돈이 좀 필요하오?"], ["I mean, have you enough gold to take you back to England in case of a misfortune?\"", "그러니까, 혹여 변고가 생겼을 때 영국으로 돌아갈 만한 금화는 충분히 가지고 있소?\""]], [["\"Sir,\"", "\"이보시오.\""], ["said Jos, majestically, \"when I want money, I know where to ask for it.", "조스가 위엄 있게 말했다. \"돈이 필요하면, 어디 가서 청해야 할지는 내가 알고 있소."], ["And as for my sister, you needn't tell me how I ought to behave to her.\"", "그리고 내 누이로 말할 것 같으면, 내가 그 애한테 어떻게 처신해야 하는지는 당신이 일러 줄 필요 없소.\""]], [["\"You speak like a man of spirit, Jos,\"", "\"기개 있는 사내답게 말하는구려, 조스.\""], ["the other answered good-naturedly, \"and I am glad that George can leave her in such good hands.", "상대가 너그럽게 대꾸했다. \"조지가 누이를 이렇게 든든한 손에 맡길 수 있게 되어 나도 기쁘오."], ["So I may give him your word of honour, may I, that in case of extremity you will stand by her?\"", "그럼 그에게 당신의 명예를 건 약속을 전해도 되겠소, 그러니까 위급한 경우엔 당신이 누이 곁을 지키리란 걸 말이오?\""]], [["\"Of course, of course,\"", "\"물론이오, 물론이지요.\""], ["answered Mr. Jos, whose generosity in money matters Dobbin estimated quite correctly.", "조스가 대답했는데, 돈 문제에서 그가 보이는 너그러움을 도빈은 아주 정확히 가늠하고 있었다."]], [["\"And you'll see her safe out of Brussels in the event of a defeat?\"", "\"그리고 패전할 경우엔 누이를 무사히 브뤼셀에서 빼내 주겠지요?\""]], [["\"A defeat!", "\"패전이라니!"], ["D---- it, sir, it's impossible.", "빌어먹을, 이보시오, 그건 있을 수 없는 일이오."], ["Don't try and frighten ME,\"", "날 겁주려 들지 마시오.\""], ["the hero cried from his bed; and Dobbin's mind was thus perfectly set at ease now that Jos had spoken out so resolutely respecting his conduct to his sister.", "영웅이 침대에서 외쳤다. 이렇게 조스가 누이에 대한 자신의 처신을 그토록 단호히 밝혀 주자, 도빈의 마음은 그제야 완전히 놓였다."], ["\"At least,\"", "\"적어도,\""], ["thought the Captain, \"there will be a retreat secured for her in case the worst should ensue.\"", "대위는 생각했다. \"최악의 사태가 벌어진다 해도 그녀에게는 피신처 하나는 확보된 셈이군.\""]], [["If Captain Dobbin expected to get any personal comfort and satisfaction from having one more view of Amelia before the regiment marched away, his selfishness was punished just as such odious egotism deserved to be.", "도빈 대위가 연대가 행군해 떠나기 전에 애밀리아를 한 번 더 보아 무슨 개인적인 위안과 만족을 얻으리라 기대했다면, 그의 이기심은 그런 가증스러운 자기중심성이 응당 받아야 할 만큼 그대로 벌을 받았다."], ["The door of Jos's bedroom opened into the sitting-room which was common to the family party, and opposite this door was that of Amelia's chamber.", "조스의 침실 문은 가족 일행이 함께 쓰는 거실로 통했고, 이 문 맞은편에 애밀리아의 방문이 있었다."], ["The bugles had wakened everybody: there was no use in concealment now.", "나팔 소리가 모두를 깨워 놓았으니, 이제 숨길 것도 없었다."], ["George's servant was packing in this room: Osborne coming in and out of the contiguous bedroom, flinging to the man such articles as he thought fit to carry on the campaign.", "조지의 하인은 이 방에서 짐을 꾸리고 있었고, 오즈번은 옆방 침실을 들락거리며 전역에 가져가기에 적당하다 싶은 물건들을 하인에게 던져 주고 있었다."], ["And presently Dobbin had the opportunity which his heart coveted, and he got sight of Amelia's face once more.", "이윽고 도빈은 제 마음이 그토록 갈망하던 기회를 얻어, 애밀리아의 얼굴을 다시 한번 보게 되었다."], ["But what a face it was!", "그러나 그 얼굴이라니!"], ["So white, so wild and despair-stricken, that the remembrance of it haunted him afterwards like a crime, and the sight smote him with inexpressible pangs of longing and pity.", "어찌나 창백하고, 어찌나 넋이 나가고 절망에 짓눌렸던지, 그 모습의 기억이 훗날 죄악처럼 그를 따라다녔고, 그 광경은 이루 말할 수 없는 갈망과 연민의 고통으로 그를 후려쳤다."]], [["She was wrapped in a white morning dress, her hair falling on her shoulders, and her large eyes fixed and without light.", "그녀는 흰 실내복으로 몸을 감싼 채, 머리칼은 어깨 위로 흘러내렸고, 커다란 두 눈은 빛을 잃고 한곳에 박혀 있었다."], ["By way of helping on the preparations for the departure, and showing that she too could be useful at a moment so critical, this poor soul had taken up a sash of George's from the drawers whereon it lay, and followed him to and fro with the sash in her hand, looking on mutely as his packing proceeded.", "출발 준비를 거들겠다고, 또 이토록 위급한 순간에 자신도 쓸모가 있음을 보이려고, 이 가엾은 사람은 서랍장 위에 놓인 조지의 장식띠를 집어 들고, 그 띠를 손에 쥔 채 그를 이리저리 따라다니며, 그의 짐 꾸리기가 진행되는 것을 말없이 바라보고 있었다."], ["She came out and stood, leaning at the wall, holding this sash against her bosom, from which the heavy net of crimson dropped like a large stain of blood.", "그녀는 방에서 나와 벽에 기대어 서서, 그 띠를 가슴에 꼭 댄 채 있었는데, 그 가슴에서 진홍빛 그물 장식이 마치 큼직한 핏자국처럼 늘어져 내렸다."], ["Our gentle-hearted Captain felt a guilty shock as he looked at her.", "마음씨 고운 우리 대위는 그녀를 바라보며 죄지은 듯한 충격을 느꼈다."], ["\"Good God,\"", "\"맙소사,\""], ["thought he, \"and is it grief like this I dared to pry into?\"", "그는 생각했다. \"내가 감히 엿보려 한 것이 바로 이런 슬픔이었단 말인가?\""], ["And there was no help: no means to soothe and comfort this helpless, speechless misery.", "그리고 어찌해 볼 도리가 없었다. 이 무력하고 말 없는 비탄을 달래고 위로할 길이 없었다."], ["He stood for a moment and looked at her, powerless and torn with pity, as a parent regards an infant in pain.", "그는 잠시 서서 그녀를 바라보았으니, 아파하는 어린아이를 바라보는 어버이처럼, 아무것도 할 수 없는 채 연민에 가슴이 찢어졌다."]], [["At last, George took Emmy's hand, and led her back into the bedroom, from whence he came out alone.", "마침내 조지가 에미의 손을 잡고 그녀를 침실로 도로 데려갔다가, 혼자서 나왔다."], ["The parting had taken place in that moment, and he was gone.", "작별은 그 순간에 이루어졌고, 그는 떠났다."]], [["\"Thank Heaven that is over,\"", "\"이제 끝났으니 천만다행이군.\""], ["George thought, bounding down the stair, his sword under his arm, as he ran swiftly to the alarm ground, where the regiment was mustered, and whither trooped men and officers hurrying from their billets; his pulse was throbbing and his cheeks flushed: the great game of war was going to be played, and he one of the players.", "조지는 칼을 옆구리에 낀 채 계단을 뛰어 내려가며 생각했다. 그는 연대가 집결한 비상소집장으로 재빨리 달려갔는데, 그리로 병사와 장교들이 제 숙영지에서 서둘러 떼 지어 몰려들고 있었다."], ["What a fierce excitement of doubt, hope, and pleasure!", "그의 맥박은 고동치고 두 뺨은 상기되었다."], ["What tremendous hazards of loss or gain!", "전쟁이라는 거대한 승부가 막 펼쳐지려는 참이었고, 그는 그 노름꾼 가운데 하나였다."], ["What were all the games of chance he had ever played compared to this one?", "의심과 희망과 쾌감의 그 얼마나 격렬한 흥분인가! 잃거나 따는 그 얼마나 어마어마한 도박인가!"], ["Into all contests requiring athletic skill and courage, the young man, from his boyhood upwards, had flung himself with all his might.", "그가 여태껏 해 본 그 어떤 도박이 이 한판에 견줄 수 있겠는가? 운동 솜씨와 용기를 요하는 온갖 겨룸에, 이 젊은이는 소년 시절부터 줄곧 온 힘을 다해 뛰어들곤 했다."], ["The champion of his school and his regiment, the bravos of his companions had followed him everywhere; from the boys'", "학교에서도 연대에서도 으뜸가는 선수였고, 동료들의 갈채가 어디서나 그를 따라다녔다."], ["cricket-match to the garrison-races, he had won a hundred of triumphs; and wherever he went women and men had admired and envied him.", "소년들의 크리켓 시합에서 수비대 경마에 이르기까지 그는 백 번의 승리를 거두었고, 그가 가는 곳마다 여자들도 남자들도 그를 흠모하고 부러워했다."], ["What qualities are there for which a man gets so speedy a return of applause, as those of bodily superiority, activity, and valour?", "사내가 그토록 신속하게 갈채로 보답받는 자질로, 육체의 우월함과 민첩함과 용맹만 한 것이 또 어디 있겠는가?"], ["Time out of mind strength and courage have been the theme of bards and romances; and from the story of Troy down to to-day, poetry has always chosen a soldier for a hero.", "아득한 옛날부터 힘과 용기는 음유시인과 로맨스의 주제였다. 그리고 트로이 이야기에서 오늘날에 이르기까지, 시는 언제나 군인을 영웅으로 골라 왔다."], ["I wonder is it because men are cowards in heart that they admire bravery so much, and place military valour so far beyond every other quality for reward and worship?", "사내들이 용맹을 그토록 흠모하고, 군사적 용맹을 다른 모든 자질보다 그토록 높이 보상하고 떠받드는 것은, 혹시 그들이 마음속으로는 겁쟁이이기 때문은 아닐까 하는 생각이 든다."]], [["So, at the sound of that stirring call to battle, George jumped away from the gentle arms in which he had been dallying; not without a feeling of shame (although his wife's hold on him had been but feeble), that he should have been detained there so long.", "그리하여 전투를 알리는 그 가슴 뛰는 나팔 소리에, 조지는 자신이 어르며 안겨 있던 그 부드러운 두 팔에서 훌쩍 떨쳐 일어났다. 그토록 오래 그곳에 붙들려 있었다는 데에 일말의 수치심이 없지 않은 채로 말이다(아내가 그를 붙든 힘이라곤 미약하기 그지없었는데도)."], ["The same feeling of eagerness and excitement was amongst all those friends of his of whom we have had occasional glimpses, from the stout senior Major, who led the regiment into action, to little Stubble, the Ensign, who was to bear its colours on that day.", "우리가 이따금 언뜻언뜻 보아 온 그의 벗들 모두에게도 똑같은 열의와 흥분의 감정이 감돌았으니, 연대를 이끌고 전투에 나서는 노련한 선임 소령부터, 그날 군기를 받들게 된 어린 소위 스터블에 이르기까지 그러했다."]], [["The sun was just rising as the march began--it was a gallant sight--the band led the column, playing the regimental march--then came the Major in command, riding upon Pyramus, his stout charger--then marched the grenadiers, their Captain at their head; in the centre were the colours, borne by the senior and junior Ensigns--then George came marching at the head of his company. He looked up, and smiled at Amelia, and passed on; and even the sound of the music died away.", "행군이 시작될 무렵 해가 막 떠오르고 있었다. 늠름한 광경이었다. 군악대가 종대의 선두에 서서 연대 행진곡을 연주했다. 이어 지휘를 맡은 소령이 자신의 듬직한 군마 피라무스에 올라타 나아갔다. 그다음 척탄병들이 그 대위를 앞세우고 행군했고, 중앙에는 선임 소위와 후임 소위가 받든 군기가 있었다. 그러고는 조지가 자기 중대의 선두에 서서 행군해 왔다. 그는 위를 올려다보며 애밀리아에게 미소 지어 보이고는 지나갔다. 그리고 음악 소리마저 이윽고 잦아들었다."]], [["In Which Jos Sedley Takes Care of His Sister\nThus all the superior officers being summoned on duty elsewhere, Jos Sedley was left in command of the little colony at Brussels, with Amelia invalided, Isidor, his Belgian servant, and the bonne, who was maid-of-all-work for the establishment, as a garrison under him.", "그리하여 상급 장교들이 모두 다른 곳으로 차출되어 떠나고 나니, 조스 세들리가 브뤼셀에 남은 이 작은 식민지의 지휘를 맡게 된 셈이었다. 병자가 된 애밀리아, 그의 벨기에인 하인 이지도르, 그리고 이 집안의 온갖 잡일을 도맡은 하녀 본느가 그의 휘하 수비대였다."], ["Though he was disturbed in spirit, and his rest destroyed by Dobbin's interruption and the occurrences of the morning, Jos nevertheless remained for many hours in bed, wakeful and rolling about there until his usual hour of rising had arrived.", "도빈의 방해와 그날 아침의 소동으로 마음이 어수선해지고 단잠을 망쳐 버렸음에도, 조스는 그럼에도 평소 일어나는 시각이 될 때까지 여러 시간을 잠 못 이룬 채 자리에 누워 이리저리 뒤척이며 침대에 머물러 있었다."], ["The sun was high in the heavens, and our gallant friends of the --th miles on their march, before the civilian appeared in his flowered dressing-gown at breakfast.", "해가 하늘 높이 솟아오르고, 우리의 용맹한 ××연대 친구들이 행군길을 몇 마일이나 나아간 뒤에야, 이 민간인 신사는 꽃무늬 실내복 차림으로 아침 식사 자리에 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["About George's absence, his brother-in-law was very easy in mind.", "조지가 자리에 없는 것에 대해서는 그 처남도 마음이 아주 편했다."], ["Perhaps Jos was rather pleased in his heart that Osborne was gone, for during George's presence, the other had played but a very secondary part in the household, and Osborne did not scruple to show his contempt for the stout civilian.", "어쩌면 조스는 속으로 오스본이 떠난 것이 오히려 흐뭇했을지도 모른다. 조지가 있는 동안 그는 집안에서 한낱 들러리 역할밖에 못 했고, 오스본은 이 뚱뚱한 민간인에 대한 경멸을 거리낌 없이 드러내곤 했기 때문이다."], ["But Emmy had always been good and attentive to him.", "그러나 에미만은 늘 그에게 다정하고 살뜰했다."], ["It was she who ministered to his comforts, who superintended the dishes that he liked, who walked or rode with him (as she had many, too many, opportunities of doing, for where was George?) and who interposed her sweet face between his anger and her husband's scorn.", "그의 편의를 보살피고, 그가 좋아하는 음식을 챙기고, 그와 함께 산책하거나 마차를 타 주고(조지는 대체 어디 있었던지, 그녀에게는 그럴 기회가 많고도 너무 많았다), 그의 노여움과 남편의 비웃음 사이에 자신의 고운 얼굴을 들이밀어 막아 준 것도 그녀였다."], ["Many timid remonstrances had she uttered to George in behalf of her brother, but the former in his trenchant way cut these entreaties short.", "오빠를 위해 그녀는 몇 번이나 조심스레 조지에게 항변해 보았으나, 조지는 그 직설적인 투로 그 애원을 짧게 잘라 버리곤 했다."], ["\"I'm an honest man,\"", "\"난 정직한 사람이야.\""], ["he said, \"and if I have a feeling I show it, as an honest man will.", "그가 말했다. \"감정이 있으면, 정직한 사람답게 그걸 드러내는 거지."], ["How the deuce, my dear, would you have me behave respectfully to such a fool as your brother?\"", "여보, 대체 자네 오빠 같은 바보한테 나더러 어떻게 깍듯이 대하란 말이오?\""], ["So Jos was pleased with George's absence.", "그러니 조스는 조지가 없는 게 흐뭇했던 것이다."], ["His plain hat, and gloves on a sideboard, and the idea that the owner was away, caused Jos I don't know what secret thrill of pleasure. \"HE won't be troubling me this morning,\"", "찬장 위에 놓인 그 수수한 모자와 장갑, 그리고 그 주인이 떠나고 없다는 생각이 조스에게 어떤 은밀한 기쁨의 전율 같은 것을 일으켰다."], ["Jos thought, \"with his dandified airs and his impudence.\"", "\"오늘 아침엔 저 친구가 그 멋쟁이 티와 무례함으로 날 성가시게 굴진 않겠군.\" 조스는 그렇게 생각했다."]], [["\"Put the Captain's hat into the ante-room,\"", "\"대위의 모자를 대기실로 갖다 놓아라.\""], ["he said to Isidor, the servant.", "그가 하인 이지도르에게 말했다."]], [["\"Perhaps he won't want it again,\"", "\"어쩌면 다시는 필요 없으실지도 모르지요.\""], ["replied the lackey, looking knowingly at his master.", "하인이 안다는 듯한 눈빛으로 주인을 바라보며 대답했다."], ["He hated George too, whose insolence towards him was quite of the English sort.", "그 역시 조지를 미워했으니, 자기에게 대하는 조지의 거만함이 영락없이 영국식이었던 것이다."]], [["\"And ask if Madame is coming to breakfast,\" Mr.", "\"그리고 마님께서 아침 식사 하러 나오실지 여쭤봐라.\""], ["Sedley said with great majesty, ashamed to enter with a servant upon the subject of his dislike for George.", "세들리 씨가 자못 위엄을 부리며 말했다. 하인을 상대로 조지에 대한 자신의 반감을 입에 올리는 게 부끄러웠던 것이다."], ["The truth is, he had abused his brother to the valet a score of times before.", "사실인즉 그는 이 하인에게 처남 욕을 이미 스무 번도 더 했던 터였다."]], [["Alas!", "아아!"], ["Madame could not come to breakfast, and cut the tartines that Mr.", "마님은 아침 식사 자리에 나와 조스 씨가 좋아하는 타르틴을 썰어 줄 수가 없었다. 마님은 너무도 심하게 앓고 계셨고, 남편이 떠난 뒤로 줄곧 끔찍한 지경에 빠져 있다고 그녀의 본느가 전했다."], ["Jos liked.", "조스는 그녀에게 차를 큰 잔으로 가득 따라 보내는 것으로 동정을 표했다."], ["Madame was a great deal too ill, and had been in a frightful state ever since her husband's departure, so her bonne said.", "그것이 그가 다정함을 드러내는 방식이었다. 게다가 그는 거기서 한술 더 떴다."], ["Jos showed his sympathy by pouring her out a large cup of tea It was his way of exhibiting kindness: and he improved on this; he not only sent her breakfast, but he bethought him what delicacies she would most like for dinner.", "아침 식사를 보냈을 뿐 아니라, 저녁상에는 그녀가 무엇을 가장 좋아할지까지 헤아렸던 것이다."]], [["Isidor, the valet, had looked on very sulkily, while Osborne's servant was disposing of his master's baggage previous to the Captain's departure: for in the first place he hated Mr.", "하인 이지도르는, 대위가 떠나기에 앞서 오스본의 하인이 주인의 짐을 챙기는 동안 몹시 시무룩한 얼굴로 지켜보았다."], ["Osborne, whose conduct to him, and to all inferiors, was generally overbearing (nor does the continental domestic like to be treated with insolence as our own better-tempered servants do), and secondly, he was angry that so many valuables should be removed from under his hands, to fall into other people's possession when the English discomfiture should arrive.", "첫째로 그는 오스본 씨를 미워했는데, 자기를 비롯한 모든 아랫사람을 대하는 오스본의 태도가 대체로 거만했기 때문이다(게다가 대륙의 하인이란, 성정이 더 너그러운 우리네 하인들처럼 무례한 대접을 순순히 견디지 못하는 법이다). 둘째로, 그렇게나 많은 값진 물건들이 자기 손아귀에서 빠져나가, 영국군이 패주하는 날이 오면 마땅히 자기 차지가 되었어야 할 것들이 남의 손에 들어가 버리는 것이 분했다."], ["Of this defeat he and a vast number of other persons in Brussels and Belgium did not make the slightest doubt.", "이 패배에 관해서라면 그도, 또 브뤼셀과 벨기에의 수많은 사람들도 추호의 의심조차 품지 않았다."], ["The almost universal belief was, that the Emperor would divide the Prussian and English armies, annihilate one after the other, and march into Brussels before three days were over: when all the movables of his present masters, who would be killed, or fugitives, or prisoners, would lawfully become the property of Monsieur Isidor.", "거의 모두가 믿는 바로는, 황제가 프로이센군과 영국군을 갈라놓고 차례로 하나씩 섬멸한 뒤 사흘이 채 지나기 전에 브뤼셀로 진군해 들어오리라는 것이었다. 그때가 되면 죽거나 도망치거나 포로가 될 지금 주인들의 동산은 죄다 적법하게 이지도르 씨의 소유가 될 터였다."]], [["As he helped Jos through his toilsome and complicated daily toilette, this faithful servant would calculate what he should do with the very articles with which he was decorating his master's person.", "조스가 고되고 복잡한 매일의 몸치장을 마치도록 거드는 동안, 이 충직한 하인은 자기가 지금 주인의 몸을 꾸미는 데 쓰고 있는 바로 그 물건들을 가지고 무엇을 할지 궁리하곤 했다."], ["He would make a present of the silver essence-bottles and toilet knicknacks to a young lady of whom he was fond; and keep the English cutlery and the large ruby pin for himself.", "은제 향수병과 화장 도구들은 자기가 마음에 둔 어느 아가씨에게 선물할 것이요, 영국제 면도칼과 큼직한 루비 핀은 자기가 챙겨 둘 작정이었다."], ["It would look very smart upon one of the fine frilled shirts, which, with the gold-laced cap and the frogged frock coat, that might easily be cut down to suit his shape, and the Captain's gold-headed cane, and the great double ring with the rubies, which he would have made into a pair of beautiful earrings, he calculated would make a perfect Adonis of himself, and render Mademoiselle Reine an easy prey.", "멋들어진 주름 셔츠 가운데 하나에 그 핀을 꽂으면 무척이나 근사해 보일 테고, 거기에 금몰을 두른 모자와 자기 몸에 맞게 줄여 입기 쉬운 장식 단추 달린 프록코트, 대위의 금장식 지팡이, 그리고 한 쌍의 아름다운 귀고리로 만들어 둘 작정인 루비 박힌 커다란 겹반지까지 갖추면, 그야말로 자기 자신이 완벽한 아도니스가 되어 렌 양쯤은 손쉬운 먹잇감이 되리라 그는 계산했다. \"저 소매 단추가 내게 얼마나 잘 어울릴까!\""], ["\"How those sleeve-buttons will suit me!\"", "그는 세들리 씨의 살지고 통통한 손목에 단추 한 쌍을 채우면서 생각했다. \"소매 단추가 정말 갖고 싶어."], ["thought he, as he fixed a pair on the fat pudgy wrists of Mr. Sedley.", "그리고 옆방에 있는 놋쇠 박차 달린 대위의 부츠, 코르블뢰!"], ["\"I long for sleeve-buttons; and the Captain's boots with brass spurs, in the next room, corbleu!", "알레 베르트 거리에서 얼마나 멋들어진 효과를 낼까!\" 그리하여 이지도르 씨가 육신의 손가락으로는 주인의 코를 붙잡고 조스의 얼굴 아랫부분을 면도하는 동안, 그의 상상은 장식 단추 달린 코트에 레이스까지 차려입고 렌 양과 짝을 지어 그린 애비뉴를 거닐고 있었다."], ["what an effect they will make in the Allee Verte!\"", "그는 마음속으로 운하 둑을 어슬렁거리며, 시원한 나무 그늘 아래 천천히 미끄러져 가는 거룻배들을 살펴보거나, 라켄으로 가는 길가 맥줏집 벤치에 앉아 파로 한 잔으로 목을 축이고 있었던 것이다."], ["So while Monsieur Isidor with bodily fingers was holding on to his master's nose, and shaving the lower part of Jos's face, his imagination was rambling along the Green Avenue, dressed out in a frogged coat and lace, and in company with Mademoiselle Reine; he was loitering in spirit on the banks, and examining the barges sailing slowly under the cool shadows of the trees by the canal, or refreshing himself with a mug of Faro at the bench of a beer-house on the road to Laeken.", "* 코르블뢰: \"맙소사\"에 해당하는 프랑스어 감탄사. 파로: 벨기에산 맥주의 일종."]], [["But Mr. Joseph Sedley, luckily for his own peace, no more knew what was passing in his domestic's mind than the respected reader, and I suspect what John or Mary, whose wages we pay, think of ourselves.", "그러나 조지프 세들리 씨는, 다행스럽게도 자신의 마음의 평화를 위해서는, 자기 하인의 마음속에 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 전혀 알지 못했다."], ["What our servants think of us!--Did we know what our intimates and dear relations thought of us, we should live in a world that we should be glad to quit, and in a frame of mind and a constant terror, that would be perfectly unbearable.", "마치 존경하는 독자 여러분도 모르고, 또 우리가 품삯을 주는 존이나 메리가 우리를 어떻게 여기는지 우리도 알지 못하는 것이나 매한가지였다. 우리 하인들이 우리를 어떻게 생각하는지!"], ["So Jos's man was marking his victim down, as you see one of Mr.", "만약 우리가 가까운 벗과 정다운 친척들이 우리를 어떻게 여기는지 알게 된다면, 우리는 차라리 떠나고 싶은 세상에서, 도저히 견딜 수 없는 마음가짐과 끊임없는 공포 속에서 살아가게 될 것이다."], ["Paynter's assistants in Leadenhall Street ornament an unconscious turtle with a placard on which is written, \"Soup to-morrow.\"", "그리하여 조스의 하인은 자기 희생양을 점찍어 두고 있었던 것이다. 마치 리든홀 거리에서 페인터 씨의 점원이 영문도 모르는 거북에게 \"내일 수프\"라고 적힌 푯말을 걸어 장식해 두는 것을 보듯이 말이다."]], [["Amelia's attendant was much less selfishly disposed.", "애밀리아의 시중을 드는 이는 훨씬 덜 이기적인 성품이었다."], ["Few dependents could come near that kind and gentle creature without paying their usual tribute of loyalty and affection to her sweet and affectionate nature.", "그 다정하고 온화한 사람 곁에 다가오는 아랫사람이라면, 그 사랑스럽고 정 많은 천성에 으레 바치게 되는 충성과 애정을 바치지 않고는 못 배기는 법이었다."], ["And it is a fact that Pauline, the cook, consoled her mistress more than anybody whom she saw on this wretched morning; for when she found how Amelia remained for hours, silent, motionless, and haggard, by the windows in which she had placed herself to watch the last bayonets of the column as it marched away, the honest girl took the lady's hand, and said, Tenez, Madame, est-ce qu'il n'est pas aussi a l'armee, mon homme a moi?", "그리고 사실인즉, 요리사 폴린은 이 비참한 아침에 애밀리아가 만난 그 누구보다도 안주인을 더 많이 위로해 주었다. 행군해 나가는 종대의 마지막 총검이라도 지켜보려고 창가에 자리 잡고 앉은 애밀리아가 몇 시간이고 말없이, 미동도 없이, 초췌한 얼굴로 머물러 있는 것을 보고, 이 정직한 처녀는 마님의 손을 잡고 말했다."], ["with which she burst into tears, and Amelia falling into her arms, did likewise, and so each pitied and soothed the other.", "\"있잖아요, 마님, 제 남자도 군대에 가 있는 것 아닌가요?\" 그러고는 울음을 터뜨렸고, 애밀리아도 그녀의 품에 안겨 똑같이 울음을 쏟으니, 둘은 서로를 가엾이 여기며 위로했던 것이다."]], [["Several times during the forenoon Mr.", "오전 동안 여러 차례, 조스 씨의 하인 이지도르는 거처에서 시내로 나갔다."], ["Jos's Isidor went from his lodgings into the town, and to the gates of the hotels and lodging-houses round about the Parc, where the English were congregated, and there mingled with other valets, couriers, and lackeys, gathered such news as was abroad, and brought back bulletins for his master's information.", "영국 사람들이 모여 있는 파르크 주변의 호텔과 하숙집 문간으로 가서, 다른 하인들과 사자(使者)들과 종복들 틈에 끼어 떠도는 소식을 그러모아, 주인에게 알려 줄 소식 보따리를 들고 돌아왔다."], ["Almost all these gentlemen were in heart partisans of the Emperor, and had their opinions about the speedy end of the campaign.", "이들 신사 양반들은 거의 모두가 속으로는 황제의 지지자였고, 이 전역(戰役)이 곧 끝나리라는 것에 저마다 의견이 있었다."], ["The Emperor's proclamation from Avesnes had been distributed everywhere plentifully in Brussels.", "황제가 아베슨에서 내린 포고문은 브뤼셀 곳곳에 넘쳐나게 뿌려져 있었다."], ["\"Soldiers!\"", "\"병사들이여!\""], ["it said, \"this is the anniversary of Marengo and Friedland, by which the destinies of Europe were twice decided.", "포고문은 이렇게 말했다. \"오늘은 유럽의 운명을 두 번이나 결정지은 마렝고와 프리틀란트의 기념일이다."], ["Then, as after Austerlitz, as after Wagram, we were too generous.", "그때, 아우스터리츠 이후나 바그람 이후처럼, 우리는 너무 관대했다."], ["We believed in the oaths and promises of princes whom we suffered to remain upon their thrones.", "우리는 그 왕좌에 그대로 머물도록 허락한 군주들의 맹세와 약속을 믿었다."], ["Let us march once more to meet them.", "다시 한번 진군하여 그들을 맞이하자."], ["We and they, are we not still the same men?", "우리와 그들이, 우리는 여전히 같은 사람들이 아니던가?"], ["Soldiers!", "병사들이여!"], ["these same Prussians who are so arrogant to-day, were three to one against you at Jena, and six to one at Montmirail.", "오늘날 저토록 오만한 바로 그 프로이센군은 예나에서 너희에게 삼 대 일로 맞섰고, 몽미라유에서는 육 대 일이었다."], ["Those among you who were prisoners in England can tell their comrades what frightful torments they suffered on board the English hulks.", "너희 가운데 영국에 포로로 잡혔던 자들은 영국 감옥선 위에서 얼마나 끔찍한 고초를 겪었는지 동료들에게 말해 줄 수 있으리라. 미친 자들이여!"], ["Madmen! a moment of prosperity has blinded them, and if they enter into France it will be to find a grave there!\"", "한순간의 번영이 그들의 눈을 멀게 했으니, 그들이 프랑스로 쳐들어온다면 그것은 거기서 무덤을 찾기 위함이리라!\""], ["But the partisans of the French prophesied a more speedy extermination of the Emperor's enemies than this; and it was agreed on all hands that Prussians and British would never return except as prisoners in the rear of the conquering army.", "그러나 프랑스 지지자들은 이보다도 더 빠른 황제의 적들의 섬멸을 예언했고, 프로이센군과 영국군은 승리한 군대의 후미에 포로로 끌려오지 않는 한 결코 돌아오지 못하리라는 데에 모두 입을 모았다."]], [["These opinions in the course of the day were brought to operate upon Mr. Sedley.", "이 의견들이 그날이 가는 동안 세들리 씨에게도 작용을 미치게 되었다."], ["He was told that the Duke of Wellington had gone to try and rally his army, the advance of which had been utterly crushed the night before.", "그는 웰링턴 공작이 전날 밤 완전히 박살 난 자기 군대의 선봉을 다시 추스르려고 떠났다는 말을 들었다."]], [["\"Crushed, psha!\"", "\"박살이라니, 흥!\""], ["said Jos, whose heart was pretty stout at breakfast-time.", "조스가 말했다. 아침 식사 때라 그의 배짱은 제법 두둑했다."], ["\"The Duke has gone to beat the Emperor as he has beaten all his generals before.\"", "\"공작은 황제를 무찌르러 가신 거야. 예전에 황제의 장군들을 죄다 무찌른 것처럼 말이지.\""]], [["\"His papers are burned, his effects are removed, and his quarters are being got ready for the Duke of Dalmatia,\"", "\"황제의 서류는 불태워졌고, 소지품은 옮겨졌으며, 그 거처는 달마티아 공작을 맞을 채비를 하고 있답니다.\""], ["Jos's informant replied.", "조스에게 소식을 전하는 자가 대답했다."], ["\"I had it from his own maitre d'hotel.", "\"그분의 집사한테서 직접 들은 얘기입니다."], ["Milor Duc de Richemont's people are packing up everything.", "리슈몽 공작 댁 사람들은 죄다 짐을 싸고 있어요."], ["His Grace has fled already, and the Duchess is only waiting to see the plate packed to join the King of France at Ostend.\"", "공작 각하는 이미 달아나셨고, 공작부인은 식기 챙기는 것만 보고 오스텐더에서 프랑스 왕과 합류하려고 기다리는 중이고요.\""]], [["\"The King of France is at Ghent, fellow,\"", "\"프랑스 왕은 헨트에 계신다, 이 사람아.\""], ["replied Jos, affecting incredulity.", "조스가 못 믿겠다는 시늉을 하며 대답했다."]], [["\"He fled last night to Bruges, and embarks today from Ostend.", "\"어젯밤 브뤼주로 달아나셔서, 오늘 오스텐더에서 배를 타십니다."], ["The Duc de Berri is taken prisoner.", "베리 공작은 포로로 잡혔고요."], ["Those who wish to be safe had better go soon, for the dykes will be opened to-morrow, and who can fly when the whole country is under water?\"", "무사하고 싶은 자들은 어서 떠나는 게 좋을 겁니다. 내일이면 제방을 터뜨릴 테니, 온 나라가 물에 잠기면 누가 달아날 수 있겠습니까?\""]], [["\"Nonsense, sir, we are three to one, sir, against any force Boney can bring into the field,\"", "\"허튼소리, 이 사람아. 우리는 보니가 전장에 끌어낼 수 있는 어떤 병력에도 삼 대 일로 우세하단 말이다.\""], ["Mr. Sedley objected; \"the Austrians and the Russians are on their march.", "세들리 씨가 반박했다."], ["He must, he shall be crushed,\"", "\"오스트리아군과 러시아군이 진군해 오고 있어."], ["Jos said, slapping his hand on the table.", "황제는 박살 날 수밖에 없고, 박살 나고 말 거야.\" 조스가 손바닥으로 탁자를 내리치며 말했다."]], [["\"The Prussians were three to one at Jena, and he took their army and kingdom in a week.", "\"프로이센군은 예나에서 삼 대 일이었지만, 황제는 일주일 만에 그들의 군대와 왕국을 차지했습니다."], ["They were six to one at Montmirail, and he scattered them like sheep.", "몽미라유에서는 육 대 일이었지만, 황제는 그들을 양 떼처럼 흩어 버렸지요."], ["The Austrian army is coming, but with the Empress and the King of Rome at its head; and the Russians, bah!", "오스트리아 군대가 오고는 있지만, 황후와 로마 왕을 선두에 앞세우고 옵니다. 러시아군은, 흥!"], ["the Russians will withdraw.", "러시아군은 물러갈 겁니다."], ["No quarter is to be given to the English, on account of their cruelty to our braves on board the infamous pontoons.", "영국군에게는 자비를 베풀지 않을 거예요. 그 악명 높은 부교(浮橋) 위에서 우리 용사들에게 저지른 잔학 행위 때문이지요."], ["Look here, here it is in black and white.", "여기 보십시오, 흑백으로 똑똑히 적혀 있잖습니까."], ["Here's the proclamation of his Majesty the Emperor and King,\"", "여기 황제 폐하이자 왕이신 분의 포고문이 있어요.\""], ["said the now declared partisan of Napoleon, and taking the document from his pocket, Isidor sternly thrust it into his master's face, and already looked upon the frogged coat and valuables as his own spoil.", "이제 노골적으로 나폴레옹의 지지자가 된 그가 말했다. 그러고는 주머니에서 그 문서를 꺼내 이지도르는 그것을 주인의 얼굴 앞에 매섭게 들이밀었고, 이미 그 장식 단추 코트며 값진 물건들을 제 전리품으로 여기는 눈치였다."]], [["Jos was, if not seriously alarmed as yet, at least considerably disturbed in mind.", "조스는, 아직 심각하게 겁먹은 것은 아닐지언정, 적어도 마음이 상당히 어지러워졌다."], ["\"Give me my coat and cap, sir,\"", "\"내 코트와 모자를 다오.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, \"and follow me.", "\"그리고 나를 따라오너라."], ["I will go myself and learn the truth of these reports.\"", "내가 직접 가서 이 소문들의 진위를 알아보겠다.\""], ["Isidor was furious as Jos put on the braided frock.", "조스가 장식 단추 달린 프록코트를 걸치자 이지도르는 노발대발했다."], ["\"Milor had better not wear that military coat,\"", "\"나리, 그 군복 같은 코트는 입지 않으시는 게 좋겠습니다.\""], ["said he; \"the Frenchmen have sworn not to give quarter to a single British soldier.\"", "그가 말했다. \"프랑스 놈들은 영국 군인이라면 단 한 명도 살려 두지 않겠다고 맹세했으니까요.\""]], [["\"Silence, sirrah!\"", "\"닥쳐라, 이놈!\""], ["said Jos, with a resolute countenance still, and thrust his arm into the sleeve with indomitable resolution, in the performance of which heroic act he was found by Mrs.", "조스가 여전히 단호한 표정으로 말하고는, 굽힐 줄 모르는 결의로 소매에 팔을 쑤셔 넣었다."], ["Rawdon Crawley, who at this juncture came up to visit Amelia, and entered without ringing at the antechamber door.", "바로 이 영웅적인 동작을 행하는 중에 그는 로던 크롤리 부인에게 발각되고 말았으니, 그녀는 마침 이때 애밀리아를 찾아왔다가 대기실 문에서 종도 울리지 않고 들어선 참이었다."]], [["Rebecca was dressed very neatly and smartly, as usual: her quiet sleep after Rawdon's departure had refreshed her, and her pink smiling cheeks were quite pleasant to look at, in a town and on a day when everybody else's countenance wore the appearance of the deepest anxiety and gloom.", "레베카는 여느 때처럼 아주 단정하고 맵시 있게 차려입은 모습이었다. 로던이 떠난 뒤의 차분한 잠이 그녀를 산뜻하게 회복시켜 주었고, 분홍빛으로 미소 짓는 그녀의 두 볼은, 다른 사람들은 죄다 더없이 깊은 근심과 우수가 어린 얼굴을 하고 있는 이 도시, 이런 날에 보기에 무척 흐뭇한 것이었다."], ["She laughed at the attitude in which Jos was discovered, and the struggles and convulsions with which the stout gentleman thrust himself into the braided coat.", "그녀는 조스가 발각된 그 자세하며, 이 뚱뚱한 신사가 장식 단추 코트에 몸을 쑤셔 넣느라 벌이는 안간힘과 발버둥을 보고 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["\"Are you preparing to join the army, Mr.", "\"군대에 합류하실 준비를 하시는 건가요, 조지프 씨?\""], ["Joseph?\" she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"Is there to be nobody left in Brussels to protect us poor women?\"", "\"브뤼셀에는 우리 가엾은 여자들을 지켜 줄 사람 하나 남지 않는단 말인가요?\""], ["Jos succeeded in plunging into the coat, and came forward blushing and stuttering out excuses to his fair visitor.", "조스는 가까스로 코트 속으로 파고드는 데 성공하고는, 얼굴을 붉히며 더듬더듬 변명을 늘어놓으면서 어여쁜 방문객 앞으로 나아갔다."], ["\"How was she after the events of the morning--after the fatigues of the ball the night before?\"", "\"오늘 아침의 일들을 겪고 나서, 또 어젯밤 무도회의 피로 끝에 그녀는 좀 어떠한지?\""], ["Monsieur Isidor disappeared into his master's adjacent bedroom, bearing off the flowered dressing-gown.", "이지도르 씨는 꽃무늬 실내복을 들고 주인의 옆방 침실로 사라졌다."]], [["\"How good of you to ask,\"", "\"이렇게 물어봐 주시다니 어쩜 이리도 친절하실까.\""], ["said she, pressing one of his hands in both her own.", "그녀가 두 손으로 그의 한 손을 꼭 잡으며 말했다."], ["\"How cool and collected you look when everybody else is frightened!", "\"다들 겁에 질려 있는데 이토록 침착하고 의젓해 보이시다니!"], ["How is our dear little Emmy?", "우리 사랑스러운 어린 에미는 좀 어때요?"], ["It must have been an awful, awful parting.\"", "정말 끔찍하고도 끔찍한 이별이었겠지요.\""]], [["\"Tremendous,\"", "\"끔찍했지요.\""], ["Jos said.", "조스가 말했다."]], [["\"You men can bear anything,\"", "\"남자분들은 무엇이든 견뎌 내실 수 있지요.\""], ["replied the lady.", "부인이 대답했다."], ["\"Parting or danger are nothing to you.", "\"이별이든 위험이든 당신들께는 아무것도 아니니까요."], ["Own now that you were going to join the army and leave us to our fate.", "자, 솔직히 인정하세요. 당신도 군대에 합류해서 우리를 운명에 내맡긴 채 떠나려 하셨던 거잖아요."], ["I know you were--something tells me you were.", "다 알아요. 무언가가 제게 그렇게 일러 준다고요."], ["I was so frightened, when the thought came into my head (for I do sometimes think of you when I am alone, Mr. Joseph), that I ran off immediately to beg and entreat you not to fly from us.\"", "그 생각이 머릿속에 떠오르자(혼자 있을 때면 저도 가끔은 당신을 생각한답니다, 조지프 씨) 어찌나 겁이 나던지, 우리를 버리고 달아나지 말아 달라고 빌고 또 애원하려고 곧장 달려온 거예요.\""]], [["This speech might be interpreted, \"My dear sir, should an accident befall the army, and a retreat be necessary, you have a very comfortable carriage, in which I propose to take a seat.\"", "이 말은 이렇게 풀이할 수도 있었다. \"친애하는 선생님, 만에 하나 군대에 변고가 생겨 후퇴가 불가피해진다면, 선생님께는 아주 안락한 마차가 있으니 저는 거기 한자리 차지하고 싶답니다.\""], ["I don't know whether Jos understood the words in this sense.", "조스가 이 말을 그런 뜻으로 알아들었는지는 모르겠다."], ["But he was profoundly mortified by the lady's inattention to him during their stay at Brussels.", "그러나 그는 브뤼셀에 머무는 동안 부인이 자기에게 무심했던 일로 마음 깊이 상해 있던 터였다. 그는 로던 크롤리의 대단한 지인 중 누구에게도 소개받은 적이 없었고, 레베카의 모임에 초대받은 적도 거의 없었다."], ["He had never been presented to any of Rawdon Crawley's great acquaintances: he had scarcely been invited to Rebecca's parties; for he was too timid to play much, and his presence bored George and Rawdon equally, who neither of them, perhaps, liked to have a witness of the amusements in which the pair chose to indulge.", "카드놀이를 크게 할 만큼 배짱이 없는 데다, 그가 자리에 있으면 조지와 로던 둘 다 똑같이 지루해했기 때문이다. 어쩌면 둘 중 누구도 그 두 사람이 즐기기로 작정한 유흥을 지켜보는 목격자를 곁에 두고 싶지 않았던 것이리라."], ["\"Ah!\"", "\"흥!\""], ["thought Jos, \"now she wants me she comes to me.", "조스는 생각했다. \"이제 내가 필요하니까 내게 오는군."], ["When there is nobody else in the way she can think about old Joseph Sedley!\"", "곁에 아무도 없을 때라야 늙은 조지프 세들리를 생각해 준단 말이지!\""], ["But besides these doubts he felt flattered at the idea Rebecca expressed of his courage.", "그러나 이런 의구심에도 불구하고, 그는 레베카가 자기 용기를 두고 한 말에 우쭐한 기분이 들었다."]], [["He blushed a good deal, and put on an air of importance.", "그는 얼굴을 한껏 붉히고는 거드름 피우는 태도를 지었다."], ["\"I should like to see the action,\"", "\"나도 그 전투를 보고는 싶소.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"Every man of any spirit would, you know.", "\"기개 있는 사내라면 누구나 그렇겠지, 알다시피."], ["I've seen a little service in India, but nothing on this grand scale.\"", "인도에서 자그마한 군 경험을 좀 쌓아 보긴 했지만, 이만한 규모는 처음이오.\""]], [["\"You men would sacrifice anything for a pleasure,\"", "\"당신들 남자분들은 즐거움을 위해서라면 무엇이든 희생하시지요.\""], ["Rebecca answered.", "레베카가 대답했다."], ["\"Captain Crawley left me this morning as gay as if he were going to a hunting party.", "\"크롤리 대위는 오늘 아침 마치 사냥 모임에라도 가는 사람처럼 신이 나서 절 떠났답니다."], ["What does he care?", "그이가 무얼 신경 쓰겠어요?"], ["What do any of you care for the agonies and tortures of a poor forsaken woman? (I wonder whether he could really have been going to the troops, this great lazy gourmand?) Oh!", "당신들 가운데 누가 버림받은 가엾은 여자의 고통과 괴로움을 신경이나 쓰겠어요?(그이가 정말로 부대로 가긴 갔으려나, 이 게으르고 먹성 좋은 양반이?) 오!"], ["dear Mr. Sedley, I have come to you for comfort--for consolation.", "친애하는 세들리 씨, 저는 당신께 위안을 구하러, 위로를 구하러 온 거예요."], ["I have been on my knees all the morning.", "아침 내내 무릎 꿇고 기도했답니다."], ["I tremble at the frightful danger into which our husbands, our friends, our brave troops and allies, are rushing.", "우리 남편들이, 우리 벗들이, 우리 용감한 군대와 동맹군이 뛰어들고 있는 그 끔찍한 위험을 생각하면 몸이 떨려요."], ["And I come here for shelter, and find another of my friends--the last remaining to me--bent upon plunging into the dreadful scene!\"", "그래서 피신처를 찾아 여기 왔더니, 또 다른 벗이, 제게 마지막 남은 벗이 그 무시무시한 전장에 뛰어들기로 작정하고 있지 뭐예요!\""]], [["\"My dear madam,\"", "\"친애하는 부인.\""], ["Jos replied, now beginning to be quite soothed, \"don't be alarmed.", "이제 마음이 자못 풀어지기 시작한 조스가 대답했다. \"겁내지 마시오."], ["I only said I should like to go--what Briton would not?", "나는 그저 가 보고 싶다고 말했을 뿐이오. 어느 영국인인들 그러지 않겠소?"], ["But my duty keeps me here: I can't leave that poor creature in the next room.\"", "하지만 내 의무가 나를 여기 붙들어 두고 있소. 옆방의 저 가엾은 사람을 두고 떠날 수가 없으니까.\""], ["And he pointed with his finger to the door of the chamber in which Amelia was.", "그러면서 그는 애밀리아가 있는 방문 쪽을 손가락으로 가리켰다."]], [["\"Good noble brother!\"", "\"착하고 고결하신 오라버니!\""], ["Rebecca said, putting her handkerchief to her eyes, and smelling the eau-de-cologne with which it was scented.", "레베카가 손수건을 눈에 갖다 대며, 거기 배인 오드콜로뉴 향을 맡으면서 말했다. \"제가 당신을 잘못 보았네요."], ["\"I have done you injustice: you have got a heart.", "당신께는 따뜻한 마음이 있는걸요."], ["I thought you had not.\"", "없는 줄로만 알았는데.\""]], [["\"O, upon my honour!\"", "\"오, 내 명예를 걸고!\""], ["Jos said, making a motion as if he would lay his hand upon the spot in question.", "조스가 마치 그 문제의 부위에 손을 얹기라도 하려는 듯한 시늉을 하며 말했다."], ["\"You do me injustice, indeed you do--my dear Mrs.", "\"당신은 정말 나를 잘못 보고 있소, 정말이오."], ["Crawley.\"", "친애하는 크롤리 부인.\""]], [["\"I do, now your heart is true to your sister.", "\"네, 이제 당신의 마음이 누이동생에게 진실하니 그렇네요."], ["But I remember two years ago--when it was false to me!\"", "하지만 이 년 전 일은 기억하고 있어요."], ["Rebecca said, fixing her eyes upon him for an instant, and then turning away into the window.", "그 마음이 제게는 거짓이었던 때 말이에요!\" 레베카가 한순간 그에게 시선을 박았다가, 이내 돌아서서 창가로 향하며 말했다."]], [["Jos blushed violently.", "조스는 격렬하게 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["That organ which he was accused by Rebecca of not possessing began to thump tumultuously.", "레베카에게 없다고 책망당한 바로 그 기관이 요란하게 쿵쿵 뛰기 시작했다."], ["He recalled the days when he had fled from her, and the passion which had once inflamed him--the days when he had driven her in his curricle: when she had knit the green purse for him: when he had sate enraptured gazing at her white arms and bright eyes.", "그는 그녀에게서 달아났던 날들과, 한때 자기를 불타게 했던 그 열정을 떠올렸다. 이륜마차에 그녀를 태우고 몰던 날들, 그녀가 그에게 초록색 돈지갑을 떠 주던 날들, 그녀의 흰 팔과 빛나는 두 눈을 황홀히 바라보며 넋을 잃고 앉아 있던 날들을."]], [["\"I know you think me ungrateful,\"", "\"저를 배은망덕하다고 생각하시겠지요.\""], ["Rebecca continued, coming out of the window, and once more looking at him and addressing him in a low tremulous voice.", "레베카가 창가에서 나오며, 다시 한번 그를 바라보고는 나직하게 떨리는 목소리로 그에게 말을 이었다."], ["\"Your coldness, your averted looks, your manner when we have met of late--when I came in just now, all proved it to me.", "\"당신의 그 냉랭함, 외면하는 눈길, 요즘 우리가 마주칠 때마다의 그 태도, 방금 제가 들어왔을 때 그 모습, 모두 그걸 제게 증명해 주었어요."], ["But were there no reasons why I should avoid you?", "하지만 제가 당신을 피할 만한 이유가 없었을까요?"], ["Let your own heart answer that question.", "그 물음에는 당신의 마음이 답하게 하세요."], ["Do you think my husband was too much inclined to welcome you?", "제 남편이 당신을 그리도 반겨 맞이하려 했다고 보세요?"], ["The only unkind words I have ever had from him (I will do Captain Crawley that justice) have been about you--and most cruel, cruel words they were.\"", "제가 그이에게서 들은 모진 말이라고는(크롤리 대위에게 이만한 공정함은 베풀어야겠지요) 죄다 당신에 관한 것이었어요. 그리고 그건 더없이 모질고도 모진 말들이었지요.\""]], [["\"Good gracious!", "\"이런 맙소사!"], ["what have I done?\"", "내가 대체 무엇을 했단 말이오?\""], ["asked Jos in a flurry of pleasure and perplexity; \"what have I done--to--to--?\"", "조스가 기쁨과 당혹이 뒤섞인 허둥거림 속에서 물었다. \"내가 대체 무엇을 했길래, 그, 그, 그래서?\""]], [["\"Is jealousy nothing?\"", "\"질투가 아무것도 아니란 말인가요?\""], ["said Rebecca.", "레베카가 말했다."], ["\"He makes me miserable about you.", "\"그이는 당신 때문에 저를 비참하게 만들어요."], ["And whatever it might have been once--my heart is all his.", "그리고 한때야 어떠했든, 제 마음은 온전히 그이의 것이랍니다."], ["I am innocent now.", "저는 이제 결백해요."], ["Am I not, Mr. Sedley?\"", "그렇지 않나요, 세들리 씨?\""]], [["All Jos's blood tingled with delight, as he surveyed this victim to his attractions.", "자기 매력에 희생된 이 여인을 찬찬히 바라보는 사이, 조스의 온몸의 피가 환희로 짜릿짜릿해졌다."], ["A few adroit words, one or two knowing tender glances of the eyes, and his heart was inflamed again and his doubts and suspicions forgotten.", "능란한 몇 마디 말, 다 안다는 듯한 다정한 눈길 한두 번에, 그의 마음은 다시 불붙고 의혹과 의심은 까맣게 잊혔다."], ["From Solomon downwards, have not wiser men than he been cajoled and befooled by women?", "솔로몬 이래로, 그보다 더 지혜로운 사내들도 여자에게 농락당하고 우롱당하지 않았던가?"], ["\"If the worst comes to the worst,\"", "\"최악의 경우가 닥치더라도,\" 베키는 생각했다."], ["Becky thought, \"my retreat is secure; and I have a right-hand seat in the barouche.\"", "\"내 퇴로는 확보된 셈이지. 사륜마차의 오른쪽 좋은 자리도 차지할 권리가 생겼고.\""]], [["There is no knowing into what declarations of love and ardour the tumultuous passions of Mr.", "만약 바로 이 순간 하인 이지도르가 다시 나타나 집안일로 부산을 떨기 시작하지 않았더라면, 조지프 씨의 들끓는 열정이 그를 어떤 사랑과 정열의 고백으로 몰고 갔을지는 알 수 없는 일이다."], ["Joseph might have led him, if Isidor the valet had not made his reappearance at this minute, and begun to busy himself about the domestic affairs.", "막 한마디 고백을 토해 내려던 조스는 억눌러야만 했던 그 격정에 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이었다."], ["Jos, who was just going to gasp out an avowal, choked almost with the emotion that he was obliged to restrain.", "레베카 역시 이제는 안으로 들어가 사랑하는 애밀리아를 위로해 줄 때라는 데에 생각이 미쳤다. \"오 르부아르.\""], ["Rebecca too bethought her that it was time she should go in and comfort her dearest Amelia. \"Au revoir,\"", "그녀는 조지프 씨에게 손에 입맞춤을 던지며 그렇게 말하고는, 그의 누이가 있는 방문을 가볍게 두드렸다."], ["she said, kissing her hand to Mr. Joseph, and tapped gently at the door of his sister's apartment.", "그녀가 안으로 들어가 등 뒤로 문을 닫자, 그는 의자에 털썩 주저앉아 멍하니 바라보며 한숨을 쉬고 의미심장하게 씩씩거렸다."], ["As she entered and closed the door on herself, he sank down in a chair, and gazed and sighed and puffed portentously.", "\"그 코트는 나리께 너무 꽉 낍니다.\" 여전히 장식 단추에 눈독을 들이며 이지도르가 말했지만, 주인은 그 말을 듣지 못했다."], ["\"That coat is very tight for Milor,\"", "그의 생각은 다른 데 가 있었던 것이다."], ["Isidor said, still having his eye on the frogs; but his master heard him not: his thoughts were elsewhere: now glowing, maddening, upon the contemplation of the enchanting Rebecca: anon shrinking guiltily before the vision of the jealous Rawdon Crawley, with his curling, fierce mustachios, and his terrible duelling pistols loaded and cocked.", "어떤 때는 매혹적인 레베카를 그리며 달아오르고 미칠 듯하다가, 또 어떤 때는 곱슬곱슬 사나운 콧수염을 기르고 무시무시한 결투용 권총에 탄을 재어 공이를 당겨 든 질투심에 찬 로던 크롤리의 환영 앞에서 죄지은 듯 움츠러들었다. * 오 르부아르: \"또 봬요\"에 해당하는 프랑스어 작별 인사."]], [["Rebecca's appearance struck Amelia with terror, and made her shrink back.", "레베카가 나타나자 애밀리아는 공포에 사로잡혀 뒷걸음질 쳤다."], ["It recalled her to the world and the remembrance of yesterday.", "그것은 그녀를 다시 현실 세계로, 어제의 기억으로 불러들였던 것이다."], ["In the overpowering fears about to-morrow she had forgotten Rebecca--jealousy--everything except that her husband was gone and was in danger.", "내일에 대한 압도적인 두려움 속에서 그녀는 레베카도, 질투도, 남편이 떠나 위험에 처해 있다는 사실 외에는 모든 것을 잊고 있었던 것이다."], ["Until this dauntless worldling came in and broke the spell, and lifted the latch, we too have forborne to enter into that sad chamber.", "이 겁 없는 속물이 들어와 그 주술을 깨뜨리고 빗장을 들어 올리기까지는, 우리 또한 그 슬픈 방 안으로 차마 들어서지 못했던 것이다."], ["How long had that poor girl been on her knees!", "그 가엾은 처녀가 무릎 꿇고 있던 시간이 얼마였던가!"], ["what hours of speechless prayer and bitter prostration had she passed there!", "그녀가 거기서 보낸 말없는 기도와 쓰라린 부복(俯伏)의 시간들이 얼마였던가!"], ["The war-chroniclers who write brilliant stories of fight and triumph scarcely tell us of these.", "싸움과 승리의 찬란한 이야기를 써 내려가는 전쟁 연대기 작가들은 이런 것들에 대해서는 좀처럼 들려주지 않는다."], ["These are too mean parts of the pageant: and you don't hear widows'", "이런 것들은 그 화려한 행렬에서 너무도 하찮은 부분인 것이다."], ["cries or mothers' sobs in the midst of the shouts and jubilation in the great Chorus of Victory.", "그리고 여러분은 승리의 위대한 합창 한가운데서 터지는 함성과 환호 속에서는 과부의 울부짖음이나 어머니의 흐느낌을 듣지 못한다."], ["And yet when was the time that such have not cried out: heart-broken, humble protestants, unheard in the uproar of the triumph!", "그러나 그런 이들이 울부짖지 않았던 때가 언제 한 번이라도 있었던가. 가슴 찢어진, 비천한 항변자들이여, 승리의 소란 속에 묻혀 끝내 들리지 못하는구나!"]], [["After the first movement of terror in Amelia's mind--when Rebecca's green eyes lighted upon her, and rustling in her fresh silks and brilliant ornaments, the latter tripped up with extended arms to embrace her--a feeling of anger succeeded, and from being deadly pale before, her face flushed up red, and she returned Rebecca's look after a moment with a steadiness which surprised and somewhat abashed her rival.", "애밀리아의 마음에 첫 공포의 동요가 일고 나서, 레베카의 초록빛 두 눈이 그녀에게 와닿고, 후자가 산뜻한 비단옷과 번쩍이는 장신구를 바스락거리며 두 팔을 벌리고 안으려 종종걸음으로 다가오자, 뒤이어 노여움의 감정이 솟구쳤다. 그리하여 좀 전까지 죽은 듯 창백하던 그녀의 얼굴이 붉게 달아올랐고, 한순간 뒤 그녀는 그 큰 눈으로 레베카의 시선을 흔들림 없이 되받았으니, 그 단호함에 연적은 놀라고 다소 무안해질 지경이었다."]], [["\"Dearest Amelia, you are very unwell,\"", "\"사랑하는 애밀리아, 안색이 무척 안 좋구나.\""], ["the visitor said, putting forth her hand to take Amelia's.", "방문객이 애밀리아의 손을 잡으려 손을 내밀며 말했다."], ["\"What is it?", "\"무슨 일이니?"], ["I could not rest until I knew how you were.\"", "네가 어떤지 알기 전까지는 도무지 마음을 놓을 수가 없었어.\""]], [["Amelia drew back her hand--never since her life began had that gentle soul refused to believe or to answer any demonstration of good-will or affection.", "애밀리아는 손을 도로 거두어들였다. 그 온화한 영혼은 평생 단 한 번도 호의나 애정의 표시를 믿지 않거나 거기에 응하기를 거부한 적이 없었다."], ["But she drew back her hand, and trembled all over.", "그러나 그녀는 손을 도로 거두고 온몸을 부들부들 떨었다."], ["\"Why are you here, Rebecca?\"", "\"여긴 왜 왔니, 레베카?\""], ["she said, still looking at her solemnly with her large eyes.", "그녀가 여전히 큰 눈으로 엄숙히 그녀를 바라보며 말했다."], ["These glances troubled her visitor.", "이 눈빛이 방문객을 불안하게 했다."]], [["\"She must have seen him give me the letter at the ball,\"", "'무도회에서 그이가 내게 편지를 건네는 걸 본 게 틀림없어.'"], ["Rebecca thought.", "레베카는 생각했다."], ["\"Don't be agitated, dear Amelia,\"", "\"흥분하지 마, 사랑하는 애밀리아.\""], ["she said, looking down.", "그녀가 눈을 내리깔며 말했다."], ["\"I came but to see if I could--if you were well.\"", "\"난 그저 네가 좀, 네가 괜찮은지 보려고 왔을 뿐이야.\""]], [["\"Are you well?\"", "\"넌 괜찮니?\""], ["said Amelia.", "애밀리아가 말했다."], ["\"I dare say you are.", "\"그렇겠지."], ["You don't love your husband.", "넌 네 남편을 사랑하지 않으니까."], ["You would not be here if you did.", "사랑한다면 여기 와 있지도 않았을 테지."], ["Tell me, Rebecca, did I ever do you anything but kindness?\"", "말해 봐, 레베카, 내가 너한테 친절 말고 다른 짓을 한 적이 있었니?\""]], [["\"Indeed, Amelia, no,\"", "\"정말이지, 애밀리아, 없었어.\""], ["the other said, still hanging down her head.", "상대가 여전히 고개를 떨군 채 말했다."]], [["\"When you were quite poor, who was it that befriended you?", "\"네가 무일푼으로 가난했을 때, 너를 거두어 준 게 누구였니?"], ["Was I not a sister to you?", "내가 네게 자매 같은 사람이 아니었니?"], ["You saw us all in happier days before he married me.", "그이가 나랑 결혼하기 전, 더 행복했던 시절에 넌 우리 모두를 보았잖아."], ["I was all in all then to him; or would he have given up his fortune, his family, as he nobly did to make me happy?", "그때 난 그이에게 전부였어. 아니면 그이가 나를 행복하게 해 주려고 그토록 고결하게 자기 재산과 가문을 포기했겠니?"], ["Why did you come between my love and me?", "그런데 넌 왜 내 사랑과 나 사이에 끼어들었니?"], ["Who sent you to separate those whom God joined, and take my darling's heart from me--my own husband?", "하느님이 맺어 주신 사람들을 갈라놓고, 내 사랑하는 이의 마음을, 내 남편의 마음을 내게서 빼앗아 가라고 누가 널 보냈니?"], ["Do you think you could love him as I did?", "네가 나만큼 그이를 사랑할 수 있을 것 같니?"], ["His love was everything to me.", "그이의 사랑은 내게 전부였어."], ["You knew it, and wanted to rob me of it.", "넌 그걸 알면서도 내게서 그걸 훔쳐 가려 했지. 부끄러운 줄 알아, 레베카."], ["For shame, Rebecca; bad and wicked woman--false friend and false wife.\"", "나쁘고 사악한 여자. 거짓된 벗에 거짓된 아내.\""]], [["\"Amelia, I protest before God, I have done my husband no wrong,\"", "\"애밀리아, 하느님 앞에 맹세코 난 내 남편에게 아무 잘못도 하지 않았어.\""], ["Rebecca said, turning from her.", "레베카가 그녀에게서 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["\"Have you done me no wrong, Rebecca?", "\"내겐 아무 잘못도 안 했고, 레베카?"], ["You did not succeed, but you tried.", "넌 성공하진 못했지만, 시도는 했잖아."], ["Ask your heart if you did not.\"", "그러지 않았는지 네 마음에 물어봐.\""]], [["She knows nothing, Rebecca thought.", "'아무것도 모르는군.' 레베카는 생각했다."]], [["\"He came back to me.", "\"그이는 내게 돌아왔어."], ["I knew he would.", "그럴 줄 알았어."], ["I knew that no falsehood, no flattery, could keep him from me long.", "그 어떤 거짓도, 그 어떤 아첨도 그이를 내게서 오래 떼어 놓을 수 없다는 걸 난 알고 있었어."], ["I knew he would come.", "그이가 돌아오리란 걸 알았어."], ["I prayed so that he should.\"", "그렇게 되게 해 달라고 그토록 기도했으니까.\""]], [["The poor girl spoke these words with a spirit and volubility which Rebecca had never before seen in her, and before which the latter was quite dumb.", "가엾은 처녀는 레베카가 전에 한 번도 본 적 없는 기개와 거침없는 말솜씨로 이 말들을 쏟아 냈고, 그 앞에서 레베카는 그만 말문이 막혀 버렸다."], ["\"But what have I done to you,\"", "\"하지만 내가 너한테 무슨 잘못을 했길래,\""], ["she continued in a more pitiful tone, \"that you should try and take him from me?", "그녀가 더 가련한 어조로 말을 이었다. \"넌 그이를 내게서 빼앗으려 한 거니?"], ["I had him but for six weeks.", "난 겨우 육 주 동안 그이를 가졌을 뿐인데."], ["You might have spared me those, Rebecca.", "그것만은 내게 남겨 둘 수 있었잖아, 레베카."], ["And yet, from the very first day of our wedding, you came and blighted it.", "그런데도 넌 우리 결혼 첫날부터 찾아와서 그걸 망쳐 놓았어."], ["Now he is gone, are you come to see how unhappy I am?\"", "이제 그이가 떠나고 없으니, 내가 얼마나 불행한지 구경하러 온 거니?\""], ["she continued.", "그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["\"You made me wretched enough for the past fortnight: you might have spared me to-day.\"", "\"지난 보름 동안 넌 나를 충분히 비참하게 만들었어. 오늘만은 나를 놔둘 수도 있었잖아.\""]], [["\"I--I never came here,\"", "\"난, 난 여기 온 적 없어.\""], ["interposed Rebecca, with unlucky truth.", "레베카가 운 나쁘게도 사실대로 끼어들었다."]], [["\"No.", "\"그래."], ["You didn't come.", "넌 온 적 없지."], ["You took him away.", "넌 그이를 데려갔으니까."], ["Are you come to fetch him from me?\"", "이제 그이를 내게서 데려가려고 온 거니?\""], ["she continued in a wilder tone.", "그녀가 더 격앙된 어조로 말을 이었다."], ["\"He was here, but he is gone now.", "\"그이가 여기 있었는데, 이젠 가 버렸어."], ["There on that very sofa he sate.", "저기 바로 저 소파에 그이가 앉았었어."], ["Don't touch it.", "손대지 마."], ["We sate and talked there.", "우린 거기 앉아서 이야기를 나눴어."], ["I was on his knee, and my arms were round his neck, and we said 'Our Father.'", "난 그이 무릎에 앉았고, 두 팔로 그이 목을 감았고, 우린 '주기도문'을 외웠어."], ["Yes, he was here: and they came and took him away, but he promised me to come back.\"", "그래, 그이는 여기 있었어. 그런데 사람들이 와서 그이를 데려갔지만, 그이는 내게 돌아오겠다고 약속했어.\""]], [["\"He will come back, my dear,\"", "\"그이는 돌아올 거야, 얘야.\""], ["said Rebecca, touched in spite of herself.", "레베카가 자기도 모르게 마음이 움직여 말했다."]], [["\"Look,\"", "\"봐.\""], ["said Amelia, \"this is his sash--isn't it a pretty colour?\"", "애밀리아가 말했다. \"이게 그이의 장식 띠야."], ["and she took up the fringe and kissed it.", "빛깔이 예쁘지 않니?\""], ["She had tied it round her waist at some part of the day.", "그러면서 그녀는 그 술 장식을 집어 들어 입을 맞추었다."], ["She had forgotten her anger, her jealousy, the very presence of her rival seemingly.", "그녀는 그날 어느 무렵엔가 그것을 자기 허리에 둘러 매어 두었던 것이다."], ["For she walked silently and almost with a smile on her face, towards the bed, and began to smooth down George's pillow.", "그녀는 노여움도, 질투도, 심지어 연적이 곁에 있다는 것조차 잊은 듯했다. 말없이, 거의 얼굴에 미소를 띤 채 침대 쪽으로 걸어가 조지의 베개를 매만지기 시작했으니 말이다."]], [["Rebecca walked, too, silently away.", "레베카도 말없이 자리를 떴다."], ["\"How is Amelia?\"", "\"애밀리아는 좀 어떻소?\""], ["asked Jos, who still held his position in the chair.", "여전히 의자에 자리 잡고 앉아 있던 조스가 물었다."]], [["\"There should be somebody with her,\"", "\"누군가 곁에 있어 주어야겠어요.\""], ["said Rebecca.", "레베카가 말했다."], ["\"I think she is very unwell\": and she went away with a very grave face, refusing Mr.", "\"제 생각엔 무척 안 좋은 것 같아요.\" 그러고는 무척 침통한 얼굴로 자리를 떠났다."], ["Sedley's entreaties that she would stay and partake of the early dinner which he had ordered.", "자기가 시켜 둔 이른 저녁을 함께 들고 가라는 세들리 씨의 간청도 마다하고서였다."]], [["Rebecca was of a good-natured and obliging disposition; and she liked Amelia rather than otherwise.", "레베카는 성품이 선량하고 남을 잘 위하는 사람이었다. 그리고 애밀리아를 싫어한다기보다는 오히려 좋아하는 편이었다."], ["Even her hard words, reproachful as they were, were complimentary--the groans of a person stinging under defeat. Meeting Mrs.", "책망하는 말이긴 했어도, 그 모진 말들조차 그녀에게는 일종의 찬사였다."], ["O'Dowd, whom the Dean's sermons had by no means comforted, and who was walking very disconsolately in the Parc, Rebecca accosted the latter, rather to the surprise of the Major's wife, who was not accustomed to such marks of politeness from Mrs.", "패배의 아픔에 따끔거리는 사람이 내지르는 신음이었던 것이다. 학장의 설교에도 도무지 위안을 얻지 못한 채 파르크를 더없이 쓸쓸하게 거닐던 오다우드 부인과 마주치자, 레베카가 먼저 그녀에게 말을 건넸다."], ["Rawdon Crawley, and informing her that poor little Mrs.", "로던 크롤리 부인에게서 이런 정중한 표시를 받는 데 익숙지 않았던 소령 부인은 다소 놀랐다."], ["Osborne was in a desperate condition, and almost mad with grief, sent off the good-natured Irishwoman straight to see if she could console her young favourite.", "레베카는 가엾은 어린 오스본 부인이 절망적인 지경에 빠져 슬픔으로 거의 실성할 지경이라고 알리고는, 그 마음씨 고운 아일랜드 여인을 곧장 보내어 자기가 아끼는 어린 벗을 위로할 수 있을지 가 보게 했다."]], [["\"I've cares of my own enough,\" Mrs.", "\"제 코가 석 자랍니다.\""], ["O'Dowd said, gravely, \"and I thought poor Amelia would be little wanting for company this day.", "오다우드 부인이 침통하게 말했다. \"그리고 가엾은 애밀리아는 오늘 같은 날 곁에 있어 줄 사람이 거의 없겠거니 했지요."], ["But if she's so bad as you say, and you can't attend to her, who used to be so fond of her, faith I'll see if I can be of service.", "하지만 당신 말처럼 그 애가 그렇게나 안 좋고, 그토록 그 애를 아끼던 당신마저 돌볼 수 없다면, 정말이지 내가 도움이 될 수 있을지 가 보지요."], ["And so good marning to ye, Madam\"; with which speech and a toss of her head, the lady of the repayther took a farewell of Mrs.", "그럼 안녕히 계세요, 부인.\" 이 말과 함께 고개를 한 번 까딱하고는, 그 회중시계의 임자이신 부인은 크롤리 부인에게 작별을 고했다."], ["Crawley, whose company she by no means courted.", "그녀와 어울리는 것 따위는 조금도 바라지 않았던 것이다."]], [["Becky watched her marching off, with a smile on her lip.", "베키는 그녀가 성큼성큼 떠나가는 모습을 입가에 미소를 머금고 지켜보았다."], ["She had the keenest sense of humour, and the Parthian look which the retreating Mrs. O'Dowd flung over her shoulder almost upset Mrs.", "그녀에게는 더없이 예리한 유머 감각이 있었던지라, 물러가는 오다우드 부인이 어깨너머로 던진 파르티아식 눈초리에 크롤리 부인은 하마터면 그 침착함을 잃을 뻔했다."], ["Crawley's gravity. \"My service to ye, me fine Madam, and I'm glad to see ye so cheerful,\"", "'잘 가시구려, 곱디고운 부인, 그리도 명랑하신 걸 보니 반갑소.'"], ["thought Peggy.", "페기는 생각했다."], ["\"It's not YOU that will cry your eyes out with grief, anyway.\"", "'어쨌든 당신은 슬픔에 겨워 눈이 짓무르도록 울 사람은 아니지.'"], ["And with this she passed on, and speedily found her way to Mrs. Osborne's lodgings.", "그러고는 그녀는 계속 나아가, 이내 오스본 부인의 거처로 향하는 길을 찾아냈다."]], [["The poor soul was still at the bedside, where Rebecca had left her, and stood almost crazy with grief.", "* 파르티아식 눈초리: 고대 파르티아 기병이 후퇴하는 척하다 돌아서서 활을 쏘던 전술에서 유래한 표현으로, 떠나면서 던지는 마지막 일격을 뜻한다."], ["The Major's wife, a stronger-minded woman, endeavoured her best to comfort her young friend.", "가엾은 그 영혼은 레베카가 두고 떠난 그 침대 곁에 여전히 있었고, 슬픔으로 거의 실성한 채 서 있었다. 마음이 더 굳센 여인인 소령 부인은 어린 벗을 위로하려 최선을 다했다."], ["\"You must bear up, Amelia, dear,\"", "\"정신을 차려야 한단다, 사랑하는 애밀리아.\""], ["she said kindly, \"for he mustn't find you ill when he sends for you after the victory.", "그녀가 다정하게 말했다. \"승리한 뒤에 그이가 너를 부를 때, 네가 앓고 있는 걸 보여선 안 되잖니."], ["It's not you are the only woman that are in the hands of God this day.\"", "오늘 하느님의 손에 맡겨진 여자가 너 혼자만은 아니란다.\""]], [["\"I know that.", "\"알아요."], ["I am very wicked, very weak,\"", "전 너무 못됐고, 너무 약해요.\""], ["Amelia said.", "애밀리아가 말했다."], ["She knew her own weakness well enough.", "그녀는 자기 자신의 약함을 잘 알고 있었다."], ["The presence of the more resolute friend checked it, however; and she was the better of this control and company.", "그러나 더 결연한 벗이 곁에 있으니 그 약함이 다잡아졌고, 그녀는 이 다독임과 동무 덕에 한결 나아졌다."], ["They went on till two o'clock; their hearts were with the column as it marched farther and farther away.", "두 사람은 두 시까지 그렇게 함께 있었다. 그들의 마음은 점점 더 멀리 행군해 가는 그 종대와 함께 있었던 것이다."], ["Dreadful doubt and anguish--prayers and fears and griefs unspeakable--followed the regiment.", "끔찍한 의혹과 고뇌, 말로 다 못 할 기도와 두려움과 비탄이 연대를 뒤따랐다."], ["It was the women's tribute to the war.", "그것은 여인들이 전쟁에 바치는 공물이었다."], ["It taxes both alike, and takes the blood of the men, and the tears of the women.", "전쟁은 남녀 양쪽에 똑같이 세금을 매겨, 남자에게서는 피를, 여자에게서는 눈물을 거두어 가는 것이다."]], [["At half-past two, an event occurred of daily importance to Mr.", "두 시 반, 조지프 씨에게는 날마다 중요한 사건이 일어났다."], ["Joseph: the dinner-hour arrived.", "저녁 시간이 닥친 것이다."], ["Warriors may fight and perish, but he must dine.", "전사들이야 싸우다 스러질지언정, 그는 식사를 해야만 했다."], ["He came into Amelia's room to see if he could coax her to share that meal. \"Try,\"", "그는 애밀리아의 방으로 들어가 그녀를 구슬려 그 식사를 함께하게 할 수 있을지 보았다."], ["said he; \"the soup is very good.", "\"좀 들어 봐.\" 그가 말했다."], ["Do try, Emmy,\"", "\"수프가 아주 맛있단다."], ["and he kissed her hand.", "한번 들어 보렴, 에미.\""], ["Except when she was married, he had not done so much for years before.", "그러고는 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추었다. 그녀가 결혼하던 때를 빼면, 그가 몇 해 동안 그토록 다정하게 군 적이 없었다."], ["\"You are very good and kind, Joseph,\"", "\"오라버니는 참 착하고 친절하세요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"Everybody is, but, if you please, I will stay in my room to-day.\"", "\"다들 그래요. 하지만 괜찮으시면, 전 오늘 제 방에 있을래요.\""]], [["The savour of the soup, however, was agreeable to Mrs.", "그러나 수프의 향내는 오다우드 부인의 콧구멍에는 흡족한 것이었다."], ["O'Dowd's nostrils: and she thought she would bear Mr.", "그래서 그녀는 조스 씨와 동무해 주어야겠다고 생각했다. 그리하여 두 사람은 식사 앞에 마주 앉았다."], ["Jos company. So the two sate down to their meal.", "\"이 음식에 하느님의 축복이 있기를.\""], ["\"God bless the meat,\"", "소령 부인이 엄숙히 말했다."], ["said the Major's wife, solemnly: she was thinking of her honest Mick, riding at the head of his regiment: \"'Tis but a bad dinner those poor boys will get to-day,\"", "그녀는 자기 연대의 선두에서 말을 몰고 있을 정직한 믹을 생각하고 있었던 것이다. \"오늘 그 가엾은 아이들이 먹게 될 저녁이라곤 보잘것없겠지요.\""], ["she said, with a sigh, and then, like a philosopher, fell to.", "그녀가 한숨지으며 말하고는, 이내 철학자처럼 식사에 달려들었다."]], [["Jos's spirits rose with his meal.", "조스는 식사와 함께 기분이 좋아졌다."], ["He would drink the regiment's health; or, indeed, take any other excuse to indulge in a glass of champagne.", "그는 연대의 건강을 위해 건배라도 하고 싶었다. 아니, 사실 샴페인 한 잔을 들이켜기 위해서라면 무슨 핑계라도 댈 참이었다."], ["\"We'll drink to O'Dowd and the brave --th,\"", "\"오다우드와 용감한 ××연대를 위하여 마십시다.\""], ["said he, bowing gallantly to his guest.", "그가 손님에게 늠름하게 고개 숙여 인사하며 말했다."], ["\"Hey, Mrs. O'Dowd?", "\"어떻소, 오다우드 부인?"], ["Fill Mrs. O'Dowd's glass, Isidor.\"", "이지도르, 오다우드 부인 잔을 채워라.\""]], [["But all of a sudden, Isidor started, and the Major's wife laid down her knife and fork.", "그런데 별안간 이지도르가 흠칫 놀랐고, 소령 부인은 나이프와 포크를 내려놓았다."], ["The windows of the room were open, and looked southward, and a dull distant sound came over the sun-lighted roofs from that direction.", "남쪽으로 난 그 방의 창문들이 열려 있었는데, 햇살 비치는 지붕들 너머 그쪽 방향에서 둔탁하고 아득한 소리가 밀려왔던 것이다. \"저게 뭐지?\""], ["\"What is it?\"", "조스가 말했다."], ["said Jos. \"Why don't you pour, you rascal?\"", "\"이놈아, 왜 안 따르고 있는 게냐?\""]], [["\"Cest le feu!\"", "\"세 르 푀!\""], ["said Isidor, running to the balcony.", "이지도르가 발코니로 달려가며 말했다."]], [["\"God defend us; it's cannon!\"", "\"하느님, 우리를 지켜 주소서. 대포예요!\""], ["Mrs. O'Dowd cried, starting up, and followed too to the window.", "오다우드 부인이 벌떡 일어서며 외치고는, 그녀 역시 창가로 따라갔다."], ["A thousand pale and anxious faces might have been seen looking from other casements.", "다른 창문들에서도 새파랗게 질려 근심에 찬 수천의 얼굴이 내다보는 것이 보였으리라."], ["And presently it seemed as if the whole population of the city rushed into the streets.", "이윽고 도시의 온 주민이 거리로 쏟아져 나오는 것만 같았다. * 세 르 푀: \"포격이에요!\"에 해당하는 프랑스어."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXII", "# CHAPTER XXXII"]], [["In Which Jos Takes Flight, and the War Is Brought to a Close\nWe of peaceful London City have never beheld--and please God never shall witness--such a scene of hurry and alarm, as that which Brussels presented.", "평화로운 런던 시에 사는 우리는 브뤼셀이 보여 준 그런 황급함과 경악의 광경을 일찍이 목도한 적이 없으며, 부디 신의 가호로 앞으로도 보지 않기를 바란다."], ["Crowds rushed to the Namur gate, from which direction the noise proceeded, and many rode along the level chaussee, to be in advance of any intelligence from the army.", "군중은 소음이 들려오는 방향, 나뮈르 성문 쪽으로 몰려갔고, 많은 이들이 군대로부터 들려올 어떤 소식이라도 먼저 알아내려고 평탄한 큰길을 따라 말을 달렸다."], ["Each man asked his neighbour for news; and even great English lords and ladies condescended to speak to persons whom they did not know.", "사람마다 옆 사람에게 소식을 물었고, 신분 높은 영국 귀족 신사 숙녀들조차 알지도 못하는 사람들에게 말을 거는 일까지 마다하지 않았다."], ["The friends of the French went abroad, wild with excitement, and prophesying the triumph of their Emperor.", "프랑스 편을 드는 이들은 흥분에 들떠 밖으로 나돌며 자기네 황제의 승리를 예언했다."], ["The merchants closed their shops, and came out to swell the general chorus of alarm and clamour.", "상인들은 가게를 닫고 거리로 나와 경악과 아우성의 전체 합창에 가세했다."], ["Women rushed to the churches, and crowded the chapels, and knelt and prayed on the flags and steps.", "여인들은 교회로 몰려가 예배당을 가득 메우고는 돌바닥과 계단에 무릎 꿇고 기도했다."], ["The dull sound of the cannon went on rolling, rolling.", "대포의 둔중한 소리는 끊임없이 우르릉, 우르릉 굴러갔다."], ["Presently carriages with travellers began to leave the town, galloping away by the Ghent barrier.", "이윽고 여행객을 태운 마차들이 헨트 관문 쪽으로 질주하며 도시를 떠나기 시작했다."], ["The prophecies of the French partisans began to pass for facts.", "프랑스 편을 드는 자들의 예언이 사실로 통하기 시작했다."], ["\"He has cut the armies in two,\"", "\"그가 양쪽 군대를 둘로 갈라놓았다네.\""], ["it was said.", "사람들이 말했다."], ["\"He is marching straight on Brussels.", "\"곧장 브뤼셀로 진군하고 있다는군."], ["He will overpower the English, and be here to-night.\"", "영국군을 짓밟고 오늘 밤이면 여기 들이닥칠 거야.\""], ["\"He will overpower the English,\"", "\"영국군을 짓밟고,\""], ["shrieked Isidor to his master, \"and will be here to-night.\"", "이지도르가 주인에게 비명을 지르듯 말했다."], ["The man bounded in and out from the lodgings to the street, always returning with some fresh particulars of disaster.", "\"오늘 밤이면 여기 들이닥칠 겁니다.\" 이 하인은 숙소와 거리 사이를 들락날락 뛰어다니며 그때마다 새로운 참사의 세부 소식을 물어 왔다."], ["Jos's face grew paler and paler.", "조스의 얼굴은 점점 더 창백해졌다."], ["Alarm began to take entire possession of the stout civilian.", "이 살진 민간인은 차츰 공포에 완전히 사로잡히기 시작했다."], ["All the champagne he drank brought no courage to him.", "그가 마신 샴페인은 그 어느 것도 그에게 용기를 가져다주지 못했다."], ["Before sunset he was worked up to such a pitch of nervousness as gratified his friend Isidor to behold, who now counted surely upon the spoils of the owner of the laced coat.", "해가 지기 전에 그는 신경이 곤두설 대로 곤두선 지경에 이르렀고, 그 친구 이지도르는 그 꼴을 보며 흡족해했다. 이지도르는 이제 그 금몰을 두른 외투 주인의 노획물을 틀림없는 제 것으로 셈하고 있었던 것이다."]], [["The women were away all this time.", "여자들은 이 내내 자리에 없었다."], ["After hearing the firing for a moment, the stout Major's wife bethought her of her friend in the next chamber, and ran in to watch, and if possible to console, Amelia.", "한순간 포성을 듣고 나자, 그 건장한 소령의 부인은 옆방의 벗이 떠올라 애밀리아를 살피고 가능하면 위로하려고 안으로 달려 들어갔다."], ["The idea that she had that helpless and gentle creature to protect, gave additional strength to the natural courage of the honest Irishwoman.", "저 가련하고 가냘픈 사람을 자기가 지켜 주어야 한다는 생각이, 정직한 아일랜드 여인이 본래 지닌 용기에 한층 힘을 보태 주었다."], ["She passed five hours by her friend's side, sometimes in remonstrance, sometimes talking cheerfully, oftener in silence and terrified mental supplication.", "그녀는 다섯 시간을 벗의 곁에서 보냈다. 때로는 타이르고, 때로는 명랑하게 이야기를 건네고, 더 자주는 말없이 겁에 질린 마음속 기도를 올리면서였다."], ["\"I never let go her hand once,\"", "\"단 한 번도 그 애 손을 놓지 않았답니다.\""], ["said the stout lady afterwards, \"until after sunset, when the firing was over.\"", "그 건장한 부인은 나중에 이렇게 말했다. \"해가 지고 포성이 멎을 때까지는요.\""], ["Pauline, the bonne, was on her knees at church hard by, praying for son homme a elle.", "하녀 폴린은 가까운 교회에서 무릎을 꿇고 자기 사내를 위해 기도하고 있었다."]], [["When the noise of the cannonading was over, Mrs.", "포성이 멎자 오다우드 부인은 애밀리아의 방에서 나와 붙어 있는 거실로 들어갔다."], ["O'Dowd issued out of Amelia's room into the parlour adjoining, where Jos sate with two emptied flasks, and courage entirely gone.", "거기에는 조스가 빈 술병 두 개를 앞에 놓고, 용기란 용기는 죄다 사라진 채 앉아 있었다."], ["Once or twice he had ventured into his sister's bedroom, looking very much alarmed, and as if he would say something.", "그는 한두 번 누이의 침실로 들어가 보려고 했는데, 몹시 겁에 질린 얼굴로 마치 무슨 말이라도 하려는 듯했다."], ["But the Major's wife kept her place, and he went away without disburthening himself of his speech.", "그러나 소령의 부인이 자리를 지키고 있었으므로, 그는 가슴에 담은 말을 털어놓지 못한 채 물러났다."], ["He was ashamed to tell her that he wanted to fly.", "그는 자기가 달아나고 싶다는 말을 그녀에게 차마 하지 못했던 것이다."]], [["But when she made her appearance in the dining-room, where he sate in the twilight in the cheerless company of his empty champagne bottles, he began to open his mind to her.", "그러나 그녀가 식당에 모습을 드러냈을 때―그는 황혼 속에 빈 샴페인 병들의 적막한 동무를 곁에 두고 앉아 있었다―그는 비로소 그녀에게 속내를 열기 시작했다."]], [["\"Mrs. O'Dowd,\"", "\"오다우드 부인,\""], ["he said, \"hadn't you better get Amelia ready?\"", "그가 말했다. \"애밀리아를 채비시키는 게 좋지 않겠습니까?\""]], [["\"Are you going to take her out for a walk?\"", "\"산책이라도 데리고 나가시려고요?\" 소령의 부인이 말했다."], ["said the Major's lady; \"sure she's too weak to stir.\"", "\"그 애는 너무 약해져서 꼼짝도 못 할 텐데요.\""]], [["\"I--I've ordered the carriage,\"", "\"내가―내가 마차를 불러 두었습니다.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said, \"and--and post-horses; Isidor is gone for them,\"", "\"그리고―그리고 역마도요. 이지도르가 구하러 갔지요.\""], ["Jos continued.", "조스가 말을 이었다."]], [["\"What do you want with driving to-night?\"", "\"이 밤중에 마차를 몰아서 뭘 하시려고요?\""], ["answered the lady.", "부인이 대답했다."], ["\"Isn't she better on her bed?", "\"그 애는 침대에 누워 있는 게 낫지 않겠어요?"], ["I've just got her to lie down.\"", "이제 막 눕혀 놓았는데요.\""]], [["\"Get her up,\"", "\"일으키세요.\""], ["said Jos; \"she must get up, I say\": and he stamped his foot energetically.", "조스가 말했다. \"그 애는 일어나야 합니다, 내 말은."], ["\"I say the horses are ordered--yes, the horses are ordered.", "말을 불러 두었다니까요―그래요, 말을 불러 두었어요."], ["It's all over, and--\"", "이제 다 끝났고, 그리고―\""]], [["\"And what?\"", "\"그리고 뭐요?\""], ["asked Mrs. O'Dowd.", "오다우드 부인이 물었다."]], [["\"I'm off for Ghent,\"", "\"나는 헨트로 떠납니다.\""], ["Jos answered.", "조스가 대답했다."], ["\"Everybody is going; there's a place for you!", "\"다들 떠나고 있어요. 부인 자리도 하나 있고요!"], ["We shall start in half-an-hour.\"", "삼십 분 안에 출발할 겁니다.\""]], [["The Major's wife looked at him with infinite scorn.", "소령의 부인은 한없는 경멸을 담아 그를 바라보았다."], ["\"I don't move till O'Dowd gives me the route,\"", "\"오다우드가 진로를 알려 줄 때까지는 한 발짝도 움직이지 않겠어요.\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"You may go if you like, Mr.", "\"원하신다면 떠나세요, 세들리 씨."], ["Sedley; but, faith, Amelia and I stop here.\"", "하지만 정말이지, 애밀리아와 나는 여기 남겠습니다.\""]], [["\"She SHALL go,\"", "\"그 애는 가야 합니다.\""], ["said Jos, with another stamp of his foot.", "조스가 또 한 번 발을 구르며 말했다."], ["Mrs. O'Dowd put herself with arms akimbo before the bedroom door.", "오다우드 부인은 두 팔을 허리에 떡 붙이고 침실 문 앞에 버티고 섰다."]], [["\"Is it her mother you're going to take her to?\"", "\"그 애를 어미한테 데려다주시려는 거예요?\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said; \"or do you want to go to Mamma yourself, Mr. Sedley?", "\"아니면 본인이 어머니한테 가고 싶으신 건가요, 세들리 씨?"], ["Good marning--a pleasant journey to ye, sir.", "그럼 안녕히, 즐거운 여행 되시길요, 나리. 봉 부아야주, 그쪽 말로 하자면 말이지요."], ["Bon voyage, as they say, and take my counsel, and shave off them mustachios, or they'll bring you into mischief.\"", "그리고 내 충고를 하나 새겨들으세요, 그 콧수염일랑 밀어 버리는 게 좋을 거예요. 안 그러면 그게 당신을 화근으로 끌어들일 테니까요.\""]], [["\"D--n!\"", "\"빌어먹을!\""], ["yelled out Jos, wild with fear, rage, and mortification; and Isidor came in at this juncture, swearing in his turn.", "조스가 공포와 분노와 굴욕에 미쳐 날뛰며 고함을 질렀다. 마침 이 순간 이지도르가 들어왔는데, 그도 그 나름대로 욕설을 퍼붓고 있었다."], ["\"Pas de chevaux, sacre bleu!\"", "\"빌어먹을, 말이 없어요!\""], ["hissed out the furious domestic.", "분노에 찬 하인이 씩씩거리며 내뱉었다."], ["All the horses were gone.", "말이란 말은 죄다 사라지고 없었다."], ["Jos was not the only man in Brussels seized with panic that day.", "그날 브뤼셀에서 공황에 사로잡힌 사람은 조스만이 아니었던 것이다."]], [["But Jos's fears, great and cruel as they were already, were destined to increase to an almost frantic pitch before the night was over.", "그러나 조스의 공포는, 이미 크고 가혹한 것이긴 했지만, 그 밤이 다 가기 전에 거의 광란의 지경에까지 커지도록 운명 지어져 있었다."], ["It has been mentioned how Pauline, the bonne, had son homme a elle also in the ranks of the army that had gone out to meet the Emperor Napoleon.", "앞서 말했듯이, 하녀 폴린에게도 나폴레옹 황제를 맞아 싸우러 나간 군대의 대열 속에 자기 사내가 있었다."], ["This lover was a native of Brussels, and a Belgian hussar.", "이 연인은 브뤼셀 토박이로, 벨기에 경기병이었다."], ["The troops of his nation signalised themselves in this war for anything but courage, and young Van Cutsum, Pauline's admirer, was too good a soldier to disobey his Colonel's orders to run away.", "그 나라 군대는 이 전쟁에서 용기 말고는 무엇으로든 명성을 떨쳤고, 폴린의 흠모하는 이 젊은 판 카쇰은 줄행랑을 치라는 대령의 명령을 어기기에는 지나치게 훌륭한 군인이었다."], ["Whilst in garrison at Brussels young Regulus (he had been born in the revolutionary times) found his great comfort, and passed almost all his leisure moments, in Pauline's kitchen; and it was with pockets and holsters crammed full of good things from her larder, that he had take leave of his weeping sweetheart, to proceed upon the campaign a few days before.", "브뤼셀에 주둔하는 동안 이 젊은 레굴루스는(그는 혁명기에 태어났다) 폴린의 부엌에서 더없는 위안을 찾았고 거의 모든 한가한 시간을 거기서 보냈다. 그리고 그가 며칠 전 출정에 나서며 울먹이는 연인과 작별했을 때, 그 주머니며 권총집은 그녀의 식료품 저장실에서 나온 좋은 것들로 가득 채워져 있었다."]], [["As far as his regiment was concerned, this campaign was over now.", "그의 연대로 말하자면, 이 출정은 이제 끝난 것이나 다름없었다."], ["They had formed a part of the division under the command of his Sovereign apparent, the Prince of Orange, and as respected length of swords and mustachios, and the richness of uniform and equipments, Regulus and his comrades looked to be as gallant a body of men as ever trumpet sounded for.", "그들은 그의 명목상 군주인 오라녜 공의 지휘 아래 있는 사단의 일부를 이루고 있었는데, 칼의 길이와 콧수염, 군복과 장비의 화려함으로 따지자면 레굴루스와 그 전우들은 나팔 소리에 맞춰 진군한 그 어떤 부대 못지않게 용맹스러워 보이는 무리였다."]], [["When Ney dashed upon the advance of the allied troops, carrying one position after the other, until the arrival of the great body of the British army from Brussels changed the aspect of the combat of Quatre Bras, the squadrons among which Regulus rode showed the greatest activity in retreating before the French, and were dislodged from one post and another which they occupied with perfect alacrity on their part.", "네가 연합군의 선봉을 향해 돌격하여 진지를 하나씩 차례로 빼앗아 나가다가, 브뤼셀에서 영국군 본대가 도착하면서 카트르브라 전투의 양상이 뒤바뀌었을 때, 레굴루스가 속한 기병대는 프랑스군 앞에서 후퇴하는 데 가장 활발한 동작을 보였고, 자기네가 차지하고 있던 진지를 하나씩 더없이 민첩하게 내주며 물러났다."], ["Their movements were only checked by the advance of the British in their rear.", "그들의 움직임을 멈춰 세운 것은 오로지 후방에서 진격해 오는 영국군뿐이었다."], ["Thus forced to halt, the enemy's cavalry (whose bloodthirsty obstinacy cannot be too severely reprehended) had at length an opportunity of coming to close quarters with the brave Belgians before them; who preferred to encounter the British rather than the French, and at once turning tail rode through the English regiments that were behind them, and scattered in all directions.", "그렇게 멈춰 설 수밖에 없게 되자, 적의 기병대는(그 피에 굶주린 집요함은 아무리 준엄하게 나무라도 지나치지 않다) 마침내 앞에 선 용감한 벨기에군과 백병전을 벌일 기회를 잡았다. 그러나 벨기에군은 프랑스군보다는 영국군과 부딪치는 편이 낫겠다 여겨, 곧장 꽁무니를 빼고는 자기네 뒤에 있던 영국 연대들 사이를 뚫고 달아나 사방으로 흩어졌다."], ["The regiment in fact did not exist any more.", "사실 그 연대는 이제 더는 존재하지 않았다."], ["It was nowhere.", "어디에도 없었다."], ["It had no head-quarters.", "본부도 없었다."], ["Regulus found himself galloping many miles from the field of action, entirely alone; and whither should he fly for refuge so naturally as to that kitchen and those faithful arms in which Pauline had so often welcomed him?", "레굴루스는 전장에서 수 마일이나 떨어진 곳을 홀로 내달리고 있는 자신을 발견했다. 그러니 피신처를 찾아 달려갈 곳이라면, 폴린이 그토록 자주 그를 반겨 주었던 그 부엌과 그 충실한 두 팔 말고 어디가 더 자연스러웠겠는가?"]], [["At some ten o'clock the clinking of a sabre might have been heard up the stair of the house where the Osbornes occupied a story in the continental fashion.", "밤 열 시쯤, 오스본 일가가 대륙식으로 한 층을 차지하고 있던 집의 계단을 올라오는 군도가 짤그랑거리는 소리가 들렸을지도 모른다."], ["A knock might have been heard at the kitchen door; and poor Pauline, come back from church, fainted almost with terror as she opened it and saw before her her haggard hussar.", "부엌문을 두드리는 소리도 들렸을 것이다. 교회에서 돌아온 가엾은 폴린은 문을 열고 자기 앞에 선 초췌한 경기병을 보고는 공포로 거의 까무러칠 뻔했다."], ["He looked as pale as the midnight dragoon who came to disturb Leonora.", "그는 레오노라를 깨우러 온 한밤중의 기병처럼 창백한 얼굴이었다."], ["Pauline would have screamed, but that her cry would have called her masters, and discovered her friend.", "폴린은 비명을 지를 뻔했지만, 그 소리가 주인들을 부르고 제 연인을 들통나게 할까 봐 비명을 삼켰다."], ["She stifled her scream, then, and leading her hero into the kitchen, gave him beer, and the choice bits from the dinner, which Jos had not had the heart to taste.", "그러고는 자기 영웅을 부엌으로 이끌어 들여 맥주와, 조스가 차마 입에 댈 엄두를 내지 못한 만찬에서 가장 좋은 부위들을 내주었다."], ["The hussar showed he was no ghost by the prodigious quantity of flesh and beer which he devoured--and during the mouthfuls he told his tale of disaster.", "경기병은 자기가 유령이 아니라는 것을, 게걸스레 먹어 치운 어마어마한 양의 고기와 맥주로 증명해 보였다. 그리고 우물거리는 사이사이 자기 참사의 이야기를 들려주었다."]], [["His regiment had performed prodigies of courage, and had withstood for a while the onset of the whole French army.", "그의 연대는 엄청난 용기를 발휘하여 한동안 프랑스 전군의 공세를 버텨 냈다."], ["But they were overwhelmed at last, as was the whole British army by this time.", "그러나 결국 압도당하고 말았으니, 그때쯤이면 영국군 전체가 그러했듯이 말이다."], ["Ney destroyed each regiment as it came up.", "네는 밀려드는 연대를 하나씩 박살 냈다."], ["The Belgians in vain interposed to prevent the butchery of the English.", "벨기에군이 영국군의 학살을 막아 보려 끼어들었으나 헛일이었다."], ["The Brunswickers were routed and had fled--their Duke was killed.", "브룬스비크군은 패주하여 달아났고, 그 공작은 전사했다."], ["It was a general debacle.", "그것은 전반적인 궤멸이었다."], ["He sought to drown his sorrow for the defeat in floods of beer.", "그는 패배의 슬픔을 맥주의 홍수에 빠뜨려 잊어 보려 했다."]], [["Isidor, who had come into the kitchen, heard the conversation and rushed out to inform his master.", "부엌에 들어왔다가 이 대화를 들은 이지도르는 주인에게 알리려고 뛰쳐나갔다. \"다 끝났습니다.\""], ["\"It is all over,\" he shrieked to Jos.", "그가 조스에게 비명을 지르듯 외쳤다."], ["\"Milor Duke is a prisoner; the Duke of Brunswick is killed; the British army is in full flight; there is only one man escaped, and he is in the kitchen now--come and hear him.\"", "\"공작 나리는 포로가 되었고, 브룬스비크 공작은 전사했고, 영국군은 완전히 패주 중이랍니다. 살아남은 사람은 단 하나뿐인데, 지금 부엌에 있어요."], ["So Jos tottered into that apartment where Regulus still sate on the kitchen table, and clung fast to his flagon of beer.", "와서 직접 들어 보세요.\" 그리하여 조스는 휘청거리며 그 방으로 들어갔다."], ["In the best French which he could muster, and which was in sooth of a very ungrammatical sort, Jos besought the hussar to tell his tale.", "레굴루스는 여전히 부엌 식탁에 앉아 맥주 단지를 꼭 붙들고 있었다. 조스는 자기가 짜낼 수 있는 가장 그럴듯한 프랑스어로―실상 문법이라고는 형편없는 종류의 것이었지만―경기병에게 그 이야기를 들려 달라고 간청했다."], ["The disasters deepened as Regulus spoke.", "레굴루스가 말을 이어 갈수록 참사는 깊어졌다."], ["He was the only man of his regiment not slain on the field.", "그는 자기 연대에서 전장에서 죽지 않은 유일한 사람이었다."], ["He had seen the Duke of Brunswick fall, the black hussars fly, the Ecossais pounded down by the cannon.", "그는 브룬스비크 공작이 쓰러지고, 검은 경기병들이 달아나고, 스코틀랜드 부대가 포화에 박살 나는 것을 보았다. \"그럼 ××연대는?\""], ["\"And the --th?\" gasped Jos.", "조스가 헐떡이며 물었다."]], [["\"Cut in pieces,\"", "\"산산조각 났지요.\""], ["said the hussar--upon which Pauline cried out, \"O my mistress, ma bonne petite dame,\"", "경기병이 말했다. 그 말에 폴린이 \"오 우리 아씨, 우리 가엾은 아씨\""], ["went off fairly into hysterics, and filled the house with her screams.", "하고 울부짖더니 그대로 발작을 일으켜 온 집 안을 비명으로 가득 채웠다."]], [["Wild with terror, Mr. Sedley knew not how or where to seek for safety.", "공포에 미쳐 날뛰던 세들리 씨는 어떻게 어디서 안전을 구해야 할지 알 수가 없었다."], ["He rushed from the kitchen back to the sitting-room, and cast an appealing look at Amelia's door, which Mrs.", "그는 부엌에서 거실로 다시 뛰쳐나가, 오다우드 부인이 면전에서 닫고 잠가 버린 애밀리아의 방문을 향해 애원하는 눈길을 던졌다."], ["O'Dowd had closed and locked in his face; but he remembered how scornfully the latter had received him, and after pausing and listening for a brief space at the door, he left it, and resolved to go into the street, for the first time that day.", "그러나 그 부인이 자기를 얼마나 경멸하며 맞이했는지 떠올리고는, 문 앞에서 잠시 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울이다가 문을 떠나 그날 처음으로 거리로 나가기로 마음먹었다."], ["So, seizing a candle, he looked about for his gold-laced cap, and found it lying in its usual place, on a console-table, in the anteroom, placed before a mirror at which Jos used to coquet, always giving his side-locks a twirl, and his cap the proper cock over his eye, before he went forth to make appearance in public.", "그리하여 그는 촛불을 집어 들고 금몰을 두른 모자를 두리번거리며 찾았는데, 그것은 평소 놓이던 자리, 곧 대기실의 콘솔 탁자 위, 조스가 모양을 내곤 하던 거울 앞에 놓여 있었다. 그는 공식 석상에 모습을 드러내러 나가기 전이면 늘 그 거울 앞에서 옆머리를 비비 꼬고 모자를 눈 위로 적당히 비스듬히 얹어 보곤 했던 것이다."], ["Such is the force of habit, that even in the midst of his terror he began mechanically to twiddle with his hair, and arrange the cock of his hat.", "습관의 힘이란 이러해서, 그는 공포의 한가운데서도 기계적으로 머리카락을 만지작거리고 모자의 기울기를 매만지기 시작했다."], ["Then he looked amazed at the pale face in the glass before him, and especially at his mustachios, which had attained a rich growth in the course of near seven weeks, since they had come into the world.", "그러다 그는 앞에 놓인 거울 속의 창백한 얼굴을, 특히 세상에 나온 지 일곱 주가량 되는 동안 무성하게 자라난 콧수염을 보고 화들짝 놀랐다. 패배한 영국군 전체가 위협받고 있다는 학살에 대한 이지도르의 경고를 떠올리며, 그는 생각했다."], ["They WILL mistake me for a military man, thought he, remembering Isidor's warning as to the massacre with which all the defeated British army was threatened; and staggering back to his bedchamber, he began wildly pulling the bell which summoned his valet.", "저자들이 나를 군인으로 오해하겠구나. 그러고는 비틀거리며 침실로 돌아가, 하인을 부르는 종을 미친 듯이 당기기 시작했다."]], [["Isidor answered that summons.", "이지도르가 그 부름에 응답해 왔다."], ["Jos had sunk in a chair--he had torn off his neckcloths, and turned down his collars, and was sitting with both his hands lifted to his throat.", "조스는 의자에 푹 주저앉아 있었다. 그는 목도리를 잡아 뜯고 옷깃을 접어 내린 채, 두 손을 다 목께로 들어 올리고 앉아 있었다."]], [["\"Coupez-moi, Isidor,\"", "\"잘라 줘, 이지도르.\""], ["shouted he; \"vite!", "그가 소리쳤다. \"빨리!"], ["Coupez-moi!\"", "날 잘라 줘!\""]], [["Isidor thought for a moment he had gone mad, and that he wished his valet to cut his throat.", "이지도르는 한순간 주인이 미쳐서 제 하인더러 목을 따 달라는 줄로 생각했다."]], [["\"Les moustaches,\"", "\"콧수염 말이야.\" 조스가 헐떡이며 말했다."], ["gasped Joe; \"les moustaches--coupy, rasy, vite!\"--his French was of this sort--voluble, as we have said, but not remarkable for grammar.", "\"콧수염―잘라, 밀어, 빨리!\" 그의 프랑스어란 이런 식이었다―앞서 말했듯이 거침은 없으나 문법으로 칭찬받을 만한 것은 못 되었다."]], [["Isidor swept off the mustachios in no time with the razor, and heard with inexpressible delight his master's orders that he should fetch a hat and a plain coat.", "이지도르는 면도칼로 순식간에 콧수염을 밀어 버렸고, 모자와 평범한 외투를 가져오라는 주인의 명령을 형언할 수 없는 기쁨으로 들었다."], ["\"Ne porty ploo--habit militair--bonn--bonny a voo, prenny dehors\"--were Jos's words--the coat and cap were at last his property.", "\"이제 안 입어―군복―좋아―네 거야, 가져가\"가 조스의 말이었다. 그 외투와 모자는 마침내 이지도르의 것이 되었다."]], [["This gift being made, Jos selected a plain black coat and waistcoat from his stock, and put on a large white neckcloth, and a plain beaver.", "이 선물을 베푼 뒤, 조스는 가진 옷가지 중에서 수수한 검정 외투와 조끼를 골라 입고, 커다란 흰 목도리를 두르고 평범한 비버 모자를 썼다."], ["If he could have got a shovel hat he would have worn it.", "챙 넓은 성직자 모자를 구할 수 있었다면 그것을 썼을 것이다."], ["As it was, you would have fancied he was a flourishing, large parson of the Church of England.", "그 차림으로 보자면, 누구라도 그를 잘나가는 풍채 좋은 영국 국교회 목사쯤으로 여겼을 것이다."]], [["\"Venny maintenong,\"", "\"이제 와.\""], ["he continued, \"sweevy--ally--party--dong la roo.\"", "그가 말을 이었다. \"따라와―가자―출발―거리로.\""], ["And so having said, he plunged swiftly down the stairs of the house, and passed into the street.", "이렇게 말하고 나서 그는 잽싸게 계단을 곤두박질치듯 내려가 거리로 나섰다."]], [["Although Regulus had vowed that he was the only man of his regiment or of the allied army, almost, who had escaped being cut to pieces by Ney, it appeared that his statement was incorrect, and that a good number more of the supposed victims had survived the massacre.", "레굴루스는 자기가 그 연대에서, 아니 연합군 전체에서 거의 유일하게 네의 칼날에 산산조각 나는 것을 면한 사람이라고 단언했지만, 그 진술은 부정확했던 모양으로, 희생되었으리라 여겨진 자들 가운데 상당수가 그 학살에서 살아남은 듯했다."], ["Many scores of Regulus's comrades had found their way back to Brussels, and all agreeing that they had run away--filled the whole town with an idea of the defeat of the allies.", "레굴루스의 전우 수십 명이 브뤼셀로 돌아오는 길을 찾아냈고, 하나같이 자기가 달아났다고 입을 모았으니, 온 도시가 연합군이 패했다는 생각으로 가득 찼다."], ["The arrival of the French was expected hourly; the panic continued, and preparations for flight went on everywhere.", "시시각각 프랑스군의 도래가 예상되었고, 공황은 계속되었으며, 도주를 위한 채비가 곳곳에서 진행되었다. 말이 없다니!"], ["No horses! thought Jos, in terror.", "조스는 공포에 사로잡혀 생각했다."], ["He made Isidor inquire of scores of persons, whether they had any to lend or sell, and his heart sank within him, at the negative answers returned everywhere.", "그는 이지도르를 시켜 수십 명에게 빌려주거나 팔 말이 있는지 물어보게 했는데, 어디서나 돌아오는 부정의 대답에 가슴이 철렁 내려앉았다. 걸어서 가야 하나?"], ["Should he take the journey on foot? Even fear could not render that ponderous body so active.", "그러나 공포조차도 그 육중한 몸뚱이를 그토록 날래게 만들어 주지는 못했다."]], [["Almost all the hotels occupied by the English in Brussels face the Parc, and Jos wandered irresolutely about in this quarter, with crowds of other people, oppressed as he was by fear and curiosity.", "브뤼셀에서 영국인들이 묵는 호텔은 거의 다 공원을 마주 보고 있었고, 조스는 공포와 호기심에 짓눌린 채 다른 무수한 사람들과 함께 이 일대를 우유부단하게 어슬렁거렸다."], ["Some families he saw more happy than himself, having discovered a team of horses, and rattling through the streets in retreat; others again there were whose case was like his own, and who could not for any bribes or entreaties procure the necessary means of flight.", "자기보다 운 좋은 가족들도 더러 보였으니, 그들은 말 한 무리를 구해 거리를 덜컹거리며 빠져나가는 중이었다. 또 더러는 그와 처지가 같아, 아무리 뇌물을 쓰고 사정을 해도 도주에 필요한 수단을 구하지 못하는 이들도 있었다."], ["Amongst these would-be fugitives, Jos remarked the Lady Bareacres and her daughter, who sate in their carriage in the porte-cochere of their hotel, all their imperials packed, and the only drawback to whose flight was the same want of motive power which kept Jos stationary.", "이 달아나려는 무리 가운데 조스는 베리에이커스 부인과 그 딸을 알아보았는데, 그들은 호텔의 마차 출입구에 세워 둔 마차에 앉아 있었고, 큰 짐가방은 죄다 꾸려져 있었으며, 그들의 도주를 가로막는 단 한 가지 걸림돌이란 조스를 발 묶어 놓은 것과 똑같은 동력의 부재였다."]], [["Rebecca Crawley occupied apartments in this hotel; and had before this period had sundry hostile meetings with the ladies of the Bareacres family.", "리베카 크롤리는 이 호텔에 거처를 두고 있었는데, 그전에 이미 베리에이커스 일가의 부인들과 여러 차례 적대적인 마주침을 겪은 터였다."], ["My Lady Bareacres cut Mrs. Crawley on the stairs when they met by chance; and in all places where the latter's name was mentioned, spoke perseveringly ill of her neighbour.", "베리에이커스 부인은 우연히 계단에서 마주칠 때면 크롤리 부인을 본체만체했고, 후자의 이름이 거론되는 자리마다 그 이웃을 끈질기게 험담했다."], ["The Countess was shocked at the familiarity of General Tufto with the aide-de-camp's wife.", "백작 부인은 터프토 장군이 부관의 아내와 허물없이 구는 것에 충격을 받았다."], ["The Lady Blanche avoided her as if she had been an infectious disease.", "블랜치 양은 그녀를 전염병이라도 되는 양 피했다."], ["Only the Earl himself kept up a sly occasional acquaintance with her, when out of the jurisdiction of his ladies.", "오직 백작 본인만이 자기 집안 여자들의 관할에서 벗어나 있을 때면 이따금 그녀와 은근한 친분을 이어 갔다."]], [["Rebecca had her revenge now upon these insolent enemies. It became known in the hotel that Captain Crawley's horses had been left behind, and when the panic began, Lady Bareacres condescended to send her maid to the Captain's wife with her Ladyship's compliments, and a desire to know the price of Mrs.", "리베카는 이제 이 오만한 적들에게 복수할 기회를 잡았다."], ["Crawley's horses. Mrs.", "크롤리 대위의 말들이 남겨져 있다는 사실이 호텔에 알려졌고, 공황이 시작되자 베리에이커스 부인은 황송하게도 제 하녀를 대위 부인에게 보내 부인의 안부 인사와 함께 크롤리 부인의 말값을 알고 싶다는 뜻을 전했다."], ["Crawley returned a note with her compliments, and an intimation that it was not her custom to transact bargains with ladies' maids.", "크롤리 부인은 안부 인사와 함께, 자기는 하녀와 흥정하는 법이 없다는 뜻을 적은 쪽지로 답했다."]], [["This curt reply brought the Earl in person to Becky's apartment; but he could get no more success than the first ambassador.", "이 무뚝뚝한 답장에 백작이 몸소 베키의 거처로 찾아왔지만, 첫 번째 사절보다 나을 것이 없었다. \"나한테 하녀를 보내다니!\""], ["\"Send a lady's maid to ME!\" Mrs.", "크롤리 부인이 노발대발하며 외쳤다."], ["Crawley cried in great anger; \"why didn't my Lady Bareacres tell me to go and saddle the horses!", "\"차라리 베리에이커스 부인이 나더러 가서 말에 안장을 얹으라고 하시지 그래요!"], ["Is it her Ladyship that wants to escape, or her Ladyship's femme de chambre?\"", "도망치고 싶은 게 부인이에요, 아니면 부인의 하녀예요?\""], ["And this was all the answer that the Earl bore back to his Countess.", "그리고 이것이 백작이 자기 부인에게 가지고 돌아간 대답의 전부였다."]], [["What will not necessity do?", "궁하면 못 할 일이 무엇이랴?"], ["The Countess herself actually came to wait upon Mrs. Crawley on the failure of her second envoy.", "두 번째 사절마저 실패하자 백작 부인이 친히 크롤리 부인을 찾아왔다."], ["She entreated her to name her own price; she even offered to invite Becky to Bareacres House, if the latter would but give her the means of returning to that residence.", "그녀는 부르는 값을 직접 매겨 보라고 간청했다. 심지어 자기 저택으로 돌아갈 수단만 마련해 준다면 베키를 베리에이커스 저택으로 초대하겠다고까지 했다."], ["Mrs. Crawley sneered at her.", "크롤리 부인은 그녀를 비웃었다."]], [["\"I don't want to be waited on by bailiffs in livery,\"", "\"제복 입은 빚쟁이의 시중을 받고 싶지는 않네요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said; \"you will never get back though most probably--at least not you and your diamonds together.", "\"하긴 부인은 십중팔구 돌아가지도 못하실 텐데요. 적어도 부인과 다이아몬드가 함께는 말이죠."], ["The French will have those.", "그건 프랑스군 차지가 될 테니까요."], ["They will be here in two hours, and I shall be half way to Ghent by that time.", "두 시간 안에 여기 들이닥칠 테고, 그때쯤이면 나는 헨트로 가는 길 절반쯤에 가 있을 거예요."], ["I would not sell you my horses, no, not for the two largest diamonds that your Ladyship wore at the ball.\"", "부인께 내 말을 팔지는 않겠어요. 안 팔아요, 무도회에서 부인이 걸쳤던 가장 큰 다이아몬드 두 개를 준다 해도 말이죠.\""], ["Lady Bareacres trembled with rage and terror.", "베리에이커스 부인은 분노와 공포로 부들부들 떨었다."], ["The diamonds were sewed into her habit, and secreted in my Lord's padding and boots.", "그 다이아몬드들은 그녀의 옷에 꿰매어져 있었고, 백작의 패딩과 장화 속에 숨겨져 있었다."], ["\"Woman, the diamonds are at the banker's, and I WILL have the horses,\"", "\"이 계집아, 다이아몬드는 은행에 있고, 나는 기어이 그 말을 가져가야겠다.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["Rebecca laughed in her face.", "리베카는 그 얼굴에 대고 웃었다."], ["The infuriate Countess went below, and sate in her carriage; her maid, her courier, and her husband were sent once more through the town, each to look for cattle; and woe betide those who came last!", "격분한 백작 부인은 아래층으로 내려가 마차에 앉았다. 그녀의 하녀와 심부름꾼과 남편이 다시 한번 도시를 누비러 보내졌으니, 저마다 말을 찾으러였다."], ["Her Ladyship was resolved on departing the very instant the horses arrived from any quarter--with her husband or without him.", "그리고 가장 늦게 돌아오는 자에게 화 있을진저! 부인은 어느 쪽에서든 말이 도착하는 바로 그 순간 떠나기로―남편이 있든 없든―단단히 마음먹고 있었다."]], [["Rebecca had the pleasure of seeing her Ladyship in the horseless carriage, and keeping her eyes fixed upon her, and bewailing, in the loudest tone of voice, the Countess's perplexities.", "리베카는 말 없는 마차에 앉은 부인을 바라보며, 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 않고 더없이 큰 목소리로 백작 부인의 곤경을 애통해하는 즐거움을 누렸다."], ["\"Not to be able to get horses!\"", "\"말을 못 구하시다니!\""], ["she said, \"and to have all those diamonds sewed into the carriage cushions!", "그녀가 말했다. \"게다가 그 다이아몬드를 죄다 마차 방석에 꿰매어 두셨다니!"], ["What a prize it will be for the French when they come!--the carriage and the diamonds, I mean; not the lady!\"", "프랑스군이 오면 얼마나 횡재가 되겠어요! 마차와 다이아몬드 말이에요."], ["She gave this information to the landlord, to the servants, to the guests, and the innumerable stragglers about the courtyard.", "부인 말고요!\" 그녀는 이 소식을 주인장에게, 하인들에게, 손님들에게, 그리고 안뜰을 어슬렁대는 무수한 부랑자들에게 떠벌렸다."], ["Lady Bareacres could have shot her from the carriage window.", "베리에이커스 부인은 마차 창문에서 그녀를 쏘아 죽이고 싶을 지경이었다."]], [["It was while enjoying the humiliation of her enemy that Rebecca caught sight of Jos, who made towards her directly he perceived her.", "리베카가 이렇게 적의 굴욕을 즐기고 있을 때, 그녀의 눈에 조스가 들어왔다. 그는 그녀를 알아보자마자 곧장 그녀에게로 다가왔다."]], [["That altered, frightened, fat face, told his secret well enough.", "저 달라지고 겁에 질린 살진 얼굴이 그의 속내를 충분히 일러 주었다."], ["He too wanted to fly, and was on the look-out for the means of escape.", "그 역시 달아나고 싶어 했고, 도주의 수단을 두리번거리고 있었던 것이다."], ["\"HE shall buy my horses,\"", "\"저자가 내 말들을 사게 만들어야지.\""], ["thought Rebecca, \"and I'll ride the mare.\"", "리베카는 생각했다. \"그리고 나는 암말을 타면 되겠어.\""]], [["Jos walked up to his friend, and put the question for the hundredth time during the past hour, \"Did she know where horses were to be had?\"", "조스는 자기 벗에게로 걸어가, 지난 한 시간 동안 백 번째로 그 질문을 던졌다. 어디서 말을 구할 수 있는지 아십니까?"]], [["\"What, YOU fly?\"", "\"아니, 당신이 달아난다고요?\""], ["said Rebecca, with a laugh.", "리베카가 웃으며 말했다."], ["\"I thought you were the champion of all the ladies, Mr. Sedley.\"", "\"나는 당신이 모든 숙녀의 수호자인 줄 알았는데요, 세들리 씨.\""]], [["\"I--I'm not a military man,\"", "\"나는―나는 군인이 아니라서요.\""], ["gasped he.", "그가 헐떡이며 말했다."]], [["\"And Amelia?--Who is to protect that poor little sister of yours?\"", "\"그럼 애밀리아는요? 그 가엾은 여동생은 누가 지켜 주죠?\""], ["asked Rebecca.", "리베카가 물었다."], ["\"You surely would not desert her?\"", "\"설마 그 애를 버리지는 않으시겠죠?\""]], [["\"What good can I do her, suppose--suppose the enemy arrive?\"", "\"내가 그 애한테 무슨 도움이 되겠어요, 만약―만약 적이 들이닥친다면요?\" 조스가 대답했다."], ["Jos answered.", "\"그자들은 여자들은 살려 줄 겁니다."], ["\"They'll spare the women; but my man tells me that they have taken an oath to give no quarter to the men--the dastardly cowards.\"", "하지만 내 하인 말로는, 남자들에게는 절대 자비를 베풀지 않겠다고 맹세했다는군요. 그 비겁한 겁쟁이들이 말이에요.\""]], [["\"Horrid!\"", "\"끔찍하기도 해라!\""], ["cried Rebecca, enjoying his perplexity.", "리베카가 그의 곤혹을 즐기며 외쳤다."]], [["\"Besides, I don't want to desert her,\"", "\"게다가 나는 그 애를 버리고 싶지 않아요.\""], ["cried the brother.", "오라비가 외쳤다."], ["\"She SHAN'T be deserted.", "\"그 애를 버려서는 안 되죠."], ["There is a seat for her in my carriage, and one for you, dear Mrs.", "내 마차에 그 애 자리가 하나 있고, 당신 자리도 하나 있어요, 친애하는 크롤리 부인, 만약 함께 가 주신다면요."], ["Crawley, if you will come; and if we can get horses--\"", "그리고 말만 구할 수 있다면―\""], ["sighed he--", "그가 한숨지었다."]], [["\"I have two to sell,\" the lady said.", "\"내가 팔 말이 두 마리 있어요.\""], ["Jos could have flung himself into her arms at the news.", "부인이 말했다. 조스는 그 소식에 그녀의 품에라도 뛰어들 뻔했다."], ["\"Get the carriage, Isidor,\"", "\"마차를 가져와, 이지도르.\""], ["he cried; \"we've found them--we have found them.\"", "그가 외쳤다. \"찾았다―말을 찾았어.\""]], [["\"My horses never were in harness,\"", "\"내 말들은 마구를 채워 본 적이 없어요.\""], ["added the lady.", "부인이 덧붙였다."], ["\"Bullfinch would kick the carriage to pieces, if you put him in the traces.\"", "\"불핀치는 멍에를 씌우면 마차를 발길질로 박살 내 버릴 거예요.\""]], [["\"But he is quiet to ride?\"", "\"하지만 타기에는 얌전한가요?\""], ["asked the civilian.", "민간인이 물었다."]], [["\"As quiet as a lamb, and as fast as a hare,\"", "\"양처럼 얌전하고 토끼처럼 빠르죠.\""], ["answered Rebecca.", "리베카가 대답했다."]], [["\"Do you think he is up to my weight?\"", "\"내 몸무게를 감당할 수 있을까요?\""], ["Jos said.", "조스가 말했다."], ["He was already on his back, in imagination, without ever so much as a thought for poor Amelia.", "그는 가엾은 애밀리아는 까맣게 잊은 채 벌써 상상 속에서 그 말 등에 올라타 있었다."], ["What person who loved a horse-speculation could resist such a temptation?", "말 흥정을 좋아하는 사람이라면 누군들 이런 유혹을 떨칠 수 있겠는가?"]], [["In reply, Rebecca asked him to come into her room, whither he followed her quite breathless to conclude the bargain.", "대답 대신 리베카는 그에게 자기 방으로 들어오라고 청했고, 그는 흥정을 매듭짓고자 숨을 헐떡이며 그녀를 따라 들어갔다."], ["Jos seldom spent a half-hour in his life which cost him so much money.", "조스가 평생 보낸 시간 중에 그토록 큰돈이 든 삼십 분은 좀처럼 없었다."], ["Rebecca, measuring the value of the goods which she had for sale by Jos's eagerness to purchase, as well as by the scarcity of the article, put upon her horses a price so prodigious as to make even the civilian draw back.", "리베카는 팔 물건의 값을 조스가 사고 싶어 안달하는 정도와 그 물건의 희소함으로 가늠하여, 자기 말들에 어마어마한 값을 매겨 그 민간인조차 움찔 물러서게 만들었다."], ["\"She would sell both or neither,\" she said, resolutely.", "\"두 마리 다 사든지 아니면 한 마리도 안 팔든지 하겠어요.\""], ["Rawdon had ordered her not to part with them for a price less than that which she specified.", "그녀가 단호하게 말했다. 로던이 자기가 정한 값보다 싸게는 절대 내주지 말라고 명했다는 것이었다."], ["Lord Bareacres below would give her the same money--and with all her love and regard for the Sedley family, her dear Mr. Joseph must conceive that poor people must live--nobody, in a word, could be more affectionate, but more firm about the matter of business.", "아래층의 베리에이커스 경도 같은 값을 쳐줄 거라고, 그리고 세들리 일가를 향한 온갖 애정과 존경에도 불구하고, 친애하는 조지프 씨도 가난한 사람은 먹고살아야 한다는 것쯤은 이해해 주셔야 한다고, 한마디로 그녀보다 더 다정한 사람은 없을 테지만 사업 문제에서만큼은 그녀보다 더 단호한 사람도 없을 거라는 것이었다."]], [["Jos ended by agreeing, as might be supposed of him.", "조스는 결국, 그다운 일이지만, 응낙하고 말았다. 그가 그녀에게 치러야 할 액수가 너무도 커서 그는 기한을 늦춰 달라고 청할 수밖에 없었다."], ["The sum he had to give her was so large that he was obliged to ask for time; so large as to be a little fortune to Rebecca, who rapidly calculated that with this sum, and the sale of the residue of Rawdon's effects, and her pension as a widow should he fall, she would now be absolutely independent of the world, and might look her weeds steadily in the face.", "그것은 리베카에게는 작은 재산이라 할 만큼 큰 액수였으니, 그녀는 재빨리 계산해 보았다. 이 돈에다가 로던의 남은 물건들을 판 돈, 그리고 그가 쓰러질 경우 받을 미망인 연금까지 더하면, 이제 자기는 세상으로부터 완전히 독립하여 상복을 입은 자기 모습도 흔들림 없이 마주할 수 있겠다고 말이다."]], [["Once or twice in the day she certainly had herself thought about flying.", "그날 하루에도 한두 번쯤은 그녀 자신도 분명 도주를 생각해 보았다."], ["But her reason gave her better counsel.", "그러나 이성이 그녀에게 더 나은 조언을 들려주었다."], ["\"Suppose the French do come,\"", "\"정말로 프랑스군이 온다 한들,\""], ["thought Becky, \"what can they do to a poor officer's widow?", "베키는 생각했다. \"가난한 장교의 미망인에게 무슨 짓을 할 수 있겠어?"], ["Bah!", "흥!"], ["the times of sacks and sieges are over.", "약탈하고 포위하던 시대는 이제 지났어."], ["We shall be let to go home quietly, or I may live pleasantly abroad with a snug little income.\"", "우리는 조용히 집으로 돌려보내질 테고, 아니면 나는 아담한 수입으로 외국에서 즐겁게 살 수도 있겠지.\""]], [["Meanwhile Jos and Isidor went off to the stables to inspect the newly purchased cattle.", "그러는 사이 조스와 이지도르는 새로 산 말을 살펴보러 마구간으로 갔다."], ["Jos bade his man saddle the horses at once.", "조스는 하인에게 당장 말에 안장을 얹으라 일렀다."], ["He would ride away that very night, that very hour.", "그는 바로 그날 밤, 바로 그 시각에 말을 타고 떠날 작정이었다."], ["And he left the valet busy in getting the horses ready, and went homewards himself to prepare for his departure.", "그러고는 하인에게 말 채비를 시켜 놓고 자기는 떠날 준비를 하러 집으로 향했다."], ["It must be secret.", "이는 비밀이어야 했다."], ["He would go to his chamber by the back entrance.", "그는 뒷문으로 해서 자기 방으로 갈 참이었다."], ["He did not care to face Mrs. O'Dowd and Amelia, and own to them that he was about to run.", "그는 오다우드 부인과 애밀리아를 마주하고서 자기가 달아나려 한다고 실토할 마음이 내키지 않았던 것이다."]], [["By the time Jos's bargain with Rebecca was completed, and his horses had been visited and examined, it was almost morning once more.", "조스가 리베카와의 흥정을 매듭짓고 자기 말들을 둘러보고 살펴보았을 무렵에는 다시 거의 아침이 되어 있었다."], ["But though midnight was long passed, there was no rest for the city; the people were up, the lights in the houses flamed, crowds were still about the doors, and the streets were busy.", "그러나 자정이 한참 지났는데도 도시에는 쉴 틈이 없었다. 사람들은 깨어 있었고, 집집마다 불빛이 타올랐으며, 군중은 여전히 문간을 에워싸고 거리는 분주했다."], ["Rumours of various natures went still from mouth to mouth: one report averred that the Prussians had been utterly defeated; another that it was the English who had been attacked and conquered: a third that the latter had held their ground.", "갖가지 소문이 여전히 입에서 입으로 옮겨 다녔다. 한 소문은 프로이센군이 완전히 패했다 주장했고, 또 하나는 공격당해 정복된 것이 영국군이라 했으며, 세 번째 것은 영국군이 진지를 지켜 냈다고 했다."], ["This last rumour gradually got strength.", "이 마지막 소문이 차츰 힘을 얻어 갔다."], ["No Frenchmen had made their appearance.", "프랑스군은 모습을 드러내지 않았다."], ["Stragglers had come in from the army bringing reports more and more favourable: at last an aide-de-camp actually reached Brussels with despatches for the Commandant of the place, who placarded presently through the town an official announcement of the success of the allies at Quatre Bras, and the entire repulse of the French under Ney after a six hours'", "낙오병들이 점점 더 유리한 소식을 가지고 군대에서 들어왔다. 마침내 한 부관이 실제로 브뤼셀에 도착하여 이 도시의 사령관에게 전갈을 전했고, 사령관은 이내 도시 곳곳에 카트르브라에서 연합군이 거둔 승리와 여섯 시간의 전투 끝에 네 휘하의 프랑스군을 완전히 격퇴했다는 공식 발표를 내붙였다."], ["battle. The aide-de-camp must have arrived sometime while Jos and Rebecca were making their bargain together, or the latter was inspecting his purchase.", "그 부관은 조스와 리베카가 흥정을 벌이거나 그가 사들인 말을 둘러보던 어느 사이에 도착했음이 틀림없었다."], ["When he reached his own hotel, he found a score of its numerous inhabitants on the threshold discoursing of the news; there was no doubt as to its truth.", "그가 자기 호텔에 이르렀을 때, 그 안에 묵는 수많은 거주민 중 스무 명가량이 문턱에 모여 그 소식을 논하고 있었다. 그것이 사실임에는 의심의 여지가 없었다."], ["And he went up to communicate it to the ladies under his charge.", "그래서 그는 자기가 책임진 부인들에게 그것을 알리러 올라갔다."], ["He did not think it was necessary to tell them how he had intended to take leave of them, how he had bought horses, and what a price he had paid for them.", "다만 자기가 어떻게 그들과 작별하려 했는지, 어떻게 말을 샀는지, 그 값으로 얼마를 치렀는지는 그들에게 말할 필요가 없다고 여겼다."]], [["But success or defeat was a minor matter to them, who had only thought for the safety of those they loved.", "그러나 사랑하는 이들의 안위만을 생각하는 그들에게 승리냐 패배냐는 사소한 문제였다."], ["Amelia, at the news of the victory, became still more agitated even than before.", "애밀리아는 승전 소식에 전보다도 한층 더 동요했다."], ["She was for going that moment to the army.", "그녀는 당장이라도 군대로 가겠다고 했다."], ["She besought her brother with tears to conduct her thither.", "눈물을 흘리며 오라비에게 자기를 그리로 데려다 달라고 간청했다."], ["Her doubts and terrors reached their paroxysm; and the poor girl, who for many hours had been plunged into stupor, raved and ran hither and thither in hysteric insanity--a piteous sight.", "그녀의 의심과 공포는 극에 달했고, 여러 시간 혼수에 가까운 상태에 빠져 있던 가엾은 처녀는 발작적인 광란에 사로잡혀 이리저리 헛소리를 하며 뛰어다녔으니, 보기에도 가련한 광경이었다."], ["No man writhing in pain on the hard-fought field fifteen miles off, where lay, after their struggles, so many of the brave--no man suffered more keenly than this poor harmless victim of the war.", "십오 마일 떨어진 그 격전지에서―숱한 용사가 분투 끝에 누워 있는 그곳에서―고통에 몸부림치는 그 어떤 사내도, 전쟁의 이 가엾고 죄 없는 희생자보다 더 모질게 괴로워하지는 않았다."], ["Jos could not bear the sight of her pain.", "조스는 그녀의 고통을 차마 볼 수가 없었다."], ["He left his sister in the charge of her stouter female companion, and descended once more to the threshold of the hotel, where everybody still lingered, and talked, and waited for more news.", "그는 누이를 더 건장한 여자 동무에게 맡겨 두고, 다시 한번 호텔 문턱으로 내려갔다. 거기에는 여전히 모두가 서성이며 이야기를 나누고 더 많은 소식을 기다리고 있었다."]], [["It grew to be broad daylight as they stood here, and fresh news began to arrive from the war, brought by men who had been actors in the scene.", "그들이 그곳에 서 있는 사이 날이 훤히 밝았고, 전장의 실제 당사자였던 이들이 가져온 새로운 소식이 도착하기 시작했다."], ["Wagons and long country carts laden with wounded came rolling into the town; ghastly groans came from within them, and haggard faces looked up sadly from out of the straw.", "부상병을 가득 실은 짐마차와 기다란 시골 수레들이 덜컹거리며 도시로 굴러 들어왔다. 그 안에서는 끔찍한 신음 소리가 새어 나왔고, 초췌한 얼굴들이 짚더미 사이로 슬프게 올려다보았다."], ["Jos Sedley was looking at one of these carriages with a painful curiosity--the moans of the people within were frightful--the wearied horses could hardly pull the cart.", "조스 세들리는 고통스러운 호기심으로 그런 수레 하나를 바라보고 있었다. 안에 탄 사람들의 신음은 무시무시했고, 지친 말들은 수레를 좀처럼 끌지 못했다."], ["\"Stop!", "\"멈춰요!"], ["stop!\"", "멈춰요!\""], ["a feeble voice cried from the straw, and the carriage stopped opposite Mr. Sedley's hotel.", "짚더미 속에서 가냘픈 목소리가 외쳤고, 수레는 세들리 씨의 호텔 맞은편에 멈춰 섰다."]], [["\"It is George, I know it is!\"", "\"조지예요, 분명 조지라고요!\""], ["cried Amelia, rushing in a moment to the balcony, with a pallid face and loose flowing hair.", "애밀리아가 외치며, 창백한 얼굴에 머리를 풀어 헤친 채 순식간에 발코니로 달려 나왔다."], ["It was not George, however, but it was the next best thing: it was news of him.", "그러나 조지는 아니었다. 다만 그다음으로 좋은 것이었으니, 그에 관한 소식이었다."]], [["It was poor Tom Stubble, who had marched out of Brussels so gallantly twenty-four hours before, bearing the colours of the regiment, which he had defended very gallantly upon the field.", "그것은 가엾은 톰 스터블이었다. 그는 스물네 시간 전 연대기를 들고 그토록 늠름하게 브뤼셀을 행군해 나갔고, 전장에서 그 깃발을 더없이 용감하게 지켜 냈다."], ["A French lancer had speared the young ensign in the leg, who fell, still bravely holding to his flag.", "프랑스 창기병 하나가 이 젊은 기수의 다리를 창으로 찔렀고, 그는 쓰러지면서도 여전히 용감하게 깃발을 부여잡고 있었다."], ["At the conclusion of the engagement, a place had been found for the poor boy in a cart, and he had been brought back to Brussels.", "교전이 끝나자 사람들이 이 가엾은 소년에게 수레의 자리 하나를 마련해 주었고, 그는 브뤼셀로 실려 돌아온 것이었다."]], [["\"Mr. Sedley, Mr.", "\"세들리 씨, 세들리 씨!\""], ["Sedley!\" cried the boy, faintly, and Jos came up almost frightened at the appeal.", "소년이 가냘프게 외쳤고, 조스는 그 부름에 거의 겁먹은 채로 다가갔다."], ["He had not at first distinguished who it was that called him.", "그는 처음에는 자기를 부른 사람이 누구인지 알아보지 못했다."]], [["Little Tom Stubble held out his hot and feeble hand.", "어린 톰 스터블은 뜨겁고 힘없는 손을 내밀었다."], ["\"I'm to be taken in here,\"", "\"저는 여기로 들어가게 될 거예요.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"Osborne--and--and Dobbin said I was; and you are to give the man two napoleons: my mother will pay you.\"", "\"오스본이―그리고―그리고 도빈이 그랬어요. 그리고 마부에게 나폴레옹 금화 두 닢을 주셔야 해요."], ["This young fellow's thoughts, during the long feverish hours passed in the cart, had been wandering to his father's parsonage which he had quitted only a few months before, and he had sometimes forgotten his pain in that delirium.", "우리 어머니가 갚으실 거예요.\" 수레에서 열에 들떠 보낸 그 긴 시간 동안, 이 젊은 친구의 생각은 불과 몇 달 전에 떠나온 아버지의 목사관을 헤매고 있었고, 그 헛소리 속에서 그는 이따금 고통조차 잊었던 것이다."]], [["The hotel was large, and the people kind, and all the inmates of the cart were taken in and placed on various couches.", "호텔은 넓었고 사람들은 친절했으므로, 수레에 탄 이들은 모두 안으로 옮겨져 갖가지 침상에 뉘었다."], ["The young ensign was conveyed upstairs to Osborne's quarters.", "젊은 기수는 위층 오스본의 거처로 옮겨졌다."], ["Amelia and the Major's wife had rushed down to him, when the latter had recognised him from the balcony.", "발코니에서 그를 알아본 소령의 부인과 애밀리아가 그에게로 달려 내려와 있었다."], ["You may fancy the feelings of these women when they were told that the day was over, and both their husbands were safe; in what mute rapture Amelia fell on her good friend's neck, and embraced her; in what a grateful passion of prayer she fell on her knees, and thanked the Power which had saved her husband.", "그날의 전투가 끝났고 두 사람의 남편이 모두 무사하다는 말을 들었을 때 이 여인들의 심정이 어떠했을지 짐작이 갈 것이다. 애밀리아가 어떤 말 없는 환희에 젖어 그 좋은 벗의 목에 매달려 끌어안았는지, 어떤 감사에 사무친 기도의 격정으로 무릎을 꿇고 자기 남편을 구해 주신 그분께 감사드렸는지를 말이다."]], [["Our young lady, in her fevered and nervous condition, could have had no more salutary medicine prescribed for her by any physician than that which chance put in her way.", "열에 들뜨고 신경이 곤두선 우리 아가씨에게는, 그 어떤 의사가 처방해 준다 한들 우연이 그녀 앞에 놓아 준 것보다 더 약이 되는 것은 없었을 것이다."], ["She and Mrs. O'Dowd watched incessantly by the wounded lad, whose pains were very severe, and in the duty thus forced upon her, Amelia had not time to brood over her personal anxieties, or to give herself up to her own fears and forebodings after her wont.", "그녀와 오다우드 부인은 고통이 몹시 심한 부상당한 소년의 곁을 잠시도 떠나지 않고 지켰고, 그리하여 떠맡게 된 이 임무 덕분에 애밀리아는 제 개인적인 근심을 곱씹거나 평소 버릇대로 제 공포와 불길한 예감에 자신을 내맡길 겨를이 없었다."], ["The young patient told in his simple fashion the events of the day, and the actions of our friends of the gallant --th.", "젊은 환자는 그 소박한 방식으로 그날의 사건들과, 그 용맹한 ××연대에 속한 우리 벗들의 활약을 들려주었다."], ["They had suffered severely.", "그들은 큰 피해를 입었다."], ["They had lost very many officers and men.", "숱한 장교와 병사를 잃었다."], ["The Major's horse had been shot under him as the regiment charged, and they all thought that O'Dowd was gone, and that Dobbin had got his majority, until on their return from the charge to their old ground, the Major was discovered seated on Pyramus's carcase, refreshing him-self from a case-bottle.", "연대가 돌격할 때 소령은 타고 있던 말이 그 밑에서 총에 맞아 쓰러졌고, 다들 오다우드가 죽었으며 도빈이 소령 자리를 물려받았으리라 여겼다. 그러나 돌격을 마치고 원래 자리로 돌아와 보니, 소령은 죽은 피라무스의 시체 위에 걸터앉아 휴대용 술병으로 기운을 차리고 있었다."], ["It was Captain Osborne that cut down the French lancer who had speared the ensign.", "그 기수를 창으로 찌른 프랑스 창기병을 베어 쓰러뜨린 것은 오스본 대위였다."], ["Amelia turned so pale at the notion, that Mrs. O'Dowd stopped the young ensign in this story.", "이 대목에서 애밀리아가 어찌나 새파랗게 질렸던지, 오다우드 부인은 젊은 기수의 이야기를 멈춰 세웠다."], ["And it was Captain Dobbin who at the end of the day, though wounded himself, took up the lad in his arms and carried him to the surgeon, and thence to the cart which was to bring him back to Brussels. And it was he who promised the driver two louis if he would make his way to Mr.", "그리고 그날이 저물 무렵, 자기도 부상을 입은 몸으로 소년을 두 팔에 안아 군의관에게로, 다시 그를 브뤼셀로 실어다 줄 수레로 데려간 것은 도빈 대위였다."], ["Sedley's hotel in the city; and tell Mrs. Captain Osborne that the action was over, and that her husband was unhurt and well.", "그리고 마부에게 도시에 있는 세들리 씨의 호텔까지 길을 찾아가 주면 루이 금화 두 닢을 주겠노라 약속하고, 오스본 대위 부인에게 전투가 끝났으며 남편은 다치지 않고 무사하다고 전해 달라 한 것도 바로 그였다."]], [["\"Indeed, but he has a good heart that William Dobbin,\" Mrs.", "\"정말이지, 그 윌리엄 도빈은 마음씨가 참 곱답니다.\""], ["O'Dowd said, \"though he is always laughing at me.\"", "오다우드 부인이 말했다. \"늘 나를 보고 웃어 대긴 하지만요.\""]], [["Young Stubble vowed there was not such another officer in the army, and never ceased his praises of the senior captain, his modesty, his kindness, and his admirable coolness in the field.", "어린 스터블은 군대에 그런 장교는 또 없다고 단언하며, 그 선임 대위의 겸손함과 친절함과 전장에서의 감탄스러운 침착함에 대한 칭찬을 그칠 줄 몰랐다."], ["To these parts of the conversation, Amelia lent a very distracted attention: it was only when George was spoken of that she listened, and when he was not mentioned, she thought about him.", "이런 대목의 이야기에 애밀리아는 몹시 산만한 주의밖에 기울이지 못했다. 그녀가 귀를 기울이는 것은 오로지 조지의 이야기가 나올 때뿐이었고, 그가 거론되지 않을 때면 그녀는 그를 생각하고 있었다."]], [["In tending her patient, and in thinking of the wonderful escapes of the day before, her second day passed away not too slowly with Amelia.", "환자를 돌보는 일과, 전날의 그 놀라운 모면들을 생각하는 일로, 애밀리아의 둘째 날은 너무 더디지 않게 지나갔다."], ["There was only one man in the army for her: and as long as he was well, it must be owned that its movements interested her little.", "그녀에게 군대 안에 사내라고는 오직 하나뿐이었다. 그리고 그가 무사한 한, 군대의 움직임이 그녀의 관심을 거의 끌지 못했다는 것은 인정해야 하리라."], ["All the reports which Jos brought from the streets fell very vaguely on her ears; though they were sufficient to give that timorous gentleman, and many other people then in Brussels, every disquiet.", "조스가 거리에서 가져온 온갖 소식은 그녀의 귀에 매우 어렴풋하게만 들렸다. 비록 그 소식이 저 겁 많은 신사와 그때 브뤼셀에 있던 숱한 사람들에게는 온갖 불안을 안겨 주기에 충분했지만 말이다."], ["The French had been repulsed certainly, but it was after a severe and doubtful struggle, and with only a division of the French army.", "프랑스군이 격퇴된 것은 분명했으나, 그것은 혹독하고 승패를 가늠하기 어려운 격전 끝에, 그것도 프랑스군의 한 개 사단만을 상대로 한 일이었다."], ["The Emperor, with the main body, was away at Ligny, where he had utterly annihilated the Prussians, and was now free to bring his whole force to bear upon the allies.", "본대를 거느린 황제는 리니에 가 있었는데, 거기서 프로이센군을 완전히 섬멸하고, 이제 전군을 연합군에 쏟아부을 수 있게 자유로운 처지였다."], ["The Duke of Wellington was retreating upon the capital, and a great battle must be fought under its walls probably, of which the chances were more than doubtful.", "웰링턴 공작은 수도를 향해 후퇴하고 있었고, 십중팔구 그 성벽 아래서 대전투를 치러야 할 터인데 그 승산이란 매우 미덥지 못했다."], ["The Duke of Wellington had but twenty thousand British troops on whom he could rely, for the Germans were raw militia, the Belgians disaffected, and with this handful his Grace had to resist a hundred and fifty thousand men that had broken into Belgium under Napoleon.", "웰링턴 공작에게는 믿을 만한 영국군이 이만 명뿐이었으니, 독일군은 풋내기 민병대요 벨기에군은 불만에 차 있는 터라, 이 한 줌의 병력으로 공작 각하는 나폴레옹의 지휘 아래 벨기에로 쳐들어온 십오만 대군에 맞서야 했다. 나폴레옹의 지휘라니!"], ["Under Napoleon! What warrior was there, however famous and skilful, that could fight at odds with him?", "아무리 이름났고 솜씨 좋은 무장이라 한들, 그를 상대로 불리한 형세에서 싸울 수 있는 자가 어디 있단 말인가?"]], [["Jos thought of all these things, and trembled.", "조스는 이 모든 것을 생각하며 떨었다."], ["So did all the rest of Brussels--where people felt that the fight of the day before was but the prelude to the greater combat which was imminent.", "브뤼셀의 나머지 사람들도 다 마찬가지였으니, 사람들은 전날의 싸움이 임박한 더 큰 전투의 서막에 불과하다고 느끼고 있었다."], ["One of the armies opposed to the Emperor was scattered to the winds already.", "황제에 맞선 두 군대 중 하나는 이미 바람결에 흩어져 버렸다."], ["The few English that could be brought to resist him would perish at their posts, and the conqueror would pass over their bodies into the city.", "그에게 맞서 싸우러 나설 수 있는 얼마 안 되는 영국군은 제 자리에서 스러질 것이고, 정복자는 그들의 시체를 밟고 도시로 들어설 것이었다."], ["Woe be to those whom he found there!", "그가 거기서 발견하는 자들에게 화 있을진저!"], ["Addresses were prepared, public functionaries assembled and debated secretly, apartments were got ready, and tricoloured banners and triumphal emblems manufactured, to welcome the arrival of His Majesty the Emperor and King.", "환영의 인사말이 준비되었고, 공직자들이 모여 은밀히 의논했으며, 거처들이 갖춰지고, 황제 폐하이자 국왕이신 분의 도래를 맞이하기 위해 삼색기와 개선의 상징물들이 만들어졌다."]], [["The emigration still continued, and wherever families could find means of departure, they fled.", "피난은 여전히 계속되었고, 가족들은 떠날 방도를 찾을 수 있는 곳이면 어디서든 달아났다."], ["When Jos, on the afternoon of the 17th of June, went to Rebecca's hotel, he found that the great Bareacres' carriage had at length rolled away from the porte-cochere.", "조스가 6월 17일 오후에 리베카의 호텔로 가 보니, 그 으리으리한 베리에이커스의 마차가 마침내 마차 출입구를 빠져나가 굴러간 뒤였다."], ["The Earl had procured a pair of horses somehow, in spite of Mrs. Crawley, and was rolling on the road to Ghent.", "백작은 크롤리 부인에도 아랑곳없이 어떻게든 말 한 쌍을 손에 넣어, 헨트로 가는 길을 굴러가고 있었다."], ["Louis the Desired was getting ready his portmanteau in that city, too.", "백성들이 그토록 고대하던 루이 왕도 그 도시에서 자기 여행 가방을 꾸리고 있는 참이었다."], ["It seemed as if Misfortune was never tired of worrying into motion that unwieldy exile.", "불운은 저 굼뜬 망명객을 들쑤셔 몰아대는 일에 도무지 지칠 줄을 모르는 듯했다."]], [["Jos felt that the delay of yesterday had been only a respite, and that his dearly bought horses must of a surety be put into requisition.", "조스는 어제의 지체가 한낱 잠깐의 유예에 지나지 않았으며, 비싼 값을 치르고 산 자기 말들을 분명 곧 동원해야 하리라는 것을 느꼈다."], ["His agonies were very severe all this day.", "그날 내내 그의 고뇌는 몹시 혹독했다."], ["As long as there was an English army between Brussels and Napoleon, there was no need of immediate flight; but he had his horses brought from their distant stables, to the stables in the court-yard of the hotel where he lived; so that they might be under his own eyes, and beyond the risk of violent abduction.", "브뤼셀과 나폴레옹 사이에 영국군이 버티고 있는 한 당장 달아날 필요는 없었다. 그러나 그는 멀리 떨어진 마구간에 있던 자기 말들을 자기가 사는 호텔 안뜰의 마구간으로 옮겨 오게 했으니, 그래야 제 눈으로 지켜볼 수 있고 난폭하게 빼앗길 위험에서도 벗어날 수 있었기 때문이다."], ["Isidor watched the stable-door constantly, and had the horses saddled, to be ready for the start.", "이지도르는 마구간 문을 끊임없이 지키며 말에 안장을 얹어, 언제든 출발할 수 있게 준비해 두었다."], ["He longed intensely for that event.", "그는 그 순간이 오기를 간절히 갈망했다."]], [["After the reception of the previous day, Rebecca did not care to come near her dear Amelia.", "전날의 푸대접을 받은 뒤로, 리베카는 사랑하는 애밀리아 곁에 갈 마음이 내키지 않았다."], ["She clipped the bouquet which George had brought her, and gave fresh water to the flowers, and read over the letter which he had sent her.", "그녀는 조지가 갖다준 꽃다발을 다듬고, 꽃에 새 물을 주고, 그가 보낸 편지를 거듭 읽었다."], ["\"Poor wretch,\"", "\"가엾은 것.\""], ["she said, twirling round the little bit of paper in her fingers, \"how I could crush her with this!--and it is for a thing like this that she must break her heart, forsooth--for a man who is stupid--a coxcomb--and who does not care for her.", "그녀가 그 작은 종잇조각을 손가락으로 빙글빙글 돌리며 말했다. \"이걸로 내가 저 애를 짓뭉갤 수도 있는데!"], ["My poor good Rawdon is worth ten of this creature.\"", "게다가 이런 것 때문에 저 애가 가슴이 찢어진다는 거잖아. 멍청하고, 멋이나 부리고, 저 애한테는 관심도 없는 사내 때문에 말이지."], ["And then she fell to thinking what she should do if--if anything happened to poor good Rawdon, and what a great piece of luck it was that he had left his horses behind.", "우리 가엾고 착한 로던이 이런 작자 열 명만큼은 값어치가 있는데.\" 그러고는 만약 가엾고 착한 로던에게 무슨 일이 생긴다면 자기는 어찌해야 할지를 생각하기 시작했고, 그가 말들을 남기고 떠난 것이 얼마나 큰 행운인지를 곱씹었다."]], [["In the course of this day too, Mrs.", "또한 이날 하루 동안, 베리에이커스 일행이 떠나는 것을 노여움 없이 보지는 못했던 크롤리 부인은, 백작 부인이 취했던 예방책을 떠올리고는 제 잇속을 위해 약간의 바느질을 했다."], ["Crawley, who saw not without anger the Bareacres party drive off, bethought her of the precaution which the Countess had taken, and did a little needlework for her own advantage; she stitched away the major part of her trinkets, bills, and bank-notes about her person, and so prepared, was ready for any event--to fly if she thought fit, or to stay and welcome the conqueror, were he Englishman or Frenchman.", "그녀는 제 패물과 어음과 지폐의 태반을 몸에 꿰매어 두었고, 그렇게 채비를 갖추고는 어떤 일이 닥치든 대비할 수 있었다. 적당하다 싶으면 달아날 수도, 아니면 남아서 정복자를 맞이할 수도 있었으니, 그가 영국인이든 프랑스인이든 상관없었다."], ["And I am not sure that she did not dream that night of becoming a duchess and Madame la Marechale, while Rawdon wrapped in his cloak, and making his bivouac under the rain at Mount Saint John, was thinking, with all the force of his heart, about the little wife whom he had left behind him.", "그리고 나는 그녀가 그날 밤 공작 부인이나 원수 부인이 되는 꿈을 꾸지 않았으리라고는 장담하지 못하겠다. 그러는 사이 로던은 외투를 두르고 몽생장의 빗속에서 노숙하며, 자기가 뒤에 두고 온 그 작은 아내를 가슴이 사무치도록 온 힘을 다해 생각하고 있었다."]], [["The next day was a Sunday.", "이튿날은 일요일이었다."], ["And Mrs. Major O'Dowd had the satisfaction of seeing both her patients refreshed in health and spirits by some rest which they had taken during the night.", "그리고 오다우드 소령 부인은 자기가 돌보는 두 환자가 밤사이 취한 약간의 휴식으로 건강과 기운을 되찾은 것을 보고 흐뭇해했다."], ["She herself had slept on a great chair in Amelia's room, ready to wait upon her poor friend or the ensign, should either need her nursing.", "그녀 자신은 애밀리아의 방 큰 의자에서 잠을 잤는데, 가엾은 벗이나 기수가 간호를 필요로 하면 언제든 시중들 채비를 한 채였다."], ["When morning came, this robust woman went back to the house where she and her Major had their billet; and here performed an elaborate and splendid toilette, befitting the day.", "아침이 오자 이 건장한 여인은 자기와 소령이 묵는 숙소로 돌아가, 그날에 걸맞은 공들이고 화려한 몸단장을 했다."], ["And it is very possible that whilst alone in that chamber, which her husband had inhabited, and where his cap still lay on the pillow, and his cane stood in the corner, one prayer at least was sent up to Heaven for the welfare of the brave soldier, Michael O'Dowd.", "그리고 남편이 거처했던 그 방, 그의 모자가 여전히 베개 위에 놓이고 지팡이가 구석에 세워져 있는 그 방에 홀로 있는 동안, 적어도 한 가지 기도만큼은 그 용감한 군인 마이클 오다우드의 안녕을 위해 하늘로 올려보냈을 가능성이 매우 높다."]], [["When she returned she brought her prayer-book with her, and her uncle the Dean's famous book of sermons, out of which she never failed to read every Sabbath; not understanding all, haply, not pronouncing many of the words aright, which were long and abstruse--for the Dean was a learned man, and loved long Latin words--but with great gravity, vast emphasis, and with tolerable correctness in the main.", "돌아올 때 그녀는 기도서와, 학장인 숙부의 그 유명한 설교집을 가지고 왔다. 그녀는 안식일이면 어김없이 그 책에서 한 대목을 읽곤 했는데, 다 이해하지는 못했을 테고, 길고 난해한 낱말들을 더러 제대로 발음하지도 못했을 것이다."], ["How often has my Mick listened to these sermons, she thought, and me reading in the cabin of a calm!", "학장이 학식 있는 인물이라 긴 라틴어 낱말을 좋아한 까닭이었다. 그러나 그녀는 자못 엄숙하게, 한껏 힘주어, 대체로는 그런대로 정확하게 읽어 나갔다."], ["She proposed to resume this exercise on the present day, with Amelia and the wounded ensign for a congregation.", "우리 미크가 이 설교를 얼마나 자주 들었던가, 그녀는 생각했다. 잔잔한 바다 위 선실에서 내가 읽어 주면 말이지!"], ["The same service was read on that day in twenty thousand churches at the same hour; and millions of British men and women, on their knees, implored protection of the Father of all.", "그녀는 오늘은 애밀리아와 부상당한 기수를 회중 삼아 이 일과를 다시 해 볼 작정이었다. 같은 예배가 그날 그 시각에 이만 곳의 교회에서 거행되고 있었고, 수백만의 영국 남녀가 무릎을 꿇고 만유의 아버지께 가호를 빌고 있었다."]], [["They did not hear the noise which disturbed our little congregation at Brussels.", "그들에게는 브뤼셀의 우리 작은 회중을 어지럽힌 그 소음이 들리지 않았다."], ["Much louder than that which had interrupted them two days previously, as Mrs. O'Dowd was reading the service in her best voice, the cannon of Waterloo began to roar.", "이틀 전 그들의 예배를 가로막았던 것보다 훨씬 더 우렁차게, 오다우드 부인이 가장 고운 목소리로 예배문을 읽고 있던 그때, 워털루의 대포가 포효하기 시작했다."]], [["When Jos heard that dreadful sound, he made up his mind that he would bear this perpetual recurrence of terrors no longer, and would fly at once.", "조스는 그 무시무시한 소리를 듣자, 이 끝없이 되풀이되는 공포를 더는 견디지 못하겠으며 당장 달아나겠노라 마음을 굳혔다."], ["He rushed into the sick man's room, where our three friends had paused in their prayers, and further interrupted them by a passionate appeal to Amelia.", "그는 우리 세 벗이 기도를 멈춘 그 병자의 방으로 뛰어들어, 애밀리아에게 격정에 찬 호소를 퍼부으며 그들을 한 번 더 가로막았다."]], [["\"I can't stand it any more, Emmy,\"", "\"더는 못 참겠다, 에미.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said; \"I won't stand it; and you must come with me.", "\"안 참을 거야. 그러니 너는 나랑 같이 가야 해."], ["I have bought a horse for you--never mind at what price--and you must dress and come with me, and ride behind Isidor.\"", "너를 위해 말 한 마리를 사 두었어―값이 얼마든 상관없으니까―어서 채비하고 나랑 같이 가서 이지도르 뒤에 타거라.\""]], [["\"God forgive me, Mr. Sedley, but you are no better than a coward,\"", "\"하느님 용서하소서, 세들리 씨, 하지만 당신은 겁쟁이나 진배없군요.\""], ["Mrs. O'Dowd said, laying down the book.", "오다우드 부인이 책을 내려놓으며 말했다."]], [["\"I say come, Amelia,\"", "\"가자니까, 애밀리아.\" 민간인이 말을 이었다."], ["the civilian went on; \"never mind what she says; why are we to stop here and be butchered by the Frenchmen?\"", "\"저 여자가 뭐라든 신경 쓰지 마. 우리가 왜 여기 남아서 프랑스 놈들한테 도륙당해야 하느냐고?\""]], [["\"You forget the --th, my boy,\"", "\"형은 ××연대를 잊으셨어요.\" 부상당한 영웅 어린 스터블이 침대에서 말했다."], ["said the little Stubble, the wounded hero, from his bed--\"and and you won't leave me, will you, Mrs. O'Dowd?\"", "\"그리고―그리고 부인은 저를 두고 가지 않으실 거죠, 오다우드 부인?\""]], [["\"No, my dear fellow,\"", "\"그럼, 안 두고말고, 얘야.\""], ["said she, going up and kissing the boy.", "그녀가 다가가 소년에게 입을 맞추며 말했다."], ["\"No harm shall come to you while I stand by.", "\"내가 곁에 있는 한 너한테는 아무 일도 없을 거다."], ["I don't budge till I get the word from Mick.", "나는 미크한테서 기별이 올 때까지는 꿈쩍도 안 할 거야."], ["A pretty figure I'd be, wouldn't I, stuck behind that chap on a pillion?\"", "안 그래도 저 사내 뒤에 안장에 끼어 앉으면 내 꼴이 가관이겠다, 안 그러냐?\""]], [["This image caused the young patient to burst out laughing in his bed, and even made Amelia smile.", "이 광경이 떠올라 젊은 환자는 침대에서 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 애밀리아마저 미소 짓게 만들었다. \"저 여자한테 부탁하는 게 아니야.\""], ["\"I don't ask her,\"", "조스가 소리쳤다."], ["Jos shouted out--\"I don't ask that--that Irishwoman, but you Amelia; once for all, will you come?\"", "\"저―저 아일랜드 여자한테 부탁하는 게 아니라 너한테 하는 거다, 애밀리아. 마지막으로 묻는다, 가겠느냐?\""]], [["\"Without my husband, Joseph?\"", "\"제 남편도 없이요, 조지프 오라버니?\""], ["Amelia said, with a look of wonder, and gave her hand to the Major's wife.", "애밀리아가 의아한 눈빛으로 말하며 소령의 부인에게 손을 내밀었다."], ["Jos's patience was exhausted.", "조스의 인내심은 바닥나고 말았다."]], [["\"Good-bye, then,\"", "\"그럼 잘 있어라.\""], ["he said, shaking his fist in a rage, and slamming the door by which he retreated.", "그가 화가 나서 주먹을 흔들며 말하고는, 물러나면서 문을 쾅 닫았다."], ["And this time he really gave his order for march: and mounted in the court-yard. Mrs.", "그리고 이번에는 정말로 진군 명령을 내렸으니, 안뜰에서 말에 올라탔다."], ["O'Dowd heard the clattering hoofs of the horses as they issued from the gate; and looking on, made many scornful remarks on poor Joseph as he rode down the street with Isidor after him in the laced cap.", "오다우드 부인은 말들이 문을 빠져나가며 또각거리는 발굽 소리를 들었다. 그리고 내다보며, 가엾은 조지프가 금몰 모자를 쓴 이지도르를 뒤에 거느리고 거리를 내려가는 모습을 두고 갖은 비웃음 섞인 말을 늘어놓았다."], ["The horses, which had not been exercised for some days, were lively, and sprang about the street.", "며칠 동안 부려지지 않은 말들은 기운이 펄펄해서 거리를 이리저리 날뛰었다."], ["Jos, a clumsy and timid horseman, did not look to advantage in the saddle.", "둔하고 겁 많은 기수인 조스는 안장 위에서 볼품없는 꼴이었다."], ["\"Look at him, Amelia dear, driving into the parlour window.", "\"저것 좀 봐라, 애밀리아야, 응접실 창문으로 처박을 기세구나."], ["Such a bull in a china-shop I never saw.\"", "도자기 가게에 든 황소가 따로 없네.\""], ["And presently the pair of riders disappeared at a canter down the street leading in the direction of the Ghent road, Mrs. O'Dowd pursuing them with a fire of sarcasm so long as they were in sight.", "그러더니 이내 두 기수는 헨트 가도로 이어지는 거리를 따라 느린 구보로 사라져 갔고, 오다우드 부인은 그들이 시야에 보이는 동안 내내 빈정거림의 포화를 퍼부어 댔다."]], [["All that day from morning until past sunset, the cannon never ceased to roar.", "그날 아침부터 해 질 녘이 지나도록 대포는 포효를 멈추지 않았다."], ["It was dark when the cannonading stopped all of a sudden.", "포성이 별안간 그쳤을 때는 이미 어둑했다."]], [["All of us have read of what occurred during that interval.", "우리 모두 그 사이에 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 읽어서 안다."], ["The tale is in every Englishman's mouth; and you and I, who were children when the great battle was won and lost, are never tired of hearing and recounting the history of that famous action.", "그 이야기는 영국인이라면 누구나 입에 올리는 것이며, 그 대전투가 이기고 진 무렵에 어린아이였던 당신과 나는 그 유명한 전투의 역사를 듣고 또 들려주기를 결코 지겨워하지 않는다."], ["Its remembrance rankles still in the bosoms of millions of the countrymen of those brave men who lost the day.", "그날의 패배를 맛본 저 용감한 자들의 동포 수백만의 가슴속에는 그 기억이 여전히 사무쳐 있다."], ["They pant for an opportunity of revenging that humiliation; and if a contest, ending in a victory on their part, should ensue, elating them in their turn, and leaving its cursed legacy of hatred and rage behind to us, there is no end to the so-called glory and shame, and to the alternations of successful and unsuccessful murder, in which two high-spirited nations might engage.", "그들은 그 굴욕을 갚을 기회를 갈망한다. 그리고 만약 그들 편의 승리로 끝나는 또 한 번의 다툼이 벌어져, 이번에는 그들을 우쭐하게 만들고 그 저주받은 증오와 분노의 유산을 우리에게 남긴다면, 두 혈기 왕성한 민족이 벌일 그 이른바 영광과 치욕, 성공한 살육과 실패한 살육의 번갈음에는 끝이 없을 것이다."], ["Centuries hence, we Frenchmen and Englishmen might be boasting and killing each other still, carrying out bravely the Devil's code of honour.", "수백 년이 흐른 뒤에도 우리 프랑스인과 영국인은 여전히 서로 으스대며 죽이고 있을지도 모른다. 악마의 명예 규범을 용감하게 받들면서 말이다."]], [["All our friends took their share and fought like men in the great field.", "우리 벗들은 모두 그 대전장에서 제 몫을 다하며 사내답게 싸웠다."], ["All day long, whilst the women were praying ten miles away, the lines of the dauntless English infantry were receiving and repelling the furious charges of the French horsemen.", "온종일, 여인들이 십 마일 떨어진 곳에서 기도하는 동안, 겁 없는 영국 보병의 대열은 프랑스 기병의 맹렬한 돌격을 받아 내고 또 물리쳤다. 브뤼셀에서까지 들리던 대포가 그들의 대열을 갈아엎었고, 전우들이 쓰러졌으며, 굳센 생존자들은 빈자리를 메우며 다가섰다."], ["Guns which were heard at Brussels were ploughing up their ranks, and comrades falling, and the resolute survivors closing in.", "저녁 무렵이 되자, 그토록 거듭되고 그토록 용감하게 저지당한 프랑스군의 공격은 그 맹위가 누그러졌다."], ["Towards evening, the attack of the French, repeated and resisted so bravely, slackened in its fury.", "그들에게는 영국군 말고도 상대해야 할 다른 적들이 있었거나, 아니면 마지막 일격을 준비하고 있었던 것이다."], ["They had other foes besides the British to engage, or were preparing for a final onset.", "마침내 그것이 왔다. 황제 근위대의 종대가 생장 언덕을 올라왔다."], ["It came at last: the columns of the Imperial Guard marched up the hill of Saint Jean, at length and at once to sweep the English from the height which they had maintained all day, and spite of all: unscared by the thunder of the artillery, which hurled death from the English line--the dark rolling column pressed on and up the hill.", "마침내, 그리고 단번에, 영국군을 그들이 온종일 무슨 일이 있어도 지켜 낸 그 고지에서 쓸어버리기 위해서였다. 영국군 대열에서 죽음을 쏟아붓는 포의 천둥소리에도 겁먹지 않고, 그 검은 종대는 언덕을 따라 위로 밀고 올라왔다."], ["It seemed almost to crest the eminence, when it began to wave and falter.", "그것이 거의 그 정상을 차지할 듯하더니, 흔들리며 휘청거리기 시작했다."], ["Then it stopped, still facing the shot.", "그러고는 멈춰 섰으니, 여전히 포화를 마주한 채였다."], ["Then at last the English troops rushed from the post from which no enemy had been able to dislodge them, and the Guard turned and fled.", "그러다 마침내 영국군이, 그 어떤 적도 몰아내지 못했던 그 진지에서 쏟아져 나왔고, 근위대는 등을 돌려 달아났다."]], [["No more firing was heard at Brussels--the pursuit rolled miles away. Darkness came down on the field and city: and Amelia was praying for George, who was lying on his face, dead, with a bullet through his heart.", "브뤼셀에서는 더 이상 포성이 들리지 않았다. 추격은 수 마일 멀리로 굴러갔다. 어둠이 전장과 도시 위로 내려앉았다. 그리고 애밀리아는 조지를 위해 기도하고 있었다. 그 조지는 얼굴을 땅에 묻은 채, 심장을 꿰뚫은 총탄을 맞고 죽어 누워 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXIII", "# CHAPTER XXXIII"]], [["In Which Miss Crawley's Relations Are Very Anxious About Her\nThe kind reader must please to remember--while the army is marching from Flanders, and, after its heroic actions there, is advancing to take the fortifications on the frontiers of France, previous to an occupation of that country--that there are a number of persons living peaceably in England who have to do with the history at present in hand, and must come in for their share of the chronicle.", "친애하는 독자께서는, 군대가 플랑드르에서 진군해 나와 그곳에서 영웅적인 전투를 치른 뒤 프랑스 국경의 요새를 점령하고자, 그 나라를 점령하기에 앞서 나아가고 있는 동안에도, 지금 다루고 있는 이야기와 관련된 사람들이 영국에서 평온하게 살아가고 있으며, 그들 또한 이 연대기에서 제 몫을 차지해야 마땅하다는 점을 부디 기억해 주시기 바란다."], ["During the time of these battles and dangers, old Miss Crawley was living at Brighton, very moderately moved by the great events that were going on.", "이 전투와 위난의 시절 내내, 늙은 크롤리 양은 브라이턴에서 지내며, 한창 벌어지고 있는 그 위대한 사건들에 그다지 마음을 동요하지 않았다."], ["The great events rendered the newspapers rather interesting, to be sure, and Briggs read out the Gazette, in which Rawdon Crawley's gallantry was mentioned with honour, and his promotion was presently recorded.", "물론 그 위대한 사건들 덕분에 신문이 제법 흥미로워지기는 했고, 브리그스가 관보를 소리 내어 읽어 주었는데, 거기에는 로던 크롤리의 무용이 명예롭게 언급되어 있었으며, 그의 진급도 곧 기록되었다."]], [["\"What a pity that young man has taken such an irretrievable step in the world!\"", "\"그 젊은이가 세상에서 그토록 돌이킬 수 없는 걸음을 내디뎠다니 얼마나 딱한 일인가!\" 그의 고모가 말했다."], ["his aunt said; \"with his rank and distinction he might have married a brewer's daughter with a quarter of a million--like Miss Grains; or have looked to ally himself with the best families in England.", "\"그만한 지위와 명망이라면 그레인스 양처럼 이십오만 파운드를 지닌 양조업자의 딸과 결혼할 수도 있었을 테고, 아니면 영국에서 가장 좋은 가문들과 연을 맺어 볼 수도 있었을 게다. 언젠가는 내 재산을 물려받았을 테고, 아니면 그 자식들이라도 물려받았을 테지."], ["He would have had my money some day or other; or his children would--for I'm not in a hurry to go, Miss Briggs, although you may be in a hurry to be rid of me; and instead of that, he is a doomed pauper, with a dancing-girl for a wife.\"", "나는 그리 서둘러 갈 생각이 없으니까 말이다, 브리그스 양, 비록 자네는 어서 나를 떨쳐 버리고 싶어 안달이 났을지 몰라도. 그런데 그러기는커녕, 그 애는 춤추는 계집을 아내로 둔, 파멸한 거지 신세로구나.\""]], [["\"Will my dear Miss Crawley not cast an eye of compassion upon the heroic soldier, whose name is inscribed in the annals of his country's glory?\"", "\"그렇지만 친애하는 크롤리 양께서는, 조국 영광의 연대기에 그 이름이 새겨진 그 영웅적인 군인에게 연민의 눈길 한 번 주시지 않으렵니까?\""], ["said Miss Briggs, who was greatly excited by the Waterloo proceedings, and loved speaking romantically when there was an occasion.", "브리그스 양이 말했다. 그녀는 워털루 사건에 몹시 흥분해 있었고, 기회만 있으면 낭만적으로 말하기를 좋아하는 사람이었다."], ["\"Has not the Captain--or the Colonel as I may now style him--done deeds which make the name of Crawley illustrious?\"", "\"그 대위께서—아니 이제는 대령이라 불러도 될 분이지요—크롤리라는 이름을 빛나게 할 위업을 이루지 않으셨습니까?\""]], [["\"Briggs, you are a fool,\"", "\"브리그스, 자네는 바보로구나.\""], ["said Miss Crawley: \"Colonel Crawley has dragged the name of Crawley through the mud, Miss Briggs.", "크롤리 양이 말했다. \"크롤리 대령은 크롤리라는 이름을 진흙탕에 끌고 들어갔어, 브리그스 양."], ["Marry a drawing-master's daughter, indeed!--marry a dame de compagnie--for she was no better, Briggs; no, she was just what you are--only younger, and a great deal prettier and cleverer.", "미술 선생의 딸과 결혼을 하다니, 원!—말동무로 데리고 있던 계집과 결혼을 하다니."], ["Were you an accomplice of that abandoned wretch, I wonder, of whose vile arts he became a victim, and of whom you used to be such an admirer?", "그 애도 그 이상은 못 되는 것이었어, 브리그스. 아니, 그 애는 딱 자네 같은 처지였지—다만 더 젊고, 훨씬 더 예쁘고 영악했을 뿐."], ["Yes, I daresay you were an accomplice.", "자네는 혹시 그 못된 계집의 공범이었던 게 아닌가 싶구나."], ["But you will find yourself disappointed in my will, I can tell you: and you will have the goodness to write to Mr.", "그 애의 더러운 술수에 걸려든 게 그 애였고, 자네는 그 계집을 그토록 떠받들었으니 말이야. 그래, 분명 자네도 공범이었을 게다."], ["Waxy, and say that I desire to see him immediately.\"", "하지만 내 유언장에서는 실망하게 될 게야, 그건 내가 장담하지."], ["Miss Crawley was now in the habit of writing to Mr.", "그러니 수고스럽겠지만 왁시 씨에게 편지를 써서, 내가 그 사람을 당장 보고 싶어 한다고 전해 주게.\""], ["Waxy her solicitor almost every day in the week, for her arrangements respecting her property were all revoked, and her perplexity was great as to the future disposition of her money.", "크롤리 양은 이제 거의 일주일에 하루도 빠짐없이 변호사 왁시 씨에게 편지를 쓰는 버릇이 들어 있었다. 자기 재산에 관한 처분을 모조리 무효로 돌려놓은 터라, 앞으로 그 돈을 어떻게 처리할지를 두고 그녀의 고민이 이만저만이 아니었기 때문이다."]], [["The spinster had, however, rallied considerably; as was proved by the increased vigour and frequency of her sarcasms upon Miss Briggs, all which attacks the poor companion bore with meekness, with cowardice, with a resignation that was half generous and half hypocritical--with the slavish submission, in a word, that women of her disposition and station are compelled to show.", "그러나 이 노처녀는 상당히 기력을 되찾은 상태였다. 브리그스 양을 향한 그녀의 빈정거림이 한층 기세등등해지고 잦아진 것이 그 증거였다."], ["Who has not seen how women bully women?", "가엾은 말동무는 그 모든 공격을 온순하게, 겁먹은 채로, 반쯤은 너그럽고 반쯤은 위선적인 체념으로—한마디로, 그녀와 같은 기질과 처지의 여자들이 어쩔 수 없이 보여야 하는 노예 같은 굴종으로 견뎌 냈다."], ["What tortures have men to endure, comparable to those daily repeated shafts of scorn and cruelty with which poor women are riddled by the tyrants of their sex?", "여자가 여자를 어떻게 들볶는지 보지 못한 사람이 어디 있겠는가? 가엾은 여자들이 같은 성(性)의 폭군들에게서 날마다 거듭 쏘아 맞는 그 멸시와 잔혹의 화살에 견줄 만한 고문을, 남자들이 과연 견뎌 본 적이 있던가?"], ["Poor victims!", "가엾은 희생자들이여!"], ["But we are starting from our proposition, which is, that Miss Crawley was always particularly annoying and savage when she was rallying from illness--as they say wounds tingle most when they are about to heal.", "그런데 우리는 우리의 명제에서 벗어나고 있다. 그 명제란, 크롤리 양은 병에서 회복될 때면 언제나 유난히 사람을 성가시게 하고 사나워졌다는 것이다—상처가 아물려고 할 때 가장 욱신거린다고들 하듯이 말이다."]], [["While thus approaching, as all hoped, to convalescence, Miss Briggs was the only victim admitted into the presence of the invalid; yet Miss Crawley's relatives afar off did not forget their beloved kinswoman, and by a number of tokens, presents, and kind affectionate messages, strove to keep themselves alive in her recollection.", "이렇듯 모두가 바라던 대로 회복기에 접어드는 동안, 브리그스 양만이 그 환자 앞에 들 수 있는 유일한 희생양이었다. 그렇지만 멀리 떨어진 크롤리 양의 친척들은 그 사랑하는 친족을 잊지 않았으니, 갖가지 정표와 선물, 다정하고 애정 어린 안부를 끊임없이 보내며 그녀의 기억 속에서 자기들을 잊히지 않게 하려고 애를 썼다."]], [["In the first place, let us mention her nephew, Rawdon Crawley.", "먼저, 그녀의 조카 로던 크롤리부터 이야기하자."], ["A few weeks after the famous fight of Waterloo, and after the Gazette had made known to her the promotion and gallantry of that distinguished officer, the Dieppe packet brought over to Miss Crawley at Brighton, a box containing presents, and a dutiful letter, from the Colonel her nephew.", "저 유명한 워털루 전투가 끝나고 몇 주 뒤, 관보가 그 빼어난 장교의 진급과 무용을 그녀에게 알린 다음, 디에프발(發) 우편선이 그 조카 대령이 보낸 선물 상자 하나와 정중한 편지 한 통을 브라이턴의 크롤리 양에게 실어 왔다."], ["In the box were a pair of French epaulets, a Cross of the Legion of Honour, and the hilt of a sword--relics from the field of battle: and the letter described with a good deal of humour how the latter belonged to a commanding officer of the Guard, who having sworn that \"the Guard died, but never surrendered,\"", "상자 안에는 프랑스군 견장 한 쌍과 레지옹 도뇌르 십자 훈장 하나, 그리고 칼자루 하나가 들어 있었다—모두 전쟁터에서 거둔 유물이었다. 그리고 편지에는 그 칼자루에 얽힌 사연이 제법 익살스럽게 적혀 있었다."], ["was taken prisoner the next minute by a private soldier, who broke the Frenchman's sword with the butt of his musket, when Rawdon made himself master of the shattered weapon.", "그것은 어느 근위대 지휘관의 것이었는데, 그자가 \"근위대는 죽을지언정 결코 항복하지 않는다\"고 호언장담해 놓고는 바로 다음 순간 일개 사병에게 포로로 잡혔고, 그 사병이 머스킷 개머리판으로 그 프랑스인의 칼을 부러뜨리자 로던이 그 부서진 무기를 차지하게 되었다는 것이었다."], ["As for the cross and epaulets, they came from a Colonel of French cavalry, who had fallen under the aide-de-camp's arm in the battle: and Rawdon Crawley did not know what better to do with the spoils than to send them to his kindest and most affectionate old friend.", "십자 훈장과 견장으로 말하자면, 그것은 전투 중 부관 로던의 손에 쓰러진 어느 프랑스 기병대 대령에게서 나온 것이었다. 그리고 로던 크롤리는 그 전리품을 자신의 더없이 다정하고 더없이 애정 깊은 늙은 벗에게 보내는 것보다 더 좋은 처리법을 알지 못했다."], ["Should he continue to write to her from Paris, whither the army was marching?", "군대가 진군해 가는 파리에서도 계속 그녀에게 편지를 써도 되겠습니까?"], ["He might be able to give her interesting news from that capital, and of some of Miss Crawley's old friends of the emigration, to whom she had shown so much kindness during their distress.", "그 수도에서 벌어지는 흥미로운 소식과, 어려운 시절 크롤리 양이 그토록 친절을 베풀어 주었던 망명객 시절의 옛 벗들 몇몇에 관한 소식도 전해 드릴 수 있을 것입니다."]], [["The spinster caused Briggs to write back to the Colonel a gracious and complimentary letter, encouraging him to continue his correspondence. His first letter was so excessively lively and amusing that she should look with pleasure for its successors.--\"Of course, I know,\" she explained to Miss Briggs, \"that Rawdon could not write such a good letter any more than you could, my poor Briggs, and that it is that clever little wretch of a Rebecca, who dictates every word to him; but that is no reason why my nephew should not amuse me; and so I wish to let him understand that I am in high good humour.\"", "이 노처녀는 브리그스를 시켜 대령에게 우아하고 칭찬 가득한 답장을 써 보내, 계속 편지를 보내라고 격려하게 했다. 그의 첫 편지가 어찌나 활기차고 재미있었던지, 그녀는 다음 편지들을 즐거운 마음으로 기다리겠노라고 했다. \"물론 나도 알지.\" 그녀가 브리그스 양에게 설명했다. \"로던이 그렇게 잘 쓴 편지를 쓸 리가 없다는 건, 가엾은 우리 브리그스 자네가 못 쓰는 것이나 마찬가지야. 그건 그 영악하고 못된 작은 계집 리베카가 한 마디 한 마디 받아쓰게 한 게 분명해. 하지만 그렇다고 내 조카가 나를 즐겁게 해 주면 안 될 까닭은 없지. 그러니 내가 아주 기분이 좋다는 걸 그 애가 알게 해 주고 싶구나.\""]], [["I wonder whether she knew that it was not only Becky who wrote the letters, but that Mrs. Rawdon actually took and sent home the trophies which she bought for a few francs, from one of the innumerable pedlars who immediately began to deal in relics of the war.", "편지를 쓴 것이 베키만이 아니라, 실은 로던 부인이 그 전리품들을, 전쟁이 끝나기 무섭게 그 유물들을 팔기 시작한 수많은 행상 가운데 하나에게서 몇 프랑에 사들여 직접 집으로 부쳤다는 사실을, 과연 크롤리 양이 알고 있었을까."], ["The novelist, who knows everything, knows this also.", "모든 것을 아는 소설가는 이것 또한 알고 있다. 그러나 어찌 되었든, 크롤리 양의 우아한 답장은 우리의 젊은 벗들, 즉 로던과 그의 아내에게 큰 용기를 주었다."], ["Be this, however, as it may, Miss Crawley's gracious reply greatly encouraged our young friends, Rawdon and his lady, who hoped for the best from their aunt's evidently pacified humour: and they took care to entertain her with many delightful letters from Paris, whither, as Rawdon said, they had the good luck to go in the track of the conquering army.", "분명 누그러진 고모의 기분으로 미루어 그들은 잘되리라 기대했고, 그래서 파리에서 보내는 즐거운 편지를 잔뜩 띄워 그녀를 즐겁게 해 주는 데 신경을 썼다. 로던의 말마따나, 그들은 운 좋게도 승리한 군대의 뒤를 따라 파리로 가게 된 것이었다."]], [["To the rector's lady, who went off to tend her husband's broken collar-bone at the Rectory at Queen's Crawley, the spinster's communications were by no means so gracious.", "그러나 남편의 부러진 쇄골을 돌보러 퀸스크롤리의 목사관으로 떠나 버린 목사 부인에게 보낸 이 노처녀의 소식은 결코 그토록 우아하지가 않았다."], ["Mrs. Bute, that brisk, managing, lively, imperious woman, had committed the most fatal of all errors with regard to her sister-in-law.", "저 활기차고 일 잘하고 발랄하면서도 위압적인 여인 뷰트 부인은, 시누이를 대하는 일에서 무엇보다 치명적인 실수를 저지르고 만 것이었다."], ["She had not merely oppressed her and her household--she had bored Miss Crawley; and if poor Miss Briggs had been a woman of any spirit, she might have been made happy by the commission which her principal gave her to write a letter to Mrs.", "그녀는 크롤리 양과 그 살림을 억압했을 뿐만 아니라—크롤리 양을 따분하게 했던 것이다. 그러니 가엾은 브리그스 양이 조금이라도 기개가 있는 여자였더라면, 자기 주인이 그녀에게 맡긴 임무에 흐뭇해했을 만도 했다."], ["Bute Crawley, saying that Miss Crawley's health was greatly improved since Mrs. Bute had left her, and begging the latter on no account to put herself to trouble, or quit her family for Miss Crawley's sake.", "그 임무란, 뷰트 크롤리 부인에게 편지를 써서 크롤리 양의 건강이 뷰트 부인이 떠난 뒤로 크게 좋아졌으니, 부디 크롤리 양을 위해 수고를 하거나 가족을 버려두고 오는 일은 결코 하지 말라고 전하라는 것이었다."], ["This triumph over a lady who had been very haughty and cruel in her behaviour to Miss Briggs, would have rejoiced most women; but the truth is, Briggs was a woman of no spirit at all, and the moment her enemy was discomfited, she began to feel compassion in her favour.", "브리그스 양에게 그토록 거만하고 모질게 굴었던 부인을 상대로 거둔 이 승리를 대부분의 여자라면 기뻐했을 테지만, 사실 브리그스는 기개라고는 도무지 없는 여자여서, 자기 적이 낭패를 보는 순간 도리어 그 편을 동정하기 시작했다."]], [["\"How silly I was,\" Mrs.", "\"내가 어쩌면 그리도 어리석었던가.\""], ["Bute thought, and with reason, \"ever to hint that I was coming, as I did, in that foolish letter when we sent Miss Crawley the guinea-fowls.", "뷰트 부인은 그런 생각이 들었고, 그럴 만도 했다. \"크롤리 양에게 뿔닭을 보내면서 그 어리석은 편지에다 내가 가겠노라고 넌지시 비춘 게 잘못이었어."], ["I ought to have gone without a word to the poor dear doting old creature, and taken her out of the hands of that ninny Briggs, and that harpy of a femme de chambre.", "그 가엾고 망령 난 늙은이를 두고 한마디 말도 없이 그냥 가서, 저 멍청한 브리그스와 하피 같은 그 시녀 손아귀에서 빼내 왔어야 했는데."], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["Bute, Bute, why did you break your collar-bone?\"", "뷰트, 뷰트, 당신은 어쩌자고 쇄골을 부러뜨린 거예요?\""]], [["Why, indeed? We have seen how Mrs.", "정말이지, 어쩌자고?"], ["Bute, having the game in her hands, had really played her cards too well.", "뷰트 부인이 패를 손에 쥐고도 정작 카드를 너무 잘 쳐 버린 셈이었음을 우리는 이미 보았다."], ["She had ruled over Miss Crawley's household utterly and completely, to be utterly and completely routed when a favourable opportunity for rebellion came.", "그녀는 크롤리 양의 살림을 완전무결하게 틀어쥐고 다스렸으나, 반란의 절호의 기회가 찾아오자 완전무결하게 패주하고 만 것이었다."], ["She and her household, however, considered that she had been the victim of horrible selfishness and treason, and that her sacrifices in Miss Crawley's behalf had met with the most savage ingratitude.", "그러나 그녀와 그 식구들은 자기들이 끔찍한 이기심과 배신의 희생양이 되었으며, 크롤리 양을 위해 바친 그 모든 희생이 더없이 야만적인 배은망덕으로 되돌아왔다고 여겼다."], ["Rawdon's promotion, and the honourable mention made of his name in the Gazette, filled this good Christian lady also with alarm.", "로던의 진급과, 관보에 그의 이름이 명예롭게 언급된 일은 이 선량한 그리스도교도 부인 또한 불안에 빠뜨렸다."], ["Would his aunt relent towards him now that he was a Lieutenant-Colonel and a C.B.?", "그가 이제 중령에다 바스 훈장 수훈자가 되었으니 고모가 그에게 마음을 누그러뜨리지나 않을까?"], ["and would that odious Rebecca once more get into favour?", "그리고 저 가증스러운 리베카가 다시 한번 총애를 얻게 되지나 않을까?"], ["The Rector's wife wrote a sermon for her husband about the vanity of military glory and the prosperity of the wicked, which the worthy parson read in his best voice and without understanding one syllable of it.", "목사의 아내는 군사적 영광의 헛됨과 악인의 번영에 관한 설교문을 한 편 써서 남편에게 주었고, 이 훌륭한 목사는 그것을 한 음절도 이해하지 못한 채 가장 멋들어진 목소리로 낭독했다."], ["He had Pitt Crawley for one of his auditors--Pitt, who had come with his two half-sisters to church, which the old Baronet could now by no means be brought to frequent.", "그 청중 가운데에는 피트 크롤리도 있었다—두 이복 누이를 데리고 교회에 온 피트였다. 늙은 준남작은 이제 도무지 교회에 발걸음하게 만들 수가 없는 형편이었기 때문이다."]], [["Since the departure of Becky Sharp, that old wretch had given himself up entirely to his bad courses, to the great scandal of the county and the mute horror of his son.", "베키 샤프가 떠난 뒤로, 그 늙은 망나니는 온통 못된 짓에 빠져 지냈으니, 그 일대의 큰 추문거리가 되었고 아들에게는 말 못 할 공포의 근원이 되었다. 호럭스 양의 모자에 달린 리본은 그 어느 때보다 화려해졌다."], ["The ribbons in Miss Horrocks's cap became more splendid than ever.", "점잖은 가문들은 겁에 질려 그 저택과 주인을 피해 달아났다."], ["The polite families fled the hall and its owner in terror. Sir Pitt went about tippling at his tenants'", "피트 경은 소작인들의 집을 돌아다니며 술을 퍼마셨고, 장날이면 머드베리와 인근 마을에서 농부들과 럼주에 물을 타 마셨다."], ["houses; and drank rum-and-water with the farmers at Mudbury and the neighbouring places on market-days.", "그는 호럭스 양을 안에 태우고 사두마차를 몰아 사우샘프턴까지 갔다."], ["He drove the family coach-and-four to Southampton with Miss Horrocks inside: and the county people expected, every week, as his son did in speechless agony, that his marriage with her would be announced in the provincial paper.", "그러니 그 지방 사람들은 매주, 그 아들이 말문이 막힌 채 괴로워하며 예상하듯, 신문에 그와 그녀의 결혼이 공표되지나 않을까 기다리는 것이었다. 그것은 참으로 크롤리 씨에게 견디기 힘든 무거운 짐이었다."], ["It was indeed a rude burthen for Mr. Crawley to bear.", "그가 으레 좌장을 맡아 몇 시간씩 연설을 늘어놓곤 하던 인근의 선교 집회며 다른 종교 모임에서, 그의 웅변은 마비되어 버렸다."], ["His eloquence was palsied at the missionary meetings, and other religious assemblies in the neighbourhood, where he had been in the habit of presiding, and of speaking for hours; for he felt, when he rose, that the audience said, \"That is the son of the old reprobate Sir Pitt, who is very likely drinking at the public house at this very moment.\"", "그가 일어설 때면 청중이 \"저자가 바로 그 늙은 난봉꾼 피트 경의 아들이렷다. 그 아비는 바로 이 순간에도 술집에서 술을 퍼마시고 있기 십상이지\""], ["And once when he was speaking of the benighted condition of the king of Timbuctoo, and the number of his wives who were likewise in darkness, some gipsy miscreant from the crowd asked, \"How many is there at Queen's Crawley, Young Squaretoes?\"", "하고 수군거리는 것만 같았기 때문이다. 한번은 그가 팀북투 왕의 어두운 처지와, 마찬가지로 어둠 속에 있는 그 수많은 아내들 이야기를 하고 있는데, 군중 속의 어떤 집시 불량배가 \"퀸스크롤리에는 몇이나 있소, 점잔 빼는 젊은 양반?\""], ["to the surprise of the platform, and the ruin of Mr. Pitt's speech.", "하고 물어, 연단 사람들을 놀라게 하고 피트 씨의 연설을 망쳐 버렸다."], ["And the two daughters of the house of Queen's Crawley would have been allowed to run utterly wild (for Sir Pitt swore that no governess should ever enter into his doors again), had not Mr.", "또한 퀸스크롤리 가문의 두 딸은 하마터면 아주 제멋대로 자라도록 방치될 뻔했다(피트 경이 다시는 가정교사 따위가 자기 집 문턱을 넘게 두지 않겠다고 맹세했기 때문이다)."], ["Crawley, by threatening the old gentleman, forced the latter to send them to school.", "크롤리 씨가 그 늙은 양반을 으름장으로 윽박질러, 기어이 두 딸을 학교에 보내게 만들지 않았더라면 말이다."]], [["Meanwhile, as we have said, whatever individual differences there might be between them all, Miss Crawley's dear nephews and nieces were unanimous in loving her and sending her tokens of affection. Thus Mrs.", "그러는 동안, 앞서 말했듯, 그들 사이에 어떤 개인적인 알력이 있건 간에, 크롤리 양의 사랑하는 조카와 조카딸들은 한결같이 그녀를 사랑했고 애정의 정표를 보내는 데 의견이 일치했다."], ["Bute sent guinea-fowls, and some remarkably fine cauliflowers, and a pretty purse or pincushion worked by her darling girls, who begged to keep a LITTLE place in the recollection of their dear aunt, while Mr.", "그리하여 뷰트 부인은 뿔닭과 유난히 실한 콜리플라워 몇 통, 그리고 자기 귀여운 딸들이 손수 수놓은 예쁜 지갑인지 바늘꽂이인지를 보냈다. 딸들은 사랑하는 고모할머니의 기억 속에 조그만 자리나마 남게 해 달라고 청했다."], ["Pitt sent peaches and grapes and venison from the Hall.", "한편 피트 씨는 저택에서 복숭아와 포도, 사슴 고기를 보냈다."], ["The Southampton coach used to carry these tokens of affection to Miss Crawley at Brighton: it used sometimes to convey Mr. Pitt thither too: for his differences with Sir Pitt caused Mr.", "사우샘프턴발 마차가 이 애정의 정표들을 브라이턴의 크롤리 양에게 실어 나르곤 했고, 때로는 피트 씨 본인을 그곳으로 실어 가기도 했다."], ["Crawley to absent himself a good deal from home now: and besides, he had an attraction at Brighton in the person of the Lady Jane Sheepshanks, whose engagement to Mr.", "피트 경과의 불화 때문에 크롤리 씨가 요즘 집을 자주 비우게 된 데다, 게다가 브라이턴에는 그를 끌어당기는 사람이 하나 있었기 때문이다."], ["Crawley has been formerly mentioned in this history.", "바로 레이디 제인 십섕크스로, 그녀와 크롤리 씨의 약혼은 이 이야기에서 앞서 언급한 바 있다."], ["Her Ladyship and her sisters lived at Brighton with their mamma, the Countess Southdown, that strong-minded woman so favourably known in the serious world.", "그녀와 자매들은 어머니인 사우스다운 백작 부인과 함께 브라이턴에서 지냈는데, 이 백작 부인은 신앙심 깊은 세계에서 좋은 평판을 누리는 저 의지 굳센 여인이었다."]], [["A few words ought to be said regarding her Ladyship and her noble family, who are bound by ties of present and future relationship to the house of Crawley.", "현재와 장래의 인척 관계로 크롤리 가문과 얽혀 있는 이 부인과 그 고귀한 가문에 관해서는 몇 마디 적어 두어야겠다."], ["Respecting the chief of the Southdown family, Clement William, fourth Earl of Southdown, little need be told, except that his Lordship came into Parliament (as Lord Wolsey) under the auspices of Mr. Wilberforce, and for a time was a credit to his political sponsor, and decidedly a serious young man.", "사우스다운 가문의 수장인 클레먼트 윌리엄, 즉 제4대 사우스다운 백작으로 말하자면, 윌버포스 씨의 후원 아래 (울지 경이라는 이름으로) 의회에 입성했고 한동안은 그 정치적 후견인에게 면목이 서는 인물이자 분명 진중한 청년이었다는 것 말고는 별로 할 이야기가 없다."], ["But words cannot describe the feelings of his admirable mother, when she learned, very shortly after her noble husband's demise, that her son was a member of several worldly clubs, had lost largely at play at Wattier's and the Cocoa Tree; that he had raised money on post-obits, and encumbered the family estate; that he drove four-in-hand, and patronised the ring; and that he actually had an opera-box, where he entertained the most dangerous bachelor company.", "그러나 그 훌륭한 어머니가, 고귀한 남편이 세상을 떠난 지 얼마 지나지 않아, 자기 아들이 여러 속된 클럽의 회원이고 워티어와 코코아 트리에서 노름으로 크게 잃었으며, 사망 후 상환 조건으로 돈을 빌려 가문의 영지를 저당 잡혔고, 사두마차를 몰며 권투계를 후원하고, 실제로 오페라 박스 좌석까지 두어 거기서 가장 위험한 독신 패거리를 접대한다는 사실을 알게 되었을 때 그 심정은 이루 말로 다할 수가 없었다."], ["His name was only mentioned with groans in the dowager's circle.", "백작 미망인의 모임에서 그의 이름은 오로지 한탄과 함께 입에 오르내릴 뿐이었다."]], [["The Lady Emily was her brother's senior by many years; and took considerable rank in the serious world as author of some of the delightful tracts before mentioned, and of many hymns and spiritual pieces.", "레이디 에밀리는 오빠보다 여러 살 위였고, 앞서 언급한 그 즐거운 소책자 몇 권과 수많은 찬송가며 신앙 시편의 저자로서 신앙심 깊은 세계에서 상당한 지위를 누렸다."], ["A mature spinster, and having but faint ideas of marriage, her love for the blacks occupied almost all her feelings.", "혼기가 무르익은 노처녀로 결혼에는 그저 희미한 생각밖에 없었던 그녀는, 흑인들에 대한 사랑이 그 감정의 거의 전부를 차지했다."], ["It is to her, I believe, we owe that beautiful poem.", "저 아름다운 시도 바로 그녀가 지은 것이라고 나는 믿는다."]], [["Lead us to some sunny isle, Yonder in the western deep; Where the skies for ever smile, And the blacks for ever weep, &c.", "우리를 어느 양지바른 섬으로 이끄소서, 저 서쪽 바다 깊은 곳에 있는 그 섬으로. 하늘은 영원히 미소 짓고, 흑인들은 영원히 눈물 흘리는 그곳으로, 운운."]], [["She had correspondences with clerical gentlemen in most of our East and West India possessions; and was secretly attached to the Reverend Silas Hornblower, who was tattooed in the South Sea Islands.", "그녀는 동인도와 서인도의 우리 영지 대부분에 있는 성직자 신사들과 서신을 주고받았고, 남태평양 제도에서 문신을 한 사일러스 혼블로어 목사를 남몰래 사모하고 있었다."]], [["As for the Lady Jane, on whom, as it has been said, Mr. Pitt Crawley's affection had been placed, she was gentle, blushing, silent, and timid.", "앞서 말했듯 피트 크롤리 씨가 애정을 쏟은 레이디 제인으로 말하자면, 그녀는 온화하고, 잘 얼굴을 붉히고, 말이 없고, 수줍은 사람이었다."], ["In spite of his falling away, she wept for her brother, and was quite ashamed of loving him still.", "오빠가 타락했음에도 그녀는 오빠를 위해 눈물 흘렸고, 그러면서도 여전히 그를 사랑하는 자신이 무척 부끄러웠다."], ["Even yet she used to send him little hurried smuggled notes, and pop them into the post in private.", "그녀는 지금도 짧고 황급히 쓴 쪽지를 몰래 그에게 보내며, 남몰래 우체통에 넣곤 했다."], ["The one dreadful secret which weighed upon her life was, that she and the old housekeeper had been to pay Southdown a furtive visit at his chambers in the Albany; and found him--O the naughty dear abandoned wretch!--smoking a cigar with a bottle of Curacao before him.", "그녀의 삶을 짓누르는 한 가지 무서운 비밀은, 그녀와 늙은 가정부가 앨버니에 있는 사우스다운의 거처로 몰래 그를 찾아간 일이었다. 그리고 그가—오, 그 못된, 사랑스럽고도 타락한 망나니!—퀴라소 한 병을 앞에 두고 시가를 피우고 있는 것을 본 것이었다."], ["She admired her sister, she adored her mother, she thought Mr.", "그녀는 언니를 흠모했고, 어머니를 떠받들었으며, 크롤리 씨야말로 저 타락한 천사 사우스다운 다음으로 가장 즐겁고 교양 있는 남자라고 여겼다."], ["Crawley the most delightful and accomplished of men, after Southdown, that fallen angel: and her mamma and sister, who were ladies of the most superior sort, managed everything for her, and regarded her with that amiable pity, of which your really superior woman always has such a share to give away.", "그녀의 어머니와 언니는 더없이 우월한 부류의 숙녀들이어서 그녀를 대신해 모든 일을 처리해 주었고, 정말로 우월한 여자라면 늘 그토록 넉넉히 베풀게 마련인 그 상냥한 연민의 눈길로 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["Her mamma ordered her dresses, her books, her bonnets, and her ideas for her.", "어머니는 그녀의 옷이며 책이며 보닛이며 생각까지 대신 정해 주었다."], ["She was made to take pony-riding, or piano-exercise, or any other sort of bodily medicament, according as my Lady Southdown saw meet; and her ladyship would have kept her daughter in pinafores up to her present age of six-and-twenty, but that they were thrown off when Lady Jane was presented to Queen Charlotte.", "그녀는 레이디 사우스다운이 적당하다고 보는 대로 조랑말 타기나 피아노 연습, 혹은 그 밖의 갖가지 신체 처방을 받게 되었다. 그리고 백작 부인은 딸을 지금의 나이인 스물여섯이 되도록 앞치마 차림으로 묶어 두었을 테지만, 레이디 제인이 샬럿 왕비를 알현하면서 그 앞치마를 벗어 던지게 된 것이었다."]], [["When these ladies first came to their house at Brighton, it was to them alone that Mr. Crawley paid his personal visits, contenting himself by leaving a card at his aunt's house, and making a modest inquiry of Mr.", "이 숙녀들이 처음 브라이턴 저택에 왔을 때, 크롤리 씨가 손수 방문을 한 것은 오직 그들에게뿐이었고, 고모의 집에는 명함 한 장을 두고 보울스 씨나 그 하급 하인에게 환자의 건강에 관해 조심스레 묻는 것으로 만족했다."], ["Bowls or his assistant footman, with respect to the health of the invalid. When he met Miss Briggs coming home from the library with a cargo of novels under her arm, Mr.", "도서관에서 소설을 한 아름 옆구리에 끼고 집으로 돌아오던 브리그스 양과 마주쳤을 때, 크롤리 씨는 그로서는 무척이나 드물게 얼굴을 붉히며 앞으로 나서서 고모의 말동무와 악수를 나누었다."], ["Crawley blushed in a manner quite unusual to him, as he stepped forward and shook Miss Crawley's companion by the hand.", "그는 마침 함께 걷고 있던 부인, 즉 레이디 제인 십섕크스에게 브리그스 양을 소개하며 말했다."], ["He introduced Miss Briggs to the lady with whom he happened to be walking, the Lady Jane Sheepshanks, saying, \"Lady Jane, permit me to introduce to you my aunt's kindest friend and most affectionate companion, Miss Briggs, whom you know under another title, as authoress of the delightful 'Lyrics of the Heart,'", "\"레이디 제인, 제 고모의 더없이 다정한 벗이자 더없이 애정 깊은 말동무인 브리그스 양을 소개해 드리겠습니다. 당신이 그토록 좋아하시는 저 즐거운 시집 『마음의 노래』의 저자로, 다른 이름으로 알고 계신 바로 그분이지요.\""], ["of which you are so fond.\" Lady Jane blushed too as she held out a kind little hand to Miss Briggs, and said something very civil and incoherent about mamma, and proposing to call on Miss Crawley, and being glad to be made known to the friends and relatives of Mr.", "레이디 제인도 얼굴을 붉히며 브리그스 양에게 다정한 작은 손을 내밀고는, 어머니에 관해 무어라 매우 공손하지만 두서없는 말을 하며 크롤리 양을 방문하겠다는 뜻과, 크롤리 씨의 벗과 친척들을 알게 되어 기쁘다는 말을 했다."], ["Crawley; and with soft dove-like eyes saluted Miss Briggs as they separated, while Pitt Crawley treated her to a profound courtly bow, such as he had used to H.H. the Duchess of Pumpernickel, when he was attache at that court.", "그러고는 헤어지면서 비둘기처럼 부드러운 눈길로 브리그스 양에게 인사를 건넸고, 피트 크롤리는 그가 풍퍼니켈 공작 부인 전하의 궁정에서 수행원으로 있던 시절 그분께 올리던 것 같은, 더없이 정중하고 격식 차린 인사를 그녀에게 보냈다."]], [["The artful diplomatist and disciple of the Machiavellian Binkie!", "저 교활한 외교가, 마키아벨리식 빈키의 제자여! 가엾은 브리그스의 초기 시집을 레이디 제인에게 선물한 것도 바로 그였다."], ["It was he who had given Lady Jane that copy of poor Briggs's early poems, which he remembered to have seen at Queen's Crawley, with a dedication from the poetess to his father's late wife; and he brought the volume with him to Brighton, reading it in the Southampton coach and marking it with his own pencil, before he presented it to the gentle Lady Jane.", "그는 그 시집을 퀸스크롤리에서 본 기억이 있었는데, 거기에는 그 시인이 자기 아버지의 작고한 전처에게 바친 헌사가 적혀 있었다. 그는 그 책을 브라이턴까지 가져와, 사우샘프턴발 마차 안에서 읽으며 손수 연필로 표시를 해 둔 다음, 그 온화한 레이디 제인에게 건넨 것이었다."]], [["It was he, too, who laid before Lady Southdown the great advantages which might occur from an intimacy between her family and Miss Crawley--advantages both worldly and spiritual, he said: for Miss Crawley was now quite alone; the monstrous dissipation and alliance of his brother Rawdon had estranged her affections from that reprobate young man; the greedy tyranny and avarice of Mrs.", "레이디 사우스다운 앞에, 자기 가문과 크롤리 양 사이의 친교에서 생겨날 수 있는 큰 이점을—세속적인 이점과 영적인 이점 양쪽 모두를, 그라고 했다—펼쳐 보인 것도 바로 그였다. 크롤리 양은 이제 완전히 혼자였다."], ["Bute Crawley had caused the old lady to revolt against the exorbitant pretensions of that part of the family; and though he himself had held off all his life from cultivating Miss Crawley's friendship, with perhaps an improper pride, he thought now that every becoming means should be taken, both to save her soul from perdition, and to secure her fortune to himself as the head of the house of Crawley.", "동생 로던의 그 끔찍한 방탕과 혼인이 그 망나니 같은 젊은이로부터 그녀의 애정을 멀어지게 했고, 뷰트 크롤리 부인의 탐욕스러운 횡포와 인색함이 그 노부인으로 하여금 그 식구들의 지나친 요구에 반기를 들게 만들었다. 그리고 비록 그 자신은 평생토록, 어쩌면 부적절한 자존심으로 크롤리 양과의 친교를 가꾸기를 멀리해 왔으나, 이제는 그녀의 영혼을 파멸에서 구하기 위해서도, 또 크롤리 가문의 수장인 자신에게 그 재산을 확보하기 위해서도 온갖 합당한 수단을 강구해야 마땅하다고 그는 생각했다."]], [["The strong-minded Lady Southdown quite agreed in both proposals of her son-in-law, and was for converting Miss Crawley off-hand.", "저 의지 굳센 레이디 사우스다운은 사위 될 사람의 두 가지 제안에 전적으로 동의했고, 당장에 크롤리 양을 개종시키자는 쪽이었다."], ["At her own home, both at Southdown and at Trottermore Castle, this tall and awful missionary of the truth rode about the country in her barouche with outriders, launched packets of tracts among the cottagers and tenants, and would order Gaffer Jones to be converted, as she would order Goody Hicks to take a James's powder, without appeal, resistance, or benefit of clergy.", "자기 영지인 사우스다운과 트로터모어 성에서 진리의 이 키 크고 위풍당당한 선교사는 마부를 거느린 사륜마차를 타고 시골을 누비며, 오두막 사람들과 소작인들에게 소책자 꾸러미를 뿌려 댔고, 가퍼 존스를 개종시키라고 명하기를, 마치 구디 힉스에게 제임스 가루약을 먹으라고 명하듯 했으니, 항변이고 저항이고 성직자의 면죄도 허락되지 않았다."], ["My Lord Southdown, her late husband, an epileptic and simple-minded nobleman, was in the habit of approving of everything which his Matilda did and thought.", "그녀의 작고한 남편 사우스다운 경은 간질을 앓는 단순한 성품의 귀족으로, 자기 마틸다가 하고 생각하는 모든 일을 으레 옳다 하는 사람이었다."], ["So that whatever changes her own belief might undergo (and it accommodated itself to a prodigious variety of opinion, taken from all sorts of doctors among the Dissenters) she had not the least scruple in ordering all her tenants and inferiors to follow and believe after her.", "그러니 그녀 자신의 믿음이 어떤 변화를 겪든(그것은 비국교파의 온갖 종류의 박사들에게서 따온 실로 어마어마하게 다양한 견해에 맞춰 변신했다), 그녀는 자기 소작인과 아랫사람들에게 자기를 따라 자기처럼 믿으라고 명하는 데 조금의 거리낌도 없었다."], ["Thus whether she received the Reverend Saunders McNitre, the Scotch divine; or the Reverend Luke Waters, the mild Wesleyan; or the Reverend Giles Jowls, the illuminated Cobbler, who dubbed himself Reverend as Napoleon crowned himself Emperor--the household, children, tenantry of my Lady Southdown were expected to go down on their knees with her Ladyship, and say Amen to the prayers of either Doctor.", "그리하여 그녀가 스코틀랜드 신학자 손더스 맥니터 목사를 맞이하든, 온건한 웨슬리파 루크 워터스 목사를 맞이하든, 나폴레옹이 스스로 황제 관을 썼듯 스스로를 목사라 칭한 저 깨우친 구두장이 자일스 자울스 목사를 맞이하든, 레이디 사우스다운의 식솔과 자식과 소작인들은 부인과 함께 무릎을 꿇고 그 어느 박사의 기도에든 아멘을 외쳐야 했다."], ["During these exercises old Southdown, on account of his invalid condition, was allowed to sit in his own room, and have negus and the paper read to him.", "이런 예배가 진행되는 동안, 늙은 사우스다운은 병약한 처지인 까닭에 자기 방에 앉아 니거스를 마시며 신문을 읽어 주는 것을 들어도 좋다는 허락을 받았다."], ["Lady Jane was the old Earl's favourite daughter, and tended him and loved him sincerely: as for Lady Emily, the authoress of the \"Washerwoman of Finchley Common,\"", "레이디 제인은 늙은 백작이 가장 아끼는 딸로, 그를 돌보며 진심으로 사랑했다."], ["her denunciations of future punishment (at this period, for her opinions modified afterwards) were so awful that they used to frighten the timid old gentleman her father, and the physicians declared his fits always occurred after one of her Ladyship's sermons.", "한편 『핀칠리 공유지의 빨래하는 여인』의 저자 레이디 에밀리로 말하자면, 그녀가 (이 무렵에 그러했다는 것이고, 그녀의 견해는 나중에 누그러졌다) 내세의 형벌을 규탄하는 것이 어찌나 무시무시했던지, 아버지인 그 소심한 노인을 늘 겁에 질리게 했고, 의사들은 그의 발작이 언제나 부인의 설교 뒤에 일어난다고 단언할 정도였다."]], [["\"I will certainly call,\"", "\"꼭 방문하지요.\""], ["said Lady Southdown then, in reply to the exhortation of her daughter's pretendu, Mr.", "그러자 레이디 사우스다운이 딸의 약혼자 피트 크롤리 씨의 간곡한 권유에 답하여 말했다."], ["Pitt Crawley--\"Who is Miss Crawley's medical man?\"", "\"크롤리 양의 주치의는 누구인가요?\""]], [["Mr. Crawley mentioned the name of Mr. Creamer.", "크롤리 씨는 크리머 씨의 이름을 댔다."]], [["\"A most dangerous and ignorant practitioner, my dear Pitt.", "\"더없이 위험하고 무지한 돌팔이로구려, 친애하는 피트."], ["I have providentially been the means of removing him from several houses: though in one or two instances I did not arrive in time.", "나는 섭리의 인도로 여러 집에서 그자를 쫓아내는 수단이 되어 주었지요. 한두 경우에는 제때 당도하지 못하기도 했지만 말이오."], ["I could not save poor dear General Glanders, who was dying under the hands of that ignorant man--dying.", "그 가엾은 글랜더스 장군은 끝내 구하지 못했어요. 그 무지한 자의 손에서 죽어 가고 있었지—죽어 가고 있었어요."], ["He rallied a little under the Podgers' pills which I administered to him; but alas!", "내가 먹인 포저스 환약 덕분에 잠시 기운을 차리긴 했지만, 아!"], ["it was too late.", "너무 늦었던 거지요."], ["His death was delightful, however; and his change was only for the better; Creamer, my dear Pitt, must leave your aunt.\"", "그렇지만 그의 임종은 더없이 평안했고, 그의 변화는 오직 더 나은 쪽으로의 것이었어요. 크리머는, 친애하는 피트, 당신 고모 곁에서 반드시 떠나야 합니다.\""]], [["Pitt expressed his perfect acquiescence.", "피트는 전적으로 찬동하는 뜻을 표했다."], ["He, too, had been carried along by the energy of his noble kinswoman, and future mother-in-law.", "그 역시 이 고귀한 친족이자 장래의 장모 될 사람의 기세에 휩쓸려 온 터였다."], ["He had been made to accept Saunders McNitre, Luke Waters, Giles Jowls, Podgers' Pills, Rodgers'", "그는 손더스 맥니터, 루크 워터스, 자일스 자울스, 포저스 환약, 로저스 환약, 포키 영약(靈藥) 등, 영적이든 세속적이든 부인의 온갖 처방을 받아들이게 되었다."], ["Pills, Pokey's Elixir, every one of her Ladyship's remedies spiritual or temporal.", "그는 그 집을 나설 때마다 부인의 엉터리 신학서와 약을 한 무더기씩 공손히 가지고 나오지 않은 적이 없었다."], ["He never left her house without carrying respectfully away with him piles of her quack theology and medicine.", "오, 허영의 시장에서 함께 떠도는 나의 형제 동무들이여, 그대들 가운데 이런 인자한 폭군을 알지도, 그 밑에서 고통받지도 않는 사람이 어디 있겠는가?"], ["O, my dear brethren and fellow-sojourners in Vanity Fair, which among you does not know and suffer under such benevolent despots?", "그들에게 \"친애하는 부인, 작년에 부인의 명대로 포저스 특효약을 먹었고 그것을 믿고 있습니다."], ["It is in vain you say to them, \"Dear Madam, I took Podgers' specific at your orders last year, and believe in it.", "한데 왜, 어째서 이제 와서 그것을 부정하고 로저스의 신조를 받아들여야 한단 말입니까?\""], ["Why, why am I to recant and accept the Rodgers'", "하고 말해 봐야 소용없는 일이다."], ["articles now?\"", "도리가 없다."], ["There is no help for it; the faithful proselytizer, if she cannot convince by argument, bursts into tears, and the refusant finds himself, at the end of the contest, taking down the bolus, and saying, \"Well, well, Rodgers'", "그 독실한 전도사는 논리로 설득하지 못하면 눈물을 터뜨리고, 그러면 거부하던 사람은 다툼의 끝에 가서 결국 그 알약을 받아 삼키며 \"알겠습니다, 알겠어요, 로저스 약으로 하지요\""], ["be it.\"", "하고 마는 것이다."]], [["\"And as for her spiritual state,\"", "\"그리고 그분의 영적인 상태로 말할 것 같으면,\" 부인이 말을 이었다."], ["continued the Lady, \"that of course must be looked to immediately: with Creamer about her, she may go off any day: and in what a condition, my dear Pitt, in what a dreadful condition!", "\"그건 물론 당장 살펴봐야 할 일이지요. 크리머가 곁에 붙어 있으니 언제 떠나실지 모르는 노릇이고, 그것도 어떤 처지로 떠나신단 말입니까, 친애하는 피트, 얼마나 끔찍한 처지로!"], ["I will send the Reverend Mr. Irons to her instantly.", "당장 아이언스 목사를 그분께 보내겠어요."], ["Jane, write a line to the Reverend Bartholomew Irons, in the third person, and say that I desire the pleasure of his company this evening at tea at half-past six.", "제인, 바살러뮤 아이언스 목사께 삼인칭으로 짧은 편지를 써서, 오늘 저녁 여섯 시 반에 차를 함께 들 영광을 베풀어 주십사 청한다고 전하렴."], ["He is an awakening man; he ought to see Miss Crawley before she rests this night.", "그분은 영혼을 일깨우는 분이야. 크롤리 양이 오늘 밤 잠들기 전에 꼭 그분을 뵈어야 한다."], ["And Emily, my love, get ready a packet of books for Miss Crawley. Put up 'A Voice from the Flames,'", "그리고 에밀리, 얘야, 크롤리 양에게 보낼 책 꾸러미를 준비하렴."], ["'A Trumpet-warning to Jericho,' and the 'Fleshpots Broken; or, the Converted Cannibal.'\"", "『불길 속에서 들려오는 목소리』, 『예리코를 향한 나팔 경고』, 그리고 『깨진 고기 가마솥, 혹은 개종한 식인종』을 챙겨 넣어라.\""]], [["\"And the 'Washerwoman of Finchley Common,' Mamma,\"", "\"그리고 『핀칠리 공유지의 빨래하는 여인』도요, 어머니.\""], ["said Lady Emily.", "레이디 에밀리가 말했다."], ["\"It is as well to begin soothingly at first.\"", "\"처음에는 부드럽게 시작하는 편이 나으니까요.\""]], [["\"Stop, my dear ladies,\"", "\"잠깐만요, 친애하는 숙녀분들.\""], ["said Pitt, the diplomatist.", "외교가 피트가 말했다."], ["\"With every deference to the opinion of my beloved and respected Lady Southdown, I think it would be quite unadvisable to commence so early upon serious topics with Miss Crawley.", "\"제가 사랑하고 존경하는 레이디 사우스다운의 의견을 전적으로 존중하옵니다만, 크롤리 양에게 그토록 일찍부터 진중한 주제로 시작하는 것은 결코 권할 만한 일이 못 된다고 생각합니다."], ["Remember her delicate condition, and how little, how very little accustomed she has hitherto been to considerations connected with her immortal welfare.\"", "그분의 섬약한 상태와, 또 그분이 지금까지 자신의 불멸의 안녕에 관한 일을 얼마나 적게, 정말이지 얼마나 적게 생각하며 지내 오셨는지를 헤아려 주십시오.\""]], [["\"Can we then begin too early, Pitt?\"", "\"그러면 우리가 너무 일찍 시작할 수도 있다는 건가요, 피트?\""], ["said Lady Emily, rising with six little books already in her hand.", "레이디 에밀리가 벌써 작은 책 여섯 권을 손에 든 채 일어서며 말했다."]], [["\"If you begin abruptly, you will frighten her altogether.", "\"그렇게 다짜고짜 시작하면 그분을 통째로 겁먹게 할 뿐입니다."], ["I know my aunt's worldly nature so well as to be sure that any abrupt attempt at conversion will be the very worst means that can be employed for the welfare of that unfortunate lady.", "저는 제 고모의 세속적인 천성을 너무도 잘 알기에, 어떤 식으로든 다짜고짜 개종을 시도하는 것이야말로 그 불운한 부인의 안녕을 위해 쓸 수 있는 가장 나쁜 수단이 되리라고 확신합니다."], ["You will only frighten and annoy her.", "그분을 겁먹고 성가시게 할 뿐이지요."], ["She will very likely fling the books away, and refuse all acquaintance with the givers.\"", "십중팔구 책을 내던지고는 그것을 준 사람들과는 아예 상종도 하지 않으려 할 겁니다.\""]], [["\"You are as worldly as Miss Crawley, Pitt,\"", "\"당신도 크롤리 양만큼이나 세속적이군요, 피트.\""], ["said Lady Emily, tossing out of the room, her books in her hand.", "레이디 에밀리가 책을 손에 든 채 방 밖으로 휙 나가며 말했다."]], [["\"And I need not tell you, my dear Lady Southdown,\" Pitt continued, in a low voice, and without heeding the interruption, \"how fatal a little want of gentleness and caution may be to any hopes which we may entertain with regard to the worldly possessions of my aunt. Remember she has seventy thousand pounds; think of her age, and her highly nervous and delicate condition; I know that she has destroyed the will which was made in my brother's (Colonel Crawley's) favour: it is by soothing that wounded spirit that we must lead it into the right path, and not by frightening it; and so I think you will agree with me that--that--'", "\"그리고 굳이 말씀드릴 것까지도 없겠습니다만, 친애하는 레이디 사우스다운,\" 피트가 그 방해에 아랑곳하지 않고 낮은 목소리로 말을 이었다. \"온화함과 신중함이 조금만 모자라도, 제 고모의 세속적 재산에 관해 우리가 품을 만한 어떤 기대에든 얼마나 치명적일 수 있는지 말입니다. 그분에게 칠만 파운드가 있다는 것을 기억해 주십시오. 그분의 연세와, 더없이 신경이 예민하고 섬약한 상태를 헤아려 주십시오. 저는 그분이 제 동생(크롤리 대령)에게 유리하게 작성했던 유언장을 이미 없애 버렸다는 것을 알고 있습니다. 그 상처받은 마음을 어루만져 줌으로써 올바른 길로 이끌어야지, 겁을 주어서는 안 됩니다. 그러니 부인께서도 제 말에 동의하시리라 생각합니다, 그러니까—그러니까—\""]], [["\"Of course, of course,\"", "\"물론이지요, 물론이고말고요.\""], ["Lady Southdown remarked.", "레이디 사우스다운이 말했다."], ["\"Jane, my love, you need not send that note to Mr. Irons.", "\"제인, 얘야, 그 편지는 아이언스 씨께 보내지 않아도 되겠다."], ["If her health is such that discussions fatigue her, we will wait her amendment.", "그분 건강이 토론으로 피로해질 정도라면, 차도를 기다리도록 하자."], ["I will call upon Miss Crawley tomorrow.\"", "내일 크롤리 양을 방문하마.\""]], [["\"And if I might suggest, my sweet lady,\"", "\"그리고 한 가지 여쭙자면, 친애하는 부인,\" 피트가 부드러운 어조로 말했다."], ["Pitt said in a bland tone, \"it would be as well not to take our precious Emily, who is too enthusiastic; but rather that you should be accompanied by our sweet and dear Lady Jane.\"", "\"너무 열성적인 우리 소중한 에밀리는 데려가지 않는 편이 좋겠습니다. 오히려 우리의 상냥하고 다정한 레이디 제인을 동행하게 하시는 것이 어떨까 합니다.\""]], [["\"Most certainly, Emily would ruin everything,\"", "\"틀림없이 에밀리는 모든 걸 망쳐 놓을 거예요.\""], ["Lady Southdown said; and this time agreed to forego her usual practice, which was, as we have said, before she bore down personally upon any individual whom she proposed to subjugate, to fire in a quantity of tracts upon the menaced party (as a charge of the French was always preceded by a furious cannonade).", "레이디 사우스다운이 말했다. 그리하여 그녀는 이번만큼은 평소의 관행을 포기하기로 했으니, 그 관행이란, 앞서 말했듯 굴복시키려고 점찍은 사람에게 몸소 들이닥치기에 앞서 그 위협받는 대상에게 소책자를 한 무더기 퍼붓는 것이었다(마치 프랑스군의 돌격에 앞서 늘 맹렬한 포격이 선행되었듯이 말이다)."], ["Lady Southdown, we say, for the sake of the invalid's health, or for the sake of her soul's ultimate welfare, or for the sake of her money, agreed to temporise.", "다시 말하건대, 레이디 사우스다운은 환자의 건강을 위해서든, 그 영혼의 궁극적 안녕을 위해서든, 아니면 그 돈을 위해서든, 잠시 형편을 보아 가며 미루기로 한 것이었다."]], [["The next day, the great Southdown female family carriage, with the Earl's coronet and the lozenge (upon which the three lambs trottant argent upon the field vert of the Southdowns, were quartered with sable on a bend or, three snuff-mulls gules, the cognizance of the house of Binkie), drove up in state to Miss Crawley's door, and the tall serious footman handed in to Mr. Bowls her Ladyship's cards for Miss Crawley, and one likewise for Miss Briggs. By way of compromise, Lady Emily sent in a packet in the evening for the latter lady, containing copies of the \"Washerwoman,\" and other mild and favourite tracts for Miss B.'s own perusal; and a few for the servants' hall, viz.: \"Crumbs from the Pantry,\" \"The Frying Pan and the Fire,\" and \"The Livery of Sin,\" of a much stronger kind.", "이튿날, 백작의 관(冠)과 마름모꼴 문장(거기에는 사우스다운가의 초록 바탕에 은빛으로 종종걸음 치는 양 세 마리가, 빈키 가문의 표지인 금빛 띠 위 붉은 코담뱃갑 세 개와 검은빛으로 사등분되어 있었다)을 새긴 사우스다운가의 거대한 여성용 가족 마차가 위풍당당하게 크롤리 양의 집 문 앞에 다다랐고, 키 크고 진중한 하인이 보울스 씨에게 크롤리 양 앞으로 된 부인의 명함과 브리그스 양 앞으로 된 명함을 한 장씩 건넸다. 타협의 표시로 레이디 에밀리는 그날 저녁 브리그스 양에게 꾸러미를 하나 보냈는데, 거기에는 브리그스 양 본인이 읽을 『빨래하는 여인』 몇 부와 그 밖의 온건하고 애독되는 소책자들이 들어 있었고, 하인들 방에 둘 것으로 훨씬 강도 높은 책 몇 권, 즉 『식료품 저장실에서 나온 부스러기』, 『프라이팬과 불』, 『죄의 제복』 따위도 들어 있었다."]], [["James Crawley's Pipe Is Put Out\nThe amiable behaviour of Mr. Crawley, and Lady Jane's kind reception of her, highly flattered Miss Briggs, who was enabled to speak a good word for the latter, after the cards of the Southdown family had been presented to Miss Crawley.", "크롤리 씨의 상냥한 처신과 제인 부인의 다정한 환대는 브리그스 양을 무척이나 우쭐하게 만들었으니, 사우스다운 가문의 명함이 크롤리 양에게 전해진 뒤로 그녀는 제인 부인을 위해 좋은 말 한마디쯤 거들 수 있게 된 것이었다."], ["A Countess's card left personally too for her, Briggs, was not a little pleasing to the poor friendless companion.", "게다가 백작 부인의 명함이 가엾고 의지가지없는 말동무인 브리그스 자신 앞으로도 직접 남겨졌다는 사실은 적잖이 기쁜 일이었다."], ["\"What could Lady Southdown mean by leaving a card upon you, I wonder, Miss Briggs?\"", "\"사우스다운 부인이 대체 무슨 생각으로 자네에게까지 명함을 남기고 갔는지 모르겠군, 브리그스 양?\""], ["said the republican Miss Crawley; upon which the companion meekly said \"that she hoped there could be no harm in a lady of rank taking notice of a poor gentlewoman,\"", "공화주의자인 크롤리 양이 말했다. 그러자 말동무는 다소곳이 대꾸하기를 \"지체 높은 부인께서 가난한 양가 출신 여인을 눈여겨봐 주신다고 해서 나쁠 것은 없지 않겠어요\"라고 했고, 그 명함을 자신의 반짇고리 안, 가장 소중히 간직하는 개인 보물들 사이에 고이 넣어 두었다."], ["and she put away this card in her work-box amongst her most cherished personal treasures. Furthermore, Miss Briggs explained how she had met Mr.", "더 나아가 브리그스 양은 전날 크롤리 씨가 사촌이자 오랜 약혼녀와 함께 산책하는 모습을 어떻게 마주쳤는지 설명했다."], ["Crawley walking with his cousin and long affianced bride the day before: and she told how kind and gentle-looking the lady was, and what a plain, not to say common, dress she had, all the articles of which, from the bonnet down to the boots, she described and estimated with female accuracy.", "그러면서 그 아가씨가 얼마나 상냥하고 온화해 보이던지, 또 차림새가 얼마나 수수하다 못해 평범하던지 이야기했고, 보닛에서 부츠에 이르기까지 그 모든 물건을 여성 특유의 정확함으로 묘사하고 값을 매겨 보였다."]], [["Miss Crawley allowed Briggs to prattle on without interrupting her too much.", "크롤리 양은 브리그스가 너무 많이 끊기지 않게 두면서 마음껏 재잘대게 내버려 두었다."], ["As she got well, she was pining for society. Mr.", "몸이 차차 나아지자 그녀는 사람들과 어울리고 싶어 안달이 났다."], ["Creamer, her medical man, would not hear of her returning to her old haunts and dissipation in London.", "주치의인 크리머 씨는 그녀가 런던의 옛 소굴과 방탕한 생활로 돌아가는 것을 들으려 하지 않았다."], ["The old spinster was too glad to find any companionship at Brighton, and not only were the cards acknowledged the very next day, but Pitt Crawley was graciously invited to come and see his aunt.", "늙은 노처녀는 브라이턴에서나마 말벗을 찾을 수 있어 더없이 기뻤고, 그리하여 바로 다음 날 명함에 답례를 했을 뿐 아니라, 피트 크롤리는 고모를 뵈러 오라는 황송한 초대까지 받았다. 그는 사우스다운 부인과 그 딸을 데리고 찾아왔다."], ["He came, bringing with him Lady Southdown and her daughter.", "노부인은 크롤리 양의 영혼의 상태에 대해서는 한마디도 꺼내지 않았다."], ["The dowager did not say a word about the state of Miss Crawley's soul; but talked with much discretion about the weather: about the war and the downfall of the monster Bonaparte: and above all, about doctors, quacks, and the particular merits of Dr. Podgers, whom she then patronised.", "다만 날씨에 대해, 전쟁과 저 괴물 보나파르트의 몰락에 대해, 그리고 무엇보다 의사들이며 돌팔이들이며 당시 자신이 후원하던 포저스 박사의 각별한 미덕에 대해 무척 신중하게 이야기를 늘어놓았다."]], [["During their interview Pitt Crawley made a great stroke, and one which showed that, had his diplomatic career not been blighted by early neglect, he might have risen to a high rank in his profession.", "이 회견 도중 피트 크롤리는 큰 한 수를 두었으니, 만약 그의 외교관 경력이 일찌감치 도외시되어 망가지지만 않았던들 그가 그 분야에서 높은 지위까지 올랐으리라는 것을 보여 주는 한 수였다."], ["When the Countess Dowager of Southdown fell foul of the Corsican upstart, as the fashion was in those days, and showed that he was a monster stained with every conceivable crime, a coward and a tyrant not fit to live, one whose fall was predicted, &c., Pitt Crawley suddenly took up the cudgels in favour of the man of Destiny.", "당시 유행에 따라 사우스다운 백작 미망인이 저 코르시카 출신의 벼락출세한 자를 맹렬히 비난하며, 그가 상상할 수 있는 온갖 죄로 더럽혀진 괴물이요, 살아 있을 자격도 없는 겁쟁이이자 폭군이요, 그 몰락이 이미 예언된 자라는 둥 떠들어 대자, 피트 크롤리는 별안간 저 운명의 사나이를 옹호하며 무기를 들고 나선 것이었다."], ["He described the First Consul as he saw him at Paris at the peace of Amiens; when he, Pitt Crawley, had the gratification of making the acquaintance of the great and good Mr.", "그는 아미앵 평화조약 때 파리에서 본 제1통령의 모습을 묘사했다. 바로 그때 그 자신, 피트 크롤리는 위대하고 선량한 폭스 씨와 친분을 맺는 만족을 누렸던 것이다."], ["Fox, a statesman whom, however much he might differ with him, it was impossible not to admire fervently--a statesman who had always had the highest opinion of the Emperor Napoleon.", "폭스 씨로 말하면, 그와 견해가 아무리 다르다 한들 뜨겁게 흠모하지 않을 수 없는 정치가요, 나폴레옹 황제를 늘 더없이 높이 평가해 온 정치가였다."], ["And he spoke in terms of the strongest indignation of the faithless conduct of the allies towards this dethroned monarch, who, after giving himself generously up to their mercy, was consigned to an ignoble and cruel banishment, while a bigoted Popish rabble was tyrannising over France in his stead.", "그리고 그는 폐위된 그 군주를 향한 연합국들의 신의 없는 처사에 대해 가장 강한 분노의 말을 쏟아 냈으니, 너그럽게 제 몸을 그들의 자비에 맡긴 그를 비천하고 잔혹한 유배지로 보내 버린 한편, 그를 대신해 편협한 가톨릭 무리가 프랑스를 폭정으로 다스리고 있다는 것이었다."]], [["This orthodox horror of Romish superstition saved Pitt Crawley in Lady Southdown's opinion, whilst his admiration for Fox and Napoleon raised him immeasurably in Miss Crawley's eyes.", "가톨릭 미신에 대한 이 정통 신앙적 혐오 덕분에 피트 크롤리는 사우스다운 부인의 눈에 들었고, 폭스와 나폴레옹에 대한 그의 흠모는 크롤리 양의 눈에 그를 한량없이 높여 놓았다."], ["Her friendship with that defunct British statesman was mentioned when we first introduced her in this history.", "이 이야기 첫머리에 그녀를 처음 소개할 때 언급했듯, 크롤리 양은 이미 고인이 된 그 영국 정치가와 친분이 있었다."], ["A true Whig, Miss Crawley had been in opposition all through the war, and though, to be sure, the downfall of the Emperor did not very much agitate the old lady, or his ill-treatment tend to shorten her life or natural rest, yet Pitt spoke to her heart when he lauded both her idols; and by that single speech made immense progress in her favour.", "진정한 휘그당원인 크롤리 양은 전쟁 내내 야당 편이었고, 물론 황제의 몰락이 노부인을 그리 동요시키지 않았으며 그가 받은 학대가 그녀의 수명이나 타고난 안식을 줄일 만한 것도 아니었지만, 피트가 그녀의 두 우상을 모두 칭송하자 그것은 그녀의 마음을 정통으로 울렸다. 그리고 그 한 차례 연설만으로 그는 그녀의 호감을 사는 데 엄청난 진척을 이루었다."]], [["\"And what do you think, my dear?\"", "\"넌 어떻게 생각하니, 얘야?\" 크롤리 양이 그 젊은 아가씨에게 물었다."], ["Miss Crawley said to the young lady, for whom she had taken a liking at first sight, as she always did for pretty and modest young people; though it must be owned her affections cooled as rapidly as they rose.", "그녀는 늘 그러하듯 예쁘고 얌전한 젊은 사람에게는 첫눈에 호감을 품었기에 이 아가씨도 마음에 들어 했던 것이다. 다만 그 애정이 솟구치는 속도만큼이나 빨리 식어 버린다는 점은 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["Lady Jane blushed very much, and said \"that she did not understand politics, which she left to wiser heads than hers; but though Mamma was, no doubt, correct, Mr.", "제인 부인은 얼굴이 새빨개져서는 이렇게 말했다. \"저는 정치는 잘 몰라서, 그건 저보다 현명한 분들께 맡겨 두는 편이에요."], ["Crawley had spoken beautifully.\"", "하지만 어머니 말씀이 옳으신 것은 분명하지만, 크롤리 씨가 참 아름답게 말씀하셨어요.\""], ["And when the ladies were retiring at the conclusion of their visit, Miss Crawley hoped \"Lady Southdown would be so kind as to send her Lady Jane sometimes, if she could be spared to come down and console a poor sick lonely old woman.\"", "그리고 방문을 마치고 숙녀들이 물러가려 할 때, 크롤리 양은 \"사우스다운 부인께서 부디 친절을 베푸셔서, 가엾고 병들고 외로운 늙은이를 위로해 줄 수 있도록 이따금 제인 부인을 보내 주셨으면\" 한다고 청했다."], ["This promise was graciously accorded, and they separated upon great terms of amity.", "이 약속은 흔쾌히 받아들여졌고, 그들은 더없이 정다운 사이가 되어 헤어졌다."]], [["\"Don't let Lady Southdown come again, Pitt,\"", "\"사우스다운 부인은 다시 오지 못하게 하렴, 피트.\""], ["said the old lady.", "노부인이 말했다."], ["\"She is stupid and pompous, like all your mother's family, whom I never could endure.", "\"그 사람은 네 어머니 집안 사람들이 다 그렇듯 멍청하고 거드름만 피우는데, 난 그 집안 사람들을 도무지 견딜 수가 없었지."], ["But bring that nice good-natured little Jane as often as ever you please.\"", "하지만 그 착하고 마음씨 고운 어린 제인은 네가 원하는 만큼 얼마든지 자주 데려오너라.\""], ["Pitt promised that he would do so.", "피트는 그렇게 하겠노라 약속했다."], ["He did not tell the Countess of Southdown what opinion his aunt had formed of her Ladyship, who, on the contrary, thought that she had made a most delightful and majestic impression on Miss Crawley.", "그는 고모가 사우스다운 백작 부인에 대해 어떤 평가를 내렸는지 그 부인에게 일러바치지 않았으니, 그 부인은 도리어 자신이 크롤리 양에게 더없이 유쾌하고 위엄 있는 인상을 남겼다고 여기고 있었다."]], [["And so, nothing loth to comfort a sick lady, and perhaps not sorry in her heart to be freed now and again from the dreary spouting of the Reverend Bartholomew Irons, and the serious toadies who gathered round the footstool of the pompous Countess, her mamma, Lady Jane became a pretty constant visitor to Miss Crawley, accompanied her in her drives, and solaced many of her evenings.", "그리하여 병든 부인을 위로하는 일이 싫지 않았던 데다, 바살러뮤 아이언스 목사의 따분한 설교와, 거드름 피우는 백작 부인인 제 어머니의 발치에 모여드는 진지한 아첨꾼들에게서 이따금 풀려나는 것이 마음속으로 그리 서운하지도 않았던 제인 부인은 크롤리 양의 꽤 단골 방문객이 되어, 마차 산책을 함께하고 적적한 저녁나절을 여러 차례 달래 주었다."], ["She was so naturally good and soft, that even Firkin was not jealous of her; and the gentle Briggs thought her friend was less cruel to her when kind Lady Jane was by.", "그녀는 천성이 어찌나 선하고 부드러운지 퍼킨조차 그녀를 질투하지 않았고, 온순한 브리그스는 다정한 제인 부인이 곁에 있을 때면 친구가 자신을 덜 모질게 대한다고 생각했다."], ["Towards her Ladyship Miss Crawley's manners were charming.", "제인 부인을 향한 크롤리 양의 태도는 더없이 매력적이었다."], ["The old spinster told her a thousand anecdotes about her youth, talking to her in a very different strain from that in which she had been accustomed to converse with the godless little Rebecca; for there was that in Lady Jane's innocence which rendered light talking impertinence before her, and Miss Crawley was too much of a gentlewoman to offend such purity.", "늙은 노처녀는 자신의 젊은 시절에 관한 일화를 수천 가지나 들려주었는데, 그 어조는 신을 모르는 어린 레베카와 이야기를 나눌 때와는 사뭇 달랐다. 제인 부인의 순진무구함에는 그녀 앞에서 가벼운 말을 무례하게 만드는 무언가가 있었던 데다, 크롤리 양 또한 그런 순결함을 더럽히기에는 너무나 양가의 여인이었던 까닭이다."], ["The young lady herself had never received kindness except from this old spinster, and her brother and father: and she repaid Miss Crawley's engoument by artless sweetness and friendship.", "이 젊은 아가씨는 이 늙은 노처녀와 자신의 오라비와 아버지를 제외하고는 그 누구에게서도 다정한 대접을 받아 본 적이 없었기에, 크롤리 양의 열성적인 호의를 꾸밈없는 다정함과 우정으로 갚았다."]], [["In the autumn evenings (when Rebecca was flaunting at Paris, the gayest among the gay conquerors there, and our Amelia, our dear wounded Amelia, ah!", "가을 저녁이면(레베카가 파리에서 가장 들떠 있는 정복자들 가운데서도 가장 화려하게 으스대고 있을 때, 또 우리의 애밀리아, 우리의 가엾고 상처 입은 애밀리아, 아아!"], ["where was she?) Lady Jane would be sitting in Miss Crawley's drawing-room singing sweetly to her, in the twilight, her little simple songs and hymns, while the sun was setting and the sea was roaring on the beach.", "그녀는 어디 있었던가?) 제인 부인은 크롤리 양의 응접실에 앉아 황혼 녘에 자신의 소박한 노래와 찬송가를 그녀에게 다정스레 불러 주곤 했으니, 그동안 해는 저물어 가고 바다는 모래밭에 부딪쳐 울부짖었다."], ["The old spinster used to wake up when these ditties ceased, and ask for more.", "늙은 노처녀는 이 가락들이 멎으면 잠에서 깨어나 더 불러 달라고 청하는 것이었다."], ["As for Briggs, and the quantity of tears of happiness which she now shed as she pretended to knit, and looked out at the splendid ocean darkling before the windows, and the lamps of heaven beginning more brightly to shine--who, I say can measure the happiness and sensibility of Briggs?", "브리그스로 말할 것 같으면, 뜨개질을 하는 척하면서 창밖으로 어둑해지는 찬란한 바다와 더 환히 빛나기 시작하는 하늘의 등불들을 내다보며 그녀가 흘리던 그 숱한 행복의 눈물 — 대체 누가, 내 말하건대, 브리그스의 행복과 감수성을 가늠할 수 있단 말인가?"]], [["Pitt meanwhile in the dining-room, with a pamphlet on the Corn Laws or a Missionary Register by his side, took that kind of recreation which suits romantic and unromantic men after dinner.", "그러는 동안 피트는 식당에서 옥수수법에 관한 소책자나 선교 잡지를 곁에 두고, 저녁 식사 후 낭만적인 남자에게도 그렇지 못한 남자에게도 어울리는 그런 종류의 휴식을 취했다."], ["He sipped Madeira: built castles in the air: thought himself a fine fellow: felt himself much more in love with Jane than he had been any time these seven years, during which their liaison had lasted without the slightest impatience on Pitt's part--and slept a good deal.", "그는 마데이라 포도주를 홀짝이고, 공중누각을 짓고, 자신을 멋진 사내라 여기고, 지난 칠 년 동안 — 그동안 두 사람의 연애는 피트 쪽에서 추호의 조바심도 없이 이어져 왔는데 — 어느 때보다도 제인을 사랑한다고 느끼고, 그러고는 한참을 잤다."], ["When the time for coffee came, Mr. Bowls used to enter in a noisy manner, and summon Squire Pitt, who would be found in the dark very busy with his pamphlet.", "커피 시간이 되면 보울스 씨가 요란하게 들어와 피트 도련님을 불렀고, 그러면 도련님은 어둠 속에서 소책자에 골몰해 있는 모습으로 발견되곤 했다."]], [["\"I wish, my love, I could get somebody to play piquet with me,\"", "\"얘야, 누가 나하고 피케 한판 쳐 줄 사람이 있었으면 좋겠구나.\""], ["Miss Crawley said one night when this functionary made his appearance with the candles and the coffee. \"Poor Briggs can no more play than an owl, she is so stupid\"", "어느 날 밤, 이 일꾼이 촛불과 커피를 들고 나타났을 때 크롤리 양이 말했다."], ["(the spinster always took an opportunity of abusing Briggs before the servants); \"and I think I should sleep better if I had my game.\"", "\"가엾은 브리그스는 어찌나 멍청한지 올빼미만큼도 카드를 못 친단다\"(노처녀는 늘 하인들 앞에서 브리그스를 욕할 기회를 놓치지 않았다). \"한판 치고 나면 잠이 더 잘 올 것 같은데 말이야.\""]], [["At this Lady Jane blushed to the tips of her little ears, and down to the ends of her pretty fingers; and when Mr.", "이 말에 제인 부인은 그 작은 귓불 끝까지, 또 그 예쁜 손가락 끝까지 새빨개졌다."], ["Bowls had quitted the room, and the door was quite shut, she said:", "그리고 보울스 씨가 방을 나가고 문이 완전히 닫히자, 그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"Miss Crawley, I can play a little.", "\"크롤리 양, 저 조금 칠 줄 알아요."], ["I used to--to play a little with poor dear papa.\"", "예전에 — 가엾고 그리운 아버지하고 조금 치곤 했어요.\""]], [["\"Come and kiss me.", "\"이리 와서 입 맞춰 다오."], ["Come and kiss me this instant, you dear good little soul,\"", "당장 이리 와서 입 맞춰 다오, 이 사랑스럽고 착한 것아.\""], ["cried Miss Crawley in an ecstasy: and in this picturesque and friendly occupation Mr.", "크롤리 양이 황홀해하며 외쳤다."], ["Pitt found the old lady and the young one, when he came upstairs with him pamphlet in his hand.", "그리고 피트가 소책자를 손에 든 채 위층으로 올라왔을 때, 그는 늙은 부인과 젊은 부인이 이 그림 같고 다정한 일에 빠져 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["How she did blush all the evening, that poor Lady Jane!", "그 가엾은 제인 부인이 저녁 내내 얼마나 얼굴을 붉히던지!"]], [["It must not be imagined that Mr. Pitt Crawley's artifices escaped the attention of his dear relations at the Rectory at Queen's Crawley.", "피트 크롤리 씨의 술책이 퀸스크롤리의 목사관에 사는 그의 정다운 친척들의 주의를 비켜 갔으리라 여겨서는 안 된다."], ["Hampshire and Sussex lie very close together, and Mrs. Bute had friends in the latter county who took care to inform her of all, and a great deal more than all, that passed at Miss Crawley's house at Brighton.", "햄프셔와 서식스는 아주 가까이 붙어 있는 데다, 뷰트 부인은 서식스 주에 친구들이 있어서, 브라이턴의 크롤리 양 집에서 벌어지는 일이라면 무엇이든, 아니 그 전부에다 훨씬 더 많은 것까지 그녀에게 빠짐없이 알려 주었던 것이다."], ["Pitt was there more and more.", "피트는 점점 더 그 집에 드나들었다."], ["He did not come for months together to the Hall, where his abominable old father abandoned himself completely to rum-and-water, and the odious society of the Horrocks family.", "그가 몇 달이고 본가에는 한 번도 들르지 않았으니, 그곳에서는 그의 가증스러운 늙은 아버지가 럼주에 물 탄 술과 호록스 가족의 역겨운 무리에 완전히 빠져 있었다."], ["Pitt's success rendered the Rector's family furious, and Mrs. Bute regretted more (though she confessed less) than ever her monstrous fault in so insulting Miss Briggs, and in being so haughty and parsimonious to Bowls and Firkin, that she had not a single person left in Miss Crawley's household to give her information of what took place there.", "피트의 성공은 목사 가족을 격분케 했고, 뷰트 부인은 브리그스 양을 그토록 모욕하고 보울스와 퍼킨에게 그토록 거만하고 인색하게 굴어, 크롤리 양의 집안에서 그곳 일을 알려 줄 사람이 단 한 명도 남지 않게 만든 자신의 끔찍한 실수를 그 어느 때보다도 더 후회했다(물론 입 밖으로 내는 것은 덜했지만)."], ["\"It was all Bute's collar-bone,\"", "\"다 뷰트의 쇄골 탓이었어요.\""], ["she persisted in saying; \"if that had not broke, I never would have left her.", "그녀는 끈질기게 주장했다. \"그게 부러지지만 않았어도 절대 그분 곁을 떠나지 않았을 거예요."], ["I am a martyr to duty and to your odious unclerical habit of hunting, Bute.\"", "나는 의무와, 당신의 그 역겹고 목사답지 못한 사냥 버릇 때문에 순교한 거라고요, 뷰트.\""]], [["\"Hunting; nonsense!", "\"사냥이라니, 말도 안 되는 소리!"], ["It was you that frightened her, Barbara,\"", "그 사람을 겁먹게 한 건 당신이었어, 바버라.\""], ["the divine interposed.", "목사가 끼어들었다."], ["\"You're a clever woman, but you've got a devil of a temper; and you're a screw with your money, Barbara.\"", "\"당신은 똑똑한 여자지, 그렇지만 성질머리가 지독해. 게다가 돈에는 어찌나 인색한지, 바버라.\""]], [["\"You'd have been screwed in gaol, Bute, if I had not kept your money.\"", "\"내가 당신 돈을 챙겨 두지 않았으면 당신은 빚더미에 눌려 감옥에 갇혔을걸요, 뷰트.\""]], [["\"I know I would, my dear,\"", "\"그랬을 테지, 나도 알아, 여보.\""], ["said the Rector, good-naturedly.", "목사가 사람 좋게 말했다."], ["\"You ARE a clever woman, but you manage too well, you know\": and the pious man consoled himself with a big glass of port.", "\"당신은 똑똑한 여자야, 그렇지만 일을 너무 야무지게 처리한단 말이야, 알지?\" 그러고는 이 경건한 사내는 큼직한 포도주 한 잔으로 스스로를 위로했다."]], [["\"What the deuce can she find in that spooney of a Pitt Crawley?\"", "\"대체 그 사람이 그 얼간이 피트 크롤리한테서 뭘 본단 말이야?\""], ["he continued.", "그가 말을 이었다."], ["\"The fellow has not pluck enough to say Bo to a goose.", "\"그 녀석은 거위한테 야단칠 배짱도 없는 위인이라고."], ["I remember when Rawdon, who is a man, and be hanged to him, used to flog him round the stables as if he was a whipping-top: and Pitt would go howling home to his ma--ha, ha!", "사내대장부인 로던이, 빌어먹을 녀석이지만, 그놈을 팽이 돌리듯 마구간 둘레로 채찍질하며 쫓아다니던 게 기억나는데. 그러면 피트는 엄마한테로 엉엉 울며 달려가곤 했지 — 하, 하!"], ["Why, either of my boys would whop him with one hand.", "아니, 우리 집 애들 중 누구라도 한 손으로 그놈을 두들겨 팰 수 있을걸."], ["Jim says he's remembered at Oxford as Miss Crawley still--the spooney.", "짐 말로는 옥스퍼드에서 아직도 그놈을 '크롤리 양'이라고 부른다더군 — 그 얼간이를.\""]], [["\"I say, Barbara,\"", "\"여보, 바버라.\""], ["his reverence continued, after a pause.", "잠시 후 목사가 말을 이었다."]], [["\"What?\"", "\"뭐예요?\""], ["said Barbara, who was biting her nails, and drumming the table.", "손톱을 물어뜯으며 식탁을 톡톡 두드리던 바버라가 말했다."]], [["\"I say, why not send Jim over to Brighton to see if he can do anything with the old lady.", "\"내 말은, 짐을 브라이턴에 보내서 그 노부인한테 뭐라도 해 볼 수 있는지 알아보면 어떻겠냐는 거야."], ["He's very near getting his degree, you know.", "알다시피 그 녀석 학위도 거의 다 따 가잖아."], ["He's only been plucked twice--so was I--but he's had the advantages of Oxford and a university education.", "두 번밖에 낙제 안 했는데 — 나도 그랬지 — 어쨌든 그 녀석은 옥스퍼드와 대학 교육의 혜택을 받았어."], ["He knows some of the best chaps there.", "거기 제일 잘나가는 친구들도 몇 알고 지내고."], ["He pulls stroke in the Boniface boat.", "보니페이스 보트팀에서 스트로크도 맡고 있어."], ["He's a handsome feller.", "잘생긴 녀석이지."], ["D---- it, ma'am, let's put him on the old woman, hey, and tell him to thrash Pitt if he says anything.", "제기랄, 부인, 그 녀석을 그 노파한테 들이밀어 봅시다, 응? 그리고 피트가 뭐라도 지껄이거든 흠씬 패 주라고 일러 두는 거지."], ["Ha, ha, ha!", "하, 하, 하!\""]], [["\"Jim might go down and see her, certainly,\"", "\"짐이 내려가서 그분을 뵐 수야 있겠지요.\""], ["the housewife said; adding with a sigh, \"If we could but get one of the girls into the house; but she could never endure them, because they are not pretty!\"", "안주인이 말하고는, 한숨을 쉬며 덧붙였다. \"딸애들 중 하나라도 그 집에 들여놓을 수 있다면 좋으련만, 그분은 예쁘지 않다고 도무지 우리 애들을 못 견뎌 하니.\""], ["Those unfortunate and well-educated women made themselves heard from the neighbouring drawing-room, where they were thrumming away, with hard fingers, an elaborate music-piece on the piano-forte, as their mother spoke; and indeed, they were at music, or at backboard, or at geography, or at history, the whole day long.", "그 불운하고 잘 교육받은 처녀들은 어머니가 말하는 동안 옆 응접실에서 자기들 존재를 알리고 있었으니, 거기서 그들은 굳은 손가락으로 피아노포르테로 복잡한 곡 하나를 둥당거리고 있던 참이었다. 실은 그 처녀들은 하루 종일 음악이 아니면 자세 교정판이나 지리나 역사에 매달려 살았다."], ["But what avail all these accomplishments, in Vanity Fair, to girls who are short, poor, plain, and have a bad complexion? Mrs.", "그러나 허영의 시장에서 키 작고 가난하고 못생긴 데다 안색마저 나쁜 처녀들에게, 이 모든 교양이 다 무슨 소용이란 말인가?"], ["Bute could think of nobody but the Curate to take one of them off her hands; and Jim coming in from the stable at this minute, through the parlour window, with a short pipe stuck in his oilskin cap, he and his father fell to talking about odds on the St.", "뷰트 부인은 그 딸들 중 하나를 떠맡아 줄 사람으로 보좌 목사 말고는 아무도 떠올릴 수 없었다."], ["Leger, and the colloquy between the Rector and his wife ended.", "바로 이때 짐이 기름 먹인 모자에 짧은 파이프를 꽂은 채 응접실 창문을 통해 마구간에서 들어왔고, 그와 그의 아버지는 세인트레저 경마의 배당률 이야기로 빠져들었으니, 그리하여 목사와 그 아내 사이의 대화는 끝이 났다."]], [["Mrs. Bute did not augur much good to the cause from the sending of her son James as an ambassador, and saw him depart in rather a despairing mood.", "뷰트 부인은 아들 제임스를 사절로 보낸다고 해서 이 일에 큰 성과가 있으리라고는 기대하지 않았기에, 자못 절망적인 심정으로 그가 떠나는 것을 지켜보았다."], ["Nor did the young fellow himself, when told what his mission was to be, expect much pleasure or benefit from it; but he was consoled by the thought that possibly the old lady would give him some handsome remembrance of her, which would pay a few of his most pressing bills at the commencement of the ensuing Oxford term, and so took his place by the coach from Southampton, and was safely landed at Brighton on the same evening with his portmanteau, his favourite bull-dog Towzer, and an immense basket of farm and garden produce, from the dear Rectory folks to the dear Miss Crawley.", "또한 자신의 임무가 무엇인지 들었을 때, 젊은이 자신도 거기서 큰 즐거움이나 이득을 바라지 않았다. 다만 그는 어쩌면 그 노부인이 자신을 기념할 만한 멋진 물건을 줄지도 모르고, 그러면 다가오는 옥스퍼드 학기 초에 가장 급한 청구서 몇 장은 갚을 수 있으리라는 생각으로 위안을 삼고는, 사우샘프턴발 마차에 자리를 잡고 같은 날 저녁 브라이턴에 무사히 내렸다."], ["Considering it was too late to disturb the invalid lady on the first night of his arrival, he put up at an inn, and did not wait upon Miss Crawley until a late hour in the noon of next day.", "그와 함께 여행 가방과, 그가 아끼는 불도그 타우저와, 정다운 목사관 식구들이 정다운 크롤리 양에게 보내는 농장과 정원의 산물을 가득 담은 어마어마한 바구니도 도착했다. 도착한 첫날 밤에 병든 부인을 깨우기에는 너무 늦었다고 여겨, 그는 여관에 묵었고, 이튿날 한낮이 다 되어서야 크롤리 양을 찾았다."]], [["James Crawley, when his aunt had last beheld him, was a gawky lad, at that uncomfortable age when the voice varies between an unearthly treble and a preternatural bass; when the face not uncommonly blooms out with appearances for which Rowland's Kalydor is said to act as a cure; when boys are seen to shave furtively with their sister's scissors, and the sight of other young women produces intolerable sensations of terror in them; when the great hands and ankles protrude a long way from garments which have grown too tight for them; when their presence after dinner is at once frightful to the ladies, who are whispering in the twilight in the drawing-room, and inexpressibly odious to the gentlemen over the mahogany, who are restrained from freedom of intercourse and delightful interchange of wit by the presence of that gawky innocence; when, at the conclusion of the second glass, papa says, \"Jack, my boy, go out and see if the evening holds up,\"", "제임스 크롤리는 고모가 마지막으로 보았을 때만 해도 어색한 풋내기였으니, 목소리가 기괴한 고음과 비정상적인 저음 사이를 오가는 그 거북한 나이였고, 흔히 얼굴에는 롤런드의 칼리도르가 치료제 노릇을 한다는 그런 것들이 만발하는 나이였고, 사내아이들이 누이의 가위로 몰래 면도하는 모습이 눈에 띄고, 다른 젊은 여자들을 보기만 해도 견딜 수 없는 공포의 감정에 휩싸이는 나이였고, 큼직한 손과 발목이 너무 작아진 옷자락에서 한참이나 비어져 나오는 나이였으며, 저녁 식사 후의 그 존재가 황혼 녘 응접실에서 소곤대는 숙녀들에게는 무섭게 느껴지는 동시에, 마호가니 식탁에 둘러앉은 신사들에게는 형언할 수 없이 거슬리는 — 그 어색한 순진함의 존재 탓에 자유로운 교류와 유쾌한 재담의 주고받음이 가로막히는 — 그런 나이였고, 두 번째 잔을 비울 무렵이면 아버지가 \"잭, 얘야, 나가서 저녁 날씨가 갤 것 같은지 좀 보고 오너라\""], ["and the youth, willing to be free, yet hurt at not being yet a man, quits the incomplete banquet.", "하고, 그러면 자유로워지고 싶으면서도 아직 어른 대접을 못 받는 것이 서운한 그 청년이 미완성의 연회 자리를 떠나는, 그런 나이였다."], ["James, then a hobbadehoy, was now become a young man, having had the benefits of a university education, and acquired the inestimable polish which is gained by living in a fast set at a small college, and contracting debts, and being rusticated, and being plucked.", "그때만 해도 풋내기였던 제임스가 이제는 어엿한 청년이 되어 있었으니, 대학 교육의 혜택을 누렸고, 작은 칼리지에서 방탕한 무리와 어울리며 빚을 지고, 정학을 당하고, 낙제하는 과정에서 얻는 그 값을 매길 수 없는 세련됨까지 익힌 터였다."]], [["He was a handsome lad, however, when he came to present himself to his aunt at Brighton, and good looks were always a title to the fickle old lady's favour.", "그래도 브라이턴에서 고모 앞에 인사를 드리러 나섰을 때 그는 잘생긴 청년이었고, 잘생긴 외모란 늘 저 변덕스러운 노부인의 호감을 사는 자격이 되어 주었다."], ["Nor did his blushes and awkwardness take away from it: she was pleased with these healthy tokens of the young gentleman's ingenuousness.", "그의 얼굴 붉힘과 어색함도 그 호감을 깎아내리지 않았으니, 그녀는 이 젊은 신사의 천진함을 드러내는 그 건강한 표시들이 마음에 들었던 것이다."]], [["He said \"he had come down for a couple of days to see a man of his college, and--and to pay my respects to you, Ma'am, and my father's and mother's, who hope you are well.\"", "그는 이렇게 말했다. \"제 칼리지 친구 하나를 만나러 이틀쯤 내려왔는데요, 그리고 — 그리고 고모님께 문안 인사도 드리려고요. 아버지하고 어머니가 고모님 안녕하시길 바라신다고 하셨어요.\""]], [["Pitt was in the room with Miss Crawley when the lad was announced, and looked very blank when his name was mentioned.", "청년의 도착이 알려졌을 때 피트는 크롤리 양과 함께 방에 있었고, 그 이름이 거론되자 매우 떨떠름한 표정을 지었다."], ["The old lady had plenty of humour, and enjoyed her correct nephew's perplexity.", "노부인은 유머가 넘치는 사람이라 그 반듯한 조카의 당혹스러움을 즐겼다."], ["She asked after all the people at the Rectory with great interest; and said she was thinking of paying them a visit.", "그녀는 목사관 식구들 모두의 안부를 큰 관심을 보이며 물었고, 그들을 한번 찾아갈까 생각 중이라고 했다."], ["She praised the lad to his face, and said he was well-grown and very much improved, and that it was a pity his sisters had not some of his good looks; and finding, on inquiry, that he had taken up his quarters at an hotel, would not hear of his stopping there, but bade Mr.", "그녀는 청년을 면전에서 칭찬하며, 잘 자랐고 아주 많이 나아졌다고, 또 그의 누이들이 그 잘생긴 외모를 조금이라도 닮지 못한 것이 안타깝다고 했다. 그리고 물어보아 그가 호텔에 묵고 있다는 것을 알고는, 그곳에 머무는 것을 들으려고도 하지 않고, 보울스 씨에게 당장 제임스 크롤리 씨의 짐을 가져오라고 명했다."], ["Bowls send for Mr.", "\"그리고 잘 듣게, 보울스.\""], ["James Crawley's things instantly; \"and hark ye, Bowls,\"", "그녀가 자못 너그럽게 덧붙였다."], ["she added, with great graciousness, \"you will have the goodness to pay Mr. James's bill.\"", "\"수고스럽겠지만 제임스 씨의 숙박비도 좀 치러 주게나.\""]], [["She flung Pitt a look of arch triumph, which caused that diplomatist almost to choke with envy.", "그녀는 피트에게 짓궂은 승리의 눈길을 던졌고, 그 바람에 이 외교가는 시샘으로 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이었다."], ["Much as he had ingratiated himself with his aunt, she had never yet invited him to stay under her roof, and here was a young whipper-snapper, who at first sight was made welcome there.", "그토록 고모의 환심을 사 왔건만 그녀는 그를 단 한 번도 자기 집에 머물도록 청한 적이 없었는데, 여기 한 애송이가 첫눈에 그곳에서 환대를 받고 있는 것이었다."]], [["\"I beg your pardon, sir,\"", "\"송구합니다만, 나리.\""], ["says Bowls, advancing with a profound bow; \"what 'otel, sir, shall Thomas fetch the luggage from?\"", "보울스가 정중히 절하며 다가와 말했다. \"토머스를 시켜 어느 호텔에서 짐을 가져오게 할까요, 나리?\""]], [["\"O, dam,\"", "\"아, 이런.\""], ["said young James, starting up, as if in some alarm, \"I'll go.\"", "젊은 제임스가 무언가 놀란 듯 벌떡 일어서며 말했다. \"제가 가겠습니다.\""]], [["\"What!\"", "\"무어라고?\""], ["said Miss Crawley.", "크롤리 양이 말했다."]], [["\"The Tom Cribb's Arms,\"", "\"톰 크립스 암스요.\""], ["said James, blushing deeply.", "제임스가 얼굴이 시뻘게져서 말했다."]], [["Miss Crawley burst out laughing at this title. Mr.", "크롤리 양은 그 이름에 폭소를 터뜨렸다."], ["Bowls gave one abrupt guffaw, as a confidential servant of the family, but choked the rest of the volley; the diplomatist only smiled.", "보울스 씨도 이 집안의 믿음직한 하인으로서 갑작스레 껄껄 한바탕 웃음을 터뜨렸으나, 나머지 한바탕은 꿀꺽 삼켰다. 외교가는 그저 빙긋 웃을 뿐이었다."]], [["\"I--I didn't know any better,\"", "\"저 — 저는 더 좋은 데를 몰라서요.\""], ["said James, looking down.", "제임스가 고개를 숙이며 말했다."], ["\"I've never been here before; it was the coachman told me.\"", "\"여기 와 본 적이 없어서, 마부가 일러 준 데로 갔어요.\""], ["The young story-teller!", "이 어린 거짓말쟁이 같으니!"], ["The fact is, that on the Southampton coach, the day previous, James Crawley had met the Tutbury Pet, who was coming to Brighton to make a match with the Rottingdean Fibber; and enchanted by the Pet's conversation, had passed the evening in company with that scientific man and his friends, at the inn in question.", "사실인즉, 전날 사우샘프턴 마차에서 제임스 크롤리는 로팅딘 피버와 시합을 하러 브라이턴으로 오던 터트베리 펫을 만났고, 그 펫의 입담에 홀딱 빠진 나머지 바로 그 여관에서 그 권투 전문가와 그 친구들과 어울려 저녁나절을 보냈던 것이다."]], [["\"I--I'd best go and settle the score,\"", "\"제 — 제가 가서 셈을 치르는 게 제일 좋겠어요.\""], ["James continued.", "제임스가 말을 이었다."], ["\"Couldn't think of asking you, Ma'am,\"", "\"고모님께 부탁드릴 생각은 없었어요.\""], ["he added, generously.", "그가 후하게 덧붙였다."]], [["This delicacy made his aunt laugh the more.", "이 갸륵함에 고모는 더욱 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["\"Go and settle the bill, Bowls,\"", "\"가서 셈을 치르게, 보울스.\""], ["she said, with a wave of her hand, \"and bring it to me.\"", "그녀가 한 손을 휘저으며 말했다. \"그리고 청구서는 내게 가져오고.\""]], [["Poor lady, she did not know what she had done!", "가엾은 부인, 그녀는 자신이 무슨 일을 저질렀는지 알지 못했다!"], ["\"There--there's a little dawg,\"", "\"저 — 저기 작은 개도 한 마리 있는데요.\""], ["said James, looking frightfully guilty.", "제임스가 끔찍하게 켕기는 표정으로 말했다."], ["\"I'd best go for him.", "\"제가 가서 데려오는 게 좋겠어요."], ["He bites footmen's calves.\"", "그놈이 하인들 장딴지를 물거든요.\""]], [["All the party cried out with laughing at this description; even Briggs and Lady Jane, who was sitting mute during the interview between Miss Crawley and her nephew: and Bowls, without a word, quitted the room.", "이 묘사에 자리에 있던 사람들이 모두 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 크롤리 양과 조카가 회견하는 동안 말없이 앉아 있던 브리그스와 제인 부인까지도 그랬다. 그리고 보울스는 한마디 없이 방을 나갔다."]], [["Still, by way of punishing her elder nephew, Miss Crawley persisted in being gracious to the young Oxonian.", "그래도 손위 조카를 벌주려는 셈으로, 크롤리 양은 그 젊은 옥스퍼드 학생에게 한사코 자상하게 굴었다."], ["There were no limits to her kindness or her compliments when they once began.", "그녀의 친절과 칭찬은 일단 시작되면 한도 끝도 없었다."], ["She told Pitt he might come to dinner, and insisted that James should accompany her in her drive, and paraded him solemnly up and down the cliff, on the back seat of the barouche.", "그녀는 피트에게 저녁 식사에 와도 좋다고 했고, 제임스가 자기 마차 산책에 동행해야 한다고 우기며, 사륜마차 뒷좌석에 그를 태우고 절벽 위를 점잖게 오르내리며 데리고 다녔다."], ["During all this excursion, she condescended to say civil things to him: she quoted Italian and French poetry to the poor bewildered lad, and persisted that he was a fine scholar, and was perfectly sure he would gain a gold medal, and be a Senior Wrangler.", "이 외출 내내 그녀는 황송하게도 그에게 정겨운 말을 건넸다. 그녀는 이탈리아어와 프랑스어 시를 인용해 이 가엾고 어리둥절한 청년에게 들려주었고, 그가 훌륭한 학자라고 우기며, 틀림없이 금메달을 따고 수석 우등생이 될 것이라고 단언했다."]], [["\"Haw, haw,\"", "\"하, 하.\""], ["laughed James, encouraged by these compliments; \"Senior Wrangler, indeed; that's at the other shop.\"", "제임스가 이 칭찬에 우쭐해져 웃었다. \"수석 우등생이라니, 원, 그건 다른 가게 이야기예요.\""]], [["\"What is the other shop, my dear child?\"", "\"다른 가게라니 그게 무슨 소리니, 얘야?\""], ["said the lady.", "부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Senior Wranglers at Cambridge, not Oxford,\"", "\"수석 우등생은 옥스퍼드가 아니라 케임브리지에 있는 거예요.\""], ["said the scholar, with a knowing air; and would probably have been more confidential, but that suddenly there appeared on the cliff in a tax-cart, drawn by a bang-up pony, dressed in white flannel coats, with mother-of-pearl buttons, his friends the Tutbury Pet and the Rottingdean Fibber, with three other gentlemen of their acquaintance, who all saluted poor James there in the carriage as he sate.", "학자가 아는 체하는 태도로 말했다. 그러고는 아마 더 허물없이 이야기를 늘어놓았을 테지만, 별안간 절벽 위에 멋들어진 조랑말이 끄는 짐마차 한 대가 나타났으니, 자개 단추가 달린 흰 플란넬 외투를 입은 그의 친구들, 터트베리 펫과 로팅딘 피버가, 안면 있는 다른 신사 세 명과 함께 타고 있었고, 그들 모두가 마차에 앉아 있는 가엾은 제임스에게 인사를 건넸다."], ["This incident damped the ingenuous youth's spirits, and no word of yea or nay could he be induced to utter during the rest of the drive.", "이 일로 천진한 청년은 기가 꺾여, 남은 산책 내내 예니 아니오니 한마디도 입에 올리게 만들 수가 없었다."]], [["On his return he found his room prepared, and his portmanteau ready, and might have remarked that Mr.", "돌아와 보니 그의 방은 준비되어 있었고 여행 가방도 풀려 있었다."], ["Bowls's countenance, when the latter conducted him to his apartments, wore a look of gravity, wonder, and compassion. But the thought of Mr.", "보울스 씨가 그를 방으로 안내할 때 그 얼굴에 자못 엄숙하고 의아하고 측은해하는 빛이 어려 있는 것을 알아챘을 법도 하건만, 보울스 씨에 대한 생각 따위는 그의 머리에 들어오지 않았다."], ["Bowls did not enter his head. He was deploring the dreadful predicament in which he found himself, in a house full of old women, jabbering French and Italian, and talking poetry to him.", "그는 자신이 처한 끔찍한 처지를 한탄하느라 정신이 없었으니, 프랑스어와 이탈리아어를 지껄여 대고 자신에게 시를 읊어 대는 늙은 여자들로 가득한 집에 갇혀 버린 것이었다."], ["\"Reglarly up a tree, by jingo!\"", "\"이거 완전히 나무 위에 갇힌 신세로군, 제기랄!\" 이 수줍은 청년이 외쳤다."], ["exclaimed the modest boy, who could not face the gentlest of her sex--not even Briggs--when she began to talk to him; whereas, put him at Iffley Lock, and he could out-slang the boldest bargeman.", "그는 여자라면 누구든, 가장 온순한 브리그스조차도, 자신에게 말을 걸기 시작하면 마주 볼 수가 없었다. 그러면서도 이프리 갑문에 그를 갖다 놓으면 가장 입 거친 뱃사공도 욕설로 눌러 버릴 수 있는 위인이었다."]], [["At dinner, James appeared choking in a white neckcloth, and had the honour of handing my Lady Jane downstairs, while Briggs and Mr. Crawley followed afterwards, conducting the old lady, with her apparatus of bundles, and shawls, and cushions.", "저녁 식사 자리에 제임스는 흰 넥타이에 목이 졸리는 듯한 모습으로 나타났고, 제인 부인을 아래층으로 모시고 내려가는 영광을 누렸으며, 그동안 브리그스와 크롤리 씨는 보따리며 숄이며 방석이며 온갖 장비를 갖춘 노부인을 부축하며 뒤따라 내려왔다."], ["Half of Briggs's time at dinner was spent in superintending the invalid's comfort, and in cutting up chicken for her fat spaniel.", "식사 동안 브리그스는 절반의 시간을 병든 부인의 편의를 살피고 그녀의 살진 스패니얼에게 닭고기를 잘라 주는 데 썼다."], ["James did not talk much, but he made a point of asking all the ladies to drink wine, and accepted Mr. Crawley's challenge, and consumed the greater part of a bottle of champagne which Mr.", "제임스는 말은 별로 하지 않았으나, 모든 숙녀에게 술을 권하기를 잊지 않았고, 크롤리 씨의 잔 권유에 응했으며, 보울스 씨가 그를 위해 내오라는 명을 받은 샴페인 한 병의 대부분을 비웠다."], ["Bowls was ordered to produce in his honour. The ladies having withdrawn, and the two cousins being left together, Pitt, the ex-diplomatist, he came very communicative and friendly.", "숙녀들이 물러가고 두 사촌만 남게 되자, 전직 외교관 피트는 매우 살갑고 다정해졌다."], ["He asked after James's career at college--what his prospects in life were--hoped heartily he would get on; and, in a word, was frank and amiable.", "그는 제임스의 대학 생활은 어떤지, 앞날의 전망은 어떤지 물었고, 잘되기를 진심으로 바란다고 했으니, 한마디로 솔직하고 상냥했다."], ["James's tongue unloosed with the port, and he told his cousin his life, his prospects, his debts, his troubles at the little-go, and his rows with the proctors, filling rapidly from the bottles before him, and flying from Port to Madeira with joyous activity.", "포도주가 들어가자 제임스의 혀가 풀려, 그는 사촌에게 자신의 삶이며 전망이며 빚이며 예비시험에서 겪은 고초며 학감들과의 다툼까지 털어놓았으니, 앞에 놓인 병들에서 부지런히 잔을 채우며 즐거이 포트와인에서 마데이라로 옮겨 다녔다."]], [["\"The chief pleasure which my aunt has,\" said Mr.", "\"우리 고모님이 가장 즐기시는 일은 말이야,\""], ["Crawley, filling his glass, \"is that people should do as they like in her house.", "크롤리 씨가 자기 잔을 채우며 말했다. \"사람들이 당신 집에서 제 좋을 대로 하는 거란다."], ["This is Liberty Hall, James, and you can't do Miss Crawley a greater kindness than to do as you please, and ask for what you will.", "여긴 자유의 전당이야, 제임스, 그러니 자네가 하고 싶은 대로 하고 원하는 걸 청하는 것보다 크롤리 양을 더 기쁘게 해 드릴 길은 없어."], ["I know you have all sneered at me in the country for being a Tory.", "자네들이 시골에서 내가 토리당이라고 다들 비웃었던 걸 안다."], ["Miss Crawley is liberal enough to suit any fancy.", "크롤리 양은 어떤 취향에도 다 맞출 만큼 진보적인 분이지."], ["She is a Republican in principle, and despises everything like rank or title.\"", "원칙으로는 공화주의자라서, 신분이니 작위니 하는 것은 죄다 경멸하신다고.\""]], [["\"Why are you going to marry an Earl's daughter?\"", "\"그럼 형님은 왜 백작 따님하고 결혼하려는 거예요?\""], ["said James.", "제임스가 말했다."]], [["\"My dear friend, remember it is not poor Lady Jane's fault that she is well born,\"", "\"이보게, 가엾은 제인 부인이 좋은 가문에서 태어난 게 그 사람 잘못이 아니라는 걸 잊지 말게.\""], ["Pitt replied, with a courtly air.", "피트가 점잖은 태도로 대꾸했다."], ["\"She cannot help being a lady.", "\"그 사람이 숙녀로 태어난 걸 어쩌겠나."], ["Besides, I am a Tory, you know.\"", "게다가 나는 토리당이잖나, 알다시피.\""]], [["\"Oh, as for that,\"", "\"오, 그거라면,\" 짐이 말했다."], ["said Jim, \"there's nothing like old blood; no, dammy, nothing like it.", "\"오래된 혈통만 한 게 없죠. 암요, 빌어먹을, 그만한 게 없고말고요."], ["I'm none of your radicals.", "저는 형님 같은 급진주의자가 아니에요."], ["I know what it is to be a gentleman, dammy.", "신사가 뭔지 저도 안다고요, 빌어먹을. 보트 경주에서 사내들을 보세요."], ["See the chaps in a boat-race; look at the fellers in a fight; aye, look at a dawg killing rats--which is it wins?", "격투하는 녀석들을 보시고요. 그래요, 쥐 잡는 개를 보시라고요 — 어느 놈이 이기나요?"], ["the good-blooded ones.", "혈통 좋은 놈들이라고요."], ["Get some more port, Bowls, old boy, whilst I buzz this bottle here.", "보울스, 영감, 포트 좀 더 가져와요, 내가 여기 이 병을 비울 동안에."], ["What was I asaying?\"", "내가 무슨 말 하고 있었지?\""]], [["\"I think you were speaking of dogs killing rats,\"", "\"개가 쥐 잡는 이야기를 하던 것 같은데.\""], ["Pitt remarked mildly, handing his cousin the decanter to \"buzz.\"", "피트가 점잖게 말하며, 사촌에게 \"비우라고\" 술병을 건넸다."]], [["\"Killing rats was I?", "\"쥐 잡는 거요?"], ["Well, Pitt, are you a sporting man?", "그래, 피트 형님, 형님은 운동 좋아해요?"], ["Do you want to see a dawg as CAN kill a rat?", "쥐를 정말 잘 잡는 개를 보고 싶어요?"], ["If you do, come down with me to Tom Corduroy's, in Castle Street Mews, and I'll show you such a bull-terrier as--Pooh!", "그렇다면 저랑 캐슬 가 마구간 골목에 있는 톰 코듀로이네로 같이 가요, 제가 그런 불테리어를 보여 드리죠 — 풉!"], ["gammon,\"", "시시한 소리.\""], ["cried James, bursting out laughing at his own absurdity--\"YOU don't care about a dawg or rat; it's all nonsense.", "제임스가 자기 헛소리에 웃음을 터뜨리며 외쳤다. \"형님은 개니 쥐니 관심도 없잖아요."], ["I'm blest if I think you know the difference between a dog and a duck.\"", "다 헛소리지. 장담컨대 형님은 개하고 오리도 구별 못 할걸요.\""]], [["\"No; by the way,\"", "\"아니지, 그건 그렇고,\""], ["Pitt continued with increased blandness, \"it was about blood you were talking, and the personal advantages which people derive from patrician birth.", "피트가 한층 더 부드러워진 태도로 말을 이었다. \"자네가 이야기하던 건 혈통이었지, 그리고 귀족으로 태어나서 사람들이 누리는 개인적 이점 말이야."], ["Here's the fresh bottle.\"", "자, 새 병 여기 있네.\""]], [["\"Blood's the word,\"", "\"혈통이 핵심이죠.\""], ["said James, gulping the ruby fluid down.", "제임스가 그 루비빛 액체를 꿀꺽 들이켜며 말했다."], ["\"Nothing like blood, sir, in hosses, dawgs, AND men.", "\"혈통만 한 게 없어요, 형님, 말이든 개든, 사람이든요."], ["Why, only last term, just before I was rusticated, that is, I mean just before I had the measles, ha, ha--there was me and Ringwood of Christchurch, Bob Ringwood, Lord Cinqbars' son, having our beer at the Bell at Blenheim, when the Banbury bargeman offered to fight either of us for a bowl of punch.", "아니, 바로 지난 학기에, 제가 정학당하기 직전에, 그러니까 제 말은, 홍역에 걸리기 직전에 말예요, 하, 하 — 저랑 크라이스트처치 칼리지의 링우드, 그러니까 시크바스 경 아들 밥 링우드가 블레넘의 벨에서 맥주를 마시고 있는데, 밴버리 뱃사공 녀석이 펀치 한 사발 내기로 우리 둘 중 누구든 한판 붙자고 하지 뭐예요."], ["I couldn't.", "저는 못 했어요."], ["My arm was in a sling; couldn't even take the drag down--a brute of a mare of mine had fell with me only two days before, out with the Abingdon, and I thought my arm was broke.", "팔을 삼각건에 매고 있었거든요. 마차 브레이크조차 내릴 수가 없었어요 — 그 이틀 전에 애빙던 사냥에 나갔다가 제 암말, 그 망할 놈의 짐승이 저를 넘어뜨려서, 팔이 부러진 줄 알았다니까요."], ["Well, sir, I couldn't finish him, but Bob had his coat off at once--he stood up to the Banbury man for three minutes, and polished him off in four rounds easy.", "그래서, 제가 그놈을 못 끝장냈는데, 밥은 단번에 웃옷을 벗어 던지고는 — 그 밴버리 녀석을 상대로 삼 분을 버티다가 네 라운드 만에 가뿐히 해치워 버렸어요."], ["Gad, how he did drop, sir, and what was it?", "세상에, 그놈이 어떻게 나가떨어지던지, 형님, 근데 그게 뭐 때문이었겠어요?"], ["Blood, sir, all blood.\"", "혈통이에요, 형님, 다 혈통 덕이라고요.\""]], [["\"You don't drink, James,\"", "\"자네 안 마시는군, 제임스.\""], ["the ex-attache continued.", "전직 대사관원이 말을 이었다."], ["\"In my time at Oxford, the men passed round the bottle a little quicker than you young fellows seem to do.\"", "\"내가 옥스퍼드 다닐 때는 다들 자네 젊은 친구들보다 술병을 좀 더 빨리 돌렸는데 말이야.\""]], [["\"Come, come,\"", "\"이러지, 이러지 마세요.\""], ["said James, putting his hand to his nose and winking at his cousin with a pair of vinous eyes, \"no jokes, old boy; no trying it on on me.", "제임스가 코에 손을 갖다 대고 술기운 어린 두 눈으로 사촌에게 윙크하며 말했다. \"농담 마세요, 형님, 저한테 수작 부리지 마시라고요."], ["You want to trot me out, but it's no go.", "절 떠보려는 거지만, 안 통해요."], ["In vino veritas, old boy.", "인 비노 베리타스, 형님."], ["Mars, Bacchus, Apollo virorum, hey?", "마르스, 바쿠스, 아폴로 비로룸, 안 그래요?"], ["I wish my aunt would send down some of this to the governor; it's a precious good tap.\"", "우리 고모님이 이걸 좀 아버지한테 보내 주셨으면 좋겠네, 끝내주는 술인데 말이야.\""]], [["\"You had better ask her,\"", "\"그럼 고모님께 청해 보지 그러나.\""], ["Machiavel continued, \"or make the best of your time now.", "마키아벨리 같은 피트가 말을 이었다. \"아니면 지금 이 시간을 최대한 즐기든가."], ["What says the bard?", "시인이 뭐라 했더라?"], ["'Nunc vino pellite curas, Cras ingens iterabimus aequor,'\"", "'눈크 비노 펠리테 쿠라스, 크라스 인겐스 이테라비무스 아이쿠오르.'\""], ["and the Bacchanalian, quoting the above with a House of Commons air, tossed off nearly a thimbleful of wine with an immense flourish of his glass.", "그러자 이 술꾼은 하원 의원 같은 태도로 위 구절을 읊으며, 잔을 어마어마하게 휘저어 골무만큼의 포도주를 털어 넣었다."]], [["At the Rectory, when the bottle of port wine was opened after dinner, the young ladies had each a glass from a bottle of currant wine. Mrs.", "목사관에서는 저녁 식사 후 포트와인 한 병을 따면, 처녀들은 각자 까치밥나무 열매주 한 병에서 한 잔씩 받았다."], ["Bute took one glass of port, honest James had a couple commonly, but as his father grew very sulky if he made further inroads on the bottle, the good lad generally refrained from trying for more, and subsided either into the currant wine, or to some private gin-and-water in the stables, which he enjoyed in the company of the coachman and his pipe.", "뷰트 부인은 포트와인 한 잔을 들었고, 정직한 제임스는 보통 두어 잔을 마셨지만, 그가 그 병에 더 손을 대면 아버지가 몹시 심통을 부리는 통에, 이 착한 청년은 대개 더 마시려는 시도를 삼가고 까치밥나무 열매주로 옮겨 가거나, 아니면 마구간에서 마부와 자기 파이프를 벗 삼아 몰래 마시는 진토닉으로 만족하곤 했다. 옥스퍼드에서는 포도주의 양이 무제한이었으나 질은 떨어졌다."], ["At Oxford, the quantity of wine was unlimited, but the quality was inferior: but when quantity and quality united as at his aunt's house, James showed that he could appreciate them indeed; and hardly needed any of his cousin's encouragement in draining off the second bottle supplied by Mr. Bowls.", "그런데 고모 집에서처럼 양과 질이 하나로 합쳐지자, 제임스는 자신이 그것을 정말로 음미할 줄 안다는 것을 보여 주었으니, 보울스 씨가 내온 두 번째 병을 비우는 데 사촌의 부추김 따위는 거의 필요치 않았다."]], [["When the time for coffee came, however, and for a return to the ladies, of whom he stood in awe, the young gentleman's agreeable frankness left him, and he relapsed into his usual surly timidity; contenting himself by saying yes and no, by scowling at Lady Jane, and by upsetting one cup of coffee during the evening.", "그러나 커피 시간이 되고, 그가 어려워하는 숙녀들에게로 돌아갈 때가 되자, 이 젊은 신사의 그 유쾌한 솔직함은 사라져 버리고, 그는 평소의 무뚝뚝한 소심함으로 되돌아가 그저 예니 아니오니 하는 것으로, 제인 부인을 노려보는 것으로, 그리고 저녁나절 커피 한 잔을 엎지르는 것으로 만족했다."]], [["If he did not speak he yawned in a pitiable manner, and his presence threw a damp upon the modest proceedings of the evening, for Miss Crawley and Lady Jane at their piquet, and Miss Briggs at her work, felt that his eyes were wildly fixed on them, and were uneasy under that maudlin look.", "말을 하지 않을 때면 그는 가엾을 만큼 하품을 해 댔고, 그의 존재는 저녁의 조촐한 일과에 찬물을 끼얹었다. 피케를 치는 크롤리 양과 제인 부인도, 뜨개질을 하는 브리그스 양도, 그의 두 눈이 자기들에게 게슴츠레 고정되어 있는 것을 느끼고는 그 게슴츠레한 시선 아래 불편해했던 것이다."]], [["\"He seems a very silent, awkward, bashful lad,\"", "\"저 애는 무척 과묵하고 어색하고 수줍음 많은 청년인 것 같구나.\""], ["said Miss Crawley to Mr. Pitt.", "크롤리 양이 피트 씨에게 말했다."]], [["\"He is more communicative in men's society than with ladies,\"", "\"숙녀들과 있을 때보다 남자들 사이에서 더 말이 많은 편이지요.\""], ["Machiavel dryly replied: perhaps rather disappointed that the port wine had not made Jim speak more.", "마키아벨리 같은 피트가 무뚝뚝하게 대꾸했다. 어쩌면 포트와인이 짐의 입을 더 열어 주지 못한 것에 다소 실망한 것이었으리라."]], [["He had spent the early part of the next morning in writing home to his mother a most flourishing account of his reception by Miss Crawley.", "그는 이튿날 아침 일찍 어머니에게 크롤리 양의 환대를 더없이 화려하게 부풀린 편지를 쓰며 보냈다. 그러나 아아!"], ["But ah! he little knew what evils the day was bringing for him, and how short his reign of favour was destined to be.", "그는 그날이 자신에게 어떤 재앙을 가져올지, 또 그의 총애받는 치세가 얼마나 짧게 끝나도록 정해져 있는지 까맣게 몰랐다."], ["A circumstance which Jim had forgotten--a trivial but fatal circumstance--had taken place at the Cribb's Arms on the night before he had come to his aunt's house.", "짐이 까맣게 잊고 있던 한 가지 사정 — 사소하지만 치명적인 한 가지 사정이 — 그가 고모 집에 오기 전날 밤 크립스 암스에서 벌어졌던 것이다."], ["It was no other than this--Jim, who was always of a generous disposition, and when in his cups especially hospitable, had in the course of the night treated the Tutbury champion and the Rottingdean man, and their friends, twice or thrice to the refreshment of gin-and-water--so that no less than eighteen glasses of that fluid at eightpence per glass were charged in Mr. James Crawley's bill.", "다름이 아니라 — 늘 후한 성품인 데다 특히 술에 취하면 더욱 손님 대접이 융숭한 짐이, 그날 밤 내내 터트베리 챔피언과 로팅딘 사내와 그들의 친구들에게 진토닉을 두세 차례나 대접한 것이었다 — 그리하여 한 잔에 팔 펜스 하는 그 술이 무려 열여덟 잔이나 제임스 크롤리 씨의 계산서에 올랐다."], ["It was not the amount of eightpences, but the quantity of gin which told fatally against poor James's character, when his aunt's butler, Mr. Bowls, went down at his mistress's request to pay the young gentleman's bill.", "고모의 집사 보울스 씨가 안주인의 부탁으로 이 젊은 신사의 청구서를 치르러 내려갔을 때, 가엾은 제임스의 평판에 치명타를 가한 것은 그 팔 펜스의 합계가 아니라 진의 양이었다."], ["The landlord, fearing lest the account should be refused altogether, swore solemnly that the young gent had consumed personally every farthing's worth of the liquor: and Bowls paid the bill finally, and showed it on his return home to Mrs.", "여관 주인은 행여 그 계산이 통째로 거부될까 두려워, 그 젊은 신사가 그 술을 한 푼어치도 빠짐없이 혼자 다 마셨노라고 엄숙히 맹세했다. 결국 보울스는 청구서를 치렀고, 집에 돌아와 그것을 퍼킨 부인에게 보여 주었으니, 그녀는 그 무시무시한 진 낭비에 경악했다."], ["Firkin, who was shocked at the frightful prodigality of gin; and took the bill to Miss Briggs as accountant-general; who thought it her duty to mention the circumstance to her principal, Miss Crawley.", "그리고 그 청구서는 회계 총책임자 격인 브리그스 양에게 전해졌고, 그녀는 그 사정을 자신의 주인인 크롤리 양에게 고하는 것이 자신의 의무라고 여겼다."]], [["Had he drunk a dozen bottles of claret, the old spinster could have pardoned him. Mr.", "만약 그가 클라레 열두 병을 마셨더라면 늙은 노처녀는 그를 용서할 수 있었으리라."], ["Fox and Mr. Sheridan drank claret.", "폭스 씨도 셰리든 씨도 클라레를 마셨다."], ["Gentlemen drank claret.", "신사들은 클라레를 마셨다."], ["But eighteen glasses of gin consumed among boxers in an ignoble pot-house--it was an odious crime and not to be pardoned readily.", "그러나 비천한 술집에서 권투 선수들 틈에 끼어 진 열여덟 잔을 비웠다니 — 그것은 가증스러운 죄였고 선뜻 용서할 수 있는 일이 아니었다. 모든 것이 그 청년에게 불리하게 돌아갔다."], ["Everything went against the lad: he came home perfumed from the stables, whither he had been to pay his dog Towzer a visit--and whence he was going to take his friend out for an airing, when he met Miss Crawley and her wheezy Blenheim spaniel, which Towzer would have eaten up had not the Blenheim fled squealing to the protection of Miss Briggs, while the atrocious master of the bull-dog stood laughing at the horrible persecution.", "그는 마구간에서 향수 냄새를 풍기며 돌아왔으니, 거기서 자기 개 타우저를 보러 갔다가, 그 친구를 데리고 바람을 쐬러 나오려던 참에 크롤리 양과 그녀의 쌕쌕거리는 블레넘 스패니얼과 마주쳤던 것이다. 그 블레넘이 깩깩거리며 브리그스 양의 보호 아래로 달아나지 않았더라면 타우저가 그놈을 잡아먹었을 터인데, 그 흉포한 불도그의 주인은 그 끔찍한 박해를 보며 웃고 서 있었다."]], [["This day too the unlucky boy's modesty had likewise forsaken him.", "이날따라 그 불운한 청년에게서는 수줍음마저 떠나 버렸다."], ["He was lively and facetious at dinner.", "그는 저녁 식사 자리에서 활기차고 익살을 부렸다."], ["During the repast he levelled one or two jokes against Pitt Crawley: he drank as much wine as upon the previous day; and going quite unsuspiciously to the drawing-room, began to entertain the ladies there with some choice Oxford stories.", "식사 중에 그는 피트 크롤리를 겨냥해 한두 마디 농을 던졌고, 전날만큼이나 포도주를 들이켰으며, 아무런 의심 없이 응접실로 가서는 옥스퍼드의 한가락 하는 이야기들로 숙녀들을 즐겁게 해 주기 시작했다."], ["He described the different pugilistic qualities of Molyneux and Dutch Sam, offered playfully to give Lady Jane the odds upon the Tutbury Pet against the Rottingdean man, or take them, as her Ladyship chose: and crowned the pleasantry by proposing to back himself against his cousin Pitt Crawley, either with or without the gloves.", "그는 몰리뉴와 더치 샘의 권투 기량의 차이를 묘사했고, 제인 부인에게 로팅딘 사내를 상대로 터트베리 펫에게 배당을 걸어 주겠다고, 혹은 부인이 원한다면 그 반대로 걸겠다고 장난스레 제안했다. 그러고는 사촌 피트 크롤리를 상대로, 글러브를 끼든 안 끼든 자기 자신에게 돈을 걸겠노라 제안하는 것으로 그 익살의 절정을 장식했다."], ["\"And that's a fair offer, my buck,\"", "\"그건 공정한 제안이라고요, 형님.\""], ["he said, with a loud laugh, slapping Pitt on the shoulder, \"and my father told me to make it too, and he'll go halves in the bet, ha, ha!\"", "그가 큰 소리로 웃으며 피트의 어깨를 탁 치며 말했다. \"그리고 우리 아버지도 이렇게 제안하라고 하셨다고요, 아버지도 그 내기에 절반 거실 거예요, 하, 하!\""], ["So saying, the engaging youth nodded knowingly at poor Miss Briggs, and pointed his thumb over his shoulder at Pitt Crawley in a jocular and exulting manner.", "그렇게 말하면서, 이 사랑스러운 청년은 가엾은 브리그스 양에게 아는 체하며 고갯짓을 하고는, 어깨너머로 엄지손가락을 들어 피트 크롤리를 장난스럽고도 의기양양하게 가리켰다."]], [["Pitt was not pleased altogether perhaps, but still not unhappy in the main.", "피트는 어쩌면 그리 유쾌하지는 않았겠으나, 그래도 대체로 기분이 나쁘지는 않았다. 가엾은 짐은 실컷 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["Poor Jim had his laugh out: and staggered across the room with his aunt's candle, when the old lady moved to retire, and offered to salute her with the blandest tipsy smile: and he took his own leave and went upstairs to his bedroom perfectly satisfied with himself, and with a pleased notion that his aunt's money would be left to him in preference to his father and all the rest of the family.", "그리고 노부인이 자리를 뜨려 하자 그녀의 촛불을 들고 비틀비틀 방을 가로질렀고, 더없이 부드러운 취중 미소로 그녀에게 입을 맞추려 했다. 그러고는 스스로 작별을 고하고 위층 자기 침실로 올라갔으니, 자기 자신에게 더없이 만족했고, 고모의 재산이 아버지와 나머지 온 가족을 제치고 자신에게 남겨지리라는 흐뭇한 생각에 젖어 있었다."]], [["Once up in the bedroom, one would have thought he could not make matters worse; and yet this unlucky boy did.", "일단 침실에 올라가고 나면 더는 일을 그르칠 수 없으리라 여길 법도 하건만, 이 불운한 청년은 기어이 그르치고 말았다."], ["The moon was shining very pleasantly out on the sea, and Jim, attracted to the window by the romantic appearance of the ocean and the heavens, thought he would further enjoy them while smoking.", "달이 바다 위로 무척 기분 좋게 빛나고 있었고, 짐은 바다와 하늘의 낭만적인 정경에 이끌려 창가로 가서는, 담배를 피우며 그것을 더 만끽하리라 생각했다."], ["Nobody would smell the tobacco, he thought, if he cunningly opened the window and kept his head and pipe in the fresh air.", "창문을 슬쩍 열고 머리와 파이프를 바깥 신선한 공기 속에 내밀고 있으면 아무도 담배 냄새를 맡지 못하리라고 그는 생각했다."], ["This he did: but being in an excited state, poor Jim had forgotten that his door was open all this time, so that the breeze blowing inwards and a fine thorough draught being established, the clouds of tobacco were carried downstairs, and arrived with quite undiminished fragrance to Miss Crawley and Miss Briggs.", "그는 그렇게 했다. 그러나 흥분한 상태였던 가엾은 짐은 그동안 내내 방문이 열려 있다는 것을 깜박 잊고 있었으니, 그리하여 바람이 안으로 불어 들어 멋들어진 통풍이 이루어지면서, 담배 연기 구름이 아래층으로 실려 내려가 그 향기를 조금도 잃지 않은 채 크롤리 양과 브리그스 양에게 당도하고 만 것이다."]], [["The pipe of tobacco finished the business: and the Bute-Crawleys never knew how many thousand pounds it cost them.", "담배 한 파이프가 모든 일을 결딴내고 말았으니, 뷰트 크롤리 일가는 그것이 자기들에게 수천 파운드의 대가를 치르게 했다는 것을 끝내 알지 못했다."], ["Firkin rushed downstairs to Bowls who was reading out the \"Fire and the Frying Pan\" to his aide-de-camp in a loud and ghostly voice.", "퍼킨이 아래층 보울스에게 달려 내려갔는데, 그는 부관 격인 젊은이에게 크고 으스스한 목소리로 「불과 프라이팬」을 낭독해 주고 있던 참이었다."], ["The dreadful secret was told to him by Firkin with so frightened a look, that for the first moment Mr. Bowls and his young man thought that robbers were in the house, the legs of whom had probably been discovered by the woman under Miss Crawley's bed.", "퍼킨이 어찌나 겁에 질린 얼굴로 그 끔찍한 비밀을 그에게 일러 주던지, 처음 한순간 보울스 씨와 그 젊은이는 집 안에 강도가 들었다고, 아마 크롤리 양의 침대 밑에서 그 여자가 강도의 다리를 발견한 것이라고 여겼다."], ["When made aware of the fact, however--to rush upstairs at three steps at a time to enter the unconscious James's apartment, calling out, \"Mr. James,\"", "그러나 사실을 알게 되자 — 한 번에 세 계단씩 위층으로 달려 올라가, 아무것도 모르는 제임스의 방으로 들어가 놀라움에 숨이 막힌 목소리로 \"제임스 씨\"를 부르고는 \"제발이지, 나리, 그 파이프 좀 그만 피우십시오\""], ["in a voice stifled with alarm, and to cry, \"For Gawd's sake, sir, stop that 'ere pipe,\"", "하고 외치는 것이, 보울스 씨에게는 일 분 만에 해치운 일이었다."], ["was the work of a minute with Mr. Bowls.", "\"오, 제임스 씨, 대체 무슨 짓을 하신 겁니까!\""], ["\"O, Mr. James, what 'AVE you done!\"", "그가 그 물건을 창밖으로 내던지며 더없이 비통한 목소리로 말했다."], ["he said in a voice of the deepest pathos, as he threw the implement out of the window.", "\"무슨 짓을 하신 거예요, 나리!"], ["\"What 'ave you done, sir! Missis can't abide 'em.\"", "마님은 그걸 도무지 못 견디신다고요.\""]], [["\"Missis needn't smoke,\"", "\"마님은 안 피우면 되잖아요.\""], ["said James with a frantic misplaced laugh, and thought the whole matter an excellent joke. But his feelings were very different in the morning, when Mr.", "제임스가 미친 듯한, 번지수 틀린 웃음을 터뜨리며 말했으니, 그는 이 모든 일을 그저 멋들어진 농담쯤으로 여겼다."], ["Bowls's young man, who operated upon Mr.", "그러나 이튿날 아침 그의 심정은 사뭇 달라졌다."], ["James's boots, and brought him his hot water to shave that beard which he was so anxiously expecting, handed a note in to Mr. James in bed, in the handwriting of Miss Briggs.", "보울스 씨의 젊은이가 제임스 씨의 부츠를 손질하고, 그가 그토록 애타게 기다리던 그 수염을 깎으라고 더운물을 가져다주면서, 침대에 누운 제임스 씨에게 브리그스 양의 필체로 쓰인 쪽지 한 장을 건넸던 것이다."]], [["\"Dear sir,\" it said, \"Miss Crawley has passed an exceedingly disturbed night, owing to the shocking manner in which the house has been polluted by tobacco; Miss Crawley bids me say she regrets that she is too unwell to see you before you go--and above all that she ever induced you to remove from the ale-house, where she is sure you will be much more comfortable during the rest of your stay at Brighton.\"", "\"친애하는 선생께.\" 쪽지에는 이렇게 적혀 있었다. \"크롤리 양께서는 집 안이 담배로 더럽혀진 그 충격적인 처사로 인해 지극히 어지러운 밤을 보내셨습니다. 크롤리 양께서는 떠나시기 전에 뵙기에는 몸이 너무 편찮으신 것이 유감이라고, 또 무엇보다 선생을 그 술집에서 옮겨 오시게 한 것 자체를 유감으로 여기신다고 전하라 하셨습니다. 브라이턴에 머무시는 남은 기간 동안 그곳이 분명 훨씬 더 편안하시리라는 것이지요.\""]], [["And herewith honest James's career as a candidate for his aunt's favour ended.", "이로써 고모의 총애를 얻고자 한 정직한 제임스의 입후보 경력은 막을 내렸다."], ["He had in fact, and without knowing it, done what he menaced to do.", "그는 사실, 자기도 모르게, 자신이 으름장 놓았던 바로 그 일을 해낸 셈이었다."], ["He had fought his cousin Pitt with the gloves.", "그는 사촌 피트와 글러브를 끼고 한판 붙은 것이었다."]], [["Where meanwhile was he who had been once first favourite for this race for money?", "그렇다면 그동안, 한때 이 돈을 노린 경주에서 가장 유력한 후보였던 그 사내는 어디에 있었던가?"], ["Becky and Rawdon, as we have seen, were come together after Waterloo, and were passing the winter of 1815 at Paris in great splendour and gaiety.", "베키와 로던은, 우리가 이미 보았듯, 워털루 이후 다시 합쳐져 1815년 겨울을 파리에서 대단한 호화로움과 흥겨움 속에 보내고 있었다."], ["Rebecca was a good economist, and the price poor Jos Sedley had paid for her two horses was in itself sufficient to keep their little establishment afloat for a year, at the least; there was no occasion to turn into money \"my pistols, the same which I shot Captain Marker,\"", "레베카는 살림을 잘 꾸리는 솜씨가 있었으니, 가엾은 조스 세들리가 그녀에게서 말 두 마리를 사느라 치른 값만으로도 그들의 자그마한 살림을 적어도 일 년은 너끈히 꾸려 갈 만했다. \"마커 대위를 쏘았던 바로 그 권총\"이라든가, 금 화장도구함이라든가, 검은담비 모피를 댄 망토 따위를 돈으로 바꿀 필요는 없었다."], ["or the gold dressing-case, or the cloak lined with sable.", "베키는 그 망토를 자기 외투로 고쳐 만들어, 그것을 입고 불로뉴 숲을 거닐며 모든 이의 찬탄을 받았다."], ["Becky had it made into a pelisse for herself, in which she rode in the Bois de Boulogne to the admiration of all: and you should have seen the scene between her and her delighted husband, whom she rejoined after the army had entered Cambray, and when she unsewed herself, and let out of her dress all those watches, knick-knacks, bank-notes, cheques, and valuables, which she had secreted in the wadding, previous to her meditated flight from Brussels!", "또 군대가 캉브레에 입성한 뒤 그녀가 남편과 다시 만났을 때, 그녀가 옷의 솔기를 뜯어 브뤼셀에서 도주를 궁리하기에 앞서 속을 채운 솜 속에 감춰 두었던 그 온갖 시계며 자질구레한 장신구며 지폐며 수표며 귀중품을 죄다 꺼내 보였을 때, 그녀와 기뻐 어쩔 줄 모르는 남편 사이에 벌어진 그 광경을 여러분이 보았어야 하는데!"], ["Tufto was charmed, and Rawdon roared with delighted laughter, and swore that she was better than any play he ever saw, by Jove.", "터프토는 매료되었고, 로던은 기뻐서 너털웃음을 터뜨리며, 맹세코 그녀가 자기가 본 어떤 연극보다도 낫다고 단언했다."], ["And the way in which she jockeyed Jos, and which she described with infinite fun, carried up his delight to a pitch of quite insane enthusiasm.", "그리고 그녀가 조스를 어떻게 속여 넘겼는지, 그것을 한없이 재미나게 묘사하자 그의 기쁨은 그야말로 미친 듯한 열광의 경지에까지 치솟았다."], ["He believed in his wife as much as the French soldiers in Napoleon.", "그는 프랑스 병사들이 나폴레옹을 믿었던 만큼이나 자기 아내를 믿었다."]], [["Her success in Paris was remarkable.", "파리에서 그녀의 성공은 눈부셨다."], ["All the French ladies voted her charming.", "프랑스 부인들은 모두 그녀를 매력적이라고 평했다."], ["She spoke their language admirably.", "그녀는 그들의 언어를 훌륭하게 구사했다."], ["She adopted at once their grace, their liveliness, their manner.", "그녀는 그들의 우아함이며 활기며 몸가짐을 단번에 제 것으로 만들었다."], ["Her husband was stupid certainly--all English are stupid--and, besides, a dull husband at Paris is always a point in a lady's favour.", "그녀의 남편은 분명 멍청했고 — 영국인은 다 멍청하니까 — 게다가 파리에서 따분한 남편이란 늘 부인에게 유리한 점이 되어 주었다."], ["He was the heir of the rich and spirituelle Miss Crawley, whose house had been open to so many of the French noblesse during the emigration.", "그는 부유하고 재기 넘치는 크롤리 양의 상속자였는데, 그 부인의 집은 망명 시절 프랑스 귀족들에게 두루 열려 있었던 터였다."], ["They received the colonel's wife in their own hotels--\"Why,\"", "그들은 대령의 아내를 자기들 저택으로 맞아들였다."], ["wrote a great lady to Miss Crawley, who had bought her lace and trinkets at the Duchess's own price, and given her many a dinner during the pinching times after the Revolution--\"Why does not our dear Miss come to her nephew and niece, and her attached friends in Paris?", "어느 지체 높은 부인이 크롤리 양에게 편지를 썼는데 — 그녀는 그 공작부인이 부르는 값으로 레이스와 장신구를 사 주고, 혁명 이후의 궁핍한 시절에 여러 차례 만찬에 초대해 주었던 사람이었다 — \"우리의 다정한 미스께서는 어찌하여 파리에 있는 조카 내외분과, 또 그토록 정다운 벗들에게 와 주시지 않는 건가요?"], ["All the world raffoles of the charming Mistress and her espiegle beauty.", "온 세상이 그 매력적인 부인과 그 장난스러운 미모에 홀딱 빠져 있답니다."], ["Yes, we see in her the grace, the charm, the wit of our dear friend Miss Crawley!", "그래요, 우리는 그분에게서 우리의 다정한 벗 크롤리 양의 우아함과 매력과 재치를 본답니다!"], ["The King took notice of her yesterday at the Tuileries, and we are all jealous of the attention which Monsieur pays her.", "어제 튀일리궁에서 국왕께서 그분을 눈여겨보셨고, 무슈께서 그분에게 베푸시는 관심에 우리 모두 질투가 날 지경이에요."], ["If you could have seen the spite of a certain stupid Miladi Bareacres (whose eagle-beak and toque and feathers may be seen peering over the heads of all assemblies) when Madame, the Duchess of Angouleme, the august daughter and companion of kings, desired especially to be presented to Mrs. Crawley, as your dear daughter and protegee, and thanked her in the name of France, for all your benevolence towards our unfortunates during their exile!", "어떤 멍청한 베어에이커스 부인이 — 그 매부리코며 토크 모자며 깃털 장식이 어느 모임에서나 사람들 머리 위로 삐죽 솟아오르는 그 부인이 — 얼마나 심술이 났던지 여러분이 보셨어야 하는데, 왕가의 고귀한 따님이자 동반자이신 앙굴렘 공작부인께서 특별히 크롤리 부인을, 당신의 그 사랑하는 딸이자 피후견인을 소개받고 싶다 청하셔서, 망명 시절 우리 불우한 이들에게 베풀어 주신 당신의 온갖 자비에 대해 프랑스의 이름으로 그분께 감사를 표하셨을 때 말이에요!"], ["She is of all the societies, of all the balls--of the balls--yes--of the dances, no; and yet how interesting and pretty this fair creature looks surrounded by the homage of the men, and so soon to be a mother!", "그분은 모든 모임에, 모든 무도회에 — 무도회에는요 — 그래요 — 춤을 추느냐 하면, 그건 아니에요. 그런데도 사내들의 흠모에 둘러싸여, 또 머지않아 어머니가 될 이 어여쁜 여인이 어찌나 흥미롭고 예뻐 보이는지요!"], ["To hear her speak of you, her protectress, her mother, would bring tears to the eyes of ogres.", "그분이 당신을, 자신의 보호자요 어머니인 당신을 이야기하는 것을 들으면 식인귀의 눈에도 눈물이 맺힐 거예요."], ["How she loves you!", "그분이 당신을 얼마나 사랑하는지요!"], ["how we all love our admirable, our respectable Miss Crawley!\"", "우리 모두 우리의 훌륭하고 존경스러운 크롤리 양을 얼마나 사랑하는지요!\""]], [["It is to be feared that this letter of the Parisian great lady did not by any means advance Mrs. Becky's interest with her admirable, her respectable, relative.", "이 파리 귀부인의 편지가 베키 부인의 이익을 그 훌륭하고 존경스러운 친척에게 조금도 보탬이 되게 하지 못했으리라는 것은 짐작하고도 남는다."], ["On the contrary, the fury of the old spinster was beyond bounds, when she found what was Rebecca's situation, and how audaciously she had made use of Miss Crawley's name, to get an entree into Parisian society. Too much shaken in mind and body to compose a letter in the French language in reply to that of her correspondent, she dictated to Briggs a furious answer in her own native tongue, repudiating Mrs.", "도리어 늙은 노처녀의 분노는, 레베카의 처지가 어떠한지, 또 파리 사교계에 발을 들이려고 그녀가 얼마나 뻔뻔스럽게 크롤리 양의 이름을 이용했는지 알게 되자 그야말로 끝 간 데를 몰랐다."], ["Rawdon Crawley altogether, and warning the public to beware of her as a most artful and dangerous person. But as Madame the Duchess of X--had only been twenty years in England, she did not understand a single word of the language, and contented herself by informing Mrs.", "너무도 마음이 흔들리고 몸이 쇠약해진 탓에 그 서신의 답장을 프랑스어로 지을 수가 없어, 그녀는 브리그스에게 자기 모국어로 격노에 찬 답장을 받아쓰게 했으니, 로던 크롤리 부인을 통째로 부인하고, 세상 사람들에게 그녀가 더없이 교활하고 위험한 인물이니 조심하라고 경고하는 내용이었다."], ["Rawdon Crawley at their next meeting, that she had received a charming letter from that chere Mees, and that it was full of benevolent things for Mrs. Crawley, who began seriously to have hopes that the spinster would relent.", "그러나 X 공작부인은 영국에 와서 산 지 겨우 이십 년밖에 되지 않은 터라 그 언어를 한마디도 알아듣지 못했고, 다음번에 만났을 때 로던 크롤리 부인에게, 그 사랑하는 미스에게서 매력적인 편지를 받았는데 크롤리 부인을 위한 자애로운 말들로 가득하더라고 일러 주는 것으로 만족했으니, 그리하여 크롤리 부인은 그 노처녀가 마음을 누그러뜨릴지도 모른다고 진지하게 기대하기 시작했다."]], [["Meanwhile, she was the gayest and most admired of Englishwomen: and had a little European congress on her reception-night.", "그러는 동안 그녀는 영국 여인들 가운데 가장 흥겹고 가장 흠모받는 여인이었고, 손님 맞는 날 밤이면 자신의 집에서 자그마한 유럽 회의를 열었다."], ["Prussians and Cossacks, Spanish and English--all the world was at Paris during this famous winter: to have seen the stars and cordons in Rebecca's humble saloon would have made all Baker Street pale with envy.", "프로이센인이며 코사크인이며 스페인인이며 영국인이며 — 이 유명한 겨울 동안 온 세상이 파리에 와 있었던 것이다. 레베카의 소박한 응접실에 모인 그 훈장들과 약장들을 보았더라면 베이커 거리 전체가 시샘으로 새파래졌으리라."], ["Famous warriors rode by her carriage in the Bois, or crowded her modest little box at the Opera.", "이름난 무사들이 불로뉴 숲에서 그녀의 마차 곁을 말 달렸고, 오페라극장의 그녀의 자그마한 박스석에 빼곡히 모여들었다."], ["Rawdon was in the highest spirits.", "로던은 더없이 기분이 좋았다."], ["There were no duns in Paris as yet: there were parties every day at Very's or Beauvilliers'; play was plentiful and his luck good.", "파리에는 아직 빚쟁이가 없었고, 베리네나 보빌리에네에서 날마다 파티가 열렸으며, 도박은 흔했고 그의 운도 좋았다."], ["Tufto perhaps was sulky. Mrs.", "터프토는 어쩌면 심통이 났을 것이다."], ["Tufto had come over to Paris at her own invitation, and besides this contretemps, there were a score of generals now round Becky's chair, and she might take her choice of a dozen bouquets when she went to the play.", "터프토 부인이 자청해서 파리로 건너온 데다, 이 낭패에 더하여 이제는 수십 명의 장군들이 베키의 의자를 에워쌌고, 그녀가 연극을 보러 갈 때면 열 몇 개의 꽃다발 가운데서 고를 수 있게 되었던 것이다."], ["Lady Bareacres and the chiefs of the English society, stupid and irreproachable females, writhed with anguish at the success of the little upstart Becky, whose poisoned jokes quivered and rankled in their chaste breasts.", "베어에이커스 부인과 영국 사교계의 우두머리들, 그 멍청하고 나무랄 데 없는 여인들은 저 자그마한 벼락출세자 베키의 성공에 고통으로 몸부림쳤으니, 베키의 독 묻은 농담이 그들의 정숙한 가슴속에 박혀 부르르 떨며 욱신거렸다."], ["But she had all the men on her side.", "그러나 사내들은 죄다 그녀 편이었다."], ["She fought the women with indomitable courage, and they could not talk scandal in any tongue but their own.", "그녀는 불굴의 용기로 여인들과 맞서 싸웠고, 여인들은 자기네 언어가 아니고서는 험담조차 할 수가 없었다."]], [["So in fetes, pleasures, and prosperity, the winter of 1815-16 passed away with Mrs. Rawdon Crawley, who accommodated herself to polite life as if her ancestors had been people of fashion for centuries past--and who from her wit, talent, and energy, indeed merited a place of honour in Vanity Fair.", "그리하여 갖가지 축제와 향락과 번영 속에서 1815년에서 16년에 걸친 겨울이 로던 크롤리 부인과 함께 지나갔으니, 그녀는 마치 자기 조상들이 수백 년 전부터 멋쟁이였기라도 한 듯 상류 생활에 척척 적응했고, 그 재치와 재능과 활력으로 보아 참으로 허영의 시장에서 명예로운 자리를 차지할 만했다."], ["In the early spring of 1816, Galignani's Journal contained the following announcement in an interesting corner of the paper: \"On the 26th of March--the Lady of Lieutenant-Colonel Crawley, of the Life Guards Green--of a son and heir.\"", "1816년 이른 봄, 갈리냐니 신문의 한 흥미로운 구석에 다음과 같은 소식이 실렸다. \"3월 26일 — 근위 기병대 그린 소속 크롤리 중령의 부인, 대를 이을 아들을 출산.\""]], [["This event was copied into the London papers, out of which Miss Briggs read the statement to Miss Crawley, at breakfast, at Brighton.", "이 소식은 런던 신문들에도 옮겨 실렸고, 브라이턴에서 아침 식사 자리에 브리그스 양이 그 기사를 크롤리 양에게 읽어 주었다."], ["The intelligence, expected as it might have been, caused a crisis in the affairs of the Crawley family.", "어느 정도 예상되었던 일이긴 했으나, 이 소식은 크롤리 가문의 일에 한바탕 위기를 불러왔다."], ["The spinster's rage rose to its height, and sending instantly for Pitt, her nephew, and for the Lady Southdown, from Brunswick Square, she requested an immediate celebration of the marriage which had been so long pending between the two families.", "노처녀의 분노는 절정에 달했으니, 그녀는 당장 조카 피트와, 브런즈윅 광장에 있는 사우스다운 부인을 불러, 두 집안 사이에 그토록 오래 미루어져 온 혼인을 즉각 거행해 달라고 청했다."], ["And she announced that it was her intention to allow the young couple a thousand a year during her lifetime, at the expiration of which the bulk of her property would be settled upon her nephew and her dear niece, Lady Jane Crawley.", "그러고는 자신이 살아 있는 동안 그 젊은 부부에게 해마다 천 파운드를 주고, 자신이 세상을 떠나면 재산의 대부분을 조카와, 사랑하는 조카며느리 제인 크롤리 부인에게 물려줄 작정이라고 선언했다."], ["Waxy came down to ratify the deeds--Lord Southdown gave away his sister--she was married by a Bishop, and not by the Rev. Bartholomew Irons--to the disappointment of the irregular prelate.", "왁시가 그 증서들을 확정하러 내려왔고 — 사우스다운 경이 누이를 신랑에게 넘겨주었으며 — 그녀는 바살러뮤 아이언스 목사가 아니라 주교의 주례로 결혼했으니 — 그 변칙적인 고위 성직자에게는 실망스러운 일이었다."]], [["When they were married, Pitt would have liked to take a hymeneal tour with his bride, as became people of their condition.", "결혼하고 나자 피트는 자기들 처지에 걸맞게 신부와 함께 신혼여행을 떠나고 싶었다."], ["But the affection of the old lady towards Lady Jane had grown so strong, that she fairly owned she could not part with her favourite.", "그러나 제인 부인을 향한 노부인의 애정이 어찌나 깊어졌던지, 그녀는 자기가 그 귀염둥이와 떨어질 수 없노라고 솔직히 털어놓았다. 그리하여 피트와 그 아내는 들어와 크롤리 양과 함께 살게 되었다."], ["Pitt and his wife came therefore and lived with Miss Crawley: and (greatly to the annoyance of poor Pitt, who conceived himself a most injured character--being subject to the humours of his aunt on one side, and of his mother-in-law on the other). Lady Southdown, from her neighbouring house, reigned over the whole family--Pitt, Lady Jane, Miss Crawley, Briggs, Bowls, Firkin, and all.", "그리고 (가엾은 피트로서는 큰 골칫거리였으니, 그는 한쪽에서는 고모의 변덕에, 다른 쪽에서는 장모의 변덕에 시달리며 자신이 더없이 부당한 처지에 놓였다고 여겼다) 사우스다운 부인은 이웃한 자기 집에서 온 식구를 — 피트며, 제인 부인이며, 크롤리 양이며, 브리그스며, 보울스며, 퍼킨이며 죄다 — 군림하며 다스렸다."], ["She pitilessly dosed them with her tracts and her medicine, she dismissed Creamer, she installed Rodgers, and soon stripped Miss Crawley of even the semblance of authority.", "그녀는 가차 없이 그들에게 자기 소책자와 약을 들이부었고, 크리머를 내쫓고 로저스를 앉혔으며, 머지않아 크롤리 양에게서 권위라고는 그 시늉마저 죄다 벗겨 버렸다."], ["The poor soul grew so timid that she actually left off bullying Briggs any more, and clung to her niece, more fond and terrified every day.", "가엾은 그녀는 어찌나 소심해졌던지 실제로 더는 브리그스를 들볶지 않게 되었고, 날마다 더욱 다정하면서도 겁에 질려 조카며느리에게 매달렸다."], ["Peace to thee, kind and selfish, vain and generous old heathen!--We shall see thee no more.", "다정하면서도 이기적이고, 허영에 차 있으면서도 너그러웠던, 늙은 이교도여, 그대에게 평안이 깃들기를!"], ["Let us hope that Lady Jane supported her kindly, and led her with gentle hand out of the busy struggle of Vanity Fair.", "우리는 이제 다시 그대를 보지 못하리라. 부디 제인 부인이 그녀를 다정히 부축하여, 허영의 시장의 분주한 아귀다툼에서 부드러운 손으로 이끌어 내 주었기를 바라자."]], [["Widow and Mother\nThe news of the great fights of Quatre Bras and Waterloo reached England at the same time.", "카트르브라와 워털루의 대전투 소식은 같은 시각에 영국에 당도했다."], ["The Gazette first published the result of the two battles; at which glorious intelligence all England thrilled with triumph and fear.", "관보가 가장 먼저 그 두 전투의 결과를 발표했고, 그 영광스러운 소식에 온 영국이 승리감과 두려움으로 전율했다."], ["Particulars then followed; and after the announcement of the victories came the list of the wounded and the slain.", "이어 자세한 내용이 뒤따랐다. 그리고 승전 소식 다음에는 부상자와 전사자 명단이 실렸다."], ["Who can tell the dread with which that catalogue was opened and read!", "그 명단을 펼쳐 읽어 내려갈 때의 그 공포를 누가 능히 말할 수 있겠는가!"], ["Fancy, at every village and homestead almost through the three kingdoms, the great news coming of the battles in Flanders, and the feelings of exultation and gratitude, bereavement and sickening dismay, when the lists of the regimental losses were gone through, and it became known whether the dear friend and relative had escaped or fallen.", "세 왕국에 걸친 거의 모든 마을과 농가에 플랑드르 전투의 대뉴스가 당도하는 광경을, 그리고 연대별 손실 명단을 훑어 내려가며 사랑하는 벗이며 친지가 무사한지 쓰러졌는지를 알게 될 때의 그 환희와 감사, 사별과 속이 메스꺼워지는 절망의 심정을 떠올려 보라. 수고를 마다하지 않고 그 시절의 신문철을 들춰 보는 사람이라면, 누구든 지금이라도 그 숨 막히는 기대의 정적을 간접적으로나마 느낄 수 있을 것이다."], ["Anybody who will take the trouble of looking back to a file of the newspapers of the time, must, even now, feel at second-hand this breathless pause of expectation.", "사상자 명단은 날마다 계속 이어진다. 다음 호에 계속되는 이야기를 읽다가 한복판에서 멈추는 것처럼 말이다."], ["The lists of casualties are carried on from day to day: you stop in the midst as in a story which is to be continued in our next.", "그 신문들이 인쇄기에서 갓 나온 채 연달아 당도할 때의 심정이 어떠했을지 생각해 보라."], ["Think what the feelings must have been as those papers followed each other fresh from the press; and if such an interest could be felt in our country, and about a battle where but twenty thousand of our people were engaged, think of the condition of Europe for twenty years before, where people were fighting, not by thousands, but by millions; each one of whom as he struck his enemy wounded horribly some other innocent heart far away.", "그리고 우리 국민 가운데 겨우 이만 명이 참전한 전투 하나를 두고 우리나라에서 그토록 큰 관심이 일었다면, 그 이십 년 전, 사람들이 수천 명이 아니라 수백만 명 단위로 싸웠던 유럽의 형편을 생각해 보라. 그 한 사람 한 사람이 적을 칠 때마다 저 멀리 어딘가에 있는 또 다른 죄 없는 가슴 하나를 끔찍하게 찢어 놓았던 것이다."]], [["The news which that famous Gazette brought to the Osbornes gave a dreadful shock to the family and its chief.", "그 유명한 관보가 오스본 일가에 전한 소식은 가족과 그 가장에게 무서운 충격을 안겼다."], ["The girls indulged unrestrained in their grief.", "딸들은 아무런 거리낌 없이 슬픔에 몸을 내맡겼다."], ["The gloom-stricken old father was still more borne down by his fate and sorrow.", "침통에 짓눌린 늙은 아버지는 그 운명과 비탄에 더더욱 짓눌렸다."], ["He strove to think that a judgment was on the boy for his disobedience.", "그는 아들의 불순종에 내려진 심판이라고 생각하려 애썼다."], ["He dared not own that the severity of the sentence frightened him, and that its fulfilment had come too soon upon his curses.", "그 선고의 가혹함이 자신을 두렵게 한다는 것을, 그리고 그 저주에 너무도 빨리 응보가 떨어졌다는 것을 차마 인정할 수 없었다."], ["Sometimes a shuddering terror struck him, as if he had been the author of the doom which he had called down on his son.", "때로는 마치 자신이 아들에게 내려 달라고 빌었던 그 파멸의 장본인이라도 되는 양, 부르르 떨리는 공포가 그를 엄습했다."], ["There was a chance before of reconciliation.", "전에는 화해의 기회가 있었다."], ["The boy's wife might have died; or he might have come back and said, Father I have sinned.", "아들의 아내가 죽을 수도 있었고, 아니면 아들이 돌아와 \"아버지, 제가 죄를 지었습니다\"라고 말할 수도 있었다."], ["But there was no hope now.", "그러나 이제는 아무 희망도 없었다."], ["He stood on the other side of the gulf impassable, haunting his parent with sad eyes.", "아들은 건널 수 없는 심연의 저편에 서서, 슬픈 눈으로 아비를 따라다니며 괴롭혔다."], ["He remembered them once before so in a fever, when every one thought the lad was dying, and he lay on his bed speechless, and gazing with a dreadful gloom.", "그는 전에 한 번 그 눈을 본 일을 떠올렸다. 열병에 걸려 모두가 그 아이가 죽어 간다고 여겼을 때, 그 아이는 침대에 누워 말도 못 한 채 무서운 침울함을 띠고 멍하니 바라보고 있었다."], ["Good God!", "오, 하느님!"], ["how the father clung to the doctor then, and with what a sickening anxiety he followed him: what a weight of grief was off his mind when, after the crisis of the fever, the lad recovered, and looked at his father once more with eyes that recognised him.", "그때 아비가 의사에게 얼마나 매달렸던지, 또 얼마나 속이 타들어 가는 불안으로 그의 뒤를 좇았던지. 열병의 고비가 지나고 그 아이가 회복되어 다시금 아비를 알아보는 눈으로 그를 바라보았을 때, 그의 마음에서 떨어져 나간 비탄의 무게는 또 얼마나 컸던지."], ["But now there was no help or cure, or chance of reconcilement: above all, there were no humble words to soothe vanity outraged and furious, or bring to its natural flow the poisoned, angry blood.", "그러나 지금은 도움도, 치유도, 화해의 기회도 없었다. 무엇보다도, 모욕당해 격노한 허영을 달래거나 독과 분노로 끓어오른 피를 본래의 흐름으로 되돌릴, 그 어떤 겸손한 말 한마디도 없었다."], ["And it is hard to say which pang it was that tore the proud father's heart most keenly--that his son should have gone out of the reach of his forgiveness, or that the apology which his own pride expected should have escaped him.", "그리고 이 거만한 아비의 가슴을 가장 날카롭게 찢어 놓은 고통이 어느 것이었는지는 말하기 어렵다. 아들이 그의 용서가 미칠 수 없는 곳으로 가 버렸다는 것인지, 아니면 그 자신의 자존심이 기대해 마지않던 그 사과의 말을 영영 듣지 못하게 되었다는 것인지."]], [["Whatever his sensations might have been, however, the stern old man would have no confidant.", "그러나 그의 심정이 어떠했든, 이 준엄한 늙은이는 누구에게도 속을 털어놓으려 하지 않았다."], ["He never mentioned his son's name to his daughters; but ordered the elder to place all the females of the establishment in mourning; and desired that the male servants should be similarly attired in deep black.", "그는 딸들에게 아들의 이름을 결코 입에 올리지 않았다. 다만 큰딸에게 명하여 집안의 모든 여자에게 상복을 입히게 했고, 남자 하인들도 그와 마찬가지로 짙은 검정 옷을 입히도록 분부했다."], ["All parties and entertainments, of course, were to be put off.", "물론 모든 파티와 연회는 미루기로 되었다."], ["No communications were made to his future son-in-law, whose marriage-day had been fixed: but there was enough in Mr.", "혼삿날까지 정해져 있던 장차의 사위에게는 아무런 통보도 가지 않았다."], ["Osborne's appearance to prevent Mr. Bullock from making any inquiries, or in any way pressing forward that ceremony.", "그러나 오스본 씨의 안색만으로도 불록 씨가 어떤 문의를 하거나 어떤 식으로든 그 예식을 서두르지 못하기에 충분했다."], ["He and the ladies whispered about it under their voices in the drawing-room sometimes, whither the father never came.", "그와 숙녀들은 이따금 응접실에서 그 일을 두고 목소리를 낮춰 소곤거렸는데, 그곳에 아버지는 결코 발을 들이지 않았다."], ["He remained constantly in his own study; the whole front part of the house being closed until some time after the completion of the general mourning.", "그는 줄곧 자기 서재에만 틀어박혀 있었고, 전체 상기(喪期)가 끝나고도 한참 뒤까지 집의 앞쪽 부분은 모두 닫혀 있었다."]], [["About three weeks after the 18th of June, Mr. Osborne's acquaintance, Sir William Dobbin, called at Mr.", "6월 18일로부터 삼 주쯤 지난 어느 날, 오스본 씨의 지인인 윌리엄 도빈 경이 몹시 창백하고 동요한 얼굴로 러셀 광장의 오스본 씨 댁을 찾아와 한사코 그를 만나야겠다고 청했다."], ["Osborne's house in Russell Square, with a very pale and agitated face, and insisted upon seeing that gentleman. Ushered into his room, and after a few words, which neither the speaker nor the host understood, the former produced from an inclosure a letter sealed with a large red seal.", "방으로 안내되어 들어가서, 말하는 쪽도 듣는 쪽도 알아듣지 못한 몇 마디를 나눈 뒤, 손님은 봉투에서 커다란 빨간 봉인이 찍힌 편지 한 통을 꺼냈다."], ["\"My son, Major Dobbin,\"", "\"내 아들 도빈 소령이,\""], ["the Alderman said, with some hesitation, \"despatched me a letter by an officer of the --th, who arrived in town to-day.", "시 참사회원이 다소 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"오늘 시내에 도착한 ××연대의 한 장교 편에 내게 편지를 보냈소."], ["My son's letter contains one for you, Osborne.\"", "그 아이 편지 안에 당신 앞으로 온 편지가 한 통 들어 있더이다, 오스본.\""], ["The Alderman placed the letter on the table, and Osborne stared at him for a moment or two in silence.", "참사회원은 그 편지를 탁자 위에 올려놓았고, 오스본은 잠시 말없이 그를 빤히 바라보았다."], ["His looks frightened the ambassador, who after looking guiltily for a little time at the grief-stricken man, hurried away without another word.", "그의 눈빛에 그 사자(使者)는 겁을 먹었고, 슬픔에 짓눌린 사내를 잠깐 죄지은 듯한 표정으로 바라보다가 더는 한마디도 없이 황급히 자리를 떴다."]], [["The letter was in George's well-known bold handwriting.", "그 편지는 조지의 익히 알려진, 굵고 대담한 필체로 쓰여 있었다."], ["It was that one which he had written before daybreak on the 16th of June, and just before he took leave of Amelia.", "그것은 그가 6월 16일 동트기 전, 애밀리아와 작별하기 직전에 쓴 바로 그 편지였다."], ["The great red seal was emblazoned with the sham coat of arms which Osborne had assumed from the Peerage, with \"Pax in bello\"", "그 큰 빨간 봉인에는 오스본이 귀족 명감에서 빌려와 제멋대로 쓰던 가짜 문장(紋章)이 새겨져 있었고, \"전쟁 중에도 평화\"라는 명구가 적혀 있었다."], ["for a motto; that of the ducal house with which the vain old man tried to fancy himself connected.", "이 허영심 많은 늙은이가 자신과 인연이 닿는다고 멋대로 상상하던 어느 공작 가문의 문장이었다."], ["The hand that signed it would never hold pen or sword more.", "그 편지에 서명한 손은 이제 다시는 펜도 칼도 쥐지 못할 터였다."], ["The very seal that sealed it had been robbed from George's dead body as it lay on the field of battle.", "그것을 봉한 바로 그 봉인은 전장에 쓰러져 있던 조지의 시신에서 약탈당한 것이었다."], ["The father knew nothing of this, but sat and looked at the letter in terrified vacancy.", "아비는 그런 사실을 전혀 알지 못한 채, 앉아서 겁에 질린 공허한 눈으로 편지를 바라보았다."], ["He almost fell when he went to open it.", "그것을 열려고 했을 때 그는 거의 쓰러질 뻔했다."]], [["Have you ever had a difference with a dear friend?", "당신은 사랑하는 벗과 사이가 틀어져 본 적이 있는가?"], ["How his letters, written in the period of love and confidence, sicken and rebuke you!", "사랑과 신뢰의 시절에 쓰인 그 사람의 편지들이 얼마나 사람의 속을 메스껍게 하고 또 책망하는지!"], ["What a dreary mourning it is to dwell upon those vehement protests of dead affection!", "죽어 버린 애정의 그 격렬한 맹세들을 곱씹는 일이 얼마나 음울한 애도인지!"], ["What lying epitaphs they make over the corpse of love!", "그것들은 사랑의 시신 위에 새겨진 얼마나 거짓된 비문(碑文)인가!"], ["What dark, cruel comments upon Life and Vanities!", "인생과 허영에 대한 얼마나 어둡고 잔인한 주석인가!"], ["Most of us have got or written drawers full of them.", "우리 대부분은 그런 편지가 서랍 가득 들어 있거나 그런 편지를 써 본 적이 있다."], ["They are closet-skeletons which we keep and shun.", "그것들은 우리가 간직하면서도 외면하는, 벽장 속 해골이다."], ["Osborne trembled long before the letter from his dead son.", "오스본은 죽은 아들의 편지 앞에서 오랫동안 떨었다."]], [["The poor boy's letter did not say much.", "그 가엾은 아이의 편지에는 별다른 말이 없었다."], ["He had been too proud to acknowledge the tenderness which his heart felt.", "그는 너무도 거만해서 자기 가슴에 어린 애정을 인정하지 못했던 것이다."], ["He only said, that on the eve of a great battle, he wished to bid his father farewell, and solemnly to implore his good offices for the wife--it might be for the child--whom he left behind him.", "그는 다만 대전투를 앞둔 전날 밤, 아버지께 작별을 고하고 싶다는 것과, 자기가 뒤에 남기고 가는 아내를—어쩌면 그 아이까지도—위해 부디 보살펴 주시기를 엄숙히 간청한다고만 적었다."], ["He owned with contrition that his irregularities and his extravagance had already wasted a large part of his mother's little fortune.", "그는 자신의 방탕과 사치로 이미 어머니가 남긴 얼마 안 되는 재산의 큰 부분을 탕진했음을 뉘우치며 고백했다."], ["He thanked his father for his former generous conduct; and he promised him that if he fell on the field or survived it, he would act in a manner worthy of the name of George Osborne.", "또한 지난날 아버지가 베풀어 준 너그러운 처사에 감사하며, 전장에서 쓰러지든 살아남든 조지 오스본이라는 이름에 부끄럽지 않게 처신하겠노라고 약속했다."]], [["His English habit, pride, awkwardness perhaps, had prevented him from saying more.", "그의 영국식 습성과 자존심, 어쩌면 서투름이 더는 말을 하지 못하게 막았던 것이다."], ["His father could not see the kiss George had placed on the superscription of his letter.", "아비는 조지가 편지 겉봉에 입맞춤을 남긴 것을 알아보지 못했다."], ["Mr. Osborne dropped it with the bitterest, deadliest pang of balked affection and revenge.", "오스본 씨는 애정과 복수가 함께 좌절된 가장 쓰라리고 치명적인 고통과 더불어 그 편지를 떨어뜨렸다."], ["His son was still beloved and unforgiven.", "그의 아들은 여전히 사랑받았으나 용서받지는 못했다."]], [["About two months afterwards, however, as the young ladies of the family went to church with their father, they remarked how he took a different seat from that which he usually occupied when he chose to attend divine worship; and that from his cushion opposite, he looked up at the wall over their heads.", "그러나 그로부터 두 달쯤 지났을 무렵, 이 집안의 아가씨들이 아버지와 함께 교회에 갔다가, 아버지가 예배에 참석하기로 마음먹었을 때 늘 앉던 자리와 다른 자리를 잡는 것을, 그리고 맞은편 방석에서 자기들 머리 너머의 벽을 올려다보는 것을 알아챘다."], ["This caused the young women likewise to gaze in the direction towards which their father's gloomy eyes pointed: and they saw an elaborate monument upon the wall, where Britannia was represented weeping over an urn, and a broken sword and a couchant lion indicated that the piece of sculpture had been erected in honour of a deceased warrior.", "이에 아가씨들도 아버지의 침울한 눈길이 향하는 쪽을 바라보게 되었고, 벽에 정교한 기념비 하나가 걸려 있는 것을 보았다. 거기에는 브리타니아 여신이 유골 단지 위에서 눈물 흘리는 모습이 새겨져 있었고, 부러진 검과 엎드린 사자가 그 조각이 어느 전사한 무인을 기리어 세워진 것임을 일러 주고 있었다."], ["The sculptors of those days had stocks of such funereal emblems in hand; as you may see still on the walls of St. Paul's, which are covered with hundreds of these braggart heathen allegories.", "그 시절의 조각가들은 그런 장례용 표상들을 잔뜩 갖춰 두고 있었으니, 지금도 세인트폴 대성당의 벽에서 볼 수 있듯, 그 벽은 이런 허세 가득한 이교적 우의(寓意) 수백 개로 뒤덮여 있다."], ["There was a constant demand for them during the first fifteen years of the present century.", "금세기의 첫 십오 년 동안 그런 것들에 대한 수요가 끊이지 않았던 것이다."]], [["Under the memorial in question were emblazoned the well-known and pompous Osborne arms; and the inscription said, that the monument was \"Sacred to the memory of George Osborne, Junior, Esq., late a Captain in his Majesty's --th regiment of foot, who fell on the 18th of June, 1815, aged 28 years, while fighting for his king and country in the glorious victory of Waterloo.", "문제의 그 기념비 아래에는 그 유명하고 거창한 오스본 가문의 문장이 새겨져 있었다. 그리고 비문에는 이 기념비가 \"국왕 폐하의 ××보병연대 대위로 복무하다가 1815년 6월 18일, 워털루의 영광스러운 승전에서 국왕과 조국을 위해 싸우던 중 향년 스물여덟에 쓰러진 고(故) 조지 오스본 2세 님을 추모하여 바친다."], ["Dulce et decorum est pro patria mori.\"", "조국을 위해 죽는 것은 감미롭고 명예로운 일이로다\"라고 적혀 있었다."]], [["The sight of that stone agitated the nerves of the sisters so much, that Miss Maria was compelled to leave the church.", "그 비석을 보고 자매들은 어찌나 신경이 동요했던지, 마리아 양은 그만 교회를 나가지 않을 수 없었다."], ["The congregation made way respectfully for those sobbing girls clothed in deep black, and pitied the stern old father seated opposite the memorial of the dead soldier.", "신자들은 짙은 상복 차림으로 흐느끼는 그 아가씨들에게 정중하게 길을 비켜 주었고, 전사한 군인의 기념비 맞은편에 앉은 준엄한 늙은 아버지를 가엾이 여겼다."], ["\"Will he forgive Mrs. George?\"", "\"아버지가 조지의 부인을 용서하실까?\""], ["the girls said to themselves as soon as their ebullition of grief was over.", "슬픔의 격발이 가라앉자마자 아가씨들은 속으로 그렇게 생각했다."], ["Much conversation passed too among the acquaintances of the Osborne family, who knew of the rupture between the son and father caused by the former's marriage, as to the chance of a reconciliation with the young widow.", "아들의 결혼이 빚어낸 부자간의 절연을 알고 있던 오스본 가문의 지인들 사이에서도, 그 젊은 미망인과 화해할 가능성을 두고 많은 이야기가 오갔다."], ["There were bets among the gentlemen both about Russell Square and in the City.", "러셀 광장 일대에서도 시티에서도 신사들 사이에 내기가 벌어졌다."]], [["If the sisters had any anxiety regarding the possible recognition of Amelia as a daughter of the family, it was increased presently, and towards the end of the autumn, by their father's announcement that he was going abroad.", "만약 자매들이 애밀리아가 이 집안의 딸로 인정받을지도 모른다는 데 대해 조금이라도 불안을 품고 있었다면, 그 불안은 이내, 그러니까 가을이 저물어 갈 무렵, 아버지가 외국에 나가겠다고 알리면서 한층 커졌다."], ["He did not say whither, but they knew at once that his steps would be turned towards Belgium, and were aware that George's widow was still in Brussels.", "그는 어디로 가는지는 말하지 않았으나, 자매들은 그의 발길이 벨기에로 향하리라는 것을 단번에 알아챘고, 조지의 미망인이 아직 브뤼셀에 머물고 있음을 알고 있었다."], ["They had pretty accurate news indeed of poor Amelia from Lady Dobbin and her daughters.", "사실 그들은 도빈 부인과 그 딸들을 통해 가엾은 애밀리아의 소식을 제법 정확히 듣고 있었다."], ["Our honest Captain had been promoted in consequence of the death of the second Major of the regiment on the field; and the brave O'Dowd, who had distinguished himself greatly here as upon all occasions where he had a chance to show his coolness and valour, was a Colonel and Companion of the Bath.", "우리의 정직한 대위는 연대의 둘째 소령이 전장에서 전사한 덕분에 진급했고, 용맹한 오다우드는 침착함과 용기를 보일 기회가 있을 때마다 늘 그러했듯 이번에도 크게 무공을 떨쳐 대령에 올랐고 바스 훈장 동반 작위를 받았다."]], [["Very many of the brave --th, who had suffered severely upon both days of action, were still at Brussels in the autumn, recovering of their wounds.", "양일간의 전투에서 큰 손실을 입은 용맹한 ××연대의 수많은 병사들이 가을에도 여전히 브뤼셀에 남아 부상을 회복하고 있었다."], ["The city was a vast military hospital for months after the great battles; and as men and officers began to rally from their hurts, the gardens and places of public resort swarmed with maimed warriors, old and young, who, just rescued out of death, fell to gambling, and gaiety, and love-making, as people of Vanity Fair will do.", "그 도시는 대전투가 끝난 뒤 여러 달 동안 거대한 군 병원이나 다름없었다. 그리고 병사며 장교들이 부상에서 기운을 차리기 시작하자, 정원이며 공공장소마다 사지가 성치 않은 노소(老少)의 무인들이 우글거렸으니, 죽음에서 막 건져 올려진 이들이 허영의 시장 사람들이 으레 그러하듯 도박과 흥청거림과 사랑놀음에 빠져들었던 것이다."], ["Mr. Osborne found out some of the --th easily.", "오스본 씨는 ××연대 사람들 몇몇을 손쉽게 찾아냈다."], ["He knew their uniform quite well, and had been used to follow all the promotions and exchanges in the regiment, and loved to talk about it and its officers as if he had been one of the number.", "그는 그 군복을 아주 잘 알고 있었고, 연대의 모든 진급과 전출을 빠짐없이 챙겨 보는 데 익숙했으며, 마치 자신이 그 일원이라도 되는 양 그 연대와 장교들에 대해 이야기하기를 좋아했다."], ["On the day after his arrival at Brussels, and as he issued from his hotel, which faced the park, he saw a soldier in the well-known facings, reposing on a stone bench in the garden, and went and sate down trembling by the wounded convalescent man.", "브뤼셀에 도착한 다음 날, 공원을 마주한 호텔에서 나오다가 그는 익히 아는 그 군복 깃을 한 병사 하나가 정원의 돌 벤치에서 쉬고 있는 것을 보았고, 다가가 그 부상에서 회복 중인 사내 곁에 떨면서 앉았다."]], [["\"Were you in Captain Osborne's company?\"", "\"오스본 대위 중대에 있었나?\""], ["he said, and added, after a pause, \"he was my son, sir.\"", "그가 물었고, 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 덧붙였다. \"그 사람이 내 아들이라네.\""]], [["The man was not of the Captain's company, but he lifted up his unwounded arm and touched his cap sadly and respectfully to the haggard broken-spirited gentleman who questioned him.", "그 사내는 대위의 중대 소속이 아니었으나, 성한 팔을 들어 올려 자기에게 묻는 그 초췌하고 기운 꺾인 신사에게 슬프고도 정중하게 군모에 손을 가져다 댔다."], ["\"The whole army didn't contain a finer or a better officer,\"", "\"전군을 통틀어도 그분보다 더 훌륭하고 더 나은 장교는 없었습니다.\""], ["the soldier said.", "병사가 말했다."], ["\"The Sergeant of the Captain's company (Captain Raymond had it now), was in town, though, and was just well of a shot in the shoulder.", "\"다만 대위 중대(이제는 레이먼드 대위가 맡고 있습니다)의 부사관 하나가 시내에 있는데, 어깨에 총상을 입었다가 막 회복한 참입니다."], ["His honour might see him if he liked, who could tell him anything he wanted to know about--about the --th's actions.", "나리께서 원하시면 그 사람을 만나 보실 수 있을 겁니다. ××연대의 전투에 관해 알고 싶으신 건 무엇이든 그가 말씀드릴 수 있을 테니까요."], ["But his honour had seen Major Dobbin, no doubt, the brave Captain's great friend; and Mrs.", "하지만 나리께서는 분명 도빈 소령님을 뵈셨겠지요, 그 용맹한 대위의 둘도 없는 벗 말입니다."], ["Osborne, who was here too, and had been very bad, he heard everybody say.", "그리고 여기 계셨던 오스본 부인도요. 다들 그분이 몹시 안 좋으셨다고들 하더군요."], ["They say she was out of her mind like for six weeks or more.", "한 육 주 넘게 거의 정신이 나가 계셨다고들 합니다."], ["But your honour knows all about that--and asking your pardon\"--the man added.", "하지만 나리께서야 그런 일은 다 알고 계실 테고요—외람된 말씀입니다만,\" 사내가 덧붙였다."]], [["Osborne put a guinea into the soldier's hand, and told him he should have another if he would bring the Sergeant to the Hotel du Parc; a promise which very soon brought the desired officer to Mr.", "오스본은 병사의 손에 1기니를 쥐여 주며, 그 부사관을 파르크 호텔로 데려와 준다면 한 닢을 더 주겠다고 일렀다. 그 약속에 곧바로 원하던 장교가 오스본 씨 앞에 나타났다."], ["Osborne's presence. And the first soldier went away; and after telling a comrade or two how Captain Osborne's father was arrived, and what a free-handed generous gentleman he was, they went and made good cheer with drink and feasting, as long as the guineas lasted which had come from the proud purse of the mourning old father.", "그리고 처음의 그 병사는 자리를 떴고, 동료 한둘에게 오스본 대위의 아버지가 와 있다는 것과 그가 얼마나 손이 큰 너그러운 신사인지를 떠벌린 뒤, 그들은 상중인 늙은 아버지의 거만한 지갑에서 나온 그 기니가 다 떨어질 때까지 술과 잔치로 한껏 흥청거렸다."]], [["In the Sergeant's company, who was also just convalescent, Osborne made the journey of Waterloo and Quatre Bras, a journey which thousands of his countrymen were then taking.", "오스본은 그 부사관과 동행하여—이 부사관 역시 막 회복한 참이었다—워털루와 카트르브라로 여정을 떠났으니, 그 무렵 그의 동포 수천 명이 똑같이 밟고 있던 길이었다."], ["He took the Sergeant with him in his carriage, and went through both fields under his guidance.", "그는 부사관을 자기 마차에 태우고 그 안내를 받으며 두 전장을 두루 돌아보았다."], ["He saw the point of the road where the regiment marched into action on the 16th, and the slope down which they drove the French cavalry who were pressing on the retreating Belgians.", "그는 16일에 연대가 전투에 투입되며 행군해 들어간 길목과, 퇴각하는 벨기에군을 압박해 오던 프랑스 기병대를 몰아붙여 내려간 비탈을 보았다."], ["There was the spot where the noble Captain cut down the French officer who was grappling with the young Ensign for the colours, the Colour-Sergeants having been shot down.", "군기 부사관들이 모두 총에 맞아 쓰러지자, 군기를 두고 젊은 기수와 맞붙어 싸우던 프랑스 장교를 그 고결한 대위가 베어 넘긴 바로 그 자리도 있었다."], ["Along this road they retreated on the next day, and here was the bank at which the regiment bivouacked under the rain of the night of the seventeenth.", "그들이 이튿날 후퇴해 간 길도 이 길을 따라 나 있었고, 17일 밤 빗속에서 연대가 노숙했던 둑도 여기 있었다."], ["Further on was the position which they took and held during the day, forming time after time to receive the charge of the enemy's horsemen and lying down under the shelter of the bank from the furious French cannonade.", "더 멀리에는 그들이 그날 하루 동안 점령하고 지켜 낸 진지가 있었으니, 거기서 그들은 적 기병대의 돌격을 받기 위해 몇 번이고 대형을 짜며, 프랑스군의 맹렬한 포격을 피해 둑 그늘에 엎드려 있었다."], ["And it was at this declivity when at evening the whole English line received the order to advance, as the enemy fell back after his last charge, that the Captain, hurraying and rushing down the hill waving his sword, received a shot and fell dead.", "그리고 적이 마지막 돌격 끝에 물러서고, 저녁이 되어 영국 전선 전체가 진격하라는 명령을 받았을 때, 바로 이 비탈에서 대위가 환호하며 칼을 휘두르며 언덕 아래로 내달리다가 총에 맞아 쓰러져 숨졌던 것이다."], ["\"It was Major Dobbin who took back the Captain's body to Brussels,\"", "\"대위의 시신을 브뤼셀로 모셔 온 것은 도빈 소령님이었습니다.\""], ["the Sergeant said, in a low voice, \"and had him buried, as your honour knows.\"", "부사관이 나직이 말했다. \"그리고 나리께서도 아시다시피, 매장을 치러 주셨지요.\""], ["The peasants and relic-hunters about the place were screaming round the pair, as the soldier told his story, offering for sale all sorts of mementoes of the fight, crosses, and epaulets, and shattered cuirasses, and eagles.", "병사가 그 이야기를 풀어놓는 동안, 그 일대의 농부들과 유물 사냥꾼들이 두 사람 주위에 몰려들어 빽빽 소리치며, 십자 훈장이며 견장이며 박살 난 흉갑이며 독수리 군기 장식이며 온갖 전투 기념품을 사라고 내밀었다."]], [["Osborne gave a sumptuous reward to the Sergeant when he parted with him, after having visited the scenes of his son's last exploits.", "오스본은 아들의 마지막 무용담의 현장들을 둘러본 뒤, 부사관과 헤어지면서 그에게 후한 보수를 베풀었다."], ["His burial-place he had already seen.", "아들의 매장지는 이미 본 터였다."], ["Indeed, he had driven thither immediately after his arrival at Brussels.", "사실 그는 브뤼셀에 도착하자마자 곧장 그리로 마차를 몰았던 것이다."], ["George's body lay in the pretty burial-ground of Laeken, near the city; in which place, having once visited it on a party of pleasure, he had lightly expressed a wish to have his grave made.", "조지의 시신은 도시 인근 라켄의 아름다운 묘지에 안치되어 있었다. 일찍이 유람 삼아 한 번 그곳을 찾았을 때, 그는 가벼운 마음으로 자기 무덤을 거기에 두고 싶다는 뜻을 비친 적이 있었다."], ["And there the young officer was laid by his friend, in the unconsecrated corner of the garden, separated by a little hedge from the temples and towers and plantations of flowers and shrubs, under which the Roman Catholic dead repose.", "그리하여 이 젊은 장교는 벗의 손에 의해, 정원의 축성받지 못한 한구석에 묻혔으니, 로마 가톨릭교도 망자들이 잠든 사원이며 탑이며 꽃과 관목을 심은 화단으로부터 자그마한 산울타리 하나로 갈라진 자리였다."], ["It seemed a humiliation to old Osborne to think that his son, an English gentleman, a captain in the famous British army, should not be found worthy to lie in ground where mere foreigners were buried.", "영국 신사이자 명예로운 영국군의 대위였던 자기 아들이, 한낱 외국인들이 묻힌 땅에 누울 자격조차 갖지 못한 것으로 여겨졌다는 사실이, 늙은 오스본에게는 모욕처럼 느껴졌다."], ["Which of us is there can tell how much vanity lurks in our warmest regard for others, and how selfish our love is?", "우리 가운데 누가 능히 말할 수 있겠는가, 남을 향한 우리의 가장 따뜻한 정 속에 얼마나 많은 허영이 도사리고 있는지, 또 우리의 사랑이 얼마나 이기적인지를?"], ["Old Osborne did not speculate much upon the mingled nature of his feelings, and how his instinct and selfishness were combating together.", "늙은 오스본은 자기 감정의 그 뒤섞인 본질에 대해, 또 자신의 본능과 이기심이 어떻게 서로 다투고 있는지에 대해 깊이 헤아려 보지 않았다."], ["He firmly believed that everything he did was right, that he ought on all occasions to have his own way--and like the sting of a wasp or serpent his hatred rushed out armed and poisonous against anything like opposition.", "그는 자신이 하는 모든 일이 옳으며, 어떤 경우에도 자기 뜻대로 해야 마땅하다고 굳게 믿었다. 그리고 말벌이나 뱀의 독침처럼, 그의 증오는 무엇이든 반대 비슷한 것을 향해 독을 품고 무장한 채 튀어나왔다."], ["He was proud of his hatred as of everything else.", "그는 다른 모든 것에 대해서와 마찬가지로 자신의 증오를 자랑스러워했다."], ["Always to be right, always to trample forward, and never to doubt, are not these the great qualities with which dullness takes the lead in the world?", "늘 옳은 것, 늘 짓밟으며 나아가는 것, 결코 의심하지 않는 것—이것들이야말로 우둔함이 세상에서 앞장서 나아가게 하는 그 위대한 자질들이 아니겠는가?"]], [["As after the drive to Waterloo, Mr. Osborne's carriage was nearing the gates of the city at sunset, they met another open barouche, in which were a couple of ladies and a gentleman, and by the side of which an officer was riding.", "워털루로 다녀온 길에, 해 질 녘 오스본 씨의 마차가 도시의 성문에 가까워졌을 때, 그들은 또 다른 무개 사륜마차 한 대와 마주쳤다."], ["Osborne gave a start back, and the Sergeant, seated with him, cast a look of surprise at his neighbour, as he touched his cap to the officer, who mechanically returned his salute.", "그 안에는 숙녀 두 사람과 신사 한 사람이 타고 있었고, 그 옆으로는 장교 하나가 말을 타고 따르고 있었다. 오스본은 흠칫 몸을 뒤로 뺐고, 그와 함께 앉아 있던 부사관은 옆 사람을 놀란 눈으로 흘끗 보면서 그 장교에게 군모에 손을 가져다 대 인사했고, 장교는 기계적으로 그 경례에 답했다."], ["It was Amelia, with the lame young Ensign by her side, and opposite to her her faithful friend Mrs.", "다리를 저는 젊은 기수를 곁에 두고, 맞은편에는 충직한 벗 오다우드 부인을 앉힌 애밀리아였다."], ["O'Dowd. It was Amelia, but how changed from the fresh and comely girl Osborne knew.", "애밀리아였다, 그러나 오스본이 알던 그 싱그럽고 어여쁜 아가씨에서 얼마나 변해 버렸는지."], ["Her face was white and thin.", "그녀의 얼굴은 핏기 없이 야위어 있었다."], ["Her pretty brown hair was parted under a widow's cap--the poor child.", "그 고운 갈색 머리는 미망인의 모자 아래로 가르마가 타져 있었으니—가엾은 아이였다."], ["Her eyes were fixed, and looking nowhere.", "그녀의 두 눈은 한곳에 고정된 채 아무 데도 보고 있지 않았다."], ["They stared blank in the face of Osborne, as the carriages crossed each other, but she did not know him; nor did he recognise her, until looking up, he saw Dobbin riding by her: and then he knew who it was.", "마차가 서로 엇갈릴 때 그 눈은 오스본의 얼굴을 멍하니 응시했으나, 그녀는 그를 알아보지 못했다. 그도 고개를 들어 그녀 곁에서 말을 타고 가는 도빈을 보기 전까지는 그녀를 알아보지 못했다."], ["He hated her.", "그러고 나서야 그는 그게 누구인지 알았다."], ["He did not know how much until he saw her there.", "그는 그녀를 증오했다."], ["When her carriage had passed on, he turned and stared at the Sergeant, with a curse and defiance in his eye cast at his companion, who could not help looking at him--as much as to say \"How dare you look at me?", "그녀가 거기 있는 것을 보기 전까지는 자신이 얼마나 그녀를 증오하는지 미처 알지 못했다. 그녀의 마차가 지나가 버리자, 그는 돌아앉아 부사관을 노려보았으니, 그 눈에는 저주와 적의가 어려 있어 자기 옆 사람을 향해 던져졌고, 부사관은 그를 쳐다보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Damn you! I do hate her.", "그 눈빛은 마치 이렇게 말하는 듯했다."], ["It is she who has tumbled my hopes and all my pride down.\"", "\"어디 감히 나를 쳐다보는가? 빌어먹을!"], ["\"Tell the scoundrel to drive on quick,\"", "그래, 나는 저 여자가 증오스럽다."], ["he shouted with an oath, to the lackey on the box.", "내 모든 희망과 내 모든 자존심을 무너뜨린 게 바로 저 여자란 말이다.\""], ["A minute afterwards, a horse came clattering over the pavement behind Osborne's carriage, and Dobbin rode up.", "\"저 망할 놈더러 어서 빨리 몰라고 해라.\" 그가 욕설을 내뱉으며 마부석의 하인에게 소리쳤다."], ["His thoughts had been elsewhere as the carriages passed each other, and it was not until he had ridden some paces forward, that he remembered it was Osborne who had just passed him.", "잠시 후, 오스본의 마차 뒤로 말 한 마리가 포석 위를 또각또각 달려왔고, 도빈이 말을 몰아 다가왔다. 마차들이 서로 엇갈릴 때 그의 생각은 다른 데 가 있었던 터라, 몇 걸음 앞으로 나아가고 나서야 비로소 방금 자기를 지나친 사람이 오스본이었음을 기억해 냈다."], ["Then he turned to examine if the sight of her father-in-law had made any impression on Amelia, but the poor girl did not know who had passed.", "그리고 그는 장인을 본 것이 애밀리아에게 어떤 동요라도 일으켰는지 살피려고 돌아보았으나, 가엾은 아가씨는 누가 지나갔는지조차 알지 못했다."], ["Then William, who daily used to accompany her in his drives, taking out his watch, made some excuse about an engagement which he suddenly recollected, and so rode off.", "그러자 날마다 그녀의 마차 산책에 동행하곤 하던 윌리엄은 시계를 꺼내 보더니, 문득 떠오른 약속이 있다는 둥 둘러대고는 말을 몰아 가 버렸다."], ["She did not remark that either: but sate looking before her, over the homely landscape towards the woods in the distance, by which George marched away.", "그녀는 그것 또한 알아채지 못했다. 다만 앞만 바라보며 앉아, 조지가 행군해 떠나간 저 멀리 숲 쪽으로 펼쳐진 수수한 풍경을 응시할 따름이었다."]], [["\"Mr. Osborne, Mr.", "\"오스본 씨, 오스본 씨!\""], ["Osborne!\" cried Dobbin, as he rode up and held out his hand.", "도빈이 말을 몰아 다가가 손을 내밀며 외쳤다."], ["Osborne made no motion to take it, but shouted out once more and with another curse to his servant to drive on.", "오스본은 그 손을 잡을 기색도 없이, 또 한 번 욕설과 함께 하인에게 어서 몰라고 소리쳤다."]], [["Dobbin laid his hand on the carriage side.", "도빈이 마차 옆면에 손을 얹었다."], ["\"I will see you, sir,\"", "\"당신을 꼭 만나야겠습니다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"I have a message for you.\"", "\"전해 드릴 말씀이 있어서요.\""]], [["\"From that woman?\"", "\"그 여자한테서 온 말이오?\""], ["said Osborne, fiercely.", "오스본이 사납게 말했다."]], [["\"No,\"", "\"아닙니다.\""], ["replied the other, \"from your son\"; at which Osborne fell back into the corner of his carriage, and Dobbin allowing it to pass on, rode close behind it, and so through the town until they reached Mr.", "상대가 대답했다. \"당신 아드님에게서 온 것입니다.\""], ["Osborne's hotel, and without a word. There he followed Osborne up to his apartments.", "그 말에 오스본은 마차 구석으로 털썩 물러앉았고, 도빈은 마차가 지나가게 둔 채 그 바로 뒤를 바싹 따라 말을 몰아, 한마디도 없이 그렇게 시내를 지나 마침내 오스본 씨의 호텔에 이르렀다."], ["George had often been in the rooms; they were the lodgings which the Crawleys had occupied during their stay in Brussels.", "거기서 그는 오스본을 따라 그의 방까지 올라갔다. 조지도 그 방에 자주 와 있던 적이 있었으니, 크롤리 부부가 브뤼셀에 머무는 동안 묵었던 바로 그 셋방이었다."]], [["\"Pray, have you any commands for me, Captain Dobbin, or, I beg your pardon, I should say MAJOR Dobbin, since better men than you are dead, and you step into their SHOES?\"", "\"그래, 내게 무슨 분부가 있으신가, 도빈 대위?"], ["said Mr.", "아, 이거 실례했군, 도빈 소령이라고 해야겠지."], ["Osborne, in that sarcastic tone which he sometimes was pleased to assume.", "당신보다 나은 사람들이 죽었으니 당신이 그 자리를 꿰찼을 테니 말이오.\" 오스본 씨가 이따금 기꺼이 취하곤 하던 그 빈정대는 어조로 말했다."]], [["\"Better men ARE dead,\"", "\"더 나은 사람들이 죽은 게 맞습니다.\""], ["Dobbin replied.", "도빈이 대답했다."], ["\"I want to speak to you about one.\"", "\"바로 그 한 사람에 대해 당신과 이야기를 나누고 싶습니다.\""]], [["\"Make it short, sir,\"", "\"짧게 하시오.\""], ["said the other with an oath, scowling at his visitor.", "상대가 욕설을 내뱉으며, 손님에게 험한 눈초리를 보내며 말했다."]], [["\"I am here as his closest friend,\"", "\"저는 그의 가장 가까운 벗으로서,\""], ["the Major resumed, \"and the executor of his will.", "소령이 말을 이었다. \"또 그의 유언 집행인으로서 여기 왔습니다."], ["He made it before he went into action.", "그는 전투에 나가기 전에 유언장을 작성했습니다."], ["Are you aware how small his means are, and of the straitened circumstances of his widow?\"", "그의 재산이 얼마나 보잘것없는지, 또 그 미망인의 형편이 얼마나 옹색한지 알고 계십니까?\""]], [["\"I don't know his widow, sir,\"", "\"나는 그 사람 미망인을 모르오.\""], ["Osborne said.", "오스본이 말했다."], ["\"Let her go back to her father.\"", "\"그 여자는 제 아비한테로 돌아가라고 하시오.\""], ["But the gentleman whom he addressed was determined to remain in good temper, and went on without heeding the interruption.", "그러나 그가 말을 건넨 신사는 평정을 잃지 않기로 작정한 터라, 그 방해에도 아랑곳없이 말을 이어 갔다."]], [["\"Do you know, sir, Mrs. Osborne's condition?", "\"오스본 부인의 처지를 아십니까?"], ["Her life and her reason almost have been shaken by the blow which has fallen on her.", "그녀에게 떨어진 그 일격으로 그녀의 목숨과 정신이 거의 다 흔들리고 말았습니다."], ["It is very doubtful whether she will rally.", "그녀가 다시 기력을 차릴지조차 매우 불확실합니다."], ["There is a chance left for her, however, and it is about this I came to speak to you.", "그러나 그녀에게 한 가지 희망이 남아 있고, 바로 그 일로 당신께 말씀드리러 온 것입니다."], ["She will be a mother soon.", "그녀는 머지않아 어머니가 됩니다."], ["Will you visit the parent's offence upon the child's head?", "당신은 아비의 죄를 그 아이의 머리에 물으시렵니까?"], ["or will you forgive the child for poor George's sake?\"", "아니면 가엾은 조지를 봐서라도 그 아이를 용서하시렵니까?\""]], [["Osborne broke out into a rhapsody of self-praise and imprecations;--by the first, excusing himself to his own conscience for his conduct; by the second, exaggerating the undutifulness of George.", "오스본은 자화자찬과 저주를 한바탕 늘어놓기 시작했다. 자화자찬으로는 자기 양심에 대고 자신의 처사를 변명했고, 저주로는 조지의 불효를 부풀렸다."], ["No father in all England could have behaved more generously to a son, who had rebelled against him wickedly.", "영국 천지 어느 아비도, 그토록 사악하게 자신에게 반역한 아들에게 그보다 더 너그럽게 행동할 수는 없었으리라."], ["He had died without even so much as confessing he was wrong.", "그 아이는 자기가 잘못했다는 말 한마디조차 없이 죽었다."], ["Let him take the consequences of his undutifulness and folly.", "제 불효와 어리석음의 대가를 치르게 하라."], ["As for himself, Mr. Osborne, he was a man of his word.", "자신으로 말하자면, 오스본 씨는 한번 한 말은 지키는 사람이었다."], ["He had sworn never to speak to that woman, or to recognize her as his son's wife.", "그는 그 여자에게는 결코 말을 걸지도, 그 여자를 자기 아들의 아내로 인정하지도 않겠다고 맹세했다."], ["\"And that's what you may tell her,\"", "\"그게 당신이 그 여자에게 전할 말이오.\""], ["he concluded with an oath; \"and that's what I will stick to to the last day of my life.\"", "그가 욕설로 매듭지었다. \"그리고 그게 내가 죽는 날까지 지킬 바요.\""]], [["There was no hope from that quarter then.", "그렇다면 그쪽에서는 아무런 희망도 없었다."], ["The widow must live on her slender pittance, or on such aid as Jos could give her.", "미망인은 그 쥐꼬리만 한 푼돈으로 살아가거나, 조스가 보태 줄 수 있는 만큼의 도움에 기대어 살아가는 수밖에 없었다."], ["\"I might tell her, and she would not heed it,\"", "'그녀에게 말해 본들, 그녀는 마음에 두지도 않겠지.'"], ["thought Dobbin, sadly: for the poor girl's thoughts were not here at all since her catastrophe, and, stupefied under the pressure of her sorrow, good and evil were alike indifferent to her.", "도빈은 서글피 생각했다. 그 가엾은 아가씨의 마음은 그 참변 이후로 여기에 조금도 가 있지 않았고, 슬픔의 압박에 멍하니 마비된 채 선이든 악이든 그녀에게는 매한가지로 무심한 것이 되어 버렸기 때문이다."]], [["So, indeed, were even friendship and kindness.", "그렇다, 우정이나 친절조차도 정말이지 그러했다."], ["She received them both uncomplainingly, and having accepted them, relapsed into her grief.", "그녀는 그 두 가지를 모두 군말 없이 받아들였고, 받아들이고 나서는 다시 슬픔 속으로 가라앉았다."]], [["Suppose some twelve months after the above conversation took place to have passed in the life of our poor Amelia.", "위의 대화가 오간 뒤로 우리의 가엾은 애밀리아의 삶에 열두 달쯤이 흘렀다고 치자."], ["She has spent the first portion of that time in a sorrow so profound and pitiable, that we who have been watching and describing some of the emotions of that weak and tender heart, must draw back in the presence of the cruel grief under which it is bleeding.", "그녀는 그 첫머리를 너무도 깊고 가련한 슬픔 속에서 보냈으니, 그 연약하고 다정한 가슴의 몇몇 감정을 지켜보고 또 그려 온 우리로서도, 그 가슴이 피 흘리고 있는 그 잔혹한 비탄 앞에서는 물러서지 않을 수 없다."], ["Tread silently round the hapless couch of the poor prostrate soul.", "가엾게 쓰러진 영혼의 그 불행한 침상 둘레를 소리 없이 걸으라."], ["Shut gently the door of the dark chamber wherein she suffers, as those kind people did who nursed her through the first months of her pain, and never left her until heaven had sent her consolation.", "그 첫 몇 달의 고통을 견디도록 그녀를 간호하고, 하늘이 그녀에게 위안을 보내 줄 때까지 곁을 결코 떠나지 않았던 그 친절한 이들이 그러했듯, 그녀가 괴로워하는 어두운 방의 문을 가만히 닫으라."], ["A day came--of almost terrified delight and wonder--when the poor widowed girl pressed a child upon her breast--a child, with the eyes of George who was gone--a little boy, as beautiful as a cherub.", "어느 날—거의 두려움마저 깃든 기쁨과 경이의 날—마침내 그 가엾은 미망인 아가씨가 한 아기를 가슴에 끌어안았다. 떠나 버린 조지의 그 눈을 닮은 아기를, 천사처럼 어여쁜 사내아이를."], ["What a miracle it was to hear its first cry!", "그 첫 울음소리를 듣는 것이 얼마나 큰 기적이었던지!"], ["How she laughed and wept over it--how love, and hope, and prayer woke again in her bosom as the baby nestled there.", "그녀가 그 아이를 두고 얼마나 웃고 또 울었던지—그 아기가 가슴에 파고들 때, 사랑과 희망과 기도가 그녀의 가슴속에서 얼마나 다시 깨어났던지."], ["She was safe.", "그녀는 무사했다."], ["The doctors who attended her, and had feared for her life or for her brain, had waited anxiously for this crisis before they could pronounce that either was secure.", "그녀를 돌보며 그 목숨과 정신을 염려하던 의사들은, 둘 중 어느 쪽도 안전하다고 단언하기에 앞서 바로 이 고비를 애타게 기다려 온 터였다."], ["It was worth the long months of doubt and dread which the persons who had constantly been with her had passed, to see her eyes once more beaming tenderly upon them.", "그녀의 눈이 다시금 그들을 다정하게 바라보며 빛나는 것을 보는 일은, 줄곧 그녀 곁을 지켜 온 이들이 견뎌 낸 그 길고 긴 의혹과 공포의 여러 달과 맞바꿀 만한 가치가 있었다."]], [["Our friend Dobbin was one of them.", "우리의 벗 도빈도 그들 가운데 하나였다."], ["It was he who brought her back to England and to her mother's house; when Mrs. O'Dowd, receiving a peremptory summons from her Colonel, had been forced to quit her patient.", "오다우드 부인이 자기 대령에게서 단호한 호출을 받고 환자 곁을 부득이 떠나야 했을 때, 그녀를 영국으로, 그리고 그 어머니의 집으로 데려온 사람도 바로 그였다."], ["To see Dobbin holding the infant, and to hear Amelia's laugh of triumph as she watched him, would have done any man good who had a sense of humour.", "도빈이 아기를 안고 있는 모습과, 그것을 지켜보며 의기양양하게 웃는 애밀리아의 웃음소리는, 익살을 아는 사람이라면 누구에게나 흐뭇한 광경이었을 것이다."], ["William was the godfather of the child, and exerted his ingenuity in the purchase of cups, spoons, pap-boats, and corals for this little Christian.", "윌리엄은 그 아이의 대부가 되었고, 이 어린 그리스도교도를 위해 잔이며 숟가락이며 미음 그릇이며 산호 노리개를 사들이는 데 온 재간을 다 짜냈다."]], [["How his mother nursed him, and dressed him, and lived upon him; how she drove away all nurses, and would scarce allow any hand but her own to touch him; how she considered that the greatest favour she could confer upon his godfather, Major Dobbin, was to allow the Major occasionally to dandle him, need not be told here.", "그 어머니가 어떻게 아이를 보살피고, 옷을 입히고, 그 아이만 바라보며 살았는지, 어떻게 모든 유모를 물리치고 자기 손 말고는 어떤 손도 아이에게 닿는 것을 좀처럼 허락하지 않았는지, 또 아이의 대부인 도빈 소령에게 베풀 수 있는 가장 큰 호의란 이따금 소령이 아이를 어르도록 허락해 주는 것이라고 여겼는지는, 여기서 굳이 말할 필요가 없다."], ["This child was her being.", "이 아이가 그녀의 존재 그 자체였다."], ["Her existence was a maternal caress.", "그녀의 삶은 어머니의 애무 한 가지였다."], ["She enveloped the feeble and unconscious creature with love and worship.", "그녀는 그 가냘프고 아무것도 모르는 생명을 사랑과 경배로 감쌌다."], ["It was her life which the baby drank in from her bosom.", "아기가 그녀의 가슴에서 빨아들이는 것은 바로 그녀의 생명이었다."], ["Of nights, and when alone, she had stealthy and intense raptures of motherly love, such as God's marvellous care has awarded to the female instinct--joys how far higher and lower than reason--blind beautiful devotions which only women's hearts know.", "밤이면, 그리고 홀로 있을 때면, 그녀는 은밀하고도 격렬한 모성의 황홀경에 빠졌으니, 하느님의 그 놀라운 보살핌이 여성의 본능에 내려 준 것—이성보다 한없이 더 높고도 한없이 더 낮은 기쁨—오직 여인의 가슴만이 아는 그 눈멀고도 아름다운 헌신이었다."], ["It was William Dobbin's task to muse upon these movements of Amelia's, and to watch her heart; and if his love made him divine almost all the feelings which agitated it, alas!", "애밀리아의 이런 마음의 움직임을 곰곰이 헤아리고 그 가슴을 지켜보는 것이 윌리엄 도빈의 몫이었다. 그리고 그의 사랑이 그로 하여금 그 가슴을 뒤흔드는 거의 모든 감정을 꿰뚫어 보게 했을지언정, 아아!"], ["he could see with a fatal perspicuity that there was no place there for him.", "그는 거기에 자기가 들어설 자리가 없다는 것을 치명적인 명민함으로 똑똑히 볼 수 있었다."], ["And so, gently, he bore his fate, knowing it, and content to bear it.", "그리하여 그는 자신의 운명을 알면서도 잠자코 견뎌 냈고, 그것을 견디는 데 만족했다."]], [["I suppose Amelia's father and mother saw through the intentions of the Major, and were not ill-disposed to encourage him; for Dobbin visited their house daily, and stayed for hours with them, or with Amelia, or with the honest landlord, Mr. Clapp, and his family.", "애밀리아의 아버지와 어머니는 소령의 속내를 꿰뚫어 보고도 그를 부추기는 데 그리 박하지 않았던 듯하다."], ["He brought, on one pretext or another, presents to everybody, and almost every day; and went, with the landlord's little girl, who was rather a favourite with Amelia, by the name of Major Sugarplums.", "도빈이 날마다 그들의 집을 찾아와 그들과, 혹은 애밀리아와, 혹은 정직한 집주인 클랩 씨와 그 가족과 몇 시간씩 머물렀기 때문이다."], ["It was this little child who commonly acted as mistress of the ceremonies to introduce him to Mrs.", "그는 이런저런 핑계로 거의 날마다 모두에게 선물을 가져왔고, 애밀리아가 제법 귀여워하던 집주인의 어린 딸아이에게서 '사탕과자 소령님'이라는 별명을 얻었다."], ["Osborne. She laughed one day when Major Sugarplums'", "그를 오스본 부인에게 안내하는 의전관 노릇을 으레 도맡은 것이 바로 이 어린 아이였다."], ["cab drove up to Fulham, and he descended from it, bringing out a wooden horse, a drum, a trumpet, and other warlike toys, for little Georgy, who was scarcely six months old, and for whom the articles in question were entirely premature.", "어느 날 '사탕과자 소령님'의 마차가 풀럼으로 들어와 그가 거기서 내리며, 태어난 지 겨우 여섯 달 남짓밖에 안 된 어린 조지를 위해 나무 말이며 북이며 나팔이며 그 밖의 전쟁놀이 장난감들을 꺼내 오는 것을 보고 그녀는 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 문제의 그 물건들은 아이에게는 한참 이른 것들이었던 것이다."]], [["The child was asleep.", "아이는 잠들어 있었다."], ["\"Hush,\"", "\"쉿.\""], ["said Amelia, annoyed, perhaps, at the creaking of the Major's boots; and she held out her hand; smiling because William could not take it until he had rid himself of his cargo of toys.", "애밀리아가, 어쩌면 소령의 장화가 삐걱대는 소리에 짜증이 났는지 그렇게 말하며 손을 내밀었다. 윌리엄이 한 짐 가득한 장난감을 내려놓기 전에는 그 손을 잡을 수 없었으므로 그녀는 미소 지었다."], ["\"Go downstairs, little Mary,\"", "\"아래층으로 내려가렴, 메리야.\""], ["said he presently to the child, \"I want to speak to Mrs.", "그가 이내 아이에게 말했다. \"오스본 부인과 할 이야기가 있단다.\""], ["Osborne.\" She looked up rather astonished, and laid down the infant on its bed.", "아이는 다소 놀란 듯 그를 올려다보고는 아기를 침대에 내려놓았다."]], [["\"I am come to say good-bye, Amelia,\"", "\"작별 인사를 하러 왔습니다, 애밀리아.\""], ["said he, taking her slender little white hand gently.", "그가 그녀의 가냘프고 자그마한 하얀 손을 가만히 잡으며 말했다."]], [["\"Good-bye?", "\"작별이라고요?"], ["and where are you going?\"", "어디로 가시는데요?\""], ["she said, with a smile.", "그녀가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["\"Send the letters to the agents,\"", "\"편지는 대리인들에게 보내세요.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said; \"they will forward them; for you will write to me, won't you?", "\"그들이 전달해 줄 겁니다. 편지해 주실 거지요?"], ["I shall be away a long time.\"", "한참 동안 떠나 있을 테니까요.\""]], [["\"I'll write to you about Georgy,\"", "\"조지에 관한 소식을 편지로 전할게요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"Dear William, how good you have been to him and to me.", "\"고마운 윌리엄, 이 아이한테도 저한테도 어쩌면 그렇게 잘해 주셨는지요."], ["Look at him.", "이 아이 좀 보세요."], ["Isn't he like an angel?\"", "꼭 천사 같지 않아요?\""]], [["The little pink hands of the child closed mechanically round the honest soldier's finger, and Amelia looked up in his face with bright maternal pleasure.", "아이의 작은 분홍빛 손이 기계적으로 그 정직한 군인의 손가락을 감싸 쥐었고, 애밀리아는 환한 모성의 기쁨에 차 그의 얼굴을 올려다보았다."], ["The cruellest looks could not have wounded him more than that glance of hopeless kindness.", "그 어떤 모진 눈빛도, 희망 없는 친절이 담긴 그 눈길보다 더 그를 아프게 할 수는 없었으리라."], ["He bent over the child and mother.", "그는 아이와 어머니 위로 몸을 숙였다."], ["He could not speak for a moment.", "한순간 그는 말을 할 수가 없었다."], ["And it was only with all his strength that he could force himself to say a God bless you.", "그리고 온 힘을 다 짜내고서야 겨우 \"신의 가호가 있기를\"이라는 말을 억지로 내뱉을 수 있었다."], ["\"God bless you,\"", "\"신의 가호가 있기를.\""], ["said Amelia, and held up her face and kissed him.", "애밀리아가 그렇게 말하며 얼굴을 들어 그에게 입을 맞추었다."]], [["\"Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["Don't wake Georgy!\"", "조지를 깨우지 마세요!\""], ["she added, as William Dobbin went to the door with heavy steps.", "윌리엄 도빈이 무거운 걸음으로 문을 향해 갈 때, 그녀가 덧붙였다."], ["She did not hear the noise of his cab-wheels as he drove away: she was looking at the child, who was laughing in his sleep.", "그녀는 그가 마차를 몰고 떠나며 내는 바퀴 소리를 듣지 못했다. 그녀는 잠결에 웃고 있는 아이를 바라보고 있었던 것이다."]], [["How to Live Well on Nothing a Year\nI suppose there is no man in this Vanity Fair of ours so little observant as not to think sometimes about the worldly affairs of his acquaintances, or so extremely charitable as not to wonder how his neighbour Jones, or his neighbour Smith, can make both ends meet at the end of the year.", "무릇 우리네 이 허영의 시장에 사는 사람치고, 이따금 자기 지인들의 살림 형편을 헤아려 보지 않을 만큼 무심한 이도 없을 것이고, 옆집 사는 존스나 또 옆집 사는 스미스가 어떻게 연말에 수지를 맞추는지 궁금해하지 않을 만큼 한없이 너그러운 이도 없을 것이라 짐작한다. 일례로 나는 젱킨스 일가를 더없이 존중하면서도(한 철에 두세 번은 그 집에서 만찬을 드는 처지이니), 죽는 날까지 한 가지만은 솔직히 인정하지 않을 수 없다."], ["With the utmost regard for the family, for instance (for I dine with them twice or thrice in the season), I cannot but own that the appearance of the Jenkinses in the park, in the large barouche with the grenadier-footmen, will surprise and mystify me to my dying day: for though I know the equipage is only jobbed, and all the Jenkins people are on board wages, yet those three men and the carriage must represent an expense of six hundred a year at the very least--and then there are the splendid dinners, the two boys at Eton, the prize governess and masters for the girls, the trip abroad, or to Eastbourne or Worthing, in the autumn, the annual ball with a supper from Gunter's (who, by the way, supplies most of the first-rate dinners which J.", "척탄병처럼 늠름한 하인들이 딸린 커다란 사륜마차를 타고 공원에 나타나는 젱킨스 일가의 모습은 두고두고 나를 놀라게 하고 어리둥절하게 만들 것이라는 사실 말이다. 그 마차가 그저 세를 낸 것이고 젱킨스네 사람들이 죄다 식사를 곁들인 급료로 부려지는 처지임을 안다 해도, 그 세 사내와 마차를 유지하자면 적어도 일 년에 육백 파운드는 들 것임에 틀림없다."], ["gives, as I know very well, having been invited to one of them to fill a vacant place, when I saw at once that these repasts are very superior to the common run of entertainments for which the humbler sort of J.'s acquaintances get cards)--who, I say, with the most good-natured feelings in the world, can help wondering how the Jenkinses make out matters?", "게다가 그 호화로운 만찬이며, 이튼에 다니는 두 아들이며, 딸들에게 붙인 일류 가정교사와 선생들이며, 가을이면 떠나는 외국 여행이나 이스트본 또는 워딩으로의 나들이며, 건터 가게에서 야식까지 대 가며 해마다 여는 무도회까지 있는 것이다(여담이지만 J.가 베푸는 일류 만찬은 대개 이 건터 가게에서 대는 것임을 나는 익히 알고 있다. 한번은 빈자리를 채우라고 그런 만찬에 초대받은 적이 있었는데, 그 자리에서 단번에 알아챘던 것이다."], ["What is Jenkins?", "이런 진수성찬은, J.의 지인들 가운데 좀 더 변변찮은 축들이 초대장을 받는 흔해 빠진 연회와는 격이 사뭇 다르다는 것을)."], ["We all know--Commissioner of the Tape and Sealing Wax Office, with 1200 pounds a year for a salary.", "그러니 세상에 둘도 없이 선량한 마음을 지닌 사람이라 한들, 젱킨스 일가가 대체 어떻게 살림을 꾸려 가는지 궁금해하지 않을 도리가 어디 있겠는가? 젱킨스가 무엇 하는 사람인가?"], ["Had his wife a private fortune?", "우리 모두가 알다시피, 봉인용 끈과 봉랍을 관장하는 관청의 위원으로, 연봉이 천이백 파운드인 사람이다."], ["Pooh!--Miss Flint--one of eleven children of a small squire in Buckinghamshire.", "그 아내에게 따로 재산이라도 있었던가? 천만에!"], ["All she ever gets from her family is a turkey at Christmas, in exchange for which she has to board two or three of her sisters in the off season, and lodge and feed her brothers when they come to town.", "그녀는 플린트 양이었으니, 버킹엄셔의 자그마한 시골 지주의 열한 자녀 가운데 하나였다. 친정에서 받는 것이라고는 크리스마스 칠면조 한 마리가 고작이건만, 그 대가로 그녀는 한가한 철이면 제 자매 두셋을 거두어 먹여야 하고, 형제들이 상경할 때면 재워 주고 먹여 주어야 하는 것이다."], ["How does Jenkins balance his income?", "젱킨스가 어떻게 수입의 균형을 맞춘단 말인가?"], ["I say, as every friend of his must say, How is it that he has not been outlawed long since, and that he ever came back (as he did to the surprise of everybody) last year from Boulogne?", "그의 벗이라면 누구나 그러하듯, 나도 묻지 않을 수 없다. 어찌하여 그는 진작 파산자로 추방당하지 않았으며, 어떻게 작년에(모두를 놀라게 하며 그랬듯이) 불로뉴에서 돌아올 수 있었단 말인가?"]], [["\"I\" is here introduced to personify the world in general--the Mrs.", "여기서 \"나\"라는 것은 세상 일반을, 곧 존경하는 독자 여러분 각자가 속한 사교계의 그 그런디 부인을 의인화한 것이니, 독자 모두가 자기 지인들 가운데 무슨 수로 사는지 아무도 모르는 집안 몇몇을 손꼽을 수 있을 것이다."], ["Grundy of each respected reader's private circle--every one of whom can point to some families of his acquaintance who live nobody knows how. Many a glass of wine have we all of us drunk, I have very little doubt, hob-and-nobbing with the hospitable giver and wondering how the deuce he paid for it.", "우리 모두가 후하게 술을 권하는 주인과 잔을 부딪치며 술을 들이켜면서도, 대체 무슨 돈으로 이걸 다 댔을까 의아해한 적이 한두 번이 아니었음을 나는 거의 의심치 않는다."]], [["Some three or four years after his stay in Paris, when Rawdon Crawley and his wife were established in a very small comfortable house in Curzon Street, May Fair, there was scarcely one of the numerous friends whom they entertained at dinner that did not ask the above question regarding them. The novelist, it has been said before, knows everything, and as I am in a situation to be able to tell the public how Crawley and his wife lived without any income, may I entreat the public newspapers which are in the habit of extracting portions of the various periodical works now published not to reprint the following exact narrative and calculations--of which I ought, as the discoverer (and at some expense, too), to have the benefit? My son, I would say, were I blessed with a child--you may by deep inquiry and constant intercourse with him learn how a man lives comfortably on nothing a year. But it is best not to be intimate with gentlemen of this profession and to take the calculations at second hand, as you do logarithms, for to work them yourself, depend upon it, will cost you something considerable.", "파리에 머물던 때로부터 서너 해가 지나, 로던 크롤리와 그의 아내가 메이페어의 커즌 거리에 자리한 아주 자그마하면서도 안락한 집에 자리 잡았을 무렵, 그들이 만찬에 청한 그 숱한 벗들 가운데 그들을 두고 앞서와 같은 의문을 품지 않은 이는 거의 없었다. 앞서도 말했듯이 소설가는 모든 것을 알고 있는 법이거니와, 나는 크롤리 부부가 한 푼의 수입도 없이 어떻게 살았는지를 세상에 알려 줄 수 있는 처지에 있다. 그러니 요즘 발간되는 갖가지 정기 간행물에서 글의 일부를 발췌해 싣곤 하는 공공 신문들에 한 가지 간청을 해도 될는지. 부디 다음의 정확한 서술과 계산을 베껴 싣지 말아 달라는 것이다. 발견자인 나로서는(그것도 적잖은 비용을 들여 알아낸 것이니) 마땅히 그 덕을 보아야 할 것 아닌가? 나에게 자식 복이 있어 아이가 하나 있다면, 나는 이렇게 말하리라. 아들아, 이런 부류의 사람과 깊이 캐묻고 끊임없이 어울려 지내다 보면, 한 사람이 한 푼의 수입도 없이 어떻게 안락하게 살아가는지를 배울 수 있을 게다. 허나 이런 직업을 가진 신사들과는 가까이 지내지 않는 편이 상책이요, 그 계산법은 로그표를 쓰듯 남이 해 놓은 것을 받아 쓰는 편이 낫다. 직접 풀어 보려 들다가는 분명 적잖은 대가를 치르게 될 테니 말이다."]], [["On nothing per annum then, and during a course of some two or three years, of which we can afford to give but a very brief history, Crawley and his wife lived very happily and comfortably at Paris.", "그리하여 한 푼의 연 수입도 없이, 두세 해에 걸쳐 크롤리 부부는 파리에서 더없이 행복하고 안락하게 살았다. 그 사연은 아주 간략하게밖에 전할 수 없지만 말이다."], ["It was in this period that he quitted the Guards and sold out of the army.", "그가 근위대를 그만두고 군적을 판 것도 이 무렵이었다."], ["When we find him again, his mustachios and the title of Colonel on his card are the only relics of his military profession.", "다시 그를 만나게 될 즈음에는, 콧수염과 명함에 박힌 대령이라는 직함만이 그의 군인 시절을 말해 주는 유일한 흔적으로 남게 된다."]], [["It has been mentioned that Rebecca, soon after her arrival in Paris, took a very smart and leading position in the society of that capital, and was welcomed at some of the most distinguished houses of the restored French nobility.", "레베카는 파리에 도착하고 얼마 안 되어 그 수도의 사교계에서 아주 멋스럽고 앞서가는 자리를 차지했으며, 왕정복고로 되돌아온 프랑스 귀족들의 가장 이름난 저택 몇몇에서 환대받았다는 것은 앞서 언급한 바 있다."], ["The English men of fashion in Paris courted her, too, to the disgust of the ladies their wives, who could not bear the parvenue.", "파리에 있던 영국의 멋쟁이 신사들 또한 그녀에게 추파를 보냈으니, 이는 그 아내인 숙녀들의 비위를 거스르는 일이었다."], ["For some months the salons of the Faubourg St.", "그들은 이 벼락출세한 여자를 견디지 못했던 것이다."], ["Germain, in which her place was secured, and the splendours of the new Court, where she was received with much distinction, delighted and perhaps a little intoxicated Mrs.", "몇 달 동안 포부르 생제르맹의 살롱들에서 그녀의 자리는 굳건했고, 그녀가 융숭한 대접을 받았던 새 궁정의 화려함은 크롤리 부인을 기쁘게 했으며, 어쩌면 다소 도취하게 만들었는지도 모른다."], ["Crawley, who may have been disposed during this period of elation to slight the people--honest young military men mostly--who formed her husband's chief society.", "이 의기양양한 시절 동안 그녀는, 남편의 주된 교제 상대인 사람들, 대개는 정직한 젊은 군인들을 업신여기는 마음을 품었을 법도 했다."]], [["But the Colonel yawned sadly among the Duchesses and great ladies of the Court.", "그러나 대령은 궁정의 공작부인들과 귀부인들 틈에서 서글프게 하품만 해 댔다."], ["The old women who played ecarte made such a noise about a five-franc piece that it was not worth Colonel Crawley's while to sit down at a card-table.", "에카르테 노름을 하는 노부인들은 오 프랑짜리 동전 한 닢을 두고도 어찌나 시끄럽게 떠들어 대는지, 크롤리 대령으로서는 카드 탁자에 앉을 만한 가치도 없었다."], ["The wit of their conversation he could not appreciate, being ignorant of their language.", "그들 대화의 재치는 그 언어를 모르는 그로서는 음미할 도리가 없었다."], ["And what good could his wife get, he urged, by making curtsies every night to a whole circle of Princesses?", "게다가 아내가 밤마다 공주님들이 빙 둘러앉은 자리에서 무릎을 굽혀 절을 해 댄들 무슨 좋은 일이 생기겠느냐고 그는 따져 물었다."], ["He left Rebecca presently to frequent these parties alone, resuming his own simple pursuits and amusements amongst the amiable friends of his own choice.", "그는 이내 레베카가 이런 모임에 혼자 드나들도록 내버려 두고는, 제 손으로 고른 마음 맞는 벗들 틈에서 자기만의 소박한 소일거리와 오락을 다시 즐기기 시작했다."]], [["The truth is, when we say of a gentleman that he lives elegantly on nothing a year, we use the word \"nothing\"", "사실인즉, 한 신사가 한 푼의 연 수입도 없이 우아하게 산다고 말할 때, 우리는 그 \"한 푼도 없다\"는 말을 무언가 알 수 없는 것을 가리키는 뜻으로 쓰는 것이다."], ["to signify something unknown; meaning, simply, that we don't know how the gentleman in question defrays the expenses of his establishment.", "다시 말해, 문제의 그 신사가 어떻게 살림 비용을 대는지 우리가 모른다는 뜻일 따름이다."], ["Now, our friend the Colonel had a great aptitude for all games of chance: and exercising himself, as he continually did, with the cards, the dice-box, or the cue, it is natural to suppose that he attained a much greater skill in the use of these articles than men can possess who only occasionally handle them.", "그런데 우리의 벗인 대령은 온갖 도박에 대단한 소질이 있었다. 늘 그러했듯 카드며 주사위 통이며 당구 큐를 끊임없이 놀려 단련한 터이니, 어쩌다 한 번씩만 이런 물건들을 다루는 사람들보다 훨씬 뛰어난 솜씨를 익혔으리라 짐작하는 것은 당연한 일이다."], ["To use a cue at billiards well is like using a pencil, or a German flute, or a small-sword--you cannot master any one of these implements at first, and it is only by repeated study and perseverance, joined to a natural taste, that a man can excel in the handling of either.", "당구에서 큐를 능란하게 다루는 것은 연필이나 독일제 플루트나 세검을 다루는 것과 같다. 이런 도구는 무엇 하나 처음부터 통달할 수 있는 것이 아니어서, 타고난 소질에다 거듭된 연마와 끈기가 보태져야만 비로소 어느 것이든 뛰어나게 다룰 수 있는 것이다."], ["Now Crawley, from being only a brilliant amateur, had grown to be a consummate master of billiards.", "그런데 크롤리는 한낱 빼어난 아마추어에서 출발하여 마침내 당구의 완벽한 명수가 되었다."], ["Like a great General, his genius used to rise with the danger, and when the luck had been unfavourable to him for a whole game, and the bets were consequently against him, he would, with consummate skill and boldness, make some prodigious hits which would restore the battle, and come in a victor at the end, to the astonishment of everybody--of everybody, that is, who was a stranger to his play.", "위대한 장군이 그러하듯 그의 천재성은 위기가 닥칠수록 솟구쳤으니, 한 판 내내 운이 따라 주지 않아 판돈이 불리하게 걸린 상황에서도 더없이 완숙한 솜씨와 대담함으로 놀라운 한 방을 날려 전세를 뒤집고는, 끝내 모두를 경악케 하며 승자로 마무리하곤 했다. 그의 노름 솜씨를 모르는 사람이라면 누구나 경악했다는 말이다."], ["Those who were accustomed to see it were cautious how they staked their money against a man of such sudden resources and brilliant and overpowering skill.", "그것을 익히 보아 온 이들은 그처럼 느닷없는 임기응변과 눈부시고 압도적인 솜씨를 지닌 사내를 상대로 돈을 거는 데 신중을 기했다."]], [["At games of cards he was equally skilful; for though he would constantly lose money at the commencement of an evening, playing so carelessly and making such blunders, that newcomers were often inclined to think meanly of his talent; yet when roused to action and awakened to caution by repeated small losses, it was remarked that Crawley's play became quite different, and that he was pretty sure of beating his enemy thoroughly before the night was over.", "카드 노름에서도 그는 그에 못지않은 솜씨를 발휘했다. 저녁나절 처음에는 어찌나 무심하게 노름을 하고 또 어찌나 실수를 연발하는지 줄곧 돈을 잃기 일쑤여서, 처음 보는 이들은 흔히 그의 재주를 얕잡아 보기 십상이었다."], ["Indeed, very few men could say that they ever had the better of him.", "그러나 자잘한 손실을 거듭 보다 못해 정신을 차리고 분발하게 되면, 크롤리의 노름 솜씨는 사뭇 달라져서 밤이 다 가기 전에 상대를 보기 좋게 이겨 먹는 것이 거의 틀림없는 일이라고들 했다."], ["His successes were so repeated that no wonder the envious and the vanquished spoke sometimes with bitterness regarding them.", "정말이지 그를 이겨 본 적이 있다고 말할 수 있는 사람은 극히 드물었다."], ["And as the French say of the Duke of Wellington, who never suffered a defeat, that only an astonishing series of lucky accidents enabled him to be an invariable winner; yet even they allow that he cheated at Waterloo, and was enabled to win the last great trick: so it was hinted at headquarters in England that some foul play must have taken place in order to account for the continuous successes of Colonel Crawley.", "그의 승전이 하도 거듭되니, 시샘하는 자들과 패한 자들이 이따금 그 일을 두고 신랄하게 입을 놀린 것도 무리는 아니었다. 프랑스인들이 단 한 번도 패배한 적 없는 웰링턴 공작을 두고, 그저 놀라우리만치 잇따른 행운의 우연 덕분에 한결같이 승자가 될 수 있었을 뿐이라 말하면서도 워털루에서는 속임수를 써서 마지막 큰 판을 거머쥘 수 있었던 것이라고 인정하듯이, 영국의 군 수뇌부에서도 크롤리 대령이 줄곧 이긴 까닭을 설명하려면 필시 어떤 부정한 짓이 있었을 거라는 수군거림이 일었다."]], [["Though Frascati's and the Salon were open at that time in Paris, the mania for play was so widely spread that the public gambling-rooms did not suffice for the general ardour, and gambling went on in private houses as much as if there had been no public means for gratifying the passion.", "당시 파리에는 프라스카티며 살롱 같은 도박장이 열려 있었지만, 노름에 대한 광기가 어찌나 널리 퍼졌는지 공공 도박장만으로는 그 보편적인 열기를 다 감당할 수가 없어, 마치 그 열정을 채울 공공의 수단이 아예 없기라도 한 양 개인 저택에서도 노름판이 벌어졌다."], ["At Crawley's charming little reunions of an evening this fatal amusement commonly was practised--much to good-natured little Mrs. Crawley's annoyance.", "크롤리네 저녁의 그 멋들어진 모임에서도 이 치명적인 오락이 으레 행해졌으니, 이는 마음씨 고운 가냘픈 크롤리 부인을 적잖이 속상하게 하는 일이었다."], ["She spoke about her husband's passion for dice with the deepest grief; she bewailed it to everybody who came to her house.", "그녀는 더없이 깊은 슬픔에 잠겨 남편의 노름벽을 입에 올렸으며, 자기 집을 찾는 이마다 붙잡고 그 일을 한탄했다."], ["She besought the young fellows never, never to touch a box; and when young Green, of the Rifles, lost a very considerable sum of money, Rebecca passed a whole night in tears, as the servant told the unfortunate young gentleman, and actually went on her knees to her husband to beseech him to remit the debt, and burn the acknowledgement.", "그녀는 젊은 친구들에게 절대로, 절대로 주사위 통에 손대지 말라고 간청했다. 소총대의 젊은 그린이 상당한 액수의 돈을 잃었을 때는, 하인이 그 가엾은 젊은 신사에게 전한 바에 따르면, 레베카가 밤새도록 눈물로 지새우고는 남편 앞에 무릎까지 꿇고서 그 빚을 탕감해 주고 차용증을 태워 버리라고 애원했다는 것이다."], ["How could he?", "어찌 그럴 수 있었겠는가?"], ["He had lost just as much himself to Blackstone of the Hussars, and Count Punter of the Hanoverian Cavalry.", "그 자신도 경기병대의 블랙스톤과 하노버 기병대의 푼터 백작에게 그만한 돈을 잃은 터였다."], ["Green might have any decent time; but pay?--of course he must pay; to talk of burning IOU's was child's play.", "그린에게 갚을 시간이야 얼마든지 줄 수 있지만, 갚는 것 자체야 물론 갚아야지. 차용증을 태운다는 둥 하는 소리는 애들 장난 같은 소리였다."]], [["Other officers, chiefly young--for the young fellows gathered round Mrs. Crawley--came from her parties with long faces, having dropped more or less money at her fatal card-tables.", "다른 장교들, 주로 젊은 축들도(젊은 친구들이 크롤리 부인 둘레로 몰려들었으니) 그녀의 그 치명적인 카드 탁자에서 적든 많든 돈을 떨군 채 울상이 되어 그 집 모임을 나서곤 했다."], ["Her house began to have an unfortunate reputation.", "그녀의 집은 좋지 못한 평판을 얻기 시작했다."], ["The old hands warned the less experienced of their danger.", "노련한 자들은 풋내기들에게 그 위험을 일러 주었다."], ["Colonel O'Dowd, of the --th regiment, one of those occupying in Paris, warned Lieutenant Spooney of that corps.", "파리에 주둔한 부대 가운데 하나인 제 모 연대의 오다우드 대령은 그 부대의 스푸니 중위에게 경고를 했다."], ["A loud and violent fracas took place between the infantry Colonel and his lady, who were dining at the Cafe de Paris, and Colonel and Mrs. Crawley; who were also taking their meal there.", "파리 카페에서 식사를 하던 그 보병 대령 부부와, 역시 그곳에서 끼니를 들고 있던 크롤리 대령 부부 사이에 요란하고 격렬한 한판 소동이 벌어졌다."], ["The ladies engaged on both sides. Mrs.", "양편의 부인들이 가세했다."], ["O'Dowd snapped her fingers in Mrs. Crawley's face and called her husband \"no betther than a black-leg.\"", "오다우드 부인은 크롤리 부인의 얼굴에 대고 손가락을 튕기며 그 남편을 \"사기 도박꾼이나 다름없는 자\"라고 불렀다."], ["Colonel Crawley challenged Colonel O'Dowd, C.B.", "크롤리 대령은 무공훈장 수훈자인 오다우드 대령에게 결투를 신청했다."], ["The Commander-in-Chief hearing of the dispute sent for Colonel Crawley, who was getting ready the same pistols \"which he shot Captain Marker,\"", "그 분쟁을 전해 들은 총사령관은 크롤리 대령을 불러들였는데, 마침 대령은 \"마커 대위를 쏘았던\" 바로 그 권총을 챙기던 참이었다."], ["and had such a conversation with him that no duel took place.", "사령관이 그와 어떤 이야기를 나눈 끝에 결투는 끝내 벌어지지 않았다."], ["If Rebecca had not gone on her knees to General Tufto, Crawley would have been sent back to England; and he did not play, except with civilians, for some weeks after.", "레베카가 터프토 장군 앞에 무릎 꿇고 매달리지 않았던들 크롤리는 영국으로 송환되었을 것이며, 그 뒤로 몇 주 동안 그는 민간인을 상대로 하는 노름밖에는 하지 않았다."]], [["But, in spite of Rawdon's undoubted skill and constant successes, it became evident to Rebecca, considering these things, that their position was but a precarious one, and that, even although they paid scarcely anybody, their little capital would end one day by dwindling into zero.", "그러나 로던의 의심할 바 없는 솜씨와 끊임없는 승전에도 불구하고, 이런저런 사정을 헤아려 본 레베카에게는 그들의 처지가 위태롭기 짝이 없으며, 갚을 사람에게 거의 한 푼도 갚지 않는다 해도 그들의 보잘것없는 밑천이 언젠가는 결국 영으로 쪼그라들고 말 것임이 분명해졌다. 그녀는 이렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["\"Gambling,\" she would say, \"dear, is good to help your income, but not as an income itself.", "\"여보, 노름은 수입을 거드는 데는 좋지만, 그 자체를 수입으로 삼을 수는 없는 거예요."], ["Some day people may be tired of play, and then where are we?\"", "언젠가 사람들이 노름에 싫증을 내면, 그땐 우리 신세가 어찌 되겠어요?\""], ["Rawdon acquiesced in the justice of her opinion; and in truth he had remarked that after a few nights of his little suppers, &c., gentlemen were tired of play with him, and, in spite of Rebecca's charms, did not present themselves very eagerly.", "로던도 그 말이 옳다고 수긍했다. 사실 그도, 자기가 베푸는 그 자그마한 야식 모임 따위가 며칠 밤 거듭되고 나면 신사들이 자기와 노름하는 데 싫증을 내고, 레베카의 매력에도 불구하고 그리 신통하게 모여들지 않는다는 것을 눈치챈 터였다."]], [["Easy and pleasant as their life at Paris was, it was after all only an idle dalliance and amiable trifling; and Rebecca saw that she must push Rawdon's fortune in their own country.", "파리에서의 그들의 삶이 아무리 수월하고 즐거웠다 한들, 그것은 결국 한가로운 희롱이요 사근사근한 시간 죽이기에 지나지 않았다. 레베카는 자기네 나라에서 로던의 출셋길을 밀어주어야겠다고 깨달았다."], ["She must get him a place or appointment at home or in the colonies, and she determined to make a move upon England as soon as the way could be cleared for her.", "본국에서든 식민지에서든 그에게 자리나 직책 하나를 얻어 주어야 했기에, 길이 트이는 대로 곧장 영국으로 나아가기로 마음먹었다."], ["As a first step she had made Crawley sell out of the Guards and go on half-pay.", "그 첫걸음으로 그녀는 크롤리에게 근위대 군적을 팔고 반액 봉급 신세가 되게 했다."], ["His function as aide-de-camp to General Tufto had ceased previously.", "터프토 장군의 부관 직무는 그보다 앞서 끝난 터였다."], ["Rebecca laughed in all companies at that officer, at his toupee (which he mounted on coming to Paris), at his waistband, at his false teeth, at his pretensions to be a lady-killer above all, and his absurd vanity in fancying every woman whom he came near was in love with him.", "레베카는 어느 자리에서나 그 장교를 두고 웃음거리로 삼았으니, 그가 파리에 오면서 얹은 가발이며, 허리띠며, 틀니며, 무엇보다 여자 깨나 후린다는 그 허세며, 가까이 가는 여자마다 죄다 자기에게 반했다고 믿는 그 우스꽝스러운 허영심을 비웃었다. 이제 장군이 관심을 옮긴 상대는 군수품 담당관 브렌트 씨의, 눈썹이 짙은 아내 브렌트 부인이었다."], ["It was to Mrs. Brent, the beetle-browed wife of Mr.", "꽃다발이며 음식점 만찬이며 오페라 관람석이며 자질구레한 장신구가 죄다 그리로 갔다."], ["Commissary Brent, to whom the general transferred his attentions now--his bouquets, his dinners at the restaurateurs', his opera-boxes, and his knick-knacks. Poor Mrs.", "가엾은 터프토 부인은 예전만큼도 행복하지 못해서, 여전히 긴긴 저녁을 딸들과 단둘이 보내야 했다."], ["Tufto was no more happy than before, and had still to pass long evenings alone with her daughters, knowing that her General was gone off scented and curled to stand behind Mrs.", "제 장군이 향수 뿌리고 머리 지지고는 극장에서 브렌트 부인의 의자 뒤에 서 있으려 나갔다는 것을 빤히 알면서 말이다. 물론 베키에게는 그를 대신할 흠모자가 여남은 명이나 있었고, 재치로 연적을 갈가리 난도질할 수도 있었다."], ["Brent's chair at the play. Becky had a dozen admirers in his place, to be sure, and could cut her rival to pieces with her wit.", "그러나 앞서 말했듯이 그녀는 이 한가로운 사교 생활에 점점 싫증이 나고 있었다."], ["But, as we have said, she was growing tired of this idle social life: opera-boxes and restaurateur dinners palled upon her: nosegays could not be laid by as a provision for future years: and she could not live upon knick-knacks, laced handkerchiefs, and kid gloves.", "오페라 관람석이며 음식점 만찬에 물려 버린 것이다. 꽃다발은 훗날을 위한 밑천으로 갈무리해 둘 수 있는 것이 아니었고, 자질구레한 장신구며 레이스 손수건이며 새끼 염소 가죽 장갑만으로 먹고살 수도 없는 노릇이었다."], ["She felt the frivolity of pleasure and longed for more substantial benefits.", "그녀는 쾌락의 덧없음을 절감하며 좀 더 알찬 실속을 갈망했다."]], [["At this juncture news arrived which was spread among the many creditors of the Colonel at Paris, and which caused them great satisfaction.", "이런 고비에 한 가지 소식이 날아들어 파리에 있는 대령의 숱한 채권자들 사이에 퍼졌으니, 그들에게는 더없이 흡족한 소식이었다."], ["Miss Crawley, the rich aunt from whom he expected his immense inheritance, was dying; the Colonel must haste to her bedside.", "그가 그 막대한 유산을 물려받으리라 기대하던 부유한 고모 크롤리 양이 죽어 가고 있었던 것이다. 대령은 서둘러 그 머리맡으로 달려가야 했다."], ["Mrs. Crawley and her child would remain behind until he came to reclaim them.", "크롤리 부인과 아이는 그가 와서 다시 데려갈 때까지 뒤에 남기로 했다."], ["He departed for Calais, and having reached that place in safety, it might have been supposed that he went to Dover; but instead he took the diligence to Dunkirk, and thence travelled to Brussels, for which place he had a former predilection.", "그는 칼레로 떠났고, 무사히 그곳에 닿았으니 응당 도버로 건너갔으리라 짐작할 만했다. 그러나 그는 도버 대신 됭케르크행 역마차를 잡아탔고, 거기서 브뤼셀로 갔다."], ["The fact is, he owed more money at London than at Paris; and he preferred the quiet little Belgian city to either of the more noisy capitals.", "전부터 마음이 끌리던 곳이었다. 실은 그는 파리보다 런던에 더 많은 빚을 지고 있었던 데다, 그 두 시끄러운 수도보다 조용하고 자그마한 그 벨기에 도시를 더 좋아했던 것이다."]], [["Her aunt was dead. Mrs.", "고모는 세상을 떠났다."], ["Crawley ordered the most intense mourning for herself and little Rawdon.", "크롤리 부인은 자기와 어린 로던을 위해 더없이 짙은 상복을 맞추었다."], ["The Colonel was busy arranging the affairs of the inheritance.", "대령은 유산을 정리하는 일로 분주했다."], ["They could take the premier now, instead of the little entresol of the hotel which they occupied.", "이제 그들은 묵고 있던 호텔의 자그마한 중이층 대신 가장 좋은 층을 쓸 수 있게 되었다."], ["Mrs. Crawley and the landlord had a consultation about the new hangings, an amicable wrangle about the carpets, and a final adjustment of everything except the bill.", "크롤리 부인은 새 커튼을 두고 주인과 상의를 했고, 양탄자를 두고 다정하게 옥신각신했으며, 청구서만 빼고 모든 것을 마침내 매듭지었다."], ["She went off in one of his carriages; her French bonne with her; the child by her side; the admirable landlord and landlady smiling farewell to her from the gate.", "그녀는 주인의 마차 한 대를 얻어 타고 떠났다. 프랑스인 하녀를 데리고, 아이는 곁에 앉히고서."], ["General Tufto was furious when he heard she was gone, and Mrs.", "더없이 훌륭한 주인 내외는 대문에서 그녀에게 미소 지으며 작별을 고했다."], ["Brent furious with him for being furious; Lieutenant Spooney was cut to the heart; and the landlord got ready his best apartments previous to the return of the fascinating little woman and her husband.", "그녀가 떠났다는 소식을 듣고 터프토 장군은 노발대발했으며, 브렌트 부인은 그가 노발대발한다 하여 그에게 노발대발했다. 스푸니 중위는 가슴이 찢어지는 듯했고, 호텔 주인은 그 매혹적인 가냘픈 여인과 그 남편이 돌아올 것에 대비해 가장 좋은 방을 준비해 두었다."], ["He serréd the trunks which she left in his charge with the greatest care.", "그는 그녀가 맡기고 간 트렁크들을 더없이 정성스레 간수했다."], ["They had been especially recommended to him by Madame Crawley.", "크롤리 부인이 그에게 각별히 잘 보아 달라 당부한 것들이었다."], ["They were not, however, found to be particularly valuable when opened some time after.", "그러나 얼마 뒤 열어 보니, 그것들은 딱히 값나가는 물건이 아닌 것으로 드러났다."]], [["But before she went to join her husband in the Belgic capital, Mrs. Crawley made an expedition into England, leaving behind her her little son upon the continent, under the care of her French maid.", "그러나 벨기에 수도로 남편을 만나러 가기에 앞서, 크롤리 부인은 어린 아들을 프랑스인 하녀에게 맡겨 대륙에 남겨 둔 채 영국으로 한 차례 길을 떠났다."]], [["The parting between Rebecca and the little Rawdon did not cause either party much pain.", "레베카와 어린 로던의 이별은 어느 쪽에도 그리 큰 아픔을 안기지 않았다."], ["She had not, to say truth, seen much of the young gentleman since his birth.", "사실대로 말하자면 그녀는 이 어린 신사가 태어난 뒤로 그를 자주 본 적이 없었다."], ["After the amiable fashion of French mothers, she had placed him out at nurse in a village in the neighbourhood of Paris, where little Rawdon passed the first months of his life, not unhappily, with a numerous family of foster-brothers in wooden shoes.", "프랑스 어머니들의 그 사근사근한 방식을 좇아, 그녀는 아이를 파리 인근 마을의 유모에게 맡겨 길렀던 것이다. 어린 로던은 거기서 나막신을 신은 숱한 젖형제들 틈에 끼여 생애 첫 몇 달을 그런대로 불행하지 않게 보냈다."], ["His father would ride over many a time to see him here, and the elder Rawdon's paternal heart glowed to see him rosy and dirty, shouting lustily, and happy in the making of mud-pies under the superintendence of the gardener's wife, his nurse.", "아버지는 여러 번 말을 달려 아이를 보러 왔는데, 아이가 발그레하고 꾀죄죄한 얼굴로 우렁차게 소리를 질러 대며 유모인 정원사 아내의 보살핌 아래 진흙 떡 빚기에 신이 난 모습을 보면, 아버지 로던의 부정 어린 가슴은 따뜻하게 달아올랐다."]], [["Rebecca did not care much to go and see the son and heir.", "레베카는 그 아들이자 후계자를 보러 가는 일에 그다지 마음을 쓰지 않았다."], ["Once he spoiled a new dove-coloured pelisse of hers.", "한번은 아이가 그녀의 새 비둘기색 외투를 망가뜨린 적이 있었다."], ["He preferred his nurse's caresses to his mamma's, and when finally he quitted that jolly nurse and almost parent, he cried loudly for hours.", "아이는 엄마의 손길보다 유모의 손길을 더 좋아했으니, 마침내 그 정 많은 유모이자 거의 부모나 다름없던 이의 곁을 떠나게 되었을 때는 몇 시간이고 목 놓아 울었다."], ["He was only consoled by his mother's promise that he should return to his nurse the next day; indeed the nurse herself, who probably would have been pained at the parting too, was told that the child would immediately be restored to her, and for some time awaited quite anxiously his return.", "그 애는 내일이면 유모에게 돌아가게 해 주겠다는 어머니의 약속에야 비로소 달래어졌다. 정작 그 유모 역시 이별을 슬퍼했을 법한데도, 아이를 곧 도로 데려다주겠노라는 말을 들은 터라 한동안은 자못 애타게 아이가 돌아오기를 기다렸다."]], [["In fact, our friends may be said to have been among the first of that brood of hardy English adventurers who have subsequently invaded the Continent and swindled in all the capitals of Europe.", "사실 우리의 벗들은, 훗날 대륙으로 쳐들어가 유럽의 온갖 수도에서 사기 행각을 벌인 그 강인한 영국 모험가 무리의 첫 축에 든다고 해도 좋을 것이다."], ["The respect in those happy days of 1817-18 was very great for the wealth and honour of Britons.", "1817년에서 18년에 이르는 그 행복한 시절에는 영국인의 부와 명예에 대한 존경심이 대단했다."], ["They had not then learned, as I am told, to haggle for bargains with the pertinacity which now distinguishes them.", "내가 듣기로 그때는 영국인들이 아직, 오늘날 그들을 특징짓는 그 끈질긴 흥정 솜씨를 익히지 못한 때였다."], ["The great cities of Europe had not been as yet open to the enterprise of our rascals.", "유럽의 큰 도시들은 아직 우리네 악당들의 모험에 문을 열지 않은 터였다."], ["And whereas there is now hardly a town of France or Italy in which you shall not see some noble countryman of our own, with that happy swagger and insolence of demeanour which we carry everywhere, swindling inn-landlords, passing fictitious cheques upon credulous bankers, robbing coach-makers of their carriages, goldsmiths of their trinkets, easy travellers of their money at cards, even public libraries of their books--thirty years ago you needed but to be a Milor Anglais, travelling in a private carriage, and credit was at your hand wherever you chose to seek it, and gentlemen, instead of cheating, were cheated. It was not for some weeks after the Crawleys'", "오늘날에는 프랑스든 이탈리아든 어디를 가든, 우리가 가는 곳마다 떨치는 그 거들먹거리는 행색과 오만한 거동으로 여관 주인을 등쳐 먹고, 곧이듣는 은행가에게 가짜 수표를 떠넘기고, 마차 제조공에게서 마차를, 금세공인에게서 장신구를, 만만한 여행객에게서 카드로 돈을, 심지어 공공 도서관에서 책까지 털어 가는 우리 동포 귀족을 보지 않을 도시가 거의 없게 되었지만, 삼십 년 전만 해도 그저 자가용 마차를 타고 여행하는 영국 귀족 나리이기만 하면, 어디서든 청하는 곳마다 외상이 척척 통했고, 신사들은 속이기는커녕 도리어 속아 넘어가는 쪽이었던 것이다."], ["departure that the landlord of the hotel which they occupied during their residence at Paris found out the losses which he had sustained: not until Madame Marabou, the milliner, made repeated visits with her little bill for articles supplied to Madame Crawley; not until Monsieur Didelot from Boule d'Or in the Palais Royal had asked half a dozen times whether cette charmante Miladi who had bought watches and bracelets of him was de retour.", "크롤리 부부가 파리에 머무는 동안 묵었던 그 호텔 주인이 자기가 입은 손실을 알아챈 것은 그들이 떠나고도 몇 주가 지나서였다. 모자 장수 마라부 부인이 크롤리 부인에게 대 준 물건값 청구서를 들고 거듭 찾아오고 나서야, 또 팔레 루아얄의 불 도르 상점에서 온 디들로 씨가 자기에게서 시계며 팔찌를 사 간 그 매력 넘치는 마님이 돌아왔느냐고 예닐곱 번이나 묻고 나서야 알아챈 것이다."], ["It is a fact that even the poor gardener's wife, who had nursed madame's child, was never paid after the first six months for that supply of the milk of human kindness with which she had furnished the lusty and healthy little Rawdon.", "심지어 마님의 아이에게 젖을 먹여 키운 그 가엾은 정원사 아내조차, 그 토실토실하고 튼실한 어린 로던에게 베푼 인정의 젖값을 처음 여섯 달치 말고는 끝내 한 푼도 받지 못했다는 것이 엄연한 사실이다. 그렇다, 유모조차 돈을 받지 못한 것이다."], ["No, not even the nurse was paid--the Crawleys were in too great a hurry to remember their trifling debt to her.", "크롤리 부부는 그녀에게 진 그 보잘것없는 빚을 떠올리기에는 너무도 황망히 서두르는 처지였던 것이다."], ["As for the landlord of the hotel, his curses against the English nation were violent for the rest of his natural life.", "호텔 주인으로 말하면, 그는 타고난 수명이 다하는 날까지 영국이라는 나라에 대고 격렬한 저주를 퍼부었다."], ["He asked all travellers whether they knew a certain Colonel Lor Crawley--avec sa femme une petite dame, tres spirituelle.", "그는 여행객을 만날 때마다 크롤리 로 대령이라는 자를 아느냐고, 아주 재치 있는 자그마한 부인을 데리고 다니던 그 사람을 아느냐고 물었다."], ["\"Ah, Monsieur!\"", "\"아, 손님!\""], ["he would add--\"ils m'ont affreusement vole.\"", "그는 이렇게 덧붙이곤 했다."], ["It was melancholy to hear his accents as he spoke of that catastrophe.", "\"그자들이 나를 끔찍하게 등쳐 먹었지 뭡니까.\" 그 참변을 이야기할 때 그의 어조는 듣기에 처량했다."]], [["Rebecca's object in her journey to London was to effect a kind of compromise with her husband's numerous creditors, and by offering them a dividend of ninepence or a shilling in the pound, to secure a return for him into his own country.", "레베카가 런던으로 길을 떠난 목적은, 남편의 숱한 채권자들과 일종의 화해를 매듭짓고, 그들에게 일 파운드당 구 펜스나 일 실링의 배당을 제안함으로써 남편이 제 나라로 돌아올 길을 마련하려는 것이었다. 이 더없이 까다로운 협상을 이끌어 가며 그녀가 밟은 단계를 일일이 좇는 것은 우리가 할 일이 아니다."], ["It does not become us to trace the steps which she took in the conduct of this most difficult negotiation; but, having shown them to their satisfaction that the sum which she was empowered to offer was all her husband's available capital, and having convinced them that Colonel Crawley would prefer a perpetual retirement on the Continent to a residence in this country with his debts unsettled; having proved to them that there was no possibility of money accruing to him from other quarters, and no earthly chance of their getting a larger dividend than that which she was empowered to offer, she brought the Colonel's creditors unanimously to accept her proposals, and purchased with fifteen hundred pounds of ready money more than ten times that amount of debts.", "그러나 그녀는 자기가 내놓을 수 있는 금액이 남편이 동원할 수 있는 자본의 전부임을 그들이 흡족하도록 보여 주었고, 크롤리 대령이 빚을 청산하지 않은 채 본국에 머무느니 차라리 대륙에서 영영 은둔하는 편을 택하리라는 것을 납득시켰으며, 다른 데서 그에게 돈이 들어올 가능성이라고는 없고 자기가 내놓는 것보다 더 큰 배당을 받을 가망 또한 이 세상에 없음을 입증해 보였다. 그리하여 그녀는 대령의 채권자들이 만장일치로 자기 제안을 받아들이게 만들었고, 현금 천오백 파운드로 그 열 배가 넘는 빚을 사들였다."]], [["Mrs. Crawley employed no lawyer in the transaction. The matter was so simple, to have or to leave, as she justly observed, that she made the lawyers of the creditors themselves do the business. And Mr. Lewis representing Mr. Davids, of Red Lion Square, and Mr. Moss acting for Mr. Manasseh of Cursitor Street (chief creditors of the Colonel's), complimented his lady upon the brilliant way in which she did business, and declared that there was no professional man who could beat her.", "크롤리 부인은 이 거래에 변호사를 한 사람도 쓰지 않았다. 그녀가 정확히 짚었듯이 받든지 말든지 하라는 식의 더없이 단순한 사안이었으니, 그녀는 채권자들 자신의 변호사들에게 그 일을 떠맡겼다. 그래서 레드 라이언 광장의 데이비즈 씨를 대리하는 루이스 씨와, 커시터 거리의 마나세 씨를(이 둘이 대령의 주된 채권자였다) 대리하는 모스 씨는 그 일을 처리하는 그녀의 눈부신 솜씨에 찬사를 보내며, 그 어떤 전문가도 그녀를 당해 낼 수 없을 것이라고 단언했다."]], [["Rebecca received their congratulations with perfect modesty; ordered a bottle of sherry and a bread cake to the little dingy lodgings where she dwelt, while conducting the business, to treat the enemy's lawyers: shook hands with them at parting, in excellent good humour, and returned straightway to the Continent, to rejoin her husband and son and acquaint the former with the glad news of his entire liberation. As for the latter, he had been considerably neglected during his mother's absence by Mademoiselle Genevieve, her French maid; for that young woman, contracting an attachment for a soldier in the garrison of Calais, forgot her charge in the society of this militaire, and little Rawdon very narrowly escaped drowning on Calais sands at this period, where the absent Genevieve had left and lost him.", "레베카는 완벽한 겸양으로 그들의 찬사를 받았다. 그녀는 그 일을 처리하는 동안 묵었던 자그맣고 누추한 셋방으로 셰리주 한 병과 빵 과자를 시켜다가 적군의 변호사들을 대접했으며, 헤어질 때는 더없이 기분 좋게 그들과 악수를 나누고는 곧장 대륙으로 돌아가, 남편과 아들을 다시 만나고 남편에게 그가 빚에서 완전히 풀려났다는 기쁜 소식을 전했다. 아들로 말하자면, 어머니가 없는 동안 그녀의 프랑스인 하녀 주느비에브 양에게 적잖이 방치되어 있었다. 그 젊은 여자가 칼레 수비대의 한 병사에게 정을 붙이는 바람에, 이 군인과 어울리느라 제가 맡은 아이를 까맣게 잊었던 것이다. 어린 로던은 이 무렵 칼레 모래밭에서 하마터면 물에 빠져 죽을 뻔했다. 자리를 비운 주느비에브가 거기에 아이를 놓아둔 채 잃어버렸던 것이다."]], [["And so, Colonel and Mrs.", "그리하여 크롤리 대령 부부는 런던으로 왔다."], ["Crawley came to London: and it is at their house in Curzon Street, May Fair, that they really showed the skill which must be possessed by those who would live on the resources above named.", "그들이 앞서 말한 그런 밑천으로 살아가려는 자들이라면 반드시 지녀야 할 그 솜씨를 진정으로 보여 준 곳이, 바로 메이페어의 커즌 거리에 자리한 그들의 집이었다."]], [["The Subject Continued\nIn the first place, and as a matter of the greatest necessity, we are bound to describe how a house may be got for nothing a year. These mansions are to be had either unfurnished, where, if you have credit with Messrs.", "우선, 무엇보다 절실한 문제부터 짚어 두자면, 어떻게 하면 집을 일 년에 한 푼도 안 들이고 얻을 수 있는지를 설명할 의무가 우리에게 있다."], ["Gillows or Bantings, you can get them splendidly montees and decorated entirely according to your own fancy; or they are to be let furnished, a less troublesome and complicated arrangement to most parties.", "이런 저택들은 가구 없이 빌릴 수도 있는데, 길로 상회나 밴팅 상회와 거래할 신용이 있다면 전적으로 자기 취향에 따라 훌륭하게 꾸미고 장식할 수 있다. 아니면 가구가 딸린 채로 세를 줄 수도 있는데, 대부분의 사람에게는 이쪽이 덜 번거롭고 덜 복잡한 방식이다."], ["It was so that Crawley and his wife preferred to hire their house.", "크롤리와 그 아내가 집을 빌리며 택한 쪽이 바로 이 방식이었다."]], [["Before Mr. Bowls came to preside over Miss Crawley's house and cellar in Park Lane, that lady had had for a butler a Mr.", "* 몽테: 가구를 갖추어 꾸민, 정돈된 상태를 뜻하는 프랑스어."], ["Raggles, who was born on the family estate of Queen's Crawley, and indeed was a younger son of a gardener there.", "볼스 씨가 파크 레인에 있는 크롤리 양의 저택과 술 창고를 관리하게 되기 전에, 그 부인에게는 래글스 씨라는 집사가 있었다."], ["By good conduct, a handsome person and calves, and a grave demeanour, Raggles rose from the knife-board to the footboard of the carriage; from the footboard to the butler's pantry.", "그는 퀸스크롤리의 가문 영지에서 태어났는데, 사실 그곳 정원사의 막내아들이었다. 품행이 단정하고, 풍채와 종아리가 훤칠하며, 점잖은 몸가짐을 갖춘 덕분에 래글스는 칼 닦는 자리에서 마차의 발판으로, 다시 발판에서 집사의 식료품 창고로 출세를 거듭했다."], ["When he had been a certain number of years at the head of Miss Crawley's establishment, where he had had good wages, fat perquisites, and plenty of opportunities of saving, he announced that he was about to contract a matrimonial alliance with a late cook of Miss Crawley's, who had subsisted in an honourable manner by the exercise of a mangle, and the keeping of a small greengrocer's shop in the neighbourhood.", "크롤리 양의 살림을 도맡은 지 몇 해가 지나 후한 급료와 두둑한 부수입, 그리고 저축할 기회를 잔뜩 누린 끝에, 그는 크롤리 양 댁의 전직 요리사와 혼인 동맹을 맺을 참이라고 밝혔다. 그 여자는 다림질 기계를 부리고 인근에서 자그마한 채소 가게를 꾸리며 떳떳하게 생계를 이어 온 사람이었다."], ["The truth is, that the ceremony had been clandestinely performed some years back; although the news of Mr. Raggles'", "사실인즉, 그 혼례는 몇 년 전에 이미 은밀히 치러진 터였다."], ["marriage was first brought to Miss Crawley by a little boy and girl of seven and eight years of age, whose continual presence in the kitchen had attracted the attention of Miss Briggs.", "다만 래글스 씨가 혼인했다는 소식이 크롤리 양에게 처음 전해진 것은, 일곱 살과 여덟 살쯤 된 사내아이와 계집아이를 통해서였다. 그 아이들이 줄곧 부엌에 들락거리는 바람에 브리그스 양의 눈에 띄었던 것이다."]], [["Mr. Raggles then retired and personally undertook the superintendence of the small shop and the greens.", "이윽고 래글스 씨는 은퇴하여 자그마한 가게와 채소 장사를 손수 도맡아 꾸려 나갔다."], ["He added milk and cream, eggs and country-fed pork to his stores, contenting himself whilst other retired butlers were vending spirits in public houses, by dealing in the simplest country produce.", "다른 은퇴한 집사들이 술집에서 독한 술을 팔고 다니는 동안, 그는 가게에 우유와 크림, 달걀과 시골에서 기른 돼지고기까지 들여놓고 가장 소박한 시골 산물을 다루는 것으로 만족했다."], ["And having a good connection amongst the butlers in the neighbourhood, and a snug back parlour where he and Mrs. Raggles received them, his milk, cream, and eggs got to be adopted by many of the fraternity, and his profits increased every year.", "그리고 인근 집사들과 두루 좋은 친분을 쌓아 두었고, 그와 래글스 부인이 그들을 맞이하는 아늑한 안쪽 응접실까지 갖추고 있던 터라, 그의 우유와 크림과 달걀은 그 동업자들 다수에게 채택되었고 이익은 해마다 늘어났다."], ["Year after year he quietly and modestly amassed money, and when at length that snug and complete bachelor's residence at No.", "해가 가고 또 가는 동안 그는 조용히, 그리고 겸손하게 돈을 모았다."], ["201, Curzon Street, May Fair, lately the residence of the Honourable Frederick Deuceace, gone abroad, with its rich and appropriate furniture by the first makers, was brought to the hammer, who should go in and purchase the lease and furniture of the house but Charles Raggles?", "마침내 메이페어 커즌 가 201번지에 있던 그 아늑하고 완벽한 독신자 거처가, 곧 외국으로 떠난 프레더릭 듀스에이스 귀하의 거처였던 그곳이, 일류 제작자들이 만든 호화롭고 격에 맞는 가구와 더불어 경매에 부쳐지자, 들어가서 그 집의 임차권과 가구를 사들인 이가 다름 아닌 찰스 래글스였으니 말이다."], ["A part of the money he borrowed, it is true, and at rather a high interest, from a brother butler, but the chief part he paid down, and it was with no small pride that Mrs.", "물론 그 돈의 일부는 어느 동업 집사에게서, 그것도 제법 높은 이자를 물고 빌린 것이 사실이었다."], ["Raggles found herself sleeping in a bed of carved mahogany, with silk curtains, with a prodigious cheval glass opposite to her, and a wardrobe which would contain her, and Raggles, and all the family.", "그러나 대부분은 현금으로 치렀고, 래글스 부인이 비단 휘장이 드리워진 조각 마호가니 침대에 누워, 맞은편의 어마어마한 체경과, 자신과 래글스와 온 가족이 다 들어가고도 남을 옷장을 바라보게 되었을 때 느낀 자부심은 결코 작지 않았다."]], [["Of course, they did not intend to occupy permanently an apartment so splendid.", "물론 그들은 그토록 호화로운 거처에 영영 머무를 작정은 아니었다."], ["It was in order to let the house again that Raggles purchased it.", "래글스가 그 집을 산 것은 다시 세를 놓기 위함이었다."], ["As soon as a tenant was found, he subsided into the greengrocer's shop once more; but a happy thing it was for him to walk out of that tenement and into Curzon Street, and there survey his house--his own house--with geraniums in the window and a carved bronze knocker.", "세입자가 나타나기가 무섭게 그는 또다시 채소 가게로 가라앉았다. 그러나 그 셋집에서 나와 커즌 가로 걸어 들어가, 거기 서서 자기 집을—자기 소유의 집을—창가에 제라늄이 놓이고 청동 조각 문고리가 달린 그 집을 바라보는 것은 그에게 더없이 흐뭇한 일이었다."], ["The footman occasionally lounging at the area railing, treated him with respect; the cook took her green stuff at his house and called him Mr.", "이따금 지하 출입구 난간에 기대어 빈둥거리던 하인은 그를 정중히 대했고, 요리사는 그의 가게에서 채소를 사며 그를 \"집주인 어른\"이라 불렀다."], ["Landlord, and there was not one thing the tenants did, or one dish which they had for dinner, that Raggles might not know of, if he liked.", "그리고 세입자들이 하는 일이나 저녁 식탁에 올리는 요리 가운데, 마음만 먹으면 래글스가 모를 일이라곤 하나도 없었다."]], [["He was a good man; good and happy.", "그는 선량한 사람이었다."], ["The house brought him in so handsome a yearly income that he was determined to send his children to good schools, and accordingly, regardless of expense, Charles was sent to boarding at Dr.", "선량하고 행복했다. 그 집이 어찌나 두둑한 연 수입을 안겨 주던지, 그는 아이들을 좋은 학교에 보내기로 마음먹었다."], ["Swishtail's, Sugar-cane Lodge, and little Matilda to Miss Peckover's, Laurentinum House, Clapham.", "그리하여 비용은 아랑곳하지 않고 찰스는 슈거케인 로지에 있는 스위시테일 박사의 기숙학교로 보냈고, 어린 머틸다는 클래펌의 로렌티넘 하우스에 있는 페코버 양의 학교로 보냈다."]], [["Raggles loved and adored the Crawley family as the author of all his prosperity in life.", "래글스는 크롤리 가문을 평생 누린 모든 번영의 원천으로 여겨 사랑하고 떠받들었다. 그는 안쪽 가게에 여주인의 실루엣 초상을 걸어 두었고, 그 노처녀가 손수 먹물로 그린 퀸스크롤리 수위실 그림도 간직하고 있었다."], ["He had a silhouette of his mistress in his back shop, and a drawing of the Porter's Lodge at Queen's Crawley, done by that spinster herself in India ink--and the only addition he made to the decorations of the Curzon Street House was a print of Queen's Crawley in Hampshire, the seat of Sir Walpole Crawley, Baronet, who was represented in a gilded car drawn by six white horses, and passing by a lake covered with swans, and barges containing ladies in hoops, and musicians with flags and periwigs.", "그리고 커즌 가 집의 장식에 그가 보탠 단 하나의 물건은 햄프셔에 있는 퀸스크롤리, 곧 준남작 월폴 크롤리 경의 저택을 그린 판화였다. 그 그림 속에서 경은 백마 여섯 필이 끄는 금박 마차에 탄 모습으로, 백조들과, 후프 스커트를 입은 숙녀들이며 깃발과 가발을 갖춘 악사들을 태운 거룻배들로 뒤덮인 호수 곁을 지나가고 있었다."], ["Indeed Raggles thought there was no such palace in all the world, and no such august family.", "정말이지 래글스는 온 세상에 그만한 궁전도, 그만큼 위풍당당한 가문도 없다고 여겼다."]], [["As luck would have it, Raggles' house in Curzon Street was to let when Rawdon and his wife returned to London.", "운 좋게도, 로던과 그 아내가 런던으로 돌아왔을 때 커즌 가의 래글스 집은 세를 놓을 참이었다."], ["The Colonel knew it and its owner quite well; the latter's connection with the Crawley family had been kept up constantly, for Raggles helped Mr.", "대령은 그 집과 그 주인을 아주 잘 알았다. 래글스가 크롤리 가문과 맺은 인연은 한결같이 이어져 왔으니, 크롤리 양이 손님을 맞을 때마다 그가 볼스 씨를 거들었기 때문이다."], ["Bowls whenever Miss Crawley received friends. And the old man not only let his house to the Colonel but officiated as his butler whenever he had company; Mrs.", "그리고 그 노인은 대령에게 집을 세놓았을 뿐 아니라, 대령이 손님을 칠 때마다 집사 노릇까지 해 주었다."], ["Raggles operating in the kitchen below and sending up dinners of which old Miss Crawley herself might have approved.", "래글스 부인은 아래층 부엌에서 솜씨를 부려, 늙은 크롤리 양조차 흡족해할 만한 만찬을 차려 올렸다. 자, 이것이 바로 크롤리가 집을 한 푼도 안 들이고 얻은 방식이었다."], ["This was the way, then, Crawley got his house for nothing; for though Raggles had to pay taxes and rates, and the interest of the mortgage to the brother butler; and the insurance of his life; and the charges for his children at school; and the value of the meat and drink which his own family--and for a time that of Colonel Crawley too--consumed; and though the poor wretch was utterly ruined by the transaction, his children being flung on the streets, and himself driven into the Fleet Prison: yet somebody must pay even for gentlemen who live for nothing a year--and so it was this unlucky Raggles was made the representative of Colonel Crawley's defective capital.", "비록 래글스가 세금과 부과금을 물어야 했고, 동업 집사에게 저당 이자를 치러야 했으며, 자기 생명 보험료도, 아이들 학비도, 자기 가족이—그리고 한동안은 크롤리 대령의 가족까지—먹고 마신 음식 값도 다 부담해야 했지만, 그리고 비록 이 가엾은 사내가 이 거래로 완전히 거덜이 나 아이들은 거리로 내쫓기고 자신은 플리트 감옥으로 끌려가게 되었지만, 일 년에 한 푼도 안 들이고 사는 신사들을 위해서도 누군가는 값을 치러야 하는 법이다. 그렇게 이 불운한 래글스가 크롤리 대령의 모자란 자본을 대신하는 인물로 세워진 것이다."]], [["I wonder how many families are driven to roguery and to ruin by great practitioners in Crawley's way?--how many great noblemen rob their petty tradesmen, condescend to swindle their poor retainers out of wretched little sums and cheat for a few shillings?", "* 플리트 감옥: 빚을 갚지 못한 채무자를 가두던 런던의 감옥. 대체 얼마나 많은 가족이 크롤리 같은 식의 대가들 손에 부정과 파멸로 내몰리고 있는 것일까?"], ["When we read that a noble nobleman has left for the Continent, or that another noble nobleman has an execution in his house--and that one or other owes six or seven millions, the defeat seems glorious even, and we respect the victim in the vastness of his ruin.", "얼마나 많은 위대한 귀족이 자기 거래하는 영세 상인의 것을 빼앗고, 가엾은 하인들에게서 비참할 만큼 푼돈을 갈취하며, 고작 몇 실링을 두고 사기를 치는 것일까? 어느 고귀하신 귀족이 대륙으로 떠났다거나, 또 다른 고귀하신 귀족의 집에 차압이 들어갔다거나, 둘 중 하나가 육칠백만 파운드를 빚졌다는 글을 읽으면, 그 몰락마저 영예롭게 보이고, 우리는 그 거대한 파멸 속의 희생자를 존경하기까지 한다."], ["But who pities a poor barber who can't get his money for powdering the footmen's heads; or a poor carpenter who has ruined himself by fixing up ornaments and pavilions for my lady's dejeuner; or the poor devil of a tailor whom the steward patronizes, and who has pledged all he is worth, and more, to get the liveries ready, which my lord has done him the honour to bespeak?", "그러나 하인들 머리에 분 발라 준 값을 받지 못하는 가엾은 이발사를, 마님의 오찬을 위해 장식과 정자를 꾸미느라 거덜 난 가엾은 목수를, 집사의 비호를 받으며 영주께서 황송하게도 주문해 주신 제복을 마련하느라 가진 것을 죄다, 아니 그 이상을 저당 잡힌 그 가엾은 재단사 녀석을 가엾이 여기는 이가 어디 있단 말인가?"], ["When the great house tumbles down, these miserable wretches fall under it unnoticed: as they say in the old legends, before a man goes to the devil himself, he sends plenty of other souls thither.", "그 거대한 저택이 무너져 내릴 때, 이 비참한 녀석들은 그 아래 깔려 아무도 모르게 사라지고 만다. 옛 전설에 이르듯, 사람이 제 발로 악마에게 가기에 앞서, 다른 영혼들을 잔뜩 먼저 그리로 보내는 법이다."]], [["Rawdon and his wife generously gave their patronage to all such of Miss Crawley's tradesmen and purveyors as chose to serve them.", "로던과 그 아내는 크롤리 양의 거래 상인이며 납품업자 가운데 자기들을 모시겠다고 나서는 이들에게는 누구에게나 후하게 단골 노릇을 베풀었다."], ["Some were willing enough, especially the poor ones.", "더러는 기꺼이 응했는데, 특히 가난한 이들이 그랬다."], ["It was wonderful to see the pertinacity with which the washerwoman from Tooting brought the cart every Saturday, and her bills week after week. Mr.", "투팅에서 온 세탁부 여인이 토요일마다 수레를 끌고 오고 주마다 계산서를 들이미는 그 끈질김이란 참으로 볼만한 것이었다."], ["Raggles himself had to supply the greengroceries.", "래글스 씨 본인은 채소를 대야 했다."], ["The bill for servants' porter at the Fortune of War public house is a curiosity in the chronicles of beer.", "'전쟁의 운수'라는 술집에서 하인들에게 외상으로 댄 흑맥주 값의 청구서는 맥주의 연대기에서 진귀한 물건감이었다."], ["Every servant also was owed the greater part of his wages, and thus kept up perforce an interest in the house.", "하인들도 저마다 급료의 태반을 못 받은 상태였으니, 그렇게 어쩔 수 없이 그 집에 매여 있는 셈이었다."], ["Nobody in fact was paid.", "사실 누구 하나 돈을 받지 못했다."], ["Not the blacksmith who opened the lock; nor the glazier who mended the pane; nor the jobber who let the carriage; nor the groom who drove it; nor the butcher who provided the leg of mutton; nor the coals which roasted it; nor the cook who basted it; nor the servants who ate it: and this I am given to understand is not unfrequently the way in which people live elegantly on nothing a year.", "자물쇠를 따 준 대장장이도, 깨진 유리창을 갈아 끼운 유리장이도, 마차를 빌려준 임대업자도, 그것을 몬 마부도, 양 다릿살을 댄 푸주한도, 그것을 구운 석탄 값도, 그것에 기름을 발라 구운 요리사도, 그것을 먹은 하인들도 마찬가지였다. 그리고 듣자 하니, 일 년에 한 푼도 안 들이고 우아하게 사는 이들의 방식이 이러한 경우가 적지 않다고 한다."]], [["In a little town such things cannot be done without remark.", "자그마한 동네에서라면 이런 일들이 입길에 오르지 않고 넘어갈 리 없다. 그런 데서는 이웃이 우유를 얼마나 들이는지 우리가 알고, 그 집 저녁상에 올라가는 고깃점이나 닭을 곁눈질로 살핀다."], ["We know there the quantity of milk our neighbour takes and espy the joint or the fowls which are going in for his dinner.", "그러니 아마 커즌 가의 200번지와 202번지는 그 사이에 낀 집에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 알고 있었을 것이다. 하인들이 지하 출입구 난간 너머로 소식을 주고받았을 테니 말이다."], ["So, probably, 200 and 202 in Curzon Street might know what was going on in the house between them, the servants communicating through the area-railings; but Crawley and his wife and his friends did not know 200 and 202.", "그러나 크롤리와 그 아내, 그 친구들은 200번지도 202번지도 몰랐다. 201번지에 들어서면 진심 어린 환대와 다정한 미소, 훌륭한 만찬, 그리고 주인 내외의 정겨운 악수가 있었으니, 마치 그들이 일 년에 삼사천 파운드를 거느린 명실상부한 주인이기라도 한 듯했다."], ["When you came to 201 there was a hearty welcome, a kind smile, a good dinner, and a jolly shake of the hand from the host and hostess there, just for all the world as if they had been undisputed masters of three or four thousand a year--and so they were, not in money, but in produce and labour--if they did not pay for the mutton, they had it: if they did not give bullion in exchange for their wine, how should we know?", "사실 그들은 그런 주인이었다—돈으로가 아니라 산물과 노동으로 말이다. 양고기 값을 치르지는 않았어도 양고기는 손에 넣었고, 포도주 값으로 금붙이를 내놓지 않았다 한들 그걸 우리가 어찌 알겠는가?"], ["Never was better claret at any man's table than at honest Rawdon's; dinners more gay and neatly served.", "정직한 로던의 식탁만큼 좋은 클라레가 오르는 식탁도 없었고, 그만큼 흥겹고 말끔하게 차려진 만찬도 없었다. 그의 응접실은 상상할 수 있는 가장 어여쁘고 자그마하며 정갈한 살롱이었다."], ["His drawing-rooms were the prettiest, little, modest salons conceivable: they were decorated with the greatest taste, and a thousand knick-knacks from Paris, by Rebecca: and when she sat at her piano trilling songs with a lightsome heart, the stranger voted himself in a little paradise of domestic comfort and agreed that, if the husband was rather stupid, the wife was charming, and the dinners the pleasantest in the world.", "더없이 뛰어난 안목으로, 파리에서 가져온 천 가지 자질구레한 장식품으로, 레베카가 손수 꾸민 방이었다. 그리고 그녀가 피아노 앞에 앉아 경쾌한 마음으로 노래를 가락 굴려 부르노라면, 손님은 자신이 가정의 안락이 깃든 자그마한 낙원에 든 듯 여기며, 비록 남편은 다소 우둔할지언정 아내는 매혹적이고 만찬은 세상에서 가장 즐겁다는 데 동의하는 것이었다."]], [["Rebecca's wit, cleverness, and flippancy made her speedily the vogue in London among a certain class.", "레베카의 재치와 영민함, 그리고 가벼운 입담은 곧 런던의 어떤 부류 사이에서 그녀를 인기인으로 만들었다."], ["You saw demure chariots at her door, out of which stepped very great people.", "그녀의 문 앞에는 점잖은 사두마차들이 늘어섰고, 거기서 대단히 지체 높은 이들이 내렸다."], ["You beheld her carriage in the park, surrounded by dandies of note.", "그녀의 마차가 공원에서 이름난 멋쟁이들에게 둘러싸인 모습도 볼 수 있었다."], ["The little box in the third tier of the opera was crowded with heads constantly changing; but it must be confessed that the ladies held aloof from her, and that their doors were shut to our little adventurer.", "오페라극장 삼층의 자그마한 특별석은 끊임없이 바뀌는 머리들로 붐볐다. 그러나 인정해야 할 것은, 숙녀들만은 그녀를 멀리했고, 우리의 자그마한 모험가에게 그들의 문은 닫혀 있었다는 점이다."]], [["With regard to the world of female fashion and its customs, the present writer of course can only speak at second hand.", "여성 사교계와 그 관습에 관해서라면, 물론 이 글을 쓰는 사람으로서는 전해 들은 것만 이야기할 수 있을 따름이다. 남자란, 숙녀들이 만찬 뒤 위층으로 올라가 무슨 이야기를 나누는지 알 수 없는 것이나 마찬가지로, 그 신비를 꿰뚫어 보거나 이해할 수 없는 법이다."], ["A man can no more penetrate or understand those mysteries than he can know what the ladies talk about when they go upstairs after dinner.", "다만 캐묻고 끈기를 부린 끝에야 이따금 그런 비밀의 단서를 얻게 될 뿐이다. 그와 같은 부지런함으로, 펠멜의 보도를 밟고 이 대도시의 클럽들을 드나드는 이라면 누구나, 제 경험을 통해서든, 함께 당구를 치거나 고기를 나눠 먹는 어떤 지인을 통해서든, 런던의 점잖은 세계에 대해 무언가를 알게 된다."], ["It is only by inquiry and perseverance that one sometimes gets hints of those secrets; and by a similar diligence every person who treads the Pall Mall pavement and frequents the clubs of this metropolis knows, either through his own experience or through some acquaintance with whom he plays at billiards or shares the joint, something about the genteel world of London, and how, as there are men (such as Rawdon Crawley, whose position we mentioned before) who cut a good figure to the eyes of the ignorant world and to the apprentices in the park, who behold them consorting with the most notorious dandies there, so there are ladies, who may be called men's women, being welcomed entirely by all the gentlemen and cut or slighted by all their wives. Mrs.", "그리고 어떻게 해서, 무지한 세상의 눈이나 공원의 견습공들 눈에는 그럴듯해 보이는 남자들이—이를테면 앞서 그 처지를 언급한 로던 크롤리 같은 이들, 거기서 가장 악명 높은 멋쟁이들과 어울리는 모습을 견습공들이 목도하는 그런 남자들이—있는 것처럼, 이른바 남자들의 여자라 불릴 만한 숙녀들도 있어, 신사들에게는 죄다 환대받으면서도 그들의 아내들에게는 죄다 외면당하거나 푸대접받는다는 것을 알게 되는 것이다."], ["Firebrace is of this sort; the lady with the beautiful fair ringlets whom you see every day in Hyde Park, surrounded by the greatest and most famous dandies of this empire.", "파이어브레이스 부인이 그런 부류다. 날마다 하이드 파크에서 이 제국의 가장 위대하고 이름난 멋쟁이들에게 둘러싸인, 아름다운 금빛 곱슬머리를 늘어뜨린 그 부인 말이다."], ["Mrs. Rockwood is another, whose parties are announced laboriously in the fashionable newspapers and with whom you see that all sorts of ambassadors and great noblemen dine; and many more might be mentioned had they to do with the history at present in hand.", "록우드 부인도 또 한 사람인데, 그녀가 여는 연회는 유행지 신문마다 애써 알려지고, 온갖 대사며 위대한 귀족들이 그녀와 함께 식사하는 모습을 볼 수 있다."], ["But while simple folks who are out of the world, or country people with a taste for the genteel, behold these ladies in their seeming glory in public places, or envy them from afar off, persons who are better instructed could inform them that these envied ladies have no more chance of establishing themselves in \"society,\"", "지금 다루는 이야기와 관련만 있다면 더 많은 이를 들 수도 있으리라. 그러나 세상 물정에 어두운 순박한 이들이나, 점잖은 것에 취미를 둔 시골 사람들이 이 숙녀들을 공공장소에서 짐짓 영광스러운 모습으로 바라보거나 멀찍이서 부러워하는 동안, 더 잘 아는 이들은 그들에게 일러 줄 수 있으리라—그 부러움을 사는 숙녀들이 \"사교계\"에 자리 잡을 가망이란, 「모닝 포스트」에서 그들의 행적을 읽는 서머싯셔의 무지한 시골 지주 아내만큼이나 없다는 것을 말이다."], ["than the benighted squire's wife in Somersetshire who reads of their doings in the Morning Post.", "런던 일대에 사는 남자들은 이 무서운 진실들을 알고 있다."], ["Men living about London are aware of these awful truths. You hear how pitilessly many ladies of seeming rank and wealth are excluded from this \"society.\"", "지체와 부를 갖춘 듯 보이는 숱한 숙녀들이 이 \"사교계\"에서 얼마나 무자비하게 배제되는지 듣게 되는 것이다."], ["The frantic efforts which they make to enter this circle, the meannesses to which they submit, the insults which they undergo, are matters of wonder to those who take human or womankind for a study; and the pursuit of fashion under difficulties would be a fine theme for any very great person who had the wit, the leisure, and the knowledge of the English language necessary for the compiling of such a history.", "이 무리에 끼어들려고 그들이 벌이는 광적인 노력, 굴종하는 비굴함, 견뎌 내는 모욕은, 인간이나 여성을 연구 대상으로 삼는 이들에게 경탄거리이다. 그리고 온갖 난관 속에서 유행을 좇는 일이란, 그런 역사를 엮어 내는 데 필요한 재치와 여가와 영어 지식을 갖춘 어느 대단한 인물에게는 더없이 훌륭한 주제가 될 것이다."]], [["Now the few female acquaintances whom Mrs. Crawley had known abroad not only declined to visit her when she came to this side of the Channel, but cut her severely when they met in public places.", "자, 크롤리 부인이 외국에서 알고 지내던 몇 안 되는 여성 지인들은 그녀가 해협 이편으로 건너오자 방문을 마다했을 뿐 아니라, 공공장소에서 마주치면 매몰차게 외면했다."], ["It was curious to see how the great ladies forgot her, and no doubt not altogether a pleasant study to Rebecca.", "그 지체 높은 숙녀들이 어찌나 그녀를 깡그리 잊어버리던지 보기에 흥미로울 지경이었고, 레베카에게는 분명 그리 유쾌한 연구거리는 아니었으리라."], ["When Lady Bareacres met her in the waiting-room at the opera, she gathered her daughters about her as if they would be contaminated by a touch of Becky, and retreating a step or two, placed herself in front of them, and stared at her little enemy.", "베어에이커스 부인은 오페라극장 대기실에서 그녀와 마주치자, 베키의 손길 한 번에 더럽혀지기라도 할 듯 딸들을 제 곁으로 그러모으고는, 한두 걸음 물러서서 그들 앞을 막아서며 자그마한 적수를 쏘아보았다."], ["To stare Becky out of countenance required a severer glance than even the frigid old Bareacres could shoot out of her dismal eyes.", "베키를 노려보아 무안하게 만들려면, 그 싸늘한 노부인 베어에이커스가 음울한 눈에서 쏘아 보낼 수 있는 것보다 한층 매서운 눈빛이 필요했다."], ["When Lady de la Mole, who had ridden a score of times by Becky's side at Brussels, met Mrs. Crawley's open carriage in Hyde Park, her Ladyship was quite blind, and could not in the least recognize her former friend.", "브뤼셀에서 베키 곁을 스무 번도 넘게 말 달리던 드 라 몰 부인은, 하이드 파크에서 크롤리 부인의 무개 마차와 마주치자 영락없이 눈먼 사람이 되어, 옛 친구를 조금도 알아보지 못했다."], ["Even Mrs. Blenkinsop, the banker's wife, cut her at church.", "은행가의 아내인 블렌킨솝 부인조차 교회에서 그녀를 외면했다."], ["Becky went regularly to church now; it was edifying to see her enter there with Rawdon by her side, carrying a couple of large gilt prayer-books, and afterwards going through the ceremony with the gravest resignation.", "베키는 이제 꼬박꼬박 교회에 다녔는데, 그녀가 로던을 곁에 거느린 채, 큼직한 금박 기도서 두 권을 들고 들어와, 그 뒤로 더없이 경건한 체념의 자세로 예배를 치르는 모습은 가히 교화적이었다."]], [["Rawdon at first felt very acutely the slights which were passed upon his wife, and was inclined to be gloomy and savage.", "로던은 처음에는 아내에게 가해지는 푸대접을 몹시 예민하게 느껴, 침울하고 사나워지기 일쑤였다."], ["He talked of calling out the husbands or brothers of every one of the insolent women who did not pay a proper respect to his wife; and it was only by the strongest commands and entreaties on her part that he was brought into keeping a decent behaviour.", "그는 자기 아내에게 마땅한 경의를 표하지 않는 그 무례한 여자들의 남편이나 형제를 하나하나 결투에 불러내겠다고 떠들어 댔다. 그가 점잖은 처신을 지키게 된 것은 오로지 아내가 가장 단호히 명하고 간청한 덕분이었다."], ["\"You can't shoot me into society,\"", "\"당신이 나를 사교계로 쏘아 넣어 줄 수는 없잖아요.\""], ["she said good-naturedly.", "그녀가 너그러이 말했다."], ["\"Remember, my dear, that I was but a governess, and you, you poor silly old man, have the worst reputation for debt, and dice, and all sorts of wickedness.", "\"잊지 말아요, 여보, 나는 한낱 가정교사였고, 당신은, 이 가엾고 어리석은 양반은, 빚이며 노름이며 온갖 못된 짓으로 더없이 나쁜 평판을 달고 있다는 걸요. 머지않아 우리도 필요한 만큼 친구를 두루 얻게 될 거예요."], ["We shall get quite as many friends as we want by and by, and in the meanwhile you must be a good boy and obey your schoolmistress in everything she tells you to do.", "그러니 그동안 당신은 착한 아이처럼 굴면서, 당신 여선생이 시키는 건 뭐든지 고분고분 따라야 해요. 당신 고모님이 거의 전부를 피트와 그 아내에게 남겼다는 소식을 들었을 때, 당신이 얼마나 길길이 날뛰었는지 기억나요?"], ["When we heard that your aunt had left almost everything to Pitt and his wife, do you remember what a rage you were in?", "내가 화를 누르게 하지 않았더라면 당신은 온 파리에 떠들고 다녔을 테고, 그랬으면 지금 어디 있겠어요?"], ["You would have told all Paris, if I had not made you keep your temper, and where would you have been now?--in prison at Ste.", "빚 때문에 생트펠라지 감옥에 갇혀 있을 거예요. 런던의 멋진 집에서 온갖 안락을 누리며 자리 잡는 대신에 말이에요."], ["Pelagie for debt, and not established in London in a handsome house, with every comfort about you--you were in such a fury you were ready to murder your brother, you wicked Cain you, and what good would have come of remaining angry?", "당신은 어찌나 펄펄 뛰던지 동생을 죽일 기세였지요, 이 못된 카인 같은 양반아. 한데 화를 품고 있어서 좋을 게 뭐가 있었겠어요?"], ["All the rage in the world won't get us your aunt's money; and it is much better that we should be friends with your brother's family than enemies, as those foolish Butes are.", "세상의 온갖 분노를 다 끌어모은들 고모님 돈을 우리한테 가져다주지는 못해요. 그러니 저 어리석은 뷰트 식구들처럼 당신 동생네와 원수가 되느니, 친구로 지내는 편이 훨씬 나아요."], ["When your father dies, Queen's Crawley will be a pleasant house for you and me to pass the winter in.", "당신 아버님이 돌아가시면, 퀸스크롤리는 당신과 내가 겨울을 나기에 아주 쾌적한 집이 될 거예요."], ["If we are ruined, you can carve and take charge of the stable, and I can be a governess to Lady Jane's children.", "우리가 망한다 해도, 당신은 고기를 썰고 마구간을 맡으면 되고, 나는 제인 부인의 아이들 가정교사가 되면 그만이지요."], ["Ruined!", "망한다고요!"], ["fiddlede-dee!", "어림없는 소리!"], ["I will get you a good place before that; or Pitt and his little boy will die, and we will be Sir Rawdon and my lady.", "그 전에 내가 당신한테 좋은 자리를 마련해 줄 테니까요. 아니면 피트와 그 어린 아들이 죽어서, 우리가 로던 경과 마님이 될 수도 있고요."], ["While there is life, there is hope, my dear, and I intend to make a man of you yet.", "목숨이 붙어 있는 한 희망은 있는 거예요, 여보. 나는 기어이 당신을 어엿한 인물로 만들 작정이라고요."], ["Who sold your horses for you?", "누가 당신 말들을 팔아 줬어요?"], ["Who paid your debts for you?\"", "누가 당신 빚을 갚아 줬고요?\""], ["Rawdon was obliged to confess that he owed all these benefits to his wife, and to trust himself to her guidance for the future.", "로던은 이 모든 은혜를 아내에게 입었음을, 그리고 앞날을 그녀의 인도에 맡기겠음을 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["Indeed, when Miss Crawley quitted the world, and that money for which all her relatives had been fighting so eagerly was finally left to Pitt, Bute Crawley, who found that only five thousand pounds had been left to him instead of the twenty upon which he calculated, was in such a fury at his disappointment that he vented it in savage abuse upon his nephew; and the quarrel always rankling between them ended in an utter breach of intercourse.", "실로, 크롤리 양이 세상을 뜨고, 모든 친척이 그토록 악착같이 다투던 그 돈이 마침내 피트에게 돌아가자, 자기가 헤아리던 이만 파운드 대신 고작 오천 파운드만 받게 된 것을 안 뷰트 크롤리는, 그 낭패에 어찌나 격분했던지 조카에게 사나운 욕설을 퍼붓는 것으로 화풀이를 했다. 그리고 둘 사이에 늘 응어리진 그 다툼은 끝내 왕래의 완전한 단절로 이어졌다."], ["Rawdon Crawley's conduct, on the other hand, who got but a hundred pounds, was such as to astonish his brother and delight his sister-in-law, who was disposed to look kindly upon all the members of her husband's family.", "한편 고작 백 파운드만 받은 로던 크롤리의 처신은 형을 놀라게 하고 형수를 기쁘게 할 만한 것이었으니, 형수는 남편 집안 사람이라면 누구에게나 너그러운 마음을 품으려는 사람이었다."], ["He wrote to his brother a very frank, manly, good-humoured letter from Paris.", "그는 파리에서 형에게 더없이 솔직하고 사내답고 호의 어린 편지를 써 보냈다."], ["He was aware, he said, that by his own marriage he had forfeited his aunt's favour; and though he did not disguise his disappointment that she should have been so entirely relentless towards him, he was glad that the money was still kept in their branch of the family, and heartily congratulated his brother on his good fortune.", "자신이 멋대로 혼인함으로써 고모의 총애를 잃었음을 잘 알고 있노라고 그는 적었다. 그리고 고모가 자기에게 그토록 끝끝내 매정했다는 데 대한 실망을 감추지는 않으면서도, 그 돈이 그래도 자기네 집안 줄기 안에 남게 되어 기쁘며, 형의 행운을 진심으로 축하한다고 했다."], ["He sent his affectionate remembrances to his sister, and hoped to have her good-will for Mrs.", "그는 형수에게 다정한 안부를 전하며, 자기 아내 로던 부인에게도 호의를 베풀어 주기를 바란다고 했다."], ["Rawdon; and the letter concluded with a postscript to Pitt in the latter lady's own handwriting.", "그리고 편지는 그 아내가 손수 쓴, 피트에게 보내는 추신으로 끝맺어졌다."], ["She, too, begged to join in her husband's congratulations.", "그녀 역시 남편의 축하에 함께하기를 청한다고 했다."], ["She should ever remember Mr. Crawley's kindness to her in early days when she was a friendless orphan, the instructress of his little sisters, in whose welfare she still took the tenderest interest.", "자신이 의지할 곳 없는 고아로서, 그분의 어린 누이들을 가르치던 시절에 크롤리 씨가 베풀어 준 친절을 영원히 기억하겠으며, 그 누이들의 안녕에 지금도 더없이 애틋한 관심을 두고 있다고 했다."], ["She wished him every happiness in his married life, and, asking his permission to offer her remembrances to Lady Jane (of whose goodness all the world informed her), she hoped that one day she might be allowed to present her little boy to his uncle and aunt, and begged to bespeak for him their good-will and protection.", "그의 결혼 생활에 온갖 행복이 깃들기를 빌며, 제인 부인께(온 세상이 그 인품을 일러 주더라고 했다) 자기 안부를 전하도록 허락해 주기를 청하면서, 언젠가 자기 어린 아들을 그 백부와 백모께 인사시킬 수 있기를 바라며, 그 아이를 위해 두 분의 호의와 보살핌을 미리 부탁드린다고 했다."]], [["Pitt Crawley received this communication very graciously--more graciously than Miss Crawley had received some of Rebecca's previous compositions in Rawdon's handwriting; and as for Lady Jane, she was so charmed with the letter that she expected her husband would instantly divide his aunt's legacy into two equal portions and send off one-half to his brother at Paris.", "피트 크롤리는 이 서신을 더없이 정중하게 받아들였다. 일찍이 크롤리 양이 로던의 필체로 적힌 레베카의 글들을 받아들이던 것보다 한결 정중하게 말이다. 그리고 제인 부인으로 말하면, 그 편지에 어찌나 마음이 끌렸던지, 남편이 당장 고모의 유산을 똑같이 둘로 나누어 그 절반을 파리의 동생에게 부쳐 줄 것이라 기대할 정도였다."]], [["To her Ladyship's surprise, however, Pitt declined to accommodate his brother with a cheque for thirty thousand pounds.", "그러나 부인이 놀랍게도, 피트는 동생에게 삼만 파운드짜리 수표를 끊어 주기를 사양했다."], ["But he made Rawdon a handsome offer of his hand whenever the latter should come to England and choose to take it; and, thanking Mrs.", "다만 동생이 영국에 와서 받기를 원할 때면 언제든 손을 내밀겠다는 후한 제안을 했다."], ["Crawley for her good opinion of himself and Lady Jane, he graciously pronounced his willingness to take any opportunity to serve her little boy.", "그리고 자신과 제인 부인을 좋게 보아 준 데 대해 크롤리 부인에게 감사하면서, 그 어린 아들을 위해서라면 어떤 기회든 기꺼이 돕겠노라고 정중히 밝혔다."]], [["Thus an almost reconciliation was brought about between the brothers.", "이렇게 하여 형제 사이에 거의 화해라 할 만한 것이 이루어졌다."], ["When Rebecca came to town Pitt and his wife were not in London.", "레베카가 런던에 왔을 때 피트와 그 아내는 런던에 없었다."], ["Many a time she drove by the old door in Park Lane to see whether they had taken possession of Miss Crawley's house there.", "그녀는 그들이 거기 있는 크롤리 양의 집을 차지했는지 보려고, 파크 레인의 그 낡은 문 앞을 몇 번이나 마차로 지나다녔다."], ["But the new family did not make its appearance; it was only through Raggles that she heard of their movements--how Miss Crawley's domestics had been dismissed with decent gratuities, and how Mr.", "그러나 새 식구는 모습을 드러내지 않았다. 그녀가 그들의 움직임을 전해 들은 것은 오로지 래글스를 통해서였다."], ["Pitt had only once made his appearance in London, when he stopped for a few days at the house, did business with his lawyers there, and sold off all Miss Crawley's French novels to a bookseller out of Bond Street.", "크롤리 양의 하인들이 점잖은 사례금과 함께 해고되었다는 것, 그리고 피트 씨가 런던에 모습을 보인 것은 단 한 번, 그 집에 며칠 묵으며 거기서 변호사들과 일을 보고 크롤리 양의 프랑스 소설을 죄다 본드 가의 어느 책방에 팔아넘긴 때뿐이었다는 것 말이다."], ["Becky had reasons of her own which caused her to long for the arrival of her new relation.", "베키에게는 새 친척이 오기를 애타게 기다릴 만한 나름의 이유가 있었다."], ["\"When Lady Jane comes,\"", "'제인 부인이 오면,'"], ["thought she, \"she shall be my sponsor in London society; and as for the women!", "그녀는 생각했다, '그 사람이 런던 사교계에서 내 후원자가 되어 줄 거야. 그리고 여자들로 말하면!"], ["bah!", "흥!"], ["the women will ask me when they find the men want to see me.\"", "남자들이 나를 보고 싶어 한다는 걸 알게 되면 여자들이 알아서 나를 청할 테지.'"]], [["An article as necessary to a lady in this position as her brougham or her bouquet is her companion.", "이런 처지의 숙녀에게 마차나 꽃다발 못지않게 긴요한 물건이 바로 말동무다."], ["I have always admired the way in which the tender creatures, who cannot exist without sympathy, hire an exceedingly plain friend of their own sex from whom they are almost inseparable.", "나는 동정 없이는 살아가지 못하는 그 가녀린 존재들이, 자기와 거의 떨어질 수 없는 지극히 평범한 동성 친구 하나를 들이는 그 방식에 늘 감탄해 왔다."], ["The sight of that inevitable woman in her faded gown seated behind her dear friend in the opera-box, or occupying the back seat of the barouche, is always a wholesome and moral one to me, as jolly a reminder as that of the Death's-head which figured in the repasts of Egyptian bon-vivants, a strange sardonic memorial of Vanity Fair. What?", "빛바랜 가운을 걸친 그 빠질 수 없는 여자가 오페라 특별석에서 사랑하는 벗 뒤에 앉아 있거나 사륜마차의 뒷자리를 차지하고 있는 광경은, 내게는 언제나 건전하고 교훈적인 것이다."], ["even battered, brazen, beautiful, conscienceless, heartless, Mrs. Firebrace, whose father died of her shame: even lovely, daring Mrs.", "이집트의 미식가들이 잔치 때 끌어다 놓던 해골만큼이나 유쾌한 환기물이요, 허영의 시장을 향한 야릇하고 냉소적인 기념물인 것이다."], ["Mantrap, who will ride at any fence which any man in England will take, and who drives her greys in the park, while her mother keeps a huckster's stall in Bath still--even those who are so bold, one might fancy they could face anything, dare not face the world without a female friend.", "무어라고? 저 닳고 닳은, 뻔뻔하고, 아름답고, 양심도 없고, 인정머리도 없는 파이어브레이스 부인, 그 수치 때문에 아비가 죽었다는 그 여자조차도, 또 영국의 어느 남자든 넘을 만한 울타리라면 다 넘어 말을 달리고 공원에서 회색 말들을 손수 모는, 그 어미가 아직도 배스에서 행상 좌판을 보고 있는, 그 사랑스럽고 대담한 맨트랩 부인조차도, 그토록 대담하여 무엇이든 맞설 수 있을 성싶은 그런 여자들조차도, 여자 친구 하나 없이는 감히 세상과 맞서지 못하는 것이다."], ["They must have somebody to cling to, the affectionate creatures!", "그들에게는 매달릴 누군가가 있어야 하는 법이다, 그 정 많은 존재들에게는!"], ["And you will hardly see them in any public place without a shabby companion in a dyed silk, sitting somewhere in the shade close behind them.", "그러니 어느 공공장소에서든, 물들인 비단옷을 걸친 초라한 말동무 하나가 그들 바로 뒤 그늘 어딘가에 앉아 있지 않은 모습을 보기란 좀처럼 어려울 것이다."]], [["\"Rawdon,\"", "\"로던.\""], ["said Becky, very late one night, as a party of gentlemen were seated round her crackling drawing-room fire (for the men came to her house to finish the night; and she had ice and coffee for them, the best in London): \"I must have a sheep-dog.\"", "어느 날 밤, 신사들 한 무리가 그녀의 탁탁 타오르는 응접실 난롯가에 둘러앉아 있을 때 베키가 아주 늦은 시각에 입을 열었다(남자들은 밤을 마무리하러 그녀의 집으로 모여들었고, 그녀는 그들에게 얼음과 커피를 내놓았는데 런던에서 으뜸가는 것이었다). \"나 양치기 개가 한 마리 있어야겠어요.\""]], [["\"A what?\"", "\"뭐가 있어야겠다고?\""], ["said Rawdon, looking up from an ecarte table.", "로던이 에카르테 판에서 고개를 들며 말했다."]], [["\"A sheep-dog!\"", "\"양치기 개라뇨!\""], ["said young Lord Southdown.", "젊은 사우스다운 경이 말했다."], ["\"My dear Mrs. Crawley, what a fancy!", "\"크롤리 부인, 별 희한한 생각을 다 하시는군요!"], ["Why not have a Danish dog?", "차라리 덴마크 개를 들이시지 그래요?"], ["I know of one as big as a camel-leopard, by Jove. It would almost pull your brougham.", "맹세코 기린만 한 놈을 하나 알고 있는데, 그놈이면 부인의 사륜마차도 끌 만하다오."], ["Or a Persian greyhound, eh? (I propose, if you please); or a little pug that would go into one of Lord Steyne's snuff-boxes?", "아니면 페르시아 그레이하운드는 어떻소, 응?(괜찮으시다면 패를 집겠소.) 아니면 스테인 경의 코담뱃갑에 쏙 들어갈 만한 자그마한 퍼그는요?"], ["There's a man at Bayswater got one with such a nose that you might--I mark the king and play--that you might hang your hat on it.\"", "베이스워터에 사는 어떤 사람이 한 마리 길렀는데 그놈 코가 어찌나—내가 킹을 내고 칩니다—그 코에 모자를 걸어 둘 수 있을 정도라오.\""]], [["\"I mark the trick,\"", "\"내가 그 패를 먹지.\""], ["Rawdon gravely said.", "로던이 점잖게 말했다."], ["He attended to his game commonly and didn't much meddle with the conversation, except when it was about horses and betting.", "그는 으레 제 노름에만 골몰할 뿐, 말이나 내기에 관한 게 아니면 대화에 좀처럼 끼어들지 않았다."]], [["\"What CAN you want with a shepherd's dog?\"", "\"대체 양치기 개로 뭘 하시려는 거요?\""], ["the lively little Southdown continued.", "활달하고 자그마한 사우스다운이 말을 이었다."]], [["\"I mean a MORAL shepherd's dog,\"", "\"내 말은, 도덕적인 양치기 개요.\""], ["said Becky, laughing and looking up at Lord Steyne.", "베키가 웃으며 스테인 경을 올려다보고 말했다."]], [["\"What the devil's that?\"", "\"그건 또 뭐란 말이오?\""], ["said his Lordship.", "경이 말했다."]], [["\"A dog to keep the wolves off me,\"", "\"나한테서 늑대들을 쫓아 줄 개죠.\""], ["Rebecca continued.", "레베카가 말을 이었다."], ["\"A companion.\"", "\"말동무 말이에요.\""]], [["\"Dear little innocent lamb, you want one,\"", "\"가엾고도 천진한 어린 양 같으니, 정말 한 마리 들이셔야겠소.\""], ["said the marquis; and his jaw thrust out, and he began to grin hideously, his little eyes leering towards Rebecca.", "후작이 말했다. 그러고는 턱을 쑥 내밀고 흉하게 이를 드러내며 웃기 시작했고, 자그마한 두 눈은 레베카 쪽으로 음흉하게 곁눈질을 보냈다."]], [["The great Lord of Steyne was standing by the fire sipping coffee.", "위대하신 스테인 경은 난롯가에 서서 커피를 홀짝이고 있었다."], ["The fire crackled and blazed pleasantly.", "불은 기분 좋게 탁탁거리며 활활 타올랐다."], ["There was a score of candles sparkling round the mantel piece, in all sorts of quaint sconces, of gilt and bronze and porcelain.", "벽난로 선반 둘레에는, 금박이며 청동이며 도자기로 된 갖가지 기묘한 촛대에 꽂힌 촛불 스무 개가 반짝였다. 그 불빛은 화사한 꽃무늬가 덮인 소파에 앉은 레베카의 자태를 더없이 곱게 비추었다."], ["They lighted up Rebecca's figure to admiration, as she sat on a sofa covered with a pattern of gaudy flowers.", "그녀는 장미처럼 싱그러워 보이는 분홍빛 드레스를 입었고, 눈부시게 흰 두 팔과 어깨는 얇고 어렴풋한 스카프로 반쯤 가렸는데 그 너머로 살결이 반짝였다."], ["She was in a pink dress that looked as fresh as a rose; her dazzling white arms and shoulders were half-covered with a thin hazy scarf through which they sparkled; her hair hung in curls round her neck; one of her little feet peeped out from the fresh crisp folds of the silk: the prettiest little foot in the prettiest little sandal in the finest silk stocking in the world.", "머리칼은 목덜미를 따라 곱슬곱슬 늘어졌고, 자그마한 한쪽 발이 싱그럽게 빳빳한 비단 주름 사이로 살짝 내비쳤다. 세상에서 가장 고운 비단 양말에, 가장 고운 자그마한 샌들을 신은, 가장 고운 자그마한 발이었다."]], [["The candles lighted up Lord Steyne's shining bald head, which was fringed with red hair.", "촛불은 스테인 경의 번들거리는 대머리를 비추었는데, 그 둘레로는 붉은 머리칼이 술처럼 둘려 있었다."], ["He had thick bushy eyebrows, with little twinkling bloodshot eyes, surrounded by a thousand wrinkles.", "그는 짙고 덥수룩한 눈썹에, 천 갈래 주름으로 둘러싸인, 핏발 서고 반짝이는 작은 눈을 지녔다."], ["His jaw was underhung, and when he laughed, two white buck-teeth protruded themselves and glistened savagely in the midst of the grin.", "턱은 아래로 튀어나와 있어서, 웃을 때면 하얀 토끼 같은 앞니 두 개가 비죽 나와 그 웃음 한가운데서 사납게 번득였다."], ["He had been dining with royal personages, and wore his garter and ribbon.", "그는 왕족들과 만찬을 들고 온 터라 가터 훈장과 그 띠를 두르고 있었다."], ["A short man was his Lordship, broad-chested and bow-legged, but proud of the fineness of his foot and ankle, and always caressing his garter-knee.", "경은 키가 작고, 가슴팍이 떡 벌어졌으며, 다리는 휘었으나, 제 발과 발목이 곱다는 것을 자랑스러워하여 늘 가터를 두른 무릎을 어루만지곤 했다."]], [["\"And so the shepherd is not enough,\"", "\"그래, 양치기로는 부족하다는 거요.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, \"to defend his lambkin?\"", "\"제 어린 양을 지키기에 말이오?\""]], [["\"The shepherd is too fond of playing at cards and going to his clubs,\"", "\"양치기가 카드놀이며 클럽 출입을 너무 좋아해서 탈이지요.\""], ["answered Becky, laughing.", "베키가 웃으며 대꾸했다."]], [["\"'Gad, what a debauched Corydon!\"", "\"저런, 어찌 그리 방탕한 코리돈이람!\""], ["said my lord--\"what a mouth for a pipe!\"", "경이 말했다. \"피리나 불 입이로군!\""]], [["\"I take your three to two,\"", "\"두 점 받고 석 점 부르겠소.\""], ["here said Rawdon, at the card-table.", "카드 탁자에서 로던이 끼어들었다."]], [["\"Hark at Meliboeus,\"", "\"멜리보이우스 좀 보게나.\""], ["snarled the noble marquis; \"he's pastorally occupied too: he's shearing a Southdown.", "고귀하신 후작이 으르렁대듯 말했다. \"저 사람도 목가적인 일에 몰두하고 있군."], ["What an innocent mutton, hey?", "사우스다운의 털을 깎는 중이지. 어찌나 천진한 양인지, 응?"], ["Damme, what a snowy fleece!\"", "빌어먹을, 어찌나 새하얀 양털인지!\""]], [["Rebecca's eyes shot out gleams of scornful humour.", "* 코리돈, 멜리보이우스: 베르길리우스 목가시에 나오는 양치기들의 이름. 사우스다운은 양 품종 이름이기도 하여 말장난이 된다."], ["\"My lord,\"", "레베카의 두 눈이 경멸 어린 익살의 광채를 쏘아 보냈다."], ["she said, \"you are a knight of the Order.\"", "\"각하.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["He had the collar round his neck, indeed--a gift of the restored princes of Spain.", "\"각하야말로 그 기사단의 기사이시지요.\" 과연 그는 목에 그 목걸이를 두르고 있었으니, 복위한 에스파냐 왕가가 내린 선물이었다."]], [["Lord Steyne in early life had been notorious for his daring and his success at play.", "스테인 경은 젊은 시절 노름판에서의 대담함과 승운으로 악명이 자자했다. 그는 폭스 씨와 더불어 꼬박 이틀 밤낮을 주사위 노름에 매달려 앉아 있은 적도 있었다."], ["He had sat up two days and two nights with Mr. Fox at hazard.", "이 나라에서 가장 존엄한 인물들에게서 돈을 따냈고, 소문에 따르면 자신의 후작 작위마저 노름판에서 따냈다고 했다."], ["He had won money of the most august personages of the realm: he had won his marquisate, it was said, at the gaming-table; but he did not like an allusion to those bygone fredaines.", "그러나 그는 그 옛 방종을 들먹이는 것을 좋아하지 않았다. 레베카는 그 묵직한 이마 위로 노여운 빛이 서리는 것을 보았다."], ["Rebecca saw the scowl gathering over his heavy brow.", "* 프르데느: 젊은 시절의 방탕한 짓, 무모한 장난을 뜻하는 프랑스어."]], [["She rose up from her sofa and went and took his coffee cup out of his hand with a little curtsey. \"Yes,\"", "그녀는 소파에서 일어나 다가가서는, 가볍게 무릎을 굽혀 절하며 그의 손에서 커피잔을 받아 들었다."], ["she said, \"I must get a watchdog.", "\"그래요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["But he won't bark at YOU.\"", "\"나는 파수견을 한 마리 들여야겠어요."], ["And, going into the other drawing-room, she sat down to the piano and began to sing little French songs in such a charming, thrilling voice that the mollified nobleman speedily followed her into that chamber, and might be seen nodding his head and bowing time over her.", "하지만 그 개가 각하께 짖는 일은 없을 거예요.\" 그러고는 옆 응접실로 가 피아노 앞에 앉더니, 어찌나 매혹적이고 가슴 떨리는 목소리로 자그마한 프랑스 노래들을 부르기 시작하던지, 마음이 누그러진 귀족은 부랴부랴 그녀를 따라 그 방으로 들어갔고, 이내 그녀 위로 고개를 끄덕이며 박자에 맞춰 몸을 굽실대는 모습을 볼 수 있었다."]], [["Rawdon and his friend meanwhile played ecarte until they had enough.", "그러는 동안 로던과 그 친구는 진력이 날 때까지 에카르테를 쳤다."], ["The Colonel won; but, say that he won ever so much and often, nights like these, which occurred many times in the week--his wife having all the talk and all the admiration, and he sitting silent without the circle, not comprehending a word of the jokes, the allusions, the mystical language within--must have been rather wearisome to the ex-dragoon.", "대령이 땄다. 그러나 그가 아무리 많이, 아무리 자주 딴다 한들, 일주일에도 몇 번씩 되풀이되는 이런 밤들이란—아내가 온갖 화제와 온갖 찬사를 독차지하고, 자기는 그 무리 바깥에 말없이 앉아 그 안에서 오가는 농담이며 빗댄 말이며 알 듯 모를 듯한 언사를 한마디도 알아듣지 못한 채 있어야 하는 이런 밤들이란—전직 용기병에게는 자못 따분한 노릇이었으리라."]], [["\"How is Mrs. Crawley's husband?\"", "\"크롤리 부인의 바깥양반은 안녕하신가?\""], ["Lord Steyne used to say to him by way of a good day when they met; and indeed that was now his avocation in life.", "스테인 경은 그와 마주칠 때면 인사 삼아 이렇게 말하곤 했다. 그리고 사실 그것이 이제 그의 인생살이가 된 셈이었다."], ["He was Colonel Crawley no more.", "그는 더 이상 크롤리 대령이 아니었다."], ["He was Mrs. Crawley's husband.", "그는 크롤리 부인의 바깥양반이었다."]], [["About the little Rawdon, if nothing has been said all this while, it is because he is hidden upstairs in a garret somewhere, or has crawled below into the kitchen for companionship.", "어린 로던에 관해 여태 한마디도 없었다면, 그건 그 아이가 위층 어느 다락방엔가 숨겨져 있거나, 동무가 그리워 아래층 부엌으로 기어 내려가 있기 때문이다."], ["His mother scarcely ever took notice of him.", "어머니는 그 아이에게 좀처럼 눈길을 주는 법이 없었다."], ["He passed the days with his French bonne as long as that domestic remained in Mr. Crawley's family, and when the Frenchwoman went away, the little fellow, howling in the loneliness of the night, had compassion taken on him by a housemaid, who took him out of his solitary nursery into her bed in the garret hard by and comforted him.", "아이는 프랑스인 유모가 크롤리 씨 집에 머무는 동안에는 그 유모와 더불어 나날을 보냈고, 그 프랑스 여자가 떠나자, 밤의 외로움 속에서 울부짖던 그 어린것을 어느 하녀가 가엾이 여겨, 그 호젓한 육아방에서 데리고 나와 바로 곁 다락방 제 침대에 뉘고 달래 주었다."]], [["Rebecca, my Lord Steyne, and one or two more were in the drawing-room taking tea after the opera, when this shouting was heard overhead.", "이 울음소리가 머리 위에서 들려왔을 때, 레베카와 스테인 경, 그리고 한두 사람이 더 오페라가 끝난 뒤 응접실에서 차를 들고 있었다."], ["\"It's my cherub crying for his nurse,\"", "\"내 천사가 유모를 찾으며 우는 거예요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["She did not offer to move to go and see the child.", "그녀는 아이를 보러 가겠다고 선뜻 나서지 않았다. \"아이를 찾아간다고 공연히 마음 쓸 것 없소.\""], ["\"Don't agitate your feelings by going to look for him,\"", "스테인 경이 냉소조로 말했다. \"쳇!\""], ["said Lord Steyne sardonically. \"Bah!\"", "다른 쪽이 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 대꾸했다."], ["replied the other, with a sort of blush, \"he'll cry himself to sleep\"; and they fell to talking about the opera.", "\"울다가 제풀에 잠들 거예요.\" 그러고는 그들은 오페라 이야기로 빠져들었다."]], [["Rawdon had stolen off though, to look after his son and heir; and came back to the company when he found that honest Dolly was consoling the child.", "그러나 로던은 슬그머니 자리를 빠져나가 제 아들이자 후사를 살피러 갔다. 그리고 마음씨 고운 돌리가 아이를 달래고 있는 것을 보고서야 손님들 곁으로 돌아왔다."], ["The Colonel's dressing-room was in those upper regions.", "대령의 화장방이 그 위층에 있었다."], ["He used to see the boy there in private.", "그는 거기서 남몰래 아이를 보곤 했다."], ["They had interviews together every morning when he shaved; Rawdon minor sitting on a box by his father's side and watching the operation with never-ceasing pleasure.", "그가 면도하는 아침마다 둘은 만남을 가졌다. 어린 로던은 아버지 곁 상자 위에 앉아 그 면도하는 모습을 끝없는 즐거움으로 지켜보았다."], ["He and the sire were great friends.", "그 아이와 아비는 둘도 없는 동무였다."], ["The father would bring him sweetmeats from the dessert and hide them in a certain old epaulet box, where the child went to seek them, and laughed with joy on discovering the treasure; laughed, but not too loud: for mamma was below asleep and must not be disturbed.", "아버지는 후식 중에서 단것을 가져다가 낡은 견장 상자 속에 감춰 두곤 했고, 아이는 그것을 찾으러 가서 보물을 발견하고는 기뻐 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 웃되, 너무 큰 소리로는 웃지 않았다."], ["She did not go to rest till very late and seldom rose till after noon.", "엄마가 아래층에서 자고 있으니 깨워서는 안 되었던 것이다. 엄마는 아주 늦어서야 잠자리에 들었고 정오가 지나서야 겨우 일어나는 일이 잦았다."]], [["Rawdon bought the boy plenty of picture-books and crammed his nursery with toys.", "로던은 아이에게 그림책을 잔뜩 사 주고 육아방을 장난감으로 가득 채웠다."], ["Its walls were covered with pictures pasted up by the father's own hand and purchased by him for ready money. When he was off duty with Mrs.", "방의 벽은 아버지가 손수 풀로 붙인, 그가 현금으로 사들인 그림들로 뒤덮여 있었다."], ["Rawdon in the park, he would sit up here, passing hours with the boy; who rode on his chest, who pulled his great mustachios as if they were driving-reins, and spent days with him in indefatigable gambols.", "로던 부인과 더불어 공원에 나가지 않는 비번일 때면, 그는 이 위층에 들어앉아 아이와 더불어 몇 시간씩 보냈다. 아이는 그의 가슴팍에 올라타고, 그의 커다란 콧수염을 마치 마차 고삐라도 되는 양 잡아당겼으며, 그와 함께 지칠 줄 모르고 뛰놀며 하루를 보냈다."], ["The room was a low room, and once, when the child was not five years old, his father, who was tossing him wildly up in his arms, hit the poor little chap's skull so violently against the ceiling that he almost dropped the child, so terrified was he at the disaster.", "천장이 낮은 방이었던 터라, 아이가 채 다섯 살도 되기 전 어느 날, 아버지가 아이를 두 팔로 거칠게 위로 던져 올리다가 그 가엾은 어린것의 머리통을 천장에 어찌나 세게 부딪쳤던지, 그 변고에 어찌나 질겁했던지 하마터면 아이를 떨어뜨릴 뻔했다."]], [["Rawdon minor had made up his face for a tremendous howl--the severity of the blow indeed authorized that indulgence; but just as he was going to begin, the father interposed.", "어린 로던은 어마어마하게 울어 댈 양으로 얼굴을 잔뜩 찌푸렸다—사실 그만큼 세게 부딪쳤으니 그렇게 울어 댈 만도 했다. 그러나 막 울음을 터뜨리려는 찰나, 아버지가 가로막았다."]], [["\"For God's sake, Rawdy, don't wake Mamma,\"", "\"제발, 로디, 엄마 깨우지 마라.\""], ["he cried.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["And the child, looking in a very hard and piteous way at his father, bit his lips, clenched his hands, and didn't cry a bit.", "그러자 아이는 아주 모질고도 가련한 눈빛으로 아버지를 바라보더니, 입술을 깨물고 두 주먹을 꼭 쥔 채, 조금도 울지 않았다."], ["Rawdon told that story at the clubs, at the mess, to everybody in town.", "로던은 그 이야기를 클럽에서도, 장교 식당에서도, 온 동네 누구에게나 떠벌렸다."], ["\"By Gad, sir,\"", "\"맹세코, 여보게.\""], ["he explained to the public in general, \"what a good plucked one that boy of mine is--what a trump he is!", "그는 세상 사람들에게 두루 설명했다. \"우리 애가 어찌나 배짱 좋은 녀석인지—어찌나 대단한 놈인지!"], ["I half-sent his head through the ceiling, by Gad, and he wouldn't cry for fear of disturbing his mother.\"", "맹세코, 그 머리통을 거의 천장에 처박을 뻔했는데도, 글쎄 제 어미 깨울까 봐 울지를 않더라니까.\""]], [["Sometimes--once or twice in a week--that lady visited the upper regions in which the child lived.", "이따금—일주일에 한두 번쯤—그 부인이 아이가 사는 위층을 찾아왔다."], ["She came like a vivified figure out of the Magasin des Modes--blandly smiling in the most beautiful new clothes and little gloves and boots.", "그녀는 마치 「유행 백화점」에서 막 살아 나온 인물처럼, 더없이 아름다운 새 옷에 자그마한 장갑과 부츠 차림으로 환하게 미소 지으며 나타났다."], ["Wonderful scarfs, laces, and jewels glittered about her.", "놀라운 스카프와 레이스, 보석들이 그녀의 몸에 반짝였다."], ["She had always a new bonnet on, and flowers bloomed perpetually in it, or else magnificent curling ostrich feathers, soft and snowy as camellias.", "늘 새 보닛을 쓰고 있었고, 거기에는 꽃이 끊임없이 피어 있거나, 아니면 동백처럼 보드랍고 새하얀, 으리으리하게 굽이치는 타조 깃털이 꽂혀 있었다."], ["She nodded twice or thrice patronizingly to the little boy, who looked up from his dinner or from the pictures of soldiers he was painting.", "그녀는 저녁밥을 먹다가, 혹은 군인 그림을 색칠하다가 고개를 든 어린 아들에게 짐짓 생색내듯 두세 번 고개를 끄덕여 보였다."], ["When she left the room, an odour of rose, or some other magical fragrance, lingered about the nursery.", "그녀가 방을 나가고 나면 장미 향이나 그 밖의 마법 같은 향내가 육아방에 감돌았다."], ["She was an unearthly being in his eyes, superior to his father--to all the world: to be worshipped and admired at a distance.", "아이의 눈에 그녀는 이 세상 것이 아닌 존재였으니, 아버지보다도—온 세상 누구보다도 위에 있어, 멀찍이서 우러르고 흠모해야 할 존재였다."], ["To drive with that lady in the carriage was an awful rite: he sat up in the back seat and did not dare to speak: he gazed with all his eyes at the beautifully dressed Princess opposite to him.", "그 부인과 마차를 타고 나가는 것은 엄숙한 의식이었다. 아이는 뒷자리에 꼿꼿이 앉아 감히 입도 떼지 못한 채, 맞은편의 아름답게 차려입은 공주님을 두 눈 가득 응시했다."], ["Gentlemen on splendid prancing horses came up and smiled and talked with her.", "으리으리하게 껑충대는 말을 탄 신사들이 다가와 미소 지으며 그녀와 이야기를 나누었다."], ["How her eyes beamed upon all of them!", "그 신사들 모두에게 그녀의 눈은 어찌나 환히 빛나던지!"], ["Her hand used to quiver and wave gracefully as they passed.", "그들이 지나갈 때면 그녀의 손은 우아하게 떨리듯 나풀거렸다."], ["When he went out with her he had his new red dress on.", "아이는 그녀와 함께 외출할 때면 새 빨간 옷을 입었다."], ["His old brown holland was good enough when he stayed at home.", "집에 있을 때는 낡은 갈색 무명옷으로 족했다."], ["Sometimes, when she was away, and Dolly his maid was making his bed, he came into his mother's room.", "이따금 그녀가 외출하고 없을 때, 하녀 돌리가 침대를 정리하노라면, 아이는 어머니 방으로 들어가 보았다."], ["It was as the abode of a fairy to him--a mystic chamber of splendour and delights.", "아이에게 그곳은 요정의 거처와도 같았다—찬란함과 환희가 깃든 신비로운 방이었다."], ["There in the wardrobe hung those wonderful robes--pink and blue and many-tinted.", "그 옷장에는 그 놀라운 옷들이—분홍빛, 푸른빛, 갖가지 빛깔의 옷들이 걸려 있었다."], ["There was the jewel-case, silver-clasped, and the wondrous bronze hand on the dressing-table, glistening all over with a hundred rings.", "은 걸쇠가 달린 보석함도, 화장대 위에서 백 개의 반지로 온통 번쩍이는 그 신비로운 청동 손 모양 장식도 있었다."], ["There was the cheval-glass, that miracle of art, in which he could just see his own wondering head and the reflection of Dolly (queerly distorted, and as if up in the ceiling), plumping and patting the pillows of the bed.", "그리고 그 예술의 기적이라 할 체경이 있어, 거기에 어리둥절해하는 제 머리통과, 침대 베개를 두드려 부풀리는 돌리의 모습(괴상하게 일그러진 채, 마치 천장 위에 있기라도 한 듯이)이 비치는 것을 아이는 겨우 들여다볼 수 있었다."], ["Oh, thou poor lonely little benighted boy!", "오, 가엾고 외롭고 앞 못 보는 어린것이여!"], ["Mother is the name for God in the lips and hearts of little children; and here was one who was worshipping a stone!", "어린아이들의 입과 마음에서 어머니란 곧 하느님을 일컫는 이름이거늘, 여기 돌덩이를 떠받드는 한 아이가 있었구나!"]], [["Now Rawdon Crawley, rascal as the Colonel was, had certain manly tendencies of affection in his heart and could love a child and a woman still.", "그런데 로던 크롤리는, 비록 대령이 불한당이긴 했어도, 마음속에 사내다운 정의 기미를 얼마간 지니고 있어, 아이 하나와 여자 하나만은 여전히 사랑할 줄 알았다."], ["For Rawdon minor he had a great secret tenderness then, which did not escape Rebecca, though she did not talk about it to her husband.", "그는 그 무렵 어린 로던에게 남모를 깊은 애정을 품고 있었는데, 비록 아내에게 그 이야기를 떠벌리지는 않았지만 레베카의 눈을 피하지는 못했다."], ["It did not annoy her: she was too good-natured.", "그래도 그녀는 언짢아하지 않았다. 그러기에는 천성이 너무 무던했다."], ["It only increased her scorn for him.", "그 일은 그저 그에 대한 그녀의 경멸을 더할 뿐이었다."], ["He felt somehow ashamed of this paternal softness and hid it from his wife--only indulging in it when alone with the boy.", "그는 어쩐지 이 아비로서의 다정함이 부끄러워, 아내에게는 그것을 숨긴 채, 아이와 단둘이 있을 때만 마음껏 누렸다."]], [["He used to take him out of mornings when they would go to the stables together and to the park.", "그는 아침이면 아이를 데리고 나가 함께 마구간으로, 또 공원으로 가곤 했다."], ["Little Lord Southdown, the best-natured of men, who would make you a present of the hat from his head, and whose main occupation in life was to buy knick-knacks that he might give them away afterwards, bought the little chap a pony not much bigger than a large rat, the donor said, and on this little black Shetland pygmy young Rawdon's great father was pleased to mount the boy, and to walk by his side in the park.", "사람 좋기로는 으뜸가는, 제 머리에 쓴 모자라도 선뜻 선물로 줄 위인이요, 평생의 주된 일이라곤 나중에 남에게 줄 자질구레한 물건들을 사들이는 것뿐인 자그마한 사우스다운 경은, 그 어린것에게 조랑말 한 마리를 사 주었는데, 주는 이의 말로는 큰 쥐만 한 크기밖에 안 되는 놈이었다. 어린 로던의 위대한 아버지는 이 자그마한 검은 셰틀랜드산 난쟁이 말에 아이를 기꺼이 태우고는, 공원에서 그 곁을 나란히 걸었다."], ["It pleased him to see his old quarters, and his old fellow-guardsmen at Knightsbridge: he had begun to think of his bachelorhood with something like regret.", "옛 주둔지를, 그리고 나이츠브리지의 옛 근위대 동료들을 보는 것이 그에게는 흐뭇했다. 그는 독신 시절을 얼마간 아쉬움 비슷한 마음으로 떠올리기 시작하던 참이었다."], ["The old troopers were glad to recognize their ancient officer and dandle the little colonel.", "옛 기병들은 자기네 옛 장교를 알아보고 그 어린 대령을 어르며 반가워했다. 크롤리 대령은 장교 식당에서, 또 동료 장교들과 더불어 식사하는 것이 무척 즐거웠다."], ["Colonel Crawley found dining at mess and with his brother-officers very pleasant.", "\"젠장, 난 그 사람한테 어울릴 만큼 영리하질 못해—나도 알아."], ["\"Hang it, I ain't clever enough for her--I know it.", "그 사람은 내가 없어도 아쉬워하지 않을걸.\""], ["She won't miss me,\"", "그는 이렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["he used to say: and he was right, his wife did not miss him.", "그리고 그 말이 옳아서, 아내는 그가 없어도 아쉬워하지 않았다."]], [["Rebecca was fond of her husband.", "레베카는 남편을 아꼈다."], ["She was always perfectly good-humoured and kind to him.", "그녀는 그에게 늘 더없이 무던하고 다정했다."], ["She did not even show her scorn much for him; perhaps she liked him the better for being a fool.", "그에 대한 경멸조차 좀처럼 드러내지 않았다. 어쩌면 그가 바보였기에 그를 더 좋아했는지도 모른다."], ["He was her upper servant and maitre d'hotel.", "그는 그녀의 우두머리 하인이요 집사장이었다."], ["He went on her errands; obeyed her orders without question; drove in the carriage in the ring with her without repining; took her to the opera-box, solaced himself at his club during the performance, and came punctually back to fetch her when due.", "그는 그녀의 심부름을 다녔고, 그녀의 분부에 두말없이 따랐으며, 군소리 없이 그녀와 더불어 마차를 몰아 승마장을 돌았고, 오페라 특별석까지 그녀를 데려다준 뒤 공연이 진행되는 동안에는 클럽에서 마음을 달래다가, 때가 되면 어김없이 그녀를 데리러 돌아왔다."], ["He would have liked her to be a little fonder of the boy, but even to that he reconciled himself.", "그는 아내가 아이를 조금만 더 사랑해 주기를 바랐지만, 그것마저도 체념하고 받아들였다."], ["\"Hang it, you know she's so clever,\"", "\"젠장, 알다시피 그 사람은 어찌나 영리한지.\""], ["he said, \"and I'm not literary and that, you know.\"", "그가 말했다. \"한데 나는 글재주도 그런 것도 없고 말이야, 알잖나.\""], ["For, as we have said before, it requires no great wisdom to be able to win at cards and billiards, and Rawdon made no pretensions to any other sort of skill.", "앞서 말했듯, 카드와 당구에서 이기는 데는 대단한 지혜가 필요치 않거니와, 로던은 그 밖의 어떤 솜씨도 내세우는 법이 없었다."]], [["When the companion came, his domestic duties became very light.", "말동무가 들어오자 그의 집안일은 무척 가벼워졌다."], ["His wife encouraged him to dine abroad: she would let him off duty at the opera.", "아내는 그가 밖에서 식사하도록 부추겼고, 오페라에서도 그의 임무를 면제해 주곤 했다."], ["\"Don't stay and stupefy yourself at home to-night, my dear,\"", "\"오늘 밤엔 집에 남아 멍청하게 굴 것 없어요, 여보.\""], ["she would say.", "그녀는 이렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["\"Some men are coming who will only bore you.", "\"당신은 따분하기만 할 사람들이 올 거예요."], ["I would not ask them, but you know it's for your good, and now I have a sheep-dog, I need not be afraid to be alone.\"", "안 부르고 싶지만, 다 당신 잘되라고 그러는 거 알잖아요. 게다가 이제 나한테 양치기 개가 생겼으니, 혼자 있어도 무서울 게 없고요.\""]], [["\"A sheep-dog--a companion!", "'양치기 개라니—말동무라니!"], ["Becky Sharp with a companion!", "베키 샤프한테 말동무가 다 있다니!"], ["Isn't it good fun?\"", "이거 참 우습지 않아?'"], ["thought Mrs. Crawley to herself.", "크롤리 부인은 속으로 생각했다."], ["The notion tickled hugely her sense of humour.", "그 발상이 그녀의 유머 감각을 무던히도 간질였다."]], [["One Sunday morning, as Rawdon Crawley, his little son, and the pony were taking their accustomed walk in the park, they passed by an old acquaintance of the Colonel's, Corporal Clink, of the regiment, who was in conversation with a friend, an old gentleman, who held a boy in his arms about the age of little Rawdon.", "어느 일요일 아침, 로던 크롤리와 그 어린 아들, 그리고 조랑말이 여느 때처럼 공원을 거닐다가, 대령의 옛 지인인 연대의 클링크 상병 곁을 지나게 되었다. 그는 한 친구, 곧 어린 로던과 비슷한 또래의 사내아이를 품에 안은 어느 노신사와 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["This other youngster had seized hold of the Waterloo medal which the Corporal wore, and was examining it with delight.", "그 다른 어린것은 상병이 단 워털루 훈장을 움켜쥐고는 신이 나서 들여다보던 참이었다."]], [["\"Good morning, your Honour,\"", "\"안녕하십니까, 나리.\""], ["said Clink, in reply to the \"How do, Clink?\" of the Colonel.", "클링크가 대령의 \"잘 지냈나, 클링크?\"라는 인사에 답하여 말했다."], ["\"This ere young gentleman is about the little Colonel's age, sir,\"", "\"이 어린 신사분이 우리 어린 대령님과 엇비슷한 또래입죠, 나리.\""], ["continued the corporal.", "상병이 말을 이었다."]], [["\"His father was a Waterloo man, too,\"", "\"이 애 아버지도 워털루에 참전한 사람이었지요.\""], ["said the old gentleman, who carried the boy.", "아이를 안은 노신사가 말했다."], ["\"Wasn't he, Georgy?\"", "\"그렇지, 조지?\""]], [["\"Yes,\"", "\"네.\""], ["said Georgy.", "조지가 말했다."], ["He and the little chap on the pony were looking at each other with all their might--solemnly scanning each other as children do.", "그 아이와 조랑말 위의 어린것은 서로를 있는 힘껏 바라보고 있었다—아이들이 으레 그러듯 진지하게 서로를 뜯어보면서."]], [["\"In a line regiment,\"", "\"보병 연대였습죠.\""], ["Clink said with a patronizing air.", "클링크가 으스대는 투로 말했다."]], [["\"He was a Captain in the --th regiment,\"", "\"제 사위는 제××연대의 대위였소이다.\""], ["said the old gentleman rather pompously.", "노신사가 다소 거드름을 피우며 말했다."], ["\"Captain George Osborne, sir--perhaps you knew him.", "\"조지 오스본 대위라는 사람이지요, 나리—혹 아셨을지도 모르겠소."], ["He died the death of a hero, sir, fighting against the Corsican tyrant.\"", "그 사람은 영웅답게 죽었소이다, 나리, 코르시카의 폭군에 맞서 싸우다 말이오.\""], ["Colonel Crawley blushed quite red.", "크롤리 대령은 얼굴이 새빨개졌다."], ["\"I knew him very well, sir,\"", "\"그 사람이라면 아주 잘 알았지요, 어르신.\""], ["he said, \"and his wife, his dear little wife, sir--how is she?\"", "그가 말했다. \"그리고 그 사람 아내, 그 사랑스럽고 자그마한 부인도요, 어르신—그분은 어떻게 지내십니까?\""]], [["\"She is my daughter, sir,\"", "\"그 애가 제 딸이올시다, 나리.\""], ["said the old gentleman, putting down the boy and taking out a card with great solemnity, which he handed to the Colonel.", "노신사가 아이를 내려놓고는 자못 엄숙하게 명함 한 장을 꺼내 대령에게 건네며 말했다."], ["On it written--", "거기에는 이렇게 적혀 있었다—"]], [["\"Mr.", "\"세들리."], ["Sedley, Sole Agent for the Black Diamond and Anti-Cinder Coal Association, Bunker's Wharf, Thames Street, and Anna-Maria Cottages, Fulham Road West.\"", "흑다이아몬드 무재석탄 협회 단독 대리인. 템스 가 벙커스 부두 및 풀럼 로드 웨스트 애나마리아 코티지.\""]], [["Little Georgy went up and looked at the Shetland pony.", "어린 조지가 다가가 셰틀랜드 조랑말을 들여다보았다."]], [["\"Should you like to have a ride?\"", "\"한번 타 볼래?\""], ["said Rawdon minor from the saddle.", "안장에 앉은 어린 로던이 말했다."]], [["\"Yes,\"", "\"응.\""], ["said Georgy.", "조지가 말했다."], ["The Colonel, who had been looking at him with some interest, took up the child and put him on the pony behind Rawdon minor.", "얼마간 흥미롭게 그 아이를 바라보던 대령은 아이를 안아 올려 어린 로던 뒤에 태워 주었다."]], [["\"Take hold of him, Georgy,\"", "\"이 애를 꽉 붙들어, 조지.\""], ["he said--\"take my little boy round the waist--his name is Rawdon.\"", "그가 말했다—\"우리 어린 아들 허리를 잡으렴—얘 이름은 로던이란다.\""], ["And both the children began to laugh.", "그러자 두 아이가 다 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["\"You won't see a prettier pair I think, THIS summer's day, sir,\"", "\"이 여름날, 이보다 더 어여쁜 한 쌍은 못 보실 겁니다, 나리.\""], ["said the good-natured Corporal; and the Colonel, the Corporal, and old Mr.", "사람 좋은 상병이 말했다."], ["Sedley with his umbrella, walked by the side of the children.", "그리고 대령과 상병, 그리고 우산을 든 늙은 세들리 씨는 두 아이 곁을 나란히 걸어갔다."]], [["A Family in a Very Small Way\nWe must suppose little George Osborne has ridden from Knightsbridge towards Fulham, and will stop and make inquiries at that village regarding some friends whom we have left there.", "어린 조지 오즈번이 나이츠브리지에서 풀럼 쪽으로 말을 달려갔다고 치자. 그러면 그 마을에 잠시 멈춰, 우리가 거기 남겨 두고 온 몇몇 벗들의 소식을 물어보아야겠다."], ["How is Mrs. Amelia after the storm of Waterloo?", "워털루의 폭풍이 지나간 뒤 애밀리아 부인은 어떻게 지내고 있을까?"], ["Is she living and thriving?", "살아서 잘 지내고 있을까?"], ["What has come of Major Dobbin, whose cab was always hankering about her premises?", "늘 그녀의 집 근처를 어슬렁거리던 그 마차의 주인, 도빈 소령은 어찌 되었을까?"], ["And is there any news of the Collector of Boggley Wollah?", "그리고 보글리 월라의 징세관에 대한 소식은 없을까?"], ["The facts concerning the latter are briefly these:", "후자에 관한 사실은 간단히 이러하다."]], [["Our worthy fat friend Joseph Sedley returned to India not long after his escape from Brussels.", "우리의 존경스러운 뚱뚱한 벗 조지프 세들리는 브뤼셀에서 빠져나온 지 얼마 지나지 않아 인도로 돌아갔다."], ["Either his furlough was up, or he dreaded to meet any witnesses of his Waterloo flight.", "휴가가 끝났던 탓인지, 아니면 워털루에서 도망친 자신을 본 목격자들과 마주치기가 두려웠던 탓인지 모를 일이었다."], ["However it might be, he went back to his duties in Bengal very soon after Napoleon had taken up his residence at St. Helena, where Jos saw the ex-Emperor.", "어쨌거나 그는 나폴레옹이 세인트헬레나에 거처를 정한 직후 벵골의 직무로 돌아갔는데, 그곳에서 조스는 그 폐위된 황제를 보았다."], ["To hear Mr. Sedley talk on board ship you would have supposed that it was not the first time he and the Corsican had met, and that the civilian had bearded the French General at Mount St.", "세들리 씨가 배 위에서 떠드는 소리를 들었더라면, 그와 코르시카 사내가 만난 것이 이번이 처음이 아니며, 이 민간인이 몽생장에서 그 프랑스 장군에게 맞서기라도 한 줄로 알았을 것이다."], ["John. He had a thousand anecdotes about the famous battles; he knew the position of every regiment and the loss which each had incurred.", "그는 그 유명한 전투들에 대해 천 가지나 되는 일화를 늘어놓았고, 모든 연대의 위치며 각 연대가 입은 손실까지 다 꿰고 있었다."], ["He did not deny that he had been concerned in those victories--that he had been with the army and carried despatches for the Duke of Wellington.", "자신이 그 승리들에 관여했다는 것을, 군대와 함께 있으면서 웰링턴 공작의 급보를 전달했다는 것을 그는 부정하지 않았다."], ["And he described what the Duke did and said on every conceivable moment of the day of Waterloo, with such an accurate knowledge of his Grace's sentiments and proceedings that it was clear he must have been by the conqueror's side throughout the day; though, as a non-combatant, his name was not mentioned in the public documents relative to the battle.", "그리고 워털루의 그날, 생각할 수 있는 모든 순간에 공작이 무엇을 하고 무슨 말을 했는지, 각하의 심정과 행적을 어찌나 정확히 아는 듯 묘사하는지, 그가 그날 종일 그 정복자의 곁에 있었던 게 틀림없어 보일 정도였다. 비록 비전투원인지라 그 전투에 관한 공식 문서에 그의 이름은 언급되지 않았지만 말이다."], ["Perhaps he actually worked himself up to believe that he had been engaged with the army; certain it is that he made a prodigious sensation for some time at Calcutta, and was called Waterloo Sedley during the whole of his subsequent stay in Bengal.", "어쩌면 그는 정말로 스스로를 부추겨 자신이 군대와 함께 싸웠다고 믿게 되었는지도 모른다. 분명한 것은, 그가 한동안 캘커타에서 굉장한 파란을 일으켰고, 그 후 벵골에 머무는 내내 워털루 세들리라고 불렸다는 사실이다."]], [["The bills which Jos had given for the purchase of those unlucky horses were paid without question by him and his agents.", "조스가 그 불운한 말들을 사들이면서 끊어 준 어음은 그와 그의 대리인들이 군말 없이 지불했다."], ["He never was heard to allude to the bargain, and nobody knows for a certainty what became of the horses, or how he got rid of them, or of Isidor, his Belgian servant, who sold a grey horse, very like the one which Jos rode, at Valenciennes sometime during the autumn of 1815.", "그가 그 거래를 입에 올렸다는 말은 한 번도 들린 적이 없었고, 그 말들이 어찌 되었는지, 그가 그것들을 어떻게 처분했는지, 또 그의 벨기에인 하인 이지도르가 어찌 되었는지 확실히 아는 이는 아무도 없었다. 그 이지도르라는 자는 1815년 가을 어느 무렵 발랑시엔에서, 조스가 타던 것과 아주 흡사한 회색 말 한 마리를 팔아 치웠던 것이다."]], [["Jos's London agents had orders to pay one hundred and twenty pounds yearly to his parents at Fulham.", "조스의 런던 대리인들은 매년 백이십 파운드를 풀럼의 부모에게 지불하라는 지시를 받았다."], ["It was the chief support of the old couple; for Mr.", "그것이 이 노부부의 주된 생계 수단이었다."], ["Sedley's speculations in life subsequent to his bankruptcy did not by any means retrieve the broken old gentleman's fortune.", "세들리 씨가 파산 이후로 인생에서 벌인 투기들은 그 영락한 노신사의 무너진 재산을 결코 되돌려 주지 못했기 때문이다."], ["He tried to be a wine-merchant, a coal-merchant, a commission lottery agent, &c., &c.", "그는 포도주 상인, 석탄 상인, 복권 위탁 판매원 따위가 되어 보려고 애썼다."], ["He sent round prospectuses to his friends whenever he took a new trade, and ordered a new brass plate for the door, and talked pompously about making his fortune still.", "새 장사를 시작할 때마다 친구들에게 안내장을 돌렸고, 문에 달 새 놋쇠 명패를 주문했으며, 아직도 한밑천 잡겠노라고 거드름을 피우며 떠들었다."], ["But Fortune never came back to the feeble and stricken old man.", "그러나 행운은 이 쇠약하고 타격을 입은 노인에게 다시는 돌아오지 않았다."], ["One by one his friends dropped off, and were weary of buying dear coals and bad wine from him; and there was only his wife in all the world who fancied, when he tottered off to the City of a morning, that he was still doing any business there.", "친구들은 하나둘 떨어져 나갔고, 그에게서 비싼 석탄과 형편없는 포도주를 사는 데 진력이 났다. 그리고 아침이면 그가 비틀거리며 시내로 나설 때 그가 아직도 거기서 무슨 사업이라도 하고 있는 줄로 여기는 사람은 온 세상에 그의 아내뿐이었다."], ["At evening he crawled slowly back; and he used to go of nights to a little club at a tavern, where he disposed of the finances of the nation.", "저녁이면 그는 천천히 기다시피 돌아왔다. 그리고 밤이면 어느 선술집의 자그마한 클럽에 가곤 했는데, 거기서 그는 국가의 재정을 좌지우지했다."], ["It was wonderful to hear him talk about millions, and agios, and discounts, and what Rothschild was doing, and Baring Brothers.", "그가 수백만이며, 환차익이며, 할인이며, 로스차일드가 무얼 하는지, 베어링 브라더스가 어떤지를 떠드는 소리를 듣자면 참으로 놀라울 따름이었다."], ["He talked of such vast sums that the gentlemen of the club (the apothecary, the undertaker, the great carpenter and builder, the parish clerk, who was allowed to come stealthily, and Mr.", "그가 어찌나 어마어마한 액수를 입에 올리던지, 클럽의 신사들은—약제사며, 장의사며, 큰 목수이자 건축업자며, 몰래 끼어들어 오는 게 허용된 교구 서기며, 우리의 옛 지인 클랩 씨까지—그 노신사를 깍듯이 받들었다."], ["Clapp, our old acquaintance,) respected the old gentleman.", "\"나도 한때는 살림이 더 나았소이다, 선생.\""], ["\"I was better off once, sir,\"", "그는 \"그 방을 드나드는\""], ["he did not fail to tell everybody who \"used the room.\"", "누구에게나 어김없이 이렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["\"My son, sir, is at this minute chief magistrate of Ramgunge in the Presidency of Bengal, and touching his four thousand rupees per mensem.", "\"내 아들은 말이오, 선생, 지금 이 순간 벵골 관할구 람건지의 수석 행정관이라, 한 달에 사천 루피를 만지고 있다오."], ["My daughter might be a Colonel's lady if she liked.", "내 딸은 마음만 먹으면 대령 부인이 될 수도 있었지요."], ["I might draw upon my son, the first magistrate, sir, for two thousand pounds to-morrow, and Alexander would cash my bill, down sir, down on the counter, sir.", "나는 내일이라도 당장 수석 행정관인 내 아들 앞으로 이천 파운드 어음을 끊을 수 있고, 그러면 알렉산더가 그 어음을 현금으로 바꿔 줄 게요, 선생, 두말없이, 그 자리에서 말이오, 선생. 하지만 세들리 집안은 본디 자존심 강한 가문이었소이다.\""], ["But the Sedleys were always a proud family.\"", "친애하는 독자여, 그대도 나도 언젠가 이런 처지에 빠질 수 있다."], ["You and I, my dear reader, may drop into this condition one day: for have not many of our friends attained it?", "우리의 벗들 중 여럿이 이미 그 지경에 이르지 않았던가? 우리의 운이 다할 수도, 우리의 능력이 우리를 저버릴 수도, 무대 위 우리의 자리를 더 낫고 더 젊은 배우들에게 빼앗길 수도 있다."], ["Our luck may fail: our powers forsake us: our place on the boards be taken by better and younger mimes--the chance of life roll away and leave us shattered and stranded.", "인생의 기회란 굴러가 버리고 우리를 산산이 부서져 좌초된 채 남겨 두는 법이다. 그러면 사람들은 그대를 마주치면 길을 건너가 버릴 것이다."], ["Then men will walk across the road when they meet you--or, worse still, hold you out a couple of fingers and patronize you in a pitying way--then you will know, as soon as your back is turned, that your friend begins with a \"Poor devil, what imprudences he has committed, what chances that chap has thrown away!\"", "아니, 그보다 더 고약하게는, 손가락 두 개만 내밀며 가엾다는 듯 생색을 낼 것이다. 그때 그대는 알게 되리라, 그대가 등을 돌리기가 무섭게 그대의 벗이 \"딱한 친구로다, 어쩌면 그리 경솔한 짓들을 저질렀는지, 그 작자가 차 버린 기회들이 얼마나 많은지!\""], ["Well, well--a carriage and three thousand a year is not the summit of the reward nor the end of God's judgment of men.", "하고 입을 떼리라는 것을. 글쎄다, 글쎄다—마차 한 대와 연 삼천 파운드가 보상의 정점도 아니요, 인간에 대한 신의 심판의 끝도 아니다."], ["If quacks prosper as often as they go to the wall--if zanies succeed and knaves arrive at fortune, and, vice versa, sharing ill luck and prosperity for all the world like the ablest and most honest amongst us--I say, brother, the gifts and pleasures of Vanity Fair cannot be held of any great account, and that it is probable . .", "만약 돌팔이들이 망하는 만큼이나 자주 번창하고—만약 어릿광대들이 성공하고 악당들이 한밑천 잡으며, 또 그 반대로, 우리 가운데 가장 유능하고 정직한 자들과 똑같이 불운과 번영을 나누어 갖는다면—내 말하건대, 형제여, 허영의 시장이 주는 선물과 쾌락이란 결코 대단한 것으로 여길 수 없으며, 아마도……"], [". but we are wandering out of the domain of the story.", "그러나 우리는 이야기의 영역에서 벗어나 헤매고 있다."]], [["Had Mrs. Sedley been a woman of energy, she would have exerted it after her husband's ruin and, occupying a large house, would have taken in boarders.", "세들리 부인이 기운찬 여자였더라면, 남편이 파멸한 뒤에 그 기운을 발휘하여 큰 집을 얻고 하숙인을 들였을 것이다."], ["The broken Sedley would have acted well as the boarding-house landlady's husband; the Munoz of private life; the titular lord and master: the carver, house-steward, and humble husband of the occupier of the dingy throne.", "영락한 세들리는 하숙집 안주인의 남편 노릇을 곧잘 해냈을 것이다. 사생활의 무뇨스라고나 할까."], ["I have seen men of good brains and breeding, and of good hopes and vigour once, who feasted squires and kept hunters in their youth, meekly cutting up legs of mutton for rancorous old harridans and pretending to preside over their dreary tables--but Mrs.", "명목상의 주인이자 가장, 고기를 썰어 나누는 자, 집안 살림을 거드는 자, 그 칙칙한 왕좌를 차지한 여인의 비천한 남편 말이다. 나는 한때 명석한 두뇌와 좋은 가문에, 좋은 전망과 활력을 지녔던 사내들, 젊은 시절 향사들을 접대하고 사냥말을 거느렸던 사내들이, 앙심 가득한 늙은 노파들을 위해 순하게 양 다리 고기를 썰어 주며 그 음울한 식탁을 주재하는 척하는 꼴을 본 적이 있다."], ["Sedley, we say, had not spirit enough to bustle about for \"a few select inmates to join a cheerful musical family,\" such as one reads of in the Times.", "그러나 우리가 말하건대, 세들리 부인에게는 타임스에서 읽을 법한 \"유쾌한 음악 가정에 합류할 엄선된 입주자 몇 분\"을 구하러 분주히 돌아다닐 기운이 없었다."], ["She was content to lie on the shore where fortune had stranded her--and you could see that the career of this old couple was over.", "그녀는 운명이 자신을 좌초시켜 놓은 그 물가에 누워 만족했다. 그러니 이 노부부의 인생 행로가 끝났음을 누구라도 알 수 있었던 것이다."]], [["I don't think they were unhappy.", "나는 그들이 불행했다고는 생각하지 않는다."], ["Perhaps they were a little prouder in their downfall than in their prosperity. Mrs.", "어쩌면 그들은 번영하던 시절보다 영락한 처지에서 더 자존심이 셌는지도 모른다."], ["Sedley was always a great person for her landlady, Mrs. Clapp, when she descended and passed many hours with her in the basement or ornamented kitchen.", "세들리 부인은 지하실이나 잘 꾸며진 부엌으로 내려가 집주인 클랩 부인과 여러 시간을 함께 보낼 때면, 늘 클랩 부인에게 대단한 인물로 행세했다."], ["The Irish maid Betty Flanagan's bonnets and ribbons, her sauciness, her idleness, her reckless prodigality of kitchen candles, her consumption of tea and sugar, and so forth occupied and amused the old lady almost as much as the doings of her former household, when she had Sambo and the coachman, and a groom, and a footboy, and a housekeeper with a regiment of female domestics--her former household, about which the good lady talked a hundred times a day.", "아일랜드 출신 하녀 베티 플래너건의 보닛과 리본이며, 그 건방진 태도며, 게으름이며, 부엌 양초를 무모하게 펑펑 써 대는 버릇이며, 차와 설탕을 축내는 일 따위가, 옛날 자기 집안 식구들이 하던 일이나 진배없이 이 노부인을 사로잡고 즐겁게 해 주었다. 그 옛 살림이라면 샘보와 마부, 마구간지기, 사동, 그리고 한 무리의 여자 하인들을 거느린 가정부까지 있던 시절이었으니—이 마음씨 좋은 부인은 하루에도 백 번씩 그 옛 살림 이야기를 했다."], ["And besides Betty Flanagan, Mrs. Sedley had all the maids-of-all-work in the street to superintend.", "게다가 베티 플래너건 말고도, 세들리 부인은 그 거리의 잡일 하녀들을 죄다 감독했다."], ["She knew how each tenant of the cottages paid or owed his little rent. She stepped aside when Mrs.", "그녀는 오두막의 세입자 하나하나가 그 자그마한 집세를 어떻게 내고 있는지, 또 얼마나 밀렸는지까지 다 알았다."], ["Rougemont the actress passed with her dubious family. She flung up her head when Mrs.", "여배우 루즈몽 부인이 그 수상쩍은 식구들을 거느리고 지나가면 그녀는 옆으로 비켜섰다."], ["Pestler, the apothecary's lady, drove by in her husband's professional one-horse chaise.", "약제사 부인 페슬러 부인이 남편의 영업용 일두마차를 타고 지나가면 그녀는 고개를 빳빳이 쳐들었다."], ["She had colloquies with the greengrocer about the pennorth of turnips which Mr.", "세들리 씨가 좋아하는 한 푼어치 순무를 두고 채소 장수와 입씨름을 벌이는가 하면, 우유 장수와 빵집 사동을 감시하기도 했고, 정육점에 행차하기도 했다."], ["Sedley loved; she kept an eye upon the milkman and the baker's boy; and made visitations to the butcher, who sold hundreds of oxen very likely with less ado than was made about Mrs.", "그 정육점 주인은 세들리 부인의 양 허릿살 한 덩이를 두고 벌어지는 소동보다 훨씬 덜한 수선으로 황소 수백 마리를 팔아 치웠을 게 분명했다."], ["Sedley's loin of mutton: and she counted the potatoes under the joint on Sundays, on which days, dressed in her best, she went to church twice and read Blair's Sermons in the evening.", "또 그녀는 일요일이면 고깃덩이 아래 깔린 감자알을 세었는데, 그날이면 가장 좋은 옷을 차려입고 교회에 두 번 갔으며, 저녁에는 블레어의 설교집을 읽었다."]], [["On that day, for \"business\"", "그날이면—평일에는 \"사업\""], ["prevented him on weekdays from taking such a pleasure, it was old Sedley's delight to take out his little grandson Georgy to the neighbouring parks or Kensington Gardens, to see the soldiers or to feed the ducks.", "때문에 그런 즐거움을 누릴 수 없었으니—늙은 세들리는 어린 손자 조지를 데리고 근처 공원이나 켄싱턴 정원에 나가 군인들을 구경시키거나 오리에게 먹이를 주는 것을 큰 낙으로 삼았다."], ["Georgy loved the redcoats, and his grandpapa told him how his father had been a famous soldier, and introduced him to many sergeants and others with Waterloo medals on their breasts, to whom the old grandfather pompously presented the child as the son of Captain Osborne of the --th, who died gloriously on the glorious eighteenth.", "조지는 붉은 군복을 좋아했고, 할아버지는 그 애 아버지가 이름난 군인이었다는 이야기를 들려주며, 가슴에 워털루 훈장을 단 여러 하사관들과 다른 이들에게 그 애를 소개했다. 늙은 할아버지는 거드름을 피우며 그 아이를, 저 영광스러운 십팔일에 영광스럽게 전사한 ××연대 오즈번 대위의 아들이라고 소개했던 것이다."], ["He has been known to treat some of these non-commissioned gentlemen to a glass of porter, and, indeed, in their first Sunday walks was disposed to spoil little Georgy, sadly gorging the boy with apples and parliament, to the detriment of his health--until Amelia declared that George should never go out with his grandpapa unless the latter promised solemnly, and on his honour, not to give the child any cakes, lollipops, or stall produce whatever.", "그가 이 하사관 양반들 몇몇에게 흑맥주 한 잔씩을 대접한 일도 알려져 있거니와, 사실 처음 몇 번의 일요일 산책에서는 어린 조지를 응석받이로 만들어, 그 애 건강을 해칠 만큼 사과며 박하 사탕을 잔뜩 먹였다. 마침내 애밀리아가, 할아버지가 그 아이에게 어떤 케이크도, 막대 사탕도, 노점에서 파는 어떤 군것질거리도 주지 않겠노라고 명예를 걸고 엄숙히 약속하지 않는 한, 조지를 결코 할아버지와 함께 내보내지 않겠다고 선언하기에 이르렀다."]], [["Between Mrs. Sedley and her daughter there was a sort of coolness about this boy, and a secret jealousy--for one evening in George's very early days, Amelia, who had been seated at work in their little parlour scarcely remarking that the old lady had quitted the room, ran upstairs instinctively to the nursery at the cries of the child, who had been asleep until that moment--and there found Mrs.", "세들리 부인과 그녀의 딸 사이에는 이 아이를 두고 일종의 냉랭함과 은밀한 시샘이 감돌았다."], ["Sedley in the act of surreptitiously administering Daffy's Elixir to the infant. Amelia, the gentlest and sweetest of everyday mortals, when she found this meddling with her maternal authority, thrilled and trembled all over with anger.", "조지가 아주 어렸던 시절 어느 날 저녁, 작은 거실에 앉아 바느질을 하던 애밀리아가, 노부인이 방을 나간 줄도 미처 모르고 있다가, 그 순간까지 잠들어 있던 아이의 울음소리에 본능적으로 위층 육아실로 달려 올라간 일이 있었던 것이다."], ["Her cheeks, ordinarily pale, now flushed up, until they were as red as they used to be when she was a child of twelve years old.", "그리고 거기서 그녀는 세들리 부인이 갓난아기에게 몰래 대피의 영약을 먹이고 있는 광경을 목격했다. 일상의 인간들 가운데 가장 온순하고 다정한 애밀리아는, 자신의 모성적 권위를 이렇게 침범당했음을 알고는 분노로 온몸을 부르르 떨었다."], ["She seized the baby out of her mother's arms and then grasped at the bottle, leaving the old lady gaping at her, furious, and holding the guilty tea-spoon.", "여느 때는 창백하던 그녀의 두 볼이 이제 발갛게 달아올라, 열두 살 어린아이 시절 그랬던 것처럼 빨개졌다. 그녀는 어머니의 품에서 아기를 낚아챈 다음 병을 와락 붙잡았고, 노부인은 죄지은 듯한 찻숟가락을 든 채 격분하여 입을 딱 벌리고 그녀를 멍하니 바라보았다."]], [["Amelia flung the bottle crashing into the fire-place.", "애밀리아는 그 병을 벽난로 속으로 와장창 내던졌다."], ["\"I will NOT have baby poisoned, Mamma,\"", "\"엄마, 우리 아기 독살되게 둘 수 없어요.\""], ["cried Emmy, rocking the infant about violently with both her arms round him and turning with flashing eyes at her mother.", "에미가 두 팔로 아기를 감싸 안고 거칠게 어르며, 번득이는 눈으로 어머니를 돌아보고 소리쳤다."]], [["\"Poisoned, Amelia!\"", "\"독살이라니, 애밀리아!\""], ["said the old lady; \"this language to me?\"", "노부인이 말했다. \"나한테 그런 말을 해?\""]], [["\"He shall not have any medicine but that which Mr. Pestler sends for him.", "\"이 아이는 페슬러 씨가 보내 주는 약 말고는 어떤 약도 먹어선 안 돼요."], ["He told me that Daffy's Elixir was poison.\"", "그분이 대피의 영약은 독이라고 하셨다고요.\""]], [["\"Very good: you think I'm a murderess then,\"", "\"좋아, 그래, 그럼 넌 내가 살인자라고 생각하는 거구나.\" 세들리 부인이 대꾸했다."], ["replied Mrs. Sedley.", "\"이게 네 어미한테 쓰는 말투냐."], ["\"This is the language you use to your mother.", "내가 불운을 겪었지. 인생에서 밑바닥까지 떨어졌어."], ["I have met with misfortunes: I have sunk low in life: I have kept my carriage, and now walk on foot: but I did not know I was a murderess before, and thank you for the NEWS.\"", "마차를 굴리던 몸이 이제 두 발로 걸어 다니지. 그래도 내가 살인자인 줄은 여태 몰랐구나, 그 소식 일러 줘서 고맙다.\""]], [["\"Mamma,\"", "\"엄마.\""], ["said the poor girl, who was always ready for tears--\"you shouldn't be hard upon me.", "늘 눈물을 쏟을 준비가 되어 있는 이 가엾은 딸이 말했다. \"저한테 너무 모질게 굴지 마세요."], ["I--I didn't mean--I mean, I did not wish to say you would do any wrong to this dear child, only--\"", "저는—저는 그런 뜻이 아니었어요—제 말은, 엄마가 이 소중한 아이한테 무슨 해를 끼친다는 뜻이 아니라, 다만—\""]], [["\"Oh, no, my love,--only that I was a murderess; in which case I had better go to the Old Bailey.", "\"오, 아니지, 얘야—다만 내가 살인자라는 거지. 그렇다면 차라리 올드 베일리로 가는 게 낫겠구나."], ["Though I didn't poison YOU, when you were a child, but gave you the best of education and the most expensive masters money could procure.", "하기야 난 네가 어릴 적에 독을 먹이기는커녕, 돈으로 구할 수 있는 가장 좋은 교육과 가장 비싼 선생들을 붙여 주었지만 말이다."], ["Yes; I've nursed five children and buried three; and the one I loved the best of all, and tended through croup, and teething, and measles, and hooping-cough, and brought up with foreign masters, regardless of expense, and with accomplishments at Minerva House--which I never had when I was a girl--when I was too glad to honour my father and mother, that I might live long in the land, and to be useful, and not to mope all day in my room and act the fine lady--says I'm a murderess.", "그래, 나는 다섯 아이를 길러 셋을 묻었어. 그리고 그중에서도 내가 가장 사랑했던 아이, 후두염이며 이앓이며 홍역이며 백일해를 다 거두며 보살폈고, 비용 따위 아랑곳없이 외국인 선생들을 붙이고, 미네르바 학원에서—내가 처녀 시절엔 받아 본 적도 없던 온갖 교양을 배우게 해 준 그 아이가—오래오래 이 땅에서 살고자 부모를 공경하고, 쓸모 있는 사람이 되려 하고, 종일 제 방에서 시무룩하니 귀부인 행세나 하지 않던 그 시절의 나로서는 그저 감지덕지였던 그 자식이—이제 와서 나더러 살인자라는구나."], ["Ah, Mrs. Osborne!", "아, 오즈번 부인!"], ["may YOU never nourish a viper in your bosom, that's MY prayer.\"", "부디 그대만은 가슴속에 독사를 품어 기르는 일이 없기를, 그게 내 기도라오.\""]], [["\"Mamma, Mamma!\"", "\"엄마, 엄마!\""], ["cried the bewildered girl; and the child in her arms set up a frantic chorus of shouts.", "어쩔 줄 모르는 딸이 부르짖었다. 그녀의 품에 안긴 아이도 미친 듯이 합창하듯 울음을 터뜨렸다."], ["\"A murderess, indeed!", "\"살인자라니, 정말로!"], ["Go down on your knees and pray to God to cleanse your wicked ungrateful heart, Amelia, and may He forgive you as I do.\"", "무릎 꿇고 앉아, 그 사악하고 배은망덕한 마음을 깨끗이 씻어 달라고 하느님께 기도해라, 애밀리아. 그리고 내가 너를 용서하듯, 하느님께서도 너를 용서하시기를.\""], ["And Mrs. Sedley tossed out of the room, hissing out the word poison once more, and so ending her charitable benediction.", "그러고는 세들리 부인이 다시 한번 독이라는 말을 쉭 내뱉으며 방에서 휙 나가 버렸으니, 이로써 자비로운 축복을 끝맺은 것이었다."]], [["Till the termination of her natural life, this breach between Mrs. Sedley and her daughter was never thoroughly mended.", "세들리 부인이 천수를 다할 때까지, 그녀와 딸 사이의 이 균열은 끝내 온전히 메워지지 않았다."], ["The quarrel gave the elder lady numberless advantages which she did not fail to turn to account with female ingenuity and perseverance.", "이 다툼은 노부인에게 무수한 이점을 안겨 주었고, 그녀는 그것을 여자 특유의 영악함과 끈기로 빠짐없이 활용했다."], ["For instance, she scarcely spoke to Amelia for many weeks afterwards.", "이를테면, 그 후로 그녀는 여러 주 동안 애밀리아에게 거의 말을 걸지 않았다."], ["She warned the domestics not to touch the child, as Mrs. Osborne might be offended.", "그녀는 하인들에게, 오즈번 부인이 노여워할지 모르니 아이를 건드리지 말라고 일렀다."], ["She asked her daughter to see and satisfy herself that there was no poison prepared in the little daily messes that were concocted for Georgy.", "그녀는 딸에게, 조지를 위해 매일 끓이는 그 자그마한 죽에 독이 들어 있지 않은지 직접 보고 확인하라고 청했다."], ["When neighbours asked after the boy's health, she referred them pointedly to Mrs. Osborne.", "이웃들이 아이의 건강을 물으면, 그녀는 들으라는 듯이 오즈번 부인에게 물어보라고 했다."], ["SHE never ventured to ask whether the baby was well or not.", "그녀는 아기가 잘 있는지 어떤지 감히 묻는 법이 없었다."], ["SHE would not touch the child although he was her grandson, and own precious darling, for she was not USED to children, and might kill it. And whenever Mr.", "그녀는 그 아이가 제 손자요 더없이 소중한 자기 핏줄인데도 손도 대려 하지 않았는데, 아이를 다루는 데 익숙하지 않아 자칫 죽일지도 모른다는 까닭에서였다."], ["Pestler came upon his healing inquisition, she received the doctor with such a sarcastic and scornful demeanour, as made the surgeon declare that not Lady Thistlewood herself, whom he had the honour of attending professionally, could give herself greater airs than old Mrs. Sedley, from whom he never took a fee.", "그리고 페슬러 씨가 치료차 진찰하러 올 때면, 그녀는 어찌나 빈정대며 경멸하는 태도로 의사를 맞이하던지, 그 외과의가 말하기를, 자기가 직업상 모시는 영광을 누리는 시슬우드 귀부인 본인조차도 늙은 세들리 부인보다 더 거드름을 피우지는 못할 것이라고 할 정도였다—더구나 그는 그녀에게서 진료비 한 푼 받지 않으면서 말이다."], ["And very likely Emmy was jealous too, upon her own part, as what mother is not, of those who would manage her children for her, or become candidates for the first place in their affections.", "그리고 아마 에미도 제 나름대로 시샘을 했을 것이다. 어느 어머니인들, 제 자식을 대신 건사하려 들거나 그 애정의 으뜸 자리를 노리는 이들에게 시샘하지 않겠는가."], ["It is certain that when anybody nursed the child, she was uneasy, and that she would no more allow Mrs. Clapp or the domestic to dress or tend him than she would have let them wash her husband's miniature which hung up over her little bed--the same little bed from which the poor girl had gone to his; and to which she retired now for many long, silent, tearful, but happy years.", "분명한 것은, 누가 그 아이를 돌보기라도 하면 그녀가 마음 졸였다는 사실이며, 또한 그녀가 클랩 부인이나 하녀에게 그 아이를 입히거나 보살피게 하느니, 차라리 자기 작은 침대 위에 걸린 남편의 세밀화를 그들에게 닦게 하는 편이 나을 만큼 그것을 허락하지 않았다는 사실이다—그 작은 침대란, 이 가엾은 딸이 남편에게로 떠나갈 때 일어났던 바로 그 침대요, 이제 그녀가 길고 고요하고 눈물겹지만 행복한 여러 해를 보내려 돌아온 그 침대였다."]], [["In this room was all Amelia's heart and treasure.", "이 방에 애밀리아의 온 마음과 보물이 깃들어 있었다."], ["Here it was that she tended her boy and watched him through the many ills of childhood, with a constant passion of love.", "바로 이곳에서 그녀는 한결같은 사랑의 정열로 아들을 돌보고, 어린 시절의 그 숱한 병치레를 지켜보았다."], ["The elder George returned in him somehow, only improved, and as if come back from heaven.", "손위 조지가 어쩐지 이 아이 속에 되살아난 듯했는데, 다만 더 나아진 모습으로, 천국에서 돌아온 듯한 모습으로였다."], ["In a hundred little tones, looks, and movements, the child was so like his father that the widow's heart thrilled as she held him to it; and he would often ask the cause of her tears.", "백 가지나 되는 자그마한 말투며 표정이며 몸짓에서 이 아이는 제 아버지를 어찌나 닮았던지, 미망인이 아이를 가슴에 안을 때면 그 가슴이 떨려 오곤 했다. 그러면 아이는 곧잘 어머니가 우는 까닭을 물었다."], ["It was because of his likeness to his father, she did not scruple to tell him.", "네가 아버지를 닮아서 그렇단다, 하고 그녀는 거리낌 없이 말해 주었다."], ["She talked constantly to him about this dead father, and spoke of her love for George to the innocent and wondering child; much more than she ever had done to George himself, or to any confidante of her youth.", "그녀는 죽은 아버지에 대해 끊임없이 아이에게 이야기했고, 그 천진하고 의아해하는 아이에게 조지를 향한 자신의 사랑을 들려주었다—조지 본인에게나 처녀 시절 자신의 어떤 절친한 벗에게 했던 것보다 훨씬 더 많이."], ["To her parents she never talked about this matter, shrinking from baring her heart to them.", "그녀는 부모에게는 이 일을 결코 입에 올리지 않았으니, 그들에게 자기 마음을 드러내기를 꺼린 것이었다."], ["Little George very likely could understand no better than they, but into his ears she poured her sentimental secrets unreservedly, and into his only.", "어린 조지가 그들보다 더 잘 알아들었을 리 없었지만, 그녀는 그 아이의 귀에다, 오직 그 아이의 귀에만 자신의 감상적인 비밀들을 거리낌 없이 쏟아부었다."], ["The very joy of this woman was a sort of grief, or so tender, at least, that its expression was tears.", "이 여인의 기쁨이라는 것 자체가 일종의 슬픔이었고, 적어도 어찌나 애틋했던지 그 표현이 눈물로 나타날 정도였다."], ["Her sensibilities were so weak and tremulous that perhaps they ought not to be talked about in a book.", "그녀의 감수성은 어찌나 여리고 떨림이 많은지, 어쩌면 책에서 이야기하지 않는 편이 나을지도 모르겠다."], ["I was told by Dr. Pestler (now a most flourishing lady's physician, with a sumptuous dark green carriage, a prospect of speedy knighthood, and a house in Manchester Square) that her grief at weaning the child was a sight that would have unmanned a Herod.", "페슬러 박사(이제는 더없이 번창하는 여성 전문의로, 호사스러운 짙은 초록색 마차를 굴리고, 머지않아 기사 작위를 받을 전망에다, 맨체스터 광장에 집을 두고 있다)에게 들은 바로는, 아이의 젖을 뗄 때 그녀가 보인 슬픔은 헤롯 왕마저도 무너뜨릴 만한 광경이었다고 한다."], ["He was very soft-hearted many years ago, and his wife was mortally jealous of Mrs. Amelia, then and long afterwards.", "그는 여러 해 전에는 무척 마음이 여린 사람이었고, 그의 아내는 그때나 그 후로도 오래도록 애밀리아 부인을 죽도록 시샘했다."]], [["Perhaps the doctor's lady had good reason for her jealousy: most women shared it, of those who formed the small circle of Amelia's acquaintance, and were quite angry at the enthusiasm with which the other sex regarded her.", "어쩌면 그 의사 부인의 시샘에는 그럴 만한 까닭이 있었는지도 모른다. 애밀리아와 알고 지내던 그 자그마한 무리에 속한 여인들 대부분이 그 시샘을 함께 나누었으며, 이성들이 그녀를 우러러보는 그 열의에 대해 적잖이 분개했던 것이다."], ["For almost all men who came near her loved her; though no doubt they would be at a loss to tell you why.", "그녀에게 가까이 다가오는 사내라면 거의 모두가 그녀를 사랑했기 때문이다. 비록 그 까닭을 대라면 다들 난감해했겠지만 말이다."], ["She was not brilliant, nor witty, nor wise over much, nor extraordinarily handsome.", "그녀는 빛나지도, 재치 있지도, 그리 지혜롭지도, 유난히 아름답지도 않았다."], ["But wherever she went she touched and charmed every one of the male sex, as invariably as she awakened the scorn and incredulity of her own sisterhood.", "그러나 그녀는 가는 곳마다 모든 남자의 마음을 건드리고 사로잡았는데, 그것이 자기 자매들의 경멸과 불신을 불러일으키는 것만큼이나 한결같았다."], ["I think it was her weakness which was her principal charm--a kind of sweet submission and softness, which seemed to appeal to each man she met for his sympathy and protection. We have seen how in the regiment, though she spoke but to few of George's comrades there, all the swords of the young fellows at the mess-table would have leapt from their scabbards to fight round her; and so it was in the little narrow lodging-house and circle at Fulham, she interested and pleased everybody.", "내 생각에 그녀의 주된 매력은 바로 그 연약함이었다—일종의 달콤한 순종과 부드러움, 마주치는 사내마다 그 동정과 보호를 호소하는 듯한 그런 것 말이다."], ["If she had been Mrs.", "우리는 앞서 보았다."], ["Mango herself, of the great house of Mango, Plantain, and Co., Crutched Friars, and the magnificent proprietress of the Pineries, Fulham, who gave summer dejeuners frequented by Dukes and Earls, and drove about the parish with magnificent yellow liveries and bay horses, such as the royal stables at Kensington themselves could not turn out--I say had she been Mrs.", "연대에서 그녀가 조지의 동료들 중 몇몇에게만 말을 건넸을 뿐인데도, 식탁에 둘러앉은 젊은이들의 칼이란 칼은 모두 칼집에서 뛰쳐나와 그녀를 둘러싸고 싸우려 들었을 것임을. 풀럼의 그 비좁은 하숙집과 작은 무리에서도 마찬가지였으니, 그녀는 누구에게나 흥미를 자아내고 호감을 샀다."], ["Mango herself, or her son's wife, Lady Mary Mango (daughter of the Earl of Castlemouldy, who condescended to marry the head of the firm), the tradesmen of the neighbourhood could not pay her more honour than they invariably showed to the gentle young widow, when she passed by their doors, or made her humble purchases at their shops.", "설령 그녀가 망고 부인 본인이었다 한들—크러치드 프라이어스 소재 망고·플랜틴 상회의 대단한 가문 출신이요, 풀럼의 파인어리스를 호화롭게 소유한 그 안주인, 공작이며 백작들이 드나드는 여름 오찬을 베풀고, 켄싱턴의 왕실 마구간에서조차 내놓지 못할 휘황한 노란 제복과 밤색 말들을 거느리고 교구를 돌아다니던 그 망고 부인 말이다—내 말하건대, 설령 그녀가 망고 부인 본인이었다 한들, 아니 그 아들의 아내 메리 망고 부인(상회의 우두머리와 결혼하기를 황송히 승낙한 캐슬몰디 백작의 딸)이었다 한들, 이 동네의 상인들이 그 온순한 젊은 미망인에게, 그녀가 자기네 가게 문 앞을 지나거나 변변찮은 물건을 사러 들를 때마다 한결같이 표하던 그 이상의 경의를 표하지는 못했을 것이다."]], [["Thus it was not only Mr. Pestler, the medical man, but Mr.", "이리하여 의사인 페슬러 씨뿐만 아니라, 하녀들과 영세 상인들을 진료하며 진료소에서 타임스를 읽는 모습을 언제든 볼 수 있던 젊은 조수 린턴 씨까지도 공공연히 오즈번 부인의 노예임을 자처했다."], ["Linton the young assistant, who doctored the servant maids and small tradesmen, and might be seen any day reading the Times in the surgery, who openly declared himself the slave of Mrs. Osborne.", "그는 풍채 좋은 젊은 신사로, 세들리 부인의 하숙집에서는 그의 상관보다도 더 환영받았다."], ["He was a personable young gentleman, more welcome at Mrs. Sedley's lodgings than his principal; and if anything went wrong with Georgy, he would drop in twice or thrice in the day to see the little chap, and without so much as the thought of a fee.", "조지에게 무슨 탈이라도 나면, 그는 하루에 두세 번씩 들러 그 꼬마를 살피곤 했는데, 진료비 따위는 생각조차 하지 않았다."], ["He would abstract lozenges, tamarinds, and other produce from the surgery-drawers for little Georgy's benefit, and compounded draughts and mixtures for him of miraculous sweetness, so that it was quite a pleasure to the child to be ailing.", "그는 어린 조지를 위해 진료소 서랍에서 마름모 사탕이며 타마린드며 그 밖의 것들을 슬쩍 빼내 왔고, 기적처럼 달콤한 물약이며 합제를 조제해 주었으니, 그 아이로서는 아픈 것이 차라리 즐거운 일이 될 지경이었다."], ["He and Pestler, his chief, sat up two whole nights by the boy in that momentous and awful week when Georgy had the measles; and when you would have thought, from the mother's terror, that there had never been measles in the world before.", "그와 그의 상관 페슬러는, 조지가 홍역을 앓던 그 중대하고도 무시무시한 한 주 동안 꼬박 이틀 밤을 그 아이 곁에서 지새웠다—그것도 어머니가 보인 그 공포로 미루어 보자면, 세상에 홍역이라는 것이 일찍이 없었던 양 호들갑을 떨던 그 시절에 말이다."], ["Would they have done as much for other people?", "그들이 다른 사람들을 위해서도 그만큼 했을까?"], ["Did they sit up for the folks at the Pineries, when Ralph Plantagenet, and Gwendoline, and Guinever Mango had the same juvenile complaint?", "파인어리스의 식구들을 위해, 랠프 플랜태저넷이며 그웬돌린이며 귀네버 망고가 똑같은 어린이 병을 앓을 때 밤을 지새웠을까?"], ["Did they sit up for little Mary Clapp, the landlord's daughter, who actually caught the disease of little Georgy?", "집주인의 딸인 어린 메리 클랩이 실제로 어린 조지에게서 그 병을 옮았을 때, 그 아이를 위해 밤을 지새웠을까?"], ["Truth compels one to say, no.", "진실을 말하자면, 아니었다."], ["They slept quite undisturbed, at least as far as she was concerned--pronounced hers to be a slight case, which would almost cure itself, sent her in a draught or two, and threw in bark when the child rallied, with perfect indifference, and just for form's sake.", "그들은 적어도 그 아이에 관한 한 아주 편안히 잠들었고—그 애 병은 가벼운 경우라 거의 저절로 나을 거라고 진단했으며, 물약 한두 첩을 보내 주고, 아이가 차도를 보이자 완전히 무심한 태도로, 그저 형식치레로 키니네를 처방해 주었을 뿐이다."]], [["Again, there was the little French chevalier opposite, who gave lessons in his native tongue at various schools in the neighbourhood, and who might be heard in his apartment of nights playing tremulous old gavottes and minuets on a wheezy old fiddle.", "또 길 건너편에는 자그마한 프랑스인 기사가 있어, 동네의 여러 학교에서 자기 모국어를 가르쳤는데, 밤이면 그의 거처에서 그가 쌕쌕거리는 낡은 바이올린으로 떨리는 옛 가보트며 미뉴에트를 켜는 소리를 들을 수 있었다."], ["Whenever this powdered and courteous old man, who never missed a Sunday at the convent chapel at Hammersmith, and who was in all respects, thoughts, conduct, and bearing utterly unlike the bearded savages of his nation, who curse perfidious Albion, and scowl at you from over their cigars, in the Quadrant arcades at the present day--whenever the old Chevalier de Talonrouge spoke of Mistress Osborne, he would first finish his pinch of snuff, flick away the remaining particles of dust with a graceful wave of his hand, gather up his fingers again into a bunch, and, bringing them up to his mouth, blow them open with a kiss, exclaiming, Ah!", "분 바르고 정중한 이 노인은 해머스미스의 수녀원 예배당의 일요일 미사를 한 번도 거르지 않았고, 생각이며 행실이며 거동이며 모든 점에서, 영악한 앨비언을 저주하며 오늘날 콰드런트 아케이드에서 시가를 문 채 사람을 노려보는 그 수염투성이 동족 야만인들과는 전혀 딴판이었다—이 탈롱루주 노기사가 오즈번 부인 이야기를 할 때면, 그는 먼저 코담배 한 줌을 다 들이마신 다음, 손을 우아하게 휘저어 남은 가루를 털어 내고, 다시 손가락을 한데 모아 입가로 가져가서는 입맞춤으로 후 불어 펼치며 외치곤 했다. 아!"], ["la divine creature!", "라 디빈 크레아튀르!"], ["He vowed and protested that when Amelia walked in the Brompton Lanes flowers grew in profusion under her feet.", "(아, 저 거룩한 여인이여!) 그는 애밀리아가 브롬프턴 골목을 거닐면 그 발밑에서 꽃들이 흐드러지게 피어난다고 맹세코 단언했다."], ["He called little Georgy Cupid, and asked him news of Venus, his mamma; and told the astonished Betty Flanagan that she was one of the Graces, and the favourite attendant of the Reine des Amours.", "그는 어린 조지를 큐피드라고 불렀고, 그 애 엄마인 비너스의 안부를 물었으며, 어리둥절해하는 베티 플래너건에게 그대는 삼미신 중 한 분이요, 사랑의 여왕을 모시는 총애받는 시녀라고 했다."]], [["Instances might be multiplied of this easily gained and unconscious popularity. Did not Mr.", "이처럼 손쉽게 얻은, 본인은 의식조차 못 하는 인기의 사례들은 얼마든지 더 들 수 있다."], ["Binny, the mild and genteel curate of the district chapel, which the family attended, call assiduously upon the widow, dandle the little boy on his knee, and offer to teach him Latin, to the anger of the elderly virgin, his sister, who kept house for him?", "이 가족이 다니던 구역 예배당의 온화하고 점잖은 부목사 비니 씨가, 부지런히 미망인을 찾아와 어린 사내아이를 무릎에 앉혀 어르고, 그 애에게 라틴어를 가르쳐 주겠노라 제안했던 일도 있지 않은가—그의 살림을 맡아 하던 노처녀 누이의 노여움을 사면서까지 말이다."], ["\"There is nothing in her, Beilby,\"", "\"저 여자한테는 별것 없어요, 베일비.\""], ["the latter lady would say.", "그 누이는 이렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["\"When she comes to tea here she does not speak a word during the whole evening.", "\"여기 차 마시러 와서는 저녁 내내 한마디도 안 하잖아요."], ["She is but a poor lackadaisical creature, and it is my belief has no heart at all.", "그저 가련하고 맥없는 여자일 뿐이고, 내 보기엔 마음이라고는 아예 없는 것 같다니까."], ["It is only her pretty face which all you gentlemen admire so.", "댁들 신사분들이 그토록 탄복하는 건 그저 그 예쁘장한 얼굴뿐이라고요."], ["Miss Grits, who has five thousand pounds, and expectations besides, has twice as much character, and is a thousand times more agreeable to my taste; and if she were good-looking I know that you would think her perfection.\"", "오천 파운드에다 그 밖의 기대까지 있는 그리츠 양이 그 여자보다 인품이 두 배는 낫고, 내 취향으론 천 배는 더 마음에 들어요. 게다가 그리츠 양이 미인이기까지 했다면, 오라버니도 그녀를 완벽하다 여겼으리란 걸 난 알아요.\""]], [["Very likely Miss Binny was right to a great extent.", "아마도 비니 양의 말은 상당 부분 옳았을 것이다."], ["It IS the pretty face which creates sympathy in the hearts of men, those wicked rogues.", "사내들의 마음에, 그 사악한 악당들의 마음에 동정을 불러일으키는 것은 바로 예쁜 얼굴인 것이다."], ["A woman may possess the wisdom and chastity of Minerva, and we give no heed to her, if she has a plain face.", "한 여자가 미네르바의 지혜와 정결을 다 갖추었다 한들, 얼굴이 못생겼다면 우리는 그녀에게 눈길조차 주지 않는다."], ["What folly will not a pair of bright eyes make pardonable?", "한 쌍의 빛나는 눈이 어떤 어리석음인들 용서받게 하지 못하랴?"], ["What dulness may not red lips and sweet accents render pleasant?", "붉은 입술과 달콤한 말씨가 어떤 따분함인들 즐겁게 만들지 못하랴?"], ["And so, with their usual sense of justice, ladies argue that because a woman is handsome, therefore she is a fool.", "그리하여 숙녀들은 그 특유의 공정함으로, 여자가 아름다우니 그러므로 바보임에 틀림없다고 따지는 것이다."], ["O ladies, ladies!", "오, 숙녀들이여, 숙녀들이여!"], ["there are some of you who are neither handsome nor wise.", "그대들 중에는 아름답지도 지혜롭지도 못한 이들이 더러 있도다."]], [["These are but trivial incidents to recount in the life of our heroine.", "이런 것들은 우리 여주인공의 삶에서 들려줄 만한 자질구레한 사건들에 지나지 않는다."], ["Her tale does not deal in wonders, as the gentle reader has already no doubt perceived; and if a journal had been kept of her proceedings during the seven years after the birth of her son, there would be found few incidents more remarkable in it than that of the measles, recorded in the foregoing page.", "그녀의 이야기는, 점잖은 독자께서 이미 의심할 바 없이 알아채셨듯, 경이로운 일들을 다루지 않는다. 그러니 그 아들이 태어난 후 칠 년 동안의 그녀의 행적을 일지로 적어 두었다 한들, 앞 쪽에 기록된 그 홍역 사건보다 더 눈에 띄는 일은 거의 찾아볼 수 없었을 것이다."], ["Yes, one day, and greatly to her wonder, the Reverend Mr.", "그렇다, 어느 날, 그녀로서는 무척이나 놀랍게도, 방금 언급한 비니 목사가 그녀에게 오즈번이라는 성을 자기 성으로 바꾸지 않겠느냐고 청했다."], ["Binny, just mentioned, asked her to change her name of Osborne for his own; when, with deep blushes and tears in her eyes and voice, she thanked him for his regard for her, expressed gratitude for his attentions to her and to her poor little boy, but said that she never, never could think of any but--but the husband whom she had lost.", "그러자 그녀는 깊이 얼굴을 붉히고 눈에도 목소리에도 눈물을 머금은 채, 자신을 그토록 마음에 두어 준 데 대해 감사하고, 자신과 가엾은 어린 아들에게 베풀어 준 친절에 고마움을 표하면서도, 자신은 결코, 결코 다른 누구도 생각할 수 없으며 오직—오직 자신이 잃은 그 남편만을 생각할 수밖에 없노라고 말했다."]], [["On the twenty-fifth of April, and the eighteenth of June, the days of marriage and widowhood, she kept her room entirely, consecrating them (and we do not know how many hours of solitary night-thought, her little boy sleeping in his crib by her bedside) to the memory of that departed friend.", "사월 이십오일과 유월 십팔일, 결혼한 날과 미망인이 된 날이면, 그녀는 온종일 방에 틀어박혀, 그 떠나간 벗을 추억하는 데 그날들을 (그리고 어린 아들이 침대 곁 요람에서 잠든 가운데, 외로운 밤 생각에 잠겨 보낸 그 얼마나 많은 시간을—우리로서는 알 수 없는—) 오롯이 바쳤다."], ["During the day she was more active.", "낮 동안에는 좀 더 활기차게 지냈다."], ["She had to teach George to read and to write and a little to draw.", "그녀는 조지에게 읽기와 쓰기, 그리고 그림도 조금 가르쳐야 했다."], ["She read books, in order that she might tell him stories from them.", "책에서 이야기를 들려주려고 그녀는 책을 읽었다."], ["As his eyes opened and his mind expanded under the influence of the outward nature round about him, she taught the child, to the best of her humble power, to acknowledge the Maker of all, and every night and every morning he and she--(in that awful and touching communion which I think must bring a thrill to the heart of every man who witnesses or who remembers it)--the mother and the little boy--prayed to Our Father together, the mother pleading with all her gentle heart, the child lisping after her as she spoke.", "그 아이의 눈이 뜨이고 주위를 둘러싼 바깥 자연의 영향 아래 그 마음이 자라남에 따라, 그녀는 자신의 변변찮은 능력을 다하여 그 아이에게 만물의 창조주를 우러르도록 가르쳤다. 그리하여 밤마다 아침마다 그 아이와 그녀는—(이 광경을 목격하거나 기억하는 사람이라면 누구나 그 가슴이 떨려 오리라 믿는 저 경외롭고 가슴 뭉클한 교감 속에서)—어머니와 어린 사내아이는 함께 하늘에 계신 우리 아버지께 기도드렸다."], ["And each time they prayed to God to bless dear Papa, as if he were alive and in the room with them.", "어머니는 그 온유한 마음을 다해 간구하고, 아이는 어머니가 말하는 대로 더듬더듬 따라 외었다."], ["To wash and dress this young gentleman--to take him for a run of the mornings, before breakfast, and the retreat of grandpapa for \"business\"--to make for him the most wonderful and ingenious dresses, for which end the thrifty widow cut up and altered every available little bit of finery which she possessed out of her wardrobe during her marriage--for Mrs.", "그리고 그때마다 그들은 사랑하는 아빠를 축복해 달라고 하느님께 기도했으니, 마치 그가 살아서 그들과 한방에 있기라도 한 듯이 말이다. 이 어린 신사를 씻기고 입히는 일—아침이면 식사 전에, 또 할아버지가 \"사업\""], ["Osborne herself (greatly to her mother's vexation, who preferred fine clothes, especially since her misfortunes) always wore a black gown and a straw bonnet with a black ribbon--occupied her many hours of the day. Others she had to spare, at the service of her mother and her old father.", "보러 물러가기 전에 그 애를 데리고 한바탕 뛰어다니는 일—그 애를 위해 더없이 신기하고 기발한 옷들을 지어 주는 일, 이를 위해 알뜰한 미망인은 결혼 시절에 지녔던 옷장 속 쓸 만한 고운 옷가지란 옷가지는 죄다 잘라 고쳐 썼다—오즈번 부인 자신은 (특히 영락한 뒤로 고운 옷을 더 좋아하게 된 어머니로서는 무척 속상한 일이었지만) 늘 검은 드레스에 검은 리본을 단 밀짚 보닛을 쓰고 다녔으니—이런 일들이 그녀의 하루 많은 시간을 차지했다."], ["She had taken the pains to learn, and used to play cribbage with this gentleman on the nights when he did not go to his club.", "남는 시간은 어머니와 늙은 아버지를 돌보는 데 썼다. 그녀는 일부러 익혀 두었다가, 아버지가 클럽에 가지 않는 밤이면 그와 크리비지 카드놀이를 하곤 했다."], ["She sang for him when he was so minded, and it was a good sign, for he invariably fell into a comfortable sleep during the music.", "아버지가 그러고 싶어 할 때면 노래도 불러 드렸는데, 그것은 좋은 징조였으니, 그가 그 음악이 흐르는 동안 어김없이 편안한 잠에 빠져들었기 때문이다."], ["She wrote out his numerous memorials, letters, prospectuses, and projects.", "그녀는 아버지의 그 숱한 진정서며 편지며 안내장이며 사업 계획서를 대신 써 주었다."], ["It was in her handwriting that most of the old gentleman's former acquaintances were informed that he had become an agent for the Black Diamond and Anti-Cinder Coal Company and could supply his friends and the public with the best coals at --s.", "노신사의 옛 지인들 대부분에게, 그가 흑금강석 무연탄 회사의 대리인이 되어 친구들과 일반에게 최상의 석탄을 한 차당 몇 실링에 공급할 수 있게 되었다고 알린 것도 바로 그녀의 필체였다."], ["per chaldron. All he did was to sign the circulars with his flourish and signature, and direct them in a shaky, clerklike hand.", "노신사가 한 일이라고는 화려한 서명을 휘갈겨 회람장에 이름을 적고, 떨리는 사무원 같은 글씨로 수신처를 쓴 것뿐이었다."], ["One of these papers was sent to Major Dobbin,--Regt., care of Messrs. Cox and Greenwood; but the Major being in Madras at the time, had no particular call for coals.", "이런 회람장 한 통이 콕스 앤드 그린우드 상회 편으로 ××연대 도빈 소령에게 부쳐졌으나, 당시 마드라스에 있던 소령으로서는 석탄이 딱히 필요할 일이 없었다."], ["He knew, though, the hand which had written the prospectus.", "그래도 그는 그 안내장을 쓴 필체가 누구의 것인지 알아보았다."], ["Good God!", "맙소사!"], ["what would he not have given to hold it in his own!", "그것을 제 손에 쥐기 위해서라면 그가 무엇인들 내주지 않았으랴!"], ["A second prospectus came out, informing the Major that J.", "두 번째 안내장이 나와, J."], ["Sedley and Company, having established agencies at Oporto, Bordeaux, and St. Mary's, were enabled to offer to their friends and the public generally the finest and most celebrated growths of ports, sherries, and claret wines at reasonable prices and under extraordinary advantages.", "세들리 상회가 오포르토와 보르도, 세인트메리스에 대리점을 설치하여 친구들과 일반에게 가장 좋고 가장 이름난 포트와인과 셰리주와 클라레 와인을 합리적인 가격에, 더없이 유리한 조건으로 제공할 수 있게 되었음을 소령에게 알렸다."], ["Acting upon this hint, Dobbin furiously canvassed the governor, the commander-in-chief, the judges, the regiments, and everybody whom he knew in the Presidency, and sent home to Sedley and Co.", "이 언질을 받아들여 도빈은 총독이며, 총사령관이며, 판사들이며, 연대들이며, 그 관할구에서 자기가 아는 사람이란 사람은 죄다 미친 듯이 찾아다니며 권유했고, 세들리 상회 앞으로 와인 주문을 보냈으니, 세들리 씨와 그 사업의 \"상회\""], ["orders for wine which perfectly astonished Mr. Sedley and Mr.", "격인 클랩 씨가 그만 어안이 벙벙해질 정도였다."], ["Clapp, who was the Co. in the business.", "그러나 그 첫 행운의 폭발 이후로는 더 이상 주문이 들어오지 않았다."], ["But no more orders came after that first burst of good fortune, on which poor old Sedley was about to build a house in the City, a regiment of clerks, a dock to himself, and correspondents all over the world.", "가엾은 늙은 세들리는 그 한 번의 행운에 기대어 시내에 사옥을 짓고, 한 무리의 사무원을 두고, 자기만의 부두를 갖추고, 전 세계에 거래처를 두려던 참이었건만. 노신사의 옛 와인 감식 솜씨는 사라지고 없었다."], ["The old gentleman's former taste in wine had gone: the curses of the mess-room assailed Major Dobbin for the vile drinks he had been the means of introducing there; and he bought back a great quantity of the wine and sold it at public outcry, at an enormous loss to himself.", "식당의 동료 장교들이 퍼붓는 욕설이, 그 형편없는 술을 그곳에 들여놓은 장본인인 도빈 소령에게로 쏟아졌다. 그래서 그는 그 와인을 상당량 도로 사들여 공개 경매에 부쳐, 자신에게 막대한 손해를 보며 팔아 치웠다."], ["As for Jos, who was by this time promoted to a seat at the Revenue Board at Calcutta, he was wild with rage when the post brought him out a bundle of these Bacchanalian prospectuses, with a private note from his father, telling Jos that his senior counted upon him in this enterprise, and had consigned a quantity of select wines to him, as per invoice, drawing bills upon him for the amount of the same.", "한편 이 무렵에는 캘커타의 세무국 위원 자리로 승진해 있던 조스로 말하면, 우편으로 이 술꾼 안내장 한 묶음이 배달되어 왔을 때 분통이 터져 펄펄 뛰었다. 거기에는 아버지가 따로 적은 쪽지가 들어 있었는데, 아비가 이 사업에서 너를 믿고 있으며, 송장에 적힌 대로 엄선한 와인을 얼마간 너에게 부쳤으니 그 대금만큼 너 앞으로 어음을 끊는다는 내용이었다."], ["Jos, who would no more have it supposed that his father, Jos Sedley's father, of the Board of Revenue, was a wine merchant asking for orders, than that he was Jack Ketch, refused the bills with scorn, wrote back contumeliously to the old gentleman, bidding him to mind his own affairs; and the protested paper coming back, Sedley and Co.", "조스는 자기 아버지, 그러니까 세무국 위원인 조스 세들리의 아버지가 주문이나 구걸하는 와인 장수라고 여겨지는 것을, 차라리 잭 케치 사형 집행인으로 여겨지는 것만큼이나 견딜 수 없었던지라, 그 어음을 경멸하며 거절하고는, 노신사에게 당신 일이나 신경 쓰라며 무례하기 짝이 없는 답장을 써 보냈다."], ["had to take it up, with the profits which they had made out of the Madras venture, and with a little portion of Emmy's savings.", "그리하여 부도난 어음이 되돌아오자, 세들리 상회는 마드라스 거래에서 거둔 이익과 에미가 모아 둔 돈의 얼마간을 보태어 그것을 메워야 했다."]], [["Besides her pension of fifty pounds a year, there had been five hundred pounds, as her husband's executor stated, left in the agent's hands at the time of Osborne's demise, which sum, as George's guardian, Dobbin proposed to put out at 8 per cent in an Indian house of agency. Mr.", "연 오십 파운드의 연금 외에도, 남편의 유언 집행인이 밝힌 바에 따르면, 오즈번이 세상을 떠날 당시 대리인의 수중에 오백 파운드가 남아 있었는데, 조지의 후견인인 도빈은 이 돈을 인도의 어느 대리점에 연 팔 푼 이자로 맡겨 두자고 제안했다."], ["Sedley, who thought the Major had some roguish intentions of his own about the money, was strongly against this plan; and he went to the agents to protest personally against the employment of the money in question, when he learned, to his surprise, that there had been no such sum in their hands, that all the late Captain's assets did not amount to a hundred pounds, and that the five hundred pounds in question must be a separate sum, of which Major Dobbin knew the particulars.", "소령이 그 돈에 대해 무슨 협잡 속셈을 품고 있다고 여긴 세들리 씨는 이 계획에 한사코 반대했다. 그래서 그는 문제의 그 돈을 그렇게 굴리는 데 직접 항의하려고 대리인을 찾아갔다가, 놀랍게도 그들 수중에는 그런 돈이 애당초 없었으며, 작고한 대위의 자산을 다 합쳐도 백 파운드에 미치지 못했고, 문제의 그 오백 파운드는 분명 별도의 금액으로 그 내막은 도빈 소령이 알고 있으리라는 사실을 알게 되었다."], ["More than ever convinced that there was some roguery, old Sedley pursued the Major.", "무슨 협잡이 있다는 확신이 그 어느 때보다 굳어진 늙은 세들리는 소령을 추궁하고 나섰다."], ["As his daughter's nearest friend, he demanded with a high hand a statement of the late Captain's accounts.", "그는 딸의 가장 가까운 친지로서, 고압적인 태도로 작고한 대위의 회계 내역을 내놓으라고 요구했다."], ["Dobbin's stammering, blushing, and awkwardness added to the other's convictions that he had a rogue to deal with, and in a majestic tone he told that officer a piece of his mind, as he called it, simply stating his belief that the Major was unlawfully detaining his late son-in-law's money.", "도빈이 말을 더듬고, 얼굴을 붉히고, 어쩔 줄 몰라 하는 꼴은, 자기가 상대하는 자가 협잡꾼이라는 세들리의 확신을 더욱 부채질했다. 그는 위풍당당한 어조로, 자기 말마따나 그 장교에게 속에 담아 둔 말을 한바탕 쏟아냈으니, 소령이 작고한 사위의 돈을 불법으로 가로채고 있는 것으로 믿는다고 단도직입적으로 말한 것이다."]], [["Dobbin at this lost all patience, and if his accuser had not been so old and so broken, a quarrel might have ensued between them at the Slaughters'", "이에 도빈은 인내심을 모두 잃었고, 만약 그를 비난하는 자가 그토록 늙고 영락한 처지가 아니었다면, 슬로터스 커피하우스에서 두 사람 사이에 한바탕 싸움이 벌어졌을지도 모른다."], ["Coffee-house, in a box of which place of entertainment the gentlemen had their colloquy.", "두 신사는 그 유흥 장소의 칸막이 좌석에서 이야기를 나누던 참이었다. \"위층으로 올라가시지요, 선생.\""], ["\"Come upstairs, sir,\"", "소령이 떠듬떠듬 내뱉었다."], ["lisped out the Major.", "\"기어이 위층으로 올라가셔야겠습니다."], ["\"I insist on your coming up the stairs, and I will show which is the injured party, poor George or I\"; and, dragging the old gentleman up to his bedroom, he produced from his desk Osborne's accounts, and a bundle of IOU's which the latter had given, who, to do him justice, was always ready to give an IOU.", "어느 쪽이 손해 본 당사자인지, 가엾은 조지인지 나인지, 내 보여 드리지요.\" 그러고는 노신사를 자기 침실까지 끌고 올라가 책상에서 오즈번의 회계 장부와, 오즈번이 끊어 준 차용증 한 묶음을 꺼냈다."], ["\"He paid his bills in England,\"", "공정을 기하자면, 오즈번은 늘 차용증을 끊어 줄 준비가 되어 있는 사람이었다. \"그는 영국에서 진 빚은 다 갚았습니다.\""], ["Dobbin added, \"but he had not a hundred pounds in the world when he fell.", "도빈이 덧붙였다. \"하지만 전사했을 때 그에게는 이 세상에 백 파운드도 없었습니다."], ["I and one or two of his brother officers made up the little sum, which was all that we could spare, and you dare tell us that we are trying to cheat the widow and the orphan.\"", "나와 동료 장교 한둘이 그 얼마 안 되는 돈을 마련했는데, 그게 우리가 내놓을 수 있는 전부였습니다. 그런데도 당신은 감히, 우리가 미망인과 고아를 속여 먹으려 든다고 말씀하시는 겁니까.\""], ["Sedley was very contrite and humbled, though the fact is that William Dobbin had told a great falsehood to the old gentleman; having himself given every shilling of the money, having buried his friend, and paid all the fees and charges incident upon the calamity and removal of poor Amelia.", "세들리는 몹시 뉘우치며 풀이 죽었다—사실인즉 윌리엄 도빈이 노신사에게 새빨간 거짓말을 한 것이었지만 말이다. 그 돈은 한 푼까지 도빈 자신이 내놓은 것이었고, 벗을 손수 묻어 주었으며, 그 참변과 가엾은 애밀리아의 이주에 따르는 온갖 비용과 부담을 다 떠맡은 것도 그였기 때문이다."]], [["About these expenses old Osborne had never given himself any trouble to think, nor any other relative of Amelia, nor Amelia herself, indeed.", "이런 비용에 대해 늙은 오즈번은 한 번도 골치 아프게 생각해 본 적이 없었고, 애밀리아의 다른 어떤 친지도, 사실 애밀리아 본인도 그러했다."], ["She trusted to Major Dobbin as an accountant, took his somewhat confused calculations for granted, and never once suspected how much she was in his debt.", "그녀는 회계 담당으로서 도빈 소령을 믿었고, 다소 뒤죽박죽인 그의 계산을 곧이곧대로 받아들였으며, 자신이 그에게 얼마나 신세를 지고 있는지는 단 한 번도 의심해 본 적이 없었다."]], [["Twice or thrice in the year, according to her promise, she wrote him letters to Madras, letters all about little Georgy.", "약속대로 그녀는 일 년에 두세 번씩 마드라스로 그에게 편지를 썼는데, 죄다 어린 조지에 관한 편지였다. 그가 이 편지들을 얼마나 소중히 간직했던지!"], ["How he treasured these papers! Whenever Amelia wrote he answered, and not until then.", "애밀리아가 편지를 쓸 때마다 그는 답장을 했고, 그러기 전에는 결코 쓰지 않았다."], ["But he sent over endless remembrances of himself to his godson and to her.", "그러나 그는 자신을 기억해 달라는 뜻으로 끝없는 선물들을 대자와 그녀에게 보냈다."], ["He ordered and sent a box of scarfs and a grand ivory set of chess-men from China.", "그는 중국에서 스카프 한 상자와 훌륭한 상아 체스 한 벌을 주문해 보냈다."], ["The pawns were little green and white men, with real swords and shields; the knights were on horseback, the castles were on the backs of elephants.", "졸은 초록색과 흰색의 작은 병사들로, 진짜 칼과 방패를 들고 있었다. 기사들은 말을 탔고, 성은 코끼리 등에 얹혀 있었다."], ["\"Mrs. Mango's own set at the Pineries was not so fine,\"", "\"파인어리스에 있는 망고 부인의 체스 한 벌도 이만큼 훌륭하지는 않더군요.\""], ["Mr. Pestler remarked.", "페슬러 씨가 한마디 했다."], ["These chess-men were the delight of Georgy's life, who printed his first letter in acknowledgement of this gift of his godpapa.", "이 체스 말들은 조지의 삶에 더없는 기쁨이었으니, 그 아이는 대부의 이 선물에 답례하느라 난생처음으로 글자를 또박또박 써 보냈다."], ["He sent over preserves and pickles, which latter the young gentleman tried surreptitiously in the sideboard and half-killed himself with eating.", "그는 잼이며 피클도 보내 주었는데, 피클이라는 것을 이 어린 신사가 찬장에서 몰래 맛보다가 너무 먹어 하마터면 죽을 뻔했다."], ["He thought it was a judgement upon him for stealing, they were so hot.", "어찌나 매웠던지, 그 애는 그것이 몰래 훔쳐 먹은 데 대한 천벌이라고 여겼다."], ["Emmy wrote a comical little account of this mishap to the Major: it pleased him to think that her spirits were rallying and that she could be merry sometimes now.", "에미는 이 작은 사고를 익살스럽게 적어 소령에게 편지했다. 그녀의 기운이 차츰 되살아나, 이제 가끔은 명랑해질 수도 있게 되었구나 싶어 그는 흐뭇했다."], ["He sent over a pair of shawls, a white one for her and a black one with palm-leaves for her mother, and a pair of red scarfs, as winter wrappers, for old Mr. Sedley and George.", "그는 숄도 두 장 보냈는데, 그녀에게는 흰 것을, 그녀의 어머니에게는 종려잎 무늬가 든 검은 것을 보냈고, 늙은 세들리 씨와 조지에게는 겨울 목도리로 쓰라고 붉은 스카프 두 장을 보냈다."], ["The shawls were worth fifty guineas apiece at the very least, as Mrs. Sedley knew.", "세들리 부인이 아는 바로는, 그 숄은 적어도 한 장에 오십 기니는 족히 나가는 물건이었다."], ["She wore hers in state at church at Brompton, and was congratulated by her female friends upon the splendid acquisition.", "그녀는 그것을 브롬프턴의 교회에 위풍당당하게 두르고 갔고, 그 멋들어진 새 물건을 두고 여자 친구들에게서 축하를 받았다."], ["Emmy's, too, became prettily her modest black gown.", "에미의 것 역시 그녀의 수수한 검은 드레스에 곱게 어울렸다."], ["\"What a pity it is she won't think of him!\" Mrs.", "\"저 애가 그분을 마음에 두지 않으려 하다니, 참으로 안타까운 일이지요!\""], ["Sedley remarked to Mrs. Clapp and to all her friends of Brompton.", "세들리 부인은 클랩 부인에게며 브롬프턴의 친구들 모두에게 이렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["\"Jos never sent us such presents, I am sure, and grudges us everything.", "\"조스는 우리한테 이런 선물 한 번 보낸 적이 없고, 뭐든 인색하게 굴기만 한다니까요."], ["It is evident that the Major is over head and ears in love with her; and yet, whenever I so much as hint it, she turns red and begins to cry and goes and sits upstairs with her miniature.", "소령이 저 애한테 홀딱 빠져 있는 게 분명한데도, 내가 그 이야기를 조금이라도 꺼낼라치면 저 애는 얼굴이 빨개져서 울기 시작하더니 위층으로 올라가 그 세밀화를 끼고 앉아 있는다오."], ["I'm sick of that miniature.", "난 그 세밀화라면 신물이 나요."], ["I wish we had never seen those odious purse-proud Osbornes.\"", "저 밉살스럽고 돈 자랑이나 하는 오즈번 집안 사람들을 아예 보지 않았더라면 좋았을 것을.\""]], [["Amidst such humble scenes and associates George's early youth was passed, and the boy grew up delicate, sensitive, imperious, woman-bred--domineering the gentle mother whom he loved with passionate affection.", "이처럼 보잘것없는 정경과 사람들 틈에서 조지의 어린 시절이 흘러갔고, 그 아이는 섬세하고, 감수성 예민하고, 군림하려 들고, 여자 손에 길러진 아이로 자라났다—자기가 열렬한 애정으로 사랑하는 그 온순한 어머니를 휘어잡으면서 말이다."], ["He ruled all the rest of the little world round about him.", "그 아이는 주위의 작은 세상 모두를 다스렸다."], ["As he grew, the elders were amazed at his haughty manner and his constant likeness to his father.", "자라면서 그 아이가 보이는 거만한 태도와 한결같이 제 아버지를 빼닮은 모습에 어른들은 혀를 내둘렀다."], ["He asked questions about everything, as inquiring youth will do.", "그 아이는 캐묻기 좋아하는 여느 아이들처럼 무엇에 대해서나 질문을 던졌다."], ["The profundity of his remarks and interrogatories astonished his old grandfather, who perfectly bored the club at the tavern with stories about the little lad's learning and genius.", "그 말과 물음의 깊이에 늙은 할아버지는 놀라 자빠질 지경이라, 선술집 클럽 사람들을 그 꼬마의 학식과 천재성에 관한 이야기로 진저리 나게 만들곤 했다."], ["He suffered his grandmother with a good-humoured indifference.", "그 아이는 할머니를 너그러운 무관심으로 견뎌 냈다."], ["The small circle round about him believed that the equal of the boy did not exist upon the earth.", "그 아이를 둘러싼 작은 무리는 이 사내아이에 견줄 만한 아이가 이 세상에 없다고 믿었다."], ["Georgy inherited his father's pride, and perhaps thought they were not wrong.", "조지는 제 아버지의 자존심을 물려받았으니, 어쩌면 그들의 생각이 틀리지 않다고 여겼는지도 모른다."]], [["When he grew to be about six years old, Dobbin began to write to him very much.", "그 아이가 여섯 살쯤 되었을 무렵, 도빈은 그 애에게 무척 자주 편지를 쓰기 시작했다."], ["The Major wanted to hear that Georgy was going to a school and hoped he would acquit himself with credit there: or would he have a good tutor at home?", "소령은 조지가 학교에 다니게 되었다는 소식을 듣고 싶어 했고, 그곳에서 칭찬받을 만큼 잘 해내기를 바랐다. 아니면 집에서 좋은 가정교사를 두는 게 어떻겠느냐고 했다."], ["It was time that he should begin to learn; and his godfather and guardian hinted that he hoped to be allowed to defray the charges of the boy's education, which would fall heavily upon his mother's straitened income.", "이제 그 애가 공부를 시작할 때가 되었다는 것이었다. 그리고 대부이자 후견인인 그는, 그 아이의 교육비를 자기가 부담할 수 있게 해 주었으면 한다는 뜻을 넌지시 비쳤다."], ["The Major, in a word, was always thinking about Amelia and her little boy, and by orders to his agents kept the latter provided with picture-books, paint-boxes, desks, and all conceivable implements of amusement and instruction.", "그 비용이 어머니의 빠듯한 수입에 큰 짐이 될 터였기 때문이다. 한마디로 소령은 늘 애밀리아와 그 어린 아들을 생각하고 있었고, 대리인들에게 지시하여 그 아이에게 그림책이며 물감 상자며 책상이며, 생각할 수 있는 온갖 놀이와 학습 도구를 빠짐없이 마련해 주었다."], ["Three days before George's sixth birthday a gentleman in a gig, accompanied by a servant, drove up to Mr. Sedley's house and asked to see Master George Osborne: it was Mr.", "조지의 여섯 번째 생일을 사흘 앞둔 어느 날, 하인을 대동한 한 신사가 이륜마차를 타고 세들리 씨의 집으로 들이닥쳐 조지 오즈번 도련님을 뵙겠다고 청했다."], ["Woolsey, military tailor, of Conduit Street, who came at the Major's order to measure the young gentleman for a suit of clothes.", "그는 콘듀잇 거리의 군복 재단사 울지 씨로, 소령의 주문을 받아 그 어린 신사의 옷 한 벌 치수를 재러 온 것이었다."], ["He had had the honour of making for the Captain, the young gentleman's father.", "그는 일찍이 그 어린 신사의 아버지인 대위의 옷을 짓는 영광을 누린 바 있었다."], ["Sometimes, too, and by the Major's desire no doubt, his sisters, the Misses Dobbin, would call in the family carriage to take Amelia and the little boy to drive if they were so inclined.", "또 때로는, 의심할 바 없이 소령의 부탁에 따라, 그의 누이들인 도빈 양들이 가족 마차를 타고 찾아와, 애밀리아와 어린 아들이 내키기만 하면 함께 드라이브를 나가자고 청하기도 했다."], ["The patronage and kindness of these ladies was very uncomfortable to Amelia, but she bore it meekly enough, for her nature was to yield; and, besides, the carriage and its splendours gave little Georgy immense pleasure.", "이 숙녀들이 베푸는 후원과 친절은 애밀리아에게 무척 거북한 것이었으나, 그녀는 그것을 꽤나 온순히 견뎌 냈으니, 양보하는 것이 그녀의 천성이었기 때문이다. 게다가 그 마차와 그 호화로움이 어린 조지에게는 더없는 기쁨을 안겨 주었다."], ["The ladies begged occasionally that the child might pass a day with them, and he was always glad to go to that fine garden-house at Denmark Hill, where they lived, and where there were such fine grapes in the hot-houses and peaches on the walls.", "숙녀들은 이따금 그 아이가 하루쯤 자기들과 함께 지낼 수 있게 해 달라고 청했고, 그 아이는 그들이 사는 덴마크힐의 그 멋진 정원 딸린 저택에 가는 것을 언제나 좋아했다. 그곳 온실에는 그토록 좋은 포도가, 담장에는 복숭아가 열려 있었던 것이다."]], [["One day they kindly came over to Amelia with news which they were SURE would delight her--something VERY interesting about their dear William.", "어느 날 그들은 애밀리아가 들으면 틀림없이 기뻐하리라 확신하는 소식을 가지고 그녀를 친절히 찾아왔다—그들의 소중한 윌리엄에 관한, 무척이나 흥미로운 소식이라는 것이었다."]], [["\"What was it: was he coming home?\"", "\"무슨 소식인데요? 그분이 귀국하시나요?\""], ["she asked with pleasure beaming in her eyes.", "그녀는 두 눈에 기쁨을 환히 빛내며 물었다."]], [["\"Oh, no--not the least--but they had very good reason to believe that dear William was about to be married--and to a relation of a very dear friend of Amelia's--to Miss Glorvina O'Dowd, Sir Michael O'Dowd's sister, who had gone out to join Lady O'Dowd at Madras--a very beautiful and accomplished girl, everybody said.\"", "\"오, 아니에요—그건 전혀—다만 자기네 소중한 윌리엄이 곧 결혼할 모양이라고 믿을 만한 충분한 근거가 있다는 것이었다—그것도 애밀리아의 아주 절친한 벗의 친척과 말이다—마이클 오다우드 경의 누이인 글로비나 오다우드 양으로, 마드라스의 오다우드 부인에게 합류하러 떠난 처녀였는데—누구나 입을 모아 더없이 아름답고 교양 있는 아가씨라고 하더라는 것이었다.\""]], [["Amelia said \"Oh!\"", "애밀리아는 \"오!\""], ["Amelia was very VERY happy indeed.", "하고 말했다. 애밀리아는 정말로, 정말로 무척 행복했다."], ["But she supposed Glorvina could not be like her old acquaintance, who was most kind--but--but she was very happy indeed.", "그러나 그녀 생각에 글로비나가 더없이 친절한 그 옛 지인만 할 리는 없었지만—그래도—그래도 그녀는 정말로 무척 행복했다."], ["And by some impulse of which I cannot explain the meaning, she took George in her arms and kissed him with an extraordinary tenderness.", "그러고는 그 의미를 나로서는 설명할 수 없는 어떤 충동에 사로잡혀, 그녀는 조지를 품에 끌어안고 유난스러운 애틋함으로 입을 맞추었다."], ["Her eyes were quite moist when she put the child down; and she scarcely spoke a word during the whole of the drive--though she was so very happy indeed.", "아이를 내려놓을 때 그녀의 두 눈은 흠뻑 젖어 있었다. 그리고 드라이브를 하는 내내 그녀는 거의 한마디도 하지 않았다—정말로 그토록 무척 행복했으면서도 말이다."]], [["A Cynical Chapter\nOur duty now takes us back for a brief space to some old Hampshire acquaintances of ours, whose hopes respecting the disposal of their rich kinswoman's property were so woefully disappointed.", "이제 우리의 소임은 우리를 잠시 햄프셔의 옛 지인들에게로 다시 데려간다. 부유한 친척 노부인의 재산 처분을 둘러싼 그들의 기대가 그토록 처참하게 무너진 바로 그 사람들이다."], ["After counting upon thirty thousand pounds from his sister, it was a heavy blow to Bute Crawley to receive but five; out of which sum, when he had paid his own debts and those of Jim, his son at college, a very small fragment remained to portion off his four plain daughters.", "누이에게서 삼만 파운드를 받으리라 셈하고 있던 뷰트 크롤리에게, 고작 오천 파운드를 받는다는 것은 호된 타격이었다. 게다가 그 돈에서 자기 빚과 대학에 다니는 아들 짐의 빚까지 갚고 나니, 못생긴 네 딸을 시집보내는 데 쓸 몫으로는 그야말로 푼돈밖에 남지 않았다."], ["Mrs. Bute never knew, or at least never acknowledged, how far her own tyrannous behaviour had tended to ruin her husband.", "자신의 폭압적인 처신이 남편을 파멸시키는 데 얼마나 일조했는지를, 뷰트 부인은 끝내 알지 못했다."], ["All that woman could do, she vowed and protested she had done.", "적어도 인정하려 들지는 않았다. 여자로서 할 수 있는 일은 다 했노라고, 그녀는 맹세코 단언하며 우겼다."], ["Was it her fault if she did not possess those sycophantic arts which her hypocritical nephew, Pitt Crawley, practised?", "위선자 조카 핏 크롤리가 부리는 그런 아첨의 재주를 자기가 갖지 못한 것이 어디 자기 잘못이란 말인가? 그녀는 부정하게 거머쥔 재산으로 그가 누릴 자격이 있는 만큼의 온갖 행복을 누리기를 빌어 주었다."], ["She wished him all the happiness which he merited out of his ill-gotten gains.", "\"적어도 그 돈은 집안에 남는 셈이지.\" 그녀가 너그러운 척 말했다."], ["\"At least the money will remain in the family,\" she said charitably.", "\"핏은 그 돈을 절대 쓰지 않을 거예요, 여보, 그건 아주 확실해요."], ["\"Pitt will never spend it, my dear, that is quite certain; for a greater miser does not exist in England, and he is as odious, though in a different way, as his spendthrift brother, the abandoned Rawdon.\"", "영국에 그보다 더한 구두쇠는 없으니까. 게다가 그 애는, 방식은 다를망정, 제 동생인 저 낭비벽 심한 탕아 로던 못지않게 밉살스러운 인간이거든요.\""]], [["So Mrs. Bute, after the first shock of rage and disappointment, began to accommodate herself as best she could to her altered fortunes and to save and retrench with all her might.", "그리하여 뷰트 부인은 처음의 격분과 실망이라는 충격이 가라앉자, 달라진 형편에 되도록 자신을 맞추고 온 힘을 다해 절약하고 긴축하기 시작했다."], ["She instructed her daughters how to bear poverty cheerfully, and invented a thousand notable methods to conceal or evade it.", "그녀는 딸들에게 가난을 명랑하게 견디는 법을 가르쳤고, 그것을 감추거나 모면할 천 가지 그럴싸한 방법을 고안해 냈다. 그녀는 칭찬받아 마땅한 정력으로 딸들을 이끌고 이웃의 무도회며 공공장소를 누볐다."], ["She took them about to balls and public places in the neighbourhood, with praiseworthy energy; nay, she entertained her friends in a hospitable comfortable manner at the Rectory, and much more frequently than before dear Miss Crawley's legacy had fallen in.", "아니, 그뿐 아니라 목사관에서 손님들을 후하고 안락하게 접대하기까지 했는데, 그것도 사랑하는 크롤리 양의 유산이 굴러들기 전보다 훨씬 잦게 그리하였다. 그녀의 겉으로 드러난 태도만 보아서는 이 집안이 기대를 저버린 일을 겪었으리라고 아무도 짐작하지 못했을 것이고, 그녀가 그토록 자주 사람들 앞에 모습을 드러내는 것을 보고서 집에서는 얼마나 옹색하게 굶주리며 사는지 알아챌 사람도 없었을 것이다."], ["From her outward bearing nobody would have supposed that the family had been disappointed in their expectations, or have guessed from her frequent appearance in public how she pinched and starved at home.", "딸들은 어느 때보다 많은 의상 장신구를 갖추었다. 그들은 끈질기게도 윈체스터와 사우샘프턴의 사교 모임에 모습을 비추었다."], ["Her girls had more milliners' furniture than they had ever enjoyed before.", "카우스까지 파고들어 그곳의 경마 무도회와 보트 경기의 흥겨움을 즐겼다."], ["They appeared perseveringly at the Winchester and Southampton assemblies; they penetrated to Cowes for the race-balls and regatta-gaieties there; and their carriage, with the horses taken from the plough, was at work perpetually, until it began almost to be believed that the four sisters had had fortunes left them by their aunt, whose name the family never mentioned in public but with the most tender gratitude and regard.", "그리고 쟁기를 끌던 말을 떼어다 맨 그들의 마차는 쉴 새 없이 굴러다녔으니, 마침내는 그 네 자매가 고모에게서 재산을 물려받았다고 거의 믿어질 지경이 되었다. 그 고모의 이름을 그 집안은 사람들 앞에서 입에 올릴 때면 늘 더없이 애틋한 감사와 경의를 담아서만 언급했던 것이다."], ["I know no sort of lying which is more frequent in Vanity Fair than this, and it may be remarked how people who practise it take credit to themselves for their hypocrisy, and fancy that they are exceedingly virtuous and praiseworthy, because they are able to deceive the world with regard to the extent of their means.", "나는 이런 종류의 거짓말보다 허영의 시장에서 더 흔한 거짓말을 알지 못한다. 그리고 이런 짓을 일삼는 사람들이 자신의 위선을 도리어 제 공으로 돌리며, 자기 형편의 실상에 관해 세상을 속일 수 있다는 이유만으로 스스로 더없이 덕스럽고 칭찬받을 만하다고 우쭐대는 모습은 눈여겨볼 만한 것이다."]], [["Mrs. Bute certainly thought herself one of the most virtuous women in England, and the sight of her happy family was an edifying one to strangers.", "뷰트 부인은 분명 스스로를 영국에서 가장 덕망 높은 여인 가운데 하나로 여겼고, 그녀의 화목한 가족이라는 광경은 모르는 이들에게 교화적인 본보기였다."], ["They were so cheerful, so loving, so well-educated, so simple!", "그들은 그토록 명랑하고, 그토록 다정하고, 그토록 잘 교육받았으며, 그토록 꾸밈없었던 것이다!"], ["Martha painted flowers exquisitely and furnished half the charity bazaars in the county.", "마사는 꽃 그림을 절묘하게 그려서 그 주의 자선 바자회 절반을 채웠다."], ["Emma was a regular County Bulbul, and her verses in the Hampshire Telegraph were the glory of its Poet's Corner.", "에마는 어엿한 주(州)의 음유시인이어서, 그녀가 햄프셔 텔레그래프에 실은 시는 그 신문 시(詩) 난(欄)의 영광이었다."], ["Fanny and Matilda sang duets together, Mamma playing the piano, and the other two sisters sitting with their arms round each other's waists and listening affectionately.", "패니와 마틸다는 함께 이중창을 불렀고, 그동안 어머니는 피아노를 쳤으며, 나머지 두 자매는 서로의 허리를 팔로 감싸고 앉아 애정 어린 표정으로 귀를 기울였다."], ["Nobody saw the poor girls drumming at the duets in private.", "그 가엾은 딸들이 남몰래 그 이중창을 어설프게 두들기며 연습하는 모습은 아무도 보지 못했다."], ["No one saw Mamma drilling them rigidly hour after hour. In a word, Mrs.", "어머니가 한 시간 또 한 시간 그들을 혹독하게 훈련시키는 모습도 아무도 보지 못했다."], ["Bute put a good face against fortune and kept up appearances in the most virtuous manner.", "한마디로, 뷰트 부인은 운명에 맞서 태연한 얼굴을 꾸미고 더없이 덕스러운 방식으로 체면을 지켰던 것이다."]], [["Everything that a good and respectable mother could do Mrs. Bute did.", "선량하고 점잖은 어머니가 할 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든 뷰트 부인은 했다."], ["She got over yachting men from Southampton, parsons from the Cathedral Close at Winchester, and officers from the barracks there.", "그녀는 사우샘프턴에서 요트 타는 신사들을, 윈체스터 대성당 구역에서 목사들을, 그곳 병영에서 장교들을 끌어들였다. 순회재판이 열릴 때면 젊은 변호사들을 꾀어 보려 했고, 짐이 H."], ["She tried to inveigle the young barristers at assizes and encouraged Jim to bring home friends with whom he went out hunting with the H.", "H. 사냥대와 함께 사냥을 다니며 사귄 친구들을 집으로 데려오도록 부추겼다."], ["H. What will not a mother do for the benefit of her beloved ones?", "사랑하는 자식들의 이익을 위해서라면 어머니가 못 할 일이 무엇이 있겠는가?"]], [["Between such a woman and her brother-in-law, the odious Baronet at the Hall, it is manifest that there could be very little in common.", "이런 여인과, 저택에 사는 그 밉살스러운 준남작인 시동생 사이에 공통점이라곤 거의 있을 수 없었음은 분명한 일이다."], ["The rupture between Bute and his brother Sir Pitt was complete; indeed, between Sir Pitt and the whole county, to which the old man was a scandal.", "뷰트와 그의 형 핏 경 사이의 결렬은 완전한 것이었다. 아니, 사실은 핏 경과 그 주 전체 사이의 결렬이 그러했으니, 그 노인은 온 고을의 망신거리였던 것이다."], ["His dislike for respectable society increased with age, and the lodge-gates had not opened to a gentleman's carriage-wheels since Pitt and Lady Jane came to pay their visit of duty after their marriage.", "점잖은 사교계에 대한 그의 혐오는 나이가 들수록 깊어 갔고, 핏과 제인 부인이 결혼 후 의례적인 인사차 다녀간 이래로 저택 정문이 신사의 마차 바퀴를 향해 열린 적은 한 번도 없었다."]], [["That was an awful and unfortunate visit, never to be thought of by the family without horror.", "그것은 끔찍하고도 불운한 방문이었으니, 그 집안 사람들이 진저리를 치지 않고서는 결코 떠올릴 수 없는 것이었다."], ["Pitt begged his wife, with a ghastly countenance, never to speak of it, and it was only through Mrs.", "핏은 새파랗게 질린 얼굴로 아내에게 그 일은 절대 입 밖에 내지 말라고 사정했다."], ["Bute herself, who still knew everything which took place at the Hall, that the circumstances of Sir Pitt's reception of his son and daughter-in-law were ever known at all.", "그래서 핏 경이 아들과 며느리를 맞이한 정황이 그나마 세상에 알려진 것은 오로지, 여전히 저택에서 일어나는 모든 일을 죄다 꿰고 있던 뷰트 부인 자신을 통해서였다."]], [["As they drove up the avenue of the park in their neat and well-appointed carriage, Pitt remarked with dismay and wrath great gaps among the trees--his trees--which the old Baronet was felling entirely without license.", "말끔하고 잘 갖춰진 마차를 타고 공원 가로수 길을 올라가면서, 핏은 나무들 사이로 — 자기 나무들 사이로 — 군데군데 휑하게 뚫린 큰 구멍들을 보고 경악과 분노를 느꼈다. 늙은 준남작이 아무런 허락도 없이 죄다 베어 버린 자리였다."], ["The park wore an aspect of utter dreariness and ruin.", "공원은 황량하고 폐허 같은 모습을 띠고 있었다."], ["The drives were ill kept, and the neat carriage splashed and floundered in muddy pools along the road.", "마찻길은 제대로 관리되지 않아, 그 말끔한 마차가 길을 따라 진흙 웅덩이에 흙탕물을 튀기며 허우적댔다."], ["The great sweep in front of the terrace and entrance stair was black and covered with mosses; the once trim flower-beds rank and weedy.", "테라스와 현관 계단 앞의 너른 진입로는 거무튀튀하니 이끼로 뒤덮였고, 한때 단정했던 화단은 무성하게 잡초가 우거져 있었다. 저택의 거의 전 면에 걸쳐 덧창이 닫혀 있었다."], ["Shutters were up along almost the whole line of the house; the great hall-door was unbarred after much ringing of the bell; an individual in ribbons was seen flitting up the black oak stair, as Horrocks at length admitted the heir of Queen's Crawley and his bride into the halls of their fathers.", "종을 한참 울린 끝에야 커다란 현관문의 빗장이 풀렸다. 호록스가 마침내 퀸스크롤리의 상속자와 그 신부를 선조들의 저택 안으로 들였을 때, 리본을 두른 어떤 인물이 검은 떡갈나무 계단을 휙 올라가는 것이 눈에 띄었다."], ["He led the way into Sir Pitt's \"Library,\" as it was called, the fumes of tobacco growing stronger as Pitt and Lady Jane approached that apartment, \"Sir Pitt ain't very well,\"", "그는 이른바 핏 경의 \"서재\"라는 곳으로 앞장서 안내했는데, 핏과 제인 부인이 그 방에 가까워질수록 담배 연기 냄새가 점점 짙어졌다."], ["Horrocks remarked apologetically and hinted that his master was afflicted with lumbago.", "\"핏 경께서 그리 평안치 못하십니다.\" 호록스가 변명조로 말하며, 주인이 요통으로 고생하신다는 뜻을 넌지시 비쳤다."]], [["The library looked out on the front walk and park.", "서재는 앞쪽 산책로와 공원이 내다보이는 방이었다."], ["Sir Pitt had opened one of the windows, and was bawling out thence to the postilion and Pitt's servant, who seemed to be about to take the baggage down.", "핏 경은 창문 하나를 열어젖히고는, 짐을 막 내리려는 듯 보이는 역마차 마부와 핏의 하인을 향해 거기서 고래고래 소리를 지르고 있었다."]], [["\"Don't move none of them trunks,\"", "\"그 트렁크들 하나도 내리지 마라.\""], ["he cried, pointing with a pipe which he held in his hand.", "그가 손에 쥔 담뱃대로 가리키며 외쳤다."], ["\"It's only a morning visit, Tucker, you fool.", "\"잠깐 아침 인사나 하러 온 거다, 터커, 이 멍청아."], ["Lor, what cracks that off hoss has in his heels!", "저런, 저 바깥쪽 말은 발굽 뒤가 어쩌면 저렇게 다 갈라졌누!"], ["Ain't there no one at the King's Head to rub 'em a little?", "킹스헤드에 그놈 발굽 좀 문질러 줄 사람도 없단 말이냐?"], ["How do, Pitt?", "잘 있었느냐, 핏?"], ["How do, my dear?", "잘 있었나, 얘야?"], ["Come to see the old man, hay?", "늙은이 보러 왔단 말이지, 응?"], ["'Gad--you've a pretty face, too.", "허, 너 얼굴도 곱구나."], ["You ain't like that old horse-godmother, your mother.", "네 그 늙어 빠진 말상 어미 같지가 않아."], ["Come and give old Pitt a kiss, like a good little gal.\"", "이리 와서 이 늙은 핏한테 입 한 번 맞춰 다오, 착한 아가씨처럼.\""]], [["The embrace disconcerted the daughter-in-law somewhat, as the caresses of the old gentleman, unshorn and perfumed with tobacco, might well do.", "그 포옹은 며느리를 다소 당혹스럽게 했다. 면도도 하지 않고 담배 냄새를 풍기는 늙은 노인의 애무이고 보니 그럴 만도 했다."], ["But she remembered that her brother Southdown had mustachios, and smoked cigars, and submitted to the Baronet with a tolerable grace.", "그러나 그녀는 자기 오라비 사우스다운도 콧수염을 기르고 시가를 피운다는 사실을 떠올리며, 그런대로 우아하게 준남작에게 몸을 내맡겼다."]], [["\"Pitt has got vat,\"", "\"핏이 살이 쪘구나.\""], ["said the Baronet, after this mark of affection.", "이런 애정의 표시를 하고 나서 준남작이 말했다. \"이 녀석이 너한테 아주 긴 설교를 늘어놓더냐, 얘야?"], ["\"Does he read ee very long zermons, my dear?", "백 번째 시편이니, 저녁 찬송이니, 그런 거 말이다, 응 핏?"], ["Hundredth Psalm, Evening Hymn, hay Pitt?", "호록스, 가서 제인 부인께 맘지 포도주 한 잔하고 케이크 좀 가져오너라, 이 덩치만 큰 멍청아."], ["Go and get a glass of Malmsey and a cake for my Lady Jane, Horrocks, you great big booby, and don't stand stearing there like a fat pig.", "살찐 돼지처럼 멀거니 서서 빤히 쳐다보지 말고. 머물다 가라고는 안 하마, 얘야."], ["I won't ask you to stop, my dear; you'll find it too stoopid, and so should I too along a Pitt.", "너무 따분할 테니까. 나도 핏하고 함께 있자면 따분할 거다."], ["I'm an old man now, and like my own ways, and my pipe and backgammon of a night.\"", "난 이제 늙은이라, 내 식대로 사는 게 좋아. 밤에는 내 담뱃대하고 주사위 놀이나 하면서 말이지.\""]], [["\"I can play at backgammon, sir,\"", "\"저도 주사위 놀이를 할 줄 알아요, 어르신.\""], ["said Lady Jane, laughing.", "제인 부인이 웃으며 말했다."], ["\"I used to play with Papa and Miss Crawley, didn't I, Mr. Crawley?\"", "\"아빠랑 크롤리 양하고 곧잘 했었거든요, 그렇죠, 크롤리 씨?\""]], [["\"Lady Jane can play, sir, at the game to which you state that you are so partial,\"", "\"제인 부인도 할 줄 압니다, 어르신. 그토록 즐기신다고 말씀하시는 그 놀이 말입니다.\""], ["Pitt said haughtily.", "핏이 거만하게 말했다."]], [["\"But she wawn't stop for all that.", "\"그래도 묵고 가진 않을 게다."], ["Naw, naw, goo back to Mudbury and give Mrs.", "아냐, 아냐, 머드버리로 돌아가서 린서 부인 장사나 좀 거들어 주려무나."], ["Rincer a benefit; or drive down to the Rectory and ask Buty for a dinner.", "아니면 목사관으로 내려가서 뷰티한테 저녁 한 끼 청하든지. 그 녀석은 너희를 보면 아주 반가워할 게다, 알지?"], ["He'll be charmed to see you, you know; he's so much obliged to you for gettin' the old woman's money.", "늙은 여편네 돈 손에 넣게 해 줬다고 너희한테 어찌나 고마워하는데."], ["Ha, ha!", "하, 하!"], ["Some of it will do to patch up the Hall when I'm gone.\"", "그중 얼마는 내가 죽고 나면 이 저택 손보는 데 쓰이게 될 테고.\""]], [["\"I perceive, sir,\"", "\"보아하니, 어르신,\""], ["said Pitt with a heightened voice, \"that your people will cut down the timber.\"", "핏이 목소리를 높이며 말했다. \"어르신 사람들이 그 나무들을 베어 넘기는 모양입니다만.\""]], [["\"Yees, yees, very fine weather, and seasonable for the time of year,\"", "\"그래, 그래, 날씨 참 좋구나. 이맘때치고는 철에 맞는 날씨야.\""], ["Sir Pitt answered, who had suddenly grown deaf.", "핏 경이 대답했는데, 갑자기 귀가 먹은 척했다."], ["\"But I'm gittin' old, Pitt, now.", "\"하지만 나도 이젠 늙어 가는 거다, 핏."], ["Law bless you, you ain't far from fifty yourself.", "원, 이런, 너도 쉰이 멀지 않았잖느냐."], ["But he wears well, my pretty Lady Jane, don't he?", "그래도 이이는 정정하지요, 어여쁜 제인 부인, 안 그렇소?"], ["It's all godliness, sobriety, and a moral life.", "다 경건함과 절제와 도덕적인 생활 덕분이지."], ["Look at me, I'm not very fur from fowr-score--he, he\"; and he laughed, and took snuff, and leered at her and pinched her hand.", "날 좀 보시오, 난 여든이 멀지 않았는데도 — 허, 허.\" 그러면서 그는 웃고, 코담배를 들이켜고, 그녀를 곁눈질하며 그녀의 손을 꼬집었다."]], [["Pitt once more brought the conversation back to the timber, but the Baronet was deaf again in an instant.", "핏은 다시 한 번 화제를 나무 쪽으로 돌렸지만, 준남작은 또 그 즉시 귀가 먹어 버렸다."]], [["\"I'm gittin' very old, and have been cruel bad this year with the lumbago.", "\"난 아주 늙어 가는 데다, 올해는 요통으로 지독히도 고생했단다."], ["I shan't be here now for long; but I'm glad ee've come, daughter-in-law.", "이제 여기 오래 있지는 못할 게다. 그래도 네가 와 주어서 기쁘구나, 며늘아기야."], ["I like your face, Lady Jane: it's got none of the damned high-boned Binkie look in it; and I'll give ee something pretty, my dear, to go to Court in.\"", "난 네 얼굴이 마음에 든다, 제인 부인. 거기엔 그 빌어먹을 광대뼈 튀어나온 빙키 집안 꼴이 하나도 없거든."], ["And he shuffled across the room to a cupboard, from which he took a little old case containing jewels of some value.", "그래서 너한테 예쁜 걸 하나 주마, 얘야, 궁정에 갈 때 차고 가게.\" 그러더니 그는 발을 질질 끌며 방을 가로질러 찬장으로 다가가, 거기서 제법 값나가는 보석이 든 작고 오래된 함을 하나 꺼냈다."], ["\"Take that,\"", "\"이걸 받아라.\""], ["said he, \"my dear; it belonged to my mother, and afterwards to the first Lady Binkie.", "그가 말했다. \"얘야, 이건 내 어머니 것이었고, 그 후엔 첫 번째 빙키 부인 것이었지."], ["Pretty pearls--never gave 'em the ironmonger's daughter.", "고운 진주란다. 그 철물장수 딸년한테는 절대 주지 않았어."], ["No, no. Take 'em and put 'em up quick,\"", "아무렴, 아니고말고."], ["said he, thrusting the case into his daughter's hand, and clapping the door of the cabinet to, as Horrocks entered with a salver and refreshments.", "받아서 얼른 챙겨 넣어라.\" 그가 함을 며느리의 손에 떠넘기며 말하고는, 호록스가 쟁반에 다과를 받쳐 들고 들어오는 것을 보고 찬장 문을 탁 닫았다."]], [["\"What have you a been and given Pitt's wife?\"", "\"핏의 마누라한테 뭘 주신 거예요?\""], ["said the individual in ribbons, when Pitt and Lady Jane had taken leave of the old gentleman.", "핏과 제인 부인이 노인에게 작별 인사를 하고 떠난 뒤, 그 리본을 두른 인물이 말했다."], ["It was Miss Horrocks, the butler's daughter--the cause of the scandal throughout the county--the lady who reigned now almost supreme at Queen's Crawley.", "집사의 딸인 호록스 양이었다. 온 고을에 퍼진 추문의 장본인이자, 이제 퀸스크롤리에서 거의 무소불위의 권세를 누리는 여인이었다."]], [["The rise and progress of those Ribbons had been marked with dismay by the county and family.", "그 리본 양의 등장과 부상은 그 고을과 그 집안 사람들의 경악 속에 진행되었다."], ["The Ribbons opened an account at the Mudbury Branch Savings Bank; the Ribbons drove to church, monopolising the pony-chaise, which was for the use of the servants at the Hall.", "리본 양은 머드버리 지점 저축은행에 계좌를 텄다. 리본 양은 저택 하인들이 쓰라고 있는 조랑말 마차를 독차지하고 그것을 몰아 교회에 갔다."], ["The domestics were dismissed at her pleasure.", "하인들은 그녀의 기분 내키는 대로 해고되었다."], ["The Scotch gardener, who still lingered on the premises, taking a pride in his walls and hot-houses, and indeed making a pretty good livelihood by the garden, which he farmed, and of which he sold the produce at Southampton, found the Ribbons eating peaches on a sunshiny morning at the south-wall, and had his ears boxed when he remonstrated about this attack on his property.", "아직 저택에 남아, 자기가 가꾼 담장과 온실에 자부심을 느끼며, 사실상 자기가 도지를 부쳐 사우샘프턴에 그 소출을 내다 팔아 제법 괜찮은 생계를 꾸리던 스코틀랜드인 정원사는, 어느 화창한 아침 리본 양이 남쪽 담장에서 복숭아를 따 먹고 있는 것을 보고는 자기 소유물에 대한 이 침범에 항의하다가 따귀를 얻어맞았다."], ["He and his Scotch wife and his Scotch children, the only respectable inhabitants of Queen's Crawley, were forced to migrate, with their goods and their chattels, and left the stately comfortable gardens to go to waste, and the flower-beds to run to seed.", "그와 그의 스코틀랜드인 아내, 그리고 그의 스코틀랜드인 아이들, 퀸스크롤리의 유일하게 점잖은 거주자였던 그들은 가재도구와 세간을 챙겨 떠나도록 내몰렸고, 위풍당당하고 안락하던 정원은 황폐해지도록, 화단은 멋대로 씨앗을 맺도록 버려졌다."], ["Poor Lady Crawley's rose-garden became the dreariest wilderness.", "가엾은 크롤리 부인의 장미 정원은 더없이 황량한 황무지가 되었다."], ["Only two or three domestics shuddered in the bleak old servants' hall.", "음산한 옛 하인 식당에는 하인 두세 명만이 추위에 떨고 있었다."], ["The stables and offices were vacant, and shut up, and half ruined.", "마구간과 사무실은 텅 비고 닫혀 반쯤 허물어졌다."], ["Sir Pitt lived in private, and boozed nightly with Horrocks, his butler or house-steward (as he now began to be called), and the abandoned Ribbons.", "핏 경은 은둔 생활을 했고, 밤마다 (이제 그렇게 불리기 시작한) 집사 겸 가령(家令) 호록스와, 그리고 그 타락한 리본 양과 어울려 술을 진탕 마셨다."], ["The times were very much changed since the period when she drove to Mudbury in the spring-cart and called the small tradesmen \"Sir.\"", "그녀가 짐마차를 몰고 머드버리로 나가 자잘한 장사꾼들을 \"나리\"라고 불러 주던 시절과는 사정이 많이도 달라진 것이다."], ["It may have been shame, or it may have been dislike of his neighbours, but the old Cynic of Queen's Crawley hardly issued from his park-gates at all now.", "부끄러워서였는지, 아니면 이웃들이 싫어서였는지, 퀸스크롤리의 늙은 냉소가는 이제 공원 정문 밖으로 좀처럼 나서지 않았다. 그는 대리인들과 다투었고 소작인들을 편지로 닦달했다."], ["He quarrelled with his agents and screwed his tenants by letter.", "그의 나날은 직접 서신을 처리하며 흘러갔다."], ["His days were passed in conducting his own correspondence; the lawyers and farm-bailiffs who had to do business with him could not reach him but through the Ribbons, who received them at the door of the housekeeper's room, which commanded the back entrance by which they were admitted; and so the Baronet's daily perplexities increased, and his embarrassments multiplied round him.", "그와 거래할 일이 있는 변호사며 농장 관리인들은 리본 양을 통하지 않고는 그에게 닿을 수 없었으니, 그녀는 자기들이 드나드는 뒷문을 굽어보는 가정부 방 문간에서 그들을 맞이했다. 그리하여 준남작의 나날의 골칫거리는 늘어 갔고, 곤경은 그의 주위에서 점점 쌓여만 갔다."]], [["The horror of Pitt Crawley may be imagined, as these reports of his father's dotage reached the most exemplary and correct of gentlemen.", "아버지가 노망이 들었다는 이런 소식들이 더없이 모범적이고 단정한 신사인 핏 크롤리에게 닿았을 때 그가 느낀 경악은 능히 짐작할 만하다. 그는 행여 그 리본 양이 자신의 두 번째 법적 장모로 선포되었다는 소식을 듣게 될까 봐 날마다 떨었다."], ["He trembled daily lest he should hear that the Ribbons was proclaimed his second legal mother-in-law.", "그 처음이자 마지막 방문 이후로, 핏의 우아하고 점잖은 집안에서는 아버지의 이름이 결코 입에 오르지 않았다."], ["After that first and last visit, his father's name was never mentioned in Pitt's polite and genteel establishment.", "그것은 그 집안의 해골이었고, 온 식구가 두려움과 침묵 속에 그 곁을 지나다녔다. 사우스다운 백작 부인은 더없이 자극적인 종교 소책자를 — 머리카락이 쭈뼛 곤두설 만한 소책자를 — 계속 우편 마차 편으로 저택 정문에 떨어뜨렸다."], ["It was the skeleton in his house, and all the family walked by it in terror and silence.", "목사관의 뷰트 부인은 밤마다 밖을 내다보며, 저택이 가려 서 있는 느릅나무 뒤로 하늘이 벌겋게 물들어 그 저택에 불이 난 것은 아닌지 살폈다."], ["The Countess Southdown kept on dropping per coach at the lodge-gate the most exciting tracts, tracts which ought to frighten the hair off your head.", "그 집안의 오랜 벗인 G. 왑샷 경과 H."], ["Mrs. Bute at the parsonage nightly looked out to see if the sky was red over the elms behind which the Hall stood, and the mansion was on fire.", "퍼들스톤 경은 사계 법원에서 핏 경과 한자리에 앉으려 하지 않았고, 사우샘프턴 큰길에서 그 파렴치한이 더럽고 늙은 손을 그들에게 내미는데도 본 척도 하지 않고 지나쳤다."], ["Sir G. Wapshot and Sir H.", "무엇 하나 그에게는 통하지 않았다."], ["Fuddlestone, old friends of the house, wouldn't sit on the bench with Sir Pitt at Quarter Sessions, and cut him dead in the High Street of Southampton, where the reprobate stood offering his dirty old hands to them.", "그는 두 손을 호주머니에 찔러 넣은 채 너털웃음을 터뜨리며 사두마차에 기어올랐다. 그는 사우스다운 부인의 소책자를 보고도 너털웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["Nothing had any effect upon him; he put his hands into his pockets, and burst out laughing, as he scrambled into his carriage and four; he used to burst out laughing at Lady Southdown's tracts; and he laughed at his sons, and at the world, and at the Ribbons when she was angry, which was not seldom.", "그리고 그는 자기 아들들을 보고도, 세상을 보고도, 그리고 리본 양이 화를 낼 때면 — 그런 일이 드물지 않았다 — 그녀를 보고도 웃어 댔다."]], [["Miss Horrocks was installed as housekeeper at Queen's Crawley, and ruled all the domestics there with great majesty and rigour.", "호록스 양은 퀸스크롤리의 가정부로 자리를 잡고 그곳의 모든 하인을 대단한 위엄과 엄격함으로 다스렸다. 모든 하인은 그녀를 \"마님\""], ["All the servants were instructed to address her as \"Mum,\"", "혹은 \"부인\"이라고 부르도록 지시받았다."], ["or \"Madam\"--and there was one little maid, on her promotion, who persisted in calling her \"My Lady,\" without any rebuke on the part of the housekeeper.", "그리고 새로 진급한 어린 하녀 하나는 그녀를 \"마님\"이라 한사코 불러 댔는데, 가정부 쪽에서 이를 나무라는 일은 전혀 없었다."], ["\"There has been better ladies, and there has been worser, Hester,\" was Miss Horrocks'", "\"더 나은 마님도 있었고, 더 못한 마님도 있었지, 헤스터.\""], ["reply to this compliment of her inferior; so she ruled, having supreme power over all except her father, whom, however, she treated with considerable haughtiness, warning him not to be too familiar in his behaviour to one \"as was to be a Baronet's lady.\"", "이것이 손아랫사람의 이 아부에 대한 호록스 양의 대답이었다. 그렇게 그녀는 자기 아버지를 빼고 모두 위에 군림하며 다스렸는데, 아버지에게조차 상당히 거만하게 굴며, \"장차 준남작의 마님이 될 사람\"에게 너무 허물없이 굴지 말라고 경고했다."], ["Indeed, she rehearsed that exalted part in life with great satisfaction to herself, and to the amusement of old Sir Pitt, who chuckled at her airs and graces, and would laugh by the hour together at her assumptions of dignity and imitations of genteel life.", "정말이지 그녀는 그 드높은 인생의 배역을 스스로 대단히 흡족해하며 연습했고, 늙은 핏 경은 이를 재미있어했다. 그는 그녀의 거드름과 점잔을 보고 낄낄거렸고, 그녀가 위엄을 떠는 시늉이며 상류 생활을 흉내 내는 꼴을 보며 한 시간이고 내리 웃어 댔다."], ["He swore it was as good as a play to see her in the character of a fine dame, and he made her put on one of the first Lady Crawley's court-dresses, swearing (entirely to Miss Horrocks' own concurrence) that the dress became her prodigiously, and threatening to drive her off that very instant to Court in a coach-and-four.", "그는 그녀가 귀부인 역을 하는 모습을 보는 것이 연극 한 편 못지않다고 단언하며, 첫 번째 크롤리 부인의 궁정 예복 한 벌을 그녀에게 입혀 놓고는, 그 옷이 그녀에게 기막히게 잘 어울린다고 (전적으로 호록스 양 본인도 같은 생각이었거니와) 떠벌리며, 당장 이 순간 사두마차에 태워 궁정으로 데려가겠다고 으름장을 놓았다."], ["She had the ransacking of the wardrobes of the two defunct ladies, and cut and hacked their posthumous finery so as to suit her own tastes and figure.", "그녀는 작고한 두 부인의 옷장을 마음대로 뒤졌고, 그들이 남기고 간 화려한 옷가지들을 자기 취향과 몸매에 맞도록 자르고 마구 잘라 냈다."], ["And she would have liked to take possession of their jewels and trinkets too; but the old Baronet had locked them away in his private cabinet; nor could she coax or wheedle him out of the keys.", "그리고 그들의 보석과 장신구도 차지하고 싶었으나, 늙은 준남작이 그것들을 자기 개인 함 속에 넣어 잠가 두었던 데다, 아무리 구슬리고 어르고 해도 그에게서 열쇠를 빼낼 수가 없었다."], ["And it is a fact, that some time after she left Queen's Crawley a copy-book belonging to this lady was discovered, which showed that she had taken great pains in private to learn the art of writing in general, and especially of writing her own name as Lady Crawley, Lady Betsy Horrocks, Lady Elizabeth Crawley, &c.", "그리고 이건 엄연한 사실인데, 그녀가 퀸스크롤리를 떠나고 얼마 후 이 여인의 글씨 연습장이 하나 발견되었으니, 거기에는 그녀가 남몰래 글쓰기 전반을, 특히 자기 이름을 크롤리 부인, 베치 호록스 부인, 엘리자베스 크롤리 부인 따위로 쓰는 법을 익히느라 무던히도 공을 들였음이 드러나 있었다."]], [["Though the good people of the Parsonage never went to the Hall and shunned the horrid old dotard its owner, yet they kept a strict knowledge of all that happened there, and were looking out every day for the catastrophe for which Miss Horrocks was also eager.", "목사관의 선량한 사람들은 저택에 결코 발걸음을 하지 않았고 그 주인인 끔찍한 늙은 노망난이를 멀리했으나, 그래도 거기서 일어나는 모든 일을 빈틈없이 파악하고 있었고, 호록스 양 역시 학수고대하던 그 파국을 날마다 기다리고 있었다."], ["But Fate intervened enviously and prevented her from receiving the reward due to such immaculate love and virtue.", "그러나 운명이 시샘하듯 끼어들어, 그토록 티 없는 사랑과 미덕에 마땅히 돌아갈 보상을 그녀가 받지 못하도록 막아 버렸다."]], [["One day the Baronet surprised \"her ladyship,\"", "어느 날 준남작은, 그가 농조로 부르듯 \"마님\"께서 응접실의 그 낡고 음도 맞지 않는 피아노 앞에 — 베키 샤프가 카드리유 곡을 친 이래 거의 손도 댄 적 없던 그 피아노 앞에 — 앉아 있는 모습을 불시에 보게 되었다."], ["as he jocularly called her, seated at that old and tuneless piano in the drawing-room, which had scarcely been touched since Becky Sharp played quadrilles upon it--seated at the piano with the utmost gravity and squalling to the best of her power in imitation of the music which she had sometimes heard.", "그녀는 더없이 진지한 표정으로 피아노 앞에 앉아, 언젠가 들은 적 있는 음악을 흉내 내며 있는 힘껏 빽빽거리고 있었다. 새로 진급한 어린 부엌 하녀는 그 작업이 진행되는 동안 자기 안주인 곁에 서서 자못 황홀해하며 고개를 위아래로 까딱거리고 외쳐 댔다."], ["The little kitchen-maid on her promotion was standing at her mistress's side, quite delighted during the operation, and wagging her head up and down and crying, \"Lor, Mum, 'tis bittiful\"--just like a genteel sycophant in a real drawing-room.", "\"어머, 마님, 정말 아름다워요.\" — 마치 진짜 응접실에 있는 점잖은 아첨꾼이라도 된 양 말이다."]], [["This incident made the old Baronet roar with laughter, as usual.", "이 사건에 늙은 준남작은 늘 그러하듯 와르르 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["He narrated the circumstance a dozen times to Horrocks in the course of the evening, and greatly to the discomfiture of Miss Horrocks.", "그는 그날 저녁 내내 그 정황을 호록스에게 열두 번도 더 떠벌렸는데, 호록스 양으로서는 여간 낭패스러운 노릇이 아니었다."], ["He thrummed on the table as if it had been a musical instrument, and squalled in imitation of her manner of singing.", "그는 식탁을 무슨 악기라도 되는 양 둥둥 두들기며, 그녀가 노래하던 식을 흉내 내어 빽빽거렸다."], ["He vowed that such a beautiful voice ought to be cultivated and declared she ought to have singing-masters, in which proposals she saw nothing ridiculous.", "그는 그렇게 아름다운 목소리는 갈고닦아야 마땅하다고 단언하며, 그녀에게 성악 선생을 붙여 줘야겠다고 선언했는데, 정작 그녀는 이런 제안에서 우스운 구석이라곤 조금도 보지 못했다."], ["He was in great spirits that night, and drank with his friend and butler an extraordinary quantity of rum-and-water--at a very late hour the faithful friend and domestic conducted his master to his bedroom.", "그날 밤 그는 기분이 한껏 들떠서, 자기 벗이자 집사인 자와 더불어 럼주에 물 탄 술을 어마어마하게 마셔 댔다. 아주 늦은 시각에야 그 충실한 벗이자 하인은 주인을 침실로 모셔 갔다."]], [["Half an hour afterwards there was a great hurry and bustle in the house.", "삼십 분쯤 후, 저택 안이 한바탕 분주하게 들썩였다."], ["Lights went about from window to window in the lonely desolate old Hall, whereof but two or three rooms were ordinarily occupied by its owner.", "외따로 떨어진 황량한 옛 저택, 그중 평소 주인이 쓰는 방이라곤 두세 칸뿐인 그 저택의 창에서 창으로 불빛들이 오갔다."], ["Presently, a boy on a pony went galloping off to Mudbury, to the Doctor's house there.", "이윽고 한 소년이 조랑말을 타고 머드버리에 있는 의사 집을 향해 쏜살같이 달려갔다."], ["And in another hour (by which fact we ascertain how carefully the excellent Mrs. Bute Crawley had always kept up an understanding with the great house), that lady in her clogs and calash, the Reverend Bute Crawley, and James Crawley, her son, had walked over from the Rectory through the park, and had entered the mansion by the open hall-door.", "그리고 다시 한 시간 후에 (이 사실로 미루어 그 훌륭한 뷰트 크롤리 부인이 늘 큰 저택과 얼마나 빈틈없이 내통하고 있었는지를 헤아릴 수 있거니와) 나막신을 신고 외출용 두건을 쓴 그 부인과, 뷰트 크롤리 목사와, 그녀의 아들 제임스 크롤리가 목사관에서 공원을 가로질러 걸어와, 열린 현관문으로 저택에 들어섰다."]], [["They passed through the hall and the small oak parlour, on the table of which stood the three tumblers and the empty rum-bottle which had served for Sir Pitt's carouse, and through that apartment into Sir Pitt's study, where they found Miss Horrocks, of the guilty ribbons, with a wild air, trying at the presses and escritoires with a bunch of keys.", "그들은 현관과 작은 떡갈나무 응접실을 지났는데, 그 응접실 탁자 위에는 핏 경의 술판에 쓰인 술잔 세 개와 빈 럼주 병이 놓여 있었다. 그리고 그 방을 지나 핏 경의 서재로 들어섰다."], ["She dropped them with a scream of terror, as little Mrs.", "거기서 그들은 그 죄 많은 리본을 두른 호록스 양이, 미친 듯한 기색으로 열쇠 꾸러미를 들고 장롱과 서랍장들을 열어 보려 애쓰고 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["Bute's eyes flashed out at her from under her black calash.", "검은 두건 아래로 자그마한 뷰트 부인의 두 눈이 번뜩이며 자신을 노려보자, 그녀는 공포의 비명을 지르며 열쇠 꾸러미를 떨어뜨렸다."]], [["\"Look at that, James and Mr. Crawley,\"", "\"저것 좀 봐라, 제임스, 그리고 크롤리 씨.\""], ["cried Mrs. Bute, pointing at the scared figure of the black-eyed, guilty wench.", "뷰트 부인이, 겁에 질린 그 검은 눈의 죄 많은 계집의 모습을 가리키며 외쳤다."]], [["\"He gave 'em me; he gave 'em me!\"", "\"나리께서 주신 거예요, 나리께서 주셨다고요!\""], ["she cried.", "그녀가 울부짖었다."]], [["\"Gave them you, you abandoned creature!\"", "\"네게 주셨다고, 이 타락한 것아!\""], ["screamed Mrs. Bute.", "뷰트 부인이 비명을 질렀다."], ["\"Bear witness, Mr.", "\"증인이 되어 주세요, 크롤리 씨."], ["Crawley, we found this good-for-nothing woman in the act of stealing your brother's property; and she will be hanged, as I always said she would.\"", "우리는 이 쓸모없는 여자가 당신 형님의 재산을 훔치는 현장을 잡은 겁니다. 그러니 이 여자는 교수형을 당할 거예요, 내가 늘 그렇게 될 거라고 말했듯이 말이지요.\""]], [["Betsy Horrocks, quite daunted, flung herself down on her knees, bursting into tears.", "베치 호록스는 잔뜩 기가 죽어 무릎을 털썩 꿇고는 울음을 터뜨렸다."], ["But those who know a really good woman are aware that she is not in a hurry to forgive, and that the humiliation of an enemy is a triumph to her soul.", "그러나 진정으로 선량한 여인을 아는 이들은, 그런 여인이 용서를 서두르는 법이 없으며, 적이 굴욕당하는 것이 그 영혼에는 하나의 승리라는 것을 잘 안다."]], [["\"Ring the bell, James,\"", "\"종을 울려라, 제임스.\""], ["Mrs. Bute said.", "뷰트 부인이 말했다."], ["\"Go on ringing it till the people come.\"", "\"사람들이 올 때까지 계속 울려.\""], ["The three or four domestics resident in the deserted old house came presently at that jangling and continued summons.", "그 버려진 옛 저택에 살던 서너 명의 하인이 그 요란하게 거듭되는 종소리에 곧 모여들었다."]], [["\"Put that woman in the strong-room,\"", "\"저 여자를 광에 가둬라.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"We caught her in the act of robbing Sir Pitt. Mr.", "\"이 여자가 핏 경의 재산을 훔치는 현장을 잡았으니까."], ["Crawley, you'll make out her committal--and, Beddoes, you'll drive her over in the spring cart, in the morning, to Southampton Gaol.\"", "크롤리 씨, 당신은 이 여자 구속영장을 작성해 주세요. 그리고 베도스, 자네는 아침에 짐마차로 이 여자를 사우샘프턴 감옥까지 몰고 가게.\""]], [["\"My dear,\"", "\"여보,\""], ["interposed the Magistrate and Rector--\"she's only--\"", "치안판사이자 목사인 그가 끼어들었다. \"이 여자는 그저—\""]], [["\"Are there no handcuffs?\"", "\"수갑은 없는 게냐?\""], ["Mrs. Bute continued, stamping in her clogs.", "뷰트 부인이 나막신을 신은 발을 쿵쿵 구르며 계속했다."], ["\"There used to be handcuffs.", "\"예전엔 수갑이 있었는데."], ["Where's the creature's abominable father?\"", "이것의 그 망할 아비는 어디 있어?\""]], [["\"He DID give 'em me,\"", "\"나리께서 진짜로 주셨다니까요.\""], ["still cried poor Betsy; \"didn't he, Hester?", "가엾은 베치가 여전히 울부짖었다. \"안 그래, 헤스터?"], ["You saw Sir Pitt--you know you did--give 'em me, ever so long ago--the day after Mudbury fair: not that I want 'em.", "네가 봤잖아 — 너도 알잖아 — 핏 경께서 나한테 주신 거 말이야, 한참 전에 — 머드버리 장날 다음 날에."], ["Take 'em if you think they ain't mine.\"", "내가 이걸 갖고 싶어서 그런 게 아니야."], ["And here the unhappy wretch pulled out from her pocket a large pair of paste shoe-buckles which had excited her admiration, and which she had just appropriated out of one of the bookcases in the study, where they had lain.", "내 게 아니라고 생각하면 가져가라고.\" 그러면서 이 가련한 것은 호주머니에서 커다란 모조 보석 박힌 구두 버클 한 쌍을 꺼냈는데, 그것은 그녀의 탐심을 자극했던 물건으로, 서재 책장 하나에 놓여 있던 것을 방금 슬쩍 가로챈 것이었다."]], [["\"Law, Betsy, how could you go for to tell such a wicked story!\" said Hester, the little kitchen-maid late on her promotion--\"and to Madame Crawley, so good and kind, and his Rev'rince (with a curtsey), and you may search all MY boxes, Mum, I'm sure, and here's my keys as I'm an honest girl, though of pore parents and workhouse bred--and if you find so much as a beggarly bit of lace or a silk stocking out of all the gownds as YOU'VE had the picking of, may I never go to church agin.\"", "\"세상에, 베치, 어쩌면 그렇게 못된 거짓말을 할 수가 있어!\" 얼마 전에야 진급한 어린 부엌 하녀 헤스터가 말했다. \"그것도 마님 크롤리께, 저렇게 선량하고 친절하신 분께, 그리고 목사님께 (무릎을 굽혀 절하며) 말이야. 제 상자는 전부 다 뒤져 보셔도 좋아요, 마님, 정말이에요. 그리고 여기 제 열쇠도 있어요, 저는 정직한 아이거든요, 비록 가난한 부모 밑에서 태어나 구빈원에서 자라긴 했지만 말이에요. 그리고 만약 마님께서 골라 가지신 그 모든 옷가지 중에서 변변찮은 레이스 한 조각이나 비단 양말 한 짝이라도 제게서 나오거든, 다시는 교회에 안 가도 좋아요.\""]], [["\"Give up your keys, you hardened hussy,\"", "\"네 열쇠나 내놔, 이 닳고 닳은 화냥년아.\""], ["hissed out the virtuous little lady in the calash.", "두건을 쓴 그 덕망 높은 자그마한 부인이 쉭쉭거리며 내뱉었다."]], [["\"And here's a candle, Mum, and if you please, Mum, I can show you her room, Mum, and the press in the housekeeper's room, Mum, where she keeps heaps and heaps of things, Mum,\"", "\"그리고 여기 촛불 있어요, 마님, 그리고 괜찮으시다면, 마님, 제가 저 여자 방을 보여 드릴게요, 마님, 그리고 가정부 방에 있는 장롱도요, 마님, 저 여자가 잔뜩 쌓아 둔 물건들이 거기 있거든요, 마님.\""], ["cried out the eager little Hester with a profusion of curtseys.", "의욕에 찬 어린 헤스터가 연신 무릎을 굽혀 대며 외쳤다."]], [["\"Hold your tongue, if you please.", "\"제발 입 좀 다물려무나."], ["I know the room which the creature occupies perfectly well. Mrs.", "저것이 쓰는 방쯤은 나도 똑똑히 알고 있으니까."], ["Brown, have the goodness to come with me, and Beddoes don't you lose sight of that woman,\"", "브라운 부인, 미안하지만 나와 함께 가 주시오. 그리고 베도스, 자네는 저 여자에게서 눈을 떼지 말게.\""], ["said Mrs. Bute, seizing the candle.", "뷰트 부인이 촛불을 집어 들며 말했다."], ["\"Mr. Crawley, you had better go upstairs and see that they are not murdering your unfortunate brother\"--and the calash, escorted by Mrs.", "\"크롤리 씨, 당신은 위층으로 올라가서 저들이 당신의 가엾은 형님을 죽이고 있는 건 아닌지 살펴보는 게 좋겠어요.\""], ["Brown, walked away to the apartment which, as she said truly, she knew perfectly well.", "— 그러고는 그 두건은 브라운 부인을 거느린 채, 그녀가 사실대로 말한 대로 자기가 똑똑히 알고 있는 그 방으로 걸어갔다."]], [["Bute went upstairs and found the Doctor from Mudbury, with the frightened Horrocks over his master in a chair.", "뷰트는 위층으로 올라갔다가, 머드버리에서 온 의사와, 의자에 앉은 주인 위로 굽어선 겁에 질린 호록스를 발견했다."], ["They were trying to bleed Sir Pitt Crawley.", "그들은 핏 크롤리 경의 피를 뽑으려 애쓰고 있었다."]], [["With the early morning an express was sent off to Mr. Pitt Crawley by the Rector's lady, who assumed the command of everything, and had watched the old Baronet through the night.", "이른 아침에, 만사를 손수 지휘하며 밤새도록 늙은 준남작을 지켜본 목사 부인이 핏 크롤리 씨에게 급보를 띄웠다."], ["He had been brought back to a sort of life; he could not speak, but seemed to recognize people.", "그는 일종의 소생 상태로 돌아와 있었다. 말은 못 했으나 사람을 알아보는 듯했다."], ["Mrs. Bute kept resolutely by his bedside.", "뷰트 부인은 결연히 그의 침상 곁을 지켰다."], ["She never seemed to want to sleep, that little woman, and did not close her fiery black eyes once, though the Doctor snored in the arm-chair.", "그 자그마한 여인은 잠 따위는 조금도 원치 않는 듯, 의사가 안락의자에서 코를 고는 동안에도 그 불타는 검은 두 눈을 한 번도 감지 않았다."], ["Horrocks made some wild efforts to assert his authority and assist his master; but Mrs. Bute called him a tipsy old wretch and bade him never show his face again in that house, or he should be transported like his abominable daughter.", "호록스는 자기 권위를 내세우며 주인을 돌보려 몇 차례 거칠게 애써 보았으나, 뷰트 부인은 그를 술 취한 늙은 망나니라 부르며, 다시는 이 집에 얼굴을 비치지 말라고, 안 그러면 그 못된 딸년처럼 유형에 처해질 거라고 호통쳤다."]], [["Terrified by her manner, he slunk down to the oak parlour where Mr.", "그녀의 서슬에 겁먹은 그는 제임스 씨가 있는 떡갈나무 응접실로 슬그머니 내려갔다."], ["James was, who, having tried the bottle standing there and found no liquor in it, ordered Mr.", "제임스 씨는 거기 놓인 술병을 기울여 보고 술이 한 방울도 없자, 호록스 씨에게 럼주 한 병을 더 가져오라 시켰다."], ["Horrocks to get another bottle of rum, which he fetched, with clean glasses, and to which the Rector and his son sat down, ordering Horrocks to put down the keys at that instant and never to show his face again.", "호록스가 깨끗한 잔과 함께 그것을 가져오자, 목사와 그 아들은 거기 자리를 잡고 앉아, 호록스에게 당장 이 순간 열쇠를 내려놓고 다시는 얼굴을 비치지 말라고 명했다."]], [["Cowed by this behaviour, Horrocks gave up the keys, and he and his daughter slunk off silently through the night and gave up possession of the house of Queen's Crawley.", "이런 처사에 기가 꺾인 호록스는 열쇠를 내놓았고, 그와 그의 딸은 밤을 틈타 소리 없이 슬그머니 빠져나가 퀸스크롤리 저택의 점유권을 포기했다."]], [["In Which Becky Is Recognized by the Family\nThe heir of Crawley arrived at home, in due time, after this catastrophe, and henceforth may be said to have reigned in Queen's Crawley.", "크롤리 가문의 후계자는 이 참변이 있은 뒤 머지않아 본가에 당도했고, 이제부터는 퀸스크롤리를 다스렸다고 해도 좋을 것이다."], ["For though the old Baronet survived many months, he never recovered the use of his intellect or his speech completely, and the government of the estate devolved upon his elder son.", "늙은 준남작은 그 후로도 여러 달을 더 살아 있었지만 지력도 언어능력도 끝내 온전히 되찾지 못했으므로, 영지의 통치권은 그 장남에게로 넘어갔던 것이다. 피트가 마주한 영지의 형편은 참으로 기묘한 것이었다."], ["In a strange condition Pitt found it.", "피트 준남작 어른은 늘 무언가를 사들이고 또 저당 잡히는 사람이었다."], ["Sir Pitt was always buying and mortgaging; he had twenty men of business, and quarrels with each; quarrels with all his tenants, and lawsuits with them; lawsuits with the lawyers; lawsuits with the Mining and Dock Companies in which he was proprietor; and with every person with whom he had business.", "거래하는 사무 대리인이 스무 명이나 되었고, 그 하나하나와 다투었다. 소작인 전부와도 다투며 그들과 소송을 벌였고, 변호사들과도 소송을 벌였으며, 자신이 지분을 가진 광산 회사며 부두 회사와도 소송을 벌였고, 사업으로 얽힌 모든 사람과 소송을 벌였다."], ["To unravel these difficulties and to set the estate clear was a task worthy of the orderly and persevering diplomatist of Pumpernickel, and he set himself to work with prodigious assiduity.", "이 난마처럼 얽힌 곤경을 풀어내고 영지를 깨끗이 정리하는 일은 펌퍼니켈의 그 질서정연하고 끈기 있는 외교가에게나 어울릴 만한 과업이었고, 그는 어마어마한 근면함으로 그 일에 착수했다."], ["His whole family, of course, was transported to Queen's Crawley, whither Lady Southdown, of course, came too; and she set about converting the parish under the Rector's nose, and brought down her irregular clergy to the dismay of the angry Mrs Bute.", "물론 그의 온 가족이 퀸스크롤리로 옮겨 왔고, 사우스다운 부인 역시 당연히 함께 따라왔다. 그녀는 목사 코앞에서 교구를 개종시키는 일에 착수했고, 자신을 따르는 비국교 성직자들까지 불러들여 성난 뷰트 부인을 경악하게 만들었다."], ["Sir Pitt had concluded no bargain for the sale of the living of Queen's Crawley; when it should drop, her Ladyship proposed to take the patronage into her own hands and present a young protege to the Rectory, on which subject the diplomatic Pitt said nothing.", "피트 준남작은 퀸스크롤리 목사직의 매각 계약을 아직 맺지 않은 터였다. 그 자리가 비게 되면 부인은 그 임명권을 자기 수중에 넣어 어느 젊은 피후견인을 목사관에 앉힐 작정이었는데, 이 문제에 대해서는 외교가다운 피트가 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["Mrs. Bute's intentions with regard to Miss Betsy Horrocks were not carried into effect, and she paid no visit to Southampton Gaol.", "베치 호럭스 양에 관한 뷰트 부인의 의도는 끝내 실행에 옮겨지지 못했고, 그녀는 사우샘프턴 감옥을 찾아갈 일이 없었다."], ["She and her father left the Hall when the latter took possession of the Crawley Arms in the village, of which he had got a lease from Sir Pitt.", "베치와 그 아비는, 그 아비가 마을의 크롤리암스 여관을 피트 준남작에게서 빌려 차지하게 되자 본가를 떠났다."], ["The ex-butler had obtained a small freehold there likewise, which gave him a vote for the borough.", "그 전직 집사는 그곳에 자그마한 자유보유지까지 손에 넣었고, 그 덕분에 자치구 선거에서 투표권을 한 표 얻었다."], ["The Rector had another of these votes, and these and four others formed the representative body which returned the two members for Queen's Crawley.", "목사도 그런 투표권을 한 표 가지고 있었으니, 이 두 표와 다른 네 표가 퀸스크롤리에서 두 명의 의원을 선출하는 선거인단을 이루었다."]], [["There was a show of courtesy kept up between the Rectory and the Hall ladies, between the younger ones at least, for Mrs.", "목사관과 본가의 숙녀들 사이에는, 적어도 젊은 축들 사이에서는 겉으로나마 예의가 유지되고 있었다."], ["Bute and Lady Southdown never could meet without battles, and gradually ceased seeing each other.", "뷰트 부인과 사우스다운 부인은 만나기만 하면 한바탕 싸움이 벌어지지 않는 법이 없어서 차츰 서로 만나는 일을 그만두게 되었던 것이다."], ["Her Ladyship kept her room when the ladies from the Rectory visited their cousins at the Hall. Perhaps Mr.", "목사관의 숙녀들이 본가의 사촌들을 찾아올 때면 사우스다운 부인은 자기 방에 틀어박혀 있었다."], ["Pitt was not very much displeased at these occasional absences of his mamma-in-law.", "아마 피트는 장모의 이런 이따금의 부재가 그리 못마땅하지만은 않았을 것이다."], ["He believed the Binkie family to be the greatest and wisest and most interesting in the world, and her Ladyship and his aunt had long held ascendency over him; but sometimes he felt that she commanded him too much.", "그는 빙키 가문이 세상에서 가장 위대하고 가장 현명하며 가장 흥미로운 가문이라고 믿었고, 부인과 그의 고모는 오랫동안 그를 손아귀에 쥐고 있었다. 그러나 때로 그는 부인이 자신을 지나치게 좌지우지한다고 느끼는 것이었다."], ["To be considered young was complimentary, doubtless, but at six-and-forty to be treated as a boy was sometimes mortifying.", "젊다고 여겨지는 것이야 의심할 바 없이 듣기 좋은 말이지만, 마흔여섯 살이 되어서도 어린아이 취급을 당하는 일은 이따금 자존심 상하는 노릇이었다."], ["Lady Jane yielded up everything, however, to her mother.", "그렇지만 제인 부인은 모든 것을 어머니에게 양보했다."], ["She was only fond of her children in private, and it was lucky for her that Lady Southdown's multifarious business, her conferences with ministers, and her correspondence with all the missionaries of Africa, Asia, and Australasia, &c., occupied the venerable Countess a great deal, so that she had but little time to devote to her granddaughter, the little Matilda, and her grandson, Master Pitt Crawley.", "그녀가 제 아이들을 어여삐 여기는 것은 오직 남몰래일 때뿐이었으니, 사우스다운 부인의 그 잡다한 일거리며, 장관들과의 회담이며, 아프리카와 아시아와 오스트랄라시아 등지의 온갖 선교사들과 주고받는 서신이 그 존엄하신 백작 부인을 워낙 바쁘게 만든 덕분에, 손녀인 어린 마틸다와 손자인 피트 크롤리 도련님에게 쏟을 시간이 거의 없었던 것은 그녀로서는 다행이었다."], ["The latter was a feeble child, and it was only by prodigious quantities of calomel that Lady Southdown was able to keep him in life at all.", "손자는 허약한 아이여서, 사우스다운 부인이 엄청난 양의 감홍을 먹여야 겨우 목숨을 부지할 수 있을 정도였다."]], [["As for Sir Pitt he retired into those very apartments where Lady Crawley had been previously extinguished, and here was tended by Miss Hester, the girl upon her promotion, with constant care and assiduity.", "피트 준남작 어른으로 말하면, 그는 예전에 크롤리 부인이 숨을 거둔 바로 그 방으로 물러갔고, 그곳에서 출세할 기회를 잡은 처녀 헤스터 양의 한결같은 보살핌과 정성스러운 시중을 받았다. 보수를 두둑이 받는 간병인의 사랑과 충직함과 한결같음에 견줄 만한 것이 세상에 또 어디 있겠는가?"], ["What love, what fidelity, what constancy is there equal to that of a nurse with good wages?", "그들은 베개를 매만지고 칡뿌리 죽을 쑨다. 밤중에 일어나고, 환자의 불평과 짜증을 견딘다."], ["They smooth pillows; and make arrowroot; they get up at nights; they bear complaints and querulousness; they see the sun shining out of doors and don't want to go abroad; they sleep on arm-chairs and eat their meals in solitude; they pass long long evenings doing nothing, watching the embers, and the patient's drink simmering in the jug; they read the weekly paper the whole week through; and Law's Serious Call or the Whole Duty of Man suffices them for literature for the year--and we quarrel with them because, when their relations come to see them once a week, a little gin is smuggled in in their linen basket.", "문밖에 해가 비치는 것을 보고도 나가고 싶어 하지 않는다. 안락의자에서 잠을 자고 끼니는 홀로 때운다."], ["Ladies, what man's love is there that would stand a year's nursing of the object of his affection?", "기나긴 저녁 시간을 아무것도 하지 않고 잿불을 들여다보거나 단지 속에서 보글거리는 환자의 마실 거리를 지켜보며 보낸다. 주간 신문 한 부를 일주일 내내 읽고, 로의 「엄숙한 부르심」이나 「인간의 온전한 본분」이 일 년치 읽을거리로 충분하다."], ["Whereas a nurse will stand by you for ten pounds a quarter, and we think her too highly paid.", "그런데도 우리는 그들의 친척이 일주일에 한 번 찾아올 때 빨래 바구니에 진 한 병이 몰래 숨겨져 들어온다는 이유로 그들과 다투는 것이다."], ["At least Mr.", "숙녀 여러분, 사랑하는 이를 일 년 내내 병구완하며 버텨 낼 남자의 사랑이 어디 있단 말인가?"], ["Crawley grumbled a good deal about paying half as much to Miss Hester for her constant attendance upon the Baronet his father.", "반면 간병인은 분기마다 십 파운드만 주면 곁을 지켜 줄 텐데, 우리는 그조차 너무 많이 쳐준다고 여기는 것이다. 적어도 크롤리는 자기 아버지인 준남작을 한결같이 시중든 헤스터 양에게 그 절반을 치르는 일을 두고도 어지간히 투덜거렸다."]], [["Of sunshiny days this old gentleman was taken out in a chair on the terrace--the very chair which Miss Crawley had had at Brighton, and which had been transported thence with a number of Lady Southdown's effects to Queen's Crawley.", "볕이 좋은 날이면 이 노인은 의자에 실려 테라스로 나가곤 했다. 그 의자란 다름 아닌 크롤리 양이 브라이턴에서 쓰던 것으로, 사우스다운 부인의 여러 세간과 함께 그곳에서 퀸스크롤리로 옮겨 온 것이었다."], ["Lady Jane always walked by the old man, and was an evident favourite with him.", "제인 부인은 늘 노인 곁에서 함께 걸었고, 분명 노인이 아끼는 사람이었다."], ["He used to nod many times to her and smile when she came in, and utter inarticulate deprecatory moans when she was going away.", "노인은 그녀가 들어오면 몇 번이고 고개를 끄덕이며 미소 지었고, 그녀가 떠나려 하면 알아들을 수 없는 만류의 신음을 흘리는 것이었다."], ["When the door shut upon her he would cry and sob--whereupon Hester's face and manner, which was always exceedingly bland and gentle while her lady was present, would change at once, and she would make faces at him and clench her fist and scream out \"Hold your tongue, you stoopid old fool,\"", "문이 그녀의 뒤로 닫히면 그는 울며 흐느꼈다. 그러면 안주인이 곁에 있을 때면 늘 더없이 상냥하고 부드럽던 헤스터의 얼굴과 태도가 즉시 돌변하여, 그를 향해 인상을 쓰고 주먹을 불끈 쥐며 \"입 좀 다물어요, 이 멍청한 늙은 바보야\""], ["and twirl away his chair from the fire which he loved to look at--at which he would cry more.", "하고 소리치고는, 그가 그토록 바라보기 좋아하던 난롯불에서 그의 의자를 홱 돌려놓는 것이었다."], ["For this was all that was left after more than seventy years of cunning, and struggling, and drinking, and scheming, and sin and selfishness--a whimpering old idiot put in and out of bed and cleaned and fed like a baby.", "그러면 노인은 더욱 서럽게 울었다. 칠십 년이 넘는 세월의 교활함과 발버둥과 술과 모략과 죄와 이기심 끝에 남은 것이 이것뿐이었으니, 갓난아기처럼 침대에 누였다 일으켜졌다 하고, 씻기고 먹여지는, 훌쩍거리는 늙은 백치 하나였던 것이다."]], [["At last a day came when the nurse's occupation was over.", "이윽고 간병인의 소임이 끝나는 날이 왔다."], ["Early one morning, as Pitt Crawley was at his steward's and bailiff's books in the study, a knock came to the door, and Hester presented herself, dropping a curtsey, and said,", "어느 날 이른 아침, 피트 크롤리가 서재에서 집사며 영지 관리인의 장부를 들여다보고 있는데 문 두드리는 소리가 났다. 헤스터가 무릎을 굽혀 절을 하며 나타나더니 이렇게 말했다."]], [["\"If you please, Sir Pitt, Sir Pitt died this morning, Sir Pitt.", "\"송구합니다만, 피트 준남작님, 어르신께서 오늘 아침에 돌아가셨습니다, 준남작님."], ["I was a-making of his toast, Sir Pitt, for his gruel, Sir Pitt, which he took every morning regular at six, Sir Pitt, and--I thought I heard a moan-like, Sir Pitt--and--and--and--\"", "제가 어르신 죽에 넣어 드릴 토스트를 굽고 있었습죠, 준남작님, 매일 아침 여섯 시면 어김없이 드시던 그 죽 말입니다요, 준남작님, 그런데 신음 비슷한 소리가 들리지 뭡니까, 준남작님, 그래서, 그래서, 그래서요.\""], ["She dropped another curtsey.", "그녀는 다시 한 번 무릎을 굽혀 절했다."]], [["What was it that made Pitt's pale face flush quite red?", "피트의 창백한 얼굴이 새빨갛게 달아오른 것은 무엇 때문이었을까?"], ["Was it because he was Sir Pitt at last, with a seat in Parliament, and perhaps future honours in prospect?", "그가 마침내 피트 준남작이 되어 의회에 자리를 얻고, 어쩌면 앞날의 영예까지 내다보게 되었기 때문이었을까?"], ["\"I'll clear the estate now with the ready money,\"", "\"이제 가진 돈으로 영지를 깨끗이 정리해야지.\""], ["he thought and rapidly calculated its incumbrances and the improvements which he would make.", "그는 그렇게 생각하며 영지에 걸린 부담과 자신이 해 나갈 개량 사업들을 재빨리 계산해 보았다."], ["He would not use his aunt's money previously lest Sir Pitt should recover and his outlay be in vain.", "그는 그 전까지는 고모의 돈에 손대지 않으려 했으니, 혹여 피트 준남작이 회복하기라도 하면 그 지출이 헛수고가 될까 염려해서였다."]], [["All the blinds were pulled down at the Hall and Rectory: the church bell was tolled, and the chancel hung in black; and Bute Crawley didn't go to a coursing meeting, but went and dined quietly at Fuddleston, where they talked about his deceased brother and young Sir Pitt over their port.", "본가와 목사관의 차양은 모두 내려졌다. 교회 종이 조종을 울렸고, 성단소에는 검은 천이 드리워졌다."], ["Miss Betsy, who was by this time married to a saddler at Mudbury, cried a good deal. The family surgeon rode over and paid his respectful compliments, and inquiries for the health of their ladyships.", "뷰트 크롤리는 사냥 모임에 가는 대신 퍼들스턴으로 가서 조용히 식사를 했고, 거기서 사람들은 포도주를 기울이며 고인이 된 그의 형과 젊은 피트 준남작에 대해 이야기를 나누었다."], ["The death was talked about at Mudbury and at the Crawley Arms, the landlord whereof had become reconciled with the Rector of late, who was occasionally known to step into the parlour and taste Mr.", "이 무렵 머드베리의 마구 장수에게 시집간 베치 양은 한참을 울었다. 집안 주치의가 말을 타고 찾아와 정중히 조의를 표하고 부인들의 안부를 여쭈었다."], ["Horrocks' mild beer.", "머드베리에서도 크롤리암스 여관에서도 그 죽음이 입에 오르내렸는데, 여관 주인은 근래 들어 목사와 화해한 사이였고, 목사는 이따금 그 응접실에 들러 호럭스의 순한 맥주를 맛보는 것으로 알려져 있었다."]], [["\"Shall I write to your brother--or will you?\"", "\"제가 당신 동생에게 편지를 쓸까요, 아니면 당신이 쓰시겠어요?\""], ["asked Lady Jane of her husband, Sir Pitt.", "제인 부인이 남편인 피트 준남작에게 물었다."]], [["\"I will write, of course,\"", "\"당연히 내가 써야지.\" 피트 준남작이 말했다."], ["Sir Pitt said, \"and invite him to the funeral: it will be but becoming.\"", "\"장례식에 초대해야겠소. 그게 도리에 맞는 일이오.\""]], [["\"And--and--Mrs. Rawdon,\"", "\"그리고, 그리고, 로던 부인도요.\""], ["said Lady Jane timidly.", "제인 부인이 조심스레 말했다."]], [["\"Jane!\"", "\"제인!\""], ["said Lady Southdown, \"how can you think of such a thing?\"", "사우스다운 부인이 말했다. \"어찌 그런 생각을 다 한단 말이냐?\""]], [["\"Mrs. Rawdon must of course be asked,\"", "\"로던 부인도 당연히 청해야지요.\""], ["said Sir Pitt, resolutely.", "피트 준남작이 단호하게 말했다."]], [["\"Not whilst I am in the house!\"", "\"내가 이 집에 있는 동안은 안 됩니다!\""], ["said Lady Southdown.", "사우스다운 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Your Ladyship will be pleased to recollect that I am the head of this family,\"", "\"부인께서는 제가 이 집안의 가장이라는 점을 부디 기억해 주시기 바랍니다.\""], ["Sir Pitt replied.", "피트 준남작이 대꾸했다."], ["\"If you please, Lady Jane, you will write a letter to Mrs. Rawdon Crawley, requesting her presence upon this melancholy occasion.\"", "\"제인, 미안하지만 로던 크롤리 부인에게 편지를 써서 이 슬픈 자리에 와 줄 것을 청해 주시오.\""]], [["\"Jane, I forbid you to put pen to paper!\"", "\"제인, 펜에 손도 대지 못하게 하겠다!\""], ["cried the Countess.", "백작 부인이 소리쳤다."]], [["\"I believe I am the head of this family,\"", "\"제가 이 집안의 가장인 줄로 압니다.\""], ["Sir Pitt repeated; \"and however much I may regret any circumstance which may lead to your Ladyship quitting this house, must, if you please, continue to govern it as I see fit.\"", "피트 준남작이 거듭 말했다. \"그리고 부인께서 이 집을 떠나시게 될 어떤 사정이라도 생긴다면 제가 무척 유감스럽겠지만, 송구하나마 저는 제가 옳다고 보는 대로 이 집을 다스려 나가야겠습니다.\""]], [["Lady Southdown rose up as magnificent as Mrs. Siddons in Lady Macbeth and ordered that horses might be put to her carriage.", "사우스다운 부인은 「맥베스」의 맥베스 부인 역을 맡은 시던스 부인만큼이나 위풍당당하게 일어서서 자기 마차에 말을 매라고 분부했다."], ["If her son and daughter turned her out of their house, she would hide her sorrows somewhere in loneliness and pray for their conversion to better thoughts.", "아들과 딸이 제 집에서 자신을 내쫓는다면, 그녀는 어딘가 외로운 곳에 가서 슬픔을 감추고 그들이 더 나은 생각으로 회개하기를 기도하겠노라고 했다."]], [["\"We don't turn you out of our house, Mamma,\"", "\"저희는 어머니를 집에서 내쫓는 게 아니에요, 어머니.\""], ["said the timid Lady Jane imploringly.", "소심한 제인 부인이 애원하듯 말했다."]], [["\"You invite such company to it as no Christian lady should meet, and I will have my horses to-morrow morning.\"", "\"그리스도교도 숙녀라면 결코 마주해서는 안 될 그런 손님을 이 집에 부르겠다니, 나는 내일 아침 내 말을 부르겠다.\""]], [["\"Have the goodness to write, Jane, under my dictation,\"", "\"미안하지만 받아 적어 주시오, 제인. 내가 부르는 대로요.\""], ["said Sir Pitt, rising and throwing himself into an attitude of command, like the portrait of a Gentleman in the Exhibition, \"and begin.", "피트 준남작이 자리에서 일어나 전람회에 걸린 어느 신사의 초상화처럼 명령조의 자세를 취하며 말했다. \"자, 적으시오."], ["'Queen's Crawley, September 14, 1822.--My dear brother--'\"", "'퀸스크롤리에서, 1822년 9월 14일. 친애하는 동생에게.'\""]], [["Hearing these decisive and terrible words, Lady Macbeth, who had been waiting for a sign of weakness or vacillation on the part of her son-in-law, rose and, with a scared look, left the library.", "이 단호하고도 무시무시한 말을 듣자, 사위 쪽에서 행여 약해지거나 흔들리는 기색을 보일까 기다리고 있던 맥베스 부인은 자리에서 일어나 겁먹은 얼굴로 서재를 떠났다."], ["Lady Jane looked up to her husband as if she would fain follow and soothe her mamma, but Pitt forbade his wife to move.", "제인 부인은 어머니를 따라가 달래 주고 싶다는 듯 남편을 올려다보았으나, 피트는 아내에게 움직이지 말라고 일렀다."]], [["\"She won't go away,\" he said.", "\"장모님은 떠나지 않으실 거요.\""], ["\"She has let her house at Brighton and has spent her last half-year's dividends.", "그가 말했다. \"브라이턴의 집을 세놓으셨고 지난 반년 치 배당금도 다 써 버리셨거든."], ["A Countess living at an inn is a ruined woman.", "여관에 묵는 백작 부인이라니 망한 신세나 다름없지."], ["I have been waiting long for an opportunity--to take this--this decisive step, my love; for, as you must perceive, it is impossible that there should be two chiefs in a family: and now, if you please, we will resume the dictation.", "나는 오래전부터 이런 결정적인 한 걸음을 내딛을 기회를 기다려 왔다오, 여보. 당신도 알다시피 한 집안에 우두머리가 둘이나 있을 수는 없는 노릇이니까."], ["'My dear brother, the melancholy intelligence which it is my duty to convey to my family must have been long anticipated by,'\"", "자, 미안하지만 받아쓰던 것을 마저 합시다. '친애하는 동생에게, 내가 우리 가족에게 전해야 할 의무를 진 이 슬픈 소식은 진작부터 예견되었을 줄로—'\""], ["&c.", "운운."]], [["In a word, Pitt having come to his kingdom, and having by good luck, or desert rather, as he considered, assumed almost all the fortune which his other relatives had expected, was determined to treat his family kindly and respectably and make a house of Queen's Crawley once more.", "요컨대 피트는 자기 왕국에 올랐고, 그가 스스로 여기기로는 행운으로, 아니 차라리 그만한 자격으로, 다른 친척들이 기대했던 재산을 거의 모조리 거머쥐게 되었으니, 가족을 너그럽고 점잖게 대우하고 퀸스크롤리를 다시금 번듯한 집안으로 만들겠다고 작정했다."], ["It pleased him to think that he should be its chief.", "그가 그 집안의 우두머리가 되리라는 생각은 그를 흐뭇하게 했다."], ["He proposed to use the vast influence that his commanding talents and position must speedily acquire for him in the county to get his brother placed and his cousins decently provided for, and perhaps had a little sting of repentance as he thought that he was the proprietor of all that they had hoped for. In the course of three or four days'", "그는 자신의 뛰어난 재능과 지위가 머지않아 이 주에서 가져다줄 막대한 영향력을 활용하여 동생에게 자리를 마련해 주고 사촌들을 그럭저럭 살게 해 줄 작정이었는데, 그들이 바라던 모든 것의 소유주가 다름 아닌 자신이라는 생각에 어쩌면 양심의 가책이 살짝 따끔거렸는지도 모른다."], ["reign his bearing was changed and his plans quite fixed: he determined to rule justly and honestly, to depose Lady Southdown, and to be on the friendliest possible terms with all the relations of his blood.", "사나흘간 다스리는 동안 그의 태도는 달라졌고 그의 계획은 확고히 굳어졌다. 그는 공정하고 정직하게 다스리고, 사우스다운 부인을 끌어내리며, 피붙이인 모든 친척과 더없이 다정한 사이로 지내겠노라 마음먹었다."]], [["So he dictated a letter to his brother Rawdon--a solemn and elaborate letter, containing the profoundest observations, couched in the longest words, and filling with wonder the simple little secretary, who wrote under her husband's order.", "그리하여 그는 동생 로던에게 보낼 편지를 받아쓰게 했다. 더없이 심오한 견해를 담고 더없이 긴 낱말들로 짜인, 엄숙하고도 공들인 편지였으니, 남편의 분부를 받아 받아 적던 그 순박한 작은 비서는 경탄을 금치 못했다."], ["\"What an orator this will be,\" thought she, \"when he enters the House of Commons\"", "\"이이가 하원에 들어가면 얼마나 굉장한 웅변가가 될까.\""], ["(on which point, and on the tyranny of Lady Southdown, Pitt had sometimes dropped hints to his wife in bed); \"how wise and good, and what a genius my husband is!", "그녀는 생각했다(이 점에 대해서나 사우스다운 부인의 횡포에 대해서나, 피트는 이따금 잠자리에서 아내에게 슬쩍 운을 떼곤 했다). \"얼마나 현명하고 선량한지, 우리 남편은 정말 천재야!"], ["I fancied him a little cold; but how good, and what a genius!\"", "좀 차가운 사람인 줄로만 알았는데, 이렇게나 선량하고 이렇게나 천재일 줄이야!\""]], [["The fact is, Pitt Crawley had got every word of the letter by heart and had studied it, with diplomatic secrecy, deeply and perfectly, long before he thought fit to communicate it to his astonished wife.", "사실인즉 피트 크롤리는 이 편지를 깜짝 놀랄 아내에게 들려주는 것이 적당하다고 여기기 한참 전에, 외교가다운 은밀함으로 한 자 한 자 깊고 완벽하게 익히고 또 연구하여 죄다 외워 두었던 것이다."]], [["This letter, with a huge black border and seal, was accordingly despatched by Sir Pitt Crawley to his brother the Colonel, in London.", "이리하여 이 편지는, 검은 테두리와 봉인을 큼지막하게 두른 채, 피트 크롤리 준남작의 손을 떠나 런던에 있는 그 동생 대령에게로 부쳐졌다."], ["Rawdon Crawley was but half-pleased at the receipt of it.", "로던 크롤리는 그것을 받고도 그저 반쯤만 기뻐했다."], ["\"What's the use of going down to that stupid place?\"", "\"그 따분한 동네까지 내려가서 뭐 한단 말이야?\""], ["thought he.", "그는 생각했다."], ["\"I can't stand being alone with Pitt after dinner, and horses there and back will cost us twenty pound.\"", "\"식사 끝나고 피트와 단둘이 앉아 있는 건 견딜 수가 없고, 오가는 말 삯만 해도 이십 파운드는 들 텐데.\""]], [["He carried the letter, as he did all difficulties, to Becky, upstairs in her bedroom--with her chocolate, which he always made and took to her of a morning.", "그는 온갖 골치 아픈 일이 생길 때마다 그러했듯이, 이 편지를 들고 위층 침실로 베키를 찾아갔다. 매일 아침 손수 만들어 가져다주는 그 코코아와 함께였다."]], [["He put the tray with the breakfast and the letter on the dressing-table, before which Becky sat combing her yellow hair.", "그는 아침 식사가 담긴 쟁반과 편지를 화장대 위에 내려놓았는데, 베키는 그 앞에 앉아 노란 머리칼을 빗고 있었다."], ["She took up the black-edged missive, and having read it, she jumped up from the chair, crying \"Hurray!\"", "그녀는 검은 테를 두른 그 편지를 집어 들어 읽고 나더니, 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 \"만세!\""], ["and waving the note round her head.", "하고 외치며 그 쪽지를 머리 위로 빙빙 휘둘렀다."]], [["\"Hurray?\"", "\"만세라고?\""], ["said Rawdon, wondering at the little figure capering about in a streaming flannel dressing-gown, with tawny locks dishevelled.", "로던이, 황갈색 머리칼을 헝클어뜨린 채 펄럭이는 플란넬 실내복 차림으로 깡충깡충 뛰어다니는 그 자그마한 사람을 의아하게 바라보며 말했다."], ["\"He's not left us anything, Becky.", "\"우리한테 남긴 거라곤 아무것도 없잖아, 베키."], ["I had my share when I came of age.\"", "내 몫은 성년이 됐을 때 이미 받았는걸.\""]], [["\"You'll never be of age, you silly old man,\" Becky replied. \"Run out now to Madam Brunoy's, for I must have some mourning: and get a crape on your hat, and a black waistcoat--I don't think you've got one; order it to be brought home to-morrow, so that we may be able to start on Thursday.\"", "\"당신은 영영 성년이 될 일이 없는 사람이에요, 이 어리석은 양반아.\" 베키가 대꾸했다. \"어서 브뤼노이 부인네로 달려가요. 상복이 있어야 하니까. 그리고 당신 모자에 검은 상장(喪章)을 두르고 검은 조끼도 마련해요. 아마 한 벌도 없을 거예요. 내일 집으로 가져오라고 시켜요. 그래야 목요일에 떠날 수 있으니까.\""]], [["\"You don't mean to go?\"", "\"갈 생각인 건 아니지?\""], ["Rawdon interposed.", "로던이 끼어들었다."]], [["\"Of course I mean to go.", "\"당연히 갈 생각이죠."], ["I mean that Lady Jane shall present me at Court next year.", "내년에 제인 부인이 나를 궁정에 소개시켜 줘야 하니까요."], ["I mean that your brother shall give you a seat in Parliament, you stupid old creature.", "당신 형이 당신에게 의회 자리 하나를 마련해 줘야 하니까요, 이 어리석은 양반아."], ["I mean that Lord Steyne shall have your vote and his, my dear, old silly man; and that you shall be an Irish Secretary, or a West Indian Governor: or a Treasurer, or a Consul, or some such thing.\"", "스테인 경이 당신 표와 형님 표를 얻도록 해야 하니까요, 여보. 그래서 당신이 아일랜드 차관이나 서인도 제도 총독이 되든가, 아니면 재무관이나 영사나 뭐 그런 자리에 오르도록요.\""]], [["\"Posting will cost a dooce of a lot of money,\"", "\"역마 삯이 어마어마하게 들 텐데.\""], ["grumbled Rawdon.", "로던이 투덜거렸다."]], [["\"We might take Southdown's carriage, which ought to be present at the funeral, as he is a relation of the family: but, no--I intend that we shall go by the coach.", "\"사우스다운의 마차를 빌릴 수도 있겠죠. 그분도 가문의 친척이니 장례식에 참석하는 게 마땅하니까."], ["They'll like it better.", "하지만 아니에요, 우리는 역마차를 타고 가는 게 좋겠어요."], ["It seems more humble--\"", "그쪽이 더 마음에 들 거예요. 더 겸손해 보이거든요.\""]], [["\"Rawdy goes, of course?\"", "\"로디도 당연히 가는 거지?\""], ["the Colonel asked.", "대령이 물었다."]], [["\"No such thing; why pay an extra place?", "\"그럴 리가요. 자리 하나를 더 사느라 돈을 왜 써요?"], ["He's too big to travel bodkin between you and me.", "그 애는 너무 커서 당신과 나 사이에 끼여 갈 수도 없어요."], ["Let him stay here in the nursery, and Briggs can make him a black frock.", "육아실에 그냥 두고, 브리그스더러 검은 옷이나 하나 지어 입히라고 해요."], ["Go you, and do as I bid you.", "당신이나 가서 내가 시키는 대로 하세요."], ["And you had best tell Sparks, your man, that old Sir Pitt is dead and that you will come in for something considerable when the affairs are arranged.", "그리고 하인 스파크스한테는, 늙은 피트 준남작이 죽었으니 일이 정리되면 당신이 상당한 재산을 물려받게 될 거라고 일러두는 게 좋겠어요."], ["He'll tell this to Raggles, who has been pressing for money, and it will console poor Raggles.\"", "그러면 그가 래글스한테 그 말을 전할 텐데, 래글스가 돈을 갚으라고 졸라 대고 있으니, 가엾은 래글스에게 위안이 되겠죠.\""], ["And so Becky began sipping her chocolate.", "그러고서 베키는 코코아를 홀짝이기 시작했다."]], [["When the faithful Lord Steyne arrived in the evening, he found Becky and her companion, who was no other than our friend Briggs, busy cutting, ripping, snipping, and tearing all sorts of black stuffs available for the melancholy occasion.", "저녁이 되어 충직한 스테인 경이 당도했을 때, 그는 베키와 그녀의 말동무가—다름 아닌 우리의 벗 브리그스였다—이 슬픈 자리에 쓸 만한 온갖 검은 천을 자르고 뜯고 가위질하고 찢느라 분주한 모습을 보았다."]], [["\"Miss Briggs and I are plunged in grief and despondency for the death of our Papa,\"", "\"브리그스 양과 저는 우리 아버님의 죽음에 슬픔과 비탄에 잠겨 있답니다.\""], ["Rebecca said.", "레베카가 말했다."], ["\"Sir Pitt Crawley is dead, my lord.", "\"피트 크롤리 준남작이 돌아가셨거든요, 경."], ["We have been tearing our hair all the morning, and now we are tearing up our old clothes.\"", "오전 내내 머리칼을 쥐어뜯다가, 이제는 낡은 옷가지를 쥐어뜯고 있지요.\""]], [["\"Oh, Rebecca, how can you--\"", "\"오, 레베카, 어쩌면 그렇게—\""], ["was all that Briggs could say as she turned up her eyes.", "브리그스는 눈을 치켜뜨며 그렇게밖에 말하지 못했다."]], [["\"Oh, Rebecca, how can you--\"", "\"오, 레베카, 어쩌면 그렇게—\""], ["echoed my Lord.", "경도 따라 말했다."], ["\"So that old scoundrel's dead, is he?", "\"그래, 그 늙은 악당이 죽었단 말이지?"], ["He might have been a Peer if he had played his cards better.", "패만 좀 더 잘 썼더라면 귀족이 될 수도 있었을 텐데."], ["Mr. Pitt had very nearly made him; but he ratted always at the wrong time.", "피트 씨가 거의 다 만들어 줄 뻔했는데, 늘 엉뚱한 때에 변절을 했단 말이야."], ["What an old Silenus it was!\"", "어쩜 그렇게 늙은 실레노스 같았는지!\""]], [["\"I might have been Silenus's widow,\"", "\"하마터면 내가 그 실레노스의 미망인이 될 뻔했지요.\""], ["said Rebecca.", "레베카가 말했다."], ["\"Don't you remember, Miss Briggs, how you peeped in at the door and saw old Sir Pitt on his knees to me?\"", "\"브리그스 양, 기억나지 않아요? 당신이 문틈으로 들여다봤을 때 늙은 피트 준남작이 내 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 있었던 거 말이에요.\""], ["Miss Briggs, our old friend, blushed very much at this reminiscence, and was glad when Lord Steyne ordered her to go downstairs and make him a cup of tea.", "우리의 옛 벗 브리그스 양은 이 회상에 얼굴이 몹시 붉어졌고, 스테인 경이 그녀더러 아래층에 내려가 차 한 잔을 끓여 오라고 분부했을 때 적이 안도했다."]], [["Briggs was the house-dog whom Rebecca had provided as guardian of her innocence and reputation.", "브리그스는 레베카가 자기 순결과 평판을 지킬 파수꾼으로 마련해 둔 집 지키는 개였다."], ["Miss Crawley had left her a little annuity.", "크롤리 양은 그녀에게 자그마한 연금을 남겼었다."], ["She would have been content to remain in the Crawley family with Lady Jane, who was good to her and to everybody; but Lady Southdown dismissed poor Briggs as quickly as decency permitted; and Mr.", "그녀로서는 자신에게도 모든 이에게도 다정한 제인 부인과 함께 크롤리 가문에 그대로 머무는 것으로 흡족했을 것이다."], ["Pitt (who thought himself much injured by the uncalled-for generosity of his deceased relative towards a lady who had only been Miss Crawley's faithful retainer a score of years) made no objection to that exercise of the dowager's authority.", "그러나 사우스다운 부인은 예의가 허락하는 한 최대한 빠르게 가엾은 브리그스를 내보냈고, 피트는(이십 년 남짓 크롤리 양의 충직한 하인 노릇을 했을 뿐인 여자에게 고인이 된 친척이 청하지도 않은 후의를 베푼 탓에 자신이 큰 손해를 봤다고 여겼다) 그 노부인이 권한을 행사하는 데 아무 이의도 달지 않았다."], ["Bowls and Firkin likewise received their legacies and their dismissals, and married and set up a lodging-house, according to the custom of their kind.", "보울스와 퍼킨 역시 유산과 더불어 해고 통지를 받았고, 그런 부류의 관례에 따라 결혼하여 하숙집을 차렸다."]], [["Briggs tried to live with her relations in the country, but found that attempt was vain after the better society to which she had been accustomed.", "브리그스는 시골에 사는 친척들과 함께 살아 보려 했으나, 익숙해진 더 나은 사교계를 겪은 뒤라 그 시도가 헛된 것임을 깨달았다."], ["Briggs's friends, small tradesmen, in a country town, quarrelled over Miss Briggs's forty pounds a year as eagerly and more openly than Miss Crawley's kinsfolk had for that lady's inheritance.", "어느 시골 읍내의 영세 상인인 브리그스의 친지들은, 크롤리 양의 친척들이 그 부인의 유산을 두고 다투었던 것 못지않게, 아니 그보다 더 노골적으로 브리그스 양의 연 사십 파운드를 두고 다투었던 것이다."], ["Briggs's brother, a radical hatter and grocer, called his sister a purse-proud aristocrat, because she would not advance a part of her capital to stock his shop; and she would have done so most likely, but that their sister, a dissenting shoemaker's lady, at variance with the hatter and grocer, who went to another chapel, showed how their brother was on the verge of bankruptcy, and took possession of Briggs for a while.", "급진파 모자 장수 겸 식료품상인 브리그스의 오라비는, 누이가 제 가게에 물건을 채워 넣을 자본의 일부를 대주려 하지 않는다는 이유로 그녀를 돈푼깨나 있다고 거드름 피우는 귀족 나부랭이라고 불렀다. 사실 그녀는 그렇게 해 주었을 공산이 컸지만, 다른 예배당에 다니는 그 모자 장수 겸 식료품상과 사이가 틀어진 자매—비국교파 구두장이의 부인 되는 이가—오라비가 파산 직전에 몰려 있음을 일러 주고는 한동안 브리그스를 차지하고 말았다."], ["The dissenting shoemaker wanted Miss Briggs to send his son to college and make a gentleman of him.", "그 비국교파 구두장이는 브리그스 양이 자기 아들을 대학에 보내 신사로 만들어 주기를 바랐다."], ["Between them the two families got a great portion of her private savings out of her, and finally she fled to London followed by the anathemas of both, and determined to seek for servitude again as infinitely less onerous than liberty.", "두 집안은 작당하여 그녀의 사사로운 저축 가운데 상당 부분을 우려냈고, 마침내 그녀는 양쪽의 저주를 등에 진 채 런던으로 달아났으니, 하녀살이가 자유보다 한없이 덜 고된 것이라 여기고 다시금 그 일자리를 구하기로 마음먹은 것이었다."], ["And advertising in the papers that a \"Gentlewoman of agreeable manners, and accustomed to the best society, was anxious to,\"", "그리하여 \"더없이 상냥한 몸가짐에 최상류 사교계에 익숙한 숙녀가 간절히 바라는 바는\""], ["&c., she took up her residence with Mr. Bowls in Half Moon Street, and waited the result of the advertisement.", "운운하는 광고를 신문에 내고, 그녀는 하프문 거리의 보울스 씨 집에 거처를 정한 채 그 광고의 결과를 기다렸다."]], [["So it was that she fell in with Rebecca. Mrs.", "레베카와 마주친 것이 바로 그러한 연유에서였다."], ["Rawdon's dashing little carriage and ponies was whirling down the street one day, just as Miss Briggs, fatigued, had reached Mr. Bowls's door, after a weary walk to the Times Office in the City to insert her advertisement for the sixth time.", "어느 날 로던 부인의 맵시 좋은 작은 마차와 조랑말들이 거리를 휙 달려 내려가는데, 마침 브리그스 양이 광고를 여섯 번째로 내려고 시내의 타임스 사무소까지 고단하게 걸어갔다가 지친 몸으로 보울스 씨의 문 앞에 막 다다른 참이었다."], ["Rebecca was driving, and at once recognized the gentlewoman with agreeable manners, and being a perfectly good-humoured woman, as we have seen, and having a regard for Briggs, she pulled up the ponies at the doorsteps, gave the reins to the groom, and jumping out, had hold of both Briggs's hands, before she of the agreeable manners had recovered from the shock of seeing an old friend.", "레베카가 고삐를 쥐고 있다가 단번에 그 상냥한 몸가짐의 숙녀를 알아보고는, 앞서 보았듯 더없이 쾌활한 여자인 데다 브리그스에게 호감을 품고 있던 터라, 문간 계단 앞에서 조랑말을 멈추고 고삐를 마부에게 넘긴 뒤 훌쩍 뛰어내려, 그 상냥한 몸가짐의 여인이 옛 벗을 본 충격에서 미처 헤어나기도 전에 브리그스의 두 손을 덥석 붙잡았다."]], [["Briggs cried, and Becky laughed a great deal and kissed the gentlewoman as soon as they got into the passage; and thence into Mrs.", "브리그스는 울었고 베키는 한참을 웃었으며, 복도로 들어서자마자 그 숙녀에게 입을 맞추었다."], ["Bowls's front parlour, with the red moreen curtains, and the round looking-glass, with the chained eagle above, gazing upon the back of the ticket in the window which announced \"Apartments to Let.\"", "그러고는 거기서 보울스 부인의 앞쪽 응접실로 들어갔으니, 붉은 모린 천 커튼과 둥근 거울이 있고 그 위에 사슬에 묶인 독수리 장식이 얹혀 있어, 창가에 놓인 \"셋방 있음\"이라고 적힌 안내판의 뒷면을 내려다보는 방이었다."]], [["Briggs told all her history amidst those perfectly uncalled-for sobs and ejaculations of wonder with which women of her soft nature salute an old acquaintance, or regard a rencontre in the street; for though people meet other people every day, yet some there are who insist upon discovering miracles; and women, even though they have disliked each other, begin to cry when they meet, deploring and remembering the time when they last quarrelled.", "브리그스는 자신의 온 사연을 털어놓았는데, 그 사이사이 그처럼 여린 천성의 여인들이 옛 지인을 맞거나 길거리의 우연한 해후를 대할 때면 으레 그러하듯, 도무지 그럴 까닭이 없는 흐느낌과 놀라움의 감탄사가 섞여 들었다. 사람들이야 날마다 다른 사람들과 마주치는 법이건만, 그중에는 한사코 그것을 기적인 양 여기려 드는 이들이 있다."], ["So, in a word, Briggs told all her history, and Becky gave a narrative of her own life, with her usual artlessness and candour.", "게다가 여인들이란 서로 미워했던 사이라도 막상 만나면 울음부터 터뜨리며, 마지막으로 다투었던 그때를 한탄하고 떠올리는 것이다. 그리하여 한마디로 브리그스는 자신의 온 사연을 털어놓았고, 베키는 늘 그러하듯 천연덕스럽고 솔직한 태도로 제 인생 이야기를 들려주었다."]], [["Mrs. Bowls, late Firkin, came and listened grimly in the passage to the hysterical sniffling and giggling which went on in the front parlour.", "전에 퍼킨이었던 보울스 부인이 복도로 나와, 앞쪽 응접실에서 벌어지는 그 신경질적인 훌쩍거림과 키득거림을 못마땅한 얼굴로 엿들었다."], ["Becky had never been a favourite of hers.", "베키는 한 번도 그녀의 마음에 든 적이 없었다."], ["Since the establishment of the married couple in London they had frequented their former friends of the house of Raggles, and did not like the latter's account of the Colonel's menage.", "이 부부가 런던에 자리를 잡은 뒤로 그들은 래글스 집안의 옛 친구들과 자주 어울렸는데, 대령네 살림에 대한 래글스의 이야기가 영 마음에 들지 않았던 것이다."], ["\"I wouldn't trust him, Ragg, my boy,\"", "\"난 그자를 못 믿겠네, 래그.\""], ["Bowls remarked; and his wife, when Mrs.", "보울스가 한마디 했다."], ["Rawdon issued from the parlour, only saluted the lady with a very sour curtsey; and her fingers were like so many sausages, cold and lifeless, when she held them out in deference to Mrs.", "그리하여 그의 아내는, 로던 부인이 응접실에서 나오자 그저 더없이 떨떠름한 절로 그 부인을 맞이했을 뿐이었다. 그 손가락은 마치 소시지를 여러 개 늘어놓은 듯 차갑고 생기 없었으니, 한사코 이 전직 하녀와 악수를 하려 드는 로던 부인을 향해 마지못해 손을 내밀었던 것이다."], ["Rawdon, who persisted in shaking hands with the retired lady's maid. She whirled away into Piccadilly, nodding with the sweetest of smiles towards Miss Briggs, who hung nodding at the window close under the advertisement-card, and at the next moment was in the park with a half-dozen of dandies cantering after her carriage.", "부인은 광고판 바로 아래 창가에 매달려 고개를 끄덕이는 브리그스 양을 향해 더없이 다정한 미소로 고갯짓을 보내며 피커딜리 쪽으로 휙 내달렸고, 다음 순간 그녀는 대여섯의 멋쟁이들이 마차 뒤를 천천히 달려 따라오는 가운데 공원에 들어서 있었다."]], [["When she found how her friend was situated, and how having a snug legacy from Miss Crawley, salary was no object to our gentlewoman, Becky instantly formed some benevolent little domestic plans concerning her.", "그녀는 옛 벗이 처한 형편을 알게 되었고, 크롤리 양에게서 받은 짭짤한 유산이 있어 우리의 그 숙녀에게 봉급 따위는 문제가 되지 않는다는 것을 알게 되자, 베키는 즉시 그녀와 관련하여 자비로운 가정사 계획을 몇 가지 세웠다."], ["This was just such a companion as would suit her establishment, and she invited Briggs to come to dinner with her that very evening, when she should see Becky's dear little darling Rawdon.", "그녀의 살림에 딱 어울릴 만한 말동무였던 것이다. 베키는 바로 그날 저녁 함께 식사하자며 브리그스를 초대했으니, 그 자리에서 베키의 사랑스러운 어린 로던도 볼 수 있다고 했다."]], [["Mrs. Bowls cautioned her lodger against venturing into the lion's den, \"wherein you will rue it, Miss B., mark my words, and as sure as my name is Bowls.\"", "보울스 부인은 자기 하숙인에게 사자 굴로 함부로 들어가지 말라고 경고했다."], ["And Briggs promised to be very cautious.", "\"거기 들어가면 후회하실 거예요, 브리그스 양, 내 말을 잘 새겨들으세요."], ["The upshot of which caution was that she went to live with Mrs.", "내 이름이 보울스인 게 틀림없는 것만큼이나 분명한 일이라고요.\" 그러자 브리그스는 무척 조심하겠노라 다짐했다."], ["Rawdon the next week, and had lent Rawdon Crawley six hundred pounds upon annuity before six months were over.", "그 다짐의 결말인즉, 그녀는 이튿날 한 주가 지나 로던 부인과 함께 살러 들어갔고, 채 반년도 지나기 전에 로던 크롤리에게 육백 파운드를 연금 조건으로 빌려주었다는 것이다."]], [["In Which Becky Revisits the Halls of Her Ancestors\nSo the mourning being ready, and Sir Pitt Crawley warned of their arrival, Colonel Crawley and his wife took a couple of places in the same old High-flyer coach by which Rebecca had travelled in the defunct Baronet's company, on her first journey into the world some nine years before.", "그리하여 상복이 갖추어지고 피트 크롤리 경에게도 그들의 도착이 알려지자, 크롤리 대령과 그의 아내는 바로 그 낡은 하이플라이어 역마차에 두 자리를 잡았다. 레베카가 구 년쯤 전 처음 세상으로 나서던 그 길에, 지금은 고인이 된 준남작과 함께 타고 갔던 바로 그 마차였다."], ["How well she remembered the Inn Yard, and the ostler to whom she refused money, and the insinuating Cambridge lad who wrapped her in his coat on the journey!", "그녀는 그 여관 마당이며, 돈을 거절했던 마부며, 길 위에서 자기 외투로 그녀를 감싸 주던 그 알랑거리던 케임브리지 청년을 어찌나 또렷이 기억하고 있던지!"], ["Rawdon took his place outside, and would have liked to drive, but his grief forbade him.", "로던은 바깥자리를 잡았고 직접 말을 몰고 싶었으나 슬픔이 그것을 허락지 않았다."], ["He sat by the coachman and talked about horses and the road the whole way; and who kept the inns, and who horsed the coach by which he had travelled so many a time, when he and Pitt were boys going to Eton.", "그는 마부 옆에 앉아 가는 내내 말이며 길에 대해, 그리고 누가 그 여관들을 운영하는지, 그와 피트가 소년 시절 이튼으로 가던 길에 숱하게 탔던 그 마차에 누가 말을 대 주는지 따위를 이야기했다."], ["At Mudbury a carriage and a pair of horses received them, with a coachman in black.", "머드베리에 이르자 검은 옷을 입은 마부가 모는 한 쌍의 말이 끄는 마차가 그들을 맞이했다."], ["\"It's the old drag, Rawdon,\"", "\"옛날 그 마차네요, 로던.\""], ["Rebecca said as they got in.", "레베카가 올라타며 말했다."], ["\"The worms have eaten the cloth a good deal--there's the stain which Sir Pitt--ha!", "\"좀이 천을 꽤나 갉아먹었군요. 저기 얼룩 좀 봐요, 피트 경이, 아!"], ["I see Dawson the Ironmonger has his shutters up--which Sir Pitt made such a noise about.", "철물상 도슨이 덧문을 닫아 두었네요, 피트 경이 어찌나 야단법석을 떨었던지요."], ["It was a bottle of cherry brandy he broke which we went to fetch for your aunt from Southampton.", "우리가 당신 고모님 드린다고 사우샘프턴에서 가져오던 그 체리 브랜디 한 병을 깨뜨렸을 때 말이에요."], ["How time flies, to be sure!", "정말이지 세월 참 빠르기도 하지!"], ["That can't be Polly Talboys, that bouncing girl standing by her mother at the cottage there.", "저기 오두막에서 어머니 곁에 서 있는 저 통통한 처녀, 설마 폴리 톨보이스는 아니겠지요."], ["I remember her a mangy little urchin picking weeds in the garden.\"", "정원에서 잡초나 뜯던 옴 오른 꼬마였던 게 기억나는데.\""]], [["\"Fine gal,\"", "\"고운 처녀로군.\""], ["said Rawdon, returning the salute which the cottage gave him, by two fingers applied to his crape hatband.", "로던이 그 오두막에서 보내온 인사에, 상장(喪章)을 두른 모자챙에 손가락 두 개를 갖다 대며 답례하고는 말했다."], ["Becky bowed and saluted, and recognized people here and there graciously.", "베키는 고개 숙여 인사하며 여기저기서 사람들을 우아하게 알아보았다."], ["These recognitions were inexpressibly pleasant to her.", "이런 알은체는 그녀에게 말할 수 없이 기분 좋은 일이었다."], ["It seemed as if she was not an imposter any more, and was coming to the home of her ancestors.", "이제 자신이 더는 사기꾼이 아니라, 조상 대대로 내려온 집안의 고향으로 돌아오고 있는 듯한 기분이었다."], ["Rawdon was rather abashed and cast down, on the other hand.", "반면 로던은 다소 멋쩍고 풀이 죽어 있었다."], ["What recollections of boyhood and innocence might have been flitting across his brain?", "소년 시절과 순수했던 날들의 어떤 기억이 그의 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔을까?"], ["What pangs of dim remorse and doubt and shame?", "어렴풋한 회한과 의혹과 부끄러움의 어떤 아픔이 일었을까?"]], [["\"Your sisters must be young women now,\"", "\"당신 누이들도 이제 다 큰 처녀가 되었겠어요.\""], ["Rebecca said, thinking of those girls for the first time perhaps since she had left them.", "레베카가 말했다. 어쩌면 그 아이들 곁을 떠난 이래 처음으로 그들을 떠올린 것이었다."]], [["\"Don't know, I'm shaw,\"", "\"글쎄, 나야 모르지.\""], ["replied the Colonel.", "대령이 대답했다."], ["\"Hullo!", "\"어이!"], ["here's old Mother Lock.", "저기 락 할멈이 있군."], ["How-dy-do, Mrs. Lock?", "어떻게 지내시오, 락 부인?"], ["Remember me, don't you?", "날 기억하지요, 안 그렇소?"], ["Master Rawdon, hey?", "로던 도련님이오, 응?"], ["Dammy how those old women last; she was a hundred when I was a boy.\"", "제기랄, 저 할멈들은 어찌 저리 오래 사는지. 내가 어릴 적에도 백 살은 돼 보였는데.\""]], [["They were going through the lodge-gates kept by old Mrs.", "그들은 락 할멈이 지키는 정문을 지나는 참이었다."], ["Lock, whose hand Rebecca insisted upon shaking, as she flung open the creaking old iron gate, and the carriage passed between the two moss-grown pillars surmounted by the dove and serpent.", "할멈이 삐걱대는 낡은 철문을 활짝 열어젖히자, 레베카는 굳이 그 손을 잡고 흔들어야겠다고 고집을 부렸고, 마차는 비둘기와 뱀 조각이 얹힌 이끼 낀 두 기둥 사이를 지나갔다."]], [["\"The governor has cut into the timber,\"", "\"형님이 나무를 베어 냈군.\""], ["Rawdon said, looking about, and then was silent--so was Becky.", "로던이 주위를 둘러보며 말하고는 입을 다물었다. 베키도 마찬가지였다."], ["Both of them were rather agitated, and thinking of old times.", "둘 다 다소 마음이 동요되어 옛 시절을 떠올리고 있었다."], ["He about Eton, and his mother, whom he remembered, a frigid demure woman, and a sister who died, of whom he had been passionately fond; and how he used to thrash Pitt; and about little Rawdy at home.", "그는 이튼을, 그리고 차갑고 새침했던 여인으로 기억되는 자기 어머니를, 또 자기가 끔찍이도 아꼈으나 죽고 만 누이를 떠올렸다. 또 자기가 피트를 어떻게 두들겨 패곤 했는지를, 그리고 집에 있는 어린 로디를 떠올렸다."], ["And Rebecca thought about her own youth and the dark secrets of those early tainted days; and of her entrance into life by yonder gates; and of Miss Pinkerton, and Joe, and Amelia.", "레베카는 자기 자신의 어린 시절과 일찍부터 더럽혀진 그 나날의 어두운 비밀들을, 그리고 저 문을 지나 세상에 첫발을 들이던 일을, 그리고 핑커튼 양과 조와 애밀리아를 떠올렸다."]], [["The gravel walk and terrace had been scraped quite clean.", "자갈길과 테라스는 말끔히 쓸려 깨끗했다."], ["A grand painted hatchment was already over the great entrance, and two very solemn and tall personages in black flung open each a leaf of the door as the carriage pulled up at the familiar steps.", "큰 현관 위에는 이미 화려하게 그려진 문장판이 걸려 있었고, 검은 옷을 입은 두 명의 더없이 근엄하고 키 큰 인물이 마차가 그 낯익은 계단 앞에 멈춰 서자 각기 문짝 한 짝씩을 활짝 열어젖혔다."], ["Rawdon turned red, and Becky somewhat pale, as they passed through the old hall, arm in arm.", "로던은 얼굴이 붉어졌고 베키는 다소 창백해진 채로, 둘은 팔짱을 끼고 그 옛 현관을 지나갔다."], ["She pinched her husband's arm as they entered the oak parlour, where Sir Pitt and his wife were ready to receive them.", "떡갈나무 응접실로 들어설 때 그녀는 남편의 팔을 꼬집었다. 그곳에서는 피트 경과 그의 아내가 그들을 맞이할 채비를 하고 있었다."], ["Sir Pitt in black, Lady Jane in black, and my Lady Southdown with a large black head-piece of bugles and feathers, which waved on her Ladyship's head like an undertaker's tray.", "검은 옷을 입은 피트 경, 검은 옷을 입은 제인 부인, 그리고 사우스다운 부인은 유리구슬과 깃털로 장식한 커다란 검은 머리 장식을 얹고 있었는데, 그것이 부인의 머리 위에서 마치 장의사의 운구 쟁반처럼 흔들거렸다."]], [["Sir Pitt had judged correctly, that she would not quit the premises.", "피트 경의 판단은 옳았다. 그녀는 이 저택을 떠나지 않을 작정이었다."], ["She contented herself by preserving a solemn and stony silence, when in company of Pitt and his rebellious wife, and by frightening the children in the nursery by the ghastly gloom of her demeanour.", "그녀는 피트와 그 반항적인 아내가 함께 있는 자리에서는 엄숙하고 돌처럼 굳은 침묵을 지키는 것으로, 또 그 음산하리만치 침울한 거동으로 육아실의 아이들을 겁주는 것으로 만족하고 있었다."], ["Only a very faint bending of the head-dress and plumes welcomed Rawdon and his wife, as those prodigals returned to their family.", "저 탕아들이 가족의 품으로 돌아왔건만, 로던과 그의 아내를 맞이한 것이라곤 그저 그 머리 장식과 깃털을 아주 희미하게 한 번 까딱이는 것뿐이었다."]], [["To say the truth, they were not affected very much one way or other by this coolness.", "사실대로 말하자면, 두 사람은 이 냉랭함에 이러나저러나 크게 마음 쓰지 않았다."], ["Her Ladyship was a person only of secondary consideration in their minds just then--they were intent upon the reception which the reigning brother and sister would afford them.", "그 순간 그들의 마음속에서 부인은 그저 부차적인 고려 대상에 지나지 않았던 것이다. 그들은 지금 이 집안을 다스리는 형과 형수가 자신들을 어떻게 맞아 줄지에만 온통 마음을 쏟고 있었다."]], [["Pitt, with rather a heightened colour, went up and shook his brother by the hand, and saluted Rebecca with a hand-shake and a very low bow.", "피트는 다소 상기된 얼굴로 다가와 동생의 손을 잡고 흔들었고, 레베카에게는 악수와 함께 더없이 깊은 절로 인사했다."], ["But Lady Jane took both the hands of her sister-in-law and kissed her affectionately.", "그러나 제인 부인은 시동생의 아내의 두 손을 모두 잡고 다정하게 입을 맞추었다."], ["The embrace somehow brought tears into the eyes of the little adventuress--which ornaments, as we know, she wore very seldom.", "그 포옹은 어쩐 일인지 이 작은 모험가의 두 눈에 눈물을 자아냈는데, 우리가 알다시피 그녀가 좀처럼 두르지 않는 장신구였다."], ["The artless mark of kindness and confidence touched and pleased her; and Rawdon, encouraged by this demonstration on his sister's part, twirled up his mustachios and took leave to salute Lady Jane with a kiss, which caused her Ladyship to blush exceedingly.", "그 꾸밈없는 친절과 신뢰의 표시가 그녀의 마음을 움직이고 기쁘게 했다. 그리고 로던은 누이가 보여 준 이런 살가운 표현에 용기를 얻어 콧수염을 비비 꼬며, 제인 부인에게 실례를 무릅쓰고 입맞춤으로 인사했는데, 이 때문에 부인은 얼굴이 몹시 붉어졌다."]], [["\"Dev'lish nice little woman, Lady Jane,\"", "\"정말이지 괜찮은 작은 부인이야, 제인 부인 말이야.\""], ["was his verdict, when he and his wife were together again.", "그와 그의 아내가 다시 둘만 있게 되었을 때 그가 내린 평이었다."], ["\"Pitt's got fat, too, and is doing the thing handsomely.\"", "\"피트도 살이 올랐고, 일을 훌륭하게 처리하고 있더군.\""], ["\"He can afford it,\"", "\"그럴 형편이 되니까요.\""], ["said Rebecca and agreed in her husband's farther opinion \"that the mother-in-law was a tremendous old Guy--and that the sisters were rather well-looking young women.\"", "레베카가 말했고, 남편의 또 다른 견해, 즉 \"장모 되는 노친네는 어마어마한 늙은 허수아비고, 누이들은 제법 인물이 반반한 처녀들\"이라는 데에도 동의했다."]], [["They, too, had been summoned from school to attend the funeral ceremonies.", "그 처녀들 역시 장례식에 참석하라고 학교에서 불려 온 터였다."], ["It seemed Sir Pitt Crawley, for the dignity of the house and family, had thought right to have about the place as many persons in black as could possibly be assembled.", "보아하니 피트 크롤리 경은 가문과 집안의 위신을 위해, 가능한 한 많은 검은 옷 입은 사람들을 저택에 불러 모아 두는 것이 옳다고 여긴 모양이었다. 집안의 모든 하인과 하녀들, 선대 피트 경이 응당 받을 몫의 상당 부분을 가로채 속여 먹었던 양로원의 노파들, 교구 서기의 가족, 그리고 본가와 목사관 양쪽의 특별한 가신들이 모두 검은 상복을 입었다."], ["All the men and maids of the house, the old women of the Alms House, whom the elder Sir Pitt had cheated out of a great portion of their due, the parish clerk's family, and the special retainers of both Hall and Rectory were habited in sable; added to these, the undertaker's men, at least a score, with crapes and hatbands, and who made goodly show when the great burying show took place--but these are mute personages in our drama; and having nothing to do or say, need occupy a very little space here.", "거기에 더해, 장의사가 부리는 일꾼들이 적어도 스무 명은 상장과 모자띠를 두르고 있었는데, 이들은 그 성대한 장례 행렬이 펼쳐질 때면 그럴듯한 볼거리가 되어 주었다. 그러나 이들은 우리 극에서 말 없는 인물들이니, 할 일도 할 말도 없는 만큼 여기서는 아주 작은 자리만 차지하면 될 것이다."]], [["With regard to her sisters-in-law Rebecca did not attempt to forget her former position of Governess towards them, but recalled it frankly and kindly, and asked them about their studies with great gravity, and told them that she had thought of them many and many a day, and longed to know of their welfare.", "시누이들에 관해서라면, 레베카는 예전 그들의 가정교사였던 자기 처지를 굳이 잊으려 들지 않았다. 오히려 그것을 솔직하고 다정하게 떠올리며, 더없이 진지한 태도로 그들의 공부에 대해 묻고, 숱한 나날 동안 그들을 생각했으며 그들이 잘 지내는지 무척 알고 싶었노라고 말했다."], ["In fact you would have supposed that ever since she had left them she had not ceased to keep them uppermost in her thoughts and to take the tenderest interest in their welfare.", "사실 당신이라면 그녀가 그들 곁을 떠난 이래 줄곧 그들을 마음속 가장 윗자리에 두고 그들의 안녕에 더없이 애틋한 관심을 기울여 왔으리라 여겼을 것이다."], ["So supposed Lady Crawley herself and her young sisters.", "크롤리 부인 본인과 그 어린 시누이들도 그렇게 여겼다."]], [["\"She's hardly changed since eight years,\"", "\"팔 년이 지났는데도 거의 변하지 않았네.\""], ["said Miss Rosalind to Miss Violet, as they were preparing for dinner.", "로잘린드 양이 만찬 채비를 하면서 바이올렛 양에게 말했다."]], [["\"Those red-haired women look wonderfully well,\"", "\"저런 빨간 머리 여자들은 놀랄 만큼 정정하게 보인다니까.\""], ["replied the other.", "다른 쪽이 대답했다."]], [["\"Hers is much darker than it was; I think she must dye it,\"", "\"언니 머리는 예전보다 훨씬 짙어졌어. 염색을 한 게 분명해.\""], ["Miss Rosalind added.", "로잘린드 양이 덧붙였다."], ["\"She is stouter, too, and altogether improved,\"", "\"몸집도 더 통통해졌고, 전체적으로 나아졌어.\""], ["continued Miss Rosalind, who was disposed to be very fat.", "살집이 꽤 있는 편인 로잘린드 양이 말을 이었다."]], [["\"At least she gives herself no airs and remembers that she was our Governess once,\"", "\"적어도 거드름은 안 피우고, 한때 우리 가정교사였다는 걸 기억하고는 있더라.\" 바이올렛 양이 말했다."], ["Miss Violet said, intimating that it befitted all governesses to keep their proper place, and forgetting altogether that she was granddaughter not only of Sir Walpole Crawley, but of Mr. Dawson of Mudbury, and so had a coal-scuttle in her scutcheon.", "모든 가정교사란 제 분수에 맞는 자리를 지켜야 마땅하다는 뜻을 내비친 것인데, 정작 자기 자신이 월폴 크롤리 경의 손녀일 뿐 아니라 머드베리의 도슨 씨의 손녀이기도 하다는 것, 그래서 제 문장(紋章) 속에 석탄통 하나가 들어 있다는 것은 까맣게 잊고 있었다."], ["There are other very well-meaning people whom one meets every day in Vanity Fair who are surely equally oblivious.", "허영의 시장에서 우리가 날마다 마주치는, 분명 이만큼이나 잘 잊어버리는 선량한 사람들이 또 있는 법이다."]], [["\"It can't be true what the girls at the Rectory said, that her mother was an opera-dancer--\"", "\"목사관 처녀들이 한 말이 사실일 리 없어. 저 언니 어머니가 오페라 무희였다던데.\""]], [["\"A person can't help their birth,\"", "\"태생이야 어쩔 수 없는 일이지.\""], ["Rosalind replied with great liberality.", "로잘린드가 무척 너그럽게 대답했다."], ["\"And I agree with our brother, that as she is in the family, of course we are bound to notice her.", "\"그리고 나도 오빠 말에 동의해. 집안 식구가 된 이상, 당연히 우리도 알은체를 해야 하는 거야."], ["I am sure Aunt Bute need not talk; she wants to marry Kate to young Hooper, the wine-merchant, and absolutely asked him to come to the Rectory for orders.\"", "정말이지 뷰트 고모는 남 말 할 처지가 못 돼. 케이트를 술장수 후퍼 그 젊은이한테 시집보내려고 안달이 나서는, 글쎄 그 사람더러 목사관에 와서 주문받아 가라고까지 했다잖아.\""]], [["\"I wonder whether Lady Southdown will go away, she looked very glum upon Mrs.", "\"사우스다운 부인은 떠나실까 모르겠네. 로던 부인을 무척 못마땅한 눈으로 보시던데.\""], ["Rawdon,\" the other said.", "다른 쪽이 말했다."]], [["\"I wish she would.", "\"제발 좀 가셨으면."], ["I won't read the Washerwoman of Finchley Common,\"", "나는 '핀칠리 광야의 세탁부' 따위 안 읽을 거야.\""], ["vowed Violet; and so saying, and avoiding a passage at the end of which a certain coffin was placed with a couple of watchers, and lights perpetually burning in the closed room, these young women came down to the family dinner, for which the bell rang as usual.", "바이올렛이 단언했다. 그러고는 어떤 관(棺) 하나가 두 사람의 밤샘꾼과 함께 놓여 있고 닫힌 방 안에서 등불이 끊임없이 타고 있는 복도 끝을 피해, 두 젊은 처녀는 평소처럼 종이 울린 가족 만찬 자리로 내려갔다."]], [["But before this, Lady Jane conducted Rebecca to the apartments prepared for her, which, with the rest of the house, had assumed a very much improved appearance of order and comfort during Pitt's regency, and here beholding that Mrs.", "그러나 그에 앞서, 제인 부인은 레베카를 그녀를 위해 마련된 방으로 안내했다. 그 방은 집안의 다른 곳과 마찬가지로 피트가 다스리는 동안 한결 정돈되고 안락한 모습으로 나아져 있었다."], ["Rawdon's modest little trunks had arrived, and were placed in the bedroom and dressing-room adjoining, helped her to take off her neat black bonnet and cloak, and asked her sister-in-law in what more she could be useful.", "그곳에서 로던 부인의 자그마하고 검소한 트렁크들이 이미 도착해 침실과 그에 딸린 화장실에 놓여 있는 것을 보고는, 부인은 그녀가 단정한 검은 보닛과 망토를 벗는 것을 거들어 주며, 시동생의 아내에게 더 도와줄 일이 없는지 물었다."]], [["\"What I should like best,\"", "\"제가 가장 하고 싶은 일은요,\""], ["said Rebecca, \"would be to go to the nursery and see your dear little children.\"", "레베카가 말했다. \"육아실에 가서 그 사랑스러운 아이들을 보는 거예요.\""], ["On which the two ladies looked very kindly at each other and went to that apartment hand in hand.", "그 말에 두 부인은 더없이 다정한 눈으로 서로를 바라보고는, 손을 맞잡고 그 방으로 갔다."]], [["Becky admired little Matilda, who was not quite four years old, as the most charming little love in the world; and the boy, a little fellow of two years--pale, heavy-eyed, and large-headed--she pronounced to be a perfect prodigy in point of size, intelligence, and beauty.", "베키는 아직 네 살도 채 안 된 어린 마틸다를 세상에서 가장 사랑스러운 꼬마라며 칭찬했다. 그리고 두 살배기 사내아이는, 창백하고 눈이 처지고 머리가 큰 아이였는데, 크기로 보나 영민함으로 보나 아름다움으로 보나 완벽한 신동이라고 단언했다."]], [["\"I wish Mamma would not insist on giving him so much medicine,\"", "\"어머니가 저 애한테 약을 그렇게 많이 먹이라고 고집만 안 부리셨으면 좋겠어요.\""], ["Lady Jane said with a sigh.", "제인 부인이 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."], ["\"I often think we should all be better without it.\"", "\"약 없이도 우리 모두 더 잘 지낼 텐데, 하는 생각이 자주 들어요.\""], ["And then Lady Jane and her new-found friend had one of those confidential medical conversations about the children, which all mothers, and most women, as I am given to understand, delight in.", "그러고는 제인 부인과 새로 사귄 벗은 아이들에 관한 그런 은밀한 의학 담소를 나누었으니, 내가 듣기로는 모든 어머니가, 또 대다수 여인들이 즐겨 하는 그런 이야기였다."], ["Fifty years ago, and when the present writer, being an interesting little boy, was ordered out of the room with the ladies after dinner, I remember quite well that their talk was chiefly about their ailments; and putting this question directly to two or three since, I have always got from them the acknowledgement that times are not changed.", "오십 년 전, 지금 이 글을 쓰는 사람이 사랑스러운 어린 소년이던 시절, 만찬이 끝나고 숙녀들과 함께 방에서 물러가라는 명을 받곤 했는데, 그때 그분들의 이야기가 주로 자기들 병치레에 관한 것이었음을 나는 똑똑히 기억한다. 그 뒤로 두어 사람에게 이 질문을 대놓고 던져 보았더니, 한결같이 세월이 달라진 게 없다는 시인을 얻어 냈다."], ["Let my fair readers remark for themselves this very evening when they quit the dessert-table and assemble to celebrate the drawing-room mysteries.", "나의 어여쁜 여성 독자들도 바로 오늘 저녁, 디저트 식탁을 떠나 응접실의 비밀 의식을 거행하러 모일 때 스스로 한번 눈여겨보시기를."], ["Well--in half an hour Becky and Lady Jane were close and intimate friends--and in the course of the evening her Ladyship informed Sir Pitt that she thought her new sister-in-law was a kind, frank, unaffected, and affectionate young woman.", "아무튼, 반 시간 만에 베키와 제인 부인은 가깝고 친밀한 벗이 되었다. 그리고 그날 저녁이 다 가기도 전에, 부인은 피트 경에게 새로 맞은 시동생의 아내가 친절하고 솔직하고 꾸밈없고 다정한 젊은 여자인 것 같다고 말했다."]], [["And so having easily won the daughter's good-will, the indefatigable little woman bent herself to conciliate the august Lady Southdown.", "이렇게 손쉽게 며느리의 호의를 얻어 낸 이 지칠 줄 모르는 작은 여인은, 위풍당당한 사우스다운 부인을 구슬리는 일에 착수했다."], ["As soon as she found her Ladyship alone, Rebecca attacked her on the nursery question at once and said that her own little boy was saved, actually saved, by calomel, freely administered, when all the physicians in Paris had given the dear child up.", "부인이 혼자 있는 것을 발견하자마자, 레베카는 대뜸 육아 문제로 그녀를 공략하여, 파리의 모든 의사가 그 가엾은 아이를 포기했을 때 감홍(甘汞)을 넉넉히 먹여 자기 어린 아들을 살려 냈노라고, 실제로 살려 냈노라고 말했다."], ["And then she mentioned how often she had heard of Lady Southdown from that excellent man the Reverend Lawrence Grills, Minister of the chapel in May Fair, which she frequented; and how her views were very much changed by circumstances and misfortunes; and how she hoped that a past life spent in worldliness and error might not incapacitate her from more serious thought for the future.", "그러고는 자기가 다니던 메이페어 예배당의 목사이신 저 훌륭한 분, 로런스 그릴스 목사님께 사우스다운 부인에 관한 이야기를 얼마나 자주 들었는지 모른다고, 또 자기 생각이 갖가지 사정과 불행으로 무척 달라졌노라고, 그리고 지난날을 세속과 과오 속에 보냈다 하여 앞으로 더 진지한 사색을 못 하게 되는 일은 없기를 바란다고 말했다."], ["She described how in former days she had been indebted to Mr.", "그녀는 예전에 크롤리 씨에게 신앙의 가르침을 입었던 일을 들려주고, 더없이 큰 유익을 얻으며 읽었다는 '핀칠리 광야의 세탁부'"], ["Crawley for religious instruction, touched upon the Washerwoman of Finchley Common, which she had read with the greatest profit, and asked about Lady Emily, its gifted author, now Lady Emily Hornblower, at Cape Town, where her husband had strong hopes of becoming Bishop of Caffraria.", "이야기를 슬쩍 꺼냈으며, 그 재능 있는 저자인 에밀리 부인, 이제는 케이프타운에서 에밀리 혼블로어 부인이 된 그분의 안부를 물었다. 그 남편은 그곳에서 카프라리아의 주교가 되리라는 큰 희망을 품고 있다고 했다."]], [["But she crowned all, and confirmed herself in Lady Southdown's favour, by feeling very much agitated and unwell after the funeral and requesting her Ladyship's medical advice, which the Dowager not only gave, but, wrapped up in a bed-gown and looking more like Lady Macbeth than ever, came privately in the night to Becky's room with a parcel of favourite tracts, and a medicine of her own composition, which she insisted that Mrs.", "그러나 그녀가 모든 것에 화룡점정을 찍어 사우스다운 부인의 환심을 굳힌 것은, 장례를 치른 뒤 무척 마음이 동요되고 몸이 좋지 않다며 부인의 의학적 조언을 청한 일이었다. 노부인은 조언을 해 주었을 뿐 아니라, 잠옷을 두르고 그 어느 때보다 맥베스 부인을 닮은 모습으로, 밤중에 몰래 베키의 방으로 찾아왔다."], ["Rawdon should take.", "손에는 자기가 아끼는 소책자 꾸러미와 손수 조제한 약 한 가지를 들고서, 로던 부인이 그 약을 꼭 먹어야 한다고 우겼다."]], [["Becky first accepted the tracts and began to examine them with great interest, engaging the Dowager in a conversation concerning them and the welfare of her soul, by which means she hoped that her body might escape medication.", "베키는 우선 소책자들을 받아 들고 무척 흥미로운 양 살펴보기 시작하면서, 그 책들과 자기 영혼의 안녕에 관한 이야기로 노부인을 붙들어 두었으니, 이로써 자기 몸뚱이만은 약을 면할 수 있기를 바란 것이었다."], ["But after the religious topics were exhausted, Lady Macbeth would not quit Becky's chamber until her cup of night-drink was emptied too; and poor Mrs.", "그러나 종교적 화제가 바닥나고 나서도, 맥베스 부인은 그 밤 약 잔이 다 비워지기 전에는 베키의 방을 떠나려 하지 않았다."], ["Rawdon was compelled actually to assume a look of gratitude, and to swallow the medicine under the unyielding old Dowager's nose, who left her victim finally with a benediction.", "그리하여 가엾은 로던 부인은 정말로 감사한 표정을 지어 보이고는, 한 치도 물러서지 않는 그 노부인의 코앞에서 약을 삼킬 수밖에 없었다. 노부인은 마침내 축복의 말을 남기고 제 희생양을 떠났다."]], [["It did not much comfort Mrs.", "그 일은 로던 부인에게 별 위안이 되지 못했다."], ["Rawdon; her countenance was very queer when Rawdon came in and heard what had happened; and his explosions of laughter were as loud as usual, when Becky, with a fun which she could not disguise, even though it was at her own expense, described the occurrence and how she had been victimized by Lady Southdown.", "로던이 들어와 무슨 일이 있었는지 듣자 그녀의 표정은 무척이나 묘했다. 그리고 베키가, 비록 제 손해를 보는 이야기였음에도 차마 감출 수 없는 그 익살로 그 일을, 즉 자기가 사우스다운 부인에게 어떻게 희생당했는지를 묘사하자, 로던은 여느 때처럼 큰 소리로 폭소를 터뜨렸다."], ["Lord Steyne, and her son in London, had many a laugh over the story when Rawdon and his wife returned to their quarters in May Fair.", "로던과 그의 아내가 메이페어의 거처로 돌아온 뒤, 스타인 경과 런던에 있는 그녀의 아들도 이 이야기로 숱하게 웃었다."], ["Becky acted the whole scene for them.", "베키는 그들 앞에서 그 장면 전체를 연기해 보였다."], ["She put on a night-cap and gown.", "그녀는 나이트캡을 쓰고 잠옷을 걸쳤다."], ["She preached a great sermon in the true serious manner; she lectured on the virtue of the medicine which she pretended to administer, with a gravity of imitation so perfect that you would have thought it was the Countess's own Roman nose through which she snuffled.", "그러고는 진짜 엄숙한 투로 일장 설교를 늘어놓았다. 약의 효능에 관해 강론하며 그것을 먹이는 시늉을 하는데, 그 흉내가 어찌나 완벽하게 진지하던지, 그녀가 코로 흥흥거리는 것이 정말로 백작 부인 본인의 로마식 매부리코인 줄로만 알았을 것이다."], ["\"Give us Lady Southdown and the black dose,\" was a constant cry amongst the folks in Becky's little drawing-room in May Fair.", "\"사우스다운 부인이랑 그 검은 약 한 사발 좀 보여 줘요\"는 메이페어에 있는 베키의 자그마한 응접실에 모인 사람들 사이에 끊이지 않는 외침이었다."], ["And for the first time in her life the Dowager Countess of Southdown was made amusing.", "그리하여 사우스다운 백작 미망인은 난생처음으로 사람들에게 웃음거리가 되었던 것이다."]], [["Sir Pitt remembered the testimonies of respect and veneration which Rebecca had paid personally to himself in early days, and was tolerably well disposed towards her.", "피트 경은 레베카가 지난날 자기에게 몸소 바쳤던 존경과 흠모의 표시들을 기억하고 있던 터라, 그녀에게 제법 호의적이었다."], ["The marriage, ill-advised as it was, had improved Rawdon very much--that was clear from the Colonel's altered habits and demeanour--and had it not been a lucky union as regarded Pitt himself?", "그 결혼은 분별없는 짓이었음에도 로던을 무척이나 나아지게 만들었는데, 이는 대령의 달라진 습관과 거동으로 보아 분명한 일이었다. 게다가 피트 자신에게도 그것은 운 좋은 결합이 아니었던가?"], ["The cunning diplomatist smiled inwardly as he owned that he owed his fortune to it, and acknowledged that he at least ought not to cry out against it.", "이 교활한 책략가는 그 결혼 덕에 제 재산을 얻게 되었음을 속으로 인정하며 빙긋 웃었고, 적어도 자기만은 그 일을 두고 비난할 처지가 못 된다는 것을 시인했다."], ["His satisfaction was not removed by Rebecca's own statements, behaviour, and conversation.", "그의 흡족함은 레베카 본인의 말과 행동과 대화로 인해 조금도 줄어들지 않았다."]], [["She doubled the deference which before had charmed him, calling out his conversational powers in such a manner as quite to surprise Pitt himself, who, always inclined to respect his own talents, admired them the more when Rebecca pointed them out to him.", "그녀는 전에 그를 매료시켰던 그 깍듯함을 곱절로 늘려, 그의 대화 능력을 끌어내되 피트 자신마저 깜짝 놀랄 정도로 그렇게 해 주었다."], ["With her sister-in-law, Rebecca was satisfactorily able to prove that it was Mrs.", "늘 제 재능을 높이 사는 경향이 있던 그는, 레베카가 그것을 짚어 보여 주자 더욱더 그 재능을 흠모하게 된 것이다."], ["Bute Crawley who brought about the marriage which she afterwards so calumniated; that it was Mrs. Bute's avarice--who hoped to gain all Miss Crawley's fortune and deprive Rawdon of his aunt's favour--which caused and invented all the wicked reports against Rebecca.", "시누이를 상대로 레베카는, 자기가 나중에 그토록 헐뜯음을 당한 그 결혼을 실은 뷰트 크롤리 부인이 성사시킨 것임을, 또 크롤리 양의 재산을 몽땅 차지하고 로던에게서 고모의 총애를 빼앗으려던 뷰트 부인의 탐욕이야말로 레베카에 관한 그 온갖 못된 소문들을 지어내고 퍼뜨린 장본인임을 흡족하게 입증해 보일 수 있었다."], ["\"She succeeded in making us poor,\"", "\"그분은 우리를 가난뱅이로 만드는 데는 성공했지요.\""], ["Rebecca said with an air of angelical patience; \"but how can I be angry with a woman who has given me one of the best husbands in the world?", "레베카가 천사 같은 인내의 빛을 띠고 말했다. \"하지만 세상에서 가장 좋은 남편 중 하나를 제게 주신 분에게 어떻게 제가 화를 낼 수 있겠어요?"], ["And has not her own avarice been sufficiently punished by the ruin of her own hopes and the loss of the property by which she set so much store?", "게다가 그분의 탐욕은, 제 희망이 무너지고 그토록 애지중지하던 재산을 잃은 것으로 충분히 벌을 받지 않았던가요? 가난이라뇨!\""], ["Poor!\" she cried.", "그녀가 외쳤다."], ["\"Dear Lady Jane, what care we for poverty?", "\"제인 부인, 가난 따위가 우리에게 무슨 대수겠어요?"], ["I am used to it from childhood, and I am often thankful that Miss Crawley's money has gone to restore the splendour of the noble old family of which I am so proud to be a member.", "저야 어릴 적부터 가난에 익숙한걸요. 그리고 저는 제가 그 일원임을 그토록 자랑스러워하는 이 유서 깊은 명문가의 영광을 되살리는 데 크롤리 양의 돈이 쓰이게 된 것이 도리어 고마운 때가 많답니다."], ["I am sure Sir Pitt will make a much better use of it than Rawdon would.\"", "피트 경께서 로던보다 그 돈을 훨씬 더 잘 쓰시리라 믿어요.\""]], [["All these speeches were reported to Sir Pitt by the most faithful of wives, and increased the favourable impression which Rebecca made; so much so that when, on the third day after the funeral, the family party were at dinner, Sir Pitt Crawley, carving fowls at the head of the table, actually said to Mrs.", "이 모든 말은 더없이 충실한 아내의 입을 통해 피트 경에게 고스란히 전해졌고, 레베카가 남긴 호의적인 인상을 한층 더 키워 주었다. 그리하여 장례를 치른 지 사흘째 되던 날, 식구들이 모여 만찬을 들고 있을 때, 피트 크롤리 경은 식탁 상석에서 새고기를 썰다가 로던 부인에게 실제로 이렇게 말했던 것이다."], ["Rawdon, \"Ahem!", "\"에헴!"], ["Rebecca, may I give you a wing?\"--a speech which made the little woman's eyes sparkle with pleasure.", "레베카, 날개 살 한 점 들겠나?\" 이 한마디에 이 작은 여인의 두 눈은 기쁨으로 반짝였다."]], [["While Rebecca was prosecuting the above schemes and hopes, and Pitt Crawley arranging the funeral ceremonial and other matters connected with his future progress and dignity, and Lady Jane busy with her nursery, as far as her mother would let her, and the sun rising and setting, and the clock-tower bell of the Hall ringing to dinner and to prayers as usual, the body of the late owner of Queen's Crawley lay in the apartment which he had occupied, watched unceasingly by the professional attendants who were engaged for that rite.", "레베카가 앞서 말한 그 계략과 희망들을 추진하는 동안, 피트 크롤리는 장례 의식과 더불어 제 장래의 출세와 위신에 관한 여러 일들을 챙겼고, 제인 부인은 어머니가 허락하는 한도 내에서 육아실 일로 분주했으며, 해는 뜨고 또 졌고, 저택의 시계탑 종은 평소처럼 만찬과 기도 시간을 알리며 울렸다. 그러는 사이 퀸스크롤리의 고인이 된 옛 주인의 시신은, 그가 쓰던 방에 누워, 그 의식을 위해 고용된 전문 일꾼들의 끊임없는 지킴을 받고 있었다."], ["A woman or two, and three or four undertaker's men, the best whom Southampton could furnish, dressed in black, and of a proper stealthy and tragical demeanour, had charge of the remains which they watched turn about, having the housekeeper's room for their place of rendezvous when off duty, where they played at cards in privacy and drank their beer.", "여인 한둘과 장의사가 부리는 사내 서넛, 사우샘프턴이 댈 수 있는 가장 솜씨 좋은 자들이 검은 옷을 입고 적절히 은밀하고 비극적인 거동으로, 번갈아 가며 유해를 지켰다. 그들은 비번일 때면 가정부 방을 만남의 장소로 삼아 그곳에서 은밀히 카드놀이를 하고 맥주를 마셨다."]], [["The members of the family and servants of the house kept away from the gloomy spot, where the bones of the descendant of an ancient line of knights and gentlemen lay, awaiting their final consignment to the family crypt.", "집안 식구들과 하인들은 그 음산한 자리를 멀리했다. 그곳에는 고대 기사와 신사들의 핏줄을 이어받은 한 사내의 뼈가, 가족 납골당으로 마지막 안치될 날을 기다리며 누워 있었다."], ["No regrets attended them, save those of the poor woman who had hoped to be Sir Pitt's wife and widow and who had fled in disgrace from the Hall over which she had so nearly been a ruler.", "그를 애도하는 이는 아무도 없었으니, 다만 피트 경의 아내가, 또 그 미망인이 되기를 바라다가 거의 그 안주인이 될 뻔했던 저택에서 망신을 당하고 달아난 그 가엾은 여인의 슬픔만이 있을 뿐이었다."], ["Beyond her and a favourite old pointer he had, and between whom and himself an attachment subsisted during the period of his imbecility, the old man had not a single friend to mourn him, having indeed, during the whole course of his life, never taken the least pains to secure one.", "그녀와, 그가 아끼던 늙은 포인터 한 마리—치매를 앓던 시절 그와 이 개 사이에는 어떤 정이 오갔다—를 빼면, 이 노인에게는 그를 애도해 줄 벗이 단 하나도 없었다. 실로 그는 평생토록 단 한 명의 벗을 얻으려는 수고조차 손톱만큼도 들인 적이 없었던 것이다."], ["Could the best and kindest of us who depart from the earth have an opportunity of revisiting it, I suppose he or she (assuming that any Vanity Fair feelings subsist in the sphere whither we are bound) would have a pang of mortification at finding how soon our survivors were consoled.", "이 땅을 떠나는 우리 가운데 가장 선하고 다정한 이라 할지라도, 만일 다시 이곳을 찾아올 기회가 주어진다면, 짐작하건대 그 또는 그녀는(우리가 향하는 그 세계에도 허영의 시장의 감정이라는 것이 조금이나마 남아 있다고 친다면) 우리 뒤에 남은 이들이 어쩌면 그리도 빨리 위로를 받는지를 알고는 굴욕의 아픔을 느낄 것이다."], ["And so Sir Pitt was forgotten--like the kindest and best of us--only a few weeks sooner.", "그리하여 피트 경은, 우리 가운데 가장 선하고 다정한 이들과 마찬가지로, 잊혀졌다. 다만 몇 주쯤 더 빨랐을 뿐이다."]], [["Those who will may follow his remains to the grave, whither they were borne on the appointed day, in the most becoming manner, the family in black coaches, with their handkerchiefs up to their noses, ready for the tears which did not come; the undertaker and his gentlemen in deep tribulation; the select tenantry mourning out of compliment to the new landlord; the neighbouring gentry's carriages at three miles an hour, empty, and in profound affliction; the parson speaking out the formula about \"our dear brother departed.\"", "원하는 이라면 그의 유해를 따라 무덤까지 가 보아도 좋다. 정해진 날, 더없이 격식에 맞는 모양새로 그것은 운구되었다."], ["As long as we have a man's body, we play our Vanities upon it, surrounding it with humbug and ceremonies, laying it in state, and packing it up in gilt nails and velvet; and we finish our duty by placing over it a stone, written all over with lies.", "가족은 검은 마차에 타고, 손수건을 코에 갖다 댄 채 끝내 나오지 않는 눈물에 대비하고 있었다. 장의사와 그 일꾼들은 깊은 비탄에 잠겼고, 선별된 소작인들은 새 지주에 대한 예우 차원에서 애도했으며, 이웃 신사들의 마차는 시속 삼 마일로, 텅 빈 채 깊은 슬픔에 잠겨 따라왔고, 목사는 \"세상을 떠난 우리 사랑하는 형제\"에 관한 정해진 문구를 읊었다."], ["Bute's curate, a smart young fellow from Oxford, and Sir Pitt Crawley composed between them an appropriate Latin epitaph for the late lamented Baronet, and the former preached a classical sermon, exhorting the survivors not to give way to grief and informing them in the most respectful terms that they also would be one day called upon to pass that gloomy and mysterious portal which had just closed upon the remains of their lamented brother.", "우리는 사람의 몸뚱이가 남아 있는 한 그 위에서 우리의 허영을 연출하며, 그것을 허튼수작과 의식으로 둘러싸고, 정중히 안치하고, 금박 못과 벨벳으로 싸 둔다. 그리고 그 위에 거짓말로 빼곡히 새겨진 비석 하나를 얹는 것으로 우리의 의무를 끝낸다."], ["Then the tenantry mounted on horseback again, or stayed and refreshed themselves at the Crawley Arms.", "뷰트의 부목사, 옥스퍼드 출신의 영민한 젊은이가 피트 크롤리 경과 함께 고인이 된 준남작을 위해 적절한 라틴어 묘비명을 지었고, 그 부목사는 고전적인 설교 한 편을 읊어 살아남은 자들에게 슬픔에 잠기지 말 것을 권하면서, 더없이 정중한 말투로 그들 역시 언젠가는 방금 그들의 애도받는 형제의 유해 위로 닫힌 저 음울하고 신비로운 문을 지나게 되리라고 알려 주었다."], ["Then, after a lunch in the servants'", "그러고 나서 소작인들은 다시 말에 올랐거나, 아니면 남아서 크롤리 암스 주막에서 요기를 했다."], ["hall at Queen's Crawley, the gentry's carriages wheeled off to their different destinations: then the undertaker's men, taking the ropes, palls, velvets, ostrich feathers, and other mortuary properties, clambered up on the roof of the hearse and rode off to Southampton.", "그러고 나서 퀸스크롤리의 하인 식당에서 점심을 든 뒤, 신사들의 마차는 제각기 다른 목적지를 향해 바퀴를 굴려 떠났다. 그러고 나서 장의사 일꾼들은 밧줄과 관보와 벨벳과 타조 깃털과 그 밖의 장례 도구들을 챙겨 영구차 지붕 위로 기어올라 사우샘프턴으로 떠났다."], ["Their faces relapsed into a natural expression as the horses, clearing the lodge-gates, got into a brisker trot on the open road; and squads of them might have been seen, speckling with black the public-house entrances, with pewter-pots flashing in the sunshine.", "말들이 정문을 빠져나가 트인 길로 들어서며 더 활기찬 속보로 달리기 시작하자, 그들의 얼굴은 자연스러운 표정으로 되돌아왔다. 그리고 그들 한 무리가 술집 입구를 검게 점점이 물들이고 있는 광경을, 백랍 술잔이 햇살에 번뜩이는 모습을 볼 수 있었을 것이다."], ["Sir Pitt's invalid chair was wheeled away into a tool-house in the garden; the old pointer used to howl sometimes at first, but these were the only accents of grief which were heard in the Hall of which Sir Pitt Crawley, Baronet, had been master for some threescore years.", "피트 경의 환자용 의자는 정원의 연장 창고로 굴려 보내졌다. 늙은 포인터가 처음 얼마간은 이따금 울부짖곤 했으나, 피트 크롤리 준남작이 육십 년 가까이 주인 노릇을 했던 그 저택에서 들려온 슬픔의 가락이라곤 오직 그것뿐이었다."]], [["As the birds were pretty plentiful, and partridge shooting is as it were the duty of an English gentleman of statesmanlike propensities, Sir Pitt Crawley, the first shock of grief over, went out a little and partook of that diversion in a white hat with crape round it.", "새가 제법 많은 데다 자고새 사냥이란 정치가다운 기질을 지닌 영국 신사의 의무나 다름없는 일이었으므로, 피트 크롤리 경은 슬픔의 첫 충격이 가시고 나자 상장을 두른 흰 모자를 쓰고 잠깐씩 나가 그 소일거리를 즐겼다."], ["The sight of those fields of stubble and turnips, now his own, gave him many secret joys.", "이제는 제 것이 된 저 그루터기 밭과 순무밭을 바라보노라면 그는 남모를 기쁨이 솟곤 했다."], ["Sometimes, and with an exquisite humility, he took no gun, but went out with a peaceful bamboo cane; Rawdon, his big brother, and the keepers blazing away at his side.", "더러는, 더없이 절묘한 겸양을 부리며 총을 들지 않고 평화로운 대나무 지팡이만 짚고 나서기도 했는데, 형인 로던과 사냥지기들이 그 곁에서 총을 쏘아 댔다."], ["Pitt's money and acres had a great effect upon his brother.", "피트의 돈과 토지는 동생에게 큰 영향을 미쳤다."], ["The penniless Colonel became quite obsequious and respectful to the head of his house, and despised the milksop Pitt no longer.", "무일푼인 대령은 제 집안의 가장에게 완전히 굽실거리며 깍듯해졌고, 더는 그 물러터진 피트를 업신여기지 않았다."], ["Rawdon listened with sympathy to his senior's prospects of planting and draining, gave his advice about the stables and cattle, rode over to Mudbury to look at a mare, which he thought would carry Lady Jane, and offered to break her, &c.: the rebellious dragoon was quite humbled and subdued, and became a most creditable younger brother.", "로던은 형이 펼쳐 보이는 식목과 배수 계획을 공감하며 귀 기울여 들었고, 마구간과 가축에 관해 조언했으며, 제인 부인이 타기에 좋겠다 싶은 암말 한 마리를 보러 머드베리까지 말을 달려갔다가 그 말을 길들여 주겠다고 나서기도 했다. 그 반항적이던 용기병은 완전히 고분고분해지고 누그러져, 더없이 떳떳한 동생이 되었다."], ["He had constant bulletins from Miss Briggs in London respecting little Rawdon, who was left behind there, who sent messages of his own.", "그는 런던에 남겨 둔 어린 로던에 관해 브리그스 양에게서 끊임없이 소식을 받았는데, 아이도 제 나름의 전갈을 보내왔다. \"저는 아주 잘 있어요.\""], ["\"I am very well,\"", "아이는 이렇게 썼다."], ["he wrote. \"I hope you are very well.", "\"아빠도 아주 잘 계시기를 바라요."], ["I hope Mamma is very well.", "엄마도 아주 잘 계시기를 바라요."], ["The pony is very well.", "조랑말도 아주 잘 있어요."], ["Grey takes me to ride in the park. I can canter.", "그레이가 저를 공원에 데리고 가서 태워 줘요."], ["I met the little boy who rode before.", "저는 가볍게 달릴 줄 알아요."], ["He cried when he cantered. I do not cry.\"", "전에 앞서서 말 타던 그 꼬마를 만났어요."], ["Rawdon read these letters to his brother and Lady Jane, who was delighted with them.", "그 애는 가볍게 달릴 때 울었어요. 저는 안 울어요.\""], ["The Baronet promised to take charge of the lad at school, and his kind-hearted wife gave Rebecca a bank-note, begging her to buy a present with it for her little nephew.", "로던은 이 편지들을 형과 제인 부인에게 읽어 주었고, 부인은 무척 기뻐했다. 준남작은 그 아이를 학교에 보내 돌봐 주겠다고 약속했고, 마음씨 고운 그의 아내는 레베카에게 은행권 한 장을 건네며 그 돈으로 어린 조카에게 줄 선물을 사 달라고 부탁했다."]], [["One day followed another, and the ladies of the house passed their life in those calm pursuits and amusements which satisfy country ladies.", "하루가 가고 또 하루가 갔고, 집안의 부인들은 시골 부인들을 만족시키는 그 잔잔한 일과와 놀이 속에서 나날을 보냈다."], ["Bells rang to meals and to prayers.", "종은 식사와 기도 시간을 알리며 울렸다."], ["The young ladies took exercise on the pianoforte every morning after breakfast, Rebecca giving them the benefit of her instruction.", "젊은 처녀들은 아침마다 식후에 피아노로 연습을 했는데, 레베카가 그들에게 가르침을 베풀어 주었다."], ["Then they put on thick shoes and walked in the park or shrubberies, or beyond the palings into the village, descending upon the cottages, with Lady Southdown's medicine and tracts for the sick people there.", "그러고 나서 그들은 두꺼운 신을 신고 공원이나 관목 숲을 거닐거나, 울타리 너머 마을로 나가, 사우스다운 부인의 약과 소책자를 들고 그곳 병자들을 찾아 오두막마다 들렀다."], ["Lady Southdown drove out in a pony-chaise, when Rebecca would take her place by the Dowager's side and listen to her solemn talk with the utmost interest.", "사우스다운 부인이 조랑말 마차를 타고 나설 때면, 레베카는 노부인 곁에 자리 잡고 앉아 그 엄숙한 이야기를 더없이 흥미진진하게 귀담아들었다."], ["She sang Handel and Haydn to the family of evenings, and engaged in a large piece of worsted work, as if she had been born to the business and as if this kind of life was to continue with her until she should sink to the grave in a polite old age, leaving regrets and a great quantity of consols behind her--as if there were not cares and duns, schemes, shifts, and poverty waiting outside the park gates, to pounce upon her when she issued into the world again.", "그녀는 저녁이면 식구들에게 헨델과 하이든을 불러 주었고, 마치 그 일에 타고나기라도 한 듯, 또 이런 식의 삶이 그녀와 함께 죽 이어져 그녀가 점잖은 노년에 이르러 무덤으로 잠겨 들며 못다 한 아쉬움과 어마어마한 액수의 공채(公債)를 뒤에 남기게 되기라도 할 듯, 커다란 털실 자수 작업에 매달렸다. 마치 그녀가 다시 세상으로 나서면 공원 문밖에서 근심과 빚쟁이들, 갖은 계략과 임시변통과 가난이 도사리고 있다가 덮쳐 오기라도 할 것이 아니라는 듯이 말이다."]], [["\"It isn't difficult to be a country gentleman's wife,\"", "\"시골 신사의 아내가 되는 건 어렵지 않아.\""], ["Rebecca thought.", "레베카는 생각했다."], ["\"I think I could be a good woman if I had five thousand a year.", "\"연 오천 파운드만 있으면 나도 착한 여자가 될 수 있을 것 같아."], ["I could dawdle about in the nursery and count the apricots on the wall.", "육아실에서 빈둥거리며 담장에 열린 살구를 세고 있을 수도 있겠지."], ["I could water plants in a green-house and pick off dead leaves from the geraniums.", "온실에서 화초에 물을 주고 제라늄에서 시든 잎을 따 줄 수도 있고."], ["I could ask old women about their rheumatisms and order half-a-crown's worth of soup for the poor.", "노파들에게 류머티즘이 어떠냐고 물어보고, 가난한 이들에게 반 크라운어치 수프를 주문해 줄 수도 있을 거야."], ["I shouldn't miss it much, out of five thousand a year.", "연 오천 파운드에서 그쯤이야 아쉬울 것도 없지."], ["I could even drive out ten miles to dine at a neighbour's, and dress in the fashions of the year before last.", "십 마일쯤 마차를 몰고 가서 이웃집에서 만찬을 들 수도 있고, 재작년 유행을 따라 차려입을 수도 있어."], ["I could go to church and keep awake in the great family pew, or go to sleep behind the curtains, with my veil down, if I only had practice.", "교회에 가서 큰 가족석에서 깨어 있거나, 아니면 익숙해지기만 한다면 베일을 내리고 커튼 뒤에서 잠들어 있을 수도 있지."], ["I could pay everybody, if I had but the money.", "돈만 있으면 누구에게든 다 갚을 수 있어."], ["This is what the conjurors here pride themselves upon doing.", "여기 이 요술쟁이들이 우쭐대는 게 바로 그것이지."], ["They look down with pity upon us miserable sinners who have none.", "그들은 돈 한 푼 없는 우리 가엾은 죄인들을 가엾다는 듯 내려다보지."], ["They think themselves generous if they give our children a five-pound note, and us contemptible if we are without one.\"", "우리 아이들에게 오 파운드짜리 지폐 한 장 쥐여 주면 자기들이 후하다고 여기고, 우리에게 그게 없으면 우리를 한심하다 여기지.\""], ["And who knows but Rebecca was right in her speculations--and that it was only a question of money and fortune which made the difference between her and an honest woman?", "어쩌면 레베카의 이런 짐작이 옳았는지도, 그녀와 정숙한 여인 사이의 차이를 가른 것이 그저 돈과 재산의 문제였는지도 모를 일이다."], ["If you take temptations into account, who is to say that he is better than his neighbour?", "유혹이라는 것을 셈에 넣는다면, 누가 자기가 이웃보다 낫다고 말할 수 있겠는가?"], ["A comfortable career of prosperity, if it does not make people honest, at least keeps them so.", "안락한 번영의 삶이란, 설령 사람을 정직하게 만들지는 못할지언정, 적어도 정직한 채로 머물게는 해 준다."], ["An alderman coming from a turtle feast will not step out of his carriage to steal a leg of mutton; but put him to starve, and see if he will not purloin a loaf.", "자라 잔치에서 돌아오는 시참사회원이 양 다리 고기 한 덩이를 훔치려고 제 마차에서 내리는 일은 없을 것이다. 그러나 그를 굶겨 보라, 그가 빵 한 덩이를 슬쩍하지 않나 보라."], ["Becky consoled herself by so balancing the chances and equalizing the distribution of good and evil in the world.", "베키는 이렇게 세상의 가능성을 저울질하고 선과 악의 분배를 고르게 따져 봄으로써 스스로를 위로했다."]], [["The old haunts, the old fields and woods, the copses, ponds, and gardens, the rooms of the old house where she had spent a couple of years seven years ago, were all carefully revisited by her.", "옛날에 드나들던 곳들, 옛 들판과 숲, 잡목림과 연못과 정원들, 칠 년 전 그녀가 두어 해를 보냈던 그 낡은 저택의 방들을, 그녀는 모두 정성껏 다시 둘러보았다. 그곳에서 그녀는 젊었었다, 적어도 비교적 젊었었다."], ["She had been young there, or comparatively so, for she forgot the time when she ever WAS young--but she remembered her thoughts and feelings seven years back and contrasted them with those which she had at present, now that she had seen the world, and lived with great people, and raised herself far beyond her original humble station.", "사실 그녀는 자기가 한때 젊었던 시절이라는 것 자체를 잊고 살았으니 말이다. 그러나 그녀는 칠 년 전의 제 생각과 감정들을 기억해 냈고, 이제 세상을 두루 보고 지체 높은 이들과 더불어 살며 원래의 미천한 처지에서 까마득히 높이 올라선 지금의 생각과 감정에 견주어 보았다."]], [["\"I have passed beyond it, because I have brains,\"", "\"내가 그 처지를 벗어난 건 머리가 있기 때문이야.\""], ["Becky thought, \"and almost all the rest of the world are fools.", "베키는 생각했다. \"그리고 세상 나머지 거의 모두는 바보들이지."], ["I could not go back and consort with those people now, whom I used to meet in my father's studio.", "이제 와서 돌아가 우리 아버지 화실에서 마주치곤 하던 그런 사람들과 어울릴 수는 없어."], ["Lords come up to my door with stars and garters, instead of poor artists with screws of tobacco in their pockets.", "주머니에 담배 부스러기나 넣고 다니는 가난한 화가들 대신, 별과 가터 훈장을 단 귀족들이 내 집 문 앞을 찾아오지."], ["I have a gentleman for my husband, and an Earl's daughter for my sister, in the very house where I was little better than a servant a few years ago.", "몇 해 전만 해도 내가 하녀나 다름없던 바로 그 집에서, 이제 신사를 남편으로, 백작의 딸을 시누이로 두고 있어."], ["But am I much better to do now in the world than I was when I was the poor painter's daughter and wheedled the grocer round the corner for sugar and tea?", "하지만 내가 가난한 화가의 딸로 모퉁이 식료품상을 구슬려 설탕이며 차를 얻어 내던 그때보다, 지금 세상에서 형편이 그리 많이 나아진 걸까?"], ["Suppose I had married Francis who was so fond of me--I couldn't have been much poorer than I am now.", "나를 그토록 좋아하던 프랜시스와 결혼했다고 쳐 봐. 지금보다 그리 가난할 것도 없었을 거야."], ["Heigho!", "아이고!"], ["I wish I could exchange my position in society, and all my relations for a snug sum in the Three Per Cent.", "사교계에서의 내 지위와 내 친척들을 죄다, 삼 푼 이자 공채에 든 아늑한 한밑천하고 바꿀 수 있다면 좋으련만.\""], ["Consols\"; for so it was that Becky felt the Vanity of human affairs, and it was in those securities that she would have liked to cast anchor.", "베키가 인간사의 허영을 느낀 것이 바로 이런 식이었고, 그녀가 닻을 내리고 싶어 한 것이 바로 그런 유가증권들이었던 것이다."]], [["It may, perhaps, have struck her that to have been honest and humble, to have done her duty, and to have marched straightforward on her way, would have brought her as near happiness as that path by which she was striving to attain it.", "어쩌면 그녀의 머릿속에는 이런 생각이 스쳤을지도 모른다. 정직하고 겸손하게 살며 제 본분을 다하고 제 길을 곧장 걸어왔더라면, 지금 그녀가 행복에 닿으려 발버둥 치는 그 길만큼이나 그녀를 행복에 가까이 데려다주었으리라는 생각 말이다."], ["But--just as the children at Queen's Crawley went round the room where the body of their father lay--if ever Becky had these thoughts, she was accustomed to walk round them and not look in.", "그러나, 마치 퀸스크롤리의 아이들이 제 아비의 시신이 누운 방을 빙 돌아 지나가듯, 설령 베키에게 이런 생각이 들었더라도 그녀는 으레 그 둘레를 빙 돌아 갈 뿐 안을 들여다보지는 않았다."], ["She eluded them and despised them--or at least she was committed to the other path from which retreat was now impossible.", "그녀는 그런 생각들을 슬쩍 피하고 멸시했다. 아니, 적어도 그녀는 이제 물러설 수 없는 다른 길에 발을 들여놓은 터였다."], ["And for my part I believe that remorse is the least active of all a man's moral senses--the very easiest to be deadened when wakened, and in some never wakened at all.", "그리고 내 생각으로는, 회한이란 인간의 모든 도덕적 감각 중에서 가장 활동성이 떨어지는 것이다. 일단 깨어났다가도 잠재우기가 더없이 쉬운 것이요, 어떤 이들에게서는 아예 깨어나는 법이 없는 것이다."], ["We grieve at being found out and at the idea of shame or punishment, but the mere sense of wrong makes very few people unhappy in Vanity Fair.", "우리는 들통난 것을 두고, 또 수치나 벌을 받으리라는 생각에 슬퍼하지만, 그저 잘못을 저질렀다는 자각만으로 불행해지는 사람은 허영의 시장에 지극히 드문 법이다."]], [["So Rebecca, during her stay at Queen's Crawley, made as many friends of the Mammon of Unrighteousness as she could possibly bring under control.", "그리하여 레베카는 퀸스크롤리에 머무는 동안, 자기가 손에 넣을 수 있는 한 많은 불의의 재물을 벗으로 삼았다."], ["Lady Jane and her husband bade her farewell with the warmest demonstrations of good-will.", "제인 부인과 그 남편은 더없이 따뜻한 호의의 표시와 함께 그녀에게 작별을 고했다."], ["They looked forward with pleasure to the time when, the family house in Gaunt Street being repaired and beautified, they were to meet again in London.", "그들은 곤트 가의 가문 저택을 수리하고 아름답게 단장한 뒤 런던에서 다시 만나게 될 그때를 기쁜 마음으로 고대했다."], ["Lady Southdown made her up a packet of medicine and sent a letter by her to the Rev. Lawrence Grills, exhorting that gentleman to save the brand who \"honoured\"", "사우스다운 부인은 그녀에게 약 꾸러미 하나를 챙겨 주고, 로런스 그릴스 목사 앞으로 보내는 편지 한 통을 들려 보내, 이 편지를 \"영광되이 전하는\""], ["the letter from the burning.", "그 불씨를 불길에서 건져 내라고 그 신사를 권면했다."], ["Pitt accompanied them with four horses in the carriage to Mudbury, having sent on their baggage in a cart previously, accompanied with loads of game.", "피트는 미리 짐을 짐수레에 실어, 사냥감을 잔뜩 곁들여 먼저 보낸 뒤, 네 마리 말이 끄는 마차로 그들을 머드베리까지 배웅했다."]], [["\"How happy you will be to see your darling little boy again!\"", "\"사랑하는 아드님을 다시 만나면 얼마나 기쁘시겠어요!\""], ["Lady Crawley said, taking leave of her kinswoman.", "크롤리 부인이 친척에게 작별을 고하며 말했다."]], [["\"Oh so happy!\"", "\"아, 얼마나 기쁘겠어요!\""], ["said Rebecca, throwing up the green eyes.", "레베카가 초록빛 눈을 치뜨며 말했다."], ["She was immensely happy to be free of the place, and yet loath to go.", "그녀는 이곳을 벗어나게 된 것이 더없이 기뻤지만, 그러면서도 떠나기가 못내 아쉬웠다."], ["Queen's Crawley was abominably stupid, and yet the air there was somehow purer than that which she had been accustomed to breathe.", "퀸스크롤리는 지긋지긋하게 따분한 곳이었으나, 그곳의 공기는 어쩐지 그녀가 늘 들이마시던 공기보다 더 맑았다."], ["Everybody had been dull, but had been kind in their way.", "다들 따분한 사람들이긴 했어도, 저마다의 방식으로 친절했던 것이다."], ["\"It is all the influence of a long course of Three Per Cents,\"", "\"이게 다 삼 푼 이자 공채를 오래 끼고 산 영향이지.\""], ["Becky said to herself, and was right very likely.", "베키는 속으로 중얼거렸고, 아마 그 말이 옳았을 것이다."]], [["However, the London lamps flashed joyfully as the stage rolled into Piccadilly, and Briggs had made a beautiful fire in Curzon Street, and little Rawdon was up to welcome back his papa and mamma.", "그러나 역마차가 피커딜리로 굴러 들어설 때 런던의 등불들이 즐거이 번쩍였고, 브리그스는 커즌 가에 아름다운 불을 지펴 놓았으며, 어린 로던은 아빠와 엄마를 맞으려 잠도 자지 않고 기다리고 있었다."]], [["Which Treats of the Osborne Family\nConsiderable time has elapsed since we have seen our respectable friend, old Mr.", "우리의 존경할 만한 벗, 러셀 광장의 오스본 노인을 마지막으로 본 지 어느덧 상당한 시간이 흘렀다."], ["Osborne of Russell Square. He has not been the happiest of mortals since last we met him.", "마지막으로 만난 이래로 그는 인간들 가운데 가장 행복한 축에 들지 못했다."], ["Events have occurred which have not improved his temper, and in more instances than one he has not been allowed to have his own way.", "그의 성미를 누그러뜨리는 데 아무런 도움이 안 되는 일들이 벌어졌고, 한두 번이 아니게 제 뜻대로 일이 풀리지 못했던 것이다."], ["To be thwarted in this reasonable desire was always very injurious to the old gentleman; and resistance became doubly exasperating when gout, age, loneliness, and the force of many disappointments combined to weigh him down.", "이 합당한 욕망이 좌절되는 것은 이 노신사에게 언제나 몹시 해로운 일이었다. 게다가 통풍에다 나이, 외로움, 그리고 숱한 실망의 무게까지 한데 겹쳐 그를 짓누르니, 그 저항감은 곱절로 더 사람을 노엽게 만들었다."], ["His stiff black hair began to grow quite white soon after his son's death; his face grew redder; his hands trembled more and more as he poured out his glass of port wine.", "빳빳하던 검은 머리는 아들이 죽은 직후부터 완연히 세기 시작했고, 얼굴은 더 벌게졌으며, 포트와인을 잔에 따를 때면 두 손이 점점 더 떨렸다."], ["He led his clerks a dire life in the City: his family at home were not much happier.", "시내에서 그는 점원들을 끔찍한 지경으로 부려 먹었다. 집안 식구들이라고 해서 더 행복할 것도 없었다."], ["I doubt if Rebecca, whom we have seen piously praying for Consols, would have exchanged her poverty and the dare-devil excitement and chances of her life for Osborne's money and the humdrum gloom which enveloped him.", "우리가 콘솔 공채를 위해 경건히 기도하는 모습을 본 적 있는 그 리베카라 하더라도, 자신의 가난과 그 무모할 만큼 짜릿한 흥분과 인생의 모험을 오스본의 돈과 그를 휘감은 단조로운 우울과 맞바꾸려 했을지 나는 의심스럽다."], ["He had proposed for Miss Swartz, but had been rejected scornfully by the partisans of that lady, who married her to a young sprig of Scotch nobility.", "그는 스워츠 양에게 청혼했지만, 그녀를 스코틀랜드 귀족 가문의 풋내기 청년과 결혼시켜 버린 그 부인의 후원자들에게 경멸 어린 거절을 당하고 말았다."], ["He was a man to have married a woman out of low life and bullied her dreadfully afterwards; but no person presented herself suitable to his taste, and, instead, he tyrannized over his unmarried daughter, at home.", "그는 천한 집안의 여자를 아내로 맞아 그 뒤로 끔찍하게 들볶을 법한 사내였지만, 자기 취향에 맞는 여자가 나타나지 않는 통에, 그 대신 집에서 미혼인 딸을 폭군처럼 억눌렀다."], ["She had a fine carriage and fine horses and sat at the head of a table loaded with the grandest plate.", "그녀에게는 훌륭한 마차와 훌륭한 말이 있었고, 더없이 호화로운 식기로 그득한 식탁의 상석에 앉았다."], ["She had a cheque-book, a prize footman to follow her when she walked, unlimited credit, and bows and compliments from all the tradesmen, and all the appurtenances of an heiress; but she spent a woeful time.", "수표책이 있었고, 외출할 때면 뒤따르는 일급 하인이 있었으며, 무제한의 신용과 상인들 모두의 절과 아첨, 그리고 상속녀에게 따르는 온갖 부속물을 거느렸다. 그러나 그녀는 비참한 나날을 보냈다."], ["The little charity-girls at the Foundling, the sweeperess at the crossing, the poorest under-kitchen-maid in the servants' hall, was happy compared to that unfortunate and now middle-aged young lady.", "고아원의 어린 자선 소녀들이며, 길모퉁이의 청소부 여인이며, 하인 방의 가장 미천한 보조 부엌데기조차도, 저 불운하고 이제는 중년에 접어든 처녀에 비하면 행복했다."]], [["Frederick Bullock, Esq., of the house of Bullock, Hulker, and Bullock, had married Maria Osborne, not without a great deal of difficulty and grumbling on Mr. Bullock's part.", "불록·헐커·불록 상사의 프레더릭 불록 귀하는 마리아 오스본과 결혼했는데, 불록 측의 적잖은 어려움과 불평이 없지 않은 끝에 이루어진 일이었다."], ["George being dead and cut out of his father's will, Frederick insisted that the half of the old gentleman's property should be settled upon his Maria, and indeed, for a long time, refused, \"to come to the scratch\" (it was Mr.", "조지가 죽고 아버지의 유언장에서 잘려 나간 마당이니, 프레더릭은 노신사 재산의 절반을 자기 마리아 앞으로 양도해야 한다고 우겼고, 실제로 오랫동안 그 밖의 어떤 조건으로도 \"결판 짓기를\"(프레더릭 본인의 표현이었다) 거부했다."], ["Frederick's own expression) on any other terms. Osborne said Fred had agreed to take his daughter with twenty thousand, and he should bind himself to no more.", "오스본은 프레드가 자기 딸을 이만 파운드와 함께 데려가기로 동의했으니, 그 이상은 한 푼도 묶어 두지 않겠노라고 했다."], ["\"Fred might take it, and welcome, or leave it, and go and be hanged.\"", "\"프레드가 그걸 받아들이겠다면 기꺼이 환영이고, 마다하겠다면 가서 목이나 매라지.\""], ["Fred, whose hopes had been raised when George had been disinherited, thought himself infamously swindled by the old merchant, and for some time made as if he would break off the match altogether.", "조지가 상속에서 제외되었을 때 잔뜩 부풀어 올랐던 기대를 품었던 프레드는 늙은 상인에게 파렴치하게 사기당했다고 여겨, 한동안 혼사를 아예 깨 버릴 듯이 굴었다."], ["Osborne withdrew his account from Bullock and Hulker's, went on 'Change with a horsewhip which he swore he would lay across the back of a certain scoundrel that should be nameless, and demeaned himself in his usual violent manner.", "오스본은 불록·헐커 상사에서 거래 계좌를 빼냈고, 이름은 밝히지 않겠으나 어느 악당의 등짝을 후려쳐 주겠노라 맹세하며 말채찍을 들고 거래소에 나갔으며, 평소의 그 난폭한 태도로 처신했다."], ["Jane Osborne condoled with her sister Maria during this family feud.", "이 집안싸움이 벌어지는 동안 제인 오스본은 동생 마리아를 위로했다."], ["\"I always told you, Maria, that it was your money he loved and not you,\"", "\"내가 늘 말했잖니, 마리아, 그 사람이 사랑한 건 네가 아니라 네 돈이라고.\""], ["she said, soothingly.", "그녀가 달래듯 말했다."]], [["\"He selected me and my money at any rate; he didn't choose you and yours,\"", "\"어쨌든 그 사람은 나와 내 돈을 골랐지, 언니와 언니 돈을 고르진 않았잖아.\""], ["replied Maria, tossing up her head.", "마리아가 머리를 홱 치켜들며 대꾸했다."]], [["The rapture was, however, only temporary.", "그러나 그 격분은 한때의 것일 뿐이었다."], ["Fred's father and senior partners counselled him to take Maria, even with the twenty thousand settled, half down, and half at the death of Mr.", "프레드의 아버지와 선임 동업자들은 그에게, 이만 파운드를 양도하되 절반은 즉시 받고 나머지 절반은 오스본 씨가 죽을 때 받는 조건으로, 그리고 장차 재산이 더 나뉠 가능성까지 끼고서, 마리아를 받아들이라고 권했다. 그리하여 그는 다시 자기 표현을 빌리자면 \"굽히고\""], ["Osborne, with the chances of the further division of the property.", "들어가, 늙은 헐커를 화해의 사절로 오스본에게 보냈다."], ["So he \"knuckled down,\" again to use his own phrase, and sent old Hulker with peaceable overtures to Osborne.", "그가 말하기를, 혼사를 도무지 들으려 하지 않고 온갖 난관을 만든 것은 자기 아버지였으며, 자기로서는 약혼을 지키고 싶어 안달이 났다는 것이었다."], ["It was his father, he said, who would not hear of the match, and had made the difficulties; he was most anxious to keep the engagement.", "오스본 씨는 뚱한 얼굴로 그 변명을 받아들였다. 헐커와 불록은 시내 귀족계의 명문가로, 웨스트엔드의 \"거물들\"과도 연줄이 닿아 있었다."], ["The excuse was sulkily accepted by Mr. Osborne.", "\"내 아들이 말이야, 헐커·불록 상사의 사람이지."], ["Hulker and Bullock were a high family of the City aristocracy, and connected with the \"nobs\"", "내 딸의 사촌은 메리 망고 부인이고, 캐슬몰디 백작 각하의 따님이라네.\""], ["at the West End. It was something for the old man to be able to say, \"My son, sir, of the house of Hulker, Bullock, and Co., sir; my daughter's cousin, Lady Mary Mango, sir, daughter of the Right Hon.", "이렇게 말할 수 있다는 것은 노인에게 자못 대단한 일이었다."], ["The Earl of Castlemouldy.\" In his imagination he saw his house peopled by the \"nobs.\"", "그는 상상 속에서 자기 집안이 \"거물들\"로 그득한 광경을 그려 보았다."], ["So he forgave young Bullock and consented that the marriage should take place.", "그리하여 그는 젊은 불록을 용서하고 혼례를 치르는 데 동의했다."]], [["It was a grand affair--the bridegroom's relatives giving the breakfast, their habitations being near St.", "그것은 성대한 행사였다."], ["George's, Hanover Square, where the business took place. The \"nobs of the West End\"", "신랑 측 친지들이 피로연을 베풀었으며, 그들의 거처가 행사가 거행될 하노버 광장의 세인트조지 교회 근처에 있었던 것이다."], ["were invited, and many of them signed the book. Mr.", "\"웨스트엔드의 거물들\"이 초대되었고, 그들 중 다수가 방명록에 서명했다."], ["Mango and Lady Mary Mango were there, with the dear young Gwendoline and Guinever Mango as bridesmaids; Colonel Bludyer of the Dragoon Guards (eldest son of the house of Bludyer Brothers, Mincing Lane), another cousin of the bridegroom, and the Honourable Mrs.", "망고 씨와 메리 망고 부인이 참석했고, 사랑스러운 젊은 그웬덜린 망고와 귀네버 망고가 들러리를 섰다."], ["Bludyer; the Honourable George Boulter, Lord Levant's son, and his lady, Miss Mango that was; Lord Viscount Castletoddy; Honourable James McMull and Mrs. McMull (formerly Miss Swartz); and a host of fashionables, who have all married into Lombard Street and done a great deal to ennoble Cornhill.", "신랑의 또 다른 사촌인 용기병 근위대의 블러디어 대령(민싱 레인의 블러디어 형제 상사 장남)과 그 부인 블러디어 귀부인이 왔고, 르밴트 경의 아들인 조지 볼터 귀하와 망고 양이었던 그 부인이, 캐슬토디 자작이, 제임스 맥멀 귀하와 (예전의 스워츠 양인) 맥멀 부인이, 그 밖에 롬바드 거리와 혼맥을 맺어 콘힐을 귀하게 빛내는 데 크게 이바지한 멋쟁이들이 한 무리 참석했다."]], [["The young couple had a house near Berkeley Square and a small villa at Roehampton, among the banking colony there.", "이 젊은 부부는 버클리 광장 근처에 집을, 그리고 로햄프턴의 은행가 거주 단지에 자그마한 별장을 마련했다."], ["Fred was considered to have made rather a mesalliance by the ladies of his family, whose grandfather had been in a Charity School, and who were allied through the husbands with some of the best blood in England.", "프레드는 자기 집안 부인네들이 보기에는 다소 격이 낮은 혼사를 한 셈이었는데, 그 부인네들의 할아버지는 자선 학교 출신인 데다 그 부인네들은 남편들을 통해 영국에서 가장 고귀한 혈통과 연이 닿아 있었던 것이다."], ["And Maria was bound, by superior pride and great care in the composition of her visiting-book, to make up for the defects of birth, and felt it her duty to see her father and sister as little as possible.", "그래서 마리아는 빼어난 자존심과 방문객 명부를 꾸미는 데 들이는 각별한 정성으로 출생의 결함을 메워야만 했고, 아버지와 언니를 되도록 멀리하는 것이 자기 의무라고 여겼다."]], [["That she should utterly break with the old man, who had still so many scores of thousand pounds to give away, is absurd to suppose.", "그녀가, 아직 수십만 파운드나 되는 돈을 나누어 줄 여력이 있는 그 노인과 완전히 인연을 끊으리라 짐작하는 것은 어불성설이다."], ["Fred Bullock would never allow her to do that.", "프레드 불록이 결코 그녀에게 그런 짓을 허락할 리 없었다."], ["But she was still young and incapable of hiding her feelings; and by inviting her papa and sister to her third-rate parties, and behaving very coldly to them when they came, and by avoiding Russell Square, and indiscreetly begging her father to quit that odious vulgar place, she did more harm than all Frederick's diplomacy could repair, and perilled her chance of her inheritance like a giddy heedless creature as she was.", "그러나 그녀는 아직 젊었고 자기 감정을 감출 줄 몰랐다. 그래서 아버지와 언니를 자신의 삼류 연회에나 초대하고, 그들이 오면 몹시 쌀쌀맞게 대하며, 러셀 광장에는 발길을 끊고, 분별없이 아버지더러 그 꼴사납고 천박한 동네를 떠나라고 졸라 댐으로써, 프레더릭의 온갖 외교술로도 메우지 못할 만큼 일을 그르치고 말았으니, 분별없고 경솔한 인간이 다 그렇듯 그녀는 자기 상속 기회를 위태롭게 만들었던 것이다."]], [["\"So Russell Square is not good enough for Mrs. Maria, hay?\"", "\"그러니까 러셀 광장이 마리아 마님께는 성에 차지 않으신다 이거지, 응?\""], ["said the old gentleman, rattling up the carriage windows as he and his daughter drove away one night from Mrs. Frederick Bullock's, after dinner.", "어느 날 밤, 프레더릭 불록 부인의 집에서 만찬을 마치고 딸과 함께 마차를 몰아 떠나면서, 노신사는 마차 창문을 덜컹덜컹 올리며 말했다."], ["\"So she invites her father and sister to a second day's dinner (if those sides, or ontrys, as she calls 'em, weren't served yesterday, I'm d--d), and to meet City folks and littery men, and keeps the Earls and the Ladies, and the Honourables to herself.", "\"그래서 제 아비와 언니를 이튿날 다시 데운 만찬에나 초대하시고(그 곁들이 요리인지 앙트레인지 하는 것들이 어제 안 나온 거라면 내 손에 장을 지진다), 시내 사람들이며 글쟁이들이나 만나게 하고, 백작이며 귀부인이며 귀하들은 죄다 저 혼자 끼고 앉으셨다 이거지."], ["Honourables?", "귀하라고?"], ["Damn Honourables.", "빌어먹을 귀하 같으니."], ["I am a plain British merchant I am, and could buy the beggarly hounds over and over.", "나는 평범한 영국 상인이야, 나는. 그깟 거지 같은 작자들쯤은 통째로 사고도 남지."], ["Lords, indeed!--why, at one of her swarreys I saw one of 'em speak to a dam fiddler--a fellar I despise.", "영주라, 참! 어허, 그 연회인지 뭔지 하는 자리에서 내가 그중 하나가 빌어먹을 깽깽이쟁이한테 말 거는 꼴을 봤다니까, 내가 경멸하는 그런 작자한테 말이야."], ["And they won't come to Russell Square, won't they?", "그러고선 러셀 광장에는 안 오시겠다 이거지, 응?"], ["Why, I'll lay my life I've got a better glass of wine, and pay a better figure for it, and can show a handsomer service of silver, and can lay a better dinner on my mahogany, than ever they see on theirs--the cringing, sneaking, stuck-up fools.", "어디, 내 목숨을 걸고 말하건대, 내가 더 좋은 와인을 갖고 있고, 그걸 더 비싼 값에 사고, 더 멋진 은식기 한 벌을 내보이고, 내 마호가니 식탁에 그자들이 제 식탁에서 차려 본 것보다 더 나은 만찬을 차릴 수 있어 — 그 굽실거리고 비굴하고 거드름 피우는 멍청이들 같으니."], ["Drive on quick, James: I want to get back to Russell Square--ha, ha!\"", "어서 몰아라, 제임스. 어서 러셀 광장으로 돌아가자고 — 하, 하!\""], ["and he sank back into the corner with a furious laugh.", "그러고는 사나운 웃음을 터뜨리며 구석에 털썩 기댔다."], ["With such reflections on his own superior merit, it was the custom of the old gentleman not unfrequently to console himself.", "노신사는 자신의 우월한 됨됨이를 두고 이렇게 곱씹으며 적잖이 스스로를 위로하곤 했던 것이다."]], [["Jane Osborne could not but concur in these opinions respecting her sister's conduct; and when Mrs.", "제인 오스본도 동생의 처신을 두고 이런 견해에 동조하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Frederick's first-born, Frederick Augustus Howard Stanley Devereux Bullock, was born, old Osborne, who was invited to the christening and to be godfather, contented himself with sending the child a gold cup, with twenty guineas inside it for the nurse.", "그리고 프레더릭 부인의 맏아이인 프레더릭 오거스터스 하워드 스탠리 데버루 불록이 태어났을 때, 세례식에 대부로 초대받은 늙은 오스본은 갓난아이에게 금잔 하나를 보내고 그 안에 유모 몫으로 이십 기니를 넣어 주는 것으로 그쳤다."], ["\"That's more than any of your Lords will give, I'LL warrant,\"", "\"이만하면 네놈 영주들 누구도 못 내놓을 액수일 게다, 내 장담하지.\""], ["he said and refused to attend at the ceremony.", "그는 그렇게 말하고는 식에 참석하기를 거절했다."]], [["The splendour of the gift, however, caused great satisfaction to the house of Bullock.", "그러나 그 선물의 호화로움은 불록 집안에 큰 만족을 안겼다."], ["Maria thought that her father was very much pleased with her, and Frederick augured the best for his little son and heir.", "마리아는 아버지가 자기를 무척 기특해한다고 생각했고, 프레더릭은 자기 어린 아들이자 후계자에게 더없이 좋은 앞날을 점쳤다."]], [["One can fancy the pangs with which Miss Osborne in her solitude in Russell Square read the Morning Post, where her sister's name occurred every now and then, in the articles headed \"Fashionable Reunions,\"", "러셀 광장의 고독 속에서 오스본 양이 「모닝 포스트」를 읽으며 느꼈을 가슴앓이는 능히 짐작이 간다. 그 신문에는 \"사교계 모임\"이라는 표제 아래 기사가 실릴 때마다 동생의 이름이 심심찮게 등장했고, 그녀는 프레데리카 불록 귀부인의 손에 이끌려 응접실에 인사 들었을 적의 프레더릭 불록 부인의 의상에 관한 묘사까지 읽을 기회를 얻었던 것이다."], ["and where she had an opportunity of reading a description of Mrs.", "앞서 말했듯, 제인 자신의 삶에는 그런 호사라곤 없었다."], ["F. Bullock's costume, when presented at the drawing room by Lady Frederica Bullock.", "그것은 끔찍한 생활이었다."], ["Jane's own life, as we have said, admitted of no such grandeur. It was an awful existence.", "그녀는 깜깜한 겨울 아침이면 일어나 인상을 잔뜩 찌푸린 늙은 아버지의 아침상을 차려야 했다."], ["She had to get up of black winter's mornings to make breakfast for her scowling old father, who would have turned the whole house out of doors if his tea had not been ready at half-past eight.", "여덟 시 반까지 차가 준비되어 있지 않으면 온 집안을 문밖으로 내쫓을 양반이었던 것이다."], ["She remained silent opposite to him, listening to the urn hissing, and sitting in tremor while the parent read his paper and consumed his accustomed portion of muffins and tea.", "그녀는 차 끓이는 항아리가 쉭쉭거리는 소리에 귀 기울이며 그 맞은편에 말없이 앉아, 아버지가 신문을 읽고 늘 먹던 양만큼의 머핀과 차를 비우는 동안 벌벌 떨며 자리를 지켰다."], ["At half-past nine he rose and went to the City, and she was almost free till dinner-time, to make visitations in the kitchen and to scold the servants; to drive abroad and descend upon the tradesmen, who were prodigiously respectful; to leave her cards and her papa's at the great glum respectable houses of their City friends; or to sit alone in the large drawing-room, expecting visitors; and working at a huge piece of worsted by the fire, on the sofa, hard by the great Iphigenia clock, which ticked and tolled with mournful loudness in the dreary room.", "아홉 시 반이 되면 아버지가 일어나 시내로 나가고, 그러면 그녀는 만찬 때까지 거의 자유로운 몸이 되어, 부엌을 순시하며 하인들을 꾸짖고, 마차로 외출해 굉장히 공손한 상인들을 덮치듯 찾아가고, 시내 친구들의 크고 우중충하고 점잖은 저택들에 자기 명함과 아버지 명함을 두고 오고, 아니면 손님을 기다리며 그 커다란 응접실에 홀로 앉아 있곤 했다. 벽난로 곁 소파에 앉아, 그 음울한 방 안에서 구슬프도록 큰 소리로 똑딱이며 시각을 알리는 커다란 이피게니아 시계 바로 옆에서, 거대한 털실 수예품을 짜면서 말이다."], ["The great glass over the mantelpiece, faced by the other great console glass at the opposite end of the room, increased and multiplied between them the brown Holland bag in which the chandelier hung, until you saw these brown Holland bags fading away in endless perspectives, and this apartment of Miss Osborne's seemed the centre of a system of drawing-rooms.", "벽난로 선반 위의 커다란 거울은 방 반대편 끝에 마주 놓인 또 하나의 커다란 콘솔 거울과 맞보며, 그 둘 사이에서 샹들리에를 씌운 갈색 무명 자루를 자꾸자꾸 늘려 비추어, 마침내 그 갈색 무명 자루들이 끝없는 원근 속으로 아득히 사라지는 광경이 보였으니, 오스본 양의 이 거실은 마치 연이은 응접실들로 이루어진 한 우주의 중심처럼 보였다."], ["When she removed the cordovan leather from the grand piano and ventured to play a few notes on it, it sounded with a mournful sadness, startling the dismal echoes of the house.", "그녀가 그랜드 피아노에서 코르도바 가죽 덮개를 걷어 내고 음 몇 개를 쳐 볼라치면, 그 소리는 구슬픈 서글픔을 띠고 울리며 집 안의 음산한 메아리를 깨우는 것이었다."], ["George's picture was gone, and laid upstairs in a lumber-room in the garret; and though there was a consciousness of him, and father and daughter often instinctively knew that they were thinking of him, no mention was ever made of the brave and once darling son.", "조지의 초상화는 사라져 다락방의 허섭스레기 광에 처박혀 있었다. 그를 의식하는 마음이 늘 있었고, 아버지와 딸이 서로 그를 생각하고 있음을 본능적으로 알아챌 때가 잦았지만, 그 용감하고 한때 그토록 귀애받던 아들의 이야기는 결코 입에 오르지 않았다."]], [["At five o'clock Mr. Osborne came back to his dinner, which he and his daughter took in silence (seldom broken, except when he swore and was savage, if the cooking was not to his liking), or which they shared twice in a month with a party of dismal friends of Osborne's rank and age. Old Dr. Gulp and his lady from Bloomsbury Square; old Mr. Frowser, the attorney, from Bedford Row, a very great man, and from his business, hand-in-glove with the \"nobs at the West End\"; old Colonel Livermore, of the Bombay Army, and Mrs. Livermore, from Upper Bedford Place; old Sergeant Toffy and Mrs. Toffy; and sometimes old Sir Thomas Coffin and Lady Coffin, from Bedford Square. Sir Thomas was celebrated as a hanging judge, and the particular tawny port was produced when he dined with Mr. Osborne.", "다섯 시가 되면 오스본 씨가 만찬을 들러 돌아왔다. 그는 딸과 함께 말없이 식사를 했고(요리가 마음에 들지 않으면 욕설을 퍼붓고 사납게 굴 때 말고는 그 침묵이 좀처럼 깨지지 않았다), 한 달에 두 번쯤은 오스본과 같은 신분에 같은 연배인 음울한 친구들 몇과 함께 식탁을 나누었다. 블룸즈버리 광장에서 온 늙은 걸프 박사와 그 부인, 베드퍼드 로에서 온 늙은 변호사 프라우저 씨—사업 덕분에 \"웨스트엔드의 거물들\"과 막역한 사이인 대단한 인물이었다—어퍼 베드퍼드 플레이스에서 온 봄베이 주둔군의 늙은 리버모어 대령과 리버모어 부인, 늙은 토피 상사와 토피 부인, 그리고 이따금 베드퍼드 광장에서 온 늙은 토머스 코핀 경과 코핀 부인이 있었다. 토머스 경은 사형을 즐겨 선고하는 판사로 유명했는데, 그가 오스본 씨와 만찬을 들 때면 그 특별한 황갈색 포트와인이 나왔다."]], [["These people and their like gave the pompous Russell Square merchant pompous dinners back again.", "이런 사람들과 그 부류는 거드름 떠는 그 러셀 광장 상인에게 거드름 떠는 만찬으로 답례하곤 했다."], ["They had solemn rubbers of whist, when they went upstairs after drinking, and their carriages were called at half past ten. Many rich people, whom we poor devils are in the habit of envying, lead contentedly an existence like that above described.", "그들은 술을 마신 뒤 위층으로 올라가 엄숙한 휘스트 게임을 벌였고, 열 시 반이면 마차를 불렀다."], ["Jane Osborne scarcely ever met a man under sixty, and almost the only bachelor who appeared in their society was Mr.", "우리네 가엾은 인간들이 곧잘 부러워하는 부자들 중 다수가 위에서 묘사한 것과 같은 생활을 흡족하게 이어 가는 것이다."], ["Smirk, the celebrated ladies' doctor.", "제인 오스본은 예순 살 아래의 남자를 만나는 일이 거의 없었고, 그들의 사교계에 모습을 보이는 거의 유일한 독신남이라곤 저 유명한 부인과 의사 스머크 씨뿐이었다."]], [["I can't say that nothing had occurred to disturb the monotony of this awful existence: the fact is, there had been a secret in poor Jane's life which had made her father more savage and morose than even nature, pride, and over-feeding had made him.", "이 끔찍한 생활의 단조로움을 흔드는 일이 전혀 없었노라고는 말 못 하겠다. 실은 가엾은 제인의 인생에는 한 가지 비밀이 있었으니, 그것이 그 아버지를 천성과 자존심과 과식이 만들어 놓은 것보다도 더 사납고 침울하게 만들었던 것이다."], ["This secret was connected with Miss Wirt, who had a cousin an artist, Mr.", "이 비밀은 워트 양과 얽혀 있었는데, 그녀에게는 화가인 사촌, 스미 씨가 있었다."], ["Smee, very celebrated since as a portrait-painter and R.A., but who once was glad enough to give drawing lessons to ladies of fashion. Mr.", "그는 훗날 초상화가이자 왕립 미술원 회원으로 자못 유명해졌지만, 한때는 유행을 좇는 부인네들에게 그림 수업을 해 주는 것을 기꺼워하던 인물이었다."], ["Smee has forgotten where Russell Square is now, but he was glad enough to visit it in the year 1818, when Miss Osborne had instruction from him.", "스미 씨는 이제 러셀 광장이 어디 붙어 있는지조차 잊어버렸지만, 오스본 양이 그에게 가르침을 받던 1818년에는 그곳을 기꺼이 드나들었던 것이다."]], [["Smee (formerly a pupil of Sharpe of Frith Street, a dissolute, irregular, and unsuccessful man, but a man with great knowledge of his art) being the cousin of Miss Wirt, we say, and introduced by her to Miss Osborne, whose hand and heart were still free after various incomplete love affairs, felt a great attachment for this lady, and it is believed inspired one in her bosom.", "스미는(전에 프리스 거리의 샤프 문하에 있던 자로, 방탕하고 종잡을 수 없고 변변찮은 사람이었으나 자기 예술에 대해서는 상당한 식견을 갖춘 사람이었다) 우리가 말했듯 워트 양의 사촌으로서 그녀의 소개로 오스본 양과 알게 되었는데, 그 무렵 오스본 양은 여러 차례 어설픈 연애 끝에 손도 마음도 아직 자유로운 처지였다. 그는 이 부인에게 깊은 애착을 느꼈고, 그녀의 가슴에도 그런 마음을 불러일으켰다고 전해진다."], ["Miss Wirt was the confidante of this intrigue.", "워트 양은 이 밀애의 심복이었다. 그녀가 선생과 제자가 그림 그리는 방을 나가 주어, 제삼자가 있는 자리에서는 도무지 살갑게 주고받기 어려운 그 맹세와 정담을 나눌 기회를 마련해 주곤 했는지, 나는 알지 못한다."], ["I know not whether she used to leave the room where the master and his pupil were painting, in order to give them an opportunity for exchanging those vows and sentiments which cannot be uttered advantageously in the presence of a third party; I know not whether she hoped that should her cousin succeed in carrying off the rich merchant's daughter, he would give Miss Wirt a portion of the wealth which she had enabled him to win--all that is certain is that Mr.", "자기 사촌이 그 부유한 상인의 딸을 채어 가는 데 성공하면, 그를 그렇게 만들어 준 대가로 그가 워트 양에게 그 재산의 일부를 떼어 주리라 기대했는지, 그것도 나는 알지 못한다. 다만 확실한 것은, 오스본 씨가 이 거래에 대한 낌새를 어렴풋이 챘다는 것이다."], ["Osborne got some hint of the transaction, came back from the City abruptly, and entered the drawing-room with his bamboo cane; found the painter, the pupil, and the companion all looking exceedingly pale there; turned the former out of doors with menaces that he would break every bone in his skin, and half an hour afterwards dismissed Miss Wirt likewise, kicking her trunks down the stairs, trampling on her bandboxes, and shaking his fist at her hackney coach as it bore her away.", "그는 시내에서 느닷없이 돌아와 등나무 지팡이를 들고 응접실로 들어섰고, 거기서 화가와 제자와 말동무가 죄다 새파랗게 질려 있는 꼴을 발견했다. 그러고는 화가의 뼈란 뼈는 죄다 부러뜨려 주겠다고 으름장을 놓으며 그를 문밖으로 내쫓았고, 반 시간 뒤에는 워트 양마저 내보내며 그녀의 트렁크를 계단 아래로 걷어차고 모자 상자를 짓밟고, 그녀를 태우고 떠나는 삯마차를 향해 주먹을 휘둘렀다."]], [["Jane Osborne kept her bedroom for many days.", "제인 오스본은 여러 날 동안 침실에 틀어박혔다."], ["She was not allowed to have a companion afterwards.", "그 뒤로 그녀에게 말동무가 허락되는 일은 없었다."], ["Her father swore to her that she should not have a shilling of his money if she made any match without his concurrence; and as he wanted a woman to keep his house, he did not choose that she should marry, so that she was obliged to give up all projects with which Cupid had any share.", "아버지는 만약 그녀가 자기 동의 없이 어떤 혼사라도 맺는다면 자기 돈은 한 푼도 못 가져갈 줄 알라고 못 박아 맹세했다. 그리고 자기 집을 건사할 여자가 필요했기에, 그는 그녀가 결혼하는 것을 바라지 않았으니, 그녀는 큐피드와 조금이라도 관련된 온갖 계획을 단념할 수밖에 없었다."], ["During her papa's life, then, she resigned herself to the manner of existence here described, and was content to be an old maid.", "그리하여 아버지가 살아 있는 동안 그녀는 여기 묘사한 그런 생활 방식에 자신을 내맡기고 노처녀로 사는 데 만족했다."], ["Her sister, meanwhile, was having children with finer names every year and the intercourse between the two grew fainter continually.", "그러는 동안 그녀의 동생은 해마다 더 그럴듯한 이름을 지닌 아이들을 낳았고, 두 자매 사이의 왕래는 끊임없이 점점 더 뜸해져 갔다."], ["\"Jane and I do not move in the same sphere of life,\"", "\"제인과 나는 같은 생활권에서 움직이는 사이가 아니에요.\""], ["Mrs. Bullock said.", "불록 부인은 말했다."], ["\"I regard her as a sister, of course\"--which means--what does it mean when a lady says that she regards Jane as a sister?", "\"물론 언니로 여기긴 하지요\"—이 말이 뜻하는 바는—어느 부인이 제인을 언니로 여긴다고 말할 때 그게 대체 무슨 뜻이란 말인가?"]], [["It has been described how the Misses Dobbin lived with their father at a fine villa at Denmark Hill, where there were beautiful graperies and peach-trees which delighted little Georgy Osborne.", "도빈가의 아가씨들이 아버지와 함께 덴마크 힐의 멋진 별장에서 살았다는 것은 앞서 묘사한 바 있다. 그곳에는 어린 조지 오스본이 좋아하던 아름다운 포도밭과 복숭아나무가 있었다."], ["The Misses Dobbin, who drove often to Brompton to see our dear Amelia, came sometimes to Russell Square too, to pay a visit to their old acquaintance Miss Osborne.", "도빈가의 아가씨들은 우리의 사랑스러운 애밀리아를 보러 곧잘 브롬프턴으로 마차를 몰았는데, 이따금 러셀 광장에도 와서 옛 지인인 오스본 양을 방문하곤 했다."], ["I believe it was in consequence of the commands of their brother the Major in India (for whom their papa had a prodigious respect), that they paid attention to Mrs.", "그들이 조지 부인에게 마음을 쓴 것은, 내 생각에 인도에 있는 그들의 오라비 소령(그들의 아버지가 굉장히 존경하던 인물이었다)의 분부 때문이었던 듯하다."], ["George; for the Major, the godfather and guardian of Amelia's little boy, still hoped that the child's grandfather might be induced to relent towards him and acknowledge him for the sake of his son.", "애밀리아의 어린 아들의 대부이자 후견인인 소령은, 그 아이의 할아버지가 아들을 봐서라도 마음을 누그러뜨려 그 아이를 손자로 인정하게 될지도 모른다고 여전히 바라고 있었던 것이다. 도빈가의 아가씨들은 오스본 양에게 애밀리아의 형편을 소상히 알려 주었다."], ["The Misses Dobbin kept Miss Osborne acquainted with the state of Amelia's affairs; how she was living with her father and mother; how poor they were; how they wondered what men, and such men as their brother and dear Captain Osborne, could find in such an insignificant little chit; how she was still, as heretofore, a namby-pamby milk-and-water affected creature--but how the boy was really the noblest little boy ever seen--for the hearts of all women warm towards young children, and the sourest spinster is kind to them.", "그녀가 부모와 함께 살고 있다는 것, 그들이 얼마나 가난한지, 그들의 오라비와 사랑하는 오스본 대위 같은 사내들이 그토록 보잘것없는 자그마한 계집애에게서 대체 무엇을 보았던 건지 자기들로서는 도무지 의아하다는 것, 그녀가 여전히 예나 지금이나 물 탄 듯 밍밍하고 새침을 떠는 감상적인 여자라는 것—그러나 그 아이만큼은 정말이지 여태껏 본 중 가장 의젓한 사내아이라는 것을 말이다. 어린아이를 보면 모든 여인의 마음이 따뜻해지는 법이고, 아무리 심술궂은 노처녀라도 아이들에게는 다정해지기 마련이니까."]], [["One day, after great entreaties on the part of the Misses Dobbin, Amelia allowed little George to go and pass a day with them at Denmark Hill--a part of which day she spent herself in writing to the Major in India.", "어느 날, 도빈가 아가씨들의 거듭된 간청 끝에, 애밀리아는 어린 조지가 덴마크 힐에 가서 그들과 하루를 보내도록 허락해 주었다. 그날의 한나절을 그녀 자신은 인도에 있는 소령에게 편지를 쓰며 보냈다."], ["She congratulated him on the happy news which his sisters had just conveyed to her.", "그녀는 그의 누이들이 방금 전해 준 기쁜 소식을 두고 그를 축하했다."], ["She prayed for his prosperity and that of the bride he had chosen.", "그의 번영과 그가 택한 신부의 번영을 빌었다."], ["She thanked him for a thousand thousand kind offices and proofs of steadfast friendship to her in her affliction.", "자신이 고통에 빠져 있을 때 그가 베풀어 준 천만 가지 다정한 배려와 변함없는 우정의 증표에 감사했다."], ["She told him the last news about little Georgy, and how he was gone to spend that very day with his sisters in the country.", "어린 조지의 최근 소식을 들려주며, 그 아이가 바로 그날 그의 누이들과 함께 시골에서 하루를 보내러 갔다고 적었다."], ["She underlined the letter a great deal, and she signed herself affectionately his friend, Amelia Osborne.", "그녀는 편지 곳곳에 잔뜩 밑줄을 그었고, 다정한 그대의 벗, 애밀리아 오스본이라고 서명했다."], ["She forgot to send any message of kindness to Lady O'Dowd, as her wont was--and did not mention Glorvina by name, and only in italics, as the Major's BRIDE, for whom she begged blessings.", "그녀는 평소의 버릇과 달리 오다우드 부인에게 안부의 말을 전하는 것을 깜빡했고—글로비나의 이름은 거명하지 않았으며, 오직 이탤릭체로 소령의 신부라고만 적어, 그분에게 축복이 깃들기를 빌었다."], ["But the news of the marriage removed the reserve which she had kept up towards him.", "그러나 그 결혼 소식은 그녀가 그를 향해 지켜 온 거리감을 거두어 가게 했다."], ["She was glad to be able to own and feel how warmly and gratefully she regarded him--and as for the idea of being jealous of Glorvina (Glorvina, indeed!), Amelia would have scouted it, if an angel from heaven had hinted it to her.", "그녀는 자신이 그를 얼마나 따뜻하고 고맙게 여기는지 인정하고 또 느낄 수 있게 된 것이 기뻤다—그리고 글로비나를 질투한다는 생각에 이르러서는(글로비나라니, 참!), 설령 하늘에서 내려온 천사가 그런 기미를 넌지시 비쳤더라도 애밀리아는 콧방귀를 뀌며 일축했을 것이다."], ["That night, when Georgy came back in the pony-carriage in which he rejoiced, and in which he was driven by Sir Wm.", "그날 밤, 조지가 자기가 무척 좋아하는, 윌리엄 도빈 경의 늙은 마부가 모는 조랑말 마차를 타고 돌아왔을 때, 그 아이의 목에는 멋진 금줄과 금시계가 걸려 있었다."], ["Dobbin's old coachman, he had round his neck a fine gold chain and watch.", "그는 어떤 노부인이, 예쁘지는 않은데, 그것을 주었다고 말했다."], ["He said an old lady, not pretty, had given it him, who cried and kissed him a great deal.", "자기한테 자꾸 울면서 입을 맞추더라고 했다. 하지만 자기는 그 부인이 마음에 안 든다고 했다."], ["But he didn't like her.", "자기는 포도가 무척 좋다고 했다."], ["He liked grapes very much.", "그리고 자기는 엄마만 좋아한다고 했다."], ["And he only liked his mamma.", "애밀리아는 움찔하며 흠칫했다."], ["Amelia shrank and started; the timid soul felt a presentiment of terror when she heard that the relations of the child's father had seen him.", "그 소심한 영혼은 아이 아버지의 친척들이 그 아이를 보았다는 말을 듣고 두려움의 예감에 사로잡혔던 것이다."]], [["Miss Osborne came back to give her father his dinner.", "오스본 양은 아버지의 만찬을 차려 드리러 돌아왔다."], ["He had made a good speculation in the City, and was rather in a good humour that day, and chanced to remark the agitation under which she laboured.", "그는 시내에서 투기로 재미를 본 터라 그날따라 기분이 제법 좋았고, 우연히 딸이 안절부절못하고 있는 것을 알아챘다."], ["\"What's the matter, Miss Osborne?\"", "\"무슨 일이냐, 오스본 양?\""], ["he deigned to say.", "그가 황송하게도 입을 열었다."]], [["The woman burst into tears.", "여자는 울음을 터뜨렸다."], ["\"Oh, sir,\"", "\"아, 아버지.\""], ["she said, \"I've seen little George.", "그녀가 말했다. \"어린 조지를 봤어요."], ["He is as beautiful as an angel--and so like him!\"", "천사처럼 어여쁜 데다—그 애를 어쩌면 그렇게 빼닮았는지!\""], ["The old man opposite to her did not say a word, but flushed up and began to tremble in every limb.", "그녀 맞은편의 노인은 한마디도 하지 않았으나, 얼굴이 시뻘게지더니 온몸을 떨기 시작했다."]], [["In Which the Reader Has to Double the Cape\nThe astonished reader must be called upon to transport himself ten thousand miles to the military station of Bundlegunge, in the Madras division of our Indian empire, where our gallant old friends of the --th regiment are quartered under the command of the brave Colonel, Sir Michael O'Dowd.", "독자는 놀랍겠지만, 이제 일만 마일을 날아 우리 인도 제국의 마드라스 관구에 있는 번들건지 주둔지로 자리를 옮겨 달라는 부탁을 받아야겠다. 우리의 용맹한 옛 벗들, ××연대가 그 용감한 대령 마이클 오다우드 경의 지휘 아래 주둔하고 있는 곳이다."], ["Time has dealt kindly with that stout officer, as it does ordinarily with men who have good stomachs and good tempers and are not perplexed over much by fatigue of the brain.", "세월은 이 건장한 장교에게 너그러웠으니, 위장이 튼튼하고 성품이 너그러우며 두뇌의 피로에 그리 시달리지 않는 사내들에게 흔히 그러하듯 말이다."], ["The Colonel plays a good knife and fork at tiffin and resumes those weapons with great success at dinner.", "대령은 점심 식사 때 칼과 포크를 솜씨 좋게 놀리고, 저녁 식사 때 다시 그 무기들을 들고 대단한 성공을 거둔다."], ["He smokes his hookah after both meals and puffs as quietly while his wife scolds him as he did under the fire of the French at Waterloo.", "두 끼의 식사 뒤에는 늘 물담배를 피우는데, 워털루에서 프랑스군의 포화 아래에서 그러했던 것만큼이나, 아내가 그를 나무라는 동안에도 조용히 연기를 뿜는다."], ["Age and heat have not diminished the activity or the eloquence of the descendant of the Malonys and the Molloys.", "나이와 더위도 멀로니 가문과 몰로이 가문 후손의 활력이나 달변을 조금도 줄이지 못했다."], ["Her Ladyship, our old acquaintance, is as much at home at Madras as at Brussels in the cantonment as under the tents.", "우리의 옛 지인인 부인께서는 브뤼셀에서나 마드라스에서나, 막사 안에서나 천막 아래에서나 똑같이 편안해하신다."], ["On the march you saw her at the head of the regiment seated on a royal elephant, a noble sight.", "행군 중에는 왕실용 코끼리 위에 올라 연대 선두에 앉은 그녀를 볼 수 있었으니, 참으로 당당한 광경이었다."], ["Mounted on that beast, she has been into action with tigers in the jungle, she has been received by native princes, who have welcomed her and Glorvina into the recesses of their zenanas and offered her shawls and jewels which it went to her heart to refuse.", "그 짐승에 올라탄 채 그녀는 정글에서 호랑이를 상대로 전투에 나섰고, 토착 군주들의 영접을 받았으니, 그들은 그녀와 글로비나를 자기네 규방 깊숙이 맞아들이고 숄과 보석을 권했으며, 그녀로서는 그것을 거절하는 일이 가슴 아플 지경이었다."], ["The sentries of all arms salute her wherever she makes her appearance, and she touches her hat gravely to their salutation.", "어느 병과의 보초든 그녀가 나타나는 곳마다 경례를 올렸고, 그녀는 그 경례에 진중하게 모자에 손을 대어 답했다."], ["Lady O'Dowd is one of the greatest ladies in the Presidency of Madras--her quarrel with Lady Smith, wife of Sir Minos Smith the puisne judge, is still remembered by some at Madras, when the Colonel's lady snapped her fingers in the Judge's lady's face and said SHE'D never walk behind ever a beggarly civilian.", "오다우드 부인은 마드라스 관구에서 가장 지체 높은 숙녀 가운데 하나이니, 배석 판사 마이노스 스미스 경의 부인 스미스 부인과 벌인 그녀의 다툼은 지금도 마드라스의 몇몇 사람이 기억하고 있다. 그때 대령 부인은 판사 부인의 면전에서 손가락을 딱 튕기며, 자기는 한낱 거지 같은 문관 따위의 뒤를 절대 따라 걷지 않겠노라고 말했던 것이다."], ["Even now, though it is five-and-twenty years ago, people remember Lady O'Dowd performing a jig at Government House, where she danced down two Aides-de-Camp, a Major of Madras cavalry, and two gentlemen of the Civil Service; and, persuaded by Major Dobbin, C.B., second in command of the --th, to retire to the supper-room, lassata nondum satiata recessit.", "지금까지도, 그것이 이십오 년 전 일이건만, 사람들은 오다우드 부인이 총독 관저에서 지그 춤을 추던 모습을 기억한다. 그녀는 부관 두 명과 마드라스 기병대의 소령 한 명, 그리고 문관 두 명을 춤으로 거꾸러뜨렸으며, ××연대 부지휘관인 도빈 소령(바스 훈작사)이 야식방으로 물러가시라 권하자, 지쳤으나 아직 흡족하지 못한 채 물러갔던 것이다."]], [["Peggy O'Dowd is indeed the same as ever, kind in act and thought; impetuous in temper; eager to command; a tyrant over her Michael; a dragon amongst all the ladies of the regiment; a mother to all the young men, whom she tends in their sickness, defends in all their scrapes, and with whom Lady Peggy is immensely popular.", "* 라사타 논둠 사티아타 레케시트: \"지쳤으나 아직 흡족하지 못한 채 물러갔다\"는 뜻의 라틴어 구절. 페기 오다우드는 참으로 변함이 없으니, 행동과 생각에서 다정하고, 성미는 불같으며, 명령하기를 좋아하고, 남편 마이클에게는 폭군이요, 연대의 모든 부인들 사이에서는 용 같은 존재이며, 젊은 사내들 모두에게는 어머니 같은 존재였다."], ["But the Subalterns' and Captains'", "그들이 병들면 보살피고, 곤경에 처하면 두둔해 주니, 페기 부인은 그 청년들 사이에서 엄청난 인기를 누렸다."], ["ladies (the Major is unmarried) cabal against her a good deal.", "그러나 소위와 대위의 부인들은(소령은 미혼이었다) 그녀를 두고 꽤나 작당하여 험담을 늘어놓았다."], ["They say that Glorvina gives herself airs and that Peggy herself is intolerably domineering. She interfered with a little congregation which Mrs.", "그들 말로는 글로비나가 잘난 척한다느니, 페기 본인이 견딜 수 없을 만큼 위세를 부린다느니 하는 것이었다."], ["Kirk had got up and laughed the young men away from her sermons, stating that a soldier's wife had no business to be a parson--that Mrs.", "그녀는 커크 부인이 꾸린 자그마한 기도 모임에 끼어들어, 젊은 사내들을 비웃어 그 설교 자리에서 쫓아 버렸다."], ["Kirk would be much better mending her husband's clothes; and, if the regiment wanted sermons, that she had the finest in the world, those of her uncle, the Dean.", "군인의 아내가 목사 노릇을 할 일이 아니라느니, 커크 부인은 차라리 남편 옷이나 깁는 편이 훨씬 나을 거라느니 하면서, 연대에 설교가 필요하다면 자기에게 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 설교, 곧 자기 숙부인 수석 사제의 설교가 있다고 했다."], ["She abruptly put a termination to a flirtation which Lieutenant Stubble of the regiment had commenced with the Surgeon's wife, threatening to come down upon Stubble for the money which he had borrowed from her (for the young fellow was still of an extravagant turn) unless he broke off at once and went to the Cape on sick leave. On the other hand, she housed and sheltered Mrs.", "또한 그녀는 연대의 스터블 중위가 외과의의 아내와 시작한 시시덕거림을 단칼에 끝장내 버렸으니, 당장 그만두고 병가를 내어 케이프로 떠나지 않으면 그가 자기에게서 빌린 돈을 받아 내겠다고 으름장을 놓은 것이다(그 청년은 여전히 헤픈 성미였다)."], ["Posky, who fled from her bungalow one night, pursued by her infuriate husband, wielding his second brandy bottle, and actually carried Posky through the delirium tremens and broke him of the habit of drinking, which had grown upon that officer, as all evil habits will grow upon men.", "다른 한편으로 그녀는 어느 밤 격분한 남편이 두 번째 브랜디 병을 휘두르며 뒤쫓는 가운데 자기 방갈로에서 도망쳐 나온 포스키 부인을 거두어 보살펴 주었고, 또 실제로 포스키를 진전섬망에서 끌어내어, 모든 악습이 사내에게 그러하듯 그 장교에게 점점 자라난 음주 습관을 끊게 만들었다."], ["In a word, in adversity she was the best of comforters, in good fortune the most troublesome of friends, having a perfectly good opinion of herself always and an indomitable resolution to have her own way.", "한마디로, 역경에 처해서는 더없이 훌륭한 위로자였고, 행운이 따를 때는 더없이 성가신 벗이었으니, 늘 자기 자신에 대해 더없이 흡족한 견해를 품고, 무슨 일이든 제 뜻대로 하고야 말겠다는 꺾을 수 없는 결의를 지니고 있었다."]], [["Among other points, she had made up her mind that Glorvina should marry our old friend Dobbin.", "여러 가지 가운데서도 그녀는 글로비나를 우리의 옛 벗 도빈과 짝지어야겠다고 마음을 굳혔다."], ["Mrs. O'Dowd knew the Major's expectations and appreciated his good qualities and the high character which he enjoyed in his profession.", "오다우드 부인은 소령의 재산 기대치를 알았고, 그의 훌륭한 자질과 그 직업에서 누리는 높은 명망을 높이 샀다."], ["Glorvina, a very handsome, fresh-coloured, black-haired, blue-eyed young lady, who could ride a horse, or play a sonata with any girl out of the County Cork, seemed to be the very person destined to insure Dobbin's happiness--much more than that poor good little weak-spur'ted Amelia, about whom he used to take on so.--\"Look at Glorvina enter a room,\"", "글로비나는 더없이 잘생기고 혈색이 싱그러우며 검은 머리에 푸른 눈을 한 젊은 숙녀로, 콕 주의 어느 처녀 못지않게 말을 탈 줄도, 소나타를 연주할 줄도 알았으니, 도빈의 행복을 보장해 줄 운명을 타고난 바로 그 사람으로 보였다. 그가 늘 그토록 애를 태우던 저 가엾고 착하고 자그마하고 기개 없는 애밀리아보다 훨씬 더 어울렸던 것이다."], ["Mrs. O'Dowd would say, \"and compare her with that poor Mrs.", "\"글로비나가 방에 들어서는 걸 보세요.\""], ["Osborne, who couldn't say boo to a goose.", "오다우드 부인은 말하곤 했다."], ["She'd be worthy of you, Major--you're a quiet man yourself, and want some one to talk for ye.", "\"그러고는 그 가엾은 오스본 부인하고 비교해 보세요, 거위한테 훠이 소리 하나 못 지르는 그 여자 말이에요. 글로비나야말로 소령님께 어울리는 사람이에요."], ["And though she does not come of such good blood as the Malonys or Molloys, let me tell ye, she's of an ancient family that any nobleman might be proud to marry into.\"", "소령님은 본디 말수가 적은 분이니, 대신 말해 줄 사람이 있어야지요. 그리고 비록 그 애가 멀로니 가문이나 몰로이 가문만큼 좋은 핏줄은 아니라 해도, 분명히 말씀드리지만 그 애는 어떤 귀족이라도 사돈으로 맺기를 자랑스러워할 만한 유서 깊은 집안 출신이랍니다.\""]], [["But before she had come to such a resolution and determined to subjugate Major Dobbin by her endearments, it must be owned that Glorvina had practised them a good deal elsewhere.", "그러나 글로비나가 그런 결심에 이르러 자신의 애교로 도빈 소령을 굴복시키기로 작정하기 전에, 그녀가 다른 데서 그 애교를 꽤나 갈고닦았다는 사실은 인정해야겠다."], ["She had had a season in Dublin, and who knows how many in Cork, Killarney, and Mallow?", "그녀는 더블린에서 한 사교철을 보냈고, 콕과 킬라니, 맬로에서는 또 몇 차례나 보냈는지 누가 알겠는가?"], ["She had flirted with all the marriageable officers whom the depots of her country afforded, and all the bachelor squires who seemed eligible.", "그녀는 자기 고장의 주둔지가 배출한 결혼 적령기의 장교들 모두와, 그리고 자격이 있어 보이는 독신 향사들 모두와 시시덕거렸다."], ["She had been engaged to be married a half-score times in Ireland, besides the clergyman at Bath who used her so ill.", "그녀를 그토록 모질게 대한 바스의 그 목사 말고도, 아일랜드에서 약혼한 것만 열 번이 넘었다."], ["She had flirted all the way to Madras with the Captain and chief mate of the Ramchunder East Indiaman, and had a season at the Presidency with her brother and Mrs.", "람천더호 동인도 무역선의 선장과 일등 항해사를 상대로는 마드라스로 오는 내내 시시덕거렸고, 연대 소령이 주둔지 지휘를 맡는 동안 마침 그곳에 머물던 오빠와 오다우드 부인과 함께 관구에서 한 사교철을 보냈다."], ["O'Dowd, who was staying there, while the Major of the regiment was in command at the station.", "그곳에서 다들 그녀를 흠모했고, 다들 그녀와 춤을 추었으나, 결혼할 만한 가치가 있는 자로서 청혼한 이는 아무도 없었다."], ["Everybody admired her there; everybody danced with her; but no one proposed who was worth the marrying--one or two exceedingly young subalterns sighed after her, and a beardless civilian or two, but she rejected these as beneath her pretensions--and other and younger virgins than Glorvina were married before her.", "지극히 어린 소위 한둘이 그녀를 그리며 한숨을 쉬었고, 수염도 안 난 문관 한둘이 그러했으나, 그녀는 이런 자들을 제 격에 못 미친다 하여 물리쳤다. 그러는 사이 글로비나보다 더 어린 처녀들이 그녀보다 먼저 시집을 갔다."], ["There are women, and handsome women too, who have this fortune in life.", "인생에서 이런 팔자를 타고난 여자들이 있으니, 잘생긴 여자도 그러하다."], ["They fall in love with the utmost generosity; they ride and walk with half the Army-list, though they draw near to forty, and yet the Misses O'Grady are the Misses O'Grady still: Glorvina persisted that but for Lady O'Dowd's unlucky quarrel with the Judge's lady, she would have made a good match at Madras, where old Mr.", "그들은 더없이 후하게 사랑에 빠지고, 마흔에 가까워지도록 육군 명부의 절반과 더불어 말을 타고 산책을 하건만, 그래도 오그래디 가의 처녀들은 여전히 오그래디 가의 처녀들로 남는 것이다. 글로비나는, 오다우드 부인이 판사 부인과 그 불운한 다툼만 벌이지 않았더라면 마드라스에서 좋은 혼처를 잡았을 거라고 우겼다."], ["Chutney, who was at the head of the civil service (and who afterwards married Miss Dolby, a young lady only thirteen years of age who had just arrived from school in Europe), was just at the point of proposing to her.", "문관의 우두머리였던 노신사 처트니 씨가(그는 나중에 막 유럽의 학교를 마치고 도착한 겨우 열세 살짜리 젊은 숙녀 돌비 양과 결혼했다) 그녀에게 막 청혼하려던 참이었다는 것이다."]], [["Well, although Lady O'Dowd and Glorvina quarrelled a great number of times every day, and upon almost every conceivable subject--indeed, if Mick O'Dowd had not possessed the temper of an angel two such women constantly about his ears would have driven him out of his senses--yet they agreed between themselves on this point, that Glorvina should marry Major Dobbin, and were determined that the Major should have no rest until the arrangement was brought about.", "자, 비록 오다우드 부인과 글로비나가 날마다 수도 없이, 그것도 거의 생각할 수 있는 온갖 주제를 두고 다투기는 했지만(사실 믹 오다우드가 천사 같은 성품을 지니지 않았더라면, 그런 두 여자가 끊임없이 귓전에서 들볶아 댄 통에 정신이 나가고 말았을 것이다), 그래도 이 한 가지 점에서만큼은 둘이 뜻을 같이했으니, 글로비나가 도빈 소령과 결혼해야 한다는 것이었다. 그리고 그 일이 성사되기 전에는 소령이 절대 마음 편할 날이 없도록 하겠다고 작심한 것이었다."], ["Undismayed by forty or fifty previous defeats, Glorvina laid siege to him.", "마흔, 쉰 번에 이르는 앞선 패배에도 굴하지 않고 글로비나는 그에게 공성을 펼쳤다."], ["She sang Irish melodies at him unceasingly.", "그녀는 끊임없이 그를 향해 아일랜드 가락을 불렀다."], ["She asked him so frequently and pathetically, Will ye come to the bower?", "\"그대 정자로 오시려나요?\""], ["that it is a wonder how any man of feeling could have resisted the invitation.", "하고 어찌나 자주, 또 어찌나 애절하게 물었는지, 감정 있는 사내라면 그 초대를 어떻게 거역할 수 있었을지 신기할 따름이었다."], ["She was never tired of inquiring, if Sorrow had his young days faded, and was ready to listen and weep like Desdemona at the stories of his dangers and his campaigns.", "\"슬픔의 젊은 날도 시들었던가요?\" 하고 묻기를 결코 지치는 법이 없었고, 그의 위험과 출정 이야기를 들으면 데스데모나처럼 귀 기울이며 눈물 흘릴 채비가 되어 있었다."], ["It has been said that our honest and dear old friend used to perform on the flute in private; Glorvina insisted upon having duets with him, and Lady O'Dowd would rise and artlessly quit the room when the young couple were so engaged.", "우리의 정직하고 다정한 옛 벗이 남몰래 플루트를 연주하곤 했다는 말은 앞서도 했거니와, 글로비나는 그와 이중주를 하겠다고 고집을 부렸고, 그 젊은 두 사람이 그러고 있을 때면 오다우드 부인은 자리에서 일어나 짐짓 자연스러운 척 방을 나가곤 했다."], ["Glorvina forced the Major to ride with her of mornings.", "글로비나는 아침마다 소령에게 자기와 함께 말을 타라고 강요했다."], ["The whole cantonment saw them set out and return.", "주둔지 전체가 그들이 나섰다가 돌아오는 모습을 보았다."], ["She was constantly writing notes over to him at his house, borrowing his books, and scoring with her great pencil-marks such passages of sentiment or humour as awakened her sympathy.", "그녀는 끊임없이 그의 집으로 쪽지를 써 보내고, 그의 책을 빌려 가서는 자기 마음에 와닿는 감상적이거나 익살스러운 구절마다 굵직한 연필 자국으로 줄을 그어 놓았다."], ["She borrowed his horses, his servants, his spoons, and palanquin--no wonder that public rumour assigned her to him, and that the Major's sisters in England should fancy they were about to have a sister-in-law.", "그의 말도, 하인도, 숟가락도, 가마도 빌렸으니, 세상 소문이 그녀를 그의 짝으로 점찍고, 영국에 있는 소령의 누이들이 곧 올케를 보게 되리라 짐작한 것도 당연한 일이었다."]], [["Dobbin, who was thus vigorously besieged, was in the meanwhile in a state of the most odious tranquillity.", "이렇게 맹렬한 공성에 시달리던 도빈은, 그러는 내내 더없이 얄미울 만큼 태평한 상태였다. 연대의 젊은 친구들이 글로비나가 그에게 베푸는 노골적인 관심을 두고 놀려 댈 때면 그는 웃어넘기곤 했다."], ["He used to laugh when the young fellows of the regiment joked him about Glorvina's manifest attentions to him.", "\"흥!\" 그가 말했다."], ["\"Bah!\"", "\"그저 솜씨를 녹슬지 않게 하는 거라고."], ["said he, \"she is only keeping her hand in--she practises upon me as she does upon Mrs.", "토저 부인의 피아노를 가지고 연습하듯이 날 가지고 연습하는 거야. 주둔지에서 가장 손쉬운 악기니까."], ["Tozer's piano, because it's the most handy instrument in the station.", "나는 너무 닳고 늙어서 글로비나 같은 어여쁜 젊은 숙녀에게는 어림없는 상대거든.\" 그러면서 그는 계속 그녀와 말을 타고, 그녀의 앨범에 악보와 시구를 베껴 적어 주고, 더없이 고분고분하게 그녀와 체스를 두었다."], ["I am much too battered and old for such a fine young lady as Glorvina.\"", "인도에 주둔하는 어떤 장교들은 이런 소박한 놀이로 한가한 시간을 흘려보내는 데 익숙하기 때문이다. 반면 가정적인 기질이 덜한 다른 장교들은 멧돼지를 사냥하고 도요새를 쏘거나, 노름을 하고 여송연을 피우며 브랜디 물에 빠져 지냈다."], ["And so he went on riding with her, and copying music and verses into her albums, and playing at chess with her very submissively; for it is with these simple amusements that some officers in India are accustomed to while away their leisure moments, while others of a less domestic turn hunt hogs, and shoot snipes, or gamble and smoke cheroots, and betake themselves to brandy-and-water.", "마이클 오다우드 경으로 말하자면, 부인과 그 처제가 둘 다 소령을 불러 분명히 입장을 밝히게 하고 그렇게 부끄러운 짓으로 가엾고 죄 없는 처녀를 계속 괴롭히지 못하게 하라고 그를 다그쳤건만, 노병은 그런 모의에 일절 끼어들기를 딱 잘라 거부했다. \"이런, 소령은 다 큰 사람이니 알아서 고를 거요.\""], ["As for Sir Michael O'Dowd, though his lady and her sister both urged him to call upon the Major to explain himself and not keep on torturing a poor innocent girl in that shameful way, the old soldier refused point-blank to have anything to do with the conspiracy.", "마이클 경이 말했다. \"자네가 필요하면 자네한테 청할 거요.\""], ["\"Faith, the Major's big enough to choose for himself,\"", "그러지 않으면 그는 그 일을 농으로 돌려 버리며, \"도빈은 살림을 차리기엔 너무 어려서 집에다 엄마 허락을 받겠다고 편지를 써 보냈다네\"라고 단언하곤 했다. 아니, 그는 한 걸음 더 나아가, 소령과 사사로이 이야기를 나눌 때면 그를 조심시키고 놀려 대기까지 했다."], ["Sir Michael said; \"he'll ask ye when he wants ye\"; or else he would turn the matter off jocularly, declaring that \"Dobbin was too young to keep house, and had written home to ask lave of his mamma.\"", "\"정신 똑바로 차리게, 도브, 이 사람아. 그 처자들이 무슨 일을 벌이려고 단단히 작정했어."], ["Nay, he went farther, and in private communications with his Major would caution and rally him, crying, \"Mind your oi, Dob, my boy, them girls is bent on mischief--me Lady has just got a box of gowns from Europe, and there's a pink satin for Glorvina, which will finish ye, Dob, if it's in the power of woman or satin to move ye.\"", "우리 부인이 방금 유럽에서 옷 한 상자를 받았는데, 글로비나한테 줄 분홍 새틴 드레스가 들어 있다네. 여자나 새틴이 자넬 움직일 힘이 있다면, 그게 자넬 끝장내 버릴 거야, 도브.\""]], [["But the truth is, neither beauty nor fashion could conquer him.", "그러나 진실을 말하자면, 미모도 유행도 그를 정복하지 못했다."], ["Our honest friend had but one idea of a woman in his head, and that one did not in the least resemble Miss Glorvina O'Dowd in pink satin.", "우리의 정직한 벗은 머릿속에 여자에 대한 단 하나의 관념만을 품고 있었고, 그 하나는 분홍 새틴을 두른 글로비나 오다우드 양과는 조금도 닮지 않았다."], ["A gentle little woman in black, with large eyes and brown hair, seldom speaking, save when spoken to, and then in a voice not the least resembling Miss Glorvina's--a soft young mother tending an infant and beckoning the Major up with a smile to look at him--a rosy-cheeked lass coming singing into the room in Russell Square or hanging on George Osborne's arm, happy and loving--there was but this image that filled our honest Major's mind, by day and by night, and reigned over it always.", "검은 옷을 입은 부드럽고 자그마한 여인, 큰 눈에 갈색 머리를 하고, 말을 거는 때 말고는 좀처럼 입을 열지 않으며, 그럴 때면 글로비나 양과는 조금도 닮지 않은 목소리로 말하는 여인. 갓난아기를 돌보며 미소로 소령을 불러 아이를 들여다보게 하는 부드러운 젊은 어머니."], ["Very likely Amelia was not like the portrait the Major had formed of her: there was a figure in a book of fashions which his sisters had in England, and with which William had made away privately, pasting it into the lid of his desk, and fancying he saw some resemblance to Mrs.", "러셀 광장의 방으로 노래하며 들어오거나, 행복하게 사랑에 겨워 조지 오스본의 팔에 매달려 있던 발그레한 볼의 아가씨. 우리의 정직한 소령의 마음을 밤낮으로 채우고 늘 그 마음을 지배한 것은 오직 이 모습뿐이었다."], ["Osborne in the print, whereas I have seen it, and can vouch that it is but the picture of a high-waisted gown with an impossible doll's face simpering over it--and, perhaps, Mr.", "어쩌면 애밀리아는 소령이 마음속에 빚어 둔 초상과 닮지 않았을지도 모른다. 영국에 있는 누이들이 가지고 있던 유행 화보집 속에 한 인물이 있었는데, 윌리엄은 그것을 남몰래 빼돌려 자기 책상 뚜껑 안쪽에 붙여 두고는, 그 그림이 오스본 부인과 어딘가 닮았다고 상상하곤 했다."], ["Dobbin's sentimental Amelia was no more like the real one than this absurd little print which he cherished.", "그러나 나는 그것을 보았고, 그것이 한낱 허리선이 높은 가운 그림에 불과하며 그 위로 도무지 있을 법하지 않은 인형 같은 얼굴이 새치름하게 웃고 있을 뿐임을 장담할 수 있다."], ["But what man in love, of us, is better informed?--or is he much happier when he sees and owns his delusion? Dobbin was under this spell.", "어쩌면 도빈 씨의 감상에 젖은 애밀리아도, 그가 소중히 간직한 이 우스꽝스러운 작은 그림이 실물과 닮지 않은 것 이상으로 실물과 닮지 않았을지 모른다."], ["He did not bother his friends and the public much about his feelings, or indeed lose his natural rest or appetite on account of them.", "그러나 우리 가운데 사랑에 빠진 사내치고 누가 그보다 더 잘 안단 말인가? 또 자기 환상을 똑똑히 보고 인정한들 그가 더 행복해지기나 하던가?"], ["His head has grizzled since we saw him last, and a line or two of silver may be seen in the soft brown hair likewise.", "도빈은 이 마법에 걸려 있었다. 그는 자기 감정을 두고 벗들이나 세상 사람들을 그리 성가시게 하지 않았고, 그 때문에 타고난 잠이나 식욕을 잃지도 않았다."], ["But his feelings are not in the least changed or oldened, and his love remains as fresh as a man's recollections of boyhood are.", "우리가 그를 마지막으로 본 이래 그의 머리는 희끗희끗해졌고, 부드러운 갈색 머리칼 사이로도 은빛 줄기 한두 가닥이 보였다. 그러나 그의 감정은 조금도 변하거나 늙지 않았고, 그의 사랑은 사내가 소년 시절을 떠올릴 때만큼이나 싱그럽게 남아 있었다."]], [["We have said how the two Misses Dobbin and Amelia, the Major's correspondents in Europe, wrote him letters from England, Mrs. Osborne congratulating him with great candour and cordiality upon his approaching nuptials with Miss O'Dowd.", "소령의 유럽 쪽 서신 상대인 도빈 자매와 애밀리아가 그에게 영국에서 편지를 보냈다는 이야기는 앞서 했거니와, 오스본 부인은 그가 오다우드 양과 곧 혼인하게 된 것을 두고 더없이 솔직하고 다정하게 축하의 말을 전했다."], ["\"Your sister has just kindly visited me,\"", "\"댁의 누이께서 방금 친절하게도 저를 찾아 주셔서,\""], ["Amelia wrote in her letter, \"and informed me of an INTERESTING EVENT, upon which I beg to offer my MOST SINCERE CONGRATULATIONS.", "애밀리아는 편지에 이렇게 적었다. \"한 흥미로운 사건을 알려 주셨는데, 그에 대해 더없이 진심 어린 축하를 드리고자 합니다."], ["I hope the young lady to whom I hear you are to be UNITED will in every respect prove worthy of one who is himself all kindness and goodness.", "듣자 하니 당신과 맺어지실 그 젊은 숙녀가, 그 자신이 더없이 다정하고 선량한 분께 모든 면에서 걸맞은 사람으로 드러나기를 바랍니다."], ["The poor widow has only her prayers to offer and her cordial cordial wishes for YOUR PROSPERITY!", "가엾은 과부에게는 그저 기도를 올리고 당신의 번영을 진심으로, 진심으로 빌어 드리는 일밖에 없네요!"], ["Georgy sends his love to HIS DEAR GODPAPA and hopes that you will not forget him.", "조지가 사랑하는 대부님께 안부를 전하며, 부디 자기를 잊지 말아 달라고 합니다."], ["I tell him that you are about to form OTHER TIES, with one who I am sure merits ALL YOUR AFFECTION, but that, although such ties must of course be the strongest and most sacred, and supersede ALL OTHERS, yet that I am sure the widow and the child whom you have ever protected and loved will always HAVE A CORNER IN YOUR HEART.\"", "저는 그 애에게, 당신께서 당신의 모든 애정을 받기에 마땅한 분과 새로운 인연을 맺으려 하신다고, 그리고 그런 인연이 물론 가장 강하고 신성한 것이어서 다른 모든 인연을 앞설 테지만, 그래도 당신께서 늘 지켜 주고 사랑해 주신 과부와 아이에게도 분명 당신 마음 한구석에 자리가 있을 거라고 일러 주었답니다.\""], ["The letter, which has been before alluded to, went on in this strain, protesting throughout as to the extreme satisfaction of the writer.", "앞서 언급한 그 편지는 이런 투로 이어지면서, 글쓴이가 더없이 흡족하다는 것을 처음부터 끝까지 거듭 주장하고 있었다."]], [["This letter, which arrived by the very same ship which brought out Lady O'Dowd's box of millinery from London (and which you may be sure Dobbin opened before any one of the other packets which the mail brought him), put the receiver into such a state of mind that Glorvina, and her pink satin, and everything belonging to her became perfectly odious to him.", "이 편지는 다름 아닌, 런던에서 오다우드 부인의 모자 상자를 실어 온 바로 그 배편으로 도착했는데(그리고 도빈이 우편으로 받은 다른 어떤 꾸러미보다 이것을 먼저 뜯어 보았으리라는 것은 굳이 말할 필요도 없다), 받은 이의 마음을 어찌나 어지럽혀 놓았던지 글로비나도, 그녀의 분홍 새틴도, 그녀에게 딸린 모든 것이 그에게는 완전히 역겨워졌다."], ["The Major cursed the talk of women, and the sex in general.", "소령은 여자들의 수다를, 그리고 여성이라는 부류 전체를 저주했다."], ["Everything annoyed him that day--the parade was insufferably hot and wearisome.", "그날은 모든 것이 그를 짜증나게 했으니, 사열은 견딜 수 없이 덥고 따분했다."], ["Good heavens!", "맙소사!"], ["was a man of intellect to waste his life, day after day, inspecting cross-belts and putting fools through their manoeuvres?", "지성을 갖춘 사내가 날이면 날마다 가죽 멜빵이나 점검하고 멍청이들에게 제식 훈련이나 시키며 인생을 허비해야 한단 말인가?"], ["The senseless chatter of the young men at mess was more than ever jarring.", "식당에서 떠드는 젊은이들의 분별없는 잡담이 그 어느 때보다 거슬렸다."], ["What cared he, a man on the high road to forty, to know how many snipes Lieutenant Smith had shot, or what were the performances of Ensign Brown's mare?", "마흔을 향해 한길로 달려가는 사내가, 스미스 중위가 도요새를 몇 마리나 잡았는지, 브라운 소위의 암말이 어떤 솜씨를 부렸는지 안들 무슨 상관이란 말인가?"], ["The jokes about the table filled him with shame.", "식탁에서 오가는 농담은 그를 부끄러움으로 가득 채웠다."], ["He was too old to listen to the banter of the assistant surgeon and the slang of the youngsters, at which old O'Dowd, with his bald head and red face, laughed quite easily.", "그는 너무 늙어서 외과 보조의의 익살이나 젊은이들의 속어 따위를 듣고 앉아 있을 수가 없었다. 대머리에 붉은 얼굴을 한 늙은 오다우드는 그런 농담에도 아주 수월하게 웃어 댔다."], ["The old man had listened to those jokes any time these thirty years--Dobbin himself had been fifteen years hearing them.", "노인은 지난 삼십 년 내내 그 농담들을 들어 왔고, 도빈 자신도 십오 년째 그것들을 듣고 있었다."], ["And after the boisterous dulness of the mess-table, the quarrels and scandal of the ladies of the regiment!", "게다가 식탁의 요란하고 따분한 분위기 뒤에는, 연대 부인들의 다툼과 험담이 있었으니! 견딜 수 없고 부끄러운 노릇이었다."], ["It was unbearable, shameful.", "\"오, 애밀리아, 애밀리아.\""], ["\"O Amelia, Amelia,\"", "그는 생각했다."], ["he thought, \"you to whom I have been so faithful--you reproach me!", "\"내가 그토록 변함없이 충실했던 그대가, 나를 나무라다니!"], ["It is because you cannot feel for me that I drag on this wearisome life.", "그대가 나를 헤아려 줄 수 없기에 내가 이 따분한 삶을 질질 끌고 가는 거요."], ["And you reward me after years of devotion by giving me your blessing upon my marriage, forsooth, with this flaunting Irish girl!\"", "그런데 그대는 몇 해에 걸친 헌신의 대가로, 글쎄, 이 요란뻑적지근한 아일랜드 처녀와의 결혼을 축복해 주다니!\""], ["Sick and sorry felt poor William; more than ever wretched and lonely.", "가엾은 윌리엄은 속이 상하고 서글펐고, 그 어느 때보다 비참하고 외로웠다."], ["He would like to have done with life and its vanity altogether--so bootless and unsatisfactory the struggle, so cheerless and dreary the prospect seemed to him.", "그는 삶과 그 허영 전부와 영영 인연을 끊고 싶었으니, 그 몸부림이 그토록 헛되고 보람 없으며, 그 앞날이 그토록 황량하고 쓸쓸해 보였던 것이다. 그는 그날 밤 내내 한잠도 못 이루고 누워, 고향으로 돌아가기를 갈망했다."], ["He lay all that night sleepless, and yearning to go home.", "애밀리아의 편지는 그에게 텅 빈 공백처럼 떨어졌다."], ["Amelia's letter had fallen as a blank upon him.", "그 어떤 충절도, 변함없는 진심과 열정도 그녀를 따뜻하게 움직일 수 없었다."], ["No fidelity, no constant truth and passion, could move her into warmth.", "그녀는 그가 자기를 사랑한다는 것을 보려 하지 않았다. 침대에서 뒤척이며 그는 그녀에게 소리 내어 말했다."], ["She would not see that he loved her.", "\"맙소사, 애밀리아!\" 그가 말했다."], ["Tossing in his bed, he spoke out to her. \"Good God, Amelia!\"", "\"내가 이 세상에서 오직 그대만을 사랑한다는 걸 모른단 말이오."], ["he said, \"don't you know that I only love you in the world--you, who are a stone to me--you, whom I tended through months and months of illness and grief, and who bade me farewell with a smile on your face, and forgot me before the door shut between us!\"", "내게는 돌덩이 같은 그대를. 몇 달이고 몇 달이고 병과 슬픔에 잠긴 그대를 돌보았건만, 얼굴에 미소를 띤 채 내게 작별을 고하고는, 우리 사이에 문이 닫히기도 전에 나를 잊어버린 그대를!\""], ["The native servants lying outside his verandas beheld with wonder the Major, so cold and quiet ordinarily, at present so passionately moved and cast down.", "그의 베란다 바깥에 누워 있던 토착 하인들은, 평소에는 그토록 냉정하고 조용하던 소령이 지금은 이토록 격정에 휩싸여 풀이 죽은 모습을 보며 의아해했다. 그녀가 그를 보았더라면 가엾이 여겼을까?"], ["Would she have pitied him had she seen him?", "그는 그녀에게서 받은 모든 편지를 읽고 또 읽었다."], ["He read over and over all the letters which he ever had from her--letters of business relative to the little property which he had made her believe her husband had left to her--brief notes of invitation--every scrap of writing that she had ever sent to him--how cold, how kind, how hopeless, how selfish they were!", "남편이 그녀에게 남긴 것이라 그가 믿게 만든 자그마한 재산에 관한 사무적인 편지들, 짤막한 초대 쪽지들, 그녀가 그에게 보낸 글이라면 글자 하나하나까지. 그 편지들이 얼마나 차갑고, 얼마나 다정하고, 얼마나 가망 없고, 얼마나 이기적이던가!"]], [["Had there been some kind gentle soul near at hand who could read and appreciate this silent generous heart, who knows but that the reign of Amelia might have been over, and that friend William's love might have flowed into a kinder channel?", "이 말 없고 너그러운 마음을 읽고 헤아려 줄 따뜻하고 부드러운 영혼이 가까이 있었더라면, 어쩌면 애밀리아의 치세가 끝나고 우리 벗 윌리엄의 사랑이 더 다정한 물줄기로 흘러들었을지 누가 알겠는가?"], ["But there was only Glorvina of the jetty ringlets with whom his intercourse was familiar, and this dashing young woman was not bent upon loving the Major, but rather on making the Major admire HER--a most vain and hopeless task, too, at least considering the means that the poor girl possessed to carry it out.", "그러나 그가 허물없이 사귀는 사람이라고는 칠흑 같은 곱슬머리의 글로비나뿐이었고, 이 늠름한 젊은 여자는 소령을 사랑하려는 게 아니라 오히려 소령이 자기를 흠모하게 만들려는 데 마음을 쏟고 있었다. 그것도 더없이 헛되고 가망 없는 일이었으니, 적어도 가엾은 처녀가 그 일을 해낼 만한 수단을 가진 것으로 보아 그러했다."], ["She curled her hair and showed her shoulders at him, as much as to say, did ye ever see such jet ringlets and such a complexion?", "그녀는 머리를 곱슬곱슬 말고 그를 향해 어깨를 드러내 보였으니, 마치 이렇게 칠흑 같은 곱슬머리와 이런 살결을 본 적 있느냐고 말하는 듯했다."], ["She grinned at him so that he might see that every tooth in her head was sound--and he never heeded all these charms.", "그를 향해 활짝 웃어 보여 머릿속 이가 하나하나 다 성하다는 것을 보이려 했건만, 그는 이 모든 매력에 도무지 눈길도 주지 않았다."], ["Very soon after the arrival of the box of millinery, and perhaps indeed in honour of it, Lady O'Dowd and the ladies of the King's Regiment gave a ball to the Company's Regiments and the civilians at the station.", "모자 상자가 도착하고 얼마 지나지 않아, 아마도 정말로 그것을 기념하여, 오다우드 부인과 국왕 연대의 부인들은 주둔지의 회사 소속 연대와 문관들을 위해 무도회를 열었다."], ["Glorvina sported the killing pink frock, and the Major, who attended the party and walked very ruefully up and down the rooms, never so much as perceived the pink garment.", "글로비나는 그 사람 잡을 분홍 드레스를 뽐냈고, 무도회에 참석하여 더없이 처량하게 방 안을 오락가락하던 소령은 그 분홍 옷을 알아보기는커녕 눈길조차 주지 않았다."], ["Glorvina danced past him in a fury with all the young subalterns of the station, and the Major was not in the least jealous of her performance, or angry because Captain Bangles of the Cavalry handed her to supper.", "글로비나가 주둔지의 젊은 소위들과 어울려 약이 오른 채 그의 곁을 스치며 춤추었건만, 소령은 그녀의 춤 솜씨를 조금도 시샘하지 않았고, 기병대의 뱅글스 대위가 그녀를 야식방으로 데려간다고 화를 내지도 않았다."], ["It was not jealousy, or frocks, or shoulders that could move him, and Glorvina had nothing more.", "그를 움직일 수 있는 것은 질투도, 드레스도, 어깨도 아니었고, 글로비나에게는 그 이상 가진 것이 없었다."]], [["So these two were each exemplifying the Vanity of this life, and each longing for what he or she could not get.", "그리하여 이 두 사람은 각기 이 삶의 허영을 몸소 보여 주고 있었으니, 저마다 손에 넣을 수 없는 것을 갈망했던 것이다."], ["Glorvina cried with rage at the failure.", "글로비나는 실패에 분이 받쳐 울었다."], ["She had set her mind on the Major \"more than on any of the others,\"", "그녀는 흐느끼며, 자기가 \"다른 누구보다도\" 소령에게 마음을 두었노라고 털어놓았다."], ["she owned, sobbing. \"He'll break my heart, he will, Peggy,\"", "\"그이가 내 가슴을 찢어 놓을 거야, 정말이야, 페기.\""], ["she would whimper to her sister-in-law when they were good friends; \"sure every one of me frocks must be taken in--it's such a skeleton I'm growing.\"", "그녀는 올케와 사이가 좋을 때면 이렇게 칭얼대곤 했다. \"정말이지 내 드레스를 다 줄여야 한다니까."], ["Fat or thin, laughing or melancholy, on horseback or the music-stool, it was all the same to the Major.", "내가 점점 해골처럼 되어 가고 있단 말이야.\" 살이 찌든 빠지든, 웃든 우울하든, 말을 타든 피아노 의자에 앉든, 소령에게는 매한가지였다."], ["And the Colonel, puffing his pipe and listening to these complaints, would suggest that Glory should have some black frocks out in the next box from London, and told a mysterious story of a lady in Ireland who died of grief for the loss of her husband before she got ere a one.", "그리고 대령은 파이프를 뻐끔거리며 이런 푸념을 듣다가, 글로리는 다음번 런던에서 오는 상자에 검은 드레스나 몇 벌 받는 게 좋겠다고 권하고는, 아일랜드의 어느 부인이 남편을 잃은 슬픔으로 죽었는데 그것도 남편을 채 하나 얻기도 전이었다는 알쏭달쏭한 이야기를 들려주는 것이었다."]], [["While the Major was going on in this tantalizing way, not proposing, and declining to fall in love, there came another ship from Europe bringing letters on board, and amongst them some more for the heartless man.", "소령이 이렇게 사람 애간장 태우는 식으로, 청혼도 하지 않고 사랑에 빠지기도 거부하며 지내는 동안, 유럽에서 또 다른 배 한 척이 편지를 싣고 들어왔는데, 그중에는 이 무정한 사내에게 온 편지도 몇 통 있었다. 이것들은 앞선 꾸러미보다 더 이른 소인이 찍힌 고향 편지들이었다."], ["These were home letters bearing an earlier postmark than that of the former packets, and as Major Dobbin recognized among his the handwriting of his sister, who always crossed and recrossed her letters to her brother--gathered together all the possible bad news which she could collect, abused him and read him lectures with sisterly frankness, and always left him miserable for the day after \"dearest William\" had achieved the perusal of one of her epistles--the truth must be told that dearest William did not hurry himself to break the seal of Miss Dobbin's letter, but waited for a particularly favourable day and mood for doing so.", "도빈 소령이 그 가운데서 누이의 필체를 알아보았으니—그녀는 오라비에게 편지를 쓸 때 늘 가로로 쓰고 그 위에 다시 세로로 겹쳐 썼고, 그러모을 수 있는 온갖 나쁜 소식이란 소식은 죄다 긁어모아, 누이다운 솔직함으로 그를 나무라고 훈계를 늘어놓았으며, \"더없이 사랑하는 윌리엄\"이 그녀의 서한 한 통을 다 읽고 나면 으레 하루 종일 비참한 기분에 빠뜨려 놓곤 했다—고로, 진실을 말하자면, 더없이 사랑받는 윌리엄은 도빈 양의 편지 봉인을 서둘러 뜯지 않고, 그러기에 유달리 좋은 날과 기분을 기다린 것이었다."], ["A fortnight before, moreover, he had written to scold her for telling those absurd stories to Mrs. Osborne, and had despatched a letter in reply to that lady, undeceiving her with respect to the reports concerning him and assuring her that \"he had no sort of present intention of altering his condition.\"", "게다가 보름 전, 그는 그녀가 오스본 부인에게 그런 터무니없는 이야기를 전한 것을 꾸짖는 편지를 써 보냈고, 그 부인에게도 답장을 부쳐, 자기에 관한 소문에 대해 그 오해를 풀어 주면서 \"현재로서는 자기 처지를 바꿀 어떤 생각도 전혀 없다\"고 다짐해 둔 터였다."]], [["Two or three nights after the arrival of the second package of letters, the Major had passed the evening pretty cheerfully at Lady O'Dowd's house, where Glorvina thought that he listened with rather more attention than usual to the Meeting of the Wathers, the Minsthrel Boy, and one or two other specimens of song with which she favoured him (the truth is, he was no more listening to Glorvina than to the howling of the jackals in the moonlight outside, and the delusion was hers as usual), and having played his game at chess with her (cribbage with the surgeon was Lady O'Dowd's favourite evening pastime), Major Dobbin took leave of the Colonel's family at his usual hour and retired to his own house.", "두 번째 편지 꾸러미가 도착하고 나서 이삼 일이 지난 어느 밤, 소령은 오다우드 부인의 집에서 꽤 유쾌하게 저녁을 보냈는데, 글로비나는 그가 평소보다 좀 더 주의 깊게 자기 노래에 귀 기울인다고 여겼다. \"강물의 만남\"이며 \"음유 소년\"이며, 그 밖에 한두 곡의 노래를 그에게 들려주었던 것이다(사실인즉 그는 바깥 달빛 아래에서 자칼이 울부짖는 소리에 귀 기울이지 않는 것만큼이나 글로비나의 노래에도 귀를 기울이지 않았으니, 그 착각은 늘 그렇듯 그녀의 몫이었다). 그러고는 그녀와 체스 한 판을 둔 뒤(외과의와 하는 크리비지가 오다우드 부인이 가장 좋아하는 저녁 소일거리였다), 도빈 소령은 늘 그러던 시각에 대령 가족에게 작별을 고하고 자기 집으로 물러갔다."]], [["There on his table, his sister's letter lay reproaching him.", "거기, 그의 탁자 위에는 누이의 편지가 그를 나무라듯 놓여 있었다."], ["He took it up, ashamed rather of his negligence regarding it, and prepared himself for a disagreeable hour's communing with that crabbed-handed absent relative. .", "그는 그것을 그동안 소홀히 한 것이 다소 부끄러워 집어 들고는, 그 갈겨쓴 글씨의 멀리 있는 친지와 한 시간 남짓 불쾌한 교감을 나눌 채비를 했다……"], [". .", "소령이 대령 집을 떠나고 한 시간쯤 지났을 무렵이었으리라."], ["It may have been an hour after the Major's departure from the Colonel's house--Sir Michael was sleeping the sleep of the just; Glorvina had arranged her black ringlets in the innumerable little bits of paper, in which it was her habit to confine them; Lady O'Dowd, too, had gone to her bed in the nuptial chamber, on the ground-floor, and had tucked her musquito curtains round her fair form, when the guard at the gates of the Commanding-Officer's compound beheld Major Dobbin, in the moonlight, rushing towards the house with a swift step and a very agitated countenance, and he passed the sentinel and went up to the windows of the Colonel's bedchamber.", "마이클 경은 의인의 잠을 자고 있었고, 글로비나는 검은 곱슬머리를 평소처럼 그것을 단단히 마는 데 쓰는 수많은 자그마한 종잇조각으로 둘둘 말아 놓았으며, 오다우드 부인 역시 일층 신방의 침대에 들어 그 고운 몸뚱이에 모기장을 둘러친 참이었다. 그때 지휘관 관저 울안의 문을 지키던 경비병은 달빛 속에서 도빈 소령이 빠른 걸음에 몹시 동요한 낯빛으로 집을 향해 달려오는 모습을 보았고, 그가 보초를 지나쳐 대령 침실 창문 앞으로 다가가는 것을 보았다."]], [["\"O'Dowd--Colonel!\"", "\"오다우드—대령!\""], ["said Dobbin and kept up a great shouting.", "도빈이 외치며 큰 소리로 부르기를 그치지 않았다."]], [["\"Heavens, Meejor!\"", "\"어머나, 소령님!\""], ["said Glorvina of the curl-papers, putting out her head too, from her window.", "머리에 종이를 만 글로비나도 자기 창문에서 머리를 내밀며 말했다."]], [["\"What is it, Dob, me boy?\"", "\"무슨 일인가, 도브, 이 사람아?\""], ["said the Colonel, expecting there was a fire in the station, or that the route had come from headquarters.", "대령이 주둔지에 불이라도 났거나 본부에서 진군 명령이 내려온 줄 알고 말했다."]], [["\"I--I must have leave of absence.", "\"저는—저는 휴가를 얻어야겠습니다."], ["I must go to England--on the most urgent private affairs,\"", "영국으로 가야 해요—더없이 긴급한 사사로운 일로요.\""], ["Dobbin said.", "도빈이 말했다."]], [["\"Good heavens, what has happened!\"", "\"맙소사, 무슨 일이 일어난 거지!\""], ["thought Glorvina, trembling with all the papillotes.", "글로비나는 종이 말이를 온통 떨면서 생각했다."]], [["\"I want to be off--now--to-night,\"", "\"저는 떠나야 합니다—지금—오늘 밤에요.\""], ["Dobbin continued; and the Colonel getting up, came out to parley with him.", "도빈이 말을 이었고, 대령은 자리에서 일어나 그와 이야기를 나누러 나왔다."]], [["In the postscript of Miss Dobbin's cross-letter, the Major had just come upon a paragraph, to the following effect:--\"I drove yesterday to see your old ACQUAINTANCE, Mrs.", "도빈 양이 가로세로로 겹쳐 쓴 편지의 추신에서, 소령은 마침 다음과 같은 대목을 맞닥뜨린 참이었다. \"어제 마차를 타고 오라버니의 옛 지인인 오스본 부인을 보러 갔어요."], ["Osborne. The wretched place they live at, since they were bankrupts, you know--Mr.", "그들이 파산한 뒤로 사는 그 비참한 곳 말이에요, 아시잖아요."], ["S., to judge from a BRASS PLATE on the door of his hut (it is little better) is a coal-merchant.", "세들리 씨는, 그 오두막(별로 나을 것도 없어요) 문에 붙은 놋쇠 명패로 미루어 보건대 석탄 장수더군요."], ["The little boy, your godson, is certainly a fine child, though forward, and inclined to be saucy and self-willed.", "그 어린 사내아이, 오라버니의 대자는 분명 잘생긴 아이이긴 한데, 조숙하고 건방지고 고집스러운 구석이 있어요."], ["But we have taken notice of him as you wish it, and have introduced him to his aunt, Miss O., who was rather pleased with him.", "그래도 우리는 오라버니가 바라시는 대로 그 애를 눈여겨보아 왔고, 그 애 고모인 오스본 양에게 소개해 주었더니 고모도 그 애를 제법 마음에 들어 했어요."], ["Perhaps his grandpapa, not the bankrupt one, who is almost doting, but Mr. Osborne, of Russell Square, may be induced to relent towards the child of your friend, HIS ERRING AND SELF-WILLED SON.", "어쩌면 그 애의 할아버지가, 거의 노망이 든 그 파산한 할아버지 말고 러셀 광장의 오스본 씨 말이에요, 자기의 그릇되고 고집 센 아들의 벗인 오라버니의 그 대자를 너그러이 받아들이도록 설득될 수도 있겠지요."], ["And Amelia will not be ill-disposed to give him up.", "그리고 애밀리아도 그 애를 내주기를 그리 꺼리지 않을 거예요."], ["The widow is CONSOLED, and is about to marry a reverend gentleman, the Rev. Mr.", "그 과부는 마음을 추슬렀고, 곧 어느 목사님과 결혼한다는군요."], ["Binny, one of the curates of Brompton. A poor match.", "브롬프턴의 부목사 가운데 한 분인 비니 목사라나요."], ["But Mrs. O.", "변변찮은 혼처죠."], ["is getting old, and I saw a great deal of grey in her hair--she was in very good spirits: and your little godson overate himself at our house.", "하지만 오스본 부인도 점점 나이가 들어서, 그 머리에 흰 것이 잔뜩 보이더군요. 그래도 그녀는 아주 기분이 좋아 보였어요."], ["Mamma sends her love with that of your affectionate, Ann Dobbin.\"", "그리고 오라버니의 그 어린 대자는 우리 집에서 과식을 했답니다. 어머니가 사랑을 전하시고, 오라버니를 아끼는 누이 앤 도빈도 사랑을 전합니다.\""]], [["A Round-about Chapter between London and Hampshire\nOur old friends the Crawleys'", "우리의 옛 벗 크롤리 집안의 그레이트곤트 거리 저택은, 피트 크롤리 경의 별세를 애도하는 표시로 내걸린 문장(紋章)을 아직도 정면에 달고 있었다."], ["family house, in Great Gaunt Street, still bore over its front the hatchment which had been placed there as a token of mourning for Sir Pitt Crawley's demise, yet this heraldic emblem was in itself a very splendid and gaudy piece of furniture, and all the rest of the mansion became more brilliant than it had ever been during the late baronet's reign.", "그러나 이 문장 장식 자체가 실은 더없이 화려하고 요란한 장식물이었으니, 저택의 나머지 부분도 작고한 준남작이 다스리던 시절보다 한층 더 눈부시게 단장되어 갔다. 벽돌을 뒤덮고 있던 검은 외피가 벗겨지니, 벽돌들은 흰 줄무늬가 섞인 발그레하고 명랑한 얼굴을 드러냈다."], ["The black outer-coating of the bricks was removed, and they appeared with a cheerful, blushing face streaked with white: the old bronze lions of the knocker were gilt handsomely, the railings painted, and the dismallest house in Great Gaunt Street became the smartest in the whole quarter, before the green leaves in Hampshire had replaced those yellowing ones which were on the trees in Queen's Crawley Avenue when old Sir Pitt Crawley passed under them for the last time.", "문고리에 달린 낡은 청동 사자들은 보기 좋게 도금되었고, 난간은 새로 칠해졌다. 그리하여 그레이트곤트 거리에서 가장 음울하던 집은, 늙은 피트 크롤리 경이 마지막으로 그 아래를 지나갈 때 퀸스크롤리 가로수 길의 나무들에 매달려 있던 누런 잎들이 햄프셔의 푸른 잎으로 갈리기도 전에, 그 일대에서 가장 말쑥한 집이 되어 있었다."]], [["A little woman, with a carriage to correspond, was perpetually seen about this mansion; an elderly spinster, accompanied by a little boy, also might be remarked coming thither daily.", "이 저택 주위에는 거기에 걸맞은 마차를 거느린 자그마한 여인 하나가 끊임없이 모습을 드러냈다. 또한 어린 사내아이를 데리고 날마다 그곳을 찾아오는 나이 지긋한 노처녀의 모습도 눈에 띄곤 했다."], ["It was Miss Briggs and little Rawdon, whose business it was to see to the inward renovation of Sir Pitt's house, to superintend the female band engaged in stitching the blinds and hangings, to poke and rummage in the drawers and cupboards crammed with the dirty relics and congregated trumperies of a couple of generations of Lady Crawleys, and to take inventories of the china, the glass, and other properties in the closets and store-rooms.", "브리그스 양과 어린 로던이었다. 이들이 맡은 일이란 피트 경 저택의 내부 수리를 챙기는 것, 블라인드와 휘장을 꿰매는 여자 일꾼들을 감독하는 것, 두 세대에 걸친 크롤리 부인들의 더러운 유물과 한데 쌓인 잡동사니로 빼곡한 서랍과 찬장을 쑤시고 뒤지는 것, 그리고 벽장과 광에 든 도자기며 유리그릇이며 그 밖의 살림살이를 일일이 목록으로 작성하는 것이었다."]], [["Mrs. Rawdon Crawley was general-in-chief over these arrangements, with full orders from Sir Pitt to sell, barter, confiscate, or purchase furniture, and she enjoyed herself not a little in an occupation which gave full scope to her taste and ingenuity.", "로던 크롤리 부인은 이 모든 정비 작업의 총사령관이었으니, 피트 경으로부터 가구를 팔고, 바꾸고, 압류하고, 사들이라는 전권을 위임받은 터였다."], ["The renovation of the house was determined upon when Sir Pitt came to town in November to see his lawyers, and when he passed nearly a week in Curzon Street, under the roof of his affectionate brother and sister.", "그녀는 자신의 안목과 재간을 마음껏 펼칠 수 있는 이 일에 적잖이 즐거워했다. 저택을 새로 단장하기로 결정된 것은, 피트 경이 십일월에 변호사들을 만나러 상경하여, 애정 깊은 아우 부부의 지붕 아래 커즌 거리에서 거의 일주일을 머물던 무렵이었다."]], [["He had put up at an hotel at first, but, Becky, as soon as she heard of the Baronet's arrival, went off alone to greet him, and returned in an hour to Curzon Street with Sir Pitt in the carriage by her side.", "피트 경은 처음에는 호텔에 묵었으나, 베키는 준남작이 도착했다는 소식을 듣자마자 혼자서 그를 맞으러 달려갔고, 한 시간 뒤에는 피트 경을 마차에 나란히 태우고 커즌 거리로 돌아왔다."], ["It was impossible sometimes to resist this artless little creature's hospitalities, so kindly were they pressed, so frankly and amiably offered.", "이 천진난만한 작은 인물의 환대란, 어찌나 다정하게 권하고 또 어찌나 솔직하고 살갑게 베풀어지는지 도무지 뿌리칠 수가 없을 때가 더러 있는 법이었다."], ["Becky seized Pitt's hand in a transport of gratitude when he agreed to come.", "피트가 오겠노라 응낙하자, 베키는 감격에 겨워 그의 손을 덥석 잡았다."], ["\"Thank you,\"", "\"고마워요.\""], ["she said, squeezing it and looking into the Baronet's eyes, who blushed a good deal; \"how happy this will make Rawdon!\"", "그녀는 그 손을 꼭 쥐고는, 얼굴이 벌게진 준남작의 눈을 들여다보며 말했다. \"로던이 얼마나 기뻐할까요!\""], ["She bustled up to Pitt's bedroom, leading on the servants, who were carrying his trunks thither.", "그녀는 피트의 침실로 종종걸음 쳐 올라가며, 그의 트렁크를 그리로 나르는 하인들을 앞장서서 이끌었다."], ["She came in herself laughing, with a coal-scuttle out of her own room.", "이윽고 그녀 자신이 자기 방에서 석탄 통을 들고 웃으며 들어왔다."]], [["A fire was blazing already in Sir Pitt's apartment (it was Miss Briggs's room, by the way, who was sent upstairs to sleep with the maid).", "피트 경의 방에는 이미 불이 활활 타오르고 있었다(덧붙이자면, 그 방은 본래 브리그스 양의 방으로, 브리그스 양은 위층으로 보내져 하녀와 함께 자게 되었다)."], ["\"I knew I should bring you,\"", "\"내가 기어이 모시고 올 줄 알았다니까요.\""], ["she said with pleasure beaming in her glance.", "그녀가 두 눈에 기쁨을 반짝이며 말했다."], ["Indeed, she was really sincerely happy at having him for a guest.", "사실 그녀는 그를 손님으로 맞은 것이 진심으로 기뻤던 것이다."]], [["Becky made Rawdon dine out once or twice on business, while Pitt stayed with them, and the Baronet passed the happy evening alone with her and Briggs.", "피트가 머무는 동안, 베키는 로던에게 한두 번 볼일을 핑계로 밖에서 저녁을 들게 했고, 그리하여 준남작은 그녀와 브리그스와 더불어 단란한 저녁을 보냈다."], ["She went downstairs to the kitchen and actually cooked little dishes for him.", "그녀는 손수 부엌으로 내려가 정말로 그를 위해 자잘한 요리를 만들었다. \"이 살미* 맛있지 않아요?\""], ["\"Isn't it a good salmi?\"", "그녀가 말했다."], ["she said; \"I made it for you.", "\"아주버님 드리려고 만든 거예요."], ["I can make you better dishes than that, and will when you come to see me.\"", "이보다 더 맛난 것도 해 드릴 수 있어요, 저를 보러 오시면 꼭 해 드릴게요.\""]], [["\"Everything you do, you do well,\"", "\"자네가 하는 일은 무엇이든 다 훌륭해.\""], ["said the Baronet gallantly.", "준남작이 정중하게 말했다."], ["\"The salmi is excellent indeed.\"", "\"이 살미도 정말이지 일품이로군.\""]], [["\"A poor man's wife,\"", "\"가난한 사내의 아내란,\" 리베카가 명랑하게 받았다."], ["Rebecca replied gaily, \"must make herself useful, you know\"; on which her brother-in-law vowed that \"she was fit to be the wife of an Emperor, and that to be skilful in domestic duties was surely one of the most charming of woman's qualities.\"", "\"쓸모 있는 사람이 되어야 하잖아요.\" 그러자 시아주버니는 \"자네는 황제의 아내가 되어도 손색이 없네, 살림 솜씨가 능란하다는 것이야말로 여인의 가장 매혹적인 덕목 가운데 하나일 게 분명하지\"라고 단언했다."], ["And Sir Pitt thought, with something like mortification, of Lady Jane at home, and of a certain pie which she had insisted on making, and serving to him at dinner--a most abominable pie.", "그러면서 피트 경은 집에 있는 제인 부인을 떠올렸으니, 어딘가 분해지는 심정이었다. 그녀가 기어이 만들겠다고 우겨 저녁 식탁에 올렸던 어떤 파이, 그 더없이 끔찍한 파이를 떠올린 것이었다."]], [["Besides the salmi, which was made of Lord Steyne's pheasants from his lordship's cottage of Stillbrook, Becky gave her brother-in-law a bottle of white wine, some that Rawdon had brought with him from France, and had picked up for nothing, the little story-teller said; whereas the liquor was, in truth, some White Hermitage from the Marquis of Steyne's famous cellars, which brought fire into the Baronet's pallid cheeks and a glow into his feeble frame.", "살미 — 그것은 스테인 경이 자신의 스틸브룩 별장에서 보내온 꿩으로 만든 것이었다 — 말고도, 베키는 시아주버니에게 백포도주 한 병을 내놓았다. 로던이 프랑스에서 가져온 것으로, 거저나 다름없이 손에 넣은 것이라고 이 작은 이야기꾼은 둘러댔지만, 사실 그 술은 스테인 후작의 이름난 술 창고에서 나온 화이트 에르미타주였다. 그것이 준남작의 창백한 두 뺨에 불을 지피고 그 쇠약한 몸뚱이에 화색을 돌게 했다."]], [["Then when he had drunk up the bottle of petit vin blanc, she gave him her hand, and took him up to the drawing-room, and made him snug on the sofa by the fire, and let him talk as she listened with the tenderest kindly interest, sitting by him, and hemming a shirt for her dear little boy.", "이윽고 그가 그 백포도주 한 병을 다 비우자, 그녀는 그에게 손을 내밀어 응접실로 데려가서는 난롯가 소파에 아늑하게 자리 잡게 하고, 그가 이야기하도록 내버려 둔 채 더없이 자상하고 다정한 관심으로 귀 기울이며 그의 곁에 앉아, 사랑하는 어린 아들을 위해 셔츠 한 벌을 감치고 있었다."], ["Whenever Mrs. Rawdon wished to be particularly humble and virtuous, this little shirt used to come out of her work-box.", "로던 부인이 유독 겸손하고 정숙한 모습을 보이고 싶을 때면, 이 자그마한 셔츠가 으레 반짇고리에서 나오곤 했다."], ["It had got to be too small for Rawdon long before it was finished.", "그것은 다 짓기도 전에 진작에 로던에게는 너무 작아져 있었다."]], [["Well, Rebecca listened to Pitt, she talked to him, she sang to him, she coaxed him, and cuddled him, so that he found himself more and more glad every day to get back from the lawyer's at Gray's Inn, to the blazing fire in Curzon Street--a gladness in which the men of law likewise participated, for Pitt's harangues were of the longest--and so that when he went away he felt quite a pang at departing.", "자, 리베카는 피트의 이야기에 귀 기울였고, 그에게 말을 건넸고, 그에게 노래를 불러 주었고, 그를 어르고 보듬었다. 그리하여 피트는 그레이스 인의 변호사 사무실에서 커즌 거리의 활활 타는 난롯가로 돌아오는 일이 날이 갈수록 점점 더 즐거워졌다."], ["How pretty she looked kissing her hand to him from the carriage and waving her handkerchief when he had taken his place in the mail!", "이 즐거움은 법률가들도 함께 누리는 것이었으니, 피트의 일장 연설이란 더없이 긴 축에 들었기 때문이다. 그리하여 막상 떠날 때가 되자 그는 작별이 사뭇 가슴 아렸다."], ["She put the handkerchief to her eyes once.", "우편 마차에 자리를 잡고 앉은 그를 향해, 그녀가 마차에서 손에 입 맞추어 보이고 손수건을 흔들던 그 모습이 어찌나 어여뻤던지! 그녀는 한 번 손수건을 눈가에 갖다 댔다."], ["He pulled his sealskin cap over his, as the coach drove away, and, sinking back, he thought to himself how she respected him and how he deserved it, and how Rawdon was a foolish dull fellow who didn't half appreciate his wife; and how mum and stupid his own wife was compared to that brilliant little Becky.", "마차가 떠나가자 그는 물개 가죽 모자를 눈 위까지 푹 눌러쓰고는, 등받이에 몸을 묻은 채 속으로 생각했다. 그녀가 나를 얼마나 존경하는지, 또 내가 그 존경을 받아 마땅한 사람인지, 로던이란 제 아내의 값어치를 절반도 못 알아보는 어리석고 둔한 사내라는 것을, 또 저 영민하고 작은 베키에 견주면 제 아내가 얼마나 말없고 멍청한 여자인지를 말이다."], ["Becky had hinted every one of these things herself, perhaps, but so delicately and gently that you hardly knew when or where.", "어쩌면 이 모든 생각을 베키가 하나하나 넌지시 일러 준 것이었는지도 모른다. 다만 어찌나 섬세하고 부드럽게 흘렸던지 언제 어디서 그랬는지조차 알기 어려웠을 따름이다."], ["And, before they parted, it was agreed that the house in London should be redecorated for the next season, and that the brothers' families should meet again in the country at Christmas.", "그리고 헤어지기 전에 두 사람은, 다음 사교철을 위해 런던 저택을 새로 단장하기로, 또 두 형제의 가족이 성탄절에 시골에서 다시 만나기로 합의를 보았다."]], [["\"I wish you could have got a little money out of him,\"", "\"당신이 그 양반한테서 돈푼이라도 좀 우려냈으면 좋았을 텐데.\""], ["Rawdon said to his wife moodily when the Baronet was gone.", "준남작이 떠난 뒤 로던이 침울하게 아내에게 말했다."], ["\"I should like to give something to old Raggles, hanged if I shouldn't.", "\"라글스 영감한테 뭐라도 좀 쥐여 주고 싶단 말이오, 정말이지 안 그러면 사람이 아니지."], ["It ain't right, you know, that the old fellow should be kept out of all his money.", "그 영감이 받을 돈을 못 받고 있는 건 옳지 않은 일이오, 알잖소."], ["It may be inconvenient, and he might let to somebody else besides us, you know.\"", "불편한 노릇일 테고, 또 우리 말고 딴 사람한테 세를 줘 버릴 수도 있는 거요, 알다시피.\""]], [["\"Tell him,\"", "\"그 사람한테 이렇게 말해요.\""], ["said Becky, \"that as soon as Sir Pitt's affairs are settled, everybody will be paid, and give him a little something on account.", "베키가 말했다. \"피트 경의 재산 문제가 정리되는 대로 모두에게 빚을 갚을 거라고요."], ["Here's a cheque that Pitt left for the boy,\"", "그리고 우선 갚는 셈으로 얼마쯤 쥐여 줘요. 여기 피트가 아이 앞으로 두고 간 수표가 있어요.\""], ["and she took from her bag and gave her husband a paper which his brother had handed over to her, on behalf of the little son and heir of the younger branch of the Crawleys.", "그러면서 그녀는 가방에서 종이 한 장을 꺼내 남편에게 건넸다. 시아주버니가 크롤리 가문 작은집의 어린 아들이자 상속자를 위해 그녀에게 넘겨준 것이었다."]], [["The truth is, she had tried personally the ground on which her husband expressed a wish that she should venture--tried it ever so delicately, and found it unsafe.", "사실 그녀는 남편이 한번 부딪쳐 보라고 바랐던 그 일을, 손수 떠보았던 것이다. 더없이 조심스럽게 떠보았으나, 안전하지 않다는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["Even at a hint about embarrassments, Sir Pitt Crawley was off and alarmed.", "곤궁하다는 기색을 슬쩍 비치기만 해도 피트 크롤리 경은 화들짝 놀라 꽁무니를 뺐다."], ["And he began a long speech, explaining how straitened he himself was in money matters; how the tenants would not pay; how his father's affairs, and the expenses attendant upon the demise of the old gentleman, had involved him; how he wanted to pay off incumbrances; and how the bankers and agents were overdrawn; and Pitt Crawley ended by making a compromise with his sister-in-law and giving her a very small sum for the benefit of her little boy.", "그는 일장 연설을 늘어놓기 시작했으니, 자기야말로 돈 문제로 얼마나 쪼들리는지, 소작인들이 어찌나 돈을 안 내는지, 부친의 재산 문제와 노인의 별세에 따른 비용 탓에 어떻게 얽매여 있는지, 저당 잡힌 빚을 갚아야 한다는 것, 은행과 대리인들에게 진 빚이 한도를 넘어섰다는 것 따위를 설명했다. 그리하여 피트 크롤리는 결국 제수와 적당히 타협하여, 그녀의 어린 아들을 위해 아주 적은 액수를 쥐여 주는 것으로 마무리 지었다."]], [["Pitt knew how poor his brother and his brother's family must be.", "피트는 제 아우와 그 가족이 얼마나 가난한지 알고 있었다."], ["It could not have escaped the notice of such a cool and experienced old diplomatist that Rawdon's family had nothing to live upon, and that houses and carriages are not to be kept for nothing.", "로던네 식구가 살아갈 거리라고는 아무것도 없다는 것, 그리고 집과 마차란 거저 굴러가는 게 아니라는 것이, 이토록 냉철하고 노련한 늙은 외교가의 눈을 피해 갈 리 없었다."], ["He knew very well that he was the proprietor or appropriator of the money, which, according to all proper calculation, ought to have fallen to his younger brother, and he had, we may be sure, some secret pangs of remorse within him, which warned him that he ought to perform some act of justice, or, let us say, compensation, towards these disappointed relations.", "그는 자기가 모든 정당한 셈법대로라면 아우에게 돌아갔어야 할 돈을 차지한, 아니 가로챈 자라는 것을 잘 알고 있었다. 그러니 그의 가슴속에는 — 우리는 이를 확신해도 좋으리라 — 어떤 은밀한 양심의 가책이 있었으니, 이 낙담한 친지들에게 무언가 정의로운 행위를, 아니 보상이라 해 두자, 그런 것을 베풀어야 한다고 그를 일깨우는 가책이었다."], ["A just, decent man, not without brains, who said his prayers, and knew his catechism, and did his duty outwardly through life, he could not be otherwise than aware that something was due to his brother at his hands, and that morally he was Rawdon's debtor.", "정의롭고 점잖으며 머리도 제법 돌아가는 사람, 기도를 올리고 교리문답을 외우며 평생 겉으로는 제 도리를 다해 온 사람인 그로서는, 제 손으로 아우에게 무언가 갚을 것이 있다는 것, 도덕적으로 자신이 로던에게 빚진 자라는 것을 모를 수가 없었던 것이다."]], [["But, as one reads in the columns of the Times newspaper every now and then, queer announcements from the Chancellor of the Exchequer, acknowledging the receipt of 50 pounds from A.", "그러나 이따금 타임스 신문 지면에서 읽게 되는, 재무장관이 내는 그 기묘한 공고를 보라."], ["B., or 10 pounds from W.", "A."], ["T., as conscience-money, on account of taxes due by the said A. B.", "B.에게서 50파운드를, 혹은 W."], ["or W. T., which payments the penitents beg the Right Honourable gentleman to acknowledge through the medium of the public press--so is the Chancellor no doubt, and the reader likewise, always perfectly sure that the above-named A.", "T.에게서 10파운드를 양심 부담금으로 받았음을, 그 A."], ["B. and W.", "B.나 W."], ["T. are only paying a very small instalment of what they really owe, and that the man who sends up a twenty-pound note has very likely hundreds or thousands more for which he ought to account.", "T.가 마땅히 내야 할 세금조로 받았음을 알리는 공고 말이다."], ["Such, at least, are my feelings, when I see A. B.", "그 참회자들은 자기네가 낸 돈을 존경하는 장관께서 공공 언론을 통해 확인해 주기를 청하는 것이다."], ["or W. T.'s insufficient acts of repentance.", "그런데 재무장관도, 또한 독자도, 의심할 바 없이 늘 확신하기를, 앞서 말한 A."], ["And I have no doubt that Pitt Crawley's contrition, or kindness if you will, towards his younger brother, by whom he had so much profited, was only a very small dividend upon the capital sum in which he was indebted to Rawdon.", "B.와 W. T.는 실제로 빚진 액수 가운데 아주 적은 할부금 한 몫을 치르는 것일 뿐이며, 20파운드 지폐 한 장을 부쳐 오는 자는 십중팔구 마땅히 셈해야 할 돈을 수백, 수천 파운드는 더 가지고 있으리라는 것이다."], ["Not everybody is willing to pay even so much.", "적어도 A. B.나 W."], ["To part with money is a sacrifice beyond almost all men endowed with a sense of order.", "T.의 그 미진한 참회 행위를 볼 때 내 심정은 그러하다. 그러니 피트 크롤리가 그토록 많은 이득을 본 아우를 향해 보인 그 뉘우침, 아니 호의라 해도 좋으나, 그것 역시 로던에게 진 빚의 원금에 대한 아주 작은 배당금 한 몫에 지나지 않았으리라는 것을 나는 의심치 않는다."], ["There is scarcely any man alive who does not think himself meritorious for giving his neighbour five pounds.", "그나마 그만큼이라도 기꺼이 내놓는 이가 흔치 않다. 돈을 내놓는다는 것은, 질서 감각을 타고난 사람이라면 거의 누구에게나 감당하기 어려운 희생인 것이다."], ["Thriftless gives, not from a beneficent pleasure in giving, but from a lazy delight in spending.", "이웃에게 5파운드를 베풀면서 제가 갸륵한 일을 했다고 여기지 않을 사람이란 세상에 거의 없다. 헤픈 자는 베푸는 자선의 기쁨에서가 아니라 흥청대며 쓰는 게으른 쾌락에서 돈을 내놓는다."], ["He would not deny himself one enjoyment; not his opera-stall, not his horse, not his dinner, not even the pleasure of giving Lazarus the five pounds.", "그는 자기 즐거움이라면 단 하나도 마다하지 않는다. 오페라 특별석도, 말도, 만찬도, 심지어 라자로에게 5파운드를 베푸는 그 즐거움조차 마다하지 않는다."], ["Thrifty, who is good, wise, just, and owes no man a penny, turns from a beggar, haggles with a hackney-coachman, or denies a poor relation, and I doubt which is the most selfish of the two.", "반면 아끼는 자는 선량하고 현명하고 정의로우며 누구에게도 단 한 푼 빚지지 않건만, 거지를 외면하고, 삯마차꾼과 흥정하며, 가난한 친척을 매정하게 내친다. 그러니 둘 중 어느 쪽이 더 이기적인지 나는 분간하기 어렵다."], ["Money has only a different value in the eyes of each.", "돈이란 그저 저마다의 눈에 다른 값어치를 지닐 뿐이다."]], [["So, in a word, Pitt Crawley thought he would do something for his brother, and then thought that he would think about it some other time.", "그리하여 한마디로, 피트 크롤리는 아우를 위해 무언가 해 주어야겠다고 생각했고, 이내 그 일은 언젠가 다른 때 다시 생각해 보자고 마음먹었다."]], [["And with regard to Becky, she was not a woman who expected too much from the generosity of her neighbours, and so was quite content with all that Pitt Crawley had done for her.", "베키로 말할 것 같으면, 이웃의 관대함에 너무 많은 것을 기대하는 여자가 아니었으니, 피트 크롤리가 그녀를 위해 해 준 일에 자못 만족했다. 그녀는 가문의 수장으로부터 인정을 받은 셈이었다."], ["She was acknowledged by the head of the family.", "설령 피트가 그녀에게 아무것도 내주지 않는다 해도, 언젠가는 그녀를 위해 무언가 마련해 줄 터였다."], ["If Pitt would not give her anything, he would get something for her some day.", "시아주버니에게서 돈을 못 받았다 한들, 그녀는 돈이나 다름없는 것, 곧 신용을 얻었다."], ["If she got no money from her brother-in-law, she got what was as good as money--credit.", "라글스는 두 형제가 화목하게 지내는 광경에다 그 자리에서 받은 약간의 돈, 그리고 머지않아 훨씬 큰 액수를 떼어 주겠다는 약속에 마음이 제법 놓였다. 그리고 리베카는 브리그스 양에게 — 그녀가 빌려준 자그마한 돈에 대한 성탄절 배당금을, 베키는 마치 제 금고가 황금으로 넘쳐흐르기라도 하는 양 거리낌 없이 기쁜 얼굴로 치러 주었거니와 — 그러니까 리베카는 브리그스 양에게 은밀히 털어놓기를, 재정 전문가로 이름난 피트 경과 다른 누구도 아닌 브리그스를 위해 의논을 했노라고, 브리그스 양에게 남은 자본을 어떻게 가장 수익성 있게 굴릴지 상의했노라고 했다."], ["Raggles was made rather easy in his mind by the spectacle of the union between the brothers, by a small payment on the spot, and by the promise of a much larger sum speedily to be assigned to him.", "피트 경이 곰곰이 궁리한 끝에, 브리그스가 돈을 굴릴 더없이 안전하고 유리한 길을 생각해 냈다는 것이었다. 작고한 크롤리 양과 온 집안에 정성을 다한 벗인 그녀를 특별히 마음에 두고 있는 데다, 런던을 떠나기 한참 전에 이미 권하기를, 피트 경이 눈여겨보아 둔 주식을 가장 유리한 기회에 사들일 수 있도록 언제든 즉시 돈을 내놓을 채비를 해 두라 했다는 것이다."], ["And Rebecca told Miss Briggs, whose Christmas dividend upon the little sum lent by her Becky paid with an air of candid joy, and as if her exchequer was brimming over with gold--Rebecca, we say, told Miss Briggs, in strict confidence that she had conferred with Sir Pitt, who was famous as a financier, on Briggs's special behalf, as to the most profitable investment of Miss B.'s remaining capital; that Sir Pitt, after much consideration, had thought of a most safe and advantageous way in which Briggs could lay out her money; that, being especially interested in her as an attached friend of the late Miss Crawley, and of the whole family, and that long before he left town, he had recommended that she should be ready with the money at a moment's notice, so as to purchase at the most favourable opportunity the shares which Sir Pitt had in his eye.", "가엾은 브리그스 양은 피트 경의 이런 배려에 무척이나 고마워했다. 청하지도 않았는데 베풀어 준 것이라며, 자기로서는 공채에서 돈을 빼낼 생각은 꿈에도 못 했을 거라고 했다."], ["Poor Miss Briggs was very grateful for this mark of Sir Pitt's attention--it came so unsolicited, she said, for she never should have thought of removing the money from the funds--and the delicacy enhanced the kindness of the office; and she promised to see her man of business immediately and be ready with her little cash at the proper hour.", "그 섬세한 마음씀씀이가 호의를 한층 돋보이게 했다. 그녀는 곧장 자기 거래 담당자를 만나 적당한 때에 그 자그마한 현금을 내놓을 채비를 해 두겠노라 약속했다."]], [["And this worthy woman was so grateful for the kindness of Rebecca in the matter, and for that of her generous benefactor, the Colonel, that she went out and spent a great part of her half-year's dividend in the purchase of a black velvet coat for little Rawdon, who, by the way, was grown almost too big for black velvet now, and was of a size and age befitting him for the assumption of the virile jacket and pantaloons.", "이 갸륵한 여인은 이 일에서 베풀어진 리베카의 친절과, 그 너그러운 은인인 대령의 친절에 어찌나 고마워했던지, 밖에 나가 반년치 배당금의 상당 부분을 털어 어린 로던에게 줄 검정 벨벳 외투를 사 왔다. 그런데 덧붙이자면, 그 아이는 이제 검정 벨벳을 입기에는 몸집이 거의 너무 커져, 사내다운 재킷과 바지를 입혀도 좋을 만한 키와 나이에 이르러 있었다."]], [["He was a fine open-faced boy, with blue eyes and waving flaxen hair, sturdy in limb, but generous and soft in heart, fondly attaching himself to all who were good to him--to the pony--to Lord Southdown, who gave him the horse (he used to blush and glow all over when he saw that kind young nobleman)--to the groom who had charge of the pony--to Molly, the cook, who crammed him with ghost stories at night, and with good things from the dinner--to Briggs, whom he plagued and laughed at--and to his father especially, whose attachment towards the lad was curious too to witness.", "아이는 얼굴이 훤하고 솔직한 사내아이로, 파란 눈에 물결치는 아마빛 머리를 하고, 팔다리는 단단했으나 마음씨는 너그럽고 여렸으며, 자기에게 잘해 주는 이라면 누구에게나 살갑게 정을 붙였다. 조랑말에게도, 그 말을 준 사우스다운 경에게도(그 다정한 젊은 귀족을 보면 아이는 온몸이 발그레 달아오르곤 했다), 조랑말을 돌보는 마부에게도, 밤이면 유령 이야기와 식탁에 오른 맛난 것들을 잔뜩 안겨 주는 요리사 몰리에게도, 늘 놀려 대고 비웃던 브리그스에게도, 그리고 특히 제 아버지에게도 그러했으니, 그 사내아이를 향한 아버지의 애정 또한 보기에 흥미로운 것이었다."], ["Here, as he grew to be about eight years old, his attachments may be said to have ended.", "아이가 여덟 살쯤 자랄 무렵, 그가 정을 붙인 대상은 여기까지였다고 해도 좋으리라."], ["The beautiful mother-vision had faded away after a while.", "어머니라는 아름다운 환영은 얼마 못 가 빛이 바래 버렸다."], ["During near two years she had scarcely spoken to the child.", "거의 두 해 가까이 그녀는 아이에게 거의 말을 건네지 않았다."], ["She disliked him.", "그녀는 아이를 싫어했다."], ["He had the measles and the hooping-cough.", "아이는 홍역에 걸렸고 백일해도 앓았다."], ["He bored her.", "아이는 그녀를 따분하게 했다."], ["One day when he was standing at the landing-place, having crept down from the upper regions, attracted by the sound of his mother's voice, who was singing to Lord Steyne, the drawing room door opening suddenly, discovered the little spy, who but a moment before had been rapt in delight, and listening to the music.", "어느 날 아이가 어머니의 목소리에 이끌려 위층에서 몰래 기어 내려와 층계참에 서 있었으니, 어머니가 스테인 경에게 노래를 불러 주던 참이었다. 그때 응접실 문이 별안간 열리며, 바로 조금 전까지 황홀경에 잠겨 그 노랫소리에 귀 기울이던 이 작은 엿보는 아이가 들통이 났다."]], [["His mother came out and struck him violently a couple of boxes on the ear.", "어머니가 나오더니 아이의 귀싸대기를 사납게 두어 대 후려쳤다."], ["He heard a laugh from the Marquis in the inner room (who was amused by this free and artless exhibition of Becky's temper) and fled down below to his friends of the kitchen, bursting in an agony of grief.", "아이는 안쪽 방에서 후작이 터뜨리는 웃음소리를 들었고(후작은 거리낌 없이 천연덕스럽게 드러난 베키의 성깔에 재미있어했다), 슬픔에 겨워 울음을 터뜨리며 아래층 부엌의 제 벗들에게로 달아났다."]], [["\"It is not because it hurts me,\"", "\"아파서 그러는 게 아니에요.\" 어린 로던이 헐떡이며 말했다."], ["little Rawdon gasped out--\"only--only\"--sobs and tears wound up the sentence in a storm.", "\"다만, 다만……\" 그러고는 흐느낌과 눈물이 한바탕 휘몰아치며 말끝을 삼켜 버렸다."], ["It was the little boy's heart that was bleeding.", "피 흘리는 것은 이 어린 사내아이의 마음이었다."], ["\"Why mayn't I hear her singing?", "\"왜 나는 엄마 노래를 들으면 안 되는 거예요?"], ["Why don't she ever sing to me--as she does to that baldheaded man with the large teeth?\"", "왜 엄마는 나한텐 한 번도 노래를 안 불러 줘요, 이빨 큰 그 대머리 아저씨한테는 불러 주면서?\""], ["He gasped out at various intervals these exclamations of rage and grief.", "아이는 분노와 슬픔에 찬 이 외침들을 띄엄띄엄 토해 냈다."], ["The cook looked at the housemaid, the housemaid looked knowingly at the footman--the awful kitchen inquisition which sits in judgement in every house and knows everything--sat on Rebecca at that moment.", "요리사는 하녀를 쳐다보았고, 하녀는 하인을 의미심장하게 쳐다보았다. 집집마다 자리 잡고 앉아 심판을 내리며 모든 것을 다 아는, 저 무시무시한 부엌의 종교재판소가 그 순간 리베카를 판결대에 올린 것이었다."]], [["After this incident, the mother's dislike increased to hatred; the consciousness that the child was in the house was a reproach and a pain to her.", "이 일이 있은 뒤로 어머니의 미움은 증오로 커져 갔다. 아이가 집 안에 있다는 의식 자체가 그녀에게는 책망이요 고통이었다."], ["His very sight annoyed her.", "그 모습만 보아도 짜증이 났다."], ["Fear, doubt, and resistance sprang up, too, in the boy's own bosom.", "한편 아이의 가슴속에도 두려움과 의심과 반항심이 솟아났다."], ["They were separated from that day of the boxes on the ear.", "귀싸대기를 맞던 그날 이후, 두 사람은 완전히 갈라서고 말았다."]], [["Lord Steyne also heartily disliked the boy.", "스테인 경 또한 그 사내아이를 진심으로 싫어했다."], ["When they met by mischance, he made sarcastic bows or remarks to the child, or glared at him with savage-looking eyes.", "어쩌다 마주치면 그는 아이에게 빈정대듯 인사를 하거나 말을 던졌고, 사납게 번뜩이는 눈으로 아이를 노려보았다."], ["Rawdon used to stare him in the face and double his little fists in return.", "로던은 그럴 때마다 그의 얼굴을 마주 노려보며 자그마한 두 주먹을 불끈 쥐어 보였다."], ["He knew his enemy, and this gentleman, of all who came to the house, was the one who angered him most.", "아이는 제 원수를 알아본 것이었으니, 이 집을 드나드는 모든 이들 가운데 아이를 가장 화나게 하는 이가 바로 그 신사였다."], ["One day the footman found him squaring his fists at Lord Steyne's hat in the hall.", "어느 날 하인이, 아이가 현관에서 스테인 경의 모자를 향해 주먹을 들이대고 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["The footman told the circumstance as a good joke to Lord Steyne's coachman; that officer imparted it to Lord Steyne's gentleman, and to the servants' hall in general.", "하인은 그 일을 우스운 농담거리 삼아 스테인 경의 마부에게 일러바쳤고, 그 마부는 다시 스테인 경의 시종에게, 또 하인방 전체에 두루 퍼뜨렸다."], ["And very soon afterwards, when Mrs. Rawdon Crawley made her appearance at Gaunt House, the porter who unbarred the gates, the servants of all uniforms in the hall, the functionaries in white waistcoats, who bawled out from landing to landing the names of Colonel and Mrs.", "그리고 머지않아, 로던 크롤리 부인이 곤트 하우스에 모습을 드러내자, 빗장을 풀어 문을 열어 주는 문지기며, 현관에 늘어선 갖가지 제복 차림의 하인들이며, 층계참마다 로던 크롤리 대령 부부의 이름을 외쳐 대는 흰 조끼 차림의 종복들이, 죄다 그녀에 대한 일을 알고 있거나, 알고 있다고 여겼다."], ["Rawdon Crawley, knew about her, or fancied they did. The man who brought her refreshment and stood behind her chair, had talked her character over with the large gentleman in motley-coloured clothes at his side.", "그녀에게 다과를 가져다주고 그 의자 뒤에 서 있던 사내는, 곁에 선 알록달록한 옷차림의 덩치 큰 사내와 더불어 그녀의 됨됨이를 입에 올린 적이 있었던 것이다."], ["Bon Dieu!", "맙소사!"], ["it is awful, that servants' inquisition!", "끔찍한 노릇이다, 저 하인들의 종교재판소란!"], ["You see a woman in a great party in a splendid saloon, surrounded by faithful admirers, distributing sparkling glances, dressed to perfection, curled, rouged, smiling and happy--Discovery walks respectfully up to her, in the shape of a huge powdered man with large calves and a tray of ices--with Calumny (which is as fatal as truth) behind him, in the shape of the hulking fellow carrying the wafer-biscuits.", "어느 화려한 살롱의 성대한 연회에서, 충실한 흠모자들에 둘러싸여 반짝이는 눈길을 흩뿌리며, 더없이 완벽하게 차려입고, 머리를 곱슬곱슬 말고, 연지를 바르고, 미소 짓는 행복한 한 여인을 보라. 그때 정체의 폭로가 그녀에게 공손히 다가오니, 그것은 종아리가 굵고 얼음과자 쟁반을 든 분 바른 거구의 사내 형상이다."], ["Madam, your secret will be talked over by those men at their club at the public-house to-night.", "그 뒤로는 비방이(이것은 진실만큼이나 치명적인 것이다) 따라오니, 곧 살구씨 비스킷을 나르는 그 우람한 녀석의 형상이다. 부인이여, 당신의 비밀은 오늘 밤 저 사내들이 술집의 자기네 모임에서 입방아에 올릴 것이다."], ["Jeames will tell Chawles his notions about you over their pipes and pewter beer-pots.", "제임스는 담배 연기와 백랍 맥주잔을 사이에 두고 찰스에게 당신에 대한 제 견해를 늘어놓을 것이다."], ["Some people ought to have mutes for servants in Vanity Fair--mutes who could not write.", "허영의 시장에서는 더러 벙어리 하인을 두어야 할 사람도 있는 법이다, 글도 쓸 줄 모르는 벙어리 말이다."], ["If you are guilty, tremble.", "죄가 있거든 떨지어다."], ["That fellow behind your chair may be a Janissary with a bow-string in his plush breeches pocket.", "당신의 의자 뒤에 선 저 녀석이, 어쩌면 비단 반바지 호주머니에 활시위를 감춘 친위병일지도 모르니."], ["If you are not guilty, have a care of appearances, which are as ruinous as guilt.", "죄가 없거든 겉모습을 조심할지어다. 겉모습이란 죄과 못지않게 사람을 파멸로 몰아가는 것이니."]], [["\"Was Rebecca guilty or not?\"", "\"리베카는 죄가 있었던가, 없었던가?\""], ["the Vehmgericht of the servants' hall had pronounced against her.", "하인방의 비밀재판소는 이미 그녀에게 유죄를 선고한 터였다."]], [["And, I shame to say, she would not have got credit had they not believed her to be guilty.", "그리고 부끄럽게도 한마디 덧붙이자면, 그들이 그녀를 죄 있는 여자로 믿지 않았더라면 그녀는 외상 신용조차 얻지 못했을 것이다."], ["It was the sight of the Marquis of Steyne's carriage-lamps at her door, contemplated by Raggles, burning in the blackness of midnight, \"that kep him up,\"", "그녀가 신용을 얻은 것은 무엇보다도, 라글스가 한밤중 칠흑 같은 어둠 속에서 그녀의 집 문 앞에 타오르는 스테인 후작의 마차 등불을 본 광경 덕분이었다. 그가 훗날 말한 대로 \"그게 사람을 버티게 해 준\""], ["as he afterwards said, that even more than Rebecca's arts and coaxings.", "것이었으니, 그것은 리베카의 온갖 수완과 아양보다도 한층 더한 것이었다."]], [["And so--guiltless very likely--she was writhing and pushing onward towards what they call \"a position in society,\" and the servants were pointing at her as lost and ruined.", "그리하여 — 십중팔구 죄 없는 몸으로 — 그녀는 사람들이 이른바 \"사교계의 지위\"라 부르는 것을 향해 몸부림치며 떠밀려 나아가고 있었고, 하인들은 그녀를 타락하고 파멸한 여자라 손가락질하고 있었다."], ["So you see Molly, the housemaid, of a morning, watching a spider in the doorpost lay his thread and laboriously crawl up it, until, tired of the sport, she raises her broom and sweeps away the thread and the artificer.", "그러니 보라, 어느 아침 하녀 몰리가 문설주에서 거미 한 마리가 줄을 자아 힘겹게 기어오르는 것을 지켜보다가, 이내 그 장난에 싫증이 나 빗자루를 들어 그 줄과 그 솜씨꾼을 함께 쓸어 버리는 것을."]], [["A day or two before Christmas, Becky, her husband and her son made ready and went to pass the holidays at the seat of their ancestors at Queen's Crawley.", "성탄절을 하루 이틀 앞두고, 베키와 그녀의 남편과 아들은 채비를 갖추어 조상 대대로 내려오는 영지인 퀸스크롤리로 휴가를 보내러 떠났다."], ["Becky would have liked to leave the little brat behind, and would have done so but for Lady Jane's urgent invitations to the youngster, and the symptoms of revolt and discontent which Rawdon manifested at her neglect of her son.", "베키는 그 어린 녀석을 두고 가고 싶어 했고, 실제로 그러했을 것이다. 다만 제인 부인이 그 아이를 한사코 청한 데다, 로던이 제 아들을 소홀히 대하는 아내에게 반항과 불만의 기색을 드러낸 탓에 그러지 못했다."], ["\"He's the finest boy in England,\"", "\"이 아이는 영국에서 제일가는 사내아이요.\""], ["the father said in a tone of reproach to her, \"and you don't seem to care for him, Becky, as much as you do for your spaniel.", "아버지는 나무라는 어조로 그녀에게 말했다. \"그런데 당신은 이 아이를 당신 애완견만큼도 아끼지 않는 것 같단 말이오, 베키."], ["He shan't bother you much; at home he will be away from you in the nursery, and he shall go outside on the coach with me.\"", "이 아이가 당신을 그리 성가시게 하지도 않을 거요. 집에 가면 육아방에 있을 테니 당신 곁에서 떨어져 있을 거고, 나랑 같이 마차 바깥자리에 타고 가면 되니까.\""]], [["\"Where you go yourself because you want to smoke those filthy cigars,\"", "\"당신이야 그 더러운 시가를 피우고 싶어서 바깥자리에 타는 거잖아요.\""], ["replied Mrs. Rawdon.", "로던 부인이 받아쳤다."]], [["\"I remember when you liked 'em though,\"", "\"하지만 당신도 한때는 그걸 좋아하지 않았소.\""], ["answered the husband.", "남편이 대꾸했다."]], [["Becky laughed; she was almost always good-humoured.", "베키는 웃었다. 그녀는 거의 언제나 기분이 좋았다."], ["\"That was when I was on my promotion, Goosey,\"", "\"그건 내가 한창 출세하려고 애쓰던 시절 얘기죠, 멍청한 양반.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"Take Rawdon outside with you and give him a cigar too if you like.\"", "\"로던을 바깥자리에 데리고 타요, 그러고 싶으면 그 애한테도 시가를 한 대 주든가요.\""]], [["Rawdon did not warm his little son for the winter's journey in this way, but he and Briggs wrapped up the child in shawls and comforters, and he was hoisted respectfully onto the roof of the coach in the dark morning, under the lamps of the White Horse Cellar; and with no small delight he watched the dawn rise and made his first journey to the place which his father still called home.", "로던은 겨울철 여행에 어린 아들을 그런 식으로 데우지는 않았으나, 그와 브리그스는 아이를 숄과 목도리로 둘둘 감쌌고, 아이는 어둑한 아침 화이트호스셀러의 등불 아래서 정중하게 마차 지붕 위로 올려졌다. 그리하여 아이는 적잖은 기쁨 속에 동이 트는 광경을 지켜보았고, 아버지가 여전히 집이라 부르는 그곳으로 처음 길을 떠났다."], ["It was a journey of infinite pleasure to the boy, to whom the incidents of the road afforded endless interest, his father answering to him all questions connected with it and telling him who lived in the great white house to the right, and whom the park belonged to.", "그것은 아이에게 더없이 즐거운 여행이었으니, 길에서 마주치는 일들이 끝없는 흥미를 불러일으켰고, 아버지는 그에 얽힌 온갖 물음에 일일이 답해 주며, 오른편 저 커다란 흰 집에는 누가 사는지, 저 사슴 동산은 누구 것인지 일러 주었다."], ["His mother, inside the vehicle, with her maid and her furs, her wrappers, and her scent bottles, made such a to-do that you would have thought she never had been in a stage-coach before--much less, that she had been turned out of this very one to make room for a paying passenger on a certain journey performed some half-score years ago.", "한편 마차 안에 탄 어머니는 하녀와 모피며 무릎 덮개며 향수병을 잔뜩 늘어놓고 어찌나 부산을 떨던지, 마치 역마차라곤 난생처음 타 본 사람인 줄 알았을 것이다. 하물며 그녀가 십 년쯤 전 어느 길에서, 삯을 치르는 손님에게 자리를 내주려고 바로 이 마차에서 내쫓겼던 적이 있었다는 것은 더더욱 상상도 못 했으리라."]], [["It was dark again when little Rawdon was wakened up to enter his uncle's carriage at Mudbury, and he sat and looked out of it wondering as the great iron gates flew open, and at the white trunks of the limes as they swept by, until they stopped, at length, before the light windows of the Hall, which were blazing and comfortable with Christmas welcome.", "어린 로던이 머드베리에서 큰아버지의 마차로 갈아타려고 잠에서 깨어났을 때는 다시 날이 어두워져 있었다. 아이는 자리에 앉아 마차 밖을 내다보며, 커다란 철문이 활짝 열리는 것을, 또 휙휙 스쳐 가는 보리수의 하얀 줄기들을 신기한 눈으로 바라보았다."], ["The hall-door was flung open--a big fire was burning in the great old fire-place--a carpet was down over the chequered black flags--\"It's the old Turkey one that used to be in the Ladies'", "그러다 마침내 마차는, 성탄절을 맞아 환히 밝혀진 채 아늑하고 따스한 저택의 불 켜진 창문 앞에 멈추어 섰다. 현관문이 활짝 열렸다."], ["Gallery,\"", "커다란 옛 벽난로에서는 큰 불이 타오르고 있었고, 흑백이 엇갈린 바닥돌 위로는 양탄자가 깔려 있었다."], ["thought Rebecca, and the next instant was kissing Lady Jane.", "\"예전에 부인 회랑에 깔려 있던 그 낡은 터키 양탄자로구나.\" 리베카는 그렇게 생각하고는, 다음 순간 제인 부인에게 입을 맞추고 있었다."]], [["She and Sir Pitt performed the same salute with great gravity; but Rawdon, having been smoking, hung back rather from his sister-in-law, whose two children came up to their cousin; and, while Matilda held out her hand and kissed him, Pitt Binkie Southdown, the son and heir, stood aloof rather and examined him as a little dog does a big dog.", "그녀와 피트 경도 더없이 엄숙하게 같은 인사를 나누었다. 그러나 로던은 시가를 피운 터라 제수에게서 다소 멀찍이 물러섰고, 그 사이 제수의 두 아이가 사촌에게 다가왔다. 마틸다가 손을 내밀어 아이에게 입을 맞추는 동안, 아들이자 상속자인 피트 빈키 사우스다운은 다소 떨어져 서서, 마치 작은 개가 큰 개를 살피듯 아이를 뜯어보았다."]], [["Then the kind hostess conducted her guests to the snug apartments blazing with cheerful fires.", "이윽고 다정한 안주인은 손님들을 명랑한 불길이 타오르는 아늑한 방으로 안내했다."], ["Then the young ladies came and knocked at Mrs. Rawdon's door, under the pretence that they were desirous to be useful, but in reality to have the pleasure of inspecting the contents of her band and bonnet-boxes, and her dresses which, though black, were of the newest London fashion.", "그러자 젊은 아가씨들이 로던 부인의 방문을 두드리고 찾아왔으니, 겉으로는 도울 일이 없을까 싶어서라는 핑계였으나, 실은 그녀의 끈 상자와 모자 상자에 든 것들을, 또 비록 검은색이긴 해도 런던의 최신 유행을 따른 그녀의 드레스들을 구경하는 즐거움을 누리기 위해서였다."], ["And they told her how much the Hall was changed for the better, and how old Lady Southdown was gone, and how Pitt was taking his station in the county, as became a Crawley in fact.", "그들은 저택이 얼마나 좋게 달라졌는지, 늙은 사우스다운 부인이 어떻게 떠나갔는지, 피트가 어떻게 한 사람의 크롤리답게 이 고을에서 제 자리를 잡아 가고 있는지를 그녀에게 들려주었다."], ["Then the great dinner-bell having rung, the family assembled at dinner, at which meal Rawdon Junior was placed by his aunt, the good-natured lady of the house, Sir Pitt being uncommonly attentive to his sister-in-law at his own right hand.", "이윽고 만찬을 알리는 큰 종이 울리자, 온 가족이 식탁에 둘러앉았다. 이 자리에서 어린 로던은 인심 좋은 안주인인 큰어머니 곁에 앉았고, 피트 경은 제 오른편의 제수에게 유난히 살뜰한 관심을 기울였다."]], [["Little Rawdon exhibited a fine appetite and showed a gentlemanlike behaviour.", "어린 로던은 왕성한 식욕을 드러내면서도 신사다운 몸가짐을 보였다."]], [["\"I like to dine here,\"", "\"여기서 밥 먹는 게 좋아요.\""], ["he said to his aunt when he had completed his meal, at the conclusion of which, and after a decent grace by Sir Pitt, the younger son and heir was introduced, and was perched on a high chair by the Baronet's side, while the daughter took possession of the place and the little wine-glass prepared for her near her mother.", "식사를 다 마친 아이가 큰어머니에게 말했다. 식사가 끝나고 피트 경이 점잖게 감사 기도를 올린 뒤, 작은아들이자 상속자가 불려 나와 준남작 곁의 높은 의자에 앉혀졌고, 딸아이는 어머니 옆에 마련된 제자리와 작은 포도주 잔을 차지했다."], ["\"I like to dine here,\"", "\"여기서 밥 먹는 게 좋아요.\""], ["said Rawdon Minor, looking up at his relation's kind face.", "작은 로던이 큰어머니의 다정한 얼굴을 올려다보며 말했다."]], [["\"Why?\"", "\"왜 그러니?\""], ["said the good Lady Jane.", "마음씨 고운 제인 부인이 물었다."]], [["\"I dine in the kitchen when I am at home,\"", "\"집에서는 부엌에서 밥을 먹거든요.\""], ["replied Rawdon Minor, \"or else with Briggs.\"", "작은 로던이 대답했다. \"아니면 브리그스랑 같이 먹든가요.\""], ["But Becky was so engaged with the Baronet, her host, pouring out a flood of compliments and delights and raptures, and admiring young Pitt Binkie, whom she declared to be the most beautiful, intelligent, noble-looking little creature, and so like his father, that she did not hear the remarks of her own flesh and blood at the other end of the broad shining table.", "그러나 베키는 집주인인 준남작을 상대하기에 여념이 없었다. 온갖 찬사와 기쁨과 감탄을 쏟아 내며, 어린 피트 빈키를 두고 세상에서 가장 어여쁘고 영민하며 기품 있게 생긴 작은 아이라고, 제 아버지를 쏙 빼닮았다고 추켜세우느라, 저 너머 넓고 반들거리는 식탁 끝자락에서 제 핏줄이 하는 말은 듣지도 못했다."]], [["As a guest, and it being the first night of his arrival, Rawdon the Second was allowed to sit up until the hour when tea being over, and a great gilt book being laid on the table before Sir Pitt, all the domestics of the family streamed in, and Sir Pitt read prayers.", "손님인 데다 도착한 첫날 밤이었던 만큼, 둘째 로던은 차를 다 마실 때까지 자지 않고 앉아 있도록 허락받았다. 차가 끝나자 커다란 금박 책 한 권이 피트 경 앞 식탁에 놓였고, 집안의 하인들이 모두 줄지어 들어왔으며, 피트 경이 기도문을 읽었다."], ["It was the first time the poor little boy had ever witnessed or heard of such a ceremonial.", "이 가엾은 어린 사내아이가 그런 의식을 직접 보거나 들어 본 것은 그때가 처음이었다."]], [["The house had been much improved even since the Baronet's brief reign, and was pronounced by Becky to be perfect, charming, delightful, when she surveyed it in his company.", "저택은 준남작의 짧은 치세 이래로도 한층 더 손질이 되어 있었으니, 베키가 피트 경과 더불어 둘러보고는 완벽하다고, 매력적이라고, 더없이 흐뭇하다고 단언할 정도였다."], ["As for little Rawdon, who examined it with the children for his guides, it seemed to him a perfect palace of enchantment and wonder.", "어린 로던으로 말하자면, 아이들을 길잡이 삼아 저택을 살펴본 그에게 그곳은 마법과 경이로 가득한 완벽한 궁전처럼 보였다."], ["There were long galleries, and ancient state bedrooms, there were pictures and old China, and armour.", "기다란 회랑들이 있었고, 고풍스러운 손님용 침실들이 있었으며, 그림과 오래된 도자기와 갑옷이 있었다."], ["There were the rooms in which Grandpapa died, and by which the children walked with terrified looks.", "또 할아버지가 돌아가신 방들이 있었으니, 아이들은 겁먹은 얼굴로 그 곁을 지나다녔다."], ["\"Who was Grandpapa?\"", "\"할아버지가 누군데?\""], ["he asked; and they told him how he used to be very old, and used to be wheeled about in a garden-chair, and they showed him the garden-chair one day rotting in the out-house in which it had lain since the old gentleman had been wheeled away yonder to the church, of which the spire was glittering over the park elms.", "아이가 묻자, 아이들은 할아버지가 무척 늙은 분이었다고, 정원용 바퀴 의자에 실려 이리저리 다니곤 했다고 일러 주었다. 그러고는 어느 날 그 정원용 의자를 보여 주었으니, 노인이 저 너머 교회로 — 그 첨탑이 사슴 동산의 느릅나무들 위로 반짝이고 있었다 — 실려 간 이래로 줄곧 처박혀 있던 헛간에서 썩어 가고 있었다."]], [["The brothers had good occupation for several mornings in examining the improvements which had been effected by Sir Pitt's genius and economy.", "두 형제는 며칠 아침 동안, 피트 경의 재능과 절약으로 이루어 낸 개량의 성과들을 살펴보는 좋은 소일거리를 얻었다."], ["And as they walked or rode, and looked at them, they could talk without too much boring each other.", "그렇게 거닐거나 말을 타고 다니며 그것들을 둘러보는 동안, 둘은 서로를 너무 지루하게 하지 않고도 이야기를 나눌 수 있었다."], ["And Pitt took care to tell Rawdon what a heavy outlay of money these improvements had occasioned, and that a man of landed and funded property was often very hard pressed for twenty pounds.", "그리고 피트는 이런 개량에 돈이 얼마나 많이 들었는지, 또 토지와 공채를 지닌 사람도 더러 단돈 20파운드에 쪼들릴 때가 있다는 것을 잊지 않고 로던에게 일러 주었다."], ["\"There is that new lodge-gate,\"", "\"저기 저 새 정문도 그렇다네.\""], ["said Pitt, pointing to it humbly with the bamboo cane, \"I can no more pay for it before the dividends in January than I can fly.\"", "피트가 대나무 지팡이로 겸손하게 그것을 가리키며 말했다. \"일월에 배당금이 나오기 전엔 저 값을 치르기란, 내가 하늘을 나는 것만큼이나 어림없는 일이야.\""]], [["\"I can lend you, Pitt, till then,\"", "\"그때까진 내가 빌려줄 수 있네, 피트.\""], ["Rawdon answered rather ruefully; and they went in and looked at the restored lodge, where the family arms were just new scraped in stone, and where old Mrs.", "로던이 다소 멋쩍게 대답했다. 그러고는 둘은 안으로 들어가 새로 고친 수위실을 둘러보았으니, 가문의 문장이 막 돌에 새로 새겨진 참이었고, 또 늙은 록 부인은 실로 오랜 세월 만에 처음으로 빈틈없는 문과 성한 지붕과 멀쩡한 창문을 갖게 된 터였다."], ["Lock, for the first time these many long years, had tight doors, sound roofs, and whole windows.", "* 살미: 들새 고기를 와인 소스에 익혀 만드는 프랑스식 스튜 요리."]], [["Between Hampshire and London\nSir Pitt Crawley had done more than repair fences and restore dilapidated lodges on the Queen's Crawley estate.", "피트 크롤리 경은 퀸스크롤리 영지에서 울타리를 고치고 허물어진 별채를 손보는 일 이상의 것을 해냈다."], ["Like a wise man he had set to work to rebuild the injured popularity of his house and stop up the gaps and ruins in which his name had been left by his disreputable and thriftless old predecessor.", "현명한 사람답게 그는 가문의 손상된 명망을 다시 일으켜 세우고, 평판 나쁘고 헤픈 노선대가 자기 이름에 남겨 놓은 틈과 폐허를 메우는 일에 착수했던 것이다."], ["He was elected for the borough speedily after his father's demise; a magistrate, a member of parliament, a county magnate and representative of an ancient family, he made it his duty to show himself before the Hampshire public, subscribed handsomely to the county charities, called assiduously upon all the county folk, and laid himself out in a word to take that position in Hampshire, and in the Empire afterwards, to which he thought his prodigious talents justly entitled him.", "그는 아버지가 세상을 뜨자 곧바로 그 선거구의 의원으로 선출되었다. 치안판사이자 하원의원이며 카운티의 유력 인사요 유서 깊은 가문의 대표자가 된 그는, 햄프셔 주민들 앞에 자기 모습을 드러내는 것을 의무로 삼아 카운티의 자선사업에 후하게 기부하고, 카운티의 인사들을 부지런히 찾아다녔으며, 한마디로 햄프셔에서, 그리고 나아가 제국 전체에서, 자신의 비범한 재능에 마땅히 걸맞다고 여기는 그 지위를 차지하기 위해 갖은 노력을 기울였다."], ["Lady Jane was instructed to be friendly with the Fuddlestones, and the Wapshots, and the other famous baronets, their neighbours.", "제인 부인은 이웃에 사는 퍼들스턴 일가며 왑샷 일가며 그 밖의 이름난 준남작들과 친하게 지내라는 지시를 받았다. 이제 퀸스크롤리의 가로수 길에서는 그들의 마차를 자주 볼 수 있었다."], ["Their carriages might frequently be seen in the Queen's Crawley avenue now; they dined pretty frequently at the Hall (where the cookery was so good that it was clear Lady Jane very seldom had a hand in it), and in return Pitt and his wife most energetically dined out in all sorts of weather and at all sorts of distances.", "그들은 저택에서 제법 자주 만찬을 들었고(요리가 어찌나 훌륭했던지 제인 부인이 손을 댄 적은 거의 없음이 분명했다), 그 답례로 피트와 그 아내는 날씨가 어떻든 거리가 얼마나 멀든 가리지 않고 열성껏 만찬에 나갔다. 피트는 본디 화롯불도 식욕도 변변찮은 냉랭한 사람이라 흥겨운 자리를 좋아하지 않았지만, 손님을 후하게 대접하고 사람들에게 너그럽게 처신하는 것이야말로 자기 지위에 따르는 책무라고 여겼던 것이다."], ["For though Pitt did not care for joviality, being a frigid man of poor hearth and appetite, yet he considered that to be hospitable and condescending was quite incumbent on his station, and every time that he got a headache from too long an after-dinner sitting, he felt that he was a martyr to duty.", "그래서 식후에 너무 오래 앉아 있다가 두통이 도질 때마다, 그는 자신이 의무에 순교하는 것이라고 느꼈다. 그는 카운티에서 가장 내로라하는 시골 신사들과 어울려 작황이며 곡물법이며 정치를 논했다."], ["He talked about crops, corn-laws, politics, with the best country gentlemen.", "또한 (전에는 이런 문제에서 한심한 자유사상가 노릇을 즐기던 그가) 밀렵 단속과 사냥감 보호에 열을 올렸다. 그는 사냥을 하지 않았다."], ["He (who had been formerly inclined to be a sad free-thinker on these points) entered into poaching and game preserving with ardour.", "사냥하는 부류의 사람이 아니었던 것이다. 그는 책을 가까이하고 조용한 습성을 지닌 사람이었다."], ["He didn't hunt; he wasn't a hunting man; he was a man of books and peaceful habits; but he thought that the breed of horses must be kept up in the country, and that the breed of foxes must therefore be looked to, and for his part, if his friend, Sir Huddlestone Fuddlestone, liked to draw his country and meet as of old the F.", "그러나 시골에서는 말의 혈통을 잘 보존해야 하고 따라서 여우의 혈통도 보살펴야 한다고 생각했으며, 자기 친구인 허들스턴 퍼들스턴 경이 옛날 퍼들스턴 사냥개들이 퀸스크롤리에서 그러했듯 자기 영지로 사냥을 나오고 싶어 한다면 기꺼이 그를, 그리고 퍼들스턴 사냥대의 신사분들을 맞이하겠노라고 했다."], ["hounds used to do at Queen's Crawley, he should be happy to see him there, and the gentlemen of the Fuddlestone hunt.", "게다가 사우스다운 부인을 경악케 한 일이거니와, 그는 날이 갈수록 정통 신앙 쪽으로 기울어 갔다."], ["And to Lady Southdown's dismay too he became more orthodox in his tendencies every day; gave up preaching in public and attending meeting-houses; went stoutly to church; called on the Bishop and all the Clergy at Winchester; and made no objection when the Venerable Archdeacon Trumper asked for a game of whist.", "공개적으로 설교하거나 집회소에 다니는 일을 그만두고, 꿋꿋이 교회에 나갔으며, 윈체스터의 주교와 모든 성직자를 찾아가 인사했고, 트럼퍼 부주교 예하가 휘스트 한판을 청했을 때도 마다하지 않았다."], ["What pangs must have been those of Lady Southdown, and what an utter castaway she must have thought her son-in-law for permitting such a godless diversion!", "그토록 불경한 오락을 허락하다니, 사우스다운 부인은 얼마나 큰 마음의 고통을 겪었을 것이며, 또 사위를 얼마나 구제 불능의 타락한 인간으로 여겼겠는가!"], ["And when, on the return of the family from an oratorio at Winchester, the Baronet announced to the young ladies that he should next year very probably take them to the \"county balls,\"", "그리고 일가족이 윈체스터의 오라토리오에서 돌아왔을 때, 준남작이 젊은 숙녀들에게 내년에는 아마도 \"카운티 무도회\"에 데려가 주겠노라고 알리자, 그들은 그 친절에 그를 떠받들었다."], ["they worshipped him for his kindness. Lady Jane was only too obedient, and perhaps glad herself to go.", "제인 부인은 더없이 순종적이었고, 어쩌면 자신도 가고 싶었던 것인지도 모른다."], ["The Dowager wrote off the direst descriptions of her daughter's worldly behaviour to the authoress of the Washerwoman of Finchley Common at the Cape; and her house in Brighton being about this time unoccupied, returned to that watering-place, her absence being not very much deplored by her children.", "노부인은 케이프에 있는 「핀칠리 코먼의 세탁부 아낙」의 저자에게 딸의 세속적인 행실을 더없이 끔찍한 표현으로 적어 보냈다. 그리고 마침 이 무렵 브라이턴의 자기 집이 비게 되자, 그녀는 그 휴양지로 돌아갔는데, 자녀들은 그녀가 떠난 것을 그리 애석해하지 않았다."], ["We may suppose, too, that Rebecca, on paying a second visit to Queen's Crawley, did not feel particularly grieved at the absence of the lady of the medicine chest; though she wrote a Christmas letter to her Ladyship, in which she respectfully recalled herself to Lady Southdown's recollection, spoke with gratitude of the delight which her Ladyship's conversation had given her on the former visit, dilated on the kindness with which her Ladyship had treated her in sickness, and declared that everything at Queen's Crawley reminded her of her absent friend.", "또한 레베카가 두 번째로 퀸스크롤리를 찾았을 때, 그 약상자의 여주인이 없다는 사실을 그리 슬퍼하지 않았으리라고 짐작해도 좋을 것이다. 그러면서도 그녀는 사우스다운 부인에게 크리스마스 편지를 써 보내, 자신을 부디 기억해 달라며 정중히 안부를 전하고, 지난 방문 때 부인의 대화가 안겨 준 기쁨에 감사한다 말하고, 자신이 병중일 때 부인이 베풀어 준 친절을 길게 늘어놓고는, 퀸스크롤리의 모든 것이 멀리 떠난 그 벗을 떠올리게 한다고 했던 것이다."]], [["A great part of the altered demeanour and popularity of Sir Pitt Crawley might have been traced to the counsels of that astute little lady of Curzon Street.", "피트 크롤리 경의 달라진 태도와 높아진 인기의 상당 부분은, 커즌 거리의 그 약삭빠른 작은 부인이 일러 준 조언에서 비롯된 것이라 해도 무방했다."], ["\"You remain a Baronet--you consent to be a mere country gentleman,\"", "\"당신은 그저 준남작에 머무르려는 건가요. 한낱 시골 신사로 만족하시겠다고요.\""], ["she said to him, while he had been her guest in London.", "그가 런던에서 그녀의 손님으로 머무를 때 그녀가 말했다."], ["\"No, Sir Pitt Crawley, I know you better.", "\"아니지요, 피트 크롤리 경, 저는 당신을 더 잘 알아요."], ["I know your talents and your ambition.", "저는 당신의 재능과 야망을 알지요."], ["You fancy you hide them both, but you can conceal neither from me.", "당신은 그 둘을 다 숨긴다고 여기시지만, 제게는 어느 것 하나 감출 수 없답니다."], ["I showed Lord Steyne your pamphlet on malt.", "저는 스타인 경에게 맥아에 관한 당신의 소책자를 보여 드렸어요."], ["He was familiar with it, and said it was in the opinion of the whole Cabinet the most masterly thing that had appeared on the subject.", "그분은 이미 그것을 잘 알고 계셨고, 내각 전체의 의견으로도 그 주제에 관해 나온 글 가운데 가장 명석한 것이라고 하셨답니다."], ["The Ministry has its eye upon you, and I know what you want.", "정부가 당신을 주목하고 있어요. 그리고 저는 당신이 무엇을 원하는지 알지요."], ["You want to distinguish yourself in Parliament; every one says you are the finest speaker in England (for your speeches at Oxford are still remembered).", "당신은 의회에서 두각을 나타내고 싶으신 거예요. 다들 당신이 영국에서 가장 빼어난 연설가라고 하니까요(옥스퍼드에서 하신 연설들이 아직도 회자되고 있거든요)."], ["You want to be Member for the County, where, with your own vote and your borough at your back, you can command anything.", "당신은 카운티의 의원이 되고 싶으신 거예요. 거기서라면 당신 자신의 표와 당신을 뒷받침하는 선거구를 등에 업고 무엇이든 좌지우지할 수 있을 테니까요."], ["And you want to be Baron Crawley of Queen's Crawley, and will be before you die.", "그리고 당신은 퀸스크롤리의 크롤리 남작이 되고 싶으신 거고, 죽기 전에 반드시 그리되실 거예요. 저는 그 모든 걸 다 알았답니다."], ["I saw it all. I could read your heart, Sir Pitt.", "저는 당신의 마음을 읽을 수 있었어요, 피트 경."], ["If I had a husband who possessed your intellect as he does your name, I sometimes think I should not be unworthy of him--but--but I am your kinswoman now,\"", "만약 제게 당신의 이름뿐 아니라 당신의 지성까지 지닌 남편이 있었더라면, 저는 가끔 제가 그이에게 부끄럽지 않은 사람이 되지 않았을까 생각하곤 해요. 하지만, 하지만 이제 저는 당신의 친척이지요.\""], ["she added with a laugh.", "그녀가 웃으며 덧붙였다."], ["\"Poor little penniless, I have got a little interest--and who knows, perhaps the mouse may be able to aid the lion.\"", "\"한 푼 없는 가엾은 것이지만, 저도 약간의 연줄은 있답니다. 그러니 누가 알겠어요."], ["Pitt Crawley was amazed and enraptured with her speech.", "어쩌면 생쥐가 사자를 도울 수 있을지도요.\" 피트 크롤리는 그녀의 말에 깜짝 놀라며 황홀해했다."], ["\"How that woman comprehends me!\" he said.", "\"저 여자는 나를 어쩌면 그리도 잘 이해하는가!\""], ["\"I never could get Jane to read three pages of the malt pamphlet.", "그가 말했다. \"제인은 그 맥아 소책자를 세 쪽도 채 읽게 만들 수가 없었지."], ["She has no idea that I have commanding talents or secret ambition.", "그 사람은 내게 탁월한 재능이나 은밀한 야망이 있다는 걸 짐작도 못 해."], ["So they remember my speaking at Oxford, do they?", "그래, 그자들이 옥스퍼드에서 하던 내 연설을 기억한다 이거지?"], ["The rascals!", "못된 것들 같으니!"], ["Now that I represent my borough and may sit for the county, they begin to recollect me!", "이제 내가 선거구를 대표하고 카운티 의원으로 나설 수 있게 되니 나를 기억하기 시작한다 이 말이지! 아니, 스타인 경은 작년 알현식에서 나를 본 척도 안 하지 않았던가."], ["Why, Lord Steyne cut me at the levee last year; they are beginning to find out that Pitt Crawley is some one at last.", "이제야 사람들이 피트 크롤리도 결국 어엿한 인물임을 알아차리기 시작하는군. 그래, 이 사람들이 무시하던 그 사람은 늘 한결같았어."], ["Yes, the man was always the same whom these people neglected: it was only the opportunity that was wanting, and I will show them now that I can speak and act as well as write.", "다만 기회가 없었을 뿐이지. 이제 내가 글뿐 아니라 말도 하고 행동도 할 수 있다는 걸 저들에게 보여 주리라."], ["Achilles did not declare himself until they gave him the sword.", "아킬레우스도 그들이 칼을 쥐여 주기 전에는 자기 정체를 드러내지 않았지."], ["I hold it now, and the world shall yet hear of Pitt Crawley.\"", "이제 내가 그 칼을 쥐었으니, 세상은 머지않아 피트 크롤리의 이름을 듣게 될 것이다.\""]], [["Therefore it was that this roguish diplomatist has grown so hospitable; that he was so civil to oratorios and hospitals; so kind to Deans and Chapters; so generous in giving and accepting dinners; so uncommonly gracious to farmers on market-days; and so much interested about county business; and that the Christmas at the Hall was the gayest which had been known there for many a long day.", "그리하여 이 교활한 책략가는 그토록 손님을 후하게 대접하게 되었고, 오라토리오와 병원에 그토록 정중해졌으며, 주임사제며 참사회에 그토록 친절해졌고, 만찬을 베풀고 받는 데 그토록 후해졌으며, 장날이면 농부들에게 유달리 상냥해졌고, 카운티의 사무에 그토록 관심을 쏟게 되었던 것이다. 그리하여 그해 저택의 크리스마스는 오랜 세월 동안 그곳에서 볼 수 없었던 가장 흥겨운 크리스마스가 되었다."]], [["On Christmas Day a great family gathering took place.", "크리스마스 날에는 성대한 가족 모임이 열렸다. 목사관의 크롤리 일가가 모두 만찬에 왔다."], ["All the Crawleys from the Rectory came to dine. Rebecca was as frank and fond of Mrs.", "레베카는 마치 상대가 한 번도 자기 적이었던 적이 없었다는 듯 뷰트 부인에게 솔직하고 다정하게 굴었다."], ["Bute as if the other had never been her enemy; she was affectionately interested in the dear girls, and surprised at the progress which they had made in music since her time, and insisted upon encoring one of the duets out of the great song-books which Jim, grumbling, had been forced to bring under his arm from the Rectory. Mrs.", "사랑스러운 딸들에게 애정 어린 관심을 보였고, 자기가 있던 때 이후로 그 애들의 음악 실력이 늘어난 데 놀라워했으며, 짐이 투덜대면서도 어쩔 수 없이 목사관에서 옆구리에 끼고 와야 했던 그 두꺼운 노래책 가운데 한 곡의 이중창을 기어이 한 번 더 부르게 했다."], ["Bute, perforce, was obliged to adopt a decent demeanour towards the little adventuress--of course being free to discourse with her daughters afterwards about the absurd respect with which Sir Pitt treated his sister-in-law.", "뷰트 부인은 마지못해 이 어린 모험가에게 그럴듯한 태도를 취할 수밖에 없었다. 물론 나중에 딸들과 더불어, 피트 경이 제수에게 보이는 그 터무니없는 존중을 두고 마음껏 수군거릴 자유는 있었지만 말이다."], ["But Jim, who had sat next to her at dinner, declared she was a trump, and one and all of the Rector's family agreed that the little Rawdon was a fine boy.", "그러나 만찬에서 그녀 옆에 앉았던 짐은 그녀가 멋진 사람이라고 단언했고, 목사 일가는 너 나 할 것 없이 어린 로던이 잘생긴 사내아이라는 데 의견을 모았다."], ["They respected a possible baronet in the boy, between whom and the title there was only the little sickly pale Pitt Binkie.", "그들은 그 아이에게서 장차 준남작이 될 가능성을 존중했던 것이다. 작위와 그 아이 사이를 가로막은 것이라곤 병약하고 창백한 자그마한 피트 빙키뿐이었으니까."]], [["The children were very good friends.", "아이들은 매우 사이가 좋았다."], ["Pitt Binkie was too little a dog for such a big dog as Rawdon to play with; and Matilda being only a girl, of course not fit companion for a young gentleman who was near eight years old, and going into jackets very soon.", "피트 빙키는 로던처럼 덩치 큰 강아지가 함께 놀기에는 너무 작은 강아지였고, 마틸다는 한낱 계집아이일 뿐이니, 여덟 살이 다 되어 가고 머지않아 짧은 상의를 입게 될 어린 신사에게는 당연히 어울리는 동무가 못 되었다."], ["He took the command of this small party at once--the little girl and the little boy following him about with great reverence at such times as he condescended to sport with them.", "로던은 이 작은 무리의 지휘권을 단번에 거머쥐었다. 어린 계집아이와 어린 사내아이는 그가 황송하게도 함께 놀아 줄 때면 깊은 경의를 품고 그를 졸졸 따라다녔다."], ["His happiness and pleasure in the country were extreme.", "시골에서 그가 누린 행복과 즐거움은 더할 나위 없었다."], ["The kitchen garden pleased him hugely, the flowers moderately, but the pigeons and the poultry, and the stables when he was allowed to visit them, were delightful objects to him.", "채마밭이 그를 무척 흐뭇하게 했고, 꽃은 그저 그랬으나, 비둘기며 가금이며, 가도 좋다는 허락을 받을 때면 마구간이 그에게는 더없이 즐거운 구경거리였다."], ["He resisted being kissed by the Misses Crawley, but he allowed Lady Jane sometimes to embrace him, and it was by her side that he liked to sit when, the signal to retire to the drawing-room being given, the ladies left the gentlemen to their claret--by her side rather than by his mother.", "그는 크롤리 양들이 입맞춤하려 들면 뿌리쳤지만, 제인 부인이 이따금 끌어안는 것은 허락했다. 그리고 응접실로 물러가라는 신호가 떨어져 숙녀들이 신사들을 클라레와 함께 남겨 두고 자리를 뜰 때면, 그는 제 어머니 곁보다 그 부인 곁에 앉기를 좋아했다."], ["For Rebecca, seeing that tenderness was the fashion, called Rawdon to her one evening and stooped down and kissed him in the presence of all the ladies.", "어느 날 저녁, 다정함이 유행이라는 것을 알아챈 레베카가 모든 숙녀가 보는 앞에서 로던을 자기에게 불러 몸을 굽혀 입을 맞추었기 때문이다."]], [["He looked her full in the face after the operation, trembling and turning very red, as his wont was when moved.", "그 의식이 끝난 뒤 아이는 그녀의 얼굴을 똑바로 쳐다보며, 마음이 동할 때면 늘 그러하듯 부들부들 떨면서 얼굴이 새빨개졌다."], ["\"You never kiss me at home, Mamma,\"", "\"엄마는 집에선 한 번도 내게 입맞춰 준 적 없잖아요.\""], ["he said, at which there was a general silence and consternation and a by no means pleasant look in Becky's eyes.", "그가 말했다. 그러자 좌중에 일제히 침묵과 경악이 흘렀고, 베키의 두 눈에는 결코 유쾌하다 할 수 없는 빛이 어렸다."]], [["Rawdon was fond of his sister-in-law, for her regard for his son.", "로던은 자기 형수가 아들을 아껴 주는 까닭에 그녀를 좋아했다."], ["Lady Jane and Becky did not get on quite so well at this visit as on occasion of the former one, when the Colonel's wife was bent upon pleasing.", "제인 부인과 베키는, 대령의 아내가 환심을 사려 안달이던 지난번 방문 때만큼 이번에는 사이가 그리 좋지 않았다."], ["Those two speeches of the child struck rather a chill.", "아이가 내뱉은 그 두 마디 말이 분위기에 적잖이 찬물을 끼얹었다."], ["Perhaps Sir Pitt was rather too attentive to her.", "어쩌면 피트 경이 그녀에게 지나치게 살가웠던 탓도 있었으리라."]], [["But Rawdon, as became his age and size, was fonder of the society of the men than of the women, and never wearied of accompanying his sire to the stables, whither the Colonel retired to smoke his cigar--Jim, the Rector's son, sometimes joining his cousin in that and other amusements.", "그러나 로던은 제 나이와 덩치에 걸맞게 여자들보다 남자들과 어울리기를 더 좋아해서, 대령이 시가를 피우러 물러가는 마구간으로 아비를 따라가는 일에 결코 싫증을 내는 법이 없었다. 목사의 아들 짐이 가끔 사촌과 함께 그런저런 놀이에 어울리기도 했다."], ["He and the Baronet's keeper were very close friends, their mutual taste for \"dawgs\" bringing them much together.", "로던은 준남작의 사냥터지기와 매우 가까운 벗이 되었는데, \"개\"에 대한 서로의 취향이 그들을 자주 한데 어울리게 만들었던 것이다."], ["On one day, Mr. James, the Colonel, and Horn, the keeper, went and shot pheasants, taking little Rawdon with them.", "어느 날 제임스 군과 대령, 그리고 사냥터지기 혼이 어린 로던을 데리고 꿩 사냥을 나갔다."], ["On another most blissful morning, these four gentlemen partook of the amusement of rat-hunting in a barn, than which sport Rawdon as yet had never seen anything more noble.", "또 어느 더없이 행복한 아침에는, 이 네 신사가 헛간에서 쥐 사냥의 재미를 함께 누렸는데, 로던은 그때껏 그보다 더 고귀한 놀이를 본 적이 없었다. 그들은 헛간에 난 어떤 배수구의 한쪽 끝을 막고, 다른 쪽 구멍으로 흰담비를 들이밀고는, 손에 막대기를 치켜든 채 조용히 한쪽에 떨어져 섰다."], ["They stopped up the ends of certain drains in the barn, into the other openings of which ferrets were inserted, and then stood silently aloof, with uplifted stakes in their hands, and an anxious little terrier (Mr.", "그러는 동안 잔뜩 안달이 난 자그마한 테리어(다름 아닌 제임스 군의 그 유명한 \"개\" 포셉스였다)는 흥분으로 숨도 제대로 못 쉰 채, 세 발로 꼼짝 않고 서서 발밑에서 들려오는 쥐들의 희미한 찍찍 소리에 귀를 기울였다."], ["James's celebrated \"dawg\" Forceps, indeed) scarcely breathing from excitement, listening motionless on three legs, to the faint squeaking of the rats below.", "마침내 막다른 데 몰린 짐승들은 죽기 살기로 대담하게 땅 위로 뛰쳐나왔다."], ["Desperately bold at last, the persecuted animals bolted above-ground--the terrier accounted for one, the keeper for another; Rawdon, from flurry and excitement, missed his rat, but on the other hand he half-murdered a ferret.", "한 마리는 테리어가, 또 한 마리는 사냥터지기가 처치했다. 로던은 허둥대고 흥분한 나머지 제 쥐는 놓쳤지만, 그 대신 흰담비 한 마리를 반쯤 죽여 놓았다."]], [["But the greatest day of all was that on which Sir Huddlestone Fuddlestone's hounds met upon the lawn at Queen's Crawley.", "그러나 무엇보다 가장 큰 날은, 허들스턴 퍼들스턴 경의 사냥개들이 퀸스크롤리의 잔디밭에 모인 날이었다."]], [["That was a famous sight for little Rawdon.", "그것은 어린 로던에게 굉장한 구경거리였다. 열 시 반에 허들스턴 퍼들스턴 경의 사냥꾼 톰 무디가 가도를 따라 잰걸음으로 올라오는 모습이 보였고, 그 뒤로 늠름한 사냥개 떼가 빽빽이 무리를 이루어 따라왔다."], ["At half-past ten, Tom Moody, Sir Huddlestone Fuddlestone's huntsman, was seen trotting up the avenue, followed by the noble pack of hounds in a compact body--the rear being brought up by the two whips clad in stained scarlet frocks--light hard-featured lads on well-bred lean horses, possessing marvellous dexterity in casting the points of their long heavy whips at the thinnest part of any dog's skin who dares to straggle from the main body, or to take the slightest notice, or even so much as wink, at the hares and rabbits starting under their noses.", "행렬의 맨 뒤는 얼룩진 진홍빛 사냥복 차림의 몰이꾼 둘이 맡았다. 몸집이 가볍고 야무진 얼굴을 한 젊은이들로, 혈통 좋은 비쩍 마른 말에 올라타 있었으며, 무리에서 벗어나 어슬렁대거나, 코앞에서 튀어 오르는 토끼와 산토끼에게 조금이라도 눈길을 주거나 심지어 곁눈질이라도 하는 개가 있으면, 그 길고 묵직한 채찍 끝을 가장 살갗이 얇은 자리에 정확히 후려치는 놀라운 솜씨를 지니고 있었다."]], [["Next comes boy Jack, Tom Moody's son, who weighs five stone, measures eight-and-forty inches, and will never be any bigger.", "다음으로는 톰 무디의 아들 잭 소년이 왔는데, 무게는 다섯 스톤에 키는 마흔여덟 인치였고, 앞으로도 더 자라지는 못할 아이였다."], ["He is perched on a large raw-boned hunter, half-covered by a capacious saddle.", "그는 큼직한 안장에 절반쯤 가려진, 뼈만 앙상한 커다란 사냥말 위에 올라타 있었다."], ["This animal is Sir Huddlestone Fuddlestone's favourite horse the Nob.", "이 말은 허들스턴 퍼들스턴 경이 아끼는 말 노브였다."], ["Other horses, ridden by other small boys, arrive from time to time, awaiting their masters, who will come cantering on anon.", "다른 어린 소년들이 탄 다른 말들도 이따금 도착하여, 곧 천천히 말을 몰고 올 주인들을 기다렸다."]], [["Tom Moody rides up to the door of the Hall, where he is welcomed by the butler, who offers him drink, which he declines.", "톰 무디가 저택 문 앞까지 말을 몰고 오자, 집사가 나와 그를 맞이하며 마실 것을 권했으나 그는 사양했다."], ["He and his pack then draw off into a sheltered corner of the lawn, where the dogs roll on the grass, and play or growl angrily at one another, ever and anon breaking out into furious fight speedily to be quelled by Tom's voice, unmatched at rating, or the snaky thongs of the whips.", "그런 다음 그와 그의 개 떼는 잔디밭의 한적한 구석으로 물러났다. 거기서 개들은 풀밭을 뒹굴며 장난을 치거나 서로 으르렁댔고, 이따금 사납게 싸움이 붙기도 했지만, 으름장 놓는 데 둘째가라면 서러운 톰의 목소리나 채찍의 뱀 같은 가죽끈에 금세 제압되곤 했다."]], [["Many young gentlemen canter up on thoroughbred hacks, spatter-dashed to the knee, and enter the house to drink cherry-brandy and pay their respects to the ladies, or, more modest and sportsmanlike, divest themselves of their mud-boots, exchange their hacks for their hunters, and warm their blood by a preliminary gallop round the lawn.", "순종 승용마에 올라탄 젊은 신사들이 무릎까지 진흙을 튀긴 채 잇따라 천천히 다가와서는, 집 안으로 들어가 체리 브랜디를 마시고 숙녀들에게 인사를 올렸다. 더 겸손하고 사냥꾼다운 이들은 진흙 묻은 장화를 벗고 승용마를 사냥말로 바꾸어 탄 뒤, 잔디밭을 한 바퀴 미리 내달려 몸을 덥혔다."], ["Then they collect round the pack in the corner and talk with Tom Moody of past sport, and the merits of Sniveller and Diamond, and of the state of the country and of the wretched breed of foxes.", "그런 다음 그들은 구석에 모인 개 떼 주위로 모여들어 톰 무디와 지난 사냥 이야기며, 스니벨러와 다이아몬드의 기량이며, 고장의 형편이며, 형편없는 여우의 혈통에 대해 이야기를 나누었다."]], [["Sir Huddlestone presently appears mounted on a clever cob and rides up to the Hall, where he enters and does the civil thing by the ladies, after which, being a man of few words, he proceeds to business.", "이윽고 허들스턴 경이 영리한 조랑말에 올라타 나타나서는 저택까지 말을 몰고 와 안으로 들어가 숙녀들에게 예의를 차렸고, 그런 뒤에는 본디 말수가 적은 사람답게 곧장 일에 착수했다."], ["The hounds are drawn up to the hall-door, and little Rawdon descends amongst them, excited yet half-alarmed by the caresses which they bestow upon him, at the thumps he receives from their waving tails, and at their canine bickerings, scarcely restrained by Tom Moody's tongue and lash.", "사냥개들이 저택 문 앞으로 끌려오자, 어린 로던이 그 한가운데로 내려갔다. 개들이 자기에게 안겨 오는 어리광과, 흔들리는 꼬리에 얻어맞는 통과, 톰 무디의 목소리와 채찍에도 좀처럼 가라앉지 않는 그 개들끼리의 으르렁거림에 신이 나면서도 반쯤은 겁을 먹은 채였다."]], [["Meanwhile, Sir Huddlestone has hoisted himself unwieldily on the Nob: \"Let's try Sowster's Spinney, Tom,\"", "그러는 동안 허들스턴 경은 굼뜬 몸을 끌어 노브 위에 올라탔다. \"소스터의 잡목 숲을 한번 둘러보세, 톰.\""], ["says the Baronet, \"Farmer Mangle tells me there are two foxes in it.\"", "준남작이 말했다. \"맹글 농부 말로는 거기에 여우가 두 마리 있다더군.\""], ["Tom blows his horn and trots off, followed by the pack, by the whips, by the young gents from Winchester, by the farmers of the neighbourhood, by the labourers of the parish on foot, with whom the day is a great holiday, Sir Huddlestone bringing up the rear with Colonel Crawley, and the whole cortege disappears down the avenue.", "톰이 뿔나팔을 불며 잰걸음으로 떠나자, 그 뒤를 사냥개 떼가, 몰이꾼들이, 윈체스터에서 온 젊은 신사들이, 인근의 농부들이, 그리고 이날을 큰 축제로 여기는 교구의 일꾼들이 걸어서 뒤따랐다. 허들스턴 경은 크롤리 대령과 함께 행렬의 맨 뒤를 맡았고, 이 모든 행렬은 가도 저편으로 사라져 갔다."]], [["The Reverend Bute Crawley (who has been too modest to appear at the public meet before his nephew's windows), whom Tom Moody remembers forty years back a slender divine riding the wildest horses, jumping the widest brooks, and larking over the newest gates in the country--his Reverence, we say, happens to trot out from the Rectory Lane on his powerful black horse just as Sir Huddlestone passes; he joins the worthy Baronet.", "뷰트 크롤리 목사는(조카의 창문 앞에서 열리는 공개 집결에 모습을 드러내기에는 너무 겸손했다) 톰 무디가 사십 년 전을 기억하기로, 가장 사나운 말을 타고 가장 넓은 개울을 뛰어넘고 고장에서 가장 새로 세운 울타리들을 신나게 넘나들던 호리호리한 신학자였다. 우리가 이야기하는 그 예하께서는, 마침 허들스턴 경이 지나갈 무렵 그 힘센 검은 말에 올라타 목사관 골목에서 잰걸음으로 나오던 참이었다."], ["Hounds and horsemen disappear, and little Rawdon remains on the doorsteps, wondering and happy.", "그는 그 훌륭한 준남작과 합류했다. 사냥개들과 기마 행렬이 사라지자, 어린 로던은 경이로움과 행복에 젖은 채 문간 계단에 남았다."]], [["During the progress of this memorable holiday, little Rawdon, if he had got no special liking for his uncle, always awful and cold and locked up in his study, plunged in justice-business and surrounded by bailiffs and farmers--has gained the good graces of his married and maiden aunts, of the two little folks of the Hall, and of Jim of the Rectory, whom Sir Pitt is encouraging to pay his addresses to one of the young ladies, with an understanding doubtless that he shall be presented to the living when it shall be vacated by his fox-hunting old sire.", "이 기억할 만한 휴일이 이어지는 동안, 어린 로던은 늘 위압적이고 냉랭하며 서재에 틀어박혀 재판 업무에 파묻혀 집행관과 농부들에게 둘러싸여 있는 백부에게는 딱히 정을 붙이지 못했지만, 결혼한 숙모와 시집 안 간 숙모들의, 저택의 두 꼬마의, 그리고 목사관의 짐의 호감을 사는 데는 성공했다. 피트 경은 짐이 젊은 숙녀들 가운데 하나에게 구애하도록 부추기고 있었는데, 거기에는 두말할 것 없이 여우 사냥이나 즐기는 그의 늙은 아비가 비우게 될 성직록을 짐에게 물려주겠다는 양해가 깔려 있었다."], ["Jim has given up that sport himself and confines himself to a little harmless duck- or snipe-shooting, or a little quiet trifling with the rats during the Christmas holidays, after which he will return to the University and try and not be plucked, once more.", "짐은 그 사냥을 이미 그만두고, 크리스마스 휴가 동안에는 그저 무해한 오리나 도요새 사냥, 혹은 쥐를 데리고 하는 조용한 소일거리에 만족하고 있었다. 그러고 나면 그는 대학으로 돌아가 다시 한번 낙제하지 않으려 애쓸 참이었다."], ["He has already eschewed green coats, red neckcloths, and other worldly ornaments, and is preparing himself for a change in his condition.", "그는 이미 초록 상의며 빨간 목도리며 그 밖의 세속적 치장을 끊어 버리고, 처지의 변화에 대비해 자신을 가다듬고 있었다."], ["In this cheap and thrifty way Sir Pitt tries to pay off his debt to his family.", "이런 값싸고 알뜰한 방식으로 피트 경은 가문에 진 빚을 갚으려 했던 것이다."]], [["Also before this merry Christmas was over, the Baronet had screwed up courage enough to give his brother another draft on his bankers, and for no less a sum than a hundred pounds, an act which caused Sir Pitt cruel pangs at first, but which made him glow afterwards to think himself one of the most generous of men.", "또한 이 즐거운 크리스마스가 끝나기 전에, 준남작은 동생에게 거래 은행 앞으로 또 한 장의 어음을 끊어 줄 만큼 용기를 그러모았는데, 그 액수가 무려 백 파운드나 되었다. 이 일로 피트 경은 처음에는 살을 에는 듯한 고통을 느꼈으나, 나중에는 자신을 더없이 너그러운 사람 가운데 하나로 여기며 흐뭇해 마지않았다."], ["Rawdon and his son went away with the utmost heaviness of heart.", "로던과 그 아들은 더없이 무거운 마음으로 떠났다."], ["Becky and the ladies parted with some alacrity, however, and our friend returned to London to commence those avocations with which we find her occupied when this chapter begins.", "그러나 베키와 숙녀들은 제법 후련하게 작별했다. 그리고 우리의 벗은 런던으로 돌아가, 이 장이 시작될 때 그녀가 몰두해 있던 그 소일거리에 다시 착수했다."], ["Under her care the Crawley House in Great Gaunt Street was quite rejuvenescent and ready for the reception of Sir Pitt and his family, when the Baronet came to London to attend his duties in Parliament and to assume that position in the country for which his vast genius fitted him.", "그녀의 보살핌 아래 그레이트곤트 거리의 크롤리 저택은 완전히 새 단장을 하여, 준남작이 의회의 직무를 수행하고 그 막대한 천재성에 걸맞은 지위를 이 나라에서 차지하고자 런던에 올라올 때, 피트 경과 그 가족을 맞이할 채비를 갖추었다."]], [["For the first session, this profound dissembler hid his projects and never opened his lips but to present a petition from Mudbury.", "첫 회기 동안 이 깊숙한 위장의 명수는 자기 계획을 감추고, 머드버리의 청원을 제출할 때 말고는 입을 떼는 법이 없었다."], ["But he attended assiduously in his place and learned thoroughly the routine and business of the House.", "그러나 그는 자기 자리에 부지런히 출석하여 의회의 관례와 업무를 철저히 익혔다."], ["At home he gave himself up to the perusal of Blue Books, to the alarm and wonder of Lady Jane, who thought he was killing himself by late hours and intense application.", "집에서는 청서 정독에 몰두했는데, 늦은 시각까지 일하고 지나치게 골몰하느라 제 몸을 상하게 하는 것이 아닌가 하여 제인 부인이 놀라워하며 걱정할 정도였다."], ["And he made acquaintance with the ministers, and the chiefs of his party, determining to rank as one of them before many years were over.", "그리고 그는 장관들이며 당의 수뇌들과 친분을 맺어, 여러 해가 가기 전에 자신도 그들 가운데 하나로 손꼽히겠다고 단단히 마음먹었다."]], [["Lady Jane's sweetness and kindness had inspired Rebecca with such a contempt for her ladyship as the little woman found no small difficulty in concealing.", "제인 부인의 상냥함과 친절함은 레베카에게 그 부인에 대한 경멸을 불러일으켰는데, 그 작은 부인은 그것을 감추기가 여간 어렵지 않았다."], ["That sort of goodness and simplicity which Lady Jane possessed annoyed our friend Becky, and it was impossible for her at times not to show, or to let the other divine, her scorn.", "제인 부인이 지닌 그런 종류의 선량함과 천진함이 우리의 벗 베키를 짜증나게 했고, 그녀로서는 이따금 그 경멸을 드러내거나 상대가 눈치채게 만들지 않을 수 없었던 것이다."], ["Her presence, too, rendered Lady Jane uneasy.", "게다가 그녀가 곁에 있으면 제인 부인은 마음이 불편해졌다."], ["Her husband talked constantly with Becky.", "남편이 끊임없이 베키와 이야기를 나누었기 때문이다."], ["Signs of intelligence seemed to pass between them, and Pitt spoke with her on subjects on which he never thought of discoursing with Lady Jane.", "둘 사이에는 알아듣는다는 눈짓이 오가는 듯했고, 피트는 제인 부인과는 함께 논할 생각조차 하지 않던 화제들을 베키와는 논했다."], ["The latter did not understand them, to be sure, but it was mortifying to remain silent; still more mortifying to know that you had nothing to say, and hear that little audacious Mrs.", "물론 제인 부인은 그런 이야기를 알아듣지 못했지만, 잠자코 앉아 있는 것은 굴욕적이었다."], ["Rawdon dashing on from subject to subject, with a word for every man, and a joke always pat; and to sit in one's own house alone, by the fireside, and watching all the men round your rival.", "더욱 굴욕적인 것은 자기가 할 말이 하나도 없음을 깨닫는 것이요, 그 자그마한 당돌한 로던 부인이 이 화제에서 저 화제로 거침없이 내달리며 모든 사람에게 한마디씩 건네고 늘 때맞춰 농담을 던지는 것을 듣는 것이요, 제 집 안에서 홀로 난롯가에 앉아 자기 연적 주위로 모여드는 남자들을 지켜보는 것이었다."]], [["In the country, when Lady Jane was telling stories to the children, who clustered about her knees (little Rawdon into the bargain, who was very fond of her), and Becky came into the room, sneering with green scornful eyes, poor Lady Jane grew silent under those baleful glances.", "시골에서 제인 부인이 무릎 둘레로 모여든 아이들에게(꼬마 로던도 한몫 끼었으니, 그 아이는 부인을 무척 따랐다) 이야기를 들려주고 있을 때, 베키가 초록빛 경멸의 눈을 번득이며 방으로 들어오면, 가엾은 제인 부인은 그 악의 어린 눈초리 아래 입을 다물어 버렸다."], ["Her simple little fancies shrank away tremulously, as fairies in the story-books, before a superior bad angel.", "그녀의 소박하고 자그마한 상상은, 동화책 속 요정들이 자기보다 우월한 사악한 천사 앞에서 그러하듯 부들부들 떨며 움츠러들었다."], ["She could not go on, although Rebecca, with the smallest inflection of sarcasm in her voice, besought her to continue that charming story.", "레베카가 목소리에 아주 살짝 비꼼을 실어 그 매혹적인 이야기를 계속해 달라고 청해도, 그녀는 도무지 이어 갈 수가 없었다."], ["And on her side gentle thoughts and simple pleasures were odious to Mrs.", "한편 베키 부인 쪽에서는, 다정한 마음씨와 소박한 즐거움이 그저 가증스러울 따름이었다. 그것들은 그녀와 어울리지 않았다."], ["Becky; they discorded with her; she hated people for liking them; she spurned children and children-lovers.", "그녀는 그런 것을 좋아하는 사람들을 미워했다. 그녀는 아이들도, 아이를 사랑하는 이들도 경멸했다."], ["\"I have no taste for bread and butter,\"", "\"나는 버터 바른 빵 따위에는 취미가 없답니다.\""], ["she would say, when caricaturing Lady Jane and her ways to my Lord Steyne.", "그녀는 스타인 경에게 제인 부인과 그 행실을 우스꽝스럽게 흉내 내며 그렇게 말하곤 했다."]], [["\"No more has a certain person for holy water,\"", "\"성수에 취미가 없는 어떤 사람도 마찬가지지.\""], ["his lordship replied with a bow and a grin and a great jarring laugh afterwards.", "경께서는 절을 하고 히죽 웃으며 대꾸하더니, 이내 귀에 거슬리는 큰 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["So these two ladies did not see much of each other except upon those occasions when the younger brother's wife, having an object to gain from the other, frequented her.", "그리하여 이 두 부인은, 동생의 아내가 형수에게서 얻어 낼 무언가가 있어 드나들 때를 제외하고는 서로 얼굴을 마주하는 일이 별로 없었다. 그들은 부지런히 \"내 사랑\""], ["They my-loved and my-deared each other assiduously, but kept apart generally, whereas Sir Pitt, in the midst of his multiplied avocations, found daily time to see his sister-in-law.", "\"내 귀여운 이\"를 주고받았지만 대체로 멀찍이 떨어져 지냈다. 반면 피트 경은 그 갖가지 분주한 일거리 한복판에서도 날마다 제수를 만날 짬을 냈다."]], [["On the occasion of his first Speaker's dinner, Sir Pitt took the opportunity of appearing before his sister-in-law in his uniform--that old diplomatic suit which he had worn when attache to the Pumpernickel legation.", "첫 의장 만찬이 있던 날, 피트 경은 제수 앞에 자기 정복을 차려입고 나설 기회를 잡았다. 그것은 그가 펌퍼니클 공사관의 수행원으로 있을 때 입었던 그 낡은 외교관 예복이었다."]], [["Becky complimented him upon that dress and admired him almost as much as his own wife and children, to whom he displayed himself before he set out.", "베키는 그 차림새를 칭찬하며, 그가 집을 나서기 전에 제 모습을 보여 준 그의 아내와 아이들 못지않게 그를 우러러보았다."], ["She said that it was only the thoroughbred gentleman who could wear the Court suit with advantage: it was only your men of ancient race whom the culotte courte became.", "그녀는 궁정 예복을 멋들어지게 차려입을 수 있는 것은 오로지 순종 혈통의 신사뿐이며, 짧은 반바지가 어울리는 것은 오로지 유서 깊은 가문의 사내뿐이라고 했다."], ["Pitt looked down with complacency at his legs, which had not, in truth, much more symmetry or swell than the lean Court sword which dangled by his side--looked down at his legs, and thought in his heart that he was killing.", "피트는 흐뭇한 마음으로 자기 다리를 내려다보았는데, 사실 그 다리는 옆구리에 달랑거리는 가느다란 궁정용 검보다 균형도 볼록함도 그리 나을 게 없었다. 그래도 그는 제 다리를 내려다보며, 속으로 자신이 죽여주게 멋지다고 생각했다."]], [["When he was gone, Mrs. Becky made a caricature of his figure, which she showed to Lord Steyne when he arrived.", "그가 떠나자 베키 부인은 그의 모습을 우스꽝스럽게 그린 그림을 한 장 그려서, 스타인 경이 당도하자 그에게 보여 주었다."], ["His lordship carried off the sketch, delighted with the accuracy of the resemblance.", "경께서는 그 닮은 정도가 어찌나 정확한지 흡족해하며 그 그림을 가져갔다."], ["He had done Sir Pitt Crawley the honour to meet him at Mrs. Becky's house and had been most gracious to the new Baronet and member.", "그는 베키 부인의 집에서 피트 크롤리 경을 만나는 영광을 베풀었고, 이 새 준남작이자 의원에게 더없이 정중하게 대했다."], ["Pitt was struck too by the deference with which the great Peer treated his sister-in-law, by her ease and sprightliness in the conversation, and by the delight with which the other men of the party listened to her talk.", "피트 또한 그 위대한 귀족이 자기 제수를 대하는 정중함과, 대화에서 보이는 그녀의 여유롭고 발랄한 태도며, 자리에 함께한 다른 남자들이 그녀의 이야기에 귀 기울이는 그 즐거워하는 기색에 적잖이 놀랐다."], ["Lord Steyne made no doubt but that the Baronet had only commenced his career in public life, and expected rather anxiously to hear him as an orator; as they were neighbours (for Great Gaunt Street leads into Gaunt Square, whereof Gaunt House, as everybody knows, forms one side) my lord hoped that as soon as Lady Steyne arrived in London she would have the honour of making the acquaintance of Lady Crawley.", "스타인 경은 준남작이 이제 막 공적 생활의 첫발을 내디뎠을 뿐임을 의심치 않았고, 다소 조바심을 내며 그가 연설가로 활약하는 모습을 보기를 고대했다. 그들은 이웃지간이었으므로(그레이트곤트 거리는 곤트 광장으로 이어지는데, 누구나 알다시피 곤트 저택은 그 광장의 한 면을 이루고 있다), 경께서는 스타인 부인이 런던에 도착하는 대로 크롤리 부인과 안면을 트는 영광을 누리기를 바랐다."], ["He left a card upon his neighbour in the course of a day or two, having never thought fit to notice his predecessor, though they had lived near each other for near a century past.", "그는 하루 이틀 사이에 이웃에게 명함을 두고 갔는데, 전임자와는 거의 백 년 가까이 가까이 살았으면서도 한 번도 아는 체할 생각조차 하지 않았던 것이다."]], [["In the midst of these intrigues and fine parties and wise and brilliant personages Rawdon felt himself more and more isolated every day.", "이런 책략과 화려한 연회와 현명하고 빛나는 인사들 한복판에서, 로던은 날이 갈수록 점점 더 고립되어 가는 자신을 느꼈다."], ["He was allowed to go to the club more; to dine abroad with bachelor friends; to come and go when he liked, without any questions being asked.", "이제 그는 클럽에 더 자주 드나들고, 미혼인 벗들과 밖에서 만찬을 들고, 아무런 추궁도 받지 않은 채 마음대로 오가도록 허락받았다."], ["And he and Rawdon the younger many a time would walk to Gaunt Street and sit with the lady and the children there while Sir Pitt was closeted with Rebecca, on his way to the House, or on his return from it.", "그는 어린 로던과 함께 곤트 거리로 걸어가, 피트 경이 의회로 가는 길에 혹은 의회에서 돌아오는 길에 레베카와 단둘이 틀어박혀 있는 동안, 거기서 그 부인과 아이들과 더불어 앉아 있곤 했다."]], [["The ex-Colonel would sit for hours in his brother's house very silent, and thinking and doing as little as possible.", "전직 대령은 형의 집에서 입을 꾹 다문 채 몇 시간이고 앉아, 되도록 생각도 행동도 하지 않으려 했다."], ["He was glad to be employed of an errand; to go and make inquiries about a horse or a servant, or to carve the roast mutton for the dinner of the children.", "그는 심부름이라도 시키면 반가워했다. 말이나 하인에 관해 알아보러 가거나, 아이들 저녁 식사로 양고기 구이를 썰어 주는 일 따위 말이다."], ["He was beat and cowed into laziness and submission.", "그는 두들겨 맞고 기가 꺾여 게으름과 순종에 길들었다."], ["Delilah had imprisoned him and cut his hair off, too.", "델릴라가 그를 가두고 머리카락까지 잘라 버린 것이다."], ["The bold and reckless young blood of ten-years back was subjugated and was turned into a torpid, submissive, middle-aged, stout gentleman.", "십 년 전의 그 대담하고 무모한 젊은 혈기는 정복되어, 둔하고 고분고분한 중년의 뚱뚱한 신사로 변해 버렸다."]], [["And poor Lady Jane was aware that Rebecca had captivated her husband, although she and Mrs.", "그리고 가엾은 제인 부인은, 자기와 로던 부인이 만나는 날마다 \"내 귀여운 이\" \"내 사랑\""], ["Rawdon my-deared and my-loved each other every day they met.", "하며 서로를 불러 댔건만, 레베카가 자기 남편을 사로잡았다는 것을 알고 있었다."]], [["Struggles and Trials\nOur friends at Brompton were meanwhile passing their Christmas after their fashion and in a manner by no means too cheerful.", "고난과 시련 한편 브롬프턴에 있는 우리의 벗들은 그동안 그들 나름의 방식으로, 그리고 결코 그리 유쾌하다고는 할 수 없는 식으로 성탄절을 보내고 있었다."]], [["Out of the hundred pounds a year, which was about the amount of her income, the Widow Osborne had been in the habit of giving up nearly three-fourths to her father and mother, for the expenses of herself and her little boy.", "연간 백 파운드 남짓이 수입의 거의 전부였는데, 미망인이 된 오즈번 부인은 그 가운데 거의 사분의 삼을 자신과 어린 아들의 생활비 명목으로 친정 부모에게 내놓는 것이 버릇이 되어 있었다."], ["With 120l. more, supplied by Jos, this family of four people, attended by a single Irish servant who also did for Clapp and his wife, might manage to live in decent comfort through the year, and hold up their heads yet, and be able to give a friend a dish of tea still, after the storms and disappointments of their early life.", "거기에 조스가 보태는 백이십 파운드를 더하면, 클랩 부부의 시중까지 함께 드는 아일랜드인 하녀 하나의 도움을 받아 가며 이 네 식구는 그럭저럭 한 해를 점잖고 안락하게 꾸려 나갈 수 있었고, 아직은 고개를 들고 살 수 있었으며, 젊은 날의 풍파와 좌절을 겪고도 여전히 벗에게 차 한 잔쯤은 대접할 수 있는 형편이었다."], ["Sedley still maintained his ascendency over the family of Mr. Clapp, his ex-clerk.", "세들리는 옛 사무원이던 클랩 씨의 가족에게 여전히 그 위세를 유지하고 있었다."], ["Clapp remembered the time when, sitting on the edge of the chair, he tossed off a bumper to the health of \"Mrs. S--, Miss Emmy, and Mr.", "클랩은 러셀 광장에 자리한 그 상인의 풍성한 식탁에서 의자 끝에 걸터앉아 \"인도에 계신 세들리 마님, 에미 아가씨, 조지프 도련님\"의 건강을 위해 잔을 단숨에 비우던 시절을 기억했다."], ["Joseph in India,\" at the merchant's rich table in Russell Square.", "세월은 이 정직한 사무원의 가슴속에서 그 추억의 광휘를 한층 더 부풀려 놓았다."], ["Time magnified the splendour of those recollections in the honest clerk's bosom. Whenever he came up from the kitchen-parlour to the drawing-room and partook of tea or gin-and-water with Mr.", "부엌 옆 거실에서 응접실로 올라와 세들리 씨와 차나 진을 탄 물을 함께 마실 때면, 그는 늘 \"이런 건 한때 나리께서 익숙하시던 게 아니지요\"라고 말하면서, 그 집안이 더없이 번성하던 시절에 그랬던 것처럼 엄숙하고 경건하게 숙녀분들의 건강을 위해 잔을 들었다."], ["Sedley, he would say, \"This was not what you was accustomed to once, sir,\" and as gravely and reverentially drink the health of the ladies as he had done in the days of their utmost prosperity.", "그는 에밀리아 아가씨의 연주가 여태껏 연주된 그 어떤 것보다 신성한 음악이라 여겼고, 그녀를 더없이 고귀한 숙녀라 믿었다."], ["He thought Miss 'Melia's playing the divinest music ever performed, and her the finest lady. He never would sit down before Sedley at the club even, nor would he have that gentleman's character abused by any member of the society.", "그는 클럽에서조차 세들리 앞에서는 결코 먼저 앉는 법이 없었고, 그 신사의 인품이 그 모임의 어느 회원에게라도 모욕당하는 것을 두고 보지 못했다."], ["He had seen the first men in London shaking hands with Mr.", "그는 런던의 일류 인사들이 세들리 씨와 악수를 나누는 광경을 직접 목격한 사람이었다."], ["S--; he said, \"He'd known him in times when Rothschild might be seen on 'Change with him any day, and he owed him personally everythink.\"", "그는 \"로스차일드가 거래소에서 그분과 언제든 어울리던 시절부터 그분을 알아 왔고, 자기가 가진 모든 게 다 그분 덕\"이라고 말했다."]], [["Clapp, with the best of characters and handwritings, had been able very soon after his master's disaster to find other employment for himself. \"Such a little fish as me can swim in any bucket,\"", "클랩은 더없이 훌륭한 인품과 필체를 지닌 덕분에, 주인이 파산한 직후 곧바로 다른 일자리를 구할 수 있었다."], ["he used to remark, and a member of the house from which old Sedley had seceded was very glad to make use of Mr. Clapp's services and to reward them with a comfortable salary.", "\"나 같은 송사리는 어느 물통에서든 헤엄칠 수 있죠\"라고 그는 입버릇처럼 말하곤 했는데, 노년의 세들리가 떨어져 나온 그 상회의 한 회원이 클랩 씨의 일손을 기꺼이 빌려 쓰고 넉넉한 봉급으로 보답해 준 것이었다."], ["In fine, all Sedley's wealthy friends had dropped off one by one, and this poor ex-dependent still remained faithfully attached to him.", "요컨대 세들리의 부유한 벗들은 하나둘 떨어져 나갔건만, 가난한 옛 식객인 이 사람만은 여전히 충직하게 그의 곁을 지킨 셈이었다."]], [["Out of the small residue of her income which Amelia kept back for herself, the widow had need of all the thrift and care possible in order to enable her to keep her darling boy dressed in such a manner as became George Osborne's son, and to defray the expenses of the little school to which, after much misgiving and reluctance and many secret pangs and fears on her own part, she had been induced to send the lad.", "애밀리아가 자신을 위해 따로 떼어 둔 얼마 안 되는 수입의 나머지에서, 미망인은 사랑하는 아들에게 조지 오즈번의 아들에 걸맞은 옷차림을 시키고, 또 자신으로서는 숱한 망설임과 주저 끝에, 또 남몰래 가슴 졸이고 두려워한 끝에 마침내 아들을 보내기로 결심한 그 자그마한 학교의 비용을 대기 위해, 동원할 수 있는 모든 절약과 알뜰함이 필요했다."], ["She had sat up of nights conning lessons and spelling over crabbed grammars and geography books in order to teach them to Georgy.", "그녀는 조지에게 가르쳐 주려고 밤을 새워 가며 수업 내용을 익히고, 까다로운 문법책과 지리책을 한 자 한 자 읽어 나갔다."], ["She had worked even at the Latin accidence, fondly hoping that she might be capable of instructing him in that language.", "그 언어로 아이를 가르칠 수 있을지도 모른다는 가냘픈 희망을 품고, 라틴어 어형 변화까지 공부했다."], ["To part with him all day, to send him out to the mercy of a schoolmaster's cane and his schoolfellows' roughness, was almost like weaning him over again to that weak mother, so tremulous and full of sensibility.", "종일토록 아이와 떨어져 지내고, 교사의 회초리와 학교 친구들의 거친 손길에 아이를 내맡기는 일은, 떨림 많고 감수성 가득한 그 여린 어머니에게는 마치 아이를 한 번 더 젖떼는 일이나 다름없었다."], ["He, for his part, rushed off to the school with the utmost happiness.", "그러나 아이는 아이대로 더없이 행복해하며 학교로 달려갔다."], ["He was longing for the change.", "그 변화를 손꼽아 기다리던 터였다."], ["That childish gladness wounded his mother, who was herself so grieved to part with him.", "그 천진한 기쁨이 어머니에게는 상처가 되었으니, 정작 그녀 자신은 아이와 떨어지는 일이 그토록 서글펐던 것이다."], ["She would rather have had him more sorry, she thought, and then was deeply repentant within herself for daring to be so selfish as to wish her own son to be unhappy.", "아이가 좀 더 슬퍼해 주었으면 싶다가도, 자기 아들이 불행하기를 바랄 만큼 이기적인 마음을 감히 품었다는 데에 속으로 깊이 뉘우치는 것이었다."]], [["Georgy made great progress in the school, which was kept by a friend of his mother's constant admirer, the Rev. Mr.", "조지는 그 학교에서 큰 진전을 보였는데, 그 학교는 그녀를 늘 흠모하는 비니 목사의 한 벗이 운영하는 곳이었다."], ["Binny. He brought home numberless prizes and testimonials of ability.", "아이는 수도 없이 많은 상과 능력을 증명하는 표창장을 집으로 가져왔다."], ["He told his mother countless stories every night about his school-companions: and what a fine fellow Lyons was, and what a sneak Sniffin was, and how Steel's father actually supplied the meat for the establishment, whereas Golding's mother came in a carriage to fetch him every Saturday, and how Neat had straps to his trowsers--might he have straps?--and how Bull Major was so strong (though only in Eutropius) that it was believed he could lick the Usher, Mr.", "매일 밤 학교 친구들 이야기를 어머니에게 끝도 없이 들려주었다. 라이언스는 얼마나 멋진 녀석인지, 스니핀은 또 얼마나 비열한 놈인지, 스틸의 아버지는 실제로 그 학교에 고기를 대 주는 사람이고, 골딩의 어머니는 매주 토요일마다 마차를 타고 아들을 데리러 온다는 것, 니트는 바지에 가죽끈을 달았다는데 자기도 가죽끈을 달면 안 되겠느냐는 것, 그리고 불 메이저는 (비록 에우트로피우스 책에서만 그렇긴 하나) 어찌나 힘이 센지 조교인 워드 선생까지도 때려눕힐 수 있을 거라고들 한다는 것이었다."], ["Ward, himself. So Amelia learned to know every one of the boys in that school as well as Georgy himself, and of nights she used to help him in his exercises and puzzle her little head over his lessons as eagerly as if she was herself going in the morning into the presence of the master.", "그리하여 애밀리아는 그 학교의 아이들을 하나하나, 조지 자신이 아는 만큼이나 속속들이 알게 되었고, 밤이면 아이의 숙제를 거들어 주면서, 마치 자기가 아침에 직접 선생 앞으로 나아가기라도 할 것처럼 그 작은 머리로 아이의 수업 내용을 골똘히 궁리하는 것이었다."], ["Once, after a certain combat with Master Smith, George came home to his mother with a black eye, and bragged prodigiously to his parent and his delighted old grandfather about his valour in the fight, in which, if the truth was known he did not behave with particular heroism, and in which he decidedly had the worst.", "한번은 스미스라는 아이와 한판 붙고 나서, 조지가 눈에 멍이 든 채 어머니에게 돌아와서는, 그 싸움에서 보인 자기 용맹을 어머니와 기뻐 어쩔 줄 모르는 늙은 외할아버지에게 엄청나게 떠벌렸다. 사실대로 말하자면 그 싸움에서 아이가 특별히 영웅적으로 처신한 것도 아니었고, 오히려 확실히 더 호되게 당한 쪽이었지만 말이다."], ["But Amelia has never forgiven that Smith to this day, though he is now a peaceful apothecary near Leicester Square.", "그래도 애밀리아는 오늘날까지 그 스미스를 결코 용서하지 않았으니, 그 아이가 지금은 레스터 광장 근처에서 점잖게 약방을 꾸리고 있는데도 그러했다."]], [["In these quiet labours and harmless cares the gentle widow's life was passing away, a silver hair or two marking the progress of time on her head and a line deepening ever so little on her fair forehead.", "이런 조용한 수고와 해될 것 없는 근심 속에서 이 온순한 미망인의 삶은 흘러가고 있었고, 은빛 머리카락이 한두 올 돋아 그 머리 위에 세월의 자취를 새겼으며, 그 고운 이마에는 주름 한 줄이 아주 조금씩 깊어 갔다."], ["She used to smile at these marks of time.", "그녀는 이런 세월의 흔적들을 보며 미소 짓곤 했다."], ["\"What matters it,\"", "\"이런 게 무슨 대수겠어요?\""], ["she asked, \"For an old woman like me?\"", "그녀는 물었다. \"나처럼 늙은 여자한테?\""], ["All she hoped for was to live to see her son great, famous, and glorious, as he deserved to be.", "그녀가 바라는 것이라곤, 아들이 마땅히 받아야 할 만큼 위대하고, 유명하고, 영광스러운 사람이 되는 것을 살아서 보는 일뿐이었다."], ["She kept his copy-books, his drawings, and compositions, and showed them about in her little circle as if they were miracles of genius.", "그녀는 아이의 습자책과 그림과 작문을 고이 간직했다가, 마치 천재의 기적이라도 되는 양 자기 작은 모임 사람들에게 두루 보여 주었다."], ["She confided some of these specimens to Miss Dobbin, to show them to Miss Osborne, George's aunt, to show them to Mr. Osborne himself--to make that old man repent of his cruelty and ill feeling towards him who was gone.", "그녀는 그런 표본 몇 가지를 도빈 양에게 맡겨, 조지의 고모인 오즈번 양에게 보여 주게 하고, 또 오즈번 씨 본인에게까지 보여 주어, 그 노인이 세상을 떠난 이에게 품었던 잔인함과 모진 감정을 뉘우치도록 만들고자 했다."], ["All her husband's faults and foibles she had buried in the grave with him: she only remembered the lover, who had married her at all sacrifices, the noble husband, so brave and beautiful, in whose arms she had hung on the morning when he had gone away to fight, and die gloriously for his king.", "남편의 온갖 결점과 약점은 모조리 그와 함께 무덤에 묻어 버린 그녀였다. 오로지 온갖 희생을 무릅쓰고 자신과 결혼해 준 그 연인을, 그토록 용맹하고 아름답던 그 고귀한 남편을, 임금을 위해 싸우고 영광스럽게 죽으러 떠나던 그 아침에 자신이 매달려 안겼던 그 품을 기억할 뿐이었다."], ["From heaven the hero must be smiling down upon that paragon of a boy whom he had left to comfort and console her.", "천국에서 그 영웅은 분명, 그녀를 위로하고 다독이라고 남겨 두고 간 그 더없이 빼어난 아들을 내려다보며 미소 짓고 있으리라."], ["We have seen how one of George's grandfathers (Mr. Osborne), in his easy chair in Russell Square, daily grew more violent and moody, and how his daughter, with her fine carriage, and her fine horses, and her name on half the public charity-lists of the town, was a lonely, miserable, persecuted old maid.", "조지의 외할아버지 가운데 한 분(오즈번 씨)이 러셀 광장의 안락의자에 앉은 채 날이 갈수록 더욱 사납고 침울해져 갔다는 것, 그리고 그 딸이 으리으리한 마차와 으리으리한 말들을 거느리고 도시의 공공 자선 명부 절반에 제 이름을 올려 두고도 외롭고 비참하며 핍박받는 노처녀였다는 것은 우리가 이미 본 바와 같다."], ["She thought again and again of the beautiful little boy, her brother's son, whom she had seen.", "그녀는 자기가 본 그 아름다운 어린 사내아이를, 오라비의 아들을 몇 번이고 거듭 떠올렸다."], ["She longed to be allowed to drive in the fine carriage to the house in which he lived, and she used to look out day after day as she took her solitary drive in the park, in hopes that she might see him.", "그 으리으리한 마차를 타고 아이가 사는 집까지 가 보아도 좋다는 허락을 받기를 간절히 바랐고, 공원에서 홀로 마차를 몰 때면 행여 아이를 볼 수 있을까 하는 희망에 날이면 날마다 밖을 살피곤 했다."], ["Her sister, the banker's lady, occasionally condescended to pay her old home and companion a visit in Russell Square.", "은행가의 부인인 그녀의 동생은 이따금 러셀 광장의 옛 집과 옛 길동무를 찾아오는 황송한 너그러움을 베풀었다."], ["She brought a couple of sickly children attended by a prim nurse, and in a faint genteel giggling tone cackled to her sister about her fine acquaintance, and how her little Frederick was the image of Lord Claud Lollypop and her sweet Maria had been noticed by the Baroness as they were driving in their donkey-chaise at Roehampton.", "그녀는 깔끔한 척하는 유모가 시중드는 병약한 아이 둘을 데려와서는, 고상한 척 힘없이 키득거리는 어조로 자기의 멋진 교제권에 대해 언니에게 재잘거렸다. 자기네 어린 프레더릭이 클로드 롤리팝 경을 빼다 박았다느니, 자기네 사랑스러운 마리아가 로햄프턴에서 당나귀 수레를 타고 가는데 남작 부인이 눈여겨보았다느니 하는 식이었다."], ["She urged her to make her papa do something for the darlings.", "그녀는 아빠한테 이 귀여운 아이들을 위해 뭐라도 해 주시도록 졸라 달라고 언니를 채근했다."], ["Frederick she had determined should go into the Guards; and if they made an elder son of him (and Mr.", "프레더릭은 근위대에 보내기로 작정했다는 것이었다."], ["Bullock was positively ruining and pinching himself to death to buy land), how was the darling girl to be provided for?", "그런데 만약 그 아이를 장남으로 삼는다면(불록 씨는 땅을 사들이느라 그야말로 자기 살을 깎고 졸라매며 죽을 지경이었다), 그 귀여운 딸아이는 무엇으로 살림 밑천을 마련해 주느냐는 것이었다."], ["\"I expect YOU, dear,\" Mrs.", "\"난 언니만 믿어요.\""], ["Bullock would say, \"for of course my share of our Papa's property must go to the head of the house, you know.", "불록 부인은 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"당연히 아빠 재산에서 내 몫은 우리 집안의 가장한테 돌아가야 하잖아, 그렇지?"], ["Dear Rhoda McMull will disengage the whole of the Castletoddy property as soon as poor dear Lord Castletoddy dies, who is quite epileptic; and little Macduff McMull will be Viscount Castletoddy.", "가엾고 사랑스러운 캐슬토디 경이 돌아가시면, 그분이 간질이 아주 심하시거든, 사랑하는 로다 맥멀이 캐슬토디 영지 전부를 풀어낼 거고, 어린 맥더프 맥멀이 캐슬토디 자작이 될 거예요."], ["Both the Mr. Bludyers of Mincing Lane have settled their fortunes on Fanny Bludyer's little boy.", "민싱 레인의 블러디어 형제분도 둘 다 패니 블러디어의 어린 아들한테 재산을 물려주기로 했고요."], ["My darling Frederick must positively be an eldest son; and--and do ask Papa to bring us back his account in Lombard Street, will you, dear?", "우리 사랑스러운 프레더릭은 무슨 일이 있어도 꼭 장남이 돼야 해요. 그리고, 그리고 아빠한테 롬바드 거리에 맡긴 계좌를 우리 쪽으로 도로 옮겨 달라고 부탁 좀 해 줘요, 언니."], ["It doesn't look well, his going to Stumpy and Rowdy's.\"", "아빠가 스텀피 앤드 라우디한테 거래를 맡기시는 게 영 보기 안 좋잖아.\""], ["After which kind of speeches, in which fashion and the main chance were blended together, and after a kiss, which was like the contact of an oyster--Mrs. Frederick Bullock would gather her starched nurslings and simper back into her carriage.", "유행과 잇속이 한데 뒤섞인 이런 따위의 연설을 늘어놓고는, 굴 껍데기에 닿는 듯한 입맞춤을 나눈 뒤, 프레더릭 불록 부인은 풀 먹인 옷을 입은 제 새끼들을 챙겨 거드름스럽게 다시 마차에 올라타는 것이었다."]], [["Every visit which this leader of ton paid to her family was more unlucky for her.", "이 사교계의 선도자가 친정을 찾아올 때마다 그것은 그녀에게 더욱 불운한 일이 되었다."], ["Her father paid more money into Stumpy and Rowdy's.", "아버지는 스텀피 앤드 라우디에 더 많은 돈을 맡겼다."], ["Her patronage became more and more insufferable.", "그녀의 거들먹거리는 시혜는 갈수록 더 견딜 수 없는 것이 되어 갔다."], ["The poor widow in the little cottage at Brompton, guarding her treasure there, little knew how eagerly some people coveted it.", "브롬프턴의 작은 집에서 제 보물을 지키는 가난한 미망인은, 어떤 이들이 그것을 얼마나 간절히 탐내고 있는지 까맣게 몰랐던 것이다."]], [["On that night when Jane Osborne had told her father that she had seen his grandson, the old man had made her no reply, but he had shown no anger--and had bade her good-night on going himself to his room in rather a kindly voice.", "제인 오즈번이 아버지에게 손자를 보았노라고 말한 그날 밤, 노인은 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았으나, 화를 내지도 않았다. 그리고 자기 방으로 들어가면서 제법 다정한 목소리로 잘 자라는 인사를 건넸다."], ["And he must have meditated on what she said and have made some inquiries of the Dobbin family regarding her visit, for a fortnight after it took place, he asked her where was her little French watch and chain she used to wear?", "그는 분명 딸이 한 말을 곰곰이 곱씹고, 그 방문에 관해 도빈 가족에게 얼마간 알아보았던 듯했다. 그로부터 보름이 지난 어느 날, 그는 딸에게 평소 차고 다니던 그 작은 프랑스제 시계와 줄이 어디 있느냐고 물었던 것이다."]], [["\"I bought it with my money, sir,\"", "\"제 돈으로 산 거예요, 아버지.\""], ["she said in a great fright.", "그녀가 잔뜩 겁에 질려 대답했다."]], [["\"Go and order another like it, or a better if you can get it,\"", "\"가서 그것과 똑같은 걸로, 아니 더 좋은 걸 구할 수 있거든 더 좋은 걸로 하나 새로 맞추거라.\""], ["said the old gentleman and lapsed again into silence.", "노신사는 이렇게 말하고는 다시 침묵에 잠겼다."]], [["Of late the Misses Dobbin more than once repeated their entreaties to Amelia, to allow George to visit them.", "근래 들어 도빈 자매는 한 번 이상, 조지가 자기네 집을 방문하게 허락해 달라고 애밀리아에게 거듭 간청했다."], ["His aunt had shown her inclination; perhaps his grandfather himself, they hinted, might be disposed to be reconciled to him.", "아이의 고모가 그런 마음을 내비쳤다는 것이었다. 어쩌면 아이의 외할아버지 본인도 손자와 화해할 생각이 있을지 모른다고 그들은 넌지시 비쳤다."], ["Surely, Amelia could not refuse such advantageous chances for the boy.", "애밀리아가 아이에게 이토록 유리한 기회를 차마 마다할 수는 없는 노릇이었다."], ["Nor could she, but she acceded to their overtures with a very heavy and suspicious heart, was always uneasy during the child's absence from her, and welcomed him back as if he was rescued out of some danger.", "실제로 그녀도 마다하지 못했지만, 더없이 무겁고 미심쩍은 마음으로 그들의 제안을 받아들였고, 아이가 곁을 떠나 있는 동안에는 늘 안절부절못했으며, 마치 무슨 위험에서 구해 내기라도 한 듯 아이를 맞이하곤 했다. 아이는 돈과 장난감을 받아 왔는데, 미망인은 그것을 불안과 질투의 눈길로 바라보았다."], ["He brought back money and toys, at which the widow looked with alarm and jealousy; she asked him always if he had seen any gentleman--\"Only old Sir William, who drove him about in the four-wheeled chaise, and Mr.", "그녀는 늘 아이에게 어떤 신사를 만났느냐고 물었다. \"사륜마차에 태워 데리고 다니신 윌리엄 경 할아버지하고, 오후에 그 멋진 밤색 말을 타고 오신 도빈 아저씨밖에 없었어요."], ["Dobbin, who arrived on the beautiful bay horse in the afternoon--in the green coat and pink neck-cloth, with the gold-headed whip, who promised to show him the Tower of London and take him out with the Surrey hounds.\"", "초록색 외투에 분홍색 목도리를 두르고, 금 손잡이 채찍을 든 분 말이에요. 런던 탑을 구경시켜 주고 서리 사냥개들이랑 같이 사냥에 데려가 주겠다고 약속하셨어요.\""], ["At last, he said, \"There was an old gentleman, with thick eyebrows, and a broad hat, and large chain and seals.\"", "그러더니 마침내 아이가 말했다. \"눈썹이 짙고, 챙 넓은 모자를 쓰고, 큼직한 줄에 도장을 단 할아버지가 한 분 계셨어요.\""], ["He came one day as the coachman was lunging Georgy round the lawn on the gray pony.", "마부가 회색 조랑말에 조지를 태우고 잔디밭을 빙빙 돌리던 어느 날 그분이 오셨다는 것이었다."], ["\"He looked at me very much. He shook very much.", "\"그 할아버지가 절 아주 빤히 쳐다보셨어요."], ["I said 'My name is Norval'", "그리고 막 떠셨어요."], ["after dinner. My aunt began to cry.", "저녁 먹고 나서 제가 '내 이름은 노벌'을 외웠더니 고모가 울기 시작했어요."], ["She is always crying.\"", "고모는 늘 울어요.\""], ["Such was George's report on that night.", "그날 밤 조지가 전한 보고는 그러했다."]], [["Then Amelia knew that the boy had seen his grandfather; and looked out feverishly for a proposal which she was sure would follow, and which came, in fact, in a few days afterwards. Mr. Osborne formally offered to take the boy and make him heir to the fortune which he had intended that his father should inherit. He would make Mrs. George Osborne an allowance, such as to assure her a decent competency. If Mrs. George Osborne proposed to marry again, as Mr. O. heard was her intention, he would not withdraw that allowance. But it must be understood that the child would live entirely with his grandfather in Russell Square, or at whatever other place Mr. O. should select, and that he would be occasionally permitted to see Mrs. George Osborne at her own residence. This message was brought or read to her in a letter one day, when her mother was from home and her father absent as usual in the City.", "그제야 애밀리아는 아이가 외할아버지를 보았다는 것을 알았고, 분명히 뒤따라올 것이라 확신한 어떤 제안을 안달하며 기다렸는데, 과연 며칠 뒤에 그것이 들이닥쳤다. 오즈번 씨는 아이를 데려가, 본래 그 아비에게 물려주려 했던 재산의 상속자로 삼겠노라고 정식으로 제의했다. 조지 오즈번 부인에게는 점잖게 살아갈 만한 생활비를 보장할 만큼의 수당을 주겠다는 것이었다. 만약 조지 오즈번 부인이 다시 결혼할 생각이라면 — 오즈번 씨가 듣기로는 그것이 그녀의 뜻이라 했다 — 그래도 그 수당을 거두지는 않겠다고 했다. 다만 아이는 전적으로 러셀 광장의 외할아버지와 함께 살거나, 오즈번 씨가 정하는 다른 어떤 곳에서 살아야 하며, 이따금 조지 오즈번 부인의 거처로 가서 그녀를 만나는 것은 허락하겠다는 것을 분명히 해 두어야 한다고 했다. 이 전갈은 어느 날 편지에 담겨 그녀에게 전해졌는데, 마침 어머니는 집을 비우고 아버지는 여느 때처럼 시내에 나가 있을 때였다."]], [["She was never seen angry but twice or thrice in her life, and it was in one of these moods that Mr.", "그녀가 평생 화를 내는 모습을 보인 것은 두세 번에 지나지 않았는데, 오즈번 씨의 변호사는 그런 분위기에 휩싸인 그녀를 목도하는 행운을 누렸다."], ["Osborne's attorney had the fortune to behold her. She rose up trembling and flushing very much as soon as, after reading the letter, Mr.", "편지를 다 읽은 포 씨가 그것을 그녀에게 건네자마자, 그녀는 몸을 부들부들 떨며 얼굴이 시뻘게져서는 벌떡 일어나, 종이를 백 조각으로 찢어 발로 짓밟았다."], ["Poe handed it to her, and she tore the paper into a hundred fragments, which she trod on.", "\"내가 다시 결혼을 한다고요! 자식과 갈라서는 대가로 돈을 받는다고요!"], ["\"I marry again! I take money to part from my child!", "누가 감히 이런 걸 제안해서 날 모욕하는 거예요?"], ["Who dares insult me by proposing such a thing? Tell Mr.", "오즈번 씨한테 가서 이건 비겁한 편지라고 전하세요."], ["Osborne it is a cowardly letter, sir--a cowardly letter--I will not answer it.", "비겁한 편지라고요. 답장 따위는 안 하겠어요."], ["I wish you good morning, sir--and she bowed me out of the room like a tragedy Queen,\"", "좋은 아침 보내세요.\" \"그러더니 그녀는 비극 속의 여왕처럼 머리 숙여 인사하며 날 방에서 내보내더군요.\""], ["said the lawyer who told the story.", "이 이야기를 들려준 그 변호사의 말이었다."]], [["Her parents never remarked her agitation on that day, and she never told them of the interview.", "그날 그녀의 부모는 그녀의 동요를 전혀 눈치채지 못했고, 그녀도 그 면담에 대해 그들에게 일언반구 하지 않았다."], ["They had their own affairs to interest them, affairs which deeply interested this innocent and unconscious lady.", "그들에게는 따로 마음 쓸 일이 있었으니, 이 순진하고 아무것도 모르는 부인과도 깊이 얽힌 일이었다."], ["The old gentleman, her father, was always dabbling in speculation.", "그녀의 아버지인 노신사는 늘 투기에 손을 댔다."], ["We have seen how the wine company and the coal company had failed him.", "포도주 회사와 석탄 회사가 그를 어떻게 망쳐 놓았는지는 우리가 이미 본 바와 같다."], ["But, prowling about the City always eagerly and restlessly still, he lighted upon some other scheme, of which he thought so well that he embarked in it in spite of the remonstrances of Mr. Clapp, to whom indeed he never dared to tell how far he had engaged himself in it.", "그러나 여전히 안달하며 한시도 가만있지 못하고 시내를 어슬렁대던 그는, 또 다른 사업 하나에 눈독을 들였고, 어찌나 마음에 들었던지 클랩 씨의 만류에도 아랑곳없이 거기에 발을 들였는데, 사실 클랩 씨에게는 자기가 거기에 얼마나 깊이 발을 담갔는지 차마 털어놓을 엄두를 내지 못했다."], ["And as it was always Mr. Sedley's maxim not to talk about money matters before women, they had no inkling of the misfortunes that were in store for them until the unhappy old gentleman was forced to make gradual confessions.", "게다가 여자들 앞에서는 돈 문제를 입에 올리지 않는 것이 세들리 씨의 변함없는 신조였던 터라, 가족들은 불행한 노신사가 어쩔 수 없이 조금씩 실토하기 전까지는 자기들에게 닥쳐올 불운을 까맣게 모르고 있었다."]], [["The bills of the little household, which had been settled weekly, first fell into arrear.", "매주 정산하던 이 작은 살림의 청구서들이 먼저 밀리기 시작했다."], ["The remittances had not arrived from India, Mr. Sedley told his wife with a disturbed face.", "인도에서 송금이 도착하지 않았노라고, 세들리 씨는 근심 어린 얼굴로 아내에게 말했다."], ["As she had paid her bills very regularly hitherto, one or two of the tradesmen to whom the poor lady was obliged to go round asking for time were very angry at a delay to which they were perfectly used from more irregular customers.", "여태껏 청구서를 꼬박꼬박 갚아 온 터라, 가엾은 부인이 일일이 찾아다니며 기한을 미뤄 달라고 사정해야 했던 상인들 가운데 한둘은, 형편이 더 들쭉날쭉한 손님들에게는 으레 익숙했을 그 지체를 두고 몹시 노여워했다."], ["Emmy's contribution, paid over cheerfully without any questions, kept the little company in half-rations however.", "그래도 에미가 아무 질문 없이 선뜻 내놓는 돈이 이 작은 식구를 절반쯤 굶주린 채나마 지탱해 주었다."], ["And the first six months passed away pretty easily, old Sedley still keeping up with the notion that his shares must rise and that all would be well.", "그리하여 처음 여섯 달은 제법 수월하게 흘러갔고, 노년의 세들리는 여전히 자기 주식이 틀림없이 오를 것이고 만사가 잘 풀리리라는 생각을 버리지 않았다."]], [["No sixty pounds, however, came to help the household at the end of the half year, and it fell deeper and deeper into trouble--Mrs.", "그러나 그 반년이 끝나도록 살림을 거들 육십 파운드는 들어오지 않았고, 형편은 점점 더 깊은 곤경 속으로 빠져들었다."], ["Sedley, who was growing infirm and was much shaken, remained silent or wept a great deal with Mrs. Clapp in the kitchen.", "점점 쇠약해지고 몹시 충격을 받은 세들리 부인은 입을 다물고 있거나, 부엌에서 클랩 부인과 함께 하염없이 우는 일이 잦았다."], ["The butcher was particularly surly, the grocer insolent: once or twice little Georgy had grumbled about the dinners, and Amelia, who still would have been satisfied with a slice of bread for her own dinner, could not but perceive that her son was neglected and purchased little things out of her private purse to keep the boy in health.", "정육점 주인은 유독 무뚝뚝했고, 식료품 장수는 무례했다. 한두 번은 어린 조지가 저녁상에 대해 투덜거렸고, 자기 저녁이라면 빵 한 조각으로도 만족했을 애밀리아였지만, 아들이 푸대접받고 있음을 알아채지 않을 수 없어, 아이의 건강을 지키려고 자기 사사로운 지갑을 털어 자질구레한 것들을 사들이곤 했다."]], [["At last they told her, or told her such a garbled story as people in difficulties tell.", "마침내 그들은 그녀에게 사정을 털어놓았다. 아니, 곤경에 빠진 사람들이 으레 그러듯 두루뭉술하게 얼버무린 이야기를 들려주었다."], ["One day, her own money having been received, and Amelia about to pay it over, she, who had kept an account of the moneys expended by her, proposed to keep a certain portion back out of her dividend, having contracted engagements for a new suit for Georgy.", "어느 날, 자기 몫의 돈이 들어와서 애밀리아가 그것을 막 건네려던 참이었는데, 자신이 쓴 돈의 내역을 꼼꼼히 적어 두던 그녀가, 조지의 새 옷 한 벌을 맞추기로 약속한 일이 있어 자기 배당금에서 일정액을 떼어 두겠다고 했다."]], [["Then it came out that Jos's remittances were not paid, that the house was in difficulties, which Amelia ought to have seen before, her mother said, but she cared for nothing or nobody except Georgy.", "그러자 비로소 진상이 드러났다. 조스의 송금이 들어오지 않았고, 집안이 곤경에 처했다는 것, 애밀리아가 진작 알아챘어야 했건만 조지 말고는 아무것도 아무도 안중에 없었던 탓이라고 어머니가 말했다."], ["At this she passed all her money across the table, without a word, to her mother, and returned to her room to cry her eyes out.", "이 말에 그녀는 한마디 없이 제 돈을 모조리 탁자 너머로 어머니에게 밀어 넘기고는, 제 방으로 돌아가 눈이 빠지도록 울었다."], ["She had a great access of sensibility too that day, when obliged to go and countermand the clothes, the darling clothes on which she had set her heart for Christmas Day, and the cut and fashion of which she had arranged in many conversations with a small milliner, her friend.", "그날 그녀는 또 한 차례 격한 감정에 휩싸였으니, 그 옷을, 성탄절을 위해 애지중지 마음에 품어 두었던 그 사랑스러운 옷을, 그 마름질과 모양새를 작은 모자 가게 주인이자 벗인 이와 숱한 대화를 나누며 정해 두었던 그 옷을, 가서 취소해야만 했기 때문이다."]], [["Hardest of all, she had to break the matter to Georgy, who made a loud outcry.", "무엇보다 견디기 힘들었던 것은 이 일을 조지에게 꺼내야 한다는 것이었는데, 아이는 큰 소리로 떼를 썼다."], ["Everybody had new clothes at Christmas.", "성탄절에는 누구나 새 옷을 입는다는 것이었다."], ["The others would laugh at him.", "다른 아이들이 자기를 비웃을 거라고 했다."], ["He would have new clothes.", "새 옷을 갖고 말겠다고 했다."], ["She had promised them to him.", "엄마가 약속하지 않았느냐고 했다."], ["The poor widow had only kisses to give him.", "가엾은 미망인이 아이에게 줄 수 있는 것이라곤 입맞춤뿐이었다."], ["She darned the old suit in tears.", "그녀는 눈물을 흘리며 헌 옷을 기웠다."], ["She cast about among her little ornaments to see if she could sell anything to procure the desired novelties.", "그녀는 그토록 바라는 새것을 마련할 무엇이라도 팔 수 있을까 하여 자기의 자그마한 패물들을 이리저리 뒤졌다."], ["There was her India shawl that Dobbin had sent her.", "도빈이 보내 준 그 인도산 숄이 있었다."], ["She remembered in former days going with her mother to a fine India shop on Ludgate Hill, where the ladies had all sorts of dealings and bargains in these articles.", "그녀는 예전에 어머니와 함께 러드게이트 힐에 있는 어느 멋진 인도 물품점에 가던 일을 떠올렸다. 그곳에서 숙녀들은 그런 물건들을 두고 온갖 거래와 흥정을 벌이곤 했던 것이다."], ["Her cheeks flushed and her eyes shone with pleasure as she thought of this resource, and she kissed away George to school in the morning, smiling brightly after him.", "이 방도를 떠올리자 그녀의 두 볼은 발갛게 달아오르고 두 눈은 기쁨으로 반짝였으며, 이튿날 아침 그녀는 조지에게 입을 맞춰 학교로 보내면서 그 뒤로 환히 미소 지었다."], ["The boy felt that there was good news in her look.", "아이는 어머니의 표정에 좋은 소식이 깃들어 있음을 알아챘다."]], [["Packing up her shawl in a handkerchief (another of the gifts of the good Major), she hid them under her cloak and walked flushed and eager all the way to Ludgate Hill, tripping along by the park wall and running over the crossings, so that many a man turned as she hurried by him and looked after her rosy pretty face.", "손수건에 숄을 싸서 (이 또한 그 마음씨 고운 소령이 보내 준 선물이었다) 망토 밑에 감춘 채, 그녀는 상기되고 들뜬 얼굴로 러드게이트 힐까지 줄곧 걸어갔는데, 공원 담장을 따라 종종걸음 치고 건널목을 뛰어 건너는 통에, 그녀가 황급히 지나칠 때면 숱한 남자가 돌아서서 그 발그레하고 어여쁜 얼굴을 바라보았다."], ["She calculated how she should spend the proceeds of her shawl--how, besides the clothes, she would buy the books that he longed for, and pay his half-year's schooling; and how she would buy a cloak for her father instead of that old great-coat which he wore.", "그녀는 숄을 판 돈을 어떻게 쓸지 셈을 해 보았다. 옷 말고도 아이가 그토록 갖고 싶어 하던 책들을 사 주고, 반년 치 학비를 내고, 또 아버지에게는 그 낡은 외투 대신 새 망토를 사 드릴 작정이었다."], ["She was not mistaken as to the value of the Major's gift.", "그 소령의 선물이 지닌 값어치를 그녀가 잘못 안 것은 아니었다."], ["It was a very fine and beautiful web, and the merchant made a very good bargain when he gave her twenty guineas for her shawl.", "그것은 더없이 곱고 아름다운 직물이었고, 상인이 숄값으로 이십 기니를 쳐준 것은 그로서는 아주 남는 흥정을 한 셈이었다."]], [["She ran on amazed and flurried with her riches to Darton's shop, in St. Paul's Churchyard, and there purchased the Parents'", "자기가 거머쥔 재물에 어리둥절하고 들떠서 그녀는 세인트폴 성당 묘지에 있는 다턴 서점으로 달려가, 조지가 그토록 갖고 싶어 하던 「부모의 조력자」와 「샌드퍼드와 머튼」을 사고는, 꾸러미를 안은 채 거기서 합승마차에 올라 의기양양하게 집으로 돌아왔다."], ["Assistant and the Sandford and Merton Georgy longed for, and got into the coach there with her parcel, and went home exulting. And she pleased herself by writing in the fly-leaf in her neatest little hand, \"George Osborne, A Christmas gift from his affectionate mother.\"", "그러고는 더없이 정갈한 작은 글씨로 속표지에 \"조지 오즈번, 사랑하는 엄마가 주는 성탄절 선물\"이라고 적어 넣으며 흐뭇해했다."], ["The books are extant to this day, with the fair delicate superscription.", "그 책들은 그 곱고 섬세한 글귀와 함께 오늘날까지 전해 온다."]], [["She was going from her own room with the books in her hand to place them on George's table, where he might find them on his return from school, when in the passage, she and her mother met.", "그녀가 책을 손에 들고 자기 방에서 나와, 학교에서 돌아온 조지가 찾아볼 수 있도록 그것을 아이의 책상에 놓아 두려던 참에, 복도에서 그녀와 어머니가 마주쳤다."], ["The gilt bindings of the seven handsome little volumes caught the old lady's eye.", "그 잘생긴 작은 일곱 권의 금박 장정이 노부인의 눈에 띄었다."]], [["\"What are those?\"", "\"그게 뭐냐?\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["\"Some books for Georgy,\"", "\"조지한테 줄 책 몇 권이에요.\""], ["Amelia replied--\"I--I promised them to him at Christmas.\"", "애밀리아가 대답했다. \"제가, 제가 성탄절에 사 주겠다고 약속했거든요.\""]], [["\"Books!\"", "\"책이라고!\""], ["cried the elder lady indignantly, \"Books, when the whole house wants bread!", "노부인이 분개하여 외쳤다. \"온 집안이 빵이 없어 굶을 판에 책이라니!"], ["Books, when to keep you and your son in luxury, and your dear father out of gaol, I've sold every trinket I had, the India shawl from my back even down to the very spoons, that our tradesmen mightn't insult us, and that Mr. Clapp, which indeed he is justly entitled, being not a hard landlord, and a civil man, and a father, might have his rent.", "너랑 네 아들은 호강시키고 네 사랑하는 아버지는 감옥에 안 가시게 하려고, 내가 가진 패물이란 패물은 죄다 팔아 치우고, 내 등에 두른 인도 숄에서부터 숟가락 하나까지 다 팔아서, 우리 거래처들이 우리를 모욕하지 못하게 하고, 클랩 씨가, 인색한 집주인도 아니고 점잖은 사람이고 한 집안의 가장인 그 사람이, 받아 마땅한 집세나마 받을 수 있게 해 놨건만, 책이라니!"], ["Oh, Amelia!", "오, 애밀리아!"], ["you break my heart with your books and that boy of yours, whom you are ruining, though part with him you will not.", "너는 그 책이며, 네가 망쳐 놓고 있으면서도 떼어 놓지는 못하는 그 아들놈이며로 내 가슴을 찢어 놓는구나."], ["Oh, Amelia, may God send you a more dutiful child than I have had!", "오, 애밀리아, 부디 하느님께서 내가 둔 자식보다 더 효성스러운 자식을 네게 점지해 주시기를!"], ["There's Jos, deserts his father in his old age; and there's George, who might be provided for, and who might be rich, going to school like a lord, with a gold watch and chain round his neck--while my dear, dear old man is without a sh--shilling.\"", "조스는 늙은 아비를 노년에 저버렸고, 살림 밑천을 마련받을 수도 있고 부자가 될 수도 있는 조지는, 목에 금시계와 금줄을 두르고 귀족 도련님처럼 학교에 다니는데, 정작 내 사랑하는, 사랑하는 늙은 양반은 단 한 푼, 단 한 푼도 없으니!\""], ["Hysteric sobs and cries ended Mrs. Sedley's speech--it echoed through every room in the small house, whereof the other female inmates heard every word of the colloquy.", "발작하듯 흐느끼고 부르짖는 소리로 세들리 부인의 연설은 끝이 났는데, 그 소리는 이 작은 집의 방마다 메아리쳐, 다른 여자 식구들은 그 대화를 한마디도 빠짐없이 들었다."]], [["\"Oh, Mother, Mother!\"", "\"오, 어머니, 어머니!\""], ["cried poor Amelia in reply.", "가엾은 애밀리아가 대답하여 외쳤다."], ["\"You told me nothing--I--I promised him the books.", "\"어머니가 제게 아무 말씀도 안 하셨잖아요. 저는, 저는 아이한테 책을 사 주겠다고 약속했고요."], ["I--I only sold my shawl this morning.", "저는, 저는 오늘 아침에야 제 숄을 팔았을 뿐이에요. 돈을 가지세요."], ["Take the money--take everything\"--and with quivering hands she took out her silver, and her sovereigns--her precious golden sovereigns, which she thrust into the hands of her mother, whence they overflowed and tumbled, rolling down the stairs.", "다 가지세요.\" 그러면서 그녀는 떨리는 손으로 은화와 금화를, 자기의 그 소중한 금화 소버린을 꺼내 어머니의 손에 떠넘겼고, 돈은 그 손에서 흘러넘쳐 떨어지더니 계단을 따라 데구루루 굴러 내려갔다."]], [["And then she went into her room, and sank down in despair and utter misery.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 자기 방으로 들어가, 절망과 더없는 비참 속에 주저앉았다."], ["She saw it all now.", "이제 그녀는 모든 것을 깨달았다."], ["Her selfishness was sacrificing the boy.", "자기의 이기심이 아이를 희생시키고 있었던 것이다."], ["But for her he might have wealth, station, education, and his father's place, which the elder George had forfeited for her sake.", "자기만 아니었다면 아이는 부와 지위와 교육을, 그리고 아비인 조지가 자신을 위해 잃어버린 그 자리를 누릴 수도 있었을 것이다."], ["She had but to speak the words, and her father was restored to competency and the boy raised to fortune.", "그저 한마디 말만 하면 되었다. 그러면 아버지는 넉넉한 형편을 되찾고, 아이는 큰 재물의 주인이 될 터였다."], ["Oh, what a conviction it was to that tender and stricken heart!", "오, 그 여리고 상처 입은 가슴에 그것은 얼마나 사무치는 깨달음이었던가!"]], [["Gaunt House\nAll the world knows that Lord Steyne's town palace stands in Gaunt Square, out of which Great Gaunt Street leads, whither we first conducted Rebecca, in the time of the departed Sir Pitt Crawley. Peering over the railings and through the black trees into the garden of the Square, you see a few miserable governesses with wan-faced pupils wandering round and round it, and round the dreary grass-plot in the centre of which rises the statue of Lord Gaunt, who fought at Minden, in a three-tailed wig, and otherwise habited like a Roman Emperor. Gaunt House occupies nearly a side of the Square. The remaining three sides are composed of mansions that have passed away into dowagerism--tall, dark houses, with window-frames of stone, or picked out of a lighter red. Little light seems to be behind those lean, comfortless casements now, and hospitality to have passed away from those doors as much as the laced lacqueys and link-boys of old times, who used to put out their torches in the blank iron extinguishers that still flank the lamps over the steps. Brass plates have penetrated into the square--Doctors, the Diddlesex Bank Western Branch--the English and European Reunion, &c.--it has a dreary look--nor is my Lord Steyne's palace less dreary. All I have ever seen of it is the vast wall in front, with the rustic columns at the great gate, through which an old porter peers sometimes with a fat and gloomy red face--and over the wall the garret and bedroom windows, and the chimneys, out of which there seldom comes any smoke now. For the present Lord Steyne lives at Naples, preferring the view of the Bay and Capri and Vesuvius to the dreary aspect of the wall in Gaunt Square.", "세상이 다 알다시피, 스타인 경의 도시 저택은 곤트 광장에 자리하고 있다. 그곳에서 그레이트 곤트 거리가 뻗어 나가는데, 우리가 고인이 된 피트 크롤리 경의 시절에 레베카를 처음 데려갔던 바로 그 거리이다. 난간 너머로, 검은 나무들 사이로 광장의 정원을 들여다보면, 핼쑥한 얼굴의 학생 몇을 데리고 정원을 빙빙 도는 가엾은 가정교사 몇몇이 보인다. 그 한가운데에는 음울한 잔디밭이 펼쳐져 있고, 거기에는 민덴 전투에서 싸웠던 곤트 경의 동상이, 세 갈래 가발을 쓰고 그 밖의 차림은 로마 황제 같은 모습으로 솟아 있다. 곤트 저택은 광장의 거의 한 면을 통째로 차지하고 있다. 나머지 세 면은 과부살이로 영락한 저택들로 이루어져 있다. 돌로 된 창틀이나, 좀 더 밝은 붉은빛으로 가장자리를 두른 창틀이 달린, 높다랗고 어두침침한 집들 말이다. 이제 그 야위고 적막한 여닫이창 안쪽에는 불빛이라곤 거의 보이지 않고, 손님을 맞이하는 환대도 그 문에서는 사라져 버렸다. 옛 시절 레이스 두른 하인들과 횃불 든 소년들이 사라진 것이나 진배없이 말이다. 그 소년들은 계단 위 등불 양옆에 지금도 남아 있는, 텅 빈 쇠 소화기에 횃불을 끄곤 했던 것이다. 이제 광장에는 놋쇠 명패들이 파고들었다. 개업의들, 디들섹스 은행 서부 지점, 영국유럽연합회 따위가 말이다. 광장은 음울한 행색을 띠고 있다. 그렇다고 스타인 경의 저택이 덜 음울한 것도 아니다. 내가 그 저택에서 본 것이라곤 앞쪽의 거대한 담장, 큰 대문에 세워진 투박한 기둥들이 전부였다. 그 사이로 살지고 음침한 붉은 얼굴의 늙은 문지기가 이따금 빠끔히 내다보곤 했다. 그리고 담장 너머로는 다락방과 침실의 창문들, 그리고 이제는 좀처럼 연기가 피어오르지 않는 굴뚝들이 보일 뿐이었다. 지금의 스타인 경은 나폴리에 살고 있어서, 곤트 광장 담장의 음울한 모습보다는 만과 카프리섬과 베수비오 화산의 경치를 더 좋아하기 때문이다."]], [["A few score yards down New Gaunt Street, and leading into Gaunt Mews indeed, is a little modest back door, which you would not remark from that of any of the other stables.", "뉴 곤트 거리를 따라 몇십 야드 내려가, 실제로 곤트 마구간으로 통하는 곳에는, 다른 어느 마구간 문과도 구별되지 않을 만큼 수수한 작은 뒷문 하나가 있다."], ["But many a little close carriage has stopped at that door, as my informant (little Tom Eaves, who knows everything, and who showed me the place) told me.", "하지만 나에게 이 이야기를 들려준 사람(모르는 것이 없는 데다 이곳을 직접 보여 준 꼬마 톰 이브스)의 말로는, 작고 은밀한 마차들이 그 문 앞에 수도 없이 멈춰 섰다고 한다. \"왕자님과 퍼디타가 저 문을 들락거렸지요, 손님.\""], ["\"The Prince and Perdita have been in and out of that door, sir,\"", "그는 종종 내게 말하곤 했다. \"마리안 클라크도 ××공작과 함께 저 문으로 들어갔답니다."], ["he had often told me; \"Marianne Clarke has entered it with the Duke of ------.", "저 문은 스타인 경의 그 유명한 작은 별실로 통하지요. 손님, 한 방은 온통 상아와 흰 새틴으로, 또 한 방은 흑단과 검은 벨벳으로 꾸며져 있답니다."], ["It conducts to the famous petits appartements of Lord Steyne--one, sir, fitted up all in ivory and white satin, another in ebony and black velvet; there is a little banqueting-room taken from Sallust's house at Pompeii, and painted by Cosway--a little private kitchen, in which every saucepan was silver and all the spits were gold.", "폼페이의 살루스티우스 저택에서 본떠 코스웨이가 그림을 그린 작은 연회실도 있고요. 또 작은 전용 주방이 있는데, 거기엔 냄비란 냄비가 죄다 은이고 꼬챙이는 모조리 금이었답니다."], ["It was there that Egalite Orleans roasted partridges on the night when he and the Marquis of Steyne won a hundred thousand from a great personage at ombre.", "바로 거기서 오를레앙의 에갈리테가 자고새를 구웠지요. 그가 스타인 후작과 함께 옴브르 노름으로 어느 귀하신 분에게서 십만을 따낸 그날 밤에 말입니다."], ["Half of the money went to the French Revolution, half to purchase Lord Gaunt's Marquisate and Garter--and the remainder--\"", "그 돈의 절반은 프랑스 혁명에 들어갔고, 절반은 곤트 경의 후작 작위와 가터 훈장을 사는 데 쓰였지요. 그리고 그 나머지는……\""], ["but it forms no part of our scheme to tell what became of the remainder, for every shilling of which, and a great deal more, little Tom Eaves, who knows everybody's affairs, is ready to account.", "하지만 그 나머지가 어찌 되었는지를 전하는 것은 우리 이야기의 범위에 들지 않는다. 모든 이의 사정을 다 아는 꼬마 톰 이브스라면, 그 한 푼 한 푼까지, 아니 그보다 훨씬 더 많은 것까지 기꺼이 설명할 준비가 되어 있겠지만 말이다."]], [["Besides his town palace, the Marquis had castles and palaces in various quarters of the three kingdoms, whereof the descriptions may be found in the road-books--Castle Strongbow, with its woods, on the Shannon shore; Gaunt Castle, in Carmarthenshire, where Richard II was taken prisoner--Gauntly Hall in Yorkshire, where I have been informed there were two hundred silver teapots for the breakfasts of the guests of the house, with everything to correspond in splendour; and Stillbrook in Hampshire, which was my lord's farm, an humble place of residence, of which we all remember the wonderful furniture which was sold at my lord's demise by a late celebrated auctioneer.", "도시 저택 말고도, 후작은 삼 왕국의 곳곳에 성과 궁을 거느리고 있었다. 그 묘사는 여행 안내서에서 찾아볼 수 있다. 섀넌강 기슭의 숲을 거느린 스트롱보 성, 리처드 이세가 포로로 잡혔던 카마던셔의 곤트 성, 그리고 요크셔의 곤틀리 홀이 있는데, 내가 들은 바로는 그곳에는 저택 손님들의 아침 식사를 위한 은제 찻주전자가 이백 개나 있었고, 그 밖의 온갖 것도 그 호화로움에 걸맞았다고 한다. 또 햄프셔에는 후작의 농장인 스틸브룩이 있었는데, 소박한 거처이긴 했으나, 후작이 세상을 떠나면서 근래 이름난 경매인이 처분했던 그 놀라운 가구들을 우리 모두 기억하고 있다."]], [["The Marchioness of Steyne was of the renowned and ancient family of the Caerlyons, Marquises of Camelot, who have preserved the old faith ever since the conversion of the venerable Druid, their first ancestor, and whose pedigree goes far beyond the date of the arrival of King Brute in these islands.", "스타인 후작 부인은 카멜롯의 후작인 케어라이언 가문의, 저 유명하고 유서 깊은 일족 출신이었다. 이 가문은 그들의 첫 조상인 존경하는 드루이드가 개종한 이래로 줄곧 옛 신앙을 지켜 왔고, 그 가계도는 브루투스 왕이 이 섬에 도래한 시점을 한참이나 거슬러 올라간다."], ["Pendragon is the title of the eldest son of the house.", "이 가문 장남의 칭호는 펜드래건이다."], ["The sons have been called Arthurs, Uthers, and Caradocs, from immemorial time.", "그 아들들은 아득한 옛날부터 아서, 우서, 카라독이라 불려 왔다."], ["Their heads have fallen in many a loyal conspiracy.", "그 가문의 머리는 충성스러운 음모에 휘말려 숱하게 떨어져 나갔다."], ["Elizabeth chopped off the head of the Arthur of her day, who had been Chamberlain to Philip and Mary, and carried letters between the Queen of Scots and her uncles the Guises.", "엘리자베스 여왕은 당대의 아서, 그러니까 필립과 메리의 시종이었으며 스코틀랜드 여왕과 그 외삼촌인 기즈 가문 사이에 편지를 날랐던 그 아서의 목을 베어 버렸다."], ["A cadet of the house was an officer of the great Duke and distinguished in the famous Saint Bartholomew conspiracy.", "가문의 차남 한 사람은 대공의 장교로서 저 유명한 성 바르톨로메오 음모에서 두각을 나타냈다."], ["During the whole of Mary's confinement, the house of Camelot conspired in her behalf.", "메리가 유폐되어 있던 내내, 카멜롯 가문은 그녀를 위해 음모를 꾸몄다."], ["It was as much injured by its charges in fitting out an armament against the Spaniards, during the time of the Armada, as by the fines and confiscations levied on it by Elizabeth for harbouring of priests, obstinate recusancy, and popish misdoings.", "이 가문은 무적함대 시절 에스파냐에 맞서 군비를 갖추느라 들인 비용으로 큰 손해를 보았고, 그에 못지않게 사제를 숨겨 주고, 완강하게 국교를 거부하고, 교황파의 악행을 저질렀다 하여 엘리자베스에게 벌금과 몰수를 당해 손해를 보았다."], ["A recreant of James's time was momentarily perverted from his religion by the arguments of that great theologian, and the fortunes of the family somewhat restored by his timely weakness.", "제임스 시절의 한 변절자는 저 위대한 신학자의 논변에 잠시 신앙을 저버렸고, 그 시의적절한 나약함 덕분에 가문의 재산은 다소 회복되었다."], ["But the Earl of Camelot, of the reign of Charles, returned to the old creed of his family, and they continued to fight for it, and ruin themselves for it, as long as there was a Stuart left to head or to instigate a rebellion.", "그러나 찰스 치세의 카멜롯 백작은 가문의 옛 신조로 돌아갔고, 그들은 반란을 이끌거나 부추길 스튜어트 왕가의 후예가 한 사람이라도 남아 있는 한, 그 신앙을 위해 싸우고 그 신앙을 위해 스스로를 파멸시키기를 멈추지 않았다."]], [["Lady Mary Caerlyon was brought up at a Parisian convent; the Dauphiness Marie Antoinette was her godmother.", "메리 케어라이언 영애는 파리의 한 수녀원에서 자랐으며, 황태자비 마리 앙투아네트가 그녀의 대모였다."], ["In the pride of her beauty she had been married--sold, it was said--to Lord Gaunt, then at Paris, who won vast sums from the lady's brother at some of Philip of Orleans's banquets.", "그 미모가 한창 자랑스럽던 시절, 그녀는 당시 파리에 있던 곤트 경과 결혼했다. 사람들 말로는, 팔려 갔다고들 했다."], ["The Earl of Gaunt's famous duel with the Count de la Marche, of the Grey Musqueteers, was attributed by common report to the pretensions of that officer (who had been a page, and remained a favourite of the Queen) to the hand of the beautiful Lady Mary Caerlyon.", "곤트 경은 오를레앙 공 필립의 연회 자리에서 그 영애의 오라비에게서 막대한 돈을 따낸 사람이었다. 곤트 백작이 회색 총사대의 라마르슈 백작과 벌인 그 유명한 결투는, 세간의 소문에 따르면 그 장교(한때 시동이었고 여전히 여왕의 총애를 받던 자)가 아름다운 메리 케어라이언 영애의 손을 넘본 데서 비롯된 것이라 했다."], ["She was married to Lord Gaunt while the Count lay ill of his wound, and came to dwell at Gaunt House, and to figure for a short time in the splendid Court of the Prince of Wales.", "그녀는 백작이 부상으로 앓아누워 있는 동안 곤트 경과 혼인하여, 곤트 저택에 거처를 정하고 잠시나마 웨일스 공의 화려한 궁정에서 빛을 발했다. 폭스는 그녀를 위해 축배를 들었다."], ["Fox had toasted her. Morris and Sheridan had written songs about her.", "모리스와 셰리든은 그녀에 관한 노래를 지었다."], ["Malmesbury had made her his best bow; Walpole had pronounced her charming; Devonshire had been almost jealous of her; but she was scared by the wild pleasures and gaieties of the society into which she was flung, and after she had borne a couple of sons, shrank away into a life of devout seclusion.", "맘즈베리는 그녀에게 최고의 예를 갖추었고, 월폴은 그녀를 매혹적이라 평했으며, 데번셔는 그녀를 거의 질투할 지경이었다. 그러나 그녀는 자신이 내던져진 그 사교계의 거친 향락과 흥청거림에 겁을 먹었고, 아들 둘을 낳은 뒤로는 경건한 칩거의 삶 속으로 움츠러들고 말았다."], ["No wonder that my Lord Steyne, who liked pleasure and cheerfulness, was not often seen after their marriage by the side of this trembling, silent, superstitious, unhappy lady.", "향락과 명랑함을 좋아하던 스타인 경이 결혼 후 이 떨고, 말없고, 미신에 사로잡히고, 불행한 부인 곁에 좀처럼 모습을 드러내지 않았던 것도 무리는 아니었다."]], [["The before-mentioned Tom Eaves (who has no part in this history, except that he knew all the great folks in London, and the stories and mysteries of each family) had further information regarding my Lady Steyne, which may or may not be true.", "앞서 언급한 톰 이브스(런던의 모든 명사를 알고 각 가문의 사연과 비밀을 죄다 알고 있다는 점 말고는 이 이야기에 아무런 몫도 없는 인물이다)는 스타인 부인에 관해 더 많은 정보를 가지고 있었는데, 사실인지 아닌지는 알 수 없다. \"그 여자가 제 집에서 감내해야 했던 모욕이란,\""], ["\"The humiliations,\"", "톰은 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"정말이지 끔찍한 것이었지요."], ["Tom used to say, \"which that woman has been made to undergo, in her own house, have been frightful; Lord Steyne has made her sit down to table with women with whom I would rather die than allow Mrs.", "스타인 경은 그 부인을, 나 같으면 차라리 죽었으면 죽었지 내 아내 이브스 부인과는 어울리게 하지 않을 그런 여자들과 한 식탁에 앉게 했답니다. 크래큰베리 부인이라든가, 치펀햄 부인이라든가, 프랑스인 서기관의 아내인 드라크뤼슈카세 부인이라든가 하는 여자들과 말이지요(그 여자들을 알고 지내려고 제 아내라도 기꺼이 바쳤을 톰 이브스로 말하자면, 그 여자들 누구에게서든 인사 한번 받거나 만찬 한번 얻어먹으면 더없이 기뻐했겠지만요)."], ["Eaves to associate--with Lady Crackenbury, with Mrs.", "한마디로, 당대를 주름잡는 총애받는 여인과 한 식탁에 앉게 했던 것이지요. 그런데 부르봉 가문 못지않게 콧대 높은 그 가문 출신의 여자, 스타인 가문 따위는 한낱 하인이요 어제오늘 솟아난 버섯에 불과한 그런 가문 출신의 여자가(따지고 보면 그들은 옛 곤트 가문이 아니라 그 가문의 변변찮고 미심쩍은 방계 출신이거든요), 영국에서 가장 도도한 그 스타인 후작 부인이, 무슨 까닭도 없이 남편 앞에 그토록 고분고분 머리를 조아렸으리라고 생각하십니까(독자께서는 늘 톰 이브스가 하는 말이라는 것을 명심하시기 바란다)?"], ["Chippenham, with Madame de la Cruchecassee, the French secretary's wife (from every one of which ladies Tom Eaves--who would have sacrificed his wife for knowing them--was too glad to get a bow or a dinner) with the REIGNING FAVOURITE in a word.", "천만의 말씀! 내 말하건대, 거기엔 은밀한 사정이 있는 겁니다."], ["And do you suppose that that woman, of that family, who are as proud as the Bourbons, and to whom the Steynes are but lackeys, mushrooms of yesterday (for after all, they are not of the Old Gaunts, but of a minor and doubtful branch of the house); do you suppose, I say (the reader must bear in mind that it is always Tom Eaves who speaks) that the Marchioness of Steyne, the haughtiest woman in England, would bend down to her husband so submissively if there were not some cause?", "내 말하건대, 망명 시절에 이곳에 와서 퓌제, 탱트니악과 함께 키브롱 사건에 가담했던 라마르슈 신부, 그자가 바로 팔육년에 스타인이 결투를 벌였던 그 회색 총사대의 대령이었던 거지요. 그자와 후작 부인이 다시 만났고, 그 성직자가 된 대령이 브르타뉴에서 총에 맞아 죽은 다음부터, 스타인 부인이 지금까지 이어 오는 그 극단적인 신앙 행위에 빠져들었던 겁니다."], ["Pooh! I tell you there are secret reasons.", "그녀는 날마다 고해 신부와 단둘이 틀어박혀 지내고, 아침마다 스패니시 플레이스에서 예배를 드리지요."], ["I tell you that, in the emigration, the Abbe de la Marche who was here and was employed in the Quiberoon business with Puisaye and Tinteniac, was the same Colonel of Mousquetaires Gris with whom Steyne fought in the year '86--that he and the Marchioness met again--that it was after the Reverend Colonel was shot in Brittany that Lady Steyne took to those extreme practices of devotion which she carries on now; for she is closeted with her director every day--she is at service at Spanish Place, every morning, I've watched her there--that is, I've happened to be passing there--and depend on it, there's a mystery in her case.", "내가 거기서 그녀를 지켜봤으니까요. 아니, 어쩌다 그 앞을 지나가게 된 거지만요."], ["People are not so unhappy unless they have something to repent of,\"", "장담컨대, 그 여자한테는 무슨 비밀이 있는 겁니다. 뉘우칠 무언가가 있지 않고서야 사람이 그렇게까지 불행할 수는 없는 노릇이지요.\""], ["added Tom Eaves with a knowing wag of his head; \"and depend on it, that woman would not be so submissive as she is if the Marquis had not some sword to hold over her.\"", "톰 이브스는 다 안다는 듯 고개를 까딱이며 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"그리고 장담컨대, 후작이 그 여자 머리 위에 들이밀 칼자루를 쥐고 있지 않았다면, 그 여자가 그렇게까지 고분고분하지는 않았을 겁니다.\""]], [["So, if Mr. Eaves's information be correct, it is very likely that this lady, in her high station, had to submit to many a private indignity and to hide many secret griefs under a calm face.", "그러니, 이브스 씨의 정보가 정확하다면, 그토록 높은 지위에 오른 이 부인이 남몰래 숱한 수모를 감내하고 차분한 얼굴 뒤에 숱한 은밀한 슬픔을 감춰야 했을 가능성도 다분하다."], ["And let us, my brethren who have not our names in the Red Book, console ourselves by thinking comfortably how miserable our betters may be, and that Damocles, who sits on satin cushions and is served on gold plate, has an awful sword hanging over his head in the shape of a bailiff, or an hereditary disease, or a family secret, which peeps out every now and then from the embroidered arras in a ghastly manner, and will be sure to drop one day or the other in the right place.", "그러니 명사 인명록에 이름이 오르지 못한 우리 형제들이여, 우리네 윗사람들이 얼마나 비참할 수 있는지를 흐뭇하게 떠올리며 스스로를 위로하자. 새틴 방석에 앉아 금 접시로 시중을 받는 다모클레스의 머리 위에는, 집달리든, 유전병이든, 가문의 비밀이든 하는 모양을 한 무시무시한 칼이 매달려 있는 법이며, 그 비밀이라는 것은 수놓은 벽걸이 천 사이로 이따금 섬뜩하게 빠끔히 고개를 내밀다가, 언젠가는 반드시 떨어질 자리에 떨어지고야 마는 것이다."]], [["In comparing, too, the poor man's situation with that of the great, there is (always according to Mr. Eaves) another source of comfort for the former.", "또한 가난한 자의 처지를 높으신 분의 처지와 견주어 볼 때, (역시 이브스 씨에 따르면) 가난한 자에게는 또 하나의 위안거리가 있다."], ["You who have little or no patrimony to bequeath or to inherit, may be on good terms with your father or your son, whereas the heir of a great prince, such as my Lord Steyne, must naturally be angry at being kept out of his kingdom, and eye the occupant of it with no very agreeable glances.", "물려줄 가산도 물려받을 가산도 거의 없거나 아예 없는 그대는, 아버지나 아들과 사이좋게 지낼 수 있다. 반면 스타인 경 같은 대공의 후계자는, 제 왕국에서 밀려나 있다는 데 자연히 분개할 수밖에 없고, 그 자리를 차지하고 있는 자를 그리 곱지 않은 눈초리로 바라볼 수밖에 없는 것이다."], ["\"Take it as a rule,\"", "\"이건 정해진 이치라고 보면 됩니다.\""], ["this sardonic old Eaves would say, \"the fathers and elder sons of all great families hate each other.", "이 냉소적인 늙은 이브스는 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"온 명문가의 아비와 장남은 서로 미워하는 법이지요."], ["The Crown Prince is always in opposition to the crown or hankering after it.", "왕세자는 늘 왕관에 반기를 들거나 그걸 갈망하는 법이고요."], ["Shakespeare knew the world, my good sir, and when he describes Prince Hal (from whose family the Gaunts pretend to be descended, though they are no more related to John of Gaunt than you are) trying on his father's coronet, he gives you a natural description of all heirs apparent.", "셰익스피어는 세상을 알았던 사람입니다, 손님. 그가 핼 왕자(곤트 가문이 그 후손이라 자처하지만, 따지고 보면 손님이 곤트의 존과 무관한 것만큼이나 그들도 무관하지요)가 제 아비의 왕관을 한번 써 보는 장면을 그릴 때, 그건 모든 추정 상속인의 본성을 있는 그대로 그려 낸 것이랍니다."], ["If you were heir to a dukedom and a thousand pounds a day, do you mean to say you would not wish for possession?", "손님이 공작 작위와 하루 천 파운드의 상속인이라면, 그걸 손에 넣고 싶지 않을 거라고 말씀하실 텐가요?"], ["Pooh!", "천만의 말씀!"], ["And it stands to reason that every great man, having experienced this feeling towards his father, must be aware that his son entertains it towards himself; and so they can't but be suspicious and hostile.", "그러니 모든 위인이, 제 아비를 향해 이런 감정을 품어 봤으니, 제 아들도 자신을 향해 그 감정을 품고 있다는 걸 모를 리 없다는 게 당연한 이치지요. 그러니 그들은 서로 의심하고 적대할 수밖에 없는 겁니다."]], [["\"Then again, as to the feeling of elder towards younger sons.", "\"그리고 또 형이 동생들을 어떻게 보느냐 하는 문제도 그렇습니다."], ["My dear sir, you ought to know that every elder brother looks upon the cadets of the house as his natural enemies, who deprive him of so much ready money which ought to be his by right.", "손님, 아셔야 할 것은, 모든 맏형이 가문의 차남들을 타고난 적으로 본다는 점이에요. 마땅히 제 것이어야 할 현금을 그만큼 빼앗아 가는 자들이라 이 말입니다."], ["I have often heard George Mac Turk, Lord Bajazet's eldest son, say that if he had his will when he came to the title, he would do what the sultans do, and clear the estate by chopping off all his younger brothers'", "바자제트 경의 장남인 조지 맥 터크가 이런 말을 하는 걸 나는 종종 들었지요. 자기가 작위를 물려받으면 마음대로 할 수만 있다면 술탄들처럼 해서, 동생들 목을 한꺼번에 죄다 쳐 버려 영지를 깨끗이 정리하겠다고 말입니다."], ["heads at once; and so the case is, more or less, with them all.", "정도의 차이는 있을지언정 그들 모두가 다 이런 식이지요."], ["I tell you they are all Turks in their hearts.", "내 말하건대, 그자들은 죄다 속으로는 투르크인입니다."], ["Pooh! sir, they know the world.\"", "흥, 손님, 그자들은 세상을 알거든요.\""], ["And here, haply, a great man coming up, Tom Eaves's hat would drop off his head, and he would rush forward with a bow and a grin, which showed that he knew the world too--in the Tomeavesian way, that is.", "그러다가 마침 위인 한 사람이 다가오기라도 하면, 톰 이브스의 모자는 머리에서 벗겨지고, 그는 절을 하며 히죽 웃는 얼굴로 달려 나갔는데, 그 모습은 그 역시 세상을 안다는 것을 보여 주는 것이었다. 그러니까, 톰 이브스 식으로 말이다."], ["And having laid out every shilling of his fortune on an annuity, Tom could afford to bear no malice to his nephews and nieces, and to have no other feeling with regard to his betters but a constant and generous desire to dine with them.", "그리고 자기 재산을 한 푼도 남김없이 연금에 쏟아부은 톰으로서는, 조카들에게 아무런 악감정도 품지 않을 만한 여유가 있었고, 윗사람들에 대해서는 그들과 함께 만찬을 들고 싶다는 변함없고 너그러운 소망 말고는 다른 감정을 품지 않을 여유가 있었다."]], [["Between the Marchioness and the natural and tender regard of mother for children, there was that cruel barrier placed of difference of faith.", "후작 부인과, 자식을 향한 어미의 자연스럽고 애틋한 정 사이에는, 신앙의 차이라는 저 잔인한 장벽이 가로놓여 있었다."], ["The very love which she might feel for her sons only served to render the timid and pious lady more fearful and unhappy.", "그녀가 아들들에게 느낄 법한 바로 그 사랑이, 오히려 이 소심하고 경건한 부인을 더욱 두렵고 불행하게 만들 따름이었다."], ["The gulf which separated them was fatal and impassable.", "그들을 갈라놓은 심연은 치명적이고 건널 수 없는 것이었다."], ["She could not stretch her weak arms across it, or draw her children over to that side away from which her belief told her there was no safety.", "그녀는 그 위로 가냘픈 두 팔을 뻗을 수도, 자기 신앙이 일러 주는바 안전이라곤 없는 저쪽으로부터 자식들을 이쪽으로 끌어당길 수도 없었다."], ["During the youth of his sons, Lord Steyne, who was a good scholar and amateur casuist, had no better sport in the evening after dinner in the country than in setting the boys'", "아들들이 어리던 시절, 학식이 깊고 궤변에도 일가견이 있던 스타인 경은, 시골에서 만찬을 든 뒤의 저녁나절에 이보다 더 좋은 소일거리가 없었다."], ["tutor, the Reverend Mr. Trail (now my Lord Bishop of Ealing) on her ladyship's director, Father Mole, over their wine, and in pitting Oxford against St.", "와인을 앞에 두고, 아들들의 가정교사인 트레일 목사(지금의 일링 주교)와 부인의 고해 신부인 몰 신부를 맞붙이고, 옥스퍼드와 생타셸을 서로 겨루게 하는 일 말이다."], ["Acheul. He cried \"Bravo, Latimer!", "그는 번갈아 가며 \"잘한다, 래티머!"], ["Well said, Loyola!\"", "옳거니, 로욜라!\""], ["alternately; he promised Mole a bishopric if he would come over, and vowed he would use all his influence to get Trail a cardinal's hat if he would secede.", "하고 외쳤다. 몰에게는 개종하면 주교 자리를 주겠노라 약속했고, 트레일에게는 탈교하면 추기경 모자를 얻어 주는 데 자기 영향력을 다 쓰겠노라 맹세했다."], ["Neither divine allowed himself to be conquered, and though the fond mother hoped that her youngest and favourite son would be reconciled to her church--his mother church--a sad and awful disappointment awaited the devout lady--a disappointment which seemed to be a judgement upon her for the sin of her marriage.", "두 성직자 어느 쪽도 굴복하려 들지 않았고, 자애로운 어미는 막내이자 가장 아끼는 아들이 제 교회, 그러니까 어미의 교회와 화해하기를 바랐건만, 이 경건한 부인을 기다리고 있던 것은 슬프고도 끔찍한 실망이었다. 그것은 마치 그녀의 결혼이라는 죄에 대한 심판처럼 보이는 실망이었다."]], [["My Lord Gaunt married, as every person who frequents the Peerage knows, the Lady Blanche Thistlewood, a daughter of the noble house of Bareacres, before mentioned in this veracious history.", "귀족 명감을 들춰 본 사람이라면 누구나 알다시피, 곤트 경은 블랑슈 시슬우드 영애와 결혼했다. 이 진실한 이야기에 앞서 언급된 바 있는 명문 베어에이커스 가문의 딸이었다."], ["A wing of Gaunt House was assigned to this couple; for the head of the family chose to govern it, and while he reigned to reign supreme; his son and heir, however, living little at home, disagreeing with his wife, and borrowing upon post-obits such moneys as he required beyond the very moderate sums which his father was disposed to allow him.", "곤트 저택의 한쪽 별채가 이 부부에게 배정되었다. 가문의 수장이 친히 저택을 다스리기를 택했고, 다스리는 동안에는 절대적으로 군림하기를 택했기 때문이다."], ["The Marquis knew every shilling of his son's debts.", "그러나 그의 아들이자 후계자는 집에 거의 머물지 않았고, 아내와 사이가 틀어졌으며, 아버지가 내주려는 지극히 인색한 액수를 넘어서서 필요한 돈은 상속 담보 차용증으로 빌려다 썼다."], ["At his lamented demise, he was found himself to be possessor of many of his heir's bonds, purchased for their benefit, and devised by his Lordship to the children of his younger son.", "후작은 아들의 빚을 한 푼까지 죄다 알고 있었다. 애석한 그의 죽음 뒤에, 후작 자신이 후계자의 채권을 여럿 소유하고 있었음이 드러났는데, 그것들은 그들을 위한답시고 사들였던 것을, 후작이 막내아들의 자식들에게 유증한 것이었다."]], [["As, to my Lord Gaunt's dismay, and the chuckling delight of his natural enemy and father, the Lady Gaunt had no children--the Lord George Gaunt was desired to return from Vienna, where he was engaged in waltzing and diplomacy, and to contract a matrimonial alliance with the Honourable Joan, only daughter of John Johnes, First Baron Helvellyn, and head of the firm of Jones, Brown, and Robinson, of Threadneedle Street, Bankers; from which union sprang several sons and daughters, whose doings do not appertain to this story.", "곤트 경에게는 낭패스럽게도, 또 그의 타고난 적이자 아비 되는 사람에게는 키득거릴 만큼 고소하게도, 곤트 부인에게는 자식이 없었다. 그리하여 빈에서 왈츠와 외교에 몰두하던 조지 곤트 경에게 돌아오라는 명이 떨어졌고, 명예로운 조앤 양과 혼인하라는 명이 떨어졌다. 그녀는 헬벨린의 초대 남작이자 스레드니들 거리의 은행가, 존스 브라운 로빈슨 상사의 수장인 존 존스의 외동딸이었다. 이 결합에서 여러 아들딸이 태어났으나, 그들의 행적은 이 이야기와 무관하다."]], [["The marriage at first was a happy and prosperous one.", "이 결혼은 처음에는 행복하고 순탄했다."], ["My Lord George Gaunt could not only read, but write pretty correctly.", "조지 곤트 경은 글을 읽을 줄 알았을 뿐만 아니라 제법 정확하게 쓸 줄도 알았다."], ["He spoke French with considerable fluency; and was one of the finest waltzers in Europe.", "프랑스어도 상당히 유창하게 구사했고, 유럽에서 손꼽히는 왈츠 명수이기도 했다."], ["With these talents, and his interest at home, there was little doubt that his lordship would rise to the highest dignities in his profession.", "이런 재능에다 본가의 후광까지 더해졌으니, 그가 외교관으로서 최고의 영예에 오르리라는 것은 의심할 여지가 거의 없었다."], ["The lady, his wife, felt that courts were her sphere, and her wealth enabled her to receive splendidly in those continental towns whither her husband's diplomatic duties led him.", "그의 아내인 부인은 궁정이야말로 자기 무대라고 느꼈고, 그 재력 덕분에 남편의 외교 임무가 이끄는 대륙의 도시들에서 호화로운 접대를 베풀 수 있었다."], ["There was talk of appointing him minister, and bets were laid at the Travellers'", "그를 공사로 임명한다는 말이 나돌았고, 트래블러스 클럽에서는 머지않아 그가 대사가 되리라는 데 내기가 걸렸다."], ["that he would be ambassador ere long, when of a sudden, rumours arrived of the secretary's extraordinary behaviour.", "그런데 별안간, 그 서기관의 괴이한 행동에 관한 소문이 날아들었다."], ["At a grand diplomatic dinner given by his chief, he had started up and declared that a pate de foie gras was poisoned.", "그의 상관이 베푼 성대한 외교 만찬에서, 그가 벌떡 일어나 거위 간 파테에 독이 들었다고 외쳤다는 것이었다."], ["He went to a ball at the hotel of the Bavarian envoy, the Count de Springbock-Hohenlaufen, with his head shaved and dressed as a Capuchin friar.", "그는 바이에른 공사인 슈프링복호헨라우펜 백작의 저택에서 열린 무도회에, 머리를 빡빡 밀고 카푸친 수도사 차림을 하고 나타났다."], ["It was not a masked ball, as some folks wanted to persuade you.", "어떤 이들이 그대에게 믿게 하려던 것처럼 가장무도회였던 것도 아니었다."], ["It was something queer, people whispered.", "뭔가 이상한 일이라고, 사람들은 수군거렸다."], ["His grandfather was so.", "그의 할아버지도 그랬다는 것이었다."], ["It was in the family.", "집안 내력이라는 것이었다."]], [["His wife and family returned to this country and took up their abode at Gaunt House.", "그의 아내와 자식들은 본국으로 돌아와 곤트 저택에 거처를 정했다."], ["Lord George gave up his post on the European continent, and was gazetted to Brazil.", "조지 경은 유럽 대륙의 직책을 내놓았고, 브라질로 발령이 났다고 관보에 실렸다. 그러나 사람들은 더 잘 알고 있었다."], ["But people knew better; he never returned from that Brazil expedition--never died there--never lived there--never was there at all.", "그는 그 브라질 원정에서 돌아온 적이 없었다. 그곳에서 죽은 적도, 그곳에서 산 적도, 애초에 그곳에 가 본 적도 없었다."], ["He was nowhere; he was gone out altogether.", "그는 아무 데도 없었다. 아예 사라져 버린 것이었다."], ["\"Brazil,\"", "\"브라질이라니,\""], ["said one gossip to another, with a grin--\"Brazil is St. John's Wood.", "한 입방아꾼이 다른 이에게 히죽거리며 말했다."], ["Rio de Janeiro is a cottage surrounded by four walls, and George Gaunt is accredited to a keeper, who has invested him with the order of the Strait-Waistcoat.\"", "\"브라질이란 세인트존스우드를 말하는 거지. 리우데자네이루란 사방이 담장으로 둘러싸인 별채 한 채를 말하는 거고, 조지 곤트는 그자를 구속복 훈장으로 치장해 준 어느 간수에게 파견된 셈이라네.\""], ["These are the kinds of epitaphs which men pass over one another in Vanity Fair.", "이런 것들이야말로 사람들이 허영의 시장에서 서로에게 바치는 부류의 묘비명인 것이다."]], [["Twice or thrice in a week, in the earliest morning, the poor mother went for her sins and saw the poor invalid.", "일주일에 두세 번씩, 이른 새벽이면 가엾은 어미는 제 죄를 갚으러 그 가엾은 병자를 보러 갔다."], ["Sometimes he laughed at her (and his laughter was more pitiful than to hear him cry); sometimes she found the brilliant dandy diplomatist of the Congress of Vienna dragging about a child's toy, or nursing the keeper's baby's doll.", "어떤 때는 그가 어미를 보고 웃었고(그 웃음은 우는 소리를 듣는 것보다 더 측은했다), 또 어떤 때는 빈 회의 시절의 그 눈부신 멋쟁이 외교관이 아이 장난감을 질질 끌고 다니거나 간수네 아기 인형을 어르고 있는 모습을 보기도 했다."], ["Sometimes he knew her and Father Mole, her director and companion; oftener he forgot her, as he had done wife, children, love, ambition, vanity.", "어떤 때는 그가 어미와, 어미의 고해 신부이자 길동무인 몰 신부를 알아보았으나, 더 자주 어미를 잊어버렸다. 아내며 자식이며 사랑이며 야망이며 허영을 잊어버린 것처럼 말이다."], ["But he remembered his dinner-hour, and used to cry if his wine-and-water was not strong enough.", "그러나 그는 제 식사 시간만은 기억하고 있어서, 물 탄 와인이 충분히 진하지 않으면 울곤 했다."]], [["It was the mysterious taint of the blood; the poor mother had brought it from her own ancient race.", "그것은 핏줄에 깃든 알 수 없는 오점이었다. 가엾은 어미가 제 유서 깊은 가문에서 그것을 가져온 것이었다."], ["The evil had broken out once or twice in the father's family, long before Lady Steyne's sins had begun, or her fasts and tears and penances had been offered in their expiation.", "그 화근은 스타인 부인의 죄가 시작되기도, 그 죄를 속죄하느라 그녀의 단식과 눈물과 고행이 바쳐지기도 한참 전에, 아비 쪽 집안에서도 한두 번 터져 나온 적이 있었다."], ["The pride of the race was struck down as the first-born of Pharaoh.", "그 가문의 자랑거리는 파라오의 맏아들처럼 쓰러졌다."], ["The dark mark of fate and doom was on the threshold--the tall old threshold surmounted by coronets and caned heraldry.", "운명과 파멸의 어두운 표지가 그 문지방에 새겨져 있었다. 작위 관과 새겨진 문장이 위에 얹힌, 높다랗고 오래된 그 문지방에 말이다."]], [["The absent lord's children meanwhile prattled and grew on quite unconscious that the doom was over them too.", "한편 그 부재중인 경의 자식들은, 자기들 머리 위에도 그 파멸이 드리워져 있다는 것을 까맣게 모른 채 재잘거리며 자라났다."], ["First they talked of their father and devised plans against his return.", "처음에 아이들은 아버지 이야기를 하며 그가 돌아오면 무얼 할지 궁리를 짜냈다."], ["Then the name of the living dead man was less frequently in their mouth--then not mentioned at all.", "그러다가 산 채로 죽은 그 사람의 이름이 그들 입에 오르는 일이 차츰 뜸해졌고, 마침내 아예 입에 오르지 않게 되었다."], ["But the stricken old grandmother trembled to think that these too were the inheritors of their father's shame as well as of his honours, and watched sickening for the day when the awful ancestral curse should come down on them.", "그러나 충격에 잠긴 늙은 할머니는, 이 아이들 또한 제 아비의 명예뿐 아니라 그 수치까지 물려받은 후계자들이라는 생각에 몸을 떨었고, 저 무시무시한 조상 대대로의 저주가 이 아이들에게 내려올 그날을 병들 듯 노심초사하며 지켜보았다."]], [["This dark presentiment also haunted Lord Steyne.", "이 어두운 예감은 스타인 경 또한 따라다니며 괴롭혔다."], ["He tried to lay the horrid bedside ghost in Red Seas of wine and jollity, and lost sight of it sometimes in the crowd and rout of his pleasures.", "그는 그 끔찍한 머리맡의 유령을 와인과 흥청거림이라는 홍해에 가라앉히려 애썼고, 향락의 무리와 소동 속에서 이따금 그것을 시야에서 놓치곤 했다."], ["But it always came back to him when alone, and seemed to grow more threatening with years.", "그러나 그것은 혼자 있을 때면 늘 그에게 되돌아왔고, 세월이 흐를수록 더욱 위협적으로 자라나는 듯했다."], ["\"I have taken your son,\"", "\"네 아들을 데려갔거늘,\""], ["it said, \"why not you?", "그것은 말했다."], ["I may shut you up in a prison some day like your son George.", "\"어찌 너라고 못 데려가랴? 언젠가 네 아들 조지처럼 너를 감방에 가둘 수도 있느니."], ["I may tap you on the head to-morrow, and away go pleasure and honours, feasts and beauty, friends, flatterers, French cooks, fine horses and houses--in exchange for a prison, a keeper, and a straw mattress like George Gaunt's.\"", "내일이라도 네 머리를 툭 칠 수 있느니. 그러면 향락도 명예도, 잔치도 미색도, 벗도 아첨꾼도, 프랑스 요리사도, 좋은 말과 집도 다 사라지고, 그 대신 감방과 간수와 조지 곤트의 것 같은 짚 깔개가 남으리라.\""], ["And then my lord would defy the ghost which threatened him, for he knew of a remedy by which he could baulk his enemy.", "그러면 후작은 자기를 위협하는 그 유령에게 맞섰다. 적의 코를 납작하게 만들 수 있는 비책을 알고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["So there was splendour and wealth, but no great happiness perchance, behind the tall caned portals of Gaunt House with its smoky coronets and ciphers.", "그리하여 곤트 저택의, 그을음 낀 작위 관과 머리글자가 새겨진 높다란 문 안쪽에는 화려함과 부가 있었으나, 어쩌면 그리 대단한 행복은 없었던 것이다."], ["The feasts there were of the grandest in London, but there was not overmuch content therewith, except among the guests who sat at my lord's table.", "그곳의 잔치는 런던에서 가장 성대한 축에 들었으나, 후작의 식탁에 앉은 손님들 사이를 빼고는 거기에 그리 큰 만족이라곤 없었다."], ["Had he not been so great a Prince very few possibly would have visited him; but in Vanity Fair the sins of very great personages are looked at indulgently.", "그가 그토록 대단한 대공이 아니었던들, 그를 찾아오는 이는 아마 몇 안 되었을 것이다. 그러나 허영의 시장에서는 아주 높으신 분들의 죄란 너그럽게 보아 넘겨지는 법이다."], ["\"Nous regardons a deux fois\" (as the French lady said) before we condemn a person of my lord's undoubted quality.", "(어느 프랑스 부인이 말했듯이) \"누 르가르동 아 되 푸아\"—후작처럼 의심할 여지 없이 지체 높은 인물을 단죄하기에 앞서 우리는 \"두 번 들여다보는\""], ["Some notorious carpers and squeamish moralists might be sulky with Lord Steyne, but they were glad enough to come when he asked them.", "것이다. 이름난 트집쟁이나 까다로운 도덕군자 몇몇이 스타인 경에게 뾰로통할 수도 있었지만, 그가 청하면 그들은 기꺼이 달려왔다."]], [["\"Lord Steyne is really too bad,\"", "\"스타인 경은 정말이지 너무 못됐어요.\""], ["Lady Slingstone said, \"but everybody goes, and of course I shall see that my girls come to no harm.\"", "슬링스톤 부인은 말했다. \"하지만 다들 가니까, 나도 물론 우리 딸들이 해를 입지 않도록 잘 살펴야지요.\""], ["\"His lordship is a man to whom I owe much, everything in life,\"", "\"그분으로 말하자면 내가 많은 신세를, 아니 인생의 모든 것을 빚진 분이지요.\""], ["said the Right Reverend Doctor Trail, thinking that the Archbishop was rather shaky, and Mrs. Trail and the young ladies would as soon have missed going to church as to one of his lordship's parties.", "트레일 주교는 이렇게 말했는데, 대주교 자리가 다소 위태롭다고 여기던 터였고, 트레일 부인과 그 댁 따님들은 교회에 빠지는 것이나 그분의 연회에 빠지는 것이나 똑같이 꺼릴 지경이었다."], ["\"His morals are bad,\"", "\"그분 품행은 나빠.\""], ["said little Lord Southdown to his sister, who meekly expostulated, having heard terrific legends from her mamma with respect to the doings at Gaunt House; \"but hang it, he's got the best dry Sillery in Europe!\"", "어린 사우스다운 경은, 곤트 저택에서 벌어지는 일들에 관해 어머니에게서 무시무시한 전설을 들은 터라 얌전히 항변하던 누이에게 이렇게 말했다. \"하지만 어쩌겠어, 그 댁엔 유럽에서 가장 좋은 드라이 실레리가 있는걸!\""], ["And as for Sir Pitt Crawley, Bart.--Sir Pitt that pattern of decorum, Sir Pitt who had led off at missionary meetings--he never for one moment thought of not going too.", "그리고 준남작 피트 크롤리 경으로 말하자면—저 예법의 본보기인 피트 경, 선교회 모임에서 앞장서 사회를 보던 피트 경으로 말하자면—그 역시 가지 않겠다는 생각은 한순간도 하지 않았다."], ["\"Where you see such persons as the Bishop of Ealing and the Countess of Slingstone, you may be pretty sure, Jane,\"", "\"일링 주교와 슬링스톤 백작 부인 같은 분들이 보이는 자리라면, 제인, 우리가 틀릴 리는 없다고 봐도 무방하지.\""], ["the Baronet would say, \"that we cannot be wrong.", "준남작은 이렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["The great rank and station of Lord Steyne put him in a position to command people in our station in life.", "\"스타인 경의 높은 지위와 신분으로 보아, 그분은 우리 같은 처지의 사람들을 좌지우지할 만한 위치에 계신 거요."], ["The Lord Lieutenant of a County, my dear, is a respectable man.", "한 주의 부지사라면, 여보, 존경할 만한 인물 아니겠소."], ["Besides, George Gaunt and I were intimate in early life; he was my junior when we were attaches at Pumpernickel together.\"", "게다가 조지 곤트와 나는 젊은 시절에 가깝게 지냈고, 우리가 품퍼니켈에서 함께 외교관 보좌로 있을 때 그는 내 후배였단 말이오.\""]], [["In a word everybody went to wait upon this great man--everybody who was asked, as you the reader (do not say nay) or I the writer hereof would go if we had an invitation.", "한마디로, 청을 받은 사람이라면 누구나 이 대단한 인물에게 문안을 드리러 갔다. 독자인 그대(아니라고 하지 마시라)나 이 글을 쓰는 나라도, 초대를 받았더라면 갔을 것처럼 말이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLVIII", "# CHAPTER XLVIII"]], [["In Which the Reader Is Introduced to the Very Best of Company\nAt last Becky's kindness and attention to the chief of her husband's family were destined to meet with an exceeding great reward, a reward which, though certainly somewhat unsubstantial, the little woman coveted with greater eagerness than more positive benefits.", "마침내 베키가 남편 집안의 어른에게 베푼 친절과 정성은 더없이 큰 보상을 받게 될 운명이었다. 비록 그 보상이 다소 실속 없는 것이긴 했어도, 이 자그마한 여인은 그보다 더 확실한 이득보다도 그것을 한층 더 열렬히 갈망했다."], ["If she did not wish to lead a virtuous life, at least she desired to enjoy a character for virtue, and we know that no lady in the genteel world can possess this desideratum, until she has put on a train and feathers and has been presented to her Sovereign at Court.", "비록 그녀가 정숙한 삶을 살고 싶어 한 것은 아니었더라도, 적어도 정숙하다는 평판만큼은 누리고 싶어 했던 것이다. 그리고 우리가 알다시피, 상류 사회의 어떤 숙녀도 끌리는 옷자락과 깃 장식을 두르고 궁정에서 군주를 알현하기 전까지는 이 절실한 소망을 손에 넣을 수가 없는 법이다."], ["From that august interview they come out stamped as honest women.", "그 장엄한 알현을 마치고 나오면 그들은 정숙한 여인이라는 도장이 찍힌 셈이 된다."], ["The Lord Chamberlain gives them a certificate of virtue.", "시종장이 그들에게 정숙함의 증명서를 발급해 주는 것이다."], ["And as dubious goods or letters are passed through an oven at quarantine, sprinkled with aromatic vinegar, and then pronounced clean, many a lady, whose reputation would be doubtful otherwise and liable to give infection, passes through the wholesome ordeal of the Royal presence and issues from it free from all taint.", "그리고 미심쩍은 물품이나 편지가 검역소에서 화덕을 거치고 향초를 뿌린 뒤에야 깨끗하다고 선언되듯이, 그러지 않았다면 평판이 미심쩍어 전염병을 옮길 우려가 있었을 숱한 숙녀들이, 왕실 알현이라는 이 위생적인 시련을 거치고 나면 온갖 오염에서 벗어난 몸으로 나오는 것이었다."]], [["It might be very well for my Lady Bareacres, my Lady Tufto, Mrs. Bute Crawley in the country, and other ladies who had come into contact with Mrs.", "베어에이커스 부인이나 터프토 부인, 시골의 뷰트 크롤리 부인, 그리고 로던 크롤리 부인과 마주친 적이 있는 다른 숙녀들로서야, 그 가증스러운 자그마한 모험가가 군주 앞에서 무릎을 굽혀 인사를 올린다는 발상에 흥하고 콧방귀를 뀌며, 만약 어질고 인자한 샬럿 왕비가 살아 계셨더라면 그토록 단정치 못한 인물을 그 정결한 응접실에 결코 들이지 않으셨으리라 단언했을 법도 하다."], ["Rawdon Crawley to cry fie at the idea of the odious little adventuress making her curtsey before the Sovereign, and to declare that, if dear good Queen Charlotte had been alive, she never would have admitted such an extremely ill-regulated personage into her chaste drawing-room.", "그러나 로던 부인이 그 시험을 치르고, 말하자면 평판이라는 학위를 받은 자리가 다름 아닌 유럽 제일의 신사가 친히 임석한 자리였음을 헤아려 본다면, 그녀의 정숙함을 더 이상 의심한다는 것은 분명 노골적인 불충임이 틀림없을 것이다."], ["But when we consider that it was the First Gentleman in Europe in whose high presence Mrs.", "나로 말하자면, 역사 속의 그 위대한 인물을 사랑과 경외의 마음으로 돌이켜 본다."], ["Rawdon passed her examination, and as it were, took her degree in reputation, it surely must be flat disloyalty to doubt any more about her virtue. I, for my part, look back with love and awe to that Great Character in history.", "아, 저 존경스럽고 장엄한 존재가 이 제국의 세련되고 교양 있는 부류의 만장일치 갈채에 힘입어 그 왕국의 으뜸가는 신사라는 칭호를 받았을 때, 허영의 시장에는 숙녀의 자질에 대한 얼마나 드높고 고결한 안목이 있었던 것인지!"], ["Ah, what a high and noble appreciation of Gentlewomanhood there must have been in Vanity Fair, when that revered and august being was invested, by the universal acclaim of the refined and educated portion of this empire, with the title of Premier Gentilhomme of his Kingdom. Do you remember, dear M--, oh friend of my youth, how one blissful night five-and-twenty years since, the \"Hypocrite\"", "친애하는 엠— 군, 오 내 젊은 날의 벗이여, 그대는 기억하는가, 스물다섯 해 전 어느 더없이 행복한 밤을, 「위선자」가 상연되고 엘리스턴이 극장 지배인이었으며 다우턴과 리스턴이 배우로 나왔던 그날, 슬로터하우스 학교에서 교육받던 두 소년이 충성스러운 선생들의 허락을 받아 학교를 빠져나와, 왕을 맞이하려 거기 모인 군중 틈에 끼어 드루리 레인 극장 무대에 모습을 드러냈던 일을 말이다."], ["being acted, Elliston being manager, Dowton and Liston performers, two boys had leave from their loyal masters to go out from Slaughter-House School where they were educated and to appear on Drury Lane stage, amongst a crowd which assembled there to greet the king.", "왕이라고?"], ["THE KING? There he was.", "그분이 거기 계셨다."], ["Beefeaters were before the august box; the Marquis of Steyne (Lord of the Powder Closet) and other great officers of state were behind the chair on which he sat, HE sat--florid of face, portly of person, covered with orders, and in a rich curling head of hair--how we sang God save him!", "그 장엄한 특별석 앞에는 근위 호위병들이 있었고, 화장방 시종장이신 스타인 후작과 다른 고관대작들이 그분이 앉으신 의자 뒤에 서 있었으며, 그분이 앉아 계셨다—불그레한 얼굴에 풍채는 당당하고, 온갖 훈장을 두르고, 풍성하게 곱슬거리는 머리채를 하신 채로. 우리가 그분을 위해 「하느님 폐하를 지키소서」를 어떻게 불렀던가!"], ["How the house rocked and shouted with that magnificent music.", "그 장엄한 음악에 극장이 어떻게 흔들리며 함성으로 들끓었던가."], ["How they cheered, and cried, and waved handkerchiefs.", "사람들이 어떻게 환호하고, 외치고, 손수건을 흔들었던가."], ["Ladies wept; mothers clasped their children; some fainted with emotion.", "숙녀들은 울었고, 어머니들은 자식을 끌어안았으며, 더러는 감격에 겨워 정신을 잃기도 했다."], ["People were suffocated in the pit, shrieks and groans rising up amidst the writhing and shouting mass there of his people who were, and indeed showed themselves almost to be, ready to die for him.", "일층 관람석에서는 사람들이 숨이 막힐 지경이었고, 그분을 위해서라면 기꺼이 죽을 각오가 되어 있던—실로 거의 그래 보이기까지 했던—백성들이 몸부림치며 외쳐 대는 무리 속에서 비명과 신음이 솟아올랐다. 그렇다, 우리는 그분을 보았다."], ["Yes, we saw him. Fate cannot deprive us of THAT.", "운명도 그것만큼은 우리에게서 앗아 가지 못한다."], ["Others have seen Napoleon.", "더러는 나폴레옹을 보았을 것이다."], ["Some few still exist who have beheld Frederick the Great, Doctor Johnson, Marie Antoinette, &c.--be it our reasonable boast to our children, that we saw George the Good, the Magnificent, the Great.", "프리드리히 대왕이며 존슨 박사며 마리 앙투아네트 따위를 직접 본 이도 아직 몇몇 살아 있을 것이다. 우리는 자손들에게 우리가 선량하고 장엄하며 위대하신 조지 임금을 뵈었노라고 자랑해도 마땅하리라."]], [["Well, there came a happy day in Mrs.", "자, 이윽고 로던 크롤리 부인의 삶에 행복한 날이 찾아왔다."], ["Rawdon Crawley's existence when this angel was admitted into the paradise of a Court which she coveted, her sister-in-law acting as her godmother.", "그토록 갈망하던 궁정이라는 낙원에 이 천사가 받아들여지고, 손위 동서가 그녀의 후견인 노릇을 한 것이었다."], ["On the appointed day, Sir Pitt and his lady, in their great family carriage (just newly built, and ready for the Baronet's assumption of the office of High Sheriff of his county), drove up to the little house in Curzon Street, to the edification of Raggles, who was watching from his greengrocer's shop, and saw fine plumes within, and enormous bunches of flowers in the breasts of the new livery-coats of the footmen.", "정해진 날이 되자, 피트 경과 그 부인이 큼직한 가족용 마차에 올라(이 마차는 그 준남작이 주의 주장관 직무를 맡게 된 것에 대비해 막 새로 지어진 것이었다) 커즌 거리의 자그마한 집으로 다가왔는데, 채소 가게에서 지켜보던 래글스에게는 그야말로 교화가 될 만한 광경이었다. 안에 꽂힌 고운 깃 장식이 보였고, 새로 지은 하인들의 제복 가슴팍에는 커다란 꽃다발이 달려 있었다."]], [["Sir Pitt, in a glittering uniform, descended and went into Curzon Street, his sword between his legs.", "피트 경은 번쩍이는 예복 차림으로 마차에서 내려, 다리 사이에 검을 찬 채 커즌 거리로 들어갔다."], ["Little Rawdon stood with his face against the parlour window-panes, smiling and nodding with all his might to his aunt in the carriage within; and presently Sir Pitt issued forth from the house again, leading forth a lady with grand feathers, covered in a white shawl, and holding up daintily a train of magnificent brocade.", "어린 로던은 응접실 창유리에 얼굴을 바짝 댄 채, 마차 안에 있는 숙모를 향해 온 힘을 다해 미소 짓고 고개를 끄덕였다. 이윽고 피트 경이 다시 집에서 나오면서 한 숙녀를 이끌고 나왔는데, 그녀는 화려한 깃 장식을 꽂고 흰 숄을 두른 채, 더없이 화려한 브로케이드 자락을 우아하게 추켜들고 있었다."], ["She stepped into the vehicle as if she were a princess and accustomed all her life to go to Court, smiling graciously on the footman at the door and on Sir Pitt, who followed her into the carriage.", "그녀는 마치 공주라도 된 양, 평생토록 궁정 출입을 해 온 사람인 양 마차에 올라타며, 문가의 하인에게도 자기를 따라 마차에 오른 피트 경에게도 우아하게 미소를 지어 보였다."]], [["Then Rawdon followed in his old Guards' uniform, which had grown woefully shabby, and was much too tight.", "뒤이어 로던이 옛 근위대 예복 차림으로 따라 올랐는데, 그 옷은 한심하리만큼 낡아빠진 데다 몸에 너무 끼었다."], ["He was to have followed the procession and waited upon his sovereign in a cab, but that his good-natured sister-in-law insisted that they should be a family party.", "본래 그는 행렬을 뒤따라 따로 삯마차를 타고 가서 군주를 알현하기로 되어 있었으나, 인심 좋은 손위 동서가 한 가족끼리 다 함께 가야 한다고 우긴 것이었다."], ["The coach was large, the ladies not very big, they would hold their trains in their laps--finally, the four went fraternally together, and their carriage presently joined the line of royal equipages which was making its way down Piccadilly and St. James's Street, towards the old brick palace where the Star of Brunswick was in waiting to receive his nobles and gentlefolks.", "마차는 컸고 숙녀들은 그리 덩치가 크지 않았으니, 옷자락은 무릎 위에 받쳐 들면 될 일이었다—마침내 네 사람은 화목하게 함께 길을 나섰고, 그들의 마차는 이내 피커딜리와 세인트 제임스 거리를 따라, 브런즈윅의 별이 귀족과 양가의 사람들을 맞이하려 기다리고 있는 그 오래된 벽돌 궁전을 향해 내려가는 왕실 마차들의 행렬에 끼어들었다."]], [["Becky felt as if she could bless the people out of the carriage windows, so elated was she in spirit, and so strong a sense had she of the dignified position which she had at last attained in life.", "베키는 어찌나 마음이 들떴던지, 또 마침내 인생에서 도달한 그 위엄 있는 지위가 어찌나 강하게 느껴졌던지, 마차 창밖으로 사람들에게 축복이라도 내려 주고 싶은 심정이었다."], ["Even our Becky had her weaknesses, and as one often sees how men pride themselves upon excellences which others are slow to perceive: how, for instance, Comus firmly believes that he is the greatest tragic actor in England; how Brown, the famous novelist, longs to be considered, not a man of genius, but a man of fashion; while Robinson, the great lawyer, does not in the least care about his reputation in Westminster Hall, but believes himself incomparable across country and at a five-barred gate--so to be, and to be thought, a respectable woman was Becky's aim in life, and she got up the genteel with amazing assiduity, readiness, and success.", "우리의 베키마저도 약점이 있었던 것이다. 흔히 보다시피 사람들이란 남들은 좀처럼 알아주지 않는 장기를 두고 스스로 우쭐대는 법이어서—이를테면 코머스는 자기가 영국 제일의 비극 배우라고 굳게 믿고, 그 유명한 소설가 브라운은 천재로 여겨지기보다 멋쟁이로 여겨지기를 갈망하며, 위대한 법률가 로빈슨은 웨스트민스터 법정에서의 평판 따위에는 도통 관심이 없고 들판에서 다섯 단짜리 울타리를 뛰어넘는 솜씨에서만큼은 자기가 견줄 자 없다고 믿는 것이다—그와 마찬가지로 점잖은 여인이 되는 것, 그리고 점잖은 여인으로 여겨지는 것이 바로 베키의 인생 목표였고, 그녀는 놀라우리만치 부지런하고 능숙하게, 또 성공적으로 그 우아함을 익혀 나갔다."], ["We have said, there were times when she believed herself to be a fine lady and forgot that there was no money in the chest at home--duns round the gate, tradesmen to coax and wheedle--no ground to walk upon, in a word.", "앞서 말했듯이, 그녀는 이따금 자신이 정말로 귀부인이라고 믿으며, 집 안 금고에는 돈 한 푼 없다는 것도—문 앞에 빚쟁이들이 진을 치고 있고, 상인들을 어르고 구슬려야 하며, 한마디로 발붙일 땅조차 없다는 것도 잊어버리곤 했다."], ["And as she went to Court in the carriage, the family carriage, she adopted a demeanour so grand, self-satisfied, deliberate, and imposing that it made even Lady Jane laugh.", "그래서 마차를, 그러니까 그 가족용 마차를 타고 궁정으로 가면서, 그녀는 어찌나 거창하고 흡족하고 신중하고 위풍당당한 태도를 취했던지 제인 부인마저도 웃음을 터뜨릴 지경이었다."], ["She walked into the royal apartments with a toss of the head which would have befitted an empress, and I have no doubt had she been one, she would have become the character perfectly.", "그녀는 황후에게나 어울릴 법한 도도한 고갯짓으로 왕실 거실에 걸어 들어갔으니, 만약 그녀가 정말 황후였더라면 그 배역을 완벽하게 소화해 냈으리라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없다."]], [["We are authorized to state that Mrs. Rawdon Crawley's costume de cour on the occasion of her presentation to the Sovereign was of the most elegant and brilliant description.", "로던 크롤리 부인이 군주를 알현하던 그날 차려입은 궁정 예복은 더없이 우아하고 눈부신 것이었음을 우리는 자신 있게 밝힐 수 있다."], ["Some ladies we may have seen--we who wear stars and cordons and attend the St. James's assemblies, or we, who, in muddy boots, dawdle up and down Pall Mall and peep into the coaches as they drive up with the great folks in their feathers--some ladies of fashion, I say, we may have seen, about two o'clock of the forenoon of a levee day, as the laced-jacketed band of the Life Guards are blowing triumphal marches seated on those prancing music-stools, their cream-coloured chargers--who are by no means lovely and enticing objects at that early period of noon.", "별 모양 훈장과 띠를 두르고 세인트 제임스 궁의 모임에 참석하는 우리, 혹은 진흙투성이 장화를 신고 펠멜 거리를 어슬렁거리며 깃 장식을 단 귀인들을 태우고 다가오는 마차 안을 기웃거리는 우리라면, 알현일 오전 두 시쯤에 더러 멋쟁이 숙녀들을 본 적이 있을 것이다."], ["A stout countess of sixty, decolletee, painted, wrinkled with rouge up to her drooping eyelids, and diamonds twinkling in her wig, is a wholesome and edifying, but not a pleasant sight. She has the faded look of a St.", "그 시각이면 끈으로 장식한 제복 차림의 근위 기병대 악단이 껑충대는 음악 의자 같은 크림색 군마에 올라앉아 개선 행진곡을 불어 대는데, 그 한낮 이른 시각에 그 숙녀들은 결코 사랑스럽거나 매혹적인 대상이 아니었다."], ["James's Street illumination, as it may be seen of an early morning, when half the lamps are out, and the others are blinking wanly, as if they were about to vanish like ghosts before the dawn.", "예순 살 먹은 뚱뚱한 백작 부인이 가슴을 깊이 드러내고, 분칠을 하고, 처진 눈꺼풀까지 연지가 주름 속에 끼어 있고, 가발에는 다이아몬드가 반짝이는 모습이란, 보기에 유익하고 교화가 될지언정 결코 즐거운 광경은 아니다. 그 모습은 세인트 제임스 거리의 불빛이 이른 아침에 절반쯤 꺼지고 나머지는 마치 새벽 앞에서 유령처럼 사라지려는 듯 희미하게 깜박이는 그 시들한 꼴과도 같다."], ["Such charms as those of which we catch glimpses while her ladyship's carriage passes should appear abroad at night alone.", "부인의 마차가 지나갈 때 우리가 언뜻 보게 되는 그런 매력이란 오직 밤에만 바깥에 나타나야 마땅한 것이다."], ["If even Cynthia looks haggard of an afternoon, as we may see her sometimes in the present winter season, with Phoebus staring her out of countenance from the opposite side of the heavens, how much more can old Lady Castlemouldy keep her head up when the sun is shining full upon it through the chariot windows, and showing all the chinks and crannies with which time has marked her face!", "신시아조차도, 우리가 올겨울 철에 이따금 보듯, 하늘 맞은편에서 포이보스가 뚫어지게 쏘아보아 무안하게 만들 때면 오후엔 초췌해 보이거늘, 하물며 늙은 캐슬몰디 부인이 마차 창으로 햇빛이 정통으로 쏟아져 들어 세월이 그 얼굴에 새겨 놓은 온갖 금과 틈을 죄다 드러낼 때 어찌 고개를 들고 있을 수 있겠는가! 안 될 일이다."], ["No. Drawing-rooms should be announced for November, or the first foggy day, or the elderly sultanas of our Vanity Fair should drive up in closed litters, descend in a covered way, and make their curtsey to the Sovereign under the protection of lamplight.", "알현은 십일월이나 첫 안개 끼는 날로 잡아야 하든가, 아니면 우리 허영의 시장의 늙은 여왕님들이 닫힌 가마를 타고 와서 가린 통로로 내려, 등불의 비호 아래 군주께 무릎을 굽혀 인사를 올려야 할 것이다."]], [["Our beloved Rebecca had no need, however, of any such a friendly halo to set off her beauty.", "그러나 우리의 사랑스러운 레베카에게는 그 미모를 돋보이게 할 그런 다정한 후광 따위가 전혀 필요치 않았다."], ["Her complexion could bear any sunshine as yet, and her dress, though if you were to see it now, any present lady of Vanity Fair would pronounce it to be the most foolish and preposterous attire ever worn, was as handsome in her eyes and those of the public, some five-and-twenty years since, as the most brilliant costume of the most famous beauty of the present season.", "그녀의 살결은 아직 어떤 햇살도 견딜 수 있었고, 그녀의 옷은—만약 지금 그것을 본다면 오늘날 허영의 시장의 어느 숙녀라도 여태 입어 본 가장 어리석고 터무니없는 차림새라고 단언하겠지만—이십오 년쯤 전 당시에는 그녀의 눈에도 세상 사람들의 눈에도, 이번 철 가장 이름난 미인의 가장 눈부신 의상만큼이나 멋들어진 것이었다."], ["A score of years hence that too, that milliner's wonder, will have passed into the domain of the absurd, along with all previous vanities.", "이십 년쯤 지나면 그 의상 역시, 그 모자 가게 장인의 걸작 역시 이전의 모든 허영과 더불어 우스꽝스러움의 영역으로 넘어가 있을 것이다."], ["But we are wandering too much. Mrs.", "그러나 우리는 너무 곁길로 새고 말았다."], ["Rawdon's dress was pronounced to be charmante on the eventful day of her presentation.", "그녀가 알현하던 그 뜻깊은 날, 로던 부인의 옷차림은 매혹적이라는 평을 들었다."], ["Even good little Lady Jane was forced to acknowledge this effect, as she looked at her kinswoman, and owned sorrowfully to herself that she was quite inferior in taste to Mrs. Becky.", "마음씨 고운 자그마한 제인 부인조차도 그 친척을 바라보며 그 효과를 인정하지 않을 수 없었고, 자기는 베키 부인에 비하면 취향이 한참 떨어진다고 서글피 속으로 시인했다."]], [["She did not know how much care, thought, and genius Mrs. Rawdon had bestowed upon that garment.", "제인 부인은 로던 부인이 그 옷에 얼마나 많은 정성과 궁리와 재간을 쏟았는지 알지 못했다."], ["Rebecca had as good taste as any milliner in Europe, and such a clever way of doing things as Lady Jane little understood.", "레베카는 유럽의 어느 모자 가게 장인 못지않은 안목을 지녔고, 일을 처리하는 솜씨가 어찌나 영리했던지 제인 부인으로서는 도무지 짐작도 못 할 정도였다."], ["The latter quickly spied out the magnificence of the brocade of Becky's train, and the splendour of the lace on her dress.", "제인 부인은 곧 베키의 옷자락을 이룬 브로케이드의 화려함과 옷에 달린 레이스의 눈부심을 알아챘다."]], [["The brocade was an old remnant, Becky said; and as for the lace, it was a great bargain.", "브로케이드는 오래된 자투리 천이라고 베키는 말했다. 레이스로 말하자면 아주 싸게 산 것이라고 했다."], ["She had had it these hundred years.", "백 년도 더 전부터 가지고 있던 것이라는 얘기였다."]], [["\"My dear Mrs. Crawley, it must have cost a little fortune,\"", "\"크롤리 부인, 분명 적잖은 값이 나갔을 거예요.\""], ["Lady Jane said, looking down at her own lace, which was not nearly so good; and then examining the quality of the ancient brocade which formed the material of Mrs.", "제인 부인은 그러면서 자기 레이스를 내려다보았는데, 그것은 베키 것에 비하면 한참 못한 것이었다."], ["Rawdon's Court dress, she felt inclined to say that she could not afford such fine clothing, but checked that speech, with an effort, as one uncharitable to her kinswoman.", "그러고는 로던 부인의 궁정 예복 옷감인 그 오래된 브로케이드의 품질을 살펴보고는, 자기는 그런 고급 옷감은 엄두도 못 낸다고 말하고 싶은 마음이 들었으나, 친척에게 야박한 말이라 여겨 애써 그 말을 삼갔다."]], [["And yet, if Lady Jane had known all, I think even her kindly temper would have failed her.", "그러나 만약 제인 부인이 전말을 다 알았더라면, 그토록 인정 많은 성품마저도 무너지고 말았으리라 싶다."], ["The fact is, when she was putting Sir Pitt's house in order, Mrs. Rawdon had found the lace and the brocade in old wardrobes, the property of the former ladies of the house, and had quietly carried the goods home, and had suited them to her own little person.", "사실인즉, 로던 부인은 피트 경의 집을 정돈하던 중에 그 집안의 옛 안주인들 소유였던 레이스와 브로케이드를 낡은 옷장에서 발견하고는, 슬그머니 그 물건들을 집으로 가져와 자기의 자그마한 몸에 맞게 손질한 것이었다."], ["Briggs saw her take them, asked no questions, told no stories; but I believe quite sympathised with her on this matter, and so would many another honest woman.", "브리그스는 그녀가 그것을 가져가는 것을 보았으나 아무것도 묻지 않았고 어디에도 발설하지 않았다. 다만 나는 브리그스가 이 문제에서 그녀에게 깊이 공감했으리라 믿으며, 정직한 다른 많은 여인들 또한 그러했을 것이다."]], [["And the diamonds--\"Where the doose did you get the diamonds, Becky?\"", "그리고 다이아몬드는—\"대체 그 다이아몬드는 어디서 났소, 베키?\""], ["said her husband, admiring some jewels which he had never seen before and which sparkled in her ears and on her neck with brilliance and profusion.", "남편이 물었다. 그는 여태 한 번도 본 적 없는, 그녀의 귀와 목에서 눈부시고도 풍성하게 반짝이는 그 보석들을 감탄하며 바라보았다."]], [["Becky blushed a little and looked at him hard for a moment.", "베키는 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 잠시 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다."], ["Pitt Crawley blushed a little too, and looked out of window.", "피트 크롤리도 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 창밖으로 시선을 돌렸다."], ["The fact is, he had given her a very small portion of the brilliants; a pretty diamond clasp, which confined a pearl necklace which she wore--and the Baronet had omitted to mention the circumstance to his lady.", "사실인즉, 그 보석들 가운데 아주 일부를 그가 그녀에게 준 것이었다. 그녀가 두른 진주 목걸이를 여미는 예쁜 다이아몬드 잠금쇠 말이다—그런데 준남작은 그 사정을 자기 부인에게 미처 말해 두지 않았던 것이다."]], [["Becky looked at her husband, and then at Sir Pitt, with an air of saucy triumph--as much as to say, \"Shall I betray you?\"", "베키는 남편을 보고, 다음엔 피트 경을 보았다. 마치 \"제가 비밀을 폭로해 버릴까요?\" 하고 말하는 듯, 짓궂게 의기양양한 기색을 띤 채로."]], [["\"Guess!\"", "\"맞혀 보세요!\""], ["she said to her husband.", "그녀가 남편에게 말했다. \"어머, 이 어리석은 양반아.\""], ["\"Why, you silly man,\"", "그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["she continued, \"where do you suppose I got them?--all except the little clasp, which a dear friend of mine gave me long ago.", "\"이걸 대체 어디서 났겠어요?—오래전에 어느 정다운 친구가 준 작은 잠금쇠 하나만 빼고 말이에요. 빌린 거랍니다, 그럼요."], ["I hired them, to be sure.", "코번트리 거리의 폴로니어스 씨 가게에서 빌렸어요."], ["I hired them at Mr. Polonius's, in Coventry Street.", "궁정에 드나드는 다이아몬드가 죄다 그걸 두른 사람 것이라고 생각하시면 안 돼요."], ["You don't suppose that all the diamonds which go to Court belong to the wearers; like those beautiful stones which Lady Jane has, and which are much handsomer than any which I have, I am certain.\"", "제인 부인이 두른 저 아름다운 보석들처럼요. 저 보석들은 제가 가진 그 어떤 것보다도 훨씬 멋지답니다, 틀림없이요.\""]], [["\"They are family jewels,\" said Sir Pitt, again looking uneasy.", "\"저건 집안 대대로 내려온 보석이라오.\""], ["And in this family conversation the carriage rolled down the street, until its cargo was finally discharged at the gates of the palace where the Sovereign was sitting in state.", "피트 경이 또다시 거북한 기색으로 말했다. 이렇게 집안끼리 담소를 나누는 사이에 마차는 거리를 굴러 내려갔고, 마침내 그 짐을 군주가 위풍당당하게 앉아 계신 궁전 문 앞에 부려 놓았다."]], [["The diamonds, which had created Rawdon's admiration, never went back to Mr.", "로던의 감탄을 자아냈던 그 다이아몬드는 코번트리 거리의 폴로니어스 씨에게 결코 되돌아가지 않았고, 그 신사 또한 반환을 요구하는 일이 없었다."], ["Polonius, of Coventry Street, and that gentleman never applied for their restoration, but they retired into a little private repository, in an old desk, which Amelia Sedley had given her years and years ago, and in which Becky kept a number of useful and, perhaps, valuable things, about which her husband knew nothing.", "다이아몬드는 애밀리아 세들리가 오래오래전에 그녀에게 준 낡은 책상 안 자그마한 비밀 보관함으로 들어갔는데, 베키는 거기에 쓸모 있고, 어쩌면 값나가기도 할 물건들을 잔뜩 간직해 두었으니, 남편은 그에 대해 아무것도 몰랐다."], ["To know nothing, or little, is in the nature of some husbands.", "아무것도, 혹은 거의 아무것도 모르는 것이 어떤 남편들의 천성인 것이다."], ["To hide, in the nature of how many women?", "그리고 감추는 것은 얼마나 많은 여인들의 천성이던가?"], ["Oh, ladies!", "오, 숙녀 여러분!"], ["how many of you have surreptitious milliners' bills?", "여러분 가운데 몇이나 몰래 쌓인 모자 가게 청구서를 두고 있는지요?"], ["How many of you have gowns and bracelets which you daren't show, or which you wear trembling?--trembling, and coaxing with smiles the husband by your side, who does not know the new velvet gown from the old one, or the new bracelet from last year's, or has any notion that the ragged-looking yellow lace scarf cost forty guineas and that Madame Bobinot is writing dunning letters every week for the money!", "몇이나 차마 내보이지 못하거나, 입을 때마다 벌벌 떨게 되는 가운과 팔찌를 두고 있는지요?—벌벌 떨면서, 곁에 앉은 남편에게 미소로 알랑거리지요. 새 벨벳 가운을 헌 것과 구분하지도, 새 팔찌를 작년 것과 구별하지도 못하며, 그 너덜너덜해 보이는 노란 레이스 스카프가 사십 기니나 나갔고 보비노 부인이 그 돈을 받겠다고 매주 독촉 편지를 보내고 있다는 것은 짐작도 못 하는 그 남편에게 말이에요!"]], [["Thus Rawdon knew nothing about the brilliant diamond ear-rings, or the superb brilliant ornament which decorated the fair bosom of his lady; but Lord Steyne, who was in his place at Court, as Lord of the Powder Closet, and one of the great dignitaries and illustrious defences of the throne of England, and came up with all his stars, garters, collars, and cordons, and paid particular attention to the little woman, knew whence the jewels came and who paid for them.", "이리하여 로던은 자기 부인의 고운 가슴을 장식한 눈부신 다이아몬드 귀고리며 화려한 보석 장신구에 대해 아무것도 몰랐다. 그러나 화장방 시종장으로서 궁정의 제자리를 지키고 있던 스타인 경, 영국 왕좌의 고관대작이요 빛나는 수호자 가운데 한 사람이며, 온갖 별 모양 훈장과 가터와 목걸이와 띠를 두르고 나와서 그 자그마한 여인에게 각별한 관심을 보인 그 사람은, 그 보석들이 어디서 났는지, 또 누가 그 값을 치렀는지를 알고 있었다."]], [["As he bowed over her he smiled, and quoted the hackneyed and beautiful lines from The Rape of the Lock about Belinda's diamonds, \"which Jews might kiss and infidels adore.\"", "그는 그녀에게 허리를 굽히며 미소 짓고는, 「머리타래의 강탈」에서 벨린다의 다이아몬드를 두고 읊은, 진부하나 아름다운 그 시구를 인용했다. \"유대인도 입 맞추려 들고 이교도도 흠모하리라.\""]], [["\"But I hope your lordship is orthodox,\"", "\"하지만 각하께선 정통 신자이시기를 바라요.\""], ["said the little lady with a toss of her head.", "그 자그마한 숙녀가 고갯짓을 하며 말했다."], ["And many ladies round about whispered and talked, and many gentlemen nodded and whispered, as they saw what marked attention the great nobleman was paying to the little adventuress.", "그리고 그 거물 귀족이 그 자그마한 모험가에게 얼마나 두드러진 관심을 쏟는지 본 주변의 숱한 숙녀들이 수군거리며 이야기를 나누었고, 숱한 신사들이 고개를 끄덕이며 귀엣말을 주고받았다."]], [["What were the circumstances of the interview between Rebecca Crawley, nee Sharp, and her Imperial Master, it does not become such a feeble and inexperienced pen as mine to attempt to relate.", "처녀 적 성이 샤프였던 레베카 크롤리와 그 황제 같은 군주 사이의 알현이 어떠한 정황이었는지는, 나처럼 미약하고 미숙한 붓으로 감히 옮기려 들 일이 못 된다."], ["The dazzled eyes close before that Magnificent Idea.", "그 장엄한 관념 앞에서는 눈이 부셔 절로 감기는 법이다."], ["Loyal respect and decency tell even the imagination not to look too keenly and audaciously about the sacred audience-chamber, but to back away rapidly, silently, and respectfully, making profound bows out of the August Presence.", "충성스러운 경의와 예의가, 상상력에게마저도 그 신성한 알현실을 너무 날카롭고 대담하게 휘둘러보지 말고, 그 장엄한 어전에서 깊이 허리 굽혀 절을 올리며 재빠르고 조용하고 공손하게 뒷걸음질로 물러나라 이르는 것이다."]], [["This may be said, that in all London there was no more loyal heart than Becky's after this interview.", "다만 이 정도는 말할 수 있겠다. 이 알현을 마친 뒤 온 런던을 통틀어 베키보다 더 충성스러운 마음을 지닌 이는 없었다는 것을 말이다."], ["The name of her king was always on her lips, and he was proclaimed by her to be the most charming of men.", "그녀의 입에는 늘 임금의 이름이 오르내렸고, 그녀는 그분을 세상에서 가장 매력적인 분이라 떠받들었다."], ["She went to Colnaghi's and ordered the finest portrait of him that art had produced, and credit could supply.", "그녀는 콜나기 가게로 가서 예술이 빚어낼 수 있고 외상이 허락하는 한도 안에서 가장 빼어난 그분의 초상화를 주문했다."], ["She chose that famous one in which the best of monarchs is represented in a frock-coat with a fur collar, and breeches and silk stockings, simpering on a sofa from under his curly brown wig.", "그녀가 고른 것은 저 유명한 초상, 곧 그 더없이 훌륭한 군주께서 모피 깃 달린 프록코트에 반바지와 비단 양말 차림으로, 곱슬거리는 갈색 가발 아래로 소파에 기댄 채 점잔 빼며 웃고 있는 그림이었다."], ["She had him painted in a brooch and wore it--indeed she amused and somewhat pestered her acquaintance with her perpetual talk about his urbanity and beauty.", "그녀는 그분을 브로치에 그려 넣게 하여 달고 다녔다—실로 그녀는 그분의 정중함과 미모에 대해 끊임없이 떠벌려 대며 지인들을 즐겁게도, 다소 성가시게도 했다."], ["Who knows!", "누가 알랴!"], ["Perhaps the little woman thought she might play the part of a Maintenon or a Pompadour.", "어쩌면 이 자그마한 여인은 자기가 맹트농이나 퐁파두르 같은 역할을 해낼 수 있으리라 여겼는지도 모를 일이다."]], [["But the finest sport of all after her presentation was to hear her talk virtuously.", "그러나 그녀가 알현을 마친 뒤로 무엇보다 가장 큰 구경거리는 그녀가 정숙한 척 떠들어 대는 소리를 듣는 일이었다."], ["She had a few female acquaintances, not, it must be owned, of the very highest reputation in Vanity Fair.", "그녀에게는 몇몇 여자 지인이 있었는데, 인정하건대 허영의 시장에서 그리 드높은 평판을 지닌 부류는 아니었다."], ["But being made an honest woman of, so to speak, Becky would not consort any longer with these dubious ones, and cut Lady Crackenbury when the latter nodded to her from her opera-box, and gave Mrs.", "그러나 말하자면 정숙한 여인으로 거듭난 마당에 베키는 더 이상 이 미심쩍은 여인들과 어울리려 들지 않았다. 그래서 크래컨베리 부인이 자기 오페라 특별석에서 고개를 끄덕여 인사하자 못 본 척했고, 하이드 파크의 승마길에서 워싱턴 화이트 부인을 모른 척 지나쳐 버렸다."], ["Washington White the go-by in the Ring. \"One must, my dear, show one is somebody,\"", "\"이봐요, 사람은 자기가 어엿한 신분이라는 걸 보여야 하는 법이에요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀는 말했다."], ["\"One mustn't be seen with doubtful people.", "\"미심쩍은 사람들과 함께 있는 모습을 보여선 안 돼요."], ["I pity Lady Crackenbury from my heart, and Mrs.", "크래컨베리 부인은 진심으로 가엾게 여겨요."], ["Washington White may be a very good-natured person.", "워싱턴 화이트 부인도 어쩌면 아주 좋은 사람일지 모르고요."], ["YOU may go and dine with them, as you like your rubber.", "당신이야 카드놀이가 좋으면 가서 그들과 식사를 해도 좋아요."], ["But I mustn't, and won't; and you will have the goodness to tell Smith to say I am not at home when either of them calls.\"", "하지만 나는 안 돼요, 절대 안 할 거예요. 그러니 둘 중 누가 찾아오거든 내가 집에 없다고 하라고 스미스에게 일러 줘요.\""]], [["The particulars of Becky's costume were in the newspapers--feathers, lappets, superb diamonds, and all the rest.", "베키의 옷차림은 세세한 부분까지 신문에 실렸다—깃 장식이며, 늘어뜨린 장식 천이며, 화려한 다이아몬드며, 그 밖의 모든 것이."], ["Lady Crackenbury read the paragraph in bitterness of spirit and discoursed to her followers about the airs which that woman was giving herself. Mrs.", "크래컨베리 부인은 그 기사를 비통한 심정으로 읽고서 자기 추종자들에게 저 여자가 부리는 도도한 거드름에 대해 늘어놓았다."], ["Bute Crawley and her young ladies in the country had a copy of the Morning Post from town, and gave a vent to their honest indignation.", "시골의 뷰트 크롤리 부인과 그 딸들도 도시에서 보내온 「모닝 포스트」 한 부를 받아 보고는 의분을 터뜨렸다."], ["\"If you had been sandy-haired, green-eyed, and a French rope-dancer's daughter,\" Mrs.", "\"네가 만약 모래빛 머리에 초록 눈을 했고 프랑스 줄타기 광대의 딸로 태어났더라면,\""], ["Bute said to her eldest girl (who, on the contrary, was a very swarthy, short, and snub-nosed young lady), \"You might have had superb diamonds forsooth, and have been presented at Court by your cousin, the Lady Jane.", "뷰트 부인이 맏딸에게 말했는데(이 딸은 그와는 정반대로 살갗이 거뭇하고 키가 작고 들창코였다), \"암, 너도 화려한 다이아몬드쯤은 두를 수 있었을 테고, 네 사촌 제인 부인을 따라 궁정에 알현하러 갔을지도 모르지."], ["But you're only a gentlewoman, my poor dear child.", "하지만 가엾은 우리 딸아, 너는 그저 양가의 규수일 뿐이란다."], ["You have only some of the best blood in England in your veins, and good principles and piety for your portion.", "네 핏줄에는 영국에서 가장 고귀한 피가 흐를 뿐이고, 곧은 도리와 신앙심이 네 몫이지."], ["I, myself, the wife of a Baronet's younger brother, too, never thought of such a thing as going to Court--nor would other people, if good Queen Charlotte had been alive.\"", "준남작 동생의 아내인 나조차도 궁정에 간다는 따위는 꿈도 꿔 본 적이 없단다—만약 어진 샬럿 왕비가 살아 계셨더라면 다른 사람들도 그랬을 게야.\""], ["In this way the worthy Rectoress consoled herself, and her daughters sighed and sat over the Peerage all night.", "이런 식으로 그 갸륵한 목사 부인은 스스로를 위로했고, 딸들은 한숨을 내쉬며 밤새도록 「귀족 명감」을 들여다보고 앉아 있었다."]], [["A few days after the famous presentation, another great and exceeding honour was vouchsafed to the virtuous Becky. Lady Steyne's carriage drove up to Mr.", "그 이름난 알현이 있고 며칠 뒤, 정숙한 베키에게 또 한 번 크나큰 영광이 베풀어졌다."], ["Rawdon Crawley's door, and the footman, instead of driving down the front of the house, as by his tremendous knocking he appeared to be inclined to do, relented and only delivered in a couple of cards, on which were engraven the names of the Marchioness of Steyne and the Countess of Gaunt.", "스타인 부인의 마차가 로던 크롤리 씨의 집 문 앞으로 다가왔는데, 하인은 그 어마어마한 문 두드리는 소리로 미루어 집을 통째로 부숴 버릴 기세였으나, 마음을 누그러뜨려 그저 명함 두 장을 안에 들여보냈을 뿐이었다. 거기에는 스타인 후작 부인과 곤트 백작 부인의 이름이 새겨져 있었다."], ["If these bits of pasteboard had been beautiful pictures, or had had a hundred yards of Malines lace rolled round them, worth twice the number of guineas, Becky could not have regarded them with more pleasure.", "만약 이 마분지 조각들이 아름다운 그림이었거나, 그 두 배의 기니에 맞먹는 백 야드짜리 말린 레이스로 감겨 있었더라도, 베키가 이보다 더 큰 기쁨으로 그것을 바라보지는 못했을 것이다."], ["You may be sure they occupied a conspicuous place in the china bowl on the drawing-room table, where Becky kept the cards of her visitors. Lord!", "짐작하다시피 그 명함들은 베키가 방문객들의 명함을 보관해 두는 응접실 탁자 위의 도자기 그릇 안에서 가장 눈에 띄는 자리를 차지했다."], ["lord! how poor Mrs.", "아이고, 세상에!"], ["Washington White's card and Lady Crackenbury's card--which our little friend had been glad enough to get a few months back, and of which the silly little creature was rather proud once--Lord! lord!", "가엾은 워싱턴 화이트 부인의 명함이며 크래컨베리 부인의 명함이—몇 달 전만 해도 우리의 자그마한 친구가 받고서 그리도 기뻐했고, 한때는 그 어리석은 자그마한 여인이 제법 우쭐대기까지 했던 그 명함들이—아이고, 세상에, 다시 말하지만, 이 거창한 궁정 카드들이 나타나자마자 그 가엾고 자그마한 찬밥 신세의 끗수 낮은 패들이 어찌나 순식간에 카드 뭉치 맨 밑바닥으로 가라앉아 버렸던지!"], ["I say, how soon at the appearance of these grand court cards, did those poor little neglected deuces sink down to the bottom of the pack.", "스타인! 베어에이커스, 헬벨린의 존스 가문!"], ["Steyne! Bareacres, Johnes of Helvellyn!", "그리고 카멜롯의 캐릴런 가문이라니!"], ["and Caerylon of Camelot! we may be sure that Becky and Briggs looked out those august names in the Peerage, and followed the noble races up through all the ramifications of the family tree.", "분명 베키와 브리그스는 「귀족 명감」에서 그 장엄한 이름들을 찾아내어, 가문의 나뭇가지를 모든 갈래까지 거슬러 올라가며 그 고귀한 혈통을 좇아 보았으리라."]], [["My Lord Steyne coming to call a couple of hours afterwards, and looking about him, and observing everything as was his wont, found his ladies' cards already ranged as the trumps of Becky's hand, and grinned, as this old cynic always did at any naive display of human weakness.", "두어 시간 뒤에 스타인 경이 찾아와서는, 늘 그러듯 주위를 둘러보고 모든 것을 눈여겨보다가, 자기 집 부인들의 명함이 이미 베키가 쥔 패의 으뜸패처럼 가지런히 놓여 있는 것을 발견하고는, 인간의 나약함이 순진하게 드러나는 광경을 볼 때마다 늘 그러듯 이 늙은 냉소가는 히죽 웃었다."], ["Becky came down to him presently; whenever the dear girl expected his lordship, her toilette was prepared, her hair in perfect order, her mouchoirs, aprons, scarfs, little morocco slippers, and other female gimcracks arranged, and she seated in some artless and agreeable posture ready to receive him--whenever she was surprised, of course, she had to fly to her apartment to take a rapid survey of matters in the glass, and to trip down again to wait upon the great peer.", "베키는 이내 그에게로 내려왔다. 이 다정한 아가씨는 각하가 오시리라 예상될 때면 으레 몸단장을 갖추어 두었으니, 머리는 흐트러짐 없이 가지런하고, 손수건이며 앞치마며 스카프며 자그마한 모로코가죽 슬리퍼며 그 밖의 여성용 자질구레한 장식들이 정돈되어 있었으며, 그를 맞이할 채비를 갖춘 채 무심한 듯 우아한 자세로 앉아 있는 것이었다—물론 불시에 놀랄 때면 자기 방으로 날듯이 달려가 거울로 매무새를 후딱 살피고는 다시 종종걸음으로 내려와 그 거물 귀족을 모셨다."]], [["She found him grinning over the bowl.", "그녀는 그가 도자기 그릇을 들여다보며 히죽거리고 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["She was discovered, and she blushed a little.", "들킨 셈이라 그녀는 살짝 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["\"Thank you, Monseigneur,\"", "\"고맙습니다, 각하.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"You see your ladies have been here.", "\"보시다시피 부인들께서 다녀가셨어요."], ["How good of you!", "어찌나 친절하신지요!"], ["I couldn't come before--I was in the kitchen making a pudding.\"", "진작 내려오질 못했어요—부엌에서 푸딩을 만들고 있었거든요.\""]], [["\"I know you were, I saw you through the area-railings as I drove up,\"", "\"알고 있소, 마차를 타고 오면서 지하 부엌 난간 틈으로 당신을 보았으니.\""], ["replied the old gentleman.", "그 노신사가 대꾸했다."]], [["\"You see everything,\"", "\"각하께선 뭐든 다 보시는군요.\""], ["she replied.", "그녀가 받아넘겼다."]], [["\"A few things, but not that, my pretty lady,\"", "\"몇 가지는 보지만, 그건 못 봤소, 어여쁜 부인.\" 그가 너그럽게 말했다."], ["he said good-naturedly.", "\"이 어리석은 자그마한 거짓말쟁이 같으니!"], ["\"You silly little fibster!", "나는 당신이 위층 방에 있는 소리를 들었소."], ["I heard you in the room overhead, where I have no doubt you were putting a little rouge on--you must give some of yours to my Lady Gaunt, whose complexion is quite preposterous--and I heard the bedroom door open, and then you came downstairs.\"", "거기서 분명 연지를 좀 바르고 있었을 테지—그 연지를 곤트 부인한테 좀 나눠 주구려. 그 사람 낯빛은 정말 영 형편없으니 말이오—그러고는 침실 문이 열리는 소리가 났고, 이내 당신이 아래층으로 내려왔지.\""]], [["\"Is it a crime to try and look my best when YOU come here?\" answered Mrs.", "\"각하께서 여기 오시는데 가장 곱게 보이려 애쓰는 게 죄인가요?\""], ["Rawdon plaintively, and she rubbed her cheek with her handkerchief as if to show there was no rouge at all, only genuine blushes and modesty in her case.", "로던 부인이 가련한 목소리로 대꾸하며, 자기 경우엔 연지라곤 전혀 없고 오로지 진짜 홍조와 수줍음뿐이라는 것을 보이려는 듯 손수건으로 뺨을 문질렀다."], ["About this who can tell?", "이것에 대해서야 누가 알 수 있으랴?"], ["I know there is some rouge that won't come off on a pocket-handkerchief, and some so good that even tears will not disturb it.", "손수건에 묻어나지 않는 연지도 있고, 어찌나 좋은지 눈물로도 흐트러지지 않는 연지도 있다는 것을 나는 안다."]], [["\"Well,\"", "\"흠.\""], ["said the old gentleman, twiddling round his wife's card, \"you are bent on becoming a fine lady.", "그 노신사가 자기 아내의 명함을 빙글빙글 돌리며 말했다. \"당신은 기어이 귀부인이 되겠다는 작정이로군."], ["You pester my poor old life out to get you into the world.", "나를 사교계에 끼워 넣어 달라고 이 가엾고 늙은 목숨을 들들 볶아 대니 말이오."], ["You won't be able to hold your own there, you silly little fool.", "거기서 당신은 제 몫을 지켜 내지 못할 거요, 이 어리석은 자그마한 바보 같으니."], ["You've got no money.\"", "당신한텐 돈이 없잖소.\""]], [["\"You will get us a place,\"", "\"각하께서 우릴 위해 자리 하나 마련해 주실 거잖아요.\""], ["interposed Becky, \"as quick as possible.\"", "베키가 끼어들었다. \"되도록 빨리요.\""]], [["\"You've got no money, and you want to compete with those who have.", "\"당신한테는 돈이 없는데, 돈 가진 사람들과 겨루겠다는 거요."], ["You poor little earthenware pipkin, you want to swim down the stream along with the great copper kettles.", "이 가엾은 자그마한 질그릇 단지 같으니, 거대한 구리 솥들과 함께 물살을 타고 떠내려가겠다는 거지."], ["All women are alike.", "여자들은 죄다 똑같다니까."], ["Everybody is striving for what is not worth the having!", "다들 가질 가치도 없는 것을 손에 넣겠다고 안달이지!"], ["Gad! I dined with the King yesterday, and we had neck of mutton and turnips.", "원, 어제는 임금님과 식사를 했는데 양고기 목살에 순무가 나왔소."], ["A dinner of herbs is better than a stalled ox very often.", "살진 소를 잡아 차린 잔칫상보다 푸성귀 한 접시가 더 나을 때가 허다하단 말이지."], ["You will go to Gaunt House.", "당신은 곤트 하우스에 가게 될 거요."], ["You give an old fellow no rest until you get there.", "거기 가기 전엔 이 늙은이를 도무지 가만 놔두질 않을 테니."], ["It's not half so nice as here.", "거기는 여기만큼 절반도 좋지 않소."], ["You'll be bored there. I am.", "당신은 거기서 따분해질 거요."], ["My wife is as gay as Lady Macbeth, and my daughters as cheerful as Regan and Goneril.", "나도 따분하니까. 내 아내는 맥베스 부인만큼이나 명랑하고, 내 딸들은 리건과 거너릴만큼이나 쾌활하지."], ["I daren't sleep in what they call my bedroom.", "나는 내 침실이라고들 부르는 데서는 감히 잠도 못 잔다오."], ["The bed is like the baldaquin of St. Peter's, and the pictures frighten me.", "침대는 성 베드로 대성당의 닫집 같고, 그림들은 나를 겁에 질리게 하니 말이오."], ["I have a little brass bed in a dressing-room, and a little hair mattress like an anchorite.", "나는 화장실에 자그마한 놋쇠 침대 하나와 은수자 같은 자그마한 털 매트리스 하나를 두고 지낸다오."], ["I am an anchorite.", "나는 은수자라 그 말이지."], ["Ho!", "호!"], ["ho!", "호!"], ["You'll be asked to dinner next week.", "당신은 다음 주에 만찬 초대를 받을 거요."], ["And gare aux femmes, look out and hold your own!", "그러니 여자들을 조심하시오, 단단히 정신 차리고 제 몫을 지켜요!"], ["How the women will bully you!\"", "그 여자들이 당신을 얼마나 못살게 굴는지!\""], ["This was a very long speech for a man of few words like my Lord Steyne; nor was it the first which he uttered for Becky's benefit on that day.", "이것은 스타인 경처럼 말수 적은 사람에게는 무척이나 긴 연설이었다. 게다가 그날 그가 베키를 위해 늘어놓은 첫 연설도 아니었다."]], [["Briggs looked up from the work-table at which she was seated in the farther room and gave a deep sigh as she heard the great Marquis speak so lightly of her sex.", "브리그스는 저쪽 방에서 자기가 앉아 있던 작업대에서 고개를 들었다가, 그 거물 후작이 자기네 여성을 그토록 가벼이 말하는 것을 듣고는 깊은 한숨을 내쉬었다."]], [["\"If you don't turn off that abominable sheep-dog,\"", "\"저 가증스러운 양치기 개를 내쫓지 않으면,\""], ["said Lord Steyne, with a savage look over his shoulder at her, \"I will have her poisoned.\"", "스타인 경이 어깨너머로 그녀를 흉포하게 노려보며 말했다. \"독을 먹여 죽여 버리겠소.\""]], [["\"I always give my dog dinner from my own plate,\"", "\"저는 늘 제 접시에 든 걸로 우리 개한테 밥을 준답니다.\" 레베카가 짓궂게 웃으며 말했다."], ["said Rebecca, laughing mischievously; and having enjoyed for some time the discomfiture of my lord, who hated poor Briggs for interrupting his tete-a-tete with the fair Colonel's wife, Mrs. Rawdon at length had pity upon her admirer, and calling to Briggs, praised the fineness of the weather to her and bade her to take out the child for a walk.", "그러고는 어여쁜 대령 부인과의 단둘이만의 자리를 방해한다는 이유로 가엾은 브리그스를 미워하는 각하의 낭패한 꼴을 한동안 즐긴 뒤, 마침내 로던 부인은 자기 숭배자에게 인정을 베풀어, 브리그스를 불러서는 날씨가 참 좋다고 추켜세우며 아이를 데리고 산책이나 다녀오라고 일렀다."]], [["\"I can't send her away,\"", "\"저 사람을 내보낼 수가 없어요.\""], ["Becky said presently, after a pause, and in a very sad voice.", "베키가 잠시 뜸을 들였다가, 무척 서글픈 목소리로 말했다."], ["Her eyes filled with tears as she spoke, and she turned away her head.", "그렇게 말하면서 그녀의 눈에는 눈물이 그렁그렁 차올랐고, 그녀는 고개를 돌려 외면했다."]], [["\"You owe her her wages, I suppose?\"", "\"저 여자한테 품삯을 빚지고 있나 보군?\""], ["said the Peer.", "그 귀족이 말했다."]], [["\"Worse than that,\"", "\"그보다 더 나빠요.\""], ["said Becky, still casting down her eyes; \"I have ruined her.\"", "베키가 여전히 눈을 내리깐 채 말했다. \"제가 저 사람을 파산시켰는걸요.\""]], [["\"Ruined her?", "\"파산시켰다고?"], ["Then why don't you turn her out?\"", "그렇다면 왜 내쫓지 않는 거요?\""], ["the gentleman asked.", "그 신사가 물었다."]], [["\"Men do that,\"", "\"남자들이나 그러지요.\""], ["Becky answered bitterly.", "베키가 씁쓸하게 대답했다."], ["\"Women are not so bad as you.", "\"여자들은 당신네만큼 모질지 않답니다."], ["Last year, when we were reduced to our last guinea, she gave us everything.", "작년에 우리가 마지막 한 기니까지 거덜 났을 때, 저 사람이 우리한테 가진 걸 죄다 내주었어요."], ["She shall never leave me, until we are ruined utterly ourselves, which does not seem far off, or until I can pay her the utmost farthing.\"", "우리 형편이 아주 거덜 날 때까지는—그런데 그날이 멀지 않은 것 같지만요—아니면 제가 저 사람한테 마지막 한 푼까지 갚을 수 있을 때까지는, 저 사람은 결코 제 곁을 떠나지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["\"------ it, how much is it?\"", "\"빌어먹을, 대체 얼마요?\""], ["said the Peer with an oath.", "그 귀족이 욕설을 내뱉으며 말했다."], ["And Becky, reflecting on the largeness of his means, mentioned not only the sum which she had borrowed from Miss Briggs, but one of nearly double the amount.", "그러자 베키는 그의 재력이 어마어마하다는 데에 생각이 미쳐, 브리그스 양에게서 빌린 금액뿐 아니라 그 거의 곱절에 달하는 액수를 일러 주었다."]], [["This caused the Lord Steyne to break out in another brief and energetic expression of anger, at which Rebecca held down her head the more and cried bitterly.", "이 말에 스타인 경은 또 한 번 짧고도 격렬한 분노의 말을 터뜨렸고, 그러자 레베카는 고개를 더욱 푹 숙이며 비통하게 울었다."], ["\"I could not help it.", "\"어쩔 도리가 없었어요."], ["It was my only chance.", "그게 제 유일한 기회였는걸요."], ["I dare not tell my husband.", "감히 남편한테는 말도 못 해요."], ["He would kill me if I told him what I have done.", "제가 무슨 짓을 했는지 알면 그이는 절 죽이려 들 거예요."], ["I have kept it a secret from everybody but you--and you forced it from me.", "각하 말고는 누구한테도 비밀로 해 왔어요—그런데 각하께서 억지로 캐내신 거고요."], ["Ah, what shall I do, Lord Steyne?", "아, 어쩌면 좋아요, 스타인 경?"], ["for I am very, very unhappy!\"", "저는 정말, 정말 불행한걸요!\""]], [["Lord Steyne made no reply except by beating the devil's tattoo and biting his nails.", "스타인 경은 손가락으로 악마의 북소리를 두드려 대고 손톱을 깨물 뿐 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다."], ["At last he clapped his hat on his head and flung out of the room.", "마침내 그는 모자를 머리에 탁 눌러쓰고는 방에서 휙 나가 버렸다."], ["Rebecca did not rise from her attitude of misery until the door slammed upon him and his carriage whirled away.", "레베카는 문이 그의 등 뒤로 쾅 닫히고 그의 마차가 휭 하니 떠나가 버릴 때까지 그 비탄에 잠긴 자세에서 일어나지 않았다."], ["Then she rose up with the queerest expression of victorious mischief glittering in her green eyes.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 더없이 기묘한, 의기양양하고 짓궂은 기색이 그 초록 눈에 반짝이는 채로 몸을 일으켰다."], ["She burst out laughing once or twice to herself, as she sat at work, and sitting down to the piano, she rattled away a triumphant voluntary on the keys, which made the people pause under her window to listen to her brilliant music.", "그녀는 일감 앞에 앉아 한두 번 혼자 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 피아노 앞에 앉아 건반 위로 의기양양한 즉흥곡을 빠르게 두드려 댔으니, 그 눈부신 연주에 사람들이 그녀의 창문 아래 걸음을 멈추고 귀를 기울일 정도였다."]], [["That night, there came two notes from Gaunt House for the little woman, the one containing a card of invitation from Lord and Lady Steyne to a dinner at Gaunt House next Friday, while the other enclosed a slip of gray paper bearing Lord Steyne's signature and the address of Messrs.", "그날 밤, 곤트 하우스에서 그 자그마한 여인 앞으로 쪽지 두 통이 왔다."], ["Jones, Brown, and Robinson, Lombard Street.", "하나에는 다음 금요일 곤트 하우스에서 열리는 만찬에 스타인 경 부부가 초대한다는 초청장이 들어 있었고, 다른 하나에는 스타인 경의 서명과 롬바드 거리의 존스 브라운 로빈슨 상회 주소가 적힌 회색 종잇조각 한 장이 들어 있었다."]], [["Rawdon heard Becky laughing in the night once or twice.", "로던은 그날 밤 베키가 한두 번 웃는 소리를 들었다."], ["It was only her delight at going to Gaunt House and facing the ladies there, she said, which amused her so.", "자기를 그렇게 웃게 만든 건 그저 곤트 하우스에 가서 거기 숙녀들과 맞설 생각에 신이 나서 그런 것뿐이라고 그녀는 말했다."], ["But the truth was that she was occupied with a great number of other thoughts.", "그러나 사실인즉 그녀는 다른 숱한 생각들에 골몰해 있었던 것이다."], ["Should she pay off old Briggs and give her her conge?", "늙은 브리그스에게 빚을 갚고 떠나라고 해 버릴까?"], ["Should she astonish Raggles by settling his account?", "래글스에게 외상값을 청산해 주어 깜짝 놀라게 해 줄까?"], ["She turned over all these thoughts on her pillow, and on the next day, when Rawdon went out to pay his morning visit to the Club, Mrs. Crawley (in a modest dress with a veil on) whipped off in a hackney-coach to the City: and being landed at Messrs.", "그녀는 베개를 베고 이 온갖 생각들을 이리저리 굴려 보았고, 이튿날 로던이 아침에 클럽으로 인사차 나가자, 크롤리 부인은(수수한 옷차림에 베일을 쓴 채) 삯마차를 타고 부리나케 도심으로 달려갔다."], ["Jones and Robinson's bank, presented a document there to the authority at the desk, who, in reply, asked her \"How she would take it?\"", "그러고는 존스 로빈슨 은행 앞에 내려 창구의 담당자에게 서류 한 장을 내밀자, 그가 그 답으로 \"어떻게 받으시겠습니까?\" 하고 물었다."]], [["She gently said \"she would take a hundred and fifty pounds in small notes and the remainder in one note\": and passing through St.", "그녀는 \"백오십 파운드는 작은 지폐로 받고, 나머지는 한 장짜리 지폐로 받겠어요\" 하고 상냥하게 말했다."], ["Paul's Churchyard stopped there and bought the handsomest black silk gown for Briggs which money could buy; and which, with a kiss and the kindest speeches, she presented to the simple old spinster.", "그러고는 세인트 폴 대성당 묘지를 지나는 길에 그곳에 들러 돈으로 살 수 있는 가장 멋진 검은 비단 가운을 브리그스에게 줄 것으로 사서는, 입맞춤과 더없이 다정한 말과 함께 그 순박한 늙은 노처녀에게 선사했다."]], [["Then she walked to Mr. Raggles, inquired about his children affectionately, and gave him fifty pounds on account.", "그런 다음 그녀는 래글스 씨에게 걸어가 그의 아이들 안부를 다정하게 물은 뒤 외상값으로 오십 파운드를 건넸다."], ["Then she went to the livery-man from whom she jobbed her carriages and gratified him with a similar sum.", "그러고는 자기가 마차를 빌려 쓰던 마차 임대업자에게 가서 그에게도 같은 액수를 쥐여 주어 흡족하게 했다."], ["\"And I hope this will be a lesson to you, Spavin,\"", "\"그리고 이게 자네한테 교훈이 되길 바라요, 스페이빈.\""], ["she said, \"and that on the next drawing-room day my brother, Sir Pitt, will not be inconvenienced by being obliged to take four of us in his carriage to wait upon His Majesty, because my own carriage is not forthcoming.\"", "그녀가 말했다. \"다음 알현일에는 우리 시아주버니 피트 경께서 폐하를 알현하러 가시는데 내 마차가 준비되지 못한 탓에 우리 넷을 그분 마차에 다 태우셔야 하는 폐를 끼치는 일이 없도록 말이에요.\""], ["It appears there had been a difference on the last drawing-room day.", "지난번 알현일에 의견 충돌이 있었던 모양이다."], ["Hence the degradation which the Colonel had almost suffered, of being obliged to enter the presence of his Sovereign in a hack cab.", "그래서 대령은 삯마차를 타고 군주의 어전에 들어가야 하는 망신을 하마터면 당할 뻔했던 것이다."]], [["These arrangements concluded, Becky paid a visit upstairs to the before-mentioned desk, which Amelia Sedley had given her years and years ago, and which contained a number of useful and valuable little things--in which private museum she placed the one note which Messrs.", "이런 일들을 다 마무리 짓고 나서, 베키는 위층으로 올라가 앞서 말한 그 책상을 찾았다."], ["Jones and Robinson's cashier had given her.", "애밀리아 세들리가 오래오래전에 그녀에게 준 것으로, 쓸모 있고 값나가는 자그마한 물건들이 잔뜩 들어 있는 그 책상 말이다—그녀는 그 개인 박물관에 존스 로빈슨 상회의 출납계원이 건네준 한 장짜리 지폐를 간직해 두었다."]], [["In Which We Enjoy Three Courses and a Dessert\nWhen the ladies of Gaunt House were at breakfast that morning, Lord Steyne (who took his chocolate in private and seldom disturbed the females of his household, or saw them except upon public days, or when they crossed each other in the hall, or when from his pit-box at the opera he surveyed them in their box on the grand tier) his lordship, we say, appeared among the ladies and the children who were assembled over the tea and toast, and a battle royal ensued apropos of Rebecca.", "그날 아침 곤트 저택의 숙녀들이 아침 식사를 들고 있을 때, 스타인 경이(그는 초콜릿을 혼자 들었고 집안의 여자들을 거의 방해하는 법이 없었으며, 공식 행사가 있는 날이나 복도에서 마주칠 때, 혹은 오페라 극장의 일층 특별석에서 그랜드 티어에 자리한 그녀들의 박스석을 바라볼 때가 아니면 좀처럼 그들을 보지 않았다) — 그러니까 그분이, 차와 토스트를 앞에 두고 모여 앉은 숙녀들과 아이들 틈에 모습을 드러냈고, 레베카를 둘러싸고 한바탕 큰 싸움이 벌어졌다."]], [["\"My Lady Steyne,\"", "\"스타인 부인.\""], ["he said, \"I want to see the list for your dinner on Friday; and I want you, if you please, to write a card for Colonel and Mrs.", "그가 말했다. \"금요일 만찬의 손님 명단을 보고 싶소."], ["Crawley.\"", "그리고 괜찮다면 크롤리 대령 부부 앞으로 초대장을 한 장 써 주었으면 하오.\""]], [["\"Blanche writes them,\"", "\"그건 블랜치가 씁니다.\""], ["Lady Steyne said in a flutter.", "스타인 부인이 안절부절못하며 말했다."], ["\"Lady Gaunt writes them.\"", "\"곤트 부인이 쓰지요.\""]], [["\"I will not write to that person,\"", "\"저는 그런 사람한테는 쓰지 않겠어요.\""], ["Lady Gaunt said, a tall and stately lady, who looked up for an instant and then down again after she had spoken.", "곤트 부인이 말했다. 큰 키에 위엄 있는 부인이었는데, 그렇게 말하고는 잠깐 눈을 들었다가 이내 다시 내리깔았다."], ["It was not good to meet Lord Steyne's eyes for those who had offended him.", "스타인 경의 비위를 거스른 자들로서는 그의 눈을 마주하는 것이 결코 이로운 일이 아니었다."]], [["\"Send the children out of the room.", "\"애들을 방에서 내보내. 나가!\""], ["Go!\" said he pulling at the bell-rope.", "그가 종 줄을 잡아당기며 말했다."], ["The urchins, always frightened before him, retired: their mother would have followed too.", "그 앞에서는 늘 겁에 질려 있던 어린것들이 물러갔다. 아이들의 어머니도 따라 나가려 했다."], ["\"Not you,\"", "\"당신은 아니오.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"You stop.\"", "\"당신은 남아.\""]], [["\"My Lady Steyne,\"", "\"스타인 부인.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said, \"once more will you have the goodness to go to the desk and write that card for your dinner on Friday?\"", "\"한 번 더 부탁하지. 책상으로 가서 금요일 만찬 초대장을 써 주는 친절을 베풀어 주겠소?\""]], [["\"My Lord, I will not be present at it,\"", "\"각하, 저는 그 자리에 참석하지 않겠어요.\""], ["Lady Gaunt said; \"I will go home.\"", "곤트 부인이 말했다. \"친정으로 가겠습니다.\""]], [["\"I wish you would, and stay there.", "\"그래 주면 좋겠군, 가서 거기 눌러앉아 있어."], ["You will find the bailiffs at Bareacres very pleasant company, and I shall be freed from lending money to your relations and from your own damned tragedy airs.", "베리에이커스의 집달리들이 아주 유쾌한 길동무가 되어 줄 게야. 나는 자네 친정 식구들에게 돈을 꿔 주는 일에서도, 자네의 그 빌어먹을 비극 배우 같은 거드름에서도 풀려나겠지."], ["Who are you to give orders here?", "자네가 대체 누구라고 여기서 명령을 내려?"], ["You have no money.", "돈도 없잖아."], ["You've got no brains.", "머리도 텅 비었고."], ["You were here to have children, and you have not had any.", "자네는 아이를 낳으라고 여기 들어온 건데, 하나도 낳지 못했어."], ["Gaunt's tired of you, and George's wife is the only person in the family who doesn't wish you were dead.", "곤트는 자네라면 진절머리를 내고, 이 집안에서 자네가 죽기를 바라지 않는 사람이라곤 조지의 아내뿐이지."], ["Gaunt would marry again if you were.\"", "자네만 죽으면 곤트는 다시 장가들 텐데.\""]], [["\"I wish I were,\"", "\"저도 죽었으면 좋겠어요.\""], ["her Ladyship answered with tears and rage in her eyes.", "부인이 눈에 눈물과 분노를 가득 담은 채 대답했다."]], [["\"You, forsooth, must give yourself airs of virtue, while my wife, who is an immaculate saint, as everybody knows, and never did wrong in her life, has no objection to meet my young friend Mrs.", "\"새삼스레, 자네는 정숙한 척 거드름을 피워야 직성이 풀리는 모양이지. 누구나 알다시피 티 없는 성녀요 평생 그른 짓이라곤 한 적 없는 내 아내는 내 젊은 벗 크롤리 부인을 만나는 데 아무 거리낌이 없는데 말이야."], ["Crawley. My Lady Steyne knows that appearances are sometimes against the best of women; that lies are often told about the most innocent of them.", "스타인 부인은 알지, 때로는 가장 훌륭한 여인일수록 겉모양이 불리하게 비치고, 가장 순결한 여인일수록 거짓말이 따라붙는다는 것을."], ["Pray, madam, shall I tell you some little anecdotes about my Lady Bareacres, your mamma?\"", "부인, 이왕이면 자네 어머니 베리에이커스 부인에 관한 자그마한 일화들을 몇 가지 들려줄까?\""]], [["\"You may strike me if you like, sir, or hit any cruel blow,\"", "\"때리고 싶으시면 때리세요, 각하. 어떤 모진 매질이라도 하셔도 좋아요.\""], ["Lady Gaunt said.", "곤트 부인이 말했다."], ["To see his wife and daughter suffering always put his Lordship into a good humour.", "아내와 며느리가 괴로워하는 꼴을 보는 것은 늘 각하의 기분을 흐뭇하게 했다."]], [["\"My sweet Blanche,\"", "\"내 어여쁜 블랜치.\""], ["he said, \"I am a gentleman, and never lay my hand upon a woman, save in the way of kindness.", "그가 말했다. \"나는 신사라서 여자에게 손찌검을 하는 법이 없지, 친절을 베푸는 경우라면 모를까."], ["I only wish to correct little faults in your character.", "그저 자네 성품의 자그마한 흠들을 바로잡아 주고 싶을 따름이야."], ["You women are too proud, and sadly lack humility, as Father Mole, I'm sure, would tell my Lady Steyne if he were here.", "자네들 여자들은 너무 거만해, 그리고 안타깝게도 겸손이 부족하지. 몰 신부님이 여기 계셨다면 분명 스타인 부인에게도 그렇게 일러 주셨을 게야."], ["You mustn't give yourselves airs; you must be meek and humble, my blessings.", "거드름을 피워선 안 되지. 온순하고 겸허해야 해, 내 사랑스러운 사람들."], ["For all Lady Steyne knows, this calumniated, simple, good-humoured Mrs. Crawley is quite innocent--even more innocent than herself.", "스타인 부인이 다 알 수야 없지만, 저 모함받는 순박하고 싹싹한 크롤리 부인은 어쩌면 아주 결백한 사람일지도 몰라 — 부인 자신보다 더 결백할지도 모르지."], ["Her husband's character is not good, but it is as good as Bareacres', who has played a little and not paid a great deal, who cheated you out of the only legacy you ever had and left you a pauper on my hands.", "그 남편의 평판이야 좋지 않지만, 자네가 받았던 단 하나의 유산을 사기로 가로채고 자네를 빈털터리로 만들어 내 손에 떠넘긴 베리에이커스 정도는 되지. 노름판에 좀 끼어들었다가 빚은 거의 갚지 않은 그 작자 말이야."], ["And Mrs. Crawley is not very well-born, but she is not worse than Fanny's illustrious ancestor, the first de la Jones.\"", "그리고 크롤리 부인의 가문이야 그리 대단치 않지만, 패니의 그 저명한 조상이신 초대 들라존 경보다 못할 것도 없지.\""]], [["\"The money which I brought into the family, sir,\"", "\"제가 이 집안에 가지고 들어온 돈으로 말씀드리면, 각하.\""], ["Lady George cried out--", "조지 부인이 소리쳤다 —"]], [["\"You purchased a contingent reversion with it,\"", "\"자네는 그걸로 불확실한 상속 차례나 사들인 셈이지.\""], ["the Marquis said darkly.", "후작이 음울하게 말했다."], ["\"If Gaunt dies, your husband may come to his honours; your little boys may inherit them, and who knows what besides?", "\"곤트가 죽으면 자네 남편이 그 작위를 물려받을지도 모르고, 자네 어린 아들들이 그것을 이어받을지도 모르지. 그 밖에 또 무엇이 있을지 누가 알겠나?"], ["In the meanwhile, ladies, be as proud and virtuous as you like abroad, but don't give ME any airs.", "그러니 그동안에는, 부인들, 바깥에서야 마음껏 거만하고 정숙하게 굴어도 좋지만, 내게는 거드름 부리지 마."], ["As for Mrs. Crawley's character, I shan't demean myself or that most spotless and perfectly irreproachable lady by even hinting that it requires a defence.", "크롤리 부인의 평판으로 말할 것 같으면, 그 변호가 필요하다는 암시조차 입에 담아 나 자신이나 그 더없이 티 없고 흠잡을 데 없는 부인을 욕보일 생각은 없네."], ["You will be pleased to receive her with the utmost cordiality, as you will receive all persons whom I present in this house.", "자네들은 그 부인을 더없이 살갑게 맞아 주는 것이 좋을 게야. 내가 이 집에 들이는 모든 사람을 그렇게 맞아 주듯이 말이지."], ["This house?\"", "이 집이라고?\""], ["He broke out with a laugh.", "그가 웃음을 터뜨리며 말했다."], ["\"Who is the master of it?", "\"이 집의 주인이 누구지?"], ["and what is it?", "이 집이 무엇인가?"], ["This Temple of Virtue belongs to me.", "이 미덕의 전당은 내 것이야."], ["And if I invite all Newgate or all Bedlam here, by ------ they shall be welcome.\"", "그러니 내가 뉴게이트 감옥 전체나 베들럼 정신병원 전체를 이리로 부른다 해도, 빌어먹을, 그들은 환영받을 게야.\""]], [["After this vigorous allocution, to one of which sort Lord Steyne treated his \"Hareem\" whenever symptoms of insubordination appeared in his household, the crestfallen women had nothing for it but to obey.", "집안에 불복종의 기미가 보일 때마다 스타인 경이 자신의 \"하렘\"에게 으레 베풀곤 하던 이런 종류의 일장 연설을 한 차례 듣고 나자, 풀이 죽은 여인들에게는 그저 순종하는 것 말고는 도리가 없었다."], ["Lady Gaunt wrote the invitation which his Lordship required, and she and her mother-in-law drove in person, and with bitter and humiliated hearts, to leave the cards on Mrs.", "곤트 부인은 각하가 요구한 초대장을 썼고, 시어머니와 함께 쓰라리고 모욕당한 마음을 안은 채 몸소 마차를 몰아 로던 부인의 집에 명함을 두고 왔다."], ["Rawdon, the reception of which caused that innocent woman so much pleasure.", "그리고 그 명함을 받아 든 일은 그 순진한 부인에게 더없는 기쁨을 안겨 주었다."]], [["There were families in London who would have sacrificed a year's income to receive such an honour at the hands of those great ladies.", "런던에는 그 지체 높은 숙녀들의 손에서 그런 영예를 받기 위해서라면 일 년치 수입이라도 기꺼이 바쳤을 집안들이 있었다."], ["Mrs. Frederick Bullock, for instance, would have gone on her knees from May Fair to Lombard Street, if Lady Steyne and Lady Gaunt had been waiting in the City to raise her up and say, \"Come to us next Friday\"--not to one of the great crushes and grand balls of Gaunt House, whither everybody went, but to the sacred, unapproachable, mysterious, delicious entertainments, to be admitted to one of which was a privilege, and an honour, and a blessing indeed.", "이를테면 프레더릭 불록 부인은, 만약 스타인 부인과 곤트 부인이 시티에서 기다리고 있다가 그녀를 일으켜 세우며 \"다음 금요일에 우리 집으로 오세요\"라고 말해 준다면, 메이페어에서 롬바드 거리까지 무릎으로 기어서라도 갔을 것이다 — 그것도 누구나 가는 곤트 저택의 그 떠들썩한 대연회나 성대한 무도회가 아니라, 그 신성하고 범접할 수 없으며 신비롭고 황홀한 연회, 그중 하나에 끼는 것만으로도 하나의 특권이요 영예요 참으로 축복이라 할 만한 그런 자리에 말이다."]], [["Severe, spotless, and beautiful, Lady Gaunt held the very highest rank in Vanity Fair.", "엄격하고, 티 없고, 아름다운 곤트 부인은 허영의 시장에서 그야말로 최고의 지위를 차지하고 있었다."], ["The distinguished courtesy with which Lord Steyne treated her charmed everybody who witnessed his behaviour, caused the severest critics to admit how perfect a gentleman he was, and to own that his Lordship's heart at least was in the right place.", "스타인 경이 그녀를 대하는 그 빼어난 예의는 그의 처신을 지켜본 모든 이를 매료시켰고, 가장 신랄한 비평가들마저 그가 얼마나 완벽한 신사인지 인정하게 했으며, 적어도 각하의 마음만큼은 제자리에 똑바로 놓여 있다는 것을 시인하게 만들었다."]], [["The ladies of Gaunt House called Lady Bareacres in to their aid, in order to repulse the common enemy.", "곤트 저택의 숙녀들은 공동의 적을 물리치기 위해 베리에이커스 부인을 원군으로 불러들였다."], ["One of Lady Gaunt's carriages went to Hill Street for her Ladyship's mother, all whose equipages were in the hands of the bailiffs, whose very jewels and wardrobe, it was said, had been seized by those inexorable Israelites.", "곤트 부인의 마차 한 대가 그 어머니를 모시러 힐 거리로 갔다. 부인의 마차란 마차는 죄다 집달리들의 손아귀에 들어가 있었고, 그 보석과 의상마저도 저 무자비한 유대인들에게 압류당했다는 소문이었다."], ["Bareacres Castle was theirs, too, with all its costly pictures, furniture, and articles of vertu--the magnificent Vandykes; the noble Reynolds pictures; the Lawrence portraits, tawdry and beautiful, and, thirty years ago, deemed as precious as works of real genius; the matchless Dancing Nymph of Canova, for which Lady Bareacres had sat in her youth--Lady Bareacres splendid then, and radiant in wealth, rank, and beauty--a toothless, bald, old woman now--a mere rag of a former robe of state.", "베리에이커스 성도 그들 손에 넘어갔는데, 거기 딸린 값비싼 그림과 가구와 미술 골동품도 모조리 함께였다 — 그 웅장한 반다이크 작품들, 그 고귀한 레이놀즈의 그림들, 야하면서도 아름다워 삼십 년 전만 해도 진짜 천재의 작품 못지않게 귀히 여겨지던 로런스의 초상화들, 그리고 베리에이커스 부인이 젊은 시절 모델로 앉았던 카노바의 비할 데 없는 「춤추는 님프」상까지 — 그 시절 베리에이커스 부인은 눈부셨고, 재산과 지위와 미모로 광채를 발했건만, 이제는 이도 빠지고 머리도 벗어진 노파, 한때의 화려한 예복에서 떨어져 나온 한낱 걸레 조각에 지나지 않았다."], ["Her lord, painted at the same time by Lawrence, as waving his sabre in front of Bareacres Castle, and clothed in his uniform as Colonel of the Thistlewood Yeomanry, was a withered, old, lean man in a greatcoat and a Brutus wig, slinking about Gray's Inn of mornings chiefly and dining alone at clubs.", "같은 시기에 로런스가 그린 그 남편의 초상화는 베리에이커스 성을 배경으로 군도를 휘두르며 시슬우드 의용기병대 대령 군복을 입은 모습이었지만, 실제로는 외투에 브루투스식 가발을 쓴 시들고 마른 늙은이로, 주로 아침나절이면 그레이스인 법학원 근처를 슬그머니 어슬렁거리고 클럽에서 혼자 식사를 했다."], ["He did not like to dine with Steyne now.", "그는 이제 스타인과 함께 식사하기를 좋아하지 않았다."], ["They had run races of pleasure together in youth when Bareacres was the winner.", "젊은 시절에는 둘이 향락의 경주를 함께 벌였고 그때는 베리에이커스가 승자였다."], ["But Steyne had more bottom than he and had lasted him out.", "그러나 스타인이 그보다 끈기가 있어 그를 끝까지 견뎌 냈다."], ["The Marquis was ten times a greater man now than the young Lord Gaunt of '85, and Bareacres nowhere in the race--old, beaten, bankrupt, and broken down.", "후작은 이제 1785년의 젊은 곤트 경보다 열 배는 더 대단한 인물이 되었고, 베리에이커스는 그 경주에서 한참 뒤처졌다 — 늙고, 패배하고, 파산하고, 무너져 내렸다."], ["He had borrowed too much money of Steyne to find it pleasant to meet his old comrade often.", "스타인에게 너무 많은 돈을 빌린 터라 그 옛 동지를 자주 만나는 것이 즐거울 리 없었다."], ["The latter, whenever he wished to be merry, used jeeringly to ask Lady Gaunt why her father had not come to see her.", "후작은 흥겹게 굴고 싶을 때면 곤트 부인에게 어째서 아버지가 딸을 보러 오지 않느냐고 조롱하듯 묻곤 했다."], ["\"He has not been here for four months,\"", "\"넉 달째 발길을 끊었구먼.\""], ["Lord Steyne would say.", "스타인 경은 이렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["\"I can always tell by my cheque-book afterwards, when I get a visit from Bareacres.", "\"베리에이커스가 다녀가고 나면 나중에 내 수표책을 보면 늘 알 수 있지. 부인들, 이 얼마나 다행스러운 일이오."], ["What a comfort it is, my ladies, I bank with one of my sons' fathers-in-law, and the other banks with me!\"", "나는 내 한 아들의 장인 은행에 돈을 맡기고, 또 다른 아들의 장인은 내 은행에 돈을 맡기니 말이오!\""]], [["Of the other illustrious persons whom Becky had the honour to encounter on this her first presentation to the grand world, it does not become the present historian to say much.", "베키가 이 첫 사교계 입성에서 만나는 영예를 누린 다른 저명인사들에 관해서는, 지금의 이 사가가 길게 늘어놓는 것이 어울리지 않을 것이다."], ["There was his Excellency the Prince of Peterwaradin, with his Princess--a nobleman tightly girthed, with a large military chest, on which the plaque of his order shone magnificently, and wearing the red collar of the Golden Fleece round his neck.", "페테르바라딘 대공 각하가 대공비와 함께 와 있었다 — 허리를 잔뜩 졸라맨 귀족으로, 떡 벌어진 군인다운 가슴에는 훈장의 패가 찬란하게 빛났고, 목에는 황금양모기사단의 붉은 깃을 두르고 있었다."], ["He was the owner of countless flocks.", "그는 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 양 떼의 주인이었다."], ["\"Look at his face.", "\"저 얼굴 좀 보세요."], ["I think he must be descended from a sheep,\"", "양의 후손이 분명한 것 같아요.\""], ["Becky whispered to Lord Steyne.", "베키가 스타인 경에게 속삭였다."], ["Indeed, his Excellency's countenance, long, solemn, and white, with the ornament round his neck, bore some resemblance to that of a venerable bell-wether.", "과연 각하의 용모는, 길쭉하고 근엄하며 허연 데다 목에 두른 그 장식까지 더해져, 어딘가 위풍당당한 늙은 길잡이 양을 닮은 데가 있었다."]], [["There was Mr. John Paul Jefferson Jones, titularly attached to the American Embassy and correspondent of the New York Demagogue, who, by way of making himself agreeable to the company, asked Lady Steyne, during a pause in the conversation at dinner, how his dear friend, George Gaunt, liked the Brazils?", "미국 대사관에 명목상 소속되어 있고 「뉴욕 데마고그」지의 통신원인 존 폴 제퍼슨 존스 씨도 와 있었는데, 그는 좌중의 환심을 사 보겠답시고, 만찬 중 대화가 잠시 끊긴 틈을 타 스타인 부인에게 그의 절친한 벗 조지 곤트가 브라질 생활을 마음에 들어 하더냐고 물었다."], ["He and George had been most intimate at Naples and had gone up Vesuvius together. Mr.", "그와 조지는 나폴리에서 더없이 절친한 사이였고 둘이 함께 베수비오 화산에 올랐다는 것이었다."], ["Jones wrote a full and particular account of the dinner, which appeared duly in the Demagogue.", "존스 씨는 그 만찬에 관해 빠짐없이 시시콜콜 적은 기사를 썼고, 그것은 어김없이 「데마고그」지에 실렸다."], ["He mentioned the names and titles of all the guests, giving biographical sketches of the principal people.", "그는 손님들의 이름과 작위를 일일이 거론하며 주요 인사들의 약전을 곁들였다."], ["He described the persons of the ladies with great eloquence; the service of the table; the size and costume of the servants; enumerated the dishes and wines served; the ornaments of the sideboard; and the probable value of the plate.", "숙녀들의 자태를 청산유수로 묘사하고, 식탁의 차림새며, 하인들의 키와 복장이며, 차려 나온 요리와 포도주를 낱낱이 열거하고, 찬장의 장식이며, 식기의 짐작 가는 값어치까지 적었다."], ["Such a dinner he calculated could not be dished up under fifteen or eighteen dollars per head.", "그는 이런 만찬이라면 일 인당 십오 달러 내지 십팔 달러는 들었으리라고 어림했다."], ["And he was in the habit, until very lately, of sending over proteges, with letters of recommendation to the present Marquis of Steyne, encouraged to do so by the intimate terms on which he had lived with his dear friend, the late lord.", "그리고 그는 바로 얼마 전까지만 해도, 현 스타인 후작에게 추천장을 들려 피보호자들을 보내곤 했는데, 절친했던 벗 곧 작고한 선대 후작과 가까이 지낸 사이라는 점에 고무되어 그래 온 것이었다."], ["He was most indignant that a young and insignificant aristocrat, the Earl of Southdown, should have taken the pas of him in their procession to the dining-room.", "그는 어리고 보잘것없는 귀족 사우스다운 백작이 식당으로 행진해 들어갈 때 자기 앞자리를 가로챈 데에 몹시 분개했다."], ["\"Just as I was stepping up to offer my hand to a very pleasing and witty fashionable, the brilliant and exclusive Mrs.", "\"내가 막 한 발 나서서 더없이 상냥하고 재기 넘치는 사교계의 명사, 저 눈부시고 도도한 로던 크롤리 부인에게 손을 내밀려던 참인데,\""], ["Rawdon Crawley,\"--he wrote--\"the young patrician interposed between me and the lady and whisked my Helen off without a word of apology.", "그는 이렇게 썼다. \"그 어린 귀족이 나와 부인 사이로 끼어들더니 한마디 사과도 없이 내 헬레네를 채 가 버렸다."], ["I was fain to bring up the rear with the Colonel, the lady's husband, a stout red-faced warrior who distinguished himself at Waterloo, where he had better luck than befell some of his brother redcoats at New Orleans.\"", "나는 하는 수 없이 그 부인의 남편인 대령과 함께 행렬의 후미를 맡아야 했는데, 이 건장하고 불그레한 얼굴의 무인은 워털루에서 공을 세운 자로, 그곳에서 그는 뉴올리언스에서 몇몇 동료 붉은 군복들에게 닥친 것보다 나은 운을 누린 인물이었다.\""]], [["The Colonel's countenance on coming into this polite society wore as many blushes as the face of a boy of sixteen assumes when he is confronted with his sister's schoolfellows.", "이 점잖은 사교계에 들어선 대령의 얼굴은, 누이의 학교 친구들과 맞닥뜨린 열여섯 살 소년의 얼굴이 그러하듯, 온통 붉게 물들어 있었다."], ["It has been told before that honest Rawdon had not been much used at any period of his life to ladies' company.", "정직한 로던이 평생 어느 시기에도 숙녀들과 어울리는 일에 그다지 익숙하지 못했다는 것은 앞서 이미 이야기한 바 있다."], ["With the men at the Club or the mess room, he was well enough; and could ride, bet, smoke, or play at billiards with the boldest of them.", "클럽이나 장교 식당의 사내들과는 곧잘 어울렸다. 그중 가장 대담한 자들과 어깨를 나란히 하여 말도 타고, 내기도 걸고, 담배도 피우고, 당구도 칠 줄 알았다."], ["He had had his time for female friendships too, but that was twenty years ago, and the ladies were of the rank of those with whom Young Marlow in the comedy is represented as having been familiar before he became abashed in the presence of Miss Hardcastle.", "그에게도 여인들과 정을 나누던 시절이 있긴 했지만 그것은 이십 년 전 일이었고, 그 숙녀들이란 저 희극 속 청년 말로가 하드캐슬 양 앞에서 수줍어하기 전에 허물없이 지냈다는 부류의 여인들과 같은 신분의 여인들이었다."], ["The times are such that one scarcely dares to allude to that kind of company which thousands of our young men in Vanity Fair are frequenting every day, which nightly fills casinos and dancing-rooms, which is known to exist as well as the Ring in Hyde Park or the Congregation at St. James's--but which the most squeamish if not the most moral of societies is determined to ignore.", "시절이 이러하여, 허영의 시장의 수많은 우리 젊은이들이 날마다 드나드는, 밤마다 카지노와 무도장을 가득 채우는, 하이드 파크의 승마장이나 세인트제임스의 예배 모임만큼이나 분명히 존재한다고 알려진 — 그러나 가장 도덕적이라고는 못 해도 가장 까다로운 사교계가 짐짓 모른 체하기로 작정한 — 그런 부류의 동무들을 입에 올리기조차 좀처럼 엄두 내기 어렵다."], ["In a word, although Colonel Crawley was now five-and-forty years of age, it had not been his lot in life to meet with a half dozen good women, besides his paragon of a wife.", "한마디로, 비록 크롤리 대령이 이제 마흔다섯 살이 되었지만, 모범 그 자체인 자기 아내 말고는 변변한 여인을 대여섯 명도 채 만나 보지 못한 것이 그의 인생 행로였다."], ["All except her and his kind sister Lady Jane, whose gentle nature had tamed and won him, scared the worthy Colonel, and on occasion of his first dinner at Gaunt House he was not heard to make a single remark except to state that the weather was very hot.", "그 아내와, 부드러운 천성으로 그를 길들이고 사로잡은 다정한 누이 제인 부인을 빼면, 어떤 여인이든 이 훌륭한 대령을 주눅 들게 했고, 곤트 저택에서의 첫 만찬 자리에서도 그가 입을 떼는 소리라곤 날씨가 무척 덥다고 한 말 한마디뿐이었다."], ["Indeed Becky would have left him at home, but that virtue ordained that her husband should be by her side to protect the timid and fluttering little creature on her first appearance in polite society.", "사실 베키로서는 그를 집에 두고 오고 싶었지만, 사교계에 처음 모습을 드러내는 이 겁 많고 안절부절못하는 가녀린 여인을 지켜 줄 남편이 곁에 있어야 한다는 것이 정숙함의 법도였던 것이다."]], [["On her first appearance Lord Steyne stepped forward, taking her hand, and greeting her with great courtesy, and presenting her to Lady Steyne, and their ladyships, her daughters.", "그녀가 처음 들어서자 스타인 경이 앞으로 나서서 그녀의 손을 잡고 더없이 정중하게 맞이한 뒤, 스타인 부인과 그 딸인 두 부인에게 그녀를 소개했다."], ["Their ladyships made three stately curtsies, and the elder lady to be sure gave her hand to the newcomer, but it was as cold and lifeless as marble.", "두 부인은 위엄 있는 절을 세 번 했고, 손위 부인은 과연 새로 온 손님에게 손을 내밀긴 했으나, 그 손은 대리석처럼 차갑고 생기가 없었다."]], [["Becky took it, however, with grateful humility, and performing a reverence which would have done credit to the best dancer-master, put herself at Lady Steyne's feet, as it were, by saying that his Lordship had been her father's earliest friend and patron, and that she, Becky, had learned to honour and respect the Steyne family from the days of her childhood.", "그래도 베키는 고마운 듯 겸허하게 그 손을 잡고, 으뜸가는 무용 선생도 칭찬할 만한 절을 올리며, 이를테면 스타인 부인의 발치에 자신을 낮추어 이렇게 말했다. 각하께서는 제 아버지의 가장 오래된 벗이요 후원자이셨으며, 자기 베키는 어린 시절부터 스타인 가문을 우러르고 존경하도록 배워 왔노라고."], ["The fact is that Lord Steyne had once purchased a couple of pictures of the late Sharp, and the affectionate orphan could never forget her gratitude for that favour.", "사실인즉 스타인 경이 일찍이 작고한 샤프에게서 그림 두어 점을 사들인 일이 있었고, 정 많은 그 고아 딸은 그 호의에 대한 고마움을 결코 잊을 수 없었던 것이다."]], [["The Lady Bareacres then came under Becky's cognizance--to whom the Colonel's lady made also a most respectful obeisance: it was returned with severe dignity by the exalted person in question.", "이어 베리에이커스 부인이 베키의 눈에 들어왔다 — 대령 부인은 그녀에게도 더없이 공손하게 절을 올렸으나, 그 지체 높은 분께서는 서릿발 같은 위엄으로 그것을 받았다."]], [["\"I had the pleasure of making your Ladyship's acquaintance at Brussels, ten years ago,\"", "\"십 년 전 브뤼셀에서 부인을 알게 되는 기쁨을 누렸지요.\""], ["Becky said in the most winning manner.", "베키가 더없이 사람의 마음을 끄는 태도로 말했다."], ["\"I had the good fortune to meet Lady Bareacres at the Duchess of Richmond's ball, the night before the Battle of Waterloo.", "\"워털루 전투 전날 밤, 리치먼드 공작부인의 무도회에서 베리에이커스 부인을 뵙는 행운을 누렸답니다."], ["And I recollect your Ladyship, and my Lady Blanche, your daughter, sitting in the carriage in the porte-cochere at the Inn, waiting for horses.", "그리고 부인과 따님이신 블랜치 부인께서 여관 정문 마차 통로에 세워 둔 마차 안에 앉아 말을 기다리시던 모습이 기억나는걸요."], ["I hope your Ladyship's diamonds are safe.\"", "부인의 다이아몬드가 무사하기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["Everybody's eyes looked into their neighbour's.", "좌중의 모든 눈이 서로 옆 사람의 눈치를 살폈다."], ["The famous diamonds had undergone a famous seizure, it appears, about which Becky, of course, knew nothing.", "그 유명한 다이아몬드는 보아하니 그 유명한 압류를 당한 모양이었는데, 베키야 물론 그런 일을 까맣게 모르고 있었다."], ["Rawdon Crawley retreated with Lord Southdown into a window, where the latter was heard to laugh immoderately, as Rawdon told him the story of Lady Bareacres wanting horses and \"knuckling down by Jove,\" to Mrs.", "로던 크롤리는 사우스다운 경과 함께 창가로 물러나 있었는데, 로던이 베리에이커스 부인이 말을 구하지 못해 크롤리 부인에게 \"맹세코, 무릎까지 꿇었다\"는 이야기를 들려주자 사우스다운 경이 주체 못 할 만큼 웃어 대는 소리가 들렸다."], ["Crawley. \"I think I needn't be afraid of THAT woman,\"", "\"저 여자라면 무서워할 것 없겠어.\""], ["Becky thought.", "베키는 생각했다."], ["Indeed, Lady Bareacres exchanged terrified and angry looks with her daughter and retreated to a table, where she began to look at pictures with great energy.", "과연 베리에이커스 부인은 딸과 겁에 질린 성난 눈길을 주고받더니, 그림이 놓인 탁자 쪽으로 물러나 무척이나 열심히 그림을 들여다보기 시작했다."]], [["When the Potentate from the Danube made his appearance, the conversation was carried on in the French language, and the Lady Bareacres and the younger ladies found, to their farther mortification, that Mrs.", "도나우 강가에서 온 그 군주가 모습을 드러내자 대화는 프랑스어로 이어졌고, 베리에이커스 부인과 젊은 숙녀들은 한층 더 분한 노릇으로, 크롤리 부인이 자기들보다 그 언어에 훨씬 능통하며 훨씬 좋은 억양으로 구사한다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["Crawley was much better acquainted with that tongue, and spoke it with a much better accent than they.", "베키는 1816년에서 17년 사이 프랑스에 주둔한 군대에서 다른 헝가리 귀족들을 만난 적이 있었다."], ["Becky had met other Hungarian magnates with the army in France in 1816-17.", "그녀는 깊은 관심을 보이며 그 벗들의 안부를 물었다."], ["She asked after her friends with great interest. The foreign personages thought that she was a lady of great distinction, and the Prince and the Princess asked severally of Lord Steyne and the Marchioness, whom they conducted to dinner, who was that petite dame who spoke so well?", "그 외국 인사들은 그녀를 지체 높은 귀부인이라 여겼고, 대공과 대공비는 각각 만찬장으로 안내해 준 스타인 경과 후작 부인에게 저토록 말솜씨가 빼어난 저 자그마한 부인이 누구냐고 물었다."]], [["Finally, the procession being formed in the order described by the American diplomatist, they marched into the apartment where the banquet was served, and which, as I have promised the reader he shall enjoy it, he shall have the liberty of ordering himself so as to suit his fancy.", "마침내 행렬이 저 미국 외교관이 묘사한 순서대로 갖추어지자, 그들은 연회가 차려진 그 방으로 줄지어 들어갔다. 그리고 그 방으로 말하자면, 독자께서 마음껏 즐기시도록 해 드리겠다고 약속한 바 있으니, 독자께서는 제 취향에 맞게 직접 주문하실 자유를 누리시면 되겠다."]], [["But it was when the ladies were alone that Becky knew the tug of war would come.", "그러나 정작 한판 큰 싸움이 벌어지리라는 것을 베키가 안 것은 숙녀들만 남게 되었을 때였다."], ["And then indeed the little woman found herself in such a situation as made her acknowledge the correctness of Lord Steyne's caution to her to beware of the society of ladies above her own sphere.", "그리고 과연 그제야 이 작은 여인은, 제 분수 위에 있는 숙녀들의 무리를 경계하라던 스타인 경의 충고가 옳았음을 인정하지 않을 수 없는 처지에 놓이게 되었다."], ["As they say, the persons who hate Irishmen most are Irishmen; so, assuredly, the greatest tyrants over women are women.", "흔히들 아일랜드 사람을 가장 미워하는 자는 아일랜드 사람이라고들 하는데, 마찬가지로 여자에게 가장 모진 폭군은 분명 여자인 것이다."], ["When poor little Becky, alone with the ladies, went up to the fire-place whither the great ladies had repaired, the great ladies marched away and took possession of a table of drawings.", "가엾은 작은 베키가 숙녀들과만 남게 되어, 지체 높은 숙녀들이 모여 있는 벽난로 쪽으로 다가가자, 그 지체 높은 숙녀들은 자리를 떠 그림이 놓인 탁자를 차지해 버렸다."], ["When Becky followed them to the table of drawings, they dropped off one by one to the fire again.", "베키가 그들을 따라 그림 탁자로 가자, 그들은 하나둘씩 다시 벽난로 쪽으로 흩어졌다."], ["She tried to speak to one of the children (of whom she was commonly fond in public places), but Master George Gaunt was called away by his mamma; and the stranger was treated with such cruelty finally, that even Lady Steyne herself pitied her and went up to speak to the friendless little woman.", "그녀는 아이들 가운데 하나에게 말을 걸어 보려 했지만(그녀는 공공장소에서는 으레 아이들을 귀여워했다), 어린 조지 곤트 도련님은 제 어머니가 불러 데려가 버렸다. 그리하여 이 이방인은 끝내 어찌나 모진 대접을 받았던지, 스타인 부인마저 그녀를 가엾이 여겨 친구 하나 없는 그 작은 여인에게 말을 걸러 다가갔다."]], [["\"Lord Steyne,\"", "\"스타인 경 말씀이,\""], ["said her Ladyship, as her wan cheeks glowed with a blush, \"says you sing and play very beautifully, Mrs.", "부인이 핼쑥한 두 볼을 발그레 붉히며 말했다."], ["Crawley--I wish you would do me the kindness to sing to me.\"", "\"크롤리 부인께서 노래도 연주도 무척 아름답게 하신다더군요 — 제게 노래를 한 곡 들려주는 친절을 베풀어 주시면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["\"I will do anything that may give pleasure to my Lord Steyne or to you,\"", "\"스타인 경 각하나 부인을 기쁘게 해 드릴 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든 하겠어요.\""], ["said Rebecca, sincerely grateful, and seating herself at the piano, began to sing.", "레베카가 진심으로 고마워하며 이렇게 말하고는 피아노 앞에 앉아 노래하기 시작했다."]], [["She sang religious songs of Mozart, which had been early favourites of Lady Steyne, and with such sweetness and tenderness that the lady, lingering round the piano, sat down by its side and listened until the tears rolled down her eyes.", "그녀는 스타인 부인이 어린 시절부터 즐겨 듣던 모차르트의 종교곡들을 어찌나 감미롭고 부드럽게 불렀던지, 피아노 곁을 서성이던 부인은 그 옆에 앉아 두 눈에 눈물이 흘러내릴 때까지 귀를 기울였다."], ["It is true that the opposition ladies at the other end of the room kept up a loud and ceaseless buzzing and talking, but the Lady Steyne did not hear those rumours.", "방 저편의 적대적인 숙녀들이 줄곧 시끄럽고 그칠 줄 모르게 웅성거리며 떠들어 댄 것은 사실이었지만, 스타인 부인의 귀에는 그 소란이 들리지 않았다."], ["She was a child again--and had wandered back through a forty years' wilderness to her convent garden.", "그녀는 다시 어린아이가 되어, 사십 년의 황야를 거슬러 수녀원 정원으로 되돌아가 있었다."], ["The chapel organ had pealed the same tones, the organist, the sister whom she loved best of the community, had taught them to her in those early happy days.", "예배당의 오르간이 바로 그 가락을 울려 퍼뜨렸고, 그 오르간 연주자, 곧 수도원에서 그녀가 가장 사랑하던 수녀가 그 행복하던 어린 시절에 이 곡들을 가르쳐 주었던 것이다."], ["She was a girl once more, and the brief period of her happiness bloomed out again for an hour--she started when the jarring doors were flung open, and with a loud laugh from Lord Steyne, the men of the party entered full of gaiety.", "그녀는 다시 한 번 소녀가 되었고, 그 짧았던 행복의 시절이 한 시간 동안 다시 꽃피어났다 — 삐걱거리는 문이 활짝 열어젖혀지고 스타인 경의 요란한 웃음소리와 함께 일행의 남자들이 흥에 겨워 들어서자 그녀는 흠칫 놀랐다."]], [["He saw at a glance what had happened in his absence, and was grateful to his wife for once.", "그는 자기가 없는 사이에 무슨 일이 있었는지 한눈에 알아채고는, 이번만큼은 아내에게 고마워했다."], ["He went and spoke to her, and called her by her Christian name, so as again to bring blushes to her pale face--\"My wife says you have been singing like an angel,\"", "그는 다가가 아내에게 말을 건네고 세례명으로 그녀를 불러, 다시금 그 창백한 얼굴에 홍조가 떠오르게 했다 — \"내 아내가 그러는데, 부인이 천사처럼 노래했다더군.\""], ["he said to Becky.", "그가 베키에게 말했다."], ["Now there are angels of two kinds, and both sorts, it is said, are charming in their way.", "그런데 천사에는 두 종류가 있고, 그 두 종류가 다 저마다의 방식으로 매혹적이라는 말이 있다."]], [["Whatever the previous portion of the evening had been, the rest of that night was a great triumph for Becky.", "그날 저녁 앞부분이 어떠했든 간에, 그날 밤의 나머지는 베키에게 대단한 승리였다."], ["She sang her very best, and it was so good that every one of the men came and crowded round the piano.", "그녀는 그야말로 최선을 다해 노래했는데, 어찌나 훌륭했던지 남자들이 하나도 빠짐없이 다가와 피아노 둘레에 몰려들었다."], ["The women, her enemies, were left quite alone. And Mr.", "그녀의 적인 여자들은 완전히 외톨이가 되어 버렸다."], ["Paul Jefferson Jones thought he had made a conquest of Lady Gaunt by going up to her Ladyship and praising her delightful friend's first-rate singing.", "그리고 폴 제퍼슨 존스 씨는 곤트 부인에게 다가가 부인의 매력적인 벗이 일류로 노래한다고 칭찬함으로써 그 부인의 마음을 사로잡았노라 여겼다."]], [["Contains a Vulgar Incident\nThe Muse, whoever she be, who presides over this Comic History must now descend from the genteel heights in which she has been soaring and have the goodness to drop down upon the lowly roof of John Sedley at Brompton, and describe what events are taking place there.", "이 희극적 이야기를 주관하는 뮤즈가 누구든 간에, 그 여신은 이제 자기가 높이 날아오르던 고상한 정점에서 내려와, 황송하게도 브롬프턴에 자리한 존 세들리의 누추한 지붕 위로 내려앉아, 그곳에서 어떤 일들이 벌어지고 있는지를 그려 보여 주어야 한다."], ["Here, too, in this humble tenement, live care, and distrust, and dismay. Mrs.", "이 초라한 셋집에도 근심과 불신과 낙담이 깃들어 살고 있는 것이다."], ["Clapp in the kitchen is grumbling in secret to her husband about the rent, and urging the good fellow to rebel against his old friend and patron and his present lodger. Mrs.", "부엌에서는 클랩 부인이 집세를 두고 남편에게 남몰래 투덜대며, 이 마음씨 좋은 사내더러 오랜 벗이자 후원자이며 지금은 세입자가 된 사람에게 들고 일어나라고 부추긴다."], ["Sedley has ceased to visit her landlady in the lower regions now, and indeed is in a position to patronize Mrs. Clapp no longer.", "세들리 부인은 이제 아래층 영역에 있는 집주인을 찾아가는 일도 그만두었고, 사실 더는 클랩 부인을 너그러이 보살펴 줄 처지가 못 되었다."], ["How can one be condescending to a lady to whom one owes a matter of forty pounds, and who is perpetually throwing out hints for the money?", "사십 파운드쯤 빚진 데다 줄곧 그 돈에 대한 암시를 흘려 대는 부인에게, 어떻게 거들먹거리며 너그러운 척할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["The Irish maidservant has not altered in the least in her kind and respectful behaviour; but Mrs. Sedley fancies that she is growing insolent and ungrateful, and, as the guilty thief who fears each bush an officer, sees threatening innuendoes and hints of capture in all the girl's speeches and answers.", "아일랜드 출신 하녀의 친절하고 공손한 태도는 조금도 변함이 없었으나, 세들리 부인은 그 아이가 점점 무례하고 배은망덕해진다고 여겼고, 가는 곳마다 덤불마다 포졸로 보인다는 도둑이 제 발 저리듯, 그 아이의 모든 말과 대답에서 위협적인 빈정거림이며 붙잡으려는 기색을 읽어 냈다."], ["Miss Clapp, grown quite a young woman now, is declared by the soured old lady to be an unbearable and impudent little minx.", "클랩 양은 이제 어엿한 처녀로 자랐는데, 심술궂어진 노부인은 그 아이를 참아 줄 수 없는 건방진 계집애라고 단언했다."], ["Why Amelia can be so fond of her, or have her in her room so much, or walk out with her so constantly, Mrs. Sedley cannot conceive.", "애밀리아가 어찌하여 그 아이를 그토록 좋아하는지, 어찌하여 제 방에 그토록 자주 들이는지, 어찌하여 그토록 늘 함께 산책을 나가는지, 세들리 부인으로서는 도무지 헤아릴 수가 없었다."], ["The bitterness of poverty has poisoned the life of the once cheerful and kindly woman.", "가난의 쓰라림이 한때 명랑하고 다정했던 부인의 삶을 독으로 물들여 버린 것이었다."], ["She is thankless for Amelia's constant and gentle bearing towards her; carps at her for her efforts at kindness or service; rails at her for her silly pride in her child and her neglect of her parents.", "부인은 애밀리아가 자신에게 한결같이 부드럽게 대하는 데도 고마운 줄 몰랐고, 친절을 베풀거나 도우려는 노력을 트집 잡았으며, 제 자식을 자랑스러워하는 어리석은 자긍심과 부모를 소홀히 한다며 딸에게 욕설을 퍼부었다."], ["Georgy's house is not a very lively one since Uncle Jos's annuity has been withdrawn and the little family are almost upon famine diet.", "조스 외삼촌의 연금이 끊긴 뒤로 어린 식구가 거의 굶주리다시피 지내게 되면서, 조지의 집은 그리 활기찬 곳이 못 되었다."]], [["Amelia thinks, and thinks, and racks her brain, to find some means of increasing the small pittance upon which the household is starving.", "애밀리아는 생각하고 또 생각하며, 온 식구가 굶주리고 있는 그 적은 돈을 조금이라도 늘릴 방도를 찾으려고 머리를 쥐어짰다. 무언가를 가르쳐 볼 수 있을까?"], ["Can she give lessons in anything?", "카드꽂이에 그림을 그려 볼까?"], ["paint card-racks? do fine work?", "고운 바느질을 해 볼까?"], ["She finds that women are working hard, and better than she can, for twopence a day.", "그녀는 여자들이 하루 이 펜스를 받고, 자기보다 더 솜씨 좋게, 그토록 고되게 일하고 있음을 알게 되었다."], ["She buys a couple of begilt Bristol boards at the Fancy Stationer's and paints her very best upon them--a shepherd with a red waistcoat on one, and a pink face smiling in the midst of a pencil landscape--a shepherdess on the other, crossing a little bridge, with a little dog, nicely shaded.", "그녀는 잡화 문구점에서 도금한 브리스틀 판지 두 장을 사다가 거기에 정성을 다해 그림을 그렸다. 한 장에는 빨간 조끼를 입고 연필로 그린 풍경 한가운데서 발그레한 얼굴로 미소 짓는 양치기를, 다른 한 장에는 작은 다리를 건너는 양치기 처녀와 음영을 곱게 넣은 작은 개를 그렸다."], ["The man of the Fancy Repository and Brompton Emporium of Fine Arts (of whom she bought the screens, vainly hoping that he would repurchase them when ornamented by her hand) can hardly hide the sneer with which he examines these feeble works of art.", "잡화점 겸 브롬프턴 미술품 상회의 주인은(그녀는 그에게서 그 가리개를 샀고, 자기 손으로 장식을 하고 나면 그가 도로 사 주리라는 헛된 기대를 품었다) 이 보잘것없는 미술품을 살펴보면서 비웃음을 좀처럼 감추지 못했다."], ["He looks askance at the lady who waits in the shop, and ties up the cards again in their envelope of whitey-brown paper, and hands them to the poor widow and Miss Clapp, who had never seen such beautiful things in her life, and had been quite confident that the man must give at least two guineas for the screens.", "그는 가게에서 기다리는 부인을 곁눈질하더니, 카드를 다시 누르스름한 갈색 종이 봉투에 싸서 가엾은 미망인과 클랩 양에게 건넸다. 클랩 양은 평생 그토록 아름다운 물건을 본 적이 없는지라, 그 주인이 가리개에 적어도 이 기니는 쳐 주리라고 철석같이 믿고 있던 터였다."], ["They try at other shops in the interior of London, with faint sickening hopes.", "두 사람은 희미하고 메스꺼운 희망을 품은 채 런던 안쪽의 다른 가게들을 찾아다녔다. \"필요 없소이다.\""], ["\"Don't want 'em,\" says one.", "한 가게에서는 이렇게 말했다."], ["\"Be off,\"", "\"썩 꺼지슈.\""], ["says another fiercely.", "다른 가게에서는 사납게 쏘아붙였다."], ["Three-and-sixpence has been spent in vain--the screens retire to Miss Clapp's bedroom, who persists in thinking them lovely.", "삼 실링 육 펜스만 헛되이 쓴 셈이었다. 가리개는 클랩 양의 침실로 물러갔고, 그녀는 그것이 사랑스럽다고 한사코 믿어 마지않았다."]], [["She writes out a little card in her neatest hand, and after long thought and labour of composition, in which the public is informed that \"A Lady who has some time at her disposal, wishes to undertake the education of some little girls, whom she would instruct in English, in French, in Geography, in History, and in Music--address A.", "그녀는 가장 정갈한 글씨로 작은 카드 한 장을 적었다. 오랜 궁리와 문안 작성의 수고 끝에, 거기에는 사람들에게 이렇게 알리는 내용이 담겼다."], ["O., at Mr. Brown's\"; and she confides the card to the gentleman of the Fine Art Repository, who consents to allow it to lie upon the counter, where it grows dingy and fly-blown.", "\"다소의 여가가 있는 한 숙녀가 어린 소녀들의 교육을 맡고자 하오니, 영어와 프랑스어와 지리와 역사와 음악을 가르쳐 드리겠습니다."], ["Amelia passes the door wistfully many a time, in hopes that Mr.", "브라운 씨 댁의 A. O."], ["Brown will have some news to give her, but he never beckons her in.", "앞으로 연락 주시기 바랍니다.\" 그녀는 그 카드를 미술품 상회 주인에게 맡겼고, 그는 그것을 계산대 위에 놓아 두기로 했다."], ["When she goes to make little purchases, there is no news for her.", "카드는 거기서 점점 거뭇거뭇 때가 끼고 파리똥이 슬었다. 애밀리아는 브라운 씨가 무슨 소식이라도 전해 주기를 바라며 그 가게 앞을 몇 번이고 애틋하게 지나쳤으나, 그는 한 번도 그녀를 안으로 손짓해 부르지 않았다."], ["Poor simple lady, tender and weak--how are you to battle with the struggling violent world?", "그녀가 소소한 물건을 사러 들를 때에도, 그녀에게 들려줄 소식이라곤 없었다. 가엾고 순박한 부인, 여리고 약한 이여, 그대가 어찌 이 아등바등 사나운 세상과 맞서 싸운단 말인가?"]], [["She grows daily more care-worn and sad, fixing upon her child alarmed eyes, whereof the little boy cannot interpret the expression.", "그녀는 나날이 더 수심에 잠기고 서글퍼졌으며, 자기 자식에게 불안한 눈길을 붙박았으나, 어린 소년은 그 표정의 뜻을 헤아리지 못했다."], ["She starts up of a night and peeps into his room stealthily, to see that he is sleeping and not stolen away.", "그녀는 밤이면 화들짝 일어나 살그머니 아이의 방을 들여다보며, 아이가 잠들어 있는지, 누가 채 가지는 않았는지 살폈다."], ["She sleeps but little now.", "그녀는 이제 잠을 거의 이루지 못했다."], ["A constant thought and terror is haunting her.", "끊임없는 생각과 공포가 그녀를 사로잡고 있었다."], ["How she weeps and prays in the long silent nights--how she tries to hide from herself the thought which will return to her, that she ought to part with the boy, that she is the only barrier between him and prosperity.", "그 길고 고요한 밤들에 그녀가 어찌나 울며 기도했던지, 자꾸만 되돌아오는 그 생각을—자기가 아이와 헤어져야 한다는 생각을, 자기야말로 아이와 그 영달 사이를 가로막는 유일한 장벽이라는 생각을—어떻게든 스스로에게서 감추려고 어찌나 애썼던지."], ["She can't, she can't.", "그녀는 차마, 차마 그럴 수 없었다."], ["Not now, at least.", "적어도 지금은."], ["Some other day.", "언젠가 다른 날에."], ["Oh!", "아아!"], ["it is too hard to think of and to bear.", "그것은 떠올리기도 견디기도 너무나 모진 일이었다."]], [["A thought comes over her which makes her blush and turn from herself--her parents might keep the annuity--the curate would marry her and give a home to her and the boy.", "한 가지 생각이 그녀를 스쳐, 얼굴을 붉히며 스스로에게서 고개를 돌리게 했다. 부모님이 연금을 그대로 받게 할 수도 있다는 생각이었다."], ["But George's picture and dearest memory are there to rebuke her.", "그 부목사가 자기와 결혼해 자기와 아이에게 보금자리를 마련해 줄 것이었다."], ["Shame and love say no to the sacrifice.", "그러나 거기에는 조지의 초상과 더없이 소중한 추억이 있어 그녀를 꾸짖었다."], ["She shrinks from it as from something unholy, and such thoughts never found a resting-place in that pure and gentle bosom.", "수치심과 사랑이 그 희생에 안 된다고 말했다. 그녀는 무언가 부정한 것에서 움츠러들듯 그 생각에서 움츠러들었고, 그토록 순결하고 부드러운 가슴속에는 그런 생각이 결코 머물 자리를 찾지 못했다."]], [["The combat, which we describe in a sentence or two, lasted for many weeks in poor Amelia's heart, during which she had no confidante; indeed, she could never have one, as she would not allow to herself the possibility of yielding, though she was giving way daily before the enemy with whom she had to battle.", "우리가 한두 문장으로 그려 내는 이 싸움은, 가엾은 애밀리아의 가슴속에서 여러 주에 걸쳐 이어졌고, 그동안 그녀에게는 속내를 털어놓을 사람 하나 없었다. 사실 그녀는 그런 사람을 둘 수도 없었으니, 날마다 맞서 싸우는 적 앞에서 날마다 무너지면서도, 자기가 굴복할 가능성이 있다는 것 자체를 스스로 인정하려 들지 않았기 때문이다."], ["One truth after another was marshalling itself silently against her and keeping its ground.", "진실들이 하나씩 하나씩 소리 없이 그녀를 향해 도열하더니 그 자리를 지켰다."], ["Poverty and misery for all, want and degradation for her parents, injustice to the boy--one by one the outworks of the little citadel were taken, in which the poor soul passionately guarded her only love and treasure.", "모두에게 닥칠 가난과 비참, 부모님에게 닥칠 궁핍과 영락, 아이에게 저지르는 부당함—가엾은 영혼이 자기의 유일한 사랑이자 보물을 애틋하게 지키고 있던 그 작은 성채의 외곽 보루가 하나씩 하나씩 함락되었다."]], [["At the beginning of the struggle, she had written off a letter of tender supplication to her brother at Calcutta, imploring him not to withdraw the support which he had granted to their parents and painting in terms of artless pathos their lonely and hapless condition.", "이 싸움이 시작될 무렵, 그녀는 캘커타에 있는 오라비에게 애틋한 간청의 편지 한 통을 써 보냈었다. 부모님께 베풀어 온 부양을 거두지 말아 달라고 애원하며, 두 분의 외롭고 가련한 처지를 꾸밈없는 비통함으로 그려 낸 편지였다."], ["She did not know the truth of the matter.", "그녀는 그 일의 진상을 알지 못했다."], ["The payment of Jos's annuity was still regular, but it was a money-lender in the City who was receiving it: old Sedley had sold it for a sum of money wherewith to prosecute his bootless schemes.", "조스의 연금은 여전히 어김없이 지급되고 있었으나, 그것을 받아 챙기는 사람은 시내의 한 고리대금업자였다. 늙은 세들리가 자기의 헛된 사업을 벌일 돈을 마련하려고 그 연금을 팔아넘긴 것이었다."], ["Emmy was calculating eagerly the time that would elapse before the letter would arrive and be answered.", "에미는 그 편지가 도착해 답장이 올 때까지 얼마나 걸릴지를 애타게 헤아렸다."], ["She had written down the date in her pocket-book of the day when she dispatched it.", "그녀는 편지를 부친 날짜를 수첩에 적어 두었다."], ["To her son's guardian, the good Major at Madras, she had not communicated any of her griefs and perplexities.", "자기 아들의 후견인인, 마드라스의 그 선량한 소령에게는 자기의 슬픔과 곤경을 조금도 알리지 않았다."], ["She had not written to him since she wrote to congratulate him on his approaching marriage.", "그녀는 그의 결혼이 임박했음을 축하하는 편지를 쓴 뒤로는 그에게 한 번도 편지하지 않았다."], ["She thought with sickening despondency, that that friend--the only one, the one who had felt such a regard for her--was fallen away.", "그녀는 메스꺼운 낙담에 잠겨, 그 벗이—유일한 벗, 자기에게 그토록 깊은 정을 품었던 그 사람이—이제는 멀어져 떠나 버렸다고 생각했다."]], [["One day, when things had come to a very bad pass--when the creditors were pressing, the mother in hysteric grief, the father in more than usual gloom, the inmates of the family avoiding each other, each secretly oppressed with his private unhappiness and notion of wrong--the father and daughter happened to be left alone together, and Amelia thought to comfort her father by telling him what she had done.", "어느 날, 사정이 매우 고약한 지경에 이르러—채권자들은 졸라 대고, 어머니는 발작적인 슬픔에 빠지고, 아버지는 평소보다 더 침울하고, 식구들은 저마다 남모르는 불행과 억울한 심사에 짓눌린 채 서로를 피하고 있던 그때—아버지와 딸이 우연히 단둘이 남게 되었고, 애밀리아는 자기가 한 일을 들려주어 아버지를 위로하고자 했다."], ["She had written to Joseph--an answer must come in three or four months.", "조지프에게 편지를 써 두었으니, 서너 달 안에는 답장이 올 것이라고 했다."], ["He was always generous, though careless.", "그 애는 늘 무심하긴 해도 너그러운 사람이었다."], ["He could not refuse, when he knew how straitened were the circumstances of his parents.", "부모님의 형편이 얼마나 군색한지 알게 되면 거절하지 못할 것이었다."]], [["Then the poor old gentleman revealed the whole truth to her--that his son was still paying the annuity, which his own imprudence had flung away.", "그러자 가엾은 노신사는 딸에게 진실을 모조리 털어놓았다. 자기 아들은 여전히 연금을 보내고 있지만, 자신이 분별없이 그것을 내던져 버렸다는 것이었다."], ["He had not dared to tell it sooner.", "그는 더 일찍 그 말을 꺼낼 엄두를 내지 못했다."], ["He thought Amelia's ghastly and terrified look, when, with a trembling, miserable voice he made the confession, conveyed reproaches to him for his concealment.", "떨리고 비참한 목소리로 그 고백을 했을 때, 애밀리아의 핼쑥하고 겁에 질린 표정이 자기의 은폐를 책망하는 것이라고 그는 여겼다."], ["\"Ah!\"", "\"아!\""], ["said he with quivering lips and turning away, \"you despise your old father now!\"", "그가 입술을 떨며 고개를 돌리고 말했다. \"이제 너도 이 늙은 애비를 경멸하는 게로구나!\""]], [["\"Oh, papa!", "\"오, 아버지!"], ["it is not that,\"", "그게 아니에요.\""], ["Amelia cried out, falling on his neck and kissing him many times.", "애밀리아가 부르짖으며 그의 목을 끌어안고 몇 번이고 입을 맞추었다."], ["\"You are always good and kind.", "\"아버지는 언제나 선하고 자상하셨어요."], ["You did it for the best.", "다 잘되라고 하신 일이잖아요."], ["It is not for the money--it is--my God!", "돈 때문이 아니에요. 그건—오, 하느님!"], ["my God!", "하느님!"], ["have mercy upon me, and give me strength to bear this trial\"; and she kissed him again wildly and went away.", "저를 가엾이 여기시어, 이 시련을 견딜 힘을 주소서.\" 그러고는 다시 미친 듯이 아버지에게 입을 맞추고는 자리를 떠났다."]], [["Still the father did not know what that explanation meant, and the burst of anguish with which the poor girl left him.", "그래도 아버지는 그 말이 무슨 뜻인지, 가엾은 딸이 자기를 두고 떠나며 터뜨린 그 비통함이 무엇인지 알지 못했다."], ["It was that she was conquered.", "그것은 그녀가 졌다는 뜻이었다."], ["The sentence was passed.", "선고가 내려진 것이었다."], ["The child must go from her--to others--to forget her.", "아이는 그녀를 떠나야 했다. 다른 이들에게로—그녀를 잊으러."], ["Her heart and her treasure--her joy, hope, love, worship--her God, almost!", "그녀의 가슴이자 보물, 그녀의 기쁨이요 희망이요 사랑이요 경배의 대상, 거의 그녀의 하느님이나 다름없는 아이를!"], ["She must give him up, and then--and then she would go to George, and they would watch over the child and wait for him until he came to them in Heaven.", "그녀는 아이를 내주어야 했고, 그러고 나면—그러고 나면 조지에게로 가서, 둘이 함께 아이를 굽어보며 아이가 천국에서 자기들에게 올 때까지 기다리리라."]], [["She put on her bonnet, scarcely knowing what she did, and went out to walk in the lanes by which George used to come back from school, and where she was in the habit of going on his return to meet the boy.", "그녀는 자기가 무얼 하는지도 거의 모르는 채 보닛을 쓰고, 조지가 학교에서 돌아오던 길, 그녀가 늘 아이를 마중하러 나가곤 하던 그 골목길로 걸어 나갔다. 때는 오월이었고, 반공일이었다."], ["It was May, a half-holiday.", "잎이 죄다 돋아나고 날씨는 눈부셨다."], ["The leaves were all coming out, the weather was brilliant; the boy came running to her flushed with health, singing, his bundle of school-books hanging by a thong.", "아이는 건강으로 발그레한 얼굴로 노래를 부르며, 가죽끈에 책 꾸러미를 매단 채 그녀에게 뛰어왔다. 거기 아이가 있었다."], ["There he was. Both her arms were round him.", "그녀는 두 팔로 아이를 끌어안았다."], ["No, it was impossible.", "아니, 불가능한 일이었다."], ["They could not be going to part.", "둘은 헤어질 수 없었다."], ["\"What is the matter, Mother?\"", "\"왜 그러세요, 엄마?\" 아이가 말했다."], ["said he; \"you look very pale.\"", "\"얼굴이 아주 창백하세요.\""]], [["\"Nothing, my child,\"", "\"아무것도 아니란다, 우리 아가.\""], ["she said and stooped down and kissed him.", "그녀는 그렇게 말하고는 몸을 숙여 아이에게 입을 맞추었다."]], [["That night Amelia made the boy read the story of Samuel to her, and how Hannah, his mother, having weaned him, brought him to Eli the High Priest to minister before the Lord.", "그날 밤 애밀리아는 아이에게 사무엘의 이야기를, 그리고 그의 어머니 한나가 그를 젖 떼게 한 뒤 대제사장 엘리에게 데려가 주님 앞에서 섬기게 한 대목을 읽게 했다."], ["And he read the song of gratitude which Hannah sang, and which says, who it is who maketh poor and maketh rich, and bringeth low and exalteth--how the poor shall be raised up out of the dust, and how, in his own might, no man shall be strong.", "아이는 한나가 부른 감사의 노래를 읽었으니, 거기에는 누가 사람을 가난하게도 부유하게도 하시며, 누가 낮추기도 높이기도 하시는지가 적혀 있었다. 가난한 자가 어떻게 흙먼지 속에서 일으켜 세워지는지, 그리고 어떤 사람도 제 힘으로는 강해질 수 없다는 것이."], ["Then he read how Samuel's mother made him a little coat and brought it to him from year to year when she came up to offer the yearly sacrifice.", "그러고 나서 아이는 사무엘의 어머니가 그에게 작은 겉옷을 지어, 해마다 제사를 드리러 올라올 때마다 가져다주었다는 대목을 읽었다."], ["And then, in her sweet simple way, George's mother made commentaries to the boy upon this affecting story.", "그러자 조지의 어머니는 다정하고 소박한 방식으로 이 가슴 저미는 이야기를 아이에게 풀이해 주었다. 한나가 아들을 그토록 사랑했으면서도 자기의 서원 때문에 어떻게 아들을 내주었는지를."], ["How Hannah, though she loved her son so much, yet gave him up because of her vow.", "그리고 그녀가 멀리 떨어진 집에 앉아 그 작은 겉옷을 지으면서 늘 아이를 생각했음이 틀림없다고."], ["And how she must always have thought of him as she sat at home, far away, making the little coat; and Samuel, she was sure, never forgot his mother; and how happy she must have been as the time came (and the years pass away very quick) when she should see her boy and how good and wise he had grown.", "또 사무엘은 분명 어머니를 결코 잊지 않았으리라고. 그리고 자기 아이를 다시 보게 될 그때가(세월은 참 빨리도 흘러가는 법이다) 오면, 또 아이가 얼마나 착하고 지혜롭게 자랐는지를 보게 되면, 그녀가 얼마나 행복했겠느냐고."], ["This little sermon she spoke with a gentle solemn voice, and dry eyes, until she came to the account of their meeting--then the discourse broke off suddenly, the tender heart overflowed, and taking the boy to her breast, she rocked him in her arms and wept silently over him in a sainted agony of tears.", "이 작은 설교를, 그녀는 부드럽고 엄숙한 목소리로, 마른 눈으로 이어 갔다. 그러다 두 사람이 다시 만나는 대목에 이르자—이야기는 갑자기 끊기고, 그 애틋한 가슴이 북받쳐 넘쳐흘렀으며, 그녀는 아이를 가슴에 끌어안고 두 팔로 어르며 거룩한 눈물의 고뇌에 잠겨 소리 없이 아이 위로 울었다."]], [["Her mind being made up, the widow began to take such measures as seemed right to her for advancing the end which she proposed.", "마음을 굳힌 미망인은, 자기가 작정한 그 끝을 향해 나아가고자 스스로 옳다고 여겨지는 조처를 취하기 시작했다."], ["One day, Miss Osborne, in Russell Square (Amelia had not written the name or number of the house for ten years--her youth, her early story came back to her as she wrote the superscription) one day Miss Osborne got a letter from Amelia which made her blush very much and look towards her father, sitting glooming in his place at the other end of the table.", "어느 날, 러셀 광장의 오스본 양은(애밀리아는 십 년 동안 그 집의 이름도 번지도 적어 본 적이 없었다—겉봉을 적으면서 그녀의 젊은 날이, 지난날의 이야기가 되살아났다) 어느 날 오스본 양은 애밀리아에게서 편지 한 통을 받았는데, 그 편지에 그녀는 얼굴을 몹시 붉히며 식탁 저편 제자리에 침울하게 앉아 있는 아버지 쪽을 바라보았다."]], [["In simple terms, Amelia told her the reasons which had induced her to change her mind respecting her boy.", "애밀리아는 소박한 말로, 자기 아이를 두고 마음을 바꾸게 된 사연을 그녀에게 들려주었다."], ["Her father had met with fresh misfortunes which had entirely ruined him. Her own pittance was so small that it would barely enable her to support her parents and would not suffice to give George the advantages which were his due.", "아버지가 새로운 불운을 만나 완전히 파산하셨다는 것, 자기 몫의 푼돈이 너무도 적어 부모님을 겨우 봉양할 수 있을 뿐 조지에게 마땅히 누려야 할 혜택을 베풀어 줄 수는 없다는 것이었다."], ["Great as her sufferings would be at parting with him she would, by God's help, endure them for the boy's sake.", "아이와 헤어지는 고통이 아무리 크더라도, 하느님의 도우심으로, 아이를 위해 그것을 견뎌 내겠노라고 했다."], ["She knew that those to whom he was going would do all in their power to make him happy.", "그녀는 아이가 가게 될 그곳 사람들이 아이를 행복하게 해 주려고 힘닿는 데까지 다 하리라는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["She described his disposition, such as she fancied it--quick and impatient of control or harshness, easily to be moved by love and kindness.", "그녀는 자기가 짐작하는 대로 아이의 성정을 그려 보였다. 영민하나 구속이나 모진 대우를 견디지 못하고, 사랑과 친절에는 쉽게 마음이 움직인다고."], ["In a postscript, she stipulated that she should have a written agreement, that she should see the child as often as she wished--she could not part with him under any other terms.", "추신에서는 한 가지를 못 박아 두었으니, 자기가 원할 때면 언제든 아이를 볼 수 있다는 서면 합의가 있어야 한다는 것, 그 밖의 어떤 조건으로도 아이와 헤어질 수 없다는 것이었다."]], [["\"What? Mrs.", "\"뭐라고?"], ["Pride has come down, has she?\"", "그 도도하신 부인께서 굽히고 들어오셨다, 이거지?\""], ["old Osborne said, when with a tremulous eager voice Miss Osborne read him the letter.", "오스본 양이 떨리고 다급한 목소리로 편지를 읽어 주자 늙은 오스본이 말했다."], ["\"Reg'lar starved out, hey?", "\"꼼짝없이 굶겨 내쫓겼다, 응?"], ["Ha, ha!", "하, 하!"], ["I knew she would.\"", "그럴 줄 알았다니까.\""], ["He tried to keep his dignity and to read his paper as usual--but he could not follow it.", "그는 위엄을 지키며 여느 때처럼 신문을 읽으려 했으나, 도무지 글이 눈에 들어오지 않았다."], ["He chuckled and swore to himself behind the sheet.", "그는 신문지 뒤에서 혼자 낄낄거리며 욕설을 중얼거렸다."]], [["At last he flung it down and, scowling at his daughter, as his wont was, went out of the room into his study adjoining, from whence he presently returned with a key.", "마침내 그는 신문을 내던지고, 평소 버릇대로 딸을 노려보고는, 방을 나가 옆에 붙은 서재로 들어갔다가 곧 열쇠 하나를 들고 돌아왔다."], ["He flung it to Miss Osborne.", "그는 그것을 오스본 양에게 던졌다."]], [["\"Get the room over mine--his room that was--ready,\" he said.", "\"내 방 위에 있는 방을—그 애 방이었던 데를—치워 두어라.\""], ["\"Yes, sir,\"", "그가 말했다."], ["his daughter replied in a tremble.", "\"네, 아버지.\" 딸이 떨면서 대답했다."], ["It was George's room.", "그것은 조지의 방이었다."], ["It had not been opened for more than ten years.", "십 년이 넘도록 열린 적이 없는 방이었다."], ["Some of his clothes, papers, handkerchiefs, whips and caps, fishing-rods and sporting gear, were still there.", "그의 옷가지 몇 벌과 서류, 손수건, 채찍과 모자, 낚싯대와 사냥 도구가 아직도 그대로 있었다."], ["An Army list of 1814, with his name written on the cover; a little dictionary he was wont to use in writing; and the Bible his mother had given him, were on the mantelpiece, with a pair of spurs and a dried inkstand covered with the dust of ten years.", "표지에 그의 이름이 적힌 1814년판 육군 명부, 그가 글을 쓸 때 즐겨 쓰던 작은 사전, 그리고 어머니가 그에게 준 성경이 박차 한 쌍과 십 년 묵은 먼지가 뽀얗게 앉은 마른 잉크병과 함께 벽난로 선반 위에 놓여 있었다."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["since that ink was wet, what days and people had passed away!", "그 잉크가 채 마르지 않았던 그 시절 이래로, 얼마나 많은 날과 사람들이 스러져 갔는가!"], ["The writing-book, still on the table, was blotted with his hand.", "아직도 탁자 위에 놓인 습자책에는 그의 손길이 남긴 얼룩이 묻어 있었다."]], [["Miss Osborne was much affected when she first entered this room with the servants under her.", "오스본 양은 하인들을 거느리고 이 방에 처음 들어섰을 때 몹시 마음이 흔들렸다."], ["She sank quite pale on the little bed.", "그녀는 핼쑥하게 질려 그 작은 침대에 주저앉았다."], ["\"This is blessed news, m'am--indeed, m'am,\"", "\"이건 복된 소식이에요, 아씨—정말이지요, 아씨.\""], ["the housekeeper said; \"and the good old times is returning, m'am.", "가정부가 말했다. \"그 좋던 옛 시절이 돌아오는 거예요, 아씨."], ["The dear little feller, to be sure, m'am; how happy he will be!", "그 귀여운 도련님이, 암요, 아씨, 얼마나 행복해하시겠어요!"], ["But some folks in May Fair, m'am, will owe him a grudge, m'am\"; and she clicked back the bolt which held the window-sash and let the air into the chamber.", "하지만 메이페어의 어떤 사람들은, 아씨, 도련님을 두고 원한을 품을걸요, 아씨.\" 그러고는 창틀을 잡고 있던 빗장을 딸깍 젖혀, 방 안으로 바람이 들어오게 했다."]], [["\"You had better send that woman some money,\" Mr.", "\"그 여자한테 돈을 좀 보내 주는 게 좋겠다.\""], ["Osborne said, before he went out.", "오스본 씨가 나가기 전에 말했다."], ["\"She shan't want for nothing.", "\"부족한 게 없게 해 줘야지."], ["Send her a hundred pound.\"", "백 파운드를 보내라.\""]], [["\"And I'll go and see her to-morrow?\"", "\"그럼 제가 내일 그분을 뵈러 가도 될까요?\""], ["Miss Osborne asked.", "오스본 양이 물었다."]], [["\"That's your look out.", "\"그거야 네가 알아서 할 일이고."], ["She don't come in here, mind.", "그 여자는 여기 발을 들이지 못한다, 알겠느냐."], ["No, by ------, not for all the money in London.", "안 돼, 절대로—런던의 돈을 다 준대도 안 될 일이야."], ["But she mustn't want now.", "하지만 이제 궁색하게 두어선 안 돼."], ["So look out, and get things right.\" With which brief speeches Mr.", "그러니 잘 챙겨서 모든 걸 제대로 해 두어라.\""], ["Osborne took leave of his daughter and went on his accustomed way into the City.", "이런 짤막한 말로 오스본 씨는 딸과 작별하고는 여느 때처럼 시내로 향했다."]], [["\"Here, Papa, is some money,\"", "\"여기, 아버지, 돈이에요.\""], ["Amelia said that night, kissing the old man, her father, and putting a bill for a hundred pounds into his hands.", "그날 밤 애밀리아가 늙은 아버지에게 입을 맞추고 백 파운드짜리 어음을 그의 손에 쥐여 주며 말했다."], ["\"And--and, Mamma, don't be harsh with Georgy.", "\"그리고—그리고, 어머니, 조지에게 모질게 굴지 마세요."], ["He--he is not going to stop with us long.\"", "그 애는—그 애는 이제 우리와 오래 머물지 못할 거예요.\""], ["She could say nothing more, and walked away silently to her room.", "그녀는 더는 아무 말도 하지 못하고, 말없이 자기 방으로 걸어갔다."], ["Let us close it upon her prayers and her sorrow.", "그녀의 기도와 슬픔 위로 방문을 닫아 두기로 하자."], ["I think we had best speak little about so much love and grief.", "이토록 큰 사랑과 비탄에 대해서는 말을 아끼는 편이 가장 나으리라 싶다."]], [["Miss Osborne came the next day, according to the promise contained in her note, and saw Amelia.", "이튿날 오스본 양은 자기 편지에 담긴 약속대로 찾아와 애밀리아를 만났다."], ["The meeting between them was friendly.", "두 사람의 만남은 다정했다."], ["A look and a few words from Miss Osborne showed the poor widow that, with regard to this woman at least, there need be no fear lest she should take the first place in her son's affection.", "오스본 양의 눈빛과 몇 마디 말은, 적어도 이 여자에 관한 한, 그녀가 자기 아들의 애정에서 으뜸가는 자리를 차지하지나 않을까 두려워할 까닭이 없음을 가엾은 미망인에게 일러 주었다."], ["She was cold, sensible, not unkind.", "그녀는 차갑고 분별 있었으나, 매정하지는 않았다."], ["The mother had not been so well pleased, perhaps, had the rival been better looking, younger, more affectionate, warmer-hearted.", "어쩌면 이 연적이 더 잘생기고, 더 젊고, 더 다정하고, 더 따뜻한 마음씨를 지녔더라면, 어머니는 이토록 흡족하지 못했을 것이다."], ["Miss Osborne, on the other hand, thought of old times and memories and could not but be touched with the poor mother's pitiful situation.", "한편 오스본 양은 지난날과 추억을 떠올리며, 이 가엾은 어머니의 애처로운 처지에 마음이 움직이지 않을 수 없었다."], ["She was conquered, and laying down her arms, as it were, she humbly submitted.", "그녀는 굴복했고, 말하자면 무기를 내려놓고서 겸허히 받아들였다."], ["That day they arranged together the preliminaries of the treaty of capitulation.", "그날 두 사람은 함께 항복 조약의 예비 조항들을 정해 두었다."]], [["George was kept from school the next day, and saw his aunt.", "이튿날 조지는 학교를 쉬고 고모를 만났다."], ["Amelia left them alone together and went to her room.", "애밀리아는 두 사람을 단둘이 남겨 두고 자기 방으로 갔다."], ["She was trying the separation--as that poor gentle Lady Jane Grey felt the edge of the axe that was to come down and sever her slender life.", "그녀는 그 이별을 미리 견뎌 보고 있었다—저 가엾고 온순한 제인 그레이 부인이 곧 내리쳐 자기의 가녀린 목숨을 끊을 도끼의 날을 더듬어 보았듯이."], ["Days were passed in parleys, visits, preparations.", "며칠은 협상과 방문과 준비로 지나갔다."], ["The widow broke the matter to Georgy with great caution; she looked to see him very much affected by the intelligence.", "미망인은 조지에게 그 일을 무척 조심스레 꺼냈으니, 그 소식에 아이가 몹시 슬퍼하리라 여겼던 것이다. 그러나 아이는 오히려 들떠 했고, 가엾은 부인은 서글피 고개를 돌렸다."], ["He was rather elated than otherwise, and the poor woman turned sadly away.", "그날 아이는 학교에서 친구들에게 그 소식을 자랑했다."], ["He bragged about the news that day to the boys at school; told them how he was going to live with his grandpapa, his father's father, not the one who comes here sometimes; and that he would be very rich, and have a carriage, and a pony, and go to a much finer school, and when he was rich he would buy Leader's pencil-case and pay the tart-woman.", "자기가 할아버지와, 그러니까 아버지의 아버지와—이따금 여기 오는 그 할아버지 말고—함께 살게 되었다고 일러 주었다. 자기는 아주 부자가 되어 마차와 조랑말을 갖게 되고, 훨씬 더 멋진 학교에 다니게 될 거라고, 부자가 되면 리더의 연필갑을 사고 파이 파는 아주머니에게 외상값도 갚아 주겠다고 했다."], ["The boy was the image of his father, as his fond mother thought.", "다정한 어머니의 생각에, 아이는 제 아버지를 빼다 박은 모습이었다."]], [["Indeed I have no heart, on account of our dear Amelia's sake, to go through the story of George's last days at home.", "사실 나는, 우리의 사랑하는 애밀리아를 생각하면, 조지가 집에서 보낸 마지막 날들의 이야기를 끝까지 들려줄 엄두가 나지 않는다."]], [["At last the day came, the carriage drove up, the little humble packets containing tokens of love and remembrance were ready and disposed in the hall long since--George was in his new suit, for which the tailor had come previously to measure him. He had sprung up with the sun and put on the new clothes, his mother hearing him from the room close by, in which she had been lying, in speechless grief and watching. Days before she had been making preparations for the end, purchasing little stores for the boy's use, marking his books and linen, talking with him and preparing him for the change--fondly fancying that he needed preparation.", "마침내 그날이 왔다. 마차가 다가와 멈춰 섰고, 사랑과 추억의 정표가 담긴 초라하고 작은 꾸러미들은 진작부터 채비를 갖춰 현관에 놓여 있었다. 조지는 새 옷을 입고 있었으니, 앞서 재단사가 와서 치수를 재 갔던 그 옷이었다. 아이는 해와 함께 벌떡 일어나 새 옷을 입었고, 그 소리를 바로 옆방에서 어머니가 들었다. 그녀는 말 못 할 슬픔에 잠긴 채 그 방에 누워 지켜보고 있었던 것이다. 며칠 전부터 그녀는 그 끝을 위해 채비를 하고 있었다. 아이가 쓸 자잘한 물건들을 사들이고, 아이의 책과 옷가지에 이름을 적어 넣고, 아이와 이야기를 나누며 그 변화를 맞을 마음의 준비를 시키고 있었다. 아이에게 마음의 준비가 필요하다고 애틋하게 여기면서."]], [["So that he had change, what cared he? He was longing for it.", "자기 처지에 변화만 생긴다면, 아이가 무엇을 마음 쓰겠는가?"], ["By a thousand eager declarations as to what he would do, when he went to live with his grandfather, he had shown the poor widow how little the idea of parting had cast him down.", "아이는 그 변화를 간절히 바라고 있었다. 할아버지와 함께 살게 되면 무얼 하겠다는 갖가지 들뜬 다짐을 늘어놓음으로써, 아이는 이별이라는 생각에 자기가 얼마나 풀이 죽지 않았는지를 가엾은 미망인에게 고스란히 드러내 보였다."], ["\"He would come and see his mamma often on the pony,\"", "\"조랑말을 타고 엄마를 자주 보러 올게요.\""], ["he said.", "아이가 말했다."], ["\"He would come and fetch her in the carriage; they would drive in the park, and she should have everything she wanted.\"", "\"마차를 타고 와서 엄마를 모셔 갈 거예요. 공원에서 함께 마차를 타고, 엄마가 원하는 건 뭐든 다 갖게 해 드릴게요.\""], ["The poor mother was fain to content herself with these selfish demonstrations of attachment, and tried to convince herself how sincerely her son loved her.", "가엾은 어머니는 이 이기적인 애정의 표시에나마 만족하려 애썼고, 제 아들이 얼마나 진심으로 자기를 사랑하는지를 스스로에게 납득시키려 했다. 아이는 자기를 사랑하는 게 틀림없었다."], ["He must love her.", "아이들이란 다 그런 법이니까."], ["All children were so: a little anxious for novelty, and--no, not selfish, but self-willed.", "새로운 것에 조금 마음이 쏠리고, 또—아니, 이기적인 게 아니라, 제멋대로일 뿐이지."], ["Her child must have his enjoyments and ambition in the world.", "내 아이는 세상에서 누릴 즐거움과 야망을 가져야 마땅하다."], ["She herself, by her own selfishness and imprudent love for him had denied him his just rights and pleasures hitherto.", "자기야말로 자신의 이기심과 분별없는 사랑 때문에 지금껏 아이에게서 마땅한 권리와 즐거움을 빼앗아 온 것이라고, 그녀는 생각했다."]], [["I know few things more affecting than that timorous debasement and self-humiliation of a woman.", "여자의 그 소심한 자기 비하와 자기 낮춤보다 더 가슴 저미는 것을 나는 별로 알지 못한다."], ["How she owns that it is she and not the man who is guilty; how she takes all the faults on her side; how she courts in a manner punishment for the wrongs which she has not committed and persists in shielding the real culprit!", "죄지은 자가 남자가 아니라 자기라고 어떻게 인정하는지, 모든 허물을 어떻게 제 쪽으로 떠안는지, 저지르지도 않은 잘못에 대한 벌을 어떻게 자청하다시피 하며 진짜 죄인을 한사코 감싸려 드는지!"], ["It is those who injure women who get the most kindness from them--they are born timid and tyrants and maltreat those who are humblest before them.", "여자에게 상처 주는 자들이야말로 여자에게서 가장 큰 친절을 받는 법이다. 여자들은 본디 소심하게 태어나, 자기 앞에서 가장 비굴한 자들을 폭군처럼 짓밟고 학대한다."]], [["So poor Amelia had been getting ready in silent misery for her son's departure, and had passed many and many a long solitary hour in making preparations for the end.", "그리하여 가엾은 애밀리아는 말없는 비참 속에서 아들의 떠남을 준비해 오며, 그 끝을 위해 채비하는 데에 길고 긴 외로운 시간을 숱하게 보냈다."], ["George stood by his mother, watching her arrangements without the least concern.", "조지는 어머니 곁에 서서, 조금의 근심도 없이 그 채비를 지켜보았다."], ["Tears had fallen into his boxes; passages had been scored in his favourite books; old toys, relics, treasures had been hoarded away for him, and packed with strange neatness and care--and of all these things the boy took no note.", "아이의 상자들 안으로는 눈물이 떨어졌고, 아이가 가장 좋아하던 책들에는 군데군데 줄이 그어졌으며, 낡은 장난감과 유품과 보물들은 아이를 위해 따로 간직되어 야릇하리만치 정갈하고 정성스럽게 꾸려졌다. 그러나 이 모든 것을 아이는 거들떠보지도 않았다."], ["The child goes away smiling as the mother breaks her heart.", "어머니의 가슴이 미어지는 동안 아이는 웃으며 떠나간다."], ["By heavens it is pitiful, the bootless love of women for children in Vanity Fair.", "맙소사, 허영의 시장에서 아이를 향한 여자들의 그 헛된 사랑이란 참으로 가련한 것이다."]], [["A few days are past, and the great event of Amelia's life is consummated.", "며칠이 지나고, 애밀리아의 생애에서 가장 큰 사건이 마침내 이루어졌다."], ["No angel has intervened.", "그 어떤 천사도 끼어들지 않았다."], ["The child is sacrificed and offered up to fate, and the widow is quite alone.", "아이는 운명에 제물로 바쳐졌고, 미망인은 이제 홀로 남았다."]], [["The boy comes to see her often, to be sure.", "물론 아이는 그녀를 보러 자주 온다."], ["He rides on a pony with a coachman behind him, to the delight of his old grandfather, Sedley, who walks proudly down the lane by his side.", "뒤에 마부를 거느린 채 조랑말을 타고 오는데, 그 곁에서 자랑스레 골목길을 걸어 내려오는 늙은 할아버지 세들리가 여간 기뻐하는 게 아니다."], ["She sees him, but he is not her boy any more.", "그녀는 아이를 보지만, 아이는 이제 더는 그녀의 아이가 아니다."], ["Why, he rides to see the boys at the little school, too, and to show off before them his new wealth and splendour.", "게다가 아이는 그 작은 학교의 친구들을 보러, 또 그들 앞에서 자기의 새 부와 영화를 뽐내러 말을 타고 가기도 한다."], ["In two days he has adopted a slightly imperious air and patronizing manner.", "이틀 만에 아이는 다소 거만한 태도와 거들먹거리는 몸가짐을 익혔다."], ["He was born to command, his mother thinks, as his father was before him.", "제 아버지가 그러했듯, 이 아이도 남을 부리도록 타고났다고, 어머니는 생각한다."]], [["It is fine weather now.", "이제 날씨가 화창하다."], ["Of evenings on the days when he does not come, she takes a long walk into London--yes, as far as Russell Square, and rests on the stone by the railing of the garden opposite Mr.", "아이가 오지 않는 날 저녁이면, 그녀는 런던까지 먼 길을 걸어 나간다—그렇다, 러셀 광장까지, 그 먼 데까지 가서, 오스본 씨 집 맞은편 정원 난간 옆의 돌 위에 앉아 쉰다."], ["Osborne's house. It is so pleasant and cool.", "거기는 어찌나 쾌적하고 시원한지."], ["She can look up and see the drawing-room windows illuminated, and, at about nine o'clock, the chamber in the upper story where Georgy sleeps.", "그녀는 고개를 들어, 불 밝힌 응접실 창문을, 그리고 아홉 시쯤이면 조지가 잠드는 위층의 방을 바라볼 수 있다."], ["She knows--he has told her.", "그녀는 안다—아이가 일러 주었으니까."], ["She prays there as the light goes out, prays with an humble heart, and walks home shrinking and silent.", "불빛이 꺼지면 그녀는 거기서 기도한다. 겸허한 마음으로 기도하고는, 움츠러든 채 말없이 집으로 걸어온다."], ["She is very tired when she comes home.", "집에 돌아올 때면 그녀는 몹시 지쳐 있다."], ["Perhaps she will sleep the better for that long weary walk, and she may dream about Georgy.", "어쩌면 그 길고 고단한 걸음 덕분에 잠이 더 잘 들지도 모르고, 조지의 꿈을 꿀 수 있을지도 모른다."]], [["One Sunday she happened to be walking in Russell Square, at some distance from Mr.", "어느 일요일, 그녀는 마침 러셀 광장을, 오스본 씨 집에서 좀 떨어진 곳에서(그래도 멀리서 그 집이 보이긴 했다) 거닐고 있었는데, 그때 안식일을 알리는 종이 죄다 울려 퍼졌고, 조지와 그의 고모가 교회에 가려고 집을 나섰다."], ["Osborne's house (she could see it from a distance though) when all the bells of Sabbath were ringing, and George and his aunt came out to go to church; a little sweep asked for charity, and the footman, who carried the books, tried to drive him away; but Georgy stopped and gave him money.", "어린 굴뚝 청소부 하나가 적선을 청하자, 책을 들고 있던 하인이 그 아이를 쫓아내려 했다. 그러나 조지가 걸음을 멈추고 그 아이에게 돈을 주었다."], ["May God's blessing be on the boy!", "부디 그 아이에게 하느님의 축복이 있기를!"], ["Emmy ran round the square and, coming up to the sweep, gave him her mite too.", "에미는 광장을 빙 돌아 그 굴뚝 청소부에게 다가가 자기도 얼마 안 되는 돈을 쥐여 주었다."], ["All the bells of Sabbath were ringing, and she followed them until she came to the Foundling Church, into which she went.", "안식일을 알리는 종이 죄다 울려 퍼지고 있었고, 그녀는 그 종소리를 따라가다 마침내 파운들링 교회에 이르러 안으로 들어갔다."], ["There she sat in a place whence she could see the head of the boy under his father's tombstone.", "거기서 그녀는 아이의 머리가, 그 아버지의 묘비 아래로 보이는 자리에 앉았다."], ["Many hundred fresh children's voices rose up there and sang hymns to the Father Beneficent, and little George's soul thrilled with delight at the burst of glorious psalmody.", "그곳에서는 수백 명의 싱그러운 아이들 목소리가 솟아올라, 자비로우신 아버지께 찬송을 불렀고, 어린 조지의 영혼은 그 영광스러운 시편 노래의 물결에 기쁨으로 떨렸다."], ["His mother could not see him for awhile, through the mist that dimmed her eyes.", "어머니는 한동안, 두 눈을 흐리게 하는 안개 너머로 아이를 볼 수 없었다."]], [["# CHAPTER LI", "# CHAPTER LI"]], [["In Which a Charade Is Acted Which May or May Not Puzzle the Reader", "독자를 어리둥절하게 할 수도 있고 안 할 수도 있는 무언극이 펼쳐지는 장"]], [["After Becky's appearance at my Lord Steyne's private and select parties, the claims of that estimable woman as regards fashion were settled, and some of the very greatest and tallest doors in the metropolis were speedily opened to her--doors so great and tall that the beloved reader and writer hereof may hope in vain to enter at them.", "베키가 스테인 경의 사사롭고 엄선된 모임에 모습을 드러낸 뒤로, 이 가상한 여인이 사교계에서 차지하는 위상은 확고하게 자리 잡았고, 머지않아 수도에서 가장 으리으리하고 키 큰 문들이 몇 개씩 그녀 앞에 활짝 열렸다. 어찌나 으리으리하고 키 큰 문이었던지, 이 글을 사랑하는 독자도, 이 글을 쓰는 사람도 그 문으로 들어서기를 바라 봤자 헛일일 것이다."], ["Dear brethren, let us tremble before those august portals.", "친애하는 형제들이여, 저 위풍당당한 대문 앞에서 우리 모두 떨도록 하자."], ["I fancy them guarded by grooms of the chamber with flaming silver forks with which they prong all those who have not the right of the entree.", "내가 보기에 그 문들은 번쩍이는 은 쇠스랑을 든 시종들이 지키고 서서, 입장할 권리가 없는 자들을 죄다 그 쇠스랑으로 찔러 내쫓는 것만 같다."], ["They say the honest newspaper-fellow who sits in the hall and takes down the names of the great ones who are admitted to the feasts dies after a little time.", "사람들 말로는, 그 큰 잔치에 입장이 허락된 명사들의 이름을 받아 적느라 현관에 앉아 있는 저 정직한 신문 기자 양반은 얼마 못 가 죽고 만다고 한다."], ["He can't survive the glare of fashion long.", "그는 사교계의 광채를 오래 견디지 못하는 것이다."], ["It scorches him up, as the presence of Jupiter in full dress wasted that poor imprudent Semele--a giddy moth of a creature who ruined herself by venturing out of her natural atmosphere.", "그 광채가 그를 바싹 태워 버리고 마는 셈인데, 마치 정장을 갖춰 입고 나타난 유피테르가 저 가엾고 경솔한 세멜레를 태워 없앤 것과 같다. 자기 본래의 대기권 밖으로 무모하게 나섰다가 스스로를 파멸시킨, 경박한 나방 같은 존재 말이다."], ["Her myth ought to be taken to heart amongst the Tyburnians, the Belgravians--her story, and perhaps Becky's too.", "타이번 사람들이며 벨그레이비어 사람들은 마땅히 그 신화를 가슴에 새겨야 할 것이다. 그 이야기를, 그리고 어쩌면 베키의 이야기까지도."], ["Ah, ladies!--ask the Reverend Mr.", "아, 숙녀분들이여! 투리퍼 목사에게 한번 물어보시라."], ["Thurifer if Belgravia is not a sounding brass and Tyburnia a tinkling cymbal.", "벨그레이비어가 울리는 꽹과리요 타이번이 소리 나는 징이 아닌지를."], ["These are vanities.", "이런 것들은 다 허영이다."], ["Even these will pass away.", "이런 것들조차 사라지고 말 것이다."], ["And some day or other (but it will be after our time, thank goodness) Hyde Park Gardens will be no better known than the celebrated horticultural outskirts of Babylon, and Belgrave Square will be as desolate as Baker Street, or Tadmor in the wilderness.", "그리고 언젠가는(다행히도 그것은 우리 시대가 지난 뒤의 일이겠지만) 하이드 파크 가든스도 저 이름난 바빌론의 원예 교외만큼이나 알려지지 않은 곳이 될 것이며, 벨그레이브 광장도 베이커 거리나 광야의 타드모르만큼이나 황량해질 것이다."]], [["Ladies, are you aware that the great Pitt lived in Baker Street?", "숙녀분들이여, 그 위대한 피트가 베이커 거리에 살았다는 사실을 알고 계신가?"], ["What would not your grandmothers have given to be asked to Lady Hester's parties in that now decayed mansion?", "지금은 영락한 그 저택에서 헤스터 부인의 모임에 초대받기 위해서라면, 그대들의 할머니들은 무엇인들 내놓지 않았겠는가? 나는 그곳에서 식사를 해 본 사람이다."], ["I have dined in it--moi qui vous parle, I peopled the chamber with ghosts of the mighty dead.", "지금 여러분께 말씀드리는 이 몸이 말이다. 나는 그 방을 위대한 망자들의 유령으로 가득 채웠다."], ["As we sat soberly drinking claret there with men of to-day, the spirits of the departed came in and took their places round the darksome board.", "우리가 오늘날의 사람들과 더불어 진지하게 클라레를 마시며 앉아 있노라니, 떠난 이들의 혼령이 들어와 그 어두컴컴한 식탁 둘레에 제자리를 잡았다. 폭풍을 헤쳐 나간 그 키잡이는 영혼의 포트와인을 큰 잔으로 죽 들이켰고, 던다스의 망령은 한 방울도 남기지 않았다."], ["The pilot who weathered the storm tossed off great bumpers of spiritual port; the shade of Dundas did not leave the ghost of a heeltap.", "애딩턴은 허리를 굽실거리며 섬뜩한 표정으로 히죽거리고 앉아, 소리 없는 술병이 돌아올 때면 결코 뒤지지 않으려 들었다. 스콧은 텁수룩한 눈썹 아래로 술잔 가장자리에 떠도는 거품의 허깨비를 향해 눈을 찡긋했다."], ["Addington sat bowing and smirking in a ghastly manner, and would not be behindhand when the noiseless bottle went round; Scott, from under bushy eyebrows, winked at the apparition of a beeswing; Wilberforce's eyes went up to the ceiling, so that he did not seem to know how his glass went up full to his mouth and came down empty; up to the ceiling which was above us only yesterday, and which the great of the past days have all looked at.", "윌버포스는 두 눈을 천장으로 치켜올린 통에 자기 잔이 어떻게 가득 차서 입까지 올라갔다가 빈 채로 내려오는지도 모르는 듯했다. 바로 어제까지도 우리 머리 위에 있던 그 천장, 지난날의 위인들이 모두 우러러보던 그 천장으로 말이다."], ["They let the house as a furnished lodging now.", "지금은 그 집을 가구 딸린 셋집으로 내놓고 있다."], ["Yes, Lady Hester once lived in Baker Street, and lies asleep in the wilderness.", "그렇다, 헤스터 부인은 한때 베이커 거리에 살았으나, 지금은 광야에 잠들어 있다."], ["Eothen saw her there--not in Baker Street, but in the other solitude.", "이오텐이 거기서 그녀를 보았다. 베이커 거리가 아니라 또 다른 적막한 곳에서 말이다."]], [["It is all vanity to be sure, but who will not own to liking a little of it?", "확실히 이 모든 것이 허영이긴 하나, 그 허영을 조금쯤 좋아한다는 사실을 누가 인정하지 않겠는가?"], ["I should like to know what well-constituted mind, merely because it is transitory, dislikes roast beef?", "한낱 덧없는 것이라는 이유만으로 구운 쇠고기를 싫어하는, 그런 제대로 된 정신의 소유자가 과연 어디 있는지 나는 알고 싶다. 그것도 허영이다."], ["That is a vanity, but may every man who reads this have a wholesome portion of it through life, I beg: aye, though my readers were five hundred thousand.", "그러나 이 글을 읽는 모든 이가 평생토록 그 허영을 넉넉히 누리기를 나는 비는 바이다. 그렇다, 설령 내 독자가 오십만 명이라 해도 말이다."], ["Sit down, gentlemen, and fall to, with a good hearty appetite; the fat, the lean, the gravy, the horse-radish as you like it--don't spare it.", "자, 신사 여러분, 자리에 앉아 왕성한 식욕으로 마음껏 드시라. 비계든 살코기든 고깃국물이든, 그리고 서양고추냉이는 입맛대로, 아끼지 말고 드시라."], ["Another glass of wine, Jones, my boy--a little bit of the Sunday side.", "와인 한 잔 더 하게, 존스, 이 사람아. 그 일요일 만찬용으로 남겨 둔 고기도 한 점 들게."], ["Yes, let us eat our fill of the vain thing and be thankful therefor.", "그렇다, 이 허영의 것을 배불리 먹고 그에 감사하도록 하자."], ["And let us make the best of Becky's aristocratic pleasures likewise--for these too, like all other mortal delights, were but transitory.", "그리고 베키의 그 귀족적인 쾌락도 마찬가지로 우리가 한껏 즐기도록 하자. 이런 것들도 다른 온갖 인간사의 기쁨과 마찬가지로 한낱 덧없는 것에 지나지 않았으니 말이다."]], [["The upshot of her visit to Lord Steyne was that His Highness the Prince of Peterwaradin took occasion to renew his acquaintance with Colonel Crawley, when they met on the next day at the Club, and to compliment Mrs.", "베키가 스테인 경을 방문한 결과는 이러했다. 페테르바라딘 대공 전하께서 이튿날 클럽에서 크롤리 대령을 만났을 때 그와의 친분을 새로이 다질 기회를 잡았고, 하이드 파크의 승마장에서는 크롤리 부인에게 모자를 깊숙이 벗어 들며 정중히 인사를 건넨 것이다."], ["Crawley in the Ring of Hyde Park with a profound salute of the hat.", "그녀와 그녀의 남편은 곧바로 대공이 르방 저택에서 여는 소규모 모임 가운데 하나에 초대받았다."], ["She and her husband were invited immediately to one of the Prince's small parties at Levant House, then occupied by His Highness during the temporary absence from England of its noble proprietor.", "르방 저택은 그 고귀한 소유주가 잠시 영국을 비운 사이 대공 전하께서 머무르고 계신 곳이었다."], ["She sang after dinner to a very little comite.", "그녀는 만찬 뒤에 아주 조촐한 모임 앞에서 노래를 불렀다."], ["The Marquis of Steyne was present, paternally superintending the progress of his pupil.", "스테인 후작이 그 자리에 함께하여, 제자가 발전해 가는 모습을 아비 같은 마음으로 지켜보았다."]], [["At Levant House Becky met one of the finest gentlemen and greatest ministers that Europe has produced--the Duc de la Jabotiere, then Ambassador from the Most Christian King, and subsequently Minister to that monarch.", "르방 저택에서 베키는 유럽이 배출한 가장 빼어난 신사이자 가장 위대한 대신 가운데 한 사람을 만났다. 다름 아닌 자보티에르 공작이었으니, 당시 가장 그리스도교적인 국왕이 보낸 대사였고 훗날 그 군주의 대신이 된 인물이었다."], ["I declare I swell with pride as these august names are", "이 위풍당당한 이름들을 적어 내려가노라니 정말이지 나 자신이 자랑스러워 가슴이 부풀어 오르는 것이다."]], [["Becky is moving.", "베키는 차츰 사교계를 누비고 다녔다."], ["She became a constant guest at the French Embassy, where no party was considered to be complete without the presence of the charming Madame Ravdonn Cravley.", "그녀는 프랑스 대사관의 단골손님이 되었으니, 그곳에서는 매혹적인 로던 크롤리 부인이 자리하지 않으면 어떤 모임도 완전하다고 여겨지지 않았다."], ["Messieurs de Truffigny (of the Perigord family) and Champignac, both attaches of the Embassy, were straightway smitten by the charms of the fair Colonel's wife, and both declared, according to the wont of their nation (for who ever yet met a Frenchman, come out of England, that has not left half a dozen families miserable, and brought away as many hearts in his pocket-book?), both, I say, declared that they were au mieux with the charming Madame Ravdonn.", "두 사람 다 대사관 부속 직원이던 트뤼피니 씨(페리고르 가문 출신이었다)와 샹피냐크 씨는 대번에 그 어여쁜 대령 부인의 매력에 사로잡혔고, 둘 다 자기 나라의 관습에 따라 선언하기를(영국에서 나온 프랑스인치고 적어도 대여섯 가문을 비참하게 만들어 놓고 그만큼의 마음을 자기 수첩에 챙겨 가지 않은 자를 여태 본 적이 있던가?), 거듭 말하거니와 둘 다 매혹적인 로던 부인과 더할 나위 없이 가까운 사이라고 선언했다."]], [["But I doubt the correctness of the assertion.", "그러나 나는 그 주장이 옳은지 의심스럽다."], ["Champignac was very fond of ecarte, and made many parties with the Colonel of evenings, while Becky was singing to Lord Steyne in the other room; and as for Truffigny, it is a well-known fact that he dared not go to the Travellers', where he owed money to the waiters, and if he had not had the Embassy as a dining-place, the worthy young gentleman must have starved.", "샹피냐크는 에카르테를 무척 좋아하여 저녁마다 대령과 여러 판을 벌이곤 했는데, 그동안 베키는 옆방에서 스테인 경에게 노래를 들려주고 있었다. 그리고 트뤼피니로 말할 것 같으면, 그가 트래블러스 클럽에는 감히 발걸음을 못 했다는 것이 익히 알려진 사실이다."], ["I doubt, I say, that Becky would have selected either of these young men as a person on whom she would bestow her special regard.", "거기 급사들에게 진 빚이 있었던 까닭이다. 그러니 식사할 곳으로 대사관이 없었더라면 이 가상한 젊은 신사는 영락없이 굶주릴 판이었다."], ["They ran of her messages, purchased her gloves and flowers, went in debt for opera-boxes for her, and made themselves amiable in a thousand ways.", "거듭 말하거니와, 나는 베키가 이 두 젊은이 가운데 누구를 골라 각별한 정을 베풀었으리라고는 의심스럽게 여긴다."], ["And they talked English with adorable simplicity, and to the constant amusement of Becky and my Lord Steyne, she would mimic one or other to his face, and compliment him on his advance in the English language with a gravity which never failed to tickle the Marquis, her sardonic old patron.", "그들은 그녀의 심부름을 다녀오고, 장갑과 꽃을 사다 바치고, 그녀를 위해 빚을 내어 오페라 박스석을 마련하는가 하면, 천 가지 방식으로 살갑게 굴었다. 그리고 그들은 사랑스러우리만치 어수룩한 영어를 구사했는데, 베키와 스테인 경은 그것을 두고 늘 즐거워했고, 베키는 그 둘 가운데 누구든 그 사람 면전에서 흉내를 내고는 영어 실력이 늘었다고 짐짓 점잖게 칭찬을 건넸으니, 그 진지한 태도가 그녀의 냉소적인 늙은 후원자인 후작을 어김없이 간질여 웃기는 것이었다."], ["Truffigny gave Briggs a shawl by way of winning over Becky's confidante, and asked her to take charge of a letter which the simple spinster handed over in public to the person to whom it was addressed, and the composition of which amused everybody who read it greatly.", "트뤼피니는 베키의 심복인 브리그스의 마음을 사려고 그녀에게 숄을 선물하고는, 편지 한 통을 맡아 달라고 부탁했는데, 순진한 그 노처녀는 그 편지를 여러 사람이 보는 앞에서 받는 사람 본인에게 그대로 건네 버렸고, 그 편지의 문구는 그것을 읽은 모든 이를 크게 즐겁게 했다."], ["Lord Steyne read it, everybody but honest Rawdon, to whom it was not necessary to tell everything that passed in the little house in May Fair.", "스테인 경도 그것을 읽었고, 정직한 로던만 빼고 모두가 읽었다. 메이 페어의 그 작은 집에서 벌어지는 일을 그에게 죄다 일러 줄 필요는 없었던 것이다."]], [["Here, before long, Becky received not only \"the best\" foreigners (as the phrase is in our noble and admirable society slang), but some of the best English people too.", "이곳에서 머지않아 베키는 \"최상류\"의 외국인들뿐 아니라(우리의 고상하고 감탄스러운 사교계 은어로 하면 그렇다는 말이다) 최상류의 영국인들 가운데 일부도 맞아들이게 되었다."], ["I don't mean the most virtuous, or indeed the least virtuous, or the cleverest, or the stupidest, or the richest, or the best born, but \"the best,\"--in a word, people about whom there is no question--such as the great Lady Fitz-Willis, that Patron Saint of Almack's, the great Lady Slowbore, the great Lady Grizzel Macbeth (she was Lady G.", "가장 덕망 높은 자들이라거나, 사실 가장 덕망 없는 자들이라거나, 가장 영리한 자들이라거나, 가장 어리석은 자들이라거나, 가장 부유한 자들이라거나, 가장 가문 좋은 자들을 말하는 것이 아니다. 그저 \"최상류\"라는 것이다."], ["Glowry, daughter of Lord Grey of Glowry), and the like.", "한마디로, 이러쿵저러쿵 따질 여지가 없는 사람들 말이다."], ["When the Countess of Fitz-Willis (her Ladyship is of the Kingstreet family, see Debrett and Burke) takes up a person, he or she is safe.", "이를테면 올맥의 수호성인이신 저 위대한 피츠윌리스 부인, 위대한 슬로보어 부인, 위대한 그리젤 맥베스 부인(이분은 글로리의 그레이 경의 따님인 그리젤 글로리 부인이었다) 같은 분들이다. 피츠윌리스 백작 부인(이 마님은 킹 스트리트 가문 출신이니, 데브렛과 버크를 보시라)이 한번 누군가를 받아들이면, 그 남자든 여자든 안전한 것이다."], ["There is no question about them any more.", "더 이상 그들에 대해 왈가왈부할 것이 없어진다. 그렇다고 피츠윌리스 마님이 남들보다 더 나은 사람이라는 말은 아니다."], ["Not that my Lady Fitz-Willis is any better than anybody else, being, on the contrary, a faded person, fifty-seven years of age, and neither handsome, nor wealthy, nor entertaining; but it is agreed on all sides that she is of the \"best people.\"", "오히려 반대로, 쉰일곱 살 먹은 빛바랜 인물이요, 미인도 아니고 부유하지도 않고 재미있지도 않은 분이다. 그러나 그분이 \"최상류 인사\"에 속한다는 데에는 모두가 한목소리로 동의한다."], ["Those who go to her are of the best: and from an old grudge probably to Lady Steyne (for whose coronet her ladyship, then the youthful Georgina Frederica, daughter of the Prince of Wales's favourite, the Earl of Portansherry, had once tried), this great and famous leader of the fashion chose to acknowledge Mrs.", "그분을 찾아가는 사람들은 다 최상류인 것이다. 그리고 아마도 스테인 부인에 대한 해묵은 원한 탓이었겠지만(당시 젊은 조지나 프레더리카, 즉 웨일스 공의 총신이던 포탠셰리 백작의 따님이던 이 마님이 한때 그 후작 부인 자리를 노린 적이 있었다), 이 위대하고 이름난 사교계의 선도자께서 로던 크롤리 부인을 인정하기로 마음먹은 것이다."], ["Rawdon Crawley; made her a most marked curtsey at the assembly over which she presided; and not only encouraged her son, St. Kitts (his lordship got his place through Lord Steyne's interest), to frequent Mrs.", "자신이 주관하던 그 모임에서 베키에게 누구보다 눈에 띄게 무릎을 굽혀 인사를 건넸고, 자기 아들 세인트키츠(이 도련님은 스테인 경의 주선으로 그 자리를 얻었다)에게 크롤리 부인의 집을 자주 드나들도록 부추겼을 뿐 아니라, 그녀를 자신의 저택으로 초대하기까지 했으며 만찬 자리에서 더없이 공개적이고 자상한 태도로 그녀에게 두 차례나 말을 건넸다."], ["Crawley's house, but asked her to her own mansion and spoke to her twice in the most public and condescending manner during dinner.", "그날 밤 이 중대한 사실이 런던 전역에 알려졌다."], ["The important fact was known all over London that night.", "크롤리 부인을 두고 망측하다며 험담하던 사람들이 입을 다물었다."], ["People who had been crying fie about Mrs. Crawley were silent.", "재사이자 변호사요 스테인 경의 오른팔인 웬엄은 곳곳을 돌아다니며 그녀를 칭송했다."], ["Wenham, the wit and lawyer, Lord Steyne's right-hand man, went about everywhere praising her: some who had hesitated, came forward at once and welcomed her; little Tom Toady, who had warned Southdown about visiting such an abandoned woman, now besought to be introduced to her.", "머뭇거리던 몇몇은 대번에 나서서 그녀를 환대했고, 사우스다운에게 그런 타락한 여자를 찾아가지 말라고 경고했던 꼬마 톰 토디는 이제 그녀에게 소개시켜 달라고 사정했다. 한마디로, 그녀는 \"최상류\""], ["In a word, she was admitted to be among the \"best\" people.", "인사 축에 들었음을 인정받은 것이다."], ["Ah, my beloved readers and brethren, do not envy poor Becky prematurely--glory like this is said to be fugitive.", "아, 사랑하는 독자들이여, 형제들이여, 가엾은 베키를 섣불리 부러워하지 마시라. 이런 영광이란 덧없는 것이라 하지 않는가."], ["It is currently reported that even in the very inmost circles, they are no happier than the poor wanderers outside the zone; and Becky, who penetrated into the very centre of fashion and saw the great George IV face to face, has owned since that there too was Vanity.", "흔히들 전하는 말로는, 그 가장 깊숙한 핵심 모임 안에서조차 그들이 그 권역 바깥을 떠도는 가엾은 방랑자들보다 더 행복할 것도 없다고 한다. 그리고 사교계의 한복판까지 꿰뚫고 들어가 저 위대한 조지 4세를 코앞에서 마주 본 베키도, 그곳에 역시 허영이 있더라고 그 뒤로 인정한 바 있다."]], [["We must be brief in descanting upon this part of her career.", "그녀의 이 시절을 논함에 있어서는 우리도 간략히 해야겠다."], ["As I cannot describe the mysteries of freemasonry, although I have a shrewd idea that it is a humbug, so an uninitiated man cannot take upon himself to portray the great world accurately, and had best keep his opinions to himself, whatever they are.", "내가 프리메이슨의 비밀을 묘사할 수 없는 것은—그것이 허튼수작이라는 짐작이 제법 들기는 하지만—마찬가지로, 입문하지 않은 자가 상류 사회를 정확하게 그려 보이겠노라 나설 수는 없는 노릇이니, 그 견해가 무엇이든 간에 혼자 속으로 간직하는 편이 상책인 것이다."]], [["Becky has often spoken in subsequent years of this season of her life, when she moved among the very greatest circles of the London fashion.", "베키는 그 뒤로 여러 해 동안, 런던 사교계의 가장 위대한 모임을 누비고 다니던 이 시절의 이야기를 자주 입에 올리곤 했다. 그녀의 성공은 그녀를 들뜨게 하고, 우쭐하게 하고, 그러다 마침내 따분하게 만들었다."], ["Her success excited, elated, and then bored her. At first no occupation was more pleasant than to invent and procure (the latter a work of no small trouble and ingenuity, by the way, in a person of Mrs.", "처음에는 더없이 어여쁜 새 옷과 장신구를 고안하고 또 마련해 들이는 일만큼 즐거운 소일거리가 없었다(덧붙이자면, 후자, 즉 그것을 마련해 들이는 일은 로던 크롤리 부인처럼 형편이 빠듯한 사람에게는 적잖은 수고와 재간이 드는 일이었다)."], ["Rawdon Crawley's very narrow means)--to procure, we say, the prettiest new dresses and ornaments; to drive to fine dinner parties, where she was welcomed by great people; and from the fine dinner parties to fine assemblies, whither the same people came with whom she had been dining, whom she had met the night before, and would see on the morrow--the young men faultlessly appointed, handsomely cravatted, with the neatest glossy boots and white gloves--the elders portly, brass-buttoned, noble-looking, polite, and prosy--the young ladies blonde, timid, and in pink--the mothers grand, beautiful, sumptuous, solemn, and in diamonds.", "멋진 만찬 모임으로 마차를 몰고 가면 명사들이 그녀를 반겨 주었고, 멋진 만찬 모임에서 다시 멋진 무도회로 옮겨 가면, 그곳에는 함께 식사한 바로 그 사람들, 전날 밤 만났고 이튿날 또 보게 될 그 사람들이 오는 것이었다. 흠잡을 데 없이 차려입고, 멋들어진 넥타이를 매고, 더없이 매끈하게 윤이 나는 부츠에 흰 장갑을 낀 젊은이들이며, 풍채 좋고 놋쇠 단추를 단, 고결해 보이고 정중하면서도 따분한 노인들이며, 금발에 수줍어하며 분홍 옷을 차려입은 젊은 숙녀들이며, 위풍당당하고 아름답고 화려하고 근엄하며 다이아몬드를 두른 그 어머니들이 말이다."], ["They talked in English, not in bad French, as they do in the novels.", "그들은 소설에서처럼 서툰 프랑스어가 아니라 영어로 이야기를 나누었다. 그들은 서로의 저택이며 사람됨이며 가문에 대해 떠들어 댔으니, 꼭 존스네가 스미스네 이야기를 하는 것과 다를 바 없었다."], ["They talked about each others'", "베키의 옛 지인들은 그녀를 미워하고 시기했다."], ["houses, and characters, and families--just as the Joneses do about the Smiths.", "그러나 가엾은 그녀 자신은 속으로 하품을 하고 있었다. \"이 바닥에서 벗어났으면 좋겠어.\""], ["Becky's former acquaintances hated and envied her; the poor woman herself was yawning in spirit.", "그녀는 혼자 중얼거렸다. \"이러느니 차라리 목사 아내가 되어 주일학교를 가르치는 편이 낫겠어."], ["\"I wish I were out of it,\" she said to herself.", "아니면 부사관 부인이 되어 연대 마차나 타고 다니거나."], ["\"I would rather be a parson's wife and teach a Sunday school than this; or a sergeant's lady and ride in the regimental waggon; or, oh, how much gayer it would be to wear spangles and trousers and dance before a booth at a fair.\"", "아니, 그게 아니라, 차라리 번쩍이는 장식을 단 옷에 바지를 입고 장터 천막 앞에서 춤추는 편이 얼마나 더 흥겨울까.\""]], [["\"You would do it very well,\"", "\"당신이라면 아주 잘 해낼 거요.\""], ["said Lord Steyne, laughing.", "스테인 경이 웃으며 말했다."], ["She used to tell the great man her ennuis and perplexities in her artless way--they amused him.", "그녀는 위대한 인물인 그에게 자신의 권태와 곤혹스러움을 천연덕스러운 투로 털어놓곤 했는데, 그것이 그를 즐겁게 했다."]], [["\"Rawdon would make a very good Ecuyer--Master of the Ceremonies--what do you call him--the man in the large boots and the uniform, who goes round the ring cracking the whip?", "\"로던이라면 곡마단 단장 노릇을, 그러니까 의전관이라고 해야 하나, 뭐라고 부르더라, 그 커다란 부츠에 제복을 입고 채찍을 휘두르며 마장을 도는 그 사람 말이에요, 아주 훌륭하게 해낼 거예요."], ["He is large, heavy, and of a military figure.", "그이는 덩치가 크고 묵직한 데다 군인다운 풍채가 있잖아요."], ["I recollect,\"", "기억이 나요.\""], ["Becky continued pensively, \"my father took me to see a show at Brookgreen Fair when I was a child, and when we came home, I made myself a pair of stilts and danced in the studio to the wonder of all the pupils.\"", "베키는 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 듯 말을 이었다. \"어릴 적에 아버지가 브룩그린 장터에 구경을 데려가 주셨는데, 집에 돌아와서는 죽마를 만들어 화실에서 춤을 추어 모든 제자들을 놀라게 한 적이 있어요.\""]], [["\"I should have liked to see it,\"", "\"그걸 봤더라면 좋았을 것을.\""], ["said Lord Steyne.", "스테인 경이 말했다."]], [["\"I should like to do it now,\"", "\"지금 한번 해 보고 싶어요.\""], ["Becky continued.", "베키가 말을 이었다."], ["\"How Lady Blinkey would open her eyes, and Lady Grizzel Macbeth would stare!", "\"블린키 부인은 눈을 휘둥그렇게 뜰 거고, 그리젤 맥베스 부인은 빤히 쳐다보겠지요!"], ["Hush!", "쉿!"], ["silence!", "조용히!"], ["there is Pasta beginning to sing.\"", "파스타가 노래를 시작하네요.\""], ["Becky always made a point of being conspicuously polite to the professional ladies and gentlemen who attended at these aristocratic parties--of following them into the corners where they sat in silence, and shaking hands with them, and smiling in the view of all persons.", "베키는 이런 귀족들의 모임에 불려 온 전문 가수며 연주자며 하는 분들에게는 늘 유난히 정중하게 구는 것을 잊지 않았다. 그들이 말없이 앉아 있는 구석 자리까지 따라가 손을 맞잡고, 모든 사람이 보는 앞에서 그들에게 미소를 지어 보이는 것이었다."], ["She was an artist herself, as she said very truly; there was a frankness and humility in the manner in which she acknowledged her origin, which provoked, or disarmed, or amused lookers-on, as the case might be.", "그녀가 참으로 옳게 말했듯, 그녀 자신도 예술가였다. 자신의 출신을 인정하는 그 솔직하고 겸허한 태도가, 보는 이에 따라 어떤 이의 부아를 돋우는가 하면, 어떤 이의 적의를 누그러뜨리고, 또 어떤 이를 즐겁게 했다."], ["\"How cool that woman is,\"", "\"저 여자는 어쩌면 저렇게 태연할까.\""], ["said one; \"what airs of independence she assumes, where she ought to sit still and be thankful if anybody speaks to her!\"", "누군가는 이렇게 말했다. \"가만히 앉아서 누가 말이라도 걸어 주면 감지덕지해야 할 처지에, 무슨 독립이라도 한 양 저런 허세를 부린담!\""], ["\"What an honest and good-natured soul she is!\"", "\"참으로 정직하고 마음씨 고운 사람이로군!\" 또 다른 이는 이렇게 말했다."], ["said another.", "\"교활한 계집애 같으니!\""], ["\"What an artful little minx\" said a third.", "세 번째 사람은 이렇게 말했다."], ["They were all right very likely, but Becky went her own way, and so fascinated the professional personages that they would leave off their sore throats in order to sing at her parties and give her lessons for nothing.", "어쩌면 그들 모두 옳았을 것이다. 그러나 베키는 제 갈 길을 갔고, 그 전문가들을 어찌나 사로잡았던지 그들은 목이 아픈 것도 마다하고 그녀의 모임에서 노래를 불러 주고 거저 가르침까지 주는 것이었다."]], [["Yes, she gave parties in the little house in Curzon Street.", "그렇다, 그녀는 커즌 거리의 그 작은 집에서 모임을 열었다."], ["Many scores of carriages, with blazing lamps, blocked up the street, to the disgust of No. 100, who could not rest for the thunder of the knocking, and of 102, who could not sleep for envy.", "등불을 환히 밝힌 마차 수십 대가 거리를 가로막아, 문 두드리는 소리가 천둥처럼 울려 도무지 쉴 수가 없던 100번지 집의 부아를 돋우고, 시기심에 잠을 못 이루던 102번지 집의 속을 뒤집어 놓았다."], ["The gigantic footmen who accompanied the vehicles were too big to be contained in Becky's little hall, and were billeted off in the neighbouring public-houses, whence, when they were wanted, call-boys summoned them from their beer.", "그 마차들을 따라온 거구의 하인들은 너무 덩치가 커서 베키의 작은 현관에 다 들어가지를 못해 이웃 술집에 나누어 머물렀고, 필요할 때면 사환들이 그들을 맥주잔 앞에서 불러왔다."], ["Scores of the great dandies of London squeezed and trod on each other on the little stairs, laughing to find themselves there; and many spotless and severe ladies of ton were seated in the little drawing-room, listening to the professional singers, who were singing according to their wont, and as if they wished to blow the windows down.", "런던의 멋쟁이 신사 수십 명이 그 작은 계단에서 서로 밀치고 발을 밟아 가며, 자신들이 그곳에 와 있다는 사실에 즐거워 웃어 댔다. 그리고 더없이 흠잡을 데 없고 근엄한 사교계의 숙녀들이 그 작은 응접실에 앉아, 전문 가수들의 노래에 귀를 기울였으니, 그 가수들은 으레 그러하듯, 꼭 창문이라도 날려 버릴 듯이 목청껏 노래를 불러 댔다."], ["And the day after, there appeared among the fashionable reunions in the Morning Post a paragraph to the following effect:", "그리고 이튿날이면 「모닝 포스트」의 사교계 동정란에 다음과 같은 취지의 기사 한 토막이 실리는 것이었다."]], [["\"Yesterday, Colonel and Mrs. Crawley entertained a select party at dinner at their house in May Fair. Their Excellencies the Prince and Princess of Peterwaradin, H. E. Papoosh Pasha, the Turkish Ambassador (attended by Kibob Bey, dragoman of the mission), the Marquess of Steyne, Earl of Southdown, Sir Pitt and Lady Jane Crawley, Mr. Wagg, &c. After dinner Mrs. Crawley had an assembly which was attended by the Duchess (Dowager) of Stilton, Duc de la Gruyere, Marchioness of Cheshire, Marchese Alessandro Strachino, Comte de Brie, Baron Schapzuger, Chevalier Tosti, Countess of Slingstone, and Lady F. Macadam, Major-General and Lady G. Macbeth, and (2) Miss Macbeths; Viscount Paddington, Sir Horace Fogey, Hon. Sands Bedwin, Bobachy Bahawder,\" and an &c., which the reader may fill at his pleasure through a dozen close lines of small type.", "\"어제 크롤리 대령 부부는 메이 페어의 저택에서 엄선된 손님들을 모시고 만찬을 베풀었다. 페테르바라딘 대공 및 대공비 전하, 터키 대사인 파푸시 파샤 각하(공사관 통역관인 키봅 베이 동행), 스테인 후작, 사우스다운 백작, 피트 크롤리 경 부부, 왜그 씨 등이 참석했다. 만찬 뒤에 크롤리 부인은 모임을 열었으니, 스틸턴 공작 미망인, 그뤼예르 공작, 체셔 후작 부인, 알레산드로 스트라키노 후작, 브리 백작, 샵추거 남작, 토스티 기사, 슬링스톤 백작 부인, 그리고 F. 매캐덤 부인, 매캐덤 소장 부부, 그리고 (두 분의) 매베스 양들, 패딩턴 자작, 호러스 포기 경, 샌즈 베드윈 각하, 보바치 바하더 등이 참석했다.\" 그리고 등등의 내역이 빽빽한 작은 활자로 열 몇 줄 더 이어졌으니, 독자께서는 마음껏 채워 넣으시기 바란다."]], [["And in her commerce with the great our dear friend showed the same frankness which distinguished her transactions with the lowly in station.", "명사들과 교제하는 자리에서도 우리의 사랑스러운 벗은, 신분 낮은 이들과 거래할 때 보여 주던 것과 똑같은 솔직함을 드러냈다."], ["On one occasion, when out at a very fine house, Rebecca was (perhaps rather ostentatiously) holding a conversation in the French language with a celebrated tenor singer of that nation, while the Lady Grizzel Macbeth looked over her shoulder scowling at the pair.", "한번은 아주 으리으리한 저택에 갔을 때, 리베카가 (어쩌면 좀 과시하는 투로) 그 나라의 이름난 테너 가수와 프랑스어로 대화를 나누고 있었는데, 그리젤 맥베스 부인이 그 어깨 너머로 두 사람을 노려보고 있었다."]], [["\"How very well you speak French,\"", "\"프랑스어를 참 잘하시는군요.\""], ["Lady Grizzel said, who herself spoke the tongue in an Edinburgh accent most remarkable to hear.", "그리젤 부인이 말했다. 정작 그녀 자신은 그 언어를 듣기에 더없이 별난 에든버러 억양으로 구사했다."]], [["\"I ought to know it,\"", "\"마땅히 알아야지요.\""], ["Becky modestly said, casting down her eyes.", "베키는 두 눈을 내리깔며 겸손하게 말했다."], ["\"I taught it in a school, and my mother was a Frenchwoman.\"", "\"제가 학교에서 프랑스어를 가르쳤거든요. 게다가 어머니가 프랑스 분이셨고요.\""]], [["Lady Grizzel was won by her humility and was mollified towards the little woman.", "그리젤 부인은 그녀의 겸손함에 마음이 사로잡혀, 이 자그마한 여인에게 누그러졌다."], ["She deplored the fatal levelling tendencies of the age, which admitted persons of all classes into the society of their superiors, but her ladyship owned that this one at least was well behaved and never forgot her place in life.", "부인은 온갖 계층의 사람들을 윗사람들의 모임에 끼워 주는, 이 시대의 그 고약한 평준화 풍조를 개탄했지만, 그래도 적어도 이 여자만큼은 처신이 발라서 제 분수를 잊는 법이 없다고 마님은 인정했다. 그녀는 참으로 선량한 부인이었다."], ["She was a very good woman: good to the poor; stupid, blameless, unsuspicious.", "가난한 이들에게 너그럽고, 어리석고, 흠잡을 데 없고, 남을 의심할 줄 모르는 분이었다."], ["It is not her ladyship's fault that she fancies herself better than you and me. The skirts of her ancestors'", "자신이 그대나 나보다 더 낫다고 여기는 것이 그 마님의 잘못은 아니다."], ["garments have been kissed for centuries; it is a thousand years, they say, since the tartans of the head of the family were embraced by the defunct Duncan's lords and councillors, when the great ancestor of the House became King of Scotland.", "그녀의 조상들이 입던 옷자락은 수백 년 동안 입맞춤을 받아 왔다. 사람들 말로는, 그 가문의 위대한 시조가 스코틀랜드의 왕이 되었을 때 작고한 던컨의 영주들과 고문들이 그 가문 수장의 타탄 격자무늬에 입을 맞춘 지가 천 년이나 되었다고 한다."]], [["Lady Steyne, after the music scene, succumbed before Becky, and perhaps was not disinclined to her.", "음악 소동이 있고 난 뒤, 스테인 부인은 베키 앞에 무릎을 꿇었고, 어쩌면 그녀에게 호의가 없지도 않았다."], ["The younger ladies of the house of Gaunt were also compelled into submission.", "곤트 가문의 젊은 부인들도 어쩔 수 없이 굴복하고 말았다."], ["Once or twice they set people at her, but they failed.", "한두 번 사람을 시켜 그녀에게 시비를 걸어 보았으나 실패로 돌아갔다."], ["The brilliant Lady Stunnington tried a passage of arms with her, but was routed with great slaughter by the intrepid little Becky.", "눈부신 스터닝턴 부인이 그녀와 한판 설전을 벌여 보았으나, 겁 없는 자그마한 베키에게 처참하게 패하고 말았다."], ["When attacked sometimes, Becky had a knack of adopting a demure ingenue air, under which she was most dangerous.", "이따금 공격을 받을 때면, 베키는 새침하고 천진한 처녀의 태도를 취하는 재간이 있었는데, 바로 그 태도 아래에 있을 때 그녀가 가장 위험했다."], ["She said the wickedest things with the most simple unaffected air when in this mood, and would take care artlessly to apologize for her blunders, so that all the world should know that she had made them.", "이런 기분일 때면 그녀는 더없이 단순하고 꾸밈없는 태도로 가장 짓궂은 말을 내뱉고는, 짐짓 천연덕스럽게 자기 실언을 사과하여 온 세상이 그녀가 그런 말을 했음을 알게 만드는 것이었다."]], [["Mr. Wagg, the celebrated wit, and a led captain and trencher-man of my Lord Steyne, was caused by the ladies to charge her; and the worthy fellow, leering at his patronesses and giving them a wink, as much as to say, \"Now look out for sport,\"", "이름난 재사이자 스테인 경에게 빌붙어 식객 노릇을 하는 왜그 씨가 숙녀들의 사주를 받아 그녀를 공격하게 되었다."], ["one evening began an assault upon Becky, who was unsuspiciously eating her dinner.", "이 가상한 친구는 자기 후원자인 마님들을 곁눈질하며 \"자, 이제 재미난 구경거리를 기대하시라\"는 듯 한쪽 눈을 찡긋해 보이고는, 어느 날 저녁 아무것도 모른 채 식사를 하고 있던 베키를 향해 공격을 개시했다."], ["The little woman, attacked on a sudden, but never without arms, lighted up in an instant, parried and riposted with a home-thrust, which made Wagg's face tingle with shame; then she returned to her soup with the most perfect calm and a quiet smile on her face.", "느닷없이 공격을 당하긴 했으나 결코 무방비 상태인 적이 없던 이 자그마한 여인은, 순식간에 눈을 번뜩이며 받아넘기더니, 정통으로 찌르는 한 방으로 되받아쳐 왜그의 얼굴을 수치심에 화끈거리게 만들었다. 그러고는 더없이 완벽하게 침착한 태도로, 얼굴에 잔잔한 미소를 띤 채 다시 수프로 돌아갔다."], ["Wagg's great patron, who gave him dinners and lent him a little money sometimes, and whose election, newspaper, and other jobs Wagg did, gave the luckless fellow such a savage glance with the eyes as almost made him sink under the table and burst into tears.", "왜그에게 만찬을 베풀고 이따금 약간의 돈도 꾸어 주며, 왜그가 선거며 신문이며 온갖 잡일을 도맡아 처리해 주던 그 위대한 후원자는, 그 가련한 친구에게 두 눈으로 어찌나 사나운 눈초리를 던졌던지 그가 식탁 아래로 꺼지듯 주저앉아 울음을 터뜨릴 지경이었다."], ["He looked piteously at my lord, who never spoke to him during dinner, and at the ladies, who disowned him.", "그는 가련한 눈길로 후작을 바라보았으나 후작은 만찬 내내 그에게 말 한마디 건네지 않았고, 숙녀들을 바라보았으나 그들도 그를 모르는 체했다."], ["At last Becky herself took compassion upon him and tried to engage him in talk.", "마침내 베키 자신이 그를 가엾이 여겨 대화에 끌어들이려 애썼다. 그는 이후 여섯 주 동안 만찬에 다시 초대받지 못했다."], ["He was not asked to dinner again for six weeks; and Fiche, my lord's confidential man, to whom Wagg naturally paid a good deal of court, was instructed to tell him that if he ever dared to say a rude thing to Mrs. Crawley again, or make her the butt of his stupid jokes, Milor would put every one of his notes of hand into his lawyer's hands and sell him up without mercy.", "그리고 왜그가 자연히 온갖 비위를 맞추던 후작의 심복 피슈는, 만일 그가 다시 한번 감히 크롤리 부인에게 무례한 말을 하거나 그녀를 자신의 어리석은 농담거리로 삼는다면, 후작 나리께서 그의 약속어음을 죄다 변호사 손에 넘겨 인정사정없이 그를 파산시켜 버리겠노라고 전하라는 분부를 받았다."], ["Wagg wept before Fiche and implored his dear friend to intercede for him.", "왜그는 피슈 앞에서 눈물을 흘리며, 친애하는 벗이여 부디 자신을 위해 중재해 달라고 애원했다."], ["He wrote a poem in favour of Mrs.", "그는 R."], ["R.", "C."], ["C., which appeared in the very next number of the Harum-scarum Magazine, which he conducted.", "부인을 찬양하는 시 한 편을 지었고, 그것은 그가 주관하던 「허럼스캐럼 매거진」 바로 다음 호에 실렸다."], ["He implored her good-will at parties where he met her.", "그는 그녀를 만나는 모임마다 그녀의 호의를 간청했다."], ["He cringed and coaxed Rawdon at the club.", "그는 클럽에서 로던에게 굽실거리며 비위를 맞추었다."], ["He was allowed to come back to Gaunt House after a while.", "얼마 지나서 그는 곤트 저택에 다시 드나드는 것을 허락받았다."], ["Becky was always good to him, always amused, never angry.", "베키는 늘 그에게 다정했고, 늘 즐거워했으며, 결코 화를 내는 법이 없었다."]], [["His lordship's vizier and chief confidential servant (with a seat in parliament and at the dinner table), Mr. Wenham, was much more prudent in his behaviour and opinions than Mr. Wagg. However much he might be disposed to hate all parvenus (Mr. Wenham himself was a staunch old True Blue Tory, and his father a small coal-merchant in the north of England), this aide-de-camp of the Marquis never showed any sort of hostility to the new favourite, but pursued her with stealthy kindnesses and a sly and deferential politeness which somehow made Becky more uneasy than other people's overt hostilities.", "후작의 재상이자 으뜸가는 심복(국회와 만찬 식탁에 다 자리를 가진)인 웬엄 씨는 왜그 씨보다 처신도, 견해도 훨씬 더 신중했다. 그가 졸부라면 모조리 미워할 마음이 제아무리 굴뚝같았다 한들(웬엄 씨 자신은 골수 보수 토리당원이었고, 그의 아버지는 잉글랜드 북부의 자그마한 석탄 상인이었다), 이 후작의 부관은 새 총신에게 결코 어떤 적의도 드러내는 법이 없었으며, 오히려 은밀한 친절과, 어딘가 음흉하면서도 깍듯한 정중함으로 그녀를 대했으니, 그것이 다른 이들의 노골적인 적의보다 어쩐지 더 베키를 불안하게 만드는 것이었다."]], [["How the Crawleys got the money which was spent upon the entertainments with which they treated the polite world was a mystery which gave rise to some conversation at the time, and probably added zest to these little festivities.", "크롤리 부부가 상류 사회를 대접하던 그 향응에 쏟아부은 돈을 어디서 마련했는가 하는 것은 하나의 수수께끼였고, 당시 적잖은 입방아에 올라, 아마도 이 자그마한 잔치에 흥미를 한층 돋우어 주었을 것이다."], ["Some persons averred that Sir Pitt Crawley gave his brother a handsome allowance; if he did, Becky's power over the Baronet must have been extraordinary indeed, and his character greatly changed in his advanced age.", "어떤 이들은 피트 크롤리 경이 동생에게 두둑한 생활비를 대 준다고 단언했다. 만일 그랬다면, 베키가 준남작에게 휘두른 위력은 정말이지 비상했을 것이고, 그의 성품도 노년에 이르러 크게 달라졌을 것이다."], ["Other parties hinted that it was Becky's habit to levy contributions on all her husband's friends: going to this one in tears with an account that there was an execution in the house; falling on her knees to that one and declaring that the whole family must go to gaol or commit suicide unless such and such a bill could be paid.", "또 다른 이들은 베키가 남편의 친구들 모두에게서 돈을 거두어들이는 버릇이 있다고 넌지시 일렀다. 이 사람에게는 눈물을 흘리며 집에 차압이 들어왔다고 둘러대며 찾아가고, 저 사람에게는 무릎을 꿇고서 이러이러한 어음을 갚지 못하면 온 가족이 감옥에 가거나 자살할 수밖에 없다고 하소연한다는 것이었다."], ["Lord Southdown, it was said, had been induced to give many hundreds through these pathetic representations.", "들리는 말로는 사우스다운 경이 이런 가련한 호소에 넘어가 수백 파운드를 내준 적이 한두 번이 아니라고 했다."], ["Young Feltham, of the --th Dragoons (and son of the firm of Tiler and Feltham, hatters and army accoutrement makers), and whom the Crawleys introduced into fashionable life, was also cited as one of Becky's victims in the pecuniary way.", "제××용기병대의 펠섬 도련님(군모와 군장을 만드는 타일러 앤드 펠섬 상회의 아들이었다)도, 크롤리 부부가 사교계에 끌어들인 인물인데, 금전 면에서 베키의 희생양 가운데 하나로 거론되었다."], ["People declared that she got money from various simply disposed persons, under pretence of getting them confidential appointments under Government.", "사람들은 그녀가 정부의 비밀스러운 자리를 알선해 주겠다는 핑계로, 마음씨 단순한 여러 사람에게서 돈을 우려냈다고 단언했다."], ["Who knows what stories were or were not told of our dear and innocent friend?", "우리의 사랑스럽고 순진한 벗에 대해 무슨 이야기가 떠돌았는지, 또 떠돌지 않았는지 누가 알겠는가?"], ["Certain it is that if she had had all the money which she was said to have begged or borrowed or stolen, she might have capitalized and been honest for life, whereas,--but this is advancing matters.", "다만 확실한 것은, 그녀가 구걸했다거나 빌렸다거나 훔쳤다고 일컬어지는 그 돈을 죄다 가지고 있었다면, 능히 그것을 밑천 삼아 평생 정직하게 살 수도 있었으리라는 점이다. 그런데도 실상은 그렇지 않았으니, 그러나 이는 이야기를 너무 앞질러 가는 것이다."]], [["The truth is, that by economy and good management--by a sparing use of ready money and by paying scarcely anybody--people can manage, for a time at least, to make a great show with very little means: and it is our belief that Becky's much-talked-of parties, which were not, after all was said, very numerous, cost this lady very little more than the wax candles which lighted the walls.", "진실인즉슨, 알뜰한 살림과 빈틈없는 운영으로, 즉 현금을 아껴 쓰고 누구에게도 좀처럼 갚지 않음으로써, 사람은 적어도 한동안은 쥐꼬리만 한 재산으로도 거창한 겉치레를 꾸려 갈 수 있는 법이다. 그리고 우리가 믿기로는, 그토록 입방아에 오르내린 베키의 모임도, 따지고 보면 그리 잦았던 것도 아니지만, 이 부인에게는 그 벽을 환히 밝힌 양초값보다 그리 많은 비용이 들지 않았다."], ["Stillbrook and Queen's Crawley supplied her with game and fruit in abundance.", "스틸브룩과 퀸스 크롤리에서는 그녀에게 사냥한 고기와 과일을 넉넉히 보내 주었다."], ["Lord Steyne's cellars were at her disposal, and that excellent nobleman's famous cooks presided over her little kitchen, or sent by my lord's order the rarest delicacies from their own.", "스테인 경의 술 곳간은 그녀가 마음대로 쓸 수 있었고, 그 훌륭한 귀족의 이름난 요리사들이 그녀의 작은 부엌을 주관하거나, 후작의 분부를 받아 자기네 부엌에서 진귀한 산해진미를 보내 주었다."], ["I protest it is quite shameful in the world to abuse a simple creature, as people of her time abuse Becky, and I warn the public against believing one-tenth of the stories against her.", "단언하건대 세상이 이렇듯 순박한 사람을 두고, 그 시절 사람들이 베키를 두고 그러하듯 헐뜯어 대는 것은 참으로 부끄러운 일이며, 나는 그녀에 대한 이야기를 십분의 일이라도 믿지 말라고 대중에게 경고하는 바이다."], ["If every person is to be banished from society who runs into debt and cannot pay--if we are to be peering into everybody's private life, speculating upon their income, and cutting them if we don't approve of their expenditure--why, what a howling wilderness and intolerable dwelling Vanity Fair would be!", "만일 빚을 지고 갚지 못하는 사람을 죄다 사교계에서 추방해야 한다면, 만일 우리가 모든 사람의 사생활을 들여다보고 그들의 수입을 따져 보며 그들의 씀씀이가 마음에 들지 않으면 인연을 끊어야 한다면, 아니, 그렇다면 허영의 시장은 얼마나 울부짖는 황야요 견디기 힘든 거처가 되겠는가!"], ["Every man's hand would be against his neighbour in this case, my dear sir, and the benefits of civilization would be done away with.", "그렇게 된다면, 친애하는 선생, 너 나 할 것 없이 서로 이웃에게 손을 치켜들고 맞설 것이며, 문명의 혜택은 깡그리 사라지고 말 것이다."], ["We should be quarrelling, abusing, avoiding one another.", "우리는 서로 다투고, 헐뜯고, 피하게 될 것이다."], ["Our houses would become caverns, and we should go in rags because we cared for nobody.", "우리 집은 동굴이 될 것이고, 누구 하나 거들떠보지 않으니 우리는 누더기를 걸치고 다니게 될 것이다."], ["Rents would go down.", "집세는 떨어질 것이다."], ["Parties wouldn't be given any more.", "모임은 더 이상 열리지 않을 것이다."], ["All the tradesmen of the town would be bankrupt.", "온 도시의 상인들이 파산할 것이다."], ["Wine, wax-lights, comestibles, rouge, crinoline-petticoats, diamonds, wigs, Louis-Quatorze gimcracks, and old china, park hacks, and splendid high-stepping carriage horses--all the delights of life, I say,--would go to the deuce, if people did but act upon their silly principles and avoid those whom they dislike and abuse.", "와인이며, 양초며, 식료품이며, 연지며, 크리놀린 페티코트며, 다이아몬드며, 가발이며, 루이 14세풍 자질구레한 장식품이며, 골동 자기며, 공원에서 타는 승용마며, 멋들어지게 발을 높이 차올리는 마차용 말이며—거듭 말하건대 인생의 온갖 즐거움이 죄다 결딴나고 말 것이다. 만일 사람들이 정말로 그 어리석은 원칙대로 행동하여 자기가 싫어하고 헐뜯는 사람들을 피한다면 말이다."], ["Whereas, by a little charity and mutual forbearance, things are made to go on pleasantly enough: we may abuse a man as much as we like, and call him the greatest rascal unhanged--but do we wish to hang him therefore?", "반면에, 약간의 너그러움과 서로 간의 너그러운 인내만 있으면, 만사가 제법 즐겁게 굴러가는 법이다. 우리는 누군가를 얼마든지 헐뜯고, 교수대를 면한 가장 큰 악당이라 욕할 수도 있지만, 그렇다고 그를 목매달기를 바라는가?"], ["No.", "천만에."], ["We shake hands when we meet.", "우리는 만나면 악수를 한다."], ["If his cook is good we forgive him and go and dine with him, and we expect he will do the same by us.", "그 집 요리사 솜씨가 좋으면 우리는 그를 용서하고 그 집에 가서 식사를 하며, 그도 우리에게 똑같이 해 주리라 기대한다."], ["Thus trade flourishes--civilization advances; peace is kept; new dresses are wanted for new assemblies every week; and the last year's vintage of Lafitte will remunerate the honest proprietor who reared it.", "이렇게 장사가 번창하고, 문명이 발전하며, 평화가 유지되고, 매주 새 무도회를 위해 새 옷이 필요해지며, 작년에 빚은 라피트 포도주는 그것을 길러 낸 정직한 주인에게 보답을 안겨 주는 것이다."]], [["At the time whereof we are writing, though the Great George was on the throne and ladies wore gigots and large combs like tortoise-shell shovels in their hair, instead of the simple sleeves and lovely wreaths which are actually in fashion, the manners of the very polite world were not, I take it, essentially different from those of the present day: and their amusements pretty similar.", "우리가 이 글을 쓰는 이 시절, 비록 위대한 조지가 왕좌에 앉아 있었고 숙녀들이 지금 실제로 유행하는 그 단순한 소매와 사랑스러운 화관 대신 양다리 모양 소매를 달고 머리에는 거북딱지로 만든 부삽 같은 큼직한 빗을 꽂고 다니던 시절이긴 했으나, 더없이 고상한 상류 사회의 풍속은, 내가 보기에 오늘날과 본질적으로 다르지 않았다. 그리고 그들의 오락도 자못 비슷했다."], ["To us, from the outside, gazing over the policeman's shoulders at the bewildering beauties as they pass into Court or ball, they may seem beings of unearthly splendour and in the enjoyment of an exquisite happiness by us unattainable.", "바깥에서 경찰관의 어깨너머로, 궁정이나 무도회로 들어가는 그 황홀한 미인들을 바라보는 우리에게는, 그들이 이 세상 것이 아닌 광채를 두른 존재요, 우리로서는 다다를 수 없는 더없이 황홀한 행복을 누리는 존재로 보일지도 모른다."], ["It is to console some of these dissatisfied beings that we are narrating our dear Becky's struggles, and triumphs, and disappointments, of all of which, indeed, as is the case with all persons of merit, she had her share.", "바로 이렇듯 불만에 찬 몇몇 사람을 위로하고자, 우리는 우리의 사랑스러운 베키의 분투와 승리와 좌절을 이야기하는 것이니, 실로 그녀도 다른 모든 잘난 사람들과 마찬가지로 그 분투와 승리와 좌절을 제 몫만큼 겪었던 것이다."]], [["At this time the amiable amusement of acting charades had come among us from France, and was considerably in vogue in this country, enabling the many ladies amongst us who had beauty to display their charms, and the fewer number who had cleverness to exhibit their wit.", "이즈음 무언극 놀이라는 이 사랑스러운 오락이 프랑스에서 우리에게 건너와, 이 나라에서 자못 크게 유행했다. 그 덕에 우리 가운데 미모를 갖춘 많은 숙녀들은 그 매력을 뽐낼 수 있었고, 재기를 갖춘 더 적은 수의 숙녀들은 그 기지를 펼쳐 보일 수 있었다."], ["My Lord Steyne was incited by Becky, who perhaps believed herself endowed with both the above qualifications, to give an entertainment at Gaunt House, which should include some of these little dramas--and we must take leave to introduce the reader to this brilliant reunion, and, with a melancholy welcome too, for it will be among the very last of the fashionable entertainments to which it will be our fortune to conduct him.", "스테인 경은 베키의 부추김을 받아—아마도 베키는 자신이 위의 두 자질을 모두 타고났다고 여겼던 모양이다—곤트 저택에서 이런 자그마한 촌극 몇 가지를 곁들인 여흥을 베풀게 되었으니, 우리는 외람되나마 독자를 이 화려한 모임으로 안내하고자 한다. 다만 서글픈 환영의 마음으로 안내하는 것인데, 이는 우리가 독자를 모시고 갈 행운을 누리게 될 마지막 사교계 여흥 가운데 하나가 될 터이기 때문이다."]], [["A portion of that splendid room, the picture gallery of Gaunt House, was arranged as the charade theatre.", "곤트 저택의 미술관이라는 그 으리으리한 방의 일부가 무언극 무대로 꾸며졌다."], ["It had been so used when George III was king; and a picture of the Marquis of Gaunt is still extant, with his hair in powder and a pink ribbon, in a Roman shape, as it was called, enacting the part of Cato in Mr.", "조지 3세가 왕이던 시절에도 그렇게 쓰인 적이 있었으니, 머리에 분을 뿌리고 분홍 리본을 단 채 이른바 로마식 차림을 한 곤트 후작의 그림이 지금도 전해지는데, 애디슨 씨의 비극 「카토」에서 카토 역을 연기하는 모습이다."], ["Addison's tragedy of that name, performed before their Royal Highnesses the Prince of Wales, the Bishop of Osnaburgh, and Prince William Henry, then children like the actor.", "그 연극은 웨일스 공 전하와 오스나브뤼흐 주교, 그리고 윌리엄 헨리 왕자 앞에서 공연되었는데, 그분들도 당시에는 그 배우와 마찬가지로 어린아이들이었다."], ["One or two of the old properties were drawn out of the garrets, where they had lain ever since, and furbished up anew for the present festivities.", "그 뒤로 줄곧 다락방에 처박혀 있던 한두 가지 옛 소도구를 끌어내어, 이번 잔치를 위해 새로 손질해 두었다."]], [["Young Bedwin Sands, then an elegant dandy and Eastern traveller, was manager of the revels.", "당시 우아한 멋쟁이이자 동방 여행가이던 젊은 베드윈 샌즈가 이 여흥의 진행을 맡았다."], ["An Eastern traveller was somebody in those days, and the adventurous Bedwin, who had published his quarto and passed some months under the tents in the desert, was a personage of no small importance.", "그 시절 동방 여행가란 제법 대단한 인물이었으니, 사절판 책까지 펴내고 사막의 천막 아래서 몇 달을 지낸 적이 있는 이 모험심 강한 베드윈은 결코 만만찮은 거물이었다."], ["In his volume there were several pictures of Sands in various oriental costumes; and he travelled about with a black attendant of most unprepossessing appearance, just like another Brian de Bois Guilbert.", "그의 책에는 갖가지 동방 의상을 차려입은 샌즈의 그림이 여러 장 실려 있었다. 그리고 그는 더없이 험상궂게 생긴 흑인 시종을 데리고 다녔으니, 꼭 또 한 명의 브라이언 드 부아길베르 같았다."], ["Bedwin, his costumes, and black man, were hailed at Gaunt House as very valuable acquisitions.", "베드윈과 그의 의상과 흑인은 곤트 저택에서 더없이 값진 수확으로 환영받았다."]], [["He led off the first charade.", "그가 첫 번째 무언극의 막을 열었다."], ["A Turkish officer with an immense plume of feathers (the Janizaries were supposed to be still in existence, and the tarboosh had not as yet displaced the ancient and majestic head-dress of the true believers) was seen couched on a divan, and making believe to puff at a narghile, in which, however, for the sake of the ladies, only a fragrant pastille was allowed to smoke.", "깃털을 어마어마하게 단 터키 장교(예니체리가 아직 존속하는 것으로 여겨지던 시절이라, 타르부시가 아직 그 진실한 신자들의 유서 깊고 위풍당당한 머리 장식을 밀어내지 못한 때였다)가 긴 의자에 비스듬히 누워, 물담배를 빠는 시늉을 하고 있는 모습이 보였다. 다만 숙녀들을 위해 그 안에서는 향긋한 향환만 피우도록 허락되었다."], ["The Turkish dignitary yawns and expresses signs of weariness and idleness.", "이 터키 고관이 하품을 하며 권태롭고 무료한 기색을 내비친다."], ["He claps his hands and Mesrour the Nubian appears, with bare arms, bangles, yataghans, and every Eastern ornament--gaunt, tall, and hideous.", "그가 손뼉을 치자 누비아인 메스루르가 등장하니, 맨팔에 팔찌를 끼고 언월도를 차고 온갖 동방 장신구를 두른, 깡마르고 키 크고 흉측한 모습이다."], ["He makes a salaam before my lord the Aga.", "그가 아가 나리 앞에 살람 인사를 올린다."]], [["A thrill of terror and delight runs through the assembly.", "전율과 환희가 좌중을 훑고 지나간다."], ["The ladies whisper to one another.", "숙녀들은 서로 귓속말을 주고받는다."], ["The black slave was given to Bedwin Sands by an Egyptian pasha in exchange for three dozen of Maraschino.", "저 흑인 노예는 어느 이집트 파샤가 마라스키노 술 서른여섯 병과 맞바꾸어 베드윈 샌즈에게 준 것이다."], ["He has sewn up ever so many odalisques in sacks and tilted them into the Nile.", "그는 그동안 숱한 후궁들을 자루에 꿰매 넣어 나일강에 던져 버렸다."]], [["\"Bid the slave-merchant enter,\"", "\"노예 상인을 들라 하라.\""], ["says the Turkish voluptuary with a wave of his hand.", "터키의 호색한이 손을 한 번 흔들며 말한다."], ["Mesrour conducts the slave-merchant into my lord's presence; he brings a veiled female with him.", "메스루르가 노예 상인을 후작 나리 앞으로 데려오는데, 그는 베일을 쓴 여인 하나를 함께 데려온다."], ["He removes the veil.", "그가 베일을 벗긴다."], ["A thrill of applause bursts through the house.", "박수갈채의 전율이 온 좌중에 터져 나온다."], ["It is Mrs. Winkworth (she was a Miss Absolom) with the beautiful eyes and hair.", "다름 아닌 윈크워스 부인(처녀 적 이름은 앱설롬 양이었다)으로, 그 아름다운 눈과 머리칼의 소유자였다."], ["She is in a gorgeous oriental costume; the black braided locks are twined with innumerable jewels; her dress is covered over with gold piastres.", "그녀는 화려한 동방 의상을 차려입었으니, 까맣게 땋아 늘인 머리채에는 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 보석이 엮여 있고, 옷에는 금화가 온통 뒤덮여 있다."], ["The odious Mahometan expresses himself charmed by her beauty.", "저 가증스러운 이슬람교도가 그녀의 미모에 홀딱 반했음을 드러낸다."], ["She falls down on her knees and entreats him to restore her to the mountains where she was born, and where her Circassian lover is still deploring the absence of his Zuleikah.", "그녀가 무릎을 꿇고서, 자신이 태어난 산악으로, 자신의 체르케스 연인이 여태 줄레이카가 없는 것을 한탄하고 있는 그곳으로 돌려보내 달라고 그에게 애원한다."], ["No entreaties will move the obdurate Hassan.", "그러나 어떤 애원으로도 완고한 하산의 마음은 움직이지 않는다."], ["He laughs at the notion of the Circassian bridegroom.", "그는 체르케스 신랑이라는 생각에 코웃음을 친다."], ["Zuleikah covers her face with her hands and drops down in an attitude of the most beautiful despair.", "줄레이카는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고는, 더없이 아름다운 절망의 자태로 무너져 내린다."], ["There seems to be no hope for her, when--when the Kislar Aga appears.", "그녀에게는 도무지 희망이 없어 보이는데, 그때—그때 키슬라르 아가가 등장한다."]], [["The Kislar Aga brings a letter from the Sultan.", "키슬라르 아가가 술탄이 보낸 편지를 가져온다."], ["Hassan receives and places on his head the dread firman.", "하산이 그 무시무시한 칙령을 받아 자기 머리에 얹는다."], ["A ghastly terror seizes him, while on the Negro's face (it is Mesrour again in another costume) appears a ghastly joy.", "섬뜩한 공포가 그를 사로잡는 한편, 흑인의 얼굴에는(또다시 다른 의상을 입은 메스루르다) 섬뜩한 기쁨이 떠오른다. \"자비를!"], ["\"Mercy! mercy!\"", "자비를 베푸소서!\""], ["cries the Pasha: while the Kislar Aga, grinning horribly, pulls out--a bow-string.", "파샤가 부르짖는다. 그동안 키슬라르 아가는 무섭게 이를 드러내며 활시위 하나를 꺼낸다."]], [["The curtain draws just as he is going to use that awful weapon.", "그가 그 무시무시한 무기를 막 쓰려는 찰나에 막이 내린다."], ["Hassan from within bawls out, \"First two syllables\"--and Mrs.", "안에서 하산이 큰 소리로 외친다. \"첫 두 음절이오.\""], ["Rawdon Crawley, who is going to act in the charade, comes forward and compliments Mrs. Winkworth on the admirable taste and beauty of her costume.", "그러자 이 무언극에 출연하기로 되어 있는 로던 크롤리 부인이 앞으로 나와, 윈크워스 부인에게 그 의상의 빼어난 안목과 아름다움을 칭찬한다."]], [["The second part of the charade takes place.", "무언극의 두 번째 부분이 시작된다."], ["It is still an Eastern scene.", "여전히 동방의 한 장면이다."], ["Hassan, in another dress, is in an attitude by Zuleikah, who is perfectly reconciled to him.", "다른 옷으로 갈아입은 하산이, 이제는 완전히 마음을 푼 줄레이카 곁에 멋진 자세로 서 있다."], ["The Kislar Aga has become a peaceful black slave.", "키슬라르 아가는 온순한 흑인 노예가 되어 있다."], ["It is sunrise on the desert, and the Turks turn their heads eastwards and bow to the sand.", "사막에 해가 떠오르고, 터키인들이 동쪽으로 고개를 돌려 모래에 절을 한다."], ["As there are no dromedaries at hand, the band facetiously plays \"The Camels are coming.\"", "단봉낙타가 가까이에 없는 까닭에, 악단이 익살스럽게 「낙타가 온다」를 연주한다."], ["An enormous Egyptian head figures in the scene.", "거대한 이집트인 두상이 그 장면에 등장한다."], ["It is a musical one--and, to the surprise of the oriental travellers, sings a comic song, composed by Mr. Wagg.", "그것은 노래하는 두상이라, 동방 여행가들이 놀라워하는 가운데, 왜그 씨가 지은 익살스러운 노래를 부른다."], ["The Eastern voyagers go off dancing, like Papageno and the Moorish King in The Magic Flute.", "동방의 나그네들이 「마술 피리」의 파파게노와 무어인 왕처럼 춤을 추며 퇴장한다."], ["\"Last two syllables,\"", "\"마지막 두 음절이오.\""], ["roars the head.", "두상이 우렁차게 외친다."]], [["The last act opens.", "마지막 막이 열린다."], ["It is a Grecian tent this time.", "이번에는 그리스풍 천막이다."], ["A tall and stalwart man reposes on a couch there.", "키가 크고 건장한 사내가 그 안의 침상에 누워 있다."], ["Above him hang his helmet and shield.", "그의 머리 위에는 투구와 방패가 걸려 있다."], ["There is no need for them now.", "이제는 그것들이 필요 없다."], ["Ilium is down.", "일리움은 함락되었다."], ["Iphigenia is slain.", "이피게네이아는 살해되었다."], ["Cassandra is a prisoner in his outer halls.", "카산드라는 그의 바깥 홀에 포로로 잡혀 있다."], ["The king of men (it is Colonel Crawley, who, indeed, has no notion about the sack of Ilium or the conquest of Cassandra), the anax andron is asleep in his chamber at Argos.", "인간들의 왕(다름 아닌 크롤리 대령으로, 실은 일리움의 약탈에 대해서도 카산드라의 정복에 대해서도 아무것도 모른다), 그 아낙스 안드론이 아르고스의 침실에서 잠들어 있다."], ["A lamp casts the broad shadow of the sleeping warrior flickering on the wall--the sword and shield of Troy glitter in its light.", "등불 하나가 잠든 전사의 널따란 그림자를 벽 위에 일렁이게 비추고, 트로이의 검과 방패가 그 불빛에 번득인다."], ["The band plays the awful music of Don Juan, before the statue enters.", "악단이 석상이 들어오기 전 「돈 후안」의 그 무시무시한 곡을 연주한다."]], [["Aegisthus steals in pale and on tiptoe.", "아이기스토스가 창백한 얼굴로 발끝을 세워 살그머니 들어온다."], ["What is that ghastly face looking out balefully after him from behind the arras?", "휘장 뒤에서 음험하게 그의 뒤를 내다보는 저 섬뜩한 얼굴은 무엇인가?"], ["He raises his dagger to strike the sleeper, who turns in his bed, and opens his broad chest as if for the blow.", "그가 잠든 자를 찌르려고 단검을 치켜드는데, 잠자던 자가 침상에서 몸을 뒤척이며 마치 그 일격을 받겠다는 듯 떡 벌어진 가슴을 드러낸다."], ["He cannot strike the noble slumbering chieftain.", "그는 차마 고귀하게 잠든 그 족장을 찌르지 못한다."], ["Clytemnestra glides swiftly into the room like an apparition--her arms are bare and white--her tawny hair floats down her shoulders--her face is deadly pale--and her eyes are lighted up with a smile so ghastly that people quake as they look at her.", "클리타임네스트라가 유령처럼 재빨리 방 안으로 미끄러져 들어온다. 그녀의 두 팔은 맨살에 새하얗고, 적갈색 머리채가 어깨 위로 흘러내리고, 얼굴은 죽은 듯이 창백하며, 두 눈은 어찌나 섬뜩한 미소로 빛나는지 보는 이들이 부르르 떤다."]], [["A tremor ran through the room.", "전율이 온 좌중을 훑고 지나간다."], ["\"Good God!\"", "\"맙소사!\""], ["somebody said, \"it's Mrs.", "누군가가 말했다."], ["Rawdon Crawley.\"", "\"로던 크롤리 부인이잖아.\""]], [["Scornfully she snatches the dagger out of Aegisthus's hand and advances to the bed.", "그녀는 경멸하는 태도로 아이기스토스의 손에서 단검을 낚아채 침상으로 다가간다."], ["You see it shining over her head in the glimmer of the lamp, and--and the lamp goes out, with a groan, and all is dark.", "그 단검이 등불의 희미한 빛 속에서 그녀의 머리 위로 번득이는 것이 보이는데—그러다 등불이 신음 소리와 함께 꺼지고, 사방이 캄캄해진다."]], [["The darkness and the scene frightened people.", "그 어둠과 그 장면이 사람들을 겁에 질리게 했다."], ["Rebecca performed her part so well, and with such ghastly truth, that the spectators were all dumb, until, with a burst, all the lamps of the hall blazed out again, when everybody began to shout applause.", "리베카가 자기 역을 어찌나 잘, 그리고 어찌나 섬뜩하리만치 실감 나게 해냈던지, 구경꾼들은 모두 말문이 막혀 버렸다. 그러다 와락 홀의 등불이 죄다 다시 환히 타오르자, 비로소 모두가 갈채를 보내기 시작했다."], ["\"Brava!", "\"브라바!"], ["brava!\"", "브라바!\""], ["old Steyne's strident voice was heard roaring over all the rest.", "늙은 스테인의 새된 목소리가 다른 모든 소리를 누르고 우렁차게 울려 퍼졌다."], ["\"By--, she'd do it too,\" he said between his teeth.", "\"맹세코, 저 여자라면 정말 해치우고도 남을걸.\""], ["The performers were called by the whole house, which sounded with cries of \"Manager!", "그가 이를 악물고 중얼거렸다. 출연자들이 온 좌중의 부름을 받았으니, 홀에는 \"진행자!"], ["Clytemnestra!\"", "클리타임네스트라!\""], ["Agamemnon could not be got to show in his classical tunic, but stood in the background with Aegisthus and others of the performers of the little play.", "하는 외침이 울려 퍼졌다. 아가멤논은 그 고전풍 짧은 옷차림으로 도무지 모습을 드러내려 하지 않고, 아이기스토스며 그 짧은 연극의 다른 출연자들과 함께 뒤편에 서 있었다."], ["Mr. Bedwin Sands led on Zuleikah and Clytemnestra.", "베드윈 샌즈 씨가 줄레이카와 클리타임네스트라를 데리고 나왔다."], ["A great personage insisted on being presented to the charming Clytemnestra.", "어느 고귀한 분이 매혹적인 클리타임네스트라에게 자신을 소개해 달라고 우겼다. \"어허?"], ["\"Heigh ha? Run him through the body.", "칼로 푹 찔러 그 몸뚱이를 꿰뚫겠다는 거지."], ["Marry somebody else, hay?\"", "그러고 나서 딴 남자랑 결혼하겠다, 응?\""], ["was the apposite remark made by His Royal Highness.", "전하께서 이렇게 딱 들어맞는 말씀을 하셨다."]], [["\"Mrs. Rawdon Crawley was quite killing in the part,\"", "\"로던 크롤리 부인은 그 역에 아주 기막히게 어울리더군요.\""], ["said Lord Steyne.", "스테인 경이 말했다."], ["Becky laughed, gay and saucy looking, and swept the prettiest little curtsey ever seen.", "베키는 명랑하고 발랄한 표정으로 웃으며, 여태껏 본 적 없는 가장 어여쁜 무릎 인사로 좌중을 휩쓸었다."]], [["Servants brought in salvers covered with numerous cool dainties, and the performers disappeared to get ready for the second charade-tableau.", "하인들이 갖가지 시원한 진미를 담은 큰 쟁반을 들여왔고, 출연자들은 두 번째 무언극 활인화를 준비하러 사라졌다."]], [["The three syllables of this charade were to be depicted in pantomime, and the performance took place in the following wise:", "이 무언극의 세 음절은 무언의 몸짓으로 묘사하기로 되어 있었고, 공연은 다음과 같은 식으로 펼쳐졌다."]], [["First syllable.", "첫째 음절."], ["Colonel Rawdon Crawley, C.B., with a slouched hat and a staff, a great-coat, and a lantern borrowed from the stables, passed across the stage bawling out, as if warning the inhabitants of the hour.", "C.B. 훈장을 단 로던 크롤리 대령이 챙이 늘어진 모자를 쓰고 지팡이와 외투를 갖춘 채, 마구간에서 빌려 온 등불을 들고 무대를 가로질러 가며, 주민들에게 시각을 알리듯 큰 소리로 외친다."], ["In the lower window are seen two bagmen playing apparently at the game of cribbage, over which they yawn much.", "아래층 창문 안으로는 행상인 둘이 크리비지 놀이를 하는 듯 보이는데, 그 위에서 연신 하품을 해 댄다."], ["To them enters one looking like Boots (the Honourable G.", "그들에게 구두닦이 보츠처럼 보이는 자(G."], ["Ringwood), which character the young gentleman performed to perfection, and divests them of their lower coverings; and presently Chambermaid (the Right Honourable Lord Southdown) with two candlesticks, and a warming-pan.", "링우드 각하)가 들어오니, 이 도련님이 그 역할을 완벽하게 해내며, 그들의 아랫도리를 벗겨 간다. 이윽고 객실 하녀(사우스다운 경 각하)가 촛대 둘과 발 데우는 냄비를 들고 등장한다."], ["She ascends to the upper apartment and warms the bed.", "그녀가 위층 방으로 올라가 침대를 데운다."], ["She uses the warming-pan as a weapon wherewith she wards off the attention of the bagmen.", "그녀는 그 발 데우는 냄비를 무기 삼아 행상인들이 집적거리는 것을 막아 낸다."], ["She exits.", "그녀가 퇴장한다."], ["They put on their night-caps and pull down the blinds.", "그들은 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자를 쓰고 차양을 내린다."], ["Boots comes out and closes the shutters of the ground-floor chamber.", "보츠가 나와 일 층 방의 덧문을 닫는다."], ["You hear him bolting and chaining the door within.", "안에서 그가 문에 빗장을 지르고 쇠사슬을 거는 소리가 들린다."], ["All the lights go out.", "모든 불이 꺼진다."], ["The music plays Dormez, dormez, chers Amours.", "악단이 「잠드소서, 잠드소서, 사랑하는 이들이여」를 연주한다."], ["A voice from behind the curtain says, \"First syllable.\"", "막 뒤에서 한 목소리가 말한다. \"첫째 음절이오.\""]], [["Second syllable.", "둘째 음절."], ["The lamps are lighted up all of a sudden.", "갑자기 등불이 모두 환하게 켜진다."], ["The music plays the old air from John of Paris, Ah quel plaisir d'etre en voyage.", "악단이 「파리의 요한」에 나오는 그 옛 가락 「아, 여행하는 즐거움이여」를 연주한다."], ["It is the same scene.", "같은 장면이다."], ["Between the first and second floors of the house represented, you behold a sign on which the Steyne arms are painted.", "그려진 그 집의 일 층과 이 층 사이에는, 스테인 가문의 문장이 그려진 간판이 보인다."], ["All the bells are ringing all over the house.", "집 안 곳곳에서 종이 울려 댄다."], ["In the lower apartment you see a man with a long slip of paper presenting it to another, who shakes his fists, threatens and vows that it is monstrous.", "아래층 방에서는 한 남자가 긴 종잇조각을 또 다른 남자에게 내미는 것이 보이는데, 그 남자는 주먹을 흔들며 으르고 이건 말도 안 된다고 펄펄 뛴다."], ["\"Ostler, bring round my gig,\"", "\"마부 양반, 내 이륜마차를 돌려 오게.\""], ["cries another at the door.", "또 다른 남자가 문간에서 외친다."], ["He chucks Chambermaid (the Right Honourable Lord Southdown) under the chin; she seems to deplore his absence, as Calypso did that of that other eminent traveller Ulysses.", "그가 객실 하녀(사우스다운 경 각하)의 턱을 쓰다듬는다. 그녀는 칼립소가 또 다른 그 이름난 여행가 율리시스의 부재를 한탄했듯, 그가 떠나는 것을 애석해하는 듯하다."], ["Boots (the Honourable G.", "보츠(G."], ["Ringwood) passes with a wooden box, containing silver flagons, and cries \"Pots\"", "링우드 각하)가 은제 큰 술병이 든 나무 상자를 들고 지나가며 \"주전자요\""], ["with such exquisite humour and naturalness that the whole house rings with applause, and a bouquet is thrown to him.", "하고 어찌나 절묘하게 익살스럽고 자연스럽게 외쳐 대는지, 온 좌중이 박수갈채로 울리고 그에게 꽃다발이 던져진다."], ["Crack, crack, crack, go the whips.", "채찍이 짝, 짝, 짝 소리를 낸다."], ["Landlord, chambermaid, waiter rush to the door, but just as some distinguished guest is arriving, the curtains close, and the invisible theatrical manager cries out \"Second syllable.\"", "주인장과 객실 하녀와 급사가 문간으로 달려가는데, 어느 귀하신 손님이 막 당도하려는 바로 그 순간 막이 닫히고, 보이지 않는 연극 진행자가 외친다. \"둘째 음절이오.\""]], [["\"I think it must be 'Hotel,'\"", "\"내 생각엔 '호텔'인 것 같은데.\""], ["says Captain Grigg of the Life Guards; there is a general laugh at the Captain's cleverness.", "근위 기병대의 그리그 대위가 말한다. 대위의 영민함에 좌중에 한바탕 웃음이 인다."], ["He is not very far from the mark.", "그는 정답에서 그리 멀지 않다."]], [["While the third syllable is in preparation, the band begins a nautical medley--\"All in the Downs,\" \"Cease Rude Boreas,\"", "셋째 음절이 준비되는 동안, 악단이 뱃노래 모음곡을 연주하기 시작한다."], ["\"Rule Britannia,\" \"In the Bay of Biscay O!\"--some maritime event is about to take place.", "「다운스의 모든 이」, 「거센 북풍이여 그쳐라」, 「룰 브리타니아」, 「오, 비스케이만에서!」—무언가 바다와 관련된 사건이 벌어지려는 참이다."], ["A bell is heard ringing as the curtain draws aside.", "막이 옆으로 젖혀지면서 종소리가 들린다."], ["\"Now, gents, for the shore!\"", "\"자, 여러분, 뭍으로 갑시다!\""], ["a voice exclaims.", "한 목소리가 외친다."], ["People take leave of each other.", "사람들이 서로 작별 인사를 나눈다."], ["They point anxiously as if towards the clouds, which are represented by a dark curtain, and they nod their heads in fear.", "그들은 검은 막으로 표현된 구름을 향하듯 불안하게 손가락질을 하며, 두려운 듯 고개를 끄덕인다."], ["Lady Squeams (the Right Honourable Lord Southdown), her lap-dog, her bags, reticules, and husband sit down, and cling hold of some ropes.", "스퀴엄스 부인(사우스다운 경 각하)이 자기 애완견과 가방들과 손가방들과 남편과 함께 자리에 앉아, 밧줄을 붙들고 매달린다."], ["It is evidently a ship.", "분명 배다."]], [["The Captain (Colonel Crawley, C.B.), with a cocked hat and a telescope, comes in, holding his hat on his head, and looks out; his coat tails fly about as if in the wind.", "선장(C.B. 훈장의 크롤리 대령)이 삼각모를 쓰고 망원경을 든 채, 모자를 손으로 머리에 누르며 들어와 밖을 살핀다."], ["When he leaves go of his hat to use his telescope, his hat flies off, with immense applause.", "그의 외투 자락이 바람에 날리듯 펄럭인다. 그가 망원경을 쓰려고 모자에서 손을 떼자, 그의 모자가 날아가 버려 어마어마한 갈채가 쏟아진다."], ["It is blowing fresh.", "바람이 거세게 분다."], ["The music rises and whistles louder and louder; the mariners go across the stage staggering, as if the ship was in severe motion.", "음악이 점점 높아지며 점점 더 크게 휘파람 소리를 내고, 선원들이 마치 배가 심하게 요동치는 듯 비틀거리며 무대를 가로지른다. 사환(G."], ["The Steward (the Honourable G. Ringwood) passes reeling by, holding six basins.", "링우드 각하)이 대야 여섯 개를 든 채 비틀비틀 지나간다."], ["He puts one rapidly by Lord Squeams--Lady Squeams, giving a pinch to her dog, which begins to howl piteously, puts her pocket-handkerchief to her face, and rushes away as for the cabin.", "그가 그중 하나를 잽싸게 스퀴엄스 경 곁에 놓는다. 스퀴엄스 부인은 자기 개를 꼬집어 그 개가 가련하게 울부짖기 시작하자, 손수건을 얼굴에 갖다 대고 선실로 가듯 황급히 달려 나간다."], ["The music rises up to the wildest pitch of stormy excitement, and the third syllable is concluded.", "음악이 폭풍처럼 격앙된 가장 격렬한 절정으로 치솟고, 셋째 음절이 막을 내린다."]], [["There was a little ballet, \"Le Rossignol,\" in which Montessu and Noblet used to be famous in those days, and which Mr.", "「르 로시뇰」이라는 짤막한 발레가 있었으니, 그 시절 몽테쉬와 노블레가 이것으로 이름을 떨치곤 했는데, 왜그 씨가 이를 영국 무대에 오페라로 옮겨 와, 솜씨 좋은 작사가이던 그가 그 발레의 어여쁜 가락에 자기 노랫말을 붙였다."], ["Wagg transferred to the English stage as an opera, putting his verse, of which he was a skilful writer, to the pretty airs of the ballet. It was dressed in old French costume, and little Lord Southdown now appeared admirably attired in the disguise of an old woman hobbling about the stage with a faultless crooked stick.", "그것은 옛 프랑스풍 의상을 차려입고 공연되었으니, 이제 자그마한 사우스다운 경이 흠잡을 데 없이 구부정한 지팡이를 짚고 무대를 절뚝거리며 돌아다니는 노파로 감쪽같이 분장하여 등장했다."]], [["Trills of melody were heard behind the scenes, and gurgling from a sweet pasteboard cottage covered with roses and trellis work.", "낭랑한 가락이 무대 뒤에서 들려오니, 장미와 격자 세공으로 뒤덮인 어여쁜 판지 오두막에서 흘러나온 것이었다."], ["\"Philomele, Philomele,\"", "\"필로멜, 필로멜!\""], ["cries the old woman, and Philomele comes out.", "노파가 외치자, 필로멜이 나온다."]], [["More applause--it is Mrs.", "또다시 갈채가 터진다."], ["Rawdon Crawley in powder and patches, the most ravissante little Marquise in the world.", "다름 아닌 로던 크롤리 부인으로, 머리에 분을 뿌리고 애교점을 찍은, 이 세상에서 가장 매혹적인 자그마한 후작 부인이다."]], [["She comes in laughing, humming, and frisks about the stage with all the innocence of theatrical youth--she makes a curtsey.", "그녀는 웃으며 콧노래를 흥얼거리며 들어와, 연극 속 젊음의 온갖 천진함을 담아 무대 위를 깡충깡충 뛰어다닌다. 그녀가 무릎 인사를 한다."], ["Mamma says \"Why, child, you are always laughing and singing,\"", "엄마가 말한다. \"아니, 얘야, 너는 어쩌면 늘 웃고 노래만 부르는구나.\""], ["and away she goes, with--", "그러자 그녀가 이런 노래와 함께 뛰쳐나간다."]], [["# THE ROSE UPON MY BALCONY", "# THE ROSE UPON MY BALCONY"]], [["The rose upon my balcony the morning air perfuming Was leafless all the winter time and pining for the spring; You ask me why her breath is sweet and why her cheek is blooming, It is because the sun is out and birds begin to sing.", "내 발코니의 장미\n내 발코니의 장미, 아침 공기를 향기롭게 적시는 그 꽃은 겨우내 잎 하나 없이 봄을 애타게 그리워했지요. 그대는 묻네요, 어찌하여 그 숨결이 달콤하고 그 뺨이 활짝 피었는지를. 그것은 해가 떠오르고 새들이 노래하기 시작한 까닭이랍니다."]], [["The nightingale, whose melody is through the greenwood ringing, Was silent when the boughs were bare and winds were blowing keen: And if, Mamma, you ask of me the reason of his singing, It is because the sun is out and all the leaves are green.", "푸른 숲을 가로질러 그 가락이 울려 퍼지는 저 나이팅게일은 가지가 헐벗고 바람이 매섭게 불 적엔 말없이 잠잠했지요. 엄마, 그가 노래하는 까닭을 제게 물으신다면, 그것은 해가 떠오르고 온 잎새가 푸르른 까닭이랍니다."]], [["Thus each performs his part, Mamma, the birds have found their voices, The blowing rose a flush, Mamma, her bonny cheek to dye; And there's sunshine in my heart, Mamma, which wakens and rejoices, And so I sing and blush, Mamma, and that's the reason why.", "이렇듯 저마다 제 몫을 다하지요, 엄마, 새들은 제 목소리를 찾았고, 활짝 핀 장미는, 엄마, 그 고운 뺨을 물들일 홍조를 얻었답니다. 그리고 제 마음속에도, 엄마, 햇살이 있어 깨어나 기뻐하니, 그래서 저는 노래하고 얼굴을 붉히지요, 엄마, 바로 그것이 그 까닭이랍니다."]], [["During the intervals of the stanzas of this ditty, the good-natured personage addressed as Mamma by the singer, and whose large whiskers appeared under her cap, seemed very anxious to exhibit her maternal affection by embracing the innocent creature who performed the daughter's part.", "이 노래의 절과 절 사이사이에, 노래하는 이에게서 엄마라고 불리는 그 마음씨 좋은 인물은—그 모자 아래로 커다란 구레나룻이 비어져 나와 있었는데—딸 역을 맡은 그 천진한 존재를 끌어안아 자기 모정을 한껏 드러내 보이고 싶어 안달이 난 듯했다."], ["Every caress was received with loud acclamations of laughter by the sympathizing audience.", "그 다정한 손길 하나하나가 공감하는 관객들의 요란한 웃음 갈채로 맞아들여졌다."], ["At its conclusion (while the music was performing a symphony as if ever so many birds were warbling) the whole house was unanimous for an encore: and applause and bouquets without end were showered upon the Nightingale of the evening.", "노래가 끝나자(악단이 마치 무수한 새들이 지저귀듯 교향곡을 연주하는 동안) 온 좌중이 한목소리로 앙코르를 외쳤다. 그리고 그날 저녁의 나이팅게일에게는 갈채와 끝없는 꽃다발이 쏟아졌다."], ["Lord Steyne's voice of applause was loudest of all.", "스테인 경의 갈채하는 목소리가 그 모든 것 가운데 가장 우렁찼다."], ["Becky, the nightingale, took the flowers which he threw to her and pressed them to her heart with the air of a consummate comedian.", "나이팅게일인 베키는 그가 던져 준 꽃을 받아, 노련한 희극 배우다운 태도로 그것을 가슴에 꼭 껴안았다."], ["Lord Steyne was frantic with delight.", "스테인 경은 기쁨에 미칠 지경이었다."], ["His guests' enthusiasm harmonized with his own.", "손님들의 열광이 그 자신의 열광과 어우러졌다."], ["Where was the beautiful black-eyed Houri whose appearance in the first charade had caused such delight?", "첫 번째 무언극에서 그 등장만으로 그토록 큰 환희를 자아냈던, 그 아름답고 검은 눈의 천국 미녀는 어디로 갔는가?"], ["She was twice as handsome as Becky, but the brilliancy of the latter had quite eclipsed her.", "그녀는 베키보다 갑절은 어여뻤건만, 베키의 광채가 그녀를 완전히 가려 버린 것이었다."], ["All voices were for her.", "모든 목소리가 그녀를 향했다."], ["Stephens, Caradori, Ronzi de Begnis, people compared her to one or the other, and agreed with good reason, very likely, that had she been an actress none on the stage could have surpassed her.", "스티븐스, 카라도리, 론치 데 베니스—사람들은 그녀를 이 사람 저 사람에 견주어 보았고, 어쩌면 합당한 근거로, 그녀가 만일 여배우였다면 무대 위 그 누구도 그녀를 능가하지 못했으리라는 데 의견을 같이했다."], ["She had reached her culmination: her voice rose trilling and bright over the storm of applause, and soared as high and joyful as her triumph.", "그녀는 자기 절정에 다다른 것이었다. 그녀의 목소리는 갈채의 폭풍 위로 낭랑하고 밝게 솟아올라, 그녀의 승리만큼이나 드높고 즐겁게 날아올랐다."], ["There was a ball after the dramatic entertainments, and everybody pressed round Becky as the great point of attraction of the evening.", "연극 여흥이 끝난 뒤에는 무도회가 있었고, 모두가 그날 저녁의 가장 큰 관심의 표적인 베키 주위로 몰려들었다."], ["The Royal Personage declared with an oath that she was perfection, and engaged her again and again in conversation.", "그 왕족 인사는 그녀가 완벽 그 자체라고 맹세코 단언하며, 거듭거듭 그녀와 이야기를 나누었다."], ["Little Becky's soul swelled with pride and delight at these honours; she saw fortune, fame, fashion before her.", "이런 영예에 자그마한 베키의 영혼은 자긍심과 환희로 부풀어 올랐다. 그녀는 행운과 명성과 영화가 자기 앞에 펼쳐진 것을 보았다."], ["Lord Steyne was her slave, followed her everywhere, and scarcely spoke to any one in the room beside, and paid her the most marked compliments and attention.", "스테인 경은 그녀의 노예가 되어, 어디든 그녀를 따라다니며 그 방에서 그녀 말고는 거의 아무에게도 말을 건네지 않았고, 더없이 눈에 띄게 그녀에게 찬사와 관심을 바쳤다."], ["She still appeared in her Marquise costume and danced a minuet with Monsieur de Truffigny, Monsieur Le Duc de la Jabotiere's attache; and the Duke, who had all the traditions of the ancient court, pronounced that Madame Crawley was worthy to have been a pupil of Vestris, or to have figured at Versailles.", "그녀는 여전히 후작 부인 의상 차림으로, 자보티에르 공작의 부속 직원인 트뤼피니 씨와 미뉴에트를 추었다. 그리고 옛 궁정의 온갖 전통을 다 익힌 그 공작은, 크롤리 부인이 베스트리스의 제자였어도 손색이 없을 만하며 베르사유에서 한자리를 차지했어도 좋았으리라고 단언했다."], ["Only a feeling of dignity, the gout, and the strongest sense of duty and personal sacrifice prevented his Excellency from dancing with her himself, and he declared in public that a lady who could talk and dance like Mrs.", "다만 위엄을 지켜야 한다는 마음과 통풍, 그리고 더없이 강한 의무감과 자기희생의 정신만 아니었더라면, 각하께서는 친히 그녀와 춤을 추었을 것이다. 그리고 그는 크롤리 부인처럼 말하고 춤출 줄 아는 숙녀라면 유럽 어느 궁정의 대사 부인이 되어도 손색이 없으리라고 사람들 앞에서 단언했다."], ["Rawdon was fit to be ambassadress at any court in Europe. He was only consoled when he heard that she was half a Frenchwoman by birth.", "그가 위안을 얻은 것은 그녀가 출생으로 보아 반은 프랑스 여인이라는 말을 듣고서야였다."], ["\"None but a compatriot,\"", "\"동포가 아니고서야,\""], ["his Excellency declared, \"could have performed that majestic dance in such a way.\"", "각하께서 단언하기를, \"그토록 위엄 있는 춤을 그런 식으로 추어 낼 수는 없는 노릇이지요.\""]], [["Then she figured in a waltz with Monsieur de Klingenspohr, the Prince of Peterwaradin's cousin and attache.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 페테르바라딘 대공의 사촌이자 부속 직원인 클링겐슈포어 씨와 함께 왈츠를 추었다."], ["The delighted Prince, having less retenue than his French diplomatic colleague, insisted upon taking a turn with the charming creature, and twirled round the ball-room with her, scattering the diamonds out of his boot-tassels and hussar jacket until his Highness was fairly out of breath.", "기뻐 어쩔 줄 모르던 대공은, 프랑스인 외교 동료보다 절제력이 부족했던지라, 그 매혹적인 여인과 한바탕 춤을 추겠다고 우기더니, 그녀와 함께 무도회장을 빙글빙글 돌며 부츠 술과 경기병 재킷에서 다이아몬드를 흩뿌려 댔으니, 마침내 전하께서는 그야말로 숨이 턱에 차고 말았다."], ["Papoosh Pasha himself would have liked to dance with her if that amusement had been the custom of his country.", "파푸시 파샤 본인도, 만일 춤이라는 것이 자기 나라의 풍습이었더라면 그녀와 춤추고 싶어 했을 것이다."], ["The company made a circle round her and applauded as wildly as if she had been a Noblet or a Taglioni.", "좌중이 그녀를 둘러싸고 원을 그리며, 마치 그녀가 노블레나 탈리오니라도 되는 양 미친 듯이 갈채를 보냈다."], ["Everybody was in ecstacy; and Becky too, you may be sure.", "모두가 황홀경에 빠졌으니, 베키 역시 그러했음은 더 말할 나위가 없다."], ["She passed by Lady Stunnington with a look of scorn.", "그녀는 경멸하는 눈길로 스터닝턴 부인 곁을 지나쳤다."], ["She patronized Lady Gaunt and her astonished and mortified sister-in-law--she ecrased all rival charmers.", "그녀는 곤트 부인과 그 어리둥절하고 굴욕에 찬 시누이에게 짐짓 너그러운 태도를 베풀었다. 그녀는 경쟁하는 모든 미녀들을 짓뭉개 버렸다."], ["As for poor Mrs. Winkworth, and her long hair and great eyes, which had made such an effect at the commencement of the evening--where was she now?", "가엾은 윈크워스 부인으로 말하자면, 그날 저녁 첫머리에 그토록 큰 반향을 일으켰던 그 긴 머리채와 큰 눈을 가진 그녀는 이제 어디 있는가?"], ["Nowhere in the race.", "이 경주에서는 어디에도 없었다."], ["She might tear her long hair and cry her great eyes out, but there was not a person to heed or to deplore the discomfiture.", "그녀는 그 긴 머리채를 쥐어뜯고 그 큰 눈에서 눈물을 쏟을 수도 있었으련만, 그 낭패를 거들떠보거나 애석해할 사람은 하나도 없었다."]], [["The greatest triumph of all was at supper time.", "무엇보다 가장 큰 승리는 야식 시간에 찾아왔다."], ["She was placed at the grand exclusive table with his Royal Highness the exalted personage before mentioned, and the rest of the great guests.", "그녀는 앞서 말한 그 고귀한 인물인 왕족 전하와 그 밖의 귀한 손님들과 더불어, 더없이 특별한 상석에 자리를 잡았다."], ["She was served on gold plate.", "그녀는 금접시에 음식을 받았다."], ["She might have had pearls melted into her champagne if she liked--another Cleopatra--and the potentate of Peterwaradin would have given half the brilliants off his jacket for a kind glance from those dazzling eyes.", "만일 그녀가 원했다면 또 한 명의 클레오파트라처럼 진주를 녹여 자기 샴페인에 타 마실 수도 있었을 것이며, 페테르바라딘의 그 군주는 저 눈부신 눈에서 다정한 눈길 한 번을 받기 위해서라면 자기 재킷에 달린 보석의 절반이라도 내주었을 것이다."], ["Jabotiere wrote home about her to his government.", "자보티에르는 본국 정부에 그녀에 대한 보고문을 써 보냈다."], ["The ladies at the other tables, who supped off mere silver and marked Lord Steyne's constant attention to her, vowed it was a monstrous infatuation, a gross insult to ladies of rank.", "다른 식탁에서 한낱 은접시에 야식을 들며 스테인 경이 그녀에게 끊임없이 관심을 쏟는 것을 지켜보던 숙녀들은, 그것이야말로 괴이쩍은 홀림이요, 지체 높은 숙녀들에 대한 심한 모욕이라고 입을 모았다."], ["If sarcasm could have killed, Lady Stunnington would have slain her on the spot.", "만일 비아냥으로 사람을 죽일 수 있었다면, 스터닝턴 부인은 그 자리에서 당장 그녀를 죽여 버렸을 것이다."]], [["Rawdon Crawley was scared at these triumphs.", "로던 크롤리는 이런 승리들에 겁이 났다."], ["They seemed to separate his wife farther than ever from him somehow.", "그것들은 어쩐지 그 어느 때보다도 더 자기 아내를 자기에게서 멀리 떼어 놓는 것만 같았다."], ["He thought with a feeling very like pain how immeasurably she was his superior.", "그는 그녀가 자기보다 얼마나 헤아릴 수 없이 뛰어난 사람인지를 생각하니, 고통에 가까운 심정이 되었다."]], [["When the hour of departure came, a crowd of young men followed her to her carriage, for which the people without bawled, the cry being caught up by the link-men who were stationed outside the tall gates of Gaunt House, congratulating each person who issued from the gate and hoping his Lordship had enjoyed this noble party.", "떠날 시간이 되자, 한 무리의 젊은이들이 그녀를 따라 그녀의 마차까지 갔으니, 그 마차를 두고 밖에서 사람들이 큰 소리로 불러 댔고, 그 외침을 곤트 저택의 키 높은 대문 밖에 진을 친 횃불 든 사내들이 받아 이어 갔다. 그들은 대문에서 나오는 사람마다 축하의 말을 건네며, 그 나리께서 이 고귀한 모임을 즐거이 누리셨기를 바란다고 했다."]], [["Mrs. Rawdon Crawley's carriage, coming up to the gate after due shouting, rattled into the illuminated court-yard and drove up to the covered way.", "한바탕 큰 소리로 부른 끝에 대문으로 다가온 로던 크롤리 부인의 마차가 환히 밝혀진 안마당으로 덜컹거리며 들어와, 지붕 덮인 통로 앞에 와 멈춰 섰다."], ["Rawdon put his wife into the carriage, which drove off.", "로던이 아내를 마차에 태웠고, 마차는 떠나갔다."], ["Mr. Wenham had proposed to him to walk home, and offered the Colonel the refreshment of a cigar.", "웬엄 씨가 그에게 걸어서 집에 가자고 청하며, 대령에게 시가 한 대로 입가심을 하자고 권했다."]], [["They lighted their cigars by the lamp of one of the many link-boys outside, and Rawdon walked on with his friend Wenham.", "그들은 밖에 늘어선 그 많은 횃불 든 소년들 가운데 하나의 등불에 시가를 붙였고, 로던은 벗인 웬엄과 더불어 걸어갔다. 두 사람이 무리에서 떨어져 나와 그 두 신사의 뒤를 따랐다."], ["Two persons separated from the crowd and followed the two gentlemen; and when they had walked down Gaunt Square a few score of paces, one of the men came up and, touching Rawdon on the shoulder, said, \"Beg your pardon, Colonel, I vish to speak to you most particular.\"", "그리고 그들이 곤트 광장을 몇십 걸음쯤 걸어 내려갔을 때, 그 사내들 가운데 하나가 다가와 로던의 어깨를 툭 치며 말했다. \"실례합니다, 대령님, 긴히 드릴 말씀이 있습니다요.\""], ["This gentleman's acquaintance gave a loud whistle as the latter spoke, at which signal a cab came clattering up from those stationed at the gate of Gaunt House--and the aide-de-camp ran round and placed himself in front of Colonel Crawley.", "이 사내가 말하는 동안 그 일행이 큰 소리로 휘파람을 불자, 그 신호에 곤트 저택 대문 앞에 늘어서 있던 마차들 가운데 한 대가 덜컹거리며 달려왔다. 그러자 그 부관이 빙 돌아 크롤리 대령 앞을 가로막아 섰다."]], [["That gallant officer at once knew what had befallen him.", "그 용감한 장교는 자신에게 무슨 일이 닥쳤는지 단번에 알아차렸다."], ["He was in the hands of the bailiffs.", "그는 집행관들의 손아귀에 들어간 것이었다."], ["He started back, falling against the man who had first touched him.", "그는 흠칫 물러서다가, 맨 처음 자기를 건드렸던 그 사내에게 부딪혔다."]], [["\"We're three on us--it's no use bolting,\"", "\"우리는 셋이올시다요, 달아나 봤자 소용없습니다요.\""], ["the man behind said.", "뒤에 있던 사내가 말했다."]], [["\"It's you, Moss, is it?\"", "\"자네 모스로군, 그렇지?\""], ["said the Colonel, who appeared to know his interlocutor.", "대령이 말했다. 그는 상대를 아는 모양이었다."], ["\"How much is it?\"", "\"얼마인가?\""]], [["\"Only a small thing,\"", "\"별것 아닙니다요.\""], ["whispered Mr.", "챈서리 레인 커시터 거리에 사는, 미들섹스 주장관의 보조 집행관인 모스 씨가 속삭였다."], ["Moss, of Cursitor Street, Chancery Lane, and assistant officer to the Sheriff of Middlesex--\"One hundred and sixty-six, six and eight-pence, at the suit of Mr. Nathan.\"", "\"백육십육 파운드 육 실링 팔 펜스, 네이선 씨가 거신 소송입니다요.\""]], [["\"Lend me a hundred, Wenham, for God's sake,\"", "\"백 파운드만 좀 빌려주게, 웬엄, 제발 부탁이네.\""], ["poor Rawdon said--\"I've got seventy at home.\"", "가엾은 로던이 말했다. \"집에 칠십 파운드가 있으니.\""]], [["\"I've not got ten pounds in the world,\" said poor Mr.", "\"내겐 이 세상에 십 파운드도 없다네.\""], ["Wenham--\"Good night, my dear fellow.\"", "가엾은 웬엄 씨가 말했다. \"잘 가게, 이 사람아.\""]], [["\"Good night,\"", "\"잘 가게.\""], ["said Rawdon ruefully.", "로던이 침울하게 말했다."], ["And Wenham walked away--and Rawdon Crawley finished his cigar as the cab drove under Temple Bar.", "그리고 웬엄은 걸어가 버렸다. 그리고 로던 크롤리는 마차가 템플 바 아래로 달려 들어가는 동안 시가를 마저 다 피웠다."]], [["In Which Lord Steyne Shows Himself in a Most Amiable Light\nWhen Lord Steyne was benevolently disposed, he did nothing by halves, and his kindness towards the Crawley family did the greatest honour to his benevolent discrimination.", "스타인 경은 일단 자비를 베풀 마음이 들면 결코 어중간하게 하는 법이 없었으니, 크롤리 가족을 향한 그 친절은 그의 자비로운 안목에 더없는 영예를 안겨 주는 것이었다. 그분은 호의를 어린 로던에게까지 넓혔다."], ["His lordship extended his good-will to little Rawdon: he pointed out to the boy's parents the necessity of sending him to a public school, that he was of an age now when emulation, the first principles of the Latin language, pugilistic exercises, and the society of his fellow-boys would be of the greatest benefit to the boy.", "그는 아이의 부모에게, 이 아이를 퍼블릭 스쿨에 보낼 필요가 있다고 일러 주었다. 이제 아이가, 경쟁심이며 라틴어의 기초며 권투 단련이며 또래 친구들과의 교제 따위가 더없이 큰 유익이 될 나이가 되었다는 것이었다."], ["His father objected that he was not rich enough to send the child to a good public school; his mother that Briggs was a capital mistress for him, and had brought him on (as indeed was the fact) famously in English, the Latin rudiments, and in general learning: but all these objections disappeared before the generous perseverance of the Marquis of Steyne.", "아버지는 좋은 퍼블릭 스쿨에 아이를 보낼 만큼 넉넉하지 못하다고 반대했고, 어머니는 브리그스가 아이에게 더없이 훌륭한 가정교사라서 (실제로 그러했듯이) 영어와 라틴어 기초와 일반 교양에서 아이를 빼어나게 키워 왔다고 반대했다. 그러나 이 모든 반대도 스타인 후작의 너그러운 끈기 앞에서는 자취를 감추고 말았다."], ["His lordship was one of the governors of that famous old collegiate institution called the Whitefriars.", "그분은 화이트프라이어스라는 이름의, 저 유명하고 오래된 학료의 운영 이사 가운데 하나였던 것이다."], ["It had been a Cistercian Convent in old days, when the Smithfield, which is contiguous to it, was a tournament ground.", "그곳은 옛날에는 시토 수도회의 수도원이었으니, 거기에 맞붙은 스미스필드가 마상 창 시합장이던 시절의 일이다."], ["Obstinate heretics used to be brought thither convenient for burning hard by.", "완고한 이단자들이 바로 그 근처에서 화형당하기에 알맞도록 그곳으로 끌려오곤 했다."], ["Henry VIII, the Defender of the Faith, seized upon the monastery and its possessions and hanged and tortured some of the monks who could not accommodate themselves to the pace of his reform.", "신앙의 수호자 헨리 8세는 그 수도원과 그 재산을 차지하고, 자신의 개혁 보조에 발맞추지 못하는 수도사 몇몇을 목매달고 고문했다."], ["Finally, a great merchant bought the house and land adjoining, in which, and with the help of other wealthy endowments of land and money, he established a famous foundation hospital for old men and children.", "마침내 어느 큰 상인이 그 저택과 그에 인접한 땅을 사들였고, 그곳에서, 그리고 토지와 금전으로 이루어진 또 다른 부유한 기부의 도움을 받아, 노인과 아이들을 위한 유명한 자선 구호원을 세웠다."], ["An extern school grew round the old almost monastic foundation, which subsists still with its middle-age costume and usages--and all Cistercians pray that it may long flourish.", "거의 수도원이나 다름없던 그 오래된 시설 둘레로 통학생 학교가 자라났으니, 그곳은 중세풍의 복식과 관습을 그대로 간직한 채 지금도 존속하고 있다. 그리고 모든 시토회 수사들은 그곳이 오래도록 번창하기를 기도하는 것이다."]], [["Of this famous house, some of the greatest noblemen, prelates, and dignitaries in England are governors: and as the boys are very comfortably lodged, fed, and educated, and subsequently inducted to good scholarships at the University and livings in the Church, many little gentlemen are devoted to the ecclesiastical profession from their tenderest years, and there is considerable emulation to procure nominations for the foundation.", "이 유명한 학교의 운영 이사로는 영국에서 가장 지체 높은 귀족과 고위 성직자와 명사 들이 이름을 올리고 있다. 그리고 학생들은 더없이 안락하게 거처하고 먹고 교육받으며, 이후 대학의 좋은 장학금과 교회의 성직록까지 받게 되니, 어린 신사 여럿이 더없이 여린 시절부터 성직에 바쳐지고, 이 시설의 추천 자리를 따내려는 경쟁이 자못 치열한 것이다."], ["It was originally intended for the sons of poor and deserving clerics and laics, but many of the noble governors of the Institution, with an enlarged and rather capricious benevolence, selected all sorts of objects for their bounty.", "본래는 가난하나 그럴 만한 자격을 갖춘 성직자와 평신도의 아들들을 위한 것이었으나, 그 시설의 지체 높은 운영 이사들 가운데 다수는 폭넓고도 다분히 변덕스러운 자비심으로 온갖 부류를 골라 그 은혜의 대상으로 삼았다."], ["To get an education for nothing, and a future livelihood and profession assured, was so excellent a scheme that some of the richest people did not disdain it; and not only great men's relations, but great men themselves, sent their sons to profit by the chance--Right Rev.", "거저 교육을 받고, 장차의 생계와 직업까지 보장받는다는 것은 워낙 훌륭한 방책이라, 가장 부유한 이들조차 그것을 마다하지 않았다."], ["prelates sent their own kinsmen or the sons of their clergy, while, on the other hand, some great noblemen did not disdain to patronize the children of their confidential servants--so that a lad entering this establishment had every variety of youthful society wherewith to mingle.", "그리하여 지체 높은 이들의 친척뿐 아니라 지체 높은 이들 자신이 그 기회의 덕을 보라고 제 아들을 보냈으니, 존경하올 고위 성직자들은 자기 친족이나 휘하 성직자의 아들을 보냈고, 다른 한편으로는 어떤 큰 귀족들은 제 심복 하인의 자식을 후원하기를 마다하지 않았다. 그리하여 이 시설에 들어온 소년은 어울릴 만한 온갖 부류의 어린 동무를 만날 수 있었던 것이다."]], [["Rawdon Crawley, though the only book which he studied was the Racing Calendar, and though his chief recollections of polite learning were connected with the floggings which he received at Eton in his early youth, had that decent and honest reverence for classical learning which all English gentlemen feel, and was glad to think that his son was to have a provision for life, perhaps, and a certain opportunity of becoming a scholar.", "로던 크롤리는 비록 평생 공부한 책이라고는 경마 일정표뿐이었고, 고상한 학문에 관한 기억이라고 해 봐야 젊은 시절 이튼에서 받은 매질과 얽힌 것이 고작이었으나, 영국 신사라면 누구나 품는 그 고전 학문에 대한 점잖고 진솔한 경의만큼은 지니고 있었다. 그래서 제 아들이 어쩌면 평생의 살길과 학자가 될 만한 확실한 기회를 얻으리라 생각하니 기뻤다."], ["And although his boy was his chief solace and companion, and endeared to him by a thousand small ties, about which he did not care to speak to his wife, who had all along shown the utmost indifference to their son, yet Rawdon agreed at once to part with him and to give up his own greatest comfort and benefit for the sake of the welfare of the little lad.", "그리고 그 아이는 그에게 가장 큰 위안이자 동무였고, 아내에게는 굳이 말하고 싶지 않은 자잘한 인연 천 갈래로 그를 살뜰히 붙들어 매고 있었으나(아내는 처음부터 줄곧 제 아들에게 더없는 무관심을 보여 왔다), 로던은 곧바로 아이와 떨어지는 데, 어린아이의 안녕을 위해 제 가장 큰 안락과 즐거움을 포기하는 데 동의했다."], ["He did not know how fond he was of the child until it became necessary to let him go away.", "그는 아이를 떠나보내야 할 처지가 되고서야 비로소 자기가 그 아이를 얼마나 사랑하는지 깨달았다."], ["When he was gone, he felt more sad and downcast than he cared to own--far sadder than the boy himself, who was happy enough to enter a new career and find companions of his own age.", "아이가 떠나고 나자 그는 차마 내색하고 싶지 않을 만큼 슬프고 풀이 죽었다. 새로운 인생을 시작하고 또래 동무들을 만나게 되어 마냥 행복해하던 아이 자신보다 훨씬 더 슬펐던 것이다."], ["Becky burst out laughing once or twice when the Colonel, in his clumsy, incoherent way, tried to express his sentimental sorrows at the boy's departure.", "대령이 어설프고 두서없는 말투로 아이의 떠남에 대한 감상적 슬픔을 표현해 보려 할 때마다, 베키는 한두 번 까르르 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["The poor fellow felt that his dearest pleasure and closest friend was taken from him.", "가엾은 사내는 제 더없는 즐거움이자 가장 가까운 벗을 빼앗겼다고 느꼈다."], ["He looked often and wistfully at the little vacant bed in his dressing-room, where the child used to sleep.", "그는 아이가 잠들곤 하던, 자기 화장방의 그 작고 텅 빈 침대를 자주, 그리고 애틋하게 바라보았다."], ["He missed him sadly of mornings and tried in vain to walk in the park without him.", "그는 아침마다 아이가 사무치게 그리웠고, 아이 없이 공원을 거닐어 보려 했으나 헛일이었다."], ["He did not know how solitary he was until little Rawdon was gone.", "어린 로던이 떠나고 나서야 그는 자기가 얼마나 외로운지를 알았다."], ["He liked the people who were fond of him, and would go and sit for long hours with his good-natured sister Lady Jane, and talk to her about the virtues, and good looks, and hundred good qualities of the child.", "그는 아이를 아껴 주는 사람들이 좋았고, 마음씨 고운 누이 제인 부인에게 가서 몇 시간이고 함께 앉아, 아이의 미덕이며 잘생긴 얼굴이며 백 가지 좋은 점들에 대해 이야기하곤 했다."]], [["Young Rawdon's aunt, we have said, was very fond of him, as was her little girl, who wept copiously when the time for her cousin's departure came.", "젊은 로던의 큰어머니가 그를 무척 귀애했다는 것은 앞서 말한 바와 같으니, 그 어린 딸도 마찬가지여서 사촌이 떠날 때가 되자 펑펑 눈물을 쏟았다."], ["The elder Rawdon was thankful for the fondness of mother and daughter.", "형뻘 되는 로던은 모녀의 그 다정함에 고마워했다."], ["The very best and honestest feelings of the man came out in these artless outpourings of paternal feeling in which he indulged in their presence, and encouraged by their sympathy.", "그가 두 사람 앞에서 마음껏 쏟아 낸 그 꾸밈없는 부정(父情)의 토로 속에, 또 그들의 공감에 힘입어, 이 사내의 더없이 선량하고 진솔한 감정이 고스란히 드러났다."], ["He secured not only Lady Jane's kindness, but her sincere regard, by the feelings which he manifested, and which he could not show to his own wife.", "그는 제 아내에게는 보여 줄 수 없었던 그 감정들을 드러냄으로써 제인 부인의 친절뿐 아니라 그 진심 어린 존경까지 얻어 냈다."], ["The two kinswomen met as seldom as possible.", "두 동서는 되도록 마주치는 일이 드물었다."], ["Becky laughed bitterly at Jane's feelings and softness; the other's kindly and gentle nature could not but revolt at her sister's callous behaviour.", "베키는 제인의 감상과 무름을 두고 신랄하게 비웃었고, 제인의 친절하고 온화한 천성은 동서의 그 냉담한 처신에 반발하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["It estranged Rawdon from his wife more than he knew or acknowledged to himself.", "이런 일은 로던 자신이 알거나 스스로 인정하는 것 이상으로 그를 아내에게서 멀어지게 했다."], ["She did not care for the estrangement.", "아내는 그 소원해짐을 개의치 않았다."], ["Indeed, she did not miss him or anybody.", "사실 그녀는 그도, 다른 누구도 그리워하지 않았다."], ["She looked upon him as her errand-man and humble slave.", "그녀는 그를 제 심부름꾼이자 비천한 노예쯤으로 여겼다."], ["He might be ever so depressed or sulky, and she did not mark his demeanour, or only treated it with a sneer.", "그가 아무리 풀이 죽어 있거나 시무룩해 있어도, 그녀는 그 기색을 알아채지 못했고, 알아챈대도 그저 비웃음으로 받아넘길 뿐이었다."], ["She was busy thinking about her position, or her pleasures, or her advancement in society; she ought to have held a great place in it, that is certain.", "그녀는 제 지위며, 제 즐거움이며, 제 사교계에서의 출세를 궁리하느라 바빴다. 그녀가 그곳에서 대단한 자리를 차지하는 것이 마땅하다는 것은 분명한 일이었다."]], [["It was honest Briggs who made up the little kit for the boy which he was to take to school.", "아이가 학교에 가져갈 자그마한 짐을 꾸려 준 것은 정직한 브리그스였다."], ["Molly, the housemaid, blubbered in the passage when he went away--Molly kind and faithful in spite of a long arrear of unpaid wages.", "아이가 떠날 때 가정부 몰리는 복도에서 엉엉 울었으니, 밀린 품삯이 오래도록 쌓여 있는데도 친절하고 충실하기 그지없는 몰리였다."], ["Mrs. Becky could not let her husband have the carriage to take the boy to school.", "베키 부인은 아이를 학교에 데려다주는 데 남편이 마차를 쓰도록 허락하지 않았다."], ["Take the horses into the City!--such a thing was never heard of.", "말들을 시내까지 끌고 가다니! 그런 일은 들어 본 적도 없었다."], ["Let a cab be brought.", "삯마차나 불러 오라는 것이었다."], ["She did not offer to kiss him when he went, nor did the child propose to embrace her; but gave a kiss to old Briggs (whom, in general, he was very shy of caressing), and consoled her by pointing out that he was to come home on Saturdays, when she would have the benefit of seeing him.", "그녀는 아이가 떠날 때 입맞춤을 청하지도 않았고, 아이 역시 그녀를 끌어안으려 하지 않았다. 다만 늙은 브리그스에게는 입을 맞추었고(평소에는 그녀를 어루만지기를 몹시 수줍어하던 아이였다), 토요일마다 집에 돌아와 그녀가 자기를 볼 수 있게 되리라고 일러 주며 그녀를 달랬다."], ["As the cab rolled towards the City, Becky's carriage rattled off to the park.", "삯마차가 시내를 향해 굴러갈 무렵, 베키의 마차는 덜컹대며 공원으로 달려가고 있었다."], ["She was chattering and laughing with a score of young dandies by the Serpentine as the father and son entered at the old gates of the school--where Rawdon left the child and came away with a sadder purer feeling in his heart than perhaps that poor battered fellow had ever known since he himself came out of the nursery.", "아버지와 아들이 학교의 오래된 정문으로 들어설 때, 그녀는 서펜타인 못가에서 스무 명 남짓한 젊은 멋쟁이들과 재잘대며 웃고 있었다. 로던은 거기에 아이를 남겨 두고 돌아섰으니, 그 마음에는, 어쩌면 이 가엾고 닳고 닳은 사내가 자기 자신이 어린 시절을 벗어난 이래 한 번도 느껴 본 적 없을 만큼, 더 슬프고도 더 순수한 감정이 깃들어 있었다."]], [["He walked all the way home very dismally, and dined alone with Briggs.", "그는 집까지 줄곧 더없이 침울하게 걸어와, 브리그스와 단둘이 저녁을 들었다."], ["He was very kind to her and grateful for her love and watchfulness over the boy.", "그는 그녀에게 무척 다정했고, 아이를 향한 그녀의 사랑과 돌봄에 고마워했다."], ["His conscience smote him that he had borrowed Briggs's money and aided in deceiving her.", "그가 브리그스의 돈을 빌렸고 또 그녀를 속이는 데 한몫했다는 양심의 가책이 그를 찔러 댔다."], ["They talked about little Rawdon a long time, for Becky only came home to dress and go out to dinner--and then he went off uneasily to drink tea with Lady Jane, and tell her of what had happened, and how little Rawdon went off like a trump, and how he was to wear a gown and little knee-breeches, and how young Blackball, Jack Blackball's son, of the old regiment, had taken him in charge and promised to be kind to him.", "두 사람은 어린 로던에 대해 오래도록 이야기를 나누었으니, 베키는 그저 옷을 차려입고 만찬에 나가려고 집에 들렀을 뿐이었다. 그러고 나서 그는 마음이 편치 않은 채 제인 부인에게 차를 마시러 가서, 무슨 일이 있었는지를, 어린 로던이 얼마나 야무지게 떠났는지를, 어떻게 그 아이가 학생복과 짧은 반바지를 입게 되었는지를, 또 옛 연대 시절의 잭 블랙볼의 아들인 젊은 블랙볼이 어떻게 그 아이를 맡아 잘 돌봐 주겠노라 약속했는지를 이야기했다."]], [["In the course of a week, young Blackball had constituted little Rawdon his fag, shoe-black, and breakfast toaster; initiated him into the mysteries of the Latin Grammar; and thrashed him three or four times, but not severely.", "일주일도 채 못 되어 젊은 블랙볼은 어린 로던을 제 잔심부름꾼이자 구두닦이이자 아침 토스트 굽는 시종으로 삼았고, 라틴어 문법의 신비를 일러 주었으며, 서너 차례 매질을 했으나 모질게는 아니었다."], ["The little chap's good-natured honest face won his way for him.", "그 꼬마의 선량하고 정직한 얼굴이 그를 위해 길을 터 주었던 것이다."], ["He only got that degree of beating which was, no doubt, good for him; and as for blacking shoes, toasting bread, and fagging in general, were these offices not deemed to be necessary parts of every young English gentleman's education?", "그가 받은 매질이라고는 분명 그에게 약이 될 만한 정도였을 따름이다. 그리고 구두를 닦고 빵을 굽고 자질구레한 심부름을 하는 것으로 말하자면, 그런 일들이야말로 영국의 어린 신사라면 누구나 받아야 할 교육의 필수 과정으로 여겨지지 않았던가?"]], [["Our business does not lie with the second generation and Master Rawdon's life at school, otherwise the present tale might be carried to any indefinite length.", "우리가 다룰 이야기는 그 둘째 세대며 로던 도련님의 학교생활에 있지 않으니, 그러지 않았다면 이 이야기는 한없이 길게 늘어졌을 것이다."], ["The Colonel went to see his son a short time afterwards and found the lad sufficiently well and happy, grinning and laughing in his little black gown and little breeches.", "대령은 얼마 지나지 않아 아들을 보러 갔는데, 그 꼬마가 자그마한 검은 학생복과 자그마한 반바지 차림으로 싱글벙글 웃으며 더없이 건강하고 행복하게 지내는 것을 보았다."]], [["His father sagaciously tipped Blackball, his master, a sovereign, and secured that young gentleman's good-will towards his fag.", "아버지는 약삭빠르게도 그 아이의 상급자인 블랙볼에게 1소버린을 쥐여 주고, 제 잔심부름꾼을 향한 그 젊은 신사의 호의를 확보해 두었다."], ["As a protege of the great Lord Steyne, the nephew of a County member, and son of a Colonel and C.B., whose name appeared in some of the most fashionable parties in the Morning Post, perhaps the school authorities were disposed not to look unkindly on the child.", "위대한 스타인 경의 비호를 받는 아이요, 어느 주(州) 하원의원의 조카요, 그 이름이 모닝 포스트의 가장 화려한 연회 기사 몇몇에 오르내리는 대령이자 바스 훈작사의 아들이었으니, 어쩌면 학교 당국도 이 아이를 박정하게 대할 마음은 들지 않았을 것이다."], ["He had plenty of pocket-money, which he spent in treating his comrades royally to raspberry tarts, and he was often allowed to come home on Saturdays to his father, who always made a jubilee of that day.", "그에게는 용돈이 넉넉했고, 그는 그 돈으로 동무들에게 산딸기 파이를 푸짐하게 한턱내며 썼으며, 토요일이면 종종 아버지에게 돌아오도록 허락받았는데, 아버지는 늘 그날을 잔칫날로 삼았다."], ["When free, Rawdon would take him to the play, or send him thither with the footman; and on Sundays he went to church with Briggs and Lady Jane and his cousins.", "짬이 나면 로던은 아이를 연극 구경에 데려갔고, 그러지 못할 때는 하인을 딸려 보냈다. 일요일이면 아이는 브리그스와 제인 부인과 사촌들과 함께 교회에 갔다."], ["Rawdon marvelled over his stories about school, and fights, and fagging.", "로던은 학교며 싸움이며 잔심부름에 관한 아이의 이야기에 경탄했다."], ["Before long, he knew the names of all the masters and the principal boys as well as little Rawdon himself.", "머지않아 그는 어린 로던 못지않게 모든 선생과 주요 학생들의 이름을 죄다 외게 되었다."], ["He invited little Rawdon's crony from school, and made both the children sick with pastry, and oysters, and porter after the play.", "그는 학교에서 사귄 어린 로던의 단짝을 초대했고, 연극이 끝난 뒤 두 아이에게 파이와 굴과 흑맥주를 먹여 둘 다 탈이 나게 만들었다."], ["He tried to look knowing over the Latin grammar when little Rawdon showed him what part of that work he was \"in.\"", "어린 로던이 라틴어 문법책의 어느 대목을 \"배우는 중\"인지 보여 주면, 그는 아는 체하는 표정을 지어 보이려 애썼다."], ["\"Stick to it, my boy,\"", "\"꾸준히 하거라, 얘야.\""], ["he said to him with much gravity, \"there's nothing like a good classical education!", "그는 자못 엄숙하게 아이에게 말했다. \"훌륭한 고전 교육만 한 것이 없단다."], ["Nothing!\"", "그만한 게 없어!\""]], [["Becky's contempt for her husband grew greater every day.", "남편을 향한 베키의 멸시는 나날이 더해 갔다. \"당신 하고 싶은 대로 해요."], ["\"Do what you like--dine where you please--go and have ginger-beer and sawdust at Astley's, or psalm-singing with Lady Jane--only don't expect me to busy myself with the boy.", "좋을 대로 어디서든 저녁을 들고, 애슬리 곡마장에 가서 생강 맥주에 톱밥이나 먹든지, 제인 부인이랑 찬송가나 부르든지. 다만 내가 그 애한테 매달리기를 바라진 말아요."], ["I have your interests to attend to, as you can't attend to them yourself.", "난 당신 이익을 챙겨야 하니까. 당신은 스스로 그걸 챙기지도 못하잖아요."], ["I should like to know where you would have been now, and in what sort of a position in society, if I had not looked after you.\"", "만약 내가 당신을 보살피지 않았더라면 지금쯤 당신이 어디서, 사교계의 어떤 자리에 있을지 정말 알고 싶군요.\""], ["Indeed, nobody wanted poor old Rawdon at the parties whither Becky used to go.", "사실 베키가 드나들던 연회 자리에서 가엾은 늙은 로던을 찾는 사람은 아무도 없었다."], ["She was often asked without him now.", "이제 그녀는 그 없이 초대받는 일이 잦았다."], ["She talked about great people as if she had the fee-simple of May Fair, and when the Court went into mourning, she always wore black.", "그녀는 마치 메이페어를 송두리째 제 소유라도 한 양 지체 높은 이들에 대해 떠들어 댔고, 궁정이 상중에 들면 그녀도 늘 검은 옷을 입었다."]], [["Little Rawdon being disposed of, Lord Steyne, who took such a parental interest in the affairs of this amiable poor family, thought that their expenses might be very advantageously curtailed by the departure of Miss Briggs, and that Becky was quite clever enough to take the management of her own house.", "어린 로던이 처리되고 나자, 이 사랑스러운 가난한 가족의 일에 그토록 아비다운 관심을 기울이던 스타인 경은, 브리그스 양을 내보내면 그들의 지출을 자못 유익하게 줄일 수 있겠다고, 또 베키가 제 살림을 손수 꾸려 갈 만큼 충분히 영리하다고 생각했다."], ["It has been narrated in a former chapter how the benevolent nobleman had given his protegee money to pay off her little debt to Miss Briggs, who however still remained behind with her friends; whence my lord came to the painful conclusion that Mrs.", "그 자비로운 귀족이 제 피후견인에게 브리그스 양에게 진 자그마한 빚을 갚으라고 돈을 주었던 일은 앞 장에서 이야기한 바 있는데, 그런데도 브리그스는 여전히 그 벗들 곁에 남아 있었다."], ["Crawley had made some other use of the money confided to her than that for which her generous patron had given the loan.", "그리하여 경은, 크롤리 부인이 제 너그러운 후원자가 빌려준 돈을 그 본래 목적과는 다른 데 썼으리라는 괴로운 결론에 이르렀다."], ["However, Lord Steyne was not so rude as to impart his suspicions upon this head to Mrs.", "그러나 스타인 경은 이 점에 관한 의심을 베키 부인에게 굳이 털어놓을 만큼 무례하지는 않았다."], ["Becky, whose feelings might be hurt by any controversy on the money-question, and who might have a thousand painful reasons for disposing otherwise of his lordship's generous loan.", "돈 문제로 입씨름을 벌이면 그녀의 감정이 상할 수도 있었고, 또 그녀에게는 그분의 너그러운 차용금을 달리 처분할 만한 괴로운 사정이 천 가지나 있을지도 몰랐던 것이다."], ["But he determined to satisfy himself of the real state of the case, and instituted the necessary inquiries in a most cautious and delicate manner.", "그러나 그는 사정의 실상을 제 눈으로 확인해 두기로 마음먹고, 더없이 조심스럽고 은근한 방식으로 필요한 탐문에 나섰다."]], [["In the first place he took an early opportunity of pumping Miss Briggs.", "우선 그는 일찌감치 기회를 잡아 브리그스 양을 떠보았다."], ["That was not a difficult operation.", "그것은 어려운 일이 아니었다."], ["A very little encouragement would set that worthy woman to talk volubly and pour out all within her. And one day when Mrs.", "조금만 부추겨 주면 그 갸륵한 여인은 청산유수로 떠들며 제 속을 죄다 쏟아 놓곤 했던 것이다."], ["Rawdon had gone out to drive (as Mr. Fiche, his lordship's confidential servant, easily learned at the livery stables where the Crawleys kept their carriage and horses, or rather, where the livery-man kept a carriage and horses for Mr.", "그러던 어느 날, 로던 부인이 마차를 타고 나간 사이(크롤리 부부가 마차와 말을 두던 대여 마구간에서, 아니 그보다는 마구간 주인이 크롤리 부부를 위해 마차와 말을 두고 있던 그곳에서, 경의 심복 하인 피시 씨가 수월히 알아낸 바였다), 경은 커즌 거리의 그 집에 불쑥 들렀다."], ["and Mrs. Crawley)--my lord dropped in upon the Curzon Street house--asked Briggs for a cup of coffee--told her that he had good accounts of the little boy at school--and in five minutes found out from her that Mrs.", "그는 브리그스에게 커피 한 잔을 청하고, 학교에 있는 그 어린 아들에 관해 좋은 소식을 들었다고 말했다."], ["Rawdon had given her nothing except a black silk gown, for which Miss Briggs was immensely grateful.", "그러고는 오 분 만에, 로던 부인이 검은 비단 드레스 한 벌 말고는 그녀에게 아무것도 주지 않았으며, 브리그스 양은 그 드레스를 더없이 고마워하고 있다는 사실을 알아냈다."]], [["He laughed within himself at this artless story.", "그는 이 꾸밈없는 이야기에 속으로 웃었다."], ["For the truth is, our dear friend Rebecca had given him a most circumstantial narration of Briggs's delight at receiving her money--eleven hundred and twenty-five pounds--and in what securities she had invested it; and what a pang Becky herself felt in being obliged to pay away such a delightful sum of money.", "사실인즉, 우리의 친애하는 벗 레베카는 그에게, 돈을—천백이십오 파운드를—받은 브리그스가 얼마나 기뻐했는지, 또 그 돈을 무슨 증권에 투자했는지를 더없이 소상하게 늘어놓았던 것이다. 게다가 그토록 흐뭇한 거금을 내놓아야 하는 데 베키 자신이 얼마나 가슴 아팠는지까지."], ["\"Who knows,\"", "\"누가 알겠어.\""], ["the dear woman may have thought within herself, \"perhaps he may give me a little more?\"", "이 사랑스러운 여인은 속으로 이렇게 생각했을지도 모른다. \"어쩌면 조금 더 줄지도.\""], ["My lord, however, made no such proposal to the little schemer--very likely thinking that he had been sufficiently generous already.", "그러나 경은 이 자그마한 책략가에게 그런 제안을 하지 않았다. 십중팔구 자기가 이미 충분히 너그러웠노라 생각했을 것이다."]], [["He had the curiosity, then, to ask Miss Briggs about the state of her private affairs--and she told his lordship candidly what her position was--how Miss Crawley had left her a legacy--how her relatives had had part of it--how Colonel Crawley had put out another portion, for which she had the best security and interest--and how Mr.", "이어 그는 브리그스 양에게 그녀 자신의 형편이 어떤지를 물어볼 호기심이 일었고, 그녀는 경에게 제 처지를 솔직하게 털어놓았다."], ["and Mrs. Rawdon had kindly busied themselves with Sir Pitt, who was to dispose of the remainder most advantageously for her, when he had time.", "어떻게 크롤리 양이 자기에게 유산을 남겼는지, 어떻게 친척들이 그 일부를 가져갔는지, 어떻게 크롤리 대령이 또 다른 몫을 투자해 주었고 자기는 그 대가로 더없이 확실한 담보와 이자를 받고 있는지, 또 로던 부부가 친절하게도 피트 경에게 나머지를 부탁하느라 애써 주었고, 피트 경은 짬이 나면 그것을 자기에게 가장 유리하게 처분해 주기로 했는지를."], ["My lord asked how much the Colonel had already invested for her, and Miss Briggs at once and truly told him that the sum was six hundred and odd pounds.", "경이 대령이 이미 그녀를 위해 얼마를 투자했느냐고 묻자, 브리그스 양은 즉시, 그리고 사실대로 그 액수가 육백 몇 파운드라고 일러 주었다."]], [["But as soon as she had told her story, the voluble Briggs repented of her frankness and besought my lord not to tell Mr. Crawley of the confessions which she had made.", "그러나 이야기를 다 털어놓기가 무섭게, 수다스러운 브리그스는 제 솔직함을 후회하며, 자기가 한 그 고백을 크롤리 씨에게는 일러바치지 말아 달라고 경에게 간청했다."], ["\"The Colonel was so kind--Mr.", "\"대령이 워낙 친절하셔서요."], ["Crawley might be offended and pay back the money, for which she could get no such good interest anywhere else.\"", "크롤리 씨가 기분이 상하셔서 돈을 도로 갚으실지도 모르는데, 그러면 그만한 이자는 어디서도 받지 못할 테니까요.\""], ["Lord Steyne, laughing, promised he never would divulge their conversation, and when he and Miss Briggs parted he laughed still more.", "스타인 경은 웃으며 두 사람의 대화를 결코 발설하지 않겠노라 약속했고, 브리그스 양과 헤어질 때는 한층 더 크게 웃었다."]], [["\"What an accomplished little devil it is!\"", "\"어쩌면 저토록 노련한 꼬마 악마람!\""], ["thought he.", "그는 생각했다."], ["\"What a splendid actress and manager!", "\"기막힌 여배우요, 수완가가 아닌가!"], ["She had almost got a second supply out of me the other day; with her coaxing ways.", "그 살살거리는 솜씨로 며칠 전엔 나한테서 두 번째 돈까지 거의 우려낼 뻔했지."], ["She beats all the women I have ever seen in the course of all my well-spent life.", "내가 잘 보낸 평생 동안 보아 온 모든 여자를 통틀어 단연 최고야."], ["They are babies compared to her.", "그 여자들은 저 여자에 비하면 갓난애에 불과해."], ["I am a greenhorn myself, and a fool in her hands--an old fool.", "나야말로 풋내기요, 저 여자 손아귀에서 노는 바보지. 늙은 바보 말이야."], ["She is unsurpassable in lies.\"", "거짓말에 있어선 누구도 따를 자가 없어.\""], ["His lordship's admiration for Becky rose immeasurably at this proof of her cleverness.", "그녀의 영리함을 보여 주는 이 증거 앞에서 베키를 향한 경의 감탄은 한량없이 치솟았다."], ["Getting the money was nothing--but getting double the sum she wanted, and paying nobody--it was a magnificent stroke.", "돈을 얻어 낸 것이야 아무것도 아니지만, 필요한 액수의 곱절을 얻어 내고도 아무에게도 갚지 않다니, 그것은 참으로 멋들어진 한 수였다."], ["And Crawley, my lord thought--Crawley is not such a fool as he looks and seems.", "그리고 크롤리로 말하자면, 하고 경은 생각했다. 크롤리는 겉보기처럼 그렇게 바보가 아니야."], ["He has managed the matter cleverly enough on his side.", "제 쪽에서도 이 일을 제법 영리하게 꾸려 온 거지."], ["Nobody would ever have supposed from his face and demeanour that he knew anything about this money business; and yet he put her up to it, and has spent the money, no doubt.", "그 얼굴이며 태도만 보아선 이 돈 문제를 조금이라도 안다고는 누구도 짐작 못 할 텐데, 알고 보면 그자가 여자를 부추긴 거고, 그 돈도 분명 그자가 썼을 거야."], ["In this opinion my lord, we know, was mistaken, but it influenced a good deal his behaviour towards Colonel Crawley, whom he began to treat with even less than that semblance of respect which he had formerly shown towards that gentleman. It never entered into the head of Mrs.", "우리도 알다시피 이 견해에서 경은 틀렸으나, 그것은 크롤리 대령을 향한 그의 처신에 적잖이 영향을 미쳐, 그는 전에 그 신사에게 보이던 그 시늉뿐인 존경심마저 더욱 줄여 가며 대하기 시작했다."], ["Crawley's patron that the little lady might be making a purse for herself; and, perhaps, if the truth must be told, he judged of Colonel Crawley by his experience of other husbands, whom he had known in the course of the long and well-spent life which had made him acquainted with a great deal of the weakness of mankind.", "그 자그마한 부인이 제 몫의 돈주머니를 챙기고 있을지도 모른다는 생각은 크롤리 부인의 후원자의 머릿속에 도무지 떠오르지 않았다. 그리고, 사실대로 말하자면, 그는 자기가 그 오랜 세월, 인류의 숱한 약점을 익히 알게 해 준 그 잘 보낸 평생 동안 알고 지낸 다른 남편들에 비추어 크롤리 대령을 가늠했던 것이다."], ["My lord had bought so many men during his life that he was surely to be pardoned for supposing that he had found the price of this one.", "경은 평생토록 워낙 많은 사내를 사들여 왔으니, 이 사내의 값도 알아냈다고 짐작한 것쯤은 분명 너그러이 봐줄 만한 일이었다."]], [["He taxed Becky upon the point on the very first occasion when he met her alone, and he complimented her, good-humouredly, on her cleverness in getting more than the money which she required.", "그는 베키를 단둘이 만난 첫 자리에서 이 점을 두고 그녀를 추궁했고, 필요한 액수보다 더 많은 돈을 얻어 낸 그 영리함을 두고 사람 좋게 그녀를 칭찬했다."], ["Becky was only a little taken aback.", "베키는 그저 살짝 당황했을 뿐이다."], ["It was not the habit of this dear creature to tell falsehoods, except when necessity compelled, but in these great emergencies it was her practice to lie very freely; and in an instant she was ready with another neat plausible circumstantial story which she administered to her patron.", "이 사랑스러운 여인은 어쩔 수 없이 떠밀릴 때를 빼고는 거짓말을 하는 버릇이 없었으나, 이런 다급한 고비에서는 거리낌 없이 거짓말을 늘어놓는 것이 그녀의 수법이었다. 그리하여 그녀는 순식간에, 또 하나의 그럴듯하고 정연하며 소상한 이야기를 지어내어 후원자에게 들이밀 채비를 갖추었다."], ["The previous statement which she had made to him was a falsehood--a wicked falsehood--she owned it.", "앞서 그에게 한 진술은 거짓이었다고, 못된 거짓말이었다고, 그녀는 인정했다. 그러나 누가 그녀로 하여금 그런 말을 하게 만들었던가?"], ["But who had made her tell it?", "\"아, 경이시여.\""], ["\"Ah, my Lord,\"", "그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, \"you don't know all I have to suffer and bear in silence; you see me gay and happy before you--you little know what I have to endure when there is no protector near me.", "\"제가 말없이 견디고 감내해야 하는 그 모든 것을 경께서는 모르세요. 경 앞에서는 명랑하고 행복한 제 모습만 보시지요."], ["It was my husband, by threats and the most savage treatment, forced me to ask for that sum about which I deceived you.", "곁에 지켜 줄 이 하나 없을 때 제가 무엇을 견뎌야 하는지는 거의 모르세요. 경을 속이고 청했던 그 돈은, 제 남편이 협박과 더없이 사나운 학대로 저를 몰아붙여 요구하게 만든 거예요."], ["It was he who, foreseeing that questions might be asked regarding the disposal of the money, forced me to account for it as I did.", "돈의 처분에 관해 추궁이 있으리라 미리 내다보고, 제가 그런 식으로 둘러대도록 떠민 것도 바로 그 사람이고요."], ["He took the money.", "돈은 그 사람이 가져갔어요."], ["He told me he had paid Miss Briggs; I did not want, I did not dare to doubt him.", "그이는 브리그스 양에게 갚았다고 했어요. 저는 그 말을 의심하고 싶지도, 감히 의심할 수도 없었죠."], ["Pardon the wrong which a desperate man is forced to commit, and pity a miserable, miserable woman.\"", "절박한 사내가 어쩔 수 없이 저지른 잘못을 부디 용서해 주시고, 가엾고 가엾은 이 여자를 가엾이 여겨 주세요.\""], ["She burst into tears as she spoke.", "그녀는 이렇게 말하며 와락 울음을 터뜨렸다."], ["Persecuted virtue never looked more bewitchingly wretched.", "박해받는 미덕이 이보다 더 매혹적으로 가련해 보인 적은 없었다."]], [["They had a long conversation, driving round and round the Regent's Park in Mrs. Crawley's carriage together, a conversation of which it is not necessary to repeat the details, but the upshot of it was that, when Becky came home, she flew to her dear Briggs with a smiling face and announced that she had some very good news for her.", "두 사람은 크롤리 부인의 마차를 함께 타고 리젠트 공원을 빙글빙글 돌면서 오랫동안 대화를 나누었으니, 그 자세한 내용을 여기 되풀이할 필요는 없겠으나, 그 결말인즉, 베키가 집에 돌아오자 미소 띤 얼굴로 사랑하는 브리그스에게 달려가, 그녀에게 들려줄 더없이 좋은 소식이 있노라 알렸다는 것이다."], ["Lord Steyne had acted in the noblest and most generous manner.", "스타인 경께서 더없이 고귀하고 너그러운 처신을 하셨다는 것이었다."], ["He was always thinking how and when he could do good.", "그분은 언제 어떻게 선행을 베풀 수 있을지 늘 궁리하시는 분이라고."], ["Now that little Rawdon was gone to school, a dear companion and friend was no longer necessary to her.", "이제 어린 로던이 학교에 갔으니, 다정한 길동무이자 벗은 더는 필요하지 않다고."], ["She was grieved beyond measure to part with Briggs, but her means required that she should practise every retrenchment, and her sorrow was mitigated by the idea that her dear Briggs would be far better provided for by her generous patron than in her humble home.", "브리그스와 헤어지는 것이 이루 말할 수 없이 슬프지만, 제 형편상 온갖 절약을 다 해야 하는 처지라고. 그래도 사랑하는 브리그스가 제 초라한 집에 있느니 그 너그러운 후원자 곁에서 훨씬 더 나은 살길을 얻으리라는 생각에 슬픔이 누그러진다고."], ["Mrs. Pilkington, the housekeeper at Gauntly Hall, was growing exceedingly old, feeble, and rheumatic: she was not equal to the work of superintending that vast mansion, and must be on the look out for a successor.", "곤틀리 저택의 가정부 필킹턴 부인이 부쩍 늙고 쇠약해진 데다 류머티즘까지 앓고 있어서, 그 광대한 저택을 관리하는 일을 감당하지 못하니 후임을 찾아야 한다는 것이었다."], ["It was a splendid position.", "그것은 더없이 훌륭한 자리였다."], ["The family did not go to Gauntly once in two years.", "그 집안 사람들은 이 년에 한 번도 곤틀리에 가지 않았다."], ["At other times the housekeeper was the mistress of the magnificent mansion--had four covers daily for her table; was visited by the clergy and the most respectable people of the county--was the lady of Gauntly, in fact; and the two last housekeepers before Mrs.", "그렇지 않은 때면 가정부가 그 으리으리한 저택의 안주인이었으니, 매일 제 식탁에 네 사람분 식기를 차렸고, 성직자들과 그 고을의 가장 점잖은 이들의 방문을 받았다."], ["Pilkington had married rectors of Gauntly--but Mrs. P.", "말하자면 그녀가 곤틀리의 마님이었던 셈이다."], ["could not, being the aunt of the present Rector. The place was not to be hers yet, but she might go down on a visit to Mrs.", "필킹턴 부인 이전의 두 가정부는 곤틀리의 교구 목사들과 혼인까지 했으나, 필킹턴 부인은 현재 그 목사의 고모인지라 그럴 수 없었다."], ["Pilkington and see whether she would like to succeed her.", "그 자리가 아직 브리그스의 차지가 된 것은 아니었으나, 그녀가 필킹턴 부인을 찾아가 머물면서 그 뒤를 잇고 싶은지 가늠해 볼 수는 있었다."]], [["What words can paint the ecstatic gratitude of Briggs!", "브리그스의 그 황홀한 감격을 무슨 말로 그려 낼 수 있으랴!"], ["All she stipulated for was that little Rawdon should be allowed to come down and see her at the Hall.", "그녀가 단 하나 조건으로 내건 것은, 어린 로던이 저택으로 내려와 자기를 보러 오게 해 달라는 것뿐이었다."], ["Becky promised this--anything.", "베키는 그러마고—무엇이든 약속했다."], ["She ran up to her husband when he came home and told him the joyful news.", "그녀는 남편이 돌아오자 그에게 달려가 그 기쁜 소식을 전했다."], ["Rawdon was glad, deuced glad; the weight was off his conscience about poor Briggs's money.", "로던은 기뻤다, 지독히도 기뻤다. 가엾은 브리그스의 돈을 두고 마음을 짓누르던 짐이 덜어진 것이다."], ["She was provided for, at any rate, but--but his mind was disquiet.", "어쨌거나 그녀의 살길은 마련된 셈이었다. 그러나—그러나 그의 마음은 편치 않았다."], ["He did not seem to be all right, somehow.", "어쩐지 무언가 미덥지가 않은 듯했다."], ["He told little Southdown what Lord Steyne had done, and the young man eyed Crawley with an air which surprised the latter.", "그는 어린 사우스다운에게 스타인 경이 한 일을 이야기했는데, 그 젊은이는 크롤리를 놀라게 할 만한 표정으로 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["He told Lady Jane of this second proof of Steyne's bounty, and she, too, looked odd and alarmed; so did Sir Pitt.", "그는 제인 부인에게 스타인의 너그러움을 보여 주는 이 두 번째 증거를 이야기했는데, 그녀 역시 묘하고 놀란 기색이었다. 피트 경도 마찬가지였다."], ["\"She is too clever and--and gay to be allowed to go from party to party without a companion,\"", "\"그 사람은 너무 영리하고—또—명랑해서, 길동무 하나 없이 이 연회 저 연회를 떠돌게 둘 수가 없어요.\" 두 사람은 입을 모아 말했다."], ["both said.", "\"로던, 자네가 그 사람이 가는 데마다 함께 가야 하네. 그리고 누군가 곁에 두어야 해."], ["\"You must go with her, Rawdon, wherever she goes, and you must have somebody with her--one of the girls from Queen's Crawley, perhaps, though they were rather giddy guardians for her.\"", "어쩌면 퀸스크롤리의 처녀들 가운데 하나라도 좋겠지. 하기야 그 애들은 그 사람을 지켜 줄 보호자로는 좀 들떠 있긴 하지만 말이야.\""]], [["Somebody Becky should have.", "베키에게는 누군가 곁에 두어야 했다."], ["But in the meantime it was clear that honest Briggs must not lose her chance of settlement for life, and so she and her bags were packed, and she set off on her journey.", "그러나 그러는 사이에도, 정직한 브리그스가 평생 자리 잡을 기회를 놓쳐서는 안 된다는 것만큼은 분명했다. 그리하여 그녀와 그녀의 짐 보따리가 꾸려졌고, 그녀는 여정에 올랐다."], ["And so two of Rawdon's out-sentinels were in the hands of the enemy.", "그렇게 해서 로던의 전초병 둘이 적의 손아귀에 떨어진 셈이었다."]], [["Sir Pitt went and expostulated with his sister-in-law upon the subject of the dismissal of Briggs and other matters of delicate family interest.", "피트 경은 동서를 찾아가, 브리그스를 내보낸 일이며 그 밖에 집안의 민감한 일들을 두고 따져 물었다."], ["In vain she pointed out to him how necessary was the protection of Lord Steyne for her poor husband; how cruel it would be on their part to deprive Briggs of the position offered to her.", "가엾은 제 남편에게 스타인 경의 비호가 얼마나 긴요한지, 또 브리그스에게 주어진 그 자리를 빼앗는 것이 자기들로서는 얼마나 모진 짓일지를 그녀가 아무리 일러 보아야 헛일이었다."], ["Cajolements, coaxings, smiles, tears could not satisfy Sir Pitt, and he had something very like a quarrel with his once admired Becky.", "알랑거림도, 살살거림도, 미소도, 눈물도 피트 경을 만족시키지 못했고, 그는 한때 흠모하던 베키와 거의 다툼이나 다름없는 언쟁을 벌였다."], ["He spoke of the honour of the family, the unsullied reputation of the Crawleys; expressed himself in indignant tones about her receiving those young Frenchmen--those wild young men of fashion, my Lord Steyne himself, whose carriage was always at her door, who passed hours daily in her company, and whose constant presence made the world talk about her.", "그는 집안의 명예를, 크롤리 가문의 티끌 하나 없는 명성을 입에 올렸다. 그녀가 그 젊은 프랑스인들을—그 들뜬 멋쟁이 젊은이들을, 또 늘 그녀의 문 앞에 마차를 대 놓고 매일같이 몇 시간씩 그녀와 함께 지내며 그 끊임없는 출입으로 세상이 그녀를 입에 올리게 만드는 스타인 경 자신을—받아들이는 데 대해 분개한 어조로 말했다."], ["As the head of the house he implored her to be more prudent.", "집안의 가장으로서 그는 그녀에게 좀 더 신중하기를 간청했다."], ["Society was already speaking lightly of her.", "사교계가 이미 그녀를 두고 가벼이 입방아를 찧고 있었던 것이다."], ["Lord Steyne, though a nobleman of the greatest station and talents, was a man whose attentions would compromise any woman; he besought, he implored, he commanded his sister-in-law to be watchful in her intercourse with that nobleman.", "스타인 경은 비록 더없이 지체 높고 재능이 빼어난 귀족이긴 하나, 그 관심을 받았다 하면 어느 여인의 평판이든 더럽혀 놓고 마는 사내였다. 그는 동서에게 그 귀족과의 교제에 신중하기를 빌고, 간청하고, 명령했다."]], [["Becky promised anything and everything Pitt wanted; but Lord Steyne came to her house as often as ever, and Sir Pitt's anger increased.", "베키는 피트가 바라는 것이라면 무엇이든 다 약속했다. 그러나 스타인 경은 여전히 변함없이 그녀의 집을 드나들었고, 피트 경의 분노는 더해 갔다."], ["I wonder was Lady Jane angry or pleased that her husband at last found fault with his favourite Rebecca?", "제 남편이 마침내 그가 총애하던 레베카의 흠을 잡았으니, 제인 부인은 화가 났을까, 아니면 기뻤을까?"], ["Lord Steyne's visits continuing, his own ceased, and his wife was for refusing all further intercourse with that nobleman and declining the invitation to the charade-night which the marchioness sent to her; but Sir Pitt thought it was necessary to accept it, as his Royal Highness would be there.", "스타인 경의 방문이 이어지자 피트 자신의 방문은 끊겼고, 그 아내는 그 귀족과의 모든 왕래를 거절하려 했으며 후작 부인이 보내온 패러디극 공연의 밤 초대마저 사양하려 했다. 그러나 피트 경은 황태자 전하께서 거기 납시므로 그것은 받아들여야 한다고 생각했다."]], [["Although he went to the party in question, Sir Pitt quitted it very early, and his wife, too, was very glad to come away.", "그 연회에 가긴 했으나, 피트 경은 아주 일찌감치 자리를 떴고, 그 아내 역시 일어서게 되어 무척 기뻐했다."], ["Becky hardly so much as spoke to him or noticed her sister-in-law.", "베키는 그에게 말을 건네기는커녕 동서를 거들떠보지도 않다시피 했다."], ["Pitt Crawley declared her behaviour was monstrously indecorous, reprobated in strong terms the habit of play-acting and fancy dressing as highly unbecoming a British female, and after the charades were over, took his brother Rawdon severely to task for appearing himself and allowing his wife to join in such improper exhibitions.", "피트 크롤리는 그녀의 행실이 터무니없이 무례하다고 단언했고, 연극을 흉내 내고 분장 차림을 하는 그 버릇이 영국 여성에게 도무지 어울리지 않는다고 강한 어조로 나무랐으며, 패러디극이 끝나자 동생 로던을 호되게 꾸짖었다. 제가 직접 무대에 나서고, 또 제 아내가 그런 부적절한 구경거리에 끼어들도록 내버려 두었다는 것이었다."]], [["Rawdon said she should not join in any more such amusements--but indeed, and perhaps from hints from his elder brother and sister, he had already become a very watchful and exemplary domestic character.", "로던은 그녀가 다시는 그런 놀음에 끼지 못하게 하겠노라고 말했다. 그런데 사실 그는, 어쩌면 형과 형수의 귀띔에서 비롯된 것이겠으나, 이미 더없이 빈틈없고 모범적인 가장이 되어 있었다."], ["He left off his clubs and billiards.", "그는 클럽도 당구도 그만두었다."], ["He never left home.", "결코 집을 비우는 법이 없었다."], ["He took Becky out to drive; he went laboriously with her to all her parties.", "그는 베키를 마차에 태워 데리고 다녔고, 그녀의 모든 연회에 꾸역꾸역 따라다녔다."], ["Whenever my Lord Steyne called, he was sure to find the Colonel.", "스타인 경이 찾아올 때면 영락없이 대령이 그 자리에 있었다."], ["And when Becky proposed to go out without her husband, or received invitations for herself, he peremptorily ordered her to refuse them: and there was that in the gentleman's manner which enforced obedience.", "그리고 베키가 남편 없이 외출하려 들거나 저 혼자 초대를 받으면, 그는 단호하게 그녀에게 거절하라고 명했다. 그리고 이 신사의 태도에는 복종을 강제하는 무언가가 깃들어 있었다."], ["Little Becky, to do her justice, was charmed with Rawdon's gallantry.", "자그마한 베키는, 공평하게 말하자면, 로던의 그 의젓한 태도에 매료되었다."], ["If he was surly, she never was.", "그가 시무룩할 때도 그녀는 결코 그러지 않았다."], ["Whether friends were present or absent, she had always a kind smile for him and was attentive to his pleasure and comfort.", "벗들이 있든 없든 그녀는 늘 그에게 다정한 미소를 지어 보였고, 그의 즐거움과 안락을 살뜰히 챙겼다. 그것은 결혼 초의 나날이 다시 돌아온 듯했다."], ["It was the early days of their marriage over again: the same good humour, prevenances, merriment, and artless confidence and regard.", "똑같은 명랑함, 살가운 배려, 즐거움, 그리고 꾸밈없는 믿음과 정이었다. \"마차에 그 어리석은 늙은 브리그스 대신 당신이 곁에 있으니 얼마나 더 즐거운지 몰라요!\""], ["\"How much pleasanter it is,\"", "그녀는 이렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["she would say, \"to have you by my side in the carriage than that foolish old Briggs!", "\"우리 늘 이렇게 지내요, 사랑하는 로던. 우리한테 돈만 있다면 얼마나 좋을까, 얼마나 늘 행복할까!\""], ["Let us always go on so, dear Rawdon.", "그는 저녁을 든 뒤 의자에 앉은 채 잠들곤 했다."], ["How nice it would be, and how happy we should always be, if we had but the money!\"", "그는 맞은편의, 초췌하고 지치고 무서운 그 얼굴을 보지 못했다."], ["He fell asleep after dinner in his chair; he did not see the face opposite to him, haggard, weary, and terrible; it lighted up with fresh candid smiles when he woke.", "그가 깨어나면 그 얼굴은 새삼 천진한 미소로 환히 빛났다. 그 얼굴은 명랑하게 그에게 입을 맞추었다."], ["It kissed him gaily. He wondered that he had ever had suspicions.", "그는 어쩌다 자기가 의심 따위를 품었던가 의아해했다."], ["No, he never had suspicions; all those dumb doubts and surly misgivings which had been gathering on his mind were mere idle jealousies.", "아니, 그는 한 번도 의심한 적 없었다. 그의 마음속에 쌓여 가던 그 모든 말 없는 의구심과 시무룩한 불안이라고는 그저 부질없는 질투에 지나지 않았다."], ["She was fond of him; she always had been.", "그녀는 그를 사랑했다. 늘 그래 왔다."], ["As for her shining in society, it was no fault of hers; she was formed to shine there.", "그녀가 사교계에서 빛나는 것으로 말하면, 그것은 그녀의 잘못이 아니었다. 그녀는 거기서 빛나도록 타고난 사람이었다."], ["Was there any woman who could talk, or sing, or do anything like her?", "그녀처럼 말하고, 노래하고, 무엇이든 해낼 수 있는 여인이 어디 또 있단 말인가?"], ["If she would but like the boy!", "그녀가 그 아이만 좋아해 준다면!"], ["Rawdon thought.", "하고 로던은 생각했다."], ["But the mother and son never could be brought together.", "그러나 어미와 아들은 도무지 화목하게 어울리지 못했다."]], [["And it was while Rawdon's mind was agitated with these doubts and perplexities that the incident occurred which was mentioned in the last chapter, and the unfortunate Colonel found himself a prisoner away from home.", "그리고 로던의 마음이 이런 의심과 갈피 모를 혼란으로 어지럽던 바로 그 무렵, 앞 장에서 이야기한 그 사건이 벌어졌으니, 가엾은 대령은 집에서 멀리 떨어진 채 갇힌 몸이 되고 만 것이다."]], [["A Rescue and a Catastrophe\nFriend Rawdon drove on then to Mr. Moss's mansion in Cursitor Street, and was duly inducted into that dismal place of hospitality.", "친구 로던은 그 길로 마차를 몰아 커시터 거리에 있는 모스 씨의 저택으로 향했고, 그 음울한 환대의 장소로 정식으로 안내되었다."], ["Morning was breaking over the cheerful house-tops of Chancery Lane as the rattling cab woke up the echoes there.", "덜컹거리는 마차가 그곳의 메아리를 깨울 무렵, 챈서리 거리의 활기찬 지붕들 위로 아침이 밝아 오고 있었다."], ["A little pink-eyed Jew-boy, with a head as ruddy as the rising morn, let the party into the house, and Rawdon was welcomed to the ground-floor apartments by Mr.", "떠오르는 아침처럼 발그레한 머리에 눈이 발그레한 자그마한 유대인 소년이 일행을 집 안으로 들였고, 로던은 길동무이자 주인인 모스 씨에게 일 층 방으로 환영받았다."], ["Moss, his travelling companion and host, who cheerfully asked him if he would like a glass of something warm after his drive.", "모스 씨는 마차를 타고 오느라 고생했으니 따뜻한 것을 한 잔 들겠느냐고 명랑하게 물었다."]], [["The Colonel was not so depressed as some mortals would be, who, quitting a palace and a placens uxor, find themselves barred into a spunging-house; for, if the truth must be told, he had been a lodger at Mr.", "대저택과 사랑스러운 아내를 떠나 채무자 임시 구류소에 갇히는 신세가 되면 어떤 사람은 풀이 죽기 마련이지만, 대령은 그만큼 의기소침하지는 않았다."], ["Moss's establishment once or twice before.", "사실대로 말하자면, 그는 전에도 한두 번 모스 씨의 시설에 묵은 적이 있었기 때문이다."], ["We have not thought it necessary in the previous course of this narrative to mention these trivial little domestic incidents: but the reader may be assured that they can't unfrequently occur in the life of a man who lives on nothing a year.", "우리는 이 이야기를 풀어 오는 가운데 이런 사소하고 자질구레한 집안 사건들을 굳이 언급할 필요가 없다고 여겼다. 그러나 독자께서는 일 년에 한 푼도 안 들이고 살아가는 사람의 인생에서 이런 일이 심심찮게 일어날 수 있음을 분명히 믿으셔도 좋다."]], [["Upon his first visit to Mr.", "모스 씨를 처음 찾았을 때, 당시 독신이었던 대령은 고모의 너그러운 처사 덕에 풀려났다."], ["Moss, the Colonel, then a bachelor, had been liberated by the generosity of his aunt; on the second mishap, little Becky, with the greatest spirit and kindness, had borrowed a sum of money from Lord Southdown and had coaxed her husband's creditor (who was her shawl, velvet-gown, lace pocket-handkerchief, trinket, and gim-crack purveyor, indeed) to take a portion of the sum claimed and Rawdon's promissory note for the remainder: so on both these occasions the capture and release had been conducted with the utmost gallantry on all sides, and Moss and the Colonel were therefore on the very best of terms.", "두 번째 불운 때는 어린 베키가 더없는 기백과 친절을 발휘해 사우스다운 경에게서 얼마간 돈을 빌렸고, 남편의 채권자(사실 이자는 그녀에게 숄이며 벨벳 가운이며 레이스 손수건이며 장신구며 자잘한 패물 따위를 대 주던 사람이었다)를 구슬려 청구액 가운데 일부와 나머지에 대한 로던의 약속어음을 받아 가게 했다. 그리하여 이 두 번의 경우 모두 체포와 석방이 사방의 더없는 호기로움 속에 이루어졌고, 그래서 모스와 대령은 더없이 좋은 사이였다."]], [["\"You'll find your old bed, Colonel, and everything comfortable,\"", "\"전에 쓰시던 침대를 그대로 쓰실 거예요, 대령님. 모든 게 다 편안하답니다.\""], ["that gentleman said, \"as I may honestly say.", "그 신사가 말했다. \"정직하게 말씀드릴 수 있어요."], ["You may be pretty sure its kep aired, and by the best of company, too.", "잘 환기해 두었으니 안심하셔도 되고, 게다가 최고급 손님들이 쓰던 거랍니다."], ["It was slep in the night afore last by the Honorable Capting Famish, of the Fiftieth Dragoons, whose Mar took him out, after a fortnight, jest to punish him, she said.", "그저께 밤에는 제오십 용기병 연대 소속의 페미시 대위님이 주무셨지요. 그분 어머님이 보름 만에 데려가셨는데, 벌을 좀 줘야겠다고 하시더라고요."], ["But, Law bless you, I promise you, he punished my champagne, and had a party ere every night--reglar tip-top swells, down from the clubs and the West End--Capting Ragg, the Honorable Deuceace, who lives in the Temple, and some fellers as knows a good glass of wine, I warrant you.", "그런데, 원, 정말이지, 그분은 제 샴페인한테 벌을 주셨고요, 밤마다 파티를 여셨죠. 클럽이며 웨스트엔드에서 내려온 어엿한 일류 멋쟁이들이었어요."], ["I've got a Doctor of Diwinity upstairs, five gents in the coffee-room, and Mrs.", "래그 대위님, 템플에 사시는 듀세이스 명예 귀하, 그리고 술맛 좀 아는 양반들이었다니까요, 장담합니다."], ["Moss has a tably-dy-hoty at half-past five, and a little cards or music afterwards, when we shall be most happy to see you.\"", "위층엔 신학 박사님 한 분, 휴게실엔 신사분이 다섯, 그리고 모스 부인이 다섯 시 반에 정식을 차립니다. 그 뒤엔 카드놀이나 음악도 좀 즐기는데, 그때 대령님을 뵙게 되면 더없이 기쁘겠습니다요.\""]], [["\"I'll ring when I want anything,\"", "\"필요한 게 있으면 종을 울리지.\""], ["said Rawdon and went quietly to his bedroom.", "로던은 그렇게 말하고 조용히 자기 침실로 들어갔다."], ["He was an old soldier, we have said, and not to be disturbed by any little shocks of fate.", "앞서 말했듯 그는 노련한 군인이라 운명의 자잘한 충격쯤에 흔들릴 사람이 아니었다."], ["A weaker man would have sent off a letter to his wife on the instant of his capture.", "더 나약한 사람이었다면 잡혀 들어온 그 순간 곧장 아내에게 편지를 보냈으리라."], ["\"But what is the use of disturbing her night's rest?\"", "\"그러나 굳이 밤잠을 깨워 봐야 무슨 소용인가?\""], ["thought Rawdon.", "로던은 생각했다."], ["\"She won't know whether I am in my room or not.", "\"내가 방에 있는지 없는지도 모를 텐데."], ["It will be time enough to write to her when she has had her sleep out, and I have had mine.", "그 사람이 푹 자고 나서, 나도 좀 자고 나서 편지를 써도 늦지 않아."], ["It's only a hundred-and-seventy, and the deuce is in it if we can't raise that.\"", "고작 백칠십 파운드인데, 그걸 못 마련할 리야 없지.\""], ["And so, thinking about little Rawdon (whom he would not have know that he was in such a queer place), the Colonel turned into the bed lately occupied by Captain Famish and fell asleep.", "그래서 대령은, 자기가 이런 묘한 곳에 와 있다는 걸 알리고 싶지 않은 어린 로던을 떠올리며, 얼마 전 페미시 대위가 쓰던 그 침대로 들어가 잠이 들었다."], ["It was ten o'clock when he woke up, and the ruddy-headed youth brought him, with conscious pride, a fine silver dressing-case, wherewith he might perform the operation of shaving.", "잠에서 깨니 열 시였고, 발그레한 머리의 소년이 자랑스러운 표정으로 면도를 할 수 있도록 멋진 은제 화장 도구함을 가져다주었다."], ["Indeed Mr. Moss's house, though somewhat dirty, was splendid throughout.", "사실 모스 씨의 집은 다소 지저분하기는 해도 구석구석이 호화로웠다."], ["There were dirty trays, and wine-coolers en permanence on the sideboard, huge dirty gilt cornices, with dingy yellow satin hangings to the barred windows which looked into Cursitor Street--vast and dirty gilt picture frames surrounding pieces sporting and sacred, all of which works were by the greatest masters--and fetched the greatest prices, too, in the bill transactions, in the course of which they were sold and bought over and over again.", "찬장에는 지저분한 쟁반과 포도주 냉각기가 늘 놓여 있었고, 커다랗고 지저분한 금박 처마 장식이 둘러져 있었으며, 커시터 거리가 내다보이는 창살 박힌 창문에는 칙칙한 노란 공단 휘장이 드리워 있었다. 거대하고 지저분한 금박 액자에 끼워진 사냥 그림과 종교화가 사방에 걸려 있었는데, 죄다 일류 거장들의 작품이라 어음 거래에서도 최고가에 팔렸으며, 그 거래 과정에서 몇 번이고 거듭 사고팔리는 것이었다."], ["The Colonel's breakfast was served to him in the same dingy and gorgeous plated ware.", "대령의 아침 식사도 똑같이 칙칙하면서도 호화로운 도금 식기에 담겨 나왔다."], ["Miss Moss, a dark-eyed maid in curl-papers, appeared with the teapot, and, smiling, asked the Colonel how he had slep?", "종이로 머리를 만 검은 눈동자의 하녀 모스 양이 찻주전자를 들고 나타나, 빙긋 웃으며 대령에게 잘 주무셨느냐고 물었다."], ["And she brought him in the Morning Post, with the names of all the great people who had figured at Lord Steyne's entertainment the night before.", "그리고 그녀는 전날 밤 스타인 경의 연회에서 활약한 온갖 고관대작의 이름이 실린 모닝 포스트를 가져다주었다."], ["It contained a brilliant account of the festivities and of the beautiful and accomplished Mrs. Rawdon Crawley's admirable personifications.", "거기에는 그 잔치에 대한 화려한 기사와, 아름답고 재능 넘치는 로던 크롤리 부인의 감탄스러운 연기에 대한 기사가 실려 있었다."]], [["After a lively chat with this lady (who sat on the edge of the breakfast table in an easy attitude displaying the drapery of her stocking and an ex-white satin shoe, which was down at heel), Colonel Crawley called for pens and ink, and paper, and being asked how many sheets, chose one which was brought to him between Miss Moss's own finger and thumb.", "이 숙녀(그녀는 편안한 자세로 아침 식탁 가장자리에 걸터앉아 스타킹의 주름과 뒤축이 닳아빠진 한물간 흰 공단 신발을 드러내고 있었다)와 활기찬 잡담을 나눈 뒤, 크롤리 대령은 펜과 잉크와 종이를 청했고, 몇 장이 필요하냐는 물음에 한 장만 골랐는데 그것은 모스 양이 직접 엄지와 검지로 집어 건네주었다."], ["Many a sheet had that dark-eyed damsel brought in; many a poor fellow had scrawled and blotted hurried lines of entreaty and paced up and down that awful room until his messenger brought back the reply.", "검은 눈동자의 그 처녀가 그렇게 들여온 종이가 한두 장이 아니었다. 가엾은 사내들이 그 무시무시한 방을 서성이며 황급히 애원의 글을 갈겨쓰고 얼룩지게 한 끝에, 심부름꾼이 답장을 받아 오기를 기다린 일이 한두 번이 아니었다."], ["Poor men always use messengers instead of the post.", "가난한 사람들은 늘 우편 대신 심부름꾼을 쓰는 법이다."], ["Who has not had their letters, with the wafers wet, and the announcement that a person is waiting in the hall?", "봉인 풀이 채 마르지도 않은 편지를 받아 본 적이 없는 이가 어디 있겠으며, 누가 현관에서 기다리고 있다는 전갈을 받아 본 적이 없는 이가 어디 있겠는가?"]], [["Now on the score of his application, Rawdon had not many misgivings.", "그런데 자신의 청을 두고 로던은 별다른 걱정을 하지 않았다."]], [["DEAR BECKY, (Rawdon wrote)", "베키에게 (로던은 이렇게 썼다)"]], [["I HOPE YOU SLEPT WELL.", "잘 잤기를 바라오."], ["Don't be FRIGHTENED if I don't bring you in your COFFY.", "내가 당신 커피를 갖다주지 못해도 놀라지 마시오."], ["Last night as I was coming home smoaking, I met with an ACCADENT.", "어젯밤 담배를 피우며 집으로 오던 길에 사고를 당했소."], ["I was NABBED by Moss of Cursitor Street--from whose GILT AND SPLENDID PARLER I write this--the same that had me this time two years.", "커시터 거리의 모스한테 붙잡혔는데, 지금 그자의 금박 입힌 으리으리한 응접실에서 이 편지를 쓰고 있소. 두 해 전 이맘때 나를 잡아갔던 바로 그자요."], ["Miss Moss brought in my tea--she is grown very FAT, and, as usual, had her STOCKENS DOWN AT HEAL.", "모스 양이 차를 들여왔는데, 아주 살이 쪘고 여전히 스타킹 뒤축이 흘러내려 있더이다."]], [["It's Nathan's business--a hundred-and-fifty--with costs, hundred-and-seventy. Please send me my desk and some CLOTHS--I'm in pumps and a white tye (something like Miss M's stockings)--I've seventy in it. And as soon as you get this, Drive to Nathan's--offer him seventy-five down, and ASK HIM TO RENEW--say I'll take wine--we may as well have some dinner sherry; but not PICTURS, they're too dear.", "네이선 일이오. 백오십 파운드인데, 비용까지 하면 백칠십이오. 부탁이니 내 책상과 옷가지 좀 보내 주오. 지금 무도화에 흰 넥타이 차림이오(모스 양 스타킹 꼴이랑 비슷하지). 책상 안에 칠십 파운드가 있소. 이 편지를 받는 즉시 네이선한테 마차를 몰고 가서, 칠십오 파운드를 당장 내겠다고 하고 갱신을 부탁하시오. 포도주는 받겠다고 하고, 저녁에 마실 셰리주쯤은 받아 두는 게 좋겠소. 다만 그림은 안 되오, 너무 비싸니까."]], [["If he won't stand it.", "그자가 안 들어주면, 내 회중시계와 당신 물건 중 떼어 줄 만한 걸 챙겨서 볼스한테 보내시오."], ["Take my ticker and such of your things as you can SPARE, and send them to Balls--we must, of coarse, have the sum to-night.", "어쨌든 오늘 밤 안으로 그 돈을 마련해야 하오. 미루는 건 안 될 일이오, 내일이 일요일이니 말이오."], ["It won't do to let it stand over, as to-morrow's Sunday; the beds here are not very CLEAN, and there may be other things out against me--I'm glad it an't Rawdon's Saturday for coming home.", "이곳 침대는 그리 깨끗하지 못하고, 나를 상대로 다른 청구가 또 들어와 있을지도 모르오. 로던이 집에 오는 토요일이 아니라 다행이오."], ["God bless you.", "신의 가호가 있기를."]], [["Yours in haste, R. C.", "바삐 쓰며, R."], ["P.S.", "C."], ["Make haste and come.", "추신. 서둘러 와 주오."]], [["This letter, sealed with a wafer, was dispatched by one of the messengers who are always hanging about Mr. Moss's establishment, and Rawdon, having seen him depart, went out in the court-yard and smoked his cigar with a tolerably easy mind--in spite of the bars overhead--for Mr.", "봉인 풀로 봉한 이 편지는 모스 씨의 시설 근처를 늘 어슬렁거리는 심부름꾼 가운데 하나가 받아 갔고, 로던은 그가 떠나는 것을 지켜본 뒤 마당으로 나가 머리 위 창살에도 아랑곳없이 제법 편안한 마음으로 시가를 피웠다."], ["Moss's court-yard is railed in like a cage, lest the gentlemen who are boarding with him should take a fancy to escape from his hospitality.", "모스 씨의 마당은 새장처럼 철책으로 둘러싸여 있었는데, 그 집에 묵는 신사들이 주인의 환대에서 달아나고 싶은 마음이 들지 않도록 한 것이었다."]], [["Three hours, he calculated, would be the utmost time required, before Becky should arrive and open his prison doors, and he passed these pretty cheerfully in smoking, in reading the paper, and in the coffee-room with an acquaintance, Captain Walker, who happened to be there, and with whom he cut for sixpences for some hours, with pretty equal luck on either side.", "베키가 도착해 자신의 감옥 문을 열어 주기까지는 길어야 세 시간이면 되리라고 그는 헤아렸다. 그리고 이 시간을 그는 담배를 피우고 신문을 읽으며 제법 명랑하게 보냈고, 마침 그곳에 있던 지인 워커 대위와 함께 휴게실에서 몇 시간 동안 육 펜스씩을 걸고 카드를 떼었는데, 양쪽 다 운이 엇비슷했다."]], [["But the day passed away and no messenger returned--no Becky. Mr.", "그러나 하루가 다 지나가도록 심부름꾼은 돌아오지 않았다."], ["Moss's tably-dy-hoty was served at the appointed hour of half-past five, when such of the gentlemen lodging in the house as could afford to pay for the banquet came and partook of it in the splendid front parlour before described, and with which Mr.", "베키도 오지 않았다. 모스 씨의 정식은 정해진 시각인 다섯 시 반에 차려졌고, 그 잔치 값을 치를 형편이 되는 하숙객 신사들이 앞서 묘사한 그 으리으리한 앞쪽 응접실로 와서 함께 들었다."], ["Crawley's temporary lodging communicated, when Miss M. (Miss Hem, as her papa called her) appeared without the curl-papers of the morning, and Mrs.", "크롤리 씨의 임시 처소는 그 응접실과 통해 있었다."], ["Hem did the honours of a prime boiled leg of mutton and turnips, of which the Colonel ate with a very faint appetite. Asked whether he would \"stand\"", "이때 모스 양(그녀의 아버지는 그녀를 헴 양이라 불렀다)이 아침의 종이 머리말이는 떼고 나타났고, 헴 부인은 일등급 삶은 양 다리와 순무 요리의 시중을 들었는데 대령은 영 입맛이 없는 채로 그것을 먹었다."], ["a bottle of champagne for the company, he consented, and the ladies drank to his 'ealth, and Mr. Moss, in the most polite manner, \"looked towards him.\"", "일행에게 샴페인 한 병을 \"쏘겠느냐\"는 물음에 그는 응했고, 숙녀들은 그의 \"건강\"을 빌며 마셨으며, 모스 씨는 더없이 정중한 태도로 \"그를 향해 잔을 들었다.\""]], [["In the midst of this repast, however, the doorbell was heard--young Moss of the ruddy hair rose up with the keys and answered the summons, and coming back, told the Colonel that the messenger had returned with a bag, a desk and a letter, which he gave him.", "그런데 이 식사가 한창일 때 초인종 소리가 들렸다. 발그레한 머리의 모스 청년이 열쇠를 들고 일어나 응대하더니, 돌아와서는 심부름꾼이 가방과 책상과 편지를 가지고 돌아왔다고 대령에게 알리며 편지를 건넸다."], ["\"No ceramony, Colonel, I beg,\" said Mrs.", "\"격식 차리지 마세요, 대령님, 부디.\""], ["Moss with a wave of her hand, and he opened the letter rather tremulously.", "모스 부인이 손을 내저으며 말했고, 그는 다소 떨리는 손으로 편지를 뜯었다."], ["It was a beautiful letter, highly scented, on a pink paper, and with a light green seal.", "향내가 짙게 밴 아름다운 편지로, 분홍색 종이에 연두색 봉인이 찍혀 있었다."]], [["MON PAUVRE CHER PETIT, (Mrs. Crawley wrote)", "가엾은 내 사랑 작은 양반에게 (크롤리 부인은 이렇게 썼다)"]], [["I could not sleep ONE WINK for thinking of what had become of my odious old monstre, and only got to rest in the morning after sending for Mr.", "밉살스럽고 늙은 내 괴물 양반한테 무슨 일이 생겼나 생각하느라 한숨도 못 잤어요."], ["Blench (for I was in a fever), who gave me a composing draught and left orders with Finette that I should be disturbed ON NO ACCOUNT.", "아침이 되어서야 겨우 잠들었는데, (열이 펄펄 끓어서) 블렌치 씨를 불렀더니 진정제를 주고는 무슨 일이 있어도 절대 나를 깨우지 말라고 피네트에게 일러두고 갔지 뭐예요."], ["So that my poor old man's messenger, who had bien mauvaise mine Finette says, and sentoit le Genievre, remained in the hall for some hours waiting my bell.", "그래서 내 가엾은 양반의 심부름꾼은, 피네트 말로는 행색이 영 험악하고 진 냄새를 풍기더라는데, 내가 종을 울리기를 기다리며 몇 시간이나 현관에 죽치고 있었답니다."], ["You may fancy my state when I read your poor dear old ill-spelt letter.", "가엾고 사랑스러운 당신의 철자투성이 편지를 읽었을 때 내 심정이 어땠을지 짐작이나 가요?"]], [["Ill as I was, I instantly called for the carriage, and as soon as I was dressed (though I couldn't drink a drop of chocolate--I assure you I couldn't without my monstre to bring it to me), I drove ventre a terre to Nathan's.", "몸이 그렇게 아픈데도 나는 곧장 마차를 부르라고 했고, 옷을 차려입자마자(초콜릿은 한 방울도 마실 수 없었어요. 정말이지 내 괴물 양반이 가져다주지 않으면 못 마신다니까요) 쏜살같이 네이선한테 달려갔어요."], ["I saw him--I wept--I cried--I fell at his odious knees.", "그를 만나서, 울고, 부르짖고, 그 밉살스러운 무릎 앞에 엎드렸지요. 그래도 그 끔찍한 인간은 조금도 마음을 누그러뜨리지 않더군요."], ["Nothing would mollify the horrid man.", "돈을 다 받든가, 아니면 내 가엾은 괴물 양반을 감옥에 가둬 두겠다지 뭐예요. 나는 큰아버지 댁에 그 서글픈 방문을 할 작정으로 집에 돌아왔어요(내 장신구를 죄다 당신 마음대로 쓰셔도 좋아요."], ["He would have all the money, he said, or keep my poor monstre in prison.", "다 합쳐도 백 파운드는 안 되겠지만요. 알다시피 몇 개는 이미 그 큰아버지한테 가 있으니까요)."], ["I drove home with the intention of paying that triste visite chez mon oncle (when every trinket I have should be at your disposal though they would not fetch a hundred pounds, for some, you know, are with ce cher oncle already), and found Milor there with the Bulgarian old sheep-faced monster, who had come to compliment me upon last night's performances.", "그런데 집에 와 보니 그 양 같은 얼굴을 한 늙은 불가리아 괴물이랑 같이 백작님이 와 계셨어요. 어젯밤 공연에 대한 찬사를 전하러 오신 거였죠."], ["Paddington came in, too, drawling and lisping and twiddling his hair; so did Champignac, and his chef--everybody with foison of compliments and pretty speeches--plaguing poor me, who longed to be rid of them, and was thinking every moment of the time of mon pauvre prisonnier.", "패딩턴도 와서 느릿느릿 혀짤배기소리를 내며 제 머리칼을 배배 꼬고 있었고, 샹피냐크도, 그의 요리장도 왔어요. 다들 찬사며 듣기 좋은 소리를 한 무더기씩 늘어놓으며, 그들을 어서 떼어 버리고 싶고 시시각각 내 가엾은 죄수 양반의 시간만 생각하던 가엾은 나를 들볶았답니다."]], [["When they were gone, I went down on my knees to Milor; told him we were going to pawn everything, and begged and prayed him to give me two hundred pounds.", "그들이 다 가고 나서, 나는 백작님 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 우리가 모든 걸 전당포에 잡혀야 할 처지라고 말씀드리며 이백 파운드를 빌려 달라고 간청하고 또 애원했어요."], ["He pish'd and psha'd in a fury--told me not to be such a fool as to pawn--and said he would see whether he could lend me the money.", "그분은 노발대발하며 흥, 쳇 하시더니, 그런 어리석은 짓 하지 말라고, 전당포에 잡히지 말라고 하셨어요. 그러고는 돈을 빌려줄 수 있을지 알아보겠다고 하시더군요."], ["At last he went away, promising that he would send it me in the morning: when I will bring it to my poor old monster with a kiss from his affectionate", "마침내 그분은 아침에 보내 주겠노라 약속하며 떠나셨어요. 그러면 그 돈을 내 가엾고 늙은 괴물 양반한테, 그분을 사랑하는 베키의 입맞춤과 함께 가져다드리겠어요."]], [["I am writing in bed.", "베키\n침대에 누워서 쓰고 있어요."], ["Oh I have such a headache and such a heartache!", "아, 머리도 너무 아프고 가슴도 너무 아파요!"]], [["When Rawdon read over this letter, he turned so red and looked so savage that the company at the table d'hote easily perceived that bad news had reached him.", "로던이 이 편지를 다 읽고 나자 얼굴이 어찌나 붉어지고 표정이 어찌나 험악해졌던지, 정식 식탁에 앉은 일행은 그에게 나쁜 소식이 전해졌음을 쉽게 알아챘다."], ["All his suspicions, which he had been trying to banish, returned upon him.", "떨쳐 내려 애써 오던 온갖 의심이 그에게 되살아났다."], ["She could not even go out and sell her trinkets to free him.", "그녀는 자기를 풀어 주려고 밖에 나가 장신구를 팔 생각조차 하지 않았다."], ["She could laugh and talk about compliments paid to her, whilst he was in prison.", "그가 감옥에 갇혀 있는 동안 그녀는 자기에게 쏟아진 찬사를 두고 웃고 떠들 수 있었던 것이다."], ["Who had put him there?", "그를 거기 처넣은 자가 누구인가?"], ["Wenham had walked with him.", "웬엄이 그와 함께 걸었었다."], ["Was there....", "혹시……"], ["He could hardly bear to think of what he suspected.", "그는 자신이 의심하는 바를 차마 생각조차 할 수가 없었다."], ["Leaving the room hurriedly, he ran into his own--opened his desk, wrote two hurried lines, which he directed to Sir Pitt or Lady Crawley, and bade the messenger carry them at once to Gaunt Street, bidding him to take a cab, and promising him a guinea if he was back in an hour.", "그는 황급히 방을 나와 자기 방으로 뛰어 들어가 책상을 열고 급히 두 줄을 적은 뒤, 그것을 피트 경 또는 크롤리 부인 앞으로 적었다. 그러고는 심부름꾼에게 마차를 잡아 타고 그것을 당장 곤트 거리로 가져가라 이르며, 한 시간 안에 돌아오면 일 기니를 주겠노라 약속했다."]], [["In the note he besought his dear brother and sister, for the sake of God, for the sake of his dear child and his honour, to come to him and relieve him from his difficulty.", "쪽지에서 그는 사랑하는 형과 형수에게, 하느님을 봐서, 사랑하는 자기 아이와 자기 명예를 봐서, 자기에게 와서 이 곤경에서 벗어나게 해 달라고 간청했다."], ["He was in prison, he wanted a hundred pounds to set him free--he entreated them to come to him.", "자기는 감옥에 갇혀 있고 풀려나려면 백 파운드가 필요하다고, 부디 와 달라고 애원했다."]], [["He went back to the dining-room after dispatching his messenger and called for more wine.", "심부름꾼을 보낸 뒤 그는 식당으로 돌아와 포도주를 더 청했다."], ["He laughed and talked with a strange boisterousness, as the people thought.", "그는 사람들이 보기에 묘하게 떠들썩한 태도로 웃고 떠들었다."], ["Sometimes he laughed madly at his own fears and went on drinking for an hour, listening all the while for the carriage which was to bring his fate back.", "때로는 자기 자신의 두려움을 두고 미친 듯이 웃기도 하면서, 한 시간 동안 줄곧 자신의 운명을 실어 올 마차 소리에 귀를 기울이며 술을 들이켰다."]], [["At the expiration of that time, wheels were heard whirling up to the gate--the young janitor went out with his gate-keys.", "그 시간이 다 지났을 무렵, 바퀴 소리가 문을 향해 굴러오는 것이 들렸다. 젊은 문지기가 문 열쇠를 들고 나갔다."], ["It was a lady whom he let in at the bailiff's door.", "그가 채무 집행관용 문으로 들여보낸 것은 한 숙녀였다."]], [["\"Colonel Crawley,\"", "\"크롤리 대령님을.\""], ["she said, trembling very much.", "그녀가 몹시 떨면서 말했다."], ["He, with a knowing look, locked the outer door upon her--then unlocked and opened the inner one, and calling out, \"Colonel, you're wanted,\"", "그는 무언가 안다는 듯한 표정으로 그녀 뒤로 바깥문을 잠그고는, 안쪽 문을 풀어 열고 \"대령님, 손님이오.\""], ["led her into the back parlour, which he occupied.", "하고 외친 뒤, 그녀를 자신이 쓰는 안쪽 응접실로 안내했다."]], [["Rawdon came in from the dining-parlour where all those people were carousing, into his back room; a flare of coarse light following him into the apartment where the lady stood, still very nervous.", "로던은 그 모든 사람이 흥청대는 식당에서 자기 안쪽 방으로 들어왔다. 거친 빛 한 줄기가 그를 따라, 여전히 몹시 긴장한 채 숙녀가 서 있는 방으로 흘러들었다."]], [["\"It is I, Rawdon,\"", "\"저예요, 로던.\""], ["she said in a timid voice, which she strove to render cheerful.", "그녀가 애써 명랑한 척하며 겁먹은 목소리로 말했다."], ["\"It is Jane.\"", "\"제인이에요.\""], ["Rawdon was quite overcome by that kind voice and presence.", "로던은 그 다정한 목소리와 존재에 완전히 압도되었다."], ["He ran up to her--caught her in his arms--gasped out some inarticulate words of thanks and fairly sobbed on her shoulder.", "그는 그녀에게 달려가, 두 팔로 끌어안고, 알아들을 수 없는 감사의 말을 헐떡이며 내뱉더니, 정말로 그녀의 어깨에 얼굴을 묻고 흐느꼈다."], ["She did not know the cause of his emotion.", "그녀는 그가 그토록 격해진 까닭을 알지 못했다."]], [["The bills of Mr. Moss were quickly settled, perhaps to the disappointment of that gentleman, who had counted on having the Colonel as his guest over Sunday at least; and Jane, with beaming smiles and happiness in her eyes, carried away Rawdon from the bailiff's house, and they went homewards in the cab in which she had hastened to his release.", "모스 씨의 청구액은 곧 정산되었는데, 적어도 일요일까지는 대령을 손님으로 붙들어 둘 셈이던 그 신사에게는 어쩌면 실망스러운 일이었을 것이다."], ["\"Pitt was gone to a parliamentary dinner,\"", "환한 미소와 행복으로 눈을 반짝이며 제인은 채무 집행관의 집에서 로던을 데리고 나왔고, 두 사람은 그녀가 그를 풀어 주려고 황급히 타고 온 그 마차를 타고 집으로 향했다."], ["she said, \"when Rawdon's note came, and so, dear Rawdon, I--I came myself\"; and she put her kind hand in his.", "\"로던의 쪽지가 왔을 때 피트는 의회 만찬에 가고 없었어요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["Perhaps it was well for Rawdon Crawley that Pitt was away at that dinner.", "\"그래서, 로던, 제가, 제가 직접 온 거예요.\" 그러면서 그녀는 그의 손에 자신의 다정한 손을 포갰다."], ["Rawdon thanked his sister a hundred times, and with an ardour of gratitude which touched and almost alarmed that soft-hearted woman.", "어쩌면 피트가 그 만찬에 가 있었던 것이 로던 크롤리에게는 다행한 일이었으리라. 로던은 형수에게 백 번이고 고마워했는데, 그 감사의 열렬함이 그 마음씨 고운 여인을 감동시켰고 거의 놀라게 할 정도였다."], ["\"Oh,\"", "\"아아.\""], ["said he, in his rude, artless way, \"you--you don't know how I'm changed since I've known you, and--and little Rawdy.", "그가 투박하고 꾸밈없는 태도로 말했다. \"형수님은, 형수님은 모르실 거예요, 제가 형수님을, 그리고 어린 로디를 알고부터 얼마나 달라졌는지를요."], ["I--I'd like to change somehow.", "저는, 저는 어떻게든 달라지고 싶어요."], ["You see I want--I want--to be--\"", "보세요, 저는 되고 싶어요, 되고 싶다고요, 그러니까……\""], ["He did not finish the sentence, but she could interpret it.", "그는 말을 맺지 못했지만, 그녀는 그 뜻을 헤아릴 수 있었다."], ["And that night after he left her, and as she sat by her own little boy's bed, she prayed humbly for that poor way-worn sinner.", "그리고 그날 밤, 그가 떠난 뒤 자기 어린 아들의 침대맡에 앉아 그녀는 그 가엾고 세상에 지친 죄인을 위해 겸허히 기도했다."]], [["Rawdon left her and walked home rapidly. It was nine o'clock at night.", "로던은 그녀와 헤어져 빠른 걸음으로 집을 향해 걸었다."], ["He ran across the streets and the great squares of Vanity Fair, and at length came up breathless opposite his own house.", "밤 아홉 시였다. 그는 허영의 시장의 거리와 큰 광장들을 가로질러 달려, 마침내 숨을 헐떡이며 자기 집 맞은편에 다다랐다."], ["He started back and fell against the railings, trembling as he looked up.", "그는 흠칫 물러서며 난간에 기대섰고, 위를 올려다보며 부들부들 떨었다."], ["The drawing-room windows were blazing with light.", "응접실 창문들이 환한 불빛으로 타오르고 있었다."], ["She had said that she was in bed and ill.", "그녀는 자기가 아파서 침대에 누워 있다고 말했었다."], ["He stood there for some time, the light from the rooms on his pale face.", "그는 그 자리에 한동안 서 있었고, 방에서 새어 나온 불빛이 그의 창백한 얼굴 위로 떨어졌다."]], [["He took out his door-key and let himself into the house.", "그는 현관 열쇠를 꺼내 집 안으로 들어섰다. 위층 방에서 웃음소리가 들려왔다."], ["He could hear laughter in the upper rooms.", "그는 전날 밤 붙잡힐 때 입었던 무도복 차림 그대로였다."], ["He was in the ball-dress in which he had been captured the night before.", "그는 소리 없이 계단을 올라 계단 꼭대기에서 난간에 기대섰다."], ["He went silently up the stairs, leaning against the banisters at the stair-head.", "집 안에 움직이는 사람이라곤 아무도 없었다. 하인들이 죄다 내보내진 것이었다."], ["Nobody was stirring in the house besides--all the servants had been sent away.", "로던은 안에서 들려오는 웃음소리를, 웃음소리와 노랫소리를 들었다."], ["Rawdon heard laughter within--laughter and singing.", "베키가 전날 밤의 노래 한 자락을 부르고 있었다."], ["Becky was singing a snatch of the song of the night before; a hoarse voice shouted \"Brava!", "쉰 목소리가 \"브라보! 브라보!\""], ["Brava!\"--it was Lord Steyne's.", "하고 외쳤다. 스타인 경의 목소리였다."]], [["Rawdon opened the door and went in.", "로던은 문을 열고 들어갔다."], ["A little table with a dinner was laid out--and wine and plate.", "저녁상이 차려진 자그마한 탁자가 놓여 있었다. 포도주와 식기도."], ["Steyne was hanging over the sofa on which Becky sat.", "스타인은 베키가 앉아 있는 소파 위로 몸을 숙이고 있었다."], ["The wretched woman was in a brilliant full toilette, her arms and all her fingers sparkling with bracelets and rings, and the brilliants on her breast which Steyne had given her.", "그 가련한 여인은 휘황찬란한 정장 차림으로, 두 팔과 손가락마다 팔찌와 반지를 반짝였고, 가슴에는 스타인이 그녀에게 준 보석이 빛나고 있었다."], ["He had her hand in his, and was bowing over it to kiss it, when Becky started up with a faint scream as she caught sight of Rawdon's white face.", "그는 그녀의 손을 잡고 거기에 입 맞추려 몸을 숙이던 참이었는데, 그때 베키가 로던의 새하얀 얼굴을 알아채고는 가느다란 비명을 지르며 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["At the next instant she tried a smile, a horrid smile, as if to welcome her husband; and Steyne rose up, grinding his teeth, pale, and with fury in his looks.", "다음 순간 그녀는 남편을 반기려는 듯 미소를, 끔찍한 미소를 지어 보였다. 스타인은 이를 갈며, 창백한 얼굴에 분노를 띤 채 일어섰다."]], [["He, too, attempted a laugh--and came forward holding out his hand.", "그 역시 웃어 보이려 애쓰며 손을 내밀고 앞으로 나섰다."], ["\"What, come back!", "\"아니, 돌아왔구려!"], ["How d'ye do, Crawley?\"", "어떻게 지내시오, 크롤리?\""], ["he said, the nerves of his mouth twitching as he tried to grin at the intruder.", "그가 침입자에게 억지로 웃어 보이려 애쓰자 입가의 신경이 씰룩거렸다."]], [["There was that in Rawdon's face which caused Becky to fling herself before him.", "로던의 얼굴에 어린 무언가에 베키는 그의 앞으로 몸을 던졌다. \"저는 결백해요, 로던.\""], ["\"I am innocent, Rawdon,\"", "그녀가 말했다."], ["she said; \"before God, I am innocent.\"", "\"하느님 앞에 맹세코, 저는 결백해요.\""], ["She clung hold of his coat, of his hands; her own were all covered with serpents, and rings, and baubles.", "그녀는 그의 외투에, 그의 두 손에 매달렸다. 그녀 자신의 손은 온통 뱀 모양 장신구며 반지며 패물로 뒤덮여 있었다."], ["\"I am innocent.", "\"저는 결백해요."], ["Say I am innocent,\"", "제가 결백하다고 말해 주세요.\""], ["she said to Lord Steyne.", "그녀가 스타인 경에게 말했다."]], [["He thought a trap had been laid for him, and was as furious with the wife as with the husband.", "그는 자기에게 덫이 놓인 것이라 여겼고, 남편에게만큼이나 아내에게도 노발대발했다. \"네가 결백하다고?"], ["\"You innocent!", "빌어먹을.\""], ["Damn you,\" he screamed out.", "그가 비명처럼 소리쳤다."], ["\"You innocent!", "\"네가 결백하다고?"], ["Why every trinket you have on your body is paid for by me.", "네 몸에 걸친 장신구란 장신구는 죄다 내가 값을 치른 것들이다."], ["I have given you thousands of pounds, which this fellow has spent and for which he has sold you.", "나는 네게 수천 파운드를 주었고, 그걸 이자가 다 써 버렸고, 그 대가로 너를 팔아넘긴 거다."], ["Innocent, by ----!", "결백하다고, 빌어먹을!"], ["You're as innocent as your mother, the ballet-girl, and your husband the bully.", "네가 결백하긴, 발레 무희였던 네 어미나 불한당인 네 서방만큼이나 결백하겠지."], ["Don't think to frighten me as you have done others.", "다른 사람들한테 했듯이 나를 겁줄 생각일랑 마라. 비켜라, 이 사람아, 좀 지나가게.\""], ["Make way, sir, and let me pass\"; and Lord Steyne seized up his hat, and, with flame in his eyes, and looking his enemy fiercely in the face, marched upon him, never for a moment doubting that the other would give way.", "그러고는 스타인 경이 모자를 집어 들고, 눈에 불을 켠 채 적수의 얼굴을 사납게 노려보며 그를 향해 다가섰다. 상대가 길을 비켜 주리라는 것을 한순간도 의심치 않은 채로."]], [["But Rawdon Crawley springing out, seized him by the neckcloth, until Steyne, almost strangled, writhed and bent under his arm.", "그러나 로던 크롤리가 펄쩍 뛰어나와 그의 넥타이를 움켜쥐었고, 스타인은 거의 목이 졸려 그의 팔 아래에서 몸부림치며 구부러졌다."], ["\"You lie, you dog!\"", "\"거짓말 마라, 이 개 같은 놈아!\""], ["said Rawdon.", "로던이 말했다."], ["\"You lie, you coward and villain!\"", "\"거짓말 마라, 이 비겁하고 악랄한 놈아!\""], ["And he struck the Peer twice over the face with his open hand and flung him bleeding to the ground.", "그러고는 손바닥을 펴 그 귀족의 얼굴을 두 번 후려치고는, 피 흘리는 그를 바닥에 내동댕이쳤다."], ["It was all done before Rebecca could interpose.", "레베카가 끼어들 틈도 없이 모든 일이 벌어졌다."], ["She stood there trembling before him.", "그녀는 그 앞에 서서 부들부들 떨었다."], ["She admired her husband, strong, brave, and victorious.", "그녀는 강하고 용감하고 승리한 자기 남편에게 감탄했다."]], [["\"Come here,\"", "\"이리 와.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["She came up at once.", "그녀는 곧장 다가왔다."]], [["\"Take off those things.\"", "\"그것들을 벗어.\""], ["She began, trembling, pulling the jewels from her arms, and the rings from her shaking fingers, and held them all in a heap, quivering and looking up at him.", "그녀는 떨면서 팔에서 보석을 빼고 떨리는 손가락에서 반지를 빼기 시작하더니, 그것들을 한 무더기로 그러쥔 채 부들부들 떨며 그를 올려다보았다. \"내려놔.\""], ["\"Throw them down,\" he said, and she dropped them.", "그가 말하자 그녀는 그것들을 떨어뜨렸다."], ["He tore the diamond ornament out of her breast and flung it at Lord Steyne.", "그는 그녀의 가슴에서 다이아몬드 장신구를 잡아 뜯어 스타인 경에게 던졌다."], ["It cut him on his bald forehead.", "그것이 그의 벗어진 이마를 베었다."], ["Steyne wore the scar to his dying day.", "스타인은 죽는 날까지 그 흉터를 지니고 살았다."]], [["\"Come upstairs,\"", "\"위층으로 올라가.\""], ["Rawdon said to his wife.", "로던이 아내에게 말했다."], ["\"Don't kill me, Rawdon,\"", "\"죽이지 마세요, 로던.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["He laughed savagely.", "그는 사납게 웃었다."], ["\"I want to see if that man lies about the money as he has about me.", "\"저 인간이 돈에 대해서도 나에 대해서처럼 거짓말을 하는지 봐야겠어."], ["Has he given you any?\"", "저자가 당신한테 돈을 준 게 있나?\""]], [["\"No,\"", "\"없어요.\""], ["said Rebecca, \"that is--\"", "레베카가 말했다. \"그러니까……\""]], [["\"Give me your keys,\"", "\"열쇠를 내놔.\""], ["Rawdon answered, and they went out together.", "로던이 대답했고, 두 사람은 함께 나갔다."]], [["Rebecca gave him all the keys but one, and she was in hopes that he would not have remarked the absence of that.", "레베카는 열쇠 하나만 빼고 모두 그에게 내주었는데, 그 하나가 없는 것을 그가 알아채지 못하기를 바랐다."], ["It belonged to the little desk which Amelia had given her in early days, and which she kept in a secret place.", "그것은 애밀리아가 옛날에 그녀에게 준 자그마한 책상의 열쇠로, 그녀는 그것을 은밀한 곳에 보관해 두고 있었다."], ["But Rawdon flung open boxes and wardrobes, throwing the multifarious trumpery of their contents here and there, and at last he found the desk.", "그러나 로던은 상자며 옷장이며 마구 열어젖히고 그 안에 든 잡다한 허섭스레기들을 이리저리 내던지다가, 마침내 그 책상을 찾아냈다."], ["The woman was forced to open it.", "여자는 어쩔 수 없이 그것을 열었다."], ["It contained papers, love-letters many years old--all sorts of small trinkets and woman's memoranda.", "그 안에는 서류며, 여러 해 묵은 연애편지며, 온갖 자잘한 장신구와 여자의 비망록이 들어 있었다."], ["And it contained a pocket-book with bank-notes.", "그리고 지폐가 든 지갑 하나가 들어 있었다."], ["Some of these were dated ten years back, too, and one was quite a fresh one--a note for a thousand pounds which Lord Steyne had given her.", "그중에는 십 년 전 날짜의 것도 있었고, 하나는 아주 새것으로, 스타인 경이 그녀에게 준 천 파운드짜리 어음이었다."]], [["\"Did he give you this?\"", "\"이걸 저자가 당신한테 줬나?\""], ["Rawdon said.", "로던이 말했다."]], [["\"Yes,\"", "\"네.\""], ["Rebecca answered.", "레베카가 대답했다."]], [["\"I'll send it to him to-day,\"", "\"오늘 안으로 그자에게 돌려보내겠어.\""], ["Rawdon said (for day had dawned again, and many hours had passed in this search), \"and I will pay Briggs, who was kind to the boy, and some of the debts.", "로던이 말했다(다시 날이 밝아 있었고, 이렇게 뒤지느라 여러 시간이 지나간 것이었다). \"그리고 그 아이한테 잘해 준 브리그스에게 갚고, 빚도 좀 갚겠어."], ["You will let me know where I shall send the rest to you.", "나머지는 어디로 보내면 될지 알려 줘."], ["You might have spared me a hundred pounds, Becky, out of all this--I have always shared with you.\"", "베키, 이 많은 것 중에서 나한테 백 파운드쯤은 떼어 줄 수도 있었잖아. 나는 늘 당신과 나눠 가졌는데.\""]], [["\"I am innocent,\"", "\"저는 결백해요.\""], ["said Becky.", "베키가 말했다."], ["And he left her without another word.", "그리고 그는 더는 한마디도 없이 그녀를 떠났다."]], [["What were her thoughts when he left her?", "그가 떠났을 때 그녀는 무슨 생각을 했을까?"], ["She remained for hours after he was gone, the sunshine pouring into the room, and Rebecca sitting alone on the bed's edge.", "그가 가 버린 뒤로도 그녀는 몇 시간이나 그대로 있었고, 햇살이 방 안으로 쏟아져 들어오는데 레베카는 홀로 침대 가장자리에 앉아 있었다."], ["The drawers were all opened and their contents scattered about--dresses and feathers, scarfs and trinkets, a heap of tumbled vanities lying in a wreck.", "서랍이란 서랍은 죄다 열려 그 속의 것들이 사방에 흩어져 있었다. 드레스며 깃털 장식이며 스카프며 장신구며, 한 무더기의 어지러운 허영의 물건들이 폐허처럼 널브러져 있었다."], ["Her hair was falling over her shoulders; her gown was torn where Rawdon had wrenched the brilliants out of it.", "머리칼은 어깨 위로 흘러내렸고, 가운은 로던이 거기서 보석을 잡아 뜯은 자리가 찢겨 있었다."], ["She heard him go downstairs a few minutes after he left her, and the door slamming and closing on him.", "그가 자기를 떠난 지 몇 분 만에 계단을 내려가는 소리가, 그리고 그의 등 뒤로 문이 쾅 닫히는 소리가 들렸다. 그녀는 그가 결코 돌아오지 않으리라는 것을 알았다."], ["She knew he would never come back.", "그는 영영 가 버린 것이었다."], ["He was gone forever.", "그가 자살할까?"], ["Would he kill himself?--she thought--not until after he had met Lord Steyne.", "그녀는 생각했다. 스타인 경을 만나기 전까지는 아니리라."], ["She thought of her long past life, and all the dismal incidents of it.", "그녀는 자신의 길고도 지나온 삶을, 그 모든 음울한 사건들을 떠올렸다."], ["Ah, how dreary it seemed, how miserable, lonely and profitless!", "아아, 그것은 얼마나 황량하고, 얼마나 비참하고, 외롭고, 보람 없어 보였던가!"], ["Should she take laudanum, and end it, to have done with all hopes, schemes, debts, and triumphs?", "아편을 먹고 그것으로 끝내, 온갖 희망이며 계략이며 빚이며 승리며 죄다 끝내 버릴까?"], ["The French maid found her in this position--sitting in the midst of her miserable ruins with clasped hands and dry eyes.", "프랑스 하녀가 이런 자세로 있는 그녀를 발견했다. 두 손을 맞잡고 메마른 눈을 한 채 자신의 비참한 잔해 한가운데에 앉아 있는 모습을."], ["The woman was her accomplice and in Steyne's pay.", "그 여자는 그녀의 공범이자 스타인의 돈을 받는 끄나풀이었다."], ["\"Mon Dieu, madame, what has happened?\"", "\"맙소사, 마님, 무슨 일이세요?\""], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["What had happened?", "무슨 일이 있었던가?"], ["Was she guilty or not?", "그녀는 죄가 있는가, 없는가?"], ["She said not, but who could tell what was truth which came from those lips, or if that corrupt heart was in this case pure?", "그녀는 없다고 했지만, 그 입술에서 나온 것이 진실인지, 또 그 타락한 마음이 이 경우에는 정녕 깨끗했는지를 누가 알 수 있겠는가?"]], [["All her lies and her schemes, and her selfishness and her wiles, all her wit and genius had come to this bankruptcy.", "그녀의 온갖 거짓말과 계략, 이기심과 술수, 그 모든 기지와 재능이 이런 파산으로 귀결된 것이었다."], ["The woman closed the curtains and, with some entreaty and show of kindness, persuaded her mistress to lie down on the bed.", "그 여자는 커튼을 치고는, 얼마간 사정하고 친절을 가장하며 여주인을 설득해 침대에 눕게 했다."], ["Then she went below and gathered up the trinkets which had been lying on the floor since Rebecca dropped them there at her husband's orders, and Lord Steyne went away.", "그러고는 아래층으로 내려가, 레베카가 남편의 명령에 따라 떨어뜨린 뒤로 바닥에 그대로 놓여 있던 장신구들을, 스타인 경이 떠난 뒤로 줄곧 거기 있던 그것들을 주워 모았다."]], [["Sunday After the Battle\nThe mansion of Sir Pitt Crawley, in Great Gaunt Street, was just beginning to dress itself for the day, as Rawdon, in his evening costume, which he had now worn two days, passed by the scared female who was scouring the steps and entered into his brother's study.", "피트 크롤리 경의 저택은 그레이트 곤트 거리에 자리 잡고 있었는데, 이제 막 하루를 맞을 채비를 시작하던 참이었다. 로던은 이틀째 그대로 걸치고 있는 야회복 차림으로, 현관 계단을 닦다가 화들짝 놀란 하녀를 지나쳐 형의 서재로 들어섰다."], ["Lady Jane, in her morning-gown, was up and above stairs in the nursery superintending the toilettes of her children and listening to the morning prayers which the little creatures performed at her knee.", "제인 부인은 실내복 차림으로 이미 일어나 이층 육아실에 올라가 아이들의 몸단장을 챙기며, 어린것들이 그녀의 무릎 곁에서 올리는 아침 기도를 듣고 있었다."], ["Every morning she and they performed this duty privately, and before the public ceremonial at which Sir Pitt presided and at which all the people of the household were expected to assemble.", "그녀와 아이들은 매일 아침 이 의무를 단둘이 먼저 치렀고, 그런 뒤에야 피트 경이 주재하며 온 집안 식구가 모두 모이도록 되어 있는 공식 예배가 이어졌다. 로던은 서재 안 준남작의 책상 앞에 앉았다."], ["Rawdon sat down in the study before the Baronet's table, set out with the orderly blue books and the letters, the neatly docketed bills and symmetrical pamphlets, the locked account-books, desks, and dispatch boxes, the Bible, the Quarterly Review, and the Court Guide, which all stood as if on parade awaiting the inspection of their chief.", "그 책상에는 가지런히 정리된 푸른 표지의 의회 보고서와 편지들, 깔끔하게 분류해 정리한 청구서들과 좌우 대칭으로 놓인 소책자들, 자물쇠를 채운 회계 장부들, 책상받침과 문서함들, 성경, 계간평론지, 궁정 인명록 따위가 늘어서 있었는데, 모두가 마치 사열이라도 받듯 그 주인의 점검을 기다리며 도열해 있는 듯했다."]], [["A book of family sermons, one of which Sir Pitt was in the habit of administering to his family on Sunday mornings, lay ready on the study table, and awaiting his judicious selection.", "피트 경이 일요일 아침마다 식구들에게 한 편씩 들려주는 버릇이 있는 가족용 설교집 한 권이 서재 책상 위에 펼칠 채비를 갖춘 채, 그분의 사려 깊은 선택을 기다리고 있었다."], ["And by the sermon-book was the Observer newspaper, damp and neatly folded, and for Sir Pitt's own private use.", "그리고 설교집 옆에는 옵서버 신문이 물기에 살짝 젖어 단정히 접힌 채, 피트 경 개인이 쓰도록 놓여 있었다."], ["His gentleman alone took the opportunity of perusing the newspaper before he laid it by his master's desk.", "그분의 시종만이 그것을 주인의 책상 옆에 놓아두기 전에 미리 읽어볼 기회를 누렸다."], ["Before he had brought it into the study that morning, he had read in the journal a flaming account of \"Festivities at Gaunt House,\"", "그 시종은 그날 아침 신문을 서재로 들이기에 앞서, \"곤트 하우스의 향연\"에 관한 화려한 기사를 그 신문에서 이미 읽은 터였다."], ["with the names of all the distinguished personages invited by the Marquis of Steyne to meet his Royal Highness.", "거기에는 스타인 후작이 왕세자 전하를 모시고자 초대한 저명인사들의 이름이 죄다 실려 있었다."], ["Having made comments upon this entertainment to the housekeeper and her niece as they were taking early tea and hot buttered toast in the former lady's apartment, and wondered how the Rawding Crawleys could git on, the valet had damped and folded the paper once more, so that it looked quite fresh and innocent against the arrival of the master of the house.", "시종은 가정부와 그 조카가 가정부의 방에서 이른 차와 버터를 바른 뜨거운 토스트를 들고 있을 적에 이 연회에 대해 한마디씩 평을 늘어놓았고, 로던 크롤리 부부가 대체 어떻게 살아가는지 궁금해하기도 하다가, 그러고 나서 신문을 다시 물기에 적셔 접어 두어, 집주인이 도착할 즈음에는 마치 갓 배달된 듯 말짱하고 손도 안 댄 것처럼 보이게 해 놓았던 것이다."]], [["Poor Rawdon took up the paper and began to try and read it until his brother should arrive.", "가엾은 로던은 신문을 집어 들고 형이 올 때까지 어떻게든 읽어 보려 애썼다."], ["But the print fell blank upon his eyes, and he did not know in the least what he was reading.", "그러나 활자는 그의 눈에 그저 텅 비어 떨어질 뿐, 자기가 무엇을 읽고 있는지 조금도 알 수가 없었다."], ["The Government news and appointments (which Sir Pitt as a public man was bound to peruse, otherwise he would by no means permit the introduction of Sunday papers into his household), the theatrical criticisms, the fight for a hundred pounds a side between the Barking Butcher and the Tutbury Pet, the Gaunt House chronicle itself, which contained a most complimentary though guarded account of the famous charades of which Mrs. Becky had been the heroine--all these passed as in a haze before Rawdon, as he sat waiting the arrival of the chief of the family.", "정부 소식과 인사 발령(공인인 피트 경으로서는 반드시 정독해야 하는 것이었으니, 그렇지 않았던들 일요일 신문이 집 안에 들어오는 것을 결코 허락하지 않았을 터였다), 연극 비평, 바킹의 푸주한과 터트버리의 귀염둥이가 양편에 백 파운드씩 걸고 벌인 시합, 그리고 베키 부인이 여주인공으로 활약한 그 유명한 무언극을 더없이 찬사 어린, 그러나 조심스러운 어조로 전하는 곤트 하우스 소식 그 자체까지—이 모든 것이 가문의 어른이 도착하기를 앉아 기다리는 로던의 눈앞에서 안개에 싸인 듯 흘러갈 따름이었다."]], [["Punctually, as the shrill-toned bell of the black marble study clock began to chime nine, Sir Pitt made his appearance, fresh, neat, smugly shaved, with a waxy clean face, and stiff shirt collar, his scanty hair combed and oiled, trimming his nails as he descended the stairs majestically, in a starched cravat and a grey flannel dressing-gown--a real old English gentleman, in a word--a model of neatness and every propriety.", "검은 대리석으로 만든 서재 시계의 날카로운 종소리가 아홉 점을 알리기 시작하자, 어김없이 피트 경이 모습을 드러냈다. 산뜻하고 단정하며 말끔히 면도를 한, 밀랍처럼 깨끗한 얼굴에 빳빳한 와이셔츠 깃을 세우고, 듬성듬성한 머리칼은 빗어 기름을 발랐으며, 풀 먹인 크라바트에 회색 플란넬 실내복을 걸친 채로 위풍당당하게 계단을 내려오면서 손톱을 다듬고 있었다."], ["He started when he saw poor Rawdon in his study in tumbled clothes, with blood-shot eyes, and his hair over his face.", "한마디로 진정한 구식 영국 신사요, 단정함과 온갖 예법의 표본이었다. 그는 흐트러진 옷차림에 핏발 선 눈, 얼굴을 덮은 머리칼을 한 가엾은 로던이 서재에 있는 것을 보고 흠칫 놀랐다."], ["He thought his brother was not sober, and had been out all night on some orgy.", "그는 동생이 술이 덜 깬 채로, 밤새 어디서 진탕 놀다 온 것이라고 여겼다."], ["\"Good gracious, Rawdon,\"", "\"이런 세상에, 로던.\""], ["he said, with a blank face, \"what brings you here at this time of the morning?", "그가 어리둥절한 얼굴로 말했다. \"이 아침 시간에 여기는 웬일이냐?"], ["Why ain't you at home?\"", "어째서 집에 있지 않고?\""]], [["\"Home,\"", "\"집이라뇨.\""], ["said Rawdon with a wild laugh.", "로던이 거친 웃음을 터뜨리며 말했다."], ["\"Don't be frightened, Pitt.", "\"놀라지 마세요, 피트 형."], ["I'm not drunk.", "저 취하지 않았어요."], ["Shut the door; I want to speak to you.\"", "문 좀 닫으세요. 형한테 할 얘기가 있어요.\""]], [["Pitt closed the door and came up to the table, where he sat down in the other arm-chair--that one placed for the reception of the steward, agent, or confidential visitor who came to transact business with the Baronet--and trimmed his nails more vehemently than ever.", "피트는 문을 닫고 책상으로 다가와, 다른 안락의자에 앉았다. 그것은 집사나 대리인, 혹은 준남작과 볼일을 보러 오는 막역한 손님을 맞기 위해 놓아둔 의자였다. 그러고는 그 어느 때보다 격렬하게 손톱을 다듬었다."]], [["\"Pitt, it's all over with me,\"", "\"피트 형, 저는 이제 끝장났어요.\""], ["the Colonel said after a pause.", "잠시 침묵하던 끝에 대령이 말했다."], ["\"I'm done.\"", "\"저는 끝났어요.\""]], [["\"I always said it would come to this,\"", "\"이렇게 될 줄 내가 늘 말했지.\""], ["the Baronet cried peevishly, and beating a tune with his clean-trimmed nails.", "준남작이 짜증스럽게 외치며, 말끔히 다듬은 손톱으로 박자를 두드렸다."], ["\"I warned you a thousand times.", "\"내가 천 번도 더 경고했잖느냐."], ["I can't help you any more.", "더는 너를 도와줄 수가 없다."], ["Every shilling of my money is tied up.", "내 돈은 한 푼까지 죄다 묶여 있어."], ["Even the hundred pounds that Jane took you last night were promised to my lawyer to-morrow morning, and the want of it will put me to great inconvenience.", "어젯밤 제인이 가져다준 그 백 파운드조차 내일 아침에 내 변호사한테 주기로 약속한 돈이라, 그게 없으면 나는 크나큰 곤란을 겪게 된다."], ["I don't mean to say that I won't assist you ultimately.", "끝내 너를 돕지 않겠다는 말은 아니다."], ["But as for paying your creditors in full, I might as well hope to pay the National Debt.", "하지만 네 빚쟁이들한테 빚을 전부 갚아 준다는 거라면, 차라리 국채를 다 갚겠다고 바라는 편이 낫겠다."], ["It is madness, sheer madness, to think of such a thing.", "그런 걸 생각한다는 건 미친 짓이야, 순전히 미친 짓이지."], ["You must come to a compromise.", "너는 화해 절충을 봐야 해."], ["It's a painful thing for the family, but everybody does it.", "집안으로서는 괴로운 일이다만, 다들 그렇게 하는 거다."], ["There was George Kitely, Lord Ragland's son, went through the Court last week, and was what they call whitewashed, I believe.", "래글런드 경의 아들 조지 키틀리도 지난주에 파산 법정을 거쳤는데, 사람들 말로 이른바 깨끗이 면책을 받았다더구나."], ["Lord Ragland would not pay a shilling for him, and--\"", "래글런드 경은 그 애를 위해 한 푼도 내놓지 않았고, 그리고—\""]], [["\"It's not money I want,\"", "\"제가 원하는 건 돈이 아니에요.\""], ["Rawdon broke in.", "로던이 말을 끊었다."], ["\"I'm not come to you about myself.", "\"제 일로 형을 찾아온 게 아니라고요."], ["Never mind what happens to me.\"", "제게 무슨 일이 닥치든 그건 신경 쓰지 마세요.\""]], [["\"What is the matter, then?\"", "\"그럼 대체 무슨 일이냐?\""], ["said Pitt, somewhat relieved.", "피트가 다소 안도하며 물었다."]], [["\"It's the boy,\"", "\"애 때문이에요.\""], ["said Rawdon in a husky voice.", "로던이 잠긴 목소리로 말했다."], ["\"I want you to promise me that you will take charge of him when I'm gone.", "\"제가 떠나고 나면 그 애를 형이 맡아 주겠다고 약속해 주셨으면 해요."], ["That dear good wife of yours has always been good to him; and he's fonder of her than he is of his .", "그 착하고 다정한 형수님은 그 애한테 늘 잘해 주셨어요. 그 애도 제 어미보다 형수님을 더 따른다고요…—제기랄."], [". .--Damn it.", "들어 보세요, 피트 형."], ["Look here, Pitt--you know that I was to have had Miss Crawley's money.", "제가 크롤리 고모님 돈을 물려받기로 되어 있었던 거 형도 아시잖아요."], ["I wasn't brought up like a younger brother, but was always encouraged to be extravagant and kep idle.", "저는 차남처럼 길러진 게 아니라, 늘 헤프게 쓰고 빈둥거리도록 부추김을 받으며 자랐어요."], ["But for this I might have been quite a different man.", "이것만 아니었던들 저도 아주 딴사람이 되었을지 몰라요."], ["I didn't do my duty with the regiment so bad.", "연대에서도 제 임무를 그리 못하지는 않았어요."], ["You know how I was thrown over about the money, and who got it.\"", "제가 그 돈 문제로 어떻게 내쳐졌는지, 그걸 누가 차지했는지 형도 알잖아요.\""]], [["\"After the sacrifices I have made, and the manner in which I have stood by you, I think this sort of reproach is useless,\"", "\"내가 치른 희생을 생각하면, 또 내가 너를 어떻게 감싸 왔는지를 생각하면, 이런 식의 책망은 부질없는 것 같구나.\""], ["Sir Pitt said.", "피트 경이 말했다."], ["\"Your marriage was your own doing, not mine.\"", "\"네 결혼은 내가 한 게 아니라 네가 한 거다.\""]], [["\"That's over now,\"", "\"그건 이제 끝났어요.\""], ["said Rawdon.", "로던이 말했다."], ["\"That's over now.\"", "\"이제 끝났다고요.\""], ["And the words were wrenched from him with a groan, which made his brother start.", "그 말은 신음과 함께 그에게서 비틀려 나왔고, 그 소리에 형은 흠칫 놀랐다."]], [["\"Good God! is she dead?\"", "\"맙소사!"], ["Sir Pitt said with a voice of genuine alarm and commiseration.", "그 사람이 죽기라도 했느냐?\" 피트 경이 진정 놀라고 측은해하는 목소리로 말했다."]], [["\"I wish I was,\" Rawdon replied.", "\"차라리 제가 죽었으면 싶어요.\""], ["\"If it wasn't for little Rawdon I'd have cut my throat this morning--and that damned villain's too.\"", "로던이 대답했다. \"어린 로던만 아니었던들 오늘 아침에 제 목을 베어 버렸을 거예요—그 빌어먹을 악당의 목도요.\""]], [["Sir Pitt instantly guessed the truth and surmised that Lord Steyne was the person whose life Rawdon wished to take.", "피트 경은 곧바로 진상을 알아채고, 로던이 목숨을 빼앗고자 하는 자가 스타인 경임을 짐작했다. 대령은 형에게 간략하게, 그리고 끊어지는 말투로 이 사건의 정황을 들려주었다."], ["The Colonel told his senior briefly, and in broken accents, the circumstances of the case.", "\"그 악당과 그 여자 사이에 짜고 친 계략이었어요.\" 그가 말했다."], ["\"It was a regular plan between that scoundrel and her,\" he said.", "\"저한테 집달리들을 붙였죠."], ["\"The bailiffs were put upon me; I was taken as I was going out of his house; when I wrote to her for money, she said she was ill in bed and put me off to another day.", "저는 그자의 집에서 나오던 참에 붙잡혔어요. 제가 그 여자한테 돈을 보내 달라고 편지했더니, 자기는 아파서 누워 있다며 다음 날로 미루더군요."], ["And when I got home I found her in diamonds and sitting with that villain alone.\"", "그러다 제가 집에 와 보니, 그 여자가 다이아몬드를 두르고 그 악당과 단둘이 앉아 있는 거예요.\""], ["He then went on to describe hurriedly the personal conflict with Lord Steyne.", "그러고 나서 그는 스타인 경과 몸으로 맞붙은 일을 황급히 설명했다."], ["To an affair of that nature, of course, he said, there was but one issue, and after his conference with his brother, he was going away to make the necessary arrangements for the meeting which must ensue.", "물론 그런 성질의 일에는 한 가지 결말밖에 없으며, 형과 의논을 마치고 나면 뒤따를 수밖에 없는 결투를 위해 필요한 채비를 하러 떠날 작정이라고 그는 말했다. \"그리고 그게 저한테 치명적으로 끝날지도 모르니,\""], ["\"And as it may end fatally with me,\"", "로던이 끊어지는 목소리로 말했다."], ["Rawdon said with a broken voice, \"and as the boy has no mother, I must leave him to you and Jane, Pitt--only it will be a comfort to me if you will promise me to be his friend.\"", "\"그 애한테는 어미가 없으니, 그 애를 형과 형수님께 맡길 수밖에 없어요, 피트 형—다만 형이 그 애의 벗이 되어 주겠다고 약속만 해 주신다면 저한테는 그게 위안이 될 거예요.\""]], [["The elder brother was much affected, and shook Rawdon's hand with a cordiality seldom exhibited by him.", "형은 크게 마음이 움직여, 좀처럼 보이지 않던 다정함으로 로던의 손을 잡고 흔들었다."], ["Rawdon passed his hand over his shaggy eyebrows.", "로던은 더부룩한 눈썹 위로 손을 쓸어내렸다."], ["\"Thank you, brother,\"", "\"고마워요, 형.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"I know I can trust your word.\"", "\"형의 약속은 믿을 수 있다는 거 압니다.\""]], [["\"I will, upon my honour,\"", "\"내 명예를 걸고 그러마.\""], ["the Baronet said.", "준남작이 말했다."], ["And thus, and almost mutely, this bargain was struck between them.", "그리하여, 거의 말없이, 두 사람 사이에 이 약조가 맺어졌다."]], [["Then Rawdon took out of his pocket the little pocket-book which he had discovered in Becky's desk, and from which he drew a bundle of the notes which it contained.", "이윽고 로던은 베키의 책상에서 찾아낸 그 자그마한 수첩을 주머니에서 꺼내, 그 안에 든 지폐 한 묶음을 끄집어냈다. \"여기 육백 파운드예요.\""], ["\"Here's six hundred,\"", "그가 말했다."], ["he said--\"you didn't know I was so rich.", "\"제가 이렇게 부자인 줄 형은 몰랐죠."], ["I want you to give the money to Briggs, who lent it to us--and who was kind to the boy--and I've always felt ashamed of having taken the poor old woman's money.", "이 돈을 브리그스한테 줬으면 해요. 우리한테 빌려준 사람이거든요—게다가 그 애한테도 잘해 줬고요—저는 그 가엾은 노부인의 돈을 받은 게 늘 부끄러웠어요."], ["And here's some more--I've only kept back a few pounds--which Becky may as well have, to get on with.\"", "그리고 여기 좀 더 있어요—저는 몇 파운드밖에 안 챙겼어요—이건 베키가 살림을 꾸려 나가는 데 쓰도록 가지면 되겠죠.\""], ["As he spoke he took hold of the other notes to give to his brother, but his hands shook, and he was so agitated that the pocket-book fell from him, and out of it the thousand-pound note which had been the last of the unlucky Becky's winnings.", "그렇게 말하며 그는 형에게 건네주려고 나머지 지폐를 집어 들었으나, 손이 떨리고 어찌나 동요했던지 수첩이 그의 손에서 떨어졌고, 그 안에서 천 파운드짜리 지폐 한 장이 빠져나왔다. 그것은 불운한 베키가 마지막으로 딴 돈이었다."]], [["Pitt stooped and picked them up, amazed at so much wealth.", "피트는 몸을 굽혀 그것들을 주워 들고는, 그 엄청난 액수에 깜짝 놀랐다."], ["\"Not that,\"", "\"그건 아니에요.\""], ["Rawdon said.", "로던이 말했다."], ["\"I hope to put a bullet into the man whom that belongs to.\"", "\"저는 그게 임자인 그자한테 총알을 박아 넣을 작정이에요.\""], ["He had thought to himself, it would be a fine revenge to wrap a ball in the note and kill Steyne with it.", "그는 그 지폐로 총알을 싸서 스타인을 죽인다면 멋진 복수가 되리라고 속으로 생각했던 것이다."]], [["After this colloquy the brothers once more shook hands and parted.", "이 대화를 마친 뒤 형제는 다시 한번 손을 맞잡고 헤어졌다."], ["Lady Jane had heard of the Colonel's arrival, and was waiting for her husband in the adjoining dining-room, with female instinct, auguring evil.", "제인 부인은 대령이 왔다는 소식을 듣고, 여자의 직감으로 불길한 예감에 사로잡힌 채 옆방 식당에서 남편을 기다리고 있었다."], ["The door of the dining-room happened to be left open, and the lady of course was issuing from it as the two brothers passed out of the study.", "식당 문이 마침 열려 있던 터라, 두 형제가 서재에서 나오는 참에 부인은 으레 그러하듯 그 문에서 나오고 있었다."], ["She held out her hand to Rawdon and said she was glad he was come to breakfast, though she could perceive, by his haggard unshorn face and the dark looks of her husband, that there was very little question of breakfast between them.", "그녀는 로던에게 손을 내밀며 아침 식사를 하러 와서 반갑다고 말했지만, 그의 초췌하고 면도도 안 한 얼굴과 남편의 어두운 낯빛으로 미루어, 두 사람 사이에 아침 식사 따위는 문제가 되지 않는다는 것을 알아챌 수 있었다."], ["Rawdon muttered some excuses about an engagement, squeezing hard the timid little hand which his sister-in-law reached out to him.", "로던은 약속이 있다며 무어라 변명을 중얼거리고는, 형수가 내민 그 소심하고 자그마한 손을 꽉 쥐었다."], ["Her imploring eyes could read nothing but calamity in his face, but he went away without another word.", "애원하는 그녀의 눈은 그의 얼굴에서 재앙 외에는 아무것도 읽어 낼 수 없었으나, 그는 더는 한마디도 없이 떠나갔다."], ["Nor did Sir Pitt vouchsafe her any explanation.", "피트 경 역시 그녀에게 아무 설명도 해 주지 않았다."], ["The children came up to salute him, and he kissed them in his usual frigid manner.", "아이들이 그에게 인사하러 다가오자, 그는 여느 때의 그 싸늘한 태도로 아이들에게 입을 맞추었다."], ["The mother took both of them close to herself, and held a hand of each of them as they knelt down to prayers, which Sir Pitt read to them, and to the servants in their Sunday suits or liveries, ranged upon chairs on the other side of the hissing tea-urn.", "어머니는 두 아이를 자기 곁으로 바싹 끌어당겨, 아이들이 기도하러 무릎을 꿇을 때 저마다 한 손씩 잡아 주었다. 피트 경은 아이들에게, 그리고 쉭쉭 김을 뿜는 차 주전자 맞은편으로 의자에 줄지어 앉은, 일요일 정장이나 제복을 차려입은 하인들에게 기도문을 읽어 주었다."], ["Breakfast was so late that day, in consequence of the delays which had occurred, that the church-bells began to ring whilst they were sitting over their meal; and Lady Jane was too ill, she said, to go to church, though her thoughts had been entirely astray during the period of family devotion.", "그날은 지체된 일들 탓에 아침 식사가 어찌나 늦어졌는지, 그들이 식탁에 둘러앉아 있는 동안 벌써 교회 종이 울리기 시작했다. 그리고 제인 부인은 몸이 너무 좋지 않다며 교회에 가지 못하겠다고 했는데, 사실 그녀의 마음은 가족 예배가 진행되는 내내 온통 딴 데 가 있었던 것이다."]], [["Rawdon Crawley meanwhile hurried on from Great Gaunt Street, and knocking at the great bronze Medusa's head which stands on the portal of Gaunt House, brought out the purple Silenus in a red and silver waistcoat who acts as porter of that palace.", "한편 로던 크롤리는 그레이트 곤트 거리를 서둘러 빠져나가, 곤트 하우스 정문에 달린 커다란 청동 메두사 머리를 두드려, 붉은빛과 은빛의 조끼를 걸친, 그 궁전의 문지기 노릇을 하는 자줏빛 실레노스 같은 사내를 불러냈다."], ["The man was scared also by the Colonel's dishevelled appearance, and barred the way as if afraid that the other was going to force it.", "이 사내도 대령의 헝클어진 행색에 화들짝 놀라, 마치 상대가 억지로 밀고 들어올까 봐 겁이라도 난 듯 길을 막아섰다."], ["But Colonel Crawley only took out a card and enjoined him particularly to send it in to Lord Steyne, and to mark the address written on it, and say that Colonel Crawley would be all day after one o'clock at the Regent Club in St. James's Street--not at home.", "그러나 크롤리 대령은 그저 명함 한 장을 꺼내, 그것을 스타인 경에게 꼭 전하라고, 그리고 거기 적힌 주소를 눈여겨보라고, 또 크롤리 대령은 한 시 이후로 종일 세인트제임스 거리의 리전트 클럽에 있을 것이며 집에는 없을 것이라고 전하라고 각별히 일렀다."], ["The fat red-faced man looked after him with astonishment as he strode away; so did the people in their Sunday clothes who were out so early; the charity-boys with shining faces, the greengrocer lolling at his door, and the publican shutting his shutters in the sunshine, against service commenced.", "살집 있고 붉은 얼굴의 사내는 그가 성큼성큼 걸어가는 뒷모습을 놀란 눈으로 바라보았다. 그렇게 일찍 나와 있던, 일요일 옷차림을 한 사람들도, 얼굴이 반들반들 빛나는 자선 학교 아이들도, 가게 문간에 기대선 채소 장수도, 예배가 시작되기 전에 햇살 속에서 덧창을 닫던 선술집 주인도 마찬가지였다."], ["The people joked at the cab-stand about his appearance, as he took a carriage there, and told the driver to drive him to Knightsbridge Barracks.", "그가 그곳에서 마차를 잡아 마부에게 나이츠브리지 병영으로 가자고 이르자, 마차 대기소의 사람들이 그의 행색을 두고 농지거리를 했다."]], [["All the bells were jangling and tolling as he reached that place.", "그가 그곳에 다다랐을 무렵 온 동네의 종이 일제히 뎅그렁뎅그렁 울려 대고 있었다."], ["He might have seen his old acquaintance Amelia on her way from Brompton to Russell Square, had he been looking out.", "만약 그가 살펴보고 있었더라면, 옛 지인 애밀리아가 브롬프턴에서 러셀 광장으로 가는 길에 있는 것을 보았을지도 모른다. 무리 지은 학교 아이들이 교회를 향해 행진하고 있었고, 교외의 번들거리는 포장도로며 마차 지붕 위까지 일요일 나들이에 나선 사람들로 붐볐다."], ["Troops of schools were on their march to church, the shiny pavement and outsides of coaches in the suburbs were thronged with people out upon their Sunday pleasure; but the Colonel was much too busy to take any heed of these phenomena, and, arriving at Knightsbridge, speedily made his way up to the room of his old friend and comrade Captain Macmurdo, who Crawley found, to his satisfaction, was in barracks.", "그러나 대령은 너무도 바쁜 나머지 이런 광경에는 조금도 눈길을 주지 않았고, 나이츠브리지에 도착하자마자 옛 친구이자 전우인 맥머도 대위의 방으로 곧장 올라갔다. 크롤리는 그가 병영에 있는 것을 보고 흡족해했다."]], [["Captain Macmurdo, a veteran officer and Waterloo man, greatly liked by his regiment, in which want of money alone prevented him from attaining the highest ranks, was enjoying the forenoon calmly in bed.", "맥머도 대위는 워털루를 겪은 노련한 장교로 연대에서 크게 사랑받는 인물이었으나, 오로지 돈이 없는 탓에 최고위직에 오르지 못한 사람이었다. 그는 침대에 누운 채 오전 한나절을 느긋하게 즐기고 있었다."], ["He had been at a fast supper-party, given the night before by Captain the Honourable George Cinqbars, at his house in Brompton Square, to several young men of the regiment, and a number of ladies of the corps de ballet, and old Mac, who was at home with people of all ages and ranks, and consorted with generals, dog-fanciers, opera-dancers, bruisers, and every kind of person, in a word, was resting himself after the night's labours, and, not being on duty, was in bed.", "그는 전날 밤 명예로운 조지 싱크바스 대위가 브롬프턴 광장의 자택에서 연대의 젊은 장교 몇몇과 발레단 숙녀 여럿을 초대해 베푼 흥겨운 만찬 자리에 다녀온 터였다. 그리고 늙은 맥은 나이와 신분을 막론하고 누구와도 격의 없이 어울리는 사람이라, 장군들이며 개 애호가들이며 오페라 무용수들이며 권투 선수들이며 한마디로 온갖 부류의 사람들과 사귀는 터였으니, 밤새 수고한 뒤 휴식을 취하는 중이었고, 당번도 아니어서 침대에 누워 있었던 것이다."]], [["His room was hung round with boxing, sporting, and dancing pictures, presented to him by comrades as they retired from the regiment, and married and settled into quiet life.", "그의 방은 전우들이 연대를 떠나 결혼하고 조용한 삶에 정착하면서 선물한 권투며 사냥이며 무용 그림들로 빙 둘러 걸려 있었다. 그리고 그는 이제 나이가 쉰에 가까웠고 그중 스물네 해를 군단에서 보냈으니, 진기한 박물관을 하나 갖춘 셈이었다."], ["And as he was now nearly fifty years of age, twenty-four of which he had passed in the corps, he had a singular museum.", "그는 영국에서 손꼽히는 명사수 가운데 하나였고, 덩치 큰 사람치고는 손꼽히는 명기수 가운데 하나이기도 했다."], ["He was one of the best shots in England, and, for a heavy man, one of the best riders; indeed, he and Crawley had been rivals when the latter was in the Army. To be brief, Mr.", "사실 크롤리가 군에 있던 시절 그와 크롤리는 맞수였다."], ["Macmurdo was lying in bed, reading in Bell's Life an account of that very fight between the Tutbury Pet and the Barking Butcher, which has been before mentioned--a venerable bristly warrior, with a little close-shaved grey head, with a silk nightcap, a red face and nose, and a great dyed moustache.", "요컨대 맥머도 씨는 침대에 누워, 앞서 언급된 바로 그 시합, 즉 터트버리의 귀염둥이와 바킹의 푸주한이 벌인 시합에 관한 기사를 벨스 라이프에서 읽고 있었던 것이다. 짧게 깎은 자그마한 잿빛 머리에 비단 잠자리 모자를 쓰고, 붉은 얼굴과 코에, 물들인 커다란 콧수염을 기른, 수염이 까칠한 노전사였다."]], [["When Rawdon told the Captain he wanted a friend, the latter knew perfectly well on what duty of friendship he was called to act, and indeed had conducted scores of affairs for his acquaintances with the greatest prudence and skill.", "로던이 대위에게 입회인이 한 사람 필요하다고 말하자, 맥머도는 자기가 어떤 우정의 임무를 맡으라는 부름을 받았는지 더없이 잘 알아챘으니, 실로 그는 지인들을 위해 그런 일을 수십 건이나 더없이 신중하고 능란하게 처리해 온 사람이었다."], ["His Royal Highness the late lamented Commander-in-Chief had had the greatest regard for Macmurdo on this account, and he was the common refuge of gentlemen in trouble.", "고인이 되신 애도받는 총사령관 전하께서도 이 점 때문에 맥머도를 더없이 아끼셨고, 그는 곤경에 처한 신사들이 흔히 찾는 피난처였다."]], [["\"What's the row about, Crawley, my boy?\"", "\"무슨 소동이냐, 크롤리, 이 사람아?\""], ["said the old warrior.", "늙은 전사가 말했다."], ["\"No more gambling business, hay, like that when we shot Captain Marker?\"", "\"또 노름 문제는 아니겠지, 응? 우리가 마커 대위를 쐈을 때처럼 말이야.\""]], [["\"It's about--about my wife,\"", "\"그게—제 아내에 관한 일이에요.\""], ["Crawley answered, casting down his eyes and turning very red.", "크롤리가 눈을 내리깔고 얼굴을 새빨갛게 붉히며 대답했다."]], [["The other gave a whistle.", "상대는 휘파람을 불었다."], ["\"I always said she'd throw you over,\"", "\"내가 늘 말했잖아, 그 여자가 자넬 차 버릴 거라고.\""], ["he began--indeed there were bets in the regiment and at the clubs regarding the probable fate of Colonel Crawley, so lightly was his wife's character esteemed by his comrades and the world; but seeing the savage look with which Rawdon answered the expression of this opinion, Macmurdo did not think fit to enlarge upon it further.", "그가 입을 열었다—실제로 연대에서나 클럽에서나 크롤리 대령에게 닥칠 법한 운명을 두고 내기가 오갈 만큼, 그의 아내의 평판은 전우들과 세상 사람들 사이에서 그토록 하찮게 여겨졌던 것이다. 그러나 로던이 이런 의견 표명에 답하는 그 사나운 눈빛을 보고는, 맥머도는 더 길게 늘어놓는 것이 마땅치 않다고 여겼다."]], [["\"Is there no way out of it, old boy?\"", "\"빠져나갈 길이 없는 건가, 이 사람아?\""], ["the Captain continued in a grave tone.", "대위가 진지한 어조로 이어 말했다."], ["\"Is it only suspicion, you know, or--or what is it?", "\"그저 의심에 불과한 건가, 아니면—아니면 대체 무슨 일인가?"], ["Any letters?", "편지라도 있나?"], ["Can't you keep it quiet?", "조용히 덮을 수는 없겠나?"], ["Best not make any noise about a thing of that sort if you can help it.\"", "그런 종류의 일은 어쩔 수 없는 게 아니라면 떠들어 대지 않는 게 상책이야.\""], ["\"Think of his only finding her out now,\"", "'이제야 그 여자 정체를 알아챘다니.'"], ["the Captain thought to himself, and remembered a hundred particular conversations at the mess-table, in which Mrs. Crawley's reputation had been torn to shreds.", "대위는 속으로 생각하며, 식당 식탁에서 크롤리 부인의 평판이 갈가리 찢기던 그 숱한 대화들을 떠올렸다."]], [["\"There's no way but one out of it,\"", "\"빠져나갈 길은 단 하나뿐이에요.\""], ["Rawdon replied--\"and there's only a way out of it for one of us, Mac--do you understand?", "로던이 대답했다. \"그리고 그 길은 우리 둘 중 한 사람한테만 열려 있어요, 맥—무슨 말인지 아시겠어요?"], ["I was put out of the way--arrested--I found 'em alone together.", "저는 길에서 치워졌어요—붙잡혔다고요—그러고는 두 사람이 단둘이 같이 있는 걸 봤죠."], ["I told him he was a liar and a coward, and knocked him down and thrashed him.\"", "그자더러 거짓말쟁이에 비겁한 놈이라고 말하고, 때려눕혀서 흠씬 두들겨 줬어요.\""]], [["\"Serve him right,\"", "\"당해도 싸지.\""], ["Macmurdo said.", "맥머도가 말했다."], ["\"Who is it?\"", "\"그게 누군가?\""]], [["Rawdon answered it was Lord Steyne.", "로던은 스타인 경이라고 대답했다."]], [["\"The deuce!", "\"이런 빌어먹을!"], ["a Marquis!", "후작이라니!"], ["they said he--that is, they said you--\"", "사람들은 그자가—그러니까, 사람들은 자네가—\""]], [["\"What the devil do you mean?\"", "\"대체 무슨 소릴 하는 거예요?\""], ["roared out Rawdon; \"do you mean that you ever heard a fellow doubt about my wife and didn't tell me, Mac?\"", "로던이 버럭 고함을 질렀다. \"그럼 어떤 작자가 내 아내를 두고 수군대는 걸 들으면서도 나한테 말 안 해 줬다는 거예요, 맥?\""]], [["\"The world's very censorious, old boy,\"", "\"세상은 입방아가 심한 법이야, 이 사람아.\""], ["the other replied.", "상대가 대답했다."], ["\"What the deuce was the good of my telling you what any tom-fools talked about?\"", "\"어떤 멍청이들이 떠들어 댄 소릴 자네한테 일러바쳐 봤자 무슨 소용이 있었겠나?\""]], [["\"It was damned unfriendly, Mac,\"", "\"그건 정말 우정 없는 짓이었어요, 맥.\""], ["said Rawdon, quite overcome; and, covering his face with his hands, he gave way to an emotion, the sight of which caused the tough old campaigner opposite him to wince with sympathy.", "로던이 완전히 무너진 채 말하고는, 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 북받치는 감정에 그대로 몸을 내맡겼다. 그 광경에 맞은편에 앉은 강인한 늙은 노병마저 측은한 마음에 움찔할 정도였다."], ["\"Hold up, old boy,\"", "\"기운 내게, 이 사람아.\""], ["he said; \"great man or not, we'll put a bullet in him, damn him.", "그가 말했다. \"후작이든 뭐든, 우리가 그놈한테 총알을 박아 줄 테니까, 빌어먹을 놈."], ["As for women, they're all so.\"", "여자들로 말하면, 죄다 그 모양이지.\""]], [["\"You don't know how fond I was of that one,\"", "\"제가 그 여자를 얼마나 사랑했는지 형은 몰라요.\""], ["Rawdon said, half-inarticulately.", "로던이 거의 알아들을 수 없게 더듬더듬 말했다."], ["\"Damme, I followed her like a footman.", "\"제기랄, 저는 하인처럼 그 여자를 졸졸 따라다녔어요."], ["I gave up everything I had to her.", "제가 가진 걸 죄다 그 여자한테 내줬다고요."], ["I'm a beggar because I would marry her.", "그 여자와 결혼하겠다고 거지가 됐어요."], ["By Jove, sir, I've pawned my own watch in order to get her anything she fancied; and she--she's been making a purse for herself all the time, and grudged me a hundred pound to get me out of quod.\"", "맹세컨대, 그 여자가 갖고 싶어 하는 거라면 뭐든 사 주려고 제 시계까지 전당포에 잡혔어요. 그런데 그 여자는—그 여자는 그동안 내내 제 주머니만 채우면서, 제가 감옥에 갇혔을 때 빼내 줄 백 파운드조차 아까워했다고요.\""], ["He then fiercely and incoherently, and with an agitation under which his counsellor had never before seen him labour, told Macmurdo the circumstances of the story.", "그러고 나서 그는 사납고 두서없이, 그리고 그의 조언자가 한 번도 본 적 없는 동요 속에서, 맥머도에게 사건의 정황을 들려주었다."], ["His adviser caught at some stray hints in it.", "그의 조언자는 그 이야기 속에 흘러나온 몇몇 단서를 붙들었다."], ["\"She may be innocent, after all,\" he said.", "\"어쩌면 그래도 그 여자가 결백할지도 모르지.\""], ["\"She says so.", "그가 말했다."], ["Steyne has been a hundred times alone with her in the house before.\"", "\"그 여자도 그렇게 말한다며. 스타인은 전에도 그 집에서 그 여자와 백 번도 더 단둘이 있었잖나.\""]], [["\"It may be so,\"", "\"그럴지도 모르죠.\" 로던이 슬프게 대답했다."], ["Rawdon answered sadly, \"but this don't look very innocent\": and he showed the Captain the thousand-pound note which he had found in Becky's pocket-book.", "\"하지만 이건 그리 결백해 보이지가 않아요.\" 그러고는 베키의 수첩에서 찾아낸 그 천 파운드짜리 지폐를 대위에게 보여 주었다."], ["\"This is what he gave her, Mac, and she kep it unknown to me; and with this money in the house, she refused to stand by me when I was locked up.\"", "\"이게 그자가 그 여자한테 준 거예요, 맥. 그런데 그 여자는 제 모르게 이걸 숨겨 뒀고요."], ["The Captain could not but own that the secreting of the money had a very ugly look.", "집 안에 이 돈을 두고도, 제가 갇혔을 때 절 편들기를 거부했단 말이에요.\" 대위도 그 돈을 숨겨 둔 것이 무척이나 보기 흉하다는 점은 인정할 수밖에 없었다."]], [["Whilst they were engaged in their conference, Rawdon dispatched Captain Macmurdo's servant to Curzon Street, with an order to the domestic there to give up a bag of clothes of which the Colonel had great need.", "이렇게 두 사람이 의논을 나누는 동안, 로던은 맥머도 대위의 하인을 커즌 거리로 보내, 그 집 하인에게 대령이 몹시 필요로 하는 옷 가방 하나를 내주라는 지시를 전하게 했다."], ["And during the man's absence, and with great labour and a Johnson's Dictionary, which stood them in much stead, Rawdon and his second composed a letter, which the latter was to send to Lord Steyne.", "그리고 그 하인이 자리를 비운 사이, 로던과 그의 입회인은 무척 애를 쓰며 존슨 사전의 큰 도움을 받아—그것이 그들에게 요긴하게 쓰였다—편지 한 통을 작성했는데, 입회인이 그것을 스타인 경에게 보내기로 했다."], ["Captain Macmurdo had the honour of waiting upon the Marquis of Steyne, on the part of Colonel Rawdon Crawley, and begged to intimate that he was empowered by the Colonel to make any arrangements for the meeting which, he had no doubt, it was his Lordship's intention to demand, and which the circumstances of the morning had rendered inevitable.", "맥머도 대위는 로던 크롤리 대령을 대리하여 스타인 후작을 방문하는 영광을 누리게 되었으며, 자신은 대령으로부터, 각하께서 요구하실 의향임이 분명한, 그리고 오늘 아침의 정황으로 말미암아 불가피해진 결투에 관하여 어떠한 합의든 맺을 권한을 위임받았음을 알리고자 한다는 내용이었다."], ["Captain Macmurdo begged Lord Steyne, in the most polite manner, to appoint a friend, with whom he (Captain M.M.) might communicate, and desired that the meeting might take place with as little delay as possible.", "맥머도 대위는 더없이 정중한 태도로 스타인 경에게 입회인을 한 사람 지명하여 자신(맥머도 대위)이 그와 연락할 수 있도록 해 주기를 청하고, 결투가 가능한 한 지체 없이 이루어지기를 바란다고 했다."]], [["In a postscript the Captain stated that he had in his possession a bank-note for a large amount, which Colonel Crawley had reason to suppose was the property of the Marquis of Steyne.", "추신에서 대위는, 크롤리 대령이 스타인 후작의 소유물이라고 추정할 만한 거액의 은행권 한 장을 자신이 소지하고 있다고 적었다."], ["And he was anxious, on the Colonel's behalf, to give up the note to its owner.", "그리고 자신은 대령을 대신하여 그 지폐를 임자에게 돌려주기를 간절히 바란다고 했다."]], [["By the time this note was composed, the Captain's servant returned from his mission to Colonel Crawley's house in Curzon Street, but without the carpet-bag and portmanteau, for which he had been sent, and with a very puzzled and odd face.", "이 편지가 다 작성될 무렵, 대위의 하인이 커즌 거리에 있는 크롤리 대령의 집으로 갔던 심부름에서 돌아왔는데, 가지러 보냈던 융단 가방과 여행용 트렁크는 없이, 몹시 어리둥절하고 야릇한 얼굴로 돌아왔다."]], [["\"They won't give 'em up,\"", "\"그것들을 안 내주는데요.\""], ["said the man; \"there's a regular shinty in the house, and everything at sixes and sevens.", "하인이 말했다. \"집 안이 온통 한바탕 난리가 났고, 모든 게 뒤죽박죽이에요."], ["The landlord's come in and took possession.", "집주인이 들어와서 다 차지해 버렸고요."], ["The servants was a drinkin' up in the drawingroom.", "하인들은 응접실에서 술을 퍼마시고 있더라고요."], ["They said--they said you had gone off with the plate, Colonel\"--the man added after a pause--\"One of the servants is off already.", "그자들 말로는—대령님이 은식기를 가지고 달아났다더군요.\" 하인이 잠시 멈췄다가 덧붙였다."], ["And Simpson, the man as was very noisy and drunk indeed, says nothing shall go out of the house until his wages is paid up.\"", "\"하인 하나는 벌써 떠나 버렸고요. 그리고 심슨이라는 자는, 아주 시끄럽게 떠들고 곤드레만드레 취한 자인데, 자기 품삯을 다 받기 전에는 집에서 아무것도 못 나간다고 합니다.\""]], [["The account of this little revolution in May Fair astonished and gave a little gaiety to an otherwise very triste conversation.", "메이페어에서 벌어진 이 자그마한 변란에 관한 이야기는, 안 그래도 무척 침울하던 대화에 놀라움과 더불어 약간의 흥취를 더해 주었다."], ["The two officers laughed at Rawdon's discomfiture.", "두 장교는 로던의 낭패를 두고 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["\"I'm glad the little 'un isn't at home,\"", "\"꼬마가 집에 없어서 다행이에요.\""], ["Rawdon said, biting his nails.", "로던이 손톱을 깨물며 말했다."], ["\"You remember him, Mac, don't you, in the Riding School?", "\"그 애 기억하시죠, 맥? 승마 학교에서 말이에요."], ["How he sat the kicker to be sure!", "그 펄쩍펄쩍 뛰는 말에 어찌나 잘 올라타 있던지!"], ["didn't he?\"", "안 그래요?\""]], [["\"That he did, old boy,\"", "\"그랬지, 이 사람아.\""], ["said the good-natured Captain.", "마음씨 좋은 대위가 말했다."]], [["Little Rawdon was then sitting, one of fifty gown boys, in the Chapel of Whitefriars School, thinking, not about the sermon, but about going home next Saturday, when his father would certainly tip him and perhaps would take him to the play.", "그때 어린 로던은 화이트프라이어스 학교 예배당에서 쉰 명 남짓한 가운 입은 학생들 가운데 하나로 앉아, 설교가 아니라 다음 토요일에 집에 가는 일을 생각하고 있었다. 그날이면 아버지가 분명 용돈을 쥐여 줄 테고 어쩌면 연극 구경에 데려가 줄지도 몰랐다."]], [["\"He's a regular trump, that boy,\"", "\"그 녀석은 정말 야무진 아이예요.\""], ["the father went on, still musing about his son.", "아버지가 여전히 아들 생각에 잠긴 채 이어 말했다."], ["\"I say, Mac, if anything goes wrong--if I drop--I should like you to--to go and see him, you know, and say that I was very fond of him, and that.", "\"이봐요, 맥, 만약 일이 잘못되면—제가 쓰러지면—형이 그 애를 가서 한번 봐 주셨으면 해요, 아시겠죠. 그래서 제가 그 애를 무척 사랑했다고, 그렇게 전해 주세요."], ["And--dash it--old chap, give him these gold sleeve-buttons: it's all I've got.\"", "그리고—제기랄—이 사람아, 그 애한테 이 금 소맷단추를 주세요."], ["He covered his face with his black hands, over which the tears rolled and made furrows of white.", "제가 가진 건 이게 전부예요.\" 그는 검은 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸고, 그 위로 눈물이 흘러내려 하얀 골을 냈다."], ["Mr. Macmurdo had also occasion to take off his silk night-cap and rub it across his eyes.", "맥머도 씨도 비단 잠자리 모자를 벗어 두 눈을 문지를 일이 생겼다."]], [["\"Go down and order some breakfast,\"", "\"내려가서 아침 식사 좀 시켜 와라.\""], ["he said to his man in a loud cheerful voice.", "그가 큰 소리로 명랑하게 하인에게 말했다."], ["\"What'll you have, Crawley?", "\"뭘 들겠나, 크롤리?"], ["Some devilled kidneys and a herring--let's say.", "매콤하게 양념한 콩팥 요리하고 청어—그걸로 하지."], ["And, Clay, lay out some dressing things for the Colonel: we were always pretty much of a size, Rawdon, my boy, and neither of us ride so light as we did when we first entered the corps.\"", "그리고 클레이, 대령님 입으실 옷 좀 챙겨 놔라. 우리는 늘 덩치가 거의 비슷했지, 로던, 이 사람아."], ["With which, and leaving the Colonel to dress himself, Macmurdo turned round towards the wall, and resumed the perusal of Bell's Life, until such time as his friend's toilette was complete and he was at liberty to commence his own.", "그리고 처음 군단에 들어왔을 때만큼 우리 둘 다 몸이 가볍지가 않으니 말이야.\" 그러고는 대령이 옷을 입도록 내버려 둔 채, 맥머도는 벽 쪽으로 돌아누워, 친구가 몸단장을 마치고 자기가 자유로이 단장을 시작할 수 있게 될 때까지 다시 벨스 라이프를 읽어 나갔다."]], [["This, as he was about to meet a lord, Captain Macmurdo performed with particular care.", "이번에는 귀족을 만나러 가는 길이었으므로, 맥머도 대위는 이 단장을 각별히 공들여 해냈다."], ["He waxed his mustachios into a state of brilliant polish and put on a tight cravat and a trim buff waistcoat, so that all the young officers in the mess-room, whither Crawley had preceded his friend, complimented Mac on his appearance at breakfast and asked if he was going to be married that Sunday.", "그는 콧수염에 밀랍을 발라 번쩍번쩍 윤이 나게 다듬고, 꽉 죄는 크라바트에 말끔한 황갈색 조끼를 차려입었다. 그래서 크롤리가 친구보다 앞서 들어가 있던 식당의 젊은 장교들이 모두 아침 식사 자리에서 맥의 차림새를 칭찬하며, 이번 일요일에 결혼이라도 하느냐고 물을 정도였다."]], [["In Which the Same Subject is Pursued\nBecky did not rally from the state of stupor and confusion in which the events of the previous night had plunged her intrepid spirit until the bells of the Curzon Street Chapels were ringing for afternoon service, and rising from her bed she began to ply her own bell, in order to summon the French maid who had left her some hours before.", "전날 밤의 사건들이 그 담대한 영혼을 빠뜨려 놓은 망연자실하고 혼란스러운 상태에서 베키가 정신을 차린 것은, 커즌 거리의 예배당 종들이 오후 예배를 알리며 울릴 무렵이었다. 그녀는 침대에서 일어나 자기 손으로 종을 당기기 시작했다. 몇 시간 전에 그녀 곁을 떠난 프랑스인 하녀를 부르기 위함이었다."]], [["Mrs.", "로던 크롤리 부인은 여러 차례 종을 당겼으나 헛수고였다."], ["Rawdon Crawley rang many times in vain; and though, on the last occasion, she rang with such vehemence as to pull down the bell-rope, Mademoiselle Fifine did not make her appearance--no, not though her mistress, in a great pet, and with the bell-rope in her hand, came out to the landing-place with her hair over her shoulders and screamed out repeatedly for her attendant.", "마지막에는 종 끈이 뜯겨 나갈 만큼 거세게 당겼건만, 피핀 양은 끝내 모습을 드러내지 않았다. 그렇다, 여주인이 잔뜩 약이 올라 종 끈을 손에 쥔 채, 머리카락을 어깨에 늘어뜨리고 층계참까지 나와 시중드는 하녀를 거듭 불러 댔어도 소용없었다."]], [["The truth is, she had quitted the premises for many hours, and upon that permission which is called French leave among us.", "사실인즉, 그녀는 벌써 여러 시간 전에 그 집을 떠나 버린 것이었다. 우리네 사이에서 이른바 \"프랑스식 작별\"이라 부르는, 그런 식의 무단이탈이었다."], ["After picking up the trinkets in the drawing-room, Mademoiselle had ascended to her own apartments, packed and corded her own boxes there, tripped out and called a cab for herself, brought down her trunks with her own hand, and without ever so much as asking the aid of any of the other servants, who would probably have refused it, as they hated her cordially, and without wishing any one of them good-bye, had made her exit from Curzon Street.", "응접실에 떨어진 장신구들을 주워 챙긴 뒤, 마드무아젤은 자기 방으로 올라가 그곳에서 제 상자들을 꾸리고 끈으로 묶었으며, 종종걸음으로 빠져나가 손수 삯마차를 부르고, 제 손으로 트렁크들을 끌고 내려왔다. 다른 하인들에게는 도움을 청하지도 않았는데(어차피 그들은 그녀를 진심으로 미워했으니 거절했을 것이 뻔했다), 그 누구에게도 작별 인사 한마디 없이 커즌 거리를 떠나 버린 것이었다."]], [["The game, in her opinion, was over in that little domestic establishment. Fifine went off in a cab, as we have known more exalted persons of her nation to do under similar circumstances: but, more provident or lucky than these, she secured not only her own property, but some of her mistress's (if indeed that lady could be said to have any property at all)--and not only carried off the trinkets before alluded to, and some favourite dresses on which she had long kept her eye, but four richly gilt Louis Quatorze candlesticks, six gilt albums, keepsakes, and Books of Beauty, a gold enamelled snuff-box which had once belonged to Madame du Barri, and the sweetest little inkstand and mother-of-pearl blotting book, which Becky used when she composed her charming little pink notes, had vanished from the premises in Curzon Street together with Mademoiselle Fifine, and all the silver laid on the table for the little festin which Rawdon interrupted. The plated ware Mademoiselle left behind her was too cumbrous, probably for which reason, no doubt, she also left the fire irons, the chimney-glasses, and the rosewood cottage piano.", "그녀가 보기에, 그 자그마한 살림집에서는 이미 판이 끝난 셈이었다. 피핀은 삯마차를 타고 떠났다. 우리는 비슷한 처지에서 그녀와 같은 나라의 더 고귀한 인물들도 그렇게 떠나는 것을 알고 있다. 다만 그녀는 그런 이들보다 한결 빈틈없고 운이 좋아서, 제 물건뿐 아니라 여주인의 물건 일부까지(과연 그 부인에게 무슨 물건이라 할 것이 조금이라도 있었다면 말이지만) 잘 챙겨 두었다. 앞서 말한 그 장신구들과, 진작부터 눈독을 들여 온 마음에 드는 드레스 몇 벌만 가져간 것이 아니었다. 화려하게 도금한 루이 십사세풍 촛대 네 개, 금박 입힌 앨범과 기념첩과 미인 화보집 여섯 권, 한때 뒤바리 부인의 것이었던 금에 칠보를 입힌 코담뱃갑, 그리고 베키가 그 사랑스러운 분홍빛 짧은 편지를 쓸 때 쓰던 더없이 앙증맞은 잉크스탠드와 자개로 장식한 압지첩까지, 모조리 마드무아젤 피핀과 함께 커즌 거리의 그 집에서 자취를 감추었다. 로던이 들이닥쳐 망쳐 버린 그 자그마한 잔치를 위해 식탁에 차려 놓았던 은식기도 죄다 사라졌다. 마드무아젤이 남겨 둔 도금 식기들은 너무 거추장스러웠던 모양이고, 아마 그런 까닭에 부지깽이 따위 난로 도구들과 벽난로 거울, 그리고 자단으로 만든 오두막형 피아노도 함께 남겨 둔 것이 분명했다."]], [["A lady very like her subsequently kept a milliner's shop in the Rue du Helder at Paris, where she lived with great credit and enjoyed the patronage of my Lord Steyne.", "훗날 그녀와 무척 닮은 한 부인이 파리 엘데 거리에서 모자 가게를 운영하며 신용을 두텁게 쌓고 스테인 경의 후원까지 누렸다."], ["This person always spoke of England as of the most treacherous country in the world, and stated to her young pupils that she had been affreusement vole by natives of that island.", "이 인물은 늘 영국을 세상에서 가장 음흉한 나라라고 말하고 다녔으며, 어린 제자들에게 자신이 그 섬나라 토박이들에게 끔찍하게 도둑맞았노라고 이야기하곤 했다."], ["It was no doubt compassion for her misfortunes which induced the Marquis of Steyne to be so very kind to Madame de Saint-Amaranthe.", "스테인 후작이 생타마랑트 부인에게 그토록 친절을 베푼 것은 의심할 바 없이 그녀의 불운에 대한 동정심 때문이었으리라."], ["May she flourish as she deserves--she appears no more in our quarter of Vanity Fair.", "부디 그녀가 받아 마땅한 만큼 번창하기를 빈다. 그녀는 우리 허영의 시장 한 모퉁이에는 더 이상 나타나지 않는다."]], [["Hearing a buzz and a stir below, and indignant at the impudence of those servants who would not answer her summons, Mrs. Crawley flung her morning robe round her and descended majestically to the drawing-room, whence the noise proceeded.", "아래층에서 웅성거리는 소리와 부산한 기척이 들려오자, 자신의 부름에 응하지 않는 하인들의 뻔뻔함에 분개한 크롤리 부인은 실내복을 휙 걸치고는, 그 소리가 새어 나오는 응접실로 위풍당당하게 내려갔다."]], [["The cook was there with blackened face, seated on the beautiful chintz sofa by the side of Mrs.", "거기에는 요리사가 시커멓게 그을린 얼굴로, 그 아름다운 사라사 무명 소파에 래글스 부인과 나란히 앉아 그녀에게 마라스키노 술을 따라 주고 있었다."], ["Raggles, to whom she was administering Maraschino.", "설탕 덩어리 모양 단추가 달린 옷을 입고, 베키의 분홍빛 짧은 편지를 들고 다니며 그녀의 작은 마차에 그토록 날렵하게 뛰어오르던 시동 녀석은, 지금 크림 접시에 손가락을 처박고 있었다."], ["The page with the sugar-loaf buttons, who carried about Becky's pink notes, and jumped about her little carriage with such alacrity, was now engaged putting his fingers into a cream dish; the footman was talking to Raggles, who had a face full of perplexity and woe--and yet, though the door was open, and Becky had been screaming a half-dozen of times a few feet off, not one of her attendants had obeyed her call.", "하인은 래글스에게 말을 건네고 있었는데, 래글스의 얼굴은 당혹과 비탄으로 가득 차 있었다. 그런데도, 문이 열려 있고 베키가 불과 몇 발짝 떨어진 곳에서 대여섯 번이나 소리쳐 불렀건만, 시중드는 자들 가운데 그 누구도 그녀의 부름에 따르지 않았던 것이다."], ["\"Have a little drop, do'ee now, Mrs. Raggles,\"", "\"한 방울만 더 들어요, 자 어서요, 래글스 부인.\""], ["the cook was saying as Becky entered, the white cashmere dressing-gown flouncing around her.", "베키가 흰 캐시미어 실내복 자락을 펄럭이며 들어섰을 때 요리사가 그렇게 말하고 있었다."]], [["\"Simpson!", "\"심프슨!"], ["Trotter!\"", "트로터!\""], ["the mistress of the house cried in great wrath.", "이 집의 여주인이 노발대발하여 외쳤다."], ["\"How dare you stay here when you heard me call?", "\"내가 부르는 소리를 들었으면서 어찌 감히 여기 머물러 있는 게냐?"], ["How dare you sit down in my presence?", "어찌 감히 내 앞에서 앉아 있어?"], ["Where's my maid?\"", "내 하녀는 어디 있느냐?\""], ["The page withdrew his fingers from his mouth with a momentary terror, but the cook took off a glass of Maraschino, of which Mrs.", "시동은 순간적인 공포에 손가락을 입에서 빼냈으나, 요리사는 마라스키노 한 잔을 비웠다. 래글스 부인은 이미 충분히 마신 터였다."], ["Raggles had had enough, staring at Becky over the little gilt glass as she drained its contents.", "요리사는 작은 도금 잔의 술을 들이켜며 그 너머로 베키를 빤히 노려보았다."], ["The liquor appeared to give the odious rebel courage.", "그 술이 이 가증스러운 반역자에게 용기를 불어넣은 모양이었다."]], [["\"YOUR sofy, indeed!\" Mrs.", "\"당신 소파라니, 참!\""], ["Cook said. \"I'm a settin'", "요리사가 말했다."], ["on Mrs. Raggles's sofy.", "\"난 래글스 부인 소파에 앉아 있는 거예요."], ["Don't you stir, Mrs. Raggles, Mum.", "일어나지 마세요, 래글스 부인 마님."], ["I'm a settin' on Mr.", "난 래글스 내외분 소파에 앉아 있는 거라고요."], ["and Mrs. Raggles's sofy, which they bought with honest money, and very dear it cost 'em, too.", "두 분이 정직하게 번 돈으로 산 거고, 값도 어지간히 비싸게 치른 물건이지요."], ["And I'm thinkin' if I set here until I'm paid my wages, I shall set a precious long time, Mrs.", "그리고 내 생각엔, 내가 받을 품삯을 받을 때까지 여기 앉아 있으려면 한참을 앉아 있어야 할 것 같네요, 래글스 부인."], ["Raggles; and set I will, too--ha!", "그래, 앉아 있고말고요. 하하!\""], ["ha!\" and with this she filled herself another glass of the liquor and drank it with a more hideously satirical air.", "그러고는 그 술을 한 잔 더 따라, 한결 더 흉측하고 비아냥거리는 태도로 들이켰다."]], [["\"Trotter!", "\"트로터!"], ["Simpson!", "심프슨!"], ["turn that drunken wretch out,\"", "저 술 취한 망나니를 당장 내쫓아라.\""], ["screamed Mrs. Crawley.", "크롤리 부인이 비명을 질렀다."]], [["\"I shawn't,\"", "\"안 할 거요.\""], ["said Trotter the footman; \"turn out yourself.", "하인 트로터가 말했다. \"직접 내쫓으시구려."], ["Pay our selleries, and turn me out too.", "우리 품삯이나 내놓고, 나도 내쫓아 보슈."], ["WE'LL go fast enough.\"", "그럼 우린 잽싸게 나가 줄 테니.\""]], [["\"Are you all here to insult me?\"", "\"너희 모두 나를 모욕하려고 여기 모여 있는 게냐?\""], ["cried Becky in a fury; \"when Colonel Crawley comes home I'll--\"", "베키가 격분하여 외쳤다. \"크롤리 대령이 집에 돌아오면 내가—\""]], [["At this the servants burst into a horse haw-haw, in which, however, Raggles, who still kept a most melancholy countenance, did not join.", "이 말에 하인들이 거칠게 와하하 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 다만 여전히 더없이 침울한 낯빛을 짓고 있던 래글스만은 그 웃음에 끼지 않았다."], ["\"He ain't a coming back,\"", "\"그 양반은 안 돌아와요.\""], ["Mr. Trotter resumed.", "트로터가 말을 이었다."], ["\"He sent for his things, and I wouldn't let 'em go, although Mr.", "\"사람을 보내 짐을 가져가려고 했지요. 래글스 씨는 내주려 했지만 내가 안 내줬소."], ["Raggles would; and I don't b'lieve he's no more a Colonel than I am.", "게다가 그자가 대령이라는 것도 나만큼이나 못 믿겠더이다."], ["He's hoff, and I suppose you're a goin' after him.", "그 양반은 줄행랑쳤고, 당신도 그 뒤를 쫓아갈 셈인가 본데."], ["You're no better than swindlers, both on you.", "당신네 둘 다 사기꾼이나 진배없어."], ["Don't be a bullyin' ME.", "나한테 으름장 놓을 생각일랑 마슈."], ["I won't stand it.", "안 통하니까."], ["Pay us our selleries, I say.", "우리 품삯이나 내놓으라고."], ["Pay us our selleries.\"", "품삯 말이오.\""], ["It was evident, from Mr. Trotter's flushed countenance and defective intonation, that he, too, had had recourse to vinous stimulus.", "트로터의 벌게진 얼굴과 어눌한 말투로 보아, 그 역시 술기운을 빌린 것이 분명했다."]], [["\"Mr. Raggles,\"", "\"래글스 씨.\""], ["said Becky in a passion of vexation, \"you will not surely let me be insulted by that drunken man?\"", "베키가 분통이 치밀어 말했다. \"설마 저 술 취한 자에게 내가 모욕당하도록 가만 내버려 두지는 않으시겠지요?\""], ["\"Hold your noise, Trotter; do now,\"", "\"조용히 해요, 트로터. 자 어서요.\""], ["said Simpson the page.", "시동 심프슨이 말했다."], ["He was affected by his mistress's deplorable situation, and succeeded in preventing an outrageous denial of the epithet \"drunken\" on the footman's part.", "그는 여주인의 딱한 처지에 마음이 흔들린 터라, \"술 취한\"이라는 말에 하인이 무지막지하게 발끈하며 부인하려는 것을 가까스로 막아 냈다."]], [["\"Oh, M'am,\"", "\"오, 마님.\""], ["said Raggles, \"I never thought to live to see this year day: I've known the Crawley family ever since I was born.", "래글스가 말했다. \"이런 꼴을 보고 살게 될 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐습니다요."], ["I lived butler with Miss Crawley for thirty years; and I little thought one of that family was a goin'", "저는 태어났을 때부터 크롤리 집안을 알고 지냈지요. 크롤리 아씨 댁에서 삼십 년이나 집사로 일했고요."], ["to ruing me--yes, ruing me\"--said the poor fellow with tears in his eyes.", "그 집안 사람이 저를 망하게 할 줄이야, 그래요, 저를 망하게 할 줄이야 어찌 알았겠습니까.\" 가엾은 사내는 눈물을 글썽이며 말했다."], ["\"Har you a goin' to pay me?", "\"값을 치러 주시겠습니까요?"], ["You've lived in this 'ouse four year.", "마님은 이 집에서 사 년을 사셨지요."], ["You've 'ad my substance: my plate and linning.", "제 재산을, 제 식기며 침구를 다 쓰셨고요."], ["You ho me a milk and butter bill of two 'undred pound, you must 'ave noo laid heggs for your homlets, and cream for your spanil dog.\"", "우유랑 버터 값만 이백 파운드 외상이 있습니다요. 오믈렛 만드신다고 갓 낳은 달걀이 있어야 했지요, 애완견한테 먹일 크림도 있어야 했고요.\""]], [["\"She didn't care what her own flesh and blood had,\"", "\"제 핏줄이 뭘 먹는지도 아랑곳하지 않으셨다니까요.\""], ["interposed the cook.", "요리사가 끼어들었다."], ["\"Many's the time, he'd have starved but for me.\"", "\"내가 없었으면 그 도련님은 여러 번 굶어 죽었을 거예요.\""]], [["\"He's a charaty-boy now, Cooky,\" said Mr.", "\"그 도련님은 이제 자선 학교 학생이라우, 요리사 양반.\""], ["Trotter, with a drunken \"ha!", "트로터가 술 취한 목소리로 \"하하!\""], ["ha!\"--and honest Raggles continued, in a lamentable tone, an enumeration of his griefs.", "웃으며 말했다. 그리고 정직한 래글스는 비통한 어조로 자기 한탄을 줄줄이 늘어놓았다."], ["All he said was true.", "그가 한 말은 모두 사실이었다."], ["Becky and her husband had ruined him.", "베키와 그 남편이 그를 망쳐 놓은 것이다."], ["He had bills coming due next week and no means to meet them.", "다음 주에 만기가 돌아오는 어음들이 있는데 갚을 길이 없었다."], ["He would be sold up and turned out of his shop and his house, because he had trusted to the Crawley family.", "크롤리 집안을 믿었던 탓에, 그는 가게와 집을 차압당하고 쫓겨날 판이었다."], ["His tears and lamentations made Becky more peevish than ever.", "그의 눈물과 한탄에 베키는 그 어느 때보다 짜증이 났다."]], [["\"You all seem to be against me,\"", "\"다들 나에게 등을 돌린 모양이로구나.\""], ["she said bitterly.", "그녀가 매섭게 말했다."], ["\"What do you want?", "\"무엇들을 바라느냐?"], ["I can't pay you on Sunday.", "일요일에는 너희에게 돈을 줄 수가 없다."], ["Come back to-morrow and I'll pay you everything.", "내일 다시 오면 죄다 갚아 주마."], ["I thought Colonel Crawley had settled with you.", "나는 크롤리 대령이 너희와 셈을 마친 줄 알았다."], ["He will to-morrow.", "내일 그가 갚아 줄 게다."], ["I declare to you upon my honour that he left home this morning with fifteen hundred pounds in his pocket-book.", "내 명예를 걸고 너희에게 말하건대, 그이는 오늘 아침에 천오백 파운드를 수첩에 넣고 집을 나갔다."], ["He has left me nothing.", "나에게는 한 푼도 안 남겼어."], ["Apply to him.", "그이에게 청구해라."], ["Give me a bonnet and shawl and let me go out and find him.", "모자와 숄을 다오, 내가 나가서 그이를 찾아오마."], ["There was a difference between us this morning.", "오늘 아침에 우리 사이에 다툼이 있었다."], ["You all seem to know it.", "너희 모두 그걸 아는 모양이니."], ["I promise you upon my word that you shall all be paid.", "내 맹세코 너희 모두에게 돈을 치러 주마."], ["He has got a good appointment.", "그이는 좋은 자리를 얻었어."], ["Let me go out and find him.\"", "내가 나가서 그이를 찾게 해 다오.\""]], [["This audacious statement caused Raggles and the other personages present to look at one another with a wild surprise, and with it Rebecca left them.", "이 대담무쌍한 말에 래글스를 비롯해 거기 있던 다른 자들은 어안이 벙벙하여 서로를 마주 보았고, 레베카는 그 틈에 그들을 떠났다."], ["She went upstairs and dressed herself this time without the aid of her French maid.", "그녀는 위층으로 올라가, 이번에는 프랑스인 하녀의 도움 없이 직접 옷을 차려입었다."], ["She went into Rawdon's room, and there saw that a trunk and bag were packed ready for removal, with a pencil direction that they should be given when called for; then she went into the Frenchwoman's garret; everything was clean, and all the drawers emptied there.", "로던의 방에 들어가 보니, 트렁크 하나와 가방 하나가 옮겨 갈 채비를 마치고 꾸려져 있었고, 연필로 적은 쪽지가 붙어 있어 찾으러 오면 내주라고 일러두고 있었다. 이어 프랑스인 하녀의 다락방에 들어가 보니 모든 것이 말끔히 치워져 있었고, 그곳 서랍들도 죄다 비어 있었다."], ["She bethought herself of the trinkets which had been left on the ground and felt certain that the woman had fled.", "그녀는 바닥에 떨어져 있던 장신구들이 떠올랐고, 그 여자가 달아났음을 확신했다."], ["\"Good Heavens!", "\"맙소사!"], ["was ever such ill luck as mine?\"", "나 같은 불운이 또 어디 있단 말인가?\""], ["she said; \"to be so near, and to lose all.", "그녀가 말했다. \"이토록 가까이 와서, 모든 걸 잃다니."], ["Is it all too late?\"", "정녕 다 너무 늦은 걸까?\""], ["No; there was one chance more.", "아니다. 아직 한 번의 기회가 더 남아 있었다."]], [["She dressed herself and went away unmolested this time, but alone.", "그녀는 옷을 차려입고, 이번에는 아무에게도 방해받지 않고 길을 나섰으나, 혼자였다."], ["It was four o'clock.", "네 시였다."], ["She went swiftly down the streets (she had no money to pay for a carriage), and never stopped until she came to Sir Pitt Crawley's door, in Great Gaunt Street.", "그녀는 거리들을 빠르게 걸어 내려갔고(마차 삯을 치를 돈이 없었던 것이다), 그레이트 곤트 거리에 있는 피트 크롤리 경의 집 문 앞에 다다를 때까지 한 번도 멈추지 않았다."], ["Where was Lady Jane Crawley?", "제인 크롤리 부인은 어디 있는가?"], ["She was at church.", "그녀는 교회에 가 있었다."], ["Becky was not sorry.", "베키로서는 다행스러운 일이었다."], ["Sir Pitt was in his study, and had given orders not to be disturbed--she must see him--she slipped by the sentinel in livery at once, and was in Sir Pitt's room before the astonished Baronet had even laid down the paper.", "피트 경은 서재에 있었고 방해하지 말라고 일러 둔 터였으나, 그녀는 그를 꼭 만나야 했다. 그녀는 제복 입은 문지기를 단숨에 지나쳐, 놀란 준남작이 미처 신문을 내려놓기도 전에 피트 경의 방 안에 들어서 있었다."]], [["He turned red and started back from her with a look of great alarm and horror.", "그는 얼굴이 벌게져, 크게 놀라고 두려워하는 기색으로 그녀에게서 움찔 물러섰다."]], [["\"Do not look so,\"", "\"그런 눈으로 보지 마세요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"I am not guilty, Pitt, dear Pitt; you were my friend once.", "\"저는 죄가 없어요, 피트, 다정한 피트. 당신은 한때 제 벗이었잖아요."], ["Before God, I am not guilty.", "하느님 앞에 맹세코 저는 죄가 없어요."], ["I seem so.", "그렇게 보일 뿐이에요."], ["Everything is against me.", "모든 것이 저에게 불리하지요."], ["And oh! at such a moment!", "아, 게다가 이런 순간에!"], ["just when all my hopes were about to be realized: just when happiness was in store for us.\"", "제 모든 희망이 막 이루어지려던 바로 그때에, 우리에게 행복이 마련되어 있던 바로 그때에 말이에요.\""]], [["\"Is this true, what I see in the paper then?\"", "\"그렇다면, 내가 신문에서 본 게 사실이란 말이오?\""], ["Sir Pitt said--a paragraph in which had greatly surprised him.", "피트 경이 말했다. 거기에 실린 한 단락이 그를 몹시 놀라게 했던 것이다."]], [["\"It is true.", "\"사실이에요."], ["Lord Steyne told me on Friday night, the night of that fatal ball.", "스테인 경이 금요일 밤에, 그 끔찍한 무도회가 있던 밤에 제게 말해 주었어요."], ["He has been promised an appointment any time these six months. Mr.", "그이는 이 여섯 달 사이 언제든 자리를 주겠노라는 약속을 받아 두었지요."], ["Martyr, the Colonial Secretary, told him yesterday that it was made out.", "식민지 담당 장관인 마터 씨가 어제 그분께, 발령이 났다고 말했대요."], ["That unlucky arrest ensued; that horrible meeting.", "그런데 그 운 나쁜 체포 소동이 벌어졌고, 그 끔찍한 마주침이 일어난 거예요."], ["I was only guilty of too much devotedness to Rawdon's service.", "제가 지은 죄라고는 로던을 위해 너무 헌신했다는 것뿐이에요."], ["I have received Lord Steyne alone a hundred times before.", "저는 전에도 백 번이나 스테인 경을 혼자 만났어요."], ["I confess I had money of which Rawdon knew nothing.", "고백하건대, 저에게는 로던이 모르는 돈이 좀 있었어요."], ["Don't you know how careless he is of it, and could I dare to confide it to him?\"", "그이가 돈을 얼마나 함부로 쓰는지 모르세요? 그런 그이에게 제가 감히 그 돈을 맡길 수 있었겠어요?\""], ["And so she went on with a perfectly connected story, which she poured into the ears of her perplexed kinsman.", "그렇게 그녀는 완벽하게 앞뒤가 맞는 이야기를 줄줄 풀어, 어리둥절해하는 시숙의 귀에 쏟아부었다."]], [["It was to the following effect.", "그 내용인즉 다음과 같았다."], ["Becky owned, and with perfect frankness, but deep contrition, that having remarked Lord Steyne's partiality for her (at the mention of which Pitt blushed), and being secure of her own virtue, she had determined to turn the great peer's attachment to the advantage of herself and her family.", "베키는 더없이 솔직하게, 그러나 깊이 뉘우치는 태도로 털어놓았다. 스테인 경이 자신에게 호의를 보이는 것을 알아채고(이 대목에서 피트는 얼굴을 붉혔다), 또 제 정조에는 자신이 있었기에, 그 위대한 귀족의 애착을 자신과 가족의 이익을 위해 돌려보기로 마음먹었노라고."], ["\"I looked for a peerage for you, Pitt,\"", "\"저는 당신을 위해 귀족 작위를 얻어 보려고 했어요, 피트.\""], ["she said (the brother-in-law again turned red).", "그녀가 말했다(시숙은 또다시 얼굴이 벌게졌다)."], ["\"We have talked about it.", "\"우린 그 얘기를 나눴잖아요."], ["Your genius and Lord Steyne's interest made it more than probable, had not this dreadful calamity come to put an end to all our hopes.", "당신의 재능과 스테인 경의 영향력이면, 이 끔찍한 재앙이 닥쳐 우리 모든 희망을 끝장내지만 않았더라도 충분히 가망이 있었지요."], ["But, first, I own that it was my object to rescue my dear husband--him whom I love in spite of all his ill usage and suspicions of me--to remove him from the poverty and ruin which was impending over us.", "하지만 무엇보다 먼저, 제 목적은 사랑하는 남편을, 그이가 저를 그토록 푸대접하고 의심했음에도 제가 사랑하는 그 사람을 구해 내는 것이었어요. 우리 머리 위로 들이닥치던 가난과 파멸에서 그이를 건져 내는 것 말이에요."], ["I saw Lord Steyne's partiality for me,\"", "저는 스테인 경이 저에게 호의를 보이는 것을 알았어요.\""], ["she said, casting down her eyes.", "그녀가 눈을 내리깔며 말했다."], ["\"I own that I did everything in my power to make myself pleasing to him, and as far as an honest woman may, to secure his--his esteem.", "\"고백하건대, 저는 그분 마음에 들기 위해, 정숙한 여인으로서 할 수 있는 한도 안에서, 그분의—그분의 존중을 얻기 위해 제가 할 수 있는 모든 것을 했어요."], ["It was only on Friday morning that the news arrived of the death of the Governor of Coventry Island, and my Lord instantly secured the appointment for my dear husband.", "코번트리 섬 총독이 죽었다는 소식이 온 것이 바로 금요일 아침이었는데, 그분께서 즉시 사랑하는 제 남편을 위해 그 자리를 확보해 주셨지요."], ["It was intended as a surprise for him--he was to see it in the papers to-day.", "그건 그이를 위한 깜짝 선물로 마련된 것이었어요. 그이가 오늘 신문에서 보게 될 참이었지요."], ["Even after that horrid arrest took place (the expenses of which Lord Steyne generously said he would settle, so that I was in a manner prevented from coming to my husband's assistance), my Lord was laughing with me, and saying that my dearest Rawdon would be consoled when he read of his appointment in the paper, in that shocking spun--bailiff's house.", "그 끔찍한 체포 소동이 벌어진 뒤에도(그 비용은 스테인 경이 너그럽게 치러 주겠다고 하셔서, 저는 어떤 의미로는 남편을 도우러 갈 수가 없게 되었지요), 그분은 저와 함께 웃으시며, 사랑하는 로던이 그 끔찍한 빚쟁이 집에서 자기 발령 소식을 신문에서 읽으면 위로받을 거라고 말씀하셨어요. 그러고 나서—그러고 나서 그이가 집에 돌아온 거예요."], ["And then--then he came home.", "그이의 의심에 불이 붙었지요."], ["His suspicions were excited,--the dreadful scene took place between my Lord and my cruel, cruel Rawdon--and, O my God, what will happen next?", "그 끔찍한 장면이 그분과, 잔인하고 잔인한 제 로던 사이에 벌어졌어요. 아, 하느님, 이제 무슨 일이 벌어질까요?"], ["Pitt, dear Pitt! pity me, and reconcile us!\"", "피트, 다정한 피트!"], ["And as she spoke she flung herself down on her knees, and bursting into tears, seized hold of Pitt's hand, which she kissed passionately.", "저를 가엾이 여겨 주시고, 우리를 화해시켜 주세요!\" 그렇게 말하며 그녀는 무릎을 꿇고 엎드려, 울음을 터뜨리며 피트의 손을 붙잡고는 격정적으로 그 손에 입을 맞췄다."]], [["It was in this very attitude that Lady Jane, who, returning from church, ran to her husband's room directly she heard Mrs. Rawdon Crawley was closeted there, found the Baronet and his sister-in-law.", "바로 이 자세를 하고 있을 때, 교회에서 돌아온 제인 부인이 로던 크롤리 부인이 그 안에 들어앉아 있다는 말을 듣자마자 남편의 방으로 달려와, 준남작과 그 시숙의 아내를 발견한 것이었다."]], [["\"I am surprised that woman has the audacity to enter this house,\"", "\"저 여자가 뻔뻔하게 이 집에 들어왔다니 기가 막히는군요.\""], ["Lady Jane said, trembling in every limb and turning quite pale. (Her Ladyship had sent out her maid directly after breakfast, who had communicated with Raggles and Rawdon Crawley's household, who had told her all, and a great deal more than they knew, of that story, and many others besides).", "제인 부인이 온몸을 부들부들 떨며, 얼굴이 새파랗게 질려 말했다(부인은 아침 식사 직후에 하녀를 내보냈는데, 그 하녀가 래글스 및 로던 크롤리 댁 식솔들과 이야기를 나누고 왔으며, 그들은 자기들이 아는 것뿐 아니라 그 이상의 것까지 그 이야기를, 그리고 그 밖의 숱한 이야기들까지 하녀에게 죄다 일러바친 터였다)."], ["\"How dare Mrs. Crawley to enter the house of--of an honest family?\"", "\"어찌 감히 크롤리 부인이—정직한 집안의—집에 발을 들인단 말입니까?\""]], [["Sir Pitt started back, amazed at his wife's display of vigour.", "피트 경은 아내가 이토록 기세를 부리는 데 놀라 움찔 물러섰다."], ["Becky still kept her kneeling posture and clung to Sir Pitt's hand.", "베키는 여전히 무릎 꿇은 자세로 피트 경의 손에 매달려 있었다."]], [["\"Tell her that she does not know all: Tell her that I am innocent, dear Pitt,\"", "\"제가 다 알지는 못한다고 부인께 말해 주세요. 제가 결백하다고 말해 주세요, 다정한 피트.\""], ["she whimpered out.", "그녀가 훌쩍이며 말했다."]], [["\"Upon my word, my love, I think you do Mrs. Crawley injustice,\"", "\"정말이지, 여보, 당신이 크롤리 부인에게 부당하게 구는 것 같소.\""], ["Sir Pitt said; at which speech Rebecca was vastly relieved.", "피트 경이 말했다. 이 말에 레베카는 크게 안도했다."], ["\"Indeed I believe her to be--\"", "\"정말로 나는 이 사람이—\""]], [["\"To be what?\"", "\"이 사람이 뭐라고요?\""], ["cried out Lady Jane, her clear voice thrilling and, her heart beating violently as she spoke.", "제인 부인이 외쳤다. 그녀의 맑은 목소리가 떨려 나왔고, 말하는 동안 가슴이 격렬하게 뛰었다."], ["\"To be a wicked woman--a heartless mother, a false wife?", "\"사악한 여자라고요? 매정한 어미요, 부정한 아내라고요?"], ["She never loved her dear little boy, who used to fly here and tell me of her cruelty to him.", "저 여자는 제 어린 아들을 사랑한 적이 한 번도 없어요. 그 아이는 이리로 도망쳐 와서, 어미가 자기에게 얼마나 모질게 구는지 제게 이야기하곤 했지요."], ["She never came into a family but she strove to bring misery with her and to weaken the most sacred affections with her wicked flattery and falsehoods.", "저 여자는 어느 집안에 들어가든 불행을 끌고 들어와서, 그 사악한 아첨과 거짓말로 가장 거룩한 정마저 약하게 만들려고 안달이었어요."], ["She has deceived her husband, as she has deceived everybody; her soul is black with vanity, worldliness, and all sorts of crime.", "저 여자는 남편을 속였어요, 모든 이를 속였듯이 말이에요. 그 영혼은 허영과 속물근성과 온갖 죄악으로 새카맣지요."], ["I tremble when I touch her.", "저 여자에게 손이 닿기만 해도 몸서리가 쳐져요."], ["I keep my children out of her sight.\"", "제 아이들은 저 여자 눈에 띄지 않게 해 둔답니다.\""]], [["\"Lady Jane!\"", "\"제인!\""], ["cried Sir Pitt, starting up, \"this is really language--\"", "피트 경이 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다. \"이건 정말이지 말이 지나친—\""], ["\"I have been a true and faithful wife to you, Sir Pitt,\"", "\"저는 당신께 진실하고 충실한 아내였어요, 피트.\" 제인 부인이 굴하지 않고 말을 이었다."], ["Lady Jane continued, intrepidly; \"I have kept my marriage vow as I made it to God and have been obedient and gentle as a wife should.", "\"저는 하느님 앞에 세운 혼인 서약을 지켜 왔고, 아내로서 마땅히 그래야 하듯 순종하고 다정하게 굴어 왔어요. 하지만 올바른 순종에도 한계가 있는 법이에요."], ["But righteous obedience has its limits, and I declare that I will not bear that--that woman again under my roof; if she enters it, I and my children will leave it.", "그러니 분명히 말씀드리는데, 저 여자가 다시 우리 지붕 아래 들어오는 것을 저는 결코 참지 못하겠어요. 저 여자가 들어온다면, 저와 제 아이들은 이 집을 나가겠어요."], ["She is not worthy to sit down with Christian people.", "저 여자는 그리스도교도와 한자리에 앉을 자격이 없어요."], ["You--you must choose, sir, between her and me\"; and with this my Lady swept out of the room, fluttering with her own audacity, and leaving Rebecca and Sir Pitt not a little astonished at it.", "당신은—당신은 저 여자와 저 가운데 하나를 택하셔야 해요.\" 그러고는 부인은 제 대담함에 스스로 떨면서 방을 휙 나가 버렸고, 레베카와 피트 경은 적잖이 놀란 채 남겨졌다."]], [["As for Becky, she was not hurt; nay, she was pleased.", "베키로 말하자면, 상처받기는커녕 오히려 흐뭇해했다."], ["\"It was the diamond-clasp you gave me,\"", "\"다이아몬드 걸쇠는 당신이 주신 거였어요.\""], ["she said to Sir Pitt, reaching him out her hand; and before she left him (for which event you may be sure my Lady Jane was looking out from her dressing-room window in the upper story) the Baronet had promised to go and seek out his brother, and endeavour to bring about a reconciliation.", "그녀가 피트 경에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. 그리고 그녀가 그곳을 떠나기 전에(이 장면을 제인 부인이 위층 화장실 창문으로 내다보고 있었으리라는 것은 틀림없는 일이다), 준남작은 가서 동생을 찾아 화해를 주선해 보겠노라고 약속했다."]], [["Rawdon found some of the young fellows of the regiment seated in the mess-room at breakfast, and was induced without much difficulty to partake of that meal, and of the devilled legs of fowls and soda-water with which these young gentlemen fortified themselves.", "로던은 연대의 젊은 친구들 몇이 회식실에 앉아 아침을 먹고 있는 것을 발견했고, 별 어려움 없이 그 식사 자리에, 그리고 이 젊은 신사들이 기운을 차리고자 들고 있던 매운 양념 닭다리 구이며 소다수에 끼게 되었다."], ["Then they had a conversation befitting the day and their time of life: about the next pigeon-match at Battersea, with relative bets upon Ross and Osbaldiston; about Mademoiselle Ariane of the French Opera, and who had left her, and how she was consoled by Panther Carr; and about the fight between the Butcher and the Pet, and the probabilities that it was a cross.", "이어 그들은 그 요일과 제 나이에 어울리는 대화를 나누었다. 배터시에서 열릴 다음 비둘기 사격 시합과, 거기에 로스와 오스발디스턴을 두고 건 내기 이야기, 프랑스 오페라단의 아리안 양과, 누가 그녀를 버렸는지, 그리고 팬서 카가 어떻게 그녀를 위로해 주었는지에 대한 이야기, 또 도살자와 애완동물 사이의 시합이며 그것이 짜고 친 판일 가능성에 대한 이야기 따위였다."], ["Young Tandyman, a hero of seventeen, laboriously endeavouring to get up a pair of mustachios, had seen the fight, and spoke in the most scientific manner about the battle and the condition of the men.", "콧수염 한 쌍을 기르려고 안간힘을 쓰고 있는 열일곱 살의 영웅 탠디먼 청년은 그 시합을 직접 보고 왔고, 더없이 전문가다운 태도로 그 경기와 선수들의 상태에 대해 떠들어 댔다."], ["It was he who had driven the Butcher on to the ground in his drag and passed the whole of the previous night with him.", "그가 바로 자기 사두마차에 도살자를 태워 경기장까지 데려다주고, 전날 밤 내내 그와 함께 지낸 장본인이었다."], ["Had there not been foul play he must have won it.", "부정만 없었더라면 도살자가 틀림없이 이겼으리라는 것이었다."], ["All the old files of the Ring were in it; and Tandyman wouldn't pay; no, dammy, he wouldn't pay.", "권투계의 그 노련한 패거리가 죄다 끼어 있었고, 탠디먼은 돈을 못 내겠다는 것이었다. 그래, 빌어먹을, 못 내겠다는 것이었다."], ["It was but a year since the young Cornet, now so knowing a hand in Cribb's parlour, had a still lingering liking for toffy, and used to be birched at Eton.", "지금은 크리브의 술집에서 제법 빠삭한 솜씨를 자랑하는 이 젊은 기수가, 토피 사탕을 여전히 좋아하고 이튼에서 회초리를 맞던 것이 불과 일 년 전 일이었다."]], [["So they went on talking about dancers, fights, drinking, demireps, until Macmurdo came down and joined the boys and the conversation.", "그렇게 그들은 무희며 시합이며 술이며 화류계 여자들 이야기를 이어 갔고, 이윽고 맥머도가 내려와 청년들과 그 대화에 합류했다. 그는 그들의 풋내기 시절에 무슨 각별한 존중을 보여야 한다고는 여기지 않는 듯했다."], ["He did not appear to think that any especial reverence was due to their boyhood; the old fellow cut in with stories, to the full as choice as any the youngest rake present had to tell--nor did his own grey hairs nor their smooth faces detain him.", "그 노인은 자기 이야기를 척척 끼워 넣었는데, 그것은 거기 있던 가장 어린 난봉꾼이 들려줄 수 있는 그 어떤 이야기 못지않게 진한 것이었다. 자기 머리가 희끗희끗하다거나 그들 얼굴이 매끈하다거나 하는 것도 그를 멈추게 하지 못했다."], ["Old Mac was famous for his good stories.", "늙은 맥은 그 걸쭉한 이야기 솜씨로 유명했다."], ["He was not exactly a lady's man; that is, men asked him to dine rather at the houses of their mistresses than of their mothers.", "그는 딱히 부인들에게 인기 있는 부류는 아니었다. 그러니까, 사람들이 그를 식사에 초대할 때는 어머니 댁보다는 정부의 집으로 부르는 쪽이었다는 말이다."], ["There can scarcely be a life lower, perhaps, than his, but he was quite contented with it, such as it was, and led it in perfect good nature, simplicity, and modesty of demeanour.", "아마 그의 삶보다 더 낮은 삶이란 거의 없을 테지만, 그는 그런대로 제 삶에 흡족해했고, 더없이 선량하고 소박하며 겸손한 태도로 그 삶을 살아갔다."]], [["By the time Mac had finished a copious breakfast, most of the others had concluded their meal.", "맥이 푸짐한 아침 식사를 끝마칠 무렵에는 다른 이들 대부분이 식사를 마친 뒤였다."], ["Young Lord Varinas was smoking an immense Meerschaum pipe, while Captain Hugues was employed with a cigar: that violent little devil Tandyman, with his little bull-terrier between his legs, was tossing for shillings with all his might (that fellow was always at some game or other) against Captain Deuceace; and Mac and Rawdon walked off to the Club, neither, of course, having given any hint of the business which was occupying their minds.", "젊은 바리나스 경은 어마어마하게 큰 해포석 파이프를 피우고 있었고, 위그 대위는 시가를 즐기고 있었다. 저 사납고 자그마한 악동 탠디먼은 두 다리 사이에 자기 작은 불테리어를 끼고서, 듀시스 대위를 상대로 동전 던지기에 온 힘을 쏟고 있었다(그 친구는 언제나 무슨 노름이든 하고 있었다)."], ["Both, on the other hand, had joined pretty gaily in the conversation, for why should they interrupt it?", "그리고 맥과 로던은 함께 클럽으로 발길을 옮겼는데, 물론 두 사람 다 자기들 머릿속을 차지하고 있는 그 용건에 대해서는 한마디도 내비치지 않았다."], ["Feasting, drinking, ribaldry, laughter, go on alongside of all sorts of other occupations in Vanity Fair--the crowds were pouring out of church as Rawdon and his friend passed down St.", "도리어 두 사람 다 그 대화에 제법 명랑하게 어울렸으니, 굳이 흥을 깰 까닭이 있겠는가? 허영의 시장에서는 잔치며 음주며 음담패설이며 웃음이, 온갖 다른 일들과 나란히 굴러가는 법이다."], ["James's Street and entered into their Club.", "로던과 그의 벗이 세인트제임스 거리를 내려가 클럽에 들어설 무렵, 사람들이 교회에서 쏟아져 나오고 있었다."]], [["The old bucks and habitues, who ordinarily stand gaping and grinning out of the great front window of the Club, had not arrived at their posts as yet--the newspaper-room was almost empty. One man was present whom Rawdon did not know; another to whom he owed a little score for whist, and whom, in consequence, he did not care to meet; a third was reading the Royalist (a periodical famous for its scandal and its attachment to Church and King) Sunday paper at the table, and looking up at Crawley with some interest, said, \"Crawley, I congratulate you.\"", "평소 클럽의 큰 정면 창문 밖을 멍하니 입 벌리고 히죽거리며 내다보곤 하는 늙은 멋쟁이들과 단골들은 아직 제자리에 당도하지 않은 터였다. 신문 열람실은 거의 텅 비어 있었다. 한 사람은 로던이 모르는 자였고, 또 한 사람은 휘스트 노름으로 그에게 약간의 빚이 있어 그런 까닭에 그가 마주치고 싶지 않은 자였다. 세 번째 사람은 탁자에서 일요판 〈로열리스트〉(추문 보도와 교회 및 왕실에 대한 충성으로 유명한 정기 간행물이었다)를 읽고 있었는데, 자못 관심 어린 눈으로 크롤리를 올려다보며 말했다. \"크롤리, 축하하오.\""]], [["\"What do you mean?\"", "\"무슨 말씀이오?\""], ["said the Colonel.", "대령이 말했다."]], [["\"It's in the Observer and the Royalist too,\"", "\"〈옵서버〉에도 〈로열리스트〉에도 났던데.\""], ["said Mr. Smith.", "스미스 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"What?\" Rawdon cried, turning very red.", "\"뭐가 말이오?\""], ["He thought that the affair with Lord Steyne was already in the public prints.", "로던이 얼굴이 새빨개지며 외쳤다. 그는 스테인 경과의 그 사건이 벌써 인쇄물에 났구나 싶었던 것이다."], ["Smith looked up wondering and smiling at the agitation which the Colonel exhibited as he took up the paper and, trembling, began to read.", "스미스는 대령이 신문을 집어 들고 떨면서 읽기 시작하는 모습에 드러난 그 동요를, 의아한 눈빛으로 미소 지으며 올려다보았다."]], [["Mr. Smith and Mr. Brown (the gentleman with whom Rawdon had the outstanding whist account) had been talking about the Colonel just before he came in.", "스미스 씨와 브라운 씨(로던과 휘스트 빚을 셈해야 할 사이인 신사였다)는 대령이 들어오기 직전까지 그에 대해 이야기를 나누던 참이었다."]], [["\"It is come just in the nick of time,\"", "\"마침 딱 좋은 때에 들어왔구먼.\""], ["said Smith.", "스미스가 말했다."], ["\"I suppose Crawley had not a shilling in the world.\"", "\"내 짐작에 크롤리는 한 푼도 없는 신세였을 거요.\""]], [["\"It's a wind that blows everybody good,\"", "\"바람이 누구에게나 좋게 부는 격이지.\""], ["Mr. Brown said.", "브라운 씨가 말했다."], ["\"He can't go away without paying me a pony he owes me.\"", "\"이제 그자가 나한테 진 빚 이십오 파운드를 안 갚고는 떠나지 못할 테니.\""]], [["\"What's the salary?\"", "\"봉급이 얼마요?\""], ["asked Smith.", "스미스가 물었다."]], [["\"Two or three thousand,\"", "\"이삼천 파운드쯤.\""], ["answered the other.", "상대가 대답했다."], ["\"But the climate's so infernal, they don't enjoy it long.", "\"하지만 기후가 어찌나 지독한지, 오래 누리지를 못한다오."], ["Liverseege died after eighteen months of it, and the man before went off in six weeks, I hear.\"", "리버시지는 십팔 개월 만에 죽었고, 그 전 사람은 육 주 만에 갔다더군.\""]], [["\"Some people say his brother is a very clever man.", "\"어떤 이들은 그자의 형이 아주 영리한 사람이라고들 하더이다."], ["I always found him a d------ bore,\"", "난 늘 그 인간이 지독하게 따분하기만 하던데.\""], ["Smith ejaculated.", "스미스가 내뱉었다."], ["\"He must have good interest, though.", "\"그래도 든든한 연줄이 있는 게 분명해."], ["He must have got the Colonel the place.\"", "그가 대령에게 그 자리를 얻어 준 게 틀림없으니까.\""]], [["\"He!\"", "\"그가?\""], ["said Brown, with a sneer.", "브라운이 코웃음을 치며 말했다."], ["\"Pooh.", "\"쳇."], ["It was Lord Steyne got it.\"", "그 자리를 얻어 준 건 스테인 경이라오.\""]], [["\"How do you mean?\"", "\"그게 무슨 말이오?\""]], [["\"A virtuous woman is a crown to her husband,\"", "\"정숙한 아내는 남편의 면류관이라지 않소.\""], ["answered the other enigmatically, and went to read his papers.", "상대가 알쏭달쏭하게 대답하고는, 신문을 마저 읽으러 가 버렸다."]], [["Rawdon, for his part, read in the Royalist the following astonishing paragraph:", "한편 로던은 〈로열리스트〉에서 다음과 같은 놀라운 단락을 읽었다."]], [["GOVERNORSHIP OF COVENTRY ISLAND.--H.M.S.", "코번트리 섬 총독직.—국왕 폐하의 군함 옐로잭 호(함장 존더스)가 코번트리 섬에서 서신과 서류를 가져왔다."], ["Yellowjack, Commander Jaunders, has brought letters and papers from Coventry Island. H.", "총독 토머스 리버시지 경 각하께서 스왐프턴에 창궐한 열병의 희생양이 되셨다."], ["E. Sir Thomas Liverseege had fallen a victim to the prevailing fever at Swampton.", "번창하던 식민지에서 그의 죽음을 깊이 애도하는 분위기다."], ["His loss is deeply felt in the flourishing colony. We hear that the Governorship has been offered to Colonel Rawdon Crawley, C.B., a distinguished Waterloo officer.", "듣건대 그 총독직이 워털루 전투의 공로로 이름난 장교, 무공훈장 수훈자 로던 크롤리 대령에게 제안되었다고 한다."], ["We need not only men of acknowledged bravery, but men of administrative talents to superintend the affairs of our colonies, and we have no doubt that the gentleman selected by the Colonial Office to fill the lamented vacancy which has occurred at Coventry Island is admirably calculated for the post which he is about to occupy.", "우리 식민지의 사무를 관장하는 데에는 공인된 용맹을 갖춘 인물뿐 아니라 행정적 재능을 갖춘 인물도 필요한 법인데, 코번트리 섬에서 일어난 이 애통한 공석을 메우고자 식민지청이 선발한 신사라면 그가 곧 맡게 될 그 직책에 더없이 걸맞은 인물임이 분명하리라 의심치 않는다."]], [["\"Coventry Island!", "\"코번트리 섬이라!"], ["Where was it?", "그게 어디였더라?"], ["Who had appointed him to the government?", "누가 자네를 그 총독 자리에 임명했단 말인가?"], ["You must take me out as your secretary, old boy,\"", "자넨 나를 비서관으로 데려가야 하네, 친구.\""], ["Captain Macmurdo said laughing; and as Crawley and his friend sat wondering and perplexed over the announcement, the Club waiter brought in to the Colonel a card on which the name of Mr.", "맥머도 대위가 웃으며 말했다. 크롤리와 그의 벗이 그 발표를 두고 어리둥절하고 당혹스러워하며 앉아 있는데, 클럽 종업원이 대령에게 명함 한 장을 가져왔다."], ["Wenham was engraved, who begged to see Colonel Crawley.", "거기에는 웬햄 씨라는 이름이 새겨져 있었고, 크롤리 대령을 뵙기를 청한다고 적혀 있었다."]], [["The Colonel and his aide-de-camp went out to meet the gentleman, rightly conjecturing that he was an emissary of Lord Steyne.", "대령과 그의 부관은 그 신사를 맞으러 나갔는데, 그가 스테인 경의 사자(使者)이리라고 옳게 짐작한 터였다."], ["\"How d'ye do, Crawley?", "\"잘 지내시오, 크롤리?"], ["I am glad to see you,\"", "만나서 반갑소이다.\""], ["said Mr. Wenham with a bland smile, and grasping Crawley's hand with great cordiality.", "웬햄 씨가 부드러운 미소를 지으며, 더없이 다정하게 크롤리의 손을 잡고 말했다."]], [["\"You come, I suppose, from--\"", "\"당신은 짐작건대, 어느 분의—\""]], [["\"Exactly,\"", "\"바로 그렇소.\""], ["said Mr. Wenham.", "웬햄 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"Then this is my friend Captain Macmurdo, of the Life Guards Green.\"", "\"그렇다면 이쪽은 내 벗인 근위 기병 녹색 부대의 맥머도 대위요.\""]], [["\"Delighted to know Captain Macmurdo, I'm sure,\" Mr.", "\"맥머도 대위를 알게 되어 진심으로 기쁘오.\""], ["Wenham said and tendered another smile and shake of the hand to the second, as he had done to the principal.", "웬햄 씨가 말하며, 앞서 주인에게 그랬듯 부관에게도 또 한 번 미소와 악수를 건넸다."], ["Mac put out one finger, armed with a buckskin glove, and made a very frigid bow to Mr. Wenham over his tight cravat.", "맥은 사슴 가죽 장갑을 낀 손가락 하나만 내밀고는, 빳빳한 넥타이 위로 웬햄 씨에게 더없이 싸늘한 인사를 보냈다."], ["He was, perhaps, discontented at being put in communication with a pekin, and thought that Lord Steyne should have sent him a Colonel at the very least.", "그는 아마도 민간인 나부랭이와 상대하게 된 것이 못마땅했던 모양이고, 스테인 경이라면 적어도 대령 정도는 보냈어야 했다고 여기는 듯했다."]], [["\"As Macmurdo acts for me, and knows what I mean,\"", "\"맥머도가 나를 대신하여 행동하고, 내 뜻을 잘 아는 터이니.\""], ["Crawley said, \"I had better retire and leave you together.\"", "크롤리가 말했다. \"내가 물러나서 두 분이 함께 있게 하는 편이 낫겠소.\""]], [["\"Of course,\"", "\"물론이지.\""], ["said Macmurdo.", "맥머도가 말했다."]], [["\"By no means, my dear Colonel,\"", "\"천만의 말씀이오, 대령.\""], ["Mr.", "웬햄 씨가 말했다."], ["Wenham said; \"the interview which I had the honour of requesting was with you personally, though the company of Captain Macmurdo cannot fail to be also most pleasing.", "\"내가 영광스럽게도 청한 이 면담은 당신 개인과의 자리였소. 물론 맥머도 대위가 함께 계시는 것 또한 더없이 흐뭇한 일임에 틀림없지만 말이오."], ["In fact, Captain, I hope that our conversation will lead to none but the most agreeable results, very different from those which my friend Colonel Crawley appears to anticipate.\"", "사실, 대위, 나는 우리 대화가 더없이 흡족한 결과만을 낳기를 바라는 바요. 내 벗 크롤리 대령이 예상하는 듯한 그런 결과와는 사뭇 다른 것 말이오.\""]], [["\"Humph!\"", "\"흠!\""], ["said Captain Macmurdo.", "맥머도 대위가 말했다."], ["Be hanged to these civilians, he thought to himself, they are always for arranging and speechifying.", "이 민간인 나부랭이들이란, 그는 속으로 생각했다, 늘 일을 조정하니 연설을 늘어놓느니 한단 말이야."], ["Mr. Wenham took a chair which was not offered to him--took a paper from his pocket, and resumed--", "웬햄 씨는 권하지도 않은 의자를 끌어다 앉아, 주머니에서 종이 한 장을 꺼내고는 말을 이었다."]], [["\"You have seen this gratifying announcement in the papers this morning, Colonel?", "\"오늘 아침 신문에서 이 기쁜 발표를 보셨겠지요, 대령?"], ["Government has secured a most valuable servant, and you, if you accept office, as I presume you will, an excellent appointment.", "정부는 더없이 값진 일꾼을 확보했고, 당신은 그 직책을 받아들이신다면, 짐작건대 받아들이실 테지만, 훌륭한 자리를 얻으신 셈이오."], ["Three thousand a year, delightful climate, excellent government-house, all your own way in the Colony, and a certain promotion.", "연봉 삼천 파운드에, 쾌적한 기후, 더할 나위 없는 총독 관저, 식민지에서 모든 일을 마음대로 할 수 있는 권한, 그리고 확실한 승진까지."], ["I congratulate you with all my heart.", "진심으로 축하드리오."], ["I presume you know, gentlemen, to whom my friend is indebted for this piece of patronage?\"", "두 분께서는 짐작건대, 내 벗이 누구의 후원으로 이 자리를 얻게 되었는지 아시겠지요?\""]], [["\"Hanged if I know,\"", "\"내가 알 게 뭐람.\""], ["the Captain said; his principal turned very red.", "대위가 말했다. 그의 주인은 얼굴이 새빨개졌다."]], [["\"To one of the most generous and kindest men in the world, as he is one of the greatest--to my excellent friend, the Marquis of Steyne.\"", "\"세상에서 가장 너그럽고 친절한 분 가운데 하나이자, 또한 가장 위대한 분 가운데 하나인 분—내 훌륭한 벗, 스테인 후작 덕분이오.\""]], [["\"I'll see him d---- before I take his place,\"", "\"그의 자리를 받느니 차라리 그자가 지옥에 떨어지는 꼴을 보겠소.\""], ["growled out Rawdon.", "로던이 으르렁거렸다."]], [["\"You are irritated against my noble friend,\" Mr.", "\"당신은 내 고귀한 벗에게 노여움을 품고 있구려.\""], ["Wenham calmly resumed; \"and now, in the name of common sense and justice, tell me why?\"", "웬햄 씨가 침착하게 말을 이었다. \"자, 그렇다면 상식과 정의의 이름으로, 어째서 그런지 말해 보시오.\""]], [["\"WHY?\"", "\"어째서라니?\""], ["cried Rawdon in surprise.", "로던이 놀라 외쳤다."]], [["\"Why? Dammy!\"", "\"어째서냐고?"], ["said the Captain, ringing his stick on the ground.", "빌어먹을!\" 대위가 지팡이로 바닥을 쾅 치며 말했다."]], [["\"Dammy, indeed,\" said Mr.", "\"빌어먹을, 그래요.\""], ["Wenham with the most agreeable smile; \"still, look at the matter as a man of the world--as an honest man--and see if you have not been in the wrong.", "웬햄 씨가 더없이 상냥한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"그래도, 세상 물정 아는 사람으로서—정직한 사람으로서—이 일을 한번 보시오."], ["You come home from a journey, and find--what?--my Lord Steyne supping at your house in Curzon Street with Mrs. Crawley.", "그리고 당신이 그릇된 것은 아니었는지 따져 보시오."], ["Is the circumstance strange or novel?", "당신은 여행에서 돌아와 무엇을 보았소?"], ["Has he not been a hundred times before in the same position?", "스테인 경께서 커즌 거리의 당신 댁에서 크롤리 부인과 저녁을 들고 계신 광경 아니오."], ["Upon my honour and word as a gentleman\"--Mr.", "그게 무슨 괴이하거나 새삼스러운 일이오?"], ["Wenham here put his hand on his waistcoat with a parliamentary air--\"I declare I think that your suspicions are monstrous and utterly unfounded, and that they injure an honourable gentleman who has proved his good-will towards you by a thousand benefactions--and a most spotless and innocent lady.\"", "그분이 전에도 백 번이나 똑같은 상황에 계시지 않았소? 신사로서 내 명예와 말을 걸고서\"—웬햄 씨는 여기서 의회 연설하듯 한 손을 조끼에 얹었다—\"단언컨대 당신의 의심은 터무니없고 전혀 근거가 없으며, 그 의심이 당신에게 천 가지 은혜를 베풀어 호의를 입증한 명예로운 신사 한 분과, 더없이 흠 없고 결백한 부인 한 분을 해치고 있다고 나는 보는 바요.\""]], [["\"You don't mean to say that--that Crawley's mistaken?\"", "\"그렇다면—크롤리가 잘못 짚었다는 말씀이오?\""], ["said Mr. Macmurdo.", "맥머도 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"I believe that Mrs. Crawley is as innocent as my wife, Mrs.", "\"나는 크롤리 부인이 내 아내 웬햄 부인만큼이나 결백하다고 믿소.\""], ["Wenham,\" Mr.", "웬햄 씨가 힘주어 말했다."], ["Wenham said with great energy. \"I believe that, misled by an infernal jealousy, my friend here strikes a blow against not only an infirm and old man of high station, his constant friend and benefactor, but against his wife, his own dearest honour, his son's future reputation, and his own prospects in life.\"", "\"내가 믿기로는, 여기 내 벗이 못된 질투에 사로잡혀 그만, 높은 지위에 있는 노쇠한 노인이자 한결같은 벗이요 은인인 분을 칠 뿐 아니라, 제 아내와 자신의 더없이 소중한 명예와 아들의 장래 평판과 제 자신의 앞날까지 함께 치고 있는 것이오.\""]], [["\"I will tell you what happened,\" Mr.", "\"무슨 일이 있었는지 말씀드리리다.\""], ["Wenham continued with great solemnity; \"I was sent for this morning by my Lord Steyne, and found him in a pitiable state, as, I need hardly inform Colonel Crawley, any man of age and infirmity would be after a personal conflict with a man of your strength.", "웬햄 씨가 자못 엄숙하게 말을 이었다. \"오늘 아침 스테인 경께서 나를 부르셨는데, 가 보니 그분은 가련한 상태에 계셨소."], ["I say to your face; it was a cruel advantage you took of that strength, Colonel Crawley.", "당신처럼 힘센 자와 몸싸움을 벌인 뒤라면 나이 들고 노쇠한 누구든 그러할 것임은 크롤리 대령께 굳이 말씀드릴 필요도 없겠지요."], ["It was not only the body of my noble and excellent friend which was wounded--his heart, sir, was bleeding.", "면전에 대고 말하건대, 당신은 그 힘을 비겁하게 이용한 것이오, 크롤리 대령."], ["A man whom he had loaded with benefits and regarded with affection had subjected him to the foulest indignity.", "내 고귀하고 훌륭한 벗은 몸만 다친 것이 아니오. 그 마음이, 그래요, 그 마음이 피 흘리고 있었소."], ["What was this very appointment, which appears in the journals of to-day, but a proof of his kindness to you?", "그분이 온갖 은혜를 베풀고 애정으로 대하던 자가, 그분에게 더없이 추악한 모욕을 안긴 것이오."], ["When I saw his Lordship this morning I found him in a state pitiable indeed to see, and as anxious as you are to revenge the outrage committed upon him, by blood.", "오늘 신문에 난 바로 이 발령이야말로, 그분이 당신에게 베푼 친절의 증거가 아니고 무엇이겠소? 오늘 아침 내가 뵌 그분은 참으로 보기에 가련한 상태였고, 자신이 당한 그 모욕을 피로써 갚으려고 당신 못지않게 안달하고 계셨소."], ["You know he has given his proofs, I presume, Colonel Crawley?\"", "짐작건대, 그분이 그런 일에 용기를 입증하셨다는 것은 알고 계시겠지요, 크롤리 대령?\""]], [["\"He has plenty of pluck,\"", "\"그자는 배짱 하나는 두둑하지.\""], ["said the Colonel.", "대령이 말했다."], ["\"Nobody ever said he hadn't.\"", "\"그 점을 부인한 사람은 아무도 없었소.\""]], [["\"His first order to me was to write a letter of challenge, and to carry it to Colonel Crawley.", "\"그분이 제게 내리신 첫 명령은, 결투장을 써서 그것을 크롤리 대령에게 전하라는 것이었소. 그분은 '우리 둘 중 하나는,'"], ["One or other of us,\"", "이렇게 말씀하셨소."], ["he said, \"must not survive the outrage of last night.\"", "'간밤의 그 모욕을 겪고서 살아남아서는 안 된다'고 말이오.\""]], [["Crawley nodded.", "크롤리가 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["\"You're coming to the point, Wenham,\"", "\"본론으로 들어오는구려, 웬햄.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"I tried my utmost to calm Lord Steyne.", "\"나는 스테인 경을 진정시키려고 온 힘을 다했소."], ["'Good God! sir,'", "'맙소사, 경이시여,'"], ["I said, 'how I regret that Mrs.", "내가 말했지요."], ["Wenham and myself had not accepted Mrs. Crawley's invitation to sup with her!'\"", "'웬햄 부인과 제가 크롤리 부인의 저녁 초대를 받아들이지 않은 것이 어찌나 후회스러운지요!'\""]], [["\"She asked you to sup with her?\"", "\"부인이 당신더러 저녁을 함께 들자고 청했소?\""], ["Captain Macmurdo said.", "맥머도 대위가 말했다."]], [["\"After the opera.", "\"오페라가 끝난 뒤에 말이오."], ["Here's the note of invitation--stop--no, this is another paper--I thought I had it, but it's of no consequence, and I pledge you my word to the fact. If we had come--and it was only one of Mrs.", "여기 그 초대장이—잠깐만—아니, 이건 다른 종이로군—분명 가지고 있었는데, 뭐 그건 중요한 게 아니고, 그 사실에 대해서는 내 말을 걸고 보증하리다."], ["Wenham's headaches which prevented us--she suffers under them a good deal, especially in the spring--if we had come, and you had returned home, there would have been no quarrel, no insult, no suspicion--and so it is positively because my poor wife has a headache that you are to bring death down upon two men of honour and plunge two of the most excellent and ancient families in the kingdom into disgrace and sorrow.\"", "우리가 갔더라면—웬햄 부인의 두통만 아니었어도 갔을 텐데, 그 사람이 두통을 꽤나 앓는다오, 특히 봄철에는 더하지요—우리가 가고, 당신이 집에 돌아왔더라면, 다툼도 모욕도 의심도 없었을 거요. 그러니 가엾은 내 아내가 두통을 앓았다는 바로 그 이유로, 당신은 명예로운 두 사내에게 죽음을 끌어내리고, 이 왕국에서 가장 훌륭하고 유서 깊은 두 가문을 치욕과 비탄에 빠뜨리려 하는 셈이오.\""]], [["Mr. Macmurdo looked at his principal with the air of a man profoundly puzzled, and Rawdon felt with a kind of rage that his prey was escaping him.", "맥머도 씨는 몹시 어리둥절한 사람의 표정으로 자기 주인을 바라보았고, 로던은 일종의 분노 속에서 자기 먹잇감이 빠져나가고 있음을 느꼈다."], ["He did not believe a word of the story, and yet, how discredit or disprove it?", "그는 그 이야기를 한마디도 믿지 않았지만, 그렇다고 어찌 그것을 헐뜯거나 반박한단 말인가?"]], [["Mr. Wenham continued with the same fluent oratory, which in his place in Parliament he had so often practised--\"I sat for an hour or more by Lord Steyne's bedside, beseeching, imploring Lord Steyne to forego his intention of demanding a meeting.", "웬햄 씨는, 의회의 자기 의석에서 그토록 자주 갈고닦은 그 유창한 웅변을 그대로 이어 갔다."], ["I pointed out to him that the circumstances were after all suspicious--they were suspicious.", "\"나는 한 시간 남짓 스테인 경의 머리맡에 앉아, 결투를 청하려는 뜻을 거두어 달라고 그분께 간청하고 애원했소."], ["I acknowledge it--any man in your position might have been taken in--I said that a man furious with jealousy is to all intents and purposes a madman, and should be as such regarded--that a duel between you must lead to the disgrace of all parties concerned--that a man of his Lordship's exalted station had no right in these days, when the most atrocious revolutionary principles, and the most dangerous levelling doctrines are preached among the vulgar, to create a public scandal; and that, however innocent, the common people would insist that he was guilty.", "나는 그 정황이 결국 의심을 살 만했다고 그분께 지적했소—과연 의심스러운 상황이었지요. 나는 그것을 인정하오—당신 처지에 있는 사람이라면 누구든 속아 넘어갔을 거요—나는, 질투에 미친 사람은 사실상 미치광이나 다름없으니 그렇게 여겨야 마땅하다고, 두 분의 결투는 관련된 모든 이의 치욕으로 이어질 수밖에 없다고, 그분처럼 드높은 지위에 계신 분이, 더없이 흉악한 혁명 사상과 더없이 위험한 평등 교리가 천한 무리 사이에 설파되는 요즘 같은 시절에 공공연한 추문을 일으킬 권리는 없다고, 그리고 아무리 결백하다 한들 평민들은 한사코 그분을 죄인으로 몰 거라고 말씀드렸소."], ["In fine, I implored him not to send the challenge.\"", "요컨대 나는 그분께 결투장을 보내지 말아 달라고 애원했소.\""]], [["\"I don't believe one word of the whole story,\"", "\"나는 그 이야기를 한마디도 믿지 않소.\""], ["said Rawdon, grinding his teeth.", "로던이 이를 갈며 말했다."], ["\"I believe it a d------ lie, and that you're in it, Mr. Wenham.", "\"그건 빌어먹을 거짓말이고, 당신도 거기 한패라고 믿소, 웬햄 씨."], ["If the challenge don't come from him, by Jove it shall come from me.\"", "그자에게서 도전장이 오지 않는다면, 맹세코 내 쪽에서 보내고 말 거요.\""]], [["Mr. Wenham turned deadly pale at this savage interruption of the Colonel and looked towards the door.", "웬햄 씨는 대령의 이 사나운 가로막음에 새파랗게 질려 문 쪽을 바라보았다."]], [["But he found a champion in Captain Macmurdo.", "그러나 그는 맥머도 대위라는 옹호자를 만났다."], ["That gentleman rose up with an oath and rebuked Rawdon for his language.", "그 신사는 욕설을 내뱉으며 일어서더니, 로던의 말투를 나무랐다."], ["\"You put the affair into my hands, and you shall act as I think fit, by Jove, and not as you do. You have no right to insult Mr.", "\"자네가 이 일을 내 손에 맡겼으니, 맹세코 자네 뜻대로가 아니라 내가 옳다고 여기는 대로 처리해야 하네."], ["Wenham with this sort of language; and dammy, Mr.", "자네에게 이런 식의 말로 웬햄 씨를 모욕할 권리는 없어."], ["Wenham, you deserve an apology.", "그리고 빌어먹을, 웬햄 씨, 당신은 사과를 받아 마땅하오."], ["And as for a challenge to Lord Steyne, you may get somebody else to carry it, I won't.", "또 스테인 경에게 보낼 도전장으로 말하자면, 그건 다른 사람을 시켜 전하시게. 나는 안 하겠네."], ["If my lord, after being thrashed, chooses to sit still, dammy let him. And as for the affair with--with Mrs.", "흠씬 두들겨 맞고도 그분이 가만히 있기로 한다면, 빌어먹을, 그러시라고 하지."], ["Crawley, my belief is, there's nothing proved at all: that your wife's innocent, as innocent as Mr.", "그리고 크롤리 부인과의—그 일로 말할 것 같으면, 내 생각엔 입증된 게 아무것도 없네. 자네 아내는 결백해, 웬햄 씨 말마따나 결백하다고."], ["Wenham says she is; and at any rate that you would be a d--fool not to take the place and hold your tongue.\"", "그러니 아무튼 자네가 그 자리를 받아들이고 입을 다물지 않는다면 그거야말로 빌어먹을 바보짓이지.\""]], [["\"Captain Macmurdo, you speak like a man of sense,\" Mr.", "\"맥머도 대위, 당신은 분별 있는 사람답게 말씀하시는구려.\""], ["Wenham cried out, immensely relieved--\"I forget any words that Colonel Crawley has used in the irritation of the moment.\"", "웬햄 씨가 더없이 안도하여 외쳤다. \"크롤리 대령이 순간의 격앙 속에서 쓰신 말은 모두 잊겠소이다.\""]], [["\"I thought you would,\"", "\"그럴 줄 알았소.\""], ["Rawdon said with a sneer.", "로던이 코웃음을 치며 말했다."]], [["\"Shut your mouth, you old stoopid,\"", "\"입 다물게, 이 고집불통 늙은이야.\""], ["the Captain said good-naturedly. \"Mr.", "대위가 선량하게 말했다."], ["Wenham ain't a fighting man; and quite right, too.\"", "\"웬햄 씨는 싸움을 할 사람이 아니야. 그리고 그게 지극히 옳고.\""]], [["\"This matter, in my belief,\"", "\"이 일은, 내 생각엔,\""], ["the Steyne emissary cried, \"ought to be buried in the most profound oblivion.", "스테인 경의 사자가 외쳤다. \"더없이 깊은 망각 속에 묻혀야 마땅하오."], ["A word concerning it should never pass these doors.", "이에 관한 말은 한마디도 이 문밖으로 새어 나가서는 안 되오."], ["I speak in the interest of my friend, as well as of Colonel Crawley, who persists in considering me his enemy.\"", "나는 내 벗을 위해서, 또한 한사코 나를 적으로 여기시는 크롤리 대령을 위해서도 하는 말이오.\""]], [["\"I suppose Lord Steyne won't talk about it very much,\"", "\"스테인 경께서야 이 일을 그리 떠벌리지 않으실 테고,\""], ["said Captain Macmurdo; \"and I don't see why our side should.", "맥머도 대위가 말했다. \"우리 쪽에서도 그래야 할 까닭이 없겠지요."], ["The affair ain't a very pretty one, any way you take it, and the less said about it the better.", "어느 모로 보든 그리 보기 좋은 일이 아니니, 말을 적게 할수록 좋소."], ["It's you are thrashed, and not us; and if you are satisfied, why, I think, we should be.\"", "두들겨 맞은 건 그쪽이지 우리가 아니오. 그러니 그쪽이 만족한다면, 글쎄, 우리도 만족해야겠지요.\""]], [["Mr. Wenham took his hat, upon this, and Captain Macmurdo following him to the door, shut it upon himself and Lord Steyne's agent, leaving Rawdon chafing within.", "이 말에 웬햄 씨는 모자를 집어 들었고, 맥머도 대위가 그를 문까지 배웅하여 자기와 스테인 경의 대리인을 안에 남겨진 로던과 떼어 놓듯 문을 닫았다."], ["When the two were on the other side, Macmurdo looked hard at the other ambassador and with an expression of anything but respect on his round jolly face.", "두 사람이 문 저편으로 나서자, 맥머도는 그 둥글고 쾌활한 얼굴에 도무지 존경이라고는 할 수 없는 표정을 지으며 또 한 명의 사절을 빤히 노려보았다."]], [["\"You don't stick at a trifle, Mr. Wenham,\"", "\"당신은 사소한 일쯤은 개의치 않는구려, 웬햄 씨.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"You flatter me, Captain Macmurdo,\"", "\"과찬이시오, 맥머도 대위.\""], ["answered the other with a smile.", "상대가 미소 지으며 대답했다."], ["\"Upon my honour and conscience now, Mrs. Crawley did ask us to sup after the opera.\"", "\"이제 내 명예와 양심을 걸고 말하건대, 크롤리 부인은 정말로 오페라가 끝난 뒤 우리에게 저녁을 함께 들자고 청했소.\""]], [["\"Of course; and Mrs.", "\"물론 그랬겠지요."], ["Wenham had one of her head-aches.", "그리고 웬햄 부인은 두통이 도지셨고."], ["I say, I've got a thousand-pound note here, which I will give you if you will give me a receipt, please; and I will put the note up in an envelope for Lord Steyne.", "이보시오, 내게 천 파운드짜리 어음이 한 장 있는데, 영수증을 써 주신다면 이걸 당신께 드리리다. 그러면 그 어음을 봉투에 넣어 스테인 경께 보내겠소."], ["My man shan't fight him.", "우리 사람은 그분과 싸우지 않을 거요."], ["But we had rather not take his money.\"", "하지만 우리는 그분 돈을 받지 않는 편이 낫겠소.\""]], [["\"It was all a mistake--all a mistake, my dear sir,\"", "\"모두 오해였소—다 오해였던 거요, 친애하는 선생.\""], ["the other said with the utmost innocence of manner; and was bowed down the Club steps by Captain Macmurdo, just as Sir Pitt Crawley ascended them.", "상대가 더없이 천진한 태도로 말했다. 그리고 맥머도 대위가 그를 클럽 계단 아래로 인사하며 배웅할 때, 마침 피트 크롤리 경이 그 계단을 올라오고 있었다."], ["There was a slight acquaintance between these two gentlemen, and the Captain, going back with the Baronet to the room where the latter's brother was, told Sir Pitt, in confidence, that he had made the affair all right between Lord Steyne and the Colonel.", "이 두 신사는 안면이 약간 있는 사이였다. 대위는 준남작과 함께 그의 동생이 있는 방으로 돌아가며, 스테인 경과 대령 사이의 일을 자기가 잘 마무리했노라고 피트 경에게 은밀히 일러 주었다."]], [["Sir Pitt was well pleased, of course, at this intelligence, and congratulated his brother warmly upon the peaceful issue of the affair, making appropriate moral remarks upon the evils of duelling and the unsatisfactory nature of that sort of settlement of disputes.", "피트 경은 물론 이 소식에 무척 흐뭇해하며, 일이 평화롭게 매듭지어진 것을 두고 동생에게 따뜻이 축하의 말을 건넸고, 결투의 폐해와 그런 식의 분쟁 해결이 지닌 불만족스러운 성격에 대해 적절한 도덕적 논평을 곁들였다."]], [["And after this preface, he tried with all his eloquence to effect a reconciliation between Rawdon and his wife.", "그리고 이 서두를 늘어놓은 뒤, 그는 온갖 능변을 동원하여 로던과 그 아내 사이의 화해를 성사시키려 애썼다."], ["He recapitulated the statements which Becky had made, pointed out the probabilities of their truth, and asserted his own firm belief in her innocence.", "그는 베키가 한 진술들을 다시 간추려 들려주고, 그것이 참일 가능성을 짚어 주었으며, 자신은 그녀의 결백을 굳게 믿는다고 단언했다."]], [["But Rawdon would not hear of it.", "그러나 로던은 그 말을 들으려 하지 않았다."], ["\"She has kep money concealed from me these ten years,\"", "\"그 사람은 이 십 년 동안 나한테 돈을 감춰 왔소.\""], ["he said \"She swore, last night only, she had none from Steyne.", "그가 말했다. \"바로 간밤에도 스테인한테서 받은 돈은 없다고 맹세했지요."], ["She knew it was all up, directly I found it.", "내가 알아챈 순간, 그 사람은 다 끝장났다는 걸 알았소."], ["If she's not guilty, Pitt, she's as bad as guilty, and I'll never see her again--never.\"", "결백하지 않다면, 형님, 그 사람은 죄지은 거나 다름없이 못된 거고, 나는 다시는 그 사람을 보지 않겠소—절대로.\""], ["His head sank down on his chest as he spoke the words, and he looked quite broken and sad.", "그 말을 하면서 그의 고개가 가슴팍으로 푹 떨어졌고, 그는 완전히 무너져 슬픔에 잠긴 듯이 보였다."]], [["\"Poor old boy,\"", "\"가엾은 친구.\""], ["Macmurdo said, shaking his head.", "맥머도가 고개를 저으며 말했다."]], [["Rawdon Crawley resisted for some time the idea of taking the place which had been procured for him by so odious a patron, and was also for removing the boy from the school where Lord Steyne's interest had placed him.", "로던 크롤리는 그토록 가증스러운 후원자가 마련해 준 자리를 받아들인다는 생각에 한동안 저항했고, 또한 스테인 경의 영향력으로 들어가게 된 그 학교에서 아들을 빼내려고도 했다."], ["He was induced, however, to acquiesce in these benefits by the entreaties of his brother and Macmurdo, but mainly by the latter, pointing out to him what a fury Steyne would be in to think that his enemy's fortune was made through his means.", "그러나 그는 형과 맥머도의 간청에, 특히 맥머도가, 자기 원수의 출세가 제 손으로 이루어진 것임을 알면 스테인이 얼마나 길길이 날뛰겠느냐고 짚어 준 데에 마음이 움직여, 결국 그 혜택들을 받아들이는 데 동의했다."]], [["When the Marquis of Steyne came abroad after his accident, the Colonial Secretary bowed up to him and congratulated himself and the Service upon having made so excellent an appointment.", "스테인 후작이 그 사고에서 회복하여 바깥출입을 하게 되자, 식민지 담당 장관이 그에게 다가가 인사하며, 그토록 훌륭한 인사 발령을 해낸 것을 두고 자신과 관청을 추어올렸다."], ["These congratulations were received with a degree of gratitude which may be imagined on the part of Lord Steyne.", "이 축하의 말을 스테인 경이 어느 정도의 고마움으로 받아들였을지는 능히 짐작이 갈 것이다."]], [["The secret of the rencontre between him and Colonel Crawley was buried in the profoundest oblivion, as Wenham said; that is, by the seconds and the principals.", "그와 크롤리 대령 사이에 벌어진 그 충돌의 비밀은, 웬햄의 말마따나 더없이 깊은 망각 속에 묻혔다. 그러니까, 입회인들과 당사자들에 의해서 말이다."], ["But before that evening was over it was talked of at fifty dinner-tables in Vanity Fair.", "그러나 그날 저녁이 다 가기도 전에, 그 일은 허영의 시장의 쉰 군데 만찬 식탁에서 입에 오르내렸다."], ["Little Cackleby himself went to seven evening parties and told the story with comments and emendations at each place.", "캐클비라는 자는 직접 저녁 모임 일곱 군데에 가서, 그때마다 논평과 윤색을 곁들여 그 이야기를 들려주었다."], ["How Mrs. Washington White revelled in it!", "워싱턴 화이트 부인이 그것을 얼마나 흥겹게 떠벌렸던가!"], ["The Bishopess of Ealing was shocked beyond expression; the Bishop went and wrote his name down in the visiting-book at Gaunt House that very day.", "일링의 주교 부인은 이루 말할 수 없을 만큼 충격을 받았고, 주교는 바로 그날 곤트 하우스에 가서 방문록에 제 이름을 적어 두었다."], ["Little Southdown was sorry; so you may be sure was his sister Lady Jane, very sorry. Lady Southdown wrote it off to her other daughter at the Cape of Good Hope.", "어린 사우스다운은 안타까워했고, 그의 누이 제인 부인 역시 몹시 안타까워했음은 틀림없는 일이다."], ["It was town-talk for at least three days, and was only kept out of the newspapers by the exertions of Mr.", "사우스다운 부인은 그 소식을, 희망봉에 가 있는 다른 딸에게 적어 보냈다."], ["Wagg, acting upon a hint from Mr. Wenham.", "그것은 적어도 사흘간 장안의 화젯거리가 되었고, 웬햄 씨의 귀띔에 따라 움직인 왜그 씨의 분투 덕분에 가까스로 신문에는 실리지 않았다."]], [["The bailiffs and brokers seized upon poor Raggles in Curzon Street, and the late fair tenant of that poor little mansion was in the meanwhile--where?", "집행관들과 중개인들이 커즌 거리의 가엾은 래글스에게 들이닥쳤고, 그 가련하고 자그마한 저택의 한때 어여뻤던 세입자는 그동안—어디에 있었던가? 누가 알 바였으랴!"], ["Who cared! Who asked after a day or two?", "하루 이틀이 지나고서 누가 그녀의 안부를 물었던가?"], ["Was she guilty or not?", "그녀는 죄가 있었던가, 없었던가?"], ["We all know how charitable the world is, and how the verdict of Vanity Fair goes when there is a doubt.", "우리는 세상이 얼마나 너그러운지, 그리고 의심이 있을 때 허영의 시장의 평결이 어디로 기우는지를 익히 알고 있다."], ["Some people said she had gone to Naples in pursuit of Lord Steyne, whilst others averred that his Lordship quitted that city and fled to Palermo on hearing of Becky's arrival; some said she was living in Bierstadt, and had become a dame d'honneur to the Queen of Bulgaria; some that she was at Boulogne; and others, at a boarding-house at Cheltenham.", "어떤 이들은 그녀가 스테인 경을 쫓아 나폴리로 갔다고 했고, 또 어떤 이들은 그분이 베키가 도착했다는 소식을 듣고 그 도시를 떠나 팔레르모로 달아났다고 단언했다. 더러는 그녀가 비르슈타트에 살면서 불가리아 왕비의 여관(女官)이 되었다고 했고, 더러는 불로뉴에 있다고, 또 다른 이들은 첼트넘의 하숙집에 있다고 했다."]], [["Rawdon made her a tolerable annuity, and we may be sure that she was a woman who could make a little money go a great way, as the saying is.", "로던은 그녀에게 그런대로 괜찮은 연금을 마련해 주었는데, 흔히들 하는 말마따나 그녀가 적은 돈을 야무지게 늘려 쓸 줄 아는 여자임은 틀림없다."], ["He would have paid his debts on leaving England, could he have got any Insurance Office to take his life, but the climate of Coventry Island was so bad that he could borrow no money on the strength of his salary.", "그는 자기 생명을 받아 줄 보험 회사를 구할 수만 있었더라면 영국을 떠나면서 빚을 갚았을 테지만, 코번트리 섬의 기후가 어찌나 고약한지 봉급을 담보로는 한 푼도 빌릴 수가 없었다."], ["He remitted, however, to his brother punctually, and wrote to his little boy regularly every mail.", "그래도 그는 형에게는 꼬박꼬박 송금했고, 어린 아들에게는 우편선이 뜰 때마다 어김없이 편지를 썼다."], ["He kept Macmurdo in cigars and sent over quantities of shells, cayenne pepper, hot pickles, guava jelly, and colonial produce to Lady Jane.", "그는 맥머도에게 시가가 떨어지지 않게 보내 주었고, 제인 부인에게는 조개껍데기며 카옌 고춧가루며 매운 절임이며 구아바 젤리며 식민지 산물을 잔뜩 보냈다."], ["He sent his brother home the Swamp Town Gazette, in which the new Governor was praised with immense enthusiasm; whereas the Swamp Town Sentinel, whose wife was not asked to Government House, declared that his Excellency was a tyrant, compared to whom Nero was an enlightened philanthropist.", "형에게는 〈스왐프 타운 가제트〉를 보내 주었는데, 거기서는 새 총독을 어마어마한 열의로 칭송하고 있었다. 반면 그 부인이 총독 관저에 초대받지 못한 〈스왐프 타운 센티넬〉은, 그 총독 각하야말로 폭군이며 네로조차 그에 비하면 깨인 박애주의자라고 단언했다."], ["Little Rawdon used to like to get the papers and read about his Excellency.", "어린 로던은 그 신문들을 받아 각하에 관한 기사를 읽기를 좋아하곤 했다."]], [["His mother never made any movement to see the child.", "그의 어머니는 그 아이를 만나려는 어떤 움직임도 보이지 않았다."], ["He went home to his aunt for Sundays and holidays; he soon knew every bird's nest about Queen's Crawley, and rode out with Sir Huddlestone's hounds, which he admired so on his first well-remembered visit to Hampshire.", "아이는 일요일과 휴일이면 고모 집으로 갔다. 그는 곧 퀸스크롤리 인근의 새 둥지란 둥지는 죄다 꿰게 되었고, 햄프셔를 처음 찾았던 그 잊지 못할 방문 때 그토록 감탄했던 허들스턴 경의 사냥개들을 따라 말을 달리곤 했다."]], [["Georgy is Made a Gentleman\nGeorgy Osborne was now fairly established in his grandfather's mansion in Russell Square, occupant of his father's room in the house and heir apparent of all the splendours there.", "조지 오스본은 이제 러셀 광장에 자리한 외할아버지의 저택에 어엿하게 자리를 잡았고, 그 집에서 아버지가 쓰던 방을 차지했으며, 그곳의 온갖 호사를 물려받을 후계자가 되었다."], ["The good looks, gallant bearing, and gentlemanlike appearance of the boy won the grandsire's heart for him.", "잘생긴 외모와 늠름한 거동, 신사다운 풍채를 지닌 그 소년은 외할아버지의 마음을 단번에 사로잡았다."], ["Mr. Osborne was as proud of him as ever he had been of the elder George.", "오스본 씨는 일찍이 형뻘인 조지를 자랑스러워했던 것 못지않게 이 아이를 자랑스러워했다."]], [["The child had many more luxuries and indulgences than had been awarded his father.", "이 아이는 제 아버지가 누렸던 것보다 훨씬 많은 사치와 응석을 누렸다."], ["Osborne's commerce had prospered greatly of late years.", "오스본의 사업은 근래 몇 해 동안 크게 번창했다."], ["His wealth and importance in the City had very much increased.", "그가 런던 상업계에서 차지하는 부와 비중도 한층 커진 터였다."], ["He had been glad enough in former days to put the elder George to a good private school; and a commission in the army for his son had been a source of no small pride to him; for little George and his future prospects the old man looked much higher.", "지난날 그는 형뻘 조지를 괜찮은 사립학교에 보낼 수 있었던 것만으로도 충분히 흐뭇해했고, 아들에게 군 장교 임관 자리를 사 준 일도 그에게는 적잖은 자랑거리였다. 그러나 어린 조지와 그 장래에 대해서라면 이 노인은 훨씬 더 높은 곳을 바라보았다."], ["He would make a gentleman of the little chap, was Mr. Osborne's constant saying regarding little Georgy.", "이 꼬마를 어엿한 신사로 만들겠다는 것이 어린 조지를 두고 오스본 씨가 입버릇처럼 하는 말이었다."], ["He saw him in his mind's eye, a collegian, a Parliament man, a Baronet, perhaps.", "그는 마음속 눈으로 손자가 대학생이 되고, 국회의원이 되고, 어쩌면 준남작이 되는 모습까지 그려 보았다."], ["The old man thought he would die contented if he could see his grandson in a fair way to such honours.", "손자가 그런 영예로 나아가는 길에 들어선 것을 볼 수만 있다면 흡족하게 눈감을 수 있으리라고 노인은 생각했다. 그 애를 가르칠 사람으로는 오직 최고 명문 대학 출신만을 두려 했다."], ["He would have none but a tip-top college man to educate him--none of your quacks and pretenders--no, no.", "그런 돌팔이며 사기꾼 따위는 어림도 없었다. 절대로."], ["A few years before, he used to be savage, and inveigh against all parsons, scholars, and the like declaring that they were a pack of humbugs, and quacks that weren't fit to get their living but by grinding Latin and Greek, and a set of supercilious dogs that pretended to look down upon British merchants and gentlemen, who could buy up half a hundred of 'em.", "몇 해 전만 해도 그는 사납게 굴며 목사며 학자며 그런 부류를 죄다 싸잡아 욕하곤 했다. 그자들은 한낱 사기꾼이며 돌팔이 떼거리로, 라틴어와 그리스어나 갈아 대는 것 말고는 제 밥벌이도 못 할 위인들이며, 자기네라면 한꺼번에 쉰 명쯤은 사 버릴 수도 있는 영국 상인과 신사들을 깔보는 척하는 거만한 개 같은 자들이라는 것이었다."], ["He would mourn now, in a very solemn manner, that his own education had been neglected, and repeatedly point out, in pompous orations to Georgy, the necessity and excellence of classical acquirements.", "그러던 그가 이제는 자신이 제대로 교육받지 못한 것을 자못 엄숙하게 한탄하며, 조지를 앞에 두고 거창한 일장 연설을 늘어놓으면서 고전 교양의 필요성과 탁월함을 거듭 일러 주는 것이었다."]], [["When they met at dinner the grandsire used to ask the lad what he had been reading during the day, and was greatly interested at the report the boy gave of his own studies, pretending to understand little George when he spoke regarding them.", "식사 자리에서 만나면 외할아버지는 그 아이에게 하루 동안 무엇을 읽었는지 묻곤 했고, 아이가 제 공부에 관해 들려주는 보고에 크나큰 흥미를 보였으며, 어린 조지가 그 이야기를 할 때면 알아듣는 척했다. 그는 백 번도 더 헛다리를 짚었고, 여러 차례 제 무지를 드러냈다."], ["He made a hundred blunders and showed his ignorance many a time.", "그 일은 아이가 윗사람에게 품은 존경심을 키워 주지 못했다."], ["It did not increase the respect which the child had for his senior.", "영민한 머리와 다른 데서 받은 더 나은 교육 덕분에, 아이는 이내 제 외할아버지가 둔재임을 알아차렸고, 따라서 그를 부리고 깔보기 시작했다."], ["A quick brain and a better education elsewhere showed the boy very soon that his grandsire was a dullard, and he began accordingly to command him and to look down upon him; for his previous education, humble and contracted as it had been, had made a much better gentleman of Georgy than any plans of his grandfather could make him.", "비록 보잘것없고 옹색했을지언정, 그가 앞서 받은 교육이 외할아버지의 그 어떤 계획보다도 조지를 훨씬 더 나은 신사로 만들어 놓았기 때문이다. 그는 마음씨 곱고 여리고 다정한 한 여인의 손에서 자랐다."], ["He had been brought up by a kind, weak, and tender woman, who had no pride about anything but about him, and whose heart was so pure and whose bearing was so meek and humble that she could not but needs be a true lady.", "그녀는 오직 아들에 대해서만 자부심을 가졌을 뿐 다른 무엇에도 으스대는 법이 없었고, 그 마음은 더없이 순결하고 그 몸가짐은 더없이 온순하고 겸손하여, 누가 뭐래도 진정한 숙녀일 수밖에 없었다. 그녀는 다정한 소임과 조용한 의무에 늘 분주했다."], ["She busied herself in gentle offices and quiet duties; if she never said brilliant things, she never spoke or thought unkind ones; guileless and artless, loving and pure, indeed how could our poor little Amelia be other than a real gentlewoman!", "결코 번지르르한 말을 하는 법은 없었으나, 모진 말을 입에 담거나 모진 생각을 품는 법도 없었다. 꾸밈없고 천진하고, 사랑이 넘치고 순결한 우리의 가엾은 어린 애밀리아가 어찌 진정한 숙녀가 아닐 수 있겠는가!"]], [["Young Georgy lorded over this soft and yielding nature; and the contrast of its simplicity and delicacy with the coarse pomposity of the dull old man with whom he next came in contact made him lord over the latter too.", "어린 조지는 이 부드럽고 순한 천성 위에 군림했다. 그리고 그것의 소박함과 섬세함이, 그다음으로 마주하게 된 둔한 노인의 거칠고 거만한 태도와 대비를 이루는 바람에, 조지는 그 노인 위에도 군림하게 되었다."], ["If he had been a Prince Royal he could not have been better brought up to think well of himself.", "설령 그가 왕세자였다 한들, 제 잘난 줄 알도록 이보다 더 잘 키워질 수는 없었으리라."]], [["Whilst his mother was yearning after him at home, and I do believe every hour of the day, and during most hours of the sad lonely nights, thinking of him, this young gentleman had a number of pleasures and consolations administered to him, which made him for his part bear the separation from Amelia very easily.", "그의 어머니가 집에서 그를 그리워하며, 내가 진정 믿건대 하루의 매시간을, 그리고 서글프고 외로운 밤의 거의 모든 시간을 그를 생각하며 보내는 동안, 이 어린 신사에게는 여러 가지 즐거움과 위안이 베풀어졌고, 그래서 그로서는 애밀리아와의 이별을 아주 가뿐하게 견뎌 냈다."], ["Little boys who cry when they are going to school cry because they are going to a very uncomfortable place.", "학교에 가면서 우는 어린 사내아이들은 더없이 불편한 곳으로 가게 되어 우는 것이다."], ["It is only a few who weep from sheer affection.", "순전히 애정에서 우는 아이는 극소수에 불과하다."], ["When you think that the eyes of your childhood dried at the sight of a piece of gingerbread, and that a plum cake was a compensation for the agony of parting with your mamma and sisters, oh my friend and brother, you need not be too confident of your own fine feelings.", "당신의 어린 시절 그 눈이 생강 과자 한 조각만 보면 마르고, 자두 케이크 한 조각이 엄마와 누이들과 헤어지는 고통의 보상이 되었음을 떠올려 본다면, 오, 나의 벗이요 형제여, 당신은 제 고운 감정을 너무 자신해서는 안 될 것이다."]], [["Well, then, Master George Osborne had every comfort and luxury that a wealthy and lavish old grandfather thought fit to provide.", "자, 그러니 조지 오스본 도련님은 부유하고 손이 큰 늙은 외할아버지가 마련해 줌 직하다고 여긴 온갖 안락과 사치를 다 누렸다."], ["The coachman was instructed to purchase for him the handsomest pony which could be bought for money, and on this George was taught to ride, first at a riding-school, whence, after having performed satisfactorily without stirrups, and over the leaping-bar, he was conducted through the New Road to Regent's Park, and then to Hyde Park, where he rode in state with Martin the coachman behind him.", "마부는 돈으로 살 수 있는 가장 멋진 조랑말을 그에게 사 주라는 지시를 받았고, 조지는 그 말에 타는 법을 배웠다. 처음에는 승마 학교에서였는데, 그곳에서 등자 없이, 또 도약대를 넘으며 만족스럽게 솜씨를 보인 뒤에는, 뉴 로드를 거쳐 리젠트 공원으로, 다시 하이드 공원으로 인도되었고, 거기서 마부 마틴을 뒤에 거느린 채 위풍당당하게 말을 달렸다."], ["Old Osborne, who took matters more easily in the City now, where he left his affairs to his junior partners, would often ride out with Miss O. in the same fashionable direction.", "이제는 런던 상업계의 일들을 한결 느긋하게 처리하며 사업을 젊은 동업자들에게 맡겨 둔 늙은 오스본도, 오스본 양과 함께 같은 멋쟁이들의 거리로 자주 마차를 몰고 나가곤 했다."], ["As little Georgy came cantering up with his dandified air and his heels down, his grandfather would nudge the lad's aunt and say, \"Look, Miss O.\"", "어린 조지가 멋을 한껏 부린 자태로, 발뒤꿈치를 아래로 내린 채 가볍게 말을 달려 다가오면, 외할아버지는 아이의 고모를 슬쩍 찌르며 말하는 것이었다. \"저것 좀 봐, 오스본 양.\""], ["And he would laugh, and his face would grow red with pleasure, as he nodded out of the window to the boy, as the groom saluted the carriage, and the footman saluted Master George. Here too his aunt, Mrs.", "그러고는 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 창밖으로 아이에게 고개를 끄덕이며, 마부가 마차에 경례하고 하인이 조지 도련님에게 경례하는 사이, 그 얼굴은 흐뭇함에 발갛게 달아오르는 것이었다."], ["Frederick Bullock (whose chariot might daily be seen in the Ring, with bullocks or emblazoned on the panels and harness, and three pasty-faced little Bullocks, covered with cockades and feathers, staring from the windows) Mrs. Frederick Bullock, I say, flung glances of the bitterest hatred at the little upstart as he rode by with his hand on his side and his hat on one ear, as proud as a lord.", "여기에는 또한 그의 고모인 프레더릭 불록 부인도 있었으니(그 부인의 마차는 날마다 승마장에서 볼 수 있었는데, 차문 판자며 마구에 황소가, 아니 문장이 새겨져 있었고, 모표와 깃털을 단 창백한 얼굴의 어린 불록 셋이 창밖을 빤히 내다보고 있었다), 거듭 말하건대 프레더릭 불록 부인은, 옆구리에 손을 얹고 모자를 한쪽 귀에 걸친 채 영주처럼 거만하게 말을 달려 지나가는 이 어린 벼락출세자를 향해 더없이 쓰디쓴 증오의 눈길을 쏘아 댔다."]], [["Though he was scarcely eleven years of age, Master George wore straps and the most beautiful little boots like a man.", "아직 열한 살도 채 못 되었건만, 조지 도련님은 어른처럼 바짓단 끈을 매고 더없이 아름다운 작은 부츠를 신었다."], ["He had gilt spurs, and a gold-headed whip, and a fine pin in his handkerchief, and the neatest little kid gloves which Lamb's Conduit Street could furnish.", "도금한 박차에, 금 손잡이가 달린 채찍에, 손수건에 꽂는 멋진 핀에, 램스 컨듀잇 거리에서 구할 수 있는 가장 말쑥한 작은 새끼염소 가죽 장갑까지 갖추었다."], ["His mother had given him a couple of neckcloths, and carefully hemmed and made some little shirts for him; but when her Eli came to see the widow, they were replaced by much finer linen.", "어머니는 그에게 목도리 두 장을 주었고, 작은 셔츠 몇 벌을 정성껏 단을 박아 지어 주었다. 그러나 그녀의 엘리가 미망인을 보러 왔을 무렵에는 그것들이 훨씬 고운 아마포로 바뀌어 있었다."], ["He had little jewelled buttons in the lawn shirt fronts.", "고운 무명 셔츠 가슴팍에는 보석 박힌 작은 단추들이 달려 있었다."], ["Her humble presents had been put aside--I believe Miss Osborne had given them to the coachman's boy.", "그녀의 소박한 선물들은 한쪽으로 치워진 채였다. 짐작건대 오스본 양이 그것들을 마부의 아들에게 주어 버린 것이리라."], ["Amelia tried to think she was pleased at the change.", "애밀리아는 이 변화가 흐뭇하다고 생각하려 애썼다."], ["Indeed, she was happy and charmed to see the boy looking so beautiful.", "사실 그녀는 아이가 그토록 아름답게 차려입은 모습을 보며 행복하고 흐뭇했다."]], [["She had had a little black profile of him done for a shilling, and this was hung up by the side of another portrait over her bed. One day the boy came on his accustomed visit, galloping down the little street at Brompton, and bringing, as usual, all the inhabitants to the windows to admire his splendour, and with great eagerness and a look of triumph in his face, he pulled a case out of his great-coat--it was a natty white great-coat, with a cape and a velvet collar--pulled out a red morocco case, which he gave her.", "그녀는 일 실링을 주고 아이의 옆얼굴을 검은 윤곽으로 그린 작은 그림을 하나 마련해 두었는데, 이것을 침대 위 또 다른 초상화 옆에 걸어 두었다. 어느 날 그 아이가 으레 하던 대로 찾아왔다. 브롬프턴의 그 작은 거리를 따라 말을 달려 내려오자, 여느 때처럼 거리의 주민들이 죄다 창가로 몰려 나와 그 휘황함에 감탄했다. 아이는 잔뜩 들뜬 기색에 의기양양한 얼굴로 외투에서 상자 하나를 꺼냈다. 그것은 망토가 달리고 벨벳 깃을 단 맵시 있는 하얀 외투였는데, 그 안에서 붉은 모로코가죽 상자를 꺼내 어머니에게 건넸다."]], [["\"I bought it with my own money, Mamma,\"", "\"제 돈으로 산 거예요, 엄마.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said. \"I thought you'd like it.\"", "\"엄마가 좋아하실 것 같아서요.\""]], [["Amelia opened the case, and giving a little cry of delighted affection, seized the boy and embraced him a hundred times.", "애밀리아는 상자를 열고는, 기쁨에 겨운 애정의 작은 탄성을 지르며 아이를 와락 끌어안고 백 번도 더 입을 맞추었다."], ["It was a miniature of himself, very prettily done (though not half handsome enough, we may be sure, the widow thought).", "그것은 그 아이 자신의 세밀 초상이었는데, 아주 예쁘게 그려져 있었다(물론 미망인의 생각에는 실제 잘생긴 모습의 절반에도 못 미쳤겠지만, 그건 분명한 일이다)."], ["His grandfather had wished to have a picture of him by an artist whose works, exhibited in a shop-window, in Southampton Row, had caught the old gentleman's eye; and George, who had plenty of money, bethought him of asking the painter how much a copy of the little portrait would cost, saying that he would pay for it out of his own money and that he wanted to give it to his mother.", "그의 외할아버지가 한 화가에게 손자의 초상을 그리게 하고 싶어 했는데, 사우샘프턴 로의 어느 상점 진열창에 걸린 그 화가의 작품이 노신사의 눈길을 끌었던 것이다. 돈이 넉넉했던 조지는 문득 그 화가에게 작은 초상화 사본 하나가 얼마나 드는지 물어볼 생각이 떠올랐고, 제 돈으로 값을 치를 테니 그것을 어머니에게 드리고 싶다고 했다."], ["The pleased painter executed it for a small price, and old Osborne himself, when he heard of the incident, growled out his satisfaction and gave the boy twice as many sovereigns as he paid for the miniature.", "흐뭇해진 화가는 헐값에 그것을 그려 주었고, 이 일을 전해 들은 늙은 오스본은 만족스러운 듯 으르렁대며 그 세밀 초상화 값의 곱절이나 되는 금화를 아이에게 주었다."]], [["But what was the grandfather's pleasure compared to Amelia's ecstacy?", "그러나 외할아버지의 흐뭇함이 애밀리아의 황홀함에 어찌 견주랴?"], ["That proof of the boy's affection charmed her so that she thought no child in the world was like hers for goodness.", "아이의 애정을 보여 주는 그 증거가 그녀를 어찌나 매료시켰던지, 그녀는 세상 어느 아이도 제 아이만큼 착하지는 못하리라 여겼다."], ["For long weeks after, the thought of his love made her happy.", "그 후로 여러 주 동안, 아이의 사랑을 떠올리는 것만으로도 그녀는 행복했다."], ["She slept better with the picture under her pillow, and how many many times did she kiss it and weep and pray over it!", "그 초상화를 베개 밑에 두고서 그녀는 한결 잘 잤고, 또 얼마나 자주, 얼마나 여러 번 그것에 입 맞추며 울고 그것을 두고 기도했던가!"], ["A small kindness from those she loved made that timid heart grateful.", "사랑하는 이들이 베푸는 작은 친절 하나가 그 소심한 마음을 감사로 가득 채웠다."], ["Since her parting with George she had had no such joy and consolation.", "조지와 헤어진 이래로 그녀에게는 그런 기쁨도 위안도 없었던 것이다."]], [["At his new home Master George ruled like a lord; at dinner he invited the ladies to drink wine with the utmost coolness, and took off his champagne in a way which charmed his old grandfather.", "새 보금자리에서 조지 도련님은 영주처럼 군림했다. 식사 자리에서는 더없이 태연하게 숙녀분들에게 함께 와인을 들자고 청했고, 샴페인을 들이켜는 그 모습이 늙은 외할아버지를 매료시켰다."], ["\"Look at him,\"", "\"저 녀석 좀 보게.\""], ["the old man would say, nudging his neighbour with a delighted purple face, \"did you ever see such a chap?", "노인은 흐뭇함에 자줏빛으로 물든 얼굴로 옆 사람을 슬쩍 찌르며 말하곤 했다. \"저런 녀석 본 적 있나?"], ["Lord, Lord!", "원, 세상에!"], ["he'll be ordering a dressing-case next, and razors to shave with; I'm blessed if he won't.\"", "다음번엔 화장 도구함이며 면도할 면도칼까지 주문하겠어. 정말이지 안 그럴 리가 없다니까.\""]], [["The antics of the lad did not, however, delight Mr. Osborne's friends so much as they pleased the old gentleman.", "그러나 그 아이의 별난 짓거리가 노신사를 흐뭇하게 한 만큼 오스본 씨의 벗들까지 즐겁게 한 것은 아니었다."], ["It gave Mr. Justice Coffin no pleasure to hear Georgy cut into the conversation and spoil his stories.", "코핀 판사로서는 조지가 대화에 끼어들어 제 이야기를 망쳐 놓는 것이 영 즐겁지 않았다."], ["Colonel Fogey was not interested in seeing the little boy half tipsy. Mr.", "포기 대령도 그 어린 사내아이가 반쯤 거나해진 꼴을 보는 데 흥미를 느끼지 못했다."], ["Sergeant Toffy's lady felt no particular gratitude, when, with a twist of his elbow, he tilted a glass of port-wine over her yellow satin and laughed at the disaster; nor was she better pleased, although old Osborne was highly delighted, when Georgy \"whopped\"", "토피 변호사 부인은, 아이가 팔꿈치를 휙 꺾는 바람에 포도주 한 잔이 자기 노란 새틴 위로 엎질러지고 그 낭패를 두고 아이가 깔깔대며 웃었을 때 별달리 고마운 마음이 들지 않았다."], ["her third boy (a young gentleman a year older than Georgy, and by chance home for the holidays from Dr. Tickleus's at Ealing School) in Russell Square.", "또한 늙은 오스본은 더없이 흐뭇해했지만, 부인 쪽으로 말하자면, 조지가 러셀 광장에서 자기 셋째 아들을(조지보다 한 살 위인 어린 신사로, 마침 일링 학교의 티클리어스 박사 댁에서 방학을 맞아 집에 와 있던 참이었다) \"한 대 갈겼을\""], ["George's grandfather gave the boy a couple of sovereigns for that feat and promised to reward him further for every boy above his own size and age whom he whopped in a similar manner.", "때도 그리 흡족해하지 않았다. 조지의 외할아버지는 그 무용담을 두고 아이에게 금화 두 닢을 주었고, 앞으로도 제 덩치와 나이보다 위인 아이를 그런 식으로 갈겨 줄 때마다 더 상을 내리겠노라고 약속했다."], ["It is difficult to say what good the old man saw in these combats; he had a vague notion that quarrelling made boys hardy, and that tyranny was a useful accomplishment for them to learn.", "노인이 이런 싸움질에서 대체 무슨 좋은 점을 보았는지는 말하기 어렵다. 다만 그는 다투는 것이 사내아이를 단단하게 만들고, 남을 윽박지르는 것이 그들이 익혀 둘 만한 쓸모 있는 재간이라는 막연한 생각을 품고 있었다."], ["English youth have been so educated time out of mind, and we have hundreds of thousands of apologists and admirers of injustice, misery, and brutality, as perpetrated among children.", "영국의 젊은이들은 아주 오래전부터 그런 식으로 길러져 왔고, 우리에게는 아이들 사이에 자행되는 불의와 비참함과 잔혹함을 변호하고 떠받드는 자가 수십만이나 있는 것이다."], ["Flushed with praise and victory over Master Toffy, George wished naturally to pursue his conquests further, and one day as he was strutting about in prodigiously dandified new clothes, near St.", "토피 도련님을 이기고 칭찬에 우쭐해진 조지는 당연하게도 그 정복을 더 밀고 나가고 싶어 했다."], ["Pancras, and a young baker's boy made sarcastic comments upon his appearance, the youthful patrician pulled off his dandy jacket with great spirit, and giving it in charge to the friend who accompanied him (Master Todd, of Great Coram Street, Russell Square, son of the junior partner of the house of Osborne and Co.), George tried to whop the little baker.", "그러던 어느 날, 굉장히 멋을 부린 새 옷을 차려입고 세인트팽크러스 근처를 으스대며 거닐다가, 어린 빵집 사환 녀석이 그 행색을 두고 빈정대자, 이 젊은 귀족 나리는 패기 넘치게 그 멋쟁이 윗도리를 벗어, 동행한 친구에게(러셀 광장 그레이트 코럼 거리에 사는 토드 도련님으로, 오스본 상회의 젊은 동업자의 아들이었다) 맡기고는, 그 어린 빵집 녀석을 갈겨 주려 했다."], ["But the chances of war were unfavourable this time, and the little baker whopped Georgy, who came home with a rueful black eye and all his fine shirt frill dabbled with the claret drawn from his own little nose.", "그러나 이번에는 전세가 불리하여, 어린 빵집 녀석이 조지를 갈겨 주었다. 조지는 가엾게도 눈에 시퍼런 멍이 들고, 자기 코에서 흘러나온 붉은 피로 그 고운 셔츠 주름 장식을 온통 적신 채 집에 돌아왔다."], ["He told his grandfather that he had been in combat with a giant, and frightened his poor mother at Brompton with long, and by no means authentic, accounts of the battle.", "그는 외할아버지에게 거인과 싸웠노라 둘러댔고, 브롬프턴의 가엾은 어머니에게는 그 싸움에 관해 장황하고 결코 사실과는 거리가 먼 이야기를 늘어놓아 어머니를 겁에 질리게 했다."]], [["This young Todd, of Coram Street, Russell Square, was Master George's great friend and admirer.", "러셀 광장 코럼 거리에 사는 이 어린 토드는 조지 도련님의 둘도 없는 친구이자 숭배자였다."], ["They both had a taste for painting theatrical characters; for hardbake and raspberry tarts; for sliding and skating in the Regent's Park and the Serpentine, when the weather permitted; for going to the play, whither they were often conducted, by Mr.", "둘은 다 같이 연극 인물들을 그려 넣는 취미를 즐겼고, 딱딱한 사탕과 라즈베리 타르트를 좋아했으며, 날씨가 허락할 때면 리젠트 공원과 서펜타인 호수에서 미끄럼을 타고 스케이트를 지치는 것을 즐겼고, 또 연극 구경을 좋아했다."], ["Osborne's orders, by Rowson, Master George's appointed body-servant, with whom they sat in great comfort in the pit.", "연극을 보러 갈 때는 오스본 씨의 분부에 따라 조지 도련님 전속으로 정해진 시종 로슨이 종종 데리고 갔는데, 셋이서 일등석에 아주 편안히 자리를 잡고 앉았다."]], [["In the company of this gentleman they visited all the principal theatres of the metropolis; knew the names of all the actors from Drury Lane to Sadler's Wells; and performed, indeed, many of the plays to the Todd family and their youthful friends, with West's famous characters, on their pasteboard theatre.", "이 신사를 동무 삼아 그들은 수도의 주요 극장이란 극장은 다 다녔고, 드루리 레인에서 새들러스 웰스에 이르기까지 모든 배우의 이름을 꿰뚫었다. 또한 실제로 웨스트의 그 유명한 인물 종이 인형들로, 자기네 판지 무대에서 토드네 식구들과 그 어린 동무들을 앞에 두고 숱한 연극을 공연하기도 했다."], ["Rowson, the footman, who was of a generous disposition, would not unfrequently, when in cash, treat his young master to oysters after the play, and to a glass of rum-shrub for a night-cap.", "하인 로슨은 너그러운 성품이라, 주머니에 돈이 있을 때면 연극이 끝난 뒤 어린 주인에게 굴 요리를 자주 사 주었고, 잠자리에 들기 전 마시는 럼주 한 잔도 한턱냈다."], ["We may be pretty certain that Mr. Rowson profited in his turn by his young master's liberality and gratitude for the pleasures to which the footman inducted him.", "그러나 로슨 씨 또한 그가 어린 주인을 그 즐거움으로 인도해 준 데 대한 어린 주인의 후한 인심과 감사에서 제 나름대로 한몫 톡톡히 챙겼으리라는 것은 우리도 거의 확신할 만하다."]], [["A famous tailor from the West End of the town--Mr. Osborne would have none of your City or Holborn bunglers, he said, for the boy (though a City tailor was good enough for HIM)--was summoned to ornament little George's person, and was told to spare no expense in so doing.", "런던 서쪽 끝의 한 유명한 재단사가―오스본 씨는 이 아이를 위해서라면 그런 런던 상업지구나 홀번의 서툰 작자들은 절대 안 된다고 했다(정작 자기에게는 런던 상업지구 재단사로도 충분했지만)―어린 조지를 치장하라는 부름을 받았고, 그 일에 비용을 아끼지 말라는 분부를 받았다."], ["So, Mr. Woolsey, of Conduit Street, gave a loose to his imagination and sent the child home fancy trousers, fancy waistcoats, and fancy jackets enough to furnish a school of little dandies.", "그리하여 컨듀잇 거리의 울지 씨는 상상력을 한껏 펼쳐, 어린 멋쟁이들로 학교 하나를 꾸리고도 남을 만큼 멋진 바지며 멋진 조끼며 멋진 윗도리를 잔뜩 지어 보냈다."], ["Georgy had little white waistcoats for evening parties, and little cut velvet waistcoats for dinners, and a dear little darling shawl dressing-gown, for all the world like a little man.", "조지는 저녁 모임용으로 흰 작은 조끼들을 갖추었고, 만찬용으로는 무늬를 넣어 자른 벨벳 조끼들을 갖추었으며, 영락없이 작은 어른 같은 사랑스러운 작은 숄 실내복도 한 벌 가졌다."], ["He dressed for dinner every day, \"like a regular West End swell,\"", "그는 외할아버지의 말마따나 \"영락없는 서쪽 끝의 멋쟁이처럼\""], ["as his grandfather remarked; one of the domestics was affected to his special service, attended him at his toilette, answered his bell, and brought him his letters always on a silver tray.", "날마다 만찬을 위해 차려입었다. 하인 하나가 그의 전속으로 배정되어 몸단장 시중을 들고, 종소리에 응답하고, 늘 은쟁반에 받쳐 편지를 가져다주었다."]], [["Georgy, after breakfast, would sit in the arm-chair in the dining-room and read the Morning Post, just like a grown-up man.", "조지는 아침을 먹고 나면 식당의 안락의자에 앉아, 영락없는 어른처럼 「모닝 포스트」를 읽곤 했다. \"저 어린 게 어찌나 욕지거리를 해 대고 빌어먹을 소리를 하던지.\""], ["\"How he DU dam and swear,\" the servants would cry, delighted at his precocity.", "하인들은 그 조숙함에 흐뭇해하며 떠들어 댔다."], ["Those who remembered the Captain his father, declared Master George was his Pa, every inch of him.", "그의 아버지인 대위를 기억하는 이들은 조지 도련님이 머리끝부터 발끝까지 그 아버지 빼다박았다고 단언했다."], ["He made the house lively by his activity, his imperiousness, his scolding, and his good-nature.", "그는 활기와 거만함과 잔소리와 사람 좋은 성미로 집안에 생기를 불어넣었다."]], [["George's education was confided to a neighbouring scholar and private pedagogue who \"prepared young noblemen and gentlemen for the Universities, the senate, and the learned professions: whose system did not embrace the degrading corporal severities still practised at the ancient places of education, and in whose family the pupils would find the elegances of refined society and the confidence and affection of a home.\"", "조지의 교육은 이웃에 사는 한 학자이자 사숙 훈장에게 맡겨졌으니, 그는 \"젊은 귀족과 신사들을 대학과 의회와 학식을 요하는 직업으로 나아가도록 준비시키며, 그의 방식은 유서 깊은 교육 기관에서 여태 행해지는 저 품위를 떨어뜨리는 체벌을 포함하지 아니하고, 그의 집안에서 문하생들은 세련된 사교계의 우아함과 더불어 한 가정의 신뢰와 애정을 누리게 될 것\"이라고 광고했다."], ["It was in this way that the Reverend Lawrence Veal of Hart Street, Bloomsbury, and domestic Chaplain to the Earl of Bareacres, strove with Mrs. Veal his wife to entice pupils.", "블룸즈버리 하트 거리에 사는 베어에이커스 백작의 전속 사제 로런스 빌 목사가 그의 아내인 빌 부인과 더불어 문하생을 꾀어들이려 애쓴 방법이 바로 이러했다."]], [["By thus advertising and pushing sedulously, the domestic Chaplain and his Lady generally succeeded in having one or two scholars by them--who paid a high figure and were thought to be in uncommonly comfortable quarters.", "이렇듯 부지런히 광고를 내고 밀어붙인 덕에, 이 전속 사제와 그 부인은 대체로 한두 명의 문하생을 곁에 두는 데 성공했다. 그들은 높은 수업료를 냈고, 유달리 안락한 거처에 머무는 것으로 여겨졌다."], ["There was a large West Indian, whom nobody came to see, with a mahogany complexion, a woolly head, and an exceedingly dandyfied appearance; there was another hulking boy of three-and-twenty whose education had been neglected and whom Mr. and Mrs.", "마호가니빛 안색에 곱슬머리, 지독히 멋을 부린 행색의 서인도제도 출신 학생이 하나 있었는데, 그를 찾아오는 이는 아무도 없었다."], ["Veal were to introduce into the polite world; there were two sons of Colonel Bangles of the East India Company's Service: these four sat down to dinner at Mrs.", "또 스물세 살이나 된 우람한 사내아이가 하나 있었는데, 교육이 소홀했던 그를 빌 씨 부부가 상류 사교계에 소개해 줄 참이었다. 그리고 동인도회사 소속 뱅글스 대령의 두 아들이 있었다."], ["Veal's genteel board, when Georgy was introduced to her establishment.", "조지가 빌 부인의 학원에 들어왔을 때, 이 넷이 빌 부인의 품격 있는 식탁에 둘러앉았다."]], [["Georgy was, like some dozen other pupils, only a day boy; he arrived in the morning under the guardianship of his friend Mr.", "조지는 다른 열두어 명의 문하생과 마찬가지로 통학생일 뿐이었다."], ["Rowson, and if it was fine, would ride away in the afternoon on his pony, followed by the groom.", "그는 아침이면 친구 로슨 씨의 보호를 받으며 도착했고, 날이 좋으면 오후에는 마부를 뒤에 거느리고 조랑말을 타고 떠나곤 했다."], ["The wealth of his grandfather was reported in the school to be prodigious.", "그 외할아버지의 재산이 어마어마하다는 소문이 학원에 자자했다."], ["The Rev. Mr.", "빌 목사는 조지를 면전에서 그 점을 두고 추켜세우곤 했는데, 그가 높은 지위에 오를 운명을 타고났으며, 따라서 젊을 때 근면과 순종으로써 장성한 뒤 맡게 될 높은 소임에 대비하는 것이 마땅하고, 아이 적의 순종이야말로 어른이 되어 명령을 내리는 데 가장 좋은 준비라고 일러 주었다."], ["Veal used to compliment Georgy upon it personally, warning him that he was destined for a high station; that it became him to prepare, by sedulity and docility in youth, for the lofty duties to which he would be called in mature age; that obedience in the child was the best preparation for command in the man; and that he therefore begged George would not bring toffee into the school and ruin the health of the Masters Bangles, who had everything they wanted at the elegant and abundant table of Mrs. Veal.", "그러니 조지더러 학원에 토피 사탕을 가져와, 빌 부인의 우아하고 풍성한 식탁에서 원하는 것은 무엇이든 다 갖춘 뱅글스 도련님들의 건강을 망쳐 놓는 일이 없도록 해 달라고 당부하는 것이었다."]], [["With respect to learning, \"the Curriculum,\" as Mr.", "학문에 관해 말하자면, 빌 씨가 즐겨 부르던 대로 \"교과 과정\"이란 그 폭이 어마어마하여, 하트 거리의 어린 신사들은 알려진 온갖 학문을 조금씩이나마 배울 수 있었다."], ["Veal loved to call it, was of prodigious extent, and the young gentlemen in Hart Street might learn a something of every known science. The Rev.", "빌 목사는 태양계 의(儀)며, 발전기며, 녹로며, (세탁실에 차린) 극장이며, 화학 실험 기구며, 그리고 그가 고대와 현대의 온갖 언어로 된 최고 저자들의 모든 저작을 가려 뽑은 장서라고 부르는 것을 갖추고 있었다."], ["Mr. Veal had an orrery, an electrifying machine, a turning lathe, a theatre (in the wash-house), a chemical apparatus, and what he called a select library of all the works of the best authors of ancient and modern times and languages.", "그는 아이들을 대영박물관에 데려가 그곳의 고대 유물과 자연사 표본들을 논했는데, 어찌나 청산유수였던지 그가 이야기를 하면 사람들이 그 주위로 몰려들었고, 블룸즈버리 사람들 모두가 그를 어마어마하게 박식한 사람이라며 떠받들었다."], ["He took the boys to the British Museum and descanted upon the antiquities and the specimens of natural history there, so that audiences would gather round him as he spoke, and all Bloomsbury highly admired him as a prodigiously well-informed man. And whenever he spoke (which he did almost always), he took care to produce the very finest and longest words of which the vocabulary gave him the use, rightly judging that it was as cheap to employ a handsome, large, and sonorous epithet, as to use a little stingy one.", "그리고 그는 입을 열 때마다(거의 늘 입을 열고 있었지만) 어휘가 허락하는 가장 곱고 가장 긴 낱말을 골라 쓰는 데 신경을 썼으니, 곱고 큼직하고 낭랑하게 울리는 형용사를 쓰는 것이나 인색하게 작은 낱말을 쓰는 것이나 드는 품은 매한가지라고 옳게 판단했던 것이다."]], [["Thus he would say to George in school, \"I observed on my return home from taking the indulgence of an evening's scientific conversation with my excellent friend Doctor Bulders--a true archaeologian, gentlemen, a true archaeologian--that the windows of your venerated grandfather's almost princely mansion in Russell Square were illuminated as if for the purposes of festivity.", "이를테면 그는 학원에서 조지에게 이렇게 말하는 것이었다. \"내가 나의 훌륭한 벗 불더스 박사와―제군, 그분은 참된 고고학자이시네, 참된 고고학자이시지―더불어 어느 저녁나절 학문적 담소의 즐거움을 누리고 집으로 돌아오는 길에 보니, 러셀 광장에 있는 자네의 존경스러운 외할아버님의 거의 궁궐과도 같은 저택의 창문들이 마치 잔치를 벌이려는 듯 환히 밝혀져 있더군."], ["Am I right in my conjecture that Mr. Osborne entertained a society of chosen spirits round his sumptuous board last night?\"", "오스본 씨께서 어젯밤 그 호사스러운 식탁에 가려 뽑은 명사들의 모임을 베푸셨으리라는 내 짐작이 옳은가?\""]], [["Little Georgy, who had considerable humour, and used to mimic Mr. Veal to his face with great spirit and dexterity, would reply that Mr. V. was quite correct in his surmise.", "상당한 익살을 지닌 어린 조지는 빌 씨를 면전에서 굉장한 패기와 솜씨로 흉내 내곤 했는데, 빌 선생님의 짐작이 지극히 옳다고 대답하는 것이었다."]], [["\"Then those friends who had the honour of partaking of Mr. Osborne's hospitality, gentlemen, had no reason, I will lay any wager, to complain of their repast.", "\"그렇다면 제군, 오스본 씨의 환대를 함께 누리는 영예를 입은 그 벗들은, 내 무엇이든 걸어도 좋으니, 그 식사를 두고 불평할 이유가 없었을 게야."], ["I myself have been more than once so favoured.", "나 자신도 한 번 이상 그런 은혜를 입은 바 있으니까.(그런데 오스본 도련님, 자네 오늘 아침 좀 늦게 왔더군."], ["(By the way, Master Osborne, you came a little late this morning, and have been a defaulter in this respect more than once.) I myself, I say, gentlemen, humble as I am, have been found not unworthy to share Mr.", "이 점에서 자네가 규칙을 어긴 게 한두 번이 아니야.) 거듭 말하건대 제군, 나 자신은 비록 미천하나, 오스본 씨의 우아한 환대를 함께 누릴 만한 자격이 없지 않다고 인정받았네."], ["Osborne's elegant hospitality. And though I have feasted with the great and noble of the world--for I presume that I may call my excellent friend and patron, the Right Honourable George Earl of Bareacres, one of the number--yet I assure you that the board of the British merchant was to the full as richly served, and his reception as gratifying and noble.", "그리고 내 비록 세상의 위대하고 고귀한 분들과 더불어 향연을 베푼 적이 있으되―나의 훌륭한 벗이자 후원자이신 베어에이커스 백작 조지 각하를 그 한 분으로 꼽아도 좋으리라 여기네만―그래도 단언하건대, 영국 상인의 식탁도 그에 못지않게 푸짐하게 차려져 있었고, 그 접대 또한 그만큼 흡족하고 고귀하였네."], ["Mr. Bluck, sir, we will resume, if you please, that passage of Eutropis, which was interrupted by the late arrival of Master Osborne.\"", "블럭 군, 괜찮다면 오스본 도련님의 지각으로 중단되었던 에우트로피우스의 그 대목을 다시 시작하기로 하지.\""]], [["To this great man George's education was for some time entrusted.", "이 위대한 인물에게 조지의 교육이 한동안 맡겨졌다."], ["Amelia was bewildered by his phrases, but thought him a prodigy of learning.", "애밀리아는 그의 어투에 어리둥절했으나, 그를 학문의 경이로 여겼다."], ["That poor widow made friends of Mrs. Veal, for reasons of her own.", "그 가엾은 미망인은 제 나름의 이유에서 빌 부인과 가까이 지냈다."], ["She liked to be in the house and see Georgy coming to school there. She liked to be asked to Mrs.", "그녀는 그 집에 머물며 조지가 그곳으로 학교에 오는 것을 보는 게 좋았다."], ["Veal's conversazioni, which took place once a month (as you were informed on pink cards, with AOHNH [Transcriber's Note: The name of the Greek goddess Athene; the \"O\" represents a capital theta.] engraved on them), and where the professor welcomed his pupils and their friends to weak tea and scientific conversation.", "그녀는 한 달에 한 번 열리는 빌 부인의 담화회에 초대받는 것이 좋았는데(분홍색 초대장에 「아테나」라고 새겨진 것으로 알려 주었다), 그 자리에서 교수는 자기 문하생들과 그 벗들을 맞이하여 멀건 차와 학문적 담소를 베풀었다."], ["Poor little Amelia never missed one of these entertainments and thought them delicious so long as she might have Georgy sitting by her.", "가엾은 어린 애밀리아는 이런 모임에 한 번도 빠지지 않았고, 조지를 곁에 앉힐 수만 있다면 그 모임이 더없이 즐겁다고 여겼다."], ["And she would walk from Brompton in any weather, and embrace Mrs. Veal with tearful gratitude for the delightful evening she had passed, when, the company having retired and Georgy gone off with Mr.", "그녀는 날씨가 어떻든 브롬프턴에서부터 걸어왔고, 모임이 파하고 조지가 시종 로슨 씨와 함께 떠나 버린 뒤, 가엾은 오스본 부인이 집으로 걸어 돌아갈 채비로 외투와 숄을 걸칠 때면, 즐거운 저녁을 보냈노라며 눈물 어린 감사로 빌 부인을 끌어안는 것이었다."], ["Rowson, his attendant, poor Mrs.", "* 아테나: 그리스 여신 아테네의 이름."], ["Osborne put on her cloaks and her shawls preparatory to walking home.", "원문에는 그리스 문자로 새겨져 있다."]], [["As for the learning which Georgy imbibed under this valuable master of a hundred sciences, to judge from the weekly reports which the lad took home to his grandfather, his progress was remarkable.", "조지가 백 가지 학문에 통달한 이 귀한 스승 밑에서 흡수한 학식으로 말하자면, 아이가 외할아버지에게 가져가는 주간 성적표로 판단하건대 그 진척이 놀라웠다."], ["The names of a score or more of desirable branches of knowledge were printed in a table, and the pupil's progress in each was marked by the professor.", "바람직한 지식의 분야 스무남은 개의 이름이 표에 인쇄되어 있었고, 그 각각에서 문하생이 거둔 진척을 교수가 표시해 주었다."], ["In Greek Georgy was pronounced aristos, in Latin optimus, in French tres bien, and so forth; and everybody had prizes for everything at the end of the year. Even Mr.", "그리스어에서 조지는 아리스토스, 라틴어에서는 옵티무스, 프랑스어에서는 트레비앵 등의 평가를 받았다."], ["Swartz, the wooly-headed young gentleman, and half-brother to the Honourable Mrs.", "그리고 연말에는 모두가 무엇으로든 상을 탔다."], ["Mac Mull, and Mr. Bluck, the neglected young pupil of three-and-twenty from the agricultural district, and that idle young scapegrace of a Master Todd before mentioned, received little eighteen-penny books, with \"Athene\"", "곱슬머리의 어린 신사이자 존경하는 맥멀 부인의 이복형제인 스워츠 군조차, 또 농촌 출신으로 교육이 소홀했던 스물세 살의 블럭 군조차, 또 앞서 말한 게으르고 망나니 같은 토드 도련님조차, \"아테네\"라는 글자가 새겨지고 교수가 어린 벗들에게 보내는 거창한 라틴어 헌사가 적힌 십팔 펜스짜리 작은 책들을 받았다."], ["engraved on them, and a pompous Latin inscription from the professor to his young friends.", "* 아리스토스, 옵티무스, 트레비앵: 각각 그리스어, 라틴어, 프랑스어로 '최우수'를 뜻하는 평가어."]], [["The family of this Master Todd were hangers-on of the house of Osborne.", "이 토드 도련님의 집안은 오스본 가문에 빌붙어 사는 식객들이었다."], ["The old gentleman had advanced Todd from being a clerk to be a junior partner in his establishment.", "노신사는 토드를 일개 사무원에서 자기 회사의 젊은 동업자로 끌어올려 주었던 것이다."]], [["Mr. Osborne was the godfather of young Master Todd (who in subsequent life wrote Mr.", "오스본 씨는 어린 토드 도련님의 대부였고(그는 훗날 명함에 '오스본 토드'라고 써넣었으며, 영락없는 멋쟁이가 되었다), 오스본 양은 마리아 토드 양과 함께 세례식에 참석했으며, 자기가 후원하는 그 아이에게 해마다 기도서며, 소책자 모음이며, 더없이 저교회파다운 시집이며, 그 비슷한 자선의 기념품을 주었다."], ["Osborne Todd on his cards and became a man of decided fashion), while Miss Osborne had accompanied Miss Maria Todd to the font, and gave her protegee a prayer-book, a collection of tracts, a volume of very low church poetry, or some such memento of her goodness every year. Miss O.", "오스본 양은 이따금 토드네 식구들을 제 마차에 태워 바람을 쐬게 해 주었다."], ["drove the Todds out in her carriage now and then; when they were ill, her footman, in large plush smalls and waistcoat, brought jellies and delicacies from Russell Square to Coram Street. Coram Street trembled and looked up to Russell Square indeed, and Mrs.", "그들이 앓아누우면, 큼직한 플러시 천 반바지에 조끼를 입은 그녀의 하인이 러셀 광장에서 코럼 거리로 젤리며 별미를 날라 갔다."], ["Todd, who had a pretty hand at cutting out paper trimmings for haunches of mutton, and could make flowers, ducks, &c., out of turnips and carrots in a very creditable manner, would go to \"the Square,\" as it was called, and assist in the preparations incident to a great dinner, without even so much as thinking of sitting down to the banquet.", "과연 코럼 거리는 러셀 광장 앞에서 벌벌 떨며 우러러보았고, 양고기 다리에 두를 종이 장식 오리는 솜씨가 제법이고 순무와 당근으로 꽃이며 오리 따위를 그럴듯하게 빚어낼 줄 알던 토드 부인은, 사람들이 \"광장\"이라 부르던 그곳으로 가서 큰 만찬에 따르는 준비를 거들면서도, 정작 그 잔칫상에 앉을 생각은 꿈에도 하지 않았다."], ["If any guest failed at the eleventh hour, Todd was asked to dine. Mrs.", "막판에 손님 하나가 빠지기라도 하면 그제야 토드가 만찬에 청해졌다."], ["Todd and Maria came across in the evening, slipped in with a muffled knock, and were in the drawing-room by the time Miss Osborne and the ladies under her convoy reached that apartment--and ready to fire off duets and sing until the gentlemen came up.", "토드 부인과 마리아는 저녁에 건너와, 소리 죽인 노크와 함께 슬그머니 들어와서는, 오스본 양과 그녀가 거느린 숙녀들이 응접실에 다다를 무렵이면 이미 그곳에 자리를 잡고 앉아, 신사들이 올라올 때까지 이중창을 펼치고 노래할 채비를 갖추고 있었다."], ["Poor Maria Todd; poor young lady!", "가엾은 마리아 토드, 가엾은 아가씨!"], ["How she had to work and thrum at these duets and sonatas in the Street, before they appeared in public in the Square!", "이 이중창과 소나타를 \"광장\"에서 공개로 선보이기 전에, 그녀는 그 \"거리\"에서 그것들을 얼마나 연습하고 또 줄을 뜯어 대야 했던가!"]], [["Thus it seemed to be decreed by fate that Georgy was to domineer over everybody with whom he came in contact, and that friends, relatives, and domestics were all to bow the knee before the little fellow.", "이렇듯 조지가 마주치는 모든 이를 깔아뭉개며 군림하고, 벗이며 친척이며 하인이 죄다 그 어린 녀석 앞에 무릎을 꿇어야 한다는 것이 운명으로 정해진 듯했다."], ["It must be owned that he accommodated himself very willingly to this arrangement.", "그가 이런 처지에 기꺼이 순순히 적응했다는 것은 인정해야겠다. 대부분의 사람들이 그러는 법이다."], ["Most people do so. And Georgy liked to play the part of master and perhaps had a natural aptitude for it.", "그리고 조지는 주인 노릇 하기를 좋아했고, 아마도 거기에 타고난 소질이 있었으리라."]], [["In Russell Square everybody was afraid of Mr. Osborne, and Mr.", "러셀 광장에서는 모두가 오스본 씨를 두려워했고, 오스본 씨는 조지를 두려워했다."], ["Osborne was afraid of Georgy. The boy's dashing manners, and offhand rattle about books and learning, his likeness to his father (dead unreconciled in Brussels yonder) awed the old gentleman and gave the young boy the mastery.", "아이의 활달한 거동과, 책이며 학문에 관해 거침없이 떠벌리는 모습, 그리고 (저 멀리 브뤼셀에서 화해하지 못한 채 죽어 간) 제 아버지를 빼닮은 점이 노신사를 주눅 들게 했고, 어린아이에게 주도권을 쥐여 주었다."], ["The old man would start at some hereditary feature or tone unconsciously used by the little lad, and fancy that George's father was again before him.", "노인은 어린 녀석이 무심코 내비치는 어떤 집안 내력의 이목구비나 말투에 흠칫 놀라며, 조지의 아버지가 다시 눈앞에 서 있는 듯 여기곤 했다."], ["He tried by indulgence to the grandson to make up for harshness to the elder George.", "그는 손자에게 너그럽게 굴어, 형뻘 조지에게 모질게 대했던 것을 만회해 보려 했다."], ["People were surprised at his gentleness to the boy.", "사람들은 그가 그 아이에게 다정한 데 놀랐다."], ["He growled and swore at Miss Osborne as usual, and would smile when George came down late for breakfast.", "그는 여느 때처럼 오스본 양에게는 으르렁대며 욕을 퍼부었지만, 조지가 아침 식사에 늦게 내려오면 미소를 지었다."]], [["Miss Osborne, George's aunt, was a faded old spinster, broken down by more than forty years of dulness and coarse usage.", "조지의 고모인 오스본 양은 사십 년이 넘는 따분함과 거친 대우에 짓눌려 시들어 버린 늙은 노처녀였다."], ["It was easy for a lad of spirit to master her.", "패기 있는 사내아이가 그녀를 휘어잡기란 쉬운 일이었다."], ["And whenever George wanted anything from her, from the jam-pots in her cupboards to the cracked and dry old colours in her paint-box (the old paint-box which she had had when she was a pupil of Mr. Smee and was still almost young and blooming), Georgy took possession of the object of his desire, which obtained, he took no further notice of his aunt.", "조지는 그녀의 찬장에 든 잼 단지에서부터 물감 상자에 든 갈라지고 말라붙은 낡은 물감에 이르기까지(그것은 그녀가 스미 씨의 문하생이던 시절, 그러니까 아직 거의 젊고 한창 꽃피던 시절에 쓰던 낡은 물감 상자였다) 그녀에게서 무언가를 원할 때면, 제가 탐내는 물건을 차지해 버렸고, 일단 손에 넣고 나면 더는 고모에게 눈길도 주지 않았다."]], [["For his friends and cronies, he had a pompous old schoolmaster, who flattered him, and a toady, his senior, whom he could thrash.", "그의 벗이자 단짝으로는, 그를 추켜세워 주는 거만한 늙은 훈장과, 그가 마구 두들겨 줄 수 있는 자기보다 나이 많은 아첨꾼 하나가 있었다."], ["It was dear Mrs. Todd's delight to leave him with her youngest daughter, Rosa Jemima, a darling child of eight years old.", "사랑하는 토드 부인의 더없는 기쁨은 그를 자기 막내딸인 로자 제미마와 함께 두는 것이었으니, 그 아이는 여덟 살 난 귀여운 꼬마였다."], ["The little pair looked so well together, she would say (but not to the folks in \"the Square,\"", "그 어린 한 쌍이 어찌나 잘 어울리던지, 그녀는(물론 \"광장\" 사람들에게는 절대 그러지 않았으리라는 게 분명하지만) 이렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["we may be sure) \"who knows what might happen?", "\"누가 알겠어요, 무슨 일이 생길지? 정말 예쁜 한 쌍 아닌가요?\""], ["Don't they make a pretty little couple?\" the fond mother thought.", "그 다정한 어머니는 그렇게 생각하는 것이었다."]], [["The broken-spirited, old, maternal grandfather was likewise subject to the little tyrant.", "기가 꺾인 늙은 외할아버지 또한 이 어린 폭군에게 휘둘렸다."], ["He could not help respecting a lad who had such fine clothes and rode with a groom behind him.", "그는 그토록 고운 옷을 입고 마부를 뒤에 거느린 채 말을 타는 아이를 존경하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Georgy, on his side, was in the constant habit of hearing coarse abuse and vulgar satire levelled at John Sedley by his pitiless old enemy, Mr. Osborne.", "조지 쪽에서는, 무자비한 옛 원수인 오스본 씨가 존 세들리에게 퍼붓는 거친 욕설과 천박한 비아냥을 늘 듣곤 했다."], ["Osborne used to call the other the old pauper, the old coal-man, the old bankrupt, and by many other such names of brutal contumely.", "오스본은 상대편을 늙은 거지, 늙은 석탄 장수, 늙은 파산자, 그 밖의 잔인하고 모욕적인 온갖 이름으로 불렀다."], ["How was little George to respect a man so prostrate?", "그토록 짓밟힌 사람을 어린 조지가 어찌 존경하겠는가?"], ["A few months after he was with his paternal grandfather, Mrs. Sedley died.", "친할아버지 댁에 온 지 몇 달 뒤, 세들리 부인이 세상을 떠났다."], ["There had been little love between her and the child.", "그녀와 아이 사이에는 애정이라 할 것이 별로 없었다."], ["He did not care to show much grief.", "그는 별다른 슬픔을 내보이려 들지 않았다."], ["He came down to visit his mother in a fine new suit of mourning, and was very angry that he could not go to a play upon which he had set his heart.", "그는 멋진 새 상복 차림으로 어머니를 보러 내려왔으나, 잔뜩 벼르던 연극을 보러 갈 수 없게 되었다고 몹시 화를 냈다."]], [["The illness of that old lady had been the occupation and perhaps the safeguard of Amelia.", "그 노부인의 병환은 애밀리아에게 하나의 소일거리였고, 어쩌면 하나의 보호막이기도 했다."], ["What do men know about women's martyrdoms?", "사내들이 여인의 순교에 대해 무엇을 알겠는가?"], ["We should go mad had we to endure the hundredth part of those daily pains which are meekly borne by many women.", "우리라면 숱한 여인들이 온순하게 견뎌 내는 그 나날의 고통의 백분의 일이라도 감내해야 한다면 미쳐 버리고 말 것이다."], ["Ceaseless slavery meeting with no reward; constant gentleness and kindness met by cruelty as constant; love, labour, patience, watchfulness, without even so much as the acknowledgement of a good word; all this, how many of them have to bear in quiet, and appear abroad with cheerful faces as if they felt nothing.", "아무 보답도 없는 끝없는 종살이, 한결같은 잔인함으로 되돌아오는 한결같은 온유와 친절, 따뜻한 말 한마디 인정받지 못하는 사랑과 노고와 인내와 보살핌. 이 모든 것을 그 얼마나 많은 여인들이 잠자코 견디며, 마치 아무것도 느끼지 못하는 양 밝은 얼굴로 세상에 나서야 하는 것인가."], ["Tender slaves that they are, they must needs be hypocrites and weak.", "다정한 노예인 그들은 위선자도 약자도 될 수밖에 없는 것이다."]], [["From her chair Amelia's mother had taken to her bed, which she had never left, and from which Mrs.", "애밀리아의 어머니는 의자에서 자리보전을 하게 되었고, 다시는 그 자리를 뜨지 못했다."], ["Osborne herself was never absent except when she ran to see George.", "오스본 부인 자신도 조지를 보러 달려갈 때를 빼고는 결코 그 곁을 떠나지 않았다."], ["The old lady grudged her even those rare visits; she, who had been a kind, smiling, good-natured mother once, in the days of her prosperity, but whom poverty and infirmities had broken down.", "노부인은 그 드문 나들이조차 못마땅해했다. 한때, 그러니까 형편이 넉넉하던 시절에는 다정하고 잘 웃고 사람 좋은 어머니였으나, 가난과 병약함이 그녀를 무너뜨려 놓았던 것이다."], ["Her illness or estrangement did not affect Amelia.", "그녀의 병환이나 소원해진 마음이 애밀리아를 괴롭히지는 않았다."], ["They rather enabled her to support the other calamity under which she was suffering, and from the thoughts of which she was kept by the ceaseless calls of the invalid.", "오히려 그 덕분에 그녀는 자신이 시달리던 또 다른 재앙을 견뎌 낼 수 있었으니, 환자의 끊임없는 부름이 그 재앙에 대한 생각에서 그녀를 떼어 놓아 주었기 때문이다."], ["Amelia bore her harshness quite gently; smoothed the uneasy pillow; was always ready with a soft answer to the watchful, querulous voice; soothed the sufferer with words of hope, such as her pious simple heart could best feel and utter, and closed the eyes that had once looked so tenderly upon her.", "애밀리아는 어머니의 모진 태도를 더없이 온순하게 견뎌 냈다. 불편한 베개를 고쳐 주었고, 살피듯 칭얼대는 그 목소리에 늘 부드러운 대답을 준비해 두었으며, 그 신실하고 소박한 마음이 가장 절절히 느끼고 입에 올릴 수 있는 희망의 말들로 병자를 달랬고, 한때 그토록 다정하게 자신을 바라보던 그 두 눈을 감겨 드렸다."]], [["Then all her time and tenderness were devoted to the consolation and comfort of the bereaved old father, who was stunned by the blow which had befallen him, and stood utterly alone in the world.", "그러고 나서 그녀의 모든 시간과 다정함은, 닥쳐온 일격에 망연자실하여 세상에 외따로 남겨진 채 서 있던 늙은 아버지를 위로하고 보살피는 데 바쳐졌다."], ["His wife, his honour, his fortune, everything he loved best had fallen away from him.", "아내며, 명예며, 재산이며, 그가 가장 사랑하던 모든 것이 그에게서 떨어져 나가 버렸다."], ["There was only Amelia to stand by and support with her gentle arms the tottering, heart-broken old man.", "비틀거리며 가슴 무너진 그 노인을 곁에서 그 다정한 팔로 떠받쳐 줄 사람은 오직 애밀리아뿐이었다."], ["We are not going to write the history: it would be too dreary and stupid.", "우리는 그 사연을 적지는 않으련다. 너무도 음울하고 따분할 테니."], ["I can see Vanity Fair yawning over it d'avance.", "허영의 시장이 그것을 앞두고 미리 하품을 하는 모습이 눈에 선하다."]], [["One day as the young gentlemen were assembled in the study at the Rev. Mr.", "어느 날, 빌 목사 댁 서재에 어린 신사들이 모여 있고, 베어에이커스 백작 각하의 전속 사제가 여느 때처럼 청산유수로 떠벌리고 있을 때, 아테네 여신상으로 장식된 문 앞으로 멋들어진 마차 한 대가 다가와 멈춰 섰고, 두 신사가 내렸다."], ["Veal's, and the domestic chaplain to the Right Honourable the Earl of Bareacres was spouting away as usual, a smart carriage drove up to the door decorated with the statue of Athene, and two gentlemen stepped out. The young Masters Bangles rushed to the window with a vague notion that their father might have arrived from Bombay.", "어린 뱅글스 도련님들은 어쩌면 아버지가 봄베이에서 돌아오셨나 하는 막연한 생각에 창가로 달려갔다."], ["The great hulking scholar of three-and-twenty, who was crying secretly over a passage of Eutropius, flattened his neglected nose against the panes and looked at the drag, as the laquais de place sprang from the box and let out the persons in the carriage.", "에우트로피우스의 한 대목을 두고 남몰래 울고 있던 그 우람한 스물세 살 학생은, 마차의 마부석에서 안내인이 뛰어내려 마차 안의 사람들을 내려 주는 사이, 자기의 푸대접받은 코를 유리창에 납작 들이대고 그 사두마차를 바라보았다."]], [["\"It's a fat one and a thin one,\" Mr.", "\"한 명은 뚱뚱하고 한 명은 말랐네.\""], ["Bluck said as a thundering knock came to the door.", "블럭 군이 말했을 때, 문에서 우렁찬 노크 소리가 울렸다."]], [["Everybody was interested, from the domestic chaplain himself, who hoped he saw the fathers of some future pupils, down to Master Georgy, glad of any pretext for laying his book down.", "모두가 흥미를 보였으니, 미래의 문하생들의 아버지를 보는 것이기를 바라던 그 전속 사제 본인부터, 책을 내려놓을 핑계라면 무엇이든 반가웠던 어린 조지 도련님에 이르기까지 다 그러했다."]], [["The boy in the shabby livery with the faded copper buttons, who always thrust himself into the tight coat to open the door, came into the study and said, \"Two gentlemen want to see Master Osborne.\"", "빛바랜 구리 단추가 달린 초라한 제복을 입은 사환 아이가―늘 문을 열어 주려고 그 꽉 끼는 윗도리 속에 제 몸을 욱여넣곤 했다―서재로 들어와 말했다. \"두 신사분이 오스본 도련님을 뵙겠다는데요.\""], ["The professor had had a trifling altercation in the morning with that young gentleman, owing to a difference about the introduction of crackers in school-time; but his face resumed its habitual expression of bland courtesy as he said, \"Master Osborne, I give you full permission to go and see your carriage friends--to whom I beg you to convey the respectful compliments of myself and Mrs.", "교수는 그날 아침 폭죽을 학원에 들여온 일을 두고 의견 차이가 생겨 그 어린 신사와 사소한 언쟁을 벌인 터였으나, 이제 그 얼굴에 평소의 부드럽고 정중한 표정을 되찾으며 말했다. \"오스본 도련님, 마차로 찾아온 자네 벗들을 만나러 가도 좋다고 전적으로 허락하네."], ["Veal.\"", "그분들께 나와 빌 부인의 정중한 안부를 전해 주기 바라네.\""]], [["Georgy went into the reception-room and saw two strangers, whom he looked at with his head up, in his usual haughty manner.", "조지는 응접실로 들어가, 여느 때처럼 거만한 태도로 고개를 빳빳이 든 채 두 낯선 사람을 바라보았다."], ["One was fat, with mustachios, and the other was lean and long, in a blue frock-coat, with a brown face and a grizzled head.", "한 사람은 뚱뚱하고 콧수염을 길렀으며, 다른 한 사람은 마르고 키가 컸는데, 푸른 프록코트 차림에 갈색 얼굴과 희끗희끗한 머리를 하고 있었다."]], [["\"My God, how like he is!\"", "\"맙소사, 어쩌면 이리도 닮았는지!\""], ["said the long gentleman with a start.", "키 큰 신사가 흠칫 놀라며 말했다."], ["\"Can you guess who we are, George?\"", "\"우리가 누구인지 알아맞히겠니, 조지?\""]], [["The boy's face flushed up, as it did usually when he was moved, and his eyes brightened.", "아이의 얼굴이 발갛게 달아올랐다. 마음이 동요할 때면 늘 그러했는데, 두 눈도 밝게 빛났다."], ["\"I don't know the other,\"", "\"다른 분은 모르겠지만,\""], ["he said, \"but I should think you must be Major Dobbin.\"", "그가 말했다. \"이쪽은 도빈 소령님이 틀림없으실 것 같은데요.\""]], [["Indeed it was our old friend.", "과연 그는 우리의 옛 친구였다."], ["His voice trembled with pleasure as he greeted the boy, and taking both the other's hands in his own, drew the lad to him.", "그는 그 아이에게 인사를 건네며 기쁨에 목소리가 떨렸고, 아이의 두 손을 제 두 손에 마주 쥐고는 그 녀석을 제 쪽으로 끌어당겼다."]], [["\"Your mother has talked to you about me--has she?\"", "\"네 어머니가 내 이야기를 너에게 해 주었구나, 그렇지?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"That she has,\"", "\"그럼요.\""], ["Georgy answered, \"hundreds and hundreds of times.\"", "조지가 대답했다. \"수백 번 수천 번도 더요.\""]], [["Eothen\nIt was one of the many causes for personal pride with which old Osborne chose to recreate himself that Sedley, his ancient rival, enemy, and benefactor, was in his last days so utterly defeated and humiliated as to be forced to accept pecuniary obligations at the hands of the man who had most injured and insulted him.", "늙은 오스본이 즐겨 스스로를 위로하던 여러 개인적 자부심의 근거들 가운데 하나는, 그의 오랜 맞수이자 적이며 은인이기도 했던 세들리가 말년에 이르러 어찌나 철저히 패배하고 굴욕을 당했던지, 자기에게 가장 큰 해를 끼치고 모욕을 안긴 바로 그 사내의 손에서 금전적 신세를 지지 않을 수 없게 되었다는 사실이었다."], ["The successful man of the world cursed the old pauper and relieved him from time to time.", "세상에서 성공을 거둔 그 사내는 늙은 빈민을 저주하면서도 때때로 그를 구제해 주었다."], ["As he furnished George with money for his mother, he gave the boy to understand by hints, delivered in his brutal, coarse way, that George's maternal grandfather was but a wretched old bankrupt and dependant, and that John Sedley might thank the man to whom he already owed ever so much money for the aid which his generosity now chose to administer.", "그는 조지에게 어머니에게 전할 돈을 마련해 주면서, 특유의 거칠고 야비한 방식으로 넌지시 흘리는 말로, 조지의 외조부란 그저 비참한 늙은 파산자이자 식객에 불과하며, 존 세들리로 말하자면 이미 그토록 많은 돈을 빚진 사내가 지금 너그러이 베푸는 그 도움에 감사할 줄 알아야 한다는 것을 어린 소년에게 일깨워 주었다."], ["George carried the pompous supplies to his mother and the shattered old widower whom it was now the main business of her life to tend and comfort.", "조지는 그 거드름 어린 돈을 어머니에게, 그리고 이제 그녀의 삶의 가장 중요한 소임이 보살피고 위로하는 일이 되어 버린 그 무너진 늙은 홀아비에게 날라다 주었다."], ["The little fellow patronized the feeble and disappointed old man.", "어린 녀석은 그 쇠약하고 실의에 빠진 노인에게 마치 시혜라도 베풀듯 굴었다."]], [["It may have shown a want of \"proper pride\" in Amelia that she chose to accept these money benefits at the hands of her father's enemy.", "애밀리아가 아버지의 적의 손에서 이런 금전적 혜택을 받아들이기로 한 것은, 어쩌면 \"마땅한 자존심\"이 결여된 처사로 보일지도 모른다."], ["But proper pride and this poor lady had never had much acquaintance together.", "그러나 마땅한 자존심이라는 것과 이 가엾은 부인은 애초에 그리 친분이 두텁지 못했다."], ["A disposition naturally simple and demanding protection; a long course of poverty and humility, of daily privations, and hard words, of kind offices and no returns, had been her lot ever since womanhood almost, or since her luckless marriage with George Osborne.", "본디 단순하여 누군가의 보호를 필요로 하는 천성, 그리고 거의 처녀 시절부터, 아니 조지 오스본과의 그 불운한 결혼 이후로 줄곧 이어진 가난과 굴종, 나날의 결핍과 모진 말, 베풀고도 보답받지 못한 친절의 기나긴 세월이 그녀에게 주어진 몫이었다."], ["You who see your betters bearing up under this shame every day, meekly suffering under the slights of fortune, gentle and unpitied, poor, and rather despised for their poverty, do you ever step down from your prosperity and wash the feet of these poor wearied beggars?", "매일같이 자기보다 나은 이들이 이런 수치를 묵묵히 견디며, 운명의 푸대접 아래 온순하게 시달리고, 가엾이 여겨지지도 못한 채 점잖고, 가난하며, 그 가난 탓에 도리어 멸시받는 모습을 보는 당신은, 과연 자신의 번영에서 한 걸음 내려와 그 가엾고 지친 거지들의 발을 씻겨 준 적이 있는가?"], ["The very thought of them is odious and low.", "그들을 떠올리는 것조차 가증스럽고 천박한 일이렷다."], ["\"There must be classes--there must be rich and poor,\"", "\"계급이라는 건 있어야 마땅하지—부자도 있고 가난한 자도 있어야 하는 법이야.\""], ["Dives says, smacking his claret (it is well if he even sends the broken meat out to Lazarus sitting under the window).", "부자가 클라레를 들이켜며 그렇게 말한다(창문 아래 앉은 나사로에게 먹다 남은 고기 부스러기라도 내보낸다면 그나마 다행이다). 지당한 말씀이다."], ["Very true; but think how mysterious and often unaccountable it is--that lottery of life which gives to this man the purple and fine linen and sends to the other rags for garments and dogs for comforters.", "그러나 생각해 보라. 어떤 이에게는 자줏빛 옷과 고운 아마포를 안기고, 다른 이에게는 누더기를 옷으로, 개를 위로꾼으로 보내는 저 인생의 추첨이라는 것이 얼마나 신비롭고, 또 얼마나 헤아릴 수 없는 것인지를."]], [["So I must own that, without much repining, on the contrary with something akin to gratitude, Amelia took the crumbs that her father-in-law let drop now and then, and with them fed her own parent.", "그러니 나는 인정하지 않을 수 없다. 애밀리아는 별다른 원망 없이, 아니 도리어 감사에 가까운 마음으로, 시아버지가 이따금 떨어뜨려 주는 부스러기를 받아, 그것으로 제 친정아버지를 봉양했던 것이다."], ["Directly she understood it to be her duty, it was this young woman's nature (ladies, she is but thirty still, and we choose to call her a young woman even at that age) it was, I say, her nature to sacrifice herself and to fling all that she had at the feet of the beloved object.", "그것이 자신의 의무임을 깨닫는 순간, 자기 자신을 희생하고 가진 모든 것을 사랑하는 대상의 발치에 내던지는 것이 이 젊은 여인의 천성이었다(숙녀 여러분, 그녀는 아직 서른밖에 되지 않았으니, 우리는 그 나이에도 그녀를 젊은 여인이라 부르기로 한다). 그러니까 그것이 그녀의 천성이었던 것이다."], ["During what long thankless nights had she worked out her fingers for little Georgy whilst at home with her; what buffets, scorns, privations, poverties had she endured for father and mother!", "어린 조지가 집에 함께 있던 시절, 그 아이를 위해 손가락이 닳도록 일하며 보낸 그 길고 보답 없는 밤들은 또 얼마였던가. 아버지와 어머니를 위해 견뎌 낸 그 숱한 매질과 멸시와 결핍과 가난은 또 어떠했던가!"], ["And in the midst of all these solitary resignations and unseen sacrifices, she did not respect herself any more than the world respected her, but I believe thought in her heart that she was a poor-spirited, despicable little creature, whose luck in life was only too good for her merits.", "그리고 이 모든 외로운 체념과 남모를 희생의 한가운데서도, 그녀는 세상이 그녀를 존중하지 않은 만큼이나 자기 자신도 존중하지 않았으니, 도리어 마음속으로는 자신이 기개 없고 비루한 보잘것없는 존재이며, 그 삶의 행운조차 제 분수에는 과분한 것이라고 여겼던 것이리라."], ["O you poor women!", "오, 가엾은 여인들이여!"], ["O you poor secret martyrs and victims, whose life is a torture, who are stretched on racks in your bedrooms, and who lay your heads down on the block daily at the drawing-room table; every man who watches your pains, or peers into those dark places where the torture is administered to you, must pity you--and--and thank God that he has a beard.", "오, 삶 자체가 고문이며, 침실에서는 형틀에 묶이고, 매일같이 응접실 식탁에서 단두대에 머리를 얹는 그대들, 가엾고 은밀한 순교자이자 희생자들이여. 그대들의 고통을 지켜보거나, 그대들에게 그 고문이 가해지는 어두운 곳을 들여다보는 사내라면 누구든 그대들을 가엾이 여기지 않을 수 없으리라—그리고—그리고 제게 수염이 있음을 하느님께 감사하지 않을 수 없으리라."], ["I recollect seeing, years ago, at the prisons for idiots and madmen at Bicetre, near Paris, a poor wretch bent down under the bondage of his imprisonment and his personal infirmity, to whom one of our party gave a halfpenny worth of snuff in a cornet or \"screw\"", "여러 해 전, 파리 인근 비세트르의 백치와 광인을 가두는 감옥에서, 나는 갇힌 몸의 속박과 제 몸의 병약함에 짓눌려 굽은 한 가엾은 자를 본 기억이 있다. 우리 일행 중 한 사람이 그에게 종이를 비비 꼬아 만든 봉지에 반 페니어치의 코담배를 담아 건넸다."], ["of paper. The kindness was too much for the poor epileptic creature.", "그 친절은 그 가엾은 간질 환자에게는 너무도 벅찬 것이었다."], ["He cried in an anguish of delight and gratitude: if anybody gave you and me a thousand a year, or saved our lives, we could not be so affected.", "그는 환희와 감사에 못 이겨 괴로움에 사무친 듯 울었던 것이다. 만약 누군가가 그대나 나에게 연 천 파운드를 주거나 목숨을 구해 준다 한들, 우리는 그토록 감격하지는 못하리라."], ["And so, if you properly tyrannize over a woman, you will find a ha'p'orth of kindness act upon her and bring tears into her eyes, as though you were an angel benefiting her.", "그러니 여자를 제대로 짓밟아 본다면, 반 페니어치의 친절만으로도 그녀의 마음을 움직여, 마치 그대가 그녀에게 은혜를 베푸는 천사라도 되는 양 두 눈에 눈물이 고이게 할 수 있음을 알게 될 것이다."]], [["Some such boons as these were the best which Fortune allotted to poor little Amelia.", "운명이 가엾은 어린 애밀리아에게 허락한 최선의 은혜라는 것이 바로 이런 종류의 것이었다."], ["Her life, begun not unprosperously, had come down to this--to a mean prison and a long, ignoble bondage.", "그리 불운하지 않게 시작된 그녀의 삶이 이 지경에까지 떨어진 것이었다—초라한 감옥과, 길고도 비천한 속박으로."], ["Little George visited her captivity sometimes and consoled it with feeble gleams of encouragement.", "어린 조지가 이따금 갇힌 어미를 찾아와, 희미하게 깜박이는 격려의 빛으로 그 처지를 달래 주었다."], ["Russell Square was the boundary of her prison: she might walk thither occasionally, but was always back to sleep in her cell at night; to perform cheerless duties; to watch by thankless sick-beds; to suffer the harassment and tyranny of querulous disappointed old age.", "러셀 광장이 그녀가 갇힌 감옥의 경계였다. 그녀는 가끔 그쪽으로 걸음을 옮길 수도 있었으나, 밤이면 언제나 제 감방으로 돌아와 잠들고, 즐거움이라곤 없는 의무를 다하고, 보답 없는 병상을 지키고, 투정 부리는 실의에 찬 노년의 시달림과 횡포를 견뎌야 했다."], ["How many thousands of people are there, women for the most part, who are doomed to endure this long slavery?--who are hospital nurses without wages--sisters of Charity, if you like, without the romance and the sentiment of sacrifice--who strive, fast, watch, and suffer, unpitied, and fade away ignobly and unknown.", "이 기나긴 노예살이를 견디도록 운명 지어진 사람들이, 그것도 대부분 여자들이, 도대체 몇천 명이나 될 것인가?—삯도 받지 못하는 병원 간병인들, 굳이 말하자면 희생의 낭만도 감상도 없는 자선 수녀들, 가엾이 여겨지지도 못한 채 애쓰고, 굶고, 지켜보고, 고통받다가, 비천하게 알려지지도 못한 채 시들어 가는 그 여인들이 말이다."]], [["The hidden and awful Wisdom which apportions the destinies of mankind is pleased so to humiliate and cast down the tender, good, and wise, and to set up the selfish, the foolish, or the wicked.", "인류의 운명을 나누어 주는 저 은밀하고 두려운 지혜는, 다정하고 선량하며 지혜로운 이들을 그토록 굴욕에 빠뜨려 내치고, 이기적이고 어리석거나 사악한 이들을 떠받들기를 즐긴다."], ["Oh, be humble, my brother, in your prosperity!", "오, 형제여, 그대의 번영 속에서 부디 겸손하라!"], ["Be gentle with those who are less lucky, if not more deserving.", "그대보다 덜 운이 좋은 이들에게, 비록 더 합당한 자격을 갖추지는 못했더라도 부드럽게 대하라."], ["Think, what right have you to be scornful, whose virtue is a deficiency of temptation, whose success may be a chance, whose rank may be an ancestor's accident, whose prosperity is very likely a satire.", "생각해 보라. 그대의 미덕이라는 것이 그저 유혹이 없었던 탓일 뿐이요, 그대의 성공이라는 것이 어쩌면 한낱 우연일 뿐이며, 그대의 신분이라는 것이 조상이 거머쥔 우연의 산물일 뿐이고, 그대의 번영이라는 것이 십중팔구 하나의 풍자에 지나지 않을진대, 무슨 권리로 그대가 남을 멸시한단 말인가."]], [["They buried Amelia's mother in the churchyard at Brompton, upon just such a rainy, dark day as Amelia recollected when first she had been there to marry George.", "사람들은 애밀리아의 어머니를 브롬프턴 교회 묘지에 묻었다. 그날은 마침, 애밀리아가 조지와 결혼하기 위해 처음 그곳에 갔던 날 기억나던 바로 그런, 비 내리고 어두운 날이었다."], ["Her little boy sat by her side in pompous new sables.", "어린 아들은 으리으리한 새 상복을 입고 그녀 곁에 앉아 있었다."], ["She remembered the old pew-woman and clerk.", "그녀는 그 늙은 좌석 안내 여자와 서기를 떠올렸다."], ["Her thoughts were away in other times as the parson read.", "목사가 성경을 읽는 동안 그녀의 생각은 다른 시절로 멀리 달아나 있었다."], ["But that she held George's hand in her own, perhaps she would have liked to change places with....", "만약 조지의 손을 제 손에 쥐고 있지 않았더라면, 어쩌면 그녀는 그 자리를 바꾸고 싶었을지도…."], ["Then, as usual, she felt ashamed of her selfish thoughts and prayed inwardly to be strengthened to do her duty.", "그러다 그녀는 늘 그렇듯 제 이기적인 생각이 부끄러워져, 의무를 다할 힘을 달라고 속으로 기도했다."]], [["So she determined with all her might and strength to try and make her old father happy.", "그리하여 그녀는 온 힘과 정성을 다해 늙은 아버지를 행복하게 해 드리려 애쓰기로 마음먹었다."], ["She slaved, toiled, patched, and mended, sang and played backgammon, read out the newspaper, cooked dishes for old Sedley, walked him out sedulously into Kensington Gardens or the Brompton Lanes, listened to his stories with untiring smiles and affectionate hypocrisy, or sat musing by his side and communing with her own thoughts and reminiscences, as the old man, feeble and querulous, sunned himself on the garden benches and prattled about his wrongs or his sorrows.", "그녀는 종처럼 일하고, 고되게 애쓰고, 깁고 꿰매고, 노래하고 주사위 놀이를 함께 하고, 신문을 소리 내어 읽어 주고, 늙은 세들리를 위해 요리를 하고, 그를 켄싱턴 정원이나 브롬프턴의 오솔길로 부지런히 데리고 나가 산책시키고, 지칠 줄 모르는 미소와 애정 어린 위선으로 그의 이야기에 귀 기울이거나, 쇠약하고 투정 많은 노인이 정원 벤치에서 볕을 쬐며 제 억울함이나 슬픔을 늘어놓는 동안 그 곁에 앉아 생각에 잠긴 채 자신의 상념과 추억과 마주하곤 했다. 이 과부의 생각이란 얼마나 서글프고 채워지지 않는 것이었던지!"], ["What sad, unsatisfactory thoughts those of the widow were!", "정원의 비탈과 넓은 길을 오르내리며 뛰노는 아이들은 그녀에게서 빼앗겨 간 조지를 떠올리게 했다."], ["The children running up and down the slopes and broad paths in the gardens reminded her of George, who was taken from her; the first George was taken from her; her selfish, guilty love, in both instances, had been rebuked and bitterly chastised.", "첫 번째 조지도 그녀에게서 빼앗겨 갔던 것이다. 두 경우 모두 그녀의 이기적이고 죄 많은 사랑은 질책당했고 혹독하게 징벌당했다."], ["She strove to think it was right that she should be so punished.", "그녀는 자신이 그토록 벌받는 것이 마땅하다고 생각하려 애썼다."], ["She was such a miserable wicked sinner.", "자신은 그토록 비참하고 사악한 죄인이라고."], ["She was quite alone in the world.", "자신은 이 세상에 철저히 홀로라고."]], [["I know that the account of this kind of solitary imprisonment is insufferably tedious, unless there is some cheerful or humorous incident to enliven it--a tender gaoler, for instance, or a waggish commandant of the fortress, or a mouse to come out and play about Latude's beard and whiskers, or a subterranean passage under the castle, dug by Trenck with his nails and a toothpick: the historian has no such enlivening incident to relate in the narrative of Amelia's captivity. Fancy her, if you please, during this period, very sad, but always ready to smile when spoken to; in a very mean, poor, not to say vulgar position of life; singing songs, making puddings, playing cards, mending stockings, for her old father's benefit. So, never mind, whether she be a heroine or no; or you and I, however old, scolding, and bankrupt--may we have in our last days a kind soft shoulder on which to lean and a gentle hand to soothe our gouty old pillows.", "나도 안다. 이런 종류의 외로운 유폐 이야기란, 그것을 활기차게 만들어 줄 명랑하거나 익살스러운 사건—이를테면 마음 따뜻한 간수라든가, 익살스러운 요새 사령관이라든가, 라튀드의 턱수염과 구레나룻 둘레를 기어 나와 노니는 생쥐라든가, 트렌크가 손톱과 이쑤시개로 파낸 성 밑의 지하 통로 같은 것—이 곁들여지지 않는 한, 견딜 수 없이 지루하다는 것을. 그러나 역사가에게는 애밀리아의 유폐를 서술하면서 들려줄 그런 흥겨운 사건이 하나도 없다. 부디 상상해 보시라. 이 시기 동안 그녀가 몹시 슬프면서도, 누가 말을 걸면 언제나 기꺼이 미소 지을 채비가 되어 있고, 천박하다고까지는 말하지 않더라도 몹시 초라하고 가난한 처지에서, 늙은 아버지를 위해 노래를 부르고, 푸딩을 만들고, 카드놀이를 하고, 양말을 깁고 있는 모습을. 그러니 그녀가 여주인공이든 아니든 마음 쓸 것 없다. 또한 그대나 나도, 아무리 늙고 잔소리 심하고 파산했다 한들—부디 우리의 말년에, 기댈 수 있는 부드럽고 다정한 어깨 하나와, 통풍으로 쑤시는 늙은 베개를 어루만져 줄 너그러운 손길 하나만은 있기를."]], [["Old Sedley grew very fond of his daughter after his wife's death, and Amelia had her consolation in doing her duty by the old man.", "늙은 세들리는 아내가 죽은 뒤로 딸을 무척 아끼게 되었고, 애밀리아는 그 노인에게 자신의 의무를 다하는 데서 위안을 얻었다."]], [["But we are not going to leave these two people long in such a low and ungenteel station of life.", "그러나 우리는 이 두 사람을 이토록 비천하고 품위 없는 처지에 오래 내버려 둘 생각은 없다."], ["Better days, as far as worldly prosperity went, were in store for both.", "세속적인 번영으로 말하자면, 두 사람 모두에게 더 나은 날들이 예비되어 있었다."], ["Perhaps the ingenious reader has guessed who was the stout gentleman who called upon Georgy at his school in company with our old friend Major Dobbin.", "어쩌면 눈치 빠른 독자라면, 우리의 오랜 벗 도빈 소령과 함께 조지를 학교로 찾아온 그 뚱뚱한 신사가 누구였는지 이미 짐작했을지도 모른다."], ["It was another old acquaintance returned to England, and at a time when his presence was likely to be of great comfort to his relatives there.", "그는 영국으로 돌아온 또 한 사람의 옛 지인이었으며, 그것도 그가 나타난 것이 그곳에 있던 친척들에게 큰 위안이 될 만한 시기에 돌아온 것이었다."]], [["Major Dobbin having easily succeeded in getting leave from his good-natured commandant to proceed to Madras, and thence probably to Europe, on urgent private affairs, never ceased travelling night and day until he reached his journey's end, and had directed his march with such celerity that he arrived at Madras in a high fever.", "도빈 소령은 마음씨 좋은 사령관에게서 긴급한 사적인 용무로 마드라스로, 그리고 거기서 다시 아마도 유럽으로 떠나도 좋다는 허락을 어렵잖이 얻어 내고는, 목적지에 다다를 때까지 밤낮으로 쉼 없이 여행했다."], ["His servants who accompanied him brought him to the house of the friend with whom he had resolved to stay until his departure for Europe in a state of delirium; and it was thought for many, many days that he would never travel farther than the burying-ground of the church of St.", "어찌나 급히 행로를 재촉했던지 마드라스에 도착했을 때는 고열에 시달리고 있었다. 그를 수행한 하인들은 그를, 유럽으로 떠날 때까지 머물기로 마음먹었던 친구의 집으로 인사불성의 상태로 데려왔다."], ["George's, where the troops should fire a salvo over his grave, and where many a gallant officer lies far away from his home.", "여러 날 동안 사람들은, 그가 성 조지 교회의 묘지보다 더 멀리는 결코 가지 못하리라고, 그곳에서 군대가 그의 무덤 위로 조총을 쏘아 올리고, 수많은 용맹한 장교들이 고향에서 멀리 떨어진 곳에 누워 있는 바로 그 자리에 그도 묻히게 되리라고 여겼다."]], [["Here, as the poor fellow lay tossing in his fever, the people who watched him might have heard him raving about Amelia.", "그 가엾은 사내가 고열에 몸부림치며 누워 있는 동안, 그를 지켜보던 사람들은 그가 애밀리아의 이름을 부르며 헛소리하는 것을 들었을 법하다."], ["The idea that he should never see her again depressed him in his lucid hours.", "그녀를 다시는 보지 못하리라는 생각이, 정신이 맑은 순간이면 그를 침울하게 했다."], ["He thought his last day was come, and he made his solemn preparations for departure, setting his affairs in this world in order and leaving the little property of which he was possessed to those whom he most desired to benefit.", "그는 자신의 마지막 날이 왔다고 여기고, 엄숙하게 떠날 채비를 갖추어, 이 세상에서의 신변을 정리하고, 자신이 가진 보잘것없는 재산을 가장 혜택을 베풀고 싶은 이들에게 남겼다. 그가 머물던 집의 친구가 그 유언의 증인이 되어 주었다."], ["The friend in whose house he was located witnessed his testament.", "그는 자신이 목에 두르고 다니던 작은 갈색 머리카락 사슬과 함께 묻히기를 바랐는데, 굳이 진실을 밝히자면, 그것은 그가 브뤼셀에서 애밀리아의 하녀에게서 얻은 것이었다."], ["He desired to be buried with a little brown hair-chain which he wore round his neck and which, if the truth must be known, he had got from Amelia's maid at Brussels, when the young widow's hair was cut off, during the fever which prostrated her after the death of George Osborne on the plateau at Mount St. John.", "젊은 과부가 성 장 산 고원에서 조지 오스본이 전사한 뒤 그 충격으로 앓아누웠을 때, 열병을 앓는 동안 잘라 낸 그녀의 머리카락이었던 것이다."]], [["He recovered, rallied, relapsed again, having undergone such a process of blood-letting and calomel as showed the strength of his original constitution.", "그는 차도를 보였다가, 기력을 회복했다가, 다시 도졌으니, 그 과정에서 받은 사혈과 감홍 투약이 어찌나 혹독했던지 그의 타고난 체질의 강건함이 도리어 드러날 지경이었다."], ["He was almost a skeleton when they put him on board the Ramchunder East Indiaman, Captain Bragg, from Calcutta, touching at Madras, and so weak and prostrate that his friend who had tended him through his illness prophesied that the honest Major would never survive the voyage, and that he would pass some morning, shrouded in flag and hammock, over the ship's side, and carrying down to the sea with him the relic that he wore at his heart.", "캘커타에서 출발해 마드라스에 기항한 동인도 회사 선박 람춘더호—브래그 선장의 배였다—에 그를 태웠을 때, 그는 거의 해골과 다름없었고, 어찌나 쇠약하고 기진해 있던지, 병중 내내 그를 보살펴 준 친구는 이 정직한 소령이 항해에서 결코 살아남지 못하리라고, 어느 날 아침이면 깃발과 해먹에 싸인 채 뱃전 너머로 던져져, 가슴에 품고 있던 그 유품과 함께 바닷속으로 가라앉으리라고 예언했다."], ["But whether it was the sea air, or the hope which sprung up in him afresh, from the day that the ship spread her canvas and stood out of the roads towards home, our friend began to amend, and he was quite well (though as gaunt as a greyhound) before they reached the Cape.", "그러나 바닷바람 덕분이었는지, 아니면 배가 돛을 펴고 정박지를 벗어나 고향을 향해 나아가던 날부터 그의 안에서 새로이 솟아오른 희망 덕분이었는지, 우리의 벗은 차도를 보이기 시작하더니, 희망봉에 닿기도 전에 (비록 사냥개처럼 비쩍 야위긴 했으나) 완전히 건강을 되찾았다."], ["\"Kirk will be disappointed of his majority this time,\"", "\"이번에는 커크가 소령 진급에서 물 먹게 생겼군.\" 그가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["he said with a smile; \"he will expect to find himself gazetted by the time the regiment reaches home.\"", "\"연대가 본국에 도착할 즈음이면 자기 진급이 관보에 실리리라 잔뜩 기대하고 있을 텐데 말이야.\" 사정인즉슨 이러했다."], ["For it must be premised that while the Major was lying ill at Madras, having made such prodigious haste to go thither, the gallant --th, which had passed many years abroad, which after its return from the West Indies had been baulked of its stay at home by the Waterloo campaign, and had been ordered from Flanders to India, had received orders home; and the Major might have accompanied his comrades, had he chosen to wait for their arrival at Madras.", "소령이 그토록 엄청난 속도로 마드라스로 달려갔다가 그곳에서 병들어 누워 있는 동안, 여러 해 해외에서 복무했던 그 용맹한 ××연대—서인도제도에서 돌아온 뒤로 워털루 전역 때문에 본국에 머물 기회를 빼앗기고, 플랑드르에서 인도로 파견되었던 그 연대가 본국 귀환 명령을 받았던 것이다. 그러니 만약 소령이 동료들이 마드라스에 도착하기를 기다리기로 했더라면, 그들과 함께 돌아갈 수도 있었을 터였다."]], [["Perhaps he was not inclined to put himself in his exhausted state again under the guardianship of Glorvina.", "어쩌면 그는 기진맥진한 처지에 다시 글로비나의 보호 아래 자신을 맡기고 싶지 않았는지도 모른다. \"오다우드 양이 배에 함께 탔더라면 나는 끝장났을 거요.\""], ["\"I think Miss O'Dowd would have done for me,\"", "그가 동승객에게 웃으며 말했다."], ["he said laughingly to a fellow-passenger, \"if we had had her on board, and when she had sunk me, she would have fallen upon you, depend upon it, and carried you in as a prize to Southampton, Jos, my boy.\"", "\"그리고 나를 침몰시키고 나면, 그다음엔 분명히 당신을 덮쳤을 거요, 틀림없이. 그러고는 당신을 전리품 삼아 사우샘프턴까지 끌고 갔을 테지, 조스, 이 사람아.\""]], [["For indeed it was no other than our stout friend who was also a passenger on board the Ramchunder.", "실로 그 동승객이란 바로 다름 아닌, 람춘더호에 같이 타고 있던 우리의 뚱뚱한 벗이었던 것이다."], ["He had passed ten years in Bengal.", "그는 십 년 세월을 벵골에서 보냈다."], ["Constant dinners, tiffins, pale ale and claret, the prodigious labour of cutcherry, and the refreshment of brandy-pawnee which he was forced to take there, had their effect upon Waterloo Sedley.", "끊임없이 이어지는 만찬과 점심 연회, 페일에일과 클라레, 법정 사무의 엄청난 노고, 그리고 그곳에서 어쩔 수 없이 들이켜야 했던 브랜디 물의 자양분이 워털루의 영웅 세들리에게 그 나름의 영향을 미쳤다."], ["A voyage to Europe was pronounced necessary for him--and having served his full time in India and had fine appointments which had enabled him to lay by a considerable sum of money, he was free to come home and stay with a good pension, or to return and resume that rank in the service to which his seniority and his vast talents entitled him.", "그에게는 유럽으로의 항해가 필요하다는 진단이 내려졌다. 인도에서 복무 기간을 꽉 채웠고, 상당한 액수의 돈을 모을 수 있게 해 준 좋은 직책들을 거친 덕분에, 그는 후한 연금을 받으며 본국에 돌아와 머물 수도 있었고, 아니면 다시 돌아가 연공과 비범한 재능에 걸맞은 그 직위를 이어받을 수도 있는 자유로운 몸이었다."]], [["He was rather thinner than when we last saw him, but had gained in majesty and solemnity of demeanour.", "그는 우리가 마지막으로 보았을 때보다 다소 야위었으나, 거동의 위엄과 근엄함만은 더 늘어 있었다."], ["He had resumed the mustachios to which his services at Waterloo entitled him, and swaggered about on deck in a magnificent velvet cap with a gold band and a profuse ornamentation of pins and jewellery about his person.", "그는 워털루에서의 공로로 기를 자격을 얻은 콧수염을 다시 길렀고, 금띠를 두른 화려한 벨벳 모자를 쓴 채, 온몸에 핀과 보석붙이를 잔뜩 치장하고서 갑판 위를 거들먹거리며 돌아다녔다."], ["He took breakfast in his cabin and dressed as solemnly to appear on the quarter-deck as if he were going to turn out for Bond Street, or the Course at Calcutta.", "그는 선실에서 아침을 들었고, 마치 본드 거리나 캘커타의 경마장에라도 나서려는 양 근엄하게 차려입고서 후갑판에 모습을 드러냈다."], ["He brought a native servant with him, who was his valet and pipe-bearer and who wore the Sedley crest in silver on his turban.", "그는 원주민 하인 하나를 데려왔는데, 그자는 시종 겸 담뱃대 담당으로, 터번에 세들리 가문의 문장을 은빛으로 달고 있었다."], ["That oriental menial had a wretched life under the tyranny of Jos Sedley.", "그 동방의 하인은 조스 세들리의 횡포 아래 비참한 삶을 살았다."], ["Jos was as vain of his person as a woman, and took as long a time at his toilette as any fading beauty.", "조스는 여자처럼 제 외모에 허영을 부렸고, 한물간 미인 못지않게 몸치장에 긴 시간을 들였다."], ["The youngsters among the passengers, Young Chaffers of the 150th, and poor little Ricketts, coming home after his third fever, used to draw out Sedley at the cuddy-table and make him tell prodigious stories about himself and his exploits against tigers and Napoleon.", "승객 중 젊은 축들, 150연대의 젊은 채퍼스와, 세 번째 열병을 앓고서 귀향길에 오른 가엾은 어린 리케츠는 식당 식탁에서 곧잘 세들리의 말문을 트게 하여, 그가 자기 자신과 호랑이 및 나폴레옹을 상대로 한 무용담에 대해 어마어마한 이야기를 늘어놓게 만들곤 했다."], ["He was great when he visited the Emperor's tomb at Longwood, when to these gentlemen and the young officers of the ship, Major Dobbin not being by, he described the whole battle of Waterloo and all but announced that Napoleon never would have gone to Saint Helena at all but for him, Jos Sedley.", "그는 롱우드에 있는 황제의 무덤을 찾았을 때 한껏 거창해졌으니, 도빈 소령이 곁에 없을 때면 이 신사들과 배의 젊은 장교들에게 워털루 전투의 전모를 묘사하며, 자기 조스 세들리가 아니었더라면 나폴레옹은 애당초 세인트헬레나에 가지도 않았으리라고 거의 떠벌리다시피 했다."]], [["After leaving St. Helena he became very generous, disposing of a great quantity of ship stores, claret, preserved meats, and great casks packed with soda-water, brought out for his private delectation.", "세인트헬레나를 떠난 뒤로 그는 무척 후해져서, 자신의 개인적 향락을 위해 가져왔던 엄청난 양의 선상 비축품, 클라레, 저장 고기, 소다수를 가득 채운 커다란 통들을 아낌없이 나누어 주었다."], ["There were no ladies on board; the Major gave the pas of precedency to the civilian, so that he was the first dignitary at table, and treated by Captain Bragg and the officers of the Ramchunder with the respect which his rank warranted.", "배에는 숙녀가 한 명도 없었다. 소령이 민간인에게 우선권을 양보한 터라, 그는 식탁에서 가장 높은 자리에 앉는 인물이 되었고, 브래그 선장과 람춘더호의 장교들에게서 그 지위에 걸맞은 대우를 받았다."], ["He disappeared rather in a panic during a two-days' gale, in which he had the portholes of his cabin battened down, and remained in his cot reading the Washerwoman of Finchley Common, left on board the Ramchunder by the Right Honourable the Lady Emily Hornblower, wife of the Rev.", "그는 이틀간 이어진 폭풍 동안 다소 공황에 빠져 자취를 감추었으니, 선실의 현창들을 단단히 틀어막게 하고는 침상에 누워 「핀칠리 공유지의 빨래하는 여인」을 읽었다."], ["Silas Hornblower, when on their passage out to the Cape, where the Reverend gentleman was a missionary; but, for common reading, he had brought a stock of novels and plays which he lent to the rest of the ship, and rendered himself agreeable to all by his kindness and condescension.", "그 책은 사일러스 혼블로어 목사의 부인이자 명예로운 에밀리 혼블로어 부인이, 그 목사가 선교사로 일하게 될 희망봉으로 가는 항해 중에 람춘더호에 남기고 간 것이었다. 그러나 평소의 읽을거리로는 소설과 희곡을 잔뜩 가져와 배 안의 다른 이들에게 빌려주었고, 그 친절과 너그러운 겸양으로 모두에게 호감을 샀다."]], [["Many and many a night as the ship was cutting through the roaring dark sea, the moon and stars shining overhead and the bell singing out the watch, Mr.", "밤이면 밤마다, 배가 으르렁대는 어두운 바다를 가르고 나아가고, 머리 위로는 달과 별이 빛나며, 종이 당직 시각을 알리는 동안, 세들리 씨와 소령은 배의 후갑판에 앉아 고향 이야기를 나누곤 했다."], ["Sedley and the Major would sit on the quarter-deck of the vessel talking about home, as the Major smoked his cheroot and the civilian puffed at the hookah which his servant prepared for him.", "소령은 엽궐련을 피웠고, 민간인은 하인이 준비해 준 물담배를 뻐끔거렸다."]], [["In these conversations it was wonderful with what perseverance and ingenuity Major Dobbin would manage to bring the talk round to the subject of Amelia and her little boy.", "이런 대화 중에 도빈 소령이 어쩌면 그토록 끈질기고 교묘하게 이야기를 애밀리아와 그녀의 어린 아들 쪽으로 끌고 갈 수 있었는지는 참으로 놀라운 일이었다. 조스는 제 아버지의 불행과, 자기에게 무람없이 손을 벌리는 행태에 다소 부아가 나 있었으나, 노인의 불운과 노령을 짚어 주는 소령의 말에 누그러졌다."], ["Jos, a little testy about his father's misfortunes and unceremonious applications to him, was soothed down by the Major, who pointed out the elder's ill fortunes and old age.", "어쩌면 조스로서는 그 노부부와 함께 사는 것이 내키지 않을 수도 있었으니, 다른 부류의 교제에 익숙한 젊은이에게 그들의 생활 방식이나 생활 시간이 맞지 않을 수도 있었기 때문이다(조스는 이 찬사에 고개를 까딱였다)."], ["He would not perhaps like to live with the old couple, whose ways and hours might not agree with those of a younger man, accustomed to different society (Jos bowed at this compliment); but, the Major pointed out, how advantageous it would be for Jos Sedley to have a house of his own in London, and not a mere bachelor's establishment as before; how his sister Amelia would be the very person to preside over it; how elegant, how gentle she was, and of what refined good manners.", "그러나 소령은, 조스 세들리가 예전처럼 한낱 독신 남자의 살림이 아니라, 런던에 자기 소유의 집을 갖는 것이 얼마나 이로운 일인지, 그 집을 다스릴 적임자로 누이 애밀리아만 한 사람이 어디 있는지, 그녀가 얼마나 우아하고 온화하며 또 얼마나 세련되고 고상한 몸가짐을 지녔는지를 짚어 주었다. 그는 조지 오스본 부인이 지난날 브뤼셀에서, 그리고 런던에서 거둔 성공담을 들려주었으니, 그곳에서 그녀가 대단히 지체 높은 멋쟁이들에게서 많은 흠모를 받았다는 것이었다."], ["He recounted stories of the success which Mrs. George Osborne had had in former days at Brussels, and in London, where she was much admired by people of very great fashion; and he then hinted how becoming it would be for Jos to send Georgy to a good school and make a man of him, for his mother and her parents would be sure to spoil him.", "그러고는 조지를 좋은 학교에 보내 사내대장부로 키우는 것이 조스에게 얼마나 어울리는 일인지를 넌지시 일렀으니, 어미와 그 외조부모로 말하자면 틀림없이 아이를 응석받이로 망쳐 놓을 것이기 때문이었다."], ["In a word, this artful Major made the civilian promise to take charge of Amelia and her unprotected child.", "한마디로, 이 교활한 소령은 민간인에게서 애밀리아와 그 의지가지없는 아이를 돌보겠다는 약속을 받아 냈던 것이다."], ["He did not know as yet what events had happened in the little Sedley family, and how death had removed the mother, and riches had carried off George from Amelia.", "그는 그 작은 세들리 일가에 어떤 일들이 벌어졌는지, 또 어떻게 죽음이 어머니를 데려갔고 부유함이 조지를 애밀리아에게서 앗아 갔는지는 아직 알지 못했다."], ["But the fact is that every day and always, this love-smitten and middle-aged gentleman was thinking about Mrs.", "그러나 사실인즉, 사랑에 빠진 이 중년의 신사는 날마다, 그리고 언제나 오스본 부인을 생각하고 있었고, 그의 온 마음은 그녀에게 좋은 일을 해 주는 데 쏠려 있었던 것이다."], ["Osborne, and his whole heart was bent upon doing her good.", "그는 조스 세들리를 어르고, 구슬리고, 달래고, 추켜세웠으니, 그 끈기와 정성은 어쩌면 본인조차 미처 자각하지 못할 정도였다."], ["He coaxed, wheedled, cajoled, and complimented Jos Sedley with a perseverance and cordiality of which he was not aware himself, very likely; but some men who have unmarried sisters or daughters even, may remember how uncommonly agreeable gentlemen are to the male relations when they are courting the females; and perhaps this rogue of a Dobbin was urged by a similar hypocrisy.", "그러나 미혼의 누이나 심지어 딸을 둔 어떤 사내들이라면, 여자에게 구애하는 신사가 그 남자 친척들에게는 얼마나 유난히도 상냥하게 구는지를 기억할 것이다. 그러니 이 능청맞은 도빈 또한 어쩌면 그와 비슷한 위선에 떠밀린 것이었으리라."]], [["The truth is, when Major Dobbin came on board the Ramchumder, very sick, and for the three days she lay in the Madras Roads, he did not begin to rally, nor did even the appearance and recognition of his old acquaintance, Mr.", "진실인즉 이러했다. 도빈 소령이 몹시 앓는 몸으로 람춘더호에 올랐을 때, 그리고 배가 마드라스 정박지에 머물던 사흘 동안, 그는 차도를 보이지 않았고, 옛 지인인 세들리 씨가 배에 타고 있는 것을 보고 알아본 것조차 그를 그리 기운 나게 하지 못했다."], ["Sedley, on board much cheer him, until after a conversation which they had one day, as the Major was laid languidly on the deck.", "어느 날, 소령이 갑판 위에 맥없이 누워 있을 때 두 사람이 나눈 대화가 있고 나서야 비로소 달라졌던 것이다. 그때 그는 자신의 운이 다한 것 같다고 말했다."], ["He said then he thought he was doomed; he had left a little something to his godson in his will, and he trusted Mrs.", "유언으로 대자에게 약간의 것을 남겼고, 오스본 부인이 자기를 다정히 기억해 주기를, 그리고 그녀가 곧 맺으려는 결혼에서 행복하기를 바란다고 했다."], ["Osborne would remember him kindly and be happy in the marriage she was about to make.", "\"결혼이라니?"], ["\"Married?", "천만에.\""], ["not the least,\"", "조스가 대답했다."], ["Jos answered; \"he had heard from her: she made no mention of the marriage, and by the way, it was curious, she wrote to say that Major Dobbin was going to be married, and hoped that HE would be happy.\"", "\"나는 그 애한테서 소식을 들었는데, 결혼 얘기는 한마디도 없었어. 그리고 말이 나왔으니 말인데, 참 묘하지, 그 애가 편지에 쓰기를 도빈 소령이 결혼할 거라며, 그가 행복하기를 바란다고 했단 말이지.\""], ["What were the dates of Sedley's letters from Europe?", "세들리가 유럽에서 받은 편지들의 날짜는 언제였던가?"], ["The civilian fetched them.", "민간인이 그것들을 가져왔다."], ["They were two months later than the Major's; and the ship's surgeon congratulated himself upon the treatment adopted by him towards his new patient, who had been consigned to shipboard by the Madras practitioner with very small hopes indeed; for, from that day, the very day that he changed the draught, Major Dobbin began to mend.", "그 편지들은 소령이 받은 것보다 두 달이나 뒤의 것이었다. 그러자 배의 군의관은, 마드라스의 의사가 거의 희망이 없다며 선상으로 떠넘기다시피 한 새 환자에게 자신이 취한 치료법을 두고 흐뭇해했다."], ["And thus it was that deserving officer, Captain Kirk, was disappointed of his majority.", "왜냐하면 그날부터, 바로 그가 물약을 바꾼 그날부터 도빈 소령이 차도를 보이기 시작했기 때문이다. 그리하여 그 자격 있는 장교 커크 대위는 소령 진급에서 물을 먹게 되었던 것이다."]], [["After they passed St. Helena, Major Dobbin's gaiety and strength was such as to astonish all his fellow passengers.", "세인트헬레나를 지난 뒤로 도빈 소령의 쾌활함과 기력은 동승객 모두를 놀라게 할 정도였다."], ["He larked with the midshipmen, played single-stick with the mates, ran up the shrouds like a boy, sang a comic song one night to the amusement of the whole party assembled over their grog after supper, and rendered himself so gay, lively, and amiable that even Captain Bragg, who thought there was nothing in his passenger, and considered he was a poor-spirited feller at first, was constrained to own that the Major was a reserved but well-informed and meritorious officer.", "그는 사관후보생들과 장난을 치고, 항해사들과 막대기 검술을 겨루고, 소년처럼 삭구를 타고 오르고, 어느 날 밤에는 저녁 식사 후 그로그주를 들고 모여든 일행 전부를 즐겁게 하려고 익살스러운 노래를 부르는가 하면, 어찌나 명랑하고 활기차고 상냥하게 굴었던지, 처음에는 자기 승객에게서 별것 없다고 여기며 기개 없는 사내라고 생각했던 브래그 선장조차 그 소령이 말수는 적으나 박식하고 훌륭한 장교임을 인정하지 않을 수 없었다. \"그자한텐 '세련된'"], ["\"He ain't got distangy manners, dammy,\"", "매너라곤 없단 말이지, 젠장.\" 브래그가 일등 항해사에게 말했다."], ["Bragg observed to his first mate; \"he wouldn't do at Government House, Roper, where his Lordship and Lady William was as kind to me, and shook hands with me before the whole company, and asking me at dinner to take beer with him, before the Commander-in-Chief himself; he ain't got manners, but there's something about him--\"", "\"총독 관저에서는 어림도 없을 거요, 로퍼. 거기서 각하랑 윌리엄 부인은 나한테 어찌나 친절하던지, 좌중 전부가 보는 앞에서 나랑 악수를 하고, 총사령관 본인이 보는 앞에서 나더러 만찬에서 맥주를 같이 들자고까지 했단 말이오."], ["And thus Captain Bragg showed that he possessed discrimination as a man, as well as ability as a commander.", "그자한텐 매너는 없지만, 뭔가가 있긴 해—\" 이렇게 브래그 선장은 자신이 지휘관으로서의 능력뿐 아니라 사람을 보는 안목까지 갖추고 있음을 보여 주었다."]], [["But a calm taking place when the Ramchunder was within ten days' sail of England, Dobbin became so impatient and ill-humoured as to surprise those comrades who had before admired his vivacity and good temper.", "그러나 람춘더호가 영국까지 열흘 항해 거리에 들었을 때 바다가 잔잔해지자, 도빈은 어찌나 안달하며 언짢아하던지, 전에는 그의 활기와 좋은 성정에 감탄했던 동료들마저 놀라게 했다."], ["He did not recover until the breeze sprang up again, and was in a highly excited state when the pilot came on board.", "그는 다시 미풍이 불어올 때까지 평정을 되찾지 못했고, 수로 안내인이 배에 올랐을 때는 몹시 흥분한 상태였다."], ["Good God, how his heart beat as the two friendly spires of Southampton came in sight.", "맙소사, 정겨운 사우샘프턴의 두 첨탑이 시야에 들어왔을 때 그의 심장은 어찌나 두근거렸던가."]], [["Our Friend the Major\nOur Major had rendered himself so popular on board the Ramchunder that when he and Mr. Sedley descended into the welcome shore-boat which was to take them from the ship, the whole crew, men and officers, the great Captain Bragg himself leading off, gave three cheers for Major Dobbin, who blushed very much and ducked his head in token of thanks.", "우리의 소령은 램천더 호 선상에서 어찌나 사람들의 사랑을 받았던지, 그와 세들리 씨가 배에서 내려 자기들을 뭍으로 데려다줄 반가운 거룻배에 올라타자 선원이며 사관이며 할 것 없이 온 승무원이, 위대한 브래그 선장이 손수 앞장서서 도빈 소령을 위해 세 번 만세를 불렀고, 소령은 몹시 얼굴을 붉히며 고개를 숙여 감사의 뜻을 표했다."], ["Jos, who very likely thought the cheers were for himself, took off his gold-laced cap and waved it majestically to his friends, and they were pulled to shore and landed with great dignity at the pier, whence they proceeded to the Royal George Hotel.", "그 만세가 십중팔구 제 몫이라 여겼을 조스는 금몰을 두른 모자를 벗어 들고 위풍당당하게 친구들을 향해 흔들어 보였으며, 그렇게 두 사람은 뭍으로 끌려가 더없이 위엄 있게 부두에 내려선 다음, 그곳에서 로열조지 호텔로 향했다."]], [["Although the sight of that magnificent round of beef, and the silver tankard suggestive of real British home-brewed ale and porter, which perennially greet the eyes of the traveller returning from foreign parts who enters the coffee-room of the George, are so invigorating and delightful that a man entering such a comfortable snug homely English inn might well like to stop some days there, yet Dobbin began to talk about a post-chaise instantly, and was no sooner at Southampton than he wished to be on the road to London.", "조지 호텔의 커피룸에 들어서는, 이국땅에서 돌아온 여행객의 눈을 사철 맞이하는 저 으리으리한 통구이 쇠고기 한 덩이며, 진짜 영국산 가양주 에일과 포터를 떠올리게 하는 은빛 술잔의 광경이란, 너무도 기운을 북돋우고 흐뭇하여, 이토록 아늑하고 푸근하고 정겨운 영국식 여관에 들어선 사람이라면 며칠쯤 거기 머물고 싶어 할 법도 하건만, 도빈은 곧장 역마차 이야기를 꺼냈고, 사우샘프턴에 도착하기가 무섭게 런던으로 가는 길에 오르고 싶어 했다."], ["Jos, however, would not hear of moving that evening.", "그러나 조스는 그날 저녁에 움직인다는 말은 들으려고도 하지 않았다."], ["Why was he to pass a night in a post-chaise instead of a great large undulating downy feather-bed which was there ready to replace the horrid little narrow crib in which the portly Bengal gentleman had been confined during the voyage?", "어찌하여 그가, 항해 내내 저 풍채 좋은 벵골 신사를 가둬 두었던 끔찍하게 비좁은 침상을 대신해 줄, 거기 떡하니 마련된 크고 넓은 깃털 침대의 출렁이는 보드라움을 마다하고, 역마차 안에서 밤을 지새워야 한단 말인가?"], ["He could not think of moving till his baggage was cleared, or of travelling until he could do so with his chillum.", "짐이 통관을 마치기 전에는 움직일 생각이 없었고, 물담뱃대를 곁에 두고 갈 수 있게 되기 전에는 길을 떠날 생각도 없었다."], ["So the Major was forced to wait over that night, and dispatched a letter to his family announcing his arrival, entreating from Jos a promise to write to his own friends.", "그리하여 소령은 하는 수 없이 그날 밤을 묵어 가야 했고, 자기 가족에게 도착을 알리는 편지를 부치면서, 조스에게도 제 친지들에게 편지하겠다는 약속을 받아 냈다."], ["Jos promised, but didn't keep his promise.", "조스는 약속했으나 그 약속을 지키지 않았다."], ["The Captain, the surgeon, and one or two passengers came and dined with our two gentlemen at the inn, Jos exerting himself in a sumptuous way in ordering the dinner and promising to go to town the next day with the Major.", "선장과 선의, 그리고 한두 명의 승객이 여관으로 와서 우리 두 신사와 함께 저녁을 들었는데, 조스는 호화롭게 저녁상을 차리느라 한껏 솜씨를 부렸고 이튿날 소령과 함께 도시로 가겠노라 약속했다."], ["The landlord said it did his eyes good to see Mr. Sedley take off his first pint of porter.", "여관 주인은 세들리 씨가 첫 잔의 포터를 비우는 모습을 보니 눈이 다 시원해진다고 말했다."], ["If I had time and dared to enter into digressions, I would write a chapter about that first pint of porter drunk upon English ground.", "내게 시간이 있고 또 곁길로 새는 일을 무릅쓸 용기가 있다면, 영국 땅에서 들이켜는 그 첫 잔의 포터에 관해 한 장을 통째로 쓰고 싶을 지경이다."], ["Ah, how good it is!", "아, 그것이 얼마나 좋은지!"], ["It is worth-while to leave home for a year, just to enjoy that one draught.", "그 한 모금을 즐기기 위해서라도 일 년쯤 집을 떠나 있을 만한 가치가 있는 것이다."]], [["Major Dobbin made his appearance the next morning very neatly shaved and dressed, according to his wont.", "도빈 소령은 이튿날 아침 여느 때 버릇대로 말끔히 면도하고 옷을 차려입은 채 모습을 드러냈다."], ["Indeed, it was so early in the morning that nobody was up in the house except that wonderful Boots of an inn who never seems to want sleep; and the Major could hear the snores of the various inmates of the house roaring through the corridors as he creaked about in those dim passages.", "사실 너무도 이른 아침이라, 집 안에 일어나 있는 사람이라고는 도무지 잠이라곤 필요 없어 보이는 저 신통한 여관 구두닦이뿐이었다. 소령은 그 어둑한 복도를 삐걱대며 돌아다니는 동안, 집 안 여기저기 묵는 손님들의 코 고는 소리가 복도를 따라 우렁차게 울려 퍼지는 것을 들을 수 있었다."], ["Then the sleepless Boots went shirking round from door to door, gathering up at each the Bluchers, Wellingtons, Oxonians, which stood outside.", "이윽고 잠 없는 구두닦이가 이 문 저 문을 살금살금 돌아다니며, 문밖에 내놓인 블루처 구두며 웰링턴 부츠며 옥스퍼드화를 하나하나 거두어 갔다."], ["Then Jos's native servant arose and began to get ready his master's ponderous dressing apparatus and prepare his hookah; then the maidservants got up, and meeting the dark man in the passages, shrieked, and mistook him for the devil.", "그다음에는 조스의 인도인 하인이 일어나 주인의 묵직한 세면 도구를 챙기고 물담뱃대를 준비하기 시작했고, 뒤이어 하녀들이 일어났다가 복도에서 그 까무잡잡한 사내와 마주치고는 비명을 지르며 그를 악마로 착각했다."], ["He and Dobbin stumbled over their pails in the passages as they were scouring the decks of the Royal George.", "그 하인과 도빈은 하녀들이 로열조지 호의 갑판을 닦느라 복도에 내놓은 물통에 발이 걸려 비틀거렸다."], ["When the first unshorn waiter appeared and unbarred the door of the inn, the Major thought that the time for departure was arrived, and ordered a post-chaise to be fetched instantly, that they might set off.", "면도도 하지 않은 첫 급사가 나타나 여관 문빗장을 풀자, 소령은 떠날 때가 왔다 여기고 당장 역마차를 불러오라 일러, 길을 나설 채비를 했다."]], [["He then directed his steps to Mr. Sedley's room and opened the curtains of the great large family bed wherein Mr.", "그러고 나서 그는 세들리 씨의 방으로 걸음을 옮겨, 조스 씨가 코를 골고 있는 그 크고 넓은 가족용 침대의 휘장을 걷었다."], ["Jos was snoring.", "\"자, 일어나요!"], ["\"Come, up!", "세들리,\""], ["Sedley,\"", "소령이 말했다."], ["the Major said, \"it's time to be off; the chaise will be at the door in half an hour.\"", "\"떠날 시간이오. 삼십 분 뒤면 마차가 문 앞에 와 있을 거요.\""]], [["Jos growled from under the counterpane to know what the time was; but when he at last extorted from the blushing Major (who never told fibs, however they might be to his advantage) what was the real hour of the morning, he broke out into a volley of bad language, which we will not repeat here, but by which he gave Dobbin to understand that he would jeopardy his soul if he got up at that moment, that the Major might go and be hanged, that he would not travel with Dobbin, and that it was most unkind and ungentlemanlike to disturb a man out of his sleep in that way; on which the discomfited Major was obliged to retreat, leaving Jos to resume his interrupted slumbers.", "조스는 이불 밑에서 으르렁대며 몇 시냐고 물었다. 그러나 마침내 얼굴을 붉히는 소령에게서(아무리 제게 득이 될 일이라 해도 결코 거짓말이라곤 하지 못하는 사람이었다) 아침의 진짜 시각을 알아내자, 그는 차마 여기에 옮겨 적지 못할 험한 말을 한바탕 쏟아 냈는데, 그 욕설을 통해 도빈에게 알린 바인즉, 이런 시각에 일어났다가는 제 영혼을 위태롭게 할 것이며, 소령이야 가서 목이라도 매달리든 말든 알 바 아니고, 자기는 도빈과 함께 길을 떠나지 않을 것이며, 잠든 사람을 그런 식으로 깨우는 것이야말로 더없이 야박하고 신사답지 못한 짓이라는 것이었다. 그리하여 무안해진 소령은 물러날 수밖에 없었고, 조스가 중단된 잠을 다시 청하도록 내버려 둘 수밖에 없었다."]], [["The chaise came up presently, and the Major would wait no longer.", "이윽고 마차가 당도하자 소령은 더 이상 기다리려 하지 않았다."]], [["If he had been an English nobleman travelling on a pleasure tour, or a newspaper courier bearing dispatches (government messages are generally carried much more quietly), he could not have travelled more quickly.", "설령 그가 유람길에 오른 영국 귀족이거나 급보를 전하는 신문사 급사였다 해도(정부의 전갈은 대개 훨씬 조용히 운반되는 법이다), 그보다 더 빨리 달리지는 못했을 것이다."], ["The post-boys wondered at the fees he flung amongst them.", "역마차 마부들은 그가 자기들에게 던져 주는 삯에 어리둥절해했다."], ["How happy and green the country looked as the chaise whirled rapidly from mile-stone to mile-stone, through neat country towns where landlords came out to welcome him with smiles and bows; by pretty roadside inns, where the signs hung on the elms, and horses and waggoners were drinking under the chequered shadow of the trees; by old halls and parks; rustic hamlets clustered round ancient grey churches--and through the charming friendly English landscape.", "마차가 이정표에서 이정표로 쏜살같이 굴러갈 때, 그 시골 풍경은 어찌나 행복하고 푸르러 보이던지. 여관 주인들이 미소와 절로 그를 맞으러 나오는 아담한 시골 읍내를 지나고, 느릅나무에 간판이 걸리고 말과 짐마차꾼들이 얼룩덜룩한 나무 그늘 아래서 목을 축이는 어여쁜 길가 여관을 지나고, 오래된 저택과 정원을 지나고, 고색창연한 잿빛 교회 주위로 옹기종기 모여든 시골 마을을 지나고 — 그렇게 매혹적이고 정겨운 영국의 풍경 속을 지나갔다."], ["Is there any in the world like it?", "세상에 이만한 곳이 또 있을까?"], ["To a traveller returning home it looks so kind--it seems to shake hands with you as you pass through it.", "고향으로 돌아오는 나그네에게 그 풍경은 어찌나 다정해 보이는지 — 지나가는 동안 마치 당신과 악수를 청하는 듯한 것이다."], ["Well, Major Dobbin passed through all this from Southampton to London, and without noting much beyond the milestones along the road.", "그렇게 도빈 소령은 사우샘프턴에서 런던에 이르도록 이 모든 풍경을 지나쳤으나, 길가의 이정표 너머로는 별달리 눈여겨보지 않았다."], ["You see he was so eager to see his parents at Camberwell.", "보다시피 그는 캠버웰의 부모님을 어서 뵙고 싶은 마음에 그토록 조급했던 것이다."]], [["He grudged the time lost between Piccadilly and his old haunt at the Slaughters', whither he drove faithfully.", "그는 피카딜리에서, 충실히 마차를 몰고 찾아간 옛 단골집 슬로터스 사이에서 허비한 시간을 아까워했다."], ["Long years had passed since he saw it last, since he and George, as young men, had enjoyed many a feast, and held many a revel there.", "그곳을 마지막으로 본 지, 그러니까 그와 조지가 젊은 시절 그곳에서 숱하게 잔치를 벌이고 숱하게 흥청거리던 그 시절 이후로 긴 세월이 흘렀다."], ["He had now passed into the stage of old-fellow-hood.", "이제 그는 노년의 문턱에 접어든 처지였다."], ["His hair was grizzled, and many a passion and feeling of his youth had grown grey in that interval.", "머리는 희끗희끗해졌고, 젊은 날의 숱한 열정과 감정도 그사이 잿빛으로 시들어 버렸다."], ["There, however, stood the old waiter at the door, in the same greasy black suit, with the same double chin and flaccid face, with the same huge bunch of seals at his fob, rattling his money in his pockets as before, and receiving the Major as if he had gone away only a week ago.", "그러나 거기 문간에는 그 옛날의 늙은 급사가, 그 기름때 전 검은 옷차림 그대로, 그 이중 턱과 축 늘어진 얼굴 그대로, 회중시계 주머니에 그 큼직한 인장 꾸러미를 매단 그대로, 예전처럼 주머니 속 동전을 짤랑이며 서 있다가, 마치 소령이 떠난 지 일주일밖에 안 된 양 그를 맞이했다."], ["\"Put the Major's things in twenty-three, that's his room,\"", "\"소령님 짐은 이십삼호실에 들여놓게, 거기가 소령님 방이니까.\""], ["John said, exhibiting not the least surprise.", "존이 조금도 놀라는 기색 없이 말했다."], ["\"Roast fowl for your dinner, I suppose.", "\"저녁으로는 통구이 닭이겠지요."], ["You ain't got married?", "결혼은 안 하셨고요?"], ["They said you was married--the Scotch surgeon of yours was here.", "결혼하셨다고들 하던데 — 그 댁 스코틀랜드 군의관이 여기 왔었거든요."], ["No, it was Captain Humby of the thirty-third, as was quartered with the --th in Injee.", "아니지, 인도에서 모 연대랑 같이 주둔했던 삼십삼 연대 험비 대위였구먼."], ["Like any warm water?", "더운물 좀 드릴까요?"], ["What do you come in a chay for--ain't the coach good enough?\"", "그런데 어쩌자고 역마차를 타고 오셨대요 — 합승마차로는 부족하셨나?\""], ["And with this, the faithful waiter, who knew and remembered every officer who used the house, and with whom ten years were but as yesterday, led the way up to Dobbin's old room, where stood the great moreen bed, and the shabby carpet, a thought more dingy, and all the old black furniture covered with faded chintz, just as the Major recollected them in his youth.", "그러고는 이 충실한 급사는, 이 집을 드나든 사관이라면 누구든 다 알고 다 기억하며, 십 년 세월쯤은 어제 일과 다름없는 사람이라, 앞장서서 도빈을 그의 옛 방으로 안내했다. 거기에는 그 커다란 모린 천 침대와, 한층 더 거무죽죽해진 듯한 낡은 양탄자, 그리고 빛바랜 사라사 천을 씌운 그 검은 가구들이, 소령이 젊은 시절에 기억하던 그대로 놓여 있었다."]], [["He remembered George pacing up and down the room, and biting his nails, and swearing that the Governor must come round, and that if he didn't, he didn't care a straw, on the day before he was married.", "그는 조지가 결혼 전날 그 방 안을 서성이며 손톱을 물어뜯고, 아버지가 분명 마음을 돌릴 거라고, 만약 안 돌린다 해도 자기는 눈곱만큼도 상관없다고 호언하던 모습을 떠올렸다."], ["He could fancy him walking in, banging the door of Dobbin's room, and his own hard by--", "그는 조지가 도빈의 방문과, 바로 옆에 붙은 제 방 문을 쾅쾅 닫으며 걸어 들어오는 모습을 눈앞에 그려 볼 수 있었다 —"]], [["\"You ain't got young,\"", "\"젊어지진 않으셨네요.\""], ["John said, calmly surveying his friend of former days.", "존이 옛 시절의 벗을 차분히 훑어보며 말했다."]], [["Dobbin laughed.", "도빈은 웃었다."], ["\"Ten years and a fever don't make a man young, John,\"", "\"십 년 세월에 열병까지 앓고 나면 사람이 젊어질 리가 있나, 존.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"It is you that are always young--no, you are always old.\"", "\"늘 젊은 건 자네지 — 아니, 자네는 늘 늙어 있구먼.\""]], [["\"What became of Captain Osborne's widow?\"", "\"오스본 대위님 미망인은 어찌 됐답니까?\""], ["John said.", "존이 말했다."], ["\"Fine young fellow that.", "\"그 양반 참 훤칠한 젊은이였는데."], ["Lord, how he used to spend his money.", "세상에, 돈을 어찌나 펑펑 쓰시던지."], ["He never came back after that day he was marched from here.", "여기서 행군해 나가시던 그날 이후로 영영 안 돌아오셨지요."], ["He owes me three pound at this minute.", "지금 이 순간까지도 제게 삼 파운드를 빚지셨다오."], ["Look here, I have it in my book.", "자 보세요, 제 장부에 적혀 있어요."], ["'April 10, 1815, Captain Osborne: 3 pounds.'", "'1815년 4월 10일, 오스본 대위: 3파운드.'"], ["I wonder whether his father would pay me,\"", "그 양반 부친이 갚아 주려나 모르겠네요.\""], ["and so saying, John of the Slaughters' pulled out the very morocco pocket-book in which he had noted his loan to the Captain, upon a greasy faded page still extant, with many other scrawled memoranda regarding the bygone frequenters of the house.", "그러면서 슬로터스의 존은, 대위에게 빌려준 돈을 적어 둔 바로 그 모로코가죽 수첩을 꺼내 보였는데, 그 기름때 전 빛바랜 책장은 아직도 멀쩡히 남아 있었고, 거기에는 이 집을 드나들던 지난날의 단골들에 관한 다른 갈겨쓴 비망록도 잔뜩 적혀 있었다."]], [["Having inducted his customer into the room, John retired with perfect calmness; and Major Dobbin, not without a blush and a grin at his own absurdity, chose out of his kit the very smartest and most becoming civil costume he possessed, and laughed at his own tanned face and grey hair, as he surveyed them in the dreary little toilet-glass on the dressing-table.", "손님을 방에 들여놓은 뒤, 존은 더없이 태연하게 물러갔다. 도빈 소령은 제 우스꽝스러움에 얼굴을 붉히고 픽 웃지 않을 수 없으면서도, 행장 중에서 자기가 가진 가장 맵시 있고 가장 잘 어울리는 평상복을 골라냈고, 화장대 위 음울하고 자그마한 거울에 비친 제 햇볕에 그을린 얼굴과 희끗한 머리를 들여다보며 스스로를 비웃었다."]], [["\"I'm glad old John didn't forget me,\"", "\"늙은 존이 날 잊지 않았다니 다행이군.\""], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["\"She'll know me, too, I hope.\"", "\"그녀도 날 알아봐 주면 좋으련만.\""], ["And he sallied out of the inn, bending his steps once more in the direction of Brompton.", "그러고는 여관을 나서서, 다시 한번 브롬프턴 쪽으로 걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["Every minute incident of his last meeting with Amelia was present to the constant man's mind as he walked towards her house.", "그 한결같은 사내가 그녀의 집을 향해 걸어가는 동안, 애밀리아와의 마지막 만남의 사소한 장면 하나하나가 그의 마음속에 또렷이 떠올랐다."], ["The arch and the Achilles statue were up since he had last been in Piccadilly; a hundred changes had occurred which his eye and mind vaguely noted.", "그가 마지막으로 피카딜리에 와 본 이후로 개선문과 아킬레우스 동상이 세워졌고, 그의 눈과 마음이 어렴풋이 알아챈 변화가 백 가지는 일어나 있었다."], ["He began to tremble as he walked up the lane from Brompton, that well-remembered lane leading to the street where she lived.", "그는 브롬프턴에서 골목길을 올라가는 동안, 그녀가 사는 거리로 이어지는 그 또렷이 기억에 남은 골목길을 오르는 동안 떨리기 시작했다."], ["Was she going to be married or not?", "그녀는 결혼하게 될 것인가, 아닌가?"], ["If he were to meet her with the little boy--Good God, what should he do?", "만약 그녀를 어린 아들과 함께 만난다면 — 맙소사, 그러면 어떻게 해야 한단 말인가?"], ["He saw a woman coming to him with a child of five years old--was that she?", "그는 다섯 살쯤 된 아이를 데리고 자기 쪽으로 다가오는 한 여자를 보았다 — 저게 그녀일까?"], ["He began to shake at the mere possibility.", "그는 그 가능성만으로도 떨리기 시작했다."], ["When he came up to the row of houses, at last, where she lived, and to the gate, he caught hold of it and paused.", "마침내 그녀가 사는 그 줄지어 선 집들에 이르러 대문 앞에 다다르자, 그는 대문을 붙잡고 멈춰 섰다."], ["He might have heard the thumping of his own heart.", "제 심장이 쿵쾅거리는 소리가 들릴 지경이었다."], ["\"May God Almighty bless her, whatever has happened,\"", "\"무슨 일이 있었든, 전능하신 하느님께서 그녀에게 축복을 내리시기를.\""], ["he thought to himself.", "그는 속으로 생각했다."], ["\"Psha!", "\"쳇!"], ["she may be gone from here,\"", "그녀가 여기서 떠나 버렸을지도 모르지.\""], ["he said and went in through the gate.", "그렇게 말하고 그는 대문을 지나 안으로 들어섰다."]], [["The window of the parlour which she used to occupy was open, and there were no inmates in the room.", "예전에 그녀가 쓰던 응접실 창문은 열려 있었으나 방 안에는 아무도 없었다."], ["The Major thought he recognized the piano, though, with the picture over it, as it used to be in former days, and his perturbations were renewed.", "그래도 소령은 그 위에 걸린 그림과 함께, 옛날 그대로 놓여 있는 그 피아노를 알아본 듯했고, 그의 불안이 다시 일었다."], ["Mr. Clapp's brass plate was still on the door, at the knocker of which Dobbin performed a summons.", "문에는 여전히 클랩 씨의 놋쇠 명패가 붙어 있었고, 도빈은 그 문고리로 사람을 불렀다."]], [["A buxom-looking lass of sixteen, with bright eyes and purple cheeks, came to answer the knock and looked hard at the Major as he leant back against the little porch.", "열여섯쯤 된, 통통하고 두 눈이 반짝이며 두 볼이 발그레한 처녀가 노크에 답하러 나와서는, 자그마한 현관에 기대선 소령을 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["He was as pale as a ghost and could hardly falter out the words--\"Does Mrs.", "그는 유령처럼 창백해진 얼굴로 가까스로 더듬더듬 말을 꺼냈다."], ["Osborne live here?\"", "\"여기 오스본 부인이 사십니까?\""]], [["She looked him hard in the face for a moment--and then turning white too--said, \"Lord bless me--it's Major Dobbin.\"", "처녀는 잠시 그의 얼굴을 빤히 들여다보더니 — 이내 자기도 새하얗게 질려서는 — 말했다. \"어머나 세상에 — 도빈 소령님이시네.\""], ["She held out both her hands shaking--\"Don't you remember me?\" she said.", "그녀는 떨리는 두 손을 내밀었다."], ["\"I used to call you Major Sugarplums.\"", "\"절 기억 못 하세요?\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["On which, and I believe it was for the first time that he ever so conducted himself in his life, the Major took the girl in his arms and kissed her.", "\"제가 소령님을 사탕과자 소령님이라고 부르곤 했잖아요.\" 그러자, 평생 처음 있는 일이었으리라 싶은데, 소령은 그 처녀를 두 팔로 끌어안고 입을 맞추었다."], ["She began to laugh and cry hysterically, and calling out \"Ma, Pa!\"", "처녀는 발작하듯 웃다가 울다가 하더니, 있는 힘껏 \"엄마, 아빠!\""], ["with all her voice, brought up those worthy people, who had already been surveying the Major from the casement of the ornamental kitchen, and were astonished to find their daughter in the little passage in the embrace of a great tall man in a blue frock-coat and white duck trousers.", "하고 소리쳐 그 착한 양주를 불러왔다. 두 사람은 이미 운치 있는 부엌의 여닫이창으로 소령을 살피던 참이었는데, 좁은 복도에서 제 딸이 푸른 프록코트에 흰 무명 바지 차림의 키 큰 사내 품에 안겨 있는 것을 보고는 깜짝 놀랐다."]], [["\"I'm an old friend,\"", "\"전 옛 친구랍니다.\""], ["he said--not without blushing though.", "그가 말했다 — 그러면서도 얼굴을 붉히긴 했지만."], ["\"Don't you remember me, Mrs. Clapp, and those good cakes you used to make for tea?", "\"절 기억 못 하시겠어요, 클랩 부인, 차 마실 때 만들어 주시던 그 맛난 케이크 말입니다?"], ["Don't you recollect me, Clapp?", "절 못 알아보겠소, 클랩?"], ["I'm George's godfather, and just come back from India.\"", "난 조지의 대부요, 인도에서 막 돌아온 길이라오.\""], ["A great shaking of hands ensued--Mrs. Clapp was greatly affected and delighted; she called upon heaven to interpose a vast many times in that passage.", "한바탕 악수가 오갔다 — 클랩 부인은 몹시 감격하고 또 기뻐하며, 그 복도에서 수도 없이 하늘에 가호를 빌었다."]], [["The landlord and landlady of the house led the worthy Major into the Sedleys' room (whereof he remembered every single article of furniture, from the old brass ornamented piano, once a natty little instrument, Stothard maker, to the screens and the alabaster miniature tombstone, in the midst of which ticked Mr.", "이 집의 주인 내외는 사람 좋은 소령을 세들리네 방으로 안내했는데(그는 그 방의 가구라면, 한때 스토서드 사가 만든 산뜻하고 자그마한 악기였던 그 놋쇠 장식 낡은 피아노에서부터, 가리개며 설화석고로 만든 자그마한 묘비 모양 장식품 — 그 한가운데서 세들리 씨의 금시계가 째깍대고 있었다 — 에 이르기까지 하나하나 다 기억하고 있었다), 그가 그 빈 셋방살이용 안락의자에 앉자, 아버지와 어머니와 딸이 이야기 중간중간 수도 없이 감탄사를 터뜨려 가며, 우리가 이미 아는 바를 소령에게 일러 주었다."], ["Sedley's gold watch), and there, as he sat down in the lodger's vacant arm-chair, the father, the mother, and the daughter, with a thousand ejaculatory breaks in the narrative, informed Major Dobbin of what we know already, but of particulars in Amelia's history of which he was not aware--namely of Mrs. Sedley's death, of George's reconcilement with his grandfather Osborne, of the way in which the widow took on at leaving him, and of other particulars of her life.", "다만 애밀리아의 사연 가운데 그가 미처 모르던 자세한 부분들 — 즉 세들리 부인의 죽음, 조지가 할아버지 오스본과 화해한 일, 미망인이 아들을 떠나보내며 얼마나 가슴 아파했는지, 그리고 그녀의 삶에 관한 다른 세세한 일들도 함께 일러 주었다."], ["Twice or thrice he was going to ask about the marriage question, but his heart failed him.", "두세 번 그는 결혼 문제를 물어보려 했으나 그때마다 용기가 나지 않았다."], ["He did not care to lay it bare to these people. Finally, he was informed that Mrs.", "그는 이런 사람들 앞에 그 속내를 드러내고 싶지 않았다."], ["O. was gone to walk with her pa in Kensington Gardens, whither she always went with the old gentleman (who was very weak and peevish now, and led her a sad life, though she behaved to him like an angel, to be sure), of a fine afternoon, after dinner.", "마침내 그는, 오스본 부인이 화창한 오후 식사를 마친 뒤면 늘 노신사와 함께 그러하듯, 켄싱턴 공원으로 아버지와 산책을 나갔다는 말을 들었다(노인은 이제 몹시 쇠약하고 짜증이 심해 그녀를 고달프게 했으나, 그녀는 정말이지 천사처럼 그를 대했다)."]], [["\"I'm very much pressed for time,\"", "\"제가 시간이 몹시 촉박해서요.\""], ["the Major said, \"and have business to-night of importance.", "소령이 말했다. \"오늘 밤에 중요한 볼일이 있거든요."], ["I should like to see Mrs. Osborne tho'.", "그래도 오스본 부인은 좀 뵙고 싶군요."], ["Suppose Miss Polly would come with me and show me the way?\"", "폴리 양이 저와 함께 가서 길을 안내해 주시면 어떨까요?\""]], [["Miss Polly was charmed and astonished at this proposal. She knew the way.", "폴리 양은 이 제안에 매료되었고 또 놀랐다."], ["She would show Major Dobbin.", "그녀는 길을 알았다."], ["She had often been with Mr. Sedley when Mrs.", "그녀가 도빈 소령에게 길을 안내해 드리겠노라 했다."], ["O. was gone--was gone Russell Square way--and knew the bench where he liked to sit.", "오스본 부인이 외출했을 때면 — 러셀 광장 쪽으로 갔을 때면 — 자기가 세들리 씨를 모시고 자주 갔었기에, 노인이 즐겨 앉는 그 벤치도 알고 있었다."], ["She bounced away to her apartment and appeared presently in her best bonnet and her mamma's yellow shawl and large pebble brooch, of which she assumed the loan in order to make herself a worthy companion for the Major.", "그녀는 폴짝폴짝 제 방으로 뛰어가더니, 이내 가장 좋은 보닛에다, 소령에게 어울리는 길동무가 되겠다며 빌려 걸친 어머니의 노란 숄과 커다란 마노 브로치까지 두르고 나타났다."]], [["That officer, then, in his blue frock-coat and buckskin gloves, gave the young lady his arm, and they walked away very gaily.", "그리하여 그 사관은, 푸른 프록코트에 사슴가죽 장갑 차림으로, 젊은 아가씨에게 팔을 내주었고, 두 사람은 더없이 흥겹게 걸어갔다."], ["He was glad to have a friend at hand for the scene which he dreaded somehow.", "그는 어쩐지 두려운 그 장면을 앞두고 곁에 동무가 있어 다행이라 여겼다."], ["He asked a thousand more questions from his companion about Amelia: his kind heart grieved to think that she should have had to part with her son.", "그는 동행에게 애밀리아에 관해 천 가지도 더 되는 질문을 던졌다. 그 다정한 마음은, 그녀가 아들과 헤어져야 했다는 생각에 가슴 아파했다."], ["How did she bear it?", "그녀는 그 일을 어떻게 견뎌 냈을까?"], ["Did she see him often?", "자주 아들을 보러 갈까?"], ["Was Mr. Sedley pretty comfortable now in a worldly point of view?", "세들리 씨는 이제 세속적인 형편으로 보아 그런대로 편안한 처지일까?"], ["Polly answered all these questions of Major Sugarplums to the very best of her power.", "폴리는 사탕과자 소령님의 이 모든 질문에 제가 할 수 있는 한 최선을 다해 대답했다."]], [["And in the midst of their walk an incident occurred which, though very simple in its nature, was productive of the greatest delight to Major Dobbin.", "그렇게 걸어가던 도중에 한 가지 일이 일어났는데, 그 성격이야 지극히 단순했으나 도빈 소령에게는 더없이 큰 기쁨을 안겨 준 일이었다."], ["A pale young man with feeble whiskers and a stiff white neckcloth came walking down the lane, en sandwich--having a lady, that is, on each arm.", "성긴 구레나룻에 빳빳한 흰 목도리를 두른 한 창백한 젊은이가, 양팔에 숙녀를 하나씩 낀 샌드위치 꼴로 골목길을 걸어 내려오고 있었다."], ["One was a tall and commanding middle-aged female, with features and a complexion similar to those of the clergyman of the Church of England by whose side she marched, and the other a stunted little woman with a dark face, ornamented by a fine new bonnet and white ribbons, and in a smart pelisse, with a rich gold watch in the midst of her person.", "한 사람은 키가 크고 위풍당당한 중년 여자로, 그 옆을 나란히 걷는 영국 국교회 목사와 이목구비며 안색이 비슷했고, 다른 한 사람은 거무튀튀한 얼굴에 키가 자그마한 여자로, 산뜻한 새 보닛에 흰 리본으로 멋을 내고 맵시 있는 외투를 걸쳤으며, 몸 한가운데에는 값나가는 금시계를 차고 있었다."], ["The gentleman, pinioned as he was by these two ladies, carried further a parasol, shawl, and basket, so that his arms were entirely engaged, and of course he was unable to touch his hat in acknowledgement of the curtsey with which Miss Mary Clapp greeted him.", "이 두 숙녀에게 양팔이 죄다 붙들린 신사는 게다가 양산과 숄과 바구니까지 들고 있어서 두 팔이 완전히 묶여 버린 터라, 메리 클랩 양이 무릎을 굽혀 인사하는 데도 모자에 손을 대어 답례할 수가 없었던 것이다."]], [["He merely bowed his head in reply to her salutation, which the two ladies returned with a patronizing air, and at the same time looking severely at the individual in the blue coat and bamboo cane who accompanied Miss Polly.", "그는 그저 고개만 까닥여 그녀의 인사에 답했고, 두 숙녀는 생색내는 태도로 그 인사를 받으면서, 동시에 폴리 양과 동행한 푸른 코트에 등나무 지팡이를 든 사내를 매섭게 뜯어보았다."]], [["\"Who's that?\"", "\"저 사람들은 누구죠?\""], ["asked the Major, amused by the group, and after he had made way for the three to pass up the lane.", "소령이, 그 일행이 골목길을 올라가도록 길을 비켜 준 뒤, 그 무리가 재미있다는 듯 물었다."], ["Mary looked at him rather roguishly.", "메리는 다소 장난스럽게 그를 쳐다보았다."]], [["\"That is our curate, the Reverend Mr.", "\"저분이 우리 부목사님이세요, 비니 목사님요(도빈 소령의 얼굴이 움찔했다)."], ["Binny (a twitch from Major Dobbin), and his sister Miss B.", "그리고 저 누님 되시는 비니 양요."], ["Lord bless us, how she did use to worret us at Sunday-school; and the other lady, the little one with a cast in her eye and the handsome watch, is Mrs.", "어휴 세상에, 주일 학교에서 우릴 어찌나 들볶으셨는지. 그리고 다른 부인, 저 사팔뜨기에 멋진 시계 찬 자그마한 분은 비니 부인이세요 — 처녀 적엔 그리츠 양이었지요."], ["Binny--Miss Grits that was; her pa was a grocer, and kept the Little Original Gold Tea Pot in Kensington Gravel Pits.", "친정아버지가 식료품상이셨는데, 켄싱턴 그래블핏츠에서 '리틀 오리지널 골드 티 포트'를 하셨대요."], ["They were married last month, and are just come back from Margate.", "지난달에 결혼해서 마게이트에서 막 돌아왔답니다."], ["She's five thousand pound to her fortune; but her and Miss B., who made the match, have quarrelled already.\"", "재산이 오천 파운드나 된대요. 그런데 그 부인이랑, 이 혼사를 맺어 준 비니 양이 벌써 사이가 틀어졌다지 뭐예요.\""]], [["If the Major had twitched before, he started now, and slapped the bamboo on the ground with an emphasis which made Miss Clapp cry, \"Law,\"", "소령은 아까도 얼굴이 움찔했지만, 이번에는 화들짝 놀라며 등나무 지팡이로 어찌나 힘껏 땅을 내리쳤던지 클랩 양이 \"아이고머니나\""], ["and laugh too.", "하고 외치며 깔깔 웃었다."], ["He stood for a moment, silent, with open mouth, looking after the retreating young couple, while Miss Mary told their history; but he did not hear beyond the announcement of the reverend gentleman's marriage; his head was swimming with felicity.", "그는 잠시 입을 벌린 채 말없이 서서, 멀어져 가는 그 젊은 부부의 뒷모습을 바라보았다. 메리 양이 그들의 내력을 이야기하는 동안에도, 그는 그 목사가 결혼했다는 말 이후로는 아무것도 귀에 들어오지 않았다."], ["After this rencontre he began to walk double quick towards the place of his destination--and yet they were too soon (for he was in a great tremor at the idea of a meeting for which he had been longing any time these ten years)--through the Brompton lanes, and entering at the little old portal in Kensington Garden wall.", "그의 머릿속은 행복으로 빙빙 돌고 있었다. 이 마주침 이후로 그는 목적지를 향해 곱절로 빨리 걷기 시작했다 — 그런데도 너무 일찍 당도하고 말았다(지난 십 년 동안 줄곧 갈망해 온 그 만남을 앞두고 그는 몹시 떨고 있었으니까) — 브롬프턴의 골목길을 지나, 켄싱턴 공원 담벼락의 자그마한 옛 문으로 들어섰다."]], [["\"There they are,\"", "\"저기 계시네요.\""], ["said Miss Polly, and she felt him again start back on her arm.", "폴리 양이 말하자, 그녀는 소령이 또 한 번 제 팔에 매달린 채 흠칫 뒤로 물러서는 것을 느꼈다."], ["She was a confidante at once of the whole business.", "그녀는 어느새 이 일의 전모를 다 아는 심복이 되어 있었다."], ["She knew the story as well as if she had read it in one of her favourite novel-books--Fatherless Fanny, or the Scottish Chiefs.", "그녀는 제가 좋아하는 소설책 — 『아비 없는 패니』나 『스코틀랜드의 족장들』 — 에서 읽기라도 한 듯 그 사연을 훤히 알고 있었던 것이다."]], [["\"Suppose you were to run on and tell her,\"", "\"아가씨가 먼저 달려가서 부인께 알려 드리는 게 어떻겠소.\""], ["the Major said.", "소령이 말했다."], ["Polly ran forward, her yellow shawl streaming in the breeze.", "폴리는 노란 숄을 산들바람에 나부끼며 앞으로 달려 나갔다."]], [["Old Sedley was seated on a bench, his handkerchief placed over his knees, prattling away, according to his wont, with some old story about old times to which Amelia had listened and awarded a patient smile many a time before.", "세들리 노인은 벤치에 앉아 무릎 위에 손수건을 펼쳐 놓은 채, 여느 때 버릇대로 옛 시절에 관한 어떤 오래된 이야기를 주절주절 늘어놓고 있었다. 애밀리아가 전에도 숱하게 귀 기울이며 참을성 있는 미소로 받아 주던 그런 이야기였다."], ["She could of late think of her own affairs, and smile or make other marks of recognition of her father's stories, scarcely hearing a word of the old man's tales.", "요즈음 그녀는 아버지의 이야기에 미소를 짓거나 그 밖의 알아듣는 시늉을 하면서도, 노인의 옛이야기는 한마디도 거의 귀에 담지 않은 채, 제 자신의 일을 골똘히 생각할 수 있게 되었다."], ["As Mary came bouncing along, and Amelia caught sight of her, she started up from her bench.", "메리가 폴짝폴짝 달려오고 애밀리아가 그 모습을 알아보자, 그녀는 벤치에서 벌떡 일어났다."], ["Her first thought was that something had happened to Georgy, but the sight of the messenger's eager and happy face dissipated that fear in the timorous mother's bosom.", "첫 번째 든 생각은 조지에게 무슨 일이 생겼나 하는 것이었으나, 그 전령의 들뜨고 행복한 얼굴을 보자 겁 많은 어미의 가슴속 그 두려움은 이내 사라졌다."]], [["\"News!", "\"소식이에요!"], ["News!\"", "소식! \""], ["cried the emissary of Major Dobbin.", "도빈 소령의 사자가 외쳤다."], ["\"He's come!", "\"그분이 오셨어요!"], ["He's come!\"", "오셨다고요!\""]], [["\"Who is come?\"", "\"누가 왔다는 거니?\""], ["said Emmy, still thinking of her son.", "에미가 여전히 아들 생각을 하며 말했다."]], [["\"Look there,\"", "\"저기 보세요.\""], ["answered Miss Clapp, turning round and pointing; in which direction Amelia looking, saw Dobbin's lean figure and long shadow stalking across the grass.", "클랩 양이 몸을 돌려 손가락으로 가리키며 대답했다. 애밀리아가 그쪽을 바라보니, 도빈의 야윈 몸과 긴 그림자가 잔디밭을 가로질러 성큼성큼 다가오고 있었다."], ["Amelia started in her turn, blushed up, and, of course, began to cry.", "이번에는 애밀리아가 흠칫하더니, 얼굴이 발그레 달아오르며, 그러고는 으레 그렇듯 울기 시작했다."], ["At all this simple little creature's fetes, the grandes eaux were accustomed to play.", "이 순박한 자그마한 사람의 온갖 행사에는 늘 그 큰 분수의 물줄기가 터지곤 했던 것이다."], ["He looked at her--oh, how fondly--as she came running towards him, her hands before her, ready to give them to him.", "그녀가 그를 향해, 두 손을 앞으로 내밀어 그에게 건넬 채비를 하고 달려오는 동안, 그는 그녀를 바라보았다 — 아, 얼마나 애틋한 눈길로."], ["She wasn't changed.", "그녀는 변하지 않았다."], ["She was a little pale, a little stouter in figure.", "조금 창백하고, 몸매가 조금 더 통통해졌을 뿐."], ["Her eyes were the same, the kind trustful eyes.", "두 눈은 여전했다, 그 다정하고 믿음직한 두 눈은."], ["There were scarce three lines of silver in her soft brown hair.", "그 부드러운 갈색 머리카락에 은빛 머리칼이라곤 채 세 가닥도 되지 않았다."], ["She gave him both her hands as she looked up flushing and smiling through her tears into his honest homely face.", "그녀는 눈물 너머로 발그레한 얼굴에 미소를 띤 채, 그의 정직하고 수수한 얼굴을 올려다보며 두 손을 다 그에게 내밀었다."], ["He took the two little hands between his two and held them there.", "그는 그 자그마한 두 손을 자기 두 손 사이에 감싸 쥐고 그대로 잡고 있었다."], ["He was speechless for a moment.", "그는 잠시 말을 잇지 못했다."], ["Why did he not take her in his arms and swear that he would never leave her?", "어찌하여 그는 그녀를 두 팔로 끌어안고 결코 그녀를 떠나지 않겠노라 맹세하지 않았던가?"], ["She must have yielded: she could not but have obeyed him.", "그녀는 분명 응했을 텐데. 그의 말에 따르지 않을 수 없었을 텐데."]], [["\"I--I've another arrival to announce,\"", "\"또 — 또 한 사람의 도착을 알려 드릴 게 있습니다.\""], ["he said after a pause.", "그가 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 말했다."]], [["\"Mrs. Dobbin?\"", "\"도빈 부인이요?\""], ["Amelia said, making a movement back--why didn't he speak?", "애밀리아가 뒤로 한 걸음 물러서며 말했다 — 어째서 그는 말을 않는 걸까?"]], [["\"No,\"", "\"아니요.\""], ["he said, letting her hands go: \"Who has told you those lies?", "그가 그녀의 두 손을 놓으며 말했다. \"누가 그런 거짓말을 했답니까?"], ["I mean, your brother Jos came in the same ship with me, and is come home to make you all happy.\"", "제 말은, 부인의 오라버니 조스가 저와 같은 배로 와서, 여러분 모두를 행복하게 해 드리려고 고향에 돌아왔다는 겁니다.\""]], [["\"Papa, Papa!\"", "\"아버지, 아버지!\""], ["Emmy cried out, \"here are news!", "에미가 외쳤다. \"소식이에요!"], ["My brother is in England.", "오라버니가 영국에 왔어요."], ["He is come to take care of you.", "아버지를 보살펴 드리려고요."], ["Here is Major Dobbin.\"", "여기 도빈 소령님도 오셨고요.\""]], [["Mr. Sedley started up, shaking a great deal and gathering up his thoughts.", "세들리 씨는 몹시 떨면서 생각을 가다듬으며 벌떡 일어났다."], ["Then he stepped forward and made an old-fashioned bow to the Major, whom he called Mr. Dobbin, and hoped his worthy father, Sir William, was quite well.", "그러고는 앞으로 나아가, 그를 도빈 씨라 부르며 구식의 절을 하고는, 그의 훌륭하신 부친 윌리엄 경께서 평안하시냐고 안부를 물었다."], ["He proposed to call upon Sir William, who had done him the honour of a visit a short time ago.", "그는 얼마 전 자기를 찾아 주시는 영예를 베푸신 윌리엄 경께 답방을 가겠노라 했다."], ["Sir William had not called upon the old gentleman for eight years--it was that visit he was thinking of returning.", "윌리엄 경이 이 노신사를 찾아온 일은 팔 년 전의 일이었으니 — 노인은 바로 그 방문에 답례할 생각을 하고 있었던 것이다."]], [["\"He is very much shaken,\"", "\"아버지가 많이 쇠약해지셨어요.\""], ["Emmy whispered as Dobbin went up and cordially shook hands with the old man.", "도빈이 다가가 다정하게 노인과 악수를 나누는 동안 에미가 속삭였다."]], [["Although he had such particular business in London that evening, the Major consented to forego it upon Mr. Sedley's invitation to him to come home and partake of tea.", "소령은 그날 저녁 런던에서 그토록 긴한 볼일이 있었음에도, 집으로 가서 차를 함께 들자는 세들리 씨의 권유에 그 볼일을 미루기로 했다."], ["Amelia put her arm under that of her young friend with the yellow shawl and headed the party on their return homewards, so that Mr. Sedley fell to Dobbin's share.", "애밀리아는 노란 숄을 두른 어린 동무의 팔에 제 팔을 끼고 집으로 돌아가는 일행의 앞장을 섰고, 그리하여 세들리 씨는 도빈의 몫으로 떨어졌다."], ["The old man walked very slowly and told a number of ancient histories about himself and his poor Bessy, his former prosperity, and his bankruptcy.", "노인은 아주 느릿느릿 걸으며, 자기 자신과 가엾은 베시에 관한, 한때의 번영과 파산에 관한 케케묵은 옛이야기를 잔뜩 늘어놓았다."], ["His thoughts, as is usual with failing old men, were quite in former times.", "쇠약해진 노인들이 흔히 그러하듯, 그의 생각은 온통 지난 시절에 가 있었다."], ["The present, with the exception of the one catastrophe which he felt, he knew little about.", "그가 절감하는 그 한 가지 재앙만 빼면, 그는 현재에 관해서는 아는 바가 거의 없었다."], ["The Major was glad to let him talk on.", "소령은 노인이 마음껏 이야기하도록 내버려 두어 기뻤다."], ["His eyes were fixed upon the figure in front of him--the dear little figure always present to his imagination and in his prayers, and visiting his dreams wakeful or slumbering.", "그의 두 눈은 앞서가는 그 모습에 — 그의 상상 속에, 그의 기도 속에 늘 함께하고, 깨어 있든 잠들어 있든 그의 꿈을 찾아오던 그 사랑스럽고 자그마한 모습에 — 못 박혀 있었다."]], [["Amelia was very happy, smiling, and active all that evening, performing her duties as hostess of the little entertainment with the utmost grace and propriety, as Dobbin thought.", "애밀리아는 그날 저녁 내내 더없이 행복하게, 미소를 머금고 분주히 움직이며, 도빈이 보기에 더할 나위 없는 우아함과 예의로 그 자그마한 다과회의 안주인 노릇을 해냈다."], ["His eyes followed her about as they sat in the twilight.", "그들이 땅거미 속에 앉아 있는 동안, 그의 두 눈은 그녀를 따라 이리저리 움직였다."], ["How many a time had he longed for that moment and thought of her far away under hot winds and in weary marches, gentle and happy, kindly ministering to the wants of old age, and decorating poverty with sweet submission--as he saw her now.", "그가 얼마나 숱하게 그 순간을 갈망했던가. 뜨거운 바람 아래서, 고단한 행군 속에서, 멀리 떨어진 그녀를 — 지금 그가 보고 있는 그대로, 온화하고 행복하게, 노년의 필요를 다정히 돌보며, 다디단 순종으로 가난을 곱게 단장하는 그녀를 떠올렸던가."], ["I do not say that his taste was the highest, or that it is the duty of great intellects to be content with a bread-and-butter paradise, such as sufficed our simple old friend; but his desires were of this sort, whether for good or bad, and, with Amelia to help him, he was as ready to drink as many cups of tea as Doctor Johnson.", "그의 취향이 가장 고상했다거나, 우리의 순박한 옛 벗에게 흡족했던 것과 같은 빵과 버터의 낙원에 만족하는 것이 위대한 지성의 의무라고 말하려는 것은 아니다. 그러나 좋든 나쁘든 그의 소망은 이런 부류의 것이었고, 애밀리아가 곁에서 거들어 준다면야, 그는 존슨 박사에 못지않게 몇 잔이고 차를 들이켤 준비가 되어 있었다."]], [["Amelia seeing this propensity, laughingly encouraged it and looked exceedingly roguish as she administered to him cup after cup.", "애밀리아는 이런 성향을 알아채고 웃으며 부추겼고, 그에게 차를 한 잔 또 한 잔 따라 주면서 무척이나 장난스러운 표정을 지었다."], ["It is true she did not know that the Major had had no dinner and that the cloth was laid for him at the Slaughters', and a plate laid thereon to mark that the table was retained, in that very box in which the Major and George had sat many a time carousing, when she was a child just come home from Miss Pinkerton's school.", "사실 그녀는 소령이 저녁을 거른 줄도, 슬로터스에 그를 위한 식탁보가 깔리고 그 위에 자리를 맡아 두었다는 표시로 접시 하나가 놓여 있는 줄도 알지 못했다. 바로 그 칸막이 자리, 그녀가 핑커튼 양의 학교에서 막 돌아온 어린아이였던 시절에 소령과 조지가 숱하게 마주 앉아 흥청대던 그 자리에 말이다."]], [["The first thing Mrs. Osborne showed the Major was Georgy's miniature, for which she ran upstairs on her arrival at home.", "오스본 부인이 소령에게 맨 먼저 보여 준 것은 조지의 초상화였는데, 집에 도착하자마자 그것을 가지러 그녀는 위층으로 뛰어 올라갔다."], ["It was not half handsome enough of course for the boy, but wasn't it noble of him to think of bringing it to his mother?", "물론 그 그림은 그 아이의 절반만큼도 잘생기게 그려지지 못했지만, 그래도 그것을 어머니에게 가져다줄 생각을 했다니 얼마나 기특한 일인가?"], ["Whilst her papa was awake she did not talk much about Georgy. To hear about Mr.", "아버지가 깨어 있는 동안에는 그녀가 조지 이야기를 그리 많이 하지 않았다."], ["Osborne and Russell Square was not agreeable to the old man, who very likely was unconscious that he had been living for some months past mainly on the bounty of his richer rival, and lost his temper if allusion was made to the other.", "오스본 씨와 러셀 광장에 관한 이야기는 노인이 듣기 좋아하지 않았으니, 노인은 지난 몇 달 동안 주로 그 더 부유한 연적의 적선으로 살아온 줄도 아마 의식하지 못한 채, 그쪽을 입에 올리기만 해도 성을 내곤 했던 것이다."]], [["Dobbin told him all, and a little more perhaps than all, that had happened on board the Ramchunder, and exaggerated Jos's benevolent dispositions towards his father and resolution to make him comfortable in his old days.", "도빈은 램천더 호 선상에서 있었던 일을 노인에게 죄다, 어쩌면 죄다보다 조금 더 보태어 들려주었고, 아버지를 향한 조스의 자비로운 마음과 노년을 편안히 모시겠다는 결심을 부풀려 이야기했다."], ["The truth is that during the voyage the Major had impressed this duty most strongly upon his fellow-passenger and extorted promises from him that he would take charge of his sister and her child.", "사실인즉 항해 동안 소령은 이 의무를 동행자에게 더없이 강력하게 새겨 넣고, 누이와 그 아이를 책임지겠다는 약속까지 받아 냈던 것이다."], ["He soothed Jos's irritation with regard to the bills which the old gentleman had drawn upon him, gave a laughing account of his own sufferings on the same score and of the famous consignment of wine with which the old man had favoured him, and brought Mr. Jos, who was by no means an ill-natured person when well-pleased and moderately flattered, to a very good state of feeling regarding his relatives in Europe.", "그는 노인이 자기 앞으로 발행한 어음 문제로 조스가 부아를 내는 것을 달래 주었고, 같은 일로 자기가 겪은 고초와 노인이 자기에게 보내 준 그 유명한 포도주 위탁 건을 웃음거리 삼아 들려주면서, 기분이 좋고 적당히 추켜세워 주면 결코 심성이 고약하지 않은 조스 씨를, 유럽에 있는 친지들에 대해 아주 흐뭇한 심정에 이르게 만들었다."]], [["And in fine I am ashamed to say that the Major stretched the truth so far as to tell old Mr. Sedley that it was mainly a desire to see his parent which brought Jos once more to Europe.", "그리고 끝내, 부끄러운 말이지만, 소령은 진실을 한껏 늘여, 조스가 다시 유럽으로 돌아온 것은 주로 부친을 뵙고 싶은 마음 때문이었노라고 세들리 노인에게 일러 주었다."]], [["At his accustomed hour Mr. Sedley began to doze in his chair, and then it was Amelia's opportunity to commence her conversation, which she did with great eagerness--it related exclusively to Georgy.", "여느 때의 시각이 되자 세들리 씨는 의자에 앉은 채 꾸벅꾸벅 졸기 시작했고, 그제야 비로소 애밀리아는 이야기를 시작할 기회를 얻어, 더없이 간절한 마음으로 입을 열었다 — 그것은 오로지 조지에 관한 이야기였다."], ["She did not talk at all about her own sufferings at breaking from him, for indeed, this worthy woman, though she was half-killed by the separation from the child, yet thought it was very wicked in her to repine at losing him; but everything concerning him, his virtues, talents, and prospects, she poured out.", "그녀는 아들과 헤어질 때 제가 겪은 고통에 관해서는 일절 입에 올리지 않았다. 실로 이 갸륵한 여인은, 아이와의 이별로 반쯤 죽을 지경이었으면서도, 아들을 잃었다고 한탄하는 것은 제가 몹시 못된 짓을 저지르는 거라 여겼던 것이다."], ["She described his angelic beauty; narrated a hundred instances of his generosity and greatness of mind whilst living with her; how a Royal Duchess had stopped and admired him in Kensington Gardens; how splendidly he was cared for now, and how he had a groom and a pony; what quickness and cleverness he had, and what a prodigiously well-read and delightful person the Reverend Lawrence Veal was, George's master. \"He knows EVERYTHING,\"", "다만 그 아이에 관한 것이라면 무엇이든 — 그 미덕이며 재주며 앞날이며 — 죄다 쏟아 냈다."], ["Amelia said. \"He has the most delightful parties.", "그녀는 그 아이의 천사 같은 아름다움을 그려 보였고, 함께 사는 동안 그 아이가 보여 준 너그러움과 큰 도량의 사례를 백 가지나 늘어놓았다."], ["You who are so learned yourself, and have read so much, and are so clever and accomplished--don't shake your head and say no--HE always used to say you were--you will be charmed with Mr.", "어느 왕실 공작부인이 켄싱턴 공원에서 걸음을 멈추고 그 아이에게 감탄했다는 이야기며, 지금은 얼마나 융숭하게 보살핌을 받고 있으며, 마부와 조랑말까지 거느리고 있다는 이야기, 그 아이가 얼마나 영민하고 똑똑한지, 그리고 조지의 스승인 로런스 빌 목사가 얼마나 놀랄 만큼 박식하고 유쾌한 분인지 하는 이야기까지. \"그분은 모르는 게 없으세요.\""], ["Veal's parties.", "애밀리아가 말했다."], ["The last Tuesday in every month.", "\"더없이 유쾌한 모임을 여신답니다."], ["He says there is no place in the bar or the senate that Georgy may not aspire to.", "소령님처럼 그토록 학식 있고, 그토록 책을 많이 읽으셨고, 그토록 영리하고 교양 있는 분이라면 — 고개를 저으며 아니라고 하지 마세요 — 그분이 늘 소령님더러 그렇다고 하셨거든요 — 빌 씨의 모임에 분명 반하실 거예요."], ["Look here,\"", "매달 마지막 화요일에 열린답니다."], ["and she went to the piano-drawer and drew out a theme of Georgy's composition.", "그분 말씀이, 조지가 법조계든 의회든 못 오를 자리가 없다지 뭐예요. 자 보세요.\""], ["This great effort of genius, which is still in the possession of George's mother, is as follows:", "그러고 그녀는 피아노 서랍으로 가서 조지가 쓴 작문 한 편을 꺼냈다. 아직도 조지의 어머니가 간직하고 있는 이 위대한 천재의 역작은 다음과 같다."]], [["On Selfishness--Of all the vices which degrade the human character, Selfishness is the most odious and contemptible.", "이기심에 관하여 — 인간의 품성을 떨어뜨리는 온갖 악덕 가운데, 이기심은 가장 가증스럽고 경멸할 만한 것이다."], ["An undue love of Self leads to the most monstrous crimes and occasions the greatest misfortunes both in States and Families.", "자기 자신에 대한 지나친 사랑은 가장 끔찍한 죄악으로 이어지며, 국가에든 가정에든 가장 큰 불행을 불러온다."], ["As a selfish man will impoverish his family and often bring them to ruin, so a selfish king brings ruin on his people and often plunges them into war.", "이기적인 사람이 제 집안을 가난하게 만들고 흔히 파멸로 몰아넣듯이, 이기적인 임금은 제 백성에게 파멸을 가져오고 흔히 그들을 전쟁으로 몰아넣는다."]], [["Example: The selfishness of Achilles, as remarked by the poet Homer, occasioned a thousand woes to the Greeks--muri Achaiois alge etheke--(Hom. Il. A. 2). The selfishness of the late Napoleon Bonaparte occasioned innumerable wars in Europe and caused him to perish, himself, in a miserable island--that of Saint Helena in the Atlantic Ocean.", "예: 시인 호메로스가 지적한 바, 아킬레우스의 이기심은 그리스인들에게 천 가지 재앙을 안겨 주었다 — 무리 아카이오이스 알게 에테케 —(호메로스 『일리아스』 제1권 2행). 고(故) 나폴레옹 보나파르트의 이기심은 유럽에 헤아릴 수 없는 전쟁을 일으켰고, 그 자신을 비참한 섬 — 대서양의 세인트헬레나 섬 — 에서 죽게 만들었다."]], [["We see by these examples that we are not to consult our own interest and ambition, but that we are to consider the interests of others as well as our own.", "우리는 이 사례들을 통해, 우리가 우리 자신의 이익과 야심만을 좇아서는 안 되며, 우리 자신의 이익과 더불어 남의 이익도 헤아려야 함을 알 수 있다."]], [["George S.", "조지 S."], ["Osborne Athene House, 24 April, 1827", "오스본 아테네 학사, 1827년 4월 24일"]], [["\"Think of him writing such a hand, and quoting Greek too, at his age,\"", "\"이 나이에 이런 글씨를 쓰고, 게다가 그리스어까지 인용한다고 생각해 보세요.\""], ["the delighted mother said.", "흐뭇해하는 어머니가 말했다."], ["\"Oh, William,\"", "\"오, 윌리엄,\""], ["she added, holding out her hand to the Major, \"what a treasure Heaven has given me in that boy!", "그녀가 소령에게 손을 내밀며 덧붙였다. \"하늘이 저 아이로 제게 얼마나 큰 보물을 주셨는지요!"], ["He is the comfort of my life--and he is the image of--of him that's gone!\"", "그 아이는 제 삶의 위안이에요 — 그리고 그 아이는 떠나가신 — 그분을 꼭 빼닮았답니다!\""]], [["\"Ought I to be angry with her for being faithful to him?\"", "\"그녀가 그에게 한결같다고 해서 내가 화를 내야 옳단 말인가?\""], ["William thought.", "윌리엄은 생각했다."], ["\"Ought I to be jealous of my friend in the grave, or hurt that such a heart as Amelia's can love only once and for ever?", "\"무덤 속의 내 벗을 질투해야 옳단 말인가, 아니면 애밀리아 같은 마음이 단 한 번 영원토록만 사랑할 수 있다 하여 상처받아야 옳단 말인가?"], ["Oh, George, George, how little you knew the prize you had, though.\"", "오, 조지, 조지, 그러면서도 자네는 자네가 얻은 보물이 얼마나 값진 줄 어찌 그리 몰랐던가.\""], ["This sentiment passed rapidly through William's mind as he was holding Amelia's hand, whilst the handkerchief was veiling her eyes.", "이 감회가 윌리엄의 마음을 빠르게 스쳐 지나가는 동안, 그는 애밀리아의 손을 잡고 있었고, 그녀는 손수건으로 두 눈을 가리고 있었다."]], [["\"Dear friend,\"", "\"고마운 친구분,\""], ["she said, pressing the hand which held hers, \"how good, how kind you always have been to me!", "그녀가 제 손을 잡은 그 손을 꼭 쥐며 말했다. \"소령님은 늘 제게 얼마나 다정하고, 얼마나 친절하셨는지요!"], ["See! Papa is stirring.", "저것 봐요!"], ["You will go and see Georgy tomorrow, won't you?\"", "아버지가 깨시려나 봐요. 내일 조지를 보러 가실 거죠, 그렇죠?\""]], [["\"Not to-morrow,\"", "\"내일은 안 됩니다.\""], ["said poor old Dobbin.", "가엾은 늙은 도빈이 말했다."], ["\"I have business.\"", "\"볼일이 있어서요.\""], ["He did not like to own that he had not as yet been to his parents' and his dear sister Anne--a remissness for which I am sure every well-regulated person will blame the Major.", "그는 아직 부모님과 사랑하는 누이 앤에게조차 들르지 못했다는 사실을 차마 털어놓고 싶지 않았다 — 이 태만함을 두고 분별 있는 사람이라면 누구나 소령을 나무라리라고 나는 확신한다."], ["And presently he took his leave, leaving his address behind him for Jos, against the latter's arrival.", "이윽고 그는 작별을 고하면서, 조스가 당도하거든 전하라며 제 주소를 남겨 두고 떠났다."], ["And so the first day was over, and he had seen her.", "그리하여 첫째 날이 저물었고, 그는 그녀를 본 것이다."]], [["When he got back to the Slaughters', the roast fowl was of course cold, in which condition he ate it for supper.", "그가 슬로터스로 돌아왔을 때 통구이 닭은 물론 식어 있었고, 그는 그 식은 그대로를 저녁으로 먹었다."], ["And knowing what early hours his family kept, and that it would be needless to disturb their slumbers at so late an hour, it is on record, that Major Dobbin treated himself to half-price at the Haymarket Theatre that evening, where let us hope he enjoyed himself.", "그리고 제 가족이 얼마나 일찍 잠자리에 드는지 잘 알고 있었기에, 이토록 늦은 시각에 그들의 잠을 굳이 깨울 필요가 없다 여기고서, 도빈 소령은 그날 저녁 헤이마켓 극장에서 반값 입장으로 호사를 누렸다고 기록에 전한다. 부디 그가 거기서 즐거운 시간을 보냈기를 바라 마지않는다."]], [["The Old Piano\nThe Major's visit left old John Sedley in a great state of agitation and excitement.", "소령의 방문은 늙은 존 세들리를 큰 동요와 흥분 상태에 빠뜨렸다."], ["His daughter could not induce him to settle down to his customary occupations or amusements that night.", "그날 밤 딸은 아무리 애써도 그를 평소의 일거리나 소일거리에 진득이 붙어 있게 만들 수 없었다."], ["He passed the evening fumbling amongst his boxes and desks, untying his papers with trembling hands, and sorting and arranging them against Jos's arrival.", "그는 저녁 내내 상자며 책상을 뒤적거렸고, 떨리는 손으로 서류 뭉치를 풀어 헤쳐, 조스가 도착할 때를 대비해 그것들을 분류하고 정리했다."], ["He had them in the greatest order--his tapes and his files, his receipts, and his letters with lawyers and correspondents; the documents relative to the wine project (which failed from a most unaccountable accident, after commencing with the most splendid prospects), the coal project (which only a want of capital prevented from becoming the most successful scheme ever put before the public), the patent saw-mills and sawdust consolidation project, &c., &c.", "그는 모든 것을 더없이 가지런하게 갖추어 두었다. 끈이며 서류철이며, 영수증이며, 변호사며 거래처와 주고받은 편지며, 와인 사업과 관련된 서류들이며(이 사업은 더없이 찬란한 전망으로 시작했으나 도무지 설명할 수 없는 사고로 실패하고 말았다), 석탄 사업에 관한 것들이며(이것은 자본 부족이 없었더라면 일찍이 세상에 선보인 가장 성공적인 사업이 되었을 것이다), 특허받은 제재소와 톱밥 압축 사업에 관한 것들이며 등등을."], ["All night, until a very late hour, he passed in the preparation of these documents, trembling about from one room to another, with a quivering candle and shaky hands.", "그는 밤새도록, 아주 늦은 시각까지 이 서류들을 정리하느라, 가물거리는 촛불과 떨리는 손을 들고 이 방 저 방을 비척비척 옮겨 다녔다. 여기는 와인 서류, 여기는 톱밥, 여기는 석탄."], ["Here's the wine papers, here's the sawdust, here's the coals; here's my letters to Calcutta and Madras, and replies from Major Dobbin, C.B., and Mr.", "여기는 내가 캘커타와 마드라스로 보낸 편지들, 그리고 그에 대한 도빈 소령 무공훈장 수훈자와 조지프 세들리 씨의 답장들."], ["Joseph Sedley to the same. \"He shall find no irregularity about ME, Emmy,\"", "\"이 아비한테서는 흐트러진 구석 하나 못 찾을 게다, 에미.\""], ["the old gentleman said.", "노신사가 말했다."]], [["Emmy smiled.", "에미는 미소 지었다."], ["\"I don't think Jos will care about seeing those papers, Papa,\"", "\"조스가 그 서류들을 보고 싶어 할 것 같진 않은데요, 아버지.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"You don't know anything about business, my dear,\"", "\"넌 사업이라는 걸 통 모르는구나, 얘야.\""], ["answered the sire, shaking his head with an important air.", "아비가 자못 의젓한 태도로 고개를 가로저으며 대답했다."], ["And it must be confessed that on this point Emmy was very ignorant, and that is a pity some people are so knowing.", "그리고 이 점에서 에미가 몹시 무지했음은 인정하지 않을 수 없거니와, 더러 그토록 사정에 밝은 사람들이 있다는 것이 안타까운 일이기도 한 것이다."], ["All these twopenny documents arranged on a side table, old Sedley covered them carefully over with a clean bandanna handkerchief (one out of Major Dobbin's lot) and enjoined the maid and landlady of the house, in the most solemn way, not to disturb those papers, which were arranged for the arrival of Mr.", "이 모든 하찮은 서류들을 곁탁자 위에 정리해 둔 다음, 늙은 세들리는 깨끗한 인도식 손수건 한 장으로(도빈 소령에게서 받은 것 가운데 하나였다) 그것들을 정성껏 덮어 두었다."], ["Joseph Sedley the next morning, \"Mr. Joseph Sedley of the Honourable East India Company's Bengal Civil Service.\"", "그러고는 하녀와 집주인 여자에게 더없이 엄숙한 태도로, 이튿날 아침 조지프 세들리 씨, \"명예로운 동인도회사 벵골 행정청 소속 조지프 세들리 씨\"의 도착을 위해 정리해 둔 그 서류들을 절대 건드리지 말라고 단단히 일러두었다."]], [["Amelia found him up very early the next morning, more eager, more hectic, and more shaky than ever.", "이튿날 아침 애밀리아가 보니 그는 어느 때보다도 일찍 일어나, 더욱 들떠 있고, 더욱 상기되어 있고, 더욱 떨고 있었다."], ["\"I didn't sleep much, Emmy, my dear,\"", "\"잠을 통 못 잤다, 에미야, 얘야.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"I was thinking of my poor Bessy.", "\"가엾은 베시 생각을 했단다."], ["I wish she was alive, to ride in Jos's carriage once again.", "그 사람이 살아서 조스의 마차를 한 번 더 타 볼 수 있다면 얼마나 좋을까."], ["She kept her own and became it very well.\"", "제 마차를 가지고 다녔는데, 참 잘 어울렸지.\""], ["And his eyes filled with tears, which trickled down his furrowed old face.", "그의 두 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁 차오르더니 주름진 늙은 얼굴을 타고 흘러내렸다."], ["Amelia wiped them away, and smilingly kissed him, and tied the old man's neckcloth in a smart bow, and put his brooch into his best shirt frill, in which, in his Sunday suit of mourning, he sat from six o'clock in the morning awaiting the arrival of his son.", "애밀리아는 그 눈물을 닦아 주고, 미소 지으며 입을 맞추고, 노인의 목수건을 맵시 있게 나비 모양으로 매어 주고, 그의 가장 좋은 셔츠 가슴 장식에 브로치를 꽂아 주었다. 그렇게 차려입은 그는 일요일에 입는 상복 차림으로 아침 여섯 시부터 앉아 아들이 도착하기를 기다렸다."]], [["However, when the postman made his appearance, the little party were put out of suspense by the receipt of a letter from Jos to his sister, who announced that he felt a little fatigued after his voyage, and should not be able to move on that day, but that he would leave Southampton early the next morning and be with his father and mother at evening.", "그러나 우편배달부가 모습을 드러내자, 이 작은 식구는 조스가 누이에게 보낸 편지를 받고 마음 졸이던 상태에서 풀려났다. 편지에서 그는 항해 끝에 조금 피곤하여 그날은 움직일 수 없을 것 같으나, 이튿날 아침 일찍 사우샘프턴을 떠나 저녁이면 아버지와 어머니 곁에 닿겠노라고 알렸다."], ["Amelia, as she read out the letter to her father, paused over the latter word; her brother, it was clear, did not know what had happened in the family.", "애밀리아는 아버지에게 편지를 소리 내어 읽어 주다가 그 끝 대목에서 잠시 멈칫했다. 오라버니가 집안에 무슨 일이 일어났는지 모르고 있는 것이 분명했다."], ["Nor could he, for the fact is that, though the Major rightly suspected that his travelling companion never would be got into motion in so short a space as twenty-four hours, and would find some excuse for delaying, yet Dobbin had not written to Jos to inform him of the calamity which had befallen the Sedley family, being occupied in talking with Amelia until long after post-hour.", "알 리가 없었다. 사실 소령은 자기 길동무가 스물네 시간이라는 그토록 짧은 시간 안에 도무지 몸을 일으키지 못하고 무슨 핑계든 대어 미적거리리라 정확히 짐작하기는 했으나, 우편 시간이 한참 지나도록 애밀리아와 이야기 나누느라 정신이 팔려, 세들리 집안에 닥친 불행을 알리는 편지를 조스에게 써 보내지 못했던 것이다."]], [["There are some splendid tailors'", "사우샘프턴의 번화가에는 멋들어진 양복점 몇이 있는데, 그 고운 판유리 창에는 비단과 벨벳, 금빛과 진홍빛으로 된 온갖 화려한 조끼가 걸려 있고, 최신 유행을 그린 그림들이 걸려 있다."], ["shops in the High Street of Southampton, in the fine plate-glass windows of which hang gorgeous waistcoats of all sorts, of silk and velvet, and gold and crimson, and pictures of the last new fashions, in which those wonderful gentlemen with quizzing glasses, and holding on to little boys with the exceeding large eyes and curly hair, ogle ladies in riding habits prancing by the Statue of Achilles at Apsley House.", "그 그림 속에서는 외알 안경을 든 멋쟁이 신사들이, 눈이 유난히 크고 머리가 곱슬곱슬한 어린 사내아이들의 손을 잡은 채, 애플리 하우스의 아킬레스 동상 곁을 말 타고 사뿐사뿐 지나가는 승마복 차림의 숙녀들에게 추파를 던지고 있다. 조스는 캘커타에서 마련할 수 있는 가장 화려한 조끼들을 갖추고 있었음에도, 이런 옷 한두 벌을 갖추기 전에는 도시로 갈 수 없다고 여겼다."], ["Jos, although provided with some of the most splendid vests that Calcutta could furnish, thought he could not go to town until he was supplied with one or two of these garments, and selected a crimson satin, embroidered with gold butterflies, and a black and red velvet tartan with white stripes and a rolling collar, with which, and a rich blue satin stock and a gold pin, consisting of a five-barred gate with a horseman in pink enamel jumping over it, he thought he might make his entry into London with some dignity.", "그리하여 금빛 나비를 수놓은 진홍 새틴 조끼 하나와, 흰 줄무늬에 둥글게 접히는 깃이 달린 붉고 검은 벨벳 타탄 조끼 하나를 골랐다. 거기에 고급 푸른 새틴 목장식과, 분홍 에나멜을 입힌 기마인이 다섯 단짜리 울타리 문을 뛰어넘는 모양의 금 핀까지 갖추면, 어느 정도 위엄을 차리고 런던에 입성할 수 있으리라 생각했다."], ["For Jos's former shyness and blundering blushing timidity had given way to a more candid and courageous self-assertion of his worth.", "예전에 조스가 보이던 수줍음, 어물거리며 얼굴을 붉히던 소심함은 이제 자기 값어치에 대한 한결 솔직하고 당당한 자기주장으로 바뀌어 있었다. \"나는 굳이 부인하지 않겠소.\""], ["\"I don't care about owning it,\"", "워털루 세들리는 벗들에게 이렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["Waterloo Sedley would say to his friends, \"I am a dressy man\"; and though rather uneasy if the ladies looked at him at the Government House balls, and though he blushed and turned away alarmed under their glances, it was chiefly from a dread lest they should make love to him that he avoided them, being averse to marriage altogether.", "\"나는 멋 부리기를 좋아하는 사람이오.\" 그러면서도 그는 관저 무도회에서 숙녀들이 자기를 쳐다보면 다소 불편해했고, 그들의 눈길을 받으면 얼굴을 붉히며 놀라 외면했는데, 이는 주로 그들이 자기에게 연정을 품을까 봐 두려워서 피한 것이었으니, 결혼이라는 것 자체를 질색하던 사람이었다."], ["But there was no such swell in Calcutta as Waterloo Sedley, I have heard say, and he had the handsomest turn-out, gave the best bachelor dinners, and had the finest plate in the whole place.", "그러나 캘커타에 워털루 세들리만 한 멋쟁이는 없었다고들 한다. 그는 가장 멋진 마차를 갖추었고, 가장 훌륭한 독신자 만찬을 베풀었으며, 그 일대에서 가장 좋은 은식기를 소유하고 있었다."]], [["To make these waistcoats for a man of his size and dignity took at least a day, part of which he employed in hiring a servant to wait upon him and his native and in instructing the agent who cleared his baggage, his boxes, his books, which he never read, his chests of mangoes, chutney, and curry-powders, his shawls for presents to people whom he didn't know as yet, and the rest of his Persicos apparatus.", "이 조끼들을 그의 몸집과 위엄에 걸맞게 짓는 데는 적어도 하루가 걸렸는데, 그 시간의 일부를 그는 자기와 자기 인도인 하인의 시중을 들 하인 하나를 고용하는 데, 그리고 자기 짐과 상자, 한 번도 읽지 않는 책들, 망고와 처트니와 카레 가루가 든 궤짝들, 아직 알지도 못하는 사람들에게 선물할 숄들, 그 밖의 페르시아풍 호사스러운 물품 일체를 통관시킬 대리인에게 지시하는 데 썼다."]], [["At length, he drove leisurely to London on the third day and in the new waistcoat, the native, with chattering teeth, shuddering in a shawl on the box by the side of the new European servant; Jos puffing his pipe at intervals within and looking so majestic that the little boys cried Hooray, and many people thought he must be a Governor-General.", "* 페르시아풍 호사: 호라티우스의 시구 \"페르시아풍 사치를 나는 싫어하노라\"에서 따온 표현으로, 동방의 호화로운 물품을 가리킨다. 마침내 사흘째 되는 날 그는 새 조끼를 차려입고 느긋이 마차를 몰아 런던으로 향했다."], ["HE, I promise, did not decline the obsequious invitation of the landlords to alight and refresh himself in the neat country towns.", "인도인 하인은 마부석에서 새 유럽인 하인 곁에 앉아 숄을 두른 채 이를 딱딱 마주치며 떨고 있었고, 조스는 마차 안에서 이따금 파이프를 뻐끔거리며 어찌나 위풍당당한 모습이던지, 어린 사내아이들이 만세를 외쳤고 많은 사람이 그를 무슨 총독쯤으로 여겼을 정도였다."], ["Having partaken of a copious breakfast, with fish, and rice, and hard eggs, at Southampton, he had so far rallied at Winchester as to think a glass of sherry necessary.", "장담하건대, 그는 깔끔한 시골 마을마다 여관 주인들이 내려서 요기하고 가시라고 굽실거리며 청하는 것을 마다하지 않았다. 사우샘프턴에서 생선과 밥과 삶은 달걀을 곁들인 푸짐한 아침을 든 그는, 윈체스터에 이르러서는 어지간히 기운을 차린지라 셰리주 한 잔이 필요하겠다고 여겼다."], ["At Alton he stepped out of the carriage at his servant's request and imbibed some of the ale for which the place is famous.", "올턴에서는 하인의 청에 따라 마차에서 내려, 그 고장에서 유명한 에일을 들이켰다."], ["At Farnham he stopped to view the Bishop's Castle and to partake of a light dinner of stewed eels, veal cutlets, and French beans, with a bottle of claret.", "판햄에서는 주교의 성을 구경하느라 멈추고, 스튜로 끓인 장어와 송아지 고기 커틀릿과 강낭콩에 클라레 한 병을 곁들인 가벼운 식사를 들었다. 배그숏 들판을 지날 무렵에는 추위가 닥쳐, 인도인 하인은 점점 더 이를 떨었고 조스 사힙은 브랜디에 물을 타 마셨다."], ["He was cold over Bagshot Heath, where the native chattered more and more, and Jos Sahib took some brandy-and-water; in fact, when he drove into town he was as full of wine, beer, meat, pickles, cherry-brandy, and tobacco as the steward's cabin of a steam-packet.", "사실 그가 도시로 마차를 몰고 들어왔을 때는, 와인이며 맥주며 고기며 절임이며 체리브랜디며 담배로, 증기선 사무장 선실만큼이나 가득 차 있었다. 그의 마차가 브롬프턴의 작은 문 앞으로 요란하게 들이닥쳤을 때는 저녁 무렵이었다."], ["It was evening when his carriage thundered up to the little door in Brompton, whither the affectionate fellow drove first, and before hieing to the apartments secured for him by Mr. Dobbin at the Slaughters'.", "그 다정한 사내는 도빈 씨가 슬로터스 여관에 잡아 둔 숙소로 가기에 앞서, 먼저 이곳으로 마차를 몰았던 것이다."]], [["All the faces in the street were in the windows; the little maidservant flew to the wicket-gate; the Mesdames Clapp looked out from the casement of the ornamented kitchen; Emmy, in a great flutter, was in the passage among the hats and coats; and old Sedley in the parlour inside, shaking all over.", "거리의 온갖 얼굴들이 창가에 모여들었다. 어린 하녀는 쪽문으로 날아갔고, 클랩 부인 모녀는 장식 달린 부엌 여닫이창으로 내다보았다."], ["Jos descended from the post-chaise and down the creaking swaying steps in awful state, supported by the new valet from Southampton and the shuddering native, whose brown face was now livid with cold and of the colour of a turkey's gizzard.", "에미는 잔뜩 들떠서, 모자며 외투가 걸린 복도에 나와 있었고, 안쪽 거실의 늙은 세들리는 온몸을 떨고 있었다. 조스는 사우샘프턴에서 데려온 새 시종과, 갈색 얼굴이 이제는 추위로 시퍼레져 칠면조 모래주머니 빛깔이 된 채 덜덜 떠는 인도인 하인의 부축을 받으며, 위풍당당한 모습으로 역마차에서 내려 삐걱대며 흔들리는 발판을 밟고 내려섰다."], ["He created an immense sensation in the passage presently, where Mrs.", "이윽고 그는 복도에서 엄청난 소동을 일으켰다."], ["and Miss Clapp, coming perhaps to listen at the parlour door, found Loll Jewab shaking upon the hall-bench under the coats, moaning in a strange piteous way, and showing his yellow eyeballs and white teeth.", "거실 문에 귀를 대고 엿들으려고 나온 듯한 클랩 부인과 그 딸은, 외투들 아래 현관 의자에서 롤 주왑이 덜덜 떨며 묘하게 가련한 소리로 신음하고, 누런 눈알과 흰 이를 드러내고 있는 것을 발견했다."]], [["For, you see, we have adroitly shut the door upon the meeting between Jos and the old father and the poor little gentle sister inside.", "까닭인즉, 보다시피, 우리가 조스와 늙은 아버지, 그리고 안에 있던 가엾고 작고 온순한 누이의 상봉을 향해 슬며시 문을 닫아 버렸기 때문이다."], ["The old man was very much affected; so, of course, was his daughter; nor was Jos without feeling.", "노인은 몹시 감격했다. 물론 그의 딸도 그러했고, 조스 또한 감정이 북받치지 않은 것은 아니었다."], ["In that long absence of ten years, the most selfish will think about home and early ties.", "십 년이라는 그 긴 부재 동안에는, 아무리 이기적인 사람이라도 고향과 어린 시절의 인연을 떠올리는 법이다."], ["Distance sanctifies both.", "거리는 그 둘을 모두 거룩하게 만든다."], ["Long brooding over those lost pleasures exaggerates their charm and sweetness.", "잃어버린 그 즐거움들을 오래도록 곱씹다 보면 그 매력과 달콤함은 부풀려지기 마련이다."], ["Jos was unaffectedly glad to see and shake the hand of his father, between whom and himself there had been a coolness--glad to see his little sister, whom he remembered so pretty and smiling, and pained at the alteration which time, grief, and misfortune had made in the shattered old man.", "조스는 그동안 서먹한 사이였던 아버지를 보고 그 손을 잡으며 꾸밈없이 기뻐했고, 그토록 예쁘고 잘 웃던 모습으로 기억하는 누이동생을 보고 기뻐했으며, 세월과 슬픔과 불운이 저 망가진 노인에게 빚어 놓은 변화에 마음 아파했다."], ["Emmy had come out to the door in her black clothes and whispered to him of her mother's death, and not to speak of it to their father.", "에미는 검은 옷차림으로 문간에 나와, 어머니가 돌아가셨다는 것을 그에게 속삭이며 아버지 앞에서는 그 일을 입에 올리지 말라고 일렀다."], ["There was no need of this caution, for the elder Sedley himself began immediately to speak of the event, and prattled about it, and wept over it plenteously.", "그러나 그런 당부는 필요하지 않았으니, 늙은 세들리 본인이 곧장 그 일을 입에 올리기 시작하여, 재잘재잘 이야기하고, 펑펑 눈물을 쏟았기 때문이다."], ["It shocked the Indian not a little and made him think of himself less than the poor fellow was accustomed to do.", "그것은 인도에서 온 사람을 적잖이 충격에 빠뜨렸고, 그 가엾은 사내로 하여금 평소 버릇과는 달리 제 생각만 하지는 못하게 만들었다."]], [["The result of the interview must have been very satisfactory, for when Jos had reascended his post-chaise and had driven away to his hotel, Emmy embraced her father tenderly, appealing to him with an air of triumph, and asking the old man whether she did not always say that her brother had a good heart?", "그 만남의 결과는 무척 흡족한 것이었음이 틀림없다. 조스가 다시 역마차에 올라 자기 호텔로 떠나고 나자, 에미는 의기양양한 기색으로 아버지에게 호소하듯 다정하게 끌어안으며, 오라버니의 마음씨가 곱다고 자기가 늘 말하지 않았더냐고 노인에게 물었던 것이다."]], [["Indeed, Joseph Sedley, affected by the humble position in which he found his relations, and in the expansiveness and overflowing of heart occasioned by the first meeting, declared that they should never suffer want or discomfort any more, that he was at home for some time at any rate, during which his house and everything he had should be theirs: and that Amelia would look very pretty at the head of his table--until she would accept one of her own.", "과연 조지프 세들리는, 자기 식구들이 처한 초라한 형편에 마음이 움직이고, 첫 상봉이 불러일으킨 벅차오르고 넘쳐흐르는 감정에 젖어, 이제 다시는 식구들이 궁핍이나 불편을 겪는 일은 없을 것이며, 자기가 어쨌든 한동안은 본국에 머물 터이니 그동안 자기 집과 가진 것 모두를 식구들의 것으로 삼겠노라고, 그리고 애밀리아가 제 살림을 차리기 전까지는 자기 식탁의 안주인 노릇을 하면 무척 예뻐 보일 것이라고 선언했다."]], [["She shook her head sadly and had, as usual, recourse to the waterworks.", "그녀는 서글피 고개를 가로젓고, 늘 그렇듯 또 눈물 보따리를 풀었다."], ["She knew what he meant.", "그녀는 오라버니의 말뜻을 알아챘던 것이다."], ["She and her young confidante, Miss Mary, had talked over the matter most fully, the very night of the Major's visit, beyond which time the impetuous Polly could not refrain from talking of the discovery which she had made, and describing the start and tremor of joy by which Major Dobbin betrayed himself when Mr.", "그녀와 어린 속내 친구 메리 양은 바로 소령이 방문하던 그날 밤, 이 문제를 아주 속속들이 이야기 나누었다. 충동적인 폴리는 그날 밤이 지나기까지 자기가 알아낸 사실을 입에 담지 않고는 배기지 못했으니, 비니 씨가 신부를 데리고 지나가고 소령이 이제 두려워할 연적이 없음을 알게 되었을 때 도빈 소령이 깜짝 놀라며 기쁨에 떨던 모습을 본 그대로 그려 보였던 것이다."], ["Binny passed with his bride and the Major learned that he had no longer a rival to fear.", "\"글쎄, 아씨가 그분께 결혼하셨냐고 여쭈었을 때 그분이 온몸을 떨면서 '누가 그런 거짓말을 합디까?'"], ["\"Didn't you see how he shook all over when you asked if he was married and he said, 'Who told you those lies?'", "하시던 거 못 보셨어요? 아유, 아씨.\""], ["Oh, M'am,\"", "폴리가 말했다."], ["Polly said, \"he never kept his eyes off you, and I'm sure he's grown grey athinking of you.\"", "\"그분은 아씨한테서 눈을 떼질 못하셨다니까요. 분명 아씨 생각에 머리가 다 세도록 애태우신 거예요.\""]], [["But Amelia, looking up at her bed, over which hung the portraits of her husband and son, told her young protegee never, never, to speak on that subject again; that Major Dobbin had been her husband's dearest friend and her own and George's most kind and affectionate guardian; that she loved him as a brother--but that a woman who had been married to such an angel as that, and she pointed to the wall, could never think of any other union.", "그러나 애밀리아는, 남편과 아들의 초상화가 걸려 있는 침대를 올려다보며, 그 어린 보호 대상에게 다시는, 두 번 다시는 그 이야기를 입에 올리지 말라고 일렀다. 도빈 소령은 자기 남편의 가장 소중한 벗이자 자기와 조지에게 더없이 다정하고 자애로운 보호자였으며, 자기는 그를 오라비처럼 사랑한다고."], ["Poor Polly sighed: she thought what she should do if young Mr.", "그러나 저런 천사 같은 사람과 결혼했던 여자가—그러면서 그녀는 벽을 가리켰다—다른 그 누구와 다시 맺어지는 일을 생각이나 할 수 있겠느냐고. 가엾은 폴리는 한숨을 쉬었다."], ["Tomkins, at the surgery, who always looked at her so at church, and who, by those mere aggressive glances had put her timorous little heart into such a flutter that she was ready to surrender at once,--what she should do if he were to die?", "그녀는 진찰소의 그 젊은 톰킨스 씨가 죽기라도 하면 자기는 어찌해야 하나 생각했다. 교회에서 늘 자기를 그렇게 바라보고, 그저 그런 도발적인 눈길만으로도 겁 많은 자기 작은 가슴을 어찌나 두근거리게 하던지 당장이라도 항복하고 싶게 만들던 그 사람 말이다."], ["She knew he was consumptive, his cheeks were so red and he was so uncommon thin in the waist.", "그가 죽으면 어쩌나? 그녀는 그가 폐병을 앓고 있음을 알고 있었으니, 두 볼이 어찌나 붉고 허리가 어찌나 유난히 가늘던지."]], [["Not that Emmy, being made aware of the honest Major's passion, rebuffed him in any way, or felt displeased with him.", "그렇다고 에미가 그 정직한 소령의 연정을 알게 되었다 하여 어떤 식으로든 그를 매정하게 물리쳤다거나 그가 못마땅했던 것은 아니다."], ["Such an attachment from so true and loyal a gentleman could make no woman angry.", "그토록 진실하고 의리 있는 신사가 보내는 그런 사모의 정에 화를 낼 여자는 없는 법이다."], ["Desdemona was not angry with Cassio, though there is very little doubt she saw the Lieutenant's partiality for her (and I for my part believe that many more things took place in that sad affair than the worthy Moorish officer ever knew of); why, Miranda was even very kind to Caliban, and we may be pretty sure for the same reason.", "데스데모나도 캐시오에게 화를 내지 않았으니, 그녀가 그 부관이 자기에게 호감을 품은 것을 알아챘으리라는 데는 의심의 여지가 거의 없다(또한 나로서는 저 가련한 사건에서, 그 훌륭한 무어인 장교가 결코 알지 못한 일들이 훨씬 더 많이 벌어졌으리라 믿는다). 미란다조차 칼리반에게 무척 다정하지 않았던가, 그리고 우리는 그것이 같은 까닭에서였음을 거의 확신할 수 있다."], ["Not that she would encourage him in the least--the poor uncouth monster--of course not.", "그렇다고 그녀가 그를—저 가엾고 흉측한 괴물을—조금이라도 부추겼다는 말은 물론 아니다."], ["No more would Emmy by any means encourage her admirer, the Major.", "마찬가지로 에미도 결코 자기를 흠모하는 소령을 무슨 식으로든 부추길 마음은 없었다."], ["She would give him that friendly regard, which so much excellence and fidelity merited; she would treat him with perfect cordiality and frankness until he made his proposals, and THEN it would be time enough for her to speak and to put an end to hopes which never could be realized.", "그녀는 그토록 빼어난 인품과 충심에 합당한 다정한 호의를 그에게 베풀 것이요, 그가 청혼하기 전까지는 더없이 진심 어린 솔직한 태도로 그를 대할 것이며, 그때가 되면 비로소 입을 열어, 결코 이루어질 수 없는 그 희망을 끝맺을 시간도 넉넉하리라고 생각했다."]], [["She slept, therefore, very soundly that evening, after the conversation with Miss Polly, and was more than ordinarily happy, in spite of Jos's delaying.", "그리하여 그녀는 그날 밤 폴리 양과의 대화를 마치고는 아주 곤히 잠들었고, 조스가 도착을 미룬 데도 아랑곳없이 여느 때보다 더 행복했다."], ["\"I am glad he is not going to marry that Miss O'Dowd,\"", "\"그이가 그 오다우드 양과 결혼하지 않게 되어 다행이야.\""], ["she thought.", "그녀는 생각했다."], ["\"Colonel O'Dowd never could have a sister fit for such an accomplished man as Major William.\"", "\"오다우드 대령에게 윌리엄 소령처럼 교양 있는 사람에게 어울릴 누이가 있을 리 없지.\""], ["Who was there amongst her little circle who would make him a good wife?", "자기의 좁은 교제권 안에 그에게 좋은 아내가 되어 줄 사람이 누가 있을까?"], ["Not Miss Binny, she was too old and ill-tempered; Miss Osborne?", "비니 양은 아니지, 너무 나이 들고 성미가 까다로우니까."], ["too old too.", "오스본 양?"], ["Little Polly was too young. Mrs.", "그 사람도 너무 나이 들었고."], ["Osborne could not find anybody to suit the Major before she went to sleep.", "어린 폴리는 너무 어렸다. 오스본 부인은 잠들기 전까지 소령에게 어울릴 사람을 끝내 찾아내지 못했다."]], [["The same morning brought Major Dobbin a letter to the Slaughters'", "같은 날 아침, 도빈 소령에게도 사우샘프턴의 친구가 슬로터스 커피점으로 보낸 편지 한 통이 도착했다."], ["Coffee-house from his friend at Southampton, begging dear Dob to excuse Jos for being in a rage when awakened the day before (he had a confounded headache, and was just in his first sleep), and entreating Dob to engage comfortable rooms at the Slaughters'", "다정한 도브, 전날 잠에서 깨워졌을 때 화를 낸 것을 부디 너그러이 봐 달라며(지독한 두통이 있던 데다 막 첫잠이 든 참이었다는 것이다), 슬로터스에 세들리 씨와 그 하인들이 묵을 편안한 방을 잡아 달라고 도브에게 청하는 내용이었다. 항해하는 동안 소령은 조스에게 없어서는 안 될 사람이 되어 있었다."], ["for Mr. Sedley and his servants.", "조스는 그에게 정이 들었고 그에게 기댔다."], ["The Major had become necessary to Jos during the voyage.", "다른 승객들은 모두 런던으로 떠나 버렸다."], ["He was attached to him, and hung upon him. The other passengers were away to London.", "젊은 리케츠와 어린 채퍼스는 그날 역마차로 떠났는데, 리케츠는 마부석에 올라 보틀리에게서 고삐를 넘겨받았다."], ["Young Ricketts and little Chaffers went away on the coach that day--Ricketts on the box, and taking the reins from Botley; the Doctor was off to his family at Portsea; Bragg gone to town to his co-partners; and the first mate busy in the unloading of the Ramchunder. Mr.", "의사는 포트시의 식구들에게로 떠났고, 브래그는 동업자들에게로 도시로 갔으며, 일등 항해사는 램춘더호의 짐을 부리느라 바빴다."], ["Joe was very lonely at Southampton, and got the landlord of the George to take a glass of wine with him that day, at the very hour at which Major Dobbin was seated at the table of his father, Sir William, where his sister found out (for it was impossible for the Major to tell fibs) that he had been to see Mrs. George Osborne.", "조 씨는 사우샘프턴에서 몹시 외로워, 그날 조지 여관 주인을 붙잡고 함께 와인을 한 잔 들었으니, 바로 그 시각에 도빈 소령은 아버지 윌리엄 경의 식탁에 앉아 있었고, 거기서 그의 누이는 그가 조지 오스본 부인을 만나고 왔다는 것을 알아냈던 것이다(소령으로서는 거짓말을 하기가 도무지 불가능했으므로)."]], [["Jos was so comfortably situated in St. Martin's Lane, he could enjoy his hookah there with such perfect ease, and could swagger down to the theatres, when minded, so agreeably, that, perhaps, he would have remained altogether at the Slaughters'", "조스는 세인트마틴스 레인에 어찌나 안락하게 자리 잡았던지, 그곳에서 더없이 편안하게 후카를 즐길 수 있었고, 마음 내키면 어찌나 기분 좋게 어슬렁어슬렁 극장으로 내려갈 수 있었던지, 만일 친구인 소령이 곁에서 떠밀지 않았더라면 아예 슬로터스에 눌러앉아 버렸을지도 모른다."], ["had not his friend, the Major, been at his elbow.", "그 신사는 조스가 애밀리아와 아버지를 위해 집을 마련하겠다던 약속을 지킬 때까지 그 벵골 사람을 가만 놓아두지 않았다."], ["That gentleman would not let the Bengalee rest until he had executed his promise of having a home for Amelia and his father.", "조스는 누구의 손아귀에서든 물러 터진 사람이었고, 도빈은 제 일만 빼고는 누구의 일에든 가장 적극적인 사람이었다."], ["Jos was a soft fellow in anybody's hands, Dobbin most active in anybody's concerns but his own; the civilian was, therefore, an easy victim to the guileless arts of this good-natured diplomatist and was ready to do, to purchase, hire, or relinquish whatever his friend thought fit. Loll Jewab, of whom the boys about St.", "그리하여 이 행정관은 마음씨 좋은 이 술책가의 천연덕스러운 수완에 손쉬운 먹잇감이 되어, 친구가 마땅하다고 여기는 것이라면 무엇이든 하고, 사고, 빌리고, 내놓을 준비가 되어 있었다."], ["Martin's Lane used to make cruel fun whenever he showed his dusky countenance in the street, was sent back to Calcutta in the Lady Kicklebury East Indiaman, in which Sir William Dobbin had a share, having previously taught Jos's European the art of preparing curries, pilaus, and pipes.", "세인트마틴스 레인 일대의 사내아이들이 거무튀튀한 낯짝을 거리에 내밀 때마다 짓궂게 놀려 대던 롤 주왑은, 캘커타로 돌아가는 동인도회사 선박 레이디 키클베리호에 태워 돌려보내졌으니, 윌리엄 도빈 경이 그 배에 지분을 가지고 있던 터였다. 그 전에 그는 조스의 유럽인 하인에게 카레와 필래프와 파이프 만드는 법을 가르쳐 두었다."], ["It was a matter of great delight and occupation to Jos to superintend the building of a smart chariot which he and the Major ordered in the neighbouring Long Acre: and a pair of handsome horses were jobbed, with which Jos drove about in state in the park, or to call upon his Indian friends.", "조스에게는 자기와 소령이 이웃한 롱에이커에 주문해 둔 맵시 있는 사륜마차의 제작을 감독하는 일이 더없는 즐거움이자 소일거리였다. 잘생긴 말 두 마리도 삯을 주고 빌려 와서, 조스는 그것을 몰고 위풍당당하게 공원을 누비거나 인도 시절 친구들을 찾아다녔다."], ["Amelia was not seldom by his side on these excursions, when also Major Dobbin would be seen in the back seat of the carriage.", "이런 나들이에는 애밀리아가 그의 곁에 함께 타는 일도 드물지 않았고, 그럴 때면 도빈 소령이 마차 뒷자리에 앉아 있는 모습도 볼 수 있었다."], ["At other times old Sedley and his daughter took advantage of it, and Miss Clapp, who frequently accompanied her friend, had great pleasure in being recognized as she sat in the carriage, dressed in the famous yellow shawl, by the young gentleman at the surgery, whose face might commonly be seen over the window-blinds as she passed.", "또 어떤 때는 늙은 세들리와 그 딸이 마차를 이용했는데, 자주 자기 벗을 따라나서던 클랩 양은, 그 유명한 노란 숄을 두르고 마차에 앉아 진찰소의 젊은 신사에게 알아봄을 받는 것에 큰 기쁨을 느꼈다. 그녀가 지나갈 때면 그 젊은이의 얼굴이 흔히 창의 차양 너머로 보이곤 했던 것이다."]], [["Shortly after Jos's first appearance at Brompton, a dismal scene, indeed, took place at that humble cottage at which the Sedleys had passed the last ten years of their life.", "조스가 브롬프턴에 처음 모습을 드러내고 얼마 지나지 않아, 세들리 일가가 지난 십 년의 삶을 보낸 그 초라한 오두막에서는 참으로 서글픈 한 장면이 펼쳐졌다."], ["Jos's carriage (the temporary one, not the chariot under construction) arrived one day and carried off old Sedley and his daughter--to return no more.", "어느 날 조스의 마차가(만들고 있던 사륜마차가 아니라 임시로 빌린 마차였다) 도착하여, 늙은 세들리와 그 딸을 태우고 떠나갔으니—다시는 돌아오지 않을 길이었다."], ["The tears that were shed by the landlady and the landlady's daughter at that event were as genuine tears of sorrow as any that have been outpoured in the course of this history.", "그 일을 두고 집주인 여자와 그 딸이 흘린 눈물은, 이 이야기를 거쳐 오는 동안 쏟아진 그 어떤 눈물 못지않게 진정 슬픔에서 우러난 눈물이었다."], ["In their long acquaintanceship and intimacy they could not recall a harsh word that had been uttered by Amelia.", "그 오랜 사귐과 친분 속에서도 그들은 애밀리아가 모진 말 한마디 내뱉은 것을 떠올릴 수 없었다."], ["She had been all sweetness and kindness, always thankful, always gentle, even when Mrs. Clapp lost her own temper and pressed for the rent.", "그녀는 그저 더없이 상냥하고 친절했으며, 클랩 부인이 성을 내며 집세를 독촉할 때조차도 늘 고마워하고 늘 온순했다."], ["When the kind creature was going away for good and all, the landlady reproached herself bitterly for ever having used a rough expression to her--how she wept, as they stuck up with wafers on the window, a paper notifying that the little rooms so long occupied were to let!", "그 다정한 사람이 영영 떠나가게 되자, 집주인 여자는 자기가 일찍이 그녀에게 거친 말을 했던 것을 두고 쓰라리게 자책했다. 그토록 오래 쓰이던 작은 방들을 세놓는다는 종이를 창에 풀로 붙여 두면서, 그 여자가 얼마나 울었던가!"], ["They never would have such lodgers again, that was quite clear.", "다시는 그런 세입자들을 두지 못하리라는 것은 너무도 분명했다."], ["After-life proved the truth of this melancholy prophecy, and Mrs. Clapp revenged herself for the deterioration of mankind by levying the most savage contributions upon the tea-caddies and legs of mutton of her locataires.", "훗날의 삶이 이 우울한 예언이 옳았음을 증명해 주었으니, 클랩 부인은 인류가 변변치 못해진 데 대한 분풀이로, 세든 이들의 차통과 양 다리 고기에 더없이 사납게 분담금을 매겨 댔다."], ["Most of them scolded and grumbled; some of them did not pay; none of them stayed.", "그들 대부분은 잔소리하고 투덜거렸고, 더러는 돈을 내지 않았으며, 누구도 오래 머물지 않았다."], ["The landlady might well regret those old, old friends, who had left her.", "그 집주인 여자가 자기를 떠나 버린 그 오래되고 오래된 벗들을 그리워한 것도 무리는 아니었다."]], [["As for Miss Mary, her sorrow at Amelia's departure was such as I shall not attempt to depict.", "메리 양으로 말하자면, 애밀리아가 떠나는 데 대한 그 슬픔이 어떠했는지는 내가 굳이 그려 보려 들지 않으련다."], ["From childhood upwards she had been with her daily and had attached herself so passionately to that dear good lady that when the grand barouche came to carry her off into splendour, she fainted in the arms of her friend, who was indeed scarcely less affected than the good-natured girl.", "어릴 적부터 그녀는 날마다 애밀리아와 함께 지냈고, 그 다정하고 선량한 부인에게 어찌나 열렬히 마음을 붙였던지, 호화로운 사륜마차가 그녀를 화려한 곳으로 데려가려고 왔을 때, 그녀는 친구의 품에 안겨 까무러쳤다. 사실 그 친구 또한 마음씨 고운 처녀 못지않게 감정이 북받쳤던 것이다."], ["Amelia loved her like a daughter.", "애밀리아는 그녀를 딸처럼 사랑했다."], ["During eleven years the girl had been her constant friend and associate.", "십일 년 동안 그 처녀는 그녀의 변함없는 벗이자 길동무였다."], ["The separation was a very painful one indeed to her.", "그러니 이별은 그녀에게 참으로 가슴 아픈 것이었다."], ["But it was of course arranged that Mary was to come and stay often at the grand new house whither Mrs. Osborne was going, and where Mary was sure she would never be so happy as she had been in their humble cot, as Miss Clapp called it, in the language of the novels which she loved.", "그러나 물론, 메리가 오스본 부인이 옮겨 가는 그 으리으리한 새집에 자주 와서 머물기로 약속이 되었거니와, 그 집에서는 메리가 이 초라한 오두막에서—클랩 양이 자기가 좋아하는 소설의 말투로 부르던 표현을 빌리자면—누렸던 만큼 결코 행복하지는 못하리라고 그녀는 확신했다."]], [["Let us hope she was wrong in her judgement.", "부디 그녀의 판단이 틀렸기를 바라자."], ["Poor Emmy's days of happiness had been very few in that humble cot.", "가엾은 에미가 그 초라한 오두막에서 행복했던 날들은 참으로 적었다."], ["A gloomy Fate had oppressed her there.", "그곳에서는 음울한 운명이 그녀를 짓눌렀다."], ["She never liked to come back to the house after she had left it, or to face the landlady who had tyrannized over her when ill-humoured and unpaid, or when pleased had treated her with a coarse familiarity scarcely less odious.", "그녀는 그 집을 떠난 뒤로는 다시 그리로 돌아가는 것을, 또 자기를 횡포하게 굴던 그 집주인 여자와 마주하는 것을 결코 좋아하지 않았다. 그 여자는 기분이 언짢고 집세를 못 받았을 때면 그녀에게 폭군처럼 굴었고, 기분이 좋을 때면 그에 못지않게 역겨운 천박한 허물없음으로 그녀를 대했다."], ["Her servility and fulsome compliments when Emmy was in prosperity were not more to that lady's liking.", "에미가 형편이 폈을 때 보인 그 비굴함과 낯간지러운 아첨도 그 부인의 마음에 들기는 마찬가지였다."], ["She cast about notes of admiration all over the new house, extolling every article of furniture or ornament; she fingered Mrs.", "그 여자는 새집을 온통 돌아다니며 감탄사를 쏟아 내고, 가구며 장식품 하나하나를 추켜세웠다. 그녀는 오스본 부인의 옷들을 만지작거리며 그 값을 가늠해 보았다."], ["Osborne's dresses and calculated their price. Nothing could be too good for that sweet lady, she vowed and protested.", "저 사랑스러운 부인에게는 무엇이든 아무리 좋아도 지나치지 않다고, 그 여자는 맹세하고 또 다짐했다."], ["But in the vulgar sycophant who now paid court to her, Emmy always remembered the coarse tyrant who had made her miserable many a time, to whom she had been forced to put up petitions for time, when the rent was overdue; who cried out at her extravagance if she bought delicacies for her ailing mother or father; who had seen her humble and trampled upon her.", "그러나 이제 자기에게 알랑거리는 이 천박한 아첨꾼에게서, 에미는 늘 자기를 숱하게 비참하게 만들었던 그 거친 폭군을 떠올렸다. 집세가 밀렸을 때 어쩔 수 없이 말미를 달라고 사정해야 했던 상대, 병든 어머니나 아버지를 위해 별미라도 사면 사치를 부린다고 호통치던 상대, 그녀가 비굴하게 구는 것을 보고 짓밟던 그 상대를 말이다."]], [["Nobody ever heard of these griefs, which had been part of our poor little woman's lot in life.", "이 슬픔들에 대해서는 아무도 전해 듣지 못했으니, 그것은 우리의 가엾은 작은 부인이 살면서 짊어진 몫의 일부였다."], ["She kept them secret from her father, whose improvidence was the cause of much of her misery.", "그녀는 그것을 아버지에게 숨겼는데, 그 아버지의 무분별함이야말로 그녀가 겪은 비참함의 많은 부분의 원인이었다."], ["She had to bear all the blame of his misdoings, and indeed was so utterly gentle and humble as to be made by nature for a victim.", "그녀는 아버지가 저지른 일에 대한 비난을 죄다 떠안아야 했고, 사실 어찌나 더없이 온순하고 겸손했던지 천성적으로 희생양이 되도록 태어난 사람이었다."]], [["I hope she is not to suffer much more of that hard usage.", "그녀가 그런 모진 대접을 더는 많이 겪지 않기를 바란다."], ["And, as in all griefs there is said to be some consolation, I may mention that poor Mary, when left at her friend's departure in a hysterical condition, was placed under the medical treatment of the young fellow from the surgery, under whose care she rallied after a short period.", "그리고 모든 슬픔에는 어떤 위안이 깃들어 있다고들 하니, 한마디 덧붙여 두자면, 가엾은 메리는 벗이 떠나간 뒤 발작 상태에 빠져 있다가 진찰소의 그 젊은이에게 진료를 받게 되었고, 그의 보살핌 아래 얼마 지나지 않아 기운을 되찾았다."], ["Emmy, when she went away from Brompton, endowed Mary with every article of furniture that the house contained, only taking away her pictures (the two pictures over the bed) and her piano--that little old piano which had now passed into a plaintive jingling old age, but which she loved for reasons of her own.", "에미는 브롬프턴을 떠날 때, 집 안에 있던 가구를 하나도 빠짐없이 메리에게 다 주고, 다만 자기 그림들(침대 위에 걸린 두 점의 그림)과 피아노만은 가져갔다. 그 작고 오래된 피아노는 이제 구슬프게 댕그랑거리는 노년에 접어들어 있었으나, 그녀는 그것을 자기만의 까닭으로 사랑했다."], ["She was a child when first she played on it, and her parents gave it her.", "그녀가 그것을 처음 친 것은 어린아이 적이었고, 부모가 그것을 그녀에게 선물해 주었다."], ["It had been given to her again since, as the reader may remember, when her father's house was gone to ruin and the instrument was recovered out of the wreck.", "그리고 독자가 기억할지 모르나, 그 뒤 아버지의 집이 풍비박산 나고 그 악기가 그 잔해 속에서 되찾아졌을 때, 그것은 그녀에게 다시 한번 주어진 것이었다."]], [["Major Dobbin was exceedingly pleased when, as he was superintending the arrangements of Jos's new house--which the Major insisted should be very handsome and comfortable--the cart arrived from Brompton, bringing the trunks and bandboxes of the emigrants from that village, and with them the old piano.", "도빈 소령은—소령은 조스의 새집이 아주 멋지고 안락해야 한다고 우겼는데—그 집 단장을 감독하던 차에, 브롬프턴에서 손수레가 도착하여 그 마을을 떠나온 이들의 트렁크와 모자 상자들을, 그리고 그것들과 함께 그 오래된 피아노를 싣고 오자 무척 기뻐했다."], ["Amelia would have it up in her sitting-room, a neat little apartment on the second floor, adjoining her father's chamber, and where the old gentleman sat commonly of evenings.", "애밀리아는 그것을 자기 거실에 두려 했으니, 그곳은 이층에 있는 아담하고 작은 방으로 아버지 방과 붙어 있었고, 노신사가 저녁이면 흔히 앉아 있던 곳이었다."]], [["When the men appeared then bearing this old music-box, and Amelia gave orders that it should be placed in the chamber aforesaid, Dobbin was quite elated.", "이윽고 일꾼들이 이 오래된 악기 상자를 메고 나타나 애밀리아가 그것을 앞서 말한 그 방에 들이라고 분부하자, 도빈은 무척 들떴다."], ["\"I'm glad you've kept it,\"", "\"이걸 간직하고 계셨다니 기쁩니다.\""], ["he said in a very sentimental manner.", "그가 자못 감상에 젖은 태도로 말했다."], ["\"I was afraid you didn't care about it.\"", "\"마음에 두지 않으셨을까 봐 걱정했었지요.\""]], [["\"I value it more than anything I have in the world,\"", "\"저는 이걸 이 세상에 가진 그 무엇보다 소중히 여겨요.\""], ["said Amelia.", "애밀리아가 말했다."]], [["\"Do you, Amelia?\"", "\"그렇습니까, 애밀리아?\""], ["cried the Major.", "소령이 외쳤다."], ["The fact was, as he had bought it himself, though he never said anything about it, it never entered into his head to suppose that Emmy should think anybody else was the purchaser, and as a matter of course he fancied that she knew the gift came from him.", "사실인즉, 그것을 직접 산 사람은 그 자신이었으나, 그는 그에 대해 한 번도 입을 연 적이 없었으므로, 에미가 그것을 다른 누군가 산 것으로 여기리라고는 그의 머릿속에 떠오른 적조차 없었다. 그래서 그는 으레 그녀가 그 선물이 자기에게서 온 것임을 알고 있으리라 짐작했던 것이다."], ["\"Do you, Amelia?\"", "\"그렇습니까, 애밀리아?\""], ["he said; and the question, the great question of all, was trembling on his lips, when Emmy replied--", "그가 말했다. 그리고 그 물음, 무엇보다 중대한 그 물음이 그의 입술에 떨려 나오려는 찰나, 에미가 대답했다—"]], [["\"Can I do otherwise?--did not he give it me?\"", "\"어찌 그러지 않겠어요?—그이가 제게 준 것인데요?\""]], [["\"I did not know,\"", "\"저는 몰랐습니다.\""], ["said poor old Dob, and his countenance fell.", "가엾은 늙은 도브가 말했고, 그의 얼굴빛이 어두워졌다."]], [["Emmy did not note the circumstance at the time, nor take immediate heed of the very dismal expression which honest Dobbin's countenance assumed, but she thought of it afterwards.", "에미는 그때는 그 일을 알아차리지 못했고, 정직한 도빈의 얼굴에 떠오른 그 몹시 침울한 표정도 곧바로 마음에 두지 않았으나, 나중에 가서 그것을 떠올렸다."], ["And then it struck her, with inexpressible pain and mortification too, that it was William who was the giver of the piano, and not George, as she had fancied.", "그리고 그때, 형언할 수 없는 아픔과 또한 굴욕감과 함께, 그 피아노를 준 사람이 자기가 여겨 왔던 조지가 아니라 윌리엄이었다는 사실이 그녀의 가슴을 쳤다."], ["It was not George's gift; the only one which she had received from her lover, as she thought--the thing she had cherished beyond all others--her dearest relic and prize.", "그것은 조지의 선물이 아니었다. 그녀가 생각하기에 자기가 연인에게서 받은 유일한 선물—다른 그 무엇보다 애지중지하던 것—가장 소중한 유품이자 보물이었던 것인데."], ["She had spoken to it about George; played his favourite airs upon it; sat for long evening hours, touching, to the best of her simple art, melancholy harmonies on the keys, and weeping over them in silence.", "그녀는 그것을 향해 조지 이야기를 했고, 그것으로 그가 좋아하던 곡들을 쳤으며, 긴 저녁 시간을 앉아서 자기의 서툰 솜씨껏 그 건반으로 구슬픈 화음을 짚으며, 말없이 그 위에 눈물을 흘리곤 했었다."], ["It was not George's relic.", "그것은 조지의 유품이 아니었다."], ["It was valueless now.", "이제 그것은 아무 값어치도 없었다."], ["The next time that old Sedley asked her to play, she said it was shockingly out of tune, that she had a headache, that she couldn't play.", "다음번에 늙은 세들리가 한 곡 쳐 보라고 청하자, 그녀는 음이 형편없이 어긋났다느니, 머리가 아프다느니, 칠 수가 없다느니 했다."]], [["Then, according to her custom, she rebuked herself for her pettishness and ingratitude and determined to make a reparation to honest William for the slight she had not expressed to him, but had felt for his piano.", "이윽고 그녀는 늘 그러던 버릇대로, 자기의 토라짐과 배은망덕을 꾸짖고, 그 피아노를 두고 정직한 윌리엄에게—입 밖에 내지는 않았으나 마음으로 느꼈던—그 푸대접을 보상하기로 마음먹었다."], ["A few days afterwards, as they were seated in the drawing-room, where Jos had fallen asleep with great comfort after dinner, Amelia said with rather a faltering voice to Major Dobbin--", "며칠 뒤, 조스가 저녁을 먹고 더없이 편안히 잠들어 버린 응접실에 그들이 앉아 있을 때, 애밀리아가 자못 떨리는 목소리로 도빈 소령에게 말했다—"]], [["\"I have to beg your pardon for something.\"", "\"한 가지 용서를 빌 일이 있어요.\""]], [["\"About what?\"", "\"무엇 말입니까?\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"About--about that little square piano.", "\"저—저 작은 사각 피아노 말이에요."], ["I never thanked you for it when you gave it me, many, many years ago, before I was married.", "아주아주 오래전, 제가 결혼하기 전에 그걸 주셨을 때 저는 한 번도 고맙다는 말을 못 드렸어요."], ["I thought somebody else had given it.", "다른 사람이 준 거라고 여겼거든요."], ["Thank you, William.\"", "고마워요, 윌리엄.\""], ["She held out her hand, but the poor little woman's heart was bleeding; and as for her eyes, of course they were at their work.", "그녀가 손을 내밀었으나, 그 가엾은 작은 부인의 가슴은 피 흘리고 있었다. 그리고 그 두 눈으로 말하면, 물론 또 제 할 일을 하고 있었다."]], [["But William could hold no more.", "그러나 윌리엄은 더는 참을 수가 없었다."], ["\"Amelia, Amelia,\"", "\"애밀리아, 애밀리아.\""], ["he said, \"I did buy it for you.", "그가 말했다. \"내가 당신을 위해 그것을 산 게 맞습니다."], ["I loved you then as I do now.", "그때도 지금처럼 당신을 사랑했어요."], ["I must tell you.", "말해야겠습니다."], ["I think I loved you from the first minute that I saw you, when George brought me to your house, to show me the Amelia whom he was engaged to.", "나는 당신을 처음 본 그 순간부터 사랑했던 것 같습니다. 조지가 자기와 약혼한 애밀리아를 보여 주겠다고 나를 당신 집으로 데려갔을 때 말입니다."], ["You were but a girl, in white, with large ringlets; you came down singing--do you remember?--and we went to Vauxhall.", "당신은 아직 어린 처녀였고, 흰 옷을 입고 큼직한 곱슬머리를 늘어뜨린 채, 노래를 부르며 내려왔지요—기억하세요?—그리고 우리는 복스홀에 갔었지요."], ["Since then I have thought of but one woman in the world, and that was you.", "그 뒤로 나는 이 세상에서 단 한 여인만을 생각해 왔으니, 그게 바로 당신이었습니다."], ["I think there is no hour in the day has passed for twelve years that I haven't thought of you.", "지난 십이 년 동안 하루도, 당신을 생각하지 않고 지나간 시간은 없었던 것 같습니다."], ["I came to tell you this before I went to India, but you did not care, and I hadn't the heart to speak.", "인도로 떠나기 전에 이 말을 하려고 당신을 찾아갔었지만, 당신은 마음에 두지 않았고, 나는 차마 입을 뗄 용기가 없었지요."], ["You did not care whether I stayed or went.\"", "당신은 내가 머물든 떠나든 아랑곳하지 않았으니까요.\""]], [["\"I was very ungrateful,\"", "\"제가 참 배은망덕했어요.\""], ["Amelia said.", "애밀리아가 말했다."]], [["\"No, only indifferent,\"", "\"아니요, 그저 무심했을 뿐입니다.\""], ["Dobbin continued desperately.", "도빈이 자포자기한 듯 말을 이었다."], ["\"I have nothing to make a woman to be otherwise.", "\"내게는 여자가 달리 마음먹게 만들 만한 구석이 없으니까요."], ["I know what you are feeling now.", "지금 당신이 무엇을 느끼고 있는지 압니다."], ["You are hurt in your heart at the discovery about the piano, and that it came from me and not from George.", "그 피아노를 두고 알게 된 사실에, 그게 조지가 아니라 나에게서 온 것이라는 데에 마음이 상해 있는 거지요."], ["I forgot, or I should never have spoken of it so.", "내가 깜빡했지, 그렇지 않았다면 결코 그런 식으로 그 이야기를 꺼내지 않았을 겁니다."], ["It is for me to ask your pardon for being a fool for a moment, and thinking that years of constancy and devotion might have pleaded with you.\"", "잠시나마 어리석은 사람이 되어, 여러 해의 한결같음과 헌신이 당신의 마음을 움직였을지도 모른다고 여긴 것에 대해 오히려 내가 용서를 빌어야겠군요.\""]], [["\"It is you who are cruel now,\"", "\"이제 잔인하신 건 당신이에요.\""], ["Amelia said with some spirit.", "애밀리아가 다소 발끈하여 말했다."], ["\"George is my husband, here and in heaven.", "\"조지는 이 땅에서도 하늘에서도 제 남편이에요."], ["How could I love any other but him?", "어찌 제가 그이 아닌 다른 누구를 사랑할 수 있겠어요?"], ["I am his now as when you first saw me, dear William.", "저는 당신이 저를 처음 보았던 그때와 똑같이 지금도 그이의 사람이에요, 윌리엄."], ["It was he who told me how good and generous you were, and who taught me to love you as a brother.", "당신이 얼마나 선량하고 너그러운 사람인지 제게 일러 준 것도 그이였고, 당신을 오라비처럼 사랑하라고 가르쳐 준 것도 그이였어요."], ["Have you not been everything to me and my boy?", "당신은 저와 제 아이에게 모든 것이 되어 주지 않았나요?"], ["Our dearest, truest, kindest friend and protector?", "우리의 가장 소중하고, 가장 진실하고, 가장 다정한 벗이자 보호자가 아니었나요?"], ["Had you come a few months sooner perhaps you might have spared me that--that dreadful parting.", "당신이 몇 달만 더 일찍 와 주었더라면 어쩌면 제게 그—그 끔찍한 이별을 면하게 해 주었을지도 모르는데."], ["Oh, it nearly killed me, William--but you didn't come, though I wished and prayed for you to come, and they took him too away from me.", "아, 그것은 저를 거의 죽일 뻔했어요, 윌리엄—하지만 당신은 오지 않았어요, 제가 와 달라고 그토록 바라고 기도했는데도. 그리고 그들은 그 애마저 제게서 데려가 버렸어요."], ["Isn't he a noble boy, William?", "그 애는 고귀한 아이지요, 윌리엄?"], ["Be his friend still and mine\"--and here her voice broke, and she hid her face on his shoulder.", "부디 변함없이 그 애의, 그리고 저의 벗으로 남아 주세요\"—여기서 그녀의 목소리가 메었고, 그녀는 그의 어깨에 얼굴을 묻었다."]], [["The Major folded his arms round her, holding her to him as if she was a child, and kissed her head.", "소령은 두 팔로 그녀를 감싸, 마치 그녀가 어린아이라도 되는 양 자기 품에 끌어안고, 그 머리에 입을 맞추었다."], ["\"I will not change, dear Amelia,\"", "\"변치 않겠습니다, 사랑하는 애밀리아.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"I ask for no more than your love.", "\"당신의 사랑보다 더 많은 것은 바라지 않습니다."], ["I think I would not have it otherwise.", "그 이상은 바라지 않는 편이 차라리 나을 것 같습니다."], ["Only let me stay near you and see you often.\"", "그저 당신 곁에 머물며 자주 당신을 볼 수 있게만 해 주세요.\""]], [["\"Yes, often,\"", "\"네, 자주요.\""], ["Amelia said.", "애밀리아가 말했다."], ["And so William was at liberty to look and long--as the poor boy at school who has no money may sigh after the contents of the tart-woman's tray.", "그리하여 윌리엄은 마음껏 바라보고 마음껏 그리워해도 좋게 되었으니—돈 한 푼 없는 가엾은 학교 소년이 파이 장수 쟁반에 담긴 것들을 두고 한숨짓는 것이나 다를 바 없는 일이었다."]], [["Returns to the Genteel World\nGood fortune now begins to smile upon Amelia.", "이제 행운이 애밀리아에게 미소 짓기 시작했다."], ["We are glad to get her out of that low sphere in which she has been creeping hitherto and introduce her into a polite circle--not so grand and refined as that in which our other female friend, Mrs.", "우리는 그녀를 여태껏 기어다니던 그 비천한 세계에서 끌어내어 점잖은 사교계로 안내하게 되어 기쁘다. 우리의 또 다른 여자 친구 베키 부인이 모습을 드러냈던 그곳만큼 으리으리하고 세련된 곳은 아니지만, 그래도 품위와 유행을 자처하기에 결코 손색없는 그런 곳이었다."], ["Becky, has appeared, but still having no small pretensions to gentility and fashion. Jos's friends were all from the three presidencies, and his new house was in the comfortable Anglo-Indian district of which Moira Place is the centre.", "조스의 친구들은 모두 인도의 세 관구 출신이었고, 그의 새집은 모이라 플레이스를 중심으로 한 그 안락한 영국계 인도인 지구에 있었다."], ["Minto Square, Great Clive Street, Warren Street, Hastings Street, Ochterlony Place, Plassy Square, Assaye Terrace (\"gardens\" was a felicitous word not applied to stucco houses with asphalt terraces in front, so early as 1827)--who does not know these respectable abodes of the retired Indian aristocracy, and the quarter which Mr.", "민토 광장, 그레이트 클라이브 거리, 워런 거리, 헤이스팅스 거리, 옥털로니 플레이스, 플래시 광장, 어세이 테라스(\"가든\"이라는 운치 있는 말이, 앞쪽에 아스팔트 테라스를 깐 회벽칠한 집들에까지 붙은 것은 1827년 무렵엔 아직 아니었다)—이 은퇴한 인도 귀족들의 점잖은 거처들을, 그리고 한마디로 웨넘 씨가 블랙홀이라 부르는 그 동네를 누가 모르겠는가?"], ["Wenham calls the Black Hole, in a word?", "조스의 사회적 지위는 모이라 플레이스에 집을 가질 만큼 대단하지는 않았다."], ["Jos's position in life was not grand enough to entitle him to a house in Moira Place, where none can live but retired Members of Council, and partners of Indian firms (who break, after having settled a hundred thousand pounds on their wives, and retire into comparative penury to a country place and four thousand a year); he engaged a comfortable house of a second- or third-rate order in Gillespie Street, purchasing the carpets, costly mirrors, and handsome and appropriate planned furniture by Seddons from the assignees of Mr.", "그곳에는 은퇴한 참사회 의원이나 인도 상사의 동업자들(아내 앞으로 십만 파운드를 떼어 놓은 뒤 파산하고, 연 사천 파운드 수입에 시골집 하나를 두고 상대적인 빈곤 속으로 은퇴하는 부류)밖에 살 수 없었기 때문이다. 그는 길레스피 거리에 자리한, 이류나 삼류쯤 되는 안락한 집 한 채를 빌렸고, 카펫과 값비싼 거울들과 세든스가 만든 보기 좋고 알맞게 설계된 가구들을 스케이프 씨의 재산 관리인들에게서 사들였다."], ["Scape, lately admitted partner into the great Calcutta House of Fogle, Fake, and Cracksman, in which poor Scape had embarked seventy thousand pounds, the earnings of a long and honourable life, taking Fake's place, who retired to a princely park in Sussex (the Fogles have been long out of the firm, and Sir Horace Fogle is about to be raised to the peerage as Baron Bandanna)--admitted, I say, partner into the great agency house of Fogle and Fake two years before it failed for a million and plunged half the Indian public into misery and ruin.", "스케이프 씨로 말하자면, 얼마 전 캘커타의 대상사인 포글, 페이크 앤드 크랙스맨 상회의 동업자로 받아들여진 사람이었는데, 가엾은 스케이프는 거기에 자신의 길고 떳떳한 평생의 벌이인 칠만 파운드를 쏟아부었고, 서식스의 어느 으리으리한 사유지로 은퇴한 페이크의 자리를 대신한 것이었다(포글 집안은 오래전에 그 상사에서 손을 뗐고, 호러스 포글 경은 곧 밴대너 남작으로 작위를 받아 귀족이 될 참이다)—그러니까 내 말은, 포글 앤드 페이크라는 그 대규모 대리상회의 동업자로 받아들여졌는데, 그 상사가 백만 파운드의 빚을 지고 무너지며 인도 대중의 절반을 비참과 파멸 속으로 몰아넣기 꼭 이 년 전의 일이었다."]], [["Scape, ruined, honest, and broken-hearted at sixty-five years of age, went out to Calcutta to wind up the affairs of the house.", "스케이프는 파산하고, 정직하고, 예순다섯의 나이에 상심에 잠긴 채 상사의 일을 정리하러 캘커타로 떠났다."], ["Walter Scape was withdrawn from Eton and put into a merchant's house.", "월터 스케이프는 이튼에서 자퇴하고 어느 상인의 집에 들어갔다."], ["Florence Scape, Fanny Scape, and their mother faded away to Boulogne, and will be heard of no more.", "플로런스 스케이프와 패니 스케이프, 그리고 그들의 어머니는 불로뉴로 스러져 갔고, 다시는 그들의 소식을 듣지 못할 것이다."], ["To be brief, Jos stepped in and bought their carpets and sideboards and admired himself in the mirrors which had reflected their kind handsome faces.", "간단히 말해, 조스가 끼어들어 그들의 카펫과 찬장을 사들였고, 그들의 다정하고 잘생긴 얼굴을 비추던 거울 앞에서 제 모습을 흐뭇하게 들여다본 것이다."], ["The Scape tradesmen, all honourably paid, left their cards, and were eager to supply the new household.", "스케이프네 상인들은 모두 떳떳이 대금을 받은 터라 명함을 두고 갔고, 새 살림에 물건을 대고 싶어 안달이었다."], ["The large men in white waistcoats who waited at Scape's dinners, greengrocers, bank-porters, and milkmen in their private capacity, left their addresses and ingratiated themselves with the butler. Mr.", "스케이프네 만찬 때 시중들던 흰 조끼 차림의 덩치 큰 사내들, 즉 사적인 신분으로는 채소 장수요 은행 짐꾼이요 우유 장수인 자들이 저마다 주소를 남기고 집사의 환심을 사려 들었다."], ["Chummy, the chimney-purifier, who had swept the last three families, tried to coax the butler and the boy under him, whose duty it was to go out covered with buttons and with stripes down his trousers, for the protection of Mrs. Amelia whenever she chose to walk abroad.", "마지막 세 가문의 굴뚝을 청소해 온 굴뚝 청소부 처미 씨는, 집사와 그 아래 소년의 비위를 맞추려 애썼는데, 그 소년의 임무란 애밀리아 부인이 바깥나들이를 할 때면 그분을 호위하기 위해 단추가 줄줄이 달리고 바짓가랑이에 줄무늬가 들어간 옷을 입고 나서는 것이었다."]], [["It was a modest establishment.", "소박한 살림이었다."], ["The butler was Jos's valet also, and never was more drunk than a butler in a small family should be who has a proper regard for his master's wine.", "집사는 조스의 시종까지 겸했고, 주인의 와인을 마땅히 아낄 줄 아는, 작은 집안의 집사가 으레 취하는 정도 이상으로는 결코 취하지 않았다."], ["Emmy was supplied with a maid, grown on Sir William Dobbin's suburban estate; a good girl, whose kindness and humility disarmed Mrs.", "에미에게는 윌리엄 도빈 경의 교외 영지에서 자란 하녀 하나가 붙여졌는데, 착한 처녀였다."], ["Osborne, who was at first terrified at the idea of having a servant to wait upon herself, who did not in the least know how to use one, and who always spoke to domestics with the most reverential politeness.", "그 친절함과 겸손함이 오즈번 부인의 경계심을 풀어 주었으니, 부인은 처음엔 자기 시중을 들어 줄 하인을 둔다는 생각에 겁부터 났고, 하인을 어떻게 부려야 하는지 도무지 몰랐으며, 늘 하인들에게 더없이 공손하고 정중하게 말을 건넸던 것이다."], ["But this maid was very useful in the family, in dexterously tending old Mr.", "그러나 이 하녀는 세들리 노인의 시중을 솜씨 좋게 드는 일로 집안에 큰 보탬이 되었다."], ["Sedley, who kept almost entirely to his own quarter of the house and never mixed in any of the gay doings which took place there.", "노인은 집의 자기 구역에만 거의 틀어박혀 지냈고, 그곳에서 벌어지는 그 어떤 흥겨운 일에도 끼지 않았다."]], [["Numbers of people came to see Mrs. Osborne.", "수많은 사람이 오즈번 부인을 보러 찾아왔다."], ["Lady Dobbin and daughters were delighted at her change of fortune, and waited upon her.", "도빈 부인과 딸들은 그녀의 달라진 운수에 기뻐하며 그녀를 방문했다."], ["Miss Osborne from Russell Square came in her grand chariot with the flaming hammer-cloth emblazoned with the Leeds arms.", "러셀 광장의 오즈번 양은 리즈 가문의 문장을 화려하게 새긴 마부석 덮개가 번쩍이는 으리으리한 사륜마차를 타고 왔다."], ["Jos was reported to be immensely rich.", "조스는 어마어마한 부자라고 소문이 났다."], ["Old Osborne had no objection that Georgy should inherit his uncle's property as well as his own.", "오즈번 노인은 조지가 제 재산뿐 아니라 외삼촌의 재산까지 물려받는 데 이의가 없었다."], ["\"Damn it, we will make a man of the feller,\"", "\"젠장, 우리가 그 녀석을 어엿한 사내로 만들어 줘야지.\""], ["he said; \"and I'll see him in Parliament before I die.", "그가 말했다. \"내 죽기 전에 그 녀석이 의회에 들어가는 걸 봐야겠어."], ["You may go and see his mother, Miss O., though I'll never set eyes on her\": and Miss Osborne came.", "네가 가서 그 애 어미를 만나도 좋다, 오 양아. 나는 그 여자를 두 번 다시 안 볼 테지만 말이다.\""], ["Emmy, you may be sure, was very glad to see her, and so be brought nearer to George.", "그리하여 오즈번 양이 찾아왔다. 짐작하다시피 에미는 그녀를 만나 더없이 기뻤으니, 그로써 조지에게 한 발짝 더 가까워졌기 때문이다."], ["That young fellow was allowed to come much more frequently than before to visit his mother.", "그 어린 친구는 전보다 훨씬 자주 어머니를 보러 오도록 허락받았다."], ["He dined once or twice a week in Gillespie Street and bullied the servants and his relations there, just as he did in Russell Square.", "그는 일주일에 한두 번씩 길레스피 거리에서 저녁을 먹었고, 러셀 광장에서 그러듯 거기서도 하인들과 친척들을 윽박질렀다."]], [["He was always respectful to Major Dobbin, however, and more modest in his demeanour when that gentleman was present.", "그러나 그는 도빈 소령에게만은 늘 깍듯했고, 그 신사가 자리에 있을 때면 처신도 한결 얌전해졌다."], ["He was a clever lad and afraid of the Major.", "그는 영리한 소년이었고 소령을 두려워했다."], ["George could not help admiring his friend's simplicity, his good humour, his various learning quietly imparted, his general love of truth and justice.", "조지는 친구의 소박함과 좋은 성품, 조용히 나누어 주는 해박한 학식, 진실과 정의를 두루 사랑하는 마음을 흠모하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["He had met no such man as yet in the course of his experience, and he had an instinctive liking for a gentleman.", "그는 여태껏 살아오며 그런 사람을 만난 적이 없었고, 본능적으로 신사를 좋아했던 것이다."], ["He hung fondly by his godfather's side, and it was his delight to walk in the parks and hear Dobbin talk.", "그는 대부 곁에 다정하게 붙어 다녔고, 공원을 거닐며 도빈의 이야기를 듣는 것을 더없는 낙으로 삼았다."], ["William told George about his father, about India and Waterloo, about everything but himself.", "윌리엄은 조지에게 그의 아버지 이야기며 인도와 워털루 이야기를 들려주었는데, 자기 자신에 관한 것만 빼고 무엇이든 다 들려주었다."], ["When George was more than usually pert and conceited, the Major made jokes at him, which Mrs. Osborne thought very cruel.", "조지가 유난히 건방지고 우쭐댈 때면 소령은 그를 두고 농담을 던지곤 했는데, 오즈번 부인은 그것을 몹시 야박하다고 여겼다."], ["One day, taking him to the play, and the boy declining to go into the pit because it was vulgar, the Major took him to the boxes, left him there, and went down himself to the pit.", "어느 날, 그를 데리고 연극을 보러 갔는데 소년이 일반석이 천박하다며 들어가기를 마다하자, 소령은 그를 특별석으로 데려가 거기 남겨 두고 자기는 일반석으로 내려갔다."], ["He had not been seated there very long before he felt an arm thrust under his and a dandy little hand in a kid glove squeezing his arm.", "그가 거기 앉은 지 얼마 안 되어, 누군가 자기 팔 밑으로 팔을 끼우고 새끼염소 가죽 장갑을 낀 멋쟁이 같은 작은 손이 제 팔을 꼭 쥐는 것을 느꼈다."], ["George had seen the absurdity of his ways and come down from the upper region.", "조지가 제 행동이 우스꽝스러움을 깨닫고 위층 자리에서 내려온 것이었다."], ["A tender laugh of benevolence lighted up old Dobbin's face and eyes as he looked at the repentant little prodigal.", "뉘우치는 그 어린 탕아를 바라보는 도빈 노인의 얼굴과 두 눈에는 인자하고 부드러운 웃음이 환히 피어올랐다."], ["He loved the boy, as he did everything that belonged to Amelia.", "그는 애밀리아에게 속한 모든 것을 사랑하듯 그 소년을 사랑했다."], ["How charmed she was when she heard of this instance of George's goodness!", "조지의 이 착한 행실에 관한 이야기를 들었을 때 그녀가 얼마나 기뻐했던가!"], ["Her eyes looked more kindly on Dobbin than they ever had done.", "그녀의 두 눈이 그 어느 때보다 다정하게 도빈을 바라보았다."], ["She blushed, he thought, after looking at him so.", "그렇게 그를 바라본 뒤 그녀가 얼굴을 붉혔다고, 그는 생각했다."]], [["Georgy never tired of his praises of the Major to his mother.", "조지는 어머니에게 소령을 칭찬하는 데 도무지 싫증을 내지 않았다. \"난 그 아저씨가 좋아요, 엄마."], ["\"I like him, Mamma, because he knows such lots of things; and he ain't like old Veal, who is always bragging and using such long words, don't you know?", "워낙 아는 게 많거든요. 그리고 늘 으스대면서 그렇게 긴 단어만 써 대는 늙은 빌 선생하곤 영 딴판이에요, 아시겠어요?"], ["The chaps call him 'Longtail'", "학교 애들은 그 선생을 '꼬랑지'라고 불러요."], ["at school.", "내가 붙여 준 별명인데, 기막히지 않아요?"], ["I gave him the name; ain't it capital?", "그런데 도브 아저씨는 라틴어를 영어처럼 술술 읽고, 프랑스어도 그렇고, 그런 걸 다 한다니까요. 그리고 우리가 같이 나가면 우리 아빠 이야기를 들려주지, 자기 얘긴 절대 안 해요."], ["But Dob reads Latin like English, and French and that; and when we go out together he tells me stories about my Papa, and never about himself; though I heard Colonel Buckler, at Grandpapa's, say that he was one of the bravest officers in the army, and had distinguished himself ever so much.", "그래도 난 할아버지 댁에서 버클러 대령님이 그러는 걸 들었는걸요. 도브 아저씨가 군대에서 가장 용감한 장교 중 하나였고 무척이나 큰 공을 세웠다고요."], ["Grandpapa was quite surprised, and said, 'THAT feller!", "할아버지는 깜짝 놀라서 그러셨어요. '그 녀석이!"], ["Why, I didn't think he could say Bo to a goose'--but I know he could, couldn't he, Mamma?\"", "아니, 난 그자가 거위 한 마리 앞에서도 찍소리 못 낼 줄 알았다'고요. 하지만 난 아저씨가 그럴 수 있다는 걸 알아요, 그렇죠 엄마?\""]], [["Emmy laughed: she thought it was very likely the Major could do thus much.", "에미는 웃었다. 소령이 그 정도는 충분히 할 수 있으리라 생각한 것이다."]], [["If there was a sincere liking between George and the Major, it must be confessed that between the boy and his uncle no great love existed.", "조지와 소령 사이에 진실한 호감이 있었던 것이라면, 그 소년과 외삼촌 사이에는 그다지 큰 정이 없었다는 것을 인정해야겠다."], ["George had got a way of blowing out his cheeks, and putting his hands in his waistcoat pockets, and saying, \"God bless my soul, you don't say so,\" so exactly after the fashion of old Jos that it was impossible to refrain from laughter.", "조지는 양 볼을 부풀리고 두 손을 조끼 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채 \"맙소사, 설마 그럴 리가\"라고 말하는 버릇이 있었는데, 그것이 조스 노인의 투를 어찌나 똑같이 흉내 낸 것인지 도저히 웃음을 참을 수가 없었다."], ["The servants would explode at dinner if the lad, asking for something which wasn't at table, put on that countenance and used that favourite phrase.", "식탁에 없는 무언가를 달라고 하면서 그 소년이 그런 표정을 짓고 그 애용하는 말투를 쓰면, 하인들은 만찬 자리에서 그만 웃음을 터뜨리고 마는 것이었다."], ["Even Dobbin would shoot out a sudden peal at the boy's mimicry.", "도빈조차 소년의 흉내에 별안간 웃음을 한바탕 터뜨리곤 했다."], ["If George did not mimic his uncle to his face, it was only by Dobbin's rebukes and Amelia's terrified entreaties that the little scapegrace was induced to desist.", "조지가 외삼촌의 면전에서 그를 흉내 내지 않은 것은 오로지 도빈의 꾸지람과 애밀리아의 겁에 질린 간청 덕분이었으니, 그 작은 말썽꾼은 그제야 마지못해 그만두는 것이었다."], ["And the worthy civilian being haunted by a dim consciousness that the lad thought him an ass, and was inclined to turn him into ridicule, used to be extremely timorous and, of course, doubly pompous and dignified in the presence of Master Georgy.", "그리고 이 점잖은 문관은 그 소년이 자기를 멍청이로 여기고 우스갯거리로 삼으려 든다는 어렴풋한 의식에 사로잡혀, 어린 조지 도련님 앞에서는 몹시 주눅이 들었고, 당연히 그만큼 곱절로 거드름을 피우며 위엄을 부리곤 했다."], ["When it was announced that the young gentleman was expected in Gillespie Street to dine with his mother, Mr. Jos commonly found that he had an engagement at the Club.", "그 젊은 도련님이 어머니와 저녁을 먹으러 길레스피 거리에 오기로 했다는 전갈이 오면, 조스 씨는 으레 클럽에 약속이 있다는 것을 발견하곤 했다."], ["Perhaps nobody was much grieved at his absence. On those days Mr.", "아마 그의 부재를 그리 서운해한 이는 아무도 없었을 것이다."], ["Sedley would commonly be induced to come out from his place of refuge in the upper stories, and there would be a small family party, whereof Major Dobbin pretty generally formed one.", "그런 날이면 세들리 씨는 으레 위층의 은신처에서 나오도록 권유받았고, 그리하여 단출한 가족 모임이 이루어졌는데, 도빈 소령은 거의 빠짐없이 그 일원이 되었다."], ["He was the ami de la maison--old Sedley's friend, Emmy's friend, Georgy's friend, Jos's counsel and adviser.", "그는 이 집안의 벗이었으니—세들리 노인의 벗이요, 에미의 벗이요, 조지의 벗이요, 조스의 자문이자 조언자였다."], ["\"He might almost as well be at Madras for anything WE see of him,\"", "\"우리가 그분을 보는 걸로 치면 마드라스에 가 계신 거나 마찬가지지.\""], ["Miss Ann Dobbin remarked at Camberwell.", "캠버웰에서 앤 도빈 양이 말했다."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["Miss Ann, did it not strike you that it was not YOU whom the Major wanted to marry?", "앤 양, 소령이 결혼하고 싶어 한 사람이 당신은 아니라는 생각이 들지 않던가요?"]], [["Joseph Sedley then led a life of dignified otiosity such as became a person of his eminence.", "그리하여 조지프 세들리는 그 지체 높은 인물에게 걸맞은, 위엄 있는 무위도식의 나날을 보냈다."], ["His very first point, of course, was to become a member of the Oriental Club, where he spent his mornings in the company of his brother Indians, where he dined, or whence he brought home men to dine.", "물론 그가 가장 먼저 한 일은 오리엔탈 클럽의 회원이 되는 것이었으니, 그곳에서 그는 같은 인도 출신 동료들과 어울려 오전을 보냈고, 그곳에서 저녁을 먹거나 그곳에서 사람들을 집으로 데려와 저녁을 대접했다."]], [["Amelia had to receive and entertain these gentlemen and their ladies.", "애밀리아는 이 신사들과 그 부인들을 맞이하고 대접해야 했다."], ["From these she heard how soon Smith would be in Council; how many lacs Jones had brought home with him, how Thomson's House in London had refused the bills drawn by Thomson, Kibobjee, and Co., the Bombay House, and how it was thought the Calcutta House must go too; how very imprudent, to say the least of it, Mrs.", "그녀는 이들에게서 듣게 되었다."], ["Brown's conduct (wife of Brown of the Ahmednuggur Irregulars) had been with young Swankey of the Body Guard, sitting up with him on deck until all hours, and losing themselves as they were riding out at the Cape; how Mrs. Hardyman had had out her thirteen sisters, daughters of a country curate, the Rev: Felix Rabbits, and married eleven of them, seven high up in the service; how Hornby was wild because his wife would stay in Europe, and Trotter was appointed Collector at Ummerapoora.", "스미스가 머지않아 참사회 의원이 되리라는 것, 존스가 얼마나 많은 락의 돈을 챙겨 왔는지, 런던의 톰슨 상회가 봄베이의 톰슨, 키봅지 앤드 코 상회가 발행한 어음을 부도 처리했다는 것, 그리고 캘커타 상회 또한 무너질 것이라 여겨진다는 것, 적어도 점잖게 말해도 너무나 경솔했던 브라운 부인(아메드누거 비정규군 브라운의 아내)의 처신, 즉 친위대의 젊은 스왱키와 밤늦도록 갑판에 앉아 있었고 케이프에서 함께 말을 타고 나갔다가 길을 잃었다는 것, 하디먼 부인이 시골 부목사인 펠릭스 래비츠 목사의 딸들인 자기 자매 열셋을 데려와 그중 열하나를 시집보냈고 일곱은 관직 높은 자리에 시집보냈다는 것, 혼비가 아내가 유럽에 눌러앉으려 해서 길길이 날뛰었다는 것, 트로터가 우메라푸라의 징세관으로 임명되었다는 것을 말이다."], ["This and similar talk took place at the grand dinners all round.", "이런저런 비슷한 이야기들이 사방의 성대한 만찬에서 오갔다."], ["They had the same conversation; the same silver dishes; the same saddles of mutton, boiled turkeys, and entrees.", "다들 똑같은 대화를 나누었고, 똑같은 은접시에, 똑같은 양고기 안심 구이며 삶은 칠면조며 앙트레가 나왔다."], ["Politics set in a short time after dessert, when the ladies retired upstairs and talked about their complaints and their children.", "후식이 끝나고 얼마 지나지 않아 정치 이야기가 시작되면, 부인들은 위층으로 물러가 자기네 병치레와 아이들 이야기를 나누었다."]], [["Mutato nomine, it is all the same.", "이름만 바꾸면 다 매한가지인 것이다."], ["Don't the barristers' wives talk about Circuit?", "변호사 부인들은 순회재판 이야기를 하지 않던가?"], ["Don't the soldiers' ladies gossip about the Regiment?", "군인 부인들은 연대 이야기를 수다 떨지 않던가?"], ["Don't the clergymen's ladies discourse about Sunday-schools and who takes whose duty?", "목사 부인들은 주일학교며 누가 누구 일을 대신 맡는지를 논하지 않던가?"], ["Don't the very greatest ladies of all talk about that small clique of persons to whom they belong?", "가장 지체 높은 귀부인들조차 자기들이 속한 그 작은 패거리 이야기를 하지 않던가?"], ["And why should our Indian friends not have their own conversation?--only I admit it is slow for the laymen whose fate it sometimes is to sit by and listen.", "그런데 어찌하여 우리 인도 친구들만은 자기네 이야기를 하면 안 된단 말인가?—다만 곁에 앉아 들어야 하는 신세가 더러 되는 문외한에게는 그것이 따분한 일임을 나도 인정한다."]], [["Before long Emmy had a visiting-book, and was driving about regularly in a carriage, calling upon Lady Bludyer (wife of Major-General Sir Roger Bludyer, K.C.B., Bengal Army); Lady Huff, wife of Sir G.", "머지않아 에미는 방문 명부를 갖추고 정기적으로 마차를 타고 돌아다니며 사람들을 찾았다. 블러디어 부인(벵골군 소장 로저 블러디어 경, 바스 훈작사의 아내), 허프 부인(봄베이군의 G."], ["Huff, Bombay ditto; Mrs. Pice, the Lady of Pice the Director, &c.", "허프 경의 아내), 파이스 부인(중역 파이스의 부인) 등을 방문한 것이다."], ["We are not long in using ourselves to changes in life.", "우리는 인생의 변화에 익숙해지는 데 오래 걸리지 않는 법이다."], ["That carriage came round to Gillespie Street every day; that buttony boy sprang up and down from the box with Emmy's and Jos's visiting-cards; at stated hours Emmy and the carriage went for Jos to the Club and took him an airing; or, putting old Sedley into the vehicle, she drove the old man round the Regent's Park.", "그 마차는 날마다 길레스피 거리로 돌아왔고, 그 단추 달린 소년은 에미와 조스의 명함을 들고 마부석에서 폴짝폴짝 오르내렸으며, 정해진 시각이면 에미는 마차를 타고 클럽으로 조스를 데리러 가 그에게 바람을 쐬어 주었다. 또는 세들리 노인을 마차에 태우고 리젠트 파크를 한 바퀴 돌게 해 드렸다."], ["The lady's maid and the chariot, the visiting-book and the buttony page, became soon as familiar to Amelia as the humble routine of Brompton.", "하녀와 사륜마차, 방문 명부와 단추 달린 사동은, 곧 브롬프턴에서의 소박한 일상만큼이나 애밀리아에게 친숙한 것이 되었다."], ["She accommodated herself to one as to the other.", "그녀는 이쪽에 적응했듯 저쪽에도 적응했다."], ["If Fate had ordained that she should be a Duchess, she would even have done that duty too.", "만약 운명이 그녀를 공작부인으로 점지했더라면, 그녀는 그 소임마저 능히 해냈을 것이다."], ["She was voted, in Jos's female society, rather a pleasing young person--not much in her, but pleasing, and that sort of thing.", "조스의 여성 사교계에서 그녀는 제법 호감 가는 젊은 여자라는 평을 들었다—대단할 건 없지만 호감이 가고, 뭐 그런 부류라는 것이었다."]], [["The men, as usual, liked her artless kindness and simple refined demeanour.", "남자들은 으레 그렇듯 그녀의 꾸밈없는 친절함과 소박하고 세련된 처신을 좋아했다."], ["The gallant young Indian dandies at home on furlough--immense dandies these--chained and moustached--driving in tearing cabs, the pillars of the theatres, living at West End hotels--nevertheless admired Mrs.", "휴가를 받아 본국에 돌아온 멋쟁이 인도 청년들—실로 대단한 멋쟁이들이었으니—목줄 같은 장식에 콧수염을 기르고, 요란한 이륜마차를 몰며, 극장의 기둥서방 노릇을 하고, 웨스트엔드의 호텔에 묵는 그자들조차 오즈번 부인을 흠모하여, 공원에서 그녀의 마차에 인사하기를 좋아했고, 오전에 그녀를 방문하는 영예를 누리게 되기를 좋아했다."], ["Osborne, liked to bow to her carriage in the park, and to be admitted to have the honour of paying her a morning visit.", "그 위험한 청년이자 지금 휴가를 받아 와 있는 인도군 전체에서 가장 멋쟁이인 친위대의 스왱키 본인도, 어느 날 도빈 소령에게 애밀리아와 단둘이 마주 앉아 있는 모습을 들켰는데, 그는 그녀에게 멧돼지 사냥의 묘미를 무척이나 유쾌하고 능변으로 늘어놓고 있었다."], ["Swankey of the Body Guard himself, that dangerous youth, and the greatest buck of all the Indian army now on leave, was one day discovered by Major Dobbin tete-a-tete with Amelia, and describing the sport of pig-sticking to her with great humour and eloquence; and he spoke afterwards of a d--d king's officer that's always hanging about the house--a long, thin, queer-looking, oldish fellow--a dry fellow though, that took the shine out of a man in the talking line.", "그러고는 나중에 이렇게 말했다. 이 집에 늘 빌붙어 있는 빌어먹을 국왕 직속 장교 하나가 있는데—길쭉하고 비쩍 마르고 묘하게 생긴 늙수그레한 자라며—그래도 만만찮은 자라서, 말솜씨로는 사람을 영 무색하게 만든다는 것이었다."]], [["Had the Major possessed a little more personal vanity he would have been jealous of so dangerous a young buck as that fascinating Bengal Captain.", "소령이 개인적인 허영심을 조금만 더 가졌더라면, 저 매혹적인 벵골 대위처럼 위험한 멋쟁이 청년을 질투했을 것이다."], ["But Dobbin was of too simple and generous a nature to have any doubts about Amelia.", "그러나 도빈은 애밀리아에 대해 추호의 의심도 품기에는 너무나 소박하고 너그러운 천성이었다."], ["He was glad that the young men should pay her respect, and that others should admire her.", "그는 젊은 남자들이 그녀에게 경의를 표하고 다른 이들이 그녀를 흠모하는 것이 기뻤다."], ["Ever since her womanhood almost, had she not been persecuted and undervalued?", "그녀가 여인이 된 이래로 거의 줄곧, 박해받고 푸대접받아 오지 않았던가?"], ["It pleased him to see how kindness bought out her good qualities and how her spirits gently rose with her prosperity.", "그는 친절함이 그녀의 좋은 자질들을 끌어내고, 형편이 나아짐에 따라 그녀의 기운이 은은히 살아나는 것을 보는 게 흐뭇했다."], ["Any person who appreciated her paid a compliment to the Major's good judgement--that is, if a man may be said to have good judgement who is under the influence of Love's delusion.", "그녀를 알아주는 사람은 누구든 소령의 안목을 칭찬하는 셈이었다—말하자면, 사랑의 미혹에 빠진 사내에게 안목이 있다고 할 수 있다면 말이다."]], [["After Jos went to Court, which we may be sure he did as a loyal subject of his Sovereign (showing himself in his full court suit at the Club, whither Dobbin came to fetch him in a very shabby old uniform) he who had always been a staunch Loyalist and admirer of George IV, became such a tremendous Tory and pillar of the State that he was for having Amelia to go to a Drawing-room, too.", "조스가 궁정 알현을 하러 간 뒤로—군주의 충성스러운 신하로서 그가 그리했으리라는 것은 우리도 익히 짐작할 수 있는데(궁정 정장을 완전히 차려입고 클럽에 나타났고, 도빈은 무척 낡고 초라한 옛 군복 차림으로 그를 데리러 왔다)—늘 확고한 왕당파요 조지 4세의 숭배자였던 그는, 이제 어마어마한 토리당원이자 국가의 기둥이 되어, 애밀리아도 궁정 접견식에 데려가야 한다고 했다."], ["He somehow had worked himself up to believe that he was implicated in the maintenance of the public welfare and that the Sovereign would not be happy unless Jos Sedley and his family appeared to rally round him at St. James's.", "그는 어쩐 일인지, 자신이 공공의 안녕을 지키는 데 한몫 끼어 있으며, 조스 세들리와 그 가족이 세인트제임스 궁에 나타나 군주를 옹위하지 않으면 그분이 흡족해하지 못하리라고 스스로 믿어 버린 것이었다."]], [["Emmy laughed.", "에미는 웃었다."], ["\"Shall I wear the family diamonds, Jos?\"", "\"집안 다이아몬드라도 걸고 갈까요, 조스 오빠?\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"I wish you would let me buy you some,\"", "\"내가 좀 사 줄 수 있게 해 주면 좋으련만.\""], ["thought the Major.", "소령은 생각했다."], ["\"I should like to see any that were too good for you.\"", "\"당신한테 과분한 물건이 어디 있을지 어디 한번 보고 싶군.\""]], [["In Which Two Lights are Put Out", "칠십일장."]], [["There came a day when the round of decorous pleasures and solemn gaieties in which Mr. Jos Sedley's family indulged was interrupted by an event which happens in most houses.", "* 칠십일장: 원문의 챕터 번호는 LXI(61)이나, 본문 첫머리 제목은 그대로 옮긴다."], ["As you ascend the staircase of your house from the drawing towards the bedroom floors, you may have remarked a little arch in the wall right before you, which at once gives light to the stair which leads from the second story to the third (where the nursery and servants'", "세상의 대부분 집에서 일어나는 그런 일이 닥쳐, 조스 세들리 씨 가족이 빠져 있던 점잖은 즐거움과 엄숙한 흥겨움의 순환이 중단되는 날이 찾아왔다. 응접실 층에서 침실 층으로 집 안 계단을 올라가다 보면, 바로 눈앞 벽에 자그마한 아치가 나 있는 것을 눈여겨본 적이 있을 것이다."], ["chambers commonly are) and serves for another purpose of utility, of which the undertaker's men can give you a notion.", "그것은 이 층에서 (대개 아이 방과 하인 방이 있는) 삼 층으로 통하는 계단에 빛을 들이는 동시에, 또 다른 쓸모를 위해 있는 것이기도 한데, 그 쓸모란 장의사 일꾼들이 일러 줄 만한 것이다."], ["They rest the coffins upon that arch, or pass them through it so as not to disturb in any unseemly manner the cold tenant slumbering within the black ark.", "그들은 그 아치 위에 관을 받쳐 두거나, 그 사이로 관을 통과시키는데, 검은 방주 안에서 잠든 차디찬 입주자를 행여 점잖지 못하게 흔들어 깨우지 않으려는 것이다."]], [["That second-floor arch in a London house, looking up and down the well of the staircase and commanding the main thoroughfare by which the inhabitants are passing; by which cook lurks down before daylight to scour her pots and pans in the kitchen; by which young master stealthily ascends, having left his boots in the hall, and let himself in after dawn from a jolly night at the Club; down which miss comes rustling in fresh ribbons and spreading muslins, brilliant and beautiful, and prepared for conquest and the ball; or Master Tommy slides, preferring the banisters for a mode of conveyance, and disdaining danger and the stair; down which the mother is fondly carried smiling in her strong husband's arms, as he steps steadily step by step, and followed by the monthly nurse, on the day when the medical man has pronounced that the charming patient may go downstairs; up which John lurks to bed, yawning, with a sputtering tallow candle, and to gather up before sunrise the boots which are awaiting him in the passages--that stair, up or down which babies are carried, old people are helped, guests are marshalled to the ball, the parson walks to the christening, the doctor to the sick-room, and the undertaker's men to the upper floor--what a memento of Life, Death, and Vanity it is--that arch and stair--if you choose to consider it, and sit on the landing, looking up and down the well!", "런던 집의 그 이 층 아치, 계단 통로의 위아래를 굽어보며, 사는 이들이 오가는 중심 통로를 한눈에 거느린 그 아치. 날이 밝기도 전에 요리사가 부엌에서 냄비와 솥을 닦으려고 그 아래로 살그머니 내려가고, 젊은 주인이 현관에 장화를 벗어 두고 클럽에서의 흥겨운 밤을 보낸 뒤 동틀 무렵 몰래 들어와 그 위로 슬그머니 올라가며, 아가씨가 산뜻한 리본을 두르고 모슬린 자락을 펼치며 눈부시고 아름답게, 정복과 무도회를 채비하고 사락사락 그 아래로 내려오고, 토미 도련님이 위험도 계단도 아랑곳 않고 난간을 운반 수단 삼아 그 아래로 미끄러져 내려오며, 의사 선생이 그 매력적인 환자가 이제 아래층으로 내려가도 좋다고 선언한 날, 한 걸음 한 걸음 묵직하게 내딛는 건장한 남편의 두 팔에 안겨 산후 간호사를 거느린 채 어머니가 미소 지으며 사랑스레 그 아래로 옮겨지고, 존이 지지직거리는 수지 양초를 들고 하품을 하며 그 위로 자러 슬그머니 올라갔다가 해 뜨기 전에 복도에서 자기를 기다리는 장화를 거두어 가는 그 통로."], ["The doctor will come up to us too for the last time there, my friend in motley. The nurse will look in at the curtains, and you take no notice--and then she will fling open the windows for a little and let in the air.", "그 위로, 혹은 그 아래로 갓난아기가 안겨 오르내리고, 노인이 부축을 받으며, 손님이 무도회로 인도되고, 목사가 세례식을 위해 걸음을 옮기고, 의사가 병실로, 장의사 일꾼들이 위층으로 향하는 그 계단."], ["Then they will pull down all the front blinds of the house and live in the back rooms--then they will send for the lawyer and other men in black, &c.", "그것은 — 그 아치와 계단은 — 그대가 굳이 헤아려 보고자 한다면, 계단참에 앉아 통로의 위아래를 바라본다면, 삶과 죽음과 허영의 얼마나 절절한 기념물이란 말인가! 알록달록한 옷을 입은 나의 벗이여, 그 의사 선생은 마지막으로 그곳에서 우리에게도 올라올 것이다."], ["Your comedy and mine will have been played then, and we shall be removed, oh, how far, from the trumpets, and the shouting, and the posture-making.", "간호사가 휘장 사이로 들여다봐도 그대는 아랑곳하지 않을 테고, 그러면 그녀는 잠시 창문을 활짝 열어젖혀 공기를 들일 것이다. 그런 다음 사람들은 집의 정면 차양을 모두 끌어내리고 뒷방에서 지낼 것이며, 그러고는 변호사며 검은 옷을 입은 다른 사람들 따위를 부르러 보낼 것이다."], ["If we are gentlefolks they will put hatchments over our late domicile, with gilt cherubim, and mottoes stating that there is \"Quiet in Heaven.\"", "그대의 희극도 나의 희극도 그때면 다 끝났을 테고, 우리는 나팔 소리와 환호와 거드름 피우는 몸짓에서, 오, 얼마나 멀리, 치워져 있을 것인가. 우리가 양반붙이라면 사람들은 우리가 살던 거처 위에 도금한 천사상과 \"천국에는 안식이 있도다\"라고 적힌 문구를 곁들인 상장(喪章)을 걸어 둘 것이다."], ["Your son will new furnish the house, or perhaps let it, and go into a more modern quarter; your name will be among the \"Members Deceased\"", "그대의 아들은 집을 새로 단장하거나, 어쩌면 세를 놓고 더 현대적인 동네로 옮겨 갈 것이며, 그대의 이름은 이듬해 그대가 속한 클럽의 명부에서 \"사망한 회원\""], ["in the lists of your clubs next year.", "가운데 오를 것이다."], ["However much you may be mourned, your widow will like to have her weeds neatly made--the cook will send or come up to ask about dinner--the survivor will soon bear to look at your picture over the mantelpiece, which will presently be deposed from the place of honour, to make way for the portrait of the son who reigns.", "그대가 아무리 애도를 받는다 한들, 그대의 미망인은 자기 상복이 맵시 있게 지어지기를 바랄 것이고, 요리사는 만찬에 관해 묻고자 사람을 보내거나 올라올 것이며, 살아남은 자는 머잖아 벽난로 위에 걸린 그대의 초상화를 견딜 만하게 바라보게 될 것이다. 그 초상화는 이윽고 그 영예로운 자리에서 끌어내려져, 이제 군림하는 아들의 초상화에 자리를 내어 줄 것이다."]], [["Which of the dead are most tenderly and passionately deplored?", "죽은 이들 가운데 누가 가장 애틋하고 격렬하게 애도받는가?"], ["Those who love the survivors the least, I believe.", "살아남은 이들을 가장 덜 사랑한 이들이라고, 나는 생각한다."], ["The death of a child occasions a passion of grief and frantic tears, such as your end, brother reader, will never inspire.", "자식의 죽음은 격정적인 슬픔과 미친 듯한 눈물을 자아내는데, 형제 같은 독자여, 그대의 임종은 결코 그런 것을 불러일으키지 못할 것이다."], ["The death of an infant which scarce knew you, which a week's absence from you would have caused to forget you, will strike you down more than the loss of your closest friend, or your first-born son--a man grown like yourself, with children of his own.", "그대를 거의 알지도 못했고, 그대와 일주일만 떨어져 있어도 그대를 잊었을 갓난아기의 죽음은, 그대의 가장 가까운 벗이나 맏아들 — 자기 자식까지 거느린, 그대와 같은 어엿한 성인 — 을 잃는 것보다 더 그대를 무너뜨릴 것이다."], ["We may be harsh and stern with Judah and Simeon--our love and pity gush out for Benjamin, the little one.", "우리는 유다와 시므온에게는 모질고 준엄할지 모르나, 우리의 사랑과 연민은 막내인 어린 베냐민에게로 솟구쳐 흐른다."], ["And if you are old, as some reader of this may be or shall be old and rich, or old and poor--you may one day be thinking for yourself--\"These people are very good round about me, but they won't grieve too much when I am gone.", "그리고 그대가 늙었다면 — 이 글을 읽는 누군가는 이미 늙고 부유하거나, 늙고 가난하거나, 혹은 장차 그리될지도 모르니 — 그대는 어느 날 혼자 이런 생각을 할지도 모른다. \"내 주위의 이 사람들은 참 착하지만, 내가 떠나도 그리 깊이 슬퍼하지는 않을 게야."], ["I am very rich, and they want my inheritance--or very poor, and they are tired of supporting me.\"", "나는 무척 부유하고, 저들은 내 유산을 원하지. 아니면 내가 무척 가난해서, 저들은 나를 부양하는 데 지쳤거나.\""]], [["The period of mourning for Mrs. Sedley's death was only just concluded, and Jos scarcely had had time to cast off his black and appear in the splendid waistcoats which he loved, when it became evident to those about Mr.", "세들리 부인의 죽음에 대한 애도 기간이 막 끝나고, 조스가 미처 상복을 벗어 던지고 그가 그토록 좋아하던 화려한 조끼 차림으로 나설 새도 없을 무렵, 세들리 씨 곁의 사람들에게는 또 다른 사건이 임박했다는 것이, 그리고 노인이 자기보다 먼저 떠난 아내를 찾아 그 어두운 나라로 가려 한다는 것이 분명해졌다."], ["Sedley that another event was at hand, and that the old man was about to go seek for his wife in the dark land whither she had preceded him.", "\"아버님 건강 상태가,\" 조스 세들리는 클럽에서 엄숙하게 말하곤 했다."], ["\"The state of my father's health,\"", "\"이번 철에는 도무지 큰 연회를 열 수가 없다네."], ["Jos Sedley solemnly remarked at the Club, \"prevents me from giving any LARGE parties this season: but if you will come in quietly at half-past six, Chutney, my boy, and fake a homely dinner with one or two of the old set--I shall be always glad to see you.\"", "하지만 처트니, 이 사람아, 자네가 여섯 시 반에 조용히 와서 옛 친구 한둘과 함께 소박한 저녁을 들어 준다면, 나야 언제든 반갑게 맞이하지.\""], ["So Jos and his acquaintances dined and drank their claret among themselves in silence, whilst the sands of life were running out in the old man's glass upstairs.", "그리하여 조스와 그의 지인들은 위층에서 노인의 생명의 모래시계가 다해 가는 동안, 자기들끼리 말없이 만찬을 들고 클라레를 마셨다."], ["The velvet-footed butler brought them their wine, and they composed themselves to a rubber after dinner, at which Major Dobbin would sometimes come and take a hand; and Mrs.", "벨벳처럼 발소리 없는 집사가 그들에게 포도주를 가져다주었고, 그들은 만찬 뒤 휘스트 한 판으로 자리를 가다듬었는데, 거기에는 이따금 도빈 소령이 와서 한 손 끼기도 했다."], ["Osborne would occasionally descend, when her patient above was settled for the night, and had commenced one of those lightly troubled slumbers which visit the pillow of old age.", "그리고 오스본 부인도 이따금 내려왔으니, 위층의 그녀 환자가 잠자리에 들고 노년의 베개를 찾아드는, 그 가볍게 뒤척이는 선잠 가운데 하나에 빠져들었을 때였다."]], [["The old man clung to his daughter during this sickness.", "노인은 이 병중에 딸에게 매달렸다."], ["He would take his broths and medicines from scarcely any other hand.", "거의 다른 어떤 손으로도 미음이며 약을 받으려 들지 않았다."], ["To tend him became almost the sole business of her life.", "그를 돌보는 일이 거의 그녀 삶의 유일한 일이 되었다."], ["Her bed was placed close by the door which opened into his chamber, and she was alive at the slightest noise or disturbance from the couch of the querulous invalid.", "그녀의 침상은 그의 방으로 통하는 문 바로 곁에 놓였고, 그녀는 그 까탈스러운 병자의 침상에서 들려오는 아주 작은 소리나 기척에도 깨어났다."], ["Though, to do him justice, he lay awake many an hour, silent and without stirring, unwilling to awaken his kind and vigilant nurse.", "하지만 노인을 위해 공정히 말하자면, 그는 친절하고 빈틈없는 자기 간병인을 깨우고 싶지 않아, 말없이 꼼짝도 않고 여러 시간 깨어 누워 있곤 했다."]], [["He loved his daughter with more fondness now, perhaps, than ever he had done since the days of her childhood.", "그는 이제, 어쩌면 그녀의 어린 시절 이래로 그 어느 때보다 더 애틋하게 딸을 사랑했다."], ["In the discharge of gentle offices and kind filial duties, this simple creature shone most especially.", "다정한 시중과 자식 된 자의 갸륵한 도리를 다하는 데서, 이 소박한 사람은 그 무엇보다 빛났다."], ["\"She walks into the room as silently as a sunbeam,\"", "\"햇살처럼 소리 없이 방으로 걸어 들어오는군.\""], ["Mr. Dobbin thought as he saw her passing in and out from her father's room, a cheerful sweetness lighting up her face as she moved to and fro, graceful and noiseless.", "도빈 씨는 그녀가 아버지의 방을 드나드는 모습을, 우아하고 소리 없이 이리저리 움직일 때 그 얼굴을 환히 밝히는 명랑한 다정함을 바라보며 그렇게 생각했다."], ["When women are brooding over their children, or busied in a sick-room, who has not seen in their faces those sweet angelic beams of love and pity?", "여인들이 제 아이를 어르고 굽어볼 때나 병실에서 분주할 때, 그 얼굴에 어린 그 달콤하고 천사 같은 사랑과 연민의 광채를 보지 못한 이가 어디 있겠는가?"]], [["A secret feud of some years' standing was thus healed, and with a tacit reconciliation.", "이렇게 하여 몇 해 묵은 은밀한 반목이 치유되었으니, 암묵적인 화해와 더불어서였다."], ["In these last hours, and touched by her love and goodness, the old man forgot all his grief against her, and wrongs which he and his wife had many a long night debated: how she had given up everything for her boy; how she was careless of her parents in their old age and misfortune, and only thought of the child; how absurdly and foolishly, impiously indeed, she took on when George was removed from her.", "이 마지막 시간에, 그녀의 사랑과 선량함에 마음이 움직여, 노인은 그녀에게 품었던 온갖 서운함과, 그가 아내와 더불어 숱한 긴 밤을 두고 따지던 잘못들을 모두 잊었다. 그녀가 어떻게 자기 아들을 위해 모든 것을 내던졌는지, 부모가 노년의 불운에 처했을 때 어떻게 그들을 등한히 하고 오로지 그 아이만 생각했는지, 조지가 그녀에게서 떼어져 나갔을 때 어떻게 그토록 터무니없고 어리석게, 참으로 불경스럽게 슬퍼했는지."], ["Old Sedley forgot these charges as he was making up his last account, and did justice to the gentle and uncomplaining little martyr.", "늙은 세들리는 자기 마지막 셈을 치르면서 이런 책망들을 잊고, 그 온화하고 불평 없는 작은 순교자에게 정당한 평가를 베풀었다."], ["One night when she stole into his room, she found him awake, when the broken old man made his confession.", "어느 밤 그녀가 그의 방으로 살그머니 들어가니, 그가 깨어 있는 것을 보았고, 그때 그 쇠잔한 노인은 자기 고백을 했다."], ["\"Oh, Emmy, I've been thinking we were very unkind and unjust to you,\"", "\"오, 에미야, 우리가 너에게 참 매정하고 부당했다는 생각이 드는구나.\""], ["he said and put out his cold and feeble hand to her.", "그가 말하며 차갑고 힘없는 손을 그녀에게 내밀었다."], ["She knelt down and prayed by his bedside, as he did too, having still hold of her hand.", "그녀는 그의 침상 곁에 무릎 꿇고 기도했고, 그도 여전히 그녀의 손을 잡은 채 함께 기도했다."], ["When our turn comes, friend, may we have such company in our prayers!", "벗이여, 우리 차례가 올 때, 우리도 기도 가운데 그런 길동무를 곁에 두기를!"]], [["Perhaps as he was lying awake then, his life may have passed before him--his early hopeful struggles, his manly successes and prosperity, his downfall in his declining years, and his present helpless condition--no chance of revenge against Fortune, which had had the better of him--neither name nor money to bequeath--a spent-out, bootless life of defeat and disappointment, and the end here!", "어쩌면 그때 그가 깨어 누워 있는 동안, 그의 삶이 눈앞을 스쳐 지나갔을지도 모른다. 그 젊은 날의 희망에 찬 분투, 사내다운 성공과 번영, 노쇠한 만년의 몰락, 그리고 지금의 속수무책인 처지 — 자기를 이긴 운명에 복수할 기회도 없고, 물려줄 이름도 돈도 없으며, 패배와 실망으로 다 타 버린 부질없는 삶, 그리고 그 끝이 여기로다!"], ["Which, I wonder, brother reader, is the better lot, to die prosperous and famous, or poor and disappointed?", "형제 같은 독자여, 어느 쪽이 나은 운명일지 나는 궁금하다. 번영하고 명망 있게 죽는 것일까, 아니면 가난하고 실망한 채 죽는 것일까?"], ["To have, and to be forced to yield; or to sink out of life, having played and lost the game?", "가진 채로 그것을 내놓도록 강요당하는 것과, 승부를 벌여 패한 채로 삶에서 가라앉는 것 가운데?"], ["That must be a strange feeling, when a day of our life comes and we say, \"To-morrow, success or failure won't matter much, and the sun will rise, and all the myriads of mankind go to their work or their pleasure as usual, but I shall be out of the turmoil.\"", "우리 생의 어느 하루가 닥쳐와 이렇게 말할 때면, 그것은 분명 야릇한 느낌일 것이다. \"내일이면 성공도 실패도 별로 중요치 않을 것이고, 해는 떠오를 것이며, 무수한 인류는 여느 때처럼 제 일과 제 즐거움을 찾아갈 테지만, 나는 그 소란 바깥에 있겠지.\""]], [["So there came one morning and sunrise when all the world got up and set about its various works and pleasures, with the exception of old John Sedley, who was not to fight with fortune, or to hope or scheme any more, but to go and take up a quiet and utterly unknown residence in a churchyard at Brompton by the side of his old wife.", "그리하여 온 세상이 일어나 저마다의 갖가지 일과 즐거움에 나서는 어느 아침과 해돋이가 찾아왔으니, 다만 늙은 존 세들리만은 예외였다. 그는 더는 운명과 싸우지도, 바라지도, 궁리하지도 않고, 다만 브롬튼의 한 묘지로 가서, 옛 아내 곁에 고요하고 더없이 알려지지 않은 거처를 마련하게 되었다."]], [["Major Dobbin, Jos, and Georgy followed his remains to the grave, in a black cloth coach.", "도빈 소령과 조스와 조지가 검은 천을 두른 마차를 타고 그의 유해를 무덤까지 따라갔다."], ["Jos came on purpose from the Star and Garter at Richmond, whither he retreated after the deplorable event.", "조스는 그 비통한 사건 뒤로 물러나 있던 리치먼드의 스타 앤드 가터에서 일부러 왔다."], ["He did not care to remain in the house, with the--under the circumstances, you understand.", "그는 그 집에 머물고 싶어 하지 않았으니 — 그 정황으로 보아, 무슨 말인지 짐작이 갈 것이다."], ["But Emmy stayed and did her duty as usual.", "그러나 에미는 남아서 여느 때처럼 제 도리를 다했다."], ["She was bowed down by no especial grief, and rather solemn than sorrowful.", "그녀는 어떤 각별한 슬픔에 짓눌리지도 않았고, 비통하다기보다는 오히려 엄숙했다."], ["She prayed that her own end might be as calm and painless, and thought with trust and reverence of the words which she had heard from her father during his illness, indicative of his faith, his resignation, and his future hope.", "그녀는 자신의 임종도 그처럼 평온하고 고통 없기를 기도했으며, 아버지가 병중에 자신에게 들려준 말들, 그의 믿음과 체념과 미래의 소망을 드러내던 그 말들을 신뢰와 경외로 되새겼다."]], [["Yes, I think that will be the better ending of the two, after all.", "그렇다, 따지고 보면 둘 중 그쪽이 더 나은 끝맺음이라고 나는 생각한다."], ["Suppose you are particularly rich and well-to-do and say on that last day, \"I am very rich; I am tolerably well known; I have lived all my life in the best society, and thank Heaven, come of a most respectable family.", "그대가 유달리 부유하고 넉넉하여 그 마지막 날에 이렇게 말한다고 치자. \"나는 무척 부유하다."], ["I have served my King and country with honour.", "나는 제법 이름이 알려져 있다."], ["I was in Parliament for several years, where, I may say, my speeches were listened to and pretty well received.", "나는 평생 최상류 사교계에서 살아왔고, 하늘에 감사하게도 더없이 점잖은 가문 출신이다. 나는 명예롭게 임금과 나라를 섬겼다."], ["I don't owe any man a shilling: on the contrary, I lent my old college friend, Jack Lazarus, fifty pounds, for which my executors will not press him.", "나는 여러 해 의회에 몸담았고, 거기서 내 연설이 경청되고 제법 호평을 받았다고 말할 수 있다. 나는 누구에게도 단 한 푼 빚지지 않았다."], ["I leave my daughters with ten thousand pounds apiece--very good portions for girls; I bequeath my plate and furniture, my house in Baker Street, with a handsome jointure, to my widow for her life; and my landed property, besides money in the funds, and my cellar of well-selected wine in Baker Street, to my son.", "외려 옛 대학 동무 잭 라자루스에게 오십 파운드를 빌려주었는데, 내 유언 집행자들도 그에게 갚으라 다그치지 않을 것이다. 나는 두 딸에게 각기 만 파운드씩을 남기니 — 처녀들에게는 아주 훌륭한 몫이다."], ["I leave twenty pound a year to my valet; and I defy any man after I have gone to find anything against my character.\"", "나의 식기와 가구, 베이커 거리의 내 집은, 넉넉한 과부 급여와 더불어 내 미망인에게 평생 누리도록 물려준다. 그리고 나의 토지와, 공채에 들어 있는 돈과, 베이커 거리의 잘 골라 채운 포도주 저장고는 내 아들에게 물려준다."], ["Or suppose, on the other hand, your swan sings quite a different sort of dirge and you say, \"I am a poor blighted, disappointed old fellow, and have made an utter failure through life.", "나는 내 시종에게 연 이십 파운드를 남기며, 내가 떠난 뒤 누구든 내 인품에서 흠 하나라도 잡아낼 수 있거든 어디 한번 잡아 보라.\" 아니면 그 반대로, 그대의 백조가 사뭇 다른 가락의 만가(輓歌)를 부르며 이렇게 말한다고 치자."], ["I was not endowed either with brains or with good fortune, and confess that I have committed a hundred mistakes and blunders.", "\"나는 시들고 실망한 가련한 늙은이로, 평생 철저히 실패하고 말았다. 나는 두뇌도 행운도 타고나지 못했고, 백 가지 실수와 실책을 저질렀음을 고백한다."], ["I own to having forgotten my duty many a time.", "나는 숱하게 내 도리를 잊었음을 인정한다."], ["I can't pay what I owe.", "나는 진 빚도 갚을 수 없다."], ["On my last bed I lie utterly helpless and humble, and I pray forgiveness for my weakness and throw myself, with a contrite heart, at the feet of the Divine Mercy.\"", "나는 마지막 자리에 속수무책으로, 비천하게 누워, 내 약함에 대한 용서를 빌며, 뉘우치는 마음으로 신의 자비 발치에 나를 내던진다.\""], ["Which of these two speeches, think you, would be the best oration for your own funeral?", "이 두 연설 가운데 어느 쪽이 그대 자신의 장례에 가장 어울리는 식사(式辭)일 것 같은가?"], ["Old Sedley made the last; and in that humble frame of mind, and holding by the hand of his daughter, life and disappointment and vanity sank away from under him.", "늙은 세들리는 후자를 택했다. 그리고 그 겸허한 마음가짐으로, 딸의 손을 잡은 채, 삶과 실망과 허영이 그의 발밑에서 스러져 갔다."]], [["\"You see,\"", "\"보아라,\""], ["said old Osborne to George, \"what comes of merit, and industry, and judicious speculations, and that.", "늙은 오스본이 조지에게 말했다. \"공덕과 근면과 분별 있는 투자며 그런 것에서 무엇이 나오는지."], ["Look at me and my banker's account.", "나와 내 은행 계좌를 보아라."], ["Look at your poor Grandfather Sedley and his failure.", "네 가엾은 외할아버지 세들리와 그의 파산을 보아라."], ["And yet he was a better man than I was, this day twenty years--a better man, I should say, by ten thousand pound.\"", "그래도 이십 년 전 오늘날에는 그가 나보다 나은 사람이었지 — 그래, 만 파운드만큼은 나은 사람이었다고 해 두자.\""]], [["Beyond these people and Mr. Clapp's family, who came over from Brompton to pay a visit of condolence, not a single soul alive ever cared a penny piece about old John Sedley, or remembered the existence of such a person.", "이 사람들과, 조의를 표하러 브롬튼에서 건너온 클랩 씨네 식구 말고는, 살아 있는 어느 누구도 늙은 존 세들리를 단 한 푼어치도 마음에 두거나, 그런 사람이 존재했다는 것을 기억하지 않았다."]], [["When old Osborne first heard from his friend Colonel Buckler (as little Georgy had already informed us) how distinguished an officer Major Dobbin was, he exhibited a great deal of scornful incredulity and expressed his surprise how ever such a feller as that should possess either brains or reputation.", "늙은 오스본이 (어린 조지가 이미 우리에게 일러 준 대로) 자기 벗 버클러 대령에게서 도빈 소령이 얼마나 뛰어난 장교인지 처음 들었을 때, 그는 한껏 비웃음 섞인 의혹을 드러내며 그깟 사내 따위가 어떻게 두뇌든 명성이든 갖추었겠느냐고 놀라움을 표했다."], ["But he heard of the Major's fame from various members of his society.", "그러나 그는 자기 사교계의 여러 사람에게서 소령의 명성을 거듭 들었다."], ["Sir William Dobbin had a great opinion of his son and narrated many stories illustrative of the Major's learning, valour, and estimation in the world's opinion.", "윌리엄 도빈 경은 자기 아들을 대단히 높이 평가하며, 소령의 학식과 무용과 세평을 보여 주는 이야기를 숱하게 들려주었다."], ["Finally, his name appeared in the lists of one or two great parties of the nobility, and this circumstance had a prodigious effect upon the old aristocrat of Russell Square.", "마침내 소령의 이름이 귀족들의 큰 연회 한두 곳의 명단에 올랐고, 이 사실은 러셀 광장의 그 늙은 귀족 행세꾼에게 엄청난 영향을 미쳤다."]], [["The Major's position, as guardian to Georgy, whose possession had been ceded to his grandfather, rendered some meetings between the two gentlemen inevitable; and it was in one of these that old Osborne, a keen man of business, looking into the Major's accounts with his ward and the boy's mother, got a hint, which staggered him very much, and at once pained and pleased him, that it was out of William Dobbin's own pocket that a part of the fund had been supplied upon which the poor widow and the child had subsisted.", "조지의 후견인으로서 — 조지의 보호권은 외할아버지에게 양도된 터였다 — 소령의 처지로 인해 두 신사 사이의 몇 차례 만남은 불가피했다. 그리고 그 만남 가운데 한 번, 빈틈없는 사업가인 늙은 오스본이 소령과 자기 피후견인 및 그 아이 어머니에 관한 회계를 들여다보다가, 그를 몹시 놀라게 하고 동시에 가슴 아프게도 또 흐뭇하게도 하는 단서를 잡아냈으니, 그 가련한 미망인과 아이가 연명해 온 그 자금의 일부가 다름 아닌 윌리엄 도빈 자신의 호주머니에서 나왔다는 것이었다."]], [["When pressed upon the point, Dobbin, who could not tell lies, blushed and stammered a good deal and finally confessed.", "그 점에 대해 추궁받자, 거짓말을 할 줄 모르는 도빈은 얼굴을 붉히고 한참 더듬거리다가 마침내 털어놓았다."], ["\"The marriage,\"", "\"그 결혼은,\""], ["he said (at which his interlocutor's face grew dark) \"was very much my doing.", "그가 말하자 상대의 낯빛이 어두워졌다. \"거의 제가 한 일이나 다름없었습니다."], ["I thought my poor friend had gone so far that retreat from his engagement would have been dishonour to him and death to Mrs.", "제 가엾은 벗이 너무 멀리 와 버려서, 약혼에서 발을 빼는 것은 그에게는 불명예요 오스본 부인에게는 죽음이나 마찬가지라고 생각했습니다."], ["Osborne, and I could do no less, when she was left without resources, than give what money I could spare to maintain her.\"", "그리고 그녀가 아무런 재력도 없이 남겨졌을 때, 제가 떼어 둘 수 있는 돈을 그녀의 생계를 위해 내어 주는 것, 그 정도는 해야 한다고 여겼지요.\""]], [["\"Major D.,\" Mr.", "\"도빈 소령,\""], ["Osborne said, looking hard at him and turning very red too--\"you did me a great injury; but give me leave to tell you, sir, you are an honest feller.", "오스본 씨가 그를 뚫어지게 바라보며, 자기도 얼굴이 시뻘게진 채 말했다. \"자네는 나에게 큰 해를 끼쳤네."], ["There's my hand, sir, though I little thought that my flesh and blood was living on you--\"", "하지만, 이 말은 해야겠네, 자네는 정직한 사람일세. 자, 내 손을 잡게."], ["and the pair shook hands, with great confusion on Major Dobbin's part, thus found out in his act of charitable hypocrisy.", "내 핏줄이 자네에게 얹혀살았으리라고는 꿈에도 생각 못 했네만 —\" 그렇게 두 사람은 악수했는데, 이렇게 자선의 위선 행각을 들켜 버린 도빈 소령 쪽이 무척 당혹스러워했다."]], [["He strove to soften the old man and reconcile him towards his son's memory.", "그는 노인의 마음을 누그러뜨려 아들의 추억과 화해시키려 애썼다."], ["\"He was such a noble fellow,\"", "\"그는 얼마나 고귀한 사내였는지요.\""], ["he said, \"that all of us loved him, and would have done anything for him.", "그가 말했다. \"우리 모두 그를 사랑했고, 그를 위해서라면 무엇이든 했을 겁니다."], ["I, as a young man in those days, was flattered beyond measure by his preference for me, and was more pleased to be seen in his company than in that of the Commander-in-Chief.", "그 시절 젊은이였던 저는 그가 저를 각별히 아껴 준 데에 더없이 우쭐했고, 총사령관과 함께 있는 것보다 그와 함께 있는 모습을 남들에게 보이는 게 더 기뻤습니다."], ["I never saw his equal for pluck and daring and all the qualities of a soldier\"; and Dobbin told the old father as many stories as he could remember regarding the gallantry and achievements of his son.", "저는 담력과 대담함, 그리고 군인다운 온갖 자질에서 그에게 견줄 사람을 본 적이 없습니다.\" 그러고는 도빈은 그 늙은 아버지에게 자기 아들의 용맹과 무공에 관해 기억나는 대로 숱한 이야기를 들려주었다."], ["\"And Georgy is so like him,\"", "\"그리고 조지는 그를 어찌나 닮았는지요.\""], ["the Major added.", "소령이 덧붙였다."]], [["\"He's so like him that he makes me tremble sometimes,\"", "\"그 애가 어찌나 그를 닮았는지 가끔은 내가 다 몸이 떨린다네.\""], ["the grandfather said.", "외할아버지가 말했다."]], [["On one or two evenings the Major came to dine with Mr. Osborne (it was during the time of the sickness of Mr.", "한두 차례 저녁에 소령은 오스본 씨와 만찬을 들러 왔고 (세들리 씨가 병중이던 무렵이었다), 두 사람이 만찬 뒤 저녁에 함께 앉아 있노라면 그들의 이야기는 온통 그 떠난 영웅에 관한 것이었다."], ["Sedley), and as the two sat together in the evening after dinner, all their talk was about the departed hero. The father boasted about him according to his wont, glorifying himself in recounting his son's feats and gallantry, but his mood was at any rate better and more charitable than that in which he had been disposed until now to regard the poor fellow; and the Christian heart of the kind Major was pleased at these symptoms of returning peace and good-will.", "아버지는 늘 하던 버릇대로 그를 자랑했고, 아들의 위업과 용맹을 늘어놓으며 스스로 우쭐해했지만, 어쨌든 그의 심사는 지금껏 그 가련한 사내를 두고 품어 온 것보다 한결 누그러지고 너그러워졌다."], ["On the second evening old Osborne called Dobbin William, just as he used to do at the time when Dobbin and George were boys together, and the honest gentleman was pleased by that mark of reconciliation.", "그리고 친절한 소령의 그리스도교적인 마음은 평화와 선의가 되돌아오는 이런 조짐들에 흐뭇해졌다. 둘째 날 저녁, 늙은 오스본은 도빈과 조지가 함께 소년이던 시절에 그러던 것처럼 도빈을 윌리엄이라 불렀고, 그 정직한 신사는 이 화해의 표시에 흐뭇해했다."]], [["On the next day at breakfast, when Miss Osborne, with the asperity of her age and character, ventured to make some remark reflecting slightingly upon the Major's appearance or behaviour--the master of the house interrupted her.", "이튿날 아침 식사 자리에서, 오스본 양이 제 나이와 성격의 모진 데를 드러내며 소령의 외모나 거동을 깎아내리는 한마디를 감히 던지자, 이 집 주인이 그녀의 말을 가로막았다."], ["\"You'd have been glad enough to git him for yourself, Miss O.", "\"너야말로 그 사람을 차지하고 싶었을 게다, 오스본 양."], ["But them grapes are sour.", "하지만 그건 신 포도지."], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["Major William is a fine feller.\"", "윌리엄 소령은 훌륭한 사내야.\""]], [["\"That he is, Grandpapa,\"", "\"맞아요, 할아버지.\""], ["said Georgy approvingly; and going up close to the old gentleman, he took a hold of his large grey whiskers, and laughed in his face good-humouredly, and kissed him.", "조지가 맞장구치며 말했다. 그러고는 늙은 신사 곁으로 바짝 다가가 그 커다란 잿빛 구레나룻을 한 줌 거머쥐고는 그 얼굴에 대고 흐뭇하게 웃으며 입을 맞추었다."], ["And he told the story at night to his mother, who fully agreed with the boy.", "그리고 그 애는 밤에 어머니에게 그 이야기를 들려주었고, 어머니는 아이의 말에 전적으로 동의했다."], ["\"Indeed he is,\"", "\"정말 그렇단다.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"Your dear father always said so.", "\"네 사랑하는 아버지도 늘 그렇게 말씀하셨지."], ["He is one of the best and most upright of men.\"", "그분은 더없이 훌륭하고 올곧은 분들 가운데 한 분이란다.\""], ["Dobbin happened to drop in very soon after this conversation, which made Amelia blush perhaps, and the young scapegrace increased the confusion by telling Dobbin the other part of the story.", "마침 이 대화가 있고 얼마 지나지 않아 도빈이 불쑥 들렀는데, 그 때문에 어쩌면 애밀리아의 얼굴이 붉어졌을 것이고, 그 어린 개구쟁이는 이야기의 나머지 대목을 도빈에게 들려주어 그 당혹스러움을 한층 더했다. \"있잖아요, 돕 아저씨.\""], ["\"I say, Dob,\"", "그가 말했다."], ["he said, \"there's such an uncommon nice girl wants to marry you.", "\"아저씨랑 결혼하고 싶어 하는 굉장히 멋진 아가씨가 있어요."], ["She's plenty of tin; she wears a front; and she scolds the servants from morning till night.\"", "돈도 잔뜩 있고, 가발도 쓰고, 아침부터 밤까지 하인들을 닦달하지요.\""], ["\"Who is it?\"", "\"그게 누군데?\""], ["asked Dobbin.", "도빈이 물었다."], ["\"It's Aunt O.,\"", "\"오스본 고모예요.\""], ["the boy answered.", "아이가 대답했다."], ["\"Grandpapa said so.", "\"할아버지가 그러셨어요."], ["And I say, Dob, how prime it would be to have you for my uncle.\"", "그리고 있잖아요, 돕 아저씨, 아저씨가 우리 외삼촌이 되면 얼마나 근사할까요.\""], ["Old Sedley's quavering voice from the next room at this moment weakly called for Amelia, and the laughing ended.", "바로 이때 옆방에서 늙은 세들리의 떨리는 목소리가 힘없이 애밀리아를 불렀고, 웃음은 끝이 났다."]], [["That old Osborne's mind was changing was pretty clear.", "늙은 오스본의 마음이 변해 가고 있다는 것은 꽤 분명했다."], ["He asked George about his uncle sometimes, and laughed at the boy's imitation of the way in which Jos said \"God-bless-my-soul\"", "그는 이따금 조지에게 그 외삼촌에 관해 물었고, 조스가 \"맙소사\" 하고 내뱉으며 수프를 게걸스레 들이켜던 모양을 아이가 흉내 내는 것을 보고 웃었다."], ["and gobbled his soup.", "그러고는 이렇게 말했다."], ["Then he said, \"It's not respectful, sir, of you younkers to be imitating of your relations.", "\"너희 젊은것들이 친척을 흉내 내다니 점잖지 못한 짓이다."], ["Miss O., when you go out adriving to-day, leave my card upon Mr. Sedley, do you hear?", "오스본 양, 오늘 마차 타고 나가거든 세들리 씨 댁에 내 명함을 두고 오너라, 알겠느냐?"], ["There's no quarrel betwigst me and him anyhow.\"", "어쨌거나 나와 그 사람 사이에 다툴 일은 없으니 말이다.\""]], [["The card was returned, and Jos and the Major were asked to dinner--to a dinner the most splendid and stupid that perhaps ever Mr.", "명함은 답례로 돌아왔고, 조스와 소령은 만찬에 초대받았다. 아마도 오스본 씨가 베푼 만찬 가운데 가장 호사스럽고 가장 따분한 만찬이었으리라."], ["Osborne gave; every inch of the family plate was exhibited, and the best company was asked.", "집안의 식기란 식기는 한 점도 빠짐없이 진열되었고, 가장 훌륭한 손님들이 초대되었다."], ["Mr. Sedley took down Miss O.", "세들리 씨가 오스본 양을 만찬석으로 이끌었고, 그녀는 그에게 무척 상냥했다."], ["to dinner, and she was very gracious to him; whereas she hardly spoke to the Major, who sat apart from her, and by the side of Mr. Osborne, very timid.", "반면 소령에게는 거의 말도 걸지 않았으니, 소령은 그녀에게서 떨어져 오스본 씨 곁에 앉아 무척 주눅 들어 있었다."], ["Jos said, with great solemnity, it was the best turtle soup he had ever tasted in his life, and asked Mr. Osborne where he got his Madeira.", "조스는 자못 엄숙하게, 평생 맛본 가운데 가장 훌륭한 거북 수프라고 말하며, 오스본 씨에게 그 마데이라 포도주를 어디서 구했는지 물었다."]], [["\"It is some of Sedley's wine,\"", "\"세들리 씨네 포도주 가운데 하나입니다요.\""], ["whispered the butler to his master.", "집사가 주인에게 속삭였다. \"그건 오래전부터 갖고 있던 거라네."], ["\"I've had it a long time, and paid a good figure for it, too,\" Mr.", "값도 두둑이 치렀고 말이야.\""], ["Osborne said aloud to his guest, and then whispered to his right-hand neighbour how he had got it \"at the old chap's sale.\"", "오스본 씨가 손님에게 소리 내어 말하고는, 오른편 자리에 앉은 이에게 그 \"영감 경매에서\" 그걸 손에 넣었다고 속삭였다."]], [["More than once he asked the Major about--about Mrs. George Osborne--a theme on which the Major could be very eloquent when he chose.", "그는 한 번 이상 소령에게 — 조지 오스본 부인에 관해 물었는데, 소령이 마음만 먹으면 무척 능변을 토할 수 있는 화제였다."], ["He told Mr. Osborne of her sufferings--of her passionate attachment to her husband, whose memory she worshipped still--of the tender and dutiful manner in which she had supported her parents, and given up her boy, when it seemed to her her duty to do so.", "그는 오스본 씨에게 그녀가 겪은 고초를 — 지금도 그 기억을 흠모하는 남편을 향한 그녀의 격정적인 애착을, 그리고 자기 도리라고 여겨졌을 때 부모를 봉양하고 제 아들을 내어 준 그 다정하고 갸륵한 처신을 들려주었다."], ["\"You don't know what she endured, sir,\"", "\"그녀가 무엇을 견뎌 냈는지 어르신은 모르십니다.\""], ["said honest Dobbin with a tremor in his voice, \"and I hope and trust you will be reconciled to her.", "정직한 도빈이 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"그리고 부디 어르신께서 그녀와 화해하시기를 저는 바라고 또 믿습니다."], ["If she took your son away from you, she gave hers to you; and however much you loved your George, depend on it, she loved hers ten times more.\"", "그녀가 어르신께 아드님을 빼앗아 갔다면, 자기 아들을 어르신께 내어 드린 것이니까요. 그리고 어르신이 조지를 아무리 사랑하셨다 한들, 정녕 그녀는 자기 아들을 그 열 배는 더 사랑했습니다.\""]], [["\"By God, you are a good feller, sir,\"", "\"맙소사, 자네는 좋은 사람일세.\""], ["was all Mr. Osborne said.", "오스본 씨가 한 말은 그것뿐이었다."], ["It had never struck him that the widow would feel any pain at parting from the boy, or that his having a fine fortune could grieve her.", "그 미망인이 아이와 헤어지는 데에 무슨 고통을 느끼리라거나, 아이가 큰 재산을 갖게 된 것이 그녀를 슬프게 할 수도 있다는 것은 그의 머릿속에 한 번도 떠오른 적이 없었다."], ["A reconciliation was announced as speedy and inevitable, and Amelia's heart already began to beat at the notion of the awful meeting with George's father.", "화해는 곧, 또 피할 수 없이 이루어질 것으로 알려졌고, 애밀리아의 가슴은 조지의 아버지와 그 두려운 만남을 가질 생각에 벌써부터 두근거리기 시작했다."]], [["It was never, however, destined to take place.", "그러나 그 만남은 끝내 이루어질 운명이 아니었다."], ["Old Sedley's lingering illness and death supervened, after which a meeting was for some time impossible.", "늙은 세들리의 길어진 병환과 죽음이 닥쳐왔고, 그 뒤로 한동안 만남은 불가능했다."], ["That catastrophe and other events may have worked upon Mr. Osborne.", "그 참변과 다른 사건들이 오스본 씨의 마음에 작용했는지도 모른다."], ["He was much shaken of late, and aged, and his mind was working inwardly.", "그는 요즘 들어 몹시 흔들렸고, 부쩍 늙었으며, 그의 마음은 안으로 곱씹고 있었다."], ["He had sent for his lawyers, and probably changed something in his will.", "그는 변호사들을 불러들였고, 아마도 유언장에서 무언가를 고쳤을 것이다."], ["The medical man who looked in pronounced him shaky, agitated, and talked of a little blood and the seaside; but he took neither of these remedies.", "들여다보러 온 의사는 그가 기력이 쇠하고 동요한다고 진단하며 피를 좀 뽑고 바닷가에 가 보라고 했지만, 그는 그 어느 처방도 따르지 않았다."]], [["One day when he should have come down to breakfast, his servant missing him, went into his dressing-room and found him lying at the foot of the dressing-table in a fit.", "어느 날, 그가 아침 식사 자리에 내려왔어야 할 시각에 그가 보이지 않자, 그의 하인이 화장실로 들어가 보니 그가 발작을 일으킨 채 화장대 발치에 쓰러져 있었다."], ["Miss Osborne was apprised; the doctors were sent for; Georgy stopped away from school; the bleeders and cuppers came.", "오스본 양에게 기별이 갔고, 의사들이 불려 왔으며, 조지는 학교에 가지 않았고, 사혈사와 부항사가 왔다."], ["Osborne partially regained cognizance, but never could speak again, though he tried dreadfully once or twice, and in four days he died.", "오스본은 잠시 의식을 되찾았으나 두 번 다시 말은 하지 못했으니, 한두 차례 안간힘을 써 보았지만 끝내 그러했고, 나흘 만에 그는 숨졌다."], ["The doctors went down, and the undertaker's men went up the stairs, and all the shutters were shut towards the garden in Russell Square.", "의사들은 아래층으로 내려갔고, 장의사 일꾼들은 위층으로 올라갔으며, 러셀 광장 정원 쪽 덧창은 모조리 닫혔다."], ["Bullock rushed from the City in a hurry.", "불럭이 황급히 시내에서 달려왔다."], ["\"How much money had he left to that boy?", "\"그가 그 애한테 돈을 얼마나 남겼지?"], ["Not half, surely?", "설마 절반은 아니겠지?"], ["Surely share and share alike between the three?\"", "분명히 세 사람이 똑같이 나눠 갖는 거겠지?\""], ["It was an agitating moment.", "마음 졸이게 하는 순간이었다."]], [["What was it that poor old man tried once or twice in vain to say?", "그 가련한 노인이 한두 번 헛되이 말하려 했던 것은 무엇이었을까?"], ["I hope it was that he wanted to see Amelia and be reconciled before he left the world to one dear and faithful wife of his son: it was most likely that, for his will showed that the hatred which he had so long cherished had gone out of his heart.", "나는 그것이, 그가 세상을 떠나기 전에 애밀리아를 보고 자기 아들의 사랑스럽고 충직한 아내와 화해하고 싶었던 것이기를 바란다. 십중팔구 그러했을 것이니, 그가 그토록 오래 품어 온 증오가 그의 가슴에서 사라졌음을 그의 유언장이 보여 주었기 때문이다."]], [["They found in the pocket of his dressing-gown the letter with the great red seal which George had written him from Waterloo.", "사람들은 그의 실내복 호주머니에서, 조지가 워털루에서 그에게 써 보낸, 커다란 붉은 봉인이 찍힌 그 편지를 찾아냈다."], ["He had looked at the other papers too, relative to his son, for the key of the box in which he kept them was also in his pocket, and it was found the seals and envelopes had been broken--very likely on the night before the seizure--when the butler had taken him tea into his study, and found him reading in the great red family Bible.", "그는 아들에 관한 다른 서류들도 들여다본 터였으니, 그것들을 넣어 두던 상자의 열쇠 역시 그의 호주머니에 있었고, 봉인과 봉투가 뜯겨 있는 것이 발견되었다. 아마도 발작이 일어나기 전날 밤, 집사가 서재로 차를 가져갔다가 그가 커다란 붉은 가족용 성경을 읽고 있는 것을 본 그때였을 것이다."]], [["When the will was opened, it was found that half the property was left to George, and the remainder between the two sisters.", "유언장이 개봉되자, 재산의 절반은 조지에게, 나머지는 두 자매에게 나누어 남겨졌음이 드러났다."], ["Mr. Bullock to continue, for their joint benefit, the affairs of the commercial house, or to go out, as he thought fit.", "불럭 씨는 적절하다고 판단하는 대로, 그 공동의 이익을 위해 그 상사(商社)의 일을 계속하든지 손을 떼든지 하도록 했다."], ["An annuity of five hundred pounds, chargeable on George's property, was left to his mother, \"the widow of my beloved son, George Osborne,\" who was to resume the guardianship of the boy.", "조지의 재산에 부담 지우는 연 오백 파운드의 연금이 그의 어머니, \"내 사랑하는 아들 조지 오스본의 미망인\"에게 남겨졌으며, 그녀가 아이의 후견을 다시 맡도록 했다."]], [["\"Major William Dobbin, my beloved son's friend,\"", "\"내 사랑하는 아들의 벗, 윌리엄 도빈 소령\"이 유언 집행자로 지명되었다."], ["was appointed executor; \"and as out of his kindness and bounty, and with his own private funds, he maintained my grandson and my son's widow, when they were otherwise without means of support\" (the testator went on to say) \"I hereby thank him heartily for his love and regard for them, and beseech him to accept such a sum as may be sufficient to purchase his commission as a Lieutenant-Colonel, or to be disposed of in any way he may think fit.\"", "\"그리고 그가 친절과 후의로, 또 자신의 사사로운 자금으로, 달리 생계를 꾸릴 길이 없던 내 손자와 내 아들의 미망인을 부양해 주었기에 (유언자는 이렇게 이어 갔다), 나는 그들을 향한 그의 사랑과 배려에 진심으로 감사하며, 그가 중령 임관을 사들이기에 넉넉한 금액을, 혹은 그가 적절하다고 여기는 어떤 방식으로든 처분할 만한 금액을 받아 주기를 간곡히 청하는 바이다.\""]], [["When Amelia heard that her father-in-law was reconciled to her, her heart melted, and she was grateful for the fortune left to her.", "애밀리아는 시아버지가 자기와 화해했다는 것을 들었을 때, 마음이 녹아내렸고, 자기에게 남겨진 재산에 감사했다."], ["But when she heard how Georgy was restored to her, and knew how and by whom, and how it was William's bounty that supported her in poverty, how it was William who gave her her husband and her son--oh, then she sank on her knees, and prayed for blessings on that constant and kind heart; she bowed down and humbled herself, and kissed the feet, as it were, of that beautiful and generous affection.", "그러나 조지가 어떻게 자기에게 되돌아왔는지를 듣고, 그것이 어떻게, 또 누구에 의해서였는지를 알고, 가난 속에서 자기를 떠받쳐 준 것이 윌리엄의 후의였음을, 자기에게 남편을, 또 아들을 안겨 준 것이 윌리엄이었음을 알았을 때 — 오, 그때 그녀는 무릎 꿇고 그 한결같고 친절한 마음에 축복을 빌었다. 그녀는 몸을 숙여 자신을 낮추고, 그 아름답고 너그러운 애정의 발에 입맞춤하다시피 했다."]], [["And gratitude was all that she had to pay back for such admirable devotion and benefits--only gratitude!", "그리고 그토록 갸륵한 헌신과 은혜에 그녀가 갚을 수 있는 것이라고는 감사뿐이었다 — 오로지 감사뿐!"], ["If she thought of any other return, the image of George stood up out of the grave and said, \"You are mine, and mine only, now and forever.\"", "만일 그녀가 그 밖의 다른 보답을 생각하기라도 하면, 조지의 형상이 무덤에서 일어나 이렇게 말했다. \"당신은 내 것이오, 오로지 내 것, 지금도 영원토록.\""]], [["William knew her feelings: had he not passed his whole life in divining them?", "윌리엄은 그녀의 심정을 알았다. 그가 평생을 그것을 헤아리는 데 바쳐 오지 않았던가?"]], [["When the nature of Mr. Osborne's will became known to the world, it was edifying to remark how Mrs.", "오스본 씨 유언장의 내막이 세상에 알려지자, 조지 오스본 부인이 자기 교제권을 이루는 사람들의 평가 속에서 얼마나 격상되는지를 지켜보는 것은 자못 교훈적이었다."], ["George Osborne rose in the estimation of the people forming her circle of acquaintance. The servants of Jos's establishment, who used to question her humble orders and say they would \"ask Master\"", "조스네 살림을 꾸리던 하인들은, 그녀의 겸손한 분부에 토를 달고 따라야 할지 말아야 할지 \"주인 나리께 여쭤보겠다\"고 말하곤 했지만, 이제는 그런 식의 호소 따위는 떠올리지도 않았다."], ["whether or not they could obey, never thought now of that sort of appeal. The cook forgot to sneer at her shabby old gowns (which, indeed, were quite eclipsed by that lady's finery when she was dressed to go to church of a Sunday evening), the others no longer grumbled at the sound of her bell, or delayed to answer that summons.", "요리사는 그녀의 초라한 낡은 드레스를 비웃던 일을 잊었고 (사실 그 드레스는 그녀가 일요일 저녁 예배에 가려고 차려입었을 때 그 부인의 호사스러운 차림에 완전히 빛이 바래는 것이었다), 다른 이들도 더는 그녀의 종소리에 투덜대거나 그 부름에 굼뜨게 응하지 않았다."], ["The coachman, who grumbled that his 'osses should be brought out and his carriage made into an hospital for that old feller and Mrs. O., drove her with the utmost alacrity now, and trembling lest he should be superseded by Mr.", "자기 말들을 끌어내야 하고 자기 마차가 그 늙은 영감과 오스본 부인을 위한 병원 노릇이나 한다고 투덜대던 마부는, 이제 그녀를 더없이 부지런히 모셨고, 행여 오스본 씨의 마부에게 밀려날까 벌벌 떨면서, \"그 러셀 광장 마부 녀석들이 도시에 대해 뭘 안다고, 숙녀분 앞에서 마부석에 앉을 자격이나 되겠느냐\"고 물었다."], ["Osborne's coachman, asked \"what them there Russell Square coachmen knew about town, and whether they was fit to sit on a box before a lady?\"", "조스의 친구들은 남녀를 막론하고 갑자기 에미에게 관심을 갖게 되었고, 그녀의 현관 탁자에는 조의를 표하는 명함이 늘어 갔다."], ["Jos's friends, male and female, suddenly became interested about Emmy, and cards of condolence multiplied on her hall table. Jos himself, who had looked on her as a good-natured harmless pauper, to whom it was his duty to give victuals and shelter, paid her and the rich little boy, his nephew, the greatest respect--was anxious that she should have change and amusement after her troubles and trials, \"poor dear girl\"--and began to appear at the breakfast-table, and most particularly to ask how she would like to dispose of the day.", "그녀를 그저 사람 좋고 해 끼치지 않는 빈털터리쯤으로, 먹을 것과 거처를 마련해 주는 것이 자기 도리라 여기던 조스 본인도, 이제 그녀와 그 부유한 어린 사내아이, 자기 조카에게 더없는 경의를 표했고, \"가엾은 우리 애\"가 그 온갖 고초와 시련을 겪고 나서 기분 전환과 여흥을 누리기를 바랐으며, 아침 식사 자리에 모습을 드러내기 시작하더니, 특히나 그날 하루를 어떻게 보내고 싶은지를 꼬박꼬박 물었다."]], [["In her capacity of guardian to Georgy, she, with the consent of the Major, her fellow-trustee, begged Miss Osborne to live in the Russell Square house as long as ever she chose to dwell there; but that lady, with thanks, declared that she never could think of remaining alone in that melancholy mansion, and departed in deep mourning to Cheltenham, with a couple of her old domestics.", "조지의 후견인 자격으로, 그녀는 공동 수탁자인 소령의 동의를 얻어, 오스본 양에게 원하는 한 얼마든지 러셀 광장 저택에서 살아 달라고 청했다. 그러나 그 부인은 감사를 표하면서도, 그 음울한 저택에서 혼자 지낼 생각은 도무지 할 수 없노라 말하고, 옛 하인 두엇을 거느린 채 깊은 상복 차림으로 첼튼엄으로 떠났다."], ["The rest were liberally paid and dismissed, the faithful old butler, whom Mrs. Osborne proposed to retain, resigning and preferring to invest his savings in a public-house, where, let us hope, he was not unprosperous.", "나머지 하인들에게는 후하게 보수를 치르고 해고했는데, 오스본 부인이 계속 두고자 했던 그 충직한 늙은 집사는 사직하고 제 저축을 술집에 투자하는 편을 택했으니, 거기서 그가 그럭저럭 번창했기를 바라 본다."], ["Miss Osborne not choosing to live in Russell Square, Mrs. Osborne also, after consultation, declined to occupy the gloomy old mansion there.", "오스본 양이 러셀 광장에서 살기를 마다하니, 오스본 부인도 의논 끝에 그 음침한 옛 저택에 들어가 사는 것을 사양했다."], ["The house was dismantled; the rich furniture and effects, the awful chandeliers and dreary blank mirrors packed away and hidden, the rich rosewood drawing-room suite was muffled in straw, the carpets were rolled up and corded, the small select library of well-bound books was stowed into two wine-chests, and the whole paraphernalia rolled away in several enormous vans to the Pantechnicon, where they were to lie until Georgy's majority.", "집은 해체되었고, 호화로운 가구와 세간, 그 으리으리한 샹들리에와 음산하게 텅 빈 거울들은 꾸려져 치워졌으며, 값진 자단(紫檀) 응접실 세트는 짚으로 감싸였고, 양탄자들은 둘둘 말려 노끈으로 묶였으며, 단단히 제본된 책들로 알차게 추린 작은 서가는 포도주 상자 두 개에 챙겨 넣어졌고, 그 일습은 거대한 짐마차 여러 대에 실려 팬테크니콘으로 옮겨져, 조지가 성년이 될 때까지 거기 놓이게 되었다."], ["And the great heavy dark plate-chests went off to Messrs. Stumpy and Rowdy, to lie in the cellars of those eminent bankers until the same period should arrive.", "그리고 그 크고 묵직한 검은 식기 상자들은 스텀피 앤드 라우디 상회로 보내져, 같은 시기가 올 때까지 그 명망 높은 은행가들의 지하 금고에 놓이게 되었다."]], [["One day Emmy, with George in her hand and clad in deep sables, went to visit the deserted mansion which she had not entered since she was a girl.", "어느 날 에미는 조지의 손을 잡고 깊은 상복을 두른 채, 처녀 시절 이후로 들어가 본 적 없던 그 버려진 저택을 찾아갔다."], ["The place in front was littered with straw where the vans had been laden and rolled off.", "집 앞은 짐마차에 짐을 싣고 굴려 보낸 자리에 짚이 어지러이 널려 있었다."], ["They went into the great blank rooms, the walls of which bore the marks where the pictures and mirrors had hung.", "그들은 텅 빈 큰 방들로 들어갔는데, 그 벽에는 그림과 거울이 걸려 있던 자국이 남아 있었다."], ["Then they went up the great blank stone staircases into the upper rooms, into that where grandpapa died, as George said in a whisper, and then higher still into George's own room.", "그런 다음 텅 빈 큰 돌계단을 올라 위층 방들로, 조지가 속삭이듯 말한 대로 할아버지가 돌아가신 그 방으로 들어갔다가, 더 위로 올라가 조지 자신의 방으로 갔다."], ["The boy was still clinging by her side, but she thought of another besides him.", "아이는 여전히 그녀 곁에 매달려 있었지만, 그녀는 그 아이 말고 또 다른 사람을 생각하고 있었다."], ["She knew that it had been his father's room as well as his own.", "그녀는 그 방이 아이의 방인 동시에 아이 아버지의 방이기도 했음을 알고 있었다."]], [["She went up to one of the open windows (one of those at which she used to gaze with a sick heart when the child was first taken from her), and thence as she looked out she could see, over the trees of Russell Square, the old house in which she herself was born, and where she had passed so many happy days of sacred youth.", "그녀는 열린 창문 가운데 하나로 (아이가 처음 그녀에게서 떼어졌을 때, 가슴 아파하며 내다보곤 하던 바로 그런 창문 가운데 하나였다) 다가갔고, 거기서 밖을 내다보니 러셀 광장의 나무들 너머로, 자기가 태어난 옛집, 신성한 청춘의 그토록 많은 행복한 나날을 보낸 그 집이 보였다."], ["They all came back to her, the pleasant holidays, the kind faces, the careless, joyful past times, and the long pains and trials that had since cast her down.", "그 모든 것이 그녀에게 되돌아왔다. 그 즐거운 휴일들, 다정한 얼굴들, 근심 없고 기쁘던 지난날, 그리고 그 뒤로 그녀를 무너뜨린 길고 긴 고통과 시련."], ["She thought of these and of the man who had been her constant protector, her good genius, her sole benefactor, her tender and generous friend.", "그녀는 이런 것들을, 그리고 자기의 한결같은 보호자였고, 자기의 선한 수호신이었으며, 자기의 유일한 은인이자, 다정하고 너그러운 벗이었던 그 사람을 떠올렸다."]], [["\"Look here, Mother,\"", "\"이것 봐요, 엄마.\""], ["said Georgy, \"here's a G.O.", "조지가 말했다."], ["scratched on the glass with a diamond, I never saw it before, I never did it.\"", "\"여기 유리에 다이아몬드로 G.O.라고 긁혀 있어요. 한 번도 본 적 없는데, 내가 한 게 아니에요.\""]], [["\"It was your father's room long before you were born, George,\"", "\"그건 네가 태어나기 훨씬 전부터 네 아버지 방이었단다, 조지.\""], ["she said, and she blushed as she kissed the boy.", "그녀는 그렇게 말하며, 아이에게 입맞추면서 얼굴을 붉혔다."]], [["She was very silent as they drove back to Richmond, where they had taken a temporary house: where the smiling lawyers used to come bustling over to see her (and we may be sure noted the visit in the bill): and where of course there was a room for Major Dobbin too, who rode over frequently, having much business to transact on behalf of his little ward.", "그들이 임시로 집을 얻어 둔 리치먼드로 마차를 타고 돌아오는 동안 그녀는 무척 말이 없었다. 그곳으로는 싱글거리는 변호사들이 부산스레 그녀를 만나러 건너오곤 했고 (그 방문을 청구서에 적어 넣었으리라는 것은 우리가 능히 짐작할 만하다), 그곳에는 물론 도빈 소령을 위한 방도 마련되어 있었으니, 그는 자기 어린 피후견인을 위해 처리할 일이 많아 자주 말을 달려 건너왔다."]], [["Georgy at this time was removed from Mr. Veal's on an unlimited holiday, and that gentleman was engaged to prepare an inscription for a fine marble slab, to be placed up in the Foundling under the monument of Captain George Osborne.", "이 무렵 조지는 빌 씨네에서 데려와 기한 없는 방학을 누리게 되었고, 그 신사는 파운들링 안에 조지 오스본 대위의 기념비 아래 세울 고운 대리석 판에 새길 비문을 짓는 일을 맡았다."]], [["The female Bullock, aunt of Georgy, although despoiled by that little monster of one-half of the sum which she expected from her father, nevertheless showed her charitableness of spirit by being reconciled to the mother and the boy.", "조지의 고모인 불럭 부인은, 비록 그 어린 괴물에게 아버지에게서 받으려던 액수의 절반을 빼앗기긴 했어도, 어머니와 아이에게 화해의 손을 내밀어 그 너그러운 심성을 내보였다."], ["Roehampton is not far from Richmond, and one day the chariot, with the golden bullocks emblazoned on the panels, and the flaccid children within, drove to Amelia's house at Richmond; and the Bullock family made an irruption into the garden, where Amelia was reading a book, Jos was in an arbour placidly dipping strawberries into wine, and the Major in one of his Indian jackets was giving a back to Georgy, who chose to jump over him.", "로햄프턴은 리치먼드에서 멀지 않았고, 어느 날 차체 옆면에 황금빛 황소 문장이 새겨진, 축 늘어진 아이들을 태운 마차가 리치먼드의 애밀리아 집으로 달려왔다. 그리고 불럭 가족은 정원으로 들이닥쳤으니, 거기서는 애밀리아가 책을 읽고 있었고, 조스는 정자에서 느긋하게 딸기를 포도주에 적시고 있었으며, 인도풍 웃옷을 입은 소령은 조지에게 등을 내주어 조지가 그 위로 뛰어넘게 하고 있었다."], ["He went over his head and bounded into the little advance of Bullocks, with immense black bows in their hats, and huge black sashes, accompanying their mourning mamma.", "조지는 소령의 머리 위를 뛰어넘어, 모자에 커다란 검은 리본을 달고 허리에 큼직한 검은 띠를 두른 채 상복 차림의 엄마를 뒤따르던 어린 불럭 무리 한가운데로 폴짝 뛰어들었다."]], [["\"He is just of the age for Rosa,\"", "\"저 애가 딱 로자 또래로구나.\""], ["the fond parent thought, and glanced towards that dear child, an unwholesome little miss of seven years of age.", "그 자식 사랑 지극한 어머니는 그렇게 생각하며, 일곱 살배기 병약한 어린 아가씨인 그 사랑스러운 아이 쪽을 흘끗 바라보았다."]], [["\"Rosa, go and kiss your dear cousin,\"", "\"로자야, 가서 네 사랑하는 사촌에게 입맞춤하렴.\""], ["Mrs. Frederick said.", "프레더릭 부인이 말했다."], ["\"Don't you know me, George?", "\"조지, 나를 모르겠니?"], ["I am your aunt.\"", "내가 네 고모란다.\""]], [["\"I know you well enough,\"", "\"잘 알아요.\" 조지가 말했다."], ["George said; \"but I don't like kissing, please\"; and he retreated from the obedient caresses of his cousin.", "\"하지만 전 입맞춤은 싫은데요.\" 그러고는 고분고분한 사촌이 어르듯 다가오는 것을 피해 물러섰다."]], [["\"Take me to your dear mamma, you droll child,\" Mrs.", "\"네 사랑하는 엄마한테 데려다주렴, 이 익살맞은 녀석아.\""], ["Frederick said, and those ladies accordingly met, after an absence of more than fifteen years.", "프레더릭 부인이 말했고, 그리하여 그 두 부인은 십오 년이 넘는 절연 끝에 만나게 되었다."], ["During Emmy's cares and poverty the other had never once thought about coming to see her, but now that she was decently prosperous in the world, her sister-in-law came to her as a matter of course.", "에미가 시름과 가난에 잠겨 있던 동안 다른 한쪽은 단 한 번도 그녀를 보러 올 생각을 하지 않았지만, 이제 그녀가 세상에서 그럭저럭 번창하게 되자, 그 손위 동서는 당연하다는 듯 그녀를 찾아왔다."]], [["So did numbers more.", "그렇게 찾아온 이들이 더 숱하게 많았다."], ["Our old friend, Miss Swartz, and her husband came thundering over from Hampton Court, with flaming yellow liveries, and was as impetuously fond of Amelia as ever.", "우리의 옛 벗 스워츠 양도 남편과 더불어 불타는 듯한 노란색 제복을 두르고 햄프턴 코트에서 요란하게 달려왔으며, 여전히 변함없이 격하게 애밀리아를 좋아했다. 스워츠 양은 볼 수만 있었더라면 늘 그녀를 좋아했을 것이다."], ["Miss Swartz would have liked her always if she could have seen her.", "그 점만은 그녀를 위해 공정히 인정해 주어야 한다."], ["One must do her that justice.", "하지만 어쩌겠는가?"], ["But, que voulez vous?--in this vast town one has not the time to go and seek one's friends; if they drop out of the rank they disappear, and we march on without them.", "이 광대한 도시에서는 누구도 벗을 찾아 나설 겨를이 없는 것을. 벗들이 대열에서 떨어져 나가면 사라져 버리고, 우리는 그들 없이 행진을 이어 간다."], ["Who is ever missed in Vanity Fair?", "허영의 시장에서 그리워지는 이가 도대체 누구란 말인가?"]], [["But so, in a word, and before the period of grief for Mr. Osborne's death had subsided, Emmy found herself in the centre of a very genteel circle indeed, the members of which could not conceive that anybody belonging to it was not very lucky.", "그러나 한마디로, 오스본 씨의 죽음에 대한 애도의 기간이 채 가시기도 전에, 에미는 어느새 자못 점잖은 한 교제권의 한복판에 놓인 자신을 발견했으니, 그 일원들은 거기 속한 누군가가 무척 운이 좋지 않을 수 있다는 것을 도무지 상상조차 못 하는 이들이었다."], ["There was scarce one of the ladies that hadn't a relation a Peer, though the husband might be a drysalter in the City.", "그 부인들 가운데 남편이 시내의 건어물상일지언정 친척 중에 귀족 한 명쯤 두지 않은 이가 거의 없었다."], ["Some of the ladies were very blue and well informed, reading Mrs. Somerville and frequenting the Royal Institution; others were severe and Evangelical, and held by Exeter Hall.", "더러는 무척 학식 높은 식자(識者)로, 서머빌 부인의 책을 읽고 왕립 연구소를 드나들었으며, 더러는 준엄한 복음주의자로 엑서터 홀을 지지했다."], ["Emmy, it must be owned, found herself entirely at a loss in the midst of their clavers, and suffered woefully on the one or two occasions on which she was compelled to accept Mrs. Frederick Bullock's hospitalities.", "솔직히 말하건대, 에미는 그들의 수다 한복판에서 도무지 갈피를 못 잡았고, 프레더릭 불럭 부인의 환대를 받아들이지 않을 수 없었던 한두 번의 자리에서는 비참하리만치 곤욕을 치렀다."], ["That lady persisted in patronizing her and determined most graciously to form her.", "그 부인은 한사코 그녀를 후원하려 들었고, 더없이 자애롭게 그녀를 길들이기로 작정했다."], ["She found Amelia's milliners for her and regulated her household and her manners.", "그녀는 애밀리아에게 모자 장수를 구해 주고 그녀의 살림과 몸가짐을 단속했다."], ["She drove over constantly from Roehampton and entertained her friend with faint fashionable fiddle-faddle and feeble Court slip-slop.", "그녀는 로햄프턴에서 끊임없이 마차를 몰고 건너와, 시들한 사교계 시시콜콜 잡담과 맥 빠진 궁정 객설로 자기 벗을 즐겁게 해 주었다."], ["Jos liked to hear it, but the Major used to go off growling at the appearance of this woman, with her twopenny gentility.", "조스는 그것을 듣기 좋아했지만, 소령은 그 이 푼짜리 점잔을 빼는 여자가 나타나면 으레 투덜대며 자리를 떴다."], ["He went to sleep under Frederick Bullock's bald head, after dinner, at one of the banker's best parties (Fred was still anxious that the balance of the Osborne property should be transferred from Stumpy and Rowdy's to them), and whilst Amelia, who did not know Latin, or who wrote the last crack article in the Edinburgh, and did not in the least deplore, or otherwise, Mr. Peel's late extraordinary tergiversation on the fatal Catholic Relief Bill, sat dumb amongst the ladies in the grand drawing-room, looking out upon velvet lawns, trim gravel walks, and glistening hot-houses.", "그는 그 은행가의 가장 성대한 연회 중 한 자리에서, 만찬 뒤 프레더릭 불럭의 벗어진 머리 아래에서 잠이 들었고 (프레드는 여전히 오스본 재산의 잔액이 스텀피 앤드 라우디 상회에서 자기네 쪽으로 옮겨지기를 안달했다), 한편 라틴어도 모르고, 에든버러 리뷰에 실린 최신 명문(名文)을 누가 썼는지도 모르며, 저 운명적인 가톨릭 구제 법안에 관한 필 씨의 근래의 그 놀라운 변절을 조금도 개탄하거나 어쩌거나 하지 않는 애밀리아는, 으리으리한 응접실에서 부인들 가운데 말없이 앉아, 벨벳 같은 잔디밭과 말끔히 손질된 자갈길과 반짝이는 온실들을 내다보고 있었다."]], [["\"She seems good-natured but insipid,\" said Mrs.", "\"사람은 좋아 보이는데 어딘가 밋밋하군요.\""], ["Rowdy; \"that Major seems to be particularly epris.\"", "라우디 부인이 말했다. \"저 소령은 단단히 반한 모양이고요.\""]], [["\"She wants ton sadly,\"", "\"세련된 데가 영 모자라요.\""], ["said Mrs. Hollyock.", "홀리오크 부인이 말했다."], ["\"My dear creature, you never will be able to form her.\"", "\"이봐요, 당신은 절대로 저 여자를 길들이지 못할 거예요.\""]], [["\"She is dreadfully ignorant or indifferent,\"", "\"끔찍하게 무식하거나 무관심하더군요.\""], ["said Mrs. Glowry with a voice as if from the grave, and a sad shake of the head and turban.", "글로리 부인이 무덤에서 울려 나오는 듯한 목소리로, 머리와 터번을 슬프게 가로저으며 말했다."], ["\"I asked her if she thought that it was in 1836, according to Mr. Jowls, or in 1839, according to Mr.", "\"내가 그 여자에게, 졸스 씨 말대로 1836년에, 아니면 왭샷 씨 말대로 1839년에 교황이 몰락할 것 같으냐고 물었더니, 그 여자가 하는 말이, '가엾은 교황님!"], ["Wapshot, that the Pope was to fall: and she said--'Poor Pope!", "그러지 않기를 바라요."], ["I hope not--What has he done?'\"", "그분이 무슨 잘못을 하셨길래요?'라지 뭐예요.\""]], [["\"She is my brother's widow, my dear friends,\" Mrs.", "\"그 여자는 제 오라비의 미망인이에요, 여러분.\""], ["Frederick replied, \"and as such I think we're all bound to give her every attention and instruction on entering into the world.", "프레더릭 부인이 대답했다. \"그러니만큼 그 여자가 세상에 발을 들이는 데에 우리 모두가 온갖 보살핌과 가르침을 베풀 의무가 있다고 생각해요."], ["You may fancy there can be no MERCENARY motives in those whose DISAPPOINTMENTS are well known.\"", "다들 잘 아시다시피 실망스러운 처지에 놓인 사람을 대하는 데에야 무슨 잇속을 차리는 속셈 따위가 있을 리 없잖아요.\""]], [["\"That poor dear Mrs. Bullock,\"", "\"그 가엾은 불럭 부인 말이에요.\""], ["said Rowdy to Hollyock, as they drove away together--\"she is always scheming and managing.", "라우디가 홀리오크에게 함께 마차를 몰고 떠나며 말했다."], ["She wants Mrs.", "\"늘 무슨 꿍꿍이를 꾸미고 주무르려 든다니까요."], ["Osborne's account to be taken from our house to hers--and the way in which she coaxes that boy and makes him sit by that blear-eyed little Rosa is perfectly ridiculous.\"", "오스본 부인의 거래를 우리 은행에서 자기네 쪽으로 옮기려는 거예요. 그리고 그 사내아이를 어르고 달래서 눈곱 낀 그 어린 로자 옆에 앉히는 꼴이라니, 정말 가관이지 뭐예요.\""]], [["\"I wish Glowry was choked with her Man of Sin and her Battle of Armageddon,\"", "\"글로리는 자기가 떠받드는 그 죄악의 사람이며 아마겟돈 전투에 목이나 콱 막혀 버렸으면 좋겠어요.\""], ["cried the other, and the carriage rolled away over Putney Bridge.", "다른 한 사람이 외쳤고, 마차는 퍼트니 다리 위로 굴러갔다."]], [["But this sort of society was too cruelly genteel for Emmy, and all jumped for joy when a foreign tour was proposed.", "그러나 이런 부류의 사교계는 에미에게는 너무나 모질도록 점잔을 빼는 것이었고, 그래서 해외 여행이 제안되자 다들 기뻐 날뛰었다."]], [["Am Rhein\nThe above everyday events had occurred, and a few weeks had passed, when on one fine morning, Parliament being over, the summer advanced, and all the good company in London about to quit that city for their annual tour in search of pleasure or health, the Batavier steamboat left the Tower-stairs laden with a goodly company of English fugitives.", "앞서 말한 그런 일상사들이 다 벌어지고 몇 주가 흐른 뒤, 화창한 어느 아침이었다. 의회 회기가 끝나고 여름이 무르익어, 런던의 상류 인사들이 해마다 즐기는 쾌락이나 건강을 좇는 유람을 위해 막 그 도시를 떠나려던 무렵, 바타비르 증기선 한 척이 한 무리의 잉글랜드 도망객들을 가득 싣고 타워 부두 계단을 떠났다."], ["The quarter-deck awnings were up, and the benches and gangways crowded with scores of rosy children, bustling nursemaids; ladies in the prettiest pink bonnets and summer dresses; gentlemen in travelling caps and linen-jackets, whose mustachios had just begun to sprout for the ensuing tour; and stout trim old veterans with starched neckcloths and neat-brushed hats, such as have invaded Europe any time since the conclusion of the war, and carry the national Goddem into every city of the Continent.", "뒷갑판에는 차양이 쳐졌고, 벤치며 통로마다 발그레한 아이들이 떼를 지어, 분주한 유모들이, 더없이 어여쁜 분홍 보닛에 여름옷을 차려입은 숙녀들이, 여행용 모자에 아마포 재킷을 걸친, 이번 유람을 앞두고 막 콧수염이 돋기 시작한 신사들이, 그리고 풀 먹인 넥타이에 말끔히 솔질한 모자를 쓴 다부지고 단정한 노병들이 빼곡했으니, 이들은 전쟁이 끝난 이래 줄곧 유럽으로 쳐들어가 대륙의 도시마다 영국식 욕설을 퍼뜨리고 다닌 그런 부류였다."], ["The congregation of hat-boxes, and Bramah desks, and dressing-cases was prodigious.", "모자 상자며 브래마 책상이며 화장 도구함이 산더미처럼 쌓여 있었다."], ["There were jaunty young Cambridge-men travelling with their tutor, and going for a reading excursion to Nonnenwerth or Konigswinter; there were Irish gentlemen, with the most dashing whiskers and jewellery, talking about horses incessantly, and prodigiously polite to the young ladies on board, whom, on the contrary, the Cambridge lads and their pale-faced tutor avoided with maiden coyness; there were old Pall Mall loungers bound for Ems and Wiesbaden and a course of waters to clear off the dinners of the season, and a little roulette and trente-et-quarante to keep the excitement going; there was old Methuselah, who had married his young wife, with Captain Papillon of the Guards holding her parasol and guide-books; there was young May who was carrying off his bride on a pleasure tour (Mrs.", "가정교사를 대동하고 노넨베르트나 쾨니히스빈터로 독서 유람을 떠나는 멋쟁이 케임브리지 청년들이 있었고, 더없이 화려한 구레나룻과 보석으로 치장한 채 쉴 새 없이 말 이야기만 늘어놓으며 배에 탄 젊은 숙녀들에게는 지나치도록 정중하게 구는 아일랜드 신사들도 있었는데, 정작 케임브리지 청년들과 그 창백한 얼굴의 가정교사들은 처녀처럼 수줍어하며 그 숙녀들을 피해 다녔다. 또 엠스며 비스바덴으로 가서, 한 시즌의 만찬으로 더부룩해진 속을 씻어 내리려 온천 요법을 받고, 또 흥을 돋우려 룰렛과 트랑테카랑트 노름도 좀 즐기려는, 폴몰 거리의 늙은 한량들도 있었다."], ["Winter that was, and who had been at school with May's grandmother); there was Sir John and my Lady with a dozen children, and corresponding nursemaids; and the great grandee Bareacres family that sat by themselves near the wheel, stared at everybody, and spoke to no one.", "근위대의 파피용 대위에게 자기 양산과 안내책자를 들리고 다니는, 젊은 아내를 얻은 늙은 므두셀라도 있었고, 신부를 데리고 신혼 유람을 떠나는 젊은 메이도 있었으니(이 신부는 본디 윈터 부인이라 불리던 이로, 메이의 할머니와 학교를 같이 다닌 사이였다), 자식 열둘에 그에 걸맞은 수의 유모를 거느린 존 경 부부도 있었으며, 바퀴 근처에 자기들끼리만 따로 앉아 누구든 빤히 쳐다보면서도 아무에게도 말을 걸지 않는 저 거물 베어에이커스 일가도 있었다."], ["Their carriages, emblazoned with coronets and heaped with shining imperials, were on the foredeck, locked in with a dozen more such vehicles: it was difficult to pass in and out amongst them; and the poor inmates of the fore-cabin had scarcely any space for locomotion.", "그들의 마차는 작은 관 문장이 새겨지고 번쩍이는 짐 상자들이 잔뜩 실린 채 앞갑판에 놓여, 그와 같은 마차 열두어 대와 함께 단단히 묶여 있었다. 그 사이를 비집고 드나들기란 여간 어려운 일이 아니었고, 앞 선실의 가엾은 승객들은 움직일 자리조차 거의 없었다."], ["These consisted of a few magnificently attired gentlemen from Houndsditch, who brought their own provisions, and could have bought half the gay people in the grand saloon; a few honest fellows with mustachios and portfolios, who set to sketching before they had been half an hour on board; one or two French femmes de chambre who began to be dreadfully ill by the time the boat had passed Greenwich; a groom or two who lounged in the neighbourhood of the horse-boxes under their charge, or leaned over the side by the paddle-wheels, and talked about who was good for the Leger, and what they stood to win or lose for the Goodwood cup.", "그 승객들이란 자기네 먹을 것을 직접 싸 들고 와서는 대형 살롱에 탄 화려한 사람들 절반쯤은 너끈히 사 버릴 수 있을 법한, 하운즈디치 출신의 호화롭게 차려입은 신사 몇몇과, 콧수염을 기르고 화첩을 든 채 배에 탄 지 반 시간도 안 되어 그림을 그려 대기 시작한 정직한 사내 몇몇과, 배가 그리니치를 지날 무렵에는 벌써 끔찍하게 멀미를 앓기 시작한 프랑스인 하녀 한둘과, 자기들이 맡은 마구간 칸 근처를 어슬렁거리거나 외륜 옆 뱃전에 기대어 누가 레저 경마에서 유망한지, 굿우드컵으로 얼마를 따거나 잃을지를 떠들어 대는 마부 한둘이었다."]], [["All the couriers, when they had done plunging about the ship and had settled their various masters in the cabins or on the deck, congregated together and began to chatter and smoke; the Hebrew gentlemen joining them and looking at the carriages.", "수행 안내인들은 다들 배 안을 여기저기 헤집고 다니다가 저마다의 주인을 선실이나 갑판에 자리 잡게 해 준 뒤 한데 모여 수다를 떨고 담배를 피우기 시작했다. 히브리 신사들도 거기 끼어들어 마차들을 살펴보았다."], ["There was Sir John's great carriage that would hold thirteen people; my Lord Methuselah's carriage, my Lord Bareacres' chariot, britzska, and fourgon, that anybody might pay for who liked.", "열세 사람은 너끈히 태울 만한 존 경의 거대한 마차가 있었고, 므두셀라 경의 마차, 베어에이커스 경의 사륜마차며 브리츠카며 짐마차도 있었는데, 마음 있는 자라면 누구든 그 값을 치러도 좋을 것이었다."], ["It was a wonder how my Lord got the ready money to pay for the expenses of the journey.", "베어에이커스 경이 이 여행 경비를 댈 현금을 어디서 구했는지는 참으로 알 수 없는 노릇이었다."], ["The Hebrew gentlemen knew how he got it.", "하지만 히브리 신사들은 그가 어떻게 구했는지를 알고 있었다."], ["They knew what money his Lordship had in his pocket at that instant, and what interest he paid for it, and who gave it him.", "그분의 호주머니에 바로 그 순간 얼마가 들어 있는지, 그가 거기에 무슨 이자를 치르는지, 누가 그 돈을 댔는지를 그들은 알았다."], ["Finally there was a very neat, handsome travelling carriage, about which the gentlemen speculated.", "끝으로, 더없이 말끔하고 멋들어진 여행용 마차 한 대가 있었으니, 신사들은 그 마차를 두고 이런저런 추측을 늘어놓았다."]], [["\"A qui cette voiture la?\"", "\"저 마차는 누구 거요?\""], ["said one gentleman-courier with a large morocco money-bag and ear-rings to another with ear-rings and a large morocco money-bag.", "큼직한 모로코가죽 돈주머니에 귀고리를 단 한 신사 안내인이, 귀고리에 큼직한 모로코가죽 돈주머니를 찬 다른 안내인에게 물었다."]], [["\"C'est a Kirsch je bense--je l'ai vu toute a l'heure--qui brenoit des sangviches dans la voiture,\"", "\"내 생각엔 키르슈 거요. 아까 보니까 마차 안에서 샌드위치를 꺼내고 있더군요.\""], ["said the courier in a fine German French.", "안내인이 그럴듯한 독일식 프랑스어로 대답했다."]], [["Kirsch emerging presently from the neighbourhood of the hold, where he had been bellowing instructions intermingled with polyglot oaths to the ship's men engaged in secreting the passengers'", "키르슈는 이내 짐칸 근처에서 모습을 드러냈는데, 거기서 그는 승객들의 짐을 챙겨 넣는 선원들에게 갖은 나라말로 욕설을 섞어 가며 고래고래 지시를 내리던 참이었다. 그는 동료 통역들에게 제 사정을 일러 주러 왔다."], ["luggage, came to give an account of himself to his brother interpreters.", "그러고는 그 마차가 캘커타와 자메이카에서 온 어마어마한 부자 나리, 자기가 모시고 여행하기로 계약한 그 나리의 것이라고 알려 주었다."], ["He informed them that the carriage belonged to a Nabob from Calcutta and Jamaica enormously rich, and with whom he was engaged to travel; and at this moment a young gentleman who had been warned off the bridge between the paddle-boxes, and who had dropped thence on to the roof of Lord Methuselah's carriage, from which he made his way over other carriages and imperials until he had clambered on to his own, descended thence and through the window into the body of the carriage, to the applause of the couriers looking on.", "바로 그때 한 젊은 신사가, 외륜 칸 사이의 다리에 올라가지 말라는 주의를 받고는 거기서 므두셀라 경의 마차 지붕으로 뛰어내려, 또 거기서 다른 마차들과 짐 상자들을 타고 넘어 자기네 마차에까지 기어오르더니, 거기서 다시 내려와 창문을 통해 마차 안으로 들어갔다. 지켜보던 안내인들의 갈채가 쏟아졌다."]], [["\"Nous allons avoir une belle traversee, Monsieur George,\"", "\"오늘 항해는 아주 순조롭겠습니다, 조지 도련님.\""], ["said the courier with a grin, as he lifted his gold-laced cap.", "안내인이 금실로 장식된 모자를 들어 올리며 히죽 웃고 말했다."]], [["\"D---- your French,\"", "\"네 프랑스어 따위 집어치워.\""], ["said the young gentleman, \"where's the biscuits, ay?\"", "젊은 신사가 말했다. \"비스킷은 어디 있어, 응?\""], ["Whereupon Kirsch answered him in the English language or in such an imitation of it as he could command--for though he was familiar with all languages, Mr. Kirsch was not acquainted with a single one, and spoke all with indifferent volubility and incorrectness.", "그러자 키르슈는 영어로, 아니 그가 구사할 수 있는 영어 비슷한 것으로 대답했으니, 키르슈 씨는 온갖 언어에 익숙하긴 했어도 어느 한 언어도 제대로 아는 게 없어서, 어느 말이든 그저 어지간히 술술, 또 어지간히 틀리게 늘어놓을 뿐이었다."]], [["The imperious young gentleman who gobbled the biscuits (and indeed it was time to refresh himself, for he had breakfasted at Richmond full three hours before) was our young friend George Osborne.", "비스킷을 게걸스레 먹어 치우던 그 거만한 젊은 신사는(사실 요기를 할 때도 되긴 했으니, 그는 꼬박 세 시간 전 리치먼드에서 아침을 먹은 터였다) 다름 아닌 우리의 젊은 벗 조지 오즈번이었다."], ["Uncle Jos and his mamma were on the quarter-deck with a gentleman of whom they used to see a good deal, and the four were about to make a summer tour.", "조스 외삼촌과 그의 어머니는 뒷갑판에서, 평소에 자주 만나 보던 한 신사와 함께 있었으니, 이 네 사람이 막 여름 유람을 떠나려는 참이었다."]], [["Jos was seated at that moment on deck under the awning, and pretty nearly opposite to the Earl of Bareacres and his family, whose proceedings absorbed the Bengalee almost entirely.", "조스는 그 순간 갑판 차양 아래에, 베어에이커스 백작 부부와 그 가족의 거의 정면에 앉아 있었는데, 그 일가의 일거수일투족이 이 벵골 사람의 마음을 송두리째 사로잡았다."], ["Both the noble couple looked rather younger than in the eventful year '15, when Jos remembered to have seen them at Brussels (indeed, he always gave out in India that he was intimately acquainted with them). Lady Bareacres'", "그 고귀한 부부는 둘 다, 사건 많던 1815년에 조스가 브뤼셀에서 보았던(사실 그는 인도에서 늘 그들과 막역한 사이라고 떠벌리고 다녔다) 그때보다 오히려 젊어 보였다."], ["hair, which was then dark, was now a beautiful golden auburn, whereas Lord Bareacres' whiskers, formerly red, were at present of a rich black with purple and green reflections in the light.", "그때만 해도 검던 베어에이커스 부인의 머리칼은 이제 고운 황금빛 적갈색이 되었고, 한편 예전엔 붉던 베어에이커스 경의 구레나룻은 이제 짙은 검은빛으로, 빛을 받으면 보랏빛과 초록빛이 어른거렸다."], ["But changed as they were, the movements of the noble pair occupied Jos's mind entirely.", "그러나 그렇게 달라졌어도, 이 고귀한 부부의 거동은 조스의 마음을 완전히 사로잡았다."], ["The presence of a Lord fascinated him, and he could look at nothing else.", "귀족의 존재가 그를 매혹시켜, 그는 다른 어떤 것도 눈에 들어오지 않았다."]], [["\"Those people seem to interest you a good deal,\"", "\"저 사람들한테 꽤나 마음이 끌리시나 봅니다.\""], ["said Dobbin, laughing and watching him.", "도빈이 웃으며 그를 지켜보다가 말했다."], ["Amelia too laughed.", "애밀리아도 웃었다."], ["She was in a straw bonnet with black ribbons, and otherwise dressed in mourning, but the little bustle and holiday of the journey pleased and excited her, and she looked particularly happy.", "그녀는 검은 리본을 단 밀짚 보닛을 쓰고, 그 밖에는 상복 차림이었지만, 여행의 자그마한 분주함과 들뜬 기분이 그녀를 즐겁고 설레게 하여, 유난히 행복해 보였다."]], [["\"What a heavenly day!\"", "\"정말 더없이 좋은 날이에요!\""], ["Emmy said and added, with great originality, \"I hope we shall have a calm passage.\"", "에미가 말하더니, 더없이 독창적이게도 한마디 덧붙였다. \"잔잔한 항해가 됐으면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["Jos waved his hand, scornfully glancing at the same time under his eyelids at the great folks opposite.", "조스는 손을 내저으면서, 그러는 동시에 눈을 내리뜬 채 맞은편의 귀하신 분들을 곁눈질했다."], ["\"If you had made the voyages we have,\"", "\"자네가 우리처럼 항해를 해 봤다면,\""], ["he said, \"you wouldn't much care about the weather.\"", "그가 말했다. \"날씨 따위는 그리 신경 쓰지 않을 걸세.\""], ["But nevertheless, traveller as he was, he passed the night direfully sick in his carriage, where his courier tended him with brandy-and-water and every luxury.", "그러나 그런 말을 해도, 그 자칭 여행통은 그날 밤 자기 마차 안에서 끔찍하게 멀미를 앓으며 보냈고, 안내인이 브랜디에 물을 탄 음료며 온갖 호사스러운 것들로 그를 보살펴 주었다."]], [["In due time this happy party landed at the quays of Rotterdam, whence they were transported by another steamer to the city of Cologne.", "이윽고 이 행복한 일행은 로테르담 부두에 내렸고, 거기서 또 다른 증기선으로 옮겨 타 쾰른 시까지 갔다."], ["Here the carriage and the family took to the shore, and Jos was not a little gratified to see his arrival announced in the Cologne newspapers as \"Herr Graf Lord von Sedley nebst Begleitung aus London.\"", "여기서 마차와 일가족은 뭍에 올랐는데, 조스는 자기 도착 소식이 쾰른 신문에 \"런던에서 온 폰 세들리 백작 경 일행\"이라고 실린 것을 보고 적잖이 흐뭇해했다."], ["He had his court dress with him; he had insisted that Dobbin should bring his regimental paraphernalia; he announced that it was his intention to be presented at some foreign courts, and pay his respects to the Sovereigns of the countries which he honoured with a visit.", "그는 궁정 예복을 챙겨 왔고, 도빈에게도 연대 정장 일습을 가져오라고 우겼다. 그는 몇몇 외국 궁정에 알현하여, 자기가 방문의 영광을 베푸는 나라들의 군주들께 경의를 표할 작정이라고 공언했다."]], [["Wherever the party stopped, and an opportunity was offered, Mr.", "일행이 어디에 머물든, 기회만 생기면 조스 씨는 \"우리 공사 각하\""], ["Jos left his own card and the Major's upon \"Our Minister.\"", "댁에 자기 명함과 소령의 명함을 두고 왔다."], ["It was with great difficulty that he could be restrained from putting on his cocked hat and tights to wait upon the English consul at the Free City of Judenstadt, when that hospitable functionary asked our travellers to dinner.", "유덴슈타트 자유시의 영국 영사가 그 손님 후한 관리로서 우리 여행자들을 만찬에 초대했을 때, 조스가 삼각모를 쓰고 꽉 끼는 바지를 입고서 그를 찾아가겠다는 것을 말리기란 여간 어려운 일이 아니었다."], ["He kept a journal of his voyage and noted elaborately the defects or excellences of the various inns at which he put up, and of the wines and dishes of which he partook.", "그는 항해 일지를 꼬박꼬박 적어, 자기가 묵은 여러 여관의 흠이나 장점을, 또 자기가 맛본 포도주와 요리를 일일이 공들여 기록했다."]], [["As for Emmy, she was very happy and pleased.", "에미로 말하자면, 그녀는 더없이 행복하고 즐거웠다."], ["Dobbin used to carry about for her her stool and sketch-book, and admired the drawings of the good-natured little artist as they never had been admired before.", "도빈은 늘 그녀를 위해 접의자와 스케치북을 들고 다녔고, 마음씨 고운 그 자그마한 화가의 그림을 일찍이 어느 누구도 그래 본 적 없을 만큼 감탄하며 칭찬했다."], ["She sat upon steamers' decks and drew crags and castles, or she mounted upon donkeys and ascended to ancient robber-towers, attended by her two aides-de-camp, Georgy and Dobbin.", "그녀는 증기선 갑판에 앉아 절벽이며 성채를 그렸고, 당나귀에 올라타 옛 도적들의 탑까지 올라가기도 했는데, 그럴 때면 두 부관, 즉 조지와 도빈이 그녀를 따랐다."], ["She laughed, and the Major did too, at his droll figure on donkey-back, with his long legs touching the ground.", "그녀는 당나귀 등에 올라탄 도빈의 우스꽝스러운 모습을, 긴 다리가 땅에 닿는 그 꼴을 보고 웃었고, 소령 자신도 함께 웃었다."], ["He was the interpreter for the party; having a good military knowledge of the German language, and he and the delighted George fought the campaigns of the Rhine and the Palatinate.", "그는 일행의 통역이었으니, 군대에서 익힌 독일어 실력이 제법이었기 때문이다. 그래서 그와 신이 난 조지는 라인란트와 팔츠 지방의 전투들을 함께 누볐다."], ["In the course of a few weeks, and by assiduously conversing with Herr Kirsch on the box of the carriage, Georgy made prodigious advance in the knowledge of High Dutch, and could talk to hotel waiters and postilions in a way that charmed his mother and amused his guardian.", "조지는 몇 주 만에, 마부석에 앉은 키르슈 씨와 부지런히 떠들어 댄 덕에 고지 독일어 실력이 놀랍도록 늘어, 호텔 종업원이며 마부에게 말을 걸어 제 어머니를 흐뭇하게 하고 제 후견인을 즐겁게 했다."]], [["Mr. Jos did not much engage in the afternoon excursions of his fellow-travellers.", "조스 씨는 동행들의 오후 나들이에는 별로 끼지 않았다."], ["He slept a good deal after dinner, or basked in the arbours of the pleasant inn-gardens.", "그는 만찬을 들고 나면 한참 잠을 자거나, 운치 있는 여관 정원의 정자에서 볕을 쬐었다."], ["Pleasant Rhine gardens!", "정겨운 라인 강가의 정원들이여!"], ["Fair scenes of peace and sunshine--noble purple mountains, whose crests are reflected in the magnificent stream--who has ever seen you that has not a grateful memory of those scenes of friendly repose and beauty?", "평화와 햇살의 고운 풍경이여, 그 봉우리가 장엄한 강물에 비치는 저 고귀한 자줏빛 산들이여, 그대들을 한 번이라도 본 사람이라면 그 정답고 아름다운 안식의 풍경을 어찌 고마운 마음으로 기억하지 않으랴?"], ["To lay down the pen and even to think of that beautiful Rhineland makes one happy.", "펜을 내려놓고 그저 그 아름다운 라인란트를 떠올리는 것만으로도 마음이 행복해진다."], ["At this time of summer evening, the cows are trooping down from the hills, lowing and with their bells tinkling, to the old town, with its old moats, and gates, and spires, and chestnut-trees, with long blue shadows stretching over the grass; the sky and the river below flame in crimson and gold; and the moon is already out, looking pale towards the sunset.", "이런 여름 저녁 무렵이면, 소들이 음매 울며 방울을 딸랑이며 언덕에서 줄지어 내려와, 옛 해자와 성문과 첨탑과 밤나무가 있는, 풀밭 위로 길게 푸른 그림자가 드리운 옛 고을로 향한다. 하늘과 그 아래 강물은 진홍과 황금빛으로 불타오르고, 달은 벌써 떠올라 지는 해 쪽을 향해 창백하게 걸려 있다."], ["The sun sinks behind the great castle-crested mountains, the night falls suddenly, the river grows darker and darker, lights quiver in it from the windows in the old ramparts, and twinkle peacefully in the villages under the hills on the opposite shore.", "해가 거대한 성채 얹힌 산봉우리 뒤로 가라앉으면 밤이 문득 내려앉고, 강물은 점점 더 어두워지며, 옛 성벽의 창문에서 불빛이 강물에 일렁이고, 건너편 기슭 언덕 아래 마을들에서 평화롭게 반짝인다."]], [["So Jos used to go to sleep a good deal with his bandanna over his face and be very comfortable, and read all the English news, and every word of Galignani's admirable newspaper (may the blessings of all Englishmen who have ever been abroad rest on the founders and proprietors of that piratical print!", "그리하여 조스는 반다나 손수건을 얼굴에 덮고 한참 잠을 자며 더없이 편안하게 지냈고, 영국 소식을 죄다 읽었으며, 갈리냐니의 그 훌륭한 신문은 한 자도 빠짐없이 읽었다(일찍이 외국에 나가 본 적 있는 모든 영국인의 축복이, 그 해적판 신문을 세운 이들과 그 소유주들에게 깃들기를!)."], [") and whether he woke or slept, his friends did not very much miss him.", "그가 깨어 있든 잠들어 있든, 그의 벗들은 그를 별로 아쉬워하지 않았다."], ["Yes, they were very happy.", "그렇다, 그들은 더없이 행복했다."], ["They went to the opera often of evenings--to those snug, unassuming, dear old operas in the German towns, where the noblesse sits and cries, and knits stockings on the one side, over against the bourgeoisie on the other; and His Transparency the Duke and his Transparent family, all very fat and good-natured, come and occupy the great box in the middle; and the pit is full of the most elegant slim-waisted officers with straw-coloured mustachios, and twopence a day on full pay.", "그들은 저녁이면 자주 오페라를 보러 갔다. 독일 고을들의 그 아늑하고 수수하고 정겨운 옛 오페라 극장으로 말이다."], ["Here it was that Emmy found her delight, and was introduced for the first time to the wonders of Mozart and Cimarosa. The Major's musical taste has been before alluded to, and his performances on the flute commended.", "거기서는 한쪽에 귀족들이 앉아 눈물을 흘리며 양말을 뜨개질하고, 맞은편에는 부르주아들이 앉으며, 다들 몹시 살지고 인심 좋은, 더없이 투명하신 공작 전하와 그 투명하신 가족이 한가운데 큰 특별석을 차지하러 온다."], ["But perhaps the chief pleasure he had in these operas was in watching Emmy's rapture while listening to them.", "그리고 일층 좌석은 더없이 우아하게 허리가 잘록한, 짚빛 콧수염을 기른 장교들로 가득한데, 이들은 정식 봉급이래야 하루 이 펜스를 받는 자들이었다."], ["A new world of love and beauty broke upon her when she was introduced to those divine compositions; this lady had the keenest and finest sensibility, and how could she be indifferent when she heard Mozart?", "바로 이곳에서 에미는 기쁨을 찾았고, 모차르트와 치마로사의 경이를 난생처음 접했다. 소령의 음악적 취향에 대해서는 앞서 언급한 바 있고, 그의 플루트 연주가 칭찬받았다는 것도 말한 바 있다."], ["The tender parts of \"Don Juan\" awakened in her raptures so exquisite that she would ask herself when she went to say her prayers of a night whether it was not wicked to feel so much delight as that with which \"Vedrai Carino\"", "그러나 이 오페라들에서 그가 누린 가장 큰 즐거움은, 아마도 그것을 듣는 동안 황홀경에 빠진 에미의 얼굴을 지켜보는 데 있었을 것이다."], ["and \"Batti Batti\" filled her gentle little bosom?", "저 거룩한 곡들을 처음 접했을 때, 그녀에게는 사랑과 아름다움의 새로운 세계가 활짝 열렸다."], ["But the Major, whom she consulted upon this head, as her theological adviser (and who himself had a pious and reverent soul), said that for his part, every beauty of art or nature made him thankful as well as happy, and that the pleasure to be had in listening to fine music, as in looking at the stars in the sky, or at a beautiful landscape or picture, was a benefit for which we might thank Heaven as sincerely as for any other worldly blessing.", "이 부인은 더없이 예민하고 섬세한 감수성을 지녔으니, 모차르트를 들으면서 어찌 무덤덤할 수 있었으랴? \"돈 후안\"의 애틋한 대목들은 그녀의 마음에 너무도 황홀한 환희를 일깨워, 밤에 기도를 드리러 갈 때면 \"베드라이 카리노\"와 \"바티 바티\"가 그 다정하고 자그마한 가슴을 가득 채운 그토록 큰 기쁨을 느끼는 것이 죄가 아닌지 스스로 묻곤 했다."], ["And in reply to some faint objections of Mrs. Amelia's (taken from certain theological works like the Washerwoman of Finchley Common and others of that school, with which Mrs.", "그러나 그녀가 이 문제를 두고 신학 자문역으로 의논한 소령은(그 자신도 경건하고 신실한 영혼을 지닌 이였다) 자기로 말하자면 예술이든 자연이든 온갖 아름다움이 자기를 행복하게 할 뿐 아니라 감사하게 만든다고, 또 훌륭한 음악을 듣는 데서 얻는 기쁨이란 하늘의 별이나 아름다운 풍경이나 그림을 바라보는 것과 마찬가지로, 다른 어떤 세속의 축복 못지않게 진심으로 하늘에 감사할 만한 은혜라고 말했다."], ["Osborne had been furnished during her life at Brompton) he told her an Eastern fable of the Owl who thought that the sunshine was unbearable for the eyes and that the Nightingale was a most overrated bird.", "그리고 애밀리아 부인이 미약하게나마 이의를 제기하자(이는 핀칠리 코먼의 빨래하는 여인 따위, 오즈번 부인이 브롬프턴에서 지내는 동안 받아 본 그런 부류의 어떤 신학서들에서 따온 것이었다) 그는 그녀에게, 햇빛은 눈에 견딜 수 없는 것이며 나이팅게일은 더없이 과대평가된 새라고 여기던 부엉이에 관한 동방의 우화 하나를 들려주었다."], ["\"It is one's nature to sing and the other's to hoot,\"", "\"노래하는 게 한쪽의 본성이라면 부엉부엉 우는 게 다른 쪽의 본성이지요.\""], ["he said, laughing, \"and with such a sweet voice as you have yourself, you must belong to the Bulbul faction.\"", "그가 웃으며 말했다. \"그리고 부인처럼 그토록 고운 목소리를 지녔으니, 부인은 응당 나이팅게일 편에 속하는 사람입니다.\""]], [["I like to dwell upon this period of her life and to think that she was cheerful and happy.", "나는 그녀의 인생에서 이 시절을 즐겨 곱씹으며, 그녀가 명랑하고 행복했다고 생각하기를 좋아한다."], ["You see, she has not had too much of that sort of existence as yet, and has not fallen in the way of means to educate her tastes or her intelligence.", "보다시피 그녀는 아직 그런 부류의 삶을 그리 많이 누려 보지 못했고, 자기 취향이나 지성을 갈고닦을 길도 만나 보지 못했다."], ["She has been domineered over hitherto by vulgar intellects.", "그녀는 지금껏 천박한 지성들에게 짓눌려 지내 왔다."], ["It is the lot of many a woman.", "이는 숱한 여인의 운명이다."], ["And as every one of the dear sex is the rival of the rest of her kind, timidity passes for folly in their charitable judgments; and gentleness for dulness; and silence--which is but timid denial of the unwelcome assertion of ruling folks, and tacit protestantism--above all, finds no mercy at the hands of the female Inquisition.", "그리고 저 사랑스러운 여성이라는 종족은 하나하나가 모두 제 동족 나머지의 경쟁자인지라, 그들의 너그러운 판단 속에서 수줍음은 어리석음으로 통하고, 온순함은 둔함으로 통하며, 침묵은 — 그것이란 지배하는 자들의 달갑잖은 주장에 대한 소심한 부정이요 말 없는 항변일 뿐인데 — 무엇보다도 저 여성 종교재판소의 손에서 자비라곤 얻지 못한다."], ["Thus, my dear and civilized reader, if you and I were to find ourselves this evening in a society of greengrocers, let us say, it is probable that our conversation would not be brilliant; if, on the other hand, a greengrocer should find himself at your refined and polite tea-table, where everybody was saying witty things, and everybody of fashion and repute tearing her friends to pieces in the most delightful manner, it is possible that the stranger would not be very talkative and by no means interesting or interested.", "그러니 친애하고 교양 있는 독자여, 만약 그대와 내가 오늘 저녁 이를테면 채소 장수들의 모임 한가운데 끼게 된다면, 우리의 대화가 빛나지는 못할 공산이 크다. 반대로, 채소 장수 하나가, 누구나 재치 있는 말을 주고받고, 잘난 체하며 평판 있는 부인네들이 더없이 흐뭇한 솜씨로 제 친구들을 갈가리 헐뜯어 대는, 그대의 세련되고 정중한 다과상 자리에 끼게 된다면, 그 낯선 손님은 그리 말수도 많지 않을 것이요, 결코 흥미롭지도 흥미를 느끼지도 못할 것이다."]], [["And it must be remembered that this poor lady had never met a gentleman in her life until this present moment.", "그리고 잊지 말아야 할 것은, 이 가엾은 부인이 바로 이 순간에 이르기까지 평생 단 한 번도 진정한 신사를 만나 본 적이 없다는 사실이다."], ["Perhaps these are rarer personages than some of us think for.", "어쩌면 그런 인물은 우리들 중 더러가 생각하는 것보다 더 드문지도 모른다."], ["Which of us can point out many such in his circle--men whose aims are generous, whose truth is constant, and not only constant in its kind but elevated in its degree; whose want of meanness makes them simple; who can look the world honestly in the face with an equal manly sympathy for the great and the small?", "우리 가운데 누가 제 주위에서 그런 이를 여럿 가리켜 보일 수 있겠는가? 그 뜻이 너그럽고, 그 진실이 한결같으며, 한결같을 뿐 아니라 그 정도가 고결하고, 비루함이 없는지라 도리어 소박하며, 크고 작은 모든 이를 한결같이 의젓하게 헤아리는 마음으로 세상을 정직하게 마주 볼 수 있는 사내 말이다."], ["We all know a hundred whose coats are very well made, and a score who have excellent manners, and one or two happy beings who are what they call in the inner circles, and have shot into the very centre and bull's-eye of the fashion; but of gentlemen how many?", "옷을 아주 잘 빼입은 자라면 우리는 백 명도 알고, 매너가 빼어난 자라면 스무 명도 알며, 이른바 상류 사교계의 일원이 되어 유행의 한복판, 그 과녁의 정중앙에 명중한 행복한 자도 한둘은 안다. 하지만 진정한 신사로 말하면 과연 몇이나 되겠는가?"], ["Let us take a little scrap of paper and each make out his list.", "우리 모두 종이 한 조각을 꺼내 저마다 그 명단을 적어 보자."]], [["My friend the Major I write, without any doubt, in mine.", "내 벗인 소령을, 나는 한 점 의심 없이 내 명단에 적어 넣는다."], ["He had very long legs, a yellow face, and a slight lisp, which at first was rather ridiculous.", "그는 다리가 무척 길었고, 누런 얼굴에, 처음엔 다소 우스꽝스러웠던 가벼운 혀짤배기소리를 냈다."], ["But his thoughts were just, his brains were fairly good, his life was honest and pure, and his heart warm and humble.", "그러나 그 생각은 올발랐고, 머리는 제법 좋았으며, 삶은 정직하고 순결했고, 마음은 따뜻하고 겸손했다."], ["He certainly had very large hands and feet, which the two George Osbornes used to caricature and laugh at; and their jeers and laughter perhaps led poor little Emmy astray as to his worth.", "그에게 분명 무척 큰 손과 발이 있긴 했으니, 두 조지 오즈번이 그것을 곧잘 흉내 내며 비웃곤 했다. 어쩌면 그들의 조롱과 웃음이 가엾은 어린 에미로 하여금 그의 진가를 그르쳐 보게 만들었는지도 모른다."], ["But have we not all been misled about our heroes and changed our opinions a hundred times?", "그러나 우리 모두 우리의 영웅들에 대해 잘못 알았다가 백 번도 더 생각을 바꾼 적이 있지 않은가?"], ["Emmy, in this happy time, found that hers underwent a very great change in respect of the merits of the Major.", "에미는 이 행복한 시절에, 소령의 진가에 대한 제 생각이 몹시도 크게 달라졌음을 깨달았다."]], [["Perhaps it was the happiest time of both their lives, indeed, if they did but know it--and who does?", "어쩌면 이때가 두 사람 모두의 인생에서 가장 행복한 시절이었는지도 모른다. 정말로, 그들이 그것을 알기만 했더라면 — 한데 누가 그것을 알겠는가?"], ["Which of us can point out and say that was the culmination--that was the summit of human joy?", "우리 가운데 누가, 저것이 절정이었노라, 저것이 인간 기쁨의 정점이었노라 하고 가리켜 말할 수 있겠는가?"], ["But at all events, this couple were very decently contented, and enjoyed as pleasant a summer tour as any pair that left England that year.", "어쨌든 이 두 사람은 제법 흡족하게 만족하여, 그해 잉글랜드를 떠난 그 어느 짝 못지않게 즐거운 여름 유람을 누렸다."], ["Georgy was always present at the play, but it was the Major who put Emmy's shawl on after the entertainment; and in the walks and excursions the young lad would be on ahead, and up a tower-stair or a tree, whilst the soberer couple were below, the Major smoking his cigar with great placidity and constancy, whilst Emmy sketched the site or the ruin.", "조지는 늘 연극을 보러 따라왔지만, 공연이 끝나고 에미의 숄을 어깨에 걸쳐 주는 이는 소령이었다. 그리고 산책이나 나들이를 할 때면 그 소년은 앞장서 탑 계단이나 나무 위로 올라가 있었고, 그동안 좀 더 진중한 두 사람은 아래에서, 소령은 더없이 차분하고 한결같이 시가를 피우고 에미는 그 자리며 폐허를 스케치하곤 했다."], ["It was on this very tour that I, the present writer of a history of which every word is true, had the pleasure to see them first and to make their acquaintance.", "한 마디 한 마디가 모두 진실인 이 이야기를 지금 적고 있는 나 자신이, 그들을 처음 보고 알게 되는 기쁨을 누린 것도 바로 이 유람에서였다."]], [["It was at the little comfortable Ducal town of Pumpernickel (that very place where Sir Pitt Crawley had been so distinguished as an attache; but that was in early early days, and before the news of the Battle of Austerlitz sent all the English diplomatists in Germany to the right about) that I first saw Colonel Dobbin and his party.", "내가 도빈 대령과 그 일행을 처음 본 것은, 아담하고 안락한 공국의 고을 품퍼니켈에서였다(다름 아닌, 피트 크롤리 경이 외교관 수습으로 그토록 두각을 나타냈던 바로 그곳인데, 그건 아주 아주 옛날, 아우스터리츠 전투 소식이 독일에 있던 잉글랜드 외교관들을 죄다 우향우시켜 돌려보내기 이전의 일이었다)."], ["They had arrived with the carriage and courier at the Erbprinz Hotel, the best of the town, and the whole party dined at the table d'hote.", "그들은 마차와 안내인을 거느리고 그 고을 최고의 여관인 에르프린츠 호텔에 도착했고, 일행 모두 정찬에 함께 자리했다."], ["Everybody remarked the majesty of Jos and the knowing way in which he sipped, or rather sucked, the Johannisberger, which he ordered for dinner.", "누구나 조스의 위풍당당함과, 그가 정찬용으로 시킨 요하니스베르거 포도주를 홀짝이는, 아니 빨아 마시는 그 능청스러운 솜씨를 눈여겨보았다."], ["The little boy, too, we observed, had a famous appetite, and consumed schinken, and braten, and kartoffeln, and cranberry jam, and salad, and pudding, and roast fowls, and sweetmeats, with a gallantry that did honour to his nation.", "우리는 그 어린 소년에게도 굉장한 식욕이 있어서, 햄이며 구운 고기며 감자며 크랜베리 잼이며 샐러드며 푸딩이며 통닭 구이며 과자며를, 제 나라의 명예에 누가 되지 않을 만큼 늠름하게 먹어 치우는 것을 보았다. 그는 열다섯 접시쯤 비운 뒤 후식으로 식사를 마무리했는데, 그 후식 중 일부는 아예 바깥으로 들고 나가기까지 했다."], ["After about fifteen dishes, he concluded the repast with dessert, some of which he even carried out of doors, for some young gentlemen at table, amused with his coolness and gallant free-and-easy manner, induced him to pocket a handful of macaroons, which he discussed on his way to the theatre, whither everybody went in the cheery social little German place.", "식탁에 앉아 있던 젊은 신사 몇몇이 그의 능청스러움과 의젓하고 거리낌 없는 태도에 재미를 느껴, 마카롱 한 줌을 호주머니에 챙기라고 부추겼던 것이다. 그는 명랑하고 사람 좋은 그 자그마한 독일 고을에서 다들 향하는 극장으로 가는 길에 그 마카롱을 처치했다."], ["The lady in black, the boy's mamma, laughed and blushed, and looked exceedingly pleased and shy as the dinner went on, and at the various feats and instances of espieglerie on the part of her son.", "검은 옷을 입은 부인, 그 소년의 어머니는 정찬이 이어지는 내내, 또 제 아들이 부린 갖가지 묘기며 장난스러운 짓을 보며 웃고 얼굴을 붉히고, 더없이 흐뭇하면서도 수줍어하는 기색이었다."], ["The Colonel--for so he became very soon afterwards--I remember joked the boy with a great deal of grave fun, pointing out dishes which he hadn't tried, and entreating him not to baulk his appetite, but to have a second supply of this or that.", "대령은 — 그는 그 뒤로 곧 대령이 되었으니 — 내 기억으로는 자못 진지한 익살을 부려 그 소년을 놀려 댔으니, 아직 먹어 보지 않은 요리를 일러 주며, 식욕을 꺾지 말고 이것저것 한 번 더 청해 먹으라고 권하는 것이었다."]], [["It was what they call a gast-rolle night at the Royal Grand Ducal Pumpernickelisch Hof--or Court theatre--and Madame Schroeder Devrient, then in the bloom of her beauty and genius, performed the part of the heroine in the wonderful opera of Fidelio.", "그날 밤은 품퍼니켈 대공 전하의 궁정 극장에서 이른바 객원 출연의 밤이라, 당시 그 미모와 천재성이 한창 무르익은 슈뢰더 데브리엔트 여사가 저 경이로운 오페라 \"피델리오\"에서 여주인공 역을 맡았다. 우리는 일층 좌석에서, 에르프린츠 호텔의 슈벤들러가 제일 귀한 손님들을 위해 잡아 두는 그 특별석에 앉은, 정찬 자리에서 본 우리의 네 친구를 바라볼 수 있었다."], ["From our places in the stalls we could see our four friends of the table d'hote in the loge which Schwendler of the Erbprinz kept for his best guests, and I could not help remarking the effect which the magnificent actress and music produced upon Mrs. Osborne, for so we heard the stout gentleman in the mustachios call her.", "그리고 나는 그 빼어난 여배우와 음악이 오즈번 부인에게 — 콧수염 기른 그 뚱뚱한 신사가 그녀를 그렇게 부르는 것을 들었던 터라 — 자아내는 효과를 눈여겨보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["During the astonishing Chorus of the Prisoners, over which the delightful voice of the actress rose and soared in the most ravishing harmony, the English lady's face wore such an expression of wonder and delight that it struck even little Fipps, the blase attache, who drawled out, as he fixed his glass upon her, \"Gayd, it really does one good to see a woman caypable of that stayt of excaytement.\"", "그 여배우의 황홀한 목소리가 더없이 매혹적인 화음으로 솟구쳐 오르던 저 놀라운 죄수들의 합창 대목에서, 그 잉글랜드 부인의 얼굴에는 어찌나 경이와 환희에 찬 표정이 어렸던지, 시들해진 외교관 어린 핍스마저도 거기에 마음이 동해, 그녀에게 안경을 들이대며 느릿느릿 내뱉었다. \"허 참, 저토록 감동할 줄 아는 여자를 보니 정말 마음이 흐뭇해지는걸.\""], ["And in the Prison Scene, where Fidelio, rushing to her husband, cries, \"Nichts, nichts, mein Florestan,\" she fairly lost herself and covered her face with her handkerchief.", "그리고 피델리오가 제 남편에게 달려가며 \"안 돼요, 안 돼요, 내 플로레스탄\"이라고 외치는 감옥 장면에서, 그녀는 완전히 정신을 잃고 손수건으로 얼굴을 가렸다."], ["Every woman in the house was snivelling at the time, but I suppose it was because it was predestined that I was to write this particular lady's memoirs that I remarked her.", "그때 극장 안의 여인이란 여인은 죄다 훌쩍거리고 있었지만, 내가 유독 그녀를 눈여겨본 것은 아마도 바로 이 부인의 회고록을 쓰도록 미리 정해진 운명이었기 때문이리라."]], [["The next day they gave another piece of Beethoven, Die Schlacht bei Vittoria.", "이튿날에는 베토벤의 또 다른 곡, \"비토리아 전투\"를 연주했다."], ["Malbrook is introduced at the beginning of the performance, as indicative of the brisk advance of the French army.", "공연 첫머리에는 프랑스군의 기세 좋은 진격을 나타내는 표지로 \"말브룩\"이 도입되었다."], ["Then come drums, trumpets, thunders of artillery, and groans of the dying, and at last, in a grand triumphal swell, \"God Save the King\" is performed.", "이어 북소리, 나팔 소리, 우레 같은 포성, 죽어 가는 자들의 신음이 잇따랐고, 마침내 장엄하게 한껏 부풀어 오르며 \"신이여 임금을 지키소서\"가 연주되었다."]], [["There may have been a score of Englishmen in the house, but at the burst of that beloved and well-known music, every one of them, we young fellows in the stalls, Sir John and Lady Bullminster (who had taken a house at Pumpernickel for the education of their nine children), the fat gentleman with the mustachios, the long Major in white duck trousers, and the lady with the little boy upon whom he was so sweet, even Kirsch, the courier in the gallery, stood bolt upright in their places and proclaimed themselves to be members of the dear old British nation.", "극장 안에 잉글랜드 사람이라곤 스무 명쯤밖에 없었을 것이다. 그러나 그 정겹고 익숙한 곡조가 터져 나오자, 그 한 사람 한 사람이, 일층 좌석에 앉은 우리 젊은 패거리며, (아홉 자식을 교육시키려 품퍼니켈에 집을 얻은) 존 불민스터 경 부부며, 콧수염 기른 뚱뚱한 신사며, 흰 무명 바지를 입은 키 큰 소령이며, 그가 그토록 살뜰히 챙기던 어린 소년을 데리고 온 부인이며, 심지어 위층 관람석에 있던 안내인 키르슈까지, 다들 제자리에서 꼿꼿이 일어나 자기들이 정겨운 옛 영국 민족의 일원임을 천명했다."], ["As for Tapeworm, the Charge d'Affaires, he rose up in his box and bowed and simpered, as if he would represent the whole empire.", "대리공사 테이프웜으로 말하면, 그는 마치 제국 전체를 대표하기라도 하는 양 제 특별석에서 일어나 절을 하고 알랑거리는 미소를 지었다."], ["Tapeworm was nephew and heir of old Marshal Tiptoff, who has been introduced in this story as General Tiptoff, just before Waterloo, who was Colonel of the --th regiment in which Major Dobbin served, and who died in this year full of honours, and of an aspic of plovers'", "테이프웜은 늙은 팁토프 원수의 조카이자 상속자였는데, 이 원수는 이 이야기에서 워털루 직전에 팁토프 장군으로 소개된 바 있는 인물로, 도빈 소령이 복무하던 ××연대의 연대장이었으며, 올해 온갖 영예를 누리다가 물떼새 알 젤리를 먹고 세상을 떠났다."], ["eggs; when the regiment was graciously given by his Majesty to Colonel Sir Michael O'Dowd, K.C.B. who had commanded it in many glorious fields.", "그 뒤 폐하께서는 황공하게도 그 연대를, 숱한 영광스러운 전장에서 그것을 지휘했던 바스 훈위 받은 마이클 오다우드 대령 경에게 하사하셨다."]], [["Tapeworm must have met with Colonel Dobbin at the house of the Colonel's Colonel, the Marshal, for he recognized him on this night at the theatre, and with the utmost condescension, his Majesty's minister came over from his own box and publicly shook hands with his new-found friend.", "테이프웜은 분명 그 대령의 연대장인 원수의 댁에서 도빈 대령을 만난 적이 있을 것이다. 그날 밤 극장에서 그를 알아보았으니 말이다. 그리고 폐하의 공사는 더없이 황송한 겸양을 베풀어, 제 특별석에서 건너와 새로 찾은 벗과 공공연히 악수를 나누었다."]], [["\"Look at that infernal sly-boots of a Tapeworm,\"", "\"저 흉악한 능구렁이 테이프웜 좀 보게나.\""], ["Fipps whispered, examining his chief from the stalls.", "핍스가 일층 좌석에서 제 상관을 살피며 속삭였다."], ["\"Wherever there's a pretty woman he always twists himself in.\"", "\"어디든 예쁜 여자만 있으면 어김없이 비집고 들어간다니까.\""], ["And I wonder what were diplomatists made for but for that?", "한데 외교관이란 본디 그러라고 만들어진 게 아니라면 도대체 무엇하러 있단 말인가?"]], [["\"Have I the honour of addressing myself to Mrs. Dobbin?\"", "\"도빈 부인을 뵙는 영광을 누리는 것이 맞습니까?\""], ["asked the Secretary with a most insinuating grin.", "그 서기관이 더없이 알랑거리는 웃음을 지으며 물었다."]], [["Georgy burst out laughing and said, \"By Jove, that was a good 'un.\"", "조지가 웃음을 터뜨리며 말했다. \"거참, 그거 한 방 먹였네.\""], ["Emmy and the Major blushed: we saw them from the stalls.", "에미와 소령은 얼굴을 붉혔다. 우리는 일층 좌석에서 그것을 보았다."]], [["\"This lady is Mrs. George Osborne,\"", "\"이 부인은 조지 오즈번 부인입니다.\""], ["said the Major, \"and this is her brother, Mr.", "소령이 말했다."], ["Sedley, a distinguished officer of the Bengal Civil Service: permit me to introduce him to your lordship.\"", "\"그리고 이쪽은 그 오라버니 되시는 세들리 씨로, 벵골 문관청의 저명한 관리이십니다. 각하께 소개하는 영광을 베풀어 주십시오.\""]], [["My lord nearly sent Jos off his legs with the most fascinating smile.", "각하께서는 더없이 매혹적인 미소로 조스가 거의 다리에 힘이 풀리게 만들었다."], ["\"Are you going to stop in Pumpernickel?\"", "\"품퍼니켈에 머무르실 작정입니까?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"It is a dull place, but we want some nice people, and we would try and make it SO agreeable to you. Mr.--Ahum--Mrs.--Oho.", "\"따분한 곳이긴 합니다만, 우리에겐 멋진 분들이 좀 필요하니, 여러분께 이곳을 더없이 즐거운 곳으로 만들어 드리도록 애써 보지요."], ["I shall do myself the honour of calling upon you to-morrow at your inn.\"", "어 — 음 — 그러니까 — 오호. 내일 여관으로 찾아뵙는 영광을 누리겠습니다.\""], ["And he went away with a Parthian grin and glance which he thought must finish Mrs. Osborne completely.", "그러고는 오즈번 부인을 완전히 끝장내 버리리라 자신하는 작별의 능청스러운 미소와 눈길을 던지며 자리를 떴다."]], [["The performance over, the young fellows lounged about the lobbies, and we saw the society take its departure.", "공연이 끝나자, 우리 젊은 패거리는 로비를 어슬렁거렸고, 우리는 그 사교계 인사들이 떠나는 것을 지켜보았다."], ["The Duchess Dowager went off in her jingling old coach, attended by two faithful and withered old maids of honour, and a little snuffy spindle-shanked gentleman in waiting, in a brown jasey and a green coat covered with orders--of which the star and the grand yellow cordon of the order of St. Michael of Pumpernickel were most conspicuous.", "노공작 미망인은 충직하고 늙어 시든 시녀 둘과, 갈색 가발에 갖가지 훈장으로 뒤덮인 초록 외투를 걸친, 코담배에 절은 비쩍 마른 다리의 자그마한 시종 하나를 거느리고 짤랑거리는 낡은 마차에 올라 떠나갔는데, 그 훈장 가운데서도 품퍼니켈 성 미카엘 훈장의 별과 큼직한 노란 띠가 가장 눈에 띄었다."], ["The drums rolled, the guards saluted, and the old carriage drove away.", "북소리가 울리고, 근위병들이 받들어총을 하자, 그 낡은 마차가 떠나갔다."]], [["Then came his Transparency the Duke and Transparent family, with his great officers of state and household.", "이어서 더없이 투명하신 공작 전하와 그 투명하신 가족이, 국정과 궁정의 고관대작들을 거느리고 나왔다."], ["He bowed serenely to everybody.", "그는 누구에게나 잔잔하게 절을 했다."], ["And amid the saluting of the guards and the flaring of the torches of the running footmen, clad in scarlet, the Transparent carriages drove away to the old Ducal schloss, with its towers and pinacles standing on the schlossberg.", "그리고 근위병들의 받들어총과 진홍색 옷을 입은 횃불잡이 시종들의 활활 타오르는 횃불 속에서, 그 투명하신 마차들은 성채 언덕 위에 탑과 첨봉을 우뚝 세운 옛 공작 성을 향해 달려갔다."], ["Everybody in Pumpernickel knew everybody.", "품퍼니켈에서는 누구나 서로를 다 알았다."], ["No sooner was a foreigner seen there than the Minister of Foreign Affairs, or some other great or small officer of state, went round to the Erbprinz and found out the name of the new arrival.", "외지인이 그곳에 나타나기가 무섭게 외무 장관이나, 아니면 다른 크고 작은 관리 하나가 에르프린츠 호텔로 돌아다니며 그 새 손님의 이름을 알아냈다."]], [["We watched them, too, out of the theatre.", "우리도 그들이 극장에서 나오는 것을 지켜보았다."], ["Tapeworm had just walked off, enveloped in his cloak, with which his gigantic chasseur was always in attendance, and looking as much as possible like Don Juan.", "테이프웜은 이미 망토를 두르고 걸어 나간 뒤였는데, 그 망토는 언제나 그의 거구의 사냥꾼 하인이 들고 시중을 들었고, 그 모습은 영락없이 돈 후안 같았다."], ["The Prime Minister's lady had just squeezed herself into her sedan, and her daughter, the charming Ida, had put on her calash and clogs; when the English party came out, the boy yawning drearily, the Major taking great pains in keeping the shawl over Mrs.", "총리의 부인은 막 자기 가마에 몸을 욱여넣은 참이었고, 그 딸인 매력적인 이다는 두건 모자를 쓰고 나막신을 신은 차림이었다."], ["Osborne's head, and Mr. Sedley looking grand, with a crush opera-hat on one side of his head and his hand in the stomach of a voluminous white waistcoat.", "그때 잉글랜드 일행이 나왔으니, 소년은 따분한 듯 하품을 하고, 소령은 오즈번 부인의 머리 위로 숄을 잘 여며 주느라 무던히도 애를 쓰고, 세들리 씨는 접이식 오페라 모자를 머리 한쪽에 비스듬히 얹고, 폭 넉넉한 흰 조끼의 배 부분에 한 손을 찔러 넣은 채 위풍당당하게 보였다."], ["We took off our hats to our acquaintances of the table d'hote, and the lady, in return, presented us with a little smile and a curtsey, for which everybody might be thankful.", "우리는 정찬 자리에서 알게 된 이들에게 모자를 벗어 인사했고, 그 부인은 답례로 자그마한 미소와 무릎 굽혀 인사를 건넸으니, 누구라도 고마워할 만한 것이었다."]], [["The carriage from the inn, under the superintendence of the bustling Mr.", "분주한 키르슈 씨의 지휘 아래, 여관에서 온 마차가 일행을 모시려 대기하고 있었다."], ["Kirsch, was in waiting to convey the party; but the fat man said he would walk and smoke his cigar on his way homewards, so the other three, with nods and smiles to us, went without Mr. Sedley, Kirsch, with the cigar case, following in his master's wake.", "그러나 그 뚱뚱한 남자는 집으로 가는 길에 걸어가며 시가를 피우겠다고 하여, 나머지 셋은 우리에게 고갯짓과 미소를 보내며 세들리 씨 없이 떠나갔고, 키르슈는 시가 상자를 들고 제 주인의 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["We all walked together and talked to the stout gentleman about the agremens of the place.", "우리는 다 함께 걸으며 그 뚱뚱한 신사에게 그곳의 즐길 거리에 대해 이야기를 나누었다."], ["It was very agreeable for the English.", "잉글랜드 사람들에게는 더없이 쾌적한 곳이었다."], ["There were shooting-parties and battues; there was a plenty of balls and entertainments at the hospitable Court; the society was generally good; the theatre excellent; and the living cheap.", "사냥 모임이며 몰이사냥이 있었고, 손님 후한 궁정에서는 무도회와 연회가 풍성했으며, 사교계도 대체로 훌륭했고, 극장은 더없이 빼어났으며, 생활비는 저렴했다."]], [["\"And our Minister seems a most delightful and affable person,\"", "\"그리고 우리 공사 각하께서도 더없이 유쾌하고 상냥한 분이신 것 같더군요.\""], ["our new friend said.", "우리의 새 친구가 말했다."], ["\"With such a representative, and--and a good medical man, I can fancy the place to be most eligible.", "\"그런 분이 우리를 대표하고, 또 — 또 솜씨 좋은 의사 양반까지 있다면, 이곳이 더없이 살 만한 곳이라 여겨집니다그려."], ["Good-night, gentlemen.\"", "그럼 안녕히들 주무십시오, 신사 여러분.\""], ["And Jos creaked up the stairs to bedward, followed by Kirsch with a flambeau.", "그러고는 조스가 삐걱삐걱 계단을 올라 잠자리로 향했고, 키르슈가 횃불을 들고 그 뒤를 따랐다."], ["We rather hoped that nice-looking woman would be induced to stay some time in the town.", "우리는 그 어여쁜 부인이 이 고을에 한동안 머물도록 이끌리기를 자못 바랐다."]], [["In Which We Meet an Old Acquaintance", "오랜 지인을 만나다"]], [["Such polite behaviour as that of Lord Tapeworm did not fail to have the most favourable effect upon Mr.", "테이프웜 경의 그토록 정중한 처신은 세들리 씨의 마음에 더없이 호의적인 영향을 미치지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Sedley's mind, and the very next morning, at breakfast, he pronounced his opinion that Pumpernickel was the pleasantest little place of any which he had visited on their tour.", "바로 이튿날 아침 식사 자리에서 그는 펌퍼니클이야말로 이번 여행에서 들른 그 어느 곳보다 가장 유쾌한 작은 고장이라는 의견을 내놓았던 것이다."], ["Jos's motives and artifices were not very difficult of comprehension, and Dobbin laughed in his sleeve, like a hypocrite as he was, when he found, by the knowing air of the civilian and the offhand manner in which the latter talked about Tapeworm Castle and the other members of the family, that Jos had been up already in the morning, consulting his travelling Peerage.", "조스의 속셈과 술수란 그리 알아채기 어려운 것이 아니었으니, 도빈은 위선자답게 — 실제로 그는 위선자였다 — 속으로 웃었다. 조스가 그 아침에 벌써 일어나 휴대용 귀족 명감을 들춰 보았다는 사실을, 이 인도 관리의 짐짓 아는 체하는 태도와 테이프웜 성이며 그 가문의 다른 일원들을 거침없이 입에 올리는 무심한 말투에서 알아차렸기 때문이다."], ["Yes, he had seen the Right Honourable the Earl of Bagwig, his lordship's father; he was sure he had, he had met him at--at the Levee--didn't Dob remember?", "그렇다, 그는 그분의 부친이신 배그위그 백작 각하를 뵌 적이 있었다. 분명히 뵈었고, 그분을 — 그 알현식에서 만났는데 — 도빈은 기억나지 않느냐는 것이었다."], ["and when the Diplomatist called on the party, faithful to his promise, Jos received him with such a salute and honours as were seldom accorded to the little Envoy.", "그리고 그 외교관이 약속을 지켜 일행을 찾아오자, 조스는 그 자그마한 공사에게 좀처럼 베풀어지지 않는 경례와 예우로 그를 맞이했다."], ["He winked at Kirsch on his Excellency's arrival, and that emissary, instructed before-hand, went out and superintended an entertainment of cold meats, jellies, and other delicacies, brought in upon trays, and of which Mr. Jos absolutely insisted that his noble guest should partake.", "그는 각하가 도착하자 키르슈에게 눈짓을 보냈고, 미리 지시받은 그 심부름꾼은 밖으로 나가 쟁반에 담겨 들어오는 냉육이며 젤리며 그 밖의 진미들로 차린 다과상을 지휘 감독했으니, 조스 씨는 자신의 고귀한 손님이 반드시 그것을 들어야 한다고 한사코 우겼던 것이다."]], [["Tapeworm, so long as he could have an opportunity of admiring the bright eyes of Mrs. Osborne (whose freshness of complexion bore daylight remarkably well) was not ill pleased to accept any invitation to stay in Mr.", "테이프웜은 오스본 부인의 빛나는 두 눈을 — 그 산뜻한 안색은 대낮에도 놀랄 만큼 잘 견뎌 냈다 — 흠모할 기회를 누릴 수 있는 한, 세들리 씨의 거처에 머물러 달라는 그 어떤 초대도 기꺼이 받아들였다."], ["Sedley's lodgings; he put one or two dexterous questions to him about India and the dancing-girls there; asked Amelia about that beautiful boy who had been with her; and complimented the astonished little woman upon the prodigious sensation which she had made in the house; and tried to fascinate Dobbin by talking of the late war and the exploits of the Pumpernickel contingent under the command of the Hereditary Prince, now Duke of Pumpernickel.", "그는 세들리 씨에게 인도며 그곳의 무희들에 관해 한두 가지 능란한 질문을 던졌고, 애밀리아에게는 그녀와 함께 있던 그 아름다운 소년에 대해 물었으며, 그녀가 이 집안에 일으킨 어마어마한 반향을 두고 어리둥절해하는 이 자그마한 부인에게 찬사를 보냈다. 또한 지난 전쟁과, 지금은 펌퍼니클 공작이 된 당시의 세습 공자 휘하에서 펌퍼니클 파견 부대가 세운 무공을 화제 삼아 도빈을 매료시키려 들었다."]], [["Lord Tapeworm inherited no little portion of the family gallantry, and it was his happy belief that almost every woman upon whom he himself cast friendly eyes was in love with him.", "테이프웜 경은 그 가문 특유의 여성 편력 기질을 적잖이 물려받은 사람이었고, 자신이 친근한 눈길을 던지는 여인이라면 거의 누구나 자기에게 반했다고 믿는 행복한 신념의 소유자였다."], ["He left Emmy under the persuasion that she was slain by his wit and attractions and went home to his lodgings to write a pretty little note to her.", "그는 에미를 두고 자신의 재치와 매력에 그녀가 넘어갔다고 굳게 믿으며 자리를 떴고, 거처로 돌아가서는 그녀에게 보낼 예쁘장한 짧은 쪽지를 썼다."], ["She was not fascinated, only puzzled, by his grinning, his simpering, his scented cambric handkerchief, and his high-heeled lacquered boots.", "그러나 그녀는 그의 히죽거리는 웃음과 새치름한 미소, 향내 나는 고급 무명 손수건, 굽 높은 칠피 부츠에 매료되기는커녕 그저 어리둥절할 따름이었다."], ["She did not understand one-half the compliments which he paid; she had never, in her small experience of mankind, met a professional ladies'", "그녀는 그가 늘어놓는 찬사의 절반도 알아듣지 못했다. 사람을 겪어 본 그 짧은 경험 안에서 그녀는 직업적인 여성 편력가를 아직 한 번도 만나 본 적이 없었던 것이다."], ["man as yet, and looked upon my lord as something curious rather than pleasant; and if she did not admire, certainly wondered at him.", "그래서 그녀는 이 나리를 유쾌하다기보다 신기한 존재쯤으로 여겼고, 흠모하지는 않았더라도 신기해한 것만은 분명했다."], ["Jos, on the contrary, was delighted.", "반면 조스는 더없이 기뻐했다."], ["\"How very affable his Lordship is,\"", "\"각하께서는 어찌나 상냥하신지.\""], ["he said; \"How very kind of his Lordship to say he would send his medical man!", "그가 말했다. \"의사 양반을 보내 주시겠다니, 각하께서 어찌나 친절하신지!"], ["Kirsch, you will carry our cards to the Count de Schlusselback directly; the Major and I will have the greatest pleasure in paying our respects at Court as soon as possible.", "키르슈, 자네는 당장 슐뤼셀바흐 백작께 우리 명함을 전하게. 소령과 나는 되도록 빨리 궁정에 문안을 올리는 더없는 영광을 누리겠네."], ["Put out my uniform, Kirsch--both our uniforms.", "내 예복을 꺼내 두게, 키르슈 — 우리 둘 예복 다 말일세."], ["It is a mark of politeness which every English gentleman ought to show to the countries which he visits to pay his respects to the sovereigns of those countries as to the representatives of his own.\"", "영국 신사라면 누구나, 자신이 방문하는 나라의 군주들께 자기 나라 대표를 대하듯 문안을 올리는 것이 마땅히 보여야 할 예의의 표시지.\""]], [["When Tapeworm's doctor came, Doctor von Glauber, Body Physician to H.S.H.", "테이프웜의 주치의가 왔으니, 공작 전하의 시의인 폰 글라우버 박사였다."], ["the Duke, he speedily convinced Jos that the Pumpernickel mineral springs and the Doctor's particular treatment would infallibly restore the Bengalee to youth and slimness.", "그는 펌퍼니클의 광천수와 자신의 특별 치료법이라면 이 벵골 신사를 틀림없이 젊음과 날씬함으로 되돌려 놓으리라고 조스를 금세 납득시켰다. \"작년에 여기 오신 분이 있디오.\""], ["\"Dere came here last year,\"", "그가 말했다."], ["he said, \"Sheneral Bulkeley, an English Sheneral, tvice so pic as you, sir.", "\"벌클리 장군이라고, 영국 장군인데, 선생보다 두 배는 뚱뚱했디오."], ["I sent him back qvite tin after tree months, and he danced vid Baroness Glauber at the end of two.\"", "석 달 만에 아주 홀쭉하게 만들어 돌려보냈고, 두 달 만에 그분은 글라우버 남작 부인과 춤을 추었디오.\""]], [["Jos's mind was made up; the springs, the Doctor, the Court, and the Charge d'Affaires convinced him, and he proposed to spend the autumn in these delightful quarters.", "조스의 마음은 정해졌다. 광천수며 박사며 궁정이며 대리 공사가 그를 설득해 냈으니, 그는 이 유쾌한 거처에서 가을을 보내기로 작정했다."], ["And punctual to his word, on the next day the Charge d'Affaires presented Jos and the Major to Victor Aurelius XVII, being conducted to their audience with that sovereign by the Count de Schlusselback, Marshal of the Court.", "그리고 약속에 어김없이, 이튿날 대리 공사는 궁정 의전관인 슐뤼셀바흐 백작의 안내를 받아 조스와 소령을 빅토르 아우렐리우스 17세께 알현시켰다."]], [["They were straightway invited to dinner at Court, and their intention of staying in the town being announced, the politest ladies of the whole town instantly called upon Mrs.", "그들은 곧바로 궁정 만찬에 초대받았고, 이 고장에 머물 작정이라는 소식이 알려지자 온 도시에서 가장 정중한 부인들이 즉각 오스본 부인을 예방했다."], ["Osborne; and as not one of these, however poor they might be, was under the rank of a Baroness, Jos's delight was beyond expression.", "그리고 이들 중에는 아무리 가난한 이라도 남작 부인보다 신분이 낮은 자가 하나도 없었으니, 조스의 기쁨은 이루 말할 수가 없었다."], ["He wrote off to Chutney at the Club to say that the Service was highly appreciated in Germany, that he was going to show his friend, the Count de Schlusselback, how to stick a pig in the Indian fashion, and that his august friends, the Duke and Duchess, were everything that was kind and civil.", "그는 클럽의 처트니에게 편지를 부쳐, 독일에서는 인도 근무 경력이 대단히 높이 평가받고 있다는 것, 자신이 벗인 슐뤼셀바흐 백작에게 인도식으로 멧돼지를 창으로 찌르는 법을 가르쳐 줄 작정이라는 것, 그리고 자신의 존귀한 벗인 공작 부처가 더없이 친절하고 정중하더라는 것을 알렸다."]], [["Emmy, too, was presented to the august family, and as mourning is not admitted in Court on certain days, she appeared in a pink crape dress with a diamond ornament in the corsage, presented to her by her brother, and she looked so pretty in this costume that the Duke and Court (putting out of the question the Major, who had scarcely ever seen her before in an evening dress, and vowed that she did not look five-and-twenty) all admired her excessively.", "에미 또한 그 존귀한 가문에 소개되었으니, 특정한 날에는 궁정에서 상복이 허용되지 않으므로, 그녀는 오라비가 선물한 다이아몬드 장식을 가슴께에 단 분홍 크레이프 드레스 차림으로 나섰다. 이 의상을 걸친 그녀가 어찌나 어여뻤던지, 공작과 궁정 사람들은 — 그녀가 야회복을 입은 모습을 거의 본 적이 없어 도무지 스물다섯 살로는 보이지 않는다고 장담한 소령은 논외로 치더라도 — 모두 그녀를 지나치게 흠모했다."]], [["In this dress she walked a Polonaise with Major Dobbin at a Court ball, in which easy dance Mr.", "이 드레스 차림으로 그녀는 궁정 무도회에서 도빈 소령과 폴로네즈를 추었는데, 이 느긋한 춤에서 조스 씨는 슐뤼셀바흐 백작 부인을 인도하는 영광을 누렸다."], ["Jos had the honour of leading out the Countess of Schlusselback, an old lady with a hump back, but with sixteen good quarters of nobility and related to half the royal houses of Germany.", "등이 굽은 노부인이었지만 열여섯 대를 거슬러 올라가는 어엿한 귀족 혈통을 지녔고, 독일 왕가의 절반과 연이 닿은 분이었다."]], [["Pumpernickel stands in the midst of a happy valley through which sparkles--to mingle with the Rhine somewhere, but I have not the map at hand to say exactly at what point--the fertilizing stream of the Pump.", "펌퍼니클은 어느 행복한 골짜기 한복판에 자리하고 있는데, 그 골짜기를 가로질러 비옥한 펌프 강 줄기가 반짝이며 흐른다 — 어딘가에서 라인 강과 합류하긴 하지만, 지금 지도가 손에 없어 정확히 어느 지점인지는 말할 수 없다. 어떤 곳에서 이 강은 나룻배 한 척을 띄울 만큼 넓고, 또 어떤 곳에서는 물방앗간 하나를 돌릴 만하다."], ["In some places the river is big enough to support a ferry-boat, in others to turn a mill; in Pumpernickel itself, the last Transparency but three, the great and renowned Victor Aurelius XIV built a magnificent bridge, on which his own statue rises, surrounded by water-nymphs and emblems of victory, peace, and plenty; he has his foot on the neck of a prostrate Turk--history says he engaged and ran a Janissary through the body at the relief of Vienna by Sobieski--but, quite undisturbed by the agonies of that prostrate Mahometan, who writhes at his feet in the most ghastly manner, the Prince smiles blandly and points with his truncheon in the direction of the Aurelius Platz, where he began to erect a new palace that would have been the wonder of his age had the great-souled Prince but had funds to complete it.", "펌퍼니클 시 자체에는, 지금으로부터 네 분 전의 전하이신 위대하고 명성 높은 빅토르 아우렐리우스 14세께서 웅장한 다리 하나를 세우셨으니, 그 위에는 물의 요정들과 승리, 평화, 풍요의 상징들에 둘러싸인 그분 자신의 조각상이 솟아 있다. 그분은 엎드린 터키인의 목을 발로 밟고 있는데 — 역사에 따르면 소비에스키의 빈 구원전 당시 그분이 한 예니체리 병사와 맞붙어 그 몸을 창으로 꿰뚫었다고 한다 — 그러나 발치에서 더없이 끔찍한 모습으로 몸부림치는 그 엎드린 마호메트교도의 고통 따위에는 전혀 아랑곳없이, 공자는 부드럽게 미소 지으며 지휘봉으로 아우렐리우스 광장 쪽을 가리키고 있다."], ["But the completion of Monplaisir (Monblaisir the honest German folks call it) was stopped for lack of ready money, and it and its park and garden are now in rather a faded condition, and not more than ten times big enough to accommodate the Court of the reigning Sovereign.", "바로 그곳에 그분이 새 궁전을 짓기 시작했으니, 만약 이 도량 넓은 공자께 그것을 완성할 자금만 있었더라면 당대의 경이가 되었을 터였다. 그러나 몽플레지르(정직한 독일 사람들은 몽블레지르라 부른다)의 완공은 현금 부족으로 중단되었고, 그것과 그 정원이며 뜰은 이제 다소 빛바랜 상태에 놓여, 현 군주의 궁정을 수용하기에 열 배 이상은 너끈할 정도밖에 안 되게 되었다."]], [["The gardens were arranged to emulate those of Versailles, and amidst the terraces and groves there are some huge allegorical waterworks still, which spout and froth stupendously upon fete-days, and frighten one with their enormous aquatic insurrections. There is the Trophonius'", "정원은 베르사유의 그것을 본떠 꾸며졌으니, 테라스와 숲 사이로는 거대한 우의적 분수 장치 몇 점이 아직도 남아 있어, 축제일이면 어마어마하게 물을 뿜고 거품을 일으키며 그 엄청난 물의 봉기로 사람을 놀라게 한다."], ["cave in which, by some artifice, the leaden Tritons are made not only to spout water, but to play the most dreadful groans out of their lead conchs--there is the nymphbath and the Niagara cataract, which the people of the neighbourhood admire beyond expression, when they come to the yearly fair at the opening of the Chamber, or to the fetes with which the happy little nation still celebrates the birthdays and marriage-days of its princely governors.", "트로포니우스의 동굴이 있는데, 거기서는 어떤 장치로 인해 납으로 된 트리톤들이 물을 뿜을 뿐 아니라 자신들의 납 소라고둥에서 더없이 끔찍한 신음을 토해 내도록 되어 있다. 또 요정의 목욕탕과 나이아가라 폭포가 있으니, 인근 주민들은 의회가 열리는 시기의 연례 장날에 오거나, 이 행복한 작은 나라가 여전히 자기네 공자 통치자들의 생일과 결혼일을 기념하는 축제 때 와서 이것들을 이루 말할 수 없이 흠모하는 것이다."]], [["Then from all the towns of the Duchy, which stretches for nearly ten mile--from Bolkum, which lies on its western frontier bidding defiance to Prussia, from Grogwitz, where the Prince has a hunting-lodge, and where his dominions are separated by the Pump River from those of the neighbouring Prince of Potzenthal; from all the little villages, which besides these three great cities, dot over the happy principality--from the farms and the mills along the Pump come troops of people in red petticoats and velvet head-dresses, or with three-cornered hats and pipes in their mouths, who flock to the Residenz and share in the pleasures of the fair and the festivities there.", "그러면 거의 십 마일에 걸쳐 뻗어 있는 이 공국의 온 도시에서 — 프로이센에 도전하듯 서쪽 변경에 자리한 볼쿰에서, 공자가 사냥용 별장을 둔 그로크비츠에서, 그곳에서는 펌프 강이 그의 영토를 이웃 포첸탈 공의 영토와 갈라놓는데 — 이 세 대도시 말고도 이 행복한 공국에 점점이 흩어진 온갖 자그마한 마을들에서, 펌프 강을 따라 늘어선 농가와 물방앗간에서, 빨간 페티코트에 벨벳 머리쓰개를 한 사람들이며, 삼각모를 쓰고 입에 파이프를 문 사람들이 무리 지어, 수도로 몰려와 그곳의 장날과 축제의 즐거움을 함께 나눈다."], ["Then the theatre is open for nothing, then the waters of Monblaisir begin to play (it is lucky that there is company to behold them, for one would be afraid to see them alone)--then there come mountebanks and riding troops (the way in which his Transparency was fascinated by one of the horse-riders is well known, and it is believed that La Petite Vivandiere, as she was called, was a spy in the French interest), and the delighted people are permitted to march through room after room of the Grand Ducal palace and admire the slippery floor, the rich hangings, and the spittoons at the doors of all the innumerable chambers.", "그러면 극장은 무료로 개방되고, 몽블레지르의 분수가 물을 뿜기 시작하며(그것을 구경할 일행이 있다는 건 다행스러운 일이다, 혼자 본다면 무서울 테니까), 그러면 약장수들과 곡마단이 찾아온다(전하께서 그 곡마 단원 하나에게 매혹되신 사연은 널리 알려져 있고, 사람들이 라 프티트 비방디에르라 부르던 그 여자가 프랑스 측 첩자였다는 설이 있다). 그러면 들뜬 사람들은 대공 궁전의 이 방 저 방을 줄지어 행진하며 미끄러운 마룻바닥이며 화려한 휘장이며 그 수많은 방마다 문가에 놓인 타구를 흠모하도록 허락받는다."], ["There is one Pavilion at Monblaisir which Aurelius Victor XV had arranged--a great Prince but too fond of pleasure--and which I am told is a perfect wonder of licentious elegance.", "몽블레지르에는 아우렐리우스 빅토르 15세께서 — 위대한 공자였으나 향락을 지나치게 좋아하셨던 분이다 — 꾸미신 별관이 하나 있는데, 내가 듣기로는 음탕한 우아함의 완벽한 경이라 한다."], ["It is painted with the story of Bacchus and Ariadne, and the table works in and out of the room by means of a windlass, so that the company was served without any intervention of domestics.", "그곳은 바쿠스와 아리아드네의 이야기로 그려져 있고, 식탁은 권양기를 이용해 방 안팎으로 드나들게 되어 있어, 일행은 하인의 시중을 전혀 받지 않고도 식사 대접을 받을 수 있었다."], ["But the place was shut up by Barbara, Aurelius XV's widow, a severe and devout Princess of the House of Bolkum and Regent of the Duchy during her son's glorious minority, and after the death of her husband, cut off in the pride of his pleasures.", "그러나 이곳은 아우렐리우스 15세의 미망인 바르바라에 의해 폐쇄되었으니, 볼쿰 가문 출신의 엄격하고 독실한 공녀이자, 향락의 절정에서 꺾인 남편이 세상을 떠난 뒤 아들의 영광스러운 미성년기 동안 공국을 다스린 섭정이었다."]], [["The theatre of Pumpernickel is known and famous in that quarter of Germany.", "펌퍼니클의 극장은 독일의 그 지방에서 알려지고 또 유명한 곳이다."], ["It languished a little when the present Duke in his youth insisted upon having his own operas played there, and it is said one day, in a fury, from his place in the orchestra, when he attended a rehearsal, broke a bassoon on the head of the Chapel Master, who was conducting, and led too slow; and during which time the Duchess Sophia wrote domestic comedies, which must have been very dreary to witness.", "현 공작이 젊은 시절 자신이 작곡한 오페라를 그곳에서 상연하라고 우긴 탓에 한동안 침체되기도 했다. 들리는 말로는 어느 날 그가 연습을 참관하다가 오케스트라석에서 격분하여, 너무 느리게 지휘하던 악장의 머리를 바순으로 내리쳐 부러뜨렸다고 한다."], ["But the Prince executes his music in private now, and the Duchess only gives away her plays to the foreigners of distinction who visit her kind little Court.", "그동안 소피아 공작 부인은 가정극을 썼는데, 그것을 지켜보기란 무척이나 따분한 노릇이었을 게 틀림없다. 그러나 공자는 이제 자신의 음악을 사사로이 연주하고, 공작 부인은 그저 자신의 친절한 작은 궁정을 찾는 명망 높은 외국인들에게 자기 희곡을 나눠 줄 뿐이다."]], [["It is conducted with no small comfort and splendour.", "이 궁정은 적잖은 안락과 화려함으로 운영된다."], ["When there are balls, though there may be four hundred people at supper, there is a servant in scarlet and lace to attend upon every four, and every one is served on silver.", "무도회가 열리면 만찬에 사백 명이 모인다 해도 네 사람마다 진홍색 정장에 레이스를 두른 하인 하나가 시중을 들고, 모두 은식기로 대접받는다."], ["There are festivals and entertainments going continually on, and the Duke has his chamberlains and equerries, and the Duchess her mistress of the wardrobe and ladies of honour, just like any other and more potent potentates.", "축제와 연회가 끊임없이 이어지고, 공작에게는 시종들과 마부장들이 있으며 공작 부인에게는 의상 담당 상궁과 시녀들이 있으니, 다른 더 강성한 군주들과 다를 바가 없다."]], [["The Constitution is or was a moderate despotism, tempered by a Chamber that might or might not be elected.", "이 나라의 헌정 체제는 — 지금도 그렇거니와 — 의회로 다소 완화된 온건한 전제정이었는데, 그 의회란 선출될 수도 있고 안 될 수도 있는 것이었다."], ["I never certainly could hear of its sitting in my time at Pumpernickel.", "적어도 내가 펌퍼니클에 머무는 동안 그 의회가 열렸다는 소리는 한 번도 듣지 못한 게 확실하다."], ["The Prime Minister had lodgings in a second floor, and the Foreign Secretary occupied the comfortable lodgings over Zwieback's Conditorey.", "총리는 어느 건물 삼 층에 거처를 두었고, 외무 장관은 츠비바크 제과점 위층의 안락한 거처에 묵었다."], ["The army consisted of a magnificent band that also did duty on the stage, where it was quite pleasant to see the worthy fellows marching in Turkish dresses with rouge on and wooden scimitars, or as Roman warriors with ophicleides and trombones--to see them again, I say, at night, after one had listened to them all the morning in the Aurelius Platz, where they performed opposite the cafe where we breakfasted.", "군대라곤 무대에서도 임무를 수행하는 멋들어진 악단 하나뿐이어서, 그 훌륭한 친구들이 연지를 바르고 나무 언월도를 차고 터키 복장으로 행진하는 모습이나, 오피클라이드와 트롬본을 든 로마 전사 차림으로 나오는 모습을 보는 것은 자못 유쾌한 노릇이었다 — 다시 말하건대, 아침 내내 아우렐리우스 광장에서 그들의 연주를 들은 뒤에 — 그곳에서 그들은 우리가 아침을 먹던 카페 맞은편에서 연주했다 — 밤에 다시 그들을 보는 것 말이다."], ["Besides the band, there was a rich and numerous staff of officers, and, I believe, a few men.", "악단 말고도 풍성하고 수많은 장교진이 있었고, 내 생각엔 사병도 몇 명 있었던 것 같다."], ["Besides the regular sentries, three or four men, habited as hussars, used to do duty at the Palace, but I never saw them on horseback, and au fait, what was the use of cavalry in a time of profound peace?--and whither the deuce should the hussars ride?", "정규 보초들 말고도, 후사르 차림을 한 서너 명이 궁전에서 근무하곤 했으나, 나는 그들이 말 탄 모습을 한 번도 본 적이 없다. 하긴, 깊은 평화의 시절에 기병이 무슨 소용이며 — 도대체 후사르들이 어디로 말을 달려간단 말인가?"]], [["Everybody--everybody that was noble of course, for as for the bourgeois we could not quite be expected to take notice of THEM--visited his neighbour. H.", "누구나 — 물론 귀족 신분인 이는 누구나, 부르주아로 말하자면 우리가 그들에게까지 관심을 둘 수야 없는 노릇이니 — 이웃을 방문했다."], ["E. Madame de Burst received once a week, H.", "폰 부르스트 부인 각하는 일주일에 한 번 손님을 맞았고, 폰 슈누르바르트 부인 각하는 자기 야회 날이 따로 있었다."], ["E. Madame de Schnurrbart had her night--the theatre was open twice a week, the Court graciously received once, so that a man's life might in fact be a perfect round of pleasure in the unpretending Pumpernickel way.", "극장은 일주일에 두 번 열렸고, 궁정은 너그럽게도 일주일에 한 번 손님을 맞았으니, 실로 사람이 이 허세 없는 펌퍼니클식 방식대로 완벽하게 즐거움이 끊이지 않는 삶을 누릴 수 있는 셈이었다."]], [["That there were feuds in the place, no one can deny.", "이곳에 반목이 없었다고는 누구도 부인할 수 없을 것이다."], ["Politics ran very high at Pumpernickel, and parties were very bitter.", "펌퍼니클에서는 정치가 대단히 격렬했고, 당파 간의 알력도 몹시 신랄했다."], ["There was the Strumpff faction and the Lederlung party, the one supported by our envoy and the other by the French Charge d'Affaires, M.", "슈트룸프 파와 레더룽 파가 있었으니, 전자는 우리 공사가 후원하고 후자는 프랑스 대리 공사 마카보 씨가 후원했다."], ["de Macabau. Indeed it sufficed for our Minister to stand up for Madame Strumpff, who was clearly the greater singer of the two, and had three more notes in her voice than Madame Lederlung her rival--it sufficed, I say, for our Minister to advance any opinion to have it instantly contradicted by the French diplomatist.", "사실 우리 공사로서는, 둘 중 분명 더 뛰어난 가수이고 경쟁자인 레더룽 부인보다 음역이 세 음이나 높은 슈트룸프 부인의 편을 들기만 하면 그만이었다 — 다시 말하건대, 우리 공사가 무슨 의견을 내놓기만 하면 그 프랑스 외교관이 즉각 반박하기에 충분했다는 말이다."]], [["Everybody in the town was ranged in one or other of these factions.", "도시의 모든 이가 이 두 파 가운데 어느 한쪽에 줄을 섰다."], ["The Lederlung was a prettyish little creature certainly, and her voice (what there was of it) was very sweet, and there is no doubt that the Strumpff was not in her first youth and beauty, and certainly too stout; when she came on in the last scene of the Sonnambula, for instance, in her night-chemise with a lamp in her hand, and had to go out of the window, and pass over the plank of the mill, it was all she could do to squeeze out of the window, and the plank used to bend and creak again under her weight--but how she poured out the finale of the opera!", "레더룽은 확실히 제법 어여쁘고 자그마한 여자였고, 그 목소리는(있는 만큼은) 무척 감미로웠다. 그리고 슈트룸프가 첫 젊음과 미모의 시절을 지난 데다 분명히 너무 뚱뚱했다는 것도 의심할 여지가 없다."], ["and with what a burst of feeling she rushed into Elvino's arms--almost fit to smother him!", "가령 그녀가 「몽유병의 여인」 마지막 장면에서 잠옷 차림으로 손에 등잔을 들고 나와 창문을 넘어 물방앗간 널빤지를 건너야 할 때면, 창문으로 몸을 비집고 나오는 것만도 그녀로선 힘에 부쳤고, 널빤지는 그 무게에 휘어지며 다시금 삐걱대곤 했다 — 그러나 그녀가 오페라의 피날레를 얼마나 쏟아 내듯 불렀던가!"], ["Whereas the little Lederlung--but a truce to this gossip--the fact is that these two women were the two flags of the French and the English party at Pumpernickel, and the society was divided in its allegiance to those two great nations.", "또 얼마나 북받치는 감정으로 엘비노의 품에 뛰어들었던가 — 그를 거의 질식시킬 지경이었다! 반면 자그마한 레더룽으로 말하자면 — 그러나 이런 잡담은 이쯤 해 두자 — 사실인즉 이 두 여자는 펌퍼니클에서 프랑스 파와 영국 파의 두 깃발이었고, 사교계는 이 두 위대한 나라에 대한 충성을 두고 둘로 갈라져 있었던 것이다."]], [["We had on our side the Home Minister, the Master of the Horse, the Duke's Private Secretary, and the Prince's Tutor; whereas of the French party were the Foreign Minister, the Commander-in-Chief's Lady, who had served under Napoleon, and the Hof-Marschall and his wife, who was glad enough to get the fashions from Paris, and always had them and her caps by M. de Macabau's courier.", "우리 편에는 내무 장관, 마사관, 공작의 비서, 공자의 가정교사가 있었던 반면, 프랑스 파에는 외무 장관과, 나폴레옹 휘하에서 복무했던 총사령관의 부인, 그리고 궁정 의전관과 그의 아내가 있었으니, 이 아내는 파리에서 유행을 들여오는 것을 기뻐하여 늘 마카보 씨의 급사를 통해 유행 물품과 모자를 받아 보았다."], ["The Secretary of his Chancery was little Grignac, a young fellow, as malicious as Satan, and who made caricatures of Tapeworm in all the albums of the place.", "그 공사관 비서는 자그마한 그리냐크라는 젊은이로, 사탄처럼 짓궂어서 이 고장의 온갖 앨범마다 테이프웜의 풍자화를 그려 넣곤 했다."]], [["Their headquarters and table d'hote were established at the Pariser Hof, the other inn of the town; and though, of course, these gentlemen were obliged to be civil in public, yet they cut at each other with epigrams that were as sharp as razors, as I have seen a couple of wrestlers in Devonshire, lashing at each other's shins and never showing their agony upon a muscle of their faces.", "그들의 본거지와 정식 회식 장소는 이 도시의 다른 여관인 파리저 호프에 자리하고 있었다. 그리고 물론 이 신사들은 공석에서야 정중하게 굴 수밖에 없었지만, 마치 데번셔에서 한 쌍의 씨름꾼이 서로의 정강이를 후려치면서도 얼굴 근육 하나 까딱 않고 고통을 감추는 것을 내가 본 것처럼, 면도날같이 예리한 경구로 서로를 베어 댔다."], ["Neither Tapeworm nor Macabau ever sent home a dispatch to his government without a most savage series of attacks upon his rival.", "테이프웜도 마카보도 자기 정부에 공문을 보낼 때면 경쟁자에 대한 더없이 잔혹한 일련의 공격을 빼놓는 법이 없었다. 가령 우리 쪽에서는 이렇게 쓰는 것이었다."], ["For instance, on our side we would write, \"The interests of Great Britain in this place, and throughout the whole of Germany, are perilled by the continuance in office of the present French envoy; this man is of a character so infamous that he will stick at no falsehood, or hesitate at no crime, to attain his ends.", "\"이곳, 그리고 독일 전역에서 영국의 이익은 현 프랑스 공사의 유임으로 위태로워지고 있습니다. 이자는 그 성품이 어찌나 파렴치한지, 제 목적을 이루기 위해서라면 어떤 거짓말도 마다하지 않고 어떤 죄악도 서슴지 않을 위인입니다."], ["He poisons the mind of the Court against the English minister, represents the conduct of Great Britain in the most odious and atrocious light, and is unhappily backed by a minister whose ignorance and necessities are as notorious as his influence is fatal.\"", "그는 궁정의 마음에 영국 공사에 대한 독을 풀어 넣고, 영국의 처신을 더없이 가증스럽고 흉악한 것으로 그려 보이는데, 불행히도 그 무지와 궁핍이 그 영향력만큼이나 치명적이고 또 악명 높은 한 장관의 비호를 받고 있습니다.\""], ["On their side they would say, \"M.", "그쪽 편에서는 또 이렇게 쓰는 것이었다."], ["de Tapeworm continues his system of stupid insular arrogance and vulgar falsehood against the greatest nation in the world.", "\"테이프웜 씨는 세계 최대의 국민을 상대로 멍청한 섬나라식 오만과 천박한 거짓말의 행태를 계속하고 있다."], ["Yesterday he was heard to speak lightly of Her Royal Highness Madame the Duchess of Berri; on a former occasion he insulted the heroic Duke of Angouleme and dared to insinuate that H.R.H. the Duke of Orleans was conspiring against the august throne of the lilies.", "어제 그는 베리 공작 부인 전하를 가볍게 입에 올리는 것이 목격되었고, 이전에는 영웅적인 앙굴렘 공작을 모욕했으며 오를레앙 공작 전하께서 백합의 존엄한 왕좌에 맞서 음모를 꾸미고 있다고 감히 넌지시 비추었다."], ["His gold is prodigated in every direction which his stupid menaces fail to frighten.", "그의 황금은 그 멍청한 위협이 겁주는 데 실패한 모든 방면에 헤프게 뿌려진다."], ["By one and the other, he has won over creatures of the Court here--and, in fine, Pumpernickel will not be quiet, Germany tranquil, France respected, or Europe content until this poisonous viper be crushed under heel\": and so on.", "이런저런 수단으로 그는 이곳 궁정의 앞잡이들을 매수해 왔으니 — 요컨대 이 독사를 발뒤꿈치로 짓밟아 버리기 전에는 펌퍼니클도 잠잠해지지 않고, 독일도 평온해지지 않으며, 프랑스도 존중받지 못하고, 유럽도 만족하지 못할 것이다.\""], ["When one side or the other had written any particularly spicy dispatch, news of it was sure to slip out.", "등등. 어느 한쪽이 유달리 자극적인 공문을 쓰면, 그 소식은 으레 새어 나가게 마련이었다."]], [["Before the winter was far advanced, it is actually on record that Emmy took a night and received company with great propriety and modesty.", "겨울이 미처 깊어지기도 전에, 에미가 자기 야회 날을 정해 더없이 단정하고 겸손하게 손님을 맞았다는 것이 실제로 기록에 남아 있다. 그녀에게는 프랑스어 선생이 있었는데, 그는 그녀의 정확한 발음과 빠른 습득력을 칭찬했다."], ["She had a French master, who complimented her upon the purity of her accent and her facility of learning; the fact is she had learned long ago and grounded herself subsequently in the grammar so as to be able to teach it to George; and Madam Strumpff came to give her lessons in singing, which she performed so well and with such a true voice that the Major's windows, who had lodgings opposite under the Prime Minister, were always open to hear the lesson.", "사실 그녀는 오래전에 이미 배웠고, 그 후 조지를 가르칠 수 있도록 문법을 스스로 다져 두었던 것이다. 그리고 슈트룸프 부인이 와서 노래 수업을 해 주었는데, 그녀가 어찌나 노래를 잘하고 또 그 목소리가 어찌나 진실했던지, 총리 댁 아래층 맞은편에 거처를 둔 소령의 창문은 그 수업을 들으려 늘 열려 있었다."], ["Some of the German ladies, who are very sentimental and simple in their tastes, fell in love with her and began to call her du at once.", "취향이 무척 감상적이고 소박한 독일 부인들 몇몇은 그녀에게 반해서 대뜸 그녀를 너라고 친근하게 부르기 시작했다."], ["These are trivial details, but they relate to happy times.", "이런 것들은 사소한 자질구레한 이야기지만, 행복한 시절에 얽힌 것들이다."], ["The Major made himself George's tutor and read Caesar and mathematics with him, and they had a German master and rode out of evenings by the side of Emmy's carriage--she was always too timid, and made a dreadful outcry at the slightest disturbance on horse-back.", "소령은 스스로 조지의 가정교사가 되어 그와 함께 카이사르와 수학을 읽었고, 그들에게는 독일어 선생도 있었으며, 저녁이면 에미의 마차 곁에서 말을 타고 나갔다 — 그녀는 늘 너무 겁이 많아서 말 위에서 조금만 무슨 일이 생겨도 끔찍하게 비명을 질러 댔던 것이다."], ["So she drove about with one of her dear German friends, and Jos asleep on the back-seat of the barouche.", "그래서 그녀는 정다운 독일 친구 하나를 데리고 마차로 돌아다녔고, 조스는 사륜마차 뒷자리에서 잠들어 있었다."]], [["He was becoming very sweet upon the Grafinn Fanny de Butterbrod, a very gentle tender-hearted and unassuming young creature, a Canoness and Countess in her own right, but with scarcely ten pounds per year to her fortune, and Fanny for her part declared that to be Amelia's sister was the greatest delight that Heaven could bestow on her, and Jos might have put a Countess's shield and coronet by the side of his own arms on his carriage and forks; when--when events occurred, and those grand fetes given upon the marriage of the Hereditary Prince of Pumpernickel with the lovely Princess Amelia of Humbourg-Schlippenschloppen took place.", "그는 그라핀 파니 데 부터브로트에게 무척 다정해지고 있었으니, 더없이 온순하고 마음씨 곱고 가식 없는 젊은 처자로, 어엿한 본인 자격의 수녀회원이자 백작 영애였지만 재산이라곤 일 년에 십 파운드도 채 안 되는 사람이었다. 그리고 파니는 파니대로 애밀리아의 자매가 되는 것이야말로 하늘이 자기에게 내려 줄 수 있는 가장 큰 기쁨이라고 단언했다. 조스로서는 자기 마차와 포크에 새겨진 자기 문장 옆에 백작 영애의 방패꼴 문장과 관 문장을 나란히 새겨 넣어도 좋았을 것이다 — 그런데 — 그런데 이런저런 사건이 일어났고, 펌퍼니클의 세습 공자와 사랑스러운 훔부르크슐리펜슐로펜의 애밀리아 공주의 결혼을 기념하는 그 성대한 축제가 거행되었던 것이다."]], [["At this festival the magnificence displayed was such as had not been known in the little German place since the days of the prodigal Victor XIV.", "이 축제에서 펼쳐진 화려함이란 저 낭비벽 심하던 빅토르 14세의 시절 이래로 이 작은 독일 고장에서 일찍이 본 적이 없는 것이었다."], ["All the neighbouring Princes, Princesses, and Grandees were invited to the feast.", "인근의 모든 공자와 공녀와 귀족이 이 잔치에 초대되었다."], ["Beds rose to half a crown per night in Pumpernickel, and the Army was exhausted in providing guards of honour for the Highnesses, Serenities, and Excellencies who arrived from all quarters.", "펌퍼니클에서는 잠자리 값이 하룻밤에 반 크라운까지 치솟았고, 사방에서 도착하는 전하며 각하며 나리들을 위해 의장대를 마련하느라 군대가 동났다."], ["The Princess was married by proxy, at her father's residence, by the Count de Schlusselback.", "공주는 부친의 거처에서 슐뤼셀바흐 백작을 통해 대리 혼례를 올렸다."], ["Snuff-boxes were given away in profusion (as we learned from the Court jeweller, who sold and afterwards bought them again), and bushels of the Order of Saint Michael of Pumpernickel were sent to the nobles of the Court, while hampers of the cordons and decorations of the Wheel of St. Catherine of Schlippenschloppen were brought to ours.", "코담뱃갑이 아낌없이 하사되었고(이 사실은 그것을 팔았다가 나중에 도로 사들인 궁정 보석상에게서 우리가 알게 되었다), 펌퍼니클의 성 미카엘 훈장이 궁정 귀족들에게 수두룩이 보내졌으며, 우리 쪽으로는 슐리펜슐로펜의 성 카타리나 수레바퀴 훈장의 약장과 장식이 광주리째 들어왔다."], ["The French envoy got both.", "프랑스 공사는 둘 다 받았다."], ["\"He is covered with ribbons like a prize cart-horse,\"", "\"저자는 우승한 짐말처럼 리본으로 뒤덮였군.\""], ["Tapeworm said, who was not allowed by the rules of his service to take any decorations: \"Let him have the cordons; but with whom is the victory?\"", "테이프웜이 말했으니, 그는 직무 규정상 어떤 훈장도 받는 것이 허용되지 않는 사람이었다. \"약장이야 받으라지."], ["The fact is, it was a triumph of British diplomacy, the French party having proposed and tried their utmost to carry a marriage with a Princess of the House of Potztausend-Donnerwetter, whom, as a matter of course, we opposed.", "하지만 승리는 누구의 것인가?\" 사실인즉 이것은 영국 외교의 승리였으니, 프랑스 파가 포츠타우젠트도너베터 가문의 공녀와의 혼인을 제안하고 그것을 성사시키려 안간힘을 썼으나, 당연히 우리가 그것을 좌절시켰던 것이다."]], [["Everybody was asked to the fetes of the marriage.", "누구나 이 결혼 축제에 초대받았다."], ["Garlands and triumphal arches were hung across the road to welcome the young bride.", "어린 신부를 맞이하기 위해 길을 가로질러 화환과 개선문이 내걸렸다."], ["The great Saint Michael's Fountain ran with uncommonly sour wine, while that in the Artillery Place frothed with beer.", "거대한 성 미카엘 분수에서는 유달리 신 포도주가 흘렀고, 포병 광장의 분수에서는 맥주가 거품을 일으켰다."], ["The great waters played; and poles were put up in the park and gardens for the happy peasantry, which they might climb at their leisure, carrying off watches, silver forks, prize sausages hung with pink ribbon, &c., at the top.", "거대한 분수들이 물을 뿜었다. 또 행복한 농민들을 위해 공원과 정원에 장대가 세워졌으니, 그들이 마음껏 그것을 기어올라 꼭대기에 매단 시계며 은포크며 분홍 리본을 단 상품 소시지 따위를 떼어 갈 수 있게 한 것이었다."], ["Georgy got one, wrenching it off, having swarmed up the pole to the delight of the spectators, and sliding down with the rapidity of a fall of water.", "조지도 하나를 얻었는데, 구경꾼들이 즐거워하는 가운데 장대를 기어올라 그것을 비틀어 떼어 낸 다음, 폭포수처럼 빠르게 미끄러져 내려왔다."], ["But it was for the glory's sake merely.", "그러나 그것은 그저 영광을 위해서였다."], ["The boy gave the sausage to a peasant, who had very nearly seized it, and stood at the foot of the mast, blubbering, because he was unsuccessful.", "소년은 그 소시지를, 자기가 거의 다 잡을 뻔하다가 놓쳐 장대 밑에서 엉엉 울고 있던 한 농부에게 주어 버렸다."]], [["At the French Chancellerie they had six more lampions in their illumination than ours had; but our transparency, which represented the young Couple advancing and Discord flying away, with the most ludicrous likeness to the French Ambassador, beat the French picture hollow; and I have no doubt got Tapeworm the advancement and the Cross of the Bath which he subsequently attained.", "프랑스 공사관에서는 우리보다 등불을 여섯 개 더 달아 야간 조명을 밝혔다. 그러나 우리 쪽의 투명화는 젊은 부부가 앞으로 나아오고 '불화'가 달아나는 모습을 그린 것이었는데, 그 '불화'가 프랑스 대사를 더없이 우스꽝스럽게 빼닮은 까닭에 프랑스 쪽 그림을 완전히 압도했다. 그리고 나는 그것이 테이프웜에게, 그가 나중에 얻은 승진과 배스 훈장을 안겨 주었으리라 믿어 의심치 않는다."]], [["Crowds of foreigners arrived for the fetes, and of English, of course.", "축제를 보러 외국인 무리가 몰려들었고, 물론 영국인도 몰려들었다."], ["Besides the Court balls, public balls were given at the Town Hall and the Redoute, and in the former place there was a room for trente-et-quarante and roulette established, for the week of the festivities only, and by one of the great German companies from Ems or Aix-la-Chapelle.", "궁정 무도회 말고도 시청과 레두트에서 공개 무도회가 열렸는데, 시청 쪽에는 트랑테카랑트와 룰렛을 할 수 있는 방이 마련되었으니, 오로지 이 축제 주간 동안만, 엠스나 엑스라샤펠에서 온 독일의 큰 회사 하나가 운영하는 것이었다."], ["The officers or inhabitants of the town were not allowed to play at these games, but strangers, peasants, ladies were admitted, and any one who chose to lose or win money.", "이 고장의 장교나 주민은 이런 도박을 하는 것이 허락되지 않았지만, 외지인이며 농민이며 부인들은 입장할 수 있었고, 돈을 잃든 따든 마음 내키는 사람은 누구나 끼어들 수 있었다."]], [["That little scapegrace Georgy Osborne amongst others, whose pockets were always full of dollars and whose relations were away at the grand festival of the Court, came to the Stadthaus Ball in company of his uncle's courier, Mr.", "그 가운데 저 자그마한 망나니 조지 오스본도 있었으니, 주머니에는 늘 달러가 가득했고 그 친척들은 궁정의 성대한 축제에 가 있던 차였다. 그는 외삼촌의 급사인 키르슈 씨를 따라 시청 무도회에 왔다."], ["Kirsch, and having only peeped into a play-room at Baden-Baden when he hung on Dobbin's arm, and where, of course, he was not permitted to gamble, came eagerly to this part of the entertainment and hankered round the tables where the croupiers and the punters were at work.", "그는 도빈의 팔에 매달려 바덴바덴의 도박장을 빠끔히 들여다본 적이 한 번 있었을 뿐이고, 거기서야 물론 노름이 허락되지 않았던 터라, 이 연회의 이 대목으로 신이 나서 달려와 노름 진행자들과 노름꾼들이 손을 놀리는 탁자 주위를 어슬렁거리며 군침을 흘렸다. 여자들도 노름을 하고 있었다."], ["Women were playing; they were masked, some of them; this license was allowed in these wild times of carnival.", "그중 몇몇은 가면을 쓰고 있었으니, 이 광란의 사육제 시절에는 그런 방종이 허용되었던 것이다."]], [["A woman with light hair, in a low dress by no means so fresh as it had been, and with a black mask on, through the eyelets of which her eyes twinkled strangely, was seated at one of the roulette-tables with a card and a pin and a couple of florins before her.", "밝은색 머리에, 이전만큼은 결코 산뜻하지 않은 가슴이 파인 드레스를 입고, 검은 가면을 쓴 한 여자가 — 그 가면의 눈구멍 사이로 그녀의 두 눈이 야릇하게 반짝였다 — 룰렛 탁자 하나에 앉아, 앞에 카드 한 장과 핀 하나와 플로린 두 닢을 놓아두고 있었다."], ["As the croupier called out the colour and number, she pricked on the card with great care and regularity, and only ventured her money on the colours after the red or black had come up a certain number of times.", "노름 진행자가 색깔과 숫자를 외칠 때마다 그녀는 그것을 무척 조심스럽고 규칙적으로 카드에 핀으로 콕콕 찍었고, 빨강이나 검정이 일정한 횟수만큼 나온 뒤에야 비로소 색깔에 돈을 걸어 보는 것이었다."], ["It was strange to look at her.", "그녀를 바라보는 것은 야릇한 노릇이었다."]], [["But in spite of her care and assiduity she guessed wrong and the last two florins followed each other under the croupier's rake, as he cried out with his inexorable voice the winning colour and number.", "그러나 그 조심성과 끈기에도 불구하고 그녀는 잘못 짚었으니, 마지막 두 플로린마저 노름 진행자의 갈퀴 밑으로 잇따라 끌려 들어갔고, 그는 그 가차 없는 목소리로 딴 색깔과 숫자를 외쳤다."], ["She gave a sigh, a shrug with her shoulders, which were already too much out of her gown, and dashing the pin through the card on to the table, sat thrumming it for a while.", "그녀는 한숨을 내쉬며, 이미 드레스 밖으로 너무 많이 드러난 어깨를 한 번 으쓱하더니, 카드 위에 핀을 푹 찔러 탁자에 꽂고는 한동안 그것을 손가락으로 퉁기며 앉아 있었다."], ["Then she looked round her and saw Georgy's honest face staring at the scene.", "그러다 주위를 둘러보다가, 조지가 그 정직한 얼굴로 이 광경을 빤히 쳐다보고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["The little scamp!", "그 자그마한 개구쟁이!"], ["What business had he to be there?", "녀석이 거기서 무얼 하고 있단 말인가?"]], [["When she saw the boy, at whose face she looked hard through her shining eyes and mask, she said, \"Monsieur n'est pas joueur?\"", "그 소년을 보자 그녀는, 반짝이는 두 눈과 가면 너머로 그 얼굴을 뚫어지게 바라보며 말했다. \"므슈는 노름꾼이 아니신가요?\""]], [["\"Non, Madame,\"", "\"네, 마담.\""], ["said the boy; but she must have known, from his accent, of what country he was, for she answered him with a slight foreign tone.", "소년이 말했다. 그러나 그녀는 그의 억양에서 그가 어느 나라 사람인지 분명 알아챘을 것이니, 약간 외국 말씨가 섞인 어조로 그에게 답했던 것이다."], ["\"You have nevare played--will you do me a littl' favor?\"", "\"한 번도 노름을 해 보신 적이 없으시군요 — 제게 자그마한 부탁 하나 들어주시겠어요?\""]], [["\"What is it?\"", "\"뭔데요?\""], ["said Georgy, blushing again. Mr.", "조지가 다시 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["Kirsch was at work for his part at the rouge et noir and did not see his young master.", "키르슈 씨는 자기대로 루주에누아르에 열중하느라 어린 주인을 보지 못했다."]], [["\"Play this for me, if you please; put it on any number, any number.\"", "\"부디 이걸 저 대신 걸어 주세요. 아무 숫자에나, 아무 숫자에나요.\""], ["And she took from her bosom a purse, and out of it a gold piece, the only coin there, and she put it into George's hand.", "그러고는 가슴께에서 지갑을 꺼내, 그 안에서 거기 든 유일한 동전인 금화 한 닢을 꺼내어 조지의 손에 쥐여 주었다."], ["The boy laughed and did as he was bid.", "소년은 웃으며 시키는 대로 했다."]], [["The number came up sure enough.", "그 숫자가 정말로 나왔다."], ["There is a power that arranges that, they say, for beginners.", "사람들 말로는 초심자를 위해 그런 일을 마련해 주는 어떤 힘이 있다고 한다."]], [["\"Thank you,\"", "\"고마워요.\""], ["said she, pulling the money towards her, \"thank you.", "그녀가 그 돈을 자기 쪽으로 끌어당기며 말했다. \"고마워요."], ["What is your name?\"", "이름이 뭐죠?\""]], [["\"My name's Osborne,\"", "\"제 이름은 오스본이에요.\""], ["said Georgy, and was fingering in his own pockets for dollars, and just about to make a trial, when the Major, in his uniform, and Jos, en Marquis, from the Court ball, made their appearance.", "조지가 말하며 자기 주머니에서 달러를 만지작거리다가 막 한 번 시험 삼아 걸어 보려던 참이었는데, 그때 예복 차림의 소령과, 후작 차림으로 궁정 무도회에서 온 조스가 모습을 드러냈다."], ["Other people, finding the entertainment stupid and preferring the fun at the Stadthaus, had quitted the Palace ball earlier; but it is probable the Major and Jos had gone home and found the boy's absence, for the former instantly went up to him and, taking him by the shoulder, pulled him briskly back from the place of temptation.", "다른 사람들도 그 연회가 따분하다고 여기고 시청 쪽의 흥겨움을 더 좋아한 나머지 궁정 무도회를 일찌감치 떠난 터였다. 그러나 짐작건대 소령과 조스는 집에 돌아갔다가 소년이 없는 것을 알아챈 모양이었으니, 소령이 즉각 그에게 다가가 어깨를 붙잡고는 유혹의 장소에서 그를 휙 끌어냈던 것이다."], ["Then, looking round the room, he saw Kirsch employed as we have said, and going up to him, asked how he dared to bring Mr. George to such a place.", "그러고는 방 안을 둘러보다가, 앞서 말한 대로 키르슈가 노름에 빠져 있는 것을 보고 그에게 다가가, 어찌 감히 조지 도련님을 이런 곳에 데려왔느냐고 따져 물었다."]], [["\"Laissez-moi tranquille,\" said Mr.", "\"날 좀 가만 내버려 두쇼.\""], ["Kirsch, very much excited by play and wine.", "키르슈 씨가 노름과 술에 잔뜩 취해 말했다."], ["\"Il faut s'amuser, parbleu.", "\"즐겨야 할 거 아니오, 젠장."], ["Je ne suis pas au service de Monsieur.\"", "난 나리 부하가 아니란 말이오.\""]], [["Seeing his condition the Major did not choose to argue with the man, but contented himself with drawing away George and asking Jos if he would come away.", "그의 상태를 본 소령은 그 사내와 다툴 마음이 내키지 않아, 그저 조지를 데려가는 것으로 만족하고는 조스에게 같이 가겠느냐고 물었다."], ["He was standing close by the lady in the mask, who was playing with pretty good luck now, and looking on much interested at the game.", "조스는 가면 쓴 여자 바로 곁에 서 있었는데, 그 여자는 이제 제법 운이 좋게 노름을 하고 있었고, 조스는 그 게임을 무척 흥미롭게 지켜보고 있었다."]], [["\"Hadn't you better come, Jos,\"", "\"같이 가는 게 낫지 않겠소, 조스.\""], ["the Major said, \"with George and me?\"", "소령이 말했다. \"조지하고 나하고 말이오.\""]], [["\"I'll stop and go home with that rascal, Kirsch,\"", "\"난 남았다가 저 망나니 키르슈하고 같이 들어가겠소.\""], ["Jos said; and for the same reason of modesty, which he thought ought to be preserved before the boy, Dobbin did not care to remonstrate with Jos, but left him and walked home with Georgy.", "조스가 말했다. 그리고 소년 앞에서는 마땅히 지켜야 한다고 여긴 그 똑같은 체면 때문에, 도빈은 조스에게 굳이 잔소리할 마음이 내키지 않아, 그를 두고 조지와 함께 걸어서 집으로 갔다."]], [["\"Did you play?\"", "\"너 노름했니?\""], ["asked the Major when they were out and on their way home.", "두 사람이 밖으로 나와 집으로 가는 길에 소령이 물었다."]], [["The boy said \"No.\"", "소년은 \"아니요\"라고 말했다."]], [["\"Give me your word of honour as a gentleman that you never will.\"", "\"신사로서 명예를 걸고 절대 하지 않겠다고 약속하렴.\""]], [["\"Why?\"", "\"왜요?\""], ["said the boy; \"it seems very good fun.\"", "소년이 말했다. \"무척 재미있어 보이던데요.\""], ["And, in a very eloquent and impressive manner, the Major showed him why he shouldn't, and would have enforced his precepts by the example of Georgy's own father, had he liked to say anything that should reflect on the other's memory.", "그러자 소령은 무척이나 설득력 있고 인상적인 태도로 그가 왜 노름을 해서는 안 되는지 일러 주었으니, 만약 조지의 친아버지에 대한 기억을 깎아내릴 만한 말을 하고 싶었더라면 바로 그 아비를 본보기로 들어 자신의 훈계를 못 박았을 것이다."], ["When he had housed him, he went to bed and saw his light, in the little room outside of Amelia's, presently disappear.", "그는 소년을 집에 들여보낸 뒤 잠자리에 들었고, 애밀리아 방 바깥의 그 작은 방에서 새어 나오던 소년의 불빛이 이윽고 사라지는 것을 보았다."], ["Amelia's followed half an hour afterwards.", "애밀리아의 불빛은 그로부터 반 시간 뒤에 따라 꺼졌다."], ["I don't know what made the Major note it so accurately.", "무엇이 소령으로 하여금 그것을 그토록 정확히 눈여겨보게 했는지 나는 알지 못한다."]], [["Jos, however, remained behind over the play-table; he was no gambler, but not averse to the little excitement of the sport now and then, and he had some Napoleons chinking in the embroidered pockets of his court waistcoat.", "그러나 조스는 노름 탁자에 남았다. 그는 노름꾼은 아니었으나 이따금 노름의 자그마한 흥분을 마다하지는 않는 편이었고, 궁정 조끼의 수놓인 주머니에는 나폴레옹 금화 몇 닢이 짤랑이고 있었다."], ["He put down one over the fair shoulder of the little gambler before him, and they won.", "그는 앞에 앉은 그 자그마한 노름꾼 여자의 어여쁜 어깨너머로 한 닢을 걸었고, 그들은 땄다."], ["She made a little movement to make room for him by her side, and just took the skirt of her gown from a vacant chair there.", "그녀는 자기 곁에 그가 앉을 자리를 마련해 주려고 살짝 몸을 움직이며, 거기 빈 의자에서 자기 드레스 자락을 막 거두어들였다."]], [["\"Come and give me good luck,\"", "\"이리 오셔서 제게 행운을 가져다주세요.\""], ["she said, still in a foreign accent, quite different from that frank and perfectly English \"Thank you,\" with which she had saluted Georgy's coup in her favour.", "그녀가 여전히 외국 말씨로 말했으니, 조지의 횡재를 두고 인사하며 건넸던 그 솔직하고 완벽하게 영국적인 \"고마워요\"와는 사뭇 다른 어조였다."], ["The portly gentleman, looking round to see that nobody of rank observed him, sat down; he muttered--\"Ah, really, well now, God bless my soul.", "그 풍채 좋은 신사는 자기를 알아볼 만한 신분의 사람이 있는지 둘러보고는 자리에 앉았다. 그는 중얼거렸다 — \"아, 이거 참, 그러니까, 맙소사."], ["I'm very fortunate; I'm sure to give you good fortune,\"", "제가 운이 무척 좋군요. 분명 제가 부인께 행운을 가져다드릴 겁니다.\""], ["and other words of compliment and confusion.", "그러고는 또 다른 찬사와 횡설수설을 늘어놓았다."], ["\"Do you play much?\"", "\"노름을 많이 하시나요?\""], ["the foreign mask said.", "그 가면 쓴 외국 여자가 말했다."]], [["\"I put a Nap or two down,\"", "\"한두 닢 걸어 봅니다.\""], ["said Jos with a superb air, flinging down a gold piece.", "조스가 거만한 태도로 말하며 금화 한 닢을 던졌다."]], [["\"Yes; ay nap after dinner,\"", "\"그렇군요. 식후의 한두 닢이로군요.\""], ["said the mask archly.", "가면이 짓궂게 말했다."], ["But Jos looking frightened, she continued, in her pretty French accent, \"You do not play to win.", "그러나 조스가 겁먹은 기색을 보이자 그녀는 그 어여쁜 프랑스 말씨로 말을 이었다. \"당신은 따려고 노름하시는 게 아니군요."], ["No more do I.", "저도 그래요."], ["I play to forget, but I cannot.", "저는 잊으려고 노름하지만, 잊을 수가 없어요."], ["I cannot forget old times, monsieur.", "저는 옛 시절을 잊을 수가 없답니다, 므슈."], ["Your little nephew is the image of his father; and you--you are not changed--but yes, you are.", "당신의 그 어린 조카는 그 아버지를 빼닮았더군요. 그리고 당신은 — 당신은 변하지 않으셨어요 — 아니, 변하셨네요."], ["Everybody changes, everybody forgets; nobody has any heart.\"", "누구나 변하고, 누구나 잊지요. 누구에게도 마음이라곤 없어요.\""]], [["\"Good God, who is it?\"", "\"맙소사, 대체 누구시오?\""], ["asked Jos in a flutter.", "조스가 가슴을 떨며 물었다."]], [["\"Can't you guess, Joseph Sedley?\"", "\"못 알아보시겠어요, 조지프 세들리?\""], ["said the little woman in a sad voice, and undoing her mask, she looked at him.", "그 자그마한 여자가 서글픈 목소리로 말하며 가면을 벗고 그를 바라보았다."], ["\"You have forgotten me.\"", "\"저를 잊으셨군요.\""]], [["\"Good heavens!", "\"세상에!"], ["Mrs. Crawley!\"", "크롤리 부인!\""], ["gasped out Jos.", "조스가 숨을 헐떡이며 내뱉었다."]], [["\"Rebecca,\"", "\"리베카예요.\""], ["said the other, putting her hand on his; but she followed the game still, all the time she was looking at him.", "그 여자가 그의 손에 자기 손을 얹으며 말했다. 그러나 그녀는 그를 바라보는 내내 여전히 노름을 좇고 있었다."]], [["\"I am stopping at the Elephant,\"", "\"저는 엘레팡 여관에 묵고 있어요.\""], ["she continued.", "그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["\"Ask for Madame de Raudon.", "\"로던 부인을 찾으시면 돼요."], ["I saw my dear Amelia to-day; how pretty she looked, and how happy!", "오늘 제 사랑하는 애밀리아를 봤어요. 어찌나 어여쁘고, 어찌나 행복해 보이던지!"], ["So do you!", "당신도 그렇고요!"], ["Everybody but me, who am wretched, Joseph Sedley.\"", "비참한 저만 빼고 다들 그렇네요, 조지프 세들리.\""], ["And she put her money over from the red to the black, as if by a chance movement of her hand, and while she was wiping her eyes with a pocket-handkerchief fringed with torn lace.", "그러고는, 마치 손이 우연히 움직이기라도 한 듯 빨강에서 검정으로 돈을 옮겨 놓더니, 찢어진 레이스가 가장자리에 달린 손수건으로 눈가를 훔쳤다."]], [["The red came up again, and she lost the whole of that stake.", "빨강이 다시 나왔고, 그녀는 그 판돈 전부를 잃었다."], ["\"Come away,\"", "\"이리 나와요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"Come with me a little--we are old friends, are we not, dear Mr. Sedley?\"", "\"잠깐 저와 함께 가요 — 우리는 오랜 친구잖아요, 그렇죠, 다정한 세들리 씨?\""]], [["And Mr. Kirsch having lost all his money by this time, followed his master out into the moonlight, where the illuminations were winking out and the transparency over our mission was scarcely visible.", "그리고 키르슈 씨는 이때쯤 자기 돈을 몽땅 잃은 터라, 주인을 따라 달빛 속으로 나섰으니, 그곳에서는 조명들이 깜박이며 꺼져 가고 우리 공사관 위의 투명화도 거의 보이지 않았다."]], [["A Vagabond Chapter\nWe must pass over a part of Mrs.", "우리는 레베카 크롤리 부인의 전기 가운데 한 부분을, 세상이 요구하는 그 가벼움과 섬세함으로 건너뛰어야겠다."], ["Rebecca Crawley's biography with that lightness and delicacy which the world demands--the moral world, that has, perhaps, no particular objection to vice, but an insuperable repugnance to hearing vice called by its proper name.", "여기서 세상이란 도덕의 세계를 말하는데, 그 세계는 악덕 자체에는 별다른 이의가 없을지 몰라도, 악덕을 제 이름 그대로 부르는 것을 듣는 일에는 이겨낼 수 없는 거부감을 품는다. 허영의 시장에는 우리가 행하고 또 빤히 알고 있으면서도 결코 입에 올리지 않는 일들이 있는 법이다."], ["There are things we do and know perfectly well in Vanity Fair, though we never speak of them: as the Ahrimanians worship the devil, but don't mention him: and a polite public will no more bear to read an authentic description of vice than a truly refined English or American female will permit the word breeches to be pronounced in her chaste hearing.", "마치 아리만교도가 악마를 숭배하되 그 이름을 입에 담지 않듯이 말이다. 점잖은 대중이란 악덕에 대한 진솔한 묘사를 차마 읽지 못하는 것이, 진정으로 세련된 영국이나 미국 여성이 그 정숙한 귀에 \"바지\"라는 낱말이 발음되는 것을 허락하지 못하는 것과 같다."], ["And yet, madam, both are walking the world before our faces every day, without much shocking us.", "그런데 부인, 그 둘은 매일같이 우리 눈앞에서 세상을 활보하면서도 우리를 그다지 충격에 빠뜨리지 않는다."], ["If you were to blush every time they went by, what complexions you would have!", "만약 그것들이 지나갈 때마다 부인께서 얼굴을 붉히신다면, 그 안색이 대체 어찌 되겠는가!"], ["It is only when their naughty names are called out that your modesty has any occasion to show alarm or sense of outrage, and it has been the wish of the present writer, all through this story, deferentially to submit to the fashion at present prevailing, and only to hint at the existence of wickedness in a light, easy, and agreeable manner, so that nobody's fine feelings may be offended.", "오로지 그 못된 이름들이 소리 내어 불릴 때라야 비로소 부인의 정숙함이 놀라움이나 모욕감을 드러낼 까닭이 생기는 것이다. 그리하여 본 작가는 이 이야기를 쓰는 내내, 지금 널리 통용되는 그 유행에 공손히 따르고자, 그저 가볍고 편안하고 듣기 좋은 방식으로 사악함의 존재를 넌지시 암시하기만 하여, 그 누구의 고상한 감정도 거스르지 않으려 애써 왔다."], ["I defy any one to say that our Becky, who has certainly some vices, has not been presented to the public in a perfectly genteel and inoffensive manner.", "분명 얼마간 악덕을 지닌 우리의 베키가 더없이 품위 있고 거슬리지 않는 방식으로 대중에게 소개되지 않았다고 감히 누가 말할 수 있겠는가."], ["In describing this Siren, singing and smiling, coaxing and cajoling, the author, with modest pride, asks his readers all round, has he once forgotten the laws of politeness, and showed the monster's hideous tail above water?", "노래하고 미소 짓고, 어르고 구슬리는 이 세이렌을 묘사하면서, 작가는 겸허한 자부심으로 사방의 독자들에게 묻노니, 단 한 번이라도 예의의 법도를 잊고 그 괴물의 흉측한 꼬리를 수면 위로 드러낸 적이 있었던가?"], ["No!", "천만에!"], ["Those who like may peep down under waves that are pretty transparent and see it writhing and twirling, diabolically hideous and slimy, flapping amongst bones, or curling round corpses; but above the waterline, I ask, has not everything been proper, agreeable, and decorous, and has any the most squeamish immoralist in Vanity Fair a right to cry fie?", "보고 싶은 이들은 제법 투명한 물결 아래를 들여다보고서, 그것이 악마처럼 흉측하고 끈적거리는 모습으로 몸부림치며 뒤틀리는 것을, 뼈들 사이에서 펄떡이거나 시체를 휘감는 것을 보아도 좋다. 그러나 수면 위로는, 묻노니, 모든 것이 점잖고 듣기 좋고 단정하지 않았던가, 그리고 허영의 시장에서 제아무리 까다로운 비도덕가라 한들 그것을 두고 망측하다 외칠 권리가 있겠는가?"], ["When, however, the Siren disappears and dives below, down among the dead men, the water of course grows turbid over her, and it is labour lost to look into it ever so curiously.", "그러나 세이렌이 사라져 물밑으로, 죽은 사내들 사이로 잠겨들면, 물론 그 위로 물이 탁해지는 법이니, 아무리 호기심에 차서 그 속을 들여다보아도 헛수고일 따름이다."], ["They look pretty enough when they sit upon a rock, twanging their harps and combing their hair, and sing, and beckon to you to come and hold the looking-glass; but when they sink into their native element, depend on it, those mermaids are about no good, and we had best not examine the fiendish marine cannibals, revelling and feasting on their wretched pickled victims.", "그것들은 바위 위에 앉아 하프를 뜯고 머리를 빗으며 노래하고, 어서 와서 거울을 들어 달라 손짓할 때는 제법 예뻐 보인다. 그러나 그것들이 제 본래의 터전으로 가라앉을 때면, 장담하건대 저 인어들은 결코 좋은 짓을 꾸미고 있는 게 아니니, 우리는 그 악마 같은 바다의 식인귀들이 가엾은 절임 신세가 된 제물들을 두고 흥청대며 잔치를 벌이는 꼴을 살펴보지 않는 편이 상책이다."], ["And so, when Becky is out of the way, be sure that she is not particularly well employed, and that the less that is said about her doings is in fact the better.", "그러니 베키가 눈앞에서 사라졌을 때면, 그녀가 딱히 떳떳한 일에 종사하고 있지 않다는 것, 그리고 그녀의 행적에 관해서는 적게 이야기할수록 오히려 나으리라는 것을 명심하기 바란다."]], [["If we were to give a full account of her proceedings during a couple of years that followed after the Curzon Street catastrophe, there might be some reason for people to say this book was improper.", "만약 우리가 커즌 거리의 그 파국에 뒤이은 이태 동안의 그녀의 행적을 낱낱이 늘어놓는다면, 사람들이 이 책더러 점잖지 못하다고 말할 만한 까닭이 생길지도 모른다."], ["The actions of very vain, heartless, pleasure-seeking people are very often improper (as are many of yours, my friend with the grave face and spotless reputation--but that is merely by the way); and what are those of a woman without faith--or love--or character?", "몹시 허영에 차고, 무정하고, 쾌락만 좇는 사람들의 행동이란 대개 점잖지 못한 법이며(근엄한 얼굴에 흠 없는 평판을 지닌 나의 벗이여, 그대의 행동도 상당수가 그러하다네 — 그러나 이건 그저 지나가는 말일 뿐이다), 하물며 신앙도 사랑도 인품도 없는 여인의 행동이야 어떠하겠는가?"], ["And I am inclined to think that there was a period in Mrs. Becky's life when she was seized, not by remorse, but by a kind of despair, and absolutely neglected her person and did not even care for her reputation.", "그리고 내 생각에는, 베키 부인의 생애에 어느 한 시기가 있었으니, 그때 그녀는 회한이 아니라 일종의 절망에 사로잡혀, 제 몸가짐을 완전히 내팽개치고 제 평판조차 아랑곳하지 않았던 것이다."]], [["This abattement and degradation did not take place all at once; it was brought about by degrees, after her calamity, and after many struggles to keep up--as a man who goes overboard hangs on to a spar whilst any hope is left, and then flings it away and goes down, when he finds that struggling is in vain.", "이러한 의기소침과 타락은 단번에 닥쳐온 것이 아니었다. 그것은 그녀의 재앙 이후, 또 버텨 보려는 숱한 몸부림 끝에, 차츰차츰 이루어진 것이었다. 마치 배 밖으로 떨어진 사람이 조금이라도 희망이 남아 있는 동안에는 부유물에 매달려 있다가, 발버둥쳐 봐야 헛일임을 깨닫고는 그것을 내던지고 가라앉아 버리는 것처럼 말이다."]], [["She lingered about London whilst her husband was making preparations for his departure to his seat of government, and it is believed made more than one attempt to see her brother-in-law, Sir Pitt Crawley, and to work upon his feelings, which she had almost enlisted in her favour. As Sir Pitt and Mr.", "그녀는 남편이 부임지로 떠날 채비를 하는 동안 런던 언저리에서 머뭇거렸고, 시아주버니인 피트 크롤리 경을 만나 그의 감정에 호소해 보려는 시도를 한두 번 했던 것으로 여겨진다."], ["Wenham were walking down to the House of Commons, the latter spied Mrs.", "사실 그녀는 거의 그의 마음을 자기편으로 끌어들였던 터였다."], ["Rawdon in a black veil, and lurking near the palace of the legislature.", "피트 경과 웬햄 씨가 하원으로 걸어 내려가던 중에, 웬햄이 검은 베일을 쓴 로던 부인이 입법부 건물 가까이에서 어슬렁거리는 것을 발견했다."], ["She sneaked away when her eyes met those of Wenham, and indeed never succeeded in her designs upon the Baronet.", "그녀는 웬햄의 눈과 마주치자 슬그머니 자취를 감추었고, 결국 준남작에 대한 그 계획은 끝내 성공하지 못했다."]], [["Probably Lady Jane interposed.", "아마도 제인 부인이 끼어든 것이리라."], ["I have heard that she quite astonished her husband by the spirit which she exhibited in this quarrel, and her determination to disown Mrs.", "듣자 하니 그녀는 이 싸움에서 보여 준 기개로, 그리고 베키 부인과의 인연을 끊겠다는 결연한 의지로 남편을 적잖이 놀라게 했다고 한다."], ["Becky. Of her own movement, she invited Rawdon to come and stop in Gaunt Street until his departure for Coventry Island, knowing that with him for a guard Mrs.", "그녀는 자진하여 로던에게, 코번트리 섬으로 떠나기 전까지 곤트 거리에 와서 머물라고 청했는데, 그가 곁에서 지키고 있으면 베키 부인이 감히 문을 밀치고 들어오려 하지 않으리라는 것을 알았기 때문이다."], ["Becky would not try to force her door; and she looked curiously at the superscriptions of all the letters which arrived for Sir Pitt, lest he and his sister-in-law should be corresponding.", "또한 그녀는 피트 경 앞으로 도착하는 모든 편지의 겉봉을 유심히 살폈으니, 그와 시누이가 서로 편지를 주고받지나 않을까 염려해서였다."], ["Not but that Rebecca could have written had she a mind, but she did not try to see or to write to Pitt at his own house, and after one or two attempts consented to his demand that the correspondence regarding her conjugal differences should be carried on by lawyers only.", "레베카가 마음만 먹었다면 편지를 쓸 수도 있었겠지만, 그녀는 피트의 집으로 그를 찾아가거나 편지를 보내려 하지 않았고, 한두 번 시도한 끝에는 부부간의 불화에 관한 연락은 오로지 변호사들을 통해서만 주고받자는 그의 요구에 동의했다."]], [["The fact was that Pitt's mind had been poisoned against her.", "사실인즉 피트의 마음은 그녀에 대한 독으로 물들어 있었다."], ["A short time after Lord Steyne's accident Wenham had been with the Baronet and given him such a biography of Mrs.", "스타인 경의 사고가 있은 지 얼마 지나지 않아 웬햄이 준남작을 찾아와, 퀸스크롤리의 그 의원을 깜짝 놀라게 할 만한 베키 부인의 전기를 들려주었던 것이다."], ["Becky as had astonished the member for Queen's Crawley.", "그는 그녀에 관한 모든 것을 알고 있었다."], ["He knew everything regarding her: who her father was; in what year her mother danced at the opera; what had been her previous history; and what her conduct during her married life--as I have no doubt that the greater part of the story was false and dictated by interested malevolence, it shall not be repeated here.", "그녀의 아버지가 누구였는지, 그녀의 어머니가 몇 년도에 오페라 무대에서 춤을 추었는지, 그녀의 지난 내력은 어떠했는지, 그리고 결혼 생활 동안의 처신은 또 어떠했는지까지. 이 이야기의 태반이 거짓이며 사사로운 악의에서 비롯된 것임을 나는 의심치 않으니, 여기서 되풀이하지는 않겠다."], ["But Becky was left with a sad sad reputation in the esteem of a country gentleman and relative who had been once rather partial to her.", "그러나 베키는, 한때 그녀에게 제법 호의를 품었던 어느 시골 신사이자 친척의 마음속에 더없이 슬프고도 슬픈 평판만을 남기고 만 것이었다."]], [["The revenues of the Governor of Coventry Island are not large.", "코번트리 섬 총독의 수입은 많지 않았다."], ["A part of them were set aside by his Excellency for the payment of certain outstanding debts and liabilities, the charges incident on his high situation required considerable expense; finally, it was found that he could not spare to his wife more than three hundred pounds a year, which he proposed to pay to her on an undertaking that she would never trouble him.", "그 일부는 각하께서 미결제로 남은 몇몇 빚과 채무를 갚는 데 떼어 두었고, 그 높은 지위에 따르는 비용도 상당히 들었다. 결국 따져 보니 그가 아내에게 떼어 줄 수 있는 돈은 연 삼백 파운드를 넘지 못했고, 그는 그녀가 다시는 자기를 괴롭히지 않겠다는 약속을 조건으로 그것을 지급하겠다고 제안했다."], ["Otherwise, scandal, separation, Doctors' Commons would ensue.", "그러지 않으면 추문이요, 별거요, 종교 법정 행이 뒤따를 터였다."], ["But it was Mr. Wenham's business, Lord Steyne's business, Rawdon's, everybody's--to get her out of the country, and hush up a most disagreeable affair.", "그러나 이 더없이 불쾌한 사건을 무마하고 그녀를 나라 밖으로 내보내는 일은 웬햄 씨의 일이요, 스타인 경의 일이요, 로던의 일이요, 그러니까 모두의 일이었던 것이다."]], [["She was probably so much occupied in arranging these affairs of business with her husband's lawyers that she forgot to take any step whatever about her son, the little Rawdon, and did not even once propose to go and see him.", "그녀는 아마도 남편의 변호사들과 이러한 사무를 처리하는 데 너무 골몰한 나머지, 제 아들인 어린 로던에 관해서는 그 어떤 조치도 취하기를 잊었고, 한 번도 그를 보러 가겠다는 말조차 꺼내지 않았다."], ["That young gentleman was consigned to the entire guardianship of his aunt and uncle, the former of whom had always possessed a great share of the child's affection.", "그 어린 신사는 고모와 고모부의 온전한 후견에 맡겨졌는데, 그중 고모는 늘 그 아이의 애정을 가장 많이 차지해 온 터였다."], ["His mamma wrote him a neat letter from Boulogne, when she quitted England, in which she requested him to mind his book, and said she was going to take a Continental tour, during which she would have the pleasure of writing to him again.", "그의 어머니는 영국을 떠날 무렵 불로뉴에서 그에게 단정한 편지 한 통을 써 보내, 학업에 힘쓰라 당부하고는 자기는 대륙 여행을 떠날 참이며 그동안 다시 편지 쓰는 기쁨을 누리겠노라고 적었다."], ["But she never did for a year afterwards, and not, indeed, until Sir Pitt's only boy, always sickly, died of hooping-cough and measles--then Rawdon's mamma wrote the most affectionate composition to her darling son, who was made heir of Queen's Crawley by this accident, and drawn more closely than ever to the kind lady, whose tender heart had already adopted him.", "그러나 그녀는 그 후로 일 년 동안 한 번도 편지하지 않았고, 늘 병약하던 피트 경의 외아들이 백일해와 홍역으로 세상을 떠나고 나서야 비로소 — 그제야 로던의 어머니는 사랑하는 아들에게 더없이 다정한 글을 써 보냈다. 이 불행한 사고로 아들은 퀸스크롤리의 상속자가 되었고, 이미 그 다정한 마음으로 아이를 거두어들였던 그 인자한 부인에게 그 어느 때보다 가까이 끌려 들어갔다."], ["Rawdon Crawley, then grown a tall, fine lad, blushed when he got the letter.", "어느덧 훤칠하고 잘생긴 소년으로 자란 로던 크롤리는 그 편지를 받고 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["\"Oh, Aunt Jane, you are my mother!\"", "\"오, 제인 고모, 고모가 제 어머니예요!\""], ["he said; \"and not--and not that one.\"", "그는 말했다. \"저 사람이 — 저 사람이 아니라요.\""], ["But he wrote back a kind and respectful letter to Mrs. Rebecca, then living at a boarding-house at Florence.", "그러나 그는 당시 피렌체의 하숙집에 살고 있던 레베카 부인에게 다정하고 정중한 답장을 써 보냈다."], ["But we are advancing matters.", "그러나 우리는 이야기를 앞질러 가고 있다."]], [["Our darling Becky's first flight was not very far.", "우리의 사랑스러운 베키가 처음 날아간 거리는 그리 멀지 않았다."], ["She perched upon the French coast at Boulogne, that refuge of so much exiled English innocence, and there lived in rather a genteel, widowed manner, with a femme de chambre and a couple of rooms, at an hotel.", "그녀는 그토록 많은 추방당한 영국식 순결이 깃드는 피난처, 불로뉴의 프랑스 해안에 내려앉아, 거기서 어느 호텔에 하녀 한 명과 방 두 칸을 두고 제법 점잖은 미망인 행세를 하며 살았다."], ["She dined at the table d'hote, where people thought her very pleasant, and where she entertained her neighbours by stories of her brother, Sir Pitt, and her great London acquaintance, talking that easy, fashionable slip-slop which has so much effect upon certain folks of small breeding.", "그녀는 정식 식탁에서 식사를 했는데, 사람들은 그녀를 무척 유쾌한 사람이라 여겼고, 그녀는 자기 오라버니인 피트 경과 런던의 쟁쟁한 지인들에 관한 이야기로 옆 사람들을 즐겁게 해 주며, 교양이 변변치 못한 어떤 부류의 사람들에게 그토록 잘 먹히는 그 편안하고 세련된 수다를 떨었다."], ["She passed with many of them for a person of importance; she gave little tea-parties in her private room and shared in the innocent amusements of the place in sea-bathing, and in jaunts in open carriages, in strolls on the sands, and in visits to the play.", "그녀는 그들 가운데 많은 이에게 대단한 인물로 통했다. 그녀는 자기 방에서 자그마한 다과회를 열었고, 해수욕이며 무개마차 나들이며 모래밭 산책이며 연극 구경이며 하는 그 고장의 천진한 오락에 끼어들었다."], ["Mrs. Burjoice, the printer's lady, who was boarding with her family at the hotel for the summer, and to whom her Burjoice came of a Saturday and Sunday, voted her charming, until that little rogue of a Burjoice began to pay her too much attention.", "인쇄업자의 부인인 버조이스 부인은 여름 동안 식구들과 함께 그 호텔에 묵고 있었고, 그녀의 버조이스 씨는 토요일과 일요일에만 찾아오곤 했는데, 부인은 그 망나니 같은 버조이스가 그녀에게 지나치게 관심을 쏟기 시작하기 전까지는 그녀를 매력적이라 여겼다."], ["But there was nothing in the story, only that Becky was always affable, easy, and good-natured--and with men especially.", "그러나 그 일에는 별다른 사연이 있었던 게 아니라, 그저 베키가 늘 상냥하고 편안하고 사람 좋게 굴었던 것뿐이었다 — 특히 남자들에게 그러했다."]], [["Numbers of people were going abroad as usual at the end of the season, and Becky had plenty of opportunities of finding out by the behaviour of her acquaintances of the great London world the opinion of \"society\" as regarded her conduct.", "으레 그러하듯 사교철이 끝날 무렵이면 숱한 사람들이 외국으로 나가는 터라, 베키는 런던 상류사회의 지인들이 보이는 태도를 통해, 자기 행실에 대해 \"사교계\"가 품은 견해를 알아낼 기회를 얼마든지 얻었다."], ["One day it was Lady Partlet and her daughters whom Becky confronted as she was walking modestly on Boulogne pier, the cliffs of Albion shining in the distance across the deep blue sea.", "어느 날 베키는 불로뉴 부두를 얌전히 거닐던 참에 파틀릿 부인과 그 딸들과 맞닥뜨렸는데, 깊고 푸른 바다 너머 저 멀리에서는 알비온의 절벽이 빛나고 있었다."], ["Lady Partlet marshalled all her daughters round her with a sweep of her parasol and retreated from the pier, darting savage glances at poor little Becky who stood alone there.", "파틀릿 부인은 양산을 한 번 휙 휘둘러 딸들을 죄다 자기 둘레로 불러 모으더니, 거기 홀로 서 있는 가엾은 작은 베키에게 사나운 눈길을 쏘아붙이며 부두에서 물러갔다."]], [["On another day the packet came in.", "또 어느 날에는 연락선이 들어왔다."], ["It had been blowing fresh, and it always suited Becky's humour to see the droll woe-begone faces of the people as they emerged from the boat.", "바람이 제법 세게 불었는데, 사람들이 배에서 내려올 때 그 우스꽝스럽게 초췌한 얼굴들을 구경하는 일은 늘 베키의 기분에 맞았다."], ["Lady Slingstone happened to be on board this day.", "마침 그날 슬링스톤 부인이 배에 타고 있었다."], ["Her ladyship had been exceedingly ill in her carriage, and was greatly exhausted and scarcely fit to walk up the plank from the ship to the pier.", "부인은 마차 안에서 몹시 멀미를 한 데다 기진맥진하여, 배에서 부두로 이어진 널다리를 오르기조차 버거운 형편이었다."], ["But all her energies rallied the instant she saw Becky smiling roguishly under a pink bonnet, and giving her a glance of scorn such as would have shrivelled up most women, she walked into the Custom House quite unsupported.", "그러나 분홍색 보닛 아래 장난스레 미소 짓는 베키를 본 순간 그녀의 기력은 죄다 되살아나, 웬만한 여자라면 오그라들고 말 멸시의 눈길을 그녀에게 한 번 던지고는, 아무런 부축도 받지 않고 세관 건물 안으로 걸어 들어갔다. 베키는 그저 웃기만 했다."], ["Becky only laughed: but I don't think she liked it.", "그러나 그녀가 그것을 마음에 들어 했으리라고는 생각지 않는다."], ["She felt she was alone, quite alone, and the far-off shining cliffs of England were impassable to her.", "그녀는 자기가 외롭다고, 아주 외롭다고 느꼈고, 저 멀리 빛나는 영국의 절벽은 그녀로서는 결코 다다를 수 없는 곳이었다."]], [["The behaviour of the men had undergone too I don't know what change.", "남자들의 태도 역시 어찌 된 영문인지 달라져 있었다."], ["Grinstone showed his teeth and laughed in her face with a familiarity that was not pleasant.", "그린스톤은 이를 드러내고 보기 싫게 허물없는 태도로 그녀의 면전에서 웃어댔다."], ["Little Bob Suckling, who was cap in hand to her three months before, and would walk a mile in the rain to see for her carriage in the line at Gaunt House, was talking to Fitzoof of the Guards (Lord Heehaw's son) one day upon the jetty, as Becky took her walk there.", "석 달 전만 해도 그녀에게 모자를 벗어 들고 굽실거리며, 곤트 하우스 앞에 줄지어 선 마차들 속에서 그녀의 마차를 찾아 주려고 빗속을 일 마일이나 걸어 주던 꼬마 밥 서클링은, 어느 날 베키가 산책하러 나간 그 방파제에서 근위대의 피츠우프(히호 경의 아들)와 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["Little Bobby nodded to her over his shoulder, without moving his hat, and continued his conversation with the heir of Heehaw.", "꼬마 보비는 모자에 손도 대지 않은 채 어깨너머로 그녀에게 까딱 고갯짓만 하고는, 히호 가의 그 상속자와 하던 대화를 계속했다."], ["Tom Raikes tried to walk into her sitting-room at the inn with a cigar in his mouth, but she closed the door upon him, and would have locked it, only that his fingers were inside.", "톰 레이크스는 입에 시가를 문 채 여관의 그녀 거실로 걸어 들어오려 했으나, 그녀는 그의 면전에서 문을 닫았고, 그의 손가락이 끼어 있지만 않았더라면 자물쇠까지 채웠을 것이다."], ["She began to feel that she was very lonely indeed.", "그녀는 정말이지 자기가 몹시 외롭다는 것을 느끼기 시작했다."], ["\"If HE'D been here,\"", "\"그이가 여기 있었더라면,\""], ["she said, \"those cowards would never have dared to insult me.\"", "그녀는 말했다. \"저 비겁한 자들이 감히 나를 모욕할 엄두도 못 냈을 텐데.\""], ["She thought about \"him\" with great sadness and perhaps longing--about his honest, stupid, constant kindness and fidelity; his never-ceasing obedience; his good humour; his bravery and courage.", "그녀는 \"그이\"를 두고 깊은 슬픔에 잠겨, 어쩌면 그리움에 잠겨 생각했다 — 그 정직하고, 우둔하고, 한결같은 친절과 충실함을, 그 그칠 줄 모르던 순종을, 그 너그러운 성품을, 그 용맹함과 담대함을."], ["Very likely she cried, for she was particularly lively, and had put on a little extra rouge, when she came down to dinner.", "십중팔구 그녀는 울었을 것이다. 저녁 식사 자리에 내려왔을 때 그녀는 유난히 명랑했고, 평소보다 연지를 조금 더 발랐던 것이다."]], [["She rouged regularly now; and--and her maid got Cognac for her besides that which was charged in the hotel bill.", "이제 그녀는 으레 연지를 발랐다. 그리고 — 그리고 그녀의 하녀는 호텔 계산서에 올라간 것 말고도 따로 코냑을 구해다 주었다."]], [["Perhaps the insults of the men were not, however, so intolerable to her as the sympathy of certain women. Mrs.", "그러나 남자들의 모욕이라 한들, 어떤 여자들의 동정만큼 그녀에게 견디기 힘든 것은 아니었으리라."], ["Crackenbury and Mrs. Washington White passed through Boulogne on their way to Switzerland.", "크래컨버리 부인과 워싱턴 화이트 부인이 스위스로 가는 길에 불로뉴를 지나갔다."], ["The party were protected by Colonel Horner, young Beaumoris, and of course old Crackenbury, and Mrs. White's little girl.", "일행은 호너 대령과 젊은 보모리스, 그리고 물론 늙은 크래컨버리와 화이트 부인의 어린 딸의 호위를 받고 있었다."], ["THEY did not avoid her.", "그들은 그녀를 피하지 않았다."], ["They giggled, cackled, tattled, condoled, consoled, and patronized her until they drove her almost wild with rage.", "그들은 키득거리고, 재잘대고, 수군대고, 위로하고, 달래고, 생색을 내며 그녀를 거의 격분에 미칠 지경으로 몰아갔다."], ["To be patronized by THEM!", "저따위 것들에게 동정을 받다니!"], ["she thought, as they went away simpering after kissing her.", "그들이 입을 맞추고 나서 헤벌쭉 웃으며 떠나갈 때 그녀는 그렇게 생각했다."], ["And she heard Beaumoris's laugh ringing on the stair and knew quite well how to interpret his hilarity.", "그리고 그녀는 계단에서 울려 퍼지는 보모리스의 웃음소리를 들었고, 그 흥겨운 웃음을 어떻게 해석해야 할지 너무도 잘 알았다."]], [["It was after this visit that Becky, who had paid her weekly bills, Becky who had made herself agreeable to everybody in the house, who smiled at the landlady, called the waiters \"monsieur,\"", "베키가 — 매주 청구서를 꼬박꼬박 치르고, 그 집안의 모든 사람에게 상냥하게 굴고, 안주인에게 미소 짓고, 종업원들을 \"므슈\"라 부르고, 객실 하녀들에게는 예의와 사과로 보답하여, 돈 문제에서 조금 인색한 점(베키는 결코 이 점에서 자유롭지 못했다)을 너끈히 메우고도 남던 그 베키가 — 그렇다, 우리가 말하건대, 바로 그 베키가 이 방문이 있은 뒤 주인으로부터 나가 달라는 통고를 받은 것이다."], ["and paid the chambermaids in politeness and apologies, what far more than compensated for a little niggardliness in point of money (of which Becky never was free), that Becky, we say, received a notice to quit from the landlord, who had been told by some one that she was quite an unfit person to have at his hotel, where English ladies would not sit down with her.", "누군가 주인에게 그녀가 자기 호텔에 둘 만한 인물이 결코 못 되며, 영국 숙녀들이 그녀와 한 식탁에 앉으려 들지 않으리라고 일러바친 것이었다."], ["And she was forced to fly into lodgings of which the dulness and solitude were most wearisome to her.", "그리하여 그녀는 그 따분함과 적막함이 더없이 진력나는 셋방으로 도망치듯 옮겨갈 수밖에 없었다."]], [["Still she held up, in spite of these rebuffs, and tried to make a character for herself and conquer scandal.", "그래도 그녀는 이런 냉대에도 굴하지 않고 버티며, 스스로 떳떳한 인품을 세우고 추문을 물리치려 애썼다."], ["She went to church very regularly and sang louder than anybody there.", "그녀는 더없이 꼬박꼬박 예배에 나가, 거기 누구보다도 큰 소리로 찬송을 불렀다."], ["She took up the cause of the widows of the shipwrecked fishermen, and gave work and drawings for the Quashyboo Mission; she subscribed to the Assembly and WOULDN'T waltz.", "그녀는 난파한 어부들의 미망인을 위한 사업을 떠맡았고, 콰시부 선교회에 일감과 그림을 내놓았다. 그녀는 무도회비를 냈으면서도 왈츠는 추지 않으려 했다."], ["In a word, she did everything that was respectable, and that is why we dwell upon this part of her career with more fondness than upon subsequent parts of her history, which are not so pleasant.", "한마디로 그녀는 점잖다고 할 만한 일은 죄다 했으니, 우리가 그녀 생애의 이 대목을 두고, 그다지 유쾌하지 못한 이후의 행적보다 더 애정을 담아 오래 머무는 것도 그 때문이다."], ["She saw people avoiding her, and still laboriously smiled upon them; you never could suppose from her countenance what pangs of humiliation she might be enduring inwardly.", "그녀는 사람들이 자기를 피하는 것을 보면서도 여전히 안간힘을 다해 그들에게 미소를 지어 보였다. 그 얼굴만 보아서는 그녀가 속으로 얼마나 굴욕의 고통을 견디고 있는지 결코 짐작할 수 없었으리라."]], [["Her history was after all a mystery.", "그녀의 내력은 따지고 보면 하나의 수수께끼였다."], ["Parties were divided about her.", "그녀를 두고 사람들의 의견은 갈렸다."], ["Some people who took the trouble to busy themselves in the matter said that she was the criminal, whilst others vowed that she was as innocent as a lamb and that her odious husband was in fault.", "굳이 수고를 들여 그 일에 끼어든 어떤 이들은 그녀가 죄인이라고 했고, 또 어떤 이들은 그녀가 어린양처럼 결백하며 그 가증스러운 남편이 잘못이라고 장담했다."], ["She won over a good many by bursting into tears about her boy and exhibiting the most frantic grief when his name was mentioned, or she saw anybody like him. She gained good Mrs.", "그녀는 제 아들 이야기에 눈물을 터뜨리고, 그 이름이 입에 오르거나 그와 닮은 누군가를 보면 더없이 미친 듯한 슬픔을 드러내 보임으로써 적잖은 사람들을 자기편으로 끌어들였다."], ["Alderney's heart in that way, who was rather the Queen of British Boulogne and gave the most dinners and balls of all the residents there, by weeping when Master Alderney came from Dr.", "그녀는 그런 식으로 마음씨 고운 올더니 부인의 마음을 얻었는데, 이 부인으로 말하면 영국인이 모인 불로뉴에서는 거의 여왕 격으로 그곳 거주자들 중 가장 많은 만찬과 무도회를 베푸는 사람이었다."], ["Swishtail's academy to pass his holidays with his mother. \"He and her Rawdon were of the same age, and so like,\"", "올더니 도련님이 방학을 어머니와 함께 보내려고 스위시테일 박사의 학원에서 돌아왔을 때, 베키는 눈물을 흘려 보였다."], ["Becky said in a voice choking with agony; whereas there was five years'", "\"그 애와 우리 로던이 동갑이고, 또 어찌나 닮았는지요.\""], ["difference between the boys'", "베키는 고통으로 목이 메는 목소리로 말했다."], ["ages, and no more likeness between them than between my respected reader and his humble servant. Wenham, when he was going abroad, on his way to Kissingen to join Lord Steyne, enlightened Mrs.", "그러나 사실 두 소년의 나이는 다섯 살이나 차이가 났고, 둘 사이의 닮은 점이라곤 존경하는 독자 여러분과 그 보잘것없는 종복 사이의 닮은 점만큼이나 없었다."], ["Alderney on this point and told her how he was much more able to describe little Rawdon than his mamma, who notoriously hated him and never saw him; how he was thirteen years old, while little Alderney was but nine, fair, while the other darling was dark--in a word, caused the lady in question to repent of her good humour.", "웬햄은 스타인 경과 합류하러 키싱겐으로 가는 길에 외국으로 나가던 중 이 점에 관해 올더니 부인의 눈을 틔워 주었으니, 어린 로던을 묘사하는 일이라면 그 어미보다 자기가 훨씬 잘할 수 있다고 — 그 어미는 소문난 대로 아이를 미워하여 한 번도 보지 않았으니 — 일러 주었다. 그 애는 열세 살인데 어린 올더니는 겨우 아홉 살이요, 그 애는 살결이 검은데 이 귀여운 도련님은 살결이 희다는 것 따위를 — 한마디로 그는 문제의 부인이 제 호의를 후회하게 만들고 만 것이었다."]], [["Whenever Becky made a little circle for herself with incredible toils and labour, somebody came and swept it down rudely, and she had all her work to begin over again.", "베키가 믿을 수 없는 노고와 수고를 들여 제 둘레에 자그마한 무리를 만들어 놓을 때마다, 누군가가 나타나 그것을 매정하게 쓸어 무너뜨렸고, 그녀는 모든 일을 다시 처음부터 시작해야 했다."], ["It was very hard; very hard; lonely and disheartening.", "그것은 몹시 고된 일이었다. 정말이지 고되고, 외롭고, 맥 빠지는 일이었다."]], [["There was Mrs. Newbright, who took her up for some time, attracted by the sweetness of her singing at church and by her proper views upon serious subjects, concerning which in former days, at Queen's Crawley, Mrs. Becky had had a good deal of instruction. Well, she not only took tracts, but she read them. She worked flannel petticoats for the Quashyboos--cotton night-caps for the Cocoanut Indians--painted handscreens for the conversion of the Pope and the Jews--sat under Mr. Rowls on Wednesdays, Mr. Huggleton on Thursdays, attended two Sunday services at church, besides Mr. Bawler, the Darbyite, in the evening, and all in vain. Mrs. Newbright had occasion to correspond with the Countess of Southdown about the Warmingpan Fund for the Fiji Islanders (for the management of which admirable charity both these ladies formed part of a female committee), and having mentioned her \"sweet friend,\" Mrs. Rawdon Crawley, the Dowager Countess wrote back such a letter regarding Becky, with such particulars, hints, facts, falsehoods, and general comminations, that intimacy between Mrs. Newbright and Mrs. Crawley ceased forthwith, and all the serious world of Tours, where this misfortune took place, immediately parted company with the reprobate. Those who know the English Colonies abroad know that we carry with us us our pride, pills, prejudices, Harvey-sauces, cayenne-peppers, and other Lares, making a little Britain wherever we settle down.", "뉴브라이트 부인이라는 이가 있어 한동안 그녀를 거두어 주었으니, 예배 때 부르는 그녀의 감미로운 노래와, 지난날 퀸스크롤리에서 베키 부인이 적잖이 가르침을 받은 바 있는 경건한 문제들에 관한 그녀의 올바른 견해에 마음이 끌린 것이었다. 그러니, 그녀는 전도지를 받아 들기만 한 게 아니라 그것을 읽기까지 했다. 그녀는 콰시부족을 위해 플란넬 속치마를 짓고 — 코코넛 인디언을 위해 무명 잠자리 모자를 만들고 — 교황과 유대인의 개종을 위해 손부채에 그림을 그리고 — 수요일에는 롤스 씨, 목요일에는 허글턴 씨의 설교 아래 앉았으며, 일요일에는 두 차례의 예배에 참석하고도 저녁이면 더비파인 볼러 씨의 설교까지 들었으나, 죄다 헛일이었다. 뉴브라이트 부인은 피지 섬 주민을 위한 보온 침대 기금 문제로 사우스다운 백작부인과 편지를 주고받을 일이 있었는데(이 훌륭한 자선사업의 운영에 두 부인 모두 여성 위원회의 일원으로 참여하고 있었다), 자기의 \"사랑스러운 벗\" 로던 크롤리 부인을 언급하자, 노백작부인은 베키에 관해 갖가지 자세한 내막과 암시와 사실과 거짓과 두루뭉술한 저주를 곁들인 답장을 써 보냈고, 그리하여 뉴브라이트 부인과 크롤리 부인의 친분은 그 즉시 끝장나고 말았다. 이 불행이 일어난 투르의 경건한 무리 전체도 그 천하의 죄인과 곧장 절교해 버렸다. 외국에 나가 있는 영국인 거류지를 아는 이라면, 우리가 어디에 자리를 잡든 우리의 자존심과 환약과 편견과 하비 소스와 카옌 후추, 그 밖의 온갖 수호신을 함께 짊어지고 다니며 거기에 작은 영국을 만들어 낸다는 것을 알 것이다."]], [["From one colony to another Becky fled uneasily.", "베키는 이 거류지에서 저 거류지로 불안하게 도망쳐 다녔다."], ["From Boulogne to Dieppe, from Dieppe to Caen, from Caen to Tours--trying with all her might to be respectable, and alas!", "불로뉴에서 디에프로, 디에프에서 캉으로, 캉에서 투르로 — 안간힘을 다해 점잖아지려 애썼건만, 아아!"], ["always found out some day or other and pecked out of the cage by the real daws.", "언제나 어느 날엔가는 정체가 드러나, 진짜 갈가마귀들에게 쪼여 새장 밖으로 내쫓기고 마는 것이었다."]], [["Mrs. Hook Eagles took her up at one of these places--a woman without a blemish in her character and a house in Portman Square.", "이런 곳들 가운데 한 곳에서 후크 이글스 부인이 그녀를 거두어 주었으니, 흠 하나 없는 인품에 포트먼 광장에 저택을 둔 여인이었다."], ["She was staying at the hotel at Dieppe, whither Becky fled, and they made each other's acquaintance first at sea, where they were swimming together, and subsequently at the table d'hote of the hotel. Mrs.", "그녀는 베키가 도망쳐 간 디에프의 그 호텔에 묵고 있었고, 두 사람은 함께 헤엄을 치던 바다에서 처음 사귀게 되었으며, 그 뒤로는 그 호텔의 정식 식탁에서 안면을 텄다."], ["Eagles had heard--who indeed had not?--some of the scandal of the Steyne affair; but after a conversation with Becky, she pronounced that Mrs.", "이글스 부인은 스타인 사건의 추문을 — 하기야 그것을 듣지 못한 사람이 어디 있었겠는가? — 얼마간 들어 알고 있었다."], ["Crawley was an angel, her husband a ruffian, Lord Steyne an unprincipled wretch, as everybody knew, and the whole case against Mrs. Crawley an infamous and wicked conspiracy of that rascal Wenham.", "그러나 베키와 한 번 이야기를 나눈 뒤 그녀는 단언하기를, 크롤리 부인은 천사요, 그 남편은 악당이요, 스타인 경은 누구나 다 알듯이 파렴치한 인간이요, 크롤리 부인에 대한 사건 전체는 저 악당 웬햄이 꾸민 더없이 파렴치하고 사악한 음모라는 것이었다."], ["\"If you were a man of any spirit, Mr. Eagles, you would box the wretch's ears the next time you see him at the Club,\"", "\"당신이 조금이라도 기개 있는 사내라면, 이글스 씨, 다음번에 클럽에서 그 못된 자를 보거든 따귀라도 한 대 갈겨야 마땅해요.\""], ["she said to her husband.", "그녀는 남편에게 말했다."], ["But Eagles was only a quiet old gentleman, husband to Mrs. Eagles, with a taste for geology, and not tall enough to reach anybody's ears.", "그러나 이글스는 그저 조용한 노신사로, 이글스 부인의 남편이요, 지질학에 취미가 있을 뿐, 누구의 따귀에 손이 닿을 만큼 키가 크지도 못했다."]], [["The Eagles then patronized Mrs. Rawdon, took her to live with her at her own house at Paris, quarrelled with the ambassador's wife because she would not receive her protegee, and did all that lay in woman's power to keep Becky straight in the paths of virtue and good repute.", "그리하여 이글스 부부는 로던 부인을 비호하여, 파리의 제 집으로 데려가 함께 살게 했고, 자기가 돌보는 사람을 받아들이려 하지 않는다 하여 대사 부인과 다투었으며, 베키를 미덕과 명예의 길에서 벗어나지 않게 하려고 한 여인이 할 수 있는 일은 죄다 했다."]], [["Becky was very respectable and orderly at first, but the life of humdrum virtue grew utterly tedious to her before long.", "베키는 처음에는 무척 점잖고 단정했으나, 단조롭게 덕을 지키는 생활이 머지않아 그녀에게는 도무지 견딜 수 없이 지루해졌다."], ["It was the same routine every day, the same dulness and comfort, the same drive over the same stupid Bois de Boulogne, the same company of an evening, the same Blair's Sermon of a Sunday night--the same opera always being acted over and over again; Becky was dying of weariness, when, luckily for her, young Mr.", "날마다 똑같은 일과요, 똑같은 따분함과 안락이요, 똑같이 멍청한 불로뉴 숲을 가로지르는 똑같은 마차 산책이요, 저녁이면 똑같은 사람들이요, 일요일 밤이면 똑같은 블레어의 설교집이요 — 똑같은 오페라가 늘 거듭거듭 상연되는 격이었다."], ["Eagles came from Cambridge, and his mother, seeing the impression which her little friend made upon him, straightway gave Becky warning.", "베키가 권태로 죽을 지경이 되었을 무렵, 다행스럽게도 젊은 이글스 군이 케임브리지에서 돌아왔고, 그의 어머니는 자기의 작은 벗이 아들에게 어떤 인상을 심어 주는지를 알아채고는 그 즉시 베키에게 나가 달라는 통고를 했다."]], [["Then she tried keeping house with a female friend; then the double menage began to quarrel and get into debt.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 어떤 여자 친구와 함께 살림을 차려 보았는데, 이내 그 두 살림은 다투기 시작했고 빚을 지게 되었다."], ["Then she determined upon a boarding-house existence and lived for some time at that famous mansion kept by Madame de Saint Amour, in the Rue Royale, at Paris, where she began exercising her graces and fascinations upon the shabby dandies and fly-blown beauties who frequented her landlady's salons.", "그러자 그녀는 하숙 생활을 하기로 마음먹고, 파리의 루아얄 거리에서 생타무르 부인이 꾸리는 그 유명한 저택에서 한동안 살았는데, 거기서 그녀는 안주인의 살롱을 드나드는 초라한 멋쟁이들과 한물간 미인들을 상대로 제 매력과 교태를 부려 보기 시작했다."], ["Becky loved society and, indeed, could no more exist without it than an opium-eater without his dram, and she was happy enough at the period of her boarding-house life.", "베키는 사교계를 사랑했으니, 아니 정말이지 아편쟁이가 한 모금 없이는 못 살 듯 사교계 없이는 살 수 없었으며, 하숙 생활을 하던 그 시절에는 제법 행복했다."], ["\"The women here are as amusing as those in May Fair,\"", "\"여기 여자들도 메이페어 여자들만큼이나 재미있어.\""], ["she told an old London friend who met her, \"only, their dresses are not quite so fresh.", "그녀는 우연히 마주친 옛 런던 친구에게 말했다. \"다만 옷차림이 그만큼 산뜻하지 못할 뿐이지."], ["The men wear cleaned gloves, and are sad rogues, certainly, but they are not worse than Jack This and Tom That.", "남자들은 빤 장갑을 끼고, 확실히 형편없는 악당들이긴 하지만, 그래도 잭 아무개니 톰 아무개니 하는 자들보다 나쁘진 않아."], ["The mistress of the house is a little vulgar, but I don't think she is so vulgar as Lady ------\"", "이 집 안주인은 좀 천박하긴 한데, 그래도 그 레이디 ◯◯◯◯만큼 천박하진 않은 것 같아.\""], ["and here she named the name of a great leader of fashion that I would die rather than reveal.", "그러고는 그녀는 어느 대단한 유행계의 우두머리 이름을 댔는데, 나는 그 이름을 밝히느니 차라리 죽고 말리라."], ["In fact, when you saw Madame de Saint Amour's rooms lighted up of a night, men with plaques and cordons at the ecarte tables, and the women at a little distance, you might fancy yourself for a while in good society, and that Madame was a real Countess.", "사실 밤이면 생타무르 부인의 방들이 환히 밝혀지고, 가슴에 훈장과 수장을 단 사내들이 에카르테 탁자에 둘러앉고, 여자들이 그 조금 떨어진 곳에 자리한 광경을 보노라면, 한동안은 자기가 상류사회에 와 있는 듯, 그리고 그 부인이 진짜 백작부인인 듯 여겨질 법도 했다."], ["Many people did so fancy, and Becky was for a while one of the most dashing ladies of the Countess's salons.", "실제로 그렇게 여기는 사람들이 많았고, 베키는 한동안 그 백작부인의 살롱에서 가장 멋들어진 부인들 가운데 하나였다."]], [["But it is probable that her old creditors of 1815 found her out and caused her to leave Paris, for the poor little woman was forced to fly from the city rather suddenly, and went thence to Brussels.", "그러나 십중팔구 1815년 무렵의 옛 채권자들이 그녀를 찾아내어 파리를 떠나게 만든 모양이니, 가엾은 작은 여인은 제법 갑작스레 그 도시를 떠나야 했고, 거기서 브뤼셀로 옮겨갔다."]], [["How well she remembered the place!", "그녀는 그곳을 어찌나 또렷이 기억하던지!"], ["She grinned as she looked up at the little entresol which she had occupied, and thought of the Bareacres family, bawling for horses and flight, as their carriage stood in the porte-cochere of the hotel.", "그녀는 자기가 묵었던 그 자그마한 중이층을 올려다보며 히죽 웃었고, 호텔 정문 차도에 마차를 세워 둔 채 말을 대령하라, 어서 도망치자, 고래고래 외쳐대던 베어에이커스 일가를 떠올렸다."], ["She went to Waterloo and to Laeken, where George Osborne's monument much struck her.", "그녀는 워털루로, 또 라컨으로 갔는데, 거기 있는 조지 오스본의 기념비가 그녀에게 적잖은 감회를 주었다."], ["She made a little sketch of it.", "그녀는 그것을 가볍게 그려 두었다."], ["\"That poor Cupid!\"", "\"그 가엾은 큐피드 같으니!\""], ["she said; \"how dreadfully he was in love with me, and what a fool he was!", "그녀가 말했다. \"어찌나 끔찍하게 나한테 빠져 있었던지, 또 어찌나 바보 같았던지!"], ["I wonder whether little Emmy is alive.", "어린 에미가 살아 있을지 모르겠네."], ["It was a good little creature; and that fat brother of hers.", "마음씨 고운 작은 사람이었지. 그 뚱뚱한 오라비도."], ["I have his funny fat picture still among my papers.", "그 우스꽝스럽게 뚱뚱한 초상화를 아직도 내 서류들 사이에 갖고 있는걸."], ["They were kind simple people.\"", "친절하고 순박한 사람들이었어.\""]], [["At Brussels Becky arrived, recommended by Madame de Saint Amour to her friend, Madame la Comtesse de Borodino, widow of Napoleon's General, the famous Count de Borodino, who was left with no resource by the deceased hero but that of a table d'hote and an ecarte table.", "베키는 생타무르 부인이 자기 친구인 보로디노 백작부인에게 그녀를 천거하여 브뤼셀에 당도했다. 보로디노 백작부인은 나폴레옹 휘하의 장군이자 그 유명한 보로디노 백작의 미망인이었는데, 세상을 떠난 그 영웅이 남겨 준 살림 밑천이라곤 정식 식탁과 에카르테 탁자가 전부였다."], ["Second-rate dandies and roues, widow-ladies who always have a lawsuit, and very simple English folks, who fancy they see \"Continental society\" at these houses, put down their money, or ate their meals, at Madame de Borodino's tables.", "이류 멋쟁이들과 난봉꾼들, 언제나 소송 한 건쯤은 끼고 사는 미망인들, 그리고 이런 집들에서 \"대륙 사교계\"를 구경하는 줄로 여기는 더없이 순진한 영국 사람들이 보로디노 부인의 식탁에 돈을 내려놓거나 식사를 했다."], ["The gallant young fellows treated the company round to champagne at the table d'hote, rode out with the women, or hired horses on country excursions, clubbed money to take boxes at the play or the opera, betted over the fair shoulders of the ladies at the ecarte tables, and wrote home to their parents in Devonshire about their felicitous introduction to foreign society.", "의기양양한 젊은 친구들은 정식 식탁에 둘러앉은 사람들에게 샴페인을 한턱냈고, 여자들과 말을 타고 나가거나 시골 나들이를 위해 말을 빌렸으며, 돈을 추렴해 연극이나 오페라의 특별석을 잡았고, 에카르테 탁자에서는 부인들의 고운 어깨너머로 돈을 걸었으며, 데번셔에 있는 부모에게는 외국 사교계에 운 좋게 발을 들였노라 편지를 써 보냈다."]], [["Here, as at Paris, Becky was a boarding-house queen, and ruled in select pensions. She never refused the champagne, or the bouquets, or the drives into the country, or the private boxes; but what she preferred was the ecarte at night,--and she played audaciously. First she played only for a little, then for five-franc pieces, then for Napoleons, then for notes: then she would not be able to pay her month's pension: then she borrowed from the young gentlemen: then she got into cash again and bullied Madame de Borodino, whom she had coaxed and wheedled before: then she was playing for ten sous at a time, and in a dire state of poverty: then her quarter's allowance would come in, and she would pay off Madame de Borodino's score and would once more take the cards against Monsieur de Rossignol, or the Chevalier de Raff.", "파리에서 그러했듯 여기서도 베키는 하숙집의 여왕이었고, 엄선된 하숙집들을 호령했다. 그녀는 샴페인도, 꽃다발도, 시골 나들이도, 특별석도 결코 마다하지 않았다. 그러나 그녀가 가장 좋아한 것은 밤에 하는 에카르테였으니 — 그녀는 대담하게 도박을 벌였다. 처음에는 그저 푼돈으로 시작하더니, 이윽고 오 프랑짜리 동전으로, 그다음엔 나폴레옹 금화로, 그다음엔 지폐로 판을 키웠다. 그러다 한 달치 하숙비를 치를 수 없게 되었다. 그러면 젊은 신사들에게 돈을 빌렸다. 그러다 다시 현금이 생기면, 전에 어르고 구슬려대던 보로디노 부인을 윽박질렀다. 그러다가 한 번에 십 수씩만 걸 처지가 되어, 끔찍한 가난에 빠졌다. 그러다 분기별 수당이 들어오면 보로디노 부인에게 진 빚을 갚고는, 다시 한번 로시뇰 씨나 라프 기사를 상대로 카드를 잡는 것이었다."]], [["When Becky left Brussels, the sad truth is that she owed three months' pension to Madame de Borodino, of which fact, and of the gambling, and of the drinking, and of the going down on her knees to the Reverend Mr. Muff, Ministre Anglican, and borrowing money of him, and of her coaxing and flirting with Milor Noodle, son of Sir Noodle, pupil of the Rev. Mr. Muff, whom she used to take into her private room, and of whom she won large sums at ecarte--of which fact, I say, and of a hundred of her other knaveries, the Countess de Borodino informs every English person who stops at her establishment, and announces that Madame Rawdon was no better than a vipere.", "베키가 브뤼셀을 떠날 무렵의 서글픈 진실인즉, 그녀는 보로디노 부인에게 석 달치 하숙비를 빚지고 있었다. 이 사실이며, 도박이며, 음주며, 영국 국교회 목사인 머프 목사 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 돈을 빌린 일이며, 머프 목사의 제자이자 누들 경의 아들인 누들 도련님을 어르고 꼬드겨 제 방으로 끌어들여 에카르테로 거액을 따낸 일이며 — 그러니까 이 사실이며 그 밖의 백 가지 못된 짓거리를, 보로디노 백작부인은 자기 집에 묵는 영국 사람마다 붙들고 일러바치면서, 로던 부인은 한낱 독사나 다름없는 여자였노라고 떠벌리는 것이다."]], [["So our little wanderer went about setting up her tent in various cities of Europe, as restless as Ulysses or Bampfylde Moore Carew.", "그리하여 우리의 작은 방랑자는 율리시스나 뱀필드 무어 캐루처럼 잠시도 가만있지 못한 채, 유럽의 여러 도시를 옮겨 다니며 천막을 쳤다."], ["Her taste for disrespectability grew more and more remarkable.", "점잖지 못함을 좇는 그녀의 취향은 갈수록 더 두드러졌다."], ["She became a perfect Bohemian ere long, herding with people whom it would make your hair stand on end to meet.", "그녀는 머지않아 완벽한 보헤미안이 되어, 마주치면 머리카락이 곤두설 만한 사람들과 어울려 지냈다."]], [["There is no town of any mark in Europe but it has its little colony of English raffs--men whose names Mr. Hemp the officer reads out periodically at the Sheriffs'", "유럽에서 이름깨나 있는 도시치고 영국인 부랑자들의 작은 거류지가 없는 곳은 없으니 — 헴프 집행관이 보안관 법정에서 그 이름을 정기적으로 읽어 올리는 사내들이요, 흔히 제법 양가의 출신이지만 그 집안이 인연을 끊어 버린 젊은 신사들이요, 당구장과 카페의 단골들이요, 외국 경마와 도박판의 후원자들이다."], ["Court--young gentlemen of very good family often, only that the latter disowns them; frequenters of billiard-rooms and estaminets, patrons of foreign races and gaming-tables. They people the debtors'", "그들은 채무자 감옥을 가득 메운다 — 술을 마시고 으스댄다 — 싸우고 주먹다짐을 벌인다 — 돈도 안 내고 달아난다 — 프랑스와 독일 장교들과 결투를 벌인다 — 에카르테로 스푸니 씨를 속여먹는다 — 돈을 손에 쥐면 으리으리한 사륜마차를 몰고 바덴으로 내뺀다 — 백전백승의 도박술을 시험해 보고는, 빈 주머니로, 초라한 협잡꾼이요 빈털터리 멋쟁이가 되어 도박 탁자 언저리를 어슬렁거리다가, 가짜 환어음으로 유대인 은행가를 등쳐먹거나 또 다른 스푸니 씨를 찾아내어 털어먹는 것이다."], ["prisons--they drink and swagger--they fight and brawl--they run away without paying--they have duels with French and German officers--they cheat Mr. Spooney at ecarte--they get the money and drive off to Baden in magnificent britzkas--they try their infallible martingale and lurk about the tables with empty pockets, shabby bullies, penniless bucks, until they can swindle a Jew banker with a sham bill of exchange, or find another Mr.", "이런 사람들이 겪는 영화와 비참의 교차란 참으로 기묘한 구경거리다."], ["Spooney to rob. The alternations of splendour and misery which these people undergo are very queer to view.", "그들의 삶이란 필시 굉장한 흥분의 연속일 것이다."], ["Their life must be one of great excitement.", "베키도 — 이것을 인정해야만 할까?"], ["Becky--must it be owned?--took to this life, and took to it not unkindly.", "— 이런 삶에 빠져들었고, 그것도 그리 싫지 않은 마음으로 빠져들었다."], ["She went about from town to town among these Bohemians.", "그녀는 이 보헤미안들 틈에 끼어 이 도시 저 도시를 떠돌았다."], ["The lucky Mrs. Rawdon was known at every play-table in Germany.", "운 좋은 로던 부인은 독일의 도박 탁자란 도박 탁자에서는 죄다 이름이 알려져 있었다."], ["She and Madame de Cruchecassee kept house at Florence together.", "그녀는 크뤼슈카세 부인과 피렌체에서 함께 살림을 차렸다."], ["It is said she was ordered out of Munich, and my friend Mr. Frederick Pigeon avers that it was at her house at Lausanne that he was hocussed at supper and lost eight hundred pounds to Major Loder and the Honourable Mr.", "듣자 하니 그녀는 뮌헨에서 추방당했다 하고, 내 벗 프레더릭 피전 씨가 단언하기로는, 자기가 로잔에 있는 그녀의 집에서 밤참 자리에 약을 먹고 정신을 잃은 채 로더 소령과 듀세이스 귀공자에게 팔백 파운드를 잃었다는 것이다."], ["Deuceace. We are bound, you see, to give some account of Becky's biography, but of this part, the less, perhaps, that is said the better.", "보다시피 우리는 베키의 전기에 대해 어느 정도 이야기를 늘어놓을 의무가 있지만, 이 대목에 관해서는 아마도 적게 이야기할수록 나을 것이다."]], [["They say that, when Mrs. Crawley was particularly down on her luck, she gave concerts and lessons in music here and there.", "듣자 하니, 크롤리 부인은 운수가 유난히 사나울 때면 여기저기서 음악회를 열고 음악 교습을 했다고 한다."], ["There was a Madame de Raudon, who certainly had a matinee musicale at Wildbad, accompanied by Herr Spoff, premier pianist to the Hospodar of Wallachia, and my little friend Mr.", "라우든 부인이라는 사람이 있어, 확실히 빌트바트에서 발라키아 영주의 수석 피아니스트인 헤르 스포프의 반주로 음악 오찬회를 열었다고 한다."], ["Eaves, who knew everybody and had travelled everywhere, always used to declare that he was at Strasburg in the year 1830, when a certain Madame Rebecque made her appearance in the opera of the Dame Blanche, giving occasion to a furious row in the theatre there.", "또 모르는 이가 없고 안 가 본 곳이 없는 내 작은 벗 이브스 씨는, 1830년에 자기가 스트라스부르에 있었는데, 그때 어느 레베크 부인이라는 사람이 오페라 「하얀 옷의 여인」에 출연하여 그곳 극장에서 한바탕 굉장한 소동을 일으켰다고 늘 단언하곤 했다. 그녀는 관객의 야유를 받으며 무대에서 쫓겨났는데, 한편으로는 그녀 자신의 무능 때문이었지만, 주로는 일층 객석(주둔지 장교들이 입장권을 받는 곳)에 있던 몇몇 사람들의 경솔한 성원 탓이었다."], ["She was hissed off the stage by the audience, partly from her own incompetency, but chiefly from the ill-advised sympathy of some persons in the parquet, (where the officers of the garrison had their admissions); and Eaves was certain that the unfortunate debutante in question was no other than Mrs. Rawdon Crawley.", "이브스는 문제의 그 불운한 신인이 다름 아닌 로던 크롤리 부인이었다고 확신했다."]], [["She was, in fact, no better than a vagabond upon this earth.", "사실 그녀는 이 땅 위의 한낱 부랑자나 다름없었다."], ["When she got her money she gambled; when she had gambled it she was put to shifts to live; who knows how or by what means she succeeded?", "돈이 생기면 도박을 했고, 도박으로 그것을 날리면 입에 풀칠하느라 갖은 수를 다 썼다. 그녀가 어떻게, 무슨 수로 그것을 해냈는지 누가 알겠는가?"], ["It is said that she was once seen at St. Petersburg, but was summarily dismissed from that capital by the police, so that there cannot be any possibility of truth in the report that she was a Russian spy at Toplitz and Vienna afterwards.", "듣자 하니 그녀가 한번은 상트페테르부르크에서 목격되었다고 하는데, 경찰에 의해 그 수도에서 즉각 쫓겨났다 하니, 그 뒤로 그녀가 토플리츠와 빈에서 러시아의 첩자 노릇을 했다는 소문에는 진실의 가능성이 조금도 있을 수 없다."], ["I have even been informed that at Paris she discovered a relation of her own, no less a person than her maternal grandmother, who was not by any means a Montmorenci, but a hideous old box-opener at a theatre on the Boulevards.", "심지어 나는 이런 이야기까지 들었으니, 그녀가 파리에서 제 친척을 한 사람 찾아냈는데, 그가 다름 아닌 외할머니였다는 것이다. 그 외할머니로 말하면 결코 몽모랑시 가의 사람 따위가 아니라, 큰길가의 어느 극장에서 일하는 흉측한 늙은 박스 안내원이었다."], ["The meeting between them, of which other persons, as it is hinted elsewhere, seem to have been acquainted, must have been a very affecting interview.", "다른 데서 넌지시 비쳤듯 다른 사람들도 알고 있었던 듯한 그 두 사람의 만남은, 필시 무척이나 감동적인 상봉이었으리라."], ["The present historian can give no certain details regarding the event.", "본 사가는 그 사건에 관해 확실한 세부 내막은 알려 줄 수가 없다."]], [["It happened at Rome once that Mrs. de Rawdon's half-year's salary had just been paid into the principal banker's there, and, as everybody who had a balance of above five hundred scudi was invited to the balls which this prince of merchants gave during the winter, Becky had the honour of a card, and appeared at one of the Prince and Princess Polonia's splendid evening entertainments.", "언젠가 로마에서 데 로던 부인의 반년치 봉급이 마침 그곳의 으뜸가는 은행가 앞으로 막 입금된 일이 있었다."], ["The Princess was of the family of Pompili, lineally descended from the second king of Rome, and Egeria of the house of Olympus, while the Prince's grandfather, Alessandro Polonia, sold wash-balls, essences, tobacco, and pocket-handkerchiefs, ran errands for gentlemen, and lent money in a small way.", "그런데 잔고가 오백 스쿠도를 넘는 사람이라면 누구나 이 상인들의 군주가 겨울 동안 베푸는 무도회에 초대받는 터라, 베키도 초대장을 받는 영예를 누려, 폴로니아 공작 부부가 베푸는 화려한 야회들 가운데 하나에 모습을 드러냈다. 공작 부인은 폼필리 가문 출신으로, 로마의 두 번째 왕과 올림포스 가문의 에게리아의 직계 후손이었다."], ["All the great company in Rome thronged to his saloons--Princes, Dukes, Ambassadors, artists, fiddlers, monsignori, young bears with their leaders--every rank and condition of man.", "한편 공작의 조부 알레산드로 폴로니아는 비누 방울이며 향수며 담배며 손수건 따위를 팔고, 신사들의 심부름을 다니고, 자그마하게 돈놀이를 하던 사람이었다. 로마의 쟁쟁한 사람들이 죄다 그의 응접실로 몰려들었으니 — 공작이며 대공이며 대사며 예술가며 바이올린 연주자며 고위 성직자며, 인도자를 거느린 풋내기 곰 같은 자들이며 — 온갖 신분과 처지의 사람이 다 모였다."], ["His halls blazed with light and magnificence; were resplendent with gilt frames (containing pictures), and dubious antiques; and the enormous gilt crown and arms of the princely owner, a gold mushroom on a crimson field (the colour of the pocket-handkerchiefs which he sold), and the silver fountain of the Pompili family shone all over the roof, doors, and panels of the house, and over the grand velvet baldaquins prepared to receive Popes and Emperors.", "그의 홀들은 불빛과 호화로움으로 번쩍였고, (그림이 든) 금빛 액자들과 진위가 의심스러운 골동품들로 눈부셨다. 그리고 그 군주다운 주인의 거대한 금빛 왕관과 문장이 — 진홍빛 바탕(그가 팔던 손수건의 색깔이다)에 황금 버섯이 새겨진 그 문장과, 폼필리 가문의 은빛 분수가 — 저택의 천장이며 문이며 벽판마다, 또 교황과 황제를 모시려고 마련된 그 웅장한 벨벳 천개마다 온통 번쩍이고 있었다."]], [["So Becky, who had arrived in the diligence from Florence, and was lodged at an inn in a very modest way, got a card for Prince Polonia's entertainment, and her maid dressed her with unusual care, and she went to this fine ball leaning on the arm of Major Loder, with whom she happened to be travelling at the time--(the same man who shot Prince Ravoli at Naples the next year, and was caned by Sir John Buckskin for carrying four kings in his hat besides those which he used in playing at ecarte )--and this pair went into the rooms together, and Becky saw a number of old faces which she remembered in happier days, when she was not innocent, but not found out.", "그리하여 피렌체에서 역마차를 타고 당도하여 더없이 검소하게 어느 여관에 묵고 있던 베키는 폴로니아 공작의 야회 초대장을 손에 넣었고, 그녀의 하녀는 평소와 달리 정성껏 그녀를 단장해 주었다. 그녀는 마침 그 무렵 함께 여행 중이던 로더 소령의 팔에 기대어 이 성대한 무도회에 갔다(이 소령으로 말하면 이듬해 나폴리에서 라볼리 공작을 쏘아 죽이고, 에카르테를 할 때 쓰는 것 말고도 모자 속에 킹 카드를 넉 장이나 숨겨 두었다가 존 벅스킨 경에게 지팡이로 두들겨 맞은 바로 그 사내다)."], ["Major Loder knew a great number of foreigners, keen-looking whiskered men with dirty striped ribbons in their buttonholes, and a very small display of linen; but his own countrymen, it might be remarked, eschewed the Major.", "이 한 쌍은 함께 방으로 들어섰고, 베키는 자기가 결백하지는 않았으되 정체가 들통나지도 않았던 더 행복한 시절에 알았던 낯익은 얼굴들을 여럿 보았다. 로더 소령은 단춧구멍에 더러운 줄무늬 리본을 꽂고 셔츠는 거의 드러내지 않은, 수염 기른 매서운 눈초리의 외국인들을 숱하게 알고 있었다."], ["Becky, too, knew some ladies here and there--French widows, dubious Italian countesses, whose husbands had treated them ill--faugh--what shall we say, we who have moved among some of the finest company of Vanity Fair, of this refuse and sediment of rascals?", "그러나 정작 그의 동포들은, 누가 보더라도, 그 소령을 피했다. 베키 역시 여기저기서 부인 몇몇을 알아보았으니 — 프랑스인 미망인들이며, 남편에게 학대받은 진위가 의심스러운 이탈리아 백작부인들이었다 — 에잇, 허영의 시장에서 제법 빼어난 사람들 틈에 섞여 본 우리가, 이 악당들의 찌꺼기요 침전물을 두고 뭐라 말할 수 있겠는가?"], ["If we play, let it be with clean cards, and not with this dirty pack.", "노름을 할 바엔 깨끗한 카드로 할 일이지, 이런 더러운 패로 할 일은 아니다."], ["But every man who has formed one of the innumerable army of travellers has seen these marauding irregulars hanging on, like Nym and Pistol, to the main force, wearing the king's colours and boasting of his commission, but pillaging for themselves, and occasionally gibbeted by the roadside.", "그러나 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 여행자 무리의 일원이 되어 본 사람이라면 누구나, 이 약탈자 같은 비정규병들이 — 마치 님과 피스톨처럼 — 본대에 들러붙어, 임금의 군기를 두르고 그의 위임장을 떠벌리면서도 제 잇속만 차려 약탈을 일삼다가, 이따금 길가에서 교수대에 매달리는 꼴을 본 적이 있을 것이다."]], [["Well, she was hanging on the arm of Major Loder, and they went through the rooms together, and drank a great quantity of champagne at the buffet, where the people, and especially the Major's irregular corps, struggled furiously for refreshments, of which when the pair had had enough, they pushed on until they reached the Duchess's own pink velvet saloon, at the end of the suite of apartments (where the statue of the Venus is, and the great Venice looking-glasses, framed in silver), and where the princely family were entertaining their most distinguished guests at a round table at supper.", "자, 그리하여 그녀는 로더 소령의 팔에 매달려 그와 함께 방들을 누비며, 간이식당에서 샴페인을 잔뜩 마셨다. 거기서는 사람들이, 특히 소령의 비정규 부대가 다과를 차지하려고 사납게 다투고 있었다."], ["It was just such a little select banquet as that of which Becky recollected that she had partaken at Lord Steyne's--and there he sat at Polonia's table, and she saw him.", "두 사람은 실컷 마시고 나자, 응접실들이 줄지어 늘어선 그 끝에 있는 공작부인의 분홍빛 벨벳 응접실(비너스 조각상과 은테를 두른 커다란 베네치아 거울들이 있는 방)에 이를 때까지 인파를 헤치고 나아갔다. 거기서는 군주 일가가 둥근 식탁에 둘러앉아 가장 존귀한 손님들을 만찬으로 대접하고 있었다."], ["The scar cut by the diamond on his white, bald, shining forehead made a burning red mark; his red whiskers were dyed of a purple hue, which made his pale face look still paler.", "그것은 베키가 스타인 경의 집에서 함께 한 적 있다고 기억하는 바로 그런 종류의 자그마하고 엄선된 연회였다 — 그런데 폴로니아의 식탁에 그가 앉아 있었으니, 그녀는 그를 본 것이다. 그의 희고 벗어진 번들거리는 이마에 다이아몬드가 그어 놓은 흉터가 벌겋게 타오르는 자국을 이루고 있었다."], ["He wore his collar and orders, his blue ribbon and garter.", "그의 붉은 구레나룻은 자줏빛으로 물들여져 있어, 그러잖아도 창백한 그의 얼굴을 더욱 창백해 보이게 했다. 그는 깃 장식과 훈장을, 청색 수장과 가터를 차고 있었다."], ["He was a greater Prince than any there, though there was a reigning Duke and a Royal Highness, with their princesses, and near his Lordship was seated the beautiful Countess of Belladonna, nee de Glandier, whose husband (the Count Paolo della Belladonna), so well known for his brilliant entomological collections, had been long absent on a mission to the Emperor of Morocco.", "거기에는 군림하는 대공 한 사람과 그 공주들을 거느린 왕족 전하 한 사람도 있었으나, 그는 그 누구보다도 위대한 군주였다. 그리고 그 경 곁에는 아름다운 벨라도나 백작부인이, 처녀 적 이름은 드 글랑디에였던 그 부인이 앉아 있었으니, 그녀의 남편(빼어난 곤충 표본 수집으로 그토록 이름난 파올로 델라 벨라도나 백작)은 모로코 황제에게 파견된 사절단의 임무로 오래도록 자리를 비운 터였다."]], [["When Becky beheld that familiar and illustrious face, how vulgar all of a sudden did Major Loder appear to her, and how that odious Captain Rook did smell of tobacco!", "베키가 그 낯익고 쟁쟁한 얼굴을 마주한 순간, 로더 소령이 별안간 얼마나 천박해 보이던지, 또 저 가증스러운 룩 대위에게서는 어쩌면 그리도 담배 냄새가 풍기던지!"], ["In one instant she reassumed her fine-ladyship and tried to look and feel as if she were in May Fair once more.", "그녀는 한순간에 다시 우아한 귀부인의 자태를 되찾고, 마치 다시 한번 메이페어에 와 있는 양 보이고 또 느끼려 애썼다."], ["\"That woman looks stupid and ill-humoured,\"", "\"저 여자는 멍청하고 심술궂어 보이는걸.\""], ["she thought; \"I am sure she can't amuse him.", "그녀는 생각했다."], ["No, he must be bored by her--he never was by me.\"", "\"분명 저런 여자가 그이를 즐겁게 해 줄 리 없어."], ["A hundred such touching hopes, fears, and memories palpitated in her little heart, as she looked with her brightest eyes (the rouge which she wore up to her eyelids made them twinkle) towards the great nobleman.", "그래, 그이는 저 여자한테 따분해하고 있을 거야 — 나한텐 한 번도 그런 적 없었지.\" 그녀가 더없이 반짝이는 눈으로(눈꺼풀까지 바른 연지가 그 눈을 반짝이게 했다) 그 위대한 귀족을 바라보는 동안, 이렇듯 가슴 저미는 백 가지 희망과 두려움과 추억이 그녀의 작은 가슴속에서 두근거렸다."], ["Of a Star and Garter night Lord Steyne used also to put on his grandest manner and to look and speak like a great prince, as he was.", "스타와 가터를 차는 밤이면 스타인 경은 으레 더없이 거창한 태도를 갖추고, 본래 그러했듯 위대한 군주처럼 보이고 또 말하곤 했다."], ["Becky admired him smiling sumptuously, easy, lofty, and stately.", "베키는 그가 호화롭게, 편안하게, 고고하고 위엄 있게 미소 짓는 모습을 우러러보았다."], ["Ah, bon Dieu, what a pleasant companion he was, what a brilliant wit, what a rich fund of talk, what a grand manner!--and she had exchanged this for Major Loder, reeking of cigars and brandy-and-water, and Captain Rook with his horsejockey jokes and prize-ring slang, and their like.", "아아, 맙소사, 그는 얼마나 유쾌한 길동무였던가, 얼마나 눈부신 재담가였던가, 얼마나 풍성한 이야깃거리를 가졌던가, 얼마나 거창한 풍채였던가! — 그런데 그녀는 이런 사람을, 시가와 브랜디 탄 물 냄새가 진동하는 로더 소령과, 마부 같은 농지거리와 권투판 은어를 늘어놓는 룩 대위와, 그 따위 작자들과 맞바꾼 것이었다."], ["\"I wonder whether he will know me,\"", "\"그이가 나를 알아볼까.\""], ["she thought.", "그녀는 생각했다."], ["Lord Steyne was talking and laughing with a great and illustrious lady at his side, when he looked up and saw Becky.", "스타인 경은 곁에 앉은 어느 위대하고 쟁쟁한 부인과 이야기를 나누며 웃다가, 문득 고개를 들어 베키를 보았다."]], [["She was all over in a flutter as their eyes met, and she put on the very best smile she could muster, and dropped him a little, timid, imploring curtsey.", "두 사람의 눈이 마주치자 그녀는 온몸이 떨려 왔고, 그녀는 짜낼 수 있는 가장 멋진 미소를 지으며 그에게 작고 수줍은, 애원하는 듯한 인사를 살포시 건넸다."], ["He stared aghast at her for a minute, as Macbeth might on beholding Banquo's sudden appearance at his ball-supper, and remained looking at her with open mouth, when that horrid Major Loder pulled her away.", "그는 한순간 그녀를 보고 아연실색하여 멍하니 바라보았는데, 마치 맥베스가 제 무도회 만찬 자리에 뱅쿼가 불쑥 나타난 것을 보았을 때처럼, 입을 떡 벌린 채 그녀를 바라보고 있었다. 바로 그때 저 끔찍한 로더 소령이 그녀를 끌어당겼다."]], [["\"Come away into the supper-room, Mrs. R.,\"", "\"이리 와서 식당 쪽으로 갑시다, R 부인.\""], ["was that gentleman's remark: \"seeing these nobs grubbing away has made me peckish too.", "이것이 그 신사의 말이었다. \"이 나리들이 먹어대는 걸 보고 있자니 나도 출출해졌단 말이오."], ["Let's go and try the old governor's champagne.\"", "가서 영감님네 샴페인이나 좀 맛봅시다.\""], ["Becky thought the Major had had a great deal too much already.", "베키가 보기에 소령은 이미 너무 많이 마신 터였다."]], [["The day after she went to walk on the Pincian Hill--the Hyde Park of the Roman idlers--possibly in hopes to have another sight of Lord Steyne.", "이튿날 그녀는 핀치오 언덕 — 로마의 한량들에게는 하이드 파크 격인 곳 — 으로 산책을 나갔는데, 어쩌면 스타인 경을 다시 한번 보게 되지 않을까 하는 기대에서였으리라."], ["But she met another acquaintance there: it was Mr.", "그러나 그녀가 거기서 마주친 것은 다른 지인이었다."], ["Fiche, his lordship's confidential man, who came up nodding to her rather familiarly and putting a finger to his hat.", "다름 아닌 그 경의 심복인 피시 씨로, 그는 제법 허물없이 그녀에게 고갯짓을 하고 모자에 손가락을 갖다 대며 다가왔다."], ["\"I knew that Madame was here,\"", "\"마담께서 여기 계신 줄 알고 있었습니다.\""], ["he said; \"I followed her from her hotel.", "그가 말했다. \"호텔에서부터 뒤따라왔지요."], ["I have some advice to give Madame.\"", "마담께 드릴 충고가 있습니다.\""]], [["\"From the Marquis of Steyne?\"", "\"스타인 후작에게서 온 건가요?\""], ["Becky asked, resuming as much of her dignity as she could muster, and not a little agitated by hope and expectation.", "베키는 짜낼 수 있는 만큼 위엄을 다시 갖추며 물었으나, 희망과 기대에 적잖이 마음이 흔들리고 있었다."]], [["\"No,\"", "\"아닙니다.\""], ["said the valet; \"it is from me.", "그 시종이 말했다. \"제가 드리는 겁니다."], ["Rome is very unwholesome.\"", "로마는 무척 몸에 해롭지요.\""]], [["\"Not at this season, Monsieur Fiche--not till after Easter.\"", "\"이 철에는 아니잖아요, 므슈 피시 — 부활절이 지나기 전에는 아니지요.\""]], [["\"I tell Madame it is unwholesome now.", "\"마담께 말씀드리건대, 지금이 해롭습니다."], ["There is always malaria for some people.", "어떤 사람들에게는 늘 말라리아가 도사리고 있지요."], ["That cursed marsh wind kills many at all seasons.", "그 저주받은 늪지 바람이 철을 가리지 않고 많은 이를 죽음으로 몰아갑니다."], ["Look, Madame Crawley, you were always bon enfant, and I have an interest in you, parole d'honneur.", "보십시오, 크롤리 부인, 부인께서는 늘 마음씨 고운 분이셨고, 저는 부인께 마음을 쓰고 있습니다, 명예를 걸고 말씀드립니다. 부디 경고를 새겨들으십시오."], ["Be warned. Go away from Rome, I tell you--or you will be ill and die.\"", "로마를 떠나십시오, 분명히 말씀드립니다 — 그러지 않으면 부인께서는 병들어 죽게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["Becky laughed, though in rage and fury.", "베키는 분노와 격분에 휩싸이면서도 웃었다."], ["\"What!", "\"어머나!"], ["assassinate poor little me?\"", "이 가엾고 작은 나를 암살이라도 한다는 건가요?\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"How romantic!", "\"어쩌면 그리 낭만적인지!"], ["Does my lord carry bravos for couriers, and stilettos in the fourgons?", "우리 나리께서는 자객을 심부름꾼으로 데리고 다니시고, 짐마차에 단검이라도 싣고 다니신답니까?"], ["Bah!", "흥!"], ["I will stay, if but to plague him.", "차라리 그분을 괴롭히기 위해서라도 머물러 있겠어요."], ["I have those who will defend me whilst I am here.\"", "여기 있는 동안 나를 지켜 줄 사람들이 있으니까요.\""]], [["It was Monsieur Fiche's turn to laugh now.", "이번에는 므슈 피시가 웃을 차례였다. \"부인을 지켜 준다고요.\""], ["\"Defend you,\"", "그가 말했다."], ["he said, \"and who?", "\"대체 누가 말입니까?"], ["The Major, the Captain, any one of those gambling men whom Madame sees would take her life for a hundred louis.", "그 소령이요, 그 대위요, 마담께서 보시는 그 도박꾼들 가운데 누구든, 백 루이만 주면 부인의 목숨을 거둬 갈 작자들입니다."], ["We know things about Major Loder (he is no more a Major than I am my Lord the Marquis) which would send him to the galleys or worse.", "우리는 로더 소령에 관해 이런저런 일들을 알고 있는데(그자가 소령인 것은 제가 후작 나리인 것이나 매한가지지요), 그걸 알면 그자를 갤리선이나 그보다 더한 곳으로 보낼 수 있을 겁니다."], ["We know everything and have friends everywhere.", "우리는 모든 것을 알고 있고, 어디에나 친구가 있습니다."], ["We know whom you saw at Paris, and what relations you found there.", "우리는 부인께서 파리에서 누구를 만나셨는지, 거기서 어떤 친척을 찾아내셨는지도 알고 있지요."], ["Yes, Madame may stare, but we do.", "그래요, 마담께서 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨셔도, 우리는 알고 있습니다."], ["How was it that no minister on the Continent would receive Madame?", "대륙의 어느 장관도 마담을 받아들이려 하지 않았던 게 어찌 된 영문이겠습니까?"], ["She has offended somebody: who never forgives--whose rage redoubled when he saw you.", "부인께서는 누군가의 비위를 거스르신 겁니다. 결코 용서하는 법이 없는 분, 부인을 보고 그 분노가 곱절이 된 분의 비위를 말입니다."], ["He was like a madman last night when he came home.", "어젯밤 댁으로 돌아오셨을 때 그분은 미친 사람 같았습니다."], ["Madame de Belladonna made him a scene about you and fired off in one of her furies.\"", "벨라도나 부인이 부인 일로 한바탕 소동을 벌이며 발작을 일으키셨거든요.\""]], [["\"Oh, it was Madame de Belladonna, was it?\"", "\"오, 그게 벨라도나 부인이었군요?\""], ["Becky said, relieved a little, for the information she had just got had scared her.", "베키는 조금 마음을 놓으며 말했다. 방금 들은 정보가 그녀를 겁먹게 했던 것이다."]], [["\"No--she does not matter--she is always jealous.", "\"아니지요 — 그 부인은 중요하지 않습니다 — 그 부인이야 늘 질투를 하니까요. 제가 말씀드리는 건 후작 나리입니다."], ["I tell you it was Monseigneur.", "부인께서 그분 앞에 모습을 드러내신 건 잘못이었어요."], ["You did wrong to show yourself to him.", "그러니 여기 머무신다면 후회하시게 될 겁니다. 제 말을 새겨들으십시오."], ["And if you stay here you will repent it.", "떠나십시오. 저기 나리의 마차가 옵니다.\""], ["Mark my words. Go.", "그러고는 베키의 팔을 붙잡고 정원의 한 샛길로 황급히 끌고 들어갔다."], ["Here is my lord's carriage\"--and seizing Becky's arm, he rushed down an alley of the garden as Lord Steyne's barouche, blazing with heraldic devices, came whirling along the avenue, borne by the almost priceless horses, and bearing Madame de Belladonna lolling on the cushions, dark, sulky, and blooming, a King Charles in her lap, a white parasol swaying over her head, and old Steyne stretched at her side with a livid face and ghastly eyes.", "마침 그때 문장 장식이 번쩍이는 스타인 경의 사륜마차가 거의 값을 매길 수 없는 명마들에 이끌려 가로수 길을 따라 휘몰아치듯 달려왔다. 그 안에는 벨라도나 부인이 방석에 비스듬히 기대어 있었으니, 검은 머리에 시무룩한 얼굴이었으나 화사하게 피어 있었고, 무릎에는 킹 찰스 종 강아지를 앉히고 머리 위로는 흰 양산을 살랑이고 있었다."], ["Hate, or anger, or desire caused them to brighten now and then still, but ordinarily, they gave no light, and seemed tired of looking out on a world of which almost all the pleasure and all the best beauty had palled upon the worn-out wicked old man.", "그 곁에는 늙은 스타인이 납빛 얼굴에 섬뜩한 눈을 하고 길게 뻗어 누워 있었다. 증오나 분노나 욕정이 이따금 아직도 그 눈을 번득이게 했으나, 평소에는 아무런 빛도 없이, 거의 모든 쾌락과 더없이 빼어난 아름다움마저 진력나 버린, 이 지치고 사악한 늙은이의 눈으로 바라보는 세상에 그저 싫증이 난 듯했다."]], [["\"Monseigneur has never recovered the shock of that night, never,\"", "\"후작 나리께서는 그날 밤의 충격에서 끝내 회복하지 못하셨습니다, 끝끝내요.\""], ["Monsieur Fiche whispered to Mrs. Crawley as the carriage flashed by, and she peeped out at it from behind the shrubs that hid her.", "마차가 휙 지나가는 동안 므슈 피시가 크롤리 부인에게 속삭였고, 그녀는 자기를 가려 주는 관목 뒤에서 그것을 빼꼼히 내다보았다."], ["\"That was a consolation at any rate,\"", "\"어쨌든 그것만은 위안이 되는걸.\""], ["Becky thought.", "베키는 생각했다."]], [["Whether my lord really had murderous intentions towards Mrs. Becky as Monsieur Fiche said (since Monseigneur's death he has returned to his native country, where he lives much respected, and has purchased from his Prince the title of Baron Ficci), and the factotum objected to have to do with assassination; or whether he simply had a commission to frighten Mrs.", "므슈 피시의 말대로 그 경이 정말로 베키 부인에게 살의를 품고 있었던 것인지(후작 나리가 세상을 떠난 뒤 그는 제 고국으로 돌아가 거기서 무척 존경받으며 살고 있으며, 제 군주로부터 피치 남작이라는 작위를 사들였다), 그래서 그 집사가 암살에 가담하기를 꺼린 것이었는지, 아니면 그저 그 경이 겨울을 나려고 정한 도시에서 — 그녀를 보는 것이 그 위대한 귀족에게는 더없이 불쾌한 일일 터이므로 — 크롤리 부인을 겁주어 쫓아내라는 임무를 받았을 뿐이었는지, 그것은 끝내 밝혀지지 않은 문제다."], ["Crawley out of a city where his Lordship proposed to pass the winter, and the sight of her would be eminently disagreeable to the great nobleman, is a point which has never been ascertained: but the threat had its effect upon the little woman, and she sought no more to intrude herself upon the presence of her old patron.", "그러나 그 협박은 그 작은 여인에게 효력을 발휘했고, 그녀는 두 번 다시 옛 후원자 앞에 끼어들려 하지 않았다."]], [["Everybody knows the melancholy end of that nobleman, which befell at Naples two months after the French Revolution of 1830; when the Most Honourable George Gustavus, Marquis of Steyne, Earl of Gaunt and of Gaunt Castle, in the Peerage of Ireland, Viscount Hellborough, Baron Pitchley and Grillsby, a Knight of the Most Noble Order of the Garter, of the Golden Fleece of Spain, of the Russian Order of Saint Nicholas of the First Class, of the Turkish Order of the Crescent, First Lord of the Powder Closet and Groom of the Back Stairs, Colonel of the Gaunt or Regent's Own Regiment of Militia, a Trustee of the British Museum, an Elder Brother of the Trinity House, a Governor of the White Friars, and D.C.L.--died after a series of fits brought on, as the papers said, by the shock occasioned to his lordship's sensibilities by the downfall of the ancient French monarchy.", "그 귀족의 서글픈 최후는 누구나 다 알고 있으니, 그것은 1830년의 프랑스 혁명이 일어난 지 두 달 뒤 나폴리에서 닥쳐왔다. 그때 더없이 존귀하신 조지 거스타버스, 스타인 후작이요, 아일랜드 귀족 명부에 오른 곤트 백작이자 곤트 캐슬 백작이요, 헬버러 자작이요, 피칠리 및 그릴스비 남작이요, 더없이 고귀한 가터 기사단과 에스파냐의 황금 양털 기사단과 러시아의 일급 성 니콜라우스 기사단과 터키의 초승달 기사단의 기사요, 화장실 시종장이자 뒤층계 시종이요, 곤트 또는 섭정 친위 민병 연대의 대령이요, 대영박물관 평의원이요, 트리니티 하우스의 원로 형제요, 화이트 프라이어스의 이사요, 민법학 박사이던 그분이 — 일련의 발작 끝에 세상을 떠난 것인데, 신문들이 전하기로는 유서 깊은 프랑스 왕정의 몰락이 그 경의 예민한 감수성에 입힌 충격이 그 원인이었다고 한다."]], [["An eloquent catalogue appeared in a weekly print, describing his virtues, his magnificence, his talents, and his good actions.", "어느 주간지에 그의 미덕과 호화로움과 재능과 선행을 기리는 유려한 송덕문이 실렸다."], ["His sensibility, his attachment to the illustrious House of Bourbon, with which he claimed an alliance, were such that he could not survive the misfortunes of his august kinsmen.", "그의 감수성과, 그가 인척 관계라 주장하던 저 쟁쟁한 부르봉 가문에 대한 애착은 어찌나 깊었던지, 그는 자기의 존귀한 친족들이 겪은 불행을 견디고 살아남을 수가 없었다는 것이다."], ["His body was buried at Naples, and his heart--that heart which always beat with every generous and noble emotion was brought back to Castle Gaunt in a silver urn.", "그의 시신은 나폴리에 묻혔고, 그의 심장은 — 언제나 온갖 고결하고 고귀한 감정으로 고동치던 그 심장은 — 은빛 항아리에 담겨 곤트 캐슬로 옮겨졌다."], ["\"In him,\" Mr.", "와그 씨는 이렇게 말했다."], ["Wagg said, \"the poor and the Fine Arts have lost a beneficent patron, society one of its most brilliant ornaments, and England one of her loftiest patriots and statesmen,\"", "\"그분의 죽음으로 가난한 이들과 예술은 인자한 후원자를 잃었고, 사교계는 가장 눈부신 장식 가운데 하나를 잃었으며, 영국은 가장 드높은 애국자이자 정치가 가운데 한 사람을 잃었다\""], ["&c., &c.", "운운."]], [["His will was a good deal disputed, and an attempt was made to force from Madame de Belladonna the celebrated jewel called the \"Jew's-eye\" diamond, which his lordship always wore on his forefinger, and which it was said that she removed from it after his lamented demise.", "그의 유언장을 두고 적잖은 다툼이 일었고, 그 경이 늘 집게손가락에 끼고 다니던 \"유대인의 눈\"이라 불리는 그 유명한 다이아몬드를 벨라도나 부인에게서 빼앗아 내려는 시도가 있었으니, 듣자 하니 그녀가 그 애통한 죽음 직후에 그 반지에서 그것을 빼내었다는 것이었다."], ["But his confidential friend and attendant, Monsieur Fiche proved that the ring had been presented to the said Madame de Belladonna two days before the Marquis's death, as were the bank-notes, jewels, Neapolitan and French bonds, &c., found in his lordship's secretaire and claimed by his heirs from that injured woman.", "그러나 그의 심복이자 시종이던 므슈 피시가 입증하기를, 그 반지는 후작이 세상을 떠나기 이틀 전에 문제의 벨라도나 부인에게 선물로 주어진 것이며, 그 경의 비밀 책상에서 발견되어 그 상속자들이 그 가엾은 여인에게서 내놓으라 요구한 지폐와 보석과 나폴리 및 프랑스 채권 따위도 마찬가지였다는 것이다."]], [["Full of Business and Pleasure\nThe day after the meeting at the play-table, Jos had himself arrayed with unusual care and splendour, and without thinking it necessary to say a word to any member of his family regarding the occurrences of the previous night, or asking for their company in his walk, he sallied forth at an early hour, and was presently seen making inquiries at the door of the Elephant Hotel. In consequence of the fetes the house was full of company, the tables in the street were already surrounded by persons smoking and drinking the national small-beer, the public rooms were in a cloud of smoke, and Mr. Jos having, in his pompous way, and with his clumsy German, made inquiries for the person of whom he was in search, was directed to the very top of the house, above the first-floor rooms where some travelling pedlars had lived, and were exhibiting their jewellery and brocades; above the second-floor apartments occupied by the etat major of the gambling firm; above the third-floor rooms, tenanted by the band of renowned Bohemian vaulters and tumblers; and so on to the little cabins of the roof, where, among students, bagmen, small tradesmen, and country-folks come in for the festival, Becky had found a little nest--as dirty a little refuge as ever beauty lay hid in.", "연극 도박판에서 만난 다음 날, 조스는 평소와 달리 유난히 공들여 화려하게 차려입었다. 그러고는 전날 밤에 일어난 일을 가족 누구에게도 굳이 한마디 비칠 필요를 느끼지 않은 채, 또 산책에 누구도 동행을 청하지 않고, 이른 시각에 길을 나섰다. 그리고 이내 코끼리 호텔 문 앞에서 무언가를 묻고 있는 모습이 눈에 띄었다. 축제 덕분에 호텔은 손님으로 가득 차 있었고, 거리에 놓인 탁자들은 벌써 담배를 피우며 그 나라 특유의 약한 맥주를 들이켜는 사람들로 둘러싸여 있었으며, 공용 객실들은 담배 연기로 자욱했다. 조스 씨는 특유의 거들먹거리는 태도와 어설픈 독일어로 찾는 사람에 대해 묻고는, 호텔 꼭대기 층으로 안내받았다. 어느 떠돌이 행상들이 묵으며 보석과 비단을 펼쳐 놓고 파는 이 층 객실을 지나, 도박 회사의 간부진이 차지한 삼 층 객실을 지나, 그 위로 이름난 보헤미아 곡예단과 재주꾼들이 묵는 사 층 객실을 지나, 그렇게 지붕 밑의 자그마한 골방까지 올라가는 것이었다. 그곳에는 학생들과 행상꾼들과 소상인들과 축제를 보러 시골에서 올라온 사람들 틈에, 베키가 작은 둥지를 틀고 있었다. 미인이 몸을 숨기기에는 더없이 더럽고 누추한 은신처였다."]], [["Becky liked the life.", "베키는 그런 생활이 마음에 들었다."], ["She was at home with everybody in the place, pedlars, punters, tumblers, students and all.", "그녀는 행상이든, 도박꾼이든, 재주꾼이든, 학생이든, 그곳의 누구와도 격의 없이 어울렸다. 그녀는 취향으로 보나 처지로 보나 둘 다 보헤미안이었던 아버지와 어머니에게서 물려받은, 거칠고 떠돌아다니기 좋아하는 천성을 지니고 있었다."], ["She was of a wild, roving nature, inherited from father and mother, who were both Bohemians, by taste and circumstance; if a lord was not by, she would talk to his courier with the greatest pleasure; the din, the stir, the drink, the smoke, the tattle of the Hebrew pedlars, the solemn, braggart ways of the poor tumblers, the sournois talk of the gambling-table officials, the songs and swagger of the students, and the general buzz and hum of the place had pleased and tickled the little woman, even when her luck was down and she had not wherewithal to pay her bill.", "곁에 귀족이 없으면 그 귀족의 시종과도 더없이 즐겁게 수다를 떨었던 것이다. 떠들썩한 소음, 부산스러움, 술, 담배 연기, 유대인 행상들의 잡담, 가난한 재주꾼들의 점잔 빼는 허풍, 도박판 직원들의 음흉한 말투, 학생들의 노래와 으스대는 꼴, 그리고 그곳 전체에서 웅웅거리며 들끓는 소란이, 운이 따르지 않아 셈도 치를 수 없을 때조차 이 자그마한 여인을 즐겁게 하고 간질였다."], ["How pleasant was all the bustle to her now that her purse was full of the money which little Georgy had won for her the night before!", "그러니 전날 밤 어린 조지가 따 준 돈으로 지갑이 두둑해진 지금, 그 모든 부산스러움이 그녀에게 얼마나 유쾌했겠는가!"]], [["As Jos came creaking and puffing up the final stairs, and was speechless when he got to the landing, and began to wipe his face and then to look for No. 92, the room where he was directed to seek for the person he wanted, the door of the opposite chamber, No.", "조스가 마지막 계단을 끽끽거리며 헐떡헐떡 올라와, 층계참에 다다라서는 말도 못 한 채 얼굴을 닦기 시작했고, 이윽고 찾는 사람을 찾으라 일러 받은 방인 92호를 두리번거리고 있을 때, 맞은편 방인 90호의 문이 열려 있었다."], ["90, was open, and a student, in jack-boots and a dirty schlafrock, was lying on the bed smoking a long pipe; whilst another student in long yellow hair and a braided coat, exceeding smart and dirty too, was actually on his knees at No. 92, bawling through the keyhole supplications to the person within.", "그 안에서는 승마 장화에 더러운 실내복 차림의 한 학생이 침대에 누워 긴 담뱃대를 빨고 있었고, 또 다른 학생은 긴 노란 머리에 장식 끈이 달린 외투를 걸치고서—역시나 몹시 멋을 부리고 또 몹시 더러웠는데—실제로 92호 앞에 무릎을 꿇은 채 열쇠 구멍에 대고 안에 있는 사람에게 애원을 늘어놓고 있었다."]], [["\"Go away,\"", "\"저리 가요.\""], ["said a well-known voice, which made Jos thrill, \"I expect somebody; I expect my grandpapa.", "어디선가 들어 본 목소리가 들려 조스를 전율케 했다. \"사람을 기다리고 있어요."], ["He mustn't see you there.\"", "우리 할아버지를 기다린다고요. 그분이 거기서 당신을 보면 안 된단 말이에요.\""]], [["\"Angel Englanderinn!\"", "\"천사 같은 영국 아가씨여!\""], ["bellowed the kneeling student with the whity-brown ringlets and the large finger-ring, \"do take compassion upon us.", "희끄무레한 갈색 곱슬머리에 큼지막한 반지를 낀, 무릎 꿇은 학생이 고래고래 외쳤다. \"부디 우리를 가엾이 여겨 주시오."], ["Make an appointment.", "약속 하나만 잡읍시다."], ["Dine with me and Fritz at the inn in the park.", "나랑 프리츠랑 공원 안 여관에서 함께 식사합시다."], ["We will have roast pheasants and porter, plum-pudding and French wine.", "꿩 구이에 흑맥주, 자두 푸딩에 프랑스 와인을 차립시다."], ["We shall die if you don't.\"", "안 와 주면 우린 죽고 말 거요.\""]], [["\"That we will,\"", "\"정말 죽고 말 거요.\""], ["said the young nobleman on the bed; and this colloquy Jos overheard, though he did not comprehend it, for the reason that he had never studied the language in which it was carried on.", "침대에 누운 젊은 귀족이 거들었다. 조스는 이 주고받는 말을 다 엿들었지만, 그 말이 오가는 언어를 한 번도 배운 적이 없었던 까닭에 무슨 뜻인지 알아듣지 못했다."]], [["\"Newmero kattervang dooze, si vous plait,\"", "\"누메로 카테르방 두즈, 실 부 플레.\""], ["Jos said in his grandest manner, when he was able to speak.", "조스가 비로소 말이 나오자 더없이 거창한 투로 말했다."]], [["\"Quater fang tooce!\"", "\"콰테르 팡 투스!\""], ["said the student, starting up, and he bounced into his own room, where he locked the door, and where Jos heard him laughing with his comrade on the bed.", "학생이 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다. 그러고는 제 방으로 후다닥 뛰어 들어가 문을 잠갔고, 조스는 그가 침대에 누운 동무와 함께 깔깔대는 소리를 들었다."]], [["The gentleman from Bengal was standing, disconcerted by this incident, when the door of the 92 opened of itself and Becky's little head peeped out full of archness and mischief.", "벵골에서 온 신사가 이 일에 당황하여 우두커니 서 있는데, 92호의 문이 저절로 열리더니 베키의 자그마한 머리가, 짓궂고 장난기 가득한 표정으로 빠끔히 내다보았다."], ["She lighted on Jos.", "그녀의 눈이 조스에게 닿았다."], ["\"It's you,\"", "\"당신이군요.\""], ["she said, coming out.", "그녀가 나오며 말했다."], ["\"How I have been waiting for you!", "\"내가 얼마나 기다렸는지 몰라요!"], ["Stop!", "잠깐만요!"], ["not yet--in one minute you shall come in.\"", "아직요—잠시만 있으면 들어오셔도 돼요.\""], ["In that instant she put a rouge-pot, a brandy bottle, and a plate of broken meat into the bed, gave one smooth to her hair, and finally let in her visitor.", "그 순간 그녀는 연지통 하나와 브랜디 병 하나, 그리고 먹다 남은 고기 한 접시를 침대 속으로 밀어 넣고, 머리를 한 번 매만진 다음, 마침내 손님을 안으로 들였다."]], [["She had, by way of morning robe, a pink domino, a trifle faded and soiled, and marked here and there with pomaturn; but her arms shone out from the loose sleeves of the dress very white and fair, and it was tied round her little waist so as not ill to set off the trim little figure of the wearer.", "그녀는 실내복 삼아 분홍색 도미노 가운을 걸치고 있었는데, 조금 빛이 바래고 더러웠으며 군데군데 포마드 자국이 묻어 있었다. 그러나 그 헐렁한 소맷자락 밖으로 드러난 두 팔은 더없이 희고 고왔고, 가운은 그 자그마한 허리에 묶여 있어 입은 이의 맵시 있는 작은 몸매를 그런대로 잘 살려 주고 있었다."], ["She led Jos by the hand into her garret.", "그녀는 조스의 손을 잡고 그를 자기 다락방으로 이끌었다."], ["\"Come in,\"", "\"들어오세요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"Come and talk to me.", "\"들어와서 저랑 이야기해요."], ["Sit yonder on the chair\"; and she gave the civilian's hand a little squeeze and laughingly placed him upon it.", "저쪽 의자에 앉으세요.\" 그러고는 그 문관 양반의 손을 살짝 꼭 쥐고 웃으면서 그를 의자에 앉혔다."], ["As for herself, she placed herself on the bed--not on the bottle and plate, you may be sure--on which Jos might have reposed, had he chosen that seat; and so there she sat and talked with her old admirer.", "자기는 침대에 자리를 잡았는데—물론 병과 접시 위는 아니었다—조스가 그 자리를 택했더라면 그가 거기 기대앉을 수도 있었던 것이다. 그렇게 그녀는 거기 앉아 옛 흠모자와 이야기를 나누었다."], ["\"How little years have changed you,\"", "\"세월이 당신을 어쩜 이렇게 조금밖에 바꾸지 못했을까요.\""], ["she said with a look of tender interest.", "그녀가 다정하고 살뜰한 눈빛으로 말했다."], ["\"I should have known you anywhere.", "\"어디서 봤어도 당신을 알아봤을 거예요."], ["What a comfort it is amongst strangers to see once more the frank honest face of an old friend!\"", "낯선 사람들 틈에서 오랜 친구의 꾸밈없고 정직한 얼굴을 다시 보는 게 얼마나 큰 위안인지 몰라요!\""]], [["The frank honest face, to tell the truth, at this moment bore any expression but one of openness and honesty: it was, on the contrary, much perturbed and puzzled in look.", "그 꾸밈없고 정직한 얼굴은, 사실대로 말하자면 이 순간 솔직함이나 정직함과는 거리가 먼 온갖 표정을 띠고 있었다. 도리어 몹시 당혹스럽고 어리둥절한 기색이었다."], ["Jos was surveying the queer little apartment in which he found his old flame.", "조스는 옛 연인이 머무는 이 기묘한 자그마한 방을 둘러보고 있었던 것이다."], ["One of her gowns hung over the bed, another depending from a hook of the door; her bonnet obscured half the looking-glass, on which, too, lay the prettiest little pair of bronze boots; a French novel was on the table by the bedside, with a candle, not of wax.", "그녀의 가운 하나가 침대 위에 걸쳐져 있었고, 또 하나는 문에 박힌 고리에 매달려 있었다. 보닛은 거울의 절반을 가리고 있었고, 그 거울 위에는 더없이 예쁘장한 청동빛 부츠 한 켤레가 놓여 있었다."], ["Becky thought of popping that into the bed too, but she only put in the little paper night-cap with which she had put the candle out on going to sleep.", "침대 머리맡 탁자에는 프랑스 소설 한 권과 양초가—밀랍은 아닌 양초가—놓여 있었다. 베키는 그것마저 침대 속에 처박을까 생각했지만, 잠자리에 들 때 촛불을 끄는 데 쓰던 자그마한 종이 촛불 끄개만 안에 넣어 두었다."]], [["\"I should have known you anywhere,\"", "\"어디서 봤어도 당신을 알아봤을 거예요.\""], ["she continued; \"a woman never forgets some things.", "그녀가 말을 이었다. \"여자는 어떤 것들은 결코 잊지 못하는 법이에요."], ["And you were the first man I ever--I ever saw.\"", "그리고 당신은 내가 난생처음—난생처음 본 남자였답니다.\""]], [["\"Was I really?\"", "\"내가 정말로요?\""], ["said Jos.", "조스가 말했다."], ["\"God bless my soul, you--you don't say so.\"", "\"이런 세상에, 당신이—당신이 설마 그럴 리가요.\""]], [["\"When I came with your sister from Chiswick, I was scarcely more than a child,\"", "\"당신 누이동생과 함께 치즈윅에서 나왔을 때, 나는 거의 어린애나 다름없었어요.\""], ["Becky said.", "베키가 말했다."], ["\"How is that, dear love?", "\"그 사랑스러운 분은 어떻게 지내요?"], ["Oh, her husband was a sad wicked man, and of course it was of me that the poor dear was jealous.", "아, 그분의 남편은 슬프게도 못된 사람이었어요. 그러니 그 가엾은 분이 질투한 게 다 나 때문이었지요."], ["As if I cared about him, heigho!", "마치 내가 그이한테 마음이라도 있었던 것처럼요, 휴!"], ["when there was somebody--but no--don't let us talk of old times\"; and she passed her handkerchief with the tattered lace across her eyelids.", "하지만 그때 다른 누군가가—아니에요—옛날 얘긴 그만둬요.\" 그러고는 너덜너덜한 레이스가 달린 손수건으로 두 눈가를 훔쳤다."]], [["\"Is not this a strange place,\"", "\"여기는 참 이상한 곳 아닌가요.\""], ["she continued, \"for a woman, who has lived in a very different world too, to be found in?", "그녀가 말을 이었다. \"그것도 전혀 다른 세계에서 살아온 여자가 머물기에는 말이에요."], ["I have had so many griefs and wrongs, Joseph Sedley; I have been made to suffer so cruelly that I am almost made mad sometimes.", "나는 너무도 많은 슬픔과 부당한 일을 겪었어요, 조지프 세들리. 너무도 모질게 고통받아서 때로는 거의 미칠 지경이랍니다."], ["I can't stay still in any place, but wander about always restless and unhappy.", "어디 한 곳에 가만히 머물지 못하고, 늘 불안하고 불행한 채로 떠돌기만 해요. 친구들이 다 나를 배신했어요—전부요."], ["All my friends have been false to me--all.", "이 세상에 정직한 남자란 없어요. 나는 세상 누구보다 진실한 아내였어요."], ["There is no such thing as an honest man in the world.", "비록 다른 누군가 때문에 홧김에 남편과 결혼했지만—그 얘긴 그만두죠. 나는 진실했는데, 그이는 나를 짓밟고 버렸어요."], ["I was the truest wife that ever lived, though I married my husband out of pique, because somebody else--but never mind that.", "나는 더없이 다정한 어머니였어요. 내게는 단 하나의 아이, 단 하나의 귀염둥이, 단 하나의 희망, 단 하나의 기쁨이 있었어요."], ["I was true, and he trampled upon me and deserted me. I was the fondest mother.", "어미의 사랑으로 내 가슴에 품었던, 내 목숨이요, 내 기도요, 내—내 축복이었던 아이를요."], ["I had but one child, one darling, one hope, one joy, which I held to my heart with a mother's affection, which was my life, my prayer, my--my blessing; and they--they tore it from me--tore it from me\"; and she put her hand to her heart with a passionate gesture of despair, burying her face for a moment on the bed.", "그런데 그자들이—그자들이 내게서 그 아이를 빼앗아 갔어요—내게서 빼앗아 갔다고요.\" 그러고는 절망에 찬 격정적인 몸짓으로 가슴에 손을 얹으며, 잠시 침대에 얼굴을 파묻었다."]], [["The brandy-bottle inside clinked up against the plate which held the cold sausage.", "침대 속의 브랜디 병이 식은 소시지가 담긴 접시에 부딪쳐 쨍그랑 소리를 냈다."], ["Both were moved, no doubt, by the exhibition of so much grief.", "두 물건 모두 그토록 큰 슬픔의 토로에 마음이 움직였던 것이 분명하다."], ["Max and Fritz were at the door, listening with wonder to Mrs. Becky's sobs and cries.", "막스와 프리츠는 문가에서 베키 부인의 흐느낌과 울부짖음에 놀라며 귀를 기울이고 있었다."], ["Jos, too, was a good deal frightened and affected at seeing his old flame in this condition.", "조스 역시 옛 연인이 이런 처지에 놓인 것을 보고 적잖이 겁이 나고 마음이 흔들렸다."], ["And she began, forthwith, to tell her story--a tale so neat, simple, and artless that it was quite evident from hearing her that if ever there was a white-robed angel escaped from heaven to be subject to the infernal machinations and villainy of fiends here below, that spotless being--that miserable unsullied martyr, was present on the bed before Jos--on the bed, sitting on the brandy-bottle.", "그러자 그녀는 곧장 자기 사연을 풀어놓기 시작했다—어찌나 깔끔하고 단순하고 꾸밈없는 이야기였던지, 그것을 듣노라면 이 지상에서 마귀들의 흉악한 음모와 악행에 시달리도록 천국에서 도망쳐 나온 흰옷 입은 천사가 정말로 있었다면, 그 티 없는 존재가—그 비참하고 더럽혀지지 않은 순교자가, 지금 조스 앞 침대 위에—브랜디 병 위에 앉아 있다는 것이 더없이 분명해 보일 지경이었다."]], [["They had a very long, amicable, and confidential talk there, in the course of which Jos Sedley was somehow made aware (but in a manner that did not in the least scare or offend him) that Becky's heart had first learned to beat at his enchanting presence; that George Osborne had certainly paid an unjustifiable court to HER, which might account for Amelia's jealousy and their little rupture; but that Becky never gave the least encouragement to the unfortunate officer, and that she had never ceased to think about Jos from the very first day she had seen him, though, of course, her duties as a married woman were paramount--duties which she had always preserved, and would, to her dying day, or until the proverbially bad climate in which Colonel Crawley was living should release her from a yoke which his cruelty had rendered odious to her.", "거기서 두 사람은 더없이 길고도 화기애애하고 은밀한 이야기를 나누었다. 그러는 사이에 조스 세들리는 (그러나 조금도 그를 겁먹게 하거나 불쾌하게 하지 않는 방식으로) 어쩐지 이런 사실들을 알게 되었다. 베키의 심장이 두근거리기를 처음 배운 것은 매혹적인 조스의 존재 앞에서였다는 것, 조지 오즈번이 분명 그녀에게 부당한 구애를 했고 그것으로 애밀리아의 질투와 두 사람 사이의 작은 불화가 설명될 법하다는 것, 그러나 베키는 그 불운한 장교에게 추호도 빌미를 준 적이 없으며, 처음 그를 본 바로 그날부터 단 한 번도 조스를 생각하지 않은 적이 없었다는 것을 말이다. 물론 결혼한 여자로서의 의무가 무엇보다 우선이었지만—그 의무를 그녀는 늘 지켜 왔고, 죽는 날까지, 아니면 크롤리 대령이 살고 있는 저 속담으로도 악명 높은 험한 기후가 그의 잔인함으로 진저리 나게 된 그 멍에에서 그녀를 풀어 줄 때까지 지켜 갈 작정이라는 것이었다."]], [["Jos went away, convinced that she was the most virtuous, as she was one of the most fascinating of women, and revolving in his mind all sorts of benevolent schemes for her welfare.", "조스는 그녀가 더없이 매혹적인 여인들 가운데 하나일 뿐 아니라 더없이 정숙한 여인이라고 확신하며 자리를 떴다. 그러면서 그녀의 안녕을 위한 온갖 자비로운 계획을 마음속으로 굴렸다."], ["Her persecutions ought to be ended: she ought to return to the society of which she was an ornament.", "그녀에 대한 박해는 끝나야 마땅했다. 그녀는 자신이 그 장식과도 같았던 사교계로 돌아가야 마땅했다."], ["He would see what ought to be done.", "무엇을 해야 할지 알아보리라."], ["She must quit that place and take a quiet lodging.", "그녀는 저곳을 떠나 조용한 하숙으로 옮겨야 했다."], ["Amelia must come and see her and befriend her.", "애밀리아가 와서 그녀를 만나고 벗이 되어 주어야 했다."], ["He would go and settle about it, and consult with the Major.", "자기가 가서 그 일을 처리하고 소령과 의논하리라."], ["She wept tears of heart-felt gratitude as she parted from him, and pressed his hand as the gallant stout gentleman stooped down to kiss hers.", "그녀는 그와 헤어지면서 진심에서 우러난 감사의 눈물을 흘렸고, 그 늠름하고 살진 신사가 허리를 숙여 그녀의 손에 입 맞추려 하자 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었다."]], [["So Becky bowed Jos out of her little garret with as much grace as if it was a palace of which she did the honours; and that heavy gentleman having disappeared down the stairs, Max and Fritz came out of their hole, pipe in mouth, and she amused herself by mimicking Jos to them as she munched her cold bread and sausage and took draughts of her favourite brandy-and-water.", "그리하여 베키는 마치 자기가 안주인 노릇을 하는 궁전이라도 되는 양 우아하게 조스를 자기 다락방에서 배웅했다. 그 묵직한 신사가 계단 아래로 사라지자, 막스와 프리츠가 담뱃대를 입에 문 채 자기네 굴에서 나왔고, 그녀는 식은 빵과 소시지를 우물거리고 애용하는 브랜디 물을 들이켜면서 그들에게 조스 흉내를 내며 즐거워했다."]], [["Jos walked over to Dobbin's lodgings with great solemnity and there imparted to him the affecting history with which he had just been made acquainted, without, however, mentioning the play business of the night before.", "조스는 더없이 엄숙한 걸음으로 도빈의 하숙으로 걸어가, 방금 알게 된 가슴 아픈 사연을 그에게 전했다. 그러나 전날 밤의 도박 이야기만은 입에 올리지 않았다."], ["And the two gentlemen were laying their heads together and consulting as to the best means of being useful to Mrs.", "그리고 두 신사는 머리를 맞대고 베키 부인을 도울 가장 좋은 방도를 의논했다."], ["Becky, while she was finishing her interrupted dejeuner a la fourchette.", "그러는 동안 그녀는 중간에 끊겼던 포크 식사를 마저 끝내고 있었다."]], [["How was it that she had come to that little town?", "그녀는 어쩌다 그 작은 도시로 흘러들게 되었을까?"], ["How was it that she had no friends and was wandering about alone?", "어쩌다 친구 하나 없이 홀로 떠돌게 되었을까?"], ["Little boys at school are taught in their earliest Latin book that the path of Avernus is very easy of descent.", "학교에 다니는 사내아이들은 가장 처음 배우는 라틴어 교본에서 아베르누스로 내려가는 길은 무척 쉽다는 것을 배운다."], ["Let us skip over the interval in the history of her downward progress.", "그녀의 추락 과정에 얽힌 사연의 그 사이 대목은 건너뛰기로 하자."], ["She was not worse now than she had been in the days of her prosperity--only a little down on her luck.", "그녀는 지금 형편이 좋던 시절에 비해 더 나빠진 것은 아니었다—그저 운이 조금 따르지 않을 뿐이었다."]], [["As for Mrs.", "* 아베르누스: 고대 로마 신화에서 저승으로 통하는 입구로 여겨진 호수."], ["Amelia, she was a woman of such a soft and foolish disposition that when she heard of anybody unhappy, her heart straightway melted towards the sufferer; and as she had never thought or done anything mortally guilty herself, she had not that abhorrence for wickedness which distinguishes moralists much more knowing.", "\"쉽게 내려간다\"는 것은 베르길리우스의 시구에서 온 표현이다. 한편 애밀리아 부인으로 말하자면, 천성이 어찌나 무르고 어리석은지 누가 불행하다는 소리만 들으면 그 고통받는 이를 향해 곧장 마음이 녹아내리는 여인이었다."], ["If she spoiled everybody who came near her with kindness and compliments--if she begged pardon of all her servants for troubling them to answer the bell--if she apologized to a shopboy who showed her a piece of silk, or made a curtsey to a street-sweeper with a complimentary remark upon the elegant state of his crossing--and she was almost capable of every one of these follies--the notion that an old acquaintance was miserable was sure to soften her heart; nor would she hear of anybody's being deservedly unhappy.", "게다가 그녀는 스스로 죽을죄가 될 만한 일을 생각하거나 저지른 적이 한 번도 없었던 까닭에, 훨씬 더 사리에 밝은 도덕가들이 보이는 그런 악에 대한 혐오를 지니지 못했다. 그녀가 곁에 다가오는 모든 이를 친절과 칭찬으로 버릇없게 만들었다 한들, 종을 울려 대답하게 하느라 번거롭게 했다고 하인들 모두에게 사과를 했다 한들, 비단 한 필을 보여 준 점원 아이에게 미안하다고 했다 한들, 길 쓰는 청소부에게 무릎을 굽혀 인사하며 그가 쓸어 놓은 길목이 참 깔끔하다고 한마디 추어올렸다 한들—그리고 그녀는 이 온갖 어리석은 짓들을 거의 하나도 빠짐없이 저지를 만한 사람이었으니—옛 지인이 비참한 처지라는 생각이 그녀의 마음을 누그러뜨릴 것은 틀림없는 일이었다."], ["A world under such legislation as hers would not be a very orderly place of abode; but there are not many women, at least not of the rulers, who are of her sort.", "또한 그녀는 누가 마땅히 벌받아 불행하다는 말 따위는 들으려고도 하지 않았다. 그녀와 같은 식의 법으로 다스려지는 세상이라면 그리 질서 정연한 거처는 못 될 것이다."], ["This lady, I believe, would have abolished all gaols, punishments, handcuffs, whippings, poverty, sickness, hunger, in the world, and was such a mean-spirited creature that--we are obliged to confess it--she could even forget a mortal injury.", "그러나 그녀 같은 부류의 여인은, 적어도 다스리는 자리에 있는 여인 중에는 그리 많지 않다. 이 부인으로 말하면, 내 생각에는 세상의 모든 감옥과 형벌과 수갑과 채찍질과 가난과 질병과 굶주림을 다 없애 버렸을 사람이며, 어찌나 줏대 없는 사람인지—우리는 이 점을 인정하지 않을 수 없는데—치명적인 모욕조차 잊어버릴 수 있는 사람이었다."]], [["When the Major heard from Jos of the sentimental adventure which had just befallen the latter, he was not, it must be owned, nearly as much interested as the gentleman from Bengal.", "소령이 조스에게서 방금 그가 겪은 감상적인 모험담을 전해 들었을 때, 그는 솔직히 말해 벵골에서 온 신사만큼 흥미를 느끼지는 않았다. 도리어 그의 흥분은 결코 유쾌한 종류의 것이 아니었다."], ["On the contrary, his excitement was quite the reverse from a pleasurable one; he made use of a brief but improper expression regarding a poor woman in distress, saying, in fact, \"The little minx, has she come to light again?\"", "그는 고난에 처한 가엾은 여인을 두고 짧지만 점잖지 못한 표현을 입에 올렸는데, 사실인즉 이렇게 말한 것이다. \"그 앙큼한 계집이 또 모습을 드러냈단 말이오?\""], ["He never had had the slightest liking for her, but had heartily mistrusted her from the very first moment when her green eyes had looked at, and turned away from, his own.", "그는 그녀에게 추호도 호감을 가져 본 적이 없었고, 그녀의 초록빛 눈이 자기 눈을 한 번 바라보았다가 돌려 버린 바로 그 순간부터 진심으로 그녀를 미덥지 못하게 여겨 왔던 것이다."]], [["\"That little devil brings mischief wherever she goes,\"", "\"그 어린 악녀는 가는 곳마다 화근을 몰고 다니지.\""], ["the Major said disrespectfully.", "소령이 무례하게 말했다."], ["\"Who knows what sort of life she has been leading?", "\"그동안 어떤 식으로 살아왔는지 누가 알겠소?"], ["And what business has she here abroad and alone?", "게다가 그 여자가 무슨 볼일로 여기 타국에 그것도 홀로 와 있단 말이오?"], ["Don't tell me about persecutors and enemies; an honest woman always has friends and never is separated from her family.", "박해니 원수니 하는 소리는 내게 하지 마시오. 정직한 여자한테는 늘 친구가 있고, 결코 가족과 떨어지는 법이 없소."], ["Why has she left her husband?", "어째서 남편을 떠났단 말이오?"], ["He may have been disreputable and wicked, as you say.", "당신 말마따나 그자가 평판 나쁘고 못된 인간이었을 수는 있소."], ["He always was.", "늘 그랬으니까."], ["I remember the confounded blackleg and the way in which he used to cheat and hoodwink poor George.", "나는 그 망할 사기 도박꾼을, 그자가 가엾은 조지를 속이고 등쳐 먹던 수법을 똑똑히 기억하오."], ["Wasn't there a scandal about their separation?", "그 두 사람이 갈라설 때 무슨 추문이 있지 않았소?"], ["I think I heard something,\"", "뭔가 들은 것 같은데.\" 도빈 소령이 외쳤다."], ["cried out Major Dobbin, who did not care much about gossip, and whom Jos tried in vain to convince that Mrs. Becky was in all respects a most injured and virtuous female.", "그는 뒷공론에는 별 관심이 없는 사람이었고, 조스는 베키 부인이 모든 면에서 더없이 피해를 입은 정숙한 여인이라고 그를 납득시키려 애썼으나 헛수고였다."]], [["\"Well, well; let's ask Mrs.", "\"좋아요, 좋아. 조지 부인한테 물어봅시다.\""], ["George,\" said that arch-diplomatist of a Major.", "그 빈틈없는 외교관 같은 소령이 말했다."], ["\"Only let us go and consult her.", "\"그저 가서 그 부인과 의논해 봅시다."], ["I suppose you will allow that she is a good judge at any rate, and knows what is right in such matters.\"", "적어도 그 부인이 훌륭한 판단을 내릴 줄 알고, 이런 일에서 무엇이 옳은지 안다는 데에는 당신도 동의할 테지요.\""]], [["\"Hm!", "\"흠!"], ["Emmy is very well,\"", "에미는 아주 괜찮은 사람이지요.\""], ["said Jos, who did not happen to be in love with his sister.", "마침 누이동생에게 푹 빠져 있지는 않았던 조스가 말했다."]], [["\"Very well?", "\"아주 괜찮다고요?"], ["By Gad, sir, she's the finest lady I ever met in my life,\"", "맹세코, 선생, 그분은 내가 평생 만나 본 가장 훌륭한 숙녀요.\""], ["bounced out the Major.", "소령이 불쑥 외쳤다."], ["\"I say at once, let us go and ask her if this woman ought to be visited or not--I will be content with her verdict.\"", "\"당장 가서 이 여자를 만나야 할지 말아야 할지 그분께 여쭤 봅시다—나는 그분의 판결에 만족하겠소.\""], ["Now this odious, artful rogue of a Major was thinking in his own mind that he was sure of his case.", "그런데 이 얄미울 만치 교활한 능구렁이 같은 소령은 속으로 자기 쪽이 이긴 게 틀림없다고 생각하고 있었다."], ["Emmy, he remembered, was at one time cruelly and deservedly jealous of Rebecca, never mentioned her name but with a shrinking and terror--a jealous woman never forgives, thought Dobbin: and so the pair went across the street to Mrs.", "그가 기억하기로 에미는 한때 레베카를 모질게, 그러나 마땅한 까닭으로 질투했고, 그 이름을 입에 올릴 때면 늘 움츠러들고 진저리를 쳤던 것이다—질투하는 여자는 결코 용서하지 않는 법이지, 도빈은 그렇게 생각했다."], ["George's house, where she was contentedly warbling at a music lesson with Madame Strumpff.", "그리하여 두 사람은 길 건너 조지 부인의 집으로 갔는데, 거기서 그녀는 슈트룸프 부인과의 음악 수업 중에 흐뭇하게 노래를 흥얼거리고 있었다."]], [["When that lady took her leave, Jos opened the business with his usual pomp of words.", "그 부인이 자리를 뜨자, 조스는 평소의 거창한 말투로 본론을 꺼냈다."], ["\"Amelia, my dear,\"", "\"애밀리아, 얘야.\""], ["said he, \"I have just had the most extraordinary--yes--God bless my soul!", "그가 말했다. \"내가 방금 더없이 기이한—그래—이런 세상에!"], ["the most extraordinary adventure--an old friend--yes, a most interesting old friend of yours, and I may say in old times, has just arrived here, and I should like you to see her.\"", "더없이 기이한 일을 겪었단다—옛 친구를—그래, 너의 더없이 흥미로운 옛 친구를, 옛날로 치자면 말이다, 방금 여기서 만났는데, 네가 그 사람을 좀 만나 봤으면 한다.\""]], [["\"Her!\" said Amelia, \"who is it?", "\"그 사람이요!\""], ["Major Dobbin, if you please not to break my scissors.\"", "애밀리아가 말했다. \"누구예요?"], ["The Major was twirling them round by the little chain from which they sometimes hung to their lady's waist, and was thereby endangering his own eye.", "도빈 소령님, 제발 제 가위 좀 망가뜨리지 마세요.\" 소령은 가위가 이따금 그 주인의 허리께에 매달려 있곤 하는 작은 사슬을 잡고 빙빙 돌리고 있어서, 자기 눈을 위태롭게 하던 참이었다."]], [["\"It is a woman whom I dislike very much,\"", "\"내가 몹시 싫어하는 여자요.\""], ["said the Major, doggedly, \"and whom you have no cause to love.\"", "소령이 고집스럽게 말했다. \"그리고 당신도 사랑할 까닭이 없는 여자고요.\""]], [["\"It is Rebecca, I'm sure it is Rebecca,\"", "\"레베카예요, 분명 레베카예요.\""], ["Amelia said, blushing and being very much agitated.", "애밀리아가 얼굴을 붉히고 몹시 동요하며 말했다."]], [["\"You are right; you always are,\"", "\"맞소. 당신은 늘 맞는 말만 하지.\""], ["Dobbin answered.", "도빈이 대답했다."], ["Brussels, Waterloo, old, old times, griefs, pangs, remembrances, rushed back into Amelia's gentle heart and caused a cruel agitation there.", "브뤼셀, 워털루, 아득히 먼 옛 시절, 슬픔, 아픔, 추억이 한꺼번에 애밀리아의 여린 가슴속으로 밀려들어 거기에 모진 동요를 일으켰다."]], [["\"Don't let me see her,\"", "\"그 사람을 만나게 하지 마세요.\""], ["Emmy continued.", "에미가 말을 이었다."], ["\"I couldn't see her.\"", "\"난 그 사람을 만날 수 없어요.\""]], [["\"I told you so,\"", "\"내가 그럴 거라 하지 않았소.\""], ["Dobbin said to Jos.", "도빈이 조스에게 말했다."]], [["\"She is very unhappy, and--and that sort of thing,\"", "\"그 사람은 아주 불행해요, 그리고—뭐 그런 처지란다.\""], ["Jos urged.", "조스가 졸랐다."], ["\"She is very poor and unprotected, and has been ill--exceedingly ill--and that scoundrel of a husband has deserted her.\"", "\"아주 가난하고 의지할 데 없고, 게다가 아팠단다—몹시 심하게 아팠어—그 남편이라는 못된 자가 그 사람을 버렸지.\""]], [["\"Ah!\"", "\"아!\""], ["said Amelia.", "애밀리아가 말했다."]], [["\"She hasn't a friend in the world,\"", "\"세상에 친구 하나 없단다.\""], ["Jos went on, not undexterously, \"and she said she thought she might trust in you.", "조스가 제법 능란하게 말을 이어 갔다. \"그러면서 그 사람이 말하길, 너한테는 기댈 수 있을 것 같다더구나."], ["She's so miserable, Emmy.", "그 사람은 너무 비참해, 에미."], ["She has been almost mad with grief.", "슬픔에 거의 미칠 지경이었어."], ["Her story quite affected me--'pon my word and honour, it did--never was such a cruel persecution borne so angelically, I may say.", "그 사연을 들으니 나도 정말 마음이 움직이더구나—맹세코, 정말 그랬다—그토록 모진 박해를 그토록 천사같이 견뎌 낸 사람은 일찍이 없었다고 할 만하지."], ["Her family has been most cruel to her.\"", "그 가족이 그 사람한테 더없이 모질게 굴었더구나.\""]], [["\"Poor creature!\"", "\"가엾은 사람!\""], ["Amelia said.", "애밀리아가 말했다."]], [["\"And if she can get no friend, she says she thinks she'll die,\"", "\"그리고 친구를 못 구하면 죽을 것 같다더구나.\""], ["Jos proceeded in a low tremulous voice.", "조스가 낮고 떨리는 목소리로 말을 이었다."], ["\"God bless my soul!", "\"이런 세상에!"], ["do you know that she tried to kill herself?", "글쎄 그 사람이 스스로 목숨을 끊으려 했다지 뭐냐?"], ["She carries laudanum with her--I saw the bottle in her room--such a miserable little room--at a third-rate house, the Elephant, up in the roof at the top of all.", "아편 정기를 늘 지니고 다닌단다—그 병을 그 방에서 봤어—그 비참하고 자그마한 방에서—삼류 호텔인 코끼리 호텔, 그것도 지붕 밑 맨 꼭대기에서 말이다."], ["I went there.\"", "내가 거기까지 갔단다.\""]], [["This did not seem to affect Emmy.", "이 말은 에미에게 별 감흥을 주지 못하는 듯했다."], ["She even smiled a little.", "그녀는 심지어 살짝 미소를 짓기까지 했다."], ["Perhaps she figured Jos to herself panting up the stair.", "아마도 조스가 헐떡거리며 계단을 오르는 모습이 떠올랐던 모양이다."]], [["\"She's beside herself with grief,\"", "\"그 사람은 슬픔에 제정신이 아니야.\""], ["he resumed.", "그가 말을 이었다."], ["\"The agonies that woman has endured are quite frightful to hear of.", "\"그 여자가 견뎌 낸 고통이란 듣기만 해도 끔찍할 지경이지."], ["She had a little boy, of the same age as Georgy.\"", "그 사람한테 어린 사내아이가 하나 있었는데, 조지하고 동갑이었단다.\""]], [["\"Yes, yes, I think I remember,\"", "\"네, 네, 기억나는 것 같아요.\""], ["Emmy remarked.", "에미가 한마디 했다."], ["\"Well?\"", "\"그래서요?\""]], [["\"The most beautiful child ever seen,\"", "\"여태껏 본 가장 아름다운 아이였지.\""], ["Jos said, who was very fat, and easily moved, and had been touched by the story Becky told; \"a perfect angel, who adored his mother.", "워낙 살집이 많고 쉽게 마음이 동하는 데다 베키가 들려준 이야기에 가슴이 뭉클해졌던 조스가 말했다. \"완벽한 천사였어, 제 어미를 끔찍이 따르던 아이였지."], ["The ruffians tore him shrieking out of her arms, and have never allowed him to see her.\"", "그 악당들이 비명을 지르는 아이를 그 사람 품에서 떼어 냈고, 두 번 다시 그 사람을 못 보게 했다는구나.\""]], [["\"Dear Joseph,\"", "\"조지프 오라버니.\""], ["Emmy cried out, starting up at once, \"let us go and see her this minute.\"", "에미가 그 자리에서 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다. \"당장 그 사람을 만나러 가요.\""], ["And she ran into her adjoining bedchamber, tied on her bonnet in a flutter, came out with her shawl on her arm, and ordered Dobbin to follow.", "그러고는 옆에 딸린 침실로 달려 들어가, 허둥지둥 보닛을 묶고는, 숄을 팔에 걸친 채 나와서 도빈에게 따라오라고 일렀다."]], [["He went and put her shawl--it was a white cashmere, consigned to her by the Major himself from India--over her shoulders.", "그는 가서 그녀의 숄을—그것은 흰 캐시미어 숄로, 소령 본인이 인도에서 보내 준 것이었다—그녀의 어깨에 둘러 주었다."], ["He saw there was nothing for it but to obey, and she put her hand into his arm, and they went away.", "그는 따르는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없음을 알았고, 그녀는 그의 팔에 손을 얹었으며, 그렇게 두 사람은 길을 나섰다."]], [["\"It is number 92, up four pair of stairs,\"", "\"92호란다, 계단을 네 층 올라가야 해.\""], ["Jos said, perhaps not very willing to ascend the steps again; but he placed himself in the window of his drawing-room, which commands the place on which the Elephant stands, and saw the pair marching through the market.", "조스가 말했는데, 아마도 그 계단을 다시 오르고 싶지 않았던 모양이다. 다만 그는 코끼리 호텔이 서 있는 광장이 내려다보이는 자기 응접실 창가에 자리를 잡고, 두 사람이 시장을 가로질러 행진해 가는 모습을 지켜보았다."]], [["It was as well that Becky saw them too from her garret, for she and the two students were chattering and laughing there; they had been joking about the appearance of Becky's grandpapa--whose arrival and departure they had witnessed--but she had time to dismiss them, and have her little room clear before the landlord of the Elephant, who knew that Mrs. Osborne was a great favourite at the Serene Court, and respected her accordingly, led the way up the stairs to the roof story, encouraging Miladi and the Herr Major as they achieved the ascent.", "베키도 자기 다락방에서 그들을 본 것이 다행이었다. 그녀와 두 학생이 거기서 재잘대며 웃고 있었던 까닭이다. 그들은 베키의 할아버지가 왔다 간 일을—그 도착과 떠남을 직접 목격했던 것이다—두고 농담을 주고받던 참이었다. 그러나 그녀에게는 그들을 내보내고 자기 작은 방을 치워 둘 시간이 있었다. 오즈번 부인이 군주의 궁정에서 큰 총애를 받는 사람임을 알고 그만큼 그녀를 깍듯이 대하던 코끼리 호텔 주인이, 미라디와 헤어 소령이 계단을 다 오르도록 격려하며 지붕 밑 층으로 가는 길을 앞장서 안내했을 때 말이다."]], [["\"Gracious lady, gracious lady!\"", "\"존귀하신 부인, 존귀하신 부인!\""], ["said the landlord, knocking at Becky's door; he had called her Madame the day before, and was by no means courteous to her.", "호텔 주인이 베키의 방문을 두드리며 말했다. 그는 전날만 해도 그녀를 그저 마담이라 불렀고 결코 정중하게 대하지 않았던 것이다."]], [["\"Who is it?\"", "\"누구세요?\""], ["Becky said, putting out her head, and she gave a little scream.", "베키가 머리를 내밀며 말하더니, 작게 비명을 질렀다."], ["There stood Emmy in a tremble, and Dobbin, the tall Major, with his cane.", "거기에 에미가 떨면서 서 있었고, 그 곁에는 키 큰 소령 도빈이 지팡이를 든 채 서 있었다."]], [["He stood still watching, and very much interested at the scene; but Emmy sprang forward with open arms towards Rebecca, and forgave her at that moment, and embraced her and kissed her with all her heart.", "그는 가만히 서서 지켜보았고, 그 장면에 무척 흥미를 느끼고 있었다. 그러나 에미는 두 팔을 활짝 벌리고 레베카를 향해 달려들었고, 그 순간 그녀를 용서했으며, 진심을 다해 그녀를 끌어안고 입을 맞추었다."], ["Ah, poor wretch, when was your lip pressed before by such pure kisses?", "아, 가엾은 것, 네 입술이 이토록 순결한 입맞춤에 닿아 본 적이 마지막으로 언제였더냐?"]], [["Amantium Irae", "『아만티움 이라이』"]], [["Frankness and kindness like Amelia's were likely to touch even such a hardened little reprobate as Becky.", "애밀리아 같은 솔직함과 다정함은 베키처럼 굳어진 작은 망나니의 마음마저 움직일 만했다."], ["She returned Emmy's caresses and kind speeches with something very like gratitude, and an emotion which, if it was not lasting, for a moment was almost genuine.", "베키는 에미의 다정한 손길과 따뜻한 말에, 거의 감사라 할 만한 무언가로, 또한 비록 오래가지는 않더라도 한순간만큼은 거의 진심에 가까운 어떤 감정으로 화답했다."], ["That was a lucky stroke of hers about the child \"torn from her arms shrieking.\"", "\"비명을 지르며 품에서 떼어 내진\" 아이 이야기는 그녀의 행운의 한 수였다."], ["It was by that harrowing misfortune that Becky had won her friend back, and it was one of the very first points, we may be certain, upon which our poor simple little Emmy began to talk to her new-found acquaintance.", "그 가슴 찢어지는 불행으로 베키는 친구의 마음을 도로 얻은 것이며, 우리의 가엾고 순박한 어린 에미가 새로 만난 옛 지인에게 말을 건넨 맨 처음 화두 가운데 하나가 바로 그것이었으리라는 점은 충분히 짐작할 만하다."]], [["\"And so they took your darling child from you?\"", "\"그래서 네 소중한 아이를 너한테서 데려갔단 말이지?\""], ["our simpleton cried out.", "우리의 순진한 사람이 외쳤다."], ["\"Oh, Rebecca, my poor dear suffering friend, I know what it is to lose a boy, and to feel for those who have lost one.", "\"오, 레베카, 가엾고 고통받는 내 친구야, 나도 사내아이를 잃는다는 게 어떤 건지, 또 아이를 잃은 사람의 마음이 어떤지 알아."], ["But please Heaven yours will be restored to you, as a merciful merciful Providence has brought me back mine.\"", "하지만 자비로우신 하느님께서 내게 내 아이를 돌려주셨듯이, 부디 네게도 네 아이를 돌려주시기를.\""]], [["\"The child, my child?", "\"그 아이, 내 아이 말이지?"], ["Oh, yes, my agonies were frightful,\"", "오, 그래, 내 고통은 끔찍했어.\""], ["Becky owned, not perhaps without a twinge of conscience.", "베키는 그렇게 인정했는데, 어쩌면 양심의 가책이 전혀 없지는 않았을 것이다."], ["It jarred upon her to be obliged to commence instantly to tell lies in reply to so much confidence and simplicity.", "이토록 큰 믿음과 순진함에 답하느라 당장 거짓말부터 늘어놓아야 한다는 것이 그녀의 마음을 거슬리게 했다."], ["But that is the misfortune of beginning with this kind of forgery.", "그러나 이런 종류의 위조를 시작하는 자가 겪는 불행이 바로 그것이다."], ["When one fib becomes due as it were, you must forge another to take up the old acceptance; and so the stock of your lies in circulation inevitably multiplies, and the danger of detection increases every day.", "거짓말 하나가, 말하자면 만기가 돌아오면, 그 옛 어음을 막기 위해 또 다른 거짓말을 위조해야 하는 법이다. 그리하여 시중에 유통되는 거짓말의 재고는 필연적으로 불어나고, 들통날 위험은 날마다 커져 가는 것이다."]], [["\"My agonies,\" Becky continued, \"were terrible (I hope she won't sit down on the bottle) when they took him away from me; I thought I should die; but I fortunately had a brain fever, during which my doctor gave me up, and--and I recovered, and--and here I am, poor and friendless.\"", "\"내 고통은 말이야,\" 베키가 말을 이었다. \"그 애를 내게서 데려갔을 때(저 여자가 술병 위에 앉지 않아야 할 텐데) 견딜 수 없을 만큼 끔찍했어. 죽는 줄로만 알았지. 하지만 다행히도 뇌염에 걸려서, 의사도 가망이 없다며 손을 놓았는데, 그래도, 그래도 나는 회복했고, 그래서, 그래서 이렇게 가난뱅이에 외톨이 신세로 여기 있는 거야.\""]], [["\"How old is he?\"", "\"그 애는 몇 살이지?\""], ["Emmy asked.", "에미가 물었다."]], [["\"Eleven,\"", "\"열한 살이야.\""], ["said Becky.", "베키가 말했다."]], [["\"Eleven!\"", "\"열한 살!\""], ["cried the other.", "에미가 외쳤다."], ["\"Why, he was born the same year with Georgy, who is--\"", "\"아니, 그럼 우리 조지랑 같은 해에 태어났네. 조지가 지금―\""]], [["\"I know, I know,\"", "\"알아, 알아.\""], ["Becky cried out, who had in fact quite forgotten all about little Rawdon's age.", "베키가 외쳤는데, 사실 그녀는 어린 로던의 나이를 까맣게 잊고 있었던 것이다."], ["\"Grief has made me forget so many things, dearest Amelia.", "\"슬픔 때문에 너무 많은 걸 잊어버렸어, 사랑하는 애밀리아."], ["I am very much changed: half-wild sometimes.", "나는 아주 많이 변했어. 가끔은 반쯤 미친 사람 같지."], ["He was eleven when they took him away from me.", "그 애가 열한 살 때 내게서 데려갔어."], ["Bless his sweet face; I have never seen it again.\"", "그 사랑스러운 얼굴에 축복이 있기를. 그 후로 다시는 못 봤어.\""]], [["\"Was he fair or dark?\"", "\"그 애는 금발이었어, 아니면 검은 머리였어?\""], ["went on that absurd little Emmy.", "그 어처구니없을 만큼 순진한 에미가 말을 이었다."], ["\"Show me his hair.\"", "\"그 애 머리카락 좀 보여 줘.\""]], [["Becky almost laughed at her simplicity.", "베키는 그녀의 순박함에 하마터면 웃음을 터뜨릴 뻔했다. \"오늘은 안 되겠어, 얘."], ["\"Not to-day, love--some other time, when my trunks arrive from Leipzig, whence I came to this place--and a little drawing of him, which I made in happy days.\"", "다음에, 내가 라이프치히에서―거기서 이곳으로 왔거든―트렁크가 도착하면. 그리고 행복했던 시절에 내가 그 애를 그린 자그마한 그림도 있어.\""]], [["\"Poor Becky, poor Becky!\"", "\"가엾은 베키, 가엾은 베키!\""], ["said Emmy.", "에미가 말했다."], ["\"How thankful, how thankful I ought to be\"; (though I doubt whether that practice of piety inculcated upon us by our womankind in early youth, namely, to be thankful because we are better off than somebody else, be a very rational religious exercise) and then she began to think, as usual, how her son was the handsomest, the best, and the cleverest boy in the whole world.", "\"나는 얼마나, 얼마나 감사해야 하는지\" (다만 우리가 어린 시절 집안 여인들에게서 주입받은 그 신심의 실천, 즉 우리가 다른 누군가보다 처지가 낫다 하여 감사해야 한다는 것이, 과연 매우 이치에 맞는 종교적 수행인지는 의문이지만) 그러고 나서 그녀는 늘 그렇듯, 자기 아들이야말로 온 세상에서 가장 잘생기고, 가장 착하고, 가장 영리한 아이라는 생각에 잠기기 시작했다."]], [["\"You will see my Georgy,\"", "\"우리 조지를 보게 될 거야.\""], ["was the best thing Emmy could think of to console Becky.", "이것이 베키를 위로하려고 에미가 떠올릴 수 있는 최선이었다."], ["If anything could make her comfortable that would.", "무엇이든 그녀를 편안하게 해 줄 수 있다면, 바로 그것이리라."]], [["And so the two women continued talking for an hour or more, during which Becky had the opportunity of giving her new friend a full and complete version of her private history.", "그리하여 두 여자는 한 시간 남짓 계속 이야기를 나누었고, 그동안 베키는 새 친구에게 자신의 사적인 내력을 빠짐없이 온전히 들려줄 기회를 얻었다. 그녀는 로던 크롤리와의 결혼이 가족에게 어떻게 늘 더없는 적의로 받아들여졌는지, 시누이(교활한 여자)가 어떻게 남편의 마음에 그녀에 대한 독을 풀어 놓았는지, 그가 어떻게 가증스러운 교제 관계를 맺어 그녀에게서 애정을 거두어 갔는지를 늘어놓았다."], ["She showed how her marriage with Rawdon Crawley had always been viewed by the family with feelings of the utmost hostility; how her sister-in-law (an artful woman) had poisoned her husband's mind against her; how he had formed odious connections, which had estranged his affections from her: how she had borne everything--poverty, neglect, coldness from the being whom she most loved--and all for the sake of her child; how, finally, and by the most flagrant outrage, she had been driven into demanding a separation from her husband, when the wretch did not scruple to ask that she should sacrifice her own fair fame so that he might procure advancement through the means of a very great and powerful but unprincipled man--the Marquis of Steyne, indeed.", "또 자신이 가장 사랑하는 사람에게서 받은 가난과 무관심과 냉대를 어떻게 다 견뎌 냈는지, 그 모든 것이 오로지 자기 아이를 위해서였는지를, 그리고 마침내 더없이 파렴치한 폭거로 인해 어떻게 남편과의 별거를 요구하지 않을 수 없는 지경에 내몰렸는지를 이야기했다. 그 비열한 인간이, 자기가 어느 대단하고 막강하지만 파렴치한 인물―바로 스타인 후작 말이다―의 힘을 빌려 출세를 도모할 수 있도록 그녀가 제 정조를 희생하라고 요구하기를 서슴지 않았다는 것이다."], ["The atrocious monster!", "그 잔악무도한 괴물 같으니!"]], [["This part of her eventful history Becky gave with the utmost feminine delicacy and the most indignant virtue.", "자신의 파란만장한 내력 가운데 이 대목을 베키는 더없이 여성스러운 섬세함과 더없이 분개에 찬 정숙함으로 들려주었다."], ["Forced to fly her husband's roof by this insult, the coward had pursued his revenge by taking her child from her.", "이 모욕 때문에 남편의 지붕 아래서 도망치지 않을 수 없게 되자, 그 비겁한 자는 그녀의 아이를 빼앗아 가는 것으로 복수를 밀어붙였다는 것이다."], ["And thus Becky said she was a wanderer, poor, unprotected, friendless, and wretched.", "그리하여 자신은 가난하고, 보호받지 못하고, 친구도 없고, 비참한 떠돌이 신세가 되었다고 베키는 말했다."]], [["Emmy received this story, which was told at some length, as those persons who are acquainted with her character may imagine that she would.", "에미는 제법 길게 이어진 이 이야기를, 그녀의 성품을 아는 이들이라면 능히 짐작할 만한 모습으로 받아들였다."], ["She quivered with indignation at the account of the conduct of the miserable Rawdon and the unprincipled Steyne.", "그녀는 그 비참한 로던과 파렴치한 스타인의 행실에 관한 이야기에 분개하여 몸을 떨었다."], ["Her eyes made notes of admiration for every one of the sentences in which Becky described the persecutions of her aristocratic relatives and the falling away of her husband.", "베키가 자기 귀족 친척들의 박해와 남편의 변심을 묘사하는 문장 하나하나마다, 에미의 두 눈은 감탄의 부호를 찍었다. (베키는 남편을 욕하지 않았다."], ["(Becky did not abuse him. She spoke rather in sorrow than in anger.", "분노라기보다는 차라리 슬픔에 찬 어조로 말했다."], ["She had loved him only too fondly: and was he not the father of her boy?) And as for the separation scene from the child, while Becky was reciting it, Emmy retired altogether behind her pocket-handkerchief, so that the consummate little tragedian must have been charmed to see the effect which her performance produced on her audience.", "그녀는 그를 너무도 애틋하게 사랑했을 뿐이며, 게다가 그가 자기 아들의 아버지가 아니던가?) 그리고 아이와 헤어지던 장면으로 말하자면, 베키가 그것을 읊는 동안 에미는 손수건 뒤로 아예 자취를 감춰 버렸으니, 그 빼어난 어린 비극 배우는 자기 연기가 관객에게 자아낸 효과를 보며 분명 흐뭇했을 것이다."]], [["Whilst the ladies were carrying on their conversation, Amelia's constant escort, the Major (who, of course, did not wish to interrupt their conference, and found himself rather tired of creaking about the narrow stair passage of which the roof brushed the nap from his hat) descended to the ground-floor of the house and into the great room common to all the frequenters of the Elephant, out of which the stair led.", "숙녀들이 대화를 이어 가는 동안, 애밀리아의 변함없는 호위자인 소령은(물론 그는 그들의 회담을 방해하고 싶지 않았고, 게다가 천장이 모자의 보풀을 스칠 만큼 좁은 계단 통로를 삐걱대며 오르내리는 데 다소 진력이 나 있었다) 집 일층으로 내려가, 계단이 통하는 그 큰 방, 곧 엘리펀트를 드나드는 온갖 사람들이 함께 쓰는 공동 방으로 들어섰다."], ["This apartment is always in a fume of smoke and liberally sprinkled with beer.", "이 방은 늘 담배 연기가 자욱하고 맥주가 흥건히 흩뿌려져 있다."], ["On a dirty table stand scores of corresponding brass candlesticks with tallow candles for the lodgers, whose keys hang up in rows over the candles.", "더러운 탁자 위에는 투숙객들을 위한 우지 양초와 그에 짝을 이루는 놋쇠 촛대가 수십 개씩 늘어서 있고, 그 양초들 위로는 객실 열쇠들이 줄지어 걸려 있다."], ["Emmy had passed blushing through the room anon, where all sorts of people were collected; Tyrolese glove-sellers and Danubian linen-merchants, with their packs; students recruiting themselves with butterbrods and meat; idlers, playing cards or dominoes on the sloppy, beery tables; tumblers refreshing during the cessation of their performances--in a word, all the fumum and strepitus of a German inn in fair time.", "에미는 조금 전 얼굴을 붉히며 이 방을 지나갔는데, 그곳에는 온갖 부류의 사람들이 모여 있었다. 짐짝을 짊어진 티롤 출신 장갑 장수들과 도나우 강변에서 온 아마포 상인들, 버터 바른 빵과 고기로 기운을 채우는 학생들, 끈적끈적하고 맥주에 전 탁자에서 카드놀이나 도미노를 하는 한량들, 공연이 잠시 멈춘 틈에 원기를 돋우는 곡예사들―한마디로, 장날의 독일 여관이 풍기는 온갖 연기와 소음이 거기 있었다."], ["The waiter brought the Major a mug of beer, as a matter of course, and he took out a cigar and amused himself with that pernicious vegetable and a newspaper until his charge should come down to claim him.", "종업원이 으레 그렇듯 소령에게 맥주 한 잔을 가져다주었고, 그는 시가 한 대를 꺼내 들고, 자기가 맡은 사람이 내려와 그를 찾을 때까지 그 해로운 풀잎과 신문으로 시간을 때웠다."]], [["Max and Fritz came presently downstairs, their caps on one side, their spurs jingling, their pipes splendid with coats of arms and full-blown tassels, and they hung up the key of No.", "이윽고 막스와 프리츠가 모자를 한쪽으로 비뚜름히 쓰고, 박차를 짤랑이며, 문장이 화려하게 새겨지고 술이 탐스럽게 달린 파이프를 물고 아래층으로 내려와, 90호 열쇠를 걸이에 걸고 버터 바른 빵과 맥주를 한 차례 주문했다."], ["90 on the board and called for the ration of butterbrod and beer.", "두 사람은 소령 옆에 앉아 대화에 빠져들었는데, 그는 어쩔 수 없이 그 일부를 듣게 되었다."], ["The pair sat down by the Major and fell into a conversation of which he could not help hearing somewhat. It was mainly about \"Fuchs\"", "대화는 주로 \"여우\"와 \"속물\"에 관한 것, 그리고 인근 쇼펜하우젠 대학에서 벌어진 결투며 술판에 관한 것이었다."], ["and \"Philister,\" and duels and drinking-bouts at the neighbouring University of Schoppenhausen, from which renowned seat of learning they had just come in the Eilwagen, with Becky, as it appeared, by their side, and in order to be present at the bridal fetes at Pumpernickel.", "보아하니 그들은 그 이름난 학문의 전당에서 베키를 곁에 태우고 막 급행 마차를 타고 와, 품퍼니켈의 결혼 축제에 참석하려는 참이었다."]], [["\"The title Englanderinn seems to be en bays de gonnoisance,\"", "\"저 영국 여자라는 칭호는 아무래도 안면이 있는 사이인 것 같아.\""], ["said Max, who knew the French language, to Fritz, his comrade.", "프랑스어를 아는 막스가 동료 프리츠에게 말했다."], ["\"After the fat grandfather went away, there came a pretty little compatriot.", "\"저 뚱뚱한 할아버지가 가고 나니까 예쁘장한 동포 아가씨가 하나 왔거든."], ["I heard them chattering and whimpering together in the little woman's chamber.\"", "그 자그마한 여자 방에서 둘이 재잘거리고 훌쩍거리는 걸 들었지.\""]], [["\"We must take the tickets for her concert,\"", "\"그 여자 음악회 표를 사 줘야겠어.\""], ["Fritz said.", "프리츠가 말했다."], ["\"Hast thou any money, Max?\"", "\"돈 있어, 막스?\""]], [["\"Bah,\"", "\"흥.\""], ["said the other, \"the concert is a concert in nubibus.", "다른 쪽이 말했다. \"그 음악회는 구름 속의 음악회야."], ["Hans said that she advertised one at Leipzig, and the Burschen took many tickets.", "한스 말로는 그 여자가 라이프치히에서도 음악회를 광고했고 대학생들이 표를 많이 샀다더군."], ["But she went off without singing.", "그런데 노래도 안 부르고 떠나 버렸대."], ["She said in the coach yesterday that her pianist had fallen ill at Dresden.", "어제 마차에서는 자기 피아노 반주자가 드레스덴에서 병이 났다고 하더라고."], ["She cannot sing, it is my belief: her voice is as cracked as thine, O thou beer-soaking Renowner!\"", "노래를 못하는 게 분명해. 목소리가 너처럼 갈라졌거든, 이 맥주에 절은 허풍쟁이 양반아!\""]], [["\"It is cracked; I hear her trying out of her window a schrecklich English ballad, called 'De Rose upon de Balgony.'\"", "\"갈라졌고말고. 창문 너머로 그 여자가 '발코니 위의 장미'라는 끔찍한 영국 발라드를 연습하는 걸 들었어.\""]], [["\"Saufen and singen go not together,\"", "\"술 마시기랑 노래 부르기는 같이 안 가는 법이지.\""], ["observed Fritz with the red nose, who evidently preferred the former amusement.", "코가 빨간 프리츠가 말했는데, 그는 분명 전자의 오락을 더 좋아했다."], ["\"No, thou shalt take none of her tickets.", "\"안 돼, 너는 그 여자 표를 한 장도 사면 안 돼."], ["She won money at the trente and quarante last night.", "그 여자 어젯밤에 트랑테 에 카랑트로 돈을 땄거든."], ["I saw her: she made a little English boy play for her.", "내가 봤어. 어린 영국 사내아이한테 대신 패를 잡게 하더군."], ["We will spend thy money there or at the theatre, or we will treat her to French wine or Cognac in the Aurelius Garden, but the tickets we will not buy.", "네 돈은 거기서나 극장에서 쓰자고. 아니면 아우렐리우스 정원에서 그 여자한테 프랑스 와인이나 코냑을 한턱내든가."], ["What sayest thou?", "하지만 표는 사지 말자."], ["Yet, another mug of beer?\"", "어떻게 생각해? 자, 맥주 한 잔 더?\""], ["and one and another successively having buried their blond whiskers in the mawkish draught, curled them and swaggered off into the fair.", "그리하여 둘은 차례로 금빛 구레나룻을 그 느끼한 술잔에 파묻었다가, 구레나룻을 비비 꼬며 거들먹거리는 걸음으로 장터로 나갔다."]], [["The Major, who had seen the key of No. 90 put up on its hook and had heard the conversation of the two young University bloods, was not at a loss to understand that their talk related to Becky.", "90호 열쇠가 걸이에 걸리는 것을 보았고 또 두 젊은 대학 멋쟁이의 대화를 들은 소령은, 그들의 이야기가 베키에 관한 것임을 어렵잖이 알아차렸다."], ["\"The little devil is at her old tricks,\"", "\"저 작은 악녀가 옛날 수법을 또 부리는군.\""], ["he thought, and he smiled as he recalled old days, when he had witnessed the desperate flirtation with Jos and the ludicrous end of that adventure.", "그는 그렇게 생각하며, 조스를 상대로 한 그 필사적인 수작질과 그 모험의 우스꽝스러운 결말을 목격했던 옛날을 떠올리고 미소 지었다."], ["He and George had often laughed over it subsequently, and until a few weeks after George's marriage, when he also was caught in the little Circe's toils, and had an understanding with her which his comrade certainly suspected, but preferred to ignore.", "그와 조지는 그 후로도 그 일을 두고 자주 함께 웃곤 했는데, 조지가 결혼하고 몇 주 지났을 무렵, 그 역시 이 작은 키르케의 그물에 걸려들어, 동료가 분명 의심하면서도 모른 척하는 편을 택한 그런 사이를 그녀와 맺기 전까지는 그랬다."], ["William was too much hurt or ashamed to ask to fathom that disgraceful mystery, although once, and evidently with remorse on his mind, George had alluded to it.", "윌리엄은 너무나 상심했거나 부끄러웠던 탓에 그 수치스러운 비밀의 진상을 캐물을 엄두를 내지 못했지만, 한 번은 조지가, 분명 마음에 회한을 품은 채 그 일을 넌지시 입에 올린 적이 있었다."], ["It was on the morning of Waterloo, as the young men stood together in front of their line, surveying the black masses of Frenchmen who crowned the opposite heights, and as the rain was coming down, \"I have been mixing in a foolish intrigue with a woman,\"", "워털루 전투가 벌어지던 날 아침, 두 젊은이가 자기네 전열 앞에 나란히 서서 맞은편 고지를 뒤덮은 프랑스군의 검은 무리를 바라보고 있을 때, 그리고 비가 내리고 있을 때의 일이었다. \"어떤 여자랑 어리석은 정사에 얽혀 들었어.\""], ["George said.", "조지가 말했다."], ["\"I am glad we were marched away.", "\"우리가 이렇게 진군해 떠나게 돼서 다행이야."], ["If I drop, I hope Emmy will never know of that business.", "내가 쓰러지면, 부디 에미가 그 일을 영영 모르기를 바라."], ["I wish to God it had never been begun!\"", "아예 시작도 안 했더라면 좋았을걸, 하느님 맙소사!\""], ["And William was pleased to think, and had more than once soothed poor George's widow with the narrative, that Osborne, after quitting his wife, and after the action of Quatre Bras, on the first day, spoke gravely and affectionately to his comrade of his father and his wife.", "그래서 윌리엄은, 오스본이 아내 곁을 떠난 뒤, 그리고 첫날 카트르브라 전투를 치른 뒤에, 동료에게 자기 아버지와 아내 이야기를 진지하고 애정 어린 어조로 했다는 사실을 떠올리며 흐뭇해했고, 또 한 번 이상 그 이야기로 가엾은 조지의 미망인을 달래 주기도 했다."], ["On these facts, too, William had insisted very strongly in his conversations with the elder Osborne, and had thus been the means of reconciling the old gentleman to his son's memory, just at the close of the elder man's life.", "윌리엄은 늙은 오스본과의 대화에서도 이 사실들을 무척 강하게 역설했고, 그리하여 그 노신사의 생애가 막 저물어 갈 무렵, 그가 아들의 기억과 화해하도록 이끄는 다리 노릇을 했던 것이다."]], [["\"And so this devil is still going on with her intrigues,\"", "\"그래, 이 악녀가 아직도 정사질을 계속하고 있군.\""], ["thought William.", "윌리엄은 생각했다."], ["\"I wish she were a hundred miles from here.", "\"여기서 백 마일은 떨어진 데 있으면 좋으련만."], ["She brings mischief wherever she goes.\"", "가는 곳마다 화근을 몰고 다닌다니까.\""], ["And he was pursuing these forebodings and this uncomfortable train of thought, with his head between his hands, and the Pumpernickel Gazette of last week unread under his nose, when somebody tapped his shoulder with a parasol, and he looked up and saw Mrs. Amelia.", "그리하여 그는 두 손에 머리를 파묻은 채, 코앞에 지난주 품퍼니켈 신문을 읽지도 않고 펼쳐 놓은 채로 이런 불길한 예감과 이 불편한 상념의 갈래를 좇고 있었는데, 그때 누군가 양산으로 그의 어깨를 톡톡 쳤고, 고개를 들어 보니 애밀리아 부인이었다."]], [["This woman had a way of tyrannizing over Major Dobbin (for the weakest of all people will domineer over somebody), and she ordered him about, and patted him, and made him fetch and carry just as if he was a great Newfoundland dog.", "이 여자는 도빈 소령을 마음대로 부리는 버릇이 있었으니(가장 나약한 사람일수록 누군가에게는 군림하려 드는 법이다), 그녀는 그에게 이래라저래라 명령하고, 토닥거리고, 마치 그가 커다란 뉴펀들랜드 개라도 되는 양 물건을 가져오라 들고 오라 시켰다. 그도 말하자면 그녀가 \"올라가, 도빈!\""], ["He liked, so to speak, to jump into the water if she said \"High, Dobbin!\" and to trot behind her with her reticule in his mouth.", "하고 외치면 물속으로 뛰어들고, 입에 그녀의 손가방을 문 채 그녀 뒤를 종종거리며 따라가는 것을 좋아했다."], ["This history has been written to very little purpose if the reader has not perceived that the Major was a spooney.", "이 이야기에서 독자가 소령이 푹 빠진 얼간이라는 사실을 알아차리지 못했다면, 이 글은 거의 헛수고로 쓰인 셈이다."]], [["\"Why did you not wait for me, sir, to escort me downstairs?\"", "\"왜 저를 데리고 내려가시려고 기다리지 않으셨어요, 소령님?\""], ["she said, giving a little toss of her head and a most sarcastic curtsey.", "그녀는 고개를 살짝 까딱이고 더없이 비꼬는 투로 무릎 굽혀 인사하며 말했다."]], [["\"I couldn't stand up in the passage,\"", "\"통로에서는 똑바로 설 수가 없었거든요.\""], ["he answered with a comical deprecatory look; and, delighted to give her his arm and to take her out of the horrid smoky place, he would have walked off without even so much as remembering the waiter, had not the young fellow run after him and stopped him on the threshold of the Elephant to make him pay for the beer which he had not consumed.", "그는 우스꽝스럽게 미안해하는 표정으로 대답했다. 그러고는 그녀에게 팔을 내주고 그 끔찍하게 연기 자욱한 곳에서 데리고 나오게 된 것이 기뻐, 종업원 생각은 까맣게 잊은 채 그냥 걸어 나가려 했는데, 그 젊은이가 뒤쫓아 와 엘리펀트 문턱에서 그를 붙잡고는 마시지도 않은 맥주값을 치르게 했다."], ["Emmy laughed: she called him a naughty man, who wanted to run away in debt, and, in fact, made some jokes suitable to the occasion and the small-beer.", "에미는 웃었다. 그녀는 그를 외상값을 떼먹고 달아나려는 짓궂은 사람이라 부르며, 사실 그 상황과 그 묽은 맥주에 어울리는 농담 몇 마디를 던졌다."], ["She was in high spirits and good humour, and tripped across the market-place very briskly.", "그녀는 기분이 한껏 들떠 명랑한 채로, 매우 경쾌하게 장터를 가로질러 종종걸음을 쳤다."], ["She wanted to see Jos that instant.", "그녀는 당장 조스를 보고 싶어 했다."], ["The Major laughed at the impetuous affection Mrs.", "소령은 애밀리아 부인이 보이는 그 성마른 애정에 웃음을 지었으니, 실은 그녀가 오빠를 \"당장\" 보고 싶어 하는 일이 그리 흔치는 않았기 때문이다."], ["Amelia exhibited; for, in truth, it was not very often that she wanted her brother \"that instant.\"", "두 사람은 그 민간인을 이층 응접실에서 발견했다. 에미가 다락방에서 친구와 단둘이 틀어박혀 있고 소령이 아래층 공동 방의 끈적이는 탁자를 두드리며 시간을 보내던 지난 한 시간 동안, 그는 방 안을 서성이고, 손톱을 물어뜯고, 적어도 백 번은 장터 너머 엘리펀트 쪽을 내다보았던 것이다."], ["They found the civilian in his saloon on the first-floor; he had been pacing the room, and biting his nails, and looking over the market-place towards the Elephant a hundred times at least during the past hour whilst Emmy was closeted with her friend in the garret and the Major was beating the tattoo on the sloppy tables of the public room below, and he was, on his side too, very anxious to see Mrs. Osborne.", "그 역시 오스본 부인이 무척 보고 싶었던 것이다."]], [["\"Well?\"", "\"그래?\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"The poor dear creature, how she has suffered!\"", "\"가엾고 가엾은 사람, 얼마나 고생을 했는지!\""], ["Emmy said.", "에미가 말했다."]], [["\"God bless my soul, yes,\"", "\"오, 하느님 맙소사, 그렇지.\""], ["Jos said, wagging his head, so that his cheeks quivered like jellies.", "조스가 고개를 끄덕이며 말하니, 그 두 볼이 젤리처럼 출렁였다."]], [["\"She may have Payne's room, who can go upstairs,\" Emmy continued.", "\"페인 방을 그 친구한테 쓰게 하면 되겠어."], ["Payne was a staid English maid and personal attendant upon Mrs.", "페인은 위층으로 올라가면 되고.\" 에미가 말을 이었다."], ["Osborne, to whom the courier, as in duty bound, paid court, and whom Georgy used to \"lark\" dreadfully with accounts of German robbers and ghosts.", "페인은 오스본 부인의 차분한 영국인 하녀이자 시중을 드는 사람으로, 안내인이 으레 그렇듯 그녀에게 알랑거렸고, 조지는 독일 강도와 유령 이야기를 늘어놓으며 그녀를 무던히도 놀려 대곤 했다."], ["She passed her time chiefly in grumbling, in ordering about her mistress, and in stating her intention to return the next morning to her native village of Clapham.", "그녀는 주로 투덜거리고, 안주인에게 이래라저래라 명령하고, 다음 날 아침이면 고향 마을 클래펌으로 돌아가겠다는 뜻을 밝히며 시간을 보냈다."], ["\"She may have Payne's room,\"", "\"페인 방을 쓰게 하면 돼.\""], ["Emmy said.", "에미가 말했다."]], [["\"Why, you don't mean to say you are going to have that woman into the house?\"", "\"아니, 설마 그 여자를 이 집에 들이겠다는 건 아니겠지?\""], ["bounced out the Major, jumping up.", "소령이 벌떡 일어서며 불쑥 내뱉었다."]], [["\"Of course we are,\"", "\"당연히 들여야지요.\""], ["said Amelia in the most innocent way in the world.", "애밀리아가 세상에 둘도 없이 천진한 투로 말했다."], ["\"Don't be angry and break the furniture, Major Dobbin.", "\"화내면서 가구 부수지 마세요, 도빈 소령님."], ["Of course we are going to have her here.\"", "당연히 그 친구를 여기 들일 거예요.\""]], [["\"Of course, my dear,\"", "\"당연하지, 얘야.\""], ["Jos said.", "조스가 말했다."]], [["\"The poor creature, after all her sufferings,\"", "\"그 가엾은 사람이, 그 온갖 고생을 하고 나서.\""], ["Emmy continued; \"her horrid banker broken and run away; her husband--wicked wretch--having deserted her and taken her child away from her\"", "에미가 말을 이었다. \"그 끔찍한 은행가는 파산해서 달아나 버렸고, 남편이라는 인간은―그 사악한 작자는―그 친구를 버리고 아이까지 빼앗아 갔는데\""], ["(here she doubled her two little fists and held them in a most menacing attitude before her, so that the Major was charmed to see such a dauntless virago) \"the poor dear thing!", "(여기서 그녀는 자그마한 두 주먹을 꽉 쥐고 더없이 위협적인 자세로 앞에 들이밀었으니, 소령은 그토록 겁 없는 여장부를 보고 흐뭇해졌다) \"그 가엾은 사람을!"], ["quite alone and absolutely forced to give lessons in singing to get her bread--and not have her here!\"", "그렇게 외톨이로, 입에 풀칠하려고 별수 없이 노래 교습까지 해야 하는데, 그런데도 여기 안 들인다고요!\""]], [["\"Take lessons, my dear Mrs. George,\"", "\"교습은 받으세요, 조지 부인.\""], ["cried the Major, \"but don't have her in the house.", "소령이 외쳤다. \"하지만 그 여자를 집에 들이지는 마세요."], ["I implore you don't.\"", "제발 부탁입니다, 그러지 마세요.\""]], [["\"Pooh,\"", "\"쳇.\""], ["said Jos.", "조스가 말했다."]], [["\"You who are always good and kind--always used to be at any rate--I'm astonished at you, Major William,\"", "\"늘 선량하고 다정하셨던 분이―적어도 예전엔 그러셨잖아요―저는 소령님이 놀랍네요, 윌리엄 소령님.\""], ["Amelia cried.", "애밀리아가 외쳤다."], ["\"Why, what is the moment to help her but when she is so miserable?", "\"아니, 그 친구가 이토록 비참할 때가 아니면 대체 언제 도와줘요?"], ["Now is the time to be of service to her.", "지금이야말로 그 친구한테 도움이 되어 줄 때라고요."], ["The oldest friend I ever had, and not--\"", "제가 사귄 가장 오래된 친구인데, 그런데도―\""]], [["\"She was not always your friend, Amelia,\"", "\"그 여자가 늘 당신 친구였던 건 아니지요, 애밀리아.\""], ["the Major said, for he was quite angry.", "소령이 말했으니, 그는 단단히 화가 나 있었던 것이다."], ["This allusion was too much for Emmy, who, looking the Major almost fiercely in the face, said, \"For shame, Major Dobbin!\"", "이 말은 에미에게 너무 지나쳤다. 그녀는 거의 사납다 할 만큼 소령의 얼굴을 똑바로 노려보며 말했다."], ["and after having fired this shot, she walked out of the room with a most majestic air and shut her own door briskly on herself and her outraged dignity.", "\"부끄러운 줄 아세요, 도빈 소령님!\" 그러고는 이 한 발을 쏘아붙인 뒤, 더없이 위엄 있는 태도로 방을 나가, 모욕당한 자존심과 함께 자기 방문을 거세게 닫아 버렸다."]], [["\"To allude to THAT!\"", "\"하필 그 일을 들먹이다니!\""], ["she said, when the door was closed.", "문이 닫히자 그녀가 말했다."], ["\"Oh, it was cruel of him to remind me of it,\"", "\"오, 그걸 내게 상기시키다니 너무 잔인해.\""], ["and she looked up at George's picture, which hung there as usual, with the portrait of the boy underneath.", "그러고는 늘 그렇듯 그곳에 걸려 있는 조지의 초상화를, 그리고 그 아래 걸린 아들의 초상화를 올려다보았다. \"잔인한 사람."], ["\"It was cruel of him.", "내가 그 일을 용서했는데, 그가 그걸 입에 올려야 했을까? 아니야."], ["If I had forgiven it, ought he to have spoken? No.", "게다가 내 질투가 얼마나 사악하고 근거 없는 것이었는지 바로 그 사람 입으로 들어서 알게 됐잖아."], ["And it is from his own lips that I know how wicked and groundless my jealousy was; and that you were pure--oh, yes, you were pure, my saint in heaven!\"", "당신은 결백했어요. 오, 그래요, 당신은 결백했어요, 하늘에 계신 나의 성인이여!\""]], [["She paced the room, trembling and indignant.", "그녀는 부들부들 떨고 분개하며 방 안을 서성였다."], ["She went and leaned on the chest of drawers over which the picture hung, and gazed and gazed at it.", "그녀는 초상화가 걸린 서랍장으로 가 거기에 기대어, 그것을 보고 또 보았다."], ["Its eyes seemed to look down on her with a reproach that deepened as she looked.", "그 두 눈은 그녀를 내려다보며 나무라는 듯했고, 그 책망은 그녀가 바라볼수록 더욱 깊어지는 것만 같았다."], ["The early dear, dear memories of that brief prime of love rushed back upon her.", "그 짧았던 사랑의 절정기에 깃든, 일찍이 소중하고 소중했던 추억들이 그녀에게 와락 되밀려 왔다."], ["The wound which years had scarcely cicatrized bled afresh, and oh, how bitterly!", "세월이 겨우 아물게 했던 상처가 새로이 피를 흘렸으니, 오, 얼마나 쓰라리게 흘렸던가!"], ["She could not bear the reproaches of the husband there before her.", "그녀는 자기 앞에 있는 남편의 책망을 견딜 수 없었다."], ["It couldn't be.", "있을 수 없는 일이었다."], ["Never, never.", "결코, 결코."]], [["Poor Dobbin; poor old William!", "가엾은 도빈, 가엾은 늙은 윌리엄! 그 불운한 한마디가 여러 해 공들인 일을―사랑과 변함없음으로 쌓아 올린 한평생의 길고 고된 건축물을, 그것도 묻혀 버린 정열과 헤아릴 수 없는 분투와 알려지지 않은 희생이 깃든 은밀하고 숨겨진 토대 위에 세워 올린 그 건축물을 허물어뜨렸다."], ["That unlucky word had undone the work of many a year--the long laborious edifice of a life of love and constancy--raised too upon what secret and hidden foundations, wherein lay buried passions, uncounted struggles, unknown sacrifices--a little word was spoken, and down fell the fair palace of hope--one word, and away flew the bird which he had been trying all his life to lure!", "작은 한마디가 내뱉어졌고, 그 아름다운 희망의 궁전이 와르르 무너져 내렸다. 한마디 말에, 그가 평생토록 꾀어 보려 애써 온 그 새가 훌쩍 날아가 버렸다!"]], [["William, though he saw by Amelia's looks that a great crisis had come, nevertheless continued to implore Sedley, in the most energetic terms, to beware of Rebecca; and he eagerly, almost frantically, adjured Jos not to receive her.", "윌리엄은 애밀리아의 표정을 보고 큰 위기가 닥쳤음을 알았지만, 그래도 더없이 힘주어 세들리에게 레베카를 조심하라고 거듭 간청했다. 그는 간절히, 거의 미친 듯이 조스에게 그녀를 받아들이지 말라고 당부했다."], ["He besought Mr. Sedley to inquire at least regarding her; told him how he had heard that she was in the company of gamblers and people of ill repute; pointed out what evil she had done in former days, how she and Crawley had misled poor George into ruin, how she was now parted from her husband, by her own confession, and, perhaps, for good reason.", "그는 세들리 씨에게 적어도 그녀에 관해 알아보기라도 하라고 청하며, 자기가 듣기로 그녀가 노름꾼과 평판 나쁜 자들과 어울려 다닌다고 일러 주었다."], ["What a dangerous companion she would be for his sister, who knew nothing of the affairs of the world!", "그녀가 지난날 어떤 못된 짓을 저질렀는지, 그녀와 크롤리가 어떻게 가엾은 조지를 잘못 이끌어 파멸로 몰아넣었는지, 그녀가 이제 자기 입으로 고백했듯이 어떻게 남편과 갈라섰는지, 어쩌면 그럴 만한 까닭이 있어서였는지를 지적했다."], ["William implored Jos, with all the eloquence which he could bring to bear, and a great deal more energy than this quiet gentleman was ordinarily in the habit of showing, to keep Rebecca out of his household.", "세상 물정이라고는 아무것도 모르는 그의 누이에게 그녀가 얼마나 위험한 동무가 되겠는가! 윌리엄은 자기가 끌어낼 수 있는 온갖 웅변을 다해, 그리고 이 조용한 신사가 평소 보이던 것보다 훨씬 더 큰 열의를 다해 조스에게 레베카를 그의 집안에 들이지 말라고 간청했다."]], [["Had he been less violent, or more dexterous, he might have succeeded in his supplications to Jos; but the civilian was not a little jealous of the airs of superiority which the Major constantly exhibited towards him, as he fancied (indeed, he had imparted his opinions to Mr.", "만약 그가 덜 격렬했거나 더 노련했더라면, 조스를 향한 그 간청에 성공했을지도 모른다. 그러나 그 민간인은 소령이 자기를 향해 늘 드러내 보인다고 여기는(실제로 그는 이 견해를 안내인 키르슈 씨에게 털어놓았는데, 도빈 소령은 이번 여행에서 그의 계산서를 일일이 따졌고, 그래서 그는 주인 편을 들었다) 그 잘난 척하는 태도를 적잖이 못마땅해하고 있었다."], ["Kirsch, the courier, whose bills Major Dobbin checked on this journey, and who sided with his master), and he began a blustering speech about his competency to defend his own honour, his desire not to have his affairs meddled with, his intention, in fine, to rebel against the Major, when the colloquy--rather a long and stormy one--was put an end to in the simplest way possible, namely, by the arrival of Mrs. Becky, with a porter from the Elephant Hotel in charge of her very meagre baggage.", "그리하여 그는 자기 명예쯤은 스스로 지킬 능력이 있다느니, 제 일에 누가 간섭하는 것을 원치 않는다느니, 한마디로 소령에게 반기를 들겠다느니 하며 큰소리치는 연설을 시작했는데, 그때 다소 길고 험악하게 이어지던 이 언쟁이 더없이 간단한 방식으로, 곧 베키 부인이 엘리펀트 호텔의 짐꾼에게 자기의 더없이 초라한 짐을 맡겨 들고 도착하는 바람에 끝이 났다."]], [["She greeted her host with affectionate respect and made a shrinking, but amicable salutation to Major Dobbin, who, as her instinct assured her at once, was her enemy, and had been speaking against her; and the bustle and clatter consequent upon her arrival brought Amelia out of her room.", "그녀는 애정 어린 공경의 태도로 주인에게 인사했고, 도빈 소령에게는 움츠러들면서도 우호적인 인사를 건넸다. 그녀의 직감은, 그가 자기의 적이며 줄곧 자기를 헐뜯어 왔음을 단박에 일러 주었던 것이다."], ["Emmy went up and embraced her guest with the greatest warmth, and took no notice of the Major, except to fling him an angry look--the most unjust and scornful glance that had perhaps ever appeared in that poor little woman's face since she was born.", "그녀가 도착하며 일으킨 부산함과 소란에 애밀리아가 방에서 나왔다. 에미는 다가가 더없이 따뜻하게 손님을 끌어안았고, 소령에게는 화난 눈길을 한 번 던질 뿐 아무 관심도 보이지 않았으니―그것은 이 가엾고 자그마한 여인이 태어난 이래 그 얼굴에 떠오른 가장 부당하고 경멸에 찬 눈빛이었을 것이다."], ["But she had private reasons of her own, and was bent upon being angry with him.", "그러나 그녀에게는 그녀 나름의 사사로운 이유가 있었고, 기어이 그에게 화를 내기로 작정한 터였다."], ["And Dobbin, indignant at the injustice, not at the defeat, went off, making her a bow quite as haughty as the killing curtsey with which the little woman chose to bid him farewell.", "그리고 도빈은 패배가 아니라 그 부당함에 분개하며, 그 자그마한 여인이 굳이 작별 인사로 보낸 그 사람 죽이는 듯한 무릎 인사 못지않게 거만한 절을 그녀에게 하고는 떠나 버렸다."]], [["He being gone, Emmy was particularly lively and affectionate to Rebecca, and bustled about the apartments and installed her guest in her room with an eagerness and activity seldom exhibited by our placid little friend.", "그가 가고 나자 에미는 레베카에게 유난히 활기차고 다정하게 굴며, 방마다 부산을 떨고 손님을 자기 방에 들이는 데 우리의 차분한 어린 친구가 좀처럼 보이지 않던 열의와 활기를 쏟았다."], ["But when an act of injustice is to be done, especially by weak people, it is best that it should be done quickly, and Emmy thought she was displaying a great deal of firmness and proper feeling and veneration for the late Captain Osborne in her present behaviour.", "그러나 부당한 일을 저지를 때는, 특히 나약한 사람일수록, 그것을 재빨리 해치우는 것이 상책이다. 그리고 에미는 지금의 처신으로 자기가 대단한 결단력과 올바른 감정을, 그리고 고인이 된 오스본 대위에 대한 깊은 존경을 드러내 보이고 있다고 여겼다."]], [["Georgy came in from the fetes for dinner-time and found four covers laid as usual; but one of the places was occupied by a lady, instead of by Major Dobbin.", "조지가 축제에서 돌아와 저녁 식사 시간에 와 보니, 식탁에는 늘 그렇듯 네 사람 분의 그릇이 차려져 있었다. 그런데 그 자리 중 하나는 도빈 소령 대신 한 숙녀가 차지하고 있었다."], ["\"Hullo!", "\"어이!"], ["where's Dob?\"", "도브는 어디 갔어요?\""], ["the young gentleman asked with his usual simplicity of language.", "어린 신사가 늘 그렇듯 거침없는 말투로 물었다."], ["\"Major Dobbin is dining out, I suppose,\"", "\"도빈 소령님은 밖에서 식사하시나 보다.\""], ["his mother said, and, drawing the boy to her, kissed him a great deal, and put his hair off his forehead, and introduced him to Mrs. Crawley.", "어머니가 그렇게 말하고는, 아이를 끌어당겨 마구 입을 맞추고, 이마에서 머리카락을 쓸어 넘긴 뒤 크롤리 부인에게 소개했다."], ["\"This is my boy, Rebecca,\"", "\"내 아들이에요, 레베카.\""], ["Mrs. Osborne said--as much as to say--can the world produce anything like that?", "오스본 부인이 말했는데―마치 이렇게 말하는 듯했다―세상에 이런 아이가 또 있을까요?"], ["Becky looked at him with rapture and pressed his hand fondly.", "베키는 황홀한 눈길로 아이를 바라보며 그 손을 애틋하게 꼭 쥐었다."], ["\"Dear boy!\"", "\"귀여운 아이!\""], ["she said--\"he is just like my--\"", "그녀가 말했다. \"꼭 우리―같구나.\""], ["Emotion choked her further utterance, but Amelia understood, as well as if she had spoken, that Becky was thinking of her own blessed child.", "북받치는 감정에 더는 말을 잇지 못했지만, 애밀리아는 베키가 마치 입 밖에 낸 것처럼 또렷이 알아들었다. 그녀가 제 사랑스러운 아이를 생각하고 있다는 것을."], ["However, the company of her friend consoled Mrs. Crawley, and she ate a very good dinner.", "어쨌든 친구와 함께 있다는 사실에 크롤리 부인은 위로를 받았고, 저녁을 아주 잘 먹었다."]], [["During the repast, she had occasion to speak several times, when Georgy eyed her and listened to her.", "식사하는 동안 그녀는 여러 차례 말할 기회가 있었고, 그럴 때면 조지가 그녀를 빤히 쳐다보며 그 말에 귀를 기울였다."], ["At the desert Emmy was gone out to superintend further domestic arrangements; Jos was in his great chair dozing over Galignani; Georgy and the new arrival sat close to each other--he had continued to look at her knowingly more than once, and at last he laid down the nutcrackers.", "후식이 나올 무렵 에미는 다른 집안일을 챙기러 나가 있었고, 조스는 큰 안락의자에 앉아 갈리냐니 신문을 보며 꾸벅꾸벅 졸고 있었다. 조지와 새로 온 손님은 바싹 붙어 앉아 있었는데―아이는 아까부터 한 번 이상 무언가 안다는 듯한 눈길로 그녀를 보고 있더니, 마침내 호두까기를 내려놓았다."]], [["\"I say,\"", "\"저기요.\""], ["said Georgy.", "조지가 말했다."]], [["\"What do you say?\"", "\"무슨 말을 하려고?\""], ["Becky said, laughing.", "베키가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["\"You're the lady I saw in the mask at the Rouge et Noir.\"", "\"아줌마는 루즈 에 누아르에서 가면 쓰고 있던 그 부인이잖아요.\""]], [["\"Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["you little sly creature,\"", "이 작은 능청꾼아.\""], ["Becky said, taking up his hand and kissing it.", "베키가 그의 손을 잡아 입을 맞추며 말했다."], ["\"Your uncle was there too, and Mamma mustn't know.\"", "\"네 외삼촌도 거기 계셨단다. 그러니 엄마가 알면 안 돼.\""]], [["\"Oh, no--not by no means,\"", "\"아, 네―절대로요.\""], ["answered the little fellow.", "어린 녀석이 대답했다."]], [["\"You see we are quite good friends already,\"", "\"보세요, 우린 벌써 아주 좋은 친구가 됐어요.\""], ["Becky said to Emmy, who now re-entered; and it must be owned that Mrs.", "이제 막 다시 들어온 에미에게 베키가 말했다."], ["Osborne had introduced a most judicious and amiable companion into her house.", "그리고 인정하지 않을 수 없는 것은, 오스본 부인이 더없이 분별 있고 상냥한 동무를 자기 집에 들여놓았다는 점이다."]], [["William, in a state of great indignation, though still unaware of all the treason that was in store for him, walked about the town wildly until he fell upon the Secretary of Legation, Tapeworm, who invited him to dinner.", "윌리엄은 자신을 기다리고 있는 온갖 배신은 아직 까맣게 모른 채로, 잔뜩 분개하여 미친 듯이 거리를 돌아다니다가 공사관 서기관 테이프웜과 마주쳤고, 그는 윌리엄을 저녁 식사에 초대했다."], ["As they were discussing that meal, he took occasion to ask the Secretary whether he knew anything about a certain Mrs.", "식사를 하면서 윌리엄은 기회를 보아 서기관에게, 자기가 알기로 런던에서 제법 떠들썩하게 굴었던 어느 로던 크롤리 부인이라는 사람에 대해 아는 게 있느냐고 물었다."], ["Rawdon Crawley, who had, he believed, made some noise in London; and then Tapeworm, who of course knew all the London gossip, and was besides a relative of Lady Gaunt, poured out into the astonished Major's ears such a history about Becky and her husband as astonished the querist, and supplied all the points of this narrative, for it was at that very table years ago that the present writer had the pleasure of hearing the tale.", "그러자 물론 런던의 온갖 소문을 다 꿰고 있는 데다 가운트 부인의 친척이기도 한 테이프웜은, 깜짝 놀란 소령의 귀에 베키와 그 남편에 관한 이야기를 어찌나 줄줄이 쏟아 냈던지 질문한 사람을 아연실색하게 만들었으니, 이 이야기의 모든 대목을 그가 채워 준 셈이다. 실은 바로 그 식탁에서, 여러 해 전에, 지금 이 글을 쓰는 사람이 그 이야기를 들을 기쁨을 누렸던 것이다."], ["Tufto, Steyne, the Crawleys, and their history--everything connected with Becky and her previous life passed under the record of the bitter diplomatist.", "터프토, 스타인, 크롤리 일가, 그리고 그들의 내력―베키와 그녀의 지난 삶에 얽힌 모든 것이 그 신랄한 외교관의 입을 거쳐 낱낱이 까발려졌다."], ["He knew everything and a great deal besides, about all the world--in a word, he made the most astounding revelations to the simple-hearted Major.", "그는 모든 것을, 그리고 온 세상 사람들에 관해 그 밖에도 숱한 것을 알고 있었다."], ["When Dobbin said that Mrs. Osborne and Mr.", "한마디로, 그는 순박한 마음씨의 소령에게 더없이 놀라운 폭로를 쏟아부었다."], ["Sedley had taken her into their house, Tapeworm burst into a peal of laughter which shocked the Major, and asked if they had not better send into the prison and take in one or two of the gentlemen in shaved heads and yellow jackets who swept the streets of Pumpernickel, chained in pairs, to board and lodge, and act as tutor to that little scapegrace Georgy.", "도빈이 오스본 부인과 세들리 씨가 그녀를 집에 들였다고 하자, 테이프웜은 소령을 질겁하게 할 만큼 한바탕 폭소를 터뜨리고는, 차라리 감옥에 사람을 보내, 머리를 빡빡 깎고 노란 죄수복을 입은 채 둘씩 쇠사슬에 묶여 품퍼니켈 거리를 쓰는 신사 한둘을 데려다가 먹이고 재우면서 그 작은 망나니 조지의 가정 교사로 삼는 편이 낫지 않겠느냐고 물었다."]], [["This information astonished and horrified the Major not a little.", "이 정보는 소령을 적잖이 놀라게 하고 또 소름 끼치게 했다."], ["It had been agreed in the morning (before meeting with Rebecca) that Amelia should go to the Court ball that night.", "그날 아침에(레베카와 마주치기 전에) 그날 밤 애밀리아가 궁정 무도회에 가기로 약속이 되어 있었다."], ["There would be the place where he should tell her.", "거기가 바로 그가 그녀에게 이야기를 꺼낼 자리였다."], ["The Major went home, and dressed himself in his uniform, and repaired to Court, in hopes to see Mrs.", "소령은 집으로 가 군복을 차려입고, 오스본 부인을 보리라는 희망을 품고 궁정으로 향했다."], ["Osborne. She never came.", "그녀는 끝내 오지 않았다."], ["When he returned to his lodgings all the lights in the Sedley tenement were put out.", "그가 숙소로 돌아왔을 때 세들리네 거처의 불은 모두 꺼져 있었다."], ["He could not see her till the morning.", "그녀를 아침까지 볼 수 없었던 것이다."], ["I don't know what sort of a night's rest he had with this frightful secret in bed with him.", "이 끔찍한 비밀을 안고 잠자리에 든 그가 그날 밤 어떤 잠을 잤을지는 나도 알 수 없다."]], [["At the earliest convenient hour in the morning he sent his servant across the way with a note, saying that he wished very particularly to speak with her.", "이튿날 아침, 적당한 가장 이른 시각에 그는 하인을 길 건너로 보내, 그녀와 꼭 긴히 이야기를 나누고 싶다는 쪽지를 전했다."], ["A message came back to say that Mrs. Osborne was exceedingly unwell and was keeping her room.", "오스본 부인이 몹시 몸이 좋지 않아 방에서 나오지 않고 있다는 전갈이 돌아왔다."]], [["She, too, had been awake all that night.", "그녀 역시 그날 밤 내내 깨어 있었다."], ["She had been thinking of a thing which had agitated her mind a hundred times before.", "그녀는 전에도 백 번은 마음을 뒤흔들었던 어떤 일을 골똘히 생각하고 있었다."], ["A hundred times on the point of yielding, she had shrunk back from a sacrifice which she felt was too much for her.", "백 번이나 마음을 굽히기 직전까지 갔다가도, 그녀는 자기에게 너무 벅차다고 느껴지는 그 희생 앞에서 번번이 움츠러들었다."], ["She couldn't, in spite of his love and constancy and her own acknowledged regard, respect, and gratitude.", "그의 사랑과 변함없음에도, 그리고 자신도 인정하는 호감과 존경과 감사에도 불구하고, 그녀는 그럴 수가 없었다."], ["What are benefits, what is constancy, or merit?", "은혜가 무엇이고, 변함없음이나 공덕이 다 무엇이란 말인가?"], ["One curl of a girl's ringlet, one hair of a whisker, will turn the scale against them all in a minute.", "한 처녀의 곱슬머리 한 가닥, 구레나룻의 터럭 한 올이면 그 모든 것을 한순간에 저울 반대편으로 기울게 하는 법이다."], ["They did not weigh with Emmy more than with other women.", "그런 것들은 다른 여자들에게나 마찬가지로 에미에게도 무게가 나가지 않았다."], ["She had tried them; wanted to make them pass; could not; and the pitiless little woman had found a pretext, and determined to be free.", "그녀는 그것들을 저울에 올려 보았고, 그것들이 통과되기를 바랐지만, 그럴 수 없었다. 그리하여 그 무자비한 자그마한 여인은 구실 하나를 찾아냈고, 자유로워지기로 작정했던 것이다."]], [["When at length, in the afternoon, the Major gained admission to Amelia, instead of the cordial and affectionate greeting, to which he had been accustomed now for many a long day, he received the salutation of a curtsey, and of a little gloved hand, retracted the moment after it was accorded to him.", "마침내 오후가 되어 소령이 애밀리아에게 들어가도 좋다는 허락을 얻었을 때, 그는 이제 오랜 세월 익숙해진 그 다정하고 따뜻한 환대 대신, 무릎 굽혀 절하는 인사와, 장갑 낀 자그마한 손 하나를―그것도 내밀자마자 곧바로 거두어들이는 손을 맞이했다."]], [["Rebecca, too, was in the room, and advanced to meet him with a smile and an extended hand.", "레베카 역시 그 방에 있었고, 미소를 띤 채 손을 내밀며 그를 맞으러 다가왔다."], ["Dobbin drew back rather confusedly, \"I--I beg your pardon, m'am,\"", "도빈은 다소 어쩔 줄 모르며 뒷걸음질 쳤다. \"저, 죄송합니다, 부인.\""], ["he said; \"but I am bound to tell you that it is not as your friend that I am come here now.\"", "그가 말했다. \"하지만 제가 지금 이곳에 온 건 당신의 친구로서가 아니라는 걸 분명히 말씀드려야겠군요.\""]], [["\"Pooh!", "\"흥!"], ["damn; don't let us have this sort of thing!\"", "제기랄, 이런 일은 그만둡시다!\""], ["Jos cried out, alarmed, and anxious to get rid of a scene.", "조스가 외쳤으니, 그는 겁에 질려 이 소동에서 벗어나고 싶어 안달이 났던 것이다."]], [["\"I wonder what Major Dobbin has to say against Rebecca?\"", "\"도빈 소령님이 레베카에 대해 무슨 험담을 하려는 건지 궁금하네요.\""], ["Amelia said in a low, clear voice with a slight quiver in it, and a very determined look about the eyes.", "애밀리아가 약간 떨리는, 낮고 또렷한 목소리로, 두 눈에 자못 단호한 빛을 띠고 말했다."]], [["\"I will not have this sort of thing in my house,\"", "\"내 집에서 이런 일은 절대 용납 못 해.\""], ["Jos again interposed.", "조스가 다시 끼어들었다."], ["\"I say I will not have it; and Dobbin, I beg, sir, you'll stop it.\"", "\"절대 안 된다니까. 그리고 도빈, 제발 부탁이네, 그만두게.\""], ["And he looked round, trembling and turning very red, and gave a great puff, and made for his door.", "그러고는 부들부들 떨며 얼굴이 새빨개진 채 주위를 둘러보더니, 크게 한숨을 한 번 내쉬고는 문 쪽으로 향했다."]], [["\"Dear friend!\"", "\"소중한 친구분!\""], ["Rebecca said with angelic sweetness, \"do hear what Major Dobbin has to say against me.\"", "레베카가 천사처럼 다정하게 말했다. \"도빈 소령님이 저에 대해 무슨 말을 하시려는지 꼭 들어 보세요.\""]], [["\"I will not hear it, I say,\"", "\"안 들어, 안 듣는다니까.\""], ["squeaked out Jos at the top of his voice, and, gathering up his dressing-gown, he was gone.", "조스가 목청껏 꽥 소리를 지르고는, 실내복 자락을 그러모아 쥐고 사라져 버렸다."]], [["\"We are only two women,\"", "\"우린 여자 둘뿐이에요.\""], ["Amelia said.", "애밀리아가 말했다."], ["\"You can speak now, sir.\"", "\"이제 말씀하셔도 돼요, 소령님.\""]], [["\"This manner towards me is one which scarcely becomes you, Amelia,\"", "\"저를 대하는 이런 태도는 당신답지 않은 것이군요, 애밀리아.\""], ["the Major answered haughtily; \"nor I believe am I guilty of habitual harshness to women.", "소령이 거만하게 대답했다. \"그리고 제가 여자들에게 늘 모질게 구는 사람도 아니라고 믿습니다."], ["It is not a pleasure to me to do the duty which I am come to do.\"", "제가 하러 온 이 의무를 다하는 것이 제게 기쁜 일은 아닙니다.\""]], [["\"Pray proceed with it quickly, if you please, Major Dobbin,\"", "\"부디 그 일을 빨리 해치우세요, 괜찮으시다면요, 도빈 소령님.\""], ["said Amelia, who was more and more in a pet.", "점점 더 심통이 난 애밀리아가 말했다."], ["The expression of Dobbin's face, as she spoke in this imperious manner, was not pleasant.", "그녀가 이토록 위압적인 투로 말할 때 도빈의 얼굴에 떠오른 표정은 보기 좋은 것이 아니었다."]], [["\"I came to say--and as you stay, Mrs. Crawley, I must say it in your presence--that I think you--you ought not to form a member of the family of my friends.", "\"제가 하러 온 말은―크롤리 부인, 당신이 자리에 있으니 당신 앞에서 말해야겠군요―당신은, 당신은 제 친구들의 가족 일원이 되어서는 안 된다고 생각한다는 것입니다."], ["A lady who is separated from her husband, who travels not under her own name, who frequents public gaming-tables--\"", "남편과 갈라서고, 자기 이름으로 다니지도 않고, 공공 도박장을 드나드는 여자는―\""]], [["\"It was to the ball I went,\"", "\"무도회에 갔던 거예요.\""], ["cried out Becky.", "베키가 외쳤다."]], [["\"--is not a fit companion for Mrs.", "\"―오스본 부인과 그 아들에게 어울리는 동무가 아닙니다.\""], ["Osborne and her son,\"", "도빈이 말을 이었다."], ["Dobbin went on: \"and I may add that there are people here who know you, and who profess to know that regarding your conduct about which I don't even wish to speak before--before Mrs. Osborne.\"", "\"그리고 한마디 덧붙이자면, 이곳에는 당신을 아는 사람들이 있고, 그들은 당신의 행실에 관해, 제가 오스본 부인 앞에서는 입에 올리고 싶지조차 않은 그 일에 관해 안다고 공언하고 있습니다.\""]], [["\"Yours is a very modest and convenient sort of calumny, Major Dobbin,\"", "\"참으로 점잖고도 편리한 종류의 중상모략이군요, 도빈 소령님.\""], ["Rebecca said.", "레베카가 말했다."], ["\"You leave me under the weight of an accusation which, after all, is unsaid.", "\"결국 입 밖에도 내지 않은 비난의 짐을 제게 지워 놓으시다니."], ["What is it?", "그게 뭔가요?"], ["Is it unfaithfulness to my husband?", "남편에 대한 부정인가요?"], ["I scorn it and defy anybody to prove it--I defy you, I say.", "저는 그런 건 경멸하며, 누구든 그걸 증명해 보라고 당당히 맞섭니다―당신에게도 맞서겠어요."], ["My honour is as untouched as that of the bitterest enemy who ever maligned me.", "제 명예는 일찍이 저를 가장 모질게 헐뜯은 원수의 명예 못지않게 깨끗합니다."], ["Is it of being poor, forsaken, wretched, that you accuse me?", "제가 가난하고, 버림받고, 비참하다고 비난하시는 건가요?"], ["Yes, I am guilty of those faults, and punished for them every day.", "그래요, 저는 그런 잘못이라면 지었고, 그 죄로 날마다 벌을 받고 있지요."], ["Let me go, Emmy.", "가게 해 주렴, 에미."], ["It is only to suppose that I have not met you, and I am no worse to-day than I was yesterday.", "그냥 내가 너를 만나지 않았다고만 치면 돼. 그럼 나는 어제보다 오늘이 더 나쁠 것도 없어."], ["It is only to suppose that the night is over and the poor wanderer is on her way.", "그저 밤이 지나가고 가엾은 떠돌이가 다시 제 길을 간다고만 여기면 돼."], ["Don't you remember the song we used to sing in old, dear old days?", "옛날, 그 소중한 옛날에 우리가 함께 부르던 노래를 기억하니?"], ["I have been wandering ever since then--a poor castaway, scorned for being miserable, and insulted because I am alone.", "나는 그때 이후로 줄곧 떠돌아다녔어. 비참하다는 이유로 멸시받고, 혼자라는 이유로 모욕당하는 가엾은 표류자로 말이야."], ["Let me go: my stay here interferes with the plans of this gentleman.\"", "가게 해 줘. 내가 여기 머무는 게 이 신사분의 계획을 방해하니까.\""]], [["\"Indeed it does, madam,\"", "\"정말 그렇습니다, 부인.\""], ["said the Major.", "소령이 말했다."], ["\"If I have any authority in this house--\"", "\"제게 이 집에서 무슨 권한이라도 있다면―\""]], [["\"Authority, none!\"", "\"권한이라니, 전혀 없어요!\""], ["broke out Amelia.", "애밀리아가 발끈했다."], ["\"Rebecca, you stay with me.", "\"레베카, 넌 나와 함께 있어."], ["I won't desert you because you have been persecuted, or insult you because--because Major Dobbin chooses to do so.", "네가 박해받았다고 해서 널 버리지 않을 거고, 도빈 소령님이 그렇게 하기로 마음먹었다고 해서―그렇다고 해서 널 모욕하지도 않을 거야."], ["Come away, dear.\"", "가자, 얘.\""], ["And the two women made towards the door.", "그러고는 두 여자가 문 쪽으로 향했다."]], [["William opened it.", "윌리엄이 문을 열어 주었다."], ["As they were going out, however, he took Amelia's hand and said--\"Will you stay a moment and speak to me?\"", "그러나 그들이 나가려는 참에, 그는 애밀리아의 손을 잡고 말했다. \"잠깐 남아서 저와 이야기 좀 하시겠어요?\""]], [["\"He wishes to speak to you away from me,\"", "\"나 없는 데서 너한테 할 말이 있나 봐.\""], ["said Becky, looking like a martyr.", "베키가 순교자 같은 표정을 지으며 말했다."], ["Amelia gripped her hand in reply.", "애밀리아는 그 대답으로 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥐었다."]], [["\"Upon my honour it is not about you that I am going to speak,\"", "\"맹세컨대 제가 하려는 말은 당신에 관한 게 아닙니다.\""], ["Dobbin said.", "도빈이 말했다."], ["\"Come back, Amelia,\"", "\"돌아오세요, 애밀리아.\""], ["and she came.", "그러자 그녀가 돌아왔다."], ["Dobbin bowed to Mrs. Crawley, as he shut the door upon her.", "도빈은 크롤리 부인을 향해 절을 하고 그녀 뒤로 문을 닫았다."], ["Amelia looked at him, leaning against the glass: her face and her lips were quite white.", "애밀리아는 거울에 기댄 채 그를 바라보았다. 그녀의 얼굴과 입술은 새하얗게 질려 있었다."]], [["\"I was confused when I spoke just now,\"", "\"방금은 제가 흥분해서 말이 헝클어졌습니다.\""], ["the Major said after a pause, \"and I misused the word authority.\"", "소령이 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 말했다. \"그리고 권한이라는 말을 잘못 썼군요.\""]], [["\"You did,\"", "\"그러셨죠.\""], ["said Amelia with her teeth chattering.", "애밀리아가 이를 딱딱 부딪치며 말했다."]], [["\"At least I have claims to be heard,\"", "\"그래도 제 말을 들어 주실 자격은 제게 있습니다.\""], ["Dobbin continued.", "도빈이 말을 이었다."]], [["\"It is generous to remind me of our obligations to you,\"", "\"당신께 진 신세를 제게 상기시키시다니 참 관대하시네요.\""], ["the woman answered.", "그 여인이 대답했다."]], [["\"The claims I mean are those left me by George's father,\"", "\"제가 말하는 자격이란, 조지의 아버님께서 제게 남겨 주신 것입니다.\""], ["William said.", "윌리엄이 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, and you insulted his memory.", "\"그래요, 그런데 당신은 그분의 기억을 모욕했어요."], ["You did yesterday.", "어제 그랬잖아요."], ["You know you did.", "당신도 알잖아요."], ["And I will never forgive you.", "그리고 난 절대 당신을 용서하지 않을 거예요."], ["Never!\"", "절대로!\""], ["said Amelia.", "애밀리아가 말했다."], ["She shot out each little sentence in a tremor of anger and emotion.", "그녀는 분노와 격정에 떨며 짧은 문장 하나하나를 쏘아붙였다."]], [["\"You don't mean that, Amelia?\"", "\"진심은 아니지요, 애밀리아?\""], ["William said sadly.", "윌리엄이 슬프게 말했다."], ["\"You don't mean that these words, uttered in a hurried moment, are to weigh against a whole life's devotion?", "\"다급한 한순간에 내뱉은 그 말들이, 한평생을 바친 헌신보다 더 무겁게 저울에 달릴 거라는 게 진심은 아니겠지요?"], ["I think that George's memory has not been injured by the way in which I have dealt with it, and if we are come to bandying reproaches, I at least merit none from his widow and the mother of his son.", "제가 조지의 기억을 다루어 온 방식 때문에 그 기억이 손상되지는 않았다고 봅니다. 그리고 우리가 서로 책망을 주고받는 지경에 이르렀다 해도, 적어도 저는 그의 미망인이자 그의 아들의 어머니에게서 책망받을 일은 없습니다."], ["Reflect, afterwards when--when you are at leisure, and your conscience will withdraw this accusation.", "나중에, 당신이 한가해질 때 다시 생각해 보세요. 그러면 당신의 양심이 이 비난을 거두어들일 겁니다."], ["It does even now.\"", "지금도 이미 거두고 있고요.\""], ["Amelia held down her head.", "애밀리아는 고개를 숙였다."]], [["\"It is not that speech of yesterday,\"", "\"당신을 움직이는 건 어제의 그 말이 아닙니다.\""], ["he continued, \"which moves you.", "그가 말을 이었다."], ["That is but the pretext, Amelia, or I have loved you and watched you for fifteen years in vain.", "\"그건 한낱 구실일 뿐이에요, 애밀리아. 아니라면, 제가 십오 년 동안 당신을 사랑하고 지켜본 것이 헛일이었던 거지요."], ["Have I not learned in that time to read all your feelings and look into your thoughts?", "제가 그 세월 동안 당신의 온갖 감정을 읽고 당신의 생각을 들여다보는 법을 배우지 못했겠습니까?"], ["I know what your heart is capable of: it can cling faithfully to a recollection and cherish a fancy, but it can't feel such an attachment as mine deserves to mate with, and such as I would have won from a woman more generous than you.", "저는 당신의 마음이 무엇을 할 수 있는지 압니다. 그 마음은 어떤 추억에 충실히 매달리고 한 환상을 소중히 품을 수는 있지만, 제 사랑이 짝이 될 만한 그런 애정은, 그리고 제가 당신보다 더 너그러운 여인에게서라면 얻어 냈을 그런 애정은 느끼지 못합니다."], ["No, you are not worthy of the love which I have devoted to you.", "아니요, 당신은 제가 바친 사랑을 받을 자격이 없습니다."], ["I knew all along that the prize I had set my life on was not worth the winning; that I was a fool, with fond fancies, too, bartering away my all of truth and ardour against your little feeble remnant of love.", "제가 평생을 걸었던 그 상이 얻을 만한 가치가 없다는 걸, 그리고 제가 어리석은 자라서, 그것도 부질없는 환상에 빠진 채, 제 진실과 열정의 전부를 당신의 그 가냘프게 남은 사랑 한 줌과 맞바꿔 왔다는 걸 저는 줄곧 알고 있었습니다."], ["I will bargain no more: I withdraw.", "더는 흥정하지 않겠습니다. 물러나겠어요."], ["I find no fault with you.", "당신을 탓하지는 않습니다."], ["You are very good-natured, and have done your best, but you couldn't--you couldn't reach up to the height of the attachment which I bore you, and which a loftier soul than yours might have been proud to share.", "당신은 무척 마음씨가 곱고, 최선을 다했지요. 하지만 당신은 끝내, 끝내 제가 당신에게 품었던 그 애정의 높이까지는, 당신보다 더 고결한 영혼이라면 함께 나눈 것을 자랑스러워했을 그 애정의 높이까지는 닿지 못했습니다."], ["Good-bye, Amelia!", "잘 있어요, 애밀리아!"], ["I have watched your struggle.", "저는 당신의 갈등을 지켜봐 왔습니다."], ["Let it end.", "이제 그만 끝냅시다."], ["We are both weary of it.\"", "우리 둘 다 그것에 지쳤으니까요.\""]], [["Amelia stood scared and silent as William thus suddenly broke the chain by which she held him and declared his independence and superiority.", "윌리엄이 이렇게 별안간, 그녀가 그를 붙들어 매고 있던 사슬을 끊어 버리고 자신의 독립과 우위를 선언하자, 애밀리아는 겁에 질려 말없이 서 있었다."], ["He had placed himself at her feet so long that the poor little woman had been accustomed to trample upon him.", "그가 너무도 오랫동안 그녀의 발치에 엎드려 있었던 탓에, 그 가엾은 자그마한 여인은 그를 짓밟는 데 익숙해져 있었던 것이다."], ["She didn't wish to marry him, but she wished to keep him.", "그녀는 그와 결혼하기를 바라지는 않았지만, 그를 곁에 붙들어 두기는 바랐다."], ["She wished to give him nothing, but that he should give her all.", "그녀는 그에게 아무것도 주지 않으면서, 그는 자기에게 모든 것을 주기를 바랐다."], ["It is a bargain not unfrequently levied in love.", "사랑에서 드물지 않게 매겨지는 거래다."]], [["William's sally had quite broken and cast her down.", "윌리엄의 일격은 그녀를 완전히 무너뜨리고 짓눌러 버렸다."], ["HER assault was long since over and beaten back.", "그녀의 공세는 진작에 끝나 버렸고 격퇴당한 터였다."]], [["\"Am I to understand then, that you are going--away, William?\"", "\"그럼 당신이 떠난다는, 멀리 가 버린다는 말로 알아들으면 되나요, 윌리엄?\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["He gave a sad laugh.", "그는 서글픈 웃음을 지었다."], ["\"I went once before,\"", "\"전에 한 번 떠났다가,\""], ["he said, \"and came back after twelve years.", "그가 말했다. \"십이 년 만에 돌아왔지요."], ["We were young then, Amelia.", "그때 우린 젊었어요, 애밀리아."], ["Good-bye.", "잘 있어요."], ["I have spent enough of my life at this play.\"", "이 연극에 제 인생을 충분히 써 버렸으니까요.\""]], [["Whilst they had been talking, the door into Mrs.", "그들이 이야기를 나누는 동안, 오스본 부인 방으로 통하는 문이 아주 조금 열려 있었다."], ["Osborne's room had opened ever so little; indeed, Becky had kept a hold of the handle and had turned it on the instant when Dobbin quitted it, and she heard every word of the conversation that had passed between these two.", "사실 베키는 손잡이를 잡고 있다가 도빈이 그 방을 나서는 순간 그것을 돌렸던 것이고, 그래서 두 사람 사이에 오간 대화를 한마디도 빠짐없이 들었다. \"저 남자는 어쩌면 저토록 고결한 마음을 지녔을까.\""], ["\"What a noble heart that man has,\"", "그녀는 생각했다."], ["she thought, \"and how shamefully that woman plays with it!\"", "\"그런데 저 여자는 어찌 저토록 부끄러운 줄도 모르고 그 마음을 가지고 노는 걸까!\""], ["She admired Dobbin; she bore him no rancour for the part he had taken against her.", "그녀는 도빈에게 감탄했다. 그가 자기를 거스르는 편에 섰다고 해서 그에게 앙심을 품지는 않았다."], ["It was an open move in the game, and played fairly.", "그것은 게임에서 드러내 놓고 둔 한 수였고, 정정당당하게 둔 것이었다."], ["\"Ah!\"", "\"아!\""], ["she thought, \"if I could have had such a husband as that--a man with a heart and brains too!", "그녀는 생각했다. \"내가 저런 남편을 가질 수 있었더라면―가슴도 있고 머리도 있는 남자를!"], ["I would not have minded his large feet\"; and running into her room, she absolutely bethought herself of something, and wrote him a note, beseeching him to stop for a few days--not to think of going--and that she could serve him with A.", "발이 좀 크다 한들 개의치 않았을 텐데.\" 그러고는 자기 방으로 달려 들어가, 정말로 무언가를 떠올리고는 그에게 쪽지를 써서, 며칠만 머물러 달라고―떠날 생각일랑 말아 달라고―그리고 자기가 A에 관해 그를 도울 수 있다고 간청했다."]], [["The parting was over.", "작별은 끝났다."], ["Once more poor William walked to the door and was gone; and the little widow, the author of all this work, had her will, and had won her victory, and was left to enjoy it as she best might.", "또 한 번 가엾은 윌리엄은 문 쪽으로 걸어가 사라졌다. 그리고 이 모든 일을 꾸민 장본인인 자그마한 미망인은 제 뜻을 이루었고, 승리를 거두었으며, 그것을 제멋대로 누리도록 남겨졌다."], ["Let the ladies envy her triumph.", "부디 숙녀들이여, 그녀의 승리를 부러워하시라."]], [["At the romantic hour of dinner, Mr. Georgy made his appearance and again remarked the absence of \"Old Dob.\"", "저녁 식사라는 낭만적인 시각에 조지 도련님이 모습을 드러내고는, 또다시 \"늙은 도브\"가 보이지 않음을 알아차렸다."], ["The meal was eaten in silence by the party.", "식사는 일행이 말없이 했다."], ["Jos's appetite not being diminished, but Emmy taking nothing at all.", "조스의 식욕은 줄지 않았지만, 에미는 아무것도 입에 대지 않았다."]], [["After the meal, Georgy was lolling in the cushions of the old window, a large window, with three sides of glass abutting from the gable, and commanding on one side the market-place, where the Elephant is, his mother being busy hard by, when he remarked symptoms of movement at the Major's house on the other side of the street.", "식사가 끝난 뒤, 조지는 오래된 창가의 방석에 비스듬히 기대 있었는데―그 큰 창은 박공에서 튀어나온 세 면이 유리로 된 것으로, 한쪽으로는 엘리펀트가 있는 장터가 내려다보였다―어머니가 바로 옆에서 부산을 떨고 있을 때, 길 건너편 소령의 집에 무언가 움직이는 기미가 있음을 알아차렸다."]], [["\"Hullo!\"", "\"어!\""], ["said he, \"there's Dob's trap--they are bringing it out of the court-yard.\"", "그가 말했다. \"도브의 마차네―마당에서 끌어내고 있어요.\""], ["The \"trap\" in question was a carriage which the Major had bought for six pounds sterling, and about which they used to rally him a good deal.", "문제의 그 \"마차\"는 소령이 육 파운드에 산 것으로, 다들 그것을 두고 그를 곧잘 놀려 대곤 했다."]], [["Emmy gave a little start, but said nothing.", "에미는 흠칫 놀랐지만 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["\"Hullo!\"", "\"어!\""], ["Georgy continued, \"there's Francis coming out with the portmanteaus, and Kunz, the one-eyed postilion, coming down the market with three schimmels.", "조지가 말을 이었다. \"프랜시스가 여행 가방을 들고 나오고, 외눈박이 역마차 마부 쿤츠가 백마 세 마리를 끌고 장터를 내려오네."], ["Look at his boots and yellow jacket--ain't he a rum one?", "저 장화랑 노란 윗도리 좀 봐요. 진짜 별난 사람 아니에요?"], ["Why--they're putting the horses to Dob's carriage.", "어라―도브의 마차에 말을 매고 있어요."], ["Is he going anywhere?\"", "어디 가시려나?\""]], [["\"Yes,\"", "\"그래.\""], ["said Emmy, \"he is going on a journey.\"", "에미가 말했다. \"여행을 떠나신단다.\""]], [["\"Going on a journey; and when is he coming back?\"", "\"여행을 떠나신다고요. 그럼 언제 돌아오시는데요?\""]], [["\"He is--not coming back,\"", "\"그분은―돌아오지 않으셔.\""], ["answered Emmy.", "에미가 대답했다."]], [["\"Not coming back!\"", "\"돌아오지 않으신다고요!\""], ["cried out Georgy, jumping up.", "조지가 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다."], ["\"Stay here, sir,\"", "\"거기 가만있어.\""], ["roared out Jos.", "조스가 버럭 소리쳤다."], ["\"Stay, Georgy,\"", "\"가만있어, 조지.\""], ["said his mother with a very sad face.", "어머니가 몹시 슬픈 얼굴로 말했다."], ["The boy stopped, kicked about the room, jumped up and down from the window-seat with his knees, and showed every symptom of uneasiness and curiosity.", "아이는 멈춰 서서 방 안을 발로 차며 돌아다니다가, 무릎으로 창가 자리에서 폴짝폴짝 오르내렸고, 온갖 불안과 호기심의 기색을 다 드러내 보였다."]], [["The horses were put to.", "말이 매어졌다."], ["The baggage was strapped on.", "짐도 끈으로 단단히 동여매졌다."], ["Francis came out with his master's sword, cane, and umbrella tied up together, and laid them in the well, and his desk and old tin cocked-hat case, which he placed under the seat.", "프랜시스가 주인의 검과 지팡이와 우산을 한데 묶어 들고 나와 마차 바닥에 놓고, 그의 책상과 낡은 양철 삼각모 상자를 좌석 밑에 넣었다."], ["Francis brought out the stained old blue cloak lined with red camlet, which had wrapped the owner up any time these fifteen years, and had manchen Sturm erlebt, as a favourite song of those days said.", "프랜시스는 붉은 낙타 모직으로 안을 댄, 얼룩진 낡은 푸른색 외투를 들고 나왔는데, 그것은 지난 십오 년 내내 그 주인을 감싸 주었고, 그 시절 즐겨 부르던 어느 노래의 가사처럼 \"숱한 풍파를 겪어 낸\" 것이었다."], ["It had been new for the campaign of Waterloo and had covered George and William after the night of Quatre Bras.", "그 외투는 워털루 원정 때 새것이었고, 카트르브라의 밤이 지난 뒤 조지와 윌리엄을 덮어 주었던 것이다."]], [["Old Burcke, the landlord of the lodgings, came out, then Francis, with more packages--final packages--then Major William--Burcke wanted to kiss him.", "숙소 주인인 늙은 부르케가 나오고, 이어서 프랜시스가 짐을 더 들고―마지막 짐이었다―나오고, 그다음에 윌리엄 소령이 나왔다. 부르케는 그에게 입을 맞추고 싶어 했다."], ["The Major was adored by all people with whom he had to do.", "소령은 그와 상대한 모든 이에게 흠모를 받았다."], ["It was with difficulty he could escape from this demonstration of attachment.", "이런 애정의 표시에서 그가 벗어나기란 여간 어려운 일이 아니었다."]], [["\"By Jove, I will go!\"", "\"맙소사, 나 갈래요!\""], ["screamed out George.", "조지가 비명을 질렀다."], ["\"Give him this,\"", "\"이걸 그분께 드리렴.\""], ["said Becky, quite interested, and put a paper into the boy's hand.", "베키가 자못 관심 어린 표정으로 말하며, 아이의 손에 종이 한 장을 쥐여 주었다."], ["He had rushed down the stairs and flung across the street in a minute--the yellow postilion was cracking his whip gently.", "아이는 순식간에 계단을 뛰어 내려가 길을 가로질러 달려갔다. 노란 옷의 마부는 채찍을 살살 휘두르고 있었다."]], [["William had got into the carriage, released from the embraces of his landlord.", "윌리엄은 숙소 주인의 포옹에서 풀려나 마차에 올라타 있었다."], ["George bounded in afterwards, and flung his arms round the Major's neck (as they saw from the window), and began asking him multiplied questions.", "조지는 뒤따라 풀쩍 뛰어올라 소령의 목을 두 팔로 끌어안고는(창문에서 그들이 보았듯이), 온갖 질문을 쏟아 내기 시작했다."], ["Then he felt in his waistcoat pocket and gave him a note.", "그러고는 조끼 주머니를 더듬어 그에게 쪽지 하나를 건넸다."], ["William seized at it rather eagerly, he opened it trembling, but instantly his countenance changed, and he tore the paper in two and dropped it out of the carriage.", "윌리엄은 다소 다급하게 그것을 낚아채 떨리는 손으로 펼쳤지만, 이내 그 얼굴빛이 변하더니, 종이를 둘로 찢어 마차 밖으로 떨어뜨렸다."], ["He kissed Georgy on the head, and the boy got out, doubling his fists into his eyes, and with the aid of Francis.", "그는 조지의 머리에 입을 맞추었고, 아이는 두 주먹으로 눈을 비비며 프랜시스의 도움을 받아 마차에서 내렸다."], ["He lingered with his hand on the panel.", "아이는 마차 옆판에 손을 얹은 채 머뭇거렸다."], ["Fort, Schwager!", "출발이오, 형씨!"], ["The yellow postilion cracked his whip prodigiously, up sprang Francis to the box, away went the schimmels, and Dobbin with his head on his breast.", "노란 옷의 마부가 채찍을 어마어마하게 휘둘렀고, 프랜시스가 마부석으로 훌쩍 뛰어올랐으며, 백마들이 내달리기 시작했고, 도빈은 고개를 가슴에 묻고 있었다."], ["He never looked up as they passed under Amelia's window, and Georgy, left alone in the street, burst out crying in the face of all the crowd.", "마차가 애밀리아의 창문 아래를 지날 때 그는 끝내 고개를 들지 않았다. 그리고 거리에 홀로 남겨진 조지는 모여든 군중이 다 보는 앞에서 그만 울음을 터뜨리고 말았다."]], [["Emmy's maid heard him howling again during the night and brought him some preserved apricots to console him.", "에미의 하녀는 밤중에도 그가 다시 흐느끼는 소리를 듣고는, 그를 달래 주려고 설탕에 절인 살구를 가져다주었다."], ["She mingled her lamentations with his.", "그녀는 자신의 탄식을 그의 탄식에 보탰다."], ["All the poor, all the humble, all honest folks, all good men who knew him, loved that kind-hearted and simple gentleman.", "그를 아는 가난한 이들, 미천한 이들, 정직한 이들, 그리고 모든 선량한 사람들이 그 마음씨 곱고 소박한 신사를 사랑했다."]], [["As for Emmy, had she not done her duty?", "에미로 말할 것 같으면, 그녀는 자기 의무를 다하지 않았던가?"], ["She had her picture of George for a consolation.", "그녀에게는 위안으로 삼을 조지의 초상화가 있었다."]], [["Which Contains Births, Marriages, and Deaths\nWhatever Becky's private plan might be by which Dobbin's true love was to be crowned with success, the little woman thought that the secret might keep, and indeed, being by no means so much interested about anybody's welfare as about her own, she had a great number of things pertaining to herself to consider, and which concerned her a great deal more than Major Dobbin's happiness in this life.", "도빈의 진실한 사랑이 마침내 성취의 영광을 누리게 될, 베키 나름의 은밀한 계획이 무엇이었든 간에, 이 자그마한 여인은 그 비밀을 당분간 묻어 두어도 좋다고 여겼다. 게다가 다른 누구의 안녕에도 자기 자신의 안녕만큼 관심을 둘 리 없는 그녀였으니, 자신과 관련된 따져 볼 일이 수두룩했고, 그것들은 이승에서 도빈 소령이 누릴 행복 따위보다 그녀에게 훨씬 더 절실한 문제였던 것이다."]], [["She found herself suddenly and unexpectedly in snug comfortable quarters, surrounded by friends, kindness, and good-natured simple people such as she had not met with for many a long day; and, wanderer as she was by force and inclination, there were moments when rest was pleasant to her.", "그녀는 뜻밖에도 별안간 아늑하고 편안한 거처에 들어앉은 자신을 발견했다. 오래도록 만나 보지 못한 부류의 벗들, 다정함, 그리고 선량하고 꾸밈없는 사람들에 둘러싸인 것이다."], ["As the most hardened Arab that ever careered across the desert over the hump of a dromedary likes to repose sometimes under the date-trees by the water, or to come into the cities, walk into the bazaars, refresh himself in the baths, and say his prayers in the mosques, before he goes out again marauding, so Jos's tents and pilau were pleasant to this little Ishmaelite.", "천성으로 보나 처지로 보나 방랑자였던 그녀였지만, 휴식이 달콤하게 느껴지는 순간들이 있었다. 사막을 가로질러 단봉낙타의 혹 위에 올라타고 내달리던 가장 모진 아랍인조차도 이따금 물가의 대추야자나무 아래서 쉬어 가기를, 혹은 도시로 들어가 시장통을 거닐고 목욕탕에서 몸을 풀며 모스크에서 기도를 올린 뒤에야 다시 약탈하러 나서기를 좋아하는 법이다."], ["She picketed her steed, hung up her weapons, and warmed herself comfortably by his fire.", "그와 마찬가지로 조스의 천막과 필라프는 이 자그마한 이스마엘의 후예에게 더없이 흐뭇한 것이었다. 그녀는 말을 매어 두고 무기를 걸어 놓고는 그의 화롯가에서 편안히 몸을 녹였다."], ["The halt in that roving, restless life was inexpressibly soothing and pleasant to her.", "그 떠돌이의, 안식 없는 삶에서 맞은 그 멈춤이 그녀에게는 말할 수 없이 위안이 되고 흐뭇한 것이었다."]], [["So, pleased herself, she tried with all her might to please everybody; and we know that she was eminent and successful as a practitioner in the art of giving pleasure.", "그리하여 스스로 흡족해진 그녀는, 온 힘을 다해 모두를 기쁘게 하려 애썼다. 그리고 우리가 알다시피, 그녀는 즐거움을 베푸는 기술에 관한 한 발군의 성공한 명인이었다."], ["As for Jos, even in that little interview in the garret at the Elephant Inn, she had found means to win back a great deal of his good-will.", "조스로 말할 것 같으면, 코끼리 여관 다락방에서의 그 짧은 만남에서조차 그녀는 이미 그의 호의를 상당 부분 되찾을 방도를 찾아낸 터였다."], ["In the course of a week, the civilian was her sworn slave and frantic admirer.", "일주일이 지나자 이 문관 나리는 그녀의 충실한 노예이자 열렬한 숭배자가 되었다."], ["He didn't go to sleep after dinner, as his custom was in the much less lively society of Amelia.", "그는 애밀리아와 함께하던 훨씬 덜 활기찬 자리에서 늘 그러했듯 식후에 잠들어 버리는 법이 없었다."], ["He drove out with Becky in his open carriage.", "그는 베키를 태우고 자신의 무개 마차를 몰고 나갔다."], ["He asked little parties and invented festivities to do her honour.", "그녀를 기리는 자그마한 모임을 청하고 온갖 여흥을 궁리해 냈다."]], [["Tapeworm, the Charge d'Affaires, who had abused her so cruelly, came to dine with Jos, and then came every day to pay his respects to Becky.", "그토록 모질게 그녀를 헐뜯었던 대리공사 테이프웜은 조스와 식사를 함께하러 왔고, 그 뒤로는 날마다 베키에게 경의를 표하러 찾아왔다."], ["Poor Emmy, who was never very talkative, and more glum and silent than ever after Dobbin's departure, was quite forgotten when this superior genius made her appearance.", "본디 말수가 적었던 데다 도빈이 떠난 뒤로 더더욱 시무룩하고 말이 없어진 가엾은 에미는, 이 빼어난 재주꾼이 등장하자 까맣게 잊힌 신세가 되었다."], ["The French Minister was as much charmed with her as his English rival.", "프랑스 공사도 영국인 적수 못지않게 그녀에게 매료되었다."], ["The German ladies, never particularly squeamish as regards morals, especially in English people, were delighted with the cleverness and wit of Mrs.", "풍속에 관한 한, 특히 영국인의 풍속에 관한 한 결코 까다롭지 않았던 독일 부인들은 오스본 부인의 이 매력적인 벗이 보여 주는 영민함과 재치에 흠뻑 빠졌다."], ["Osborne's charming friend, and though she did not ask to go to Court, yet the most august and Transparent Personages there heard of her fascinations and were quite curious to know her.", "그리고 그녀가 궁정 출입을 청한 것도 아니었건만, 그곳의 더없이 존엄하고 고귀하신 분들까지도 그녀의 매혹에 관한 이야기를 전해 듣고는 그녀를 알고 싶어 자못 궁금해했다."], ["When it became known that she was noble, of an ancient English family, that her husband was a Colonel of the Guard, Excellenz and Governor of an island, only separated from his lady by one of those trifling differences which are of little account in a country where Werther is still read and the Wahlverwandtschaften of Goethe is considered an edifying moral book, nobody thought of refusing to receive her in the very highest society of the little Duchy; and the ladies were even more ready to call her du and to swear eternal friendship for her than they had been to bestow the same inestimable benefits upon Amelia.", "그녀가 명문 출신의 귀족이며, 그 남편이 근위대 대령이요 각하이자 어느 섬의 총독이며, 다만 베르테르가 여전히 읽히고 괴테의 친화력이 교훈적인 도덕서로 여겨지는 나라에서는 대수롭지 않게 여겨지는 그런 사소한 불화 하나로 부인과 떨어져 지낼 뿐이라는 사실이 알려지자, 이 자그마한 공국의 더없이 상류한 사교계에서 그녀를 맞아들이기를 마다할 이는 아무도 없었다. 부인들은 애밀리아에게 그 더없이 값진 은혜를 베풀던 때보다도 한층 더 기꺼이 그녀를 '너'라고 부르고 영원한 우정을 맹세하려 들었다."], ["Love and Liberty are interpreted by those simple Germans in a way which honest folks in Yorkshire and Somersetshire little understand, and a lady might, in some philosophic and civilized towns, be divorced ever so many times from her respective husbands and keep her character in society.", "사랑과 자유라는 것을, 저 꾸밈없는 독일인들은 요크셔나 서머싯셔의 정직한 사람들로서는 도무지 이해할 수 없는 방식으로 해석했으니, 어떤 철학적이고 개화된 도시에서는 부인이 제 남편들과 몇 번이고 이혼을 거듭하고도 사교계에서의 평판을 고스란히 지킬 수 있는 것이었다."], ["Jos's house never was so pleasant since he had a house of his own as Rebecca caused it to be.", "조스의 집은 그가 제 집을 갖게 된 이래로, 레베카가 만들어 낸 만큼 즐거웠던 적이 없었다."], ["She sang, she played, she laughed, she talked in two or three languages, she brought everybody to the house, and she made Jos believe that it was his own great social talents and wit which gathered the society of the place round about him.", "그녀는 노래하고, 연주하고, 웃고, 두세 개 나라말로 이야기하고, 온갖 사람을 그 집으로 불러들였으며, 그러면서도 그 고장의 사교계를 그의 주위로 끌어모은 것이 다름 아닌 조스 자신의 빼어난 사교적 재능과 재치 덕분이라고 그가 믿게끔 만들었다."]], [["As for Emmy, who found herself not in the least mistress of her own house, except when the bills were to be paid, Becky soon discovered the way to soothe and please her.", "청구서를 치러야 할 때를 빼고는 제 집의 안주인 노릇이라곤 조금도 하지 못하던 에미로 말하면, 베키는 곧 그녀를 달래고 기쁘게 할 방도를 찾아냈다."], ["She talked to her perpetually about Major Dobbin sent about his business, and made no scruple of declaring her admiration for that excellent, high-minded gentleman, and of telling Emmy that she had behaved most cruelly regarding him.", "그녀는 쫓겨나다시피 떠나간 도빈 소령 이야기를 줄곧 에미에게 들려주었고, 그 훌륭하고 고결한 신사에 대한 자신의 흠모를 거리낌 없이 드러내며, 에미가 그를 대한 처사가 더없이 모질었다고 말하기를 서슴지 않았다."], ["Emmy defended her conduct and showed that it was dictated only by the purest religious principles; that a woman once, &c., and to such an angel as him whom she had had the good fortune to marry, was married forever; but she had no objection to hear the Major praised as much as ever Becky chose to praise him, and indeed, brought the conversation round to the Dobbin subject a score of times every day.", "에미는 자신의 행동을 변호하며, 그것은 오직 가장 순수한 종교적 원칙에서 비롯된 것임을, 여자란 한번 어쩌고저쩌고하면, 더구나 자신이 운 좋게 결혼할 수 있었던 그이 같은 천사에게는, 영원히 결혼한 몸이라는 것을 역설했다. 그러나 베키가 아무리 그 소령을 칭송한다 해도 그것을 듣는 데에는 조금도 거리낌이 없었고, 오히려 하루에도 스무 번씩 화제를 도빈 쪽으로 끌고 오는 것이었다."]], [["Means were easily found to win the favour of Georgy and the servants.", "조지와 하인들의 호감을 사는 방도는 손쉽게 찾아냈다."], ["Amelia's maid, it has been said, was heart and soul in favour of the generous Major.", "애밀리아의 하녀가 그 너그러운 소령의 더없이 열렬한 편이었다는 것은 앞서 말한 바 있다."], ["Having at first disliked Becky for being the means of dismissing him from the presence of her mistress, she was reconciled to Mrs. Crawley subsequently, because the latter became William's most ardent admirer and champion.", "처음에는 소령을 안주인 곁에서 내쫓는 빌미가 되었다 하여 베키를 못마땅해했으나, 나중에는 크롤리 부인과 화해했으니, 베키가 윌리엄의 더없이 열렬한 숭배자이자 옹호자가 되었기 때문이다."], ["And in those nightly conclaves in which the two ladies indulged after their parties, and while Miss Payne was \"brushing their 'airs,\" as she called the yellow locks of the one and the soft brown tresses of the other, this girl always put in her word for that dear good gentleman Major Dobbin.", "그리하여 모임을 마친 뒤 두 부인이 즐기던 밤마다의 밀담에서, 페인 양이 한 사람의 노란 머리채와 또 한 사람의 보드라운 갈색 머리채를 두고 자기 말로 \"머리 빗질\"을 해 주는 동안, 이 하녀는 늘 그 다정하고 선량한 신사 도빈 소령을 위해 한마디 거들었다."], ["Her advocacy did not make Amelia angry any more than Rebecca's admiration of him.", "그녀의 두둔이 애밀리아를 화나게 하지 않은 것은, 레베카가 그를 흠모하는 것이 화나게 하지 않은 것과 마찬가지였다."], ["She made George write to him constantly and persisted in sending Mamma's kind love in a postscript.", "그녀는 조지에게 끊임없이 소령에게 편지를 쓰게 했고, 추신으로 \"엄마의 다정한 사랑\"을 전하는 것을 잊지 않았다."], ["And as she looked at her husband's portrait of nights, it no longer reproached her--perhaps she reproached it, now William was gone.", "그리고 밤마다 남편의 초상을 바라볼 때면, 그것은 더 이상 그녀를 책망하지 않았다. 어쩌면 이제 윌리엄이 떠나 버린 지금, 그녀가 그것을 책망하고 있었는지도 모른다."]], [["Emmy was not very happy after her heroic sacrifice.", "에미는 그 영웅적인 희생을 치르고도 그다지 행복하지 않았다."], ["She was very distraite, nervous, silent, and ill to please.", "그녀는 몹시 넋이 나가 있었고, 신경이 곤두서고, 말이 없고, 비위 맞추기 어려운 사람이 되었다."], ["The family had never known her so peevish. She grew pale and ill.", "가족들도 그녀가 그토록 까탈스러운 것은 일찍이 본 적이 없었다."], ["She used to try to sing certain songs (\"Einsam bin ich nicht alleine,\"", "그녀는 창백해지고 병들어 갔다."], ["was one of them, that tender love-song of Weber's which in old-fashioned days, young ladies, and when you were scarcely born, showed that those who lived before you knew too how to love and to sing) certain songs, I say, to which the Major was partial; and as she warbled them in the twilight in the drawing-room, she would break off in the midst of the song, and walk into her neighbouring apartment, and there, no doubt, take refuge in the miniature of her husband.", "그녀는 어떤 노래들을 (\"Einsam bin ich nicht alleine\"도 그중 하나였는데, 이는 베버의 그 애틋한 사랑 노래로, 옛 시절에—젊은 숙녀 여러분, 여러분이 채 태어나기도 전에—여러분보다 먼저 살았던 이들 역시 사랑할 줄도 노래할 줄도 알았음을 보여 주는 곡이었다), 그러니까 소령이 즐겨 듣던 어떤 노래들을 부르려 애쓰곤 했다. 그리고 응접실에서 땅거미 지는 가운데 그 노래들을 흥얼거리다가는, 노래 한가운데서 뚝 그치고 옆방으로 걸어 들어가서는, 거기서 의심할 바 없이 남편의 작은 초상화 속으로 피신하는 것이었다."]], [["Some books still subsisted, after Dobbin's departure, with his name written in them; a German dictionary, for instance, with \"William Dobbin, --th Reg.,\"", "도빈이 떠난 뒤에도, 그의 이름이 적힌 책 몇 권이 아직 남아 있었다."], ["in the fly-leaf; a guide-book with his initials; and one or two other volumes which belonged to the Major.", "이를테면 면지에 \"윌리엄 도빈, 제××연대\"라고 적힌 독일어 사전 한 권, 그의 머리글자가 새겨진 안내서 한 권, 그리고 소령의 것이었던 한두 권의 책이 더 있었다."], ["Emmy cleared these away and put them on the drawers, where she placed her work-box, her desk, her Bible, and prayer-book, under the pictures of the two Georges.", "에미는 이것들을 치워 서랍장 위에 얹어 두었는데, 거기에는 두 조지의 초상화 아래로 자신의 반짇고리, 문갑, 성경, 기도서를 놓아둔 터였다."], ["And the Major, on going away, having left his gloves behind him, it is a fact that Georgy, rummaging his mother's desk some time afterwards, found the gloves neatly folded up and put away in what they call the secret-drawers of the desk.", "그리고 소령이 떠나면서 장갑을 두고 갔는데, 한참 뒤에 조지가 어머니의 문갑을 뒤지다가 그 장갑이 가지런히 접힌 채 이른바 문갑의 비밀 서랍에 넣어져 있는 것을 발견했으니, 이것은 엄연한 사실이다."]], [["Not caring for society, and moping there a great deal, Emmy's chief pleasure in the summer evenings was to take long walks with Georgy (during which Rebecca was left to the society of Mr.", "사교 모임에는 관심이 없어 그곳에서 무던히도 시름에 잠겨 지내던 에미에게, 여름 저녁의 가장 큰 낙은 조지와 함께 긴 산책을 나가는 것이었다(그동안 레베카는 조지프 씨의 말동무로 남겨졌다)."], ["Joseph), and then the mother and son used to talk about the Major in a way which even made the boy smile.", "그러고는 모자와 아들이 소령 이야기를 나누었는데, 그 방식이 어찌나 극진했던지 소년조차 미소를 지을 정도였다."], ["She told him that she thought Major William was the best man in all the world--the gentlest and the kindest, the bravest and the humblest.", "그녀는 아들에게 윌리엄 소령이야말로 온 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 사람이라고, 가장 온화하고 가장 다정하며, 가장 용감하고 가장 겸손한 사람이라고 말했다."], ["Over and over again she told him how they owed everything which they possessed in the world to that kind friend's benevolent care of them; how he had befriended them all through their poverty and misfortunes; watched over them when nobody cared for them; how all his comrades admired him though he never spoke of his own gallant actions; how Georgy's father trusted him beyond all other men, and had been constantly befriended by the good William.", "그녀는 자기네가 이 세상에서 가진 모든 것이 그 다정한 벗의 자애로운 보살핌 덕분임을, 그가 가난과 불운의 세월 내내 자기네를 도와주었음을, 아무도 자기네를 거들떠보지 않을 때 자기네를 지켜 주었음을, 그가 결코 제 용맹한 행적을 입에 올리지 않았는데도 동료들이 모두 그를 우러러보았음을, 조지의 아버지가 다른 어느 누구보다도 그를 신뢰했고 늘 그 선량한 윌리엄의 도움을 받았음을, 거듭하고 또 거듭하여 들려주었다."], ["\"Why, when your papa was a little boy,\"", "\"그래, 네 아빠가 어린 소년이었을 때,\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, \"he often told me that it was William who defended him against a tyrant at the school where they were; and their friendship never ceased from that day until the last, when your dear father fell.\"", "\"아빠는 둘이 함께 다니던 학교에서 어떤 폭군 같은 아이에게서 자기를 지켜 준 게 윌리엄이었다고 종종 내게 이야기했단다. 그리고 두 사람의 우정은 그날부터 네 사랑하는 아버지가 전사한 마지막 그날까지 결코 끊이지 않았지.\""]], [["\"Did Dobbin kill the man who killed Papa?\"", "\"도빈 아저씨가 아빠를 죽인 사람을 죽였나요?\""], ["Georgy said.", "조지가 말했다."], ["\"I'm sure he did, or he would if he could have caught him, wouldn't he, Mother?", "\"틀림없이 죽였을 거예요, 아니면 잡을 수만 있었다면 죽였을 거예요, 그렇죠, 엄마?"], ["When I'm in the Army, won't I hate the French?--that's all.\"", "제가 군대에 가면 프랑스 놈들을 미워하지 않고 배기겠어요? 그뿐이에요.\""]], [["In such colloquies the mother and the child passed a great deal of their time together.", "이런 대화로 모자는 함께하는 많은 시간을 보냈다."], ["The artless woman had made a confidant of the boy.", "그 천진한 여인은 소년을 마음을 터놓는 벗으로 삼은 것이다."], ["He was as much William's friend as everybody else who knew him well.", "그를 잘 아는 다른 모든 이들과 마찬가지로, 소년 또한 윌리엄의 벗이었다."]], [["By the way, Mrs. Becky, not to be behind hand in sentiment, had got a miniature too hanging up in her room, to the surprise and amusement of most people, and the delight of the original, who was no other than our friend Jos.", "그런데 말이 나온 김에 덧붙이자면, 감상에서 뒤지지 않으려는 베키 부인 또한 자기 방에 작은 초상화 하나를 걸어 두었으니, 대부분의 사람들은 놀라워하며 재미있어했고, 그 주인공은 더없이 기뻐했다."], ["On her first coming to favour the Sedleys with a visit, the little woman, who had arrived with a remarkably small shabby kit, was perhaps ashamed of the meanness of her trunks and bandboxes, and often spoke with great respect about her baggage left behind at Leipzig, which she must have from that city.", "그 주인공이란 다름 아닌 우리의 벗 조스였다. 처음 세들리 일가를 찾아와 신세를 지게 되었을 때, 유난히 자그맣고 초라한 짐을 들고 온 이 자그마한 여인은 어쩌면 자기 트렁크며 모자 상자가 보잘것없는 것이 부끄러웠던지, 라이프치히에 두고 와 그 도시에서 가져와야 한다는 짐 이야기를 자못 거창하게 늘어놓곤 했다."], ["When a traveller talks to you perpetually about the splendour of his luggage, which he does not happen to have with him, my son, beware of that traveller!", "어느 여행자가 정작 지금 몸에 지니고 있지도 않은 제 짐의 휘황함을 그대에게 줄곧 떠벌리거든, 내 아들아, 그 여행자를 경계하라!"], ["He is, ten to one, an impostor.", "십중팔구 그는 사기꾼이다."]], [["Neither Jos nor Emmy knew this important maxim.", "조스도 에미도 이 중요한 격언을 알지 못했다."], ["It seemed to them of no consequence whether Becky had a quantity of very fine clothes in invisible trunks; but as her present supply was exceedingly shabby, Emmy supplied her out of her own stores, or took her to the best milliner in the town and there fitted her out.", "두 사람에게는 베키가 보이지 않는 트렁크 속에 아주 고운 옷을 잔뜩 지녔든 말든 아무 상관이 없는 듯했다. 다만 그녀가 지금 갖춘 것이 너무도 초라했으므로, 에미는 자기 옷가지를 내어 그녀를 입혔고, 혹은 그녀를 데리고 그 고장에서 가장 좋은 모자 가게로 가서 거기서 그녀를 차려입혔다."], ["It was no more torn collars now, I promise you, and faded silks trailing off at the shoulder.", "이제는, 내 장담하건대, 찢어진 깃이며 어깨에서 빛바랜 채 늘어진 비단 따위는 더 이상 없었다."], ["Becky changed her habits with her situation in life--the rouge-pot was suspended--another excitement to which she had accustomed herself was also put aside, or at least only indulged in in privacy, as when she was prevailed on by Jos of a summer evening, Emmy and the boy being absent on their walks, to take a little spirit-and-water.", "베키는 처지가 바뀜에 따라 습관도 바꾸었으니, 연지 단지는 손을 떼게 되었고, 또 하나 그녀가 길들여 온 자극거리 역시 제쳐 두었다. 적어도 남몰래 즐길 때 말고는 그러했으니, 이를테면 에미와 소년이 산책 나가 자리를 비운 여름 저녁, 조스의 권유에 못 이겨 독한 술에 물을 탄 것을 조금 마실 때 정도였다."], ["But if she did not indulge--the courier did: that rascal Kirsch could not be kept from the bottle, nor could he tell how much he took when he applied to it.", "그러나 그녀는 절제했을지언정, 안내인은 그러지 못했다. 저 망나니 키르슈는 도무지 술병에서 떼어 놓을 수가 없었고, 술병에 손을 댈 때면 제가 얼마나 마셨는지도 가늠하지 못했다."], ["He was sometimes surprised himself at the way in which Mr. Sedley's Cognac diminished.", "그는 세들리 씨의 코냑이 줄어드는 모양새에 이따금 제 스스로도 놀라곤 했다."], ["Well, well, this is a painful subject.", "자, 자, 이건 괴로운 화제다."], ["Becky did not very likely indulge so much as she used before she entered a decorous family.", "베키는 아마도 이 단정한 가정에 들어오기 전만큼 그리 흥청대지는 않았을 것이다."]], [["At last the much-bragged-about boxes arrived from Leipzig; three of them not by any means large or splendid; nor did Becky appear to take out any sort of dresses or ornaments from the boxes when they did arrive.", "마침내 그토록 떠벌려 온 상자들이 라이프치히에서 도착했다. 세 개였는데, 결코 크지도 휘황하지도 않았다."], ["But out of one, which contained a mass of her papers (it was that very box which Rawdon Crawley had ransacked in his furious hunt for Becky's concealed money), she took a picture with great glee, which she pinned up in her room, and to which she introduced Jos.", "게다가 막상 도착한 그 상자들에서 베키는 옷가지든 장신구든 무엇 하나 꺼내는 기색이 없었다. 다만 그중 하나, 자기 서류 뭉치가 가득 담긴 상자에서—그것은 바로 로던 크롤리가 베키가 숨겨 둔 돈을 미친 듯이 뒤지며 샅샅이 헤집었던 바로 그 상자였다—그녀는 더없이 신이 나서 그림 한 점을 꺼냈고, 그것을 자기 방에 핀으로 꽂아 두고는 조스에게 보여 주었다."], ["It was the portrait of a gentleman in pencil, his face having the advantage of being painted up in pink.", "그것은 어느 신사를 연필로 그린 초상이었는데, 그 얼굴은 분홍빛으로 덧칠해진 덕을 보고 있었다."], ["He was riding on an elephant away from some cocoa-nut trees and a pagoda: it was an Eastern scene.", "그 신사는 코끼리에 올라타 야자나무 몇 그루와 탑 하나를 등지고 멀어져 가는 모습이었으니, 동방의 풍경이었다."]], [["\"God bless my soul, it is my portrait,\"", "\"이런 맙소사, 내 초상이잖소!\""], ["Jos cried out.", "조스가 외쳤다."], ["It was he indeed, blooming in youth and beauty, in a nankeen jacket of the cut of 1804.", "과연 그것은 1804년식 재단의 능직 무명 윗옷을 걸치고 젊음과 아름다움이 만개한 그 자신이었다."], ["It was the old picture that used to hang up in Russell Square.", "러셀 광장에 걸려 있던 바로 그 오래된 그림이었다."]], [["\"I bought it,\"", "\"제가 샀어요.\""], ["said Becky in a voice trembling with emotion; \"I went to see if I could be of any use to my kind friends.", "베키가 감정에 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"혹시라도 제 다정한 벗들께 무슨 도움이 될까 싶어 가 보았던 거예요."], ["I have never parted with that picture--I never will.\"", "저는 그 그림을 한 번도 떼어 놓지 않았어요. 앞으로도 결코 떼어 놓지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["\"Won't you?\"", "\"정말이오?\""], ["Jos cried with a look of unutterable rapture and satisfaction.", "조스가 이루 말할 수 없는 환희와 만족이 어린 표정으로 외쳤다."], ["\"Did you really now value it for my sake?\"", "\"정말로 나를 생각해서 그걸 그리 소중히 여겼단 말이오?\""]], [["\"You know I did, well enough,\"", "\"제가 그랬다는 걸 잘 아시잖아요.\" 베키가 말했다."], ["said Becky; \"but why speak--why think--why look back!", "\"하지만 무엇하러 말하고, 무엇하러 생각하고, 무엇하러 뒤를 돌아보겠어요!"], ["It is too late now!\"", "이젠 너무 늦었는걸요!\""]], [["That evening's conversation was delicious for Jos.", "그날 저녁의 대화는 조스에게 더없이 감미로운 것이었다."], ["Emmy only came in to go to bed very tired and unwell.", "에미는 그저 몹시 지치고 몸이 편치 않아 잠자리에 들려고 잠깐 들어왔을 뿐이었다."], ["Jos and his fair guest had a charming tete-a-tete, and his sister could hear, as she lay awake in her adjoining chamber, Rebecca singing over to Jos the old songs of 1815.", "조스와 그의 어여쁜 손님은 매혹적인 단둘만의 자리를 가졌고, 옆방에 누워 잠 못 이루던 누이는 레베카가 조스에게 1815년의 옛 노래들을 거듭 불러 주는 것을 들을 수 있었다."], ["He did not sleep, for a wonder, that night, any more than Amelia.", "신기하게도 그는 그날 밤 애밀리아 못지않게 도무지 잠들지 못했다."]], [["It was June, and, by consequence, high season in London; Jos, who read the incomparable Galignani (the exile's best friend) through every day, used to favour the ladies with extracts from his paper during their breakfast.", "때는 유월이었고, 따라서 런던은 한창 사교철이었다. 매일같이 비할 데 없는 갈리냐니지(망명객의 가장 좋은 벗이다)를 통독하던 조스는, 아침 식사 자리에서 부인들에게 그 신문에서 추린 기사들을 들려주곤 했다."], ["Every week in this paper there is a full account of military movements, in which Jos, as a man who had seen service, was especially interested.", "이 신문에는 매주 군대의 이동에 관한 상세한 기사가 실렸는데, 한때 군에 몸담았던 사람으로서 조스는 거기에 각별한 관심을 두었다. 한번은 그가 이런 기사를 읽어 주었다."], ["On one occasion he read out--\"Arrival of the --th regiment.", "\"제××연대의 귀환. 그레이브젠드, 유월 이십일."], ["Gravesend, June 20.--The Ramchunder, East Indiaman, came into the river this morning, having on board 14 officers, and 132 rank and file of this gallant corps.", "동인도 회사 소속 선박 람천더호가 오늘 아침 강으로 들어왔다. 이 용맹한 부대의 장교 십사 명과 병졸 백삼십이 명을 태우고 온 것이다."], ["They have been absent from England fourteen years, having been embarked the year after Waterloo, in which glorious conflict they took an active part, and having subsequently distinguished themselves in the Burmese war.", "이들은 워털루 전투 이듬해에 출항한 이래 십사 년 동안 영국을 떠나 있었으니, 그 영광스러운 전투에서 적극적으로 활약했으며, 이후 버마 전쟁에서도 빼어난 공을 세웠다."], ["The veteran colonel, Sir Michael O'Dowd, K.C.B., with his lady and sister, landed here yesterday, with Captains Posky, Stubble, Macraw, Malony; Lieutenants Smith, Jones, Thompson, F.", "노련한 연대장 마이클 오다우드 경(바스 훈작사)이 부인 및 누이와 함께 어제 이곳에 상륙했고, 포스키·스터블·매크로·맬로니 대위, 스미스·존스·톰슨·F."], ["Thomson; Ensigns Hicks and Grady; the band on the pier playing the national anthem, and the crowd loudly cheering the gallant veterans as they went into Wayte's hotel, where a sumptuous banquet was provided for the defenders of Old England.", "톰슨 중위, 힉스·그레이디 소위가 함께였다. 부두에서는 악대가 국가를 연주했고, 군중은 이 용맹한 노병들이 웨이트 호텔로 들어설 때 우렁차게 환호했으니, 그곳에는 노쇠한 영국을 지킨 이들을 위한 성대한 연회가 마련되어 있었다."], ["During the repast, which we need not say was served up in Wayte's best style, the cheering continued so enthusiastically that Lady O'Dowd and the Colonel came forward to the balcony and drank the healths of their fellow-countrymen in a bumper of Wayte's best claret.\"", "새삼 말할 것도 없이 웨이트 호텔 특유의 솜씨로 차려진 그 향연이 진행되는 동안 환호성이 어찌나 열렬히 이어졌던지, 오다우드 부인과 연대장이 발코니로 나와 웨이트 호텔 최상품 클라레 한 잔을 가득 채워 동포들의 건강을 위해 들이켰다.\""]], [["On a second occasion Jos read a brief announcement--Major Dobbin had joined the --th regiment at Chatham; and subsequently he promulgated accounts of the presentations at the Drawing-room of Colonel Sir Michael O'Dowd, K.C.B., Lady O'Dowd (by Mrs.", "또 한번은 조스가 짤막한 소식을 읽어 주었다. 도빈 소령이 채텀에서 제××연대에 합류했다는 것이었다."], ["Malloy Malony of Ballymalony), and Miss Glorvina O'Dowd (by Lady O'Dowd).", "그 뒤로 그는 마이클 오다우드 경(바스 훈작사) 연대장과, 오다우드 부인(밸리맬로니의 맬로이 맬로니 부인이 소개), 그리고 글로비나 오다우드 양(오다우드 부인이 소개)이 궁정 알현실에 소개되었다는 소식을 전했다."], ["Almost directly after this, Dobbin's name appeared among the Lieutenant-Colonels: for old Marshal Tiptoff had died during the passage of the --th from Madras, and the Sovereign was pleased to advance Colonel Sir Michael O'Dowd to the rank of Major-General on his return to England, with an intimation that he should be Colonel of the distinguished regiment which he had so long commanded.", "그 직후라 해도 좋을 만큼 곧이어, 도빈의 이름이 중령들의 명단에 올랐다. 늙은 팁토프 원수가 제××연대의 마드라스 항해 도중에 세상을 떠났던 까닭에, 군주께서는 마이클 오다우드 경 연대장이 영국으로 돌아오자 그를 소장으로 진급시키시는 한편, 그가 그토록 오래 지휘해 온 그 빼어난 연대의 연대장 직을 맡으라는 뜻을 내비치셨던 것이다."]], [["Amelia had been made aware of some of these movements.", "애밀리아는 이러한 이동의 일부를 전해 듣고 있었다."], ["The correspondence between George and his guardian had not ceased by any means: William had even written once or twice to her since his departure, but in a manner so unconstrainedly cold that the poor woman felt now in her turn that she had lost her power over him and that, as he had said, he was free.", "조지와 그 후견인 사이의 서신 왕래는 결코 끊긴 적이 없었다. 윌리엄은 떠난 뒤로 그녀에게도 한두 차례 편지를 쓰기까지 했으나, 그 어조가 어찌나 거리낌 없이 싸늘했던지, 가엾은 여인은 이번에는 자기 차례로 그에 대한 자신의 힘을 잃었음을, 그리고 그가 말했던 대로 그가 자유로운 몸이 되었음을 느꼈다."], ["He had left her, and she was wretched.", "그가 그녀를 떠났고, 그녀는 비참했다."], ["The memory of his almost countless services, and lofty and affectionate regard, now presented itself to her and rebuked her day and night.", "그가 베푼 헤아릴 수 없는 봉사들, 그 고결하고도 애정 어린 마음이 이제 그녀의 뇌리에 떠올라 밤낮없이 그녀를 책망했다."], ["She brooded over those recollections according to her wont, saw the purity and beauty of the affection with which she had trifled, and reproached herself for having flung away such a treasure.", "그녀는 늘 그러했듯 그 추억들을 곱씹으며, 자신이 하찮게 여겨 가지고 놀았던 그 애정의 순수함과 아름다움을 깨닫고, 그런 보물을 내던져 버린 자신을 책망했다."]], [["It was gone indeed.", "그것은 정녕 가 버린 것이었다."], ["William had spent it all out.", "윌리엄은 그것을 모조리 다 써 버린 것이다."], ["He loved her no more, he thought, as he had loved her.", "그는 자신이 더는 예전처럼 그녀를 사랑하지 않는다고 생각했다."], ["He never could again.", "다시는 그럴 수 없었다."], ["That sort of regard, which he had proffered to her for so many faithful years, can't be flung down and shattered and mended so as to show no scars.", "그가 그토록 오랜 세월 변함없이 그녀에게 바쳐 온 그런 마음이란, 한번 내던져져 산산조각 난 뒤에 다시 이어 붙인다고 해서 흉터 하나 없이 멀쩡할 수는 없는 법이다. 그 분별없는 자그마한 폭군이 그것을 그렇게 부수어 놓은 것이다."], ["The little heedless tyrant had so destroyed it.", "아니, 윌리엄은 거듭하고 또 거듭하여 생각했다."], ["No, William thought again and again, \"It was myself I deluded and persisted in cajoling; had she been worthy of the love I gave her, she would have returned it long ago.", "\"내가 속인 것은 나 자신이었고, 내가 끈질기게 어르고 달랜 것도 나 자신이었어. 내가 바친 사랑을 받을 만한 여자였다면 진작에 그 사랑에 응답했겠지."], ["It was a fond mistake.", "그건 어리석은 착각이었어."], ["Isn't the whole course of life made up of such?", "인생의 모든 행로가 이런 착각들로 이루어진 것 아니던가?"], ["And suppose I had won her, should I not have been disenchanted the day after my victory?", "그리고 설령 내가 그녀를 얻었다 한들, 승리한 이튿날이면 환상이 깨지지 않았겠는가?"], ["Why pine, or be ashamed of my defeat?\"", "무엇하러 애태우고, 무엇하러 내 패배를 부끄러워하겠는가?\""], ["The more he thought of this long passage of his life, the more clearly he saw his deception.", "그는 자기 인생의 이 기나긴 한 토막을 생각하면 할수록, 자신의 착각이 더욱 또렷이 보였다."], ["\"I'll go into harness again,\"", "\"다시 멍에를 메야지.\""], ["he said, \"and do my duty in that state of life in which it has pleased Heaven to place me.", "그가 말했다. \"그리고 하늘이 나를 두기로 정하신 그 처지에서 내 본분을 다해야지."], ["I will see that the buttons of the recruits are properly bright and that the sergeants make no mistakes in their accounts.", "신병들의 단추가 제대로 반들거리는지, 하사관들이 회계에서 실수하지 않는지 살피겠어."], ["I will dine at mess and listen to the Scotch surgeon telling his stories.", "장교 식당에서 식사를 하고, 스코틀랜드 군의관이 늘어놓는 이야기에 귀를 기울이겠어."], ["When I am old and broken, I will go on half-pay, and my old sisters shall scold me.", "늙고 쇠약해지면 반액 봉급을 받고 물러나, 늙은 누이들의 잔소리나 들으며 지내야지."], ["I have geliebt und gelebet, as the girl in 'Wallenstein' says.", "나는 '발렌슈타인'의 처녀가 말하듯 사랑했고 또 살았노라(geliebt und gelebet)."], ["I am done.", "나는 끝났어."], ["Pay the bills and get me a cigar: find out what there is at the play to-night, Francis; to-morrow we cross by the Batavier.\"", "청구서들을 치르고 시가나 한 대 가져오게. 오늘 밤 극장에서 무얼 하는지 알아봐 주게, 프랜시스."], ["He made the above speech, whereof Francis only heard the last two lines, pacing up and down the Boompjes at Rotterdam.", "내일 우리는 바타비르호로 건너가네.\" 그는 로테르담의 봄피스 강변을 거닐며 위와 같은 말을 했는데, 프랜시스가 알아들은 것은 마지막 두 마디뿐이었다."], ["The Batavier was lying in the basin.", "바타비르호는 선착장에 정박해 있었다."], ["He could see the place on the quarter-deck where he and Emmy had sat on the happy voyage out.", "그는 갑판 후미, 그 행복했던 출항길에 그와 에미가 함께 앉아 있던 자리를 바라볼 수 있었다."], ["What had that little Mrs. Crawley to say to him?", "그 자그마한 크롤리 부인이 그에게 무슨 할 말이 있단 말인가?"], ["Psha; to-morrow we will put to sea, and return to England, home, and duty!", "흥, 내일이면 우리는 바다로 나아가 영국으로, 집으로, 그리고 본분으로 돌아가리라!"]], [["After June all the little Court Society of Pumpernickel used to separate, according to the German plan, and make for a hundred watering-places, where they drank at the wells, rode upon donkeys, gambled at the redoutes if they had money and a mind, rushed with hundreds of their kind to gourmandise at the tables d'hote, and idled away the summer.", "유월이 지나면 펌퍼니클의 자그마한 궁정 사교계는 으레 독일식으로 뿔뿔이 흩어져, 수백 곳의 온천지로 향하곤 했다. 거기서 그들은 샘물을 마시고, 당나귀를 타고, 돈과 마음이 동하면 도박장에서 노름을 하고, 같은 부류의 수백 명과 더불어 정식 식탁으로 우르르 몰려가 먹어 대고, 여름을 빈둥거리며 흘려보냈다."], ["The English diplomatists went off to Teoplitz and Kissingen, their French rivals shut up their chancellerie and whisked away to their darling Boulevard de Gand.", "영국 외교관들은 테플리츠와 키싱겐으로 떠났고, 그들의 프랑스 적수들은 공관 문을 닫아걸고 그들이 애지중지하는 강 대로로 휙 사라졌다. 고귀하신 통치 가문도 온천으로 가거나 사냥용 별장으로 물러갔다."], ["The Transparent reigning family took too to the waters, or retired to their hunting lodges.", "점잔깨나 빼는 이라면 누구나 떠나갔으니, 물론 그 무리에는 궁정 시의 폰 글라우버 박사와 그 남작 부인도 끼어 있었다."], ["Everybody went away having any pretensions to politeness, and of course, with them, Doctor von Glauber, the Court Doctor, and his Baroness.", "온천철은 그 박사에게 가장 벌이가 짭짤한 시기였으니, 그는 일과 즐거움을 한데 겸했고, 그가 즐겨 찾는 곳은 독일인이 즐겨 드나드는 오스텐더였다."], ["The seasons for the baths were the most productive periods of the Doctor's practice--he united business with pleasure, and his chief place of resort was Ostend, which is much frequented by Germans, and where the Doctor treated himself and his spouse to what he called a \"dib\" in the sea.", "그곳에서 박사는 자신과 그 아내에게 제 말로 바닷물에 \"몸 한번 담그기\"라는 호사를 베풀었다."]], [["His interesting patient, Jos, was a regular milch-cow to the Doctor, and he easily persuaded the civilian, both for his own health's sake and that of his charming sister, which was really very much shattered, to pass the summer at that hideous seaport town.", "그의 흥미로운 환자 조스는 박사에게 그야말로 젖소나 다름없었으니, 박사는 조스 본인의 건강을 위해서뿐 아니라 실로 몹시 쇠약해진 그 매력적인 누이의 건강을 위해서라며, 그 흉측한 바닷가 도시에서 여름을 나도록 이 문관을 어렵잖게 구슬렸다."], ["Emmy did not care where she went much.", "에미는 어디로 가든 그다지 개의치 않았다."], ["Georgy jumped at the idea of a move.", "조지는 거처를 옮긴다는 생각에 펄쩍 뛰며 좋아했다."], ["As for Becky, she came as a matter of course in the fourth place inside of the fine barouche Mr. Jos had bought, the two domestics being on the box in front.", "베키로 말하면, 조스 씨가 사들인 그 멋진 사륜마차 안 네 번째 자리에 당연하다는 듯 따라 탔으니, 하인 둘은 앞쪽 마부석에 앉았다."], ["She might have some misgivings about the friends whom she should meet at Ostend, and who might be likely to tell ugly stories--but bah!", "오스텐더에서 마주칠 법한 지인들, 흉한 소문을 퍼뜨릴지도 모를 그런 이들이 마음에 걸리기야 했겠지만, 흥! 그녀는 제 앞가림쯤은 너끈히 할 만큼 단단한 여자였다."], ["she was strong enough to hold her own. She had cast such an anchor in Jos now as would require a strong storm to shake.", "이제 그녀는 조스라는 닻을 단단히 내려 두었으니, 그것을 흔들어 놓으려면 어지간한 폭풍으로는 어림없을 터였다."], ["That incident of the picture had finished him.", "그 초상화 사건이 그를 완전히 끝장내 버린 것이다."], ["Becky took down her elephant and put it into the little box which she had had from Amelia ever so many years ago.", "베키는 코끼리 그림을 떼어 내, 아주 오래전 애밀리아에게서 받은 그 작은 상자에 넣었다."], ["Emmy also came off with her Lares--her two pictures--and the party, finally, were, lodged in an exceedingly dear and uncomfortable house at Ostend.", "에미 역시 자신의 수호신들을—두 점의 초상화를—챙겨 들고 떠났고, 마침내 일행은 오스텐더의 더없이 비싸고 불편한 집에 거처를 잡았다."]], [["There Amelia began to take baths and get what good she could from them, and though scores of people of Becky's acquaintance passed her and cut her, yet Mrs.", "그곳에서 애밀리아는 해수욕을 시작하여 거기서 얻을 수 있는 효험을 보려 했다."], ["Osborne, who walked about with her, and who knew nobody, was not aware of the treatment experienced by the friend whom she had chosen so judiciously as a companion; indeed, Becky never thought fit to tell her what was passing under her innocent eyes.", "그리고 베키와 안면이 있는 숱한 사람들이 그녀 곁을 스쳐 가며 그녀를 못 본 체했건만, 그녀와 함께 거닐면서도 아는 이 하나 없던 오스본 부인은, 자신이 그토록 분별 있게 골랐다는 그 길동무가 어떤 대접을 받고 있는지 알지 못했다. 실로 베키는 그 천진한 눈앞에서 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 그녀에게 털어놓는 것이 마땅하다고 여기지 않았던 것이다."]], [["Some of Mrs. Rawdon Crawley's acquaintances, however, acknowledged her readily enough,--perhaps more readily than she would have desired.", "그러나 로던 크롤리 부인과 안면이 있는 이들 가운데 몇몇은 선뜻 그녀를 아는 체했으니, 어쩌면 그녀가 바랐을 법한 것보다 더 선뜻 그러했다."], ["Among those were Major Loder (unattached), and Captain Rook (late of the Rifles), who might be seen any day on the Dike, smoking and staring at the women, and who speedily got an introduction to the hospitable board and select circle of Mr. Joseph Sedley.", "그 가운데에는 (무소속의) 로더 소령과 (전직 소총부대) 룩 대위가 있었으니, 이들은 둑 위에서 담배를 피우며 여자들을 빤히 쳐다보는 모습을 날마다 볼 수 있었고, 이내 조지프 세들리 씨의 후한 식탁과 까다로운 모임에 끼어들 소개를 얻어 냈다."], ["In fact they would take no denial; they burst into the house whether Becky was at home or not, walked into Mrs.", "사실 이들은 거절이라는 것을 받아들이지 않았다."], ["Osborne's drawing-room, which they perfumed with their coats and mustachios, called Jos \"Old buck,\" and invaded his dinner-table, and laughed and drank for long hours there.", "베키가 집에 있든 없든 막무가내로 집 안으로 쳐들어와, 오스본 부인의 응접실로 걸어 들어가 그들의 외투와 콧수염 냄새로 방을 진동시켰고, 조스를 \"이 양반\"이라 부르며 그의 식탁을 점령하고는, 그곳에서 오래도록 웃고 마셔 댔다."]], [["\"What can they mean?\"", "\"저 사람들 도대체 무슨 속셈일까요?\""], ["asked Georgy, who did not like these gentlemen.", "이 신사들을 좋아하지 않던 조지가 물었다."], ["\"I heard the Major say to Mrs.", "\"어제 소령이 크롤리 부인한테 이렇게 말하는 걸 들었어요."], ["Crawley yesterday, 'No, no, Becky, you shan't keep the old buck to yourself.", "'안 되지, 안 돼, 베키, 저 양반을 혼자 독차지하려 들면 못쓰지."], ["We must have the bones in, or, dammy, I'll split.'", "우리도 한몫 끼워 줘야지, 안 그러면 젠장, 내가 다 까발릴 테니.'"], ["What could the Major mean, Mamma?\"", "소령이 무슨 뜻으로 그런 말을 한 걸까요, 엄마?\""]], [["\"Major!", "\"소령이라니!"], ["don't call him Major!\"", "그 사람을 소령이라 부르지 마라!\""], ["Emmy said.", "에미가 말했다."], ["\"I'm sure I can't tell what he meant.\"", "\"정말이지 그 사람이 무슨 뜻으로 그랬는지는 나도 모르겠구나.\""], ["His presence and that of his friend inspired the little lady with intolerable terror and aversion.", "그와 그의 벗이 곁에 있다는 것만으로도 이 자그마한 여인은 견딜 수 없는 공포와 혐오에 사로잡혔다."], ["They paid her tipsy compliments; they leered at her over the dinner-table.", "그들은 술 취한 입으로 그녀에게 찬사를 늘어놓았고, 식탁 너머로 그녀를 곁눈질했다."], ["And the Captain made her advances that filled her with sickening dismay, nor would she ever see him unless she had George by her side.", "그리고 대위가 그녀에게 수작을 걸어 와 그녀를 메스껍도록 질겁하게 했으니, 그녀는 조지가 곁에 있지 않으면 결코 그를 만나려 하지 않았다."]], [["Rebecca, to do her justice, never would let either of these men remain alone with Amelia; the Major was disengaged too, and swore he would be the winner of her.", "레베카는, 그녀의 공정을 기해 말하건대, 이 두 사내 가운데 누구도 애밀리아와 단둘이 있도록 결코 내버려 두지 않았다."], ["A couple of ruffians were fighting for this innocent creature, gambling for her at her own table, and though she was not aware of the rascals'", "소령 역시 임자 없는 몸이라, 자신이 그녀를 차지할 거라며 큰소리를 쳤다. 두 명의 망나니가 이 순진한 여인을 두고 다투며, 그녀의 식탁에서 그녀를 걸고 노름을 벌인 것이다."], ["designs upon her, yet she felt a horror and uneasiness in their presence and longed to fly.", "그리고 그녀는 비록 그 악당들이 자신을 노린 속셈을 알지는 못했어도, 그들이 곁에 있으면 공포와 불안을 느꼈고 어디로든 달아나기를 갈망했다."]], [["She besought, she entreated Jos to go. Not he.", "그녀는 조스에게 떠나자고 애원하고 또 간청했다."], ["He was slow of movement, tied to his Doctor, and perhaps to some other leading-strings.", "그는 들으려 하지 않았다. 그는 거동이 굼떴고, 그 박사에게, 또 어쩌면 다른 어떤 끈에도 매여 있었던 것이다."], ["At least Becky was not anxious to go to England.", "적어도 베키만큼은 영국으로 갈 마음이 없었다."]], [["At last she took a great resolution--made the great plunge.", "마침내 그녀는 큰 결심을 했다. 큰 모험에 뛰어든 것이다."], ["She wrote off a letter to a friend whom she had on the other side of the water, a letter about which she did not speak a word to anybody, which she carried herself to the post under her shawl; nor was any remark made about it, only that she looked very much flushed and agitated when Georgy met her, and she kissed him, and hung over him a great deal that night.", "그녀는 바다 건너편에 있는 어느 벗에게 편지 한 통을 써 보냈는데, 그 편지에 관해서는 아무에게도 한마디 비치지 않았고, 숄 아래 감춘 채 손수 우체국으로 가져갔다. 그것을 두고 누가 무어라 말한 바도 없었으니, 다만 조지와 마주쳤을 때 그녀가 몹시 상기되고 동요한 듯 보였고, 그날 밤 소년에게 입 맞추며 한참을 그러안고 있었을 뿐이다."], ["She did not come out of her room after her return from her walk.", "산책에서 돌아온 뒤로 그녀는 제 방에서 나오지 않았다."], ["Becky thought it was Major Loder and the Captain who frightened her.", "베키는 그녀를 겁먹게 한 것이 로더 소령과 대위라고 여겼다."]], [["\"She mustn't stop here,\"", "\"여기 머물러선 안 돼.\""], ["Becky reasoned with herself.", "베키는 혼자 따져 보았다."], ["\"She must go away, the silly little fool.", "\"저 어리석고 자그마한 바보는 떠나야 해."], ["She is still whimpering after that gaby of a husband--dead (and served right!) these fifteen years.", "아직도 그 얼간이 같은 남편을 못 잊어 훌쩍거리고 있으니—죽은 지(그것도 당연한 죗값이었지!) 벌써 십오 년이나 된 사람을 말이야."], ["She shan't marry either of these men.", "저 여자가 저 두 사내 중 누구와도 결혼하게 둬선 안 돼."], ["It's too bad of Loder.", "로더라니 너무 못됐어."], ["No; she shall marry the bamboo cane, I'll settle it this very night.\"", "안 되지, 저 여잔 그 대나무 막대기 같은 사내와 결혼시켜야지, 바로 오늘 밤 매듭을 지어야겠어.\""]], [["So Becky took a cup of tea to Amelia in her private apartment and found that lady in the company of her miniatures, and in a most melancholy and nervous condition.", "그리하여 베키는 차 한 잔을 들고 애밀리아의 사실(私室)로 들어갔다가, 이 부인이 자기 초상화들을 벗 삼아 더없이 우울하고 신경이 곤두선 채 있는 것을 보았다."], ["She laid down the cup of tea.", "베키는 찻잔을 내려놓았다."]], [["\"Thank you,\"", "\"고마워.\""], ["said Amelia.", "애밀리아가 말했다."]], [["\"Listen to me, Amelia,\"", "\"내 말 들어, 애밀리아.\""], ["said Becky, marching up and down the room before the other and surveying her with a sort of contemptuous kindness.", "베키가 그녀 앞에서 방 안을 왔다 갔다 거닐며, 일종의 경멸 어린 다정함으로 그녀를 훑어보면서 말했다."], ["\"I want to talk to you.", "\"너한테 할 말이 있어."], ["You must go away from here and from the impertinences of these men.", "너는 여기를, 그리고 저 사내들의 무례한 짓거리를 떠나야 해."], ["I won't have you harassed by them: and they will insult you if you stay.", "저들이 너를 들볶도록 두고 보지 않겠어. 네가 머무르면 저들은 너를 모욕할 거야."], ["I tell you they are rascals: men fit to send to the hulks.", "내 말하건대 저들은 망나니야. 감옥선에나 처넣어 마땅한 작자들이지."], ["Never mind how I know them.", "내가 저들을 어떻게 아는지는 묻지 마."], ["I know everybody.", "나는 누구든 다 알아."], ["Jos can't protect you; he is too weak and wants a protector himself.", "조스는 너를 지켜 줄 수 없어. 그 사람은 너무 약해 빠져서 제 몸을 지켜 줄 보호자가 필요한 처지야."], ["You are no more fit to live in the world than a baby in arms.", "너는 갓난아기나 다름없이 세상을 살아갈 깜냥이 못 돼."], ["You must marry, or you and your precious boy will go to ruin.", "너는 결혼을 해야 해, 안 그러면 너도 네 그 귀한 아들도 다 파멸하고 말 거야."], ["You must have a husband, you fool; and one of the best gentlemen I ever saw has offered you a hundred times, and you have rejected him, you silly, heartless, ungrateful little creature!\"", "너는 남편이 있어야 한다고, 이 바보야. 그런데 내 평생 본 가장 훌륭한 신사 가운데 한 사람이 네게 수백 번이나 청혼했는데, 너는 그를 거절했어, 이 어리석고 매정하고 은혜도 모르는 자그마한 것아!\""]], [["\"I tried--I tried my best, indeed I did, Rebecca,\"", "\"애써 봤어, 정말 최선을 다해 애써 봤다고, 레베카.\" 애밀리아가 자기를 변명하듯 말했다."], ["said Amelia deprecatingly, \"but I couldn't forget--\"; and she finished the sentence by looking up at the portrait.", "\"하지만 잊을 수가 없었어.\" 그러고는 초상화를 올려다보는 것으로 말끝을 맺었다."]], [["\"Couldn't forget HIM!\"", "\"그 사람을 못 잊겠다고!\" 베키가 외쳤다."], ["cried out Becky, \"that selfish humbug, that low-bred cockney dandy, that padded booby, who had neither wit, nor manners, nor heart, and was no more to be compared to your friend with the bamboo cane than you are to Queen Elizabeth.", "\"그 이기적인 사기꾼, 그 천박한 런던내기 멋쟁이, 속에 솜이나 채운 그 멍청이를! 재치도, 예의도, 마음씨도 없던 작자, 네가 엘리자베스 여왕에 견줄 수 없는 만큼이나 그 대나무 막대기 같은 네 벗에게는 견줄 수도 없던 작자를!"], ["Why, the man was weary of you, and would have jilted you, but that Dobbin forced him to keep his word.", "아니, 그 사내는 너한테 진력이 나서 너를 차 버리려 했는데, 도빈이 약속을 지키라고 다그치는 바람에 못 그런 거야."], ["He owned it to me.", "그 사람이 내게 직접 털어놓았어."], ["He never cared for you.", "그는 너를 조금도 사랑하지 않았어."], ["He used to sneer about you to me, time after time, and made love to me the week after he married you.\"", "나한테 너를 두고 비웃기를 몇 번이고 거듭했고, 너와 결혼한 그 이듬 주에 나한테 수작을 걸었다고.\""]], [["\"It's false!", "\"거짓말이야!"], ["It's false! Rebecca,\"", "거짓말이야, 레베카!\""], ["cried out Amelia, starting up.", "애밀리아가 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다."]], [["\"Look there, you fool,\"", "\"이걸 보라고, 이 바보야.\""], ["Becky said, still with provoking good humour, and taking a little paper out of her belt, she opened it and flung it into Emmy's lap.", "베키가 여전히 약 올리는 듯한 명랑함으로, 허리띠에서 작은 종이쪽지 하나를 꺼내더니, 그것을 펼쳐 에미의 무릎 위로 휙 던지며 말했다. \"너도 그 사람 필체는 알아보겠지."], ["\"You know his handwriting.", "그 사람이 이걸 내게 썼어. 나더러 자기랑 달아나자고 했지."], ["He wrote that to me--wanted me to run away with him--gave it me under your nose, the day before he was shot--and served him right!\"", "네 코앞에서 내게 건넨 거야, 총에 맞기 바로 전날 말이야. 그것도 다 죗값이었지!\""], ["Becky repeated.", "베키가 거듭 말했다."]], [["Emmy did not hear her; she was looking at the letter.", "에미는 그녀의 말이 들리지 않았다. 그녀는 그 편지를 들여다보고 있었다."], ["It was that which George had put into the bouquet and given to Becky on the night of the Duchess of Richmond's ball.", "그것은 리치먼드 공작 부인의 무도회가 있던 밤, 조지가 부케 속에 끼워 베키에게 건넸던 바로 그 쪽지였다. 그녀의 말 그대로였다."], ["It was as she said: the foolish young man had asked her to fly.", "그 어리석은 젊은이가 그녀에게 함께 달아나자고 청했던 것이다."]], [["Emmy's head sank down, and for almost the last time in which she shall be called upon to weep in this history, she commenced that work.", "에미의 고개가 떨궈졌고, 이 이야기에서 그녀가 눈물 흘리도록 청해질 거의 마지막 순간에 그녀는 그 일에 착수했다."], ["Her head fell to her bosom, and her hands went up to her eyes; and there for a while, she gave way to her emotions, as Becky stood on and regarded her.", "고개가 가슴께로 떨어지고 두 손이 눈으로 올라갔다. 그렇게 그녀는 한동안 자기 감정에 몸을 내맡겼고, 베키는 가만히 서서 그 모습을 지켜보았다."], ["Who shall analyse those tears and say whether they were sweet or bitter?", "누가 그 눈물을 헤아려, 그것이 달콤한 것인지 쓰디쓴 것인지 말할 수 있으랴?"], ["Was she most grieved because the idol of her life was tumbled down and shivered at her feet, or indignant that her love had been so despised, or glad because the barrier was removed which modesty had placed between her and a new, a real affection?", "그녀가 그토록 슬퍼한 것은 평생의 우상이 무너져 제 발치에서 산산이 부서진 까닭이었을까, 아니면 자신의 사랑이 그토록 멸시당했음에 분개한 까닭이었을까, 아니면 정숙함이 그녀와 새롭고도 참된 애정 사이에 세워 두었던 장벽이 걷힌 것이 기뻤던 까닭이었을까?"], ["\"There is nothing to forbid me now,\"", "\"이제 나를 가로막을 것은 아무것도 없어.\""], ["she thought.", "그녀는 생각했다."], ["\"I may love him with all my heart now.", "\"이제 나는 그이를 온 마음 다해 사랑해도 돼."], ["Oh, I will, I will, if he will but let me and forgive me.\"", "오, 그럴 거야, 그럴 거야, 그이가 허락만 해 준다면, 나를 용서만 해 준다면.\""], ["I believe it was this feeling rushed over all the others which agitated that gentle little bosom.", "그 자그맣고 부드러운 가슴을 뒤흔든 다른 모든 감정을 압도하고 솟구쳐 오른 것은 바로 이 마음이었으리라."]], [["Indeed, she did not cry so much as Becky expected--the other soothed and kissed her--a rare mark of sympathy with Mrs.", "실로 그녀는 베키가 예상한 만큼 그리 많이 울지는 않았다. 베키가 그녀를 달래고 입 맞추어 주었으니, 베키 부인에게서는 보기 드문 공감의 표시였다."], ["Becky. She treated Emmy like a child and patted her head.", "그녀는 에미를 어린아이처럼 대하며 그 머리를 토닥였다."], ["\"And now let us get pen and ink and write to him to come this minute,\"", "\"자, 이제 펜과 잉크를 가져와서 당장 이리로 와 달라고 그이에게 편지를 쓰자.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"I--I wrote to him this morning,\"", "\"나, 나는 오늘 아침에 이미 편지를 썼어.\""], ["Emmy said, blushing exceedingly.", "에미가 얼굴을 새빨갛게 붉히며 말했다. 베키는 깔깔거리며 폭소를 터뜨렸다."], ["Becky screamed with laughter--\"Un biglietto,\"", "\"쪽지 한 장(Un biglietto),\" 그녀가 로지나처럼 노래하듯 외쳤다."], ["she sang out with Rosina, \"eccolo qua!\"--the whole house echoed with her shrill singing.", "\"여기 있다네(eccolo qua)!\" 온 집 안이 그 날카로운 노랫소리로 울렸다."]], [["Two mornings after this little scene, although the day was rainy and gusty, and Amelia had had an exceedingly wakeful night, listening to the wind roaring, and pitying all travellers by land and by water, yet she got up early and insisted upon taking a walk on the Dike with Georgy; and there she paced as the rain beat into her face, and she looked out westward across the dark sea line and over the swollen billows which came tumbling and frothing to the shore.", "이 자그마한 장면이 있은 지 이틀째 아침, 비바람이 몰아치는 날씨였고, 애밀리아는 밤새 포효하는 바람 소리에 귀를 기울이며 뭍으로든 물로든 길 떠난 모든 이를 가엾이 여기느라 도무지 잠을 이루지 못한 터였지만, 그래도 그녀는 일찍 일어나 조지를 데리고 둑으로 산책을 나가겠다고 고집했다. 그리하여 그녀는 비가 얼굴에 들이치는 가운데 거기를 거닐며, 어둑한 수평선 너머 서쪽을 바라보았고, 부풀어 올라 굽이치며 거품을 일으키며 기슭으로 밀려드는 물결 너머를 내다보았다."], ["Neither spoke much, except now and then, when the boy said a few words to his timid companion, indicative of sympathy and protection.", "두 사람은 별말이 없었으니, 다만 이따금 소년이 겁먹은 동행에게 공감과 보호의 뜻을 담은 몇 마디를 건넬 뿐이었다."]], [["\"I hope he won't cross in such weather,\"", "\"이런 날씨에는 그분이 바다를 건너지 않으셨으면 좋겠구나.\""], ["Emmy said.", "에미가 말했다."]], [["\"I bet ten to one he does,\"", "\"십중팔구 건너실걸요.\""], ["the boy answered.", "소년이 대답했다."], ["\"Look, Mother, there's the smoke of the steamer.\"", "\"보세요, 엄마, 저기 증기선 연기가 있어요.\""], ["It was that signal, sure enough.", "과연 그것은 바로 그 신호였다."]], [["But though the steamer was under way, he might not be on board; he might not have got the letter; he might not choose to come.", "그러나 증기선이 출항했다 한들, 그가 배에 타고 있지 않을 수도 있었다. 그가 편지를 받지 못했을 수도, 오기를 마다했을 수도 있었다."], ["A hundred fears poured one over the other into the little heart, as fast as the waves on to the Dike.", "수백 가지 두려움이, 둑으로 밀려드는 파도만큼이나 빠르게 그 자그마한 가슴속으로 꼬리에 꼬리를 물고 쏟아져 들어왔다."]], [["The boat followed the smoke into sight.", "배가 연기를 뒤따라 시야에 들어왔다."], ["Georgy had a dandy telescope and got the vessel under view in the most skilful manner.", "조지에게는 멋들어진 망원경이 있어, 더없이 능란한 솜씨로 그 배를 시야에 잡아넣었다."], ["And he made appropriate nautical comments upon the manner of the approach of the steamer as she came nearer and nearer, dipping and rising in the water.", "그리고 증기선이 물 위에서 곤두박질치고 솟구치며 점점 더 가까이 다가오는 동안, 그는 그 다가오는 모양새를 두고 그럴싸한 항해 용어로 논평을 늘어놓았다."], ["The signal of an English steamer in sight went fluttering up to the mast on the pier.", "영국 증기선이 시야에 들어왔다는 신호가 부두의 돛대 위로 펄럭이며 올라갔다."], ["I daresay Mrs. Amelia's heart was in a similar flutter.", "짐작건대 애밀리아 부인의 가슴도 그와 비슷하게 펄럭이고 있었으리라."]], [["Emmy tried to look through the telescope over George's shoulder, but she could make nothing of it.", "에미는 조지의 어깨너머로 망원경을 들여다보려 했으나, 도무지 아무것도 분간할 수가 없었다."], ["She only saw a black eclipse bobbing up and down before her eyes.", "그저 검은 그림자 하나가 눈앞에서 까딱까딱 오르내리는 것만 보일 뿐이었다."]], [["George took the glass again and raked the vessel.", "조지가 다시 망원경을 잡고 배를 샅샅이 훑었다."], ["\"How she does pitch!\"", "\"야, 정말 곤두박질치네요!\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"There goes a wave slap over her bows.", "\"저기 파도가 뱃머리를 철썩 덮치는 것 좀 봐요."], ["There's only two people on deck besides the steersman.", "갑판에는 키잡이 말고 사람이 둘밖에 없어요."], ["There's a man lying down, and a--chap in a--cloak with a--Hooray!--it's Dob, by Jingo!\"", "한 사람은 누워 있고, 또 한—사람은 외투를 걸치고 있는데—와! 도빈 아저씨예요, 틀림없어요!\""], ["He clapped to the telescope and flung his arms round his mother.", "그는 망원경을 탁 접고는 어머니에게 두 팔을 둘렀다."], ["As for that lady, let us say what she did in the words of a favourite poet--\"Dakruoen gelasasa.\"", "그 부인으로 말하면, 어느 즐겨 읽는 시인의 말을 빌려 그녀가 어찌했는지 말해 두자. \"웃으며 눈물 흘렸노라(Dakruoen gelasasa).\""], ["She was sure it was William.", "그녀는 그것이 윌리엄임을 확신했다."], ["It could be no other.", "다른 누구일 리 없었다."], ["What she had said about hoping that he would not come was all hypocrisy.", "그가 오지 않기를 바란다고 했던 말은 죄다 위선이었다."], ["Of course he would come; what could he do else but come?", "물론 그는 올 것이었다. 온다는 것 말고 그가 달리 어찌하겠는가?"], ["She knew he would come.", "그녀는 그가 오리라는 것을 알고 있었던 것이다."]], [["The ship came swiftly nearer and nearer.", "배는 빠르게 점점 더 가까이 다가왔다."], ["As they went in to meet her at the landing-place at the quay, Emmy's knees trembled so that she scarcely could run.", "부두의 선착장으로 배를 맞으러 나아가는 동안, 에미는 무릎이 어찌나 떨리던지 좀처럼 뛸 수가 없었다."], ["She would have liked to kneel down and say her prayers of thanks there.", "그녀는 거기서 무릎을 꿇고 감사의 기도를 올리고 싶은 심정이었다."], ["Oh, she thought, she would be all her life saying them!", "오, 그녀는 평생토록 그 기도를 올리리라고 생각했다!"]], [["It was such a bad day that as the vessel came alongside of the quay there were no idlers abroad, scarcely even a commissioner on the look out for the few passengers in the steamer.", "날씨가 어찌나 사나웠던지 배가 부두에 옆으로 들어설 무렵에는 바깥에 빈둥거리는 이 하나 없었고, 증기선의 몇 안 되는 승객을 기다리는 짐꾼조차 좀처럼 보이지 않았다."], ["That young scapegrace George had fled too, and as the gentleman in the old cloak lined with red stuff stepped on to the shore, there was scarcely any one present to see what took place, which was briefly this:", "저 젊은 개구쟁이 조지마저 달아나 버린 터라, 붉은 천을 덧댄 낡은 외투를 걸친 그 신사가 뭍으로 발을 내디뎠을 때, 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 지켜보는 이는 거의 아무도 없었다. 그 일이란 간추리자면 이러했다."]], [["A lady in a dripping white bonnet and shawl, with her two little hands out before her, went up to him, and in the next minute she had altogether disappeared under the folds of the old cloak, and was kissing one of his hands with all her might; whilst the other, I suppose, was engaged in holding her to his heart (which her head just about reached) and in preventing her from tumbling down.", "물에 흠뻑 젖은 흰 보닛과 숄 차림의 한 부인이 자그마한 두 손을 앞으로 내민 채 그에게로 다가갔고, 다음 순간 그녀는 그 낡은 외투 자락 아래로 완전히 자취를 감추고는, 온 힘을 다해 그의 한 손에 입 맞추고 있었다. 한편 그의 다른 한 손은, 짐작건대 그녀를 제 가슴께로(그녀의 머리가 겨우 그쯤에 닿았다) 끌어안고 그녀가 쓰러지지 않도록 붙드는 데 쓰이고 있었으리라."], ["She was murmuring something about--forgive--dear William--dear, dear, dearest friend--kiss, kiss, kiss, and so forth--and in fact went on under the cloak in an absurd manner.", "그녀는 무언가를 중얼거리고 있었으니, 용서해 주세요, 사랑하는 윌리엄, 사랑하는, 사랑하는, 더없이 사랑하는 벗이여, 입맞춤, 입맞춤, 입맞춤, 그런 따위의 말들이었다. 그리고 실로 그녀는 외투 자락 아래서 우스꽝스러운 모양으로 그 짓을 이어 갔다."]], [["When Emmy emerged from it, she still kept tight hold of one of William's hands, and looked up in his face.", "에미가 그 안에서 빠져나왔을 때에도, 그녀는 여전히 윌리엄의 한 손을 꼭 붙든 채 그의 얼굴을 올려다보고 있었다."], ["It was full of sadness and tender love and pity.", "그 얼굴에는 슬픔과 다정한 사랑과 연민이 가득했다."], ["She understood its reproach and hung down her head.", "그녀는 그 책망의 빛을 알아차리고 고개를 떨궜다."]], [["\"It was time you sent for me, dear Amelia,\"", "\"진작 나를 불렀어야 했소, 사랑하는 애밀리아.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"You will never go again, William?\"", "\"다신 떠나지 않으실 거죠, 윌리엄?\""]], [["\"No, never,\"", "\"그럼, 다시는.\""], ["he answered, and pressed the dear little soul once more to his heart.", "그가 대답하고는, 그 사랑스럽고 자그마한 영혼을 다시 한번 제 가슴에 끌어안았다."]], [["As they issued out of the custom-house precincts, Georgy broke out on them, with his telescope up to his eye, and a loud laugh of welcome; he danced round the couple and performed many facetious antics as he led them up to the house.", "두 사람이 세관 구내에서 빠져나오는데, 조지가 망원경을 눈에 댄 채 우렁찬 환영의 웃음을 터뜨리며 그들에게 달려들었다. 그는 두 사람 주위를 빙빙 돌며 갖가지 우스꽝스러운 익살을 부리면서 그들을 집으로 이끌었다."], ["Jos wasn't up yet; Becky not visible (though she looked at them through the blinds).", "조스는 아직 일어나지 않았고, 베키는 모습을 보이지 않았다(그래도 그녀는 블라인드 너머로 그들을 내다보고 있었다)."], ["Georgy ran off to see about breakfast.", "조지는 아침 식사를 챙기러 달려갔다."], ["Emmy, whose shawl and bonnet were off in the passage in the hands of Mrs. Payne, now went to undo the clasp of William's cloak, and--we will, if you please, go with George, and look after breakfast for the Colonel.", "통로에서 페인 부인의 손에 숄과 보닛을 벗어 맡긴 에미는, 이제 윌리엄의 외투 걸쇠를 끄르러 다가갔는데—우리는, 괜찮으시다면, 조지와 함께 가서 연대장의 아침 식사나 챙겨 보기로 하자."], ["The vessel is in port.", "배는 항구에 들었다."], ["He has got the prize he has been trying for all his life.", "그는 평생토록 좇아 온 그 상을 마침내 손에 넣은 것이다."], ["The bird has come in at last.", "새가 마침내 품에 날아든 것이다."], ["There it is with its head on his shoulder, billing and cooing close up to his heart, with soft outstretched fluttering wings.", "보라, 그 새가 머리를 그의 어깨에 기댄 채, 그의 가슴 바로 곁에서 부리를 비비며 구구거리고, 보드라운 날개를 펼쳐 파닥이고 있지 않은가. 이것이야말로 그가 십팔 년 동안 날마다 시각마다 청해 온 것이다."], ["This is what he has asked for every day and hour for eighteen years.", "이것이야말로 그가 애태우며 그리던 것이다. 자, 여기 있다."], ["This is what he pined after.", "정점이요, 끝이다. 제3권 마지막 페이지다."], ["Here it is--the summit, the end--the last page of the third volume.", "잘 가오, 연대장. 신의 가호가 있기를, 정직한 윌리엄이여!"], ["Good-bye, Colonel--God bless you, honest William!--Farewell, dear Amelia--Grow green again, tender little parasite, round the rugged old oak to which you cling!", "잘 가오, 사랑하는 애밀리아여. 그대가 매달려 온 그 우툴두툴한 늙은 떡갈나무를 휘감고, 부드러운 자그마한 덩굴이여, 다시 푸르게 자라나기를!"]], [["Perhaps it was compunction towards the kind and simple creature, who had been the first in life to defend her, perhaps it was a dislike to all such sentimental scenes--but Rebecca, satisfied with her part in the transaction, never presented herself before Colonel Dobbin and the lady whom he married.", "어쩌면 그것은 인생에서 맨 처음으로 자기를 감싸 준 그 다정하고 꾸밈없는 사람에 대한 양심의 가책이었을지도 모르고, 어쩌면 그 모든 감상적인 장면에 대한 거부감이었을지도 모른다. 그러나 레베카는 이 일에서 자신이 맡은 역할에 만족하여, 도빈 연대장과 그가 결혼한 부인 앞에는 끝내 모습을 드러내지 않았다."], ["\"Particular business,\" she said, took her to Bruges, whither she went, and only Georgy and his uncle were present at the marriage ceremony.", "\"긴한 볼일\"이 있어 브뤼주에 가야 한다며 그녀는 그리로 떠났고, 결혼식 자리에는 조지와 그 외삼촌만이 참석했다."], ["When it was over, and Georgy had rejoined his parents, Mrs. Becky returned (just for a few days) to comfort the solitary bachelor, Joseph Sedley.", "식이 끝나고 조지가 부모에게로 돌아가자, 베키 부인은 (그저 며칠간) 돌아와 홀로 남은 독신자 조지프 세들리를 위로했다."], ["He preferred a continental life, he said, and declined to join in housekeeping with his sister and her husband.", "그는 대륙에서의 삶이 더 좋다며, 누이며 그 남편과 한 살림을 차리기를 마다했던 것이다."]], [["Emmy was very glad in her heart to think that she had written to her husband before she read or knew of that letter of George's.", "에미는 자신이 조지의 그 편지를 읽거나 알게 되기 전에 이미 남편에게 편지를 썼다는 사실을 마음속으로 무척 다행스러워했다. \"나는 처음부터 다 알고 있었소.\""], ["\"I knew it all along,\"", "윌리엄이 말했다."], ["William said; \"but could I use that weapon against the poor fellow's memory?", "\"하지만 내가 그 가엾은 친구에 대한 추억을 두고 그런 무기를 쓸 수 있었겠소?"], ["It was that which made me suffer so when you--\"", "당신이 그때 그랬을 때 내가 그토록 괴로웠던 것도 바로 그것 때문이었소.\""]], [["\"Never speak of that day again,\"", "\"그날 이야기는 다신 꺼내지 말아요.\""], ["Emmy cried out, so contrite and humble that William turned off the conversation by his account of Glorvina and dear old Peggy O'Dowd, with whom he was sitting when the letter of recall reached him.", "에미가 어찌나 뉘우치며 겸허하게 외치던지, 윌리엄은 화제를 돌려, 소환 편지가 당도했을 때 자기가 함께 앉아 있던 글로비나와 그리운 페기 오다우드 이야기를 들려주었다. \"당신이 나를 부르지 않았더라면,\""], ["\"If you hadn't sent for me,\"", "그가 웃으며 덧붙였다."], ["he added with a laugh, \"who knows what Glorvina's name might be now?\"", "\"지금쯤 글로비나의 성이 무엇이 되어 있을지 누가 알겠소?\""]], [["At present it is Glorvina Posky (now Mrs.", "지금 그녀의 이름은 글로비나 포스키(이제 포스키 소령 부인)다."], ["Major Posky); she took him on the death of his first wife, having resolved never to marry out of the regiment.", "그녀는 결코 연대 바깥의 사람과는 결혼하지 않겠다고 작정한 터라, 포스키의 첫 부인이 죽자 그를 차지한 것이다."], ["Lady O'Dowd is also so attached to it that, she says, if anything were to happen to Mick, bedad she'd come back and marry some of 'em.", "오다우드 부인 역시 연대에 어찌나 애착이 깊던지, 만일 믹에게 무슨 일이라도 생기면 맹세코 돌아와 연대원 가운데 누군가와 결혼하겠노라고 말한다."], ["But the Major-General is quite well and lives in great splendour at O'Dowdstown, with a pack of beagles, and (with the exception of perhaps their neighbour, Hoggarty of Castle Hoggarty) he is the first man of his county.", "그러나 그 소장은 더없이 정정하여 오다우드스타운에서 비글 사냥개 한 무리를 거느리고 대단히 호화롭게 지내고 있으니, (어쩌면 그들의 이웃인 캐슬호가티의 호가티를 빼고는) 그 고을에서 으뜸가는 인물이다."], ["Her Ladyship still dances jigs, and insisted on standing up with the Master of the Horse at the Lord Lieutenant's last ball.", "부인은 여전히 지그 춤을 추며, 부총독의 지난번 무도회에서는 기마관과 짝을 이루어 춤을 추겠다고 우겨 댔다."], ["Both she and Glorvina declared that Dobbin had used the latter SHEAMFULLY, but Posky falling in, Glorvina was consoled, and a beautiful turban from Paris appeased the wrath of Lady O'Dowd.", "그녀와 글로비나 모두 도빈이 글로비나를 \"수치스럽게(SHEAMFULLY)\" 대했노라고 단언했으나, 포스키가 끼어드는 바람에 글로비나는 위안을 얻었고, 파리에서 온 아름다운 터번 하나가 오다우드 부인의 노여움을 가라앉혔다."]], [["When Colonel Dobbin quitted the service, which he did immediately after his marriage, he rented a pretty little country place in Hampshire, not far from Queen's Crawley, where, after the passing of the Reform Bill, Sir Pitt and his family constantly resided now.", "도빈 연대장은 결혼하자마자 곧 군에서 물러났는데, 그러고는 햄프셔에 있는 자그마하고 어여쁜 시골 거처 하나를 세냈으니, 퀸스크롤리에서 그리 멀지 않은 곳이었다. 그곳에는 선거법 개정안이 통과된 뒤로 이제 핏 경과 그 가족이 늘 머물고 있었다."], ["All idea of a Peerage was out of the question, the Baronet's two seats in Parliament being lost.", "작위 따위는 어림도 없는 이야기가 되었으니, 준남작이 의회에 가지고 있던 두 의석을 잃었기 때문이다."], ["He was both out of pocket and out of spirits by that catastrophe, failed in his health, and prophesied the speedy ruin of the Empire.", "그는 그 재앙으로 주머니도 비고 의기도 꺾여, 건강이 상한 데다 머잖아 제국이 멸망하리라는 예언까지 늘어놓았다."]], [["Lady Jane and Mrs. Dobbin became great friends--there was a perpetual crossing of pony-chaises between the Hall and the Evergreens, the Colonel's place (rented of his friend Major Ponto, who was abroad with his family).", "제인 영부인과 도빈 부인은 둘도 없는 벗이 되었다."], ["Her Ladyship was godmother to Mrs. Dobbin's child, which bore her name, and was christened by the Rev.", "본관과, 연대장의 거처인 에버그린스 사이에는 끊임없이 조랑말 마차가 오갔다(이 거처는 연대장이, 가족과 함께 외국에 나가 있던 벗 폰토 소령에게서 세낸 것이었다)."], ["James Crawley, who succeeded his father in the living: and a pretty close friendship subsisted between the two lads, George and Rawdon, who hunted and shot together in the vacations, were both entered of the same college at Cambridge, and quarrelled with each other about Lady Jane's daughter, with whom they were both, of course, in love.", "영부인은 도빈 부인의 아이의 대모가 되어, 아이는 그녀의 이름을 받았고, 아버지의 뒤를 이어 그 성직록을 물려받은 제임스 크롤리 목사에게서 세례를 받았다. 그리고 두 소년, 조지와 로던 사이에는 제법 두터운 우정이 자리 잡았으니, 둘은 방학이면 함께 사냥과 총질을 다녔고, 케임브리지의 같은 학료에 나란히 입학했으며, 제인 영부인의 딸을 두고 서로 다투었다."], ["A match between George and that young lady was long a favourite scheme of both the matrons, though I have heard that Miss Crawley herself inclined towards her cousin.", "물론 둘 다 그 아가씨에게 반해 있었던 것이다. 조지와 그 아가씨를 짝지으려는 것은 오래도록 두 안주인이 즐겨 품어 온 계획이었으나, 정작 크롤리 양 본인은 사촌 쪽으로 마음이 기울었다고 나는 들었다."]], [["Mrs. Rawdon Crawley's name was never mentioned by either family.", "로던 크롤리 부인의 이름은 어느 집안에서도 입에 오르는 법이 없었다."], ["There were reasons why all should be silent regarding her.", "그녀에 관해서라면 모두가 입을 다물 만한 이유들이 있었던 것이다."], ["For wherever Mr. Joseph Sedley went, she travelled likewise, and that infatuated man seemed to be entirely her slave.", "조지프 세들리 씨가 가는 곳마다 그녀 역시 함께 다녔으니, 그 정신 나간 사내는 완전히 그녀의 노예가 된 듯 보였다."], ["The Colonel's lawyers informed him that his brother-in-law had effected a heavy insurance upon his life, whence it was probable that he had been raising money to discharge debts.", "연대장의 변호사들은 그에게, 처남이 자기 생명에 거액의 보험을 들어 두었다고 알려 왔으니, 이로 미루어 그가 빚을 갚느라 돈을 끌어다 쓴 모양이라고 했다."], ["He procured prolonged leave of absence from the East India House, and indeed, his infirmities were daily increasing.", "그는 동인도 회사로부터 장기 휴가를 얻어 냈는데, 실로 그의 병세는 날로 깊어 가고 있었다."]], [["On hearing the news about the insurance, Amelia, in a good deal of alarm, entreated her husband to go to Brussels, where Jos then was, and inquire into the state of his affairs.", "보험에 관한 소식을 듣자, 애밀리아는 적잖이 놀라, 남편에게 당시 조스가 있던 브뤼셀로 가서 그의 형편이 어떠한지 알아봐 달라고 간청했다."], ["The Colonel quitted home with reluctance (for he was deeply immersed in his History of the Punjaub which still occupies him, and much alarmed about his little daughter, whom he idolizes, and who was just recovering from the chicken-pox) and went to Brussels and found Jos living at one of the enormous hotels in that city. Mrs.", "연대장은 마지못해 집을 나섰으니(그는 지금도 여전히 매달려 있는 펀자브 역사 집필에 깊이 빠져 있던 데다, 자기가 그토록 끔찍이 아끼는, 마침 수두에서 막 회복되어 가던 어린 딸 때문에 몹시 마음을 졸이던 터였다), 브뤼셀로 가서 보니 조스는 그 도시의 한 거대한 호텔에서 지내고 있었다."], ["Crawley, who had her carriage, gave entertainments, and lived in a very genteel manner, occupied another suite of apartments in the same hotel.", "자기 마차를 두고 손님을 청해 대접하며 자못 고상하게 살던 크롤리 부인은, 같은 호텔의 다른 객실을 쓰고 있었다."]], [["The Colonel, of course, did not desire to see that lady, or even think proper to notify his arrival at Brussels, except privately to Jos by a message through his valet.", "연대장은 물론 그 부인을 만날 마음이 없었고, 자기가 브뤼셀에 도착했다는 것조차도 시종을 통해 조스에게만 은밀히 알리는 것이 마땅하다고 여겼다."], ["Jos begged the Colonel to come and see him that night, when Mrs. Crawley would be at a soiree, and when they could meet alone.", "조스는 크롤리 부인이 야회에 나가 둘이 단둘이 만날 수 있는 그날 밤에 와 달라고 연대장에게 청했다."], ["He found his brother-in-law in a condition of pitiable infirmity--and dreadfully afraid of Rebecca, though eager in his praises of her.", "그는 가엾으리만치 쇠약해진 처남을 보았는데, 처남은 레베카를 입에 침이 마르게 칭찬하면서도 그녀를 끔찍이 두려워하고 있었다."], ["She tended him through a series of unheard-of illnesses with a fidelity most admirable.", "그녀는 듣도 보도 못한 온갖 병을 앓는 그를 더없이 갸륵한 정성으로 간병했다는 것이다."], ["She had been a daughter to him.", "그녀는 그에게 친딸이나 다름없었다고 했다."], ["\"But--but--oh, for God's sake, do come and live near me, and--and--see me sometimes,\"", "\"하지만, 하지만, 오, 제발 와서 내 곁에서 살아 주게, 그리고, 그리고 이따금 나를 좀 보러 와 주게.\""], ["whimpered out the unfortunate man.", "이 가엾은 사내가 흐느끼듯 말했다."]], [["The Colonel's brow darkened at this.", "이 말에 연대장의 이마가 어두워졌다."], ["\"We can't, Jos,\" he said.", "\"그럴 순 없네, 조스.\""], ["\"Considering the circumstances, Amelia can't visit you.\"", "그가 말했다. \"사정이 사정인지라, 애밀리아는 자네를 찾아올 수 없네.\""]], [["\"I swear to you--I swear to you on the Bible,\"", "\"맹세코, 성경에 두고 맹세코,\""], ["gasped out Joseph, wanting to kiss the book, \"that she is as innocent as a child, as spotless as your own wife.\"", "조지프가 성경에 입 맞추려 들며 헐떡이듯 말했다. \"그 사람은 어린아이처럼 결백하고, 자네 아내만큼이나 한 점 티가 없는 사람이야.\""]], [["\"It may be so,\"", "\"그럴지도 모르지.\""], ["said the Colonel gloomily, \"but Emmy can't come to you.", "연대장이 침울하게 말했다. \"하지만 에미는 자네에게 올 수 없네."], ["Be a man, Jos: break off this disreputable connection.", "사내답게 굴게, 조스. 이 추잡한 관계를 끊어 버리게."], ["Come home to your family.", "자네 가족에게로 돌아오게."], ["We hear your affairs are involved.\"", "자네 형편이 얽혀 있다고들 하더군.\""]], [["\"Involved!\"", "\"얽혀 있다고!\""], ["cried Jos.", "조스가 외쳤다."], ["\"Who has told such calumnies?", "\"누가 그런 모함을 한단 말인가?"], ["All my money is placed out most advantageously.", "내 돈은 죄다 더없이 유리하게 굴려지고 있어."], ["Mrs. Crawley--that is--I mean--it is laid out to the best interest.\"", "크롤리 부인이, 그러니까, 내 말은, 가장 이문이 많이 남도록 굴려 두었다고.\""]], [["\"You are not in debt, then?", "\"그럼 빚은 없다는 건가?"], ["Why did you insure your life?\"", "그런데 어째서 생명 보험을 들었나?\""]], [["\"I thought--a little present to her--in case anything happened; and you know my health is so delicate--common gratitude you know--and I intend to leave all my money to you--and I can spare it out of my income, indeed I can,\" cried out William's weak brother-in-law.", "\"그건 생각해 보니, 그 사람에게 작은 선물이라도, 혹시 무슨 일이 생길 경우에 말이야. 게다가 자네도 알다시피 내 건강이 워낙 부실하니, 그저 흔한 도리상의 고마움이라고나 할까. 그리고 내 돈은 죄다 자네에게 남길 작정이야. 내 수입에서 그 정도는 떼어 둘 여유가 있어, 정말이라니까.\" 윌리엄의 그 나약한 처남이 외쳤다."]], [["The Colonel besought Jos to fly at once--to go back to India, whither Mrs. Crawley could not follow him; to do anything to break off a connection which might have the most fatal consequences to him.", "연대장은 조스에게 당장 달아나라고, 크롤리 부인이 따라올 수 없는 인도로 돌아가라고, 무슨 수를 써서라도 그에게 더없이 치명적인 결과를 가져올지 모를 그 관계를 끊으라고 간곡히 권했다."]], [["Jos clasped his hands and cried, \"He would go back to India.", "조스는 두 손을 맞잡고 부르짖었다. 인도로 돌아가겠노라고."], ["He would do anything, only he must have time: they mustn't say anything to Mrs.", "무엇이든 하겠으나 다만 시간이 좀 필요하다고. 크롤리 부인에게는 아무 말도 해선 안 된다고."], ["Crawley--she'd--she'd kill me if she knew it.", "그녀가, 그녀가 이 일을 알면 자기를 죽일 거라고."], ["You don't know what a terrible woman she is,\"", "\"자넨 그 사람이 얼마나 무서운 여자인지 몰라.\""], ["the poor wretch said.", "가엾은 가련한 사내가 말했다."]], [["\"Then, why not come away with me?\"", "\"그렇다면, 어째서 나와 함께 떠나지 않나?\""], ["said Dobbin in reply; but Jos had not the courage.", "도빈이 대답으로 말했다. 그러나 조스에게는 그럴 용기가 없었다."], ["\"He would see Dobbin again in the morning; he must on no account say that he had been there. He must go now.", "아침에 도빈을 다시 만나겠노라고, 자기가 여기 다녀갔다는 말은 절대 해선 안 된다고, 이제 그만 가야 한다고, 베키가 들어올지도 모른다고."], ["Becky might come in.\" And Dobbin quitted him, full of forebodings.", "그리하여 도빈은 온갖 불길한 예감에 사로잡힌 채 그의 곁을 떠났다."]], [["He never saw Jos more.", "그는 다시는 조스를 보지 못했다."], ["Three months afterwards Joseph Sedley died at Aix-la-Chapelle.", "석 달 뒤 조지프 세들리는 엑스라샤펠에서 죽었다."], ["It was found that all his property had been muddled away in speculations, and was represented by valueless shares in different bubble companies.", "알고 보니 그의 재산은 죄다 투기로 흐지부지 날아가, 갖가지 거품 회사의 휴지나 다름없는 주식으로 남아 있었다."], ["All his available assets were the two thousand pounds for which his life was insured, and which were left equally between his beloved \"sister Amelia, wife of, &c., and his friend and invaluable attendant during sickness, Rebecca, wife of Lieutenant-Colonel Rawdon Crawley, C.B.,\"", "그가 처분할 수 있는 자산이라곤 자기 생명에 든 보험금 이천 파운드뿐이었으니, 그것은 그가 사랑하는 \"누이 애밀리아, 즉 누구누구의 아내와, 병중에 둘도 없이 귀한 간병인이자 벗이었던 레베카, 즉 로던 크롤리 중령(바스 훈작사)의 아내\""], ["who was appointed administratrix.", "사이에 똑같이 나뉘었고, 후자가 유산 관리인으로 지명되었다."]], [["The solicitor of the insurance company swore it was the blackest case that ever had come before him, talked of sending a commission to Aix to examine into the death, and the Company refused payment of the policy.", "보험 회사의 사무 변호사는 이것이 일찍이 자기가 다룬 사건 가운데 가장 시커먼 사건이라고 단언하며, 그 죽음을 조사하기 위해 엑스로 조사단을 보내겠다고 말했고, 회사는 보험금 지급을 거부했다."], ["But Mrs., or Lady Crawley, as she styled herself, came to town at once (attended with her solicitors, Messrs. Burke, Thurtell, and Hayes, of Thavies Inn) and dared the Company to refuse the payment.", "그러나 크롤리 부인은, 혹은 스스로 일컫던 대로 크롤리 영부인은, 즉시 (테이비스 인의 사무 변호사 버크·서텔·헤이스 씨를 대동하고) 런던으로 와서 회사가 지급을 거부할 테면 거부해 보라고 으름장을 놓았다."], ["They invited examination, they declared that she was the object of an infamous conspiracy, which had been pursuing her all through life, and triumphed finally.", "그들은 조사를 청했고, 그녀야말로 평생토록 자신을 따라다닌 파렴치한 음모의 표적이라고 단언하며, 마침내 승리를 거두었다."], ["The money was paid, and her character established, but Colonel Dobbin sent back his share of the legacy to the insurance office and rigidly declined to hold any communication with Rebecca.", "보험금은 지급되었고 그녀의 평판도 바로 섰으나, 도빈 연대장은 유산 가운데 자기 몫을 보험 회사로 돌려보내고, 레베카와는 어떠한 왕래도 하지 않겠다고 단호히 거절했다."]], [["She never was Lady Crawley, though she continued so to call herself.", "그녀는 스스로 그리 일컫기를 그치지 않았으나 결코 크롤리 영부인이었던 적은 없었다."], ["His Excellency Colonel Rawdon Crawley died of yellow fever at Coventry Island, most deeply beloved and deplored, and six weeks before the demise of his brother, Sir Pitt.", "로던 크롤리 중령 각하는 코번트리 섬에서 황열병으로 죽었으니, 그의 형 핏 경이 세상을 뜨기 여섯 주 전의 일이었고, 더없이 깊은 사랑과 애도를 받았다."], ["The estate consequently devolved upon the present Sir Rawdon Crawley, Bart.", "그리하여 영지는 현재의 로던 크롤리 준남작 경에게로 넘어갔다."]], [["He, too, has declined to see his mother, to whom he makes a liberal allowance, and who, besides, appears to be very wealthy.", "그 역시 자기 어머니를 만나기를 거절했는데, 그는 어머니에게 후한 생활비를 보내고 있거니와, 게다가 그녀는 형편이 매우 넉넉해 보이기까지 한다."], ["The Baronet lives entirely at Queen's Crawley, with Lady Jane and her daughter, whilst Rebecca, Lady Crawley, chiefly hangs about Bath and Cheltenham, where a very strong party of excellent people consider her to be a most injured woman.", "준남작은 줄곧 퀸스크롤리에서 제인 영부인과 그 딸과 함께 지내고, 한편 레베카, 즉 크롤리 영부인은 주로 배스와 첼트넘 언저리를 맴도는데, 그곳에서는 더없이 훌륭한 사람들로 이루어진 막강한 무리가 그녀를 천하에 둘도 없이 억울하게 누명을 쓴 여인으로 여기고 있다. 그녀에게는 적들이 있다."], ["She has her enemies.", "적 없는 이가 어디 있으랴?"], ["Who has not? Her life is her answer to them.", "그녀의 삶이 곧 그들에게 보내는 대답이다."], ["She busies herself in works of piety.", "그녀는 경건한 일에 분주히 매달린다."], ["She goes to church, and never without a footman.", "교회에 나가는데, 결코 하인을 거느리지 않는 법이 없다."], ["Her name is in all the Charity Lists.", "그녀의 이름은 온갖 자선 명부에 올라 있다."], ["The destitute orange-girl, the neglected washerwoman, the distressed muffin-man find in her a fast and generous friend.", "헐벗은 오렌지 파는 소녀, 외면당한 빨래하는 아낙, 곤궁한 머핀 장수가 그녀에게서 변함없고 너그러운 벗을 만난다."], ["She is always having stalls at Fancy Fairs for the benefit of these hapless beings.", "그녀는 이 가엾은 이들을 위해 늘 자선 시장에 좌판을 벌인다."], ["Emmy, her children, and the Colonel, coming to London some time back, found themselves suddenly before her at one of these fairs.", "얼마 전 런던에 온 에미와 그 아이들과 연대장은, 그런 한 자선 시장에서 별안간 그녀와 마주쳤다."], ["She cast down her eyes demurely and smiled as they started away from her; Emmy scurrying off on the arm of George (now grown a dashing young gentleman) and the Colonel seizing up his little Janey, of whom he is fonder than of anything in the world--fonder even than of his History of the Punjaub.", "그녀는 다소곳이 눈을 내리깔고 미소 지었으나, 그들은 그녀를 피해 화들짝 물러났다. 에미는 (이제 늠름한 젊은 신사로 자란) 조지의 팔에 매달려 종종걸음으로 달아났고, 연대장은 세상 그 무엇보다도—심지어 자기 펀자브 역사보다도—더 끔찍이 아끼는 어린 제이니를 와락 안아 들었다."]], [["\"Fonder than he is of me,\"", "\"나보다 더 끔찍이 아끼지.\""], ["Emmy thinks with a sigh.", "에미는 한숨지으며 생각한다."], ["But he never said a word to Amelia that was not kind and gentle, or thought of a want of hers that he did not try to gratify.", "그러나 그는 애밀리아에게 다정하고 부드럽지 않은 말이라곤 한마디도 한 적이 없으며, 그녀의 바람이라면 무엇이든 채워 주려 애쓰지 않은 적이 없다."]], [["Ah!", "아!"], ["Vanitas Vanitatum!", "헛되고 헛되도다(Vanitas Vanitatum)!"], ["which of us is happy in this world?", "우리 가운데 누가 이 세상에서 행복한가?"], ["Which of us has his desire?", "누가 제 소원을 이루었는가? 혹은 이루고도 만족하는가?"], ["or, having it, is satisfied?--come, children, let us shut up the box and the puppets, for our play is played out.", "자, 얘들아, 이제 상자와 꼭두각시들을 챙겨 넣자꾸나. 우리 연극은 다 끝났으니."]]];
